const COMMENTARY = {"1 Chronicles 12:16–18": {"title": "\"We are yours, O David\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David is not king yet. He is a fugitive in a stronghold, and armed men keep arriving — some from Saul's own tribe, which is exactly the problem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question he asks them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you come to me in peace, to help me, then my heart will be knit to yours. But if you come to betray me…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts it to them straight, and leaves the judging to God rather than interrogating them or turning them away. A hunted man cannot afford either paranoia or naivety, and David does something in between: he names both possibilities out loud and waits."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Spirit comes on Amasai, and what comes out is not a speech but a chant, three beats of *peace*:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "Peace, peace to you, and peace to all who help you, for your God is your help."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not offer terms or negotiate a position. He declares whose side they are on and blesses the man they are joining — and the blessing is grounded not in David's prospects but in God being his help."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What David does with it.** He welcomed them and *made them leaders of his raiding bands* — command, straight away, to men who had been potential assassins ten minutes earlier. The chapter is a long list of people who came over to David before he had anything to give them, and the writer is keeping count on purpose."}]}, "1 Chronicles 12:23–37": {"title": "\"Men who understood the times\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A muster roll, tribe by tribe, and one entry in it is not counted the same way as the others."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the list is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The armed men who came to David at Hebron **to turn Saul's kingdom over to him**, just as the LORD had said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul is dead. David has been king over Judah alone for seven years, and this is the moment the other tribes come across."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chronicler counts them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the others are described"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every tribe gets a number and a military note:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["Judah — *carrying shield and spear*: 6,800", "Simeon — *brave warriors ready for war*: 7,100", "Zebulun — *seasoned soldiers with every weapon*: 50,000", "Asher — *seasoned for war*: 40,000", "and 120,000 from beyond the Jordan, *armed with every kind of weapon*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roughly three hundred and forty thousand men in total, described by their weapons and their readiness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then Issachar"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From Issachar, **men who understood the times and knew what Israel should do**: 200 chiefs, with all their kinsmen under their command.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two hundred. It is the smallest number of leaders in the entire list."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they are the only tribe whose contribution is not military. No shields, no spears, no seasoning for battle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They knew what time it was, and what to do about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Understood the times** — the Hebrew is discernment of the season, the moment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Knew what Israel should do** — knowledge that issues in action. Not analysis for its own sake."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Plenty of people can read a situation. The verse credits them with both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Issachar was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob's blessing over him was the saddest of the twelve: *a strong donkey, resting among the sheepfolds… he will bend his shoulder to the load and submit to hard labour.* See the note on Genesis 49:14–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tribe that put its shoulder under a burden it did not have to carry produced the men who could read a season."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Zebulun's line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Standing by David with **undivided hearts**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *not of a double heart*. Fifty thousand of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Benjamin's"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Saul's own kinsmen: 3,000, **though most of them had stayed loyal to the house of Saul until then**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chronicler is honest about it. Three thousand out of Saul's own tribe, and he notes that the rest had not come."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter ends with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All these men of war, who could keep ranks, came to Hebron with a loyal heart, to make David king over all Israel… and there was joy in Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they *were with David three days, eating and drinking, for their brethren had prepared for them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A three-day feast, with food sent down from as far as Naphtali on donkeys, camels, mules and oxen."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What David did not know how to read.** *When spring came around, the season when kings ride out to war… **but David stayed behind in Jerusalem**.* One sentence, and the chapter that follows is Bathsheba. See the note on 2 Samuel 11:1."}]}, "1 Chronicles 13:12–14": {"title": "The house it stopped at", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses, and two households come out of them differently."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fear"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That day **David was afraid of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *David feared God that day*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A verse earlier he was angry or grieved. Now he is frightened, and Chronicles records both without smoothing them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How can I ever bring the ark of God home to me?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A real question, and the right one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not know the answer. Two chapters later he does, and he says so out loud to the Levites: *we did not seek Him about the proper way*. See the note on 1 Chronicles 15:11–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The interval is what the question produces."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diversion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So he **did not take the ark into the City of David**; instead he had it **turned aside to the house of Obed-edom the Gittite**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six miles short. Thirty thousand men standing in a field, and the procession stops."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Obed-edom** — from **eved**, servant, and **Edom**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Chronicler lists him among the Levites, and the epithet **the Gittite** — a man of Gath — probably records where his family had lived."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gath is Goliath's town."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The duration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ark of God stayed with the family of Obed-edom in his house **three months**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the LORD blessed his household and everything he had**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *his house and all that he had*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel has the same sentence. Both accounts stop to record it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The point the two verses make together"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Uzzah touched it and died. Obed-edom housed it for three months and prospered, with everyone under his roof."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same object, six miles apart, within the same season."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ark was never the danger. The handling was, and Chronicles has already said so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What made David try again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then word reached David: \"The LORD has blessed Obed-edom's whole household.\"* See the note on 2 Samuel 6:12–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blessing is visible enough to be reported to a king."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had asked *how can the ark come to me?* — and the answer arrives as gossip about a neighbour's good fortune."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Obed-edom does afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not go back to farming."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he left **Obed-edom and his sixty-eight relatives** to serve with them, while Obed-edom son of Jeduthun and Hosah kept the gates.* See the note on 1 Chronicles 16:37–38."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He follows the ark to Jerusalem and takes a post."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened to his family"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Obed-edom had sons too… **for God had truly blessed him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight named sons, and sixty-two descendants listed as *capable men, strong for the work*. See the note on 1 Chronicles 26:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Chronicler, writing centuries later, is still counting them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the three months were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An interruption that nobody planned, in a house nobody had chosen, and it is the only part of the first attempt that went well."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two households prove.** Same ark, same season, six miles apart. One man died touching it and one family was blessed housing it — and the difference was never the object."}]}, "1 Chronicles 13:9–11": {"title": "The threshing floor of Kidon", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The Chronicler's account of the same event, and the differences are small and deliberate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The threshing floor of **Kidon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel calls it Nacon. The two names differ by a letter or two in Hebrew and both traditions were preserved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A threshing floor is a swept rock platform on high ground. Flat, exposed, and the surface changes underfoot."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stumble"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The oxen stumbled, and **Uzzah reached out his hand to steady the ark**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *to hold the ark*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The anger of the LORD burned against him **for putting his hand to the ark**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chronicles states it flatly. Samuel has *for his error*; the Chronicler names the act."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The death"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **he died there before God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel has *beside the ark*. Chronicles has *before God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole of the Chronicler's interest is in the presence rather than the object."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*David was **troubled** because the LORD had **broken out** against Uzzah.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew here is **charah** — burned, was angry, or was grieved. It is the same root used two verses earlier of God's anger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel is blunter: David *was angry*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And to this day that place is called **Perez-uzzah** — the breaking out against Uzzah.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Perets** — a breach, a bursting through."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David had used the same word joyfully three chapters earlier in Samuel, after a victory: *the LORD has broken through my enemies before me, like a breakthrough of water* — and named that place **Baal-perazim**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two places, two names, one noun."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Chronicler adds later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not leave the reader to work out what went wrong. David says it himself in chapter 15:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It was because you Levites did not carry it the first time that the LORD our God broke out against us, **for we did not seek Him about the proper way**.\"* See the note on 1 Chronicles 15:11–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel never explains. Chronicles does, in the king's own mouth, two chapters later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the instructions had been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Poles through rings, permanently installed, carried on the shoulders of Levites."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But they shall not touch any holy thing, lest they die.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The equipment was designed so nobody would ever need to reach out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the cart came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Philistines, who had no instructions and improvised. See the note on 1 Samuel 6:10–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel copied the improvisation and left the instructions in the book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Uzzah reached"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had lived with it. The ark had been in his father's house for a generation, and he and his brother were the ones driving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Familiarity is the thing the passage puts a finger on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David stops, and the ark spends three months somewhere else. See the note on 1 Chronicles 13:12–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the household it stops at is blessed for the duration, which is the fact that gets him moving again."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Chronicles states and Samuel does not.** The cause, twice — *for putting his hand to the ark* here, and *we did not seek Him about the proper way* in the king's own mouth two chapters on."}]}, "1 Chronicles 15:11–15": {"title": "Because we did not seek Him about it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David explains what went wrong the first time, and the sentence is unusually direct."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three months after Uzzah. The ark has been at Obed-edom's and the household has visibly prospered. See the note on 2 Samuel 6:9–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel records the second attempt as a triumph. Chronicles records the preparation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he summons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Zadok and Abiathar the priests, and the Levites **Uriel, Asaiah, Joel, Shemaiah, Eliel, and Amminadab**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six Levite family heads, named, with the number of their brethren given in the verses before — a hundred and twenty here, two hundred and twenty there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole thing is organised."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Consecrate yourselves**, you and your relatives, so that you may bring up the ark.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hitqaddeshu** — sanctify yourselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about consecration came into the first attempt. Thirty thousand men and a new cart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The admission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It was because **you Levites did not carry it the first time** that the LORD our God broke out against us, for **we did not seek Him about the proper way**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kammishpat** — according to the ordinance, the prescribed manner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two failures named: the wrong carriers, and no enquiry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second is the one David puts last, and it is the root of the first. Nobody looked it up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king, to the priests, about his own decision."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not blame Uzzah, and he does not blame the oxen. *We did not seek Him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the instruction had been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sons of Kohath shall come to carry them, **but they shall not touch any holy thing, lest they die**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *the poles shall be in the rings of the ark; they shall not be taken from it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The poles were permanently installed for exactly this. The equipment was designed so that nobody would ever need to touch it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The obedience"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the priests and Levites **consecrated themselves**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the Levites **carried the ark of God on their shoulders with the poles, just as Moses had commanded** according to the word of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**On their shoulders.** The Hebrew is **bikthephām** — on their shoulder."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ark's weight has been estimated at well over a hundred pounds, and the poles distributed it across four men walking six miles uphill."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb for carrying is **nasa** — to lift, bear, carry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the verb of Isaiah 53:4 — *surely He has **borne** our griefs*. See the note on Isaiah 53:4–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Levites bear the ark; the Servant bears the sicknesses. Same word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The help"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **God had helped the Levites who carried the ark**, they sacrificed seven bulls and seven rams.* See the note on 1 Chronicles 15:25–28."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chronicles notes that carrying it needed help, and that the help was given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the cart had been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Faster, easier, and borrowed from the Philistines — who had no instructions and got it right by accident with two milk cows. See the note on 1 Samuel 6:10–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel had the instructions and used the Philistine method."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David also arranged"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Singers. Chapter 15 lists them by name and by instrument, and chapter 16 has the psalm they sang: *oh, give thanks to the LORD! Call upon His name; make known His deeds among the peoples!*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clause David puts last.** *We did not seek Him about the proper way.* Not the cart, not the oxen, not the man who reached out — nobody had looked it up."}]}, "1 Chronicles 15:25–28": {"title": "Because God had helped them", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The ark finally moves, and Chronicles records one detail Samuel leaves out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*David and **the elders of Israel and the commanders of thousands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not thirty thousand handpicked men this time. The leadership, the priests, the Levites and the musicians."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From the house of Obed-edom, **with great rejoicing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three months earlier the procession had stopped there in fear. See the note on 2 Samuel 6:9–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **because God had helped the Levites who carried the ark**, they sacrificed **seven bulls and seven rams**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **be'zor ha'elohim** — in God's helping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chronicles states it as the reason for the sacrifice. They got the ark to Jerusalem and the thing they marked was that they had been helped to carry it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody died. Last time somebody had."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sacrifice was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven bulls and seven rams. Samuel has *an ox and a fatted calf* at six steps; Chronicles has the fuller count."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David wore"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*David was clothed in **a robe of fine linen**, as were **all the Levites who carried the ark, the singers, and Kenaniah** the leader of the music.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **butz** — fine white linen, the expensive kind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everybody in the procession is dressed the same. The king, the carriers, the singers and the choirmaster."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*David also wore **a linen ephod**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ephod is priests' clothing. He is not a priest, and he wears it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel notes the same detail and adds what his wife thought of it. See the note on 2 Samuel 6:12–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The noise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **shouts of joy**, with the sound of **the ram's horn and trumpets and cymbals**, with **harps and lyres ringing out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five instruments and a shout. Chronicles is written by somebody interested in the music department and lists the players by name in the previous verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Kenaniah was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Chief of the Levites, was instructor in charge of the music, because he was skilful.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he instructed about the carrying, because he was skilful*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A named professional with a job title, in a passage about doing it properly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The difference between the two attempts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "First: a new cart, thirty thousand men, no consecration, nobody consulted, and a death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Second: shoulders and poles, Levites consecrated, instructions checked, sacrifices, uniform linen, an appointed music director — and God's help noted as the reason for the thanksgiving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Chronicler is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Writing after the exile, for people rebuilding, and his interest is consistently in how worship was ordered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel tells the story. Chronicles tells the arrangements."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David does at the end of the day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 16: appoints Asaph and his brethren to minister before the ark *continually, as every day's work required*, and gives them a psalm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give thanks to the LORD! Call upon His name… **seek the LORD and His strength; seek His face evermore**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then everybody gets a loaf, a piece of meat and a raisin cake, and goes home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the ark stays"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the tent David pitched for it, in Jerusalem, with singers around it — while the tabernacle of Moses and the altar stayed at Gibeon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That arrangement is what Amos calls the fallen tent of David, and what James quotes at the Jerusalem council. See the note on Acts 15:13–18."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they sacrificed for.** Not the arrival. Chronicles says it plainly: *because God had helped the Levites who carried the ark* — the help with the lifting is what got the seven bulls."}]}, "1 Chronicles 16:37–38": {"title": "He stayed with it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The ark is finally in Jerusalem, and the staffing arrangements name a familiar man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ark has come up from Obed-edom's house on the shoulders of consecrated Levites, with sacrifices, singers and a linen ephod. See the note on 1 Chronicles 15:25–28."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David has given out bread, meat and raisin cakes to the whole crowd, and delivered a psalm — *oh, give thanks to the LORD! Call upon His name*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the roster."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first appointment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*David left **Asaph and his kinsmen** there before the ark of the covenant of the LORD, **to minister there regularly, according to each day's need**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *for the day's matter in its day*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Continuous. Not a festival arrangement — a daily one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Asaph is the chief musician, and twelve psalms carry his name, including *truly God is good to Israel, to such as are pure in heart. But as for me, my feet had almost stumbled*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he left **Obed-edom and his sixty-eight relatives** to serve with them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man whose house the ark had been parked at for three months. See the note on 1 Chronicles 13:12–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not go home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sixty-eight are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His household, and the number is worth setting beside chapter 26, where his descendants are counted at sixty-two, *capable men, strong for the work*. See the note on 1 Chronicles 26:6–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The post"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While **Obed-edom son of Jeduthun and Hosah kept the gates**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gatekeeper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a musician, not a priest. The man who opens up and locks the door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is happening six miles away"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses immediately after this:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he left Zadok the priest and his brethren the priests **before the tabernacle of the LORD at the high place that was at Gibeon**, to offer burnt offerings to the LORD on the altar of burnt offering regularly morning and evening.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two centres, at once."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tabernacle of Moses at Gibeon with the altar and the sacrifices and no ark. And a tent in Jerusalem with the ark, singers, and no veil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That arrangement runs until Solomon's temple, and it is what Amos means by *the tabernacle of David that is fallen*. See the note on Acts 15:13–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Obed-edom chose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tent with the ark in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had three months of it in his house. He appears to have decided that was the arrangement he wanted to keep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm they were given goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seek the LORD and His strength; **seek His face evermore**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Oh, give thanks to the LORD, for He is good! For His mercy endures forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last line becomes the refrain of Psalm 136, twenty-six times over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter ends with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then all the people departed, every man to his house; and David returned to bless his house.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everybody goes home, and the singers stay."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he did with the three months.** Kept them. The ark went up to Jerusalem and the man whose house it had been in went with it, and took a job on the door."}]}, "1 Chronicles 26:1–5": {"title": "For God had truly blessed him", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A staff roster, and the Chronicler stops in the middle of it to explain a family's size."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Duty rosters for the gatekeepers, assigned by lot to the four sides of the sanctuary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapters 23 to 27 are David's organisation of the temple staff before he dies: the Levites, the priests, the musicians, the gatekeepers, the treasurers, and the army divisions."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lists. Names and numbers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first family"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From the **Korahites**: Meshelemiah son of Kore, from the line of Asaph.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven sons, named in birth order — *Zechariah the firstborn, Jediael the second*, and so on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Korahites are descendants of the Korah who led the rebellion in Numbers and was swallowed by the ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers notes what happened to his family: *nevertheless the children of Korah did not die*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven psalms carry their name, including *as the deer pants for the water brooks*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second family"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Obed-edom had sons too.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight, named the same way: *Shemaiah the firstborn, Jehozabad the second, Joah the third, Sacar the fourth, Nethanel the fifth, Ammiel the sixth, Issachar the seventh, and Peullethai the eighth*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **God had truly blessed him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *for God blessed him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Chronicler is running a list and stops to add a reason for the number."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nowhere else in the roster does he explain why a man had a lot of sons."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the blessing started"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three months with the ark in his house, after the procession stopped six miles short of Jerusalem. See the note on 1 Chronicles 13:12–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD blessed his household and everything he had.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Chronicler wrote that sentence thirteen chapters earlier and is still tracking the consequences."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the eighth son's name means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Peullethai** — from **pe'ullah**, work or wages. Something like *my wages* or *my reward*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who these people were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Doorkeepers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not priests, not singers. The men who opened up in the morning, controlled who came in, and locked the gates at night."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were trusted, and were in charge of the storerooms and treasuries.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the next paragraph says about them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All these were descendants of Obed-edom — they, their sons, and their brothers — **sixty-two of them, capable men, strong for the work**.* See the note on 1 Chronicles 26:6–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The psalm about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed is every one who fears the LORD… your children like olive plants all around your table.* See the note on Psalms 128:3–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *behold, children are a heritage from the LORD*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the man himself asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The procession stopped at his house because David was frightened and needed somewhere to leave it. Obed-edom is not recorded volunteering, negotiating, or saying a word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He housed it for three months, and a chronicler counting doorkeepers four hundred years later is still explaining the size of his family."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clause that does not belong in a roster.** *For God had truly blessed him.* The Chronicler is listing names and numbers, and stops once, for one man, to say why the number was what it was."}]}, "1 Chronicles 26:6–11": {"title": "Capable men, strong for the work", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The roster continues, and the descriptions attached to one family are the notable part."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grandsons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Obed-edom's son **Shemaiah** also had sons who became **leaders in their father's house, for they were capable men**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **gibborei chayil**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chayil** — strength, ability, worth, and also wealth and an army. The range of the word is the point."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word in *a virtuous woman* — **eshet chayil**, a woman of chayil. See the note on Proverbs 31:10–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the angel's greeting to Gideon: *the LORD is with you, **mighty man of valour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Capable, worthy, strong, and effective, all in one noun."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Othni, Rephael, Obed, and Elzabad*, and two relatives, *Elihu and Semachiah*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six named grandsons, and four of the six have God's name inside them — Rephael, *God heals*; Elzabad, *God has given*; Elihu, *He is my God*; Semachiah, *the LORD has sustained*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The count"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All these were descendants of Obed-edom — they, their sons, and their brothers — **sixty-two of them, capable men, strong for the work**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sixty-two, from one household."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Chronicler has already explained the number: *for God had truly blessed him*. See the note on 1 Chronicles 26:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase repeated"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Strong for the work** — the Hebrew is *strength for the service*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chayil twice in three verses, of the same family."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Meshelemiah had sons and brothers, able men — **eighteen** in all.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hosah's sons and brothers came to **thirteen**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eighteen and thirteen. Obed-edom's line is sixty-two, and the Chronicler gives all three figures on the same page."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other detail"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hosah, of the Merarites, had sons: **Shimri the chief (his father made him chief though he was not the firstborn)**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An aside about a family arrangement that nobody had to record, and the Chronicler records it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pattern runs through the whole Bible — Isaac over Ishmael, Jacob over Esau, Judah over Reuben, Ephraim over Manasseh, David over seven brothers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the job was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gatekeepers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter goes on to assign them by lot: east, north, south, and the storehouse. Twenty-four posts, staffed continuously."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were trusted, and were in charge of the storerooms and treasuries.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Doorkeepers with the keys to the treasury."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The psalm about the post"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For a day in Your courts is better than a thousand. I would rather **be a doorkeeper in the house of my God** than dwell in the tents of wickedness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 84, written by the sons of Korah — the other family on this roster."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the family started"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A house six miles outside Jerusalem, and three months of storing a box nobody else would take. See the note on 1 Chronicles 13:12–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred years on, a scribe is counting sixty-two of his descendants and calling them strong for the work."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word used twice of one family.** *Chayil* — capable, worthy, strong, and wealthy, all in one noun. Sixty-two of them, against eighteen and thirteen in the two families listed alongside."}]}, "1 Chronicles 28:11–12": {"title": "The plans handed over", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David is not allowed to build the temple, so he does everything except build it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he hands over"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*David handed Solomon **the plans** — the design for the porch, the buildings, the storerooms, the upper rooms, the inner rooms, and **the room for the mercy seat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *plans* is **tabnith** — a pattern, a model, an architectural drawing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same word used of what Moses was shown on the mountain: *see that you make all things according to the **pattern** shown you on the mountain.* See the note on Exodus 25:31–40."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both buildings were copied from something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gave him the plan for everything **the Spirit had laid on his heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew reads *the pattern of all that he had by the Spirit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David worked out the specification, and the chapter says where the specification came from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the list, and then the verses following: the courses of priests, the weight of gold for each lampstand, the weight of silver for each table, the gold for the forks and basins by weight, the refined gold for the altar of incense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is left to Solomon's judgement. The design is complete down to the cutlery."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"All of this,\" David said, \"**the LORD made clear to me in writing, His own hand upon me** — every detail of the plan, made plain.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the LORD made me understand in writing by His hand upon me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David wrote it down, and says the understanding was given while he wrote."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same combination as Moses at Sinai — a pattern shown, and a man producing the drawing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he could not build"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Earlier in the chapter: *\"You shall not build a house for My name, **because you have been a man of war** and have shed blood.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is refused the job and not refused the involvement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gathered the materials: gold, silver, bronze, iron, timber, onyx, marble — and then gave his own private treasure on top of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because I have set my affection on the house of my God, I have given to the house of my God, **over and above all that I have prepared for the holy house, my own special treasure**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man builds a house he will never see, out of money he did not have to give, to a design he wrote down and handed to somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says to Solomon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Be strong and of good courage, and do it; **do not fear nor be dismayed**, for the LORD God — my God — will be with you. **He will not leave you nor forsake you**, until you have finished all the work.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he told the assembly.** *All things come from You, and of Your own we have given You.* He raised the largest building fund in Israel's history and then said nothing in it had been theirs to begin with."}]}, "1 Chronicles 28:4–5": {"title": "Out of, out of, out of", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David's last public speech, and it is a series of narrowings."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The occasion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now David assembled at Jerusalem **all the leaders of Israel**: the officers of the tribes, the captains of the divisions, the captains over thousands, the stewards over all the substance and possessions of the king and of his sons, with the officials, the valiant men, and all the mighty men of valour.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everybody who mattered, and he *rose to his feet*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An old man standing up to talk about a building he will not see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I had it in my heart to build a house of rest for the ark of the covenant of the LORD… **but God said to me, \"You shall not build a house for My name, because you have been a man of war and have shed blood.\"***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells the assembly the refusal before anything else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Yet the LORD, the God of Israel, **chose me out of my whole family** to be king over Israel forever.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Out of Judah** He chose the leaders.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **out of Judah my father's house**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **out of my father's sons** He was pleased with me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four narrowings, and each one is inside the last: the tribe, the clan, the household, the man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb is **bachar** — chose — used at every stage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it corrects"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah was not the firstborn. Reuben was, and the birthright went to Joseph's sons."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Chronicler says so a few chapters earlier: *the birthright was Joseph's… **yet Judah prevailed over his brothers**, and from him came a ruler*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Jesse's household was not distinguished. And David was the youngest of eight, left with the sheep when the prophet came to dinner: *there remains yet the youngest, and there he is, keeping the sheep*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fifth narrowing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **out of all my sons** — for the LORD gave me many — He chose **Solomon** to sit on the throne.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Solomon was not the eldest either. Amnon, Absalom and Adonijah all came first, and all three died badly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And he is Bathsheba's son, which the Chronicler does not mention and Samuel does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The throne"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To sit on the throne of **the LORD's kingdom** over Israel.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not David's kingdom. The Chronicler consistently calls it God's throne with a man sitting on it: *Solomon sat on the throne of the LORD as king instead of David his father*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David hands over"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The plans."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then David gave his son Solomon the plans for the vestibule, its houses, its treasuries… **by the Spirit**, of the courts of the house of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**All this,\" said David, \"the LORD made me understand in writing, by His hand upon me, all the works of these plans**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was not allowed to build it and he was given the drawings."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As for you, my son Solomon, **know the God of your father, and serve Him with a loyal heart and with a willing mind**… **If you seek Him, He will be found by you; but if you forsake Him, He will cast you off forever**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Be strong and of good courage, and do it; **do not fear nor be dismayed, for the LORD God — my God — will be with you. He will not leave you nor forsake you** until you have finished all the work.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same words Moses gave Joshua, from a father to a son."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had already done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stockpiled the materials with his own resources: *because I have set my affection on the house of my God, I have given to the house of my God, over and above all that I have prepared, my own special treasure of gold and silver*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the narrowing ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The book of the genealogy of Jesus Christ, the Son of David, the Son of Abraham.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah, Jesse, David, Solomon — the first fourteen names of Matthew's list run straight through this speech."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The shape of the sentence.** Out of the family, out of Judah, out of my father's house, out of my father's sons — and then out of all my sons. Five narrowings, and every one uses the word *chose*."}]}, "1 Chronicles 29:12": {"title": "\"Wealth and honor come from You\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An old king prays in front of the nation after the largest offering in Israel's history."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David has assembled everything for the temple he will not live to build — gold, silver, bronze, iron, timber, onyx, marble — and given his own treasure on top of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the leaders gave, and the people, *and they rejoiced, for they offered willingly, because with a loyal heart they offered willingly to the LORD*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This prayer follows immediately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yours, O LORD, is the greatness, the power and the glory, the victory and the majesty; for all that is in heaven and in earth is Yours.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five nouns, and then a claim about everything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Wealth and honor come from You**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **osher** — riches — and **kavod**, glory, weight, honour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it holding an inventory of what has just been donated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You rule over everything**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two in His hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**In Your hand is power and might**; in Your hand **it lies to make great and to give strength to all**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *to make great and to give strength*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verbs are causative. Whatever greatness or strength anybody has, the sentence puts the source somewhere else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **who am I, and who are my people, that we should be able to offer so willingly**? For **all things come from You, and of Your own we have given You**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence the prayer turns on. The gift was made out of what had been received."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes further: *for **we are aliens and pilgrims before You**, as were all our fathers; our days on earth are as a shadow, and without hope.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said by the king of Israel at the height of the kingdom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not for himself. *Give my son Solomon a loyal heart, to keep Your commandments… and to build the temple for which I have made provision.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David had already said about the temple"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The house to be built for the LORD must be exceedingly magnificent… **now for my house I have prepared with all my might**\"* — and he lists three thousand talents of gold from his own possession, *over and above all that I have prepared for the holy house*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was not asking the people for what he had not given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the phrase turns up again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Solomon, asked in a dream what he wants, asks for an understanding heart to judge the people. And God's answer: *I have also given you what you have not asked, **both riches and honour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two words, given to the son of the man who prayed this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So David the son of Jesse reigned over all Israel. And the time that he reigned was forty years… **and he died in a good old age, full of days and riches and honour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chronicler closes the account with the two words from the prayer."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**When it was said.** Not in poverty. Standing in front of the largest voluntary offering the nation ever gave, with the inventory in front of him, David says the wealth came from somewhere else and that the giving was out of what was received."}]}, "1 Chronicles 29:26–28": {"title": "The death of David", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses to close a reign, and the Chronicler's version leaves out most of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **David, the son of Jesse**, reigned over all Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **David ben-Yishai**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Called the son of Jesse at the end, as he was at the beginning — the eighth son, out with the sheep when Samuel came."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He reigned over Israel **forty years** — **seven years in Hebron** and **thirty-three years in Jerusalem**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven and a half in Hebron over Judah alone, while Ish-bosheth held the north."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the elders came and *anointed David king over Israel*, and he took the stronghold of Zion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The formula"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he died **at a good old age, full of days, full of riches and honor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **seivah tovah sava yamim osher vekavod**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sava yamim** — satisfied of days. The phrase used of Abraham and Isaac. See the note on Genesis 25:7–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Chronicler leaves out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bathsheba. Uriah. Nathan and *you are the man*. The child that died."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amnon and Tamar. Absalom at the gate, and the flight from Jerusalem barefoot, and *O my son Absalom*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Adonijah declaring himself king while David lay dying."}, {"k": "p", "t": "All of it is in Samuel and Kings. None of it is in Chronicles."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chronicles is written for the returned exiles, and its subject is the temple and the line that built it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not concealment — the other books were in circulation. It is a different book with a different purpose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Chronicles keeps instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ark brought up with singers and instruments. The census and the plague and the threshing floor of Ornan. The plans for the temple, the materials gathered, the Levites organised into courses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And this chapter: David giving three thousand talents of gold from his own treasure, and the leaders giving after him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer just before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Yours, O LORD, is the greatness, the power and the glory, the victory and the majesty**… **But who am I, and who are my people**, that we should be able to offer so willingly as this? For **all things come from You, and of Your own we have given You**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last recorded prayer of David, offered over a building fund."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about the span"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **we are aliens and pilgrims before You**, as were all our fathers; **our days on earth are as a shadow**, and without hope.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said in the same prayer, by a man about to be described as satisfied of days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The succession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **Solomon his son reigned in his place**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chronicles does not record the contest with Adonijah, the summons to Bathsheba, or the executions that followed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verses of the book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now the acts of King David, first and last, indeed they are written in the book of Samuel the seer, in the book of Nathan the prophet, and in the book of Gad the seer.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three sources named, all lost."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word that outlives him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**And your house and your kingdom shall be established forever** before you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nathan's promise, in 2 Samuel 7. See the note on 2 Samuel 7:12–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lamp that keeps burning through Kings, named at every low point. See the note on 1 Kings 11:41–43."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Chronicles omits.** Bathsheba, Absalom, and Adonijah. It is not concealment — Samuel and Kings were in circulation — it is a book written for people rebuilding a temple."}]}, "1 Chronicles 4:9–10": {"title": "The prayer of Jabez", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Nine chapters of genealogy, and the record stops for two verses over one man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Tucked into the long roll of names.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "1 Chronicles opens with Adam and runs through nine chapters of lists. Names, fathers, sons, clans, with almost no story attached to any of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Jabez, and then straight back to the list. Nothing before or after connects to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is said about him first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jabez was **more honored than his brothers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nikbad** — from **kabed**, heavy, weighty. The same root as glory."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verdict is given before the prayer. The chronicler states it and then explains it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His mother had named him Jabez — **which sounds like \"pain\"** — because, she said, **\"I gave birth to him in pain.\"**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **Yabets**, and **otseb** is pain, sorrow, toil. The letters are rearranged, which Hebrew naming does often."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She named him after the worst day of her life, and he carried it every time anyone called him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Jabez **would not let his name have the last word**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever else was in his family, his name was a sentence pronounced over him at birth by somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He lifted his voice to the God of Israel and prayed, **bold and plain**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four requests, and he does not soften any of them:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*\"Oh, that You would **bless me — really bless me!**\"* — the Hebrew doubles the verb, *blessing You would bless*, the intensive form.", "*\"**Enlarge my borders**, widen the space of my life.\"* — territory. He asks for more ground.", "*\"Let **Your hand be on me**, near me, holding me.\"*", "*\"And **keep me from harm**, so that I will be **free from pain**.\"*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last word of his prayer is the word in his name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he asks the enlarging from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not go and take it. He asks the God of Israel, out loud, for more."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And God… **granted him exactly what he asked. Every word of it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *God brought about what he requested*. No negotiation, no partial answer, no rebuke for asking about land and safety."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is recorded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chronicler was writing for people who had come back from exile to a ruined city, rereading their family lists to work out who they were."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Into that he puts a man whose name meant pain, who asked for enlargement, and got it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for honoured"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same root turns up over Abram coming out of Egypt — *very heavy in livestock, silver and gold*. See the note on Genesis 13:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Weight, in Hebrew, is what honour is made of."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two verses do not contain.** Any condition. He is not told to qualify, and nothing is required of him first. He asked, and the next clause is that God gave it."}]}, "1 Corinthians 1:1–3": {"title": "What he calls them", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The address on the envelope, and it is remarkable given what is in the letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sender"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From Paul, **called by the will of God to be an apostle** of Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kletos apostolos** — a called apostle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He states his commission in the first line because the Corinthians were questioning it. He defends it at length in chapter 9 and again in the second letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The co-sender"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And from **Sosthenes our brother**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Almost certainly the synagogue ruler in Acts 18 who was beaten in front of Gallio's judgment seat when the case against Paul was thrown out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "If so, the man who led the prosecution is co-signing the letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To the **church of God that is in Corinth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Of God**, and **in Corinth**. Both, in the same phrase."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was in that church"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four factions each with a favourite teacher. A man living with his father's wife. Members suing each other in pagan courts. Prostitution being defended with a slogan. Drunkenness at the Lord's table so bad that some were dividing the meal by class. Chaos in the meetings. And people denying the resurrection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "All of it is in the letter. Paul knows every item on the list as he writes the greeting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he calls them anyway"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To those who have been **set apart in Christ Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hegiasmenois** — a perfect passive participle. Having been sanctified, and standing sanctified."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Done to them, already, and holding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Called to be **His holy people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kletois hagiois** — called saints."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word *saints* in the New Testament is never a category of exceptional Christians. It is the ordinary word for the members, and here it is used of the messiest congregation Paul wrote to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The widening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Together with **everyone everywhere who calls on the name of Jesus Christ our Lord**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He addresses the letter beyond the city before he starts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who is **their Lord and ours alike**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written to a church split four ways over which teacher they belonged to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The correction is in the greeting before the argument begins."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The greeting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Grace to you and peace from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Charis** and **eirene** — Paul's standard pair, in every letter he wrote."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grace first, then peace, in that order, without exception."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek greeting was **chairein**, rejoice. Paul bends it into **charis** and adds the Hebrew **shalom** behind it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I **thank my God always concerning you** for the grace of God which was given to you by Christ Jesus.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 1:4–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six verses of thanksgiving before a word of correction, and the thanksgiving ends with *God is faithful*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the first rebuke comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 10. *Now I plead with you, brethren… that there be no divisions among you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nine verses of who they are, and then the problems."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of the letter.** Sanctified, called saints, enriched, lacking no gift, confirmed to the end — and then six chapters of what was going wrong. He puts the description first and never revises it."}]}, "1 Corinthians 1:18–25": {"title": "The message of the cross", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes the word his critics were using and keeps it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two audiences"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The message of the cross is **foolishness to those who are perishing**, but to us who are being saved **it is the power of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *foolishness* is **moria** — the root of *moron*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both participles are present continuous: those who **are perishing**, those who **are being saved**. Paul describes two directions of travel rather than two finished states."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What crucifixion meant to them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cicero called it *the most cruel and disgusting penalty* and said the very word should be kept away from Roman citizens. It was reserved for slaves, rebels and the lowest criminals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Preaching a crucified Saviour in that world was not a difficult doctrine. It was an absurdity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will destroy the wisdom of the wise**, and the cleverness of the clever I will set aside.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 29, spoken about counsellors advising Judah to trust Egypt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where is **the wise one**? Where is **the scribe**? Where is **the debater of this present age**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Greek philosopher, a Jewish scholar, and a professional arguer. Between them they cover every kind of authority in Corinth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What did not work"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Since, **in the wisdom of God**, the world **through its own wisdom did not come to know God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *in the wisdom of God* — the arrangement was deliberate. God set it up so that this route would not arrive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He chose instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It **pleased God to save those who believe through the foolishness of the message we announce**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *the message* is **kerygma** — the thing proclaimed, the herald's announcement."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul calls his own preaching foolish, and says God preferred it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two demands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Jews **ask for a sign** and the Greeks **go searching for wisdom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are reasonable requests. Both had been offered honestly for centuries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is preached instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But we proclaim **Christ crucified** — **a stumbling block to the Jews and foolishness to the Greeks**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *stumbling block* is **skandalon** — the trigger of a trap, the thing that trips you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not adjust the message for either audience. He names the offence it gives to each and preaches it unchanged."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet **to those who are called**, both Jews and Greeks, **Christ the power of God and the wisdom of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two things they were asking for — power and wisdom — are named, and both are located in the person they found offensive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the foolishness of God is wiser than men**, and **the weakness of God is stronger than men**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul concedes the terms and then measures with them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not argue that the cross is really quite clever. He grants it looks foolish and weak, and says God at His most foolish outdoes men at their wisest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about the church itself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not many wise according to the flesh, not many mighty, not many noble, are called. **But God has chosen the foolish things of the world to put to shame the wise**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The congregation is the same argument in miniature."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The strategy in the passage.** Paul never softens the offence. He states exactly what each audience objects to, agrees that it looks that way, and says God chose it on purpose."}]}, "1 Corinthians 1:26–31": {"title": "\"Look at your own calling\" — the argument from the congregation", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul makes his case by telling the Corinthians to look around the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The evidence he points at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not many of you were wise by the reckoning of the flesh, not many were powerful, not many were nobly born.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not insulting them. He is presenting them as data. If the message spread by being intellectually fashionable or socially connected, this congregation would look different than it does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the precision: *not many* — not *none*. Corinth had some people of standing, and Paul does not overstate his case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four things God chose"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["what the world calls **foolish**", "what the world calls **weak**", "the **low and the despised**", "**the things that count for nothing at all**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last one escalates past the others — literally *the things that are not*, brought in to nullify *the things that are*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the stated purpose is entirely negative: *so that no human being might boast in His presence*. The selection criteria were designed to rule something out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God chooses to work with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The principle is not confined to people. It runs through the whole Old Testament as a method:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["a **tent peg and a hammer** against nine hundred iron chariots (Judges 4)", "a **nursing lamb** on an altar while a Philistine army closed in (1 Samuel 7)", "a **shepherd's sling** against a man in full armour", "**jars and torches**, three hundred men, no swords drawn (Judges 7)"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in that list looks like a weapon. Every one of them is what God chose, and the choosing is the point — *so that no human being might boast in His presence.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A piece of bread and a small cup belong on that list. It is not that they are adequate despite being small; small is the specification. See the notes on Judges 4:17–24 and 1 Samuel 7:7–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Christ is said to have become"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wisdom from God — and everything that puts us right with God, and everything that makes us holy, and everything that sets us free.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four things, and every one of them is the sort of thing a person would otherwise be trying to achieve. Paul does not say Christ helps with them. He says Christ **became** them, for us."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The quotation at the end.** *Let the one who boasts, boast in the Lord* — from Jeremiah, where the context is a warning against boasting in wisdom, strength or riches. The three things Jeremiah names line up almost exactly with the three Paul has just said the Corinthians did not have much of. He is not improvising the argument; he is landing on a text."}]}, "1 Corinthians 1:4–9": {"title": "Not behind in a single gift", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Six verses of thanksgiving, written to the church that gave him the most trouble."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The thanks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I **thank my God for you always**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul opens almost every letter this way. Galatians is the exception — there he goes straight from the greeting to *I marvel that you are turning away so soon*. See the note on Galatians 1:6–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Corinth had more disorder in it than Galatia. It still gets the thanksgiving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he thanks God for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Thinking of **the grace of God that was given you in Christ Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not their progress, and not their conduct. What was given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The enrichment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How **in Him you were made rich in every way**, in all your **speaking** and in all your **understanding**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eploutisthete** — you were made wealthy. Passive, aorist."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Logos** and **gnosis** — word and knowledge. The two things Corinth was proudest of, and the two things it was misusing. Paul names them as gifts before he corrects their use of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The confirming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as **the testimony about Christ was confirmed and made firm among you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ebebaiothe** — a legal word. Ratified, guaranteed, made binding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that you are **not behind in a single gift**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me hustereisthai** — not lacking, not falling short."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written to people who were fighting about which gifts mattered, in a letter that spends three chapters sorting it out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are doing meanwhile"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While you **wait for our Lord Jesus Christ to be unveiled**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apekdechomenous** — waiting eagerly, with the head up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **apokalupsin** — the unveiling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He will hold you steady to the very end**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **bebaiosei** — the same root as *confirmed* two verses earlier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The testimony was confirmed among them; He will confirm them. Paul uses the word twice on purpose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **no charge can be laid against you** on the day of our Lord Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anegkletous** — unaccusable. Not *forgiven of the charges*; no charge is brought."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He writes this to the church he is about to accuse of six or seven things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The standing on that day and the state at the moment of writing are different questions, and Paul is not confused about which he is addressing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ground"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God is **faithful**, and it was **He who called you** into the shared life of His Son.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **koinonian** — fellowship, partnership, having in common."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The guarantee is not their reliability, and Paul does not pretend otherwise. It is His, and the sentence puts the calling in God's hands too."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same construction elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who has begun a good work in you **will complete it** until the day of Jesus Christ.* See the note on Philippians 1:3–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The One who calls you is faithful. **He will do it**.* See the note on 1 Thessalonians 5:23–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three letters, three closings of the same shape, and every one puts the finishing on the caller."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now I plead with you, brethren, by the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, that you all speak the same thing, and that **there be no divisions among you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 10. The thanksgiving ends and the letter's first problem starts in the same breath."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who does the confirming.** The testimony was confirmed among them, and He will confirm them to the end — same verb both times, and neither instance has the Corinthians as its subject."}]}, "1 Corinthians 10:1–5": {"title": "\"That Rock was Christ\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul lists five things every one of them had, and then says most of them died in the desert."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word he repeats"}, {"k": "p", "t": "***All*** — five times in four verses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All our fathers were **under the cloud**, and **all** of them passed through the sea, and **all** of them were baptized into Moses, and **all** of them ate the same spiritual food, and **all** of them drank the same spiritual drink.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody was left out of anything on the list."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose fathers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our fathers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is writing to a mostly Gentile church in Corinth and calls Israel's wilderness generation **our** fathers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cloud and the sea"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Baptized into Moses **in the cloud and in the sea**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pillar over them and the water either side. Paul reads the crossing as a baptism — they went in one thing and came out another, following a leader."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The food"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The same **spiritual food**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The manna, six mornings a week for forty years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The drink"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The same **spiritual drink** — for they drank from **a spiritual Rock that went with them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rock at Rephidim and the rock at Kadesh, thirty-eight years apart. See the notes on Exodus 17:4–7 and Numbers 20:10–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the Rock **went with them** — the Greek is *the accompanying Rock*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The identification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **that Rock was Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul states it flatly, in the past tense, about an object in the Sinai desert."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the strongest identification of an Old Testament thing with Christ that Paul makes anywhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And yet **with most of them God was not well pleased**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *with the majority of them*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of the adults who came out of Egypt, two entered Canaan: Caleb and Joshua."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ending"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For they were **scattered and left across the wilderness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *they were strewn about* — **katestrothesan**, laid low, spread over the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Paul is saying it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *now these things became **our examples**, to the intent that we should not lust after evil things as they also lusted.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *examples* is **typoi** — types, patterns, the impression left by a stamp."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *let him who thinks he stands **take heed lest he fall**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Corinth was a confident church. Paul had spent the previous chapter on his own discipline — *lest, when I have preached to others, I myself should become disqualified.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being argued"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that the provision was inadequate. Paul says twice that it was **the same** for all of them, and calls it spiritual both times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No temptation has overtaken you except such as is common to man; but **God is faithful, who will not allow you to be tempted beyond what you are able**, but with the temptation will also make the way of escape.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted verse in the chapter comes directly after the wilderness graves."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one clause everything hangs on.** *That Rock was Christ.* The people who drank from it are the same people Paul says were scattered across the desert, and he puts both facts in one paragraph."}]}, "1 Corinthians 10:11–13": {"title": "\"The way of escape\" — and what it is escape from", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the most quoted promises in the New Testament, sitting immediately after a list of Israel's worst failures in the desert."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just run through the golden calf, the immorality at Peor, the grumbling and the snakes. Then: *all these things happened to them as examples, and they were written down as instruction for us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for examples is **typoi** — patterns, impressions, the mark a die leaves. Paul reads Israel's history as a shape that repeats."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning that comes first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let the one who thinks he is standing take care that he does not fall.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the sentence the famous promise is attached to, and it is a warning against confidence, not an encouragement to it. The order matters: watch yourself, **then** the assurance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is promised, precisely"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the testing is *common to everyone* — not unique, not a sign of being singled out", "God is faithful, and will not allow testing beyond what you are able to bear", "with the testing He *will also make the way of escape*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last phrase is worth reading closely: the escape is **so that you are able to bear it**. Not a door out of the situation — a way through it that does not break you. Paul's own thorn, which he asked three times to have removed and did not, is the same shape."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"more than you can bear.\"** The verse is often quoted about suffering in general — God will not give you more than you can handle. Paul is talking about *temptation and testing*, and elsewhere he says flatly that he was burdened beyond his strength, so that he would rely on God who raises the dead. The comfort here is real but specific: no one is ever cornered into sin with no way out."}]}, "1 Corinthians 10:31–33": {"title": "\"Whether you eat or drink\" — the end of a long argument about dinner", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The famous line is the conclusion of three chapters about meat, and knowing that changes how it reads."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the argument was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Corinth's markets sold meat that had been sacrificed in pagan temples. Some Christians ate it without a thought; others could not, and were distressed by those who did."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's answer runs long, and it never resolves into a rule about the meat. It resolves into a question about people: what does eating this do to the person next to me?"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three groups"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give no offence to Jews, or to Greeks, or to the church of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the whole social map — the synagogue, the pagan city, and his own congregation — and asks them to consider all three. Not a private conscience question; a public one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The standard he sets himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not seeking my own profit but the profit of the many, so that they may be saved.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for *profit* is a plain commercial one — advantage, benefit, what is in it for me. Paul says he runs the calculation for other people rather than for himself, and he says it as a description of how he already lives, not as advice."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"do everything for the glory of God.\"** Lifted out, it becomes a general piety about doing your best. In place it is a tiebreaker for a specific and genuinely difficult question — when two Christians disagree about something permitted, and there is no rule to settle it, decide by what serves the other person. The very next line of the letter is *follow my example, as I follow Christ's*."}]}, "1 Corinthians 10:6–10": {"title": "Examples for us", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Five wilderness incidents, listed as warnings, to a church that thought it was doing well."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just been said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All our fathers were **under the cloud**, all passed through the sea… all ate the same spiritual food, and all drank the same spiritual drink.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But with **most of them God was not well pleased**, for their bodies were scattered in the wilderness.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 10:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five *alls*, and then the reversal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The statement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now these things happened **as examples for us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tupoi hemon** — types of us, patterns for us."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tupos** — the mark left by a blow, a stamp, a die. The word English took as *type*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that we would **not crave what is evil the way they craved it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epithumetas kakon** — cravers of evil things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which points at Numbers 11: *the mixed multitude who were among them yielded to intense craving*, and the quail."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not become **idolaters**, as some of them were; as it is written, \"**The people sat down to eat and to drink, and got up to play**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus 32, quoted exactly. The golden calf. See the note on Exodus 32:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Nor let us commit **sexual immorality**… and **twenty-three thousand of them fell in a single day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers 25, at Baal of Peor, with the women of Moab."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers says twenty-four thousand. Paul says twenty-three. Either he is counting the day rather than the plague's total, or citing from memory; the New Testament does not smooth it over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Nor let us **put Christ to the test**, as some of them tested Him, and were **destroyed by the serpents**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers 21, on the long way round Edom. See the note on Numbers 21:5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul writes **ton Christon** — Christ. He has already said the rock that followed them was Christ."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fourth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Nor let us **grumble**, as some of them grumbled, and were **destroyed by the destroyer**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **gonguzete** — an onomatopoeic word. Muttering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ho olothreutes** — the destroyer. The definite article."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What grumbling is doing in this list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Idolatry, immorality, testing God — and complaining."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul lists it fourth, with the same construction and the same consequence. The letter does not grade it as a lesser item."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he is saying it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Corinth had spiritual gifts in abundance, and Paul has just spent a chapter on his own discipline: *lest, when I have preached to others, I myself should become disqualified*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wilderness generation had every advantage — cloud, sea, manna, water from the rock — and did not arrive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now all these things happened to them as examples, and **they were written for our admonition, upon whom the ends of the ages have come**.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 10:11–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore **let him who thinks he stands take heed lest he fall**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "And immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No temptation has overtaken you except such as is common to man; but **God is faithful, who will not allow you to be tempted beyond what you are able**, but with the temptation will also make the way of escape.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ekbasin** — a way out, an exit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five warnings and then a promise, in the same paragraph."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The fourth item.** Grumbling, listed with idolatry and immorality, in the same construction and with the same outcome. Paul does not grade it lower."}]}, "1 Corinthians 11:20–22": {"title": "Not the Lord's Supper", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul says something startling about a meeting that thought it was taking communion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So when you assemble together in one place, **it is not the Lord's Supper that you eat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *you are taking it badly*. It is not that meal at all. Whatever they were doing, the name did not apply to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was happening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Each one goes ahead with his own supper, and one is left hungry while another is drunk.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Corinthian gathering was a full meal. Wealthier members arrived earlier — they were not working — and ate what they had brought. Slaves and labourers came when they could get away, to nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *goes ahead* is about taking yours first, before the others."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do you not have houses to eat and drink in? Or do you **despise the church of God**, and **shame those who have nothing**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not accuse them of bad theology about the bread. He accuses them of humiliating poor people at the table."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *shame* is to put to shame, to disgrace — a public thing done to somebody's face."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In this I do not praise you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has praised them a few lines earlier for holding to what he passed on. Here he withdraws it, in the same formula, so the reversal is unmissable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The famous words come next — *the Lord Jesus, on the night He was betrayed, took bread.* Paul recites the institution **as the correction** to what he has just described."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a few verses on, *not discerning the body* — which in this context is a gathering that cannot see the people it is eating with. See the note on 1 Corinthians 11:27–30."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The point of the whole passage.** A meal about a body given for everyone was being eaten in a way that divided the room by income. Paul's remedy at the end is two words long: *wait for one another.*"}]}, "1 Corinthians 11:23–26": {"title": "Received from the Lord", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The oldest written account of the Lord's Supper anywhere — earlier than any of the four Gospels — and Paul says where he got it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I received from the Lord\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For I **received from the Lord** what I also delivered to you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul was not in the upper room. He was not a disciple then, and he does not say Peter told him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the Lord did. The same man tells the Corinthians in his next letter about being caught up to the third heaven and hearing words no one is permitted to repeat. Whatever else Paul received, he received this — and he passed on this one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Broken for you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take, eat: this is My body, **which is broken for you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither Matthew nor Mark has this phrase. Luke has *given for you*. Paul has **broken**, and he adds the two words that make it personal: *for you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only place in the New Testament where the breaking of the bread is stated as being for the eater's sake."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 53 says *by His stripes we are healed* in the same breath as *He was wounded for our transgressions*. Two provisions in one passage, and two elements on one table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In remembrance of Me\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice — once over the bread, once over the cup. Repetition in Paul is never accidental."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek **anamnēsis** is stronger than a fond recollection; the Septuagint uses it for the memorial portion of an offering, the part that brought a thing to mind before God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The new covenant in My blood\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a renewal of the old. A different covenant, and Jeremiah 31 had promised it: *I will forgive their iniquity and remember their sin no more.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old covenant's terms were *if you obey*. The new one's terms are the blood it is named after."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"You proclaim the Lord's death until He comes\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For as often as you eat this bread and drink this cup, you **proclaim** the Lord's death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katangellō** — to announce publicly, the word used for preaching. Eating is a form of announcement."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the announcement is aimed at more than the people in the room. Something is declared every time the bread is taken."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until He comes.* The instruction has an end date, which is also the only date in it that has not yet arrived."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the passage is answering.** Paul does not recite the institution as a liturgy lesson. He recites it because the Corinthians were shaming poor people at that table. The most quoted communion passage in Scripture was written as a correction. See the notes on 1 Corinthians 11:20–22 and 11:27–30."}]}, "1 Corinthians 11:27–30": {"title": "Discerning the body", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul gives a reason for weakness and sickness in the Corinthian church, and the reason he gives is not sin in general. It is one specific failure: *not discerning the Lord's body.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the word means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Diakrino** — to distinguish, to tell one thing apart from another. Not *deserve*. Not *feel worthy of*. To recognise what is in front of you and see what it is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the failure is a failure of recognition. Something was being handled without being seen for what it is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What there was to recognise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just quoted the words of institution, four verses earlier: *this is My body, which is for you.* The bread is named as the body given on their behalf."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what was going unrecognised at Corinth — people eating in a hurry, some drunk, the wealthy shaming the poor, and the bread on the table treated as nothing in particular. Paul's whole complaint is that they were not seeing what they were holding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two things to tell apart"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Diakrino** does not only mean to notice. It means to **separate** one thing from another — to draw a line between two items and treat them differently."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There are two things on that table, and Paul has just named what each one is:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the cup** — *the new covenant in My blood*, and Matthew adds *for the forgiveness of sins*", "**the bread** — *My body, which is broken for you*, and Matthew's own Gospel says of that body, *He took our infirmities and bore our diseases*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "To take the cup for forgiveness and swallow the bread as though it were nothing in particular is to separate what He handed over together. The blood is not doubted; the body is passed over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the failure named in the verse, and Paul attaches a consequence to it in the next line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same word, on the road to Jerusalem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus uses **diakrino** in Mark 11, the morning after the fig tree: *whoever says to this mountain, be removed, and **does not doubt** in his heart…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word rendered *doubt* is this word. Doubting, in that sentence, is being divided in yourself — saying one thing and holding another, split down the middle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mountain and the fig tree are not two different kinds of work; a man who divides them will not move either. Neither are the body and the blood. See the note on Mark 11:20–26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In an unworthy way\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is an adverb. It describes **how** they ate, not who they were."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That distinction is the difference between a table and a tribunal. Paul is not saying only worthy people may come — that would empty the room, and it would contradict what the bread means. He is saying there is a way of coming to it that misses the point of it entirely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The remedy he gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not staying away. Two verses later: *wait for one another.* And in the same paragraph, *let a man examine himself, and so let him eat* — the examining leads to eating, not away from it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer to eating badly is eating properly, with the body recognised."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line, and what it implies.** *For this reason many among you are weak and sick.* Paul states a link between failing to discern the body and physical weakness — which means the reverse is worth taking seriously too. This is a table where the body broken *for you* is set in front of you, and Paul plainly thought that recognising it mattered for more than the soul."}]}, "1 Corinthians 11:31–34": {"title": "Three judgments in two verses", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*If we judged ourselves, we would not be judged. But when we are judged, we are chastened by the Lord.* Three judgment words in two lines — and in the Greek they are not all the same word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first one is a different verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If we **judged** ourselves…* — **diakrino**. To distinguish, to separate, to tell one thing apart from another."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same verb Paul used two verses earlier in *not **discerning** the Lord's body*, and the same one Jesus used in Mark 11 for the man who *does not doubt* — is not divided — in his heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the first clause is not about interrogating yourself for sin. It is the verb of telling things apart, applied to yourself at that table: judging yourself healed because of a body broken for you, and forgiven because of blood poured out for you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second and third are another verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*…we would not be **judged**. But when we are **judged**, we are chastened by the Lord.* — **krino**, both times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two different Greek verbs, rendered by one English word, in a single sentence. The distinction is invisible in translation and it is the whole hinge of the passage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And Paul says what the second word means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not leave it to be guessed. He defines it in the same breath:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are **chastened** by the Lord, **so that we would not be condemned along with the world**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chastening and the condemning are set against each other, not stacked together. Whatever *krino* is doing here, Paul's own clause rules out its being sentence and condemnation — that is what the world is under, and he says so explicitly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Chastened\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paideuo**, from **pais**, a child. It is the vocabulary of raising a son — training, instruction, correction inside a family."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews 12 uses the same word at length: *the Lord disciplines the one He loves… no discipline seems pleasant at the time, but afterward it yields the peaceable fruit of righteousness.* Correction, and a stated harvest. See the note on Hebrews 12:5–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And condemnation is off the table already"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*…so that we would not be condemned along with the world.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John 3 settles who is where: *whoever believes in Him is not condemned… he who does not believe is condemned already.* The condemnation is the world's condition, and it is described as a thing already in place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul writes to keep the Corinthians clear of that, not to put them under it. See the note on John 3:14–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The practical instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When you come together to eat, **wait for one another**. If anyone is hungry, let him eat at home.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "After all of it — the warnings, the weakness, the sleeping — the remedy is five words long, and it is about manners at a shared table."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is told to stay away."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *And the rest I will set in order when I come.* Paul closes the subject the way a man closes a letter, not the way a judge closes a case."}]}, "1 Corinthians 12:12–14": {"title": "So also is Christ", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He sets up a comparison and then does not finish it the way anyone expects."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For just as **the body is one and has many parts**, and all the parts of that one body, many as they are, **are one body**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A commonplace. Roman writers used the body-and-members image for the state, and everyone in Corinth would have heard it before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So also **is Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence should end *so also is the church*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It says Christ."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not compare the church to a body and then say Christ is the head. He says the many-parted body **is** Christ."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he learned it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Saul, Saul, **why are you persecuting Me?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been arresting Christians, and the voice on the Damascus road named the injury as its own. See the note on Acts 9:3–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The making of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **by one Spirit we were all baptized into one body**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **en heni Pneumati** — in, or by, one Spirit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ebaptisthemen** — aorist passive. It happened to them; they did not do it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two pairs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whether **Jews or Greeks**, whether **slaves or free**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two deepest divisions in that city — the racial and the legal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Corinth was a Roman colony with a large slave population. The two categories named are the ones that governed where a person could sit, eat and marry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are stated as crossed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The drink"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **we were all given the one Spirit to drink**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epotisthemen** — we were made to drink, given to drink. Passive again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two images in one verse: immersed in, and taken in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The obvious sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the body is not one part, but many**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which sounds like a truism, and is aimed at a specific problem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the problem was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Corinth had gifts in abundance — *you come short in no gift* — and was ranking them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Tongues, in particular, had become a marker of status."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If the foot should say, \"Because I am not a hand, I am not of the body,\" **is it therefore not of the body?***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And if the ear should say, \"Because I am not an eye, I am not of the body\"…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two members talking themselves out, and then two talking others out: *the eye cannot say to the hand, \"I have no need of you\"*. See the note on 1 Corinthians 12:15–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul addresses the inferiority first and the superiority second."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The unpresentable parts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Those members of the body which we think to be less honourable, **on these we bestow greater honour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But God composed the body, having given greater honour to that part which lacks it, that there should be **no schism in the body**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And if one member suffers, **all the members suffer with it**; or if one member is honoured, **all the members rejoice with it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated as physiology rather than as an instruction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes after the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And yet I show you a more excellent way.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though I speak with the tongues of men and of angels, but have not love, I have become sounding brass.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 13:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 13 is not an interruption. It is the answer to the ranking."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The sentence that does not end where it should.** *So also is Christ* — not *so also is the church*. Paul learned the identification from a voice on a road."}]}, "1 Corinthians 12:27–28": {"title": "Set in the church", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The conclusion of the body chapter, and the list at the end is not what a modern reader expects."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter has argued"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The body. One body, many members, and none of them able to resign."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If the foot should say, \"Because I am not a hand, I am not of the body,\" is it therefore not of the body?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And if the ear should say, \"Because I am not an eye, I am not of the body,\" is it therefore not of the body?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two complaints, both from parts that feel second-rate, and both answered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other direction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the eye cannot say to the hand, \"I have no need of you\"; nor again the head to the feet, \"I have no need of you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first pair is inferiority. The second is contempt. The chapter addresses both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The unpresentable parts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No, much rather, those members of the body which seem to be weaker are necessary. And those parts of the body which we think to be less honourable, **on these we bestow greater honour**; and our unpresentable parts have greater modesty.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But God composed the body, **giving greater honour to that part which lacks it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument runs the opposite way to instinct. The parts nobody sees get more covering, not less care."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That there should be **no schism in the body**, but that the members should **have the same care for one another**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **schisma** — a tear, a split."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And if one member suffers, **all the members suffer with it**; or if one member is honoured, **all the members rejoice with it**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The identification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **you are the body of Christ**, and each of you is a part of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to Corinth. The congregation with four factions, a lawsuit problem, drunkenness at the table and chaos in the meetings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say *you should be*. See the note on 1 Corinthians 1:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And God has appointed these in the church.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **etheto** — set, placed, appointed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*First **apostles**, second **prophets**, third **teachers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbered, and only these three."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After that **miracles**, then **gifts of healings**, those who **lend a hand**, those who **steer things**, different kinds of **tongues**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Unnumbered from here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Antilempseis** — helps, and it occurs once in the New Testament. The word for taking hold of something to support it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kuberneseis** — administrations, and also once. From the word for a ship's helmsman, and the root of *governor* and *cybernetics*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Helping and steering, listed among the gifts, between miracles and tongues."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where tongues comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Last."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Corinth had made it first. The whole of chapter 14 is Paul reordering the meeting, and the placement here is part of the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Are all apostles? Are all prophets? Are all teachers? Are all workers of miracles?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven rhetorical questions, and the expected answer to every one is no."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **earnestly desire the best gifts**. And yet **I show you a more excellent way**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is chapter 13, and the first thing it weighs is tongues. See the note on 1 Corinthians 13:1–3."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is in the middle of the list.** Helping and steering. Two words that occur once each in the New Testament, set between miracles and tongues, and neither of them looks like a gift."}]}, "1 Corinthians 12:29–31": {"title": "Seven questions expecting no", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks seven questions in a row, and in Greek each is framed to expect the answer *no*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Are all apostles? Are all prophets? Are all teachers? Are all workers of miracles? Do all have gifts of healings? Do all speak with tongues? Do all interpret?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The construction is unambiguous — he is not wondering. He is closing off the idea that every believer should exhibit every gift."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole argument of the chapter: a body is made of parts that are not the same, and an eye that resented not being a hand would be misunderstanding what a body is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Earnestly desire the best gifts\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately after saying not everyone has everything, he tells them to want the better ones — a plural imperative, addressed to the church as a body rather than to individuals shopping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *best* is defined two chapters later, in his own terms: what builds the church up. In chapter 14 he ranks prophecy above tongues in a gathering for exactly that reason."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"A still more excellent way\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence that ends the chapter, and it is the doorway into chapter 13 — the passage about love."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the structure people miss when 1 Corinthians 13 gets read at weddings. It is not a standalone poem; it is Paul's answer to a church quarrelling about which gifts made a person important."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How chapter 13 opens.** *If I speak in the tongues of men and of angels but have not love, I am a noisy gong.* He lists the very gifts they were competing over and prices each one at nothing without the thing he is about to describe. See the note on 1 Corinthians 14:1–5, where he picks the argument back up: *pursue love*, and then desire the gifts."}]}, "1 Corinthians 12:4–7": {"title": "\"The same Spirit\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three sentences built the same way, and the repetition is the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Different kinds of **gifts**, but the **same Spirit**.* *Different ways of **serving**, but the **same Lord**.* *Different things being **done**, but it is the **same God** who works all of them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times: variety on one side, sameness on the other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the sameness is named as Spirit, Lord, and God — Paul writes a Trinitarian sentence without pausing to explain it, in the middle of a practical passage about church meetings."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he is saying it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Corinth was ranking each other by which gift they had. The whole chapter is written against that, and the answer he gives is not to level the gifts but to point at their source."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Different — and Paul never suggests they should be the same. He simply removes the grounds for comparison."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"To each one\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **each one** the manifestation of the Spirit is given.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to the impressive ones, or the mature ones. Each one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *manifestation* is a showing, a making visible. Something invisible becoming apparent through a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the good of everyone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *toward what is profitable* — for the common advantage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the criterion Paul applies all through chapters 12 to 14. Not what a gift does for the person who has it. What it is worth to the room."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it again in chapter 14: *let all things be done for edification*, and *since you are zealous for spiritual gifts, seek to excel to the edifying of the church.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The nine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses following list them: a word of wisdom, a word of knowledge, faith, healings, the working of miracles, prophecy, discerning of spirits, tongues, and the interpretation of tongues."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And after the list: *but one and the same Spirit works all these things, **distributing to each one individually as He wills**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The distribution is His decision, not an achievement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The body"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest of the chapter is the body — the foot saying it is not a hand, the ear saying it is not an eye, and the eye unable to say to the hand *I have no need of you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Those members of the body which seem to be weaker are necessary.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes straight after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 13. *Though I speak with the tongues of men and of angels, but have not love, I have become sounding brass.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul lists the gifts, describes the body, and then interrupts himself for a chapter on love before returning to the subject."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse that opens the chapter.** *Now concerning spiritual gifts, brethren, **I do not want you to be ignorant**.* Paul's reason for the whole discussion is that they did not understand what they had."}]}, "1 Corinthians 12:8–11": {"title": "\"Just as He wills\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Nine items in a list, and the sentence that closes it is about who decides."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The nine"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**A word of wisdom**", "**A word of knowledge**", "**Faith**", "**Gifts of healings**", "**The working of miracles**", "**Prophecy**", "**The discerning of spirits**", "**Different kinds of tongues**", "**The interpretation of tongues**"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two that are words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first two are **a word** of wisdom and **a word** of knowledge — the Greek is **logos**, a saying, an utterance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not wisdom as a quality, and not knowledge as a store of learning. A word of it, for an occasion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Faith as a gift"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Faith is in the list, which is worth noticing, because every believer is said to have faith elsewhere."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This one is listed alongside healings and miracles rather than alongside salvation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Both plurals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Gifts of **healings**.* Both nouns are plural in the Greek — **charismata iamaton**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a healing gift held by a person, in Paul's phrasing. Gifts, plural, of healings, plural."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul repeats the source clause four times in three verses:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*through **the Spirit**… according to **the same Spirit**… by **the same Spirit**… by **the one Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is writing to a church that was ranking itself by which gift a person had. The repetition is the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for gifts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Charisma** — from **charis**, grace. A gift of grace, something given without being earned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same root for salvation, for celibacy, and for these."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The summary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **one and the same Spirit works all of these**, distributing to each one **individually** just as **He wills**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three claims in one sentence:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**One and the same Spirit** — the ninth repetition of the point.", "**To each one individually** — the Greek is *to each his own*. Nobody has the set.", "**Just as He wills** — the Greek is **kathos bouletai**, according to His own deliberate choice."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The distribution is not by request, merit, seniority or hunger. Paul closes the list by locating the decision entirely with the Spirit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter says three verses earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There are **diversities of gifts, but the same Spirit**. There are **differences of ministries, but the same Lord**. And there are **diversities of activities, but it is the same God** who works all in all.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three sets, and each is attributed to a different person of the Trinity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the manifestation of the Spirit is given to each one for the profit of all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *for what is advantageous* — **sumphero**, to bring together, to benefit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated before the list, and it governs it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Into the body — one body, many members, the eye that cannot say to the hand *I have no need of you*, and the parts that seem weaker being necessary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, after *earnestly desire the best gifts*: *and yet I show you a more excellent way* — and the whole of chapter 13."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the nine have in common.** Not one of them is described as produced, cultivated, or achieved. Paul lists them, names one Spirit four times, and ends on the Spirit's own will."}]}, "1 Corinthians 13:1–3": {"title": "I am nothing at all", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three sentences with the same shape, and each one puts something bigger on the scale."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the middle of three chapters about spiritual gifts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 12 lists them. Chapter 14 sorts out the disorder in the meetings. And between them, without warning, this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last line of chapter 12 is the hinge: *and yet I show you a more excellent way*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a digression. It is the middle term of the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If I speak in **the languages of men, or even of angels**, and do not have love, I am **banging metal, a cymbal clashing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **chalkos echon** — sounding brass. And **kumbalon alalazon** — a clanging cymbal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are noise-makers used in pagan temple worship in that region. Loud, hollow, and requiring no skill."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If I can **prophesy, and understand every mystery and everything there is to know**, and if I have **faith enough to move mountains**, and do not have love, **I am nothing at all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four things: prophecy, mysteries, knowledge, and mountain-moving faith."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes the highest gift on his own list and the largest promise Jesus made about faith, and weighs them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **outhen eimi** — I am nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first sentence said what he *sounds* like. This one says what he *is*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If I **give away everything I own** to feed the poor, and **hand over my body to be burned**, and do not have love, **it does me no good whatever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Total divestment and martyrdom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ouden ophelymai** — I am profited nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three verdicts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Noise. Nothing. No profit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scale goes: what I sound like, what I am, what I gain. Each sentence takes something away at a deeper level."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not being said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that the gifts are worthless. Paul spends two chapters regulating them and says *I wish you all spoke with tongues*, and *desire earnestly the best gifts*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not against any item on the list. He is saying that each of them, without one thing, is a zero."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Agape** — the word the New Testament writers took over and filled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was a rare and colourless word in classical Greek. **Eros** and **philia** were the ones with the weight, and both were about response to something attractive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next paragraph, and every clause is a verb rather than a feeling:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Love **suffers long** and **is kind**; love **does not envy**; love **does not parade itself**, is not puffed up; does not behave rudely, **does not seek its own**, is not provoked, **thinks no evil**; does not rejoice in iniquity, but rejoices in the truth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bears all things, believes all things, hopes all things, endures all things.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The permanence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Love never fails. But whether there are prophecies, they will fail; whether tongues, they will cease; whether knowledge, it will vanish away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three things he weighed in verses 1 and 2, listed as the things with an expiry date."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And now abide faith, hope, love, these three; but **the greatest of these is love**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it was written to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most gifted congregation Paul dealt with — *you come short in no gift* — and the most disorderly. See the note on 1 Corinthians 1:4–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Factions, lawsuits, drunkenness at the table, chaos in the meetings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not tell them to want fewer gifts."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the three verdicts measure.** Sound, being, and profit. Tongues without it are noise, prophecy and mountain-moving faith without it make the man nothing, and giving everything away without it gains him nothing."}]}, "1 Corinthians 13:4–8": {"title": "The way of love", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Fifteen short statements, and every one of them is a verb in the Greek."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a poem about romance. Paul is in the middle of an argument with a church that was ranking itself by which gift a person had, and this is the answer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 12 ends *and yet I show you a more excellent way*. Chapter 14 begins *pursue love*. This sits between them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grammar"}, {"k": "p", "t": "English turns them into adjectives — love is patient, love is kind. The Greek has verbs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Love **waits**, love **acts kindly**, love **does not envy**. It is a description of conduct, not of temperament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two positives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Love **waits without complaining**. Love **is kind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for the first is **makrothumei** — long-tempered, slow to burn. It is used of God's patience with people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only two of the fifteen are positive at the start. The next eight are all negatives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The eight things it does not do"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Is not jealous** — **zeloi**, the same root as *zeal*", "**Does not show itself off** — **perpereuetai**, a rare word for boasting; it occurs once in the Bible", "**Is not full of itself** — **phusioutai**, puffed up, inflated. Paul uses it six times in this letter and almost nowhere else; it was Corinth's particular problem", "**Is never rude** — **aschemonei**, behaving indecently", "**Is not out for what it can get** — *does not seek its own*", "**Is not quick to take offense** — **paroxunetai**, provoked, stirred", "**Keeps no ledger of wrongs** — **logizetai**, the accounting word. Love does not enter it in the book", "**Takes no pleasure in anything wrong**"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ledger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Love **keeps no ledger of wrongs**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is the one Paul uses eleven times in Romans 4 for God crediting righteousness and not imputing sin. See the note on Romans 4:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same bookkeeping word, applied to what love does with an offence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four \"keeps on\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Love **bears up under anything**. Love **keeps on trusting**. Love **keeps on hoping**. Love **outlasts everything**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first and last are the same Greek root twice — **stegei** (covers, roofs over) and **hupomenei** (remains under)."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Love **never fails**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **oudepote piptei** — never falls."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What does fail"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **prophecies — they will come to an end; tongues — they will fall silent; knowledge — it will be finished with**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three gifts Corinth was competing over, and Paul lists all three as temporary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the argument. Not that the gifts are worthless — he has just spent a chapter saying they come from one Spirit — but that they have an end date and this does not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For we know in part and we prophesy in part. But when that which is perfect has come, then that which is in part will be done away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For now we see in a mirror, dimly, but then face to face.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three that remain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And now abide **faith, hope, love**, these three; but **the greatest of these is love**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Faith and hope also end — you do not need either when the thing is in front of you. Paul calls love greatest and then, in the next sentence, tells them to chase it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter sits.** Between a list of nine spiritual gifts and the instructions for using them in a meeting. It is not an interlude. It is the standard the other two chapters are measured against."}]}, "1 Corinthians 14:1–5": {"title": "\"Pursue love\" — the verb, and what it is set against", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 13 has just finished — faith, hope and love, and the greatest of these is love. Then, without a break, this: *pursue love.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two different verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses one word for love and a different one for the gifts, and the pair is deliberate:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**pursue** love — *dioko*, to chase, to hunt down, to run after. The word used of persecutors pursuing their target.", "**desire earnestly** the spiritual gifts — a strong word for wanting, but not the same as running someone down."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Love is the thing you chase. The gifts are things you want. Nobody reading this in Corinth could have missed which one Paul put in the hunting verb."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure he applies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything after this is judged by one question: does it build up the church?"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the one who speaks in a tongue *builds himself up*", "the one who prophesies *builds up the church*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not dismissive — he says he wishes they all spoke in tongues, and elsewhere that he does so more than any of them. His argument is about the gathering: in a room full of people, the thing that everyone can understand does more good than the thing only one person understands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for \"builds up\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Oikodomeo** — from **oikos**, house, and **demo**, to build. To construct a house, or to repair one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses it in this chapter more than anywhere else in his letters. It governs everything he says about a meeting: *let all things be done for edification.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the speaking is to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who speaks in a tongue **does not speak to men but to God**, for no one understands him; however, in the spirit he speaks mysteries.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul states the direction before he states the ranking. The addressee is God."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What \"prophesy\" means here.** Paul defines it in the passage rather than leaving it to be assumed: speaking *to build them up, to urge them on, and to comfort them*. That is a wider and more ordinary thing than predicting the future, and all three of its stated purposes are aimed at other people."}]}, "1 Corinthians 14:13–19": {"title": "\"My spirit prays\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul describes two things that can be going on at once, and says he does both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If I pray in a tongue, **my spirit prays, but my understanding is unfruitful**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *understanding* is **nous** — the mind, the reasoning faculty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Unfruitful** is **akarpos** — bearing no fruit. Paul is not saying the praying is unfruitful. He says the mind is not the part producing anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does not conclude"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not conclude that it should stop. The next line is the resolution:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What is it then?** I will pray with the spirit, **and I will pray with the understanding also**; I will sing with the spirit, **and I will sing with the understanding also**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verbs, two pairs, and the connector is *also* rather than *instead*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer he asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore let the one who speaks in a tongue **pray that he may interpret**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Interpretation is itself something to be prayed for. Paul treats it as available on request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it matters in a room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Otherwise, if you bless with the spirit, **how will the person sitting there** who does not follow any of it **say the 'Amen'** to your thanksgiving?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the one who fills the place of the uninformed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Amen is the congregation's part. It was the response in the synagogue and it is what Paul is protecting — a person cannot agree with what they cannot follow."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The concession he makes in the same breath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **you indeed give thanks well enough**, but the other person is not built up.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not withdraw the first half. The thanksgiving is fine. The problem named is one of usefulness to somebody else in the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I thank my God that I speak with tongues more than you all**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to the church that was doing it most, and he outdoes them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody would guess it from Acts or from any of his letters, which is the point of him having to say it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Yet **in the church** I would rather speak **five words with my understanding**, so that I might **teach others also**, than **ten thousand words in a tongue**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five against ten thousand — a ratio of one to two thousand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the whole comparison is qualified at the front: **in the church**. Paul is ranking the two for a gathering, not in general."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The governing rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter states it repeatedly: *let all things be done for **edification**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Oikodome** — house-building. Paul's whole test in this chapter is whether something builds anybody up, and he applies it to a meeting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says four verses earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who speaks in a tongue **edifies himself**, but he who prophesies edifies the church.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 14:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul states both as facts and then spends the chapter on which one belongs in a public gathering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, brethren, desire earnestly to prophesy, **and do not forbid to speak with tongues**. Let all things be done decently and in order.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both clauses, in the same sentence, as his closing instruction."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul never does in the chapter.** Argue that the thing is not real, not from God, or not for them. His entire concern is the person sitting in the seat who cannot say Amen to what they did not follow."}]}, "1 Corinthians 14:20–25": {"title": "\"A sign, not to those who believe\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes Isaiah at them and then walks two visitors into an imaginary meeting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not be children in understanding**; in malice be babes, **but in understanding be mature**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things to be childlike about and one not to be. The Greek for *malice* is **kakia** — badness, ill-will."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Be an infant about that. Be grown up about thinking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**With men of other tongues and other lips I will speak to this people, and even so they will not hear Me**,\" says the Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 28. In its own setting it was a threat: since Judah would not listen to plain Hebrew from the prophets, they would hear Assyrian — the language of the invading army. See the note on Isaiah 28:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Foreign speech in the ears of the people of God, in that passage, meant judgment had arrived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion drawn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So then, **tongues are for a sign, not to those who believe but to unbelievers**; while **prophesying is not for unbelievers but for those who believe**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves are counter-intuitive and Paul states them flatly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first scenario"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If the whole church comes together in one place and **everyone speaks with tongues**, and there come in those who are **uninformed or unbelievers**, will they not say **that you are out of your minds?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **mainesthe** — you are raving, mad."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a sign, in Isaiah's sense. The visitor concludes something, and what they conclude is that these people are unreachable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second scenario"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But if all prophesy**, and an unbeliever or an uninformed person comes in.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four things happen to him, in order:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*He is **convinced by all***", "*he is **convicted by all***", "*and so **the secrets of his heart are made plain***", "*and **falling down on his face he will worship God***"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *and report that **God is truly among you**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Elencho** — to expose, to convict, to bring to light. It is the word John uses of the Spirit: *He will convict the world of sin.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What prophecy is in this chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul defined it four verses in: *he who prophesies speaks **edification and exhortation and comfort** to men.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 14:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not primarily prediction. Building, urging, comforting — in a language the room understands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the visitor does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the speaker. He falls down and reports that God is there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does with all of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses after this set out the order for a meeting: two or three at most, one at a time, and let one interpret. And if no interpreter, *let him keep silent in church, and let him speak to himself and to God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *let all things be done decently and in order.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The rule underneath.** Paul is asking one question the whole way through — what does the person who walked in off the street take away from it. Everything in the chapter is arranged around that visitor."}]}, "1 Corinthians 15:12–19": {"title": "If Christ is not risen", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes the position he is arguing against and follows it all the way down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem in Corinth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How is it that **some among you are saying there is no resurrection of the dead?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that Christ did not rise. That the dead in general are not raised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Greek thought had no difficulty with the soul surviving; a body coming back was the offensive part. Paul had been laughed off the Areopagus in Athens over exactly this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first move"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If there is **no resurrection of the dead**, then **Christ Himself has not been raised**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He runs their principle backwards. A general rule with no exceptions cannot have one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They wanted to keep the resurrection of Christ and drop the rest. Paul says the two stand or fall together, and he says it twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each consequence follows from the last:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Our **preaching is empty*** — the Greek is **kenon**, hollow, containing nothing.", "*And **your faith is empty** as well.*", "*We are found to be **false witnesses about God***, because we testified that He raised Him.", "*Your faith is **futile***, and *you are **still in your sins***.", "*Those who have **fallen asleep in Christ have perished***."]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for futile"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Mataia** — useless, pointless, without effect. It is a different word from the *kenon* of verse 14: that one means empty of content, this one means empty of result."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Still in your sins\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The clause carries the theology of the chapter. If the resurrection did not happen, the death did not settle anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says it in Romans the other way round: *delivered up because of our offences, and **raised because of our justification**.* See the note on Romans 4:23–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And those who have **fallen asleep in Christ have perished**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He includes the people they had buried. If he is wrong, the ones who died believing are simply gone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If our hope in Christ belongs to this life only, then **there is no one on earth more to be pitied than we are**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *most pitiable of all men*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say Christians would still be better off, or that the ethics would remain worth having. He says they would be the most wretched people alive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had been beaten five times, stoned once, shipwrecked three times, and was writing from Ephesus where he says he *fought with beasts*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The life only made sense on one condition, and he says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But now **Christ is risen from the dead, and has become the firstfruits** of those who have fallen asleep.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **aparche** — the first sheaf brought in, which is not a sample but a guarantee. See the note on 1 Corinthians 15:23–28."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The witnesses he had already listed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cephas, the twelve, over five hundred at once — *of whom the greater part remain to the present, but some have fallen asleep* — then James, then all the apostles, and last of all himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names people who could be asked."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul refuses to do.** Salvage anything. He takes the denial, applies it consistently, and lists everything that goes with it — including the people already buried."}]}, "1 Corinthians 15:23–28": {"title": "The last enemy", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is arguing for the resurrection against people who had decided it was nonsense, and here he sets out the order of events."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The last enemy that will be destroyed is death\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things in that sentence are easy to miss."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Enemy.** Death is not described as natural, or as a doorway, or as part of the design. It is on a list of hostile powers being put down. The Bible's usual tenderness about death — falling asleep, departing — never softens into calling it a friend."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Last.** It goes, but it goes at the end. Everything else is subdued first. That is a straight answer to the question of why death is still here if it has been defeated: Paul says the defeat is real and the removal is scheduled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ the firstfruits, and afterward those who are Christ's at His coming.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Firstfruits is a harvest word — the first sheaf brought in, which is not a sample but a guarantee. The rest of the field is coming, and it will be the same crop."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the argument of the whole chapter compressed into a phrase: what happened to Him is what happens to His."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He must reign until\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **He must reign until** He has put all enemies under His feet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reign is present tense and the subduing is in progress. Paul is not describing a future coronation — he is describing what is happening now, between the resurrection and the last enemy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is Psalm 110 again: *sit at My right hand **until** I make Your enemies Your footstool.* Both verses put the reigning and the subduing in the same window, and in both the one reigning is seated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does the putting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **He has put** all things under His feet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes Psalm 8 and then stops to clarify the grammar: *when He says all things are put under Him, it is evident that He who put all things under Him is excepted.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He interrupts his own argument to make sure nobody misreads a pronoun."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The strange ending.** Paul says that when everything has been put under Christ, the Son Himself will be subject to the Father, *so that God may be all in all.* It reads oddly against everything else the New Testament says about Christ's glory — and it is Paul's way of saying that the goal of the whole operation is not a rearranged universe with a new authority in charge, but God filling everything, with nothing left out and nothing left over."}]}, "1 Corinthians 15:3–8": {"title": "What he handed on", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The oldest summary of the Christian message in existence, and Paul says he did not write it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The transmission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For I **handed on** to you, first of all, **the very thing I myself received**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paredoka** and **parelabon** — the technical pair for passing on a fixed tradition. Rabbinic teaching used the same vocabulary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is quoting. He says so."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was converted within a few years of the crucifixion, so what he is repeating predates his own conversion — which puts this formula within a handful of years of the event."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"First of all\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *among the first things*, in order of importance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each begins with *that*, which is how a creed is set out:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*That **Christ died for our sins** according to the Scriptures*", "*That **He was buried***", "*That **He rose again the third day** according to the Scriptures*", "*That **He was seen***"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two of the four carry *according to the Scriptures*. The death and the resurrection are both attached to the Old Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The burial"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That He was buried.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A short clause doing a specific job. A buried man is a dead man, and the tomb is a location somebody could have gone to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list of witnesses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Cephas, and then **the twelve**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter first. The Gospels give the women first, which is why the omission is noticeable — Paul is listing official witnesses in a legal sense, and a woman's testimony carried no weight in that world."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After that He was seen by **more than five hundred brothers all at one time**, and **most of them are still here with us to this present day**, though some have fallen asleep.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That clause is an invitation. Most of them can be asked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After that He was seen by **James**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Lord's brother, who did not believe during His life. See the note on Acts 1:12–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And then by **all the apostles**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **last of all He was seen by me too**, as by **a child born at the wrong time**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ektroma** — a miscarriage, an untimely birth. It is a harsh word, and Paul chose it about himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse explains: *for I am the least of the apostles, who am not worthy to be called an apostle, **because I persecuted the church of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is on the list, and he says how he got there. See the note on Acts 9:3–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse after that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **by the grace of God I am what I am**, and His grace toward me was not in vain; but I laboured more abundantly than they all, **yet not I, but the grace of God which was with me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice in one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole chapter is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some in Corinth were saying there is no resurrection of the dead. Paul sets out the tradition first, and then follows their premise all the way down. See the note on 1 Corinthians 15:12–19."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul does not claim.** Authorship. He opens the most important paragraph he ever wrote by saying he is passing on what he was handed — and then puts himself at the end of the witness list with the ugliest word available."}]}, "1 Corinthians 15:42–44": {"title": "Sown and raised", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four contrasts, in identical grammar, about the same body."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody in Corinth had asked *how are the dead raised up? And with what body do they come?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's first answer is blunt: *foolish one, what you sow is not made alive unless it dies.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the wheat: *you do not sow that body that shall be, but mere grain.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What is **sown**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four times, and the word is **speiretai** — sown, as seed is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Burial is called sowing. Nobody plants a seed intending to leave it there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What is sown **decays**; what is raised is **beyond the reach of decay**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phthora** and **aphtharsia** — corruption and incorruption."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same pair Peter uses of the seed the believer was born from. See the note on 1 Peter 1:23."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What is sown goes into the ground **in dishonor**; what is raised comes up **in glory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **atimia** and **doxa**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dishonour is an honest word for a corpse. Paul does not soften what a burial is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What is sown is **weak**; what is raised is **full of power**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Astheneia** and **dunamis**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fourth pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What is sown is a **natural body**; what is raised is a **spiritual body**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **soma psuchikon** and **soma pneumatikon**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Psuchikos** is from **psuche**, soul — a soul-animated body. The older versions have *natural*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What *spiritual body* does not mean"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not immaterial. Both nouns are **soma**, body, and Paul repeats the sentence to make sure: *there is a natural body, and there is a spiritual body*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The adjective describes what animates it, not what it is made of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's account has Him settling exactly this: *a spirit does not have flesh and bones, as you can see that I have* — and then He eats a piece of fish. See the note on Luke 24:36–43."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What continues"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's whole image is agricultural, which means continuity. The wheat that comes up is related to the grain that went in, and is not identical to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God gives it a body as He pleases, and to each seed its own body.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two men"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The first man Adam became a living being; the last Adam became a life-giving spirit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As was the man of dust, so also are those who are made of dust; and as is the heavenly Man, so also are those who are heavenly.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And as we have borne the image of the man of dust, we shall also bear the image of the heavenly Man.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now this I say, brethren, that flesh and blood cannot inherit the kingdom of God.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 15:50–52."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *behold, I tell you a mystery: we shall not all sleep, but we shall all be changed*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, my beloved brethren, be steadfast, immovable, always abounding in the work of the Lord, knowing that **your labour is not in vain in the Lord**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fifty-seven verses on resurrection bodies, and the application is about work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parallel elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who will **transform our lowly body that it may be conformed to His glorious body**.* See the note on Philippians 3:20–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pattern is a body that has already been raised, and Luke says it could be touched."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the four pairs describe.** Not a replacement. Paul's word for burial is *sown* — and nothing about sowing implies that what comes up is a different thing."}]}, "1 Corinthians 15:45–49": {"title": "The first man and the last", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two men, and everybody who has ever lived belongs to one of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And so it is written: \"**The first man Adam became a living being**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 2:7: *and man became a living soul*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **psuchen zosan** — a living soul, translating the Hebrew **nephesh chayyah**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Adam received life. The verse describes something happening to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The addition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The last Adam became **a life-giving spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pneuma zoopoioun** — a spirit making alive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *received life*. Gives it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not quoting this half. He adds it, and the whole argument turns on the difference between a receiver and a source."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ho eschatos Adam** — the last Adam."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the second Adam here, though Paul says *the second Man* two verses later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Last**, because there will not be a third. The line stops."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the spiritual is not what comes first; the natural comes first, and afterward the spiritual**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is correcting a sequence, probably against a Corinthian view that the spiritual was the original and the physical a decline."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis first, then the resurrection. Dust, then glory. The order is not reversible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two origins"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The first man was **of the earth, made of dust**; the second Man is **the Lord from heaven**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **adam** and **adamah** — man and ground — are the same root. Adam's name is the soil he came out of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ex ouranou** — out of heaven."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two families"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As was the man of dust, **so are those who are made of dust**; and as is the heavenly Man, **so are those who are heavenly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everybody resembles their origin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has already stated the principle: *for as in Adam all die, even so in Christ all shall be made alive*. See the note on 1 Corinthians 15:20–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And just as we have borne the image of the man of dust, **we shall also bear the image of the heavenly Man**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phoresomen** — future. We shall wear it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Eikon** — image. The word from Genesis 1:27: *God created man in His own image*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image that was given at creation and damaged is the word used of what is coming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is arguing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some in Corinth were saying *there is no resurrection of the dead*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's answer runs the whole chapter: if there is no resurrection, Christ is not risen, the preaching is empty, the faith is futile, and *we are of all men the most pitiable*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the resurrection body is like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is sown in corruption, it is raised in incorruption. It is sown in dishonour, it is raised in glory. It is sown in weakness, it is raised in power.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is sown a natural body, it is raised a spiritual body.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 15:42–44."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four contrasts, and the same body in each — sown and raised."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The change"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold, I tell you a mystery: **we shall not all sleep, but we shall all be changed** — in a moment, in the twinkling of an eye.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 15:50–52."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the image now"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But we all, with unveiled face, beholding as in a mirror the glory of the Lord, **are being transformed into the same image from glory to glory**.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 3:16–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present tense, and the same noun."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, my beloved brethren, be steadfast, immovable, always abounding in the work of the Lord, **knowing that your labour is not in vain in the Lord**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fifty-seven verses on resurrection, and the conclusion is about what to do on Monday."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul adds to the quotation.** Genesis says Adam *became a living being*. Paul supplies the other half himself: the last Adam became a spirit that **gives** life."}]}, "1 Corinthians 15:50–52": {"title": "A mystery and a trumpet", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent a chapter arguing that the dead are raised. Here he deals with the people who are not dead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Flesh and blood **cannot inherit the kingdom of God**, and **what decays cannot inherit what never decays**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phthora** (corruption, decay) against **aphtharsia** (incorruption)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that the body is bad. That it is the wrong material for the destination."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The announcement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Listen, **I am telling you a mystery**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **musterion** — not a puzzle, but something previously undisclosed and now told."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses it of things that could not have been worked out and had to be revealed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mystery"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not all of us will fall asleep, **but all of us will be changed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses, and the second is the universal one. Dying is optional in the sentence; changing is not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Fall asleep** is the New Testament's usual word for a believer's death. Paul uses it throughout this chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The speed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In **a moment**, in **the twinkling of an eye**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *moment* is **atomos** — indivisible, uncuttable. The word behind *atom*. The smallest unit of time there is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The twinkling of an eye** — **rhipe**, a flick, a dart. It is used of the beat of a wing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The signal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At **the last trumpet**. For **the trumpet will sound**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Trumpets in Israel called the assembly, moved the camp, and announced the new moon and the Day of Atonement."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the same in Thessalonians: *the Lord Himself will descend from heaven with a shout, with the voice of an archangel, and with the trumpet of God.* See the note on 1 Thessalonians 4:16–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two groups"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the dead will be raised beyond the reach of decay**, and **we will be changed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says *we*. He wrote as a man who expected to be in the second group."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For this corruptible must put on incorruption, and this mortal must put on immortality.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *put on* is **endusasthai** — to get dressed, to put on clothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same verb elsewhere of putting on Christ, and of the armour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The taunt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**O Death, where is your sting? O Hades, where is your victory?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes Hosea, and turns a prophet's line into a mockery of the thing being quoted about."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sting of death is sin, and the strength of sin is the law. **But thanks be to God, who gives us the victory** through our Lord Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, my beloved brethren, **be steadfast, immovable, always abounding in the work of the Lord**, knowing that your labour is not in vain in the Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fifty-seven verses on the resurrection, and the application is to keep working."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the next sentence in the letter is *now concerning the collection*. See the note on 1 Corinthians 16:1–4."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is promised to everybody.** Not that nobody dies. Paul says plainly that some will and some will not, and then says all of them are changed — in a unit of time he describes as indivisible."}]}, "1 Corinthians 15:53–55": {"title": "Death swallowed up in victory", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is describing a change of clothes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This body that decays **must be clothed** with what cannot decay.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **endusasthai** — to put on, as a garment. The same verb Paul uses for putting on Christ, and for the armour in Ephesians 6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *replaced*. Put on over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Said twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This body that **decays** must be clothed with what **cannot decay**, and this body that **dies** must be clothed with **life that cannot die**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then immediately again, word for word, as a *when* clause."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two pairs — corruptible/incorruptible, mortal/immortal — stated, and then restated as the condition for what comes next."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What must"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dei** — it is necessary. Flesh and blood *cannot inherit the kingdom of God*, Paul has just said three verses earlier. See the note on 1 Corinthians 15:50–52."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The change is not an upgrade offered. It is a requirement of the destination."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trigger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the words that stand written will come true**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek perfect — *has been written and stands written*. Isaiah's sentence has been on the page for seven hundred years waiting for the event that makes it true."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Death is swallowed up in victory**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 25:8, from the chapter about the feast on the mountain. See the note on Isaiah 25:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb there is **bala** — to swallow down. Isaiah uses it twice: once of the veil over the nations, once of death."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Death, where is your sting? Grave, where is your victory?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hosea 13:14. In Hosea the line is spoken by God as a summons — *O Death, I will be your plagues; O Grave, I will be your destruction*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul turns it into a taunt shouted at an enemy already beaten."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kentron** — the goad of an ox-driver, or the sting of a bee or scorpion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Paul explains it in the next verse: *the sting of death is sin, and the strength of sin is the law.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses later he answers his own question: *thanks be to God, who gives us the victory through our Lord Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being described"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A generation of believers who never die. *We shall not all sleep, but we shall all be changed* — in a moment, at the last trumpet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The dead rise incorruptible; the living are clothed without ever taking the garment off."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where else Paul says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not that we want to be unclothed, but **further clothed**, that mortality may be swallowed up by life.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 5:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same verb, same swallowing, and there he adds that God *has given us the Spirit as a guarantee*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter ends with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, my beloved brethren, **be steadfast, immovable, always abounding in the work of the Lord**, knowing that your labour is not in vain in the Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fifty-seven verses on resurrection, and the application is: keep working."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Which direction it is swallowed.** Not life absorbed into death, which is what everyone watching a funeral would assume. Death, gulped down by victory — and the word is Isaiah's, the one he had already used for the veil."}]}, "1 Corinthians 15:56–57": {"title": "\"The strength of sin is the law\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two short clauses in the middle of Paul's chapter on resurrection, and the second one is startling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **sting of death is sin**, and the **strength of sin is the law**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things linked backwards: death gets its sting from sin, and sin gets its power from the law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Remove the last link and the whole chain goes slack."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Strength\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dunamis** — power, the word behind dynamite. Not *the measure of sin* or *the definition of sin*. Its power source."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the same thing at length in Romans 7: *I would not have known covetousness unless the law had said, \"You shall not covet.\" But sin, taking opportunity by the commandment, produced in me all manner of evil desire.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *the commandment which was to bring life, I found to bring death.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which does not make the law bad"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul defends it in the same breath: *the law is holy, and the commandment holy and just and good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fault is not in the statute. It is in what a prohibition does when it meets the old nature — a wall to push against, a line to notice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sin is not weakened by being told to stop. It is armed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this is in a chapter about resurrection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just quoted Hosea: *O Death, where is your sting? O Hades, where is your victory?* It is a taunt, thrown at a defeated enemy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he explains what made the sting sharp in the first place — and the two clauses are there to show that the whole apparatus has been dismantled at the root, not merely at the grave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **thanks be to God, who gives us the victory** through our Lord Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *who helps us achieve* it. Gives — present tense, continuous in the Greek."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The victory is handed over, and the passage names where it came from and does not name any contribution."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, my beloved brethren, be steadfast, immovable, always abounding in the work of the Lord, knowing that **your labour is not in vain** in the Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one command in the paragraph comes after the victory has been given, not before it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chain is broken.** *You are not under law but under grace* — Romans 6:14, and the promise attached to it is *sin shall not have dominion over you.* Paul puts the two in one sentence, in that order. See the note on Romans 6:12–14."}]}, "1 Corinthians 16:1–4": {"title": "On the first day of the week", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just finished the great resurrection chapter, and the next sentence is about money."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 15 ends *therefore, my beloved brethren, be steadfast, immovable, always abounding in the work of the Lord, knowing that your labour is not in vain in the Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *now about the collection.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul puts the two next to each other with nothing in between."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction is not new"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do **just as I directed the churches of Galatia** to do.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is repeating an arrangement already running elsewhere. Corinth is being brought into a scheme, not given a personal appeal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On **the first day of the week**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One of the earliest references to Christians organising anything around Sunday — this letter predates the Gospels."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the day of the resurrection, the day the Emmaus road happened, the day the disciples were gathered when He came through the door, and the day Troas met to break bread. See the note on Acts 20:7–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four parts of the instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let **each one of you** **set something aside**, **storing it up** **in keeping with how well he has done**.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Each one** — nobody exempt, nobody named.", "**Set aside** — the Greek is *put by himself*, at home, personally.", "**Storing it up** — accumulating, not a single gesture.", "**In keeping with how well he has done** — the Greek is *as he may prosper*."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "No sum is stated. No percentage is stated. The measure is each person's own account of how the week went."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why weekly"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **there will be no collections to make when I come**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not want to arrive and start fundraising. He wants it already done, so that his visit is about something else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It also means nobody gives under the pressure of an apostle standing in the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who carries it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whomever **you approve by your letters** I will send to carry your gift to Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They choose the couriers, not him, and the appointment is in writing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul keeps his own hands off the money. He says why in 2 Corinthians: *avoiding this, that anyone should blame us in this lavish gift… providing honourable things, not only in the sight of the Lord, but also in the sight of men.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"And if it is fitting that I go as well\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He treats his own involvement as optional and puts it last."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it was going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jerusalem — the same relief effort that runs across four of Paul's letters, from Gentile churches to the Jewish believers in Judea. The famine had come as Agabus said it would. See the note on Acts 11:27–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 15: the dead are raised, death is swallowed up, your labour is not in vain. Chapter 16: put something aside on Sunday."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The resurrection argument ends in a practical arrangement about a collection."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is missing from the instruction.** Any figure. Paul, who could simply have set one, tells each person to weigh their own week and put something by."}]}, "1 Corinthians 2:1–5": {"title": "\"In weakness, and in fear\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul describes his own arrival in Corinth, and every detail is a disqualification."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did not bring"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I did not come with **grand speech or impressive wisdom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **huperoche logou e sophias** — superiority of word or wisdom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Corinth was a Greek city that paid for rhetoric. A travelling speaker was judged on delivery, and Paul's opponents there later said his *bodily presence is weak, and his speech contemptible*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The decision"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **I made up my mind to know nothing** while I was among you **except Jesus Christ, and Him crucified**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ekrina** — I judged, I decided. It was a deliberate narrowing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**And Him crucified** — the perfect participle: crucified and standing crucified."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just spent a chapter on why: *we proclaim Christ crucified — a stumbling block to the Jews and foolishness to the Greeks.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 1:18–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he had come from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Athens. Acts 17 has him on the Areopagus quoting Greek poets, and the response was that some mocked and some said *we will hear you again on this matter*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *after these things Paul departed from Athens and went to Corinth.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three states"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I was with you **in weakness**, and **in fear**, and **in much trembling**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **astheneia**, **phobos**, and **tromos polys**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not describing humility as a posture. He says he was frightened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Acts confirms it: in Corinth the Lord spoke to him in a vision — *do not be afraid, but speak, and do not keep silent; for I am with you, and no one will attack you to hurt you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "You do not say that to a man who is not afraid."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the preaching was not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My speaking and my preaching were **not made of the persuasive words of man's wisdom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word for *persuasive* is rare and may have been coined here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But were **a showing of the Spirit and of power**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apodeixis** — a demonstration, a proof. It was a technical term in logic for a conclusive argument."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He rejects one kind of proof and claims another."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **your faith might not rest on the wisdom of men but on the power of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *rest on* is *be in*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul names the purpose of his own weakness: a faith built on a persuasive speaker stands on the speaker."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Corinth was like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter is written to a church with factions, a lawsuit, a case of immorality worse than the pagans', drunkenness at the Lord's table, and an argument about which gift ranked highest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That congregation came out of eighteen months of preaching by a frightened man who had decided to talk about one thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about the message next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But we speak the wisdom of God in a mystery, the hidden wisdom which God ordained before the ages for our glory, **which none of the rulers of this age knew; for had they known, they would not have crucified the Lord of glory**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he lists as his qualifications.** No rhetoric, no impressive wisdom, one subject, weakness, fear and much trembling — and he says he arranged it that way on purpose."}]}, "1 Corinthians 2:10–13": {"title": "The Spirit searches", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just said no eye has seen it. This is the next sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What no eye has seen and no ear has heard, what has never risen into the heart of anyone, is what God has prepared for those who love Him.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 2:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three channels closed: observation, report, and imagination."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That verse is usually quoted on its own, as if the point were how unknowable it all is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The next word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God has **unveiled these things to us by His Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apekalupsen** — revealed, uncovered. The root of **apokalupsis**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage is not about mystery. It is about how the knowing arrives, and Paul says so immediately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The searching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the Spirit **searches everything, even the deep places of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eraunai** — searches, examines. And **ta bathe tou theou** — the depths of God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not searching in order to find out. The word is used of exploring, of going through thoroughly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The analogy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **who among men knows what is in a man except the spirit of that man within him**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A person's interior is available to one party."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody outside a man can get at his thoughts. He has private access to himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The application"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the same way, **no one has known what is in God except the Spirit of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same closed circle Jesus describes: *no one knows the Son except the Father, and no one knows the Father except the Son*. See the note on Matthew 11:25–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the same door in it: *and anyone the Son chooses to show Him to*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was received"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And what we have received is **not the spirit of the world but the Spirit who is from God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two spirits contrasted, and the Corinthians had a great deal of the first — the letter is mostly about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that we may **know the things God has freely given us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **charisthenta** — the things graciously given. From **charis**, grace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stated reason for the Spirit's presence is that the reader should know what has already been handed over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to acquire anything. To find out what is already in the account."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These are the very things **we speak, not in words taught by man's wisdom but in words taught by the Holy Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has already said what his preaching was not: *my speech and my preaching were not with persuasive words of human wisdom, but in demonstration of the Spirit and of power*. See the note on 1 Corinthians 2:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Corinth paid to hear men speak well. He is telling them why he did not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Matching **spiritual things with what is spiritual**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pneumatikois pneumatika sunkrinontes** — and it is genuinely ambiguous."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sunkrino** can mean *compare*, *combine*, or *interpret*. And the dative can be things or people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three readings survive: comparing spiritual things with spiritual things, explaining spiritual truths to spiritual people, or expressing spiritual truths in spiritual words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the natural man does not receive the things of the Spirit of God, for they are foolishness to him; nor can he know them, **because they are spiritually discerned**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *natural* is **psuchikos** — soul-driven. The same word Paul uses of the body that is sown. See the note on 1 Corinthians 15:42–44."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For who has known the mind of the LORD that he may instruct Him?\" **But we have the mind of Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes Isaiah's rhetorical question and then answers it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the famous verse actually goes.** *No eye has seen* is not the end of the sentence. The next word is *but*, and the next clause says the Spirit has already uncovered it."}]}, "1 Corinthians 2:14–16": {"title": "We have the mind of Christ", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses on why an argument fails to land, and the reason is not intelligence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The person who **lives only by his natural life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **psuchikos anthropos** — soulish man. From **psuche**, soul, life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's three-way vocabulary in this letter: **sarkikos** (fleshly), **psuchikos** (natural), and **pneumatikos** (spiritual)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Psuchikos** does not mean stupid or immoral. It means operating on the equipment everybody has."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Does **not welcome the things of the Spirit of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ou dechetai** — does not receive, does not accept."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is about welcoming a guest. Not *cannot hear*. Does not take in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict he reaches"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For to him **they are foolishness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Moria** — folly. The word Paul used two chapters earlier: *the message of the cross is foolishness to those who are perishing*. See the note on 1 Corinthians 1:18–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **he is not able to know them**, because they are **discerned in a spiritual way**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ou dunatai gnonai** — is not able to know."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Pneumatikos anakrinetai** — spiritually examined."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Anakrino** — to examine, to sift, to investigate. A legal word for a preliminary hearing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is used three times in these two verses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The claim is not that the natural man lacks information. It is that the instrument is wrong for the material."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the one who is spiritual weighs all things, while he himself is weighed by no one**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same verb both times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that does not mean"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that a spiritual person is above correction. Paul submits himself to examination constantly: *with me it is a very small thing that I should be judged by you or by a human court*, and then *He who judges me is the Lord*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence is about category, not immunity. Someone using a different instrument cannot assess the result."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **who has known the mind of the Lord, that he should instruct Him?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 40:13: *who has directed the Spirit of the LORD, or as His counsellor has taught Him?* See the note on Isaiah 40:12–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer expected is nobody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet **we have the mind of Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hemeis de noun Christou echomen**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah's question had no answer. Paul quotes it and then answers it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pronoun"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hemeis** — we. Plural, and emphatic."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *I*, and not *some believers*. He is writing to a divided congregation with sexual scandal, lawsuits between members, drunkenness at the Lord's table, and factions named after teachers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them they have the mind of Christ."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter started"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And I, brethren, when I came to you, did not come with excellence of speech or of wisdom… **I was with you in weakness, in fear, and in much trembling**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And my speech and my preaching were not with persuasive words of human wisdom, **but in demonstration of the Spirit and of power**.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 2:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Spirit does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But God has revealed them to us through His Spirit. For **the Spirit searches all things, yes, the deep things of God**.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 2:10–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For what man knows the things of a man except the spirit of the man which is in him?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And I, brethren, could not speak to you as to spiritual people **but as to carnal, as to babes in Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 3 opens by telling the same people they are not behaving like it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul states the possession and then addresses the behaviour, in that order."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb.** **Anakrino** — to examine, to sift. Three times in two verses. The claim is about which instrument the material answers to, not about how clever anybody is."}]}, "1 Corinthians 2:6–9": {"title": "They would never have crucified Him", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just said he came in weakness and fear, and now says he does speak wisdom after all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he said a paragraph earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I determined not to know anything among you except Jesus Christ and Him crucified. I was with you in weakness, in fear, and in much trembling.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And my speech and my preaching were not with persuasive words of human wisdom, but in demonstration of the Spirit and of power.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The qualification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet **we do speak wisdom among those who are mature**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **teleiois** — the complete, the full-grown."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has not abandoned wisdom. He has abandoned one kind of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose wisdom it is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not the wisdom of **this present age**, nor of **the rulers of this age**, who are **all coming to nothing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katargoumenon** — being brought to nothing, a present passive participle. The process is already running."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Corinth was full of professional rhetoricians. The city paid to hear men speak well."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wisdom of God, **a wisdom kept hidden in mystery**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **en musterio** — in a mystery. Not a riddle; something that was concealed and has now been disclosed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it was decided"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which **God appointed before the ages for our glory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pro ton aionon** — before the ages."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a response to the fall. Settled beforehand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says the same: *He indeed was foreordained before the foundation of the world, but was manifest in these last times for you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which **none of the rulers of this age understood; for if they had understood it, they would never have crucified the Lord of glory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most striking claim in the passage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crucifixion is described as an act of ignorance. Not a miscalculation about a man — a failure to see what was in front of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the rulers were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read plainly: Pilate, Herod, Caiaphas and the council."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says it to the crowd in Jerusalem: *I know that you did it in ignorance, as did also your rulers*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And from the cross: *Father, forgive them, for they do not know what they do*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **Lord of glory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James uses the same phrase. It is the highest title Paul gives Him anywhere, and he uses it in the sentence about the execution."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What **no eye has seen and no ear has heard**, what has **never risen into the heart of anyone**, is what **God has prepared for those who love Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Closest to Isaiah 64: *since the beginning of the world men have not heard nor perceived by the ear, nor has the eye seen any God besides You, who acts for the one who waits for Him*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Three channels, all closed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eye, ear, and the heart's own imagining."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing gets in by observation, by report, or by thinking it up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse the quotation is usually severed from:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **God has revealed them to us through His Spirit**. For the Spirit searches all things, yes, the deep things of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage is not about how unknowable it all is. It is about how the knowing arrives — and Paul says it in the very next sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Spirit is compared to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For what man knows the things of a man except the spirit of the man which is in him? Even so **no one knows the things of God except the Spirit of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same argument as *no one knows the Son except the Father*. See the note on Matthew 11:25–27."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the crucifixion is called.** A failure of recognition. Paul says the rulers would not have done it if they had known — and then says the knowing comes by the Spirit, in the next line."}]}, "1 Corinthians 3:16–17": {"title": "\"That temple is what you are\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks a question he clearly thinks they should not need asked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Do you not know\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the phrase ten times in this letter. It is not gentle — it means *you already have this information.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are **the temple of God**, and **the Spirit of God makes His home in you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *temple* is **naos** — not the whole temple complex but the inner sanctuary, the holy place itself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the courts. The room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who \"you\" is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Plural, throughout. Paul is addressing the church at Corinth as a body — *you all, together, are the sanctuary.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He makes the individual point elsewhere in the same letter: *your body is the temple of the Holy Spirit who is in you.* Both are true, and this one is corporate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which church he says it to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Corinth. The letter's own list: division into factions, a man sleeping with his father's wife, believers suing each other, drunkenness at the Lord's table, chaos in the meetings, and some of them denying the resurrection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul calls that congregation the sanctuary of God, in chapter 3, before correcting any of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If anyone **ruins** the temple of God, **God will ruin him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same Greek verb both times — **phtheiro**, to corrupt, to destroy, to spoil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What you do to it is what happens to you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the ruining is, in context"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter is about the divisions: *I am of Paul… I am of Apollos.* And about building on the foundation with gold and silver or with wood, hay and straw."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thing that ruins the temple in this passage is factionalism — men splitting a congregation around their favourite teacher."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the temple of God is **holy**, and **that temple is what you are**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek puts the emphasis at the end: *which you are.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What this replaced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Solomon's temple took seven years, a hundred and eighty thousand men, cedar, gold, and stone cut silently in a quarry. See the note on 1 Kings 6:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Solomon's own verdict at the dedication: *heaven and the heaven of heavens cannot contain You. How much less this temple which I have built!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul tells a fractious church in Greece that they are the room."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Jesus said about the building.** *\"Destroy this temple, and in three days I will raise it up.\"* They thought He meant the forty-six-year construction project they were standing in. *But He was speaking of the temple of His body.*"}]}, "1 Corinthians 3:5–9": {"title": "Two men, one field, and the verb that settles the argument", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The Corinthians had split into fan clubs — some for Paul, some for Apollos. Paul answers with farming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tenses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I planted, Apollos watered, but God is the One who made it grow.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Greek the first two verbs are aorist — single, finished actions. Somebody turned up, did a job, and left. The third is imperfect: **kept on giving the growth**, continuous, still going."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole argument is in the grammar. Two men each did a discrete piece of work. One party has been doing the thing that actually matters, the whole time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Neither one is anything\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a hard sentence about himself, and Paul means it as a corrective to people building identities out of which preacher they preferred."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But he does not leave them worthless: *each will receive his own reward according to his own labour*. Not according to results. According to labour — which is the only part a planter or waterer controls."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *We are fellow workers who belong to God; you are God's field, God's building.* Paul flips the metaphor mid-sentence, from farm to construction, and he will run with the building for the next several verses. Both images have the same point: the workers are staff, and the property is somebody else's."}]}, "1 Corinthians 4:14–17": {"title": "Ten thousand guardians, not many fathers", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four chapters of correction, and then he tells them why he wrote it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says he is not doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am **not writing these things to shame you**, but to warn you as **my beloved children**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **entrepon** — to make ashamed, to turn a face away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just finished a paragraph of heavy irony: *you are already full, you are already rich, you have reigned as kings without us*, and *we are fools for Christ's sake, but you are wise*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he stops and says the point was never humiliation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for warn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nouthetein** — to put in mind, to admonish. It is the word for what a parent does, and it is paired here with **beloved children**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For even if you had **ten thousand guardians** to keep you in line in Christ, **you do not have many fathers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **murious paidagogous** — ten thousand pedagogues."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A **paidagogos** was a household slave who walked a boy to school, watched his conduct, and had authority to discipline him. Not the teacher. The escort."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same word of the law: *the law was our tutor to bring us to Christ*. See the note on Galatians 3:23–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the contrast is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Guardians can be multiplied. Fathers cannot."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Corinthians had plenty of instruction available — Apollos, Cephas, and whoever else the factions were forming around. Paul does not deny any of them their competence. He says the category is different."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was **in Christ Jesus that I became your father through the good news**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **egennesa** — I begot you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He grounds the claim in the gospel, not in office. He was there when it happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So I beg you, **be imitators of me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parakalo** — I urge, I appeal. Not a command from a position."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He repeats it later with the qualification: *imitate me, just as I also imitate Christ*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Timothy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That is why I have sent Timothy to you, **my beloved and faithful child in the Lord**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two words he has just used of the Corinthians — beloved, child."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul addresses Timothy this way in both letters to him: *my own true son in the faith*, and *a son I love dearly*. See the note on 2 Timothy 1:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Timothy is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who will **call to your mind my ways in Christ**, just as I teach them **everywhere in every church**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anamnesei** — will remind you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing new is being brought. And the last clause is the check: the same teaching everywhere, so Corinth cannot claim a private version."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the factions were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Each of you says, \"I am of Paul,\" or \"I am of Apollos,\" or \"I am of Cephas,\" or \"I am of Christ.\"* See the note on 1 Corinthians 1:10–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A church sorting itself by teacher. Paul's answer, four chapters later, is that they are sorting by the wrong category."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about the workers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I planted, Apollos watered, **but God gave the increase**.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 3:5–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Neither he who plants is anything, nor he who waters, but God who gives the increase.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He empties the office before he claims the fatherhood, which is what keeps the claim from being a bid for a faction of his own."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two categories.** Guardians escort and correct, and you can have ten thousand of them. Fathers beget, and Paul's claim is not to the office but to having been there when it happened."}]}, "1 Corinthians 5:1–2": {"title": "A grief not yet felt", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul names a situation in one sentence and then turns, immediately, to the congregation's reaction to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being reported"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Immorality of a kind **not found even among the nations around you** — that a man is living with his father's wife.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roman law itself forbade this; Cicero calls a comparable case an almost unheard-of crime. Paul's measure is not the synagogue but the pagan city, and Corinth was not a strict place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His father's wife*, not *his mother* — the phrase indicates a stepmother, and Leviticus 18 names it directly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **you** are swollen with pride over it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is the one Paul has been using through the whole letter for the Corinthians' inflation — puffed up, blown out like a bellows. He has used it in chapter 4 twice already."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses in, and the subject has changed from the man to the church."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What should have been there instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Should you not rather have been **broken with grief**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is the one used of mourning the dead. Paul does not ask for outrage, disgust, or a statement. He asks for a funeral."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And notice the order in his sentence: *so that the man… would be taken out from among you.* The mourning comes first, and the action follows from it. A church that had grieved would have known what to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they were proud"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter's whole argument gives the answer. Corinth prized wisdom, freedom, and being beyond the reach of small rules. A man doing this and remaining in fellowship was, in that atmosphere, a demonstration of how free they were."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul does not do.** He does not write to the man. The letter is addressed to the church, the pride is the church's, and the correction lands there. See the notes on 1 Corinthians 5:3–5 and 1 Corinthians 5:9–11."}]}, "1 Corinthians 5:3–5": {"title": "\"So that the spirit may be saved\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of Paul's severest paragraphs, and the last clause tells you what the whole thing is for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Present in spirit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though I am absent in body I am **present with you in spirit**, and I have already reached my judgment, as though I were standing there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the same thing to the Colossians — *absent in the flesh, with you in spirit* — and there it is warm approval of a church holding its line. Here it is a verdict."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase does both, which suggests it means something more than a fond thought at a distance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When you are gathered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the name of our Lord Jesus Christ, **when you are gathered together** — my spirit with you, and the power of our Lord Jesus Christ with you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things are named as present at the meeting: the church, Paul's spirit, and *the power of our Lord Jesus Christ*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a gathering in the name, with the power, and Paul is careful to say so before he says what is to be done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Handed over"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Such a one is to be handed over to Satan for the destruction of the flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *handed over* is the same verb used of Jesus being delivered up. It means to place into someone's hands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The flesh** here is set against **the spirit** in the next clause, and Paul's usual use of *sarx* is not the body but the whole self as it operates apart from God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*…**so that the spirit may be saved** in the day of the Lord Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in the sentence bends toward this. The severity is instrumental. Paul is not describing a punishment with an end in itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What actually happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If 2 Corinthians 2 refers to the same man — and most readers have taken it that way — the sequel is Paul urging the church to *forgive and comfort him*, in case he is *swallowed up with too much sorrow*, and to *reaffirm your love for him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The discipline worked, and Paul's next concern is that it not go too far."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter is really about.** Not the offender. Paul's anger in verse 2 is at the congregation: *and you are proud!* A church that boasted of its tolerance is the target, and the sentence in verse 5 is the shock administered to it."}]}, "1 Corinthians 5:6–8": {"title": "Which you already are", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is dealing with a scandal, and he argues from what they already are."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man in the congregation living with his father's wife, and the church proud of how tolerant it was being about it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And you are puffed up, and have not rather mourned.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rebuke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This **boasting of yours is not a good thing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kauchema** — the thing boasted in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proverb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do you not know that **a little yeast works its way through the whole batch of dough**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **zume** — leaven. And **phurama**, the lump."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes the same line in Galatians, about a different problem entirely — there it is circumcision being smuggled back in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What leaven is doing in the argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Passover every scrap of leaven had to be searched out of the house before the feast began. The seven days that follow are the Feast of Unleavened Bread."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is writing to Gentiles in Corinth and expects them to follow the picture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Clear out the old yeast, then, so that you may be **a fresh batch — bread with no yeast in it at all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ekkatharate** — clean out thoroughly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause everything turns on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which is **what you already are**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kathos este azumoi** — as you are unleavened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them to become what he has just said they are. The command and the fact sit in the same sentence, and the fact is the reason for the command."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul writes this to the church with more disorder in it than any other he corresponded with — divisions, lawsuits, drunkenness at the Lord's table, chaos in the meetings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He still calls them unleavened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **Christ, our Passover lamb, has been sacrificed for us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **etuthe** — a perfect passive. It has been sacrificed and the sacrifice stands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blood on the doorposts is why the house is clean. See the note on Exodus 12:1–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the Baptist, pointing: *behold, the Lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world*. See the note on John 1:29–34."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The feast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **let us keep the feast** — not with the old yeast, nor with the **yeast of malice and wickedness**, but with **bread made without yeast, the bread of sincerity and truth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *sincerity* is **eilikrineias** — from *judged by sunlight*. Held up to the light with nothing hidden in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two leavens named"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Malice and wickedness — **kakias** and **ponerias**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the sexual sin the chapter is about. Paul names the attitudes of the congregation watching it, not the act."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he tells them to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hand the man over — and the reason given is *that his spirit may be saved in the day of the Lord Jesus*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second letter is generally read as the sequel: *this punishment which was inflicted by the majority is sufficient… **now you ought rather to forgive and comfort him**, lest somehow such a one be swallowed up with too much sorrow.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 7:10–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The discipline in the first letter has a reinstatement in the second."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of the sentence.** *Clear out the old yeast, so that you may be a fresh batch — which is what you already are.* The status comes last in the sentence and first in the logic."}]}, "1 Corinthians 5:9–11": {"title": "The letter they misread", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul had written to Corinth before, and they had taken his instruction and turned it exactly the wrong way round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The earlier letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I wrote to you in my letter not to keep company with people who are sexually immoral.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A letter we do not have. This is one of the places where the New Testament refers plainly to correspondence that was not kept."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did not mean"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I did not mean the immoral of this world **at all** — otherwise you would have to leave the world altogether.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had read it as a rule about outsiders. Paul says the opposite was intended, and his reason is practical to the point of dryness: you would have to leave the planet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no instruction anywhere in Paul to withdraw from unbelievers. The separation he asks for runs in the other direction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did mean"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not to keep company with anyone **who is called a brother** and is…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The qualifier is the hinge of the paragraph. The list that follows applies inside the church, to someone claiming the name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six items, and they are not sorted by how shocking they are:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["sexually immoral", "**greedy**", "an idolater", "**someone who tears people down with his words**", "a drunkard", "a swindler"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "A reviler sits between an idolater and a drunkard. Paul puts the sins of the mouth and the wallet in the same sentence as the sin that started the chapter, without ranking them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Given that chapter 5 began with the church being *swollen with pride*, the inclusion of the reviler is not accidental."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Not even to sit down and eat\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The table was where fellowship was expressed. In Corinth it was also where the Lord's Supper was eaten."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Withholding the meal is the specific action named — and read against chapter 11, where the rich shamed the poor at that same table, the instruction is not about keeping the meal pure. It is about not letting the meal say something untrue."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the chapter ends.** *God judges those outside.* Paul draws the line explicitly: the church's business is the church. The verse most often used to police the world is, in its own context, a refusal to do that."}]}, "1 Corinthians 6:15–17": {"title": "One spirit with Him", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul argues against a sin by telling them what they are."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do you not know that **your bodies are parts of Christ Himself**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **mele Christou** — members, limbs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks *do you not know* six times in this chapter alone. He is not informing them; he is reminding them of something they had been told and were not using."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Corinth was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A port city with a reputation bad enough that the Greeks had coined a verb from its name. Temple prostitution on the acropolis above it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men he is writing to had been going, and defending it with a slogan: *all things are lawful for me*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The horror"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Shall I then **take what belongs to the body of Christ and join it to a prostitute? It must never be**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me genoito** — Paul's strongest negative, the one he uses fourteen times in Romans. *God forbid*, in the old versions. *Absolutely not.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument is not that it is dirty. It is that it involves taking a limb of Christ somewhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Or do you not know that the man who is **joined to a prostitute is one body with her**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kollomenos** — glued, cemented."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul refuses the idea that a transaction is only a transaction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the two, He says, shall become one flesh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 2, the marriage sentence, quoted about the least marital arrangement available in the city."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse Jesus uses about what God has joined together. Paul applies it here to show that the mechanism does not care about the intention."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the one who is joined to the Lord is one spirit with Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb — **kollomenos**, glued."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One flesh in the first case, one spirit in the second. Paul sets the two unions in the same grammar and lets the comparison do the work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it settles the question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a rule. A collision: a man already joined to the Lord in spirit cannot join his body elsewhere without dragging the first union into it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Flee sexual immorality. Every sin that a man does is outside the body, but he who commits sexual immorality **sins against his own body**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *flee* is **pheugete** — run. Present imperative, keep running."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph is the model, and he left his coat behind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Or do you not know that **your body is the temple of the Holy Spirit** who is in you, whom you have from God, and you are not your own? For **you were bought at a price**; therefore glorify God in your body and in your spirit, which are God's.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **naos** — the inner sanctuary, not the temple complex."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same image of the whole church in chapter 3. Here it is one body."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The slogans and the answers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All things are lawful for me* — *but not all things are helpful*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All things are lawful for me* — *but I will not be brought under the power of any*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Foods for the stomach and the stomach for foods* — *but the body is for the Lord, and the Lord for the body*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes their line every time and answers it every time, and never once by denying the first half."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verses before it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And such were some of you. **But you were washed, but you were sanctified, but you were justified** in the name of the Lord Jesus and by the Spirit of our God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs, all past. The list of what they used to be sits immediately before *do you not know*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word used twice.** Glued. Paul puts the two unions in identical grammar — one body with her, one spirit with Him — and the argument is that the second one is already true."}]}, "1 Corinthians 6:18–20": {"title": "\"You are not your own\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul gives one instruction about running away and then three reasons about ownership."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Flee** sexual immorality.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pheugo** — run. Not resist, not manage, not stand firm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same word for idolatry, and Joseph is the model — *he left the garment in her hand and fled outside.* See the note on Genesis 39:7–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There are things the New Testament says to stand against and things it says to run from, and Paul is clear which this is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distinction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Every other sin a person commits is **outside the body**, but the one who sins sexually **sins against his own body**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's own argument, and readers have long noticed it will not survive a pedantic reading — gluttony and drunkenness reach the body too."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not writing a taxonomy. He is saying this one goes somewhere the others do not, and his reason is in the verses either side: *the two shall become one flesh.* See the note on Genesis 2:21–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The temple"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do you not know that **your body is the temple of the Holy Spirit** who is in you, **whom you have from God**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **naos** — the inner sanctuary, not the courts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And this one is singular. Three chapters earlier Paul said the same of the church collectively; here he says it of a person. See the note on 1 Corinthians 3:16–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whom you have from God* — the Spirit is described as a possession received rather than an achievement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ownership"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**And that you are not your own?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words, and they are the argument of the whole passage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Corinth's slogan, quoted by Paul twice in this chapter, was *all things are lawful for me.* This is his answer to it: the question is not what is permitted but whose you are."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The price"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You were bought at a price.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a market word — purchased, the way you buy something at a stall."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not name the sum here. He does elsewhere: *not with corruptible things, like silver or gold… but with the precious blood of Christ.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **glorify God in your body** and in your spirit, which are God's.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note where it lands. Not *avoid* — glorify. The instruction is positive and it is about the body specifically."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Corinth was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The city had a reputation across the empire; *to Corinthianise* was a Greek verb for living immorally."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had just listed what some of them had been — and then: *and such were some of you. But you were washed, but you were sanctified, but you were justified.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs, all past tense, before any of this instruction."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the argument starts.** *Do you not know?* — six times in this one chapter. Paul's remedy for the Corinthians' behaviour is repeatedly to tell them what is already true of them."}]}, "1 Corinthians 9:11–14": {"title": "We have not used this", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He proves he has the right, and then says he has never taken it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument so far"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Who serves as a soldier at his own expense? Who plants a vineyard and does not eat its fruit? Who tends a flock and does not drink the milk?"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the law: *do not muzzle an ox while it treads out the grain*. See the note on 1 Corinthians 9:7–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three illustrations from ordinary work, and one from Deuteronomy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exchange"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If we have **sown spiritual things** among you, is it too much if we **reap material things** from you?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ta pneumatika** and **ta sarkika** — the spiritual and the fleshly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a criticism of the material. Paul is naming two categories and calling the trade proportionate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If **others share this right over you**, do we not have it all the more?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody in Corinth had been supported. Paul does not name them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He founded the church. The comparison is uncomfortable and he makes it anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet **we have not used this right**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ouk echresametha** — we did not make use of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verses establishing the entitlement, and then that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Instead **we bear all things**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **panta stegomen** — from **stego**, to cover, to hold off, to endure. The verb of a roof keeping out rain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul worked with his hands. *These hands have provided for my necessities, and for those who were with me* — tent-making, in Corinth, in the house of Aquila and Priscilla."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **we may put no hindrance in the way of the good news of Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **enkopen** — a cutting into, an obstacle. The word for breaking up a road to stop an army advancing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Corinth was full of travelling teachers who charged. Paul refused to look like one of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The temple argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do you not know that **those who do the work of the temple eat from the temple**, and that **those who serve at the altar share in what is offered at the altar**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Levites had no land allotment. They lived on the tithes and the portions of the sacrifices — the arrangement is set out in Numbers 18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone in the ancient world, Jew or Greek, understood that priests ate from the altar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the same way **the Lord has ordained that those who preach the good news should live from the good news**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dietaxen** — commanded, arranged, ordered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul attributes it to Jesus: *the labourer is worthy of his wages*, from the sending of the seventy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The strength of the claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not call it a courtesy or a kindness. He calls it an ordinance of the Lord."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then does not take it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But I have used none of these things, nor have I written these things that it should be done so to me. For **it would be better for me to die** than that anyone should make my boasting void.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kauchema** — his ground of boasting, which is that he preaches free of charge."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle underneath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **though I am free from all men, I have made myself a servant to all**, that I might win the more.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have become all things to all men, that I might by all means save some.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 9:19–23."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he accepted from elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I robbed other churches, taking wages from them to minister to you.* And from the Philippians: *even in Thessalonica you sent aid once and again for my necessities*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He took support from churches he was not currently working in, and none from the one he was."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The shape.** Eight verses proving the right belongs to him — a soldier's pay, a farmer's fruit, the law of the ox, the altar, and a command of the Lord — and then: *we have not used it*."}]}, "1 Corinthians 9:7–10": {"title": "Soldier, vineyard, flock", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul argues a right at length and then, in the same chapter, declines to use it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who ever serves as a **soldier at his own expense**? Who **plants a vineyard** and does not eat its fruit? Who **tends a flock** and does not drink of the milk?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three trades, three self-evident answers. Nobody funds their own army service, plants a vineyard for someone else's grapes, or shepherds without milk."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The principle is so ordinary that Paul does not argue it — he just asks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the law"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Am I saying these things **only as a man would**? **Does the law not say the same**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He will not rest the case on common sense. He goes to Moses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not muzzle an ox while it treads out the grain.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An ox walking in circles over the threshing floor, with grain under its feet all day, and the law forbids tying its mouth shut."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question he asks about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Is it **oxen that God is concerned about**, or does He say it entirely for our sake?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A striking piece of interpretation. Paul takes an agricultural regulation and says the point of it was never really the ox."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For our sake it was written, that **the one who ploughs ought to plough in hope**, and the one who threshes in hope of sharing in the harvest.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whoever does the work should have grounds to expect a share of what the work produces."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul concludes from it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If we have sown spiritual things for you, is it a great thing if we reap your material things?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a few verses later: *the Lord has commanded that those who preach the gospel should live from the gospel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He establishes the right about as thoroughly as it can be established."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **I have used none of these things**… For it would be better for me to die than that anyone should make my boasting void.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had the right and would not take it, and the reason he gives is *lest we hinder the gospel of Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Corinth he made tents. In Thessalonica he *worked night and day, that we might not be a burden.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Both things at once"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul holds two positions without embarrassment: that ministers ought to be supported, and that he personally would not be."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He argues the first for other people's sake and declines it for the gospel's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Timothy gets the same principle as instruction: *the elders who lead well should be counted worthy of a double portion of honour… the worker has earned his pay.* See the note on 1 Timothy 5:17–18."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says he is doing instead.** *What is my reward then? That when I preach the gospel, I may present the gospel of Christ **without charge**.* The pay he wanted was the pleasure of not charging."}]}, "1 John 1:7": {"title": "Walking in the light", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it contains a comparison people usually skip past."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is the message which we have heard from Him and declare to you, that **God is light and in Him is no darkness at all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek doubles the negative — **skotia ouk estin oudemia**. No darkness, none whatever."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the first of John's three *if we say* clauses: *if we say that we have fellowship with Him, and walk in darkness, we lie and do not practise the truth.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **if we walk in the light**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **peripatomen** — a present subjunctive. Go on walking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Light in John is a place, not a standard of conduct. In the Gospel: *I am the light of the world. He who follows Me shall not walk in darkness, but have the light of life.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The way **He is in the light**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hos autos estin en to photi** — as He is in the light."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *as brightly as He is*, which would be an impossible standard and would make the rest of the verse unnecessary. The comparison is about where, not how well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is in the light. Walking in the light is being where He is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **we are in this together**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **koinonian echomen met' allelon** — we have fellowship with one another."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The horizontal one. The verse before was about fellowship with Him; this one lands on the company."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the **blood of Jesus Christ His Son washes us clean of every sin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katharizei** — a present tense. Goes on cleansing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cleansing is happening to people who are already in the light. That is the point of the verse and the reason the clause is there at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "If walking in the light meant sinlessness there would be nothing left to cleanse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Every sin"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From **all sin** — **pases hamartias**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No category is set aside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If we say that we have no sin, **we deceive ourselves**, and the truth is not in us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John states the cleansing and immediately forbids the conclusion that there is nothing to cleanse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the one after that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If we **confess our sins**, He is **faithful and just to forgive us** our sins and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *confess* is **homologomen** — to say the same thing. To agree with what has already been said about it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the two adjectives are **pistos kai dikaios** — faithful and righteous. Not *merciful*. The forgiveness is described as an act of justice, because the ground of it was settled at the cross."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three false claims"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John lists them in a row, each starting *if we say*:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We have fellowship with Him* — while walking in darkness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We have no sin.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We have not sinned* — which, he says, *makes Him a liar*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the paragraph ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My little children, these things I write to you, **so that you may not sin**. And **if anyone sins, we have an Advocate with the Father**, Jesus Christ the righteous.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parakleton** — the same word Jesus uses of the Spirit in the upper room. One Helper here, one there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That you may know that you have eternal life.* See the note on 1 John 5:13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter about sin and light is written by a man whose stated purpose is assurance."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word doing the work.** *As He is in the light.* A comparison of location, not of performance — and the blood is described as cleansing the people already standing there."}]}, "1 John 2:15": {"title": "The world", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence and its consequence, and everything depends on what the noun means."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not give your love to **the world**, or to anything in it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me agapate ton kosmon**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present imperative — stop loving, or do not go on loving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What *kosmos* does not mean here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the planet. God made it and called it good."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not people. John's own Gospel has *God so loved the world that He gave His only begotten Son*. Same word, same author."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does mean"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John defines it himself in the next verse, which is why the two belong together:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All that is in the world — **the lust of the flesh, the lust of the eyes, and the pride of life** — is not of the Father but is of the world.* See the note on 1 John 2:16–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A system, running on three engines. Appetite, acquisition, and status."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If anyone loves the world, **the love of the Father is not in him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek allows two readings: the Father's love for him, or his love for the Father."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second fits John's usage better, and the argument does not depend on it either way. The two loves cannot occupy the same space."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parallel elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No one can serve two masters; for either he will hate the one and love the other, or else he will be loyal to the one and despise the other. **You cannot serve God and mammon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same structure. Not *should not*. Cannot."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And James, more bluntly: *do you not know that friendship with the world is enmity with God?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is not asceticism"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter is not against material things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John opens his third letter wishing his friend prosperity and health. Paul tells Timothy that God *gives us richly all things to enjoy*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The object of the verb is what is at issue. **Agapate** — set your love on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse after the definition: *and the world is passing away, and the lust of it; but **he who does the will of God abides forever***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paragetai** — is passing by, going past. A present tense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *will pass away*. It is going now, while being loved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the argument. Not that the world is bad, but that it is in motion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being written to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter addresses three groups by name — little children, young men, fathers — and repeats each."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I write to you, young men, because you have overcome the wicked one.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I write to you, fathers, because you have known Him who is from the beginning.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction about the world comes immediately after that paragraph, and the young men have just been told they are strong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John says at the end of the letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And we know that we are of God, and **the whole world lies under the sway of the wicked one**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the reason for the whole passage."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the noun means.** John supplies it in the next verse — appetite, acquisition, status. And his reason for the instruction is not that the system is evil but that it is in motion."}]}, "1 John 2:16–17": {"title": "The three cravings, and where they were first used", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John lists three things and says none of them come from the Father. The list is not arbitrary — it has been used before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the craving of the body**", "**the craving of the eyes**", "**the bragging about what a man has**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Now set that beside Genesis 3, where Eve looks at the tree:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*the fruit seemed **good to eat*** — the body", "***pleasing to the eye*** — the eyes", "***desirable for the wisdom it promised*** — the standing it would give her"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same three, in the same order, at the first temptation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And in the wilderness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three temptations of Christ run the same way. Turn the stones into bread — the body. All the kingdoms of the world shown to Him — the eyes. Throw Yourself down and let the angels catch You — the display, the proving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One test taken in a garden with everything available, and one in a desert with nothing. The first was failed and the second was not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John says about the shelf life"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The world is on its way out, and everything it makes you crave along with it. But whoever does what God wants stays forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is present continuous — the world **is passing**, in progress, already under way. He is not predicting an end so much as describing something currently draining away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The contrast is not between pleasure and misery. It is between what is going and what stays."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why it is not a list of forbidden things.** John does not name any object — no food, no possession, no specific pleasure. Every item on his list is an appetite pointed a particular direction. The problem he identifies is where the craving comes *from*, not what it happens to land on."}]}, "1 John 2:20–21": {"title": "\"There is an anointing on you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John is writing about people who had left the church claiming superior knowledge, and this is his answer to the readers who stayed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chrisma** — an anointing, and the noun is built on the same root as **Christos**, the Anointed One. John is making the connection deliberately: the Anointed has anointed them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Israel the oil went on prophets, priests and kings. John says the ordinary believers reading his letter have it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And you know all things.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is not a claim to omniscience — the next verse makes that clear: *I have not written to you because you do not know the truth, but because you **do** know it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not adding to their information. He is telling them they already have what the departing teachers claimed to be selling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses just before name the problem: people had gone out from among them, teaching that Jesus was not the Christ. The whole passage is about a congregation being told they were missing something."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's response is not a counter-argument. It is: you have the anointing, and you know."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"No lie ever comes out of the truth\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "A short, blunt line, and it is the test he gives them. Truth does not generate lies. If a teaching leads away from what they already knew about Christ, its source is settled by that fact alone."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it goes.** A few verses on: *the anointing you received from Him remains in you… as it has taught you, remain in Him.* John does not tell them to seek a fresh experience. He tells them what they have stays, and to stay in the One who gave it."}]}, "1 John 2:26–27": {"title": "\"That anointing stays right in you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John says why he wrote the paragraph, and then tells them they did not need it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have written this to you about **the people who are trying to lead you off**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **planonton** — deceiving, leading astray. The root of *planet* — the wandering star."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has been about them: *they went out from us, but they were not of us*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **that anointing from Him stays right in you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **chrisma** — an anointing, the oil itself. It occurs three times in the New Testament, all in this chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Christos** — Christ, the Anointed One — comes from the same root. The anointing and the Anointed share the word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The permanence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Stays right in you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **menei** — abides, remains. It is John's favourite verb; he uses it more than sixty times across his Gospel and letters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **nobody has to teach you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is a strange sentence to find in the middle of a letter written to teach them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just spent two chapters instructing them, and warns them about false teachers in the verse before."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence is not against teaching. It is against needing a class of people to mediate what they already have."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the anointing does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His anointing **teaches you about everything**, and **it is true, and there is no lie in it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two claims: it teaches, and it does not lie."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The false teachers are the contrast. John has just called their message a lie: *who is a liar but he who denies that Jesus is the Christ?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **just as it has taught you — you will stay in Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek can be read as a statement or an imperative. Either way the verb is **meno** again — the same word as the anointing's abiding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It abides in them; they abide in Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he said seven verses earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **you have an anointing from the Holy One, and you know all things**.* See the note on 1 John 2:20–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice in one chapter, which is how John signals something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus said about the same thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But the Helper, the Holy Spirit, whom the Father will send in My name, **He will teach you all things**, and bring to your remembrance all things that I said to you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"When He, the Spirit of truth, has come, **He will guide you into all truth**.\"* See the note on John 16:12–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John was in the room when both sentences were spoken, and he is writing decades later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter keeps doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Testing. John's letters are full of *by this we know* — five or six times over. He does not tell them to trust a feeling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tests are doctrinal and practical: what they say about Christ, and whether they love each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he was writing to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My little children.* He uses it seven times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An old man, the last of the twelve, writing to congregations he had watched people walk out of."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The odd sentence.** *Nobody has to teach you* — written by a teacher, in a letter, four verses after warning them about teachers. What he is removing is the middleman, not the instruction."}]}, "1 John 3:16": {"title": "\"Not talk, but what we do\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John gives the definition of a word everyone thought they already knew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is **how we have come to know the love of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **egnokamen** — perfect tense, we have come to know and know still."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not describing a feeling arrived at. He is pointing at an event and saying that is where the knowledge comes from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The definition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **gave His own life away for us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *He laid down His life* — **tithemi**, to put down, to place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the verb Jesus uses of Himself in John's Gospel: *no one takes it from Me. **I lay it down of My own accord**.* See the note on John 10:16–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same writer, the same word, decades apart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John refuses to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Define love by how it feels. His letters use the word more than any other book in the New Testament and he never once describes an emotion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every time, he points at the cross or at an action."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **we ought to give our own lives away for the brothers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **opheilomen** — we are indebted, we owe it. It is a financial word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But whoever has this world's goods, and **sees his brother in need**, and **shuts up his heart from him**, how does the love of God abide in him?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John immediately scales it down from dying to a coat and a meal, which is the harder test."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse it turns on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My little children, **let us not love in word or in tongue, but in deed and in truth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *not in word, neither in tongue*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "3:16 in both — John 3:16 and 1 John 3:16, the same author, the same subject, from the two sides:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God so loved the world that He gave His only begotten Son.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gave His own life away for us; and we ought to give our own lives away for the brothers.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other definition in the letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In this is love, **not that we loved God, but that He loved us**, and sent His Son to be the propitiation for our sins.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John insists on the direction every time he defines it. See the note on 1 John 4:8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it produces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And by this **we know that we are of the truth**, and shall assure our hearts before Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For if our heart condemns us, **God is greater than our heart**, and knows all things.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That last sentence comes two verses later, and it is the letter's answer to a heart that will not settle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is writing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The disciple who leaned on His chest at the supper and stood at the cross, and who was given a mother from it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He saw the laying down."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he does with the word.** Refuses to let it float. John says *this is how we know what love is* and then points at one afternoon — and the very next sentence is about a coat."}]}, "1 John 3:2–3": {"title": "We are sons right now", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two tenses in one sentence, and the first one is present."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Beloved.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **agapetoi** — loved ones. John's habitual name for his readers; he uses it six times in this short letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We **are** sons of God **right now**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present indicative. Not becoming, not hoping to be, not on probation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before it is the reason: *behold what manner of love the Father has bestowed on us, that we should be called children of God!* — and John adds *and we are*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not settled"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What we are **going to be** has not come out into the open yet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **oupo ephanerothe** — it has not yet been manifested, made visible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The status is decided. The appearance is pending."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one thing known"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **we know this much**: when He does come out into the open, **we will be like Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb again — **phanerothe**, when He is manifested."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two manifestations in two lines, and they are the same event. His showing is our showing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mechanism"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We will be **looking straight at Him, at what He actually is**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *we shall see Him as He is* — **kathos estin**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seeing is the cause of the likeness, not the reward for it. Paul says it the same way: *we all, with unveiled face, beholding as in a mirror the glory of the Lord, are being transformed into the same image*. See the note on 2 Corinthians 3:16–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Looking is how the change happens, in both apostles."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hope"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **everyone with that hope set on Him** makes himself pure, **the way He is pure**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *purifies* is **hagnizei**, and the standard given is not a rule but a person: **kathos ekeinos hagnos estin** — as He is pure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The purity is measured against Him, and the one doing the purifying is the one who already knows he is a son."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sonship first, in verse 2. Purifying second, in verse 3, and only for those who have the hope."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John never runs it the other way round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other beholding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses came down from the mountain with a face he could not see himself, and had to be told about. *The skin of his face shone while he talked with Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been looking at something for forty days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever commits sin also commits lawlessness… and you know that **He was manifested to take away our sins**, and **in Him there is no sin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb a third time. Manifested — at the incarnation, to remove sin; and manifested again at the end, so we can be looked at Him and be like Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the letter opens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That which was from the beginning, which we have heard, which **we have seen with our eyes**, which we have looked upon, and our hands have handled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John begins the letter with what he saw, and gets to what we will see."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order in two verses.** Sons now — that part is finished. Like Him later, and the way it happens is that we will be looking at Him."}]}, "1 John 3:4–5": {"title": "Sin is not in Him", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two short sentences, and the second one is about somebody else entirely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The definition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Anyone who sins is **breaking the law**, because **sin is lawbreaking**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anomia** — lawlessness. Not the breach of a particular statute but the state of being without law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John defines the word and does not soften it. Sin is not a mistake, a weakness, or a shortfall in this sentence. It is lawlessness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not follow it with a warning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And you know that **He came out into the open to carry our sins away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ephanerothe** — He was manifested. The same verb John uses twice in the verses just before, about the future appearing. See the note on 1 John 3:2–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Manifested then, to take sins away. Manifested later, so we shall be like Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **carry our sins away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **are** — to lift, to bear off, to remove."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the Baptist's verb: *behold, the Lamb of God, who **takes away** the sin of the world*. See the note on John 1:29–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not covered. Lifted and carried off."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **sin is not in Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is present tense — **hamartia en auto ouk estin**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John states the definition of sin as lawlessness, then names the one person it does not apply to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three statements about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The New Testament makes the same point three times, and each writer chooses a different angle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John: *in Him there is no sin* — nothing inside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul: *Him who **knew no sin**, He made to be sin for us*. See the note on 2 Corinthians 5:20–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter: *He committed no sin, nor was deceit found in His mouth*. See the note on 1 Peter 2:21–22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in Him, nothing known by Him, nothing done by Him. Between them the three cover the inside, the experience, and the record."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews adds the fourth: *tempted as we are, yet without sin*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the passage argues"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 6 draws the conclusion: *whoever abides in Him does not sin*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The logic runs through the person, not through effort. The One who has no sin in Him took sin away, and the one who stays in Him is in a place where sin has no business."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse before all of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold what manner of love the Father has bestowed on us, that we should be called children of God! And that is what we are.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens with status and then discusses conduct. John never runs it the other way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says a few lines later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who sins is of the devil, for the devil has sinned from the beginning. **For this purpose the Son of God was manifested, that He might destroy the works of the devil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **luse** — loose, dissolve, undo."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third *manifested* in the chapter, and this one names the purpose in terms of demolition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The letter's stated aim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That you may know that you have eternal life.* See the note on 1 John 5:13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter about sin and lawlessness is in a letter written for assurance."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the sentence lands.** He defines sin as lawlessness — and the next thing he says is that it was carried off, by somebody who has none of it in Him."}]}, "1 John 4:10": {"title": "Which direction it started", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John defines love by pointing at an event rather than a feeling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Here is **what love is**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **en touto estin he agape** — in this is love."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A definition, and it is about to be given as a piece of history."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Love **did not start with us loving God**; it started with **God loving us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *not that we loved God, but that He loved us*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John removes one option before he states the other. The sentence is built to prevent a misreading."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He did about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **sending His Son** to be **the sacrifice that answered for our sins**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hilasmon** — the word for the thing that turns away wrath, or covers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word the Greek Old Testament uses for the mercy seat — the lid on the ark that the blood was sprinkled on, over the tablets of the law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John uses it twice, here and in chapter 2: *He Himself is the propitiation for our sins, and not for ours only but also for the whole world*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The love is not the feeling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the verse describes an emotion. The definition given is a sending and a sacrifice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does the same: *God **demonstrates** His own love toward us, in that while we were still sinners, Christ died for us*. See the note on Romans 5:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both apostles define the love by an action taken at a specific time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse before it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In this the love of God was manifested toward us, that **God has sent His only begotten Son into the world, that we might live through Him***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice in two verses, and the second is the sharper: not only sent, but sent *as* the propitiation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion John draws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Beloved, if God so loved us, **we also ought to love one another**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **opheilomen** — we are under obligation, we owe it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The debt is stated only after the direction has been established."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No one has seen God at any time. If we love one another, **God abides in us, and His love has been perfected in us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **teteleiotai** — has been brought to its goal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody sees God, and the visible form the love takes is people loving each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter says a few lines later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is no fear in love; but **perfect love casts out fear**, because fear involves torment. But he who fears has not been made perfect in love.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *torment* is **kolasin** — punishment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fear is treated as a symptom, and the cure named is not effort but the same love the chapter opened by defining."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We love Him because **He first loved us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **protos** — first, in order."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole chapter has one argument, and it is about sequence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is writing it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who identifies himself in his own Gospel as *the disciple whom Jesus loved*, and does so four or five times. See the note on John 13:21–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never once calls himself the one who loved Jesus."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is being defined.** Not a feeling and not a duty. *Not that we loved God, but that He loved us* — and the evidence given is a sending."}]}, "1 John 4:16": {"title": "\"We have put our weight on it\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs about the love of God, and the second one is what makes the verse usable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Known, and trusted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We have come to know the love God has for us, and we have put our weight on it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are perfect tense — arrived at, and standing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first is knowledge: having come to see it. The second is the ordinary word for believing or trusting, and the Illumination renders it physically, which is what trust means when it is real. Not *we agree with it*. We have leaned on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A person can know something is load-bearing and still stand somewhere else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Love is what God is\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "John says it twice in this chapter, and it is the only sentence of its kind in Scripture — no other quality is described this way. Not *God is loving*, which would be an attribute among others."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything else John says about God in this letter — light, truth, righteousness — is held together by it rather than competing with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Staying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever stays in love stays in God, and God stays in him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is **meno**, John's great word: abide, remain, stay put. It runs through his Gospel — abide in Me, and I in you — and all through this letter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not describe an achievement. It describes not leaving."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What it is for.** The next verse says the purpose: *so that we can stand bold on the day of judgment* — and then, *there is no fear in love; perfect love throws fear outside.* The knowing and the leaning are what put the fear out. See the note on 1 John 4:17."}]}, "1 John 4:17": {"title": "\"What He is, that is what we are, in this world\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the boldest sentences in the New Testament, and it is easy to read past because of how quietly John drops it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because what He is, that is what we are, **in this world**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not what we will be. Not what we are working toward. The tense is present and the location is named — here, now, in this world."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not say we are like Him in some respects. He says *as He is, so are we*. The comparison is unqualified."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is given as the reason for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that we can stand bold on the day of judgment.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is **parresia** — freedom of speech, the confidence of someone who has the right to be in the room. John uses it four times in this letter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the ground of that boldness is not the reader's record. It is that their standing and Christ's are the same standing. If He is accepted, so are they — because it is the same acceptance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the sentence starts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is how love comes to full growth with us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole thing is about love being brought to completion. And the very next verse says what that maturity does: *there is no fear in love; perfect love throws fear outside.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the sequence runs — love matured, boldness at judgment, fear thrown out. Not by working on the fear, but by knowing what you are in Christ."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why it is such a strong statement.** John has just spent the chapter saying God is love, that He sent His Son, and that we love because He first loved us. This verse is the conclusion of all of it: the love that came down did not stop at rescuing us. It made us, in this world, what He is."}]}, "1 John 4:18": {"title": "\"Love grown full drives fear out the door\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse about two things that cannot occupy the same space."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Love has **no fear** in it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *there is no fear in love*. Not that love overpowers fear — that fear is not in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mechanism"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Love **grown full drives fear out the door**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Teleia agape** — love brought to completion, matured, finished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is **ballo exo** — to throw outside. It is a physical, unceremonious word; the same one used of casting out demons."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fear is not persuaded to leave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What fear brings with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Fear brings **torment** with it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kolasis** — punishment, correction, penalty. It is the word used in Matthew 25 for *everlasting punishment*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the fear John has in view is not nervousness. It is the fear of being punished, and he says it carries its own punishment inside it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Anyone who is afraid **has not come to full growth in love**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *does not love enough*, and not *is a bad Christian*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is that he has not been perfected — completed — in love."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the whole letter has been clear about whose love that is: *we love Him **because He first loved us***, in the very next verse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The growing up is in the receiving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the verse before it says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Love has been perfected among us in this: that **we may have boldness in the day of judgement**; because **as He is, so are we in this world**.* See the note on 1 John 4:17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the context. Boldness on the day everybody is afraid of, and the reason given is a present-tense comparison: *as He is* — not as He was, and not as we will be."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say fear is a sin, or tell anybody to stop being afraid."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He describes it as an indicator — the way a thermometer reports rather than accuses — and points at the remedy, which is not an effort."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other fear in Scripture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The fear of the LORD is the beginning of wisdom.* Proverbs uses a different Hebrew idea — awe, reverence, the response to size."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John is talking about the fear that expects a blow."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the letter keeps saying about the order.** *We love Him because He first loved us.* Every command in 1 John about loving comes after a statement about being loved, and this verse names the growing-up as the thing that removes the fear."}]}, "1 John 4:19": {"title": "\"We love Him because He loved us first\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Six words in Greek, and the argument of the whole letter is in the last one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Protos** — first, prior, before. It is doing all the work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John is not saying God loved us more, or better. He is saying He loved us **earlier** — before there was anything to respond to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the order matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read backwards, the verse becomes an instruction: love God, and He will love you. That is the reverse of what John spends the chapter arguing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In this is love, not that we loved God, but that He loved us and sent His Son.* He says it four verses earlier in almost the same words, ruling out the wrong order before he states the right one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it makes love"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A response. Not something worked up, and not a duty discharged — the reaction to something that already happened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why John can say elsewhere that His commands are not burdensome. A command to love, given to someone who has been loved first, is not the same weight as a command given cold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter has just been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is no fear in love; perfect love throws fear outside.* And: *what He is, that is what we are, in this world.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This verse is the summary of both. The fear goes because the love was there before we arrived, and it did not wait to see how we would turn out."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What John does with it next.** He goes straight to the brother you can see: *if anyone says \"I love God\" and hates his brother, he is a liar.* The love that came first is expected to run through and land on somebody. See the note on 1 John 4:16."}]}, "1 John 4:4": {"title": "\"The One in you is bigger\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and every clause in it is a completed fact."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he is writing to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You belong to God, **little children**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's habitual address — **teknia**, small children. He uses it seven times in this short letter, and he was an old man when he wrote it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *belong to God* is *you are of God* — out of Him, sourced in Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has already happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **you have beaten them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Perfect tense: you have overcome, and stand having overcome."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *you will*, not *you can*. It is reported as done, and it is said to people John has just been warning about false prophets."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who \"them\" are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before: *many false prophets have gone out into the world* — and John gives a test, *every spirit that confesses that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh is of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the overcoming is of a specific thing: teaching that denies the incarnation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **the One in you is bigger than the one in the world**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a straightforward comparative — greater."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two beings are named and located: one **in you**, one **in the world**. The comparison is not between you and the world."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the believer's strength enters the sentence. The victory is credited to a difference in size between two others, and the believer's only contribution is being the place one of them is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter does with this elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is the victory that has overcome the world — **our faith**.* And: *who is he who overcomes the world, but he who believes that Jesus is the Son of God?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same perfect tense, the same completed overcoming, and the mechanism named as believing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten verses later: *there is **no fear in love**, but perfect love casts out fear, because fear involves torment.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *we love Him **because He first loved us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that says the One in you is greater also says the whole thing started with being loved first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The letter's habit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John states facts about his readers before he asks anything of them. *You are of God. You have overcome. You know the truth. Your sins are forgiven. You have an anointing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The commands come after the indicatives, every time."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who wrote it.** The disciple who leaned on His chest at supper, watched Him die, ran to the tomb, and lived long enough to be the last one left. *That which we have heard, which we have seen with our eyes, which we have looked upon, and **our hands have handled**.*"}]}, "1 John 4:8": {"title": "\"Love is what God is\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and the second half of it is unlike anything else said about God in Scripture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **love is what God is**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *ho theos agape estin* — God is love. No article on *love*: not *God is the love*, and not *God is loving*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a statement of what He consists of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence does not reverse. John is not saying that love is God, or that wherever there is affection there is deity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is saying that if you took away the loving there would be no God left to describe."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other two of the same shape"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John writes three sentences like this and they are the only ones of their kind:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*God is **light**, and in Him is no darkness at all.*", "*God is **spirit**, and those who worship Him must worship in spirit and truth.*", "*God is **love**.*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not attributes He has. Categories of what He is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The negative half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever does not love **has never known God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is aorist — *did not come to know*. Not *has stopped knowing*; never started."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not argue it. He states the test and moves on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he says it twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight verses later: *God is love, and he who abides in love abides in God, and God in him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it, explains it for eight verses, and says it again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he puts in between"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In this the love of God was manifested toward us, that God has **sent His only begotten Son** into the world, that we might live through Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In this is love, **not that we loved God, but that He loved us**, and sent His Son to be the propitiation for our sins.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John will not let the word stay abstract. Every time he uses it he attaches it to an event with a date."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And he insists on the direction: not our love reaching up, but His arriving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The definition he refuses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never defines love by feeling. The nearest thing to a definition in his letters is *by this we know love, because He laid down His life for us.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it means for fear"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is **no fear in love; but perfect love casts out fear**, because fear involves torment.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verses later, and it is the practical conclusion he draws from *God is love*. See the note on 1 John 4:18."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who wrote it.** The man who called himself *the disciple whom Jesus loved* — his own description of himself, five times, in his own Gospel. He is not writing a definition from the outside."}]}, "1 John 5:12": {"title": "Two sentences, no third option", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The shortest summary in the letter, and it is built as a pair."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If **the Son is yours, the life is yours**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ho echon ton huion echei ten zoen** — he who has the Son has the life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *will have*. Present tense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the definite article: **ten zoen**, the life. A specific one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If **the Son of God is not yours, then neither is the life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same construction, negated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John gives the full title in the second half — *the Son of God* — where the first half has *the Son*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not offered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any middle position."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole letter works this way. Light or darkness. Truth or a lie. Of God or of the world. Loving the brother or abiding in death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not deal in degrees, and this verse is the pattern of the book in one line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the verse before it says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And this is the testimony: that **God has given us eternal life, and this life is in His Son**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**In His Son** — that is where it is located, and the next verse is the arithmetic that follows from it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "If the life is in a person, then having the person and having the life are one transaction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Zoe** — John's word. The life itself, as opposed to **bios**, the span or the livelihood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Gospel uses it constantly: *in Him was life*, *I am the resurrection and the life*, *I have come that they may have life*, *the words that I speak to you are spirit, and they are life*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These things I have written to you who believe in the name of the Son of God, **that you may know that you have eternal life**.* See the note on 1 John 5:13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 12 is the statement; verse 13 says why he wrote it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The purpose is not to sort people into categories. It is so the people already in the first one should know it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The testimony being summarised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If we receive the witness of men, the witness of God is greater… **He who believes in the Son of God has the witness in himself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *he who does not believe God has made Him a liar, because he has not believed the testimony that God has given of His Son*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same shape appears"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who believes in the Son has everlasting life; and he who does not believe the Son shall not see life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John the Baptist, at the end of chapter 3 of the Gospel, and the construction is identical."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter says at the very end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And we know that the Son of God has come and has given us an understanding, that we may know Him who is true; and we are in Him who is true, in His Son Jesus Christ. **This is the true God and eternal life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last sentence of the letter before the closing line identifies the two as the same thing."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The shape of it.** One sentence and its negative, and nothing in between. If the person is yours, so is the life — because the life is where the verse before says it is."}]}, "1 John 5:13": {"title": "So that you can know", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John states, near the end, why he wrote the letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose statement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have written all this to you… **so that you can know**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hina eidete** — a purpose clause."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Oida**, not **ginosko**. The knowing that is settled rather than the knowing that is acquired by process."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To you **who trust the name of the Son of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to the uncertain, and not to the outsider. The letter is addressed to believers, and the stated aim is that they should know what they have."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody was telling them they might not. The letter has been fighting it for five chapters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are to know"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That **the life that never ends is yours**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **zoen echete aionion** — you *have* eternal life. Present tense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not will have. Not may have if you finish well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Zoe** is John's word — the life itself, as opposed to **bios**, the span or livelihood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse in front of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who has the Son has life; he who does not have the Son of God does not have life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two sentences with no third option, and the possession of the life is identical to the possession of the Son."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And so that you **go on trusting** the name of the Son of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a present subjunctive — continue believing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Assurance is given as the fuel for the continuing, not as a reward at the end of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parallel in the Gospel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These are written **that you may believe** that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and that believing you may have life in His name.* See the note on John 20:30–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Gospel is written that they may believe. The letter is written that those who believe may know."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now this is the **confidence** that we have in Him, that if we ask anything according to His will, He hears us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parresia** — freedom of speech, the boldness of somebody with the right to be there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Knowing comes first in verse 13, and the boldness in prayer is built on it in verse 14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three certainties John ends on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We know that whoever is born of God does not sin.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We know that we are of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We know that the Son of God has come, and has given us an understanding.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times, **oidamen** — we know. Plus the *know* of verse 13, four in the closing paragraphs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tests through the letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John has given them plenty: love for the brothers, keeping His commandments, confessing that Jesus has come in the flesh."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not writing to make them anxious about the tests. He says so here, and the sentence he chooses is *that you may know*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter opened with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That which was from the beginning, which we have heard, which we have seen with our eyes, which we have looked upon, and our hands have handled… **that your joy may be full**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two purpose statements bracket the letter, and both are about the reader's certainty."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he wrote it for.** Not so they would try harder. *That you may know that you have eternal life* — present tense, addressed to people who already believe."}]}, "1 John 5:4": {"title": "The thing that beat the world", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two short sentences, and the tense changes between them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everything born from God **gets the better of the world**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **nika ton kosmon** — overcomes the world. Present tense: goes on overcoming."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the subject is neuter — **pan to gegennemenon**, everything that has been born. Not *everyone*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John describes the birth itself as the thing that wins, rather than the person's performance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What *the world* means in John"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the planet, and not people in general."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All that is in the world — the lust of the flesh, the lust of the eyes, and the pride of life — is not of the Father but is of the world.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The system, running on those three engines."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the thing that got the better of the world is our trust**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **he nike he nikesasa** — the victory that has overcome. An aorist participle: a completed act, in the past."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tense shifts. The first sentence is ongoing; this one is finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is named"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not our effort, our holiness, or our record."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the next verse defines it further: *who is he who overcomes the world, but **he who believes that Jesus is the Son of God**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The victory is located in an object, not in a quantity. It is not strong faith that wins; it is faith in Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the word comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In the world you will have tribulation; but be of good cheer, **I have overcome the world**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last sentence of the upper-room discourse, in the same Gospel, from the same writer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek there is **nenikeka** — a perfect: I have overcome and it stands overcome."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said hours before the arrest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's letter uses the disciple's version of his Master's verb."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By this we know that we love the children of God, when we love God and keep His commandments. For this is the love of God, that we keep His commandments. **And His commandments are not burdensome**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **bareiai** — heavy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The overcoming sentence is the explanation of that clause. The commands are not heavy because of what the birth has already done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is He who came by water and blood — Jesus Christ; not only by water, but by water and blood. And it is the Spirit who bears witness, because the Spirit is truth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *he who has the Son has life; he who does not have the Son of God does not have life*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the letter's stated purpose: *that you may know that you have eternal life*. See the note on 1 John 5:13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the verb ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven times in Revelation, at the end of each letter to the churches:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To him who overcomes I will give to eat from the tree of life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who overcomes shall not be hurt by the second death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To him who overcomes I will give to eat of the hidden manna.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the last: *to him who overcomes I will grant to sit with Me on My throne, as I also overcame and sat down with My Father on His throne.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb He used in the upper room, promised to the reader."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other overcoming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they overcame him **by the blood of the Lamb and by the word of their testimony**.* See the note on Revelation 12:10–11."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The change of tense.** Born-of-God keeps on overcoming, present. And the victory itself is in the aorist — already done, and named as faith."}]}, "1 John 5:6": {"title": "By water and by blood", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, aimed at something specific that was being taught in Ephesus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is the One who came **by water and blood** — Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **di' hudatos kai haimatos**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The insistence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not by **water alone**: by water and by blood.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The emphatic repetition is the whole point of the verse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody was saying *water alone*, and John writes the sentence twice to close it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was being taught"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The early church recorded a teacher at Ephesus, Cerinthus, who held that the Christ descended on the man Jesus at the baptism and departed before the crucifixion — so that the divine one was not the one who died."}, {"k": "p", "t": "On that account, the Christ came *by water* and not by blood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's letters are full of denials aimed at that shape of teaching: *every spirit that confesses that Jesus Christ has come in the flesh is of God*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The water"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The baptism in the Jordan, where the voice said *this is My beloved Son*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And John alone records what came out of the side: *one of the soldiers pierced His side with a spear, and immediately **blood and water** came out*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he who has seen has testified, and his testimony is true.* See the note on John 19:31–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He saw both, in that order, and writes about both here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The witness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the Spirit is the One who stands witness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **to marturoun** — the one bearing witness. A participle, present tense: continuing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the Spirit can"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **the Spirit is truth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *speaks the truth*. Is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same construction Jesus used of Himself: *I am the way, the truth, and the life*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For there are three that bear witness: **the Spirit, the water, and the blood; and these three agree as one**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eis to hen eisin** — they are unto the one thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three witnesses, which is what the law required: *by the mouth of two or three witnesses the matter shall be established*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument from testimony"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If we receive the witness of men, **the witness of God is greater**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who believes in the Son of God **has the witness in himself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John moves from external testimony to internal, and does not present them as alternatives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the witness says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And this is the testimony: that **God has given us eternal life, and this life is in His Son**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who has the Son has life; **he who does not have the Son of God does not have life**.* See the note on 1 John 5:11–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the letter was written"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These things I have written to you who believe in the name of the Son of God, **that you may know that you have eternal life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hina eidete** — that you may know."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's Gospel was written *that you may believe*. The letter was written to people who already do, so that they may know they have it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Spirit's other testimony"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit Himself bears witness with our spirit that **we are children of God**.* See the note on Romans 8:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same function, in Paul's vocabulary."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The repetition.** *Not by water alone: by water and by blood.* John says it twice in one verse because a teacher in his own city was saying the first half and stopping."}]}, "1 Kings 1:5–10": {"title": "\"His father had never once crossed him\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A prince crowns himself, and the narrator stops mid-story to explain why."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His father had **never once crossed him**, never asked, \"**Why do you do this?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *never grieved him at any time by saying*. Not once, in his whole life, had David asked him a question about his conduct."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer puts it in as the explanation. It is not an aside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it produced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Adonijah began to **strut and swell** with ambition. \"**I will be king**,\" he told himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not consult David, who is still alive. He does not consult the prophet. He says it to himself and then acts on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He got himself **chariots and horsemen, and fifty men to run ahead of him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The trappings first. He arranges the appearance of a king before there is anything behind it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he learned it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was a handsome man, **born just after Absalom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Absalom did exactly the same thing — *Absalom prepared chariots and horses, and fifty men to run before him* — and it ended with him hanging in an oak tree."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David watched one son do it and did not ask the next one a single question."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Absalom's rebellion also had a history. Amnon violated Tamar, and *when King David heard of all these things, he was very angry* — and did nothing. Two years later Absalom killed Amnon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three sons, one pattern, and the same silence behind each of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who joined him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joab, the commander of the army, and **Abiathar the priest***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The army and the priesthood. Adonijah had the two institutions that mattered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who did not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Zadok the priest, Benaiah, **Nathan the prophet**, Shimei, Rei, and David's mighty men.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nathan is the man who came to David after Bathsheba with *you are the man.* He is on the other side, and it is Nathan who goes to Bathsheba and gets Solomon crowned before the day is out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The guest list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He invited all his royal brothers and all the king's men of Judah. **But he did not invite Nathan the prophet, or Benaiah, or the mighty men, or his brother Solomon.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He knew exactly who would say no. The omissions are a complete list of everyone with the standing to stop him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the passage suggests David should have been harsh with him. David was never harsh with anybody in his family, and that is the problem the verse names."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never asked. *Why do you do this?* — one question, and it was never put."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How it ends.** Solomon lets him live: *if he proves himself a worthy man, not one hair of his shall fall to the earth.* Adonijah then asks for Abishag, the girl who had nursed David — a claim on the throne by another route — and Solomon has him executed. The strutting outlives the reprieve."}]}, "1 Kings 10:23–25": {"title": "Everyone came seeking his face", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The summary of Solomon's reign at its height, and the reason people came is stated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So King Solomon **grew greater than every king on earth**, both in **riches and in wisdom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *and King Solomon was greater than all the kings of the earth*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Riches and wisdom, in that order here, and in the opposite order in the request that produced them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Give to Your servant **an understanding heart to judge Your people**, that I may discern between good and evil.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lev shomea** — a hearing heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer: *because you have asked this thing, and have not asked long life for yourself, nor riches for yourself… **behold, I have also given you what you have not asked**: both riches and honour*. See the note on 1 Kings 3:5–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The riches arrived because they were not the request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The traffic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the whole world wanted an audience with him** — everyone came **seeking Solomon's face**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *all the earth sought the face of Solomon*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Seeking the face** is the idiom used of seeking God: *seek My face*, and *seek the LORD and His strength; seek His face evermore*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Longing **to hear the wisdom God had put in his heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the buildings, the army, or the gold."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the phrasing is careful: *which God had put in his heart*. The narrator does not let the wisdom belong to the man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gifts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every one of them brought his gift — **objects of silver and gold, robes, weapons, spices, horses, mules**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six categories, and the list is the standing inventory of international diplomacy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **steady stream of tribute, year in and year out**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the queen of Sheba had said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It was a true report which I heard in my own land… **however I did not believe the words until I came and saw with my own eyes; and indeed the half was not told me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *happy are your men and happy are these your servants, who stand continually before you and hear your wisdom*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does with her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The queen of the South will rise up in the judgment with this generation** and condemn it, for she came from the ends of the earth to hear the wisdom of Solomon; and indeed **a greater than Solomon is here**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She travelled from the far end of the known world on a rumour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens after this chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 11 opens: *but King Solomon loved many foreign women*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven hundred wives, three hundred concubines, and *his wives turned away his heart*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the LORD became angry with Solomon, because his heart had turned from the LORD God of Israel, **who had appeared to him twice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who asked for a hearing heart ends the book with a turned one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he wrote about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Trust in the LORD with all your heart, and **lean not on your own understanding**.* See the note on Proverbs 3:5–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *he who trusts in his own heart is a fool*. See the note on Proverbs 28:21–28."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chapter's own note"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in the list — silver, gold, horses, wives — is on Deuteronomy's list of what a king must not accumulate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But he shall not multiply horses for himself… neither shall he multiply wives for himself… nor shall he greatly multiply silver and gold for himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator sets the splendour down without comment and lets the next chapter do the work."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they came for.** Not the gold. *To hear the wisdom God had put in his heart* — and the narrator will not let the wisdom belong to him even at the height of it."}]}, "1 Kings 11:1–3": {"title": "Where the light dims", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The turn in the story, and the translation marks it in the first clause."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hinge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But here is where **the light dims**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew opens with a plain **vehammelek Shelomoh ahav** — and King Solomon loved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 10 ended with silver as common as stones in Jerusalem and the queen of Sheba saying *the half was not told me*. See the note on 1 Kings 10:23–25."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 11 begins here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*King Solomon **loved** many foreign women.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ahav** — the ordinary word for love."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator does not use a euphemism, and does not use a word for lust."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not only **Pharaoh's daughter**, but **Moabite** women, **Ammonites**, **Edomites**, **Sidonians**, **Hittites**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six nations. Every one of them a treaty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Royal marriage in the ancient world was diplomacy. Each wife came with an alliance and a household shrine."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Women from **the very nations the LORD had warned Israel about**, saying, \"**Do not go among them, and do not let them come among you, because they will surely turn your heart toward their gods**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 7, and the reason is stated in the law itself: *for they will turn your sons away from following Me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prohibition was never about ethnicity. The stated ground is the gods."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clinging"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Solomon **held fast to these women; he loved them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **davaq** — clung, was joined to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word from Genesis 2: *a man shall leave his father and mother and **be joined to his wife***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb for covenant loyalty, and it is used here of the wrong attachment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The numbers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seven hundred wives, princesses, and three hundred concubines.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A thousand women."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The law he broke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Neither shall he **multiply wives for himself**, lest his heart turn away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 17, the law of the king — with the reason attached, in the same words the narrator uses. See the note on Deuteronomy 17:14–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Horses, wives, silver and gold. Solomon has now done all three."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **his wives turned his heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hittu et-libbo** — inclined his heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The exact outcome the law predicted, in the law's own vocabulary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heart again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book has tracked one word through the whole reign."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give Your servant **a hearing heart**.* — the request."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **heart** as wide as the sand on the seashore.* — the gift. See the note on 1 Kings 4:26–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His wives turned **his heart**.* — the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it was so, when Solomon was old, that his wives turned his heart after other gods; and **his heart was not loyal to the LORD his God, as was the heart of his father David**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Solomon built a high place for Chemosh… and for Molech.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On the Mount of Olives, opposite Jerusalem, and they were still standing three centuries later until Josiah tore them down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the LORD became angry with Solomon, because his heart had turned from the LORD God of Israel, **who had appeared to him twice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator counts the appearances. See the note on 1 Kings 3:15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is left"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**However I will not tear away the whole kingdom; I will give one tribe to your son for the sake of My servant David**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Nevertheless, the lamp for David still burned, exactly as God had said.* See the note on 1 Kings 11:41–43."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb.** **Davaq** — clung, was joined to. The word Genesis uses of a man and his wife and the prophets use of covenant loyalty, applied here to a thousand women and their gods."}]}, "1 Kings 11:41–43": {"title": "A lamp for David", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The obituary of the wisest king, and the last clause is about somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reference"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As for everything else in Solomon's story — **all he did, and all his wisdom** — it is written down in **the book of the acts of Solomon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A source the writer of Kings had and we do not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book cites lost records repeatedly: the book of Jasher, the chronicles of the kings of Israel, the chronicles of the kings of Judah."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The length"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He reigned in Jerusalem **over all Israel for forty years**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same as David. The same as Saul."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Over all Israel** — which will not be true of his son for a single week."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The death"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Solomon **lay down to rest with his ancestors**, and he was **buried in the city of David his father**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyishkav im-avotav** — and he slept with his fathers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The standard formula, used of kings who died peacefully."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anything about his end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ecclesiastes and Proverbs are attributed to him, and the narrative of Kings gives no deathbed scene, no repentance, and no last word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David's death has a chapter of instructions. Solomon's has one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The successor"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **Rehoboam his son reigned in his place**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His mother was Naamah the Ammonitess — one of the foreign wives named in chapter 11. See the note on 1 Kings 11:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next king is the son of the problem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Rehoboam does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Goes to Shechem, is asked to lighten the yoke, consults the old men and then the young men, and answers *my father chastised you with whips, but I will chastise you with scourges*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Israel departed to their tents.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The kingdom stood **cracked and waiting** — yet **the lamp for David still burned**, exactly as God had said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew idiom is **nir leDavid** — a lamp for David."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the lamp comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**However I will not tear away the whole kingdom**; I will give one tribe to your son **for the sake of My servant David**, and for the sake of Jerusalem which I have chosen.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given is not Solomon, or Rehoboam. It is a man who has been dead forty years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise behind it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And your house and your kingdom shall be established forever before you. **Your throne shall be established forever**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nathan to David, in 2 Samuel 7. See the note on 2 Samuel 7:12–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But My mercy shall not depart from him, as I took it from Saul.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the phrase recurs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Nevertheless for David's sake the LORD his God gave him **a lamp in Jerusalem**, by setting up his son after him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet the LORD would not destroy Judah, for the sake of His servant David, as He promised him to give **a lamp to him and his sons forever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times in Kings, always at a low point, always naming David."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the line runs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The book of the genealogy of Jesus Christ, **the Son of David**, the Son of Abraham.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Solomon begot Rehoboam, Rehoboam begot Abijah…* See the note on Matthew 1:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's list runs straight through Rehoboam. The lamp is the reason there is a genealogy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus says about Solomon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Consider the lilies of the field, how they grow… **even Solomon in all his glory was not arrayed like one of these**.\"* See the note on Matthew 6:25–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And indeed **a greater than Solomon is here**.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last clause.** The kingdom cracks, and the reason it does not go out is a promise made to the previous king, named by name, forty years after he died."}]}, "1 Kings 12:6–11": {"title": "Two sets of advisers", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A new king asks the same question twice and gets two answers. The kingdom splits over which one he takes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you will be a servant to them today — if you answer them kindly — they will be your servants forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old men who had stood before Solomon give him a piece of counsel that is almost a paradox: serve them today and they are yours for life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It costs one conversation. The return they promise is permanent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My little finger is thicker than my father's waist… My father disciplined you with whips? I'll use whips with iron barbs.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men he grew up with tell him to escalate. Their advice is not strategy; it is posture — a way of appearing strong to people who had asked for relief."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read what the people actually wanted. Solomon's building programme had been paid for in forced labour, and the request was *lighten the load your father put on us, and we will serve you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were not rebelling. They were offering loyalty in exchange for relief. Both sets of advisers understood that; they simply disagreed about whether to take the offer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rehoboam takes the harsher counsel and ten tribes walk away that day. The kingdom never reunites. Every king after this, north and south, lives inside the consequences of one afternoon's advice."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The detail about who he asked.** He went to the old men first — which was right — and then went and asked again. Getting counsel and taking counsel are two different acts, and a man who keeps asking until he hears what he wanted is not being advised. See the note on Proverbs 11:8–14 on many counsellors, and what the surrounding verses assume about their quality."}]}, "1 Kings 14:25–28": {"title": "Bronze shields for gold", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Five years after Solomon dies, everything he made in gold walks out of the country."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The date"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In **the fifth year** of King Rehoboam.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Solomon reigned forty years. His son loses the treasury in five."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Shishak took"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **stripped the treasures** from the temple of the LORD and from the royal palace, taking **everything** — even **all the gold shields** Solomon had made.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter before records what those shields were: two hundred large shields of beaten gold, six hundred shekels each, and three hundred smaller ones — hung in the House of the Forest of Lebanon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were ceremonial. Nobody fought with them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The replacement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So King Rehoboam **made bronze shields to replace them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same number, same shape, same purpose, same place in the procession."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bronze looks like gold at a distance, especially polished, especially in a corridor."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail that gives it away"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whenever the king went to the temple of the LORD, **the guards carried the shields, and afterward they returned them to the guardroom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gold ones hung in the House of the Forest. Nobody had to lock those away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bronze ones go back into a locked room after every use."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You do not put a guard on a thing that is worth what it appears to be worth. The security arrangements are the confession."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter says it: *Judah did evil in the sight of the LORD… they also built for themselves high places, sacred pillars, and wooden images.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And 2 Chronicles adds the reason Shishak got as far as he did: *they had transgressed against the LORD* — and, when the king and princes humbled themselves, *they shall be his servants, that they may distinguish My service from the service of the kingdoms of the nations.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other shield in Scripture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul's, on Gilboa: *the shield of Saul, **no longer anointed with oil**.* See the note on 2 Samuel 1:17–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A shield had to be oiled to keep the leather supple and turn a blade. An unoiled shield is a shield in name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the one Paul names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Above all of it, **take up the shield, which is faith**, and with it you will be able to put out all the burning arrows of the evil one.* See the note on Ephesians 6:14–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's is the full-body **thureos**, the door-shaped shield — and it is the only piece of the armour that is described as putting fires out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was never replaced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gold did not come back. Every later reference in Kings and Chronicles to the temple treasury is to what somebody added, or what somebody else carried off."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Solomon had said would happen.** *If you or your sons at all turn from following Me… then I will cut off Israel from the land which I have given them.* God said it to him at the dedication, and the reign after his is where the gold starts moving."}]}, "1 Kings 17:1": {"title": "\"The One I stand before\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Elijah arrives in the narrative with no introduction — no father named, no calling scene, no commission recorded. One sentence and he is in front of the king."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As surely as the LORD lives — the God of Israel, **the One I stand before and serve**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is his whole credential. Not *the prophet of Israel*, not a title or an office. A description of where he stands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew *to stand before* is the language of a servant attending a king in his throne room — the phrase used of courtiers waiting on Solomon. Elijah is saying he is in attendance somewhere else, and speaking from there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about the weather"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There will be no dew and no rain in these years, not a drop, **until I say the word**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things a farming nation could not live without, and the shutting of both is put on his word. Baal was the storm god — the one whose whole portfolio was rain and fertility."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the drought is not a general judgment. It is aimed precisely at the thing Baal was supposed to supply, in a country that had gone over to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the authority came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James is the one who tells us what happened before the sentence: *he prayed hard that the rain would stop, and it stopped.* The declaration in front of Ahab is the outcome of praying, not a substitute for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He prayed it first, then announced it, and then lived on a brook and ate what ravens brought him for the duration."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"Elijah\" means \"my God is the LORD.\"** In a kingdom that had spent a generation deciding otherwise, the man's own name is the argument. And on Carmel the nation shouts it back — *the LORD, He is God* — without necessarily noticing whose name they are saying."}]}, "1 Kings 17:2–7": {"title": "The brook dried up", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He is fed twice a day by birds for the better part of a year, and then the water stops."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Leave here. Turn east** and go **hide yourself** by the Kerith Ravine.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three commands, and the third is the odd one. Elijah has just walked into Ahab's throne room and shut the sky. The next thing he is told is to disappear."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *Kerith* comes from a root meaning *to cut off*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two provisions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You'll **drink from the brook**, and **I've told the ravens** to bring you food there.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Water from the ground and food from the air."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ravens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **orev**. Ravens are on the unclean list in Leviticus, and they are birds that scavenge and do not share — Job asks *who provides food for the raven?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are also the birds that feed their own young badly. The psalm says God *gives to the beast his food, and to the young ravens which cry.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "These ones carry food past their own nests to a man in a ravine."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the meat came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bread and meat, twice a day, in a famine. Nobody in Israel was eating meat twice a day that year."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text does not say where it came from and Elijah does not ask."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The obedience"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Elijah **did exactly what the LORD said**. He went and settled by the Kerith Ravine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he went and did according to the word of the LORD*. It is the same formula used of him at Zarephath and on Carmel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rhythm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bread and meat **in the morning**, bread and meat **again in the evening**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice daily, at the hours of the morning and evening sacrifice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Enough for the day and no more. The same arrangement as manna, which could not be stored."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **after a while the brook dried up**, because no rain had fallen on the land.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He watched it go down. A brook does not stop in a morning — it narrows, slows, becomes pools, then mud, then stones."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it dried"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because no rain had fallen on the land.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The drought was the one Elijah had called for. He prayed it and then drank from a stream that it emptied."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What did not happen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No new instruction while the water was falling. The word comes in the next verse, after the brook is gone: *arise, go to Zarephath.* See the note on 1 Kings 17:8–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next place is a Gentile town in Jezebel's home territory, and the next provider is a widow with a handful of flour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ravens, then a widow, then the whole nation on Carmel. Each provision is stranger than the last and each one ends before the next is named."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he was told to do at the brook.** Hide. Not preach, not organise, not intercede. The most public prophet in the Old Testament spends the first stretch of his ministry sitting by a stream watching birds arrive."}]}, "1 Kings 17:8–16": {"title": "\"Make a little loaf for me first\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A starving widow with one meal left is asked to give it away before she eats it, and what happens next is one of the plainest pictures of provision in the Bible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A handful of flour in a jar and a little oil in a jug.* She is gathering sticks to bake it, and she says out loud what the plan is after that: *we'll eat it, and then we'll die.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is not being dramatic. That is arithmetic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she is asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Make a little loaf for me **first**, and then make something for yourself and your son.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is doing all the work. Not instead — first. The prophet is not asking her to go without; he is asking her to change the order."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the request comes with *don't be afraid* in front of it, and a promise behind it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The jar of flour will not run out, and the jug of oil will not run dry, until the day the LORD sends rain.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a barn filled overnight. A jar that does not empty. The supply is daily, and it lasts as long as the drought does — provision measured to the length of the need."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So she went and did what Elijah said.* No argument recorded. A foreign widow in Sidon — Jezebel's own country, outside the covenant — takes an Israelite prophet's word and bakes the last of her flour for him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there was food every day.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who Jesus mentions.** Preaching in Nazareth, He says there were many widows in Israel in Elijah's day, and the prophet was sent to none of them — only to this one, in Sidon. It is one of the sentences that made His hometown want to throw Him off a cliff. See the note on Luke 4:16–21."}]}, "1 Kings 18:1–6": {"title": "Obadiah in the palace", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The word comes after three years, and the chapter pauses to tell you who else was in Israel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wait"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A long time passed — **into the third year of the drought**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three years by the brook and at Zarephath. James gives the figure as three years and six months, and Jesus uses the same number in Nazareth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go, show yourself to Ahab**. I'm about to send rain on the land.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Show yourself — the opposite of the command that opened chapter 17, which was *hide yourself*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the rain is announced before the contest on Carmel has happened. God tells Elijah the outcome first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The severity"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By now **the famine in Samaria was severe**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samaria is the capital. The famine has reached the palace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Ahab's man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ahab had called for **Obadiah, the man in charge of his palace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew title is *over the house* — the highest office under the king, the royal steward."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name means **servant of the LORD**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parenthesis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*(Obadiah was a man who **feared the LORD deeply**. When Queen Jezebel had been **killing off the prophets of the LORD**, Obadiah had **taken a hundred of them and hidden them in two caves, fifty in each**, and **kept them supplied with bread and water**.)*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The historian stops the narrative to put this on record."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundred men, in two caves, fed through a famine, by the chief of staff of the household that was hunting them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The risk"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jezebel was in the same palace. Obadiah was requisitioning bread and water during a drought, for men who were officially dead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elijah did not know"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three chapters later, Elijah says twice that he is the only one left. See the note on 1 Kings 19:9–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundred prophets were alive in two caves, kept there by a man in Ahab's own house, and the historian has already told the reader."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The errand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let's **go through the land, to every spring and every valley**. Maybe we'll find enough grass to **keep the horses and mules alive** so we don't lose all the animals.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the king's priority in the third year of a national famine. The horses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahab is out looking for grass. Elijah is on the road to find him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The division"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **split the land between them** to search it — Ahab going one way, Obadiah another.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is how Obadiah, not Ahab, is the one who meets Elijah — and the meeting terrifies him. He is convinced the Spirit will carry Elijah off and Ahab will kill him for a false report."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I your servant have feared the LORD from my youth. Was it not reported to my lord what I did… how I hid a hundred men of the LORD's prophets?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the only person in the chapter who has to say his own résumé out loud."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Elijah's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As the LORD of hosts lives, before whom I stand, **I will surely present myself to him today**.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter puts side by side.** A king searching for grass to keep his horses, and his steward keeping a hundred prophets alive in caves. The famine reached both of them and they spent it differently."}]}, "1 Kings 18:17–24": {"title": "\"How long will you go limping between two opinions?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three and a half years of drought end with a confrontation, and it opens with an accusation and a correction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who troubled Israel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So it's you — the man who's brought all this trouble on Israel.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I haven't troubled Israel. You have.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahab blames the prophet who announced the drought. Elijah puts it back on the man whose house abandoned the commands and went after the Baals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word Ahab uses is a strong one — the same root used of Achan, whose theft brought disaster on the whole camp. Elijah does not soften it; he redirects it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question to the people"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How long will you go limping between two opinions?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb is the one used a few verses later of the prophets of Baal **dancing** around their altar. Israel is doing the same hop from foot to foot, and the word is chosen deliberately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had not rejected the LORD outright. They were keeping both, and Elijah's charge is that this is not a position at all — it is a limp."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people said nothing — not a word.* Nobody answers him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The terms of the test"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two bulls, two altars, no fire lit by anyone. *The God who answers by fire — He is God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a fair test and the crowd says so. Baal was the god of storm and lightning; fire from the sky was precisely his advertised speciality. Elijah lets them choose their bull first and gives them the whole morning."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"I am the only prophet of the LORD left.\"** It is how he sees it, and it is not quite true — Obadiah has a hundred hidden in caves, and the same chapter has just said so. God corrects the same thought a chapter later with the seven thousand who never bowed. Elijah's courage is real, and so is his sense of being alone in it."}]}, "1 Kings 18:25–29": {"title": "\"No voice. No answer.\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Half a day of noise, and the historian keeps returning to the same three words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You go first, since there are so many of you**. Choose your bull and prepare it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred and fifty of them against one. Elijah gives them first pick, the first turn, and the whole morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then call on your god — **but light no fire**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the entire rule of the contest, and both sides are bound by it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They called on Baal **from morning until noon**. \"**O Baal, answer us!**\" they shouted.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Baal was the storm god — the one who was supposed to bring rain and lightning. Fire from the sky was precisely his department, and Israel had been in drought for three years under his management."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **there was no voice. No one answered**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dancing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **danced around the altar** they had made.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a limping, hobbling dance — the same verb Elijah used on the crowd earlier in the chapter: *how long will you **falter** between two opinions?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had accused Israel of limping between two positions, and now the prophets of Baal limp around their altar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mockery"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At noon Elijah began to mock them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four suggestions, and each one is worse for Baal than the last:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*\"**Surely he's a god!**\"*", "*\"Maybe he's **deep in thought**, or **busy**, or **off on a journey**.\"*", "*\"Maybe he's **asleep** and needs to be woken up.\"*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of *busy* is a euphemism — he has withdrawn, he is occupied privately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elijah is not arguing theology. He is asking practical questions about where the god has got to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The escalation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they **shouted louder** and, in their custom, **cut themselves with swords and spears until the blood ran**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Self-laceration was standard Canaanite practice and the law forbade it: *you shall not make any cuttings in your flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "More volume, then more blood. When nothing answers, the only thing left is to raise the cost."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The afternoon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Noon passed, and they **raved on into the afternoon**, all the way to **the time of the evening offering**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six more hours. And the hour it ends at is the hour of the sacrifice in Jerusalem — the hour Elijah then steps forward and prays. See the note on 1 Kings 18:30–39."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **there was nothing. No voice. No answer. No one paying attention**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The historian says it twice in five verses, and the second time he adds a third clause."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *no one paying attention* is *none regarded* — nobody there to notice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it took to answer Elijah"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One prayer, sixty-three words in the Hebrew, and no shouting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And before it he drenched the altar with twelve barrels of water in the third year of a drought."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two halves of the day measure.** From morning to evening on one side, and the time it takes to say one prayer on the other. The historian gives four hundred and fifty men five verses and Elijah's request three."}]}, "1 Kings 18:30–39": {"title": "Twelve stones, four jars three times, and one prayer", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The contest on Carmel, and every detail Elijah arranges is aimed at leaving no other explanation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The twelve stones"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took twelve stones, one for each of the tribes descended from Jacob — the man to whom the word of the LORD had come, \"Israel shall be your name.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The northern kingdom had been split from Judah for generations. Elijah rebuilds the altar as though the nation were whole, and the writer stops the narrative to mention the renaming at the Jabbok."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not addressing ten tribes. He is addressing Israel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The water"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four jars, poured over the offering and the wood. Then *do it again*. Then *a third time*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve jars in total, in the middle of a drought, on a mountain — water that must have been carried up. It fills the trench."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every drop is there to remove an excuse. No hidden coals, no dry tinder, no sleight of hand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "After the prophets of Baal had spent a day shouting, dancing and cutting themselves, Elijah prays about sixty words:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "*Let it be known today that You are God in Israel, that I am Your servant, and that I have done all these things at Your word. Answer me, LORD, answer me, so these people will know that You, O LORD, are God — and that You are turning their hearts back to You.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three requests, and all three are about **them**: that they would know who God is, and that their hearts are being turned back. Nothing is asked for the prophet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it came to pass, **at the time of the offering of the evening sacrifice**, that Elijah the prophet came near.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three in the afternoon — the hour the lamb was killed in Jerusalem, in a kingdom that had not sent anyone to Jerusalem in generations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophets of Baal had been shouting since morning and finished at exactly that hour. See the note on 1 Kings 18:25–29."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The altar he repaired"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He repaired **the altar of the LORD that was broken down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There had been one on that mountain. Somebody had pulled it down, and Elijah does not build a new one — he puts the old one back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the fire of the LORD fell. It consumed the offering, the wood, the stones, the very dust — and licked up all the water in the trench.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not just the sacrifice. The stones and the dust and the water — the fire takes everything, including the things fire does not normally take."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the people say.** *The LORD — He is God! The LORD — He is God!* In Hebrew that is *YHWH hu ha-Elohim*, twice. And it is the meaning of the prophet's own name — Eliyahu, *my God is the LORD*. The nation ends the day shouting his name back at him without knowing it."}]}, "1 Kings 18:41–46": {"title": "\"I hear the sound of a heavy rain\" — before there is a cloud", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Elijah announces rain to the king before anything has changed in the sky, and then goes up the mountain to pray it in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go on up — eat and drink, for **I hear the sound of a heavy rain coming**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no rain. There has been none for three and a half years, and the sky is clear. He says he hears it anyway, tells the king to go and eat, and only then climbs Carmel to pray."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The announcement comes before the answer, and the praying comes after the announcement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The posture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He bent down to the ground and put his face between his knees.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a dignified stance. The man who had just called fire out of heaven in front of a nation folds himself onto the ground and stays there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Seven times"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go look toward the sea\"… \"There's nothing\"… Seven times Elijah said, \"Go back and look again.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six times the report comes back empty. He does not adjust the announcement or conclude he misheard. He sends the servant again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what finally arrives is *a cloud as small as a man's hand* — the least impressive possible confirmation, and enough. He immediately sends word to Ahab to get down the mountain before the rain stops him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened to him afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The power of the LORD came on Elijah — he tucked his cloak into his belt and ran ahead of Ahab's chariot all the way to Jezreel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roughly seventeen miles, on foot, ahead of a horse-drawn chariot, by a man who had been awake since before dawn and had fasted through a contest and a slaughter. The chapter records it in one sentence."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he was praying for.** God had already told him, at the start of the chapter, *I will send rain on the land.* The promise was given first; Elijah announced it, then prayed it down, then sent seven times for the evidence. The praying was not to persuade God of something. It was to bring in what had already been said."}]}, "1 Kings 19:1–8": {"title": "Under the broom tree", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The day after the greatest day of his life, he asks to die — and what he is given is sleep and a hot meal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The report"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ahab told Jezebel **everything Elijah had done**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fire, the water, the four hundred and fifty, the rain. Ahab saw all of it and reports it to his wife as news."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The threat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**May the gods deal with me, and worse, if by this time tomorrow I haven't made your life like the life of one of them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A messenger, not an arrest party. She had the power to send soldiers and sent words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And she gives him a day's notice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had just done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Outrun a chariot from Carmel to Jezreel with the hand of the LORD on him — about seventeen miles, on foot, after a full day on the mountain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Elijah **was afraid, and he ran for his life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew can be read *he saw* or *he was afraid*; the older versions have *when he saw*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who stood in front of a nation and four hundred and fifty prophets runs from one message."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How far"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He came to **Beersheba in Judah**, **left his servant there**, and went **a day's journey into the wilderness alone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Beersheba is at the far southern end of Judah — about a hundred miles, and out of Jezebel's jurisdiction entirely. He was safe when he got there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he leaves the last person with him and walks another day into the desert."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tree"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He came to **a solitary broom tree**, sat down under it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **rothem** — a desert shrub with thin foliage. It gives some shade and not much."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only shelter for miles and it is barely shelter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **prayed that he might die**. \"**I've had enough, LORD**,\" he said. \"**Take my life. I'm no better than my ancestors**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *it is enough now*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks God to kill him, a day's walk from a woman who had offered to do it for free."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I'm no better than my ancestors* — his complaint is that he has not turned out to be more than the men before him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a rebuke, not a correction, not a reminder of yesterday."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he **lay down under the tree and fell asleep**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first touch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And suddenly **an angel touched him** and said, \"**Get up and eat**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He looked, and there by his head was **a warm loaf of bread, baked over hot coals, and a jug of water**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bread and water at his head while he slept. The same two things Obadiah carried to a hundred prophets in caves. See the note on 1 Kings 18:1–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Baked on coals, so somebody had made a fire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He ate and drank and **lay back down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes back to sleep, and nothing is said about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second touch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The angel of the LORD came **a second time**, touched him, and said, \"**Get up and eat. The journey is too much for you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the way is too great for you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given for the second meal is the distance ahead, which Elijah has not been told about yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Strengthened by that food, he traveled **forty days and forty nights** until he reached **Horeb, the mountain of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty days on two meals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Horeb is Sinai — where Moses saw the bush, and where the law was given. Elijah walks to the place the covenant started."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of what he was given.** Sleep, food, sleep, food. Then, and only then, a journey and a conversation. Nothing was said to him about Jezebel, Carmel, or the prayer under the tree."}]}, "1 Kings 19:9–18": {"title": "A low, gentle whisper", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The same question is asked twice, the same answer is given twice, and in between the mountain comes apart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What are you doing here, Elijah?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an accusation and not a summons. A question, and it is the first thing said to him after forty days of walking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have been very zealous** for the LORD God Almighty. The Israelites have **abandoned Your covenant, torn down Your altars, and killed Your prophets** with the sword. **I'm the only one left** — and now they're trying to kill me too.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four complaints and a conclusion. Every fact in it is true except the last one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go out and stand on the mountain** in the presence of the LORD, for **the LORD is about to pass by**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same mountain, and nearly the same words, as Moses in the cleft of the rock: *while My glory passes by, I will put you in the cleft of the rock.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A great and powerful **wind** tore through the mountains and shattered the rocks — **but the LORD was not in the wind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After the wind came an **earthquake** — **but the LORD was not in the earthquake**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After the earthquake came a **fire** — **but the LORD was not in the fire**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times the clause repeats."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of them is how God had shown up before. Sinai had all three when the law was given, in this exact place. Elijah had watched fire fall on Carmel eight verses ago."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is shown the whole repertoire and told God is not in any of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fourth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And after the fire came **a sound. A low, gentle whisper**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **qol demamah daqqah** — a voice of thin silence. **Daq** is what is used of something ground fine, and **demamah** is stillness, hush."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Elijah heard it, he **pulled his cloak over his face**, went out, and stood at the mouth of the cave.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wind, the earthquake and the fire did not move him out of the cave. The whisper did — and he covered his face for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses hid his face at the bush."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then a voice came to him: \"**What are you doing here, Elijah?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Identical. Nothing has been explained."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Word for word the same complaint, minus one clause."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not talked out of it. He says it twice and God lets him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is given instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go back the way you came**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three anointings: **Hazael** over Aram, **Jehu** over Israel, and **Elisha** to take his place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked to die and is given work — including, in the third item, a successor. The request under the broom tree is answered in a way, and by a man rather than a grave. See the note on 1 Kings 19:1–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of the three, Elijah personally does only the last: he finds Elisha ploughing with twelve yoke of oxen and throws his cloak on him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seven thousand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Yet **I have kept seven thousand in Israel** — every knee that has **not bowed to Baal**, every mouth that has **not kissed him**. **You are not alone**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I have left*, *I have reserved*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elijah said it twice — *I'm the only one left* — and the correction comes at the end, after the anointings, not before."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been there through the whole drought. Obadiah's hundred prophets were among them, in two caves, being fed. See the note on 1 Kings 18:1–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul quotes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 11: *\"LORD, they have killed Your prophets… and I alone am left.\" But what does the divine response say to him? \"**I have reserved for Myself seven thousand men who have not bowed the knee to Baal**.\" Even so then, at this present time there is a remnant **according to the election of grace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes the answer under the mountain and calls it grace."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was never said to him.** No rebuke for running, no reference to Jezebel, no mention of Carmel. He was fed, allowed to sleep, asked a question, allowed to answer it twice, and then told he was not the only one."}]}, "1 Kings 20:22–30a": {"title": "\"A god of the hills and not of the valleys\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A defeated army works out what went wrong, and gets it exactly backwards."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The theory"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Their gods are gods of the hills** — that's why they beat us. But if we fight them **on the plains**, we'll surely be stronger.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a reasonable conclusion by the standards of the time. Gods were local. Each had territory, and you fought accordingly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had lost in the hills, so they book the rematch on flat ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reorganisation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Replace the kings with regular commanders, raise another army as big as the one you lost, **horse for horse and chariot for chariot**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing wrong with the military reasoning either. They rebuild to exact specifications and change the command structure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every adjustment they make is competent, and the premise underneath all of it is wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Israel looked like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Camped in front of the vast Aramean force, the Israelites looked like **two little flocks of goats**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is two little flocks of kids — young goats."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the writer's image, not a boast from either side, and it is the most vivid line in the chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Because the Arameans think the LORD is a god of the hills and not of the valleys**, I will hand this whole vast army over to you, and **you will know that I am the LORD**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given for the victory is not Israel's righteousness — and Ahab is one of the worst kings in the book, married to Jezebel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the sentence the Arameans said out loud."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God answers a piece of bad theology by demonstrating the opposite in the place they chose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"You will know that I am the LORD\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice in the chapter — once at the first battle in the hills, once here in the valley."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same purpose given for both terrains."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Seven days"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **seven days the two camps faced each other**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A week of standing in the valley looking at an army that outnumbers you, before anything happens."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word had already been given on day one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundred thousand foot soldiers in one day, and a wall falling on twenty-seven thousand more at Aphek."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was king"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahab. The man who *did more to provoke the LORD God of Israel to anger than all the kings of Israel before him*, according to the chapter before."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God sends him a prophet with a battle plan, twice, and delivers him both times — and the stated reason is never Ahab."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Ahab does with the victory.** Makes a treaty with Ben-Hadad and lets him go, and a prophet tells him *because you have let slip out of your hand a man whom I appointed to utter destruction, your life shall go for his life.* He wins in the valley and loses it at the negotiating table."}]}, "1 Kings 3:15": {"title": "Then Solomon woke up", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The dream ends, and what he does next is the whole verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Gibeon, where the tabernacle stood, God appeared to him in the night: *ask! What shall I give you?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Solomon asked for **lev shomea** — a hearing heart — to judge the people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because you have asked this thing, and have not asked long life for yourself, nor riches… **I have also given you what you have not asked**.* See the note on 1 Kings 3:5–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The waking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Solomon **woke up — and realized it had all been a dream**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vehinneh chalom** — and behold, a dream."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why that matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator says so plainly. It was a dream."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which raises the obvious question about whether it counted, and the rest of the verse answers it by what Solomon does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The journey"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **went back to Jerusalem**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gibeon was about six miles north-west. He had gone there to sacrifice because *the high places were still in use, for there was no house built for the name of the LORD*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gets up and travels."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Stood **before the ark of the LORD's covenant**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ark was in Jerusalem, in the tent David had pitched for it. The bronze altar and the tabernacle were at Gibeon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two had been separated since the ark came up from Obed-Edom's house. See the note on 2 Samuel 6:12–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been at the altar. He goes to the ark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not because it was required — Gibeon was the sanctioned place for sacrifice at that moment. He goes to where the covenant chest was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offerings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And offered up **burnt offerings and peace offerings**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **olah** — wholly consumed, nothing kept back. See the note on Leviticus 1:10–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **shelamim** — the peace offering, the only sacrifice the worshipper ate. A meal shared in God's presence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Total surrender, and then a shared table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The feast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he **threw a feast for his whole court**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mishteh lechol-avadav** — a drinking-feast for all his servants."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The peace offering was eaten. The private dream becomes a public dinner."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shape of the verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Woke, went, stood, offered, feasted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five verbs, and every one of them is Solomon acting on something that happened while he was asleep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now two women who were harlots came to the king, and stood before him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The living child, the dead child, the sword, and *give her the living child, and by no means kill him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And all Israel heard of the judgment which the king had rendered; and they feared the king, for they saw that **the wisdom of God was in him** to administer justice.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gift is tested in the next paragraph, on the two least important people in the kingdom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the ark was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there I will meet with you, and I will speak with you **from above the mercy seat, from between the two cherubim**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But King Solomon loved many foreign women… and **his wives turned away his heart***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the LORD became angry with Solomon, because his heart had turned from the LORD God of Israel, **who had appeared to him twice***. See the note on 1 Kings 10:23–25."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator counts the appearances. This is the first."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he does with a dream.** Gets up, travels six miles, and stands in front of the covenant chest. The verse is five verbs long and every one of them treats the dream as binding."}]}, "1 Kings 3:5–14": {"title": "A listening heart, and everything he did not ask for", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God offers a blank cheque, and the interest of the story is entirely in what Solomon does with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he actually asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The English usually says *an understanding heart*. The Hebrew is **lev shomea** — a **listening** heart. A heart that hears."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a striking thing for a king to request. Not brilliance, not judgement in the abstract, but the capacity to take in what is being said to him — by God and by the people he is about to govern."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How he gets there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the run-up. He talks about David, then says two things about himself:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*I am only a child* — he was a grown man with a throne", "*I do not know how to go out or come in*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The request comes out of an admission, and the admission comes before the ask. Then the reason: *who on his own could ever shepherd this great people of Yours?* He is not asking for wisdom to be impressive. He is asking because he has looked at the job and knows he cannot do it."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because you asked for this — not long life for yourself, not riches, not the death of your enemies…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God lists the things Solomon did not ask for, which tells you what the offer was understood to include. And then: *what you did not even ask for, I give you too.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The riches and honour arrive as an unrequested extra. They are not the reward for a clever request; they come because of what the request revealed about the man making it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conditional at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And if you walk in My ways… I will give you long life as well.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Worth reading precisely. The wisdom is given outright, no condition. The riches and honour are given outright. Only the long life carries an *if* — and Solomon's own story, which turns badly in his later years, is the reason that detail is in the text."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What this passage is not.** It is not a formula for getting more by asking for the right thing. God's pleasure is in what the request showed about Solomon's estimate of himself and the size of the task. The story records a man who knew he was out of his depth, and the whole answer follows from that."}]}, "1 Kings 4:1–6": {"title": "Solomon's chief officials", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A staff list, and the interesting thing about it is the names."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **King Solomon reigned over all Israel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **al-kol-Yisrael** — over all Israel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is a claim the book will withdraw within seven chapters. Rehoboam loses ten tribes in a day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Azariah son of Zadok** — priest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Elihoreph and Ahijah, sons of Shisha** — secretaries."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Jehoshaphat son of Ahilud** — recorder."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Benaiah son of Jehoiada** — over the army."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Zadok and Abiathar** — priests."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Azariah son of Nathan** — over the officers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Zabud son of Nathan** — priest and the king's friend."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ahishar** — over the household."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Adoniram son of Abda** — over the forced labour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What most of them have in common"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are sons of David's men."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zadok, Benaiah, Jehoshaphat and Nathan all served the previous administration. Solomon has kept the staff and promoted the children."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Benaiah"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David's bodyguard commander, and the man who *went down and killed a lion in the midst of a pit on a snowy day*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the one who executed Joab at the altar, and Adonijah, at Solomon's order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Abiathar"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Named as priest here, and the previous chapter records that Solomon removed him: *so Solomon removed Abiathar from being priest to the LORD*, and sent him to his fields at Anathoth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list may reflect an earlier point in the reign, or the office rather than the incumbent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Zabud"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A priest and **the king's trusted friend**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **re'eh hammelek** — the friend of the king."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A court title, not a description. Hushai held it under David: *Hushai the Archite, David's friend*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An official position for the man the king talks to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Adoniram"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Had charge of **the forced labour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **al-hammas** — over the levy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty thousand men conscripted for the temple, in shifts of ten thousand a month."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then King Rehoboam sent Adoram, who was in charge of the revenue; **and all Israel stoned him with stones, and he died**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same office, one reign later. The forced labour is the grievance that splits the kingdom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the people say to Rehoboam"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Your father made our yoke heavy**; now therefore, lighten the burdensome service of your father, and his heavy yoke which he put on us, and we will serve you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the young men's advice: *my little finger shall be thicker than my father's waist*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter goes on to describe"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve district governors, each supplying the court one month a year."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty kors of fine flour a day, sixty of meal, ten fat oxen, twenty pasture oxen, a hundred sheep, besides deer, gazelles, roebucks and fatted fowl."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Judah and Israel were as numerous as the sand by the sea, eating and drinking and rejoicing.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the wisdom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **God gave Solomon wisdom and understanding beyond measure**, a heart as wide as the sand on the seashore.* See the note on 1 Kings 4:26–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The administration and the wisdom are described in the same chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Solomon had asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Give to Your servant an understanding heart **to judge Your people**.\"* See the note on 1 Kings 3:5–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The request was for judgment. The chapter shows what he built with it, including the levy that brings the whole thing down."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The list.** Sons of David's men, kept on and promoted — and the last name on it holds the office whose successor gets stoned by the crowd that splits the kingdom."}]}, "1 Kings 4:26–34": {"title": "Wisdom without end", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The height of the reign, described at length, with two facts in it the reader is meant to notice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The horses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Solomon had **forty thousand stalls of horses** for his chariots and **twelve thousand horsemen**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chronicles reads four thousand. The Hebrew numerals are easily confused in transmission."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem with horses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **he shall not multiply horses for himself**, nor cause the people to return to Egypt to multiply horses.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 17, the law of the king. See the note on Deuteronomy 17:14–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator records the stalls without comment and lets the law do the work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The supply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The district governors, each in his month, supplied everything King Solomon and all who came to his table needed, and **never let a thing run short**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve governors, one month each. The system worked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They also brought **barley and straw for the chariot horses**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gift"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **God gave Solomon wisdom and understanding beyond measure**, a heart as wide as **the sand on the seashore**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **rochav lev** — breadth of heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sand on the seashore** is the covenant image, used of Abraham's descendants. Applied here to the capacity of one man's mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His wisdom surpassed the wisdom of **all the people of the East** and all the wisdom of **Egypt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two recognised centres of learning. Mesopotamian omen literature and Egyptian instruction texts were the ancient world's curriculum."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The named rivals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wiser than **Ethan the Ezrahite**, and **Heman, Calcol, and Darda**, the sons of Mahol.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ethan and Heman have psalms attributed to them — Psalm 89 and Psalm 88."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator names the competition, which means readers knew who they were."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The output"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He spoke **three thousand proverbs**, and his songs numbered **a thousand and five**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs preserves a fraction. Two psalms are attributed to him, and the Song of Songs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The subjects"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **described the trees**, from the great cedar of Lebanon to the small hyssop that grows out of the wall; he taught of **animals and birds, of creeping things and fish**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Botany from the largest to the smallest, and then the four categories of Genesis 1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His wisdom is described as natural history, not only aphorism."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The traffic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People came from **every nation** to listen to Solomon's wisdom, sent by **all the kings of the earth** who had heard how wise he was.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The queen of Sheba is the one the book gives a chapter to: *the half was not told me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on 1 Kings 10:23–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Hebrew says about the horses again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 10 closes the account: *also Solomon had horses imported from Egypt*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that begins with wisdom beyond measure ends with the trade route the law named."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **King Solomon loved many foreign women**.* See the note on 1 Kings 11:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven hundred wives, three hundred concubines, and *his wives turned his heart*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the LORD became angry with Solomon, because **his heart had turned from the LORD God of Israel, who had appeared to him twice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heart that was as wide as the sand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Give to Your servant an **understanding heart** to judge Your people.\"* See the note on 1 Kings 3:5–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew was **lev shomea** — a hearing heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book uses the word *heart* at every stage: the request, the gift, and the turning."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word.** *Heart* runs through the whole account — a hearing heart asked for, a heart wide as the sand given, and a heart turned away in chapter 11."}]}, "1 Kings 6:14–18": {"title": "\"Not a single stone in sight\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most expensive stonework in Israel's history, and none of it could be seen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was underneath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Great, costly stones, dressed square at the quarry* — chapter 5, and the finest masonry money could buy, cut and finished in the dark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What went over it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **lined the inside walls with boards of cedar** — from the floor of the house up to the rafters of the ceiling.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every internal surface panelled. Then the floors in cypress, then the whole interior overlaid with gold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All cedar, **not a single stone in sight**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the writer's own summary, and it is the point of the paragraph."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stones were quarried, hauled, squared and set with enormous care, and then covered so completely that nobody entering the building would ever see one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The work on them was not for display."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Cedar"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cedar of Lebanon is close-grained, aromatic, and resistant to rot and insects. It was the prestige timber of the ancient world, shipped down the coast in rafts by Hiram's men."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not decay, and it does not admit what usually gets into wood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The carving"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Carved with **gourds and open flowers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under the gold — which is applied in the verses following — there is carving nobody would ever see either."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gourds and open flowers, hidden under metal, in a room only priests entered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inner room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At the far end of the house he built off twenty cubits with boards of cedar, from floor to rafters, and set that space apart as **the inner sanctuary, the Most Holy Place**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A perfect cube, twenty by twenty by twenty, entered once a year. See the note on Hebrews 9:6–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was in the walls"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stone, from a quarry, cut to fit somewhere it had never been."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cedar over the stone. Gold over the cedar. And carving in between that the gold covered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Layer on layer, and the visible surface is the last one applied."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the letters take it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You also, as living stones, are being built up a spiritual house* — Peter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Paul: *in Him the whole building, being fitted together, grows into a holy temple in the Lord, in whom you also are being built together for a dwelling place of God in the Spirit.* See the notes on 1 Peter 2:4–5 and Ephesians 2:17–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stones, quarried out of the dark, covered in something that does not rot and something that does not tarnish."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Solomon said at the dedication.** *But will God indeed dwell on the earth? Behold, heaven and the heaven of heavens cannot contain You. **How much less this temple which I have built!*** Seven years of cedar and gold, and the man who paid for it says it will not do."}]}, "1 Kings 6:6–8": {"title": "\"No hammer, no chisel, no iron tool\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The largest building project in Israel's history, and the site was silent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The stone was **dressed and finished at the quarry before it ever reached the house** — so that **no hammer, no chisel, no iron tool of any kind was heard in the house** while it was going up. **Not a sound.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every stone was cut, squared and finished miles away, underground, and carried up already fitted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that requires"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The measurements had to be exact before anything was moved. A stone dressed in the dark of a quarry has to arrive and sit down into a place prepared for it, with no adjustment possible on site."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no trimming at the wall. It fits or it does not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the stones came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Eighty thousand who **cut stone up in the hill country**… at the king's command they **quarried great, costly stones, dressing them square**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The shaping happened in the quarry — Jerusalem's stone was cut from tunnels under the city, in the dark, out of sight of the building."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The noise was all somewhere else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scale"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty thousand in shifts to Lebanon, seventy thousand carrying loads, eighty thousand quarrying, and thirty-three hundred supervisors."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nearly two hundred thousand men, and the site was quiet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "No iron"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The specification is not incidental. When Israel built an altar, the law said *you shall not build it of hewn stone, for if you use your tool on it, you have profaned it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Iron on stone, in that law, spoiled it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ledges"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He set **narrow ledges all around the outside** of the house, so the supporting beams would rest on those ledges **and not be driven into the walls of the house itself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The side rooms lean on the temple and nothing is driven into it. The main structure is not pierced to carry the additions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **stairway wound up** to the middle story, and from the middle to the third.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lulim** — a spiral, a winding stair. You could not see the top from the bottom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody on it climbs in a straight line. The way up is round the outside of the house, and at every point on it the wall is between the climber and where he is going."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the door was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The doorway to the lowest side chamber was **on the south side** of the house.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One entrance, at the bottom, on one side. You did not choose your route into the chambers — there was a single door and a single stair, and it went the way it went."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three storeys"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five cubits, six cubits, seven cubits — each floor wider than the one below it, because the beams rested on ledges cut further back in the wall as it rose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The higher you went, the more room there was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Peter takes this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You also, **as living stones**, are being built up a spiritual house, a holy priesthood.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And of Christ: *a living stone, rejected indeed by men, but chosen by God and precious.* See the note on 1 Peter 2:4–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul builds the same picture: *in whom the whole building, being fitted together, grows into a holy temple in the Lord.* See the note on Ephesians 2:17–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stones in Solomon's temple were finished in the dark and set in place without a sound."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How long it took.** Seven years, from Ziv in the fourth year to Bul in the eleventh. And Solomon spent thirteen years on his own house. The book records both figures without comment."}]}, "1 Kings 7:23–26": {"title": "The Sea on twelve oxen", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A bronze basin so large the writer calls it a sea."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The size"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ten cubits across from rim to rim, five cubits deep, and thirty cubits around… **it held two thousand baths of water**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roughly fifteen feet across, seven and a half deep, and something in the order of ten to twelve thousand gallons."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was cast in bronze, a handbreadth thick, in one piece."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it replaced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tabernacle had a bronze laver, made from the mirrors of the women who served at the door, for the priests to wash their hands and feet before serving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Solomon's version is the same item at enormous scale. Chronicles says plainly what it was for: *the Sea was for the priests to wash in.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The twelve oxen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It rested on **twelve bronze oxen — three facing north, three west, three south, three east** — all their hindquarters turned inward, the Sea riding on their backs.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four groups of three, facing outward in every direction, and the whole weight of the water on them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve is the number of the tribes, and the same arrangement as the camp in the wilderness — three tribes on each side of the tabernacle, facing out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order they are listed in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "North, west, south, east."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is not the order anybody would use to describe a circle if they were going clockwise. It is the reverse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why oxen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ox is the working animal of the Old Testament, and the law protected it in the middle of its labour: *do not muzzle an ox while it treads out the grain.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes that law twice and applies it to those who preach and teach — *is it oxen God is concerned about, or does He say it entirely for our sake?* See the notes on 1 Corinthians 9:7–10 and 1 Timothy 5:17–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever else the twelve are, they are carriers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The washing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ also loved the church and gave Himself up for her, so that He might make her holy, **washing her clean with water through the word**.* See the note on Ephesians 5:25–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The water in Ephesians is the word, and Paul says so in the same sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lily"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Its rim curved out **like the rim of a cup, like the bloom of a lily**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A vessel holding twelve thousand gallons of bronze-cast water, and the writer records that the edge was shaped like a flower."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened to it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahaz cut the panels off the stands and *removed the Sea from the bronze oxen that were under it, and put it on a pavement of stones.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same Ahaz whose stairway the shadow went back on. See the note on 2 Kings 20:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And when Babylon came: *the bronze Sea that was in the house of the LORD, the Chaldeans broke in pieces, and carried all the bronze to Babylon.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Revelation puts in front of the throne.** *Before the throne there was **a sea of glass, like crystal**.* Solomon's held twelve thousand gallons for priests who needed washing. John's is glass — still, and not needed."}]}, "1 Peter 1:13": {"title": "Where Peter tells them to put their hope", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This verse is the hinge of Peter's letter. Everything before it is what God has done; everything after it is how to live. And the joint between the two is a single instruction that most readings skip over to get to the commands that follow."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Three pictures, and only one of them is the command"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**get your mind stripped down and ready to move** — the Greek is one word, literally *gird up the loins of your mind*. A man in a long robe pulled it through his belt to run or work. Nobody had to explain it.", "**keep a clear head** — the word for not being drunk, used of staying alert.", "**pin your hope … on the kindness that arrives the day Jesus is revealed**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first two are participles. The third is the actual imperative. In Greek that distinction is unmistakable: the sentence is *hope*, and the other two describe the posture of someone doing it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means the command in this verse is not \"try harder\" or \"stay sober.\" It is **hope**, and hope aimed at one specific thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he tells them to hope in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in an improved version of themselves. Not in surviving the pressure they were under — and they were under real pressure; that is why the letter exists."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them to put their hope on *the kindness that arrives* — grace, being brought to them, at the revealing of Jesus Christ. It is spoken of as something in transit, on its way, arriving."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"All of it, nothing held back\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is an adverb in the sentence — *teleios*, completely, without reserve. It can attach to either the clear head or the hoping, and translators split over it. Either way the force is the same: nothing partial. Peter is not asking for hope with a fallback plan behind it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a hard instruction for people being pressed, which is exactly who he is writing to. The alternative he is ruling out is not despair. It is hedging — hoping in grace while quietly keeping a second account open in your own effort."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it comes exactly here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look at what precedes it: an inheritance kept in heaven, a salvation ready to be revealed, prophets and angels leaning in to look at it. Peter spends twelve verses on what has been done and is coming. *So* — get your mind ready, and put your hope there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is not decorative. The commands in the rest of the letter are addressed to people who have already been told where to look."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the holiness that follows.** Three verses later comes *be holy, for I am holy*, and it is often quoted on its own as the letter's demand. In Peter's sentence it stands downstream of this one: hope fixed on grace first, and holiness described as what children of that Father look like."}]}, "1 Peter 1:14–16": {"title": "The shape you had when you knew no better", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter names them by what they are before he says anything about behaviour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are **children who listen now**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tekna hupakoes** — children of obedience. A Hebrew way of saying it: children *characterised by* listening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say *become obedient children*. He addresses them as that, and then says what follows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What came before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse in front of this one: *gird up the loins of your mind, be sober, and **rest your hope fully upon the grace that is to be brought to you** at the revelation of Jesus Christ.* See the note on 1 Peter 1:13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hope set fully on grace, and then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not let your **old cravings** press you back **into the shape you had when you knew no better**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *conforming* is **suschematizomenoi** — from **schema**, the outward form something is pressed into."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same verb: *do not be conformed to this world, but be transformed by the renewing of your mind*. See the note on Romans 12:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ignorance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When you **knew no better**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **agnoia** — the ignorance you were in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old shape belongs to a period that is over, and Peter dates it by what they did not know rather than by what they did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The One who called you is holy, so **be holy yourselves — in every last thing you do**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *in all your conduct* — **anastrophe**, the whole way of life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The standard is a person, not a code. *As He who called you is holy.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God said it Himself, and it is on record: **Be holy. I am holy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus, four times over — 11:44, 11:45, 19:2, 20:7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter quotes the Levitical formula to Gentile believers scattered across five Roman provinces."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order in the Hebrew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Leviticus sentence gives the reason after the command: *be holy, for I am holy*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The holiness of God is the ground, and He states it about Himself. Nothing in the clause asks for holiness to be manufactured first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the word means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Qadosh** in Hebrew and **hagios** in Greek — set apart, separated for a use."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The vessels in the tabernacle were holy. Nothing was done to the metal; it was designated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter says four verses later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You were **not redeemed with corruptible things**, like silver or gold… but with **the precious blood of Christ**, as of a lamb without blemish and without spot.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument runs: you were bought, at that price, out of *your aimless conduct received by tradition from your fathers*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old shape is what the price was paid to get them out of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And in the next chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are **a chosen generation, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, His own special people**.* See the note on 1 Peter 2:9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Holy, stated as a fact about them, in the same letter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The command in chapter 1 and the description in chapter 2 are about the same people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the letter is going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Suffering. *Beloved, do not think it strange concerning the fiery trial which is to try you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is writing to *pilgrims of the Dispersion* — people whose conduct is being watched, which is why *all your conduct* is the phrase he uses."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How he addresses them.** *Children who listen* — said first, before a single instruction. The old shape is described as belonging to a time when they did not know."}]}, "1 Peter 1:23": {"title": "A seed that does not rot", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter explains a second birth by naming what it grew from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have been **born a second time**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anagegennemenoi** — a perfect passive participle. Having been begotten again, and standing in that condition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Passive. Nobody in the New Testament is described as having done this to himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter uses the word twice, and both are in this chapter: *He has begotten us again to a living hope through the resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two seeds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not out of a **seed that rots**, but out of one that **never will**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phthartes** and **aphthartou** — corruptible and incorruptible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same pair Paul uses of the resurrection body: *it is sown in corruption, it is raised in incorruption*. See the note on 1 Corinthians 15:53–55."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Spora** — seed, sowing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the seed is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **word of God, alive, and still standing** when everything else has gone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **zontos kai menontos** — living and remaining."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two participles, and neither is about power. One says it is alive; the other says it stays."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter quotes Isaiah:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"All flesh is as grass, and all the glory of man as the flower of the grass. **The grass withers, and its flower falls away, but the word of the LORD endures forever**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 40, and the line before it in Isaiah is *the grass withers, the flower fades, because the breath of the LORD blows upon it*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The connection Peter makes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **this is the word which by the gospel was preached to you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The everlasting word of Isaiah 40 and the sermon they heard are identified as the same thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seed that produced the second birth is what somebody said to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just been said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Since you have purified your souls in obeying the truth… **love one another fervently with a pure heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction comes first and this verse is its reason. *Since* — because you were born from that kind of seed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verses before that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You were **not redeemed with corruptible things**, like silver or gold, from your aimless conduct received by tradition from your fathers, but with the precious blood of Christ.* See the note on 1 Peter 1:14–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same adjective — **phthartois**, corruptible — of silver and gold."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Money rots, in Peter's vocabulary. Twice in ten verses he sets the perishable against the imperishable, and puts precious metal on the perishable side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other seed passages"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The seed is the word of God.* Luke's explanation of the sower. See the note on Matthew 13:18–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man scatters seed on the ground… and he has no idea how.* See the note on Mark 4:26–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And James: *of His own will He brought us forth by the word of truth, that we might be a kind of firstfruits of His creatures.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four writers, one image, and in every case the word is the seed rather than the soil or the farmer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter opens with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, who according to His abundant mercy has begotten us again to a living hope… to **an inheritance incorruptible and undefiled and that does not fade away, reserved in heaven for you***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Incorruptible again — the third use in one chapter. The seed, the inheritance, and by implication the person."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the birth came out of.** Not a decision described as a seed, and not an experience. *The word of God* — living, and still standing when the grass has gone."}]}, "1 Peter 1:24–25": {"title": "Grass dries out; the word stands", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter quotes Isaiah and then attaches the quotation to the sermon they had heard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 40 — the chapter that opens *comfort, comfort My people*, written to exiles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The voice in the wilderness, the valleys raised, the mountains made low, and then this: *all flesh is grass, and all its loveliness is like the flower of the field.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **people are grass**. Everything splendid about us is **the little flower on the grass**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the tree, not the crop. Grass, and the flower on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The grass **dries out**. The flower **drops off**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs, and both are ordinary. Isaiah's setting explains the speed: *the grass withers, the flower fades, **because the breath of the LORD blows upon it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that climate the hot east wind off the desert could finish a field of wildflowers in a day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being compared to grass"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everything splendid about us.* The Greek is **doxa** — glory."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not human weakness. Human glory. The best of it is the part that falls off first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **what the Lord says stands, and keeps standing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **menei eis ton aiona** — remains into the age."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And that is **the message that was announced to you as good news**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter closes the loop. The eternal word Isaiah was talking about is the one his readers heard preached — the Greek is **euangelisthen**, evangelised to you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not leave it as a general statement about Scripture's durability. He points at the specific message they received."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he is saying it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *having been **born again**, not of corruptible seed but **incorruptible, through the word of God** which lives and abides forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument runs on the seed. What a thing is born from determines how long it lasts, and Peter says theirs was imperishable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he is writing to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Pilgrims of the Dispersion* — scattered believers in five Roman provinces, under pressure, and the letter keeps returning to *the fiery trial* and *suffering wrongfully*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The grass image lands differently for people whose situation is precarious."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other side of the same chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, who according to His abundant mercy has begotten us again to **a living hope**… to **an inheritance incorruptible and undefiled and that does not fade away**, reserved in heaven for you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Does not fade away** — the Greek is **amaranton**, unfading. It is a flower word, chosen deliberately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Amos fits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A famine of hearing the words of the LORD. See the note on Amos 8:11–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says the word stands. Amos describes what it is like when people cannot find it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Peter does with an old quotation.** He cites a poem about human transience and then says the permanent thing in it is what they were told when somebody preached to them."}]}, "1 Peter 1:3": {"title": "A hope with a pulse in it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first sentence of the letter proper, and it is one long burst."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Praise God, **the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eulogetos ho Theos kai Pater tou Kuriou hemon Iesou Christou**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Jewish blessing formula — **barukh** — carried into Greek. Paul opens Ephesians and 2 Corinthians the same way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is writing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peter, an apostle of Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fisherman who denied Him three times in a courtyard, writing to *the pilgrims of the Dispersion in Pontus, Galatia, Cappadocia, Asia, and Bithynia*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five Roman provinces in what is now Turkey. Believers under pressure and scattered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mercy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He had **mercy to spare**, and He spent it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kata to polu autou eleos** — according to His great mercy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Polu** — much, abundant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The birth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Giving us **a second birth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anagennesas hemas** — having begotten us again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Anagennao** — to beget anew. The verb appears twice in the New Testament, both in this chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter was in the room when Nicodemus was told *unless one is born again*. See the note on John 3:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is a birth into"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Into **a hope with a pulse in it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eis elpida zosan** — into a living hope."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The adjective"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Zosa** — living. Not *lively* or *strong*. Alive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter uses the word four times in this letter: a living hope, the living word, living stones, and *the living and enduring word of God*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why living"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus came up out of the grave, and **that is how**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **di' anastaseos Iesou Christou ek nekron** — through the resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hope is alive because its ground is a man who came back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter had before that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Peter went out and **wept bitterly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the courtyard and the empty tomb he had nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angel's message on the Sunday named him separately: *go, tell His disciples — **and Peter**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **an inheritance incorruptible and undefiled and that does not fade away**, reserved in heaven for you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three adjectives, all negatives: **aphtharton**, **amianton**, **amaranton**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cannot decay, cannot be polluted, cannot fade. See the note on 1 Peter 1:4–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is guarding it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who are **kept by the power of God** through faith for salvation ready to be revealed in the last time.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phrouroumenous** — garrisoned, held under guard."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same military verb Paul uses of the peace of God standing over the mind. See the note on Philippians 4:6–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The joy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In this you greatly rejoice, though now **for a little while, if need be, you have been grieved by various trials**.* See the note on 1 Peter 1:6–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whom they have not seen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whom having not seen you love. Though now you do not see Him, yet believing, **you rejoice with joy inexpressible and full of glory**.* See the note on 1 Peter 1:8–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anekaleto** — unspeakable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written by a man who had seen Him, to people who had not, about a joy he says cannot be described."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The adjective.** **Zosa** — living. Not a strong hope or a bright one. Alive, and the reason given is a man who came out of a grave."}]}, "1 Peter 1:4–5": {"title": "A fortune with your name on it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three Greek adjectives, all built the same way, describing what cannot happen to what is being kept for you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Nothing can rot it, nothing can dirty it, nothing can drain the colour out of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each word in Greek begins with an *a-* prefix — the negative, like our *un-*:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**aphthartos** — not perishable. It cannot decay from inside.", "**amiantos** — not defiled. Nothing can spoil it from outside.", "**amarantos** — not fading. It will not lose its colour with time."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter stacks three negatives because he is ruling out every way anything has ever been lost: rot, contamination, and the slow bleaching of age."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third word is where we get *amaranth*, the flower the ancients said never withered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is kept"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is put away in heaven, waiting for you.* The verb is perfect passive — already reserved, and standing reserved. Someone else did the putting away, and it is still there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And who is kept"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God's power stands guard over you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the second half, and it is the one people miss. The inheritance is guarded in heaven; the heir is guarded on earth. Peter uses a military word — garrisoned, protected as a city is protected."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both ends of the arrangement are held by someone other than the person inheriting."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who he was writing to.** Scattered believers about to face real persecution — the letter goes on at length about suffering. This is what he opens with: everything you were born into is out of reach of anything that is about to happen to you."}]}, "1 Peter 1:6–7": {"title": "Gold goes into the fire", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The reason for the joy is named, and then the trials are named, and Peter does not soften either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has you glad"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That is what has you so glad.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **en ho agalliasthe** — in which you greatly rejoice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Agalliao** — to exult, to leap for joy. A strong word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The antecedent is the previous paragraph: the living hope, the inheritance kept in heaven, and the garrison round it. See the note on 1 Peter 1:3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The concession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even now, when **for a little while**, if it has to be this way.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **oligon arti** — a little now."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ei deon estin** — if it is necessary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter does not claim it is short in itself. He calls it short against what he has just described."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pressure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All sorts of hard things are pressing down on you and **you feel the weight**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **lupethentes en poikilois peirasmois** — having been grieved by various trials."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Poikilos** — many-coloured, varied. The same word he uses two chapters later of God's grace: *the manifold grace of God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Varied trials, and varied grace. Peter uses one adjective for both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That the grief is imaginary, or that it should not be felt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Lupethentes** — having been made sorrowful. He grants it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gold"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Gold goes into the fire **to prove it is the real thing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dia puros dokimazomenou** — tested through fire."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dokimazo** — to assay, to test metal for purity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The refiner heats it until the impurities rise and can be lifted off, and what is left is what the metal actually was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The concession about gold"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **gold still wears out in the end**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tou apollumenou** — which perishes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most durable substance the ancient world knew, and Peter notes in passing that it does not last."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your faith, tested, is **worth more than gold**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **polutimoteron chrusiou** — more precious than gold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **it gets the fire too**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the whole argument. The fire is not evidence of neglect. It is what is done to things worth testing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when Jesus finally **steps into plain sight**, what comes out the other side is **praise, and honor, and glory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **en apokalupsei Iesou Christou** — at the revelation of Jesus Christ."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who receives the three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek does not specify."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It can read as praise to God for the tested faith, or as praise given to the believer. Peter leaves it open, and both readings sit oddly and deliberately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter had been told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Simon, Simon! Indeed, **Satan has asked for you, that he may sift you as wheat**. But I have prayed for you, that your faith should not fail.\"* See the note on Luke 22:31–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man writing about tested faith had been told his own would be sifted, and it was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whom having not seen you love. Though now you do not see Him, yet believing, **you rejoice with joy inexpressible and full of glory**.* See the note on 1 Peter 1:8–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What James says with the same vocabulary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My brethren, count it all joy when you fall into **various trials**, knowing that **the testing of your faith produces patience**.* See the note on James 1:2–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two words — **poikilois peirasmois** and **dokimion**."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The argument.** Gold is put in the fire because it is worth assaying, and Peter adds that gold perishes anyway. The fire is not a sign of neglect."}]}, "1 Peter 1:8–9": {"title": "You have never seen Him", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Written by a man who had seen Him, to people who had not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is writing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter. He had walked with Him for three years, watched the transfiguration, denied Him in a courtyard and eaten breakfast with Him on a beach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He mentions it in his second letter: *we were eyewitnesses of His majesty… we heard this voice which came from heaven when we were with Him on the holy mountain*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He knows exactly what he is saying here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have **never laid eyes on Him, and you love Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ouk idontes agapate** — not having seen, you love."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You **cannot see Him now either, and you trust Him anyway**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **arti me horontes, pisteuontes de** — not seeing at present, yet believing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses, and the second is about the present. Not only did they miss the three years; they cannot see Him this morning either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus said about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Thomas, because you have seen Me, you have believed. **Blessed are those who have not seen and yet have believed**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to a man who had insisted on the nail prints."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is describing the people that sentence was about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The joy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it makes you **so happy you cannot get it into words**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **chara aneklaleto** — joy unspeakable. **Aneklaletos** occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is **glory in that gladness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dedoxasmene** — glorified. A perfect participle: the joy has been glorified and stays so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who these people were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Pilgrims of the Dispersion in Pontus, Galatia, Cappadocia, Asia, and Bithynia.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Scattered, under pressure, and the letter is about suffering. The paragraph immediately before this is about gold in a fire."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The joy is described in a letter written to people having a hard time, and the writer does not suggest the two are in tension."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The receiving"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And you are **already collecting** what all the trusting was aiming at the whole time.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **komizomenoi** — receiving, carrying off. A present participle: it is happening now."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not at the end. In progress."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The outcome"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **end of your faith — the salvation of your souls**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **telos** — the goal, the completion, what a thing was for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Faith is described as having an aim, and the aim is arriving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Of this salvation **the prophets have inquired and searched carefully**, who prophesied of the grace that would come to you… searching what, or what manner of time, the Spirit of Christ who was in them was indicating.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *to them it was revealed that **not to themselves, but to us, they were ministering**… things which **angels desire to look into**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *look into* is **parakupsai** — to stoop and peer, the word for the disciples bending to look into the empty tomb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Prophets searching and angels craning to see, and the readers have it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter opens with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, who according to His abundant mercy has begotten us again to a living hope through the resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who wrote it.** The man who had seen Him. He is telling people who never will that not seeing does not put them behind — and the prophets and the angels are the ones straining to look."}]}, "1 Peter 2:1–3": {"title": "Howling for milk", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Five things put down, and then an appetite."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Strip all of this off and leave it lying there: **wanting to hurt people, working an angle, wearing a face that is not yours, resenting what someone else has, running your mouth to tear a person down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Malice, deceit, hypocrisy, envy, and evil speaking — five, and all five are social. Not one of them is a private vice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Apothemenoi** — having laid aside, the word for taking off a garment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word Paul uses in *put off the old man*, and the word Luke uses for the coats laid at the feet of a young man named Saul."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be like **a baby a few days old, howling for milk**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **artigenneta brephe** — newborn infants."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Epipothesate** is the verb: crave, long for intensely. It is used elsewhere of Paul aching to see people again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A newborn does not need to be trained to be hungry. Nobody teaches it to cry for milk."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind of milk"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What you want is **the word, unwatered and unmixed** — that is what makes you grow.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **adolon** — unadulterated, with nothing added."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is the negative of *dolos*, the *working an angle* from the list two verses earlier. Peter has just told them to put down deceit and now describes the milk as undeceitful."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Logikon** is the other adjective — of the word, reasonable. The milk is the word itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it produces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That is what makes you grow.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **auxethete eis soterian** — that you may grow up into salvation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Growth, not initiation. Peter is not telling them how to be born; he is telling people already born how to get bigger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After all, **you have tasted Him already — and He is kind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **egeusasthe hoti chrestos ho Kyrios** — you have tasted that the Lord is good."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is Psalm 34: *O taste and see that the LORD is good.* See the note on Psalms 34:8–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the psalm is quoted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The appetite is explained by the previous taste."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter does not say *crave the word because you should*. He says the craving follows a flavour already known."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being said about milk"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses milk as the elementary stage — *I fed you with milk and not with solid food* — and Hebrews uses it as a rebuke: *you have come to need milk and not solid food*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter does not. Here the milk is the word itself, and the craving for it is the healthy sign, not the immature one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Coming to Him as to a living stone… **you also, as living stones, are being built up a spiritual house**.* See the note on 1 Peter 2:4–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the priesthood: *a chosen generation, a royal priesthood*. See the note on 1 Peter 2:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The newborn in verse 2 is a priest by verse 9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pun the English loses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chrestos** — good, kind — sounded almost identical to **Christos** in the Greek of the day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have tasted that the Lord is good* and *you have tasted that the Lord is Christ* are one letter apart, and Peter's readers would have heard both."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the appetite comes from.** *You have tasted Him already — and He is kind.* The craving is not commanded into existence; it follows a flavour that has already been on the tongue."}]}, "1 Peter 2:21–22": {"title": "A track to walk in", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter is writing to household slaves, and this is what he tells them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being addressed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The paragraph before: *servants, be submissive to your masters with all fear, not only to the good and gentle, but also to the harsh*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **oiketai** — domestic slaves. And **skoliois**, crooked, twisted — the harsh masters are described with the word behind *scoliosis*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For what credit is it if, when you are beaten for your faults, you take it patiently? But when you do good and suffer, if you take it patiently, this is commendable before God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is **the very thing you were called into**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eis touto eklethete** — to this you were called."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *this may happen to you*. The suffering is named as the content of the calling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ **suffered for us** and **left us a track to walk in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hupogrammon** — a writing-copy. The line of letters a schoolmaster wrote at the top of a wax tablet for a child to trace underneath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Put your feet where His went.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epakolouthesete tois ichnesin autou** — follow closely in His footprints."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ichnos** — a track, a footprint. The same image as the answer given to the bride when she asked where he was: *follow the tracks of the flock*. See the note on Song of Songs 1:8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first credential"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **never sinned once**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is quoting Isaiah 53: *He had done no violence*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Nobody ever **caught a lie coming out of His mouth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah again: *nor was any deceit in His mouth*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dolos** — bait, a lure, a trick."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter picks the line about the mouth, and he is writing to people whose suffering came from being spoken to and about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who, when He was reviled, **did not revile in return**; when He suffered, **He did not threaten**, but **committed Himself to Him who judges righteously**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *committed* is **paredidou** — an imperfect. He kept handing it over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing He did with the abuse was pass it to somebody else to deal with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter watched"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was in the courtyard. See the note on Matthew 26:69–75."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He saw the trial, the silence, and the beating, and forty years later he writes about the mouth that had nothing in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He also drew a sword in the garden and had to be told to put it away, which is presumably why he stresses that Christ did not threaten."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who Himself **bore our sins in His own body on the tree**, that we, having died to sins, might live for righteousness — **by whose stripes you were healed**.* See the note on 1 Peter 2:24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The example turns into a substitution in the space of two verses. Peter starts with a copybook line to trace and ends with something nobody can imitate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For you were like sheep going astray, but **have now returned** to the Shepherd and Overseer of your souls.* See the note on 1 Peter 2:25."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word for the example.** A schoolboy's copy-line — the letters written at the top of the tablet for a child to trace. Written to household slaves with harsh masters."}]}, "1 Peter 2:24": {"title": "\"He hauled our sins up onto the wood\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter quotes Isaiah 53 and changes the tense, and the change is the whole point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He hauled our sins up onto the wood Himself, in His own body.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anaphero** — to carry up, to bear upward. It is the word used in the Greek Old Testament of a priest carrying a sacrifice **up** onto the altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter, writing about a man nailed to a raised beam, chooses the altar word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In His own body\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is precise about the location of the transaction. Not in principle, not representatively — in a body, on a piece of wood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word he uses for the cross is *xylon*, timber, the same word the Greek Old Testament uses in the line about anyone hung on a tree being under a curse. Paul quotes that verse in Galatians for the same reason."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Isaiah, in the past tense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You were healed by his wounds.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah wrote *we are healed* — a prophet describing something ahead of him. Peter writes **were**, aorist: done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the difference the cross makes to the sentence. Isaiah is looking forward; Peter is looking back at an accomplished thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that we would be dead to sin and alive for what is right.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The purpose clause is worth keeping attached. Peter does not present the bearing of sins as an end in itself but as the ground of a life lived differently — and the two verbs are both about a state, not an effort."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who he was writing to.** The paragraph is addressed to household slaves being treated unjustly, and the verses just before hold up Christ's response to suffering as the pattern: He did not retaliate, but *entrusted Himself to Him who judges justly.* The healing sentence arrives in a passage about people with no power, and Peter tells them what was carried for them."}]}, "1 Peter 2:25": {"title": "Back to the Shepherd", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and it is the last line of the passage about the cross."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who Himself bore our sins in His own body on the tree… **by whose stripes you were healed**.* See the note on 1 Peter 2:24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then this verse, with no break. The sheep sentence is the conclusion of the stripes sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wandering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You had **wandered off, the way sheep do**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is quoting Isaiah. *All we like sheep have gone astray; we have turned, every one, to his own way.* See the note on Isaiah 53:4–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **planomenoi** — straying, from the root of *planet*, the wandering stars."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sheep do not run away. They graze in a direction, one mouthful at a time, and look up somewhere else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now you **have come back**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epestraphete** — an aorist passive. *You were turned back*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter does not describe a search that succeeded. He describes people who were turned."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **your Shepherd**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **poimena**, and Peter is the man to whom this word was said three times over breakfast: *feed My lambs*, *tend My sheep*, *feed My sheep*. See the note on John 21:4–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He knew what it was to wander, and to be turned back on a beach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The One who **keeps watch over your souls**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **episkopon** — overseer, the word behind *bishop*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Shepherd and Overseer, side by side, of one flock."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Peter uses it again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Shepherd the flock of God which is among you, serving as **overseers**… and when **the Chief Shepherd** appears, you will receive the crown of glory that does not fade away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same two words, in his instructions to elders three chapters later. He is telling them what they are a copy of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three shepherd sentences"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am the good shepherd. The good shepherd **gives His life** for the sheep.* See the note on John 10:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The great Shepherd of the sheep*, in Hebrews, *brought up from the dead*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Chief Shepherd*, in Peter, *when He appears*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Good, at the cross. Great, at the resurrection. Chief, at the return."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The psalm underneath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD is my shepherd; **I shall not want**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the line in the middle of it: *He restores my soul* — the Hebrew is *He brings my soul back*, the same idea as Peter's returning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sentence does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about how far, how long, or what was done out there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah's line is *every one to his own way*, and Peter's answer to all of it is one verb in the passive."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the passage lands.** Not on the wandering, and not on a search. *You were turned back* — and the one they were turned to is named twice, as the one who feeds and the one who watches."}]}, "1 Peter 2:4–5": {"title": "A house made out of people", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter, whose name means a stone, writes about stones."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Keep coming to Him\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a present participle — continuous. Not *come once*; keep coming."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same posture the whole passage assumes: something already true, being returned to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stone that is alive"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is a stone, and **He is alive**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two words do not belong together. A stone is the standard image for what does not move, does not grow, and is not alive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter puts them side by side without explaining, and then does it again to his readers: *and you are stones too, **alive the way He is**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Rejected and chosen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People took one look and wanted nothing to do with Him — but God chose Him, and to God He is worth everything.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verdicts on the same stone, and Peter reports both. The builders' assessment was not a mistake about the stone's quality; it was simply theirs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is quoting Psalm 118 — *the stone the builders rejected has become the cornerstone* — the psalm sung at the close of Passover, on the night of the bread and the cup."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God is doing with the stones"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God is **stacking you up** into a house He lives in.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Passive. The stones are not building anything; they are being built."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the house is not a place people visit. It is where He lives, and it is made out of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Priests carrying the Spirit's own sacrifices"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are **the priests in it**, set apart, and the sacrifices you carry in are the Spirit's own — and through Jesus Christ God is glad to take them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things worth slowing down on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Priests.** Not a class within the house. The stones and the priests are the same people. Under the old covenant one tribe served and everybody else waited outside a curtain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The Spirit's own.** The offerings are called *spiritual sacrifices* — they originate on the other side. The priest carries in what he was given to carry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Through Jesus Christ God is glad to take them.* The acceptability is located in the phrase *through Jesus Christ*, and not in the quality of the offering."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The building materials.** No stone in this house was quarried square. Peter's readers were *scattered strangers*, refugees in five provinces, and he tells them they are load-bearing."}]}, "1 Peter 2:6": {"title": "The stone the corner rests on", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter quotes Isaiah, and the man quoting it was given a name meaning rock."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Coming to Him as to a living stone, rejected indeed by men, but chosen by God and precious, **you also, as living stones, are being built up a spiritual house**, a holy priesthood.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A building made of people, on a stone that is alive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Watch — **I am setting a stone down in Zion, the one the whole corner rests on**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 28:16. See the note on Isaiah 28:16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **akrogoniaion** — the extreme corner stone. The block at the angle that sets the line for both walls and carries the weight where they meet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I chose Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eklekton** — chosen, elect. The same word Peter uses of his readers in the letter's first line: *elect according to the foreknowledge of God the Father*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is **worth everything**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Entimon** — held in honour, precious, of high value."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **whoever believes on Him will not be put to shame**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek follows the Septuagint reading of Isaiah — **ou me kataischunthe**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew has *will not make haste*. Peter uses the Greek Bible, as Paul does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, to you who believe, **He is precious**; but to those who are disobedient, \"**the stone which the builders rejected has become the chief cornerstone**,\" and \"**a stone of stumbling and a rock of offence**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three stone quotations in a row: Isaiah 28, Psalm 118, and Isaiah 8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same stone does three different things depending on who is standing near it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one about the builders"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 118. Jesus quoted it at the end of the parable of the vineyard, to the chief priests, and *they sought to lay hands on Him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter had quoted it himself before the Sanhedrin: *this is the stone which was rejected by you builders, which has become the chief cornerstone*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men he said it to were the builders in question."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one about stumbling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 8: *He will be as a sanctuary, but a stone of stumbling and a rock of offence to both the houses of Israel*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sanctuary and stumbling block, in one sentence, in the original."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Peter's own name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are Simon the son of Jonah. **You shall be called Cephas**, which is translated, A Stone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **petros** — a stone, a piece of rock."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who was renamed Rock spends this passage arranging every stone text in the Old Testament around somebody else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never applies the word to himself in either letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the house is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A holy priesthood, **to offer up spiritual sacrifices acceptable to God through Jesus Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the sentence that follows the three quotations:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But you are a chosen generation, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, His own special people.* See the note on 1 Peter 2:9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the building is elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Having been built on the foundation of the apostles and prophets, **Jesus Christ Himself being the chief cornerstone**, in whom the whole building, being fitted together, grows into a holy temple in the Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul, in Ephesians, with the same word."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the writer does not do.** Peter is the man who was renamed Rock, and he spends the paragraph putting every stone verse in the Old Testament under somebody else's feet."}]}, "1 Peter 2:9": {"title": "\"But look at you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four titles handed out in one verse, and every one of them was Israel's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being addressed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's readers are *pilgrims of the Dispersion* — refugees scattered across five provinces of Asia Minor, most of them poor, some of them slaves, and under pressure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "To those people: *God picked you out.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**God picked you out** — a chosen generation, **eklektos**", "**priests in a King's house** — a royal priesthood", "**a nation set apart** — a holy nation", "**a people He keeps for Himself** — a people for His own possession"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every phrase comes from Exodus 19, spoken at Sinai: *you shall be to Me a kingdom of priests and a holy nation.* See the note on Exodus 19:7–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter takes the sentence God said to Israel at the mountain and says it to Gentile believers in Pontus and Cappadocia."}, {"k": "h", "t": "King and priest together"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A royal priesthood** — the two offices in one phrase."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Israel they were kept strictly apart. Uzziah was a king who walked into the sanctuary with a censer and came out a leper. See the note on 2 Chronicles 26:16–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only Melchizedek held both — *king of Salem, priest of God Most High* — and Zechariah was told to put a crown on a high priest's head and say *He shall be a priest on his throne.* See the notes on Genesis 14:17–20 and Zechariah 6:9–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That combination, forbidden to everybody in the old covenant, is what Peter calls his readers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The job"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there is a job in it: **say out loud how good He is**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **aretas** — excellencies, virtues, the qualities that make something what it is. The verb is to publish abroad, to tell out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priestly work named here is not sacrifice or mediation. It is speech about what He is like."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two states"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You were **in the dark**, and He called you, and **the light you walked into is more than you can take in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *His marvellous light* — wonderful, astonishing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the movement is His: *He called you.* Nobody in the verse found their own way out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse before, and after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before: *a stone of stumbling and a rock of offence* — the same stone that is precious to those who believe."}, {"k": "p", "t": "After: *who once were not a people but are now the people of God, who had not obtained mercy but now have obtained mercy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is quoting Hosea. *Lo-Ammi*, not My people, reversed — the same passage Paul uses in Romans 9. See the notes on Hosea 1:8–9 and Romans 9:25–26."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Peter had called them a few verses earlier.** *Living stones, being built up a spiritual house, a holy priesthood.* The house and the priests are the same people, and the stones were not quarried square. See the note on 1 Peter 2:4–5."}]}, "1 Peter 3:10": {"title": "\"Anyone who wants to see good days\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter is quoting a psalm, and he changes almost nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 34: *who is the man who desires life, and loves many days, that he may see good? Keep your tongue from evil, and your lips from speaking deceit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter lifts four verses of it into the middle of a letter about suffering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The psalm's own setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The title says David wrote it *when he pretended madness before Abimelech, who drove him away, and he departed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was on the run from Saul, had fled to enemy territory, was recognised, and escaped by scratching at the doors and letting spit run down his beard."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he wrote a psalm about wanting good days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wanting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Anyone who **wants to enjoy life and see good days**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse begins with a desire, not a duty. It is addressed to whoever wants this — and takes the wanting as reasonable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Days, plural"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Trouble in this psalm is singular and days are plural. The psalm says *many are the afflictions of the righteous, but the LORD delivers him out of them all* — and the delivering is what the whole psalm is about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Shut your mouth on the rotten word, and **do not let a lie past your lips**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two instructions and both are about the mouth. Nothing about circumstances, nothing about effort."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of the psalm is *keep your tongue from evil* — **natsar**, to guard, as a sentry guards a gate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows in Peter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let him **turn away from evil and do good**; let him **seek peace and pursue it**. For the eyes of the LORD are on the righteous, and His ears are open to their prayers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Turn, do, seek, pursue. And then the reason: He is watching and listening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Peter needed it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter is written to *the pilgrims of the Dispersion* — believers scattered across five Roman provinces, and the letter is full of *fiery trial*, *suffering wrongfully*, and *when you do good and suffer for it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Into that Peter puts a quotation about wanting good days, and does not qualify it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is around it in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately before: *not returning evil for evil or reviling for reviling, but on the contrary **blessing**, knowing that you were called to this, **that you may inherit a blessing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mouth again, and the blessing named as the inheritance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest of the psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Oh, taste and see that the LORD is good; blessed is the man who trusts in Him. The young lions lack and suffer hunger, but those who seek the LORD shall not lack any good thing.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Peter's own mouth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man quoting *do not let a lie past your lips* had denied three times with an oath in a courtyard, and had been restored by a charcoal fire on a beach. See the note on Luke 22:31–34."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the verse offers.** Not survival, not endurance, not compensation later. Life enjoyed and good days seen — and the condition attached is about what a man says."}]}, "1 Peter 3:11": {"title": "Seek peace and pursue it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second half of a quotation, and both verbs in it are active."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 34, quoted whole across two verses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who among you loves being alive, who wants long days full of good? Then **keep your tongue from evil**… **go after peace, chase it down**.* See the note on Psalms 34:11–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David wrote it after escaping Gath by feigning madness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who would love life and see good days, **let him refrain his tongue from evil**, and his lips from speaking deceit.* See the note on 1 Peter 3:10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let him **turn away from evil and do good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ekklinato de apo kakou kai poiesato agathon**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ekklino** — to turn aside, to swerve away from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs, and the second is not implied by the first. Turning away from evil does not automatically produce good."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let him **seek peace and pursue it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **zetesato eirenen kai dioxato auten**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Zeteo** — to seek, to look for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dioko** — to pursue, to chase, to hunt. The verb used of persecution: *blessed are those who are **persecuted** for righteousness' sake*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the second verb adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seeking is looking. Pursuing is going after something that is getting away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter keeps both from the psalm, and the escalation is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being told this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Believers scattered across five Roman provinces, under pressure, whom Peter has just told:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Finally, all of you be of one mind, having compassion for one another; love as brothers, be tenderhearted, be courteous.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not returning evil for evil or reviling for reviling, but on the contrary blessing.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because David wrote it while a fugitive in enemy territory, having escaped by pretending to be mad."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter quotes a man who knew what it cost."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows the quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the eyes of the LORD are on the righteous, and His ears are open to their prayers**; but the face of the LORD is against those who do evil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Also from Psalm 34, verse 15, continuing the quotation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **who is he who will harm you if you become followers of what is good?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kakoson** — will harm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The concession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But even if you should suffer for righteousness' sake, **you are blessed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter asks the rhetorical question and then answers it honestly. Usually nobody. Sometimes somebody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And do not be afraid of their threats, nor be troubled.* See the note on 1 Peter 3:14–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But sanctify the Lord God in your hearts, and **always be ready to give a defence** to everyone who asks you a reason for the hope that is in you, **with meekness and fear**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apologian** — a defence, the root of *apologetics*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the manner is specified: **meta prautetos kai phobou** — with gentleness and reverence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If it is possible, **as much as depends on you, live peaceably with all men**.* See the note on Romans 12:17–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two qualifications in one clause: *if possible*, and *as much as depends on you*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two verbs.** Seek, and then pursue. Peter keeps the escalation from the psalm — peace is something you have to chase, which assumes it is moving."}]}, "1 Peter 3:22": {"title": "All put under Him", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last clause of a difficult paragraph, and it is where the whole argument was going."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the sentence starts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For Christ also suffered once for sins, **the just for the unjust**, that He might bring us to God, being put to death in the flesh but made alive by the Spirit.* See the note on 1 Peter 3:18–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the spirits in prison, the days of Noah, the eight souls saved through water, and baptism as the antitype."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole passage is one long sentence in Greek, and this is its end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ascension"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who **has gone into heaven**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **poreutheis eis ouranon**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same participle used two verses earlier of going to the spirits in prison. Peter uses one verb for both journeys."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The position"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And is **at God's right hand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **en dexia tou Theou**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 110: *the LORD said to my Lord, \"Sit at My right hand, till I make Your enemies Your footstool.\"* See the note on Psalms 110:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted Old Testament verse in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three groups"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **angels and authorities and powers** all put under Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **angelon kai exousion kai dunameon**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The vocabulary Paul uses for the ranked spiritual orders: *principalities, powers, might, dominion*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter names three and does not sort them further."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hupotagenton** — having been subjected, put in order under."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aorist passive participle. Completed, and done to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that answers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter is written to believers scattered across five Roman provinces, under pressure, told to expect *the fiery trial which is to try you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter has just told slaves to submit to harsh masters, wives to submit to unbelieving husbands, and everyone to *sanctify the Lord God in your hearts, and always be ready to give a defence*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last clause of the argument is that the powers ranged against them are already under somebody's feet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where else Peter argues from position"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, who according to His abundant mercy has begotten us again to a living hope **through the resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead**.* See the note on 1 Peter 1:3–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To an inheritance incorruptible and undefiled and that does not fade away, **reserved in heaven for you**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says in the same terms"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Far above all principality and power and might and dominion… **and He put all things under His feet**.* See the note on Ephesians 1:20–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same three-part list, the same verb, the same psalm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Hebrews' qualification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have put all things in subjection under his feet. For in that He put all in subjection under him, He left nothing that is not put under him. **But now we do not yet see all things put under him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But we see Jesus.* See the note on Hebrews 2:8–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews states the tension the New Testament does not resolve: it is done, and it is not yet visible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows in Peter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, since Christ suffered for us in the flesh, **arm yourselves also with the same mind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The conclusion drawn from the enthronement is an instruction about suffering."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The tense.** **Hupotagenton** — aorist passive. Already done, and done to them. Written to people under pressure in five provinces."}]}, "1 Peter 3:5": {"title": "The godly women of long ago", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter names where a particular kind of beauty came from, and it is not a technique."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not let your adornment be merely outward — **arranging the hair, wearing gold, or putting on fine apparel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rather let it be **the hidden person of the heart**, with the incorruptible beauty of a gentle and quiet spirit, **which is very precious in the sight of God**.* See the note on 1 Peter 3:3–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ho kruptos tes kardias anthropos** — the hidden person of the heart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That is how **the godly women of long ago made themselves beautiful**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hai hagiai gunaikes** — the holy women."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ekosmoun heautas** — they adorned themselves. Imperfect: it was their habit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kosmeo** — to arrange, to order, to decorate. The root of *cosmetic*, and of **kosmos**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **put their hope in God**, and they **put themselves under their own husbands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hai elpizousai eis Theon** — the ones hoping in God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hupotassomenai tois idiois andrasin** — submitting themselves to their own husbands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hope in God first."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second clause follows from the first, and Peter does not present it the other way round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word *own*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Idiois** — their own."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to men generally. Peter's instruction is domestic and specific, and the qualifier is in the Greek every time he uses it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being addressed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wives in a mixed household. The paragraph opens *likewise you wives, be submissive to your own husbands, that **even if some do not obey the word**, they, without a word, may be won by the conduct of their wives*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A woman who has become a Christian and whose husband has not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that legal setting she had almost no standing. Peter's counsel is aimed at a real and dangerous position."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word *likewise*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Homoios** — in the same way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It refers back to the previous chapter, which was addressed to servants under harsh masters, and before that to Christ Himself: *when He was reviled, did not revile in return*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The category is not female. It is the pattern of the one who *committed Himself to Him who judges righteously*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is said to husbands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Likewise you husbands, **dwell with them with understanding**, giving honour to the wife… **as being heirs together of the grace of life, that your prayers may not be hindered**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sunkleronomois charitos zoes** — joint-heirs of the grace of life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word Paul uses of believers and Christ."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the sanction is that prayer stops working."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The example"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sarah did it — she did what Abraham said and **called him her master**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are **her daughters** when you do what is right and **will not let any scare rattle you**.* See the note on 1 Peter 3:6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Sarah actually did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Laughed at the announcement, and then denied it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Told Abraham to sleep with Hagar, and afterwards said *my wrong be upon you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one time she calls him **adoni** — my lord — is in the sentence where she is laughing to herself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter names her anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Will not let any scare rattle you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me phoboumenai medemian ptoesin** — not fearing any terror."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is a phrase from the Septuagint of Proverbs 3, and it is the note the paragraph ends on: not compliance, but fearlessness."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order.** Hope in God, then the household. Peter puts them in that sequence, and the paragraph ends on not being frightened rather than on being agreeable."}]}, "1 Peter 3:6": {"title": "\"Will not let any scare rattle you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter reaches back to Genesis for an example, and the sentence he ends on is about fear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The example"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sarah did it — she **did what Abraham said and called him her master**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kurion** — lord, master."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is quoting one word from one verse. Genesis 18: Sarah laughs behind the tent door and says to herself *after I have grown old, shall I have pleasure, **my lord** being old also?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is an aside, in her own head, and Peter builds on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she was doing when she said it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Laughing at the promise. And then denying she had laughed, and being told *no, but you did laugh*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter picks the sentence out of that scene."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who her daughters are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are **her daughters when you do what is right** and **will not let any scare rattle you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *doing good and not being afraid with any terror*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two conditions, and the second is the one with the weight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fear clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Will not let **any scare rattle you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me phoboumenai medemian ptoesin** — not fearing any intimidation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ptoesis** occurs once in the New Testament. It means a fluttering, a startling — the motion of a bird put up out of cover."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is not writing about courage in the abstract. He is writing to women in the Roman world, some of them married to unbelieving husbands, in an era when a wife's religion was supposed to be her husband's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before this address exactly that: *wives, likewise, be submissive to your own husbands, that **even if some do not obey the word, they, without a word, may be won by the conduct of their wives**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the husbands being right. The paragraph assumes some of them are not obeying the word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The adornment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not let your adornment be merely outward — arranging the hair, wearing gold, or putting on fine apparel — rather **let it be the hidden person of the heart**, with the incorruptible beauty of **a gentle and quiet spirit**, which is **very precious in the sight of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *very precious* is **poluteles** — of great cost. It is the word used of the alabaster flask of ointment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Husbands, likewise, dwell with them **with understanding**, giving honour to the wife… **that your prayers may not be hindered**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *according to knowledge*. And the sanction attached is about prayer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter says to everybody a few verses on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **do not be afraid of their threats, nor be troubled**. But sanctify the Lord God in your hearts.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes Isaiah 8 there, and the same fear-word runs through the letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for fear in this letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter uses it both ways. *Do not be afraid* of threats — and *conduct yourselves throughout the time of your sojourning here **in fear***, meaning reverence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not confuse them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the whole passage sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between instructions to servants under harsh masters and the passage about suffering for doing good, with Christ's example in the middle: *who, when He was reviled, did not revile in return.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What makes somebody Sarah's daughter, in Peter's account.** Doing right, and not letting a fright put you up out of cover — and he takes the example from a woman caught laughing at the promise."}]}, "1 Peter 3:7": {"title": "\"So that nothing gets in the way of your prayers\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse to husbands, and it ends with a consequence nobody expects."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Your turn\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter has just spent six verses on wives. This verse is the answering half, and it is shorter — but everything in it is a command and there is no let-out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Live with her thoughtfully"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Live with her **thoughtfully, with real understanding**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *according to knowledge* — **gnosis**. To dwell with her knowledgeably."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It assumes study. A man is expected to know this particular woman: what she is carrying, what she is like, what things cost her. Not women in general."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Give her honour\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Time** — the word for worth, value, price. It is the noun used of the price paid for something."}, {"k": "p", "t": "To honour her is to treat her as expensive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"As the weaker of the two\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the weaker vessel*, and **skeuos** is a vessel — a container, and in that world often a valuable one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is between two vessels. Both are vessels; one is described as weaker, and the instruction that follows is to handle it with more care, not less respect."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the reasoning you apply to the fine china rather than the tin mug."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The equality clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And as someone who comes into **the grace of life right alongside you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Greek: **sunkleronomos** — co-heir, joint inheritor. The same word Paul uses of believers being *joint heirs with Christ*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever *weaker vessel* means, Peter rules out the obvious misreading in the very next clause. She inherits everything he inherits, equally, and *alongside* is his word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*— **so that nothing gets in the way of your prayers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is to cut into, to hinder — it was used of breaking up a road to stop an army advancing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter attaches a man's prayer life to how he treats his wife, and puts it in the same sentence rather than a separate paragraph."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no equivalent warning to the wives in the passage. It is aimed at him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole passage is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is writing to scattered believers under pressure, and the household section runs servants, wives, husbands. Every part of it is about voluntary honour given where the world of that time gave none."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The husband is the one told his access to God is on the line."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul says in his version.** *Husbands, love your wives, just as Christ also loved the church and gave Himself for her.* Two apostles, two letters, and neither gives a husband anything to require — only things to give. See the note on Ephesians 5:22–24."}]}, "1 Peter 4:11": {"title": "Talk like a man handing on words", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two activities, two rules, and both rules are about the source."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The end of all things is at hand; therefore be serious and watchful in your prayers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And above all things have fervent love for one another… **be hospitable to one another without grumbling**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As each one has received a gift, minister it to one another, **as good stewards of the manifold grace of God**.* See the note on 1 Peter 4:7–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then this verse divides the gifts into two kinds."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you talk, **talk like a man handing on words that came from God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hos logia Theou** — as oracles of God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Logion** — a divine utterance. The word Stephen uses of what Moses received at Sinai — *the living oracles* — and Paul of what was entrusted to Israel: *to them were committed the oracles of God*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the rule requires"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that the speaker be eloquent, or right about everything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That what he says be handed on rather than generated. The image is of a courier, not an author."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you serve, **serve out of the strength God hands you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ex ischuos hes choregei ho Theos** — from the strength which God supplies."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Choregeo** is a rich word. In classical Athens the **choregos** was the wealthy citizen who paid for a chorus to be trained, costumed and staged — funding the whole production out of his own pocket."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb came to mean: to bear the full cost of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the two categories"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Speaking and serving. Peter divides all Christian activity into words and work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are given the same shape: the resource comes from somewhere else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That way **God gets the glory in all of it, through Jesus Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hina en pasin doxazetai ho Theos**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the reason the sources are specified. If the words are handed on and the strength is supplied, nobody in the transaction has anything to claim."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The doxology"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Praise and power belong to Him, **always and always**. Amen.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eis tous aionas ton aionon** — to the ages of the ages."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter breaks into doxology in the middle of the letter. He does it again at the very end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Beloved, **do not think it strange concerning the fiery trial which is to try you**, as though some strange thing happened to you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But rejoice to the extent that you partake of Christ's sufferings.* See the note on 1 Peter 4:12–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction on speaking and serving is followed straight into a paragraph about suffering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is writing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A fisherman who denied Him three times and was told *when you have returned to Me, strengthen your brethren*. See the note on Luke 22:31–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter is written to believers scattered across five Roman provinces, under pressure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the same thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **what do you have that you did not receive?** Now if you did indeed receive it, why do you boast as if you had not received it?* See the note on 1 Corinthians 4:6–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other place the strength word appears"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now may **He who supplies seed to the sower**… supply and multiply the seed you have sown.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb, **epichoregeo**, in Paul's chapter on giving. See the note on 2 Corinthians 9:8–9."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word.** **Choregeo** — what the Athenian patron did who paid for the entire production. The strength to serve is described as somebody else's outlay."}]}, "1 Peter 4:15": {"title": "The kind of suffering that is your own fault", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter is writing to Christians under real persecution, and he stops to draw one distinction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just make sure none of you is suffering for being **a murderer, a thief, a crook, or someone who cannot stay out of other people's business**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three crimes and then a fourth item that is not a crime at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind the last one is **allotriepiskopos** — a word that appears nowhere else in Greek literature, and Peter may have coined it. It is built from *belonging to another* and *overseer*: a person who supervises what is not theirs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is on that list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts a busybody beside a murderer and a thief. It is startling, and it is not a slip — the sentence is short and every item in it was chosen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Interfering in another person's affairs produces suffering, and Peter classes the suffering it produces with the suffering a thief brings on himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is separating"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *if you are insulted for the name of Christ, you are blessed.* The verse after: *but if anyone suffers as a Christian, let him not be ashamed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "So there are two kinds of trouble a believer can be in, and Peter will not let them be confused. One is because of Christ. The other is because of you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And a third kind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither category covers Job."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was not suffering for the name, and he was not suffering for anything he had done — chapter 1 says so and God says so out loud. His friends spent twenty-five chapters insisting it had to be the second kind, because their system had no room for anything else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's distinction is real and it does not exhaust the possibilities. A man can be in the ashes for neither reason."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter tells them to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let those who suffer according to the will of God **commit their souls to Him in doing good, as to a faithful Creator**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *commit* is a banking word — to deposit something with someone for safekeeping. It is the verb Jesus used on the cross: *into Your hands I commit My spirit.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The name he gives God in that sentence.** *A faithful Creator* — the only place in the New Testament God is called that. Peter reaches for the word for the One who made the thing, writing to people whose lives are being taken apart."}]}, "1 Peter 5:10": {"title": "\"Your little while\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four verbs at the end of the sentence, and every one of them is God's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the God all grace comes from**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the God of all grace*. Peter has used the word grace throughout the letter — *the grace that was to come to you*, *stewards of the manifold grace of God*, *God gives grace to the humble*, four verses back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names Him by it here, at the point where he is describing suffering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The calling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The One who **called us into His forever glory** through Christ Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The destination is stated before the trouble is mentioned. Glory, and it is called **eternal** — set against the *little while* in the next clause."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Through Christ Jesus.** Peter puts the route in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After you have suffered **your little while**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **oligon** — a little, a small amount. It can mean brief or slight; here it is set against *eternal*, so the sense is duration."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter does not say it is small in itself. He says it is small next to the other thing in the sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has said it before in the letter: *though now for **a little while**, if need be, you have been grieved by various trials.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Will **put you back together**, **hold you up**, **make you strong**, and **set you on rock**.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Katartizo** — to mend, to repair, to set a bone, to refit a ship. It is the word used of the disciples *mending their nets*, and of restoring a fallen brother in Galatians.", "**Sterizo** — to fix firmly, to establish. It is the word Jesus used to Peter: *when you have turned back, **strengthen** your brothers.* See the note on Luke 22:31–34.", "**Sthenoo** — to make strong, to fill with strength. It occurs once in the New Testament, here.", "**Themelioo** — to lay a foundation, to ground."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four different repairs, and the tense is future indicative. Peter states them as facts, not possibilities."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "All four verbs have the same subject: **the God of all grace**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the sentence is asked of the reader. Verses 8 and 9 gave the instructions — be sober, watch, resist, stand. This verse has none."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What restoration means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first verb is the one used for a broken bone or a torn net. Peter assumes damage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not promise that nothing will break. He promises who does the mending."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The doxology"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To Him be the glory and the dominion forever and ever. Amen.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter finishes the argument and then stops arguing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is writing it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who had collapsed completely and been put back together on a beach over breakfast. He knows the word for mending nets because he had mended them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of the sentence.** Grace first, glory second, the suffering third and called little, and then four things God does. The suffering is the shortest item in the verse."}]}, "1 Peter 5:2": {"title": "The ones right there with you", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter's instruction to elders, and every clause has a *not this, but that* in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The elders who are among you I exhort, **I who am a fellow elder** and a witness of the sufferings of Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sumpresbuteros** — co-elder. He puts himself alongside them, not above."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man traditionally regarded as first among the apostles introduces himself sideways."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look after **God's sheep**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **poimanate to poimnion tou theou** — shepherd the flock of God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whose flock is stated first. The verb is the one Jesus used on the beach: *tend My sheep*. See the note on John 21:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is passing on an instruction he received personally, after failing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The qualifier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ones **right there with you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **to en humin** — the flock among you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not sheep in general. The ones physically present."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Watch over them **because you want to, not because somebody is forcing your hand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me anagkastos alla hekousios** — not by compulsion but willingly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not because there is **money in it you'd be ashamed of**, but because **you are eager to**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **aischrokerdos** — for shameful gain. And **prothumos**, eagerly, with forwardness of mind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same disqualification Jethro named for judges: *men who hate dishonest gain*. See the note on Exodus 18:19–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Paul's list for elders: *not greedy for money*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *nor as being lords over those entrusted to you, but **being examples to the flock**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katakurieuontes** — lording it over. The word Jesus used of the Gentile rulers: *you know that the rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them… yet it shall not be so among you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What sheep need"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not driving. Eastern shepherding worked by leading — the shepherd walked in front and the flock followed his voice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He calls his own sheep by name and leads them out… and the sheep follow him, for they know his voice.* See the note on John 10:1–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The negative Peter keeps in view"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel's indictment: *woe to the shepherds of Israel who only feed themselves. Should not shepherds feed the flock?… You have not strengthened the weak or healed the sick.* See the note on Ezekiel 34:1–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's three pairs are the positive form of Ezekiel's list."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reward"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when the Chief Shepherd appears, you will receive **the crown of glory that does not fade away**.* See the note on 1 Peter 5:4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Likewise you younger people, submit yourselves to your elders. Yes, all of you be submissive to one another, and **be clothed with humility**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *clothed* is **egkombosasthai** — to tie on a garment with knots, specifically the apron a slave tied over his clothes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter had watched somebody do that with a towel. See the note on John 13:4–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line after it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**God resists the proud, but gives grace to the humble**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore **humble yourselves under the mighty hand of God**, that He may exalt you in due time, **casting all your care upon Him, for He cares for you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epiripsantes** — having thrown it on Him. A participle attached to the humbling: the way you humble yourself is by throwing the worry over."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the instruction is framed.** Three times, *not this, but that*. Not forced, not for money, not lording it — and Peter signs himself as a fellow elder."}]}, "1 Peter 5:4": {"title": "The wreath that never wilts", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The elders' pay, and it is deferred."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When **the Shepherd over all the shepherds** shows up.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **archipoimenos** — chief shepherd. It occurs once in the New Testament, and Peter coined it or close to it; it turns up in a papyrus as a job title for the head herdsman of an estate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The elders are shepherds. Above them is one more."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three shepherd titles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am **the good shepherd**. The good shepherd gives His life for the sheep.* See the note on John 10:11–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **great Shepherd** of the sheep*, in Hebrews, *brought up from the dead*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **Chief Shepherd***, here, *when He appears*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Good, at the cross. Great, at the resurrection. Chief, at the return."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The appearing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phanerothentos** — when He is manifested, brought into the open."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb John uses twice in one sentence: *when He does come out into the open, we will be like Him*. See the note on 1 John 3:2–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for crown"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will be handed **the wreath of glory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **stephanon** — the victor's wreath at the games, or the garland at a feast. Not **diadema**, the royal crown."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the winner's wreath, made of wild olive or laurel, and it was worth nothing except as proof."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The adjective"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The one that **never wilts**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **amarantinon** — unfading. From the flower **amaranth**, which was proverbial in the ancient world for not withering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An athlete's wreath was dead within days. Peter picks the one flower that does not do that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the same root earlier in the letter, of the inheritance: *incorruptible and undefiled and that **does not fade away**, reserved in heaven for you*. See the note on 1 Peter 1:23."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the same wreath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And everyone who competes for the prize is temperate in all things. Now they do it to obtain **a perishable crown**, but we for an imperishable crown.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at the end: *there is laid up for me the **crown of righteousness**, which the Lord, the righteous Judge, will give to me on that Day*. See the note on 2 Timothy 4:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the timing does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter has just told elders not to serve for money. See the note on 1 Peter 5:2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The payment named in verse 4 is one nobody collects in this life, which is the point of naming it immediately after the warning about shameful gain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the elders were facing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter is written to churches under pressure. *Beloved, do not think it strange concerning the fiery trial which is to try you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the paragraph ends: *may the God of all grace, who called us to His eternal glory by Christ Jesus, **after you have suffered a while, perfect, establish, strengthen, and settle you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verbs, all of them things done to a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the wreaths end up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The twenty-four elders fall down before Him who sits on the throne… and **cast their crowns before the throne**, saying, \"You are worthy, O Lord.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word — **stephanous**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elders, and crowns, and they put them on the floor."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word Peter picked.** *Amarantinos*, from the flower that does not wither — said to men who had just been told not to do the job for money."}]}, "1 Peter 5:6": {"title": "Get low", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, with two hands in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **get low under God's strong hand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tapeinothete oun hupo ten krataian cheira tou Theou**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tapeinoo** — to make low, to humble. And it is passive: *be humbled*, or *let yourself be brought low*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Krataia cheir** — the mighty hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Septuagint's standard phrase for the exodus: *the LORD brought you out of Egypt with a mighty hand and an outstretched arm*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hand that is above them is the one that got a nation out of slavery."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why *under*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *before*, or *toward*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The position is beneath, which is where a hand can either press or lift."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Likewise you younger people, submit yourselves to your elders. Yes, all of you be submissive to one another, and **be clothed with humility**, for \"God resists the proud, but gives grace to the humble.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs 3:34, quoted in the Septuagint's wording — the same verse James quotes. See the note on James 4:4–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for clothed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Enkomboomai** — to tie on, as a slave tied an apron over his clothes for work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter had watched somebody do exactly that in an upper room, with a towel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He will lift you up**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hupsose** — will exalt, will raise high."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The command is to go down. The lifting is somebody else's action, and Peter does not make it conditional on anything except the going down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When **the moment is right**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **en kairo** — in due time, at the right season."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kairos** — the appointed moment, not measured duration."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter does not say how long. He says there is a moment and it is not the reader's to schedule."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is writing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who said *even if I have to die with You, I will not deny You*, and did."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And who was told *when you are old, you will stretch out your hands, and another will gird you and carry you where you do not wish*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Casting all your care upon Him, **for He cares for you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epirripsantes** — having thrown upon. The verb used of the disciples throwing their garments on the colt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The participle attaches to the humbling: getting low and throwing the anxiety over are one action, not two."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be sober, be vigilant; because **your adversary the devil walks about like a roaring lion**, seeking whom he may devour.* See the note on 1 Peter 5:8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Resist him, steadfast in the faith, knowing that the same sufferings are experienced by your brotherhood in the world.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last promise in the letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But may **the God of all grace**, who called us to His eternal glory by Christ Jesus, after you have suffered a while, **perfect, establish, strengthen, and settle you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verbs, and *after you have suffered a while* is inside the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **whoever exalts himself will be humbled, and he who humbles himself will be exalted**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both verbs passive, in Luke's account of the dinner-party seating. See the note on Luke 14:7–11."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The direction.** The instruction is to go down. The lifting is in the passive, and the timing is **kairos** — a moment, not a schedule."}]}, "1 Peter 5:8": {"title": "A lion's roar in his throat", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two short commands and then a description."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep your head clear. **Stay awake**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **nepho** — to be sober, unintoxicated, clear-minded — and **gregoreo**, to be awake, to keep watch."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are single words in the Greek and both are imperatives. Nothing about fighting yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is writing it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who was told *watch and pray* in Gethsemane and fell asleep three times, and who was told at the supper table that Satan had asked for him. See the note on Luke 22:31–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the same verb here that Jesus used on him in the garden."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The enemy named"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have an enemy — **the devil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **antidikos**, adversary — a legal word, the opposing party in a lawsuit. And **diabolos**, the slanderer, the one who throws accusations across."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both words come out of a courtroom rather than a battlefield."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is out **walking around**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **peripateo** — going about. It is what the accuser says he has been doing in Job: *from going to and fro on the earth, and from walking back and forth on it.* See the note on Job 1:6–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The roar"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **a lion's roar in his throat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *as a roaring lion*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A lion that roars is a lion announcing itself. Roaring is what a lion does when it is not currently in a position to bite — the sound travels five miles, and it is meant to be heard."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is *as*. Peter says he is **like** one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hunting for **somebody to eat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *seeking whom he may devour*. **Seeking** — he is looking, which means he has not found."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who else is called a lion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **Lion of the tribe of Judah**, the Root of David, has prevailed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Revelation's answer to the announcement: John turns to look at the Lion and sees *a Lamb as though it had been slain.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter says to do about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not run and not argue. The next verse: *stand up to him. **Plant your feet in the faith**.* See the note on 1 Peter 5:9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Resist, in the Greek, is **anthistemi** — to set yourself against, to hold ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Cast **all your care** upon Him, for He cares for you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter puts the anxiety verse and the lion verse back to back, in that order. The watchfulness he asks for is not the same thing as worry, and he removes the worry first."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is being described.** A prowler, making noise, looking for somebody who is not paying attention. Peter's instruction is to be sober and awake — and then, in the next breath, to stand still."}]}, "1 Peter 5:9": {"title": "\"Plant your feet in the faith\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The answer to the lion in the verse before, and it is a standing instruction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Stand up to him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anthistemi** — from *anti*, against, and *histemi*, to stand. To set yourself opposite something and not move."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James has the same word and adds the outcome: *resist the devil and he will flee from you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in either verse is about advancing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the feet go"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Plant your feet **in the faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *steadfast in the faith* — **stereos**, solid, hard, firm. It is used of solid food as against milk."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ground is named. Not in resolve, not in experience. In the faith."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows the resisting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter does not describe a fight. He gives a piece of information instead, and it is about other people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The knowledge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And know this: **your brothers and sisters all over the world are being put through the very same suffering**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the same sufferings are being accomplished in your brotherhood in the world*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is writing to scattered believers in five Roman provinces who cannot see each other. The isolation is the point — each congregation thinks it is the only one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them what is happening elsewhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The very same\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ta auta** — the same things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not similar. The same."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Right to the end of it\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek phrase carries the sense of being completed, brought to its full measure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is a limit and it is being reached — which is the argument the next verse takes up: *after you have suffered **your little while**.* See the note on 1 Peter 5:10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for brotherhood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Adelphotes** — the brotherhood, as a single body. Peter uses it twice in this letter and it appears nowhere else in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter has already told them about suffering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Beloved, **do not think it strange** concerning the fiery trial which is to try you, **as though some strange thing happened to you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same instruction, earlier in the letter. The strangeness is what he keeps removing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Peter learned to stand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been sifted, and had been told in advance that he would be, and had been told *when you have turned back, strengthen your brothers.* See the note on Luke 22:31–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This letter is that assignment being carried out."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is offered against the lion.** Solid ground, and the knowledge that the same thing is happening to everybody else. Peter thought the second one was worth putting in the same sentence as the first."}]}, "1 Samuel 1:9–18": {"title": "Her face was no longer downcast", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "She goes in with nothing and comes out with nothing, and something has changed anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where she is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After they had eaten and drunk **at Shiloh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The annual pilgrimage. Shiloh is where the tabernacle stood before Jerusalem — the tent, in one place, for something like three centuries."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Eli the priest was **sitting on his chair beside the doorpost** of the LORD's temple.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sitting. He is old, his sons are corrupt, and he will die falling backwards off this same seat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The state"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hannah was in anguish — **a bitter, aching soul** — and she prayed to the LORD and **wept and wept**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *bitter of soul* — **marat nephesh**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has already explained why. Peninnah had children and Hannah had none, and the rival *provoked her severely, to make her miserable* — *year by year*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the note the narrator adds twice: *the LORD had closed her womb*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"O **LORD of Heaven's Armies**, if only You will **look — really look — at the misery of Your servant**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *if You will indeed look on the affliction* — the verb doubled for emphasis."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the first time in Scripture anyone addresses God as **YHWH Tsevaoth**, the LORD of hosts. A childless woman coins the title."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she asks and what she promises"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Remember me and not forget me**, if only You will **give Your servant a son**, then I will **give him back to You**, LORD, for all the days of his life. And **no razor will ever touch his head**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Remember, and not forget — the same request twice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The razor clause is the Nazirite vow. She is not asking for a child to keep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The misreading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She kept on praying before the LORD, and **Eli watched her mouth**. Hannah was **praying in her heart** — her lips were moving, but **no sound came out**. So **Eli thought she was drunk**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priest at the door of the house of God cannot tell prayer from drunkenness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Silent prayer is unusual enough in the Old Testament that he has no category for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The accusation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How long are you going to stay drunk? Put away your wine**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No, my lord**. I am a woman with **a heavy, breaking spirit**. I haven't been drinking wine or beer. I've been **pouring out my soul to the LORD**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I have poured out my soul before the LORD* — the verb used of pouring a drink offering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Please **don't take me for a worthless woman** — I've been praying here out of my great grief and pain.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *daughter of Belial*. It is the exact phrase the narrator has been using of Eli's own sons two chapters running: *the sons of Eli were corrupt* — sons of Belial."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Eli said, \"**Go in peace. And may the God of Israel grant what you've asked of Him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not apologise. He blesses her, and the blessing is generic — he still does not know what she asked for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What changed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"May your servant **find favour in your eyes**,\" she said. And she went on her way, and **she ate something**, and **her face was no longer downcast**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing has happened. She is as childless walking out as walking in, and the priest's blessing contained no promise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter says the conception happens later: *and the LORD remembered her.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She eats first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Samuel** — *heard by God*, or *asked of God*. She explains it: *because I have asked for him from the LORD*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She weans him, walks him back to Shiloh, and hands him to the man who thought she was drunk."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The song"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her prayer in chapter 2 — *my heart rejoices in the LORD… He raises the poor from the dust and lifts the beggar from the ash heap* — is the model for Mary's Magnificat. See the note on Luke 1:46–50."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two women, a thousand years apart, singing about God overturning the order of things."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What she left with.** The same empty arms and a different face. The narrator puts the eating and the face before the answer, not after it."}]}, "1 Samuel 10:7–8": {"title": "Wait seven days", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses of instruction, and everything that goes wrong later goes wrong here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel has poured oil on Saul's head privately and kissed him: *is it not because the LORD has anointed you commander over His inheritance?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then three signs, given in advance: two men at Rachel's tomb with news about the donkeys, three men at the terebinth tree of Tabor with bread, and a procession of prophets coming down from the high place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the Spirit of the LORD will come upon you, and you will prophesy with them and **be turned into another man**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Once these signs come to pass, **do whatever your hand finds to do, for God is with you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wide latitude. No brief, no list."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *do for yourself what your hand finds*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then **go down to Gilgal ahead of me**. I'll come to you there to offer burnt offerings and fellowship offerings.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A specific place, and a specific division of labour. Samuel does the offering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Wait seven days until I arrive** and tell you what to do.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shivat yamim tochel** — seven days you shall wait."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two commands in one sentence: wait the seven days, and then receive instructions."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The waiting is not the whole of it. The waiting is so that the instructions can arrive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is odd about the placement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This instruction is given at the anointing, years before the crisis it applies to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some readers take it as a standing arrangement for any future emergency; others as a specific appointment. Either way, Saul remembers it — the narrative in chapter 13 shows he was counting days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens in chapter 13"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty thousand Philistine chariots at Michmash. The army hiding in caves. Men slipping across the Jordan."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He waited seven days, according to the time set by Samuel. But Samuel did not come to Gilgal, and the people were scattered from him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He waits the full seven. Then he offers the burnt offering himself, and *as soon as he had finished presenting the burnt offering, Samuel came*. See the note on 1 Samuel 13:11–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he missed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the seventh day. He missed the second half of the instruction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wait until I come and show you what you shall do.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He kept the count and did not wait for the content. Whatever Samuel arrived to say, Saul never heard it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You have not kept the command the LORD your God gave you. **If you had, He would have established your kingdom over Israel for all time**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A dynasty, against a few minutes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Saul says for himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I saw the men scattering, and you didn't come when you said you would, and the Philistines were massing… so **I forced myself** to do it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every clause is factually correct, and he says out loud that he made himself do it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction that is easy to miss"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do whatever your hand finds to do, for God is with you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was given enormous freedom in verse 7 and one fixed point in verse 8, and the one fixed point is the one he broke."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The half he did not keep.** He waited the seven days. The instruction was *wait until I come and tell you what to do* — and the telling never happened."}]}, "1 Samuel 13:11–14": {"title": "I forced myself", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Saul loses the kingdom over a matter of minutes, and every reason he gives is true."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty thousand chariots at Michmash, the army hiding in caves and cisterns, and men slipping across the Jordan. See the note on 1 Samuel 13:5–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel had said wait seven days."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He waited seven days, according to the time set by Samuel. But Samuel did not come to Gilgal, and the people were scattered from him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He waits the full seven, and then offers the burnt offering himself. And *as soon as he had finished presenting the burnt offering, Samuel came*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What have you done?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same question God asked Eve, and Cain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three reasons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I saw the **men scattering**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **you didn't come when you said you would**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **the Philistines were massing at Michmash**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one is accurate. The army was deserting, Samuel was late by Saul's reckoning, and the enemy was three miles away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reasoning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I thought, 'The Philistines will come down on me here at Gilgal, and **I haven't sought the LORD's favor**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He frames it as piety. The thing he is worried about not having done is the religious observance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The admission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **I forced myself to do it** — I offered the burnt offering.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **va'et'appaq** — I compelled myself, I restrained myself and then broke."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the most honest sentence in the passage. He knew, and did it anyway, and says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You've acted foolishly**,\" Samuel said. \"**You have not kept the command** the LORD your God gave you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **niskalta** — you have been foolish."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If you had**, He would have **established your kingdom over Israel for all time**. But now **your kingdom will not endure**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A dynasty, lost over seven days and a few minutes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The successor"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The LORD has **sought out a man after His own heart** and appointed him ruler over His people.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David is not named. He is a boy with sheep at this point, and Samuel will not anoint him for some chapters yet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes this line in his sermon at Antioch. See the note on Acts 13:20–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason restated"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Because you did not keep what the LORD commanded you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel says it twice, at the start and the finish of the verdict."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was actually wrong"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that a king offered a sacrifice — David does, and Solomon does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The command was specific and to him: *wait seven days, till I come to you and show you what you should do*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was told to wait for instructions, and he acted before they arrived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It happens again in chapter 15, over Amalek, and the excuse is the same shape: *the people spared the best of the sheep and the oxen, to sacrifice to the LORD your God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Samuel's answer: *has the LORD as great delight in burnt offerings and sacrifices as in obeying the voice of the LORD? Behold, **to obey is better than sacrifice***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both times the disobedience is dressed as worship."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Saul says at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have sinned; yet **honour me now, please, before the elders of my people** and before Israel.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The concern is the audience."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David, confronted over something far worse: *against You, You only, have I sinned*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The most honest thing he says.** *I forced myself.* He knew what he was doing, waited the seven days, and moved a few minutes before the prophet walked in."}]}, "1 Samuel 13:15–18": {"title": "Six hundred left", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Samuel walks away, and the chapter starts counting what is left."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The departure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **Samuel got up and left Gilgal and went on his way**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He arrived just after the burnt offering, delivered the verdict, and left. See the note on 1 Samuel 13:11–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No farewell is recorded. He does not stay to help."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The remnant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Saul counted the men still with him — **about six hundred**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opened with three thousand chosen men, and before that the muster after the Ammonite crisis had been in the hundreds of thousands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six hundred."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest are in caves, thickets, rocks, pits and cisterns, or across the Jordan. See the note on 1 Samuel 13:5–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six hundred is the standing figure for a small band in this narrative."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David's men, in the wilderness, settle at six hundred too. See the note on 1 Samuel 22:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The position"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Saul and his son Jonathan and their men **stayed at Gibeah of Benjamin**, while the Philistines **camped at Michmash**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two hills with a gorge between them — the Wadi Suweinit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That gorge is where Jonathan climbs. See the note on 1 Samuel 14:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The raiders"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Raiding parties went out from the Philistine camp **in three companies**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hammashchith** — the destroyer, the spoiler."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One turned toward **Ophrah** in the land of Shual, another toward **Beth-Horon**, and the third toward **the border overlooking the Valley of Zeboim**, out toward the wilderness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "North, west, and east."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the three directions mean"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not attacking Saul. They are stripping the countryside in three directions while he sits on a hill with six hundred men and two swords."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The occupation is being administered, not fought."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not happening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul has an army, a position and a son, and the narrative records only that he stayed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next paragraph explains the equipment situation — no blacksmith, no swords. See the note on 1 Samuel 13:19–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between them, the two paragraphs make the case for doing nothing look overwhelming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What one man does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One day Jonathan, Saul's son, said to his young armour-bearer, \"**Come on — let's cross over**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *he didn't tell his father*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Saul is when it happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the outskirts of Gibeah **under a pomegranate tree** at Migron, with about six hundred men.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator supplies the tree."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Setting the arithmetic."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three thousand down to six hundred. Two swords. Three raiding parties loose in the country. Samuel gone, and the kingdom already declared forfeit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in chapter 14 happens against that page, and the narrator wrote it first on purpose."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter counts.** Men lost, weapons absent, and enemy columns out in three directions — and then Samuel walks away without being asked to stay."}]}, "1 Samuel 13:19–23": {"title": "Not a single blacksmith", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Five verses of economics, and they explain why the next chapter is remarkable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The policy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now there was **not a single blacksmith to be found anywhere in Israel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **charash** — a craftsman in metal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because the Philistines had said, \"**Otherwise the Hebrews will make swords or spears**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Disarmament by controlling the trade. Not confiscating weapons — removing the ability to make them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Philistines had iron and Israel did not. The archaeology supports it: iron working spread along the coast before it reached the hill country."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dependency"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **all Israel had to go down to the Philistines** to get their plowshares, mattocks, axes, and sickles sharpened.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Farm tools. Not weapons — the tools a man needs to eat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every farmer in the country had to walk into enemy territory once a season and pay for the privilege."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prices"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The charge was **two-thirds of a shekel** for plowshares and mattocks, and **a third of a shekel** for sharpening forks and axes and for **fixing an ox-goad**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse is famously difficult in Hebrew and the figures are reconstructions, but the shape is clear: a price list, set by the occupying power."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is on the list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An ox goad."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A long pole with a metal point, for driving cattle. Shamgar killed six hundred Philistines with one. See the note on Judges 3:31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tool named in the price list is the one a judge had used as a weapon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tally"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So on the day of battle, **not a soldier with Saul and Jonathan had a sword or spear in his hand — only Saul and his son Jonathan carried them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two swords in the national army."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six hundred men, and two blades between them. The rest have farm implements."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the passage is here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It sits between Saul's disqualification and Jonathan's raid."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter before had the army hiding in caves, thickets, rocks, pits and cisterns. See the note on 1 Samuel 13:5–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter after has two men climbing a cliff."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This paragraph is the arithmetic in between, and it is set out so that nothing in chapter 14 can be attributed to equipment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **a detachment of Philistines had gone out to the pass at Michmash**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A garrison, in a defile, above a gorge."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what Jonathan and his armour-bearer climb up to the next morning. See the note on 1 Samuel 14:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the passage does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That God had abandoned them, or that the situation was hopeless."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It reports the industrial position of a small nation under occupation, accurately, and moves on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the imagery is reversed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They shall **beat their swords into plowshares**, and their spears into pruning hooks.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah and Micah, both. The metal moves the other way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joel reverses it again for a war oracle: *beat your plowshares into swords*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel in this chapter has neither, and has to pay a foreign smith to sharpen the plough."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is on the Philistine price list.** An ox goad — the tool a judge had once killed six hundred of them with. The occupying power is charging Israel to keep it sharp."}]}, "1 Samuel 13:5–7": {"title": "Trembling at Gilgal", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The army before the battle, and the account is entirely about where people went to hide."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The odds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Thirty thousand chariots, six thousand horsemen, and troops **as countless as the sand on the seashore**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chariot figure is very large and some manuscripts read three thousand. Either way the comparison is the standard Hebrew one for a number nobody counts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Israel had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter says a few verses later: *there was no blacksmith to be found throughout all the land of Israel, for the Philistines said, \"Lest the Hebrews make swords or spears.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had to go down to the Philistines to sharpen their ploughshares."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So it came about, on the day of battle, that **there was neither sword nor spear found in the hand of any of the people** — but only with Saul and Jonathan his son.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two swords in the whole army."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They came up and camped at **Michmash**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A pass with a steep gorge, about seven miles north of Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the men did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the men of Israel saw how desperate their situation was, they **scrambled to hide in caves and thickets, among the rocks, in pits and cisterns**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five hiding places listed. The Hebrew piles them up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for their situation is **tsar** — pressed, narrow, in a tight place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The deserters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Some Hebrews even **slipped across the Jordan into Gad and Gilead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Over the river, out of the country."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The king"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **Saul stayed at Gilgal**, and his troops with him were **trembling with fear**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **charedu** — they trembled, they were terrified."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul stays where he was told to. Samuel had said *wait seven days, till I come to you and show you what you should do*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He waits seven days, the people start slipping away from him, and on the seventh day — before the day is out — he offers the burnt offering himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it happened, as soon as he had finished presenting the burnt offering, that Samuel came.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Minutes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Saul's explanation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When I saw that **the people were scattered from me**, and that you did not come within the days appointed, and that the Philistines gathered together at Michmash, then I said, 'The Philistines will now come down on me at Gilgal.'… **So I felt compelled, and offered a burnt offering**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three reasons, all of them factually correct."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I forced myself*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You have done foolishly. You have not kept the commandment of the LORD your God… **now your kingdom shall not continue. The LORD has sought for Himself a man after His own heart**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What one man does about the odds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jonathan, in the next chapter, with his armour-bearer and no army:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Come, let us go over to the garrison of these uncircumcised; **it may be that the LORD will work for us. For nothing restrains the LORD from saving by many or by few**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They climb the gorge on hands and feet and kill twenty men, and the panic spreads through the Philistine camp — *and the earth quaked; so it was a very great trembling*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word as verse 7. The trembling changes sides."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Gilgal is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Where the twelve stones from the Jordan were set up. See the note on Joshua 4:19–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul is standing on the site of a memorial to a crossing that did not need an army, waiting for a prophet."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter counts.** Not soldiers. Caves, thickets, rocks, pits and cisterns — and two swords in the entire nation."}]}, "1 Samuel 14:1–5": {"title": "He did not tell his father", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two men set out, and the narrator gives the terrain in detail because the terrain is the story."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proposal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Come on — let's cross over to the Philistine outpost on the other side**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said by Jonathan to his armour-bearer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An armour-bearer is a young man who carries the shield and the spare weapons. He is staff, not an equal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The omission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **he didn't tell his father**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew states it plainly and the narrator does not comment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whether it is initiative or insubordination is left open. Saul is the king and the commander."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Saul is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Staying **on the outskirts of Gibeah under a pomegranate tree at Migron**, with about six hundred men.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under a tree, at the edge, with the whole army."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The contrast with the two men on the cliff is not stated and does not need to be."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is with him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*(**Ahijah** was there wearing the ephod — the son of Ahitub, **Ichabod's brother**, son of Phinehas, son of Eli, the LORD's priest at Shiloh.)*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four generations, in one parenthesis."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ichabod** — *the glory has departed*. Named by his mother as she died in childbirth on the day the ark was captured and Eli fell backwards off his seat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is that family. The priesthood under judgment, still functioning, wearing the ephod, at the king's elbow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul consults him later in the chapter, and then tells him to stop mid-enquiry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ignorance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No one knew Jonathan had gone.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crossing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now to reach the Philistine outpost, Jonathan had to **pass between two rocky crags**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One was called **Bozez**, the other **Seneh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew names are usually taken as *shining* or *slippery*, and *thorny*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator gives them because somebody knew them. This is local geography, remembered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The orientation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One cliff stood **to the north facing Michmash**, the other **to the south facing Geba**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Wadi Suweinit runs between them — a steep gorge with cliffs on both sides."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The garrison is on top of the northern one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the passage establishes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The route is a climb, up a gorge, into a defended position, by two men."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever happens next cannot be attributed to numbers, to terrain, or to equipment — the previous paragraph established that there were two swords in the whole army. See the note on 1 Samuel 13:19–23."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jonathan says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Maybe the LORD will act for us. **Nothing can stop the LORD from saving, whether by many or by few**.\"* See the note on 1 Samuel 14:6–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Jonathan is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king's son and heir."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who later strips off his own robe, armour, sword, bow and belt and gives them to David — the boy who will take the throne he was born to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Jonathan loved him as his own soul.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says on that occasion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You shall be king over Israel, and I shall be next to you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He knew."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clause the narrator leaves alone.** *He did not tell his father.* Saul is under a tree with six hundred men and a priest, and his son goes over the ridge with one servant."}]}, "1 Samuel 14:11–15": {"title": "On his hands and feet", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two men climb a cliff, and by the end of the paragraph a whole army is running."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The showing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the two of them **let the Philistine garrison see them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deliberately, as agreed. See the note on 1 Samuel 14:6–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The taunt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look! **The Hebrews are crawling out of the holes they've been hiding in!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Accurate. The previous chapter had the army hiding in caves, thickets, rocks, pits and cisterns. See the note on 1 Samuel 13:5–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Philistines know exactly what the situation is, and are enjoying it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The invitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Come on up here — we've got something to teach you!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *and we will show you a thing*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Contempt. They are not forming up; they are calling two men up a cliff for sport."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is the exact wording Jonathan had set as his sign."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Climb up behind me — the LORD has handed them over to Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Perfect tense. Has handed over, before anything has happened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The *maybe* of verse 6 is gone. He had said he would take that answer as the sign, and he does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The climb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jonathan **scrambled up on his hands and feet** with his armour-bearer right behind.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *on his hands and on his feet*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both hands occupied. No shield, no weapon ready, and a garrison at the top watching him come."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the most defenceless a man can be in a fight, and it is the only route."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fighting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Enemies fell before Jonathan, and **his armour-bearer finished them off behind him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two-man work, and the division of labour is stated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The count"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In that first strike Jonathan and his armour-bearer struck down **about twenty men in an area of about half an acre**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is difficult — *within about half a furrow of an acre of land*, an agricultural measure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty men, in a space you could walk across."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The collapse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **terror swept through the whole camp — the field, the garrison, the raiders — the ground shook**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **charadah** — trembling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same root as chapter 13, where Saul's troops *were trembling*. See the note on 1 Samuel 13:5–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The trembling changes sides."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was **a panic sent by God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **cherdat Elohim** — a trembling of God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator names the cause. Twenty men fell to two; the rest is not attributed to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Saul does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His watchmen see the enemy camp *melting away and going here and there*, and he calls a roll to find out who is missing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he starts to consult the priest, and interrupts him: *withdraw your hand* — because the noise is increasing and there is no time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king is asking for guidance about a battle his son started."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the LORD saved Israel that day, and the battle shifted to Beth Aven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the Israelites who had been hiding in the hill country came out and joined in, and the Hebrews who had defected to the Philistines changed sides again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everybody who had run comes back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Saul does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Puts the army under an oath not to eat until evening — *cursed is the man who eats any food until I have taken vengeance on my enemies*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jonathan, who did not hear it, eats honey and says: *my father has troubled the land*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oath nearly costs him his life, and the people refuse to let the king kill him: *shall Jonathan die, who has accomplished this great deliverance in Israel? Certainly not!*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How he went up.** On hands and feet, both hands occupied, no shield, with the garrison watching — and the invitation to do it had been an insult."}]}, "1 Samuel 14:6–10": {"title": "By many or by few", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The best sentence anybody says in the book of Samuel, and it has a *maybe* in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proposal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Come on, let's cross over to the outpost of **these uncircumcised men**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The insult marks them as outside the covenant, and it is how Israel referred to Philistines."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David uses the same word to Goliath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hesitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Maybe** the LORD will act for us.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ulai** — perhaps, it may be."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jonathan does not claim a promise. He has had no word, no prophet, and no instruction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says *perhaps*, and goes anyway."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the honest part, and it is why the sentence carries. He is not certain and he is not paralysed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conviction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Nothing can stop the LORD from saving, whether by many or by few**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *there is no restraint to the LORD to save by many or by few*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ma'tsor** — a restraint, a hindrance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What he is sure of is not the outcome. It is that the arithmetic is not a factor."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter before had established the arithmetic precisely: two swords in the army. See the note on 1 Samuel 13:19–23."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do whatever's in your heart**,\" his armour-bearer said. \"Go ahead — **I'm with you, heart and soul**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *behold, I am with you according to your heart*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An unnamed young man, whose job is carrying somebody else's equipment, agreeing to climb a cliff into a garrison."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gets one line in Scripture and it is that one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sign"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"We'll cross over toward them and **let them see us**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deliberately. They do not sneak."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If they say, '**Wait there till we come to you**,' then we'll stay put and not go up.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But if they say, '**Come on up to us**,' then we'll climb up — because that will be our sign that the LORD has handed them over to us.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sign is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two options, and Jonathan assigns the meanings before either happens."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It reads like a fleece, and it is not quite one. Both outcomes are plausible; he is not asking for anything improbable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What he has done is commit himself in advance to whichever answer comes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why that reading is better than the alternative"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If they are told to wait, the garrison is coming down to level ground and the two of them are finished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "If they are told to come up, they climb a cliff face into a defended position, on hands and feet, with no cover."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jonathan has chosen the *worse* military option as his sign to advance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Come on up here — **we've got something to teach you**!\"* See the note on 1 Samuel 14:11–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same principle turns up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The LORD does not save with sword and spear; for **the battle is the LORD's**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David to Goliath, in the same book, with a sling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Gideon, told to send twenty-two thousand men home and then most of the remainder: *the people who are with you are too many*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Zechariah: *not by might nor by power, but by My Spirit*. See the note on Zechariah 4:6–10."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word that makes the sentence honest.** *Perhaps.* He has no promise and no prophet, and what he is certain of is only that the numbers are not the deciding factor."}]}, "1 Samuel 15:7–9": {"title": "What Saul kept", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Saul wins the battle and loses the kingdom, and the difference is a list of what he decided to keep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sorting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They kept the best of the sheep and cattle, the fat calves and lambs — everything that was good. These they were unwilling to destroy. But whatever was weak or worthless, they devoted to destruction.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He obeyed the command exactly, on everything he did not want."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator lays it out as a sorting exercise: good things kept, poor things destroyed. Obedience applied to the portion where obedience cost nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Agag"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took Agag, king of the Amalekites, alive.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king is the other thing kept. And that detail runs a long way — Haman, in the book of Esther, is called an Agagite, and he comes within a decree of destroying the Jewish people centuries later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What Saul kept alive turned up again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read on and it is the excuses that undo him. He tells Samuel he *has* performed the LORD's command; when the bleating of sheep gives him away, he says the people took them, and then that it was to sacrifice to the LORD."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel's answer is the line the chapter is remembered for: *to obey is better than sacrifice.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word for the sparing.** *They were unwilling* — the Hebrew is about not being willing, refusing. It is not that Saul lacked the opportunity or the means. Everything in the sentence turns on what he wanted to keep."}]}, "1 Samuel 16:1–3": {"title": "\"I'll show you what to do\" — guidance given one step at a time", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Samuel is sent on the most consequential errand of his life and is not told how it ends."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is given"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["go to Bethlehem", "to a man named Jesse", "I have already picked one of his sons"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, when Samuel raises a genuine danger — Saul will kill him if he hears — God does not rebuke the fear. He gives him something practical to do about it: take a heifer, say you have come to sacrifice, invite Jesse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is not given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A name. Samuel arrives knowing the family and not the son, which is why the scene that follows works the way it does — seven brothers paraded past, each one looking more like a king than the last, and the answer is a boy nobody thought to call in from the field."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I'll show you what to do.* The instruction is deliberately incomplete, and the missing piece is promised for when he gets there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence that opens it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How long are you going to grieve over Saul? I have set him aside.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It sounds harsh until you notice what it is not. Samuel's grief is real and God does not call it foolish — He calls time on it, and the reason is that a replacement has already been chosen. *I've already picked one of his sons.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the shape of the whole scene: the old arrangement has failed, the mourning is genuine, and the next thing is already decided before the prophet has filled his horn. Samuel is not being told to feel less. He is being told to fill the horn and go, because there is somewhere to go."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line that gets quoted from later in the chapter.** *People look at the outside; the LORD looks at the heart* — it arrives while Samuel is standing in front of the eldest brother, impressed. Even the prophet on the errand needed telling. The correction is not aimed at a crowd of shallow people; it is aimed at the man who had been sent, and who had already made up his mind."}]}, "1 Samuel 16:11–13": {"title": "\"We won't sit down until he's here\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Seven sons have passed and none of them is it, so Samuel asks a question."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Are these all the sons you have?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has to ask. Nobody volunteers the information."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**There's still the youngest**,\" Jesse said. \"**But he's out in the fields, keeping the sheep**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **haqqaton** — the smallest, the youngest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The *but* is Jesse's. A prophet has come to town, a sacrifice has been called, seven sons have been paraded, and the eighth was not thought worth fetching."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is looking after the animals — which in a family with eight sons is the job given to the one who counts for least."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Send for him. We won't sit down until he's here**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *we will not sit around until he comes*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole ceremony stops. Nobody eats."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was **ruddy and bright-eyed, with a fine face**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *ruddy, with beautiful eyes, and good to look at*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the joke of the chapter. God has just told Samuel not to look at the outside, and then the one He picks is the handsome one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point was never that appearance disqualifies. It was that it does not decide."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD said, \"**This is the one. Get up and anoint him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel had to be told. He had asked for the boy without knowing, and he waits for the sentence before pouring."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The anointing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Samuel took the horn of oil and anointed him **right there in front of his brothers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In front of the seven who were called first."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the chapter says the family understood what it meant. Samuel had told them he came to sacrifice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What came with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And from that day on, **the Spirit of the LORD rushed upon David and stayed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tsalach** — rushed, came mightily."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**And stayed.** The next verse says the opposite of Saul: *but the Spirit of the LORD departed from Saul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses, back to back, and the chapter turns on the contrast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Samuel did next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Samuel **went home to Ramah**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No instruction, no training, no schedule. He anoints a teenager and leaves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David goes back to the sheep, and the next thing recorded of him is carrying bread and cheese to his brothers at the front. See the note on 1 Samuel 16:20–23."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How long the wait was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Years — through Goliath, Saul's spear, the wilderness, Ziklag, and Saul's death. Roughly fifteen by most reckonings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is anointed here and crowned in 2 Samuel 5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the choosing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God has chosen the foolish things of the world to put to shame the wise; and God has chosen the weak things of the world to put to shame the things which are mighty.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 1:26–31."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What had to happen first.** Somebody had to ask. Seven sons were presented and the eighth was in a field — and the prophet stopped the whole proceeding and refused to sit down until he came in."}]}, "1 Samuel 16:20–23": {"title": "David plays for Saul", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The newly anointed king's first job is playing music for the man he is going to replace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel has come to Bethlehem, worked through seven sons, asked *are all the young men here?*, and anointed the one who was out with the sheep."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the Spirit of the LORD came upon David from that day forward.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the next verse: *but the Spirit of the LORD departed from Saul.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesse sent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesse loaded a donkey with **bread, a skin of wine, and a young goat**, and sent them to Saul **with David his son**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A gift to a king, and a boy alongside it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesse does not know what Samuel did — or if he does, he sends him anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The post"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So David came to Saul and **entered his service**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Saul felt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Saul **loved him dearly** and made him **one of his armor-bearers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *loved him greatly*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul later throws a spear at him twice, hunts him across the wilderness for years, and kills eighty-five priests for helping him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator records the loving first, without irony."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Saul sent word to Jesse: \"**Let David stay with me, for I'm pleased with him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *for he has found favour in my eyes* — **chen**, grace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word used over Noah, Joseph in Potiphar's house, and Esther before the king."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trouble"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whenever **the harmful spirit came over Saul**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 14 says where it came from: *the Spirit of the LORD departed from Saul, and a distressing spirit from the LORD troubled him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator says it plainly and does not explain it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The remedy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*David would **take the harp and play**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kinnor** — a lyre, played by hand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Saul would **find relief, the darkness would lift, and the tormenting spirit would leave him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *Saul was refreshed and was well*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is said, sung or prayed in the account. A boy plays and it lifts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David was doing beforehand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Keeping sheep, which is where Samuel found him, and where he learned the instrument."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalms attributed to him run to seventy-three, and the one everybody knows opens *the LORD is my shepherd*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Saul's servants had said about him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A skilful player on the harp, a mighty man of valour, a man of war, prudent in speech, and handsome; **and the LORD is with him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five qualities and then the last clause, which is the one that matters and the one they could not have arranged."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Goliath, in the following chapter — and Saul does not recognise him. *Whose son is this youth?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two accounts sit awkwardly together and the narrator lets them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The first thing the anointed king does.** Carries a donkey-load of bread and wine to the palace and plays the lyre for a man in the dark — and 1 Samuel says the darkness lifted every time."}]}, "1 Samuel 16:6–7": {"title": "\"The LORD looks at the heart\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The prophet gets it wrong on the first candidate, and is corrected in his own head."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is looking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Samuel took one look at Eliab, the oldest, and **thought**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *said* — but it is interior. Nothing is spoken aloud."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he concluded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Surely this is the one** — the LORD's anointed is standing right here.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel is the last judge of Israel, the man who heard a voice in the night as a boy, and who had anointed Saul."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is a genuine prophet, and his first read is wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Don't look at how handsome he is, or how tall he stands**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The height matters because of who the last king was. Saul was chosen partly for it: *from his shoulders upward he was taller than any of the people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel is applying the criteria that produced Saul."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have not chosen him**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD does not see the way people see**. People look at **the outside — the face, the height, the polish**. But **the LORD looks at the heart**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *man looks at the eyes* — what is visible — *but the LORD looks at the heart*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter has already established"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before: God tells Samuel *how long will you mourn for Saul, seeing I have rejected him from reigning over Israel? **Fill your horn with oil, and go**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel is afraid — *if Saul hears it, he will kill me* — and is told to bring a heifer and say he has come to sacrifice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven sons pass by. Abinadab, Shammah, and four more."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Samuel said to Jesse, \"**The LORD has not chosen these**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the question that breaks it open: *are all the young men here?* See the note on 1 Samuel 16:11–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was not invited"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The eighth son was not brought to the sacrifice. His own father left him with the sheep."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men in the room applied the same criteria Samuel had, and Samuel had to ask."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the sentence is quoted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Man looks at the outward appearance, but the LORD looks at the heart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It becomes one of the most quoted lines in the Old Testament, and it is spoken as a correction to a prophet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Eliab does later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the valley of Elah, when David asks about Goliath, Eliab is the brother who turns on him: *why did you come down here? And with whom have you left those few sheep in the wilderness? **I know your pride and the insolence of your heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's own theme, in the mouth of the man it was first applied to."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who is corrected.** Not the family. The prophet — silently, in his own thought, before he had said a word out loud."}]}, "1 Samuel 17:28–30": {"title": "\"Can't I even ask a question?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first opposition David meets is not the giant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When **Eliab, David's oldest brother**, heard David talking with the men.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The brother Samuel had looked at first and been told *I have not chosen him*. See the note on 1 Samuel 16:6–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eliab was in the room when the oil went on his youngest brother's head."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **burned with anger**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *his anger was kindled*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four accusations"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why did you even come down here?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **who's watching those few sheep in the wilderness?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I know how proud you are, and how wicked your heart is**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You only came to watch the battle**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each one is aimed lower than the last: his presence, his job, his character, his motive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Those few sheep** — the Hebrew is *those few sheep*. It is contempt for the work and for the size of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David had been sent by their father with bread and cheese, on their father's instructions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The irony in the accusation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I know how proud you are, and how wicked your heart is.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter before has just established the standard: *the LORD does not see the way people see… the LORD looks at the heart*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eliab makes a confident claim about a heart, and he is the man that sentence was spoken over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "David's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What have I done now?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *what have I done now?* — the *now* implies a history."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Can't I even ask a question?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek and Hebrew both come out as a mild protest. He does not defend himself and does not raise it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he **turned to someone else and asked the same thing**, and **got the same answer**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He steps around it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question he was asking — *what shall be done for the man who kills this Philistine?* — he asks again, of the next man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not argue with his brother, does not appeal to the anointing, and does not leave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had been told about himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing that he could use here. The anointing had happened *in front of his brothers* and Samuel had gone home to Ramah without explaining anything. See the note on 1 Samuel 16:11–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul hears about it, sends for him, and the exchange about the lion and the bear follows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the armour, and the five stones from the brook, and the giant's contempt. See the note on 1 Samuel 17:41–44."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two sneers in one chapter — one from his brother and one from the Philistine — and David answers the second one at length and the first one in six words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Eliab turns up later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chronicles lists his daughter as married into the royal line, and 2 Chronicles 11 names her as the mother of Rehoboam's wife."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator never mentions the quarrel again."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of the opposition.** The brother comes before the giant, and the accusation is about the heart — which is the exact category the previous chapter said only God can see."}]}, "1 Samuel 17:34–37": {"title": "The lion and the bear", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David's argument for being allowed to fight Goliath is not about courage. It is a work history."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he offers as his credentials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When a lion or a bear came and carried off a sheep from the flock, I went after it, struck it down, and rescued the sheep from its mouth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody saw this. There was no army watching, no king, no reward. A boy alone with sheep, going after a predator to recover one animal that belonged to his father."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the entire résumé, and it is enough."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he went after them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to prove anything. Because the sheep was his father's, and a shepherd who lets a lion keep it is not a shepherd."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The detail is precise — *rescued the sheep from its mouth*, and when the animal turned on him, he took it by the fur and killed it. Whoever preserved this account preserved the specifics."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion he draws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD who rescued me from the paw of the lion and the paw of the bear will rescue me from the hand of this Philistine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reasoning runs backwards from what has already happened. He is not claiming to be strong; he is naming who did the rescuing last time and expecting the same person to do it again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the change of word: paw, paw, and then **hand**. Goliath is put in the same category as the animals."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Saul says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go, and the LORD be with you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A king with no better plan sends a shepherd. And the thing that persuaded him was not a display of strength — it was an account of what God had already done where nobody was looking."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the hidden work goes.** Everything David uses in the valley he learned on the hills: the sling he had used on predators, the refusal of armour he had never worn, and the conviction that the LORD rescues. The lion and the bear are not preparation for the giant in some general sense. They are the reason he knows what he knows."}]}, "1 Samuel 17:41–44": {"title": "\"Am I a dog?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The giant's only recorded speech to David, and it is four sentences of contempt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The approach"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Philistine **came steadily closer**, his **shield-bearer walking ahead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has already described the equipment: a bronze helmet, a coat of mail weighing five thousand shekels, bronze greaves, a bronze javelin, and a spear whose *staff was like a weaver's beam*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a man in front of him carrying the shield, because the armour is too heavy to carry a shield as well."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The look"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When he **looked David over** and saw **only a boy, ruddy and handsome**, he **sneered at him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he disdained him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ruddy and handsome** is the same description Samuel was given at the anointing. See the note on 1 Samuel 16:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thing that marked him out to the prophet is the thing that makes the giant laugh."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Am I a dog**,\" he said, \"**that you come at me with sticks?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David had a staff, a sling, and five stones from the brook."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The plural — *sticks* — is contempt for the whole equipment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dog** was the strongest insult in that culture, and it is the giant applying it to himself as a measure of the offence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cursing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he **cursed David by his gods**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The threat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Come here**, and **I'll give your flesh to the birds and the wild animals**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David says back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verses, and they are the reply the chapter is built for:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You come to me with a sword, with a spear, and with a javelin. But I come to you in the name of the LORD of hosts**, the God of the armies of Israel, whom you have defied.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This day the LORD will deliver you into my hand**… and all this assembly shall know that **the LORD does not save with sword and spear; for the battle is the LORD's**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the threat is returned, expanded: *I will give the carcasses of the camp of the Philistines this day to the birds of the air and the wild beasts.* See the note on 1 Samuel 17:45–47."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the armies had been doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty days. Goliath came out morning and evening and shouted, and *when Saul and all Israel heard those words, they were dismayed and greatly afraid*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody had answered for nearly six weeks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was in the camp"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul, who was *taller than any of the people from his shoulders upward* — the one man physically closest to the challenge — offering rewards to anyone else who would go."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David had already told Saul"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your servant has killed both lion and bear… **the LORD, who delivered me from the paw of the lion and from the paw of the bear, He will deliver me** from the hand of this Philistine.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He argues from a record nobody witnessed, made while looking after sheep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The armour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul put his own armour on him and David took it off: *I cannot walk with these, for I have not tested them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes out in what he came in."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the giant assessed.** Correctly, as far as it went — a boy with a stick. The reply David gives does not dispute the inventory; it names a different party to the fight."}]}, "1 Samuel 17:45–47": {"title": "\"The battle is the LORD's\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A boy with a sling makes a speech before he makes a shot, and the speech is longer than the fight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two inventories"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You come at me with **sword and spear and javelin**, but I come at you **in the name of the LORD Almighty**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Goliath's equipment has been listed in detail earlier in the chapter — a bronze helmet, a coat of mail weighing five thousand shekels, bronze greaves, a bronze javelin, a spear whose shaft was like a weaver's beam, and a shield-bearer walking ahead of him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David's list has one item on it, and it is a name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he calls God"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD Almighty, **the God of the armies of Israel** — the very One you have defied.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**LORD of hosts** — Yahweh Sabaoth, the God of armies. The title is chosen for the occasion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the offence is relocated. Forty days of Goliath shouting at Israel, and David identifies who was actually being insulted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says before it happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This very day** the LORD will hand you over to me. **I'll strike you down and cut off your head.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past-tense certainty about a future event, spoken in the open, to the man's face, before a stone has left the pouch."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes further — *the bodies of the whole Philistine army I'll give to the birds* — announcing not just a duel won but an army routed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So the whole earth will know there is a God in Israel.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stated purpose is not David's survival or Israel's border. It is what everybody watching will conclude."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD does not save with sword or spear.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said by a man who is about to use a sling and a stone — which are also not nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not despising the weapon. He is naming what the weapon is not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**For the battle is the LORD's**, and He will give all of you into our hands.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the battle belongs to the LORD*. It is a statement of ownership."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jehoshaphat is told the same thing in almost the same words centuries later: *the battle is not yours, but God's* — and he sends singers out in front of the army. See the note on 2 Chronicles 20:16–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had already told Saul"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lion and the bear. *Your servant has killed both lion and bear, and this uncircumcised Philistine will be like one of them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had a record nobody had seen, built in a field with nobody watching, and it is what he was standing on."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What David ran toward.** *When the Philistine arose and came and drew near to meet David, **David hurried and ran toward the army** to meet the Philistine.* He did not wait to be approached, and the text notes that he ran."}]}, "1 Samuel 17:48–51": {"title": "\"David broke into a run\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The whole fight takes one verse, and the detail worth noticing is the direction David is moving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He ran at him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As Goliath moved in to attack, David **broke into a run toward** the battle line to meet him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty days the army had stood still while the giant shouted. The first thing that changes is a shepherd running the wrong way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He closes the distance himself. A sling needs room, and he gives up room to shorten it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weapons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So David won with a sling and a stone. Without a sword in his hand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator says it twice, which is how Hebrew underlines. He had refused Saul's armour a few verses earlier — it was untested and he could not walk in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sling was not a toy. Judges mentions seven hundred left-handed Benjaminites who could sling a stone at a hair and not miss. It was the ranged weapon of a man with no armour, and David had used it on lions and bears where nobody was watching. See the note on 1 Samuel 17:34–37."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The forehead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He struck the Philistine on the forehead. The stone sank in.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one place the champion was not covered. Verse 5 describes the bronze helmet, the coat of scale armour weighing about fifty kilos, greaves, a javelin — and a gap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sword"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then David ran and stood over him, drew Goliath's own sword from its sheath, and cut off his head with it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He finishes with the giant's own weapon. The thing everyone had been terrified of becomes the instrument of the ending."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What David had already said.** *The battle is the LORD's, and He will give all of you into our hands.* He announced the outcome to Goliath's face before he picked up a stone, and the account records the running, the sling and the sword as the working out of a sentence already spoken."}]}, "1 Samuel 18:1–4": {"title": "The covenant Jonathan made, and what he took off to make it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The heir to the throne meets the man who will take it from him, and gives him his clothes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When David finished speaking with Saul.* The conversation immediately after Goliath — David is holding the giant's head, and he is a shepherd nobody had heard of that morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jonathan is the crown prince, a seasoned soldier who had won his own famous victory a few chapters earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he gave"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took off the robe he was wearing and gave it to David, along with his tunic, and even his sword, his bow, and his belt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five items, and they are not gifts of affection. They are the marks of his position:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the robe** — the outer garment of a king's son", "**the tunic** — what was under it", "**sword, bow, belt** — his weapons and his rank"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He hands over the visible evidence of being next in line, to the man who is replacing him, on the day they meet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The covenant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jonathan made a covenant with David, because he loved him as himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is Jonathan who initiates it. David is not recorded as asking, and has nothing to offer in return."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That covenant is the reason David goes looking for a survivor of Saul's house years later, and the reason Mephibosheth gets a seat at the table. See the note on 2 Samuel 9:5–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jonathan knew where this went. Later he says it plainly to David: *you will be king over Israel, and I will be second to you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was never second to anyone. He died at Gilboa beside his father, and David wrote the lament."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word for the knitting.** *Something knit Jonathan's soul to David's.* The Hebrew verb is used of tying or binding fast — the same root used later of Jonathan's love for him. Whatever else is in this friendship, the text describes it as a bond made rather than a feeling that arrived."}]}, "1 Samuel 2:1–10": {"title": "Hannah's song", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A woman hands over the child she prayed for and sings about the whole world turning over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where she is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has just left Samuel at Shiloh — *as long as he lives he shall be lent to the LORD* — and the song is what she says walking away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The child is about three."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she had been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Barren, and mocked for it. *Her rival also provoked her severely, to make her miserable… year by year.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eli had watched her lips moving at the tabernacle door and assumed she was drunk."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My heart overflows with joy in the LORD**. In Him **my strength is lifted high**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *my horn is exalted* — the horn of an animal, the standard image for strength."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word appears at the start and at the very end of the song, and Hannah is the first person in Scripture to use it that way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the song is not about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her son. He is not mentioned once."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She sings about God reversing things, and the personal deliverance is one line of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The theology"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD is a God who knows, and He weighs every deed**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *by Him actions are weighed*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven pairs, and each turns over:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The **bows of the mighty are shattered**, but **those who stumbled are dressed in strength**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The **full-bellied hire themselves out for bread**, but **the hungry hunger no more**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The **barren woman gives birth to seven**, while **the mother of many withers away**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That third one is hers, and she puts it in the middle of a list rather than at the head of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The LORD **brings death, and He brings life**. He **brings down to the grave, and He raises up**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The LORD **makes poor, and He makes rich**. He **humbles, and He lifts high**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both sides of each pair are attributed to Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ash heap"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He **raises the poor from the dust**. He **lifts the needy from the ash heap**, to **seat them among princes**, to hand them **a throne of honor**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 113 uses the same lines, and so does Mary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He gives strength to **His king** and lifts high the horn of **His anointed**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **meshicho** — His Messiah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel has no king. There will not be one for decades, and the son she has just handed over will anoint the first two."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hannah sings about a king before the office exists."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the song goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mary's Magnificat is built on it, line by line: *He has put down the mighty from their thrones, and exalted the lowly. He has filled the hungry with good things, and the rich He has sent away empty.* See the note on Luke 1:39–45."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A thousand years apart, two women who had been told they would have a son."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is in the same chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eli's sons and the three-pronged fork. The chronicler alternates between the two households, and after every account of the corruption comes a line about the boy. See the note on 1 Samuel 2:12–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does afterward"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moreover his mother used to make him **a little robe, and bring it to him year by year** when she came up with her husband.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, once a year, for the rest of his childhood."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in the song.** The child. She has just given him away and sings ten verses about God turning the order of things over — and the personal reversal is one line in the middle."}]}, "1 Samuel 2:12–17": {"title": "The three-pronged fork", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The corruption is described in procedural detail, which is what makes it damning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now Eli's sons were **scoundrels**. They had **no regard for the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **bene beliyaal** — sons of worthlessness. The phrase is used of the worst men in the Old Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**No regard for the LORD** — the Hebrew is *they did not know the LORD*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are the priests at Shiloh, where the tabernacle stood. Their father is the high priest and the judge of Israel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The custom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Here was **their custom** with the people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the priests' custom with the people*. It had become the practice, not an occasional abuse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fork"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The priest's servant would show up **while the meat was still boiling**, holding **a three-pronged fork**. He'd **jab it into the pot or kettle or pan**, and **whatever the fork brought up, the priest took**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four vessels named. The detail is procedural, and that is the point — somebody set this up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus assigned the priests specific portions: the breast and the right thigh, waved and heaved. This is a fork in a pot, taking whatever comes out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it was done to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That's how they treated **all the Israelites who came to Shiloh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every pilgrim, every time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The worse half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Worse — **even before the fat was burned to the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fat belonged to God. Leviticus is explicit and repeats it: *all the fat is the LORD's… you shall eat neither fat nor blood.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were taking their portion before God's portion went on the fire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The demand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Hand over some meat for the priest to roast. **He won't take boiled meat from you — only raw**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They want it uncooked so they can roast it their own way. It is a preference."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The threat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And if the man said, \"**Let the fat be burned first**, then take what you want,\" the servant would answer, \"**No. Give it now, or I'll take it by force**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The worshipper is the one arguing for the law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **the sin of these young men was very great in the LORD's eyes**, because they were **treating the LORD's offering with contempt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *they despised the offering of the LORD*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The charge is not theft. It is contempt for what was being brought."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **men abhorred the offering of the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next clause of the passage, and it is the real damage. People stopped wanting to come."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter alternates. After every account of the sons: *but Samuel ministered before the LORD, even as a child, wearing a linen ephod.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And after the warning Eli gives them: *and the boy Samuel kept growing, both in size and in favour — with the LORD and with people.* See the note on 1 Samuel 2:22–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke uses almost the same sentence of Jesus at twelve. See the note on Luke 2:51–52."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both sons died on the same day at Aphek, the ark was captured, and Eli fell backward off his seat and broke his neck at the news."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His daughter-in-law, dying in labour, named the child **Ichabod** — *the glory has departed*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What made it great in God's eyes.** Not the amount taken. The fork went into the pot before the fat went on the fire — and the men bringing the sacrifices were the ones objecting."}]}, "1 Samuel 2:22–26": {"title": "A father's warning ignored", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Eli confronts his sons, and the chapter ends by cutting to a boy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Eli was **very old** now. And he **kept hearing** about everything his sons were doing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he heard* — repeatedly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the high priest and the judge of Israel. He knows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he heard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everything his sons were doing to **all Israel**, and how they **slept with the women who served at the entrance to the tent of meeting**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fork in the sacrificial pot was chapter 2's first charge. See the note on 1 Samuel 2:12–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the second, and it is inside the tabernacle precinct."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The confrontation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why are you doing these things?** I hear from all these people about your evil ways.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks a question and cites public report."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reproach"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No, my sons.** This is **no good report** I'm hearing — **you're leading the LORD's people into sin**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *it is no good report that I hear*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the real damage: not what they take, but what it does to everybody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If a man sins against another man, **God may mediate for him**. But **if a man sins against the LORD, who can plead his case?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the judge shall judge him*, and then: who shall intercede?"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a genuine theological question, and the Old Testament never answers it — Job asks the same thing: *there is no umpire between us, no one to lay his hand on us both.* See the note on Job 9:25–35."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer is centuries away: *there is **one Mediator** between God and men, the Man Christ Jesus*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Eli did not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anything. God's charge against him comes two chapters on, through the boy Samuel:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will judge his house forever for the iniquity which he knows, because **his sons made themselves vile, and he did not restrain them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And through the man of God in this chapter: *why do you **honour your sons more than Me**, by making yourselves fat with the best of all the offerings?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Making yourselves** — plural. Eli was eating it too."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He talked and did not act, and he ate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But his sons **would not listen to their father**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the hardest clause in the chapter: *for **it was the LORD's will to put them to death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *because the LORD desired to kill them*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator states it and does not explain it. Scripture does this occasionally — Pharaoh's hardened heart, the lying spirit in Ahab's prophets — and offers no commentary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cut"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the boy Samuel kept growing, both in size and in favour — with the LORD and with people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter alternates between the two households throughout, and this is the last line of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke uses almost the same sentence twice: of John, and of Jesus at twelve — *and Jesus increased in wisdom and stature, and in favour with God and men*. See the note on Luke 2:51–52."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The famous line opens chapter 3: *and the word of the LORD was rare in those days; there was no widespread revelation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then a voice in the night, three times, and Eli — who could not restrain his own sons — telling the boy what to say: *speak, LORD, for Your servant hears.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old man gives the right instruction to somebody else's child."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the warning lacked.** Not clarity. Eli says exactly what is wrong and asks a question nobody in the Old Testament can answer — and the chapter's charge against him, delivered later, is that he did not restrain them."}]}, "1 Samuel 22:1–2": {"title": "Everyone in distress", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David's army assembles itself, and the recruitment criteria are three kinds of trouble."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*David slipped away from there and escaped to **the cave of Adullam**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has been running for chapters. Saul's spear, Michal lowering him out of a window, Ahimelech and the showbread, and then Gath — where he pretended to be insane, scrabbling at the gates with saliva in his beard."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalms 34 and 56 come out of that week. See the note on Psalms 56:8–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Adullam is a limestone cave country in the Judean foothills, about ten miles from Bethlehem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who arrives first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When his brothers and his father's whole household heard, **they went down to him there**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The family that had left him with the sheep when Samuel came to dinner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eliab, who had said *why did you come down here? I know your pride and the insolence of your heart* on the day of Goliath. See the note on 1 Samuel 17:28–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They come to him now, because Saul is dangerous to anyone connected with him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who arrives next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And others came too — **everyone in distress, everyone in debt, everyone bitter of soul**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three categories, and the Hebrew is blunt about all three."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Matsoq** — pressure, straits."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nosheh lo** — a creditor to him. Men being pursued for money."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Mar-nephesh** — bitter of soul. Discontented, embittered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of them is described as brave, loyal or promising."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he becomes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **he became their leader**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **sar** — captain, prince, commander."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The anointed king of Israel takes command of four hundred men whose qualifications are debt and bitterness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*About four hundred men were with him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "By chapter 23 it is six hundred. It stays six hundred for the rest of the wilderness years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they become"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mighty men."}, {"k": "p", "t": "2 Samuel 23 lists them by name with their exploits: the man who killed three hundred with a spear; the one who stood in a field of lentils and defended it when everyone else ran; the three who broke through the Philistine garrison to fetch David a drink of water from the well at Bethlehem because he said out loud that he was thirsty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what he did with the water: *he would not drink it, but poured it out to the LORD*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Benaiah, who *went down and killed a lion in the midst of a pit on a snowy day*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty-seven names, and they came out of a cave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the cave was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing was done to them in it. They were not trained there, and no oath is recorded."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They gathered round somebody, and the narrative says that is what changed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalms from this period go"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be merciful to me, O God… my soul trusts in You; and in the shadow of Your wings I will make my refuge, until these calamities have passed by.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I cried out to God Most High, to God who performs all things for me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written in caves, by a man with a price on his head and four hundred debtors camped outside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews says about the type"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And what more shall I say? For the time would fail me to tell of Gideon and Barak and Samson and Jephthah, also of David and Samuel and the prophets… who **out of weakness were made strong**, became valiant in battle, turned to flight the armies of the aliens.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same gathering happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Come to Me, all you who labour and are heavy laden, and I will give you rest.* See the note on Matthew 11:28–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The qualification there is being tired, which is the same kind of qualification."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The entry requirements.** Distress, debt, and bitterness. Nothing on the list is a virtue, and thirty-seven of these men end up with their names and their exploits written down."}]}, "1 Samuel 25:2–3": {"title": "Nabal and Abigail", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two people introduced in one verse, and the writer sets them against each other word for word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His name was **Nabal**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nabal** means fool. Not a nickname the chapter awards him — his own wife says it later: *as his name is, so is he. Nabal is his name, and folly is with him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **nabal** is not the word for a simpleton. It is the moral fool of Proverbs and the Psalms — *the fool has said in his heart, there is no God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was **very rich** — three thousand sheep and a thousand goats.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer gives the figures. He is a substantial man, and shearing time was the year's payday."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two descriptions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She was a woman of **good sense and lovely to look at**, but the man was **harsh and mean** in his dealings.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for her good sense is **sekel** — insight, prudence, the wisdom that produces good outcomes. It is the same root as the word in Joshua 1:8, where meditating on the book produces *good success*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her sense is listed **before** her looks."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And his two adjectives are hard and evil in his doings. The contrast is deliberate and total."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David had protected Nabal's shepherds in the wilderness — *they were a wall to us both by night and day*, one of the servants says. At shearing, David asks for provision and is insulted: *who is David? Who is the son of Jesse?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David straps on his sword and takes four hundred men to kill every male in the household."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abigail loads two hundred loaves, two skins of wine, five dressed sheep, roasted grain, raisins and figs, and rides out to meet him without telling her husband."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The speech"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is one of the longest by a woman in the Old Testament, and it works. She takes the blame herself, feeds four hundred men, and then tells David what he is about to throw away:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When the LORD has done for my lord all the good He has promised, and made you ruler over Israel, then you will have **no cause for grief, no burden on your conscience** for shedding blood without cause.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She argues from his future rather than his anger. See the note on 1 Samuel 25:29–31."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Blessed is your advice**, and blessed are you, because you have kept me this day from coming to bloodshed and from avenging myself with my own hand.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He blesses the counsel before he blesses the woman."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What became of them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nabal held a feast *like the feast of a king* that same night, drunk. She told him in the morning, *and his heart died within him, and he became like a stone*, and ten days later he was dead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David sent for Abigail and married her."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The house he was from.** *He was of the house of Caleb* — the man who at eighty-five asked for the mountain with the giants on it. The blood was there; the spirit was not. See the note on Joshua 14:6–15."}]}, "1 Samuel 25:29–31": {"title": "\"The bundle of the living\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A woman standing in a ravine in front of four hundred armed men talks a future king out of a massacre."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The picture she gives him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The life of my lord will be **bound safe in the bundle of the living**, kept by the LORD your God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *bound in the bundle of life*. The image is of valuables tied up in a cloth and carried — the way a traveller secured what he could not afford to lose, by keeping it on his person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase entered Jewish liturgy and is still cut into gravestones."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the picture for his enemies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But the lives of your enemies He will **sling away like a stone from a sling**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is talking to the man who killed Goliath with a sling. She takes the weapon from the best day of his life and hands it to God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two images in one sentence — one for what God keeps and one for what God discards — and she picked both for this listener."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she argues from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When the LORD has done for my lord **all the good He has promised**, and made you **ruler over Israel**…\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David is a fugitive living in caves. Saul is on the throne. She speaks of the kingdom as settled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody had crowned him. She had heard the reports, worked out what he was, and addresses him accordingly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The thing she is protecting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"…then you will have **no cause for grief, no burden on your conscience** for shedding blood without cause or for taking vengeance yourself.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is her whole case. Not *spare us* — she barely mentions her household."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She argues that a man on his way to a throne cannot afford to arrive at it with this on him. The Hebrew is *no stumbling of heart*, no obstacle in the conscience."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is defending his future from his present temper."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And when the LORD deals well with my lord, **remember your servant**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One line, at the very end, and it is the same request the thief makes on the cross."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David says back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Blessed is the LORD God of Israel, who sent you this day to meet me. **Blessed is your advice**, and blessed are you, because you have kept me this day from coming to bloodshed and from **avenging myself with my own hand**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He blesses God, then the counsel, then her. And he names precisely what he was about to do: avenge himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had spared Saul twice by then, refusing to touch the LORD's anointed. He was minutes from slaughtering a household over an insult about food."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wisdom in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter introduced her as *a woman of good sense* — **sekel**, insight that produces good outcomes. This is what it looks like in operation: right words, right man, right moment, at considerable personal risk."}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on 1 Samuel 25:2–3."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What she did not do.** Tell her husband. *But she did not tell Nabal her husband.* The chapter records it without comment, and then records that when she did tell him, in the morning, when he was sober, his heart died within him."}]}, "1 Samuel 3:1–3": {"title": "\"The lamp of God had not yet gone out\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses of scene-setting, and every detail in them is about light failing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word was rare"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In those days **the word of the LORD was rare; visions rarely broke through**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *precious* — scarce because valuable, the way a rare metal is precious."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the end of the period of the judges. Eli's sons are stealing from the offerings and sleeping with the women at the door of the tabernacle, and the last line of the book of Judges is *everyone did what was right in his own eyes.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Eli's eyes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His eyes had grown so weak he could **barely see**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Physical, and the narrative means it both ways — the chapter before has the old priest unable to tell the difference between a woman praying and a woman drunk."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lamp"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **lamp of God had not yet gone out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The golden lampstand, in the holy place. The priests were charged to keep it burning: *Aaron shall tend it from evening until morning before the LORD continually.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Had not yet gone out* is a strange way to report it. Not *was burning* — the writer tells you it was close."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the last hours before dawn, the oil is low, and the light is nearly gone. That is the state of the priesthood and the nation in one clause."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the boy is lying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Samuel was lying down in the temple of the LORD, **where the ark of God was**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A child asleep on the floor beside the ark, under a lamp that is going out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who hears"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the priest. The boy — who *did not yet know the LORD, nor was the word of the LORD yet revealed to him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times he runs to Eli, and it takes Eli three tries to work out what is happening. Then the old man, whatever else is true of him, tells the child exactly what to say: *Speak, LORD, for Your servant hears.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to the lamp"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next chapter: the ark is captured, Eli's sons are killed, Eli falls backwards and breaks his neck, and a woman in labour names her child **Ichabod** — *the glory has departed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lamp went out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what happens to the boy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD was with him and **let none of his words fall to the ground**. And all Israel from Dan to Beersheba knew that Samuel had been established as a prophet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word that was rare became plentiful, and it came through the child who was sleeping under the failing light."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What God said in that first message.** A judgement on Eli's house. The first word ever given to Samuel was one he had to deliver to the man who raised him, and Eli's answer was *it is the LORD; let Him do what seems good to Him.*"}]}, "1 Samuel 3:4–9": {"title": "He ran to the wrong man three times", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A boy hears his name in the dark and gets out of bed, and he is right about everything except who is calling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *the word of the LORD was rare in those days; there was no widespread revelation*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eli is old and nearly blind, his sons are corrupt priests, and the lamp of God has not yet gone out. See the note on 1 Samuel 3:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first call"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the LORD called, \"**Samuel!**\" And Samuel answered, \"**Here I am**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hineni** — the word Abraham used on the mountain and Moses at the bush."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He answers correctly. He simply attributes it to the wrong person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He ran to Eli and said, \"Here I am — you called me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**He ran.** A boy who is not resentful about being woken and not slow about it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I didn't call,\" Eli said. \"Go back and lie down.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Again the LORD called, \"Samuel!\" And Samuel got up and went to Eli.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I didn't call, **my son**,\" Eli said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The address softens. Eli has two sons of his own, and he calls this one *my son*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The narrator's explanation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **Samuel did not yet know the LORD**; the word of the LORD had not yet been revealed to him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is placed here deliberately. Samuel has been serving in the tabernacle since he was weaned, wearing a linen ephod, ministering before the LORD."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has the job, the clothes, and the location, and the narrator says he does not yet know Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A third time the LORD called, \"Samuel!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times, and each time the boy gets up and goes to a man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The voice does not change tactics, raise itself, or explain. It says the name again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Eli works out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **Eli understood: it was the LORD calling the boy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayaven Eli** — Eli discerned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His own house is under judgment. A man of God has already come and told him what is coming. He has every reason not to want another word from the LORD in that building."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells the boy how to answer it anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go and lie down, and if He calls you, say, '**Speak, LORD, for Your servant is listening**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **dabber YHWH ki shomea avdecha**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eli, who cannot hear, teaches the boy who can."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the boy actually says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "When it comes, Samuel drops the divine name: *speak, for Your servant hears*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator records the difference and does not comment on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the message turns out to be"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold, I will do something in Israel at which both ears of everyone who hears it will tingle… **I will judge his house forever for the iniquity which he knows about**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first word Samuel ever receives is against the man who taught him how to receive it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Samuel lay down until morning… **and Samuel was afraid to tell Eli the vision***. See the note on 1 Samuel 3:15–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Eli's answer: *it is the LORD. Let Him do what seems good to Him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Samuel grew, and the LORD was with him and **let none of his words fall to the ground***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew image is of an arrow or a word dropping short."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And all Israel from Dan to Beersheba knew that Samuel had been established as a prophet of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the three trips were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not wasted. A boy learning that the voice was worth getting up for before he knew whose it was."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the narrator says at the second call.** *Samuel did not yet know the LORD.* Said of a boy who had lived in the tabernacle since he was weaned and wore the linen ephod."}]}, "1 Samuel 6:1–9": {"title": "Seven months, and a plan", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The Philistines have won a war and cannot get rid of the trophy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The duration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ark of the LORD had been in Philistine territory **seven months**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They captured it at Aphek, where Israel had fetched it from Shiloh as a talisman and lost thirty thousand men and the ark with it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eli fell backwards off his seat when he heard, and his daughter-in-law named her son Ichabod."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the seven months contained"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dagon on his face before the ark, twice — the second time with his head and hands broken off on the threshold."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Tumours in Ashdod, then Gath, then Ekron. The ark passed from city to city because each one wanted it gone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the cry of the city went up to heaven.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consultation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the Philistines called for **their priests and diviners**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not Israelite priests. Their own religious professionals."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The advice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Don't send it away **empty-handed**. Be sure to send Him **a guilt offering**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **asham** — the guilt or trespass offering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pagan diviners using the correct Levitical term."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offering with a valuation attached, and with restitution built in. See the note on Leviticus 5:14–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Five gold tumors and five gold rats**, one for each Philistine ruler.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five, for the five cities — Ashdod, Gaza, Ashkelon, Gath and Ekron."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Models of the affliction, in gold, sent back with what caused it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rats"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew mentions rats *that are ravaging the land*, which the earlier narrative does not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Tumours and rats together is a description that has interested epidemiologists for a long time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Give glory to the God of Israel**. Perhaps He will lighten His hand.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tenu kavod** — give glory."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said by Philistine diviners about a God they do not serve."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning they use"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why be stubborn like the Egyptians and Pharaoh were?** When God dealt harshly with them, didn't they finally let Israel go?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They know the story."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred years on, in a different country, the exodus is the case law the Philistine priesthood reasons from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The test"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Get **a new cart** ready, with **two cows that have calved and have never been yoked**. Hitch the cows to the cart, but **take their calves away and pen them up**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Untrained cattle, and nursing mothers separated from their young."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two variables, both loaded against the result."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The falsifiable claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then send it off, **and watch**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If it goes up toward its own territory, toward Beth-shemesh, then **the LORD is the one who brought this great disaster on us**. But if it doesn't, we'll know **it wasn't His hand** — it just happened to us by chance.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They state the null hypothesis before running the experiment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pagan priests designing a controlled test, naming both outcomes in advance, and agreeing to accept either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the cows went straight up the road toward Beth-shemesh, keeping to the highway, **lowing all the way**, not turning to the right or the left.* See the note on 1 Samuel 6:10–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the cart comes back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David uses one. New, expensive, and copied from this chapter — and a man dies. See the note on 2 Samuel 6:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Philistines improvised because they had no instructions. Israel had them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the diviners built.** A test with the null hypothesis stated first. Untrained cows, nursing calves penned up, and an agreement to accept whichever answer came."}]}, "1 Samuel 6:10–12": {"title": "The cows that would not turn", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The Philistines design an experiment, and the experiment works."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they are doing it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had captured the ark at Aphek, and it had been a disaster for them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dagon face down twice, the second time with head and hands broken off on the threshold. Tumours in Ashdod, then Gath, then Ekron, and a plague of rats."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven months of it, and their priests and diviners are asked what to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The test they design"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take **two cows that have calved, on which there has never come a yoke**, and hitch them to the cart, and **take their calves home, away from them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two variables loaded against the result."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Milk cows with unweaned calves will not leave them. Untrained cattle will not pull straight."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And watch: **if it goes up the road to its own territory, to Beth-shemesh, then He has done us this great evil. But if not**, then we shall know that it is not His hand that struck us; it happened to us by chance.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They state the null hypothesis before running it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cart"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They took two cows that had calved and **hitched them to the cart**, and **penned up their calves**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A new cart, which was the Philistines' own idea and the only method available to them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel copies it later, with a man dead as a result. See the note on 2 Samuel 6:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cargo"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ark of the LORD… along with the chest holding **the gold rats and the models of the tumors**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five golden tumours and five golden rats, one for each Philistine city."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A guilt offering, designed by pagan priests, on the advice that *perhaps He will lighten His hand from you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the cows **went straight up the road toward Beth-shemesh, keeping to the highway, lowing all the way, not turning to the right or the left**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *they turned not aside to the right hand or to the left*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Lowing all the way** — the detail that makes it. They are calling for their calves the entire journey and walking in the opposite direction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every instinct is pulling one way and they go the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The escort"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Philistine rulers followed them **as far as the border of Beth-shemesh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The five lords walk behind their own experiment to the frontier and then stop."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They watch the result and go home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it arrives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Beth-shemesh — *house of the sun* — a Levitical town, the nearest Israelite settlement across the valley."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now they of Beth-shemesh were reaping their wheat harvest in the valley; and they lifted their eyes and saw the ark, and rejoiced to see it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cows are sacrificed on the spot, using the wood of the cart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Men of Beth-shemesh look into the ark, and there is a slaughter. See the note on 1 Samuel 6:19–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Who is able to stand before this holy LORD God?** And to whom shall it go up from us?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ark comes home and the town it comes home to asks for it to be taken away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Kirjath Jearim, to the house of Abinadab, for twenty years. See the note on 1 Samuel 7:2–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Philistines proved"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their own test. Untrained cattle, separated from their calves, walking a straight road to the border of another country without a driver."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They set out to find whether it was chance, and they got their answer."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The detail that carries it.** *Lowing all the way.* The cows call for their calves for the whole journey and do not turn round — and five Philistine lords walk behind to see if they will."}]}, "1 Samuel 6:19–21": {"title": "\"Who can stand before the LORD, this holy God?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The ark comes home after seven months among the Philistines, and the welcome ends badly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God struck down some of the men of Beth-shemesh because they looked into the ark of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They opened it. The lid — the mercy seat, the place God had said He would speak from — was lifted, and what was under it was the law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers had laid down that even the Levites who carried the holy things were not to look at them, on pain of death. These were farmers in a wheat field who took the covering off."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew reads oddly — seventy men and fifty thousand — and the figure has long been discussed, since Beth-shemesh was a small town. Some manuscripts and early witnesses have only the seventy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text as it stands records both, and the mourning that followed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who can stand before the LORD, this holy God? Where can the ark go from here?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the right question, asked too late. And their answer is to send it somewhere else — the ark goes on to Kiriath-jearim, where it stays twenty years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in the chapter asks how a person might come near safely. They ask who will take it off their hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the passage is actually about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The uncovered law kills. That is the plainest reading of it, and it is the same point the New Testament makes in different words — *the letter kills, but the Spirit gives life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "What made the ark approachable on the Day of Atonement was blood on the lid and smoke over it. Take the covering away and what is left is holiness with nothing between."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Read with Exodus 25:17–22.** God had said He would meet them *above the mercy seat*. Not at the tablets — above them, over the covering. Beth-shemesh is what happens when the covering comes off, and it is why the arrangement was given in the first place."}]}, "1 Samuel 7:2–4": {"title": "Twenty years", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The ark sits in a house on a hill for twenty years and nobody comes for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ark stayed at **Kiriath-jearim**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men of Beth-shemesh had asked for it to be taken away after the deaths there, and Kirjath Jearim came and fetched it. See the note on 1 Samuel 6:19–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they brought it into the house of Abinadab on the hill, and consecrated Eleazar his son to keep the ark.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A private house. Not a sanctuary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The duration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A long time — **twenty years in all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And that is only until the events of this chapter. It stays there through the whole of Saul's reign — the Chronicler notes that *we did not enquire of it in the days of Saul*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The total is closer to a century before David goes to get it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was happening meanwhile"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tabernacle of Moses was at Nob, and later at Gibeon, with the altar and the priesthood functioning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The system ran. The sacrifices continued. The one thing missing was the ark, and nobody sent for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mourning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **all the people of Israel mourned and longed for the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyinnahu** — they lamented after the LORD."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty years of it. The verb suggests a long, low grieving rather than a crisis."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If you are truly returning to the LORD with all your hearts**, then get rid of the foreign gods and the Ashtoreths among you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel puts the inward first and the disposal second."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ashtoreth** — the Canaanite fertility goddess, Astarte. The plural in the Hebrew covers the local versions."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been keeping them. Twenty years of longing for the LORD with the idols still in the house."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Commit yourselves to the LORD and serve Him only**, and He will rescue you from the hand of the Philistines.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *prepare your hearts* — **hakinu levavkhem**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The obedience"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the Israelites **put away their Baals and Ashtoreths and served the LORD alone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrative does not pad it. Samuel says it and they do it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mizpah, and water poured out before the LORD, and fasting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Philistines hear the assembly and attack. *And the LORD thundered with a loud thunder upon the Philistines that day, and so confused them that they were overcome before Israel.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Samuel took a stone and set it up between Mizpah and Shen, and called its name **Ebenezer**, saying, \"**Thus far the LORD has helped us**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Even ha-ezer** — stone of help."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same name as the place where the ark was captured twenty years earlier. Israel had lost the battle at Ebenezer in chapter 4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts the name back on a stone at a victory."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Samuel does with the rest of his life"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he went from year to year on a circuit to Bethel, Gilgal, and Mizpah, and judged Israel in all those places.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But he always returned to Ramah, for his home was there.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David does about the ark"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Takes Jerusalem, and immediately goes for it. See the note on 2 Samuel 6:9–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing he does with a capital city is fetch the box nobody had wanted for a generation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the psalm about it remembers where it had been: *we heard of it in Ephrathah; we found it in the fields of the wood*. See the note on Psalms 132:6–7."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What twenty years of longing had not produced.** The idols were still in the houses. Samuel names the condition and the disposal happens in one sentence."}]}, "1 Samuel 7:7–11": {"title": "A nursing lamb and the thunder", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An army is coming, and the one thing Israel does about it is kill a very small lamb."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Samuel offered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Samuel took a **nursing lamb** and offered it **whole** as a burnt offering to the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a bull, not a ram, not the seven bullocks that a national emergency might seem to call for. A lamb still on its mother's milk — the smallest offering a man could bring."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *whole* — the Hebrew is the word for entire, offered complete, nothing kept back and nothing eaten."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While Samuel was offering the sacrifice, the Philistines drew near to attack.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator puts the two clauses in the same sentence on purpose. The enemy is closing while the lamb is on the altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is armed. Nobody has taken a position. Israel is at worship with an army walking toward them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who fought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But that day the **LORD thundered** with a mighty voice against the Philistines and threw them into such confusion that they were routed before Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *confusion* is the panic-word — the same one used at the Red Sea and against the Canaanite kings under Joshua. It is a specific act of God recorded in a specific verb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel's part comes second: *the men of Israel rushed out… and chased them.* They pursue a battle that has already been won over an altar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The small thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The size of the lamb and the size of the deliverance are not proportioned to each other, and the chapter does not apologise for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What the offering did was proclaim a death. Everything after the thunder is Israel walking into ground the sacrifice had already taken. See the note on 1 Corinthians 1:26–31 on what God chooses to confound the strong with."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Samuel set up afterwards.** A stone, named **Ebenezer** — *thus far the LORD has helped us.* A rock in a field, with no power in it at all, put there so that people walking past would have to remember what happened. The chapter ends with a memorial as small as the lamb it commemorates."}]}, "1 Samuel 8:1–3": {"title": "\"They were nothing like their father\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses that set up everything that follows — the demand for a king, the warning about what a king will do, and the whole monarchy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The decision"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Samuel had grown old, and so he handed the reins of Israel to his sons.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the book of Judges established succession by inheritance. Judges were raised up individually; the office did not pass from father to son. Samuel makes his sons judges anyway."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text does not editorialise. It records the appointment and then records the result."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They chased after money, took bribes, and bent the truth to line their own pockets.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bribery and perverted justice are exactly what the law forbids of judges, in almost these words — Deuteronomy warns that a bribe blinds the eyes of the wise. Samuel's sons are doing the specific thing the office exists to prevent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The echo nobody in the story mentions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel had grown up in the house of Eli, whose own sons were corrupt, and whose failure to restrain them was the reason his house was cut off. Samuel himself delivered that message as a boy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he appoints his own sons, and they turn out the same way. The book puts the two households in parallel and never comments on it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What it triggers.** The elders come to Samuel and ask for a king *like all the other nations*, and they cite his sons as the reason. Their complaint is legitimate. What they ask for as the remedy is the thing God says amounts to rejecting Him — and He tells Samuel to give it to them anyway, with a warning attached."}]}, "1 Samuel 9:6–10": {"title": "A quarter shekel and some lost donkeys", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first king of Israel is out looking for livestock when he stumbles into the appointment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul's father has lost some donkeys and sent him with a servant to find them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have been through the hill country of Ephraim, Shalisha, Shaalim and Benjamin, and found nothing. Saul is ready to turn back — *lest my father cease caring about the donkeys and become worried about us*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose idea it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the servant had an idea**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not Saul's. The servant's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The suggestion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Wait — there's **a man of God in this town**. People hold him in high honor; **everything he says comes true**. Let's go to him. **Maybe he can tell us which way to go**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They want directions to some donkeys."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man is Samuel — prophet, judge, the last of the old order, and the one who will anoint Israel's first two kings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither of them names him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But if we go,\" Saul said, \"**what would we bring him? Our food is gone, and we've got nothing to offer** a man of God. **What do we have?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Custom expected a gift. Saul checks his pockets and finds nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The solution"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look — **I've got a quarter shekel of silver right here**. I'll give it to the man of God, and he'll tell us the way.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The servant has it. A quarter shekel was a small coin — enough to be an offering and not much else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole meeting happens because a servant had loose change."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The narrator's aside"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*(In those days, if a man wanted to inquire of God, he'd say, \"**Let's go to the seer**\" — for **what we now call a prophet used to be called a seer**.)*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ro'eh** — one who sees — against **nabi**, prophet, one who speaks."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator interrupts his own story to explain a word that had gone out of use by the time he wrote."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The older title is about the eyes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The decision"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Good idea,\" Saul said. \"**Let's go**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Samuel already knew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now the LORD had told Samuel in his ear **the day before Saul came**, saying, \"Tomorrow about this time I will send you a man from the land of Benjamin, and **you shall anoint him commander over My people Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-four hours' notice, and a meal already prepared with a portion set aside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Samuel says about the donkeys"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As for **your donkeys that were lost three days ago, do not be anxious about them, for they have been found**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He deals with the errand in one sentence and then: *\"And on whom is all the desire of Israel? Is it not on you and on all your father's house?\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Saul's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Am I not a Benjamite, **of the smallest of the tribes of Israel**, and my family the least of all the families of the tribe of Benjamin? **Why then do you speak like this to me?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Benjamin had nearly been wiped out at the end of Judges. He is not being falsely modest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A meal, a bed on the roof, and then early in the morning at the edge of town: *Samuel took a flask of oil and poured it on his head, and kissed him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul goes home and tells his uncle about the donkeys. *But about the matter of the kingdom, he did not tell him.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he was looking for.** Donkeys. He arrives because a servant remembered a seer lived nearby and happened to have a quarter shekel — and the narrator stops the story to explain that a prophet used to be called somebody who sees."}]}, "1 Thessalonians 1:3–5": {"title": "Faith, love, hope — and what each one does", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's opening thanksgiving, and each of the three is given a verb."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three with their nouns"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How your **faith goes to work**, how your **love keeps laboring on**, how your **hope in our Lord Jesus Christ holds steady and patient** through everything.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is three genitive pairs:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Ergou tes pisteos** — the work of faith", "**Kopou tes agapes** — the labour of love", "**Hupomones tes elpidos** — the endurance of hope"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's triad appears throughout his letters — *faith, hope and love, these three* — and here each is attached to something it produces."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three words"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Ergon** — work, what is done", "**Kopos** — labour to weariness, toil that costs", "**Hupomone** — remaining under, holding position"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second is stronger than the first, and the third is not activity at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which goes with which"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Faith works. Love is the one that wears you out. Hope is what stays under the weight."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not shuffle them; the pairings are the same when he repeats the triad in the same letter: *putting on the breastplate of faith and love, and as a helmet the hope of salvation.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **all of it lived out in the sight of our God and Father**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Dear friends, **loved by God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a perfect passive participle — having been loved and standing loved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We know that **He chose you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eklogen** — election."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says he knows it, and then gives his evidence — which is the striking part."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The evidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **when we brought you the good news it did not arrive as words and nothing more**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *not in word only*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not argue from their sincerity, their decision, or their conduct. He argues from what the preaching was like when it landed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things it came with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It came **with power**, and **with the Holy Spirit**, and **with a deep and settled certainty**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last is **plerophoria polle** — much full assurance. Full-carrying, complete conviction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half of the evidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **you saw for yourselves the kind of men we were while we were with you**, and **it was for your sake that we were that way**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The conduct of the messengers is offered as part of it, and the reason given for the conduct is the readers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How long he was there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Acts 17: three sabbaths in the synagogue, then a mob, then Jason arrested and made to post bond, then Paul and Silas sent away by night."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Weeks. And what he says about the church that came out of it is in the next verses: *the word of the Lord rang out from you… your trust in God has gone out everywhere.* See the note on 1 Thessalonians 1:8–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest of the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And you became followers of us and of the Lord, **having received the word in much affliction, with joy of the Holy Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Affliction and joy in the same clause."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul offers as proof of election.** Not the converts' performance. The manner in which the message arrived — power, the Spirit, and full assurance — and the fact that they saw what the messengers were like."}]}, "1 Thessalonians 1:8–10": {"title": "The word that travelled out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul writes to a church he had to leave in a hurry, and tells them their reputation arrived before he could."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sound"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The word of the Lord **rang out from you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exechetai** — from the root behind *echo*. It is used of a trumpet sounding, or thunder rolling out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How far"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **not only through Macedonia and Achaia**; your trust in God has **gone out everywhere**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Macedonia and Achaia are the two Roman provinces of Greece. Then Paul widens it to *every place*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thessalonica sat on the Via Egnatia, the main road from the Adriatic to Byzantium, and it had a busy port. What was said there travelled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Paul's position"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **there is nothing left for us to say about it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He arrives somewhere to talk about the Thessalonians and finds the story already told."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is reporting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People **tell us themselves** what kind of welcome we found among you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Strangers are telling Paul about his own visit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And how you **turned away from your idols** and **turned to God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One movement with two directions, and Paul puts them in that order."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *turned* is **epestrepsate** — the ordinary word for turning round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God is called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To serve **the God who is living and real**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the living and true God* — **alethinos**, genuine as against a copy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to people who had just stopped serving statues."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things they do now"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **serve** the God who is living and real, and to **wait for His Son from heaven**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Serving and waiting, together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both Thessalonian letters are dominated by the return of Christ — every chapter of the first one ends on it — and Paul had been with them only a few weeks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Son described"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Son He **raised from the dead**, Jesus, who **rescues us from the wrath that is coming**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *rescues* is **rhuomenon** — present participle, the one who is rescuing. It is the same verb Paul uses of his own deliverance in Asia. See the note on 2 Corinthians 1:8–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What their welcome had cost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Acts 17: three sabbaths in the synagogue, then a mob, then Jason's house attacked, then Jason dragged before the city officials and made to post bond, and Paul and Silas sent away by night."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Weeks, and then an evacuation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You became followers of us and of the Lord, **having received the word in much affliction, with joy of the Holy Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about them at the top of the letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Remembering without ceasing **your work of faith, labour of love, and patience of hope**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three, in that order, with a genitive attached to each."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What travelled.** Not a report of Paul's preaching. Paul says what went out was their faith — and that he keeps meeting people who tell him about it before he can open his mouth."}]}, "1 Thessalonians 4:13–15": {"title": "Grief with something left in it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The earliest surviving Christian letter, written to people who had a specific worry: friends of theirs had died before the Lord came back, and they did not know what that meant for them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not tell them to stop grieving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that your sorrow is not the sorrow of people who have nothing left to hope for.* The comparison is not between grief and no grief. It is between two kinds of grief — one with a floor under it, one without."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That distinction has held up better than any attempt to talk Christians out of mourning. Paul assumes they will weep. He is dealing with what they weep into."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Fallen asleep\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses it three times in three verses. It was already a common euphemism, but in Christian use it acquired a specific edge, because sleep is the one state everybody expects to end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not being delicate about death — the next paragraph is trumpets and shouting and the dead rising. He is choosing a word whose whole force depends on waking up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument in one line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We believe that Jesus died and rose again; and in the same way, God will bring with Him those who have fallen asleep.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything rests on the parallel. Not on a general hope of the soul's survival — on one event that happened, and the claim that the same thing happens to those who belong to Him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The anxiety he is answering.** *Those of us who are still alive… will not go on ahead of the ones who have fallen asleep.* They seem to have feared their dead would miss it, or come second. Paul's correction is almost blunt: the dead in Christ are not at a disadvantage. If anything, they go first."}]}, "1 Thessalonians 4:16–18": {"title": "What the passage is actually for", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the most argued-over paragraph about the Lord's return, and Paul ends it with an instruction that tells you what he wrote it for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three sounds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With a shout, with the voice of the archangel, with the trumpet of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing hidden or quiet. The word for shout is a **command** shouted aloud — used of an officer to troops, or a captain to rowers. Whatever else this is, it is not secret."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Caught up\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek **harpazo** means to seize, to snatch away by force. It is used of the Spirit carrying Philip off, and of a child snatched up to God in Revelation. The Latin translation of it, *rapturo*, is where the English word rapture comes from — which is why that word is not in most English Bibles: it came in through Latin, not from a missing Greek term."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is not a gentle ascent. It is being grabbed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order Paul gives"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the Lord comes down", "the dead in Christ rise **first**", "then the living are caught up **together with them**", "and so we will be with the Lord always"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is still answering the question from the verses before: their dead are not left behind. The sequence exists to settle that worry, and *together* is the word carrying it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line is the point.** *Comfort one another with these words.* Not \"chart these events,\" not \"work out the timing.\" Paul wrote this to be said to grieving people, and the destination of the whole passage is the plainest thing in it: *we will be with the Lord always.* Everything else in the paragraph is the transport."}]}, "1 Thessalonians 4:9–12": {"title": "God Himself has been your teacher", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He starts a paragraph by saying he does not need to write it, and then writes it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The subject"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*About **loving one another as brothers**, there is no need for anyone to write you a word.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **peri de tes philadelphias** — concerning brotherly love."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Philadelphia** — the ordinary Greek word for affection between siblings, taken over by the church for affection between believers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason he does not need to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God Himself **has been your teacher** in this.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **theodidaktoi** — God-taught. One word, and it appears nowhere else in the Greek Bible or in surviving Greek literature before this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul appears to have coined it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the idea comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they shall all be taught by God.* — Isaiah, quoted by Jesus in John 6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Jeremiah's new covenant: *no more shall every man teach his neighbour… **for they all shall know Me**, from the least of them to the greatest*. See the note on Jeremiah 31:33–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is saying the promise has landed on a Gentile congregation in Macedonia."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The evidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are doing it, too — **for every brother in the whole of Macedonia**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a theory about them. Their reputation had travelled, and Paul had heard it from other churches."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Macedonian churches are the ones he later tells Corinth about: *in a great trial of affliction, the abundance of their joy and their deep poverty abounded in the riches of their liberality*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request anyway"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Still, dear friends, we urge you: **let there be more of it, and then more again**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **perisseuein mallon** — to abound more."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He affirms the thing fully and then asks for more of it. The commendation is not a way of leading into a rebuke."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three instructions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Make it your ambition to **live a quiet life**, to **tend to what is yours to tend**, and to **work with your own hands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for the first is **philotimeisthai hesuchazein** — literally *be ambitious to be quiet*. A deliberate contradiction; **philotimia** was the drive for public honour that ran Greek civic life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul tells them to compete at being unnoticed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Prassein ta idia** — to attend to your own affairs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter's second half deals with people who had stopped working because they expected the Lord's return imminently. The second letter is blunter: *if anyone will not work, neither shall he eat*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Work with your own hands, **just as we told you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Manual labour, which the Greek upper class despised and Paul practised — *labouring night and day, that we might not be a burden to any of you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had done it in front of them in Thessalonica."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the way you live will earn the respect of those outside**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **euschemonos** — becomingly, properly, in good form."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tous exo** — those outside. Paul is thinking about the neighbours watching."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **you will find yourselves lacking nothing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **medenos chreian echete** — have need of nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which can mean *lack nothing* or *need no one*. Both readings fit a paragraph about working with your hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But I do not want you to be ignorant, brethren, concerning those who have fallen asleep… **for the Lord Himself will descend from heaven with a shout**.* See the note on 1 Thessalonians 4:16–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most detailed passage on the Lord's return in Paul, and the paragraph before it is about minding your own business and holding down a job."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word.** **Theodidaktoi** — God-taught. It occurs nowhere else in Greek before this letter, and Paul uses it to explain why a paragraph he is writing did not need writing."}]}, "1 Thessalonians 5:16–18": {"title": "Joy, prayer and thanks", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three of the shortest sentences Paul ever wrote, and one of them is the shortest verse in the Greek New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be joyful **always**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pantote chairete** — two words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The shortest verse in the Greek New Testament. English translations make *Jesus wept* shorter, but in Greek this one wins."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Never stop praying.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **adialeiptos proseuchesthe** — pray unceasingly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Adialeiptos** — without interval. The word used of a persistent cough, and of continuous pain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses it of himself twice: *without ceasing I make mention of you in my prayers*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever the circumstances, **give thanks**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **en panti eucharisteite** — in everything give thanks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The preposition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**En panti** — *in* everything, not *for* everything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not asking anyone to be grateful that a thing happened. He says to give thanks inside whatever is happening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ephesians has the other construction — *giving thanks always for all things* — and the two verses are usually read together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **this is what God wants of you in Christ Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **touto gar thelema Theou en Christo Iesou eis humas**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the singular refers to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This* — **touto**, singular, after three imperatives."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three are one thing. Joy, prayer and thanks are not a list of separate duties."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter has just said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But concerning the times and the seasons, brethren, you have no need that I should write to you. For you yourselves know perfectly that **the day of the Lord so comes as a thief in the night**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore let us not sleep, as others do, but let us watch and be sober.* See the note on 1 Thessalonians 5:4–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being written to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A congregation a few months old, in Macedonia, under pressure from the start."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For you, brethren, became imitators of the churches of God which are in Judea… **for you also suffered the same things from your own countrymen**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had been run out of the city by a mob and had not been able to get back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not **quench the Spirit**. Do not **despise prophecies**. **Test all things; hold fast what is good**. Abstain from every form of evil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me sbennute** — do not extinguish, as of a fire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now may the God of peace Himself sanctify you completely; and may your **whole spirit, soul, and body** be preserved blameless at the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who calls you is faithful, who also will do it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pistos ho kalon humas, hos kai poiesei** — faithful is the one calling you, who also will do it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fifteen imperatives in the closing paragraphs, and then a sentence saying who does it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says from a cell"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rejoice in the Lord always. Again I will say, rejoice!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not be anxious about anything, but in every single thing, **by prayer and by asking, with thanks alongside**.* See the note on Philippians 4:6–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same three, in the same order, in a different letter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The singular.** *This is what God wants* — **touto**, one thing, after three imperatives. Joy, prayer and thanks are named as a single item."}]}, "1 Thessalonians 5:23–24": {"title": "He will do it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul closes his earliest letter with a prayer and then answers it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And may **the God of peace Himself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **autos de ho theos tes eirenes** — and the *Himself* is emphatic and first."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just given them fifteen rapid instructions — rejoice always, pray without ceasing, give thanks in everything, do not quench the Spirit, test all things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he stops giving instructions and prays, and the first word is *Himself*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**God of peace** — Paul's habitual sign-off. Romans, Philippians, 2 Corinthians, Hebrews all have it near the end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Shalom** behind it: not the absence of trouble but the presence of wholeness, the state of nothing being missing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Make you **holy, through and through**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hagiasai holoteleis** — sanctify you wholly. **Holoteles** occurs once in the New Testament: complete to the end, whole all the way through."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And may your whole **spirit and soul and body** be **kept without fault**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only place in Scripture the three are listed together in this order."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Pneuma**, **psuche**, **soma**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews has the sword *piercing even to the division of soul and spirit*, which implies a seam. Paul names all three and asks for all three."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second adjective"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your **whole** spirit and soul and body.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek here is **holokleron** — a different word from the one before it. Whole in every part, with nothing lacking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James uses it of the man who lets endurance finish its work: *perfect and complete, lacking nothing*. And Peter uses it in Acts of the healed man at the temple gate — *this perfect soundness*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks for both kinds of wholeness in one sentence: complete to the end, and complete in every part."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The deadline"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At **the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parousia** — the arrival, the royal visit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word occurs in every chapter of this letter. Every one of the five ends by pointing at it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be **kept**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **teretheie** — guarded, watched over, preserved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Passive. The Thessalonians are not asked to do the keeping."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The One who **calls you is faithful**. **He will do it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words in Greek: **hos kai poiesei** — who also will do it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul makes a request, and then says who it is addressed to and what He is like, and the consequence is that it will happen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The calling is present tense — **ho kalon**, the one calling you. Not a past event he is appealing to; an ongoing act."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same construction elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Being confident of this very thing, that **He who has begun a good work in you will complete it** until the day of Jesus Christ.* See the note on Philippians 1:3–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God is faithful, by whom you were called into the fellowship of His Son.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If we are faithless, He remains faithful; He cannot deny Himself.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The earliest surviving Christian document — written to a church Paul had spent about three weeks with before being run out of town."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been worried sick about them: *when I could no longer endure it, I sent to know your faith*. Timothy came back with good news, and the letter is mostly relief."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last lines"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Brethren, pray for us. Greet all the brethren with a holy kiss. I charge you by the Lord that this epistle be read to all the holy brethren.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you. Amen.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who does the sanctifying.** Every verb in the prayer is passive or has God as its subject, and Paul answers his own prayer in the next sentence: *He will do it.*"}]}, "1 Thessalonians 5:4–8": {"title": "The helmet, and what it is made of", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just described the day coming like a thief, and here is what he says about that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the thief surprises"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **you, brothers, are not living in the dark**, so that the day should come upon you the way a thief comes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thief image belongs to the previous paragraph, and Paul immediately says it does not apply to them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A thief surprises people who are asleep in the dark. His readers are described as neither."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every one of you is **a child of the light, a child of the day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew idiom — *son of* — meaning belonging to, characterised by."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We do not belong to the night, and we do not belong to the darkness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated as fact before anything is asked. Paul's habit everywhere: the identity first, then the instruction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **let us not drowse** along with everyone else; **let us stay awake, and keep our heads clear**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *keep our heads clear* is **nepho** — to be sober, unintoxicated, and by extension clear-minded."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter uses the same word: *be sober, be vigilant, because your adversary the devil walks about like a roaring lion.* See the note on 1 Peter 5:8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The observation about night"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sleepers do their sleeping at night, and those who drink themselves senseless do it at night — **but we belong to the day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A plain observation about how people behave, used as an argument about identity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The armour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Put on **faith and love as armour across the heart**, and **the hope of salvation as a helmet**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two pieces only. Paul gives the full set in Ephesians 6; here he names two, and both are placed over things that need protecting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The breastplate** covers the heart — faith and love."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The helmet** covers the head — hope."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the helmet is made of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hope. Specifically *the hope of salvation*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The head is where the arguments happen, and Paul's protection for it is not a technique for managing thoughts. It is an expectation about the outcome."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What \"salvation\" means here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The very next verse says: *for God did not appoint us to wrath, but to obtain salvation through our Lord Jesus Christ.* See the note on 1 Thessalonians 5:9–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Soteria** — the New Testament's wide word, covering rescue, healing, wholeness, preservation. The same root used of a woman made well and a sinking man pulled out of the water."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the line after that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who died for us, that **whether we wake or sleep, we should live together with Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just spent a paragraph on waking and sleeping, and then says the outcome is the same either way."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul says the readers already are.** *Not in darkness. Children of light. Not appointed to wrath.* Three things stated flatly in five verses, and the helmet is what keeps them in the head."}]}, "1 Thessalonians 5:9–11": {"title": "\"God never marked us out for wrath\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent a chapter and a half on the Lord's return, and this is the conclusion he draws from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The appointment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God never marked us out for wrath. He marked us out to come into salvation through our Lord Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is about setting or appointing — placing someone in a category. Paul denies one appointment and states another, and both are God's doing rather than the reader's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Thessalonians had been anxious. The letter has already reassured them about their dead; now it settles the other question, which is what is coming for the living."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Awake or asleep\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that awake or asleep, we are alive with Him, and alive together.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has used *asleep* for the Christian dead all through chapter 4. Here he uses both words in one clause and says the outcome is identical."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whether the Lord finds a believer alive or already buried makes no difference to the result — *alive with Him, and alive together.* It is the same reassurance he gave earlier, compressed to a phrase."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he tells them to do about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So keep encouraging one another, and keep building one another up.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The practical instruction attached to the second coming is not preparation, not calculation, and not vigilance about dates. It is to speak to each other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He said the same at the end of chapter 4 — *comfort one another with these words*. Twice, and both times the teaching about the return is handed over as material for encouraging somebody else."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last clause.** *Which is exactly what you are already doing.* Paul adds it almost in passing, and it is characteristic — he tells a church to do the thing and then notes that they are doing it. The instruction arrives as confirmation rather than correction."}]}, "1 Timothy 1:1–2": {"title": "My own true son", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses of address, and both halves are unusual."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Not by his own choosing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ — **not by any choosing of mine, but under orders from God our Savior** and from the Lord Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kat' epitagen** — by command, by injunction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses this construction only here and in Titus. Elsewhere it is *by the will of God*. **Under orders** is stronger, and it is aimed at a situation in Ephesus where Paul's authority is going to be exercised hard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title given to the Father"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God **our Savior**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul normally reserves *Savior* for Christ. In the letters to Timothy and Titus he applies it to the Father repeatedly — six times across the three letters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title given to Christ"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Lord Jesus Christ, **who is our hope**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *who gives us hope*. The hope is a person, in apposition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same construction as *Christ in you, the hope of glory*. See the note on Colossians 1:26–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To Timothy, **my own true son in the faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **gnesio tekno** — legitimate child, born in wedlock. The word is the opposite of *nothos*, illegitimate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses it of Timothy here and of Titus in the same phrasing. It is a legal word about parentage, borrowed for a man he led to Christ."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Timothy was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The son of a certain Jewish woman who believed, but his father was Greek.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mixed parentage, which in that setting made his standing ambiguous — and Paul opens with the word for a child whose standing is not ambiguous at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The genuine faith that is in you, which dwelt first in your grandmother Lois and your mother Eunice.* See the note on 2 Timothy 1:3–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same adjective, **anupokritos**, applied to the faith."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three-word greeting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*May **grace and mercy and peace** be yours.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's standing greeting is two words: *grace and peace*. To Timothy — both letters — he adds **mercy** in the middle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does it for no other church and no other man except in the same personal letters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why mercy might be there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's own account, a few verses later: *I obtained mercy because I did it ignorantly in unbelief*, and *I obtained mercy, that in me first Jesus Christ might show all longsuffering*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word he inserts into the greeting is the word he uses of himself twice in the first chapter. See the note on 1 Timothy 1:12–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As I urged you when I went into Macedonia — **remain in Ephesus that you may charge some that they teach no other doctrine**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A young man left in charge of a difficult church, addressed as a legitimate son by a man writing under orders."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To Timothy, **a son I love dearly**.* See the note on 2 Timothy 1:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The legal word becomes an affectionate one, and by then Paul is in prison writing his last letter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word for son.** **Gnesios** — legitimate, born in wedlock. A legal term about parentage, used of a man whose actual parentage was mixed and whose standing was not."}]}, "1 Timothy 3:16": {"title": "The secret at the heart of it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Six clauses in a row, and they are almost certainly a hymn the churches already sang."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **beyond all argument**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **homologoumenos** — by common confession, admittedly. It signals something everybody agreed on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **secret at the heart of a godly life** is a great one.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **to tes eusebeias musterion** — the mystery of godliness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Mystery** in Paul is not a puzzle. It is something that could not have been worked out and had to be disclosed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Godliness** — **eusebeia**, the theme word of the letter. See the note on 1 Timothy 4:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The secret of godliness turns out not to be a practice. It is a person, in six lines."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it reads as a hymn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek has six parallel clauses, each an aorist passive verb followed by a noun, in three pairs. The rhythm is regular and the structure is too tight for prose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does the same in Philippians 2 and Colossians 1 — quoting material his readers already knew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The six"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God was **shown to us in a human body**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *manifested in flesh*. Some manuscripts read *He who*, others *God* — the difference is one letter in Greek uncials, and it has been argued over since the early centuries."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Shown to be **right in the Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *justified in spirit* — vindicated, declared in the right."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans says where: *declared to be the Son of God with power… by the resurrection from the dead*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seen by **angels**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the birth, in the wilderness, in the garden, and at the tomb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Announced **among the nations**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *preached among the Gentiles*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Believed on **across the world**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Taken up **into glory**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three pairs alternate between the unseen realm and this one:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["flesh / Spirit", "angels / nations", "world / glory"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Down, up, out, and up again. It is not chronological — the ascension is last, but the preaching to the nations came after it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is thematic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits in the letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Directly after the qualifications for overseers and deacons, and the reason Paul gives for writing:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that you may know **how you ought to conduct yourself in the house of God**, which is the church of the living God, **the pillar and ground of the truth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ephesus had the temple of Artemis, one of the seven wonders, famous for its pillars."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul calls the congregation the pillar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now the Spirit expressly says that **in latter times some will depart from the faith**, giving heed to deceiving spirits.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hymn is placed between a description of church order and a warning about defection."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same word goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People who imagine that **godliness is a way of getting on in the world**.* See the note on 1 Timothy 6:3–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three uses of one noun in one letter: the mystery of it, the training for it, and the counterfeit."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the secret turns out to be.** Not a discipline. Six lines about one person, in a rhythm the church was already singing — and Paul quotes it rather than composing it."}]}, "1 Timothy 4:11–13": {"title": "\"Do not let anyone dismiss you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's instruction to a young man left running a difficult church."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give these instructions, and teach them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parangelle** — a military word, to pass on an order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not let anyone **dismiss you because you are young**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kataphroneito** — think down on, despise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Young** — **neotes** could cover anyone up to about forty in that culture. Timothy had been travelling with Paul for over a decade and was still the junior man in the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What replaces the credential"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let them see in you instead **the pattern of what a believer is**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tupos** — a stamp, a mould, the impression a die leaves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer to being dismissed is not an argument about authority."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The six"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In **what you say**, in **the way you live**, in **love**, in **spirit**, in **faith**, in **purity**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two are outward — speech and conduct. Four are inward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three public duties"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until I come, give your attention to:*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The reading of the Scriptures aloud** — the Greek is **te anagnosei**, the public reading. It was the synagogue practice carried into the church: a passage read out to a congregation where almost nobody owned a copy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Calling people up to what is right** — **paraklesei**, exhortation, the same root as the word for the Helper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Teaching** — **didaskalia**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read, exhort, teach. That is the whole job description."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Timothy was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ephesus. Paul had left him there *that you may charge some that they teach no other doctrine* — a church he had spent three years in, and had warned that *from among your own selves men will rise up*. See the note on Acts 20:28–31."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not neglect **the gift that is in you**, which was given to you by prophecy with the laying on of the hands of the eldership.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Meditate on these things; **give yourself entirely to them**, so that **your progress may be evident to all**.* See the note on 1 Timothy 4:14–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *progress* is **prokope** — a pioneering word, cutting a way forward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter has already dealt with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Myths and endless genealogies, people wanting to be teachers of the law *understanding neither what they say nor the things which they affirm*, and — in the chapter directly before this — men *forbidding to marry and commanding to abstain from foods*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's answer to that: *every creature of God is good, and nothing is to be refused if it is received with thanksgiving*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Years later, in his last: *but you — hold your ground in what you learned and came to be sure of.* See the note on 2 Timothy 3:14–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *stir up the gift of God which is in you through the laying on of my hands. For God has not given us a spirit of fear, but of power and of love and of a sound mind.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which suggests the timidity was real and persistent."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is offered against being written off.** Not seniority and not volume. A pattern visible in six things — and three public duties: read it out, urge them, teach."}]}, "1 Timothy 4:14–16": {"title": "What \"meditate\" meant, and what \"saved\" covers", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Take these things to heart, our text says — and that is the right feel for it. Underneath sits a Greek word with more packed into it than any one English phrase can carry, so here is what is in there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "It is the Psalm 1 word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word is meletao. It is the word the Greek Old Testament uses in Psalm 1 — the man whose delight is in the law, and who meditates in it day and night."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And look at what Paul said two verses earlier: give your attention to the reading of the Scriptures aloud. Then he says this. He is telling a young minister to do with these things what the blessed man of Psalm 1 does with the law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "It is not emptying your mind. It is filling it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Behind the Greek stands a Hebrew word, hagah. It does not mean sitting still and going blank. It means muttering, murmuring, going over something under your breath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same Hebrew word gets used for a lion growling over its food, and for a dove moaning. It is a noise word. Biblical meditation is closer to chewing something over out loud than it is to silence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And it also means practice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In ordinary Greek, meletao is what a public speaker does when he drills a speech — going over it until it is in him and he no longer has to reach for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So both are in the one word: turn it over, and let it become yours. Say it until it lives in you."}, {"k": "note", "t": "One thing not to push too far. The Greek word for meditate does cover quiet reflection as well — the noisy, muttering sense comes from the Hebrew word behind the Psalm, not from the Greek on its own. The claim that holds is this: biblical meditation means filling your mind with something and going over it, not emptying your mind. That much is solid, and it is enough."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why \"profiting\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is prokope. It comes from a verb meaning to cut forward — the way someone hacks a path through undergrowth ahead of everyone else. It is not money quietly accumulating. It is ground gained."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is saying: stay in this, and people will be able to see the path opening up in front of you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And now the word this block ends on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look at how it finishes: you will be the saving of yourself and of everyone who listens to you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is sozo."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sozo means to save, to rescue, to heal, to make whole, to preserve, to bring safely through. Complete salvation and complete deliverance — in any and every sense a person could need it.** That is not a devotional stretch. That is the size of the word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In English, saved has narrowed until it means one thing: getting out of hell. The Greek word never narrowed like that, and it never had to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "A picture of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Think of a man pulled out of the sea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The English word stops at the moment he stops sinking. He did not drown. That is saved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But the rescue is not finished there, and no one who has actually been rescued thinks it is. He is hauled over the side. The water is got out of him. Somebody wraps him in something dry, puts hot food in his hands, and sits with him until he stops shaking. He is carried to shore. He is taken home. In the morning he wakes up in his own bed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sozo is the whole of that. Not the instant he stopped going under — everything from the water to the front door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same word does all of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word the disciples shout when the boat is going down in the storm and they wake Jesus: save us, we are perishing. Nobody in that boat is thinking about the afterlife. They mean get us out of this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word used when a blind beggar receives his sight. It is the word used of the woman who had been ill for twelve years, when she touches Him and the bleeding stops. It is the word James uses for a sick man raised up when the church prays over him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is the word for the rescue of a life, finally and for ever."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One word, wide enough to hold all of it — because what God does with a person was never divided into compartments in the first place."}]}, "1 Timothy 4:6–8": {"title": "Train yourself", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul tells a young minister what to feed on and what to train for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep **laying all of this before the brothers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hupotithemenos** — to set before, to suggest. It is a gentle word; not commanding, but putting something in front of people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man **fed and grown strong on the words of the faith** and on **the good teaching you have followed closely all along**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **entrephomenos** — nourished on, brought up on. A word about long feeding rather than a meal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Followed closely** — **parekolouthekas**, to follow alongside, to trace out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What to leave"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Turn away from **godless tales and the old fireside stories people love to repeat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **bebelous kai graodeis muthous** — profane and old-womanish myths."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter has already named the problem: *fables and endless genealogies, which cause disputes rather than godly edification*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Train yourself **the way an athlete trains**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **gumnaze** — the root of *gymnasium*. It meant training naked, as Greek athletes did."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A word from the gymnasium, used to a man in Ephesus where the games were part of civic life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What to train for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And train for **a life turned toward God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eusebeia** — godliness, reverence. It is the theme word of both letters to Timothy; Paul uses it ten times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Working the body **has its uses, but they are small ones**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *bodily exercise profits for a little*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not dismiss it. He grants the benefit and measures it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other side"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A life turned toward God is **useful in every direction**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *profitable for all things*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it carries **a promise both for the life we are living now and for the life that is coming**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two lives named, and the promise attaches to both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter says the same about wealth two chapters later: *God gives us richly all things to enjoy*. See the note on 1 Timothy 6:17–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What godliness is, in this letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not behaviour. Paul defines it in chapter 3, and it is a person:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And beyond all argument, **the secret at the heart of a godly life is a great one: God was shown to us in a human body**.* See the note on 1 Timothy 3:16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The training is toward that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the false version looks like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters on: *people who imagine that **godliness is a way of getting on in the world**.* See the note on 1 Timothy 6:3–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same word, put to a different use, and Paul says to keep away from them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not let anyone dismiss you because you are young.* And: *give your attention to the reading of the Scriptures aloud, to calling people up to what is right, to teaching.* See the note on 1 Timothy 4:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *meditate on these things; give yourself entirely to them, so that **your progress may be evident to all**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is a faithful saying and worthy of all acceptance.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses that formula five times across the pastoral letters, and it appears to mark something the churches already said."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul grants and what he measures.** Bodily training is useful — for a little. The comparison is not against exercise; it is a proportion, with a promise attached to one side covering two lives."}]}, "1 Timothy 5:17–18": {"title": "\"Double portion of honour\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul tells Timothy how to treat the men who lead, and the word he uses does two jobs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The elders who **lead well** should be counted worthy of **a double portion of honour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *honour* is **time** — and it means both esteem and price. It is the same noun Peter uses in *give her honour* to husbands, and it is the ordinary word for the cost of a thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's proof text settles which sense he has in mind here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two quotations"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not muzzle an ox while it treads out the grain**\"* — Deuteronomy 25."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The worker has earned his pay**\"* — and this one is a saying of Jesus, from Luke 10, when He sent out the seventy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes Moses and Jesus in one breath and calls both of them *the Scripture*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Gospel of Luke was recent, and Paul is already citing it alongside the law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ox, again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He used the same verse in 1 Corinthians 9, with the same application, and there he spelled out the logic: *is it oxen God is concerned about, or does He say it entirely for our sake?* See the note on 1 Corinthians 9:7–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is an animal working with food under its feet all day. The law forbids tying its mouth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Especially those who preach and teach\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **most of all those who wear themselves out** in preaching and teaching.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is to labour to exhaustion — the word Paul uses of his own work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He distinguishes within the group: elders who lead well get the double portion, and those who wear themselves out teaching get it most."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What surrounds this in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before are about widows — who to support, who not to, at length. The verses after are about accusations against an elder: *do not receive an accusation against an elder except from two or three witnesses.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the passage is a run of practical instructions about money and reputation inside a church, and this one sits in the middle of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Paul's own practice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He took none of it. He argued the right thoroughly in Corinth and then made tents, *that we might not be a burden to any of you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He legislates generosity toward others and declines it for himself, and both are in his letters without any sense of contradiction."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse right after.** *Do not receive an accusation against an elder except from two or three witnesses.* Pay them properly and protect them from casual accusation — Paul puts the two instructions side by side, and both are about not making a hard job harder."}]}, "1 Timothy 6:11–12": {"title": "Run away, chase after, take hold", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul gives a young man three verbs and one title."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But you, **man of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anthrope theou**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the standing Old Testament title for a prophet — Moses, Samuel, Elijah, Elisha. Paul uses it of Timothy, twice in the two letters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Run away from all of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pheuge** — flee. The root of *fugitive*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**All of it** — what he has just described: men who *suppose that godliness is a means of gain*, and the love of money as a root everything grows out of. See the note on 1 Timothy 6:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's counsel there is not to resist. It is to leave."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the same about immorality — *flee sexual immorality* — and about idolatry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Chase after what is right.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dioke** — pursue, hunt down. The word used of persecutors chasing their target."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the same verb of love: *pursue love*. See the note on 1 Corinthians 14:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Run from one thing, run after another. Both are running."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After **what is right**, after **a life turned toward God**, after **faith**, **love**, **endurance**, **gentleness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six in the Greek: righteousness, godliness, faith, love, patience, meekness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last is **praupathian** — gentleness of spirit. It occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Fight **the good fight that faith fights**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **agonizou ton kalon agona tes pisteos** — a triple play on the same root. **Agon** is a contest, a struggle in the arena; the root of *agony*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a battle. A contest, with a prize at the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fourth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Take hold of **the life that lasts**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epilabou** — seize, grasp firmly. The same verb used of Jesus taking the sinking Peter by the hand, and of Him taking the man with dropsy by the arm. See the note on Luke 14:1–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The life **you were called into**, the life **you spoke for so well in front of many witnesses**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses about the past. He was called, and he made a public confession."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The taking hold is of something already given and already professed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I urge you in the sight of God **who gives life to all things**, and before Christ Jesus **who witnessed the good confession before Pontius Pilate**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Timothy's confession before witnesses, and Christ's before Pilate, in one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Timothy was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A young man Paul had picked up at Lystra, son of a Jewish mother and a Greek father, left in charge at Ephesus — the city where Paul had warned that *savage wolves will come in among you*. See the note on Acts 20:28–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second letter tells him *let no one despise your youth*, and *stir up the gift of God which is in you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Command those who are rich in this present age **not to be haughty, nor to trust in uncertain riches but in the living God, who gives us richly all things to enjoy**.* See the note on 1 Timothy 6:17–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter warns about wanting money and ends by telling the wealthy to enjoy what they have and give it away."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The four verbs.** Flee, pursue, contend, take hold. Three of them are about motion, and the last one is about something that was already handed over."}]}, "1 Timothy 6:17–19": {"title": "What to tell the rich", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul gives Timothy instructions for the wealthy people in his congregation, and they are not the ones anybody expects."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not told to stop being rich, to divest, or to feel guilty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are also not congratulated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two warnings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not to **carry themselves high**, and not to **set their hope on riches**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pride, and misplaced confidence. Both are about the inside of a person rather than a bank balance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Since **there is nothing certain about riches**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is the uncertainty of wealth. Paul states it as a plain fact about the asset class."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What to hope in instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But on **the living God, who pours out on us everything we have and means us to enjoy it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *who gives us richly all things to enjoy* — **eis apolausin**, for enjoyment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That clause is doing a lot of work. Paul, in a passage warning about money, says God supplies things generously and intends them to be enjoyed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no asceticism in the instruction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four commands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Tell them to **do good**, to be **rich in the kind of works that count**, to be **openhanded** and **quick to share**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rich in works — Paul uses the wealth vocabulary for something other than money in the same sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Openhanded** and **ready to share** are two different Greek words, and both are about willingness rather than amount."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The investment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In that way they will be **laying by a good foundation for the age that is coming**, so that they may **take hold of the life that lasts**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a banking metaphor — storing up, laying away treasure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus says it more sharply: *lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits in the letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten verses earlier is the famous one: *the love of money is a root of all kinds of evil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not money — the love of it. And Paul distinguishes carefully: *those who **desire to be rich** fall into temptation and a snare.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He addresses the ones who *desire to be rich* first, and then, separately, those who already are. Two different groups, two different instructions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the sentence in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now godliness with **contentment** is great gain.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **autarkeia** — sufficiency, having enough. The same word Paul uses of himself: *I have learned, in whatever state I am, to be content.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says just before the contentment line.** *We brought nothing into this world, and it is certain we can carry nothing out.* The argument for contentment is logistical."}]}, "1 Timothy 6:3–5": {"title": "A craving for arguments", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul describes a kind of teacher, and the diagnosis is medical."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The test"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If anyone teaches something other than this, and **will not come over to sound words, the words of our Lord Jesus Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *sound* is **hugiainousi** — healthy, sound in body. The root of *hygiene*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses it repeatedly in the pastoral letters: sound words, sound doctrine, sound in the faith. The metaphor throughout is health."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half of the test"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And to **the teaching that goes with a life turned toward God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kat' eusebeian** — according to godliness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Doctrine and life, and the teaching in question fails both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The character"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is **swollen with his own importance** and **understands nothing at all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tetuphotai** — from **tuphos**, smoke. To be puffed up, wrapped in fog."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both in one clause: inflated, and knowing nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What he has is **a sickness: a craving for disputes and for battles over words**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **noson** — a disease — and the participle means *morbidly craving*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reverses his own metaphor. The teaching is not *healthy*; the teacher is *sick*, and the sickness is an appetite for argument."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Battles over words** — **logomachias**, word-fights. A term Paul appears to have coined."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it produces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five things, listed:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Out of that come **envy, quarreling, insults, ugly suspicions**, and **the endless rubbing of one man against another**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last is **diaparatribai** — constant friction, wearing away by rubbing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every item is relational. None of them is a doctrinal error."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fruit of the teaching is what identifies it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The people"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Among people **whose minds have been ruined** and **who have been robbed of the truth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *robbed* is **apesteremenon** — defrauded, deprived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The motive"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People who **imagine that godliness is a way of getting on in the world**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *supposing that godliness is a means of gain* — **porismon**, a source of income."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep away from such people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words. Paul does not tell Timothy to argue with them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The counter-statement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **godliness with contentment is great gain**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes the word they were using — **porismos**, gain — and reassigns it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their version is godliness as a way of getting; his is godliness plus contentment as the getting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For we brought nothing into this world, and it is certain we can carry nothing out. And having food and clothing, with these we shall be content.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the warning about those determined to be rich, and the misquoted verse about the love of money. See the note on 1 Timothy 6:9–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Command those who are rich in this present age **not to be haughty, nor to trust in uncertain riches but in the living God, who gives us richly all things to enjoy**.* See the note on 1 Timothy 6:17–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul warns about wanting money and then tells the wealthy to enjoy what they have and give it away."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the false teacher is identified.** Not by a doctrinal test in the first instance. By an appetite — a craving for disputes — and by five things that come out of it, all of which happen between people."}]}, "1 Timothy 6:9–10": {"title": "\"A root, and every kind of evil grows up out of it\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most misquoted sentence in the New Testament, and the wording is precise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the verse says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the love of money** is a root, and every kind of evil grows up out of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **philarguria** — literally *silver-love*. One word, and it is the subject of the sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not money. The love of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"A root\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek has no definite article: *a root of all kinds of evil*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not claiming this is the single origin of everything wrong in the world. He is saying it is a root, and that a great variety grows off it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is described"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Men **set on getting rich**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *those who are determined to be rich* — **boulomai**, wanting, resolving, setting the will on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is describing an aim, not a bank balance. Two verses earlier he was talking about contentment; a few verses later he addresses *those who are rich in this present age* and does not tell them to stop being rich. He tells them to be generous and ready to give. See the note on 1 Timothy 6:17–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rich are given instructions. The ones determined to get rich are given a warning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trap"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Walk **straight into the bait and the trap**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **peirasmos** (testing, enticement) and **pagis** (a snare, a bird trap)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A snare works by having something in it worth walking toward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And into **a whole crowd of cravings that make no sense and do no good**, the kind that **drag a person down into ruin and destruction**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words for the ending — **olethros** and **apoleia**. Paul doubles it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two consequences"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Some have **reached out for it** and been **led away from the faith**, and have **run themselves through with sorrow upon sorrow**.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Led away from the faith** — the Greek is *wandered away*, drifted off course.", "**Run themselves through** — **peripeiro**, to impale, to pierce all the way through. A violent word, and it is reflexive. They did it to themselves."]}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul is not saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not saying money is evil, that the poor are safer, or that wealth disqualifies anybody. He says none of those things in the passage, and eight verses later he tells Timothy that God *gives us richly all things to enjoy*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The object of the warning is a craving, and the injury described is self-inflicted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The context"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens on false teachers *who suppose that godliness is a means of gain* — men using the ministry to get money."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is who Paul is writing about. The warning has a specific target in the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he offers instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **godliness with contentment is great gain**. For we brought nothing into this world, and it is certain we can carry nothing out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the profit word — **porismos**, gain — and applies it to contentment."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clause people drop.** *The love of.* Two words, and without them the sentence says something Paul does not say anywhere in his letters."}]}, "2 Chronicles 18:1–3": {"title": "\"I am as you are\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A good king ties himself to a bad one, and the chapter opens by telling you exactly how it happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now Jehoshaphat had great riches and honour, **and he joined himself to Ahab by marriage**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prosperity and the alliance are in the same breath. Jehoshaphat was one of Judah's better kings — the previous chapter has him removing idols and sending teachers out with the law through every town."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The marriage was almost certainly a sensible piece of statecraft: his son to Ahab's daughter, two kingdoms at peace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The visit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Some years later he went down to visit Ahab in Samaria. Ahab slaughtered many sheep and cattle for him… and he urged him to march up against Ramoth Gilead.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *urged* is a strong word — enticed, persuaded, worked on him. The banquet came first and the request came after."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am as you are, and my people as your people; we will join you in the war.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He agrees before asking a single question. Only afterwards does he think to say *first seek the counsel of the LORD* — and when four hundred prophets say go, and one says otherwise, and Ahab admits he hates that one for never prophesying anything good, Jehoshaphat still goes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had already given his word at the dinner."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How it ends for him.** He survives, but a prophet meets him on the way home: *should you help the wicked and love those who hate the LORD?* And years later the marriage he made brings Ahab's daughter Athaliah into Judah's royal house, where she murders almost the entire line of David. The alliance outlived the battle by a generation."}]}, "2 Chronicles 18:28–34": {"title": "\"Jehoshaphat cried out, and the LORD helped him\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two kings go into a battle they were warned about. One of them has a plan, and it involves the other one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Ahab's arrangement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will disguise myself and go into battle, but you wear your royal robes.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks his ally to stay identifiable while he becomes invisible. And the reader already knows what Ahab does not: the king of Aram has ordered his chariot commanders to fight nobody *except the king of Israel*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the disguise sends the entire enemy strike force at the man in the robes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened to the man in the robes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the chariot commanders saw Jehoshaphat, they thought, \"This is the king of Israel,\" and they turned to attack him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is surrounded, in the wrong battle, wearing the wrong clothes, because he agreed to an alliance at a banquet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Jehoshaphat cried out, and the LORD helped him. God drew them away from him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three short clauses. He was in a mess entirely of his own making, in a war he had been told not to enter, and when he shouted, God moved the army off him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No rebuke recorded at that moment. No conditions. He cried out and was helped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arrow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But someone drew his bow at random and struck the king of Israel between the sections of his armour.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A shot fired at nobody in particular, finding the one gap in a disguised man's armour. Everything Ahab had arranged to make himself safe was undone by an arrow with no aim."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chronicles reports it without comment: *at random*, and then a king propped up in his chariot until sunset, dying."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Two men, one battle.** Both were where they should not have been. One called out and was pulled clear; the other had a plan and did not survive the afternoon. The chapter puts the two outcomes side by side and leaves them there."}]}, "2 Chronicles 20:10–12": {"title": "\"We do not know what to do\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A king finishes a public prayer with an admission."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three nations are marching on Judah. Jehoshaphat *set himself to seek the LORD, and proclaimed a fast throughout all Judah*, and the whole country came to Jerusalem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He prays standing in the temple court in front of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The history he recites"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Here are the men of **Ammon and Moab and Mount Seir**. **You would not let Israel invade them** when they came out of Egypt, so **Israel turned away from them and did not destroy them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ammon and Moab are Lot's descendants; Mount Seir is Esau's country."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 2 records the instruction: *do not provoke them… I have given Mount Seir to Esau as his possession.* See the note on Deuteronomy 2:1–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel had gone round them because they were told to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The complaint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Look how they repay us** — coming to drive us out of the land You gave us to inherit.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not arguing that Judah deserves rescue. He is pointing out that the nations they spared are the ones invading."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"O our God, **will You not judge them?**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three admissions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**We have no power** against this great multitude that is coming against us.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**We do not know what to do**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But our eyes are upon You**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *no power* is *no strength*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three clauses, and the first two are concessions. He does not claim capability and does not claim a plan."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What a king is not supposed to say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jehoshaphat is standing in front of the army, the elders, the women and the children, saying he does not know what to do."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse notes who was in the room: *all Judah, **with their little ones, their wives, and their children**, stood before the LORD.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole prayer pivots on the last clause. The Hebrew is *upon You are our eyes*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is offered. The prayer ends on a direction of gaze."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then, in the middle of the assembly, **the Spirit of the LORD came on Jahaziel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Levite of the sons of Asaph — a musician's family — speaks. See the note on 2 Chronicles 20:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not be afraid or dismayed** because of this great multitude, for **the battle is not yours, but God's**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will not need to fight in this battle. Position yourselves, stand still and see the salvation of the LORD** with you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same sentence Moses gave at the sea: *stand still, and see the salvation of the LORD.* See the note on Exodus 14:10–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Appointed singers to go out in front of the army, praising *the beauty of holiness* and singing *praise the LORD, for His mercy endures forever* — the refrain of Psalm 136. See the note on Psalms 136:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when they began to sing and to praise, **the LORD set ambushes** against the people… and they were defeated.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah arrives to find the field already settled, and spends three days collecting the spoil."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the prayer ends.** Not with a strategy or a claim. Two admissions — no power, and no idea — and then a sentence about where they are looking."}]}, "2 Chronicles 20:13–15": {"title": "\"The battle is not yours\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The answer arrives while everybody is still standing there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was present"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All Judah stood there before the LORD, with **their little ones, their wives, and their children**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chronicler names them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An invading army is coming and the nation has brought its infants to a prayer meeting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the word comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then, **in the middle of the assembly**, the Spirit of the LORD came on **Jahaziel son of Zechariah — a Levite of the sons of Asaph**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the king, not a priest, and not a prophet by office."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The sons of Asaph** were the temple musicians — David's appointed singers. Twelve psalms carry Asaph's name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who speaks is from the choir."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His genealogy runs four generations deep in the verse, which is the chronicler's way of saying he was nobody in particular."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Listen, **all Judah, and you people of Jerusalem, and you, King Jehoshaphat**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king is named last."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not be afraid or dismayed** because of this great multitude.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew pair — *fear not, neither be dismayed* — is the same one Moses gave Joshua, and Joshua gave his commanders with their feet on five kings' necks. See the note on Joshua 10:22–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **the battle is not yours, but God's**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the battle is not yours but God's*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It answers the prayer's exact words. Jehoshaphat had said *we have no power against this great multitude*; the reply concedes the point and reassigns the fight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are told to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses after: *\"Tomorrow go down against them… **You will not need to fight in this battle. Position yourselves, stand still and see the salvation of the LORD** with you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four instructions and three of them are about not moving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jehoshaphat did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Jehoshaphat **bowed his head with his face to the ground**, and all Judah and the inhabitants of Jerusalem **bowed before the LORD, worshipping**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the Levites stood up *to praise the LORD God of Israel with voices loud and high*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Believe in the LORD your God, and you shall be established; believe His prophets, and you shall prosper**.\"* See the note on 2 Chronicles 20:16–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the singers were appointed *to go out before the army*, singing *praise the LORD, for His mercy endures forever*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now when they began to sing and to praise, **the LORD set ambushes** against the people of Ammon, Moab, and Mount Seir… and they were defeated.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three invading nations turned on each other. Judah arrived at the lookout and found *dead bodies fallen on the earth. No one had escaped.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they collected"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They found among them **an abundance of valuables on the dead bodies, and precious jewellery**, which they stripped off for themselves, **more than they could carry away**. And they were **three days gathering the spoil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days to pick up what fell in a battle they did not fight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name of the valley"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the fourth day they assembled in **the Valley of Berachah**, for there they blessed the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Berachah** — blessing."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who the word came through.** A man from the temple choir, in the middle of the crowd, with his genealogy given so nobody mistakes him for somebody important — and what he says is that the fight belongs to somebody else."}]}, "2 Chronicles 20:16–19": {"title": "Singers in front of the army", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three armies are coming and Judah is told the battle is not theirs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moab, Ammon and Mount Seir, marching on Jehoshaphat together. His own prayer sums it up: *we have no power against this great multitude that is coming against us, nor do we know what to do — but our eyes are on You.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He admits all three: no strength, no plan, no idea."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will not even need to fight this battle.** Take your positions, stand still, and watch the salvation of the LORD on your behalf.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same instruction Moses gave at the Red Sea, word for word in places: *stand still and see the salvation of the LORD.* See the note on Exodus 14:10–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three commands, and all three are about stopping."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And yet they go out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Tomorrow, **go out to face them**, and the LORD will be with you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not told to stay home. They have to march to the battlefield of a battle they will not fight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jehoshaphat bowed with his face to the ground, and all Judah and the people of Jerusalem fell down before the LORD to worship Him. Then the Levites… **stood up to praise the LORD with a very loud voice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole nation on the floor, and then a choir at full volume."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of march"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next morning Jehoshaphat did something no commander does. *He appointed those who should sing to the LORD, and who should praise the beauty of holiness, **as they went out before the army**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Singers in front. Unarmed men, out ahead of the soldiers, in the tribe whose name means praise. See the note on Genesis 49:8–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they sang"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Praise the LORD, for His mercy endures forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six words. It is the refrain of Psalm 136, repeated twenty-six times in that psalm, and it is what Judah took into a valley with three armies in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the enemy. Nothing about the danger. One line about His mercy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **when they began to sing and to praise**, the LORD set ambushes against the people of Ammon, Moab and Mount Seir, and they were defeated.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "When they began. The timing is in the sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the time Judah reached the lookout point there was nothing standing. It took them three days to carry away what was left."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the valley was renamed.** *Beracah* — blessing. They assembled there on the fourth day to bless the LORD, and the name stuck. A place named for what happened in it, by people who had not raised a weapon."}]}, "2 Chronicles 20:20–21": {"title": "Singers out in front", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An army marches out to a battle it has been told it will not fight, with the choir leading."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they are going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Early in the morning they rose and went out into **the wilderness of Tekoa**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Tekoa is Amos's home town, south of Bethlehem, and the road out of it drops toward the Dead Sea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three armies are coming up that way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Listen to me, Judah and people of Jerusalem. **Trust in the LORD your God, and you will be held firm. Trust His prophets, and you will succeed.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses, and the Hebrew has a play on them that does not survive translation: *ha'aminu… te'amenu* — believe, and you will be made firm. The same root, twice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second half is the unusual one. He tells them to believe the prophets, and the word that had come was *you will not need to fight this battle.* See the note on 2 Chronicles 20:16–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consultation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then, **after talking it over with the people**, he appointed singers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew notes that he consulted. It was not a royal decree issued over their heads."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he appointed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Singers for the LORD, to praise Him **in the splendour of holiness**, **as they marched out ahead of the army**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In front. Unarmed men, at the head of the column, between the soldiers and three armies."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody does this. The front rank is where the shields go."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they sang"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Give thanks to the LORD, for His love endures forever.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six words. The refrain of Psalm 136, repeated twenty-six times in that psalm, and it is what Judah carried into a valley."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the enemy. Nothing about the danger. One line about His **chesed** — the covenant word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it worked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **when they began to sing and to praise**, the LORD set ambushes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the next verse, and the timing is stated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they found"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days of collecting spoil, more than they could carry away — and a valley renamed **Beracah**, blessing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it started"}, {"k": "p", "t": "With a king who prayed *we have no power against this great multitude that is coming against us, nor do we know what to do — **but our eyes are upon You**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He admitted no strength, no plan and no idea, in public, in front of the whole assembly with their wives and children standing there."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the word had been.** *You will not even need to fight this battle. Take your positions, stand still, and watch the salvation of the LORD.* The same instruction Moses gave at the sea — *stand still and see*. See the note on Exodus 14:10–14."}]}, "2 Chronicles 20:35–37": {"title": "Ships that never sailed", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last thing recorded about a good king, and it is a fleet at the bottom of a harbour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What had just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter before this is Jehoshaphat's finest hour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three armies coming at him, and his prayer: *we have no power against this great multitude, nor do we know what to do, but **our eyes are upon You***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer: *you will not need to fight in this battle. Position yourselves, stand still, and see the salvation of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He put the singers in front of the army, and the enemy destroyed each other. It took three days to collect the plunder."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word that follows it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Later, Jehoshaphat king of Judah **made an alliance with Ahaziah king of Israel, who acted wickedly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chabar** — to join, to couple, to be tied to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahaziah is Ahab's son. Two years on the throne, and the verdict on him in Kings is *he served Baal and worshipped him, and provoked the LORD God of Israel to anger*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they built"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He joined with him to **build ships to sail to Tarshish**, and they built the ships at **Ezion-geber**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezion-geber is on the Gulf of Aqaba, at the top of the Red Sea. Solomon's port."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Tarshish ships were the largest class of merchant vessel — the ocean-going kind. Solomon's had brought back gold, silver, ivory, apes and peacocks."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is a trading venture, not a war."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prophecy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **Eliezer son of Dodavahu of Mareshah** prophesied against Jehoshaphat, saying, \"**Because you have made an alliance with Ahaziah, the LORD has broken apart what you have made**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **paratz** — to break through, burst, breach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophet is named once in Scripture and this is the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The outcome"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the ships were wrecked and could not sail to Tarshish**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Never left port."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the trade. Not the destination. The partner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophet's sentence names one cause: *because you have joined yourself with Ahaziah*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern in his reign"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the third time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He married his son to Ahab's daughter, and then went to Ramoth Gilead with Ahab — *I am as you are, and my people as your people* — and nearly died in the chariot when the Syrians mistook him for the king. See the note on 2 Chronicles 18:28–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophet Jehu met him coming home: *should you help the wicked and love those who hate the LORD? Therefore the wrath of the LORD is upon you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then he did it again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the alliance cost his house"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Athaliah, Ahab's daughter, married into the family here. When her son died she murdered every royal heir she could find and ruled for six years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The line of David survived because one baby was hidden in a bedroom in the temple."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Kings says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parallel account has Ahaziah asking to join *after* the wreck — *let my servants go with your servants in the ships* — and Jehoshaphat refusing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two accounts of a partnership that ended badly, and they disagree about the order of events."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where else the word turns up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ephraim is **joined to idols**; let him alone.* Hosea, and the verb is **chabur**, the same root."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Paul: *do not be unequally yoked together with unbelievers. For what fellowship has righteousness with lawlessness?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The summary of his life"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Jehoshaphat rested with his fathers… **the rest of the acts of Jehoshaphat, first and last, indeed they are written** in the book of Jehu.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verdict elsewhere is good: *his heart took delight in the ways of the LORD*, and he sent teachers round the towns with the book of the law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chronicler records all of it, in order, and ends the reign with the ships."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the prophet named as the cause.** Not the venture. The partner — and the sentence is one line long: *because you have joined yourself with Ahaziah.*"}]}, "2 Chronicles 26:1–5": {"title": "\"As long as he sought the LORD\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Fifty-two years on the throne, and the chronicler puts the whole reign under one condition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Sixteen years old"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is a boy when they make him king. His father Amaziah had been murdered by conspirators, and the people take the son and put him on the throne."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in his position was earned."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The man beside him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He set himself to seek God **in the days of Zechariah, who taught him to fear God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the prophet who wrote the book — an otherwise unknown man, named here only, and named because Uzziah's seeking is tied to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A young king with someone teaching him. The chronicler thought that worth recording."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **as long as he sought the LORD**, God gave him success.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *gave him success* is to make prosper, to cause to advance — the same verb used of Joseph in Potiphar's house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter goes on to list what that looked like: victories over the Philistines, tribute from the Ammonites, towers built in the desert, cisterns dug, vineyards in the hills, an army of over three hundred thousand, and engines of war on the walls of Jerusalem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And his fame spread far, for he was marvellously helped, till he was strong.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Till he was strong\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "That clause is the pivot of the whole chapter, and it is doing two jobs. It marks how far the help went — and it marks where the trouble starts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *but when he had grown strong, his heart became proud, and it led to his ruin.* See the note on 2 Chronicles 26:16–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What lifted him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the east, not the west, not the desert."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is God who judges. He brings one down and lifts another up.* The psalm says promotion has one source, and this chapter is a fifty-two-year illustration of it — including the end. See the note on Psalms 75:6–8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter's phrase lands elsewhere in Scripture.** *In the year that King Uzziah died, I saw the Lord.* Isaiah dates his vision of the throne by the death of this man — the king who went into the sanctuary he had no business in, and the prophet who saw the true throne the year that king was buried."}]}, "2 Chronicles 26:16–18": {"title": "Strength turned to pride", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The sentence that ends a fifty-two-year reign is eleven words long."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hinge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **when he had grown strong**, his heart became proud, and it led to his ruin.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The strength was God's doing. The chapter has just said so twice — *God gave him success*, *he was marvellously helped, till he was strong.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the strength is what he stumbled over. The Hebrew for *led to his ruin* is to corrupt, to spoil — the same root used of the earth being corrupt before the flood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not stop being helped and then get proud. He got proud because of how far the help had gone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What pride made him do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He went into the temple of the LORD **to burn incense** on the altar of incense.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not idolatry. Not a foreign god. He walks into the house of the LORD to worship the LORD."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offence is not what he did. It is who he was when he did it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line he crossed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is **not for you**, Uzziah, to burn incense to the LORD, but **for the priests, the sons of Aaron**, who are consecrated to do so.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two offices, and God had kept them apart the whole way through Israel's history. Saul lost the kingdom for sacrificing; the sons of Korah died for claiming the censer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Uzziah had been given a throne, an army, towers, cisterns and international fame. What he had not been given was a censer, and that is the one thing he reached for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Eighty men"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Azariah the priest went in after him, and with him **eighty priests of the LORD, courageous men**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chronicler calls them courageous, and they needed to be. They walked into the sanctuary to stand in front of a king at the height of his power and tell him to leave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they told him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Leave the sanctuary, for you have been unfaithful, and **there will be no honour for you from the LORD God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had come in to add to his honour. He is told, standing there with the censer in his hand, that this will bring him none."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Only one man ever held both offices.** A king on a throne who is also a priest — Melchizedek in Genesis, and then the promise in Zechariah, *He shall be a priest upon His throne.* Uzziah reached for it and was struck. It belonged to Somebody. See the note on 2 Chronicles 26:19–21."}]}, "2 Chronicles 26:19–21": {"title": "The leprosy at the altar", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The censer is still in his hand when it happens."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Uzziah **flew into a rage**. The censer was in his hand… and **while he raged at the priests**, leprosy broke out on his forehead.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is corrected and his answer is fury. The chronicler puts the anger and the leprosy in the same sentence, in that order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it appeared"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Leprosy **on his forehead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The forehead is where the high priest wore the gold plate engraved *HOLINESS TO THE LORD*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man standing where only the priest could stand, and the mark comes out on the one place the priest's holiness was displayed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he was standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There before the priests, in the house of the LORD, **beside the altar of incense**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The location is spelled out. Not later, not at home, not on the way out. At the altar, in the middle of the act."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two directions at once"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **rushed him out**, and **he himself hurried to leave**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priests were pushing and he was already going. A leper could not remain in that house and he knew it before anyone had to explain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because the LORD had struck him.* The chronicler names who did it and does not soften it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So King Uzziah was a leper **until the day he died**. He lived in a separate house, a leper, **cut off from the house of the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fifty-two years on the throne and the last of them spent in isolation, with his son running the kingdom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Cut off from the house of the LORD* — the one place he had forced his way into is the one place he can never enter again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shape of the reign"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*as long as he sought the LORD, God gave him success*", "*he was marvellously helped, till he was strong*", "*when he had grown strong, his heart became proud*", "*the LORD had struck him*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sought, helped, strong, proud, struck. The chronicler lays it out in order so nobody can miss which step led to which."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Isaiah saw the year this man died.** *In the year that King Uzziah died, I saw the Lord, high and lifted up, and His train filled the temple.* A king who forced his way into the sanctuary is buried, and a prophet is shown the One whose throne it actually is — with a live coal from the altar carried to his mouth by a seraph, because no one takes it for himself."}]}, "2 Chronicles 29:12–17": {"title": "Sixteen days", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The chronicler names fourteen men and then counts the days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rising"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the Levites arose**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The response to Hezekiah's speech is one verb, and then a list."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fourteen men, and the chronicler groups them by family:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Kohathites** — Mahath and Joel", "**Merari** — Kish and Azariah", "**Gershonites** — Joah and Eden", "**Elizaphan** — Shimri and Jeuel", "**Asaph** — Zechariah and Mattaniah", "**Heman** — Jehuel and Shimei", "**Jeduthun** — Shemaiah and Uzziel"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first four groups are the working divisions of Levi. The last three — Asaph, Heman and Jeduthun — are the musicians, the three families David appointed over the song."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The singers turn out for the clearing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Kohath was Korah's branch, the one that carried the holy things. See the note on Numbers 16:1–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **gathered their brothers, consecrated themselves**, and went in.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order Hezekiah gave, carried out in the order he gave it: themselves, then the building."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The authority"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As **the king had commanded**, according to **the words of the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both. The chronicler puts the royal order and the divine word in the same clause."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The division of labour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **priests** went into **the inner part** of the house of the LORD to cleanse it, and they **brought out to the courtyard** all the unclean things they found.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **Levites** took them and **carried them out to the Kidron Valley**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A chain. The priests go where only priests may go, bring things as far as the court, and the Levites take them the rest of the way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody does the whole distance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Kidron — the ravine between the temple hill and the Mount of Olives."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is where Asa burned his grandmother's idol, where Josiah burned the Asherah and beat it to dust, and where the blood from the temple sacrifices drained."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus crossed it on the way to Gethsemane."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The count"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They began the consecration on **the first day of the first month**, and on **the eighth day** they came to **the porch** of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **eight more days** they consecrated the house, and on **the sixteenth day** of the first month **they finished**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight days to get from the inner house out to the porch. Eight days on the rest. Sixteen in all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the count tells you"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chronicler could have said *they cleansed the temple*. He gives the calendar instead, twice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It took over two weeks, and the first eight days produced no visible progress beyond the porch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Passover had to be moved. Chapter 30 opens with Hezekiah keeping it in the second month rather than the first — the law's provision for those who were unclean or on a journey — *for they could not keep it at the regular time, because a sufficient number of priests had not consecrated themselves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cleaning ran past the feast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was found"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text says *all the unclean things they found in the temple* without listing them. Ahaz's Damascus altar, the dismantled lavers, whatever had accumulated behind sixteen years of shut doors."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The shape of it.** A speech, a list of names, and a date at each end. The chronicler gives more space to the calendar than to the contents, and the number he lands on is sixteen."}]}, "2 Chronicles 29:3–5": {"title": "\"In the first month of the first year\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A new king's first recorded act, and it is about a set of doors."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In **the first month of the first year of his reign**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hezekiah is twenty-five. His father Ahaz had spent sixteen years importing Assyrian altars, cutting up the temple furniture, and finally shutting the building."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The son does not wait. The chronicler dates it to the first month."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The doors"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **opened the doors of the house of the LORD and repaired them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His father had *shut up the doors of the house of the LORD*, in the previous chapter, in the same words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first act of the reign is the exact reversal of the last act of the previous one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Repaired** — the Hebrew is **chazaq**, to strengthen, to make firm. The same root as his own name, **Chizqiyahu**: *the LORD strengthens*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he calls"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He brought in **the priests and the Levites** and gathered them **in the square on the east**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The east side — the side the glory left by in Ezekiel's vision, and the side it returns by. See the note on Ezekiel 10:18–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Listen to me, Levites. **Consecrate yourselves now, and consecrate the house** of the LORD.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Themselves first, then the building. He does not send them into a defiled temple unprepared."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *consecrate* is **qadash** — to set apart, to make holy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The job"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Carry out the filth from the holy place**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **niddah** — a strong word for uncleanness, used in Leviticus for ceremonial defilement and in the prophets for what is repulsive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Carry out.** It has to be picked up and physically removed. Nothing about the instruction is symbolic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was in there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahaz's imports. He had seen an altar in Damascus, sent the plans to the priest, had a copy built, and moved the bronze altar aside to make room. He cut up the bases of the lavers, took down the sea from off the bronze oxen, and closed the doors."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters of demolition, and this is the clearing up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the whole operation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Doors opened, staff called, staff consecrated, building consecrated, filth removed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the burnt offering, then the Levites with the instruments of David, then the song, and then the Passover in chapter 30 — kept a month late because there were not enough priests ready in time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hezekiah says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them why the doors had been shut, and does not spare his own father. See the note on 2 Chronicles 29:6–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict on him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He did what was right in the sight of the LORD, according to all that his father David had done.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *his father Ahaz*. The chronicler skips a generation to find the comparison."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the reign starts.** Not with an army, a treaty, or a building programme. A young king opens a door his father closed and tells the Levites to carry the rubbish out."}]}, "2 Chronicles 29:6–9": {"title": "\"As you see with your own eyes\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The young king explains to the Levites why the building is in the state it is in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he is talking about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **our fathers** were unfaithful and did evil in the eyes of the LORD our God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Our** fathers. He includes himself, standing there as the son of the man who did it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahaz was his father. Hezekiah does not name him and does not exempt himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They **forsook Him**, **turned their faces away** from the dwelling of the LORD, and **turned their backs** on Him.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forsook, turned away, turned the back. The chronicler stacks them, and each one is a movement of the body."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did to the building"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They also **shut the doors of the porch** and **put out the lamps**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two acts, both by hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lamps were the seven-branched lampstand, which was to burn *from evening until morning before the LORD* — a continual statute, forever, in Leviticus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody put it out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What stopped"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They **did not burn incense** or **offer burnt offerings** in the holy place to the God of Israel.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The incense on the golden altar and the offering on the bronze one. See the note on Exodus 30:34–38."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both daily. Both stopped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **the anger of the LORD came upon Judah and Jerusalem**, and He made them **an object of terror, of horror, and of scorn**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words for what the nation became to the countries around it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**As you see with your own eyes**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not arguing from theory. He points at the room they are standing in and the state of the country outside it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahaz's reign had brought invasion from Aram, from Israel, and finally from the Assyrians he had paid to protect him — *Tiglath-Pileser king of Assyria came to him and distressed him, and did not assist him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cost, stated plainly"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is why our fathers have **fallen by the sword**, and **our sons and daughters and wives are in captivity**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the families in the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not blame the Levites, the previous priesthood, or the political situation. He gives one cause and traces the whole list of effects to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And he does not stop there — the next verse begins *now it is in my heart to make a covenant with the LORD God of Israel.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the diagnosis goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Straight into work. The Levites rise, consecrate themselves, and start carrying things out to the Kidron Valley the same day. See the note on 2 Chronicles 29:12–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows the cleansing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offerings, the Levites with cymbals and harps *according to the commandment of David*, the song of the LORD beginning, and the whole assembly worshipping while the burnt offering burned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *Hezekiah rejoiced, and all the people, that God had prepared the people, **for the thing came about suddenly**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How he opens.** By naming what went wrong, in the first person plural, in front of the men who are about to do the work. Nobody is told the temple got that way by accident."}]}, "2 Chronicles 3:8–9": {"title": "Six hundred talents of gold", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The chronicler gives the weight of the gold in the holiest room, and then the weight of the nails."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Its length ran **the full width of the house, twenty cubits, and twenty cubits across** as well.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A perfect square in plan, and 1 Kings adds the third dimension — twenty high. A cube, about thirty feet each way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation describes the new Jerusalem the same way: *its length, breadth and height are equal.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gold"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He overlaid it with fine gold — **six hundred talents of it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A talent is somewhere around seventy-five pounds. Six hundred of them is upward of twenty tons of gold, in one room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The nails"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The nails alone weighed **fifty shekels of gold**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chronicler weighs the nails."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the smallest item in the building account, and he records it because the point of the whole passage is that nothing in that room was ordinary — down to the fastenings holding the sheets to the wall."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is in the room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verses: two cherubim, made and overlaid with gold, with a combined wingspan of twenty cubits — wing tip to wall on both sides, and the inner wings meeting in the middle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They stood on their feet, and **they faced inward**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chronicler notes which way they were looking, and it is the one detail 1 Kings does not give."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cherubim over the ark"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Separate from these, and much smaller: the two beaten out of the mercy seat itself, facing each other with their faces toward it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There I will meet with you, and I will speak with you **from above the mercy seat, from between the two cherubim**.\"* See the note on Exodus 25:17–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where cherubim appear first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the gate of Eden, with a flaming sword, keeping the way to the tree of life. See the note on Genesis 3:20–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are guards at the beginning of the Bible, and by Exodus they are hammered into the lid of a box where God says He will meet somebody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Solomon said about the room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But will God indeed dwell on the earth? Behold, heaven and the heaven of heavens cannot contain You. **How much less this temple which I have built!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty tons of gold, gold nails, and the man who commissioned it says at the dedication that it will not do."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is in front of the throne in Revelation.** Not a cube of gold. *A sea of glass, like crystal* — and around the throne, four living creatures full of eyes, who *do not rest day or night, saying, \"Holy, holy, holy.\"*"}]}, "2 Chronicles 36:20–21": {"title": "The land kept sabbath", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The exile is explained by an accounting nobody had been keeping."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jerusalem is taken. The temple burned, the walls broken down, the vessels carried off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they burned the house of God, broke down the wall of Jerusalem, burned all its palaces with fire, and destroyed all its precious possessions.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The survivors"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Those who **escaped the sword** he carried away to Babylon, where they became **servants to him and his sons** until the kingdom of Persia came to power.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **avadim** — slaves, servants."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The explanation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **the word of the LORD spoken through Jeremiah was fulfilled**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah 25: *this whole land shall be a desolation and an astonishment, and these nations shall serve the king of Babylon **seventy years***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was fulfilled"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **land enjoyed its sabbath rests**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ratzetah ha'aretz et-shabbetoteha** — the land paid off, or made up, its sabbaths."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ratzah** — to be pleased with, or to satisfy a debt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The law behind it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Six years you shall sow your field… but **in the seventh year there shall be a sabbath of solemn rest for the land**, a sabbath to the LORD. You shall neither sow your field nor prune your vineyard.* See the note on Leviticus 25:1–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every seventh year the land lay fallow."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The threat attached"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the land shall enjoy its sabbaths as long as it lies desolate and you are in your enemies' land… **as long as it lies desolate it shall rest for the time it did not rest on your sabbaths when you dwelt in it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus 26 states the consequence in advance, using the same verb."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventy sabbath years, one every seven, is four hundred and ninety years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roughly the span from the settlement in the land to the exile."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Chronicler is saying the account had been running the whole time and nobody had noticed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All the days it lay desolate **it kept sabbath**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kol-yemei hoshammah shavatah**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land is the subject. It rests, whether or not anyone gives it permission."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest was going to happen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It could be taken in seven-year instalments, with the people on the land, or all at once with them gone. The Chronicler says which way it went."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the tally belonged to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in Israel was counting. The debt accrued silently for centuries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Daniel does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the first year of his reign I, Daniel, understood by the books the number of the years specified by the word of the LORD through Jeremiah the prophet, that **He would accomplish seventy years in the desolations of Jerusalem**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then I set my face toward the Lord God to make request by prayer.* See the note on Daniel 9:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He read the number, worked out where he was in it, and prayed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the book ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now in the first year of Cyrus king of Persia… the LORD stirred up the spirit of Cyrus, so that he made a proclamation: \"**All the kingdoms of the earth the LORD God of heaven has given me. And He has commanded me to build Him a house at Jerusalem**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Who is among you of all His people? **May the LORD his God be with him, and let him go up**!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last verse of Chronicles, and the Hebrew Bible ends on **veya'al** — let him go up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on Isaiah 45:1–4."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The accounting.** Seventy sabbath years, unkept over four hundred and ninety, taken in one instalment. The land was going to rest either way."}]}, "2 Corinthians 1:15–20": {"title": "Where the Amen actually goes", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the verse this app opens on, and it is usually quoted from halfway through. The half that gets left off is the reason Paul wrote it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He is defending a change of travel plans"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is genuinely what starts it. Paul said he would visit, then did not, and somebody in Corinth used it to suggest he was unreliable — a man whose yes meant nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His answer runs: *do I make my plans the way anyone makes them, so that with me Yes, yes and No, no come out of the same mouth in the same breath?* Then, instead of defending his diary, he moves straight to the character of the message he preached. My travel arrangements are not the point, he says. What we brought you was never *Yes and No at once*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything after this — the most quoted sentence in the letter — is Paul arguing from God's consistency, not his own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"However many promises God has made\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is deliberately open-ended. Not *some*. Not *the important ones*. However many there are, they all find their Yes in one place: **in Him.**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which locates them. The promises are not free-floating guarantees attached to circumstances, and they are not conditional on the reader's steadiness — the whole argument began with Paul's own unsteadiness. They are held in Christ, and that is why they can be described with a word as flat as *always*."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the Amen ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here is the part almost always dropped:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "*and in Him the Amen, spoken through us to the glory of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amen is a Hebrew word meaning firm, settled, so be it. It is what a congregation says back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's picture has both halves: God says Yes in Christ, and the Amen comes back *through us*. The promises are answered by God, and then answered again by the people who trust them. Nobody has to generate the Yes. What is left for a person to do is agree with it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a very different act from working to secure the promise, and it is why the sentence ends where it does — *to the glory of God*. The praise goes to the one who said Yes, not to the one who managed to believe it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it matters that this began with a broken plan"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's point lands harder because of where it started. A man whose word had just been questioned uses the moment to say: whatever you think of my reliability, God's Yes does not run on the same terms."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"a second helping of grace.\"** Paul's phrase for the visit he had planned. He thought of turning up in person as another portion of grace for them. It is a small thing, but it is the vocabulary of a man who thought of grace as something you get more of, not something you use up."}]}, "2 Corinthians 1:8–11": {"title": "\"He is pulling us out still\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul tells them how bad it got, and then uses one verb three times in three tenses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disclosure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We would rather you knew, brothers and sisters, about **the trouble that came on us in Asia**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He chooses to tell them. He never says what it was — the riot at Ephesus, an illness, a sentence, a shipwreck; the letters do not specify."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What he describes is the weight rather than the event."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How far past"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The weight of it was **far past anything our strength could carry**, so far past it that **we gave up on life itself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kath' huperbolen** — beyond all measure, hyperbolically. He piles the intensifiers up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Gave up on life** is *we despaired even of living*. The word is **exaporeomai** — to be utterly without a way out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the man who wrote *I can do all things through Christ who strengthens me*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Deep inside ourselves we had already **heard the verdict read out: death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *we had the sentence of death in ourselves* — **apokrima**, a legal ruling, the official answer of a court."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had stopped expecting to survive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But that was so we would **stop resting on ourselves** and **rest instead on the God who raises the dead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the purpose and it is a transfer of weight, from one place to another."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The God who raises the dead** is a deliberate choice of title. A man who has heard a death sentence in himself needs a God described that way and no other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three tenses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **pulled us out** of so deadly a danger, and **He is pulling us out still**, and it is on Him that our hope is set, **that He will go on pulling us out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past, present, future — one verb, **rhuomai**, to drag out of danger, to rescue by force."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul argues from what has happened to what will. The past deliverance is the evidence he uses for the next one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they can do about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While **you work alongside us by praying for us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sunupourgeo** — to work together underneath, to lend a hand from below. It occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks the Corinthians — the church that questioned his credentials, tolerated immorality and split into factions — to hold him up by praying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose of the many"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **the gift given to us through the prayers of many people** may have **many people giving thanks** for it on our behalf.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Many pray, one is delivered, many give thanks. Paul builds the circle deliberately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the letter opens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five verses earlier: *the Father of mercies and God of all comfort, who comforts us in all our tribulation, **that we may be able to comfort those who are in any trouble**, with the comfort with which we ourselves are comforted by God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for comfort appears ten times in five verses."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he did with the worst thing that happened to him.** Wrote it down and sent it to a church that had been criticising him — including the part where he gave up."}]}, "2 Corinthians 10:3–6": {"title": "The fortresses are arguments", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Military language from start to finish, and Paul names the target so there is no guessing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is defending himself against critics at Corinth who said his letters were weighty but his presence was unimpressive. The whole chapter is about that quarrel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the warfare he describes is not abstract spiritual combat. It is what he is doing in the letter — dealing with what people were saying and thinking about the gospel and about him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What gets pulled down"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We pull down **arguments**, and every high thing that lifts itself up against the knowledge of God.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**arguments** — reasonings, calculations, the conclusions people reach", "**every high thing** — the word for a rampart or a proud elevation, something raised up"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fortresses in this passage are made of ideas. That is what the weapons are for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Take every thought prisoner\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image completes the siege: the walls come down, and then the occupants are led out as captives."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And bring it into the obedience of Christ.* The destination is not destruction — it is submission. A captured thought is brought somewhere and put to work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weapons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not the weapons of the flesh at all; through God they have power to pull down fortresses.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not list them here, and the omission is deliberate; the point is what they are not. In Ephesians he does list them, and the offensive one is the word of God."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse people skip.** *We are holding ourselves ready to punish every act of disobedience, once your own obedience has been brought to the full.* Paul deals with his own house first. The order matters — the readiness to address others waits on the readiness of the people he is writing to."}]}, "2 Corinthians 12:1–4": {"title": "Visions and revelations", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is being forced into boasting by people in Corinth who boast well, and he tells the greatest thing that ever happened to him in the third person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I know a man in Christ\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is talking about himself. He says so a few verses later — *lest I should be exalted above measure by the abundance of the revelations* — but he will not put it in the first person while he is describing it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The distance in the grammar is the point of the paragraph."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Fourteen years"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Fourteen years ago.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had not mentioned it. Not in Galatians, not in Romans, not in either Thessalonian letter. Fourteen years of preaching and letter-writing and never once bringing it up, and it comes out now only because the Corinthians have pushed him into a boasting contest he did not want."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"God knows\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whether he was in the body I do not know, or out of the body I do not know; **God knows**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice. The most remarkable experience in his life, and the thing he insists on is what he is not able to tell you about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third heaven"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Paradise** in the second sentence appears to be the same place named twice — Paul restates the vision rather than describing a second stage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is Persian in origin: a walled garden, a royal park. The Greek Old Testament uses it for Eden, and Jesus uses it to the thief on the cross."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he heard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Words beyond all speaking, words that no human being is permitted to say aloud.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **arrheta** — unspoken, unspeakable — and then a second clause: it is *not permitted*. Both that they cannot be put into words and that he is not allowed to try."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the content is withheld. Whatever Paul was shown, the church did not get it here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did pass on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He kept that to himself for fourteen years. What he did hand over, he introduces in the other letter with the same claim to have got it directly: *I received from the Lord what I also delivered to you* — the bread and the cup."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of everything he was given, that is the piece he made sure everybody had. See the note on 1 Corinthians 11:23–26."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes immediately after.** The thorn. *Lest I should be exalted above measure.* Paul places the highest experience and the standing affliction in the same paragraph, and the sentence that ties them together is *My grace is sufficient for you.*"}]}, "2 Corinthians 12:8–10": {"title": "\"My grace is enough for you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks three times and gets an answer that is not the one he asked for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Three times"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Three times I begged the Lord about this, **that it might leave me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked for removal. He was specific, and he was refused."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times is also what happened in Gethsemane: *He went away again a second time and prayed… so He left them, went away again, and prayed the third time.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A thorn in the flesh* — the Greek is a stake, a splinter — and *a messenger of Satan to buffet me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says where it came from: a messenger of Satan. He does not call it God's gift."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And he says what it was doing: the Greek for *buffet* is to strike with the fist, repeatedly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What it was exactly, nobody knows. Every candidate has been proposed and the text does not say."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My grace is enough for you, for My strength is made perfect in weakness.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is present tense and continuous — *My grace is sufficient*, and goes on being."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Made perfect* is **teleitai** — brought to its completion, brought to full expression. Not that weakness improves anything about the strength. That weakness is where it comes fully into view."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does with the refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So I will **boast all the more gladly** in my weaknesses, so that **the power of Christ may come to rest upon me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *rest upon* is **episkenoo** — to pitch a tent over, to tabernacle upon. It is the verb behind the Word *dwelling* among us in John 1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked for the thorn to leave. He got a tent pitched over him instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Weaknesses, insults, hardships, persecutions, times when everything is pressed tight.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five, and every one of them is something happening to him rather than something he arranged."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I take pleasure in* them — and the phrase attached is *for the sake of Christ*, which is the only thing that makes the sentence anything other than strange."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **when I am weak, then I am strong**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not despite. **When.**"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter started"}, {"k": "p", "t": "With the third heaven, and *words beyond all speaking* he was not permitted to repeat, fourteen years earlier and never mentioned until now. See the note on 2 Corinthians 12:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thorn is introduced in the same paragraph as the revelation, and Paul links them: *lest I should be exalted above measure.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The greatest experience of his life and the standing affliction sit on the same page, and the sentence between them is *My grace is enough for you.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he had already said about it.** *We have this treasure in earthen vessels, that the excellence of the power may be of God and not of us.* The clay pot is not an apology in this letter. It is the argument."}]}, "2 Corinthians 2:14–17": {"title": "Led in His victory parade", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul reaches for a Roman street scene his readers had all watched."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who **always leads us along in His victory parade** in Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **thriambeuo** — to lead in a triumph. The Roman triumph was a specific event: a victorious general processing through the city, with his army behind him and the captives walking in the column."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Priests swung censers the whole way, so the route was full of smoke and scent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul puts himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the procession, behind the general."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls himself **doulos** — a slave of Christ — at the top of most of his letters, and the picture here is consistent with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Always\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *in every place*, *at all times*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He writes it three verses after saying he had no rest in his spirit because he could not find Titus, and one chapter before describing the trouble in Asia where he despaired of life. See the note on 2 Corinthians 1:8–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fragrance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And through us **spreads the fragrance of knowing Him everywhere we go**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **osme** — the smell itself, the thing carried on the air."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You do not see a triumph before you smell it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What we are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **to God we are the fragrance of Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The audience named is God. The scent goes up to Him first, before it does anything to anybody in the street."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek here changes to **euodia** — the same word the Greek Old Testament uses for the *sweet aroma* of an accepted sacrifice, over Noah's altar and throughout Leviticus. See the note on Genesis 8:20–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Both crowds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Both among **those who are being saved** and among **those who are perishing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same smell reaches both. Paul does not describe two different fragrances."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two effects"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To the one **a smell of death that ends in death**, to the other **a breath of life that opens into life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *death unto death* and *life unto life*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In a triumph the same incense meant two different things depending on which part of the column you were in — the returning soldiers, or the prisoners walking to execution."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **who is equal to any of this?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul stops mid-argument and asks it. He answers it two chapters later: *our sufficiency is from God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are **not like so many, hawking the word of God for profit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kapeleuo** — to be a retailer, a huckster; it carried the sense of a trader who waters the wine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**So many** — Paul says there were plenty of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We speak **in Christ, with sincerity**, as men **sent from God** and **standing in the sight of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four descriptions, and two of them are about who is watching."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the scent comes from.** Not from the man carrying it. In a triumph the smoke belongs to the procession, and the people lining the street smell the victory of somebody who was not them."}]}, "2 Corinthians 3:1–3": {"title": "You are our letter", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is being asked for credentials, and he says the people asking are the credentials."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody had arrived in Corinth with letters of recommendation, and had used them against him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Letters of introduction were the normal machinery of the ancient world. Paul writes one himself for Phoebe, and one for Onesimus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not against the practice. He is against needing one here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Are we **starting to recommend ourselves all over again**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sunistanein** — to commend, to introduce."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a weary question. He has been defending himself since chapter 1 and is tired of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Or do we need, **as some people do**, letters of recommendation to you, **or from you**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last three words are the sharp end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some of the newcomers had apparently asked Corinth for a reference. Corinth — the congregation Paul founded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are **our letter, written on our hearts**, there for anyone to know and read.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anaginoskomene hupo panton anthropon** — read by all men."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their existence is the document."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He makes the same point in the first letter: *if I am not an apostle to others, yet doubtless I am to you. For **you are the seal of my apostleship** in the Lord*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is plain that **you are a letter from Christ, delivered by us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He shifts the authorship mid-sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not his letter. Christ's, and Paul is the courier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **diakonetheisa** — served, ministered, delivered by us."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ink"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Written **not with ink but with the Spirit of the living God**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tablets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **not on tablets of stone but on tablets that are human hearts**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **plaxin lithinais** — stone tablets."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is Sinai, and everybody reading knew it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **plaxin kardiais sarkinais** — tablets that are hearts of flesh."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the phrase comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will take out of you the heart of stone, and I will give you **a heart of flesh**.* See the note on Ezekiel 36:26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Jeremiah: *I will put My law in their minds, and write it on their hearts*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is quoting both prophets in one clause, and he does not stop to say so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter goes on to argue"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our sufficiency is from God, who also made us sufficient as ministers of the new covenant, not of the letter but of the Spirit; for **the letter kills, but the Spirit gives life**.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 3:4–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the two ministries compared — the one engraved on stones, which came with glory that faded, against the one that remains. See the note on 2 Corinthians 3:7–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Moses' veil, and the unveiling. See the note on 2 Corinthians 3:16–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole chapter unfolds from the stone-and-flesh contrast in verse 3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Corinth was like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Factions, lawsuits, drunkenness at the table, a scandal in the membership."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the letter Paul is holding up as his reference."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not pretend otherwise, and he still says it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Whose letter it is.** He calls them his in one clause and corrects himself in the next — *a letter from Christ, delivered by us*. He is the courier, not the author."}]}, "2 Corinthians 3:12–15": {"title": "The veil at the reading", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes one detail out of Exodus 34 and builds a whole argument on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hope"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Since we have **a hope like that**, we speak with **complete openness, nothing held back and nothing veiled**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parrhesia** — freedom of speech, the right to say anything in a place where you belong. It is the same word Hebrews uses about entering the Holiest. See the note on Hebrews 10:19–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hope in question is the one just described: a glory that stays, against a glory that was going out. See the note on 2 Corinthians 3:7–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are **not like Moses**, who would **put a veil over his face**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus 34 records the veil. It does not say why."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Paul's reading of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that Israel **could not keep looking at the end of what was passing away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the end of that which was being abolished*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's account is that the veil was not there because the glory was too bright. It was there so nobody would watch it fade."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The shining came from forty days on the mountain, and it wore off — and Moses put the veil on afterward, between the times he went back in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The transfer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **their minds had gone hard**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eporothe** — petrified, calloused, made stony."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The veil moves from Moses' face to their understanding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is now"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **to this day, when the old covenant is read aloud, that same veil is still lying there, unlifted**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is describing a synagogue service he had sat in a thousand times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scroll is read, correctly, in Hebrew, by people who honour it — and he says something is over it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is removed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **it is in Christ that it is done away with**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is the same verb as the fading — **katargeitai**, abolished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The veil and the glory it hid are removed by the same word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The restatement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yes, **to this day, whenever Moses is read, a veil lies over their heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice in three verses, and the second time it moves from the mind to the heart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul is not saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the text being defective. He has just called the ministry of death **glorious**, and quotes the Old Testament constantly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The problem in his description is at the reader's end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What lifts it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *nevertheless **when one turns to the Lord, the veil is taken away**.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 3:16–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a middle voice — *when it turns*. And the turning is toward a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But we all, **with unveiled face**, beholding as in a mirror the glory of the Lord, are being transformed into the same image **from glory to glory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses' face faded and was covered. Paul says the readers' faces are uncovered and going the other way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the veil in the temple went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The veil of the temple was torn in two from top to bottom.* See the note on Matthew 27:51–53."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two veils, both removed, and Paul's argument here is about the one nobody could see."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The detail everything rests on.** A cloth over a face in Exodus 34, and Paul's reading of why it was there — not to protect the onlookers from the brightness, but to keep them from seeing it go out."}]}, "2 Corinthians 3:16–18": {"title": "From glory to glory", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of Paul's long comparison between the two covenants, and it finishes with a mirror."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The veil"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But whenever anyone **turns to the Lord, the veil is taken away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent the chapter on Moses coming down the mountain with his face shining, and putting a veil over it — and Paul says why, which Exodus does not: *so that the children of Israel could not look steadily at the end of what was passing away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The glory was fading, and the veil hid the fading."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The removal is not something the reader performs. *Whenever anyone turns.* The veil comes off in the turning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Freedom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now the Lord is the Spirit, and where the Spirit of the Lord is, **there is freedom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eleutheria** — liberty, the state of a free person as against a slave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mirror"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All of us, **with faces unveiled, gazing at the glory of the Lord as though into a mirror**, are being changed into that same likeness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things at once. Looking at Him, and seeing it in a mirror."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses had to veil his face because of what looking at God had done to it. Paul says every face is unveiled now, and what you see when you look is being reproduced in you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Are **being changed** into that same likeness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **metamorphoo** — the word behind metamorphosis, and the word Matthew and Mark use of the transfiguration."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is passive, and it is present continuous. Something is being done to you, and it is not finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the change comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **all of it comes from the Spirit of the Lord**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul closes the sentence by removing the reader from the mechanism entirely. Not by effort, not by discipline, not by resolve."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You look, and you are changed, and the changing is His work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"From glory to glory\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not from failure to glory. From one degree of it to the next."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *from glory into glory* — a movement between two of the same thing."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the beholding is of.** Not yourself in the mirror. *The glory of the Lord.* The chapter after this says where that glory is located: *the light of the knowledge of the glory of God **in the face of Jesus Christ**.*"}]}, "2 Corinthians 3:4–6": {"title": "\"The letter kills\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul disqualifies himself twice before saying what he is qualified for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he denies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not that we are **equal to anything in ourselves**, as though we could claim any of it as coming from us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hikanos** — sufficient, adequate, up to the job."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is writing to a church that has been comparing him unfavourably with more impressive teachers, and his answer is to concede the point entirely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what he affirms"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever we are equal to **comes from God**, who has made us equal to the work of serving a new covenant.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same word, three times in two verses. Not sufficient — sufficiency is from God — made sufficient."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two covenants"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not of the letter but of the Spirit. For **the letter kills, and the Spirit gives life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The letter* is **gramma** — the written character, the thing engraved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What follows in the chapter identifies exactly what he means: *the ministry of death, **written and engraved on stones**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only one thing in Scripture was written and engraved on stones. Ten commandments, by the finger of God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two ministries, compared"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does the comparison himself over the next few verses, and he is not shy about it:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*the ministry of **death**… was glorious* — glorious, and he calls it that", "*the ministry of the **Spirit** is more glorious*", "*the ministry of **condemnation** had glory*", "*the ministry of **righteousness** exceeds much more in glory*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never says the first was bad. He says it had glory — Moses' face shone from it. And then he says the glory was *passing away*, and that Moses veiled his face so nobody would watch it fade. See the note on 2 Corinthians 3:7–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two Greek words he sets against each other are **katargoumenon**, being abolished, and **menon**, remaining. Temporary and permanent — and the temporary one is the one that came with a face too bright to look at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day it was given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses came down the mountain with the tablets and three thousand men died at the hands of the Levites. See the note on Exodus 32:25–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at Pentecost, when the Spirit came, Peter preached and *about three thousand souls were added to them.* See the note on Acts 2:40–41."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two mountains, two givings, and the same number counted on each day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does not permit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not arguing that behaviour stops mattering. He is arguing about what produces it, and Galatians answers the question directly: *walk in the Spirit, and you shall not fulfil the lust of the flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fruit list ends with *against such there is no law* — nothing on it needs legislating. See the note on Galatians 5:22–24."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is engraved now.** *You are our epistle, written in our hearts, known and read by all men… written not with ink but by the Spirit of the living God, not on tablets of stone but on tablets of flesh, that is, of the heart.* Paul says it two verses before this one. Same finger, different surface."}]}, "2 Corinthians 3:7–11": {"title": "Glory that was fading", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul concedes everything about the first arrangement and then makes the concession his argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The names he gives the two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never calls them law and grace here. He calls them ministries:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**The ministry that dealt in death**", "**The ministry that handed down condemnation**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "against"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**The ministry of the Spirit**", "**The ministry that hands us righteousness**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two administrations, each with a job description."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The medium"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Chiseled **letter by letter into stone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written by the finger of God, on the hardest available surface. See the note on Exodus 31:18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul concedes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Arrived **in such glory** that the people of Israel **could not hold their eyes on the face of Moses**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not diminish it. Exodus 34 records that Moses came down not knowing his face shone, and *they were afraid to come near him*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A glory that was **already fading**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a present participle — being done away with, in the process of being abolished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the shine on Moses' face was on its way out while it was still bright. He returns to it in the next paragraph: Moses put a veil on *so that the children of Israel could not look steadily at the end of what was passing away*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two comparisons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If **the ministry that dealt in death** had that glory, **will the ministry of the Spirit not come with far more?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If **the ministry that handed down condemnation** had glory, **how much more does the ministry that hands us righteousness overflow**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are arguments from lesser to greater, and both start by granting the lesser its glory."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blunt line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In fact, **what once shone with glory has no glory left at all, set beside a glory so far beyond it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *has not been glorified in this respect*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that it was never glorious. That next to the other one there is nothing left of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison Paul is reaching for is a lamp in daylight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If what was **on its way out** came with glory, much more glorious is **what stays**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **to katargoumenon** (being abolished) against **to menon** (the remaining)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Temporary and permanent, and the permanent one is the second."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was written on stone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ten commandments, and Paul calls their delivery a ministry of death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not attacking the law. Romans has him calling it *holy, and just, and good*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is describing what it was appointed to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, since we have such hope, **we use great boldness of speech**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *we all, with unveiled face, beholding as in a mirror the glory of the Lord, **are being transformed into the same image from glory to glory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses' face faded. Paul says the readers' is going the other way."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul does with the argument.** He grants the old one everything — glory, divine authorship, a face too bright to look at — and then says that glory was already going out while the crowd was covering their eyes."}]}, "2 Corinthians 4:13–15": {"title": "The same spirit of faith", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul names something he shares with a psalmist who lived a thousand years before him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We have **the same spirit of faith** as the one who wrote, \"**I believed, and so I spoke**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is quoting Psalm 116. And he does not say *the same faith* — he says the same **spirit** of faith, as though it were a disposition, a way of operating, that both men were working out of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Believed**, then **spoke**. The speaking comes out of the believing, not the other way round."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We too believe, and so we speak.* Paul repeats the pattern about himself in the same sentence, which is the whole point of the quotation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalmist was in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 116 is not a victory song written afterwards. Read the line Paul is quoting in its own setting:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I believed, even when I said, \"**I am badly shaken**.\" In my alarm I said, \"Every person is a liar.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He believed and he said he was shaken, in one breath. The psalm has him with *the pains of death* around him and *the sorrows of Sheol*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the spirit of faith Paul is claiming is not one that denies the state it is in. The psalmist says both things — I am badly shaken, and I believed. See the note on Psalms 116:8–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul was in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same chapter, a few verses earlier: *hard pressed on every side… perplexed… persecuted… struck down.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And his own list later in the letter: beaten five times with thirty-nine lashes, three times with rods, once stoned, three shipwrecks, a night and a day in the open sea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the man saying *we too believe, and so we speak.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he believes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Knowing that **the One who raised up the Lord Jesus will raise us up as well**, and stand us in His presence together with you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The content of the faith is a resurrection — one that has happened, and one that will."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's confidence is not about the next few weeks. It reaches past the outcome of whatever is currently pressing on him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All of it is **for your sake**, so that the grace reaching more and more people may make **thanksgiving overflow**, to the glory of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He turns even his own beatings outward. The chapter has already said it: *death is working in us, but life in you.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verses on either side.** Before: *we have this treasure in earthen vessels, that the excellence of the power may be of God and not of us.* After: *though our outward man is perishing, yet the inward man is being renewed day by day.* The spirit of faith sits between a clay pot and a body wearing out, and speaks anyway."}]}, "2 Corinthians 4:16–18": {"title": "The scales Paul is using", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*This trouble of ours is light, and it lasts a moment.* Coming from most people that sentence would be worthless. Two chapters later Paul lists what he means by trouble: beatings, imprisonments, shipwreck, a night and a day in open water, hunger, cold, and being let down a wall in a basket to escape assassination."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has earned the sentence, which is the only reason it can be read."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two pairs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage runs on opposites, and they are stacked:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**outer person** wearing away — **inner person** being made new, *day after day*", "**light** and **a moment** — **weight** and **endless**", "**what can be seen** — **what cannot**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The middle pair is a deliberate joke in Hebrew terms. The word for glory, *kavod*, comes from a root meaning heavy. Paul sets a light affliction against a weight of glory: the very thing that feels heaviest is the lighter side of the scale."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Day after day\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The renewal is not described as a single experience but as something happening on a daily basis, in parallel with the wearing away. Both processes run at once, and Paul does not pretend the first one stops."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That honesty is what makes the paragraph usable by someone in a hospital chair. Nothing here says the outer wearing away is imaginary or will reverse."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"We keep our eyes not on what can be seen.\"** The verb is about aiming attention deliberately — the word behind our *scope*. It is not denial of the visible; Paul has just catalogued it. It is a decision about which set of facts gets to set the terms."}]}, "2 Corinthians 4:3–4": {"title": "Veiled to those perishing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul picks the veil back up, and this time it is over somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 3 ended with Moses' veil, the hardened minds, and the covering that is still there when the old covenant is read aloud. See the note on 2 Corinthians 3:12–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *therefore, since we have this ministry… **we have renounced the hidden things of shame**, not walking in craftiness nor handling the word of God deceitfully.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just said his own preaching hides nothing. This paragraph answers the obvious objection — then why does anybody miss it?"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The concession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **if the good news we bring is veiled at all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He allows the possibility rather than denying it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the veil is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is veiled **among those who are perishing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not over the message. Over an audience."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a present participle — *those who are perishing*, in progress. Paul used the same pair in the first letter: *the message of the cross is foolishness to **those who are perishing**, but to us who **are being saved** it is the power of God.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 1:18–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who did it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whose minds **the god of this present age has blinded**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **etuphlosen** — blinded, from the word for blind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The god of this age** — Paul's phrase, used once. John's Gospel has *the ruler of this world*, three times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blinding is described as an act by an agent, and the organ is the mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being blocked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **the light streaming from the good news of the glory of Christ** will not dawn on them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **augasai** — to shine upon, to dawn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is prevented is not information arriving. It is light dawning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Christ is called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ, **who is the image of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eikon** — image, likeness, exact representation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses it in Colossians: *He is the image of the invisible God*. And Hebrews: *the express image of His person*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says he does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *for **we do not preach ourselves, but Christ Jesus the Lord**, and ourselves your bondservants for Jesus' sake.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer to the blindness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it is the God who commanded light to shine out of darkness who has **shone in our hearts** to give **the light of the knowledge of the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reaches for Genesis 1 — *let there be light* — and says the same command was issued into a heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The remedy for a blinding is a creation act, and it is described in exactly those terms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vessel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **we have this treasure in earthen vessels**, that the excellence of the power may be of God and not of us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses later, and it is one of the sharpest sentences in his letters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hard-pressed on every side, yet not crushed; perplexed, but not in despair; persecuted, but not forsaken; struck down, but not destroyed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four pairs, and the whole chapter ends *the things which are seen are temporary, but the things which are not seen are eternal.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 4:16–18."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where Paul locates the difficulty.** Not in the message and not in the preaching — he has just said he hides nothing. In an audience, and in something done to their minds — and the remedy he names is the sentence God spoke over the dark on the first day."}]}, "2 Corinthians 4:8–12": {"title": "Pressed but not crushed", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four pairs, and the second half of each one is what makes the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are **pressed hard from every direction, but never squeezed into a corner with no way out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **thlibomenoi** (pressed, squeezed) against **stenochoroumenoi** (confined in a narrow space). Two words from the same field, and the second is denied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are **at a loss, but never at a loss for hope**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a deliberate pun: **aporoumenoi** but not **exaporoumenoi** — perplexed but not utterly perplexed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had used the second word about himself one chapter earlier, about Asia: *we despaired even of living*. See the note on 2 Corinthians 1:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are **hunted, but never left on our own**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Diokomenoi** — pursued, persecuted — but not **egkataleipomenoi**, abandoned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are **knocked to the ground, but never finished off**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kataballomenoi** — thrown down — but not **apollumenoi**, destroyed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is granted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in the first half of each pair. Paul concedes the pressure, the confusion, the pursuit and the falling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list is not a denial that any of it happens."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The carrying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wherever we go we **carry in our body the dying of the Lord Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **nekrosin** — the putting to death, the process of dying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **the life of Jesus may be seen in our body too**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul gives the reason for the whole list, and it is display."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The restatement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For we who are alive are **handed over to death again and again for Jesus' sake**, so that **the life of Jesus may be seen in this mortal flesh of ours**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice, and the second version specifies **mortal flesh** — the ordinary, dying body."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **death is at work in us, and life in you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **energeitai** — is operating, is at work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is one of the strangest sentences Paul wrote. The two clauses are set against each other and he does not soften the trade."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had listed elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In labours more abundant, in stripes above measure, in prisons more frequently, in deaths often. From the Jews five times I received forty stripes minus one. Three times I was beaten with rods, once I was stoned, three times I was shipwrecked.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The four pairs are not rhetorical."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **we have this treasure in earthen vessels**, that the excellence of the power may be of God and not of us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Clay pots, and the whole point is that nobody credits the container."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore we do not lose heart. Even though our outward man is perishing, yet **the inward man is being renewed day by day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For our light affliction, which is but for a moment, is working for us **a far more exceeding and eternal weight of glory**.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 4:16–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Light affliction** — written by the man who had just listed the stripes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for the glory"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Baros** — weight. Paul weighs one against the other, and calls his own list light."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What each pair does.** Concedes the first thing and denies the second. Pressed, yes. Cornered, no. Paul never argues that the pressure is imaginary — he argues about where it stops."}]}, "2 Corinthians 5:14–15": {"title": "The love that leaves no choice", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul explains his own motive, and the verb is a physical one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grip"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the love of Christ has us in its grip**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sunechei** — holds together, presses in on both sides, confines."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is used of a crowd pressing round, of a city under siege, and by Luke of a woman with a fever: *Simon's wife's mother was **taken with** a great fever*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The older versions have *constrains*. The picture is of something narrowing the options."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose love"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**He agape tou Christou** — and the Greek can mean His love for us or ours for Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The context settles it. What follows is *One died on behalf of all*, which is His."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Once we have **settled this**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **krinantas** — having judged, having reached a verdict."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A conclusion has been drawn, and the grip follows from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That **One died on behalf of all, and therefore all of them died**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **huper panton** — on behalf of all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the deduction: **ara hoi pantes apethanon** — therefore all died."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *therefore all should die*. Something happened to them when it happened to Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the same in Romans: *do you not know that as many of us as were baptised into Christ Jesus were baptised into His death?* See the note on Romans 6:6–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He died on behalf of all so that **those who go on living should live no longer for themselves**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hoi zontes** — the living."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The alternative"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **for the One who died for them and was raised**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things about Him, and the second is the one that makes the first liveable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Paul is explaining himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Corinthians had been questioning his motives. Chapters 10 to 13 are a defence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before this one: *for if we are beside ourselves, it is for God; or if we are of sound mind, it is for you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody had said he was unbalanced. He answers by naming what has hold of him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, from now on, **we regard no one according to the flesh**. Even though we have known Christ according to the flesh, yet now we know Him thus no longer.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, if anyone is in Christ, **he is a new creation**; old things have passed away.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 5:16–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The grip changes how he looks at people. That is the first thing it produces."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now all things are of God, who has reconciled us to Himself through Jesus Christ, and **has given us the ministry of reconciliation**.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 5:18–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now then, **we are ambassadors for Christ**, as though God were pleading through us.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 5:20–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole passage runs: a love with a grip, a verdict about a death, a changed way of seeing people, and then a job."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other place he names his motive"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have become all things to all men, that I might by all means save some. Now this I do for the gospel's sake.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *woe is me if I do not preach the gospel!*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John says about the direction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We love Him because **He first loved us**.* See the note on 1 John 4:19."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb.** *Sunechei* — presses in from both sides, the word for a siege or a crowd. Paul is explaining why he does what he does, and his answer is that the options have narrowed."}]}, "2 Corinthians 5:16–17": {"title": "A whole new creation", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul stops assessing people by what can be seen, and gives the reason."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The new measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From this moment on we **do not size anyone up by what the flesh can see**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *according to the flesh* — by outward appearance, by natural reckoning: background, record, credentials, family, what a man looks like he is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Even about Christ"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even if we did once size up Christ that way, **we do not know Him that way any longer**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had. He had assessed Jesus of Nazareth by the visible facts — a carpenter's son from a bad village, executed as a criminal — and drawn the obvious conclusion, and gone to Damascus with letters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is admitting his own worst misjudgement as the example."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So if anyone is in Christ — **a whole new creation**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no verb in the Greek. It reads *if anyone in Christ, new creation* — the words land without a connective, which is why translators supply one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ktisis** is the creation word. Paul does not say improved, reformed, forgiven, or repaired. New — **kainos**, new in kind rather than merely recent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The old things **are gone**; look, all things have **become new**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Are gone* is a completed action, standing done. *Have become new* likewise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither is in progress and neither is future."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that reaches"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old things being gone is stated without a list of exceptions — which is Paul's habit whenever he describes what happened at the cross. In Colossians the record against us was *wiped out… nailed to the cross*; in Romans, *there is therefore now no condemnation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever came down the line before — a family's history, a father's diagnosis, a past that had a claim — the sentence puts it under *the old things are gone.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In Christ\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole thing is located there. Not *if anyone tries hard* — *if anyone is in Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is Paul's most-used phrase, some two hundred occurrences across his letters, and it is where every benefit in his theology sits."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now all things are of God, who has reconciled us to Himself through Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the sentence the chapter is famous for: *He made Him who knew no sin to be sin for us, that we might become the righteousness of God in Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The new creation is not a self-improvement claim. It is the result of an exchange described eleven verses later."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul had been.** *A blasphemer, a persecutor, and an insolent man* — his own description, in his own letter. The man who wrote *the old things are gone* had more old things than most."}]}, "2 Corinthians 5:18–19": {"title": "\"Refusing to hold their sins against them\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul states what God did, and then what He has put into our hands, and the two are the same word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who did the reconciling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All of it comes from God, who has brought us back to Himself through Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The direction is fixed and it is worth noticing. In ordinary usage the offended party is the one who needs to be appeased — you reconcile the person you have wronged."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul never says God was reconciled to us. He says God reconciled **us** to Himself. The move came from the side that had been sinned against."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"God was in Christ\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not God sending an agent at a distance. Paul puts Him in the person doing the work — which is why the next clause can be so absolute."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bookkeeping term"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Refusing to hold their sins against them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is **logizomai** — to count, to reckon, to enter in a ledger. It is the same word Paul uses in Romans 4 for righteousness being credited to Abraham."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the claim is accounting language: the sins are not being entered. Not overlooked in a vague sense — not posted to the account."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the scope"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bringing **the world** back to Himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a narrow group. Paul then says the message of that homecoming has been placed in us, and two verses later he is pleading with people to be reconciled — so the reconciling accomplished and the appeal to receive it are both in the passage, without contradiction."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What \"the ministry of reconciliation\" actually is.** Paul defines it in the same breath: God has *placed in us the message of that homecoming.* The work handed over is not to reconcile anybody — that is done. It is to carry the news that it has been."}]}, "2 Corinthians 5:20–21": {"title": "The great exchange", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and it is built as a swap with nothing left over on either side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He made Him who never knew sin **to be sin** for us, so that in Him we might become **the righteousness of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *treated as a sinner*, and not *made righteous-ish*. Paul writes both sides at full strength:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "| Him | us | |---|---| | never knew sin | had nothing else | | **made to be sin** | **made the righteousness of God** |"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever weight the first half carries, the second carries the same. If He was genuinely made sin, then we are genuinely made righteousness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Never knew sin\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is about knowing by experience. It is not that He avoided it successfully — He had no acquaintance with it at all. That is what made the transfer possible: there was nothing of His own on His side of the ledger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In Him\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The location matters and Paul is exact about it. The righteousness is not manufactured in us and inspected. It is **in Him** — held in the same place the blessings of Ephesians 1 are held."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means it is as secure as He is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence before it is the appeal: *we come as ambassadors on behalf of Christ… come back to God.* The exchange is the reason the appeal can be made at all. An ambassador carries terms he did not set, and these are the terms."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word \"become.\"** Not *have* righteousness, as a possession that could be mislaid. Paul says we **become** it — the noun, not the adjective. It is the strongest way the sentence could have been put."}]}, "2 Corinthians 5:6–8": {"title": "Away from and at home", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul says he is confident, twice, and both times it is about dying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The confidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So we are **always full of courage**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tharrountes** — of good cheer, bold, confident."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it in verse 6 and repeats it in verse 8. It brackets the paragraph."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For we know that if our earthly house, this tent, is destroyed, we have a building from God, a house not made with hands, eternal in the heavens.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *tent* is **skenous** — a temporary structure. Paul made them for a living."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *we do not want to be unclothed, but **further clothed**, that mortality may be swallowed up by life.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two homes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As long as we are **at home in this body** we are **living away from the Lord**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a pair Paul builds for this passage: **endemountes** and **ekdemountes** — being among your own people, and being abroad."}, {"k": "p", "t": "At home here means abroad there. The two states are mutually exclusive, and he says so plainly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For we make our way by **trusting, not by seeing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dia pisteos peripatoumen, ou dia eidous** — through faith we walk, not through appearance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Eidos** is the visible form of a thing. Not *sight* as the faculty; the look of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence is dropped into the middle of an argument about death and the body, and it is the reason the argument works: the building in the heavens has not been seen by anybody writing or reading."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The preference"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And we are full of courage, and would **gladly prefer to be away from the body and at home with the Lord**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eudokoumen** — we are well pleased, we think it good."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He states a preference for dying, calmly, in a letter about his sufferings."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other place he says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For to me, to live is Christ, and to die is gain… **having a desire to depart and be with Christ, which is far better**. Nevertheless to remain in the flesh is more needful for you.* See the note on Philippians 1:21–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same preference, same reason, and there he adds that he expects to stay."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter has already said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For we who are in this tent **groan, being burdened** — not because we want to be unclothed, but further clothed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The groaning is real and Paul does not deny it. The confidence and the groaning are in the same paragraph."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The deposit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now He who has prepared us for this very thing is God, who also **has given us the Spirit as a guarantee**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **arrabona** — a down payment, earnest money. In modern Greek the word is used for an engagement ring."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Part of the sum, handed over now, on a transaction that will be completed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore we make it our aim, whether present or absent, **to be well pleasing to Him**. For we must all appear before the judgment seat of Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **bema** — the raised platform where a magistrate sat, and where prizes were awarded at the games."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The preference for departing does not remove the aim. Paul puts the two in consecutive sentences."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If anyone is in Christ, he is a new creation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *He made Him who knew no sin to be sin for us, that we might become the righteousness of God in Him*. See the note on 2 Corinthians 5:20–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The paragraph about being at home or abroad sits between the deposit and the exchange."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the confidence rests on.** Nothing visible. He says so in the middle of the paragraph: *we make our way by trusting, not by the look of it.*"}]}, "2 Corinthians 6:1–2": {"title": "\"Right now is the day of rescue\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul makes one appeal, and it is not about behaviour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Working alongside Him\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sunergountes** — working together with. Paul describes his own ministry as a partnership."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before ends chapter 5: *we are ambassadors for Christ, as though God were pleading through us: **be reconciled to God**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The appeal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We ask you as well **not to take the grace of God and let it come to nothing in you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *not to receive the grace of God in vain* — **kenos**, empty, hollow, to no purpose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is possible to receive something and get nothing out of it. That is the danger named, and it is the only thing Paul asks in this sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *do not lose it*. Do not let it be empty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**At the time of My favour I listened to you, and on the day of rescue I came to your help.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 49, from a passage about the Servant — spoken to Him, about a moment when a request would be heard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Paul's comment on it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look — **right now is the time of favour. Right now is the day of rescue.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes a prophecy about a future acceptable time and says it has arrived."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word he uses is **kairos** — not clock time but the right moment, the season when a thing is possible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repetition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Right now… right now.* Paul says it twice in one breath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same urgency Isaiah has at the end of the free invitation: *seek the LORD **while He may be found**, call upon Him **while He is near**.* See the note on Isaiah 55:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both passages hold two things at once — the offer costs nothing, and the moment is now."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul then gives one of the great lists in his letters, of what the ministry cost:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In much patience, in tribulations, in needs, in distresses, in stripes, in imprisonments, in tumults, in labours, in sleeplessness, in fastings…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the paradoxes: *as unknown, and yet well known; as dying, and behold we live; as sorrowful, yet always rejoicing; **as poor, yet making many rich**; as having nothing, and yet possessing all things.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks at the end of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*O Corinthians! **We have spoken openly to you, our heart is wide open**… Now in return for the same — I speak as to children — **you also be open**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole passage is a man asking a difficult church to stop being closed toward him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What \"in vain\" would look like.** Not losing the grace. Standing in it and getting nothing from it — which is why the chapter's ask is about an open heart rather than better conduct."}]}, "2 Corinthians 7:10–11": {"title": "Two kinds of sorrow", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul distinguishes two griefs that look identical from outside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What this is about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A letter Paul had sent that made them sorry — *for even if I made you sorry with my letter, I do not regret it, though I did regret it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had written something hard, worried about it, and then Titus brought back the news of how it landed. See the note on 2 Corinthians 8:16–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first kind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sorrow that is **shaped by God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kata theon lupe** — sorrow according to God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not sorrow about God, and not sorrow He inflicts. Sorrow shaped along His lines."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Turns a person around, **toward being saved**, and **leaves nothing to regret**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **metanoian** — a change of mind. The word usually rendered *repentance*, and it is a word about the mind turning rather than the feelings hurting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ametameleton** — not to be regretted. The one sorrow nobody looks back on and wishes they had skipped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second kind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the world's kind of sorrow produces death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tou kosmou lupe** — the sorrow of the world."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul gives it one clause and no description. The result is the whole definition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two men on the same night"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter *went out and wept bitterly*, and was on a beach three days later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judas *was remorseful* — and the Greek there is **metamelomai**, a different word, the regret one — *and he went and hanged himself*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both were sorry, and the same evening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the good sorrow produced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just look at what this very thing has produced in you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven items, and Paul lists them fast:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Such **earnestness**, such **eagerness to clear yourselves**, such **anger**, such **fear**, such **longing**, such **passion**, such **readiness to see justice done**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek opens each with **alla** — *but also, but also* — piling them up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one is energy. Nothing on the list is passive, and nothing on it is misery."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At every point you have **shown yourselves innocent** in this matter.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hagnous** — pure, clear. The same root as the purifying in 1 John 3:3. See the note on 1 John 3:2–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not still holding it against them. The chapter has already said *I have great boldness of speech toward you, I have great pride in you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The test in the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The difference is not the intensity of the feeling. Both sorrows hurt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is what is at the end of each: one arrives at a change of mind and salvation, and the other arrives at death."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says right before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For when we came to Macedonia, our bodies had no rest, but we were troubled on every side. Outside were conflicts, inside were fears. **Nevertheless God, who comforts the downcast, comforted us by the coming of Titus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul admits fear in the same chapter where he explains sorrow."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore we have been comforted in your comfort. And **we rejoiced exceedingly more for the joy of Titus**, because his spirit has been refreshed by you all.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter ends: *I have confidence in you in everything*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The difference between them.** Not how badly it hurts. Where it arrives — a turned mind, or death — and Paul says the first one is never regretted afterwards."}]}, "2 Corinthians 8:10–12": {"title": "Finish what you started", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An unfinished collection, and Paul handles it without once making it a command."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How he frames it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And here is **what I think best** about it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **gnomen didomi** — I give an opinion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the same thing about another matter in the first letter: *I have no commandment from the Lord; yet I give judgment*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is raising money and explicitly declining to order it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose benefit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Since **this is the very thing that will do you good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **touto humin sumpherei** — this is profitable for you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The collection is for the poor in Jerusalem. Paul says finishing it is to Corinth's advantage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The history"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A year ago **you were the first not only to act but to want to act**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apo perusi** — from last year."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Corinth started it. They were ahead of Macedonia, ahead of everyone. Paul had used their eagerness to stir up the other churches: *your zeal has stirred up the majority*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gap"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve months, and it is not done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not speculate about why. He names the date and moves on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **finish the doing of it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epitelesate** — complete, bring to the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **the eagerness with which you wanted it may be matched by carrying it through**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kathaper he prothumia tou thelein, houtos kai to epitelesai** — as the readiness of the willing, so also the completing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Willingness and completion, matched."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point is not that the intention was worthless. It is that an intention a year old is supposed to arrive somewhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Out of what you have"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Out of **what you have**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ek tou echein** — out of the having."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He caps it before anyone can over-reach. Macedonia gave beyond their ability. Corinth is told to give out of what is in hand. See the note on 2 Corinthians 8:3–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **where the willingness is there, a gift is welcomed for what a person has, not for what he does not have**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kathe ean eche… ou katho ouk echei**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Euprosdektos** — well-received, acceptable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the measure is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The willingness, and then what is actually in hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the amount. Not a percentage. Two things: is the will there, and what is there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who this exempts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone who has little."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's sentence protects the poor giver from feeling the gift is too small, and closes off the excuse of the rich one who has plenty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For I do not mean that others should be eased and you burdened; **but by an equality**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your abundance may supply their lack, that their abundance also may supply your lack.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the manna: *he who gathered much had nothing left over, and he who gathered little had no lack*. See the note on 2 Corinthians 8:13–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the money was handled"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul sent Titus and two other men, and explained why:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Avoiding this: that anyone should blame us in this lavish gift which is administered by us — **providing honourable things, not only in the sight of the Lord, but also in the sight of men**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He would not carry the money alone. The arrangement was designed to be above suspicion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he never does in two chapters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Name a sum, set a percentage, or command it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He reports what Macedonia did, reminds Corinth what they started, states the principle, and appeals to the example of Christ. See the note on 2 Corinthians 8:9."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The cap.** *Out of what you have.* Having praised churches who gave past their ability, he tells this one to give out of what is in hand — and says a gift is measured by what a person has."}]}, "2 Corinthians 8:16–17": {"title": "Who put the care there", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul thanks God for something that happened inside another man's chest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The collection for the poor believers in Jerusalem. Paul spends two whole chapters on it, which is longer than he spends on almost anything else practical."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It opens with the Macedonians, who *in a great trial of affliction* and *deep poverty* gave *beyond their ability, freely willing*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The thanks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Thanks be to God, **who put into the heart of Titus** the same deep care for you that we carry.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *who puts the same earnest care into the heart of Titus* — **didonti**, giving, in the present."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not thank Titus. He thanks the one who put it there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Spouden** — diligence, earnestness, haste. The same word Paul uses three verses earlier for the Corinthians' own zeal, and again at the end of the chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It runs through the passage like a thread: the care God put in Titus, the care Paul carries, the care he is asking the Corinthians for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same care"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **same** deep care for you **that we carry**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a lesser version delegated downward. The identical thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Titus is being sent because he already wants to go."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The volunteering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **took our appeal to heart**, and **being more eager still**, he **set out for you on his own initiative**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three stages in one sentence: he accepted the request, he exceeded it, and then he went before he was sent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *of his own accord* is **authairetos** — self-chosen. Paul used the same word of the Macedonians a few verses back: they gave *freely willing*, self-chosen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the accepting ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul makes the request, and Titus outruns it. The letter is careful to say both — *he accepted the exhortation* and *being more diligent, he went of his own accord*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Obedience and enthusiasm, in that order, in one man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does with the money"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He will not carry it alone. *Avoiding this: that anyone should blame us in this lavish gift which is administered by us — **providing honourable things, not only in the sight of the Lord, but also in the sight of men**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two brothers travel with Titus, one *chosen by the churches*. Paul builds the audit in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he has just written"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For you know the grace of our Lord Jesus Christ, that **though He was rich, yet for your sakes He became poor**, that you through His poverty might become rich.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the argument of the whole chapter, and everything about Titus and the brothers is the logistics under it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Titus was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Greek, uncircumcised, whom Paul refused to have circumcised as a test case in Galatians."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the one who brought Paul the news from Corinth that lifted him — *God, who comforts the downcast, comforted us by the coming of Titus*. See the note on 2 Corinthians 7:5–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul calls him *my partner and fellow worker concerning you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the desire comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is **God who works in you both to will and to do** for His good pleasure.* See the note on Philippians 2:12–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The willing is listed first, and it is God's work too."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul noticed.** A man wanted to go, and Paul's response was to thank God — because he read the wanting itself as something that had been placed there."}]}, "2 Corinthians 8:23–24": {"title": "The glory of Christ", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of Paul's letter of introduction for the men carrying the collection."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being organised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The relief fund for the poor believers in Jerusalem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul spends two chapters on it — the Macedonians who gave *beyond their ability*, the argument from Christ's poverty, and the practical arrangements."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the audit: *avoiding this, that anyone should blame us in this lavish gift which is administered by us — providing honourable things, not only in the sight of the Lord, but also in the sight of men*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He will not carry the money alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Titus"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If anyone enquires about **Titus**, he is **my partner and fellow worker** concerning you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **koinonos kai sunergos** — a sharer and a co-worker."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Titus had gone ahead willingly — *being more diligent, he went to you of his own accord*. See the note on 2 Corinthians 8:16–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The brothers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Or if our **brethren are enquired of**, they are **messengers of the churches**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apostoloi ekklesion** — apostles of the churches."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word, in its ordinary sense: somebody sent with a commission. These men are not apostles in the twelve's sense; they are delegates, appointed by congregations to travel with the money."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One of them is named earlier only as *the brother whose praise is in the gospel throughout all the churches, and not only that, but who was also **chosen by the churches** to travel with us*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elected, by vote, to handle other people's donations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They are **the glory of Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **doxa Christou**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said of two unnamed men on a fundraising errand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has used **doxa** in this letter of the face of Moses, of the ministry of the Spirit, and of the eternal weight of glory. Here it is applied to a delegation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore **show to them, and before the churches, the proof of your love** and of our boasting on your behalf.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **endeixin** — a demonstration, a showing forth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has been boasting about the Corinthians to the Macedonians — *I boast of you to them* — and now the boast has to be substantiated in front of witnesses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole chapter rests on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For you know the grace of our Lord Jesus Christ, that **though He was rich, yet for your sakes He became poor, that you through His poverty might become rich**.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 8:9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One sentence in the middle of a fundraising letter, and it is the theological centre of the whole two chapters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the next chapter says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So let each one give as he purposes in his heart, **not grudgingly or of necessity; for God loves a cheerful giver**.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 9:6–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hilaron** — hilarious, glad."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the arrangements matter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is handling a large sum of money belonging to churches he founded, destined for a church that had been suspicious of him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every safeguard in these two chapters is there because he knew how it would look."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he calls two unnamed couriers.** *The glory of Christ.* The phrase he has used of the face of Moses and of eternal weight, applied to men carrying a collection."}]}, "2 Corinthians 8:3–5": {"title": "They begged for the favor", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul reports on a collection, and the people who gave are the ones who had to persuade him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he is talking about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Macedonian churches — Philippi, Thessalonica, Berea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moreover, brethren, we make known to you the grace of God bestowed on the churches of Macedonia: that in **a great trial of affliction**, the abundance of their joy and **their deep poverty** abounded in the riches of their liberality.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 8:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kata bathous ptocheia** — down-to-the-depth poverty. **Ptocheia** is beggary, not modest means."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The testimony"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I can **vouch for them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **marturo** — I bear witness. Paul puts his own name to the figure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The amount"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They gave **as much as they were able, and past what they were able**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kata dunamin… para dunamin** — according to ability, beyond ability."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two prepositions, one noun. Up to the limit and then over it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **nobody had to ask them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **authairetoi** — self-chosen, of their own accord."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word appears twice in the New Testament, both in this chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They came **asking us, pleading with us and refusing to be put off**, for **the favor of a share in this**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meta polles parakleseos deomenoi hemon ten charin** — with much entreaty begging us for the grace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul was not fundraising among them. They had to press him to be allowed in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for the collection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Charis** — grace, favour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses it of the collection itself throughout these two chapters. Money, called grace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they wanted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That we would take their gift and **take up with them the shared work of caring for the Lord's people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **koinonian tes diakonias** — fellowship of the ministry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Koinonia** — partnership, having in common. They were not asking to donate. They were asking to be partners in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did not expect"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it went **far beyond anything we had looked for**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ou kathos elpisamen** — not as we had hoped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **gave themselves first to the Lord**, and then to us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **heautous edokan proton to Kurio**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul names the sequence, and it is the sentence the whole paragraph exists for. The money came second."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **that was what God wanted**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dia thelematos Theou** — through the will of God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the collection was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The poor believers in Jerusalem. Paul spent years organising it across the Gentile churches, and it is the practical expression of everything he argued about Jew and Gentile in one body."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it pleased those from Macedonia and Achaia to make a certain contribution for the poor among the saints who are in Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks Corinth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, as you abound in everything — in faith, in speech, in knowledge, in all diligence, and in your love for us — **see that you abound in this grace also**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A year ago you were the first not only to act but to want to act. **Now finish the doing of it**.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 8:10–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Corinth was rich, started first, and had not finished. Macedonia was destitute and had."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The example he ends on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For you know **the grace of our Lord Jesus Christ, that though He was rich, yet for your sakes He became poor**, that you through His poverty might become rich.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 8:9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **I do not mean that others should be eased and you burdened**, but by an equality.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 8:13–15."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The sequence Paul names.** *They gave themselves first to the Lord, and then to us.* The paragraph is a fundraising report and that is the line it is written for."}]}, "2 Corinthians 8:9": {"title": "The exchange, in one sentence", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is in the middle of organising a collection for the poor in Jerusalem, and he stops to give the reason. It turns out to be the whole gospel in a line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The structure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though He was rich, for your sakes He became poor, so that you, through His poverty, might become rich.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four movements, and they cross over:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "| Him | you | |---|---| | was rich | were poor | | **became poor** | **become rich** |"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is left in the middle. What was His went one way; what was ours went the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"You know the grace\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul opens with *for you know* — he is not teaching them something new. He is reminding a church that had gone cold on a promised collection what grace had already cost."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is **charis**, and here it is applied to an act rather than an attitude: the grace of our Lord Jesus Christ *is* the exchange he then describes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the argument lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never orders them to give. The next verse says he is not commanding, and a few lines on comes the sentence about God loving a cheerful giver."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Instead he sets one act beside another: Christ emptied Himself for you, so finish what you started for them. Generosity is argued from generosity received, not from obligation."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the riches are.** Paul does not spell it out here, which is why the verse travels so well. Read alongside Ephesians 1 — every blessing, already given, in the heavenly places — and the wealth being described is not narrow. What He gave up was real; what came back the other way is described in the same unqualified terms."}]}, "2 Corinthians 9:6–7": {"title": "\"A cheerful giver\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is organising a collection for the poor in Jerusalem, and he refuses to apply any pressure to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The farming image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The one who **sows sparingly** will reap sparingly too, and the one who **sows generously** will reap generously too.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *generously* is *with blessings* — literally, he who sows upon blessings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reaches for agriculture rather than commerce. A farmer's seed is not spent; it is placed. And nobody who has ever planted expects the harvest to match what went into the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The freedom clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let each one give **as he has settled it in his own heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is to purpose, to decide beforehand. It is his own decision, made privately, in advance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No amount is named anywhere in these two chapters. Paul never sets a figure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things ruled out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not with **a grudge** and not **because he is made to**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Grudgingly** — the Greek is *out of grief*, with regret."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Of necessity** — under compulsion, because someone applied pressure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are excluded, and between them they cover most of what motivates religious giving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is wanted instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it is a **cheerful giver** that God loves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hilaros** — the root of the English *hilarious*. Cheerful, merry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse is quoting Proverbs 22:8 in the Greek Old Testament, and it is the disposition rather than the sum that is named as loved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How Paul ran the collection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the two chapters and watch what he does not do."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not command. *I speak not by commandment* — chapter 8, in so many words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them the Macedonians gave *beyond their ability* out of *deep poverty*, and that they *begged us urgently* for the privilege — and then says he is only telling them this *to test the sincerity of your love*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *if there is first a willing mind, it is accepted according to what one has, and not according to what he does not have.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts the whole thing on willingness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason he gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For you know the grace of our Lord Jesus Christ, that **though He was rich, yet for your sakes He became poor**, that you through His poverty might become rich.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is chapter 8, verse 9, and it is the only leverage Paul uses in the entire appeal. See the note on 2 Corinthians 8:9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he promises"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Further down the same chapter: *God is able to make all grace abound toward you, that you, always having all sufficiency in all things, may have an abundance for every good work.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sufficiency for yourself and abundance for the work — and the Greek for *sufficiency* is **autarkeia**, contentment, having enough."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the money was for.** Not a building or a programme. A famine relief fund, carried by hand from Greek churches to Jewish believers in Jerusalem — people most of the donors would never meet."}]}, "2 Corinthians 9:8–9": {"title": "All you need, and plenty over", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence with the word *all* in it four times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the middle of a fundraising letter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is organising a collection for the poor believers in Jerusalem, and chapters 8 and 9 are the longest treatment of giving in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just said *God loves a cheerful giver* — **hilaron**, the word behind *hilarious*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **God is able** to make every grace overflow toward you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dunatei ho Theos** — God is powerful, God has the power."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say God will. He says God is able, which is what a man raising money for someone else's poor needs to be sure of first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The overflow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Perisseusai** — to abound, to exceed, to have more than enough."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb runs through both chapters. It is the word used of the twelve baskets left over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Every grace"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every grace* — **pasan charin**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Charis** is the word for grace, and in these two chapters Paul uses it for the collection itself, for the Macedonians' generosity, for God's gift, and for thanks."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same noun carries the whole transaction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four alls"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **always**, in **everything**, having **all** you need, you may have plenty over for **every** good work.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek: **pantote pasan autarkeian en panti… eis pan ergon agathon**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four occurrences of *pas* in one clause. It is deliberate piling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Autarkeia** — sufficiency, having enough."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a Stoic technical term: self-sufficiency, the philosopher's ideal of needing nothing outside himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes the word and attaches it to a supply that comes from somewhere else. The Stoic generated his own; Paul's is given."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the adjective of himself: *I have learned, in whatever state I am, to be content*. See the note on Philippians 4:11–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sufficiency is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That you may have **plenty over for every good work**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The purpose clause is not consumption. The overflow is directed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is writing to people he is asking for money, and the sentence bends back toward the giving he is asking for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As it stands written: \"**He has scattered widely, He has given to the poor; His righteousness lasts forever**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 112, describing the man who fears the LORD."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eskorpisen** — scattered, the word for broadcasting seed by hand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the *He* is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the psalm it is the generous man. In Paul's argument it has become ambiguous, and the next verse makes God the supplier: *He who supplies seed to the sower and bread for food*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now may **He who supplies seed to the sower**, and bread for food, supply and multiply the seed you have sown, and increase the fruits of your righteousness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things in the sower's hand: bread to eat, and seed to plant. The passage distinguishes them and says both are supplied."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While, through the proof of this ministry, they glorify God… **Thanks be to God for His indescribable gift!***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anekdiegeto** — indescribable, beyond narration. A word Paul appears to have made up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A chapter about a cash collection ends there."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word.** **Autarkeia** — the Stoics' self-sufficiency, the state of needing nothing from outside. Paul keeps the word and moves the source."}]}, "2 Kings 1:9–12": {"title": "Fire from heaven", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two companies of fifty men are sent to fetch a prophet, and neither comes back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahaziah, Ahab's son, has fallen through a lattice in his upper room and is injured."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He sends messengers to enquire of **Baal-Zebub, the god of Ekron** — a Philistine deity — whether he will recover."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elijah intercepts them: *is it because there is no God in Israel that you are going to enquire of Baal-Zebub?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Therefore you shall not come down from the bed to which you have gone up, but you shall surely die.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The identification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The messengers describe him to the king: *a hairy man wearing a leather belt around his waist*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he said, \"**It is Elijah the Tishbite**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first company"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the king sent **a captain with fifty men** to bring Elijah in.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fifty armed men to arrest one prophet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The captain found him **sitting on the top of a hill**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not hiding. Sitting, visible, on high ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The summons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And called up, \"**Man of God, the king says: Come down**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The title is used, and then the king's authority is set against it in the same sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Man of God** — a formal designation. The captain knows exactly who he is addressing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Elijah answered, \"**If I am a man of God, let fire come down from heaven and consume you and your fifty**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew turns on the captain's own word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He said *man of God*. Elijah says: if that is what I am, then this follows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And fire came down and consumed the captain and his fifty men.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second company"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The king sent another captain with another fifty. This one called out, \"**Man of God, the king says: Come down at once**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Worse. **Meherah** — quickly, hurry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fifty men have just died and the second captain adds urgency to the same demand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Again Elijah answered… and once more the fire came down and consumed them.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter continues. A third captain comes up the hill *and fell on his knees before Elijah, and pleaded with him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Man of God, please let my life and the life of these fifty servants of yours be precious in your sight.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not deliver the king's message. He asks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the angel says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the angel of the LORD said to Elijah, \"**Go down with him; do not be afraid of him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elijah goes, and delivers the same word to the king's face: *you shall surely die*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third captain lives, and the prophet walks down the hill with him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the two companies were doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Carrying an arrest order from a king who had just sent to a Philistine god."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The confrontation is not about Elijah's dignity. It is a continuation of Carmel — *if the LORD is God, follow Him; but if Baal, follow him*. See the note on 1 Kings 18:20–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the disciples quote it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samaritans refuse Jesus passage, and *when His disciples James and John saw this, they said, \"Lord, do You want us to command fire to come down from heaven and consume them, **just as Elijah did**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But He turned and rebuked them.* See the note on Luke 9:51–56."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had missed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The precedent existed. They cited it correctly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The village had done nothing but decline hospitality, which is not the same as a king enquiring of Baal-Zebub through fifty armed men."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two on the mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And behold, two men talked with Him, who were Moses and **Elijah**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Standing next to Him, six days after Peter's confession, and the disciples who wanted the fire were there."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The turn.** The captain called him *man of God*. Elijah answered *if I am* — and let the captain's own title supply the condition."}]}, "2 Kings 13:14–19": {"title": "He struck three times and stopped", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Elisha's last recorded act, and it ends in anger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now Elisha had fallen sick — **the illness that would take his life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophet who raised a boy, cured Naaman, floated an axe head and fed a hundred men from twenty loaves, dies of an illness in bed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **Jehoash king of Israel** came down to him, **weeping over him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A king of the northern kingdom, of whom the record says *he did evil in the sight of the LORD*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He weeps anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My father! My father! The chariots and horsemen of Israel!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The exact words Elisha himself had shouted when Elijah went up in the whirlwind, watching the chariot of fire."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jehoash is quoting the man in the bed back at him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase means: you are the real defence of this country. Not the army."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get a bow and some arrows**.\" The king took them up.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Now draw the bow**,\" Elisha told him, and the king drew it.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Elisha **laid his own hands over the king's hands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A dying man's hands on the hands of a king who is doing the pulling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The strength in the draw is Jehoash's. The hands on top are Elisha's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The window"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Open the east window**,\" he said, and the king swung it open.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "East is where Aram is. Damascus, and the territory Israel had been losing for two generations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Shoot!**\" And he shot.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One arrow, out of a window, at nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The declaration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD's arrow of victory! The arrow of victory over Aram!** You will strike Aram at Aphek until you have finished them off.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the arrow of the LORD's deliverance* — **teshuah**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is unqualified. Nothing is conditional in this sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take the arrows**.\" The king took them. \"**Strike the ground**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No number is given, and no explanation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He struck it **three times and stopped**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *and he stopped*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was not told when to stop. He decided."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The anger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the man of God was angry with him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one time in the Elisha narratives that he is angry with a king who has come to him respectfully."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You should have struck five or six times**,\" he said. \"Then you would have **beaten Aram down completely. But now you will only defeat Aram three times**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The outcome is set by the number of strikes on a floor."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three victories are recorded later in the chapter: *Jehoash the son of Jehoahaz recaptured from Ben-Hadad the son of Hazael the cities… **three times Jehoash defeated him** and recovered the cities of Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exactly three. The account keeps the count."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the test was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody told him to be enthusiastic. The instruction was *strike the ground*, and the number was left open."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The floor-striking was the measure, and he had no idea he was being measured."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens after Elisha dies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Raiders throw a body into his tomb in a hurry. *When the man was let down and touched the bones of Elisha, he revived and stood on his feet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last miracle in his ministry happens after he is dead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other open-ended vessel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bring vessels — **empty vessels; do not gather just a few**.* And the oil stops when the last jar is full. See the note on 2 Kings 4:1–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same prophet, same principle: the supply matched the number of containers, and the number of containers was the borrower's decision."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What ended the sequence.** Not the arrows running out, and not an instruction. He stopped, and the record of the wars afterwards counts exactly three."}]}, "2 Kings 2:11–12": {"title": "\"My father, my father\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He goes up mid-conversation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As they walked on, **talking together**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No ceremony, no farewell speech, no last instruction. They are walking and talking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The separation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Suddenly **a chariot of fire and horses of fire swept between them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *separated the two of them*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chariot's job in the sentence is to divide them. It is not recorded that Elijah rode in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How he went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **Elijah went up to heaven in a whirlwind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whirlwind, not the chariot. The chapter says so at the top as well: *when the LORD was about to take up Elijah into heaven **by a whirlwind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **se'arah** — a storm wind, a tempest. It is the word for what God answers Job out of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shout"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Elisha saw it and cried out, \"**My father! My father! The chariots and horsemen of Israel!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things in one breath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**My father** — he had asked for a double portion, the inheritance of a firstborn son. The word he shouts at the moment it is granted is the word for the relation it depends on. See the note on 2 Kings 2:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The chariots and horsemen of Israel** — a military title. He is calling the departing prophet the nation's actual defence, in a kingdom that had a standing army and chariotry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the words come back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha is on his own deathbed, decades later, and King Joash comes and weeps over him and says the identical sentence: *\"My father, my father, the chariots of Israel and their horsemen!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The words Elisha shouted are shouted over Elisha."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition met"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And then **he saw him no more**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He saw, which was the condition. And then it was over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tearing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **caught hold of his own clothes and tore them in two**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mourning gesture — and the last thing he does before picking up the mantle that fell."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tears his own garment and puts on his father's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had asked for and what he got"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked to inherit. He gets a shout, a torn coat, and a cloak on the ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he walks to the Jordan alone and finds the water still parts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other man who did not die"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Enoch. *And Enoch walked with God; and he was not, for God took him.* See the note on Hebrews 11:4–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two men in the whole Old Testament, and Elijah turns up again on a mountain with Moses, talking with Jesus about His departure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elijah left"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A cloak. That is the whole estate."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chariot did.** It came between them, and the man it came for went up in something else. Elisha shouts about the chariots and horsemen while watching the one he calls father go into a storm."}]}, "2 Kings 2:9–10": {"title": "A double portion", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An old prophet offers his successor anything, and the answer is about inheritance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When they had crossed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elijah had rolled up his mantle, struck the Jordan, and the water divided. They walked over on dry ground — the same river, the same way Israel came in under Joshua, going the other direction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha has followed him from Gilgal to Bethel to Jericho to the Jordan, refusing three times to be left behind: *as the LORD lives, and as your soul lives, I will not leave you.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Ask me — what can I do for you before I am taken from you?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elijah knows he is going. So do the sons of the prophets at every stop, who kept telling Elisha so and being told *yes, I know; keep silent*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Please, let me **inherit a double portion of your spirit**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *let a double portion of your spirit be upon me*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What a double portion was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not twice as much as Elijah had. It is the inheritance law from Deuteronomy: the firstborn receives *a double portion of all that he has* — two shares where the other sons get one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha is not asking to be greater than Elijah. He is asking to be treated as the heir."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word he uses is the language of a son claiming a father's estate — and when the chariot comes he shouts *my father, my father*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You have asked a hard thing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *you have made hard to ask*. Elijah does not refuse it and does not grant it. It is not his to give."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Still — **if you see me as I am taken from you, it will be yours. But if not, it will not**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One condition, and it is about looking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not about worthiness, service, or preparation. Whether his eyes are on the man at the moment he goes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had already done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Followed, refused three separations, and walked through a divided river. The condition attached to the request is the same thing he had been doing all day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He saw it. *Elisha saw it and cried out.* See the note on 2 Kings 2:11–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The miracles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old count is that Elijah's recorded miracles number eight and Elisha's sixteen — which readers have long noticed lines up with the request."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Scripture never makes the tally itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elisha did first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Picked up the mantle that fell, went back to the Jordan, struck the water and said *where is the LORD God of Elijah?* — and it parted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He crossed back into the country alone, and the sons of the prophets watching from Jericho said *the spirit of Elijah rests on Elisha.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he asked for.** Not power and not a position. The share of a firstborn son in his father's house — and the only condition was to keep watching until the end."}]}, "2 Kings 20:1–7": {"title": "\"I have heard your prayer, I have seen your tears\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A sentence of death is delivered by a prophet and reversed before he can get out of the building."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The message"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Put your house in order, for you are going to die; you will not recover.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a warning or a diagnosis. It is delivered as the word of the LORD, with no conditions attached and no *unless*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hezekiah does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hezekiah **turned his face to the wall** and prayed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not argue with Isaiah or ask for a second opinion. He turns away from the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Remember, LORD, how **I have walked before You faithfully and with a whole heart**, and have done what is good in Your eyes.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He pleads his record. It is the only argument he makes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it was a genuine record — Hezekiah tore down the high places, broke the sacred pillars, and smashed the bronze serpent when Israel started burning incense to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weeping"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **he wept — bitter, heavy tears**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he wept a great weeping*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The speed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Isaiah **had not even left the middle court** when the word of the LORD came to him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophet is still inside the palace complex. He delivered a death sentence and is sent back up the stairs with the opposite message."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says He responded to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have heard your prayer, I have seen your tears.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things, and the second is not speech."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tears are named separately, as though they were their own petition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is added"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will add fifteen years to your life.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A number, given in advance. Not *you will live* — a figure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And two more things: *on the third day you will go up to the house of the LORD*, and the city delivered from Assyria."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**For My own sake and for the sake of David My servant.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reasons given are not Hezekiah's record, which is what he had argued from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The figs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Bring a lump of figs.**\" They brought it and laid it on the boil, and Hezekiah recovered.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A poultice. Isaiah has just announced a miraculous reversal and then calls for a fruit compress."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The account puts the promise and the poultice in consecutive sentences without any apparent tension between them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Hezekiah did with the fifteen years.** Manasseh, the worst king Judah ever had, was born three years into them. And Hezekiah showed the Babylonian envoys everything in his treasury, and was told that all of it would one day be carried to Babylon — to which he answered *the word of the LORD is good*, because *there will be peace and truth in my days.*"}]}, "2 Kings 20:8–11": {"title": "The shadow that went back", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A dying king asks for proof and is offered a choice about which direction time should go."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What is the sign** that the LORD will heal me?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had already been told he would live and given a number of years. He asks for a sign anyway, and nobody in the passage rebukes him for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah simply provides one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Shall the shadow go forward ten steps, or back ten steps?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two options, and the prophet lets the king pick."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hezekiah chooses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It's easy for the shadow to go forward ten steps** — no, let it **go back** ten steps.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He rules out the easy one himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A shadow moves forward all day without anybody's help. Forward is what shadows do."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks for the one that cannot happen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Isaiah the prophet **called on the LORD**, and the LORD **drew the shadow back the ten steps** it had already gone down on the stairway of Ahaz.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the steps of Ahaz* — a stairway built by his father, evidently functioning as a sundial, with the shadow marking the hours down the stairs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahaz was the king who shut the temple doors and sacrificed his own son. His stairway is where the sign is given to the son who reopened them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Ten steps"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten of the marks the shadow had already covered. Time already spent, given back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chronicler notes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "2 Chronicles adds one line about the whole episode: *God withdrew from him, in order to test him, that He might know all that was in his heart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And 2 Chronicles also records who was watching — *the envoys of the princes of Babylon… sent to inquire about **the wonder that was done in the land**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Babylonians came to ask about the shadow. Astronomy was their profession."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other long day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sun stood still, and the moon stopped… **there has never been a day like it**, before or since — a day when the LORD listened to the voice of a man.* See the note on Joshua 10:12–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua's day was lengthened in the middle of a battle. Hezekiah's was reversed at a sickbed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah's own account adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 38 records Hezekiah's writing after his recovery — a psalm, in the first person:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I said, \"In the prime of my life I shall go to the gates of Sheol\"… **like a weaver I have cut off my life**… O LORD, by these things men live… **You have lovingly delivered my soul from the pit of corruption**, for You have cast all my sins behind Your back.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one thing he says he will do with the years.** *The living, the living man, he shall praise You, as I do this day; the father shall make known Your truth to the children.* Hezekiah's stated plan for the added time is telling his children."}]}, "2 Kings 25:4–7": {"title": "The last thing he saw", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of the kingdom of Judah, reported in four verses without comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The siege"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the city wall was breached**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eighteen months of it. The chapter opens with Nebuchadnezzar arriving in the ninth year of Zedekiah's reign and building siege works all round."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the fourth month of the eleventh year *the famine had become so severe in the city that there was no food for the people of the land*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lamentations describes what that meant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The escape"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the night, with the Babylonians ringed around the city, **the soldiers all fled through the gate between the two walls, by the king's garden**, and made for the Arabah.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A postern gate at the southern end, near the pool of Siloam. Out through the garden, in the dark, heading for the Jordan valley."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The capture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the Babylonian army pursued the king and **overtook him in the plains of Jericho**, and **all his soldiers scattered from him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jericho. Where Israel's occupation of the land began, and where its monarchy ends."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His army leaves him in the open."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They captured the king and brought him to the king of Babylon **at Riblah**, where **sentence was passed** against him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Riblah is in Syria, on the Orontes, about two hundred miles north — Nebuchadnezzar's forward headquarters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zedekiah is walked there under guard to be tried."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There his **sons were killed before his eyes**; then **his own eyes were put out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is the point, and the Babylonians knew what they were doing. The last image he carried was arranged."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chains"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **bound him in bronze chains** and took him to Babylon.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase used of Samson in Gaza. See the note on Judges 16:21–22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Blinded, in bronze, in a foreign city, grinding out the rest of his life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two prophecies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah had told him: *you shall not escape from his hand, but shall surely be taken and delivered into his hand; **your eyes shall see the eyes of the king of Babylon**, he shall speak with you face to face, and you shall go to Babylon.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel, in exile, had said the opposite-sounding thing: *I will bring him to Babylon, to the land of the Chaldeans; **yet he shall not see it**, though he shall die there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both were true. He met Nebuchadnezzar face to face at Riblah, and never saw Babylon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Zedekiah had done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He did evil in the sight of the LORD.* And he had asked Jeremiah for a word repeatedly, in private, and had not acted on any of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah's last offer to him: *if you surely surrender to the king of Babylon's princes, then your soul shall live*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was afraid of what his own officials would say."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The temple burned, the palace burned, every great house burned. The bronze pillars broken up and carried off. The walls pulled down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *the captain of the guard left some of the poor of the land as vinedressers and farmers*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last paragraph of Kings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty-seven years later, in Babylon, Jehoiachin is released from prison, given new clothes, and *he ate bread regularly before the king all the days of his life*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book that ends with a burnt city ends with a man at a table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was still standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is the covenant that I will make with the house of Israel after those days, says the LORD: **I will put My law in their minds, and write it on their hearts**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah wrote that during the siege."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the Babylonians arranged.** The order of the two acts. His sons first, and then his eyes — so that the last thing he ever saw was chosen for him."}]}, "2 Kings 3:13–15": {"title": "Bring me a musician", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three kings with an army and no water, and the prophet asks for a harp."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moab has rebelled. Three kings march against it — Jehoram of Israel, Jehoshaphat of Judah, and the king of Edom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They take a seven-day march through the wilderness of Edom and *there was no water for the army*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Alas! For the LORD has called these three kings together to deliver them into the hand of Moab.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jehoshaphat: *\"**Is there no prophet of the LORD here, that we may enquire of the LORD by him?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A servant answers: *Elisha the son of Shaphat is here, **who poured water on the hands of Elijah***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The description is of a servant's job. That is how Elisha is identified."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Elisha said to the king of Israel, \"**What do you and I have to do with each other? Go to the prophets of your father and the prophets of your mother**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahab and Jezebel. The prophets of Baal and Asherah, four hundred and fifty and four hundred, whom Elijah had faced on Carmel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha sends the king back to the family's own religious staff."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The king's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"No, for **it was the LORD who called us three kings together to hand us over to Moab**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He appeals to the LORD's name while blaming Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**As surely as the LORD Almighty lives, whom I serve** — if it were not for **my regard for Jehoshaphat king of Judah**, I would not so much as look at you or notice you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lule penei Yehoshaphat** — were it not for the face of Jehoshaphat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He will speak, and he says exactly who he is speaking for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But now, bring me a musician**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **qechu-li menaggen** — bring me one who plays."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nagan** — to play a stringed instrument. The verb used of David playing for Saul: *David would take a harp and play it with his hand*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is happening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A prophet has been asked for a word by three kings in a military emergency, in front of an army dying of thirst."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks for a harpist first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **as the musician played, the hand of the LORD came on Elisha**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayehi kenaggen hamnaggen vattehi alav yad-YHWH**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The playing is in progress when it comes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yad-YHWH** — the hand of the LORD. The phrase for prophetic possession: *the hand of the LORD was upon me* in Ezekiel, and *the hand of the LORD was there upon him*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the music"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text does not explain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What it records is that a man who had just been furious with a king asked for a harp before he would prophesy, and that the word came while it was being played."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Make this valley full of ditches… **you shall not see wind, nor shall you see rain; yet that valley shall be filled with water**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it happened in the morning… that suddenly water came by way of Edom, and the land was filled with water.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ditches dug in dry ground, no rain, and water in the morning from a storm somewhere they never saw."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Moabites see"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they saw the water on the other side as red as blood. And they said, \"**This is blood**; the kings have surely struck swords and have killed one another.\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sunrise on the water. They come out for plunder and are routed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who else played"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it happened, when the spirit from God was upon Saul, that **David would take a harp and play it with his hand**. Then Saul would become refreshed and well.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The request.** Three kings, an army without water, and a demand for a word — and the prophet's first instruction is *bring me a musician*. The hand of the LORD comes while the man is playing."}]}, "2 Kings 4:1–7": {"title": "The widow's oil", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A woman about to lose her children asks for help, and is asked a question back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she has lost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your servant **my husband is dead** — and you know he honoured the LORD. But now **a creditor has come to take my two boys as his slaves**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under the law a debtor's children could be taken in payment until the jubilee. It was legal. She has no case to make."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her husband was one of the company of prophets, and she says he feared the LORD. Being godly had not exempted him from dying in debt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How can I help you? Tell me — what do you have in the house?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha does not produce anything. He asks what is already there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your servant has **nothing there at all** — **except a small jar of oil**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She says nothing first, and then corrects herself. The oil is so small it did not count as having something."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a flask of anointing oil — the amount you would use once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instructions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Ask **all your neighbours for empty jars** — and **don't ask for just a few**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing she has to do is go and borrow emptiness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a public act. Every neighbour is going to know something is happening, and she has to explain herself at every door — or not explain, and look stranger still."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Don't ask for just a few.**\"* The only limit in the story is set here, by her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Behind a closed door"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then go inside, **shut the door behind you and your sons**, and start pouring.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gathering is public and the pouring is private. Nobody watches the miracle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha is not in the room either. He does not come, does not pray over it, and does not touch the oil."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What stops it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the jars were full, she said to her son, \"Bring me another.\" \"**There isn't one left**,\" he told her. **And then the oil stopped.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not when the jar ran dry. When the containers ran out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Illumination puts the point in the last line: *grace kept flowing until every borrowed jar was full.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go, **sell the oil and pay your debt**. **You and your sons can live on what is left**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things, in that order. The debt cleared, and an income after it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not tell her to give it away or bring it to him. She keeps the surplus and lives on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she started with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A dead husband, two boys about to be taken, no money, and one flask of oil she had not thought worth mentioning."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other widow with a jar.** At Zarephath, in Elijah's day — a handful of flour and a little oil, and she was gathering sticks to make a last meal before she and her son died. *The bin of flour was not used up, nor did the jar of oil run dry.* The prophets keep meeting women at the end of their supplies."}]}, "2 Kings 4:18–25": {"title": "\"It's all right\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A child dies at noon and his mother says one sentence to everyone she meets."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The field"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The boy grew. One day he **went out to his father among the harvesters**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The son she had been promised and had not asked for. See the note on 2 Kings 4:8–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The collapse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Suddenly he cried, \"**My head! My head!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words, repeated. Sunstroke is the usual reading; the text does not say."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The father"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Carry him to his mother**,\" the father told a servant.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not go himself. The harvest is on and he sends the boy back with a servant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the account suggests he understands how serious it is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lap"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The boy **sat on her lap until noon — and then he died**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew gives the hour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she did with the body"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She **carried him up and laid him on the bed of the man of God**, **shut the door on him**, and **went out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three actions, and none of them is what a mother would ordinarily do."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not call the household, does not wail, and does not send for the burial party."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She puts him on the prophet's bed — in the room she built, on the furniture she chose — and closes the door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she says to her husband"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Send me one of the servants and a donkey**, so I can hurry to the man of God and come back.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not tell him the boy is dead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why go to him today?** It's **not the New Moon or the Sabbath**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which tells you the local custom: you visited a prophet on holy days."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is puzzled by the schedule, not alarmed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It's all right**,\" she said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shalom** — peace, it is well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She says it three times in the chapter. Here to her husband, then to Gehazi sent to meet her on the road — *is it well with you? Is it well with your husband? Is it well with the child?* and she answers **shalom** — and only when she reaches Elisha does anything else come out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She will not say it to anyone but him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ride"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She **saddled the donkey** and told her servant, \"**Lead on. Don't slow down for me unless I tell you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *do not slacken the pace for me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Shunem to Carmel is about fifteen miles."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens at the mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha sees her coming and sends Gehazi. She goes past him, comes to Elisha and *caught him by the feet*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gehazi tries to push her away, and Elisha says: *let her alone, for **her soul is in deep distress**, and **the LORD has hidden it from me, and has not told me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not know. The prophet says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she says at last"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Did I ask a son of my lord? Did I not say, Do not deceive me?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only complaint she makes in the chapter, and it is the sentence she had said when the promise was given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gehazi is sent ahead with the staff and nothing happens. Elisha comes himself, shuts the door on the two of them, prays, and stretches himself on the child. See the note on 2 Kings 4:32–37."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the child sneezed seven times, and the child opened his eyes.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What she says to everyone on the road.** *Shalom.* A dead boy is on a bed behind a shut door and she tells her husband and the prophet's servant that it is well — and says nothing else until she has hold of Elisha's feet."}]}, "2 Kings 4:32–37": {"title": "A son raised", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Elisha raises the Shunammite's boy, and every step of it is worth counting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it took"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["he **shut the door** on the two of them", "he **prayed** to the LORD", "he **climbed onto the bed and lay over the boy** — mouth to mouth, eyes to eyes, hands to hands", "the child's body **grew warm**", "he **got up and walked back and forth** across the room", "he **bent over him once more**", "the boy **sneezed seven times** and opened his eyes"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven movements. A closed door, a prayer, physical contact along the whole length of the body, a pause, and a second attempt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophet has to give himself to it — literally lie on the boy and cover him, limb for limb."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Take your son\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Elisha said, \"**Take your son**.\" She came in, fell at his feet, and bowed to the ground. Then she took her son and went out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has to come in and collect him. And she goes to the floor first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Shunem, in the Jezreel valley. The name means double resting place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A few miles away over the ridge of Mount Moreh — whose name means teacher — is Nain, which means pleasant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two towns in the same valley, either side of one hill, and a boy raised from the dead in each of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Nain there is no closed door, no prayer, no stretching over the body, no pacing, no second attempt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Young man, I say to you, get up.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, spoken in the open air, in front of two crowds. He does not shut anybody out and He does not ask God for anything — He addresses the boy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at the end: *Jesus **gave him back to his mother**.* Elisha said *take your son.* Jesus carried him over. See the note on Luke 7:11–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elisha had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had asked for a double portion of Elijah's spirit and had received it. He is the greatest of the prophets by the count of his miracles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And this is what the best of them had to do to raise one boy."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the woman had said on the way.** Her son had died on her lap that morning, and she saddled a donkey, rode to Carmel, and when Gehazi asked if all was well she answered *it is well.* She said it to Gehazi and she said it to her husband before she left. She had not told anybody the boy was dead."}]}, "2 Kings 4:8–10": {"title": "The room on the roof", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A woman notices something about a passing prophet and builds him a room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The town"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One day Elisha was passing through **Shunem**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A village in the Jezreel valley, on the route between Carmel and the Jordan. Elisha walked it regularly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The woman"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where **a well-to-do woman** lived.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ishah gedolah** — a great woman. It can mean wealthy, prominent, or of standing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is never named. Through four chapters she is *the Shunammite*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first move"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She **urged him to stay for a meal**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *she constrained him* — pressed him to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And after that, **whenever he came by, he would stop there to eat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One meal became a habit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her reasoning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She said to her husband, \"**I know that this man who often passes our way is a holy man of God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She works it out herself. Nobody tells her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The husband is not the one who notices, and he does not object."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proposal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let's **make a small room on the roof** for him and put **a bed in it, a table, a chair, and a lamp**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four items. The Hebrew lists them one by one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A bed to sleep, a table to work, a chair to sit, and a lamp to see. It is the minimum furniture for a life, and she thought through each piece."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then **whenever he comes to us, he'll have a place to stay**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not for an occasion. For every time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she gets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha sends Gehazi to ask what can be done for her — *would you like to be spoken for to the king or to the commander of the army?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her answer: *I dwell among my own people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She wants nothing. It is Gehazi who observes that she has no son and her husband is old."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"About this time next year you shall embrace a son.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And her reply: *\"No, my lord. **Man of God, do not lie to your maidservant**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had not asked and did not want the hope."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The boy grows, goes out to the harvesters, cries *my head, my head*, and dies on her lap at noon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She carries him up to the roof room, lays him on the prophet's bed, shuts the door, and rides to Carmel. See the note on 2 Kings 4:18–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where she puts him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On the bed she built."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she says on the road"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To her husband: *it is well.* To Gehazi, sent to meet her: *it is well.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only when she reaches Elisha and takes hold of his feet does she say anything else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the story ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he called Gehazi and said, \"Call this Shunammite woman.\" So he called her. And when she came in to him, he said, \"**Pick up your son**.\" So she went in, fell at his feet, bowed to the ground, **picked up her son, and went out**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last we hear of her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 8: Elisha warns her of a seven-year famine, she leaves for Philistine country, and comes back to petition the king for her house and land — arriving at the exact moment Gehazi is telling the king the story of her son."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She gets everything back, *and all the proceeds of the field from the day that she left the land until now*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What she built.** Four pieces of furniture in a room on a roof, for a man who came through occasionally — and it is the bed she lays her dead son on years later."}]}, "2 Kings 5:1": {"title": "But he had leprosy", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse of introduction, and the last two words undo everything before them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There was a man named **Naaman**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **Na'aman** means *pleasantness*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rank"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Commander of the army of the king of **Aram**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Syria — Israel's persistent enemy. The next chapter has Aramean raiding parties in Israelite territory, and the chapter after that has Samaria under siege by the same army."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the enemy's general."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was **a great man in his master's eyes, held in high honor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **gadol… venesu panim** — great, and lifted of face."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nasa panim** — the idiom for someone whose face is raised, who is received and respected."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **through him the LORD had given Aram victory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ki-vo natan YHWH teshu'ah la'Aram**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**YHWH** — the covenant name. The narrator of an Israelite history says the God of Israel gave victory to Syria, through a Syrian, against — among others — Israel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated without comment or apology."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The soldier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was **a mighty soldier, a warrior**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **gibbor chayil** — mighty of valour. The phrase used of David's champions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **he had leprosy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **metzora** — a leper. Two words at the end of a verse that has spent itself on his greatness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Hebrew does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in the sentence is superlative until the last clause, which is introduced by a single conjunction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse is built to arrive there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind of leprosy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Syrian leprosy did not carry the Israelite legal exclusion. He is still commanding an army and still in the king's presence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But it is progressive and incurable, and everybody knows it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who knows the answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the Syrians had gone out on raids, and had brought back **captive a young girl** from the land of Israel. She waited on Naaman's wife.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then she said to her mistress, \"**If only my master were with the prophet who is in Samaria!** For he would heal him of his leprosy.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A slave, taken in a raid conducted by this man's army, tells his wife where to find help."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is not named. She has the only useful information in the chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Goes to his king, who writes to the king of Israel, who tears his clothes: *am I God, to kill and make alive?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Naaman arrives at Elisha's door with horses and chariots, ten talents of silver, six thousand shekels of gold and ten changes of clothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elisha does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Does not come out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sends a messenger: *go and wash in the Jordan seven times*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Naaman **became furious**, and went away and said, \"Indeed, I said to myself, He will surely come out to me… and wave his hand over the place.\"* See the note on 2 Kings 5:10–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who talks him into it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His servants: *if the prophet had told you to do something great, would you not have done it?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So he went down and dipped seven times… **and his flesh was restored like the flesh of a little child**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Jesus mentions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **many lepers were in Israel** in the time of Elisha the prophet, and none of them was cleansed **except Naaman the Syrian**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said in the synagogue at Nazareth, and *all those in the synagogue, when they heard these things, were filled with wrath*. See the note on Luke 4:25–27."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last clause.** Everything in the verse is superlative until two words at the end, and the sentence is built to land on them."}]}, "2 Kings 5:14": {"title": "Flesh like a little child's", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A general with an army behind him ends up standing in a muddy river doing something undignified seven times, and comes out with the skin of a child."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had brought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Naaman arrived with horses, chariots, ten talents of silver, six thousand shekels of gold and ten changes of clothes — a fortune, and a letter from his king."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha did not even come outside. He sent a servant with a message: go and wash in the Jordan seven times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Naaman's fury is recorded in detail. He had expected a ceremony. He had better rivers at home — and he names them, Abana and Pharpar, clean water against Jordan's brown."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What actually healed him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Illumination puts it plainly: *it was not his greatness that healed him, nor his gold, nor the rivers he preferred. It was the goodness of God, freely given, and received at last like a child.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything he brought was useless. The one thing required was to go down into the water he thought beneath him, the full seven times, and come up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Clean and fresh as the flesh of a little child\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is exactly that — his flesh returned like the flesh of a small boy. Not merely cured. Made new."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is a nice detail in who talked him into it. His own servants — the people with no standing at all — reason with him: if the prophet had asked something great, you would have done it. Why not this?"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The gift he tried to give afterwards.** Naaman goes back and offers the whole treasure, and Elisha refuses every coin of it. The healing was free at both ends: nothing bought it, and nothing was accepted for it. Gehazi chasing after the money in the next paragraph, and catching the leprosy, is the chapter's own comment on that."}]}, "2 Kings 6:15": {"title": "\"What shall we do?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A servant goes out early and finds the town surrounded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What led to it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king of Aram had been raiding Israel, and every ambush failed because Elisha kept telling Israel's king where the camps were."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Aramean king assumed a traitor, and was told: *Elisha, the prophet who is in Israel, **tells the king of Israel the words that you speak in your bedroom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His response was to send an army after one man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the **servant of the man of God rose early** the next morning and went out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes out first, before Elisha."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he sees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There it was — **an army with horses and chariots ringed all around the city**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the city was surrounded*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before says the Aramean king *sent horses and chariots and a great army* by night, and they came and surrounded Dothan while everyone slept."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about his report is wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Oh, my master**,\" the servant said, \"**what shall we do?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *alas, my master*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two men in a town with an army round it, and one of them is a prophet with no army at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *\"**Do not fear, for those who are with us are more than those who are with them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is, on the visible evidence, absurd. There are two of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Elisha prayed, and said, \"**LORD, I pray, open his eyes that he may see**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not pray for the army to leave, or for an escape, or for the servant's courage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He prays about eyes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the LORD opened the eyes of the young man, and he saw. And behold, **the mountain was full of horses and chariots of fire all around Elisha**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been there the whole time. Nothing was added; something was shown."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha prays again, and this time for the opposite: *strike this people, I pray, with blindness*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he walks into the middle of the blinded army and says *this is not the way, nor is this the city; follow me* — and leads them into Samaria."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king of Israel asks *shall I kill them?* twice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You shall not kill them… **set food and water before them**, that they may eat and drink and go to their master.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he prepared a great feast for them; and after they ate and drank, he sent them away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An army that came to arrest him is fed and released."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the bands of Syrian raiders **came no more into the land of Israel**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chariots turn up again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha's own master had gone up in one: *suddenly a chariot of fire and horses of fire swept between them*. See the note on 2 Kings 2:11–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what Elisha shouted then — *the chariots and horsemen of Israel!* — is what the servant is now shown a hillside of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the servant was not told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To be braver. The prophet's whole response to the panic is one prayer about sight."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What changed on the hill.** Not the odds. The Aramean army was there and stayed there. One man saw what else was on the ground, and the prayer that got him there was about eyes."}]}, "2 Kings 6:16": {"title": "\"More than those with them\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, spoken to a terrified servant who has just done the arithmetic correctly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the servant saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before: the king of Syria has sent *horses and chariots and a great army* to Dothan by night, *and they surrounded the city.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The servant gets up early, goes out, and there it is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Alas, my master! **What shall we do?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His count is right. Two men, one town, and an army round it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Don't be afraid.** Those who are with us are **more than those who are with them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha does not dispute the servant's figures. He adds a column the servant did not know about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elisha prays"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"LORD, I pray, **open his eyes that he may see**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *remove the army*. Not *get us out*. Open his eyes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophet's request is about the servant's perception, and nothing about the situation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was there the whole time"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD opened the eyes of the young man, and he saw. And behold, **the mountain was full of horses and chariots of fire all around Elisha**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It had been there before the prayer. The praying changed the seeing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha was not asking for reinforcements. He could already see them, which is why he was calm at dawn with an army outside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha prays a second time — *strike this people with blindness* — and then walks the entire Syrian army into Samaria and asks the king of Israel to feed them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Set food and water before them, that they may eat and drink and go to their master.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he prepared a great feast for them; and after they ate and drank, he sent them away.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the bands of Syrian raiders **came no more** into the land of Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The campaign ends not with a slaughter but with a meal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the army was there at all"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha had been telling the king of Israel where the Syrian ambushes were, repeatedly — *the prophet who is in Israel tells the king of Israel the words that you speak in your bedroom.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The army was sent to capture one man who knew too much."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other prayer in the chapter.** A borrowed axe head falls into the Jordan and a student cries out *alas, master, it was borrowed.* Elisha cuts a stick, throws it in, and the iron floats. Chariots of fire and a lost tool in the same chapter, and the book gives both the same attention."}]}, "2 Kings 7:3–4": {"title": "Why sit here waiting to die", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four men do the arithmetic on their own situation and find that one column is not worse than the others."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The siege"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ben-Hadad of Aram has surrounded Samaria. The famine inside is so severe that a donkey's head sells for eighty shekels, and two women have agreed to eat their sons."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king tears his clothes and the people see sackcloth underneath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elisha had said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Tomorrow about this time **a seah of fine flour shall be sold for a shekel, and two seahs of barley for a shekel**, at the gate of Samaria.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ordinary market prices, promised for the next day, in a city eating its children."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the officer on whose arm the king leaned: *if the LORD would make windows in heaven, could this thing be?* See the note on 2 Kings 7:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is at the gate"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now there were **four men with leprosy at the entrance of the city gate**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Outside the wall, as the law required. Not in the city, not in the enemy camp. In the strip between."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are the last people anyone in the story is thinking about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why sit here waiting to die?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lammah anachnu yoshevim po ad-matnu** — why are we sitting here until we die."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three options"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If we say, 'Let's go into the city' — **the famine is there, and we'll die**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **if we stay here, we'll die too**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So let's go over to the camp of the Arameans **and surrender**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They lay it out honestly. Two columns are certain death. The third is not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reasoning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If they spare us, we live; if they kill us, then we die**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is not optimism. They are not expecting mercy from a besieging army toward four lepers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole argument is that the worst outcome of moving is the same as the guaranteed outcome of staying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind of faith this is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text does not call it faith. Nobody prays, nobody quotes a promise, and Elisha's word never reaches them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are four sick men reasoning their way out of a doorway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they find"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they rose at twilight to go to the camp… **and when they had come to the outskirts of the camp, surprise — no one was there***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Lord had made the Aramean army hear the sound of chariots and horses, and they had fled at dusk leaving tents, horses, donkeys, silver, gold and clothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The camp had been empty for hours before the lepers decided to walk."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They went into one tent and **ate and drank**, and carried from it silver and gold and clothing, and went and hid them. Then they came back and entered another tent, and carried some from there also, and went and hid it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two tents' worth, buried, before anyone thinks about the city behind them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What stops them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then they said to one another, \"**What we're doing is not right. This is a day of good news, and we're keeping it to ourselves**.\"* See the note on 2 Kings 7:9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was fed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole city, through four men who were not allowed inside it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the people went out and plundered the tents of the Arameans. **Then a seah of fine flour was sold for a shekel***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exactly as Elisha had said, to the hour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The officer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the people trampled him in the gate, and he died* — the man who had said windows in heaven could not do it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha's word to him had been: *you shall see it with your eyes, but you shall not eat of it*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The arithmetic.** Three columns, two of them certain death and the third only probable. That is the entire reasoning, and it is not called faith anywhere in the chapter."}]}, "2 Kings 7:9": {"title": "A day of good news", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "They stop halfway through the looting because of something they say to each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four lepers had walked to the Aramean camp expecting to be killed and found it abandoned. See the note on 2 Kings 7:3–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They went into one tent and ate and drank, and carried from it silver and gold and clothing, and went and hid them.* And then a second tent, and hid that too."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody would blame them. They had been starving outside a gate that would not let them in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then they said to each other, \"**What we're doing is not right**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lo-chen anachnu osim** — not right, not so, are we doing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No prophet appears. No message comes. Four men stop in the middle of a second tent and say it to one another."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This is a day of good news, and we're keeping it to ourselves**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yom besorah hu** — a day of good news."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Besorah** is the noun the Septuagint renders **euangelia** — the word behind *gospel*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is behind them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A city eating its own children, whose gate they are not permitted through."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people who had excluded them are the people they go back for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are afraid of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If we wait until daylight, **punishment will overtake us**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **avon** — iniquity, and the guilt that follows it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not fear of being caught with the silver. The word is about culpability."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The decision"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Let's go at once and report this to the royal palace**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They go in the dark, which means they abandon the rest of the camp to anyone else who might wander out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had to leave"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Tents full of silver, gold, horses and clothing, unguarded, in order to reach a gatekeeper before morning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the gatekeeper does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they went and called to the gatekeepers of the city, and told them… **and they called out to the gatekeepers, and they told it to the king's household inside***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The message goes up the chain from men who could not enter the city, through the gate, to the palace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The king's response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not believe it. *I will now tell you what the Arameans have done to us. They know that we are hungry; therefore they have gone out of the camp to hide in the field.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An ambush, he says. It takes a servant proposing to send five horses to check."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was true"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And indeed all the road was full of garments and weapons which the Arameans had thrown away in their haste.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The evidence had been lying on the road the whole time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened at the gate"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the people went out and plundered the tents of the Arameans. **Then a seah of fine flour was sold for a shekel, and two seahs of barley for a shekel, according to the word of the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exactly what Elisha had said the day before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who delivered it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four men with a disease that put them outside the wall, who found the food, ate first, buried treasure, and then stopped and said *this is not right*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrative does not clean up the sequence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah does with the same word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How beautiful upon the mountains are **the feet of him who brings good news**, who proclaims peace.* See the note on Isaiah 52:7–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same root, **mevasser**, and the same picture: someone running toward a city with news of a siege lifted."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What stops them.** Not a prophet and not a command. Four men in a second tent, saying it to each other: *this is a day of good news, and we are keeping it to ourselves.*"}]}, "2 Kings 8:1": {"title": "Go away with your family", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A warning delivered to one household, seven years before it is needed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who she is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The woman **whose son he had restored to life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Shunammite. A wealthy woman who had noticed Elisha passing and built him a room on the roof — a bed, a table, a stool and a lampstand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had asked for nothing. When he offered to speak for her to the king or the commander of the army, she said *I dwell among my own people*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What had happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha promised her a son, and she said *no, my lord… do not lie to your maidservant*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The boy was born. Years later he collapsed in the harvest field — *my head, my head* — and died on her knees at noon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She laid him on the prophet's bed, saddled a donkey, rode to Carmel and would not say what was wrong. *It is well*, she kept saying. See the note on 2 Kings 4:25–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go away with your family and stay for a while wherever you can**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **quwmi ulechi at uveitech vegurri ba'asher taguri** — arise and go, you and your household, and sojourn wherever you can sojourn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Gur** — to sojourn, to live as a resident alien. Abraham's word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because **the LORD has decreed a famine in the land that will last seven years**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **qara YHWH lara'av** — the LORD has called for a famine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Called for.** As one summons a servant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The specificity"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven years, named in advance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has time to sell nothing, pack, and leave in order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it costs her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her house and her land, left standing empty in a country under famine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Property abandoned for seven years does not stay yours. The rest of the chapter is about getting it back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The woman **did as the man of God said**.* See the note on 2 Kings 8:2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No recorded question."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where she goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And stayed in **the land of the Philistines** seven years.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel's enemies. The only place with food."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Naomi's family did the same thing in Moab, and it went badly. Isaac was told to stay put and not go down to Egypt. See the note on Genesis 26:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here she is told to go, and she goes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens at the end of the seven years"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She comes back and finds her property gone, and goes to the king to appeal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the king is at that moment asking Gehazi to *tell me all the great things Elisha has done* — and Gehazi is in the middle of the story of the boy raised from the dead when she walks in. See the note on 2 Kings 8:4–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the king gives her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Give back everything that belonged to her, including all the produce of her land from the day she left the country until now**.\"* See the note on 2 Kings 8:6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land, and seven years of harvests off land she was not farming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for famine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ra'av** — hunger, famine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha's ministry runs through famine repeatedly: the poisoned stew, the twenty loaves that fed a hundred, the siege of Samaria and the four lepers at the gate. See the note on 2 Kings 7:3–4."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb.** *The LORD has called for a famine.* Summoned, as one calls a servant — and the household most likely to be ruined by it gets seven years' notice."}]}, "2 Kings 8:2": {"title": "She did as the man of God said", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse of obedience, and it costs her everything she owns for seven years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The woman **did as the man of God said**. She and her family went away and **stayed in the land of the Philistines seven years**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she was told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go away with your family and stay for a while wherever you can, because the LORD has decreed a famine in the land that will last seven years.\"* See the note on 2 Kings 8:1."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not recorded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any question."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not ask where to go, how they will live, what happens to the house, or why God would call a famine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator gives the instruction in one verse and the compliance in the next."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who she is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A **great woman** — **ishah gedolah** — of Shunem. Wealthy enough to build a room on her roof for a passing prophet and furnish it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has property, standing, and a settled place among her own people: *I dwell among my own people*, she said, when offered a word to the king on her behalf."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what she leaves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where she goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The land of the Philistines.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not Judah, not further inland. Philistia — the coastal plain, Israel's standing enemy, where Samson died and where the ark spent seven months."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gerar in that region is where Isaac was sent during a famine, and where he was told *do not go down to Egypt*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It had food."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The famine was on *the land* — Israel. The coast, with different rainfall and Egyptian trade, did not have it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parallel that went wrong"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now it came to pass in the days when the judges ruled, that **there was a famine in the land**. And a certain man of Bethlehem, Judah, went to dwell in the country of Moab, he and his wife and his two sons.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elimelech went to Moab in a famine, and he and both sons died there. Naomi came back saying *call me Mara*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book of Ruth begins with a family leaving for the same reason, and it is not presented as obedience."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The difference"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She was told to go."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the text says leaving during famine is right or wrong in itself. What the chapter records is that she did what the prophet said."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Vattelech** — and she went."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew names her as the actor. Her husband, old at the start of the story and probably dead by now, is not mentioned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She decides, she moves, and she later goes to the king alone to appeal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seven years"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The full term, exactly as announced."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator does not describe them. Seven years of living as a resident alien in enemy territory pass in half a verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she comes back to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It came to pass, at the end of seven years, that the woman returned from the land of the Philistines, and **she went to make an appeal to the king for her house and for her land**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The property is gone. Obedience did not preserve it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she gets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Give back everything that belonged to her, including all the produce of her land from the day she left the country until now**.\"* See the note on 2 Kings 8:6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven years of harvests off land she did not work, awarded by a king who happened to be hearing her story at the moment she walked in."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse.** An instruction, and a compliance, with nothing in between. Seven years in enemy territory pass in half a sentence."}]}, "2 Kings 8:4–5": {"title": "The very woman came in", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The timing of this scene is the whole point, and the narrator lays it out clause by clause."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now the king was talking with **Gehazi, the servant of the man of God**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Gehazi is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha's servant, and by this point a leper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had run after Naaman for the silver Elisha refused, lied about it, and been told *the leprosy of Naaman shall cling to you and your descendants forever*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he went out from his presence leprous, as white as snow.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem with the scene"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A leper is in conversation with the king."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either this happened before the Naaman episode, or the narrator has arranged the material by theme rather than chronology — which is how Kings often works."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The king's request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Tell me all the great things Elisha has done**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **sapperah-na li et kol-haggedolot** — recount to me all the great things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A king asking for stories about a prophet he has not sought out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The coincidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as Gehazi was telling the king **how Elisha had brought a dead boy back to life**, the very woman whose son had been restored came in.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vehinneh** — and behold."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She walks in on the sentence about her own son."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she came for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **appeal to the king for her house and her land**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has been gone seven years on the prophet's instruction, and the property is gone. See the note on 2 Kings 8:2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has no legal claim anyone would recognise. Abandoned land in a famine does not wait."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Gehazi says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My lord the king, this is the woman, and this is her son whom Elisha restored to life!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **zot ha'ishah vezeh benah**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The living proof of the story he was telling walks through the door with the boy beside her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The odds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She could have arrived any day of seven years, at any hour, and been told the king was busy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She arrives at the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the narrator does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That God arranged it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter states the sequence and lets it stand. Nothing in the text comments on the timing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she is given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The king asked the woman about it, and she told him. Then he assigned an official to her case, saying, \"**Give back everything that belonged to her, including all the produce of her land from the day she left the country until now**.\"* See the note on 2 Kings 8:6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not just the land. Seven years of harvest off it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she never asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The produce."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She came for the house and the field. The king adds the back-rent himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where she started"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I dwell among my own people**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her answer to Elisha years earlier, when he offered to speak for her *to the king, or to the commander of the army*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She declined an introduction to the king then. She gets an audience now, without one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she had said at the worst moment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It is well**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to her husband, and to Gehazi, with her son lying dead on the prophet's bed upstairs. See the note on 2 Kings 4:25–30."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clause.** *Just as Gehazi was telling the king how Elisha had brought a dead boy back to life.* She walks in on the sentence about her own son, and the narrator makes no comment."}]}, "2 Kings 8:6": {"title": "All the produce from the day she left", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "She asked for a house and a field, and the ruling covers seven years she was not there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hearing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The king asked the woman about it, and **she told him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vattesapper-lo** — and she recounted to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb used two verses earlier when the king asked Gehazi to *recount to me all the great things Elisha has done*. See the note on 2 Kings 8:4–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gehazi was telling the story. Now the woman tells her own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The officer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he **assigned an official to her case**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **saris** — a court official, or eunuch."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a promise. An administrator, appointed to see it done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ruling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Give back everything that belonged to her**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hashev et-kol-asher-lah** — restore all that is hers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb **shuv** — to return, to restore."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The addition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Including **all the produce of her land from the day she left the country until now**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ve'et kol-tevu'ot hassadeh** — and all the increase of the field."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven harvests, off land she did not sow, in a country under famine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whoever occupied it kept it working. The yield is awarded to her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Her house and her land.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The produce is not in her petition. The king adds it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The famine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seven years were a famine — *the LORD has decreed a famine in the land that will last seven years*. See the note on 2 Kings 8:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which raises the question of how much produce there was, and the ruling covers whatever there was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern in the law"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He shall make restitution for it in full, **and shall add one-fifth more to it**.* See the note on Leviticus 6:1–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The guilt offering required the original plus twenty per cent. Restoration in the law is never merely the amount taken."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other restitution"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If a man steals an ox or a sheep, and slaughters it or sells it, he shall restore **five oxen for an ox and four sheep for a sheep**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where she started"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A woman who had said *I dwell among my own people* when offered a word to the king on her behalf."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She wanted nothing from the palace, and left the country on a prophet's instruction, and came back to nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she has at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her house, her field, seven years of yield, and a court officer assigned to enforce it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was said over her at the beginning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"About this time next year you shall embrace a son.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And she said, \"No, my lord. Man of God, **do not lie to your maidservant**!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole story runs from a promise she did not want to believe."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she said with the boy dead upstairs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It is well**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice — to her husband, and to Gehazi on the road — while riding to Carmel with the body on the prophet's bed. See the note on 2 Kings 4:25–30."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the king adds.** She petitioned for the house and the field. The ruling covers seven harvests off land she was not on."}]}, "2 Peter 1:1–2": {"title": "\"Worth every bit as much as ours\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter opens his last letter by levelling the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Simon Peter here.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both names. The one he was born with and the one he was given."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the old form **Symeon** in the Greek, which is how James addresses him at the council in Acts 15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I **belong to Jesus Christ**, and He is the one who **sent me out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **doulos** — a slave — and **apostolos**, one sent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Slave first, apostle second. He puts the ownership before the office."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he is writing to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You who were **handed the same trust we were**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **lachousin** — to obtain by lot. It carries the sense of something allotted, not achieved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **in your hands it is worth every bit as much as it is in ours**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **isotimon** — of equal value, of the same honour. It occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was a commercial and civic word, used of foreigners granted the same rights as citizens."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter — an eyewitness, one of the three on the mountain, the man who preached at Pentecost — tells readers he has never met that their faith is worth exactly what his is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have it **because God is righteous, and because Jesus Christ our Savior is**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *by the righteousness of our God and Saviour Jesus Christ*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The equality is grounded in God's fairness, not in their attainment. It would be unjust for the standing to differ, because it does not come from the holder in either case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The greeting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*May **grace and peace keep arriving, more of it each time**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **plethuntheie** — be multiplied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter uses the same greeting in his first letter. Grace and peace, in that order, which is the order every New Testament letter uses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As you **come to know God, and Jesus our Lord**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epignosis** — full knowledge. It is the theme word of the whole letter; Peter uses it four times in the first chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The multiplying is attached to the knowing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His divine power has given to us **all things that pertain to life and godliness**, through the knowledge of Him who called us by glory and virtue.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the list of seven things to add. See the note on 2 Peter 1:5–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The supply is stated before anything is asked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he is writing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Knowing that shortly **I must put off my tent**, just as our Lord Jesus Christ showed me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus had told him at the lakeside how he would die."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is a man setting down what he wants remembered."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the first sentence establishes.** That the reader's faith is not a lesser edition of the writer's. Peter says it in the opening line, before any instruction, and grounds it in God's righteousness rather than theirs."}]}, "2 Peter 1:19": {"title": "A lamp in a pitch-black room", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter has just described the mountain — the voice, the brightness, the *this is My beloved Son*. He was standing there. And then he says something surprising about what is more reliable than that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Firmer now than ever\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the word the prophets spoke **stands firmer now than ever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a comparative: *more sure*, more confirmed. Peter is comparing the prophetic word to the transfiguration he has just recounted as an eyewitness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not doubt what he saw. He says the written word is the steadier of the two things to stand on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Picture a lamp lit in a pitch-black room — that is what it is.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word behind *pitch-black* is **auchmēros**: literally dry, parched, squalid, and so murky. It appears nowhere else in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lamp is not lighting a pleasant evening. It is the one light in a dry dark place, and the passage takes for granted that the place is dark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Until"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You watch it until the morning breaks through and the star that carries the day comes up **inside you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things happen at the end of the sentence, and they are different in kind:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the day dawns** — outside, the whole sky", "**the morning star rises in your hearts** — inside, personal"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Phōsphoros* is the light-bearer, the planet that rises just before sunrise. In Revelation, Jesus calls Himself *the bright morning star*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lamp is for the meantime. The star is the thing the lamp was pointing at all along."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse that follows.** Peter's next sentence is his reason: no prophecy of Scripture came about by the prophet's own working out of it. The lamp is trustworthy in the dark because of where it was lit."}]}, "2 Peter 1:5–7": {"title": "Set one thing next to another", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Seven items in a chain, and the chain is fastened at one end before Peter says anything about adding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On top of **all that**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *for this very reason*. Everything in the list is a response to what has just been stated:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His divine power has given to us **all things that pertain to life and godliness**… exceedingly great and precious promises, that through these you may be **partakers of the divine nature**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The supply is announced before the work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Set goodness down beside it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epichoregeo** — to supply, to furnish. It comes from the **choregos**, the wealthy citizen in Athens who paid for the chorus in a play: costumes, training, the whole production, out of his own pocket."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word carries lavishness, and Peter has just used the same root of God in verse 3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is already there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have **trust already**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Faith is not on the list of things to add. It is the thing everything else is set beside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seven"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Goodness** — **arete**, moral excellence, a thing performing what it is for", "**Understanding** — **gnosis**, knowledge", "**A firm hand on your own appetites** — **enkrateia**, self-control", "**Staying power to keep going when it costs you** — **hupomone**, remaining under", "**A life bent toward God** — **eusebeia**, godliness", "**Real warmth toward the family** — **philadelphia**, brotherly affection", "**Love** — **agape**"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shape of the list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each item is set beside the one before it. The Greek preposition is *in* — supply in your faith, virtue; in virtue, knowledge."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a ladder of stages, with one finished before the next begins. Peter is describing things laid alongside each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it starts and ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Faith at one end, love at the other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's ordering is the same: *faith working through love*, and *the greatest of these is love*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The effort"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not sit still. **Put everything you have into this**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **spouden pasan pareisenenkantes** — bringing in all diligence alongside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter asks for real exertion, and he asks for it on the strength of a supply he has already said was given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words for family and love"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Philadelphia** and **agape** are listed separately and in that order."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The warmth toward the household comes second-to-last, and love is set beside even that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the list is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next two verses give the reason, and it is about sight. See the note on 2 Peter 1:8–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is writing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter, in what he says is his last letter — *knowing that shortly I must put off my tent, just as our Lord Jesus Christ showed me.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise attached"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For if you do these things **you will never stumble**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order Peter uses.** God's supply first, stated in the fullest terms available. Then *for this very reason*, and then a list of things to bring alongside a faith the reader is told they already have."}]}, "2 Peter 1:8–9": {"title": "What grows and what goes blind", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two sentences, and the second one traces a problem back to something forgotten."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If those things are in you, and **there is more of them all the time**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *being in you and abounding* — both present participles, both continuous."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is not describing a completed set. He is describing something increasing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two negatives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will keep you from **standing around useless** and from **coming up empty**.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Useless** — **argos**, from *a-* and *ergon*: without work, idle. It is the word for the labourers standing in the marketplace all day in the parable.", "**Coming up empty** — **akarpos**, unfruitful."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter states the benefit as the absence of two failures."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the fruitfulness is located"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In **the knowing of our Lord Jesus Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epignosis** — full knowledge, knowledge that has landed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in ministry, in achievement, or in general. In the knowing of a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **whoever is missing them cannot see**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tuphlos** — blind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is **squinting at what sits right in front of his face and nothing past it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **muopazon** — the root of *myopic*. Short-sighted, screwing up the eyes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says blind and then immediately narrows it: not sightless, near-sighted. He can see what is close."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he has **forgotten that his old sins were washed off of him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **lethen labon** — having received forgetfulness. The root behind *Lethe*, the river of forgetting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the cause Peter lands on. Not weak will, not bad teaching, not lack of effort."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was forgotten"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That his old sins were washed off of him.* The Greek is **katharismos** — cleansing, purification. It is the word used of ritual purification, and of what Hezekiah's Levites did to the temple. See the note on 2 Chronicles 29:12–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A past event, done to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction of the argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter has just given a list of seven things to add. See the note on 2 Peter 1:5–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he explains what their absence means, and the explanation runs backward — the missing list is a symptom, and the thing underneath it is a forgotten cleansing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, brethren, be even more diligent to **make your call and election sure**, for if you do these things you will never stumble.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *I will not be negligent to **remind you always of these things**, though you know them and are established in the present truth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's stated method against the forgetting is repetition. He says it three times in four verses: *remind you*, *stir you up by reminding you*, *that you may be mindful*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he is in a hurry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Knowing that shortly I must put off my tent, just as our Lord Jesus Christ showed me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is writing what he wants remembered after he is gone."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the near-sighted man can see.** What is in front of him. The thing Peter says he has lost sight of is behind him — a washing that already happened."}]}, "2 Peter 2:4–6": {"title": "\"He did not let the ancient world off either\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter builds an argument out of three historical cases, and every one of them is treated as fact."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the angels who sinned** — thrown down, chained, kept for judgement", "**the ancient world** — the flood, with eight kept alive", "**Sodom and Gomorrah** — burned to ash"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three, in chronological order, and in each one the same two things happen: judgement falls, and somebody is preserved through it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He threw them down into **the pit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tartaroo** — a verb built from *Tartarus*, and it appears nowhere else in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Greek thought Tartarus was the deepest place, below Hades, where the Titans were confined. Peter borrows the word and uses it once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chains"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He handed them over to **chains and to the dark**, and there they are being kept until the judgement.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jude says the same in his own words: *angels who would not stay in the place that was theirs; they walked out of their own home. He has them now in chains that will never break.* See the note on Jude 1:5–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two letters, independently, describing angels held in confinement for something specific, and both put it next to Sodom and the flood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The eight"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He kept **eight alive, Noah among them**, a man who never stopped saying what was right.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *a preacher of righteousness* — a herald."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the only place in Scripture we are told Noah preached. Genesis records the building and not a word of the speaking, and Peter supplies it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundred and twenty years of hammering, and a man telling anyone who came by what it was for. Eight got in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Ash"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He burned Sodom and Gomorrah down **until there was nothing left but ash**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is to reduce to ashes — **tephroo**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the purpose: *He made of them **the specimen** of what is coming.* The word is **hupodeigma**, a pattern, an exhibit, something set out to be looked at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the argument is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is answering scoffers who say *where is the promise of His coming? Everything goes on as it always has.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His reply is a list of three times it did not. And the whole passage is one long conditional, completed in verse 9: *then the Lord knows how to deliver the godly out of temptations, and to reserve the unjust under punishment for the day of judgement.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three cases are evidence for a rescue, not only for a sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same comparison turns up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus used it Himself: *as it was in the days of Noah… and likewise as it was in the days of Lot.* Same two examples, same order."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word Peter uses for Lot.** *Righteous Lot, who was oppressed by the filthy conduct of the wicked… **tormented his righteous soul** from day to day.* Genesis gives Lot a poor account of himself, and Peter calls him righteous three times in two verses."}]}, "2 Peter 2:7–9": {"title": "He knows how to lift them out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter says three times in three verses that Lot was righteous, which nothing in Genesis prepares you for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list Peter is working through"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Angels who sinned, cast down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ancient world, and Noah *a preacher of righteousness* with seven others."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sodom and Gomorrah, turned to ashes. See the note on 2 Peter 2:4–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three examples of judgment, and in the middle of the third he stops for Lot."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rescue"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He pulled Lot out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **errusato** — delivered, dragged out of danger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis is literal about it: *while he lingered, the men took hold of his hand, his wife's hand, and the hands of his two daughters… and they brought him out and set him outside the city*. See the note on Genesis 19:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had to be pulled by the wrist."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man **in the right**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dikaion**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says it three times: righteous Lot, his righteous soul, and *the godly*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the Genesis account says it. Lot chooses the well-watered plain, pitches toward Sodom, ends up sitting in its gate, offers his daughters to a mob, argues with the angels about the destination, and ends the chapter in a cave."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter reads the same chapters and files him under righteous."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Worn down to nothing by **the filth those people lived in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kataponoumenon** — worn out, exhausted, oppressed to the point of collapse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The daily part"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **saw it, he heard it**, and **day after day their lawlessness scraped away at his soul**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ebasanizen** — tormented, tortured. The word for putting somebody to the question."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **hemeran ex hemeras** — day out of day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two senses named, and the erosion is described as continuous."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was not enjoying it. That is Peter's point, and it is the only evidence he offers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **the Lord knows exactly how to lift out the people who live toward Him when trials close in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **oiden kurios eusebeis ek peirasmou ruesthai** — the Lord knows how to rescue the godly out of trial."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Oiden** — knows how. It is a statement about competence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He knows exactly how to **hold the unjust under guard until the day of judgment**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tereien** — to keep, to guard, to reserve."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves are the same verb of knowing. He knows how to get one out and how to hold the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Genesis says about why Lot got out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it came to pass, when God destroyed the cities of the plain, that **God remembered Abraham, and sent Lot out** of the midst of the overthrow.* See the note on Genesis 19:27–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis credits Abraham's intercession. Peter credits Lot's own state."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are in the text and neither cancels the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Abraham had got to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Down to ten. Six requests, six grants, and he stopped. See the note on Genesis 18:27–33."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There were not ten. There was one, and one was enough for a rescue if not for a city."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Peter is making the argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter is about false teachers — *they promise them liberty, while they themselves are slaves of corruption*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point of the Lot paragraph is the first half of verse 9. If God got that man out of that city, the readers under pressure can be got out too."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The only evidence Peter offers.** Not Lot's decisions, which were poor. That living there hurt him, every day — and Peter builds the word *righteous* on it three times."}]}, "2 Peter 3:18": {"title": "Keep growing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last sentence Peter wrote."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep growing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **auxanete** — grow, increase. It is the ordinary word for a plant or a child."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present imperative: keep at it, continuously."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two directions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Deeper into **grace**, and deeper into **knowing Jesus Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *in the grace and knowledge of our Lord and Saviour Jesus Christ*, with one article covering both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter does not say grow in holiness, in discipline or in service. Two things, and both are about what is received."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The knowing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Gnosis** — and Peter has used the fuller form **epignosis** four times in the letter's first chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the theme of the whole letter: *grace and peace be multiplied to you **in the knowledge of God***. See the note on 2 Peter 1:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What \"instead\" answers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *beware lest you also **fall from your own steadfastness**, being led away with the error of the wicked.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter names the danger and then, instead of a defensive instruction, gives a growth one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The counter to being carried off is not to brace. It is to grow."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The doxology"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The glory is His, **now and on into the day that has no end**. Amen.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *both now and to the day of eternity* — a phrase that appears once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is unusual about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Doxologies in the New Testament are usually addressed to God the Father. This one is to Christ, and Peter closes his last letter with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Scoffers asking *where is the promise of His coming?*, the answer that *one day is with the Lord as a thousand years*, and: *the Lord is not slack concerning His promise… but is longsuffering toward us, **not willing that any should perish**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the day of the Lord coming as a thief, and the elements melting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the question: *since all these things will be dissolved, **what manner of persons ought you to be**?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about Paul two verses earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As also **our beloved brother Paul**, according to the wisdom given to him, has written to you… in which are **some things hard to understand**, which untaught and unstable people twist to their own destruction, **as they do also the rest of the Scriptures**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter calls Paul's letters Scripture, admits parts are difficult, and says people distort them. All in one sentence, and it is the only place in the New Testament where one apostle comments on another's writing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he was writing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Knowing that shortly **I must put off my tent**, just as our Lord Jesus Christ showed me. Moreover I will be careful to ensure that **you always have a reminder of these things after my decease**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is a man setting down what he wants left behind."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**His last instruction.** Not to hold the line. To keep growing — and the two things named are grace and knowing a person."}]}, "2 Peter 3:3–4": {"title": "\"Where is the promise of His coming?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter tells his readers what they will hear, and then gives the argument in the mockers' own words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prediction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the last days scoffers will come, scoffing and following their own evil desires.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not treat the objection as a surprise to be defended against. He treats its arrival as evidence — the mocking is itself one of the things that was said would happen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where is the promise of His coming? Ever since our ancestors died, everything goes on exactly as it has since the beginning of creation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a decent argument, and it is still made. Two parts:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the delay** — the people who first heard the promise have died", "**the uniformity** — nothing ever changes; the world runs on as it always has"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is disputing the facts. Peter's answer is that the second point is false as history."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How he answers it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He points at the flood. The world *did* change once, catastrophically, and the people who assumed continuity were wrong then too."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the two answers that follow: God's reckoning of time is not ours, and the delay is deliberate patience. See the note on 2 Peter 3:9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says they are doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They deliberately forget.* The Greek is about it escaping their notice on purpose — a chosen blindness rather than an oversight."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is Peter's diagnosis of the argument. Not that the scoffers lack information. That the information is available and being stepped around."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The scale he sets it in.** The chapter runs from creation, through the flood, to the elements melting and a new heavens and new earth. The mockers' claim that everything continues unchanged is answered by a history in which it has already not, and a future in which it will not again."}]}, "2 Peter 3:8": {"title": "\"A single day is the same size as a thousand years\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse about how time looks from the other side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The framing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Dear friends, **do not let this one thing slip past you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter flags it. He is about to say something he expects to be skated over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To the Lord, **a single day is the same size as a thousand years, and a thousand years is no longer than a day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both directions, and that is the point."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read only the first half and it says God is slow. Read both and it says the comparison does not apply — a day can hold a millennium and a millennium can pass like a day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not on the scale. That is the claim."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 90, Moses' psalm: *a thousand years in Your sight are like yesterday when it is past, and like a watch in the night.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oldest psalm in the book, written by a man leading a nation through a forty-year detour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter is answering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scoffers, three verses earlier: *\"**Where is the promise of His coming?** For since the fathers fell asleep, all things continue as they were from the beginning of creation.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their argument is from elapsed time. Nothing has happened, therefore nothing will."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's reply is that they are measuring with the wrong instrument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the reason for the delay"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The very next verse: *the Lord is not slack concerning His promise, as some count slackness, **but is longsuffering toward us, not willing that any should perish** but that all should come to repentance.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The waiting is not indifference or inability. It is named as patience with a purpose, and the purpose is people. See the note on 2 Peter 3:9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter has already used as evidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By the word of God the heavens were of old, and the earth standing out of water and in the water, by which the world that then existed **perished, being flooded with water**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scoffers say nothing ever changes. Peter points at the flood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is building to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The day of the Lord will come as a thief in the night… **but we, according to His promise, look for new heavens and a new earth in which righteousness dwells**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And his conclusion is practical: *what manner of persons ought you to be in holy conduct… looking for and hastening the coming of the day of God.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the verse is not for.** Peter is not offering a formula for dating anything — he opens the chapter saying the day comes like a thief, unannounced. The verse removes a calculation rather than supplying one."}]}, "2 Peter 3:9": {"title": "What looks like slowness", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter is answering scoffers who have noticed that the promised return has not happened, and he reframes the delay completely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The accusation he is answering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before it have people asking where the promise of His coming is, since everything carries on as it always has."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter does not argue about the timetable. He changes the category the delay belongs to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Lord is not dragging His feet over what He promised, whatever some people call it. He is being patient with us. He does not want anyone destroyed. He wants every last one to turn.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word behind *patient* is **makrothumeo** — long-tempered, slow to anger, the quality of someone who could act and does not yet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is being read as inability or indifference is a decision being held open."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He wants"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two statements, one negative and one positive, and both are unrestricted:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**not wanting** anyone to be destroyed", "**wanting** every last one to come to repentance"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter puts God's desire on record in the plainest possible terms, in a chapter otherwise full of fire and dissolving elements."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the delay is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the answer to the scoffers. The time that looks like absence is the time being given — and Peter says so again five verses on: *consider the patience of our Lord as salvation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The waiting is not neutral. It is rescue in progress."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse before it.** *With the Lord a day is like a thousand years, and a thousand years like a day.* Peter answers the timing question twice — once by saying God's reckoning of time is not ours, and once by saying the delay is deliberate mercy. He does not pick between them."}]}, "2 Samuel 1:17–27": {"title": "The Song of the Bow", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David writes a lament for the man who hunted him for years, and orders it taught to the nation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **ordered that the people of Judah be taught it** — the Song of the Bow, written down in the Book of Jashar.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a private grief. He has it learned by heart and archived."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Book of Jashar is cited once more in Scripture, for Joshua's long day, and has not survived. See the note on Joshua 10:12–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Oh, how the mighty have fallen!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times — at the start, in the middle, and as the last line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he will not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Don't tell it in Gath.** Don't spread the news in the streets of Ashkelon.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gath is Goliath's town, and the place David had lived as a fugitive when Saul drove him out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had every reason to want the news carried there. He asks for silence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mountains"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Mountains of Gilboa — may no dew fall on you**, no rain touch your slopes, no fields yield an offering.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He curses the ground for taking them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shield"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For there the shield of the mighty was thrown down, **the shield of Saul, no longer anointed with oil**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leather shields were rubbed with oil to keep them supple and turn a blade. An unoiled shield cracks, and stops being a shield."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever David means by it, he chose that image for Saul's end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about them both"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Saul and Jonathan — **beloved and beautiful in life, and in death they were not divided**. Swifter than eagles, stronger than lions.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul had thrown a spear at his own son over David. David's song puts them together and calls them undivided."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he credits Saul with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Daughters of Israel, weep for Saul**, who dressed you in scarlet and fine things, who put ornaments of gold on your clothing.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He finds something to praise, and it is Saul's provision for the women of Israel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is not one line of criticism in the whole lament. Not the spears, not the cave, not the years in the wilderness, not Nob."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Jonathan"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I grieve for you, Jonathan, my brother. **You were so dear to me. Your love to me was wonderful, deeper than the love of women.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The covenant between them is in 1 Samuel 18 — Jonathan gave David his robe, his armour, his sword, his bow and his belt, which is to say his claim on the throne."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Oh, how the mighty have fallen, and **the weapons of war are lost**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He ends on the weapons rather than the men."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the Amalekite fits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens with a man arriving at David's camp with Saul's crown, claiming to have finished him off, expecting a reward. See the note on 2 Samuel 1:5–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David tears his clothes, mourns until evening, has the man executed for laying a hand on the LORD's anointed — and then writes this."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he had said twice while Saul was alive.** *The LORD forbid that I should stretch out my hand against the LORD's anointed.* He had him in a cave and in a camp and did not touch him, and the song is consistent with the two occasions he had the chance."}]}, "2 Samuel 1:5–10": {"title": "The Amalekite who came to collect a reward", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A young man arrives at David's camp with Saul's crown in his hands and a story he has clearly rehearsed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he says he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*An Amalekite.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the detail the chapter is built on. Saul had been commanded to destroy the Amalekites and had spared what he chose to spare. Now, at the end of his life, it is an Amalekite standing over him — and an Amalekite who arrives to profit from his death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thing kept back came back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The story he tells"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says Saul was leaning on his spear, called him over, and asked to be finished off, and that he did it and took the crown and the armlet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The account does not match what the previous chapter recorded, where Saul fell on his own sword after his armour-bearer refused. Most readers conclude the Amalekite found the body and invented a role for himself, expecting a reward from the man Saul had hunted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he misjudged"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He read David the way the world reads: an enemy is dead, the throne is open, and the man who brings news of it will be paid."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David's response is to tear his clothes, mourn until evening, and then execute him for laying a hand on the LORD's anointed. Then he writes a lament for the man who had spent years trying to kill him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The pattern the chapter draws.** What Saul was told to deal with and did not, stood over him at the end and took the crown off his head. The narrative makes no comment. It simply gives the man's nationality in the fifth verse and lets the reader remember chapter 15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Amalek was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy describes the nation by its method: it came at Israel's back and cut down the stragglers *when you were weary and worn out*. It hunts what is already tired."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul was told to blot it out. He kept the king and the best of the livestock — what looked useful — and destroyed the rest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is spared because it still seems worth something does not stay where it was put. It stands over you at the end and takes the crown off your head, and four hundred years later it signs a decree in Persia with a king's ring on its hand. See the notes on Deuteronomy 25:17–19 and Esther 3:1–4."}]}, "2 Samuel 11:1": {"title": "\"But David stayed behind\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse of scene-setting, and everything that follows is in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The season"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When spring came around, **the season when kings ride out to war**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *at the return of the year, at the time when kings go out*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Campaigns were fought in spring — after the rains, when the roads were passable and there was fodder for the animals. It was the calendar, and everybody knew it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*David **sent Joab** out with his officers and the whole army of Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He sends. The army goes, the siege is laid, the war is prosecuted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The machinery works without him, which is part of the problem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **David stayed behind in Jerusalem**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six words in the Hebrew, and the writer puts them at the end of the verse where they cannot be missed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about it is illegal. A king delegating a siege is not a crime, and David had fought enough wars."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the writer is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives the season first, then the fact that everyone else went, then the exception."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole verse exists to place David in the wrong location at the right time of year, and then the next verse opens: *and it happened one evening that David arose from his bed and walked on the roof.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chapter it introduces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bathsheba, Uriah, the letter carried by the man it condemned, and a child who dies."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nathan's parable comes in the next chapter, and God's own summary of the charge is about Uriah rather than Bathsheba: *you have killed Uriah the Hittite with the sword… now therefore the sword shall never depart from your house.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other man in the story"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Uriah, offered leave and a night at home, sleeps at the palace door instead:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The ark and Israel and Judah are dwelling in tents… **shall I then go to my house to eat and drink and lie with my wife?** As you live, and as your soul lives, I will not do this thing.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The foreign soldier will not go home while the army is in the field. The king of Israel had not gone out with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sons of Issachar had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Men who **understood the times and knew what Israel should do**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two hundred of them came to Hebron to make David king. See the note on 1 Chronicles 12:23–37."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any judgement. The writer records where the army was and where the king was, and leaves it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How much rests on one clause.** The rest of David's reign runs downhill from this chapter — Amnon, Tamar, Absalom, the flight from Jerusalem — and the narrative traces all of it back to a spring he did not go out."}]}, "2 Samuel 15:19–23": {"title": "Ittai the Gittite", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David is leaving Jerusalem barefoot, and a foreigner refuses to be sent home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is happening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Absalom's revolt. The king walking out of his own capital with the household, up the Mount of Olives, weeping, head covered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And a messenger came to David, saying, \"**The hearts of the men of Israel are with Absalom**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everybody is leaving. The question in the chapter is who is going with him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Ittai is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ittai **the Gittite**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man of Gath. A Philistine, from Goliath's city."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You're **a foreigner, an exile far from your own home**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has six hundred men with him, and the number is the same as David's own band in the wilderness years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever brought him to Jerusalem, he is a professional soldier in a foreign king's service."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How long he has been there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You only came yesterday**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *yesterday you came*, and it need not be literal — it means recently."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David offers him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go back and stay with the new king**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls Absalom *the king*, plainly, without bitterness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Should I **drag you all over the country with me today, when I don't even know where I'm going**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most honest sentence in the passage. He does not know where he is going and says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go back, and take your countrymen with you. **May kindness and faithfulness go with you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chesed ve'emet** — steadfast love and truth. The covenant pair."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He releases him with the best words in the language."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**As surely as the LORD lives, and as my lord the king lives — wherever my lord the king may be, whether it means life or death, there your servant will be**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Philistine swearing by the LORD."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence has one condition in it and it is not a condition: *wherever*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it echoes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Entreat me not to leave you, or to turn back from following after you; for **wherever you go, I will go**… your people shall be my people, and your God, my God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ruth, to Naomi. A Moabite, on a road, refusing to be sent home."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two foreigners, two roads out of a country, and nearly the same words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**March on, then**,\" David said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in Hebrew: **lekh va'avor** — go and pass over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crossing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Ittai the Gittite marched on, **with all his men and all the families who were with him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Families. He is not just committing soldiers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The whole countryside **wept aloud** as the people passed by. **The king crossed the Kidron Valley**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens at that valley"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Jesus, when He had said these things, **went out with His disciples over the Brook Kidron**, where there was a garden.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A thousand years later, a king leaving the city at night, over the same brook, with his own people about to scatter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What became of Ittai"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three chapters on, David divides the army for the battle in the wood of Ephraim:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A third under the hand of Joab, a third under Abishai, and **a third under the hand of Ittai the Gittite**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who had been in the country a matter of weeks is given a third of the army."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he was offered and refused.** A release, with the best blessing in the language attached — from a king who admitted he did not know where he was going."}]}, "2 Samuel 19:31–40": {"title": "Barzillai turns it down", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An old man who had kept a king alive is offered a place at his table, and lists reasons why not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was he who had **kept the king supplied** while David stayed at Mahanaim.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David was in flight from Absalom, with an army and no supply line. Chapter 17 gives the inventory Barzillai brought out: beds, basins, wheat, barley, flour, parched grain, beans, lentils, honey, curds, sheep and cheese."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For they said, \"The people are hungry and weary and thirsty in the wilderness.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He backed the true king when the throne was in doubt and it was dangerous to do so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The invitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Cross over with me. Come to Jerusalem, and I will care for you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Open-ended, from a restored king, to the man who had fed him. There is no condition on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"How many years do I have left…? **I am eighty now.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then a run of questions, all in the negative:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*\"Can I still **tell good from bad**?\"*", "*\"Can I **taste** what I eat and drink?\"*", "*\"Can I still **hear** the voices of singers, men and women?\"*", "*\"Why should your servant be **a burden** to my lord the king?\"*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judgement, taste, hearing — he inventories what he has lost, and concludes from the list that the invitation is not for him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he wants to go instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Let me go back home, and let me die in my own town, near the graves of my father and mother.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks to go back and be buried where his parents are. That is his stated ambition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he sends instead of himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But here is **your servant Kimham** — let him cross over with my lord the king.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Most readers take Kimham for his son. The place at the table gets filled; Barzillai is not in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He crosses *a little way over the Jordan*, takes the king's kiss and blessing, and goes home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Set beside Caleb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Caleb, at eighty-five, is five years older than Barzillai here, and this is what he says:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am as strong this day as on the day Moses sent me; just as my strength was then, so now is my strength for war… **give me this mountain**\" — the one with the giants and the fortified cities on it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two old men, five years apart. One counts what he can no longer taste; the other asks for the hill country. See the note on Joshua 14:6–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not argue. *The king kissed Barzillai and blessed him, and Barzillai went home.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is taken away from him, and nothing is forced on him. The offer stood and he declined it, and the record is kind to him throughout — David remembers him on his deathbed and tells Solomon to show kindness to his sons."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he does not say.** He never doubts David or the kingdom. His reasons are all about himself — his age, his senses, his usefulness. The invitation was not conditional on any of them."}]}, "2 Samuel 5:6–10": {"title": "Up through the water shaft", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David takes a city nobody had taken, and the account is mostly about an insult."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he has got to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven and a half years as king in Hebron over Judah alone, and now the northern tribes have come: *we are your bone and your flesh*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty-seven and a half years old, anointed as a boy, and finally king over all Israel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing he does is march on Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who held it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **Jebusites** who lived there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Canaanite enclave that had survived the entire conquest. Joshua killed its king; Judah burned the lower city; and the Jebusites were still in the citadel four hundred years later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The children of Benjamin did not drive out the Jebusites who inhabited Jerusalem; but the Jebusites dwell with the children of Benjamin to this day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It sat on the border between Judah and Benjamin, which is part of why nobody had dealt with it — and part of why it made a good capital for a united kingdom. It belonged to neither side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The taunt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You'll never get in here. Even the blind and the lame could turn you back**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is difficult, and this is the usual reading: the defence is so strong that disabled men on the wall would be enough."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They thought the position could not be stormed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The position"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zion is a narrow spur with valleys on three sides. The only water was the Gihon spring, outside the wall at the bottom of the eastern slope."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The way in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whoever wants to strike the Jebusites, **let him go up through the water shaft**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tsinnor** — a word that occurs twice in the Bible and probably means a watercourse or conduit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Warren's Shaft, discovered in the nineteenth century, is a vertical channel connecting the spring to a tunnel inside the walls. Whether that is the one is argued about."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The city's one weakness is the thing it could not do without."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chronicles adds who went: *whoever attacks the Jebusites first shall be chief and captain. And **Joab the son of Zeruiah went up first**, and became chief.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The taking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But David **captured the fortress of Zion — the City of David**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The building"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So David settled into the fortress and called it the City of David, and **he built up the area around it, from the terraces inward**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **millo** — the filling. An earthwork or terrace system, and Solomon works on it later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **David grew greater and greater, for the LORD God of hosts was with him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *going and growing great*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same construction used of Samuel as a boy, and of the trumpet at Sinai getting louder."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason is attached: *for the LORD God of hosts was with him*. The narrative supplies it rather than leaving it to be inferred."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hiram of Tyre sends cedar and craftsmen and builds him a house. Two Philistine campaigns, both won after asking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the ark. See the note on 2 Samuel 6:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has a capital and a palace, and the next thing he wants is the box."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Zion becomes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the LORD has chosen Zion. He has desired it for His home.* See the note on Psalms 132:13–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Jebusite hill fort, taken up a water shaft, and the psalms spend the rest of the Old Testament calling it the city of God."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The way in.** The one thing the fortress could not seal — its water. A city that could not be stormed was entered through the channel that kept it alive."}]}, "2 Samuel 6:1–5": {"title": "A brand-new cart", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Everything about the first attempt is generous, expensive and sincere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The men"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*David gathered the best of Israel again — **thirty thousand handpicked men**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **bachur** — chosen, picked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An enormous escort. Nothing about this is done quietly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The destination"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They set out for **Baale-judah** to bring back the ark of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Another name for Kirjath Jearim, where the ark had been in Abinadab's house for a generation. See the note on 1 Samuel 7:2–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ark that **carries the Name — the Name of the LORD of Heaven's Armies, who sits enthroned above the winged cherubim**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the ark of God, which is called by the Name, the name of the LORD of hosts who dwells between the cherubim*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fullest title the ark is given anywhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The transport"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They loaded the ark of God onto **a brand-new cart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **agalah chadashah** — a new cart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "New, which is the point. Nothing second-hand for this. It is an act of respect."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is the Philistine method. When they sent the ark back they built a new cart because they had no instructions and were guessing. See the note on 1 Samuel 6:10–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel had the instructions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the instructions said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Poles, permanently in the rings, carried on the shoulders of the sons of Kohath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But they shall not touch any holy thing, lest they die.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Chronicler records David saying so afterwards: *because you did not do it the first time, the LORD our God broke out against us, **for we did not seek Him about the proper way**.* See the note on 1 Chronicles 15:11–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The drivers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With Abinadab's sons **Uzzah and Ahio** guiding the way. **Ahio walked out in front** of the ark.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two brothers from the household where it had been kept. One walks in front and one beside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had grown up with it in the house. Twenty years and more of walking past it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The celebration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And David and all Israel **let joy pour out of them before the LORD** — singing with everything they had, with **harps and lyres, with tambourines and rattles and clashing cymbals**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *with all their might*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five instruments listed, and the same phrase — *with all his might* — that describes David dancing on the second attempt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is right about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The motive. The scale. The expense. The music. The intention to put the ark at the centre of the capital, which nobody had done in a hundred years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is wrong with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One thing, and it is the method."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens at the threshing floor"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oxen stumble, Uzzah reaches out, and the celebration stops. See the note on 2 Samuel 6:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three months of not moving, and then the whole thing again — consecrated Levites, poles, shoulders, sacrifices, and a linen ephod. See the note on 2 Samuel 6:12–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The paragraph before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So David went and brought up the ark of God from the house of Obed-Edom to the City of David **with gladness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second procession is described with the same word as the first."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the joy needed correcting."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was new.** The cart. Israel spent money on the one part of the arrangement that had been specified for centuries, and copied it from people who had been guessing."}]}, "2 Samuel 6:12–15": {"title": "Six steps, and then he danced", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second attempt, and everything about it is different except the destination."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What started it again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then word reached David: \"**The LORD has blessed Obed-edom's whole household**… because of the ark of God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three months of a household visibly prospering, and the news travels. See the note on 2 Samuel 6:9–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The report is what gets David moving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The difference"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So David went down and brought the ark of God up from Obed-edom's house **into the city of David — this time with rejoicing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Chronicler supplies what the phrase covers:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No one may carry the ark of God but the Levites**… because you did not do it the first time, the LORD our God broke out against us, **because we did not consult Him about the proper order**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the children of the Levites **bore the ark of God on their shoulders, by its poles**, as Moses had commanded according to the word of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Shoulders and poles. No cart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The six steps"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And whenever those carrying the ark had gone **six steps**, David stopped and **sacrificed an ox and a fattened calf**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six steps."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read strictly, that is a sacrifice every few yards for six miles, which is not what happened — the Hebrew most likely means when they had gone six steps at the start, David sacrificed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way, the first act after the ark moves is blood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last time it moved, the first thing that happened was a death. This time the death happens first and it is an animal's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dancing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And David **danced before the LORD with all his might**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mekarker bekol-oz** — whirling with all strength."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A king of Israel, dancing in the street."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was wearing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A simple **linen ephod**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not royal dress. A linen ephod is priests' clothing — a plain garment, and the Chronicler adds that he also wore *a robe of fine linen*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes off what marks him as king and puts on what marks a servant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The noise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So David and all Israel brought up the ark of the LORD with **shouts of joy and the blast of the ram's horn**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The window"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now as the ark of the LORD came into the City of David, **Michal, Saul's daughter, looked through a window and saw King David leaping and whirling before the LORD; and she despised him in her heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And afterwards: *\"How glorious was the king of Israel today, uncovering himself today in the eyes of the maids of his servants, as one of the base fellows shamelessly uncovers himself!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David's answer: *\"It was before the LORD… **I will be even more undignified than this**, and will be humble in my own sight.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the chapter's last line: *therefore Michal the daughter of Saul had no children to the day of her death*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the ark ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In its place, in the midst of the tabernacle that David had erected for it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the tabernacle of Moses, which was at Gibeon with the altar and the priests. A tent David pitched in Jerusalem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For roughly forty years the ark sat in a tent in the capital with no veil in front of it, and singers around it day and night, while the sacrifices continued six miles away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That arrangement is what Amos calls *the tabernacle of David that is fallen*, and what James quotes at the Jerusalem council. See the note on Acts 15:13–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David does at the end of the day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he distributed among all the people, the whole multitude of Israel, both the women and the men, to everyone a loaf of bread, a piece of meat, and a cake of raisins.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everybody goes home with food."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the second attempt cost.** Three months of not moving, and then blood before the first six steps — and a king who took off the crown and danced in a linen ephod."}]}, "2 Samuel 6:6–8": {"title": "The breaking-out against Uzzah", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The hardest paragraph in the David narrative, and the trouble started with a cart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bringing the ark to Jerusalem. Thirty thousand chosen men, music, and *David and all the house of Israel played music before the LORD on all kinds of instruments*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a celebration, and the intention is right."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was wrong with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They set the ark of God on **a new cart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "New, expensive, and not the instruction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ark was to be carried on poles by Levites, on their shoulders. Exodus specifies it and Numbers repeats it: *they shall not touch any holy thing, lest they die*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cart was the Philistines' method. When they sent it back they had no other option — two milk cows and a new cart, and it worked, because they had not been told. See the note on 1 Samuel 6:10–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel copies the Philistines."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stumble"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When they came to **Nacon's threshing floor**, the oxen stumbled, and **the ark tipped**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A threshing floor is a flat exposed rock, usually swept. The wheels catch or the ground changes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reach"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Uzzah **reached out his hand and took hold of it to steady it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he put out his hand to the ark of God and grasped it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instinct is decent. Something holy is about to hit the dirt and he catches it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the anger of the LORD burned against Uzzah for what he had done, and **God struck him down there beside the ark**, and he died **there in the presence of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Chronicler adds the reason: *because he put his hand to the ark*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is hard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because the man was trying to help, and because the cart was somebody else's decision. Uzzah was one of the household where the ark had been kept for twenty years; he had grown up with it in the house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Familiarity is the thing the text puts its finger on. A man who has walked past it every day for two decades reaches out without thinking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "David's reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*David's heart broke open. **He was angry that the LORD had burst out against Uzzah**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both. Grief and anger, and the Hebrew records the anger without softening it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And to this day that place is called **Perez-uzzah** — \"the breaking-out against Uzzah.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **perets** — a breach, a bursting through."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word David had used three chapters earlier, and joyfully: *the LORD has broken through my enemies before me, like a breakthrough of water*. He named that place **Baal-perazim**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same noun. One is a victory and one is a death, and David names both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stops. See the note on 2 Samuel 6:9–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three months later he comes back and does it correctly, and the Chronicler records what he had worked out in the meantime:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because you did not do it the first time, the LORD our God broke out against us, **because we did not consult Him about the proper order**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the children of the Levites **bore the ark of God on their shoulders, by its poles**, as Moses had commanded.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the cart was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A shortcut. Faster, easier, and borrowed from people who did not have the instructions."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the celebration was insincere. The problem was the method, and the passage does not pretend otherwise."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two names have in common.** *Perets* — a bursting through. David used it for a victory in chapter 5 and for a death in chapter 6, and both places kept the name."}]}, "2 Samuel 6:9–11": {"title": "Three months at Obed-edom's", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The procession stops, and the ark spends three months in somebody's house."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fear"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And that day **fear settled over David**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *David was afraid of the LORD that day*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A verse earlier he was angry. Now he is frightened. Both are recorded and neither is corrected."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How can I ever bring the ark of the LORD home to me?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *how shall the ark of the LORD come to me?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A real question, and it is the right one. He does not yet know the answer, and three months later he will."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The decision"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So David **would not move the ark into his own city**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He stops the procession where it is, six miles short of Jerusalem, with thirty thousand men standing in a field."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diversion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He turned it aside instead to the house of **Obed-edom the Gittite**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Gittite** — a man of Gath. Goliath's city."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Chronicler lists him among the Levites who were gatekeepers and musicians, so he is probably a Levite who had lived in Gath, or the name is used of his origin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way, the ark is parked in the house of a man associated with the Philistines, which is where it had spent seven months a generation earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What nobody expects"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There the ark of the LORD stayed for **three months** — and **the LORD blessed Obed-edom and everyone under his roof**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *and all his household*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Uzzah touched it and died. Obed-edom houses it and is blessed, with everyone in the building."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same object."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that establishes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ark was never the problem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrative has just recorded a death and now records a household prospering for three months, and the only difference is the handling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Chronicler is explicit about the lesson David drew: *because we did not consult Him about the proper order*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the neighbours noticed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then word reached David: \"**The LORD has blessed Obed-edom's whole household, and everything he has, because of the ark of God**.\"* See the note on 2 Samuel 6:12–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blessing is public enough to be reported to the king, and it is what makes him try again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened last time it sat somewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty years at Kirjath Jearim, in the house of Abinadab, after the Philistines sent it back. See the note on 1 Samuel 7:2–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody had come for it. Saul reigned forty years and never sent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David's first act after taking Jerusalem is to go and get it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Obed-edom got afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Chronicler keeps track of him. He is listed among those appointed to minister before the ark in Jerusalem — *Obed-edom with his brethren, sixty-eight* — and later as a gatekeeper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For God blessed him*, the record notes, and gives him eight sons."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not go back to private life. Three months with it in the house, and he spends the rest of his life near it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David does in the interval"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Works out the instructions. Prepares a tent. Appoints the Levites."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"No one may carry the ark of God but the Levites, for the LORD has chosen them to carry the ark of God and to minister before Him forever.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three months of not moving, and then it is done properly."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the three months prove.** The ark had killed a man six miles back and blessed a household here. The object did not change; the handling did — and David spent the interval finding out how."}]}, "2 Samuel 9:5–8": {"title": "Mephibosheth at the king's table", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A king sends for the last surviving heir of the family he replaced, and the man arrives certain he is going to die."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jonathan's son, Saul's grandson. Crippled in both feet since he was five, when his nurse dropped him fleeing the news that his father and grandfather had been killed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the political logic of that world he was a dead man — a rival claimant living on borrowed time. That is why he had been living in **Lo Debar**, a name that means roughly *no pasture*, or *nothing there*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who is your servant, that you would even notice a dead dog like me?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is trembling, face to the ground. He does not ask for anything. He does not argue that he is harmless or that he deserves better."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls himself a dead dog."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David gives him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things, and none of them are requested:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**don't be afraid**", "**every piece of land that belonged to your grandfather Saul**", "**you yourself will always eat at my table**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land is restored, and the seat is permanent. The chapter ends by saying he ate at the king's table like one of the king's sons."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am going to show you kindness **for the sake of your father Jonathan**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason has nothing to do with Mephibosheth. It is a covenant made years earlier between David and Jonathan — see 1 Samuel 18:1–4 — and the kindness runs to him on the strength of somebody else's name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not being rewarded. He is being kept."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last verse of the chapter.** *And he was lame in both feet.* Second Samuel mentions it three times, and the final time is the very last line — after the land, the table, and the sons. The condition never changed. His place at the table was not conditional on it."}]}, "2 Thessalonians 2:1–2": {"title": "Do not be shaken loose", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul writes a second letter because somebody had forged the first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The subject"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now about **the coming of our Lord Jesus Christ**, and about **our being gathered together to Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things named, and Paul treats them as one topic."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *coming* is **parousia** — the arrival, a royal visit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **episunagoges** — a gathering together, the noun behind *synagogue* with an intensifier. It occurs twice in the New Testament; the other is Hebrews' *not forsaking the assembling of ourselves together*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had already written"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first letter, one chapter earlier in the canon: *the Lord Himself will descend from heaven with a shout… and we who are alive and remain shall be caught up together with them in the clouds*. See the note on 1 Thessalonians 4:16–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the gathering he means. He is not introducing a new subject; he is correcting the handling of an old one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are asking you, friends, **not to let yourselves be shaken loose from your senses or thrown into alarm**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **saleuthenai** — to be shaken, as a ship torn from its moorings or a building in an earthquake."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **throeisthai** — to be alarmed, startled. The same word Jesus uses in the Olivet discourse: *see that you are not troubled*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three channels"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not by any **spirit**, not by anything **said**, not by any **letter dressed up to look as though it came from us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Prophecy, preaching, and correspondence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third is the alarming one. Somebody had written to the Thessalonians in Paul's name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why he signs off this letter the way he does: *the salutation of Paul with my own hand, which is a sign in every epistle; **so I write***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had to establish a handwriting sample."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim being spread"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Claiming that **the day of Christ has already arrived**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **enesteken** — a perfect: has come and is present."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that it is near. That it has happened, and they missed it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why that was frightening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were being persecuted. The first chapter of this letter is about it: *your patience and faith in all your persecutions and tribulations that you endure*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "If the day had come and this was it, then what they were suffering was the tribulation and the gathering had not included them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's whole answer is aimed at that fear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not let anyone fool you, whatever method is used. **That day does not come until the falling away comes first, and the man of sin is uncovered**.* See the note on 2 Thessalonians 2:3–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not argue from feelings. He gives them a checkable sequence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pastoral shape of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The correction is not *stop speculating*. It is: here is what has to happen first, and it has not happened, so you can stop being frightened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had said in the first letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But concerning the times and the seasons, brethren, you have no need that I should write to you. For you yourselves know perfectly that the day of the Lord so comes as a thief in the night.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *God did not appoint us to wrath, but to obtain salvation through our Lord Jesus Christ*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he closes this section with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, brethren, **stand fast and hold the traditions which you were taught**, whether by word or our epistle.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *now may our Lord Jesus Christ Himself, and our God and Father, who has loved us and given us everlasting consolation and good hope by grace, **comfort your hearts and establish you** in every good word and work.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sterixai** — make firm. The opposite of **saleuthenai**, shaken loose."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he is answering.** Not curiosity. A forged letter telling frightened people the day had already come and they had been left in it."}]}, "2 Thessalonians 2:13–14": {"title": "Chosen from the beginning", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "After two chapters about the man of lawlessness, Paul turns and thanks God for the people reading it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **we owe God thanks for you always**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *we are bound to give thanks*. He uses the same construction at the start of the letter — an obligation rather than an inclination."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes just before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most alarming passage in either Thessalonian letter: the falling away, the man of sin sitting in the temple of God, the lawless one whom the Lord will consume with the breath of His mouth, and *strong delusion* sent on those who did not receive the love of the truth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then, without transition: **but we owe God thanks for you**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Brothers and sisters **loved by the Lord**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a perfect passive participle — *having been loved*, and standing loved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The choosing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **God chose you from the beginning to be saved**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **heileto** — took for Himself, selected."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**From the beginning** — some manuscripts read *as firstfruits*; either way the point is that the choosing predates them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **Spirit setting you apart** for Himself, and **you believing the truth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sanctification by the Spirit, and belief of the truth. Paul puts God's action and the person's response in one clause without ranking them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The calling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That is what **He called you to**, and **He called you through the good news we brought**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The eternal choice and the specific sermon are the same event described twice. Paul does not treat the preaching as incidental to the choosing — it is the means."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *good news* is **euangelion**, and *we brought* makes it concrete: Paul, Silas and Timothy, in a synagogue in Thessalonica, for three sabbaths, before a mob drove them out of town."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that you would come to **possess the glory of our Lord Jesus Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *for the obtaining of the glory*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to see it. To obtain it. Paul writes the same thing to Rome: *whom He justified, these He also glorified* — in the past tense."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Thessalonians were like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had been with them a matter of weeks. The church was young, poor, under pressure from its own countrymen, and confused enough about the return of Christ that both letters have to address it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them they were chosen from the beginning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, brethren, **stand fast and hold the traditions which you were taught**, whether by word or our epistle.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then a prayer: *now may our Lord Jesus Christ Himself, and our God and Father, who has loved us and given us everlasting consolation and good hope by grace, **comfort your hearts and establish you in every good word and work**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where Paul puts the paragraph.** Directly after the darkest passage he ever wrote about the future. The answer he gives to it is not information — it is a list of what is already true of the people who were frightened."}]}, "2 Thessalonians 2:3–4": {"title": "Two things first", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul answers a panic with a sequence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not let anyone fool you, **whatever method is used**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kata medena tropon** — by no means, in no manner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just listed three: a spirit, a word, a forged letter. See the note on 2 Thessalonians 2:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The structure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That day does not come **until** the falling away comes **first**, and the man of sin is uncovered.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two conditions, and a *first*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's answer to *has the day already come?* is: check whether these two have happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **falling away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **he apostasia** — with the definite article. A specific one, not apostasy in general."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word means a defection or revolt. In the Greek Old Testament it is used of political rebellion and of turning from God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the man of sin is uncovered** — **the son of destruction**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apokaluphthe** — revealed, unveiled. Paul uses the word three times in this passage, always of this figure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which implies he exists before he is revealed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Son of destruction** — the same phrase Jesus uses of Judas in the high-priestly prayer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four descriptions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The one who **sets himself against everything called God or given worship**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ho antikeimenos** — the one lying against, the opposer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **lifts himself over all of it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Huperairomenos** — raising himself above."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So far that he **takes his seat in God's own temple**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kathisai** — to sit down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **puts himself on display as God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Apodeiknunta heauton** — exhibiting himself, demonstrating himself to be."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the picture comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel: *the king shall do according to his own will; he shall exalt and magnify himself above every god, shall speak blasphemies against the God of gods*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the abomination of desolation, which Jesus names in the Olivet discourse: *when you see the abomination of desolation, spoken of by Daniel the prophet, standing in the holy place*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the king of Babylon in Isaiah 14: *I will ascend into heaven, I will exalt my throne above the stars of God… I will be like the Most High*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pattern in every case is self-exaltation ending in a seat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John calls him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even now **many antichrists** have come… he is antichrist who denies the Father and the Son.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John uses the word in the plural and the singular, and says the many are evidence the one is coming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The restraint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And now you know **what is restraining**, that he may be revealed in his own time… only He who now restrains will do so until He is taken out of the way.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **to katechon** — the thing holding down. Neuter in one verse and masculine in the next."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says *you know* — they had been told in person. Nobody since has been sure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The end of him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And then the lawless one will be revealed, whom the Lord will **consume with the breath of His mouth** and destroy with the brightness of His coming.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anelei to pneumati tou stomatos autou**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 11: *He shall strike the earth with the rod of His mouth, and with the breath of His lips He shall slay the wicked*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But we are bound to give thanks to God always for you, brethren beloved by the Lord, because **God from the beginning chose you for salvation**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage about the man of sin is followed, without a break, by a thanksgiving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul answers a frightened church with a timetable and then tells them they were chosen."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the answer is.** Not reassurance in general. Two named events, neither of which has happened — so the day they were told they had missed has not come."}]}, "2 Thessalonians 3:10–12": {"title": "\"Unwilling\" is the whole word", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This verse gets quoted at people in hard circumstances more often than almost any other, and usually with one word quietly dropped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word that is always dropped"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Anyone who is **unwilling** to work should not eat.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek does not say *does not work*. It says *does not want to*. Paul is describing a decision, not a situation. Everyone who cannot find work, cannot work, or has run out of options is outside the sentence entirely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he was actually writing to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next line names them: people in that church who were *drifting, doing no work at all, and yet somehow finding the hours to busy themselves in everyone else's affairs.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The likeliest cause is in the letter itself. Some of them had concluded the Lord's return was imminent and had downed tools to wait. Paul is not scolding the poor. He is dealing with a specific enthusiasm that had turned into idleness — and, as he notes drily, into meddling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he tells them to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Settle down, do your own work without all the noise, and eat food you have earned.* Then, immediately after, he tells the rest of the church not to grow weary in doing good."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both instructions in the same breath: work quietly, and keep being generous."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How Paul argues it.** He appeals to his own habits first — he paid his way in Thessalonica rather than take support, though he says outright he had the right to. He is asking of them something he had already done in front of them."}]}, "2 Thessalonians 3:3–5": {"title": "\"But the Lord is faithful\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just asked for prayer because *not all have faith*, and then turns the word around."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But the Lord is faithful.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *pray… that we may be delivered from unreasonable and wicked men, **for not all have faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul names a shortage of faith in people and answers it with the faithfulness of the Lord. Same root, opposite subject."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does the same in 2 Timothy: *if we are faithless, **He remains faithful**; He cannot deny Himself.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things He does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He is the one who will **make you steady on your feet**, and He is the one who will **stand guard over you and keep you clear of evil**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for the first is **sterizo** — to establish, to set firmly, to prop up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second is to guard, the word for a garrison keeping watch."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both verbs are His and neither is asked of the readers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul is confident about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And where you are concerned, **we are confident in the Lord**: you are doing what we told you, and you will go on doing it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note where the confidence is placed. Not *we are confident in you* — **in the Lord**, about you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's expectation of their perseverance rests on somebody other than them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"May the Lord **steer your hearts straight into the love of God**, and straight into **the patience that waits for Christ**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is to make straight, to direct — the same verb used of guiding a ship."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two destinations: God's love, and patience."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The patience that waits for Christ\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the patience of Christ*, and it has been read both ways — the endurance Christ Himself had, and the endurance that waits for Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either reading is at home in this letter. The Thessalonians had got themselves in a state about the Lord's return; some had stopped working over it, which is what the rest of the chapter is about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter is dealing with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Persecution, and confusion about the second coming, and people who had quit their jobs to wait for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's answer to the last of those is the plainest sentence in the letter: *if anyone will not work, neither shall he eat.* See the note on 2 Thessalonians 3:10–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He writes about being guarded and steadied, and then tells them to go back to work."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How Paul signs it.** *The salutation of Paul with my own hand, which is a sign in every epistle; so I write.* Somebody had been sending the Thessalonians forged letters about the day of the Lord, and Paul started signing his own."}]}, "2 Timothy 1:1–2": {"title": "A son I love dearly", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last letter Paul wrote opens with a promise of life, from a man about to lose his."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Rome, in a second imprisonment, chained — *for which I suffer trouble as an evildoer, even to the point of chains*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four chapters later: *the time of my departure is at hand*. See the note on 2 Timothy 4:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ **because God willed it so**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dia thelematos Theou** — through the will of God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first letter said *under orders*. This one says *because God willed it*, which is the same claim made more quietly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause nobody expects here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **everything about it lines up with the promise of life that is ours in Christ Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kat' epangelian zoes** — according to the promise of life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is writing under a death sentence, and defines his apostleship by a promise of life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The greeting itself is the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To Timothy, **a son I love dearly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **agapeto tekno** — beloved child."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first letter said **gnesio tekno**, legitimate child — a legal word about standing. See the note on 1 Timothy 1:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the last letter the legal word has become an affectionate one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The greeting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Grace and mercy and peace be yours from God the Father and from Christ Jesus our Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words again, with **mercy** in the middle — the same insertion he makes for Timothy and for nobody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I thank God… as **without ceasing I remember you in my prayers night and day**, greatly desiring to see you, **being mindful of your tears**.* See the note on 2 Timothy 1:3–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He remembers the young man crying, probably at their last parting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the grandmother and the mother, named."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Stir up the gift of God which is in you through the laying on of my hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anazopurein** — to fan into flame again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For God has not given us a spirit of fear, but of power and of love and of a sound mind.* See the note on 2 Timothy 1:6–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bring the cloak that I left with Carpus at Troas — and **the books, especially the parchments**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do your utmost to come before winter.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter that opens with the promise of life closes with a request for a coat and a set of notebooks, and a man hoping his son gets there before the shipping stops."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole letter is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be strong in the grace that is in Christ Jesus. And the things that you have heard from me… commit these to faithful men.* See the note on 2 Timothy 2:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Handing it on, from a cell, to a son."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clause in the greeting.** *According to the promise of life* — written by a man who knows the sentence is coming and opens his last letter with life anyway."}]}, "2 Timothy 1:6–7": {"title": "Fan the gift into flame", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's last surviving letter, written from a Roman prison to a younger man who appears to be losing his nerve."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is already there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God **placed a gift inside you** the day I laid my hands on you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past tense, and settled. Nothing in the passage asks Timothy to obtain anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole instruction assumes the gift is present and has been present for years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Fan it back up into open flame\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anazopureo** — *ana* (again) + *zoos* (alive) + *pur* (fire). To rekindle; to stir a fire back up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You do not fan an ashtray. The verb takes for granted that there are coals in there, banked and covered, still hot."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Timothy is not told to light something. He is told to stir something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Was never a small, cowering thing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What God breathed into us was never a small, cowering thing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek noun is **deilia** — cowardice, timidity. It appears nowhere else in the New Testament, and it is not a neutral word; the Greeks used it as the opposite of courage, a fault rather than a temperament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say Timothy is not afraid. He says where the fear did not come from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Three things it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gave us **power**, and He gave us **love**, and He gave us **a mind that stays clear and steady**.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**power** — *dunamis*, the plain word for ability and force", "**love** — *agape*", "**a clear and steady mind** — **sophronismos**, a word about soundness of mind, self-command, a head that is in one piece"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third is the one that gets flattened in translation. It is built from the words for *safe* and *mind*: a mind that is intact."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul lists it as something given, alongside power and love — not as a disposition Timothy has to manufacture under pressure."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul asks for at the end of the letter.** His cloak, the books, and especially the parchments. A man writing about power, love and a sound mind from a cell, and asking for a coat because winter is coming."}]}, "2 Timothy 2:1–2": {"title": "Let the grace be the thing that makes you strong", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An instruction to be strong, and it names what does the strengthening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So then, my son, **let the grace that is in Christ Jesus be the thing that makes you strong**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **endunamou** — be strengthened, and it is passive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *strengthen yourself*. Be made strong, by something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the strength comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The grace that is in Christ Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**En te chariti** — in the grace. The strengthening happens inside it, not by effort applied to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same passive of himself: *I thank Christ Jesus our Lord who has enabled me* — **endunamosanti me** — and *I can do all things through Christ who strengthens me*. See the note on Philippians 4:11–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Timothy needs it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is young, in charge of a difficult church, and Paul is in prison and not coming back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not be ashamed of the testimony of our Lord, nor of me His prisoner.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The previous chapter has just named two men who deserted — *all those in Asia have turned away from me* — and one who did not: *Onesiphorus often refreshed me, and was not ashamed of my chain*. See the note on 2 Timothy 1:15–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And everything you heard me say **with plenty of witnesses standing there**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dia pollon marturon** — through many witnesses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing private. The content Timothy is to pass on was said in public and can be checked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What to do with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Put it into the hands of **people who can be trusted**, people who **will be able to teach others in their turn**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parathou** — deposit it, entrust it. It is a banking word, used of goods left with someone for safekeeping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same root of God: *He is able to keep what I have committed to Him until that Day*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two qualifications"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Pistoi** — trustworthy. And **hikanoi** — competent, up to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Reliable and able. Paul does not ask for eloquence or seniority."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul → Timothy → trustworthy people → others."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four links, and the letter that lays it out is written by a man whose own link is about to be cut."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The time of my departure is at hand.* See the note on 2 Timothy 4:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three pictures, in a row: the soldier who does not entangle himself with civilian affairs, the athlete who is not crowned unless he competes by the rules, and the farmer who works before he eats."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The hard-working farmer must be first to partake of the crops.* See the note on 2 Timothy 2:3–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Strength that comes from grace, and then three images of effort — and Paul puts them in that order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the grace already did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who has saved us and called us with a holy calling, **not according to our works, but according to His own purpose and grace** which was given to us in Christ Jesus **before time began**.* See the note on 2 Timothy 1:8–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Given before there was anybody to give it to. That is the grace Timothy is told to be strengthened in."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb.** **Endunamou** is passive — *be made strong* — and the agent is named in the same sentence: the grace that is in Christ Jesus."}]}, "2 Timothy 2:14–15": {"title": "A clean straight road", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses on what a teacher should stop doing, and then what he should do instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has come before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be strong in the grace that is in Christ Jesus. And the things that you have heard from me… **commit these to faithful men who will be able to teach others also**.* See the note on 2 Timothy 2:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the soldier, the athlete and the farmer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep putting all this in front of them, and **charge them in the sight of the Lord to stop fighting over words**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **logomachein** — to war about words. A compound Paul appears to have coined; the noun form appears once more, in the first letter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Enopion tou Theou** — in God's presence. The charge is given under oath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What word-fighting achieves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It **settles nothing at all**, and it does **real damage to the people who have to listen**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ep' ouden chresimon** — useful for nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **epi katastrophe ton akouonton** — to the ruin of the hearers. **Katastrophe** — the word English took directly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who gets hurt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the two arguing. The people listening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is Paul's whole reason for raising it, and it is a pastoral observation rather than a theological one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Ephesus had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Some who teach otherwise and do not consent to wholesome words… **obsessed with disputes and arguments over words, from which come envy, strife, reviling, evil suspicions***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letters to Timothy keep coming back to it: *foolish and ignorant disputes, knowing that they generate strife*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The alternative"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give everything you have to **standing in front of God as someone tested and found good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **spoudason** — be diligent, make every effort, hurry to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dokimon** — approved after testing. The word used of metal that has been assayed, of coins checked for weight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose approval"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Standing in front of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the congregation. The argument in verse 14 was conducted in front of listeners; the work in verse 15 is done in front of God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The workman"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **worker with nothing to be ashamed of**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ergaten anepaischunton** — an unashamed workman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A manual word. Paul's own trade was tent-making, and the metaphor is of a craftsman whose finished job can be inspected."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Cutting **a clean straight road through the word of truth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **orthotomounta** — from **orthos**, straight, and **temno**, to cut."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word occurs twice in the Greek Old Testament, both times in Proverbs, of making a path straight: *in all your ways acknowledge Him, and **He shall direct your paths***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the image is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A road-maker driving a straight line through country, or a mason cutting stone so it sits square."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not dissecting or dividing up. Cutting straight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it matters here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The contrast is with the word-fighting of verse 14, which wanders."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One man makes a road through the text. The other argues about terms and takes his listeners nowhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **shun profane and idle babblings**, for they will increase to more ungodliness. And their message will spread like cancer.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **gangraina** — gangrene. Paul names two men who have done it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Nevertheless **the solid foundation of God stands**, having this seal: \"The Lord knows those who are His.\"* See the note on 2 Timothy 2:19–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last word on how to handle opponents"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **a servant of the Lord must not quarrel** but be gentle to all, able to teach, patient, in humility correcting those who are in opposition.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb.** **Orthotomeo** — to cut straight. A road-maker's word, set against the word-fighting of the verse before, which goes in circles and damages the people listening."}]}, "2 Timothy 2:22–23": {"title": "Run away, and run after", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs in opposite directions, and the second one is longer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being written to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Timothy, at Ephesus, from a Roman prison. Paul's last surviving letter — chapter 4 has *the time of my departure is at hand*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Timothy is not a young man by now, but he had been very young when Paul picked him up at Lystra, and Paul had written earlier: *let no one despise your youth*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes just before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In a great house there are not only vessels of gold and silver, but also of wood and clay, some for honour and some for dishonour. **Therefore if anyone cleanses himself** from the latter, he will be a vessel for honour, sanctified and useful for the Master.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Run hard away from **the cravings that come with being young**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pheuge** — flee. Present imperative: keep running."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Neoterikas epithumias** — youthful desires. Not necessarily sexual; the word covers impulsiveness, ambition, the appetite for a quarrel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The context here suggests arguments as much as anything, given what verse 23 is about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same verb goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Flee sexual immorality.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 6:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Flee from idolatry.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Flee these things* — of the love of money, in the first letter to Timothy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the running-away verb four times, and never recommends standing and arguing with any of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph is the model, and he left his coat in the woman's hand. See the note on Genesis 39:7–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **run just as hard after what is right, after faith, after love, after peace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dioke** — pursue, chase. The verb used for persecuting; it is what Saul used to do."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four things, listed. Righteousness, faith, love, peace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fleeing alone leaves a vacuum. Paul never gives the first verb without the second."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The company"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And run **in the company of everyone who calls on the Lord out of a clean heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meta ton epikaloumenon** — with those calling on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pursuit is not solitary. Paul adds a clause about who else is running."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As for arguments that are **foolish and know nothing, have nothing to do with them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **moras kai apaideutous** — stupid and uneducated. And **paraitou** — refuse, decline, beg off."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You understand perfectly well that **all they ever give birth to is a fight**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **gennosin machas** — they beget battles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say the arguments are wrong. He says they are fertile in one direction only."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And a servant of the Lord **must not quarrel but be gentle to all, able to teach, patient, in humility correcting those who are in opposition**, if God perhaps will grant them repentance, so that they may know the truth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *gentle* is **epion** — the word for a nurse with a child. Paul uses it of himself in Thessalonians: *we were gentle among you, just as a nursing mother cherishes her own children*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The letter's other instruction about words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be diligent to present yourself approved to God, a worker who does not need to be ashamed, **rightly dividing the word of truth**. But **shun profane and idle babblings**, for they will increase to more ungodliness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same pattern: do this, avoid that."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two directions.** Away from one thing and after four others, and the second list is longer. Paul never gives the fleeing without saying what to run toward."}]}, "2 Timothy 3:1–5": {"title": "The hard days ahead", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Eighteen descriptions in a row, and the first and last are the same thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Understand this, and **do not let it take you by surprise**: in the last days there will come **stretches of time that are hard to live through**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kairoi chalepoi** — difficult seasons."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chalepos** appears twice in the New Testament. The other use is Matthew's description of the Gadarene demoniacs: *exceedingly fierce*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not merely unpleasant. Dangerous."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for times"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kairoi** — not **chronoi**. Seasons, periods with a character, rather than a stretch of calendar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prediction is of recurring seasons, not one continuous decline."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first item"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People will be **in love with themselves**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **philautoi** — self-lovers. It heads the list."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **in love with money**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Philarguroi** — silver-lovers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list begins with two compounds of **phileo**, love."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last item"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their pleasures will have **far more of their love than God ever does**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Philedonoi mallon e philotheoi** — lovers of pleasure rather than lovers of God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two more **phileo** compounds, closing the list."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shape"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The catalogue opens with two wrong loves and closes with two more."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in between is what those loves produce. Paul has framed the whole list as a disorder of affection rather than a failure of rules."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will **brag about what they are**, and they will **look down on everyone who is not**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will **throw their words against God**, and they will **not listen to their own parents**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will **take everything and thank no one**, and **nothing at all will be sacred to them**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The family word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ordinary affection that holds a family together **will simply be missing in them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **astorgoi** — without **storge**, the natural love between parent and child."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The alpha-privative: the thing is absent, not corrupted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will **refuse to be reconciled**, they will **tear other people's names to pieces**, and they will have **no hold whatever on their own appetites**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Aspondoi** — literally *without libation*, without a truce. In Greek usage, a war in which no terms can be agreed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Diaboloi** — slanderers. The same word used of the devil."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last three before the pleasures"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will **betray the ones who trust them**, they will **rush ahead without a thought**, and they will be **swollen up with their own importance**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tetuphomenoi** — from **tuphos**, smoke. Puffed up, clouded, conceited."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they will **keep the outward shape of a devout life** while **denying the very power that makes such a life real**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **morphosin eusebeias** — a form of godliness. And **ten dunamin autes ernemenoi** — having denied its power."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Morphosis** — the outline, the shape, the appearance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being described"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the pagan world outside. People with a form of godliness — inside the church, and the last line makes it explicit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Have nothing to do with people like that.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apotrepou** — turn yourself away from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Timothy is not told to argue with them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But you must continue in the things which you have learned… **from childhood you have known the Holy Scriptures**.* See the note on 2 Timothy 3:14–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *all Scripture is given by inspiration of God*. See the note on 2 Timothy 3:16–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The remedy offered against eighteen items is a book and the two women who taught it to him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The frame.** Four compounds of *love* — self, money, pleasure, God — at the two ends of the list. Everything between them is what those affections produce."}]}, "2 Timothy 3:14–15": {"title": "\"Ever since you were a small child\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's last letter, and this is what he tells a young man to do when everything else is coming apart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is emphatic — *su de*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What comes before it: a list of what the last days will be like — *lovers of themselves, lovers of money, boasters, proud, blasphemers, disobedient to parents* — eighteen items, and then *evil men and impostors will grow worse and worse*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Against all of it: *but you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hold your ground **in what you learned and came to be sure of**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **mene** — remain, abide, stay put."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not advance, and not defend. Stay."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **you know the ones you learned it from**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is plural — *from whom you learned*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul names them at the start of the letter: *the genuine faith that is in you, which dwelt first in **your grandmother Lois and your mother Eunice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two women, named, three generations back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Acts adds the detail: Timothy's father was Greek. The Scripture came down the maternal line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And you know that **ever since you were a small child you have been at home in the sacred writings**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apo brephous** — from infancy. **Brephos** is the word for an unborn or newborn child; Luke uses it of John leaping in the womb."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he learned"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **sacred writings**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hiera grammata** — the standard Jewish term for the Hebrew Scriptures."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Timothy learned the Old Testament as a small boy, in a Greek town, from a Jewish mother and grandmother, years before Paul arrived in Lystra."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they can do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they are able to **make you wise for the salvation that comes through faith in Christ Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sophisai** — to make wise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The clause is careful. The writings make wise *for* salvation, and the salvation comes *through faith in Christ Jesus*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is describing what the Old Testament did for a boy who met Christ later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse everybody knows:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All Scripture is **given by inspiration of God**, and is profitable for doctrine, for reproof, for correction, for instruction in righteousness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **theopneustos** — God-breathed. One word, and it appears once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's last. He is in a cold cell — *bring the cloak that I left with Carpus* — most of his companions have gone, and *only Luke is with me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have fought the good fight, I have finished the race, I have kept the faith.* See the note on 2 Timothy 4:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks Timothy to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be diligent to come to me quickly… **come before winter**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whether he arrived is not recorded."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul tells him to fall back on.** Not new arguments. What he learned, and who he learned it from — two women who taught him the Scriptures before he could walk."}]}, "2 Timothy 3:16–17": {"title": "Breathed out by God", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's clearest statement about Scripture, written from a cell, in his last letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every Scripture is **breathed out by God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **theopneustos** — from *theos* (God) and *pneo* (to breathe). It appears once in the New Testament, here, and Paul may have coined it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the direction. Not that God breathed *into* the writings, but that they were breathed *out* — the Scripture is the exhalation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 2 has God breathing into a man's nostrils and the man becoming a living soul. The same picture, applied to a text."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Every\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek can be read *all Scripture* or *every Scripture*. Either way there is no subset — no clause exempting the genealogies, the census lists, the building specifications, or the parts nobody preaches from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is useful for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four things, in sequence:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**teaching** — showing what is true", "**showing us where we have gone wrong** — the Greek is conviction, exposure", "**setting us straight again** — restoration, putting back upright", "**training us in the life that is right** — the word is *paideia*, the whole business of raising a child"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The middle two are worth the order they are in. The exposure comes before the correction, and the correction is described as being set back on your feet rather than knocked off them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it produces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that the one who belongs to God may be **complete, fully equipped** for every good work.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *complete* is **artios** — fitted, in working order. And *fully equipped* is the same root intensified: outfitted, supplied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the vocabulary of a ship rigged for a voyage or a workman with his tools."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which Scripture Paul meant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses earlier: *from childhood you have known **the Holy Scriptures**, which are able to make you wise for salvation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Timothy's childhood Bible was the Old Testament. Paul says those writings can make a man wise for salvation through faith in Christ Jesus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How much weight a single word can carry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does it himself in Galatians 3: *it does not say, \"and to seeds,\" as though to many, but as of one — **and to your Seed**, who is Christ.* An argument on a singular noun. See the note on Galatians 3:15–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus did the same to the Sadducees, on a present-tense verb: *I **am** the God of Abraham* — therefore *He is not the God of the dead, but of the living.* See the note on Matthew 22:23–33."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both of them read Scripture as though the individual words were load-bearing, and both won the argument that way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul was when he wrote it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bring the cloak that I left with Carpus at Troas — and the books, especially the parchments.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same letter, two chapters later. A man in a Roman cell asking for his coat and his scrolls."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse that follows the famous one about the last days.** Paul lists the difficult times ahead — *lovers of themselves, lovers of money, boasters* — and his instruction to Timothy about it is *but you must continue in the things you have learned.* The remedy offered for a bad age is a book."}]}, "2 Timothy 4:6–8": {"title": "Three finished things, and a crown that is not only his", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last words we have from Paul, written from a Roman cell with no expectation of release."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Poured out like an offering\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is a drink offering — wine poured out beside a sacrifice, the last act of a ritual and the smallest part of it. Paul does not compare himself to the sacrifice. He is the libation poured alongside one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The hour of my going is here* — and the word for going is **analusis**, the noun of the verb he used in Philippians for casting off. Same picture, years later, with the ship now actually leaving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three perfects"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have fought the good fight. I have finished the race. I have kept the faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "All three are in the perfect tense — done, and the result stands. Note what he does not claim: he does not say he won, or that he did it well. He says he fought, he finished, he kept."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The middle one is not about coming first. The Greek is simply completing the course."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crown"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Laid aside and kept for me* — a verb meaning reserved, put away for someone. It is already in storage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the line that stops it being about Paul:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "and not to me only, but to every last one who has loved His appearing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The condition for the crown, stated by a man about to be executed, is not achievement or office or martyrdom. It is having loved the fact that He is coming."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Read on to verse 9.** Immediately after this — the crown, the finished race, the settled peace — Paul writes *do your best to come to me quickly*, and later asks for his cloak and his books. The grandeur and the cold cell are three sentences apart, and both are in the letter because both were true."}]}, "3 John 1:2": {"title": "\"The way your soul is already doing well\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A single verse from a letter of fourteen, written by an old man to a friend named Gaius."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he prays"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*More than anything I hope things go well for you and that your health holds up, the way your soul is already doing well.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things in one sentence: that it goes well with him generally, that his body holds up, and that both would keep pace with a soul that is already thriving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb behind *go well* is **euodoo** — literally to have a good road, a prosperous journey. It is used of travelling without obstruction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure John uses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The way your soul is already doing well.* The soul is the benchmark, and it is in good order — that much John states as fact rather than wish."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the prayer is not for prosperity in the abstract. It is that the outward life would come up level with the inward one, which John already knows is flourishing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he was confident about the soul"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse says why: travellers had come and reported that Gaius was walking in the truth, and John says nothing gives him greater joy. Then the letter commends him for how he treated strangers and missionaries passing through — at his own cost."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gaius was a generous man, and John prays for his health and his road in the same breath as praising it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In all things\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **peri panton** — concerning all things, in every respect."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not narrow it. He prays it over the whole of the man's life and then names health separately, so the health is not the whole of what *all things* means."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is writing it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The apostle who leaned on His chest at the supper, writing in old age to a private individual. It is a personal letter — the shortest book in the New Testament — and this is the sentence he opens with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls it what he wishes *above all things*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What kind of sentence it is.** It sits where a first-century letter always put a wish for the reader's health — the standard opening courtesy of the era. John takes that ordinary form and fills it with something else: a prayer that the body and the circumstances would match the state of the soul. He evidently thought the three belonged together."}]}, "Acts 1:12–14": {"title": "The room they went back to", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He has just gone up out of sight, and Luke's next sentence is about the walk home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From the hill called the Mount of Olives, which is close to the city, **a Sabbath day's walk away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roughly two thousand cubits — a little over half a mile. Luke measures it in the unit a Jewish reader would use."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ascension happened close enough to the city to walk back from before lunch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They went up to **the upstairs room** where they were staying.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek has the definite article — **the** upper room. Luke assumes his reader knows which one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is where the supper was eaten, and it is where they are again. See the note on Mark 14:12–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peter and James and John and Andrew, Philip and Thomas, Bartholomew and Matthew, James the son of Alphaeus, Simon the Zealot, and Judas the brother of James.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven. Luke lists every one by name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order has changed from the Gospel lists — Peter, James and John are moved to the front, and Judas Iscariot is simply gone. No comment is made about the absence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All of them **stayed together, praying with one heart and one mind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **homothumadon** — of one accord, one passion. Luke uses it repeatedly in the early chapters of Acts and almost nowhere else in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are waiting, because they were told to: *wait for the Promise of the Father.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The women"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Along with **the women**, and **Mary the mother of Jesus**, and **His brothers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke, who opened his Gospel with Mary, mentions her here for the last time — in a prayer meeting, named among the others, with no special place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The brothers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And His brothers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In John's Gospel: *even His brothers did not believe in Him.* They mocked Him at the Feast of Tabernacles and told Him to go show Himself to the world."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between that verse and this one, the resurrection happened, and Paul records one detail: *then He was seen by James.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James goes on to lead the church in Jerusalem and write a letter that opens *James, a bondservant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ* — without mentioning that they grew up in the same house."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How many"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *the number of names together was about **a hundred and twenty**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what was left, in one room, of three years of public ministry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens in that room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten days later: *suddenly there came a sound from heaven as of a rushing mighty wind, and it filled the whole house where they were sitting.* See the note on Acts 2:1–4."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they had been told to do.** Not to organise, not to plan, not to preach yet. *Tarry in the city of Jerusalem until you are endued with power from on high.* Luke describes ten days of exactly that, and lists everyone who was there."}]}, "Acts 1:4–5": {"title": "Wait for the promise", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last instruction before He goes, and it is to stay put."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While He was with them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The forty days between the resurrection and the ascension. Luke has just summarised them: *to whom He also presented Himself alive after His suffering by many infallible proofs, being seen by them during forty days and speaking of the things pertaining to the kingdom of God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tekmeriois** — demonstrable proofs, evidence. A legal word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He told them **not to leave Jerusalem**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The city that had just executed Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every instinct would send them home to Galilee, and Galilee is where He had told them to meet Him after the resurrection. Now they are told to stay in the capital."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But to **wait there for what the Father had promised**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **perimenein** — to wait around for, to stay expecting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The promise of the Father** — the phrase is His. Luke records it at the end of his Gospel too: *behold, I send the Promise of My Father upon you; but tarry in the city of Jerusalem until you are endued with power from on high*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they already knew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You have heard Me speak of it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not introducing a new subject. The upper-room discourse had four separate passages on the Helper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will pray the Father, and He will give you another Helper.* See the note on John 14:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is to your advantage that I go away; for if I do not go away, the Helper will not come to you.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**John baptized with water, but in a few days you are going to be baptized with the Holy Spirit**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **baptisthesesthe** — a future passive. It will be done to them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Baptizo** — to dip, to immerse, to plunge. Used in secular Greek of a ship going down and of cloth being dyed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose words these are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Baptist's. He had said it at the Jordan, before any of this:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I indeed baptize you with water unto repentance, but He who is coming after me is mightier than I… **He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus quotes John, three years later, to men waiting in a city."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In a few days.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten, as it turns out. The ascension, and then Pentecost — which was already a fixed date in the calendar, fifty days after Passover."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not give them the number."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they ask instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, is it at this time **You will restore the kingdom to Israel**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer: *\"It is not for you to know times or seasons which the Father has put in His own authority. **But you shall receive power** when the Holy Spirit has come upon you.\"* See the note on Acts 1:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They ask about a timetable and are handed an assignment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did with the waiting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These all continued with one accord in prayer and supplication, with the women and Mary the mother of Jesus, and with His brothers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "About a hundred and twenty of them, in an upper room, for ten days."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they replaced Judas, which Peter proposed and they settled by lot."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What arrived"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the Day of Pentecost had fully come, they were all with one accord in one place. And suddenly there came a sound from heaven, as of a rushing mighty wind.* See the note on Acts 2:1–4."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last instruction.** Not go — *stay*. The commission to the ends of the earth is issued three verses later, and the first order attached to it is to wait in the city."}]}, "Acts 1:6–8": {"title": "Not the timetable, the assignment", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "They ask about a date and are handed a map."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, **is this the time? Are You going to restore the kingdom to Israel now?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last question the disciples ask Him, and it is about politics."}, {"k": "p", "t": "After three years, a crucifixion, a resurrection and forty days of teaching about the kingdom of God, this is what they want to know."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is right about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The restoration itself is not corrected. He does not say the kingdom will not be restored to Israel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes it up in Romans 11: *and so all Israel will be saved*. See the note on Romans 11:25–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only the timing is refused."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is **not for you to know the times or the seasons**; the Father has set those by **His own authority**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **chronous e kairous** — durations and appointed moments. Two different words for time, and both are off the table."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus had already said it: *of that day and hour no one knows, not even the angels of heaven, nor the Son, but only the Father*. See the note on Matthew 24:36–41."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But you are going to receive power** when the Holy Spirit comes on you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dunamin** — power, capacity. Where *dynamite* comes from, though the English word carries the wrong sense: this is enablement rather than explosion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The clock is the Father's business. The power is theirs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **you will be My witnesses**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **martures** — witnesses. Where *martyr* comes from, and the word drifted to that meaning because of what happened to the people in this room."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A witness reports what he saw. It is not a role that requires expertise, only presence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's criterion for replacing Judas, a few verses later: *one of these men who have accompanied us… must become a witness with us of His resurrection*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four places"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In **Jerusalem**, and in all **Judea**, and in **Samaria**, and out to the **farthest edge of the earth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The table of contents for the whole book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapters 1 to 7: Jerusalem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 8: Judea and Samaria — and it takes a persecution to get them out. *They were all scattered throughout the regions of Judea and Samaria… therefore those who were scattered went everywhere preaching the word.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapters 13 onward: outward, and the book ends in Rome, unhindered. See the note on Acts 28:28–29."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one nobody wanted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samaria. The disciples had once offered to call down fire on a Samaritan village."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is on the list, in the middle, and Philip goes there in chapter 8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now when He had spoken these things, while they watched, **He was taken up, and a cloud received Him out of their sight**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And two men in white: *\"Men of Galilee, why do you stand gazing up into heaven? **This same Jesus, who was taken up from you into heaven, will so come in like manner** as you saw Him go into heaven.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They ask about the timing and are told to stop looking at the sky."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke says the book is about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The former account I made, O Theophilus, of all that Jesus **began** both to do and teach.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Volume one is what He began. Acts is the continuation."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He substitutes for the date.** Power, a job description, and four place names — and the last of them has no edge to it."}]}, "Acts 1:9–11": {"title": "\"This same Jesus\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "They watched Him go, and two men had to tell them to stop looking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While they were **looking straight at Him**, He was lifted up, and a cloud took Him out of their sight.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke is careful about this — they were watching. It happened in daylight, in front of eleven men, on a hillside outside Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cloud"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not weather. The cloud in Scripture is the visible sign of the presence — the pillar in the wilderness, the cloud that filled the tabernacle and then the temple, the bright cloud at the transfiguration."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Standing there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were **still standing there staring into the sky** as He went.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says it twice, and the two men in white say it back to them as a question: *why are you standing here looking into the sky?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody rebukes them for grief. They are simply moved along — there is a room in Jerusalem to go to and ten days to wait."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This same Jesus**, who has been taken from you into heaven, will come back **in the same way** you have watched Him go.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves matter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**This same Jesus.** Not a different one, not an altered one. The Man they had walked with for three years — the one who wept at a grave, cooked breakfast on a beach, touched a leper, and would not break a bruised reed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is who went up, and that is who is coming back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In the same way\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bodily, visibly, and to the same place. Zechariah says it: *in that day His feet will stand on the Mount of Olives* — which is precisely the hill they were standing on when they were told this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He was doing as He left"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's Gospel gives the detail Acts leaves out: *He lifted up His hands and blessed them. And it came to pass, while He blessed them, He was parted from them and carried up into heaven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His hands were up and He was in the middle of a blessing. The blessing was never finished — He went up mid-sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who He is now"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has taken His seat at the right hand of God's throne.* Every priest under the old covenant stood, because the work was never done. He sat down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He *always lives to make intercession for them* — the same Man, doing that, now."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last thing recorded of the disciples in that chapter.** *They returned to Jerusalem with great joy.* He had just left, and Luke says joy. They had been told He was coming back exactly as He went."}]}, "Acts 10:1–8": {"title": "A Roman officer at prayer", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that opens the church to the world starts with a soldier of the occupying army."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In Caesarea there was a man named **Cornelius, a centurion of what was called the Italian Regiment**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Caesarea was the Roman administrative capital of Judea — Pilate's seat, and the garrison town."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A centurion commanded eighty to a hundred men. Every centurion named in the New Testament comes off well: the one at Capernaum, the one at the cross, and the one who kept Paul alive on the ship."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke says about him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was **devout**, and **he and his whole household feared God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**God-fearer** was a recognised category — a Gentile who worshipped the God of Israel, attended the synagogue, and had not taken circumcision."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **gave generously to the people** and **prayed to God continually**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Alms and prayer. Both were Jewish devotional practice, and he did them as an outsider."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*About **three in the afternoon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ninth hour — the hour of the evening sacrifice, and the hour Peter and John went up to the temple in chapter 3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Roman officer is keeping Jewish prayer hours in a garrison town."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vision"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **saw plainly in a vision an angel of God** coming in to him and saying, \"**Cornelius**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says *plainly* — the Greek is *manifestly*, openly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **stared at the angel, frightened**, and said, \"**What is it, Lord?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same **kurie** that can mean *sir*. He does not know what he is speaking to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the angel says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Your prayers and your gifts to the poor have gone up before God, and He has remembered them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *have ascended for a memorial before God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The language is sacrificial — the phrase used of the grain offering's memorial portion burned on the altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Prayers and alms from a Gentile soldier, described as an offering God has kept."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the angel does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Preach. It gives an address."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now **send men to Joppa and bring back a man called Simon, who is also named Peter**. He is staying with **Simon the tanner, whose house is by the sea**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An angel appears and its message is a street direction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man is to be told by a man. Peter says the same thing at the council later: *God made a choice among us: that **by my mouth** the Gentiles should hear the message.* See the note on Acts 15:6–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tanner"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke has already told the reader where Peter is: *Peter stayed on in Joppa for some time with a tanner named Simon.* See the note on Acts 9:36–43."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A tanner was ceremonially unclean by trade, and tanneries were built outside towns."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man about to be told *what God has cleansed, you must not call unholy* is already lodging with one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He will tell you what you ought to do**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Cornelius did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He called **two of his household servants and a devout soldier** from among those who attended him. **He explained everything to them** and sent them to Joppa.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty miles down the coast. He tells them the whole thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens the next day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter goes up on the roof to pray at noon, and sees a sheet. See the note on Acts 10:9–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two visions are a day apart and neither man knows about the other."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the angel is for.** Not the message. It gives a name, a town, a host and a street — and says the man in the house will do the telling."}]}, "Acts 10:17–20": {"title": "Three men at the gate", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The interpretation of the vision knocks on the door while he is still working on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The state he is in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While Peter was **still turning the vision over in his mind, wondering what it could mean**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dieporei** — thoroughly perplexed, at a complete loss."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not understand it. Luke says so twice in four verses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sheet full of animals has not yet produced a conclusion about people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is happening downstairs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The men Cornelius had sent **had asked their way to Simon's house and were standing at the gate**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had walked thirty miles from Caesarea. Luke has been running the two scenes in parallel since the chapter opened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question they ask"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They called out, asking **whether Simon, also called Peter, was staying there**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The exact name the angel gave Cornelius. See the note on Acts 10:1–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peter was still thinking about the vision when **the Spirit said to him**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four parts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look, **three men are asking for you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Information he could not have had. He is on the roof."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get up, go downstairs**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **go with them without hesitating**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meden diakrinomenos** — making no distinction, doubting nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same root Peter uses at the council: *He made **no distinction at all** between us and them.* See the note on Acts 15:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Because I have sent them**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim in the last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men were sent by Cornelius, who was told by an angel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Spirit says *I have sent them*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sequence of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An angel to a Roman at three in the afternoon. A sheet to a Jew at noon the next day. Three men at a gate. And a sentence from the Spirit joining them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four separate movements, none of which the human parties arranged."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Peter went down to the men and said, \"Yes, I am he whom you seek. **For what reason have you come?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks. He still does not know."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They tell him about Cornelius — *a just man, one who fears God and has a good reputation among all the nation of the Jews* — and that an angel told him to send."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he invited them in and lodged them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Jew gives lodging to two Gentile servants and a Roman soldier, in a tanner's house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the first thing that changes, and it happens before he leaves Joppa."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who goes with him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And some brethren from Joppa accompanied him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six of them, as he says later in Jerusalem — witnesses he brought deliberately. See the note on Acts 11:4–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the vision meant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He works it out on the road, and states it on arrival: *God has shown me that I should call no one unholy or unclean.* See the note on Acts 10:24–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sheet had animals in it. The conclusion is about people, and Peter reaches it somewhere between Joppa and Caesarea."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**When the answer arrives.** Not in the vision. Peter is still perplexed when three men turn up at the gate — and the Spirit's instruction is simply to go with them without making distinctions."}]}, "Acts 10:24–29": {"title": "\"I am only a man myself\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter walks into a Roman officer's house and finds a congregation waiting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The preparation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Cornelius **was expecting them** and **had called together his relatives and close friends**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been told to send for a man who *will tell you what you ought to do*, and he assembled an audience for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the angel's message said to invite anybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The greeting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As Peter came in, Cornelius met him, **fell at his feet and bowed low**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Roman centurion on the floor in front of a Galilean fisherman."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Peter **lifted him up** and said, \"**Stand up. I am only a man myself**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *I myself am also a man*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul and Barnabas say the same at Lystra when the crowd tries to sacrifice to them: *we also are men with the same nature as you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the angel in Revelation, twice, when John falls down: *see that you do not do that. I am your fellow servant.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter states first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You know that it is **against our law for a Jewish man to associate with a foreigner or to visit him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **athemiton** — unlawful, taboo."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was custom rather than written Torah — the Old Testament nowhere forbids it, and Jews did trade and live among Gentiles. But the practice was real and everybody in the room knew it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter names the wall out loud before crossing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But God has shown me that I should call no one unholy or unclean**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **medena** — no man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sheet had animals in it. This is Peter's interpretation, and it is about people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He worked it out on the road from Joppa. See the note on Acts 10:9–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That is why **I came without objection when I was sent for**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anantirretos** — without contradiction, without raising an argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **may I ask why you sent for me?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has walked thirty miles and still does not know."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Cornelius answers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole story of the vision, and then: *\"**Now therefore, we are all present before God, to hear all the things commanded you by God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is one of the best sentences in Acts, and it comes from a Gentile soldier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**In truth I perceive that God shows no partiality**.\"* See the note on Acts 10:34–43."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **prosopolemptes** — a receiver of faces, one who judges by appearance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What interrupts him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While Peter was still speaking these words, **the Holy Spirit fell upon all those who heard the word**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mid-sermon. Peter reports it that way in Jerusalem: *as I began to speak, the Holy Spirit fell on them just as He did on us at the beginning.* See the note on Acts 11:15–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody had been baptised, circumcised or instructed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter asks the six men who came with him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Can anyone forbid water**, that these should not be baptised **who have received the Holy Spirit just as we have**?\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is put on trial in Jerusalem for it in the next chapter — not for preaching to Gentiles, but for eating with them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the wall goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For He Himself is our peace, who has made both one, and **has broken down the middle wall of separation**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul, in Ephesians, writing about the same barrier."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Peter says on the doorstep.** Names the custom he is breaking, states what God showed him, and then asks why he was invited — because he still does not know."}]}, "Acts 10:34–43": {"title": "\"He went about doing good\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter's sermon in a Roman officer's house, and the summary of Jesus' ministry in it is the shortest in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it opens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I see now, truly, that God shows no favouritism.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says *I see now* — he had not seen it before that week. It took a vision on a roof, three men at the gate, and a walk to Caesarea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is vivid: God is *no taker of faces*, no respecter of appearances."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The summary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How God anointed Jesus of Nazareth with the Holy Spirit and with power, and how He went about **doing good** and **healing everyone under the oppression of the devil**, because God was with Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One sentence for three years. Two verbs: doing good, and healing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is speaking to people who had heard the reports — *you know what has happened all through Judea.* He does not need to persuade them it occurred."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the healing is called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone under the oppression of the devil.* The word means being tyrannised, held down by force."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter characterises sickness there as an oppression being lifted, and healing as release rather than reward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Because God was with Him\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given for the whole ministry. Peter puts the anointing, the power, and the presence together and hangs everything on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every one of the prophets bears witness to Him, that through His name **everyone who believes** in Him receives forgiveness of sins.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the last sentence before the Spirit interrupts him. The word *everyone* is what he is saying when it happens — in a Gentile house, to people he would not have entered a room with a week earlier. See the note on Acts 10:44–48."}]}, "Acts 10:44–48": {"title": "\"Peter was still speaking\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The moment the church stopped being a Jewish movement, and it happens before the sermon finishes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peter was still speaking when the Holy Spirit fell on everyone who was listening.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is cut off mid-sentence. Nothing has been required of these people — no baptism, no circumcision, no probation, no instruction on keeping the law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke tells us what Peter had just said: *everyone who believes in Him receives forgiveness of sins through His name.* The Spirit falls on that sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was astounded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The believers of the circumcision who had come with Peter were astounded that the gift of the Holy Spirit had been poured out on the Gentiles as well.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the Gentiles. The Jewish Christians who had travelled with him — men who had needed a vision on a rooftop and a direct instruction to walk into that house at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They believed in Jesus and were still astonished at this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Peter's question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Can anyone refuse water to keep these people from being baptized, when they have received the Holy Spirit just as we did?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The logic runs backwards from what God has already done. The evidence came first; the church's practice has to catch up with it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the argument Peter will use again in Jerusalem when he is called to account for eating with Gentiles — *if God gave them the same gift, who was I to think I could stand in God's way?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Afterward they asked him to stay on with them a few days.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Jewish apostle stays in a Roman officer's house. In the culture of the time that sentence is nearly as significant as the Spirit falling."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the chapter opens.** Cornelius is described as devout, God-fearing, generous and prayerful — and none of it saved him; an angel still had to send for Peter. Luke gives him every religious credential available and then has the gospel arrive anyway, unearned."}]}, "Acts 10:9–16": {"title": "\"What God has cleansed\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter goes up to pray and comes down with the whole Gentile mission."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The next day, **as they were on their way and coming near the city**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three men from Caesarea are a mile off. Luke synchronises the two scenes so the reader can see the arrangement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peter went up on **the flat roof to pray**. It was about **noon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roofs were living space. Noon was one of the three Jewish prayer hours."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hunger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **grew hungry and wanted something to eat**, and **while the meal was being prepared** he fell into a trance.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives the physical detail. The vision arrives in a hungry man waiting for lunch, and it is about food."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sheet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Something like **a great sheet coming down, let down to the earth by its four corners**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four corners — the four quarters of the earth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contents"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All kinds of **four-footed animals of the earth, and wild beasts, and creeping things, and birds of the air**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The categories of Leviticus 11, clean and unclean together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get up, Peter. Kill and eat**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Never, Lord. I have never eaten anything unholy or unclean**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **medamos** — by no means."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He addresses Him as Lord and refuses in the same breath. He had done it before — *depart from me, for I am a sinful man*, and *You shall never wash my feet*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His record is clean and he says so. Peter is not being disobedient; he is being observant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What God has cleansed, you must not call unholy**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ekatharisen** — has purified."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The issue is not what the animals are. It is who has ruled on them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Three times"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This happened **three times**, and then the sheet was taken up again into heaven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who denied three times and was asked three times on the beach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is not about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Food. Peter says so himself when he gets to Caesarea: *God has shown me that **I should call no one unholy or unclean**.* See the note on Acts 10:24–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sheet had animals in it and the application is people. Peter works that out on the journey."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While Peter was **still turning the vision over in his mind**.* See the note on Acts 10:17–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not understand it immediately. The interpretation arrives in the form of three men at the gate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the argument goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter tells the story twice more — to the church in Jerusalem when he is put on trial for eating with Gentiles, and again in substance at the council. See the notes on Acts 11:4–14 and Acts 15:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke tells the Cornelius episode three times in his book. Nothing else gets that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mark's Gospel says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Thus He declared all foods clean.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark adds that clause himself, in a parenthesis, in the passage about what defiles a man — and Mark is traditionally held to have written down Peter's preaching."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter had been in the room when it was said, and had not drawn the conclusion."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is corrected.** Not his obedience. Peter's answer is accurate about the law and accurate about his own record — and what he is told is that a ruling has been made somewhere else."}]}, "Acts 11:15–18": {"title": "\"Who was I to stand in God's way?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter is on trial in Jerusalem for eating with Gentiles, and his defence is a report of something he did not do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The believers in Judea had heard that Gentiles had received the word, and when Peter came up to Jerusalem *those of the circumcision contended with him, saying, \"You went in to uncircumcised men and ate with them!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The charge is about a meal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How he answers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells the whole story again from the beginning — the sheet, the voice, the three men at the door, the journey to Caesarea. Luke records the entire Cornelius account twice, in chapter 10 and again here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**As I began to speak**, the Holy Spirit fell on them **just as He did on us at the beginning**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was interrupted. His sermon in chapter 10 stops mid-flow — Luke says *while Peter was still speaking these words*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing had been decided about these people. Nobody had been baptised, circumcised, or instructed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he remembered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And I remembered what the Lord had said: '**John baptized with water, but you will be baptized with the Holy Spirit**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Words spoken to him personally before the ascension, and he recognises the event as the one they described."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The logic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So if **God gave them the same gift He gave us** when we believed on the Lord Jesus Christ, **who was I to stand in God's way?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses. The premise is the identical gift; the conclusion is a question about his own authority to object."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *who was I, able to withstand God?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is conceded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **same gift**. Peter does not describe a lesser or preliminary version."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it again at the council years later: *God, who knows the heart, testified for them **by giving them the Holy Spirit just as He gave Him to us**. He made **no distinction at all**.* See the note on Acts 15:6–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When they heard this, **they had nothing more to say**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *they kept silent*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they praised God. \"So then,\" they said, \"**God has given the Gentiles too a change of mind that leads to life**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A change of mind** — **metanoia**, the same word Paul uses to the Ephesian elders. See the note on Acts 20:17–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is described as **given**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it settles and what it does not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It settles the Cornelius case. It does not settle the question — Acts 15 has the whole church convening on the same issue, and Galatians 2 has Paul confronting Peter for withdrawing from Gentile tables at Antioch."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same Peter who said *who was I to stand in God's way* got up and moved to another table when men came from James."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke reports the argument here; Paul reports the relapse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Straight to Antioch, where men from Cyprus and Cyrene *spoke to the Hellenists, preaching the Lord Jesus*, and *a great number believed and turned to the Lord*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Barnabas is sent to look into it, goes to Tarsus to find Saul, and the two of them teach there for a year."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the disciples were first called Christians in Antioch.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What his defence rests on.** Not a decision he made or a policy he adopted. Something that happened while he was still talking, which he says was the same thing that had happened to them."}]}, "Acts 11:25–26": {"title": "First called Christians", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man goes looking for someone everybody else had lost track of, and a name gets coined."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The search"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Barnabas **left for Tarsus to look for Saul**, and when he found him he brought him back to Antioch.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anazetesai** — to search out, to hunt for. The word implies the person is not easy to find."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul had been sent home to Tarsus years earlier, after the Jerusalem believers realised the Hellenists were trying to kill him. See the note on Acts 9:28–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been there somewhere between five and ten years. Nobody had come for him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Barnabas is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who had vouched for Saul in Jerusalem when the disciples were afraid of him — *Barnabas took him and brought him to the apostles*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His name means **son of encouragement**, given to him by the apostles after he sold a field and laid the money at their feet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice now he has fetched Saul into a room that would not otherwise have had him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Antioch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gospel had gone there through unnamed men from Cyprus and Cyrene who *spoke to the Hellenists, preaching the Lord Jesus* — the first deliberate Gentile preaching in the book, done by people Luke does not name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the hand of the Lord was with them, and a great number believed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jerusalem sent Barnabas to look at it, and *when he came and had seen the grace of God, he was glad*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ten charin tou Theou** — the grace of God. Luke says it was visible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The year"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **a whole year the two of them met with the church** and taught a great many people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Eniauton holon** — a full year. Luke marks the length, which he rarely does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A year of teaching, by two men, in a Gentile city, before the name appears."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it was **at Antioch that the disciples were first called Christians**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **chrematisai** — a word for being publicly styled, transacting business under a name, receiving an official designation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Christianoi** is a Latinate formation: the *-ianus* suffix marked a household or a partisan group. *Herodianoi* were Herod's people. *Christianoi* were Christ's people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it appears"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times in the New Testament, and never as a self-description."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here. Agrippa: *you almost persuade me to become a Christian*. And Peter: *if anyone suffers as a Christian, let him not be ashamed*. See the note on 1 Peter 4:15–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The believers called themselves disciples, brothers, saints, and *the Way*. The name that stuck was given to them from outside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Not in Jerusalem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name is coined in a Gentile city, by outsiders, about a congregation that a man had to be fetched from obscurity to teach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Antioch becomes the sending church — *now separate to Me Barnabas and Saul for the work to which I have called them*. See the note on Acts 13:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every missionary journey in the book starts from the city where the name started."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the two of them do next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter ends with a famine, and the Antioch believers sending relief to Judea *by the hands of Barnabas and Saul*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first recorded act of the first church called Christian is sending money to the church that had been afraid of one of them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word.** **Chrematisai** — to be publicly styled, to go by a name in the world's business. It was a designation given from outside, and it was given after a year of teaching, not before."}]}, "Acts 11:27–30": {"title": "Bread sent ahead of the famine", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A famine is announced before it arrives, and the church's response is to give money away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The messenger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One of them named **Agabus** stood up and **indicated by the Spirit** that a great famine was coming over the whole world.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Agabus** comes from a root meaning *locust*. A man named for the insect that eats a harvest stands up to announce a shortage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He appears twice in Acts and both times with a warning — here, and later at Caesarea, binding his own hands and feet with Paul's belt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The confirmation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which **happened in the days of Claudius Caesar**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke writes this after the fact and says so. Roman sources record serious grain shortages under Claudius, and Judea was hit hard in the mid-forties."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophecy is dated and checked in the same sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was warned"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Antioch. A Gentile church, a long way north, outside the famine's worst."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are told about a hardship that is going to fall on somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did with the warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the disciples, **each according to what he had**, determined to **send help** to the brothers living in Judea.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do not stockpile. Told that a famine is coming, they send their supplies south."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Each according to what he had\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *as each was prospered*. Nobody is assessed a fixed amount and nobody is told what anyone else gave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they sent it to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Living in Judea* — the mother church in Jerusalem, the people who had been suspicious of Antioch's existence in the first place. Peter had to explain himself in the previous chapter for eating with Gentiles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Gentile church sends money to the Jewish one that questioned it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who carried it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sending it to the elders by the hands of **Barnabas and Saul**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Barnabas is the man who sold a field and laid the money at the apostles' feet in chapter 4, and who vouched for Saul when nobody in Jerusalem would meet him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul is on his first recorded assignment for the church, and it is a delivery job."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Antioch was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verses earlier: *the disciples were **first called Christians** in Antioch.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name is given in this chapter, and the first thing the people carrying it do is this."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of events.** The warning came first, then the collection, then the famine. Luke sets it down in that sequence — the relief was already on the road before anyone in Judea was hungry."}]}, "Acts 11:4–14": {"title": "Peter tells it from the beginning", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Put on trial for eating with Gentiles, Peter's defence is to tell the story again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before: *those of the circumcision contended with him, saying, \"**You went in to uncircumcised men and ate with them**!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The accusation is about a meal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The method"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Peter **began at the beginning and laid it all out for them in order**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kathexes** — in sequence. It is the word Luke uses in his Gospel's preface about his own method: *to write to you an orderly account*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter does not argue. He narrates."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke tells the Cornelius story twice — chapter 10 as it happens, chapter 11 as Peter reports it — which is unusual in a book this compressed, and it is how Luke marks what matters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I was in the city of Joppa, **praying**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same town Jonah sailed from to avoid going to Gentiles."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sheet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Something like **a great sheet coming down out of heaven, let down by its four corners**, and **it came right to me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four corners — the four quarters of the earth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**It came right to me** — Peter adds that detail in the retelling. It did not appear near him; it arrived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contents"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Four-footed animals of the earth, and wild beasts, and creeping things, and birds of the air**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every category in Leviticus 11, clean and unclean together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get up, Peter. Kill and eat**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Never, Lord. Nothing unholy or unclean has ever passed my lips**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *by no means* — the strong double negative."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He addresses Him as Lord and then refuses. He had done the same in the boat — *depart from me, for I am a sinful man* — and at the foot-washing: *You shall never wash my feet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His record is clean and he says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What God has cleansed, you must not call unholy**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *cleansed* is **ekatharisen** — purified."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The issue is not what the animals are. It is who has ruled on them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Three times"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This happened three times**, and then it was all drawn back up into heaven.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is the man who denied three times and was asked three times on the beach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**At that very moment** three men, sent to me from Caesarea, arrived at the house.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The vision ends and there is knocking at the door. The interpretation walks up the street."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The Spirit told me to **go with them without hesitating**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meden diakrinomenon** — making no distinction, doubting nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same root as the word Peter uses at the council years later: *He made **no distinction at all** between us and them.* See the note on Acts 15:6–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The witnesses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **these six brothers went with me too**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter brought corroboration to Jerusalem. Six men, standing there while he speaks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Cornelius had been told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He will **speak words to you by which you and all your household will be saved**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An angel appeared to Cornelius and did not preach to him. It told him where to send for a man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As I began to speak, **the Holy Spirit fell on them just as He did on us at the beginning**.\"* See the note on Acts 11:15–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the room goes quiet."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How he defends himself.** By telling it in order, with six witnesses in the room, and letting the sequence do the arguing — a vision he refused, an instruction he could not answer, and three men at the door before it was over."}]}, "Acts 12:24–25": {"title": "But the word grew", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses closing a chapter that has had a killing, an arrest and a death in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter contained"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now about that time Herod the king stretched out his hand to harass some from the church. Then **he killed James the brother of John with the sword**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One of the three who had been on the mountain. The first apostle to die, and Luke gives him half a verse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Peter arrested, four squads of soldiers, chains, and an angel — *and his chains fell off his hands*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Rhoda, who left him standing at the door because she was too pleased to open it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the guards executed, and Herod on his throne in Caesarea being hailed as a god and struck down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the word of God grew and spread**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **euxanen kai eplethuneto** — grew and was multiplied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are agricultural. The verbs for a crop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the *but* is pointing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At everything in the chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An apostle killed. The leading apostle in prison. A king persecuting on purpose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Luke sets one word against all of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does this repeatedly. The phrase is a structural marker in Acts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the word of God spread, and the number of the disciples multiplied greatly in Jerusalem* — after the complaint about the widows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the word of the Lord grew mightily and prevailed* — after the riot in Ephesus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every time, it follows trouble."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Herod's end was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And immediately an angel of the Lord struck him, because he did not give glory to God. And he was eaten by worms and died.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Josephus records the same death independently, with the same setting — Caesarea, the games, the silver garment catching the sun, the crowd shouting that his was the voice of a god — and five days of abdominal agony."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's account and the historian's agree."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast the two verses make"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Herod is dead in verse 23. The word is growing in verse 24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke puts them next to each other without comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The return"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **Barnabas and Saul returned from Jerusalem** when they had completed their service.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had come up with famine relief from Antioch: *then the disciples, each according to his ability, determined to send relief to the brethren dwelling in Judea*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens with a king killing believers and closes with two men delivering money to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Taking with them **John, also called Mark**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His mother's house is where the prayer meeting was, earlier in the chapter — the one Rhoda answered the door of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes with them to Cyprus, leaves them at Perga, and becomes the reason Paul and Barnabas split up. See the note on Acts 13:4–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And ends up with Paul at the very end: *get Mark and bring him with you, for he is useful to me for ministry*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the Gospel that bears his name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter says about prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peter was therefore kept in prison, but **constant prayer was offered to God for him by the church**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They prayed all night, and when the answer knocked on the door they told the girl she was out of her mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the two verses do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Change the subject. From chapter 13 the book leaves Jerusalem and follows Paul."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last thing Luke says about the old centre is that the word grew."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the sentence is set against.** An apostle executed, the chief apostle in chains, and a king killing on purpose. Luke's verdict on the chapter is five words: *but the word grew*."}]}, "Acts 13:1–3": {"title": "Five men in Antioch", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most consequential meeting in Acts, and Luke starts by listing who was in the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the church at **Antioch**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not Jerusalem. The third city of the empire, in Syria, where the gospel had gone after the persecution scattered everybody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the disciples were first called Christians in Antioch.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The centre of gravity moves here, and everything from chapter 13 to the end of the book goes out from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Barnabas.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Levite from Cyprus who sold a field and laid the money at the apostles' feet, and who vouched for Saul when Jerusalem would not have him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Simeon who was called **Niger**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Latin for *black*. The name suggests an African."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lucius of **Cyrene**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "North Africa, in modern Libya."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Manaen, who had been **brought up alongside Herod the tetrarch**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **suntrophos** — a foster-brother, or a boy raised with a royal child as a companion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Herod Antipas killed John the Baptist and mocked Jesus at His trial. The man who grew up beside him is teaching in Antioch."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Saul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Last on the list. He has been in Tarsus for years, and Barnabas had to go and fetch him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the list contains"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Cypriot Levite, an African, a Libyan, a foster-brother of a king, and a former persecutor."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke did not have to name them, and he does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While they were **worshiping the Lord and fasting**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **leitourgounton** — from which *liturgy*. It is the Septuagint word for priests serving at the altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Applied here to five men in a room, and Luke uses no other word for what they were doing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Holy Spirit said, \"**Set apart for Me Barnabas and Saul, for the work I have called them to**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **aphorisate** — separate, mark off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same root Paul uses of himself in Romans: *called to be an apostle, **separated** to the gospel of God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the work I have called them to* — the calling had already happened. This is the release of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is sent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two best men they had."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Barnabas is the reason the Antioch church exists — Jerusalem had sent him to look into it. Saul is the one Barnabas fetched."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The church gives away its leadership."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So after they had **fasted and prayed and laid their hands on them, they sent them off**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three actions, and then the release."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apelusan** — dismissed, let go."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who actually sent them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *so, **being sent out by the Holy Spirit**, they went down to Seleucia*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says it twice, two ways. The church laid hands on them, and the Spirit sent them. See the note on Acts 13:4–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes out of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three missionary journeys. Cyprus, Galatia, Macedonia, Achaia, Asia. The letters to the Galatians, Thessalonians, Corinthians, Romans, Ephesians, Philippians, Colossians."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything after this in the New Testament, more or less, traces back to five men fasting in Antioch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they report back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now when they had come and gathered the church together, they **reported all that God had done with them**, and that He had opened the door of faith to the Gentiles.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They come home and tell them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who was in the room.** A Levite, an African, a Libyan, a king's foster-brother, and the man who had been arresting Christians. Luke names all five before anything happens."}]}, "Acts 13:13–15": {"title": "\"If you have any word of encouragement\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses of travel notes, and two things in them matter later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The change of name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Paul and his companions set sail from Paphos.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke has said *Barnabas and Saul* through the whole account until Cyprus. From here on it is **Paul**, and Barnabas comes second."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The switch happens in the verse about the sorcerer — *then Saul, who is also called Paul* — and the order of the names never goes back. See the note on Acts 13:9–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The departure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There **John left them and went back to Jerusalem**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John Mark. Luke gives no reason."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It becomes the sharpest recorded disagreement between two believers in the New Testament: at the start of the second journey Barnabas wants to take him and Paul refuses, *because he had departed from them in Pamphylia and had not gone with them to the work*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the contention became so sharp that they parted from one another.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Barnabas takes Mark to Cyprus; Paul takes Silas through Syria."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Decades later, in Paul's last letter: *Get Mark and bring him with you, for **he is useful to me for ministry**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Mark writes a Gospel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The synagogue"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the Sabbath day they **went into the synagogue and sat down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's method throughout Acts. He goes to the synagogue first in every city, and he says why in this very chapter: *it was necessary that the word of God be spoken to you first.* See the note on Acts 13:44–47."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of service"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After **the reading of the law and the prophets**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two readings — a portion from the Torah and one from the Prophets. The pattern is the same one Luke describes in Nazareth, where Jesus was handed Isaiah. See the note on Luke 4:16–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The invitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The rulers of the synagogue sent word to them: \"**Brothers, if you have any word of encouragement for the people, please speak**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **logos parakleseos** — a word of exhortation, of comfort. It was the standard term for the sermon after the readings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Visiting teachers were routinely asked. Nobody in the room has any idea what they are about to hear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul stands, motions with his hand, and delivers the longest of his recorded sermons — Israel's history from Egypt to David, then the resurrection, then the two sentences the whole thing was built toward. See the note on Acts 13:38–39."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the invitation cost them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the next sabbath nearly the whole city was outside the building, the leaders were contradicting him, and Paul and Barnabas were expelled from the district."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How it starts.** A polite formula at the end of a normal service — *if you have any word of encouragement* — offered to two travellers who had just sat down at the back."}]}, "Acts 13:20–22": {"title": "A man after My own heart", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's first recorded sermon, and he tells the story before he makes the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The synagogue at Antioch in Pisidia, on the first missionary journey, invited to speak after the readings: *men and brethren, if you have any word of exhortation for the people, say on*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He starts with the exodus and works forward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The judges"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And after that, for about **four hundred and fifty years**, He gave them judges, **until Samuel the prophet**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The period from Joshua's death to Samuel — Othniel, Ehud, Deborah, Gideon, Jephthah, Samson, and the rest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book's refrain: *in those days there was no king in Israel; everyone did what was right in his own eyes*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then **they asked for a king**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's phrasing puts the initiative with them, which is how Samuel records it: *now make us a king to judge us like all the nations*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And God's reading of it: *they have not rejected you, but **they have rejected Me**, that I should not reign over them*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And God gave them **Saul son of Kish, a man of the tribe of Benjamin, for forty years**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was what they had asked for: *from his shoulders upward he was taller than any of the people*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They wanted a king like the nations, and they got the best-looking man in the country."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The removal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When He had **removed him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **metastesas** — set aside, deposed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul's disqualification is dated to Gilgal, where he could not wait seven days for Samuel. See the note on 1 Samuel 13:11–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You have acted foolishly… but now your kingdom shall not continue. **The LORD has sought for Himself a man after His own heart**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The successor"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He **raised up David** to be their king.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The testimonial"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have found David son of Jesse, a man after My own heart, who will do everything I want done**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is quoting, and he is quoting two places at once."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD has sought for Himself a man after His own heart* — Samuel, at Gilgal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have found My servant David* — Psalm 89."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He splices them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the phrase means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that David was better behaved. The record includes adultery, an arranged killing, and a census that cost seventy thousand lives."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul's disqualifying sins look smaller: an offering made early, and sparing an enemy king and the best of the livestock."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The difference the narrative draws is in what each man did afterwards."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul: *I have sinned; yet **honour me now, please, before the elders of my people***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David: *against You, You only, have I sinned* — and Psalm 51."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the phrase describes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who wanted what God wanted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *a man according to His heart*, and the clause Paul adds spells it out: *who will do all My will*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the sermon goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**From this man's seed, according to the promise, God raised up for Israel a Saviour — Jesus**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything before this is the run-up. Paul spends fifteen verses on the history to land on one descendant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Therefore let it be known to you that **through this Man is preached to you the forgiveness of sins**; and by Him everyone who believes is justified from all things from which you could not be justified by the law of Moses.\"* See the note on Acts 13:38–39."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the next Sabbath almost the whole city came together to hear the word of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the opposition, and *we turn to the Gentiles*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the title is not.** Not a verdict on conduct. The man it was taken from lost the throne over an early sacrifice; the man it was given to committed adultery and arranged a killing."}]}, "Acts 13:34–37": {"title": "\"Served God's purpose in his own generation\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is preaching in a synagogue in Antioch, and he uses David to make an argument that turns on a single observable fact."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm says *You will not let Your Holy One see decay*. Paul's point is simple and almost forensic:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["David said it", "David died, was buried, and **did see decay** — his tomb was a known location", "therefore he was not talking about himself"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the One God raised up saw no decay at all.* The psalm had to be about somebody else, and Paul says who."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line about David"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After he had served God's purpose in his own generation, [he] fell asleep.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a remarkable summary of a life that filled several books. Not \"after he had won his wars\" or \"after he had built the kingdom.\" He served God's purpose **in his own generation** — the phrase locates the whole thing inside a lifetime, with a start and a finish, and treats the finish as unremarkable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David is not diminished by it. He is placed: a man who did what was his to do, in the years he had, and then slept."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two halves do together.** Paul's whole case rests on the difference — David's work ended and his body decayed; the other did not. But the way he says it about David is not dismissive. Serving God's purpose in your own generation is offered as a good and sufficient description of a life, by a man arguing that only one person ever escaped the ending."}]}, "Acts 13:38–39": {"title": "\"Everything the law could never set you right from\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The conclusion of Paul's first recorded sermon, and it is the sentence the rest of his letters unpack."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Antioch of Pisidia, in a synagogue, after the reading of the law and the prophets, at the invitation of the rulers. See the note on Acts 13:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just walked them through Israel's history — Egypt, the wilderness, the judges, Samuel, Saul, David — and arrived at the resurrection with three quotations from the Psalms and Isaiah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So let it be known to you, **brothers**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is speaking to Jews and God-fearing Gentiles in their own building, on their own sabbath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is **through this One that forgiveness of sins is announced to you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kataggelletai** — proclaimed, announced publicly. It is a herald's word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not offered on terms. Announced."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And by Him **everyone who believes is set right from everything the law of Moses could never set you right from**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is **dikaioutai** — justified, declared in the right. It is the word the whole of Romans and Galatians turns on, and this is the first time Paul is recorded using it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence has two halves and the second one is a concession about the first system."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say the law justified nobody. He says there were things it could not reach, and that from **everything** it could not reach, everyone who believes is set right."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Everyone who believes\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Pas ho pisteuon** — the same construction he will use in Romans: *the righteousness of God through faith in Jesus Christ, to all and on all **who believe**. For there is no difference.* See the note on Romans 3:21–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Beware therefore, lest what has been spoken in the prophets come upon you: '**Behold, you despisers, marvel and perish!** For I work a work in your days, a work which you will by no means believe, though one were to declare it to you.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes Habakkuk, and he ends the sermon on a warning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the Jews had gone out of the synagogue, **the Gentiles begged them to speak these same words to them the next Sabbath**.* See the note on Acts 13:42–43."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the following week nearly the whole city turned up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul urged them to do in the meantime"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Talked with them and **urged them to continue in the grace of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is Luke's summary of the follow-up conversation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same argument goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Galatians, to churches Paul planted on this same journey: *knowing that a man is not justified by the works of the law but by faith in Jesus Christ… for **by the works of the law no flesh shall be justified**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same verb, same conclusion, written to the region he was standing in."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is announced and what is not.** Forgiveness is proclaimed, not offered on conditions. And the justifying is stated as covering the ground the older system never could — which is the whole of Paul's later argument, in one sentence, in a synagogue."}]}, "Acts 13:4–8": {"title": "Sent out, and the first thing they meet", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first missionary journey begins, and the opposition is waiting at the far end of the island."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who sent them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sent out, then, **by the Holy Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says it twice in four verses. The church at Antioch had fasted and laid hands on them and *sent them away* — and then Luke corrects the record: it was the Spirit who did the sending."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction had been *separate to Me Barnabas and Saul for the work to which I have called them*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The route"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The two of them went down to **Seleucia**, and from there **sailed to Cyprus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seleucia is Antioch's port, sixteen miles downriver."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cyprus is Barnabas' home. Luke has already told us: *Joses, who was also named Barnabas by the apostles… a Levite of the country of Cyprus*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first stop on the first journey is the senior partner's own island."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The method"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When they reached Salamis they announced the word of God **in the Jewish synagogues**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pattern for the rest of Acts. Paul goes to the synagogue first in every city, and usually gets thrown out of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they had **John with them as their helper**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John Mark. The Greek is **huperetes** — an assistant, an under-rower."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He leaves them at Perga a few verses later, and the argument about whether to take him again splits Paul and Barnabas so badly that they part company."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is with Paul at the end, though: *get Mark and bring him with you, for he is useful to me for ministry*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crossing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They traveled **the whole length of the island** as far as **Paphos**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundred miles, east to west. Paphos is the Roman capital and the seat of the governor."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A certain **sorcerer, a false prophet, a Jewish man named Bar-Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **magos** — a magician, the same word as the wise men in Matthew."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Bar-Jesus** — son of Joshua, son of salvation. A good name on a bad man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he was employed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was **attached to the proconsul, Sergius Paulus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Roman governor with a court astrologer. Not unusual; Tiberius kept one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The governor"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man of **real intelligence**, who **sent for Barnabas and Saul because he wanted to hear the word of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sunetos** — intelligent, discerning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke records his rank and his character, and the invitation comes from him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interference"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **Elymas the sorcerer — that is what his name means — kept working against them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An imperfect: he kept on doing it. And the Greek is **anthistato** — stood against, resisted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Trying to **turn the proconsul away from the faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one man with access to the governor is spending it on keeping him from a hearing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul — *who is also called Paul* — looks at him and calls him *son of the devil, enemy of all righteousness*, and he goes blind *for a time*. See the note on Acts 13:9–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke switches from *Saul* to *Paul* in the middle of this scene and never goes back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Barnabas and Saul becomes Paul and his party for the rest of the book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the proconsul believed, when he saw what had been done, being astonished at the teaching of the Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first named convert of the first missionary journey is a Roman governor."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bar-Jesus.* Luke puts *son of Jesus* and *enemy of all righteousness* in the same paragraph, and does not comment."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was standing between the governor and the sermon.** His own adviser — a man whose name meant son of salvation, working full time to prevent one."}]}, "Acts 13:42–43": {"title": "\"Continue in the grace of God\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The immediate reaction to Paul's first sermon, and the instruction he gives afterward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who asks for more"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the Jews had gone out of the synagogue, **the Gentiles begged them to speak these same words to them the next Sabbath**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parekaloun** — begged, urgently requested."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**These same words.** Not more, and not something adapted. The same ones."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is asking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Gentiles in the building — God-fearers who attended synagogue without becoming proselytes. Paul had addressed them directly in the sermon: *men of Israel, **and you who fear God***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been sitting at the edge of Israel's worship for years, and the sentence about being *set right from everything the law of Moses could never set you right from* was addressed to them too. See the note on Acts 13:38–39."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who followed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when the gathering broke up, **many of the Jews and of the devout converts to Judaism followed Paul and Barnabas**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both groups. Luke is careful to record that Jews responded as well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Devout converts** — the Greek is **proselytoi**, full converts who had taken on circumcision and the law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conversation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who **talked with them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **proslalountes** — kept talking to them. It continued after the service."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **urged them to continue in the grace of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epimenein te chariti tou theou** — to remain in, to stay put in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is Luke's one-line summary of the follow-up teaching, and it is worth noticing what it is not. Not *be careful now*, not a list of next steps."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Continue** — the same verb Paul uses in Colossians: *if indeed you continue in the faith, grounded and steadfast.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word Luke uses for the message"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Charis** — grace. Luke uses it of the message repeatedly in Acts: *the word of His grace*, *the gospel of the grace of God*, and the church at Antioch commending Barnabas and Paul *to the grace of God for the work*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's own summary of his commission, to the Ephesian elders, uses the same phrase: *the ministry I received from the Lord Jesus — to testify to **the good news of the grace of God**.* See the note on Acts 20:22–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The following Sabbath **nearly the whole city gathered** to hear the word of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And with the crowd came the opposition. See the note on Acts 13:44–47."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke records the same shape in city after city: a synagogue, a hearing, a large response, and then trouble from the same building."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they were told to do.** Not to add anything, and not to guard against the message. To stay in it — and Luke gives that as the whole of the follow-up instruction."}]}, "Acts 13:44–47": {"title": "\"Nearly the whole city\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second sabbath in Antioch of Pisidia, and the crowd is the problem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turnout"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The following Sabbath **nearly the whole city gathered to hear the word of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One sermon, one week's word of mouth. See the note on Acts 13:42–43."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **when the Jews saw the crowds, they were filled with jealousy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **zelou** — zeal, jealousy. The trigger Luke names is the size of the crowd, not the content."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had invited him to speak the week before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they **contradicted what Paul was saying and spoke against it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **antelegon** — spoke against — and **blasphemountes**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Paul and Barnabas **spoke out boldly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parresiasamenoi** — from **parrhesia**, freedom of speech, the right to say anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It was **necessary that the word of God be spoken to you first**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul states the priority as an obligation. He does it in every city — synagogue first — and he writes it into Romans: *to the Jew first and also to the Greek.* See the note on Romans 1:16–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The judgment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But since you **push it away and judge yourselves unworthy of eternal life**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *push away* is **apotheisthe** — to thrust from oneself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Judge yourselves unworthy.** Paul does not pronounce them unworthy. He says the verdict is self-issued, and the word is **krinete** — you judge."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look — **we are turning to the Gentiles**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The authority"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For this is what the Lord has commanded us: '**I have made you a light for the nations, so that you may carry salvation to the ends of the earth**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 49, spoken to the Servant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul applies it to himself and Barnabas — *the Lord has commanded **us***. He reads the Servant's commission as one he is inside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simeon had quoted the same passage over the infant in the temple: *a light to bring revelation to the Gentiles, and the glory of Your people Israel.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now when the Gentiles heard this, **they were glad and glorified the word of the Lord**. And **the word of the Lord was being spread throughout all the region**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the Jews stirred up the devout and prominent women and the chief men of the city, raised up persecution against Paul and Barnabas, **and expelled them from their region**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But they shook off the dust from their feet against them, and came to Iconium. **And the disciples were filled with joy and with the Holy Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke ends the episode on the disciples' joy rather than on the expulsion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It repeats through Acts — Iconium, Lystra, Thessalonica, Berea, Corinth. Paul says it again at Corinth: *your blood be upon your own heads; I am clean. From now on I will go to the Gentiles.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And he still goes to the synagogue in the next city."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What turned them.** Not the sermon — they had asked for it and heard it out. Luke names the crowd. The following week nearly the whole city came, and that is the verse the jealousy is attached to."}]}, "Acts 13:9–12": {"title": "Cyprus and the sorcerer", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first miracle of Paul's ministry is a judgment, and it happens in front of a Roman governor."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The change of name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **Saul, who is also called Paul**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke has used *Saul* since chapter 7. From this verse on it is Paul, for the rest of the book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both were his from birth — a Hebrew name and a Roman one, which was ordinary for a Jew with citizenship. Luke switches at the point the mission turns toward the Gentile world."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And from here the pair is *Paul and Barnabas* rather than *Barnabas and Saul*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is opposing him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Elymas** the sorcerer, also called **Bar-Jesus** — son of Joshua. Luke calls him *a Jewish false prophet*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is attached to the household of Sergius Paulus, the proconsul, who had sent for Barnabas and Saul because he *sought to hear the word of God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elymas *withstood them, seeking to turn the proconsul away from the faith*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The look"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Filled with the Holy Spirit, **looked straight at him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **atenisas** — to gaze intently, to fix the eyes. Luke uses it repeatedly: Stephen looking into heaven, the disciples watching the ascension, the crowd staring at Peter and John."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You are **full of every kind of deceit and every kind of trickery**. You are **a son of the devil**, an **enemy of everything that is right**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *son of the devil* is **huie diabolou** — set against his own name, Bar-Jesus, son of Joshua."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Will you never stop **twisting the straight paths of the Lord**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *making crooked the straight ways*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is from Isaiah, quoted of John the Baptist: *make His paths straight*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look now — **the hand of the Lord is on you. You will be blind, unable even to see the sun, for a time**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**For a time** — the Greek is **achri kairou**, until a season. The blindness is given an end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had been struck blind himself on the Damascus road, and had spent three days without sight before Ananias came. See the note on Acts 9:3–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He pronounces on another man the thing that had happened to him, and includes the time limit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What followed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Immediately **a mist and a darkness came over him**, and he **went groping about, looking for someone to lead him by the hand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *mist* is **achlus**, a medical term for a clouding of the eye. Luke the physician uses it once."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Looking for someone to lead him by the hand* — the Greek is **cheiragogous**, hand-leaders. It is the same word used of Paul being led into Damascus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the proconsul saw what had happened, **he believed, astonished at the teaching of the Lord**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says he was astonished at the **teaching**, not at the blindness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sergius Paulus is described as *an intelligent man*, and an inscription naming a Sergius Paulus has been found on Cyprus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they go next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Straight to the mainland, where John Mark leaves them and Paul preaches in a synagogue in Antioch of Pisidia. See the note on Acts 13:13–15."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is on the page.** A false prophet with a Jewish name blocking a Roman's hearing, and the first thing recorded of Paul under that name is a temporary blindness — the same one he was given himself."}]}, "Acts 14:19–20": {"title": "He got up and went back in", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and the second one is harder to believe than the first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man crippled from birth had stood up at Paul's word, the crowd had decided Paul and Barnabas were Hermes and Zeus, and the priest of Zeus had brought oxen and garlands to the gates. See the note on Acts 14:8–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had torn his clothes and run in among them shouting *we also are men with the same nature as you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **Jews came from Antioch and Iconium** and won the crowds over.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They travelled to do it. Antioch of Pisidia is about a hundred miles away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **peisantes tous ochlous** — having persuaded the crowds."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same crowd. That morning they wanted to sacrifice to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stoning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they **stoned Paul and dragged him outside the city**, thinking he was dead.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stoning was the Jewish method of execution, and it was thorough — the intention is to kill."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Thinking he was dead* — the Greek is **nomizontes auton tethnekenai**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke does not say Paul was dead, and does not say he was not. He reports what the crowd concluded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they left him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Outside the city, which is where bodies went."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disciples"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But when **the disciples gathered around him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There were disciples in Lystra already. Timothy was from there, and he may well be standing in this circle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is recorded of what they did. They stood round him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **got up and went back into the city**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anastas eiselthen** — having risen, he entered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Back through the same gate, past the same people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The next morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the next day he left with Barnabas for Derbe**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A day's walk, sixty miles, the day after being stoned."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does after Derbe"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Comes back. Through Lystra, Iconium and Antioch — the three towns that had just tried to kill him — *strengthening the souls of the disciples, exhorting them to continue in the faith, and saying, \"**We must through many tribulations enter the kingdom of God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He walks back into all three."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he wrote about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From the Jews five times I received forty stripes minus one. Three times I was beaten with rods, **once I was stoned**, three times I was shipwrecked; a night and a day I have been in the deep.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One line in a list."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And to Timothy, at the end: *persecutions, afflictions, which happened to me at Antioch, at Iconium, at Lystra — what persecutions I endured. **And out of them all the Lord delivered me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Those are the three towns, named in order, in a letter to a man from the third one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other passage sometimes attached"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I know a man in Christ who fourteen years ago… was caught up to the third heaven… and heard inaccessible words.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some have connected it to this day, and Paul does not say."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Call it a miracle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He records what happened, in the order it happened, and moves on to the next town."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The sentence Luke writes flat.** *He got up and went back into the city.* Not out of the region, not to recover — back through the gate he had been dragged out of."}]}, "Acts 14:8–10": {"title": "The man who had never walked", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is preaching, and something in the audience catches his eye."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Lystra**, in the interior of what is now Turkey. A Roman colony with no synagogue mentioned — Paul and Barnabas had just been run out of Iconium and had come here on foot."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Timothy is from Lystra. He may well be in this crowd."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At Lystra there was a man **sitting who had no strength in his feet**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **adunatos tois posin** — powerless in the feet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He had **never walked in his life**; he had been **unable to since birth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke states it three ways in one sentence: no strength, never walked, from birth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke is a physician and he takes the case history first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parallel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same three-part description appears at the temple gate in chapter 3 — *a certain man lame from his mother's womb was carried, whom they laid daily at the gate*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's miracle and Paul's are told in matching language, which is one of the things Acts does deliberately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **listened while Paul was speaking**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is an imperfect — he kept listening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing else is recorded of him. He is in the crowd, sitting, hearing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Paul **looked closely at him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **atenisas** — gazed intently, fixed his eyes on. Luke's word, used fourteen times in Acts and twice elsewhere in the whole New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stephen uses it looking into heaven; the crowd uses it staring at Peter and John."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **seeing that he had faith to be made well**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **echei pistin tou sothenai** — has faith to be saved, or healed. **Sozo** does both jobs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing was said. The man had not asked, and Luke does not record him calling out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Faith arrived while a man sat listening — which is exactly what Paul writes elsewhere: *faith comes by hearing, and hearing by the word of God*. See the note on Romans 10:16–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He said **in a loud voice**, \"**Stand up straight on your feet**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anastethi orthos** — stand upright."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said loudly, in the middle of a sermon, to a man everybody in Lystra knew could not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the man **jumped up and began to walk**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hellato** — leaped. And then an imperfect: he kept walking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah's line: *then the lame shall leap like a deer*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd decides they are gods. *The gods have come down to us in the likeness of men!* — Barnabas is Zeus and Paul is Hermes *because he was the chief speaker*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priest of Zeus brings oxen and garlands to the gates."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ovid records a local legend that Zeus and Hermes had once visited this region disguised and been turned away by everyone but an old couple. Lystra was not going to make that mistake twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Tears his clothes and runs in among them: *why are you doing these things? **We also are men with the same nature as you***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the shortest summary of natural revelation in Acts: *He did not leave Himself without witness, in that He did good, gave us rain from heaven and fruitful seasons, filling our hearts with food and gladness.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the day ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Jews from Antioch and Iconium came there; and having persuaded the multitudes, **they stoned Paul and dragged him out of the city, supposing him to be dead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same crowd, the same afternoon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*However, when the disciples gathered around him, he rose up and went into the city.* And the next day he walked to Derbe."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He comes back through Lystra on the way home, *strengthening the souls of the disciples*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul was looking for while he preached.** Not attention. Luke says he fixed his eyes on a man and saw that he had faith to be healed — before a word had been said by anybody but Paul."}]}, "Acts 15:10–12": {"title": "A yoke nobody could carry", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most consequential meeting in church history, and Peter settles it with two sentences."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being argued"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Certain men came down from Judea and taught the brethren, \"**Unless you are circumcised according to the custom of Moses, you cannot be saved**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question is whether Gentile believers have to become Jews."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul and Barnabas had *no small dissension and dispute with them*, and the matter went to Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The debate"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when there had been much dispute, Peter rose up.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He reminds them of Cornelius' house: *God, who knows the heart, acknowledged them by giving them the Holy Spirit, just as He did to us, and made no distinction between us and them, **purifying their hearts by faith***. See the note on Acts 10:44–48."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is arguing from something that happened to him and that he had not planned."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So why are you now **testing God** by laying a yoke on the neck of the disciples.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **peirazete** — putting God to the test."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The yoke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That **neither our fathers nor we have been able to carry**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ischusamen bastasai** — were strong enough to bear."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says it about his own nation and his own generation, in Jerusalem, to the Jerusalem church."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that the law is bad. That nobody has ever carried it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the same to the Galatians: *stand fast therefore in the liberty by which Christ has made us free, and do not be entangled again with a yoke of bondage*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the alternative: *take My yoke upon you… for My yoke is easy and My burden is light*. See the note on Matthew 11:28–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"No — we believe that **we shall be saved through the grace of the Lord Jesus Christ, even as they**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kath' hon tropon kakeinoi** — in the same manner as they also."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The direction is the striking part. The argument in the room was whether Gentiles could be saved like Jews. Peter reverses it: we are saved the way they are."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the whole assembly fell silent**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **esigesen** — became silent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An argumentative meeting, and it stops."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reports"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they listened to **Barnabas and Paul telling all the signs and wonders God had done through them among the Gentiles**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Evidence, not argument. Two men describing what had happened on the first missionary journey."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke names Barnabas first here, which he does when they are in Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What James does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Quotes Amos. *After this I will return and rebuild the fallen tent of David… so that the rest of humanity may seek the Lord.* See the note on Acts 15:13–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The decision"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Therefore I judge that **we should not trouble those from among the Gentiles who are turning to God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four things are asked of them — abstain from things offered to idols, from blood, from things strangled, and from sexual immorality — and circumcision is not one of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It seemed good to the Holy Spirit, and to us, **to lay upon you no greater burden than these necessary things**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the response in Antioch: *when they had read it, **they rejoiced over its encouragement**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was at stake"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If the meeting had gone the other way, the church would have remained a sect within Judaism and every Gentile convert would have had to become a proselyte first."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's contribution is the sentence about the yoke, and it is an argument from experience: our fathers could not carry it either."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Which way round he puts it.** The room was asking whether Gentiles could be saved like Jews. Peter says *we shall be saved… even as they*."}]}, "Acts 15:13–18": {"title": "The fallen tent of David", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "James settles the council by quoting a prophet, and the choice of text is the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*James answered.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Lord's brother, who had not believed during the ministry — *for even His brothers did not believe in Him* — and is now presiding over the Jerusalem church."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul lists a resurrection appearance to him: *after that He was seen by James*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Brothers, listen to me**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The summary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Simeon** has described how God **first visited the Gentiles to take from them a people for His name**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the Hebrew form of Peter's name — **Symeon** — which he does nowhere else in Acts. He is speaking to a Jewish assembly and marking Peter as one of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Visited** — the Greek is **epeskepsato**, the word for God coming to look after somebody. It is in Zacharias' song: *the Lord God of Israel has visited and redeemed His people*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The test he applies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **the words of the prophets agree with this**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the other way round. James does not start with the text and deduce the policy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something happened at Cornelius' house; he checks it against the prophets and finds it fits."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**After this I will return and rebuild the fallen tent of David. I will rebuild its ruins and set it upright again.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amos 9:11. See the note on Amos 9:11–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **skenen** — tent, tabernacle. Not **oikos**, house."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the tent and not the temple"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophecy does not say God will rebuild the house of David, which would be about a dynasty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It says the **tent** — and there was a specific one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David pitched a tent in Jerusalem for the ark, with no veil, singers around it continuously, while the tabernacle of Moses stood at Gibeon six miles away with the altar and the priests. See the note on 2 Samuel 6:12–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For roughly forty years the ark sat in the open under canvas in the capital, and anyone could approach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence in the text"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So that the rest of humanity may seek the Lord — all the nations who are called by My name**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the clause James is quoting for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rebuilt tent is not for Israel's sake in Amos' sentence. It has a purpose attached, and the purpose is the nations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Hebrew and the Greek"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amos in Hebrew has *that they may possess the remnant of **Edom**, and all the Gentiles who are called by My name*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek Old Testament has *that the rest of **men** may seek the Lord*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Edom** and **adam** differ by vowel points, which were not written. And *possess* and *seek* differ by one consonant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James quotes the Greek, and the New Testament writers generally do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The signature"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Says the Lord, who does all these things. **Known to God from eternity are all His works**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The judgment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Therefore I judge that we should **not trouble those from among the Gentiles who are turning to God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four requests are made of them, and circumcision is not among them. See the note on Acts 15:10–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the tent had that the temple did not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No veil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tabernacle at Gibeon had one, and behind it nothing — the ark was in Jerusalem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David's tent had the ark, uncovered, with music round it, and no barrier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other torn veil"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And behold, the veil of the temple was torn in two from top to bottom.* See the note on Matthew 27:45–47."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Which building the prophecy names.** Not the house of David. The *tent* — and there had been one, in Jerusalem, with the ark in it and nothing in front of it."}]}, "Acts 15:6–9": {"title": "\"Cleansing their hearts by faith\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first church council, and the first speech is Peter's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being decided"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Men had come down from Judea to Antioch teaching *unless you are circumcised according to the custom of Moses, you cannot be saved.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul and Barnabas had *no small dissension and dispute* with them, and the matter was sent up to Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The procedure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The apostles and elders **came together to look into the matter**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both groups. It is a deliberation, not a ruling handed down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The debate"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After **a long discussion**, Peter stood up.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *much disputing*. Luke does not summarise it, and he does not pretend it was brief or comfortable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter appeals to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Brothers, you know that **in the early days God made a choice among us**: that **by my mouth the Gentiles should hear the message** of the good news and believe.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Cornelius episode, roughly ten years earlier. He had already had to explain it once in Jerusalem. See the note on Acts 11:15–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The choosing is God's, and Peter describes himself as the mouth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The witness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **God, who knows the heart**, testified for them **by giving them the Holy Spirit just as He gave Him to us**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *who knows the heart* is **kardiognostes** — heart-knower. It appears twice in the New Testament, both times in Acts, both times about God choosing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The evidence Peter brings is not an argument. It is something God did, and he says God is the one who testified."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Just as He gave Him to us\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is exact. Peter does not allow a two-tier arrangement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distinction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He made no distinction at all between us and them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *he discriminated nothing between*. It is the same verb Peter used of himself in chapter 11 — *the Spirit told me to go with them, **doubting nothing**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cleansing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Cleansing their hearts by faith**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katharisas** — purified. It is the ritual word, the one for making something fit to approach God with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things in the clause: what was cleansed, and how."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Their hearts** — not their diet, their calendar, or their bodies. The dispute was about an external mark, and Peter answers about an internal cleaning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**By faith** — the mechanism, stated in two words, with nothing else in the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now therefore, **why do you test God** by putting a yoke on the neck of the disciples **which neither our fathers nor we were able to bear?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the sentence the council turns on:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But we believe that **through the grace of the Lord Jesus Christ we shall be saved in the same manner as they**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is reversed. Not *they are saved like us*. **We** are saved like **them**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then all the multitude kept silent and listened to Barnabas and Paul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James then speaks, quotes Amos, and the letter goes out to Antioch with four requests and no circumcision."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What settles it.** Not a text and not a precedent. Peter reports that God gave the same Spirit and cleansed the same organ by the same means — and the room goes quiet."}]}, "Acts 16:14–15": {"title": "\"The Lord opened her heart\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first convert in Europe is a businesswoman at a prayer meeting by a river."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who she was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A woman named **Lydia**, a **seller of purple cloth** from the city of Thyatira, and she **worshipped God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Purple dye was the most expensive commodity in the ancient world — extracted a drop at a time from a shellfish, and reserved by law in some places for royalty. A dealer in it was a wealthy woman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is from Thyatira in Asia Minor, and she is in Philippi, running a business abroad."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they were at a river"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Philippi had no synagogue. Ten Jewish men were required to form one, and the city evidently did not have them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So Paul goes down to the riverside on the Sabbath *where prayer was customarily made*, and finds a group of women."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gospel enters Europe at an informal women's prayer meeting outside the city gate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She was listening, and **the Lord opened her heart** to pay attention to what Paul was saying.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things at once, and Luke puts them side by side."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She was listening — that is her. The Lord opened her heart — that is Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *opened* is the word used of opening a door, and elsewhere in Luke of opening the Scriptures on the Emmaus road and of the disciples' eyes being opened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul preached. Somebody else did the opening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her household"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When **she and her household** had been baptised.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not just her. The Greek is her house — family, servants, dependents. The whole establishment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase turns up at the end of the same chapter, over the jailer: *believe on the Lord Jesus Christ, and you will be saved — you and your household.* See the note on Acts 16:29–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two households in one chapter, at opposite ends of Philippi's social order: a purple merchant and a prison officer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she did with her house"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If you have judged me faithful** to the Lord, come into my house and stay.\" And **she prevailed on us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She makes it a question of whether they think she is genuine, which is not a request anyone can easily refuse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *prevailed* is to compel, to force — the same verb the Emmaus two used on Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What her house became"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The church at Philippi. Paul's warmest letter is addressed to it, and the last lines of this chapter have him and Silas going back to *the house of Lydia* after the earthquake, to encourage the brethren, before leaving the city."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the money went.** Philippi is the church that sent Paul support again and again — *even in Thessalonica you sent aid once and again for my necessities* — and the one he thanks in Philippians for a gift carried by Epaphroditus. The church that funded him started in a wealthy woman's front room."}]}, "Acts 16:29–31": {"title": "\"You and your household\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A prison officer at midnight, with a sword already in his hand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had done that day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was the man who took Paul and Silas after the beating, *put them into the inner prison, and fastened their feet in the stocks.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The inner prison is the deepest cell. The stocks were a punishment device as much as a restraint — the legs forced apart and held."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did that to two men whose backs had just been laid open with rods, and then went to bed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What woke him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At midnight Paul and Silas were praying and singing hymns to God, and the prisoners were listening to them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the earthquake, the doors open, everyone's chains loose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He drew his sword to kill himself, because under Roman practice a jailer answered with his own life for escaped prisoners. Paul stopped him: *do yourself no harm, for we are all here.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody ran. That is the detail that brings him in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Trembling, he **fell down before Paul and Silas**… \"Sirs, **what must I do to be saved?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is on the floor in front of two men he had put in the stocks a few hours earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Believe on the Lord Jesus Christ, and you will be saved** — you and your household.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight words to the question. No conditions, no probation, no list."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *make restitution*. Not *release us first*. Not *stop being a jailer*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"And your household\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase used over Lydia at the start of the chapter. See the note on Acts 16:14–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse says they *spoke the word of the Lord to him and to all who were in his house* — the household hears it too, and then *he and all his family were baptised immediately*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did before the night was over"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took them the same hour of the night and **washed their stripes**… and he set food before them, and **rejoiced, believing in God with all his household**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He washed the wounds he had caused, in the dark, and then fed them at his own table."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke puts the washing and the baptism in the same sentence: *he washed their stripes, and immediately he and all his family were baptised.* Water both ways, in the same hour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two households, one chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A wealthy purple merchant at a riverside prayer meeting, and a Roman jailer at midnight. The top and the bottom of that city, in one chapter, and both times Luke says *and her household*, *and his household*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Philippi cost them.** Beaten with rods, illegally — they were Roman citizens, and Paul says so the next morning. The magistrates who ordered it come down in person to ask them to leave quietly. Paul goes back to Lydia's house first."}]}, "Acts 16:32–34": {"title": "He washed their wounds", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The jailer's night, told in the order things happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What had happened first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul and Silas had been *beaten with rods*, thrown into the inner prison and *fastened in the stocks*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But at midnight Paul and Silas were praying and singing hymns to God, and the prisoners were listening to them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the earthquake, the doors open, the chains loose — and the jailer drawing his sword, because a Roman guard who lost his prisoners took their sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do yourself no harm, for **we are all here**.\"* Nobody had run."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question and the answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Sirs, **what must I do to be saved?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Believe on the Lord Jesus Christ, and you will be saved, you and your household**.\"* See the note on Acts 16:29–31."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The teaching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they **spoke the word of the Lord to him and to everyone in his house**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The household is included in the sentence, and then in the teaching, and then in everything that follows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That **same hour of the night**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Still the middle of the night. Nothing waits for morning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took them and **washed their wounds**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **elousen apo ton plegon** — washed them from the stripes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the man responsible for those stripes, or at least for the men in the cell after them. Luke does not remark on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was done to him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **he was baptized at once, he and all his family**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two washings in one sentence, in opposite directions. He washes them, and then he is washed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke sets the two verbs a few words apart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The meal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he **brought them up into his house and set food before them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Prisoners at the jailer's table, in the jailer's house, before dawn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The state he was in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he was **full of joy, along with his whole household, at having believed in God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **egalliasato** — exulted, rejoiced greatly. And **panoiki** — with all his house, a word that occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The joy is his, and the whole house is in the clause with him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens in the morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The magistrates send word to release them quietly, and Paul refuses to go."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They have beaten us openly, uncondemned Romans, and have thrown us into prison. And now do they put us out secretly? **No indeed! Let them come themselves and get us out**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said nothing about his citizenship the night before, while they were being beaten."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The magistrates come in person and ask them to leave the city."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the church at Philippi was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lydia, a dealer in purple from Thyatira, whose *heart the Lord opened*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A slave girl with a spirit of divination, whose owners started the riot when the income stopped."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a Roman jailer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three converts in one chapter, and no two of them share a language, a class or a country."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul writes to them later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I thank my God upon every remembrance of you… for **your fellowship in the gospel from the first day until now**.* See the note on Philippians 1:3–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *rejoice in the Lord always. Again I will say, rejoice.* Written from another prison, to a church founded in one. See the note on Philippians 4:4–5."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of the sentence.** He washed their wounds, and then he was washed. Luke puts both verbs in the same breath and lets them sit there."}]}, "Acts 16:6–8": {"title": "Guidance that arrives as a closed door", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three sentences, and almost nothing happens in them. Paul tries to go one way and cannot. He tries another and cannot. He ends up somewhere he was not aiming for. It is one of the most useful passages in the New Testament about being led, precisely because so little of it looks like leading."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Spirit actually does here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the verbs:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**kept** from speaking the word in Asia", "**would not allow** them into Bithynia", "so they **passed by** Mysia and came down to Troas"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a word of positive instruction. No voice, no vision, no verse. The Spirit's whole contribution in these three sentences is to stop them, twice, and the narrative does not say how — Luke, who elsewhere reports angels and trances and voices in detail, does not tell us."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were being stopped from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the part worth sitting with. They were not being stopped from sin. They were being stopped from **preaching the gospel in Asia**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There was nothing wrong with the plan. It was a good plan, made by good men, for the best possible reason. It was simply not the one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That should adjust how a closed door gets read. A blocked way is not proof that the intention was bad, and the passage never suggests Paul had made a mistake in wanting to go."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Troas is not a destination in this story; it is where you end up when you have run out of directions. And it is there — after two refusals and a journey that looks like wandering — that the vision of the man from Macedonia comes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order matters. The clear guidance arrives *last*, and it arrives to men who had kept moving while they only had the unclear kind. If they had stopped at the first refusal and waited for something positive, they would not have been at Troas to receive it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does not license"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It would be easy to turn this into a rule: every obstacle is God redirecting you. Acts will not support that. In the same book Paul is stoned, jailed, shipwrecked and opposed at length, and none of those are read as the Spirit's redirection — he goes back to several of the cities he was driven out of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The difference is not in the size of the obstacle. Luke simply tells us, in these two cases, whose hand it was. Most of the time nobody gets told, which is why the next verse matters as much as these: they were still walking when the answer came."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the word \"kept.\"** *Koluo* is an ordinary word for hindering or preventing — used of stopping someone crossing a street or forbidding an action. Luke does not dress it up. Whatever the experience was, he reports it as plainly as a road being shut."}]}, "Acts 17:22–25": {"title": "The altar to an unknown God", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul in front of the Athenian council, and he opens with something he found on a walk."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Paul stood up **in the middle of the Areopagus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mars Hill — the council that oversaw religion and morals in Athens. It had brought Socrates to trial four centuries earlier on a charge of introducing new gods."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is there on the same charge in substance: *he seems to be a proclaimer of foreign gods*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he came"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before: *while Paul waited for them at Athens, **his spirit was provoked within him** when he saw that the city was given over to idols.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paroxuneto** — the sharp-stirring word, the root of *paroxysm*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Men of Athens, I see that **in every way you are an unusually god-fearing people**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **deisidaimonesterous** — a word that can be read as *very religious* or as *superstitious*, and Paul may have chosen it because it can carry both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He begins with an observation about them rather than an accusation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had been doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As I was walking through your city and **looking carefully at the things you worship**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had studied the shrines before speaking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The find"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I even found an altar with this inscription: **TO THE UNKNOWN GOD**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Greek writers record altars in Athens *to unknown gods*, raised to cover any deity who had been overlooked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The move"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The One you worship without knowing Him** — that is **the One I am telling you about**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *your gods are wrong*. He takes their own admission of ignorance and fills it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *what you worship in ignorance, this I proclaim to you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about God"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The God who **made the world and everything in it**, being **Lord of heaven and earth**, **does not live in temples built by hands**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is standing below the Parthenon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stephen had said the same at his trial — *the Most High does not dwell in temples made with hands* — and Paul had been holding the coats."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Nor is He **served by human hands as though He needed anything**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **prosdeomenos** — as though needing something in addition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole ancient sacrificial economy assumed the gods needed feeding. Paul removes the premise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Since **it is He who gives to everyone life and breath and all things**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing goes up. Everything comes down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the speech goes on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes two Greek poets — *in Him we live and move and have our being*, and *we are also His offspring* — and argues from them that God cannot be an image of gold or silver."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No Old Testament quotation in the whole address, which is unlike every other sermon in Acts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Truly, these times of ignorance God overlooked, but **now commands all men everywhere to repent**, because He has appointed a day on which He will judge the world in righteousness by the Man whom He has ordained. He has given assurance of this to all **by raising Him from the dead**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when they heard of the resurrection of the dead, **some mocked**, while others said, \"We will hear you again on this matter.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*However, **some men joined him and believed**, among them Dionysius the Areopagite, a woman named Damaris, and others with them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A member of the council, and a named woman."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he went next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After these things Paul departed from Athens and went to Corinth* — where he says he arrived *in weakness, and in fear, and in much trembling*, determined to know nothing but Christ crucified. See the note on 1 Corinthians 2:1–5."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he starts from.** An inscription admitting ignorance. Paul does not argue them out of their religion — he points at the one altar in the city that was left blank and says he has come to fill it in."}]}, "Acts 19:1–7": {"title": "Twelve men at Ephesus", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul arrives in a city and finds a dozen men who are half-informed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While **Apollos was at Corinth**, Paul traveled through the inland regions and came to **Ephesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Apollos has just been introduced at the end of chapter 18: *an eloquent man and mighty in the Scriptures… **though he knew only the baptism of John**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aquila and Priscilla had taken him aside and *explained to him the way of God more accurately*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke puts the two stories side by side. Same gap, two different remedies."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Have you received the Holy Spirit since you came to believe?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks it on arrival. Something about them prompted the question."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**We have not so much as heard whether there is a Holy Spirit**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek can be read as *we have not heard whether the Holy Spirit has been given* — which fits better, since John the Baptist had preached about the Spirit and any Jew knew the Spirit from the Scriptures."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way: they had not heard that the promise had arrived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Into what, then, were you baptized?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reasons backwards from the gap to its cause."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Into John's baptism.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John's baptism was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"John baptized with a baptism that went with **a change of mind**, telling the people to **believe in the One coming after him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **metanoias** — repentance, a changed mind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's own account of it: *I indeed baptize you with water… but He who is coming after me is mightier than I. **He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's baptism was a pointer. It was working exactly as designed, and it was not the destination."}, {"k": "p", "t": "These men had stopped at the signpost."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That is, **in Jesus the Messiah**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One name, and it closes the gap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When they heard this, they were **baptized in the name of the Lord Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only recorded rebaptism in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The laying on of hands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when Paul **laid his hands on them, the Holy Spirit came on them**, and they **spoke in tongues and prophesied**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same pattern as Samaria in chapter 8, and the same signs as Cornelius' house in chapter 10 — where nobody laid hands on anybody and the Spirit fell mid-sermon. See the note on Acts 10:44–48."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke records the variations without systematising them. Jerusalem, Samaria, Caesarea and Ephesus all happen differently."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There were **about twelve men** in all.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke counts them. A small group, in a large city, and Ephesus becomes the base for two years: *so that all who dwelt in Asia heard the word of the Lord Jesus, both Jews and Greeks*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three months in the synagogue, then the school of Tyrannus daily for two years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the riot: *great is Diana of the Ephesians!* — two hours of shouting in a theatre, because the silversmiths' trade in shrines was collapsing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he later writes to them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In Him you also trusted, after you heard the word of truth, the gospel of your salvation; in whom also, having believed, **you were sealed with the Holy Spirit of promise**, who is the guarantee of our inheritance.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *be filled with the Spirit*. See the note on Ephesians 5:18–21."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul reasoned from.** A gap, back to its cause. He asked what they had received, then what they had been baptized into, and found men who had stopped at John's signpost."}]}, "Acts 2:1–4": {"title": "Wind, fire, and languages", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three images in four verses, and every one of them had been used before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A sound from heaven like a great rushing wind, and it filled the whole house.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke is careful: a **sound like** wind. Nothing is described as blowing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In both Hebrew and Greek the word for spirit is also the word for wind and breath. Ezekiel's valley of dry bones is raised when the breath comes; Jesus told Nicodemus the wind blows where it wishes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fire"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What looked like tongues of fire, separating and coming to rest on each one of them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fire had marked God's presence before — the bush, the pillar, the mountain, the altar on Carmel. Always one fire, in one place, that the people stood back from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here it divides, and a piece of it settles on every individual in the room. Nobody is standing at a distance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The languages"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Began to speak in other languages, as the Spirit gave them the words.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd outside is the point: fifteen nations are listed in the next paragraph, and each hears its own tongue."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Babel scattered people by confusing their language. This gathers people by overcoming it. Luke does not draw the comparison; he just lists the nations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the sermon goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter stands up and explains it from Joel — *in the last days I will pour out my Spirit on all people* — and adds the detail from Psalm 16 about the Holy One not seeing decay."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This Jesus God has raised up, and we are all witnesses of it… He has poured out what you are seeing and hearing.* The wind and the fire are presented as evidence of an ascension. See the note on Acts 2:32–33."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The day it happened.** Pentecost was the feast of firstfruits of the wheat harvest, fifty days after Passover — and by tradition, the day the law was given at Sinai. Three thousand died the day the law came down at Sinai. Three thousand were added the day the Spirit came down here."}]}, "Acts 2:14–16": {"title": "\"It is only nine in the morning\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first Christian sermon opens by dealing with a heckle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who stands up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **Peter stood up with the eleven**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven, so Matthias has been added. Luke has just recorded the casting of lots."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**With the eleven** — Peter speaks, and the other eleven stand with him. It is presented as the group's statement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who this is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who denied three times in a courtyard fifty days earlier, in the same city, addressing the crowd that includes people who were there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been told it would happen and told what to do afterward: *when you have turned back, strengthen your brothers.* See the note on Luke 22:31–34."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The voice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he **raised his voice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke notes it. Three thousand respond by the end, so the crowd is large."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Men of Judea, and **all of you staying here in Jerusalem**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The visitors from fifteen countries listed a few verses earlier. See the note on Acts 2:9–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Let me tell you what this is**. Listen carefully to my words.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *let this be known to you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He begins by taking responsibility for explaining an event nobody in the street can account for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heckle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some in the crowd had said *\"They are full of new wine.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**These men are not drunk, as you suppose — it is only nine in the morning**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the third hour of the day*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Devout Jews fasted until the morning sacrifice on a feast day. Nobody had eaten, let alone been drinking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the plainest possible reply, and it is the entire rebuttal. He does not argue about the nature of the experience."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pivot"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No, this is what was spoken of through the prophet Joel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not explain the phenomenon. He identifies it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he quotes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joel 2, at length: *\"I will pour out of My Spirit on all flesh; your sons and your daughters shall prophesy… **and whoever calls on the name of the LORD shall be saved**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes the whole passage, including the wonders in heaven and the blood and fire and smoke, and ends on the last line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the sermon goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three Old Testament passages, in order: Joel, then Psalm 16 (*You will not leave my soul in Hades*), then Psalm 110 (*The LORD said to my Lord, \"Sit at My right hand\"*). See the note on Psalms 110:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Almost the whole sermon is quotation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Him, being delivered by the determined purpose and foreknowledge of God, you have taken by lawless hands, have crucified, and put to death**; whom God raised up.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves in one sentence — God's purpose and their hands, neither cancelling the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now when they heard this, **they were cut to the heart**, and said to Peter and the rest of the apostles, \"**Men and brethren, what shall we do?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And that day about three thousand were added."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the first sermon begins.** Not with a text and not with an appeal. With a man pointing at a clock."}]}, "Acts 2:17–18": {"title": "\"In the last days\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter quotes Joel, and the small change he makes is the whole claim."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The change"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joel wrote *and it shall come to pass **afterward***. See the note on Joel 2:28–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says *and it will happen **in the last days**, says God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names which afterward. The era Joel pointed at is the one they are standing in, and Peter says so in the first line of the quotation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pouring"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **pour out My Spirit on all people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ekcheo** — to pour out, empty. The same verb the Greek Old Testament uses in Joel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**All flesh.** In front of him is a crowd from fifteen nations. See the note on Acts 2:9–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four groups"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your **sons and your daughters** will prophesy, your **young men** will see visions, and your **old men** will dream dreams.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter reverses Joel's order of the last two — Joel has old men dreaming first. Neither order changes the point: both ends of a life are included."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The servants"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yes, and on **My servants — the men and the women both** — in those days I will pour out My Spirit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joel says *the servants*. Peter says **My** servants, twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The addition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **they will prophesy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That clause is not in Joel. Peter adds it to the second group."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joel had said the sons and daughters would prophesy; Peter extends it to the slaves as well."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he is quoting it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some in the crowd had said *they are full of new wine*, and Peter answered with a clock — *it is only nine in the morning*. See the note on Acts 2:14–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then this. He does not explain the phenomenon; he identifies it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he quotes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest of Joel, unabridged: *wonders in heaven above and signs in the earth beneath: blood and fire and vapour of smoke. The sun shall be turned into darkness, and the moon into blood, before the coming of the great and awesome day of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not stop at the convenient part."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he stops"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it shall come to pass that **whoever calls on the name of the LORD shall be saved**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the last line of the quotation and the hinge of the sermon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes the same line in Romans 10 and draws the same conclusion: *there is no distinction between Jew and Greek, for the same Lord over all is rich to all who call upon Him.* See the note on Romans 10:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sermon does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three Old Testament passages: Joel, then Psalm 16 on the resurrection, then Psalm 110 on the seating. See the note on Psalms 110:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Almost the whole sermon is quotation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then those who gladly received his word were baptized; and that day about **three thousand souls** were added to them.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other three thousand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The day the law was given at Sinai, three thousand fell. See the note on Exodus 32:25–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two givings, and the same number counted on each day."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two words Peter changes.** Joel's *afterward* becomes *in the last days*, and *the servants* becomes *My servants*. He is dating the prophecy and naming who the slaves belong to."}]}, "Acts 2:19–21": {"title": "Everyone who calls", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of the passage Peter quotes to explain what has just happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being explained"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundred and twenty people speaking languages they had not learned, and a crowd from fifteen regions hearing their own dialects. See the note on Acts 2:9–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the accusation: *they are full of new wine*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's answer: *these are not drunk, as you suppose, since it is only the third hour of the day*. Nine in the morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But this is what was spoken by the prophet Joel.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he has already quoted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will pour out of My Spirit on all flesh; your sons and your daughters shall prophesy… and on My menservants and on My maidservants I will pour out My Spirit in those days.\"* See the note on Acts 2:17–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The signs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will give **wonders in the sky above and signs on the earth below** — **blood and fire and rising smoke**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joel's language. Peter quotes the whole paragraph rather than stopping at the part that has obviously happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lights"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The sun will be turned to darkness and the moon to blood**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "None of this happened on the day of Pentecost."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter quotes it anyway. He treats the whole passage as one movement that has now begun, without claiming every clause has already occurred."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joel's own phrase for it is *the day of the LORD*, and Peter keeps that too: *before that great and shining day of the Lord comes*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the sun and moon go"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah has it: *the moon will be ashamed and the sun will be put to shame, for the LORD of hosts will reign on Mount Zion*. See the note on Isaiah 24:21–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Jesus in the Olivet discourse: *immediately after the tribulation of those days the sun will be darkened, and the moon will not give its light*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And it will be that **everyone who calls on the name of the Lord will be saved**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pas hos an epikalesetai** — everyone, whoever."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The quotation stops there. That is where Peter ends it and starts preaching."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is remarkable about the stopping point"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joel's next clause is *for in Mount Zion and in Jerusalem there shall be deliverance, as the LORD has said, among the remnant whom the LORD calls*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter ends on *everyone*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who quotes it next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul, in Romans, at the end of the argument about Jew and Greek:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For there is no distinction between Jew and Greek, for the same Lord over all is rich to all who call upon Him. For **whoever calls on the name of the LORD shall be saved**.* See the note on Romans 10:9–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same line, used to make the same point about the word *whoever*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name in Joel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the Hebrew the name is **YHWH**. *Everyone who calls on the name of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter applies it, in the same sermon, to Jesus: *God has made this Jesus, whom you crucified, both Lord and Christ*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the crowd asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now when they heard this, they were **cut to the heart**, and said to Peter and the rest of the apostles, \"**Men and brethren, what shall we do?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer, and three thousand added that day. See the note on Acts 2:40–41."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where Peter stops the quotation.** One clause before Joel narrows it to a remnant in Jerusalem. He ends on *everyone who calls*."}]}, "Acts 2:32–33": {"title": "\"He has poured out what you are seeing and hearing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter is explaining a phenomenon, and his explanation runs backwards from the noise in the street to a throne nobody present can see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chain"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*This Jesus God has raised up, and we are all witnesses of it*", "*lifted up to the right hand of God*", "*having received from the Father the promised Holy Spirit*", "*He has poured out what you are seeing and hearing*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four links: resurrection, ascension, receiving, pouring. Peter builds the whole argument to explain a sound and a sight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the crowd can check"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What you are seeing and hearing.* They are not being asked to accept an invisible claim. Something audible and visible is happening in front of them, and Peter says: here is what it proves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The evidence for the ascension, in his argument, is the Spirit's arrival. Nobody watched Jesus sit down at the right hand of God — but this is what it looks like from below."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Having received… He has poured out\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Son receives from the Father and gives to the people. Peter puts all three in one sentence without explanation, and the church spent centuries working out what he had said in passing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it mattered that day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd's first suggestion was that these people were drunk at nine in the morning. Peter's answer is not a defence of behaviour. It is a claim about location: the man you crucified is at God's right hand, and this is Him acting from there."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the hearers do next.** *They were cut to the heart* and asked what to do — and Peter's answer includes *the promise is for you and your children and all who are far off.* The pouring out described here is presented as available to the crowd, not just to the hundred and twenty in the room."}]}, "Acts 2:40–41": {"title": "Three thousand added", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first day of the church, and Luke gives the number."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter kept saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He said much more than this, and he kept urging them, \"Be rescued from this crooked generation.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke tells us the sermon we have is a summary — there was much more. And the plea he records is passive: *be rescued*, be saved. Not save yourselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd's own question had been *what shall we do?*, and the answer they were given was to turn and be baptised, and to receive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That day about three thousand people were added to them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The figure is not chosen at random by history. Exodus 32 records three thousand falling on the day the law was given at Sinai — and Pentecost was, by long tradition, the anniversary of that giving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same feast, same number, and the verb reversed. Three thousand fell there; three thousand were **added** here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke does not comment. He was writing for people who knew the story."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Added\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is the ordinary one for putting something to an existing total, and Luke will use it repeatedly through the early chapters — the Lord added to their number daily."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in Acts is described as joining. They are added, and the one doing the adding is named."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the day cost them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is said about qualifications. They had, seven weeks earlier, been part of the city that called for a crucifixion — Peter says so to their faces two paragraphs before this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The response to that sermon was three thousand baptisms."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What follows immediately.** The famous four: the apostles' teaching, the fellowship, the breaking of bread, and the prayers. The number is not left as a headline; Luke goes straight on to what those three thousand then did every day."}]}, "Acts 2:42–45": {"title": "Four things they kept at", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first description of the church, given in one sentence with four items in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The context"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three thousand people were added that morning. See the note on Acts 2:40–41."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The city had a new congregation the size of a small town, converted in a day, with twelve leaders and no building."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This paragraph is what they did next."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **gave themselves steadily** to.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **proskarterountes** — persisted in, held fast to, continued steadfastly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is used of a boat kept ready for somebody, and of the disciples in the upper room devoting themselves to prayer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not attended. Persisted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **apostles' teaching**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **te didache ton apostolon**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Content, and it came from named people who had been there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And to **sharing life together**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **koinonia** — fellowship, partnership, having in common."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not sociability. The word is commercial as much as social — a **koinonos** is a business partner, which is what Luke calls James and John in the fishing trade."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 45 spells out what it meant in practice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And to **the breaking of bread**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Used in Acts of both the Lord's table and ordinary meals, and the two were not separated at this stage. Verse 46 has them *breaking bread from house to house*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fourth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And to **prayer**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek has the definite article — **tais proseuchais**, the prayers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which may mean the set hours. Peter and John go up to the temple *at the hour of prayer, the ninth hour* two verses later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What followed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A sense of **awe** came over everyone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phobos** — fear."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And many **wonders and signs** were done through the apostles.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order in the paragraph is worth noticing: the four things first, then the awe, then the signs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the fellowship meant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All the believers were together and **held everything in common**. They **sold their property and their possessions** and shared out the money to anyone in need.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hapanta koina** — all things common."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not compulsory. Peter says so explicitly to Ananias a few chapters later: *while it remained, was it not your own? And after it was sold, was it not in your own control?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The selling was voluntary, and the lie was the offence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And not permanent. It is described in Jerusalem, in this period, and Paul is taking a collection for that same church twenty years later because it had run out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is missing from the list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A building, a programme, a name, and an organisation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four practices, and Luke lists them in the order they occurred to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The summary a few verses on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So continuing daily with one accord in the temple, and breaking bread from house to house, they ate their food with gladness and simplicity of heart, praising God and having favour with all the people. **And the Lord added to the church daily those who were being saved**.* See the note on Acts 2:46–47."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The adding is His, in Luke's grammar. Nobody in the paragraph is recruiting."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb governing all four.** *Proskartereo* — persisted, held fast. Not attended, and not valued. Kept at."}]}, "Acts 2:46–47": {"title": "\"From house to house\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke's picture of what the first weeks actually looked like, and it is domestic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two places"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Day after day they were together **in the temple courts**… and they broke bread **from house to house**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both, and they are different activities. The temple was public, Jewish, and enormous — the courts held tens of thousands. The houses were small."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The teaching and the praise happened in the open; the meals happened where people lived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Day after day\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not weekly. Luke says it twice in two verses — daily in the temple, and the Lord adding to their number daily."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the shape of the early church was, it was not a Sunday event."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Breaking bread"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase covers an ordinary meal and carries the memory of the Last Supper, and Luke has just used it a few verses earlier as one of the four things they devoted themselves to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not separate the two. They *ate their meals with glad and generous hearts* — the eating and the remembering are described in one sentence, at somebody's table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words for how"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**glad** — the Greek is a strong word for rejoicing, exultation", "**generous** — literally *simplicity* of heart, the same root as the word behind God giving *with both hands* in James 1:5. Single-minded, without a hidden second motive."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is how Luke describes the atmosphere: glad and unguarded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it produced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Praising God and finding favour with all the people. And the Lord added to the church daily.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The favour is with outsiders, and the adding is God's doing. Neither is presented as the goal of the meals — both are reported as what followed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in the picture.** No building, no programme, no name for the movement yet. A temple courtyard, other people's houses, and a shared table every day."}]}, "Acts 2:5–8": {"title": "The language we were born to", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The crowd's question is not about the miracle. It is about the accents."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is in the city"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now there were **devout Jews staying in Jerusalem, from every nation under heaven**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eulabeis** — reverent, careful."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is Pentecost, one of the three pilgrim feasts. Jerusalem's population multiplied for the festivals, and Jews from across the diaspora were resident or visiting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What draws them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When **this sound went out**, a crowd came together.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phones tautes** — this sound, or this voice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 2 called it *a sound from heaven, as of a rushing mighty wind*. See the note on Acts 2:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something audible from outside the building."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bewilderment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **they were bewildered**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sunechuthe** — poured together, confounded."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb the Septuagint uses at Babel: *let Us go down and there **confuse** their language*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke uses Babel's word, at the moment the confusion runs backwards."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **each of them was hearing his own language being spoken**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **te idia dialekto** — in his own dialect."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dialektos** — not just the language, but the local form of it. The particular speech of a region."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The astonishment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were astonished, and they kept saying to one another, \"**Are not all these men who are speaking Galileans?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Galileans were known for a distinctive accent. It gave Peter away in the courtyard: *surely you are one of them, for **your speech betrays you***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd is identifying provincial fishermen by ear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then **how is it that every one of us is hearing them in the language we were born to?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **te idia dialekto hemon en he egennethemen** — in our own dialect, in which we were born."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that phrase means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the trade language. Not Greek, which everyone in the empire could manage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The speech of home — the one learned before school, the one a mother uses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Parthians and Medes and Elamites, those dwelling in Mesopotamia, Judea and Cappadocia, Pontus and Asia, Phrygia and Pamphylia, Egypt and the parts of Libya adjoining Cyrene, visitors from Rome, both Jews and proselytes, Cretans and Arabs.* See the note on Acts 2:9–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fifteen regions, running east to west. Luke names them all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was being said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**We hear them speaking in our own tongues the wonderful works of God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ta megaleia tou Theou** — the great things of God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not preaching. Not instruction. The content is praise, and the sermon has not started yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the languages"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The message could have been delivered in Greek and understood by nearly everybody present."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The miracle is not about comprehension. It is about which language it arrived in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Babel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore its name is called Babel, **because there the LORD confused the language of all the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One language became many, and the people scattered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here the many hear one message, and three thousand are added in a day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What some in the crowd conclude"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Others mocking said, \"**They are full of new wine**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Peter, standing up with the eleven: *these are not drunk, as you suppose, since it is only the third hour of the day*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Joel, and David, and *this Jesus God has raised up, of which we are all witnesses*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The phrase.** *The language we were born to* — not the trade language everyone could manage, but the one learned at home. That is what the crowd notices."}]}, "Acts 2:9–11": {"title": "The list of countries", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke stops the narrative to write out a table of nations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the list is there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He could have written *people from everywhere*. Instead he gives fifteen entries by name, and the naming is the point — every one of them heard their own language."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The geography"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list moves roughly in a circle, east to west and back:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Parthians, Medes, Elamites** — the far east, beyond the empire, in what is now Iran.", "**Mesopotamia** — where Abraham came from.", "**Judea** — the middle, and the odd one on the list.", "**Cappadocia, Pontus, Asia, Phrygia, Pamphylia** — Asia Minor, and every one of them turns up later in Acts or the letters. Peter addresses his first letter to four of them.", "**Egypt, Libya toward Cyrene** — North Africa. Simon who carried the cross was from Cyrene.", "**Visitors from Rome** — the capital, *both Jews and converts to the Jewish faith*.", "**Cretans and Arabs** — the last two, and the least expected."]}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jews of the diaspora, in Jerusalem for the feast, plus proselytes — Gentiles who had come into Judaism."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Most of them would have spoken Greek or Aramaic well enough to get by. The languages they hear are the ones from home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they heard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**We are hearing them tell the wonderful works of God** in our own tongues!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ta megaleia tou theou** — the great things, the magnificent deeds."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in the list reports hearing a sermon, an instruction or an appeal. What they hear is the works of God being told."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *tell* is the ordinary word for speaking. The content is God's own acts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's sermon comes afterward, in Aramaic or Greek, and it is a separate thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Babel is the other passage where languages multiply in one event, and there the result is that nobody understands anybody and the people scatter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here the languages multiply and everybody understands, and the people gather."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke never mentions Babel. He writes out the table of nations and leaves it there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The feast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pentecost — **Shavuot**, fifty days after Passover, the wheat harvest, and the feast at which two loaves *baked with leaven* were waved before the LORD."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jewish tradition also counted it as the anniversary of the giving of the law at Sinai."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two crowds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were all amazed and perplexed, saying to one another, \"Whatever could this mean?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Others mocking said, \"They are full of new wine.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both reactions are recorded in the next verses, from the same event."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three thousand were added that day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Sinai, on the day the law was given and the calf was made, three thousand fell. See the note on Exodus 32:25–29."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the list is for.** Luke is a careful writer and he spends three verses on place names. Every one is a claim that a specific person heard the great things of God in the language they grew up speaking."}]}, "Acts 20:17–21": {"title": "\"I kept back nothing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is in a hurry to reach Jerusalem, so he sends for the elders rather than going to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arrangement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From **Miletus** he sent to **Ephesus** and called for the elders of the church.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty miles each way. He is skipping Ephesus deliberately — Luke says *he was hurrying to be at Jerusalem, if possible, on the Day of Pentecost* — and the elders walk to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the only speech in Acts addressed to a Christian congregation. Every other one is to a crowd, a court, or a synagogue."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The record"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You know how I lived among you the whole time, from the first day I set foot in Asia**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He appeals to what they saw. He had been in Ephesus about three years, longer than anywhere else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three descriptions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Serving the Lord **with a humble heart**, **with tears**, and **through the trials that came to me by the plots of the Jews**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He mentions the tears three times in this speech — here, in verse 31 about warning them night and day, and at the end when they weep on his neck."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I kept back nothing that would help you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hupostello** — to draw back, to shrink from, to withhold. It was used of furling a sail and of a person keeping quiet out of fear."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it again in verse 27: *I have not shunned to declare to you the whole counsel of God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he taught"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But told it to you and taught you **publicly and from house to house**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two settings. The hall of Tyrannus, where Luke says he reasoned daily for two years, and the houses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he taught"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Testifying to both Jews and Greeks**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same message to both, which was not obvious in that city."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"About **a change of mind toward God**, and **faith toward our Lord Jesus Christ**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He compresses three years of teaching into two items."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A change of mind** — the Greek is **metanoia**, from *meta*, change, and *nous*, mind. It is a word about what a person thinks, and the direction is given: *toward God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Faith toward our Lord Jesus Christ** — the second half, and the object is named."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And see, now I go bound in the spirit to Jerusalem, **not knowing the things that will happen to me** there, except that the Holy Spirit testifies in every city, saying that **chains and tribulations await me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *\"**But none of these things move me**; nor do I count my life dear to myself, so that I may finish my race with joy.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the meeting ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He knelt down and prayed with them all. Then they all wept freely, and **fell on Paul's neck and kissed him**, sorrowing most of all for the words which he spoke, that **they would see his face no more**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke was there — the passage is in the *we* sections — and this is one of the few scenes in Acts he describes as an eyewitness to the emotion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one saying of Jesus"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul closes with a line found nowhere in the Gospels: *\"**It is more blessed to give than to receive**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Acts preserves it and nothing else does."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the speech is.** A man laying out three years of work in front of the people who watched him do it, and inviting them to check. The summary of the content is two phrases long."}]}, "Acts 20:22–24": {"title": "\"None of these things move me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "In the middle of his farewell to the Ephesian elders, Paul says where he is going and what he expects there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The compulsion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am going to Jerusalem **under a compulsion in the spirit I cannot shake**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dedemenos to pneumati** — bound in the spirit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word for being in chains. He is describing himself as already tied."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ignorance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Not knowing what will happen to me there**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one thing he does know"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Except that **the Holy Spirit testifies to me in every city that chains and hardships are waiting for me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "City after city, the same message."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke records one instance in the next chapter: Agabus takes Paul's belt at Caesarea, binds his own hands and feet, and says *so shall the Jews at Jerusalem bind the man who owns this belt*. And the whole company begs him not to go."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His answer there: *what do you mean by weeping and breaking my heart? For I am ready not only to be bound, but also to die at Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **none of these things move me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *I make my life of no account* — the older versions have *none of these things move me*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The valuation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Nor do I count my life dear to myself**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **timian** — precious, valuable. He declines to price it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So that I may finish my course with joy**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dromon** — a race course, a track. Paul uses it of himself three times, and the last is in his final letter: *I have fought the good fight, **I have finished the race**, I have kept the faith.* See the note on 2 Timothy 4:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**With joy.** He puts the two in the same clause — a course he expects chains at the end of, finished with joy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **the ministry I received from the Lord Jesus** — to testify to **the good news of the grace of God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names his own commission in six words, and the noun at the centre of it is grace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the outcome, the outcome's fairness, or a request for it to change."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has been told what is waiting. He states it and keeps going."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He reached Jerusalem, was seized in the temple, nearly beaten to death, rescued by Roman troops, and stopped a flogging by asking one legal question. See the note on Acts 22:25–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then two years in Caesarea, an appeal to Caesar, a shipwreck, and Rome — where Acts leaves him *preaching the kingdom of God and teaching the things which concern the Lord Jesus Christ **with all confidence, no one forbidding him***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He got to Rome in chains, as promised, and Luke's last word about it is *no one forbidding him*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he was told and what he did.** Chains and hardships, confirmed in every city he passed through — and he says the only thing he wants is to finish the course and deliver the message he was given."}]}, "Acts 20:28–31": {"title": "\"Purchased with His own blood\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The charge Paul leaves with the Ephesian elders, and the order of it is deliberate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Keep watch over yourselves** and over all the flock.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Themselves first. The Greek is **prosechete** — pay attention to, hold your mind on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same order as Hezekiah's instruction to the Levites: consecrate yourselves, and then the house. See the note on 2 Chronicles 29:3–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who appointed them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In which **the Holy Spirit has made you overseers**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **episkopous** — overseers, bishops."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke uses three words for the same men in one sentence: **presbuteroi** (elders, in verse 17), **episkopoi** (overseers), and the verb **poimainein**, to shepherd. In the New Testament they are one office."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say the church appointed them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To **shepherd the church of God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Poimainein** — to tend, to pasture. It is what Jesus told Peter to do on the beach: *tend My sheep*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The price"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Which **He purchased with His own blood**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **periepoiesato** — acquired, obtained for oneself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is one of the strongest statements in the New Testament about who died: the church of **God**, purchased with **His own** blood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some manuscripts read *the church of the Lord*; the difficulty is in the text and has been noted since the early centuries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason for the watching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I know that **after I am gone, savage wolves will come in among you**, and they **will not spare the flock**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **lukoi bareis** — heavy, grievous wolves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus had used the image: *beware of false prophets, who come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly they are ravenous wolves*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The worse half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Even from among your own selves men will rise up**, saying twisted things in order to **draw the disciples away after them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not only from outside. Paul tells the men in front of him that some of them are the problem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Saying twisted things** — the Greek is **diestrammena**, distorted, turned aside. The same root Paul used of Elymas *twisting the straight paths of the Lord*. See the note on Acts 13:9–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**After them** — the object of the drawing is named, and it is the teachers themselves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **stay awake**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **gregoreite** — the word Jesus used in Gethsemane, and Peter uses in his letter about the lion. See the note on 1 Peter 5:8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The record"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And remember that for **three years, night and day, I did not stop warning every one of you with tears**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three years — the longest Paul stayed anywhere."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Every one of you** — the Greek is *each one*, individually."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**With tears** — the third mention of them in this speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he leaves them with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *and now, brethren, **I commend you to God and to the word of His grace**, which is able to build you up and to give you an inheritance.* See the note on Acts 20:32–35."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wolves are coming, and what he hands them is a word."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the danger is located.** Outside and inside, and Paul spends more of the warning on the second. He is speaking to the overseers, and he tells them some of the men in the room will be the ones drawing people away."}]}, "Acts 20:32–35": {"title": "The word of His grace", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's last words to the Ephesian elders, and he hands them over to something rather than somebody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The handover"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And now, brothers, **I commit you to God and to the word of His grace**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paratithemai** — to deposit, to entrust, as you would leave valuables with someone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is leaving and knows he will not be back — he has just told them *you all… will see my face no more*. See the note on Acts 20:17–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not appoint a successor or leave instructions. He deposits them with two things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the word can do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Which is able to build you up** and to **give you an inheritance among all those who are set apart**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs attached to the word:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Build up** — **oikodomesai**, house-building. The same word Paul uses throughout 1 Corinthians 14 about a meeting. See the note on 1 Corinthians 14:1–5.", "**Give an inheritance** — the word is credited with doing the giving."]}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he wanted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have wanted no one's silver or gold or clothing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Clothing is on the list because in that world cloth was wealth. Achan's confession begins with a Babylonian garment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You yourselves know that **these hands provided for my own needs** and for those who were with me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is speaking to men who had watched him do it. Paul was a tentmaker, and Acts 18 has him working with Aquila and Priscilla in Corinth at the same trade."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not only his own needs — *and for those who were with me*. He funded the team."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In everything I showed you that **by working like this we must help the weak**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **antilambanesthai** — to take hold of in support. The same root Paul uses in Romans 8 of the Spirit taking hold of the other end of the load. See the note on Romans 8:26–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He worked so that he could give."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **remember the words of the Lord Jesus**, how He said, '**It is more blessed to give than to receive**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not in any Gospel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Acts preserves it and nothing else does — one sentence of Jesus that survived only because Paul quoted it to a group of elders on a beach at Miletus, and Luke was listening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*More blessed **to give** than **to receive**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a straight comparative. Both are blessed; one more so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when he had said these things, **he knelt down and prayed with them all**. Then they all wept freely, and **fell on Paul's neck and kissed him**, sorrowing most of all for the words which he spoke, that they would see his face no more.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they accompanied him to the ship.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke was there — the passage is in the *we* sections — and this is one of the few scenes in Acts where he records the emotion of a farewell."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he leaves them.** Not a structure and not a name. God, and the word of His grace — and he says outright that the word is the thing capable of building them and giving them the inheritance."}]}, "Acts 20:7–12": {"title": "The young man in the window", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke was there for this one — the *we* in the passage is his — and he tells it with a doctor's eye and a slight glint of humour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the first day of the week, when the disciples came together to break bread.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One of the earliest indications of Christians meeting on Sunday, and of what they met for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There were many lamps burning in the upper room.* Luke mentions it because it matters: many oil lamps in a crowded upper room, at night, burning the air. A young man in a window is looking for cooler air."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Paul talking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was leaving the next day, and he kept on talking until midnight.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke does not defend him. The word he uses for the sinking sleep is a medical term — being borne down, overcome by degrees. Eutychus does not doze off carelessly; he is gradually overwhelmed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fall"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He fell from the third floor, and **they picked him up dead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke, a physician, states it flatly. Not *as though dead*. Dead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Paul went down, bent over him, and took him in his arms.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The posture is Elijah's over the widow's son, and Elisha's over the Shunammite's boy — stretching himself on the child. Paul repeats an old gesture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not be alarmed. His life is in him.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he went back up, broke bread and ate, and talked with them a long while, until daybreak, and then he left.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He resumes. The meeting continues, the bread is broken, and the preaching runs till dawn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The boy is mentioned once more, in the last line: *they brought the young man home alive, and were greatly comforted.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "His name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Eutychus** — from *eu*, well, and *tuche*, fortune. It means **good fortune**, **fortunate**, **lucky**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was a common slave name in the Greek world; owners gave it the way a person might name a child Prosper. Luke, who names very few of the people in these travel sections, names this one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A boy called Good Fortune falls asleep, drops three floors, and is picked up dead. Then he is carried home alive, and the name holds after all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three floors"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke marks the height twice — the lamps in the upper room, and *he fell from the third floor*. He wants the reader to know how far it was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Sleep"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek moves through two words. First **katapheromenos** — being borne down, sinking by degrees, the medical term for going under. Then **hupnos**, deep sleep."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke is a doctor describing a descent in stages, and then a fall."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Luke leaves out.** Any comment. No sermon on the meaning of it, no rebuke of the boy, no reflection on the length of the sermon. A death, a raising, and then breakfast — reported by a man who was in the room."}]}, "Acts 22:22–24": {"title": "The word they stopped at", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke marks the exact syllable where a crowd turns."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is standing on the steps of the Antonia fortress, having just been dragged out of the temple and beaten. He asked the commander for permission to speak, motioned with his hand, and addressed the mob in Hebrew — at which *they kept all the more silent.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He then told them his whole story: Tarsus, Gamaliel, the persecution, the road, the light, Ananias."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They had **listened to him up to that word**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says it precisely. The whole testimony was heard."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word was the last one of the previous verse: *\"Depart, for I will send you far from here **to the Gentiles**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they shouted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get this man off the earth** — he should not be allowed to live!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *away with such a fellow from the earth*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is what a crowd in the same city had shouted about somebody else, in front of Pilate, a little over twenty years earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Shouting and **throwing off their coats** and **flinging dust into the air**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both gestures are Middle Eastern expressions of outrage. Dust thrown upward is what you do when you cannot reach the person you want to stone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the coats: at a stoning the witnesses laid their garments down. Paul had held them once, at Stephen's — and he had said so in this very speech, four verses earlier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is watching the coats come off again, and he described the scene from memory to the people now doing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commander's response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The commander ordered Paul **brought into the barracks** and said he should be **examined under the lash**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Claudius Lysias does not speak Hebrew. He has heard a man address a mob in a language he does not know and watched the mob try to kill him for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lash"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **mastix** — the **flagellum**, the scourge with weighted leather thongs. It was an interrogation instrument, not a punishment: the theory was that a man under it would say anything true."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that he could **find out why they were shouting** against him like this.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He wants information, and torture is the standard method for getting it out of a non-citizen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Is it lawful for you to flog a Roman citizen who has not even been condemned?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He waits until they are tying him up, and then asks a question. See the note on Acts 22:25–26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Roman law said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Lex Porcia and the Lex Julia forbade scourging a citizen, and forbade binding one without trial. Claiming citizenship falsely was a capital offence, so a claim was generally believed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had let this happen to him before without objecting — at Philippi he was beaten with rods and only mentioned his citizenship the next morning."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How far the speech got.** Everything — the arrest warrants, the light, the voice, the blindness, Ananias — was heard in silence. The sentence that broke it was about who the message was going to."}]}, "Acts 22:25–26": {"title": "The question asked while they were tying him", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He does not shout, argue or resist. He asks a legal question of the man nearest him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As they were **tying him up for it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The straps are already on. Luke puts the sentence in the middle of the preparation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he speaks to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Paul said **to the centurion standing there**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to the commander who gave the order, and not to the soldiers doing the work. To the officer supervising — the man legally responsible for what is about to happen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Is it lawful for you to flog a Roman citizen who has not even been condemned?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a claim. A question about the law, put to a man whose career depends on the answer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two illegalities are named in one sentence:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**A Roman citizen** — the Lex Porcia forbade scourging one.", "**Who has not even been condemned** — **akatakriton**, uncondemned. No trial, no verdict, no sentence."]}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not appeal to his innocence, his mission, or his God. He cites Roman statute."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had every opportunity to refuse this on other grounds and used the one that would work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The centurion's reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the centurion heard that, he **went to the commander and reported it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately, and he leaves the prisoner where he is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Be careful what you are about to do**, because this man is a Roman.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A junior officer warning his superior. The Greek is *see what you are about to do* — watch yourself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The penalties were real. Scourging a citizen could end a commander's career, and worse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the claim was believed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Falsely claiming citizenship was a capital offence. That made an assertion of it generally credible — nobody said it lightly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word Paul uses about himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Rhomaios** — a Roman. He was a Jew of Tarsus, a Pharisee trained under Gamaliel, and a citizen of the empire, and he uses each identity where it counts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had opened the speech before the mob in Hebrew and told them *I am a Jew, born in Tarsus of Cilicia*. Twenty minutes later he tells a centurion he is a Roman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both true."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one time he did not use it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Philippi he and Silas were *beaten with rods* and thrown into the inner prison, and he said nothing about citizenship until the next morning — when he used it to make the magistrates come and escort them out personally."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The commander comes and asks him directly, and finds out he was born to it while the commander himself had to buy it. See the note on Acts 22:27–29."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the question achieved.** He was still bound when he asked it, and the flogging never happened. Luke's account moves from the straps going on to the men stepping away in the space of four verses."}]}, "Acts 22:27–29": {"title": "\"But I was born with it\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A short exchange between a prisoner and the officer holding him, and the ranks reverse inside three sentences."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commander comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The commander came and said to him, \"**Tell me, are you a Roman?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He comes down himself. He does not send for Paul, and Paul is still tied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Claudius Lysias is a **chiliarch**, commander of a thousand men, the senior Roman officer in Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He said, \"**Yes**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One word. Luke gives him nothing else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost the commander"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It cost me a large sum of money** to buy this citizenship.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *with a great sum I obtained this citizenship*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Citizenship was for sale under Claudius, and the trade was notorious — the emperor's freedmen and his wife were known to be selling it. The commander's name, **Claudius** Lysias, is almost certainly the emperor's name taken at purchase, which was the custom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is a Greek who bought his way in, and he mentions the price."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Paul's reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Paul said, \"**But I was born with it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *but I was even born so*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six words, and they settle the question of rank. Inherited citizenship outranked purchased citizenship socially and absolutely."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man in chains has more standing than the man who put him in them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How Paul had it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke never explains. Tarsus was a free city but that did not confer citizenship; someone in the family must have been granted or bought it a generation or more earlier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul mentions it three times in Acts and never in a letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the men who were about to examine him **stepped away from him at once**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is they *departed from him*. The straps are dropped where they stand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commander's own state"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the commander himself was afraid** when he realized that **he had put a Roman in chains**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the flogging he nearly ordered. The binding, which had already happened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Merely chaining a citizen without trial was an offence. He is frightened of what he has already done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He keeps him — but the next morning he releases him from the chains and convenes the Sanhedrin to find out the charge."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And when a plot to ambush Paul is discovered, this same commander sends him to Caesarea under escort: two hundred soldiers, seventy horsemen, two hundred spearmen, at nine o'clock at night."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Roman army moves one prisoner out of Jerusalem with four hundred and seventy men."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The letter he writes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This man was seized by the Jews and was about to be killed by them. Coming with the troops I rescued him, **having learned that he was a Roman**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He rearranges the order of events slightly in his own favour. Luke includes the letter anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the citizenship takes him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two years later, in front of Festus: *I appeal to Caesar.* And the appeal is what puts him on a ship to Rome."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been told he would go: *as you have testified for Me in Jerusalem, so you must also bear witness at Rome.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What it was used for.** Paul does not use the citizenship to get out. He uses it to stop a flogging, and then again to appeal — and the appeal sends him further into custody, all the way to the capital."}]}, "Acts 23:1–3": {"title": "\"You whitewashed wall\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul in front of the council, and the exchange goes badly within two sentences."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Brothers, I have lived my life before God with a completely clear conscience up to this day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He addresses the Sanhedrin as equals — *brothers*, not *rulers* — and claims a clear conscience, which is not a modest thing to say to a court."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is struck on the mouth for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God is going to strike you, you whitewashed wall. You sit there to judge me according to the law, and you break the law by ordering me to be struck?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The legal point is exact. Striking an unconvicted defendant was illegal, and Paul names the contradiction precisely."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The insult is also exact. **Whitewashed wall** — clean on the outside, structurally unsound; the same image Jesus used of whitewashed tombs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bystanders tell him he is reviling God's high priest, and Paul answers: *I did not know, brothers, that he was the high priest* — and then quotes Exodus about not speaking evil of a ruler of your people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not defend the outburst. He retracts it on the spot, on the basis of a text."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Luke includes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not have to. This is Paul at his least impressive — losing his temper, and then discovering he had insulted the wrong man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke reports his hero's failures the way the Gospels report Peter's."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Whether he really did not know.** Readers have wondered for centuries: poor eyesight, a long absence from Jerusalem, or an irregular seating arrangement. The text does not say. What it does record is a man who spoke first, was corrected, and immediately took it back — which is the more useful half of the story."}]}, "Acts 26:1–3": {"title": "I count myself fortunate", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's last recorded defence, and he opens by saying he is glad to be there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Caesarea, in the audience hall, two years into custody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Festus has just taken over from Felix and inherited a case he cannot make sense of. He says so to Agrippa: *I have nothing certain to write to my lord concerning him… **for it seems to me unreasonable to send a prisoner and not to specify the charges against him***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is listening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Agrippa* — Herod Agrippa II, the last of the Herods. Great-grandson of the Herod who killed the children at Bethlehem, son of the Herod who executed James and was eaten by worms."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His sister Bernice is with him. And Festus, the governor, and *the commanders and the prominent men of the city*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke had noted the entrance: *with great pomp*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The permission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You have permission to speak for yourself**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gesture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Paul **stretched out his hand** and began his defense.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ekteinas ten cheira** — the orator's posture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is in chains. Luke mentions them at the end of the chapter and Paul refers to them himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"King Agrippa, **I count myself fortunate** that it is before you I am to make my defense today.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hegemai emauton makarion** — I consider myself blessed, happy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Makarios** is the beatitude word. *Blessed are the poor in spirit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A prisoner, two years without trial, in chains, opening with *I count myself blessed*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason he gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Above all because **you are so well acquainted with all the customs and questions that occupy the Jews**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Agrippa had the right to appoint the high priest and oversaw the temple treasury. He knew the material."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is glad of an audience who will follow the argument. That is the whole reason."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So I ask you to **hear me patiently**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **makrothumos** — with longsuffering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with the hearing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Tells his own story. Brought up in Jerusalem, a Pharisee, *the strictest sect of our religion*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I myself thought I must do many things contrary to the name of Jesus of Nazareth… **and being exceedingly enraged against them, I persecuted them even to foreign cities**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the road, the light, the voice, and the Aramaic sentence: *Saul, Saul, why are you persecuting Me? It is hard for you to kick against the goads*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commission he reports"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To open their eyes, in order to **turn them from darkness to light**, and from the power of Satan to God, that they may receive forgiveness of sins and an inheritance among those who are sanctified by faith in Me.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Paul, you are beside yourself! Much learning is driving you mad!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Festus, shouting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Paul's answer: *I am not mad, most noble Festus, but speak the words of truth and reason*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You **almost persuade me** to become a Christian.\"* See the note on Acts 26:27–29."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the verdict was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This man is doing nothing deserving of death or chains.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Agrippa said to Festus, \"**This man might have been set free if he had not appealed to Caesar**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Innocent, and going to Rome anyway — which is where he had been told he was going."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How he opens.** *I count myself blessed.* Two years in custody, in chains, before a Herod — and the reason he gives is that this listener will understand the argument."}]}, "Acts 26:27–29": {"title": "Except for these chains", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The prisoner turns the hearing round and starts questioning the king."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has told his story — Tarsus, the Pharisees, the persecution, the Damascus road, the commission."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Festus has shouted: *Paul, you are beside yourself! Much learning is driving you mad!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's reply: *I am not mad, most noble Festus, but speak the words of truth and reason. For the king, before whom I also speak freely, knows these things… **for this thing was not done in a corner***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"King Agrippa, **do you believe the prophets?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A prisoner cross-examining the man judging him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question is designed. Agrippa cannot say no — he is the custodian of the temple and the appointer of high priests, and the room knows it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And he cannot say yes without conceding Paul's whole argument, which has been that the prophets said the Messiah would suffer and rise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer Paul supplies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I know that you believe**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not wait."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Agrippa's reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You almost persuade me to become a Christian**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **en oligo me peitheis Christianon poiesai**, and it is genuinely ambiguous."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It can be read as the King James has it — *almost you persuade me* — or as *in a short time you would make me a Christian*, which most modern versions prefer, and which reads as deflection or irony."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek does not settle it, and the note does not either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Christianon** — Christian."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It occurs three times in the New Testament. Once at Antioch, where the disciples *were first called Christians*. Once in Peter's letter: *if anyone suffers as a Christian, let him not be ashamed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And here, in a Herod's mouth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whether it takes little or much**, I would pray to God that **not only you but everyone listening to me today** might become **what I am**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes the ambiguity and covers both readings — little or much, either way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not only you but everyone listening* — the governor, the tribune, the commanders, the prominent men of the city, and whoever was standing at the back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Except for these chains**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parektos ton desmon touton**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has been wearing them for two years and has just been talking with his hands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a joke, and it is the only thing in the sentence he does not wish on them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it tells you about him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He would give away everything he has except the one thing costing him something — and he says so in front of the man who could release him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the room decides"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This man is doing nothing deserving of death or chains**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Agrippa to Festus: *this man might have been set free if he had not appealed to Caesar*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Innocent, and going to Rome anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he had appealed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because Festus had asked whether he would go to Jerusalem for trial, and Paul knew about the forty men who had sworn not to eat until he was dead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And because he had been told: *be of good cheer, Paul; for as you have testified for Me in Jerusalem, **so you must also bear witness at Rome***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rome, in a rented house, receiving all who came to him, *unhindered*. See the note on Acts 28:28–29."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one exception.** He wishes everybody in the room were what he is — and then names the single item he is keeping to himself."}]}, "Acts 26:4–8": {"title": "The wolf in the evening", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's last recorded defence, before a king, and he turns it into something else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is in the room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Agrippa, Bernice, the governor Festus, the commanders and the prominent men of Caesarea, *with great pomp*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Paul, in chains, given permission to speak."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about his past"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They could testify, if they were willing, that **I lived as a Pharisee, by the strictest party of our religion**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not soften it. Elsewhere he puts it harder: *I persecuted this Way to the death, binding and delivering into prisons both men and women.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Benjamin's blessing was one line: *in the morning he devours the prey, and in the evening he divides the spoil.* Paul names his own tribe twice in his letters, and the morning is what Luke describes with the word used of an animal mangling a carcass. See the note on Genesis 49:27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says he is on trial for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And now I stand here on trial **for the hope of the promise God made to our fathers**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not for a crime, a riot, or a sect. For a hope, and he says it is the hope *our twelve tribes* are serving God night and day to see."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts himself on the same side as his accusers and says the disagreement is about whether it has happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question he asks the king"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why should it be judged something incredible by any of you that God raises the dead?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A prisoner asking the court a question. It is not a defence — nothing in it is about his innocence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with the hearing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them about the road to Damascus: the light above the brightness of the sun, the voice, and the commission — *to open their eyes, to turn them from darkness to light… that they may receive forgiveness of sins.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Festus interrupts him: *Paul, you are beside yourself! Much learning is driving you mad.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then Agrippa: *you almost persuade me to become a Christian.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "His answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I would to God that not only you, but also all who hear me today, might become both almost and altogether such as I am — **except for these chains**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is in irons, and he wishes the whole room were as he is. The only thing he excludes is the chains."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the evening dividing the spoil. Everything he took in the morning he is handing out, from a prisoner's dock, to the men who are trying him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Agrippa concluded.** *This man might have been set free if he had not appealed to Caesar.* He appealed, and it took him to Rome — which is where he wrote the letters. Philippians, Ephesians, Colossians and Philemon come out of that appeal."}]}, "Acts 27:21–26": {"title": "The angel who stood by me", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A prisoner stands up in a sinking ship and tells the crew what is going to happen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fourteen days in a storm. Luke, who is aboard, describes it: they had thrown the cargo over, then the ship's tackle with their own hands, and *when neither sun nor stars appeared for many days, and no small tempest beat on us, all hope that we would be saved was finally given up*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hunger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When they had **gone a long time without food**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not fasting by choice. Nobody could cook or keep anything down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who stands up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Paul **stood up among them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A prisoner in chains, on his way to trial in Rome, addressing the crew, the soldiers and the centurion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reproach"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Men, **you should have listened to me** and not put out from Crete.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had warned them: *I perceive that this voyage will end with disaster and much loss.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the centurion was more persuaded by the helmsman and the owner of the ship than by the things spoken by Paul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which was the reasonable decision. A tentmaker's opinion against the captain's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He mentions it once and moves on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But now I urge you: take heart**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Not one life among you will be lost — only the ship**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two hundred and seventy-six people aboard, by Luke's count."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The source"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Last night an angel of the God whose I am and whom I serve stood beside me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses about ownership and service, in a sentence to a shipful of pagans."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the angel said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not be afraid, Paul. You must stand before Caesar**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been told this in Jerusalem two years earlier: *as you have testified for Me in Jerusalem, so you must also bear witness at Rome.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gift"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And look, **God has granted you the lives of all who are sailing with you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kecharistai** — from **charis**, grace. Has graciously given."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two hundred and seventy-five other people are described as given to Paul."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The confidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **take heart**, men. **I trust God that it will turn out exactly as I was told**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says *take heart* twice in six verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one detail"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **we are going to run aground on some island**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not promise a smooth ending. The ship is lost and they hit land somewhere unspecified."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens later that night"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sailors try to escape in the lifeboat under pretence of laying anchors, and Paul tells the centurion: *unless these men stay in the ship, **you cannot be saved**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The soldiers cut the ropes and let the boat fall away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are true in the chapter: God has granted the lives, and the men have to stay aboard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The breakfast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And while day was coming on, Paul implored them all to take food… **and he took bread and gave thanks to God in the presence of them all**, and when he had broken it he began to eat.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then they were all encouraged, and also took food themselves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says grace over a meal in a wrecking ship, and everybody eats."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The count"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And so it was that **they all escaped safely to land**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they land"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Malta. See the note on Acts 28:1–6."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who is giving the orders.** A prisoner, after a fortnight of storm, on the strength of a night visit — and Luke, who was on the ship, records that the count came out exactly as he said."}]}, "Acts 27:9–12": {"title": "The prisoner who was right, and the vote that went the other way", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A ship's council, a prisoner's opinion, and a decision made the sensible way that turns out to be wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was in the room"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the pilot** — the professional navigator", "**the ship's owner** — the money", "**the centurion** — the authority, and the man who had to decide", "**Paul** — a prisoner in chains, with no standing whatsoever"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke notes that the centurion listened to the first two rather than the last, and that *most of them were in favour* of sailing. Expertise, ownership and the majority all lined up on one side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the timing mattered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sailing had now become dangerous because the Fast was already over* — the Day of Atonement, which puts this in late September or October. Ancient sailing in that sea effectively shut down for the winter, and everyone aboard knew it. The question was not whether it was risky but whether they could make one short hop to a better harbour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was a reasonable call. It was also the wrong one."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the passage does not do.** It never says God told Paul. It says *Paul warned them* and reports what he could see. Later in the chapter an angel does speak to him, and Luke says so plainly — he distinguishes the two. This part of the story is a man's judgement being right while the experts were wrong, and Luke lets that stand without dressing it up as prophecy."}]}, "Acts 28:1–6": {"title": "The fire on Melita", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two hundred and seventy-six people come ashore, and the first thing that happens is a bonfire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The island"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Once we were safe, we learned that the island was called **Melita**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Malta. The name may come from a Phoenician root for refuge."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**We** — Luke is there. This is one of the eyewitness sections."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The welcome"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people there showed us **unusual kindness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **philanthropian** — the root of *philanthropy*, love of humankind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke calls them **barbaroi** — the standard Greek word for anyone who did not speak Greek. It carried no moral weight; it meant *foreign*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fire"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **built a fire and welcomed us all**, because **it had begun to rain and it was cold**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Late autumn, after fourteen days at sea and a swim ashore."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Paul had **gathered an armful of sticks**, and as he laid them on the fire.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An apostle picking up firewood, with a Roman guard, after a shipwreck."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke notes it because of what comes next, and it is also just what Paul did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The snake"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **viper driven out by the heat fastened itself on his hand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kathepsen** — fastened, took hold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the islanders concluded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This man must be a murderer. He escaped the sea, but justice will not let him live**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek names her: **he Dike** — Justice, a goddess in Greek religion, daughter of Zeus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their theology is coherent. A man survives a shipwreck and is bitten within the hour; something is clearly pursuing him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Paul **shook the creature off into the fire and suffered no harm**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apotinaxas** — shook off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No comment, no sermon, and no reaction recorded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wait"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They kept **waiting for him to swell up or suddenly fall down dead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives the two expected symptoms, and he is a doctor."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But after **waiting a long time** and seeing that **nothing out of the ordinary happened to him**, they **changed their minds and said he was a god**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **metabalomenoi** — turned round."}, {"k": "p", "t": "From murderer to deity in one paragraph, on the same evidence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened before at Lystra"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd called Paul and Barnabas Zeus and Hermes and tried to sacrifice to them — and minutes later stoned Paul and dragged him out of the city for dead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke records both swings without comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chief man of the island, Publius, whose father was *sick of a fever and dysentery* — Luke's terms — and Paul prays and lays hands on him and he is healed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So when this was done, the rest of those on the island who had diseases also came and were healed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three months on Malta."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mark's ending says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They will take up serpents; and if they drink anything deadly, it will by no means hurt them.\"* See the note on Mark 16:15–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke does not connect them. He reports what happened at a bonfire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rome, in his own rented house, *preaching the kingdom of God and teaching the things which concern the Lord Jesus Christ with all confidence, **no one forbidding him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the last sentence of Acts."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the islanders got right and wrong.** Both times they reasoned from the same event. A man bitten must be guilty; a man unharmed must be divine — and in between, Paul shook it into the fire and went on stacking wood."}]}, "Acts 28:25–27": {"title": "Paul's last quotation", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last thing Paul is recorded saying in Acts, and it is somebody else's words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rome, under house arrest, in *his own rented house*, chained to a soldier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had called the leaders of the Jewish community together three days after arriving. They said they had received no letters about him and wanted to hear what he thought — *concerning this sect, we know that it is spoken against everywhere*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From morning until evening he explained and solemnly testified of the kingdom of God, persuading them concerning Jesus **from both the Law of Moses and the Prophets**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A full day of it. And the result: *some were persuaded by the things which were spoken, and some disbelieved*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disagreement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **disagreed among themselves and began to leave**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **asumphonoi** — not sounding together. Where the English *symphony* comes from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are walking out mid-argument, and Paul speaks over the noise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The attribution"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The Holy Spirit spoke rightly through Isaiah the prophet to our fathers**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the speaker as the Spirit and the mouthpiece as Isaiah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To our fathers* — he is still saying *our*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 6, from the commission given to the prophet the year King Uzziah died."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You will **hear and hear, and never understand**; you will **look and look, and never see**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For this people's **heart has grown dull**. They **hear with difficulty**, and **they have closed their eyes**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Heart, ears, eyes. And the last is active — *they have closed*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is prevented"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Otherwise they might **see with their eyes and hear with their ears and understand with their heart and turn, and I would heal them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five verbs in a chain, and healing at the end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same passage Jesus quotes in Matthew 13 to explain the parables. See the note on Matthew 13:10–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How often it is used"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This passage is quoted more often in the New Testament than almost any other Old Testament text — Matthew, Mark, Luke, John and Paul all use it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of them uses it at a moment when the message has been heard and not received."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses it last, and closes the book of Acts with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So let it be known to you that **this salvation of God has been sent to the Gentiles, and they will listen**.\"* See the note on Acts 28:28–29."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had written about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 9 to 11, three chapters, and the anguish is real: *I have great sorrow and continual grief in my heart. For I could wish that I myself were accursed from Christ for my brethren, my countrymen according to the flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the conclusion: *blindness in part has happened to Israel **until the fullness of the Gentiles has come in**. And so all Israel will be saved.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blindness is temporary and partial in Paul's account, and he says so in the letter he sent to this city before he ever reached it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Acts ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Paul dwelt two whole years in his own rented house, and received all who came to him, **preaching the kingdom of God and teaching the things which concern the Lord Jesus Christ, with all confidence, no one forbidding him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last word in the Greek is **akolutos** — unhindered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke ends a book about a man in chains with an adverb meaning *without hindrance*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The passage everyone reaches for.** Isaiah 6, quoted by four Gospel writers and Paul — and always at the point where the message has been delivered and not taken."}]}, "Acts 28:28–29": {"title": "They will listen", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last sentence Luke records from Paul, and it is a forecast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The announcement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **let it be known to you** that this salvation of God **has been sent to the Gentiles**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apestale** — an aorist passive. It has been sent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Apostello** — the verb behind *apostle*. The salvation is described as having been commissioned and dispatched."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **they will listen**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **autoi kai akousontai** — they also will hear."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words of prediction, and Luke's whole second half is the evidence for it: Cornelius' house, Antioch, Cyprus, Galatia, Philippi, Thessalonica, Athens, Corinth, Ephesus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just quoted Isaiah — *you will hear and hear and never understand*. See the note on Acts 28:25–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then, in the next breath, *they will hear*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb, negated in the quotation and affirmed in the forecast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Rome matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The commission was *in Jerusalem, and in all Judea and Samaria, and to the end of the earth*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rome is not the end of the earth geographically. It is the centre of the world Luke is writing in, and from Rome the roads go everywhere."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had told the Romans his real plan: *whenever I journey to Spain, I shall come to you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three times this happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is Paul's pattern, stated three times in Acts and each time after a synagogue rejects him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Antioch in Pisidia: *it was necessary that the word of God should be spoken to you first; but since you reject it… **we turn to the Gentiles***. See the note on Acts 13:38–39."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Corinth: *your blood be upon your own heads; I am clean. From now on I will go to the Gentiles.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Rome, here, the third and last."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he wrote about the order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For I am not ashamed of the gospel of Christ, for it is the power of God to salvation for everyone who believes, **for the Jew first and also for the Greek**.* See the note on Romans 1:16–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "First, not only. Paul kept the order in every city."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The departure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when he had said these words, **the Jews left, arguing among themselves**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pollen echontes en heautois suzetesin** — having much dispute among themselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do not leave united. Luke has already said *some were persuaded*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in Acts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's trial before Caesar. His release, if there was one. His death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke was in Rome and wrote the book, and stops without telling us."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The traditional explanation is that Acts was finished before the outcome was known."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last two verses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Paul dwelt two whole years in his own rented house, and **received all who came to him**, preaching the kingdom of God and teaching the things which concern the Lord Jesus Christ, **with all confidence, no one forbidding him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parresias** — the boldness word, and **akolutos**, unhindered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was written from that house"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ephesians, Philippians, Colossians and Philemon are usually dated to these two years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I want you to know, brethren, that the things which happened to me have actually turned out for the furtherance of the gospel, so that **it has become evident to the whole palace guard** that my chains are in Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A soldier chained to Paul for four hours at a stretch, replaced by another, around the clock, for two years."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last word in the book.** *Unhindered.* Luke ends an account of arrests, floggings, shipwrecks and a chain with an adverb saying nothing was in the way."}]}, "Acts 3:14–16": {"title": "The Author of life", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter's second sermon, preached to a crowd that has just watched a man walk."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Solomon's colonnade, in the temple, with a healed man holding on to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now as the lame man who was healed held on to Peter and John, all the people ran together to them in the porch which is called Solomon's, greatly amazed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sermon starts because a crowd formed around a man they all knew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the man was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A certain man **lame from his mother's womb** was carried, whom they **laid daily** at the gate of the temple which is called Beautiful.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Over forty years old, the next chapter says. Everybody entering the temple had walked past him for decades."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's line to him: *silver and gold I do not have, but what I do have I give you: in the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth, rise up and walk*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disowning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You **disowned the Holy and Righteous One**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ernesasthe** — denied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the verb used of Peter in the courtyard. He picks the word for what he himself had done, and uses it of the crowd."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exchange"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **asked for a murderer to be given to you instead**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Barabbas. Peter does not soften it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And you killed **the Author of life**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **archegon tes zoes** — the originator, the founder, the pioneer, the one who leads the way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews uses the same word twice of Christ: *the captain of their salvation*, and *the author and finisher of our faith*. See the note on Hebrews 12:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence is a deliberate collision. They killed the one life comes from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whom **God raised from the dead**, and **we are witnesses of it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pattern of every sermon in Acts: you killed Him, God raised Him, we saw it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mechanism"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And it is **His name, through faith in His name**, that has made this man strong.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **stereosen** — made firm, solid."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**This man you see and know.** Peter appeals to the crowd's own knowledge — they had been stepping over him for years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The faith that comes through Jesus** has given him this **complete health** in front of all of you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **holokleria** — wholeness in every part, nothing missing. The word occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the adjective of the whole person: *may your whole spirit, soul, and body be preserved*. See the note on 1 Thessalonians 5:23–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose faith"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says it twice, and both times the faith is routed through the name rather than through the man's conviction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in Acts 3 records the lame man believing anything. He was asking for money."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Yet now, brethren, **I know that you did it in ignorance, as did also your rulers**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same reading Paul gives: *had they known, they would not have crucified the Lord of glory*. See the note on 1 Corinthians 2:6–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Repent therefore and be converted, that your sins may be blotted out**, so that times of refreshing may come from the presence of the Lord.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *blotted out* is **exaleiphthenai** — wiped away, erased."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the day ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priests and the Sadducees arrive, *greatly disturbed that they taught the people and preached in Jesus the resurrection from the dead*, and Peter and John spend the night in custody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*However, many of those who heard the word believed; and the number of the men came to about five thousand.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The title in the middle of the accusation.** *You killed the Author of life.* Peter puts the two halves in one clause and does not explain it."}]}, "Acts 3:19–21": {"title": "Times of refreshing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter's second sermon, preached on the temple steps to a crowd that has just watched a man walk."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Solomon's Porch. The lame man from the Beautiful Gate is holding on to Peter and John, *walking, leaping, and praising God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Why do you marvel at this? Or **why look so intently at us, as though by our own power or godliness we had made this man walk?***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter deflects the attention in his first sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You denied the Holy One and the Just, and asked for a murderer to be granted to you, and **killed the Prince of life**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *\"Yet now, brethren, I know that **you did it in ignorance**, as did also your rulers.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He states it as bluntly as it can be stated, and then names it as the Leviticus 4 category — sin committed unknowingly. See the note on Leviticus 4:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **change your minds and turn back to God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **metanoesate** — from **meta** (change) and **nous** (mind)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Epistrepsate** — turn round, turn back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So that **your sins may be wiped away**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exaleiphthenai** — to wipe out, to obliterate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb for erasing writing from a wax tablet or scraping ink off papyrus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses it of the record: *having wiped out the handwriting of requirements that was against us*. See the note on Colossians 2:11–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So that **times of refreshing may come from the presence of the Lord**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kairoi anapsuxeos** — seasons of refreshing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kairos** — the opportune moment, the right time, as against **chronos**, duration."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Anapsuxis** — a cooling down, a breathing space, recovery of breath. It appears once in the New Testament, here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From the presence of the Lord* — **apo prosopou tou Kuriou**, from the face of the Lord."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And so that **He may send Jesus Christ**, who was proclaimed to you beforehand.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He is meanwhile"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The One **heaven must receive until the time comes for all things to be restored**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dei ouranon dexasthai** — heaven must receive Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Achri chronon apokatastaseos panton** — until times of restoration of all things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Apokatastasis** — restoration to a former state. A medical word for a bone set back, and an astronomical one for a body returning to its position."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words for time"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kairos** in verse 20 — the seasons of refreshing that come now."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chronos** in verse 21 — the stretch of duration before the restoration."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter uses both, and distinguishes them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The witnesses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Which God spoke of **through the mouth of all His holy prophets from the beginning of the world**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses, quoted: *the LORD your God will raise up for you a Prophet like me from your brethren*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Samuel, and all the prophets. And Abraham: *in your seed all the families of the earth shall be blessed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To you first, God, having raised up His Servant Jesus, sent Him to bless you, **in turning away every one of you from your iniquities**.* See the note on Acts 3:24–26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to Peter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now as they spoke to the people, **the priests, the captain of the temple, and the Sadducees came upon them**… and put them in custody until the next day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*However, **many of those who heard the word believed; and the number of the men came to about five thousand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Arrested mid-sermon, and the count is given anyway."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two words.** **Kairos** — the refreshing that comes now, from the face of the Lord. **Chronos** — the stretch before the restoration. Peter uses both in consecutive verses."}]}, "Acts 3:9–11": {"title": "\"They recognized him\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The crowd's reaction, and the thing Luke keeps in the frame is that everybody knew the man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they see"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All the people saw him **walking and praising God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things at once. He had gone into the temple *walking, leaping, and praising God* two verses earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The recognition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **they recognized him** — this was **the man who used to sit begging at the Beautiful Gate**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is that they knew him thoroughly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was a fixture. Luke has already said he was *carried* there **daily**, and Peter's sermon a chapter later notes he was *over forty years old*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone entering that gate had walked past him for decades."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why that matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no question about identity. Nobody in the courtyard has to be persuaded that this is the same man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Sanhedrin says as much the next day: *what shall we do to these men? For, indeed, that a notable miracle has been done through them is evident to all who dwell in Jerusalem, and **we cannot deny it**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gate"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Beautiful Gate — the entrance from the court of the Gentiles into the temple proper. Josephus describes a great bronze gate on the east side that took twenty men to close."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was placed at the door of the building every day and never went in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they were **filled with wonder and amazement** at what had happened to him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **thambos** and **ekstasis** — astonishment, and being put out of one's place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the man does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the healed man kept hold of Peter and John**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is imperfect: he was holding on to them, continuously, and would not let go."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An hour earlier he had asked them for money."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter had said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Silver and gold I do not have, but what I do have I give you: **in the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth, rise up and walk**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *he took him by the right hand and lifted him up, and immediately his feet and ankle bones received strength.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the crowd runs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All the people came running to them in **the covered walkway called Solomon's Colonnade**, utterly astonished.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same portico Jesus had been walking in at the Feast of Dedication. See the note on John 10:22–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters later the whole church is meeting there: *they were all with one accord in **Solomon's Porch**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter does with the crowd"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Turns them away from himself in the first sentence:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Men of Israel, **why do you marvel at this? Or why look so intently at us, as though by our own power or godliness we had made this man walk?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *His name, through faith in His name, has made this man strong.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it costs them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That evening Peter and John are arrested, and spend the night in custody. Five thousand men had believed by then."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why Luke keeps the man in the shot.** He is standing next to Peter throughout the sermon, holding on. When the council convenes the next morning the record notes that *the man who had been healed was standing with them* — and there was nothing to say."}]}, "Acts 4:32–35": {"title": "Everything held in common", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke's second summary of the young church, and every clause in it is worth slowing down over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "One heart and soul"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The whole company of believers was **one in heart and soul**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is literally *of one heart and one soul* — a phrase used in Greek literature of the closest friendships, applied here to thousands of people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they said about property"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not one of them said that anything he owned was **his alone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke is precise. He does not say they had no possessions. He says nobody used the word *mine* about what he had."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The change is in the pronoun before it is in the ledger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Great power, great grace"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And with **great power** the apostles kept giving their witness to the resurrection of the Lord Jesus, and **great grace** was on them all.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two adjectives are the same Greek word, and Luke uses it twice in one sentence — great power on the preaching, great grace on everybody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The power was on the apostles. The grace was on all of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Not a needy person among them\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is an echo. Deuteronomy 15 promised Israel *there shall be no poor among you* if they kept the covenant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke reports it as an accomplished fact in Jerusalem, and the mechanism he describes is not a law. It is people selling things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "As he had need"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was distributed to each one as he had need.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not equally. According to need — which requires somebody to know each person's situation. The distribution assumes a community small enough to be known in."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What this paragraph is doing where it is.** It follows the prayer of chapter 4, where the place was shaken and they spoke the word with boldness. Luke's order is: they pray, the room shakes, and then nobody owns anything alone. He puts the economics downstream of the prayer meeting."}]}, "Acts 5:1–6": {"title": "A gift and a lie", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The hardest story in Acts, and Luke reports it without a word of explanation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was not required"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Before you sold it, was it not your own? And once it was sold, was the money not yours to do with as you pleased?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter states it twice, plainly. The land was theirs. The money was theirs. Nobody had commanded them to sell anything, and no percentage was owed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That removes the obvious reading. This is not about how much they gave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Why has Satan filled your heart, so that you would **lie to the Holy Spirit** and keep back part of the price?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *keep back* is **nosphizomai** — to embezzle, to set aside for yourself out of something already dedicated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word the Greek Old Testament uses for Achan, who kept back devoted things from Jericho and hid them under his tent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was lied to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You have not lied to men. You have lied to God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says *the Holy Spirit* in one line and *God* in the next, about the same act. It is one of the clearest statements in Scripture of who the Spirit is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pretence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 4 has just described Barnabas doing exactly this — selling a field, bringing the money, laying it at the apostles' feet. Luke names him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ananias performs the same scene with a portion, and presents it as the whole. The sin is not the withholding. It is the performance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke records afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A deep fear came over everyone who heard of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is **phobos**, and Luke repeats it a few verses later after Sapphira, extending it to *the whole church*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He offers no commentary, no softening, and no moral. The young men wrap him and carry him out, and the narrative moves on to signs and wonders in the next paragraph."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where this sits in Acts.** Immediately before it, a community where *there was not a needy person among them*. Immediately after it, crowds bringing the sick into the streets. The story is set between two descriptions of extraordinary grace, and Luke placed it there."}]}, "Acts 5:12–16": {"title": "\"Every one of them was healed\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke's summary of what the early weeks looked like, and he does not hedge the numbers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scale"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People even carried the sick out into the streets and laid them on cots and mats… Crowds came in from the towns around Jerusalem too, bringing the sick and those troubled by unclean spirits, and **every one of them was healed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke uses the same phrase Matthew used of the evening at Peter's house: all of them. He is a careful writer — he says elsewhere that he investigated everything closely — and this is what he reports."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shadow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hoping that as Peter came by, at least his shadow might fall across some of them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke records what the people hoped. He does not say the shadow healed anybody, and no apostle is described as teaching it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What the detail conveys is the expectation in the city at that moment. People were carrying relatives into the street on the chance of being in the right place when Peter walked past."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two reactions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No one else dared to attach himself to them, though the people held them in high regard. And still more were added to the Lord, crowds of men and women alike.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both at once — a wariness about joining, and steady growth. Luke has just recorded the deaths of Ananias and Sapphira, and *great fear came on the whole church*. He puts the fear and the crowds in the same paragraph without resolving the tension."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they met"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Solomon's Colonnade* — the covered walkway on the east side of the temple courts. They were meeting in public, at the temple, in the open."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two paragraphs later the apostles are arrested there."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Luke says was happening among them.** *The believers all gathered with one heart.* The healings, the growth and the unity are reported in one sentence, and Luke keeps making that connection through the early chapters — see also Acts 2:40–41."}]}, "Acts 5:17–21": {"title": "\"During the night an angel opened the doors\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The apostles are locked up, let out overnight, and sent straight back to the place they were arrested."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they were arrested"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Filled with **indignation**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **zelos** — jealousy, zeal. Luke names the motive, and it is not doctrine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before this describe crowds coming from the surrounding towns with the sick, and *they were all healed*. The temple authorities are watching their standing evaporate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **during the night** an angel of the Lord opened the doors of the jail.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No earthquake, no drama recorded. Doors opened, men brought out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke reports the rescue in half a sentence and spends the rest of the paragraph on what they were told to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the angel says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go, stand in the temple, and tell the people all the words of this life.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *go home*, not *leave the city*, not *lie low*. Go back to the temple courts — the most public place in Jerusalem, where they were picked up — and preach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rescue is not from the trouble. It is back into it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"All the words of this life\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *this life* — a striking phrase."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been arrested for talking about a resurrection, and the message is described by the angel as **life**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hearing that, they went into the temple **at daybreak** and began to teach.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do not wait or discuss it. They are teaching by first light."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comedy of the next scene"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Meanwhile the high priest convenes the entire Sanhedrin, sends to the prison for the prisoners, and the officers come back with:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"We found the prison shut securely, and the guards standing outside before the doors; but when we had opened them, we found no one inside!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The doors were locked. The guards were at their posts. Nobody had left, and nobody was there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then somebody comes in and says: *\"Look, the men whom you put in prison are standing in the temple and teaching the people!\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they say when re-arrested"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**We ought to obey God rather than men.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Gamaliel's advice to the council: *if this plan is of men, it will come to nothing; but if it is of God, you cannot overthrow it — lest you even be found to fight against God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How they leave"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They departed from the presence of the council, **rejoicing that they were counted worthy to suffer shame for His name**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had just been beaten."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they did next.** *Daily in the temple, and in every house, they did not cease teaching and preaching Jesus as the Christ.* The angel's instruction was obeyed permanently."}]}, "Acts 6:5–7": {"title": "Seven Greek names", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The church's first administrative decision, and the choice of men is the interesting part."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now in those days… there arose **a complaint against the Hebrews by the Hellenists, because their widows were neglected in the daily distribution**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Greek-speaking Jewish believers versus Aramaic-speaking ones. A cultural fault line, and food was going to one side."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first recorded internal dispute in the church is about fairness to widows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The apostles' answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is not desirable that we should leave the word of God and serve tables… **seek out from among you seven men of good reputation, full of the Holy Spirit and wisdom**, whom we may appoint over this business.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But we will give ourselves continually to prayer and to the ministry of the word.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do not take it on and they do not dismiss it. They delegate it with a job specification."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who chooses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This suggestion **pleased the whole gathering, and they chose**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The congregation picks the men. The apostles set the criteria and appoint them afterwards."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seven names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Stephen, Philip, Prochorus, Nicanor, Timon, Parmenas, and Nicolas.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one is Greek."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The complaint came from the Greek-speaking side, and the entire committee is drawn from it. Nobody split the seats."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **Nicolas, a convert from Antioch**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A proselyte — a Gentile who had converted to Judaism first. He is on the list too."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Stephen, a man **full of faith and of the Holy Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Within one chapter he is doing signs and arguing in the synagogue, and by chapter 7 he is dead. See the note on Acts 6:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Philip.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "By chapter 8 he is in Samaria, and then on the Gaza road with an Ethiopian official and a scroll. See the note on Acts 8:26–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two men appointed to organise a food distribution, and both end up as the two most significant evangelists in the early chapters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commissioning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **presented these men to the apostles**, who **prayed and laid their hands on them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Prayer and hands, the same pattern as Antioch later. See the note on Acts 13:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the word of God kept spreading**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **euxanen** — grew."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's marker sentence, and he uses it after trouble every time. See the note on Acts 12:24–25."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here the trouble was a complaint about lunch, and the growth clause follows the fix."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The number of disciples in Jerusalem **multiplied greatly**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **a large company of the priests became obedient to the faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **polus ochlos ton hiereon**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Priests. The men whose livelihood was the temple system, and whose colleagues had condemned Jesus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke drops it in as the third item on a list and does not elaborate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the passage models"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A real grievance, named honestly. A structural fix rather than a rebuke. The complaining party given the whole committee. And the leadership protecting what only they could do."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the seats were allocated.** All seven to the side that raised the complaint — and two of them turn out to be Stephen and Philip."}]}, "Acts 6:8–11": {"title": "They could not hold their own", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "They lose the argument, so they change the subject to blasphemy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Stephen, **full of faith and power**, did **great wonders and signs** among the people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven men had been chosen a few verses earlier to *serve tables* — to sort out a complaint that the Greek-speaking widows were being overlooked in the daily distribution."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stephen is first on the list, and the qualification given was *full of the Holy Spirit and wisdom*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was appointed to administer a food programme and Luke records him doing signs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opposition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **some men stood up and argued with him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sunzetountes** — disputing, debating."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Men from the synagogue called **the Synagogue of the Freedmen**, along with **Cyrenians and Alexandrians** and others from **Cilicia and Asia**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Freedmen** — the Greek is **Libertinon**, from the Latin. Descendants of Jews taken to Rome as slaves and later freed, who had come back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Greek-speaking, diaspora synagogue. These are Stephen's own people, arguing with him in their own language."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Cilicia** is Tarsus, and Saul of Tarsus is standing at the end of this chapter holding the coats."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **could not hold their own against the wisdom and the Spirit by which he spoke**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ouk ischuon antistenai** — they were not strong enough to stand against."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is Luke's fulfilment of a promise: *I will give you a mouth and wisdom which **all your adversaries will not be able to contradict or resist***. See the note on Luke 21:10–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same verb, same author, deliberate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they **quietly arranged for men to say**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hupebalon** — suborned, put men up to it. It is the word for smuggling something in underneath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They lost in public and organised a lie in private."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"We have heard him speak **blasphemous words against Moses and against God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses first."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next two verses add the rest: *this man does not cease to speak blasphemous words against this holy place and the law*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two charges brought against Jesus — the temple and the law — and both times the witnesses are described as arranged."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What his face looked like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And all who sat in the council, looking steadfastly at him, **saw his face as the face of an angel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last line of the chapter, and the high priest's question comes in the next verse: *are these things so?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with the question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He answers it with the longest speech in Acts — fifty-three verses of Israel's history, from Abraham in Mesopotamia to Solomon's temple."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the argument runs: God met Abraham in Mesopotamia, Joseph in Egypt, Moses in Midian and at a bush in the desert. *The Most High does not dwell in temples made with hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The charge was that he spoke against the holy place. He answers by showing that God kept turning up outside it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The end of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You **stiff-necked and uncircumcised in heart and ears!** You always resist the Holy Spirit.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *I see the heavens opened and the Son of Man **standing** at the right hand of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everywhere else in the New Testament He is seated. Stephen sees Him on His feet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the stoning produced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Saul was consenting to his death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *at that time a great persecution arose against the church which was at Jerusalem; and they were all scattered… **therefore those who were scattered went everywhere preaching the word**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first killing of a Christian is the thing that gets the message out of the city."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the charge was invented.** Luke says it plainly: they could not hold their own in the argument. The blasphemy accusation is what happened after the debate was lost."}]}, "Acts 7:35–43": {"title": "The one they refused", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Stephen is on trial, and this is the part of his defence where the pattern becomes the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Accused before the Sanhedrin of speaking against the temple and the law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He answers with a history of Israel, running from Abraham forward, and the council listens for fifty verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This Moses, the one they refused**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **touton ton Mousen hon ernesanto** — this Moses, whom they denied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Arneomai** — to deny, to disown. The verb used of Peter in the courtyard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Who made you a ruler and a judge?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two Hebrews fighting, and Moses intervened, and one of them said it. He fled to Midian the next day and stayed forty years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This is the very one God sent as ruler and deliverer**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **archonta kai lutroten** — ruler and redeemer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The exact title they threw at him as a sneer is the one God gave him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern Stephen is building"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph: *the patriarchs, becoming envious, sold Joseph into Egypt. But God was with him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses: refused, and sent back as ruler."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rejected deliverer is the sent one. Twice, before Stephen gets anywhere near his conclusion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The forty years"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He brought them out, doing wonders and signs **in the land of Egypt, and at the Red Sea, and in the wilderness for forty years**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prophet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is that Moses who said, '**The Lord your God will raise up for you a Prophet like me from among your brothers. Listen to Him.**'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 18, quoted with its last two words: **autou akousesthe** — listen to Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The living words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The one who received **living words to hand on to us**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **logia zonta** — living oracles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stephen is on trial for speaking against the law, and he calls it living."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Our fathers were unwilling to obey him. They pushed him away, and in their hearts they turned back toward Egypt**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **aposanto** — thrust away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Estraphesan en tais kardiais auton eis Aigupton** — they turned in their hearts to Egypt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Physically out, and inwardly heading back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The calf"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They said to Aaron, '**Make us gods to go ahead of us**, because as for **this Moses**… we have no idea what has become of him.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Quoted from Exodus 32. See the note on Exodus 32:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**This Moses** — the same dismissive phrase Stephen has just used ironically at the start of the paragraph."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The celebration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In those days they made a calf, and they brought a sacrifice to the idol and **celebrated the work of their own hands**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **euphrainonto en tois ergois ton cheiron auton** — they rejoiced in the works of their hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **God turned away and gave them over** to the worship of the host of heaven.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paredoken** — handed them over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb Paul uses three times in Romans 1: *therefore God also gave them up*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The judgment is being let have what they chose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amos 5, from the Septuagint: *\"House of Israel, **was it to Me that you brought slain animals and sacrifices for forty years in the wilderness?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And I will carry you away beyond Babylon.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amos said *beyond Damascus*. Stephen extends it to the exile that actually came."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the speech ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You stiff-necked and uncircumcised in heart and ears!** You always resist the Holy Spirit; as your fathers did, so do you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Which of the prophets did your fathers not persecute?\"* See the note on Acts 7:51–53."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pattern he has been laying out for fifty verses is turned on the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They stone him, and *the witnesses laid down their clothes at the feet of a young man named Saul*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The construction.** *This Moses, the one they refused — this is the very one God sent.* Stephen states it of Joseph, then of Moses, and lets the council reach the third case themselves."}]}, "Acts 7:54–60": {"title": "The one time He is seen standing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke reports a detail here that he does not report anywhere else, and the whole scene turns on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Sitting, everywhere but here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The New Testament describes the risen Christ at the right hand of God more than a dozen times, and almost always **seated** — Hebrews makes a point of it, because a seated priest is one whose work is finished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stephen, with the court screaming at him, looks up and sees Jesus **standing**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says it twice in two verses, once as narration and once in Stephen's own words. He is not being careless with a posture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Stephen says as he dies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two sentences, and both of them are Jesus' own words from the cross with one word changed:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Lord Jesus, receive my spirit* — Jesus had said *Father, into Your hands I commit my spirit*", "*Lord, do not hold this sin against them* — Jesus had said *Father, forgive them*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stephen prays to Jesus what Jesus prayed to the Father. For the first Christian to die, that is a considerable thing to have on record, and Luke simply lets it stand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He fell asleep\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke has just described a stoning in unflinching detail, and he ends it with the gentlest verb available. It became the ordinary Christian word for a believer's death — the same one Paul uses in Thessalonians — and it did not mean the death was mild. It meant it was not final."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The coats at the young man's feet.** Luke names him: Saul. He will not mention it again for chapters, but it is here, in the first paragraph about a Christian being killed, that Paul enters the story — holding the coats."}]}, "Acts 8:26–29": {"title": "The desert road", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Philip is in the middle of a revival and is sent to an empty road."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter before this: Philip in Samaria, *and the multitudes with one accord heeded the things spoken by Philip… and there was great joy in that city.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A city-wide response, and he is told to leave it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get up and go south**, to the road that runs down from Jerusalem to Gaza.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No reason given. No name, no destination, no explanation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Luke adds his own note: ***It is a desert road.***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is sent from a crowd to a place with nobody on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The obedience"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So he **got up and went**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words. No question is recorded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is on the road"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man from Ethiopia, **a court official of Candace** the queen of the Ethiopians, **in charge of all her treasury**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The finance minister of a kingdom, in a chariot, on the way home."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Candace** is a title rather than a name — the queen mother of the Nubian kingdom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had been doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He had **come to Jerusalem to worship** and was now on his way home.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A journey of some fifteen hundred miles, each way, to worship at a temple that would not have let him past the outer court."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is a God-fearer from the ends of the known world, going home with a scroll."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is doing in the chariot"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sitting in his chariot and **reading aloud from the prophet Isaiah**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Reading aloud was normal in the ancient world — texts were read out even in private."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A scroll of Isaiah was an enormous expense. He had bought one, and he is reading it in a moving vehicle in a desert."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit said to Philip, \"**Go over to that chariot and stay near it.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angel gave the road. The Spirit gives the chariot."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Guidance arrives in two stages, and Philip had to act on the first before he got the second."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Philip **ran** up alongside.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He runs, beside a moving chariot, and hears what is being read."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage the man had reached is Isaiah 53 — *led like a sheep to the slaughter*. See the note on Acts 8:30–35."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it ends.** *The Spirit of the Lord caught Philip away… but Philip was found at Azotus.* Luke reports it flatly, and the next thing recorded is that he kept preaching through every city until he came to Caesarea. Twenty years later he is still there, with four daughters who prophesied, hosting Paul."}]}, "Acts 8:30–35": {"title": "\"Unless someone guides me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man is reading the right passage and cannot get into it, and somebody arrives running."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do you understand what you are reading?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Philip does not open with an announcement. He asks."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek has a play on it — *ginoskeis ha anaginoskeis*, do you know what you are reading — and it survives in the English *do you get what you have got*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How can I, unless someone guides me?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No defensiveness. A treasury official, in his own chariot, on his own road, says he cannot do it alone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And he *invited Philip to come up and sit with him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was reading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 53:7–8, quoted in full:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was led like a sheep to the slaughter, and like a lamb silent before the one who shears it, **so He does not open His mouth**. In His humiliation His justice was taken away. And who can tell of His generation? For **His life is taken from the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of the whole scroll, he is at that page."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question underneath the question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Tell me, please, **who is the prophet speaking about? Himself, or someone else?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the right question, and it is the one Jewish readers had argued about for centuries."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has no idea who the answer is, and he knows the passage is about somebody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Philip does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Philip opened his mouth, and **beginning from that very Scripture** he told him the good news about Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He starts where the man already was. He does not go back to Genesis or forward to a different text."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *beginning from this Scripture* — that verse, that scroll, that sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two men, one road"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Philip left a revival in Samaria for an empty desert road, because he was told to. See the note on Acts 8:26–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Ethiopian had travelled fifteen hundred miles to worship at a temple that would not let him past the outer court, and was going home with a scroll he could not understand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both journeys converge on one chariot at one moment, and neither man arranged it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look, here is water. **What is there to keep me from being baptized?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In a desert. See the note on Acts 8:36–38."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Which verse he had not reached.** Isaiah 53 goes on: *He was **wounded for our transgressions**, He was **bruised for our iniquities**… and by His stripes we are healed.* Whatever Philip said, that was a few lines further down the same column. See the note on Isaiah 53:4–6."}]}, "Acts 8:36–38": {"title": "\"Look, here is water\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man in a desert finds water and asks the only question left."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who spots it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As they went on down the road **they came to some water, and the official said, \"Look, here is water.\"***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not Philip. The Ethiopian."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke had already told us the road was a desert one. The man notices water in a place that does not have much, and knows immediately what it is for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What is there to keep me from being baptized?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every honest answer available in the first century would have been *a great deal*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is a Gentile, from a foreign court, and — as an official of that kind — a eunuch. Deuteronomy 23 barred a eunuch from the assembly of the LORD outright."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had travelled fifteen hundred miles to a temple that would not let him past the outer court."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks what is in the way, and Philip does not list anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If you believe with all your heart, you may.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"I believe that Jesus Christ is the Son of God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the entire examination."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah says about him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been reading Isaiah 53. Three chapters further on, in the same scroll, is this:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not let the eunuch say, \"Here I am, a dry tree.\" For thus says the LORD: **to the eunuchs who keep My Sabbaths… I will give them a place and a name better than that of sons and daughters. I will give them an everlasting name that shall not be cut off.***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 56, addressed to exactly his condition, written centuries earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stopping"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **gave the order to stop the chariot**, and the two of them went down into the water.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A senior official halts his own convoy in a desert to be baptised by a man who ran up alongside an hour earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When they came up out of the water, the Spirit of the Lord caught Philip away, and the official saw him no more, **and went on his way rejoicing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Philip vanishes. The man is left standing in a desert with wet clothes, no teacher, and a scroll — and Luke's note is that he went on rejoicing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the gospel had reached"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Acts 1:8 set the programme: *Jerusalem, and in all Judea and Samaria, and to the end of the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Philip had just done Samaria. This man is going home to the southern edge of the known world."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse that keeps some manuscripts apart.** Verse 37 — *if you believe with all your heart, you may* — is absent from the oldest Greek manuscripts and present in many later ones. Whatever its history, the confession it records is the only requirement anyone in the passage names."}]}, "Acts 9:10–14": {"title": "Ananias is sent", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The man who has to knock on the door, and he argues."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There was **a disciple in Damascus named Ananias**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is all Luke gives. He appears in this chapter, is mentioned in Paul's own retelling as *a devout man according to the law, having a good testimony with all the Jews who dwelt there*, and is never heard of again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The call"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the Lord spoke to him **in a vision**. \"**Ananias**,\" He said. \"**Here I am, Lord**,\" he answered.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew equivalent is **hinneni** — Abraham's word at the mountain, Moses' at the bush, Samuel's in the night. See the note on Genesis 22:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Get up and go to **the street called Straight**, and ask at **the house of Judas** for a man **from Tarsus named Saul**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A street, a householder, a hometown and a name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Street Called Straight ran east to west through Damascus and still does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives the address with the precision of a man who checked it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Saul is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He is praying at this moment**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days blind, eating and drinking nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other vision"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **he has seen in a vision a man named Ananias coming in and laying hands on him so that he might see again**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two visions again, as with Cornelius and Peter — and again neither man knows about the other's until told. See the note on Acts 10:17–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul has already seen Ananias arrive. Ananias is being asked to complete a picture that has been shown to somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Lord, I have heard from many people about this man** and **all the harm he has done to Your holy ones in Jerusalem**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kaka** — evils."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not exaggerating. Luke has recorded it: *Saul made havoc of the church, entering every house, and dragging off men and women, committing them to prison.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **he is here with authority from the chief priests to arrest everyone who calls on Your name**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The warrants are in the house on Straight Street."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ananias would be one of the names on them. *Everyone who calls on Your name* is a description of himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is being sent to lay hands on the man who came to arrest him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go, for he is a chosen vessel of Mine** to bear My name before Gentiles, kings, and the children of Israel. **For I will show him how many things he must suffer for My name's sake**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a reassurance about safety. An assignment for the other man, and a note about what it will cost him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Ananias does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Ananias went his way and entered the house; and **laying his hands on him he said, \"Brother Saul…\"**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Brother.** The first word out of his mouth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"…the Lord Jesus, who appeared to you on the road as you came, has sent me that you may receive your sight and be filled with the Holy Spirit.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Immediately there fell from his eyes something like scales, and he received his sight at once.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul remembers him saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In his own account years later, Ananias adds: *the God of our fathers has chosen you that you should know His will… **and now why are you waiting? Arise and be baptised**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Ananias is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who let Paul into the church. Everything Paul wrote came through a door somebody had to be argued into opening."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he is not given.** Any assurance for himself. He raises the objection — the man has warrants and my name is on them — and the answer is about what the other man is for."}]}, "Acts 9:3–6": {"title": "Light on the Damascus road", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four verses, and everything in Paul's life afterward comes out of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *Saul, still **breathing threats and murder** against the disciples of the Lord, went to the high priest and asked letters from him to the synagogues of Damascus, so that if he found any who were of the Way, he might bring them bound to Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is on official business with warrants in his bag. Damascus is a hundred and fifty miles from Jerusalem — a week's walk, undertaken deliberately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The light"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Suddenly **a light out of heaven blazed all around him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells it twice more in Acts, and each time adds a detail: *at midday*, and *brighter than the sun*, and *shining around me and those who journeyed with me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Midday, and it outshone the sun."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fall"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **fell to the ground**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name, twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Saul, Saul**, why are you persecuting Me?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The doubled name — *Abraham, Abraham*, *Moses, Moses*, *Samuel, Samuel*, *Martha, Martha*, *Simon, Simon*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every time in Scripture it precedes something serious, and every time it is warm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Why are you **persecuting Me**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *why are you persecuting them*. Paul had never met Jesus and had been arresting people in houses in Jerusalem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The identification is the whole of what he is told, and his letters never stop working it out: *you are the body of Christ*, and *if one member suffers, all the members suffer with it*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Who are You, Lord?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek **kurie** can be simply *sir*. He does not yet know."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is one of the few genuinely open questions in Scripture, asked by a man face down in the road."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am Jesus, whom you are persecuting**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In his own account to Agrippa he adds *Jesus **of Nazareth***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The goads"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is hard for you to **kick against the goads**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A goad was a pointed stick used to drive oxen. An animal that kicked back drove it in further."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase was a Greek proverb — it appears in Euripides — and Paul, from Tarsus, would have known it as a piece of ordinary speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The next question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Trembling and astonished, he said, \"**Lord, what do You want me to do?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second use of *Lord* in the passage, and the man asking is the one who had letters in his bag an hour earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get up and go into the city, and you will be told what you must do**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not told what the assignment is. He is told to go and wait to be told."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What followed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days without sight, eating and drinking nothing, in a house on Straight Street — and then Ananias, who had to be argued into going: *Lord, I have heard from many about this man, how much harm he has done to Your saints.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer: *Go, for **he is a chosen vessel of Mine** to bear My name before Gentiles, kings, and the children of Israel. For **I will show him how many things he must suffer** for My name's sake.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How Paul describes it later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Last of all He was seen by me too, **as by a child born at the wrong time**.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 15:3–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *I am the least of the apostles, who am not worthy to be called an apostle, because I persecuted the church of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never stops mentioning it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he was given on the road.** A name, a question, and an instruction to go and be told. The commission itself was delivered days later, through a frightened man who did not want to knock on the door."}]}, "Acts 9:36–43": {"title": "\"Tabitha, get up\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only woman in the New Testament called a disciple, and the room is full of the coats she made."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In Joppa there was **a disciple named Tabitha, which in Greek is Dorcas**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **mathetria** — the feminine of disciple. It occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tabitha** is Aramaic and **Dorcas** is Greek, and both mean *gazelle*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives both, which tells you Joppa was a mixed town."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Her life was **full of good works and acts of mercy to the poor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *full of good works and almsdeeds which she did*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The death"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In those days she **fell ill and died**, and after they had **washed her** they laid her in **an upstairs room**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The washing is the preparation for burial. In that climate burial followed within a day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They wash her and then stop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The message"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the disciples heard that Peter was there they sent two men to him, urging him, \"**Please come to us without delay**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lydda is about ten miles from Joppa. They send two men and do not say why."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the message asks for anything specific. They simply want him there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When he arrived they took him to the upstairs room, and **all the widows stood around him weeping, showing him the tunics and other clothes Dorcas had made while she was still with them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *displaying* — holding them up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are wearing the evidence. Every woman in the room has something she made."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Widows in that world had no income and no legal standing, and the early church's first administrative crisis was about their daily distribution."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peter **sent them all outside**, and he **knelt down and prayed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two actions, in that order."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had watched Jesus do exactly this in Jairus's house: *He put them all outside, and took her by the hand.* See the note on Luke 8:49–56."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter was one of the three allowed to stay in that room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he turned toward the body and said, \"**Tabitha, get up**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Aramaic would have been **Tabitha koum**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark records what Jesus said to the girl: **Talitha koum** — *little girl, get up*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One letter apart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She **opened her eyes**, and when she saw Peter she **sat up**. **He gave her his hand and helped her to her feet**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke, a physician, gives it in stages: eyes, sitting, standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The presentation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And then he called the believers and the widows and **presented her to them alive**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Word of it spread through all of Joppa, and **many believed in the Lord**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peter **stayed on in Joppa for some time with a tanner named Simon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A tanner worked with animal skins and was ceremonially unclean by trade. Observant Jews kept their distance, and tanneries were built outside towns by the sea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter lodges with one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the address the men from Caesarea come to in the next chapter, after the sheet and the voice. See the note on Acts 11:4–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The staying is a small detail and Luke puts it there deliberately — the man about to be told *what God has cleansed, you must not call unholy* is already living in a tanner's house."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the widows brought.** Not an argument. They held up the clothes and cried, and Peter put them all outside before he prayed."}]}, "Acts 9:7–9": {"title": "Three days without sight", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The aftermath on the road, and Luke gives the details in the order they happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The men with him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The men traveling with him **stood there speechless. They heard the voice but saw no one**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's account here and Paul's own retellings differ on the detail — chapter 22 has them *seeing the light but not hearing the voice of Him who spoke to me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The likeliest reading is that they heard a sound without hearing words. The Greek allows it, and the point in both accounts is the same: something happened that they registered and could not receive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are witnesses to an event they cannot interpret."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The escort"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were there to help arrest people. Saul had letters from the high priest and a party to execute them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Getting up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Saul got up from the ground, and **when he opened his eyes he could see nothing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had fallen at the light. Luke notes that he opened his eyes deliberately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **they took him by the hand and led him into Damascus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **cheiragogountes** — hand-leading."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He set out at the head of a party with warrants and arrives being led like a child."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the same word himself of Elymas the sorcerer, struck blind at Paphos, *groping about, looking for someone to lead him by the hand*. See the note on Acts 13:9–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul pronounces on another man what had happened to him — and includes the time limit that he had been given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three days"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **three days he was without sight**, and **he neither ate nor drank**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives all three: blind, no food, no water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days in a house on Straight Street, in the city he had come to raid, with nothing to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Lord tells Ananias: *\"**he is praying at this moment**.\"* See the note on Acts 9:10–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"he has seen in a vision a man named Ananias coming in and laying hands on him.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is blind and being shown something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he lost in those days"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything he had been. His letters put it this way: *what things were gain to me, these I have counted loss for Christ.* See the note on Philippians 3:2–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seven credentials he lists there were all intact on the road out of Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What ended it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ananias, a man who had to be argued into coming, laying hands on him and calling him **brother**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Immediately there fell from his eyes **something like scales**, and he received his sight at once.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **lepides** — flakes, scales. Luke, a physician, chooses the word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And immediately he **preached the Christ in the synagogues, that He is the Son of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then all who heard were amazed, and said, \"**Is this not he who destroyed those who called on this name in Jerusalem?**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three days elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pattern is not commented on in the text. Luke reports the count and moves on."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the escort saw.** Enough to stand still and say nothing. They heard something and saw nobody — and then took the man who had been leading them and walked him into the city by the hand."}]}, "Amos 3:3–6": {"title": "Does a lion roar for nothing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Seven rhetorical questions in a row, and they are all making one point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is asking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amos, a herdsman from Tekoa and a tender of sycamore figs, sent north to Israel in a prosperous decade to say the prosperity is over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I was no prophet, nor was I a son of a prophet, but I was a sheepbreeder and a tender of sycamore fruit. **Then the LORD took me as I followed the flock**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The form"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one is a question with an obvious answer, and every answer is no."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The technique is cumulative. By the seventh, the reader has said no six times and is committed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Can two people **walk together unless they have agreed to meet**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *unless they be agreed* — **no'adu**, from the root for an appointed meeting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two people on the same road at the same time did not get there by chance. Something was arranged."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Does a lion **roar in the thicket when he has caught nothing**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Does a young lion **growl in his den unless he has seized his prey**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two different verbs and two different stages. A lion roars when the kill is made, not while stalking — and the second one growls over the carcass at home."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The roar is evidence that something has already happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bird"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Does a bird **fall into a trap on the ground where no snare was set**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Does a trap **spring up from the earth when it has caught nothing** at all?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The trap does not go off by itself. Something is in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trumpet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When **the ram's horn sounds the alarm in a city, do the people not tremble**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **shofar** on the wall meant an army was visible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in a walled town heard that sound and stayed calm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When **trouble comes to a city, has the LORD not sent it**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shall there be calamity in a city, and the LORD has not done it?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hardest sentence in the passage, and it is the one the other six were built to deliver."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chain is arguing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cause and effect. Nothing in the list happens without a reason behind it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the conclusion, in the next verse:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Surely the Lord GOD does nothing, unless **He reveals His secret to His servants the prophets**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole thing is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amos is defending his right to speak."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A lion has roared! Who will not fear? The Lord GOD has spoken! **Who can but prophesy?***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not choosing to prophesy. The roar has already happened and he is the sound of it arriving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was sent to say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You only have I known of all the families of the earth; **therefore I will punish you for all your iniquities**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Privilege as the ground of accountability, not exemption."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the famous lines: *let justice run down like water, and righteousness like a mighty stream*, and *I hate, I despise your feast days*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book ends with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Behold, the days are coming,\" says the LORD, \"**when the plowman shall overtake the reaper**… I will bring back the captives of My people Israel; they shall build the waste cities and inhabit them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Harvest so heavy that the ploughman for the next season catches up with the men still bringing in this one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James quotes the same closing passage at the Jerusalem council to settle the question of Gentile believers."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the seven questions establish.** That nothing in the list is accidental — so that when the seventh one lands, the reader has already agreed to the logic six times."}]}, "Amos 8:11–14": {"title": "A famine for the words of the LORD", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The prophet names a shortage of an unusual commodity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amos was a herdsman from Tekoa and a tender of sycamore figs, not a professional prophet — he says so himself: *I was no prophet, nor was I a son of a prophet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is preaching in the northern kingdom during a boom. Amos 6 describes ivory beds, lambs from the flock, wine by the bowlful."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The announcement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The days are coming,\" declares the Lord GOD, \"when **I will send a famine over the land**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Famine is one of the standard judgments. He then withdraws the usual meaning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Not a hunger for bread, nor a thirst for water**, but **a hunger to hear the words of the LORD**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *not a famine of bread, nor a thirst for water, but of hearing the words of the LORD*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The searching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"People will **stagger from sea to sea** and **wander from north to east**, **searching everywhere for the word of the LORD**, and **they will not find it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *stagger* is the verb for reeling, wandering like a drunk."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three directions and no result. The last clause is the whole point: not that nobody looks, but that looking does not work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction they do not go"}, {"k": "p", "t": "North and east, and sea to sea — and not south. Jerusalem, the temple, and the place Amos himself had come from are south."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What made it a famine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that God stopped speaking. Amos 2 has already recorded the complaint: *I raised up some of your sons as prophets… but you gave the Nazirites wine to drink, and commanded the prophets saying, \"Do not prophesy!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in chapter 7 the priest of Bethel tells Amos to leave: *go, flee to the land of Judah… but never again prophesy at Bethel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The famine is the granting of a request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who faints"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The **lovely young women and the strong young men** will faint from thirst.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The strongest and the healthiest. A famine takes them last, which is how you know how bad it is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had been swearing by"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Those who **swear by the shame of Samaria**, who say, '**As surely as your god lives, Dan**,' and, '**As surely as the way of Beersheba lives**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three shrines. Dan had one of Jeroboam's golden calves; Beersheba was a pilgrimage site in the far south."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not irreligious. They have three functioning centres of worship and a famine of the word at the same time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Amos had already said about their services"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I hate, I despise your feast days… Take away from Me the noise of your songs… **But let justice run down like water**, and righteousness like a mighty stream.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the New Testament says stands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The grass dries out. The flower drops off. **But what the Lord says stands, and keeps standing**.* See the note on 1 Peter 1:24–25."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is being taken away.** Not the buildings, the songs, or the pilgrimages. Those stay. What goes is the word — and Amos says people will cross the country looking for it."}]}, "Amos 9:11–12": {"title": "The fallen shelter of David", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last page of the harshest of the prophets, and it turns."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has come before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight and a half chapters of judgement. Amos was a shepherd and a dresser of sycamore figs, not a professional prophet, and the book is unrelenting: *I hate, I despise your feast days… take away from Me the noise of your songs.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five visions of destruction, and then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In that day I will **raise up David's fallen shelter**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **sukkah** — a booth, a hut, the temporary shelter Israel lived in during the Feast of Tabernacles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the house of David, and not the throne. A **shelter**, and a fallen one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The dynasty is described at its lowest — not a palace but a lean-to, and that collapsed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **raise up**… I will **mend** its broken walls… **set its ruins right**… **build it as it was in the days of old**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of them is His. Nothing in the sentence is asked of anybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So they may possess what is left of Edom and all the nations that bear My name**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Edom — Esau's people, Israel's oldest enemy. And *all the nations that bear My name*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gentiles, named in the promise, in the last paragraph of Amos."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What James did with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Acts 15, the Jerusalem council. The question is whether Gentiles have to be circumcised and keep the law to belong."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter reports what happened at Cornelius's house. Paul and Barnabas describe the miracles among the nations. And then James stands up and quotes this passage:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**After this I will return and will rebuild the tabernacle of David, which has fallen down**… so that the rest of mankind may seek the LORD, even all the Gentiles who are called by My name.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His conclusion: *therefore I judge that we should not trouble those from among the Gentiles who are turning to God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse that settled the first great church controversy is the second-to-last paragraph of Amos."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the tabernacle of David was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the tabernacle of Moses. David pitched a tent for the ark in Jerusalem and left it there, with singers and musicians before it continually — no veil, no sacrifice system, and access that the Mosaic arrangement did not allow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It stood for about forty years, until Solomon's temple."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The days are coming** when the plowman will overtake the reaper.\"* See the note on Amos 9:13–15."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the book ends.** *\"I will plant them in their own land, and **never again will they be uprooted**,\" says the LORD your God.* Nine chapters of demolition, and the last word is planting."}]}, "Amos 9:13–15": {"title": "\"The plowman will overtake the reaper\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A picture of a farming year that will not fit into a year."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The days are coming when **the plowman will overtake the reaper**, and **the one who treads the grapes the one who plants the seed**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In an ordinary season these are months apart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You reap in spring, thresh, and then wait until the autumn rains soften the ground before you can plough again. You harvest grapes in late summer and sow after them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amos describes a harvest so long that the man ploughing for next year catches up with the man still bringing in this one — and a vintage still being trodden when the next sowing starts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seasons overlap and stop being a queue."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**New wine will drip from the mountains** and flow down every hill.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that the mountains will drip sweet wine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Vineyards were terraced on hillsides and the presses cut into the rock. The picture is of so much fruit that the hills themselves run with it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joel says the same thing in almost the same words: *the mountains shall drip with new wine, the hills shall flow with milk.* See the note on Joel 2:25–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four things restored"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will **rebuild the ruined cities** and live in them. They will **plant vineyards and drink their wine**. They will **make gardens and eat their fruit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point of each clause is who gets the benefit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the exact reversal of the curse in Deuteronomy 28: *you shall build a house and not dwell in it; you shall plant a vineyard and not gather its grapes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is the same promise as Psalm 128 — *you will eat the fruit of your labour.* See the note on Psalms 128:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The planting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will plant them in their own land**, and never again will they be uprooted from the land I have given them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gardener language is applied to the people. They plant vineyards; He plants them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Directly after the promise to raise up David's fallen shelter — the verse James quoted at the Jerusalem council to settle whether Gentiles could come in without the law. See the note on Amos 9:11–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The acceleration follows the rebuilding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book was before this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amos was a shepherd from Tekoa, and the book is nine chapters of judgement on Israel, Judah and six surrounding nations, with five visions of ruin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Woe to you who are at ease in Zion.\"* *\"I hate, I despise your feast days.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the last five verses."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last three words.** *Says the LORD **your God**.* The possessive is the final word of a book that spent nine chapters describing what He was going to tear down."}]}, "Colossians 1:15–20": {"title": "\"In Him all of it holds together\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Probably an early hymn, quoted by Paul, and it is the highest sustained statement about Christ in his letters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Firstborn of all creation\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase has been misread for centuries as making Him part of what was created — and the next sentence rules that out: *in Him everything was created.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Prototokos** in the Old Testament is a title of rank, not chronology. God calls Israel His firstborn, and David — the youngest of eight sons — is called firstborn in Psalm 89. It means the one with the rights of the heir."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prepositions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hymn turns on four small words, and they cover everything:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["created **in** Him", "created **through** Him", "created **for** Him", "and He is **before** all things"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the line that holds the passage together: *in Him all of it holds together.* The verb means to stand together, to cohere. The universe is described as continuously held rather than wound up and released."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two firstborns"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is *firstborn of all creation* and *firstborn from among the dead*. The hymn puts the two in parallel — first place in the old creation and in the new one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the stated purpose: *so that in everything He might hold the first place.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Through Him He should bring everything back into peace with Himself, having made that peace by the blood of His cross.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "After thrones, lordships, rulers and authorities, after all things visible and invisible held together — the hymn ends at a cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blood is what the cosmic language was building toward, which is what keeps the passage from being an abstraction."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it lands next.** *And you, who once were alienated and enemies in your mind… He has now reconciled.* Having described the whole universe being brought back, Paul turns immediately to the readers and applies it to them personally. See the note on 2 Corinthians 5:18–19."}]}, "Colossians 1:9–12": {"title": "The chain of a prayer", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of Paul's two great prayers, and it runs as a chain — each link producing the next."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The links"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**filled with the knowledge of what He wants**, with wisdom and spiritual understanding", "*so that* you **walk in a way worthy of the Lord**, pleasing Him through and through", "**bearing fruit in every good work** and **growing in the knowledge of God**", "**strengthened with every strength** His glorious power supplies", "ready for **endurance and patience** — *and all of it with joy*", "**giving thanks to the Father**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "It begins with knowing and ends with thanking. Everything in between follows from the first link."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not strength. **Knowledge of what God wants** — and the word for knowledge here is the intensified form, full or thorough knowing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's prayers consistently start here. He treats seeing rightly as the thing that produces walking rightly, rather than the other way round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The strength, and what it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Strengthened with every strength that His own glorious power supplies, ready for endurance of every kind and for patience that does not run out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Enormous language — glorious power, every strength — and then the purpose lands somewhere unexpected. Not for miracles or conquest. For **endurance and patience**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the last three words change the tone completely: *and all of it with joy.* Not grim survival. Staying power that is glad."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last link"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Giving thanks to the Father, who has made us fit to take our share of what belongs to His holy people in the light.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is about qualifying someone, making them sufficient. It is past tense and God is the subject: the fitness was conferred, not developed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the prayer goes next.** Straight into one of the highest passages about Christ in the New Testament — the image of the invisible God, in whom all things hold together. Paul prays for knowledge and then immediately supplies some."}]}, "Colossians 2:1–3": {"title": "For people who have never met him", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul describes what he is doing for strangers, and the word he uses is athletic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I want you to know **how great a struggle I am having** for you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **agona** — a contest, a fight in the arena. The root of *agony*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just used the verb: *to this end I also labour, **striving** according to His working which works in me mightily*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Praying is described as an event in a stadium."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For you, and for those in **Laodicea**, and for **everyone who has never met me face to face**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had not founded Colossae. Epaphras did — *as you also learned from Epaphras, our dear fellow servant*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is writing to a church he has never visited, in a town he has never been to, from prison."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the letter's last chapter names his condition: *remember my chains*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Laodicea"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten miles from Colossae, and the letter is to be swapped: *when this epistle is read among you, see that it is read also in the church of the Laodiceans, and that you likewise read the epistle from Laodicea*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Laodicean letter is lost."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is also the church Christ addresses last in Revelation: *because you say, \"I am rich, have become wealthy, and have need of nothing\"*. See the note on Revelation 3:14–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is asking for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That their **hearts may be given courage**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paraklethosin** — encouraged, comforted, called alongside. Same root as **parakletos**, the Helper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bound together in love.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sumbibasthentes** — knit together, joined."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The destination"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And brought into **all the wealth of an understanding that is full and sure**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **plerophorias** — full assurance, complete conviction. The word Paul uses of Abraham being *fully convinced*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks for riches, and the riches are certainty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The object"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Into **the knowledge of the mystery of God, and of the Father, and of Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epignosin** — full knowledge, thorough knowing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Mysterion** — not a puzzle. Something hidden that has now been disclosed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In whom **every treasure of wisdom and knowledge lies hidden**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apokruphoi** — hidden away, stored up. The root of *apocryphal*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words: **sophia** and **gnosis**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it matters in this letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Colossae was being sold something extra."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verses name it: *beware lest anyone cheat you through philosophy and empty deceit, according to the tradition of men*. And *let no one cheat you of your reward, taking delight in false humility and worship of angels*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the food rules: *do not touch, do not taste, do not handle*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody was offering a higher knowledge, available to initiates."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's answer is that the treasures are all in one place already, and the place is a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For in Him dwells all the fullness of the Godhead bodily; and **you are complete in Him**.* See the note on Colossians 2:8–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pepleromenoi** — having been filled full."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every word in the argument is about fullness: the fullness dwells in Him, and you have been filled in Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is missing, so nothing can be added."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The letter's summary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ in you, the hope of glory.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he wrestles for.** Not their behaviour. Certainty — and the reason given is that everything on offer elsewhere is already stored up in one person."}]}, "Colossians 2:11–15": {"title": "Nailed to the cross", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul runs five verbs together and every one of them is God's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The circumcision"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In Him you were also circumcised, with **a circumcision no human hand performed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **acheiropoietos** — not made with hands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Colossae was being pressed toward Jewish observance. Paul does not argue about the knife; he says the thing it stood for has already happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Buried and raised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You were **buried with Him** in baptism, and in that same baptism **you were raised with Him too**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both in one sentence, and both past tense."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By **trusting what God was at work doing** — the God who raised Him from the dead.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *through faith in the working of God*. The faith is in what God did, not in what the believer manages."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And you, who were **dead in your trespasses** and in the uncircumcision of your flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dead. Not weakened, not struggling. Paul's diagnosis leaves nothing for the patient to contribute."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **made alive together with Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One Greek word — **suzoopoieo**, to make alive together with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The forgiveness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Forgiving you **every trespass**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **charizomai** — from **charis**, grace. To give freely, to grant as a favour. It is not the courtroom word for acquittal; it is the word for a gift."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Every trespass** — all of them, in one clause, with no accounting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The note"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **erased the written note that stood against us**, with all its demands set down in our disfavor.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **cheirographon** — a handwritten document, specifically an IOU. A debtor wrote it out in his own hand as an acknowledgement of what he owed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Erased** is **exaleipho** — to wipe out, to sponge off. Ancient ink sat on the surface of papyrus and could be washed away, leaving the sheet blank."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Removed and nailed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has **removed it from between us**, **nailing it to the cross**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two more verbs. Taken out of the way, and then fixed to the beam."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The charge sheet is put where the body went."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **stripped the rulers and authorities of their weapons**, **put them on open display**, and **led them in triumph** in it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The triumph image again — the Roman victory procession, the general in front and the defeated walking behind. Paul uses it in 2 Corinthians of the believers. See the note on 2 Corinthians 2:14–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here the ones in the column are the powers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the display happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In it* — in the cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The place that looked like the defeat is named as the parade."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul is arguing against"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses on either side: *let no one judge you in food or in drink, or regarding a festival or a new moon or sabbaths*, and *why do you subject yourselves to regulations — do not touch, do not taste, do not handle?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list of what God did is his answer to a list of what the Colossians were being told to do."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Whose hands are in the passage.** Not the reader's. Circumcised without hands, buried, raised, made alive, forgiven, the note erased and nailed up — and the only thing asked of anybody is trusting what God was at work doing."}]}, "Colossians 2:4–7": {"title": "Keep on walking in Him", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is writing to a church he has never met, and the thing he is guarding them against is not immorality. It is persuasive speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Talk you around\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I say this so that no one may **talk you around** with words that sound persuasive.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is a reckoning word — to miscount, to defraud someone by faulty arithmetic, and so to beguile with plausible reasoning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The danger named is not error that sounds wrong. It is error that adds up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Good order and firm faith"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am glad to see the good order among you and how firm your faith in Christ stands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both nouns are military. **Taxis** is a rank drawn up in formation; **stereōma** is a solid front, a bulwark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul, writing from prison to people he has never seen, describes them as a line that is holding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as you **received** Christ Jesus the Lord, **keep on walking** in Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs, and the second is present tense — continuous. However you got in is how you go on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You received Him — you did not achieve Him, negotiate Him, or qualify for Him. Walk the same way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four participles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then four pictures stacked in one sentence, and they do not come from one world:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**rooted in Him** — agricultural, and the tense is a completed act; the root went down once", "**being built up in Him** — architectural, and this one is ongoing", "**made firm in the faith exactly as you were taught** — legal, the word used of a contract being confirmed", "**overflowing with thanksgiving** — a river over its banks"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rooted is done. Built up is happening. That order is deliberate."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter goes on to name.** Philosophy and empty deceit, human tradition, food rules, festivals, new moons, sabbaths, angel worship. All of it answered by one sentence: *in Him all the fullness of the Deity lives in bodily form, and you have been made full in Him.*"}]}, "Colossians 2:8–10": {"title": "\"In Him you have been made complete\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul warns against something and then, instead of arguing point by point, states a fact that makes the alternative unnecessary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Take care that no one carries you off as his prize.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is used of kidnapping — carrying someone off as plunder. Not persuading; taking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what does the carrying: *philosophy and hollow deceit… traditions handed down by men… the elementary lessons the world teaches.* Colossae was being sold a package of rules, visions and observances as an upgrade."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fullness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For in Him all the fullness of what God is lives in bodily form.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Pleroma** — fullness, the whole content. The word was in use among the teachers Paul is opposing, and he takes it off them: the fullness is not spread across a hierarchy of spiritual beings. It is in Christ, and it is *bodily*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence that ends the argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And in Him **you have been made complete**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek uses the same root. The fullness is in Him; you have been filled full in Him. Perfect tense — done, and standing done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why the warning works. Anyone offering an addition is offering to add to something already complete. Nothing on the market can be a next step from here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who is the head over every ruler and every authority.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The powers the false teaching was anxious about — angelic hierarchies, elemental spirits — are put under the same head the reader is joined to."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul does not do.** He never refutes the philosophy in detail. He states what Christ is and what the reader has in Him, and lets the offer collapse under the comparison. It is the same move as Ephesians 1: prayer that they would see what they already have, rather than instruction on getting more."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse before this one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The warning does not stand on its own. Paul has just written *just as you received Christ Jesus the Lord, keep on walking in Him* — rooted, built up, made firm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what makes the kidnapping possible to resist. A man who is rooted can be approached by a persuasive teacher and stay where he is. See the note on Colossians 2:4–7."}]}, "Colossians 3:1–4": {"title": "Where your life is", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The whole ethical half of Colossians hangs off the word *if*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If, then, **you were raised to life with Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek **ei** here is a first-class condition — *if, and it is the case*. Not a doubt. The sense is *since*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has already stated it: *buried with Him in baptism, in which you also were raised with Him through faith in the working of God*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep **seeking what is above**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **zeteite** — a present imperative: go on seeking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The location"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where Christ is, **seated at the right hand of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 110's line, quoted more often in the New Testament than any other Old Testament verse: *sit at My right hand, till I make Your enemies Your footstool*. See the note on Psalms 110:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Above* is not vague. Paul gives it an address and a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give your mind to what is above, **not to the things lying here on the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phroneite** — set your mind, be minded. The same verb as *let this mind be in you which was also in Christ Jesus*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seeking, and then thinking. Paul asks for both in two verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **you died**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Flat, past tense, three words. He does not argue for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The end of chapter 2 has said it already: *if you died with Christ from the basic principles of the world, why, as though living in the world, do you subject yourselves to regulations — do not touch, do not taste, do not handle?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything he is arguing against is religious rule-keeping, and the argument against it is that the person the rules were for is dead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the life is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the life you now have is **hidden away with Christ in God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kekruptai** — a perfect passive: has been hidden and stays hidden."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two layers: with Christ, and in God. Nothing gets at it without going through both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not visible"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The life is real and out of sight, and Paul says so without apology."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Colossians were being told by somebody that they needed visible extras — festivals, new moons, angel worship, ascetic practice. Paul's answer is that the thing they are being offered a supplement to is not even on display."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The appearing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when Christ, **who is our very life**, is **shown openly**, then **you also will be shown together with Him, in glory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phanerothe** — manifested, brought into the open. Twice, of the same event."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His appearing and theirs are one occurrence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John writes it identically: *what we are going to be has not come out into the open yet… when He does come out into the open, we will be like Him*. See the note on 1 John 3:2–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ, **who is our life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not who gives life. Paul has used this construction all through the letter — *Christ in you, the hope of glory*, and *in Him dwells all the fullness of the Godhead bodily*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore **put to death** your members which are on the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list of behaviours starts in verse 5, and it starts with *therefore*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul never puts the instructions first. Four verses of position, and then the conduct that follows from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parallel in Ephesians"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **raised us up together, and made us sit together in the heavenly places** in Christ Jesus.* See the note on Ephesians 2:4–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past tense there too, and the seating is already done."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of the letter.** Died, raised, hidden, and appearing — and only then a single word, *therefore*, before anything is asked of them."}]}, "Colossians 3:15–17": {"title": "Peace as the umpire", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*Let the peace of God have the deciding voice in your hearts.* The English has to spread out to say what the Greek says in one word, and that word is where this verse gets interesting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word is from the stadium"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Brabeuo.** A *brabeus* was the judge at the games — the official who watched the race and said who had won, whose call settled the matter. The verb means to act as that official: to arbitrate, to decide, to rule on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So Paul is not saying *feel peaceful*. He is saying: give peace the umpire's whistle. Let it be the thing that makes the call inside you when two courses of action are competing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a far more practical instruction than it usually gets treated as. An umpire is not an atmosphere. An umpire is a decision-maker."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being umpired"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The context is a chapter about how believers live together — putting off anger and malice, putting on compassion, kindness, humility, patience, bearing with one another and forgiving. Then this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it was into that peace you were called, in one body.* The peace is not first a private calm; it is the peace they were called into *together*. So a good deal of what this umpire is ruling on is how a person deals with other people, and the verse right after it is about the word of Christ living in them richly enough to teach and correct one another."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Reading it for guidance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This verse is used constantly as a way of finding God's will, and that use is not foreign to it. Paul does put peace in the position of the one who decides."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things keep it honest. First, it says *the peace of God*, not the absence of nerves — the same peace Jesus described as not the kind the world hands out. Someone can feel nervous and still be at peace, and can feel enthusiastic and have no peace at all. Second, it sits in a sentence about the body, and Paul immediately surrounds it with the word of Christ dwelling richly and with people teaching one another. Peace umpires; it does not umpire alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And be thankful people"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul attaches this to the end of the sentence, and it is easy to read as a pleasantry. In the paragraph it is structural: he says it here, again two lines later with the singing, and a third time at the close — *giving thanks through Him to God the Father*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times in three verses. Whatever else the umpired life looks like, Paul seems to think it is audibly grateful."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"whatever you do, in word or in work.\"** The paragraph ends by collapsing the distinction between religious activity and ordinary work — everything, in the name of the Lord Jesus. That is where a chapter about how to live together ends up: not with a special category of holy tasks, but with the whole of a life brought under one name."}]}, "Colossians 3:22–25": {"title": "The wages nobody expected", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is writing to household slaves, which is worth saying plainly rather than softening into \"servants.\" These were people with no legal standing and no prospect of wages at all — and the sentence he lands on would have been startling in that room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase that does the work"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Knowing that the Lord Himself will hand you the inheritance as your wages.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words are being deliberately jammed together. An **inheritance** is what a son receives because of who he is. **Wages** are what a worker earns. A slave could expect neither."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says they will be handed the first *as* the second. The thing they could never earn is given to them in the form of the thing they never got."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Work at it from the soul\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is literally *out of the soul*, and it is set against work that only happens while someone is watching — the phrase behind it means eye-service, work done for the eye."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction is not to work harder. It is to change who the work is aimed at: *as for the Lord and not for people*. That transfer is what makes a hidden task and a visible one worth the same."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line at the end.** *There is no playing of favorites* — and the very next verse turns to the masters and tells them they have a Master in heaven. In a world that ran on rank, Paul puts both parties under the same eye and tells the ones on the bottom that their unwitnessed work is seen."}]}, "Colossians 4:5–6": {"title": "\"With salt in them\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses near the end of the letter, about talking to people who do not believe any of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is in view"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Live wisely toward **those who are outside**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hoi exo** — those outside, the standard early-church term for people not in the congregation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul writes it from a Roman prison, to a church he had never visited."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The time"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **buy up every hour you are given**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exagorazomenoi ton kairon** — redeeming the time. **Kairos** is not clock time; it is the opportune moment, the window."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Buy up* is a market word: purchase it out, take it off the shelf before somebody else does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the same phrase in Ephesians and adds *because the days are evil*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The speech"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let your words **always be gracious**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *in grace* — **en chariti**. Not merely polite. The quality of the message is to be the quality of the delivery."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The salt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **salt in them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Greek and Roman writers used salt as a metaphor for wit — speech with some bite, not insipid."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it carries the Old Testament sense as well: salt was required on every offering, and it was called the salt of the covenant. See the note on Leviticus 2:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Salt preserves and it makes thirsty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **you know the answer that fits each person you speak with**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *how you ought to answer each one*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Each one.** Not a formula to be repeated at everybody. The answer is fitted to the person asking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the point of the salt: seasoning is measured against what it is going into."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not tell them to start conversations, to argue, or to prepare a case."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter frames it the same way: *always be ready to give a defence to everyone who **asks** you a reason for the hope that is in you, with meekness and fear.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both apostles describe answering rather than initiating."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul asks for in the same paragraph"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verses earlier: *pray for us, that God would **open to us a door for the word**, to speak the mystery of Christ, **for which I am also in chains** — that I may make it manifest, as I ought to speak.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks for a door and for the right words, about himself, immediately before telling them the same two things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits in the letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Colossians spends three chapters on Christ — *in Him dwells all the fullness of the Godhead bodily*, *hidden with Christ in God*, *He is all and in all*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the practical section, and this is nearly the last item in it: how to talk to the neighbours."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is being asked for.** Not a script. Grace in the words, salt in them, and the awareness that the person in front of you is one person rather than a category."}]}, "Daniel 1:17": {"title": "\"And to Daniel He gave more still\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it is the hinge of the whole book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the four are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel, Hananiah, Mishael and Azariah — renamed Belteshazzar, Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego by the chief of the eunuchs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judean nobility, deported as teenagers, selected for looks and aptitude and put into a three-year Babylonian training programme."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were being taught"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The language and literature of the Chaldeans* — astronomy, astrology, divination, mathematics, and the mythology of Babylon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The syllabus of a pagan court, taught by the wise men Daniel later has to compete with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before this verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The refusal of the king's food, the ten-day test on vegetables and water, and the result: *at the end of ten days their features appeared better and fatter in flesh than all the young men who ate the portion of the king's delicacies.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had asked rather than demanded — *Daniel requested of the chief of the eunuchs* — and God had given him *favour and goodwill* with the man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gift"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To these four young men **God gave knowledge and skill in all learning and wisdom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew names three: **madda** (knowledge), **haskel** (skill, insight) and **chokmah** (wisdom)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It covers the whole curriculum, including the parts of it that were pagan."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text does not say they excelled by working harder. It says God gave it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The addition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **to Daniel He gave more still: understanding in every kind of vision and dream**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chazon** — the vision word — and **chalomot**, dreams."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One of the four gets something the other three do not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole book runs on it. Nebuchadnezzar's statue in chapter 2, the tree in chapter 4, the writing on the wall in chapter 5, the ram and goat in chapter 8, the seventy weeks in chapter 9, and the visions of chapters 10 to 12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel is the only man in the Old Testament who both receives visions and interprets other people's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about it later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Standing in front of Nebuchadnezzar, asked how he did it:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This mystery was not opened to me because I have more wisdom than anyone living**.\"* See the note on Daniel 2:29–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He declines the credit at the moment it is offered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result of the training"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And in all matters of wisdom and understanding about which the king examined them, **he found them ten times better** than all the magicians and astrologers who were in all his realm.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were examined by the king and outperformed the professionals."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Joseph stands beside him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pharaoh's assessment of Joseph is nearly identical: *can we find anyone like this man, one in whom the Spirit of God lives?* See the note on Genesis 41:37–45."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two Hebrews in foreign courts, both raised by interpreting a king's dream, both given a new name and a wife by the crown."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How long Daniel lasted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Daniel continued **until the first year of King Cyrus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deported under Nebuchadnezzar, still in office when Babylon fell to Persia — roughly seventy years, through four kings and two empires."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the verse divides.** All four are given the whole curriculum. One is given something extra, and the extra is the reason there is a book."}]}, "Daniel 10:12–13": {"title": "Twenty-one days", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The messenger explains why he is late."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Daniel had been doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens: *in those days I, Daniel, was mourning three full weeks. I ate no pleasant food, no meat or wine came into my mouth, nor did I anoint myself at all, till three whole weeks were fulfilled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-one days of fasting, by an old man in Persia."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The greeting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not be afraid, Daniel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just been told *O Daniel, man greatly beloved* — the same description Gabriel gave him in chapter 9. See the note on Daniel 9:20–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**From the first day** you set your heart to understand, and to humble yourself before your God, **your words were heard**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Day one. The prayer registered immediately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he came"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have come because of your words**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The visit is caused by the praying. Not permitted by it — caused."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The delay"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **the prince of the kingdom of Persia stood against me for twenty-one days**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *withstood me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer was heard on day one and the answer arrived on day twenty-one. Daniel had spent the whole of it fasting, with no indication anything was happening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the chapter suggests his praying was deficient, and the messenger does not suggest it either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prince of Persia"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not Cyrus. The messenger goes on to say *I will return to fight with the prince of Persia; and when I have gone out, indeed the prince of Greece will come*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Kings do not follow kings that way, and empires do. The chapter treats these as authorities standing behind nations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The help"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then **Michael, one of the chief princes, came to help me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Michael appears three times in Daniel and twice in the New Testament — in Jude, disputing over the body of Moses, and in Revelation 12, at war."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel 12 calls him *the great prince who stands watch over the sons of your people*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Daniel could not see"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any of it. He fasted three weeks and got nothing, and then was told what had been going on the whole time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never asks about it and is never told to pray differently."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was in the middle of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 10 opens the last vision of the book — the long account of the kings of the north and south that runs to the end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The messenger says: *now I have come to make you understand what will happen to your people in the latter days.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The state Daniel was in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I retained no strength… my comeliness was turned in me into corruption, and I retained no strength.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is touched three times in the chapter and told twice not to be afraid. *A hand touched me, which made me tremble on my knees.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the categories"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We do not wrestle against flesh and blood, but against **principalities, against powers, against the rulers of the darkness of this age**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And immediately after listing the armour: *praying always with all prayer and supplication in the Spirit.* See the note on Ephesians 6:14–17."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the messenger says about the prayer.** That it was heard on the first day, and that he was dispatched because of it. The twenty-one days were not the wait for an answer — they were the journey."}]}, "Daniel 2:19": {"title": "\"Then Daniel blessed the God of heaven\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and the first thing he does with the answer is stop and sing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is at stake"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nebuchadnezzar has dreamed something and demanded that his wise men tell him both the dream and its meaning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They protest that no king has ever asked such a thing, *and there is no other who can tell it to the king except the gods, whose dwelling is not with flesh*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king orders every wise man in Babylon killed. Daniel and his three friends are on the list."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Daniel did first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Daniel went in and asked the king **to give him time**, that he might tell the king the interpretation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks for an extension on a death sentence, from a king who has just executed his own advisers for stalling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Daniel went to his house, and **made the decision known to Hananiah, Mishael, and Azariah, his companions**, that they might **seek mercies from the God of heaven concerning this secret**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not pray alone. He goes and gets three men."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their Babylonian names are the famous ones — Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego — and here they are used as their own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **in the night, in a vision, the mystery was opened to Daniel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Aramaic is **raz** — a secret, a mystery. It is a Persian loan-word, and it is the term the whole chapter turns on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Opened** — the Aramaic is *revealed*, uncovered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the night.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had prayed in the evening and he was asleep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Daniel **blessed the God of heaven**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before he goes to Arioch, before he goes to the king, before he tells his three friends, and before anyone is saved from execution."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The soonest possible action would have been to run."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the blessing says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verses 20 to 23, and it is one of the finest prayers in the Old Testament:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Blessed be the name of God forever and ever, for **wisdom and might are His**. And **He changes the times and the seasons; He removes kings and raises up kings**. He gives wisdom to the wise and knowledge to those who have understanding.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He reveals deep and secret things; He knows what is in the darkness, and light dwells with Him**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about himself in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I thank You and praise You, O God of my fathers; **You have given me wisdom and might, and have now made known to me what we asked of You**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**What we asked** — plural. He credits the three men who prayed with him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says to Arioch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not destroy the wise men of Babylon**; take me before the king.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first use he makes of it is to stop the execution of the astrologers who could not do it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says to the king"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This mystery was not opened to me because I have more wisdom than anyone living**.\"* See the note on Daniel 2:29–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the dream was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A statue of gold, silver, bronze, iron and clay, struck by a stone *cut out without hands* which became a mountain and filled the whole earth."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of the paragraph.** The mystery is revealed in the night, and the next recorded act is a blessing — before the king is told, before anyone is saved, and before the man is out of danger himself."}]}, "Daniel 2:29–30": {"title": "\"Not because I have more wisdom\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Standing in front of the most powerful man alive, with the answer in his hand, Daniel gives away the credit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before Nebuchadnezzar, after every wise man in Babylon has failed and the execution order has been issued."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just told the king: *there is a God in heaven who reveals secrets, and He has made known to King Nebuchadnezzar what will be in the latter days.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the king was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As you were **lying there**, O king, your thoughts turned to **what would come after this**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Aramaic is *your thoughts came into your mind upon your bed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel tells the king what he had been thinking about before he fell asleep. Nobody could have known it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An emperor lying awake wondering what happens after him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who answered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **the One who uncovers mysteries showed you what will be**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Aramaic is *the revealer of secrets*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel has already used the title twice in the chapter. It is his name for God through the whole book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disclaimer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As for me, **this mystery was not opened to me because I have more wisdom than anyone living**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is holding the one thing the king wants, and the sentence he chooses is a denial of personal capacity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been assessed as *ten times better than all the magicians and astrologers in all his realm*, and had every ground to say so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two reasons he gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It was opened **so that the king would know its meaning**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And so that **you would understand the thoughts of your own heart**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both purposes are about the king. Neither is about Daniel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second is the more surprising: the dream was given so a pagan emperor would understand what was in his own head."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had been given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To Daniel He gave more still: understanding in every kind of vision and dream.* See the note on Daniel 1:17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has the gift and refuses to be the reason."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the dream was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A statue: head of gold, chest and arms of silver, belly and thighs of bronze, legs of iron, feet of iron mixed with clay."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You watched while **a stone was cut out without hands**, which struck the image on its feet… and the stone that struck the image **became a great mountain and filled the whole earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Without hands** — the same construction Paul uses of the circumcision *no human hand performed*. See the note on Colossians 2:11–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interpretation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You are this head of gold**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then four kingdoms, and: *in the days of these kings the God of heaven will set up a kingdom which shall never be destroyed.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The king's reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then King Nebuchadnezzar fell on his face, prostrate before Daniel… and said, \"**Truly your God is the God of gods, the Lord of kings, and a revealer of secrets**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel's own title for God, in the king's mouth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Daniel does with the promotion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is made ruler over the province of Babylon and chief of the wise men — and immediately *Daniel petitioned the king, and he set Shadrach, Meshach, and Abednego over the affairs of the province of Babylon*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing he does with the office is get his three friends appointed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says at the moment of maximum leverage.** That it was not his wisdom, and that the whole thing was arranged so a king would understand his own thoughts."}]}, "Daniel 2:31–33": {"title": "The great statue", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A pagan king dreams the whole future of the world, and a Jewish captive tells him what he dreamed before interpreting it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Daniel had to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nebuchadnezzar demanded that his wise men tell him the dream itself, not just its meaning — on pain of death for all of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the description in these verses is Daniel telling the king what was in the king's own head."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The metals, top to bottom"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**head** — fine gold", "**chest and arms** — silver", "**belly and thighs** — bronze", "**legs** — iron", "**feet** — part iron, part clay"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel names the first outright: *you are that head of gold.* Babylon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then, in his own words, *another kingdom inferior to yours*, and another, and a fourth as strong as iron that breaks everything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction of travel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each metal down the statue is **less valuable** and **harder**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gold to silver to bronze to iron to iron mixed with baked clay. Worth decreasing, strength increasing, and then the last one is strong and brittle at the same time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Empires get tougher and cheaper as the statue goes down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is standing on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Its **feet** part iron and part clay.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything above rests on the weakest joint in it. Iron and fired clay do not bind — Daniel says so a few verses later: *they will not hold together, just as iron does not mix with clay.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole weight of world history, in this dream, is balanced on a mixture that cannot adhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the king saw it as"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Huge it was, **blazing with a bright and fearsome light**, and the look of it was **terrifying**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is how the kingdoms of men look from a throne — enormous, blazing, and frightening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel's God shows the same thing to Daniel as a beast-vision in chapter 7. The king saw a magnificent statue. The prophet saw animals."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in the dream yet.** A stone, cut without hands, which strikes the feet and grinds the whole statue to chaff, and then becomes a mountain filling the earth. That is the next part of what Nebuchadnezzar saw, and it is the part the dream was given for. See the note on Daniel 2:44–45."}]}, "Daniel 2:44–45": {"title": "The stone cut without hands", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The statue is enormous, blazing and terrifying, and what destroys it is a rock."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Not by human hands\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is the stone you saw, **cut from the mountain but not by human hands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one detail Daniel repeats. It is what distinguishes the stone from everything else in the dream — every empire in the statue was quarried, shaped and raised by men."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody made this one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it hits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stone strikes the **feet** — the iron and clay, the weakest joint, the part everything else is standing on. And the whole statue comes down at once."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Broke to pieces the iron, the bronze, the clay, the silver, and the gold.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the order Daniel lists them in: from the feet up to the head. It collapses from the bottom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to the pieces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before this describe it: the fragments become *like chaff on a summer threshing floor*, and the wind carries them off so that no trace of them is found."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not defeated and occupied. Gone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The kingdom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the days of those kings, **the God of heaven will set up a kingdom that will never be destroyed**. It will not be left to another people… and it will stand forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things said about it:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**never destroyed** — unlike every kingdom in the statue", "**not left to another people** — no succession, no handover; the whole statue is a chain of handovers", "**stand forever**"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the stone becomes a mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The stone that struck the statue became a great mountain and filled the whole earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It arrives small enough to be called a stone and ends up filling everything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who this was said to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Babylonian king, by a captive, about the end of Babylon and everything that would follow it — and Nebuchadnezzar's response is to fall on his face."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Truly your God is the God of gods and the Lord of kings.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The great God has made known to the king **what is yet to come**. The dream is true, and its meaning is sure.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel signs off on it. Two clauses, both about reliability."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the stone is called elsewhere.** *The stone the builders rejected has become the chief cornerstone* — Psalm 118, and quoted by Jesus of Himself the week He died, and by Peter in Acts 4. A stone the builders looked at and discarded; a stone no builder cut."}]}, "Daniel 6:23": {"title": "\"No wound was found on him\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it gives the reason at the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The king's night"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the king rejoiced greatly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Darius had spent the night fasting, refusing entertainment, unable to sleep, and had gone to the den *very early in the morning, with a lamenting voice.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who signed the decree is the one who could not rest. Daniel had a better night than the king did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lifting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gave the order to **lift Daniel out of the den**. So Daniel was lifted up.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice. He goes down by being thrown and comes up by being lifted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **no wound was found on him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Aramaic is that no injury was discovered. It is an inspection — they looked him over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that he survived, or was mauled but recovered. Nothing on him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **he had trusted in his God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole explanation the text offers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not because he was brave, or innocent, or valuable to the empire — all of which were true. Because he trusted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews puts him in the roll of faith by his act rather than his name: *who through faith… **stopped the mouths of lions**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had done to get there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The decree forbade petitions to anyone but the king for thirty days, on pain of the den."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Daniel knew that the writing was signed, he went home. And in his upper room, with his windows open toward Jerusalem, **he knelt down on his knees three times that day, and prayed and gave thanks before his God, as was his custom since early days**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not increase his praying or make a demonstration of it. He did not reduce it either. *As was his custom.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How old he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel was taken to Babylon as a youth in the first deportation. By the reign of Darius the Mede he is an old man — most reckonings put him in his eighties."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sixty years in that court, under a succession of empires, and *they could find no charge or fault, because he was faithful; nor was there any error or fault found in him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His enemies say it themselves: *we shall not find any charge against this Daniel unless we find it against him concerning the law of his God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The morning question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Darius, at dawn, at the mouth of the den: *\"Daniel, servant of the living God, has your God, whom you serve continually, been able to deliver you from the lions?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer from the dark: *\"O king, live forever! **My God sent His angel and shut the lions' mouths**.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the king wrote afterwards.** A decree to every people and nation and language: *that in every dominion of my kingdom men must tremble and fear before the God of Daniel. For He is the living God… He delivers and rescues.* A night in a pit turned into an empire-wide proclamation."}]}, "Daniel 7:1–3": {"title": "\"When he woke, he wrote it all down\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The book turns over here. For six chapters Daniel has interpreted other men's dreams; from this one on, he has his own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The date"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the **first year that Belshazzar ruled Babylon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which places it before chapter 5 — the writing on the wall, on the night Belshazzar died."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book is not in chronological order. Chapters 7 and 8 belong before 5 and 6, and Daniel has arranged the material by kind rather than by date."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapters 2 to 7 are in Aramaic — the language of the empire. Chapters 1 and 8 to 12 are in Hebrew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose dream it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Daniel had **a dream**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "For six chapters he has been the man who explains. Now he is the one who needs explaining to — and inside the vision he asks: *I came near to one of those who stood by, and asked him the truth of all this.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Visions moved through his mind **as he lay on his bed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An old man asleep in his own room. Nothing about the setting is remarkable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When he woke, **he wrote it all down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Aramaic is *he wrote the dream, telling the main facts*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Habakkuk had been told to do exactly this: *write the vision, carve it clear on tablets*. See the note on Habakkuk 2:2–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is where **the account begins**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book says so about itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sea"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the night I looked, and I saw **the four winds of heaven churning the great sea**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four winds — from every direction at once."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sea in Hebrew imagery is the place of chaos, the thing God set boundaries on at creation. Revelation ends with *there was no more sea*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **up out of that sea came four huge beasts, each one unlike the others**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A lion with eagle's wings, a bear with three ribs in its mouth, a leopard with four heads and four wings, and a fourth *dreadful and terrible, exceedingly strong* with iron teeth and ten horns."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same four empires as the statue in chapter 2 — and where Nebuchadnezzar saw gold, silver, bronze and iron, Daniel sees animals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A king dreams of a gleaming statue. A prophet dreams of what comes out of the sea."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thrones set in place, the Ancient of Days seated, *His garment white as snow*, and *a thousand thousands ministered to Him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *behold, **One like the Son of Man, coming with the clouds of heaven**… to Him was given dominion and glory and a kingdom, that all peoples, nations, and languages should serve Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Son of Man** is the title Jesus uses of Himself more than any other, and it comes from here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes this verse at His trial, which is what the high priest tears his clothes over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it did to Daniel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I, Daniel, was grieved in my spirit within my body, and **the visions of my head troubled me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at the end: *my thoughts greatly troubled me, and my countenance changed; **but I kept the matter in my heart**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not explain it or publish it. He writes it down and keeps it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The turn in the book.** The interpreter becomes the one who needs interpreting — and the first thing he does on waking is reach for something to write with."}]}, "Daniel 9:1–2": {"title": "He found it in the books", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An old man in Babylon is reading, and what he reads sends him to his knees for the rest of the chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The date"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the **first year of Darius** son of Ahasuerus, a Mede by descent, who was made ruler over the kingdom of the Babylonians.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Babylon has just fallen. The handwriting on the wall was last chapter; Belshazzar was killed that night."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel was a teenager when he was deported. He is in his eighties now."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I, Daniel, **understood from the Scriptures**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I understood by the books*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not having a vision. He is reading, and the visions in this book come to a man who was already in the text."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was reading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From **the word the LORD had given to Jeremiah the prophet**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah's letter to the exiles — the one with *I know the thoughts that I think toward you* in it — is addressed to Daniel's generation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah was alive within Daniel's lifetime. This is nearly contemporary writing being treated as Scripture, in Scripture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seventy years"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That the desolation of Jerusalem was to last **seventy years**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah 25 and 29 both give the figure. Daniel does the arithmetic and works out that the term is nearly up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *then I set my face toward the Lord God to make request by prayer and supplications, with fasting, sackcloth and ashes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just discovered that the thing is promised, dated and about to happen — and that is what makes him pray."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the reverse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the prayer is like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve verses, and almost all of it is confession — *we have sinned and committed iniquity, we have done wickedly and rebelled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says **we** throughout. Daniel is the one man in the book with nothing recorded against him, and he puts himself inside the sentence every time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is asked on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not because of our righteous deeds, but **because of Your great mercy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He states the ground of the request explicitly, and it is not the record of the people he is praying for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While I was still speaking and praying… **Gabriel** came to me in swift flight.* See the note on Daniel 9:20–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reply was dispatched before the prayer was finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened in history"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cyrus's decree, in Ezra 1, releases the exiles to go home and rebuild the house — *that the word of the LORD by the mouth of Jeremiah might be fulfilled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezra names the same prophecy Daniel was reading."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he did with a promise that was certain.** He prayed about it — for three weeks, in sackcloth. The prophecy's certainty is the reason given in the text for the praying, not an alternative to it."}]}, "Daniel 9:20–21": {"title": "\"While I was still speaking\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The answer arrives before the prayer is finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase, twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**While I was still speaking** and praying… **while I was still in prayer**, Gabriel… came to me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel says it at the start of the sentence and again at the end. He wants the timing on record."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had done the same thing three verses earlier — *while I was speaking, praying, and confessing.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was praying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Confessing **my sin and the sin of my people Israel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He includes himself. Nothing in the book is charged against Daniel — even his enemies had to invent a law about prayer to get at him — and he says *my sin*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was asking for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Laying **my plea** before the LORD my God **for His holy mountain**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not for himself, not for release, not for his position under the new Persian administration. For the temple hill, seven hundred miles away, in ruins."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who came"}, {"k": "p", "t": "***Gabriel**, the one I had seen in the vision before.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name means *man of God* or *God is my strength*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He appears four times in Scripture and every appearance is a message: twice to Daniel here, then to Zechariah in the temple about John, then to Mary in Nazareth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angel who explains Israel's timetable to Daniel is the one sent to announce its fulfilment five hundred years later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Swift flight\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *being caused to fly swiftly*, or *in weariness of flight* — the commentators divide over it. Either way it is a word about speed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*About the time of the **evening offering**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three in the afternoon. The hour of the lamb on the altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There has been no temple, no altar and no evening offering for sixty-eight years. There is nothing in Jerusalem to make it at."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel keeps the hours anyway, and the angel arrives on one of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is also the hour the veil tore."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is told first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before the message, the reason: *at the beginning of your supplications the command went out, and I have come to tell you, **for you are greatly beloved**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At the beginning* — the reply was issued when he started, and the flight is what took the twenty-one days in the following chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the message was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seventy weeks — seventy sevens decreed on his people, with the Messiah cut off in the middle of it, and a date attached."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked about seventy years and was given seventy sevens."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had been reading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah, and the seventy years of desolation. See the note on Daniel 9:1–2."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was said about him.** *Greatly beloved* — the Hebrew is *precious, treasured*. Gabriel says it to him three times across two chapters, to a civil servant in a pagan administration praying toward a demolished city."}]}, "Daniel 9:24": {"title": "Seventy sevens", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Daniel had been reading Jeremiah, counting the seventy years of the exile. The answer he gets back is about seventy of something else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The unit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seventy **sevens** are decreed for your people and your holy city.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shabuim* — sevens, weeks. Seventy sevens is four hundred and ninety."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel had been counting years; the answer comes back in the same currency, and Israel already reckoned in sevens — a sabbath year every seventh, a jubilee after seven sevens."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose clock it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Decreed for **your people** and **your holy city**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel and Jerusalem. The measure is not running on the world in general."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The six things it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "1. *to **finish the transgression*** 2. *to **put an end to sin*** 3. *to **make atonement** for wickedness* 4. *to **bring in everlasting righteousness*** 5. *to **seal up vision and prophecy*** 6. *to **anoint the Most Holy***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of them is something Israel does. All six are things done for and to them, and the third one names the mechanism: atonement — the Hebrew is **kaphar**, to cover, the word behind the Day of Atonement."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everlasting* righteousness. Not a righteousness that has to be maintained."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The division"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses that follow split the four hundred and ninety: seven sevens, then sixty-two sevens, then one seven left over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From the going forth of the command to restore and build Jerusalem until Messiah the Prince* — sixty-nine sevens. Four hundred and eighty-three years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then, in verse 26, something happens that is not in the arithmetic: *the Anointed One will be cut off and will have nothing.* See the note on Daniel 9:26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gap"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sixty-nine sevens run out at the cutting off. The seventieth is described in verse 27 and has not been described as beginning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the sixty-ninth and the seventieth there is a space, and the church age is in it. The clock in this prophecy is Israel's, and it stopped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Daniel was praying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's first half is one of the great prayers of Scripture, and it is confession — *we have sinned, we have done wickedly* — for a nation, by a man with nothing personally to confess."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gabriel arrives *at the time of the evening sacrifice*, in a city with no temple and no sacrifice for seventy years, and tells him he is *greatly beloved*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the answer is bigger than the question.** Daniel asked about seventy years. He was given seventy sevens, and the six things listed are not about getting a people home from Babylon. They are about finishing sin and bringing in a righteousness that does not end."}]}, "Daniel 9:26": {"title": "\"Cut off, and will have nothing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most extraordinary sentence in the prophets, and it is put in five words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it falls in the count"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After the sixty-two sevens…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven sevens and sixty-two sevens make sixty-nine — four hundred and eighty-three years from the command to restore and rebuild Jerusalem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Anointed One"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **Anointed One** will be cut off.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **Mashiach** — Messiah. Daniel names Him by title, and the previous verse has already used it: *until Messiah the Prince.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Cut off\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **karath** is the ordinary word for executing a covenant-breaker, and it is also the verb used for making a covenant — *to cut* a covenant, from the animals divided in Genesis 15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"And will have nothing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The clause everything turns on. The older rendering is *but not for Himself*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is cut off, and gets nothing out of it. There is no throne at the end of the sixty-nine sevens, no kingdom, no crown, no gain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah had said the same thing in his own words: *He was cut off from the land of the living; **for the transgressions of My people** He was stricken.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people of **the prince who is to come** will destroy the city and the sanctuary.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written in Babylon, in the sixth century BC, about a city that at that moment lay in ruins and had not yet been rebuilt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Titus and the Roman legions did it in AD 70 — the city and the temple, and not one stone left on another."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus said the same, weeping, on the same hill: *they will not leave one stone standing on another in you, because you did not recognise the time when God came to you.* See the note on Luke 19:41–44."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order in the verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Messiah is cut off **first**. The city and the sanctuary are destroyed **after**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel's sequence requires the Messiah to come and be killed before AD 70. The temple He had to come to was gone within forty years of the crucifixion, and the genealogies that would prove anyone's descent from David burned with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Its end will come like a flood\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is an overwhelming torrent. And then: *war will continue to the end, and desolations are decreed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence does not stop at AD 70. It runs on."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in this verse.** The seventieth seven. Verse 27 describes it — a covenant made and broken in the middle of the week — and verse 26 stands between the sixty-ninth and it, describing a cutting off, a destroyed city, and a long stretch of war and desolation. The gap is where the sentence puts it. See the note on Daniel 9:24."}]}, "Deuteronomy 1:26–33": {"title": "\"The LORD hates us\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses recalls the day the nation refused, and he quotes what they said in their tents."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **you would not go up**. You **rebelled against the command** of the LORD your God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Kadesh, in the second year. The twelve spies had come back; two said go, ten said no."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was said in private"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You **grumbled in your tents** and said.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *you murmured in your tents*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses reports the tent conversation, which is not in the Numbers account. Forty years on, in his last sermon, he tells them what they said to each other when they thought nobody was listening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD hates us**. That's why He brought us out of Egypt — to hand us over to the Amorites and destroy us.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **sinah** — hatred."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had watched the plagues, the sea, the pillar, the manna. Their conclusion about the motive was that He hated them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The report they believed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They told us the people are **bigger and taller** than we are, the **cities are huge with walls up to the sky**, and they even saw **giants — the sons of the Anakim**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every item is from the ten spies' report. Numbers has it: *we were like grasshoppers in our own sight, and so we were in their sight.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in it was untrue. The walls were high and the Anakim were there — Joshua and Caleb never disputed the facts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses said at the time"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Don't be terrified. Don't be afraid of them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The LORD your God, who **goes ahead of you**, **He Himself will fight for you**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The evidence he gave them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Just as you saw Him do **in Egypt right before your eyes**, and **in this wilderness**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things they had personally watched."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You saw how the LORD your God **carried you, the way a father carries his son**, along the whole road you traveled until you reached this place.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nasa** — to lift and bear."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A father carrying a son is not a picture of a God who hates. Moses puts it directly against the sentence they said in their tents."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Yet even after all that, **you did not trust the LORD your God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *you did not believe*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses names the failure, and it is not cowardice or bad intelligence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He had been doing the whole time"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The One who **went ahead of you on the journey, searching out a place for you to camp**, in **fire by night and in a cloud by day**, to show you the way to walk.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *searching out* is **tur** — the same verb used of the twelve spies searching out the land."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God had been scouting ahead the whole way. They sent twelve men to do the job that was already being done, and believed the report."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years of circling Mount Seir. See the note on Deuteronomy 2:1–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And of that period Moses says in the same book: *these forty years the LORD your God has been with you, and **you have lacked nothing**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is listening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The children of the people who said it. Everyone over twenty that day is dead except Caleb and Joshua."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they got wrong.** Not the height of the walls. Moses does not correct a single fact in their report — he answers the sentence about who was bringing them out and why."}]}, "Deuteronomy 11:10–12": {"title": "Not like Egypt", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses describes the land they are entering by telling them what it is not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Egypt's agriculture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where you planted your seed and watered it by foot, like a garden of vegetables.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt had no rainfall to speak of. Farming there meant the Nile, irrigation channels, and lifting water — either by foot-driven wheels or by opening and closing channels with your feet in the mud."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was reliable and it was relentless. The water was there, and getting it onto the field was entirely your own labour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The land ahead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A land of hills and valleys, drinking in the rain from heaven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hills cannot be irrigated from a river. Everything depends on rain, which nobody can dig for, schedule, or manufacture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "From a farmer's point of view this is a downgrade in security. Moses presents it as an upgrade — and the reason follows immediately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A land the LORD your God cares for. The eyes of the LORD your God are on it all the time, from the first of the year to the last.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *cares for* is a verb about seeking out and attending to. And *the eyes of the LORD are on it* — continuously, the whole year, without a season off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two descriptions belong together: a land whose water comes from above is a land where attention has to come from above too. What looks like less control is described as more care."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where this lands in the New Testament.** Egypt's watering by foot is effort applied to a channel you dug yourself; the hill country is a place you cannot irrigate and must receive. It is the same distinction Paul keeps drawing between working for a wage and receiving a promise — see the note on Romans 4:13–15."}]}, "Deuteronomy 11:13–15": {"title": "The early rain and the late", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two rains, named separately, because in that country they did two different things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the farmer's year looked like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land had a long dry summer with no rainfall at all. Everything depended on two seasons of rain at opposite ends of the growing year:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the early rain** — autumn, October to November. It softened ground baked hard all summer so a plough could break it and seed could go in.", "**the late rain** — spring, March to April. It came just before harvest and filled out the grain in the ear."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Without the first there was no planting. Without the second the crop came up thin and the harvest was poor."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why both are promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will send rain on your land in its season, the early rain and the late rain, so you may gather your grain, your new wine, and your oil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise covers the whole cycle, beginning to end. God undertakes to start the year and to finish it — and the three crops named are exactly the three the land was known for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"So you may gather\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rain is God's; the gathering is theirs. Nobody in this passage is promised a harvest that appears without a plough."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the shape of it: He supplies what no one can manufacture, and they do the work that rain makes possible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you truly listen… loving the LORD your God and serving Him with all your heart and all your soul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Covenant terms, stated plainly. And notice what the listening is described as — loving and serving, not merely complying."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the whole image matters.** These verses come three lines after Moses says the land is not like Egypt, where you watered by foot from a river. See the note on Deuteronomy 11:10–12. The two rains are the reason: a country you cannot irrigate is a country where the harvest is asked for rather than engineered."}]}, "Deuteronomy 11:18–21": {"title": "\"As many as the days the heavens are above the earth\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Where the words are to be kept, and the promise attached to keeping them there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Four places"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let these words settle into your **heart** and your **soul**. Tie them on your **hand**… bind them on your **forehead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Inward first, then outward. Hebrew thought does not separate them: what is on the hand governs what you do, what is between the eyes governs what you look at, and both are downstream of the heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Observant Jews still take the outer instruction literally — the small boxes worn on the arm and the head, and the case on the doorpost, come from this passage and its twin in chapter 6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Four times"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Talk of them when you sit at home and when you walk on the road, when you lie down and when you rise up.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two locations and two moments — indoors and out, last thing and first thing. Between them they cover the day, which is the point."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here describes a lesson. It describes conversation, in the ordinary places, at the ordinary hours."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being taught"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Teach them to your children.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction about parents' hearts arrives first and the children second. What gets passed on is what has already settled in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that your days and your children's days may be many in the land… as many as the days the heavens are above the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is a measurement that cannot be measured. As long as there is a sky over the ground — that is the span being described."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What it is attached to.** These verses come immediately after the promise of the early and late rain, and immediately before *every place where you set your foot will be yours.* The words in the heart, the rain in its season, and the ground underfoot are one passage."}]}, "Deuteronomy 11:26–28": {"title": "\"Today I am setting before you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses lays two options in front of a nation and tells them to choose. It is the clearest statement of the covenant's terms in the book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The terms"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look — today I am setting before you a blessing and a curse. The blessing, if you obey… the curse, if you will not obey.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no ambiguity in it. Blessing is attached to obedience; curse to disobedience. The whole arrangement runs on the word **if**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the Sinai covenant was: a conditional agreement, freely entered into, with both outcomes stated in advance and in detail. Deuteronomy 28 spells out both lists at length."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it assumes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offer is genuine and the choice is real. Moses is not describing a trap — he is putting the actual terms in front of people about to enter the land, and he tells them to write them on their doorframes and teach them to their children."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here is unfair. Everything depends on a performance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the New Testament takes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes this covenant structure and then says something startling about it: *Christ redeemed us from the curse of the law, having become a curse for us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two columns did not disappear. He took one of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why a Christian reads this passage from a different position than the crowd standing at the Jordan. The if-clause has been answered by somebody else's obedience — which is the argument of Romans 5:18–19."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How Moses puts it later.** In chapter 30 he sets out the same choice and adds two words that are not here: *therefore choose life.* He does not leave it balanced. Having laid out both, he tells them which one to take."}]}, "Deuteronomy 14:22–27": {"title": "What the tithe was spent on", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the tithe most people have never read, and it is a feast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who eats it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the presence of the LORD your God, at the place He chooses… **eat the tithe** of your grain, new wine, and oil, and the firstborn of your herds and flocks.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tithe is brought to the sanctuary and then eaten — by the man who brought it, with his household."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not handed over. Eaten, in God's presence, as a meal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So that you may learn to hold the LORD your God in awe always.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stated purpose of the exercise is not funding. It is that he would learn something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The travel clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But if the place is too far, and the journey is more than you can carry **because the LORD has blessed you so richly**…\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A problem of prosperity: a man does well enough that a tenth of it will not fit on the cart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then trade the tithe for silver, tie up the money, and take it with you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Convert it, carry the cash, buy again at the other end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he buys with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There, **spend it on whatever your heart desires** — cattle, sheep, wine, strong drink, **anything you long for** — and eat it there in the presence of the LORD your God, and **celebrate**, you and your household.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read that twice. It is a command to spend a tenth of the year's income on whatever he most wants, and enjoy it in God's presence with his family."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Strong drink is named specifically. *Anything you long for* is the phrase."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction is to throw a party."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the one condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **do not neglect the Levite in your towns**, for he has no portion or inheritance of his own.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one who has nothing is brought to the same table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Three different tithes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Reading Deuteronomy alongside Numbers 18, the Old Testament describes more than one:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the Levites' tithe** — Numbers 18, supporting the tribe with no land", "**this one** — the festival tithe, eaten by the giver at the sanctuary", "**the third-year tithe** — stored in the towns for the Levite, the foreigner, the fatherless and the widow. See the note on Deuteronomy 14:28–29."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only one of the three funds ministry. One is a feast, and one is for the poor."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nowhere in this passage is the tithe described as a payment, a debt, or the price of anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that you may learn to hold the LORD your God in awe.* That is the reason given, and the mechanism for learning it is a good dinner."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Israel was leaving behind.** They had spent four hundred years in a country where everything they produced belonged to somebody else. The first thing the law tells them to do with a tenth of the harvest is eat it in front of God and enjoy themselves."}]}, "Deuteronomy 14:28–29": {"title": "The third-year tithe", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Every third year the tenth does not go to the sanctuary at all. It stays in town."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is kept"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Gather a tenth of that year's produce and **store it up in your towns**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not carried to the place God chose. Left locally, where the people who need it are."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who eats it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four groups, and the passage names them in order:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the Levite**, *who has no land of his own*", "**the foreigner**", "**the fatherless**", "**the widow**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The four categories of person in Israel with no inheritance and no legal means of supporting themselves. A man without land in an agricultural economy had nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law names them together repeatedly — *the fatherless and the widow, and the stranger within your gates* — and builds provision for them into the calendar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Eat their fill\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*May come and **eat their fill**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to be satisfied, to eat to satiety. Not a ration and not a token."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason attached"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So that the LORD your God may bless you in all the work of your hands.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blessing is stated as the consequence, and it is on the work rather than on the tithe."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the three tithes cover"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Reading the law together, the tenth does three different jobs:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Numbers 18** — support for the tribe with no land", "**Deuteronomy 14:22–27** — a feast, eaten by the giver, at the sanctuary. See the note on that passage.", "**this one** — the poor, every third year"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "One of the three funds ministry. One is a celebration. One is welfare."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anybody arguing about the tithe from the Old Testament is arguing about a system that fed the widow and paid for a party."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same instinct all through the law"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gleaning: *when you reap the harvest of your land, you shall not wholly reap the corners of your field… you shall leave them for the poor and the stranger.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sabbath year: whatever grows of itself is for *the poor of your people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The jubilee: land returns, debts release."}, {"k": "p", "t": "None of it is charity in the modern sense. It is written into how the economy runs."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it turns up in the New Testament.** *Pure and undefiled religion before God and the Father is this: **to visit orphans and widows in their trouble**, and to keep oneself unspotted from the world.* James picks two of the four groups from this passage and calls attending to them the definition of religion."}]}, "Deuteronomy 15:7–11": {"title": "Open your hand wide", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A law that anticipates the calculation somebody will make, and names it before they can."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If **one of your brothers falls into poverty** in any of your towns.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **achicha** — your brother. Repeated four times in five verses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law will not let the poor man be a category. He is a relative."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two prohibitions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not **harden your heart** or **shut your hand** against him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lo te'ammetz… velo tiqpotz** — do not make firm, do not close up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Heart and hand, in that order. The hand follows the heart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Open your hand wide, and **lend him freely whatever he needs**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **patoach tiphtach** — opening you shall open. The infinitive doubled for emphasis."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dey machsoro** — sufficient for his lack."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a fixed amount. What he actually needs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The calculation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be careful not to let **this ugly thought** creep in: \"**The seventh year, the year of release, is near**\" — and then look coldly at your needy brother and give him nothing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **davar im-levavcha beliyya'al** — a word in your heart, of worthlessness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the seventh year was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At the end of every seven years you shall grant a release of debts… **every creditor who has lent anything to his neighbour shall release it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens with it. Debts are cancelled in the seventh year."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it produces the calculation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A loan made in year six is a gift, and both parties know it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law legislates the cancellation and then legislates against the arithmetic the cancellation invites. It sees its own loophole and closes it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it says about the thought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that it is unwise or ungenerous. **Beliyya'al** — the word that becomes a name for worthlessness itself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The refusal has not happened yet. The thought is what is named."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He may **cry out to the LORD against you**, and **you will carry the guilt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vehayah becha chet** — and it will be sin in you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same construction as the cry of Israel in Egypt, and the blood of Abel from the ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The poor man's complaint goes over the lender's head."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The manner"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give to him, and **give with a glad heart, not a grudging one**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **velo-yera levavcha** — and let not your heart be grieved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law regulates the mood of the giving, which most laws cannot do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **because of this the LORD your God will bless you in all your work and everything you set your hand to**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The realism"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There will **always be poor people in the land**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which sits oddly against verse 4 of the same chapter: *there shall be no poor among you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter states the ideal and then the actual, eleven verses apart, without reconciling them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion drawn from it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That is **exactly why I command you**: open your hand wide.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The permanence of poverty is not offered as a reason to give up. It is given as the reason for the command."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does with the line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **you have the poor with you always**, and whenever you wish you may do them good; but Me you do not have always.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said over a woman pouring ointment, to people objecting about the cost. He quotes this verse, and the half He quotes is the one that ends *therefore open your hand*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the law names.** Not the refusal — the arithmetic that precedes it. *The seventh year is near*, thought in year six, and the law calls that thought worthless before anyone acts on it."}]}, "Deuteronomy 17:14–20": {"title": "A king under God's law", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Legislation for a monarchy four hundred years before there is one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The anticipated request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And you say, \"**Let us set a king over us like all the nations around us**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kechol-haggoyim asher sevivotai**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel hears exactly that sentence when it comes: *now make us a king to judge us **like all the nations***, and *the thing displeased Samuel*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law records the request in advance, in the words they will use."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is permitted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **be sure to appoint the king the LORD your God chooses**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The monarchy is allowed and regulated rather than forbidden. The choosing belongs to God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He must be **one of your own people**; do not set a foreigner over you.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first prohibition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The king must not **build up great herds of horses** for himself, or **send the people back to Egypt to get more horses**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Horses meant chariots, and chariots meant a standing army. Egypt was the horse market of the region."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the LORD has told you, \"**You are never to go back that way again**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The trade would run people the other direction — out of the land they were brought into."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He must not **take many wives**, or **his heart will be led astray**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Royal marriages were treaties. Every foreign wife came with her gods and an alliance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law states the reason."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he must not **pile up silver and gold** for himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three prohibitions: military power, foreign alliances, accumulated wealth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are the three things every ancient king wanted, and the three things Solomon had in quantities the record dwells on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Solomon did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty thousand stalls of horses, imported from Egypt. Seven hundred wives and three hundred concubines. Silver *as common in Jerusalem as stones*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But King Solomon loved many foreign women… **and his wives turned away his heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three laws, three violations, in the order the law lists them. See the note on 1 Kings 10:23–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The positive command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when he takes the throne, he is to **write out for himself, on a scroll, a copy of this law**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mishneh hattorah hazzot** — a copy, a duplicate of this law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Septuagint rendered that phrase **deuteronomion** — second law — and the book got its English name from a translator's reading of this verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "By hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not commissioned from a scribe. The king writes it out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "From the right copy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From the one **the Levitical priests keep**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Checked against the authorised text. He cannot work from memory or from a version he likes better."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let it **stay beside him**, and let him **read it all the days of his life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vehayetah immo** — and it shall be with him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not consulted at coronations. Kept next to him, and read for life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two purposes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that he **learns to hold the LORD his God in awe** and to **follow carefully every word**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the one that names the danger:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **his heart is not lifted above his brothers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **levilti rum-levavo me'echav** — that his heart not be lifted above his brothers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the daily reading is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To stop a king forgetting that the men in front of him are his brothers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law's own stated reason for a monarch reading Scripture every day is not wisdom or piety. It is to prevent the elevation that the job produces automatically."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **he and his descendants will reign long** over his kingdom in Israel.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who did it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he brought out the king's son, put the crown on him, and **gave him the Testimony**.* — at Joash's coronation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Josiah, when the book was found in the temple: *he tore his clothes*, and then read it aloud to everyone."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The stated reason.** Not wisdom, and not piety. *So that his heart is not lifted above his brothers* — a daily reading prescribed against the specific hazard of the office."}]}, "Deuteronomy 2:1–7": {"title": "\"You have gone around this mountain long enough\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses recounts the years nobody talks about, and the summary at the end of it is generous."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The circling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **many days we wandered around the hill country of Seir**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *we compassed mount Seir many days*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the bulk of the forty years, and Deuteronomy gives it one sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers 33 lists the camps — about forty of them — and says almost nothing about any."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You have gone around this mountain long enough. Now turn north**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *rav lakem* — enough for you, sufficient."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The circling was not indefinite. It ended with a sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they are about to pass"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You are about to pass through the territory of **your relatives, the descendants of Esau**, who live in Seir.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob's brother's family, four centuries on. See the note on Genesis 27:38–40, where Isaac tells Esau his dwelling will be away from the dew of heaven — and Seir is exactly that country."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prohibition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not provoke them**, because **I will not give you any of their land — not even enough to set your foot on**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *not so much as a foot breadth*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel is marching to take a country, and is told there is a border they may not cross."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have given Mount Seir to Esau as his possession**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Esau, who sold the birthright and was blessed second, has a land grant from God, and Israel is not permitted to touch it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy says the same about Moab and Ammon, Lot's descendants, in the same chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they must do instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You may **buy food from them with money**… and **buy water from them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They pay. An army of that size could have taken what it wanted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The summary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **the LORD your God has blessed you in everything your hands have done**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said about the wandering years, and about a people who had been walking in circles as a consequence of refusing to go in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He has **watched over your journey through this great wilderness**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**These forty years the LORD your God has been with you, and you have lacked nothing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *you have not lacked a thing*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 8 adds the detail: *your garments did not wear out on you, nor did your foot swell, these forty years.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the people said about the same period"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Why did you drag us up out of Egypt into this terrible place?\"* See the note on Numbers 20:2–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same forty years, described by the people inside them and by Moses looking back, and the two accounts do not resemble each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Moses is standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On the plains of Moab, in the eleventh month of the fortieth year, weeks from his own death, addressing a generation who were children when it started."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verdict on the wasted years.** Blessed in everything, watched over, and lacking nothing. Moses says it about the period the nation spent going in circles."}]}, "Deuteronomy 21:15–17": {"title": "The firstborn's right", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A law about inheritance, written for a situation the book of Genesis is full of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If a man has **two wives, one loved and one unloved**, and both bear him sons, and **the firstborn is the son of the unloved wife**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *one beloved and one hated* — **senuah**, which in legal usage means the less-favoured rather than the despised."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose story this is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob's. Two wives, one loved and one not — *Jacob loved Rachel*, and *when the LORD saw that Leah was unloved, He opened her womb.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leah's firstborn was Reuben. Rachel's was Joseph."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law is written about the exact case in Genesis 29, and Moses is writing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prohibition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **may not treat the son of the loved wife as the firstborn** in place of the true firstborn.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Preference is not allowed to redraw the inheritance. The law removes the father's discretion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The requirement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He must **recognize the firstborn**… and **give him a double share of everything he owns**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **pi shenayim** — literally *a mouth of two*, two portions."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The idiom uses **peh**, mouth, for a share. The same word is the edge of a blade, and the teeth of Isaiah's threshing sledge. See the note on Isaiah 41:14–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That son is **the first sign of his father's strength**; the right of the firstborn belongs to him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the beginning of his strength* — **reshith ono**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a statement about what the child represents rather than about his character."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened in Jacob's family"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did exactly what the law forbids. Reuben lost the birthright — 1 Chronicles says so outright: *Reuben was the firstborn, but because he defiled his father's bed, **his birthright was given to the sons of Joseph***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Joseph received a double portion in the most literal sense: his two sons, Ephraim and Manasseh, were each counted as a tribe."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve tribes, thirteen names, because one man got two shares."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Elisha uses the phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Please, **let me inherit a double portion of your spirit**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same idiom. He is not asking to be twice the prophet Elijah was; he is asking to be treated as the heir. See the note on 2 Kings 2:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elijah's answer — *you have asked a hard thing* — makes sense in that light. It was not his to allot."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elisha shouted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My father! My father!**\"* — at the moment the chariot came. See the note on 2 Kings 2:11–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for the relation the double portion depends on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the New Testament calls Christ"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Prototokos** — firstborn. *The firstborn among many brethren*, *the firstborn from the dead*, *the firstborn over all creation*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And of the readers: *the church of the firstborn who are registered in heaven.* See the note on Hebrews 12:18–24."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the law protects.** Not the favourite. It takes the father's preference out of the calculation entirely and fixes the double share to birth order."}]}, "Deuteronomy 23:3–6": {"title": "No Moabite may enter", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A permanent exclusion, with the reasons given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The law"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No **Ammonite or Moabite** may enter the assembly of the LORD, nor any of their descendants, **even to the tenth generation, forever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **qehal YHWH** — the assembly of the LORD. The gathered congregation for worship and civic decision."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ad-olam** — forever. Attached to a clause that has already said ten generations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both nations descended from Lot, Abraham's nephew — from the two daughters and the cave after Sodom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Relatives, and the law names them and not the Canaanites in this paragraph."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because they **did not meet you with food and water on your way out of Egypt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an attack. A failure to offer hospitality to relatives passing through, in a desert."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And because **they hired Balaam son of Beor, from Pethor in Mesopotamia, to curse you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Balak king of Moab sent for a professional to curse Israel, and paid."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the LORD your God would not listen to Balaam**; He **turned the curse into a blessing** for you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyahaphok** — He overturned it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four times Balaam opened his mouth to curse and four oracles of blessing came out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How shall I curse whom God has not cursed?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason for the reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **the LORD your God loves you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ki ahevcha YHWH Eloheicha**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The clause is not in the treaty language of the surrounding verses. It is the reason for the reversal, and it is affection."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prohibition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **do not seek their peace or their prosperity as long as you live**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shelomam vetovatam** — their peace and their good."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is startling about the book of Ruth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heroine is *Ruth the Moabitess*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator calls her that five times. The book will not let the reader forget which nation she came from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Your people will be my people, and your God will be my God**.\"* See the note on Ruth 1:16–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gleans in Boaz's field, and Boaz says *it has been fully reported to me, all that you have done for your mother-in-law*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD repay your work, and a full reward be given you by the LORD God of Israel, **under whose wings you have come for refuge**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb, **chasah**, as Psalm 118: to take shelter. See the note on Psalms 118:8–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where she ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Boaz took Ruth and she became his wife… and she bore a son.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they called his name **Obed**. He is the father of Jesse, **the father of David**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last four verses of Ruth are a genealogy running to David."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the genealogy goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Salmon begot Boaz by Rahab, **Boaz begot Obed by Ruth**, Obed begot Jesse, and Jesse begot David the king.* See the note on Matthew 1:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew names her in the first chapter of the New Testament, in a list that names four women, two of whom are foreigners."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law says and what the story does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law stands as written. Nothing in Ruth repeals it or argues with it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book simply tells what happened, and the Moabitess is David's great-grandmother."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the wall"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For He Himself is our peace, who has made both one, and **has broken down the middle wall of separation**.* See the note on Ephesians 2:14–16."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clause that does not belong.** *Because the LORD your God loves you.* It sits in the middle of a legal exclusion, explaining why a hired curse came out the other way."}]}, "Deuteronomy 25:1–3": {"title": "Forty, but no more", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A rule about corporal punishment, and the reason given for the limit is about dignity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The court"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When two men have a dispute, they are to **bring it to court**, and the judges will decide, **clearing the innocent and condemning the guilty**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *justifying the righteous and condemning the wicked* — **tsadaq** and **rasha**, the two verbs Proverbs pairs. See the note on Proverbs 17:15–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the definition of a working court, stated in one line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If the guilty one deserves a beating, the judge shall **make him lie down and be flogged in his presence**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**In his presence** — the judge who passed the sentence has to watch it carried out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *before his face*. There is no delegating it to somewhere out of sight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The number of lashes **fitting his offense**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *according to his fault, by number*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proportion is required, and the total is to be counted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ceiling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He may give **forty, but no more**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To go beyond that would **degrade your brother before your eyes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *lest your brother be dishonoured before you* — **niqlah**, made light of, held in contempt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words matter in that sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Your brother.** He has just been convicted and sentenced, and the law still calls him that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Before your eyes.** The concern is what watching it would do — to him, and to the people watching."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Jews did with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rabbinic practice reduced it to thirty-nine, so that a miscount could not push the total over the limit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul records receiving it five times: *from the Jews five times I received **forty stripes minus one**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two hundred lashes, by his own count."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus received"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roman scourging had no limit. The **flagellum** was leather thongs weighted with bone or metal, and the number was left to the soldiers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Gospels record the fact in a single word and describe nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah says about the appearance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His visage was **marred more than any man**, and His form more than the sons of men.* See the note on Isaiah 52:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *disfigured*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Hebrew for a blow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Makkah** — a stroke, a wound, a plague. It is the word in this passage for the beating, and the same word runs through the curses of Deuteronomy 28 for the diseases. See the note on Deuteronomy 28:58–68."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for a lash and the word for a plague are the same word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse that follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You shall **not muzzle an ox while it treads out the grain**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes it twice about paying those who preach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law puts a limit on beating a convicted man and a prohibition on starving a working animal in consecutive verses."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the ceiling protects.** Not the guilty man's comfort — the beating happens. The reason Moses gives is that past a point it stops being a sentence and starts being degradation, in front of witnesses."}]}, "Deuteronomy 25:17–19": {"title": "Remember Amalek", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses about one nation, and the instruction opens and closes with the same command about memory."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How they fought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How they **met you on the road** and **cut down all the stragglers at the rear**, when you were **weary and worn out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a battle line. Amalek came at the back, where the exhausted were — the old, the sick, the ones carrying children, whoever could not keep the pace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the method of a predator, not an army. You attack what is already struggling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When they came"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel had just come out of Egypt. They had no land, no army, no experience, and they were *weary and worn out* from the road."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amalek picked the moment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"They had no fear of God\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing said about their character, and it is the explanation for the rest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Amalek is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is Esau's grandson — the flesh's line, born of the man who sold a birthright for a bowl of stew and wanted the blessing without the covenant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amalek is self-effort: the constant, wearying pressure to get it done yourself, to strive, to keep up, to secure your own position because if you do not do it nobody will. It comes at you hardest when you are already tired, and it takes the stragglers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why it has to be blotted out and not managed. Something that hunts the weary cannot be kept on a leash."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was commanded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You shall **blot out the memory of Amalek** from under heaven. **Do not forget**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two halves are held together on purpose: erase the memory of them, and do not forget to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it was to be done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the LORD your God gives you **rest** from all your enemies.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not while fighting. From rest. The command was given for the moment of settled security — which is exactly when a man decides the old enemy is no longer worth dealing with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul was sent to do it and spared Agag, the Amalekite king, and the best of the livestock — *but everything despised and worthless, that they utterly destroyed.* He kept what looked useful."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It cost him the kingdom. An Amalekite is the man standing over him claiming to have finished him on Gilboa. And four hundred years later *Haman the Agagite* has a signet ring and a decree."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is spared because it seems useful comes back with authority. See the notes on 2 Samuel 1:5–10 and Esther 3:1–4."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The first battle and the raised hands.** Exodus 17 records the fight, and Israel prevailed only while Moses' hands were held up — by Aaron and Hur, because his own arms gave out. Then the altar built and named *The LORD is my banner*, and the words *the LORD will have war with Amalek from generation to generation.* The one enemy that is never beaten by effort was beaten under raised hands somebody else was holding."}]}, "Deuteronomy 25:4": {"title": "Do not muzzle the ox", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Nine words about farm animals, quoted twice in the New Testament about people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The law"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not muzzle an ox while it treads out the grain.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lo-tachsom shor bedisho**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The work"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Threshing was done on a flat stone floor. The cut grain was spread out and oxen were driven over it, sometimes dragging a weighted sledge, until the kernels separated from the husks."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Slow work, in the heat, walking in circles."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why a muzzle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An unmuzzled ox eats as it goes. The animal is standing on food."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prohibition costs the farmer grain, which is exactly why it needs to be a law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between a law limiting judicial flogging to forty stripes — *lest your brother be humiliated in your sight* — and the law of levirate marriage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Its neighbours are about human dignity, and the section as a whole is a run of unrelated regulations on treating people and animals decently."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law is about on its face"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not cruelty, exactly. The ox is not harmed by a muzzle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is about withholding from something that is working. The animal produces the food and is denied a mouthful of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it is written in the law of Moses, \"You shall not muzzle an ox while it treads out the grain.\" **Is it oxen God is concerned about?***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul, arguing that those who preach should be supported. See the note on 1 Corinthians 9:7–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Or does He say it altogether for our sakes? For our sakes, no doubt, this is written.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not denying that God cares about animals — Deuteronomy has laws about a bird's nest and a fallen donkey, and Jesus says not a sparrow falls unnoticed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is asking who the law was written down for. A law about oxen is preserved in a book given to people, and Paul reads it as principle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the Scripture says, \"You shall not muzzle an ox while it treads out the grain,\" and, \"**The labourer is worthy of his wages**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul to Timothy, on elders who labour in word and doctrine. See the note on 1 Timothy 5:17–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second half of that verse is a saying of Jesus, quoted alongside Deuteronomy as *Scripture*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does with the right"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet **we have not used this right**; instead we bear all things, so that we may put no hindrance in the way of the good news.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 9:11–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He argues the case at length and then declines to take it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase he lands on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Lord has ordained that **those who preach the good news should live from the good news**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the ox does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Walks in circles over food, and is permitted to eat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's whole argument is that the person doing the work should not be standing on a harvest they are not allowed to touch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where else the ox turns up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where no oxen are, the trough is clean; **but much increase comes by the strength of an ox***. See the note on Proverbs 14:1–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other law about the pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You shall not plough with an ox and a donkey together*, three verses after this one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two animals of different strength and stride, yoked to the same plough. Paul takes that image too: *do not be unequally yoked together with unbelievers*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The question Paul asks.** *Is it oxen God is concerned about?* — not a denial that He is, but a question about who the law was written down for."}]}, "Deuteronomy 28:1–6": {"title": "\"Every one of these blessings will catch up with you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The blessings of the covenant, and the whole passage hangs on the first word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If** you will truly listen to the LORD your God and **carefully live out all His commands**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew doubles the verb — *if listening you will listen*. And *all* is in the sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the old covenant stated in its own terms. Everything after it depends on the clause in front of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Every one of these blessings will come and **catch up with you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nasag** — to overtake, to reach, to pursue and come up with. It is the word for an army catching a fleeing enemy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blessings are described as chasing the person down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb turns up in the curses later in the chapter, about the other list."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The six"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed **in the city**, and blessed **in the field**. Blessed **the children you bear**, the crops of your ground, and the young of your herds and flocks. Blessed **your basket and your kneading bowl**. Blessed **when you come in**, and blessed **when you go out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Town and country. Family, farm and flock. The basket you carry it in and the bowl you mix it in. Arriving and leaving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a deliberately total list — the two places you can be, the three things you can produce, the two containers you own, and the two directions you can travel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is left outside it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The kneading bowl"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The smallest item on the list, and it is in a national covenant document."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever this chapter is, it is not written at a distance from ordinary life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verses 1 to 14 are the blessings. Verses 15 to 68 are the curses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fourteen verses of one and fifty-four of the other, and the second list begins *if you do not obey.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul takes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone who rests on the works of the law is under a curse, for it is written: **cursed is everyone who does not continue in all the things written in the book of the law, to do them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is quoting the sister passage in Deuteronomy 27, and the killing word is *all* — the same *all* as in verse 1 here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And his answer: *Christ has bought us out from under the curse of the law **by becoming a curse for us**.* See the note on Galatians 3:10–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last of the blessings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The LORD will open to you **His good treasure**, the heavens, to give the rain to your land in its season… **you shall lend to many nations, but you shall not borrow**.\"* See the note on Deuteronomy 28:7–14."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter is doing structurally.** Six tribes stood on Mount Gerizim to pronounce the blessings and six on Mount Ebal for the curses, with the ark in the valley between. The blessings in this chapter were shouted across a valley by half a nation to the other half."}]}, "Deuteronomy 28:20–24": {"title": "Bronze above and iron below", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Part of the long half of the chapter, and the images in it are agricultural and exact."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 28 gives fourteen verses of blessing and fifty-four of curse. See the note on Deuteronomy 28:1–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses reads out both sides of the agreement to a nation standing at the border, before anyone has crossed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The LORD will send **confusion and frustration on everything you put your hand to**, until you are wiped out and ruined.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *cursing, confusion and rebuke* — three words for the same thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"All because **you turned your back on Him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *because of the wickedness of your doings, whereby you have forsaken Me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated once and not repeated. The chapter names one cause."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plagues"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He will make **plague after plague cling to you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *cling* is **dabaq** — to stick fast, to adhere. The word used of a man cleaving to his wife."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He will strike you with **wasting disease, with fever and burning heat, with drought and blight and mildew** that will **chase you down to your grave**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven conditions, and three of them are crop diseases rather than human ones."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Blight** and **mildew** are what happens to standing grain — the first from a hot east wind, the second from too much damp."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sky and the ground"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The sky above you will turn to bronze** and **the ground beneath you to iron**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two metals, and the direction of each is the point."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bronze does not let water through. Iron does not let a plough in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A sealed sky and an unworkable floor — nothing coming down and nothing going in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The LORD will **turn your rain to dust and powder**, drifting down from the sky until you are destroyed.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not no rain. Rain replaced by what falls in a dust storm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **khamsin** off the desert carries fine dust that settles on everything, and the image would have been familiar to every farmer listening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing version of the same clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fourteen verses earlier: *\"The LORD will **open to you His good treasure, the heavens, to give the rain to your land in its season**, and to bless all the work of your hand.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew calls the sky a treasury."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bronze in one column and an opened treasury in the other, and the same sky in both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elijah did with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Shut the sky for three and a half years, and then prayed it open. See the note on 1 Kings 17:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James notes that he was *a man with a nature like ours*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the curses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ has **redeemed us from the curse of the law**, having become a curse for us — for it is written, \"Cursed is everyone who hangs on a tree.\"* See the note on Galatians 3:10–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *redeemed* is **exagorazo** — bought out of the market."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The end of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Back to Egypt in ships, offering themselves as slaves, and nobody buying. See the note on Deuteronomy 28:58–68."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two metals.** Bronze overhead and iron underfoot. A farmer hears the whole sentence at once: nothing arriving from above, and nothing possible below."}]}, "Deuteronomy 28:58–68": {"title": "Struck down and scattered", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of the longest list of curses in Scripture, and it is read out before anybody has crossed the river."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proportion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 28 gives fourteen verses of blessing and fifty-four of curse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses is not describing what will happen. He is setting out the terms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you will **not carefully keep every word of this law** written in this book, and will not **stand in awe of this glorious and awesome name**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Every word.** The chapter's terms are total, which is the clause Paul quotes in Galatians: *cursed is everyone who does not continue in **all things** which are written in the book of the law, to do them.* See the note on Galatians 3:10–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plagues"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He will send **terrible and lasting plagues** on you and your children, **severe and unrelenting sickness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *plagues* is **makkah** — a blow, a stroke, a wound. The same word used for the lashes in the flogging law. See the note on Deuteronomy 25:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diseases of Egypt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will bring back on you **all the diseases of Egypt you dreaded**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had watched them for four hundred years. The blessing chapter had promised the opposite: *the LORD will keep every sickness far from you.* See the note on Deuteronomy 7:12–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The open clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **every sickness and plague not even written in this book**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list does not close. Whatever is not named is included."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You who were once **as many as the stars** will be left few in number.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise to Abraham, run backwards."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **just as the LORD once delighted to do you good and multiply you, so He will let you be uprooted**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scattering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will scatter you **among all the peoples, from one end of the earth to the other**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Among those nations you will find **no rest, no place for the sole of your foot**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew echoes Noah's dove, which found no rest for the sole of her foot and came back to the ark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inner state"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There the LORD will give you **a trembling heart, failing eyes, and a soul worn thin with despair**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your life will **hang in doubt** before you; night and day you will be afraid.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the morning you will say, '**If only it were evening!**' and in the evening, '**If only it were morning!**'*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most exact description of dread in the Old Testament, and it is in a legal document."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD will send you **back to Egypt in ships**, by **the very road He promised you would never travel again**; and there you will **offer yourselves for sale to your enemies as slaves — and no one will even buy you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter ends with the exodus reversed: back to Egypt, by sea, offering themselves as slaves, and unsaleable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing lower is available."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does with the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ has **redeemed us from the curse of the law**, having become a curse for us — for it is written, \"Cursed is everyone who hangs on a tree.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *redeemed* is **exagorazo** — to buy out of the market."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That the blessing of Abraham might come upon the Gentiles in Christ Jesus.* See the note on Galatians 3:10–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last curse and the cross"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 21, four chapters earlier: *he who is hanged is accursed of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes that verse and applies it to the crucifixion. The document that lists the curses also contains the sentence he uses to say where they went."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter is.** Not a prediction and not a threat. Moses reads out both sides of an agreement to a nation standing at the border, and gives fifty-four verses to one side of it."}]}, "Deuteronomy 28:7–14": {"title": "\"The head and never the tail\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The blessing list of the covenant, and it is as detailed and generous as anything in the Old Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is promised"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["enemies who come at you one way and scatter seven", "blessing on the barns and on everything you set your hand to", "established as His own holy people, so that the nations see it", "overflow of children, livestock, harvest", "*the heavens, His rich storehouse* opened for rain in season", "lending to many nations and borrowing from none", "**the head and never the tail; always on top and never underneath**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The images are agricultural and national — a farming people who wanted rain, children, animals and safety, being told they would have all four."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you listen to His commands and hold to them, never turning aside to run after other gods.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is stated at the end of the passage and it governs everything before it. This is the covenant made at Sinai: a real offer, with real terms, and the curses in the same chapter are the other half of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the chapter whole and the proportions are sobering — fourteen verses of blessing, and over fifty of curse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How a Christian stands to this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul deals with this covenant directly. *Christ redeemed us from the curse of the law, having become a curse for us* — and he says why: *so that the blessing of Abraham might come to the Gentiles.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say the blessings were withdrawn. He says the curse was taken by somebody else, and the blessing let out to people who were never under Sinai at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the difference between reading this list as terms to meet and reading it as a description of what a covenant-keeping God is like toward His people."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"The head and not the tail.\"** The Hebrew image is about position in a line — leading rather than trailing. In context it belongs with lending rather than borrowing: a nation that others come to, rather than one that depends on them."}]}, "Deuteronomy 31:19–22": {"title": "A song to stand as witness", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God's last instruction to Moses is to write a song, and He explains why in advance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now **write down this song** for yourselves, and **teach it to the Israelites**; **have them sing it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs: write, teach, sing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The song is Deuteronomy 32, and it runs forty-three verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So that it may be **a witness for Me against them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **le-ed** — for a testimony."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A legal term. The song is being entered as evidence before the offence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The forecast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **when I have brought them into the land** I promised their fathers — a land flowing with milk and honey — and they have **eaten their fill and grown sleek and comfortable, then they will turn to other gods**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *waxed fat*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The danger named is not famine or invasion. It is having enough."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses has already said it: *when you have eaten and are full, then you shall bless the LORD… beware that you do not forget the LORD*. See the note on Deuteronomy 6:10–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they will do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They will **spurn Me and break My covenant**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated as fact, in the future tense, before they have crossed the river."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When the song will work"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And when **disaster and trouble find them**, this song will **testify against them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not while things are going well. The song does its job later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason it survives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For their **descendants will never forget it, always on their lips**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *it shall not be forgotten out of the mouths of their descendants*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A song outlasts a sermon. It gets sung by people who are not paying attention to what it says, and then one day they hear it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the reason a song is chosen rather than a speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The admission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I know already what they are inclined to do, even now, before I have brought them into the land** I promised.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I know their imagination which they are working, even now*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present tense. He is describing what is going on in the camp while the instruction is being given."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He brings them in anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The obedience"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **same day Moses wrote down the song and taught it** to the Israelites.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same day. No interval."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the song says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It opens *give ear, O heavens, and I will speak; and hear, O earth, the words of my mouth* — which Isaiah borrows for his first chapter. See the note on Isaiah 1:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It includes *He found him in a desert land… He encircled him, He instructed him, He kept him as the apple of His eye* — and *as an eagle stirs up its nest, hovers over its young*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *the Rock! His work is perfect, for all His ways are justice*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes it in Romans twice, and Hebrews quotes it once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Finishes writing the law, hands it to the Levites to be put beside the ark, delivers the song, blesses the tribes one by one, and climbs Nebo. See the note on Deuteronomy 33:13–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not get to see whether the song was needed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the song is called later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses… spoke in the ears of all the assembly of Israel the words of this song until they were ended.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in Revelation: *they sing **the song of Moses**, the servant of God, and the song of the Lamb*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why a song.** Because it stays in a mouth that is not listening. God says so plainly: *it shall not be forgotten out of the mouths of their descendants.*"}]}, "Deuteronomy 32:1–4": {"title": "\"Let my teaching fall like rain\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The opening of Moses' last song, and he asks for his words to behave like weather."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Four kinds of falling water"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let my teaching fall like **rain**, let my words settle like the **dew**, like gentle **showers** on new grass, like **soft rain** on the tender green.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four terms, and every one is gentle. Nothing here is a downpour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice he specifies what it lands on: *new grass* and *the tender green* — the most fragile stage of a plant's life, when heavy rain does damage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Teaching, in this image, is measured to what can take it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The witnesses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Listen, you heavens… hear, O earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses calls sky and ground as witnesses, which is the formal language of a covenant lawsuit. The song that follows is not gentle at all — it is a long account of a people who will forget, and the consequences."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the soft rain of the opening is the manner, not the content."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he proclaims"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will proclaim the name of the LORD. Oh, tell of the greatness of our God!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before a word of the history, he states the subject. The song is about Israel's failure and it is announced as being about God's name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Rock"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is the Rock — His works are perfect, and all His ways are just. A faithful God who does no wrong, upright and true is He.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first time God is called the Rock, and Moses uses it repeatedly through the song. Five statements in one verse, and each is about consistency: perfect, just, faithful, without wrong, upright."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is establishing the standard the rest of the song will measure the people against."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**When it was sung.** Moses is about to climb Nebo and die. This song was given to be taught to the children so that when everything he predicted came true, the words would already be in their mouths — a lesson planted before it was needed."}]}, "Deuteronomy 32:19–25": {"title": "\"I will hide My face\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The Song of Moses, taught to Israel before they entered the land, and it tells them in advance what will happen and what it will look like."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it was written"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses is about to die. He has not yet let them cross the Jordan, and God tells him to write a song and teach it to the people so that *when many troubles come upon them, this song will testify against them, for it will not be forgotten.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a song about the future, given to be memorised before any of it happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will hide My face** from them,\" He said, \"and see what their end will be.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence, and it is the sentence the whole exile is described by."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *I will destroy them*. Not *I will leave them*. **Hide My face** — the Hebrew is to conceal, to cover over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What hiding is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest of the song is the proof. Read on past this passage and it goes to *the LORD will vindicate His people and have compassion on His servants… He will avenge the blood of His servants… and make atonement for His land and His people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The face is hidden and He is still working. That is the season Hosea named *Lo-Ammi*, and it is the season the book of Esther is set inside — no name of God in ten chapters, and His hand on every one of them. See the notes on Hosea 1:8–9 and Esther 1:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mirror"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They made Me jealous with **what is no god**… so I will make them jealous with **what is no people**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two halves are built to match. *No-god* answers *no-people*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul picks up exactly this line in Romans 10 and applies it to the Gentiles being brought in — the *no-people* who become *sons of the living God*. See the note on Romans 9:25–26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Children who have no faith\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is children in whom there is no **emun** — no faithfulness, no steadiness, from the root behind *amen*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not children who did wrong. Children with nothing dependable in them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the song is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD saw it and turned away, **grieved by His sons and daughters**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is grieved and they are still called sons and daughters. The relationship is named in the same verse as the turning away."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the song ends.** Not with the arrows. *Rejoice, O nations, with His people, for He will avenge the blood of His servants… and provide atonement for His land and His people.* Moses taught them the whole thing, and the last note in it is atonement."}]}, "Deuteronomy 33:13–17": {"title": "The blessing on Joseph", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The longest and richest of the twelve blessings Moses gives before he climbs the mountain to die."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it is said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 34 is Moses' death. Chapter 33 is the last thing he does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He blesses the tribes one at a time, and Joseph gets more lines than anyone except Levi."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The water"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With the **precious dew of heaven above** and the **deep waters that lie below**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *deep* is **tehom** — the same word as the deep in Genesis 1, and the springs that burst open in the flood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Water from the sky and water from under the ground. Both directions."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is Isaac's blessing on Jacob, repeated: *may God give you of the dew of heaven, of the fatness of the earth*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seasons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **the best the sun brings forth** and the **finest the months can yield**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the precious fruits brought forth by the sun, and the precious produce of the months*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Months** — the Hebrew is **yerachim**, from **yareach**, moon. Sun crops and moon crops: what ripens in heat and what ripens on the lunar calendar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hills"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With the **choicest gifts of the ancient mountains** and the **bounty of the everlasting hills**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the chief things of the ancient mountains* — **harerei qedem**, mountains of old."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Habakkuk uses the phrase of God's own coming: *the everlasting mountains were scattered, the perpetual hills bowed*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The signature"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the **favor of Him who dwelt in the burning bush**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the good will of Him who dwelt in the bush* — **soneh**, the thornbush of Exodus 3. See the note on Exodus 3:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses names God, in his final speech, by the shrub He appeared in when this whole thing started. Sixty years earlier, on the back side of the desert."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the blessing lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let all this rest **on the head of Joseph**, on the brow of **the prince among his brothers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nezir echav** — the separated one of his brothers. The same root as Nazirite: one set apart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob had used the same phrase over him: *the blessings of your father… shall be on the head of Joseph, and on the crown of the head of him who was separate from his brothers*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Separated is the accurate word for what happened to him. Sold, and gone for twenty-two years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The animal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In majesty he is like **a firstborn bull**; his horns are the **horns of a wild ox**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **reem** — the untameable one. The same beast Balaam used of Israel. See the note on Numbers 24:3–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reach"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With them he will **gore the nations, driving them to the ends of the earth**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two sons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Such are **the ten thousands of Ephraim**; such are **the thousands of Manasseh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ephraim first, and the larger number, though Manasseh was the elder."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob had crossed his hands to do exactly that, and Joseph had tried to move them: *not so, my father, for this one is the firstborn*. The answer was *I know, my son, I know*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses records the outcome as settled fact two generations later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Joseph got"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The double portion. The birthright went to him rather than to Reuben, and his two sons were counted as tribes in their own right — which is why the list of twelve has no Joseph in it, and has Ephraim and Manasseh instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he said about it himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God has made me forget all my toil* — that is Manasseh — and *God has caused me to be fruitful in the land of my affliction*, which is Ephraim."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He named his sons after what had been done to him and what came out of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How Moses signs it.** *The favour of Him who dwelt in the bush.* His last recorded blessing reaches back to a burning thornbush on the far side of the wilderness."}]}, "Deuteronomy 33:24–25": {"title": "\"May your strength match all your days\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses' last words, blessing the tribes one by one. Asher's blessing ends with a line that has outlived its context by a long way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let your gates be barred with iron and bronze, and may your strength match all your days.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is famously compressed — four words, roughly *as your days, your strength*. There is no verb in it, which is why translations differ: *as thy days, so shall thy strength be*, or *your strength will equal your days*, or *may your strength match all your days*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sense everyone agrees on is a ratio. Whatever the days require, the strength is measured to match."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why iron and bronze"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Asher's territory was the northern coastal strip — fertile, rich in olive oil, and exposed. *Let him bathe his feet in oil* is about the olive presses; the barred gates are about the border."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blessing pairs prosperity with security, and then adds strength for the days themselves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How to read it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a blessing spoken over a tribe by a dying man, not a promise of physical vigour to individuals. Read as it stands, it says the supply is matched to the demand — not stockpiled in advance, and not exhausted before the days are."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verses after it.** Moses ends the whole blessing with *the eternal God is your refuge, and underneath are the everlasting arms* — and then he climbs Nebo and dies. The last recorded thing he does is bless people who are going somewhere he is not."}]}, "Deuteronomy 34:1–4": {"title": "Moses sees the land", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last thing an old man does is climb a mountain to look at something he will not walk in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The climb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Moses **climbed up from the plains of Moab to Mount Nebo, to the top of Pisgah**, across from Jericho.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is a hundred and twenty. The chapter says two verses later that *his eyes were not dim nor his natural vigour diminished*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody carries him. He goes up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he is not crossing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Meribah, told to speak to the rock, he struck it twice and said *must we bring water for you out of this rock?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because you did not believe Me, to hallow Me in the eyes of the children of Israel, therefore you shall not bring this assembly into the land.* See the note on Numbers 20:9–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had asked to be let off: *I pray, let me cross over and see the good land*. The answer was *enough of that! Speak no more to Me of this matter*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The showing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there **the LORD showed him the whole land**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayarehu YHWH et-kol-ha'aretz**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is God who does the showing. Nebo has a view, but not this one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sweep"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Gilead as far as Dan, all of Naphtali, the land of Ephraim and Manasseh, all of Judah as far as the western sea, the Negev, and the whole plain of the Valley of Jericho, the City of Palms, all the way to Zoar.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "North to south, and then the coast."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list runs anticlockwise from the far north around to the Dead Sea. It is a survey, not a glance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tribal names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dan, Naphtali, Ephraim, Manasseh, Judah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land is described by the allotments it will have, which have not been made yet. Joshua will divide it years later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses sees the map with the names already on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This is the land I promised on oath to Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob** when I said, 'I will give it to your descendants.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oath is Genesis 12, repeated to Isaac and to Jacob."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses is being shown that a promise made six hundred years earlier is about to be kept."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have let you see it with your own eyes, but you will not cross over into it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **her'iticha ve'einecha** — I have caused you to see it with your eyes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No softening. The sentence stands exactly as it was given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **Moses the servant of the LORD died there** in the land of Moab, according to the word of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He buried him** in a valley in the land of Moab, opposite Beth Peor; **but no one knows his grave to this day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew has God doing the burying. There is no one else on the mountain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The epitaph"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **since then there has not arisen in Israel a prophet like Moses, whom the LORD knew face to face**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who did cross over"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now Joshua the son of Nun was full of the spirit of wisdom, for Moses had laid his hands on him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Moses finally stood in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And behold, two men talked with Him, who were **Moses and Elijah**, who appeared in glory and spoke of His decease which He was about to accomplish at Jerusalem.* See the note on Luke 9:28–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "On a mountain, inside the land, talking about an exodus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word Luke uses for *decease* is **exodos**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews says about all of them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These all died in faith, **not having received the promises, but having seen them afar off** were assured of them, embraced them.* See the note on Hebrews 11:13–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase fits this chapter exactly."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who does the showing.** *The LORD showed him the whole land* — north to south, by the tribal names that had not been assigned yet."}]}, "Deuteronomy 34:5–8": {"title": "A hundred and twenty, and a grave nobody found", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last paragraph about Moses, and it records three things nobody else in the Bible gets."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He was buried by God"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He buried him in the valley in Moab… but to this day no one knows where his grave is.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The subject of that verb is the LORD. Moses climbs a mountain alone, dies alone, and is buried by God — no funeral party, no family plot, no marker."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason is not given. Jude preserves a strange fragment about a dispute over the body, and the plainest guess is the simplest: a known grave of Moses would have become a shrine within a generation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The physical detail"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses was a hundred and twenty years old when he died, yet his eyes were not weak and his strength had not left him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer goes out of his way to say Moses was not failing. He did not die of old age in the ordinary sense; he died because his part was finished and the river was somebody else's to cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That detail sits in scripture alongside Psalm 90 — Moses' own psalm, with its seventy years, eighty if we are strong. The man who wrote that line lived to 120 undimmed. The Bible keeps both on the page."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Thirty days.** Israel wept for Moses for a month, the same span they later gave Aaron. Then the book ends by saying no prophet has risen since like him, whom the LORD knew face to face — and the next book opens with God telling Joshua that Moses is dead and it is time to cross. Both the grief and the moving on are given their room."}]}, "Deuteronomy 34:9": {"title": "The hands that were laid on him", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, near the last page of the Torah, about what transferred."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses has died on Nebo, shown the whole land from Gilead to the Western Sea, and *He buried him in a valley in the land of Moab, opposite Beth Peor, **but no one knows his grave to this day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then thirty days of weeping, and then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The filling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **Joshua son of Nun was filled with the spirit of wisdom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ruach chokmah** — spirit of wisdom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase used of Bezalel, who built the tabernacle. See the note on Exodus 31:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **Moses had laid his hands on him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *for Moses had laid his hands upon him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse gives a cause, and it is a gesture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the laying on happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers 27. Moses had asked God for a successor — *let the LORD set a man over the congregation… **that the congregation of the LORD may not be like sheep which have no shepherd***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the instruction: *take Joshua the son of Nun with you, a man in whom is the Spirit, and **lay your hand on him**… **and you shall give some of your authority to him**, that all the congregation of the children of Israel may be obedient.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Some of your authority.* The Hebrew is *of your honour*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the order: Joshua is already described as *a man in whom is the Spirit* before the hands go on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **the Israelites listened to him** and did what the LORD had commanded Moses.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The purpose stated in Numbers — that the congregation would obey — is recorded as having happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Joshua was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses' attendant for forty years. He had led the fight against Amalek, gone partway up Sinai, stayed in the tent of meeting when Moses left it, and been one of the two spies who said go. See the notes on Exodus 17:8–13 and Numbers 14:5–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **since then there has not arisen in Israel a prophet like Moses, whom the LORD knew face to face**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Torah ends by saying the succession was not a replacement."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua gets the wisdom and the authority. The face-to-face is not transferred, and Numbers 12 had already marked it as unique. See the note on Numbers 12:4–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the gesture goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul to Timothy: *do not neglect **the gift that is in you, which was given to you by prophecy with the laying on of the hands of the eldership**.* See the note on 1 Timothy 4:14–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the second letter: *stir up the gift of God which is in you **through the laying on of my hands***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same act, and Paul attributes the gift to it twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Joshua is told at the start of his own book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Moses my servant is gone. So now it's your turn**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"**The way I was with Moses, that's how I will be with you**.\"* See the note on Joshua 1:1–5."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the verse attributes it to.** Not experience, and not the forty years. Two hands on a head — and the nation listened to him afterward."}]}, "Deuteronomy 6:1–3": {"title": "You and your children and theirs", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The introduction to the Shema, and it names three generations before it says anything else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Deuteronomy** — from the Greek for *second law*. Moses' final speeches on the plains of Moab, to the generation that grew up in the wilderness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone who heard the law at Sinai is dead except Caleb and Joshua. These people were children then, or not born."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Here are **the commands, the laws, the rulings**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mitzvah**, **chuqqim**, **mishpatim** — commandment, statutes, judgments."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three categories recur through the book. Roughly: the charge, the decrees, and the case law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The destination"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Meant for **the land you are crossing over to possess**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law is given for a place they are not in yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three generations"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Live this way, **you and your children and your children's children after them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Named in the first sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the arrangement assumes it will be caught automatically. It has to be handed down twice to reach the third name in the list."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The posture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Holding the LORD **in awe all the days of your life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yare** — to fear, to revere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **your days will be long**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise attached to the fifth commandment, applied here to the whole."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Listen then, Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shema Yisrael** — the same two words that open the next paragraph."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 3 begins it, and verse 4 is the sentence Israel has said twice daily ever since. See the note on Deuteronomy 6:4–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **be careful to live it out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shamarta la'asot** — take care to do."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hearing and doing, joined in one clause. Hebrew has no separate word for *obey*; **shema** does both jobs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promises"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **all may go well with you** and you may **increase greatly**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A land **flowing with milk and honey**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **zavat chalav udevash**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Milk means pasture — enough grass for herds. Honey means either wild honey or date syrup, and either way it means flowering, fruitful country."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase occurs about twenty times in the Pentateuch. Its first use is at the burning bush, in the sentence where Moses is sent: *I have come down to deliver them… to a land flowing with milk and honey*. See the note on Exodus 3:4–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years between the first use and this one, and the same man is saying it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes after the Shema"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And these words which I command you today shall be in your heart. **You shall teach them diligently to your children**, and shall talk of them when you sit in your house, when you walk by the way, when you lie down, and when you rise up.\"* See the note on Deuteronomy 11:18–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *teach diligently* is **shinnantam** — from a root meaning to sharpen, to whet. Repeat until it has an edge."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the four settings cover a whole day: sitting, walking, lying down, getting up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning that follows both"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then beware, lest you forget the LORD who brought you out of the land of Egypt.\"* See the note on Deuteronomy 6:10–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The danger named is not persecution. It is a full house."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who is in the first sentence.** Three generations. The instruction is written from the start on the assumption that it has to be handed on twice to get where it is going."}]}, "Deuteronomy 6:10–12": {"title": "\"Wells you did not dig\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses lists what they are about to inherit, and every item has the same clause attached."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Great and beautiful **cities you did not build**, houses **filled with every good thing you did not fill**, **wells you did not dig**, vineyards and olive trees **you did not plant**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four possessions, four denials."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody else laid every stone, stocked every larder, cut every cistern and planted every tree."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it matters that they are wells"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Digging a cistern out of limestone by hand is the work of months. Planting an olive grove is a fifteen-year investment before the first serious crop — you plant olives for your grandchildren."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They walk into the finished version of other people's decades."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The danger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **when you have eaten your fill, then be careful**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not when you are hungry. When you are full."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The warning is attached to the point of satisfaction, and Deuteronomy makes it repeatedly — *when you have eaten and are full, and have built beautiful houses and dwell in them… beware that you do not forget.* See the note on Deuteronomy 8:17–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What forgetting means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not forget the LORD, the One who brought you out of Egypt, out of the house of slavery**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *forget* is not a memory lapse. It is ceasing to reckon with something."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the description He gives of Himself is not *the One who gave you these cities*. It is the One who brought them out of slavery — the earlier act, the one nobody could have earned."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had done for any of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing. They had walked, and complained, and buried a generation in a desert."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land is described as a gift from beginning to end, and the risk named is that possession will feel like achievement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately after the Shema — *hear, O Israel: the LORD our God, the LORD is one. You shall love the LORD your God with all your heart, with all your soul, and with all your strength.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the instruction to teach it to children, talk about it sitting and walking and lying down and rising up, bind it on the hand, write it on the doorposts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then this: and when you get the cities you did not build, do not forget."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only remembering. There is no work in the passage."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other thing Moses tells them to say.** When your son asks what the testimonies mean, *you shall say to your son: \"**We were slaves of Pharaoh in Egypt, and the LORD brought us out**.\"* The answer to every question about the law is a piece of history, told in the first person plural, by people who were not there."}]}, "Deuteronomy 6:13–15": {"title": "A God who will not share", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses immediately after the warning about a full house."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the LORD your God brings you into the land… with **great and good cities which you did not build**, houses full of all good things which you did not fill, wells you did not dig, vineyards and olive trees you did not plant — when you have eaten and are full, **then beware, lest you forget the LORD**.* See the note on Deuteronomy 6:10–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hold the LORD your God **in awe**. **Serve** Him. **Take your oaths in His name** and no other.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yare** — fear, revere. **Avad** — serve, work for. And swearing by the name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An oath in the ancient world invoked a god as witness and guarantor. Swearing by a name was a declaration of which court you expected to answer to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does with the line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Away with you, Satan! For it is written, 'You shall worship the LORD your God, and Him only you shall serve.'**\"* See the note on Matthew 4:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third temptation, answered from this verse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes *serve*, and the Greek Old Testament has *worship* where the Hebrew has *fear* — which is why the citation reads as it does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "All three of His answers in the wilderness come from Deuteronomy 6 and 8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prohibition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not go running after other gods, **the gods of the peoples all around you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not distant religions. The neighbours'."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua names the same two threats at Shechem: the gods your fathers served beyond the River, and the gods of the Amorites in whose land you now live. See the note on Joshua 24:14–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Behind them or around them, and those are the only two options he offers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the LORD your God, **right there in your midst**, is a God who **will not share your heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **el qanna** — a jealous God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Qanna** is used only of God, five times, and always in this connection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The English word carries the wrong flavour. This is not envy of somebody else's property. It is the jealousy of a spouse, and the whole prophetic tradition takes it that way — Hosea marries an unfaithful woman to make the point."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Indifference would be the alternative, and it would be worse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Right there in your midst.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **beqirbekha** — in your inward parts, among you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The jealousy is stated as a consequence of nearness. He is not a distant deity with territorial claims; He is described as living among them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not stir up His anger against you and be **swept off the face of the land**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land they are about to be given, in the same paragraph as the giving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You shall not tempt the LORD your God as you tempted Him in **Massah**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Massah — *testing*. Where they demanded water and asked *is the LORD among us or not?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the verse Jesus quotes at the second temptation, on the pinnacle. See the note on Matthew 4:5–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two of the three answers come out of this one chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter opened with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hear, O Israel: the LORD our God, the LORD is one. **And you shall love the LORD your God with all your heart**.* See the note on Deuteronomy 6:4–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The command to love, and then a paragraph about a God who will not share."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are the same subject."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the jealousy is mentioned.** Not distance. The reason given in the sentence is that He is *in your midst* — the word is the one for a marriage, and the alternative to it is indifference."}]}, "Deuteronomy 6:4–5": {"title": "Hear, O Israel", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The sentence a Jewish child learns first and a dying Jew says last."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Shema** — the first word, and the name of the prayer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hear.* An imperative, and it means listen and act, both at once. Hebrew has no separate verb for obey."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD our God, **the LORD is one**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is four words: **YHWH Eloheinu YHWH echad**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It can be read *the LORD our God is one LORD*, or *the LORD is our God, the LORD alone*. Either way the point is exclusivity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Echad** — one. And it is the word used in Genesis of a man and his wife: *they shall become **one** flesh*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the word for solitary uniqueness, which is **yachid** — the word used of Isaac, Abraham's only son."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is written"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On the doorposts. *You shall write them on the doorposts of your house and on your gates* — which is what a mezuzah is, a small case with these verses inside, on the frame of a door."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And on the arm and forehead, in the little boxes of the tefillin."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **love the LORD your God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing commanded after the declaration is an affection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not fear, not serve, not obey. Love — and it is the only religion in the ancient world that commands it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With your **whole heart**, with your **whole soul**, with **everything you have**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **levav**, **nephesh**, **meod**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Levav** — the heart, which in Hebrew is the seat of thought and will as much as feeling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nephesh** — the whole self, the life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Meod** — an adverb meaning *very*, *much*, used here as a noun. *With all your muchness*. The old translations render it *might*; the rabbis read it as property."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is left out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing. That is the design of the list."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let nothing in you be held back. Let Him have all of you.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Asked which is the first commandment of all, He recites it — including the *Hear, O Israel* line, which nobody else in the New Testament quotes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is the first commandment. And the second, like it, is this: **You shall love your neighbour as yourself**. There is no other commandment greater than these.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He adds a fourth item to the list — **dianoia**, mind — when He quotes it in Greek."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scribe's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Well said, Teacher. You have spoken the truth… and **to love Him with all the heart, with all the understanding, with all the soul, and with all the strength, and to love one's neighbour as oneself, is more than all the whole burnt offerings and sacrifices**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Jesus: *you are not far from the kingdom of God*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows in Deuteronomy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And these words which I command you today **shall be in your heart**. **You shall teach them diligently to your children**, and shall talk of them **when you sit in your house, when you walk by the way, when you lie down, and when you rise up**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four settings, covering the whole of a day. The teaching is domestic and constant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes after that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When the LORD your God brings you into the land… with **great and good cities which you did not build**, houses full of all good things which you did not fill… **then beware, lest you forget the LORD**.\"* See the note on Deuteronomy 6:10–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Shema is immediately followed by a warning about prosperity."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is commanded first.** Not obedience. *Love* — and the list of what to love Him with is built so that nothing is left outside it."}]}, "Deuteronomy 7:12–15": {"title": "\"He will keep every sickness far from you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A long sentence of blessings, and the first word of it is *if*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you **listen** to these rulings, and **keep** them, and **live them out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs, and the Hebrew of the first is *because you hear*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy is Moses' last set of sermons, delivered on the plains of Moab to a generation about to cross. The book is full of these conditional constructions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God keeps"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the LORD your God will **keep His covenant and His steadfast love** with you, **just as He swore to your fathers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chesed** — covenant loyalty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the ground given is not their obedience but an older oath: *as He swore to your fathers*. See the note on Genesis 22:15–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will **love you** and **bless you** and **multiply you**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is multiplied"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **fruit of your body** and the **fruit of your ground**, your **grain**, your **new wine**, your **oil**, the **young of your herds** and the **lambs of your flocks**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Children, crops and livestock — the whole economy of an agricultural people, itemised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Grain, new wine and oil** is the standard triad of a land under blessing, and the pair Isaac gave Jacob. See the note on Genesis 27:28–29."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will be **blessed above all peoples**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two absences"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*None among you, **man or woman, will be childless**, and **none of your livestock barren**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Barrenness is the recurring grief of the patriarchal narratives — Sarah, Rebekah, Rachel, and later Hannah."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sickness clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD will **keep every sickness far from you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the LORD will take away from you all sickness*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The named diseases"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will **not lay on you any of the terrible diseases of Egypt that you knew**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**That you knew** — they had watched them. Egypt's medical papyri record its endemic conditions, and Israel had lived there four hundred years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus 15 had already promised it, with a name attached: *I will put none of the diseases on you which I have brought on the Egyptians. For **I am the LORD who heals you**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they go instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But He will **lay them on all who hate you**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind of passage this is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the blessing half of a pair. Deuteronomy 28 sets out both at length — fourteen verses of blessing and fifty-four of curse — and Paul quotes the curse side in Galatians: *cursed is everyone who does not continue in all things which are written in the book of the law.* See the note on Galatians 3:10–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The terms are total in both directions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is in the same chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD did not set His love on you nor choose you because you were more in number than any other people, **for you were the least of all peoples**; but **because the LORD loves you**, and because He would keep the oath which He swore to your fathers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses removes the obvious reason and gives no other than the loving itself."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter promises.** Children, harvest, herds and health, itemised and without hedging — and Moses grounds the whole list in an oath sworn to men who had been dead four hundred years."}]}, "Deuteronomy 8:11–16": {"title": "Do not forget in the good times", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The danger Moses names is not poverty. It is a full stomach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be careful **not to forget the LORD your God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb for forgetting runs through the chapter. It appears three times, and it is the chapter's whole subject."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list of conditions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When you have **eaten your fill**, and **built fine houses** to live in, and your **herds and flocks have grown large**, and your **silver and gold have multiplied**, and **everything you own has increased**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five clauses, and every one of them is a blessing. Nothing on the list is a sin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are the exact things promised a few chapters earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The moment of danger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **watch your heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not during the wilderness. After the houses are built."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mechanism"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not let it **swell with pride**, so that **you forget the LORD your God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *lest your heart be lifted up*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pride first, forgetting second. Moses gives the order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is forgotten"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The One who **brought you out of Egypt, out of the house of slavery**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The identity given is the rescue. Forgetting Him means forgetting what state they were found in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wilderness, described"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He led you through **that vast and terrible wilderness**, with its **venomous snakes and scorpions**, its **thirsty ground where there was no water**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses does not romanticise it. He calls it terrible and lists what was in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God did in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **brought water for you out of the hard rock**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rock at Rephidim and the rock at Kadesh. See the notes on Exodus 17:4–7 and Numbers 20:10–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **fed you in the wilderness with manna your fathers had never known**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither is a thing they could have arranged."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Humbling you and testing you, **all for your good in the end**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *to do you good at your latter end*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses names the purpose of the hardship as their benefit, and puts it at the end of the sentence rather than the middle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then you say in your heart, \"**My power and the might of my hand have gained me this wealth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And you shall **remember the LORD your God, for it is He who gives you power to get wealth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both sentences are in the same paragraph. The forgetting produces a specific claim, and the remedy is a specific correction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the earlier part of the chapter says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He humbled you, allowed you to hunger, and fed you with manna… **that He might make you know that man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus quotes that verse in the wilderness, to the first temptation, about bread."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other warning like it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Great and good cities which you did not build, houses full of all good things which you did not fill… **then beware, lest you forget the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua uses the same list when he stands the nation at Shechem. See the note on Joshua 24:11–13."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is dangerous in the chapter.** Not the snakes and not the thirst. Moses gets through those in one verse and spends the rest of the paragraph on the houses."}]}, "Deuteronomy 8:17–18": {"title": "\"He gives you the power to gain wealth\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses names the sentence a prosperous man will say to himself, and answers it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence he warns about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not say in your heart, \"**My own power and the strength of my own hand have made me all this wealth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note where it is said — *in your heart*. Nobody announces this. It is the quiet conclusion a successful man reaches privately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *my power and the might of my hand*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When the danger arrives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the verses before it. This is a warning for the good times, not the hard ones:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When you have eaten and are full, and have built beautiful houses and dwell in them; and when your herds and flocks multiply, and your silver and gold are multiplied… **when your heart is lifted up, and you forget the LORD your God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter locates the risk precisely: after the wilderness, not during it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Remember the LORD your God — **for it is He who gives you the power to gain wealth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *He gives you wealth*. He gives the **power to get** it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **koach** — strength, ability, capacity. The energy, the wit, the health, the opportunity, the instinct for the thing: all of it named as issued."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the man's mistake is not in noticing that he worked hard. He did. The mistake is in where he thinks the working came from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why He does it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So that He may keep the covenant He swore to your fathers**, as it is today.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The purpose clause is startling. Not so that they could be comfortable — so that a promise made to Abraham, Isaac and Jacob would stand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wealth is instrumental. It is covenant-keeping, in the form of a good harvest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter says about the wilderness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He humbled you, allowed you to hunger, and fed you with manna… **that He might make you know that man shall not live by bread alone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years of a nation being taught that the food came from somewhere. Then the land, the houses, the herds — and the warning that the lesson is easier to forget at a full table than at an empty one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your garments did not wear out on you, nor did your foot swell, these forty years.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The alternative ending"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses spells out where the *my own hand* sentence goes: *if you by any means forget the LORD your God… you shall surely perish.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not soften it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Proverbs adds.** *The blessing of the LORD makes one rich, **and He adds no sorrow with it**.* Two claims — where it comes from, and what does not come attached. See the note on Proverbs 10:22–27."}]}, "Deuteronomy 8:2–5": {"title": "\"Your clothes did not wear out\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses tells them what forty years in a desert was for, and the answer is not punishment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Remember the whole long way\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Remember **the whole long way** the LORD your God **led you** through the wilderness these forty years.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Led you.* Not *allowed you to wander*. The route was chosen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three purposes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He led you there **to humble you** and **to test you**, **to see what was truly in your heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *test* is to prove, to assay — what you do to metal to find out what is in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The testing is not to inform God. It is to bring what is inside a person out where it can be seen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hunger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He humbled you. **He let you grow hungry.** Then He fed you with manna.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is stated plainly, and the hunger comes first. It is not an oversight being corrected; it is the first half of a two-part lesson."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bread you had never known and neither had your fathers.* The name **manna** comes from their own question — *man hu*, what is it?"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was teaching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Teaching you that **a person does not live by bread alone, but by every word that comes from the mouth of the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the verse Jesus quoted in the wilderness, after forty days without food, when He was told to turn stones into bread."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel had forty years and failed the lesson repeatedly. He had forty days and answered out of the book they were given."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes Deuteronomy three times in that encounter, and all three come from this stretch of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail nobody would invent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In all those forty years **your clothes did not wear out on you, and your feet did not swell**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years of walking in a desert. Sandals, robes, and the feet inside them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses says it as a matter of record to people who had lived it. Nehemiah repeats it centuries later in a prayer: *forty years You sustained them in the wilderness; they lacked nothing; their clothes did not wear out and their feet did not swell.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interpretation given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Know it in your heart: **as a father disciplines the son he loves**, so the LORD your God was disciplining you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yasar**, the same root as *musar* in Proverbs 3 — instruction, the whole business of bringing a child up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews 12 makes the identical argument in Greek with **paideia**, and quotes Proverbs to do it. See the notes on Proverbs 3:9–12 and Hebrews 12:5–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years in a desert, described by Moses as a father raising a son."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the LORD your God is bringing you into a good land* — and then the warning about what a full stomach does to the memory: *when you have eaten and are full… beware that you do not forget.* See the note on Deuteronomy 8:17–18."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they were not told at the time.** Any of this. The explanation comes forty years later, from a man about to die, to their children. Nobody eating manna in year three knew what it was for."}]}, "Deuteronomy 8:6–9": {"title": "\"A land where you will lack nothing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses describes the country ahead in the concrete terms of somebody who has been living on manna for forty years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Streams and springs, water welling up from valleys and hills; wheat and barley, vines and fig trees and pomegranates; olive oil and honey; bread without going without; stones of iron, and hills you can dig copper from.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven crops — the same seven the rabbis later called the species of the land — plus water, bread, and metal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a poetic abundance. It is an inventory: food, drink, and the raw material for tools and weapons."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two lines that frame it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before: *keep the commands of the LORD your God — walk in His ways and hold Him in awe.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "After, a few verses on: *when you have eaten and are satisfied, praise the LORD your God for the good land He has given you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The abundance sits between an instruction and a thanksgiving. Moses is not worried about their poverty. He is worried about what full barns do to memory — the very next warning is *be careful that you do not forget.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The danger he names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You may say to yourself, \"My power and the strength of my hands have produced this wealth for me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the risk this chapter exists to address, and it comes with the remedy: *remember the LORD your God, for it is He who gives you the ability to produce wealth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that the ability is unreal. That its source is elsewhere."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the manna comes first.** The chapter opens with forty years of being humbled, hungry, and fed with something they had never seen — *to teach you that man does not live on bread alone.* The lesson in the desert is what makes the land safe to enter. Jesus quotes that line in His own wilderness. See the note on Matthew 4:1–4."}]}, "Deuteronomy 9:9–14": {"title": "Forty days, and a calf at the bottom", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses retells the worst thing that happened to him, forty years later, to a generation that was not there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The forty days"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I stayed on the mountain **forty days and forty nights, eating no bread and drinking no water**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a fast he chose. He was in the cloud on Sinai and did not eat or drink for the whole of it — which is beyond what a body does unaided."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does it twice in this chapter. Forty days for the tablets, and then another forty on his face after the calf."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The two stone tablets, **written with the finger of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice in the passage. The only thing in Scripture described as written by God's own finger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get up, go down from here quickly**, for your people, the ones you brought out of Egypt, have corrupted themselves.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is handed the tablets and told in the same breath to go down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the pronouns: *your people, the ones **you** brought out of Egypt.* God says it that way to Moses; Moses says it back the other way in his prayer — *Your people, whom You brought out.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"They have turned so quickly\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *they have turned aside quickly out of the way.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six weeks. They had heard the voice out of the fire and agreed to everything — *all that the LORD has spoken we will do* — and they lasted six weeks. See the note on Exodus 19:7–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Let Me alone**, so that I may destroy them… **and I will make you into a nation mightier and greater than they are**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same offer made to Moses that was made to nobody else: start over with you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He turns it down and spends the next forty days face down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did with the tablets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses after this passage: *I took the two tablets and threw them out of my two hands and broke them before your eyes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The covenant was broken before it reached the bottom of the hill. Moses' act simply made visible what had already happened up the mountain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he is telling them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's purpose is stated at the top: *do not think in your heart… \"Because of my righteousness the LORD has brought me in to possess this land.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is telling them the calf story to stop them believing they earned the country they are about to walk into."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Understand that the LORD your God is not giving you this good land to possess because of your righteousness, for **you are a stiff-necked people**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The forty days that answer these"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus in the wilderness, forty days without food, and the first thing said to Him is *if You are the Son of God, command that these stones become bread.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He answers out of this book — *man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God* — quoting Deuteronomy 8, one chapter earlier. See the note on Deuteronomy 8:2–5."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How Moses ends the chapter.** *So I fell down before the LORD forty days and forty nights… because the LORD had said He would destroy you. Therefore I prayed.* Eighty days on that mountain without food, and he spends half of them arguing for people who had just replaced him with a calf."}]}, "Ecclesiastes 1:1–3": {"title": "Breath on a cold morning", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The book opens with the thesis, and then spends twelve chapters testing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These are the words of **the Teacher, a son of David, who once wore the crown in Jerusalem**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **Qoheleth** — one who assembles, or one who speaks to an assembly. It is a participle, not a name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A son of David who reigned in Jerusalem, and who says later *I have attained greatness, and excelled more than all who were before me in Jerusalem*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Vapor, he says. **Only vapor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hevel** — breath, vapour, mist."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Illumination reads it literally rather than as *vanity*, which came into English through the Latin *vanitas*. The Hebrew word is physical: the breath you can see on a cold morning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Breath on a cold morning, **gone before you can hold it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not worthless. Not meaningless. Untenable — it will not stay in the hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything the book examines fails the same test: it is real, and it will not keep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name Abel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hevel** is also the name of Adam's second son."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man whose whole life in the text is two verses long and ends in a field."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The superlative"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Vapor of vapors*, in the Hebrew — **havel havalim**, the same construction as *holy of holies* and *song of songs*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most vaporous of vapours. Hebrew makes superlatives by repeating the noun."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five occurrences of the word in one verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **what does a person truly gain** from all the hard work he does **under the sun**, from the sweat of every long day?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yitron** — profit, surplus, what is left over. A commercial word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "After the ledger is settled, what remains?"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Under the sun"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tachat hashemesh** — the phrase appears twenty-nine times in the book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It sets the boundary of the investigation. Everything Qoheleth examines, he examines within this world, on its own terms, without appeal to anything outside it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question is honest, and the frame is the reason the answer keeps coming back empty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he tried"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wisdom, pleasure, building projects, gardens, pools, silver, gold, singers, and *whatever my eyes desired I did not keep from them*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then I looked on all the works that my hands had done… and indeed all was vanity and grasping for the wind.* See the note on Ecclesiastes 2:9–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not conclude this from a distance. He ran the experiment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he keeps returning to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is nothing better for a man than that he should eat and drink, and that his soul should enjoy good in his labour. **This also, I saw, was from the hand of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bread, work, and company — held loosely, received as a gift."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The end of the book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us hear the conclusion of the whole matter: **fear God and keep His commandments**, for this is man's all.* See the note on Ecclesiastes 12:13–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is on the other side of the sun"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If then you were raised with Christ, **seek those things which are above**, where Christ is, sitting at the right hand of God.* See the note on Colossians 3:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one category Qoheleth's method could not reach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the New Testament does with the word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You do not know what will happen tomorrow. For **what is your life? It is even a vapour** that appears for a little time and then vanishes away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James uses the exact image, and draws it into a sentence about planning."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word.** **Hevel** — vapour, breath on a cold morning. Not worthless and not meaningless. Real, and impossible to keep hold of."}]}, "Ecclesiastes 1:4–11": {"title": "Nothing new under the sun", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four things that keep moving and never arrive anywhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The generations"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One generation walks off the stage, and another walks on. **But the earth stays where it is, unmoved, always**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **le'olam omadet** — stands forever."}, {"k": "p", "t": "People are the transient element. The ground they walk on is not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Qoheleth begins his survey by inverting what we assume."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sun"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sun rises, **tired and faithful**. The sun sets, and **hurries back to the place it will rise from again**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **sho'ef** — panting, gasping. The word for hard breathing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It runs all day, and then runs back overnight to the starting line to do it again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Round and round it goes, **turning and returning, never finished, never still**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew piles the same root up: **sovev sovev**, going around and going around."}, {"k": "p", "t": "South, north, around, back. Motion in every direction, and no progress in any."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rivers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every river runs to the sea, **yet the sea is never full**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the water goes back to the place the rivers began, so the running can start all over again.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An observation about the water cycle, made in Hebrew poetry, in a book about futility."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict on all four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everything is weary. **More weary than anyone can say**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yege'im** — wearied, exhausted. Applied to things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The senses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The eye looks and looks and **is never satisfied with seeing**. The ear listens and listens and **is never filled with hearing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two organs designed to take things in, and neither reaches capacity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sea will not fill, and neither will the eye. Qoheleth puts them in the same paragraph."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The famous line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever has been is what will be. Whatever has been done is what will be done. **There is nothing new under the sun**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **eyn kol chadash tachat hashamesh**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Under the sun* again — the frame of the whole investigation. See the note on Ecclesiastes 1:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The challenge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Is there anything of which someone says, \"**Look at this, it is new**\"? **It was already here, long before us**, in the ages that came and went.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He anticipates the objection and answers it: you only think it is new because you were not there before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last cut"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No one remembers the people who lived before. And **the people yet to come will not be remembered either** by those who come after them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not just that achievement fades. That the achievers do."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the sharpest sentence in the section, and it is aimed at the one consolation the ancient world had — being remembered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is new"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything Qoheleth says is true of what is *under the sun*, which is the boundary he set himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, if anyone is in Christ, **he is a new creation**; old things have passed away.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 5:16–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold, **I make all things new**.* See the note on Revelation 21:5–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word Qoheleth searched the world for and could not find, spoken from the throne."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is remembered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The righteous will be **in everlasting remembrance***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will write on him **the name of My God**… and **My new name***. See the note on Revelation 3:12–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Qoheleth's last complaint is that nobody is remembered, and the answer offered is a name written down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with the observation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not despair. Three chapters later: *there is a time for everything*, and *He has made everything beautiful in its time*. See the note on Ecclesiastes 3:9–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The circling is not denied. It is put under someone's hand."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The sun's day.** **Sho'ef** — panting. It runs all day, and then runs back overnight to the line it started from, to do it again tomorrow."}]}, "Ecclesiastes 12:11–12": {"title": "Goads and nails", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The Preacher's sign-off, and the two images he chooses for words are both about pressure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the book has been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve chapters of a man testing everything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wisdom, pleasure, building, planting, silver, gold, singers, work — *and indeed all was vanity and grasping for the wind*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hevel** — vapour, breath. The word appears nearly forty times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just come"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most beautiful passage about aging in the language: *remember now your Creator in the days of your youth*, and then the failing house — the keepers of the house trembling, the grinders ceasing, the doors shut in the street, the almond tree blossoming, the grasshopper a burden."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before the silver cord is loosed, or the golden bowl is broken.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The words of the wise are **like goads**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **darvonot** — ox goads."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same tool Shamgar killed six hundred men with. See the note on Judges 3:31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A goad is not decoration. It is a pointed pole for moving an animal that has stopped, and its Hebrew name is *a teacher of oxen*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Words that get you moving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the sayings of the masters are **like nails firmly driven in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **masmerot netuim** — planted nails."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Baalei asuppot** — masters of collections, which probably means the compilers of anthologies like Proverbs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A goad moves you. A nail holds something in place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two functions: disturbance and fixity. Between them they cover what teaching is for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The source"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Given by **one Shepherd**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mero'eh echad**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A single sentence, and it is the theological anchor of a book that has spent twelve chapters being sceptical about everything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever wisdom the collections contain, the Preacher attributes it to one source."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Beyond these, my child, **take care**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *be warned*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The famous line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Of the making of many books there is **no end**, and **much study wearies the body**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yegiat basar** — weariness of the flesh."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written on a scroll by a man who had written several, and it has been quoted by exhausted students ever since."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point is not that books are bad. It is that the list of them does not close, and a person does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us hear the conclusion of the whole matter: **Fear God and keep His commandments, for this is man's all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *this is the whole of man*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For God will bring every work into judgment, including every secret thing, whether good or evil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve chapters of vapour, and the last two verses land on something solid."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ecclesiastes is the honest book. It says out loud that the same thing happens to the wise man and the fool, that the race is not to the swift, and that nobody knows what comes after."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then it ends by naming one Shepherd and one duty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the goad turns up again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Saul, Saul, why are you persecuting Me? **It is hard for you to kick against the goads**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said on the Damascus road."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An ox that kicks back at the goad only drives it in further."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two images.** A goad moves what has stopped; a nail holds what would move. And the Preacher attributes both to one Shepherd, in a book that doubts nearly everything else."}]}, "Ecclesiastes 2:24–26": {"title": "\"The task of gathering and piling up\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first place in Ecclesiastes where the Preacher stops complaining and says something plain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he has been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters of trying everything. Wisdom, pleasure, wine, houses, vineyards, gardens, pools, servants, herds, silver and gold, singers, every delight — *whatever my eyes desired I did not keep from them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *I hated life… I hated all my labour, because I must leave it to the man who comes after me, and who knows whether he will be wise or a fool?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is nothing better for a person than to **eat and drink and find joy in his work**. And this, I saw, is **from the hand of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bread, a drink, and satisfaction in the day's work. That is the whole list."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the second sentence is what makes it more than resignation — he names where it comes from. Ordinary contentment is described as a gift handed over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Apart from Him\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **apart from Him, who can eat, who can find any real enjoyment?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not who can afford to eat. Who can eat and get anything out of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just proved by experiment that a man can own everything in that list and enjoy none of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To the one who **pleases Him**, God gives **wisdom and knowledge and joy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "None of the three is money."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the fourth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But to the one who misses the mark, He gives **the task of gathering and piling up, only to hand it over** to the one who pleases God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *to gather and to heap up*. It is described as an assignment — a **task** given."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does the collecting. Somebody else does the keeping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs says the same thing twice: *the wealth of the sinner is stored up for the righteous*, and *whoever swells his wealth by interest gathers it for one who is kind to the poor.* See the note on Proverbs 28:7–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"This too is vapour\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This too is vapour, a chasing after the wind.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **hebel** — breath, vapour, what you can see on a cold morning and cannot hold."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He applies it to the gathering, not to the gift. The eating and drinking and joy are *from the hand of God*; the heaping up is wind."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the book ends.** *Fear God and keep His commandments, for this is the whole duty of man.* Twelve chapters of a man with unlimited means testing every avenue, and the conclusion is two clauses long."}]}, "Ecclesiastes 2:9–11": {"title": "Then I turned and looked", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He built everything a man could build, and then walked round it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The experiment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter is a controlled test."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I said in my heart, \"Come now, I will test you with mirth; therefore enjoy pleasure.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wine, houses, vineyards, gardens, orchards, pools of water, servants, herds, silver and gold, singers and musical instruments."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I grew great. **I grew greater than anyone who lived in Jerusalem before me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vegadalti vehosaphti** — I became great and I added."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The control"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And through all of it **my wisdom stayed with me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **aph chokmati amedah li**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point of the clause is that this is not a story of a wise man losing his head. He is watching the experiment while running it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The permission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever my eyes desired, **I did not hold back from them**. **I refused my heart no joy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lo atzalti** — I did not withhold."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing was denied. Whatever limit anyone else's life has, this one did not have it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wages"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My heart took pleasure in all my work, and **that was my reward for all my labor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vezeh-hayah chelqi mikkol-amali** — this was my portion from all my labour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pleasure in the work was real, and he counts it as what he got."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But then **I turned and looked**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **uphaniti ani** — and I turned, I myself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words that change the chapter. He stops building and walks round it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he sees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I looked at **everything my hands had made**, and at **all the sweat it had cost me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **uva'amal she'amalti la'asot** — and at the labour I had laboured to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And I saw it. **All of it. Vapor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hakkol havel** — all of it is **hevel**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Breath, vapour, mist. See the note on Ecclesiastes 1:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **chasing after the wind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ur'ut ruach** — a shepherding of wind, or a striving after wind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase runs through the book seven times, always attached to the moment he stops and looks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There was **no lasting gain under the sun**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ve'eyn yitron tachat hashamesh** — and there is no profit under the sun."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yitron** — surplus, what is left over. The commercial word from chapter 1's opening question. See the note on Ecclesiastes 1:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked the question at the start of the book and here reports the answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What makes the verdict weigh"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not describing something he read about."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man saying it has the houses, the vineyards, the pools and the singers, and states plainly that he enjoyed them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he lands a few verses later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is nothing better for a man than that he should **eat and drink, and that his soul should enjoy good in his labour**. This also, I saw, was from the hand of God.* See the note on Ecclesiastes 2:24–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bread, work, and the day — received rather than accumulated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book concludes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Fear God and keep His commandments, **for this is man's all**.* See the note on Ecclesiastes 12:13–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet indeed **I also count all things loss** for the excellence of the knowledge of Christ Jesus my Lord.* See the note on Philippians 3:7–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who had the religious equivalent, and did the same arithmetic."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two words.** *And I turned.* He stops building, walks round what he has built, and the chapter's verdict is what he sees from there."}]}, "Ecclesiastes 3:11": {"title": "\"Eternity in the human heart\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it explains why nothing under the sun has ever been enough."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Directly after the famous list — *a time to be born and a time to die, a time to plant and a time to pluck up, a time to weep and a time to laugh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fourteen pairs, and then this verse is the Preacher's comment on all of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Beautiful in its time\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has made **everything beautiful in its time**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **yapheh** is beautiful, fitting, appropriate. Each thing suits the moment it belongs to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The weeping and the laughing are both on the list, and both are called beautiful in their season."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause the verse is known for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has also **set eternity in the human heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **olam** — the age, the far distance, perpetuity. The same word used of God's everlasting covenant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something with no horizon has been placed inside a creature who lives seventy years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the Preacher's diagnosis of the human problem, and it is not a complaint about sin. It is a description of design."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows from it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet **no one can fully grasp what God has done from beginning to end**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The capacity to want the whole thing, and no capacity to see it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the tension the entire book runs on. Twelve chapters of a man with unlimited means testing every avenue — wisdom, pleasure, building, wealth, work — and finding each one *vapour*, because it is finite and he is not built for finite."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the experiments failed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not because the things were bad. He says elsewhere that eating your bread with joy and enjoying your work are *from the hand of God.* See the note on Ecclesiastes 2:24–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They failed because he was measuring them against something with no edge."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other half of the same observation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God made humankind **upright**, but we have gone looking for **endless clever schemes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ecclesiastes 7:29, and it is the Preacher's other conclusion about people: made straight, and inventive about not staying that way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the Preacher lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever God does, it shall be forever. Nothing can be added to it, and nothing taken from it. God does it, that men should fear before Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the very next verse. The thing that is permanent is what God does, and the eternity in the heart is what recognises it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Augustine made of this verse.** *You have made us for Yourself, and our heart is restless until it rests in You.* Sixteen centuries of readers have taken the restlessness in this line as the point of it rather than the problem with it."}]}, "Ecclesiastes 3:14–15": {"title": "God calls back what has slipped away", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses in which the man who found nothing lasting says what does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I know that **whatever God does endures forever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **le'olam** — forever, to the vanishing point."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything Qoheleth has examined so far is **hevel**, vapour. See the note on Ecclesiastes 1:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here he names an exception, and the word he uses for it is the opposite of vapour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What cannot be done to it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Nothing can be **added** to it, and nothing **taken away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lehosif… ligroa**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same pair used of the law — *you shall not add to the word which I command you, nor take from it* — and at the end of Revelation, of the prophecy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A completed work. Human contribution can neither improve nor damage it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God has made it so, **that people would stand in awe before Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **sheyir'u milephanav** — that they may fear from before Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The permanence is deliberate and the intended effect is named. Qoheleth does not present it as an interesting fact."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever is, **has already been**. Whatever will be, **already was**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The line from chapter 1, restated: *whatever has been is what will be… there is nothing new under the sun*. See the note on Ecclesiastes 1:4–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has said it before as a complaint. Here it sits beside a sentence about God's work enduring, and the tone changes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **God calls back what has slipped away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **veha'Elohim yevaqqesh et-nirdaph** — and God seeks what is pursued, or driven away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nirdaph** is a passive participle of **radaph**, to pursue, chase, hunt. What has been chased off, what has fled, what is gone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the clause is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the most difficult line in the book, and translations divide over it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read one way, it means God requires an account of what has passed. Read another, and it is what the Illumination has: what has slipped past is fetched back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both readings agree on the grammar's main point — that the vanished is not out of reach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it matters here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything Qoheleth has complained about is loss."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No one remembers the people who lived before.* The eye never full, the sea never full, the generations walking off the stage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: God seeks what has been driven away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this shows up as a person"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What man of you, having a hundred sheep, if he loses one of them, **does not go after the one which is lost until he finds it?**\"* See the note on Luke 15:3–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is the same idea Qoheleth's Hebrew carries. Going after what is gone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **the Son of Man has come to seek and to save that which was lost**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is famous for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To everything there is a season, a time for every purpose under heaven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has made everything beautiful in its time. Also **He has put eternity in their hearts***. See the note on Ecclesiastes 3:9–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ha'olam** — the same word as *forever* in verse 14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thing God's work is, He has put inside them. Which is why the vapour is unsatisfying."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last clause.** **God seeks what is driven away.** The book's whole complaint is that things vanish and nobody goes after them — and this is the one line that says somebody does."}]}, "Ecclesiastes 4:9–12": {"title": "A cord of three strands", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four short arguments for company, from the book that spends most of its time on futility."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens on oppression — *I looked again at all the acts of oppression under the sun, and I saw the tears of the oppressed, and they had no comforter*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then a man Solomon describes a few verses earlier: *there is one alone, without companion; he has neither son nor brother. Yet there is no end to all his labours… and he never asks, \"For whom do I toil?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This paragraph is the answer to that man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Two are better than one, because they have **a good reward for their labor**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If one falls down, **his friend can lift him up**. But **pity the one who falls and has no one to help him rise**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *woe to him who is alone when he falls*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Falling is assumed. The variable is who is nearby."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And if two lie down together, **they keep each other warm**, but **how can one be warm alone?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nights in the Judean hills get cold, and a traveller's cloak was also his blanket. Two men sharing them survive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though one may be overpowered, **two can stand their ground**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *if one prevails against him, two shall withstand him*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fourth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **a cord of three strands is not quickly broken**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the threefold cord*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument has been about pairs for three verses, and the last line adds one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rope is stronger than the sum of its strands because of how it is laid — three twisted together bear more than three side by side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line's afterlife"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is read at weddings constantly, and the chapter is not about marriage. Ecclesiastes is talking about work, cold nights, muggings and the man with no brother."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The application is wider than the usual use of it, not narrower."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the book has come from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Vanity of vanities, all is vanity.* Thirty-eight uses of **hevel** — vapour, breath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the conclusions Solomon keeps reaching: nothing new under the sun, the wise man dies like the fool, and *I hated life*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Into that, this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What else the book grants"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Go. **Eat your bread with joy**, and drink your wine with a glad heart, for **God has already approved what you do**.* See the note on Ecclesiastes 9:7–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The concessions in Ecclesiastes are always concrete — bread, wine, a companion, work you can see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book ends on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us hear the conclusion of the whole matter: **Fear God and keep His commandments**, for this is man's all.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does with the principle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He sent them out **two by two**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *where two or three are gathered together in My name, I am there in the midst of them.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one the paragraph is written against.** A man with no son and no brother, working without end and never asking who for. Four arguments follow, and the last one adds a third strand to a book that had been counting to one."}]}, "Ecclesiastes 7:8–10": {"title": "Not a wise question", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three proverbs, and the last one names a question people ask without noticing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **end of a thing is better than its beginning**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tov acharit davar mereshito**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Acharit** — the end, the outcome, the latter part."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Beginnings are easy to admire. The book has spent six chapters watching things start well."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The patience"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **patience is better than pride**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tov erek-ruach miggevah-ruach** — long of spirit is better than high of spirit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two spirits, one long and one high. The idiom for patience is *long*; the idiom for arrogance is *high*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same construction as **erek appayim** — long of nostrils — used of God at Sinai."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The anger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not be quick to anger, for **anger settles down in the laps of fools and makes its home there**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ka'as becheiq kesilim yanuach** — vexation rests in the bosom of fools."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nuach** — to rest, to settle. The root of Noah's name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anger is described as a lodger. It arrives, sits down, and stays."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And do not sigh, \"**Why were the old days better than now?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **meh hayah shehayyamim harishonim hayu tovim me'elleh** — what was it that the former days were better than these?"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That is **not a wise question**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ki lo mechokmah sha'alta al-zeh** — for not out of wisdom have you asked about this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Illumination gives the reason: *you ask it out of longing, not out of understanding*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question presents itself as analysis and is actually grief."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It also assumes its own premise. Qoheleth has already established that *there is nothing new under the sun*, and that *no one remembers the people who lived before*. See the note on Ecclesiastes 1:4–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "If nothing was ever new, the old days were the same days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book has just been doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A good name is better than precious ointment, and **the day of death than the day of one's birth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is better to go to the house of mourning than to go to the house of feasting.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sorrow is better than laughter, for by a sad countenance the heart is made better.* See the note on Ecclesiastes 7:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A run of hard comparisons, and this one belongs to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the elders did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But many of the priests and Levites and heads of the fathers' houses, **old men who had seen the first temple, wept with a loud voice** when the foundation of this temple was laid before their eyes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the people could not discern the noise of the shout of joy from the noise of the weeping.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God said to them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Who is left among you who saw this temple in its former glory? And how do you see it now?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *\"The glory of this latter temple shall be greater than the former.\"* See the note on Haggai 2:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question is granted and answered in the other direction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not remember the former things, nor consider the things of old. Behold, I will do a new thing**.\"* See the note on Isaiah 43:18–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says he does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Forgetting those things which are behind and reaching forward to those things which are ahead, **I press toward the goal**.* See the note on Philippians 3:12–14."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verdict.** *Not a wise question.* It looks like analysis and is grief, and it assumes a premise the book has already dismantled."}]}, "Ecclesiastes 8:2–4": {"title": "\"His word carries power\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Practical advice about standing in front of somebody who can do whatever he likes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep the king's command, I say, **for you swore it before God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *because of the oath of God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Loyalty to the throne is grounded in an oath sworn in God's name, which makes it a matter between the subject and God rather than only between the subject and the king."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The court manners"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not **rush to leave his presence**, and do not **take up a bad cause**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leaving abruptly was an insult; a Persian or Egyptian court had elaborate rules about withdrawal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A bad cause** — the Hebrew is *an evil word*, or *an evil matter*. Do not attach yourself to a losing case in front of a man who cannot be appealed against."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the king does as he pleases**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated without approval or complaint. Ecclesiastes is describing how it is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His **word carries power**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the word of a king is authority* — **shilton**, dominion, mastery, the right to act."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A king's speech is not a request or an opinion. It is the thing itself: the word and the event are the same object."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question nobody asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **no one can say to him, \"What are you doing?\"**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same clause is used of God in Job — *who can say to Him, \"What are You doing?\"* — and in Daniel, of the Most High: *no one can restrain His hand or say to Him, \"What have You done?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ecclesiastes applies to a human throne the phrase reserved elsewhere for the throne above it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is writing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The words of the Preacher, the son of David, king in Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A king, writing about what it is like to stand in front of a king. He knew both sides of the audience chamber."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the book goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately after: *he who keeps his command will experience nothing harmful, and a wise man's heart discerns both time and judgment.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, four verses on: *no one has power over the spirit to retain the spirit, and no one has power in the day of death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word for power. The king's word rules everything except that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the New Testament says about the readers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To Him who loved us and washed us from our sins in His own blood, **and has made us kings and priests** to His God and Father.* See the note on Revelation 1:4–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other king in the book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed are you, O land, when your king is the son of nobles, and your princes feast at the proper time — for strength and not for drunkenness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ecclesiastes has opinions about what makes a throne worth serving. It just does not pretend that a bad one has less authority."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the verse is about.** Not obedience for its own sake. A word spoken from a throne is an act — and Ecclesiastes borrows the language it uses of God to say so."}]}, "Ecclesiastes 9:11–12": {"title": "\"Time and chance meet them all\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Five things that ought to be reliable, and the Preacher says none of them is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The race is **not always won by the swift**, nor the battle by the strong. Bread does not always go to the wise, nor riches to the clever, nor favour to the skilled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Speed, strength, wisdom, cleverness, skill. Five advantages, and in each case the expected outcome is denied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not saying these are worthless. He is saying they do not settle it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Time and chance meet them all\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *chance* is **pega** — an occurrence, a happening, something that befalls."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Its close relative **qarah** is the word Abraham's servant used at the well: *give me a happening today* — asking God for the right occurrence. See the note on Genesis 24:12–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same idea from two directions. The Preacher says outcomes are not controlled by the qualifications; the servant asks the One who is not limited by them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ecclesiastes is written *under the sun* — the phrase appears nearly thirty times. It is a deliberate experiment in reasoning about life from the visible evidence alone, and this is one of its findings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "From under the sun, the fastest man losing the race looks like chance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The nets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Like fish caught in a cruel net, like birds caught in a snare, the children of earth are trapped in an evil time **when it falls on them without warning**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No one knows his hour.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The images are of creatures going about their business with no idea a net exists. It is not cruelty in the passage — it is unforeseeability."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it rules out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any scheme for guaranteeing an outcome by being good enough at something."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Preacher has already tried that in chapters 1 and 2 — wisdom, pleasure, building, wealth, every avenue a man with unlimited means could take — and this verse is part of the report."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's own advice, a few verses earlier: *go, eat your bread with joy, and drink your wine with a merry heart… live joyfully with the wife whom you love… whatever your hand finds to do, do it with your might.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not paralysis. Work hard, eat well, love your wife — and hold the outcome loosely, because you were never the one holding it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the book ends.** *Fear God and keep His commandments, for this is the whole duty of man.* Twelve chapters reasoning from under the sun, and the conclusion looks up."}]}, "Ecclesiastes 9:7–9": {"title": "\"God has already approved\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The bleakest book in the Bible stops to tell somebody to enjoy their dinner."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **go**. **Eat your bread with joy**, and **drink your wine with a glad heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three imperatives, and none of them is religious."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **God has already approved what you do**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *God has already accepted your works*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The approval is in the past tense and stated before anything is asked. The eating follows it rather than earning it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clothes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let your clothes be **white, always**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "White linen in that climate was what you wore to a feast. Ordinary work clothes were undyed and dark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction is to dress for a celebration as the normal condition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oil"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **never let the oil run dry on your head**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Oil on the head was for guests and for festivity. A host anointed a visitor's head — which is the courtesy Simon the Pharisee omitted. See the note on Luke 7:44–50."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both images are things you do on a good day, and both are commanded as a standing state."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The marriage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Enjoy life with **the one you love**, through **all the fleeting days God has given you** under the sun, **all your fleeting days**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hevel** — vapour, breath, mist. It is the book's key word, used thirty-eight times, and usually translated *vanity*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Solomon repeats it twice in one verse here, and both times attached to the instruction to enjoy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sentence is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book's argument is that life is short, unpredictable, and cannot be mastered. He does not resolve that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He draws a different conclusion from it: since you cannot secure the days, receive them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the living know that they will die… **also their love, their hatred, and their envy have now perished**; nevermore will they have a share in anything done under the sun.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the *so go* follows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever your hand finds to do, **do it with your might**; for there is no work or device or knowledge or wisdom in the grave where you are going.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book calls it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is **your portion** in life, and in the labour you pour out under the sun.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **cheleq** — a share, an allotted piece. The surveying word, the one the Levites were given about God Himself. See the note on Numbers 18:8–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How often the book says this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven times, in almost the same words: *there is nothing better for a man than that he should eat and drink, and that his soul should enjoy good in his labour.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And each time it adds: *this also, I saw, was from the hand of God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the book ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us hear the conclusion of the whole matter: **Fear God and keep His commandments**, for this is man's all.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What licenses the bread and the wine.** Not that the days are secure — the book says repeatedly that they are vapour. One clause: *God has already approved*, in the past tense, before the eating starts."}]}, "Ephesians 1:15–19": {"title": "What Paul prays for people who already have everything", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just spent twelve verses listing what these believers possess. Then he prays — and he does not ask for a single additional thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit of wisdom and of unveiling, in coming to know Him — that the eyes of your understanding would be flooded with light.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word behind *unveiling* is **apokalypsis**, the uncovering of something already there. Not new information. The removal of whatever is in the way of seeing it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *the eyes of your understanding* is literally *the eyes of your heart* — the seeing organ Paul is worried about is not the intellect alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things he wants them to see"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**what the hope is that He called you into**", "**how glorious and rich a share He has among His holy people**", "**how far past measuring His power is toward us who believe**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second one is worth reading twice. The Greek most naturally means **His** inheritance in the saints — God's own share, and the people are it. Paul has already said in verse 11 that we have obtained an inheritance; now he prays they would grasp that they *are* one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer is for perception, not supply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole shape of it. Everything in chapter 1 has already been given — chosen, adopted, redeemed, forgiven, sealed. Paul's response is to pray that they would know it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does the same thing in chapter 3, where the prayer is that they would have power to grasp the dimensions of a love that surpasses knowledge."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The measure of the power.** *Power in step with the working of His mighty strength* — and then the next verse says what strength that was: the force that raised Christ and seated Him above every authority. Paul's answer to \"how much power is available\" is to point at the resurrection. See the note on Ephesians 1:20–23."}]}, "Ephesians 1:20–23": {"title": "How far above, and what it has to do with you", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is praying that they would know the power at work in them, and to describe its size he points at one event."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure he uses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That was the strength He put to work in Christ when He raised Him from the dead and seated Him at His own right hand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The resurrection is offered as the unit of measurement. Not a general statement that God is powerful — the specific force applied on one morning, named as the same force now at work toward believers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The height"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*High above every ruler and every authority and power and lordship, and above every name that can be named, not only in this present age but in the age still to come.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul empties the vocabulary. Four categories of authority, then *every name that can be named*, then both ages — this one and the next."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is deliberately leaving nothing outside the sentence. Whatever it is, whatever it is called, whenever it exists, it is under."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything so far is about Christ's exaltation. Then:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "*And He put everything under His feet, and gave Him, over all things, **as head to the church, which is His body**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The elevation is not held privately. He is given as head *to the church* — and a head and a body are not in two locations. Paul will say it outright two chapters on: seated together with Him in the heavenly places."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Where the head is seated is where the body is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Under His feet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He put **everything under His feet**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is quoted from Psalm 8 — *You have put all things under his feet* — which was written about man, not about the Messiah. Adam's commission, given back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is the posture Psalm 110 describes from the other side: *sit at My right hand until I make Your enemies **a footstool** under Your feet.* See the note on Mark 12:35–37."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A footstool is what a seated man rests on. The picture is not a struggle in progress."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the feet are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If the head is seated and the enemies are under His feet, then the part of the body that touches them is the part furthest from the head — and it is still His body."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's closing line in the letter says the same thing from the ground: *the God of peace will crush Satan **under your feet** shortly* is Romans, and Ephesians 6 has the church standing, not advancing. See the note on Ephesians 6:13–15."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"The fullness of the One who fills everything.\"** Paul calls the church that, which is an extraordinary thing to call a scattered collection of first-century congregations with the problems his letters keep addressing. He does it anyway."}]}, "Ephesians 1:3–6": {"title": "Everything already given, and where it is kept", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The opening of the longest sentence in the New Testament, and it begins with a claim in the past tense."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Has blessed us\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *will bless*. The verb is aorist — done, complete, a settled fact by the time Paul writes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the extent is total: *with every blessing the Spirit has to give*. Nothing is being held in reserve pending performance. Whatever the Christian life turns out to involve, Paul's starting position is that the giving has already happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is kept"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Up where heaven is* — in the heavenly places. Twice more in this letter Paul uses the phrase, and it is not a description of somewhere distant. It is where Christ is seated, and where he later says believers are seated with Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the blessings are located in the same place as the person they are held in. That is the force of the phrase Paul repeats through this whole passage: **in Him**, in Christ, in the One He loves. Eleven times in twelve verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Before the world was ever laid down\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The choosing is dated earlier than creation, and the purpose is stated: *so that we would stand before Him holy, without a single flaw in us, in love.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things about that. It is the goal of the choosing, not its condition. And the last two words attach either to the standing or to the choosing itself — Greek allows both, and either way love is in the sentence rather than obligation."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The reason given.** *This was simply what pleased Him, and what He wanted.* Paul offers no cause outside God's own good pleasure, and then says the point of the whole thing is *the praise of the shining beauty of His grace*. When he comes to explain why any of it happened, he does not point at anything in us."}]}, "Ephesians 1:7–10": {"title": "Bought out, and the word for it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is one long sentence into this letter and shows no sign of stopping. In Greek, verses 3 to 14 are a single unbroken sentence — the longest in the New Testament — and this is the middle of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word behind \"bought out of our captivity\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Apolutrosis.** The root is *lutron*, a ransom — the price paid to free a prisoner of war or to buy a slave out of ownership. The prefix makes it a release accomplished by that payment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a commercial word being used about people, and it carries a specific picture: someone was held, a price was named, the price was paid, and the person walked out. Paul says where the price came from in the same breath — *by His blood*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Poured over us without measure\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase for grace here is extravagant even by Paul's standards: the *sheer wealth* of His grace, and a verb meaning to make something overflow, to have more than enough of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not describing a sufficiency. He is describing a surplus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the surplus is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It brought with it every kind of wisdom and clear-eyed good sense.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is an unexpected turn. The overflow of grace does not arrive as feeling — it arrives as understanding, and specifically as being let in on something: *He let us in on the secret of what He wants.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the sentence is going.** The purpose Paul names is enormous: that everything, in heaven and on earth, would be gathered up under one head in Christ. The Greek verb is from arithmetic — adding a column of figures and writing the total at the top. All of it, summed up, in Him. That is the end the ransom was paid toward, and Paul reaches it without once pausing for a full stop."}]}, "Ephesians 2:17–19": {"title": "\"No longer guests in someone else's country\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul announces peace twice, to two groups who had nothing in common."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Both"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He came and announced the good news of peace to **you who were far away**, and peace **to those who were near**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Far away is the Gentiles. Near is Israel — who had the covenants, the law, the promises and the temple."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same message is preached to both, and the same peace is announced. Neither group gets a different arrangement."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is quoting Isaiah 57: *peace, peace to him who is far off and to him who is near.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The access"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For through Him **we both have our way in to the Father, by one Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *way in* is **prosagoge** — access, introduction, the word for being presented at court by somebody with standing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One access, one Spirit, one Father, and *we both*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three denials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So now you are **no longer strangers**, **no longer guests in someone else's country**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Xenoi** — foreigners, outsiders."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Paroikoi** — resident aliens. People living somewhere legally and permanently who will never be citizens of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That was the exact status of a God-fearing Gentile in relation to Israel: welcome as far as the outer court, and no further."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two affirmations"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You hold **the same citizenship as all God's holy people**, and **you belong to the household of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Citizenship, and family. The first is a legal standing; the second is not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You can be naturalised into a country. Nobody is naturalised into a household."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the wall was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Earlier in the chapter: *He… has broken down the middle wall of separation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the temple there was a literal one — a barrier in the outer court with notices in Greek and Latin warning that any foreigner passing it was responsible for his own death. Two of those inscriptions have been dug up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul, who had been accused of taking a Gentile past it and was in prison partly because of the riot that followed, writes that it has been demolished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the paragraph goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The very next verses:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Built on the foundation of the apostles and prophets, **Jesus Christ Himself being the chief cornerstone**, in whom the whole building, being fitted together, grows into a **holy temple** in the Lord, in whom you also are **being built together for a dwelling place of God** in the Spirit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Citizens, then family, then masonry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the stones in Solomon's temple were cut and finished in the quarry so that the site was silent. See the notes on 1 Kings 6:6–8 and 1 Peter 2:4–5."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they had been eleven verses earlier.** *Without Christ, aliens from the commonwealth of Israel, strangers from the covenants of promise, **having no hope and without God in the world**.* Five negatives in one sentence, and then *but now in Christ Jesus you who once were far off have been brought near by the blood of Christ.*"}]}, "Ephesians 2:4–7": {"title": "\"But God\" — and three verbs with \"together\" in them", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two of the most consequential words in the New Testament, and then three verbs that all say the same thing about location."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"But God\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence before this is a description of the dead: following the course of this world, by nature children of wrath. Then the turn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say *but they tried harder*, or *but God saw potential*. He says *But God — so rich in mercy, and loving us with a love so great*, and the reason given for everything that follows is entirely on His side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each one in Greek is a compound built with *syn* — together with:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**made us alive together** with Christ", "**raised us up together** with Him", "**seated us together** with Him"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not alongside as spectators. The same three things that happened to Christ — resurrection, ascension, enthronement — described as having happened to us with Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And all three are aorist: completed. Not a process under way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Even while we were dead\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The timing is stated twice for emphasis. The making-alive happened while the condition was death, not after any improvement. Dead men do not cooperate with their own resurrection, which is Paul's point — hence the parenthesis he cannot help inserting: *by grace you have been saved.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that in the ages still to come He could show the overflowing wealth of His grace.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The purpose is exhibition. We are the evidence God intends to display, and the thing being displayed is the size of His kindness — with the ages to come as the audience."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where \"up where heaven is\" leads.** Paul has already put Christ there in chapter 1, seated far above every ruler and authority. Now the same location is given to the church, because a head and a body are not in two places. See the note on Ephesians 1:20–23."}]}, "Ephesians 2:8–10": {"title": "\"We are what He has made\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses, and the third one is the reason the first two are not the end of the subject."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it is **by grace you have been saved, through faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *have been saved* is a perfect passive participle — **sesosmenoi**. Done, and standing done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**By grace** is the means; **through faith** is the channel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is the gift"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **this did not come from you** — it is **the gift of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek *touto* is neuter, and neither *grace* nor *faith* is neuter. Grammatically it points at the whole arrangement rather than at one word in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Readers have argued about this for centuries, and the neuter is what makes it arguable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is **not the result of anything you have done**, so **no one has a thing to boast about**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *not of works, lest anyone should boast*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul removes the boast by name. He does the same in Romans: *where is boasting then? It is excluded.* See the note on Romans 3:21–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens: *and you He made alive, who were **dead in trespasses and sins**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dead. That is the diagnosis the three verses answer, and it is why the passage has no human verb in it until the last line."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in between: *but God, who is rich in mercy, because of His great love with which He loved us… **made us alive together with Christ**.* See the note on Ephesians 2:4–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **we are what He has made**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **poiema** — a thing made, a work of craft. It is the root of *poem*, and it occurs twice in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just spent two verses saying salvation is not a work. Then he calls the reader a work — God's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What we were made for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Created in Christ Jesus **for good works**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The very thing excluded in verse 9 reappears in verse 10 on the other side of the equation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not works that produce the making. Works the making produces."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When they were arranged"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which God **prepared long beforehand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **proetoimasen** — prepared in advance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The works were ready before the worker."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is left to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **we would walk in them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **peripatesomen** — walk about, go about our lives."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not achieve them, build them, or generate them. Walk in ones already laid out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore remember that **you, once Gentiles in the flesh**… were **without Christ, aliens from the commonwealth of Israel, and strangers from the covenants of promise, having no hope and without God in the world**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But now in Christ Jesus you who once were far off have been **brought near by the blood of Christ**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three tenses of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dead. Made alive and seated. Walking in prepared works."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul keeps the order and never lets the third one become the cause of the first two."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word that resolves it.** *Poiema.* Not the result of anything you have done — and then, in the next line, a thing made, for works that were laid out in advance to walk in."}]}, "Ephesians 3:14–16": {"title": "Bowing my knees", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul stops arguing and kneels, and the prayer that follows is the high point of the letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The connection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is why.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **toutou charin** — for this cause."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He started the same sentence at the beginning of the chapter — *for this reason I, Paul, the prisoner of Christ Jesus for you Gentiles* — and then interrupted himself for thirteen verses about the mystery, and comes back to it here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole of Ephesians 3:2–13 is a parenthesis."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The posture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I **bend my knees** before the Father.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Standing was the ordinary posture for prayer among Jews. Kneeling marks unusual intensity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Solomon knelt at the dedication of the temple. Daniel knelt three times a day. Paul kneels twice in Acts, both times at partings, and in Gethsemane He *knelt down and prayed*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Father from whom **the whole family in heaven and on earth takes its name**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek plays on two words that sound alike: **patera** and **patria** — Father and family, or fatherhood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence says: every grouping that is called a family anywhere is named after Him. The word for the thing comes from Him, not the other way round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Out of the **sheer wealth of His glory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kata to ploutos tes doxes** — according to the riches of His glory."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kata**, according to — the measure is not *out of* the riches, as if taking from a store, but *in proportion to* them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same construction in Philippians: *my God shall supply all your need according to His riches in glory*. See the note on Philippians 4:19–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He would **strengthen you with power by His Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **krataiothenai dunamei** — to be made mighty with power."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Passive. He asks that it be done to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Deep in **the hidden person you are inside**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eis ton eso anthropon** — into the inner man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the circumstances, and not the visible life. Paul asks for power in the part nobody sees."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the phrase elsewhere: *even though our outward man is perishing, yet **the inward man is being renewed day by day***. See the note on 2 Corinthians 4:16–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the strength is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next clause: *that Christ may dwell in your hearts through faith* — and then the roots, the foundation, the four dimensions, and the fullness. See the note on Ephesians 3:17–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in the prayer is one sentence in Greek. The strengthening in verse 16 is what makes the comprehending in verse 18 possible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is not asking for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about circumstances. Paul is in prison as he writes and does not mention it, except to say *I ask that you do not lose heart at my tribulations for you, which is your glory*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now to Him who is able to do **exceedingly abundantly above all that we ask or think**, according to the power that works in us, to Him be glory in the church by Christ Jesus to all generations, forever and ever. Amen.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same **kata** again — according to the power already working."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is the last word of the letter's first half. Chapter 4 starts *I therefore, the prisoner of the Lord, beseech you to walk worthy*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three chapters of position, then three of conduct, with a prayer on the hinge."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where he asks for the power.** Not in the circumstances. *Into the inner man* — the part of a person nobody else can see."}]}, "Ephesians 3:17–19": {"title": "Four dimensions and a knowing that overflows", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is on his knees, and this is what he asks for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The posture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For this reason **I bow my knees** to the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Standing was the ordinary posture for prayer. Paul kneels twice in Acts, both times at partings."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That **Christ would settle down and live in your hearts** as you trust Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katoikesai** — to take up permanent residence, as opposed to **paroikeo**, to stay as a visitor."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written to people who already have Him. What is asked for is not arrival but settling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The roots and the foundation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With your **roots sunk deep in love** and your **whole weight resting on love**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two metaphors in one clause, and they do not mix: a plant and a building."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *rooted and grounded* — **errizomenoi** and **tethemeliomenoi**, both perfect participles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Love is the soil in the first and the bedrock in the second. Neither image makes love the thing produced."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The strength"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Would **have the strength to take in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exischusete** — to be fully able, to have the force for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Comprehension is treated as something requiring power. Paul is asking that they be *made strong enough to understand*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Together with **all God's holy people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sun pasin tois hagiois** — with all the saints."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not alone. The dimensions are too large for one person's measuring."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **width** and the **length** and the **depth** and the **height** of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul never says of what. The sentence has four dimensions and no noun."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The nearest one is *the love of Christ*, in the next line."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zophar uses the same four of God Himself — *higher than heaven… deeper than Sheol… longer than the earth and broader than the sea*. See the note on Job 11:5–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The impossible clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **to know the love of Christ, which is more than knowing can hold**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *to know the love of Christ which surpasses knowledge* — **gnonai** and **huperballousan tes gnoseos**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks them to know the thing he says exceeds knowing, in the same breath, without flinching."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Huperballo** — to throw beyond, to overshoot the mark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That you would be **filled, right up to the brim, with all the fullness of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eis pan to pleroma tou theou** — unto all the fullness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the measure of the filling is God's own fullness. Paul has already used the word of Christ in Colossians: *in Him dwells all the fullness of the Godhead bodily*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Knowing the love comes first, in verse 19. The filling is the *that* clause hanging off it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not ask for the fullness and then the love."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The doxology"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now to Him who is able to **do exceedingly abundantly above all that we ask or think**, according to the power that works in us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **huper ekperissou** — Paul stacking prefixes because no single word is large enough. The same *huper* as in *surpasses knowledge*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *according to the power that works in us* — the measure is not what we ask for but what is already operating."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To me, **the least of all the saints**, this grace was given, that I should preach among the Gentiles **the unsearchable riches of Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *unsearchable* is **anexichniaston** — untraceable, whose footprints cannot be followed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The prayer's shape.** Four dimensions with nothing named after them, a knowing that runs past what knowing holds, and then — the point of all of it — being filled to the brim."}]}, "Ephesians 3:20–21": {"title": "Far beyond anything we can picture", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The doxology that closes the first half of the letter, and Paul piles the words up because one will not do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer just before it: that they would be *strengthened with might through His Spirit in the inner man*, that Christ would dwell in their hearts by faith, and that they would *know the love of Christ which passes knowledge*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then this, as the amen on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two measures"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To Him who can do **far beyond anything we ask**, far beyond **anything we can even picture**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **huperekperissou** — a triple compound Paul appears to have built himself: *huper* (beyond) + *ek* (out of) + *perissos* (abundant)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Beyond, out of, abundantly. He stacks three intensifiers on one word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things exceeded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Asking** and **thinking**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second is the larger. A person can ask only for what they have thought of, so imagination is the wider boundary — and Paul says the doing is past that too."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The manner"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And can do it **lavishly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *exceedingly abundantly above*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure of the power"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*According to **the power already at work inside us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **energeo** — the root of *energy*. Present participle: currently operating."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not measure the promise by God's power in the abstract. He measures it by what is already running in the reader."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has already named that power in chapter 1: *the exceeding greatness of His power toward us who believe… **which He worked in Christ when He raised Him from the dead**.* See the note on Ephesians 1:20–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The resurrection is the unit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the glory goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To Him be the glory **in the church, in Christ Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two locations, and one of them is a collection of first-century congregations with the problems Paul's letters keep addressing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names them anyway, and puts them first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The duration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Through **every generation, on and on, forever and ever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *unto all the generations of the age of the ages* — the most extended formula for eternity in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not economising anywhere in the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 4: *I, therefore, the prisoner of the Lord, beseech you to **walk worthy of the calling** with which you were called, with all lowliness and gentleness, with longsuffering, bearing with one another in love.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The doxology ends and the practical half of the letter begins in the next breath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is writing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man in Roman custody. He calls himself *the prisoner of Christ Jesus for you Gentiles* at the start of the same chapter, and tells them *do not lose heart at my tribulations for you, which is your glory.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The measure Paul gives.** Not what God could do. What is already at work inside the reader — and he says the doing runs past what they would think to ask for."}]}, "Ephesians 4:1–3": {"title": "\"Guard the oneness the Spirit gives you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The hinge of the letter. Three chapters of what God has done, and then the first instruction — and it is about how to treat each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not being asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Working hard to **guard** the oneness the Spirit gives you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to create it. Not to achieve it. The unity already exists — it is the Spirit's, and it is given. The job is to keep it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That distinction changes the work entirely. Nobody is being asked to manufacture agreement. They are being asked not to break something that is already there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four qualities"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Never above anyone, gentle in your handling of people, patient, putting up with one another because you love one another.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**never above anyone** — the Greek word for lowliness of mind. In classical Greek it was an insult, a term for a grovelling nature. The New Testament picks it up and makes it a virtue.", "**gentle** — the word for strength under control, used of a broken horse", "**patient** — long-tempered, the same word used of God in 2 Peter 3:9", "**putting up with one another** — bearing with, enduring"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of them is a quality you only need when someone is being difficult. This is not a description of people who naturally get along."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The tie of peace\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a bond, a ligament — the thing that holds a joint together. Paul uses the same image later in the chapter for the body being knit together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peace is not the absence of friction here. It is the connective tissue."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"I, the Lord's prisoner.\"** Paul opens the practical half of his letter by naming where he is writing from. The man asking them to be patient with each other is in chains, and he mentions it once, without complaint, as his credential for asking."}]}, "Ephesians 4:20–24": {"title": "Take it off like a worn-out coat", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three infinitives, and they are all about clothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A description of the Gentile life they came out of: *the futility of their mind, having their understanding darkened… past feeling, they have given themselves over to lewdness*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *past feeling* is **apelgekotes** — having stopped feeling pain. Callused."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **that is not how you came to know Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *you have not so learned Christ* — and the object of the verb is a person, not a body of teaching."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody else in Greek literature writes *learn Christ*. Paul makes the phrase up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parenthesis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Assuming you have really **heard Him and been taught in Him**, and **the truth is in Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Heard *Him* — not about Him. Taught *in* Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first infinitive"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That you **take off, like a worn-out garment, the person you used to be**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apothesthai** — put off, lay aside, as clothes at the end of a day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb is used of the witnesses laying their coats at the feet of Saul at Stephen's stoning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the old self is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The old self **that is rotting away under desires that keep lying to you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phtheiromenon** — being corrupted, present tense: the process is ongoing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the desires are **tes apates** — of deceit. The lying is what they are made of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old self is not dangerous because it is strong. It is described as decaying, and its grip is a false promise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The middle infinitive"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That you **be made new in the spirit of your mind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ananeousthai** — a present passive. Be being renewed, continuously, by somebody else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two clothing verbs are aorist — done, at a point. The renewing between them is present and ongoing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third infinitive"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And that you **put on the new self**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Endusasthai** — the dressing verb. The same one Paul uses of the resurrection body: *this mortal must put on immortality*. See the note on 1 Corinthians 15:53–55."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the new self is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The one **created to be like God**, in the **righteousness and holiness that come of the truth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ktisthenta** — created. An aorist passive participle: it was made, and God made it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*According to God* — **kata theon**, on the pattern of God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis is behind it: *let Us make man in Our image, according to Our likeness*. Paul's version of the new man is a return to the original specification."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Colossians has the same pair: *put off the old man with his deeds, and have put on the new man who is **renewed in knowledge according to the image of Him who created him***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Off, renewed, on. And the new garment is not manufactured by the wearer — it is described in the passive, already created."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, **putting away lying**, let each one speak truth with his neighbour.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb, **apothemenoi**, laying aside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the list runs on: anger that does not carry into the next day, no more stealing but working with the hands, no corrupt word, no bitterness or wrath or clamour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole practical section of Ephesians hangs off the three infinitives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last one in the list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **be kind to one another, tenderhearted, forgiving one another, just as God in Christ forgave you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The standard is not an amount. It is a precedent."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the old coat is doing while you wear it.** Rotting — Paul says so in the present tense, and the desires holding it on are described as liars."}]}, "Ephesians 4:28–29": {"title": "\"Leaves grace behind in them\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul tells a thief what to do instead, and the answer is not simply *stop*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three stages"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let the one who has been stealing **steal no more**. Let him **work instead**, doing something good with his own hands, **so that he has something to share with anyone in need**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stop, work, give. The instruction does not end at the middle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who stole is not sent to neutrality. He is sent all the way over to the other side — from taking, past earning, to supplying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mouth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not let **one rotten word** come out of your mouth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sapros** — rotten, putrid. It is the word used of bad fish and spoiled fruit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *unkind* or *coarse*. Decayed — something that has gone off and should not be handed to anybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What to say instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Say only what **builds someone up where they need building**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *for edification of the need* — for the building up that this particular person, at this moment, actually requires."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It assumes you have noticed what the need is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that what they hear **leaves grace behind in them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *that it may give grace to the hearers* — **charis**, the ordinary New Testament word for grace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the test Paul applies to speech. Not whether it was true, or deserved, or accurate about the person. Whether grace was left behind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grace by definition is not earned. Words that only credit people with what they have coming are not doing this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse straight after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **do not grieve the Holy Spirit of God**, by whom you were sealed for the day of redemption.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul attaches that immediately to the paragraph about speech. The one thing named in Ephesians as grieving the Spirit is what comes out of a mouth toward another person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What surrounds it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole passage is a list of exchanges, each one with a positive on the other side:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["put off lying → **speak the truth** to your neighbour", "be angry → **do not let the sun go down** on it", "stop stealing → **work**, and then **share**", "no rotten word → words that **build** and **give grace**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of them stops at the prohibition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let all bitterness, wrath, anger, clamour and evil speaking be put away from you, with all malice. **And be kind to one another, tenderhearted, forgiving one another, just as God in Christ forgave you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The standard is not fairness. It is *just as* — the way you were treated."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The sealing.** *By whom you were **sealed** for the day of redemption.* Paul warns about grieving the Spirit in the same breath as saying He is the seal. The grieving is real, and it is not a threat to the sealing — a seal is not something you can be sad out of."}]}, "Ephesians 4:30–32": {"title": "\"Just as God, in Christ, forgave you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses: a warning, a clear-out, and a standard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **do not bring sorrow to the Holy Spirit of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **lupeite** — to grieve, to cause pain. It is the ordinary word for making somebody sad."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *do not anger*. Grief is what you cause someone who is attached to you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah says the same of the wilderness generation: *they rebelled and **grieved His Holy Spirit***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Spirit is called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit who **sealed you and marked you as God's own** for the day when **He sets you free for good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **esphragisthete** — sealed. A seal in that world marked ownership and guaranteed that something would arrive intact."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has already used it twice in the letter: *you were sealed with the Holy Spirit of promise, who is the guarantee of our inheritance.* See the note on Ephesians 1:7–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**For the day** — the sealing points forward. The warning sits inside a statement of security."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Put it all away from you: **bitterness, rage, anger, shouting, and words that tear a person's name down** — **every last trace of spite** along with it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six items, and the Greek runs from the inside out:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Pikria** — bitterness, the settled kind", "**Thumos** — rage, a flare", "**Orge** — anger, the slower-burning sort", "**Krauge** — clamour, shouting", "**Blasphemia** — slander, speech that damages a name", "**Kakia** — malice, ill-will, and Paul adds *with all*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first two are internal, the middle two come out of the mouth, and the last is the root the rest grow from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three positives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be **kind** to one another instead, **soft-hearted**, **forgiving** one another.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Chrestoi** — kind, useful, good to be around", "**Eusplanchnoi** — from **splanchna**, the guts. Tender-hearted, moved in the inward parts. The same root as the compassion Jesus felt looking at the crowds. See the note on Matthew 9:35–38.", "**Charizomenoi** — from **charis**, grace. To forgive as a gift, to cancel freely"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as **God, in Christ, forgave you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *so that* God will forgive. **Just as** He already did."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is aorist — a completed act. The forgiving named as the standard is in the past tense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says it the same way in Colossians: *forgiving one another… **even as Christ forgave you**, so you also must do.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb Paul chooses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Charizomai**, not the courtroom word. It is what you do with a debt you have decided to hand over rather than collect — the verb Luke uses of the two debtors, and Paul uses of God cancelling the note nailed to the cross. See the notes on Luke 7:40–43 and Colossians 2:11–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore **be imitators of God, as dear children**. And **walk in love**, as Christ also has loved us and given Himself for us.* See the note on Ephesians 5:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter break hides the connection. The forgiving is the last item before the imitating, and the imitating is the *therefore*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the standard is set.** Not at a level of effort. Paul names an act that has already happened to the reader and says the forgiving is to match it."}]}, "Ephesians 5:1–2": {"title": "The way dearly loved children copy their father", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks for something impossible and then names the two words that make it ordinary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **become imitators of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **mimetai** — the root of *mimic*. It occurs here about God and nowhere else in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks people to imitate God. It is the largest instruction in his letters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The qualifier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The way **dearly loved children copy their own father**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tekna agapeta** — beloved children."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what makes it possible. Imitation of a father by a small child is not an achievement; it is what happens."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody teaches a toddler to copy. It follows from being in the house and being loved there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"So\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **oun** — therefore."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It looks back at the end of chapter 4: *and be kind to one another, tenderhearted, **forgiving one another, just as God in Christ forgave you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The imitating is already under way in the sentence before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The walk"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **walk in love**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **peripateite** — the ordinary word for going about, the one Paul uses throughout Ephesians for how a life is conducted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The same love **with which Christ loved us and gave Himself up on our behalf**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not love in general. A specific act, named, with a date."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paredoken** — handed over, delivered up. It is the word used of Judas handing Him over, and the word Paul uses in Romans: *He who did not spare His own Son, but **delivered Him up** for us all.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*An **offering and a sacrifice** to God, **a fragrance sweet to Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words from Leviticus — **prosphora** and **thusia** — and then the phrase that runs through the whole sacrificial system."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A sweet-smelling aroma** is the Greek Old Testament's standard rendering of the Hebrew **reyach nichoach**, the restful smell that goes up from an accepted offering. It appears first over Noah's altar. See the note on Genesis 8:20–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offering was *on our behalf*, and the fragrance was *to God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The direction of the two clauses is different, and both are in the sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same word of a gift of money sent to him in prison — *a fragrance of sweetness, a sacrifice that is welcomed, a delight to God*. See the note on Philippians 4:17–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But fornication and all uncleanness or covetousness, **let it not even be named among you**, as is fitting for saints.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul goes straight from the sweet aroma to a list of what should not be around. The two verses are the standard the list is measured by."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter has already said about the love"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That you, being rooted and grounded in love, may be able to comprehend… **the width and length and depth and height** — to know the love of Christ which passes knowledge.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the doxology. See the note on Ephesians 3:20–21."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the instruction is not crushing.** *The way dearly loved children copy their own father.* Paul puts the impossible command and the reason it is natural in the same sentence."}]}, "Ephesians 5:15–17": {"title": "Buying back the time", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three short clauses, and the middle one is a market transaction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **watch carefully how you walk**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **blepete akribos** — look precisely, examine closely."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**How you walk** — Paul's standing word for how a life is conducted. He uses it six times in this letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not as fools walk **but as the wise do**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purchase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Buying back **the time you are given**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exagorazomenoi ton kairon** — redeeming the time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Exagorazo** is a market word: to buy out, to purchase entirely. It is the same verb Paul uses of Christ redeeming us from the curse of the law. See the note on Galatians 3:10–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kairos**, not **chronos**. Not clock time — the opportune moment, the window while it is open."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is of buying something off the shelf before somebody else does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **the days are evil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul gives one reason and it is not encouraging."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is writing from Roman custody, and the letter mentions his chains three times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For this reason **do not be foolish, but understand what the will of the Lord is**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **suniete** — comprehend, put together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say *find out*. The knowing is described as understanding rather than discovering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And do not be drunk with wine, in which is dissipation; but **be filled with the Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a present passive imperative — *be being filled*, continuously."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *speaking to one another in psalms and hymns and spiritual songs, singing and making melody in your heart to the Lord, **giving thanks always for all things**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The buying back of time is followed immediately by singing and thanks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul says it again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Live wisely toward those who are outside, and **buy up every hour you are given**.* See the note on Colossians 4:5–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Almost identical, and there the context is conversations with outsiders."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both letters were written from the same imprisonment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul means by the days being evil"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter says it in chapter 6: *we do not wrestle against flesh and blood, but against principalities, against powers, against the rulers of the darkness of this age*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *take up the whole armour of God, that you may be able to withstand in the evil day*. See the note on Ephesians 6:10–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about efficiency. The Greek is about seizing an opportunity, not about managing a schedule."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter has come from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore be imitators of God, as dear children. And walk in love.* See the note on Ephesians 5:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the whole practical half of the letter runs from chapter 4: *walk worthy of the calling with which you were called*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is being bought.** Not more hours. **Kairos** — the moment while it is available — and Paul's stated reason for the urgency is that the days are bad."}]}, "Ephesians 5:18–21": {"title": "Keep being filled", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One command, and four participles hanging off it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prohibition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not be drunk with wine, **which carries a person off into ruin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **asotia** — dissipation, wastefulness, an unsaved condition. Literally *unsavedness*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word is used of the prodigal's living: *he wasted his possessions with prodigal living*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **be filled with the Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **plerousthe** — present, passive, plural, imperative."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four things at once, and each one matters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Present**: keep on being filled, continuously. Not one event."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Passive**: it is done to you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Plural**: addressed to a congregation, not an individual."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Imperative**: it is a command, which means it is not optional and not automatic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wine and the Spirit are set against each other because both change how a person behaves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parallel is not accidental. It is the accusation at Pentecost: *these men are full of new wine*. See the note on Acts 2:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's point is about which influence is in control, and the difference in outcome — one wastes a life, the other fills it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first participle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Speaking to one another in **psalms and hymns and songs of the Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **lalountes heautois** — speaking to yourselves, or to each other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three kinds of song, and the first is the Psalter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Singing and **making melody in your heart** to the Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **psallontes** — originally to pluck a stringed instrument, and by this period to sing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In your heart* — the interior part is specified, alongside the audible part."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Giving thanks **always and for all things** to God the Father in the name of our Lord Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two universals: always, and for everything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fourth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Submitting to one another in the fear of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hupotassomenoi** — a military word for ranking under."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is the sentence that introduces the whole household section that follows: wives and husbands, children and parents, servants and masters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Submitting to one another* comes first, and governs all of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the four have in common"}, {"k": "p", "t": "None of them is an inward exercise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Being filled with the Spirit produces, in Paul's account: singing to each other, singing inwardly, thanking God, and deferring to people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The evidence he lists is entirely audible and social."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parallel passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Colossians has the same list with a different subject:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let **the word of Christ dwell in you richly** in all wisdom, teaching and admonishing one another in psalms and hymns and spiritual songs, singing with grace in your hearts to the Lord. And whatever you do in word or deed, do all in the name of the Lord Jesus, giving thanks to God the Father through Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Filled with the Spirit in Ephesians, the word dwelling richly in Colossians, and the outcome described identically."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes just before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*See then that you walk circumspectly, not as fools but as wise, **redeeming the time, because the days are evil**. Therefore do not be unwise, but **understand what the will of the Lord is**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the will of the Lord, in the very next sentence, turns out to be this."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the grammar carries.** Present, passive, plural, imperative — keep on being filled, by somebody else, together, and it is an order."}]}, "Ephesians 5:22–24": {"title": "\"He Himself is the Saviour of the body\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses that get quoted on their own, and almost everything about them depends on the sentence before and the twenty-five verses after."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb is not in the Greek"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wives, submit to your own husbands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no verb in verse 22 in the oldest manuscripts. It is carried over from the previous sentence — *submitting to one another in the fear of God* — which is addressed to everybody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The paragraph begins with mutual submission and then works out what it looks like in a household."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"As to the Lord\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The measure is set immediately. Not *because he has earned it*, and not *however he asks for it*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Head"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the husband is **head** of the wife, as Christ also is **head of the church**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is what fills the word. Whatever headship means here, it means what Christ's headship means, and Paul spends the next twelve verses saying what that is:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Husbands, love your wives, **just as Christ also loved the church and gave Himself for her**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pattern set for the husband is a man on a cross. Nothing in the passage authorises anything else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*— and **He Himself is the Saviour of the body**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul breaks his own comparison to say it. He has just lined up husband/wife with Christ/church, and then he stops and notes the point at which the parallel does not run: Christ is the Saviour of the body, and no husband is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The body.** Not the soul, not the spirit. Paul is talking about the church as a body and he says outright that Christ saves it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the same thing a few verses later: *no one ever hated his own flesh, but nourishes and cherishes it, **just as the Lord does the church** — for we are members of His body.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nourishes and cherishes.** The second Greek word is used of a bird warming her young under her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole passage is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is a great mystery, but I speak concerning **Christ and the church**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is Paul's own summary, and it comes at the end. The marriage was never the subject. It is the picture, and he says which way the picture points."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the husband is not given.** No authority to command, correct, or require anything. Every instruction in the passage addressed to him is about what he gives: love her, give yourself for her, nourish her, cherish her, love her as your own body. The list is one-directional."}]}, "Ephesians 5:25–27": {"title": "\"Washing her clean with water through the word\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The instruction to husbands is one line. The rest is about what Christ did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Husbands, **love your wives, just as Christ also loved the church and gave Himself up for her**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The standard is not reciprocity, deserving, or a fair division of effort. It is a man on a cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything addressed to the husband in this passage is about what he gives. Nothing in it authorises him to require anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three purpose clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each begins *so that*:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*so that He might **make her holy**, washing her clean*", "*so that He might **present her to Himself** a church in all her glory*", "and the state she is in when presented"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of them is His action, and the church is the object throughout."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The washing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Washing her clean **with water through the word**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *word* here is **rhema** — the spoken word, the thing said, rather than **logos**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two pictures are usually heard in it: the bath a bride took before a wedding in that world, and baptism."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the cleansing agent is named. Not effort, not time, not penance — water, and the word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus says the same to the disciples: *you are already clean **because of the word** which I have spoken to you.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she is presented as"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In all her glory, **with no stain on her and no crease and nothing else of the kind**, but **holy and flawless**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *not having spot or wrinkle or any such thing*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Spot** is a mark from outside. **Wrinkle** is what age does from inside. Neither is present."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Flawless** is **amomos** — the sacrificial word, without blemish, the term for an animal fit for the altar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does the presenting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He might present her **to Himself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He washes her, and He is also the one she is presented to. There is no third party in the transaction and no inspector."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the passage is really about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says so himself a few verses later: *this is a great mystery, **but I speak concerning Christ and the church**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The marriage is the illustration. He names the subject at the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Husbands, **live with her thoughtfully, with real understanding**… so that nothing gets in the way of your prayers.* See the note on 1 Peter 3:7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two apostles, two letters, and neither gives a husband anything to demand."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the paragraph begins.** *Submitting to one another in the fear of God* — a mutual instruction, addressed to everybody, and the verse the whole household section hangs from. See the note on Ephesians 5:22–24."}]}, "Ephesians 5:28–30": {"title": "\"Of His flesh and of His bones\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is writing about marriage and says something about the church that goes further than the illustration needed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No one ever hated his own flesh; **he feeds it and tends it warmly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *tends it warmly* is **thalpo** — to keep warm, and it is used of a bird brooding over her young in the nest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's case to husbands is not built on duty. It is built on what a man already does for his own body without being asked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then the turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*— exactly as **the Lord does the church**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two verbs, applied to Him. He feeds it and He broods over it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause that goes furthest"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **we are parts of His body, of His flesh and of His bones**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul could have stopped at *His body*. He does not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is Adam's, word for word, when he first saw Eve: *this is now **bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Adam said it about a woman taken out of his side while he slept. Paul says it about the church, and two verses later he quotes the rest of that sentence — *for this reason a man shall leave his father and mother and be joined to his wife, and the two shall become one flesh* — and adds: *this is a great mystery, but I speak concerning Christ and the church.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the union is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an arrangement, an alliance, or a membership. Paul uses the language of one body."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who is joined to the Lord is one spirit with Him*, he says in the other letter. And in this one: *God raised us up together, and made us sit together in the heavenly places in Christ Jesus* — the tense is past."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that means about the body"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The head is in one place and the body is not sick in a way the head is not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever is true of Him is what the passage says is true of the parts. *As He is, so are we in this world* — 1 John says it about now, not later. See the note on 1 John 4:17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the instruction lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Husbands ought to love their own wives as their own bodies.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything addressed to the husband in this passage is about what he gives: love her, give yourself for her, feed her, keep her warm."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was taken from His side.** Adam's bride came out of an opened side while he slept. On the cross, a soldier *pierced His side with a spear, and immediately blood and water came out.* John records it and then insists on it: *he who has seen it has borne witness, and his testimony is true.*"}]}, "Ephesians 6:1–4": {"title": "The first commandment with a promise attached", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes the fifth commandment and adds a note about it — *which is the first commandment with a promise attached to it* — and the aside is worth as much as the quotation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is pointing out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of the ten, this is the first that comes with an outcome named: *so that it may go well with you and you may live a long life on the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not inventing the promise; he is drawing attention to the fact that it is there. In a list mostly of prohibitions, one command arrives with a stated benefit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How to read the promise honestly"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a promise in the shape a promise takes in the Old Testament — a general truth about how a society and a life tend to run, not a personal guarantee. The same Bible records godly people who honoured their parents and died young."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read as it was written, it is saying that a people who hold the generations together last, and a people who do not, do not. Everyone who has watched a family either work or come apart knows the observation is sound."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line to fathers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not stir your children up to anger.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul turns and addresses the party with all the power, which in a first-century household was a genuinely radical thing to do. The word describes provoking someone into exasperation — the slow kind, built up over time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the alternative he gives is not passivity: *raise them in the training and correction of the Lord*. Not less involvement. Involvement of a different sort."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"obey\" and \"honour.\"** Paul uses both, and they are not the same. Obeying belongs to children in a household. Honouring is what the commandment asks, and it has no expiry date — it is what an adult still owes an elderly parent long after obedience has stopped being the relevant word."}]}, "Ephesians 6:10–13": {"title": "\"Having done everything, to stand\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's last section, and the verb he keeps returning to is not what people expect from a passage about warfare."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word he repeats"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Stand.** Four times in four verses: stand against the schemes, stand your ground, and — having done everything — *stand*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not advance, not conquer, not take. The posture being described is holding a position that has already been won, which is consistent with everything in the first three chapters: seated with Christ, above every ruler and authority."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You defend ground you already hold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Be strong in the Lord\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is passive — *be strengthened*. It is not an exhortation to summon resolve. And the strength named is *the strength of His might*, the same phrase Paul used in chapter 1 of the power that raised Christ."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the fight is with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not against flesh and blood.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul rules out people first. Whatever the Ephesians were facing — and the city was famous for its magic and its temple of Artemis — he tells them the opposition is not the human beings in front of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four terms follow: ruling powers, authorities, world-rulers of this present darkness, spiritual armies of wickedness in the heavenly places. He does not define them, and no one since has been able to sort them into a tidy hierarchy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The whole armour of God\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice. It is **God's own** armour — Isaiah describes the LORD putting on righteousness as a breastplate and a helmet of salvation, and Paul is handing the same equipment to the church."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing on the list is manufactured by the wearer."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is in the list.** Truth, righteousness, the gospel of peace, faith, salvation, the word of God — and then prayer. Every item is something already given in the first half of the letter. Chapter 6 is chapters 1 to 3, put on."}]}, "Ephesians 6:14–17": {"title": "The whole armour", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Six pieces, and Paul names what each one is made of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **stand**, then.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fourth time in five verses. *Stand against, withstand, having done all, stand, stand therefore.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in this passage is about advancing. The whole armour is issued for holding ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The belt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Truth fastened **around your waist like a belt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The soldier's belt held everything else — the scabbard hung from it, and the tunic was tucked into it to free the legs. Nothing else works if it is loose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The breastplate"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Righteousness worn **over your chest**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah has it first: *He put on righteousness as a breastplate.* It is God's armour before it is ours."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And whose righteousness it is has been settled earlier in the letter: *the gift of righteousness*, *accepted in the Beloved*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The feet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your feet fitted with **the readiness that the good news of peace gives**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roman military sandals were studded with hobnails, for grip."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hetoimasia** — preparedness, a firm footing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The odd part is what it is made of: *the gospel of peace*. Peace, on the feet, in a passage about a battle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shield"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Above all of it, take up the shield, which is **faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Thureos** — from *thura*, a door. The full-length Roman shield, four feet by two and a half, covering the whole man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the small round buckler. This one you get behind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arrows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With it you will be able to **put out all the burning arrows** of the evil one.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Arrows wrapped in pitch-soaked tow and lit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Roman shield was wood covered in leather, and the leather was soaked in water before a battle so that a flaming arrow sticking in it went out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the shield extinguishes them. It does not deflect them; it takes them and quenches them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The helmet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The helmet, which is **salvation**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah again: *a helmet of salvation on His head.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's other version: *the hope of salvation as a helmet.* See the note on 1 Thessalonians 5:4–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sword"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the sword the Spirit gives, which is the word of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **machaira** — the short stabbing sword, not the long broadsword. It was close-quarters equipment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *word* here is **rhema** — the spoken word, the specific saying, rather than **logos**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what Jesus used in the wilderness. Three times: *it is written.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not covered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is issued for the back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Put on the whole armour of God, that you may be able to stand against the wiles of the devil.* And the passage before all of it: *be strong in the Lord and **in the power of His might**.* See the note on Ephesians 6:10–13."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What follows the armour.** *Praying always with all prayer and supplication in the Spirit… and for me, that utterance may be given to me.* Paul finishes the armour passage by asking a church to pray for his own courage — *that I may speak boldly, as I ought to speak.* He was in chains when he wrote it."}]}, "Ephesians 6:18–20": {"title": "An ambassador in chains", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The armour passage ends, and there is no seventh piece — there is a posture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The belt, the breastplate, the shoes, the shield, the helmet and the sword. See the note on Ephesians 6:14–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six items, and then this, joined to them by a participle rather than a new sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Prayer is not listed as another item of equipment. It is how the whole thing is worn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four *alls*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek piles them up:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Praying at **every moment**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **every kind of prayer and request**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Staying wide awake to this very thing, with **all perseverance**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And pleading for **all God's people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four occurrences of **pas** in one sentence. Every time, every kind, all perseverance, all the saints."}, {"k": "h", "t": "In the Spirit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the Spirit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **en pneumati**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jude uses the same phrase: *praying in the Holy Spirit*. See the note on Jude 1:20–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The watching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Staying **wide awake** to this very thing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **agrupnountes** — sleepless, keeping watch. From roots meaning *to chase sleep*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for a sentry's night duty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he asks them to pray for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **pray for me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul, who has just described a fully armed believer, asks a congregation to pray for him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That **words may be given me**, that I may **open my mouth boldly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not release. Not comfort. Not safety."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hina moi dothe logos** — that speech may be given to me."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The subject"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To make known **the mystery of the good news**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mystery he has already defined in chapter 3: *that the Gentiles should be fellow heirs, of the same body, and partakers of His promise in Christ through the gospel*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For which I am **an ambassador in chains**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **presbeuo en halusei** — I serve as an ambassador in a chain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An ambassador is the one person a state guarantees safe passage to. Paul is one, wearing a chain, and says both in the same breath without complaint."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the same word in 2 Corinthians: *we are ambassadors for Christ, as though God were pleading through us*. See the note on 2 Corinthians 5:20–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repetition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That in it **I may speak boldly, as I ought to speak**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks for boldness twice in two verses. It is the only thing he requests."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parresiasomai** — to speak freely, openly. The word for a citizen's right to say what he thinks in the assembly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written by a man who had nothing of the kind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had prayed for them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That He would grant you, according to the riches of His glory, to be strengthened with might through His Spirit in the inner man.* See the note on Ephesians 3:14–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter has one prayer going each way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the letter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peace to the brethren, and love with faith, from God the Father and the Lord Jesus Christ. **Grace be with all those who love our Lord Jesus Christ in sincerity**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he asks a congregation to pray for.** Not release from the chain. Words, and the nerve to use them — twice, in two verses."}]}, "Esther 1:1–4": {"title": "The king who ruled the world", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The book opens on the most powerful man on earth, and God is not mentioned. He will not be mentioned anywhere in the ten chapters that follow."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is missing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no name of God in Esther. No prayer is recorded, no temple, no sacrifice, no prophet, no miracle, no angel, no law quoted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That absence is the book. It is the story of a people God had told, through Hosea, *you are not My people* — and it is set in exactly that season, when His face was hidden and His hand was not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not absent from the story. He is not named in it. There is a difference, and the whole of Esther is written to show it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The empire"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From India all the way to Cush, a hundred and twenty-seven provinces.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahasuerus is Xerxes. The reach described is the largest empire the world had seen to that point, and the writer measures it in provinces rather than miles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Jews are somewhere inside it, scattered, with no land, no king and no temple to go to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "A hundred and eighty days"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For a long while — a full **hundred and eighty days** — he laid out before them the wealth of his glorious kingdom.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six months of display. The verb is to show, to make visible; the whole of it is about being seen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then a further seven-day feast for everybody in Susa, which the next verses describe down to the marble pillars and the silver couches."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the world looks like at the start"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every visible thing belongs to Ahasuerus. The wealth is his, the law is his, the army is his, and by the end of chapter 3 the life of every Jew in the empire is his to sign away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is where the book starts, and it starts there deliberately. The reader is meant to look at all of it and ask the question the whole book answers."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The name hidden in the text.** Four times in the Hebrew of Esther the letters of the name **YHWH** stand at the start of four consecutive words, spelled out forwards twice and backwards twice. And once, in Esther 7:5, the letters of **I AM** — *ehyeh* — are formed the same way, in the sentence where the king demands to know who has dared to do this thing. The name is in the book. It is written the way He was working: present, and not announced."}]}, "Esther 1:16–20": {"title": "A small refusal, a great fear", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most powerful court in the world panics over one woman not coming to dinner, and legislates."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The escalation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Queen Vashti has not only wronged the king, but **every official and every people in all the provinces**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Memucan takes a private embarrassment and makes it an imperial crisis in one sentence. A hundred and twenty-seven provinces are now involved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were afraid of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Word of what the queen has done will get out to all the women, and they will look down on their own husbands.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fear is of a story travelling. Not of Vashti — of what people will say about her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There will be no end of contempt and quarreling.\"* The men who ruled from India to Cush are worried about arguments at home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The remedy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let him send out a royal decree, written into the laws of Persia and Media **so it cannot be revoked**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The irrevocability of Persian law is introduced here, casually, in chapter 1. It will matter enormously in chapter 8, when a death sentence on a whole people cannot be cancelled either."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer plants it early, in a trivial context, so that the reader already knows the rule when everything depends on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence that makes room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let the king give her royal place **to another woman better than she**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Memucan does not know what he has just said. He is drafting a decree about household order, and the clause he adds at the end opens the throne of Persia."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four chapters later a Jewish girl sits in that place, and the empire's entire Jewish population is alive because of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody prays. Nobody consults a prophet. Nobody mentions God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A drunk king, a proud queen, and a nervous adviser between them arrange the one thing that will be needed. See the note on Esther 1:9–12."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line of the decree.** *Then every wife will honor her husband, from the greatest to the least.* An empire-wide law was passed to make women respectful, and the historical result of it, so far as the book records, is that the Jews were not exterminated."}]}, "Esther 1:9–12": {"title": "Queen Vashti's own feast", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A queen says no, and a vacancy opens that will save a nation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seventh day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the seventh day, when the king's heart was merry with wine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer tells us the state he was in before he tells us what he ordered. Six days of drinking are behind the command."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he wanted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To bring Queen Vashti before him wearing her royal crown. He wanted to **display her beauty** to the people and the officials.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb as the six months of showing off the kingdom's wealth. She is the last item in the display — the treasury, the army, the palace, and then his wife."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Seven names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mehuman, Biztha, Harbona, Bigtha, Abagtha, Zethar, and Carkas.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer lists all seven eunuchs, and no reader has ever needed to know their names. He does the same with the seven princes a few verses later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book keeps its eye on detail like this, on court procedure and paperwork and who was standing where. That is the level at which the whole rescue is going to happen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Would not come\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in the Hebrew, and the empire tilts on them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the text explains her. No reason is given, no speech recorded. She simply does not come."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the refusal produced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The king was furious, and the anger smoldered in him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Out of that anger comes the decree, out of the decree comes the vacancy, and out of the vacancy comes a Jewish orphan on the throne of Persia four years before Haman writes his letters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in chapter 1 is trying to save anybody. A queen refuses a drunk man's summons and a place is made ready that will be needed later."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the book is doing here.** God is not named and nothing miraculous happens. A woman's pride, a king's temper and a courtier's advice do all the work — and every one of them is moving a piece into position. He is ruling through it while none of them know His name."}]}, "Esther 2:15–16": {"title": "The girl who asked for nothing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence in this passage is doing quiet work, and it is easy to read past."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What every other woman did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has already explained the arrangement: each young woman went in to the king once, and could take with her whatever she asked for from the harem. One night, one chance, and an open request."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You can imagine what most of them took."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Esther took"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She asked for nothing except what Hegai, the king's eunuch and keeper of the women, suggested.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hegai had been running that household for years. He knew the king. Esther, with one opportunity and permission to ask for anything, asked the man who actually knew what he was talking about."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not passivity — she is a woman in an impossible position playing the one card available with unusual coolness. It is judgement."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**A book that never mentions God.** Esther is the only book in the Bible in which God's name does not appear, and moments like this are why readers have always felt Him in it anyway: nothing miraculous happens, and yet the details keep landing right. The story is told entirely at ground level — a favour here, a sleepless night there — and the shape only becomes visible from the far end of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word the chapter keeps using"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Esther **obtained favour** in the sight of all who looked at her.* The Hebrew is **chen** — grace, unearned favour, the same word used of Noah finding grace in the eyes of the LORD."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter uses it about her again and again. She wins the position without qualifications: no family, no wealth, no nation to speak for her, an orphan and a captive. What she has is favour, and the writer says so every time she advances."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is what carries her in chapter 2 and it is what saves her life in chapter 5, when a golden sceptre is held out to a woman who came in unsummoned. See the note on Esther 5:1–2."}]}, "Esther 2:5–7": {"title": "A Jew in the fortress, a girl in his care", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two people are introduced, and everything the book needs is in their description."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The genealogy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mordecai son of Jair, son of Shimei, son of **Kish**, a **Benjamite**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Kish, a Benjamite, is the name of Saul's father. The writer is pointing at something."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul was the Benjamite king commanded to destroy Amalek, and he did not. Haman is introduced two chapters later as *the Agagite* — Agag being the Amalekite king Saul spared."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A descendant of Kish and a descendant of Agag, in the same court, four hundred years later. The unfinished business of 1 Samuel 15 is the plot of this book. See the note on Deuteronomy 25:17–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Carried away"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His family had been carried away from Jerusalem among the captives.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mordecai is in Susa because his people lost their land. He is not there by ambition. He is there because of the exile, in the middle of the season Hosea called *not My people*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mordecai was raising **Hadassah, that is, Esther**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hadassah** is Hebrew — the myrtle. **Esther** is the Persian name she is known by in the palace, and it is the name on the book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The myrtle is a fragrant shrub that gives off its scent most strongly when it is crushed, and Isaiah 55 puts it in a promise: *instead of the brier shall come up the myrtle tree.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her Hebrew name is hidden under a foreign one, in a book where God's name is hidden under an ordinary story."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"She had neither father nor mother\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice in three lines. The writer wants it registered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The girl who will stand between a nation and its extermination is an orphan in a foreign city, with no standing, no family, and no claim on anybody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mordecai had taken her as his own daughter.* The Hebrew is literally *took her for a daughter*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is already in place.** Before Haman is promoted, before the decree is written, before anyone knows there is a danger — a Benjamite of Kish's line is in the fortress and the girl in his house is beautiful. The pieces are set out before the game starts."}]}, "Esther 3:1–4": {"title": "Haman lifted high", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The villain is introduced with a family name, and the family name is the whole argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The Agagite\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Haman son of Hammedatha **the Agagite**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Agag was the king of Amalek whom Saul was ordered to destroy and chose to spare — 1 Samuel 15, the disobedience that cost Saul the kingdom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amalek was the nation that attacked Israel from behind in the wilderness, picking off the tired and the stragglers, and God said He would have war with them from generation to generation. See the note on Deuteronomy 25:17–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four centuries after Saul let Agag live, Agag's descendant is second in the Persian empire and signing a death warrant for every Jew alive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is not put to death does not stay small. It comes back with an office and a signet ring."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promotion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*King Ahasuerus promoted Haman… raising him higher than all the other officials.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No reason is given. Haman has done nothing in the book. Mordecai, by contrast, has just saved the king's life in the previous four verses — and got nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer puts those two facts side by side without comment. It looks like the wrong man rising, and for three chapters it is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Mordecai would not bow\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole court bows. It has been commanded. Mordecai stands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives one reason, and it is the only reason in the book: *he had told them he was a Jew.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not argue, campaign, or explain further. He is asked *day after day* and *he would not listen.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the servants were doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they told Haman, **to see whether Mordecai's reason would stand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They report him in order to watch what happens. That is the sentence that lights the fuse — a small piece of court malice, and an empire-wide massacre is drafted because of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**A Benjamite and an Amalekite.** Mordecai has already been introduced as *son of Kish, a Benjamite* — Saul's line. The old war Saul left unfinished is standing in a Persian gateway, and this time the Benjamite does not bow. See the note on Esther 2:5–7."}]}, "Esther 3:12–15": {"title": "Letters sealed with the king's ring", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The genocide is not a mob. It is paperwork, properly filed, in every language of the empire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The machinery"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The king's scribes were called… to each province in **its own script** and to each people in **its own language**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Translated for every people so that nobody could plead they had not understood. The empire's whole administrative apparatus is turned on a single ethnic group."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sealed with the king's ring.* Under Persian law that made it irrevocable — the rule the reader was taught back in chapter 1, when it was only about a queen not coming to dinner."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Four verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ordering that all Jews be **destroyed, killed, and wiped out**, young and old, little children and women, all in a single day — and their goods taken as plunder.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The piling-up is deliberate. And the phrase *young and old, little children and women* is Amalek's signature: the people at the back, the ones who cannot run."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plunder clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their goods taken as plunder.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haman is buying the empire's cooperation. Anyone who kills a Jew keeps the house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hold this clause in mind — in chapter 9, when the Jews defend themselves and win, the writer says three separate times that *they did not lay a hand on the plunder.* They were entitled to it under the counter-decree and they left it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Eleven months"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lot had fallen on the thirteenth of Adar. The letters go out in the first month, Nisan."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every Jew in the world now has a date on which they are legally to be killed, and eleven months to look at it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the king and Haman sat down to drink**, while the city of Susa reeled in confusion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two men with a glass, and a city outside the window not knowing what has hit it. The writer sets the two clauses in one line and lets them stand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the low point of the book. There is no God named, no prophet, no army, no appeal — a sealed law that cannot be revoked, and the men who wrote it are drinking."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the reader knows and Susa does not.** Nisan is the month of Passover. The decree ordering the destruction of the Jews was written on the day before the feast that commemorates their deliverance."}]}, "Esther 3:5–6": {"title": "A rage that would not stop at one man", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and a private insult becomes a policy of extermination."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Too small a thing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It seemed **too small a thing** to lay hands on Mordecai alone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Killing the man who offended him is beneath the scale of Haman's anger. He wants proportion, and the proportion he settles on is a nation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is contemptuous — it despised in his eyes. Murder was not enough."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What made it possible"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They had told him **who Mordecai's people were**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The servants' report in the verses before did this. Mordecai's answer had been *I am a Jew*, and that single fact converts one man's stubbornness into a target the size of an empire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Amalek doing what Amalek does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haman is *the Agagite*, and this is Amalek's method exactly as Deuteronomy describes it: not a declared war on soldiers, but an attack on a whole people including *young and old, little children and women* — the phrase in the decree nine verses later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amalek attacked the stragglers at the back. Haman writes them into the law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scale"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To wipe out **all the Jews, every one of them**, throughout the whole kingdom of Ahasuerus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundred and twenty-seven provinces. There was nowhere in the known world a Jew could go that this decree would not reach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel had no land, no king, no army and no temple. There was no earthly protection anywhere in the picture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God is still not mentioned. Nobody prays in this chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the reader who knows the end of the book knows what is already true while Haman is planning: a Jewish girl has been queen of Persia for four years, and a king's insomnia is coming."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The date they cast lots for.** Haman throws the *pur* — the lot — to pick the day, and it falls on the thirteenth of Adar, eleven months away. The one thing he leaves to chance gives his enemies a year to live. Proverbs says the lot is cast into the lap, but its every decision is from the LORD."}]}, "Esther 4:12–14": {"title": "For such a time as this", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Mordecai's answer is the theological centre of a book that never mentions God, and he manages to say everything without using the name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Relief and deliverance will arise from another place\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you stay silent now, **relief and deliverance for the Jews will arise from another place**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is completely certain the rescue is coming. That is not in doubt anywhere in his sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say *perhaps we will be saved*. He says the deliverance will arrive, with or without her — and he does not name where from. *Another place* is as close as this book comes to saying the name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question on the table is never whether the Jews will be delivered. It is whether Esther will be in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"You and your father's house will perish\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one who stands to lose is the one who stays out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had just sent word that going in unsummoned carries a death sentence unless the golden sceptre is held out. Mordecai answers that the danger she is weighing is not the real danger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Who knows whether you have come to the palace for such a time as this?\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every step that put her in that room was somebody else's doing. A queen's refusal, a drunk king's temper, an adviser's decree, a eunuch's advice, a beauty contest she did not enter herself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mordecai looks at that chain and reads it correctly: she did not arrive there by accident, and the position was given for a reason that is only now becoming visible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who knows* — he does not claim a revelation. There is no prophet in this book. He is reading providence from the shape of it, which is what the whole book asks its reader to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her answer is three verses later: gather the Jews, fast three days, *and if I perish, I perish.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She goes in. And the position that had been prepared for her turns out to be the place the whole nation is saved from."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Mordecai is not saying.** He is not telling her the outcome depends on her. He has already ruled that out — the deliverance is coming from another place. He is telling her there is a part with her name on it, and that she can miss it. See the note on Esther 5:1–2."}]}, "Esther 5:1–2": {"title": "The golden sceptre", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and they carry the weight of the whole book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the **third day** Esther put on her royal robes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days of fasting are behind this sentence. She had asked for them, and she comes out of them on the third day and walks in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she wore"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Her **royal robes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not sackcloth. She had been in mourning clothes at the end of chapter 4 — the Hebrew here is literally *she put on royalty*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She approaches him as a queen, in what belongs to her position, not as a petitioner in rags."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The law she is breaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had spelled it out herself two chapters earlier: anyone who goes into the inner court unsummoned is put to death, *unless the king holds out the golden sceptre.* She had not been called for thirty days."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has no right to be standing there. That is the fact the scene begins with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the king saw Queen Esther standing in the court, **she won his favour**, and he **held out to her** the golden sceptre.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *she obtained grace in his eyes* — **chen**, the same word the book has used over her from the beginning, unearned favour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She did not earn the sceptre by three days of fasting and she did not argue her way to it. It was extended."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who moves first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sceptre goes out before she says anything. She has not stated her business, made her case, or explained why she came."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *so Esther came near and **touched the top of the sceptre**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She approaches because it was held out, and what she touches is the thing that spared her. The order in those two verses is the order the whole book runs on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was on the other side of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What is your request? It shall be given you, **up to half the kingdom**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the next sentence. The law said death; the sceptre said half an empire."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the throne came from.** She is standing in a room she only occupies because Vashti refused to come when she was summoned. One woman would not come when she was called; another came when she was not called, and lived. See the note on Esther 1:9–12."}]}, "Esther 5:9–13": {"title": "One man at the gate spoils everything", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Haman has everything, and the writer lets him list it out loud so the last sentence can land."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inventory"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He boasted to them of his **glorious riches**, his **many sons**, and **every way the king had promoted him**… \"And besides all that, **Queen Esther invited no one but me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wealth, sons, rank, and a private banquet with the king and queen. In that world there was nothing else to want."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He counts it in front of an audience he has assembled for the purpose — his wife and his friends, called in to hear it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence that undoes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Yet all of this means nothing to me** as long as I see Mordecai the Jew sitting at the king's gate.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is stronger than the English can quite carry: all of it is worth nothing to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One man, sitting down, not moving. That is the entire offence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mordecai did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mordecai **did not rise or so much as tremble** before him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not insult Haman. He did not speak. He sat still."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer adds *or so much as tremble* — Haman is not even given the compliment of fear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Still, he held himself in\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haman restrains himself in public and goes home to say it. The rage is not managed; it is stored."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the end of the same conversation his wife and friends have proposed the gallows, and *the thing pleased Haman, and he had the gallows made.* Fifty cubits — seventy-five feet, higher than anything needed for the job, built to be seen across the city."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is standing when he says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just come from Esther's first banquet, and he has been invited back tomorrow. He thinks the invitation is an honour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a trap that has already closed. The reader can see it; Haman is at home counting his sons."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the boast cost him.** He builds a gallows that night for a man he will be leading through the streets on a horse by tomorrow afternoon, and he will hang on it himself before the second banquet is over. See the notes on Esther 6:10–11 and Esther 7:7–10."}]}, "Esther 6:1–3": {"title": "The night the king could not sleep", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The turning point of the book is a man who cannot get to sleep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hinge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That night **sleep would not come to the king**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is it. No angel, no dream, no prophet, no plague. The most powerful man in the world lies awake, and from that one ordinary thing everything reverses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is impersonal — sleep fled from the king. The writer does not say who took it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Count what has to line up on this one night:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["Haman is at that moment building a gallows for Mordecai", "the king cannot sleep", "of everything he could call for, he calls for the **chronicles**", "of the whole reign's records, they read the page about **Mordecai**", "the reader is told *nothing has been done for him at all*", "and Haman walks into the court at daybreak to ask for Mordecai's death"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of those is unremarkable on its own. Any one of them going differently and the book ends another way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the record was there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mordecai had uncovered the plot of Bigthana and Teresh back in chapter 2, four years earlier, and it was written down and forgotten."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That failure to reward him looked at the time like an injustice. It was a debt being held until the night it would do the most work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Nothing has been done for him at all\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The servants' answer, and it is the sentence the king cannot leave alone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not send for a treasurer. His next question is *who is in the court?* — and Haman has just arrived, early, to ask permission to hang the man the king is about to honour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is showing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no miracle in Esther 6. There is insomnia, an administrative record, and bad timing for one man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is how the whole book works: nothing supernatural on the surface, and every detail landing exactly where it has to. He is not named in the chapter. He is all over it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Haman was doing while the king lay awake.** Standing in his own courtyard watching a seventy-five-foot gallows go up. Two men in one city that night — one building, one unable to sleep — and only one of them thought he was in control of the morning."}]}, "Esther 6:10–11": {"title": "The parade he never imagined", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Haman describes, in loving detail, the honours he wants for himself, and then has to carry every one of them out for the man he came to hang."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had just designed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king had asked what should be done for the man he delights to honour, and Haman — *whom would the king delight to honour more than me?* — designed it himself: the king's own robe, the king's own horse, the crown on its head, and a noble to lead it through the city square shouting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was writing his own coronation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"For Mordecai the Jew\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do all of this for **Mordecai the Jew**, who sits at the king's gate.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king names him with the two things Haman hates most: his name, and his nation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who sits at the king's gate* — the very posture that started the whole rage. See the note on Esther 5:9–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Leave out nothing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Leave out nothing** of everything you have described.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every item. The king will not let him trim it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haman had built the list for maximum glory, and now the length of it is the length of his own humiliation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had come to ask"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is standing in that court at daybreak for one reason: to get permission to hang Mordecai on a gallows already built and waiting outside his house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never gets the sentence out. The king speaks first, and by the time Haman is allowed to answer he is holding a robe."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Haman **took** the robe and the horse, and he **dressed** Mordecai, and he **led** him through the city square… **calling out** before him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verbs, all his, all performed on the man he meant to kill. He dressed him with his own hands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is what is done for the man the king delights to honour!\"* — shouted, by Haman, in the street, all afternoon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not happening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No one has intervened. No prophet has spoken, no plague has fallen, nobody has prayed on the page."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A sleepless night, an old record, and a man's own vanity handing him the exact opposite of what he came for."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word for it.** *Whoever digs a pit will fall into it, and a stone will come back on the one who starts it rolling.* Haman's own gallows, his own robe, his own parade. Every instrument in this book turns around in the hand that made it."}]}, "Esther 6:12–14": {"title": "Home in grief", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two men go home from the same parade, and the writer records both journeys in one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Mordecai goes back to work"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Afterward **Mordecai returned to the king's gate**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Robe off, horse gone, back to his seat. Nothing is recorded of what he said or felt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had not asked for the parade, had not campaigned for it, and does not appear to have adjusted his life because of it. He goes back to the gate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Haman goes home"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Haman hurried home, **his head covered**, mourning and shaken.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Covering the head was the posture of grief and shame. In 2 Samuel it is what David does going up the Mount of Olives, and what the mourners do for the dead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was leading a parade an hour ago."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The advisers change their tune"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same wife and the same friends who suggested the gallows the previous evening now say this:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If Mordecai, before whom you have **begun to fall**, is **one of the Jewish people**, you will not overcome him. You will surely fall before him.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They give a reason, and the reason is his nation. Pagan advisers in a Persian house state the principle the book never states outright — that there is something about this people you cannot win against."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They cannot name what they are describing. They can see it working."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Begun to fall\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew has the verb twice: you have *begun to fall* before him, you will *surely fall*. Once it starts, they say, it does not stop."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing has happened yet except a robe and a horse. There has been no trial, no accusation, and Haman is still the second man in the empire."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are reading the direction of things off one afternoon, and they are right."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were still speaking when the king's messengers arrived, **hurrying Haman away** to the banquet Esther had prepared.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not get to finish the conversation. He is taken, mid-sentence, to the room where he will be exposed and sentenced within the hour."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The pace of the chapter.** Everything in Esther 6 happens in a single day, and the verbs are all urgent — *hurry*, *hastened*, *hurrying away*. Eleven months of waiting under a death decree, and then one day in which the whole thing turns over."}]}, "Esther 7:3–4": {"title": "The night she finally asked", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two banquets, an invitation held back for a day, and then the request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she had been doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king had asked her twice what she wanted, and twice offered up to half his kingdom. Both times she asked only that he and Haman come to dinner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In between the two banquets: a sleepless night for the king, the royal records read aloud, Mordecai's unrewarded loyalty discovered, and Haman paraded through the streets leading the horse of the man he meant to hang."}, {"k": "p", "t": "None of that happens if she asks on the first night."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How she asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If I have found favour in your eyes, my king, and if it pleases the king, let my own life be given me as my petition, and my people as my request.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She asks for her own life first. Until this moment the king does not know she is Jewish — the decree he sealed includes her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She puts herself inside the sentence before she mentions anybody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line about slavery"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Had we only been sold as slaves, men and women, I would have kept silent, for such a trouble would not be worth disturbing the king.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A deliberate piece of restraint. She tells a Persian king that she would not have troubled him over slavery — which makes clear that what she is troubling him about is worse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To be destroyed, to be killed, to be wiped away.* Three, and they are the exact three from Haman's decree, quoted back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is not describing the danger in her own words. She is reading his own paperwork to him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The book that never names God.** Nothing miraculous happens in Esther. A queen waits a day, a king cannot sleep, a record is read at the right hour. Only from the far end does the shape become visible — which is the whole method of the book. See the note on Esther 2:15–16."}]}, "Esther 7:5–6": {"title": "Who would dare", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The king's question, and the answer that ends Haman."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Who is he? Where is the man** who has dared in his heart to do such a thing?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king does not know. He signed the decree himself — sealed it with his own ring — and never asked which people it was for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is furious at a crime he authorised."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name written into the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the Hebrew of this verse, the first letters of four consecutive words spell out **EHYEH** — *I AM*. The name God gave Moses at the bush: *I AM WHO I AM.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It stands in the king's demand to know who has dared to do such a thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the fourth and last of the hidden names in Esther. The letters of **YHWH** are formed the same way four times elsewhere in the book — twice reading forwards and twice reading backwards."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name is never printed in this book. It is built into the text, in the places where the story turns, and it is written in the one sentence where the question being asked out loud is *who is behind this?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The man, the enemy, this wicked Haman.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three phrases, and no verb between them. In Hebrew it lands like three blows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She names him in front of the king, at the king's table, with Haman sitting at it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she had waited for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two banquets, two days. She had the sceptre and the offer of half the kingdom on the first day and asked only for another dinner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The delay put Haman in the room on the night after his humiliation, at the exact hour the king's mind had already turned against him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Haman stood frozen with terror\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that he was terrified before the king *and the queen*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had not known she was a Jew. Nobody in the palace had — Mordecai had told her not to say. The decree he wrote and sealed included the woman sitting opposite him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the king still does not know.** He never learns that God is in this. He goes to his grave believing he lost his temper twice and settled a matter of court intrigue. The book lets him. The name is in the text and not in his mouth."}]}, "Esther 7:7–10": {"title": "The gallows he built", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Everything Haman made turns around and is used on him, item by item, in the space of four verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Pleading with the woman he sentenced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Haman stayed behind to **plead with Queen Esther for his life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who wrote a decree to kill every Jew in the empire is on his knees to a Jewish woman asking her to spare him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not know that is what he is doing until the sentence before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The couch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As the king came back from the garden… there was Haman, **fallen across the couch** where Esther reclined.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is begging. The king walks in and reads it as an assault."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Will he even assault the queen while I am in the house?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing Haman did not do is the thing he is accused of, and it is the accusation that finishes him. He had built his whole career on the reading people put on appearances."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"They covered Haman's face\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And as the words left the king's mouth, they covered Haman's face.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Covering the face was what was done to a condemned man. No trial, no verdict pronounced — the servants read the room."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had gone home the day before with *his head covered* in shame. Now other hands do it, and it means execution."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Harbona"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **Harbona**, one of the king's servants, said, \"Look — the gallows Haman made for Mordecai… stands beside Haman's own house, fifty cubits high.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Harbona is named back in chapter 1, one of the seven eunuchs who were sent to fetch Vashti. The writer listed all seven names then, and it looked like nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He speaks once in the whole book, and it is this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gallows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventy-five feet, built the night before, standing beside his own house so the whole city could see it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Hang him on it.\" So they hanged Haman on the gallows he had built for Mordecai.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He chose the height. He chose the location. He supplied the instrument, and the only thing he got wrong was who would be on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it costs him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next chapter opens with his estate given to Esther, his signet ring given to Mordecai, and his household put under the man he meant to kill."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything he had listed to his friends — the riches, the rank, the sons — goes across to the other side of the ledger."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last clause.** *And the king's fury was stilled.* The same phrase closes chapter 2 after his rage at Vashti. Twice in this book a king's temper decides everything, and both times it lands where it had to for the Jews to live."}]}, "Esther 8:1–2": {"title": "\"Esther's house, Mordecai's ring\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The transfer happens in a single day, and it is total."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"That same day\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a process. The morning Haman was still second in the empire; by evening his estate belongs to the woman he sentenced to death."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The house"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*King Ahasuerus gave Queen Esther **the household of Haman, the enemy of the Jews**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer will not let his name stand alone. He is *the enemy of the Jews* here and in five other places — the title follows him to the end of the book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything he boasted about to his friends — the glorious riches, the great house — is signed over to a Jewish orphan."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"For Esther had told him what he was to her\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The secret comes out at last. Mordecai had told her at the beginning not to reveal her people or her family, and she had kept it for years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She tells the king now, and the man at the gate walks into the royal presence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ring"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The king took off his signet ring, **the one he had taken back from Haman**, and gave it to Mordecai.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same ring. Not a new one — the writer says where it came from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That ring sealed the decree ordering the death of every Jew in the empire. It is now on the hand of a Jew, and the next thing it will seal is the decree that lets them live."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instrument of the sentence becomes the instrument of the rescue."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Esther sets him over it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Esther set Mordecai over the household of Haman.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She receives it and immediately puts him in charge of it. Two verses, and the whole apparatus of the enemy is in the hands of the two people it was built against."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What has not changed.** The first decree still stands. Persian law cannot be revoked, and the thirteenth of Adar is still in the calendar — Haman is dead and the sentence he wrote is not. That is the problem chapter 8 has to solve, and it is not solved by cancelling anything. See the note on Esther 8:9–14."}]}, "Esther 8:15–17": {"title": "The city rejoiced", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The man who sat at the gate in sackcloth comes out of the palace in blue and white, and a city that had been reeling shouts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is wearing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Royal robes of **blue and white**, a great **crown of gold**, and a mantle of fine **linen and purple**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Blue and white are the colours of the Persian royal house, and they are also the colours of the tabernacle hangings. Fine linen and purple are priestly cloth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four chapters earlier the same man was in sackcloth and ashes in the middle of the city, crying out with a loud and bitter cry, and could not pass the gate because no one in sackcloth was allowed in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The city"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the city of Susa shouted and was glad**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last time Susa was described, in chapter 3, it *reeled in confusion* while the king and Haman sat down to drink."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same city, same writer, same word placement — and the reversal is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Four words for it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the Jews there was **light and gladness, joy and honour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Light** first. It is not the obvious word in a list about rejoicing, and it is the word chosen to lead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had had no prophet, no temple, no king, and no name of God spoken over them for a generation. What arrives is light."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Everywhere the decree reached"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In every province and every city, wherever the king's word and his decree arrived, there was gladness and joy among the Jews, feasting and a holiday.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gladness travels at the speed of the couriers. It is the arrival of the letter that produces it — not the thirteenth of Adar, which is still eight months off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing has happened yet. They are celebrating a word that has been sent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **many of the peoples of the land became Jews**, for the fear of the Jews had fallen upon them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Outsiders join. The Hebrew is a rare verb — they *judaised*, made themselves Jews."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people who were *not My people*, hidden and scattered, are suddenly the thing the empire wants to belong to."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**From the gate to the palace.** Mordecai never asked for any of it. He would not bow, he sat still, and he told his cousin the deliverance would come from another place whatever she did. The robe found him at the gate. See the notes on Esther 6:10–11 and Esther 4:12–14."}]}, "Esther 8:9–14": {"title": "The second decree", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first decree cannot be revoked. So a second one is written, and it does not cancel the first — it answers it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Persian law, once sealed with the king's ring, stands. The king says so himself in the verse before: *what is written in the king's name and sealed with the king's ring, no one can revoke.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order to destroy every Jew on the thirteenth of Adar is still law. Haman is dead; his decree is not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By these letters the king granted the Jews in every city **the right to gather and defend their lives**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an amnesty. A counter-decree, of equal force, issued through the same machinery."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Written the same way, deliberately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set the two side by side and the writer's care is obvious. Every element of Haman's decree in chapter 3 is repeated here:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the king's scribes summoned", "to a hundred and twenty-seven provinces, India to Cush", "each province in its own script, each people in its own language", "written in the name of the king, sealed with the king's signet ring", "sent by couriers, hurried", "for the same single day — the thirteenth of Adar", "with the same clause about *their little ones and their women*", "and the same right to take *the goods of their enemies as plunder*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Word for word, structure for structure. The same ring, the same road, the same date."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one difference is who is holding the pen. See the note on Esther 3:12–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The right to stand\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to stand for their life — to make a stand. It is defensive language throughout."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not told to go on the offensive on any other day. One day, the day already fixed against them, and on that day they may stand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Eight months' notice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letters go out in Sivan, the third month. The day is in Adar, the twelfth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haman's lot fell on a date eleven months away, and that delay is what gives the second decree time to reach India."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plunder they did not take"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The right to take the enemy's goods is granted here in so many words. Chapter 9 then reports, three separate times, that *they did not lay a hand on the plunder.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was theirs by law and they left it. Whatever the day was about, it was not about that."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What one signet ring did twice.** The same ring sealed the sentence and the deliverance. Nothing about the empire changed, nothing about the law changed, and the king never learned whose hand was on any of it."}]}, "Exodus 1:15–22": {"title": "The midwives who feared God", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two women are named in this passage. The king of Egypt is not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who gets a name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Hebrew midwives, **Shiphrah and Puah**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most powerful man in the world is *the king of Egypt* and *Pharaoh*, a title, never a name. Two midwives are recorded by name and have been read out loud for three thousand years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's judgement about who mattered is built into the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were to **put the newborn Hebrew boys to death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The boys only. It is the same shape as Haman's decree — an administrative solution to a people who are increasing, carried out through the ordinary machinery of the state."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amalek at the back of the column; Pharaoh at the birthstool. The attack is always on what is weakest and cannot resist."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the midwives **feared God**, and they refused to do it; they let the boys live.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given is not compassion, courage or ethics. It is fear of God, and the writer names it as the cause."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Notice what Deuteronomy says about Amalek: *they had no fear of God.* The same phrase, on the opposite side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the king demanded why, they answered that the Hebrew mothers were strong and gave birth before they could arrive.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They lie to Pharaoh's face. The text records it without a word of criticism, and the next sentence is about God being kind to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **God was kind to the midwives**, and because they honoured Him, **He gave them families of their own**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two women who spent their lives delivering other women's babies were given households of their own."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reward matches the work exactly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The escalation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At last Pharaoh ordered **all his people** that every newborn Hebrew boy was to be cast into the Nile.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The private instruction failed, so it becomes public policy. Every Egyptian is now deputised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the Nile — the river Egypt worshipped, the source of everything they had — is made the instrument."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes of it.** *Still the people kept increasing.* Said in the middle of the chapter and true at the end of it. And the very next paragraph has a Levite woman putting her son into the Nile in a basket, into the hands of Pharaoh's own daughter. See the note on Exodus 2:1–10."}]}, "Exodus 10:21–29": {"title": "Darkness you could feel, and light where Israel lived", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The ninth plague is the strangest of them, and the last one before the death of the firstborn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Darkness that could be touched"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew phrase is unusual — a darkness that may be *felt*, groped at. Not night. Something with substance to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For three days *no one could see anyone else, and no one moved from where he was*. Egypt stops. Nobody travels, nobody works, nobody visits. A whole civilisation sits still in the dark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The separation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But all the Israelites had light where they lived.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The plagues have been dividing Egypt from Goshen for several chapters now — the flies, the livestock, the hail — and the text keeps saying so plainly. Here the division is at its most visual: one people cannot see their hand, and a few miles away the lamps are on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this plague, and why now"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt's chief deity was the sun. The plague before this stripped the land bare; this one puts out the god. And it comes immediately before the night when the firstborn dies and Israel walks out — a rehearsal in the dark for what is coming."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last exchange.** Pharaoh tells Moses to get out and never show his face again, and Moses agrees: *you have spoken well; I will never see your face again.* It is the end of the negotiations that ran through the whole cycle — the compromises about who could go and what they could take are over, and the next thing in the book is the Passover."}]}, "Exodus 10:7–11": {"title": "The negotiation over the children", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Pharaoh has stopped saying no. That is what makes this passage worth reading slowly — the refusals have become offers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question he asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Go, worship the LORD your God. But tell me — who exactly is going?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not blocking the worship any more. He is negotiating the guest list, which is a different and more effective tactic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Moses' answer leaves nothing out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We will go with our young and our old, our sons and daughters, our flocks and herds.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four categories, and he names them deliberately: both ends of the generations, both sexes of the children, and the livelihood. There is nothing left in Egypt to come back for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Pharaoh draws the line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Only the men may go.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The children are the sticking point, and Pharaoh knows exactly what he is doing. A generation of men can go and worship in the desert and still leave everything behind that would make the departure permanent. Keep the children and you keep the future."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The rest of the pattern.** This is the third of four attempted compromises across the chapters: worship inside Egypt; do not go far; leave the children; and finally, leave the livestock. Each one concedes more than the last, and each keeps hold of something. Moses' answer to the fourth is the one that ends it — *not a hoof will be left behind.*"}]}, "Exodus 11:1–3": {"title": "The wages paid on the way out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses of instruction between the darkness and the last plague, and the instruction is about asking the neighbours for silver."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word that is usually mistranslated"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Older versions have Israel *borrow* from the Egyptians, which made generations of readers uneasy — it sounds like taking things under false pretences."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb is simply *ask*. There is no promise of return in it. What follows is a request made openly, and granted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is not theft"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred years of unpaid labour stand behind it. The law God gives later requires that a freed slave not be sent away empty-handed but supplied generously from the flock and the threshing floor — and that is exactly the shape of what happens here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel leaves as freed labourers being paid off, not as looters. Psalm 105 says it plainly: *He brought His people out with silver and gold.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The LORD had already softened the Egyptians\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The favour is not attributed to Israel's charm or to Egyptian generosity. The text says God had gone ahead of the request and changed how these people saw their slaves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then a line about Moses himself: *a great man in that land, respected by Pharaoh's officials and by the people alike*. The man who had fled Egypt as a wanted murderer is, by the end, the most respected person in it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the silver was for.** Some of it ends up in the tabernacle a few chapters later — gold, silver, bronze, blue and purple yarn, brought as freewill offerings. And some of it ends up in the golden calf. The same wealth, out of the same pockets, going two directions."}]}, "Exodus 12:1–13": {"title": "\"When I see the blood\" — who the sign was for", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most important sentence in the passage is the one that says who the mark was for, and it is not the person inside the house."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"When I see the blood\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *when you see it*. Not *when the destroyer sees it*. The seeing is God's, and the text says it twice, once at the start of the promise and once at the end — the second time as a plain repetition, as though the point were too important to risk being missed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means the protection never depended on the confidence of the family behind the door. A household could have spent that night terrified and the blood would have done what blood does. The sign was addressed outward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is ruled out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage states the negative itself: *you are not spared by your goodness or your strength. You are spared because the blood is there, and I see it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel had done nothing to deserve exemption; the plagues had fallen on Egypt and their own record over the following forty years is not flattering. Nothing about the household's quality is in view. Only whether the lamb had died and the blood was where it could be seen."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The details worth noticing"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**a lamb for a household** — and if a household is too small, share with the neighbour. It is reckoned by how many can eat, not by how much sin needs covering", "**without flaw**, and kept four days before it is killed — long enough to be looked over", "**eat it dressed to travel** — sandals on, staff in hand. They were to eat the meal in the posture of people already leaving", "**leave nothing until morning** — the whole lamb is consumed that night"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four days"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the tenth day of this month they shall take to them every man a lamb… and you shall keep it up **until the fourteenth day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four days in the house before it is killed. Long enough for a family to look it over, and for any blemish to show."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus rode into Jerusalem on the tenth of Nisan, went into the temple, and turned over the tables. From that morning to the Passover He is examined by one delegation after another — the chief priests and elders, the Pharisees and Herodians, the Sadducees, a lawyer — and each goes away with nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Pilate: *I find no fault in Him.* Herod finds none. See the note on Matthew 21:23–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The month starts here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This month is now the first of months for you, the very start of your year.* Before a single Israelite has walked out of Egypt, God resets the calendar. The year now begins at the lamb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a strange administrative detail to include in a rescue narrative, and it tells you how the event was to be reckoned: not one night among others, but the point everything is counted from."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word Passover.** *Pesach* — to pass over, and possibly also to hover or to shield. Both senses have been argued from the same root, and Isaiah uses the word of the LORD protecting Jerusalem like a bird over its nest. Either way the action belongs to God at the door, not to the people behind it."}]}, "Exodus 12:21–28": {"title": "\"He will not let the destroyer enter\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses passes the instructions to the elders, and the practical detail is what stands out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hyssop"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Take **a bunch of hyssop**, dip it in the blood in the basin, and **touch the top and both sides of your doorframe** with it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hyssop is a small bushy herb, common and worth nothing. It was the applicator — you could not do it with your hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same plant is used for cleansing a leper, and for the red heifer. David asks for it: *purge me with hyssop, and I shall be clean.* See the note on Psalms 51:3–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at the cross: *they filled a sponge with sour wine, put it on hyssop, and put it to His mouth.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Three sides"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lintel and two doorposts. Top, left and right."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing on the threshold. Nobody was to walk on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction that is easy to miss"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **not one of you is to step outside his door until morning**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blood is on the outside of the house. The safety is on the inside of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They spend the night indoors, eating, dressed to travel, while something goes through Egypt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who sees the mark"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When **He sees the blood** on the frame, He will pass over that door.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not when you see it. The mark is addressed outward, and the seeing is God's. See the note on Exodus 12:1–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The destroyer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **not let the destroyer enter** to strike your homes.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrew **mashchith** — the destroyer, the one who ruins."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse describes God passing over the door and, in the same clause, not permitting something else through it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The children's question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And when your children ask you, '**What does this ceremony mean to you?**' — tell them…\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction assumes the rite will outlive the night and that children will ask about it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That question is still asked at the Passover table — *ma nishtanah*, why is this night different — and it comes from this verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer they are given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is the Passover sacrifice to the LORD, **who passed over the houses** of the Israelites in Egypt, **and spared our homes**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer is a piece of history, told in the first person plural, by people who were not there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the people did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the people **bowed low and worshiped**. Then the Israelites went and **did exactly as the LORD had commanded**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No questions recorded. It is the first time in the book they simply do it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was outside that night.** The Egyptians had also been through nine plagues, and Exodus notes that by this point *the Egyptians urged the people to hurry out of the land.* The same night that emptied Egypt's houses filled Israel's road. See the note on Exodus 12:33–36."}]}, "Exodus 12:33–36": {"title": "The wealth of Egypt", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "They went in as guests, spent four centuries as slaves, and walked out carrying the treasury."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was doing the hurrying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **Egyptians urged the people to hurry out** of the land, for they said, \"We will all die.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt is pushing them toward the door. Nobody has to negotiate a release any more."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dough"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people took their dough **before the yeast was added**, wrapping their kneading bowls in their cloaks and carrying them on their shoulders.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is where unleavened bread comes from. Not a symbol first and a practicality second — they left before it could rise, and the feast keeps the memory of the speed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Just as Moses had told them\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **just as Moses had told them**, the Israelites asked the Egyptians for articles of silver and gold and for clothing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was instructed in advance, in chapter 11, and before that it was promised to Abram four hundred years earlier: *afterward your people will come out with great wealth.* See the note on Genesis 15:12–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody improvised this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *asked* is the ordinary verb for making a request. And the Egyptians *gave them whatever they asked.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the settlement of an account. Four hundred years of building store-cities — Pithom and Raamses — with no wages paid, and on one morning the books are balanced."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose doing it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **LORD had made the Egyptians so favourable** toward His people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *gave the people favour in the eyes of the Egyptians* — **chen**, grace, the same word used over Esther in a Persian palace and over Noah before the flood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Israelites did not persuade anybody. They asked, and the disposition of a nation had already been changed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some of it went into a golden calf a few chapters later. Most of it went into the tabernacle — the gold of the ark, the lampstand, the mercy seat, the boards overlaid, the silver sockets, the linen and the blue and purple."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt's wages built the tent God met them in."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the psalm sums it up.** *He brought His people out with silver and gold, and not one of their tribes stumbled.* Wealthy and well, in one line — the morning after the lamb. See the note on Psalms 105:37–38."}]}, "Exodus 12:37–42": {"title": "The night of the exodus", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "They leave in a hurry, and the text stops to say the date was exact."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The march"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Israelites set out from **Rameses toward Succoth** — about **six hundred thousand men on foot**, besides women and children.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shesh-me'ot eleph ragli haggevarim** — six hundred thousand men on foot."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Rameses** — one of the store cities they had built. **Succoth** means *booths*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mixed multitude"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **mixed multitude** went up with them, along with great flocks and herds.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **erev rav** — a great mixture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egyptians, and others. People who had watched ten plagues and drawn a conclusion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not Israelites and they leave with Israel. Later they cause trouble — *the mixed multitude who were among them yielded to intense craving* — and Numbers records it without retracting this verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bread"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From the dough they had brought **they baked flat loaves of unleavened bread, for it had not risen**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They had been **thrust out of Egypt with no time to prepare food** for themselves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **gorshu** — driven out, expelled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The unleavened bread is not primarily a symbol here. It is a description of what happens to dough when you are being pushed out of the country."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The feast that commemorates it keeps the fact in the diet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Israelites had lived in Egypt **four hundred and thirty years**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The precision"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And at the end of those four hundred and thirty years, **on that very day**, all the LORD's companies marched out of Egypt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **be'etzem hayyom hazzeh** — in the bone of this day. The idiom for *on that exact day*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What had been said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Know certainly that your descendants will be strangers in a land that is not theirs, and will serve them, and they will afflict them **four hundred years**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But in the fourth generation they shall return here.\"* See the note on Genesis 15:12–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to Abram in a deep sleep, with a smoking oven and a burning torch passing between the pieces."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The military word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All **the LORD's companies** marched out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tzivot YHWH** — the armies, the hosts of the LORD."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same noun in the title **YHWH Tzeva'ot**, the LORD of hosts, and the word Haggai uses fourteen times in two chapters. See the note on Haggai 1:2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A slave population walks out of a superpower and the narrator calls them armies."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vigil"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was **a night of keeping watch, the LORD's own vigil** to bring them out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **leil shimmurim** — a night of watchings."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose watch it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The LORD's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people had been indoors behind blood-marked doorposts, told *none of you shall go out of the door of his house until morning*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The watching was being done on the other side of the door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reciprocal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And so **this same night belongs to the LORD, kept as a watch by all Israel through all their generations**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He kept watch that night; they keep watch on that night, every year, since."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Passover seder is still held, and still on that date."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was carried out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Silver, gold and clothing, asked for and given: *thus they plundered the Egyptians*. See the note on Exodus 12:33–36."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Joseph's bones, as he had made them swear: *God will surely visit you, and you shall carry up my bones from here*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred years in a coffin in Egypt, and somebody remembered."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two watches.** The LORD's vigil that night, on the outside of the doors — and the vigil Israel keeps on the same night, every year since, on the inside."}]}, "Exodus 13:17–22": {"title": "The roundabout way", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first navigational decision of the exodus, and the reason given is about what they could take."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The road not taken"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God **did not lead them along the road through Philistine country**, though it was **the shorter way**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The coast road — the Via Maris — ran from Egypt up through Gaza into Canaan. It was the trade route and the army route, and it was about ten days' walk."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses notes that it was shorter. The text does not pretend the long way was more efficient."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For God said, \"**If they face war, they may lose heart and turn back to Egypt**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *lest the people change their minds when they see war*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The short road was garrisoned by Egyptian forts and then by Philistine cities. They would have hit an army in the first week."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the reason is about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the danger. Their readiness for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The decision is made about people who have been slaves for four hundred years and have never fought anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The route"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So God led the people **the roundabout way, through the wilderness toward the Red Sea**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a verb for turning aside, going about."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The long way ends at a sea with an army behind them — which is where the deliverance happens, and where Exodus 14 says *the Egyptians whom you see today, you shall see again no more forever*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Israelites went up out of Egypt **ready for battle**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *in orderly ranks*, or *armed*. They were not defenceless."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were equipped and God routed them around the fight anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bones"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses **carried the bones of Joseph** with him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For Joseph had made the Israelites swear an oath: \"**God will surely come to you — carry my bones up with you from here**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred years earlier, the second man in Egypt gave instructions about a coffin. See the note on Genesis 50:15–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody kept it, moved it, and knew where it was on the night they left. Joshua buries it at Shechem at the end of the conquest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The guide"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And here is the wonder of it: **the LORD Himself went ahead of them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By day in **a pillar of cloud** to lead the way, and by night in **a pillar of fire** to give light — **so they could travel by day or by night**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two forms, one presence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cloud is shade in a desert and the fire is light in the dark. Each is what the hour needs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The constancy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Neither the pillar of cloud by day nor the pillar of fire by night **ever left its place in front of the people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses says it as a summary, and it holds for forty years — Nehemiah still mentions it centuries later: *You did not forsake them… the pillar of cloud did not depart from them by day.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Deuteronomy says about the forty years"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He went ahead of you on the journey, **searching out a place for you to camp**, in fire by night and in a cloud by day, **to show you the way to walk**.* See the note on Deuteronomy 1:26–33."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the passage is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The shortest route was available, known, and rejected — and the sentence Moses records is about the people's hearts rather than the road's condition."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the long way.** Not because the short one was blocked. Moses says God looked at what was on it and at who was walking, and turned them."}]}, "Exodus 14:10–14": {"title": "Stand still and see", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Water in front, an army behind, and Moses tells them to stop moving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God had brought them there. Two chapters back He had told Moses to have them turn and camp by the sea — deliberately, in a place with no exit, *for Pharaoh will say, they are wandering in confusion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The trap was arranged. They were exactly where they had been led."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Were there no graves in Egypt**, that you brought us to the wilderness to die?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is bitter and it is sarcastic. And then: *\"Better to serve them than to die in the desert.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Within days of coming out, they are asking to go back in. The mind had not left Egypt when the body did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had just done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were terrified **and cried out to the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text records both. The terror and the crying out are in the same sentence, and neither is criticised."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Four instructions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not be afraid. **Stand still**, and **watch** the salvation the LORD will work for you today… The **LORD will fight for you**. You need only to **be still**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of them is a command to stop."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *stand still* is to take a position and hold it — plant yourself. And *be still* is the verb for silence: hold your peace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are given nothing to do. Not a weapon, not a formation, not a plan."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"These Egyptians you see today, **you will never see again**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rescue arrives at the last possible hour, with the chariots visible on the horizon. It could have come at any point in the previous three days. It comes when they can see the dust."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sea does not open until they are standing on the shore of it with nowhere else to be."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line the Illumination adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The battle is not yours to win. God will fight, and you may rest.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is what Moses has just said, put plainly. And it is the whole difference between this passage and every strategy a frightened person reaches for."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He says to Moses next.** *Why are you crying out to Me? Tell the Israelites to go forward.* The stillness is not passivity — it is that the fighting is His. When the word comes to move, they move into a road that was not there a minute earlier."}]}, "Exodus 14:15–18": {"title": "\"Why are you crying out to Me?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Israel is trapped between an army and a sea, Moses is praying, and God tells him to stop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Why are you crying out to Me? Tell the Israelites to move forward.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the chapter suggests Moses was praying wrongly. He had just told the people to stand still and see the salvation of the LORD, which is a fine thing to say."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But there was a word already given. Back at the burning bush, God had said He was bringing them out. The situation had not changed the instruction — it had only made it look impossible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is told to do instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lift up your staff, stretch out your hand over the sea, and divide it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The staff is the one that had already turned into a snake, already struck the Nile, already brought hail. He is not being handed something new."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer is not rebuked so much as redirected: there is a time to ask, and a time to pick up what you were already given and use it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does the dividing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction says *divide it* — spoken to Moses. Two verses on, the account says the LORD drove the sea back with a strong east wind all night."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are true, and neither cancels the other. The man stretches out his hand; God moves the water. That is the pattern the whole book runs on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why God says He is doing it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will gain honour through Pharaoh and all his army… Then the Egyptians will know that I am the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given is not only Israel's rescue. God names His own honour and Egypt's knowledge as the point — the rescue is real, and it is also a demonstration aimed at people watching."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line just before it.** *The LORD will fight for you; you need only to be still.* Moses says it, and God's next words are *tell them to move forward*. Both are in the same passage, four verses apart. Stillness and forward movement are not opposites here — the stillness is about who does the fighting."}]}, "Exodus 14:21–25": {"title": "The sea splits open", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The crossing takes all night, and the text says what did the work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The position"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pharaoh's army behind, the sea in front, and Moses saying *stand still, and see the salvation of the LORD*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And God's answer: *why do you cry to Me? **Tell the children of Israel to go forward***. See the note on Exodus 14:13–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gesture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Moses **stretched out his hand over the sea**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The staff, held out. Nothing else is asked of him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mechanism"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **all that night the LORD drove the sea back with a strong east wind**, turning it into dry land.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **beruach qadim azzah** — with a strong east wind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text names a wind and a direction, and says it blew all night."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ruach** — wind, breath, spirit. The same noun that moved over the waters in Genesis 1."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The duration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "All night."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not instantaneous. The people stood on the shore in the dark while a wind blew, and the ground appeared gradually."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The waters were divided, and **the Israelites walked through the sea on dry ground**, a wall of water on their right and on their left.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **bayabbashah** — on dry ground, not mud."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chomah** — a wall."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pursuit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Egyptians pursued them — **all Pharaoh's horses, chariots, and horsemen** followed them into the sea.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They go in. The whole army follows a slave column onto a seabed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The morning watch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **in the morning watch**, the LORD looked down on the Egyptian army **from the pillar of fire and cloud** and threw it into confusion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **be'ashmoret habboqer** — the last watch of the night, roughly two to six."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Vayyaqoshem** — He looked down. The verb is used of a hostile gaze."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pillar"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It had moved during the night: *the Angel of God who went before the camp of Israel moved and went behind them*, and *it came between the camp of the Egyptians and the camp of Israel*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So it was a cloud and darkness to the one, and it gave light by night to the other.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same object, doing opposite things to the two armies."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wheels"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **jammed their chariot wheels so they could barely move**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyasar et ophan markevotav** — He removed, or turned aside, their chariot wheels."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Samaritan text and the Septuagint read *bound*, or *clogged*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Egyptians say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Let us flee from Israel — the LORD is fighting for them against Egypt**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **YHWH nilcham lahem** — the LORD fights for them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The covenant name, spoken by the Egyptian army, in the middle of the sea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have worked it out. It is too late."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses had said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD will fight for you, and you shall hold your peace**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb, said before the crossing. The Egyptians repeat his sentence back in the last minutes of their lives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the waters returned and covered the chariots, the horsemen, and all the army of Pharaoh… **not so much as one of them remained**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Thus Israel saw the great work which the LORD had done in Egypt; so the people feared the LORD, and believed the LORD and His servant Moses.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The song"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will sing to the LORD, for He has triumphed gloriously! **The horse and its rider He has thrown into the sea**.* See the note on Exodus 15:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Miriam and the timbrels, and then three days later they cannot find water. See the note on Exodus 15:22–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm says about the path"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your way was in the sea, Your path in the great waters, **and Your footsteps were not known**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the text names.** A strong east wind, blowing all night. The people waited in the dark while the ground came up."}]}, "Exodus 14:30–31": {"title": "Lying still along the shore", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The morning after, and what they see on the sand settles the matter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the day was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That day the **LORD saved Israel from the hand of Egypt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yasha** — the verb behind **yeshua**, salvation. Its first major appearance in the story of Israel is here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses had used it the night before: *stand still, and **see the salvation of the LORD**, which He will accomplish for you today.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Israel **saw the Egyptians lying still along the shore**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *dead upon the sea shore*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had watched the army come at them from behind — *the Egyptians marched after them, and they were very afraid*. Now the same army is on the beach, and they are looking down at it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The change of position"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The night before, the enemy was between them and the wilderness and they were pinned against water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the morning they are across the water and the enemy is on the near side, face down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the geography changed except which side they were standing on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it produced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Israel saw the great power the LORD had shown against Egypt, the people **stood in awe of the LORD, and they trusted Him** — and they **trusted Moses His servant**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *they feared the LORD and believed the LORD and His servant Moses*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fear and the belief are the same reaction, named twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They had **done nothing but walk and be still**, and **God had carried them through**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses' instruction had been exactly that: *the LORD will fight for you, and **you shall hold your peace***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for that is *you shall be silent* — **tacharishun**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had said the day before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Is it because there are no graves in Egypt that you have taken us away to die in the wilderness?** It would have been better for us to serve the Egyptians than that we should die in the wilderness.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Complaint, on the record, hours before the crossing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two redemptions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blood on the doorposts got them out of Egypt. See the note on Exodus 12:1–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sea got Egypt out of the way behind them. Two separate events, three days apart, and the second one is the one they sing about."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 15 is the song: *the horse and its rider He has thrown into the sea… the LORD is a man of war; the LORD is His name.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews notices"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By faith they **passed through the Red Sea as by dry land**, whereas the Egyptians, **attempting to do so, were drowned**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two groups walked into the same corridor. The Greek for the second one is **peiran labontes** — taking a trial of it, making an attempt. See the note on Hebrews 11:23–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The water was identical. The difference is in the verbs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All were **baptized into Moses in the cloud and in the sea**… now all these things happened to them as examples, and they were written for our admonition.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 10:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He treats the crossing as a picture of something that happened to the reader."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How long the belief lasted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days. *And when they came to Marah, they could not drink the waters of Marah, for they were bitter… and the people complained against Moses.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter ends with them trusting, and the next one has them thirsty and saying so."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they contributed.** The text says it in a sentence: they walked, and they kept quiet. The bodies on the shore were there when they looked up."}]}, "Exodus 15:1–5": {"title": "The song of Moses", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first song in the Bible, sung on a beach with an army in the water behind them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The occasion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Red Sea. A strong east wind all night, dry ground, and the Egyptians going in after them and not coming out. See the note on Exodus 14:21–25."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Thus Israel saw the great work which the LORD had done in Egypt; so the people feared the LORD, and believed the LORD and His servant Moses.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first song"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Moses and the Israelites **sang this song to the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no singing recorded before this. Four hundred and thirty years in Egypt produce no hymn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first song in Scripture comes after a deliverance nobody worked for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will sing to the LORD, for **He has risen up in glory**; the horse and its rider **He has hurled into the sea**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ga'oh ga'ah** — highly exalted, the verb doubled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ramah** — hurled, shot. The word for shooting an arrow."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three-part confession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD is my strength and my song; He has become my salvation**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ozzi vezimrat Yah vayehi-li lishu'ah**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yeshu'ah** — salvation. The noun behind the name Jesus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The line appears twice more in Scripture, word for word: in Psalm 118, and in Isaiah 12. Three places, unchanged."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He is my God, and **I will praise Him**, my father's God, and **I will lift Him high**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **anvehu** — I will beautify Him, or prepare Him a dwelling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**My God**, and then **my father's God**. Personal, and inherited, in the same line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD is a warrior — the LORD is His name**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **YHWH ish milchamah** — the LORD is a man of war."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Blunt, and unusual. The Septuagint softened it to *the Lord shatters wars*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The deep waters covered them; **down they went to the depths like a stone**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yaredu bimtzolot kemo-aven**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses later: *they sank like lead in the mighty waters*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two comparisons for weight, and both are about how fast something goes down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the song does not contain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any instruction. Any request."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fifteen verses in, it turns to what will happen when the nations hear — *the people will hear and be afraid* — and then to the mountain of God's inheritance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But the first half is entirely about what has been done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Miriam"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **Miriam the prophetess, Aaron's sister**, took the timbrel in her hand; and all the women went out after her with timbrels and with dances.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Sing to the LORD, for He has triumphed gloriously!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She repeats the first line. The song is antiphonal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens three days later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For three days they traveled through the wilderness and found no water.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the people grumbled against Moses, saying, \"**What are we to drink?**\"* See the note on Exodus 15:22–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that begins with a song ends with a complaint, and the narrative puts them in the same chapter deliberately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the song turns up again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they sing **the song of Moses**, the servant of God, and the song of the Lamb, saying: \"Great and marvellous are Your works, Lord God Almighty!\"* See the note on Revelation 15:2–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Standing on a sea of glass mingled with fire."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two seas, and the same song named at both."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The first song in Scripture.** Four hundred and thirty years in Egypt produce no hymn. The singing starts on the far side of water nobody opened for themselves."}]}, "Exodus 15:22–27": {"title": "Bitter water made sweet", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three days after the greatest deliverance in their history, they cannot find a drink."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Red Sea. The song of Moses, with Miriam and the timbrels: *I will sing to the LORD, for He has triumphed gloriously*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The horse and its rider He has thrown into the sea.* See the note on Exodus 15:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The singing ends at verse 21. Verse 22 begins the journey."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three days"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **three days they traveled through the wilderness and found no water**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had just watched a sea open. Three days later there is nothing to drink."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrative puts the two things back to back without comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Marah"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They came to Marah, but **they could not drink its water, for it was bitter** — that is why the place is called **Marah, which means bitter**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Water, at last, and undrinkable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **marah** is the word Naomi uses of herself: *do not call me Naomi; call me Mara, for the Almighty has dealt very bitterly with me*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grumbling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **the people grumbled against Moses**, saying, \"**What are we to drink?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyillonu** — murmured."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the first of many. The word will recur through the wilderness narratives, and this is where it starts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses **cried out to the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyitzaq** — cried out, the same verb used of Israel groaning in Egypt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not answer the complaint. He takes it upward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the LORD showed him a piece of wood**. He threw it into the water, **and the water became sweet**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **etz** — a tree, or a piece of wood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The LORD **showed** him — **vayyorehu**, from **yarah**, to point out, to instruct. The same root as **torah**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the wood did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text does not say the wood had properties. It says God showed it to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something thrown into bitter water, and the water changes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is given there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There **the LORD gave them a rule and a standard**, and **there He tested them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **choq umishpat** — a statute and an ordinance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before Sinai. This is the first legislation in Israel's history and it is given at a spring."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If you listen carefully to the voice of the LORD your God**, and do what is right in His eyes, giving heed to His commands and keeping His decrees.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verbs for one thing: listen, do, give heed, keep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Then I will not bring on you any of the diseases I brought on the Egyptians**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The plagues, named as diseases. They had watched them happen to somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**For I am the LORD, the One who heals you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ani YHWH rophecha** — I am the LORD your physician."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Rapha** — to heal, to mend, to repair. The word used of mending pottery and of healing a wound."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the first of the compound divine names given in the wilderness, and it is given at the place named *bitter*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Elim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then they came to Elim, where there were **twelve springs of water and seventy palm trees**, and they camped there beside the water.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve springs — one for each tribe. Seventy palms — the number of the elders, and of the souls who went down into Egypt with Jacob."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elim is one stage further on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The abundance was a short walk past the bitter place, and the name was given at the bitter one."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the name is given.** At Marah — *the LORD your physician*, spoken over water that had just been undrinkable, one day's march short of twelve springs."}]}, "Exodus 16:13–15": {"title": "\"What is it?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A nation wakes up to find breakfast on the ground and has no word for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dew first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the morning **a layer of dew lay all around** the camp. **When the dew lifted**, there on the surface of the desert lay something fine and flaky.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It comes wrapped. The dew is underneath and above it — Numbers adds *when the dew fell on the camp in the night, the manna fell on it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was never in contact with the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it looked like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Something **fine and flaky, delicate as frost** on the ground.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers 11 adds the rest: *like coriander seed, pale like resin*, and it *tasted like something baked with oil.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Israelites looked at it and said to one another, \"**What is it?**\" — for they had **no idea what it was**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **man hu**. The question became the noun."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For forty years they ate something whose name was a question, because nothing in their experience had a word for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Moses' answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This is the bread the LORD has given you to eat.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not explain what it is either. He says where it came from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rules attached to it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest of the chapter is the arrangement, and every part of it is unusual:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**enough for one day** — gather a day's portion each day", "**it bred worms** if kept overnight, and stank", "**except on the sixth day**, when a double portion kept perfectly for the Sabbath", "*he who gathered much had nothing left over, and he who gathered little had no lack*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "That last line is the strangest. Whatever a man collected, it came out to an omer when it was measured."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes it in 2 Corinthians 8, about the collection for Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it had to be daily"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing you could not do with manna was store it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A nation of former slaves, who had spent four hundred years with nothing secured, was fed by something that would not keep — every day, for forty years, and it stopped the day they ate the produce of Canaan."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it was kept"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One omer of it was put in a golden pot and placed in the ark, beside Aaron's rod that budded and the tablets of the covenant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only manna that did not rot is the jar that went into the holiest place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus said about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Moses did not give you the bread from heaven**, but My Father gives you the true bread from heaven.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"Your fathers ate the manna in the wilderness, and are dead.\"* See the note on John 6:47–51."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Revelation promises.** *To him who overcomes I will give some of the **hidden manna** to eat.* The jar in the ark, offered to the reader."}]}, "Exodus 16:19–21": {"title": "Don't keep any of it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only rule attached to free food is that it cannot be stored."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses told them, \"**Don't keep any of it until morning**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **al-yoter mimennu** — let no one leave any of it over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are a month out of Egypt, in a wilderness, with no supply line. And the rule about the food is that they may not put any aside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the morning there was **a layer of dew** around the camp, and when it lifted there was **a fine flake-like thing, fine as frost**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they said to one another, \"**What is it?**\" — for they did not know what it was.* See the note on Exodus 16:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **man hu**, and the name stuck: **manna**. The food is called *what is it*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disobedience"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But some didn't listen. **They held a little back**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the reasonable thing to do. Anyone who has been hungry would."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened to it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And by morning it was **crawling with maggots and reeking**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayarum tola'im** — it bred worms. And **vayivash** — it stank."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses was angry with them.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the manna would not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Keep."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It could feed a nation of hundreds of thousands every morning for forty years, and it could not survive one night in a jar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The failure was designed in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rhythm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **each morning they gathered it fresh, every person as much as they needed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ish ke'fi ochlo** — each according to his eating."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Paul's note on it: *he who gathered much had nothing left over, and he who gathered little had no lack*. See the note on 2 Corinthians 8:13–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The deadline"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **when the sun grew hot, whatever was left simply melted away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There was a window. Gather early, or it is gone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On the sixth day they gathered twice as much, and Moses said *bake what you will bake today, and boil what you will boil, and lay up for yourselves all that remains*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they laid it up till morning, as Moses commanded; **and it did not stink, nor were there any worms in it***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same food, kept overnight, with the opposite result — because that time it was commanded."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The manna was not obeying chemistry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one jar"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Fill an omer with it, to be kept for your generations… **so Aaron laid it up before the Testimony, to be kept***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One jar of the stuff that would not keep, kept forever in front of the ark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the prayer says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give us **this day our daily bread**.* See the note on Matthew 6:9–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epiousion** — a word found almost nowhere else in ancient literature. Its likeliest sense is *for the coming day*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Today's portion, asked for today."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus says about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your fathers ate the manna in the wilderness, and are dead… **I am the living bread which came down from heaven**.\"* See the note on John 6:47–51."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The forty years"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the manna ceased on the day after they had eaten the produce of the land.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every morning for forty years, and then the day they ate from Canaan, it stopped."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What it would not do.** Keep. The same food fed a nation for forty years and could not last one night in a jar — except on the sixth day, when it was told to."}]}, "Exodus 16:22–26": {"title": "The day it did not rot", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The manna has one rule and one exception, and the exception is the Sabbath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule up to this point"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let no one leave any of it till morning.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some had ignored it: *but some of them left part of it until morning, and it bred worms and stank*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gather it every day, and only what you need. An omer each."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the note the chapter adds: *he who gathered much had nothing left over, and he who gathered little had no lack*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes that line about the collection for Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sixth day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the sixth day they gathered **twice as much — two omers each**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They did it before being told to. Something in the ground provided double and they picked it up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the leaders of the community came and told Moses.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The leaders report an anomaly. Nobody had explained the arrangement yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The explanation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is what the LORD meant: **Tomorrow is a day of rest, a holy Sabbath set apart for the LORD**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shabbaton shabbat qodesh**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the first appearance of the Sabbath as a command in Scripture. It arrives here, over food, before Sinai and before the ten commandments."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **bake what you want to bake, boil what you want to boil**, and **whatever is left over, set it aside and keep it until morning**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cooking is done the day before. The keeping is explicitly permitted — the one day the standing rule is reversed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They kept it as Moses commanded, and **this time it did not stink, and there were no maggots in it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same substance, the same overnight period, and a different outcome."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the food changed. What changed is which day it was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The confirmation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Eat it today, for today is a Sabbath to the LORD; **today you will not find any out in the field**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Six days you gather it, but the seventh day is the Sabbath — **on that day there will be none**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice, and some went out looking anyway: *and it happened that some of the people went out on the seventh day to gather, but they found none*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the double portion means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody had to work on the seventh day because the sixth had already supplied it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest was not a gap in provision. It was made possible by a provision that arrived early."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How long it went on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the children of Israel ate manna forty years, until they came to an inhabited land.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six days on, one day off, for forty years. Roughly two thousand consecutive weeks of the same pattern, including the double day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it stopped"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the manna ceased on the day after they had eaten the produce of the land; and the children of Israel had manna no longer.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The day after the Passover at Gilgal, on the other side of the Jordan. See the note on Joshua 4:19–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the house of Israel called its name **Manna**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **man hu** — *what is it?* They named it after their own question."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where a jar of it ended up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Fill an omer with it, to be kept for your generations.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Inside the ark, with the tablets and Aaron's rod."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your fathers ate the manna in the wilderness, and are dead… **I am the living bread which came down from heaven**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the prayer: *give us this day our daily bread*. See the note on Matthew 6:9–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek there is **epiousion** — a word that appears nowhere else in Greek literature. Most read it as *for the coming day*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What made the difference on the sixth day.** Nothing about the food. The same manna, kept the same length of time, and on that one night it did not rot."}]}, "Exodus 16:31–34": {"title": "A jar to remember", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The bread is described, and then a sample of it is put away where nobody will eat it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Israel named it **manna**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **man hu** — *what is it?* Exodus says so earlier in the chapter: *when the children of Israel saw it, they said to one another, \"What is it?\" For they did not know what it was.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name is the question they asked, and it stuck."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was **white, like coriander seed**, and it **tasted like wafers made with honey**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers adds: *its taste was like the taste of pastry prepared with oil*, and that they ground it in mills, beat it in mortars, cooked it in pans and made cakes of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two descriptions of the same thing, and both are about how good it was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Keep an omer of it for the generations to come**, so they can **see the bread I fed you in the wilderness when I brought you out of Egypt**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An **omer** — about two litres, and the daily ration per person named earlier in the chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One day's food, kept."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is strange"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has just established that manna could not be kept. Anyone who left it overnight found it *full of worms and stank* — except on the sabbath eve."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one jar is an exception to the rule the chapter spends twenty verses establishing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take a jar, put an omer of manna in it, and **set it before the LORD**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Aaron set it **before the Testimony**, to be preserved.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Testimony is the ark with the tablets in it. The jar goes into the holiest place in Israel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews lists the contents: *the ark of the covenant… in which were **the golden pot that had the manna**, Aaron's rod that budded, and the tablets of the covenant.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three objects: bread, a dead stick that flowered, and stone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why those three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each is a record of something the nation got wrong. The manna is the complaining in the wilderness, the rod is Korah's rebellion, and the tablets are what was broken over the calf."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ark holds the evidence, and the lid over it is the mercy seat, with blood on it once a year."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus says about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your fathers ate the manna in the wilderness, and are dead. **This is the bread which comes down from heaven, that one may eat of it and not die**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"**I am the living bread** which came down from heaven.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the discourse that emptied the room. See the note on John 6:60–65."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Revelation says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To him who overcomes I will give some of **the hidden manna** to eat.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The jar in the ark, described as hidden — and offered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they complained about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers 11: *\"**we remember the fish which we ate freely in Egypt**, the cucumbers, the melons, the leeks, the onions, and the garlic. But now our whole being is dried up; **there is nothing at all except this manna**.\"* See the note on Numbers 11:4–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same food, twenty chapters apart, described as honey wafers and as nothing at all."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the jar is.** A day's ration of bread that could not normally last a night, kept in a box in the holiest room in the nation — so that people who never ate it could see what their fathers were fed."}]}, "Exodus 17:4–7": {"title": "\"I myself will stand there before you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first time the rock is struck, and the detail everyone reads past is where God says He will be standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses cried out to the LORD, \"What am I to do with these people? **They're almost ready to stone me!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rephidim. No water, and the people have moved from complaining to picking up rocks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is told to take"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Carry in your hand **the staff you struck the Nile with**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not any staff. The one that turned a river to blood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus makes the identification, so the reader knows what kind of instrument is about to be used."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where God will be"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I myself will stand there before you, on the rock at Horeb**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *behold, I stand before you there upon the rock*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rock about to be struck is the place God says He will be standing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses is told to bring the rod of judgment and swing it at a rock with God on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Strike the rock, and water will pour out** for the people to drink.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *strike* is **nakah** — to hit, to smite, to strike down. It is the verb used of the plagues."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The witnesses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take **some of the elders of Israel** with you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Moses did it, **right in front of the elders**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Named observers, so it could be attested."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What came out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Water came — **for thirsty people who had nothing to give in return**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people had just threatened to stone him. Nothing in the passage is offered, promised or repented of before the water arrives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He named the place **Massah and Meribah — Testing and Quarreling**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both names stick. Psalm 95 uses them as the standing example of a hardened heart, and Hebrews builds two chapters on the psalm. See the notes on Psalms 95:8–11 and Hebrews 4:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question underneath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Is the LORD among us or not?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what Exodus says they were really asking, and it is a strange question from people standing under a cloud that had led them there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second time"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty-eight years later, at Kadesh, the same problem and a different instruction: *speak to the rock*. See the note on Numbers 20:7–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses struck it twice instead, and was told he would not enter the land. See the note on Numbers 20:12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says the rock was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They drank from a spiritual Rock that went with them, and **that Rock was Christ**.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 10:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes out of the pierced side"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One of the soldiers pierced His side with a spear, and **at once blood and water came out**.* See the note on John 19:31–34."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clause to notice.** *I will stand there before you, on the rock.* The thing being struck is the place God said He would be."}]}, "Exodus 17:8–13": {"title": "Hands held up until sundown", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Israel's first battle after the exodus, and it is won on a hilltop rather than in the valley."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Amalekites came and attacked Israel **at Rephidim**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name means *resting places*, or places of refreshment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had just come through the sea and been given water out of a rock, and the attack lands at the place named for rest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Amalek was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Esau's grandson, and Deuteronomy describes the method: they *met you on the road and cut down all the stragglers at the rear, when you were weary and worn out.* See the note on Deuteronomy 25:17–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They attack from behind, at the point of exhaustion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two assignments"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Choose some men and go out to fight** Amalek. **Tomorrow I will stand on top of the hill** with the staff of God in my hand.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua in the valley with a sword. Moses on the hill with a staff."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are given, and the passage is about the relationship between them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correlation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As long as Moses **held his hands up, Israel prevailed**; but whenever he **let his hands down, Amalek prevailed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua's fighting is not what varies. The men, the swords and the tactics stay the same all day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only variable in the account is a pair of arms on a hill a mile away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to Moses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Moses' arms **grew tired**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is heavy. He is eighty, and it is an all-day battle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man holding up the staff of God cannot hold up his own arms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stone and the two men"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They took a stone and put it under him, and **he sat on it**, while **Aaron and Hur held up his hands — one on each side** — so his hands **stayed steady until the sun went down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three details, and each one matters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He sits. The posture changes from standing to seated, and the account does not treat that as a failure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two men hold the hands up. Not his own strength, and not one helper — one on each side."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *stayed steady* — the Hebrew is **emunah**, the word for faithfulness, steadiness. It is the root of *amen*, and it is used here of a pair of hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Joshua **overcame the Amalekite army** with the sword.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves are true. The sword did the work in the valley, and the hands on the hill decided it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses builds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses built an altar and called its name **The-LORD-Is-My-Banner**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrew **Yahweh-Nissi**. A banner is what an army rallies to and fights under."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *the LORD will have war with Amalek from generation to generation.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The thing that cannot be won by effort"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amalek is the one enemy the Old Testament says will be fought in every generation. It is not defeated once."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul was told to finish it and did not, and an Amalekite is standing over him on Gilboa. Haman the Agagite is signing a decree centuries later. See the notes on 2 Samuel 1:5–10 and Esther 3:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one enemy that is never beaten by effort is beaten here under raised hands somebody else was holding."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the day required.** A young man fighting, an old man seated on a stone, and two friends who stood there from morning until sundown holding his arms. Exodus names all four."}]}, "Exodus 18:19–23": {"title": "Sharing the load", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The best management advice in the Bible, and it comes from a Midianite priest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jethro — Moses' father-in-law, priest of Midian, who has just arrived at the camp bringing Moses' wife and two sons back to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not an Israelite. He hears what God has done, says *now I know that the LORD is greater than all the gods*, and offers a burnt offering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The day before: *Moses sat to judge the people, and the people stood before Moses from morning until evening.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And his question: *\"**Why do you alone sit, and all the people stand before you from morning until evening?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses' answer: they come to me, and I judge, and I make known the statutes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The thing that you do is not good. Both you and these people who are with you will surely wear yourselves out**. For this thing is too much for you; **you are not able to perform it by yourself**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Blunt, and to the man who had just parted a sea."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Listen to me, and let me give you some advice, **and may God be with you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He offers counsel and immediately submits it to God's approval — and says so again at the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses keeps"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You stand before God for the people** and bring their cases to him.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Teach them his decrees and instructions**, and **show them the way to live and the work they are to do**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things: representing them upward, and teaching them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are irreducible. Jethro does not propose delegating either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The qualifications"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look for **capable men** from among all the people — men who **fear God**, who are **trustworthy** and **hate dishonest gain**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four, and only the first is about ability."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hate dishonest gain** — the Hebrew is *hating covetousness*. The test is not whether a man would take a bribe but whether the idea disgusts him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The structure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Set them over the people as leaders of thousands, hundreds, fifties, and tens**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four tiers. A man over ten families answers to a man over fifty, and so on up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the arrangement requires literacy, a building, or a budget."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Have them **bring every hard case to you**, but **let them decide the simple ones themselves**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The escalation runs one way, and the threshold is difficulty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That will **lighten your load**, for **they will carry it with you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *and they shall bear the burden with you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not instead of. With."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If you do this, and God so commands**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts the whole proposal under God's authorisation. Twice in five verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The outcome"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You'll **be able to hold up**, and **all these people will go home in peace**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two results, and the second is about the people waiting in the queue."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Standing from morning to evening was costing them as much as it was costing Moses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **Moses heeded the voice of his father-in-law and did all that he had said**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most humble man on the face of the earth takes advice from his wife's father."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Deuteronomy adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses tells it again forty years later and adds one detail: *and you answered me and said, \"The thing which you have told us to do is good.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He put it to the people first."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who gives the advice.** A Midianite priest, on a visit, who had watched one day's work and told the deliverer of Israel that what he was doing was not good."}]}, "Exodus 19:10–13": {"title": "Mark off a boundary", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three days of preparation, and the whole point of it is a fence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sinai, three months out of Egypt. The mountain Moses was sent from, at the bush, with the sign attached: *when you have brought the people out of Egypt, you shall serve God on this mountain*. See the note on Exodus 3:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sign has now been fulfilled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was said two days earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You have seen what I did to the Egyptians, and how I **bore you on eagles' wings and brought you to Myself**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You shall be to Me a kingdom of priests and a holy nation.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offer, and then the people's answer: *all that the LORD has spoken we will do*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go to the people and **set them apart today and tomorrow**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **qiddashtam** — sanctify them, make them holy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb's first use in Scripture is of a day, not a people: *God blessed the seventh day and **sanctified** it*. See the note on Genesis 2:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The washing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Have them **wash their clothes** and be ready by the third day.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Visible, physical preparation, and it is the kind that can be checked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses adds one more thing when he passes it on: *do not come near your wives*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because on that day **the LORD will come down on Mount Sinai where all the people can see him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole nation is to be a witness. Nothing about it is private."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The boundary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Mark off a boundary around the mountain** for them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hagbalta** — set limits."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days of getting ready, and the readiness is for a fence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prohibition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Be careful not to go up the mountain or even touch its edge**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the summit. The edge."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The penalty"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whoever touches the mountain must surely be **put to death**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the method: *no hand may touch the offender; they are to be **stoned or shot with arrows**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because whoever had touched the mountain was now himself untouchable. The execution had to be carried out at a distance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The extent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whether animal or human, they may not live.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A cow that wanders onto the slope."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The holiness described here is not a moral quality. It is a property of the ground, and it does not distinguish between a rebel and a straying goat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one permission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Only when the **ram's horn sounds a long blast** may they come up to the mountain.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yovel** — the ram's horn, and the word behind *jubilee*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is a signal at which they may come. It is in the passage, and Exodus does not record it being sounded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the day was like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Thunderings and lightnings, and a thick cloud on the mountain; and the sound of the trumpet was very loud, so that all the people who were in the camp trembled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Mount Sinai was completely in smoke, because the LORD descended upon it in fire.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let not God speak with us, lest we die.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For you have not come to the mountain that may be touched and that burned with fire, and to blackness and darkness and tempest… **and so terrifying was the sight that Moses said, \"I am exceedingly afraid and trembling.\"***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But you have come to Mount Zion… to Jesus the Mediator of the new covenant, and to the blood of sprinkling that speaks better things than that of Abel.* See the note on Hebrews 12:18–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole argument turns on touchability. That mountain could not be approached; this one is described as a city people have already arrived at."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What three days of sanctifying was for.** A boundary. The people wash their clothes, and the instruction they are being prepared for is not to touch the edge."}]}, "Exodus 19:16–19": {"title": "The mountain trembles", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The day the law is given, described entirely by sound and smoke."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the morning of **the third day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The day named in the preparation. Wash your clothes, be ready by the third day, and do not touch the mountain. See the note on Exodus 19:10–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days of getting ready, and this is what they were getting ready for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What arrives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Thunder cracked and lightning flashed, **a thick cloud settled over the mountain**, and a **trumpet blast sounded so loud that everyone in the camp trembled**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **qolot** — voices, and it is the ordinary word for thunder."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The trumpet is not blown by anybody. No priest is mentioned, and the shofar was not yet part of the worship — Aaron has not been consecrated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sound has no visible source."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The people"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone in the camp trembled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyecherad** — they shook."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two million people, and the text says the whole camp."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Moses **led the people out of the camp to meet with God**, and they **stood at the foot of the mountain**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He walks them toward it. That is the remarkable line: the trembling comes first and then he brings them out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At the foot* — the Hebrew is *at the lowest part*. Right up to the boundary and no further."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The smoke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mount Sinai was **wrapped in smoke, because the LORD had come down on it in fire**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason is given. Not weather — a descent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The smoke rose **like smoke from a furnace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kivshan** — a kiln, a smelting furnace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word Genesis uses of Sodom the morning after: *and he looked toward Sodom and Gomorrah… and saw the smoke of the land ascend like the smoke of a furnace*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the whole mountain shook violently**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some manuscripts have *the people trembled greatly*; the Hebrew as it stands has the mountain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The escalation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As the trumpet grew **louder and louder**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *going and growing very strong*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ordinary trumpets fade. This one increases for the duration."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exchange"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses spoke, and **God answered him in the thunder**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **bekol** — with a voice, in thunder. The same word doing both jobs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is said next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ten commandments, and then the people's reaction:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now all the people witnessed the thunderings, the lightning flashes, the sound of the trumpet, and the mountain smoking; and when the people saw it, **they trembled and stood afar off**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then they said to Moses, \"**You speak with us, and we will hear; but let not God speak with us, lest we die**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had said, three days earlier, *all that the LORD has spoken we will do*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses said afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am exceedingly afraid and trembling.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in Exodus — Hebrews supplies it, and it is not recorded anywhere in the Old Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews does with the scene"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For you have **not come to the mountain that may be touched** and that burned with fire, and to blackness and darkness and tempest, and the sound of a trumpet and the voice of words…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **you have come to Mount Zion**, and to the city of the living God… to Jesus the Mediator of the new covenant, and to the blood of sprinkling that speaks better things than that of Abel.* See the note on Hebrews 12:18–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every feature of this paragraph appears in that one, listed as what the reader has not come to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sound was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the account is decorative. The fire, the smoke, the shaking and the trumpet all say the same thing as the fence: do not come up."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one detail that does not fade.** The trumpet gets louder for as long as it sounds — and nobody in the account is blowing it."}]}, "Exodus 19:7–9": {"title": "\"We will do everything the LORD has said\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A nation makes a promise, and the whole shape of the next fifteen hundred years turns on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God had just offered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before: *you have seen what I did to the Egyptians, and how **I bore you on eagles' wings and brought you to Myself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the account of everything up to this point. Grace, from the blood on the doorposts to the sea, the manna, and the water out of the rock. Nothing had been required of them and nothing had been withheld."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And all the people answered **together, as one**: \"**We will do everything the LORD has said.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is emphatic — *all that the LORD has spoken we will do.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They say it before they have heard what it is. The commandments are not given until chapter 20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They said the same again after Sinai — *all the words which the LORD has said we will do* — and a third time when the covenant was ratified with blood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Six weeks later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A golden calf, while Moses was still up the mountain collecting the tablets. See the note on Deuteronomy 9:9–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What changed at that moment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before this exchange, God dealt with them on the basis of a promise made to Abraham. After it, on the basis of an agreement they proposed to keep."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The arrangement they accepted is the one Paul describes: *the man who does those things shall live by them.* See the note on Romans 10:5–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cloud"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am coming to you in a dense cloud**, so that the people will hear Me speaking with you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a thick cloud, a heavy darkness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what follows in the chapter is a mountain fenced off, with a boundary and a death penalty for touching it, thunder and lightning and a trumpet blast that grew louder and louder, and a mountain shaking and smoking like a furnace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the people trembled.* They ask Moses to speak instead: *let not God speak with us, lest we die.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Moses **told the LORD what the people had said**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He carries their answer up. The verse says it twice — he brought their words back — and the repetition is the writer's way of marking the moment as decisive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Hebrews sets it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have not come to the mountain that may be touched and that burned with fire, and to blackness and darkness and tempest… **But you have come to Mount Zion**, to the city of the living God… to Jesus the Mediator of the new covenant.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two mountains, and Hebrews says Christians are standing at the other one. See the note on Hebrews 12:18–24."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was on the mountain before the law.** *I bore you on eagles' wings and brought you to Myself.* That sentence was already true, without any promise from them, and it stayed true — the covenant they proposed did not create the relationship, and breaking it did not end it."}]}, "Exodus 2:1–10": {"title": "Drawn from the water", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The decree said put him in the Nile. She put him in the Nile."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Obedience of a kind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pharaoh's order was that every Hebrew boy be cast into the river. His mother hides the child three months, and when she cannot hide him any longer she does exactly what the law required — and seals him first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The basket"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A basket of **papyrus**, sealed with **tar and pitch**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word for the basket is **tebah**. It occurs in only one other place in the whole Bible: Noah's ark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the ordinary word for a box or a chest. The word for the vessel that carried one family through the water while everything else went under, used again for a container the length of a baby."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *sealed with tar and pitch* is what Noah was told to do to his — *cover it inside and out with pitch.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where she put it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Among the reeds along the bank of the Nile.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for reeds is **suph** — the same word in *Yam Suph*, the Sea of Reeds, which is the sea that will open for this child eighty years later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is drawn out of the reeds, and he brings a nation out through the reeds."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who comes down to the river"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **Pharaoh's daughter** came down to bathe at the river.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of everyone in Egypt who could have found that basket, it is found by the daughter of the man who signed the order."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Her heart went out to him.* And she names the problem out loud: *\"This is one of the Hebrew children.\"* She knows precisely what she is looking at and takes him anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sister"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The baby's sister **stepped forward**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Miriam has been standing at a distance watching. She waits for the right moment and speaks to a princess."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Shall I go and find a Hebrew woman to nurse the child for you?\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the mother got"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take this child and nurse him for me, **and I will pay you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is handed back her own son, with the protection of the palace over him, and Egypt pays her wages to raise him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had put him in the river because the law said so. She gets him back, legally, salaried, and safe."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She named him **Moses**, saying, \"I drew him out of the water.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Egyptian princess gives him a Hebrew name, or one that works in both languages. And it becomes the name of a man whose whole life is drawing people out of water."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the deliverance arrived.** No angel, no plague, no army. A basket, a princess taking a bath, a girl brave enough to speak, and a river the empire worshipped. God is named in this chapter only at the very end, when He hears the groaning — and by then He has already had the deliverer raised in Pharaoh's own house for forty years."}]}, "Exodus 20:18–21": {"title": "The people stood far off", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The law has just been spoken, and the first recorded response of the nation is to back away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they experienced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the people saw **the thunder and lightning**, heard **the trumpet**, and saw **the mountain wrapped in smoke**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *they saw the voices* — the same word for thunder and for voice. They saw the sound."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **trembled and stood far off**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *they moved and stood at a distance*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters earlier they had been told to wash their clothes and be ready on the third day. They came, and then went backward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You speak to us, and we will listen. But don't let God speak to us, or we will die**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They ask for a middleman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the origin of the office of the prophet — Moses recalls it in Deuteronomy, and God answers it there: *I will raise up for them a Prophet like you from among their brethren, and will put My words in His mouth.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had just heard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ten commandments, spoken aloud by God to the whole nation. It is the only time in Scripture He addresses an entire people directly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They asked Him not to do it again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Moses' answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Don't be afraid**. God has come **to test you**, so that **the fear of him will keep you from sinning**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two kinds of fear in one sentence, and Moses distinguishes them: do not be afraid, so that the fear of Him would stay with you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew uses the same root twice — *do not fear*, *that His fear may be before your faces*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two directions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **the people stayed at a distance**, while **Moses drew near** to the thick darkness where God was.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, two movements, opposite ways."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *thick darkness* is **araphel** — heavy cloud, gloom. It is the word used of the cloud on Sinai and of the darkness God is said to dwell in: *He made darkness His secret place.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses walked into"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thing the nation was certain would kill them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Hebrews takes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have not come to the mountain that may be touched and that burned with fire, and to blackness and darkness and tempest… **For they could not endure what was commanded**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But you have come to Mount Zion… to God the Judge of all, and to Jesus the Mediator of the new covenant.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two mountains, and the letter says which one its readers are standing at. See the note on Hebrews 12:18–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the veil later did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tabernacle formalised the distance the people asked for here — a curtain, a court, a priest, one man once a year."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Matthew records the curtain torn from top to bottom."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was on offer and what was asked for.** God spoke to the whole nation once, and the nation asked Him to stop and send somebody instead. Moses went in."}]}, "Exodus 20:3": {"title": "The first word", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first of the ten, and it is the one every other commandment sits on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am the LORD your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt, out of the house of bondage**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That sentence is verse 2, and Jewish tradition counts it as the first of the ten words — a statement rather than a command."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rescue is announced before anything is asked. The commands follow an introduction of who is speaking and what He has already done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You shall have **no other gods before me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **al panai** — before My face, in My presence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *ahead of Me in rank*. In My sight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Illumination adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let **no rival stand where I stand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am **the God who came for you**; keep your heart turned toward me alone.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything else in the list depends on it. Murder, theft, adultery and false witness are recognisable wrongs in any society; this one is the claim that makes the list a covenant rather than a legal code."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **ten words** — Hebrew **aseret hadevarim**, which is where *Decalogue* comes from. Exodus and Deuteronomy both call them words rather than laws."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who heard them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole nation, spoken aloud. It is the only time in Scripture God addresses an entire people directly, and their response was to ask Him to stop. See the note on Exodus 20:18–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was written"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Two tablets of stone, **written by the very finger of God**.* See the note on Exodus 31:18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they were broken before they reached the camp, because of what the nation was doing at the foot of the mountain while the writing was happening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the nation had said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"All that the LORD has spoken **we will do**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said before they knew what would be spoken. See the note on Exodus 19:7–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Jesus puts the first commandment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Asked which is the greatest, He does not quote Exodus 20. He quotes Deuteronomy 6:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You shall love the LORD your God with all your heart, with all your soul, and with all your mind**. This is the first and great commandment.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same content, stated as love rather than prohibition. See the note on Matthew 22:34–40."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the rich young ruler was asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus listed five commandments to him — do not commit adultery, do not murder, do not steal, do not bear false witness, honour your parents."}, {"k": "p", "t": "All five from the second table. The first table was not recited, and the one thing he was told he lacked was about what he was holding on to. See the note on Luke 18:18–23."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul locates the problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **exchanged the truth of God for the lie**, and worshipped and served the creature rather than the Creator.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts it at the head of his indictment in Romans 1, in the same position it holds here."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the sentence is about.** Not a ranking. The Hebrew is *before My face* — and the verse before it says who the face belongs to and what He had just done."}]}, "Exodus 21:1": {"title": "Rules set before them", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse of heading, and it introduces the oldest legal code in Scripture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These are **the rulings God gave Moses to set before the people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mishpatim** — judgments, decisions. The word comes from the root for judging, and it means case law: rulings on situations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The block that follows is called *the book of the covenant* in chapter 24, and it runs from here to the end of chapter 23."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Directly after the ten commandments and the people's request that God stop speaking to them. See the note on Exodus 20:18–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ten were spoken to the whole nation. These are given to Moses to set before them, which is the arrangement the people asked for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"To set before them\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *which you shall place before them*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not imposed from a distance. Laid out in front of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind of law it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Almost all of it is casuistic — *if a man does this, then that*. It reads like a collection of decided cases rather than a list of principles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ten commandments are apodictic: *you shall not*. These are the working-out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it covers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing after this verse is the Hebrew servant who may go free in the seventh year and chooses not to — the man whose ear is pierced at the doorpost. See the note on Exodus 21:2–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: injuries, an ox that gores, a pit left uncovered, theft, fire in a field, borrowed property, a garment taken in pledge, bribes, the sabbath year, the sabbath day, and three annual feasts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Oxen, pits, fires and coats."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that says about the code"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first case in it is a slave being offered his freedom, and the last chapter of it is about resting the land so the poor can eat and resting the household so the animals and the foreigner can breathe. See the notes on Exodus 23:10–11 and Exodus 23:12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bookends are both about people with no power."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word *plain*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Illumination calls them *plain, fair guidelines for how they were to live justly with one another*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The horizontal direction is right — the ten commandments open toward God and this block is almost entirely about the neighbour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens at the end of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 24: Moses reads the book of the covenant in the hearing of the people, and they say *all that the LORD has said we will do, and be obedient.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then blood is thrown on the people and on the altar: *this is the blood of the covenant which the LORD has made with you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews cites that moment: *not even the first covenant was dedicated without blood.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the ox-and-pit laws became"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus argued from them twice on sabbath days — *which one of you, having a donkey or an ox that has fallen into a pit, will not immediately pull him out?* See the note on Luke 14:1–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The case law about a pit in Exodus 21 is what He reaches for when the lawyers cannot answer him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the heading says the code is for.** Not ceremony and not devotion. Rulings, set in front of the people, about how they were to deal with each other."}]}, "Exodus 21:2–6": {"title": "The servant who would not go free", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first civil law given after the ten commandments, and it is about setting somebody free."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The right"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is to serve **six years**, and in the seventh year **he goes free, owing nothing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that he goes out free *for nothing* — no redemption price, no debt carried over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The very first case law in Israel's legal code is a limit on how long a man can be owned."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The complication"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If his master **gave him a wife** and she bore him sons or daughters, **the wife and children stay with the master**, and he goes out by himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the hard clause, and it is the one the whole passage turns on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Freedom is available, and it is available alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The choice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If the servant says plainly, \"**I love my master, my wife, and my children; I do not want to go free**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three loves named, and the master is first."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that he says it plainly — the verb implies a declaration, said out loud, so that everyone hears it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ceremony"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His master brings him **before God**, brings him **to the door or the doorpost**, and **pierces his ear with an awl**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three elements: God as witness, a doorway, and a permanent mark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ear is pierced against the door of the house he is choosing to stay in. He is literally fastened, for a moment, to the place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it makes him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From then on the servant belongs to that household **for life, by his own glad choice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A **bondservant** — the Hebrew and Greek both distinguish this from a slave. He was free, and chose not to be."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul opens his letters *Paul, a bondservant of Jesus Christ.* So do James, Peter and Jude. It is a title they claim."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm picks it up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sacrifice and offering You did not desire. **My ears You have opened**… Then I said, **see, I have come**… **I delight to do Your will**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 40, and the Hebrew is *ears You have dug for me* — the piercing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews quotes it and applies it to Christ coming into the world: *a body You have prepared for Me… behold, I have come to do Your will, O God.* See the note on Psalms 40:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two loves in the picture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The servant stays for the master and for the family he would otherwise leave behind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For even the Son of Man **did not come to be served, but to serve**, and to give His life a ransom for many.\"* See the note on Mark 10:41–45."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the law says anything about a servant's hands being marked. It is the ear — the part that hears an order."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the marks ended up.** *Behold My hands and My feet… reach your finger here, and look at My hands; and reach your hand here, and put it into My side.* The permanent marks of the one who would not go out free."}]}, "Exodus 21:28–32": {"title": "Thirty shekels of silver", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A law about a dangerous animal, and the last figure in it turns up again at a betrayal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If an ox gores a man or woman to death, **the ox is to be stoned** and its meat not eaten, and **the owner is cleared**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The animal is executed and the owner goes free. Nobody could have foreseen it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The meat is not eaten — the animal is not to be salvaged as property."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **if the ox had a habit of goring** and its owner **had been warned yet did not keep it penned**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two conditions: a known pattern, and a warning already given."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the ox is stoned and its owner too is to be put to death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The negligence is treated as capital."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ransom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If **a ransom is set for him**, he may **pay whatever is required to redeem his life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kopher** — a ransom, a covering price. It shares a root with **kaphar**, to atone, and with **kapporeth**, the mercy seat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A life is forfeit and a payment is accepted instead. That is the only place in the law where a capital sentence can be commuted for money."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The equality clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The same ruling holds whether the ox gores **a son or a daughter**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But if the ox gores **a manservant or a maidservant**, the owner pays **thirty shekels of silver** to the servant's master, and the ox is stoned.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A slave was somebody's property, so the compensation goes to the owner and the figure is fixed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty shekels is the valuation of a slave in this law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the figure turns up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah 11, in the strange passage where the prophet acts out a rejected shepherd and asks for his wages:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they weighed out for my wages **thirty pieces of silver**. And the LORD said to me, \"**Throw it to the potter**\" — that princely price they set on me. So I took the thirty pieces of silver and **threw them into the house of the LORD for the potter**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tone is contempt. It is what you pay for a slave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it turns up again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then one of the twelve, called Judas Iscariot, went to the chief priests and said, \"**What are you willing to give me if I deliver Him to you?**\" And they counted out to him **thirty pieces of silver**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And afterward: *he threw down the pieces of silver in the temple and departed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chief priests would not put it in the treasury *because it is the price of blood*, and bought **the potter's field** with it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew quotes Zechariah's line and says it was fulfilled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the price says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in the room negotiated. The chief priests named the sum, and the sum was the statutory compensation for a dead slave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Philippians says about the form"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who, being in the form of God… **made Himself of no reputation, taking the form of a bondservant**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **doulos** — a slave."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The figure in the law.** Thirty shekels, for a servant killed by somebody else's animal, paid to the owner. It is the only price in the Torah for a human life, and it is the one that was counted out in the temple."}]}, "Exodus 21:33–34": {"title": "The pit somebody left open", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses of ordinary liability law, and Jesus argues from them twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If a man **opens a pit, or digs one and does not cover it**, and **an ox or a donkey falls in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cisterns were cut into rock across that country to hold winter rain. An uncovered one is an invisible hole in the ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law names two acts — opening an existing pit and digging a new one — and treats them the same."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the fault is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in the digging. Pits were necessary; every household needed water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fault is in leaving it open."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The liability"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **owner of the pit must make it good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shall make it good* — **shalam**, from the root of **shalom**. To make whole, to complete, to restore what is missing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Restitution in this law is described as putting something back to wholeness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The settlement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **pays the animal's owner in silver**, and **the dead animal becomes his**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves. He pays full value and takes the carcass, so the loss transfers entirely and the injured party is not left holding a dead ox."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the surrounding law is like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter runs on cases like this: a goring ox, an ox that has gored before, an ox killed by another ox, a pit, a fire in a field, a borrowed animal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Farm accidents, with rulings."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Jesus uses it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If your donkey, or your ox, fell into a well on the Sabbath**, which one of you would leave him down there? **You would have him out that very moment**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to a room full of lawyers who had just watched Him heal a man on the sabbath and could not answer whether it was allowed. See the note on Luke 14:1–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in Matthew: *\"What man is there among you who has one sheep, and **if it falls into a pit on the Sabbath, will not lay hold of it and lift it out?** Of how much more value then is a man than a sheep?\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument's shape"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes a piece of livestock law they all knew and applies it upward: *of how much more value then is a man*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their own practice on the day in question is the premise. Nobody in the room disputed that they would pull the animal out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two animals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ox and the donkey appear together throughout the code — in this law, in the sabbath command, in the rule about a neighbour's beast fallen under its load."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are the working animals of a household. See the note on Exodus 23:12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the pit law assumes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That somebody made the hole. The law does not deal with an animal that wandered off and died; it deals with a hazard left uncovered by a person who knew it was there."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the ruling requires.** Full value in silver, and the carcass taken off the owner's hands. The Hebrew for making it good is the word for making whole — and it is the law Jesus reaches for when they will not answer him."}]}, "Exodus 22:1–4": {"title": "Five for an ox", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four verses of case law, and the arithmetic in them is deliberate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Book of the Covenant — the chapters immediately after the ten commandments, which apply them to cases."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You shall not steal* is one sentence. This is what the courts did with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rates"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If a man steals an **ox** or a **sheep** and **kills or sells it**, he must repay **five oxen** for the ox and **four sheep** for the sheep.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two different multiples for two different animals, and the higher one is for the working animal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An ox ploughs. Steal a man's ox and you have taken his next harvest as well as the beast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lower rate"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If the stolen animal is **found alive in his hands**, whether ox, donkey, or sheep, he **repays double**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five or four if it has been killed or sold; two if it is still standing in his yard."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scale tracks how far the theft has gone, not how bad the intention was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The night clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If a thief is **caught breaking in at night** and is struck so that he dies, **there is no bloodguilt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *no blood for him*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The daylight clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But if it happens in **daylight, there is bloodguilt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The distinction is about what the householder can know."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the dark he cannot see whether the intruder is armed or how many there are; in daylight he can, and killing him becomes homicide."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law protects the defender at night and the thief by day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What if he cannot pay"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A thief must make **full restitution**; if he has nothing, **he is to be sold** to pay for his theft.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not imprisonment. Labour, until the debt is worked off — and the Hebrew slave laws limit that to six years, with release in the seventh."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the Torah's penal code is a jail sentence. The penalties are restitution, labour, or death."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who gets paid"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The victim. Every shekel of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no fine to the state anywhere in the passage. The whole apparatus exists to put the injured party back where he was, and further."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other rate"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus sets a different figure for the man who confesses before he is caught: the value plus a fifth. See the note on Leviticus 6:1–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fourfold or fivefold when the court finds you. A hundred and twenty per cent when you come forward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the fourfold turns up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David, hearing Nathan's story about the rich man and the lamb: *he shall **restore fourfold** for the lamb, because he did this thing and had no pity.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is quoting this statute — and then Nathan says *you are the man*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Zacchaeus, at his own table: *if I have taken anything from anyone by false accusation, I restore fourfold*. See the note on Luke 19:1–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither man was compelled. Both named the number themselves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter goes on to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grazing another man's field, a fire that spreads to a neighbour's stack, goods left with a friend, a borrowed animal that dies."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, without a break: *you shall neither mistreat a stranger nor oppress him, for you were strangers in the land of Egypt*, and *you shall not afflict any widow or fatherless child*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The property law and the compassion law are in the same chapter with no seam between them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the multiples measure.** Not malice. An ox that has been killed costs five and one still standing in the yard costs two — the scale follows how much of the man's living went with it."}]}, "Exodus 22:7–9": {"title": "The one God declares guilty repays double", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A small piece of civil law about stolen property, and the word *double* appears twice in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book of the covenant — the block of case law that follows the ten commandments. The surrounding rules are about oxen, wells, borrowed animals, and fire spreading through a field."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ordinary disputes, decided in ordinary courts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If a man **entrusts his neighbor with money or goods for safekeeping**, and **they are stolen** from that neighbor's house.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bailment. Something is left with somebody for keeping and disappears."}, {"k": "h", "t": "If the thief is found"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then if the thief is found, **he repays double**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shenayim** — twofold."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Restitution in the law is nearly always more than the loss. Elsewhere in the same chapter it runs to four and five times for an ox or a sheep."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The principle is that theft costs the thief more than it cost the owner."}, {"k": "h", "t": "If he is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **owner of the house is brought before God** to swear he did not lay hands on his neighbor's property.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *brought to the judges*, and the word is **elohim** — the same word for God, used of those who judge in His name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oath settles it when the evidence cannot."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The general rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In every case of dispute — over **an ox, a donkey, a sheep, clothing, or any lost thing that one claims is his**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *that one claims is his* is *of which one says, \"This is it\"* — a man pointing at an object and saying it belongs to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The procedure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Both parties **bring their case before God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither side is presumed. Both come."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the one God declares guilty repays his neighbor double**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *declares guilty* is **rasha** — to condemn, to pronounce wicked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb appears twice in the passage: once as what the court does, and once naming the party it lands on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the ruling produces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody is condemned, and the other party ends up with twice what he lost."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two outcomes are the same sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where doubling turns up in the prophets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Instead of your shame you shall have **double honour**, and instead of confusion they shall rejoice in their portion. Therefore in their land **they shall possess double**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 61, in the chapter Jesus read in Nazareth. See the note on Isaiah 61:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Zechariah: *return to the stronghold, you prisoners of hope. Even today I declare that **I will restore double** to you.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Job received"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Indeed the LORD **gave Job twice as much as he had before**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book counts it: fourteen thousand sheep against seven, six thousand camels against three, and the same doubling through the list."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the accuser stands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah 3 has Joshua the high priest in filthy garments *and Satan standing at his right hand to oppose him*, and the LORD saying *the LORD rebuke you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *oppose* is **satan** — to accuse, to stand against in court."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Proverbs says about the reverse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Clearing the guilty and condemning the just, **the LORD hates them both alike**.* See the note on Proverbs 17:15–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both errors, equally."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The shape of the law.** Two parties come, God decides, and the party He condemns pays twice. The restoration and the condemnation are one ruling."}]}, "Exodus 23:10–11": {"title": "Rest for the land", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The shortest statement of the sabbath year, and the reason attached to it is about somebody else's stomach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the middle of the book of the covenant — the block of case law in Exodus 21 to 23, given straight after the ten commandments."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The laws around it are about oxen, wells, borrowed property, bribes and lawsuits."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The six"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For six years **sow your land and gather its harvest**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seventh"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But in the seventh year **let it rest and lie fallow**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew has two verbs — **shamat**, to release, to let drop, and **natash**, to abandon, to leave alone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Shamat** is the root of **shemittah**, the name the sabbath year still carries. It is also the word in Deuteronomy 15 for the release of debts in the same year."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Letting the field go and letting the debt go are the same word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **the poor among your people may eat from it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus gives the theological account — the land keeps a Sabbath to the LORD. See the note on Leviticus 25:1–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus gives the practical one, and it is about the people with no field."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes after them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **whatever they leave, the wild animals may have**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is: the owner does not touch it, the poor eat, and what is left goes to the animals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The owner is the only party in the sentence who gets nothing that year."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The extension"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do the same with **your vineyard and your olive grove**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Vines and olives, not just grain. Both are long-term crops and both need pruning; leaving them a year costs more than leaving a field."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is in the next verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Six days you shall do your work, and **on the seventh day you shall rest, that your ox and your donkey may rest, and the son of your female servant and the stranger may be refreshed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same shape at the scale of a week — and again the reason given is other people and animals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land gets one year in seven; the household gets one day in seven."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is unusual in the ancient world"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fallowing itself was known — leaving ground to recover is ordinary agriculture. What is unusual is fixing it to a calendar, releasing the produce to the public, and grounding it in a command rather than in soil science."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened to it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "2 Chronicles counts the exile as the sabbaths the land was owed: *as long as she lay desolate she kept Sabbath, to fulfil seventy years.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventy years of exile, one for each sabbath year not kept — which puts the neglect at four hundred and ninety years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the release goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 15, the same seventh year: *every creditor who has lent anything to his neighbour **shall release it**… **there shall be no poor among you**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two clauses.** Let it rest, and the poor eat. Exodus puts the fallow year and the food bank in one sentence and does not separate them."}]}, "Exodus 23:12": {"title": "\"So your ox and your donkey may rest\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The sabbath commandment restated, and this version gives the reason in terms of somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately after the sabbath year — six years of sowing and a seventh of letting the land go, so the poor may eat. See the note on Exodus 23:10–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same shape at two scales: one year in seven for the ground, one day in seven for the household."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Six days do your work, but **on the seventh day rest**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shabath** — to cease, to stop. The word is about stopping rather than about resting up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **your ox and your donkey may rest**, and **the son of your maidservant and the foreigner may be refreshed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four parties named, and the householder is not one of them."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**The ox and the donkey** — the working animals", "**The son of your maidservant** — a household slave born in the house", "**The foreigner** — the resident alien, with no legal standing"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list runs from livestock to the people with the least claim on the household. Every one of them is somebody who could not have taken a day off on their own account."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for refreshed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **naphash** — from **nephesh**, the soul, the breath. To take breath, to be re-souled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is used three times in the Old Testament. One of them is of God: *on the seventh day He rested and **was refreshed**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason in the other version"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus 20 grounds the sabbath in creation: *for in six days the LORD made the heavens and the earth… therefore the LORD blessed the Sabbath day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 5 grounds it in the exodus: *remember that you were a slave in the land of Egypt… **therefore** the LORD your God commanded you to keep the Sabbath day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And this one grounds it in the ox and the slave."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three statements of the same command with three different reasons attached."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Illumination adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This rest is a gift, meant to **breathe life back into everyone under your care**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not framed as an obligation discharged. Nothing in this verse is about worship, assembly or sacrifice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a labour law, and the beneficiaries are the ones with no power to claim it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus said about the day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The Sabbath was made for man, and not man for the Sabbath**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He healed on it repeatedly, arguing each time from what people would do for an animal on that day — an ox led to water, a donkey pulled out of a well. See the notes on Luke 13:10–17 and Luke 14:1–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is arguing from this verse. The animals are in the commandment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews does with the rest"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There remains therefore **a Sabbath rest for the people of God**. Anyone who has entered His rest has stopped working himself, the way God stopped when His own work was done.* See the note on Hebrews 4:8–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is missing from the verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The owner. He is the one being commanded and the only one whose benefit is not mentioned."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the command protects.** Not the householder's leisure. Two animals, a slave's son, and a foreigner — the four in the house who could not have stopped without being told to."}]}, "Exodus 23:24–26": {"title": "\"He will bless your bread and your water\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of the book of the covenant, and the promises attached to it are domestic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last paragraph of the case law that runs from Exodus 21 — oxen, pits, fires, borrowed property, the sabbath year and the sabbath day. See the note on Exodus 21:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the conclusion, and it comes with an angel: *behold, I send an Angel before you to keep you in the way… **beware of Him and obey His voice**… for My name is in Him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prohibition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do **not bow to their gods** or serve them or **follow their practices**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *nor do after their works*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things: worship, service, and imitation of practice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Instead, **tear their idols down and shatter their sacred pillars**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **matzevot** — standing stones, set up at Canaanite shrines."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Worship the LORD your God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *serve the LORD your God* — the same verb used of serving their gods two verses earlier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The service is not removed. It is redirected."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He will **bless your bread and your water**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a harvest and not wealth. Bread and water — the two things a household needed every day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blessing is put on the ordinary supply."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **take sickness away from among you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I will take sickness away from the midst of you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same promise as Exodus 15, with a name attached: *I will put none of these diseases on you which I have brought on the Egyptians. For **I am the LORD who heals you**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No woman in your land will **miscarry or be barren**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both. The Hebrew covers losing a child and never conceiving one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Barrenness is the recurring grief of the patriarchs — Sarah, Rebekah, Rachel, and later Hannah."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fourth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And I will **give you a full span of days**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the number of your days I will fulfil*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not endless life. A complete one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes with them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses after: *I will send My fear before you… I will send hornets before you… **little by little I will drive them out** from before you, until you have increased, and you inherit the land.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Little by little** — the reason given is agricultural: *lest the land become desolate and the beasts of the field become too numerous for you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The slowness is deliberate and explained."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the parallel promise says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD will keep every sickness far from you. He will not lay on you any of the terrible diseases of Egypt that you knew.* See the note on Deuteronomy 7:12–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the same list of blessings: *none among you, man or woman, will be childless, and none of your livestock barren*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two statements of the same terms, forty years apart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter has just finished saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sabbath year — *let it rest and lie fallow, so the poor among your people may eat from it* — and the sabbath day, for the ox and the donkey and the slave's son and the foreigner. See the notes on Exodus 23:10–11 and Exodus 23:12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rest for the land, rest for the household, and then bread, water, health and a full life."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the promises cover.** Not spectacle. Bread, water, no sickness, children, and a complete number of days — attached to a case-law code that had just spent three chapters on oxen and pits."}]}, "Exodus 25:10–16": {"title": "Wood and gold, and poles that never come out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first thing God tells Moses to build is not the tent. It is the box that goes inside it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Start with the ark.* Before the table, the lampstand, the curtains, the courtyard — the instructions begin at the innermost point and work outward."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The building starts where God will sit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two materials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Acacia wood** — the hard, durable timber of the desert, the only usable wood they had out there. And **pure gold**, inside and out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wood from the wilderness where they were, and gold, which in Scripture is the metal of what is divine and imperishable. The wood is not replaced by the gold or hidden by it. Both are in the finished object, inseparably."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Readers have long seen the two natures of Christ in that combination, and the instruction to overlay *inside and out* is part of why: gold on the visible surface and gold on the surface nobody would ever see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The poles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Slide them through the rings on the sides so the ark can be lifted and carried. Leave the poles in the rings — **they never come out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An odd, specific rule. The poles are not to be removed even when the ark is at rest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The practical effect is that the ark is permanently ready to move. Israel was about to spend forty years going where the cloud went, and the piece of furniture God sits over is built to travel at any hour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody had to prepare it. It was always packed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What went inside.** The Testimony — the tablets. Hebrews adds two more items that ended up in there: the jar of manna and Aaron's staff that budded. The bread He supplied, the priesthood He chose, and the law they broke, in one box, under a lid of mercy."}]}, "Exodus 25:17–22": {"title": "The mercy seat, and the place He chose to speak from", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God gives the design for the ark and then tells Moses where He will be found."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the lid was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kapporet** — from the verb to cover, to atone. It was not a decorative top. The lid *was* the place of atonement, which is why English has long called it the mercy seat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pure gold, hammered from one piece with the cherubim — not assembled. The angels and the covering are the same object."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was underneath it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Inside put the Testimony I'll give you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stone tablets. The commandments Israel had already broken before Moses got down the mountain lay inside the box, and the mercy seat lay over them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the arrangement: the law underneath, the covering above, and the blood sprinkled on the covering once a year."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the cherubim are looking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Facing each other, their eyes turned down toward the cover.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last time cherubim appeared they were at the gate of Eden with a flaming sword, guarding the way back. Here they are bent over a lid, looking down at the place where blood is applied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same creatures, different posture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence that is the point of the whole passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And this is where I'll meet you. From above that mercy seat, from the space between the two cherubim… Right there — that's where I choose to draw near and be heard.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not from the altar where the sacrifices burned, and not from the tablets. From the space above the covering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God names the address at which He will speak, and it is the one place in the tabernacle where mercy sits on top of the broken law."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul calls Jesus.** In Romans 3 he uses the same word — *hilasterion*, the term the Greek Old Testament uses for this lid. God set Him forth publicly as the mercy seat. What was hidden behind a curtain and visited once a year is the thing Paul says was displayed in the open."}]}, "Exodus 25:23–30": {"title": "The table of the Presence", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A dining table in the holy place, and the food on it is never described as being for God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is made of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A table of **acacia wood**… **overlay it with pure gold**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wood inside, gold outside — the same construction as the ark. The desert timber that does not rot, wrapped in the metal that does not tarnish."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Build **a rim around it a handbreadth wide**, with its own gold molding on top of that.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A raised lip, about four inches, running round the edge, with a border on top of that as well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is there so nothing on the table can slide off. Whatever is set on it stays on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The poles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Four gold rings… **to hold the poles for carrying**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The table was made to travel. Every piece of tabernacle furniture has rings and poles, because the whole thing moved whenever the cloud lifted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bread"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And on this table, **always — day after day — keep the bread of the Presence set out before Me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *bread of the face* — bread of the Presence, before His face."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve loaves, arranged in two rows, replaced every Sabbath, with frankincense set alongside. One loaf for each tribe, including the ones that would later be lost."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Always.* The table was never bare."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who eats it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus says: *it shall be Aaron's and his sons', and they shall eat it in a holy place.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bread sits before God for a week and is then eaten by the priests. Nothing is burned; nothing is destroyed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a meal in God's presence, with God's people at the table, in the room next to the ark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it turns up in the Gospels"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David and his men ate it when they were hungry and had nothing — *he entered the house of God and ate the showbread, which was not lawful for him to eat, nor for those who were with him, but only for the priests.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus brings that incident up Himself, to defend His disciples for picking grain on the Sabbath, and adds: *the Sabbath was made for man, and not man for the Sabbath.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He called Himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am the bread of life.* And: *I am the living bread which came down from heaven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The table with bread on it, permanently, before God's face, in a room lit only by the lampstand. See the note on Exodus 25:31–40."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern in that room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things stood in the holy place: the lampstand, the table of bread, and the altar of incense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Light, food, and prayer — and a curtain, with the ark on the other side of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Jesus said He was living on.** *My food is to do the will of Him who sent Me, and to finish His work.* Said in Samaria, to disciples who had gone to buy lunch, while a woman ran back to the town."}]}, "Exodus 25:31–40": {"title": "The golden lampstand", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only source of light in the holy place, and it is described as one object rather than seven."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Hammered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A lampstand of pure gold, **hammered out** — base, shaft, cups… **all of one solid piece**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **miqshah** — beaten work. It was not cast in a mould or assembled from parts. A talent of solid gold, worked with a hammer until it became this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Buds and branches and shaft — **all one seamless piece**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stress on its oneness is repeated three times in the passage. Six branches coming out of a central shaft, and the whole thing is one piece of metal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "No wood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything else in the tabernacle is acacia wood overlaid with gold — the ark, the table, the altar. The lampstand alone is gold all the way through, with no other substance in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Almond blossoms"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Cups shaped like **almond blossoms**, buds and petals.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-two of them in total, up the shaft and along the branches."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The almond is the first tree in that country to flower, before its own leaves and before anything else. Jeremiah's first vision is an almond branch, and God tells him *I am watching over My word to perform it* — a play on the Hebrew, since the word for almond is *shaqed*, the watcher."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Aaron's rod that budded was an almond rod. It was dead wood that blossomed overnight, and it was kept in the ark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The light goes forward"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Set them up so their **light spills forward**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that the lamps give light *over against* the lampstand — the flames are angled to illuminate what is in front, not the stand itself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lampstand exists to light the room and the table of showbread opposite it, not to be looked at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was in that room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There were no windows in the holy place. No natural light reached it at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever a priest did in there — the bread, the incense — he did by this light and no other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The talent of gold"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Use **a full talent of pure gold** for the lampstand and all its tools.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Around seventy-five pounds of gold, worked by hand into one object."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it turns up again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I turned around… and there they were: **seven lampstands, all of them gold**. In among them stood **a figure like the Son of man**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation 1. Seven separate lampstands now — the seven churches — and He is standing in the middle of them. See the note on Revelation 1:12–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one that was beaten out of a single piece has become seven, with Him among them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line of the instructions.** *Be sure you make it all **to the pattern you were shown on the mountain**.* Said twice in this chapter. Hebrews picks it up: the priests serve *the copy and shadow of the heavenly things*. Moses was shown something and told to reproduce it."}]}, "Exodus 26:15–25": {"title": "The frames and the silver sockets", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The walls of the tabernacle, and what they stand on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are made of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Upright frames of **acacia wood** to hold the tabernacle upright.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Acacia is the desert tree — hard, thorny, slow-growing, and it does not rot. It was the only usable timber in that wilderness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each frame is *ten cubits tall and a cubit and a half wide* — about fifteen feet high and just over two across."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elsewhere in the chapter they are overlaid with gold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pegs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **two pegs at the bottom set side by side** for joining — every frame made the same way.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is two hands, two tenons. Each board has exactly two, and they are what hold it in place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they stand in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Resting on **forty silver bases — two bases under each frame, one for each peg**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not driven into the sand. Every board sits in its own pair of silver sockets, and nothing touches the ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ninety-six sockets in total, one under each tenon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the silver came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus 30 and 38 give it. Every man twenty and over paid a half-shekel at the census:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The rich shall not give more and the poor shall not give less** than half a shekel, when you give an offering to the LORD, **to make atonement for yourselves**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is called *the atonement money*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Exodus 38 records what was done with it: *the hundred talents of silver were for casting the sockets of the sanctuary.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The foundation the whole tent stands on is redemption money, at a flat rate, from every man alike."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The equal price"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rich paid the same as the poor. Nobody had a larger share of the foundation, and nobody had a smaller one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter uses the same idea in his own vocabulary: *you were not redeemed with corruptible things, like silver or gold… but with the precious blood of Christ.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The frames, and what they were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Desert wood, cut, squared, covered in gold, standing upright in silver."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the timber is impressive on its own. It is what it is standing in that holds it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty boards on the south, twenty on the north, and eight at the back — with the two corner frames doubled and fitted into a single ring."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty-eight frames, held by ninety-six sockets, carrying the curtains that made the one place on earth God said He would meet people."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What it was all designed to hold up.** Ten curtains of fine linen, blue, purple and scarlet, with cherubim worked into them — and behind the veil, the ark. The boards are structure. Nobody was meant to be looking at them."}]}, "Exodus 28:30": {"title": "Lights and perfections", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and the two objects in it are never described."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they go"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Into **the breastpiece of judgment**, place the Urim and the Thummim.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The breastpiece is the folded square of gold, blue, purple and scarlet with twelve engraved stones in four rows. See the note on Exodus 39:8–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is folded double, which makes it a pouch. The Urim and Thummim go inside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Urim** — from **or**, light. Plural: lights."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Thummim** — from **tamam**, to be complete. Plural: perfections, or completenesses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two words begin with the first and last letters of the Hebrew alphabet, aleph and tav."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody knows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Scripture never describes them. No dimensions, no material, no picture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The usual guesses are two stones or lots, drawn or cast, giving a yes or a no. The narrative usage fits that: questions put to God through the priest come back with binary answers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text is silent, and the silence is worth respecting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they rest"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So they rest **over Aaron's heart** every time he comes before the LORD.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before says the same of the stones: *so Aaron shall bear the names of the sons of Israel on the breastplate of judgment **over his heart**, when he goes into the holy place*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve names and the means of decision, in the same pouch, on the same chest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Aaron always carries **the means of decision for the Israelites** over his heart.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mishpat** — judgment, decision, verdict."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The breastpiece itself is named for it: **choshen mishpat**, the breastplate of judgment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How they were used"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He shall stand before Eleazar the priest, who shall **enquire before the LORD for him by the judgment of the Urim**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua's commissioning, and the arrangement is that even the successor of Moses asks through a priest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul uses it: *therefore Saul said to the LORD God of Israel, \"Give a perfect lot\"* — and again, in his last days, when it stops working: *when Saul enquired of the LORD, the LORD did not answer him, either by dreams or by Urim or by the prophets*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When they disappear"}, {"k": "p", "t": "After the exile, the returning community hits a problem: some priests could not prove their descent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the governor said to them that they should not eat of the most holy things **till a priest could consult with the Urim and Thummim**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are waiting for something they no longer have."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the last mention in the Old Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the position is significant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Over the heart, and only when he goes in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not consulted at a distance. The priest carries the twelve names and the means of decision into the presence together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other pieces on him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two onyx stones on the shoulders, six tribal names on each — *Aaron shall bear their names before the LORD on his two shoulders as a memorial*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Shoulders and heart. The strength and the affection, and the same twelve names on both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the New Testament puts it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seeing then that we have a great High Priest who has passed through the heavens, Jesus the Son of God, let us hold fast our confession.* See the note on Hebrews 4:14–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *He always lives to make intercession for them*. See the note on Hebrews 7:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The names are still being carried in."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Scripture never tells us.** What they were. No size, no material, no picture — only where they sat, and that a priest wore them over his heart whenever he went in."}]}, "Exodus 28:36–38": {"title": "The gold plate on the forehead", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last item in the high priest's clothing, and it exists to solve a problem nobody usually thinks about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Make a plate of pure gold and engrave on it, as on a seal: HOLY TO THE LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Worn on the forehead, tied with a blue cord to the front of the turban. The first thing anyone saw when the high priest faced them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem it addresses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That way Aaron carries the weight of any guilt tied to the sacred gifts the Israelites set apart, whatever their holy offerings may be.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read that again. There was guilt attached to **the holy offerings themselves** — the gifts people brought, the worship they gave. Even the best thing Israel brought God needed something to cover it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a startling admission built into the system. Not only their sins, but their sacrifices, required a bearer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who carried it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aaron. On his forehead. Continuously — *keep it always on his forehead*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The high priest wore the guilt of the nation's worship and the words HOLY TO THE LORD in the same place, at the same time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the people and their gifts are welcomed before the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is about acceptance — being received with favour. The plate was not decoration. It was the mechanism by which imperfect worship from imperfect people was made welcome."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why it matters for prayer.** Hebrews argues at length that we now have a great high priest who has gone into heaven itself and appears before God on our account. What Aaron wore on a gold plate, the letter says Christ *is* — which means the acceptance of what we bring never depended on the quality of the bringing. See the notes on Hebrews 9:23–24 and Hebrews 4:14–16."}]}, "Exodus 3:1–3": {"title": "The bush that would not burn up", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Forty years of sheep, and then a shrub."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses was **tending the flock of his father-in-law Jethro**, the priest of Midian.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man raised in Pharaoh's household is working for his wife's father."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had killed an Egyptian, buried him in the sand, and run when it came out. Stephen dates it: forty years in Egypt, forty in Midian, forty in the wilderness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jethro is the same man who later tells him he is doing too much. See the note on Exodus 18:19–23."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How far out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One day he led the sheep out **past the edge of the wilderness** and came to **Horeb, the mountain of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the back side of the desert*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Horeb is Sinai. He will bring two million people back to this exact mountain, and the sign God gives him a few verses later is *when you have brought the people out of Egypt, you shall serve God on this mountain*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proof of the call is only verifiable after obeying it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The appearance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There **the angel of the LORD appeared to him in a flame of fire, from the heart of a bush**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *bush* is **seneh** — a thornbush. Not a tree, and not impressive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same three-letter root that gives the mountain its other name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses looked — and the bush was on fire, **burning and burning, yet it was not burning up**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a participle: burning, continuous, and *the bush was not consumed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fire that does not consume. Every other fire in Exodus does — the plague of hail, the sacrifices, the mountain itself when God descends on it *in fire*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have to go closer**,\" Moses said to himself. \"**I have to see this strange thing. Why doesn't the bush turn to ash?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I will now turn aside*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was at work with the sheep. The whole sequence turns on a shepherd deciding to walk off his route."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God waited for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the LORD saw him **turn aside to look**, God called out to him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The voice comes after the turning, not before. See the note on Exodus 3:4–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is in a thornbush"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thorns are what the ground produced after Genesis 3. *Cursed is the ground for your sake… both thorns and thistles it shall bring forth for you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And there is a crown of them, at the end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God speaks out of the cursed thing, and the cursed thing is not destroyed by the fire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Moses' story goes from here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five objections in a row — *who am I*, *what is Your name*, *they will not believe me*, *I am slow of speech*, *send somebody else* — and God answers all five."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name given at this bush is *I AM WHO I AM*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus called it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Arguing with the Sadducees about resurrection: *but even Moses showed in the burning bush passage that the dead are raised, when he called the Lord \"the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob.\" For He is not the God of the dead but of the living.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He treats the bush scene as an argument about who is alive."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What made him stop.** Not the fire — a shepherd in that country would have seen scrub burn before. The thornbush was still there afterwards."}]}, "Exodus 3:11–12": {"title": "Who am I", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He asks the wrong question and gets an answer to a different one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At a bush that burns and is not consumed, on the far side of the desert, forty years into shepherding somebody else's sheep."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have surely seen the oppression of My people… **Come now, therefore, and I will send you to Pharaoh**.\"* See the note on Exodus 3:7–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Who am I**, that I should go to Pharaoh — that I should bring the Israelites out of Egypt?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mi anochi**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is a fair question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is eighty. He left Egypt forty years ago as a fugitive, having killed a man and buried him in the sand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Israelites had already rejected him once: *who made you a prince and a judge over us?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has no standing in Egypt and no standing with the people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And God said, \"**I will be with you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ki-ehyeh immach** — for I will be with you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not answered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses asked about himself and God does not describe him, correct him, or reassure him about his qualifications."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The subject is changed from *who am I* to *I will be*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ehyeh** — I will be."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verses later, when Moses asks what name to give, the answer uses the same word: *I AM WHO I AM* — **Ehyeh asher Ehyeh**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer to *who am I* and the answer to *what is Your name* are the same Hebrew verb. See the note on Exodus 3:13–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sign"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And here is the sign that it is I who sent you: **when you have brought the people out of Egypt, you will worship God on this very mountain**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem with the sign"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It comes afterwards."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A sign is normally something given to confirm a commission before you act on it. Gideon asked for a fleece and got it that night. Hezekiah got the shadow moving backwards while he was still in bed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This one can only be verified once the whole thing is done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses is being asked to go to Pharaoh on the strength of *I will be with you*, and the confirmation is a worship service on this mountain at some point in the future."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens on that mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus 19. The same Horeb, with the whole nation at the foot of it, thunder and lightning and a thick cloud, and Moses going up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sign is fulfilled, and the book records it happening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest of the objections"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses raises four more."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Suppose they will not believe me.* — the staff, the hand, the water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am not eloquent… I am slow of speech and slow of tongue.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*O my Lord, please send by the hand of whomever else You may send.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the anger of the LORD was kindled against Moses*, and Aaron is appointed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five objections in one conversation, and only the last one draws anger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the objections are about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of them is about Moses. God's answers are almost never about Moses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will be with you**.\" \"**I AM WHO I AM**.\" \"**Who has made man's mouth?**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By faith Moses, when he became of age, refused to be called the son of Pharaoh's daughter… esteeming the reproach of Christ greater riches than the treasures in Egypt.* See the note on Hebrews 11:24–26."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The question and the answer.** He asks *who am I*, and gets *I will be*. The subject of the sentence is changed, and the same verb turns up three verses later as the name."}]}, "Exodus 3:13–15": {"title": "\"I AM\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses asks for a name and gets one that is a sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They ask me, 'What is his name?' **What shall I tell them?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A reasonable question in that world. Every nation had gods with names, and a name was what you had to know in order to call on one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I AM WHO I AM.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ehyeh asher ehyeh** — the verb *to be*, first person, twice, with *who* between them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a description of anything. It is not tied to a place, a function, a mountain, a season or an element, which is what every other name in the ancient world was tied to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the short form"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Say to the Israelites: **'I AM has sent me to you.'**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes His own sentence and gives Moses one word out of it to carry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then the third form"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Say to the Israelites, '**the LORD**, the God of your fathers — the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob — has sent me to you.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The LORD**, in capitals, is **YHWH**, and it is built from the same verb. The name and the sentence are the same root."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He attaches it immediately to three men: Abraham, Isaac, Jacob. The name that is pure existence is given to Moses fastened to a family."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"This is My name forever\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The name you are to remember Me by **from generation to generation**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrew for *remember* here is the memorial word — the name is given as something to be kept in mind, generation after generation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the name turns up hidden"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the Hebrew of Esther 7:5 — the verse where the king demands *who is he, where is the man who has dared in his heart to do such a thing?* — the letters of **EHYEH**, *I AM*, stand at the start of four consecutive words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name given at a burning bush is written into a Persian banquet hall, in the sentence asking who is behind all this, in a book that never says God once."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **YHWH** is formed the same way four times elsewhere in that book, twice forwards and twice backwards."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He said it was His name forever. It is in the one book where nobody speaks it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Jesus does with it.** *Before Abraham was, I AM.* They picked up stones, because they understood exactly which sentence He had just used. And in the garden, when the soldiers say they are looking for Jesus of Nazareth and He answers *I am* — they drew back and fell to the ground. See the note on Esther 7:5–6."}]}, "Exodus 3:16–18": {"title": "A word for the elders", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses is given the message and told, in advance, what the response will be."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The errand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go, gather the elders of Israel together**, and tell them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not Pharaoh first. The elders."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The credentials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD, the God of your fathers, has appeared to me — the God of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three names. The elders would know them; the God of the burning bush is being identified by the family He had dealt with four hundred years earlier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses had asked for this: *when they say to me, \"What is His name?\" what shall I say to them?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And had been given **I AM WHO I AM** — and then this, which is the version for the message."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have watched over you**, and **I have seen what is being done to you in Egypt**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *watched over* is **paqad** — to visit, to attend to, to take account of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word used of God visiting Sarah, and of Joseph's promise: *God will surely visit you, and bring you out of this land*. See the note on Genesis 50:15–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph used it four hundred years earlier. Moses is now told to use it, and the elders would have recognised it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **I promise to bring you up out of your misery in Egypt into the land**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Up out of**, and **into**. Both halves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The six nations"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Canaanites, the Hittites, the Amorites, the Perizzites, the Hivites, and the Jebusites.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list is given so nobody thinks the land is empty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**A land flowing with milk and honey**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase enters Scripture here and is used about twenty times afterward."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Milk means pasture; honey means flowers. It is a description of a country that supports grazing and growing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The guarantee about the response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The elders will listen to you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Told in advance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses' next line, in chapter 4, is *but suppose they will not believe me* — and God gives him three signs anyway."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus 4 records what actually happened: *so the people believed; and when they heard that the LORD had visited the children of Israel and that He had looked on their affliction, then they bowed their heads and worshipped.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Visited** — the same word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request to Pharaoh"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now please **let us take a three-day journey into the wilderness** to offer sacrifices to the LORD our God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A limited, reasonable ask. It is what Moses is instructed to say, and it is the same request repeated through the plagues."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pharaoh's answers narrow it each time: go and sacrifice in the land; go but not far; go but leave the children; go but leave the flocks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The next sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **I am sure that the king of Egypt will not let you go, no, not even by a mighty hand**. So I will stretch out My hand and strike Egypt.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is told the elders will listen and that Pharaoh will not, before either happens."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Moses is given.** A message, a name from four centuries earlier, and the outcome of both audiences in advance — that the elders will hear him and the king will not."}]}, "Exodus 3:4–6": {"title": "Do not come any nearer", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first thing God says to Moses is his name twice, and the second thing is stop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trigger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the LORD **saw him turn aside to look**, God called out to him from the middle of the bush.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The turning came first. See the note on Exodus 3:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The doubled name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Moses! Moses!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The doubling happens a handful of times in the Bible, and it always marks the same kind of moment: *Abraham, Abraham* on the mountain with the knife raised; *Samuel, Samuel* in the dark; *Saul, Saul* on the Damascus road; *Simon, Simon, Satan has asked for you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *Martha, Martha*, over dinner."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Here I am**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hinneni** — behold me. One word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word Abraham says three times in Genesis 22, and Isaiah says in the temple."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not come any nearer**,\" God said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *do not draw near here*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said *I will now turn aside*, and he is stopped before he arrives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sandals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take off your sandals**, for the ground where you are standing is **holy ground**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *holy* is **qodesh** — the word's first appearance applied to a place in Scripture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Israelite law the sandal carried a man's claim. Boaz's redemption is sealed by a shoe handed over; the ceremony in Deuteronomy for a man who refuses to redeem is to have his sandal pulled off in public."}, {"k": "p", "t": "To take the sandals off is to stand there with no claim on the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am the God of your father** — the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Singular: *your father*. He starts personal and then goes back three generations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred years since Jacob went down to Egypt, and He introduces Himself by the men He walked with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At that Moses **hid his face**, because he was **afraid to look at God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He came to see, and ends up covering his eyes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Later he asks *please, show me Your glory*, and is put in a cleft of the rock with a hand over his face."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the other son heard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A younger son comes home in rags with a rehearsed speech, and his father *ran and fell on his neck and kissed him* — then *put shoes on his feet*. See the note on Luke 15:17–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One is told not to come near and to take his sandals off. The other is run at, and sandals are put on him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the boundary goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sinai keeps the same rule. *Set bounds for the people all around, saying, \"Take heed to yourselves that you do not go up to the mountain or touch its base.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Hebrews sets it against the other mountain: *for you have not come to the mountain that may be touched… but you have come to Mount Zion*. See the note on Hebrews 12:18–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What tore"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the crucifixion, *the veil of the temple was torn in two from top to bottom* — and the writer of Hebrews calls it *a new and living way which He consecrated for us, through the veil, that is, His flesh*, with the invitation *let us draw near*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb the bush forbade."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two sentences.** *Do not draw near*, at the bush; *let us draw near with a true heart*, in Hebrews. Nothing changed about the holiness of the ground — something changed about the standing of the man on it."}]}, "Exodus 3:7–10": {"title": "\"I have seen. I have heard. I know. I have come down.\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred years of silence, and then four verbs in a row."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have seen it — I have truly seen the misery of my people… I have heard them crying out… **and I know their pain**. So I have come down to rescue them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seen, heard, known, come down. The Hebrew doubles the first — *seeing I have seen* — which is how that language says *thoroughly*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third is the one to sit with. **I know their pain.** Not *I am aware of the situation*. The verb is the ordinary word for knowing by experience, the same one used of intimacy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He was doing during the silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the chapter suggests He had been absent. The claim is the opposite: He had been watching the whole time, and the seeing is recounted before any action is described."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That matters for a people who had been in Egypt four centuries and would reasonably have concluded otherwise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He is bringing them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Out of that land into a good land, a wide-open land, a land flowing with milk and honey.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *wide-open* is spacious, broad — a word used elsewhere of being brought out into a large place after being hemmed in. Milk and honey is the shorthand for a land that produces without being forced: pasture rich enough for herds, and wildflowers enough for bees."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not describe it as adequate. He describes it as generous."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now go. I am sending you to Pharaoh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verbs of divine action, and then the fifth verb is Moses'. The rescue God has just described in the first person arrives through a reluctant eighty-year-old with a stick."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not offered.** Nowhere is Egypt made comfortable. The answer to the misery is not improved conditions in the place they were in — it is out, and then up into somewhere else entirely."}]}, "Exodus 30:34–38": {"title": "The holy incense", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A recipe with four ingredients, a rule about proportions, and a prohibition on making it for yourself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take fragrant spices — **gum resin, onycha, and galbanum** — along with **pure frankincense**.\"*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Gum resin** — the Hebrew is **nataph**, *a drop*. It is the sap that bleeds out of a cut in the bark and hardens. The word for dropping is used of speech in the Song of Solomon and of the prophets' oracles.", "**Onycha** — **shecheleth**. Taken from a shellfish, and it gives off its scent only when crushed and burned.", "**Galbanum** — **chelbenah**, from **cheleb**, fat — the word used for the choicest portion, the part of the sacrifice that belonged to God. On its own galbanum is bitter and unpleasant. In the blend it fixes the other scents so they last.", "**Frankincense** — **lebonah**, from the root for *white*. The only one specified as **pure**, and the one that is already fragrant without being burned."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three of the four have to be wounded, crushed or burned to give anything off."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proportions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All **in equal amounts**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *part for part shall it be*. No ingredient dominates, including the bitter one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The maker"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blend them into a fragrant incense, **the work of a skilled perfumer**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *rokeach* — a compounder of ointments, a professional."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The salt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seasoned with **salt**, pure and holy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Salt goes into it, as it goes into every grain offering. See the note on Leviticus 2:11–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Grind some of it into **fine powder** and set it **in front of the Testimony in the Tent of Meeting, where I'll meet with you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Testimony is the ark with the tablets in it. The incense stands directly in front of the place God said He would meet them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The golden altar of incense stood immediately outside the veil, and the smoke went over it into the Most Holy Place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Most holy\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Treat it as **most holy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *holy of holies* — the same phrase as the innermost room. Of the things made for the tabernacle, only a few carry it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prohibition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Never make incense with this formula for your own use**… Anyone who makes any like it **just to enjoy the scent** is to be **cut off from his people**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *to enjoy the scent* is *to smell it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It could not be duplicated, sold, worn, or kept at home. There was one blend and one place to burn it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who burned it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priest, morning and evening, at the golden altar. Uzziah the king tried it and came out of the temple a leper. Two of Aaron's sons offered fire that was not from the altar and died."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And two hundred and fifty men stood at the tent door with censers, and fire came out. See the note on Numbers 16:16–35."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same substance, in the wrong hand, kills."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it becomes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The four creatures and the twenty-four elders… every one of them had a harp, and **a gold bowl full of burning incense — and the incense is the prayers of God's people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on Revelation 5:7–8, and on Psalms 141:1–2, where David asks for exactly this."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the recipe is.** Four things that mostly stink or do nothing until they are crushed and set on fire, in equal parts, blended by an expert — and the result is what stands in front of the place God said He would meet them."}]}, "Exodus 31:1–5": {"title": "Bezalel, chosen by name", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first man in Scripture said to be filled with the Spirit of God is a craftsman."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The naming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look — **I've picked a man out by name**: **Bezalel, son of Uri, grandson of Hur, from the tribe of Judah**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I have called by name*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three generations given, and the tribe. He is a specific person, not a category."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hur** is likely the man who held up Moses' other arm at Rephidim while Aaron held the first. See the note on Exodus 17:8–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Bezalel** means *in the shadow of God*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The filling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I've filled him with My own Spirit**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I have filled him with the Spirit of God* — **ruach Elohim**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the first time the phrase is used of a person in the Bible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a prophet, a priest or a king. A man who works metal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the filling consists of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"With **wisdom, understanding, and skill for every kind of craft**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew names three: **chokmah** (wisdom), **tebunah** (understanding), and **daath** (knowledge) — and then *in all manner of workmanship*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same three nouns Proverbs uses of how the world was made: *the LORD by wisdom founded the earth, by understanding He established the heavens; by His knowledge the depths were broken up.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He can **dream up beautiful designs** and work them in **gold, silver, and bronze**. He can **cut and set precious stones**, **carve wood**, and **turn his hand to any art at all**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *dream up designs* is **lachshov machashavot** — to devise devices, to think out designs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three metals, stonecutting, and woodwork. Four trades in one man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tabernacle. The ark, the mercy seat with the cherubim, the table, the lampstand, the altars, the laver, and the priest's garments — everything in the tent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapters 25 to 31 are the specification. Bezalel is the one who executes it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And I have appointed with him **Aholiab the son of Ahisamach, of the tribe of Dan**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah and Dan — the first tribe and one of the last. The pairing is deliberate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest of the workforce"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **in the hearts of all who are gifted artisans I have put wisdom**, that they may make all that I have commanded you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everybody working on the tent is described the same way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the offering came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The materials had been given: gold, silver, bronze, blue and purple and scarlet thread, fine linen, goats' hair, ram skins, acacia wood, oil, spices and onyx."}, {"k": "p", "t": "All of it out of Egypt, from the morning they left. See the note on Exodus 12:33–36."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one problem in the project"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people brought too much. *So Moses gave a commandment… \"**Let neither man nor woman do any more work for the offering of the sanctuary**.\" And the people were restrained from bringing, for the material they had was sufficient — and more than enough.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sabbath. Immediately after the craftsman's commission comes *surely My Sabbaths you shall keep* — and then the two tablets, written by the finger of God. See the note on Exodus 31:18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Work, rest, and the writing, in that order, at the end of the chapter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who is filled first.** Not a prophet. A man named to his grandfather, from Judah, who could cut stone and carve wood and beat gold — and the text says God filled him with His own Spirit to do it."}]}, "Exodus 31:18": {"title": "Written by the finger of God", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it closes forty days on a mountain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The moment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when the LORD **finished speaking** with Moses on Mount Sinai, He gave him **the two tablets of the covenant**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything from chapter 20 to here — the law, the ordinances, the tabernacle pattern, the priesthood, the Sabbath — has been spoken. This verse ends it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Stone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Tablets of **stone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not scroll, not clay. The hardest available writing surface, and the one you cannot amend."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The finger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Written by **the very finger of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *written with the finger of God* — the same phrase the magicians of Egypt used when they gave up: *this is the finger of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke uses it too: *if I cast out demons **with the finger of God**, surely the kingdom of God has come upon you* — where Matthew's parallel has *by the Spirit of God*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two tablets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The usual picture is a division of subject — God on one, neighbour on the other. Ancient treaty practice suggests something else: two copies of the same document, one for each party, both kept in the shrine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both tablets went into the ark. Neither party took theirs home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is happening at the foot of the mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *when the people saw that Moses delayed coming down… they said, \"Make us gods that shall go before us.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "While the finger of God is writing, Aaron is taking the earrings."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses comes down, sees the calf, and *cast the tablets out of his hands and broke them at the foot of the mountain.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The document written by God's own finger did not survive the walk down. See the note on Exodus 32:15–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second set"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Cut two tablets of stone like the first ones, **and I will write on them** the words that were on the first tablets which you broke.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This time Moses does the cutting and God does the writing. And these are the ones that went into the ark and lasted."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the writing moves to.** *I will put My laws inside their hearts, and I will write them on their minds.* Same author, same verb, different surface — and a surface that does not shatter on the way down a hill. See the note on Hebrews 10:15–17."}]}, "Exodus 32:1–4": {"title": "The golden calf", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Forty days is too long, and the gold comes off."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Moses is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On the mountain, receiving the specification for the tabernacle — the ark, the table, the lampstand, the priestly garments, the anointing oil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapters 25 to 31 are what he is being given while chapter 32 happens below."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two scenes are simultaneous, and the book puts them side by side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Down below, the people saw that **Moses was taking a long time coming down**, and **they grew restless**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **boshesh** — delayed, tarried."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty days. He had said he was going up; he had not said for how long."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gathering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **gathered around Aaron**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyiqqahel ha'am al-Aharon** — the verb carries a hint of assembling against."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The demand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Come on — make us gods to lead the way**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **asei-lanu elohim asher yelchu lephanenu** — make us gods who will go before us."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is what the pillar of cloud had been doing, every day, since the sea."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This Moses, the man who brought us up out of Egypt** — we have no idea what has become of him.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**This Moses** — dismissive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *the man who brought us up out of Egypt*, which is a sentence God has spent thirteen chapters correcting. The credit is assigned to the man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Aaron"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Aaron told them, \"**Take off the gold earrings your wives and sons and daughters are wearing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not refuse. He does not delay. He gives an instruction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some read the demand for their families' jewellery as a stalling tactic. If so, it fails immediately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The compliance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they **stripped off their gold and handed it over**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyitparequ** — they tore off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No hesitation anywhere in the sequence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the gold came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD had given the people favour in the sight of the Egyptians… **thus they plundered the Egyptians**.* See the note on Exodus 12:33–36."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wages for four hundred and thirty years, handed over on the night they left."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is made"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Aaron took it, **melted it down, shaped it with a tool**, and made it into the form of a calf.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyatzar oto bacheret** — and he fashioned it with an engraving tool."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aaron later tells Moses *I cast it into the fire, and this calf came out*. The narrative has already said otherwise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why a calf"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt had the Apis bull. Canaan had the bull as the mount of Baal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do not invent a new god. They reach for the nearest available shape."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The declaration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Here are your gods, Israel, who brought you up out of Egypt!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same claim they had just made about Moses, transferred to an object made an hour ago."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Aaron does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Builds an altar in front of it and announces *tomorrow is a feast to the LORD* — **chag laYHWH**, using the covenant name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The intention is not to abandon God. It is to represent Him, which is exactly what the second commandment forbade about a month earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the same gold becomes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four chapters later: brooches, earrings, rings and pendants, brought for the tabernacle, until Moses has to order the giving stopped. See the note on Exodus 36:4–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same metal, from the same ears, going two directions in one book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who stands in the gap"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now therefore, let Me alone, that My wrath may burn hot against them… **and I will make of you a great nation**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Moses pleaded with the LORD his God… \"**Yet now, if You will forgive their sin — but if not, I pray, blot me out of Your book**.\"* See the note on Exodus 32:30–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is offered what Abraham was promised, and asks to be erased instead."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two scenes.** Chapters 25 to 31 are the plan for God's dwelling among them, given on the mountain. Chapter 32 is what was happening at the bottom of it at the same time."}]}, "Exodus 32:25–29": {"title": "Three thousand, at the giving of the law", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The day the tablets came down the mountain, and what happened at the bottom of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses saw that the people were running wild — Aaron had let them get out of control.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been at Sinai for weeks. Moses was on the mountain receiving the law; the camp had made a calf and was holding a festival for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *running wild* suggests being let loose, unrestrained — and the text puts the responsibility on Aaron for permitting it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line drawn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever is for the LORD, come to me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A call to choose sides, in the open, in front of everyone. The Levites come."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What follows is grim and the text does not soften it: swords through the camp, gate to gate, and about three thousand dead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three thousand, on the day the law was given."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That figure appears once more in Scripture, and the setting is deliberate: Pentecost — by tradition the anniversary of Sinai — when Peter preaches and *about three thousand people were added* to them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same festival, same number, opposite direction. Luke was writing for readers who knew Exodus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses says afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Today you have been set apart to the LORD… And so a blessing has come to you this day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tribe that answered the call becomes the priestly tribe. It is the origin of Levi's role in Israel, and the passage records both the cost and the appointment in the same sentence."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How to hold this passage.** It is a hard chapter and it should not be smoothed over. What can be said is what the New Testament says: the law came through Moses, and grace and truth came through Jesus Christ — and the two three-thousands are the plainest illustration in Scripture of what each of them produced on its opening day."}]}, "Exodus 32:30–35": {"title": "\"Perhaps I can make atonement\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The greatest intercession in the Old Testament, and it does not work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word he uses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But now I will go up to the LORD. **Perhaps** I can make atonement for what you have done.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Perhaps.** He does not know whether it can be done, and he says so before he climbs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That single word is the distance between Moses and the One he is a picture of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he offers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And yet — **if you would only forgive their sin**. But **if not, then blot me out of the book you have written**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence breaks off mid-thought in the Hebrew — *if You will forgive their sin—* and then nothing. He cannot finish it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the offer: erase me instead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same instinct Paul had: *I could wish that I myself were accursed from Christ for my brethren, my countrymen according to the flesh.* Both men offer, and neither is able."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whoever has sinned against Me, that one I will blot out of My book.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offer is declined. A man cannot substitute for another man's sin; God says so plainly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses is willing and not qualified."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is given instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now go, lead the people to the place I told you of. **My angel will go before you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An angel rather than His presence, and Moses will spend the next chapter arguing about exactly that: *if Your Presence does not go with us, do not bring us up from here.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD struck the people because of what they had done with the calf.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter ends there. No resolution, no forgiveness pronounced, a plague, and a deferred reckoning: *when the day of reckoning comes, I will hold them to account.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison the sermon is built on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses came down from forty days on the mountain to a golden calf, broke the tablets, went back up with a *perhaps*, and came down again with a face so bright he had to veil it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus came out of forty days in the wilderness and *returned in the power of the Spirit*, and went straight to a synagogue in Nazareth to read Isaiah 61 out loud."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses ended his forty days with a plague on the people. Jesus ended His forty days after the resurrection with His hands raised in blessing, and *while He blessed them, He was parted from them.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses could not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Perhaps.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "What was said from the cross was **tetelestai** — it is finished. One word, and no conditional in it. See the note on John 19:28–30."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Moses got right.** He stood between God and a people who deserved nothing, and offered himself. The offer was refused because he was not the one who could make it — but the shape of it is exactly right, and somebody else made it later and was accepted."}]}, "Exodus 34:1–4": {"title": "Cut two tablets like the first", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second set, and the difference between the two is who made the stone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened to the first set"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The tablets were the work of God, and the writing was the writing of God engraved on the tablets.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses came down, saw the calf and the dancing, *and his anger became hot, and he cast the tablets out of his hands and broke them at the foot of the mountain*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law was broken before it reached the camp."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Cut two tablets of stone like the first ones**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **pesal lekha** — cut for yourself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God made the first pair. Moses makes the second, and carries them up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is written on them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **I will write on them the words that were on the first tablets**, the ones you broke.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writing is His both times. The wording is identical."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is altered after the calf. The same ten commandments come down a second time to the same people who had just broken the first of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Be ready in the morning**, and come up Mount Sinai.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Early. The Hebrew has it twice — the command, and then Moses doing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The solitude"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No one is to come with you**; let no one be seen anywhere on the mountain. **Not even the flocks or herds may graze in front of it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first time, the boundaries were for the people. This time the exclusion extends to livestock."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mountain is emptier on the second ascent than the first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The obedience"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Moses **cut two tablets of stone like the first**, and **early in the morning he climbed Mount Sinai** as the LORD had commanded, **carrying the two stone tablets in his hands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An eighty-year-old man, quarrying stone by hand and then carrying two slabs up a mountain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The account gives it one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens at the top"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage everything in Exodus has been building to:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD passed before him and proclaimed, \"**The LORD, the LORD God, merciful and gracious, longsuffering, and abounding in goodness and truth, keeping mercy for thousands, forgiving iniquity and transgression and sin**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fullest self-description God gives anywhere in the Bible, and it is given on the trip up with the replacement tablets, immediately after the golden calf."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Quoted back to Him by Nehemiah, Joel, Jonah, and the Psalms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses had asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Please, show me Your glory.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer: *I will make all My goodness pass before you… but you cannot see My face, for no man shall see Me and live.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Here is a place by Me, and you shall stand on the rock. So it shall be, while My glory passes by, that I will put you in the cleft of the rock, and will cover you with My hand.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What his face looked like coming down"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses did not know that the skin of his face shone while he talked with Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the veil, which Paul takes up: *not like Moses, who put a veil over his face so that the children of Israel could not look steadily at the end of what was passing away*. See the note on 2 Corinthians 3:12–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the tablets went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Into the ark, under the mercy seat, with Aaron's rod and the pot of manna."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Blood on the lid, once a year, over the words on the stone."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one difference between the sets.** God cut the first stone and Moses cut the second. The writing on both is God's, and nothing in the wording changed after the calf."}]}, "Exodus 34:5–7": {"title": "\"He called out His own name\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses had asked to see His glory. This is what he got."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asked for and what came"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Show me Your glory* — chapter 33. And the answer was *I will make all My goodness pass before you, and I will proclaim the name of the LORD before you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The glory turns out to be a name spoken out loud."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does the proclaiming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD came down in the cloud and stood there with Moses, and **He called out His own name**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He introduces Himself. This is God's own description of God, and it is the passage the rest of the Old Testament quotes back to Him — David, Nehemiah, Joel, Jonah and the psalmists all cite it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The LORD, the LORD — a God **tender and kind, gracious and full of compassion, slow to anger, overflowing with steadfast love and faithfulness**. He keeps His mercy for thousands upon thousands, **forgiving wrongdoing and rebellion and sin**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six or seven adjectives before anything else, and every one of them is mercy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Overflowing with steadfast love* is **rav chesed** — abundant in covenant loyalty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Forgiving wrongdoing and rebellion and sin* — three Hebrew words covering three categories: crookedness, deliberate revolt, and missing the mark. All three are named as forgiven."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then the clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Yet He does not sweep guilt aside** as if it were nothing, and the weight of a father's sin can fall upon children and grandchildren, to the third and the fourth generation.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is emphatic — *by no means clearing the guilty*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is merciful and He does not pretend the debt is not there. Both are in one self-description, and the old covenant had no way to hold them together. A holy God cannot simply overlook it, and this verse says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the new covenant says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will be merciful to their unrighteousness, and **their sins and their lawless deeds I will remember no more**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews 8, quoting Jeremiah 31. See the note on Hebrews 8:10–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not overlooked, and not swept aside. Remembered no more — which is a different thing again, and it required somebody to satisfy the clause in Exodus 34 rather than delete it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the cross did. *Whom God set forth as a propitiation… to demonstrate His righteousness, **that He might be just and the justifier** of the one who has faith in Jesus.* Paul writes that sentence to answer exactly this verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it was spoken"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses is holding two blank stone tablets, cut by himself, to replace the ones he smashed. The golden calf was six weeks ago."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God proclaims His mercy standing over the rubble of the first covenant."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Moses does next.** *He made haste and bowed his head toward the earth and worshipped.* No question, no discussion. He heard the name and went down."}]}, "Exodus 35:20–24": {"title": "Everyone whose heart stirred him", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The materials for the tabernacle arrive out of the crowd's own possessions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The going out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the whole community of Israel went out from Moses' presence**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just read them the whole specification — the tent, the coverings, the ark, the table, the lampstand, the altar, the court, the priestly garments."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the invitation: *whoever is of a willing heart, let him bring it as an offering to the LORD*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **everyone whose heart stirred him, everyone whose spirit made him willing**, came back carrying an offering.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kol-ish asher-nesa'o libbo** — whose heart lifted him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **kol asher nadevah rucho** — whose spirit made him willing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nadav** — to volunteer, to offer freely. The root behind **nedavah**, freewill offering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A levy, a quota, or a census-based assessment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both phrases in the verse are about interior motion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the work on the tent of meeting**, for all its service, and **for the holy garments**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who brought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They came, **men and women alike**, all with willing hearts.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ha'anashim al-hannashim** — the men with the women."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Named explicitly, in a context where the priesthood is male and the whole cultic apparatus is male."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gold"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bringing **brooches and earrings, rings and pendants** — every kind of gold jewelry.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Personal ornament. Not bullion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything on the list is something somebody was wearing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the gold came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD had given the people favour in the sight of the Egyptians, so that **they granted them what they requested**. Thus they plundered the Egyptians.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred years of unpaid labour, and the wages came out at the end in jewellery."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other thing that was made of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he received the gold from their hand, and he fashioned it with an engraving tool, and **made a moulded calf**.* See the note on Exodus 32:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same category of material, out of the same ears — *break off the golden earrings which are in the ears of your wives*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 32 and chapter 35 are the same gold going two directions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gesture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Each one **lifting up his gold as a wave offering to the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tenuphah** — a wave offering, the gesture of lifting and moving something before the LORD."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The technical term for a sacrificial rite, applied to jewellery."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other materials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone who had **blue, purple, or scarlet yarn, fine linen, goat hair, ram skins dyed red, or fine leather** brought it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone who could give an offering of **silver or bronze** brought it… and everyone who had **acacia wood** the work could use brought that too.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three tiers, and the phrasing accommodates each: those who had gold, those who could give silver or bronze, those who had wood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is excluded by what they happened to own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who spins"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every woman with skill **spun with her own hands** and brought what she had spun.* See the note on Exodus 35:25–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Labour as well as material. The yarn is made, not donated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people are bringing **far more than we need**.* See the note on Exodus 36:4–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses has to issue an order to stop."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two phrases.** *Whose heart lifted him*, and *whose spirit made him willing*. No levy, no quota — the whole tabernacle is funded out of two clauses about interior motion."}]}, "Exodus 35:25–29": {"title": "Skilled hands, willing hearts", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Not everything brought to the tabernacle was owned. Some of it was made."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The spinners"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every woman with skill **spun with her own hands** and brought what she had spun.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kol-ishah chakhmat-lev** — every woman wise of heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chokmah** — wisdom. The same noun used of Bezalel two chapters later: *filled with the Spirit of God, in wisdom and understanding*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for wisdom in Hebrew covers craft skill. It is not divided into intellectual and practical."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they made"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blue, purple, and scarlet yarn and fine linen.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The four materials of the tabernacle's inner curtains, the veil, and the priestly garments."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tekhelet, argaman, tola'at shani, shesh** — the colours that recur through the specification every time something holy is described."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost to make them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Blue came from a sea creature, purple from murex shells, scarlet from a crushed insect."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Linen from flax, retted, beaten, combed, and spun by hand into thread fine enough for temple cloth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody bought this. It was months of work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second group"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And all the women **whose hearts moved them and who had the gift** spun the goat hair.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Goat hair was the second covering, over the fine curtains — the outer, rougher layer nobody would see once the tent was up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text names those women separately, and uses the same two clauses about the heart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The leaders"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The leaders brought **onyx stones and other gems** to be set in the ephod and the breastpiece.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nesi'im** — the tribal chiefs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stones with the twelve names engraved on them, worn on the priest's shoulders and over his heart whenever he went in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What else"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Along with **spices and olive oil for the light, for the anointing oil, and for the fragrant incense**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three uses: the lamp that burned continually, the oil that consecrated, and the incense burned morning and evening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The summary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the Israelites — **every man and woman whose heart made them willing** — brought a freewill offering to the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nedavah** — freewill offering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase about the heart appears five times in this chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being recorded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two kinds of giving, and the text does not rank them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some people brought what they owned — the jewellery, the yarn, the acacia wood. See the note on Exodus 35:20–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some brought what they could do. The spinners had skill, and their skill is called wisdom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The named craftsmen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*See, **the LORD has called by name Bezalel**… and I have filled him with the Spirit of God, in wisdom, in understanding, in knowledge, and in all manner of workmanship.* See the note on Exodus 31:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first person in the Bible said to be filled with the Spirit of God is a metalworker."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And I have put wisdom in the hearts of all who are gifted artisans.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was made from it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A tent, in a desert, by ex-slaves, using materials they carried out of Egypt and skills they must have learned there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the cloud covered the tabernacle of meeting, and the glory of the LORD filled the tabernacle.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The final sentence of the book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the cloud of the LORD was above the tabernacle by day, and fire was over it by night, **in the sight of all the house of Israel, throughout all their journeys**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the spinners brought.** Not something they owned — something they made. The Hebrew calls their skill **wisdom**, the same word used of the man who built the ark."}]}, "Exodus 35:30–35": {"title": "Bezalel and Oholiab", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two craftsmen are named in front of the whole nation, and one of the gifts listed is teaching."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The announcement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look — **the LORD has singled out Bezalel** son of Uri, grandson of Hur, of the tribe of Judah.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **qara YHWH beshem** — the LORD has called by name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same idiom used of God calling Cyrus, and of the Servant: *the LORD has called Me from the womb*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Betzal'el** — *in the shadow of God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His grandfather **Hur** is the man who held up Moses' other arm at Rephidim while Aaron held the first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The filling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He has filled him with the Spirit of God** — with **wisdom, understanding, and know-how** in every kind of craft.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chokmah, tevunah, da'at** — wisdom, understanding, knowledge."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same three nouns used of God at creation in Proverbs: *the LORD by wisdom founded the earth, by understanding He established the heavens, by His knowledge the depths were broken up*. See the note on Proverbs 3:19–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that means here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first person in Scripture said to be filled with the Spirit of God is a craftsman, and the filling is for metalwork."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not for prophecy, leadership or war. For making things well."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trades"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To **design artful works in gold, silver, and bronze**, to **cut and set stones**, to **carve wood**, to master every skilled craft there is.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four disciplines: metal, gem-cutting, joinery, and design."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second gift"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **He has put it in the hearts** of both Bezalel and **Oholiab** son of Ahisamach, of the tribe of Dan, **to teach others too**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ulehorot natan belibbo** — and to teach He has put in his heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yarah** — to instruct. The root behind **torah**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why that matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The skill is given, and so is the ability to hand it on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Without the second gift the tabernacle is built by two men. With it, the camp is trained."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two tribes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah and Dan."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah is the leading tribe, the one Jacob blessed with the sceptre. Dan is the last-mentioned in most lists and later the most troublesome."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two chief craftsmen come from the top and the bottom of the standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other trades"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He has filled them with the skill to do all kinds of work — as **engravers, designers, embroiderers** in blue, purple, and scarlet yarn and fine linen, and **weavers**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Textile work as well as metal. The colours are the tabernacle's own palette."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They are **masters of every craft and gifted designers**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **choshevei machashavot** — devisers of designs, thinkers of thoughts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The constraint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything they make is to a pattern shown on the mountain: *see that you make all things according to the pattern shown you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Complete skill, and no freedom of design. The gift is for execution."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who spins"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every woman with skill spun with her own hands and brought what she had spun.* See the note on Exodus 35:25–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word, **chakhmat-lev** — wise of heart — used of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about gifts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now there are diversities of gifts, but the same Spirit… **but one and the same Spirit works all these things, distributing to each one individually as He wills**.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 12:8–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole book ends with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Moses finished the work. Then the cloud covered the tabernacle of meeting, and the glory of the LORD filled the tabernacle.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Made by hand, by named men, out of donated jewellery — and filled."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The second gift.** Not only the skill, but *He has put it in his heart to teach*. Without that line the tabernacle is built by two men instead of a camp."}]}, "Exodus 36:1–3": {"title": "Morning after morning", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The workers are named, the materials are handed over, and the deliveries keep coming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **Bezalel, Oholiab, and every skilled person the LORD has gifted with the wisdom and understanding** to know how to do all the work of building the sanctuary are to carry it out.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chakham-lev** — wise of heart. The phrase for craft skill throughout these chapters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two men"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Bezalel** — *in the shadow of God*. Of the tribe of Judah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Oholiab** — *the father's tent*. Of the tribe of Dan."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah was the leading tribe. Dan was the smallest and later the most troublesome. The two men in charge come from opposite ends of the standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the skill came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD **has gifted** with the wisdom and understanding.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And I have filled him with the Spirit of God, in wisdom, in understanding, in knowledge, and in all manner of workmanship.* See the note on Exodus 31:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first man in Scripture described as filled with the Spirit of God is an artisan, and the filling is for metalwork, stone-cutting and carpentry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The constraint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Are to carry it out **exactly as the LORD has commanded**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The specification runs from chapter 25 to chapter 31 and is repeated in the execution from 35 to 40."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Skill in the highest degree, and no design freedom at all. The pattern was shown on the mountain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The calling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Moses called Bezalel and Oholiab and every skilled worker the LORD had gifted — **everyone whose heart moved him to come and do the work**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two qualifications: gifted, and moved to come."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gift alone does not put a man on the site."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The handover"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From Moses **they received all the offerings the Israelites had brought** for the work of building the sanctuary.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The materials had come in from the whole camp. See the note on Exodus 35:20–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses passes them to the craftsmen. He does not hold them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And still **the people kept bringing freewill offerings, morning after morning**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **babboqer babboqer** — in the morning, in the morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The doubling is the Hebrew way of saying *every single morning*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The work has started. The materials have been delivered. And people are still arriving with more before breakfast, day after day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Finally all the skilled workers doing the work of the sanctuary **set down their tools and came to Moses**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The people are bringing far more than we need**.\"* See the note on Exodus 36:4–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The craftsmen stop work to report a surplus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No man or woman is to make anything more** as an offering for the sanctuary.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only recorded instance in Scripture of giving having to be stopped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the gold had been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Round their ears and necks, brought out of Egypt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And once already melted into a calf, at the foot of the same mountain, four chapters earlier. See the note on Exodus 32:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the calf and this, Moses had gone back up and stayed forty days, and the LORD had said *I will make My goodness pass before you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being built"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A tent to be carried through a desert, put up and taken down at every stage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the cloud covered the tabernacle of meeting, and the glory of the LORD filled the tabernacle.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The doubled word.** **Babboqer babboqer** — morning after morning. The work had started and the materials were in hand, and people kept turning up with more before the day began."}]}, "Exodus 36:4–7": {"title": "More than enough", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only time in the Bible that people have to be told to stop giving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Finally **all the skilled workers doing the work of the sanctuary set down their tools and came to Moses**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyavo'u kol-hachakhamim** — all the wise ones came."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They stop work. Every one of them, at once, to raise the same point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The report"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The people are bringing far more than we need** to do the work the LORD commanded.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **marbim ha'am lehavi middei ha'avodah** — the people multiply to bring, beyond the requirement of the work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is complaining"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The builders. Not the treasurer, not Moses, not a committee."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people using the material are the ones who say there is too much of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Moses **sent word all through the camp**: \"**No man or woman is to make anything more as an offering for the sanctuary**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayya'aviru qol bammachaneh** — they caused a voice to pass through the camp."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A proclamation, broadcast to everybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it forbids"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Making anything more."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which tells you what a lot of the giving was. Not surplus wealth handed over. Work done — spinning, weaving, tanning. See the note on Exodus 35:25–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction has to stop production, not just donation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The restraint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And so **the people were held back from giving**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyikkale ha'am mehavi** — the people were restrained from bringing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **what was already in hand was more than enough** to do all the work, **with plenty to spare**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **dayyam… vehoter** — sufficient for them, and over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this material came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ex-slaves in a desert, four hundred years unpaid, carrying out what the Egyptians had handed them on the night they left."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Thus they plundered the Egyptians.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything on the tabernacle inventory came out of Egypt in the hands of people who owned nothing the week before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the same gold had done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he… made a moulded calf.* See the note on Exodus 32:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 32 and chapter 36 are the same category of material, from the same ears, going two directions."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The camp that had to be stopped from giving is the camp that had melted its jewellery into a calf."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The accounting afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus 38 lists it: twenty-nine talents and seven hundred and thirty shekels of gold, a hundred talents of silver, seventy talents of bronze."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is the inventory of the tabernacle… **as counted according to the commandment of Moses**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses had it audited."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was built"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the tabernacle was erected… **and the cloud covered the tabernacle of meeting, and the glory of the LORD filled the tabernacle***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Moses was not able to enter the tabernacle of meeting, because the cloud rested above it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who supervised the whole build cannot get in when it is finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about giving"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So let each one give as he purposes in his heart, not grudgingly or of necessity; for **God loves a cheerful giver**.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 9:6–7."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What had to be stopped.** Not donations of surplus — *making anything more*. The order had to halt production, because most of what was coming in was work."}]}, "Exodus 39:8–21": {"title": "Twelve names on his chest", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Fourteen verses on one garment, and most of them are about how it was fastened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits in the book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus 39 is the report of the work. Chapter 28 gave the instructions; this chapter records that they were carried out, phrase for phrase."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as the LORD had commanded Moses* appears seven times in this chapter alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The materials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Of **gold**, and of **blue, purple, and scarlet yarn**, and **finely twisted linen**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same five as the tabernacle curtains and the veil. The breastpiece is made of the building's own fabric."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shape"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was **square and folded double**, **a span long and a span wide**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A span is roughly nine inches. Folded double makes it a pouch — which is where the Urim and Thummim go. See the note on Exodus 28:30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stones"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Four rows of precious stones.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve, named individually, three to a row. The identifications are uncertain — ancient stone names do not map cleanly onto modern mineralogy — and translations differ."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The engraving"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Twelve stones there were, **one for each of the names of the sons of Israel**, each **engraved like a seal** with the name of one of the twelve tribes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **pittuchei chotam** — engravings of a signet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every tribe, named. Not a symbol or a colour code. The names, cut into stone the way a personal seal is cut."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fastening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven verses on how it stays on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two braided gold cords. Two filigree settings. Two rings at the top corners, two at the lower corners, and two more low on the shoulder pieces."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they bound the breastpiece by its rings to the rings of the ephod **with a cord of blue**, so it rested snug above the waistband.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **would never come loose from the ephod**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *that it might not be loosed from the ephod*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 28 gives the same instruction in the same words. It is stated twice, which in this material means it matters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The garment carrying the twelve names is engineered so that it cannot come off."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is on the shoulders"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Two onyx stones… **six of their names on one stone, and six names on the other stone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the reason given in chapter 28: *so Aaron shall bear their names before the LORD on his two shoulders **as a memorial***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve names on the shoulders and twelve on the chest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the two positions carry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The shoulders are where weight goes. The chest is where affection goes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same twelve names on both, and the priest cannot go in without them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the names turn up next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the wall of the city had twelve foundations, and on them were the names of the twelve apostles of the Lamb.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And she had a great and high wall with twelve gates… and names written on them, which are the names of the twelve tribes of the children of Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve stones on a chest, and twelve foundations in a wall."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stones in Revelation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The foundations of the wall of the city were adorned with all kinds of precious stones: jasper, sapphire, chalcedony, emerald, sardonyx, sardius, chrysolite, beryl, topaz, chrysoprase, jacinth, amethyst.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve, listed, and the overlap with this passage is substantial."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Moses looked over all the work, and indeed they had done it; as the LORD had commanded, so they had done. **And Moses blessed them**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clause stated twice.** *That it might not be loosed.* Seven verses of rings and cords, to make sure the piece with the twelve names on it cannot come off the man going in."}]}, "Exodus 4:1–5": {"title": "What is that in your hand", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God answers an objection by asking about an object."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But what if they don't believe me? What if they won't listen and say, 'The LORD never appeared to you'?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses' third objection. He has already asked *who am I* and *what is Your name*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bush is still burning while this conversation happens. See the note on Exodus 3:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What is that in your hand?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God does not answer the objection. He asks about equipment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"A staff.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **matteh** — a shepherd's stick."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years of tending Jethro's sheep, and it is the only thing he owns that the story mentions. A piece of wood, worn to his grip."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Throw it on the ground**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One verb. Let go of the only thing you have."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it became"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses threw it down, and **it became a snake, and he ran from it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nachash** — the word in Genesis 3. See the note on Genesis 3:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not stay to watch. The text says he fled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Reach out and take it by the tail**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every instinct in a man who handles snakes says the head. Take it by the tail and it can turn on your arm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no promise attached. He is told to grab the dangerous end of the wrong end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So he **reached out and grabbed it**, and **in his hand it turned back into a staff**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The transformation happens on contact."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stated purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This is so they may believe**,\" God said, \"that the LORD, **the God of their fathers** — the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob — has appeared to you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sign is for the audience, not for Moses. His objection was *they will not believe me*, and the answer is a demonstration they can watch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Egypt made of snakes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cobra — the uraeus — sat on the front of Pharaoh's crown. It was the symbol of the sovereignty of Lower Egypt and of the goddess Wadjet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Hebrew shepherd is shown holding one by the tail."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the staff does after this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It becomes *the rod of God*. It turns the Nile to blood, brings the plagues, splits the sea, and strikes the rock at Horeb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aaron's version swallows the magicians' snakes in Pharaoh's court."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Moses holds it up on a hill while Joshua fights Amalek, until his arms have to be propped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one time it goes wrong"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Meribah, told to *speak to the rock*, he strikes it twice with the same rod — and it costs him the land."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is in the hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question is asked once and never repeated in Scripture, but the shape of it recurs: a boy's five loaves and two fish, a widow's handful of meal, a jar with a little oil in it. See the note on 2 Kings 4:1–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha asks the widow the same kind of question — *what do you have in the house?*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was asked.** Not *do you believe*, and not *what do you need*. *What is that in your hand* — and the answer was a stick he had been leaning on for forty years."}]}, "Exodus 4:10–12": {"title": "\"Who gave man his mouth?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses' fourth objection, and the one that is usually read as modesty. It is not quite that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have never been a man of words, not before and not since you have spoken to your servant. I am slow of speech and slow of tongue.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *heavy of mouth and heavy of tongue*. Whatever the difficulty was — a speech impediment, a lost fluency in Egyptian after forty years with sheep — he presents it as a fixed fact about himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the aside: *not since you have spoken to your servant*. He is pointing out that the burning bush has not changed anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "God's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who gave man his mouth? Who makes him deaf or mute? Who gives him sight or makes him blind? Is it not I, the LORD?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four questions, and none of them deny the disability. God does not say Moses is mistaken about his speech; He asks who made the mouth in question."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a large and difficult claim, and the passage does not soften it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now go. I myself will be with your mouth, and I will teach you what to say.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs, both first person. Not *your speech will improve* — I will be with it, and I will teach you."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How it ends.** Moses objects a fifth time anyway — *please send someone else* — and this time God's anger is recorded. Aaron is appointed as the spokesman. The concession is real, and so is the displeasure, and Exodus reports both without resolving them. Moses, notably, does most of the talking from chapter 5 onward."}]}, "Exodus 8:20–24": {"title": "I will make a distinction", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The fourth plague introduces something the first three did not have."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get up early and stand before Pharaoh as he goes down to the water**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same setting as the first plague, when the Nile turned to blood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pharaoh went to the river each morning — for a ritual, or a bath. Moses is told to be waiting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The demand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Tell him: The LORD says, **let my people go, so they can worship me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **veya'avduni** — that they may serve Me."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same clause every time. The plagues are not punishment for nothing; they are the answer to a repeated refusal of a repeated request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The threat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will send **swarms of flies** on you, your officials, your people, and your houses.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **arov** — swarm. The kind of insect is not specified; the word means *mixture*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The new element"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But on that day **I will set apart the land of Goshen, where my people live** — no swarms will be there.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vehiphleti** — I will distinguish, set apart, treat differently."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is new"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first three plagues — blood, frogs, gnats — fell on everybody, Israel included."}, {"k": "p", "t": "From here on, the text says Goshen is exempt, and says so before it happens."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So that **you may know that I, the LORD, am in the midst of the land**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **beqerev ha'aretz** — in the midst of the land."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not distant. Present, and making distinctions inside Egypt's own territory."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will make a distinction between my people and yours**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **pedut** — a division, a ransom, a redemption."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The noun is used elsewhere of redemption. Here it marks a line drawn between two peoples."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This sign will come tomorrow**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Announced in advance, with a date."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the difference between a plague and a natural disaster. Anybody can observe a swarm. Nobody can schedule one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD did so. Great swarms of flies came into Pharaoh's palace and the houses of his officials, and **throughout Egypt the land lay ruined under them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tishachet ha'aretz** — the land was ruined, corrupted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Pharaoh offers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go, sacrifice to your God **in the land**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first of four negotiated offers. Stay in Egypt. Then: go, but not far. Then: go, but leave the children. Then: go, but leave the flocks."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses refuses each one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The next plague"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **the LORD will make a distinction between the livestock of Israel and of Egypt**, and nothing that belongs to Israel will die.\"* See the note on Exodus 9:1–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Pharaoh sends men to check."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the seventh"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Only in Goshen, where the Israelites lived, **was there no hail**.* See the note on Exodus 9:22–35."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ninth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But all the children of Israel had light in their dwellings**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days of darkness that could be felt, and light where the Israelites lived. See the note on Exodus 10:21–29."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tenth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**That you may know that the LORD does make a distinction between the Egyptians and Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb, at the last plague. And that time the distinction is marked by blood on a doorpost rather than by geography."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The new element.** From the fourth plague on, the text names an exemption and announces it in advance — and the stated reason is that Pharaoh should know who is in the middle of his country."}]}, "Exodus 9:1–7": {"title": "Pharaoh sent men to look", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The fifth plague, and the king checks the claim himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD, the God of the Hebrews**, says, let my people go.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **YHWH Elohei ha'Ivrim**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ivri** — Hebrew. The word outsiders used of them. Pharaoh's court would recognise it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The demand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let my people go, **so they can worship me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fifth time. The same sentence, unchanged, at every plague."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The threat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If you refuse and keep holding them, **the hand of the LORD will bring a severe plague on your livestock in the field**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yad-YHWH hoyah** — the hand of the LORD is being."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **horses, donkeys, camels, herds, and flocks**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five categories, itemised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Horses meant chariots — the army. Donkeys and camels meant transport. Herds and flocks meant food and wealth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt's economy, listed in one verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distinction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **the LORD will make a distinction between the livestock of Israel and of Egypt**, and **nothing that belongs to Israel will die**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vehiphlah** — He will distinguish."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb as the fourth plague at Goshen. See the note on Exodus 8:20–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The appointment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD **set a time**, saying, \"**Tomorrow** the LORD will do this in the land.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyasem YHWH mo'ed** — the LORD set an appointed time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Mo'ed** — an appointed meeting, the word used of the feasts and of the tent of meeting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A plague with a diary entry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The next day it happened, and **the livestock of Egypt died, but not one animal belonging to Israel died**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inspection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Pharaoh **sent men to look**, and sure enough, **not one of Israel's animals had died**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyishlach Paroh** — and Pharaoh sent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not take it on report. He sends people to Goshen to count."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that establishes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The distinction is verifiable, and it was verified by the hostile party's own inspectors."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrative includes the audit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet **his heart stayed stubborn**, and he would not let the people go.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyikbad lev Paroh** — Pharaoh's heart was heavy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has the evidence, confirmed by his own men, and it changes nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the hardening does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus uses three verbs across twenty occurrences: **kaved** (heavy), **chazaq** (strong), and **qashah** (hard)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the early plagues Pharaoh hardens his own heart. Later the text says God hardened it. Both are stated, and the book does not reconcile them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the livestock issue returns"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verses later, the sixth plague falls *on man and beast*, and the seventh kills animals left in the field."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egyptian livestock keeps appearing after this chapter says it died — which suggests *in the field* in verse 3 is the limiting phrase, and animals under shelter survived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gods"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Against all the gods of Egypt I will execute judgment.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bull Apis, the cow Hathor, the ram Khnum. The plague on livestock falls on a pantheon with animals in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses says at the seventh"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But I know that **you and your officials still do not fear the LORD God**.\"* See the note on Exodus 9:22–35."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The audit.** *Pharaoh sent men to look.* The distinction was checked by the opposing side's own inspectors, confirmed, and changed nothing."}]}, "Exodus 9:22–35": {"title": "This time I have sinned", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The seventh plague, and the only time Pharaoh says the words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gesture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Stretch out your hand toward the sky**, so hail will fall over all of Egypt.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses lifts the staff. The plagues come through it — the Nile, the frogs, the dust, the hail, the locusts, and eventually the sea."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The storm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD sent **thunder and hail, with fire flashing down to the ground**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **esh mithlaqqachath** — fire taking hold of itself, fire flashing back and forth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In a country where it almost never rains, a hailstorm with lightning is not weather. It is outside the category."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The damage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The hail struck everything in the fields throughout Egypt, both people and animals, and **it battered every plant and every tree**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exemption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Only in **Goshen, where the Israelites lived, was there no hail**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fourth time the text names it. See the note on Exodus 8:20–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The confession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Pharaoh sent for Moses and Aaron and said, \"**This time I have sinned. The LORD is in the right, and I and my people are in the wrong**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chatati happa'am** — I have sinned this time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**YHWH hatzaddiq** — the LORD is the righteous one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is remarkable about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing like it appears anywhere else in the plague narrative."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the sin as his, states that God is right, and includes his officials in the verdict."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Pray to the LORD**, for we have had enough of God's thunder and hail. I will let you go; you need not stay any longer.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When I leave the city, **I will spread out my hands to the LORD**. The thunder will stop… so that you may know **the earth belongs to the LORD**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He agrees to pray, and names the reason: not relief, but the proof of ownership."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The assessment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **I know that you and your officials still do not fear the LORD God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses says it to his face, having just agreed to intercede for him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not believe the confession, and he prays anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The agricultural note"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **flax and the barley were ruined**, for the barley was in the ear and the flax in bud, but **the wheat and spelt were not, since they ripen later**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An observation about the calendar, dropped into a plague narrative."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Barley heads out in January or February. Wheat is a month behind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It tells the reader what time of year this is, and it means the locusts of chapter 10 will find something left to eat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses left Pharaoh, went out of the city, and spread out his hands to the LORD. **The thunder and hail stopped**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did what he said."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **when Pharaoh saw that it had ceased, he sinned again and hardened his heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyosef lachato** — he added to sin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The confession lasted exactly as long as the storm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Relief, and then reversal. It happens with the frogs — *when Pharaoh saw that there was relief, he hardened his heart* — and again here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrative notes both times that the change came after the pressure lifted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His heart stayed hard, and he would not let the people go, **just as the LORD had said through Moses**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The confession.** *This time I have sinned. The LORD is in the right.* It is the only one in the book, and Moses tells him to his face that he does not believe it — then goes out and prays."}]}, "Ezekiel 1:10–11": {"title": "Four faces", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The strangest opening of any book in the Bible, and Ezekiel is straining to describe it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By the River Chebar, in the land of the Chaldeans.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A canal in Babylon, among the exiles taken with Jehoiachin. Ezekiel is thirty years old and a priest who will never serve in a temple."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The heavens were opened and I saw visions of God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How he describes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything is hedged."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **likeness** of four living creatures. **As for the likeness** of their faces. **Something like** a firmament. **The appearance of the likeness** of the glory of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is a careful writer refusing to overclaim. Four layers of qualification before he will say what he saw."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Each of the four had the face of **a man** in front, the face of **a lion** on the right, the face of **an ox** on the left, and the face of **an eagle** behind.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four faces on each creature, and four creatures — sixteen faces, facing every way at once."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They did not turn when they went.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the four are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The traditional reading takes them as the highest of four orders: man among creatures, lion among wild beasts, ox among domestic animals, eagle among birds."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they turn up again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation, round the throne, one face each rather than four: *the first like a lion, the second like a calf, the third with a face like a man, the fourth like a flying eagle*. See the note on Revelation 4:5–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in Revelation each of the four calls out one of the first four seals."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their wings were **spread upward**; two wings of each **touched the wings of the creature beside it**, and **two covered their bodies**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four wings each. Two up and joined to the next creature, two covering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah's seraphim had six: *with two he covered his face, with two he covered his feet, and with two he flew*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wheels"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next paragraph: *a wheel in the middle of a wheel*, rims full of eyes, moving without turning, *for the spirit of the living creatures was in the wheels*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is above them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And above the firmament over their heads was the likeness of a throne, in appearance like a sapphire stone; and on the likeness of the throne was **a likeness with the appearance of a man**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The vision's destination is a seat, and somebody on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This was the appearance of the likeness of the glory of the LORD. **So when I saw it, I fell on my face**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four chapters of preamble and one sentence of reaction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is told next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Son of man, stand on your feet, and I will speak to you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the scroll he is made to eat: *and it was in my mouth like honey in sweetness*. And then: *go, get to the house of Israel and speak with My words to them*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the vision came where it did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Babylon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The glory the priests had assumed was fixed to a building in Jerusalem turns up beside an irrigation canal in a foreign country, with wheels on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel later watches it leave the temple — *then the glory of the LORD departed from the threshold of the temple* — and at the end of the book he watches it come back."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How he writes it.** *The likeness of the appearance.* Four layers of qualification before he will commit to a description — and the last sentence of the chapter is that he fell over."}]}, "Ezekiel 10:18–22": {"title": "The glory leaves by stages", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most reluctant departure in Scripture, recorded in stages by a man watching from Babylon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Ezekiel is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in Jerusalem. He is by the Kebar River among the exiles, and the temple scenes in chapters 8 to 11 are shown to him in vision."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is watching a building he cannot see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first move"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the glory of the LORD **moved out from the threshold of the temple** and came to rest **above the cherubim**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It had already moved once — chapter 9 has it going up from the cherub *to the threshold of the house*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stages"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Threshold. Then above the cherubim. Then:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They stopped **at the entrance of the east gate** of the LORD's house, and the glory of the God of Israel was above them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the next chapter it goes further: *the glory of the LORD went up from the midst of the city and stood on the mountain **which is on the east side of the city**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four pauses on the way out. It stops each time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the east gate matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Mount of Olives is east of the temple. That is where the glory settles when it goes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the hill Jesus wept over the city from, and the hill of the ascension. And Ezekiel 40 has the glory returning *from the way of the east*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was in the temple by then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 8 lists what Ezekiel had been shown inside: an image of jealousy at the north gate, seventy elders burning incense to creatures carved on the walls, women weeping for Tammuz, and twenty-five men at the door of the temple with their backs to it, worshipping the sun."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The glory leaves a building that has been filled with other things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The recognition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These were the living creatures I had seen beneath the God of Israel **by the Kebar River**, and **I knew now that they were cherubim**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had described them in chapter 1 without a name for them. Standing in the temple in vision, he recognises them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cherubim were carved into the veil, embroidered on the curtains, and hammered in gold on the mercy seat. He had grown up with the images and now sees the originals."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail he repeats"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Each had **four faces and four wings**, and under their wings **the form of human hands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hands under the wings — Ezekiel notices it twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Each one **moved straight ahead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it in chapter 1 as well: *they did not turn when they went.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the departure is hesitant in its motion, however many times it stops."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Ezekiel does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not comment. Chapters 8 to 11 are almost entirely description, and the evaluation is left to the reader."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the glory had come from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tabernacle at its dedication, and Solomon's temple at its dedication, when *the priests could not continue ministering because of the cloud*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel watches the same glory go back out."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How it leaves.** Not in an instant. Threshold, cherubim, east gate, the mountain east of the city — and the prophet who records the way out is the one given the vision of it coming back the same way."}]}, "Ezekiel 18:19–20": {"title": "\"The soul who sins is the one who will die\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel is answering a proverb the exiles kept repeating, and the answer is one of the clearest statements of individual responsibility in the Old Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proverb he is dismantling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Earlier in the chapter: *the parents eat sour grapes, and the children's teeth are set on edge.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was a complaint. The exiles were saying they were suffering for their ancestors' sins — which had the convenient effect of making their own conduct beside the point."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God tells them to stop using it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ruling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The child will not share the guilt of the parent, nor the parent the guilt of the child. The righteousness of the righteous will be credited to him, and the wickedness of the wicked will be charged against him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two accounting words — **credited** and **charged**. Each account stands alone; nothing transfers between them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the principle, and Paul later uses the same vocabulary when he says righteousness is credited to Abraham through faith."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The soul who sins is the one who will die\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated twice in the chapter. It is a sentence about death being earned rather than inherited from a parent's record."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read alongside Romans, where death entered through one man and life comes through one Man, the two are addressing different questions: Ezekiel is answering people blaming their fathers, Paul is describing what Adam and Christ each transferred to those in them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter is going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole point of this argument is the invitation at the end of it: *turn and live… why will you die, house of Israel?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The individual accounting is not stated to frighten. It is stated so that nobody can say their outcome was fixed by somebody else's file — see the note on Ezekiel 33:10–11, where the same chapter's conclusion comes with an oath."}]}, "Ezekiel 20:5–6": {"title": "A land I had searched out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God gives the history from the beginning, and the beginning is an oath sworn in Egypt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The occasion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It came to pass in the seventh year, in the fifth month, on the tenth day of the month, that certain of the elders of Israel came to inquire of the LORD, and sat before me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are in Babylon. The elders come to Ezekiel with a question, and God's answer is *will you judge them? Then make known to them the abominations of their fathers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "What follows is a long recital of the nation's history, and it starts here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The choosing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the day **I chose Israel**, **I lifted My hand and swore** to the offspring of the house of Jacob.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nasati yadi** — I lifted My hand. The gesture of taking an oath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The idiom appears four times in these two verses and repeatedly through the chapter. It is Ezekiel's signature for God binding Himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He made Himself known"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I made Myself known to them **in the land of Egypt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not at Sinai. In Egypt, before the exodus, while they were still slaves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The self-disclosure comes before the deliverance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He swore"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I lifted My hand and swore, '**I am the LORD your God**.'*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ani YHWH Eloheichem**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same sentence that opens the ten commandments — *I am the LORD your God, who brought you out of the land of Egypt* — is here sworn as an oath before any commandment exists."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On that day **I promised to bring them out of Egypt into a land I had searched out for them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tarti lahem** — which I explored, spied out, searched for them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tur** is the verb used of the twelve spies: *send men to spy out the land of Canaan*. See the note on Numbers 13:17–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the verb does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God uses the scouts' word of Himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before Moses sent twelve men to survey it, God says He had already surveyed it — and chosen it for them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten of the twelve came back and said *we are not able*. See the note on Numbers 13:30–33."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A land **flowing with milk and honey**, **the loveliest of all the lands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tzevi hi lekol-ha'aratzot** — the beauty, the ornament, the glory of all lands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tzevi** — glory, splendour, and also the gazelle. A word for something both beautiful and swift."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where else Ezekiel uses it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The beauty of their ornament* — of the temple, when he speaks of what will be profaned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Daniel uses it of the same territory: **eretz hatzevi**, the Glorious Land."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter goes on to say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then I said to them, \"**Each of you, throw away the abominations which are before his eyes**\"… **but they rebelled against Me and would not obey Me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Egypt. Before the Red Sea, before the wilderness, before the golden calf."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter records four generations of the same pattern and the same refrain: *I acted for My name's sake, that it should not be profaned*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says He did instead of ending it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Nevertheless **I withheld My hand and acted for My name's sake**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same hand that was lifted in the oath is the hand withheld."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will accept you as a sweet aroma when I bring you out from the peoples**… and you shall know that I am the LORD, when I have dealt with you for My name's sake, **not according to your wicked ways nor according to your corrupt doings**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A long catalogue of failure, and that is the conclusion drawn from it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb.** **Tur** — the scouts' own word for surveying a land. God says He had already done it, for them, before twelve men were ever sent."}]}, "Ezekiel 3:1–3": {"title": "Eat this scroll", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Before he says a word, he has to swallow one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the river Chebar in Babylon, among the exiles. The visions of the first chapter — the living creatures, the wheels, the throne above the firmament — have flattened him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So when I saw it, I fell on my face.* See the note on Ezekiel 1:26–28."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then a voice, and *the Spirit entered me and set me on my feet*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was handed to him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the end of chapter 2: *a hand was stretched out to me, and behold, a scroll of a book was in it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He spread it before me; and there was writing on the inside and on the outside, and written on it were **lamentations and mourning and woe**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Scrolls were written on one side. This one is full to the edges."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He said to me, \"**Son of man**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ben-adam** — son of Adam, son of humanity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God calls Ezekiel this ninety-three times in the book, and almost never uses his name. Standing in front of the throne vision, he is addressed by what he is made of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Eat what you find here. Eat this scroll, and go, speak to the house of Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three imperatives, in order: eat, go, speak."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The speaking is third."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The obedience"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **I opened my mouth, and He fed me the scroll**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyaachileni** — and He caused me to eat it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel opens his mouth. God does the feeding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Son of man, **feed on this scroll I am giving you, and let it fill you full**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **umeecha temalle** — and fill your inward parts, your belly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a taste. The stomach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The taste"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So I ate it, and **in my mouth it was as sweet as honey**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kidvash lematoq** — like honey for sweetness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was written on it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lamentations, mourning and woe."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel's ministry consists of announcing a siege, the fall of the city, and the departure of the glory from the temple. His wife dies and he is forbidden to mourn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The content is bitter and the eating is sweet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the next verses say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Son of man, go to the house of Israel and speak with My words to them… **but the house of Israel will not listen to you, because they will not listen to Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is told in advance that it will not work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have made your face strong against their faces**… like adamant stone, harder than flint.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His name, **Yechezqel**, means *God strengthens*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where else the scroll is eaten"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then I took the little book out of the angel's hand and ate it, and **it was as sweet as honey in my mouth. But when I had eaten it, my stomach became bitter**.* See the note on Revelation 10:9–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John spells out the second half that Ezekiel's account leaves implicit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Jeremiah's version"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your words were found, and **I ate them**, and Your word was to me the joy and rejoicing of my heart.* See the note on Jeremiah 15:15–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same image, in the same century, from a prophet with the same reception."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How sweet are Your words to my taste, sweeter than honey to my mouth!* See the note on Psalms 119:97–104."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the order says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eat, then go, then speak."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing goes out that has not gone in first, and the going-in is described as a meal rather than a study."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order.** Eat, go, speak. And the scroll he was told to fill his stomach with had *lamentations and mourning and woe* written on both sides."}]}, "Ezekiel 33:10–11": {"title": "\"I take no pleasure in the death of the wicked\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God answers a sentence His people are saying to each other, and the answer comes with an oath attached."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our sins and failures weigh us down. We are wasting away because of them. How then can we live?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is not defiance. It is despair — people who have concluded the account is too far gone to be worth addressing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel is prophesying to exiles. The city has fallen; the worst has already happened. Their question is whether there is any point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As surely as I live, declares the Lord GOD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God swears by Himself, which He does rarely. Whatever follows is being underlined as hard as language allows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I take no pleasure in the death of the wicked. My joy is that the wicked turn from their ways and live.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two halves, negative and positive. The Hebrew for *pleasure* is about delight or desire — He is not describing reluctance to punish. He is denying any appetite for it at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the positive is stronger than mere permission: His *joy* is in the turning and the living."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Turn back, turn from your evil ways. Why will you die, house of Israel?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It ends on a question, and the question is almost bewildered. Given that the door is open and He has just sworn He wants them through it — why?"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it turns up again.** Peter says God *does not want anyone destroyed; He wants every last one to turn*, and Paul says God desires all people to be saved. The New Testament keeps making the same claim, and this is the oath underneath it. See the note on 2 Peter 3:9."}]}, "Ezekiel 34:1–6": {"title": "Shepherds who feed themselves", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most sustained attack on bad leadership in the Bible, and it is delivered as a job description."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is addressed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Son of man, **speak against the shepherds of Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Shepherd is the standard ancient title for a king, used across the whole Near East. Ezekiel means the rulers, and the priests with them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is writing from Babylon, after the exile has happened. The men he is indicting have already lost the country."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"How terrible for the shepherds of Israel who **only feed themselves**. **Should not shepherds feed the flock?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *woe to the shepherds who are feeding themselves*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question in the second line is rhetorical and it is the whole indictment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three products"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You eat **the curds**, you clothe yourselves with **the wool**, you slaughter **the choice animals**, but you do not feed the flock.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Milk, wool, meat. Everything a flock produces, going one direction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not accused of stealing the sheep. They are accused of taking what the sheep make and giving nothing back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five failures"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You have **not strengthened the weak** or **healed the sick** or **bound up the injured**. You have **not brought back the strays** or **searched for the lost**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five, and every one is a negative. The list is what a shepherd is for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Healed the sick** — the Hebrew is *the sick you have not healed*. It is put in the middle of a list of things God expected of them, which means it was in their remit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The manner"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You have **ruled them harshly and without gentleness**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *with force and with cruelty* — **chozqah** and **perek**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Perek** is a rare word, and its other main use is Exodus: the Egyptians made the Israelites serve *with rigour*. Ezekiel puts Israel's own leaders in the Egyptians' role."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So My sheep were scattered, for they had no shepherd**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew makes it a consequence. Not persecution, not invasion — the scattering is caused by the men who were supposed to prevent it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And scattered, they became **food for every wild beast**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The extent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"My sheep **wandered over all the mountains and every high hill**. They were scattered over the whole face of the earth, and **no one searched for them. No one went looking**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last two sentences are the same statement twice. Ezekiel will not let it go."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says He will do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I Myself will search for My sheep and seek them out**… I will deliver them from all the places where they were scattered on a cloudy and dark day.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will feed My flock, and I will make them lie down**,\" says the Lord GOD. \"**I will seek what was lost and bring back what was driven away, bind up the broken and strengthen what was sick**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Point by point, He takes over the list they failed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shepherd He appoints"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will establish **one shepherd** over them, and he shall feed them — My servant David. He shall feed them and be their shepherd.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David has been dead four hundred years when Ezekiel writes this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am the good shepherd**. The good shepherd gives His life for the sheep. But a hireling — he sees the wolf coming and leaves the sheep and flees.\"* See the note on John 10:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes Ezekiel's contrast and stands on the right side of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the parable: *what man of you, having a hundred sheep, if he loses one, does not leave the ninety-nine and **go after the one which is lost until he finds it**?* See the note on Luke 15:3–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No one went looking*, in Ezekiel. In Luke, someone goes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Matthew saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When He saw the multitudes, He was moved with compassion for them, because they were **weary and scattered, like sheep having no shepherd**.* See the note on Matthew 9:35–38."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel's exact diagnosis, six centuries later, on the same ground."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the list assumes.** *You have not healed the sick.* It is filed among the charges, which means He had expected it of them."}]}, "Ezekiel 34:25–27": {"title": "\"There will be showers of blessing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The phrase everybody knows, in a chapter about shepherds who fed themselves instead of the sheep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel 34 is God's charge against Israel's leaders: you eat the fat, you clothe yourselves with the wool, you slaughter the fattened animals, and you do not feed the flock. The weak were not strengthened, the sick not healed, the lost not sought."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *I Myself will search for My sheep.* God takes the job over personally, and these verses describe the country afterwards."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The covenant of peace"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will make with them a covenant of peace and rid the land of wild beasts, so that they may live safely in the wilderness and sleep in the forests.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Safety in the two places nobody was safe. The wilderness and the forest were where you were most exposed — and the promise is that they will sleep there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The showers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will send down showers in their season. There will be showers of blessing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is literally *showers of blessing*, and Ezekiel keeps the two rains framework of Deuteronomy — *in their season*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note where the blessing is aimed: *I will make them and the places around My hill a blessing.* The people are made into one before the rain is mentioned. The showers land on ground that has already been described as a blessing to its neighbours."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose stated at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they will know that I am the LORD, when I break the bars of their yoke and rescue them from the hands of those who enslaved them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not primarily so the crops come in. So that they know who did it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who the shepherd turns out to be.** The chapter goes on: *I will place over them one shepherd, My servant David, and he will tend them.* Written centuries after David died. Jesus picks the whole chapter up in John 10 — the good shepherd, the sheep who were scattered, the hirelings who ran. See the note on John 10:7–10."}]}, "Ezekiel 36:26": {"title": "A heart of flesh", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, four clauses, and every verb is *I will*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being addressed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exiles in Babylon, whose city and temple have been destroyed and who know exactly why."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter is blunt about it: *so I poured out My fury on them for the blood they had shed on the land, and for their idols with which they had defiled it*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why He says He is doing it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not because they deserve it, and the chapter says so twice:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I do not do this for your sake, O house of Israel, but **for My holy name's sake**, which you have profaned among the nations.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And later: *\"Not for your sake do I do this,\" says the Lord GOD, \"let it be known to you. **Be ashamed and confounded for your own ways**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The motive is stated, and it is His reputation among the watching nations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will give you a new heart**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lev chadash**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Lev** — heart, and in Hebrew it is the seat of thought and will as much as feeling. Closer to *mind* than the English word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will put a new spirit within you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ruach** — spirit, breath, wind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things given, and both are internal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will take out of you the heart of stone**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lev ha'even**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stone does not respond. It does not soften, absorb or change temperature quickly. It is inert."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the law had been written on it. Two tablets, cut from rock, and the pun is not accidental — Paul makes it explicit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fourth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **I will give you a heart of flesh, warm and living**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lev basar**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Flesh is usually the negative term in Scripture. Here it is the improvement, because the contrast is not flesh against spirit but flesh against stone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Soft, warm, and able to feel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will put My Spirit within you and cause you to walk in My statutes**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The obedience is described as an outcome of the transplant rather than a condition for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Create in me a clean heart, O God, and renew a steadfast spirit within me.* See the note on Psalms 51:10–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A king asks for it as a favour. A prophet is told it is coming as a policy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jeremiah has"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will put My law in their minds, and write it on their hearts; and I will be their God, and they shall be My people.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For I will forgive their iniquity, and their sin I will remember no more.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews quotes that passage twice, at length. See the note on Hebrews 8:10–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does with the stone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are our letter, written on our hearts… **not on tablets of stone but on tablets that are human hearts**.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 3:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes Ezekiel's contrast and applies it to a congregation, calling them the letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The desolate land shall be tilled instead of lying desolate in the sight of all who pass by. So they will say, '**This land that was desolate has become like the garden of Eden**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *I will increase them with men like a flock*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Which way the contrast runs.** Not flesh against spirit. Flesh against *stone* — and the word for the improvement is the one usually used of the problem."}]}, "Ezekiel 36:33–36": {"title": "\"Like the garden of Eden\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God describes what a ruined country is going to look like, and the order of events is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the day I cleanse you from all your sins, I will settle people in your towns again, and the ruins will be rebuilt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cleansing is stated before the rebuilding, and the rebuilding is dated from it: **on the day** I cleanse you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has already said how — *I will sprinkle clean water on you… I will give you a new heart and put a new spirit in you.* The restoration of the land follows the restoration of the people, not the other way round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison the neighbours make"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People will say, \"This land that was ruined has become like the garden of Eden.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The observation is put in the mouths of outsiders. God does not describe the country that way Himself; He says that is what will be said about it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eden is the highest comparison available in Hebrew — the garden before anything went wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the towns were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rubble, deserted and torn down*, and now *walled and full of life*. Ezekiel is writing to people who had watched Jerusalem fall and had walked away from the smoke."}, {"k": "p", "t": "To them, this is not poetry about an abstract future. It is their own streets."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why God says He does it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the nations left around you will know that I, the LORD, have rebuilt what was torn down.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stated audience is the surrounding nations. Earlier in the chapter He says it plainly: this is not for Israel's sake but for the sake of His name, which they had profaned among the nations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I, the LORD, have spoken, and I will do it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs, and the second guarantees the first. Ezekiel ends the promise the way Isaiah ends his — with the fact that it was said out loud by someone who finishes what He says. See the note on Isaiah 55:10–11."}]}, "Ezekiel 38:1–6": {"title": "Hooks in your jaws", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An invasion is announced, and the first thing said about it is who is bringing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel 37 is the valley of dry bones and the two sticks made one. See the note on Ezekiel 37:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel 40 begins the vision of the new temple, measured cubit by cubit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapters 38 and 39 sit between the restoration and the temple."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Son of man, **turn your face toward Gog, of the land of Magog**, the chief prince of **Meshech and Tubal**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **sim panecha** — set your face. Ezekiel is told to do this repeatedly, toward Jerusalem, toward the mountains, toward Egypt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Magog**, **Meshech**, **Tubal**, **Gomer**, **Beth-togarmah** — all appear in the table of nations in Genesis 10 as sons or grandsons of Japheth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Persia**, **Cush** and **Put** — modern Iran, and the regions of Sudan and Libya."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list runs to the far edges of the known world, north and south."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Gog"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **Gog** is not in Genesis 10. It is a person, not a nation — *the chief prince*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nesi rosh** — the phrase can be read *chief prince of Meshech* or *prince of Rosh, Meshech*. The grammar has been argued over for centuries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The declaration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Listen, Gog. **I am against you**, chief prince of Meshech and Tubal.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hineni elecha** — behold, I am toward you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will turn you around. I will set hooks in your jaws and lead you out**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **veshovavticha venatatti chachim bilchayecha**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chachim** — hooks, rings. The image is of a large animal, or a captive, being led by a ring through the jaw."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Assyrian reliefs show prisoners led exactly this way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the image says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The invasion is not the invader's initiative."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is described as being brought out, against his will, by the one who says *I am against you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same image is used of Sennacherib in Isaiah: *I will put My hook in your nose and My bridle in your lips, and I will turn you back*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The army"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You and all your army, **horses and horsemen fully arrayed**, a vast company carrying **shields and bucklers**, every hand upon a sword.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two sizes of shield — the large body shield and the small round buckler."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"All with shield and helmet.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scale"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Many peoples are gathered to you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven nations named, from the far north to Africa."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Gomer marches beside you**, and all its bands. **Beth-togarmah from the far north** comes down with all its ranks.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **miyyarketei tzaphon** — from the remote parts of the north."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will call for a sword against Gog** throughout all My mountains… every man's sword will be against his brother.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"**You shall fall upon the mountains of Israel**, you and all your troops.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that begins with hooks in the jaw ends with the army destroyed on the ground it came for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stated purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Thus I will magnify Myself and sanctify Myself**, and I will be known in the eyes of many nations. **Then they shall know that I am the LORD**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The refrain of the whole book. Ezekiel uses it more than sixty times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is on the other side"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapters 40 to 48: a temple measured in detail, the glory returning through the east gate, a river running from under the threshold and getting deeper, and the city's new name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the name of the city from that day shall be: **THE LORD IS THERE**.* See the note on Ezekiel 48:34–35."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The hooks.** The invader is described as being led out by the jaw. The chapter opens with an army massing and says immediately whose hand is on it."}]}, "Ezekiel 44:28–31": {"title": "\"I am their inheritance\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The tribe that got no land is told what it got instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arrangement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As for their inheritance, **I am their inheritance**. You are to give them no possession in Israel. **I am their possession**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice, in two forms, in one verse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every other tribe received territory when the land was divided. Levi received towns scattered among the others and no ground of their own."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua puts it the same way: *the LORD God of Israel was their inheritance.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they lived on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They will eat the grain offering, the sin offering, and the guilt offering, and **every devoted thing in Israel will be theirs**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their income was what came to the altar. If the nation stopped bringing, they stopped eating — which is exactly what happened in Nehemiah's day, and the Levites went back to their fields."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The firstfruits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The best of all the firstfruits** of everything… will belong to the priests.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Reshith** again — the first and the choicest. The same word as Proverbs 3:9. See the note on Proverbs 3:9–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dough"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You are to give the priests **the first of your dough**, so that **a blessing may rest on your house**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction goes back to Numbers 15 — a cake from the first of the kneading trough, given as a heave offering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the smallest possible offering. Not a herd, not a harvest — a piece of the dough before the bread is baked, from an ordinary kitchen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the reason attached is domestic: *that a blessing may rest on your house.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul takes that idea"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If the firstfruit is holy, the lump is also holy; and if the root is holy, so are the branches.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 11:16, and he is using the dough offering as an image. A portion given sanctifies the whole batch it came from. See the note on Romans 11:13–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose priesthood this is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter is about *the sons of Zadok*, who *kept charge of My sanctuary when the children of Israel went astray from Me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel's temple vision assigns the closest service to the family that stayed at the post when everybody else drifted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the priests could not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last verse is a food regulation — nothing that died of itself or was torn by beasts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And earlier in the chapter: no wine when they enter the inner court, linen only, no wool, *for they shall not clothe themselves with anything that causes sweat.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No sweat in the sanctuary. Whatever else the service was, it was not to be laboured at."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the tribe with nothing ended up holding.** The tabernacle, the ark, the law, the teaching of it, and the altar. No field anywhere, and everything that mattered passed through their hands."}]}, "Ezekiel 47:1–2": {"title": "Water from under the threshold", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel has been shown a temple in enormous detail for eight chapters. Then he is brought back to the front door and shown water."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it starts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Water flowing out **from under the threshold of the house**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not from a spring, a well or a river. From under the doorstep of the building itself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The source is the house."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which direction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Toward the east, **for the house faced east**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "East is the direction of the entrance, and the direction of the Dead Sea — which the chapter goes on to say the water heals. *The waters shall be healed… and everything will live wherever the river goes.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail he adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The water was coming down **from beneath the south side of the house, south of the altar**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He locates it precisely. It passes the altar on its way out, and it comes from the side."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The burnt offering was killed *on the north side of the altar*. The water runs out past the south side. See the note on Leviticus 1:10–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Trickling\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there **the water was trickling out** from the south side.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a small flow — seeping, oozing. At the threshold it is barely anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What follows in the chapter is the measuring: a thousand cubits, and it is ankle deep. Another thousand, knee deep. Another thousand, up to the waist. Another thousand, and *a river that could not be crossed, for the water was too deep, water in which one must swim.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four measurements, and the further it gets from the door the deeper it is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Out of his innermost being will flow rivers of living water**.\" He was speaking about the Spirit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus said it standing up in the temple courts at the Feast of Tabernacles, on the day the water-pouring ceremony did not happen. See the note on John 7:37–39."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel's water comes from under the threshold of the house. John's comes from inside a person — and Paul says *you are the temple of God, and the Spirit of God dwells in you.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the river ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Along the bank of the river, on this side and that, will grow all kinds of trees for food. Their leaves will not wither, and their fruit will not fail. They will bear fruit every month… **their fruit will be for food, and their leaves for medicine**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation 22 closes the Bible with the same picture: a pure river of water of life, proceeding from the throne, with the tree of life on either side, *and the leaves of the tree were for the healing of the nations.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the man with the measuring line does.** He walks Ezekiel out into it four times and asks him each time how deep it is. The prophet is not shown the river from the bank. He is put in it, up to the waist, and then told to swim."}]}, "Ezekiel 47:11–12": {"title": "Fruit every month", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The exception first, and then the orchard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But its **swamps and marshes will not be made fresh. They are left for salt**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *they shall be given over to salt*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The vision does not claim everything changes. Some ground stays as it is, and the text gives a reason rather than an apology: salt was needed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every grain offering was seasoned with it — *with all your offerings you shall offer salt* — and the phrase for a permanent agreement was **berith melach**, a covenant of salt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Dead Sea salt pans supplied it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Along the river, **on both banks, every kind of tree for food will grow**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *every tree for food*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not ornamental. Everything on the bank is edible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The leaves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Their leaves will not wither**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lo yibbol** — shall not fade."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 1 has the same clause of a man: *he shall be like a tree planted by the rivers of water, that brings forth its fruit in its season, whose **leaf also shall not wither***. See the note on Psalms 1:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same image, same phrase, and the psalm's tree is planted by water channels."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fruit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **their fruit will not fail. Every month they will bear fresh fruit**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *it shall bring forth new fruit according to its months*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fruit trees have a season. That is what a season is for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "These do not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because **the water that flows to them comes from the sanctuary**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *because their waters issued out of the sanctuary*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The vision explains the abnormality by naming the source. Nothing about the trees is different; the water is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two uses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Their **fruit will be for food**, and **their leaves for healing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *healing* is **terufah** — a word that occurs once in the Bible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two products from one tree, and neither is wasted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the middle of its street, and on either side of the river, was **the tree of life**, which bore twelve fruits, **each tree yielding its fruit every month**. **The leaves of the tree were for the healing of the nations**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation 22, and it is Ezekiel's paragraph almost word for word — the river, the trees on both banks, monthly fruit, and leaves for healing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John adds one thing: the tree is named. It is the tree of life, last seen in Genesis 3 with a flaming sword turning every way to guard the path to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three rivers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eden: *a river went out of Eden to water the garden, and from there it parted and became four riverheads*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel: out from under the threshold, deepening with no tributary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation: *proceeding from the throne of God and of the Lamb*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Bible opens with a river in a garden and closes with one running down a street."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus said about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He who believes in Me, as the Scripture has said, **out of his heart will flow rivers of living water**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And John's note: *this He spoke concerning the Spirit*. See the note on John 7:37–39."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says *as the Scripture has said*, and does not say which. This chapter is the strongest candidate."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the trees are abnormal.** Nothing about the trees. *Because their water comes out of the sanctuary* — and the fruit arrives every month because of where the river started."}]}, "Ezekiel 47:7–10": {"title": "Wherever the river flows", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The vision has been measuring the water. Now it looks at what the water does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel is shown water coming out from under the threshold of the temple, on the south side of the altar. See the note on Ezekiel 47:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man with the measuring line takes him out a thousand cubits at a time: ankle deep, knee deep, waist deep, and then *water in which one must swim, a river that could not be crossed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four measurements, and no tributary joins it. It deepens on its own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When I got there, I saw **a great many trees on both banks**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *very many trees on the one side and on the other*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had walked out along the river and had not noticed them until he was brought back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This water flows out toward **the eastern region** and goes down into **the Arabah**, and it **enters the sea**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Arabah is the rift valley. Down from Jerusalem to the Dead Sea is a drop of about four thousand feet in under twenty miles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sea* is the Dead Sea — a lake with no outlet, roughly ten times as salty as the ocean. Nothing lives in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When it flows into the sea, **the salt water is made fresh**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nirpa'u** — are healed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb for healing, used of water."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Wherever the river flows, every living thing that swarms will live**, and there will be a great many fish.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *every living creature that swarms* — the phrase from Genesis 1, of the fifth day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **wherever the river flows, everything will live**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice in three verses. The condition is entirely about location, and it is repeated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fishermen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Fishermen will stand along the shore**, from **En-gedi to En-eglaim** there will be places for spreading nets.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two real places, on the western shore of the Dead Sea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**En-gedi** is the oasis where David hid from Saul, and where he cut the corner off Saul's robe in a cave."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody has ever fished the Dead Sea. The vision has men drying nets on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The variety"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The fish will be of **many kinds, like the fish of the Great Sea**, a great many of them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Great Sea is the Mediterranean."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **its swamps and marshes will not be made fresh. They are left for salt**.\"* See the note on Ezekiel 47:11–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The vision is not uniform. Some of it stays salty, and Ezekiel is told why: salt was needed — every grain offering was seasoned with it, and it was the covenant word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the fishermen are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Come, follow Me, and **I will make you fishers of people**.\"* See the note on Matthew 4:18–22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to two men throwing a net into a lake."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the river reappears"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he showed me **a pure river of water of life**, clear as crystal, proceeding from the throne of God and of the Lamb. In the middle of its street, and on either side of the river, was **the tree of life**… and **the leaves of the tree were for the healing of the nations**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation's last chapter, with Ezekiel's trees and Ezekiel's healing leaves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the water starts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Out from under the threshold of the temple.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It has one source and no tributaries, and everything in the chapter depends on where it has reached."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clause said twice.** *Wherever the river flows.* Not how deep, not how wide — the only variable in the passage is whether the water has arrived."}]}, "Ezekiel 9:3–4": {"title": "The mark on the forehead", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Before anything happens to the city, one man is sent through it with a pen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The glory moves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **the glory of the God of Israel had risen from over the cherub** where it had rested and **moved to the threshold** of the house.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel tracks this through the whole book — the glory lifts from between the cherubim, goes to the threshold, then to the east gate, then to the mountain east of the city, and leaves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It moves slowly, and by stages, and it is the saddest sequence in the prophets."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here it has taken the first step."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The man with the writing case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The man **clothed in linen** who had **the writing case at his side**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six men had come in with weapons. This one has neither armour nor a weapon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Linen is priestly cloth. The writing case is a scribe's — an inkhorn worn at the belt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Pass through the midst of the city**… and **set a mark upon the foreheads**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes first. Before the six with the weapons are told to do anything, this one goes through and marks people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mark"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word is **tav** — the last letter of the Hebrew alphabet, and it is used here as the name of the mark itself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the old paleo-Hebrew script, that letter was written as a cross."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who gets it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The people who **sigh and grieve over all the abominations** that are done within it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs, and both are about grief."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the people who did nothing wrong. Not the ones who protested, or organised, or left. The ones who were still grieved by what they were living in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever they had or had not done, that is what the mark is set on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The six are told to begin, *and do not let your eye spare, nor have any pity* — and then: *\"but **do not come near anyone on whom is the mark**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction is explicit, and the mark is the only thing distinguishing anybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it begins"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Begin at My sanctuary.**\" So they began with the elders who were before the temple.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The judgement starts at the building and with the leadership."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says the same thing: *the time has come for judgement to begin at the house of God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Ezekiel does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I fell on my face and cried out, \"Ah, Lord GOD! **Will You destroy all the remnant of Israel** in pouring out Your fury on Jerusalem?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the only one in the vision who intercedes."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the mark turns up again.** Revelation 7: *do not harm the earth, the sea, or the trees **till we have sealed the servants of our God on their foreheads**.* Same order — the sealing first, and then the rest."}]}, "Ezra 2:1–2": {"title": "The long road back", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A list of names, and one of them is worth stopping on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the list is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These are the people of the province who came up out of captivity… now returning to Jerusalem and Judah, **each one to his own town**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventy years after Nebuchadnezzar took them, Cyrus the Persian issues a decree and they are allowed to go home."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not everybody went. That is the fact behind the whole book of Esther. Many Jews had built lives in Babylon and Persia across three generations and stayed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Each one to his own town\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The towns had been empty for seventy years. Most of the returners had never seen them; they are going to places they know only from their grandparents."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They came with Zerubbabel, together with Jeshua, Nehemiah, Seraiah, Reelaiah, **Mordecai**, Bilshan…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whether or not this is the man in the palace at Susa, the name is here in the list of those who went up — and it tells you the road home was open."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Jews of Esther's book are in Persia by choice. Susa is where they had settled when Jerusalem was available."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why that matters for Esther"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Esther is the story of the people who did not go back, in the season Hosea called *not My people* — no temple, no land, no prophet, and God's name never spoken in the whole of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He is at work in every chapter of it anyway. That is the thing the book was written to show: they were out of the land, out of the covenant blessings, out of His visible dealings — and not out of His hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tally"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is the tally of the men of the people of Israel, **counted by their families**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that follows is fifty-eight verses of numbers, family by family, village by village. Down to the singers, the gatekeepers, and the temple servants."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody counted, and wrote it down, and it was kept."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they came back to.** Rubble, and a foundation. When the older men who had seen the first temple saw the new foundation laid, Ezra says they wept aloud while everyone else shouted for joy, and nobody could tell the weeping from the shouting."}]}, "Ezra 6:13–15": {"title": "\"They built and they finished\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A building project that had been stopped for sixteen years gets finished in four, and Ezra names why."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What had happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The foundation was laid, and then the local officials wrote to the Persian court and the work was halted by decree. It stayed halted through the rest of Cyrus's reign and into Darius's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Tattenai the governor asked the question that reopened it — *who authorised this?* — and the search of the archives at Ecbatana turned up Cyrus's original decree."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the officials did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Tattenai and Shethar-bozenai and their companions **did exactly what King Darius had sent, and they did it carefully**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men who tried to stop it are now enforcing it. Darius's reply had ordered them not only to leave the Jews alone but to fund the work out of the provincial taxes, and to supply the sacrifices daily."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The opposition became the budget."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two reasons given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The work went well in their hands, **carried along by the words of Haggai the prophet and Zechariah son of Iddo**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Aramaic is that they prospered *through the prophesying* of these two men."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezra names the preaching as the cause. Not the decree — that came later, and the prophets started in the second year of Darius, before the ruling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haggai's message was *consider your ways* and *I am with you, says the LORD.* Zechariah's, to Zerubbabel, was *not by might nor by power, but by My Spirit* and *grace, grace to it.* See the note on Zechariah 4:6–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They preached, and men who had given up picked up their tools."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The double authorship"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as **the God of Israel had commanded**, and as **Cyrus and Darius and Artaxerxes** king of Persia had decreed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezra puts God's command and three pagan emperors' decrees in one sentence, as though they were one instruction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The date"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The house was finished on **the third day of the month Adar, in the sixth year of the reign of King Darius**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezra dates it precisely. Roughly twenty years after the foundation was laid, four years after the preaching started."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old men who had seen Solomon's temple wept when the foundation went down, because this one was so much smaller."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haggai had addressed that directly: *who is left among you who saw this temple in its former glory? And how do you see it now? Is it not in your eyes as nothing?* — and then: *the glory of this latter temple shall be greater than the former.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dedication, a hundred bulls, two hundred rams, four hundred lambs, and twelve male goats *according to the number of the tribes of Israel* — twelve, though only two tribes had come home."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then they kept the Passover."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Which building it became.** This is the temple Herod later enlarged, and the one Jesus walked into and cleared. *The glory of this latter temple shall be greater than the former* — the greater glory turned out to be a Person standing in it."}]}, "Galatians 1:15–16": {"title": "\"Set me apart from my mother's womb\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul dates his calling to before he had done anything at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God, who had **set me apart from my mother's womb** and called me **through His grace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not from the Damascus road. From the womb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *set apart* is **aphorizo** — to mark off, to separate for a purpose. It is the same root as *Pharisee*, which is what he had been, and he uses it here of what God had done long before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is excluded by the dating"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything he had listed a few verses earlier: *I advanced in Judaism beyond many of my contemporaries, being more exceedingly zealous for the traditions of my fathers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And everything on the other side of it — *I persecuted the church of God beyond measure and tried to destroy it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither the achievements nor the atrocities are the reason. He was set apart before either existed, and the mechanism named is **grace**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who else was told this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah: *before I formed you in the womb I knew you; before you were born I sanctified you; I ordained you a prophet to the nations.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah: *the LORD has called Me from the womb; from the body of My mother He has named Me.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Was pleased\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Was **pleased** to reveal His Son in me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eudokeo** — to take good pleasure in, to think it good. It is the word the Father uses at the baptism: *in whom I am well pleased.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The revealing was not a rescue operation forced on Him. It pleased Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In me\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *to me*. Paul says the Son was revealed **in** him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that I might **announce Him among the nations**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The calling and the assignment arrive together. He is not given a revelation and then left to find a use for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the moment that happened, **I did not sit down and talk it over with flesh and blood**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not go up to Jerusalem to the apostles. He went to Arabia, and came back to Damascus, and it was three years before he saw Peter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole point of the passage is where his gospel came from: *I neither received it from man, nor was I taught it, but it came through the revelation of Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he had been on the way to do.** Carrying letters to Damascus to arrest people. The commission he was actually given that morning was *to open their eyes, to turn them from darkness to light* — and he had been set apart for it before he was born."}]}, "Galatians 1:6–7": {"title": "Astonished so soon", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul opens the letter without the thing he always opens letters with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is missing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every other letter of Paul's begins with thanksgiving. *I thank my God always concerning you*, *we give thanks to God always for you all*, *I thank my God upon every remembrance of you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Even Corinth, with its factions and its lawsuits and its immorality, gets one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Galatia gets this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am **astonished**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **thaumazo** — to marvel, to be amazed. It was a standard opening in first-century letters, but used to express pleasure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes the polite formula and turns it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The speed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At **how quickly** you are walking away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tacheos** — soon, hastily."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that they left, but how fast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are leaving"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Walking away from **the One who called you in the grace of Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not from a doctrine. From a person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say they are deserting the gospel first. He says they are deserting the one who called them, and the calling is described as *in grace*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Metatithesthe** — to transfer, to change sides. It was used of soldiers deserting and of a person changing political party."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is present tense. It is happening while he writes; it is not finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are going to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Walking off toward **a different good news** — **which is not another good news at all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses two different Greek words in one sentence:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Heteron** — another of a different kind.", "**Allo** — another of the same kind."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **different** gospel, which is not **another** gospel.* Different in kind, and not a second one of the same species."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is one, and this is not a variant of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is behind it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There are simply **certain people unsettling you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tarassontes** — agitating, stirring up, throwing into confusion. It is the word for water being troubled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not name them. Later he calls them *false brethren secretly brought in*, and *he who troubles you shall bear his judgment, whoever he is.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they want"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What they want is to **twist the good news of Christ into something else**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **metastrepsai** — to turn about, to reverse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the twist was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Circumcision. The letter names it repeatedly, and the council in Acts 15 dealt with the same teaching: *unless you are circumcised according to the custom of Moses, you cannot be saved.* See the note on Acts 15:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing immoral was being proposed. An addition was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But even if we, or an angel from heaven, preach any other gospel to you than what we have preached to you, **let him be accursed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then he says it a second time, in the next verse, in case it was read as heat: *as we have said before, so now I say again.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the letter lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is **for freedom that Christ has made us free**. Stand fast therefore, and do not be entangled again with a yoke of bondage.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What alarms him.** Not the arrival of teachers and not an argument being lost. The word in the sentence is *quickly* — and what they are described as leaving is a person, not a position."}]}, "Galatians 1:8–9": {"title": "\"Let him be accursed\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the hardest thing in his letters, and then says it a second time in case it read as temper."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses after the opening. He has skipped the thanksgiving entirely — the only letter of his that does — and gone straight to *I am astonished at how quickly you are walking away*. See the note on Galatians 1:6–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first party named"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But even if **we ourselves**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He starts with himself and his own team."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rule he is about to state binds the man stating it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Or **an angel out of heaven**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The highest possible authority short of God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had received his gospel *by the revelation of Jesus Christ* — he says so in the next paragraph — and he still puts an angel under the rule."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Later in the same letter: *Satan himself transforms himself into an angel of light.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Were to announce to you **any good news other than the good news we announced to you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **par' ho** — alongside, contrary to, beside what we preached."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *the opposite of*. Beside it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Galatian teachers were not denying Christ. They were adding circumcision."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let him be **accursed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anathema** — devoted to destruction, handed over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word the Greek Old Testament uses for what was under the ban at Jericho."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repetition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have said it before and **I say it now again**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the whole thing twice, and the second time he changes one word: *if anyone announces to you any good news other than **the one you received**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "First time: what we preached. Second time: what you received."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He shifts the standard from the messenger to the message as it landed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why say it twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because it reads as anger the first time. Repeating it makes it a considered position rather than an outburst."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does the same thing at the end of the letter with the summary: *for in Christ Jesus neither circumcision nor uncircumcision avails anything, but a new creation.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For do I now persuade men, or God? Or **do I seek to please men?** For if I still pleased men, I would not be a bondservant of Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the answer to the obvious reaction. He is aware of how it sounds."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was at stake"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter's own summary of the alternative: *you have become estranged from Christ, you who attempt to be justified by law; you have fallen from grace.* See the note on Galatians 5:2–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The letter's tone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*O foolish Galatians! Who has bewitched you?* And: *I am afraid for you, lest I have laboured for you in vain.* And: *I could wish to be present with you now and to change my tone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the middle of it, the paragraph about arriving ill and being welcomed like an angel of God. See the note on Galatians 4:12–16."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who the rule binds first.** Himself. Paul puts his own name at the head of the sentence, then an angel — and then says the whole thing again so nobody can put it down to heat."}]}, "Galatians 2:15–16": {"title": "Not by works of law", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The sentence that comes out of a public argument in a dining room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter came to Antioch and ate with Gentile believers. Then men came from James, and *he withdrew and separated himself, fearing those who were of the circumcision*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the rest of the Jews also played the hypocrite with him, **so that even Barnabas was carried away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I said to Peter before them all, \"**If you, being a Jew, live in the manner of Gentiles… why do you compel Gentiles to live as Jews?**\"* See the note on Galatians 2:11–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This paragraph is what Paul said next, and it is not clear where the speech to Peter ends and the letter resumes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The concession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are **Jews by birth, not sinners out of the nations**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phusei Ioudaioi** — Jews by nature."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hamartoloi** — sinners. The standard Jewish description of Gentiles, quoted rather than endorsed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul grants the distinction and then dismantles it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And yet **we know that a person is not made right by the works of the law but through faith in Jesus Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dikaioutai** — is justified, declared righteous. A legal verdict, not a moral process."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ex ergon nomou** — out of works of law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What *works of law* means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole system of doing what the law prescribes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Galatian dispute was specifically about circumcision, food laws and calendar — but Paul's argument is not limited to those. He says *no flesh*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three statements"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says it three times in one verse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not made right by the works of the law.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that we might be made right by faith in Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And not by the works of the law.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice negative, once positive, in a single sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **by the works of the law no flesh will ever be made right**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ou dikaiothesetai pasa sarx** — not shall be justified all flesh."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is Psalm 143: *do not enter into judgment with Your servant, for **in Your sight no one living is righteous***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who *we* is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **even we** put our trust in Christ Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kai hemeis** — we also."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jews by birth, with every advantage the law provided. Paul includes himself in the group that had to do the same thing the Gentiles did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this is the letter's thesis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything after it is argument for it: Abraham believed God, the law came four hundred and thirty years later, the tutor, the sons, the two covenants, the fruit of the Spirit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse two lines later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have been crucified with Christ; it is no longer I who live, but Christ lives in me… **and I do not set aside the grace of God; for if righteousness comes through the law, then Christ died in vain**.\"* See the note on Galatians 2:20–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dorean** — for nothing, gratuitously. The same word as *freely*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter says about it later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"We believe that **through the grace of the Lord Jesus Christ we shall be saved in the same manner as they**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the Jerusalem council, in Acts 15. The man rebuked in Antioch stands up and argues Paul's case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What James writes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You see then that **a man is justified by works, and not by faith only**.* See the note on James 2:21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two apostles, the same verb, two different questions. Paul is asking how a person is declared righteous. James is asking what a claim to faith looks like when nothing follows from it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The repetition.** Three statements of the same thing in one verse — twice negatively, once positively — closing on a line borrowed from a psalm."}]}, "Galatians 2:19–20": {"title": "The faith of the Son of God", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*The life I now live in the flesh, I live by the faith **of** the Son of God, who loved me and gave Himself up for me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is how the older English versions render it, and it is the more literal reading of the Greek. It is worth knowing why, because it changes what the verse asks of you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Greek"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Pistis Christou** — *faith of Christ*. The genitive most naturally reads as belonging to Him: His faith, His faithfulness. Rendering it *faith in the Son of God* requires taking the genitive as an object instead, which is possible but is the less obvious reading of the words as they stand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it fits the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look at what Paul has just said, one clause earlier:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "*It is no longer I who live — Christ lives in me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has already removed himself as the subject. To then say that the life is sustained by the strength of his own believing would put him straight back at the centre of a sentence he has just moved himself out of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read as *the faith of the Son of God*, the whole thing runs one direction: I died, He lives in me, and the faith I live by is His."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that does to a struggling reader"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It relocates the pressure. If the Christian life runs on the quality of my trust, then every flat morning is a threat. If it runs on His faithfulness, then my believing is a response to something already holding, not the thing doing the holding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's language elsewhere runs the same way — *faithful is He who calls you, who also will do it*, and *if we are faithless, He remains faithful, for He cannot deny Himself.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The four words at the end.** *Who loved me and gave Himself up for me.* Paul writes it singular, which he almost never does. In the middle of the most combative letter he wrote, the argument narrows to one man and the love aimed at him — which is the same move the whole verse makes."}]}, "Galatians 3:1–5": {"title": "One question for you", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul stops arguing and asks a question he says he only needs one answer to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Galatians, **what has come over you?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *O foolish Galatians* — **anoetoi**, without understanding, not thinking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names them by their region and calls them senseless, twice in five verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The spell"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who has **put you under a spell**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ebaskanen** — bewitched, from the word for the evil eye."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not saying it literally. He is saying nothing else explains it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had seen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You, **before whose very eyes Jesus Christ was set out plainly**, right there among you, **as the crucified One**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **proegraphe** — placarded, posted up publicly, the way a public notice was displayed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tense of *crucified* is perfect: crucified and standing crucified."}, {"k": "p", "t": "None of them were in Jerusalem. What was placarded in front of them was the preaching."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is **only one thing I want to learn from you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He sets aside the whole argument and reduces it to a single test."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The test"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Did you **receive the Spirit by doing the works of the law, or by hearing and believing**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *by works of law or by the hearing of faith*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a question about their own experience, and they know the answer. Something happened when they believed, and it did not happen because they had kept anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not argue from Scripture here. He argues from what they remember."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You **began in the Spirit** — are you now **to be brought to completion by the flesh**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *brought to completion* is **epiteleisthe** — to finish off, to perfect."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question is about the second half of the process, not the first. Nobody in Galatia was denying how it started."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The suffering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Did you go through **so much suffering for nothing** — **if it really was for nothing**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Acts 13 and 14 record what happened when Paul was in that region: driven out of Antioch, threatened at Iconium, and stoned and left for dead at Lystra."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The converts had paid for this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**If it really was for nothing** — Paul adds the clause and then leaves it. He does not finish the thought."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So then, the One who **supplies the Spirit to you and works miracles among you** — does He do it **because you keep the works of the law, or because you heard and believed**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks it a second time, in the present tense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not only how it started — how it is still running. He says miracles are currently happening among them, and asks what the supply is connected to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *supplies* is **epichoregeo**, the word for the wealthy citizen who paid for the whole chorus out of his own pocket. Peter uses it of God and then of the reader. See the note on 2 Peter 1:5–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham, believing God, counted as righteousness — four hundred and thirty years before the law. See the note on Galatians 3:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul answers his own question with a case older than the thing in dispute."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The evidence he calls.** Not a text. Their own memory of what happened when they heard, and what is still happening among them now."}]}, "Galatians 3:10–14": {"title": "\"By becoming a curse for us\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes the law against itself and then says what was done about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is under a curse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone who **rests on the works of the law** is under a curse.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not everyone who breaks it. Everyone who rests on it — who stands on their own keeping of it as their position before God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Cursed is everyone who does not continue in ALL the things** written in the book of the law, to do them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 27, the words Israel shouted *Amen* to from Mount Ebal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The killing word is *all*. Not most, not the important ones, not on balance. Everything, continuously, without a gap."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James puts the same thing another way: *whoever keeps the whole law and yet stumbles in one point is guilty of all.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two systems, side by side"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul sets the operating principles next to each other:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*the one who is right with God **will live by faith*** — Habakkuk", "*the law is not of faith; its word is rather, **the person who does these things will live by them*** — Leviticus 18"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "One says believe. The other says do. They are not two routes to the same place; they run on incompatible currency."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Christ did with the curse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ has **bought us out from under the curse** of the law **by becoming a curse for us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *bought us out* is **exagorazo** — to purchase out of the market, the word used of buying a slave in order to free him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He did not lift the curse off. He took the position: *becoming a curse for us.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proof text"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Cursed is everyone who hangs on a tree.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 21. The body of an executed criminal hung on a tree was under God's curse and had to be taken down before nightfall so the land would not be defiled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reaches for the one verse in the law that puts a curse on a hanged man, and says that is the transaction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two purpose clauses, and both are given:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **the blessing of Abraham** might come to the nations in Christ Jesus, and so that through faith we might **receive the promise of the Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The curse was taken so the blessing could go somewhere it had never been — *to the nations.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the blessing of Abraham is the one credited in Genesis 15:6, before there was any law to keep. See the note on Genesis 15:6."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul asks them at the top of the chapter.** *Did you receive the Spirit by the works of the law, or by the hearing of faith?* He does not argue the theology first. He asks them to remember how it started."}]}, "Galatians 3:15–18": {"title": "\"Not seeds, but Seed\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul argues from contract law, and then from a singular noun."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The everyday example"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let me speak **the way people speak in ordinary matters**. Even a covenant made between human beings, once it has been **ratified**, is not set aside by anyone, and **nothing is added to it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He starts with what anybody in that world knew about wills and contracts. Once witnessed and sealed, a document stands. You cannot append to it later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The singular"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The promises were spoken to Abraham **and to his Seed**. It does not say, **\"and to seeds,\" as though to many**, but as of one: **and to your Seed, who is Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole weight is on a grammatical number."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **zera** is a collective — like the English *offspring*, it can mean one descendant or a nation. Paul knows this; he uses it collectively himself four verses later: *if you are Christ's, then you are Abraham's seed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His point is not that the collective reading is impossible. It is that the singular is available, and that it is the one that resolves the promise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The law, which came **four hundred and thirty years afterward**, cannot cancel a covenant that was **confirmed beforehand** by God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the argument in one line. The promise is older."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A later document does not annul an earlier ratified one, and Paul has just established that from ordinary practice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is at stake"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So as to leave the promise **with nothing in it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is to make void, to nullify."}, {"k": "p", "t": "If keeping the law were the condition, the promise would have been quietly converted into a contract with terms — and the terms would be the thing that mattered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two systems"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **if the inheritance comes by the law, then it no longer comes by promise**; but **God gave it to Abraham by a promise**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul treats them as mutually exclusive. Not two routes to the same place — two different kinds of thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An inheritance and a wage cannot be the same transaction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abraham had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No law, no tabernacle, no priesthood, no commandments. A promise, a night sky, and a covenant cut while he was asleep. See the notes on Genesis 15:6 and Genesis 15:12–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law was for, then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks it himself in the next verse: *what purpose then does the law serve?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His answer: *it was added because of transgressions, till the Seed should come to whom the promise was made… **the law was our tutor to bring us to Christ**, that we might be justified by faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A guardian escorting a child somewhere, and the escort's job ends on arrival."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How closely Paul reads.** A tense settled the resurrection for Jesus against the Sadducees; a singular settles the inheritance for Paul against the Judaisers. Both arguments would collapse if the words were approximate. See the note on Matthew 22:23–33."}]}, "Galatians 3:19–22": {"title": "Why the law was given", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks the question his own argument forces, and answers it in four parts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What purpose, then, does the law serve?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just proved that the promise to Abraham came four hundred and thirty years before Sinai, and that a later document cannot annul an earlier covenant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which leaves the obvious problem: then why was it given at all?"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was **added because of transgressions**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **prosetethe** — added, placed alongside. Not the main thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Because of transgressions** — the phrase is compressed and Paul unpacks it elsewhere: *by the law is the knowledge of sin*, and *I would not have known sin except through the law*. See the note on Romans 7:7–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The term"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until **the Seed should come to whom the promise had been made**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An expiry date, built into the answer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has already argued about that word: *He does not say, \"And to seeds,\" as of many, but as of one, \"And to your Seed,\" who is Christ.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it was delivered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was put in place **through angels, by the hand of a mediator**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two intermediaries. Stephen says the same at his trial — *you who have received the law by the direction of angels* — and Hebrews: *the word spoken through angels proved steadfast*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument from arithmetic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **a mediator is not a mediator for one party alone, but God is one**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One of the densest sentences Paul ever wrote, and the point is structural: a mediator implies two sides. The law is an agreement with terms on both ends."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise to Abraham had one party. God swore it by Himself, and Abraham was asleep when the covenant was cut. See the note on Genesis 22:15–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The denial"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Is the law, then, **set against the promises of God? Certainly not**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me genoito** — Paul's strongest negative."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just spent a chapter setting them in opposition and refuses the conclusion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The concession he will not make"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **if a law had been given that could give life, righteousness really would have come by the law**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Conditional, contrary to fact. If such a law existed, Paul says he would say so."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The problem is not that the route is forbidden. It is that it does not go anywhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Scripture did instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the Scripture shut everything up together under sin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sunekleisen** — locked up together, enclosed on all sides. It is used of a net closing on fish."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything, together. Paul says the same in Romans: *God has committed them all to disobedience, that He might have mercy on all.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **the promise, by faith in Jesus Christ, might be given to those who believe**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The shutting-up has a stated purpose, and the purpose is the giving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But before faith came, we were kept under guard by the law, **kept for the faith which would afterward be revealed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore the law was **our tutor to bring us to Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paidagogos** — not a teacher, but the household slave who walked a boy to school and kept him out of trouble on the way. The job ended when the boy came of age."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul carries the image straight into the next chapter. See the note on Galatians 4:1–3."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul refuses to say.** That the law is against the promises. He says it was added, that it had a term, that it came through two intermediaries, and that it could not give life — and then denies the conclusion anybody would draw."}]}, "Galatians 3:23–25": {"title": "Kept until faith came", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's word for what the law was, and it is a job title from a Greek household."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The custody"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before this faith came, we were **kept under guard by the law, shut in and waiting**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs. The Greek is **ephrouroumetha** — held in a garrison, guarded — and **sunkleiomenoi**, shut up together, enclosed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb he used four verses earlier: *the Scripture shut everything up together under sin*. See the note on Galatians 3:19–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was waiting for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the faith that was going to be revealed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The custody has a terminus built into it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the law was **our guardian**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paidagogos**, and it does not mean teacher."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A **paidagogos** was a household slave who walked a boy to school, carried his things, kept him from trouble on the road, and handed him over at the door. He taught nothing. His job was to get the child there and keep him safe on the way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The post ended when the boy came of age."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keeping us and **leading us to Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *unto Christ* — the destination."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That we might be **made right by faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not made right by the guardian. The guardian delivers the boy to the place where it happens."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **now that faith has come, we are no longer under a guardian**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *we are no longer under a paidagogos*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that the guardian was bad, or failed, or was dismissed for cause. The child arrived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul is not saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That the law was cruel. A **paidagogos** was strict by design and the household paid for one because the road was dangerous."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's argument is entirely about the term of the appointment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **you are all sons of God through faith in Christ Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **huioi** — sons, adults, not **nepioi**, infants."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *there is neither Jew nor Greek, there is neither slave nor free, there is neither male nor female; for **you are all one in Christ Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The picture continued"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next chapter takes the same household further: an heir who owns everything and is indistinguishable from a slave until the day his father set. See the note on Galatians 4:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Galatians were being told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To take on circumcision and the calendar. Paul's answer is that they are proposing to hire the escort back after arriving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it plainly at the end of chapter 4: *how is it that you turn again to the weak and beggarly elements, to which you desire again to be in bondage?*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the job was.** Not to teach and not to make anybody righteous. To keep a child safe and get him to the door — and Paul's whole point is that the appointment had an end date written into it."}]}, "Galatians 3:26–29": {"title": "\"Then you are Abraham's seed\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent three chapters arguing that the promise to Abraham came before the law and does not run through it. Here is where the argument lands on the reader."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three divisions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is neither Jew nor Greek, there is neither slave nor free, there is neither male nor female.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Those were the three great dividing lines of the ancient world — race, status, sex. A common Jewish morning prayer of the period thanked God for not making the speaker a Gentile, a slave, or a woman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes the same three categories and says that in Christ they do not determine standing. He is not abolishing the differences; he is saying they no longer decide who inherits."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Clothed yourselves with Christ\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is putting on a garment. In the Roman world a boy coming of age exchanged his child's toga for the *toga virilis* — the moment he became a legal adult with rights of inheritance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just used exactly that picture in the verses before: an heir who is a child is no different from a slave until the appointed time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And if you belong to Christ, then you are Abraham's seed, and heirs according to the promise.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *like* Abraham's seed. Paul has already argued that the promise was made to Abraham and to his *seed*, singular, and that the seed is Christ. So the route runs: Christ is the heir; you belong to Christ; therefore the promise is yours."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The inheritance arrives by belonging, not by descent and not by performance."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is inherited.** In Genesis it was land, descendants and blessing for all nations. Paul has already named the piece he is most interested in three verses earlier — *so that the blessing of Abraham might come to the Gentiles*, and the promise of the Spirit received through faith. The inheritance he is arguing for is not smaller than Genesis. It is wider."}]}, "Galatians 3:6–9": {"title": "Blessed together with Abraham", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul settles the Galatian question by going back four hundred and thirty years before the law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The precedent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is just as it was with Abraham: **he believed God, and it was counted to him as righteousness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 15:6, quoted here and in Romans 4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's argument is chronological. The verse comes before circumcision in Genesis 17, and centuries before Sinai."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Counted** — the Greek is **logizomai**, the accounting term. It is entered on a ledger by somebody else. See the note on Romans 4:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Understand, then, that **it is the people of faith who are the sons of Abraham**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *those of faith* — **hoi ek pisteos**, those out of faith."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not those of descent, and not those of observance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the sentence the whole letter turns on. The Galatians were being told they needed to become descendants of Abraham by circumcision; Paul says the descent is already established by the same thing Abraham had."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Scripture that sees ahead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the Scripture, seeing ahead of time** that God would set the nations right by faith, **announced the good news to Abraham long before it came**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **proeuengelisato** — pre-evangelised. Paul says the gospel was preached to Abraham."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He personifies Scripture as the one doing the foreseeing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was announced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**In you all the nations shall be blessed**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "From Genesis 12, and repeated in the oath after Moriah. See the note on Genesis 22:15–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reads *all the nations* as the point. The Gentiles were in the promise from the first sentence of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So then, **those who are of faith are blessed together with Abraham, who believed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *blessed **with** believing Abraham* — **sun**, alongside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not blessed like him. Blessed with him, in the same act."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For as many as are **of the works of the law are under the curse**; for it is written, \"Cursed is everyone who does not continue in all things which are written in the book of the law, to do them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes Deuteronomy, and the sting is in **all things**. The law's own terms are total."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *Christ has redeemed us from the curse of the law, **having become a curse for us** — for it is written, \"Cursed is everyone who hangs on a tree.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the letter exists"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Men had come to Galatia teaching that Gentile converts must be circumcised. Paul opens the letter without his usual thanksgiving — *I marvel that you are turning away so soon* — and this chapter begins *O foolish Galatians! Who has bewitched you?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse the argument lands on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That **the blessing of Abraham might come upon the Gentiles in Christ Jesus**, that we might receive the promise of the Spirit through faith.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the seed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He does not say, \"And to seeds,\" as of many, but as of one, \"And to your Seed,\" **who is Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He rests an argument on a singular noun."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order Paul insists on.** Faith counted as righteousness first, then circumcision, then four hundred and thirty years, then the law. He is not arguing about which is better — he is arguing about which came first, and what that makes the other one."}]}, "Galatians 4:1–3": {"title": "The heir and the slave", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul draws a picture from a wealthy household and says it is a picture of the reader's own past."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As long as **the heir is a child**, he is **no different from a slave**, though **everything belongs to him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three clauses, and the third is the one that makes it strange."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The estate is legally his. Nothing is in doubt about the ownership."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **child**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **nepios** — an infant, literally *non-speaking*. Paul uses it of the immature: *when I was a child, I spoke as a child*, and of those still on milk."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer of Hebrews uses the same word of readers who should be teaching by now. See the note on Hebrews 5:11–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No different from a slave.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not worse than a slave and not better. In terms of what he can actually do with the property, indistinguishable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The supervision"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is **under guardians and stewards**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words: **epitropous**, guardians of the person, and **oikonomous**, managers of the property."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roman law had exactly this arrangement. A minor heir had both, and neither answered to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The end date"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until **the day his father has appointed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **prothesmias** — a set term, a legally fixed date."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The father sets it. Not the boy, not the guardians, and not the calendar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The application"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **so it was with us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul turns the picture on his readers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were under"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When we were children, we were **held as slaves under the elemental things of the world**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **stoicheia tou kosmou** — the elements, the rudiments. It is the word for the letters of the alphabet, and for the basic constituents of a thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses it again at the end of the chapter, about the Galatians turning back: *how is it that you turn again to **the weak and beggarly elements**, to which you desire again to be in bondage? **You observe days and months and seasons and years**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **when the fullness of the time had come, God sent forth His Son**, born of a woman, born under the law, **to redeem those who were under the law**, that we might **receive the adoption as sons**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The appointed day arrived. See the note on Galatians 4:4–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for adoption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Huiothesia** — from **huios**, son, and **tithemi**, to place. The placing of a son."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Roman law it was a legal act that transferred a person into a family with full inheritance rights, and it could not be undone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proof"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And because you are sons, **God has sent forth the Spirit of His Son into your hearts, crying out, \"Abba, Father!\"**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the Spirit is sent **because** they are sons, not in order to make them so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore you are **no longer a slave but a son**, and if a son, **then an heir of God through Christ**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the illustration does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That the child was mistreated, or that the guardians were cruel, or that the estate was ever at risk."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole difference in the picture is the date the father set."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was always true and what changed.** The estate belonged to him the entire time. What changed is that he came of age — and Paul says the arrangement before that was indistinguishable from slavery."}]}, "Galatians 4:12–16": {"title": "The welcome you gave me", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul stops arguing for five verses and talks about the first time he came."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plea"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Brothers, **I am begging you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **deomai** — to plead out of need. The tone changes completely from the chapters either side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exchange"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Become like me, **for I became like you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had described what that cost in the first letter to Corinth: *to those who are under the law, as under the law… to those who are without law, as without law… **I have become all things to all men***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clearance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have **not wronged me at all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He removes any suggestion that this is personal grievance before saying anything else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he stopped there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You know that it was **because of a weakness of the flesh** that I preached the good news to you **the first time**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **astheneian tes sarkos** — bodily weakness, infirmity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had not planned to preach in Galatia. Something physical stopped him there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the New Testament says what it was. Paul mentions *a thorn in the flesh* in 2 Corinthians and does not identify that either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the trial that was in my flesh **you did not treat with contempt**, and you **did not turn away from me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *treat with contempt* is **exoutheneo** — to reject as worthless. The other verb is literally *you did not spit* — a gesture used against illness and the evil eye in that world."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the condition was, it was visible and it was the kind of thing people reacted to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The welcome"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You welcomed me **as an angel of God, as Christ Jesus Himself**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where, then, is **the blessing you spoke of in those days**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *where is your blessedness* — the happiness they had at the time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For I can bear you witness that, **had it been possible, you would have torn out your own eyes and handed them to me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The detail has made readers wonder about his eyes ever since. He ends this letter *see with what large letters I have written to you with my own hand*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Illumination keeps the sentence as it stands and offers no diagnosis."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **have I become your enemy now, because I tell you the truth**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **aletheuon** — telling the truth to you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not soften anything in the letter. He asks whether the telling is what changed things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the allegory of the two women and the sentence about freedom. See the notes on Galatians 4:21–27 and Galatians 4:28–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hardest argument in his letters has a personal paragraph inserted in the middle of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says two verses on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My little children, for whom **I labour in birth again until Christ is formed in you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **odino** — the word for labour pains."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul, writing as a mother."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he reminds them of.** Not his credentials. That he arrived ill, that they did not recoil, and that they were happy — and then he asks what happened to that."}]}, "Galatians 4:21–27": {"title": "Two women and two covenants", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes a family story out of Genesis and says it was always about two systems."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Tell me, **you who want to live under the law: do you not listen to what the law itself says?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is about to argue against law-keeping from the law itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two sons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abraham had **two sons, one by the slave woman and one by the free woman**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ishmael and Isaac. Hagar and Sarah."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The difference"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The son of the slave woman was **born in the ordinary way of the flesh**, and the son of the free woman was **born through the promise**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ishmael was conceived by Abram and Hagar on Sarai's suggestion, when the couple decided the promise needed help. He was born to a man of eighty-six by ordinary means."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac was born to a man of a hundred and a woman of ninety past childbearing. Hebrews says of Abraham *as good as dead*. See the note on Hebrews 11:8–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's line is between what a couple can manage and what only a promise produces."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These things **carry a deeper meaning**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **allegoroumena** — spoken allegorically."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not denying the history. He treats Genesis as an account of things that happened and as carrying a second sense."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The identification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **two women are two covenants**.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Hagar** — *the one from **Mount Sinai**, bearing children into slavery*", "**Sarah** — unnamed here, but *the Jerusalem above is free*"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The geography"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For this Hagar is **Mount Sinai in Arabia**, and she **answers to the Jerusalem that now is**, and she is **in slavery along with her children**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *answers to* is **sustoichei** — stands in the same column, corresponds in a list."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul aligns Sinai with the current Jerusalem — the city with the functioning temple, the priesthood and the sacrifices."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a startling thing to write to Jewish readers, and it is the sharpest sentence in the letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other city"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the Jerusalem above is free**, and she is **the mother of us all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews uses the same phrase: *you have come to Mount Zion and to the city of the living God, **the heavenly Jerusalem***. See the note on Hebrews 12:18–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two mountains again: Sinai and Zion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rejoice, **barren woman**, you who never bore a child; **break out and shout aloud**, you who were never in labor — for **the desolate woman has many more children than she who has a husband**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 54, the chapter directly after the suffering servant. See the note on Isaiah 54:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes it because it is about a woman with no children being told to prepare for a crowd — which is Sarah's situation, and which he says is the reader's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which side the Galatians were choosing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were being told to take on circumcision. Paul's argument places that on the Hagar side of the table — the side that produced a son by ordinary means because somebody could not wait."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now we, brothers, are **children of promise, just as Isaac was**.* See the note on Galatians 4:28–31."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul does with the story.** Reads it as two ways of getting a son — one a couple can arrange and one only a promise produces — and puts the current temple city in the first column."}]}, "Galatians 4:28–31": {"title": "Children of the free woman", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul applies the allegory, and the application has a hard edge on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the readers are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now we, brothers, are **children of promise, just as Isaac was**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not children of Abraham in general. He picks the son and names him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac's distinction is not his character. It is how he came to exist. See the note on Galatians 4:21–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The friction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But as **the one born in the ordinary way of the flesh persecuted** in those days **the one born according to the Spirit**, so it is now.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Genesis account is short about it: at the weaning feast Sarah saw Ishmael *mocking*. See the note on Genesis 21:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **metsacheq** — playing, laughing, mocking. It is the same root as Isaac's own name, which means *laughter*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reads it as persecution, and says the pattern is still running."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"So it is now\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Galatian churches were being pressured by teachers insisting on circumcision. Paul had been driven out of Antioch, stoned at Lystra, and hounded from city to city on that first journey."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He treats the current pressure as the old one continuing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet **what does the Scripture say? Cast out the slave woman and her son**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 21, and the words are Sarah's. Paul quotes a family quarrel as Scripture speaking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He drops one word: Sarah said *cast out this bondwoman **and her son**, for the son of this bondwoman shall not be heir with my son, **namely with Isaac***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the son of the slave woman shall never be heir together with the son of the free woman**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point is inheritance, not affection. Genesis records that Abraham loved Ishmael and that the demand *cut Abraham deeply*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two cannot share the estate. That is the whole argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Genesis does with Ishmael"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God tells Abraham to do what Sarah says, and then: *also of the son of the bondwoman I will make a nation, because he is your seed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the desert, when the water runs out, *God heard the voice of the lad* — and Hagar is shown a well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ishmael is provided for. He is not made the heir."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So then, brothers, **we are not children of the slave woman but of the free**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated as a fact about the readers, not as a goal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is **for freedom that Christ has made us free**. Stand fast therefore, and **do not be entangled again with a yoke of bondage**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter break hides it. The famous sentence of Galatians 5 is the *therefore* of this paragraph."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being cast out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not people. Paul is arguing about a system — Sinai in the Hagar column, and the reader in the other one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter's own summary: *you have become estranged from Christ, you who attempt to be justified by law; you have fallen from grace.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the two cannot both stay.** Not because one son was worse. Because an estate has one heir, and Paul says the two ways of getting a son cannot share it."}]}, "Galatians 4:4–7": {"title": "\"Born under the law\" — and the four steps out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul compresses the whole gospel into two sentences, and the logic runs in a chain where each link depends on the one before it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chain"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**God sent forth His Son** — born of a woman, born under the law", "**to redeem those who were under the law**", "**so that we might receive the adoption as sons**", "**and because you are sons, God has sent forth the Spirit of His Son**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two sendings, and the second follows the first. The Spirit is not sent to make sons; He is sent *because* they are sons. Paul is careful about the order, and it is the opposite of how the sequence is often felt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Born under the law\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Christ is described as entering the exact position the people He came for were in. Not visiting it — born into it, under the same obligation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is how the redemption works in Paul's argument: someone had to be genuinely inside the arrangement to buy anybody out of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit of His Son into your hearts, crying out, Abba, Father.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Notice who is doing the crying here. In Romans 8 it is *we* who cry Abba; here it is the Spirit. Paul says it both ways within two letters, which suggests he did not think of them as different events."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word *Abba* is Aramaic, left untranslated in a Greek letter to Galatia. See the note on Romans 8:15–17 for what it carries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are no longer a slave but a son; and if a son, then an heir of God through Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Slave, son, heir — three words, and the movement between them is the whole letter in miniature. Paul is writing to people being persuaded to go back under law, and his argument is not that it is wrong so much as that it is *backwards*: it is walking out of the family and asking for a job."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"The fullness of the time.\"** Paul's phrase for the timing. Not that the world was at its best, but that a particular moment had filled up — a Roman peace, Greek as a common language, roads, and a scattered Jewish diaspora with synagogues in every port. He treats the arrangement of an era as deliberate."}]}, "Galatians 5:1": {"title": "Stand firm in freedom", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and it is the hinge of the letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter division hides it. This is the *therefore* of the allegory that ends chapter 4 — Hagar and Sarah, the slave woman cast out, and *we are not children of the slave woman but of the free*. See the note on Galatians 4:28–31."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So stand firm **in the freedom by which Christ has set us free**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *for freedom Christ has made us free* — **te eleutheria**, in the dative, which can be read as the purpose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Freedom is named as the point of the setting free, not as a by-product of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Stand firm.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **stekete** — stand fast, hold your ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses it in Ephesians of the armour — *having done all, to stand* — and in Philippians: *stand fast in the Lord*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is to be advanced or won. The instruction is to remain where they were put."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is already done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By which **Christ has set us free**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is aorist. It happened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not asking them to get free. He is telling free people not to go back in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **do not let yourselves be harnessed again into a yoke of slavery**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **zugo douleias** — a yoke of bondage. A yoke is the crossbar laid over working animals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Again** — the Greek is **palin**. They had been under one and had come out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the yoke was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Circumcision and the calendar. Paul says so in the next verse: *if you become circumcised, Christ will profit you nothing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter used the same image at the council in Jerusalem: *why do you test God by putting a yoke on the neck of the disciples which neither our fathers nor we were able to bear?* See the note on Acts 15:6–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other yoke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take **My yoke** upon you and learn from Me… **for My yoke is easy and My burden is light**.\"* See the note on Matthew 11:28–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two yokes in the New Testament. One is described as slavery and one as rest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have become estranged from Christ, you who attempt to be justified by law; **you have fallen from grace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exepesate** — fallen out of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's own definition of the phrase is in the clause before it: *you who attempt to be justified by law*. See the note on Galatians 5:2–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the freedom is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **you were called to liberty; only do not use liberty as an opportunity for the flesh**, but through love serve one another.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *opportunity* is **aphorme** — the bridgehead word, the same one he used of sin using the commandment as a launching point. See the note on Romans 7:7–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names both ditches in the same chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The summary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **all the law is fulfilled in one word**, even in this: \"**You shall love your neighbour as yourself**.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The shape of the instruction.** Not go, not fight, not achieve. Stand — and the only thing named as a danger is being put back under something they had already come out from."}]}, "Galatians 5:16–18": {"title": "\"If the Spirit is leading you\" — what Paul concludes from it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses that get quoted for the fight and almost never for the conclusion. Paul describes a genuine conflict, and then draws from it a conclusion nobody expects."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise comes first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Walk by the Spirit, and you will not carry out what the flesh craves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is worth noticing. The instruction is *walk by the Spirit* — a positive, forward thing. The not-carrying-out is presented as the **result**, not as a second command to be worked at."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say fight the craving and then walk. He says walk, and the other follows. In Greek the second half is emphatic — a strong negative, closer to *you certainly will not*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fight is real"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The flesh sets its desire against the Spirit, and the Spirit against the flesh; the two of them stand opposed to each other, so that you do not do the things you want to do.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No spiritual life is being sold here as effortless. Paul describes two settled opposing pulls, and he says plainly that they get in the way of what a person means to do. Anyone who has tried to live this knows the sentence is accurate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But notice which two things are opposed. Not the believer against sin. The Spirit against the flesh. The believer is the field this happens in, not one of the two combatants — which is a large part of why the instruction is *walk*, not *win*."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion he draws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here is the sentence that gets left off:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "But if the Spirit is leading you, **you are not under the law.**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Given everything before it, you would expect the conclusion to be about victory, or about proving something. Instead Paul uses the reality of the Spirit's leading to settle a question about **jurisdiction**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole letter is about people being pushed back under law-keeping. So Paul's argument runs: you are being led by the Spirit, therefore that is not where you live. He treats being led as evidence of a legal standing, not as a performance to be assessed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why that matters for guidance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "People bring this verse to decisions — am I being led? Paul brings it to identity. He does not use it to make anyone anxious about whether their leading is good enough; he uses it to tell them what their being led already proves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the same move he makes in Romans 8, where *led by the Spirit* is immediately followed by adoption, Abba, and inheritance. Both times, leading is raised as evidence of sonship rather than as a test of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"the things you want to do.\"** The line is often read as defeat — I cannot do what I want. In context Paul is describing why a person cannot simply execute their good intentions by willpower, which is exactly the argument for walking by the Spirit rather than trying harder. It is a diagnosis, and the prescription is in the first line of the paragraph."}]}, "Galatians 5:19–21": {"title": "The works of the flesh", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul lists them, and the list is longer and more ordinary than people expect."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The works of the flesh are **out in the open, plain for anyone to see**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phanera** — manifest, evident."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No detection is required. They surface."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "***Works** of the flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Plural, and it is set against **fruit** of the Spirit in the next paragraph, which is singular. Paul chose both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Works are produced; fruit grows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventeen items, and they fall into groups:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sexual** — *adultery, sexual immorality, uncleanness, indecency*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Religious** — *idolatry, sorcery*. The Greek for sorcery is **pharmakeia**, the root of *pharmacy* — it covered drugs and potions as well as spells."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Social** — *hatred, quarrels, jealousy, outbursts of wrath, selfish ambition, dissensions, factions, envy*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Violent and excessive** — *murders, drunkenness, wild parties*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proportion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight of the seventeen are relational. Quarrels, jealousy, ambition, dissensions, factions and envy are the ones a church would recognise in itself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is writing to congregations tearing at each other over circumcision, and he had already told them: *if you bite and devour one another, beware lest you be consumed by one another.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The catch-all"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **anything else of the same kind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list is open. He is describing a category, not compiling a code."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am telling you about these **ahead of time, just as I told you before**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said it in person and is saying it again in writing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wording of the verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That those who **make a practice of such things** will not inherit the kingdom of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **prassontes** — a present participle, continuous. Those who go on doing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It describes a habit rather than an incident, and Paul is careful with the tense."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, longsuffering, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, self-control**. Against such there is no law.* See the note on Galatians 5:22–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Fruit** is singular in the Greek — one fruit with nine facets, not nine separate achievements."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction that governs both lists"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verses before the works: *I say then: **walk in the Spirit, and you shall not fulfil the lust of the flesh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And after the fruit: *if we live in the Spirit, **let us also walk in the Spirit**.* See the note on Galatians 5:25–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lists are bracketed by the same instruction, twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does not offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A method for suppressing the first list. The chapter gives one instruction, and the fruit is described as growing rather than being assembled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last two items of the fruit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Gentleness, self-control.* Both are on the second list, and neither is described as produced by effort."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the list is mostly made of.** Not the spectacular sins. Eight of the seventeen are things that happen between people in a room — and Paul was writing to churches doing exactly that to each other."}]}, "Galatians 5:2–4": {"title": "What \"fallen away from grace\" is actually describing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Few verses have frightened more people than this one, and nearly always for the opposite of the reason Paul wrote it. Read quickly, \"you have fallen away from grace\" sounds like a warning that grace can run out — that a person can sin past the edge of it and be dropped. Read in the sentence Paul actually built, it says something closer to the reverse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Look at who he is describing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul names them in the same breath: *you who are being made right by the law*. That is the person in view. Not someone who has sinned badly — someone who has decided to earn their standing with God by keeping rules. The whole letter aims at that one move."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here is addressed to the person who has failed. It is addressed to the person who has started keeping score."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two pictures underneath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words carry the verse, and both are stranger in Greek than they sound in English."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**cut off** — the word means to be left with nothing to do, put out of work, made idle. Not cut in half.", "**fallen away** — a word used of a ship blown off its course, and of a flower dropping off its stem."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Put together, the picture is not of grace pulling back from a person. It is of a person stepping off the ground they were standing on. Christ has not run out of power; He has been *given nothing to do* by someone who decided to handle their standing themselves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says two lines later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes straight on: *we, through the Spirit, wait with longing for the righteousness we hope for* — and he says **we**. Having just written the hardest sentence in the letter, he puts himself back in the same boat as the people he wrote it to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not telling them what they have permanently become. He is telling them what their position costs them while they hold it, which is a different thing. That is why the same chapter opens with *Christ has set us free*. A person cannot stand on two grounds at once, and the ground they were reaching for cannot hold anyone up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this matters practically"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If grace could be forfeited by sinning, the remedy would be to sin less. Paul's remedy is the opposite: stop trying to be made right by what you do. The fall he describes is not downward, into failure. It is sideways, out of the only thing that was holding anyone."}, {"k": "note", "t": "A word about *grace* itself. In Greek it is not leniency, and it is not a mood God happens to be in. It runs from favour freely given through to the working of that favour in a person's life. So \"fallen away from grace\" is not \"fallen out of God's good books.\" It is nearer to having stepped out of the current that was doing the work."}]}, "Galatians 5:22–24": {"title": "The fruit the Spirit grows", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Nine words, and the noun in front of them is singular."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Fruit, not fruits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **fruit** the Spirit grows is…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One fruit with nine characteristics, not nine items to be collected separately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And set it against the phrase Paul used eight verses earlier: *the **works** of the flesh are evident.* Works, plural, and manufactured. Fruit, singular, and grown."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody grows fruit. A tree does, and what the farmer does is stay attached to the soil and the season."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose fruit it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The fruit **the Spirit** grows.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The genitive is His. It is not the believer's fruit produced with the Spirit's assistance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus said the same in the vine passage: *apart from Me you can do nothing*, and the branch's whole job is to remain. See the note on John 15:5–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The nine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Love, joy, peace** — Godward, and the first three."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Patience, kindness, goodness** — toward other people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Faithfulness, gentleness, self-control** — the inward three, and it is worth noticing where self-control lands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is last, and it is fruit. Not the root of the list but the ninth thing grown out of it. The one quality most people try to start with is the one Paul puts at the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is **no law standing against things like these**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent five chapters arguing against putting believers under law, and here is his answer to the obvious objection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing on that list needs legislating. No statute was ever passed against joy. Produce this and the law has no business with you — not because it was lowered, but because it has been outgrown."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it another way in Romans: *love is the fulfilment of the law.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the flesh"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Those who belong to Christ **have crucified** the flesh with its passions and its cravings.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Perfect tense — done, and standing done. Not *should crucify*, and not *are crucifying daily*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reports it as an accomplished fact about everyone who belongs to Christ, the same way he reports it in Romans 6: *our old man was crucified with Him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction in the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is only one, and it is four verses earlier: *walk in the Spirit, and you shall not fulfil the lust of the flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A promise attached to a direction of travel. Nothing in the paragraph tells anybody to work on the list."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul feared for them.** *You have become estranged from Christ, you who attempt to be justified by law; you have fallen from grace.* That is the letter's charge — not immorality. The Galatians' problem was that they had started trying, and Paul's remedy is a fruit that grows."}]}, "Galatians 5:25–26": {"title": "Keep in step", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two sentences at the end of the fruit passage, and the second one is oddly small after everything that came before it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The premise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If the Spirit is our life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a condition being tested. Paul is stating what is already true of the people he is writing to, and building the instruction on top of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then let us **keep in step** with the Spirit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **stoicheo** — a military word, to march in line, to keep rank. It is not the word Paul used earlier in the chapter for *walk in the Spirit* (**peripateo**, the ordinary word for going about)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This one is about following a lead you did not set."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order in the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Life first, then the walk. The stepping does not produce the life; it follows it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole letter is built against the reverse order. See the note on Galatians 3:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is warned against"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us **not become full of ourselves**, **provoking** one another, **envying** one another.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three items, and they run in sequence: conceit, then provocation, then envy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *full of ourselves* is **kenodoxos** — empty glory. *Provoking* is **prokaleo**, to challenge, to call somebody out to fight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why these three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just listed the works of the flesh and the fruit of the Spirit — nine of each, in the verses immediately before."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Having named adultery, idolatry and murder on one side, he ends the passage on conceit and rivalry between believers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The small things are the ones the churches in Galatia were actually doing to each other: *if you bite and devour one another, beware lest you be consumed by one another.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the fruit is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Love, joy, peace, longsuffering, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, self-control.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Fruit** is singular in the Greek. One fruit with nine facets — not nine separate achievements to be worked up one at a time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Against such there is no law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Brethren, if a man is overtaken in any trespass, you who are spiritual **restore such a one in a spirit of gentleness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *restore* is **katartizo** — the word for setting a broken bone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the first thing Paul asks of people who are keeping in step. See the note on Galatians 6:1–2."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the marching image gives you.** In a line, nobody sets their own pace and nobody is out in front. Conceit and rivalry are simply what breaking rank looks like."}]}, "Galatians 6:9–10": {"title": "At the right season", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses near the end of an angry letter, and they are about not stopping."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes just before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not be deceived, God is not mocked; for **whatever a man sows, that he will also reap**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For he who sows to his flesh will of the flesh reap corruption, but he who sows to the Spirit will of the Spirit reap everlasting life.* See the note on Galatians 6:7–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is agricultural, and these two verses continue it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And let us **not grow tired of doing good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me enkakomen** — let us not lose heart, not become cowardly, not give in to weariness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**To kalon poiountes** — doing the beautiful thing, the noble thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is assumed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That doing good is tiring."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say it should not be. He addresses what happens when it is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **at the right season we will reap**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kairo gar idio** — for in its own **kairos**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kairos** — the appointed moment, the right time, as against **chronos**, elapsed duration."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A harvest has a season, and the season belongs to the crop rather than the farmer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If we do not give up.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me ekluomenoi** — not becoming slack, not fainting. The word for a body going limp."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The harvest is promised. The only variable is whether the sower is still standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So then, **whenever we have opportunity**, let us do good to everyone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hos kairon echomen** — as we have **kairos**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word. The season for reaping is God's; the opportunity for doing is ours, and Paul uses one noun for both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scope"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **everyone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Pros pantas** — toward all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the household of faith only. Everyone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The priority"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **most of all to those who belong to the household of faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **malista de pros tous oikeious tes pisteos** — especially to the family members of the faith."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Oikeios** — of the household, domestic. The word for people who live under one roof."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone first, then especially the family."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not narrow the circle. He states the wide obligation and then names where the pressure falls hardest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter has been about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Circumcision, the law, and men who *want to make a good showing in the flesh* pressing Gentile believers to be circumcised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has called the Galatians foolish, described Peter's face-to-face rebuke, and said *I could wish to be present with you now and to change my tone*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says with his own hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*See with what large letters I have written to you **with my own hand**!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last paragraph is written by Paul personally rather than dictated, and he says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last thing he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But God forbid that I should boast except in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ… **for neither circumcision nor uncircumcision avails anything, but a new creation**.* See the note on Galatians 6:14–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From now on let no one trouble me, for **I bear in my body the marks of the Lord Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **stigmata** — brands, the marks burned on a slave or an animal to show ownership."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two uses of one word.** *In its own **kairos** we will reap* — the harvest's timing is not ours. *As we have **kairos**, let us do good* — the opportunity is."}]}, "Genesis 1:1–2": {"title": "Hovering over the water", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and everything the Bible says afterwards assumes them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the beginning, **God made it all** — the heavens above and the earth below.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is seven words: **bereshit bara Elohim et hashamayim ve'et ha'arets**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No argument for God's existence is offered here or anywhere else in Scripture. The book opens with Him already at work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Bara** — to create. In the Hebrew Bible its subject is always God. No human being ever **bara**s anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It appears three times in the creation account: the heavens and earth, the sea creatures, and man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Elohim** — plural in form, singular in the verbs that follow it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the Hebrew has a small word that does not translate: **et**, the marker of a direct object, used twice. The first and last letters of the alphabet, aleph and tav."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am the Alpha and the Omega, the Beginning and the End.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The state"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At first the earth was **formless and empty**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tohu va-vohu** — an assonant pair, untranslatable neatly. Waste and void."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah uses **tohu** of the desert, and says of the earth: *He did not create it in vain, He formed it to be inhabited*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the six days do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Formless, and empty — two problems."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Days one to three deal with the formlessness: light and dark, sky and sea, land and vegetation. Three environments."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Days four to six fill them: sun and moon, birds and fish, animals and man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Form, then fill. The structure is stated in verse 2 and worked through in order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The darkness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **darkness lay over the deep waters**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tehom** — the deep. The word for the primeval ocean, and for what bursts open in the flood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Spirit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But God was not far off: the **Spirit of God was already there, hovering** over the waters.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **merachefet** — a participle. Hovering, fluttering, brooding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb occurs three times in the whole Hebrew Bible. One of the others is in Moses' song: *as an eagle **stirs up its nest, hovers over its young**, spreading out its wings, taking them up, carrying them on its wings*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A bird over a nest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Spirit is here in verse 2. The word is in verse 3 — *and God said*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God… **all things were made through Him**, and without Him nothing was made that was made.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John opens his Gospel with Genesis' first three words and puts Christ inside them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "How long. Nothing in the Hebrew fixes an interval between verse 1 and verse 2, and the chapter is not written to answer that question."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other beginnings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the beginning was the Word.* John."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God.* Mark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth.* And then, at the far end of the book: *now I saw a new heaven and a new earth, for the first heaven and the first earth had passed away*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The New Testament's use"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By faith we understand that the worlds were framed by the word of God, so that **the things which are seen were not made of things which are visible**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Paul: *God, who commanded light to shine out of darkness, has shone in our hearts to give the light of the knowledge of the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ*. See the note on 2 Corinthians 4:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He treats a man's conversion as day one happening again."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word for what the Spirit was doing.** *Merachefet* — used three times in the Hebrew Bible, and one of the others is an eagle over a nest of chicks."}]}, "Genesis 1:26–31": {"title": "\"Let us make human beings in our own image\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The sixth day, and the account changes register for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Let us\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything before this is *let there be*. Here God speaks about the work rather than commanding it into existence — a deliberation, in the plural."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Christian readers have long heard the Trinity in it, and the New Testament supports the direction: all things were made through the Son, and the Spirit was hovering over the waters in verse 2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the image of God He created them; male and female He created them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said three times in one verse, which is how Hebrew emphasises. And the second clause is part of the definition, not an afterthought — the image is carried by humanity as male and female together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for image is used elsewhere of a statue set up to represent a ruler in a province he could not visit in person. That is the job description: a representation, placed where the king is not visibly present."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Have children, and multiply, and fill the earth, and tend it. I am placing it all in your care.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The older translations say *subdue* and *have dominion*, and the words are strong. But they are attached to the image — this is rule exercised on behalf of someone else, by people made to look like Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Very good\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then God looked over everything He had made — and it was not merely good, it was very good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The refrain through the chapter has been *good*, five times. Only at the end, with humanity in it, does the assessment change."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter never says.** Nothing here is earned and nothing is conditional. The blessing, the food, the care of the earth and the image itself are all given before a single human being has done anything at all."}]}, "Genesis 1:3–5": {"title": "\"Let there be light\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first recorded action of God toward a dark world is that He spoke."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He was looking at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *the earth was without form and void, and darkness was on the face of the deep.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the situation. Formless, empty, dark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He did about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then God **spoke**. \"Let there be light.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not repair the darkness, examine it, or comment on how dark it was. He said what He wanted to be there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is three words: *yehi or* — and it is answered by two: *vayehi or.* Light was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"At once, effortlessly, simply because He said so\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no interval in the sentence. The saying and the being are the same moment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews puts it this way: *the worlds were framed by the word of God, so that the things which are seen were not made of things which are visible.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Light before the sun"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sun, moon and stars are not made until the fourth day. There is light on the first day and no light source in the sky for three more."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever this light is, it does not come from a lamp."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"God looked at the light and loved what He saw\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing called **good** in the Bible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He *drew a line between the light and the darkness*. He separated them rather than mixing them or managing the balance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the pattern is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God is made in no one's image; man is made in His. And the first thing recorded of Him is that He faced a dark, empty, formless situation and said what He wanted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not what He could see. What He wanted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs puts the same thing about us: *death and life are in the power of the tongue.* See the note on Proverbs 18:17–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Evening, then morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Evening came, then morning — the first day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew day runs from dark to light, not light to dark. Every one of the six days is counted that way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The direction is always toward the morning."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the New Testament puts this verse.** *For it is the God who commanded light to shine out of darkness who has shone in our hearts, to give the light of the knowledge of the glory of God in the face of Jesus Christ.* Paul quotes day one and says the same command was spoken over a person."}]}, "Genesis 1:9–13": {"title": "Each carrying its own seed", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The third day, and it happens twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gathering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Let the waters be gathered into one place, and let dry ground appear**.\" And it was so.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *dry ground* is **yabbashah**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word for what Israel walks across at the Red Sea, and again at the Jordan. See the note on Joshua 4:19–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both crossings are dry land appearing out of gathered water, and both are described in Genesis 1's vocabulary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The naming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He named the dry ground **\"land,\"** and the gathered waters He named **\"seas.\"***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Third and fourth of the namings. Day and night, heaven, and now land and seas."}, {"k": "p", "t": "After this God stops naming things, and in chapter 2 Adam starts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And God saw that it was good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice on this day — once after the land appears and once after it turns green."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third day is the only one that gets the verdict twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now let the land **grow green**,\" He said — **and it did**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *let the earth bring forth grass* — **tadshe ha-arets deshe**, a verb and noun from the same root."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land is told to produce, and it produces. The command and the compliance are one clause apart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three kinds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Grass, and **seed-bearing plants**, and **trees heavy with fruit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ascending: ground cover, plants that seed, trees whose fruit contains the seed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Each carrying **its own seed to make more of its kind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *whose seed is in itself, according to its kind* — **zera** and **min**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**After its kind** is the phrase of the whole chapter. It occurs ten times across the third, fifth and sixth days."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reproductive capacity is built into the thing on the day it is made. Nothing has to be resupplied."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not there yet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sun."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Light was made on day one; the sun, moon and stars arrive on day four. Vegetation exists for a day without the star it depends on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The evening and the morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Evening came, then morning — the third day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Evening first, in every one of the six. Which is why the Jewish day still starts at sunset."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dry land out of water, and then life coming out of the ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now after two days He will revive us; **on the third day He will raise us up**, that we may live in His sight.* Hosea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was buried, and He rose again the third day according to the Scriptures.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the seed image goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Unless a grain of wheat falls into the ground and dies, it remains alone; but if it dies, it produces much grain.* See the note on John 12:23–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *the seed is the word of God*. See the note on Matthew 13:18–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Peter: *born again, not of corruptible seed but incorruptible*. See the note on 1 Peter 1:23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of them is drawing on the clause in Genesis: the seed is inside the thing already."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will put enmity between you and the woman, and between your seed and her Seed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word from day three becomes the word of the first promise after the fall."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was built in on the third day.** Not a supply of seed. *Its own seed, in itself* — so that nothing after that day needed restocking."}]}, "Genesis 12:1–3": {"title": "The call built on promise", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Everything after Babel starts here, with one man and a sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things to leave"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Leave your country, your people, and your father's household**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three, and they narrow as they go — nation, clan, then the household he was raised in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last is the hardest and it is named last."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go to the land I will show you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a land I have shown you. **Will** show."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews notes it: *he went out, **not knowing where he was going**.* See the note on Hebrews 11:8–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is given a direction and no destination."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seven"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Count the promises:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**I will make you into a great nation**", "**I will bless you**", "**I will make your name great**", "**you will be a blessing**", "**I will bless those who bless you**", "**whoever curses you I will curse**", "**through you all the families of the earth will be blessed**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven, and six of them begin with *I will*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will make **your name great**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Babel is the chapter immediately before this. *Come, let us build ourselves a city, and a tower whose top is in the heavens; and **let us make a name for ourselves**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters, two projects, and the same phrase. One group tries to build a name and is scattered; one man is given one and told to walk."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Through you all the families of the earth will be blessed.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The nations were just divided in chapter 10 and scattered in chapter 11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing said about the man God calls out of them is that they will all be blessed through him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul calls this line *the gospel preached beforehand to Abraham* — *in you all the nations shall be blessed.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abram had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No law, no covenant sign, no Scripture, no priest and no tabernacle. A sentence, and a road."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Abram departed as the LORD had spoken to him* — the next verse — *and Abram was seventy-five years old.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Illumination's note on it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A call built entirely on promise: God asking Abram to walk, and **God Himself carrying the outcome**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the seven is contingent on his performance. The only thing asked is the leaving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest of Genesis 12 is Abram going down to Egypt in a famine and telling a king his wife was his sister. See the note on Genesis 12:10–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promises stand."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the blessing and cursing clause turns up.** *I will bless those who bless you, and whoever curses you I will curse.* Balaam is hired to curse Israel and can only bless them, and quotes this line back at Balak: *blessed is he who blesses you, and cursed is he who curses you.*"}]}, "Genesis 12:10–20": {"title": "Abram goes down", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens with the greatest promise in the Old Testament and closes with the man who received it lying to a foreign king about his wife."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Went down\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abram **went down** to Egypt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is geographical — Egypt is lower ground — and Genesis uses it that way consistently. But the direction is the whole shape of the story."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set it beside Isaiah 2: *Come, let us **go up** to the mountain of the LORD.* Up is toward the house of God. Down is toward Egypt, and Abram was not told to go."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no word from the LORD anywhere in this paragraph. That is the loudest thing in it. The chapter before is *the LORD had said to Abram*; here, nothing — he sees a famine and he moves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plan"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Say you are my sister, **so that I will be treated well for your sake** and my life will be spared because of you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He works out the arithmetic before he crosses the border. His wife is the price and his safety is the return."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is half telling the truth — Sarai was his half-sister — which is what makes it usable. It is the kind of lie that can be defended."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he gained"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Pharaoh treated Abram well for her sake, and Abram acquired sheep and cattle, donkeys, male and female servants, and **camels**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The plan worked. He came out of Egypt richer than he went in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Male and female servants.** The next chapter has Lot's herdsmen quarrelling with Abram's because their possessions were too great for the land to hold them. And a few chapters later a woman appears in the household with a name and a country: *Hagar, her Egyptian maidservant.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "What he picked up in Egypt he took home with him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who protected the promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the LORD struck Pharaoh and his household with serious diseases because of Sarai, Abram's wife.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abram does not fix this. He does not pray, confess, or intervene. God moves directly on Pharaoh's house to get Sarai back out of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise of a son through her was already given, and the promise is defended by the One who made it while the man who received it is silent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rebuke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What have you done to me? Why didn't you tell me she was your wife?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A pagan king rebukes the friend of God, and every word of the rebuke is just. Abram has no reply recorded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abram went **up** from Egypt… to the place where his tent had been at the beginning… to the place of the altar he had made there at first, and there Abram called on the name of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the first line of chapter 13. The way back is up, to the old tent site, to the altar he had already built."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not said.** Not one sentence of condemnation from God. The next time He speaks to Abram, in chapter 13, it is to enlarge the promise: *lift up your eyes and look… all the land you see I will give to you.* The detour is recorded in full, and it does not cost him the covenant."}]}, "Genesis 12:4–9": {"title": "So Abram went", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first obedience in the Abraham story, and the route is marked by altars."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command he is obeying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Get out of your country, from your family and from your father's house, to a land that I will show you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three separations, and no destination given."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews notes it: *he went out, **not knowing where he was going**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The obedience"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Abram went, **just as the LORD had told him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words in Hebrew — **vayyelekh Avram**. And Abram went."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No discussion is recorded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The passenger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **Lot went with him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis mentions it in the same breath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The call named the family as what he was leaving. His nephew comes along."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in the next seven chapters — the quarrel, the parting, the capture, the rescue, the angels, the cave — starts in this clause. See the note on Genesis 13:5–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The age"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abram was **75 years old** when he set out from Haran.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a young man starting out. The promise about descendants is made to somebody at an age when the question is settled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he took"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His wife Sarai, his nephew Lot, **all the possessions they had gathered**, and **the people they had acquired in Haran**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A household, not a solitary pilgrim. Chapter 14 records three hundred and eighteen trained men born in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arrival"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They set out for the land of Canaan, and **there they arrived**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Shechem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abram traveled through the land as far as **Shechem, to the great tree of Moreh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Moreh** means *teacher*, and the tree was probably a local shrine site."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At that time **the Canaanites lived in the land**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator adds it before the promise, not after. The land he is being given has people in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the LORD appeared to Abram and said, \"**To your offspring I will give this land**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first appearance. The call in verse 1 was a voice in Haran; this is the LORD appearing, in the country."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the promise is not to him. *To your offspring* — said to a man of seventy-five with no children."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first altar"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Abram **built an altar there to the LORD, who had appeared to him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason is given. He builds where God showed up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From there he moved on toward the hills east of Bethel and pitched his tent, with **Bethel to the west and Ai to the east**. There he **built another altar** to the LORD and **called on the name of the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Bethel** — house of God. **Ai** — ruin, or heap."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He pitches between them, and the text names both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Tent and altar. Genesis marks his whole route by them — Shechem, Bethel, later Hebron, and the altar on Moriah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tent says he owns nothing. The altar says he is staying with somebody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews: *by faith he dwelt in the land of promise as in a foreign country, **dwelling in tents**… for he waited for the city which has foundations*. See the note on Hebrews 11:8–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The continuing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Abram journeyed on, **continuing toward the Negev**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is where the famine finds him, and the next paragraph is Egypt and the lie about his wife. See the note on Genesis 12:10–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he goes back to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To the place of the altar which he had made there at first.* See the note on Genesis 13:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The altars stay on the map, and he uses one of them later."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clause that runs the next seven chapters.** *And Lot went with him.* The call had named the family as the thing to leave, and Genesis records the nephew in the same sentence as the obedience."}]}, "Genesis 13:1–4": {"title": "Back to the altar he had built", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He goes back the way he came, and stops at the exact spot he left."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Up from Egypt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Abram **went up from Egypt** to the Negev, with his wife and everything he had.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is always *up* from Egypt and *down* into it, regardless of the map. The direction of travel in Scripture is theological before it is geographical."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What he is coming up from is the episode where he called Sarai his sister and Pharaoh took her into the palace. See the note on Genesis 12:10–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he came out with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abram had become **very wealthy in livestock, silver, and gold**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kabed** — heavy. The word for glory shares the root."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He arrived in Egypt driven by famine, told a half-truth that cost him his wife, was found out by a pagan king and escorted to the border — and he leaves heavy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is taken back off him. Verse 2 sits there without a word of comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Everything he had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With his wife and **everything he had**, and Lot went with him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sarai is back. Lot is still with him — and the wealth in this verse is what makes them part company nine verses later, because *the land was not able to support them dwelling together.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The return route"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From the Negev he journeyed **from place to place** until he came back to Bethel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *by his journeys* — the same stages, in reverse. He retraces the trip."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **the spot between Bethel and Ai** where he **had first pitched his tent and built an altar**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bethel means *house of God*. Ai means *ruin*. He pitches between them, as he had before."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses records the location twice — in chapter 12 and again here — with the same wording, so the reader knows it is the same ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There Abram **called on the name of the LORD once more**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes back to the altar and picks up where he stopped."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no sacrifice recorded, no confession, no vow. He calls on the name, which is what he had been doing before he went down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No rebuke. God does not appear in chapter 13 until Lot has gone, and when He does the first words are *lift up your eyes now and look.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses tells the Egypt story plainly and then tells this one plainly, and leaves the two side by side."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the altar was for.** He built one at Shechem when the land was promised, and one here. He builds another at Hebron at the end of the chapter. The altars mark the route, and this one was still standing when he came back to it."}]}, "Genesis 13:14–18": {"title": "\"Look around you, from where you are\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God speaks to Abram at the moment he has just given away the best land."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After **Lot had gone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abram had offered his nephew first choice: *if you take the left, then I will go to the right*. Lot *lifted his eyes and saw all the plain of Jordan, that it was well watered everywhere* and took it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abram is left with the hill country, and the narrator adds a note about where Lot went: *the men of Sodom were exceedingly wicked and sinful against the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God speaks after the choice has been made and cannot be revisited."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name that had to go"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Lot** — in Hebrew, **lot**: a covering, a wrapping, a veil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word Isaiah uses for the thing spread over the faces of the nations — *the covering that is cast over all peoples*. See the note on Isaiah 25:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sight comes to Abram after the covering has separated from him, and Genesis dates it exactly that way: *after Lot had gone*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Look around you, from where you are**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *lift up your eyes now and look from the place where you are*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**From where you are.** Not from a better vantage point, and not after moving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lot had lifted his eyes and chosen. Abram is told to lift his and look at what is left."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four directions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To the **north and south**, the **east and west**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**All the land you see I will give to you and your offspring forever**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole of it, including the plain Lot took."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offspring"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will make your offspring **like the dust of the earth** — **if anyone could count the dust, then your descendants could be counted**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dust here; stars in chapter 15, when he is taken outside at night; sand on the seashore in chapter 22, after the mountain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three images, each attached to a different moment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dust is what is underfoot — and God had said in the previous chapter that Abram would walk this land."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The walking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go, walk through the length and breadth of the land**, for **I am giving it to you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present participle: *I am giving*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is told to walk over ground he does not own, on the strength of a sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same construction runs through Joshua: *every place your foot touches, I am handing over to you*. See the note on Joshua 1:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How much he owned when he died"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One field with a cave in it, at Hebron, bought for four hundred shekels of silver to bury Sarah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stephen says it at his trial: *and God gave him **no inheritance in it, not even enough to set his foot on**. But even when Abraham had no child, He promised to give it to him for a possession.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Abram **moved his tent** and went to live near **the great trees of Mamre at Hebron**, and there **he built an altar to the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He moves, and builds. Genesis marks his route by altars — Shechem, Bethel, and now Hebron."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Hebron is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Machpelah is there. The cave he later buys, where Sarah, Abraham, Isaac, Rebekah, Leah and Jacob are all buried."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He pitches his tent beside the only piece of the country his family would ever hold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews says about the tents"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By faith he dwelt in the land of promise as in a foreign country, **dwelling in tents**… for he waited for the city which has foundations, whose builder and maker is God.* See the note on Hebrews 11:8–12."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**When it is said.** After the choice went the other way. Lot lifted his eyes and picked the watered plain; Abram is told to lift his and look at everything from where he is standing."}]}, "Genesis 13:5–13": {"title": "Lot looked out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The parting of the two men, and Genesis tells you how each of them chose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now Lot, who was traveling with Abram, **also had flocks and herds and tents**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wealth is the trouble. Both men have done well, and the land cannot carry two operations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the land could not support them both while they stayed together, for **their possessions were too great**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The friction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Quarreling broke out **between Abram's herders and Lot's**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the staff, not the principals. That is how it usually starts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parenthesis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*(The **Canaanites and Perizzites** were also living in the land at that time.)*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis drops it in as an aside, and it is not decorative. The neighbours are watching two relatives fall out over grazing rights."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Let there be no strife between you and me**, or between our herders, **for we are family**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *for we are men, brothers*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Isn't the **whole land before you**? Let us part ways. **If you go to the left, I will go to the right**; if you go to the right, I will go to the left.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abram is the elder and the one the land was promised to. He gives away first choice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The looking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lot **looked out and saw** that the whole plain of the Jordan was **well watered**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyissa Lot et-enav vayyar** — Lot lifted up his eyes and saw."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Like **the garden of the LORD**, like **the land of Egypt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two comparisons in one clause, and they do not belong together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eden, and the country Abram had just come back from in disgrace after lying about his wife. See the note on Genesis 12:10–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator lets both stand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The note in brackets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*(This was **before the LORD destroyed Sodom and Gomorrah**.)*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis tells the reader what Lot cannot see. The best-watered land in the region is going to be a salt waste."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The choosing is done on visible evidence, and the visible evidence is accurate and useless."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The choice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Lot **chose for himself** the whole plain of the Jordan.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyivchar-lo** — chose for himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two settlements"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abram settled **in the land of Canaan**, while Lot **lived among the cities of the plain and pitched his tents near Sodom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *pitched his tent as far as Sodom* — a drift, not an arrival."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By chapter 14 he is *dwelling in Sodom*. By chapter 19 he is *sitting in the gate*, which is where the elders sat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three stages, and Genesis records each one without comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now the people of Sodom were **wicked, sinning greatly against the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Placed immediately after the description of where he pitched."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abram is told next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD said to Abram, **after Lot had separated from him**, \"**Lift your eyes now and look** from the place where you are.\"* See the note on Genesis 13:14–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase — lift up your eyes. Lot lifted his and chose; Abram is told to lift his and look at what is left."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what he is shown is all of it, including the plain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abram had just done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Come back from Egypt to the altar he had built before he went: *to the place of the altar which he had made there at first. And there Abram called on the name of the LORD.* See the note on Genesis 13:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes back to the last place he was right."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Genesis tells the reader and not the man.** The plain was the best land in the region and it was about to be destroyed. Lot chose accurately on everything he could see."}]}, "Genesis 14:13–16": {"title": "Three hundred and eighteen", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The one military campaign in Abram's life, and he fights it for a nephew who had left."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four kings against five. The armies of Sodom and Gomorrah routed, the survivors falling into the tar pits, and the victors carrying off everything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They also took Lot, Abram's brother's son who dwelt in Sodom, and his goods, and departed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis adds the clause: *who dwelt in Sodom*. The choice made in chapter 13 has a cost, and it arrives now. See the note on Genesis 13:5–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The messenger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **one who had escaped** came and reported this to Abram.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abram **the Hebrew**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first use of the word in Scripture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ivri** — probably from **avar**, to cross over. The one from the other side."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is what outsiders called him, and Genesis uses it here, in the paragraph where the outside world first has to deal with him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The allies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Living near the great trees of **Mamre the Amorite**, a brother of **Eshcol and Aner** — all allies of Abram.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three Amorite chiefs, named. Abram had treaty relations with the locals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They come with him, and he later insists they take their share: *let Aner, Eshcol, and Mamre take their portion*. See the note on Genesis 14:21–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trigger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Abram heard that **his relative had been taken captive**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *his brother*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lot had chosen the well-watered plain, taken first pick, and drifted into the city. None of it is mentioned."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The force"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He mustered the **318 trained men born in his household**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chanikav** — his trained men, or initiated ones. The word occurs once in the Bible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Born in his household** — not mercenaries. Men raised in the camp."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three hundred and eighteen, against a coalition that had just destroyed five city-states."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pursuit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And set out in pursuit **as far as Dan**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The northern limit of the country. He chases them the length of the land."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tactics"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*During the night Abram **divided his men to attack them**, and he routed them, chasing them **as far as Hobah, north of Damascus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A night attack in separate columns, which is what a smaller force does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past Damascus. Well outside the country, into Syria."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he got back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He recovered **all the goods** and brought back **his relative Lot**, along with his possessions, **the women, and the other people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order in the Hebrew puts the goods first and then Lot, and then everybody else — including people who had nothing to do with him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole population of Sodom is walked home by a man with three hundred and eighteen servants."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with the win"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have raised my hand to the LORD, God Most High, the Possessor of heaven and earth, **that I will take nothing, from a thread to a sandal strap**.\"* See the note on Genesis 14:21–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king of Sodom offers him the goods and he refuses on oath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who meets him on the way back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Melchizedek king of Salem brought out bread and wine.* See the note on Genesis 14:17–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A tenth of everything goes to him, and the rest goes back to its owners."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abram is told immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not be afraid, Abram. **I am your shield, your exceedingly great reward**.\"* See the note on Genesis 15:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to a man who has just turned down a fortune, and who has just discovered what it feels like to have made an enemy of four kings."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who he went for.** The nephew who took the better land and moved into the city. Genesis records the choice in chapter 13 and does not raise it in chapter 14."}]}, "Genesis 14:17–20": {"title": "Melchizedek brings out bread and wine", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A priest-king appears in three verses, blesses Abram, and vanishes from the narrative entirely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two kings come out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **king of Sodom** came out to meet him… And **Melchizedek king of Salem** brought out bread and wine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both arrive at the same moment, and Abram deals with both. One offers him goods; one offers him bread and wine and a blessing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He accepts from one and refuses the other. See the note on Genesis 14:21–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Melchizedek** — *melek tsedeq*, king of righteousness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Salem** — peace. Hebrews makes both points: *first being translated king of righteousness, and then also king of Salem, meaning king of peace.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Salem is the older name of Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he brought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bread and wine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a sacrifice, though he is called *priest of God Most High*. There is no altar and no animal in the passage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bread and wine, carried out to a man coming back from a battle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he was priest of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "***El Elyon***, God Most High — and he uses the title twice more in his blessing: *Creator of heaven and earth*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is a Canaanite king in the middle of a chapter full of pagan kings, and he knows God by name. Abram accepts the blessing without hesitation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Blessed be Abram** by God Most High… and **blessed be God Most High**, who delivered your enemies into your hand.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He blesses the man and then blesses God, and the second half tells Abram who won the battle he had just fought."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tenth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Abram gave him **a tenth of everything**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first tithe in Scripture, and everything about the setting matters:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["Four hundred and thirty years before the law", "No commandment existed, no tabernacle, no priesthood to fund", "Abram is not an Israelite; there is no Israel", "Nobody asked him for it"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had just been blessed, and he gave a tenth of the spoils to the man who blessed him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for tithe is **maaser**, and inside it sits **asar** — the root behind **osher**, wealth, and **ashir**, rich."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tenth and the riches are built on the same three letters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order in the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bread and wine first. Then the blessing. Then the tenth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is asked for and nothing is negotiated. Melchizedek arrives carrying something, says two blessings, and Abram responds."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now think how big a figure this must be. **Abraham himself, the father of the whole family**, handed him a tenth of what he had taken.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument runs on rank: the lesser is blessed by the greater. Melchizedek blessed Abraham and received from him, so Melchizedek is greater — and Levi, still in Abraham's body, paid through him. See the notes on Hebrews 7:4–5 and Hebrews 7:9–10."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the record leaves out.** No father, no mother, no genealogy, no birth, no death. Hebrews notices all four omissions and says he is *made like the Son of God, remaining a priest continually.* Three verses in Genesis, one line in Psalm 110, and two chapters of Hebrews built on them."}]}, "Genesis 14:21–24": {"title": "\"Not even a thread\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Abram turns down a fortune in a single sentence, and gives his reason."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Give me the people and keep the goods for yourself.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the war conventions of the time everything was Abram's — he had won it. The king of Sodom is asking for a favour and calling it a deal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a fortune, offered legitimately, with no strings visible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have raised my hand to the LORD, God Most High, Creator of heaven and earth, and sworn an oath.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had already sworn — the Hebrew is past tense. This was decided before the king opened his mouth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And notice the title he uses: *God Most High, Creator of heaven and earth* — word for word what Melchizedek had just called Him in the blessing three verses earlier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes the priest's language and swears by it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will accept nothing belonging to you, **not even a thread or the strap of a sandal**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two most worthless items he can name. A loose thread and a sandal lace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So that you can never say, 'I made Abram rich.'**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole of it. Not that the money was dirty, or that he did not need it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He would not have anyone else able to claim credit for his prosperity — and Sodom in particular."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did take"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will take nothing but **what my men have eaten** and **the share belonging to the men who went with me** — Aner, Eshcol, and Mamre. **Let them have their share**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He refuses on his own behalf and refuses to impose the decision on anyone else. His allies are Amorites; they get their portion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The conviction is his, and he does not legislate it for other people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two transactions, in order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Within the same hour:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**gave** a tenth to the priest of God Most High", "**refused** everything from the king of Sodom"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gave to one and would take nothing from the other, and the two decisions are the same decision seen from both ends: about who he wanted to be indebted to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on Genesis 14:17–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The very next verse — Genesis 15:1:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not be afraid, Abram. **I am your shield, your exceedingly great reward.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He turned down a reward and was told who his reward was. The Hebrew for *great reward* is the word for wages."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What happened to the goods he refused.** Sodom is destroyed five chapters later, and Lot — who went back to live in it — comes out with nothing but his daughters and the clothes he stands in. Abram, who took not a thread of it, dies *an old man and full of years*, rich in livestock, silver and gold."}]}, "Genesis 15:1": {"title": "\"I Myself am your shield\" — what Abram had just turned down", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*After all this* — three words that make the verse. To feel it you have to know what just happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abram had just done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 14: he rescues Lot by attacking a coalition of kings at night with 318 men. On the way back he meets Melchizedek, gives him a tenth of everything, and then the king of Sodom offers him all the recovered goods."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abram refuses. He will not take *a thread or a sandal strap*, so that no one can ever say the king of Sodom made him rich."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So he has just fought a war, given away a tenth, and declined a fortune. Then night falls."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Don't be afraid, Abram. I Myself am your shield, your protector. And your reward will be very great.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves answer something specific:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**shield** — he has just made permanent enemies of four kings", "**reward** — he has just refused payment"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for reward is a plain one for wages or spoils. God uses the vocabulary of the thing Abram walked away from and applies it to Himself."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Abram says next.** He does not thank God. He says: what can You give me, since I remain childless? The great promise lands on a man whose actual complaint is elsewhere entirely — and God takes him outside to look at the stars rather than rebuking him for it."}]}, "Genesis 15:12–16": {"title": "\"Afterward they will come out with great wealth\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Abram is asleep, and God tells him four hundred years of his family's history before any of it exists."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The darkness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A deep sleep fell over Abram, and **a thick, terrifying darkness** settled on him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *deep sleep* is **tardemah** — the same word used of Adam when the woman was taken out of his side."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is unconscious for the covenant. What follows in the chapter is a smoking firepot and a burning torch passing between the divided animals, and only one party walks the path. Abram is asleep for the whole thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four hundred years"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your descendants will live as strangers in a land not their own. **They will be enslaved and mistreated for four hundred years**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is told in advance. The slavery is not a surprise God responds to later; it is announced to Abram while Isaac is not yet born."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the sentence after it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But I will judge the nation that enslaves them, and afterward your people will come out with great wealth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Great wealth** — the Hebrew is a large substance, heavy possessions."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred years of unpaid work, and God says at the start how the account will be settled. What happened on the night of the exodus was not a windfall; it was the back wages, promised centuries before the first brick was laid."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus records it plainly: *the LORD had made the Egyptians so favourable toward His people that they gave them whatever they asked. And so Israel walked out carrying the wealth of Egypt.* See the note on Exodus 12:33–36."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits in Abram's life"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters earlier, Abram had come back from rescuing Lot, met Melchizedek, and *gave him a tenth of everything.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He refused a penny of the king of Sodom's — *I will not take a thread or a sandal-strap, lest you should say, \"I have made Abram rich.\"* He tithed to the priest and turned down the king."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, in chapter 15, God says *I am your shield, your **exceedingly great reward*** — and lays out the four hundred years and the great wealth on the far side of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"You will go to your ancestors in peace\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the middle of a prophecy about slavery, one line addressed to the man himself: *you will be buried in a good old age.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not going to see any of it. He is told anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Amorites"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In the fourth generation your descendants will return here, **for the wrongdoing of the Amorites is not yet complete**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason for the delay is not about Israel at all. Another people is being given four centuries."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who walked between the pieces.** In that culture both parties to a covenant passed between the divided animals, invoking the same fate on themselves if they broke it. Abram was asleep. Only the fire went through, which means the covenant was cut on one signature."}]}, "Genesis 15:2–3": {"title": "Abram's honest ache", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God has just said something enormous, and Abram answers with the one thing he does not have."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *\"Do not be afraid, Abram. **I am your shield, your exceedingly great reward**.\"* See the note on Genesis 15:1."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Abram **answered from the heart, laying his ache before God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"LORD God, **what could You possibly give me? I go on childless**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *what will You give me, seeing I go childless*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not thanking Him. He is asking what the reward is worth without the one thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is remarkable about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the passage rebukes him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God has just called Himself a shield and a great reward, and Abram effectively asks what the point is. Genesis records the complaint and then records the answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem stated"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The one who **stands to inherit everything I own is a servant in my house, Eliezer of Damascus**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under the customs of the region, a childless man could adopt a trusted servant as heir. The arrangement was legal and normal, and Abram had evidently made it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has done the sensible thing and it is not what he wants."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The restatement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You've **given me no children of my own** — a servant born under my roof will be my heir.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice. The second version is blunter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How long he has been waiting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was seventy-five when he was told *I will make you a great nation*. He is somewhere in his eighties here, and Isaac arrives when he is a hundred."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This one shall not be your heir, but one who will come from your own body shall be your heir**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then outside, at night: *\"Look now toward heaven, and count the stars if you are able to number them… **so shall your descendants be**.\"* See the note on Genesis 15:4–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he **believed in the LORD, and He accounted it to him for righteousness**.* See the note on Genesis 15:6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul builds Romans 4 and Galatians 3 on that sentence — and it is spoken over a man who has just complained."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The believing in verse 6 comes directly after the complaint in verse 2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens later in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The covenant — animals divided, birds of prey driven off, a deep sleep, and *a smoking oven and a burning torch that passed between those pieces*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abram is asleep for it. Only God walks the path between the halves, which in that ritual meant only one party carried the obligation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 16. Sarai suggests Hagar, and he agrees."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The complaint was heard and answered, and he still tried to arrange it himself. See the note on Genesis 21:8–11."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the passage permits.** The complaint. God makes the largest promise in the chapter and Abram asks what it is worth — and four verses later he is counted righteous for believing."}]}, "Genesis 15:4–5": {"title": "Count the stars", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God takes a childless old man outside at night and gives him something to look at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No. That man will not be your heir.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abram had proposed Eliezer of Damascus, his steward. It was the sensible legal arrangement of that world for a man with no son."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God refuses the workaround. *A son from your own body — he will be your heir.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Outside"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And God **led him outside into the night**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is taken out of the tent. Whatever conversation was happening indoors, it continues under the sky."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look up. **Count the stars**, if you can even number them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb is **saphar** — and it carries both senses at once. To count, and to recount: to tell, to declare, to narrate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the root behind **sepher**, a book or scroll, and **sopher**, a scribe."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the instruction has two readings sitting in one word: number them, and read what they tell."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the heavens are said to be doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 19 puts it plainly: *the heavens **declare** the glory of God, and the firmament **shows His handiwork**. Day unto day utters speech, and night unto night **reveals knowledge**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *there is no speech nor language where their voice is not heard. Their line has gone out through all the earth, and their words to the end of the world.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sky is described as speech going out to every nation, with no language barrier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The story told there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The account runs from the virgin to the lion — from **Virgo**, the woman with the seed, to **Leo**, the lion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 3:15, spoken in the garden: *He shall bruise your head, and you shall bruise His heel* — the seed of the woman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation 5:5: *the **Lion** out of Judah's tribe, the Root of David, has won.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first promise and the final victory, and the whole of it written overhead where Abram was told to look. See the notes on Genesis 3:14–15 and Revelation 5:5–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he believed the LORD, and He counted it to him as righteousness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the very next verse — the sentence Romans and Galatians are built on. It follows a night sky and nothing else. See the note on Genesis 15:6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sand, later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Genesis 22, after Isaac on the mountain, God adds the second image: *as the stars of the heaven and as the sand which is on the seashore.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two innumerable things, and both given as a picture before there was one child."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How long he had to hold it.** Isaac is born in chapter 21. Between this night and that morning is roughly a quarter of a century, and a stretch of it he spent being called *father of a multitude* with no son by his wife. See the note on Genesis 17:3–8."}]}, "Genesis 15:6": {"title": "Credited as righteousness", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Nine words in English, and three New Testament books are built on them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Abram **believed** God, and God **counted it to him as righteousness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *believed* is **aman** — to lean on, to trust, to find something firm enough to put weight on. It is the root of **amen**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was believing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before it: God takes him outside at night and says *look now toward heaven, and count the stars if you are able to number them. So shall your descendants be.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abram is seventy-five, childless, with a barren wife. There is nothing to inspect except the sky and the promise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Counted\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chashab** — to reckon, to impute, to enter into an account. Paul's Greek for it is **logizomai**, the bookkeeping word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Righteousness was **credited** to him. Not produced by him, not earned, not demonstrated. Entered on his account by the One keeping the books."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abram had not done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There was no law. Sinai is four hundred and thirty years away — no commandments, no tabernacle, no priesthood, no sacrifice system, no circumcision yet either. Paul makes a great deal of that last one: the declaration came *while he was still uncircumcised*, so that he could be the father of everyone who believes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had not built anything, kept anything, or performed anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He believed, and the entry was made."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abram did afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Went down to Egypt and told a king his wife was his sister. Took Hagar because the promise seemed slow. Laughed on his face when God said Sarah would have a son."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The entry stood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three books that quote it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul in Romans 4, to prove righteousness is credited rather than earned. Paul again in Galatians 3, to prove the blessing of Abraham comes by faith. And James 2, from the other side, on what a living faith looks like."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three arguments, one verse, and all three treat it as the founding precedent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God calls him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The offspring of **Abraham My friend**.* Isaiah says it, and 2 Chronicles and James say it too."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only man in Scripture God calls His friend, and the friendship is dated from a night when he had nothing but a sky full of stars and a sentence."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter does next.** The covenant is cut — animals divided, and Abram put into a deep sleep while a smoking firepot and a burning torch pass between the pieces alone. He was asleep for the signing. See the note on Genesis 15:12–16."}]}, "Genesis 17:1–2": {"title": "\"I am God Almighty\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Thirteen years of silence end with a new name for God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The age"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Abram was **ninety-nine years old**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ishmael was born when Abram was eighty-six, at the end of chapter 16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between that chapter and this one, Genesis records nothing. Thirteen years, and no appearance, no word, and no conversation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The silence begins after the attempt to produce the heir by arrangement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am God Almighty**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **El Shaddai**, and this is the first time it appears in Scripture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The derivation is uncertain and has been argued over for centuries — the older sense may be *the mountain one*, or connect to a root for sufficiency."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It becomes the patriarchs' name for God. Exodus says so explicitly: *I appeared to Abraham, to Isaac, and to Jacob, as **God Almighty**, but by My name LORD I was not known to them.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man of ninety-nine is about to be told he will have a son by a wife of ninety."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name given at that moment is the one about sufficiency."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Walk before Me, and be blameless**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tamim** — complete, whole, without blemish. It is the word used of a sacrificial animal, and of Noah: *Noah was a just man, **perfect** in his generations*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Walk before Me** — the Hebrew is *walk to My face*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Enoch and Noah *walked with*. Abram is told to walk before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The covenant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will **make My covenant between Me and you**, and **I will multiply you greatly**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *multiply* is doubled — *multiply you exceedingly*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abram does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Abram **fell on his face**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The names change. **Abram** — exalted father — becomes **Abraham**, father of a multitude. **Sarai** becomes **Sarah**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both new names have a **hei** inserted, the letter of the divine name, and Jewish commentary has noted it for a very long time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The laugh"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Abraham **fell on his face and laughed**, and said in his heart, \"Shall a child be born to a man who is a hundred years old? And shall Sarah, who is ninety years old, bear a child?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"**Oh, that Ishmael might live before You!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has a son already and would rather the promise attached to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No, Sarah your wife shall bear you a son**, and you shall call his name **Isaac**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yitzhaq** — *he laughs*. The name is given in the same conversation as the laughing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sarah laughs too, one chapter later, behind a tent door, and denies it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is required in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Circumcision — *the sign of the covenant*, given here and not before."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul makes the chronology his whole argument: Genesis 15 has Abram counted righteous by believing; Genesis 17 has the sign. Fourteen years apart, in that order. See the note on Romans 4:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews says about the pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By faith **Sarah herself also received strength to conceive seed**, and she bore a child when she was past the age, because she judged Him faithful who had promised.* See the note on Hebrews 11:8–12."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the name means in that room.** A man of ninety-nine, thirteen years after trying to arrange the heir himself, is told the name **El Shaddai** — and then told the son is coming through the ninety-year-old."}]}, "Genesis 17:15–16": {"title": "Sarai becomes Sarah", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The wife gets a new name too, and hers is the more improbable of the two."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The change"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As for Sarai your wife, **you are no longer to call her Sarai; her name will be Sarah**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The difference in Hebrew is one letter. **Sarai** to **Sarah** — and the letter added is *hey*, which is also the letter added into Abram to make Abraham."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same letter, out of God's own name, put into both of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sarah** is princess, noblewoman."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was true of her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ninety years old. Barren her entire life — the very first thing Genesis records about her, back in chapter 11, is *Sarai was barren; she had no child.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is her introduction in Scripture, and it stood for sixty-five years of narrative."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says over her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will bless her**, and I will give you a son by her. **I will bless her so that she will become nations; kings of peoples will come from her.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will bless her* — twice in one verse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nations.** **Kings of peoples.** Said of a woman who had never carried a child."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who has to say the name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham does. *You are no longer to call her Sarai.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "So for the year before Isaac, a man of ninety-nine called *father of a multitude* was addressing his ninety-year-old barren wife as *princess*, and the whole household heard both names every day. See the note on Genesis 17:3–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abraham did in the next verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Abraham fell on his face **and laughed**, and said in his heart, \"Shall a child be born to a man who is a hundred years old? And shall Sarah, who is ninety years old, bear a child?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He laughs, face down, doing the arithmetic."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sarah laughs too, a chapter later, behind the tent door. And the boy is named **Isaac** — *he laughs*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the New Testament says about her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By faith Sarah herself also received strength to conceive… because she judged Him faithful who had promised.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The laughing is not mentioned. The reckoning is. See the note on Hebrews 11:8–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Peter holds her up as an example to wives, calling her a woman of *a gentle and quiet spirit* — which is not the phrase most readers would have chosen for the woman who threw Hagar out."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The name in the name.** The letter added to both of them, *hey*, appears twice in the covenant name YHWH. Whatever else the renaming did, it put something of His own name into theirs before either promise came true."}]}, "Genesis 17:3–8": {"title": "Abram becomes Abraham", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God changes a man's name to a claim that is not yet true, and makes him answer to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ninety-nine years old. Twenty-four years since the promise was first made. One son, Ishmael, by a servant girl, and none by Sarah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abram fell facedown.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Abram** — exalted father. That name was already a stretch."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Abraham** — *ab hamon*, father of a multitude."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"No longer will you be called Abram; your name will be Abraham, **for I have made you the father of many nations**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I **have made** you.* Past tense, completed, spoken to a childless man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *I will make you*, though the promises around it are future — *nations will come from you, kings will be among your descendants*. This one clause is put in the past, and it is the clause the name is built on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes exactly this line in Romans 4 as his example of *the God who calls things that do not exist as though they already do.* See the note on Romans 4:16–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost him to carry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every person in that camp — servants, herdsmen, his wife, the neighbours he traded with — now had to call him *father of a multitude*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For a year. Isaac is not born until chapter 21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man of ninety-nine, with no child by his wife, introducing himself by that name for a full year. Every use of it was either a joke or an act of faith, and Genesis records no complaint from him about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Sarai too"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A few verses on: *As for Sarai your wife, you shall not call her name Sarai, but **Sarah** shall be her name.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sarah** means princess. *And I will bless her, and she shall be a mother of nations; kings of peoples shall be from her.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ninety years old, barren her whole life, and renamed princess and mother of nations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word \"everlasting\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "It appears three times in these six verses — an **everlasting** covenant, an **everlasting** possession, and *I will be their God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **olam** — perpetual, without a stated end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abraham does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abraham fell facedown **and laughed**.* That is verse 17, and Genesis reports it without softening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He laughs, Sarah laughs in the next chapter, and the boy is named **Isaac** — *he laughs*. God took the laughter and made it the child's name."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not asked of him here.** Nothing. The whole passage is *I will* — I will make, I will keep, I will give, I will be their God. Circumcision comes in the next paragraph as the sign of a covenant already made, not a condition of getting one."}]}, "Genesis 18:1–5": {"title": "Three men at noon", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The narrator tells you who it is in the first line, and then describes three men."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disclosure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD appeared to Abraham near the great trees of Mamre.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis says it before anything happens. The reader knows; Abraham does not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mamre is where he settled after Lot left, and where he built an altar. See the note on Genesis 13:14–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While he sat at the entrance of his tent **in the heat of the day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Noon, when nobody travels and nobody works."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is doing the one sensible thing available: sitting in the shade of a doorway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arrival"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abraham **looked up and saw three men standing nearby**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *and behold, three men were standing over against him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Standing, already there. No approach is described."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **hurried** from his tent to meet them and **bowed low to the ground**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is ninety-nine years old, and three days out from circumcision — chapter 17 records it, and chapter 18 opens immediately after."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything he does in this passage is at speed. The Hebrew has him running twice more in the verses that follow."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My lord**,\" he said, \"if I have found favor in your eyes, **please don't pass by your servant**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **adonai** — which is a form used both of a human superior and of God, and the manuscripts are divided on the pointing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever Abraham thought he was looking at, he treats it as somebody he does not want to lose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offer, as stated"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let **a little water** be brought so you can wash your feet and rest under the tree. Let me bring you **something to eat**, so you can be refreshed.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *a morsel of bread*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offer, as delivered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three seahs of fine meal, kneaded into cakes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A tender calf, killed and dressed — which takes hours."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Butter and milk."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He offers a bite of bread and serves a banquet, and stands under the tree while they eat rather than joining them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Hebrew hospitality code"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that world a traveller's need was an obligation, and the desert made it a matter of life. Job lists it as a defence: *the stranger did not lodge in the street, but I opened my doors to the traveller*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham exceeds it, and the passage is careful to record the gap between what he promised and what he produced."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews makes of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not forget to entertain strangers, for by so doing **some have unwittingly entertained angels**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **elathon** — they escaped notice, they did it without realising."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is exactly the situation in this passage, and it is why the writer of Hebrews can assume his readers will recognise it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the visit is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will surely return to you about this time next year, and **Sarah your wife will have a son**.\"* See the note on Genesis 18:9–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the second: *shall I hide from Abraham what I am doing?* — and two of the three go on toward Sodom while one stays. See the note on Genesis 18:27–33."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Announcement and disclosure, in one visit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who arrives at the other house that evening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The two angels arrived at Sodom in the evening, and **Lot was sitting at the city gate**.* See the note on Genesis 19:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same day, two men, two doorways. One sits at a tent flap in a field and one sits in the gate of a city."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both get up, both bow to the ground, both press the visitors to come in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis puts the two scenes back to back and does not comment."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The gap the passage records.** He offers a little water and a morsel of bread, and serves a calf, cakes and butter — and does not sit down."}]}, "Genesis 18:27–33": {"title": "Down to ten", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The longest negotiation in the Bible, and it moves in one direction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What started it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three visitors at Mamre, a meal, and the promise about Sarah. Then two of them go on toward Sodom and one stays."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD said, \"**Shall I hide from Abraham what I am doing?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The disclosure is deliberate. God says why: *for I have known him, in order that he may command his children and his household after him*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham has already asked once: *would You also destroy the righteous with the wicked? Suppose there were fifty righteous within the city… far be it from You!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the line that carries the whole argument: *\"**Shall not the Judge of all the earth do right?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer was yes — fifty, and He would spare the whole place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The self-description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now that I have been **so bold** to speak to the LORD, though **I am only dust and ashes**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **aphar va'epher** — dust and ashes, an assonant pair."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice in the passage. Every step down is prefaced with an apology."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What if the fifty are **five short**? Will You destroy the whole city **for lack of five**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not ask for forty-five. He asks about the gap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The descent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty-five. Forty. Thirty. Twenty. Ten."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six requests in all, counting the first. The intervals shrink as he goes: five, five, ten, ten, ten."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The politeness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**May the LORD not be angry**, but let me speak.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice. And the last one: *\"May the LORD not be angry, but **let me speak just once more**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the one who calls a halt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer every time"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will not do it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not once does God resist, negotiate upward, or express impatience. Every request is granted immediately and in full."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham stops before God does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why ten"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text does not say. Ten is the traditional minimum for a Jewish congregation — a **minyan** — though that is much later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham may have been counting Lot's household: Lot, his wife, two daughters, two sons-in-law, and whoever else. He may have been asking whether one family would be enough."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD went His way as soon as He had finished speaking with Abraham; and Abraham returned to his place.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There were not ten."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened anyway"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it came to pass, when God destroyed the cities of the plain, that **God remembered Abraham, and sent Lot out of the midst of the overthrow**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The city was not spared. Lot was pulled out — and the angels had to take him by the hand: *while he lingered, the men took hold of his hand… the LORD being merciful to him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham asked for the city and got his nephew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter says about Lot"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And delivered righteous Lot, who was oppressed by the filthy conduct of the wicked… **the LORD knows how to deliver the godly out of temptations**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter calls him righteous three times in two verses, which nothing in Genesis prepares you for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Abraham was standing the next morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Abraham went early in the morning to the place where he had stood before the LORD. Then he looked toward Sodom and Gomorrah… and behold, the smoke of the land which went up like the smoke of a furnace.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He went back to the spot."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who stopped.** Abraham. Six requests, six grants, no resistance at any point — and the negotiation ended because the man ran out of nerve, not because God ran out of patience."}]}, "Genesis 18:6–8": {"title": "He stood near them under the tree", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A ninety-nine-year-old man runs twice in three verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD appeared to him by the terebinth trees of Mamre, as **he was sitting in the tent door in the heat of the day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Noon. The hour nobody moves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So he lifted his eyes and looked, and behold, three men were standing by him. **When he saw them, he ran from the tent door to meet them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is thirteen years past the last appearance and three days past his own circumcision, and he runs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he offers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Please let a little water be brought, and wash your feet, and rest yourselves under the tree. And **I will bring a morsel of bread**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **pat-lechem** — a piece of bread."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He describes it as a snack."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first run"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abraham **hurried into the tent** to Sarah. \"**Quick**,\" he said, \"take **three measures of fine flour**, knead it, and bake bread.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shelosh se'im qemach solet** — three seahs of fine flour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A seah is around seven quarts. Three seahs is roughly forty pounds of flour, enough bread for well over a hundred people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For three visitors."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **he ran to the herd**, chose **a tender, good calf**, and gave it to a servant, **who quickly made it ready**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **rats** — he ran."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Meat was rare. Killing a calf for passing strangers is extravagance, and it takes hours."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four speeds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hurried, quick, ran, quickly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words for haste in three verses, all of them about an old man and his household."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The table"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He brought **curds and milk and the calf** that had been prepared, and set it before them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chemah vechalav** — curds and milk. The pair used of the promised land."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sit down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While they ate, **he stood near them under the tree**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vehu omed aleihem** — and he was standing over them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The posture of a servant waiting at table, held by a man who owns the herd."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he called it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A morsel of bread.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty pounds of flour, a calf, curds and milk, and he stands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then they said to him, \"**Where is Sarah your wife?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will certainly return to you according to the time of life, and behold, **Sarah your wife shall have a son**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sarah is listening at the tent door behind him, and she laughs. See the note on Genesis 18:9–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Is anything too hard for the LORD?**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens after the meal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the men rose from there and looked toward Sodom, and Abraham went with them to send them on the way.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD said, \"**Shall I hide from Abraham what I am doing?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the negotiation: fifty, forty-five, forty, thirty, twenty, ten. See the note on Genesis 18:22–33."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not forget to entertain strangers, for by so doing **some have unwittingly entertained angels**.* See the note on Hebrews 13:1–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the running"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He did not waver at the promise of God through unbelief, but was strengthened in faith… **being fully convinced that what He had promised He was also able to perform**.* See the note on Romans 4:20–22."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last clause.** *While they ate, he stood.* The man who owns the herd waits at the table, having called forty pounds of flour and a calf *a morsel of bread*."}]}, "Genesis 18:9–15": {"title": "Sarah laughed", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A question, an announcement, a laugh behind a curtain, and a denial."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Where is Sarah your wife?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They know her name. Abraham has not introduced her, and she has not appeared."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There, in the tent.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is where a woman would be, and not part of the conversation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The announcement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will surely return to you **about this time next year**, and **Sarah your wife will have a son**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A date. The promise had been given repeatedly since chapter 12 with no timetable attached."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Now it has a year on it, and it is said out loud where she can hear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The listening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sarah was listening at the tent entrance, **just behind him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is behind the speaker, out of sight, at the door flap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now Abraham and Sarah were old, **well past the years of childbearing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis is blunt. He is ninety-nine and she is eighty-nine."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The laugh"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Sarah **laughed quietly to herself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vattitschaq** — from **tsachaq**, to laugh."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same root as **Yitschaq** — Isaac."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham had already done it, in the previous chapter: *then Abraham fell on his face and laughed, and said in his heart, \"Shall a child be born to a man who is a hundred years old?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both of them laugh, and the child is named for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she says inside her head"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"After I am worn out and my husband is old, **am I to have this joy?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ednah** — pleasure, delight. A word connected to Eden."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody hears her. She did not speak."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the LORD said to Abraham, \"**Why did Sarah laugh?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He addresses Abraham about something his wife thought in a tent behind him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Is anything too hard for the LORD?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hayippale** — is anything too wonderful, too extraordinary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same root as the name in Isaiah 9: *His name shall be called **Wonderful**, Counsellor*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah answers it: *ah, Lord GOD! You have made the heavens and the earth by Your great power and outstretched arm. **There is nothing too hard for You**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Gabriel, to another woman getting an impossible announcement: *for with God nothing will be impossible*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repetition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**At the appointed time next year I will return, and Sarah will have a son**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it again, with the date, after the laugh."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is withdrawn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The denial"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sarah was afraid, so she denied it: \"**I didn't laugh**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *for she was afraid*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But He said, \"**Yes, you did laugh**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words, and He does not pursue it further. The next verse moves the men on toward Sodom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews says about her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By faith **Sarah herself also received strength to conceive seed**, and she bore a child when she was past the age, **because she judged Him faithful who had promised**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The laugh is not mentioned."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she says at the birth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**God has made me laugh**, and all who hear will laugh with me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb, third time, and now it is a different kind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Abraham called the name of his son who was born to him — whom Sarah bore to him — Isaac.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the name goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac, Jacob, and the twelve. The whole nation is descended from a man named after his mother's disbelieving laugh in a tent doorway."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is corrected and what is not.** He corrects the denial — *yes, you did laugh* — and repeats the promise word for word with the date still on it."}]}, "Genesis 19:1–3": {"title": "Lot in the gate", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The same day, the second household, and Genesis writes it as a deliberate echo."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two who came on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **two angels** arrived at Sodom **in the evening**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three men visited Abraham at noon. Two go on to Sodom by evening. See the note on Genesis 18:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter before had them leaving: *then the men turned away from there and went toward Sodom, but Abraham still stood before the LORD*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Lot is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Lot was **sitting at the city gate**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gate is the town's council chamber, its court and its exchange. Elders sat there; business was transacted there; Boaz calls the ten elders to the gate to settle Ruth's case."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sitting in it means position."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 13 had him pitching his tent *as far as Sodom*. Chapter 14 had him *dwelling in Sodom*. Now he is in the gate. See the note on Genesis 13:5–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three chapters, three stages."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When he saw them, **he got up to meet them and bowed with his face to the ground**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exactly what Abraham did. Genesis uses nearly identical wording."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever else is true of Lot, his instincts about strangers are his uncle's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The invitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"My lords, please **come to your servant's house. Wash your feet, spend the night**, and go on your way early in the morning.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same offer, and one addition: *early in the morning*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He wants them off the street, and he wants them gone before the town is up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No**,\" they answered, \"**we will spend the night in the square**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **barechov** — the open place, the same word Isaiah uses for where truth stumbles. See the note on Isaiah 59:12–15a."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two strangers proposing to sleep in the open square of that city."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The insistence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But he **urged them so strongly** that they went with him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyiphtsar-bam me'od** — he pressed them greatly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He knows what will happen if they stay out, and the rest of the chapter proves him right."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The meal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He prepared a meal for them, **baking unleavened bread**, and they ate.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **matstsot** — unleavened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The quickest bread there is. No time for dough to rise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham's meal took hours — a calf killed and dressed, cakes from three seahs of meal. Lot's is flatbread, fast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the two scenes have in common"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man at a doorway, getting up, bowing to the ground, pressing visitors to come in, and putting food in front of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is different"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Noon and evening. A tent flap and a city gate. Hours of cooking and unleavened bread."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what happens next: Abraham gets an announcement about a son and a conversation about a city. Lot gets a mob at the door."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis sets the two households side by side across a chapter break and lets the reader do the arithmetic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens that night"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now before they lay down, the men of the city, the men of Sodom, both old and young, all the people from every quarter, surrounded the house.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Lot goes out to them, shuts the door behind him, and offers them his daughters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they said, \"Stand back!… This one came in to stay here, and **he keeps acting as a judge**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They name his position in the gate and use it against him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter says about the man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Righteous Lot, who was oppressed by the filthy conduct of the wicked… **day after day their lawlessness scraped away at his soul**.* See the note on 2 Peter 2:7–9."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one line that dates the drift.** *Lot was sitting in the gate.* Three chapters earlier he was pitching a tent in that direction, and Genesis records each stage without a word of comment."}]}, "Genesis 19:12–14": {"title": "He seemed to be joking", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The warning goes out and comes back with nobody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mob at the door, Lot going out to them and shutting it behind him, and the angels pulling him back in and striking the men outside with blindness — *so that they became weary trying to find the door*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The visitors have stopped being guests."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do you have anyone else here — sons-in-law, sons, daughters, anyone in the city who belongs to you?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four categories, and the list is generous. Anyone connected to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get them out**, for we are about to destroy this place.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rescue is offered on the basis of relationship to Lot, not on the basis of what anybody else has done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same principle as the household at the flood, and Rahab's family in Jericho. See the note on Joshua 6:22–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The outcry to the LORD against its people is so great** that He has sent us.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **za'aqah** — a cry, a scream. The word for the shout of someone being wronged."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is what God said in chapter 18: *because the outcry against Sodom and Gomorrah is great… I will go down now and see*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The city is not being judged on a rumour. Somebody has been crying out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word is used of Israel in Egypt: *their cry came up to God because of the bondage*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The errand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Lot went out and spoke to **the men pledged to marry his daughters**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes back into the city, at night, after a mob has just tried to break his door down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever else is true of him, he went."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Hurry — get out of this place, for the LORD is about to destroy the city**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Accurate, urgent, and short."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But to them **he seemed to be joking**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kimtsacheq** — as one who mocks, jests. From **tsachaq**, to laugh."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same root as Sarah's laugh in the tent the day before, and as Isaac's name. See the note on Genesis 18:9–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two laughs in two chapters. One is a woman who cannot believe good news; the other is two men who cannot believe bad."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they did not believe him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis does not say."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The obvious reading is that a man who has lived in a city for years, sat in its gate and married his daughters into its families does not sound like a man announcing its destruction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had spent a long time being one of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he loses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sons-in-law stay."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the morning it is *your wife and your two daughters **who are here***. See the note on Genesis 19:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angels' phrasing implies there were others who were not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Lot does next morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hesitates, and has to be taken by the hand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter says about him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Righteous Lot*, three times. See the note on 2 Peter 2:7–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the reason: the place *tormented his righteous soul*, day after day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was not enjoying it, and he still could not get anybody out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abraham had been doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Asking. Fifty, forty-five, forty, thirty, twenty, ten. See the note on Genesis 18:27–33."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There were not ten. There were four, and one of them looked back."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why nobody came.** Genesis does not explain. It records that the men engaged to his daughters heard an accurate warning from a man they knew, and thought he was making a joke."}]}, "Genesis 19:15–17": {"title": "They grabbed his hand", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four people leave a city, and none of them walks out under their own power."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The urging"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At dawn the angels **urged Lot on**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **va'ya'itsu** — they pressed, they hurried him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb Lot had used on them the evening before, pressing them to come indoors. See the note on Genesis 19:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pressing has reversed direction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Quick — take your wife and your two daughters who are here**, or you will be swept away.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who are here* — the phrase implies others who are not. The sons-in-law stayed. See the note on Genesis 19:12–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hesitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When he **hesitated**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyitmahmah** — he lingered, he delayed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One word, and it is the most human thing in the chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has a house here, a position in the gate, two daughters engaged to local men, and a life. It is dawn, and he is being told to leave all of it in the next few minutes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The men **grabbed his hand and the hands of his wife and daughters**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyachaziqu** — they seized, took firm hold."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four wrists, taken. Nobody in the party is walking out voluntarily at this point."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah has the same word for God: *I, the LORD your God, will hold your right hand*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the LORD was merciful to them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **bechemlat YHWH alav** — in the LORD's compassion toward him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis supplies the motive for the dragging, and it is not impatience."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The delivery"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **led them safely out of the city**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three commands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Run for your life!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Don't look back, and don't stop anywhere in the plain**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Flee to the hills**, or you will be swept away.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Escape, do not look, do not stop, and go up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Lot says to that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He argues."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Please, no, my lords! Indeed now, your servant has found favour in your sight… **but I cannot escape to the mountains, lest some evil overtake me and I die**. See now, this city is near enough to flee to, and it is a little one. Please let me escape there.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is standing outside a city about to be destroyed, having been carried out by the wrist, negotiating the destination."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is granted: *see, I have favoured you concerning this thing also*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The town is called **Zoar** — *little*, and it is named for his argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The delay clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Hurry, escape there, for I cannot do anything until you arrive there**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angel says the destruction is waiting on him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to the one who looked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Lot's wife **looked back**, and she became a pillar of salt.* See the note on Genesis 19:23–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction had been given to all four."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Remember Lot's wife**. Whoever seeks to save his life will lose it, and whoever loses his life will preserve it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words in the middle of the Olivet material, and it is the whole reference."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He also uses the day itself: *they ate, they drank, they bought, they sold, they planted, they built; but on the day that Lot went out of Sodom it rained fire and brimstone from heaven*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing on that list is a sin. They are the ordinary activities of a Tuesday."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is remembered when it happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So when God destroyed the cities of the plain, **He remembered Abraham**, and He brought Lot out.* See the note on Genesis 19:27–29."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the four of them left.** By the wrist. Genesis names the reason for the dragging in the same sentence — *the LORD was merciful to them* — and the man being dragged spends the next paragraph negotiating."}]}, "Genesis 19:23–26": {"title": "She looked back", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The destruction takes one verse. The sentence before it and the sentence after it are about two people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sun had risen over the land **when Lot reached Zoar**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angel had said it: *hurry, escape there, for I cannot do anything until you arrive there*. See the note on Genesis 19:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The account keeps the two clocks together. He arrives, and the sun is up, and then it happens."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What fell"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the LORD **rained down burning sulfur** on Sodom and Gomorrah — **out of the heavens from the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **gophrith va'esh** — brimstone and fire."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The doubled phrasing — *from the LORD, out of the heavens* — is unusual and the rabbis and the commentators have argued over it for centuries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The extent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He overthrew **those cities and the entire plain, all who lived in the cities, and everything that grew in the land**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *overthrew* is **vayyahaphok** — turned upside down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word becomes the standard term for it. Isaiah, Jeremiah, Amos and Peter all use *the overthrow*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the vegetation is named. The plain Lot chose because it was *well watered, like the garden of the LORD* is included in the list of what stopped growing. See the note on Genesis 13:5–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wife"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **Lot's wife looked back**, and she **became a pillar of salt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vattabbet** — she looked, she gazed. Not a glance over the shoulder; the verb suggests regarding something with attention."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *from behind him* — she had fallen behind Lot, which means she had slowed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the instruction had been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not look behind you, nor stay anywhere in the plain.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Given to all four of them, at the edge of the city, by the same messenger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she is not given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis records her twice: once as one of four wrists taken by an angel, and once here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the salt is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The southern end of the Dead Sea is a region of salt formations, and Josephus reports being shown the pillar in the first century."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lake itself is roughly ten times as salty as the ocean, and nothing lives in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Remember Lot's wife**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus, in the middle of the Olivet discourse in Luke, and that is the entire quotation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence He attaches to it: *whoever seeks to save his life will lose it, and whoever loses his life will preserve it*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she was looking at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis does not say, and the surrounding sentences suggest it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A house, a city, two daughters left behind with the men they were pledged to, and the whole of an adult life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage does not present her as wicked. It presents her as somebody who turned round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was in front"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zoar, which was not the destination. The instruction had been *flee to the hills*, and Lot had argued for the small town instead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They end up in the hills anyway a few verses later — *for he was afraid to dwell in Zoar* — and the chapter finishes in a cave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Abraham went early in the morning to the place where he had stood before the LORD.* See the note on Genesis 19:27–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis cuts from the pillar of salt to a man walking back to where he had prayed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb.** Not a glance. She *gazed* — and Genesis notes she was behind him, which means she had already slowed down."}]}, "Genesis 19:27–29": {"title": "God remembered Abraham", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses of aftermath, and the last one explains the whole chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Early the next morning Abraham returned to **the place where he had stood before the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes back to the exact spot."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The negotiation had ended there — fifty, forty-five, forty, thirty, twenty, ten — and then *the LORD went His way, and Abraham returned to his place*. See the note on Genesis 18:27–33."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is there at first light, which means he had not stopped thinking about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he sees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He looked down toward Sodom and Gomorrah and all the land of the plain, and he saw **dense smoke rising from the land, like smoke from a furnace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kevshan** — a smelting kiln."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase as Sinai on the third day: *the smoke ascended like the smoke of a furnace*. See the note on Exodus 19:16–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two columns of smoke in Genesis and Exodus, described identically, and they mean opposite things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does not know"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whether Lot is alive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis has just told the reader. Abraham is looking at the smoke without that information, and the text does not say he ever found out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So when God destroyed the cities of the plain, **He remembered Abraham**, and He brought Lot out of the catastrophe.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyizkor Elohim et-Avraham**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Zakar** — remembered. It never means He had forgotten; it is the verb for God acting on a commitment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God remembered Noah* while the water was up. *God remembered His covenant with Abraham* in Egypt. *God remembered Rachel*. See the note on Genesis 30:22–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every time, something happens immediately after."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose name is in the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rescue is attributed, in the text, to the man on the hill rather than to the man in the city."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lot's own record in the chapter: he lingered, he had to be taken by the wrist, and he argued about the destination."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter attributes it to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Righteous Lot, who was oppressed by the filthy conduct of the wicked… the Lord knows how to deliver the godly out of temptations.* See the note on 2 Peter 2:7–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis credits the intercessor. Peter credits the man's own condition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are in Scripture and neither cancels the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Out of the catastrophe that overthrew **the cities where Lot had lived**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *in which Lot dwelt*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis names it one last time. Chapter 13: pitched his tent as far as Sodom. Chapter 14: dwelling in Sodom. Chapter 19: sitting in the gate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last mention of his address is in the sentence about it being destroyed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Lot ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zoar, and then afraid of Zoar, and then a cave in the mountains with his two daughters — which is where the chapter finishes, and the last episode in it is the worst thing in Genesis after the flood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The nations that come out of it, Moab and Ammon, are Israel's neighbours and enemies for the rest of the Old Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Ruth the Moabitess is David's great-grandmother."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Abraham is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On a hill, at dawn, at the place he prayed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Whose name is in the rescue sentence.** Not the man who was rescued. *God remembered Abraham* — and Genesis puts it in the same verse as Lot being brought out."}]}, "Genesis 2:1–3": {"title": "The seventh day", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first thing declared holy in the Bible is not a place or a person. It is a day, and it is a day off."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"It was finished\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And so it was finished — the heavens, the earth, and everything in them, **down to the smallest detail**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *all the host of them*, and the verb is completion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same idea is spoken from the cross: **tetelestai**, it is finished. See the note on John 19:28–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why He rested"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On that seventh day He rested. **Not because He was weary, but because the work was whole and good.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah says it outright: *the everlasting God, the LORD, the Creator of the ends of the earth, **neither faints nor is weary**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest is not recovery. It is what you do when a thing is done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was blessed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So God **blessed the seventh day and set it apart as holy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first use of the word **holy** in Scripture, and it is applied to a day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had blessed the creatures on day five and the man on day six. Now He blesses time itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What man's first day was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Adam was made on the sixth day, near the end of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the first full day of his existence was the seventh — the one God had already finished everything on and rested through."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He began in a completed world, with nothing to do, on a day that was already blessed. Everything he ate had been grown before he arrived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is missing from this day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the six days before and each closes the same way: *and there was evening, and there was morning — the first day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seventh has no evening and no morning recorded. The formula stops."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews builds on that: *there remains therefore a rest for the people of God. For he who has entered His rest has himself also ceased from his works, as God did from His.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for rested is **shabath** — to cease, to stop. It is the root of **Sabbath**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not mean to relax. It means to stop working because the work is over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus said about the day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The Sabbath was made for man, and not man for the Sabbath.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And when He healed on it and was challenged: *\"My Father has been working until now, and I have been working.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the pattern lands in the letters.** *For we who have believed do enter that rest… let us therefore be diligent to enter that rest.* Hebrews 4, and the effort named in it is the effort of stopping."}]}, "Genesis 2:15–17": {"title": "How much of the garden was permitted", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first command God gives a human being is usually remembered as a prohibition. Read the sentence and the proportions are the other way round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Count the words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Eat freely from any tree in the garden — all of it is yours to enjoy. Only one tree is not for you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Hebrew the permission is emphatic — the verb is doubled, a construction that intensifies: *eating you shall eat*. Every tree. Then one exception."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Illumination's phrase for it is *a generous word*, and that is a fair reading of the grammar. The restriction exists inside an abundance, and it is a fraction of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is at stake in the one tree"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The tree of the knowledge of good and evil.* Not a magic fruit. The name describes what taking it would mean: deciding for yourself what counts as good and what counts as evil, rather than receiving that from God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is why the serpent's pitch in the next chapter is exactly that — *you will be like God, knowing good and evil.* He is not offering something the tree does not offer. He is recommending it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"On the day you do, you will surely die.\"** They ate, and did not drop dead that afternoon, and readers have puzzled over it ever since. The Hebrew phrase can carry the sense of a sentence passed rather than an execution carried out — and the chapter that follows describes exactly that: exile, hard ground, a guarded gate, and a life now running toward dust. Something did die that day, and the rest of the Bible is about what it cost to undo."}]}, "Genesis 2:18–20": {"title": "The first thing that is not good", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Seven times in Genesis 1 something is good. Here, for the first time, something is not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It is not good for the man to be alone**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lo tov**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing has gone wrong. There is no sin in the account yet, no serpent, no fruit eaten."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first deficiency named in Scripture is loneliness in a perfect garden."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The remedy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will make **a companion who is just right for him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ezer kenegdo**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ezer** — a help, a helper. The word is used about twenty times in the Hebrew Bible, and most of the others are about God: *the LORD is my helper*, *our help is in the name of the LORD*. It is not a word for a subordinate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kenegdo** — corresponding to him, opposite him. Facing him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A help that matches, standing across from him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So out of the ground the LORD God **formed every animal of the field and every bird of the sky**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yatsar** — the potter's verb, the same one used of the man two verses earlier. See the note on Genesis 2:4–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Made of the same material, by the same method."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parade"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He brought them to the man to see what he would call them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *to see what he would call it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God brings them, and waits."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The authority"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever the man named each living creature, **that became its name**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No corrections are recorded. The naming stands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Naming is the exercise of dominion in the ancient world — a king renames a vassal, a father names a child. Adam has already been told to have dominion, and this is the first thing he does with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God had done with naming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Day, night, heaven, earth, seas. Five namings in Genesis 1, all by God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "After Adam, God does not name anything else in the chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gave names to all the livestock, the birds, and the wild animals — but among them all, **there was no true partner to match him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew repeats the phrase from verse 18: *there was not found a helper corresponding to him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The search is real and the search fails."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the exercise was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Adam has to see it. God knew the answer before the first animal was brought over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who has named every creature on earth and found none of them a match is ready for what happens next."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The deep sleep, the rib, and the first recorded human speech:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is now **bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh**; she shall be called Woman, because she was taken out of Man.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing a human being says is a poem about somebody else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the naming continues — he names her too."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other naming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Adam called his wife's name **Eve**, because she was the mother of all living.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That one happens after the fall, immediately after the curse, and the name means *life*. See the note on Genesis 3:20–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does with the order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For Adam was formed first, then Eve.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *neither was man created for the woman, but woman for the man* — followed immediately by *nevertheless, neither is man independent of woman, nor woman independent of man, in the Lord*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict reversed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore a man shall leave his father and mother and be joined to his wife, and **they shall become one flesh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Quoted by Jesus on marriage, and by Paul on Christ and the church. See the note on Ephesians 5:28–30."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The first *not good*.** Not a fault, and not a failure. A man by himself in a finished garden — and the fix involved letting him look at every animal alive first."}]}, "Genesis 2:21–25": {"title": "The first marriage", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only thing in the creation account God called *not good*, and what He did about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The deep sleep"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD God caused **a deep sleep** to fall over the man.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tardemah** — the same word used of Abram in Genesis 15, when the covenant was cut and only the fire walked between the pieces."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Adam is unconscious for the making of his bride. He contributes nothing but the material, and he was asleep while it was taken."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was taken"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God took **a part of his side** and closed the place up again.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **tsela** is usually rendered rib, and it is the ordinary word for a side — the side of the ark, the side of the tabernacle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is made from the side, not from the head or the feet, and God does the fashioning. The verb is the one used of a builder."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"And He brought her to the man\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "God performs the introduction. The first marriage has a witness and a giver, and they are the same Person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Adam says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**At last** — this one is **bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew opens with an exclamation — *this time!* He had just named every animal and found *no helper suitable for him*, which is what makes the *at last* land."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes this exact phrase and applies it to the church: *we are parts of His body, **of His flesh and of His bones**.* See the note on Ephesians 5:28–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is why a man **leaves** his father and mother and **holds fast** to his wife, and the two **become one flesh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leave, hold fast, become one. In that order, and Jesus quotes the whole sentence in Matthew 19 as the definition, then adds *what God has joined together, let not man separate.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hold fast** is **dabaq** — to cling, to be glued to. It is used of skin sticking to bone, and of Ruth clinging to Naomi."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *one flesh* is not a metaphor for cooperation. The Hebrew is one flesh."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who is joined to a harlot is one body with her, for \"the two,\" He says, \"shall become one flesh.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reaches for this verse to explain why sexual union outside marriage is not a minor thing. On his reading it does the same joining regardless of whether the covenant is there — which is his whole argument for why it is not casual."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were both naked, and **they felt no shame** — nothing to hide, nothing to fear, completely at home with each other.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two people fully known and entirely unafraid. That state lasts exactly one chapter, and the first thing the fall produces is *I was afraid, because I was naked, and I hid myself.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was not good.** *It is not good that man should be alone.* The only negative verdict in a chapter of *very good*, and it was pronounced over a man in a garden, in daily contact with God, with meaningful work and no sin. He still needed her."}]}, "Genesis 2:4–7": {"title": "Dust and breath", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two ingredients, and the account is careful about which one came from where."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the LORD God **stooped down and formed** a man from the dust of the ground.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yatsar** — the potter's word. It is used of a craftsman working clay, and it implies hands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything else in the creation account is spoken into being. *Let there be light*, and light was. This one is made by hand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dust"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From **the dust of the ground**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Adamah** is ground, soil. **Adam** is the man. The name and the material are the same word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham says it to God: *I who am but dust and ashes.* And Psalm 103: *He knows our frame; He remembers that we are dust.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The breath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He **breathed into his nostrils the breath of life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Face to face. Whatever this is, it is not done at a distance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **neshamah** is breath, and it is used almost exclusively of God's breath and of human life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the two make"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the man became **a living soul** — dust and breath, earth and God, joined together.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nephesh chayyah** — a living being. The whole man is the soul; the account does not describe a soul being installed in a body."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dust on its own is dirt. Breath on its own is not a man. The person is the join."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was not there yet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No fields yet green, no plants yet sprung up, for the LORD God had **not sent rain**, and there was **no one to work the ground**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two absences named, and only one of them is about the man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Instead a mist rose up from the earth and watered the whole surface of the soil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ground is watered before there is anybody to farm it. He arrives into a place already provided for. See the note on Genesis 2:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same breathing turns up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when He had said this, **He breathed on them**, and said to them, \"Receive the Holy Spirit.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John 20, in a locked room on the evening of the resurrection. The Greek verb **emphusao** occurs once in the New Testament, and once in the Greek Old Testament — here, in Genesis 2:7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word for the first man and the new creation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And where it goes back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the dust will return to the earth as it was, and the spirit will return to God who gave it.* Ecclesiastes 12, taking the two components apart again."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul does with the verse.** *\"The first man Adam became a living being.\" **The last Adam became a life-giving spirit.*** He quotes Genesis 2:7 and then sets a second man beside it — one who received life, and one who gives it."}]}, "Genesis 2:8–14": {"title": "The garden of Eden", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A garden planted for one man, and the text gives the map."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The planting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD God **planted a garden in the east, in a place called Eden**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyitta** — He planted. A gardener's verb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Eden** — delight, pleasure. The garden is *in* Eden, not identical with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The placing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there **He placed the man He had formed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man was made outside it and put in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyasem** — He set, He put. And the verb in verse 15 is **vayyannichehu** — He caused him to rest there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He made every kind of tree grow up from the ground — trees **lovely to look at and good to eat from**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Beauty first, then food. The Hebrew is **nechmad lemareh vetov lema'achal**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is the order of the temptation: *the woman saw that the tree was **good for food**, that it was **pleasant to the eyes***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And in the very middle of the garden stood **two trees: the tree of life, and the tree of the knowledge of good and evil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both in the middle. The forbidden one is not fenced off in a corner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The permission comes first: *of every tree of the garden you may freely eat*. The prohibition is one tree. See the note on Genesis 2:15–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The river"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A river flowed out of Eden to water the garden, and from there **it split into four streams**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ve'nahar yotze me'eden** — a river going out from Eden."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It rises in Eden and waters the garden, and divides after leaving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **Pishon**, winding through the land of **Havilah**, where there is gold — and **the gold of that land is good**, along with **bdellium and onyx stone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator stops the geography to grade the gold."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Bdolach** — bdellium, an aromatic gum resin, pale and translucent. It is the substance manna is compared to: *its colour was like the colour of bdellium*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Shoham** — onyx, or possibly carnelian. The stone set in the high priest's shoulder-pieces with the names of the tribes engraved on it, and in the breastplate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the minerals are listed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 2 spends three verses on the produce of the ground outside the garden — gold, resin, stone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are the materials of the tabernacle and the priest's dress, named on the first pages of the Bible, before there is anything to build."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **Gihon**, flowing through the land of **Cush**. The third was the **Tigris**, running east of Assyria. And the fourth was the **Euphrates**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two of the four are still on the map. Two are not identifiable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Gihon** is also the name of the spring at Jerusalem where Solomon was anointed king."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the man is given to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the LORD God took the man and put him in the garden of Eden **to tend and keep it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **le'ovdah uleshomrah** — to serve it and to guard it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both verbs are used later of the Levites' work in the tabernacle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Work exists before the fall. What the curse adds is thorns and sweat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is lost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So He drove out the man; and He placed cherubim at the east of the garden of Eden, and a flaming sword which turned every way, **to guard the way to the tree of life**.* See the note on Genesis 3:22–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb — **lishmor**, to guard. The man was put there to guard it; afterwards something else guards it against him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the river turns up again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he showed me **a pure river of water of life**, clear as crystal, proceeding from the throne of God and of the Lamb.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the middle of its street, and on either side of the river, was **the tree of life**.* See the note on Revelation 22:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A river, and the tree, and no sword."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The detour.** Three verses of the map stop to say that the gold of that land is good — the materials of the tabernacle and the priest's stones, listed before there is a priesthood."}]}, "Genesis 20:1–2": {"title": "He said it again", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and the man doing this has already done it once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From there Abraham journeyed toward the region of the Negev and **settled between Kadesh and Shur**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "South. He moves after the destruction of the plain — the smoke had been visible from his camp at Mamre. See the note on Genesis 19:27–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Shur** is on the way to Egypt. Hagar had been found at a spring on that road."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While staying in **Gerar**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Philistine country, on the coastal side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abraham said of his wife Sarah, \"**She is my sister**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same words as chapter 12, in Egypt, to Pharaoh. See the note on Genesis 12:10–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-five years apart, and the second time is worse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is worse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Since the first occasion he has been given the covenant, been renamed, been circumcised, and been told the son would be born within the year."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sarah is pregnant, or about to be. Chapter 21 opens with Isaac's birth, and the promise in chapter 18 had a date on it. See the note on Genesis 18:9–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He hands her over at the point in the story when the promise is physically at stake."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **Abimelech king of Gerar sent for Sarah and took her**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sarah is around ninety. The taking is presumably political — a marriage alliance with a wealthy chief — which is what makes the lie work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abraham says when confronted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three defences, and he uses all of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because I thought, surely **the fear of God is not in this place**; and they will kill me on account of my wife.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But indeed **she is truly my sister**. She is the daughter of my father, but not the daughter of my mother.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And it came to pass, when God caused me to wander from my father's house, that I said to her, 'This is your kindness that you should do for me: **in every place we go, say of me, \"He is my brother.\"**'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was true, it was a standing arrangement, and he was frightened. The third defence is the most damaging: he had made it a policy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says about him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now therefore, restore the man's wife; for **he is a prophet, and he will pray for you and you shall live**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to Abimelech, about the man who has just endangered his wife."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first use of the word **navi**, prophet, in the Bible, and it is applied here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who comes out well"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abimelech."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, will You slay a righteous nation also? Did he not say to me, 'She is my sister'?… **In the integrity of my heart and innocence of my hands I have done this**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And God agrees: *yes, I know that you did this in the integrity of your heart*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pagan king behaves better than the patriarch, and the text says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rebuke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Abimelech called Abraham and said to him, \"**What have you done to us? How have I offended you, that you have brought on me and on my kingdom a great sin?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You have done deeds to me that ought not to be done**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Abraham prayed to God; and **God healed Abimelech, his wife, and his female servants**. For the LORD had closed up all the wombs of the house of Abimelech.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophet prays, and the wombs open — in the chapter immediately before the one where his own wife's does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Genesis includes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing requires this chapter. The story would run without it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is here because Genesis does not tidy up its people. The man Paul builds the doctrine of justification by faith on. See the note on Romans 4:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Righteousness had been credited to him five chapters earlier, and it was not credited for this."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The worst of the three defences.** Not that it was half true and not that he was afraid. That he had arranged it in advance, as a standing policy, *in every place we go*."}]}, "Genesis 20:17–18": {"title": "The first prayer for healing in the Bible", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*So Abraham prayed to God, and God healed Abimelech.* It is the first time anyone in scripture prays for someone else's healing, and the circumstances are uncomfortable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is praying for whom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham had just told a foreign king that Sarah was his sister — the same lie he had told in Egypt — and Abimelech had taken her into his household on the strength of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the man praying is the man whose deception caused the crisis. The man being prayed for is the injured party who behaved better throughout: God Himself says Abimelech acted *with a clear conscience and clean hands*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says to do about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Earlier in the chapter God tells Abimelech to return the woman, and adds: *he is a prophet, and he will pray for you, and you will live*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction is not that Abraham deserves to be heard. It is simply what is to happen. God assigns the intercession to the person in the wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the LORD had closed up every womb in Abimelech's household.* The reason for the healing is only explained after it is granted — the writer holds it back to the last line of the chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Abraham, who has been childless for decades and is one chapter away from Isaac, prays for another household's fertility and is answered."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the passage will not support.** It is not a model of a righteous man whose prayers were effective because of his conduct. Genesis records the lie in full, without excusing it, and then records the prayer being answered. Both things happened, and the text is not embarrassed by either."}]}, "Genesis 21:1–2": {"title": "\"Right on time, at the very season God had spoken of\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-five years after the promise, two verses record its arrival, and they are almost entirely made of repetition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repeated phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD came to Sarah, just as He had said He would. He did for her exactly what He had promised… right on time, at the very season God had spoken of.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times in two verses: as He said, as He promised, at the appointed time. The Hebrew is even more insistent than the English."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer is not being redundant. He is making the only point that matters about a promise delayed by a quarter of a century — it did not arrive early, and it did not fail."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What had happened in between"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The intervening chapters are not a story of patient waiting. Abraham laughed at the promise. Sarah laughed at it. They tried to arrange it themselves through Hagar, with consequences that run for the rest of Genesis. Twice Abraham passed his wife off as his sister to save himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "None of that is edited out, and none of it changes the timetable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac means *he laughs*. The child is named after the reaction the promise provoked, and Sarah says so herself in the verses following: *God has brought me laughter, and everyone who hears about this will laugh with me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The joke told at God's expense becomes the boy's name."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line the Illumination adds.** *Nothing was too late. Nothing was forgotten.* It is a translator's summary rather than a separate clause in the Hebrew, and it is a fair rendering of what the triple repetition is doing. The passage exists to say that the delay was not neglect."}]}, "Genesis 21:14–16": {"title": "A bowshot away", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The hardest paragraph in the Abraham story, and it is written from Hagar's side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac has been born and weaned, and at the feast Sarah sees Ishmael *scoffing* and demands that the boy and his mother be sent away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the matter was very displeasing in Abraham's sight because of his son.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God tells him to listen to Sarah, and adds: *of the son of the bondwoman I will also make a nation, because he is your seed*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Abraham **rose early in the morning**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase used when he sets out for Moriah with Isaac a chapter later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice in two chapters Abraham gets up early to do something to a son."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The provisions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took **bread and a skin of water**, set them **on Hagar's shoulders**, gave her the boy, and **sent her away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole outfit. Bread and one waterskin, for a woman and a teenager going into a desert."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham is by this point an extremely wealthy man with hundreds of servants and herds."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where she goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She wandered off into the **wilderness of Beersheba**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *she departed and wandered*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is her second time out here. Years earlier she had run from Sarah's harshness while pregnant, and an angel found her at a spring — and she named God there: *You-Are-the-God-Who- Sees*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is the first person in Scripture to give God a name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When the water runs out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the water was gone, she **laid the child under one of the bushes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *she cast the child*. Not gently."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is old enough to be about sixteen — Ishmael was thirteen at circumcision and Isaac has been born and weaned since. The word for *child* here is used across a wide range of ages."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **walked a little distance away — about a bowshot** — and sat down.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundred yards or so. Far enough not to hear, close enough to see."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The measurement is given because she paced it. Of all the things in the account, the narrator records how far away she chose to sit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ishmael grows up to be *an archer*. The unit of distance in the sentence is the one his life gets measured in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I cannot watch him die**,\" she whispered.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *let me not see the death of the child*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weeping"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And she sat there and **wept aloud**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *she lifted her voice and wept*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **God heard the voice of the lad**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not hers. His."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse she is not told about: *what ails you, Hagar? Fear not, for **God has heard the voice of the lad where he is**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ishmael** means *God hears*. The name Hagar was told to give him thirteen years earlier, before he was born."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is sitting a bowshot away crying, and the boy under the bush is the one being heard, and his name has said so all along."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she is shown"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **God opened her eyes**, and she saw a well of water.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not created. Opened her eyes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What became of him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So God was with the lad; and he grew and dwelt in the wilderness, and became an archer.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve sons, twelve princes, and he is at Abraham's funeral: *and his sons Isaac and Ishmael buried him in the cave of Machpelah*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two brothers stand at the same grave."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the narrator records.** How far she sat. A bowshot — close enough to see and too far to hear, which was the point of choosing it."}]}, "Genesis 21:8–11": {"title": "The feast and the demand", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A party for a small boy, and it ends a household."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The occasion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The child grew and was **weaned**, and on that day Abraham **threw a great feast**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Weaning in that world came at two or three, and it was a milestone worth marking — the child had survived the most dangerous years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham is about a hundred and three."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Sarah saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Sarah noticed **Ishmael — the son Hagar the Egyptian had borne to Abraham — mocking**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **metsacheq** — playing, laughing, making sport."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same root as **Yitzhaq**, Isaac, which means *he laughs*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is genuinely ambiguous and translators divide over it. Genesis does not say what he was doing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reads it as persecution: *as the one born in the ordinary way of the flesh persecuted the one born according to the Spirit.* See the note on Galatians 4:28–31."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the text names them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ishmael — **the son Hagar the Egyptian had borne to Abraham**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator gives the full lineage in the middle of the sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ishmael is about seventeen at this point. He had been the only son for thirteen years before Isaac was born."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The demand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Send this slave woman and her son away**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sarah does not use either name. *This slave woman*, and *her son*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That boy **will never share the inheritance with my son, with Isaac**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shall not be heir with my son*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The issue named is the estate. Paul quotes this clause as Scripture speaking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Abraham"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The words **cut Abraham deeply**, for **Ishmael was his son too, and he loved him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the matter was very grievous in Abraham's sight because of his son*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis records the father's grief and does not minimise it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *do not let it be displeasing in your sight because of the lad and because of your bondwoman. **Whatever Sarah has said to you, listen to her voice**, for in Isaac your seed shall be called.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *yet I will also make a nation of the son of the bondwoman, **because he is your seed***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both. The demand is upheld and Ishmael is provided for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened in the desert"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bread and a skin of water, and a boy under a bush with his mother sitting a bowshot away because she could not watch him die."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **God heard the voice of the lad**… and God opened her eyes, and she saw a well of water.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name **Ishmael** means *God hears*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abraham had asked for years earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Oh, that Ishmael might live before You!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God's answer then: *Sarah your wife shall bear you a son, and you shall call his name Isaac… **and as for Ishmael, I have heard you**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two sons, one estate, and a father who loved both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reads it as two covenants. See the note on Galatians 4:21–27."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the day was.** A weaning feast — a celebration that a child had come through. Genesis puts the demand that broke the family on the same afternoon."}]}, "Genesis 22:1–2": {"title": "\"Every word landed heavier than the last\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The opening of the hardest chapter in Genesis, and the reader is told at the start what Abraham is not told."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the reader knows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sometime later, **God tested Abraham**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nissah** — to test, to prove, to put to the trial."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses tells us in the first line. Abraham has no such sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James says God tempts nobody toward evil; this is the other word. See the note on James 1:13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The call"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He called to him, \"**Abraham**.\" And Abraham answered, \"**Here I am**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hinneni** — here I am, at your disposal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it three times in this chapter: to God here, to Isaac on the way up, and to the voice that stops him. See the note on Genesis 22:11–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four descriptions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take your son — your only son, Isaac, the one you love**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four, and each narrows:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Your son**", "**Your only son** — the Hebrew is **yachid**, only, unique. Ishmael has been sent away one chapter earlier. See the note on Genesis 21:8–11.", "**Isaac** — the name means *he laughs*", "**The one you love** — and this is the first time the word **ahav**, love, appears in the Bible"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first occurrence of love"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jewish tradition notes it: the word for love enters Scripture here, in the sentence asking a father to give up the son."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The destination"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go to the land of Moriah**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "2 Chronicles: *Solomon began to build the house of the LORD at Jerusalem **on Mount Moriah***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same ridge, and about a three-day walk from Beersheba — which is what Genesis says it took."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There, **on a mountain I will show you**, offer him up to me as a burnt offering.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A mountain I will show you** — the same construction as the original call: *get out of your country… **to a land that I will show you***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years earlier, the same open ending."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not explained"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anything. No reason is given, no assurance offered, and no alternative mentioned."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line Moses adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every word landed heavier than the last: **your son, your only son, the one you love**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abraham did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Abraham **rose early in the morning**, saddled his donkey, split the wood, and went.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis gives no conversation with Sarah and no night of wrestling. It gives an itinerary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he said at the foot of the hill"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Stay here with the donkey; the lad and I will go yonder and worship, **and we will come back to you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Plural. Hebrews explains it: *he had worked it out that God could raise the dead.* See the note on Hebrews 11:17–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he said on the way up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac: *\"Behold, the fire and the wood, but **where is the lamb** for a burnt offering?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer: *\"My son, **God will provide for Himself the lamb**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was on the mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A ram caught in a thicket by its horns, behind him, already there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the place **Jehovah Jireh** — *the LORD will provide* — and Moses adds the saying still current when he wrote: *in the Mount of the LORD it shall be provided.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An oath, sworn by God on Himself, because there was nobody greater. See the note on Genesis 22:15–18."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word that enters Scripture here.** Love. Genesis has covered creation, a flood, Babel, and three generations of a family without using it — and the first time it appears, it is in the sentence about the son on the mountain."}]}, "Genesis 22:11–12": {"title": "The knife never fell", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The hand is already up. Everything in these two verses is about timing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the angel of the LORD **called to him from heaven** — \"**Abraham! Abraham!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The doubled name. It is how the urgent moments in Scripture open — *Moses, Moses* at the bush; *Samuel, Samuel* in the night; *Simon, Simon* at the supper table. See the note on Luke 22:31–34."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Here I am**,\" he answered.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hinneni** — the same word he gave when God first called him at the top of the chapter, and the same word he gave Isaac on the way up when the boy asked where the lamb was."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times in one chapter. He answers the same way to God, to his son, and to the voice that stops him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the answer the Son gives in Hebrews: *So I said, Here I am… I have come to do what You want.* See the note on Hebrews 10:5–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not lay a hand on the boy**,\" the voice said. \"**Do nothing to him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice, in two forms. The prohibition is absolute and immediate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is now known"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Now I know** that you honor God, for you have **not held back your son, your only son**, from me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *honor God* is *you are one who fears God* — **yare Elohim**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Have not held back** is **chasak** — to withhold, to spare, to keep back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same idea about God in Romans: *He who **did not spare** His own Son, but delivered Him up for us all.* The one phrase Abraham is commended for is the one turned back on God Himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Your son, your only son\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter says it three times — in the command, here, and again in the oath at the end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ishmael was alive. Isaac is the only son of the promise, the one the covenant runs through."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The knife never fell.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses does not say what Isaac did, or what was said on the way down, or whether they spoke at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was already there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verses on, Abraham looks up and there is a ram caught in a thicket by its horns — behind him, already on the mountain, while he was building the altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the place **Jehovah Jireh**: *the LORD will provide.* And Moses adds the saying that was still current when he wrote: *in the Mount of the LORD it shall be provided.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the mountain is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The land of Moriah.* 2 Chronicles: *Solomon began to build the house of the LORD at Jerusalem on Mount Moriah.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same ridge."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abraham had worked out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews says he had reckoned that God could raise the dead — which is why he told the servants *we will come back to you*. See the note on Hebrews 11:17–22."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What stopped it.** A voice, at the last possible second, with the hand in the air. The chapter that opens *take now your son* closes with a substitute already caught in a bush."}]}, "Genesis 22:13–14": {"title": "Caught by its horns", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He looks up expecting nothing and finds an animal that has been stuck there for some time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the knife stopped"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Abraham, Abraham!\"… \"**Do not lay your hand on the lad**… for now I know that you fear God.\"* See the note on Genesis 22:11–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name twice, which is how urgency is spoken in Hebrew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lift of the eyes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abraham **looked up**, and there in a thicket was **a ram, caught by its horns**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayissa Avraham et-einav** — and Abraham lifted his eyes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third time in this chapter. He lifts his eyes and sees the place, and now he lifts them and sees the ram."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The animal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ayil** — a ram. Not a lamb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had told Isaac *God will provide for Himself **the lamb***. What is in the thicket is a full grown male sheep, which is the animal a lamb becomes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The horns"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Caught by its horns.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ne'echaz basvach bekarnav** — held fast in a thicket by its horns."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An animal caught this way cannot free itself and cannot run. It has been there long enough to get thoroughly tangled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever hour it wandered in, it was before Abraham lifted his eyes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The substitution"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He went and took the ram and offered it up **in place of his son**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tachat beno** — in the place of, instead of, under."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The clearest statement of substitution in the Torah's narrative, and it is a story, not a statute."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The naming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Abraham named that place **The LORD Will Provide**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **YHWH Yireh** — the LORD will see."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ra'ah** is the verb: to see. The Hebrew for providing is God seeing to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same word Abraham used to Isaac on the way up: *God will see for Himself the lamb*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proverb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **to this day people say, \"On the mountain of the LORD it will be provided.\"**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **behar YHWH yera'eh** — on the mountain of the LORD it will be seen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A saying that outlived the event and was still current when Genesis was written down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Go to the land of **Moriah**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Solomon began to build the house of the LORD at Jerusalem **on Mount Moriah**, where the LORD had appeared to his father David.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The temple went up on the same ridge, and every sacrifice offered there for a thousand years was offered on the mountain named for a ram in a bush."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was seen there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold! **The Lamb of God** who takes away the sin of the world!* See the note on John 1:29–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham said *lamb* and got a ram. The word he used stayed open."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**By Myself I have sworn**, says the LORD, because you have done this thing… **in your seed all the nations of the earth shall be blessed**.\"* See the note on Genesis 22:15–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God swears by Himself, because *He could swear by no one greater*, and Hebrews builds an argument on that oath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the horns end up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And I looked, and behold, in the midst of the throne… **stood a Lamb as though it had been slain***. See the note on Revelation 5:6–8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The timing.** The ram was in the thicket before Abraham looked up — caught by its horns, which is a way of being stuck that takes a while to happen."}]}, "Genesis 22:15–18": {"title": "\"I swear by myself\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The voice comes a second time, and this one is an oath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second call"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The angel of the LORD called to Abraham from heaven **a second time**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first stopped the knife. See the note on Genesis 22:11–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This one comes after the ram has been found in the thicket and offered, and after the place has been named."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I swear by myself**, declares the LORD.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only time in Genesis God does this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has nothing higher to swear by, so He stakes Himself. Hebrews makes the whole argument out of that one clause. See the note on Hebrews 6:13–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Because you have done this** and **have not held back your son, your only son**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase as verse 12, repeated word for word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Have not held back** — the Hebrew is **chasak**, to withhold, to spare."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul turns it around in Romans: *He who **did not spare** His own Son, but delivered Him up for us all.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will surely bless you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew doubles the verb — *blessing I will bless you*. Hebrews carries it straight over into Greek. See the note on Hebrews 6:13–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two measures"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will make your descendants **as many as the stars in the sky** and **as the sand on the seashore**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both had been used before — the stars in Genesis 15, when Abram was taken outside at night, and the dust of the earth in Genesis 13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here both, together, at the end of the hardest thing he ever did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gates"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your descendants will **possess the gates of their enemies**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gate was where a city's business was done and where it was defended. To hold the gate is to hold the city."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **through your offspring all nations on earth will be blessed**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the clause that reaches past Israel. It was in the first call, in Genesis 12, and it is repeated here at the end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reads it precisely: *He does not say, \"And to seeds,\" as of many, but as of one, \"And to your Seed,\" **who is Christ**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it rests on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Because you have obeyed my voice**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated twice in the paragraph — *because you have done this*, and *because you have obeyed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The covenant was given free in Genesis 12 and 15, and Genesis 22 attaches an oath to it, after a mountain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the mountain was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The land of Moriah.* 2 Chronicles: *Solomon began to build the house of the LORD at Jerusalem **on Mount Moriah**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name Abraham gave the place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Jehovah Jireh** — *the LORD will provide*. And Moses adds the saying still current when he wrote: *in the Mount of the LORD it shall be provided.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Future tense, in a place where provision had just happened."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is sworn and what it is sworn on.** Descendants, gates, and blessing for all nations — and God puts His own name behind it, on a hill, next to an altar with a ram on it."}]}, "Genesis 22:3–5": {"title": "We will come back", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three days of walking, and one sentence at the end of it that the narrator repeats."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The obedience"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Abraham **got up early in the morning**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayashkem** — he rose early."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No argument is recorded. He had argued with God over Sodom, at length, down from fifty to ten. See the note on Genesis 18:22–33."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For Sodom he bargained. For this he saddles the donkey."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The preparations"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **saddled his donkey**, took **two of his servants** and **his son Isaac**, **cut the wood for the offering**, and set out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five acts in one verse, in order."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He cuts the wood himself, at home, before setting out. The wood for a burnt offering, prepared by the father, and carried three days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three days"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the third day, **Abraham looked up and saw the place far off**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **bayom hashlishi** — on the third day. The first occurrence of the phrase in Scripture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days in which he could have turned round at any hour, and did not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The command had been vague on purpose: *go to the land of Moriah, and offer him on one of the mountains **of which I shall tell you***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word to the servants"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Stay here with the donkey. The boy and I will go over there to worship, and then we will come back to you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **venashuvah** — and we will return. First person plural."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the narrator does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We will come back — **he said it, and held onto it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The translation makes explicit what the grammar carries: the plural is not a slip, and it is not a lie told to keep servants calm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews says he was thinking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By faith Abraham, when he was tested, offered up Isaac… **concluding that God was able to raise him up, even from the dead***. See the note on Hebrews 11:17–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **logisamenos** — having reasoned it out, an accounting word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did the arithmetic. The promise was *in Isaac your seed shall be called*, and Isaac was not yet married. Either the promise fails or the boy comes back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for worship"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The boy and I will go over there **to worship**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nishtachaveh** — to bow down, to prostrate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Its first appearance in the Bible is here, applied to this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaac carries"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Abraham took the wood of the burnt offering and **laid it on Isaac his son**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The son carries the wood up the hill his father prepared it for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the question: *my father… **where is the lamb** for a burnt offering?* See the note on Genesis 22:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"My son, **God will provide for Himself the lamb**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **Elohim yireh-lo** — God will see for Himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is either a father deflecting an unbearable question, or a statement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abraham looked up, and there in a thicket was a ram, caught by its horns.* See the note on Genesis 22:13–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A ram, not the lamb he had named. And the place got its name from the verb: **YHWH Yireh**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What came back down"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two of them, as he said."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Abraham returned to his young men, and they rose and went together to Beersheba.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The pronoun.** *We will come back to you.* Plural, said at the foot of the mountain by a man carrying a knife and three days of silence."}]}, "Genesis 24:10–11": {"title": "Ten camels, loaded", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*The servant took ten of his master's camels and set out, with all kinds of good things from his master in his hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The number is not incidental. Genesis counts things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Ten, and what it carries"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten camels, each loaded, standing for the whole of what Abraham had to send. The servant is not carrying a sample. He is carrying his master's substance out on an errand, and every good thing in that caravan came from the man who sent him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham is the man who had already given a tenth to Melchizedek and refused to take a thread from the king of Sodom. The wealth on those camels is what a man has when he has handled his blessing that way — held loosely, given first, received back with interest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the wealth was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not display. Every item on those animals was going to be given away — to Rebekah, to her mother, to her brother, before the servant would even eat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the shape of the whole chapter. The blessing goes out ahead of the servant, arrives before he explains himself, and secures the bride for the son."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The picture the chapter draws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Christian readers have long seen the outline here and it is hard to miss: a father sends a trusted servant to find a bride for his son, and the servant goes loaded with the father's own riches to give away on the way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The camels are how the errand is funded. Ten of them, fully laden, is Genesis saying the sending was not done by halves."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Rebekah does with them.** She waters all ten — somewhere between fifty and a hundred litres, hauled up by hand for animals belonging to a stranger. The chapter establishes her character by what she was willing to do for a caravan she had nothing to gain from, and then puts her at the head of the promise."}]}, "Genesis 24:12–14": {"title": "The prayer at the well", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A servant with ten camels and no idea what he is doing prays the most specific prayer in Genesis."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose prayer it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not Abraham's. Not Isaac's. An unnamed senior servant, sent five hundred miles to find a wife for a man he works for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the one praying, and the chapter gives more space to him than to anybody else in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Give me success today\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **qarah** — to happen, to meet, to fall out. It is the word for occurrence, for something coming to pass."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is asking for a **happening**. Not for strength, or wisdom, or a sign in the sky — for the right thing to occur."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same root gives the word Ruth's story uses when *she happened to come to the part of the field belonging to Boaz* — an accident, reported by a narrator who plainly does not think it was one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"And show kindness to my master\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chesed** — the covenant word, loyal love."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks twice, at the start and the end of the prayer, and both times the kindness he wants is for Abraham rather than himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The test he proposes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When I say to a girl, 'Please lower your jar so I may drink,' and she says, '**Drink, and I'll water your camels too**' — let her be the one.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look at what he is screening for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Giving a stranger a drink was ordinary courtesy. Watering his camels was not. Ten camels after a long journey drink something in the order of two hundred litres, drawn by hand, one jar at a time, from a well with steps."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is an hour or more of hard physical work, offered unasked, to a foreigner she has never met, with no expectation of anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not testing for beauty, family or wealth. He is testing for a particular kind of generosity — the sort that volunteers work nobody would have asked for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before he had finished speaking* — the next verse — Rebekah comes out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And she says it. *Drink, my lord… I will draw water for your camels also, until they have finished drinking.* Then *she hurried, and emptied her pitcher into the trough, and ran again to the well.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator gives her three verbs of haste."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The man, wondering at her, remained silent, to know whether the LORD had made his journey prosperous or not.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He watches. He does not announce anything until the camels are done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the gold ring and the two bracelets, and: *I bowed my head and worshipped the LORD… who had led me in the way of truth.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter says about the leading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I being in the way, **the LORD led me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His own summary, said twice. He was already moving when the leading happened."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in the prayer.** Any request to be shown the future, or to be given certainty in advance. He asks for a thing to occur and then keeps his mouth shut and watches to see whether it did."}]}, "Genesis 24:15–16": {"title": "Before he had finished praying", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The answer arrives while the request is still being made."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"O LORD God of my master Abraham, **please give me success this day**… Let it be that the young woman to whom I say, 'Please let down your pitcher that I may drink,' and she says, 'Drink, and I will also give your camels a drink' — **let her be the one You have appointed**.\"* See the note on Genesis 24:12–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *give me success* is **haqreh-na** — cause it to happen before me."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Qarah** — to meet, to encounter, to happen. The word for chance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks the God who arranges things for something that will look like coincidence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before he had even **finished praying**, there was Rebekah.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **terem killah ledabber** — before he had finished speaking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She was already walking to the well while he was still forming the request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The camels"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten of them, and they have crossed five hundred miles of desert."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A thirsty camel drinks twenty to thirty gallons. The sign the servant chose requires somebody to draw perhaps two hundred gallons by hand, up out of a well, one jar at a time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He picked a sign that costs the woman hours."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her arrival"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Coming out with **her jar on her shoulder**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her family"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She was the daughter of Bethuel son of Milcah, the wife of **Abraham's brother Nahor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator gives the genealogy before the servant knows any of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is inside the family, which is the entire point of the errand — *you shall go to my country and to my family*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The girl was **very beautiful**, a virgin; no man had known her.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tovat mareh me'od** — good of appearance, exceedingly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Me'od** — very, exceedingly. The word used in Genesis 1: *and God saw everything that He had made, and indeed it was **very** good*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The routine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She went down to the spring, filled her jar, and came back up.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs. The most ordinary sentence in the chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is doing what she does every evening. Nothing about her day has changed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the servant does not know yet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That she is Nahor's granddaughter. That she will say the words. That she will go."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is standing by a well in a foreign country with ten camels and a prayer half-finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Drink, my lord,\" she said, and quickly lowered the jar into her hands and gave him a drink.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I'll draw water for your camels too, **until they've had enough**.\"* See the note on Genesis 24:17–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She offers the second half without being asked, which was the whole sign."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The man **watched her in silence**, wondering whether the LORD had made his journey succeed.* See the note on Genesis 24:21–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says nothing while she works."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah says about the timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It shall come to pass that **before they call, I will answer; and while they are still speaking, I will hear**.* See the note on Isaiah 65:24–25."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The construction of this scene, stated as a promise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**As for me, the LORD has led me on the very road** to the house of my master's relatives.\"* See the note on Genesis 24:26–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past tense, spoken at the well before anything is settled."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The timing.** *Before he had finished praying.* She was already on the path with the jar on her shoulder while the request was still being formed."}]}, "Genesis 24:17–20": {"title": "Until they've had enough", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "She offers something nobody asked for, and it takes her most of the evening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The servant **hurried to meet her**. \"Please,\" he said, \"let me have **a little water from your jar**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **me'at-mayim** — a little water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A small ask. Anyone at a well would grant it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Drink, my lord**,\" she said, and **quickly lowered the jar into her hands** and gave him a drink.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vattemaher** — and she hastened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The jar comes down off the shoulder into her hands so he can drink from it. She holds it for him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When he had finished, she said, \"**I'll draw water for your camels too, until they've had enough**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ad im-killu lishtot** — until they have finished drinking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she has just volunteered for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten camels, off a five-hundred-mile crossing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A thirsty camel takes twenty to thirty gallons. The jar she is carrying holds a few. The well is below her and the trough is beside it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has committed herself to something like two hundred gallons, drawn by hand, in the evening, for a stranger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sign"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The servant had prayed: *let it be that the young woman to whom I say, \"Please let down your pitcher,\" and she says, \"Drink, and I will also give your camels a drink\"*. See the note on Genesis 24:12–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The exact sentence, unprompted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind of sign it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not arbitrary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was looking for a wife for Isaac. The test he devised identified generosity that goes past what is asked, and stamina, and willingness to serve somebody with no claim on her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sign is the character."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The work"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So she **quickly emptied her jar into the trough**, then **ran back to the well again and again**, drawing for all his camels.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew has three verbs of speed in four verses: **vattemaher** twice, and **vattaratz** — and she ran."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repetition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Again and again.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vattaratz od el-habber lish'ov** — and she ran again to the well to draw."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text does not compress it. She goes back, and back, and back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That she complained, slowed, or asked who he was."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The questions come later, from him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does while she works"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The man **watched her in silence**.* See the note on Genesis 24:21–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not help. He does not explain. He watches."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he does not speak"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is checking whether the sign completes. Interrupting would spoil the test."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew says he was **mishtaeh** — gazing, wondering — and the verb suggests astonishment held in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she gets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took out **a gold nose ring** weighing half a shekel and **two gold bracelets** weighing ten shekels.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Given before he knows who she is, and before any negotiation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am the daughter of Bethuel, Milcah's son, whom she bore to Nahor.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"We have both straw and feed enough, and room to lodge.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "More hospitality, offered again without being asked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What her family sees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So it came to pass, when he saw the nose ring, and the bracelets on his sister's wrists… that he went to the man.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Laban's first recorded reaction to anything is jewellery."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What she volunteered for.** Two hundred gallons, drawn by hand, at the end of the day, for a man she had never seen — and the servant had prayed for exactly that sentence."}]}, "Genesis 24:21–23": {"title": "He watched her in silence", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most economical verse in the chapter, and it is a man doing nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The watching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The man **watched her in silence**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mishtaeh lah macharish** — gazing at her, keeping silent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sha'ah** — to gaze, and in this stem to be astonished. **Charash** — to be deaf, to hold one's peace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words for the same thing: he does not move and he does not speak."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she is doing meanwhile"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Running back and forth to the well with a jar, watering ten camels off a desert crossing. See the note on Genesis 24:17–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It takes a long time. He watches all of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he does not help"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because the sign is not finished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had prayed for a specific sentence and a specific action, and she is in the middle of the action. Interrupting would make the result his."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question he is holding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wondering **whether the LORD had made his journey succeed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hahitzliach YHWH darko** — whether the LORD had prospered his way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tsalach** — to advance, to prosper, to succeed. The verb in *whatever he does shall prosper*, and in Joshua's charge: *then you will make your way prosperous, and then you will have good success*. See the note on Joshua 1:6–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The uncertainty"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator says he did not know."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has said the words. She is doing the work. And the man is still watching, because a coincidence and an answer look identical from the outside until the family is named."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gift"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the camels had finished drinking, he took out **a gold nose ring weighing half a shekel and two gold bracelets weighing ten shekels**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nezem zahav** — a gold ring, for the nose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten shekels of gold in the bracelets, which is a substantial weight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives the jewellery **before** he asks who she is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gift is not a payment for information, and it is not conditional on the answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whose daughter are you?** Tell me — **is there room in your father's house for us to spend the night?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Identity, then hospitality."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first question is the one his whole errand depends on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am the daughter of Bethuel, Milcah's son, whom she bore to Nahor.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nahor is Abraham's brother. Milcah is named in Genesis 22, in a short genealogy attached to the end of the mountain: *Milcah has also borne children to your brother Nahor*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That list exists so this moment has a name in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the man **bowed down his head and worshiped the LORD**.* See the note on Genesis 24:26–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The silence ends."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Blessed be the LORD God of my master Abraham**, who has not abandoned His kindness and faithfulness to my master.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chesed ve'emet** — the pair that becomes *grace and truth*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word he uses of himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**As for me, the LORD has led me on the very road** to the house of my master's relatives.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past tense, at a well, before the family has been met or the request made."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the young woman ran and told her mother's household these things.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb she used with the water jar. She runs."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse.** A man standing still, saying nothing, while somebody else works — because the sign was not finished and completing it himself would have proved nothing."}]}, "Genesis 24:26–27": {"title": "On the very road", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A servant bows in the dirt beside a well because a girl mentioned her grandfather."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The errand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham had made him swear: *you shall go to my country and to my family, and take a wife for my son Isaac*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is unnamed in the chapter. Genesis calls him *the servant*, *the man*, and *the oldest servant of his house* — and gives him the longest chapter in the book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had prayed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**O LORD God of my master Abraham, please give me success this day**… Let it be that the young woman to whom I say, 'Please let down your pitcher that I may drink,' and she says, 'Drink, and I will also give your camels a drink' — let her be the one.\"* See the note on Genesis 24:12–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *give me success* is **haqreh-na lephanai** — cause it to happen before me."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Qarah** — to meet, to happen, to occur by chance. He asks for a chance encounter, from the one who arranges them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it happened, **before he had finished speaking**, that behold, Rebekah… came out with her pitcher on her shoulder.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She gave him a drink, and then *I will draw water for your camels also, until they have finished drinking*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten camels, after a journey. A thirsty camel drinks twenty-five gallons. She kept going back to the well."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives her a gold nose ring and two bracelets, and asks *whose daughter are you?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am the daughter of Bethuel, **Milcah's son, whom she bore to Nahor**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nahor is Abraham's brother. She is inside the family, at the well, at the hour he prayed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At that, the man **bowed his head and worshiped the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayiqqod… vayishtachu** — he bowed low and prostrated himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By a well, in front of a girl he has just met, in a foreign town."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Blessed be the LORD, the God of my master Abraham**,\" he said, \"**who has not abandoned His kindness and faithfulness to my master**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chasdo va'amitto** — His **chesed** and His **emet**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same pair Jacob uses two chapters later — *I am not worthy of the least of all the kindness and faithfulness* — and the pair that becomes *grace and truth* in John's prologue. See the note on Genesis 32:9–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**As for me, the LORD has led me on the very road** to the house of my master's relatives.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **baderek nachani YHWH** — in the way the LORD led me."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Anochi** — *as for me* — is emphatic. He puts himself into the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Has led.* Past."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is standing at the well. He has not yet met the family, been welcomed, made the request, or been refused or accepted. Nothing is settled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls it done at the point where the sign came in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the leading was felt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not at the start. He set out with ten camels and no address, and only prayed for the sign when he arrived."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The guidance is identified retrospectively, which is how the chapter presents it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Refuses to eat until he has said his errand: *I will not eat until I have told about my business*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then tells the whole story again, at length — the oath, the prayer, the well, the girl — so that Genesis records it twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The family's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The thing comes from the LORD; we cannot speak to you either bad or good**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Rebekah's"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Will you go with this man?**\" And she said, \"**I will go**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two words, in Hebrew, that Abraham's own call required of him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The tense.** *The LORD has led me.* Said at the well, before the family had been met, the request made, or a single thing agreed."}]}, "Genesis 24:34–36": {"title": "I am Abraham's servant", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He is offered dinner and will not eat until he has said his piece, and this is how it opens."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Bethuel's house in Mesopotamia, having followed Rebekah home from the well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The family have brought water for his feet, fed his camels and set food in front of him, and he has said *I will not eat until I have told about my errand*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am Abraham's servant**,\" he began.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **eved Avraham anochi**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has been given ten camels, gold ornaments, and authority to negotiate a marriage. He begins by naming whose he is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter never gives his own name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The subject"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD has blessed my master greatly, and he has become wealthy**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **berak et-adoni me'od vayyigdal** — blessed my master greatly, and he became great."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb comes first. The blessing is the cause and the wealth is the result, in the order he states it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inventory"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"God has given him **flocks and herds, silver and gold, servants and maids, camels and donkeys**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four pairs. Livestock, metal, people, transport."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The servant lists his master's estate to a family he wants to give a daughter to, which is what anybody in that position would do — and every item is prefaced *God has given him*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not mentioned"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That Abraham left home at seventy-five with a promise and no map."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That he had waited twenty-five years for the son named in the next sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The son"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **Sarah my master's wife bore him a son in his old age**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew notes both the wife and the age."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac was born when Abraham was a hundred, after Sarah laughed at the announcement in the tent door. See the note on Genesis 18:9–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inheritance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **to that son my master has given all that he owns**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kol-asher-lo** — all that is his."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in the inventory belongs to the son."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is about to ask for a wife for that son. The family need to know what she is marrying into."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But the shape of the speech is: God blessed, the blessing produced this, and all of it is now the son's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he leaves out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ishmael, who was sent away with Hagar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And that Abraham had already had to bury Sarah — chapter 23 is the field at Machpelah, and this chapter opens *Abraham was old, well advanced in age*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next in the speech"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oath, the prayer at the well, and the sign — *let it be that the young woman to whom I say, \"Please let down your pitcher\"…* See the note on Genesis 24:12–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells the whole story again, in full, so Genesis records it twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The point he lands on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD has led me on the very road** to the house of my master's relatives.\"* See the note on Genesis 24:26–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The family's verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The thing comes from the LORD**; we cannot speak to you either bad or good.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heir"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything the servant has just listed belongs to one son, and the servant's whole errand is to find that son a wife."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not enrich himself, negotiate for himself, or take credit anywhere in the chapter. The longest chapter in Genesis is given to a man who introduces himself as somebody's servant and is never named."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word order.** *The LORD has blessed my master, and he has become wealthy.* Cause first, then result — and everything on the inventory is now the son's."}]}, "Genesis 24:42–44": {"title": "So today I came to the spring", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He tells the whole story again, and Genesis prints it twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Bethuel's house, standing in front of the family, refusing to eat until he has explained his errand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will not eat until I have told about my business.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the repetition matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis is not a wordy book. Creation gets thirty-one verses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This chapter is the longest in the book, and a large part of its length is the servant repeating what the reader has just watched."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The retelling is the point: the family must hear the sequence in order to know what they are deciding about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **today I came to the spring and prayed**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **va'avo hayyom el-ha'ayin** — and I came today to the spring."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Today**. He is telling them the timeline is hours old."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**LORD, God of my master Abraham**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same words he used at the well. He does not adjust the prayer for a foreign audience."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If You are pleased, make my journey succeed**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew here is **matzliach darki** — from **tsalach**, to prosper, to make successful."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word he swaps"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the well he prayed **haqreh-na lephanai** — cause it to happen before me, from **qarah**, to meet by chance. See the note on Genesis 24:12–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the retelling he uses **tsalach** — prosper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked for a chance encounter. Recounting it, he calls it prospering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the two words is the girl at the well."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he was standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Here I stand by the spring**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hinneh anochi nitzav al-ein hammayim**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole request is made from a fixed position. He does not go looking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sign"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When a young woman comes out to draw water and I say, '**Please give me a little water from your jar**,' and she answers, '**Drink, and I'll draw water for your camels too**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Word for word what he prayed, and word for word what she said. See the note on Genesis 24:17–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Let her be the one the LORD has chosen for my master's son**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hokiach YHWH** — the one the LORD has appointed, decided upon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is left out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The retelling omits nothing important and adds one thing: he tells them Abraham made him swear not to take a wife from the Canaanites, and that Abraham said *the LORD… will send His angel with you and prosper your way*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The family hears that the errand was under oath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says at the end of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now if you will **deal kindly and truly** with my master, tell me. And if not, tell me, that I may turn to the right hand or to the left.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chesed ve'emet** again. The pair. See the note on Genesis 24:26–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks for a plain answer and offers to leave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The family's reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The thing comes from the LORD; we cannot speak to you either bad or good**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **meYHWH yatza haddavar** — from the LORD the matter has gone out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They heard the same sequence the reader did and reached the same conclusion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Rebekah's"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Will you go with this man?**\" And she said, \"**I will go**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in Hebrew: **elech**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is asked, and the answer is hers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter ends with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Isaac brought her into his mother Sarah's tent, and he took Rebekah and she became his wife, and he loved her. **So Isaac was comforted after his mother's death**.* See the note on Genesis 24:66–67."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word he swaps.** At the well he prayed for **qarah** — a chance meeting. Retelling it, he calls it **tsalach** — prospering. The girl at the well is what happened in between."}]}, "Genesis 24:66–67": {"title": "Isaac was comforted", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last two verses of the longest chapter in Genesis."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sixty-seven verses, all about one errand: a servant sent four hundred miles to find a wife for Isaac."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis gives more space to this than to the creation. And it tells the story twice — once as it happens, and once as the servant repeats it to Rebekah's family."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The report"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the servant **told Isaac everything he had done**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The servant is never named in the chapter. He is *the eldest servant of his house, who ruled over all that he had*, and every step of it is his."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had prayed at the well for a specific sign — a woman who would offer to water ten camels — and *before he had finished speaking*, Rebekah came out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaac was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verses earlier: *and Isaac **went out to meditate in the field in the evening**; and he lifted his eyes and looked, and there, the camels were coming.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *meditate* is a rare word and translations divide over it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Isaac **brought her into the tent of his mother Sarah**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sarah had died in chapter 23 at a hundred and twenty-seven. Abraham bought the cave of Machpelah to bury her — the only piece of Canaan the family ever owned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tent has been standing empty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **he married Rebekah. She became his wife, and he loved her**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew runs: he took her, she became his wife, and he loved her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Love is third in the sentence. It is the second occurrence of **ahav** in Genesis; the first is Abraham's for Isaac on the mountain. See the note on Genesis 22:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Isaac **was comforted after the loss of his mother**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayinnachem** — was comforted, consoled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three years have passed since chapter 23. Genesis says nothing about the intervening grief and closes the chapter by saying it ended."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Isaac in this chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does almost nothing. He is not consulted, does not travel, and does not choose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A father sends, a servant prays, a woman agrees to go, and the son walks out into a field in the evening and looks up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Rebekah's decision"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they called Rebekah and said to her, \"**Will you go with this man?**\" And she said, \"**I will go**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in the Hebrew. She is asked directly and answers for herself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter began"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD had blessed Abraham in all things.* And the oath: *put your hand under my thigh… that you will not take a wife for my son from the daughters of the Canaanites.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same gesture Jacob later asks of Joseph about his burial. See the note on Genesis 47:27–31."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the servant says he was given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I being on the way, the LORD led me** to the house of my master's brethren.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice, and bows and worships in the middle of a stranger's courtyard."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the chapter ends.** Not with a wedding scene. With a man taking a woman into his dead mother's tent, and one word about grief finally lifting."}]}, "Genesis 25:27–28": {"title": "Two boys, two parents", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses of family description, and everything that goes wrong for the next twenty years is in them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two men"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Esau became **a skilled hunter, a man of the open country**, while Jacob was **a quiet man, staying among the tents**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *quiet* is **tam** — complete, sound, whole. It is the same word used of Job: *that man was blameless and upright*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not the word for timid. It carries the sense of settled, of a man without a divided purpose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tents"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Staying among the tents is what a herdsman does. Jacob is with the flocks and the household; Esau is out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parents"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Isaac loved Esau, **because he had a taste for wild game**, but Rebekah loved Jacob.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses gives the reason for one and not the other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac's reason is his stomach. It is stated as flatly as that, and it will be the exact mechanism of the deception in chapter 27 — a bowl of savoury food and a father who could no longer see."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rebekah's reason is not given. What she had been told is: *two nations are in your womb… and **the older shall serve the younger**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was already said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before either boy was born, at the oracle. Paul quotes it in Romans and adds *for the children not yet being born, nor having done any good or evil, that the purpose of God according to election might stand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The younger was named as the heir before either of them had a personality."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens in the same chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six verses later, Esau comes in from the field exhausted and sells the birthright for a bowl of red stew."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Thus Esau **despised** his birthright* — the Hebrew is to hold in contempt, to treat as worthless."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews calls him *a profane person, who for one morsel of food sold his birthright.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parenting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each parent has a favourite and they do not have the same one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It produces a wife coaching one son to deceive a blind husband, a father blessing the wrong boy, a brother planning a murder, and a mother sending her favourite four hundred miles away — where he is deceived by his uncle over a wife, and then works twenty years for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses reports the whole thing and never once says it was a good idea."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Esau** — *hairy*. **Jacob** — **Yaaqov**, from the heel he came out holding; it becomes the word for supplanter, one who takes another's place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Esau uses it as an accusation in chapter 27: *is he not rightly named Jacob?* See the note on Genesis 27:34–37."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty years later, at the Jabbok, wrestling until daybreak, and the name is changed: *your name shall no longer be called Jacob, but Israel.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two verses set up.** A father who loves by appetite and a mother who loves by the promise. Everything in chapter 27 runs down the lines drawn here."}]}, "Genesis 25:29–34": {"title": "\"So Esau treated his birthright as though it were nothing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The narrator waits until the last line to say what the story was about, and then says it flatly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Esau treated his birthright as though it were nothing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *Jacob cheated him*. Genesis is perfectly willing to record Jacob's manipulation elsewhere and does not spare him — but the summary of this scene lands on Esau, and on his estimate of what he was holding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not condemned for being hungry. He is condemned for the exchange rate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What a birthright was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The firstborn's portion: a double share of the inheritance, the headship of the family, and in this family the covenant line — the promise made to Abraham and passed to Isaac."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what was on one side of the trade. On the other side: lentils."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The speed of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Esau ate and drank, then got up and left.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verbs in a row, with no pause between them. The Hebrew reads as a rapid sequence — he did not linger, did not reflect, did not appear to think anything significant had occurred."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The transaction cost him nothing at the moment he made it. That is precisely what the writer wants you to see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I'm about to die\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look, I'm about to die. What good is a birthright to me?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was not about to die. He had come in from hunting, tired and hungry, and talked himself into an emergency — and used the emergency to justify letting go of the long thing for the immediate one."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the New Testament does with him.** Hebrews 12 calls him godless and ties him to the warning about missing God's grace — *who traded away what was his by birth for a single meal.* The word there is about a person who treats sacred things as common. Esau's failure is presented as a failure of valuation. See the note on Hebrews 12:12–17."}]}, "Genesis 25:7–10": {"title": "Full of years", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The death of Abraham, and two estranged sons at the graveside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The span"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abraham lived **a hundred and seventy-five years**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was seventy-five when he left Haran. A hundred years in the land he was promised and never owned, except for one field."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he breathed his last and died **at a good old age, full of years**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **beseivah tovah zaqen vesavea** — in a good grey age, old and satisfied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Savea** — full, satisfied, having had enough. The word used of a full stomach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Now as for you, you shall go to your fathers in peace; you shall be buried at a good old age**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to him in Genesis 15, at the covenant of the pieces, in the same conversation where he was told his descendants would be strangers in a land not theirs for four hundred years. See the note on Genesis 15:12–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise about his own death was kept word for word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gathering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he was **gathered to his people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayye'asep el-ammav**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An idiom, and a strange one for this man. His people were in Ur and Haran. He is buried alone in Canaan, five hundred miles from any of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is used separately from the burial notice, which suggests it is not about the grave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two sons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His sons **Isaac and Ishmael** buried him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ishmael was sent away with Hagar and a skin of water when Isaac was weaned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have not appeared together since. They bury their father together, and Genesis does not explain how that was arranged."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plot"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In **the cave of Machpelah**, near Mamre, in the field of **Ephron son of Zohar the Hittite** — the field **Abraham had bought** from the Hittites.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only land he ever owned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 23 records the negotiation in full — Ephron offering it free, Abraham insisting on paying, four hundred shekels of silver weighed out *according to the currency of the merchants*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He would not take it as a gift."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he bought it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To bury Sarah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am a foreigner and a visitor among you. **Give me property for a burial place among you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing he owned in the promised land was a grave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There Abraham was buried **beside his wife Sarah**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in time Isaac, Rebekah, Leah, and Jacob — who made Joseph swear to carry him out of Egypt for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six of them, in a field bought from a Hittite."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By faith he dwelt in the land of promise as in a foreign country… for he waited for the city which has foundations, **whose builder and maker is God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These all died in faith, **not having received the promises**, but having seen them afar off.* See the note on Hebrews 11:13–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same phrase later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Isaac breathed his last and died, **an old man full of years**.* See the note on Genesis 35:27–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he died at a good old age, **full of days**, full of riches and honour.* — of David. See the note on 1 Chronicles 29:26–28."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Job grew old, **full of days and full of gladness**.* See the note on Job 42:16–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four men, and the same Hebrew word each time."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word.** **Savea** — satisfied, having had enough. Used of a man who owned one field in the country he was promised, and bought it to bury his wife in."}]}, "Genesis 26:1–2": {"title": "\"Do not go down to Egypt\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A famine, and a father's example, and a different instruction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A famine in the land — **different from the earlier one in Abraham's time**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis makes the reader think of it. In that one, Abram *went down to Egypt to stay awhile, for the famine was severe* — with no word from God anywhere in the passage, and it ended in a lie about his wife and a rebuke from a pagan king. See the note on Genesis 12:10–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac is standing in the same situation his father handled badly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Isaac went to **Abimelech, king of the Philistines, in Gerar**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gerar is on the way to Egypt, not in it. He has started moving in the familiar direction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not go down to Egypt. Live in the land I tell you to live in.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two commands, and the second one does not name the place. He is told to stay and to be told later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is a hard word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt had the Nile. When the rains failed in Canaan, Egypt still had a river and granaries, and everybody in that region knew it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is told to stay in the country with the famine in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Stay in this land, and **I will be with you and bless you**… I will perform the oath which I swore to Abraham your father.\"* See the note on Genesis 26:3–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise is attached to the staying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Isaac sowed in that land, and **reaped in the same year a hundredfold**; and the LORD blessed him.* See the note on Genesis 26:12–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He plants a crop in a famine and gets a hundred times back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Philistines end up envying him and stopping his wells, and Abimelech asks him to leave because *you are much mightier than we.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one thing Isaac repeated"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He told Abimelech that Rebekah was his sister — the same lie his father had told, in the same town, to a king of the same name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis records the obedience and the inherited fault in the same chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaac is otherwise known for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Very little. He is the quietest of the three patriarchs — no journey like Abraham's, no wrestling like Jacob's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He dug his father's wells again, after the Philistines had filled them in, *and he called them by the names which his father had called them.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter is really about.** Staying put. Abraham went down and came back up. Isaac is told not to go, sows in the famine, and is the only patriarch who never leaves the land."}]}, "Genesis 26:12–14": {"title": "A hundredfold in a famine year", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses, and the timing is everything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When he planted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens with a famine — *besides the one in Abraham's time* — and God telling Isaac not to go down to Egypt but to stay put."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the sowing happens in a year when the land was not producing, in a country belonging to somebody else, by a man who had just been told not to go where the food was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The harvest"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Isaac planted crops in that land, and that same year he reaped a hundredfold, because the LORD blessed him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundredfold was extraordinary in ordinary conditions — Jesus uses it as the top end of the good-soil yield in the parable of the sower. In a famine it is absurd."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the writer gives the reason in the same sentence, without elaboration: *because the LORD blessed him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word \"sowed\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is plain agriculture: he put seed in the ground. There is no ritual described, no vow, no negotiation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What preceded it is in the verses just before — God's promise to be with him and bless him if he stayed. Isaac stayed, and then he planted, which is what a man does with land he intends to remain on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What followed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His wealth kept increasing until he became very wealthy… so many flocks and herds and servants that the Philistines grew envious of him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter does not stop there, and it is worth reading on: the envy leads to his wells being stopped up, and to Abimelech asking him to leave. The blessing brought friction with the neighbours almost immediately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three famines in Genesis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses puts the same test in front of three generations, and the chapter itself makes the comparison — *besides the one in Abraham's time*."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Abram** went down to Egypt, and came back up heavy with livestock, silver and gold. See the notes on Genesis 12:10–20 and Genesis 13:1–4.", "**Isaac** was told not to go down, stayed in Gerar, and reaped a hundredfold in the famine year.", "**Jacob** heard there was grain in Egypt and sent his sons, and the man selling it was the son he had buried in his mind twenty years earlier. See the note on Genesis 41:53–57."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three famines, three different routes, and provision in every one."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Isaac does about the wells.** He digs them again, is driven off, digs again, is driven off again, and digs a third — and names it Rehoboth, *room*, saying the LORD has made room for us. The prosperity in this chapter is not frictionless. It is a man who keeps digging."}]}, "Genesis 26:3–5": {"title": "The promise repeated to the second generation", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only chapter in Genesis where God speaks to Isaac at length, and what He says is almost entirely a repetition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Stay in this land, and I will be with you and bless you… I will make your descendants as many as the stars in the sky, and I will give them all these lands, and through your offspring all nations on earth will be blessed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every item was said to Abraham first. Isaac receives no new terms — he receives his father's covenant restated in his own hearing, with his own name on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because Abraham obeyed me and kept my charge, my commands, my decrees, and my instructions.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the striking part. The reason offered to Isaac is his father's obedience, not his own."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac is at this point a middle-aged man whose chief recorded acts are being nearly sacrificed, receiving a wife arranged for him, and — in the very next paragraph — lying about that wife exactly as his father had twice done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He inherits a promise anchored in somebody else's faithfulness. That is the shape of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Stay in this land.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One command, and it runs against the obvious response to famine. Abraham had gone down to Egypt in a famine and it went badly. Isaac is told to stay in the place of scarcity, with a promise attached."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hundredfold harvest a few verses later happens in the land he was told not to leave."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the New Testament puts the reader.** *If you belong to Christ, then you are Abraham's seed, and heirs according to the promise.* Paul makes the same move Genesis does here — an inheritance received on the strength of somebody else's faithfulness. See the note on Galatians 3:26–29."}]}, "Genesis 27:24–27": {"title": "\"The smell of a field the LORD has blessed\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A blind old man checks four times and is fooled by the fifth sense."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four checks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac tests everything he can."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hearing** — *the voice is Jacob's voice.* He gets it right and does not act on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Touch** — Rebekah had put the goatskins on Jacob's hands and neck, and *the hands are the hands of Esau.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Taste** — *bring me some of your game to eat.* Two kids from the flock, cooked the way Isaac liked venison."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Speech** — *\"Are you really my son Esau?\" \"I am.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four tests, and Jacob passes them with a lie, a costume and his mother's cooking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The kiss"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Come here, my son, and kiss me.**\" So he went and kissed him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fifth test, and Isaac may not have known he was running one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What settled it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **when Isaac caught the scent of his clothes**, he blessed him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rebekah had put *the choice clothes of her elder son Esau, which were with her in the house*, on Jacob."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Esau's clothes — a hunter's, worn outdoors, in the open country."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Ah, **the smell of my son is like the smell of a field the LORD has blessed**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what tips it. Not evidence, not proof — a smell."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the blessing that follows is the one that mattered: *the dew of heaven, the fatness of the earth, plenty of grain and wine… cursed be everyone who curses you, and blessed be those who bless you.* See the note on Genesis 27:28–29."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was on him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His own hands, wrapped in animal skin. His own voice. And a garment belonging to the firstborn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac blesses what he can smell, and what he can smell is the elder brother's coat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Esau's cry when he finds out: *\"Bless me — me also, O my father!\"* And *he cried with an exceedingly great and bitter cry.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob runs that night, and is away twenty years. He never sees his mother again; Rebekah's death is not recorded."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews uses Esau as a warning, and notes that he *found no place for repentance, though he sought it diligently with tears.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaac had been told before the boys were born"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The older shall serve the younger.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rebekah had it from the LORD during the pregnancy. The blessing went where it had been said it would go, and the family tore itself apart getting it there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaac does afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He trembles violently — *Isaac trembled exceedingly* — and then says: *I have blessed him, **and indeed he shall be blessed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not withdraw it. Whatever a blessing was in that world, once said it stood."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one thing nobody in the chapter comes out of well.** Isaac favours Esau and plans to bless him privately. Rebekah eavesdrops and organises the deception. Jacob lies to his father's face and uses God's name doing it. Esau had already sold the birthright for a bowl of stew. Genesis reports all four without a word of comment."}]}, "Genesis 27:28–29": {"title": "The blessing spoken over the wrong son", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Isaac blesses the son in front of him, believing he is blessing the other one, and the words cannot be taken back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*May God give you the dew of heaven and the richness of the earth — an abundance of grain and new wine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dew** first. In a land with no summer rainfall, dew was what kept vines and crops alive through the dry months — moisture arriving overnight from nowhere anyone could arrange."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then grain and wine, the staples of the covenant blessing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the two elements come from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two things Melchizedek carried out to Abram after the battle of the kings. See the note on Genesis 14:17–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They run through the Old Testament as the sign of a land under blessing — *grain, new wine and oil* — and they are what Isaac says he has already given when Esau comes in too late: *I have **sustained him with grain and new wine**.* See the note on Genesis 27:34–37."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the words did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A spoken blessing in that culture was an act rather than a wish. Once it left the mouth it stood — which is why the entire chapter is a race to get an old blind man to say something, and why nothing can be done afterward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*May nations serve you… be lord over your brothers… may those who curse you be cursed, and those who bless you be blessed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last line is the promise made to Abraham, handed down. Isaac is passing on the covenant, not merely wishing his son well."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The deception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob is wearing Esau's clothes with goatskins on his hands and neck, and he lies twice — *I am Esau your firstborn*, and then *the LORD your God gave me success*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis does not hide any of it, and Jacob spends twenty years in exile as a consequence, deceived in turn by Laban with a substituted older sibling. See the note on Genesis 29:21–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens when Isaac finds out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Isaac trembled violently* — and then says something remarkable: *I blessed him, and indeed he will be blessed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not revoke it. Not because he could not have tried, but because he recognises he has been overruled. The blessing had already been assigned before either boy was born: *the older will serve the younger.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Esau gets.** A blessing of a kind, and it is mostly an absence: away from the richness of the earth, living by the sword, serving his brother. The chapter that opened with a father's plan ends with the plan overturned and nobody in the family happy — and the covenant intact."}]}, "Genesis 27:34–37": {"title": "\"What is there left that I can do for you?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The blessing has already gone, and this is the sound of finding out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He let out **a loud and bitter cry**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *a great and exceedingly bitter cry*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews looks back at it: *he found no place for repentance, **though he sought it diligently with tears**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Bless me — me too, my father!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the birthright, which he had already sold for stew. The blessing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Isaac's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Your brother came deceitfully and took your blessing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac names it accurately. He does not defend Jacob and does not soften it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the chapter has already recorded his reaction on discovering it: *Isaac **trembled exceedingly**.* Then, in the same breath: *I have blessed him — **and indeed he shall be blessed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not take it back. Hebrews lists that as faith. See the note on Hebrews 11:17–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Esau's complaint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Isn't he rightly named Jacob?** This is **the second time** he has taken advantage of me. **He took my birthright, and now he has taken my blessing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yaaqov** — heel-grabber, supplanter. Esau turns the name into a verb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His accounting is half right. The birthright he sold himself, in chapter 25, for a bowl of red stew, and Genesis says he *despised* it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plea"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Haven't you saved even one blessing for me?**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was given away"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have made him **lord over you**, and I have given him **all his relatives as servants**, and I have **sustained him with grain and new wine**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three items, and Isaac lists them as things already handed over:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Lordship** over the brother", "**The household** as servants", "**Grain and new wine** — the sustaining"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *sustained* is **samak** — to lean on, to uphold, to support. It is the word for the hand laid on the head of a sacrifice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grain and the wine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two elements Melchizedek carried out to Abram at the end of a battle. See the note on Genesis 14:17–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **what is there left that I can do for you, my son?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a real question, and there is no answer to it. The blessing was one thing, and it had been spoken."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it could not be recalled"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A spoken blessing in that culture was an act, not a wish. Once said, it stood — which is why the whole chapter turns on getting the words out of the old man's mouth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Esau gets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A different word entirely, and it is mostly a description of a hard country. See the note on Genesis 27:38–40."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Isaac says he has done.** Made him lord, given him the household, sustained him with grain and wine. Not *I intended to* — the verbs are finished, and the man in front of him is the one they were meant for."}]}, "Genesis 27:38–40": {"title": "\"Away from the dew of heaven\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Esau asks a second time, and what he gets back is the first blessing with the key word reversed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do you have only one blessing**, my father? Bless me too, my father!\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weeping"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **Esau wept aloud**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *Esau lifted up his voice and wept*. It is the third description of his crying in five verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jacob had been given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"May God give you **of the dew of heaven, of the fatness of the earth**, and plenty of **grain and new wine**.\"* See the note on Genesis 27:28–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things: dew, fatness, grain and wine."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Esau is given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your dwelling will be **away from the richness of the earth, away from the dew of heaven above**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two nouns, in reverse order, with one preposition changed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew preposition **min** can mean *of* or *away from* — Jacob got *of* the dew and *of* the fatness; Esau's line uses the same word the other way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a lesser blessing. It is the first one with the direction turned round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that country dew was not decorative. Through the rainless summer, the night moisture off the Mediterranean was what kept crops alive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Edom is the country south-east of the Dead Sea — rock, cliffs, and very little of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sword"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will live by the sword**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Esau's descendants become Edom, and the Old Testament records the relationship: refusing Israel passage in Numbers, subdued by David, in revolt under Joram, condemned in Obadiah for standing by while Jerusalem fell."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Herod the Great was an Idumean — an Edomite — and he is the king on the throne when Jacob's line produces the Messiah."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The service"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **you will serve your brother**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oracle before they were born: *the older shall serve the younger.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one thing given to him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But when you grow restless, you will throw his yoke from off your neck**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *grow restless* is to become restive, to roam, to break loose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It happened. 2 Kings records it: *in his days Edom revolted from under the hand of Judah, and made a king over themselves.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in either blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither son is given the covenant in this chapter. That comes two chapters later, at Bethel, when Jacob is a fugitive sleeping on a stone: *in you and in your seed all the families of the earth shall be blessed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac blesses him again before he leaves, and this time it is explicit — *the blessing of Abraham*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Esau does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The days of mourning for my father are at hand; then I will kill my brother Jacob**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rebekah hears of it and sends Jacob to Haran, telling Isaac it is about finding a wife. She says *stay with him a few days*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She never sees him again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they meet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty years later, Jacob crossing back with his family, terrified, having sent droves of livestock ahead — and *Esau ran to meet him, and embraced him, and fell on his neck and kissed him, and they wept.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the two blessings differ.** Not in the nouns. Dew and fatness are in both — and the whole difference in Esau's is which side of them he lives on."}]}, "Genesis 28:10–15": {"title": "Jacob's stairway", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man running for his life falls asleep on a rock and is given the whole covenant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jacob **left Beersheba** and traveled toward Haran.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is running. He has just cheated his brother out of the blessing, and Esau has promised to kill him as soon as their father is buried."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is alone, with nothing recorded in his hands, on his first night away from home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pillow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took **one of the stones lying there, put it under his head**, and lay down.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the accommodation. A stone, on open ground, *because the sun had set* — not because he chose the spot."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he sees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **stairway**, its foot set on the earth and **its top reaching all the way into heaven** — and the angels of God **going up and coming down** on it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the direction. **Up** first, then down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They start from the earth. Whatever the traffic is, it begins where Jacob is lying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is at the top"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there, **above it all, stood the LORD Himself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stairway is not the point of the vision. The Person at the top is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a word about what Jacob has just done. No mention of Esau, the stolen blessing, the lie to his father, or the goatskins on his arms."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Instead: *I am the LORD, the God of Abraham your father, and the God of Isaac* — and then the covenant, handed to him."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*the ground you are lying on — I will give it*", "*your descendants will be like the dust of the earth*", "*through you and your family, **every family on earth will be blessed***"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land, the seed, and the blessing to the nations — the three promises made to Abraham, given to a runaway on his first night out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four \"I will\"s"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look — **I am with you**. **I will keep you** wherever you go, and **I will bring you back** again. **I will not leave you**. I will not stop until I have done all I have promised.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every clause is His action. Nothing is asked of Jacob in the entire speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jacob does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Surely the LORD is in this place, and I did not know it… **How awesome is this place!** This is none other than the house of God, and this is the gate of heaven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names it **Bethel**, house of God. And then he makes a bargain — *if God will be with me… then the LORD shall be my God* — which is Jacob all over: given everything unconditionally, and responding with terms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Jesus takes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I tell you the truth: from now on you will see heaven standing wide open, and **the angels of God going up and coming down on the Son of Man**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to Nathanael, in John 1, and it is a deliberate quotation of this passage. See the note on John 1:47–51."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob saw a stairway between earth and heaven. Jesus says He is it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The stone.** He set it up as a pillar and poured oil on it. Years later, coming back the other way with two wives, eleven sons and a limp, God tells him *arise, go up to Bethel, and dwell there* — back to the rock he slept on when he had nothing."}]}, "Genesis 28:16–19": {"title": "\"Surely the LORD is in this place\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man wakes up on a rock and discovers where he had been sleeping."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Surely the LORD is in this place — and I never knew it.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves are true and the second is the point."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It had been true all night. He had chosen the spot because the sun set, not because it was anything. See the note on Genesis 28:10–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The awe"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **holy awe** came over him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that he was afraid — the fear that comes with realising what you have been standing next to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he calls it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How full of wonder this place is.** This is nothing less than **the house of God**. This is **the very gate of heaven**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two names for it, and neither is what it looked like. It was open ground with stones on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**House of God** — a building, on a site with no building. **Gate of heaven** — a doorway, in a field."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took **the stone he had rested his head on**, and he set it up as a pillar, and he **poured oil over the top of it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pillow becomes a pillar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same stone, moved from horizontal to vertical, and marked with oil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the first anointing with oil in Scripture, and it is performed by a man on the run, on a rock, alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the oil came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis does not say, and he had left home in a hurry with nothing recorded in his hands. Oil was ordinary in that world — carried for lamps, for cooking, for skin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses what he had on a stone he had slept on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He named that place **Bethel** — the house of God — though the town had **once been called Luz**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Luz** means almond, or a turning aside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The town had a name already. He renames it after what he had not known was there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Jacob made a vow: \"If God will be with me, and keep me in this way… and give me bread to eat and clothing to put on, so that I come back to my father's house in peace, **then the LORD shall be my God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Given the whole covenant unconditionally in his sleep, he wakes up and negotiates."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is Jacob throughout, and Genesis records it without comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty years later, God tells him: *arise, go up to Bethel and dwell there, and make an altar there to God, who appeared to you when you fled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes back with two wives, eleven sons, a limp and a new name, to the rock he slept on when he had nothing."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Jesus does with the vision.** *\"You will see heaven standing wide open, and the angels of God going up and coming down **on the Son of Man**.\"* Said to Nathanael, in John 1. Jacob called the place the gate of heaven. Jesus says He is the stairway. See the note on John 1:47–51."}]}, "Genesis 29:15–20": {"title": "They seemed like a few days", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Seven years, and the narrator tells us how long they felt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Just because you're my relative, should you work for me for nothing? Tell me — what should your wages be?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Laban, being generous-sounding. He will use the same tone repeatedly, and change Jacob's wages ten times by Jacob's own count."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob has arrived with nothing, running from a brother who wants to kill him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two daughters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **older was named Leah**, the **younger, Rachel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Older and younger, and Genesis notes it because the story turns on birth order — as Jacob's own story has already turned on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the younger who took the elder's blessing. He is about to be given the elder in place of the younger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The descriptions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Leah's eyes were **tender**, but Rachel was **beautiful in form and lovely to look at**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *tender* is **rakkot** — soft, weak, delicate. Translators have argued about it for centuries: weak eyes, or lovely eyes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way the sentence sets one sister against the other, and the comparison is not in Leah's favour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **Jacob loved Rachel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words in Hebrew, and everything that follows comes out of them — the two wives, the rivalry, the concubines, the twelve sons, the favouritism toward Joseph."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The price"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will work seven years for you in exchange for Rachel**, your younger daughter.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bride price, paid in labour because he has no property."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven years is a large offer. Jacob names the term himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Laban's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Better that I give her to you than to another man. Stay here with me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither yes nor no, precisely. He does not say *I agree*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That ambiguity is where the trick lives, and Jacob does not hear it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Jacob served seven years for Rachel — and **they seemed to him like only a few days, because he loved her so**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *they were in his eyes as a few days*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is one of the few places in Genesis where the narrator reports how something felt from the inside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wedding, the veil, the dark, and the morning: *and behold, it was Leah!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What is this you have done to me?** Did I not serve you for Rachel? Why then have you deceived me?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Laban's answer: *it must not be done so in our country, **to give the younger before the firstborn***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The deceiver is beaten with his own sentence. Jacob had gone in to his blind father wearing his brother's clothes and goatskin on his hands, and said *I am Esau your firstborn*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second seven"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Jacob… served with Laban still another seven years for Rachel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fourteen in total, and the second seven are not described as feeling like a few days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sisters got"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the LORD saw that **Leah was unloved, He opened her womb**; but Rachel was barren.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leah has six sons and a daughter. Rachel has two, and dies giving birth to the second. See the note on Genesis 35:16–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the fourth son Leah bears is Judah — *now I will praise the LORD* — through whom the line runs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Rachel's second son comes in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Benjamin, born on the road to Bethlehem, named **Ben-Oni**, son of my sorrow, by his dying mother, and renamed **Benjamin**, son of my right hand, by his father."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line the narrator adds.** Seven years *seemed like a few days*. Genesis rarely reports how anything felt, and it does it here, about a man who was about to be cheated."}]}, "Genesis 29:21–25": {"title": "\"When morning came — there was Leah\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four words in this passage do more work than the rest of the chapter, and in Hebrew they land as bluntly as they do here: *when morning came — there was Leah.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jacob had already done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To feel this you have to remember the last time a father, a firstborn and a disguise appeared together in this family. Jacob had gone in to his blind father wearing his brother's clothes with goat's hair on his hands, and when Isaac asked who he was, he said: *I am Esau your firstborn.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Now Jacob is the one in the dark, and it is a firstborn substituted for a younger sibling that undoes him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Laban even says it out loud a few verses later — *it is not done here to give the younger before the firstborn*. It is the exact thing Jacob did, spoken back to him as local custom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question he asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What have you done to me? Didn't I serve you for Rachel? Why have you deceived me?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word for *deceived* is the one used of Jacob in the earlier story. He is asking Laban the question his brother had every right to ask him. The narrator does not comment. He does not need to."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the story is not saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a lesson that God pays people back — the chapter never says so, and what follows is not punishment but a long, tangled family in which God keeps working anyway. Leah, married by trickery and unloved, becomes the mother of Judah, and the line runs through her to David and to Christ. Not through Rachel, the one Jacob chose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some things are only visible from the other side of them. Jacob had a name for what his brother did to him and no name for what he had done himself, until he woke up on the receiving end of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the use of this passage: it puts a man in the position he once put someone else in, and it lets him hear his own words come back in another man's mouth. Nothing is explained to him. He simply sees it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On Leah.** She is the person this is being done *to*, and the story rarely gets told from where she is standing. Handed over, unwanted the next morning, and told for years that she was not the one. The chapter that follows names her sons and you can hear it in every name. And it is her son Judah, not Rachel's, whose line ends up carrying the promise. The Bible keeps doing this, and it is worth noticing every time."}]}, "Genesis 3:1–5": {"title": "The serpent's question", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Five verses, and every move in them is about what God is like."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now the serpent was **more crafty than any creature the LORD God had made**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **arum** — shrewd, prudent, subtle. Proverbs uses it approvingly of the prudent man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is a pun in the Hebrew: the previous verse ends *they were both naked* — **arummim** — and this one opens **arum**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening move"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He came to the woman with **a question that twisted things**: \"**Did God really say** you must not eat from **any tree** in the garden?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *has God indeed said*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two distortions in one sentence:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["It is a **question** rather than a claim, so nothing is asserted and everything is suggested.", "**Any tree** — the actual command was one tree out of the whole garden. He inverts the proportion."]}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God had said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Of every tree of the garden you may freely eat**; but of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil you shall not eat.\"* See the note on Genesis 2:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew doubles the permission: *eating you may eat*. It is emphatic generosity, with one exception."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The serpent quotes the exception and drops the generosity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"We may eat from the trees — only not from the tree in the middle. God said we must not eat it **or even touch it**, or we will die.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three changes:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Freely eat** becomes simply *we may eat* — the generosity flattens.", "**Or even touch it** is not in the command. She adds a restriction.", "The Hebrew of *you shall surely die* — the doubled form — becomes *lest we die*."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The permission shrinks, the prohibition grows, and the certainty weakens."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contradiction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will not die**,\" the serpent said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first flat denial in Scripture, and it is a direct negation of the sentence in Genesis 2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The motive supplied"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**God knows** that the moment you eat it, **your eyes will open, and you will be like God, knowing good and evil**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The move is not to deny the command. It is to explain the motive behind it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The suggestion is that the restriction exists to keep something back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was already true"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 1: *God created man **in His own image**; in the image of God He created him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were already like Him. The offer is of something they had."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was true in the promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your eyes will be opened.* And the next verse: *then the eyes of both of them were opened.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The serpent's factual claim was accurate. What they saw was that they were naked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the pattern repeats"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the wilderness, three temptations, and two of them open *if You are the Son of God* — which is the same move, aimed at what had just been said out loud at the Jordan: *this is My beloved Son*. See the note on Matthew 3:16–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus answers all three with Scripture, quoted accurately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the method"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But I fear, lest somehow, **as the serpent deceived Eve by his craftiness**, so your minds may be corrupted from the simplicity that is in Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same word — **panourgia**, craftiness."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is attacked first.** Not the rule. The opening question misquotes the generosity, and the closing sentence supplies a motive — and by the end the only thing in dispute is what kind of God gave the command."}]}, "Genesis 3:14–15": {"title": "The first promise, spoken to the serpent", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first announcement of a rescue in the Bible is not addressed to the people being rescued. It is spoken to the enemy, over their heads."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being talked to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God curses the serpent, and in the middle of the sentence describes a future conflict — and its outcome. Adam and Eve are standing there listening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not consulted, promised anything directly, or asked to do anything. They overhear their own hope while being sentenced."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Her seed\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Between your offspring and hers. Her seed will crush your head.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is unusual. Descent in Hebrew genealogy runs through the man, and Scripture speaks of the seed of Abraham, of David, of Israel. The seed **of a woman** is not how anyone talked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Christian readers have seen a virgin birth signalled from the first pages because of it, and Paul's phrasing in Galatians follows the same track: *God sent forth His Son, born of a woman.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two wounds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Her seed will crush your head, and you will strike his heel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both parties are injured, and the injuries are not equal. A crushed head ends the fight. A struck heel is real damage that heals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything from here to the cross is the working out of that asymmetry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the promise sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even here, in the wreckage, God spoke a promise.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before the curse on the ground, before the exile from the garden, before the garments of skin. The first thing God says after the fall is that it will not stand."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the wound cost.** *You will strike his heel* is not a small thing. Whatever the crushing of the head would take, the promise made in Genesis 3 already includes the injury to the one doing it. See the note on Isaiah 53:4–6."}]}, "Genesis 3:17–19": {"title": "Thorns, sweat, and the ground", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The sentence on the man, and what is cursed in it is not the man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is cursed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The ground is cursed** because of you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not Adam. The serpent is cursed in verse 14 and the ground is cursed here, and the man is not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *cursed is the adamah for your sake* — and **adamah** is the ground he was made out of and named after. See the note on Genesis 2:4–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*\"In **toil** you will eat from it all the days of your life\"*", "*\"It will bring you **thorns and thistles**\"*", "*\"By **the sweat of your brow** you will eat your bread\"*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Toil, thorns, and sweat. Work itself was not the curse — he had been given the garden *to tend and keep it* before any of this. What changed is what work costs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *toil* is **itstsabon**, painful labour, and it is the same word used of the woman's pain a few verses earlier. They get the same noun."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Thorns"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **qots** appears here for the first time in Scripture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are the visible mark of the curse on the ground — what grows without being planted, in soil that used to produce a garden."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A cohort of Roman soldiers twisted a circle of them and pressed it onto His head. See the note on Matthew 27:27–31."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Sweat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"By the sweat of your brow you will eat your bread.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in a garden on the Mount of Olives: *His sweat became like great drops of blood **falling to the ground**.* See the note on Luke 22:39–46."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sweat of a man's face, and the cursed ground receiving it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Until you return to the ground\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For out of it you were taken. **Dust you are, and to dust you will return.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The account closes the loop it opened. He was formed from dust and breathed into; now the dust part is named as the destination."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any statement that God has finished with him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verses later God makes them tunics of skin and clothes them — the first death in Scripture, and it is an animal killed to cover two people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And before the sentence on the man, in verse 15, the promise to the serpent: *He shall bruise your head, and you shall bruise His heel.* See the note on Genesis 3:14–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The remedy is announced before the sentence is passed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the ground is dealt with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ has redeemed us from the curse of the law, having become a curse for us* — Galatians 3, and the proof text Paul uses is *cursed is everyone who hangs on a tree.* See the note on Galatians 3:10–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thorns on His head, sweat on the ground, and a tree."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Lamech named his son for.** *This one will **comfort us** concerning our work and the toil of our hands, **because of the ground which the LORD has cursed**.* He called him Noah, which means rest — and Genesis says he was the man who found grace. See the note on Genesis 6:5–8."}]}, "Genesis 3:20–24": {"title": "Garments of skin, and the sword at the gate", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three things happen in these five verses, and they do not obviously belong together: a name, a set of clothes, and an expulsion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The man named his wife Eve, because she would become the mother of all who live.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does it immediately after being told he will return to dust. Standing under a death sentence, Adam names his wife **Life**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only thing he has to go on is the promise about her seed, three verses earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clothing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD God, tender even in judgment, made garments of animal skin and clothed them Himself — covering what they could not cover on their own.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had made coverings out of fig leaves. Those are not mentioned again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God's replacement required an animal to die — the first death in Scripture, and it happens so that two guilty people can be dressed. He does not hand them the skins. He clothes them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The expulsion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He drove the man out, and east of the garden He stationed cherubim and a flaming sword.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stated reason is the tree of life: *he must not reach out and take also from the tree of life and live forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read as pure punishment that is bleak. Read as it stands, it is a mercy — immortality in a fallen condition would have locked the ruin in permanently. The gate is shut so that the situation stays fixable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The guards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cherubim, with a sword, guarding the way back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next time cherubim appear in Scripture they are hammered out of gold at either end of the mercy seat, wings over it, looking down at the place where the blood goes. Same creatures. The sword is gone, and the way is open."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line of the chapter.** *To guard the way to the tree of life.* Not to abolish it — to guard the road. Revelation ends with that tree in the middle of the city, its leaves for the healing of the nations, and no sword mentioned anywhere."}]}, "Genesis 3:6–7": {"title": "\"Their eyes were opened — but not the way the serpent said\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The serpent had promised that their eyes would be opened and they would be like God, knowing good and evil. The eyes did open. Everything else was a lie."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things she saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The fruit seemed good to eat, pleasing to the eye, and desirable for the wisdom it promised.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three appraisals, made in order, before a hand moves. The tree had been there the whole time; what changed was the looking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's list of what the world offers runs the same three ways — the craving of the body, the craving of the eyes, the bragging about what a man has. See the note on 1 John 2:16–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Who was with her\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She took some and ate, and gave some to her husband, **who was with her**, and he ate too.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words that are easy to skim. Adam was not away in another part of the garden. He was standing there through the entire conversation, and said nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the opened eyes actually showed them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Suddenly they saw their own nakedness, and they felt exposed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise was knowledge of good and evil. The delivery was self-consciousness — the first thing the new sight produced was shame about themselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been naked in the previous chapter and it is specifically noted that they felt no shame. Nothing about their bodies changed. What changed was what they could see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fig leaves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they sewed fig leaves together and made coverings for themselves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first human response to guilt is a covering they make. It does not last — before the chapter ends God makes them garments of skin, which required a death they did not perform."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was actually lost.** Not innocence in the abstract. Read on: the sound of God walking in the garden, and two people hiding from it. The tree cost them the walk."}]}, "Genesis 3:8–13": {"title": "\"Where are you?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God's first recorded words after the fall are a question, and He already knows the answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sound"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They heard the sound of the LORD God walking in the garden in the cool of the day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The detail implies a habit. This was not the first evening He had walked there — what is new is that nobody comes out to meet Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where are you?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an inquiry. It is the opening of a conversation with people who have hidden, and it invites them to say where they have got to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every question in this passage works the same way: *who told you that you were naked?* and *what have you done?* God asks; He does not announce."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Adam actually admits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I heard You in the garden, and I was afraid, because I was naked, so I hid.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Notice what is in the answer and what is missing. Fear, nakedness, hiding — all true. The eating is not mentioned until God names it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then, when it is named: *the woman You gave me — she gave me the fruit, and I ate.* Two people blamed in one sentence, and one of them is God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The woman's answer is shorter and does the same: *the serpent deceived me.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody says *I did it*. That is the whole exchange, and it is the first conversation between God and guilty people in the Bible."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He does anyway.** The curse, then the promise about the woman's seed, and then — before the exile from the garden — *the LORD God made garments of skin for Adam and his wife, and clothed them.* They had made coverings out of leaves. He replaced them with something that required a death, and dressed them Himself."}]}, "Genesis 30:22–24": {"title": "God remembered Rachel", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses at the end of a long domestic war, and they are the turning point of Genesis."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has been happening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two sisters married to the same man, and a competition running for chapters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leah unloved and fertile; Rachel loved and barren. Two maids brought in as proxies. Eleven sons and a daughter, named as moves in an argument — *the LORD has looked on my affliction*, *now this time my husband will become attached to me*, *with great wrestlings I have wrestled with my sister*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rachel had said to Jacob: *give me children, or else I die*. And he had answered angrily: *am I in the place of God, who has withheld from you the fruit of the womb?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **God remembered Rachel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **zakar**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It never means God had forgotten. It is the word for God acting on a commitment — *God remembered Noah* while the water was still up, *God remembered His covenant with Abraham* in Egypt, *God remembered Hannah*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every time it appears, something changes immediately afterwards."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **listened to her** and **opened her womb**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *God listened to her*, which means she had been asking. None of the praying is recorded."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *opened her womb* — the same phrase used of Leah at the start of the rivalry: *when the LORD saw that Leah was unloved, He opened her womb*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He opened both, at different times, for different reasons."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She said, \"**God has taken away my disgrace**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **asaph** — gathered up, removed. And **cherpah**, reproach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Barrenness was a public shame, and she names the removal of it before she names the child."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She named him **Joseph**, saying, \"**May the LORD add to me another son**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yoseph** — may He add. From **yasaph**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs are audible in the name: **asaph**, taken away, and **yasaph**, may He add."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She names her son for both — what has been removed and what she is still asking for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing she does with a son is ask for another."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she got"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Benjamin, on the road to Bethlehem, and she died having him. She named him **Ben-Oni**, son of my sorrow, and Jacob renamed him **Benjamin**, son of my right hand. See the note on Genesis 35:16–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second son was granted and it cost her life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob immediately tells Laban he wants to go home: *send me away, that I may go to my own place and to my country*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph's birth is what starts the return to Canaan. The whole speckled-and-spotted business with the flocks follows in the same breath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the boy becomes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The coat, the dreams, the pit, Potiphar's house, the prison, the two dreams in Pharaoh's court, and a granary that keeps the family alive. See the note on Genesis 42:6–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything from Genesis 37 to the end of the book comes out of these three verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line that runs through"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But God was with him.* Genesis says it in the prison and in Potiphar's house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at the end: *you meant evil against me, but God meant it for good*. See the note on Genesis 50:15–21."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What she named him.** Not for the child she had. *May He add* — the first thing she does after years of waiting is ask for the next one."}]}, "Genesis 32:22–26": {"title": "\"I will not let You go unless You bless me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jacob sends everyone he loves across a river and stays behind, and something happens in the dark that changes his name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Alone, on purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Jacob was left alone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just sent his wives, his children and everything he owns across the ford. Esau is coming with four hundred men and Jacob has spent the evening arranging gifts to go ahead of him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he empties the camp of everybody and stays on the wrong side of the water by himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wrestling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man wrestled with him until the breaking of day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text calls him a man. Hosea, looking back, calls him the angel; Jacob will say he has seen God face to face and names the place accordingly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the man saw that he could not overpower Jacob* — which is a strange sentence about God, until you notice how it is resolved. One touch to the hip socket and the match is over. The strength was never the question."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grip"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let me go, for the day is breaking.\" But Jacob said, \"I will not let You go unless You bless me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is now injured and cannot win, and he holds on anyway. The wrestling turns into clinging at the exact moment he stops being able to fight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is asked his name — the same question his father asked him in the dark years earlier, when he answered *I am Esau your firstborn*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This time he says Jacob: heel-grabber, supplanter. He tells the truth about himself, and is immediately given a new name."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he took away.** A blessing, a name, and a limp. Genesis says the sun rose on him as he passed Penuel, *and he was limping because of his hip*. The injury does not get healed in the story. He walks into the meeting with Esau newly named and permanently marked."}]}, "Genesis 32:9–12": {"title": "I crossed with nothing but my staff", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first extended prayer in the Bible, and it is prayed by a frightened man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty years out of the country, coming home, and the messengers have just returned: *we came to your brother Esau, and he also is coming to meet you, **and four hundred men are with him***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Jacob was greatly afraid and distressed.* He divides the camp in two, so that if one is struck the other may escape."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he prays."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**God of my father Abraham, God of my father Isaac** — LORD, **You who said to me, 'Go back to your country and your people, and I will do you good'**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He starts with the covenant line, and then with the specific instruction that put him on this road."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not where he is by choice. He was told to come."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word he uses of himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am not worthy of the least** of all the kindness and faithfulness You have shown me, Your servant.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **qatonti** — I am small. One word, a verb: *I have been made little*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two nouns"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chesed ve'emet** — covenant-love and faithfulness, or kindness and truth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pair runs through the Old Testament and lands in John: *the Word became flesh… **full of grace and truth***. See the note on John 1:14–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind it is **charitos kai aletheias**, which is what the Septuagint uses for this Hebrew pair."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I crossed this Jordan with nothing but my staff**, and now **I have become two camps**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty years earlier he had left with what he could carry, running from a brother he had cheated out of a blessing. See the note on Genesis 28:10–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Now he has wives, children, servants, and enough livestock to send a herd ahead as a present in five separate droves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is the accountant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is. Nobody asks him for this comparison."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who spent twenty years outmanoeuvring Laban does the sum on his own life and says *I am small*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Rescue me, I pray, from the hand of my brother, from the hand of Esau**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names him twice — *my brother*, and then *Esau*. The relationship and the man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The honesty"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**For I am afraid of him** — afraid he will come and strike us all down, **the mothers with the children**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not dress it up. The specific fear is stated, including who he thinks will die."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But You said**, 'I will surely do you good, and make your descendants like the sand of the sea, too many to count.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He ends by quoting God back to God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer opens with a promise and closes with a promise, and the fear is in the middle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens that night"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Jacob was left alone; and a Man wrestled with him until the breaking of day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He said, \"**Your name shall no longer be called Jacob, but Israel**; for you have struggled with God and with men, and have prevailed.\"* See the note on Genesis 32:24–28."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked to be delivered from his brother and got a wrestling match with someone else, and a limp."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Esau does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Esau ran to meet him, and embraced him, and fell on his neck and kissed him, and they wept.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The four hundred men turn out to be an escort."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jacob says to him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have seen your face as though I had seen the face of God**, and you were pleased with me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is what he had said about the place he prayed in: *I have seen God face to face, and my life is preserved*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word.** **Qatonti** — I am small. One Hebrew verb, said by a man looking at two camps he did not have twenty years earlier."}]}, "Genesis 33:8–11": {"title": "\"I have all I need\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two brothers meet after twenty years, and the argument is about a gift."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jacob had expected"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had heard Esau was coming with four hundred men, *and Jacob was greatly afraid and distressed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He divided the camp, prayed, sent droves of livestock ahead in waves, and wrestled all night at the Jabbok — where his name was changed and his hip was put out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He limps into this meeting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What actually happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Esau **ran to meet him, and embraced him, and fell on his neck and kissed him, and they wept**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing Jacob planned for was needed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Esau asked, \"**What did you mean by all those herds I met on the way?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two hundred female goats, twenty males, two hundred ewes, twenty rams, thirty camels with their young, forty cows, ten bulls, twenty female donkeys and ten foals — sent in separate droves with space between them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Esau wants to know what it was for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**To find favor in your eyes, my lord**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chen** — grace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob is still buying safety from a man who has already embraced him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Esau's reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I already have plenty, my brother. Keep what is yours**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yesh li rav** — I have much."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**My brother.** He uses the word Jacob has been avoiding; Jacob keeps saying *my lord*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Jacob's insistence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"No, please… if I have found favor in your eyes, then **accept this gift from my hand**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *gift* here is **berakah** — blessing. Not the ordinary word for a present."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is handing back a blessing to the brother he took one from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **seeing your face is like seeing the face of God**, now that you have received me so kindly.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said the same thing about the night before: *I have seen God face to face, and my life is preserved* — and named the place **Peniel**, the face of God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two faces in two nights, and he uses the same phrase for both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two claims"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Esau: *I have **plenty**.* — **rav**, much."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob: *God has been gracious to me, and **I have all I need**.* — **yesh li kol**, I have everything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew words are different and the difference is the point. Much, and all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob is the one who arrived expecting to be killed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason he gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **God has been gracious to me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **channani** — from the same root as the favour he keeps asking Esau for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is asking a man for grace while saying God has already given him it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The outcome"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Jacob **urged him, and Esau accepted it**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Esau offers to travel with him. Jacob makes an excuse about the children and the flocks being tender, says he will follow to Seir, and then goes the other way, to Succoth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis records it without comment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two brothers meet once more, at their father Isaac's burial, and bury him together."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two sentences.** *I have plenty* and *I have everything*. One is said by the brother who never left home; the other by the one who came back limping, expecting to die."}]}, "Genesis 35:16–20": {"title": "Ben-oni and Benjamin", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A woman dies naming her son, and her husband changes the name before she is buried."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They journeyed on **from Bethel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bethel is where Jacob had just built an altar and been renamed Israel a second time. The family is on the road south."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The labour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rachel went into labor — and it was **a hard, bitter labor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *she had hard labour*, and the word is repeated in the next verse for emphasis."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she had asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Years earlier: *give me children, or else I die.* And when Joseph was born: *the LORD shall add to me another son* — which is what **Joseph** means."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She got the second son and it cost her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The midwife"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not be afraid; you have another son**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last words spoken to her are the answer to the prayer she had prayed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her name for him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With her last breath she named him **Ben-oni, \"son of my sorrow.\"**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **oni** is sorrow, trouble, affliction. The same root as the name Jabez carried. See the note on 1 Chronicles 4:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She names him after what it cost."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His father's name for him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **his father called him Benjamin, \"son of my right hand.\"**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ben-yamin.** The right hand is the place of strength and of favour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob does not argue with what she saw. He gives the boy a different name and it is the one that stands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last name in Scripture is given by the father."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The burial"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Rachel died and was **buried on the road to Ephrath, that is, Bethlehem**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in the family tomb at Machpelah, where Sarah, Abraham, Isaac, Rebekah and Leah are. She is buried at the roadside where she died."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ephrath is Bethlehem.** The narrator adds it so nobody misses where."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pillar"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jacob set up a pillar over her grave — **the marker of Rachel's tomb, standing there to this day**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the tomb turns up again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah: *a voice was heard in Ramah, lamentation and bitter weeping, **Rachel weeping for her children**, refusing to be comforted, because they are no more.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Matthew quotes it — over Bethlehem, after Herod's soldiers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jacob says about it at the end of his life"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fifty years later, blessing Joseph's sons in Egypt: *but as for me, when I came from Padan, Rachel died beside me… and **I buried her there on the way to Ephrath, that is, Bethlehem***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is blind and dying and still mentions the roadside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Benjamin goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He becomes the youngest of the twelve, the one Jacob will not let out of his sight, and the one the whole of the Joseph reunion turns on. See the notes on Genesis 43:1–7 and Genesis 44:1–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul was of the tribe of Benjamin, and says so twice."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two names.** One given by a dying woman out of what it cost her, and one given by the father over the same child — and Genesis records both and then only ever uses the second."}]}, "Genesis 35:27–29": {"title": "His sons buried him together", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jacob gets home, and the chapter closes with two brothers at a graveside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arrival"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At last Jacob **came home to his father Isaac at Mamre**, near Kiriath-arba — that is, Hebron.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyavo Ya'aqov el-Yitzchaq aviv** — and Jacob came to Isaac his father."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How long he has been away"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty years with Laban, and then the years at Succoth and Shechem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He left with a stolen blessing and a brother who intended to kill him. *When the days of mourning for my father are at hand, then I will kill my brother Jacob.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Esau said it about this funeral."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the place is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where **Abraham and Isaac had lived as strangers in the land**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **gar** — sojourned, lived as a resident alien."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three generations, in the country they were given, described as guests in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The span"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Isaac lived **a hundred and eighty years**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The longest life of the three patriarchs, and the least eventful."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis gives Abraham fourteen chapters and Jacob more. Isaac's own story is largely his father's and then his sons'."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The formula"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Isaac breathed his last and died, **an old man full of years**, and he was **gathered to his people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **zaqen usava yamim** — old and satisfied of days."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word used of Abraham. See the note on Genesis 25:7–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaac's later life was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Blind, in a tent, deceived by his wife and his younger son over a bowl of stew and a goatskin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who? Where is the one who hunted game and brought it to me?… **and indeed he shall be blessed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He trembled violently, and did not revoke it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The burial"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His sons **Esau and Jacob buried him together**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew names Esau first — the older son, in the correct order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that sentence carries"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two of them had met once since Jacob came back: *but Esau ran to meet him, and embraced him, and fell on his neck and kissed him, and they wept*. See the note on Genesis 32:9–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then they separated. Esau went to Seir; Jacob went to Succoth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are together again for this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ishmael and Isaac buried Abraham together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Esau and Jacob bury Isaac together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice, the displaced brother and the chosen one stand at the same grave, and Genesis records it without comment either time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 36 is Esau's genealogy — kings and chiefs of Edom, a full chapter of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now these are the generations of Esau, who is Edom.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis gives the rejected line its list before returning to Jacob's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Isaac is buried"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Machpelah, the field Abraham bought from Ephron for four hundred shekels."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob names it on his own deathbed: *there they buried Abraham and Sarah his wife, **there they buried Isaac and Rebekah his wife**, and there I buried Leah*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chapter this closes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 35 has Bethel, the burial of Rebekah's nurse under an oak, the death of Rachel on the road to Bethlehem, and Reuben's sin with Bilhah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four graves and a scandal, and then this."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The sentence.** *His sons Esau and Jacob buried him together.* Twenty years earlier one of them had said he would kill the other at exactly this funeral."}]}, "Genesis 37:1–4": {"title": "The coat", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four verses of setup, and every one of them adds a reason for what happens next."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is the account of his family.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **toledot** — generations. Genesis is organised by these headings, ten of them, and this is the last."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heading says *the generations of Jacob*, and what follows is almost entirely about Joseph."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The age"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph, **seventeen years old**, was tending the flocks with his brothers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventeen. The number turns up again at the end: Jacob lives *seventeen years* in Egypt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which brothers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sons of **Bilhah and Zilpah**, his father's wives.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The maids' sons — Dan, Naphtali, Gad and Asher. The lowest-status four."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The report"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Joseph **brought their father a bad report about them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **dibbatam ra'ah** — their evil report. The noun means slander or whispering elsewhere in Scripture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis does not say whether it was true. It says he brought it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The favouritism"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **Israel loved Joseph more than all his other sons**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated flatly. Jacob grew up in a house where his mother preferred him and his father preferred Esau, and does the same thing to his own children."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because he was **the child of his old age**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Benjamin is younger. The real reason is upstream: Joseph is Rachel's, and Rachel was the one he worked fourteen years for. See the note on Genesis 29:15–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The coat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he made him **a beautiful coat, richly woven**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ketonet passim**. What it means exactly is uncertain — a long-sleeved robe reaching to the ankles, or a coat of many colours, from the Greek version."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way it is not work clothing. Long sleeves and a long hem are what a man wears who is not expected to labour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only other **ketonet passim** in the Bible is worn by Tamar, King David's daughter, and the text notes that *the king's virgin daughters wore such apparel*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten men out in the fields in short tunics, and one boy in a garment for royalty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But when his brothers saw that their father loved him more than any of them, **they hated him**, and **they could not speak a kind word to him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *they could not speak peaceably to him* — **shalom**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They could not say *shalom*, which is what you say to somebody when you pass them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four causes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis lists them in order and does not comment: the bad report, the father's preference, the coat, and then the dreams. See the note on Genesis 37:5–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every party in the story contributes something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the coat becomes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they took Joseph's tunic, killed a kid of the goats, and **dipped the tunic in the blood**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they sent the tunic of many colours, and they brought it to their father and said, \"We have found this. Do you know whether it is your son's tunic or not?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob had once brought his blind father goatskins to feel and a dish of goat meat, saying *I am Esau your firstborn*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is now deceived by his sons with a goat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line of the paragraph"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They could not speak a kind word to him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis reports the silence before it reports anything they did."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the garment said.** Long sleeves and a long hem — the clothing of somebody who does not work, handed to one of eleven working sons."}]}, "Genesis 37:12–17": {"title": "I am looking for my brothers", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A father sends a son to check on brothers who hate him, and the son says yes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the brothers are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph's brothers went off to graze their father's flocks **near Shechem**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Shechem, of all places. It is where Simeon and Levi had massacred every male in the city after their sister was violated, and where Jacob had said *you have troubled me, making me obnoxious among the inhabitants of the land*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have taken the flocks to a town their family is hated in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has already happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The coat of many colours, and *when his brothers saw that their father loved him more than all his brothers, **they hated him and could not speak peaceably to him***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the dreams — the sheaves bowing, the sun and moon and eleven stars — and *they hated him even more*. See the note on Genesis 37:5–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has established the hatred three times before this errand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sending"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Israel said to Joseph, \"Your brothers are pasturing the flocks at Shechem. **Come, I am going to send you to them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob is called **Israel** here, the covenant name, in the verse where he sends his son."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am ready**,\" Joseph answered.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hinneni** — here I am."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word Abraham said on the mountain, and Moses at the bush, and Samuel in the dark. See the note on 1 Samuel 3:4–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is seventeen, and he knows what his brothers think of him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The errand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go and see whether all is well with your brothers** and with the flocks, and bring word back to me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **re'eh et-shelom achecha** — see the **shalom** of your brothers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is sent to check on their peace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He sent him **from the Valley of Hebron**, and Joseph came to Shechem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fifty miles, on foot, through hill country."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The man in the field"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man found him **wandering in the fields** and asked, \"**What are you looking for?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **to'eh** — wandering, straying. The word used of a lost sheep."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man is unnamed and appears once. Without him the errand fails and the story ends."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am looking for my brothers**,\" he said. \"Can you tell me where they are grazing the flock?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **et-achai anochi mevaqqesh** — my brothers I am seeking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The redirection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They have moved on from here. **I heard them say, 'Let us go to Dothan.'**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dothan is another fifteen miles north. He had gone fifty and the flock had moved on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A stranger happened to overhear where."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **Joseph followed after his brothers and found them at Dothan**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He keeps going."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is waiting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now when they saw him afar off, even before he came near them, **they conspired against him to kill him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look, this dreamer is coming! Come therefore, let us kill him and cast him into some pit.\"* See the note on Genesis 37:18–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They strip the coat, throw him in a dry cistern, and sit down to eat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Dothan is otherwise known for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The city where Elisha's servant woke to find an army surrounding them, and the prophet prayed *LORD, open his eyes* — *and behold, the mountain was full of horses and chariots of fire*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Joseph says at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It was not you who sent me here, but God**.\"* See the note on Genesis 45:1–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-two years later, to the same brothers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sending"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A father sends his son to brothers who will not receive him, and the son goes willingly and keeps going when the first place is empty."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word.** **Hinneni** — here I am. Said by a seventeen-year-old being sent fifty miles to brothers the chapter has just told us three times could not speak a kind word to him."}]}, "Genesis 37:25–28": {"title": "Then they sat down to eat", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Five words in the middle of the story that are worse than anything else in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They stripped him of the coat and threw him into a pit. The account notes: *the pit was empty; there was no water in it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Reuben had talked them out of killing him, meaning to come back for him later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The meal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **they sat down to eat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their brother is in a hole a few yards away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis says nothing more about it. The sentence is left there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Years later they say what they had left out: *we saw the anguish of his soul when he pleaded with us, and we would not hear*. See the note on Genesis 42:6–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was audible while they ate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The caravan"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Looking up, they saw a **caravan of Ishmaelites coming from Gilead**, their camels loaded with **spices, balm, and myrrh**, on their way down to Egypt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ishmaelites — descendants of Abraham's other son. Family, at a distance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cargo list is precise: **nekhot**, **tsori**, **lot** — gum, balm, myrrh. Trade goods for the Egyptian embalming industry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Balm of Gilead, on the way to Egypt, passing a pit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Judah's proposal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What do we gain if we kill our brother and cover up his blood?** Come, **let us sell him to the Ishmaelites and not lay our hands on him**. After all, **he is our brother, our own flesh**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three arguments: profit, clean hands, and family feeling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last one is the ugliest, because it is used to justify selling him rather than to stop it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah's suggestion saves his life and it is not why he makes it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The agreement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His brothers agreed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *and his brothers listened*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The price"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **sold him to the Ishmaelites for twenty pieces of silver**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus 27 sets the valuation for a male between five and twenty years old: *twenty shekels*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The going rate for a boy of that age, paid exactly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two decades later the price of a man is thirty pieces, and Judas takes it. See the note on Matthew 26:14–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both transactions are proposed by a man called Judah."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Reuben finds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Reuben returned to the pit, and indeed **Joseph was not in the pit**; and he **tore his clothes**. And he returned to his brothers and said, \"**The lad is no more; and I, where shall I go?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had a plan and was too slow."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Kill a goat, dip the coat, and send it to their father with a question: *do you know whether it is your son's tunic or not?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They never say the word *dead*. They let him say it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he said, \"It is my son's tunic. A wild beast has devoured him.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Judah does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 38 — he leaves his brothers, marries a Canaanite, loses two sons, and is caught out by Tamar at the roadside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis interrupts the Joseph story for a whole chapter to tell it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who says *what profit is there* in chapter 37 is the man who says, in chapter 44, *let your servant remain instead of the lad as a slave to my lord*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the line runs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Through Judah. *Judah, you are he whom your brothers shall praise… the sceptre shall not depart from Judah.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The sentence Genesis leaves standing.** *Then they sat down to eat.* Nothing is added to it, and twenty-two years later they admit they could hear him."}]}, "Genesis 37:5–11": {"title": "Two dreams", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two dreams, told out loud to the worst possible audience."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result stated first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Joseph had a dream, and when he told it to his brothers, **they hated him all the more**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis reports the reaction before the content. It does this twice in this passage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The invitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Listen to this dream I had**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gathers them for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the text suggests he was told to tell them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first dream"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"We were out in the field **binding sheaves of grain**, and suddenly **my sheaf rose and stood upright**, and **your sheaves gathered around and bowed down to it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A harvest image, given to a family of shepherds. They do not farm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It fits Egypt, where he ends up running a granary, better than it fits Canaan."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their reading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So you think you will reign over us? You think you will rule us?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs — reign and have dominion — and they supply the interpretation themselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not say what it meant. They did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **they hated him still more for his dreams and his words**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both. The dreams, and the telling of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second dream"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I dreamed again — this time **the sun and the moon and eleven stars were bowing down to me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven stars, and he is the twelfth. The count is exact and everyone in the room can do the arithmetic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he tells"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When he told **his father as well**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first dream went to the brothers. This one goes to the whole household."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rebuke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His father **rebuked him**. \"What is this dream? **Am I and your mother and your brothers really to come and bow to the ground before you?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob does the same as the brothers — supplies the interpretation while objecting to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And he names himself as the sun. He knows exactly what it says."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem with the moon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rachel is dead. She died giving birth to Benjamin two chapters earlier. See the note on Genesis 35:16–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob's objection includes a figure who cannot bow to anyone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two responses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His brothers were **jealous of him**, but his father **kept the matter in mind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *his father observed the saying* — **shamar**, kept, guarded, watched."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same construction Luke uses of Mary: *but Mary kept all these things and pondered them in her heart*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob rebukes him in public and files it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the sheaves come true"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So his brothers came and **bowed low before him, their faces to the ground**.* In an Egyptian granary, buying grain, twenty-two years later. See the note on Genesis 42:6–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The dream was about sheaves, and the fulfilment happens over sacks of corn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the second one does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Israel said to Joseph, \"I had not thought to see your face; but in fact, God has also shown me your offspring!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob comes down to Egypt at the end and Joseph goes up in his chariot to meet him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sun does not bow, but he comes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Joseph says at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You meant evil against me, but **God meant it for good**.\"* See the note on Genesis 50:15–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the dreams and that sentence: a pit, a caravan, a slave market, a false accusation, a prison, and two forgotten years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the dreams warned him about any of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he did with them.** Told them. Genesis records the telling twice and the hatred twice, and never says the dreams were wrong."}]}, "Genesis 39:1–6": {"title": "\"But the LORD was with Joseph\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The lowest point of his life so far, and the chapter's second word is *but*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph had been **taken down to Egypt**, and Potiphar… **bought him** from the Ishmaelites.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventeen years old, stripped of the coat, thrown in a pit by his brothers, sold to a caravan, resold in a foreign market to a man who ran executions for Pharaoh."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in that sentence is done to him. He is the object of every verb."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the LORD was with Joseph**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The clause appears four times in this chapter — twice here, and twice again after he is put in prison."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses says it at the top and at the bottom, and never in between explains how it looked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What followed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **everything went well for him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he was a man who prospers* — **tsalach**, to advance, to succeed, to push through."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said of a slave. He is still owned; the word is used anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Potiphar could see"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His Egyptian master, **who saw that the LORD was with him** and **made everything he did prosper in his hands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A pagan officer of Pharaoh's court works out that a Hebrew slave's God is the reason his house is running well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is recorded of Joseph preaching. Potiphar drew the conclusion from the results."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promotion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph **found favor** in his master's eyes and became **his personal servant**, and Potiphar **put him in charge of his household** and **entrusted everything** he owned to his care.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four stages in one verse. Favour, then personal attendance, then charge of the house, then everything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Favour** is **chen** — grace. The same word used over Noah, over Esther, and over Israel in Egypt on the morning they left. See the note on Exodus 12:33–36."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing spreads"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From the moment he gave Joseph that charge, **the LORD blessed the Egyptian's household for Joseph's sake**; the LORD's blessing rested on everything he had, **in the house and in the field**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Potiphar gets blessed. He does not believe, he owns a slave, and his crops and his household prosper because of who is in his house."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Potiphar stopped thinking about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Potiphar **left everything in Joseph's hands** and **gave no thought to anything but the food he ate**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He hands over the accounts and stops looking at them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now Joseph was **well-built and handsome**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses drops it in at the end of the paragraph without comment, and the next verse is *his master's wife cast longing eyes on Joseph.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence that closes the prosperity is the one that starts the disaster."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He refuses her, she keeps his garment, he is accused, and he goes to prison — stripped of a coat for the second time in his life by someone who wanted something from him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the prison: *the LORD was with Joseph and showed him mercy, and He gave him favor in the sight of the keeper of the prison.* The same three words again."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter measures.** Not his circumstances — those get worse. Moses tracks one thing across the whole of it, and states it four times in the same words."}]}, "Genesis 39:7–12": {"title": "\"And sin against God\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A slave in a foreign house, with nobody watching and nothing to lose, gives a reason nobody could have checked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What his position was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventeen when his brothers sold him. No family, no rights, no legal standing, and no prospect of the story ever reaching Canaan."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was, in every practical sense, unobserved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives three reasons and then a fourth, and the fourth is the one that matters."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*my master gives no thought to anything in this house*", "*he has entrusted everything he owns to me*", "*he has held nothing back from me **except you, because you are his wife***"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *\"**How then could I do such a wicked thing and sin against God?**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose wife"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say *because I would be caught*, or *because it is against the law*, or *because I would lose my position*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names two people who would be wronged — Potiphar, and God — and Potiphar is the lesser of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same instinct is in David's psalm after Bathsheba: *against You, You only, have I sinned.* See the note on Psalms 51:3–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Day after day\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Day after day she pressed him, but he would not listen; **he would not lie with her or even be near her**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two refusals. The second is the practical one — he stopped being in the room."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *to be with her*. He removed the opportunity as well as declining it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day it came to a head"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One day he went into the house to do his work, and **none of the household servants were inside**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator supplies that detail. Empty house, no witnesses, nobody to report anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She caught him by his garment.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But he **left the garment in her hand and fled outside**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He runs, and he lets go of the coat to do it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the second coat he has lost. His brothers stripped him of the long robe and dipped it in goat's blood to lie to his father with. Now a garment is left in a woman's hand and used to lie about him again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both times a coat is the evidence in a false story."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Prison. Immediately, without a hearing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's last line: *the LORD was with Joseph and showed him mercy, and gave him favour in the sight of the keeper of the prison.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same sentence that opened the chapter about Potiphar's house. Same clause, worse address."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it gained"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing he could see for two more years. The cupbearer forgot him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Pharaoh's dream, and a man who could be trusted with everything in Egypt because he had already been trusted with everything in one house and not touched the one thing withheld."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says at the end.** *You meant evil against me, but God meant it for good.* Said to the brothers, about the pit — but it covers the prison too. Nothing in these six verses looks like it is going anywhere good, and Genesis takes another eight chapters to show what it was for."}]}, "Genesis 4:8–12": {"title": "Blood that cries out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first death in the Bible is a murder, and God's response introduces an image the rest of scripture keeps using."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Your brother's blood is crying out to me\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew noun is plural — *bloods*, which the rabbis noticed and commented on for centuries: not one life only but every life that would have come from it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the verb is the ordinary word for a shout, a cry for help, the noise a wronged person makes. Blood that has been spilled is pictured as a voice, and what it says is *this was wrong and nothing has answered for it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what innocent blood does. It does not go quiet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question and the dodge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where is your brother Abel?* — God asks a question He does not need answered, exactly as He did in the garden."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Am I my brother's keeper?* is Cain's answer, and it is a good deal more brazen than Adam's. He does not hide; he throws the question back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to Cain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ground he worked will no longer yield to him, and he becomes a wanderer. But read on four verses: when Cain says the punishment is more than he can bear and that anyone finding him will kill him, God puts a mark on him to protect him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first murderer in scripture is not executed. He is marked so that nobody touches him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where this comes back.** Hebrews says the blood of Jesus *speaks better than the blood of Abel* — and the comparison only works because of this passage. Abel's blood cried out for the guilty to answer. The other blood is described as saying something better, which does not mean louder. It means it speaks in the opposite direction. See the note on Hebrews 12:18–24."}]}, "Genesis 40:5–8": {"title": "Why do you look so downcast", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A prisoner with a life sentence notices that two other men are having a bad morning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In prison, on a false accusation, having refused Potiphar's wife."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the LORD was with Joseph and showed him mercy, and He gave him favour in the sight of the keeper of the prison.* See the note on Genesis 39:19–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is running the place, and he is still in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is with him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pharaoh's **butler** and **baker** — the chief cupbearer and the chief baker, both offended their master and both put under Joseph's charge."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Court officials, and he is a foreign slave with a criminal record."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The night"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One night, **both men dreamed** — each his own dream, and **each dream carried its own meaning**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ish chalomo belaylah echad** — each man his dream in one night."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two dreams, one night, and the narrator says in advance that each had its own interpretation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the morning Joseph came to them and **saw that something was wrong; their faces were heavy with worry**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **zo'aphim** — troubled, sullen, downcast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He notices."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who has been sold by his brothers, sold in Egypt, framed by a woman, and forgotten in a prison, walks in and sees that two other men's faces are wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So he asked Pharaoh's officials… \"**Why do you look so downcast today?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has more reason to be downcast than either of them, and he asks them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"We've both had dreams, and **there's no one here to tell us what they mean**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt had a professional class of dream interpreters. In prison they have none."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Gently Joseph answered, \"**Doesn't the meaning of dreams belong to God? Tell me what you saw**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **halo lelohim pitronim** — do not interpretations belong to God?"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with the credit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deflects it before he has done anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the same thing later, in front of Pharaoh: *it is not in me; **God will give Pharaoh an answer of peace***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seventeen-year-old with the dreams — the sheaves bowing and the sun, moon and eleven stars — who told his brothers about them. See the note on Genesis 37:5–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had dreams then, and no interpretation for anyone. Now he interprets and asks nothing for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does ask for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But remember me when it is well with you**, and please show kindness to me… for indeed I was stolen away from the land of the Hebrews.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One request, to the man he told would live. See the note on Genesis 40:9–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet the chief butler **did not remember Joseph, but forgot him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last verse of the chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How long"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then it came to pass, **at the end of two full years**, that Pharaoh had a dream.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two more years, and then the butler remembers: *I remember my faults this day*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Pharaoh sent and called Joseph, and **they brought him quickly out of the dungeon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayritzuhu** — and they made him run."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirteen years of waiting, and the last stretch is at a sprint."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The question.** *Why do you look so downcast today?* Asked by the man in the room with the strongest case for looking downcast himself."}]}, "Genesis 41:25–32": {"title": "\"God is showing Pharaoh what He is about to do\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A prisoner tells the king of Egypt what his dreams meant, and the first word out of his mouth is God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Both of Pharaoh's dreams are one and the same**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two dreams — seven fat cows eaten by seven lean ones, and seven full heads of grain swallowed by seven withered ones."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt's magicians could not interpret either. Joseph says they are one dream told twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is doing it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**God is showing Pharaoh what He is about to do**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had already said it on the way in: *\"It is not in me; God will give Pharaoh an answer of peace.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And he says it again in verse 28: *\"It's as I told Pharaoh — **God has shown Pharaoh what He is about to do**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times in four verses, to a king who was worshipped as a god himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interpretation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The seven good cows are seven years, and the seven good heads of grain are seven years — **it is one dream**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The seven thin, ugly cows… and the seven empty heads scorched by the wind: they are **seven years of famine**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wind is the **khamsin**, the desert wind that blasts standing grain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The forecast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Seven years of great plenty are coming** to all of Egypt, but after them will come **seven years of famine**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The severity"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**All the plenty will be forgotten**, and the famine will **drain the land dry**. **The abundance will not even be remembered**, so severe will the famine be.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice. The seven good years will not register against the seven bad ones."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **the dream came to Pharaoh twice** because **the matter is firmly settled by God**, and **God will bring it about soon**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *established by God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The repetition is read as confirmation, not emphasis. Joseph explains the literary structure of the dream as part of the interpretation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody asked him for advice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now therefore, **let Pharaoh select a discerning and wise man**, and set him over the land of Egypt… and let them **gather all the food of those good years**… **then that food shall be a reserve for the land** for the seven years of famine.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He interprets the dream and then proposes a national policy: a fifth of the harvest, stored under officers, for seven years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the dream contained the plan."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The plan pleased Pharaoh and all his officials.* And: *\"**Since God has shown you all this, there is no one as discerning and wise as you**.\"* See the note on Genesis 41:37–45."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two Hebrew words Pharaoh uses — **chakam** and **navon** — are the ones Joseph had used in his own recommendation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph described the man needed and Pharaoh handed him the job."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had been doing beforehand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirteen years — sold at seventeen, Potiphar's house, prison on a false charge, and two more years forgotten by a butler he had helped."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had interpreted two dreams in that prison as well, and asked one man to remember him. *Yet the chief butler did not remember Joseph, but forgot him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole thing was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**God sent me before you to preserve life**.\"* See the note on Genesis 50:15–21."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How he opens.** Not with the interpretation. Three times in four verses he names who is doing the showing — standing in front of a man his own culture called divine."}]}, "Genesis 41:33–36": {"title": "Joseph's counsel", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Joseph was asked to interpret a dream. He gives the interpretation and then, without being asked, offers a national economic policy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was in a dungeon an hour ago. *They brought him quickly out of the dungeon; and he shaved, changed his clothing, and came to Pharaoh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirteen years between the pit and this room — sold, enslaved, falsely accused, imprisoned, and forgotten by the cupbearer for two more years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An interpretation. Nothing else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives it: seven years of plenty, seven of famine, *and the famine will deplete the land.* And *the dream was repeated twice because the thing is established by God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So now, **let Pharaoh look for a discerning and wise man** and set him over the land.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody requested advice. He supplies it anyway, in front of the most powerful man on earth, straight out of a cell."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plan"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["appoint **officers over the land**", "gather **a fifth** of the harvest during the seven good years", "**store up grain** under Pharaoh's authority", "**keep it safe in the cities**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "A fifth — twenty per cent, set aside across seven years of surplus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *gather* is a taxation word. It is a levy, and it is planned before there is any sign of trouble."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That food will be **a reserve for the land** through the seven years of famine… **so the land will not perish**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *reserve* is a deposit, something laid up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point is stated as survival rather than profit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the plan"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anything mystical. No prayer meeting, no further revelation, no waiting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is given seven years' notice and proposes storage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Pharaoh said to Joseph, \"Since God has shown you all this, **there is no one as discerning and wise as you**. You shall be over my house.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He describes the qualifications for the job and is handed the job."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt survives, and so does the family that threw him in the pit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the surplus goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph gathered very much grain, as the sand of the sea, **until he stopped counting, for it was immeasurable**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And when the famine came, *all countries came to Egypt to buy grain, because the famine was severe in all lands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A nation with a plan feeds the world for seven years."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says to his brothers about all of it.** *You meant evil against me, but **God meant it for good**, in order to bring it about as it is this day, to save many people alive.* The dungeon and the granary are the same story, and he says so."}]}, "Genesis 41:37–45": {"title": "\"One in whom the Spirit of God lives\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man goes from a prison cell to second in Egypt inside one morning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he had been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Pharaoh sent and called Joseph, and **they brought him hastily out of the dungeon**; and he shaved, changed his clothing, and came to Pharaoh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirteen years — sold at seventeen, in Potiphar's house, then in prison on a false charge, then forgotten for two more years by a butler who had promised to remember him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had just said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pharaoh had told him *I have heard it said of you that you can understand a dream to interpret it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph's answer: *\"**It is not in me; God will give Pharaoh an answer of peace**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same disclaimer Daniel gives Nebuchadnezzar centuries later. See the note on Daniel 2:29–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The assessment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The plan pleased Pharaoh and all his officials.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph had not only interpreted. He had added an administrative proposal — a fifth of the harvest, stored through seven years — that nobody asked him for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Pharaoh's question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Can we find anyone like this man, **one in whom the Spirit of God lives?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *a man in whom is the Spirit of God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A pagan king says it, about a Hebrew slave, in front of his whole court."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reasoning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Since God has shown you all this**, there is **no one as discerning and wise as you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pharaoh attributes the ability to God and promotes the man anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The appointment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will be over my house**, and all my people will do as you say. **Only in the throne will I be greater than you**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The insignia"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took **the signet ring from his own hand** and put it on Joseph's.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A signet ring was the seal of authority. Whatever it stamped was law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **dressed him in fine linen** and hung **a gold chain around his neck**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Linen and gold — the dress of an Egyptian official, and both appear in the tomb paintings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He had him **ride in the chariot of his second-in-command**, and men went ahead of him calling out, \"**Bow down!**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am Pharaoh, but **without your word no one will lift a hand or foot in all of Egypt**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The new name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Pharaoh gave Joseph a new name, **Zaphenath-Paneah**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The meaning is disputed. Older readings take it as *revealer of secrets*; Egyptologists have proposed *God speaks and he lives*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel is renamed Belteshazzar in the same way, by the same kind of court."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wife"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And gave him **Asenath, daughter of Potiphera the priest of On**, to be his wife.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On is Heliopolis, the centre of Egyptian sun worship. Joseph marries into its priesthood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis records it without comment, and their two sons — Manasseh and Ephraim — become two of Israel's tribes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His age"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph was **thirty years old** when he stood before Pharaoh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventeen when he was sold. Thirteen years between the pit and the palace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he names his sons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Manasseh** — *for God has made me forget all my toil and all my father's house*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ephraim** — *for God has caused me to be fruitful in the land of my affliction*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the second one after the place, not in spite of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole thing was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**God sent me before you to preserve life**.\"* And: *\"**You meant it for evil against me, but God meant it for good**.\"* See the note on Genesis 50:15–21."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Pharaoh notices.** Not the administration, first. He asks his court whether they can find another man with the Spirit of God in him — and then hands over the ring."}]}, "Genesis 41:53–57": {"title": "\"Go to Joseph\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The famine arrives on schedule, and the whole world ends up at one man's door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Exactly as he said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The seven years of plenty in Egypt came to an end, and the seven years of famine began, **just as Joseph had said**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven years earlier he was in a prison interpreting dreams for a butler and a baker. The dream he read for Pharaoh had a timetable in it, and Moses notes that the timetable held."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The famine spread to **every land**, but **throughout Egypt there was bread**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The famine is universal. The supply is in one country."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt had no more rain than anyone else. What Egypt had was seven years of somebody storing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Pharaoh's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people cried out to **Pharaoh** for food, and Pharaoh told them all, **\"Go to Joseph. Do whatever he tells you.\"**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They go to the king and the king redirects them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a strange sentence for a monarch. He has the title, the throne and the granaries in his name, and he sends the nation to a Hebrew ex-convict."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Do whatever he tells you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *what he says to you, do*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mary uses the same words at a wedding in Cana, about her Son, to the servants standing by six empty water pots. See the note on John 2:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The storehouses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph **opened the storehouses** and sold grain to the Egyptians.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were opened by the man who filled them. Nobody else had the keys or the knowledge of what was in them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Sold"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He sells it. The chapters that follow record what the price eventually was — money, then livestock, then land, then the people themselves as Pharaoh's servants."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses reports the whole transaction without editorial."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Everyone came"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And people came **from every country** to Egypt to buy grain from Joseph, because the famine was **severe over all the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That last line is the hinge of the book. The next verse of chapter 42 is Jacob saying to his sons, *why do you look at one another? I have heard that there is grain in Egypt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten brothers who sold him will walk up to the man selling grain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had been doing for seven years"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gathered grain **as the sand of the sea, very much, until he stopped counting**, for it was immeasurable.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As the sand of the sea* is the phrase used of Abraham's descendants."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did it during the good years, when nobody could see a reason to."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How he got there.** Sold at seventeen, a slave in Potiphar's house, accused and jailed, forgotten by a man who promised to remember him. Then one morning shaved, changed and standing in front of Pharaoh. Moses gives thirteen years to the descent and one chapter to the rise."}]}, "Genesis 42:1–5": {"title": "Why do you stand there looking at one another", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The famine reaches Canaan, and Jacob has to say the obvious thing out loud."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Jacob learned there was **grain in Egypt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt has grain because a Hebrew slave interpreted two dreams about cows and ears of corn and was put in charge of the storage. See the note on Genesis 41:33–36."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven years of collecting, and the whole world comes to buy. See the note on Genesis 41:53–57."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rebuke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He said to his sons, \"**Why do you just stand there looking at one another?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *why do you look at one another?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten grown men in a house with no food, waiting for somebody to say something."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-two years have passed since they sold their brother, and they have not told their father."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I've heard there's grain in Egypt. **Go down and buy some for us, so we can live and not die**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *that we may live and not die*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **ten of Joseph's brothers went down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten. Exactly the ten who were at the pit — Reuben, who tried to save him, and the nine who agreed to the sale."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who stays"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Jacob **did not send Benjamin, Joseph's brother**, along with them, for he was **afraid something might happen to him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph's brother* — his full brother, the other son of Rachel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob has already lost one son of that mother, as he believes, to a wild animal. See the note on Genesis 37:25–28."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *lest harm befall him* — **ason**, the same rare word Jacob uses again in the next chapter and nowhere else in Scripture except once more in Exodus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The others in the queue"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the sons of Israel came to buy grain **among the others who came**, because the famine had reached the land of Canaan too.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are in a line with everybody else. No special treatment, no introductions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is about to happen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So his brothers came and **bowed low before him, their faces to the ground**.* See the note on Genesis 42:6–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The dream from chapter 37 — the sheaves bowing — arrives in an Egyptian granary two decades later, and only one man in the room recognises it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jacob believes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That Joseph is dead. He has believed it for twenty-two years, since the coat came back covered in goat's blood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he mourned for his son many days… \"For I shall go down into the grave to my son in mourning.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the famine did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in the rest of Genesis follows from it — the journeys down, the silver in the sacks, the cup in Benjamin's, the revelation, and finally the whole family living in Goshen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And from Goshen comes Exodus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The words Jacob does not know he is saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Go down… that we may live and not die.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt is where the family survives, and it is where they end up as slaves for four hundred years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are true and Genesis says both."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Jacob had to tell them.** To stop looking at each other. Ten men in a famine, waiting — and the one thing that gets them moving is their father naming the obvious."}]}, "Genesis 42:29–38": {"title": "\"Everything is against me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An old man adds up his situation and reaches a conclusion that is wrong in every particular."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You are robbing me of my children. Joseph is gone, Simeon is gone, and now you want to take Benjamin. Everything is against me.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three losses counted, and then the summary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Item by item"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Joseph is gone.** Joseph is alive, second in Egypt, and is the man they have just been dealing with. He is the reason there is grain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Simeon is gone.** Simeon is being held by his own brother, in comfort, as surety. He is not in danger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Now you want to take Benjamin.** Benjamin is the one condition on which the whole family is about to be reunited and fed through five more years of famine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every fact in the list is true, and every conclusion drawn from it is false."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The silver"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As they emptied their sacks, **there was each man's bundle of silver** in his sack. When they and their father saw the silver, **they were afraid**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The money was put back by Joseph, as a gift. Grain paid for and the payment returned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They receive a kindness and are frightened by it, because they cannot imagine a reason for it that is not a trap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Everything is against me\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *all these things are against me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is one of the most quoted sentences in Genesis, and it is spoken by a man who is mistaken about all three of the things he has just listed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's line in Romans is the mirror of it: *we know that **all things work together for good** to those who love God.* See the note on Romans 8:28–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same word about the same kind of situation, from the other side of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jacob could see"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing that was actually happening. His son was ruler of the greatest empire on earth and had wept in the next room; the silver was a present; the demand for Benjamin was the last step before the reveal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "From inside the tent it looked like a family being dismantled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Reuben's offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You may put both my sons to death** if I don't bring him back.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a serious offer and a terrible one. Jacob does not even answer it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah's version, one chapter later, is the one that works: *I myself will be surety for him; from my hand you shall require him.* He offers himself rather than his children, and Jacob agrees."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says last"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"His brother is dead, and **he alone is left**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two sons of Rachel, and he speaks as though one is gone and one is all he has."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are alive, and within a chapter or two he will have them both."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says when he finds out.** *\"It is enough. **Joseph my son is still alive.** I will go and see him before I die.\"* And Genesis records that *the spirit of Jacob their father revived.*"}]}, "Genesis 42:6–17": {"title": "He knew them, and they did not know him", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The dream comes true in an Egyptian granary and only one man in the room knows it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His position"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph was **the governor of the land**, the one who sold grain to all its people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shallit** — the one with authority. He is thirty-nine or so, shaved and dressed as an Egyptian, speaking through an interpreter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So his brothers came and **bowed low before him, their faces to the ground**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The dream, from chapter 37: *behold, my sheaf arose and also stood upright; and indeed your sheaves stood all around and bowed down to my sheaf*. See the note on Genesis 37:5–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their answer at the time was *shall you indeed reign over us?* and they hated him for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The recognition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph **saw his brothers and knew them at once**, but he **acted like a stranger and spoke to them harshly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he made himself strange to them*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was seventeen when they last saw him. They are grown men who have aged twenty-two years, and he picks them out of a queue."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The asymmetry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph knew them, **though they did not know him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence is repeated in the passage because it is the engine of the whole story."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything that happens in the next three chapters depends on one party knowing and the other not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he remembered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **he remembered the dreams he'd once had about them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *Joseph remembered the dreams which he dreamed of them*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the pit. Not the silver. The dreams."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The accusation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You are spies. You've come to see where the land is unprotected**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *you have come to see the nakedness of the land*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a plausible charge. Ten Semitic men arriving together from Canaan, at the border, in a famine."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their defence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"We are **all sons of one man. We are honest men**, not spies.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *honest* is **kenim** — true, upright."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said by ten men who sold their brother and lied to their father about it for two decades."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the interrogation extracts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your servants are **twelve brothers**, sons of one man in Canaan. **The youngest is with our father right now, and one is no longer living**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what he was after. He now knows Benjamin is alive and at home, and he knows what they say about him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They volunteer the family structure under pressure without any idea who is listening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The test"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will not leave this place unless your youngest brother comes here**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole remaining plot is set here. Everything after this — the silver in the sacks, Jacob's refusal, Judah's pledge, the cup — comes out of one condition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three days"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So he put them all **in custody for three days**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days in an Egyptian cell for men who had left a brother in a pit and sat down to eat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they say on the third day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**We are truly guilty concerning our brother**, for we saw the anguish of his soul when he pleaded with us, and we would not hear. Therefore this distress has come upon us.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first time it is spoken aloud in twenty-two years. Genesis has not recorded Joseph's pleading until now — the account of the sale never mentioned it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *they did not know that Joseph understood them, for he spoke to them through an interpreter.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **he turned himself away from them and wept**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first of the times he cries in this story, and there are several. He weeps at the sight of Benjamin, he weeps when he tells them who he is, and he weeps on his father's neck."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he comes back and takes Simeon and binds him in front of them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The sentence the story runs on.** *Joseph knew them, though they did not know him.* It is stated twice, and everything for the next three chapters depends on it holding."}]}, "Genesis 43:1–7": {"title": "\"Why did you bring this trouble on me?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The grain runs out, and the argument the family had been avoiding has to happen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pressure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The famine **still lay heavy on the land**. When they had **eaten all the grain** they'd brought from Egypt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing has been decided since chapter 42. Simeon is still in an Egyptian prison and Jacob has refused to send Benjamin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have simply been eating."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go back and buy us a little more food**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob says it as though nothing else is in the way. *A little more* — as if a small trip were possible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Judah's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Judah **reminded him**, \"The man warned us plainly, '**You will not see my face unless your brother is with you**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes it twice in four verses. There is no way round it and he says so by repeating the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The terms"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If you send our brother along, **we'll go down and buy food for you**. But if you won't send him, **we can't go**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah puts the decision back where it belongs. He does not plead and does not decide for his father."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Jacob's complaint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Israel said, \"**Why did you bring this trouble on me by telling the man you had another brother?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *why did you deal so wrongfully with me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is looking for somebody to blame for a famine and a stranger's condition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The man questioned us closely** about ourselves and our family. '**Is your father still alive? Do you have another brother?**' We simply answered what he asked.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are telling the truth. Chapter 42 records the interrogation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they could not know"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How could we know he'd say, 'Bring your brother down here'?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph had been asking about his father and his mother's other son. The brothers took it for a Egyptian official's suspicion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Judah offers next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I myself will be surety for him; from my hand you shall require him**. If I do not bring him back to you and set him before you, then let me bear the blame forever.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Reuben had offered his own two sons in chapter 42 and Jacob did not answer him. See the note on Genesis 42:29–38."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah offers himself, and Jacob agrees."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Judah does later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "When the cup is found in Benjamin's sack, Judah makes the longest speech in Genesis and ends: *now therefore, please **let your servant remain instead of the lad as a slave to my lord**, and let the lad go up with his brothers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He keeps the offer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Judah had been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The brother who proposed selling Joseph in the first place: *what profit is there if we kill our brother and conceal his blood? Come, let us sell him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis puts twenty-two years and one chapter about Tamar between the two speeches."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Jacob's surrender"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take double money in your hand… **take your brother, and arise, go back to the man**. And may God Almighty give you mercy before the man, that he may release your other brother and Benjamin. **If I am bereaved, I am bereaved**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**God Almighty** — **El Shaddai**, the name given to Abraham at ninety-nine. See the note on Genesis 17:1–2."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What breaks the deadlock.** Not persuasion. The food ran out, and Judah put the terms in front of his father twice and then offered to stand for the boy himself."}]}, "Genesis 43:15–25": {"title": "Received at Joseph's house", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "They arrive expecting arrest and are shown to a dining room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they brought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the men took **the gift, double the silver, and Benjamin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things, and the third is what the whole trip was about. See the note on Genesis 43:1–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Joseph saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Joseph **saw Benjamin with them**, he said to his steward, \"**Bring these men into my house**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is looking for one face. The instruction follows immediately on seeing it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Benjamin is the only full brother he has — the other son of Rachel, born after Joseph was sold, whom he has never met as an adult."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Slaughter an animal and prepare a meal; **they will dine with me at noon**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their reading of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the men were afraid** to be taken there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It's because of the silver** returned in our sacks the first time. He wants to **fall on us, overpower us, seize us as slaves, and take our donkeys**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An invitation to lunch, read as an ambush."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not being paranoid without cause — they had found money they had not paid back in their sacks, and they are in a foreign country with no standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **at the doorway of the house** they approached Joseph's steward.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They will not go in. They intercept the steward at the threshold and make their case outside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The explanation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"We came down once before to buy food, and **each man's silver was right there in the mouth of his sack — the full amount**. We've **brought it back with us, and more silver besides**. **We don't know who put our silver in our sacks**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They produce the money before anyone accuses them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It's all right**,\" he said. \"**Don't be afraid**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shalom lakem** — peace to you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the steward says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Your God, the God of your father, must have given you treasure in your sacks. I received your silver**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An Egyptian household official tells eleven Hebrews that their father's God put money in their bags."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the second clause settles it: the account is closed. Whatever is in the sacks, the payment was made."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Simeon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he **brought Simeon out to them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The brother left in prison at the end of chapter 42, produced without ceremony."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hospitality"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The steward took the men into Joseph's house, gave them **water to wash their feet**, and **fed their donkeys**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three courtesies: shelter, water for the feet, fodder for the animals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The washing of feet was the first thing a guest received, and it is the one Simon the Pharisee skipped. See the note on Luke 7:44–50."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did with the gift"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they **laid out their gift**, for they'd heard they would eat there at noon.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Balm, honey, spices, myrrh, pistachios and almonds — Jacob's list, the best of the land, sent to a man who owned Egypt's granaries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph comes in, asks after their father, sees Benjamin, and has to leave the room. See the note on Genesis 43:26–34."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they were braced for.** Slavery. What they got was a threshold conversation, a steward saying *peace to you*, their brother released, water for their feet and hay for the donkeys — and the debt already settled."}]}, "Genesis 43:26–34": {"title": "Five times as much, and a man who had to leave the room", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Joseph gives a dinner for the brothers who sold him, and two things happen at it that they do not understand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The room he went into"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph hurried out, for his heart was overflowing at the sight of his brother, and he was about to weep. He went into his private room and wept there. Then he washed his face, came out, controlled himself, and said, \"Serve the meal.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has been the second most powerful man in Egypt for years. He sees his full brother for the first time since he was seventeen and has to leave his own house to cry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis records the washing and the composure. It is one of the most human paragraphs in the book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seating"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The brothers were seated in front of him in order, from the oldest to the youngest, and they looked at one another in amazement.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody had told the steward their ages. Ten men are placed in birth order by a household they have never visited, and they cannot account for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator gives no explanation either. He simply reports them staring at each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Benjamin's portion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Benjamin's portion was five times larger than anyone else's.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph does it deliberately, and it is a test as much as a gift. These are the men who once could not stand their father's favouritism toward one son and threw him in a pit for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Now, in front of them, the youngest is singled out again — and this time nobody objects. *So they drank and were merry together with him.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it is going.** The next chapter is the silver cup planted in Benjamin's sack, and Judah — the brother who proposed selling Joseph — offering himself as a slave in the boy's place. The dinner is the setup. Joseph is finding out whether they are the same men, and they are not."}]}, "Genesis 44:1–13": {"title": "The cup in Benjamin's sack", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last test, and Joseph builds it so that the brothers have every reason to walk away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Fill the men's sacks with **as much food as they can carry**, and put **each man's silver in the mouth of his sack**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same trick as the first trip, and it had terrified them then."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The addition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And put **my cup, the silver one, in the mouth of the youngest one's sack**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Benjamin. The one their father would not let out of his sight, and the one Judah has personally guaranteed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The staging"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At dawn the men were sent on their way. **They hadn't gone far from the city** when Joseph said to his steward, \"**Go after those men**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not far. They are close enough to be brought back and far enough to have committed to the road."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The accusation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why have you repaid good with evil?** Isn't this **the cup my master drinks from and uses for divination**? You've done a wicked thing.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Divination by cup was a known Egyptian practice — liquid poured and read."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph, who has told Pharaoh outright that interpretations belong to God, has the steward describe him in Egyptian terms. He is still in character."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your servants **would never do anything like that**. We even **brought back to you from Canaan the silver we found in our sacks**. Why would we steal silver or gold?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument is a good one. Men who returned money they could have kept do not steal a cup."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they swear"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If any of your servants is found with it, let him die, and the rest of us will become my lord's slaves**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They offer more than is asked. They are certain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The steward's terms"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Very well. But **only the one found with it will be my slave; the rest of you are free**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The condition is deliberately generous. Ten men can go home clean."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole design of the test: an exit is provided."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The search"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Each man **quickly lowered his sack to the ground** and opened it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are so confident they unload before being asked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The steward searched, **beginning with the oldest and ending with the youngest**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order draws it out. Ten sacks come up clean, one after another, and the tension rises with each one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The find"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the cup was found in Benjamin's sack**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At this **they tore their clothes in grief. They loaded their donkeys and returned to the city**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody takes the exit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-two years earlier these men sold a brother into Egypt and told their father a wild animal had eaten him. Offered the chance to leave Rachel's other son behind and go home free, all ten turn round."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what Joseph was testing, and it is the answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Judah says in the next chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The longest speech in Genesis — the whole story from their father's side, and then: *let your servant remain instead of the lad as a slave to my lord, and let the lad go up with his brothers. **For how shall I go up to my father if the lad is not with me?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who proposed the sale offers to take the place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Joseph could not restrain himself… and he cried out, \"**Make everyone go out from me!**\" And he wept aloud.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am Joseph; does my father still live?**\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the test was for.** Not the cup. Joseph gave them a clean way to abandon Rachel's remaining son and go home — and every one of them tore his clothes and went back."}]}, "Genesis 45:1–8": {"title": "I am Joseph", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He clears the room before he says who he is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What broke him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah has just offered himself in place of Benjamin: *let your servant remain instead of the lad as a slave to my lord*, and *how shall I go up to my father if the lad is not with me?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The brother who proposed selling Joseph offers to take a younger brother's punishment. See the note on Genesis 44:30–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the speech that ends the concealment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clearing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph **could no longer hold himself back** in front of all his attendants. \"**Everyone leave me!**\" he cried.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **velo-yachol Yoseph lehitappeq** — he could not restrain himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has restrained himself twice already, going out to weep and washing his face and coming back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the room is emptied"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **no one else was with him** when Joseph revealed himself to his brothers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the second man in Egypt. What is about to happen would humiliate them in front of the court."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He protects them from the disclosure at the moment of making it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sound"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he **wept so loudly that the Egyptians heard him**, and word reached Pharaoh's house.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He sent them out and they heard anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am Joseph!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ani Yoseph**. Two words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have known him for two chapters as **Zaphnath-Paaneah**, the Egyptian governor who speaks through an interpreter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Is my father really still alive?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have already told him twice that Jacob is alive. He has asked before and been answered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a request for information."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **his brothers could not answer him** — they were **stunned into silence** at the sight of him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nivhalu mippanav** — they were terrified before his face."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man they sold has absolute power over them and has just said his name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Come close to me**,\" Joseph said. So they came closer.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **geshu-na elai**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not leave them standing where they are."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The full sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am Joseph, your brother, the one you sold into Egypt**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He states it plainly. No euphemism, no softening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Your brother**, in the middle of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But don't be distressed, and don't be angry with yourselves** for selling me here.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **al-te'atzvu** — do not be grieved, and **ve'al-yichar be'eineichem** — and let it not burn in your eyes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He forbids them the self-recrimination before they have started."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Because it was to save lives that God sent me ahead of you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lemichyah shelachani Elohim** — for preservation of life God sent me."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **two years now the famine has been in the land**, and there are **five more years** with no plowing or harvest.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two down, five to go. He is not speaking in generalities."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose stated twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"God sent me ahead of you to **keep a remnant of you alive** on the earth and to **save your lives by a great deliverance**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lipletah gedolah** — for a great escape, a great deliverance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So it was not you who sent me here, but God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which does not deny that they sold him — he has said it twice in eight verses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two agencies over one act, and he names both without collapsing either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The titles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He has made me **a father to Pharaoh**, **lord of all his household**, and **ruler over all of Egypt**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three offices, all held by a man who arrived as a slave and spent years in a prison."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says at the end of the book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You meant evil against me; **but God meant it for good**, in order to bring it about as it is this day, to save many people alive.\"* See the note on Genesis 50:19–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said again, after Jacob dies and the brothers panic that he has been waiting."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order.** He empties the room, says his name, tells them plainly that they sold him, and then forbids them to torture themselves about it — in that sequence."}]}, "Genesis 45:21–28": {"title": "\"Don't quarrel on the way\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Joseph sends his brothers home loaded with gifts, and the last thing he says to them is oddly practical."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What each of them got"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To each of them he gave a new set of clothing, but to Benjamin he gave three hundred pieces of silver and five sets of clothing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just revealed himself and forgiven them outright. Then he hands out clothes — and gives Benjamin five times what anyone else received, exactly as he did at the dinner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not being careless. He knows what favouritism did to this family; he grew up in the middle of it. He does it anyway, in the open, having already told them the whole thing was God's doing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parting instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Don't quarrel on the way.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew can mean do not be agitated, or do not fall out with one another. Both fit the situation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have a three-week journey ahead with a great deal to talk about — chiefly who suggested what, twenty-two years earlier, at a pit. Joseph, who has just told them not to be distressed or angry with themselves, adds one more sentence for the road."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He forgave them and then anticipated the argument they were going to have about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What convinced Jacob"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jacob's heart went numb; he could not believe them.* Words did not do it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But when he saw the carts Joseph had sent to carry him, the spirit of their father Jacob revived.* The carts did — physical evidence that somebody in Egypt had made arrangements for an old man to travel."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says.** *It is enough. My son Joseph is still alive. I will go and see him before I die.* The Hebrew for *it is enough* is one word — rav, plenty, abundance. He has just been told he owns Egypt's best, and what he says is enough, and then names the only thing he actually wants."}]}, "Genesis 45:9–15": {"title": "Hurry back to my father", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He has just told them who he is, and the next thing he says is about the old man at home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The urgency"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Now hurry back to my father**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **maharu va'alu** — hurry and go up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice in the paragraph: *hurry*, and then at the end, *bring my father down here quickly*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-two years, and now he cannot wait a day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The message"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is what your son Joseph says: **God has made me lord of all Egypt**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells his father the news through his brothers — the ones who told the same man that a wild beast had eaten him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The invitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Come down to me — don't delay**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You will live in **the region of Goshen and be near me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **veqarovta elai** — and you shall be near to me."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Goshen was the eastern delta, good pasture, and separate enough that shepherds — *an abomination to the Egyptians* — could live undisturbed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is included"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You and your children and grandchildren, your flocks and herds and all you have**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everybody, and everything they own. Nothing is left behind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The provision"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will provide for you there**, for there are still **five years of famine** to come.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vechilkalti otcha** — and I will sustain you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two years down, five to go. He is precise about the arithmetic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So that **you and your household and all who belong to you don't fall into poverty**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **pen-tivvaresh** — lest you be dispossessed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proof"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You can see for yourselves — and my brother Benjamin can see — that it is really me speaking to you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek and Hebrew both make the point about the mouth: *your eyes see, and the eyes of my brother Benjamin, **that it is my mouth that speaks to you***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has been speaking to them through an interpreter for two chapters. Now they hear Hebrew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Benjamin"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Named separately. His only full brother, and the boy Judah had just offered to be enslaved for. See the note on Genesis 44:30–34."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The report"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Tell my father about **all the honor I have in Egypt** and about everything you have seen.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob will not believe it. *And Jacob's heart stood still, because he did not believe them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only when he sees the carts sent to carry him does the spirit of Jacob revive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The embrace"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he **threw his arms around his brother Benjamin and wept**, and Benjamin wept on his shoulder.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **al-tzavvarei Binyamin achiv** — on the neck of Benjamin his brother."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **kissed all his brothers and wept over them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "All of them. Including the ones who put him in the pit and sat down to eat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **after that his brothers talked with him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ve'acharei ken dibberu echav itto**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been *stunned into silence at the sight of him*. See the note on Genesis 45:1–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The talking starts after the weeping, and the narrator notes the order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Pharaoh says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not be concerned about your goods**, for the best of all the land of Egypt is yours.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jacob says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It is enough. Joseph my son is still alive. I will go and see him before I die**.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order.** He embraces Benjamin, kisses all of them, weeps — and only then, the narrator notes, do his brothers start talking."}]}, "Genesis 47:13–26": {"title": "The famine and the land", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four stages of a national bankruptcy, administered by one man, and Moses reports it without comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There was **no food anywhere**, for the famine was terribly severe. **Egypt and Canaan alike wasted away** because of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *wasted away* is *fainted* — the land itself is exhausted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both countries. The one Joseph was sold from and the one he was sold into."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Stage one: silver"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph gathered up **all the silver in Egypt and Canaan** in payment for the grain… and he **brought it into Pharaoh's house**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every coin in two countries, and none of it stays with Joseph. Moses notes where it went."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Stage two: livestock"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Give us food. **Why should we die before your eyes** just because the silver is gone?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Bring your livestock, and I'll give you food in exchange.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Horses, flocks, herds, donkeys. *That year he **carried them through** with food in exchange for all their livestock.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *carried through* is **nahal** — to lead gently, to guide, the word in Psalm 23 for *He leads me beside the still waters*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is used of a man taking a nation's animals off it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Stage three: land and bodies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Nothing is left for our lord but **our bodies and our land**… **Buy us and our land** in exchange for food, and we and our land will serve Pharaoh.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They propose it. Joseph does not demand it — the people come with the offer, twice, in their own words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Joseph **bought all the land of Egypt for Pharaoh**… and the land became Pharaoh's.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Only **the land of the priests he did not buy**, for the priests **received a fixed allowance from Pharaoh** and lived on it, so **they did not have to sell their land**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one group in Egypt that keeps its property is the one already being provided for. Moses states the reason plainly: they had an allowance, so they never had to trade."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Joseph gives back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Here is **seed for you to sow**. When the harvest comes, **give a fifth of it to Pharaoh**. **The other four-fifths you may keep** as seed for the fields and food for yourselves, your households, and your children.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Having taken everything, he hands back seed and sets the rate at twenty percent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four-fifths stays with the farmer. That was a light rate by ancient standards."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they said about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You have saved our lives**,\" they said. \"May we find favor in our lord's eyes; we will be Pharaoh's servants.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They call it salvation. Nobody in the chapter complains."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The law"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Joseph made it **a law over the land of Egypt, still in force to this day**, that **a fifth belongs to Pharaoh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To this day* — Moses is writing centuries later and the arrangement is still running."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The relocation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As for the people, **he moved them into the cities** from one end of Egypt to the other.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole rural population reorganised around the granaries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is happening in the same chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel is settled in Goshen with the best of the land, tax-free, provided for by the brother his sons sold. The contrast is deliberate and Moses puts them in the same chapter. See the note on Genesis 47:27–31."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The direction of the seven years.** The whole transaction runs on grain that was stored during the plenty by a man who believed a dream about the famine. See the note on Genesis 41:53–57."}]}, "Genesis 47:27–31": {"title": "\"Do not bury me in Egypt\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An old man's last request, and it is entirely about a location."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Israel settled in Egypt, in **the region of Goshen**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The best of the land, given by Pharaoh, during a famine that had just cost every Egyptian their fields. See the note on Genesis 47:13–26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened to them there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **took possession of property** there, and they were **fruitful and increased greatly in number**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *took possession* is **achaz** — to grasp, to take hold of. It is the root of the word for an inheritance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Fruitful and increased** is the language of Genesis 1 — *be fruitful and multiply* — and of the promise to Abraham."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus opens by repeating it: *the children of Israel were fruitful and increased abundantly, multiplied and grew exceedingly mighty; and the land was filled with them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blessing works in Egypt, and it is the reason a later Pharaoh becomes afraid of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jacob lived in Egypt **seventeen years**, and the years of his life came to **a hundred and forty-seven**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventeen years. Joseph was seventeen when he was sold."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The boy had seventeen years with his father at the start; the father gets seventeen with him at the end. Moses gives both numbers and lets the reader put them together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If I have found favor in your eyes**, place your hand under my thigh and promise to show me **kindness and faithfulness**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The father asks his son for a favour, in the language of a subject asking a ruler — which is what Joseph now is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kindness and faithfulness** is **chesed ve'emet** — covenant loyalty and truth. The pairing runs right through the Old Testament and turns up in John's prologue: *full of grace and truth*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Place your hand **under my thigh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same gesture Abraham required of his servant when he sent him to find a wife for Isaac. It appears twice in Scripture and both times it concerns the promise and the line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not bury me in Egypt**. When I rest with my fathers, **carry me out of Egypt and bury me where they are buried**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Goshen has been good to him. He has land, grandchildren, a son running the country, and seventeen prosperous years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His last instruction is to be taken out of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where \"where they are buried\" is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cave of Machpelah at Hebron — the one field Abraham bought and paid four hundred shekels of silver for, the only piece of Canaan any of them ever owned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham and Sarah are there, Isaac and Rebekah, and Leah."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Not enough to be promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Swear it to me**,\" Jacob said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph has already said *I will do as you say*, and it is not enough. The old man wants it under oath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Joseph swore to him, and **Israel bowed in worship at the head of his bed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is called **Israel** in that clause, not Jacob."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews takes the moment up: *by faith Jacob, when he was dying… worshiped, leaning on the top of his staff.* See the note on Hebrews 11:17–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Joseph does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters later, at his own death, he gives the same instruction about his own bones — and his coffin waits in Egypt for four hundred years until Moses carries it out on the night of the exodus."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the request says.** He is prospering in Egypt and he calls somewhere else home. Everything he had was in Goshen; the only ground he wanted was a cave in a country he had left."}]}, "Genesis 49:1–2": {"title": "\"What will happen in the days to come\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An old man on a bed calls twelve grown sons around him, and what follows is the longest piece of poetry in Genesis."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The days to come\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will tell you what will happen to you **in the days to come**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **acharit ha-yamim** — the latter days, the end of the days. It is the standard prophetic phrase; Isaiah, Hosea, Micah and Daniel all use it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not handing out farewell advice. He announces prophecy and then delivers twenty-five verses of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Both names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Come together and listen, sons of **Jacob**. Listen to **Israel** your father.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both names in one sentence, and Genesis never uses them carelessly. Jacob is the supplanter, the man who grabbed a heel; Israel is the name he was given at Peniel, after the wrestling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The father speaking is both of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind of blessings these are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the twelve and they are not uniformly pleasant. Reuben loses his birthright in three lines. Simeon and Levi are told their anger is cursed and they will be scattered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not flattering anybody. Whatever is in these verses, he is saying what he sees."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the weight falls"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two of the twelve get far more than the rest — Judah, and Joseph. Judah gets the sceptre and the line: *the sceptre shall not depart from Judah… until Shiloh comes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what is said over the others is short. A single line each, in most cases — Gad, Asher, Naphtali, Issachar, Dan, Zebulun. Two dozen words apiece, and there is a great deal packed into them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this chapter is worth going slowly through"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On the road to Emmaus, *beginning at Moses and all the Prophets, He expounded to them in all the Scriptures the things concerning Himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Beginning at Moses. Genesis is Moses, and this is the last chapter of it but one. The blessings over the twelve sons were in that sermon. See the note on Luke 24:13–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each of these sons became a tribe, and each tribe was given a piece of ground — and Jesus walked that ground, town by town."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the chapter ends.** *He drew his feet up into the bed, breathed his last, and was gathered to his people.* Twenty-five verses of prophecy and then a man pulling his feet up onto a bed. Genesis notices things like that."}]}, "Genesis 49:13": {"title": "Zebulun — a harbour for ships", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One line, about a coastline, and it was said two hundred years before the land was divided."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Zebulun will **live by the seashore**. He will be **a harbour for ships**, and his border will reach toward Sidon.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob is in Egypt, dying, with twelve sons and no territory anywhere. Nobody had surveyed anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two centuries later Joshua divides the land by lot, and Zebulun's portion runs up into the north country toward the coast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Zebulun** means dwelling. Leah named him: *now my husband will dwell with me.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He lived"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He left Nazareth and went to live in **Capernaum**, a town beside the sea in **the territory of Zebulun and Naphtali**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew says it deliberately, and then quotes Isaiah over it:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Land of Zebulun and land of Naphtali… **the people sitting in darkness have seen a great light**, and on those living in the land where death casts its shadow, light has dawned.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nazareth is in Zebulun. Capernaum is on the border of Zebulun and Naphtali. He grew up in one and made His home in the other, and both are in the tribe whose name means dwelling. See the note on Matthew 4:12–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The harbour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zebulun is *a harbour for ships*, and the lake it sits on is where the boats were."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon's boat, pushed out a little from the land, used as a pulpit and then filled until it settled low in the water. The storm crossing. The walking on the sea. Levi's tax booth at the harbour of Capernaum."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And after the resurrection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Simon Peter said, \"I am going fishing\"… and all that night they caught nothing at all.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Back on the same water, in the same trade, after everything. And He is on the beach at daybreak with a charcoal fire, bread and fish already on it, asking whether they have any food."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He fills their net, feeds them breakfast, and then restores Peter three times over a charcoal fire — the only other charcoal fire in John's Gospel is the one Peter warmed himself at while he denied Him. See the note on John 21:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Livelihood, a meal, and the thing Peter could not say. All three, in the tribe called dwelling."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Moses added over Zebulun.** *Rejoice, Zebulun, in your going out… they shall call the peoples to the mountain; there they shall offer sacrifices of righteousness, for they shall partake of the abundance of the seas.* A tribe on the trade routes, calling the nations in."}]}, "Genesis 49:14–15": {"title": "Issachar — the donkey between the burdens", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The saddest of the twelve, and it describes a man who could have had rest and chose a load instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Issachar is a **strong donkey**, resting among the sheepfolds. When he sees how good his resting place is and how pleasant the land, **he will bend his shoulder to the load and submit to hard labour**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the logic of it. He sees the rest is good. He sees the land is pleasant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And that is the reason he takes up the burden — the Hebrew is that he becomes a servant to forced labour, a slave gang."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had the rest in front of him and put his shoulder under a load."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Issachar** means *there is hire*, or *he brings wages*. Leah named him that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man whose name means wages, ending up in forced labour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ground"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Issachar's portion was the Jezreel valley — some of the best farmland in the whole country, which is what makes the *pleasant land* line so exact."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in that valley sits a town called **Nain**, which means pleasant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened at that gate"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As He came near the town gate, a dead man was being carried out — the only son his mother had, and she was a widow.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A funeral procession, in the tribe whose blessing is a donkey between two burdens. Two dead men in one town — the son on the frame, and the mother's whole future with him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He touched the frame, the bearers stood still, and He said *young man, I say to you, get up.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He did not ask for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Jesus **gave him back to his mother**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is requested. No follow, no serve, no condition. He raises the boy and hands him over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Issachar submitted to hard labour to keep a pleasant land. In the pleasant town in Issachar's own valley, a man is given his life back and is asked for nothing at all. See the note on Luke 7:11–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two burdens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The older rendering has him *couching down between two burdens* — the two packs a donkey carries, one on each side."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the Hebrew word is exactly, the picture is a strong animal lying down under weight it did not have to carry."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the tribe was later known for.** *The sons of Issachar, who had understanding of the times, to know what Israel ought to do.* Two hundred chiefs of them came to David at Hebron. The tribe that bowed its shoulder to a load became the tribe that could read a season."}]}, "Genesis 49:19": {"title": "Gad — the troop and the last word", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Eleven words in English, and in Hebrew it is a piece of wordplay Jacob almost certainly meant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name inside the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Gad** means a troop, a raiding band. And the Hebrew line runs *gad gedud yegudennu, ve-hu yagud aqeb* — the same consonants hammered five times over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A troop shall troop upon him, but he shall troop upon their heel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Gad will be **attacked by raiders**, but he will **turn and strike at their heels**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The trouble is not denied. He gets raided, and the raiders are described as a troop — more than he can meet head-on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the last clause is the last event. Whoever hits first, Gad has the final word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ground Gad was given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "East of the Jordan, on the frontier — the most exposed territory any tribe held, open to Ammon, Moab and every raiding party out of the desert."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob's line was not decorative. It described the border they would live on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Jesus walked into it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Across that lake, on the eastern shore, He met a man who could not be bound with chains, living among the tombs, cutting himself with stones."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What is your name?\" \"**Legion**,\" he answered, because a great many demons had gone into him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Roman legion was some thousands of men. It is the largest concentration of the enemy named anywhere in the Gospels, and it is standing on Gad's side of the water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He speaks, and it comes out. One word against a troop, and the man is found sitting, dressed and in his right mind. See the note on Luke 8:30–33."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will strike at their heels.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heel is where Genesis started. Chapter 3: *He shall bruise your head, and you shall bruise His heel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob's fourteenth son-blessing uses the word from the promise made in the garden, and the man who grabbed his brother's heel at birth is the one saying it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The shortest blessings carry the most.** Gad, Asher and Naphtali get one line each. That is the whole inheritance in words — a troop turned back, food fit for a king, and a doe set free — and every one of them turns up as ground Jesus stood on."}]}, "Genesis 49:20": {"title": "Asher — bread fit for a king", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One line, and it is about food."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Asher's food will be **rich**; he will provide **delicacies fit for a king**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *rich* is **shamen** — fat, the choicest part. And the second phrase is *dainties of a king*, the food set on a royal table."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Asher** means happy, blessed. Leah named him: *happy am I, for the daughters will call me blessed.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ground"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Asher's portion was the coastal strip in the far north-west, running up toward Tyre and Sidon — the richest agricultural land in Israel, olive country."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses says it in Deuteronomy 33: *let Asher dip his foot in oil.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Jesus went into it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus went out from there and departed to the region of **Tyre and Sidon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is Asher's border, and it is the only recorded time He left Israel's territory altogether."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There a Canaanite woman came after Him about her daughter, and what she got from Him was a sentence about bread:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is not right to take **the children's bread** and toss it to the little dogs.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The children's bread"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls it bread, and He calls it the children's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not argue with the arrangement. She takes the picture and finishes it: *\"Yes, Lord — and even the little dogs eat **the crumbs** that fall from their master's table.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A crumb off that table broke a demonic hold on a child in another house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Asher's line said *rich food, delicacies fit for a king*, and the meeting that happens on Asher's ground is a conversation about whose table the bread comes from. See the note on Matthew 15:21–28."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she got"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Woman, your faith is great. Let it be done for you just as you desire.\"* And her daughter was healed from that hour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She asked for a crumb and it was enough."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Moses added over Asher.** *As your days, so shall your strength be.* Set beside Jacob's line about rich food, the two blessings on this tribe are about being fed and being strong for the length of the days you get. See the note on Deuteronomy 33:24–25."}]}, "Genesis 49:21": {"title": "Naphtali — a doe set free", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The gentlest line in the chapter, and it is about something being let go."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Naphtali is **a doe set free**, that bears **beautiful fawns**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a hind loosed, released. Something that was tied is not tied any more, and what comes out of it is beautiful."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second half can be read as beautiful fawns or as beautiful words — the Hebrew carries both, and older translations render it *he gives goodly words*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Naphtali** means my wrestling. Rachel named him after the struggle with her sister."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ground"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Naphtali's territory was the north-west shore of the Sea of Galilee — Capernaum, Bethsaida, Chorazin, the Mount of Beatitudes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is where Jesus based Himself. *Leaving Nazareth, He came and dwelt in Capernaum, which is by the sea, in the regions of Zebulun and **Naphtali**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew quotes Isaiah over it: *the land of Zebulun and the land of Naphtali… the people who sat in darkness have seen a great light.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Most of the miracles happened on that shoreline, and the Sermon on the Mount was preached on that hillside. Beautiful words, on Naphtali's ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The loosing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In a synagogue in that country there was a woman bent double for eighteen years, who could not straighten up at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Woman, you are **loosed** from your infirmity.\"* And He laid His hands on her, and immediately she was made straight."}, {"k": "p", "t": "When the ruler of the synagogue objected about the Sabbath, He used the word again — *ought not this woman, being a daughter of Abraham, whom Satan has bound for eighteen years, be **loosed** from this bond on the Sabbath?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Loosed, twice, over a woman who had been tied for eighteen years, in the tribe whose blessing is a doe set free. See the note on Luke 13:10–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who did the binding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it plainly in that synagogue: *whom Satan has bound.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a discipline, not a lesson. A binding, and He unties it in a sentence."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Moses said over Naphtali.** *O Naphtali, satisfied with favour, and full of the blessing of the LORD.* The word is *ratson* — good pleasure, acceptance. Satisfied with favour, on the shore where He did most of His work."}]}, "Genesis 49:22–26": {"title": "Joseph — branches over the wall", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The longest of the twelve blessings, and Jacob spends most of it on titles for God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Joseph is a fruitful vine, a fruitful vine **beside a spring**, whose branches **climb over a wall**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things: fruitful, next to water, and not staying inside the boundary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *climb over* is to march over, to stride across. The wall does not stop it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The archers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"With bitterness archers attacked him; they shot at him and pressed him hard. **But his bow stayed steady**, and his arms were made strong **by the hands of the Mighty One of Jacob**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything true of Joseph's life is in that line — the pit, the slave sale, Potiphar's wife, the prison, the forgotten cupbearer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the strength is credited: *by the hands of the Mighty One.* His arms were made strong by somebody else's hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The names for God, stacked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four in two lines, and Jacob is running out of ways to say it:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the Mighty One of Jacob**", "**the Shepherd** — the first time in Scripture God is called this", "**the Rock of Israel**", "**the Almighty** who blesses you"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old man who wrestled at Peniel calls Him a Shepherd, and it is the first occurrence of the word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Blessings of the skies above, blessings of the deep that lies below, blessings of the breast and womb… **greater than the blessings of the ancient mountains**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Above, below, and the family line. And then a comparison — greater than the mountains, which is Hebrew for greater than the oldest and most permanent things anyone can point at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the branches went over the wall"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph's portion was Samaria. And Jacob's well was on that ground — *near the piece of ground Jacob had given to his son Joseph.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A fruitful vine beside a spring.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There was a wall between Jew and Samaritan, and it was absolute: *Jews have no dealings with Samaritans.* His disciples marvelled that He was talking with her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He went through Samaria, sat down at that well worn out from the journey, and asked a woman for a drink. See the note on John 4:1–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The branches ran over the wall on Joseph's own land."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *Let all these rest on the head of Joseph, on the brow of **the prince among his brothers**.* The Hebrew is the one separated from his brothers — the same word used of a Nazirite, one set apart. It is exactly what they did to him, turned into a title."}]}, "Genesis 49:27": {"title": "Benjamin — the morning and the evening", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last of the twelve, and it is one line about a wolf and two halves of a day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Benjamin is a **ravenous wolf**; **in the morning** he devours the prey, and **in the evening** he divides the spoil.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same animal, two different activities, split by the hours of one day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Morning: tearing. Evening: distributing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rachel died bearing him and called him **Ben-Oni**, son of my sorrow. His father renamed him **Benjamin**, son of the right hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only son of Jacob born in the land, and the only one whose name his father overruled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The most famous Benjamite"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Circumcised the eighth day, of the stock of Israel, **of the tribe of Benjamin**, a Hebrew of Hebrews.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says it about himself twice — in Philippians and in Romans."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Saul was consenting to his death… **he made havoc of the church**, entering every house, and dragging off men and women, committing them to prison.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *made havoc* is the word used of a wild animal mangling a body."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *breathing threats and murder against the disciples of the Lord*, going to Damascus with letters, to bring back any he found *bound to Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the wolf in the morning, and Luke's vocabulary for it is not accidental."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The evening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same man, later, writing from a cell: *I have learned, in whatever state I am, to be content.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fourteen letters distributed to churches across the empire. Collections carried from Macedonia and Achaia to the poor saints at Jerusalem. Timothy, Titus, Luke, Onesimus, Philemon, Priscilla and Aquila."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the evening he divides the spoil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what he divides is what he took: *what things were gain to me, these I have counted loss for Christ.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Standing before Agrippa"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And now I stand here on trial for **the hope of the promise God made to our fathers**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who dragged people to prison is in chains himself, on trial, and calls it standing for a hope. He asks the king a question rather than mounting a defence: *why should it be judged something incredible by any of you that God raises the dead?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the end of that hearing he has told Agrippa he wishes every man in the room were *as I am, except for these chains.* See the note on Acts 26:4–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it took"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One morning on the Damascus road, and a light above the brightness of the sun."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in Jacob's line says the wolf became a different animal. It says what the same animal did with the second half of the day."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other famous Benjamite.** King Saul — the first king of Israel, from this tribe, who hunted David and died on Gilboa. And then Saul of Tarsus, from the same tribe, who hunted the Church and was renamed. Two men called Saul, one tribe, and only one of them let go of the sword."}]}, "Genesis 49:3–4": {"title": "Reuben — unstable as water", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The firstborn gets the longest list of titles in the chapter and loses everything in the next line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Reuben, you are **my firstborn, my might, the first sign of my strength** — first in rank and first in power.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four titles stacked, and every one of them is genuine. The double portion, the headship of the family, the priesthood of the household — all of it was his by right of birth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob lists it in full before taking it away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Yet** you are as unruly as the churning waters, and you will no longer hold first place.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The older rendering is *unstable as water*, and it is the phrase the tribe is remembered by."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Water takes whatever shape the container gives it. It has no form of its own and no way of holding a line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For you **climbed into your father's bed** and defiled it — you went up onto my couch.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 35 records it in one sentence and moves on: *Reuben went and lay with Bilhah his father's concubine, and Israel heard about it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is all. No confrontation, no punishment, no conversation — *Israel heard about it*, and then thirty-five chapters of silence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The silence ends here, on a deathbed, in front of all eleven brothers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the flesh at its best produces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Reuben is not a villain. He is the one who tried to save Joseph from the pit — *let us not kill him… throw him into this pit*, intending to come back for him. He is the one who offered his own two sons as security for Benjamin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He means well every time, and nothing holds. Good instincts, no strength to carry them through — which is the definition of the word Jacob uses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What became of the tribe"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No judge came out of Reuben. No prophet, no king, no leader of any note."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deborah's song asks about them: *among the divisions of Reuben there were great searchings of heart… why did you sit among the sheepfolds?* They debated it at length and did not come."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Reuben first, and he loses the birthright. Simeon and Levi next, and their anger is cursed. Then Judah, and the sceptre."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three sons pass before the blessing lands anywhere, and the first three are what the natural man is: unstable, and then violent."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the birthright went.** *The birthright was given to the sons of Joseph* — 1 Chronicles 5 says so outright, and explains why: *because he defiled his father's bed.* The double portion went to Joseph, the kingship went to Judah, and the priesthood went to Levi. What Reuben held alone was split three ways and given to other men."}]}, "Genesis 49:5–7": {"title": "Simeon and Levi — the anger, not the men", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only curse in the chapter, and it is aimed carefully."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 34. Their sister Dinah was violated by Shechem, and the two brothers proposed a treaty: intermarriage, on condition that every male in the town be circumcised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it came to pass on the third day, when they were sore, that two of the sons of Jacob took each man his sword and came upon the city boldly, and slew all the males.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They waited until the men could not defend themselves, and killed the town."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In their fury they crippled oxen\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In their anger they killed men, and in their fury they **crippled oxen**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to hamstring — cut the tendon so the animal can never work again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the detail Jacob picks out. Not just the killing. Destruction beyond any purpose, on animals that had done nothing, because the rage had to go somewhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is cursed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Cursed be their anger, so fierce, and their fury, so cruel.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read it exactly. Not *cursed be Simeon and Levi*. Their anger is cursed, and their fury."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob separates the men from the thing in them. It is the only curse he pronounces in the whole chapter and it is laid on a temper rather than a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will **scatter** them throughout Jacob and **disperse** them across Israel.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had used their unity to do it — two brothers acting together. So they are separated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simeon's inheritance ended up as scattered towns inside Judah's territory, and the tribe faded; it is missing from the blessing of Moses in Deuteronomy 33 altogether."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened to the other one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Levi was scattered too — forty-eight cities among the tribes, with no land of their own."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the scattering became their office. *The LORD is their inheritance*, and the tribe with no territory is the tribe that carried the tabernacle, taught the law and served at the altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Levi at Sinai was the tribe that rallied to Moses when the calf was made. The sword that had been drawn on Shechem was put at God's disposal, and a curse ended up a priesthood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three at the start"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Reuben, unstable. Simeon and Levi, violent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first three sons in the chapter, and the whole natural man is in them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Judah."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Jacob says he will not do.** *\"Let me not enter their council; let me not join their gathering.\"* He will not be associated with the decision. He had said nothing at the time except that they had made him stink among the inhabitants of the land — and he says the rest of it here, twenty chapters later, dying."}]}, "Genesis 49:8–12": {"title": "Judah — the sceptre and the vine", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The fourth son, and the chapter changes register completely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Judah, your brothers will **praise** you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yehudah** means praise. Leah named him: *now I will praise the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first three sons produced instability and violence. The fourth is called praise and gets the throne."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What praise does in that tribe's history"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jehoshaphat, king of Judah, faced three armies with no plan, and was told: *you will not even need to fight this battle. Take your positions, stand still, and watch the salvation of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He appointed singers to go out **in front of the army**, and *when they began to sing and to praise, the LORD set ambushes* — and it was over before Judah reached the battlefield. See the note on 2 Chronicles 20:16–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Judah is a **young lion**… like a lion he crouches and lies down — who would dare rouse him?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three stages of one animal in two lines: the young lion that has come up from the prey, the mature lion, and the lioness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation names Him from this verse: *the Lion of the tribe of Judah has prevailed.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sceptre"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The sceptre will not depart from Judah, nor the ruler's staff from between his feet, **until he to whom it belongs shall come**, and the obedience of the peoples will be his.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of *he to whom it belongs* is **Shiloh** — a word that occurs nowhere else, and the oldest Jewish readings take it as a name of Messiah. Its root carries the sense of rest, tranquility."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the obedience of the peoples* — not Israel. The peoples, plural, the nations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The donkey and the vine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He will **tie his donkey to the vine**, his colt to the choicest branch.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody ties livestock to a vine. A donkey would strip it in an hour, and a vine is the most carefully tended plant a man owns."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a picture of abundance so complete that the loss does not matter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a colt, tied, waiting for a king — *tell the daughter of Zion, behold, your King is coming to you, lowly, and sitting on a donkey, a colt, the foal of a donkey.* They found it *tied* at a doorway, and He rode it into Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He will **wash his garments in wine**, his robes in **the blood of grapes**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not spilled on. Washed in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 63 takes the same image the other way: *why is Your apparel red, like one who treads in the winepress? I have trodden the winepress alone.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vine, again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am the vine; you are the branches… **apart from Me you can do nothing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it in an upper room, on the night of the cup, and the branch's whole job in that passage is to stay attached. The fruit is not the branch's work. See the note on John 15:5–8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How long the sceptre stayed.** Judah held the right to try capital cases until Rome took it away, and the old accounts record the rabbis lamenting that the sceptre had departed and Shiloh had not come. He was in Nazareth at the time."}]}, "Genesis 5:1–5": {"title": "\"And then he died\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first genealogy in Scripture, and it is built around a refrain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is **the written record** of Adam's descendants.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **sepher toledoth** — the book of the generations. Genesis is structured around this formula; it appears eleven times and divides the book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The restatement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the day God created humanity, **He made them in the likeness of God**. **Male and female** He created them, and **He blessed them** and called them \"Mankind.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses goes back to chapter 1 and repeats it before starting the list."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between then and now: a garden, a command, a serpent, an expulsion, and a murder. He restates the original terms anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **called them \"Mankind\"** on the day they were made.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **adam** — the word for humanity and the man's name. God gives the same name to both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The change of preposition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He had a son **in his own likeness, in his own image**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 1 has Adam made in the likeness of **God**. Verse 3 has Seth in the likeness of **Adam**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two words — **demuth** and **tselem** — used twice in three verses, with a different origin each time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something is now being transmitted down a line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Seth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 4 explains the name: *God has appointed another seed for me instead of Abel, whom Cain killed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The line does not run through the firstborn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The numbers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Adam lived another **800 years**… Altogether Adam lived **930 years**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And then **he died**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It comes at the end of every entry in the chapter. Seth died. Enosh died. Cainan died. Mahalalel, Jared, Methuselah, Lamech — *and he died*, eight times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the drumbeat of the chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Enoch walked with God; and he was not, for God took him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only man in the list the refrain is not said of. See the note on Hebrews 11:4–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was said in the garden"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In the day that you eat of it **you shall surely die**.\"* See the note on Genesis 2:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *dying you shall die* — the doubled construction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not fall down that afternoon. He lived nine hundred and thirty years and the process ran the whole way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul takes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Through one man sin entered the world, and death through sin, and **thus death spread to all men**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For as in Adam all die, even so in Christ all shall be made alive.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The genealogy is the demonstration. Eight entries, eight identical endings."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other genealogy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke 3 runs the same list backwards from Jesus, through Seth, and ends *the son of Adam, the son of God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew starts at Abraham and comes down. Luke goes all the way back to this chapter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter is doing.** Repeating one sentence. Long lives, sons and daughters, and then three words at the end of each entry — and one man in the list who does not get them."}]}, "Genesis 5:21–24": {"title": "The man who is missing from the list", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 5 is a genealogy with a refrain. Every entry ends the same way, and one does not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He lived so many years, he had sons and daughters, and then he died.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight times in the chapter, like a drumbeat. Even Methuselah, who outlives everyone, ends with those two words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Enoch: *Enoch walked with God, and then he was no more, because God took him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence everybody else gets is missing from his."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Walked with God\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice in four verses. The Hebrew form suggests a continuing, habitual walking — not a journey with a destination, but a way of going about."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only one other person in Genesis is described this way: Noah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the detail worth noticing is when it started. *When Enoch was 65, he fathered Methuselah. And Enoch walked with God — closely, faithfully — for 300 years after that.* The walking begins when the child arrives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shortest life in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "365 years, in a list where the others run to eight and nine hundred. By the standards of the genealogy he barely got started."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter measures everyone in years. Enoch is the one it does not need to."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Hebrews adds.** *By faith Enoch was taken from this life, so that he did not experience death… for before he was taken he was commended as one who pleased God.* The commendation came first, and the taking followed. Whatever the walking was, God enjoyed it enough to end it by simply keeping him."}]}, "Genesis 50:15–21": {"title": "\"You meant it for evil\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Seventeen years after the reconciliation, the brothers are still not sure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What triggers it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Joseph's brothers saw that **their father was dead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob has just been buried at Machpelah, with a state procession of Egyptian officials and chariots."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They waited until the funeral was over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fear"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What if Joseph still holds a grudge against us** and pays us back for all the wrong we did him?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *what if Joseph hates us*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had wept on their necks in chapter 45, told them not to be grieved or angry with themselves, kissed them, and settled them in Goshen with the best of the land. He had fed them through five years of famine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventeen years of provision, and they think it was for their father's sake."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The message"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they **sent word** to Joseph.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do not come. They send."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Before he died, **your father gave this command**: 'Say to Joseph: Please forgive your brothers' offense.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis never records Jacob saying it. Whether he did or not, they put the request in a dead man's mouth rather than their own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they call themselves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now please forgive the offense of **the servants of the God of your father**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They reach for the strongest connection they have and it is still second-hand — *your* father's God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When their message came to him, **Joseph wept**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew says nothing more. It is the seventh time Genesis records him weeping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He weeps because after seventeen years they sent a message."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prostration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then his brothers came and **threw themselves down before him**. \"**We are your slaves**,\" they said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The dream from chapter 37 — the sheaves bowing — completes itself here, and by then nobody in the room wants it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Don't be afraid. Am I in the place of God?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *am I in God's stead?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He declines the position. Punishing them would require an office he says he does not hold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You meant it for evil against me, but God meant it for good**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew uses the same verb twice — **chashab**, to weave, to devise, to reckon. Both parties intended, and the intentions ran in opposite directions over the same event."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not soften what they did. *You meant it for evil* stays in the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the good was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**To bring about what is happening today — the saving of many lives**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not his promotion. The survival of a region through seven years of famine, and of the family the covenant ran through. See the note on Genesis 41:53–57."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So don't be afraid. **I will provide for you and your little ones**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *provide* is **kul** — to sustain, to nourish. The same word he had used in chapter 45."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he **comforted them and spoke tenderly to them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *spoke to their heart*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul says the same thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And we know that **all things work together for good** to those who love God.* See the note on Romans 8:28–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Jacob's version from the other side, when he could see none of it: *all these things are against me.* See the note on Genesis 42:29–38."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says twice.** *Don't be afraid* — at the start of his answer and again at the end. The men in front of him had been forgiven for seventeen years and had spent them waiting for the bill."}]}, "Genesis 50:22–26": {"title": "Carry my bones up", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last paragraph of Genesis, and it ends with a box."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The span"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph stayed in Egypt, along with his father's whole family, and he **lived a hundred and ten years**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundred and ten was the ideal lifespan in Egyptian literature — the number that turns up in Egyptian texts as the mark of a life fully blessed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis gives him the Egyptian ideal and then has him ask to be buried elsewhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He saw **the third generation of Ephraim's children**, and the children of **Makir son of Manasseh** were **placed on his knees** as his own.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Great-grandchildren on the knees of the man his brothers threw in a pit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The knees gesture is formal adoption — the same one Jacob performed over Ephraim and Manasseh in the chapter before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The announcement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am about to die, but **God will surely come to your aid** and bring you up out of this land.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **paqod yiphqod** — visiting He will visit. The verb doubled for certainty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice, in two consecutive sentences."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The destination"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To the land He promised on oath to **Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three names. The promise is quoted back, generations later, by a man who is prime minister of somewhere else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Joseph **made the sons of Israel swear an oath**: \"God will surely come to your aid, and then you must **carry my bones up from this place**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *bury me there*. His father Jacob had asked for that and got it — a full state funeral, an Egyptian procession to Machpelah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph asks for something different. He asks to wait."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the bones"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because leaving them there is the guarantee. A coffin in Egypt with a standing instruction on it is a promise nobody can quietly forget."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred years of slavery, and there is a box somewhere with an unfinished errand attached to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse of Genesis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Joseph died at the age of a hundred and ten. They **embalmed him, and he was placed in a coffin in Egypt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word is **aron** — the same word as the ark of the covenant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book that begins *in the beginning God created* ends with a coffin in a foreign country. See the note on Genesis 1:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who kept the promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **Moses took the bones of Joseph with him**, for he had placed the children of Israel under solemn oath, saying, \"God will surely visit you, and you shall carry up my bones from here with you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus 13, in the middle of the night of the departure, with six hundred thousand men on the move."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody remembered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they ended up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The bones of Joseph, which the children of Israel had brought up out of Egypt, they buried at Shechem, in the plot of ground which Jacob had bought.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua 24, the last chapter of that book. Four hundred years in transit, and then a field Jacob had paid for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Hebrews names it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By faith **Joseph, when he was dying, made mention of the departure of the children of Israel, and gave instructions concerning his bones**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of everything in Joseph's life — the dreams, the pit, Potiphar's house, the prison, the famine, the reunion — this is the one act Hebrews lists as faith."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last thing he did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had already said to his brothers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You meant evil against me; but **God meant it for good**, in order to bring it about as it is this day, to save many people alive.\"* See the note on Genesis 50:15–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six verses before the bones."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he asked for.** Not a burial. A box, left in Egypt, with an instruction on it — so that four hundred years later somebody would still be carrying an unfinished promise."}]}, "Genesis 6:1–4": {"title": "The hundred and twenty years, and what they probably count", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four of the strangest verses in the Bible, and the honest thing to say about them first is that nobody is certain what they describe."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Their days will be a hundred and twenty years\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two readings have been argued for a very long time, and both are serious:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**a new limit on human lifespan** — from now on, people live at most 120 years", "**a countdown to the flood** — 120 years of reprieve before the waters come"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second fits the immediate context better. The sentence begins *My Spirit will not contend with humanity forever*, which sounds like a clock running out on a situation rather than a change to biology. And the flood is the next thing that happens in the narrative."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first reading has a real problem: several people after this live far longer — Noah, Shem, Abraham at 175. If it were a hard limit, it did not take."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sons of God"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sons of God saw that these daughters were beautiful, and they took wives from among them, whomever they chose.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three explanations have long competed: angelic beings, the line of Seth intermarrying with the line of Cain, or dynastic rulers taking women by right. The phrase *sons of God* elsewhere in the Old Testament usually means heavenly beings, which is why the first reading is the oldest — but nothing here settles it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the passage is doing.** Whatever the details, the direction is clear: the phrase *took… whomever they chose* is the language of power used without restraint, and it sits immediately before God looking at the earth and seeing that every inclination of the human heart was bent. Genesis is describing a world coming apart, and it does not stop to explain the mechanics."}]}, "Genesis 6:13–22": {"title": "Build an ark", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A set of building instructions, and almost every detail in them is doing two jobs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pitch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Coat it with **pitch, inside and out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kaphar** — and this is the only place in Scripture it is used of tar. Everywhere else it is the word for **atonement**: to cover, to make propitiation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the root of **kippur**, as in Yom Kippur, the Day of Atonement. The noun **kopher** is the ransom price — the word Elihu uses in Job 33: *I have found a ransom.* See the note on Job 33:23–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing keeping the water out is called covering, inside and out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measurements"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*300 cubits long, 50 wide, and 30 high.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roughly 450 feet by 75 by 45 — a displacement of some 43,000 tons, and proportions close to what modern naval architecture uses for stability in heavy seas. It is not built to sail anywhere. It is built to stay upright and float."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lower, middle, and upper decks.* Three levels."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The door"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Set **a door in its side**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One. Not one per deck, not one at each end. A single door, and it is in the side."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am the door,\"* Jesus said. *\"If anyone enters by Me, he will be saved.\"* See the note on John 10:7–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what was opened in His side was opened with a spear, after He was dead — *immediately blood and water came out.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The window"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Put a roof on it, **finished to a cubit from the top**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An opening at the very top, running round under the roof line. The only light comes from above, and there is no way to see the water from inside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whoever was in that boat could look up and could not look out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"But with you I will establish my covenant\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first occurrence of **covenant** in the Bible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It comes before the flood, not after — while the ark is still timber and the sky is still dry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who comes in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You and your sons, your wife, and your sons' wives.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight people, on the strength of one man's standing with God. Nothing is recorded about the faith of the other seven."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the animals *will come to you* — Noah is not told to go and catch them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Noah did everything just as God commanded him — **every last thing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew simply repeats it: *thus did Noah; according to all that God commanded him, so he did.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundred and twenty years of building, on a warning about weather that had never happened."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in the instructions.** A rudder, a sail, an oar, or a destination. The ark has no means of steering and nowhere to go. It was built to be shut into and carried."}]}, "Genesis 6:5–8": {"title": "Noah found grace", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first time the word **grace** appears in the Bible, and it lands in the darkest paragraph before it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every inclination of the human heart was **only evil, all the time**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three absolutes stacked: every, only, all the time. The Hebrew leaves no room — *every imagination of the thoughts of his heart was only evil continually.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for *inclination* is **yetzer**, from the verb for a potter forming clay. It is the shaping impulse, what the heart is bent toward before any act."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it did to God"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it **grieved the LORD deeply** that He had made humankind; **His heart was filled with pain**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **atsab** — to hurt, to wound, to cause sorrow. It is the same root used of the pain of childbirth in chapter 3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God is described as being in pain. Genesis does not soften it, and this is four chapters after *very good*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will wipe from the earth the human race I have created.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to blot out, to erase — the word for wiping a dish."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Noah — **Noah found grace in the eyes of the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chen**. This is its first occurrence in Scripture, and it arrives at the bottom of the worst page in Genesis."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Found.** Not earned, achieved or deserved. The verb is the one for coming across something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the verses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 8: *Noah found grace.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 9: *Noah was a just man, perfect in his generations, and Noah walked with God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grace comes first, and the character is described afterwards. The book does not say Noah was righteous and therefore found favour; it says he found favour, and then tells you what he was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What his name means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Noah** — rest, comfort. Lamech named him: *this one will comfort us concerning our work and the toil of our hands, because of the ground which the LORD has cursed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man whose name is rest is the man who found grace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Perfect in his generations\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tamim** — whole, sound, without blemish. It is the technical word Leviticus uses of an animal fit for sacrifice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole passage is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in it is God's assessment and God's decision — He saw, He grieved, He said, He will wipe."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then one man is exempted, and the reason given is not his record."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Noah had to build with.** No rain had ever fallen, according to the account in chapter 2, and he was told to build a boat inland for a flood. Hebrews puts it plainly: *by faith Noah, being divinely warned of things **not yet seen**, moved with godly fear, prepared an ark.*"}]}, "Genesis 6:9–12": {"title": "\"Noah walked with God\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three things are said about Noah, and then three about the world he lived in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is said about him"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**righteous** — right, in the sense of standing straight with God", "**blameless among the people of his time** — the word is used of sacrificial animals without defect", "**he walked with God**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third phrase is used of only one other man in Genesis: Enoch, four chapters earlier. Both are described the same way, and both stand out from a genealogy of people who simply lived and died."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word \"blameless\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not mean sinless. Genesis will show Noah drunk and shamed within a chapter of leaving the ark, and it does not edit that out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blameless among the people of his time* is a comparison. In a world the next verses call corrupt and violent, he was the one who was not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is said about the world"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The earth was corrupt in God's sight and full of violence. God looked at the earth and saw how ruined it was.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word for *corrupt* and the word for *ruined* are the same root — and it is the same root used a few verses later when God says He will destroy them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The judgement matches the diagnosis exactly. They had ruined it; it would be ruined. God is described as confirming a state of affairs rather than inventing a penalty for it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What kept Noah afloat.** Hebrews says he built the ark *by faith*, moved with godly fear, having been warned about things not yet seen. And Genesis, before any of the building starts, says the thing the whole account rests on: *Noah found favour in the eyes of the LORD.* The favour comes first, in the verse before this paragraph."}]}, "Genesis 7:1–5": {"title": "\"You and all your household\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and the two halves of it do not obviously match."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mismatch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Go into the ark, **you and all your household**, for I have seen that **you alone** are righteous in this generation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given is about one man. The invitation covers eight people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is said about Noah's wife, his sons, or his sons' wives — not a word about their character in either direction. They go in on the strength of the verdict passed on him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern this belongs to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It happens repeatedly. A household is spared for the sake of one man's standing: Noah's family here, Rahab's family in Jericho, Lot's family in Sodom, the Israelite houses at Passover reckoned by household rather than by person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Bible keeps counting people in units of *house*, and it keeps doing it at the moments of rescue."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Come into the ark\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb is *come*, not *go*. It is the word you use to invite someone toward where you already are — which means God is not sending Noah into the boat from outside. He is calling him in from somewhere He already is."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *And Noah did everything the LORD commanded him.* It is the fourth time Genesis says it in two chapters. The obedience is recorded flatly, without commentary, and then the rain starts seven days later — which is how long Noah had to sit in a finished boat under a clear sky."}]}, "Genesis 7:11–16": {"title": "\"Then the LORD shut him in\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last line of this passage is five words long in Hebrew and it is the point of the whole chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The date"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the six hundredth year of Noah's life, on **the seventeenth day of the second month**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis dates it to the day. Whatever else this account is, it is written as a record rather than a legend."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the water came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **great deep burst open** and the **floodgates of the heavens were flung wide**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both directions at once — up from below and down from above. The Hebrew for the first is that the fountains of the great deep were split open."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rain is mentioned second, and it is the lesser half."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who went in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Noah, his sons Shem, Ham, and Japheth, his wife, and his three sons' wives.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight. And every kind of creature *coming to Noah two by two* — the animals come to him; he does not go after them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the LORD shut him in** — sealed the door with His own hand, safe inside.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *and the LORD shut behind him*. Noah did not close the door."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He built the ark, gathered the food, loaded the animals, and walked in. The last act — the one that made it watertight — was done from outside by somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which name is used"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text switches. Through the building instructions it is **Elohim**, God — the name of power and creation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here it is **YHWH**, the LORD, the covenant name given at the burning bush. The shutting of the door is done under the personal name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What a shut door means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody inside could open it, and nobody outside could either."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The security of everyone in that boat had nothing to do with how tightly they were holding on. The door was sealed by the One who told them to come in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus uses the same picture: *neither shall anyone snatch them out of My hand.* See the note on John 10:27–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the word for the boat is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tebah**. It appears in only one other place in Scripture — the basket of papyrus, sealed with tar and pitch, that Moses' mother laid among the reeds. See the note on Exodus 2:1–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two vessels, both waterproofed, both carrying a life through water that killed everything else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the ark had no way of doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no rudder in the specification, no sail, no oar. Once the door was shut, nobody aboard could influence where it went."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For a hundred and fifty days they were carried."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word Jesus used about that week.** *They knew nothing about it **until the flood came** and swept them all away.* Matthew 24 — eating, drinking, marrying, right up to the day Noah entered the ark. The judgement is not what He describes as the surprise. The ordinariness is."}]}, "Genesis 8:1–5": {"title": "\"God remembered Noah\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The turning point of the flood account is a verb about memory."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"God remembered\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **God remembered Noah**, and all the wild animals and livestock with him in the ark.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **zakar**. It does not mean God had forgotten — it is the word for acting on a commitment, turning attention toward someone in order to do something about them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is used of God remembering His covenant with Abraham when Israel groaned in Egypt, and of Him remembering Hannah, and Rachel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every time it appears, something moves immediately afterwards."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What moved"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And God sent **a wind** over the earth, and the waters began to subside.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for wind is **ruach** — also breath, also Spirit. The same word as Genesis 1:2, where *the Spirit of God hovered over the face of the waters.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A world under water, and a wind moving over it. Genesis is deliberately repeating its own opening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The closing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The springs of the deep and the floodgates of the heavens **were closed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two sources that were split open in chapter 7 are shut. Both directions, in reverse order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The date"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On **the seventeenth day of the seventh month**, the ark came to rest on the mountains of Ararat.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hold that date. The flood began on the seventeenth day of the second month."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel's calendar was reset at the exodus — *this month shall be your beginning of months* — which moved the seventh month to first place. The seventeenth of that month is three days after Passover on the fourteenth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days after the lamb is killed, the ark comes to rest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Came to rest\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb is **nuach** — to rest, to settle. It is the root of **Noah**'s own name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man called rest, in a boat that finally rests, on the seventeenth day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Ararat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mountains of Ararat, in the region of ancient Urartu."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ark does not land in a valley or on a shore. It comes to rest on high ground, above everything the water had covered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Noah does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing, for months. The waters *kept receding until, on the first day of the tenth month, the tops of the mountains appeared* — and he waits, and sends a raven, and then a dove, three times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not open the door. He did not close it either. See the note on Genesis 7:11–16."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The first thing God did in this chapter.** Remembered. Not calculated, not relented, not reconsidered. The Hebrew word for what turns the flood is the word for keeping somebody in mind."}]}, "Genesis 8:13–19": {"title": "Coming out of the ark", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The ground has been dry for nearly two months and Noah is still inside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does when he sees dry land"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Noah **removed the covering** from the ark and looked out — and the surface of the ground was dry.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He looks. He does not leave."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the first day of the first month. The next date given is the twenty-seventh day of the second month — fifty-seven days later — and only then does God speak."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been shut in by the LORD's own hand and he waited to be let out the same way. See the note on Genesis 7:11–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then God said to Noah, \"**Come out** of the ark.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He went in when God said *come in*, and he comes out when God says *come out*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The entire year is bracketed by two instructions, and Noah supplies neither of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You, your wife, your sons, and their wives.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the order. Going in, it was *you and your sons, your wife, and your sons' wives* — the men and then the women."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Coming out: *you, your wife, your sons, and their wives* — the couples together. A year of confinement and the households are paired."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"So they can multiply and fill the earth\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same commission given to Adam in Genesis 1 — *be fruitful and multiply, and fill the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The world is being restarted with the original sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Every one that went in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every animal, every creeping thing, and every bird **came out of the ark, kind by kind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *by their families*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing that went in is missing. The account has already recorded that the animals came to Noah rather than being hunted, and it now records that all of them came out again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The count going in matched the count coming out, across a year, in a boat, with no port of call."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is under his feet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A world that had been scoured. Everything he had known was gone — every place, every person outside the eight, every landmark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He steps out onto ground that is completely dry and completely emptied."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *Noah built an altar to the LORD, and took of every clean animal and every clean bird, and offered burnt offerings.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been given the clean animals by sevens rather than twos — the extra ones, which had been fed and carried for a year, were for this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD smelled a soothing aroma*, and said in His heart *I will never again curse the ground for man's sake.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise not to do it again follows an altar."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How long it lasted.** He entered on the seventeenth day of the second month and came out on the twenty-seventh day of the second month the following year — a year and ten days, shut in."}]}, "Genesis 8:20–22": {"title": "Seedtime and harvest", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first thing built on the new earth is an altar, and God's answer to it holds the world's weather in place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first act"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Noah **built an altar to the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first altar named in Scripture. Cain and Abel brought offerings; nobody had built one before this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is standing on a planet with eight people on it, and this is what he does first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Taking **some of every clean animal and bird**, he offered burnt offerings on it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every clean species had come aboard by sevens rather than twos — the extra ones are on this altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The surplus that would have looked like breeding stock on an empty earth is what he burns."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The aroma"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD **smelled the pleasing aroma**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *a soothing savour* — **reyach nichoach**, a restful smell. It becomes the standard phrase for accepted sacrifice through the whole of Leviticus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reaches for it once, about Christ: *an offering and a sacrifice to God for a sweet-smelling aroma.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says in His heart"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And said **in His heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses records it as an interior resolution, not an announcement. The promise is made before Noah is told it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Never again\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Never again** will I curse the ground because of humankind… and **never again** will I destroy all living creatures as I have done.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice, in one breath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason attached"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Even though** every inclination of the human heart is bent toward evil **from childhood**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is nearly word for word what God said before the flood — *every intent of the thoughts of his heart was only evil continually* — and there it was the reason for the judgment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here the identical assessment is the thing the promise is made in spite of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the human heart has changed between chapter 6 and chapter 8. What has changed is that there is an altar in front of Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As long as the earth endures, **seedtime and harvest, cold and heat, summer and winter, day and night will never cease**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four pairs. Every one is a cycle, and the guarantee is that they keep turning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seedtime comes first and harvest second, in that order, permanently."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is guaranteed by"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not by the stability of the climate and not by anything Noah does afterward — the next chapter has him drunk in a tent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By a sentence God said to Himself over a burning altar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sign"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rainbow comes in the next chapter, given as the visible token of this. See the note on Genesis 9:8–17."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What it rests on.** God smelled a sacrifice and put the seasons beyond recall. The rhythm of the world's food supply is fastened to an altar."}]}, "Genesis 8:6–12": {"title": "The raven and the dove", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two birds are sent out of the same window, and they behave completely differently."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The window"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After forty days, Noah **opened the window he had made in the ark**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **challon** — a window. Chapter 6 had specified it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The door had been shut by God: *and the LORD shut him in*. The window is Noah's to open."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The raven"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And sent out **a raven, which flew back and forth until the waters had dried up**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yatzo vashov** — going out and returning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A raven is a scavenger. With floating carrion everywhere it could land, feed and rest without needing dry ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It never comes back in, and it never reports anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dove"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he sent out **a dove** to see if the waters had receded.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yonah**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first flight"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the dove found no place to rest her feet** and returned to him, for water still covered the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **manoach lechaph-raglah** — a resting place for the sole of her foot."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same noun **manoach** — rest — that Naomi later uses of finding Ruth a home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the dove will not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Land on what the raven landed on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two birds are given the same world and one of them cannot use it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bringing in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **Noah reached out, took her, and drew her back inside**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayishlach yado vayyiqqacheha** — he put out his hand and took her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She comes back to the window and he has to reach for her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The waiting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He waited **seven more days**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyachel** — and he waited, or writhed with waiting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice in this passage, and the same interval both times. Noah does the counting himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second flight"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This time she **returned in the evening with a freshly plucked olive leaf in her beak**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **aleh-zayit taraph** — a plucked olive leaf."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An olive leaf, not a branch. And **freshly plucked**, which means it was growing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Olives do not survive prolonged submersion easily, and a green leaf means growth has resumed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Noah concludes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **Noah knew the waters were nearly gone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One leaf, and he can date the recovery from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third flight"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He waited seven more days and sent her out once more, and **this time she did not return to him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The absence is the message."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The earth was ready.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Noah does then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing, for another two months."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then God spoke to Noah, saying, \"**Go out of the ark**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He removed the covering and saw the ground was dry, and still waited for the word. He had been shut in by God and he waits to be told to come out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first thing he builds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Noah built an altar to the LORD, and took of every clean animal and every clean bird, and offered burnt offerings.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD smelled a soothing aroma.* See the note on Genesis 8:20–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the dove turns up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Jesus, when He had been baptized, came up immediately from the water… and He saw the Spirit of God **descending like a dove and alighting upon Him**.* See the note on Matthew 3:16–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bird that could find nowhere to rest, resting."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The difference.** The raven never came back — it could live on what was floating. The dove would not land on it, and had to be reached for and drawn back in."}]}, "Genesis 9:8–17": {"title": "The bow in the cloud", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God makes a promise and then puts up something to look at, and the thing He puts up is a weapon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have set **my rainbow** in the clouds.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **qeshet** — and it is simply the word for a bow. The war bow, the hunting bow, the thing David sang about, the weapon Jonathan gave away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no separate Hebrew word for a rainbow. God says *I have set My bow in the cloud*, and the picture is of a warrior hanging up his weapon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is hung pointing away — the arc bends toward heaven, with the string toward earth. Whatever is left in it is aimed at Himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who has to remember"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whenever I bring clouds over the earth and the rainbow appears, **I will remember my covenant**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said three times in the passage: *I will remember*, *I will see it and remember*, *this is the sign of the covenant.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *when you see it, remember*. The sign is placed where He looks."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **zakar** is the same verb as *God remembered Noah* a chapter earlier — it means acting on a commitment, not recovering a lost thought. See the note on Genesis 8:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the covenant is with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"With you and your descendants after you, and **with every living creature** that was with you — the birds, the livestock, and all the wild animals.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it four times over. The animals are named as parties to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the first covenant in Scripture and it is made with the whole earth, with no conditions attached to anybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not required"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing. Read the whole passage and there is no *if*, no clause about behaviour, no term for either side to keep."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the only reason it is still standing — the generation it was made with included Ham, and Noah is drunk and naked in his tent within a few verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who brings the clouds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whenever I bring clouds** over the earth.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not promise there will be no more clouds. He says He is the one bringing them, and that when they come, the bow shows up in them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sign only appears when there is rain and sun at once. You do not get the rainbow on a clear day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To Me this is like the waters of Noah; for as I have sworn that the waters of Noah would no more cover the earth, so have I sworn that **I would not be angry with you, nor rebuke you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 54, which follows Isaiah 53 — the servant *wounded for our transgressions*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God stakes the certainty of one promise on the certainty of the other, and invites the comparison Himself. See the note on Isaiah 54:9–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is the sign of the covenant I have established between me and **all life on the earth**.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other place the bow turns up.** Ezekiel 1, and Revelation 4: *there was a rainbow around the throne, in appearance like an emerald.* The sign hung in the sky over a wet world is described as encircling the throne itself."}]}, "Habakkuk 1:1–4": {"title": "\"How long?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only book in the Bible that opens as a complaint to God and never stops being a conversation with Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is **the burden Habakkuk the prophet carried, the vision he saw**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **massa** — a burden, a load, and the standard term for a prophetic oracle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The vision he saw** — **chazon**, the word Proverbs uses: *where there is no vision the people cast off restraint*. See the note on Proverbs 29:14–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book calls its own contents a vision, and the vision starts with a question."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he is speaking to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the nation. Most prophets deliver a message from God to Israel; Habakkuk opens by addressing God about Israel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How long, LORD, must I cry for help before You hear me?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *how long shall I cry, and You will not hear*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How long must I call to You over the violence, and still You do not save?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *violence* is **chamas** — brutality, wrong done to a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The accusation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why do You make me stare at wrong? Why do You let me watch such trouble?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *why do You show me iniquity and cause me to see trouble*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not asking why it exists. He is asking why he has to look at it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he sees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Ruin and cruelty are in front of me**. Everywhere there is fighting, and **the quarreling never stops**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The legal collapse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The law goes limp. Justice never wins**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *goes limp* is **pug** — to be numbed, chilled, ineffective."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The wicked crowd around the good and **hem them in**, so that when a verdict comes, **it comes out crooked**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The courts are running and the outcomes are wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is unusual about the book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The complaint is preserved. Nobody edits it out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Habakkuk is the only prophet who argues with God through a whole book and is answered twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God answers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look among the nations and watch — be utterly astounded! For I will work a work in your days which you would not believe, though it were told you. **For indeed I am raising up the Chaldeans**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer makes the problem worse. Judah's corruption will be dealt with by Babylon — a nation Habakkuk regards as far worse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second complaint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You are of purer eyes than to behold evil… **why do You look on those who deal treacherously, and hold Your tongue when the wicked devours a person more righteous than he?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes back with a harder question."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will take my stand at my watchpost.* See the note on Habakkuk 2:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He stops arguing and waits."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer that comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Write the vision… **though it tarries, wait for it; because it will surely come**.* See the note on Habakkuk 2:2–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the middle of it: *the just shall live by his faith* — the sentence Paul quotes twice and Hebrews once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the book ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though the fig tree may not blossom, nor fruit be on the vines; though the labour of the olive may fail, and the fields yield no food; though the flock be cut off from the fold, and there be no herd in the stalls — **yet I will rejoice in the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six failures listed, and then that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never gets the answer he asked for."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the book preserves.** The complaint, unedited. Habakkuk asks how long twice, why twice, and accuses God of making him watch — and it is filed under *the vision he saw*."}]}, "Habakkuk 2:1": {"title": "\"I will take my stand\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it is a decision about where to stand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two rounds of complaint. *How long, LORD, must I cry for help before You hear me?* — and then an answer about Babylon that made it worse, and a second complaint harder than the first. See the note on Habakkuk 1:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then this. He stops arguing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The post"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **take my stand at my watchpost**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mishmarti** — my watch, my guard-post. The place a sentry is assigned."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wall"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **plant my feet on the wall** and keep watch.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I will station myself on the rampart*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A watchman on a city wall in that world had one job: to see something coming before anybody else did, and to say so."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah: *I have set watchmen on your walls, O Jerusalem, who shall never hold their peace day or night.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel is appointed one: *son of man, I have made you a watchman for the house of Israel; therefore hear a word from My mouth, and give them warning from Me.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is watching for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **look to see what He will say to me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *to see what He will speak in me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look, to see what He will say. Habakkuk mixes the senses deliberately — the thing he is watching for is a word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **what I will answer when I am corrected**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *what I shall answer when I am reproved*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He expects to be corrected. He has just accused God twice of watching and doing nothing, and he assumes an answer will include a rebuke."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He plans what to say when it comes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What actually happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the LORD answered me. **Write the vision**.* See the note on Habakkuk 2:2–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no rebuke."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The posture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the verse is about effort. He goes to a post, plants his feet, and waits for something to arrive from outside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who else stands and waits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elijah at Horeb, after wind, earthquake and fire, coming out to the cave mouth for a whisper. See the note on 1 Kings 19:9–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel, three weeks in mourning, with the answer already dispatched on day one. See the note on Daniel 10:12–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the watchman's answer turns out to be"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold, the proud, his soul is not upright in him; **but the just shall live by his faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses later, and it is the sentence Paul quotes in Romans and Galatians and Hebrews quotes in chapter 10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most consequential line in the Reformation comes out of a man standing on a wall waiting to be told off."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The end of the same chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the LORD is in His holy temple. Let all the earth keep silence before Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophet who opened by demanding an answer closes the chapter by calling for silence."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he does after two rounds of arguing.** Stops. Goes to a post, stands on a wall, and watches to see what will be said — and prepares an answer for the correction that never comes."}]}, "Habakkuk 2:12–14": {"title": "\"As the waters cover the sea\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The third of five woes, and the sentence that ends it belongs to a different scale entirely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Habakkuk opens with a complaint: *O LORD, how long shall I cry, and You will not hear?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God answers by saying He is raising up the Babylonians — which makes the prophet's problem worse, and he says so: *You are of purer eyes than to behold evil… why do You look on those who deal treacherously?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The five woes in chapter 2 are the answer, pronounced over the empire that was about to swallow Judah."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The woe"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sorrow to the one who **builds a town with blood** and **founds a city on wrongdoing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Babylon was built by deported labour. The bricks of it are still stamped with Nebuchadnezzar's name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Is it not from the LORD of hosts that **the peoples labor only to feed the fire**, that **the nations wear themselves out for nothing**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses about wasted work. The Hebrew is *the people shall labour for the very fire, and the nations shall weary themselves for nothing*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything built with blood is described as fuel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the earth will be filled with the knowledge of the glory of the LORD, as the waters cover the sea**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **for** connects it. The reason the empire's work comes to nothing is what is going to fill the earth instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As the waters cover the sea.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not water on the sea. The sea is water — the covering is total and there is nothing else there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is filling it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The knowledge of **the glory** of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah has nearly the same line: *the earth shall be full of **the knowledge of the LORD** as the waters cover the sea* — at the end of the passage about the wolf and the lamb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Habakkuk adds *the glory*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the woes go"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five in the chapter: to the plunderer, to the man who builds his house by unjust gain, to the city built on blood, to the man who makes his neighbour drunk, and to the idol-maker."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the last verse of the chapter: *but **the LORD is in His holy temple. Let all the earth keep silence before Him**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse the chapter is famous for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold the proud, his soul is not upright in him; but **the just shall live by his faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 4, eight verses before this. Paul quotes it in Romans and Galatians, and Hebrews quotes it too — the sentence the Reformation was built on comes out of a book about Babylon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the book ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though the fig tree may not blossom, nor fruit be on the vines… **yet I will rejoice in the LORD**, I will joy in the God of my salvation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Habakkuk lists six failed harvests and then says that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He got no answer to his original question. He got this."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The scale of the sentence.** It sits inside a woe over one empire, and it is about the whole earth — and what fills it is not power or peace but the knowledge of the glory."}]}, "Habakkuk 2:2–3": {"title": "\"Write it plain\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The answer to a man standing on a wall, and it begins with an instruction about handwriting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the LORD answered me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had gone to his post expecting a correction. See the note on Habakkuk 2:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gets an assignment instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Write the vision, He said. **Carve it clear on tablets**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *inscribe it upon tablets* — **luchot**, the same word as the tablets of stone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a scroll. Something durable and public."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **anyone who reads it can run to tell it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *that he may run who reads it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Readers have divided over whether it means a man running past can read it, or a man who reads it runs to spread it. The Hebrew allows both, and both need the same thing: it has to be legible at speed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Plainness is the point of the instruction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The waiting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the vision still waits for its appointed time**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mo'ed** — an appointed time, a set season. The word used of the feasts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The delay is scheduled rather than accidental."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is **racing toward the end**, and **it will not lie**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *it hastens to the end and will not lie*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things said in one clause: it is hurrying, and it will not deceive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction about the wait"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If it **seems slow, wait for it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *though it tarries, wait for it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Seems** is the operative word. The vision has a set time and it is hurrying; the slowness is in the watching."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The guarantee"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It will surely come. **It will not be late**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew doubles it — *coming it will come, it will not delay*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold, the proud, his soul is not upright in him; **but the just shall live by his faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **emunah** — faithfulness, steadfastness, reliability."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes it in Romans and Galatians. Hebrews quotes it in chapter 10, and adds the next lines: *now the just shall live by faith; but if anyone draws back, My soul has no pleasure in him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three New Testament citations of one sentence, spoken to a man waiting for something overdue."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the verse is answering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Habakkuk had asked *how long*. The reply does not give a date. It says there is one, that it is fixed, and that the thing is moving faster than it looks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews does with the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **yet a little while, and He who is coming will come and will not tarry**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Quoted directly after *you have need of endurance, so that after you have done the will of God, you may receive the promise*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The context there is a congregation that had lost property and was under pressure to go back. See the note on Hebrews 10:23–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five woes that follow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest of Habakkuk 2 is five woes on the empire the prophet was complaining about — and in the middle of them: *for the earth will be filled with the knowledge of the glory of the LORD, as the waters cover the sea.* See the note on Habakkuk 2:12–14."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he is told to do about the delay.** Write it down, large enough to read at a run — and then the reason: the appointment exists, the thing is hurrying, and what looks slow is not late."}]}, "Habakkuk 2:4–5": {"title": "The line the Reformation turned on", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Five words in Hebrew, quoted three times in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An argument. Habakkuk complains and God answers, twice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*O LORD, how long shall I cry, and You will not hear?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer is that the Babylonians are coming — *that bitter and hasty nation* — which makes the second complaint worse than the first: *why do You look on those who deal treacherously, and hold Your tongue when the wicked devours a person more righteous than he?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he goes to wait"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will stand my watch and set myself on the rampart, and **watch to see what He will say to me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He climbs the wall like a sentry and waits for an answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Write the vision and make it plain on tablets**, that he may run who reads it. For the vision is yet for an appointed time… **though it tarries, wait for it**; because it will surely come, it will not tarry.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Large letters, so a man running past can read it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look at the proud one: **he is swollen with himself, and nothing in him is straight**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **uppelah** — puffed up, swollen. And *his soul is not upright in him*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the righteous will live by his faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vetsaddiq be'emunato yichyeh**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Emunah** — faithfulness, steadiness, reliability. The word used of Moses' hands held up until sunset: *and his hands were steady until the going down of the sun*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It carries both senses at once: trusting, and staying trusting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul, in Romans: *for in it the righteousness of God is revealed from faith to faith; as it is written, \"**the just shall live by faith**.\"* See the note on Romans 1:16–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul, in Galatians: *that no one is justified by the law in the sight of God is evident, for \"the just shall live by faith.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Hebrews, at the end of the tenth chapter, leading straight into the faith chapter: *now the just shall live by faith; but if anyone draws back, My soul has no pleasure in him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three quotations, three different arguments, one sentence from a minor prophet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Reformation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luther's account of his own turning point is about Romans 1:17 and this line behind it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three great slogans — by faith alone, by grace alone, by Scripture alone — all have this clause somewhere underneath them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five words, written large on a tablet so a runner could read them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest of the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wine betrays him. The arrogant man is **never still**. He **opens his throat as wide as the grave**. Like death, **he is never satisfied**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *the grave* is **She'ol**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gathers every nation to himself and sweeps up all the peoples as his own.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An empire described as an appetite. Proverbs uses the same image of the grave: *Sheol and Abaddon are never full*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five woes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "What follows is a series of them, aimed at the conqueror: woe to him who increases what is not his, who covets evil gain, who builds a town with bloodshed, who gives drink to his neighbour, and who says to wood *awake!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the middle: *the earth will be filled with the knowledge of the glory of the LORD, as the waters cover the sea*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the book ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though the fig tree may not blossom, nor fruit be on the vines; though the labour of the olive may fail, and the fields yield no food… **yet I will rejoice in the LORD, I will joy in the God of my salvation**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who started by shouting at God finishes by listing everything that could fail and saying he will be glad anyway."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the word carries.** *Emunah* — faithfulness and trust in one word. The verse says the righteous man lives by it, and three New Testament writers build on the clause."}]}, "Haggai 1:2": {"title": "The time has not come yet", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A whole nation's excuse, quoted back to it in its own words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is what **the LORD Almighty** said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **YHWH Tzeva'ot** — the LORD of hosts, of armies."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haggai uses the title fourteen times in two short chapters. It is a book of thirty-eight verses, and God is named as commander of armies in almost every paragraph, addressed to a few thousand people with no army at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Back from Babylon. Cyrus had let them return around 538 BC, and they had laid the foundation of the temple in the second year."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the local opposition, the letters to the Persian court, and the work stopped."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Thus the work of the house of God which is at Jerusalem ceased, and it was discontinued until the second year of the reign of Darius.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sixteen years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**These people** keep saying, '**The time has not come yet. It is not the season to rebuild the house of the LORD**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lo et-bo** — not the time to come."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distance in the phrasing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *My people*. God quotes them at arm's length."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the excuse is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a refusal. A postponement."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in Jerusalem is saying the temple should not be built. They are saying the conditions are not right yet — and after sixteen years the sentence still has *yet* in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it was plausible"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was true that the opposition was real, that a decree had stopped them, and that the economy was bad. Haggai's next paragraph confirms the last part: *you have sown much and bring in little*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The excuse was accurate about the circumstances. That is what makes it worth quoting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the same sixteen years produced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Is it truly the time for you to be living in **your own paneled houses**, warm and finished, while **this house lies in ruins**?* See the note on Haggai 1:3–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word *time* comes straight back at them. They said it was not the time for one thing; God asks whether it was the time for the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sephunim** — panelled, roofed, finished. And **chareb** — desolate, a ruin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both states are the result of the same sixteen years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give careful thought to your ways.* See the note on Haggai 1:5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Repeated in verse 7, and twice more in chapter 2. The phrase is the book's refrain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens after the sermon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Zerubbabel… and Joshua… **with all the remnant of the people, obeyed the voice of the LORD their God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD stirred up the spirit of Zerubbabel… and they came and worked on the house of the LORD of hosts, their God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-three days from the first word to the first tool. It is the fastest response to a prophet recorded anywhere in the Old Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says in between"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am with you**,\" says the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words, delivered before a single stone had moved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other prophet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the same month, Zechariah began. Both men are named in Ezra: **the prophets, Haggai and Zechariah, prophesied to the Jews… and they built and finished it***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book of Ezra records that the building resumed because two men preached."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they said.** Not *no* — *not yet*. And they had been saying it for sixteen years with the word *yet* still in the sentence."}]}, "Haggai 1:3–4": {"title": "Panelled houses", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two buildings compared, and the same sixteen years produced both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The messenger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the word of the LORD came again through Haggai**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **beyad Chaggai** — by the hand of Haggai."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book uses this phrasing rather than *to* Haggai. He is the conduit, not the subject, and the book tells us nothing about him — no father, no tribe, no home town."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His name means *festal, my feast*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Is it truly the time for you** to be living in your own **paneled houses, warm and finished**, while **this house lies in ruins**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their word was **et** — time. *It is not the time.* See the note on Haggai 1:2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God takes the same noun and puts it in the question."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for panelled"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sephunim** — covered, wainscoted, roofed with finished timber."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word used of Solomon's temple: *he built the walls of the house within with boards of cedar… he covered them on the inside with wood*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wood treatment that had been reserved for the house of God is now on their own walls."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for ruins"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chareb** — desolate, dried up, wasted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the comparison is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An attack on comfortable housing. Nothing here says the houses are too grand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The charge is the pairing. One building got finished and the other did not, in the same span of years, by the same people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What their time and money went on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever was in front of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody voted to abandon the temple. They just kept doing the next necessary thing, for sixteen years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict on the result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have **sown much, and bring in little**; you eat, but do not have enough; you drink, but you are not filled with drink… and **he who earns wages, earns wages to put into a bag with holes**.* See the note on Haggai 1:6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The economy in which the houses got panelled was not working."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are told to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Give careful thought to your ways**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **simu levavchem al-darkeichem** — set your heart upon your ways. See the note on Haggai 1:5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go up into the hills. Bring down timber and build the house**. Then I will take pleasure in it, and I will be honored.\"* See the note on Haggai 1:7–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Timber again — the same material that went into the panelling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus asks about buildings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do you see these great buildings? **Not one stone shall be left upon another**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said of the temple this one eventually became, rebuilt magnificently by Herod."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the house is now"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do you not know that **you are the temple of God** and that the Spirit of God dwells in you?* See the note on 1 Corinthians 3:16–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In whom **you also are being built together for a dwelling place of God** in the Spirit.* See the note on Ephesians 2:19–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Haggai promises the second temple"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The glory of this latter temple shall be greater than the former**,\" says the LORD of hosts.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to old men who had wept when the foundation was laid because they remembered Solomon's. See the note on Haggai 2:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The building they were embarrassed by is the one a carpenter's son would walk into."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The comparison.** Not that the houses were too fine. That both buildings had sixteen years, and only one of them got a roof."}]}, "Haggai 1:5": {"title": "Stop where you are", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Eight words, and they are the hinge of the book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **stop where you are. Give careful thought to your ways**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **simu levavchem al-darkeichem** — set your heart upon your ways."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The idiom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *think about*. **Set your heart on** — the same construction used of paying close attention, of taking something to heart, of caring."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew heart is the seat of thought and decision, not primarily of feeling. The instruction is to turn the full attention onto something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What onto"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your ways* — **darkeichem**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not their theology, their politics, or their opposition. The pattern of what they have actually been doing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it appears"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four times in Haggai: here, in verse 7, and twice more in chapter 2 — *from this day forward, consider it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty-eight verses, and the same phrase four times. It is the book's refrain and its method."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What preceded it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Is it truly the time for you to be living in your own panelled houses… while this house lies in ruins?\"* See the note on Haggai 1:3–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A question, and then the instruction to sit down with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have sown much, and bring in little; you eat, but do not have enough… and **he who earns wages, earns wages to put into a bag with holes**.* See the note on Haggai 1:6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five observations, all of them about ordinary life, all of them verifiable by anyone standing there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not tell them what is wrong. He tells them to look at their own year and notice something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind of preaching this is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not new information. Every person in that crowd knew the harvest had been poor and the wages had not stretched."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophet's contribution is to stop them and ask them to put the facts next to each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the pause is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Stop where you are.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The excuse in verse 2 was about timing — *the time has not come yet*. See the note on Haggai 1:2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An excuse about timing is answered by an instruction to halt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Zerubbabel… Joshua… **with all the remnant of the people, obeyed the voice of the LORD their God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they came and worked on the house of the LORD of hosts.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-three days between the sermon and the first tool lifted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says in the gap"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am with you**,\" says the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Spoken after the obedience began and before anything was built."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David says about the same examination"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Search me, O God, and know my heart; try me, and know my anxieties; **and see if there is any wicked way in me**, and lead me in the way everlasting.* See the note on Psalms 139:23–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same request, made in the first person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **let a man examine himself**, and so let him eat of the bread and drink of the cup.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 11:27–29."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The idiom.** **Set your heart upon your ways** — not think about them. Turn the whole attention onto the pattern of what you have actually been doing."}]}, "Haggai 1:6": {"title": "A purse full of holes", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Five clauses, and every one of them is about effort that does not arrive anywhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it was said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the second year of King Darius, in the sixth month, on the first day of the month.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haggai dates everything to the day. 29 August 520 BC, by the usual reckoning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The exiles have been back from Babylon for about sixteen years. They laid the temple foundation and then stopped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The time has not come**, the time that the LORD's house should be built.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a refusal. A postponement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says about that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Is it time for you yourselves to dwell in your panelled houses, **and this temple to lie in ruins**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Panelled — the Hebrew is **sephunim**, the word used of Solomon's cedar work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not shelter. Finish work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have **planted much and gathered little**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You **eat, but you are never full**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You **drink, but you are never satisfied**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You **put on clothes, but you never feel warm**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You **earn wages, only to pour them into a purse full of holes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of the last is *he who earns wages, earns wages to put into a bag with holes*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shape of the list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every clause is a completed action followed by a missing result."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The planting happened. The eating happened. The wages were earned. Nothing is said about laziness or failure of effort."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The problem is that the output does not stay."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern behind it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 28 lists it among the curses: *you shall carry much seed out to the field but gather little in… you shall have olive trees throughout all your territory, but you shall not anoint yourself with the oil*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haggai is quoting the covenant document at people who knew it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You expected much, and it came to little. **What you brought home, I blew away**. And why?… **Because My house lies in ruins while each of you hurries home to his own**.\"* See the note on Haggai 1:9–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *you run, each man, to his own house*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the prophet asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Consider your ways**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice in the chapter, and again later. The Hebrew is *set your heart on your ways*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *repent* in the abstract. Look at the pattern."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Zerubbabel… and Joshua… **with all the remnant of the people, obeyed the voice of the LORD their God**… and they came and worked on the house of the LORD of hosts, their God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-three days later. Haggai dates that too."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am with you**,\" says the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five words, and they come before any of the work is done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the promise about the building"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The glory of this latter temple shall be greater than the former… and in this place I will give peace.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said about a building the old men had wept over because it was so much smaller than Solomon's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same warning goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not lay up for yourselves treasures on earth, where moth and rust destroy and where thieves break in and steal.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the barn-builder: *\"Fool! This night your soul will be required of you; then whose will those things be which you have provided?\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What each clause records.** The work was done. Planted, eaten, earned — and then the result is missing. Nothing on the list is about not trying."}]}, "Haggai 1:7–8": {"title": "Go up into the hills", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The instruction is three verbs long, and none of them requires a permit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repeat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is what the LORD Almighty says. \"**Give careful thought to your ways**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second time in three verses. See the note on Haggai 1:5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first was followed by five observations about their harvest. This one is followed by an instruction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go up into the hills. Bring down timber and build the house**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **alu… vahavetem… uvenu** — go up, bring, build."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Quarrying stone. Gold. Vessels. Craftsmen. Money."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Timber, from the hills nearby, carried down by hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Solomon's temple had cedar shipped from Lebanon by sea on rafts, and thirty thousand conscripted labourers. This one starts with men walking uphill with axes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scale"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The work is described as something the people standing there can begin this week."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the instruction depends on Persian permission, a better economy, or the opposition going away — all three of which had been genuine obstacles for sixteen years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise attached"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then **I will take pleasure in it, and I will be honored**,\" says the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **veertzeh-bo** — and I will be pleased with it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ratzah** is the word for God accepting a sacrifice. It is what a worshipper hoped for at the altar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ve'ekkaved** — and I will be glorified, honoured, given weight."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kavod** — glory, weight, heaviness. The root behind the noun for the presence that filled the tabernacle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that is answering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old men had wept when the foundation was laid, because they had seen the first house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who is left among you who saw this temple in its former glory? **And how do you see it now? In comparison with it, is this not in your eyes as nothing?***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They knew this building would be smaller. God's promise is not that it will look better — it is that He will be pleased with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the timber was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same material that had gone into their own panelled walls. See the note on Haggai 1:3–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction sends them back to the hills for the thing they had already been fetching for sixteen years, for a different building."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the LORD stirred up the spirit of Zerubbabel… and the spirit of Joshua… and the spirit of all the remnant of the people, and **they came and worked on the house of the LORD of hosts, their God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vaya'ar YHWH et-ruach** — the LORD stirred up the spirit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They obeyed, and the text says God moved them to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word before the work"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am with you**,\" says the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the obedience and the stirring, four words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What this house became"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rebuilt by Herod, and standing when a boy of twelve was found in it asking questions, and when a man overturned tables in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The glory of this latter temple shall be greater than the former**.\"* See the note on Haggai 2:6–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the glory ended up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the Word became flesh and **dwelt among us**, and we beheld His glory.* See the note on John 1:14–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eskenosen** — pitched His tent."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the instruction leaves out.** Money, stone, craftsmen, permission. Go up the hill, fetch wood, build — three things men standing in the crowd could start that week."}]}, "Haggai 1:9–11": {"title": "Each of you hurries home", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The diagnosis, and God states it in the first person without softening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The observation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You **expected much, and it came to little**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *you looked for much, and behold, it came to little*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same complaint as verse 6 — the planting, the eating, the wages in a torn purse. See the note on Haggai 1:6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The admission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What you brought home, **I blew away**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **venaphachti bo** — and I blew on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not neglect, and not bad luck. God claims it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**And why?** declares the LORD Almighty.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks it Himself and answers it in the next clause."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Because My house lies in ruins while each of you hurries home to his own**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **atem ratsim ish levéto** — you are running, each man to his own house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Running.* The verb is about speed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in the passage is idle. They are working hard, on their own places, at pace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The charge is not laziness. It is order of priority, and the picture is of a crowd of people each moving quickly in a different direction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The drought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So the skies above you have **held back their dew**, and the ground has **held back its harvest**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kal'u** — restrained, shut up. The same verb for both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sky and ground, described as withholding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The extent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I called for a **drought** on the fields and the hills, on **the grain and the new wine and the oil**, on everything the ground brings forth, on **people and livestock**, on all the work of your hands.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for drought is **chorev** — and it is a play on **charev**, the word for *ruins* used of the temple two verses earlier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The house lies **charev**; the land gets **chorev**. One consonant apart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haggai is a careful writer and the pun is deliberate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The triad"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grain, new wine and oil. The standard summary of that agriculture, and the same three the third horseman deals in. See the note on Revelation 6:5–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That they had stopped believing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were back from exile, they had rebuilt an altar, they had laid a foundation, and they were still calling it *the LORD's house*. They had simply concluded the time had not come."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens twenty-three days later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Zerubbabel… and Joshua… **with all the remnant of the people, obeyed the voice of the LORD their God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD stirred up the spirit of Zerubbabel… and the spirit of all the remnant of the people; and they came and worked on the house of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The obedience and the stirring are both recorded. Haggai does not choose between them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is said in between"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am with you**,\" says the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the rebuke and the work, and before any of it is done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says a month later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To men who remembered Solomon's temple and were weeping at the size of this one:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Be strong… and work; for I am with you… **according to the word that I covenanted with you when you came out of Egypt, so My Spirit remains among you. Do not fear!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The glory of this latter temple shall be greater than the former.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the priority sentence goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But seek first the kingdom of God and His righteousness, and all these things shall be added to you.\"* See the note on Matthew 6:25–34."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the charge is not.** Idleness. *Each man runs to his own house* — the verb is about speed, and the drought lands on people who were working hard in the wrong order."}]}, "Haggai 2:10–11": {"title": "Ask the priests for a ruling", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A prophet is told to put a legal question to the experts, in public, and let them answer it themselves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The date"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On **the twenty-fourth day of the ninth month, in the second year of Darius**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haggai dates every oracle. This is the fourth and last, delivered in Kislev — December, 520 BC."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three months to the day since the work restarted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The precision"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book has five dated messages across four months. Nothing else in the prophets is timestamped this closely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is what the LORD Almighty says. \"**Ask the priests for a ruling**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **she'al-na et-hakohanim torah** — ask the priests for *torah*, for instruction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Torah** here is not the five books. It is a priestly ruling on a point of law — the technical sense the word carries in Leviticus and Deuteronomy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose job this was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priests'. *They shall teach Jacob Your judgments, and Israel Your law*, and *the priest's lips should keep knowledge, and people should seek the law from his mouth*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deciding clean from unclean was the priest's daily function."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the method is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haggai does not tell them the answer. He asks them a question about their own subject and lets them state the law out loud."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does it twice, and both times the priests answer correctly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If someone carries **consecrated meat in the fold of his garment**, and that fold touches some bread or stew, some wine or oil or any other food, **does the food become holy?**\" The priests answered, \"**No**.\"* See the note on Haggai 2:12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If someone **made unclean by a dead body** touches any of these things, **does it become unclean?**\" And the priests answered, \"**Yes**.\"* See the note on Haggai 2:13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The asymmetry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Holiness does not transfer at second hand. Defilement does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the law, correctly stated by the men whose job it was to know it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is applied to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So is this people, and so is this nation before Me**,\" says the LORD, \"and so is every work of their hands; and what they offer there is unclean.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ruling is turned on the congregation. Offerings made on the altar of a people whose priority was elsewhere did not sanctify the rest of their lives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being said about the sixteen years"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been sacrificing. Ezra records the altar rebuilt and the burnt offerings resumed years before the temple was."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The worship was going on. The ruling says it was not making anything else holy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The date the promise attaches to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**From this day forward** I will bless you.\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same verse, same day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blessing is dated from the day the question was asked, not from the day the temple was finished — which was still four years away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the other direction looks like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A woman with an issue of blood, unclean for twelve years, touching the hem of a garment in a crowd. See the note on Mark 5:25–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a dead girl's hand taken by a man who then told them to give her something to eat. See the note on Mark 5:38–43."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under Haggai's ruling both contacts run one way. In Mark they run the other."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The method.** He does not preach the law at them. He asks the men whose job it is, and they state it correctly — and then he turns it round."}]}, "Haggai 2:12": {"title": "Does the food become holy", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first of two test questions, and the priests get it right."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If someone carries **consecrated meat in the fold of his garment**, and that fold touches some bread or stew, some wine or oil or any other food, **does the food become holy?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **besar-qodesh** — holy flesh. Meat from a sacrifice, which the priests were entitled to eat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Bikenaph bigdo** — in the wing, the corner, the fold of his garment. The loose fold of an outer robe used as a carrier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the question is not hypothetical"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Priests carried sacrificial meat home this way. It happened daily."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haggai picks an ordinary scene from the questioner's own working life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Holy meat → the fold of a garment → bread, stew, wine, oil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two removes. The meat touches the cloth; the cloth touches the food."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The priests answered, \"**No**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Correct. Leviticus 6 says *whatever touches its flesh must be holy*, which covers the first contact — but the transfer stops there. Holiness does not pass at second hand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law does say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever touches the altar shall be holy*, and *whatever touches them shall be holy*, of the consecrated vessels."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Direct contact, yes. Contact with something that touched something, no."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the second question asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If someone made unclean by a dead body touches any of these things, **does it become unclean?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Yes. It becomes unclean**.\"* See the note on Haggai 2:13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The asymmetry stated plainly"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under that arrangement, contamination is transmissible and consecration is not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Holiness has a range of one. Defilement does not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Haggai does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So is this people, and so is this nation before Me… **and what they offer there is unclean**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people had been sacrificing on a rebuilt altar for years while the house lay in ruins. The ruling says those offerings were not making the rest of their work holy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The direction of travel was the other way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The garment fold"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kanaph** — wing, corner, hem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word in Malachi: *the Sun of Righteousness shall arise **with healing in His wings***. See the note on Malachi 4:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the word for the corner of a garment where the tassels were commanded to be worn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to a garment's hem in Mark"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She had heard about Jesus, and came behind Him in the crowd **and touched His garment**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An unclean woman, twelve years unclean, reaching for a hem in a crowd — and *immediately the fountain of her blood was dried up*. See the note on Mark 5:25–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under this ruling, that contact should have run the other way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other direction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He came and touched the open coffin*, of the widow's son at Nain. And *He took the child by the hand*, of Jairus's daughter. And *Jesus put out His hand and touched him*, of the leper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three contacts that should have defiled Him, and each time the traffic goes the wrong way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the same asymmetry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the unbelieving husband is sanctified by the wife**… otherwise your children would be unclean, but now they are holy.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 7:12–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the reverse of the priests' ruling, stated as a fact about a household."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The answer.** *No.* Holiness had a reach of one contact; uncleanness did not — and the priests stated their own law correctly before Haggai turned it on them."}]}, "Haggai 2:13": {"title": "Yes, it becomes unclean", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second question, and the answer is the opposite of the first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If someone **made unclean by a dead body** touches any of these things, **does it become unclean?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **teme-nephesh** — unclean by a soul, the standard idiom for corpse defilement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the corpse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of all the sources of uncleanness in the law, contact with the dead was the most severe."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who touches the dead body of anyone shall be unclean seven days.* Seven days, not one, and it required the water of purification made with the ashes of a red heifer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the priests answered, \"**Yes. It becomes unclean**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Correct again. Numbers 19 says so explicitly: *whatever the unclean person touches shall be unclean*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two answers together"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Holy meat, at second hand: no."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Corpse defilement, at second hand: yes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same priests, the same day, and the two rulings do not match — because the law itself does not treat them symmetrically. See the note on Haggai 2:12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the asymmetry means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under that arrangement, holiness was fragile and defilement was not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A clean man could not make an unclean thing clean by touching it. An unclean man could make a clean thing unclean by touching it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The application"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So is this people, and so is this nation before Me**,\" says the LORD, \"and so is every work of their hands; **and what they offer there is unclean**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offerings on the altar were not sanctifying the sixteen years of neglected building. The neglect was contaminating the offerings."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the date says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**From this day forward** I will bless you.\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same oracle. The ruling about defilement is followed immediately by a blessing dated to the day it was spoken."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing had been completed. The temple would not be finished for another four years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word after the ruling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Consider now from this day forward**, from the twenty-fourth day of the ninth month… **is the seed still in the barn?** As yet the vine, the fig tree, the pomegranate, and the olive tree have not yielded fruit.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing has come up yet. And: *from this day I will bless you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise is made in December, before anything is planted, about a harvest nobody can see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the traffic reverses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He put out His hand and touched him, saying, \"I am willing; be cleansed.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He came and touched the open coffin.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He took the child by the hand and said to her, \"Talitha, cumi.\"* See the note on Mark 5:38–43."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leprosy, a corpse, and a dead child. Under Haggai's ruling every one of those contacts defiles the toucher."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The woman in the crowd"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve years unclean, reaching through a crowd for the hem of a garment — which under the law made every person she pressed past unclean too."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And immediately Jesus, knowing in Himself that power had gone out of Him.* See the note on Mark 5:30–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something went out. Nothing came in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews says about the ashes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For if the blood of bulls and goats and **the ashes of a heifer**, sprinkling the unclean, sanctifies for the purifying of the flesh, **how much more shall the blood of Christ**… cleanse your conscience.* See the note on Hebrews 9:11–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The remedy for corpse defilement is the one Hebrews argues from."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The pair.** Holy at second hand: no. Unclean at second hand: yes. The priests answered their own law correctly both times, and the two answers do not match."}]}, "Haggai 2:15–17": {"title": "Twenty measures, and there were ten", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He asks them to remember the arithmetic of the years before the building started."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now **give careful thought to this, from this day on**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fourth time the phrase appears in this short book. See the note on Haggai 1:5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**From this day on** — the twenty-fourth of the ninth month, the date the oracle carries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The marker"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Think back to **how things were before one stone was laid on another** in the temple of the LORD.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **miterem sum-even el-aven** — before stone was placed on stone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives them a fixed point in the calendar and asks them to compare the two sides of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first count"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When anyone came to a heap **expecting twenty measures, there were only ten**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A heap of harvested grain, and half of what the eye had estimated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When anyone went to the wine vat to draw off **fifty measures, there were only twenty**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Worse. Less than half."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word for the vat is **yekev** — the collecting trough beneath the winepress."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the specifics"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because everyone standing there had done the counting themselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haggai is not describing a famine. He is describing the ordinary experience of expecting a figure and finding a smaller one, year after year, which is a much more familiar thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three blows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I struck all the work of your hands with blight and mildew and hail**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shiddaphon** — scorching, the hot east wind that withers standing grain. **Yeraqon** — mildew, yellowing. **Barad** — hail."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two are slow and one is sudden. Three ways for a harvest to fail."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the list comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 28, in the warnings: *the LORD will strike you with… blight and mildew*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Solomon's prayer at the dedication of the first temple: *when there is famine in the land, pestilence or blight or mildew… whatever prayer is made by anyone**, then hear in heaven*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haggai's audience would have recognised the vocabulary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Yet you did not turn to Me**,\" declares the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ve'eyn-etchem elai** — and you were not toward Me."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The harvests failed and nobody drew the connection. That is the charge, and it is the last line of the paragraph."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the point of the striking was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To be noticed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same complaint runs through Amos: *I gave you cleanness of teeth in all your cities… **yet you have not returned to Me**, says the LORD* — five times in one chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bag with holes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who earns wages, earns wages **to put into a bag with holes**.* See the note on Haggai 1:6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image from the first sermon. Not that there were no wages. That they did not stay in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Is the seed still in the barn?** As yet the vine, the fig tree, the pomegranate, and the olive tree have not yielded fruit.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "December. Nothing sown has come up, and the four fruit trees named are all still bare."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**From this day I will bless you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise is dated to a day when the evidence is entirely absent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What had changed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three months of work on a building that was nowhere near finished, and would not be for four more years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blessing is not attached to completion."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *Yet you did not turn to Me.* The harvests had been failing for years and nobody put the two things next to each other — which is what the whole book asks them to do."}]}, "Hebrews 1:1–3": {"title": "\"He sat down\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The opening sentence of the letter, and it ends on a posture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How God used to speak"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In piece after piece, and in one way after another, and through the prophets.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in Greek, both beginning with the same sound — in many portions and in many manners. Fragments, over centuries, by dreams and visions and voices and signs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about that is criticised. It is simply described as partial."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How He speaks now"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has spoken to us **in a Son**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No article in the Greek — not *through the Son* as one more prophet in the line, but in the manner of a Son. The mode of communication has changed, not just the messenger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is said about Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God's own glory, blazing out. He is exactly what God is, pressed into view.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second image is from a seal pressed into wax — the exact impression, every feature reproduced. The word is **charakter**, which is where our word comes from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He carries the whole of it along on one powerful word.* Not holding the universe up like a pillar. Carrying it forward, by speaking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when He had cleansed our sins away Himself, **He sat down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Priests in the tabernacle did not sit. There was no chair in it — the work never finished, because the sacrifices never finished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This priest sat down. The letter comes back to it again and again, and it is the whole argument in one verb: the sitting proves the work is over."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where He sat.** *At the right hand of Majesty, in the heights.* Not merely resting — the position of authority beside the throne. Finished, and seated in the place of rule."}]}, "Hebrews 10:1–2": {"title": "Only a shadow", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer's argument turns on a question he expects the reader to answer for themselves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The law **threw a shadow** of the good that was coming; **it never showed the thing itself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **skia** (shadow) against **eikon** — the image, the actual form."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A shadow is not a lie. It is cast by something real, has the shape of it, and proves it is there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It also cannot be used. Nobody sits in the shade of a shadow."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law was a shadow of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The good that was coming.* Paul says the same in Colossians about food, drink, feasts and sabbaths: *these are a shadow of things to come, **but the substance is of Christ**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repetition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **year after year**, with **the same sacrifices offered without end**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Day of Atonement, every year, the same ritual. And the daily offerings, morning and evening, for centuries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it could not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It could **never bring the people who came to worship all the way to completion**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **teleiosai** — to bring to the end, to perfect, to finish."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The problem named is not that the sacrifices were fraudulent. It is that they never arrived anywhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If it could, **would not the offerings have stopped?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer puts the test in the reader's hands. A treatment that works is discontinued."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The temple was still running when this letter was written, and the smoke went up every day, which was the visible proof of his point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Once the worshipers had been cleansed, **they would not have gone on carrying any sense of their sins**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **suneidesin hamartion** — a consciousness of sins."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the writer says the arrangement failed to remove. Not the guilt in the abstract. The awareness of it in the worshipper."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sacrifices did instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *but in those sacrifices **there is a reminder of sins every year**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anamnesis** — the same word Jesus uses of the bread: *do this in remembrance of Me.* See the note on Luke 22:19–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One annual reminder of sins; one ongoing remembrance of Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blunt statement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it is **not possible that the blood of bulls and goats could take away sins**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer says it outright, about a system God Himself commanded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the argument goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Into Psalm 40 in the mouth of Christ — *sacrifices and offerings You did not want, but a body You prepared for Me.* See the notes on Hebrews 10:5–7 and Hebrews 10:8–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the priests who stand daily and the one who sat down. See the note on Hebrews 10:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Jeremiah: *their sins and their lawless deeds I will remember no more.* See the note on Hebrews 10:15–17."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The test he offers.** If it worked, it would have stopped. He does not argue the point further — he asks the question and moves on to what did stop."}]}, "Hebrews 10:10": {"title": "One time and never again", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and every phrase in it is load-bearing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A quotation from Psalm 40, put in Christ's mouth:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Sacrifice and offering You did not desire, **but a body You have prepared for Me**… Behold, I have come — in the volume of the book it is written of Me — **to do Your will, O God**.\"* See the note on Psalms 40:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the writer's comment: *He takes away the first that He may establish the second*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The will"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it is **by that will of His** that we have been made holy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **en ho thelemati** — in which will."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not by an achievement. By a decision, and the decision quoted two verses earlier is *I have come to do Your will*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We **have been made holy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hegiasmenoi esmen** — a perfect passive periphrastic. Having been sanctified, and standing in that state."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Perfect tense: done, and the result continues."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Passive: done to us."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Through **the body of Jesus Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not His teaching, not His example. The body — the one the psalm said was prepared."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The frequency"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Offered **one time and never again**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ephapax** — once for all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter's key word. It uses it three times: *He did this once for all when He offered up Himself*, *He entered the Most Holy Place once for all*, and here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why frequency is the argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole chapter runs on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the law, having a shadow of the good things to come… can never with these same sacrifices, which they offer continually year by year, make those who approach perfect.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For then would they not have ceased to be offered? **For the worshippers, once purified, would have had no more consciousness of sins**.* See the note on Hebrews 10:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The annual repetition was the proof that it had not worked. A cure that has to be re-administered every year is not a cure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But in those sacrifices there is a **reminder of sins every year**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Day of Atonement was an annual memo."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast in posture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And every priest **stands** ministering daily and offering repeatedly the same sacrifices, which can never take away sins.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But this Man, after He had offered one sacrifice for sins forever, **sat down** at the right hand of God.* See the note on Hebrews 10:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There were no chairs in the tabernacle. The work was never finished, so nobody sat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For by one offering **He has perfected forever those who are being sanctified**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two tenses in one clause. Perfected — perfect tense, finished. Being sanctified — present, in progress."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are true of the same people at the same time, and the letter puts them in the same sentence without embarrassment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The witness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the Holy Spirit also witnesses to us; for after He had said before, \"**This is the covenant that I will make with them… I will put My laws into their hearts**\"… then He adds, \"**Their sins and their lawless deeds I will remember no more**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now where there is remission of these, **there is no longer an offering for sin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument closes itself. If the record is not kept, the offering has no function."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, brethren, having boldness to enter the Holiest by the blood of Jesus… **let us draw near**.* See the note on Hebrews 10:22."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the number matters.** A sacrifice repeated annually is an annual reminder that it did not work. The letter's whole case rests on one word: *ephapax*."}]}, "Hebrews 10:11–13": {"title": "The priest who sat down", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The whole argument of the letter turns on a piece of furniture that was not there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The old arrangement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every priest **stands** ministering daily and offering **repeatedly the same sacrifices**, which **can never take away sins**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words doing the work: stands, daily, repeatedly, never."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was missing from the tabernacle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There were no chairs in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lampstand, the table of showbread, the altar of incense, the ark — the specifications in Exodus are exhaustive, down to the tongs and the snuffdishes, and no seat is mentioned anywhere."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A priest's work was never finished, so there was nothing to sit on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But this Man, after He had offered **one sacrifice for sins forever**, **sat down** at the right hand of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**One**, and **forever**, and then the posture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sitting is the proof. You sit when the job is done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He is doing now"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From that time waiting till **His enemies are made His footstool**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 110, which Jesus had quoted at the Pharisees Himself: *the LORD said to my Lord, sit at My right hand until I make Your enemies Your footstool.* See the note on Matthew 22:41–46."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For by **one offering He has perfected forever** those who are being sanctified.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two tenses in one sentence, and both are true at once. **Perfected forever** — done, complete, permanent. **Being sanctified** — in progress."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The finished thing and the ongoing thing are not in competition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what the Spirit witnesses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer quotes Jeremiah 31: *their sins and their lawless deeds **I will remember no more**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then his own conclusion: *now where there is remission of these, **there is no longer an offering for sin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If the debt is forgotten, there is nothing left to pay."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, brethren, having boldness to enter the Holiest by the blood of Jesus… **let us draw near**.* See the note on Hebrews 10:19–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sitting down is what makes the drawing near possible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison the letter keeps making"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Standing priests, repeated sacrifices, and a room nobody could enter except one man once a year. See the note on Hebrews 9:6–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Against one offering, one seat, and *let us draw near* addressed to everybody."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the same posture turns up.** *And He was received up into heaven, and **sat down** at the right hand of God.* Mark's last chapter. And in Revelation, John sees Him standing among the lampstands — seated at the throne, walking among the churches."}]}, "Hebrews 10:14": {"title": "Perfected forever, being made holy", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and it holds two tenses that most people would not put together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By **that one offering** He has **brought to completion, forever**, the people **He is making holy**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Brought to completion, forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **teteleioken eis to dienekes** — a perfect tense, and a phrase meaning *continuously, for all time*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Perfect tense: done, and standing done. Nothing is pending."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Teleioo** is the word the writer uses throughout — the same root as *it is finished*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people **He is making holy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tous hagiazomenous** — a present passive participle. Being sanctified, ongoing, in progress."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two together"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Completed forever, and being made holy — in one sentence, about the same people, by the same offering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer sets them side by side and does not reconcile them. One describes standing and one describes process, and both are attributed to the single offering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it answers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's argument about the old arrangement: sacrifices *year after year* that *could never bring the people who came to worship all the way to completion*. See the note on Hebrews 10:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same verb, negated. What the repetition could not do, one offering did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"One offering\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter says it four ways in a few verses:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By that will we have been sanctified through the offering of the body of Jesus Christ **once for all***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But this Man, after He had offered **one sacrifice for sins forever**, sat down at the right hand of God.* See the note on Hebrews 10:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ephapax** — once, finally, never repeated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the Holy Spirit also witnesses to us; for after He had said before, \"**This is the covenant that I will make with them**…\"* — and then Jeremiah, and *their sins and their lawless deeds I will remember no more*. See the note on Hebrews 10:15–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **where there is remission of these, there is no longer an offering for sin**.* See the note on Hebrews 10:18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument closes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the writer goes from here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, brethren, having boldness to enter the Holiest… **let us draw near**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything after verse 18 is invitation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for the people"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people He is making holy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is the same root as **hagios**, holy, saint. The New Testament's usual word for believers is the participle of this verb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are called the sanctified — a name for something being done to them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two tenses.** Finished and continuing. The writer puts a perfect and a present in one sentence and attaches both to a single offering — one is what they are, and the other is what is happening."}]}, "Hebrews 10:15–17": {"title": "\"I will remember no more\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer quotes Jeremiah for the second time in the letter, and this time he quotes it in two halves with a joint in the middle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is called the speaker"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **Holy Spirit** tells us the same thing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah wrote it. The writer of Hebrews attributes it to the Spirit, in the present tense — *tells us*, not *told them*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"First He says\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is the covenant I will make with them once those days are over, says the Lord. **I will put My laws inside their hearts**, and I will **write them on their minds**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is God's. He puts, He writes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Compare the first time: *written by the very finger of God*, on stone, handed down a mountain. Same author, same hand, and the surface has changed. See the note on Exodus 31:18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Then He adds\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**And their sins and their lawless deeds I will remember no more**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer separates the two clauses deliberately — *first He says… then He adds* — so the second one lands on its own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Greek"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ou me mnestho** — a double negative with the subjunctive, the strongest form of denial available in the language. Not *I will try not to*, and not *I will overlook*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a promise not to bring something to mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Omniscience and remembering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse is not a claim about God's memory failing. To *remember* in Hebrew usage is to act on — God *remembered Noah*, and the wind passed over the water; God *remembered* Hannah, and she conceived."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not remembering is not acting on. The record is not consulted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the writer concludes from it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **where there is remission of these, there is no longer an offering for sin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is his argument, and it is an argument about sacrifices, not about feelings. If the sins are not remembered, there is nothing left for another offering to address."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Day of Atonement came round every year precisely because there was a remembering — *in those sacrifices there is a reminder of sins every year.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The old covenant's wording"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus 34, the LORD passing before Moses: *forgiving iniquity and transgression and sin, **by no means clearing the guilty**.* See the note on Exodus 34:5–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are God speaking about Himself, three books and one covenant apart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, brethren, **having boldness to enter the Holiest** by the blood of Jesus… let us draw near with a true heart in full assurance of faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for boldness is **parrhesia** — freedom of speech, the right to say anything in a place you belong. See the note on Hebrews 10:19–22."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the quotation comes from.** Jeremiah 31, written while Jerusalem was falling to Babylon for exactly the sins in question. The promise not to remember was made in the middle of the consequences."}]}, "Hebrews 10:18": {"title": "\"No offering left to make\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and it is the conclusion of ten chapters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **where those have been forgiven**, there is **no offering for sin left to make**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *now where there is remission of these, there is no longer an offering for sin*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What \"those\" refers to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The quotation immediately before it: *\"and their sins and their lawless deeds **I will remember no more**.\"* See the note on Hebrews 10:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer quotes Jeremiah and then draws one inference from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The logic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If the sins are not being remembered, there is nothing for another offering to be about."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An offering addresses something. Remove the something, and the offering has no subject."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer does not argue that further sacrifice is forbidden, ineffective, or disrespectful. He says there is nothing left for it to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Remission** — the Greek is **aphesis**, a sending away, a release. It is the word used of prisoners being let go and of debts being cancelled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is also the word in the Greek Old Testament for the Year of Jubilee."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter has established"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["The law was a shadow, and the repetition proved it never finished the job. See the note on Hebrews 10:1–2.", "The sacrifices reopened the account annually rather than closing it. See the note on Hebrews 10:3–4.", "A body was prepared instead. See the note on Hebrews 10:5–7.", "The first arrangement was cancelled to establish the second. See the note on Hebrews 10:8–9.", "Every priest stands daily; this one sat down. See the note on Hebrews 10:11–13.", "The Spirit testifies: *I will remember no more.*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then this verse, and the argument is closed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word **therefore**, and the whole tone of the letter changes:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, brethren, having **boldness to enter the Holiest** by the blood of Jesus, by a new and living way which He consecrated for us, through the veil… **let us draw near**.* See the note on Hebrews 10:19–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything after this verse is invitation. Everything before it is argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three \"let us\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us draw near… let us hold fast the confession of our hope without wavering… and let us consider one another.* See the note on Hebrews 10:23–25."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three, in a row, and all three rest on this sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it mattered to the readers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The temple was standing. The offerings were being made daily, a few days' walk away, and the people reading this letter were under pressure to go back to them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's answer is not that the temple is unpleasant or that they will be disappointed. It is that there is nothing there to make an offering about."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The shortest verse in the argument.** Ten chapters of priesthood, covenant, oath and sacrifice, and it comes down to one clause: if the sins are not remembered, nothing remains to be offered."}]}, "Hebrews 10:19–21": {"title": "\"A road that is alive\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The letter's argument turns here from explanation to invitation, and it opens with a place nobody was allowed to enter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the way leads"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The way into the holiest place is open to us now, and we can walk in without a flinch.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The holiest place is behind the curtain — one man, one day a year, with blood and smoke. See the note on Leviticus 16:11–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word behind *without a flinch* is **parrhesia**, boldness, the freedom of somebody with the right to speak in the room. The same word the letter used at 4:16 about the throne."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What makes it open"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because of the blood of Jesus.* Not because of improvement, and not because the holiness of the place has been reduced."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"A road that is alive\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He opened up a road for us that no one had ever walked before, a road that is alive.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two claims. It is **new** — the Greek word originally meant freshly slaughtered, and came to mean recent, unused. Nobody has been down it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is **living**. A road is not usually alive. The writer is stretching the image on purpose, because the way is a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Through the curtain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It runs straight through the curtain, which is His own body.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records the temple curtain torn in two from top to bottom at the moment Jesus died. Hebrews puts the two together: the torn veil and the torn body are one event, and what they produce is a road."}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on Matthew 27:51–53."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The priest at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And we have a great Priest set over God's household.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things are now in place — an open way, a road that lives, and a priest already inside. The next verse begins *let us draw near*, and the rest of the chapter is what to do about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What stood in front of the curtain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The golden altar of incense, immediately outside the veil, with smoke going over the top of it into the room beyond."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The high priest carried a censer through on the Day of Atonement precisely so the cloud would cover the mercy seat *lest he die*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The incense was what made the approach survivable. See the note on Exodus 30:34–38."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is in the room now"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And we have **a great Priest set over God's household**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not waiting outside once a year. Inside, permanently, and the way to Him is described as open."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why \"flinch\" is the right word.** Everything in the old arrangement taught people to hesitate: the curtain, the once-a-year, the incense so the priest would not die. The letter is not asking readers to feel casual. It is telling them the reason for the hesitation has been dealt with."}]}, "Hebrews 10:22": {"title": "Let us come close", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first of three *let us* sentences, and everything the letter has argued for nine chapters lands on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, brethren, having **boldness to enter the Holiest by the blood of Jesus**, by a new and living way which He consecrated for us, **through the veil, that is, His flesh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *having a High Priest over the house of God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The *therefore* carries the whole letter. Everything in this verse is a consequence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The invitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **let us come close**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **proserchometha** — let us draw near. The word for approaching an altar or a throne."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the exact thing forbidden at the bush: *do not draw near here*. See the note on Exodus 3:4–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And forbidden at Sinai: *set bounds for the people all around*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "With nothing held back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **nothing held back**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **alethines kardias** — a true heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a good heart. A genuine one, without pretence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The certainty"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Completely certain of what we trust.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **en plerophoria pisteos** — in full assurance of faith."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Plerophoria** — full carrying, complete conviction. The word Paul uses of Abraham being *fully convinced that what He had promised He was also able to perform*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first washing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hearts **sprinkled clean of a bad conscience**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **rherantismenoi** — a perfect passive participle. Having been sprinkled, and standing sprinkled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sprinkling is the priestly action. Blood was sprinkled on the altar, on the mercy seat, on the people at Sinai, and on Aaron's garments."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is sprinkled here is the conscience."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter has said about that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How much more shall the blood of Christ… **cleanse your conscience** from dead works to serve the living God?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the argument at the start of chapter 10: *for then would they not have ceased to be offered? For the worshippers, once purified, would have had **no more consciousness of sins**.* See the note on Hebrews 10:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The repeated sacrifice was the problem, because a sacrifice repeated annually is an annual reminder."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second washing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bodies **rinsed in clean water**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **lelousmenoi** — bathed, washed all over. A different verb from the sprinkling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priests were washed once at their consecration, entirely, and after that only their hands and feet at the laver."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus makes the same distinction: *he who is bathed needs only to wash his feet, but is completely clean*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Inside and outside. Conscience and body. Sprinkled and bathed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both participles are perfect and passive. Both were done to the reader before the invitation was issued."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three sentences"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us **draw near** with a true heart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us **hold fast** the confession of our hope without wavering, for **He who promised is faithful**.* See the note on Hebrews 10:23–25."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us **consider one another** in order to stir up love and good works.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Faith, hope, love, in that order, in three consecutive verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about worthiness. The two participles have already settled it, and the letter spends nine chapters establishing that they are settled by somebody else's work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the veil was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Through the veil, that is, His flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The curtain in front of the Holy of Holies, which nobody but the high priest passed, once a year, with blood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And behold, the veil of the temple was torn in two from top to bottom.* See the note on Mark 15:33–36."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb.** *Draw near* — the one thing Moses was told not to do at the bush, issued as an invitation, on the strength of two things already done to the reader."}]}, "Hebrews 10:23–25": {"title": "Hold on to each other", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three instructions in a row, and the reason attached to the first one is not about the reader."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they come from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word **therefore** in verse 19, resting on the whole argument that ends *there is no offering for sin left to make*. See the note on Hebrews 10:18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three *let us* clauses follow: draw near, hold fast, and consider one another."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Holding on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us **not let go of what we openly believe**, and let us **not wobble**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *let us hold fast the confession of our hope **without wavering*** — **aklines**, unbending, not leaning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The One who made the promise **keeps His word**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pistos** — faithful, reliable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The steadiness asked for is not generated by the person holding on. The reason given is about the character of the one who promised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter has already spent a chapter on it: an oath, sworn on Himself, because there was nobody greater. See the notes on Hebrews 6:13–14 and Hebrews 6:16–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us **give some thought to one another**, and **look for ways to spark love and good work** in each other.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *give thought* is **katanoeo** — to observe closely, to study."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *spark* is **paroxusmos** — a sharp stirring up, a provoking. Our word *paroxysm* comes from it. In Acts it describes the sharp disagreement between Paul and Barnabas over Mark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer uses a word for irritation and points it at love and good work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it requires"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Attention. You cannot provoke somebody toward anything without knowing what moves them, which is why the observing comes first in the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not **drop out of gathering together**, the way **some have gotten into the habit** of doing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **episunagoge** — a gathering together, from the same root as synagogue."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Some have gotten into the habit** — the writer names it as an existing pattern, not a hypothetical."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they were dropping out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter says elsewhere: *you had compassion on me in my chains, and joyfully accepted the plundering of your goods.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Meeting had cost them property and put them in reach of the authorities."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What to do instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep **encouraging each other**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parakalountes** — from the same root as **parakletos**, the word for the Helper. See the note on John 14:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are asked to do for each other what the Spirit is called."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The frequency"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All the more **as you see the Day coming closer**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pressure that made them stop is the reason given for doing it more."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The warning about sinning wilfully after receiving the knowledge of the truth. See the notes on Hebrews 10:26–27 and Hebrews 10:28–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer puts *do not stop meeting* directly before it. The drifting he is warning against starts with people quietly not turning up."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of the three.** Draw near to God, hold on to the confession, and pay attention to each other — and the only reason supplied for any of them is that the One who promised keeps His word."}]}, "Hebrews 10:26–27": {"title": "No sacrifice left", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second great warning of the letter, and it uses the same argument as the good news eight verses earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being written to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrew believers under pressure to return to the temple. The whole letter is written against that one move, and the temple was still standing when it was sent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sin"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If we **choose to keep on sinning after we have received the truth and come to know it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *if we sin **wilfully*** — **hekousios**, deliberately, of one's own choice. It is a rare word, and it appears here and once in Peter, of shepherding willingly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After we have received the knowledge of the truth* — **epignosis**, full knowledge. Not ignorance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No **sacrifice for sins is left**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *there no longer remains a sacrifice for sins*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The identical clause, eight verses earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And where those have been forgiven, **there is no offering for sin left to make**.* See the note on Hebrews 10:18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same statement, in nearly the same words, and there it is the conclusion of the good news."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter has argued that there is one sacrifice, offered once, and no other. That is a comfort to a man resting on it and a terror to a man walking away from it — and the fact is the same fact either way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is described instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Only **a terror of waiting for judgment**, and **a fire, burning with His anger, that will swallow up everyone who sets himself against God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *a certain fearful expectation of judgment, and fiery indignation*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase *swallow up the adversaries* comes from Isaiah 26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sin in view is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse defines it, and it is not an ordinary lapse: *walked all over the Son of God*, *treated the blood of the covenant as something common*, *insulted the Spirit of grace*. See the note on Hebrews 10:28–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three descriptions, and all three are about repudiating the sacrifice rather than falling short of a standard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not drop out of gathering together, the way some have gotten into the habit of doing.* See the note on Hebrews 10:23–25."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer sets the warning right after a description of people quietly ceasing to turn up. He is tracing where a road goes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **recall the former days** in which, after you were illuminated, you endured a great struggle with sufferings… **you had compassion on me in my chains**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore **do not cast away your confidence**, which has great reward.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *\"**But we are not of those who draw back to perdition, but of those who believe** to the saving of the soul.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer includes himself and them in the second category, five verses after the warning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern in the letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does this every time. Chapter 6's warning is followed immediately by *but beloved, we are convinced of better things concerning you.* See the note on Hebrews 6:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Warning, then reassurance, in that order, twice."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The sentence that does both jobs.** *There is no sacrifice left.* The letter says it twice — once as the reason nothing more is needed, and once as the reason there is nowhere else to go."}]}, "Hebrews 10:28–29": {"title": "Three things done to the blood", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer defines the sin he has just warned about, and the definition is entirely about how the sacrifice is treated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Anyone who **threw out the law of Moses died with no mercy shown him**, on the word of **two or three witnesses**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy's standard for a capital case. No conviction on one voice — the same rule that governed the cities of refuge. See the note on Numbers 35:16–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument's shape"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How much **heavier a punishment** do you think a man will deserve.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer uses this form repeatedly — the lesser to the greater. If that was true under the old arrangement, how much more under this one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three descriptions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one is about the Son and His blood:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Walked all over the Son of God.** The Greek is **katapateo** — to trample underfoot. It is the word used of salt that has lost its savour, and of pearls before swine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Treated the blood of the covenant as something common.** The Greek is **koinon** — common, ordinary, unclean. It is the word Peter uses on the rooftop: *I have never eaten anything common or unclean.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To call the blood common is to rank it with the blood of bulls and goats, which the writer has just said could never take sins away. See the note on Hebrews 10:3–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Insulted the Spirit of grace.** The Greek is **enubrizo** — to treat with contempt, to outrage. It appears once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The blood **that made him holy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *by which he was sanctified*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer says it about the person being warned. Whatever else the sentence does, it does not describe someone the blood never touched."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not on the list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Failure, weakness, relapse, or a particular sin. Nothing in the three descriptions is about behaviour falling short."}, {"k": "p", "t": "All three are about the standing of the sacrifice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter is arguing against"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Going back to the temple. The offerings were still being made when this was written, a few days' walk away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man returning to that altar is saying the one on Golgotha was not sufficient — which is what *treating the blood as common* amounts to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For we know Him who said, \"**Vengeance is Mine, I will repay**,\" says the Lord. And again, \"The LORD will judge His people.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is a fearful thing to fall into the hands of the living God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **recall the former days** in which, after you were illuminated, you endured a great struggle with sufferings.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer turns immediately to what they had already come through, and ends the chapter:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **we are not of those who draw back** to perdition, **but of those who believe** to the saving of the soul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts himself and them in the second category."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice in the letter: a hard warning, followed at once by a statement that he does not think it describes them. See the notes on Hebrews 6:4–6 and Hebrews 6:9–10."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the three verbs have in common.** Trampling, calling common, insulting — every one is something done to the sacrifice rather than something done wrong by the person."}]}, "Hebrews 10:3–4": {"title": "A reminder of sins", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two short verses, and they say what the sacrifices actually accomplished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Instead, those sacrifices **drag the sins back into memory, year after year**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anamnesis** — a calling to mind, a remembrance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word Jesus uses of the bread: *do this **in remembrance** of Me.* See the note on Luke 22:19–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two remembrances, of two different things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the annual repetition mattered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's argument in the verses before is that a treatment which worked would have stopped. See the note on Hebrews 10:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Day of Atonement came round every year, which means the account was reopened every year."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because there is **no way that the blood of bulls and goats can take sins away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **adunaton** — impossible. The same word the writer uses of God lying, of pleasing Him without faith, and of renewing an apostate. See the note on Hebrews 6:4–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bluntness of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it about a system God commanded, in Leviticus, in detail, with the species and the handling of the blood specified."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not accusing the law of fraud. He is saying what it was for: *the law threw a shadow of the good that was coming.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the animals could not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Take away** — the Greek is **aphairein**, to lift off and remove."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They covered. The Hebrew **kaphar** is to cover over, and that is what the mercy seat did — the lid over the tablets, with blood on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Covered is not the same as gone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John the Baptist said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Behold! **The Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek there is **airo** — to lift up and carry off. The same root the writer says the bulls and goats could not do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the argument goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Straight into Psalm 40 in the mouth of Christ — *sacrifices and offerings You did not want, but a body You prepared for Me.* See the notes on Hebrews 10:5–7 and Hebrews 10:8–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the priest who sat down, and Jeremiah's *their sins I will remember no more*. See the notes on Hebrews 10:11–13 and Hebrews 10:15–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two rememberings, side by side"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old arrangement produced an annual reminder of sins."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The new covenant's terms are *I will remember no more*, and the meal instituted under it is a remembrance of a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter's argument ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And where those have been forgiven, **there is no offering for sin left to make**.* See the note on Hebrews 10:18."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the sacrifices were for.** Not to clear the account. The writer says they reopened it, once a year, and that the impossibility was in the material."}]}, "Hebrews 10:5–7": {"title": "\"You shaped a body for Me instead\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer puts a psalm into Christ's mouth and dates it: *as He came into the world*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 40, written by David, quoted here as words spoken at the incarnation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Sacrifices and offerings **were not what You wanted** — **You shaped a body for Me instead**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four categories"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sacrifices, offerings, whole animals burned, offerings made for sin.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four of the five Levitical offerings, named one after another and all dismissed in the same breath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one was commanded by God in Leviticus. The writer is not saying they were wrong. He is saying they were never the thing wanted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"None of it gave You any pleasure\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *You did not delight in them*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophets had said the same for centuries — *I desire mercy and not sacrifice*, *to obey is better than sacrifice* — while the altar kept burning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The body"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of Psalm 40 reads *my ears You have opened* — literally *dug*. The Greek Old Testament the writer is quoting has *a body You have prepared for me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ear is the part of the body that receives the command; the writer's version names the whole."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in Exodus, the servant who refuses to go free has his ear pierced at the doorpost and serves forever. See the note on Exodus 21:2–6, and on Psalms 40:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Here I am\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So I said, **Here I am**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer Abraham gives, and Moses at the bush, and Samuel in the night, and Isaiah in the temple. It is the standard reply of a person reporting for a task."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"It stands written about Me in the scroll\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *in the volume of the book it is written of Me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said before the ministry begins, about a book already finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have come to do what You want, God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That sentence is the entire point of the quotation. The writer says so in the next verse: *He takes away the first that He may establish the second.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first is the sacrifices. The second is the will."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it buys"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By that will **we have been sanctified** through the offering of the body of Jesus Christ **once for all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ephapax** — once, finally, not to be repeated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the priests who stand daily, and the One who sat down. See the note on Hebrews 10:11–13."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is exchanged.** Animals were brought, killed and burned, year after year, and the writer says none of it was ever wanted. What was wanted was a body and somebody willing to say *here I am* in it."}]}, "Hebrews 10:8–9": {"title": "\"He cancels the first to put the second in its place\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer takes the psalm he has just quoted and separates it into two halves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The method"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*First He says… **then** He says.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has quoted Psalm 40 whole in the verses before. See the note on Hebrews 10:5–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Now he goes back over it and splits it, because the order of the two statements is the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Sacrifices and offerings, whole animals burned, offerings made for sin — **You did not want them, and they gave You no pleasure**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four categories, listed twice in the chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause he adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **those are the very things the law requires**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer inserts this himself. It is not in the psalm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything just dismissed was commanded in Leviticus, by God, in detail — the species, the age, the handling of the blood, the portions burned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He wants the reader to notice that the psalm is dismissing what the law demands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He says, \"**Here I am. I have come to do what You want**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is the standard reply of a person reporting for duty — the answer Abraham, Moses, Samuel and Isaiah all give."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **cancels the first arrangement** in order to **put the second one in its place**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anairei** — takes away, removes, abolishes. It is also the word for putting a person to death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **histesin** — sets up, causes to stand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs, and the second is the purpose of the first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being replaced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not sacrifice by nothing. Sacrifice by a will."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first arrangement was a system of offerings. The second is a person saying *I have come to do what You want*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it has to be one or the other"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer does not allow both to run. The first is removed **in order that** the second may stand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek makes the purpose explicit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it accomplishes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *by that will **we have been sanctified** through the offering of the body of Jesus Christ **once for all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**By that will** — not by the blood alone, in the writer's phrasing. By the willingness that carried it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Once for all** — **ephapax**, once, finally, never repeated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking in the psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David wrote it. The writer of Hebrews puts it in Christ's mouth *as He came into the world*, and reads the whole thing as spoken at the incarnation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does this repeatedly in the letter — Psalm 2, Psalm 45, Psalm 110, Psalm 40."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the readers were being pulled back toward"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The temple, which was still standing and still functioning. The whole letter is written to people considering going back to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument here is that the first arrangement was removed by the second, and not by neglect."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two halves are.** A rejection of offerings and a statement of willingness, in the same psalm, in that order. The writer says the second one is why the first one ends."}]}, "Hebrews 11:1–3": {"title": "The shape of faith", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The nearest thing to a definition of faith in Scripture, and it is built out of two legal words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What we are **hoping for**, faith **already has hold of**; what **no one has ever seen**, faith is **sure of**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *already has hold of* is **hupostasis** — literally *that which stands under*. In the papyri of the period it is a title deed, the document establishing ownership of property you may never have visited."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *sure of* is **elegchos** — the word for evidence, proof, the thing that convinces a court."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Faith is described as the deed and the evidence. Not a mood, and not optimism."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is attached to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two objects, and neither is available to the senses: what is hoped for, and what has never been seen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Faith is only ever exercised toward something not yet in hand. Once you have it, the faith has finished its work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a leap in the dark, on this definition. A deed and a proof are both about certainty; they simply concern something not yet visible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is what **the people of long ago were praised for**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest of the chapter is that list — Abel, Enoch, Noah, Abraham, Sarah, Isaac, Jacob, Joseph, Moses, Rahab, Gideon, David, and the ones at the end who were sawn in two and wandered in sheepskins."}, {"k": "p", "t": "None of them are commended for achievements. They are commended for believing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the chapter says of all of them: *these all died in faith, **not having received the promises**, but having seen them afar off.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The example given first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why we understand that **everything there is came into being at God's word**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of everything the writer could have opened with, he takes creation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody watched it. There were no witnesses to Genesis 1, and no experiment can be run. It is held by faith, and the writer treats that as the model case."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **what we can see did not come from anything that can be seen**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The visible came out of the invisible — which is the same relationship this chapter says faith has to its object."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Without faith it is impossible to please Him, for he who comes to God must believe that He is, and that He is a rewarder of those who diligently seek Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things to believe: that He exists, and what He is like toward those who look for Him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the roll of faith ends.** *And all these, having obtained a good testimony through faith, **did not receive the promise**, God having provided something better for us, that they should not be made perfect apart from us.* The list waits for the reader."}]}, "Hebrews 11:17–22": {"title": "The test, and three deathbeds", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four generations, and three of the four entries are about dying men still talking about the future."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Abraham"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why Abraham, **when he was put to the test, offered Isaac up**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is perfect: he **has offered**. As far as the writer is concerned it was done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contradiction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The man who **had been handed the promises** was offering up **his only son**, the son about whom he had been told, **It is through Isaac that your line will be named**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer states the problem baldly. The promise and the command are about the same boy, and they cannot both stand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Only son** — the Greek is **monogenes**. Ishmael existed. Isaac is the only son of the promise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abraham concluded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He had **worked it out that God could raise the dead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **logizomai** — to reckon, to calculate. It is the same accounting word James uses. See the note on James 1:2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham did arithmetic. If the promise runs through Isaac and Isaac is to die, then there is one remaining possibility, and he settled on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said as much to the servants at the foot of the hill: *stay here with the donkey; the lad and I will go yonder and worship, **and we will come back to you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No resurrection had ever happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In a manner of speaking\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And in a manner of speaking, **that is how he got him back**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *in a parable* — **en parabole**. Isaac did not die, but the writer says Abraham received him back as from the dead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days' journey with the boy as good as dead in his mind, and then the voice from heaven. See the note on Genesis 22:11–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Isaac"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why Isaac blessed Jacob and Esau **with an eye on what was still to come**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one blessing recorded of Isaac is the one he was tricked into. He gave it to the wrong son in the dark, and when he found out he *trembled exceedingly* — and then said *I have blessed him, and indeed he shall be blessed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews calls that faith, and lists it without mentioning the deception."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Jacob"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why Jacob, **dying**, blessed **each of Joseph's sons**, and **worshiped bent over the head of his staff**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He crossed his hands deliberately and put the right hand on the younger — and when Joseph tried to correct him, refused."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Bent over his staff** — the writer takes the detail from the Greek Old Testament. An old man in Egypt, blind, propped on a stick, disposing of land he did not own in a country he would not be buried in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Joseph"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why Joseph, at the end of his life, **spoke of Israel going out**, and **left instructions about his bones**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Second in command of Egypt, who had been in the country for eighty years, whose sons were Egyptian-born."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His last recorded words are that they are leaving, and *you shall carry up my bones from here*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The coffin sat in Egypt for four hundred years, and Moses took it out on the night of the exodus. Joshua buried it at Shechem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the four have in common"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of them is credited with an achievement. The list is: a man who nearly killed his son, a man who was deceived, a man who could not see, and a man giving instructions about a funeral."}, {"k": "p", "t": "All four are commended for what they said about a future they would not be in."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the writer puts the emphasis.** Every entry contains a verb of speech or blessing. These are men handing something forward — and Hebrews calls the handing forward the faith."}]}, "Hebrews 11:23–29": {"title": "Moses, and the accounting he did", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Six sentences about Moses, each beginning the same way — *faith is why* — and the middle one is a calculation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the parents did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why Moses, newly born, was hidden by his parents for three months: they could see he was a beautiful child, and the king's order did not frighten them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter begins its Moses section with somebody else's faith. Two slaves defy an infanticide order and are credited with it before Moses does anything at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weighing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He weighed the disgrace of Christ against everything stored up in Egypt and found the disgrace worth more.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is a commercial one — reckoning, estimating value. This is not described as a sacrifice made against his own judgement. It is a man doing the arithmetic and concluding that one column was larger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the thing he weighed against a kingdom's treasury is called *the disgrace of Christ*, written of a man who lived over a thousand years before the crucifixion. The writer is claiming the same reproach runs through both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He could see the One nobody can see\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The line is a deliberate contradiction, and it explains how the arithmetic came out that way. Moses walked away from Egypt *without a thought for how furious the king would be* because the invisible was, to him, the more solid of the two."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The list ends in water.** *Faith is why they walked through the Red Sea as though it were dry ground — and when the Egyptians tried the same thing, the water swallowed them.* The same act, performed by two parties, with opposite outcomes. The writer's point across the whole chapter is that the difference was never in the walking."}]}, "Hebrews 11:30–31": {"title": "Rahab in the roll of honour", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two sentences, side by side, and the pairing is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The company she is in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter is a list: Abel, Enoch, Noah, Abraham, Sarah, Isaac, Jacob, Joseph, Moses. Patriarchs and prophets. Then the walls of Jericho — and then Rahab."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is one of only two women named in the chapter, and the writer does not soften her description. *Rahab the prostitute.* He could have written \"Rahab of Jericho.\" He did not, and neither did James."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What her faith is said to consist of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Since she had welcomed the spies in peace.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a confession, not a sacrifice, not a long obedience. She let two men in and did not turn them over. The writer treats a single act of hospitality toward God's people as the visible form of faith in her case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast in the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Did not die with those who refused to believe.* The Greek word is about disobedience through unbelief — refusing to be persuaded."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone in Jericho had the same evidence, as she says herself on the roof. The line the chapter draws is not between the informed and the ignorant. It is between those who were persuaded and those who were not."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**James, on the same woman.** *Was she not shown to be right by that?* James uses Rahab as his second example of faith proved by action — the first being Abraham. Between the two writers she is cited as an example of faith and as an example of works, and it is the same episode both times. The two arguments are not in conflict; see the note on James 2:17."}]}, "Hebrews 11:32–35": {"title": "The sentence that turns halfway through", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the passage people quote for triumph, and it is the passage that makes the chapter honest — because of where it stops."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Brought down kingdoms, dealt out justice, got hold of promises, shut lions' mouths, put fires out, got clear of the sword, were made strong where they had been weak, turned fierce in battle, sent foreign armies running. Women got their dead back alive.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every item is a deliverance. Faith that worked, visibly, in this life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And others were tortured and would not take the way out, so that they could rise to something better.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One word — *others* — and the same faith produces the opposite outcome. The writer does not change vocabulary, does not explain, does not suggest the second group had less of it. He puts both under the same heading."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read on past this block and it gets starker: mocked, flogged, chained, stoned, sawn in two, destitute, wandering in deserts. And then the verdict on all of them: *the world was not worthy of them.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any teaching that treats deliverance as the proof of faith has to account for the back half of this sentence, where people with the same faith were not delivered and the writer says so approvingly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Would not take the way out* is the detail that settles it. They were offered release and refused it — which means their situation was not a failure of faith but an exercise of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The end of the chapter.** *None of them received what was promised.* All of them — the delivered and the destroyed alike — are described as still waiting. Whatever this chapter is doing, it is not sorting people by outcome."}]}, "Hebrews 11:4–7": {"title": "Abel, Enoch, Noah", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three men before the flood, and the chapter's refrain starts here: *faith is why*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Abel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why Abel brought God **a better offering** than Cain did.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pleiona** — a fuller, greater sacrifice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis records the difference without explaining it: Cain brought of the fruit of the ground, Abel brought of the firstborn of his flock and of their fat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the offering did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **that offering is what marked him out as righteous**, since **God Himself vouched for what he gave**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The righteousness follows the offering, and the testimony is God's rather than the neighbours'."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis says *the LORD respected Abel and his offering* — the man and the gift, in that order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He has not stopped speaking\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is **dead, and he has not stopped speaking**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first man to die, and the writer puts him in the present tense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis has God saying *the voice of your brother's blood cries out to Me from the ground.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer picks it up again in chapter 12 — the blood of sprinkling *that speaks better things than that of Abel*. Abel's blood asks for justice; the other asks for something else. See the note on Hebrews 12:18–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Enoch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why Enoch was **taken away without ever meeting death**; **nobody could find him**, because God had taken him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis gives him six verses. *And Enoch walked with God, and he was not, for God took him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is one of two men in Scripture who did not die. The other is Elijah."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was said of him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before he was taken, this had been said of him: **God was glad of him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *he pleased God*. That is the whole of the character reference."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule drawn out of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **nobody makes God glad without faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer stops the narrative to state the principle, and then defines what the faith consists of:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Anyone coming to God has to believe **He is really there**.*", "*And that **He pays back the ones who go after Him in earnest**.*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two items. The second is a statement about what God is like — that seeking Him is not wasted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *pays back* is **misthapodotes**, a rewarder, one who gives wages."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Noah"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why Noah, **warned by God about things no one could see coming**, was afraid and **built an ark so his household would live**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundred and twenty years of carpentry on dry ground, on the strength of a warning about weather that had no precedent — Genesis says the earth was watered by a mist."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Was afraid** is the Greek for reverent fear, and the writer names it as part of the faith rather than the opposite of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the ark did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That ark was **his verdict on the world**, and what he inherited from it was **the righteousness faith brings**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The building of it condemned everything around it, without a word being recorded from Noah in the whole of Genesis."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is called *a preacher of righteousness* in 2 Peter, and no sermon of his survives. The boat was the sermon."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What all three have in common.** Not one of them is credited with a result they lived to see. Abel was killed, Enoch vanished, and Noah's proof arrived a century after he started building."}]}, "Hebrews 11:8–12": {"title": "\"He went out without knowing where he was going\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three times in five verses the writer says *faith is why*, and each one is attached to something that looks like a disadvantage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Faith is why he went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why Abraham obeyed when he was called… **he went out without knowing where he was going**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 12 records the instruction: *get out of your country… to a land that I will show you.* Not a land I have shown you. Will show."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had a direction and no destination."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Faith is why he camped"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why he **lived as a foreigner in the very land he had been promised**, camping in tents.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the strange one. The land was his by promise, and he spent his life in it as a resident alien in a tent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only piece of it he ever owned outright was a burial cave he paid four hundred shekels for, to bury Sarah in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With Isaac and Jacob, who were promised the same thing he was* — three generations in tents, in a country deeded to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was actually waiting for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was **waiting for the city with real foundations, the one God designed and built**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The contrast is between a tent and a city, and between pegs and foundations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's explanation is that Abraham was not disappointed by the tent because he was not aiming at the land."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Faith is why Sarah conceived"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faith is why **Sarah herself was given the strength to conceive** and bore a child when her body was long past it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is that she received power for the laying down of seed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the reason given: *because she **counted the One who promised as good for it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is a reckoning word — she assessed His credit and judged it sound. It is the same kind of accounting language Paul uses of Abraham."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Sarah had done at the tent door"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She laughed. *Therefore Sarah laughed within herself, saying, \"After I have grown old, shall I have pleasure, my lord being old also?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then denied it: *I did not laugh* — *for she was afraid.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The New Testament puts her in the roll of faith anyway, and says nothing about the laughing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So from **one man, and one whose body was finished**, came descendants past counting — as many as the **stars** overhead, as many as the **grains of sand** along the shore.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two images God used with Abraham himself — the stars in Genesis 15, the sand in Genesis 22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One dead body at the start of the sentence and an uncountable multitude at the end of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter says a few verses later.** *These all died in faith, **not having received the promises**, but having seen them afar off, and were assured of them, and embraced them.* Every one of them died still waiting, and the chapter calls it faith rather than failure."}]}, "Hebrews 12:1–4": {"title": "\"Do not take your eyes off Jesus\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The whole of chapter 11 was a list of names. Chapter 12 begins with *therefore*, and turns the list into a stadium."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crowd"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look at the size of the crowd around us, **every one of them a witness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *so great a cloud of witnesses*. Abel, Enoch, Noah, Abraham, Sarah, Moses, Rahab, Gideon, Samson, David — and the ones at the end of the chapter who were sawn in two and wandered in sheepskins."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A **martus** is one who testifies. They are not spectators in a gallery; they are the ones whose evidence has already been given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two things to drop"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Strip off **whatever weighs us down**, and **the sin that keeps winding itself around our legs**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two separate items. The first is a weight — not necessarily sinful, just heavy. The second is the Greek **euperistatos**, a word found nowhere else, built from *well* and *standing around*: something that surrounds you closely, like a long robe tangling your stride."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The track"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Run without quitting, all the way down **the track that has been staked out in front of us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The course is laid out. Nobody in the passage is choosing a route."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not take your eyes off Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is **aphorao** — it means to look away *from* one thing in order to look *at* another. Both halves are in the single word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You cannot simply add Him to what you are already staring at. Something has to be looked away from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He is called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is where our faith **starts** and He is the One who brings it to the **finish**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Archegos** — the originator, the pioneer, the one who goes first. And **teleiotes** — the completer, the one who finishes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both ends of it belong to Him. It is His faith from the starting line to the tape, and the instruction is simply to keep looking at the One holding both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How He ran"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A joy was waiting for Him, and to get to it **He went through the cross and counted the shame as nothing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The joy was in front of Him, not behind. He was looking at something too."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And now He has **taken His seat** at the right hand of God's throne.* Every other priest stood. He sat down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In your fight with sin **you have not yet had to bleed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written to people under real pressure who thought they had reached their limit. He puts their situation beside His and leaves it there."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter goes next.** Straight into the passage about being brought up as sons — *paideia*, upbringing. Fix your eyes on Him, and then: you are being raised, not managed. See the note on Hebrews 12:5–11."}]}, "Hebrews 12:12–17": {"title": "The root of bitterness, and what it actually grows out of", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This passage gets quoted in two directions at once. One crowd reads *chase holiness; without it nobody is going to see the Lord* and hears a threshold to clear. Another reads *nothing bitter takes root* and hears a warning about holding grudges. Both are picking up something real, and both are reading past the sentence that ties them together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Read the order of the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The warning is one sentence with two halves, and the halves are joined:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Watch that nobody misses out on God's grace**", "**and that nothing bitter takes root**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *misses out* is a word for falling short of something, coming up behind it, being left out — the same word Paul uses in *all have sinned and fall short*. And the bitterness grows out of that. Not out of what somebody did to you. Out of somebody missing the grace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole diagnosis. Bitterness is what grows in a person who is trying to get by on something other than grace. It is the fruit, not the root."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The root is a quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is not the writer's own image. He is reaching back to Deuteronomy, where Moses warns against a root among the people producing poison — someone who hears the covenant and decides privately he will be fine on his own terms. That is the person in the picture. Not a wounded person. A self-sufficient one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is why the next line lands where it does: *nobody sexually immoral or godless like Esau*. Esau is not introduced as an example of lust. He is introduced as a man who traded what was already his for something immediate, because he did not value it. Missing the grace comes first. The visible failure comes after."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other half: what \"holiness\" is doing here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Chase peace with everybody, and chase holiness; without it nobody is going to see the Lord.* Read alone, that reads like a bar to clear."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But look where it sits. Seven lines earlier, the same writer says God disciplines us *so that we come to share His holiness* — His, given, shared. The word is the same one. Holiness here is not a standard the reader manufactures and presents; it is something being handed to them, that they are told to go after."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image in the surrounding verses is medical, not judicial: hands gone limp, knees giving way, a limb about to be thrown out of joint — *healed instead*. The writer is treating an injury, not setting an entrance exam."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it looks like in practice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage never says \"watch that nobody sins.\" It says watch that nobody **misses the grace**, because that is where the trouble starts and everything else follows. It puts the attention one step upstream of where we usually put it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On Esau and \"no room for repentance.\"** This line frightens people badly. In the Greek the sentence is about *Isaac's* decision, not God's: Esau found no way to reverse the blessing his father had already spoken over Jacob, though he begged for it with tears. It is a story about a transaction that could not be undone, not a statement that a person can want God and be turned away."}]}, "Hebrews 12:18–24": {"title": "Two mountains, and which one you are standing on", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is one of the most carefully built passages in the New Testament, and its whole argument is carried by two words that are easy to read straight past: **what you have come to**. He says it twice, and the second time reverses the first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What you have come to is not a mountain a hand can touch* — fire, gloom, dark, howling storm, a trumpet, a voice. This is Sinai, where the law was given, and the writer describes it entirely in terms of what it did to the people standing at the bottom of it:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["they begged for the voice to stop", "they could not bear the command that was given", "even Moses said he was terrified and shaking"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the description is about the law being wrong. Everything in it is about what happens to people who come to God that way. It is unbearable — and the one man in the story who had God's own attention is the one who says he is shaking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule at the foot of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If even an animal so much as touches the mountain, **it is to be stoned**, or run through with a spear.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus records the boundary: a line drawn round the base, and a death penalty for crossing it — applying to livestock, which cannot read a warning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the detail the writer picks to show what the approach was like. Not the fire or the trumpet. A cow that wandered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The angels"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Angels in numbers no one can count.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **muriasin** — myriads, tens of thousands, and the word behind *myriad*. It is the largest number the language had, used as a plural."}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Sinai they were there too — Deuteronomy says *the LORD came from Sinai… He came with ten thousands of saints* — but the mountain the readers were kept off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here they are listed among the things the reader has already come to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What you have come to is Mount Zion* — and then a list that does not stop:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the city of the living God, the Jerusalem above", "angels in numbers nobody can count", "the great gathering, every name on record in heaven", "God the Judge of all", "righteous people brought to completion", "Jesus, standing between God and us", "blood that says something better than Abel's"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Notice the tense. Not *will come to*. **Have come to.** The writer is not describing a destination for later; he is telling people where they are already standing while they read."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail everyone misses: Abel's blood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage ends by comparing two kinds of blood, and it expects you to know the story. Abel's blood cried out from the ground — it called for justice against the man who spilled it. That is what innocent blood does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So when the writer says this blood *says something better*, he is not saying it speaks more loudly. He is saying it speaks in the opposite direction. Abel's blood asked for the guilty to answer. This blood answers for the guilty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole argument of the letter in one image, and it is placed at the end of the mountain comparison on purpose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two details from Genesis sharpen it. God says to Cain *your brother's blood is crying out to me from the ground* — the verb is the ordinary word for a shout for help, and the noun is plural, *bloods*, which readers have long taken to mean not one life but every life that would have come from it. Spilled blood is pictured as a voice that will not go quiet until something answers it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then notice what God actually does with Cain. He does not execute him. When Cain says the sentence is more than he can bear, God puts a mark on him so that nobody will touch him. Even in the passage that gives us blood crying for justice, the first murderer walks away marked for protection. See the note on Genesis 4:8–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it matters where you are standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two mountains are not two eras that a reader chooses between as a matter of taste. They are two ways of approaching God, and the passage says plainly that one of them terrified everybody who tried it, including Moses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A person can be at Zion by fact and still be living at Sinai by habit — still approaching on the basis of how the week went. The passage is written to move them, and it does it not by pressure but by telling them where they already are."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the fire at the end.** The next lines say our God is a fire that burns things up, and it is often read as a threat bolted onto the comfort. Look at what the fire is doing in context: it takes away *everything that can be shaken* so that *what cannot be shaken is left standing*. It is not aimed at the person. It is aimed at what will not hold them."}]}, "Hebrews 12:25–29": {"title": "What the shaking is for", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Read on its own, this sounds like the letter ending on a threat: do not turn Him down, everything is going to be shaken, our God is a fire that burns things up. Read as the finish of the argument that has been building since the two mountains, it is doing almost the opposite."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose clause is the whole thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence about shaking has a *so that* in it, and everything depends on where it points:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "the taking away of everything that can be shaken … **so that what cannot be shaken is left standing**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The shaking is not the goal. It is the method. The goal is what is still there afterwards. And the writer tells you immediately what that is: *the kingdom we are being given can never be shaken.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek makes it plainer than English does. The things that go are described with an ordinary participle — things being shaken. What remains is *asaleutos*, a single word meaning unshakable, immovable. It is not that the kingdom survives the shaking. It is that it is not the kind of thing shaking touches."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Being given, not built"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The kingdom we are being given.* Not earned, not held onto by strength, not kept by performance. Receiving is the only verb in the sentence, and it is passive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That matters for how the next line reads. *Let us hold on to grace, and by it worship God.* A person who thinks the shaking is aimed at them will hold on to their own record. The writer says hold on to grace — and then says it is *by* that grace that acceptable worship happens at all."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fire at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our God is a fire that burns things up.* This is the last line of the chapter and it is often read as the sting in the tail."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But it is a quotation from Deuteronomy, and it is the same image the passage has just spent four verses explaining. Fire is what removes what will not last. Set next to the sentence before it — a kingdom that cannot be shaken — the fire is not aimed at the person holding the kingdom. It is aimed at everything that could not have held them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What this is for, practically"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone is standing on a mix of things: what God has said, and a quieter set of supports we would rather not test — how the year went, how disciplined we have been lately, what other people think. This passage says a day comes when everything of that second kind goes, and the point of saying so is not to frighten anybody. It is so that a reader knows now which of the things they are standing on will still be there."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"how much less will we get away with turning our backs.\"** The warning is real and should not be softened. But notice its shape: it is about *refusing to hear the One who is speaking* — turning away from the voice. It is not a warning about failing. It is a warning about not listening, addressed to people the writer has just told they have already arrived at Zion."}]}, "Hebrews 12:5–11": {"title": "The word behind \"discipline,\" and what it is not", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the passage people reach for when something has gone wrong and they want to know whether God did it to them. That is a heavy question to bring to a text, and this one is usually read with the wrong word in mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the word actually is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word running through the whole passage — five times in seven verses — is *paideia*. It is built on the word for a child, and in ordinary Greek it means the whole business of bringing a child up: training, teaching, forming. It is the word a Greek would use for an education."}, {"k": "p", "t": "English \"discipline\" has drifted toward punishment, and once that is the word in your head, the passage reads as God arranging misfortunes. Put *upbringing* where discipline stands and read it again. The subject is not what God sends. It is that He is raising sons rather than managing strangers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument the writer is making"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Follow the logic and notice what it is arguing *for*:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["being brought up this way is how you know you are handled as a son", "being left out of it would mean you were not one", "our own fathers did it and we thought better of them for it, not worse"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point being pressed is not \"expect suffering.\" It is **you belong to Him.** The writer is comforting people who were beginning to wonder, and the evidence he offers them is that they are being brought up at all."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it says about the pain, exactly"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No discipline feels like a good thing while it is happening — it feels miserable.* The passage does not spiritualise that, and neither should any reading of it. It concedes the misery outright."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then it says the outcome is *a harvest of peace and righteousness* — and notice that both of those are given, not achieved. Two verses earlier the same writer says God does it *so that we come to share His holiness*. Share. His. Whatever this upbringing produces, the passage never describes it as the reader manufacturing something to present back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not say every hard thing that happens is sent by God to teach a lesson. The passage never makes that claim, and reading it in is how this text ends up being used to explain a diagnosis or a bereavement to somebody who is already on the floor."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What it says is narrower and kinder: God is bringing you up as a son, and a son is not left to raise himself. That is a claim about your standing with Him, made to people who were losing their nerve, and it is meant to steady them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"He lays the lash on every son.\"** The line quotes Proverbs, and it is the hardest sentence here. In its own context the image is a father's correction, not a judge's sentence — and the writer's use of it lands on the word *son*, which is the noun the whole passage keeps returning to. The emphasis is on who is being addressed, not on the instrument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul uses the same word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are **chastened** by the Lord, so that we would not be condemned along with the world* — 1 Corinthians 11:32, and the verb there is **paideuo**, from this same root."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul puts the chastening and the condemning on opposite sides of the sentence. Whatever the upbringing is, it is what keeps a son out of the world's verdict, not a lighter version of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the same argument this passage is making at length. See the note on 1 Corinthians 11:31–34."}]}, "Hebrews 13:1–6": {"title": "\"I will never let go of you\" — and what it is attached to", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the most quoted promises in the Bible, and it is quoted almost entirely out of its sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the promise is given as a reason for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep your way of life free of the love of money, and be satisfied with what you have, **because** He has said it Himself: I will never let go of you, and I will never walk away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the argument for contentment. The reasoning runs: you can stop grasping, because the one thing you cannot lose is Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a more useful promise than the free-floating version. It is not offered as general comfort — it is offered to a specific anxiety about money and security, as the ground for letting go of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The strength of the Greek"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence in Hebrews stacks negatives in a way English cannot reproduce — something like *I will never, no never, leave you; I will never, no never, forsake you*. Five negative particles in one line."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer is quoting God's promise to Joshua, and he piles the language up because he wants it to be unmissable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So we can say it right out: The Lord is the One helping me — I will not be afraid of anything anyone can do to me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives the promise and then supplies the answer, quoting Psalm 118. The believer's confidence is not manufactured; it is a line handed to them to say back."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The company it keeps.** The list before it is entirely practical: love each other, take in strangers, remember prisoners as though chained with them, honour marriage. Then contentment, then the promise. Whatever else this chapter is, it is not abstract — and the promise about God never letting go sits among instructions about hospitality and prison visits."}]}, "Hebrews 13:15–19": {"title": "A sacrifice made of words", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The letter has spent twelve chapters explaining that the sacrifices are finished. Then it names three that are not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Through Him let us keep bringing God an offering of praise, words out of our mouths thanking His name.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is from Hosea — *the fruit of our lips*. Under the old system you brought an animal; here the offering is speech."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two details keep it from being sentimental. It is **through Him** — the same mediation the whole letter has been arguing for. And it is *continually*, a present-tense keeping on rather than an occasion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second and third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not forget to do good and to share what you have; those are the offerings God takes pleasure in.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Praise, then generosity — and the writer uses sacrificial vocabulary for both. The word for *takes pleasure* is the one used of a sacrifice being accepted as a pleasing aroma."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the offerings that remain are: what you say about God, and what you do for people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction about leaders"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They stay awake over your souls, and they will have to give an account, so let it be a glad job for them and not a groaning one — groaning would do you no good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An unusually practical note. The reason given for cooperating is not the leaders' authority but the readers' own benefit: a discouraged shepherd is no use to the sheep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Pray for us… I ask it all the more urgently, so that I can be given back to you sooner.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer of the most theologically dense letter in the New Testament ends by asking for prayer and admitting he wants to see them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why \"through Him\" carries the weight.** Praise offered by people who know their own record can only be brought if somebody else makes it acceptable — which is precisely what the gold plate on Aaron's forehead was for, and what the letter says Christ now is. See the note on Exodus 28:36–38."}]}, "Hebrews 13:7–14": {"title": "Outside the gate", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The letter's closing chapter, and it moves from remembering leaders to walking out of a city."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The leaders"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Remember your leaders, **the ones who spoke God's word to you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are identified by what they did rather than by rank."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look hard at **how their lives turned out**, and copy their faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ten ekbasin tes anastrophes** — the outcome of their conduct, the way out, the exit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some of them had died for it. The letter has already mentioned *the former days, in which… you endured a great struggle with sufferings*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hinge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus Christ does not change — **not yesterday, not today, not ever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **echthes kai semeron ho autos kai eis tous aionas**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Placed between a sentence about leaders dying and a warning about strange teaching. The leaders change; the message does not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not get swept along by **all sorts of strange teaching**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **poikilais** — variegated, many-coloured. And **xenais**, foreign."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heart"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is good for the heart to be **settled by grace, not by rules about food**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **bebaiousthai** — established, made firm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which have **never done any good for the people who lived by them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Blunt, and about a whole system."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The altar"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We have **an altar of our own**, and those who serve at the tent **have no claim on what is eaten from it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This reverses the usual arrangement. In Leviticus the priests ate from the altar — that was their living. See the note on Leviticus 7:1–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here the priests are the ones excluded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule behind it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The bodies of the animals **whose blood the high priest carries into the holy place** for sin are **burned outside the camp**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus 6, the last verse of the chapter: if the blood went inside, the body went outside and was burnt. Nobody ate it. See the note on Leviticus 6:24–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sin offering of the Day of Atonement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The application"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That is why **Jesus suffered outside the gate**, so that He could **set the people apart as holy by His blood**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Golgotha was outside the wall. John records it: *He went out to the place called the Place of a Skull*. See the note on John 19:17–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer takes one clause of Levitical procedure and reads the geography of the crucifixion off it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **let us go out to Him there, past the edge of the camp, and carry the disgrace He carried**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ton oneidismon autou pherontes** — bearing His reproach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter is written to Jewish believers under pressure to go back. The camp is the whole system — temple, sacrifices, priesthood, respectability."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them to leave it and stand where the bodies were burnt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No city here lasts. **We are after the one that is coming**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ouk echomen menousan polin** — we have no abiding city."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jerusalem fell within a few years of this letter, by most datings."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore by Him let us **continually offer the sacrifice of praise to God**, that is, the fruit of our lips, giving thanks to His name.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But do not forget to do good and to share, for **with such sacrifices God is well pleased**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An altar, outside the camp, and the offerings on it are words and generosity."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the geography comes from.** One clause in Leviticus — if the blood went inside, the body was burnt outside. Hebrews reads the location of the cross off it, and then tells the readers to go there."}]}, "Hebrews 2:1–4": {"title": "Swept past it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first of the letter's warnings, and the danger it names is drifting rather than rebelling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 1, entirely about the Son: the brightness of God's glory, the express image of His person, upholding all things by the word of His power. See the note on Hebrews 1:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven Old Testament quotations, all showing Him higher than the angels."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore* — and the warning follows from the person, not from a rule."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We owe what we have heard **a far closer hearing than we have been giving it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **perissoteros prosechein** — to give more abundant heed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The danger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Or at some point **we will be swept right past it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pararuomen** — to flow past, to drift by. A nautical word: a ship carried past its harbour by a current."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is not a man walking away. It is a man standing still in moving water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing has to be decided for this to happen. That is the point of choosing the word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument from the lesser"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The message that came **through angels** held firm, and **every violation of it, every refusal to obey, was paid back exactly what it was owed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **endikon misthapodosian** — a just recompense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Through angels* is how the New Testament describes the giving of the law. Stephen says it — *you who have received the law by the direction of angels* — and Paul in Galatians: *it was appointed through angels by the hand of a mediator*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sinai, and the fence, and the penalty for touching the edge. See the note on Exodus 19:10–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **how will we escape, if we treat a rescue this enormous as nothing?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **telikautes** — so great."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **amelesantes** — having neglected, disregarded. Not rejected. Neglected."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same word used of the men in the parable who were invited to the wedding and *made light of it, and went their ways, one to his own farm, another to his business*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in that parable was hostile. They had other things on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three witnesses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Lord was the first to speak of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people who heard Him **made it certain to us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **God signed His name to it alongside them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three tiers: the Lord, the hearers, and God's own confirmation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer places himself in the second generation — *made it certain to us* — which is one of the reasons Hebrews is not thought to be by an eyewitness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The signature"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **signs**, with **wonders**, with **powers of every kind at work**, and with **gifts of the Holy Spirit handed out however He wanted**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sunepimarturountos** — bearing witness alongside. One word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four kinds of corroboration listed, and the last is *according to His own will*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter's warnings all have in common"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There are five of them, spread through the book, and each is embedded in an argument about who Christ is rather than a list of rules."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next one: *beware, brethren, lest there be in any of you an evil heart of unbelief in departing from the living God*. See the note on Hebrews 3:12–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being drifted past"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So great a salvation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word order puts the size first. The thing at risk is not standing; it is attention."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb chosen.** Not walking away — being carried past, like a ship missing its harbour. It describes something that happens to a person who does nothing at all."}]}, "Hebrews 2:14–16": {"title": "Slaves to a fear, and what was actually broken", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is one of the plainest statements in the New Testament about why the incarnation happened, and it gives a reason that has nothing to do with teaching or example."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Since the children are made of flesh and blood, He took on the same flesh and blood Himself* — and then a purpose clause, which is the point of the sentence:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**so that by dying He could break the one who held the power of death**", "**and set free the people who had spent their whole lives as slaves because they were afraid to die**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He became human in order to die. Not primarily to teach, not to demonstrate. The argument is that only someone with a body could do this particular thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Break\" is not \"destroy\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *katargeo* — the same word behind *cut off* in Galatians 5, and it means to make idle, to put out of work, to leave with nothing to do."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That precision matters, because the devil is plainly still active in the rest of the New Testament. The claim is not that he was annihilated at the cross. It is that the specific thing he held — *the power of death* — was taken out of his hands and he was left with nothing to hold over anybody."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The slavery is a fear, not a fate"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look closely at what the passage says people were enslaved by. Not death. **Fear of death** — and *their whole lives*, which is the phrase that gives it away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The slavery being described is not what happens at the end. It is what fear of the end does to the middle: the way a person lives who is quietly managing an outcome they cannot control. The letter treats that as a life in chains, and treats the cross as the thing that unlocks it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He takes hold of\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last line uses a vivid verb — reaching out and seizing, taking a grip on. It is used elsewhere of catching hold of someone who is sinking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is not angels He takes hold of; it is Abraham's children.* The writer is answering an unspoken question about why the rescue took the form it did. It took that form because of who was being grabbed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What this does not promise.** The passage does not say a believer will not die; the same letter goes on to list people who died in faith without receiving what was promised. What it says was broken is death's *hold* — the fear that ran a person's whole life. That is a real freedom, and it is not the same claim as exemption."}]}, "Hebrews 3:1–2": {"title": "\"The High Priest of the faith we own out loud\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and the title given to Jesus in them is unusual."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the readers are called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Brothers **set apart as holy**, sharing in **a call that came down from heaven**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before anything is asked of them, two things are said about them. They are holy, and their calling originated somewhere other than in themselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *sharing* is **metochoi** — partners, partakers. The same word is used later of *partakers of Christ*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Think hard about **Christ Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katanoeo** — to fix the mind on, to observe closely, to consider attentively. It is the word used of Peter looking into the tomb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole command in these two verses. Not do, not try. Look at Him carefully."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two titles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The One God sent* — **apostolos**, the sent one. The only time in the New Testament the word is applied to Jesus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **High Priest** of the faith we own out loud.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An apostle comes out from God to men. A high priest goes in from men to God. Both directions, one Person, in one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Of our confession\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **homologia** — from *homo* (same) and *lego* (say). To say the same thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the High Priest **of that**. Of what is said out loud."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not of our feelings, and not of our private opinions. The word means speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter keeps saying about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word turns up twice more, and both times as something to keep hold of:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Since we have a great High Priest who has passed through the heavens, Jesus the Son of God, **let us hold fast our confession**.* — 4:14"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us **hold fast the confession of our hope** without wavering, for He who promised is faithful.* — 10:23"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hold fast, and hold it without wavering, and the reason given is not about the one holding. It is *He who promised is faithful.* See the note on Hebrews 4:14–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Faithful as Moses was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was faithful to the God who appointed Him, **just as Moses was faithful in all God's house**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses is not disparaged. He is called faithful in the same clause."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the argument turns on a distinction: *this One has been counted worthy of more glory than Moses, inasmuch as He who built the house has more honour than the house.* Moses was faithful **in** the house, as a servant. Christ is faithful **over** it, as a Son."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who \"the house\" is.** *Whose house we are, if we hold fast the confidence and the rejoicing of the hope firm to the end.* The letter has just called them holy brothers, and now it calls them the building."}]}, "Hebrews 3:12–15": {"title": "\"While there is still a Today\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The application of the psalm, and the instruction in it is about each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Watch out, brothers, that **not one of you turns out to have a heart gone bad and unbelieving**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kardia ponera apistias** — an evil heart of unbelief."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two nouns are joined. The writer does not treat unbelief as an intellectual position; he puts it in the same phrase as the badness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A heart that **walks away from the living God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apostenai** — to stand off, to withdraw. The root of *apostasy*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is of stepping back rather than of falling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep at each other **every single day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parakaleite** — exhort, encourage, urge alongside. It is from the same root as **parakletos**, the Helper. See the note on John 14:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Every single day** — the Greek is *day by day*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The remedy for a heart hardening is other people, applied daily."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer says the same thing in chapter 10: *do not drop out of gathering together, the way some have gotten into the habit of doing… keep encouraging each other.* See the note on Hebrews 10:23–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The window"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For as long as **there is still a Today to say it in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word from the psalm, turned into a deadline."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **none of you gets hardened by sin and its lying**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apate tes hamartias** — the deceitfulness of sin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hardening is described as the result of being lied to rather than of choosing badly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says it the same way: *sin found its opening in the commandment, **deceived me**, and through it killed me.* See the note on Romans 7:9–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We have **come to share in Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **metochoi** — partakers, sharers. The word is used earlier in the chapter of *partakers of the heavenly calling*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Perfect tense: we have become and remain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If we **hold what we were sure of at the start firmly to the finish**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the beginning of our confidence steadfast to the end*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As long as it is still being said: \"**Today, if you hear His voice, do not let your hearts turn to stone**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer repeats the psalm's opening for the second of three times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each repetition presses a different word. See the notes on Hebrews 3:7–11 and Hebrews 3:16–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole chapter is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Comparing two houses — Moses' and Christ's — and then warning against repeating what happened to the generation Moses led."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the letter is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrew believers under pressure to go back to the temple. The whole letter is written against that one move, and this is the first of its warnings."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is prescribed against a hardening heart.** Not more effort. Other people, daily, for as long as the word *Today* is still in use."}]}, "Hebrews 3:16–19": {"title": "\"Unbelief was what kept them out\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three questions and an answer, and the writer makes the reader do the work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The method"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage is a cross-examination. Each question has an obvious answer and the writer supplies it, and the effect is that the conclusion is drawn rather than announced."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Some of them heard and pushed Him to it anyway** — though **not all of the ones Moses brought out of Egypt did**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The exception matters. Caleb and Joshua came out of Egypt and went in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer is careful not to say the whole generation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **who was it He was sick of for forty years?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Was it not **the ones who sinned, the ones whose bodies dropped in the desert?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *whose carcasses fell in the wilderness* — the phrase from Numbers 14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years of walking, and a funeral every few days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **who did He swear about, that they would never enter His rest?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The ones who refused to believe**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apeithesasin** — those who disobeyed, who refused to be persuaded."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a word about the will rather than the intellect. The same root John uses: *whoever refuses to believe the Son will not see life*. See the note on John 3:31–36."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So we can see it plainly: **they could not get in. Unbelief was what kept them out**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *because of unbelief* — **di' apistian**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One cause is named, and it is not the giants, the walls, or the Anakim."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had accurate intelligence. All twelve spies agreed on the facts. See the note on Numbers 13:26–33."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the writer does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He turns it on the reader immediately. The next verse: *therefore, since a promise remains of entering His rest, **let us fear lest any of you seem to have come short of it**.* See the note on Hebrews 4:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis he gives there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The good news came to us **just as it came to them**. But **hearing it did them no good, because it never mixed with any faith** in the ones who heard it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sunkerannumi** — blended, compounded into one substance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same message, same preaching. What was missing was the thing that combines with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the wilderness generation actually did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stood at Kadesh, heard twelve reports, listened to ten, and refused."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers records what they said: *let us select a leader and return to Egypt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, told the sentence, they changed their minds and went up anyway — and were beaten, because *the ark of the covenant of the LORD and Moses did not depart from the camp*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other kind of unbelief"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's warning is not aimed at people who never heard. It is aimed at people who heard, were on the road, and stopped at the border."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the argument lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There remains therefore a **Sabbath rest** for the people of God.* See the note on Hebrews 4:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offer outlives the generation that missed it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one cause the writer names.** Not the opposition and not the difficulty of the land. He asks three questions with obvious answers and then says it in four words: unbelief was what kept them out."}]}, "Hebrews 3:7–11": {"title": "\"Today, if you hear His voice\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer quotes Psalm 95 in full, and attributes it to the Holy Spirit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So — and **this is the Holy Spirit speaking**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David wrote the psalm. The writer of Hebrews attributes it to the Spirit, in the present tense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does the same in chapter 10 with Jeremiah. See the note on Hebrews 10:15–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Today**, if you hear His voice.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole argument of the next two chapters hangs on this word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's point is that the psalm was written four hundred years after Joshua brought Israel into Canaan — so when David says *today*, the offer is evidently still open. See the note on Hebrews 4:8–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not let your hearts turn to stone**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **skleruno** — to harden. The root of *sclerosis*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The way they did when they **pushed Him to it**, out in the desert **on the day they put Him on trial**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two Hebrew place names underlie this: **Meribah**, contention, and **Massah**, testing. See the note on Psalms 95:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both were names Moses gave to a place where there was no water and the people picked up stones."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That was where your ancestors **tried Me, and tested Me, and watched what I did for forty years**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *proved Me, and saw My works*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had the evidence. Forty years of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So that generation **made Me sick at heart**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **prosochthisa** — was grieved, was disgusted with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Their hearts are always wandering off, and they have never learned My ways**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not His acts — they saw those daily. His ways."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **in My anger I swore an oath: they will never enter My rest**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The strongest negative construction in the language."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the rest was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Canaan — the end of the marching. And the writer will argue in the next chapter that it is also something else, since God had rested on the seventh day long before there was a land to enter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the writer does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes the psalm three times across two chapters, and each time he presses a different word:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["Here, the whole passage.", "Then *Today*: *exhort one another daily, **while it is called Today***. See the note on Hebrews 3:12–15.", "Then the failure: *they could not enter in **because of unbelief***. See the note on Hebrews 3:16–19.", "Then the offer: *there remains therefore a rest.* See the note on Hebrews 4:1–3."]}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter began"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, holy brethren, partakers of the heavenly calling, **consider the Apostle and High Priest of our confession, Christ Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the comparison with Moses: *this One has been counted worthy of more glory than Moses, inasmuch as He who built the house has more honour than the house.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is on the table"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **Christ as a Son over His own house, whose house we are** if we hold fast the confidence and the rejoicing of the hope firm to the end.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word the argument stands on.** *Today.* The writer notices that David used it four hundred years after the land was taken, and builds two chapters on the fact that the offer had not closed."}]}, "Hebrews 4:1–3": {"title": "The rest still standing open", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer's argument turns on a door that has not been shut."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **let us be afraid**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phobethomen** — let us fear."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a strange way to open a paragraph about rest, and he means it: the danger is real enough to be afraid of missing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is open"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The promise of entering His rest **is still standing open**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *while a promise remains*. It has not been withdrawn or used up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **not one of you should turn out to have missed it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *lest any of you seem to have come short* — **hustereo**, to fall short, to arrive too late."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The good news came to us **just as it came to them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *we have had the gospel preached to us, as well as they*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wilderness generation had good news preached to them. The writer says so plainly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They stood at Kadesh, twelve spies came back, two said go up, and the nation refused. That was the sermon and that was the response."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it did not work"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **hearing it did them no good, because it never mixed with any faith in the ones who heard it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *not being mixed with faith in those who heard*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is **sunkerannumi** — blended, compounded together, the way ingredients are combined into one substance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The message was not deficient. It sat in them without combining."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who enters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We who have believed **are the ones who enter that rest**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present tense. The entering is described as happening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as He said, \"**So I swore in My anger: they will never enter My rest**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 95, and the writer quotes it three times across these two chapters. See the note on Psalms 95:8–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oddity at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And that, **though the work had been finished ever since the world was laid down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest they were barred from had been ready since Genesis 2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer says as much in the next verse: *God rested on the seventh day from all His works.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing had to be built. The rest existed before the people who missed it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the argument produces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There remains therefore **a Sabbath rest for the people of God**… let us **be diligent to enter that rest**.* See the note on Hebrews 4:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An exertion aimed at a rest, and the writer puts both words in one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seeing then that we have **a great High Priest** who has passed through the heavens… **let us therefore come boldly to the throne of grace**, that we may obtain mercy and find grace to help in time of need.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter about a rest that was missed ends at a throne with an open invitation."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the failure was.** Not that the news was withheld or unclear. The writer says the same gospel reached them, and names the missing element as the thing that would have combined with it."}]}, "Hebrews 4:12–13": {"title": "Alive, and cutting", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four claims about the word, and then the sentence turns and looks back at the reader."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Directly after two chapters on the wilderness generation who heard the good news and did not enter, and the instruction *let us be diligent to enter that rest*. See the note on Hebrews 4:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word that did them no good is the subject of the next sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Alive"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God's word is **alive**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **zon** — living. It is the first word of the sentence in Greek, placed for emphasis."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Force"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is **force** in it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **energes** — active, at work, operative. The root of *energy*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The edge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It has **a keener edge than any blade sharpened on both sides**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **distomos** — literally *two-mouthed*. Greek describes the edge of a sword as its mouth, and a double-edged sword as one with two."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew does the same: **peh**, mouth, is the word for a blade's edge — and Isaiah's threshing sledge has *mouths* for teeth. See the note on Isaiah 41:14–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it separates"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It goes in until it has separated **soul from spirit** and **joint from marrow**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two pairs, and each is a pair of things nobody can get between."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joint and marrow are not adjacent; marrow is inside the bone. The picture is of something reaching past the surface to what is sealed inside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it reads"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it **reads the heart** — **every thought it ever had, every intention**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kritikos** — able to judge, discerning. The root of *critic*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two objects: **enthumeseon**, the thoughts and reflections, and **ennoion**, the intentions and purposes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Nothing that was ever made **stays hidden from Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The subject changes mid-passage. Verse 12 is about the word; verse 13 is about a person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer does not mark the transition, and the effect is that the two are hard to separate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everything lies **stripped bare, throat exposed**, in front of the eyes of the One we have to answer to.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tetrachelismena** — from **trachelos**, the neck. It is the root of *trachea*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word was used of a wrestler forcing an opponent's head back to expose the throat, and of a sacrificial animal with the neck laid open for the knife."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Stripped bare** — **gumna**, naked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the next verse is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seeing then that we have **a great High Priest** who has passed through the heavens, Jesus the Son of God, **let us hold fast our confession**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *for we do not have a High Priest who cannot sympathise with our weaknesses, but was **in all points tempted as we are**, yet without sin.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us therefore **come boldly to the throne of grace**, that we may obtain mercy and find grace to help in time of need.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage about being laid open is followed directly by an invitation to walk in without hesitating — and by a priest described as knowing what it is like."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order the writer uses.** Everything exposed, and then a throne to come to boldly. He puts them in consecutive sentences and the second is the conclusion of the first."}]}, "Hebrews 4:14–16": {"title": "Walk right up", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The letter has spent four chapters on rest and on the word that cuts. Then it turns to prayer, and the instruction is startlingly forward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Walk right up\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is about coming to, approaching — and the adverb attached is **parrhesia**: boldness, freedom of speech, the frankness of someone entitled to be there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not creep in. Not petition from a distance. Approach with the freedom of a citizen speaking in his own assembly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For readers who knew the tabernacle, this is a considerable sentence. The place where God sat was behind a curtain, entered once a year, by one man, with blood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it can be said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is not a high priest who cannot feel it when we are weak. He was put to the test in every way we are, and He never sinned.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things at once. He has been through it — the verb for *feel it* is about suffering along with, sympathising from the inside. And He came through clean."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both matter. A priest who had not been tested could not sympathise; one who had failed could not represent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The throne"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The throne where grace sits.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a throne — the seat of a ruler with every right to judge. And what the writer says is on it is grace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is waiting there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mercy, and grace arriving exactly when we need help.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase for well-timed help is one word, about being in season, at the right moment. Not grace stockpiled in advance. Grace that shows up on time."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse before it.** *We have a great high priest who has gone right up through the heavens.* He went up; we walk up. The whole basis of the boldness is that somebody is already inside — see the note on Hebrews 9:23–24."}]}, "Hebrews 4:4–7": {"title": "He marks off a day and calls it Today", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An argument built entirely out of two Old Testament quotations and the gap between them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Somewhere He speaks about the seventh day like this: \"**And on the seventh day God stopped, with all His work behind Him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 2:2. See the note on Genesis 2:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Somewhere* — the writer of Hebrews quotes this way repeatedly. He assumes his readers know where."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that establishes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A rest exists, and it has existed since the seventh day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of Genesis has no evening and morning on that day. The other six all close with the formula and this one does not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And here again: \"**They will never enter My rest**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 95, about the wilderness generation. *So I swore in My wrath, \"They shall not enter My rest.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The deduction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **it still stands open for some to come in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apoleipetai** — it remains, it is left over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The logic: a rest was made, and the people it was offered to did not go in. Therefore it is unoccupied."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they did not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the ones who **got the good news first did not come in, because they would not believe**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **di' apeitheian** — through disobedience, or unpersuadedness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews has already made this point: *the word which they heard did not profit them, **not being mixed with faith** in those who heard it*. See the note on Hebrews 4:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had the message. They lacked the mixing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dating argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So He **marks off a day again and calls it Today**, speaking through David **all that long time afterward**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the hinge, and it is a chronological argument."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua brought them into the land. If the land had been the rest, the subject would be closed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But David, four hundred years after Joshua, writes *today, if you will hear His voice* — which means the offer was still open in his lifetime."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer says so explicitly two verses later: *for if Joshua had given them rest, then He would not afterward have spoken of another day*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Today.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **semeron**, and Hebrews uses it four times in this argument."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole case rests on a single adverb in a psalm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning attached"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Today, if you hear His voice, **do not let your hearts turn to stone**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me sklerunete** — do not harden."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same clause appears three times in Hebrews 3 and 4. It is the refrain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There remains therefore **a rest for the people of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sabbatismos** — a Sabbath-keeping, a Sabbath rest. The word occurs once in the New Testament and the writer appears to have coined it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For he who has entered His rest **has himself also ceased from his works as God did from His**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is to the seventh day. Not a pause in the work — the work finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us therefore **be diligent to enter that rest**, lest anyone fall according to the same example of disobedience.* See the note on Hebrews 4:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **spoudasomen** — be eager, make haste, labour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Work hard at resting, which reads as a contradiction and is the letter's point: the effort goes into stopping."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the word of God is living and powerful, and sharper than any two-edged sword.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the high priest *who was in all points tempted as we are, yet without sin* — and *let us therefore come boldly to the throne of grace*. See the note on Hebrews 4:14–16."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The argument's whole weight.** One word in a psalm. Joshua brought them in, and David wrote *today* four centuries later — which the writer takes as proof the offer had not closed."}]}, "Hebrews 4:8–11": {"title": "\"Work hard at entering that rest\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer's whole case rests on one word in a psalm and on a name that means two things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If **Joshua had given them rest**, God would **not have spoken later on about another day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua brought them into Canaan. The land was taken, the tribes settled, and the book ends *the LORD gave them rest all around*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred years later David writes *Today, if you will hear His voice, do not harden your hearts… they shall not enter My rest.* See the note on Psalms 95:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's point: you do not offer a rest that has already been given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Greek, Joshua and Jesus are the same name — **Iesous**. The older English versions print *Jesus* here, which is why the verse used to confuse readers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both mean *the LORD saves*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **there is still a Sabbath rest waiting to be kept** by the people of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sabbatismos** — a Sabbath-keeping. The word appears once in the New Testament, here, and the writer appears to have coined it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it consists of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Anyone who has entered His rest **has stopped working himself**, the way **God stopped when His own work was done**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is Genesis 2. God rested on the seventh day, and He did it because the work was finished, not because He was tired."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the model given: rest as the response to completion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **let us work hard at entering that rest**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **spoudazo** — be diligent, make every effort, hurry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An exertion, aimed at a rest. The writer puts the two words in one sentence and leaves them there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the effort is against"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **nobody falls by copying the same refusal to believe**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the same example of disobedience* — and the word for disobedience in this letter is consistently tied to unbelief. Three verses earlier: *they could not enter in because of unbelief.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thing to work at is not falling into the pattern of the generation that would not go in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern being avoided"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They stood at the border, sent twelve spies, heard two of them, and would not cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years of walking followed, and Numbers records what they said: *let us select a leader and return to Egypt.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the word of God is living and powerful, and sharper than any two-edged sword… piercing even to the division of soul and spirit, and is a discerner of the thoughts and intents of the heart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, immediately: *seeing then that we have a great High Priest… **let us therefore come boldly to the throne of grace**, that we may obtain mercy and find grace to help in time of need.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter about rest ends at a throne with an invitation to come to it without hesitating."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the writer keeps returning to.** *Today.* He quotes it three times across two chapters. The whole argument is that the day the offer is open is the one the reader is in."}]}, "Hebrews 5:11–12": {"title": "Still on milk", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer breaks off mid-argument and explains why."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is **a great deal to say about all this**, and **it is hard to get across**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just named Melchizedek for the fourth time and is about to open the argument. See the note on Hebrews 5:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He stops instead, and the digression runs to the end of chapter 6 before he picks it up again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the fault is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **you have gotten lazy about listening**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **nothroi tais akoais** — sluggish, dull, in the hearing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say the subject is beyond them. He says the difficulty is at their end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nothros** appears twice in the letter, here and at the end of chapter 6: *that you do not become **sluggish**, but imitate those who through faith and patience inherit the promises.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The expectation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By this time **you ought to be the ones teaching**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Enough time has passed. The letter is written to a congregation that had been going long enough to have produced teachers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 10 fills in what they had been through: *you had compassion on me in my chains, and joyfully accepted the plundering of your goods.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "These are not new converts. They had lost property for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they actually are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Instead **somebody has to start you over at the beginning**, with **the simplest things God has said**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ta stoicheia** — the elements, the ABCs. It is the word for the letters of the alphabet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have ended up **needing milk, not solid food**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is that they have *come to have need* of it — a state arrived at, not a state never left."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been further on and had come back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the next verses say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For everyone who partakes only of milk is unskilled in the word of righteousness, for he is a babe.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **solid food belongs to those who are of full age**, that is, those who **by reason of use have their senses exercised to discern both good and evil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *exercised* is **gegumnasmena** — trained, from the root of *gymnasium*. It is a word about practice rather than information."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, **leaving the discussion of the elementary principles of Christ, let us go on to perfection**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He refuses to go back over the foundations, lists six of them, and then delivers the warning of Hebrews 6:4–6 — followed immediately by *but beloved, we are convinced of better things concerning you*. See the notes on Hebrews 6:4–6 and Hebrews 6:9–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he returns to Melchizedek"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 7, after the anchor behind the veil. He picks up the sentence he abandoned here and spends a whole chapter on it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is blamed.** Not the difficulty of the subject. The writer says he has a great deal to say and that the obstacle is dull hearing — and then takes two chapters to get them ready before saying it."}]}, "Hebrews 5:13–14": {"title": "Food you have to chew", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer explains the difference between the two diets, and the test he gives is not about knowledge."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Anyone who is **still living on milk cannot handle what is said about righteousness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apeiros logou dikaiosunes** — inexperienced in the word of righteousness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Apeiros** means untried, unskilled — the opposite of practised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that they have not been told. That they have not handled it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is **a baby**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **nepios** — infant, literally *non-speaking*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word Paul uses of the heir who is no different from a slave. See the note on Galatians 4:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other diet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Food you have to chew belongs to **grown men and women**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **teleion** — the mature, the complete. From **telos**, the end a thing is aiming at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The definition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People who have **worked their senses hard enough, and long enough**, to **tell what is good from what is evil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **gegumnasmena** — trained, exercised. From the root of *gymnasium*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **hexin** — a settled habit, a state produced by repetition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What maturity is not, in this sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Information. The writer has just said he has *a great deal to say* and cannot say it. See the note on Hebrews 5:11–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gap is not on the input side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Discernment produced by use. The Greek is literally *having the faculties trained by practice for the distinguishing of good and evil*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A trained sense is not a fact learned. It is what a person develops by handling something repeatedly until they can tell the difference without stopping to think."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reproach"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By this time **you ought to be the ones teaching**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 12, and the diagnosis is that they had gone backwards: *you have come to have need of milk*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the writer does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, **leaving the discussion of the elementary principles of Christ, let us go on to perfection**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *perfection* is **teleioteta** — the noun of the word he has just used for the grown."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He refuses to go back over the foundations, lists six of them so they know what he is leaving, and moves on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the solid food turns out to be"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Melchizedek. He had named it four times and broken off each time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He picks it up in chapter 7 and spends a whole chapter there: the priest with no genealogy, the oath in Psalm 110, and the change of law that follows a change of priesthood. See the notes on Psalms 110:4 and Hebrews 7:20–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Milk, solid food, and then two chapters of the hardest argument in the letter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer does not simplify. He tells them what they are missing and then delivers it anyway."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The test he gives.** Not what a person knows. Whether their senses have been worked hard enough and long enough to tell one thing from another without being told."}]}, "Hebrews 5:7–8": {"title": "Prayers and tears", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses about Gethsemane, written by somebody who was not there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While He was here **in flesh and blood**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **en tais hemerais tes sarkos autou** — in the days of His flesh."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter has spent four chapters on His superiority — above angels, above Moses, a priest after the order of Melchizedek — and then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **carried His prayers and His pleading** to the One who was able to save Him from death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **deeseis te kai hiketerias** — supplications and entreaties."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hiketeria** — originally the olive branch a suppliant held out when begging for their life. It occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Praying them **out loud**, **weeping** as He prayed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meta krauges ischuras kai dakruon** — with strong crying and tears."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Krauge** — a shout, an outcry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Gospels record"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And being in agony, He prayed more earnestly. Then His sweat became like great drops of blood falling down to the ground.* See the note on Luke 22:39–46."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Gospels do not mention the tears. Hebrews does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "To whom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To the One who was **able to save Him from death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ton dunamenon sozein auton ek thanatou** — the one able to save Him out of death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The preposition can mean *from* — spare Him it — or *out of* — bring Him through it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He was heard**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eisakoustheis** — he was heard, he was heeded."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was heard, and He died."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means the hearing and the sparing are not the same thing, and Hebrews states both without resolving the tension."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why He was heard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because of **how He held God in awe**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apo tes eulabeias** — from His godly fear, His reverent submission."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Eulabeia** — caution, reverence, taking hold carefully."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer that was prayed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Father, if it is Your will, take this cup away from Me; **nevertheless not My will, but Yours, be done**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The request was made, and the second half was made too."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The next sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was **the Son**, and even so **He learned obedience**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kaiper on huios emathen aph' hon epathen ten hupakoen**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Emathen… epathen** — learned… suffered. A near-rhyme in Greek, and a proverb in the classical world: *pathei mathos*, learning through suffering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that does not mean"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That He was disobedient and improved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Manthano** — to learn by experience, to come to know something from the inside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He knew obedience as an attribute. He came to know it as a cost."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The next verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And having been perfected, He became the author of eternal salvation to all who obey Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **teleiotheis** — having been completed, brought to the goal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not moral improvement. Completion of a course."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is arguing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **every high priest taken from among men**… can have compassion on those who are ignorant and going astray, since he himself is also subject to weakness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The qualification for priesthood is having been in the position of the people you represent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For we do not have a High Priest who cannot sympathise with our weaknesses, but was in all points **tempted as we are, yet without sin**.* See the note on Hebrews 4:14–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the letter goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Of whom we have much to say, and hard to explain, since **you have become dull of hearing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The author breaks off into a rebuke about milk and solid food, and does not return to Melchizedek until chapter 7."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two facts.** *He was heard*, and He died. Hebrews puts both in one sentence and does not explain the join."}]}, "Hebrews 5:9–10": {"title": "\"Carried all the way through to the finish\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses that finish one of the hardest paragraphs in the letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who, in the days of His flesh, when He had offered up **prayers and supplications, with vehement cries and tears** to Him who was able to save Him from death, and was heard because of His godly fear.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though He was a Son, yet **He learned obedience by the things which He suffered**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gethsemane, in the writer's account of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The completion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when He had been **carried all the way through to the finish**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **teleiotheis** — perfected, brought to the end, completed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a moral word. The same root is used of a sacrifice being accepted and of a priest being consecrated — the Greek Old Testament uses it for the ordination of Aaron."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing was lacking in Him. What was completed was the qualification: He went the whole way through the experience."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He became"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He became **the reason everyone who obeys Him is saved**, and **saved forever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the author of eternal salvation* — **aitios**, the cause, the responsible party."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Forever** — the salvation is described by its duration."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is named"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God Himself **named Him High Priest** — Melchizedek's kind of priest.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **prosagoreutheis** — designated, addressed by a title. It occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The appointment is God's. The writer has already made the point: *no man takes this honour to himself, but he who is called by God, just as Aaron was.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fourth mention of Melchizedek in the letter so far, and the argument keeps being deferred."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer names it here and then breaks off, because his readers are not in a state to follow it. See the note on Hebrews 5:11–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He picks it up again at chapter 7 and spends the whole chapter on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD has sworn, and He will not take it back: \"You are a priest forever, in the order of Melchizedek.\"* See the note on Psalms 110:4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two priesthoods"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aaron's line was appointed by command and served until death. This one was appointed by oath and holds it *forever*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the tears have to do with the priesthood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's argument earlier in the chapter: *for every high priest taken from among men is appointed for men… **he can have compassion on those who are ignorant and going astray, since he himself is also subject to weakness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A high priest has to be able to sympathise. Gethsemane is in the letter because of the job description."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the writer says it directly in chapter 4: *we do not have a High Priest who cannot sympathise with our weaknesses, but was in all points tempted as we are, yet without sin.* See the note on Hebrews 4:14–16."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was completed.** Not His character. The Greek is the word for a priest being consecrated — and the writer says the consecrating ran through prayers, cries and tears before the naming."}]}, "Hebrews 6:13–14": {"title": "\"He swore by His own name\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and the whole point is the absence of anybody higher."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When God made Abraham a promise, **there was no one greater for Him to swear by**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An oath works by staking something larger than yourself. A man swears by God because God outranks him and will hold him to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For God there is no such party."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He did about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **He swore by His own name**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of Genesis 22 is *by Myself I have sworn*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The guarantee and the guarantor are the same, which sounds like a weakness and is the opposite. If God is the highest thing there is, an oath sworn on Himself is backed by everything that exists."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Bless you I will; multiply you I will**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek doubles both verbs — *blessing I will bless you, and multiplying I will multiply you*. It is the Hebrew intensive construction carried straight over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same doubling appears in Jabez's prayer: *bless me, really bless me*. See the note on 1 Chronicles 4:9–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it was sworn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 22, on the mountain, immediately after the knife was stopped. See the notes on Genesis 22:11–12 and Genesis 22:15–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not at the call in Genesis 12, and not at the covenant in Genesis 15. After the son was laid on the wood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the writer says about Abraham next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And so, **after he had patiently endured, he obtained the promise**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **makrothumesas** — long-tempered, slow to burn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-five years between the promise of a son and Isaac's birth. And Abraham never saw the descendants as the stars of the sky."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews 11 says so plainly: *these all died in faith, not having received the promises, but having seen them afar off.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the writer brings it up here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses earlier he told them to *imitate those who through faith and patience inherit the promises*. See the note on Hebrews 6:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham is the worked example. The connective is *for* — this is why he said it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the oath is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's argument does not stop at Abraham. Four verses on: *God, determining to show more abundantly **to the heirs of promise** the immutability of His counsel, confirmed it by an oath.* See the note on Hebrews 6:16–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other oath in the letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD has sworn and will not relent: You are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer makes the same move there — an oath over the priesthood that the Levitical line never had. See the note on Hebrews 7:20–22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two oaths in the letter, one about a family and one about a priest."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is unusual about it.** God does not need to swear. The writer's point is that He did it anyway, and the reason given is not doubt on His side."}]}, "Hebrews 6:16–17": {"title": "\"So God went further\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer explains how human oaths work and then says God submitted to the procedure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it works among people"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People **swear by someone greater than themselves**, and once an oath is given **to settle a matter, the argument is over**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *an oath for confirmation is to them an end of all dispute* — **antilogia**, contradiction, argument back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That was the legal function. Where evidence ran out, an oath closed the case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **God went further**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *God, wanting more abundantly* — **perissoteron**, over and above."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had already promised. The oath is on top of a promise that was already given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He wanted **the people who would inherit what He promised** to be **in no doubt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tois kleronomois tes epangelias** — the heirs of the promise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not Abraham only. The writer has widened it, and the readers of the letter are inside the category."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were to be in no doubt about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That **His mind on this would never change**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **to ametatheton tes boules autou** — the unchangeableness of His purpose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oath does not make the purpose firmer. It makes the firmness visible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mechanism"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **He backed it with an oath**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *He interposed Himself with an oath* — **mesiteuo**, from the same root as **mesites**, mediator. He stepped in between."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is strange"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God has nothing to gain from swearing and nobody to swear by. See the note on Hebrews 6:13–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's point is that the oath exists entirely for the benefit of the people reading — an accommodation to how humans settle things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That by **two immutable things**, in which **it is impossible for God to lie**, we might have **strong consolation**.* See the note on Hebrews 6:18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two are the promise and the oath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the paragraph ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This hope we have as **an anchor of the soul**, both sure and steadfast, and **which enters the Presence behind the veil**, where the forerunner has entered for us — even Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An anchor that goes upward and inward rather than down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Forerunner** is **prodromos** — the scout who goes ahead of a party, or the small boat that carried a ship's anchor into harbour when the ship itself could not yet get in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter has done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It opened with the sharpest warning in the letter and closes with an anchor inside the veil. See the note on Hebrews 6:4–6."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the oath adds.** Nothing, to God. Everything, to the reader — the writer says outright that the reason it was given was so that the heirs would be in no doubt."}]}, "Hebrews 6:18": {"title": "Two things that cannot shift", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer builds a guarantee out of two items and names them both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now there are **two things that cannot shift**, and God cannot lie in either of them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before identify them: God made a promise to Abraham, and then He swore an oath to confirm it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because He could swear by no one greater, **He swore by Himself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A promise, and an oath. Both from someone who cannot lie."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the oath at all"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God, determining to show more abundantly to the heirs of promise the **immutability of His counsel**, confirmed it by an oath.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise was already sufficient. God's word does not require notarising."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oath was added — the text says so plainly — for the sake of the people receiving it, so that they would have two immovable things instead of one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He accommodated human uncertainty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refugees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And we, **who ran for shelter** and grabbed hold of the hope held out in front of us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is the language of fleeing for refuge — the image is of somebody running to the cities of refuge in Numbers 35, where a man who had killed accidentally could run and not be touched by the avenger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You did not have to argue your case at the gate. You had to get inside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Grabbed hold\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is to seize, to take firm hold of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And note what is grabbed: *the hope held out in front of us* — set before us, offered. It is extended first and taken second."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We have **something powerful to steady us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *strong encouragement* — **parakalesis**, the noun behind *Comforter*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The anchor"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *this hope we have as **an anchor of the soul, both sure and steadfast**, and which enters the Presence behind the veil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An anchor is thrown ahead of the ship into ground the ship has not reached, and it holds the vessel by being somewhere else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And this one is described as going *behind the veil* — into the holy of holies, the room nobody could enter except the high priest, once a year, with blood. See the note on Hebrews 9:6–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The anchor is not in the harbour. It is in the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where the forerunner has entered for us, even Jesus, having become High Priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for **forerunner** is *prodromos* — the scout who goes ahead of the main body."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A forerunner is meaningless unless others are following. The word implies the rest of the fleet."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter has come from.** Some of the hardest verses in the letter, about falling away — and the writer's own comment on them is *we are confident of better things concerning you.* Then Abraham, the oath, the anchor and the forerunner. The passage that frightens people ends in a room they cannot be got out of."}]}, "Hebrews 6:4–6": {"title": "The impossible thing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The hardest paragraph in the letter, and the first thing to see is who it is written to and what they were about to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the letter is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrew believers under pressure to go back to the temple. The whole letter is one long argument that there is nothing to go back to — a better priest, a better covenant, a better sacrifice, and all of it *once for all*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The temple was still standing. The offerings were still being made daily."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is **impossible**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **adunaton** — without power, incapable. The writer uses it four times in this letter:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*It is **impossible** for the blood of bulls and goats to take away sins.*", "*Without faith it is **impossible** to please Him.*", "*It is **impossible** for God to lie.* See the note on Hebrews 6:18.", "And this one."]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five descriptions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Have once been **given light**, have **tasted the gift** that came down from heaven, have been **given a share in the Holy Spirit**, have **tasted how good the word of God is** and **the powers of the age that is coming**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five, and two of them are the verb **tasted** — **geuomai**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being described"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exposure to the whole thing. Light, the heavenly gift, the Spirit, the word, and the powers of the coming age — these people had seen all of it at close range."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they then do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And then **turn their backs on it all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parapesontas** — to fall away alongside, to defect."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The specific defection in view is the one the whole letter is written against: going back to the sacrifices."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is impossible"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is **impossible to make them new again so that they change their minds**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *to renew them again to repentance* — **metanoia**, a change of mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They are **crucifying the Son of God over again, on their own account**, and **putting Him on show for people to jeer at**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anastauroo** (crucify again) and **paradeigmatizo** (expose to public shame)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what returning to the altar amounted to in the writer's argument. If the sacrifice was not enough, then the cross has to be repeated — and the letter has just spent five chapters saying it cannot be and does not need to be."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no second cross to go to. That is the impossibility."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the writer says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not leave it there. Three verses on:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But dear friends, even when we talk like this, **we are convinced of better things where you are concerned** — the things that come with being saved.* See the note on Hebrews 6:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He states the warning and then immediately says he does not think it applies to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On an oath sworn to Abraham, and two unchangeable things, and *a hope both sure and steadfast, which enters the Presence behind the veil.* See the notes on Hebrews 6:13–14, Hebrews 6:16–17 and Hebrews 6:18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that contains the sharpest warning ends on the strongest guarantee in the letter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the passage is arguing.** Not that a person can be lost and re-lost, but that there is only one sacrifice and it has already happened. A man walking back to an altar is walking toward something the letter says was finished."}]}, "Hebrews 6:9–10": {"title": "\"We are convinced of better things\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses after the hardest warning in the letter, the writer says he does not think it applies to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **dear friends**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **agapetoi** — beloved. It is the only time in the whole letter the writer uses it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He saves it for the sentence directly after the warning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The concession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even when **we talk like this**, we are **convinced of better things where you are concerned**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pepeismetha** — perfect passive, the same word Paul uses in Romans 8: *I am fully persuaded*. See the note on Romans 8:38–39."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A settled conviction, not a hope."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the better things are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The things that **come with being saved**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *things that accompany salvation*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the category rather than listing it, and then gives his evidence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The evidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God is **not unjust**. He will **not forget the work you did and the love you put into it** for His name.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument is about God's character, not their performance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Not unjust** — the writer puts it negatively. It would be an injustice for God to overlook it, so He will not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Looking after His people, **as you are still doing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *having ministered to the saints and still ministering*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two tenses. There is a record, and it is ongoing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 10 fills it in: *you had compassion on me in my chains, and **joyfully accepted the plundering of your goods**, knowing that you have a better and enduring possession for yourselves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had visited prisoners and lost their property for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the warning was given at all"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not because they had defected, but because the letter has just described them as sluggish: *you have become dull of hearing… you need someone to teach you again the first principles… you have come to need milk and not solid food.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The warning is aimed at where drifting ends up, and the writer says plainly he does not think they are there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And we desire that **each one of you show the same diligence** to the full assurance of hope until the end, that you do not become sluggish, but **imitate those who through faith and patience inherit the promises**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Full assurance** — the Greek is **plerophoria**, full carrying, complete certainty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then he names one of the imitators: Abraham. See the note on Hebrews 6:13–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shape of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Warning, then *but beloved*, then Abraham's oath, then two unchangeable things, then an anchor behind the veil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter with the sharpest edge in it ends with the strongest guarantee in the letter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the writer bases his confidence on.** Not their record — he has just called them dull of hearing. God's justice, and the fact that there is something already done that it would be unjust to forget."}]}, "Hebrews 7:1–2": {"title": "King of righteousness, king of peace", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man who appears in three verses of Genesis and one line of a psalm, and Hebrews builds a chapter on him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Melchizedek **ruled Salem** and was **priest of God Most High**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both offices in one person, which was not how Israel worked. Kings came from Judah and priests from Levi, and the one king who tried to burn incense came out a leper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Melchizedek holds both before either tribe exists."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 14, in the middle of a war story. Abraham has just rescued Lot and defeated four kings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Melchizedek king of Salem brought out bread and wine; he was the priest of God Most High.* See the note on Genesis 14:17–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He arrives, blesses, and leaves. Genesis says nothing else about him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The meeting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abraham was on his way back from **cutting down the kings** when Melchizedek met him and **blessed him**, and Abraham **gave him a tenth of everything**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tithe is a hundred and fifty years before the law that requires it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Hebrews makes the point about the blessing: *beyond all contradiction, the lesser is blessed by the better*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Start with his name: it means **king of righteousness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **malki-tsedeq** — my king is righteousness, or king of righteousness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tsedeq**, the same root as **tsedaqah**, righteousness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then take the place he ruled, **Salem, which means peace** — so, king of peace.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Shalem**, from **shalom**. And Salem is Jerusalem — the psalm says so: *in Salem also is His tabernacle, and His dwelling place in Zion*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Righteousness first, then peace. Hebrews takes them in the order the names come."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah has the same sequence: *the work of righteousness will be peace, and the effect of righteousness, quietness and assurance forever*. See the note on Isaiah 32:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Paul: *having been justified by faith, we have peace with God*. See the note on Romans 5:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the psalm where the two meet: *righteousness and peace have kissed*. See the note on Psalms 85:10–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every time, in that order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Without father, without mother, without genealogy, having neither beginning of days nor end of life, but made like the Son of God, **remains a priest continually**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a claim that he had no parents. An argument from the silence of the record: Genesis gives his lineage nowhere, which for a priest is remarkable, since the whole Levitical system ran on documented descent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezra records priests excluded from service because *their genealogy was not found*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD has sworn and will not relent, \"**You are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 110, verse 4. See the note on Psalms 110:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written by David, about somebody he calls *my Lord*, a thousand years after Genesis 14 and a thousand before Hebrews."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the argument matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus is from Judah. *For it is evident that our Lord arose from Judah, of which tribe Moses spoke nothing concerning priesthood.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under Levitical rules He could not be a priest at all. Hebrews answers by pointing at a priest who predates Levi and outranks Abraham."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The change"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the priesthood being changed, **of necessity there is also a change of the law**.* See the note on Hebrews 7:15–17."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order the names come in.** King of righteousness, and then king of peace — and Hebrews stops to point it out before saying anything else about him."}]}, "Hebrews 7:15–17": {"title": "The power of an endless life", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The argument of the chapter, stated in one sentence about qualifications."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It becomes **plainer still** when **another priest steps forward**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **perissoteron katadelon** — far more evident."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has been building since Melchizedek arrived. See the note on Hebrews 7:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for another"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **heteros** — another of a different kind, not **allos**, another of the same kind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one more Levite. A different sort of priest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disqualification that does not apply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A priest **not by some rule about flesh and blood**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kata nomon entoles sarkines** — according to the law of a fleshly commandment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Levitical priesthood ran on descent. A man was a priest because of who his father was, and Ezra excluded men who could not produce the paperwork."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It also ran on mortality. Every high priest died and another took over — the chapter says so a few verses earlier: *there were many priests, because they were prevented by death from continuing*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The qualification that does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But by **the power of a life that cannot be ended**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dunamin zoes akatalutou** — the power of an indissoluble life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Akatalutos** — that cannot be dissolved or broken up. It occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The credential is not lineage or appointment. It is that He is not going to die."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proof text"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You are a priest forever — Melchizedek's kind of priest**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 110:4, quoted for the third time in Hebrews."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole argument rests on one line of one psalm, written by David, about a figure who appears in three verses of Genesis."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **there is an annulling of the former commandment** because of its weakness and unprofitableness, for the law made nothing perfect; on the other hand, **there is the bringing in of a better hope, through which we draw near to God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *annulling* is **athetesis** — a legal term for setting aside, cancelling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And inasmuch as **He was not made priest without an oath**… the LORD has sworn and will not relent.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aaron was appointed. Nobody swore an oath about the Levitical priesthood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anametaletos** — He will not change His mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By so much more Jesus has become **a surety of a better covenant**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **egguos** — a guarantor. Somebody who stands good for another's debt with his own person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse the chapter ends on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore He is also able to **save to the uttermost** those who come to God through Him, since **He always lives to make intercession for them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eis to panteles** — completely, to the end, all the way through."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *always lives* is the same point again: the intercession does not stop because the intercessor does not die."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The summary a verse later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For such a High Priest was fitting for us, who is **holy, harmless, undefiled, separate from sinners**, and has become higher than the heavens; who does not need daily, as those high priests, to offer up sacrifices, first for His own sins and then for the people's, for **this He did once for all when He offered up Himself**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What changed with the priesthood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the priesthood being changed, of necessity **there is also a change of the law**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's most consequential sentence, and it is an aside."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The credential.** Not descent and not appointment. *The power of an indissoluble life* — the priesthood holds because the priest does not die."}]}, "Hebrews 7:20–22": {"title": "The oath", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer has been building a case out of Genesis 14 and Psalm 110, and here he adds one more piece: an oath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being compared"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Those other priests **were made priests with no oath sworn over them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aaron's line was appointed by command. Exodus says *take Aaron your brother, and his sons with him* — an instruction, not a vow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer has combed the Old Testament for a divine oath attached to the Levitical priesthood and found none."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath that does exist"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The Lord has sworn it and will not change His mind: You are a priest forever — Melchizedek's kind of priest**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 110:4, the verse after the one about the footstool. See the note on Mark 12:35–37."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David wrote it about a descendant of his. The order named is older than Aaron by four hundred years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Will not change His mind\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *will not repent*. The same construction appears in Numbers — *God is not a man, that He should lie, nor a son of man, that He should repent.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An oath is a promise with a second layer under it. God swearing means the thing is placed beyond revision."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer makes the point outright a chapter earlier: *God, determining to show more abundantly to the heirs of promise the immutability of His counsel, **confirmed it by an oath**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Forever"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eis ton aiona** — into the age."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Levitical priest served from thirty to fifty and died. The writer says as much: *there have been many priests, because they were prevented by death from continuing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oath's subject does not vacate the post."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**That is the measure of it: Jesus has become the guarantee of a better covenant**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *guarantee* is **engyos** — a surety, the person who stands good for somebody else's debt. It appears once in the New Testament, here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A surety is not a witness or a middleman. He is liable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction it runs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elsewhere the writer calls Him **mesites**, mediator, standing between the two parties. **Engyos** is stronger: He is not holding the covenant open, He is standing behind it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The covenant's security rests on the person sworn about rather than on the people covered by it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Melchizedek came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He walks out of Genesis 14 with bread and wine, blesses Abram, receives a tenth, and walks off the page. Four verses in the whole of the Old Testament, plus one line in a psalm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer of Hebrews builds two chapters on him. See the note on Genesis 14:17–20 and on Hebrews 7:8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is unusual about the argument.** The writer's evidence is partly what is *not* in the text. No oath over Aaron, no genealogy for Melchizedek, no record of his birth or death — and he reads the silences as deliberate."}]}, "Hebrews 7:4–5": {"title": "\"Think how big a figure this must be\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer asks his readers to work out the rank of a man from a single transaction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **think how big a figure this must be**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *consider* — **theoreo**, to look at attentively, the root of *theory*. He is asking for deliberate observation rather than agreement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The evidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abraham himself, **the father of the whole family**, handed him a tenth of what he had taken.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument is entirely about who gave to whom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "To his Jewish readers, Abraham is the top of the tree. There is no higher human authority in their history — the covenant, the promise and the nation all begin with him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Abraham gave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule the argument runs on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated three verses later: *beyond all contradiction, **the lesser is blessed by the better**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Melchizedek blessed Abraham and received a tenth from him. On that rule alone, Melchizedek outranks him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison with Levi"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The law tells **Levi's sons** who take up the priesthood **to collect a tenth from the people** — from their own brothers, who came out of Abraham exactly as they did.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Levitical priests also received tithes. But note the differences the writer is setting up:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["they collected **because the law told them to** — Melchizedek received without any law existing", "they collected **from their brothers** — men of the same standing as themselves", "both parties *came out of Abraham* — nobody in that arrangement outranks anybody"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Melchizedek has no genealogy at all, and Abraham paid him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If perfection were through the Levitical priesthood… what further need was there that another priest should rise according to the order of Melchizedek?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole chapter is establishing that a better priesthood was promised — in Psalm 110, a thousand years after Abraham and centuries after Levi — and that it is not Aaron's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD has sworn and will not relent: You are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the clause that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Here mortal men receive tithes, but there **He receives them, of whom it is witnessed that He lives**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is verse 8, and it is the writer's own contrast: the Levites died and were replaced, generation after generation. The other one is testified to be alive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on Hebrews 7:8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter concludes about Him.** *Therefore He is also able to save to the uttermost those who come to God through Him, since **He always lives to make intercession for them**.* The permanence of the priesthood is the whole point, and the argument for it started with one afternoon in a valley."}]}, "Hebrews 7:6–7": {"title": "\"The lesser is blessed by the greater\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer states a rule nobody disputes and uses it to settle a question about rank."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **nobody argues with this: the lesser is blessed by the greater**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *beyond all contradiction*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that world, blessing ran downhill. A father blessed a son, a priest blessed the people, a patriarch blessed the tribes. The direction of the blessing told you who outranked whom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is being applied to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This man is **not out of their line at all** — and he **took a tenth from Abraham** and **spoke a blessing over the man who held the promises**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two facts about Melchizedek, and both point the same way: he received from Abraham, and he blessed Abraham. See the note on Genesis 14:17–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The man who held the promises\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer chooses the biggest possible description of Abraham before making him the lesser party."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *Abraham the shepherd*, or *Abraham the newcomer*. The man who held the promises — the whole covenant, the land, the nation, the seed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That man received a blessing, which means somebody greater gave it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Not out of their line at all\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Levites collected tithes because the law told them to, from brothers who came out of Abraham exactly as they did. Melchizedek has no genealogy in the record at all, and he collected from Abraham himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the argument is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole chapter is establishing that a priesthood exists which is not Aaron's, and which is better."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If perfection were through the Levitical priesthood… what further need was there that another priest should rise according to the order of Melchizedek?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 110 promised one, a thousand years after Abraham and centuries after Levi."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same principle lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody blesses himself. Blessing is received from somebody higher, by definition — that is what the rule says."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is why the great blessings in Scripture are always given rather than obtained: Melchizedek to Abraham, Isaac to Jacob, Jacob to the twelve, the priests to Israel with the Aaronic blessing, and Jesus with His hands raised over the disciples as He was carried up. See the note on Acts 1:9–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chapter's conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore He is also **able to save to the uttermost** those who come to God through Him, since **He always lives to make intercession for them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *to the uttermost* is completely, to the end, all the way through."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one who is greater.** *Here mortal men receive tithes, but there He receives them, **of whom it is witnessed that He lives**.* One verse later, and it is the writer's own summary of the difference. See the note on Hebrews 7:8."}]}, "Hebrews 7:8": {"title": "The one receiving it now is alive", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*Here, the men who collect the tenth are men who die. There, the one who collects it is the one the testimony says is alive.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and the tense in it is the thing to see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two priesthoods, two conditions"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**here** — the Levites, receiving tithes and dying, generation after generation. Aaron's robes taken off one man and put on his son, over and over.", "**there** — Melchizedek, of whom the record says only that he lives. No genealogy, no death notice, no successor."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's argument is that Christ is a priest *in the order of Melchizedek*, and that this order does not run out of men."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The present tense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb for receiving is present. The writer does not say tithes *were* received; he says they are, and that the one receiving them is testified to be living."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a striking thing to leave standing in a letter written decades after the cross. Whatever a reader concludes about how much or how often, the picture the sentence gives is of a living Priest still receiving what is brought to Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the argument needed Abraham"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham gave the tenth to Melchizedek before Levi was born — and the writer says Levi, still in his ancestor, paid it through him. The greater blesses the lesser; the lesser pays the tenth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the tenth in scripture is first an act of honour toward a priest-king who blesses, and only later a Mosaic arrangement for feeding a tribe. The order runs blessing first, response after, from the very first time it appears."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter lands.** *He is able to save completely those who come to God through Him, because He always lives to intercede for them.* The same word — He **lives** — carries both halves: the one who receives, and the one who never stops praying for the people who come."}]}, "Hebrews 7:9–10": {"title": "Levi paid it through Abraham", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer makes an argument about a man who was not born yet, and he knows how it sounds."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"You could even put it this way\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He flags it himself — *so to speak*, in most translations. He is aware he is pressing something."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then he presses it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Levi, **who collects the tenth, paid it himself** — paid it through Abraham. **He was still inside his forefather's body** when Melchizedek came out to meet him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Levi is Abraham's great-grandson. He is four generations away and centuries from existing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer says he was there, in Abraham, and that what Abraham did that afternoon counted for him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the argument is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole of Hebrews 7 is establishing that Melchizedek's priesthood outranks Levi's, and therefore that Christ's does — *You are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The logic: the lesser is blessed by the greater. Abraham gave a tenth to Melchizedek and received a blessing from him. So Melchizedek is the greater — and Levi, still in Abraham's body, was on the paying end of that transaction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle underneath it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man's act reached forward into descendants who did not exist."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same structure for Adam — *by one man's disobedience many were made sinners* — and for Christ: *by one Man's obedience many will be made righteous.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "What one man did, a whole line was counted in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Melchizedek"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*King of Salem, priest of God Most High* — Genesis 14, and he appears in three verses and vanishes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He brings out **bread and wine** to Abraham coming back from the slaughter of the kings, and blesses him. Then Abraham gives him a tenth of everything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews notes what the record leaves out: no father, no mother, no genealogy, no beginning of days, no end of life — *made like the Son of God, he remains a priest continually.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the tithe stands in the argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham gave it before the law existed. Sinai was four hundred years off; there was no commandment, no tabernacle and no Levitical system to fund."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gave it to a priest-king who came out with bread and wine, and the writer of Hebrews uses that afternoon to prove a priesthood that has no end."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Abraham refused the same day.** The king of Sodom offered him the goods and he would not take a thread or a sandal-strap — *lest you should say, \"I have made Abram rich.\"* He gave a tenth to the priest and turned down the king, on one evening."}]}, "Hebrews 8:10–12": {"title": "\"I will remember no more\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The longest Old Testament quotation in the New Testament, and the writer of Hebrews reproduces it in full because of how it ends."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Four promises, all first person"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every clause is God's own undertaking. Nothing in the new covenant is stated as the people's contribution:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**I will set My laws inside their thinking and carve them into their hearts**", "**I will be their God, and they will be My own people**", "**all of them will know Me already**", "**I will show mercy… and their sins I will remember no more**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old covenant was an agreement with terms on both sides. Read this list and notice there is only one signatory doing anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Inside, not on stone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will set My laws inside their thinking and carve them into their hearts.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is the one used of engraving. The law is not abolished in this promise — it is relocated. Out of a box, off tablets, into the person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah wrote this while watching the temple system collapse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"From the lowest of them to the highest\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in the arrangement needs an intermediary to tell them who God is. In a world of priests and Levites and scribes, that is a considerable claim, and it is made about *all of them*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their sins and their lawless deeds I will remember no more.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not overlooked, not filed. God undertakes to not remember — which is not forgetfulness in a being who knows everything, but a decision about what will be brought up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer of Hebrews quotes the whole passage twice, and both times he is building to that sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he concludes from it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where these have been forgiven, there is no longer any offering for sin.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is his argument in a line. If the sins are not remembered, there is nothing left for a sacrifice to do — which is why the priest in this letter is sitting down. See the note on Hebrews 1:1–3."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word \"new.\"** *By calling this covenant new, He has made the first one obsolete.* The writer says it plainly one verse before this quotation, and it is the whole reason he is quoting Jeremiah at all."}]}, "Hebrews 8:6–7": {"title": "If there had been nothing wrong with the first", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and the second is an argument from the existence of a second covenant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The work He has been given is **finer by far**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **diaphoroteras leitourgias** — a more excellent ministry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Leitourgia** is the priestly service word, and it is what the word *liturgy* comes from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ratio"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Exactly as much finer as **the covenant He stands in the middle of is better than the old one**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kreittonos diathekes mesites** — mediator of a better covenant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kreitton** — better. The letter's favourite word. It uses it thirteen times: a better hope, a better covenant, better promises, better sacrifices, a better country, a better resurrection."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mediator"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Mesites** — a go-between, one who stands in the middle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word appears three times in Hebrews and always of Christ."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses was the mediator of the first: *it was appointed through angels by the hand of a mediator*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promises"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And that covenant was **set up on better promises**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **nenomothetetai** — enacted, made law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promises come first and the covenant is legislated on them. Not the other way round."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the point of the whole argument: the old covenant's promises were conditional on performance. *If you diligently obey… then all these blessings shall come upon you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The new one's promises are the four in the Jeremiah quotation that follows: I will put My laws in their minds, I will be their God, they shall all know Me, I will remember their sins no more."}, {"k": "p", "t": "All four are *I will*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If there had been **nothing wrong with the first one**, **no one would have gone looking for a second**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ei amemptos** — if faultless."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The logic is simple and it is the letter's method throughout: the existence of the replacement proves the inadequacy of the original."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody drafts a second contract for a working one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the second is announced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verses quote Jeremiah 31 at length — the longest Old Testament quotation in the New Testament. See the note on Hebrews 8:10–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Behold, the days are coming… when I will make a new covenant with the house of Israel.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The announcement is in the Old Testament itself, which is the writer's strongest card. He is not arguing against Scripture; he is quoting it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the fault lay"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **finding fault with them**, He says…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek grammar is ambiguous and can be read as finding fault with the covenant or with the people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter has already made the point elsewhere: *for the law made nothing perfect*, and *what the law could not do in that it was weak through the flesh*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fault is in the material it was working with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In that He says, \"**A new covenant**,\" **He has made the first obsolete**. Now what is becoming obsolete and growing old is ready to vanish away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pepalaioken** — has made old."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written, on most datings, within a few years of AD 70, when the temple and its whole apparatus were destroyed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the mediator does now"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore He is also able to save to the uttermost those who come to God through Him, since He always lives to make intercession for them.* See the note on Hebrews 7:15–17."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The argument in one sentence.** Nobody looks for a second covenant if the first one works — and the search is recorded in the first covenant's own documents."}]}, "Hebrews 8:8–9": {"title": "\"The days are coming\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer begins the longest Old Testament quotation in the New Testament, and the first thing he does is say where the fault lay."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **if that first covenant had been faultless, then no place would have been sought for a second**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An argument from the existence of the promise. A replacement was promised, so the first was not final."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the blame goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But God does **lay the blame on them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **memphomenos autois** — finding fault with them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Them**, not it. The writer is careful. He has just spent chapters saying the law was given by God, and the fault named is in the party that could not keep it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it plainly elsewhere: *what the law could not accomplish, **weakened as it was by the old nature***. See the note on Romans 8:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah 31, and Hebrews quotes it at greater length than any other Old Testament passage in the New Testament — six verses straight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The announcement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look, **the days are coming**, says the Lord, when I will make **a new covenant** with the house of Israel and with the house of Judah.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kaine** — new in kind, not merely recent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah said it while Jerusalem was falling to Babylon, in a book otherwise full of judgment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Both houses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The house of Israel **and** the house of Judah.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The kingdom had been split for centuries and the northern tribes had been gone for over a hundred years when Jeremiah wrote. The promise names both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It will **not be like the covenant I made with their fathers** on the day I **took them by the hand and led them out of the land of Egypt**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tenderness of the image is the point. *Took them by the hand* — the way you lead a child across a road."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah does not describe the first covenant as harsh. He describes it as a hand held."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What went wrong"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**They did not stay inside that covenant of Mine**, and so **I turned away from them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of Jeremiah adds a clause the Greek renders differently: *though I was a husband to them*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way, the failure named is on the human side of the agreement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next in the quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will put My laws in their mind and write them on their hearts… **and their sins and their lawless deeds I will remember no more**.\"* See the note on Hebrews 8:10–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four promises, all in the first person, and nothing required in return."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The writer's conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In that He says, \"A new covenant,\" **He has made the first obsolete**. Now what is becoming obsolete and growing old **is ready to vanish away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **peleroken** — made old. And **aphanismou** — disappearance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written while the temple was still standing and the sacrifices were still being made."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the argument goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews quotes the same Jeremiah passage a second time in chapter 10, in two halves with a joint in the middle. See the note on Hebrews 10:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter's whole case rests on a promise made in the middle of a siege."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the fault is placed.** Not on the covenant and not on the giver. The Greek pronoun is plural and it points at the people — and the covenant it describes is one where God took them by the hand."}]}, "Hebrews 9:11–12": {"title": "He brought His own", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and every clause is a comparison with what the high priest used to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A description of the tabernacle: the lampstand, the table, the showbread, the veil, and behind it the ark with the golden pot of manna, Aaron's rod that budded, and the tablets."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the arrangement: *into the second part the high priest went alone once a year, not without blood, which he offered for himself and for the people's sins committed in ignorance*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One man, one day, once a year, and never without blood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **Christ has come**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is the **High Priest of everything good that was on its way**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *high priest of good things to come*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The good things are the substance the whole system was a shadow of: *for the law, having a shadow of the good things to come, and not the very image of the things*. See the note on Hebrews 10:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He passed through **a greater tent, a truer one, which no human hands put up** — it does not belong to this made world at all.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ou cheiropoietou** — not hand-made. And **ou tautes tes ktiseos** — not of this creation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses had been told to build his *according to the pattern shown you on the mountain*. The tabernacle was a copy, and the writer says so: *the copies of the things in the heavens*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He did not bring"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He did **not bring the blood of goats and calves**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two animals of the Day of Atonement: a bull for the priest's own sin, and goats for the people's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He did bring"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He brought **His own**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words in the English and two in the Greek — **dia tou idiou haimatos**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The high priest carried blood in a basin. This one carried His own, which means the priest and the sacrifice are the same person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter has already said it: *who does not need daily, as those high priests, to offer up sacrifices, first for His own sins and then for the people's, for this He did once for all **when He offered up Himself***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The frequency"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He went into the holy place **one time only**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ephapax** — once for all. The letter's key word, and it uses it three times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old arrangement's defining feature was repetition. Every year, the same day, the same ritual, because it had not worked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He came away **having bought us back forever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **aionian lutrosin heuramenos** — having obtained eternal redemption."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Lutrosis** — the ransom price paid to free a slave or a prisoner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Aionian** — eternal, and the adjective is doing the work. Not a year's covering, renewed annually."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For if the blood of bulls and goats… sanctifies for the purifying of the flesh, **how much more shall the blood of Christ**, who through the eternal Spirit offered Himself without spot to God, **cleanse your conscience from dead works to serve the living God**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old system reached the flesh. The claim here is that this one reaches the conscience."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The will"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For where there is a testament, there must also of necessity be the death of the testator. For a testament is in force after men are dead, since it has no power at all while the testator lives.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A pun that works in Greek and not in Hebrew: **diatheke** means both *covenant* and *will*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three appearings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has appeared to put away sin by the sacrifice of Himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now to appear in the presence of God for us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To those who eagerly wait for Him He will appear a second time, apart from sin, for salvation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past, present, future — in three consecutive sentences."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What changed between the two priests.** Not the ritual and not the building. The high priest carried blood in a basin; this one carried His own, which makes the priest and the offering the same person."}]}, "Hebrews 9:13–14": {"title": "How much more", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The argument of the whole letter in two sentences, and it runs from the lesser to the greater."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The invitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Think it through.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ei gar** — for if. Hebrews sets up a conditional and lets the reader finish it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The granted premise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The blood of bulls and goats, and **the ashes of a burned young cow** sprinkled over people who had been made unclean, **set them apart and got them clean on the outside**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews concedes that the old system worked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It did what it was designed to do. The writer does not dismiss it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three sacrifices named"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Bulls and goats** — the Day of Atonement, when the high priest went in with blood once a year."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The ashes of a heifer** — the red heifer of Numbers 19, burned outside the camp, its ashes kept and mixed with running water for anyone defiled by a corpse. See the note on Numbers 19:1–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they achieved"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Set them apart and got them clean **on the outside**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pros ten tes sarkos katharoteta** — to the purity of the flesh."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ceremonial standing. A man could re-enter the camp and go to the tabernacle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter has already said what it could not do: *sacrifices which cannot make him who performed the service perfect **in regard to the conscience***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **how much more** will the blood of Christ do.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **poso mallon**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rabbinic argument from lesser to greater. Hebrews uses it repeatedly, and Paul uses it in Romans 5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three clauses about Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who through **the eternal Spirit** offered **Himself** to God with **no flaw in Him anywhere**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dia pneumatos aioniou** — through eternal Spirit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Heauton prosenenken** — offered Himself. Priest and offering in one person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Amomon** — without blemish. The technical word for a sacrificial animal that passed inspection."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the animals lacked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Willingness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every animal was brought. This one is described as offering itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The effect"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **scour your conscience clean of all that dead work**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kathariei ten suneidesin humon apo nekron ergon** — will cleanse your conscience from dead works."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Suneidesis** — conscience. What the old system could not reach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For then would they not have ceased to be offered? For the worshippers, once purified, would have had **no more consciousness of sins**.* See the note on Hebrews 10:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Dead works"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nekron ergon** — works of a corpse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase appears twice in Hebrews, and both times among the elementary things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under Numbers 19, contact with a corpse was the thing the ashes were for. The letter takes the category and applies it to religious effort."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **you can serve the living God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **latreuein Theo zonti** — to serve as a priest serves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dead works, and the living God. The contrast is deliberate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the cleansing is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not comfort. Service."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The conscience is scoured so the person can approach and work, which is what a defiled priest could not do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And for this reason **He is the Mediator of the new covenant**… that those who are called may receive the promise of the eternal inheritance.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **without shedding of blood there is no remission**.* See the note on Hebrews 9:21–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Christ was offered once to bear the sins of many. To those who eagerly wait for Him **He will appear a second time, apart from sin, for salvation**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The premise granted.** Hebrews does not say the old system failed. It says it cleansed the outside, and asks what the greater blood does — and the answer is the conscience."}]}, "Hebrews 9:21–22": {"title": "Nearly everything", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses summarising an entire system, and the qualifier in the middle is deliberate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses at Sinai, after reading the book of the covenant aloud: *he took the blood of calves and goats, with water, scarlet wool, and hyssop, and **sprinkled both the book itself and all the people**, saying, \"This is the blood of the covenant which God has commanded you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book was sprinkled. The document itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He sprinkled blood on **the tent as well, and on everything used in the worship**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus records the anointing oil on the tabernacle and its furniture; Josephus and the rabbinic tradition preserve the blood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in the building — the table, the lampstand, the altar, the basins, the shovels."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Under the law **nearly everything is cleansed with blood**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **schedon** — nearly, almost."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The qualifier is exact. There were exceptions: a very poor man could bring flour for a sin offering — *a tenth of an ephah of fine flour… he shall put no oil on it, nor shall he put frankincense on it, for it is a sin offering*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And some purifications used water or fire."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer knows the code and does not overstate it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **where no blood is poured out, there is no forgiveness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **haimatekchusias** — a blood-pouring, and the word occurs once in the New Testament. The writer may have coined it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Aphesis** — release, dismissal, remission. The word for cancelling a debt or freeing a prisoner."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the rule comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus states it: *for the life of the flesh is in the blood, and I have given it to you upon the altar to make atonement for your souls; **for it is the blood that makes atonement for the soul**.* See the note on Leviticus 17:10–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the reason blood was reserved and never eaten."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the argument is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to establish that blood is required — the readers knew that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "To establish what follows: *therefore it was necessary that the copies of the things in the heavens should be purified with these, but **the heavenly things themselves with better sacrifices than these**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For Christ has not entered the holy places made with hands, which are copies of the true, but **into heaven itself, now to appear in the presence of God for us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not that He should offer Himself often… **He then would have had to suffer often** since the foundation of the world; but now, once at the end of the ages, He has appeared to put away sin by the sacrifice of Himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument turns on frequency. If the offering needed repeating, so would the dying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three appearings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has appeared to put away sin by the sacrifice of Himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now to appear in the presence of God for us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To those who eagerly wait for Him He will appear a second time, apart from sin, for salvation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past, present and future, in consecutive sentences."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He brought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not the blood of goats and calves. **He brought His own**.* See the note on Hebrews 9:11–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which makes the priest and the sacrifice the same person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other side of the rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In whom we have redemption through His blood, the forgiveness of sins.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *the blood of Jesus Christ His Son washes us clean of every sin*. See the note on 1 John 1:7."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word that keeps it honest.** *Nearly.* The writer states the rule about blood and adds a qualifier — because he knows the exceptions and is not willing to overstate the case."}]}, "Hebrews 9:23–24": {"title": "\"There He is now, standing before God's face on our account\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The writer has spent a chapter on the tabernacle, and here he says what all of it was a model of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Copies and the real thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ did not walk into a holy place that hands had built, a stand-in for the real one. He walked into heaven itself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *stand-in* is **antitypos** — a corresponding figure, a copy made to match a pattern. Moses had been shown the pattern on the mountain and told to build to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the tent in the desert was never the original. It was a scale model of something that already existed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not into the model. *Into heaven itself* — the place the tent was copied from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the argument of the whole letter compressed: everything Israel did once a year in a tent, He did once, in the real one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The present tense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there He is now, standing before God's face on our account.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *went and returned*. He is there, presently, in front of God — and the phrase is *on our behalf*, in our interest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb for appearing is the one used of being made visible, presented. What is presented before God is Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that does to prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gold plate on Aaron's forehead made Israel's gifts acceptable while he stood in the tent. Here the writer says the priest is inside the true holy place, permanently, and that He is there for us."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is why the letter can tell its readers to walk right up to the throne. The boldness is not confidence in ourselves; it is knowledge of who is already standing there."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line just after.** He did not go in *to offer Himself again and again*, as the high priest entered yearly with blood that was not his own. Once, at the end of the ages, to put away sin by the sacrifice of Himself. The visit is not repeated because it does not need to be."}]}, "Hebrews 9:27–28": {"title": "Once, and once", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two \"once\"s set side by side, and one of them answers the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People **die once**; that is set. And after that comes **the judgement**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *it is appointed* — laid down, assigned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two facts about everybody, stated without qualification, and the writer does not soften either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **Christ was offered one time**, to carry away the sins of many.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hapax** — once, once for all. The same word as in the first clause."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parallel is the argument: one death for people, one offering for Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the point the whole letter has been building. The priests *stand ministering daily, offering repeatedly the same sacrifices*, and He *sat down*. See the note on Hebrews 10:11–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"To carry away\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anaphero** — to bear up, to carry. It is the verb the Greek Old Testament uses of the scapegoat carrying the sins away into the wilderness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second appearance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He will appear a second time** — **sin will have nothing to do with it** — to bring salvation to those who are watching for Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The clause in the middle is the striking one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first appearance dealt with sin. The second is described as having no relation to it at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Those who are **watching for Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apekdechomai** — to await eagerly, to expect. Paul uses it of creation groaning and waiting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern underneath it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On the Day of Atonement the high priest went into the holy place with blood, and the people waited outside to see him come out again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "If he came out, the sacrifice had been accepted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter has just spent a chapter on that ceremony. See the note on Hebrews 9:6–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any second offering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter says it elsewhere in so many words: *He then would have had to suffer often since the foundation of the world; but now, **once at the end of the ages**, He has appeared to put away sin by the sacrifice of Himself.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the verse a chapter later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By one offering He has **perfected forever** those who are being sanctified.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two \"once\"s do together.** A man dies once and is judged. Christ was offered once and carries it. The writer puts them in one sentence so that the second is read as the answer to the first."}]}, "Hebrews 9:3–5": {"title": "The tent that stood on earth", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An inventory of the inner room, and then the writer stops himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The curtain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behind **a second curtain** was another room.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meta to deuteron katapetasma**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two curtains: one at the entrance to the holy place, and one before the innermost room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And this one they called **the holiest place there was**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **Hagia Hagion** — Holy of Holies. A Hebrew superlative rendered into Greek."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The high priest entered once a year, on the Day of Atonement, with blood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **gold vessel they burned incense in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Thumiaterion** — a censer, or the golden altar of incense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus places the incense altar outside the veil. Hebrews associates it with the inner room, which matches its function: it stood immediately before the curtain and its smoke went through."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chest"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the covenant chest, gold over every inch of it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ark. Acacia wood overlaid with gold inside and out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three contents"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Inside the chest **a gold jar of the bread from heaven**, and **the staff of Aaron that had budded**, and **the stone tablets the covenant was written on**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the three are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The manna** — the food that would not keep, kept. One omer laid up *before the Testimony*. See the note on Exodus 16:19–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Aaron's rod** — a dead stick that *sprouted and put forth buds, produced blossoms and yielded ripe almonds* overnight, when the priesthood was disputed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The tablets** — the second set, since Moses broke the first at the foot of the mountain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they have in common"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each one is a memorial of a rebellion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The manna, from the complaining about food. The rod, from Korah's challenge to the priesthood. The tablets, from the golden calf. See the note on Exodus 32:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three records of failure, sealed in a gold box under the mercy seat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is above"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Above the chest were **the shining winged figures**, their wings spread **over the place where mercy was given**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **Cheroubim doxes** — cherubim of glory. And **hilasterion** — the place of propitiation, the mercy seat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Septuagint uses **hilasterion** for the Hebrew **kapporet** — the lid of the ark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where else the word appears"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whom God set forth as **a propitiation** by His blood, through faith.* — of Christ, in Romans 3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same noun."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there I will meet with you, and I will speak with you **from above the mercy seat, from between the two cherubim**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Blood was sprinkled on it once a year, over the record of the broken covenant inside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is **far more that could be said about all of this, but not now**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **peri hon ouk estin nun legein kata meros** — concerning which it is not now to speak in detail."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer stops. He has a different argument to make, and the furniture is not it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he goes on to say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Christ came as High Priest… **with His own blood He entered the Most Holy Place once for all**, having obtained eternal redemption.* See the note on Hebrews 9:11–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How much more will the blood of Christ… **scour your conscience clean of all that dead work**.* See the note on Hebrews 9:13–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened to the curtain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then, behold, **the veil of the temple was torn in two from top to bottom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew notes the direction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, brethren, having boldness to enter the Holiest by the blood of Jesus, by a new and living way which He consecrated for us, **through the veil, that is, His flesh**.* See the note on Hebrews 10:19–21."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The three objects.** Manna, a budded rod, and stone tablets — memorials of three rebellions, sealed in a gold box, with the mercy seat as its lid."}]}, "Hebrews 9:6–7": {"title": "\"Only one day a year, and never without blood\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses describing an arrangement, and every clause in them is a restriction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The outer room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The priests were **in and out of the front room every day**, doing the work of the worship.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daily traffic. The lampstand to trim, the bread to change, the incense morning and evening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ordinary priests, ordinary access, ordinary work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inner room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the inner room — **only the high priest** went in there, and **only one day a year**, and **never without blood**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three limits stacked in one sentence:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["one man", "one day", "and a required condition"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Day of Atonement, Yom Kippur. Once a year, and Leviticus 16 sets out the procedure in detail — the linen garments, the washing, the incense to cover the mercy seat in cloud, the blood sprinkled seven times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aaron is warned that he *shall not come at just any time into the Holy Place*, on pain of death."}, {"k": "h", "t": "For himself first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which he offered **for himself** and for the sins the people had blundered into.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The high priest could not represent anybody until he had dealt with himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews makes the point again a few chapters later: *He does not need daily, as those high priests, to offer up sacrifices, first for His own sins and then for the people's.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The sins the people had blundered into\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **agnoema** — sins of ignorance, things done without knowing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the Day of Atonement covered. The system had provision for what people did not realise they had done, and nothing at all for deliberate, high-handed sin."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the writer concludes from it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Holy Spirit indicating this, that **the way into the Holiest of All was not yet made manifest** while the first tabernacle was still standing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The closed curtain was a message. As long as that room was shut, the point was being made that the way in was not open."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what happened to the curtain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then, behold, the veil of the temple was torn in two from top to bottom.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records it at the moment of the death. **From top to bottom** — nobody standing on the floor could have started it there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast Hebrews draws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, brethren, having boldness to enter the Holiest by the blood of Jesus, by a new and living way which He consecrated for us, through the veil, that is, His flesh… **let us draw near**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One man, one day, never without blood — and then *let us draw near*, addressed to everybody, with no day named. See the note on Hebrews 10:19–21."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was in the room.** The ark, and above it the mercy seat with the cherubim. And after the exile, according to the historians, the room was empty — the ark was gone. The high priest went in once a year, with blood, into an empty room, for centuries."}]}, "Hosea 1:8–9": {"title": "Lo-Ammi", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A prophet is told to name his son a sentence, and the child carries it around for the rest of his life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three children"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hosea's wife bears three, and each name is a message:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Jezreel** — God scatters", "**Lo-Ruhamah** — Not-Pitied, not shown mercy", "**Lo-Ammi** — Not-My-People"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophet's household is the sermon. Every time he calls his son in for dinner he says it again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For you are **not My people**, and **I am not your God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of the second clause is closer to *I am not I-AM to you* — the name from the burning bush, withdrawn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it describes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the season of the exile and everything after it. No temple that mattered, no king on David's throne, no prophet after Malachi, no ark, and the visible dealings of God with that nation suspended."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book of Esther sits inside this. Ten chapters, a Persian empire, a death decree, and the name of God never once written down. That is what *Lo-Ammi* looks like from inside it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does not mean"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not gone. Esther is the proof of it — a sleepless king, an old record read at the right hour, a gallows turned round, and a nation alive at the end of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hidden face, and not one hand withdrawn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same chapter is already going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read what stands immediately before verse 9 and immediately after it. Hosea 1:10:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet the number of the children of Israel shall be as the sand of the sea, which cannot be measured or numbered; and in the place where it was said to them, \"You are not My people,\" there it shall be said to them, \"You are the sons of the living God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reversal is in the same chapter as the sentence. It is not a later correction; the promise is written on the other side of the same page."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In the very place\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not somewhere else, and not to a different people. In the place where the words *not My people* were spoken."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes exactly that line in Romans 9 and applies it — and he applies it to Gentiles being brought in. See the note on Romans 9:25–26."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Hosea is told to do in chapter 3.** Go and love her again. He buys his own wife back for fifteen shekels and a homer and a half of barley — the price of a slave. The names of the children are what the book says; the purchase is what the book is about."}]}, "Hosea 12:10–11": {"title": "\"Vision after vision\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God describes His own method of speaking, and then names what it was spent on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I **spoke through the prophets**, gave **vision after vision**, told **parables through their mouths**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew gives three: spoke, multiplied visions, and used **similitudes** — **damah**, to liken, to compare."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The multiplying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Vision after vision.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I have multiplied visions* — **chazon**, the same word as Proverbs 29 and Habakkuk 2. See the note on Proverbs 29:14–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one vision. A supply of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third method"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Told parables through their mouths.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *by the ministry of the prophets I have used similitudes*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God says He spoke in likenesses — pictures standing for something else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is exactly what the prophets do. Jeremiah's almond branch and boiling pot, Amos's plumbline and basket of summer fruit, Ezekiel's valley of bones, Zechariah's lampstand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Hosea's own life: he is told to marry Gomer, and the marriage is the message."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does with the same method"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **without a parable He did not speak to them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew adds that it fulfilled a psalm: *I will open My mouth in parables; I will utter things kept secret from the foundation of the world.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hosea's own life was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Go, take yourself a wife of harlotry… **for the land has committed great harlotry by departing from the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three children with names that are messages: Jezreel, Lo-Ruhamah (*no mercy*), and Lo-Ammi (*not My people*)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the reversal: *I will have mercy on her who had not obtained mercy, and I will say to those who were not My people, \"You are My people.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes both in Romans 9. See the note on Romans 9:25–26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Is there **evil in Gilead**? Then **everything there has come to nothing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gilead was a Levitical territory east of the Jordan, and by Hosea's time a byword for bloodshed — chapter 6 calls it *a city of evildoers, defiled with blood*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The altars"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In **Gilgal they sacrifice bulls**, and their **altars stand like heaps of stone in a plowed field**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gilgal was where Israel first camped after crossing the Jordan and where the twelve stones were set up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By Hosea's day it was a shrine centre, and he mentions it three times, never approvingly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Like heaps of stone in a plowed field.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *heaps in the furrows of the field*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A farmer clearing a field piles the stones at the edges. The altars are described as those piles — numerous, made of what was in the way, and worthless."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is a pun underneath it: **Gilgal** and **gallim**, heaps."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the two verses do together"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first says God spoke in every way available — prophets, visions, pictures."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second says what the country did with it: multiplied altars."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By a prophet the LORD brought Israel out of Egypt, and by a prophet he was preserved.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses is not named. The point is the office."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What God says about His own speaking.** Three methods and a plural: vision after vision, through the mouths of prophets, in likenesses — and the next sentence is about stone heaps in a ploughed field."}]}, "Hosea 2:14–15": {"title": "A door of hope in the valley of trouble", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The chapter before this is a divorce proceeding. Then one word changes everything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has come before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hosea 2 opens with God laying out a case against an unfaithful wife — strip her, hedge her way with thorns, take back the grain and the wine and the wool, end the feasts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The charge is that she went after other lovers and *did not know that I gave her grain, new wine and oil.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So now, listen. I will draw her back to Myself.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew opens with *therefore* — and it is the strangest *therefore* in the prophets. Everything before it argues for judgement, and the conclusion drawn is courtship."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is to allure, to entice, to persuade. It is the word for winning somebody over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Into the wilderness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will **lead her out into the wilderness** and **speak softly to her heart**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for the speaking is *speak upon her heart* — the idiom for wooing, used of a man courting a woman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the destination is a desert. He takes her to the emptiest place available in order to talk to her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wilderness in Israel's memory is the honeymoon: *I remember you, the kindness of your youth, the love of your betrothal, when you went after Me in the wilderness.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vineyards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**There I will give her back her vineyards.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the desert. He returns the fruitfulness in the place where nothing grows, and before she is out of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The valley"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **turn the Valley of Achor, that valley of trouble, into a door of hope**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Achor** means trouble, and it is a place with a history."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua 7 — Achan took the devoted things from Jericho, Israel was defeated at Ai, and the valley where he was dealt with was named Achor because of it. *Why have you troubled us? The LORD will trouble you this day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was the site of Israel's first defeat in the land, and the name preserved the shame."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God says He will make a **door** of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not remove it, not rename it, not compensate for it. Turn the place itself into an entrance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There she will answer Me **as she did when she was young**, as on the day she first came up out of Egypt.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that she will sing there — the same verb used of Miriam at the sea with a tambourine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a chastened response. The first one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Hosea was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophet told to marry a woman who would be unfaithful, and to name his children *Not-Pitied* and *Not-My-People*. See the note on Hosea 1:8–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, in chapter 3, told to go and love her again — and he buys his own wife back for the price of a slave."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book's argument is made with his household before it is made with words."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the door leads.** *And it shall be, in that day, that you will call Me \"My Husband,\" and no longer call Me \"My Master.\"* Two Hebrew words, *ishi* and *baali*. The valley of trouble opens onto a change in what she calls Him."}]}, "Hosea 4:4–6": {"title": "For lack of knowledge", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted line in Hosea, and the verse names who was supposed to supply what was missing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being addressed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let no one accuse, let no one blame, for **your quarrel is with Me, you who are meant to be My priests**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The charge is not against the nation generally. It is against the priesthood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God stops the mutual blaming — *let no one accuse* — and takes the dispute to Himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The picture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You stumble by day**, and **the prophet stumbles with you by night**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stumbling in daylight, when there is nothing to trip over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Priest and prophet, day and night — the two offices that were meant to hold the people steady, both down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My people are destroyed for lack of knowledge**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **beli hadda'at** — for lack of *the* knowledge, with the article."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not knowledge in general. A particular body of it, which the priests held and were supposed to hand on: *the priest's lips should keep knowledge, and people should seek the law from his mouth*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the lack happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because **you have rejected knowledge**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ma'asta** — you have refused it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people lacked it because the priests had thrown it away. The verse does not blame the uninformed for being uninformed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I reject you from serving Me as priest**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb, turned round. They rejected; they are rejected."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because you have **forgotten the teaching of your God**, **I too will forget your children**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Torah** — the instruction. The word that means both *law* and *teaching*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the priests were doing instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *they eat up the sin of My people, and they set their heart on their iniquity*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sin offerings were their food, so more sin meant a fuller table. The system paid them by the transgression."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line about the people"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Like people, like priest.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the priest is, the congregation becomes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hosea's own marriage was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophet had been told to marry Gomer, and to name the children *Lo-Ruhamah* — not pitied — and *Lo-Ammi* — not My people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the reversal: *I will say to those who were not My people, \"You are My people,\" and they shall say, \"You are my God.\"* See the note on Hosea 2:21–23."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul takes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As He says also in Hosea: \"**I will call them My people, who were not My people**, and her beloved, who was not beloved.\"* See the note on Romans 9:22–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book that names the destruction is the same book Paul uses for the inclusion of the Gentiles."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the priesthood becomes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are a chosen generation, **a royal priesthood**, a holy nation, His own special people.* See the note on 1 Peter 2:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The office that failed in Hosea is handed to everybody."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the lack came from.** *Because you have rejected knowledge* — addressed to the priests, in a sentence about what happened to the people. The uninformed are not the ones being charged."}]}, "Hosea 6:1–3": {"title": "On the third day", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses that sound like a revival hymn, and the chapter turns on them a line later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The call"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Come, **let us return to the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shuv** — turn back. The word that runs through the whole book, and gives Israel's repentance and God's turning the same verb."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has **torn** us, and He will **heal** us. He has **struck us down**, and He will **bind up** our wounds.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *torn* is **taraph** — what a lion does to prey. Hosea has just used it of God in the previous chapter: *I will be like a lion to Ephraim… I will tear them and go away*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They quote His own image back at Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two days"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After **two days He will revive us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yechayyenu** — He will make us live."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the **third day He will raise us up**, and we will live before Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yeqimenu** — He will raise us up, cause us to stand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two days, then the third. Hosea uses the pattern to mean *shortly* — a Hebrew idiom for something imminent, the way *once or twice* means *a few times*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the New Testament does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was buried, and **He rose again the third day according to the Scriptures**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul writes *according to the Scriptures* without naming which. Jonah's fish is one candidate; this is the other and the language matches more closely."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in Hosea's context is about a Messiah. The sentence is about a nation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The resolve"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us know Him. **Let us press on to know the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nirdephah** — let us pursue, chase. The verb for hunting, or for an army in pursuit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same intensity as Proverbs' *if you dig for her like buried treasure*. See the note on Proverbs 2:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The certainty"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As surely as **the sun rises**, He will come to us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *His going forth is established as the morning*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rains"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Like the **winter rains**, like the **spring rains that water the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew names both: the **yoreh**, the early rain that softens the ground for ploughing in October, and the **malqosh**, the latter rain in March and April that swells the grain before harvest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James uses the same pair: *the farmer waits for the precious fruit of the earth, waiting patiently for it until it receives the early and latter rain*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says to it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *\"O Ephraim, what shall I do to you? O Judah, what shall I do to you? For **your faithfulness is like a morning cloud, and like the early dew it goes away**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people compare His coming to the reliable rain. He compares their loyalty to the dew that is gone by nine o'clock."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The beautiful poem in verses 1 to 3 is answered by a complaint that it does not last."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line He quotes twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **I desire mercy and not sacrifice**, and the knowledge of God more than burnt offerings.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six verses later. Jesus quotes it twice in Matthew — once over dinner with tax collectors, and once over grain picked on a Sabbath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both times, to people who were objecting to something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hosea is told to marry a woman who will be unfaithful, and does. The marriage is the sermon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the last chapter opens with the same word as this one: *O Israel, return to the LORD your God*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is said and what is answered.** The people's poem is three of the best verses in the prophets. God's reply is that their loyalty burns off like dew."}]}, "Isaiah 1:1": {"title": "\"This is what Isaiah saw\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse of heading, and every word in it is doing work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is what Isaiah, son of Amoz, **saw**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chazah** — to see, to behold, to perceive in vision."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The noun form is **chazon** — the vision word. See the note on Proverbs 29:14–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A book of sixty-six chapters, almost entirely speech, is introduced as something seen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yeshayahu** — *the LORD is salvation*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same root as **Yeshua**. The prophet's name is the book's subject."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The father"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Son of Amoz.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the prophet Amos — a different name in Hebrew. Nothing else is known about him, and Jewish tradition holds he was of royal descent, which would explain Isaiah's access to kings."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The subject"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A vision **of Judah and Jerusalem**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The southern kingdom and its capital. Isaiah addresses the nations too — there are oracles on Babylon, Moab, Egypt, Tyre — but the heading names the home ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four kings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the days of **Uzziah and Jotham, of Ahaz and Hezekiah**, the kings of Judah.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roughly sixty years of ministry, through four reigns."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Uzziah** — a long and prosperous reign that ended when he went into the temple to burn incense and came out a leper. Isaiah dates his call to the year Uzziah died: *in the year that King Uzziah died, I saw the Lord sitting on a throne, high and lifted up.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Jotham** — sixteen years, and the chronicler's verdict is that he *did what was right* but *the people acted corruptly*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ahaz** — the worst of the four. He burned his own son as an offering, stripped the temple to pay tribute to Assyria, and copied a Damascus altar. Isaiah offers him a sign and he refuses it, and gets one anyway: *behold, the virgin shall conceive and bear a Son*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hezekiah** — the reformer who opened the temple doors his father had shut, and to whom Isaiah brings both the death sentence and the fifteen added years. See the note on 2 Chronicles 29:3–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What one man watched"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An empire rise. Assyria took the northern kingdom and deported it during Isaiah's lifetime, and later surrounded Jerusalem itself — the siege that ends with the angel and a hundred and eighty-five thousand dead in the camp."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Hear, O heavens, and give ear, O earth! For the LORD has spoken: **I have nourished and brought up children, and they have rebelled against Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The ox knows its owner and the donkey its master's crib, **but Israel does not know**, My people do not consider.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse a few lines later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Come now, and let us reason together,\" says the LORD. \"**Though your sins are like scarlet, they shall be as white as snow**; though they are red like crimson, they shall be as wool.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other visions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel opens *the heavens were opened and I saw visions of God*. Amos, Obadiah, Nahum and Habakkuk all head their books with the same word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophetic books are consistently introduced as sight rather than speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The book's range"}, {"k": "p", "t": "From *the ox knows its owner* in chapter 1 to *behold, I create new heavens and a new earth* in chapter 65. See the note on Isaiah 65:21–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And chapter 53 in between."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word the whole book stands under.** *Saw.* Sixty-six chapters of speech, four kings, an empire's rise and a siege — filed as a vision, by a man whose name means the LORD saves."}]}, "Isaiah 1:18": {"title": "\"Come now, let us talk this through\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most generous sentence in the first chapter of Isaiah, and the chapter around it is one of the harshest in the book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A nation described as sick from head to foot, a country burned, worship God calls a burden He is weary of carrying, and hands full of blood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *come now, let us talk this through.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The invitation arrives in the middle of the indictment, not after it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a legal term — to argue a case, to reason together, to settle a dispute. It is the language of a courtroom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God summons them to the argument, and then does not argue. He offers terms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two colours"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though your sins are scarlet, they can turn white as snow. Though they are red as crimson, they can become soft and clean as wool.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both dyes named were the fastest, most permanent colours in the ancient world. Scarlet and crimson came from an insect and soaked into the fibre — cloth dyed that way did not fade, and could not be washed out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the point of choosing them. The stain being described is the kind that does not come out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they become"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not merely clean — **snow** and **wool**. Wool is what the cloth was before it was dyed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is not of a stain removed but of the material restored to its original state, as though it had never been in the vat."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What follows immediately.** *If you are willing and obedient, you will eat the good of the land.* The offer has a shape, and it belongs to the covenant Isaiah is prosecuting. What Christians have always heard in the verse is the colour chemistry: God naming the most permanent stain available and saying it can go."}]}, "Isaiah 11:1–3": {"title": "A shoot from the stump", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The chapter before this one ends with a forest being cut down. This one begins with what grows out of the stump."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stump"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A shoot will spring up **from the stump of Jesse**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not from the tree — from the stump. The house of David is described as felled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is *Jesse*, not David. Back past the throne to the farmer in Bethlehem whose youngest son was out with the sheep. The royal line is reduced to the family it came from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 10 closes with God cutting down the high trees with an axe: *the thickets of the forest He will cut down with iron, and Lebanon will fall.* Then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shoot"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **netzer** — a sprout, a green shoot. Matthew's *He shall be called a Nazarene* has been read against this word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A shoot out of a stump is what a tree does when it has been cut down and is not dead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Spirit resting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit of the LORD **will rest on him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to settle, to come to rest and stay. It is the same root as Noah's name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sevenfold list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read it as it is written and it is three pairs under one heading:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the Spirit of the LORD**", "the Spirit of **wisdom** and **understanding**", "the Spirit of **counsel** and **might**", "the Spirit of **knowledge** and **the fear of the LORD**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven, and it is one Spirit named seven ways rather than seven Spirits."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation opens with *the seven Spirits who are before His throne*, and the lampstand in Exodus had seven lamps on one hammered stand. See the notes on Revelation 1:4–6 and Exodus 25:31–40."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"His delight will be in the fear of the LORD\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that His scent, His breathing, is in it — an unusual verb, related to smell. He finds it fragrant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How He judges"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will **not judge by what his eyes see**, nor decide by what his ears hear.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two things every human court runs on — testimony and appearance — are set aside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse says what He judges by instead: *with righteousness He shall judge the poor, and decide with equity for the meek of the earth.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wolf with the lamb, the leopard with the young goat, the calf and the lion together, and a child leading them. A nursing infant playing at a cobra's hole."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They shall not hurt nor destroy in all My holy mountain, for the earth shall be full of the knowledge of the LORD as the waters cover the sea.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter ends with.** *In that day there shall be **a Root of Jesse**, who shall stand as a banner to the peoples; for the Gentiles shall seek Him.* He is the shoot out of Jesse in verse 1 and the root of Jesse in verse 10 — after him, and underneath him."}]}, "Isaiah 14:12–15": {"title": "\"I will\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A taunt-song over the king of Babylon, and five sentences in the middle of it that readers have always taken as describing something larger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter introduces it: *you will take up this **proverb against the king of Babylon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A **mashal** — a taunt, a mocking poem. It is addressed to a human king."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the language in these four verses goes beyond any human king, which is why the church has read it as describing the power behind him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How you have fallen from heaven, **O shining one, son of the dawn**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **helel ben-shachar** — shining one, son of the morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Latin Bible rendered *helel* as **Lucifer**, light-bearer, and the name entered English that way. It is a description of the morning star, not a proper name in the Hebrew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five \"I will\"s"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will** ascend to heaven. **I will** lift my throne above the stars of God. **I will** sit on the mount of assembly, in the far reaches of the north. **I will** rise above the tops of the clouds. **I will** make myself like the Most High.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five, in a row, and each one goes higher than the last."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last is the only one that names a person: *like the Most High.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is missing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any object outside himself. Every clause is *I* and a destination."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set it beside the other great five-fold statement in Scripture — *behold, I have come to do Your will, O God*, and *not what I want, what You want.* See the notes on Psalms 40:6–8 and Luke 22:39–46."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two sets of intentions, and they run in opposite directions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Yet you are brought down to the grave, to the depths of the pit.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five ascents, and one sentence in reply."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **Sheol**, and *the depths of the pit* — the lowest place named in the poem, answering the highest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel 28 does the same thing with the king of Tyre: *you were the anointed cherub who covers… you were perfect in your ways from the day you were created, till iniquity was found in you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two prophets, two pagan kings, and both oracles reach past the man in front of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I watched Satan fall out of heaven like a bolt of lightning.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke 10, to the seventy coming back excited that the demons obeyed them. He speaks as an eyewitness. See the note on Luke 10:17–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The morning star, taken back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I, Jesus… am the Root and the Offspring of David, **the Bright and Morning Star**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation 22, and it is the last self-description in the Bible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The title the shining one grasped at is claimed by somebody else at the end of the book."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the taunt-song is really about.** Verse 4 says it plainly: *the LORD has broken the staff of the wicked… the whole earth is at rest and quiet; they break forth into singing.* The poem is what the nations sing when an oppressor falls."}]}, "Isaiah 2:2–3": {"title": "Nations streaming uphill", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah's second chapter opens with a picture that requires water to do something water does not do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Streaming upward"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All the nations will **stream toward it** like rivers running home.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb **nahar** is built from the noun for a river. Rivers run down. These are running up a mountain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is deliberately impossible, and it is the first thing said about the nations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The highest of the mountains"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The mountain of the LORD's house will stand as the highest of the mountains, lifted above the hills.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mount Zion is not a large hill. It is overtopped by the Mount of Olives across the valley, and by most of the ranges around it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse is not measuring altitude."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does the inviting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Many peoples will come and say, **Come, let us go up**…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is sent to fetch them in these two verses. The invitation is on the lips of the peoples themselves, and they say it to each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they want when they get there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will teach us His ways, and we will walk in His paths.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not protection, not prosperity, not a verdict on their enemies. Teaching."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the second clause commits them before the first is answered — *we will walk*. They ask to be taught by somebody they have already decided to obey."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the teaching goes out from Zion, the word of the LORD from Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Teaching** is **torah**, which is instruction before it is legislation — the word a parent's guidance is called in Proverbs."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes next in the chapter.** Swords into ploughshares, spears into pruning hooks, and nations that *will not learn war any more*. The disarmament is placed after the teaching, not before it — in Isaiah's order, they stop fighting because they have been to the mountain."}]}, "Isaiah 24:21–23": {"title": "The sun put to shame", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of the darkest chapter in Isaiah, and it closes with a coronation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 24 is the opening of what is usually called Isaiah's apocalypse — chapters 24 to 27, the whole earth rather than one nation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It has already said *the earth is violently broken, the earth is split open, the earth is shaken exceedingly*, and *the earth shall reel to and fro like a drunkard*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two ranks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD will bring judgment on **the powers in the heights above** and **the kings of the earth below**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the host of exalted ones on high, and the kings of the earth on the earth*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two tiers, dealt with in the same sentence. Paul's list is the same shape: *principalities, powers, rulers of the darkness of this age, spiritual hosts of wickedness in the heavenly places*. See the note on Ephesians 6:10–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The holding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will be **gathered together like prisoners in a pit**. They will be **shut up in the prison**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *pit* is **bor** — a cistern. Joseph was put in one, and so was Jeremiah."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The delay"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **after many days** they will be **called to account**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *after many days they shall be visited*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Confinement first, sentencing later. Revelation has the same two-stage arrangement — bound for a thousand years, and then the judgment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter writes it too: *God did not spare the angels who sinned, but cast them down… and delivered them into chains of darkness, to be **reserved for judgment***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shaming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the moon will be ashamed** and **the sun will be put to shame**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the moon shall be confounded and the sun ashamed* — **chapherah** and **boshah**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two greater lights of Genesis 1, embarrassed. Not extinguished, and not eclipsed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something brighter walks in and they have nothing to contribute."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the **LORD of hosts will reign on Mount Zion and in Jerusalem**, and **before His elders, in glory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *before His elders* is *before His ancients* — and the last phrase, **kavod**, glory."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation's throne room has twenty-four elders around it, and a city with *no need of the sun or of the moon to shine in it, for the glory of God illuminated it*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 25, verse 1: *O LORD, You are my God. I will exalt You, I will praise Your name, for You have done wonderful things.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the feast on the same mountain, with the veil swallowed and death swallowed with it. See the note on Isaiah 25:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter break is a printer's decision. The judgment scene and the banquet scene are one continuous passage, and the address is the same: Mount Zion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two mountains"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have not come to the mountain that may be touched and that burned with fire… but you have come to **Mount Zion**.* See the note on Hebrews 12:18–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah's judgment sits on the mountain Hebrews names as the one believers have arrived at."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What happens to the lights.** Ashamed — the word is about embarrassment, not destruction. Genesis appointed them to rule the day and the night, and they are still shining when something makes them redundant."}]}, "Isaiah 25:6–9": {"title": "The feast on the mountain", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four verses, and everything in them happens on one hill."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The location"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **on this mountain** the LORD of hosts will make for all peoples a feast.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **on this mountain** He will swallow up the covering.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice, in two sentences. Isaiah has just named it in the chapter before — Mount Zion, in Jerusalem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every item on the list is sited at the same address."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The menu"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A feast of **rich food**, a feast of **well-aged wine**, of the **best cuts** and the **finest wine, refined** and poured out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *rich food* is **shemanim** — fat things. For *well-aged wine*, **shemarim** — wine left on its lees, which is how the good stuff was aged."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two pairs, and each pair says the same thing twice: the best of it, and then the best of the best."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words under the menu"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew behind *rich food* is **shemanim**, from **shemen** — oil. The word for the anointing oil, and the root behind Gethsemane, the olive press."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the older translations render the next phrase *fat things full of marrow*. The Hebrew is **memuchayim**, from **machah** — to wipe out, to blot out, to erase."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the verb of Psalm 51: *blot out my transgressions*. And of Colossians, in Greek: *having wiped out the handwriting of requirements that was against us*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The menu is oil and erasure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is invited"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **all peoples**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not Judah. The nations, and Isaiah says it twice in the same breath — *the veil that is spread over **all the nations***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The covering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will **swallow up the covering** that is cast over all peoples, **the veil** that is spread over all the nations.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *covering* is **lot** — a wrapping, something laid over a face. The word behind the veil Moses put on. See the note on 2 Corinthians 3:12–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the second word is **massekah** — a woven covering, from the root for pouring and overlaying."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something is over the faces of the nations, and it is dealt with at the same table as the food."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Bala** — to swallow, to gulp down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses it twice in two lines. He swallows up the covering, and then:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will **swallow up death in victory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb that ate the veil eats death."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where that sentence goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes it. *Death is swallowed up in victory* — at the resurrection, when the perishable puts on the imperishable. See the note on 1 Corinthians 15:53–55."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And John quotes the next line at the very end of the Bible: *God will wipe away every tear from their eyes*. See the note on Revelation 21:3–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tears"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the Lord GOD will **wipe away the tears from every face**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A gesture rather than a decree. The hand of God, on a face, one at a time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disgrace"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will **take away the disgrace of His people** from all the earth, for the LORD has spoken.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *reproach* — the taunt of the watching nations. Israel's shame is removed in front of the same audience that saw it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*See, **this is our God. We have waited for Him, and He has saved us**. This is the LORD. We waited for Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice: *we waited*. The sentence at the feast looks backwards at the waiting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody says *we found Him* or *we worked it out*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us **be glad**. Let us **rejoice in His salvation**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *His salvation* is **yeshuato**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this sits in Isaiah"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapters 24 to 27 are one unit — the earth broken up, the city of confusion, the whole world under judgment. And in the middle of it, without warning, a banquet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 24 ends with the moon confounded and the sun ashamed. Chapter 25 opens *O LORD, You are my God, I will exalt You*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is on the guest list.** All peoples. The mountain the veil is removed on is the mountain the meal is served on, and both sentences begin with the same three words."}]}, "Isaiah 26:1–6": {"title": "\"Whose mind is stayed on You\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A song about a city, with the best-known line in Isaiah in the middle of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The walls"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We have **a strong city**. **He makes salvation its walls** and its rampart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The defences are not masonry. Salvation is the wall."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah is writing to people whose actual city walls were about to be tested by Assyria."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who comes in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Open the gates, that **the righteous nation may enter, the nation that keeps faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *keeps truth* — **emunim**, faithfulness, the root of *amen*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You keep him in **perfect peace** whose **mind is stayed on You**, because **he trusts in You**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *perfect peace* is **shalom shalom** — peace, peace. The word twice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrew has no superlative form; doubling is how it says *complete*. Holy, holy, holy works the same way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Stayed\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Samak** — to lean, to prop, to rest one's weight on. It is the verb used of a man laying his hand on the head of a sacrifice and pressing down. See the note on Leviticus 1:10–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *thinks about*. Leans on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Trust first, then the leaning, then the peace. Isaiah gives the causal chain in the sentence: *because he trusts in You.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Trust in the LORD forever, for **the LORD, the LORD Himself, is an everlasting Rock**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the Rock of Ages* — **tsur olamim**, and the hymn takes its title from here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does to the other city"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has **brought low those who lived on high**, the lofty city He lays low. He lays it low to the ground. He casts it into the dust.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs of bringing down, one after another."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who tramples it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The foot tramples it down, **the feet of the poor, the steps of the needy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people with nothing walk over what the high city becomes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In that day this song will be sung* — a song written for a day that has not arrived, given to people to learn in advance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 24 to 27 is often called the little apocalypse: the earth broken open, the feast on the mountain, death swallowed up forever, and *the LORD will wipe away tears from all faces.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This song sits inside it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter says a few verses later.** *With my soul I have desired You in the night; yes, by my spirit within me I will seek You early.* The man singing about perfect peace says he was awake in the dark looking for God."}]}, "Isaiah 28:11–13": {"title": "\"This is the resting place\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A sentence about rest, sitting in the middle of one of the harshest chapters in Isaiah."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being addressed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens on *the drunkards of Ephraim* and moves to the priests and prophets of Jerusalem: *they have erred through wine… they err in vision, they stumble in judgment.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people mishandling the word of God are drunk."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had been mocking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their own words are quoted in verse 10: *tsav latsav, tsav latsav, qav laqav, qav laqav, zeer sham, zeer sham* — a sing-song jingle, mocking the prophet as though he were teaching toddlers their letters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So then, with **stammering lips and a foreign tongue** He will speak to this people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They complained that the prophet's Hebrew sounded like baby talk. They will hear a language they genuinely cannot understand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Assyrian army spoke it. Isaiah is describing an invasion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offer they refused"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ones to whom He said, \"**This is the resting place, let the weary rest here, this is the place of repose**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **menuchah** — rest, a settled place, a stopping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It shares a root with the name **Noah**, and it is the word in Psalm 23: *He leads me beside the still waters* — literally, *waters of rest*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offer had been made and it was for the weary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **they would not listen**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words, and the whole chapter turns on them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the word of the LORD will become for them **command upon command, command upon command, rule upon rule, rule upon rule, a little here, a little there**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their own mocking jingle, handed back to them as the shape of what they will get."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Having refused the rest, what is left is the accumulation — line after line, rule after rule, endlessly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The outcome"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that they **go and fall backward, and are broken and snared and captured**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verbs, each worse than the last."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul uses it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"With men of other tongues and other lips I will speak to this people, and even so they will not hear Me,\" says the Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes this passage in 1 Corinthians 14 and draws his conclusion about tongues as a sign from it. See the note on 1 Corinthians 14:20–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse that follows in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore thus says the Lord GOD: \"**Behold, I lay in Zion a stone for a foundation, a tried stone, a precious cornerstone, a sure foundation. Whoever believes will not act hastily**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter quotes it. Paul quotes it. In the middle of the chapter about the jingle and the foreign tongue, Isaiah lays a cornerstone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other rest"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There remains therefore **a rest for the people of God**.\" Let us **be diligent to enter that rest**, lest anyone fall according to the same example of disobedience.* See the note on Hebrews 4:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews uses the same argument shape: an offer of rest, a refusal, and the offer still standing."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was on offer.** *This is the resting place, let the weary rest here.* The people it was said to were the ones who would not have it — and Isaiah records the sentence anyway."}]}, "Isaiah 28:16": {"title": "The one who trusts will not be in a hurry", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse dropped into a chapter about drunk officials and a bad treaty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is happening around it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah has been describing the leaders of Jerusalem. *The priest and the prophet have erred through intoxicating drink… they stumble in judgment.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And their policy: *we have made a covenant with death, and with Sheol we are in agreement. When the overflowing scourge passes through, it will not come to us, for we have made lies our refuge.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had arranged a treaty with Egypt against Assyria, and Isaiah calls it a covenant with death."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The counter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore this is what the Lord GOD says: **See, I lay in Zion a stone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hineni yissad** — behold, I am founding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Against their arrangement, a foundation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four descriptions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **tested stone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **even bochan** — a stone of testing, or a touchstone. A stone used to test the purity of metal by the mark it leaves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **precious cornerstone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Pinnat yiqrat** — a cornerstone of value. The stone at the angle, which fixes the line of two walls."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For a **sure foundation**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Musad musad** — the noun doubled: a founded foundation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The one who trusts will **never be dismayed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lo yachish** — from **chush**, to hurry, to make haste."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The older versions have *he who believes will not make haste*. The Greek Old Testament renders it *shall not be put to shame*, and Paul and Peter both quote that version."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both readings survive in the tradition. The Hebrew says the man who trusts is not in a rush; the Greek says he is not disgraced."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Behind both is the same picture: somebody standing on something that holds."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why haste is the word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter is about panicking politicians cutting a deal because Assyria is coming."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haste, in Isaiah, is what people do when they do not believe there is a foundation. *In returning and rest you shall be saved; in quietness and confidence shall be your strength. But you would not. And you said, \"No, for we will flee on horses.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who quotes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul, in Romans, twice — *whoever believes on Him will not be put to shame*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Peter: *behold, I lay in Zion a chief cornerstone, elect, precious*. See the note on 1 Peter 2:6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both use the Greek reading, and both apply the stone to Christ."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other stone verses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The stone which the builders rejected has become the chief cornerstone.* Psalm 118, quoted by Jesus at the end of the vineyard parable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the two together in Peter: a cornerstone for those who believe, and *a stone of stumbling and a rock of offence* for those who do not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows in Isaiah"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Also I will make justice the measuring line, and righteousness the plummet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Builder's tools. The line and the plumb bob, used against the walls the officials had built."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The hail will sweep away the refuge of lies, and the waters will overflow the hiding place.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The end of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A long agricultural passage: the ploughman does not plough forever, and different seeds are threshed differently — dill with a stick, cumin with a rod, and bread grain is not crushed endlessly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This also comes from the LORD of hosts, who is wonderful in counsel and excellent in guidance.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The Hebrew word.** *Chush* — to hurry. The one who trusts a founded foundation is described, first of all, as somebody who is not in a rush."}]}, "Isaiah 28:20–22": {"title": "\"His strange work\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah calls judgement two things, and both of them mean *not His*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The bed is **too short** to stretch out on, the blanket **too narrow** to wrap yourself in.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A proverb about inadequate arrangements. Whatever they had made for themselves would not cover them — a treaty with Egypt, in the chapter's context, described a few verses earlier as *a covenant with death*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You can lie on a short bed. You cannot rest on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two battles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For the LORD will rise up as He did at **Mount Perazim**. He will rouse Himself as in the **Valley of Gibeon**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are places where God fought **for** Israel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Perazim is where David defeated the Philistines and said *the LORD has broken through my enemies before me like a breakthrough of water.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gibeon is where the hailstones fell on the Amorites and the sun stood still for Joshua."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The threat in this verse is that He will do the same thing again — with His own people on the receiving end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two names for it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To do **His work, His strange work**, to perform **His task, His alien task**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *strange* is **zuwr** — foreign, from somewhere else, not native. And *alien* is **nokri** — belonging to another."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah names judgement twice, and both times as something out of character. Not that God does not do it — that it is not the work that comes naturally to Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Set beside what is called natural"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD, the LORD — a God tender and kind, gracious and full of compassion, slow to anger, overflowing with steadfast love.* That is God's own self-description, and none of it is called strange. See the note on Exodus 34:5–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His anger lasts only a moment, but His favour lasts a lifetime.* See the note on Psalms 30:4–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lamentations says it plainly: *He does not afflict willingly, nor grieve the children of men.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **do not scoff now**, or your chains will grow heavier.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that their bonds will be made strong. Mockery tightens what is already holding them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter is going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses earlier is one of the great promises in Isaiah: *Behold, I lay in Zion a foundation stone, a tried stone, **a precious cornerstone**, a sure foundation. Whoever believes will not act hastily.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter quotes it, and Paul quotes it. In the middle of a chapter about drunkards, short beds and strange work, a cornerstone is laid."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Isaiah is arguing against.** *We have made a covenant with death, and with Sheol we are in agreement* — the leaders' own words, quoted back at them. They had made an arrangement they thought would hold, and the bed was too short."}]}, "Isaiah 32:15–17": {"title": "The work of righteousness", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A chapter of desolation turns on one word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses just above are about a land stripped: *thorns and briers*, *the palace forsaken*, *the populous city deserted*, and the women of Jerusalem told to strip themselves and put on sackcloth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The forts and towers will become lairs forever, a joy of wild donkeys, a pasture of flocks.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One word, and everything after it is reversal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pouring"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until **the Spirit is poured on us from on high**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **arah** — to be emptied out, poured until nothing is left in the vessel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**From on high** — the source is named and it is not in the land."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three stages"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the desert becomes a fruitful field**, and **the fruitful field seems like a forest**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word for *fruitful field* is **karmel** — orchard land, the same word as the mountain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three levels: wilderness, orchard, forest. The desert does not merely recover; it goes two steps past where it started, and the good land is re-graded as ordinary next to what follows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where justice lives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **justice will dwell in the desert**, and **righteousness will live in the fruitful field**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both places, not one. The word for *dwell* is the ordinary verb for settling down and staying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fruit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the fruit of righteousness will be peace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the root. Righteousness first, peace as what grows on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul in Romans: *therefore, having been justified by faith, we have **peace** with God.* The order is the same. See the note on Romans 5:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The work"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the work of righteousness will be quietness and confidence forever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *work* is **abodah** — labour, service, what a thing produces by operating."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what it produces is:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Quietness** — **shaqat**, stillness, undisturbed rest. It is the word used of a land that *had rest* from war.", "**Confidence** — **betach**, security, safety, from the root for trusting."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are states of not having to do anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oddity"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Righteousness works, and what its work amounts to is quietness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah puts an active noun and a passive result in the same clause and leaves them there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where else Isaiah says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In returning and rest you shall be saved; **in quietness and confidence shall be your strength**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same two words, in chapter 30, and there the sentence ends *but you would not*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My people will dwell in a peaceful habitation, in secure dwellings, and in quiet resting places.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words for the same thing, one after another."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it starts.** Not with reform. With something poured out from above onto a place described as thorns and briers — and everything in the paragraph after that is consequence."}]}, "Isaiah 32:18–20": {"title": "Though the forest comes down", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses, and the middle one describes a disaster happening around the promise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The work of righteousness will be peace, and the effect of righteousness, quietness and assurance forever.* See the note on Isaiah 32:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew there is **ma'aseh** and **avodah** — the work and the service of righteousness. Peace is described as its output."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My people will live in **peaceful dwellings**, in **secure homes**, in **undisturbed places of rest**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **neveh shalom**, **mishkenot mivtachim**, **menuchot sha'ananot**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Shalom** — wholeness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Mivtach** — from **batach**, to trust, to lean on. Security in the sense of confidence rather than fortification."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sha'anan** — at ease, untroubled, quiet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three nouns for the same condition, stacked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the middle word goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Mivtach** is the trusting word. *Blessed is the man who **trusts** in the LORD, and whose hope is the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The security is a leaning, not a wall."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though **hail flattens the forest** and **the city is leveled completely**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *and it shall hail, coming down on the forest, and the city shall be laid low in humiliation*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A verse about safety, then a verse about destruction, and then a blessing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah does not remove the hail. He puts the dwelling in the middle of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The forest and the city"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are Isaiah's images for proud human power. He has already used them: *the LORD of hosts shall lop off the bough with terror; the high ones of stature will be hewn down, and the haughty will be humbled. He will cut down the thickets of the forest with iron.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "What comes down in verse 19 is what verse 17 was never resting on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How blessed you will be, **sowing your seed by every stream**, letting your **cattle and donkeys range free**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *blessed are you who sow beside all waters, who send out the feet of the ox and the donkey*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sowing beside all waters — planting everywhere there is water, without needing to defend a plot."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And livestock loose. You do not turn animals out to graze freely in a country under threat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has to happen first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until **the Spirit is poured upon us from on high**, and the wilderness becomes a fruitful field.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 15, and everything in the paragraph hangs off it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same promise turns up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they shall dwell safely, and no one shall make them afraid.* Ezekiel, several times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Micah: *everyone shall sit under his vine and under his fig tree, and no one shall make them afraid*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sitting under your own tree is the standing Hebrew image for peace, and it is agricultural every time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter opened with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold, a **king will reign in righteousness**, and princes will rule with justice.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man will be as a hiding place from the wind, and a cover from the tempest, as rivers of water in a dry place, as the **shadow of a great rock in a weary land**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter starts with a king and ends with cattle grazing loose."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not removed.** The hail. Verse 18 promises undisturbed rest, verse 19 flattens the forest and levels the city, and verse 20 has people sowing."}]}, "Isaiah 38:1–3": {"title": "A word from God that did not turn out to be the last word", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A prophet arrives with a message that leaves no room in it — *you are going to die, you will not recover* — and by the end of the chapter it has not happened. This is one of the most theologically awkward passages in the Old Testament, and it is worth reading rather than smoothing over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah actually said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two statements, both flat, both introduced with *this is what the LORD says*:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["put your house in order", "you are going to die, you will not recover"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no *unless* in it. Hezekiah is not offered terms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hezekiah does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not accept it and he does not argue with Isaiah. He turns his face to the wall — away from the prophet, away from the room — and prays. Then he weeps bitterly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His prayer is short and it contains no request. He reminds God how he has walked, and he cries. What he asks for is never stated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before Isaiah has left the palace grounds, he is sent back with fifteen more years."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What this passage is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a technique. Hezekiah does not do anything a reader could copy — there is no formula, no confession, no claim. And the same book is full of prayers that did not turn out this way; the psalms are half made of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nor does it make Isaiah a false prophet. The word was true of the situation as it stood. What changed was not God's honesty but the situation, once a dying man prayed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a record that a word about the future is not always a locked door, and that God is not offended by a person who takes bad news to Him and weeps."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is worth holding carefully in both directions. It is real permission to pray about an outcome that has been declared. It is not a promise that everyone who prays gets fifteen years — the same chapter has Hezekiah writing afterwards about how the reprieve felt, and the rest of his story is not a simple triumph."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The part people forget.** The extra fifteen years are not presented as an unmixed good. Manasseh, the worst king in Judah's history, is born in them, and the chapter after this has Hezekiah showing the treasury to Babylonian envoys — the visit Isaiah tells him will end in exile. The Bible gives him the years and then tells the truth about what he did with them."}]}, "Isaiah 38:4–8": {"title": "The shadow went back ten steps", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A death sentence is reversed, and the sign given is a shadow moving the wrong way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah had come with a message that left no room: *set your house in order, for you shall die and not live*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hezekiah turned his face to the wall and wept bitterly. See the note on Isaiah 38:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turnaround"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the word of the LORD came to Isaiah**: \"Go and tell Hezekiah…\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parallel account says Isaiah had not yet reached the middle court before the word came."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophet who delivered the sentence is sent back with the reversal before he is out of the building."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How God identifies Himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is what the LORD, **the God of your father David**, says.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *the God of Israel*. The God of his father David — which is a covenant address, aimed at a man in the line the promise ran through."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things heard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have heard your prayer and seen your tears**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shamati… ra'iti** — I have heard, I have seen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are counted. The tears are named separately from the prayer, as if they were a second thing said."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You number my wanderings; **put my tears into Your bottle**; are they not in Your book?* See the note on Psalms 56:8–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am going to add fifteen years to your life**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yosef** — I will add."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A specific number, given in advance. Hezekiah is told exactly how long he has."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **I will rescue you and this city** from the hand of the king of Assyria. **I will defend this city**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sennacherib is at the gates. The personal healing and the national deliverance come in one sentence, and the king's life and the city's are tied together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sign"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And this is the sign to you from the LORD… **Look, I will make the shadow that has gone down on the stairway of Ahaz go back ten steps**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ma'alot Achaz** — the steps of Ahaz. A stairway that told the time by the shadow's descent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Built by his father, the king who *sacrificed his own son in the fire* and shut the doors of the temple."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why a shadow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of every possible sign, the one given is time running backwards on his father's instrument."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man has just been given fifteen years. The sign is the sun conceding ten steps."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **the sunlight went back the ten steps it had descended**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The account in Kings adds that Hezekiah was offered the choice — forward ten or back ten — and chose back, because *it is an easy thing for the shadow to go down ten degrees*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks for the harder one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he wrote afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The writing of Hezekiah… when he had been sick and had recovered:*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I said, \"In the prime of my life I shall go to the gates of Sheol\"… **O LORD, by these things men live**… **You have cast all my sins behind Your back***. See the note on Isaiah 38:15–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the fifteen years contained"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Berodach-Baladan sent letters and a present to Hezekiah, for he heard that he had been sick.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Hezekiah showed the envoys everything in the treasure house, and Isaiah came and told him it would all be carried to Babylon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Manasseh was twelve years old when he became king* — born inside the fifteen, and the worst king Judah had."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrative gives the extension and then records what filled it, without softening either."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The sign.** Time running backwards on his father's stairway. A man given fifteen years, and the shadow conceding ten steps to say so."}]}, "Isaiah 40:21–24": {"title": "The circle of the earth", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah asks four questions and then says something about the shape of the world."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He sits enthroned above **the circle of the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chug** — a circle, a sphere, a compass, the shape drawn by swinging a fixed radius."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah wrote it around 700 BC. Every culture around him thought the earth was flat, and it stayed that way in the popular mind for two thousand years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do you not know? Have you not heard? Was it not told to you from the beginning? Have you not understood since the earth was founded?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks them the way you ask someone who ought to know already."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grasshoppers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And its people are **like grasshoppers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The view described is from above the circle, looking down, and from there the people are insects."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a change of altitude in the middle of a sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He stretches out the heavens **like a curtain**, spreads them **like a tent** to live in.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is used elsewhere in Isaiah of the heavens being stretched out, and it is the word for pitching a tent — pulling fabric taut."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The princes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He reduces princes to nothing… **No sooner are they planted, no sooner are they sown, no sooner do they take root** — than He breathes on them and they wither.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three clauses to say how briefly. The whole career of a ruler is compressed into the time between sowing and the first root."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He *breathes* on them. It takes nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 40 opens with *comfort, comfort My people*, and this passage is inside that comfort."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point of the circle, the tent and the grasshoppers is not astronomy. It is that the people frightening you are seed that has not rooted yet, and the One above the circle knows your name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's last lines are the famous ones: *those who wait on the LORD shall renew their strength; they shall mount up with wings like eagles.* See the note on Isaiah 40:29–31."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where else the same word turns up.** Proverbs 8, the passage about Wisdom present at creation: *when He drew a **circle** on the face of the deep, I was there.* And Job says the same thing in his own words — *He drew a circle on the surface of the waters.* See the note on Job 26:5–10."}]}, "Isaiah 40:29–31": {"title": "The order the verbs come in", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*They will soar on wings like eagles. They will run and not grow weary. They will walk and not faint.* The list descends, and readers have wondered about that for centuries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the sequence goes down"}, {"k": "p", "t": "You would expect a crescendo: walk, then run, then fly. Isaiah puts them the other way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The likeliest reason is that it ends where life mostly is. Soaring is the picture; running is the effort; walking is the long ordinary stretch — and *not fainting* on that stretch is the hardest of the three. The promise is arranged to finish on the least dramatic thing, because that is where it is needed longest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is spoken to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even the young grow tired and weary, even strong young men stumble and fall.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah names the strongest people available and says they run out. That is the setup: the promise is not that some people are naturally tireless. It is that strength comes from somewhere other than youth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for \"wait\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **qavah** carries the sense of a cord being twisted — strands drawn together under tension. From that comes waiting, expecting, hoping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not passive. It is closer to being wound tight around something. And the promise attached is not that strength returns but that it is **exchanged** — the verb means to change one thing for another, as you would change clothes."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter starts.** *Comfort, comfort my people.* This is the opening of the section of Isaiah written to exiles, and the whole chapter builds toward these three verses: God measures the oceans in His hand, weighs mountains on scales, brings princes to nothing — and then gives strength to the one with nothing left. The size of Him is the argument for the promise."}]}, "Isaiah 40:3–5": {"title": "Every valley lifted, every hill brought down", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Road-building language, used of a coming that has not happened yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the image is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the ancient world, when a king travelled, work parties went ahead to make the route passable — filling in the dips, cutting down the high ground, straightening what wandered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah applies that to God arriving. The road is not being built *to* Him. It is being made ready *for* Him to come."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two directions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let every valley be lifted up, every mountain and hill made low.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both movements aim at the same result — level ground — and both are stated in the passive. The valley does not raise itself. The hill does not step down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That levelling shows up all through Scripture as the way God deals with people: the low lifted, the high brought down. Mary sings it in the Magnificat; James says the humble are exalted. Here it is described as the preparation for glory being seen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is spoken to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the opening of the second half of Isaiah, addressed to a people in exile, and it begins two verses earlier with *comfort, comfort my people*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The road being described leads home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it turns up again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "All four Gospels quote these verses of John the Baptist. Luke quotes the longest stretch, and ends where Isaiah does — *all people together will see it.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *The mouth of the LORD has spoken.* Isaiah adds it as the guarantee. The chapter goes on to say that grass withers and flowers fall, but the word of our God stands forever — and this is one of the things that word has said."}]}, "Isaiah 40:9–11": {"title": "Here is your God", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two pictures of the same arriving God, one verse apart, and they do not match."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Climb the high mountain, **you who bring good news to Zion**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mevasseret** — a feminine participle: *she who brings good news*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Septuagint translates it **euangelizomenos** — the word that becomes *gospel*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she is told to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lift your voice with strength… **Lift it, do not be afraid**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice: lift, lift. And then the reassurance, which implies there was a reason not to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The audience is a defeated people. The message will sound implausible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The message"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Say to the cities of Judah, **Here is your God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in Hebrew: **hinneh Eloheichem**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole content of the good news, in the passage's own account of it, is a pointing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter starts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Comfort, yes, comfort My people, says your God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The voice of one crying in the wilderness: prepare the way of the LORD.* See the note on Isaiah 40:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "All four Gospels apply that verse to John the Baptist, which puts this chapter at the front of every account of Jesus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first picture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look, **the Lord GOD comes with power**, and **His arm rules for Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **bechazaq** — with strength, and **zeroo** — His arm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The arm is the standard image for God's fighting power: *with a mighty hand and an outstretched arm*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His reward is with Him, His recompense goes before Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A conqueror arriving with the spoils, in the manner of a returning army."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second picture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will **tend His flock like a shepherd**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One verse later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yireh** — he will pasture, feed, shepherd."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does with the arm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will **gather the lambs into His arms** and **carry them close against His heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word — **zeroo**, arm — that ruled in verse 10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The arm that rules is the arm that carries lambs. Isaiah uses the noun twice in consecutive verses and lets the two uses sit against each other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Cheiqo** is the last word: the bosom, the fold of the garment at the chest where a shepherd carried a lamb too small to keep up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the ones still nursing their young, those He will gently lead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yenahel** — to lead to a watering place, to guide at a manageable pace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ewes with young cannot be driven. Jacob knew it: *if the men should drive them hard one day, all the flock will die*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pace of the whole flock is set by the slowest members in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the shepherd says about Himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am the good shepherd. **The good shepherd gives His life for the sheep**.* See the note on John 10:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *He calls His own sheep by name and leads them out*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gives power to the weak… **they shall mount up with wings like eagles**, they shall run and not be weary.* See the note on Isaiah 40:28–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that starts with comfort and ends with eagles has this in the middle: lambs carried, and the nursing ewes led slowly."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The arm.** *His arm rules for Him*, and one verse later that same arm is what the lambs are gathered into. Isaiah uses the word twice and does not explain the join."}]}, "Isaiah 41:11–13": {"title": "\"You will look for your enemies and not find them\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A promise about opposition disappearing, and it ends with a hand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to the opposition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All who rage against you will be ashamed and disgraced. Those who oppose you will be as nothing and perish. **You will look for your enemies and not find them.***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The middle line is the striking one. Not that the enemies are defeated in front of you — that you go looking and they are not there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice the phrase *as nothing* appears. The Hebrew is a word for non-existence, nothingness. What was threatening is described as having no substance left to locate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For I am the LORD your God, who takes hold of your right hand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise is not grounded in Israel's strength or in the enemies' weakness. It is grounded in a grip."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is specific: the **right** hand — the working hand, the sword hand. God is described as holding the hand a fighter would need free."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says while holding it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not fear. I Myself will help you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The emphatic pronoun is in the Hebrew. Not *help will come* — I, Myself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah uses this construction repeatedly in these chapters, and each time the comfort is anchored in who is speaking rather than in the circumstances improving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it was said to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exiles, or people about to be. The chapter opens with God summoning the nations to a courtroom and asking who has directed history, and it addresses Israel as *My servant, whom I have chosen, the offspring of Abraham My friend.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the standing from which the promise is made — a relationship named before a single enemy is mentioned."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse just before this passage.** *Do not fear, for I am with you; do not be dismayed, for I am your God. I will strengthen you and help you; I will uphold you with My righteous right hand.* His right hand holding theirs. The image runs through the whole section."}]}, "Isaiah 41:14–16": {"title": "\"You worm Jacob\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The address is an insult and the promise is a farm implement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not be afraid, **you worm Jacob**, you **little people** of Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tolaath** — a worm, a grub. It is the word used of the maggot in the manna that was kept overnight, and of the crimson dye made from a crushed insect."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is also the word in Psalm 22: *but I am a worm, and no man.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the address is there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter is God against the idol-makers — *let them bring forth and show us what will happen*, *behold, you are nothing, and your work is nothing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Into that argument He turns to Israel and calls them a grub."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not flattering them into confidence. He states what they are and then says what He is going to do with them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The help"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I Myself will help you**,\" says the LORD, \"**your Redeemer, the Holy One of Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two titles. **Redeemer** is **goel** — the kinsman-redeemer, the relative obliged to buy back what a family member had lost. It is Boaz's role in Ruth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The nearest relative and the Holy One, in one line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The transformation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"See, **I will make you into a threshing sledge, new and sharp, with many teeth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A **morag** — a heavy wooden sled dragged over cut grain, its underside set with stone or iron teeth, to separate the wheat from the straw."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *many teeth* is **piphiyoth** — literally *possessors of mouths*, from the word for mouth. The same construction is used of a sword: *a sword with two mouths* is the phrase behind *two-edged*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The teeth of the sledge and the edge of a sword are described in Hebrew as mouths."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it threshes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will thresh the mountains and crush them**, and **reduce the hills to chaff**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A sledge is for grain. This one is put over mountains."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scale is deliberately wrong, and the results are the same either way: what was solid becomes chaff."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You will **winnow them, and the wind will carry them off**, and a gale will scatter them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Winnowing is the step after threshing. You throw the crushed material into the air and the breeze takes the light part away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is left to sweep up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose the outcome is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But you will rejoice in the LORD, and glory in the Holy One of Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The worm ends the passage rejoicing, and not in what it did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is around it in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Fear not, for I am with you; be not dismayed, for I am your God. I will strengthen you, yes, I will help you, I will uphold you with My righteous right hand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is verse 10, four verses earlier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And after this passage: *I will open rivers in desolate heights, and fountains in the midst of the valleys… I will plant in the wilderness the cedar and the acacia tree.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The distance in three verses.** From a grub to a threshing sledge new and sharp, going over mountains — and the only thing between the two is *I Myself will help you*."}]}, "Isaiah 41:8–10": {"title": "\"Abraham My friend\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four titles handed out in three lines, and then five promises in a row."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He calls him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The offspring of **Abraham My friend**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *Abraham who loved Me* or *My beloved* — the participle works both ways, and both readings have been held."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is said of nobody else. 2 Chronicles repeats it and James repeats it: *he was called the friend of God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What earned it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing that Abraham did. The verse Scripture attaches to the title is the one about believing: *Abraham believed God, and it was accounted to him for righteousness — and he was called the friend of God.* James puts the two clauses in one sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had no law to keep. He lied about his wife twice, took Hagar, and laughed at the promise. And he is the friend. See the note on Genesis 15:6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He calls Israel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My **servant**… whom I have **chosen**… the **offspring** of Abraham.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chosen, and then the negative that goes with it: *I have chosen you **and not cast you away**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He found them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I **took you from the ends of the earth**, I **called you from its farthest corners**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The initiative is entirely His in both verbs. Nobody in the passage came looking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five promises"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **do not fear**, for I am with you. **Do not be dismayed**, for I am your God. I will **strengthen** you. I will **help** you. I will **uphold** you with My righteous right hand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two commands and three undertakings, and each command has a reason attached rather than an exhortation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Do not fear — *for I am with you*. Not because the situation is manageable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The right hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **My righteous right hand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is the right hand of My righteousness. What holds a man up in this passage is God's own righteousness, not his."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the same thing Isaiah 54 says: *you will be established in righteousness… their righteousness is from Me.* See the note on Isaiah 54:13–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He calls them a few verses later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Fear not, **you worm Jacob**, you men of Israel! I will help you,\" says the LORD and your Redeemer, the Holy One of Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's own title. And in the next breath: *I will make you into a new threshing sledge with sharp teeth; you shall thresh the mountains and beat them small.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A worm, told it will thresh mountains. The transformation is announced in the same sentence as the insult, and the only thing between them is *I will help you.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter is arguing.** It is a courtroom scene — God challenging the idols to predict anything at all. *Show the things that are to come hereafter, that we may know that you are gods.* And in the middle of the trial He turns aside to reassure a frightened people that He has hold of their hand."}]}, "Isaiah 43:18–19": {"title": "Now it springs up", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses telling a people to stop remembering the one thing they were commanded to remember."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Forget the former things. **Do not dwell on the past**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **al-tizkeru rishonot vqadmoniyyot al-titbonanu** — do not remember former things, and do not consider ancient things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why that is startling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel's whole religious life was organised around remembering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Remember this day in which you went out of Egypt.* The Passover, the feast of unleavened bread, the stones at Gilgal, the tassels on the garment — all of them memory devices."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just been said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Thus says the LORD, **who makes a way in the sea and a path through the mighty waters**, who brings forth the chariot and horse, the army and the power.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They shall lie down together, they shall not rise; they are extinguished, quenched like a wick.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Red Sea, described in the four verses immediately before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the instruction means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *forget the exodus*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just recited it, and then says: stop looking at it, because something is happening now."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The former thing being set aside is the greatest thing in the national memory."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The announcement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*See, **I am doing a new thing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hineni oseh chadashah** — behold, I am doing a new thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present participle. In progress."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **it springs up**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **attah titzmach** — now it sprouts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tzamach** — to sprout, to spring up, of plants breaking ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The related noun **tzemach** — the Branch — is a title in Isaiah, Jeremiah and Zechariah."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do you not perceive it?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **halo teda'uha** — will you not know it?"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The new thing is already visible, and the question is whether they have noticed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A sprout is not obvious. It is the smallest stage at which anything can be seen at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two images"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am making **a way in the wilderness** and **rivers in the desert**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **derek bammidbar** and **neharot bishimon**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A road, and water."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What both mean"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The former thing was a way through water — a path opened in the sea. See the note on Exodus 14:21–25."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The new thing is water in a road — rivers in a place with none."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two elements, reversed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who notices"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **wild animals honour Me, the jackals and the owls**, because I give water in the wilderness.* See the note on Isaiah 43:20–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes just before the chapter's turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Fear not, for I have redeemed you; I have called you by your name; you are Mine**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When you pass through the waters, I will be with you; and through the rivers, they shall not overflow you.\"* See the note on Isaiah 43:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You have not brought Me the sheep for your burnt offerings… **but you have burdened Me with your sins**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I, even I, am He who blots out your transgressions for My own sake; and I will not remember your sins**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that tells them not to remember ends with God saying what He will not remember."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Forgetting those things which are behind and reaching forward to those things which are ahead.* See the note on Philippians 3:12–14."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The reversal.** The old thing was a road through water. The new one is water in a road — and the question is whether anybody has noticed a sprout."}]}, "Isaiah 43:20–21": {"title": "The jackals and the owls", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first creatures to acknowledge the new thing are the ones nobody would choose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who honours Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **wild animals honour Me, the jackals and the owls**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chayyat hassadeh tannim uvenot ya'anah** — the beast of the field, jackals, and daughters of the ostrich."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two named"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tannim** — jackals. Scavengers, associated in the prophets with ruins and desolation: *Hazor shall be a dwelling for jackals*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Benot ya'anah** — literally *daughters of greed*, usually translated ostriches or owls. Listed among the unclean birds in Leviticus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are creatures of waste places, and both are ceremonially unclean."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of everything that could be named as giving God honour, the passage picks the animals that live where nothing else does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **I give water in the wilderness and rivers in the desert**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Repeating the previous verse word for word. See the note on Isaiah 43:18–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The jackals honour Him because water arrived in the one place they live."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do that Israel does not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter goes on: *but you have not called upon Me, O Jacob… you have not brought Me the sheep for your burnt offerings*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The unclean animals acknowledge it; the people who were given the covenant have not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is not stated, and the ordering makes it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **give drink to My people, My chosen**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ammi vechiri** — My people, My chosen one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The water was not for the jackals. They benefit from something aimed elsewhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The forming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people **I formed for Myself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **am-zu yatzarti li** — this people I formed for Myself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yatzar** — the potter's verb. The word used of God forming man from the dust, and of forming Jeremiah in the womb. See the note on Jeremiah 1:4–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That they might **declare My praise**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tehillati yesapperu** — My praise they shall recount."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tehillah** — praise. The root behind **Tehillim**, the Hebrew name for the book of Psalms."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Saphar** — to count, to recount, to tell."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that says about the forming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people were formed for a purpose, and the purpose is stated as speech."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not service, obedience, or conquest. Telling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But you are a chosen generation, a royal priesthood, a holy nation, His own special people, **that you may proclaim the praises of Him who called you out of darkness into His marvellous light**.* See the note on 1 Peter 2:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter assembles the phrase out of Isaiah and Exodus and applies it to scattered Gentile congregations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter opened with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Fear not, for I have redeemed you; **I have called you by your name; you are Mine**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Since you were precious in My sight, you have been honoured, and **I have loved you**.\"* See the note on Isaiah 43:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter ends with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I, even I, am He who blots out your transgressions for My own sake**; and I will not remember your sins.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lema'ani** — for My own sake."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stated reason for the blotting out is not their worthiness."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order.** Jackals and owls honour Him for the water; the people it was drawn for have not called on Him. The passage puts the two facts next to each other and says nothing."}]}, "Isaiah 44:3–5": {"title": "Water on thirsty ground", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two pourings, and the second explains the first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Yet hear now, O Jacob My servant, and Israel whom I have chosen. Thus says the LORD who made you and formed you from the womb, who will help you: **Fear not, O Jacob My servant; and you, Jeshurun, whom I have chosen**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Jeshurun** — the upright one. An affectionate name for Israel, used four times in the Bible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first pouring"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For I will **pour water on the thirsty land** and **streams on the dry ground**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tsame** — thirsty. And **yabbashah**, dry ground — the word for the dry land in Genesis 1 and at the Red Sea."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **pour out My Spirit on your children** and **My blessing on your descendants**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two lines are parallel, which is how Hebrew poetry defines its terms. Water on dry ground is the picture; Spirit on children is the thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is the same for both — **yatsaq**, to pour out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who receives it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your children… your descendants.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **zar'akha** — your seed, and **tse'etsa'ekha**, your offspring."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise is not to the generation being addressed. It is over their shoulder."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter uses the same shape at Pentecost: *the promise is to you and to your children, and to all who are afar off*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The growth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will **spring up like grass in a meadow, like willows by flowing streams**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for the second is **aravim al-yivlei mayim** — poplars or willows by water channels."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both images are of things that grow fast where there is water and not at all where there is not. Nothing is said about their effort."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same picture as the tree by the river: *he shall be like a tree planted by the rivers of water*. See the note on Psalms 1:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three declarations"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One will say, \"**I belong to the LORD**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Another will call himself by **the name of Jacob**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Another will **write on his hand, \"The LORD's,\"** and take the name Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three people, three ways of saying it, and each is more public than the last."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shall write on his hand, \"To the LORD\"*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A mark on the skin, visible, permanent, and the kind of thing a slave or a soldier carried to show whose he was."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah has already used the image the other way round: *see, **I have inscribed you on the palms of My hands**; your walls are continually before Me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both hands are written on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter goes on to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The longest satire on idol-making in the Bible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man cuts down a cedar. *He burns half of it in the fire; with this half he eats meat… and the rest of it he makes into a god, his carved image. He falls down before it and worships… and says, \"Deliver me, for you are my god!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And no one considers in his heart… \"Shall I fall down before a block of wood?\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line after that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have blotted out, like a thick cloud, your transgressions, and like a cloud, your sins. **Return to Me, for I have redeemed you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Return, because you have already been redeemed. The order is not the expected one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other promise about the children"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**As for Me,** says the LORD, **this is My covenant with them**: My Spirit who is upon you, and My words which I have put in your mouth, shall not depart from your mouth, nor from the mouth of your descendants.\"* See the note on Isaiah 59:21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three generations named there too."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the metaphor is defined.** Isaiah does not leave it to the reader. Water on dry ground in one line, and the Spirit on your children in the next — the same verb both times."}]}, "Isaiah 45:1–4": {"title": "Though you did not know Me", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A Persian king is addressed by name a century and a half before he is born."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is what the LORD says to **Cyrus, His anointed one**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **limshicho** — to His messiah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word used of Israel's kings, of the high priest, and of the coming deliverer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here it is applied to a Gentile emperor who worshipped Marduk."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Cyrus was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The founder of the Persian Empire. He took Babylon in 539 BC, and issued the decree that let the exiles go home and rebuild the temple."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Thus says Cyrus king of Persia: \"The LORD God of heaven has given me all the kingdoms of the earth. And He has commanded me to build Him a house at Jerusalem.\"* — the last verse of Chronicles, and the first of Ezra."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grip"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The man **He has taken by the right hand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hechezaqti vimino** — I have grasped his right hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Babylonian ritual, the king took the hand of the god Marduk at the new year festival to be confirmed in office."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah reverses it. The God of Israel takes the Persian's hand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clearing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will walk ahead of you and level the mountains**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will **break down the bronze gates**. I will **shatter the iron bars**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Herodotus records that Babylon's river gates were left open on the night the city fell."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The treasure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will hand you **the treasures hidden in the dark, the riches tucked away in secret places**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Babylon's accumulated wealth, and the vessels Nebuchadnezzar had carried out of the temple — which Cyrus returned, itemised, in Ezra 1."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then **you will know it was I, the LORD, the God of Israel, who called you by name**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I did this for the sake of My servant Jacob, for Israel, My chosen**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole Persian conquest is stated as being for the benefit of a small displaced people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I called you by name and gave you a title of honor, **though you did not know Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **velo yeda'tani**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Repeated in the next verse: *I will gird you, though you have not known Me*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The commissioning does not depend on the man's awareness of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cyrus is named, grasped by the hand, given the treasure, and called God's anointed — and the text says twice that he does not know who is doing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When this was written"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah is prophesying to a nation that has not yet gone into exile, about a king who will let them out of one, by name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter before ends: *who says of Cyrus, \"He is My shepherd, and he shall perform all My pleasure\"*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am the LORD, and there is no other**; there is no God besides Me. I will gird you, though you have not known Me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I form the light and create darkness, I make peace and create calamity**; I, the LORD, do all these things.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most sweeping claim of sole agency in the Old Testament, and it comes in a paragraph about a foreign king."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does with the same logic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **there is no authority except from God**, and the authorities that exist are appointed by God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is God's minister to you for good.* See the note on Romans 13:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word **diakonos** applied to a pagan magistrate who does not know whose he is."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clause repeated twice.** *Though you did not know Me.* The naming, the grip on the hand, and the title are all stated as independent of the man's awareness of them."}]}, "Isaiah 45:22–23": {"title": "\"By My own self I have sworn\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An invitation shouted at the edges of the world, and then an oath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The invitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Turn to Me and be saved, **all the ends of the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *turn* is to look, to face toward. The older rendering is *look unto Me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same posture as the bronze serpent in the wilderness — a nation dying of snakebite, told to look at something lifted up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole instruction. Not climb, not fetch, not achieve. Turn your face this way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is addressed to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All the ends of the earth.* Written to Israel, in exile, and aimed past them at everybody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For I am God, and there is no other.* The chapter says it repeatedly — Isaiah 45 is the most concentrated statement of monotheism in the Old Testament, and it is addressed partly to Cyrus, a Persian king named a century and a half before he was born."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By My own self I have sworn.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is nobody higher to swear by. Hebrews makes the point: *because He could swear by no one greater, He swore by Himself.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was sworn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My mouth has spoken **righteousness**, a word that **will not come back empty**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that it will not return — the same idea as chapter 55: *my word shall not return to Me void, but it shall accomplish what I please.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Righteousness is what the sworn word is about, and it is spoken out of His own mouth rather than required from anybody's hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before Me **every knee will bow**, by Me **every tongue will swear**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes it twice — in Romans 14, of the judgement seat, and in Philippians 2, where it is the climax of the passage about Christ emptying Himself:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That at the name of Jesus every knee should bow… and every tongue should confess that Jesus Christ is Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes a verse where God swears by Himself and applies it, without comment, to Jesus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The knee and the tongue"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of the second clause is that every tongue will *swear* — will make its own declaration."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two go together in the verse: a knee bent and a mouth open. And the next verse in Isaiah is what the mouth says: *surely in the LORD I have righteousness and strength.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in myself. In Him, and said out loud. See the note on Romans 10:5–8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes right after.** *In the LORD all the descendants of Israel shall be justified, and shall glory.* Justified — the same word Paul builds Romans on, in a prophet writing seven centuries earlier."}]}, "Isaiah 48:17–18": {"title": "\"Teaches you what is best for you\" — and the sigh that follows", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and the second one changes the tone of the first completely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God calls Himself here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your Redeemer, the Holy One of Israel* — and then two working descriptions:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**who teaches you what is best for you** — the Hebrew is the verb for profiting or benefiting. Teaching aimed at the pupil's advantage.", "**who leads you in the way you should go**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are stated as ongoing character, not as offers. This is what He is like."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the sigh"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If only you had listened to My commands, your peace would have flowed like a river, your righteousness like the waves of the sea.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is not a threat. It is regret — the kind of sentence a parent says looking at a situation that did not have to happen. And it is spoken to a people already in exile, already living with the consequences."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The images are worth noticing: a river, and sea waves. Both are pictures of something that keeps coming. Not a reservoir that could run out — a supply with a source behind it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Read on.** The very next verses do not end in the sigh. God goes straight to bringing them out, telling them to leave Babylon with a shout, and reminding them He led people through the desert without letting them go thirsty. The regret is real and it does not get the last word."}]}, "Isaiah 49:22–26": {"title": "\"I Myself will save your children\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The passage answers a question that is put in the middle of it, and the question is a good one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The objection, raised inside the text"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Can plunder be taken from a warrior? Can captives be rescued from the grip of the strong?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The expected answer is no. That is the point of asking — it is the objection of someone looking at a situation and concluding that whatever has hold of it is stronger than anything likely to intervene."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah puts the doubt in the text rather than talking past it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yes, even the captives of the warrior will be taken back… I Myself will fight those who fight you, and I Myself will save your children.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The emphatic pronoun appears twice. Not *your children will be saved* — **I Myself**. The rescue is claimed personally, by name, against a stronger captor."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The imagery at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will make your oppressors eat their own flesh. They will be drunk on their own blood as if it were sweet wine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is violent, and it should not be trimmed. Isaiah is writing about empires that took children into captivity, and the language of judgement in the prophets is deliberately unbearable — it is meant to sound like what it describes."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The setting.** This whole chapter is addressed to people who had concluded God had forgotten them; a few verses earlier is the famous *can a mother forget the child at her breast?* The promise about children is the answer to a complaint about abandonment, which is why it is stated in the first person twice over."}]}, "Isaiah 5:20": {"title": "Calling evil good", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, three pairs, and every pair is reversed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fourth of six woes in Isaiah 5. The chapter opens with the song of the vineyard — *my Well-beloved has a vineyard on a very fruitful hill* — which He digs, clears of stones, plants with the choicest vine, and builds a tower in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He expected it to bring forth good grapes, but it brought forth wild grapes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the identification: *for the vineyard of the LORD of hosts is the house of Israel*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the woes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The woes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Woe to those who join house to house and field to field."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Woe to those who rise early to pursue strong drink."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Woe to those who draw iniquity with cords of vanity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Woe to those who call evil good and good evil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Woe to those who are wise in their own eyes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Woe to those who are heroes at drinking wine, and *who justify the wicked for a bribe, and take away justice from the righteous man*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six, and this one is in the middle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sorrow to those.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hoy** — a funeral cry. The sound made at a death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a curse. A lament, spoken in advance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who **call evil good and good evil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not doing evil. Naming it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The other five woes are about behaviour — land-grabbing, drinking, bribery. This one is about vocabulary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who **trade darkness for light and light for darkness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *who put darkness for light*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who **swap bitter for sweet and sweet for bitter**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Taste, after sight. The three pairs move from the moral to the visual to the physical."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each is stated both ways round, so there is no direction it does not cover."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the exchange does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not change anything. Calling darkness light leaves the room exactly as dark, and the person in it without a word for the problem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why it gets a funeral cry. The other five woes describe people doing wrong; this one describes people who have lost the equipment to tell."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Woe to those who are **wise in their own eyes**, and prudent in their own sight!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two go together and Isaiah puts them side by side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same swap turns up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Amos: *you who turn justice to wormwood, and lay righteousness to rest in the earth*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Micah: *who hate good and love evil*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Malachi, at the end of the Old Testament: *you have wearied the LORD with your words. Yet you say, \"In what way have we wearied Him?\" In that you say, \"Everyone who does evil is good in the sight of the LORD.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Samson's riddle did with the same words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Out of the strong came something sweet.* See the note on Judges 14:12–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bitter and sweet, exchanged — in a riddle, deliberately, as a puzzle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah's is the same exchange done to a whole society's language, and he sings a funeral over it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore the anger of the LORD is aroused against His people… **and He will lift up a banner to the nations from afar, and will whistle to them from the end of the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Assyrians, summoned like a swarm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chapter after it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the year that King Uzziah died, I saw the Lord sitting on a throne, high and lifted up… \"**Woe is me, for I am undone!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six woes on the nation in chapter 5, and in chapter 6 the prophet says the seventh over himself."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What this woe is about.** Not conduct. Every other woe in the chapter names something people did — this one names what they called it."}]}, "Isaiah 51:1–3": {"title": "The rock you were cut from", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A discouraged people are told to look backwards at two very old people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Listen to Me, you who **chase after what is right**, you who **seek the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **rodephei tzedeq** — pursuers of righteousness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the indifferent. The ones already trying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look to **the rock you were cut from**, to **the quarry you were dug out of**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tzur chutzavtem** — the rock you were hewn from. And **maqqevet bor** — the hollow of the pit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Quarry language. Blocks cut out of a face and lifted from a hole."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the rock is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look to **Abraham, your father**, and to **Sarah, who gave you birth**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Sarah is named"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is mentioned by name in the Old Testament outside Genesis exactly once, here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophet is talking to people who feel too few and too weak to be a nation, and he names the woman who was barren until ninety."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The point"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When I called him, **he was only one man**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ki-echad qerativ** — for one I called him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One. Childless, seventy-five, in Ur, with no land and no son."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **I blessed him and made him many**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs, both first person. *I blessed*, and *I multiplied*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The arithmetic between *one* and *many* is entirely God's, and that is the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being addressed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exiles. A remnant in Babylon, looking at their own numbers and concluding it is over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer is not encouragement about their prospects. It is a fact about where they came from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comfort"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the LORD **will comfort Zion**. He will comfort **all her ruins**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nicham** — to comfort, to console."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb that opens chapter 40: *comfort, yes, comfort My people*. See the note on Isaiah 40:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will make her **wilderness like Eden**, her **desert like the garden of the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Midbar** — wilderness. **Eden** — delight."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only two places in Isaiah where Eden is named, and both are here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is found there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joy and gladness will be found in her, **thanksgiving and the sound of singing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **todah veqol zimrah** — thanksgiving and the voice of melody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter goes on to say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lift up your eyes to the heavens, and look on the earth beneath. For **the heavens will vanish away like smoke**, the earth will grow old like a garment… **but My salvation will be forever**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not fear the reproach of men**, nor be afraid of their insults.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The waking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Awake, awake, put on strength, O arm of the LORD!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Awake, awake! Stand up, O Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter and the next are full of the same imperative, aimed alternately at God and at the city."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the one man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore from **one man**, and him as good as dead, were born as many as the stars of the sky in multitude — innumerable as the sand which is by the seashore.* See the note on Hebrews 11:8–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And him as good as dead* — **nenekromenou** — having been deadened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other rock"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And all drank the same spiritual drink. For **they drank of that spiritual Rock that followed them, and that Rock was Christ**.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 10:1–4."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The argument.** Not encouragement about their numbers — a fact about their origin. *When I called him, he was only one man*, and both verbs in the next clause are first person."}]}, "Isaiah 52:1–2": {"title": "Shake off the dust", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Six imperatives in two verses, and every one is addressed to somebody sitting down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The doubling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wake up, wake up, O Zion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **uri uri** — the verb twice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah has just used the same doubling on God, at the end of the previous chapter: *awake, awake, put on strength, O arm of the LORD*. And before that, *awake, awake, stand up, O Jerusalem, you who have drunk the cup of His fury*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same command is aimed at God, then at the city."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clothing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Clothe yourself in **your strength**. Put on your **garments of splendor**, O Jerusalem, holy city.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *put on your beautiful garments*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her own strength, and her own clothes. Nothing is being handed over — she is told to wear what belongs to her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is over"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Never again will **the unclean and the ungodly enter you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the uncircumcised and the unclean*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The city had been walked into by Babylonian troops. This is a promise about that ending."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dust"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Shake off the dust. **Rise up. Sit enthroned**, O Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three commands about posture in one line."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of the middle one is *arise, sit* — get up off the ground and sit somewhere else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dust is the mourner's position and the prisoner's. Job sat in ashes; the exiles *sat down by the rivers of Babylon and wept*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chains"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Free yourself from the **chains around your neck**, O **captive daughter of Zion**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *loose yourself from the bonds of your neck*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is told to do the loosing. The verb is reflexive, and the chapter offers no explanation of how."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the next verse says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For thus says the LORD: \"**You have sold yourselves for nothing, and you shall be redeemed without money**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The transaction that put her there cost nothing, and the one that gets her out costs her nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How beautiful upon the mountains are the feet of him who brings good news, who proclaims peace… who says to Zion, \"**Your God reigns!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes it in Romans about preaching."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the watchmen: *they shall see eye to eye when the LORD brings back Zion.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold, My Servant shall deal prudently; He shall be exalted and extolled and be very high… **His visage was marred more than any man**, and His form more than the sons of men.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last three verses of chapter 52 are the opening of the passage everyone calls Isaiah 53. The chapter division falls in the middle of a poem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wake up, shake off the dust* is four verses before *He is despised and rejected of men*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is quoted for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul, twice, in the same shape:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Awake, you who sleep, arise from the dead, and Christ will give you light.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *the night is far spent, the day is at hand. Therefore let us cast off the works of darkness, and **let us put on the armour of light***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Take something off, put something on, and get up — the three moves of Isaiah's two verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the commands are for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not for people who need to be rescued. For people whose rescue has been announced."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter says *you shall be redeemed* in verse 3, and the imperatives are in verses 1 and 2."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not explained.** How to loose the chains. She is told to do it, told to stand up and told what to wear — and the reason given is in the verse after: *you shall be redeemed without money.*"}]}, "Isaiah 52:13–15": {"title": "\"Disfigured beyond that of any man\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The fourth Servant Song begins here, three verses before the chapter break, and it opens with the ending."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will be raised up and lifted high and greatly exalted.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs of exaltation, stacked, **before** a word about suffering. Isaiah gives away the outcome first and then tells you what it cost."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same three-fold lifting language is used elsewhere in Isaiah of God Himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disfigurement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His appearance was disfigured beyond that of any man, his form beyond human likeness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is stronger than most translations manage — a marring so severe that what people were looking at no longer registered as a man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah wrote this centuries before crucifixion existed as a Roman practice. What he describes is a body damaged past recognition, in front of appalled onlookers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"So he will startle many nations\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb has been debated for a long time. It can mean to sprinkle — the word used of a priest sprinkling blood — or to startle, to make them recoil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parallel in the sentence favours the second: *just as many were appalled at him… so he will startle many nations.* One reaction answering another. But the sprinkling sense sits underneath, and readers have heard both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Kings with nothing to say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Kings will shut their mouths because of him. For what they were never told, they will see, and what they never heard, they will understand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The silence is not respect. It is people with authority having no reply."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the reason given is that something arrives outside the report — not passed down, not heard about. Seen."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it goes next.** *Who has believed our message?* — the opening line of chapter 53. The song moves straight from kings shutting their mouths to the complaint that nobody believed it. Both are true of the same figure, and Paul quotes the second line in Romans 10."}]}, "Isaiah 53:1–3": {"title": "Nothing to look at", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens with a question about belief, not about suffering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the passage actually starts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verses earlier, at the end of chapter 52: *behold, My Servant shall deal prudently; He shall be exalted and extolled and be very high*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *His visage was marred more than any man, and His form more than the sons of men*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter division falls in the middle of a poem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who has believed **what we have heard**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shemu'atenu** — our report, our message."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Answered by John at the end of his Gospel: *but although He had done so many signs before them, they did not believe in Him, that the word of Isaiah the prophet might be fulfilled which he spoke: \"Lord, who has believed our report?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And by Paul, about preaching: *so then faith comes by hearing, and hearing by the word of God*. See the note on Romans 10:16–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **to whom has the arm of the LORD been revealed**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The arm is God's own strength. Isaiah has used it: *the LORD has made bare His holy arm in the eyes of all the nations*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a few chapters later, when nobody would intervene: *therefore His own arm brought salvation for Him*. See the note on Isaiah 59:15b–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The growing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He grew up before Him **like a tender shoot**, like **a root out of dry ground**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yoneq** — a suckling, the small shoot that comes off a stump."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah has already used the image: *there shall come forth a Rod from the stem of Jesse*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Out of dry ground** — nothing about the soil accounts for it. The family line was a stump by then; the house of David had not held a throne for centuries when this was fulfilled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The appearance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He had **no beauty, no majesty to draw us to him**, nothing in his appearance that we should desire him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *no form nor comeliness*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one physical description of the Messiah in the Old Testament, and it says there was nothing to see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the bride says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His mouth is all sweetness. **He is altogether lovely**.* See the note on Song of Songs 5:14–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Song's description runs to seven verses of gold, ivory, marble and cedar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah says there was nothing in His appearance to desire."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are in the Old Testament, and the second is what people saw."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rejection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was **despised and rejected by men**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chadal ishim** — forsaken of men, ceasing from men."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **man of sorrows**, and **well acquainted with grief**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ish makhovot** — a man of pains. And **yedua choli** — known of sickness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Makhov** is physical pain; **choli** is disease."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are the two nouns that recur in the next verse: *surely He has borne our **griefs** and carried our **sorrows**.* The same pair, and there the King James renders them the other way round. See the note on Isaiah 53:4–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew quotes that verse of a night of healings: *He Himself took our infirmities and bore our sicknesses*. See the note on Matthew 8:14–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The averted faces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was like **one from whom people hide their faces**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *as one from whom men hide the face*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gesture people make around a leper, or a disfigurement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was despised, and **we held him of no account**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *we did not esteem Him* — reckoned Him as nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We.* All the way through — our report, we hid our faces, we did not esteem Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage is a confession. The people describing His rejection are the ones who did it, and by verse 6 they are saying *all we like sheep have gone astray*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one physical description in the prophets.** No form, no comeliness, nothing to desire — and the Song of Songs, describing the same figure, spends seven verses on gold and cedar."}]}, "Isaiah 53:10–12": {"title": "\"He will see the light and be satisfied\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last three verses of the song, and they are the only ones with a future in them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hardest line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet **it was the LORD's will to crush him** and cause him to suffer.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *it pleased the LORD to bruise him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah has spent the chapter saying the servant was despised by men, rejected by men, oppressed and afflicted — and then names who was behind it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And though the LORD makes **his life an offering for guilt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **asham** — the guilt offering, the one in Leviticus 5 brought when restitution had to be made as well as atonement."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of the five offerings, this is the one about repayment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything after this is future tense, and all of it is gain:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*He will **see his offspring***", "*and **prolong his days***", "*and **the will of the LORD will prosper in his hand***"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who has just been described as cut off out of the land of the living, without descendants, sees offspring and long days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Prosper\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tsalach** — to advance, to succeed, to push through. The same word used of Joseph in Potiphar's house. See the note on Genesis 39:1–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The satisfaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After **the anguish of his soul**, he will **see the light and be satisfied**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the travail of his soul*. **Satisfied** is **saba** — full, having had enough."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something is on the far side of it that is worth what it cost, and He looks at it and is content."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the justifying works"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By **his knowledge** my righteous servant **will justify many**, and **he will bear their iniquities**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two halves in one line. He justifies, and He bears — the second is how the first happens."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Justify** is the legal word: to declare in the right. It is not the same as making somebody better."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The division"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore I will **give him a portion among the great**, and he will **divide the spoils with the strong**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Conqueror's language — the general dividing the plunder after the battle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four reasons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew gives four clauses, each stating why:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*because he **poured out his life even to death***", "*and was **counted in among the lawbreakers***", "*for he **bore the sin of many***", "*and **made intercession for the transgressors***"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Jesus quotes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I say to you that this which is written must still be accomplished in Me: **'And He was numbered with the transgressors.'** For the things concerning Me have an end.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes verse 12 about Himself, on the night of the arrest, and the next day two criminals are crucified either side of Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **made intercession for the transgressors**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah's final word about the servant is not the death. It is the praying."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke records it from the cross: *Father, forgive them, for they do not know what they do.* And Hebrews puts it in the present tense: *He always lives to make intercession for them.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter ends up.** It opens *who has believed our report?* and closes with spoils divided and a man satisfied. The turn happens at the guilt offering."}]}, "Isaiah 53:4–6": {"title": "The verbs of exchange", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Six lines, and almost every one of them is a transfer: something that was ours, moved onto him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What moves, and where it goes"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**took up** our pain — the Hebrew *nasa*, to lift and carry away", "**carried** our sorrows — *sabal*, to bear a load on the back", "pierced **for** our transgressions", "crushed **for** our iniquities", "the punishment that brought **us** peace was laid **upon him**", "the LORD has laid on him the guilt of **us all**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the passage is described as being fixed in place. Everything is moved from one party to another. That is the grammar of substitution, and it is why this chapter is quoted more often in the New Testament than almost any other in the prophets."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet we thought him stricken by God, struck down by Him, and afflicted.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the confession of the people speaking. They saw a man suffering and drew the ordinary conclusion — that God must be against him. They were exactly wrong, and the chapter is written from the far side of finding out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is worth remembering the next time suffering is read as a verdict."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"By his wounds we are healed\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew *rapha* is the ordinary word for healing, used of bodies and of nations and of God healing a people's turning away. It is broad, and Isaiah does not narrow it here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What the sentence does specify is the mechanism: the healing is *by his wounds*. Whatever is being healed, it is being healed at his cost and not at ours — which is the argument of the whole chapter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *We all, like sheep, have wandered off. Each of us has turned to his own way.* Note the two halves: a herd going astray together, and every single one going a different direction. Both are true at once, and the chapter puts the collective and the individual in the same sentence before laying all of it on one man."}]}, "Isaiah 54:1–3": {"title": "\"Make the tent wider\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens by telling a barren woman to sing, and then to build for children she does not have."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being addressed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sing, **you who never held a child**. Break into song and shout for joy, **you who never knew the pains of birth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that world this was the worst thing that could be said about a woman, and the chapter opens by naming it twice and then commanding singing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The singing comes first. Before anything has changed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 54 follows Isaiah 53 — *wounded for our transgressions… by His stripes we are healed… He shall see His seed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The barren woman sings because of what happened in the chapter before. The Servant's suffering is what produces her children."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **make the tent wider. Stretch out the curtains. Do not hold back. Lengthen your ropes and drive your stakes down deep.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five commands, and they are all construction work on an empty tent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is told to prepare accommodation before there is anyone to accommodate — to buy the cot, in effect, while still childless."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lengthen your ropes* — the guy lines go further out, because the tent is getting bigger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Drive your stakes down deep* — and the Hebrew is *strengthen your stakes*. A wider tent takes more wind; the anchoring has to go deeper as the span increases."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both at once. Wider and better anchored, not one or the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will spread out to the right and to the left.** Your children will take over the nations and settle the emptied cities.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *spread out* is to break forth, to burst through."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the passage tells her to produce a child, or to try. The enlargement is what she does; the children are what God does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the woman left alone will have more children than the woman with a husband, says the LORD.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Further down: *you will be established in righteousness… their righteousness is from Me, says the LORD.* See the note on Isaiah 54:13–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the guarantee He gives for the whole thing is a rainbow: *as I have sworn that the waters of Noah would no longer cover the earth, so have I sworn that I would not be angry with you.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who Paul says this is about.** He quotes verse 1 in Galatians 4, of *the Jerusalem above, which is the mother of us all* — the covenant of promise rather than the covenant of law. The barren woman with more children than the married one is, in his reading, grace outproducing the law."}]}, "Isaiah 54:13–14": {"title": "\"Established in righteousness\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and everything in the second one hangs off a single condition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The children"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All your children will be **taught by the LORD**, and great will be their peace.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *taught of the LORD* — not taught about Him. He is the teacher."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus quotes it in John 6: *it is written in the prophets, \"And they shall all be taught by God.\" Therefore everyone who has heard and learned from the Father comes to Me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *great will be their peace* — **shalom**, which is not the absence of noise. Wholeness, soundness, nothing missing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will be **established in righteousness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *established* is to be set firm, fixed, made to stand — the word used of a foundation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what a person is established in is named: righteousness. Not effort, not vigilance, not track record."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has already said whose righteousness it is — verse 17: *this is the heritage of the servants of the LORD, and **their righteousness is from Me**, says the LORD.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows from it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things, all of them consequences of the establishing:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Cruelty will be far from you*", "*you will have nothing to fear*", "*Terror will be far from you, and it will not come near*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *far* is distance — put away, removed to a distance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fear is not managed here. It is relocated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 54 is the chapter immediately after Isaiah 53 — the suffering servant, wounded for our transgressions, by whose stripes we are healed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And 54 opens *Sing, O barren, you who have not borne!* The whole chapter is the result of chapter 53, and it is addressed to somebody who had nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest of the promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For the mountains shall depart and the hills be removed, but **My kindness shall not depart from you**, nor shall My covenant of peace be removed,\" says the LORD, who has mercy on you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He names the guarantee: *this is like the waters of Noah to Me; for as I have sworn that the waters of Noah would no longer cover the earth, so have I sworn that I would not be angry with you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He points at a rainbow and says the two promises are the same kind."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last verse.** *No weapon formed against you shall prosper, and every tongue which rises against you in judgement you shall condemn.* It is the most quoted line in the chapter, and the sentence it ends on is *their righteousness is from Me.* The verse is anchored to whose righteousness it is."}]}, "Isaiah 54:15–16": {"title": "\"Both are Mine\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses in the middle of a chapter about a marriage, and they are about weapons."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 54 follows immediately after the suffering servant of chapter 53. It opens *sing, O barren, you who have not borne*, and it is addressed to a woman who has been left."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For your Maker is your husband… for the LORD has called you like a woman forsaken and grieved in spirit.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The concession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If **anyone attacks you, it will not be My doing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *behold, they shall surely assemble, but not because of Me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Attacks are not denied. Their origin is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same clause the chapter's most quoted verse depends on: *no weapon formed against you shall prosper* — formed, which grants that they get made."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The outcome"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever comes against you **will fall before you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shall fall for your sake*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blacksmith"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look, **I created the blacksmith** who **fans the coals into flame** and **forges the weapon for its work**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three clauses about the manufacture: the man, the fire, and the product."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God claims the craftsman."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The destroyer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **I created the destroyer to lay waste**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the waster to destroy*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Both are Mine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is emphatic. Isaiah has already said it of the whole business: *I form the light and create darkness; I make peace and create calamity; I, the LORD, do all these things.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the argument is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse is not about the weapons failing to exist. It is about who made the man who makes them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse draws the conclusion: *no weapon formed against you shall prosper, and every tongue which rises against you in judgment you shall condemn.* See the note on Isaiah 54:17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reasoning runs: the smith is Mine, so the product is not outside My reach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heritage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is **the heritage of the servants of the LORD**, and **their righteousness is from Me**,\" says the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's final sentence, and it ends on where the righteousness comes from rather than on the weapons."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is promised earlier in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For the mountains shall depart and the hills be removed, but **My kindness shall not depart from you**, nor shall My covenant of peace be removed.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"**In righteousness you shall be established**; you shall be far from oppression, for you shall not fear.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *established* is **kun** — fixed, set firm. The same word used of the priests standing in the Jordan. See the note on Joshua 3:14–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter promises about children"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**All your children shall be taught by the LORD**, and great shall be the peace of your children.\"* See the note on Isaiah 54:13–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus quotes that line in John 6: *it is written in the prophets, \"And they shall all be taught by God.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not claimed.** That nothing is made. Isaiah grants the smith, the fire and the finished weapon, and then says who made the smith."}]}, "Isaiah 54:17": {"title": "\"This is the inheritance\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last verse of the chapter, and the most quoted sentence in it depends on the clause after it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No **weapon** shaped against you will succeed, and every **tongue** that rises to accuse you, you will prove wrong.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A weapon and a tongue. Force and accusation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note what is not promised: that the weapon will not be made. The verse before says outright *I have created the blacksmith who blows the coals in the fire and brings forth an instrument for his work.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The forging happens. The prospering does not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tongue"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every tongue that **rises to accuse you**, you will prove wrong.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *you shall condemn* — a courtroom verb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only active thing the person does in the whole verse, and it is answering an accusation rather than fighting a weapon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause everything rests on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is **the inheritance of the servants of the LORD**. This is their **vindication, and it comes from Me**, says the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *vindication* is **tsedaqah** — righteousness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the last three words are the ones that hold the verse up: **from Me**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their righteousness is not theirs. The chapter says so plainly, and the promise about weapons is attached to it as its consequence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "An inheritance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nachalah** — the family portion, what you receive because of who you belong to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody earns an inheritance, and nobody keeps it by performance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter has come from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 54 follows Isaiah 53 — *He was wounded for our transgressions… by His stripes we are healed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens by telling a barren woman to sing, and to enlarge her tent for children she does not have. See the note on Isaiah 54:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *you will be **established in righteousness**… terror will be far from you.* See the note on Isaiah 54:13–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the guarantee in the middle: *as I have sworn that the waters of Noah would no longer cover the earth, **so have I sworn that I would not be angry with you**.* See the note on Isaiah 54:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A rainbow, offered as collateral."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sing. Enlarge. Be established in righteousness. And then, last: no weapon will prosper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The famous line is the conclusion, not the premise."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the servants have.** Not immunity from being shot at. An inheritance, and a righteousness that came from somewhere else, and a promise about outcomes rather than about weather."}]}, "Isaiah 54:9–10": {"title": "The oath God swears about His own anger", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God does not often swear oaths. When He does, it is worth stopping to see what He binds Himself to — and here He binds Himself twice, on the same terms, about two different things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison is doing the work"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is like the days of Noah to Me. I swore then that the waters would never again cover the earth. So now I swear that I will not be angry with you or rebuke you again.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument runs through the flood. After it, God gave a sign in the sky and a promise that took the form of a permanent renunciation: never again by water. Everyone reading Isaiah knew that promise had held for centuries."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the second oath is measured against the first. *That* is how settled this is. Whatever you believe about the reliability of the rainbow, believe this on the same footing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being sworn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that nothing hard will happen. The oath is specifically about **God's disposition**: I will not be angry with you or rebuke you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a strange thing for God to promise a people whose failures the same book catalogues at length. Isaiah is not saying they became blameless. He is saying something changed on God's side about how He will deal with them — and the chapter before this one is the reason, which is why 53 and 54 sit next to each other."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mountains"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though the mountains walk away and the hills come loose and fall, My love for you will never walk away, and My covenant of peace will never come loose.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is deliberately absurd. Mountains are the standing metaphor for permanence in Hebrew poetry — the oldest, heaviest, least movable things anyone could point at."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah says: suppose those went. Suppose the most fixed thing you can see got up and left. Even then. He picks the strongest available comparison and then says this outlasts it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is spoken to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens by addressing a barren woman, a widow, someone abandoned and ashamed. That is the audience being sworn to. Not a people at their best — a people at their least deserving of a promise about God's permanent good will."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"covenant of peace.\"** *Shalom* is wider than the absence of conflict; it is wholeness, everything in its right place. The word God attaches to His oath is not \"truce.\" It is a settled state of things that He is committing Himself to maintain."}]}, "Isaiah 55:1–3": {"title": "\"Come, buy with no money\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An invitation shouted like a market trader's cry, and the price is the strangest thing about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Come, all you who are **thirsty**… and you who **have no money**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two qualifications, and both are lacks. Thirst, and an empty purse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is required except the absence of what would normally be required."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The impossible instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Come, **buy** and eat! Come, **buy wine and milk, with no money and no cost**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Buy, with nothing to buy with. The verb is the ordinary word for a commercial purchase, and Isaiah puts it next to *without money and without price*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is deliberately absurd. There is a transaction and there is no payment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is on offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Water, wine, milk, and *the richest food* — the Hebrew is *fatness*, the choicest part."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not rations. Wine and milk are what you serve at a feast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Why **spend your silver on what is not bread**, and your labour on **what never satisfies**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two currencies wasted: money and work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah does not say the things bought are evil. He says they are *not bread* — they do not do what food does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ecclesiastes ran the same experiment at length and reported the same finding. See the note on Ecclesiastes 3:11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Listen well… **eat what is good**… let your soul delight… **Turn your ear and come to Me. Listen, and your soul will live.***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Listen** three times in two verses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *incline your ear* — the Hebrew is to stretch the ear toward, to lean in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the listening produces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Listen, and **your soul will live**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Life, in exchange for attention."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The covenant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will make **an everlasting covenant** with you, **the sure mercies promised to David**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is the faithful, dependable mercies — **chasdei David**, the covenant loyalties sworn to David in 2 Samuel 7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes exactly this phrase at Antioch, and applies it to the resurrection: *God has fulfilled this for us… \"I will give you the sure mercies of David.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 53 is the suffering servant. Isaiah 54 is the barren woman told to sing and enlarge her tent. Isaiah 55 is the free invitation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three chapters in sequence: a price paid, a household enlarged, and a meal offered at no cost. See the note on Isaiah 54:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My word… shall not return to Me void, but it shall accomplish what I please.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *the mountains and the hills shall break forth into singing before you, and all the trees of the field shall clap their hands.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last word of the invitation.** *Seek the LORD **while He may be found**, call upon Him **while He is near**.* The offer is free and it is not indefinite — and Paul says the same in his own words: *now is the accepted time; now is the day of salvation.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 6:1–2."}]}, "Isaiah 55:10–11": {"title": "\"It will not come back to Me empty\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A comparison between rain and speech, and the comparison does more work than it first appears."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What rain does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rain and snow come down from the sky and do not return there until they have soaked the earth, making it bud and bloom, giving seed to the sower and bread to the eater.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah traces the whole cycle: down, into the ground, up as a plant, out as seed and as bread. Nothing about it is instant, and nothing about it is wasted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rain does not look like bread on the day it falls."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the word does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So it is with the word that goes out from My mouth. It will not come back to Me empty. It will do what I intend and finish the work I sent it to do.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two claims: it will not return empty, and it will complete its errand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *empty* means without result, having achieved nothing — the same word used of coming back from somewhere with nothing in your hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The part the image supplies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rain that soaks into ground and disappears has not failed. It is at the beginning of its work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the comparison is for. A word that has been spoken and appears to have vanished is described here as being in the middle of doing something, on a timescale the passage has just spelled out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter is going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before this are the famous ones — *my thoughts are not your thoughts, neither are your ways my ways.* And the invitation at the top of the chapter: *come, all who are thirsty… without money and without cost.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the passage about a word that finishes what it was sent for sits inside an offer of something free."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The chapter's last image.** *You will go out in joy and be led forth in peace; the mountains and hills will burst into song, and all the trees of the field will clap their hands.* Isaiah keeps the agriculture running to the end — what the rain produces, in his telling, is a harvest that celebrates."}]}, "Isaiah 55:12–13": {"title": "\"Where the thornbush grew, the cypress will rise\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last two verses of the chapter, and they are the harvest of everything in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two exchanges"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where the thornbush grew, the cypress will rise. Where the briar grew, the myrtle will stand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thorns and briars are the first curse. When the ground was cursed in Genesis 3, what it produced was *thorns and thistles* — and they have stood for that ruin ever since."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cypress and myrtle are the opposite kind of plant: tall evergreen timber, and a fragrant shrub used at festivals. Neither is a crop. Both are things you would plant because they last and because they are good to be near."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The exchange is not thorn-for-wheat. It is thorn-for-something-permanent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they grow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where the thornbush grew.* The same ground. Nothing is relocated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the specific promise: the site of the curse becomes the site of the cypress."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The going out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will go out in joy and be led home in peace. The mountains and the hills will burst into song before you, and all the trees of the field will clap their hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written to exiles, so *go out* means leaving Babylon. The landscape is described as celebrating the road home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This will be a name for the LORD, a sign that lasts forever and is never cut off.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The trees are a monument. What the exchange is *for* is a name — a permanent, visible marker of who did it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *cut off* is the word used of felling a tree, which is a deliberate touch in a sentence about trees."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the crown of thorns fits.** The Gospels record it without comment. Readers have long noticed that the first curse on the ground ends up pressed onto a head — and Isaiah, four chapters after his Servant is pierced, ends with thorn ground growing cypress."}]}, "Isaiah 55:8–9": {"title": "\"Higher than your ways\" — what the comparison is about", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The verse gets quoted at funerals and after disappointments, as a way of saying God's reasons are beyond us. Read in its own paragraph it is doing something else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let the wicked forsake his way, and the unrighteous man his thoughts; let him return to the LORD, and He will have mercy on him, and to our God, for **He will abundantly pardon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *for My thoughts are not your thoughts, and your ways are not My ways.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The *for* connects them. The subject of the comparison is the pardoning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Human beings forgive on a scale — a bit, grudgingly, up to a point, with conditions. God's forgiving is described as operating at a different altitude altogether."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew behind *abundantly pardon* is emphatic: He will multiply to pardon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the sentence is not primarily about God's mysterious purposes in suffering. It is about how far past our instincts His mercy runs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As high as the heavens are above the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The largest distance available to an ancient writer, and Isaiah uses it twice elsewhere for God's steadfast love and for the removal of sins — *as far as the east is from the west.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this reading matters pastorally"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Quoted as a general statement about inscrutability, the verse can be used to shut down a grieving person's questions."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read as it stands, it says something warmer: whatever you would do with someone who had wronged you, His response is higher than that — and the gap is the size of the sky."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter opens.** *Come, all you who are thirsty… come, buy and eat, without money and without cost.* The whole chapter is an invitation to receive something free, and the verse about higher ways sits in the middle of it. See also the note on Isaiah 55:10–11."}]}, "Isaiah 58:8–9a": {"title": "\"Your healing will come quickly\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four promises in a row, and the chapter is careful about what they are attached to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Do this\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promises begin with a condition, and the chapter has just spelled it out at length. Israel had been fasting and complaining that God took no notice. His answer describes the fast He actually wants:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "loose the chains of injustice, share your bread with the hungry, bring the homeless poor into your house, clothe the naked, do not hide from your own flesh and blood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do this*, and then these four."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**your light will break forth like the dawn** — sudden, and unstoppable once begun", "**your healing will come quickly** — the Hebrew word is about new flesh sprouting over a wound; it is a recovery word, used of a nation's restoration and of a body's", "**your goodness will walk ahead of you**", "**the glory of the LORD will be your rear guard**"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The formation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last two are military. Something goes in front and something covers the back — the same picture as the exodus, where the pillar of cloud moved from before Israel to behind them, between the camp and Egypt's army."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A person in that formation is covered at both ends."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then you will call, and the LORD will answer. You will cry out, and He will say, \"Here I am.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last phrase is the same one Abraham and Moses and Samuel say to God — *hinneni*, here I am. Here it comes back the other way, from God to the person calling."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the condition matters.** The chapter is not describing a technique for getting light and healing. It is God telling a religious people that the observance they were proud of had left the hungry hungry — and that the breakthrough they wanted was on the other side of feeding them."}]}, "Isaiah 59:12–15a": {"title": "Truth stumbles in the square", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The nation's own confession, and it is unusually specific."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel, in the first person plural."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opened with God's answer to a complaint: *behold, the LORD's hand is not shortened, that it cannot save; nor His ear heavy, that it cannot hear. **But your iniquities have separated you from your God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verses 3 to 8 are His indictment. From verse 9 the people take over the sentence and finish it themselves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The admission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our sins are **piled high before You**. Our wrongs **testify against us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *our transgressions are multiplied before You, and our sins testify against us*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Legal language. The sins are witnesses for the prosecution."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The knowledge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We **carry our failures with us, and we know them well**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *as for our iniquities, we know them*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not ignorance. They can name the charges."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Turning our backs on the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lying about our God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Refusing to follow Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Speaking oppression and revolt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Brewing false words in our hearts and letting them spill out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five items, and four of the five are about speech."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *brewing* is **hago** — to mutter, to conceive. The picture is of words being worked up inside before they come out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **justice is pushed back**, and **righteousness stands far off**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two personified figures, backing away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The square"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Truth **stumbles in the public square**, and **honesty cannot get in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **rechov** — the open place inside the gate, where courts sat and business was done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Truth is pictured falling over in the one place it was supposed to stand up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **nekhochah**, uprightness, *cannot enter* — barred at the door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Truth is **nowhere to be found**, and **whoever turns from evil is treated like prey**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he who departs from evil makes himself a prey*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who stops doing wrong becomes a target. In a society running the way this one is, that is the cost of stepping out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD saw it, and it grieved Him… **and He was astonished that no one stepped in**. So His own arm brought salvation.* See the note on Isaiah 59:15b–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The confession ends and God's response begins in the middle of verse 15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the passage records the people asking for help."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chapter's own diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your iniquities have separated you from your God; and your sins have hidden His face from you, so that He will not hear.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the answer to the complaint the chapter opened with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul quotes this chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 3, in his chain of quotations: *their feet are swift to shed blood; destruction and misery are in their ways; and the way of peace they have not known*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is Isaiah 59:7–8, in the middle of the passage where Paul is proving *there is none righteous, no, not one*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any request. The people give an accurate account of the situation and stop."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rescue in the next verse is not answering a prayer."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the list is mostly about.** Speech. Four of the five items they confess are things said — and the picture that follows is truth falling over in the marketplace."}]}, "Isaiah 59:15b–17": {"title": "His own arm", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God looks for somebody to fix it, finds nobody, and dresses for the job Himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He is looking at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has been cataloguing it: hands defiled with blood, lips speaking lies, feet running to evil. *They hatch vipers' eggs and weave the spider's web.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the people's own admission of it. See the note on Isaiah 59:12–15a."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD saw it, and **it grieved Him that there was no justice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *it was evil in His eyes*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The search"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He saw that **there was no one to help**, and **He was astonished that no one stepped in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yishtomem** — He was appalled, astonished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **maphgia** — one who intervenes, who interposes. The word for an intercessor."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel has the same search: *I sought for a man among them who would make a wall, and stand in the gap before Me on behalf of the land, that I should not destroy it, **but I found no one**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **His own arm brought salvation**, and **His own righteousness held Him up**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *His arm brought salvation for Him, and His righteousness sustained Him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice: *His own*. No help was available, so nothing external is used."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah has already asked about that arm: *to whom has the arm of the LORD been revealed?* — the opening line of chapter 53."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The armour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He put on **righteousness like armor** and set the **helmet of salvation** on His head.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shiryon** — a breastplate, a coat of mail. And **kova yeshuah** — helmet of salvation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is where Paul's armour comes from. *The breastplate of righteousness*, and *the helmet of salvation*. See the note on Ephesians 6:14–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes God's own kit and hands it to the church."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other two pieces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He clothed Himself in **garments of vengeance** and wrapped Himself in **zeal like a cloak**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *zeal* is **qinah** — jealousy, ardour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul leaves these two out of the Ephesians list. The believer gets the breastplate and the helmet; vengeance and zeal are not distributed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So shall they fear the name of the LORD from the west, and His glory from the rising of the sun; **when the enemy comes in like a flood, the Spirit of the LORD will lift up a standard against him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on Isaiah 59:18–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The Redeemer will come to Zion**, and to those who turn from transgression in Jacob,\" says the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes it in Romans 11, at the end of his argument about Israel. See the note on Romans 11:25–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the covenant sentence: *My Spirit who is upon you, and My words which I have put in your mouth, shall not depart from your mouth, nor from the mouth of your descendants*. See the note on Isaiah 59:21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the next chapter opens with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Arise, shine, for your light has come!* See the note on Isaiah 60:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter division falls between the dressing and the dawn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other armour passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Put on the whole armour of God, that you may be able to stand against the wiles of the devil.* See the note on Ephesians 6:10–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not invent the equipment. He is quoting what God wore when nobody else would go."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the armour comes from.** God put it on first — because He looked for somebody to intervene and was astonished to find no one. Paul hands two pieces of it to the church."}]}, "Isaiah 59:18–20": {"title": "\"The Redeemer will come to Zion\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of a chapter about a nation with nothing to offer, and God supplies the whole answer Himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter has said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your iniquities have separated you from your God… your hands are defiled with blood… we grope for the wall like the blind… we look for justice, but there is none.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then, at verse 16: *He saw that there was no man, and **wondered that there was no intercessor**; therefore **His own arm brought salvation** for Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody was found, so He did it Himself. That is the setting for these verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repayment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will **repay according to what has been done**, wrath to His foes and just return to His enemies.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fear"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From **the west**, people will fear the name of the LORD, and from **the east**, His glory.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *from the going down of the sun* and *from the rising*. Both directions, which is the Hebrew way of saying everywhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The famous line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When **the enemy comes rushing in like a flood**, **the Spirit of the LORD will lift up a banner against him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew here is genuinely difficult, and translations divide over the punctuation. Some read it as the enemy coming in like a flood; others as *the Spirit of the LORD will lift up a standard against him* when *He shall come like a rushing stream*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way the two elements are a flood and a raised banner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *banner* is **nes** — a standard, a signal pole. It is the word Moses used after Amalek: *the LORD is my Banner* — **Jehovah Nissi**. See the note on Exodus 17:8–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A standard was raised so scattered troops could see where to rally."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Redeemer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The Redeemer will come to Zion**,\" says the LORD, \"**to those in Jacob who turn away from their sins**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **goel** — the kinsman-redeemer, the relative obliged to buy back what a family member had lost. Boaz's role in Ruth, and Job's word: *I know that my Redeemer lives.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul quotes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 11, at the end of three chapters about Israel: *\"**The Deliverer will come out of Zion, and He will turn away ungodliness from Jacob**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes the Greek version, which has the Redeemer doing the turning rather than the people. See the note on Romans 11:5–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows in Isaiah"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As for Me,\" says the LORD, \"**this is My covenant with them: My Spirit who is upon you, and My words which I have put in your mouth, shall not depart** from your mouth… from this time and forevermore.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then chapter 60 opens: *Arise, shine, for your light has come.* See the note on Isaiah 60:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The armour underneath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 17, between the *no man* and this passage: *for **He put on righteousness as a breastplate, and a helmet of salvation on His head**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes the same pieces and puts them on the reader in Ephesians 6. See the note on Ephesians 6:14–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Isaiah, God wears it because nobody else was found."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the chapter is built.** A list of what the people cannot do, a note that no intercessor could be found, and then everything from that point on is God's own arm, God's own armour, and a Redeemer coming to Zion."}]}, "Isaiah 59:21": {"title": "\"My Spirit, and My words\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it is God stating the terms of an agreement in His own voice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the end of the chapter about a nation with nothing to offer — *your iniquities have separated you*, *we grope for the wall like the blind* — and after the line that turns it: *He saw that there was no man, and wondered that there was no intercessor; therefore **His own arm brought salvation**.* See the note on Isaiah 59:18–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Redeemer comes to Zion in verse 20. This is the next sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **this is My covenant with them**,\" says the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls it a covenant, and He is the only party who does anything in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is asked. There are no conditions in the verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My Spirit**, which rests on you, and **My words**, which I have put in your mouth.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are possessives — **My** Spirit, **My** words. Neither originates with the person holding them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Which I have put in your mouth** — the Hebrew is *which I have placed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah's call has the same picture: *then the LORD put forth His hand and touched my mouth, and the LORD said to me, \"Behold, **I have put My words in your mouth**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is promised about them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Will not leave your mouth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shall not depart out of your mouth*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three generations"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Nor the mouths of **your children**, nor the mouths of **their children**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three generations named, and then:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**From now on and forever**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other \"shall not depart from your mouth\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua 1: *this Book of the Law **shall not depart from your mouth**, but you shall meditate in it day and night.* See the note on Joshua 1:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There it is an instruction to Joshua. Here it is a promise from God about what He has put there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the next verse is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Arise, shine, for **your light has come**, and the glory of the LORD is risen upon you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 60 opens on it, with no break — the chapter division is a later addition. See the note on Isaiah 60:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The covenant of Spirit and words is followed immediately by the light rising."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul quotes the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 11, at the end of three chapters about Israel: *\"The Deliverer will come out of Zion, and He will turn away ungodliness from Jacob. **For this is My covenant with them, when I take away their sins**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes verse 20 and the opening of verse 21 together, and completes it from Jeremiah."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The covenant Jeremiah described"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will put My laws inside their hearts, and I will write them on their minds… and their sins and their lawless deeds I will remember no more.* See the note on Hebrews 10:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same word — covenant — and the same direction: God putting something inside a person."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is in the covenant.** Not obligations. A Spirit that rests and words that stay, both described as His, and a duration of three generations and then *forever*."}]}, "Isaiah 60:1–3": {"title": "Arise and shine — with a light that is not yours", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two imperatives, and then the reason for both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Arise. Shine. **Your light has come**, and the glory of the LORD rises upon you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The commands come first and the reason second, and the reason is not an instruction to generate anything. The light *has come*. The glory *rises upon* you — the verb is the one used of the sun coming up over a horizon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is being told to produce light. They are being told to get up, because something has risen on them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The darkness is not denied"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look, darkness covers the earth, thick darkness over the peoples.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second word is heavier than the first — a deep, enveloping dark. Isaiah puts it right between the command to shine and the promise of nations coming, and does not soften it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The contrast is the point: *but the LORD rises upon you*. Both things are true at once, and the light is not described as ending the darkness elsewhere. It is described as landing here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who ends up looking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Nations will come to your light, and kings to the brightness of your dawn.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The light attracts. That is the whole mechanism given — no campaign, no persuasion. Something is lit in a dark landscape, and people walk toward it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it lands in the New Testament.** Jesus tells a hillside crowd *you are the light of the world* and *a town built on a hill cannot be hidden* — the same picture of light in a dark place drawing eyes to it. Both passages put the attention that follows on someone else: nations coming to *your* light, and people giving glory to *your Father*. See the note on Matthew 5:13–16."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What arrives once the light is up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter does not stop at the dawn. The next two verses tell the city to look around at who is walking toward it — sons from far away, daughters carried close and safe, and then the wealth of the sea and the riches of nations streaming in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is worth holding on to. The light comes first and it is not the city's own; then people come to it; and only then does anything arrive that could be counted. Isaiah never presents the traffic as the point — it is the consequence of something having risen. See the note on Isaiah 60:4–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the darkness is not said to lift everywhere. *Darkness covers the earth, thick darkness over the peoples* stays true in the same breath as *the LORD rises upon you*. Whatever this chapter is promising, it is not a world with the lights back on. It is one lit place in a dark landscape, and a road toward it."}]}, "Isaiah 60:10–14": {"title": "Your gates will stand open", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A city under reconstruction is told its walls will be built by the people who knocked them down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The builders"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Foreigners will **rebuild your walls**, and **their kings will serve you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **benei-nekar** — sons of the stranger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people who broke the walls will build them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cyrus, who funded the return. Artaxerxes, who gave Nehemiah timber from the royal forest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In My anger **I struck you**, but in My favor **I have shown you mercy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **beqitzpi hikkiticha uvirtzoni richamticha**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both first person. The prophet does not attribute the destruction to Babylon and the mercy to God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gates"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your gates will **stand open always. They will never be shut, day or night**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yomam valaylah lo yissageru**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What a shut gate meant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Security. Every walled city closed its gates at sunset, and a gate left open at night meant either peace so complete it was unnecessary, or a city that had fallen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they are open"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that people may **bring you the wealth of the nations, with their kings led in procession**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chel goyim** — the strength, the wealth of nations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The traffic never stops, so the gates cannot."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Any nation that will not serve you will perish.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **charov yecheravu** — shall be utterly wasted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage does not soften the alternative."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timber"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The glory of Lebanon will come to you, the **pine, the fir, and the cypress together**, to beautify the place of My sanctuary.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three trees. The same forests that supplied Solomon's temple, floated down the coast as rafts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The footstool"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will make **the place of My feet glorious**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **meqom raglai**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The temple as God's footstool: *the LORD is in His holy temple*, and *let us worship at His footstool*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The children of those who oppressed you **will come and bow before you**. All who once despised you will **fall down at your feet**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will call you **the City of the LORD, Zion of the Holy One of Israel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A city whose name had been *forsaken* and *desolate* gets a new one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You shall no longer be termed Forsaken… but you shall be called **Hephzibah** — My delight is in her.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter started"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Arise, shine; for your light has come! And the glory of the LORD is risen upon you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For behold, **the darkness shall cover the earth, and deep darkness the people**; but the LORD will arise over you.* See the note on Isaiah 60:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Instead of bronze I will bring you gold… **you will call your walls Salvation and your gates Praise**.* See the note on Isaiah 60:15–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sun shall no longer be your light by day, nor for brightness shall the moon give light to you; **but the LORD will be to you an everlasting light**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the gates end up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Its gates shall not be shut at all by day (**there shall be no night there**).*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they shall bring the glory and the honour of the nations into it.* See the note on Revelation 21:24–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John takes the sentence almost word for word, and adds the reason: there is no night."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The gates.** Open day and night because the traffic never stops — said to a city whose walls were rubble, about builders who would be foreigners."}]}, "Isaiah 60:15–18": {"title": "Salvation and Praise", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four substitutions, and then two names."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Once you were **forsaken and hated, with no one passing through**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **azuvah usenu'ah** — abandoned and hated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ve'eyn over** — and no one passing through. A road nobody uses, which is what a ruined city is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she becomes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now I will make you **the everlasting pride, the joy of all generations**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lige'on olam** — an eternal majesty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The nursing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will **drink the milk of nations and nurse at royal breasts**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is startling and deliberate: the powers that plundered her now feed her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It picks up an earlier promise: *kings shall be your foster fathers, and their queens your nursing mothers*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The recognition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then you will know that **I, the LORD, am your Savior, your Redeemer, the Mighty One of Jacob**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three titles: **Moshia** — saviour. **Go'el** — kinsman-redeemer. See the note on Leviticus 25:23–28."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Avir Ya'aqov** — the Mighty One of Jacob, a title from Jacob's own blessing on Joseph."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four exchanges"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Instead of **bronze** I will bring you **gold**, and **silver** instead of **iron**, **bronze** instead of **wood**, and **iron** instead of **stone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each material replaced by one a grade above it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bronze becomes gold. Iron becomes silver. Wood becomes bronze. Stone becomes iron."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is destroyed; everything moves up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that describes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rebuilding. The materials of an ordinary construction upgraded at every level."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two officials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will make **peace your governor and righteousness your ruler**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **pequddatecha shalom venogesayik tzedaqah**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nogesim** — taskmasters, oppressors. The word used of the Egyptian slave-drivers: *taskmasters to afflict them with their burdens*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The office that was oppression becomes righteousness. The word is kept and the occupant changed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No more will **violence be heard in your land**, nor **ruin or destruction within your borders**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chamas** — violence. **Shod vashever** — devastation and breaking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will call your **walls Salvation** and your **gates Praise**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **veqarat yeshu'ah chomotayik ushe'arayik tehillah**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yeshu'ah** — salvation. The noun behind the name Jesus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tehillah** — praise. The root behind **Tehillim**, the Hebrew title of the Psalms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What walls and gates are for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Walls keep things out. Gates let things in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The city is told to name the barrier *Salvation* and the opening *Praise*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the gates were doing four verses earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your gates will stand open always. They will never be shut, day or night.* See the note on Isaiah 60:10–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sun shall no longer be your light by day, nor for brightness shall the moon give light to you; **but the LORD will be to you an everlasting light**, and your God your glory.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your sun shall no longer go down, nor shall your moon withdraw itself.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The city had no need of the sun or of the moon to shine in it, for **the glory of God illuminated it**. The Lamb is its light.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Its gates shall not be shut at all by day (there shall be no night there).* See the note on Revelation 21:22–23."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I, the LORD, will hasten it in its time.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **achishennah** — I will hurry it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The names.** The wall is called *Salvation* and the gate is called *Praise* — the barrier and the opening, each given a name that says what it is for."}]}, "Isaiah 60:4–5": {"title": "\"Your heart will race and swell\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The light has risen in the verses before this. Now Isaiah tells the city to look up and see who is walking toward it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The children first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your sons come from far away, and your daughters are carried close and safe.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before any mention of wealth, the returning crowd is family. Isaiah is writing to a people who had watched their children marched off into exile, and the first thing he describes coming back is them — the daughters carried, which is the detail a parent notices."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the wealth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wealth of the sea will be brought to you, and the riches of the nations will come streaming in.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for wealth is a broad word — strength, force, resources, army. It can mean a nation's substance in every sense. And *the sea* means overseas trade, which for Judah meant the whole Mediterranean world."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is of a city that had nothing becoming the destination everything arrives at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will see it, and your face will glow. Your heart will race and swell with joy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is physical: the heart *trembles and grows wide*. It is the body's response to news too large to take in — the same verb used elsewhere of fear, here turned to joy."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On reading this as a promise of transfer.** The passage has a long history of being applied to personal finances. What it actually describes is a restored city at the centre of a world that had scattered it, and Revelation picks it up that way — the kings of the earth bringing their glory into the new Jerusalem, with the gates never shut. It is worth reading first as the thing it says before it is applied to anything smaller."}]}, "Isaiah 61:1–3": {"title": "Where He stopped reading", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The passage Jesus read out in Nazareth, and the most important thing about it is where He closed the scroll."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit of the Lord GOD is on me, because the LORD **has anointed me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mashach** — the root of *Messiah*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five things"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**bring good news to the poor**", "**bind up the brokenhearted**", "**proclaim freedom for the captives**", "**release from darkness for the prisoners**", "**proclaim the year of the LORD's favour**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one is a verb of announcement or repair."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The year of favour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the year of the LORD's acceptance*, and it points at the jubilee — every fiftieth year, when debts were released, slaves went free and land returned to the family that lost it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*…**and the day of our God's vengeance**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two clauses sit in one sentence: a **year** of favour, and a **day** of vengeance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus did with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the synagogue at Nazareth He read from this scroll — and Luke records the exact point He stopped:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To proclaim the acceptable year of the LORD.\" **Then He closed the book**, gave it back to the attendant, and sat down.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He stops mid-sentence, at the comma."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He began to say to them, \"**Today this Scripture is fulfilled in your hearing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on Luke 4:16–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest of the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The day of vengeance is not deleted. It is left unread, on the same line, in the same breath of the prophet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah wrote them together. Jesus read the first half and said *today*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows in Isaiah"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To comfort all who mourn… to give them **a crown of beauty instead of ashes**, the **oil of joy instead of mourning**, a **garment of praise instead of a spirit of despair**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three exchanges, each a swap of one thing for another."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ashes were what mourners put on their heads. A crown goes where the ashes were."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will be called **oaks of righteousness**, a planting of the LORD, **for the display of His splendour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not saplings. Oaks — the slowest-growing, longest-standing tree in that country."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened next in Nazareth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He mentioned two Gentiles — the widow at Zarephath and Naaman the Syrian — and the congregation took Him to the brow of the hill to throw Him off. See the note on Luke 4:28–30."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the scroll would have looked like.** He would have unrolled it to find the place, read aloud standing, rolled it up, handed it back, and sat down — sitting being the posture for teaching. Luke records every step, and the sermon is one sentence long."}]}, "Isaiah 62:1–5": {"title": "Hephzibah and Beulah", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two names are taken away and two are given, and the new ones are the vocabulary of a wedding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal to be quiet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For Zion's sake **I will not keep silent**. For Jerusalem's sake **I will not stay quiet**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens with God declining to say nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Until what"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until her **righteousness shines out like the dawn**, her salvation like a blazing torch.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *shines out* is the ordinary verb for going out — as the sun goes out in the morning. Nothing is added to the dawn to make it bright; it arrives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who sees it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The nations will see **your righteousness**, and all the kings your glory.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her righteousness, on display to outsiders. The chapter never says she produced it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The new name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will be given **a new name**, a name **the mouth of the LORD will choose**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a name she picks. Naming rights belong to whoever gives it, and in Scripture the one who names is the one with authority — Adam over the animals, God over Abram, Jacob, Simon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crown"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will be **a crown of splendor in the hand of the LORD**, a royal diadem in the palm of your God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not on His head. In His hand — held up, turned in the light, shown."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two names removed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No longer will they call you **Forsaken**, no longer name your land **Desolate**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **Azubah** and **Shemamah**. Forsaken is what a wife is called when her husband has left; desolate is what land is called when nobody works it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two names given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will be called **Hephzibah**, My Delight Is in Her, and your land **Beulah**, Married.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chephtsi-bah** — *my delight is in her*. **Beulah** — *married, owned, possessed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah supplies the translations inside the verse, which he rarely does. He wants the meanings heard, not just the sounds."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hephzibah was also a real woman's name — Hezekiah's wife, the mother of Manasseh, in 2 Kings. Isaiah's readers knew it as an ordinary name a man gave his wife."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason attached"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the LORD takes delight in you**, and your land will be joined to Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name is given because the delight is already there. The naming reports a fact rather than creating one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wedding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As a young man marries a young woman, so will **your Builder** marry you. As a bridegroom rejoices over his bride, **so will your God rejoice over you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *rejoices* is **sus** — to be glad, to exult. It is the word for the groom's face at the wedding, applied to God, with the reader in the bride's place."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter is written over.** Jerusalem after the fall — walls down, the land unworked, the people gone. God renames it in the middle of the ruin, and the names He gives are about how He feels rather than how it looks."}]}, "Isaiah 64:6–7": {"title": "Filthy rags", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses of the bleakest self-assessment in the Old Testament, and they are a prayer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not God. This is Israel, praying — the chapter is one long petition that begins *oh, that You would rend the heavens! That You would come down!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence everyone quotes is something the people say about themselves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The uncleanness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All of us have become like one who is **unclean**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tame** — the ritual word. The condition that barred a person from the sanctuary and made whatever they touched unclean too. See the note on Leviticus 11:29–38."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The famous clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **all our righteous acts are like filthy rags**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tsidqotenu** — our righteousnesses, plural. Not our sins."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **beged iddim** — a garment of a menstruous woman. The Hebrew is more specific and more shocking than *filthy rags*, and it belongs to the same purity system: something that made a person unclean for a period."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The good works are what is being described, not the bad ones."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The withering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We all **wither like a leaf**, and **like the wind our sins sweep us away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two images in one line: the leaf that dries and the wind that takes it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same picture as Psalm 1, in reverse. There the righteous man's leaf does not wither. See the note on Psalms 1:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No one calls on Your name or **rouses himself to take hold of You**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mitorer** — stirs himself up, wakes himself. And **lehachaziq**, to grasp, seize, hold fast."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is even trying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **You have hidden Your face from us** and left us to waste away in the grip of our wrongs.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people say the failure to pray is a consequence of the hiding, not the cause of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah does not adjudicate. It is a prayer, and it says what people in that condition say."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Oh, that You would rend the heavens! That You would come down!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *rend* is **qara** — tear."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark answers it with one verb: *the heavens **split wide open***, at the Jordan. See the note on Mark 1:9–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word Mark uses again for the temple veil."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But now, O LORD, **You are our Father; we are the clay, and You our potter**, and all we are the work of Your hand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer moves from filthy rags to a potter's wheel in three verses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not be furious, O LORD, nor remember iniquity forever. Indeed, please look — **we all are Your people**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God answers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next chapter: *\"I was sought by those who did not ask for Me; I was found by those who did not seek Me. I said, 'Here I am, here I am,' to a nation that was not called by My name.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes it in Romans, applying it to the Gentiles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer says *no one calls on Your name*. The answer says *I was found by those who did not seek Me*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other righteousness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **be found in Him, not having my own righteousness**, which is from the law, but that which is through faith in Christ.* See the note on Philippians 3:9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had more religious credentials than anyone he knew, and used the word **skubala** — refuse — of the lot."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the book ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For as the new heavens and the new earth which I will make shall remain before Me,\" says the LORD, \"so shall your descendants and your name remain.\"* See the note on Isaiah 66:7–9."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is being called filthy.** Not the sins. *Our righteousnesses* — and the Hebrew names a specific kind of ritually unclean cloth."}]}, "Isaiah 65:21–23": {"title": "\"As the days of a tree\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Part of the picture Isaiah gives of the world made new, and every clause in it is about keeping what you made."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For behold, **I create new heavens and a new earth**; and the former shall not be remembered or come to mind.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John quotes the phrase at the end of Revelation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The building"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will **build houses and dwell in them**. They will **plant vineyards and eat their fruit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two ordinary activities, each with its outcome attached."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The negative version"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No longer will they **build for another to live in**, or **plant for another to eat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the promise is set against."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy's curses had named it: *you shall build a house and not dwell in it; you shall plant a vineyard and not gather its grapes*. See the note on Deuteronomy 28:58–68."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Israel's history had run that way — invasions, deportations, and other people occupying the houses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **as the days of a tree, so will be the days of My people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Olives and oaks live for centuries. Some of the olives on the Mount of Olives are held to be a thousand years old."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah picks the longest-lived thing that grows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The enjoyment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My chosen ones will **long enjoy the work of their hands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shall long enjoy* — literally *wear out*, in the sense of using something to the full."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The labour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will **not labor in vain**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **riq** — emptiness, for nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ecclesiastes' complaint answered: *what profit has a man from all his labour in which he toils under the sun?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The children"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Or **bear children doomed to misfortune**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *bring forth for trouble*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Infant mortality in the ancient world ran near half. The clause is not abstract."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For they will be **a people blessed by the LORD**, **they and their descendants with them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two generations named, as in the covenant language."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is in the same chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wolf and the lamb shall feed together, the lion shall eat straw like the ox… **they shall not hurt nor destroy in all My holy mountain**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *it shall come to pass that **before they call, I will answer**; and while they are still speaking, I will hear.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter opens with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I was sought by those who did not ask for Me; I was found by those who did not seek Me. I said, '**Here I am, here I am**,' to a nation that was not called by My name.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes it in Romans 10 of the Gentiles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that ends with houses and vineyards begins with God saying *here I am* to people who were not looking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the tree image does elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is like **a tree planted by the water**… **it never stops bearing fruit**.* See the note on Jeremiah 17:7–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Psalm 92: *those who are planted in the house of the LORD… **shall still bear fruit in old age**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the promise consists of.** Not spectacle. Living in the house you built, eating off the vine you planted, and lasting as long as a tree."}]}, "Isaiah 65:24–25": {"title": "\"Before they call, I will answer\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses about timing, and both of them run ahead of the request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Before they call, I will answer.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer precedes the asking. Not *as soon as*, and not *while* — before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**While they are still speaking, I will hear.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that He hears mid-sentence. There is no interval between the request leaving the mouth and its arriving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two lines together remove any lag at either end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same thing happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel, three chapters into his own vision: *\"At the beginning of your supplications the command went out, and I have come to tell you… for you are greatly beloved.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gabriel says the order was issued when Daniel started praying, and the travel time accounts for the rest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Abraham's servant at the well: *before he had finished speaking*, Rebekah came out. See the note on Genesis 24:12–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The animals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The wolf and the lamb will feed together**, and **the lion will eat straw like the ox**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The predator and the prey at the same trough, and the carnivore on a vegetarian diet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 11 has the same picture at more length — the wolf with the lamb, the leopard with the young goat, a child leading them, and an infant playing at a cobra's hole. See the note on Isaiah 11:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But dust will be the serpent's food.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything else in the picture is reconciled. One creature is not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 3: *on your belly you shall go, and **you shall eat dust** all the days of your life.* The sentence in the garden is the one thing this passage leaves standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**They will neither harm nor destroy** on all My holy mountain.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that they will do no evil and no ruin."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 65 opens with *I was sought by those who did not ask for Me; I was found by those who did not seek Me* — which Paul quotes in Romans 10 about the Gentiles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the middle of it: *behold, **I create new heavens and a new earth**, and the former shall not be remembered.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation 21 opens with the same sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is also promised in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They shall not build and another inhabit; they shall not plant and another eat… **they shall not labour in vain**, nor bring forth children for trouble.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same reversal Amos and Psalm 128 describe — a man eating what he grew. See the note on Amos 9:13–15."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is said about the length of a life.** *No more shall an infant from there live but a few days, nor an old man who has not fulfilled his days… **as the days of a tree, so shall be the days of My people**.*"}]}, "Isaiah 66:7–9": {"title": "A nation born in one day", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last chapter of Isaiah, and it opens with an impossible delivery."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of events"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before she was in labor, **she gave birth**. Before the pains came upon her, **she delivered a son**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Birth without labour. The one part of the process nobody skips."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in the sentence happens in the wrong order on purpose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who has **ever heard** of such a thing? Who has **ever seen** the like?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two, and both are rhetorical. Nobody has."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one that got answered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Can **a land be born in a single day**? Can **a nation be brought forth all at once**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On 14 May 1948 the British Mandate ended and Israel declared statehood, and a nation that had not existed for nineteen centuries existed by the end of the afternoon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah asks it as an absurdity. History filed a reply."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet no sooner was **Zion** in labor than she gave birth to her children.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zion is the mother in the sentence. The city, personified, delivering the people who live in her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah has used the image before — *sing, O barren, you who have not borne… for more are the children of the desolate*. See the note on Isaiah 54:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two questions God asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Shall I bring to the **moment of birth and not deliver**? says the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Shall I, **who deliver, close the womb**? says your God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A midwife who walks out at the last minute, and a midwife who shuts what she came to open."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are unthinkable, which is the point. He is arguing from His own character rather than from Israel's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two titles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Says **the LORD*** — and then *says **your God***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second is personal. He changes how He signs the sentence between the two questions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rejoice with Jerusalem, and be glad with her, all you who love her… **that you may feed and be satisfied** with the consolation of her bosom.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *as one whom his mother comforts, so I will comfort you; and **you shall be comforted in Jerusalem**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The nursing image runs on for four more verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For as **the new heavens and the new earth** which I will make shall remain before Me,\" says the LORD, \"**so shall your descendants and your name remain**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on Isaiah 65:21–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sixty-six chapters that opened with *hear, O heavens* close with new ones."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other use of the image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul: *the whole creation groans and labours with birth pangs together until now.* And Jesus, on the wars and earthquakes: *all these are the beginning of sorrows* — the Greek is **odin**, birth pains. See the note on Matthew 24:4–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The New Testament keeps the metaphor and keeps the labour. Isaiah 66 is the one place the labour is skipped."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The question that got an answer.** *Can a nation be brought forth all at once?* Isaiah asked it to make a point about the impossible, and a certificate was signed in Tel Aviv on a Friday afternoon."}]}, "Isaiah 7:14–16": {"title": "\"The virgin will conceive\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A sign given to a frightened king, and a name that carries more than the moment it was spoken into."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahaz, king of Judah, is facing two enemy kings and is about to buy Assyrian protection instead of trusting God. Isaiah offers him a sign — *ask for anything, as deep as the grave or as high as heaven* — and Ahaz refuses, in a show of false piety."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the sign is given anyway, over his refusal. *Therefore the Lord Himself will give you a sign.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **almah** — a young woman of marriageable age. It is used a handful of times in the Old Testament and never of a married woman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two centuries before Christ, Jewish scholars translating into Greek chose **parthenos**, which means virgin specifically. That is the reading Matthew quotes, and it was made by translators with no Christian argument to advance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She will call his name Immanuel* — with us, God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew quotes the verse and then translates the name for his readers, which is how he tells you what he thinks it means."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the Gospel closes on the same note: *I am with you always, to the very end of the age.* The name at the beginning and the promise at the end are the same claim."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The near horizon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before the boy knows enough to reject the wrong and choose the right, the land whose two kings you dread will be left empty.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sign had an immediate function — a timescale for Ahaz, measured in a child's early years. Prophecy in Isaiah often works on two horizons at once, and the near one does not cancel the far one."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Ahaz did with it.** He went to Assyria anyway, sent the temple silver as tribute, and got the protection he wanted along with everything that came after. The sign of Immanuel was given to a man who had already decided not to need it."}]}, "James 1:13": {"title": "\"Nobody gets to say, God is doing this to me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "James shuts a door, and he shuts it hard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is forbidden"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the pull toward evil comes, **nobody gets to say, \"God is doing this to me.\"**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *let no one say, I am tempted by God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not correcting a misunderstanding gently. He rules the sentence out of the believer's mouth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it needed saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven verses earlier he told them to count it all joy when they land in trouble — using the noun **peirasmos**. See the note on James 1:2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here he uses the verb from the same root and says God does not do it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reader who has just been told that trials are worth something might reasonably ask whether God sends the pull toward evil as well. James answers before it is asked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two things, one word family"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Greek uses **peirazo** for both testing and enticing. English separates them into *trial* and *temptation*; the Greek does not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James separates them by argument instead. Circumstances that test faith come, and they work endurance. Solicitation to evil is a different thing with a different source, and he names the source in the next verse. See the note on James 1:14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Evil **has no hold on God whatsoever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apeirastos** — untemptable, incapable of being solicited by evil. The word appears once in the New Testament, here, and is rare in all Greek."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is nothing in Him for it to fasten to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus says the same about the ruler of this world: *he has nothing in Me.* See the note on John 14:28–31."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He pulls no one toward it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two claims: nothing pulls Him, and He pulls nobody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James closes it from both sides."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What this rules out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oldest instinct in Scripture is to relocate the blame. Adam: *the woman whom You gave to be with me.* The pointing runs past Eve and lands on God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James is writing to people under real pressure — scattered, poor, dragged into court by the rich — and the sentence he takes away from them is the one that would have been easiest to say."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say the pressure is imaginary, or that suffering has no purpose, or that God is absent from it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The claim is narrow and total: God is not the one doing the enticing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows the whole paragraph"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every good gift and every perfect gift is from above, and comes down from the Father of lights, **with whom there is no variation or shadow of turning**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verses later, and it is his closing argument. The one who cannot be pulled toward evil is also the one who does not change."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The shape of the paragraph.** James removes one explanation, supplies the true one in the next verse, and then says where the good things come from instead."}]}, "James 1:14": {"title": "\"Here is what actually happens\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Having ruled out one explanation, James gives the real one in a single sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Here is what actually happens.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *but each one is tempted…* — and the **each one** matters. It is not a description of humanity in general but of one person at a time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The previous verse removed God from the account. See the note on James 1:13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your **own** craving.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epithumia** — desire, longing, appetite. The word is not automatically negative; Jesus uses it of Himself: *with fervent desire I have desired to eat this Passover with you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "What makes it lethal here is the possessive. **His own.**"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hauls you off and **baits you in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are hunting and fishing words, and James chose them as a pair:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Exelko** — to drag out, to draw away. It is what you do to a fish, pulling it out of cover.", "**Deleazo** — from **delear**, bait. To lure with something on a hook."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is deliberate. First drawn out of position, then baited."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No devil, and no God. James is at his most stripped-down here — he mentions the devil elsewhere in the letter (*resist the devil and he will flee from you*), but not in this diagnosis."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mechanism he describes needs no external agent at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then, when desire has conceived, **it gives birth to sin**; and sin, when it is full-grown, **brings forth death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He switches metaphors mid-paragraph, from fishing to childbirth, and lays out a sequence: desire, conception, sin, maturity, death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the chain is instantaneous. Every stage takes time, and each one is the parent of the next."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The point of describing it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James is not writing a psychology. He is writing so that the thing can be recognised at the first stage rather than the last."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse after the sequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not be deceived, my beloved brethren.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, immediately, the good gifts and the Father of lights."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts the two accounts side by side: what comes up out of a man, and what comes down from above."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word he uses again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Epithumia** turns up in the letter's fourth chapter: *you lust and do not have… you ask and do not receive, because you ask amiss, that you may spend it on your pleasures.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same root. James's diagnosis is consistent from one end of the letter to the other."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is honest about it.** The bait works because it is wanted. James does not pretend otherwise — he says the pull comes from the person's own appetite, and calls it theirs."}]}, "James 1:17": {"title": "\"He never swings around to throw a shadow\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "James reaches for astronomy to say something about God's character, and the metaphor is doing precise work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim about gifts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Anything good that ever came into your hands, anything that reached you whole and unspoiled, came down from up there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in the Greek — every good *giving* and every perfect *gift*. The act and the thing given. Both are traced to one source."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The direction is stated: **down from above**, from the Father of lights."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why lights"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From the One who made every light burning in the sky.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heavenly bodies were the ancient world's picture of reliability — and also of variation. The sun moves through the year, the moon waxes and wanes, constellations rise and set. Everything in the sky changes by turning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James names God as the maker of all of them and then says the thing that separates Him from what He made."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He never dims, never shifts, never swings around to throw a shadow He was not throwing before.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**no variation** — the astronomical word for change or alteration", "**no shadow cast by turning** — the word is *trope*, the root of *tropic*, used of the solstice, the point where the sun turns back"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything that gives light in the sky produces shadow as it moves. God does not turn, so there is no side of Him that is dark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why James says it here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look at the verse before: *do not be deceived, my beloved brothers.* He has just been saying that God tempts nobody, and that people are drawn away by their own desire."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the correction to a suspicion — that God might be behind the trouble, or might have a colder side that shows sometimes. James says the light is unvarying, and everything good that ever reached you came from it."}]}, "James 1:2": {"title": "\"When you land in the middle of trouble\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first instruction in the letter, and it is about arithmetic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "***Count it** nothing but joy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hegeomai** — to reckon, to consider, to lead the mind to a conclusion. It is an accounting word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James does not say *feel* joy. He says work it out and put it in that column."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Nothing but\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Count it **nothing but joy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *all joy* — **pasan charan**. Not partly, not on balance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The landing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When you **land in the middle of trouble**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **peripipto** — to fall around, to fall among. It is the word Luke uses of the man on the Jericho road who *fell among thieves*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You do not walk into it deliberately. It happens on the way somewhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"When\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *if*. James assumes it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Every kind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every kind of it there is.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **poikilos** — many-coloured, variegated, of every shade."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter uses the identical word two verses apart in his letter: *though now for a little while you have been grieved by **various** trials*, and then *stewards of the **manifold** grace of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same adjective for the trouble and for the grace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for trouble"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Peirasmos** — a trial, a test, an examination. It is also the word in *lead us not into temptation*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James uses it here about circumstances that come at a person from outside, and eleven verses later he uses the related verb about being drawn toward evil — and says flatly that God does not do that. See the notes on James 1:13 and James 1:14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same word family, two different things, deliberately separated inside one chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he can say it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because of the next verse, which starts *you know why*. See the note on James 1:3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The joy is not asked for in the abstract. It is the conclusion of an argument James is about to make."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is writing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*James, a bondservant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ* — the Lord's brother, who did not believe in Him during His life, and who led the Jerusalem church through the persecutions in Acts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is writing to *the twelve tribes which are scattered abroad* — refugees."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not being asked for.** Not that the trouble be enjoyed, and not that it be called good. James asks for a calculation, and gives the working in the next sentence."}]}, "James 1:21": {"title": "What \"saved\" actually covers", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Our text says the message has power to save your souls. Both of those words — saved and souls — are wider in Greek than they have become in English."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same word, in this same letter, means healed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is sozo. James uses it again at the end of his letter: the prayer offered in faith will sozo the sick man. Same verb. There it plainly means made well — a man getting up off his bed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So inside one short letter James uses this one word for the rescue of a person's whole life, and for a sick man being restored. That is not a stretch or a clever reading. It is simply how he uses it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "It is a rescue word, not a religious word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sozo means to bring someone safely through. It is used of a drowning man being pulled out, of a ship that survives a storm, of someone brought out of danger in one piece. Whatever the threat is, sozo is coming through it intact."}, {"k": "p", "t": "English saved has narrowed to mean one thing: not going to hell. The Greek word never narrowed like that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And \"souls\" is wider too"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is psyche. In English, soul makes us think only of the invisible part that outlives the body. In Greek it means the life, the self, the whole person — you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "When Jesus asked what it profits a man to gain the whole world and lose his psyche, He was not talking about one compartment of a person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So save your souls is not keep the spiritual part of you out of hell. It is nearer to: rescue the whole of you, and bring you through in one piece."}, {"k": "note", "t": "Do not overreach with this. Sozo genuinely does include eternal salvation — that is not being denied here, and this is not a case of everyone having had the word wrong. The claim that holds is that saved is WIDER than we usually hear it, not that it means something other than we thought. And the width of a word does not by itself promise a particular outcome in a particular case. What it tells you is the size of what God is dealing with: not a compartment of you, but you."}]}, "James 1:3": {"title": "\"You know why\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The reason attached to the instruction in the verse before it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"You know\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You know why.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ginoskontes** — knowing, being aware. James assumes they already have this; he is reminding, not informing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The joy of verse 2 rests on a piece of knowledge, and James supplies it immediately. See the note on James 1:2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The testing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Testing your faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dokimion** — the noun for the process of assaying metal. The related adjective **dokimos** means *proved genuine*, and it is stamped on coinage that has been tested and found to be what it claims."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is about verification rather than damage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it produces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Works **endurance** into you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hupomone** — from *hupo*, under, and *meno*, to remain. To stay under."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not passive. Older versions render it *patience*, which now sounds like waiting quietly; the Greek is the quality of a soldier holding a position, or a load-bearing wall under weight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction of the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Faith is what is tested. Endurance is what comes out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James does not say the trouble produces the faith. The faith was already there — that is what makes the testing possible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Peter says the same"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The genuineness of your faith, **being much more precious than gold that perishes, though it is tested by fire**, may be found to praise, honour and glory.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter uses the same word, **dokimion**, and the same picture of the refiner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gold goes into the fire because it is gold. The fire does not make it gold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We also glory in tribulations, knowing that **tribulation produces perseverance; and perseverance, character; and character, hope**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same word for perseverance, and the same *knowing* at the front of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three writers, one chain of reasoning, and all three put a knowing before the rejoicing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let endurance have its full effect, that you may be **mature and complete, lacking nothing**.* See the note on James 1:4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, immediately: *if any of you lacks wisdom, let him ask of God, who gives to all liberally and without reproach.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James says *lacking nothing*, and the very next sentence tells them what to do if they lack something."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the verse does.** It answers the obvious objection to verse 2. James does not ask for joy and leave it there — he says *you know why* and then says why."}]}, "James 1:4": {"title": "\"No piece of you left unfinished\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, and the two words at the end of it are not about moral perfection."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will come out grown up and whole, with no piece of you left unfinished.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**teleios** — brought to its end, complete, mature. Used of a full-grown adult as against a child, and of a thing that has reached what it was for.", "**holokleros** — literally *whole-lot*, having all its parts. It was used of a body with nothing missing, and of a stone fit for building because it had no defect."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither word means sinless. Both mean **nothing lacking**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James then says it a third way in case it was missed: *lacking in nothing*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What produces it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So let that endurance run all the way to the finish.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before names the mechanism — testing produces endurance — and this one says: now let the endurance finish its work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction is not to generate anything. It is to let something already underway complete rather than cutting it short."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being described"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a person who has suffered and become tougher. A person with no piece missing — the parts that only get built under pressure now present."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why James opens the letter by telling scattered, harassed believers to count trials as joy. Not because the trial is good, but because of what it is in the middle of making."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The next verse.** *If any of you is short on wisdom, ask God.* It follows directly, and the connection is easy to miss: a person in the middle of an unfinished process, told to let it run, is exactly a person who will not understand what is happening. So the immediate provision is wisdom, given generously and without being made to feel small for asking. See the note on James 1:5."}]}, "James 1:5": {"title": "The two words that take the sting out of asking", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One short verse, and two Greek words in it are doing something the English has to work hard to carry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"With both hands\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is **haplos**. It means singly, simply, without folds — and by extension, without a hidden second motive. It is the opposite of giving with one hand while keeping an account with the other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the promise is not merely that God gives generously in quantity. It is that He gives *straightforwardly*: no strings running back, no ledger being kept, no part of Him withholding while another part hands something over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He never once makes you feel small for asking\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second phrase is **me oneidizontos** — not reproaching. *Oneidizo* is the word for upbraiding somebody, throwing something in their face, shaming them with it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James puts it in the sentence because he knows exactly what stops people asking. It is not doubt that God can. It is the suspicion that the asking will be remembered — that the answer will arrive with a comment attached about why you needed it, or how often you have needed it lately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He rules that out in four words."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this sits in his letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It comes immediately after James has told them to count it joy when they fall into various trials, and that endurance has a work to finish."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the wisdom on offer is not academic. It is for people in the middle of something they do not understand, who need to know what to do next. That is when a person is least confident of a welcome, and it is precisely there that James puts the promise about not being shamed for asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no qualification about the asker's state. Not *if any of you is worthy*, not *if you have been faithful*. The condition is **short on wisdom** — the lack itself is the qualification."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the outcome is stated with equal plainness: *it will be put right into your hands*. Not considered. Given."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the verses that follow.** James goes on to talk about asking in faith and not being double-minded, and that is sometimes used to load the promise back up with conditions. Read what he actually describes: a person in two minds about whether to ask God at all, pulled one way and then the other. He is not setting a standard of confidence to reach. He is saying: decide who you are asking, and then ask him."}]}, "James 2:10": {"title": "Trip over one", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence about how the law works as a unit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep **every last piece of the law** and **trip over one**, and you are **answerable for the whole of it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *whoever keeps the whole law and stumbles in one point is guilty of all*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ptaio** — to stumble, to trip. Not to abandon or to defy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James picks the mildest available word. A stumble is what happens to somebody who is walking in the right direction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Enochos** — liable, answerable, under charge. It is a courtroom word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *has broken all of them*. Answerable for all of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it works that way"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse after gives the reason: *for **He who said**, \"Do not commit adultery,\" **also said**, \"Do not murder.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law is one lawgiver's word, not a list of separate requirements. Offending at one point is not failing a subsection; it is contradicting the person who spoke all of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James makes the argument about the speaker rather than the statute."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is arguing against"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The context is favouritism. The chapter opens with a rich man in fine clothes and a poor man in filthy ones walking into the same meeting, and the rich man being given the good seat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James calls that a breach: *if you show partiality, you commit sin, and are convicted by the law as transgressors.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody was evidently treating it as a minor lapse, and this verse is the answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The royal law"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you really fulfil **the royal law** according to the Scripture, \"You shall love your neighbour as yourself,\" you do well.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls it royal — **basilikon**, the king's law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul says the same thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For as many as are of the works of the law are under the curse; for it is written, \"Cursed is everyone who does not continue in **all things** which are written in the book of the law, to do them.\"* See the note on Galatians 3:10–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both writers land on the same word. Paul quotes Deuteronomy's *all things*; James says *guilty of all*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are usually set against each other on faith and works, and on this point they are identical."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What James says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So speak and so do **as those who will be judged by the law of liberty**. For **judgment is without mercy to the one who has shown no mercy**. **Mercy triumphs over judgment**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The paragraph that contains the strictest statement of the law's unity ends on that sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *triumphs over* is **katakauchatai** — boasts against, exults over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chain image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old illustration is a chain over a pit: it does not matter which link goes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James's own argument is not about links. It is about one voice."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How light the offence is in the sentence.** A stumble, at one point, by somebody keeping the rest. James chooses the smallest possible failure and attaches the largest possible liability to it."}]}, "James 2:17": {"title": "Does James contradict Paul?", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the verse people set against Paul. Paul says a person is put right with God through faith and not by works. James says faith without works is dead. Here are seven reasons they are not fighting each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "1. They are answering two different questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is answering: how does a guilty person get right with God? James is answering: how can you tell a living faith from a dead one? Same words, different question. Neither of them has to lose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "2. They are arguing with two different people"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's opponent thinks doing good earns you standing with God. James's opponent thinks saying you believe is enough and nothing needs to follow. Neither man is attacking what the other one taught."}, {"k": "h", "t": "3. James tells you exactly which faith he is rejecting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verses back, the man SAYS he has faith. It is a claim, not a life. And two verses on, the belief being tested is simply believing God exists — which, as James points out, the demons manage too, and it makes them shake."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not attacking trust in God. He is attacking a counterfeit that he carefully describes first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "4. The little word \"that\" — the strongest point of all"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Greek there is a small word, the, which James attaches to faith every single time he points at the counterfeit: 2:14, this verse, 2:20, 2:22 and 2:26. Your text shows it — that faith, this faith. He means the one he has just described."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And he drops that little word in exactly one place: 2:24, where he stops describing the fake and states the general rule."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So every time he means the counterfeit, the word is there. The one time he states the principle, it is gone. That is grammar, not opinion — it sits in the text whether anyone likes it or not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "5. Abraham's dates settle the order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham believed God and was counted righteous in Genesis 15. He put Isaac on the altar in Genesis 22 — chapters later, years later. James quotes the earlier moment and says the altar filled it full. The standing came first; the deed filled it out. There is more on this at 2:21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "6. Chapter 1 had already settled it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before this argument even begins James has said four things in order: God acted first and nobody talked Him into it (1:18); the message is already planted in you, and it is the message that saves you (1:21); now carry it out (1:22); and you are blessed IN the doing, not paid for it (1:25)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gift comes before the work — twenty verses before anyone mentions works."}, {"k": "h", "t": "7. The whole passage is about showing, not paying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Show me. I will show you. Do you need showing. Look at what happened. You see it now. Over and over. James is asking for evidence that the faith is alive, not for payment to make it count."}, {"k": "note", "t": "One thing not to claim: do not say Paul and James use different words. They use the same ones — faith, works, justified, righteousness, the same Abraham, and the same Old Testament verse about him. That is precisely why this passage has been argued over for centuries. The claim that holds up is not that they used different words, but that they used the same words to answer different questions."}]}, "James 2:21": {"title": "Shown to be right, or made righteous?", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This verse has been argued over for five hundred years. Here is why our text reads shown to be right rather than justified or made righteous."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word itself means both"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word is dikaioo. It carries two senses, and both are ordinary:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["made right — a verdict, the way a judge declares it", "shown to be right — proof of something that was already true"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither side can win this by pointing at the word. It will not choose between them. So the question has to be settled from the passage, not the dictionary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "James answers it with dates"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things happened in Abraham's life, a long way apart. In Genesis 15 he believed God, and God counted him righteous. In Genesis 22 he put Isaac on the altar. Those are chapters apart, and years apart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Now the key move: in the next verse but one, James quotes the earlier moment — the believing — and says the altar filled it full."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So James himself puts the righteousness first and the altar second. If Abraham had been made righteous at the altar, the verse James quotes would be describing something that had not happened yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "A picture for it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man passes his exams and is licensed as a doctor. Years later he saves a life in an emergency. Did the emergency make him a doctor? No. He already was one. The emergency showed it, in public, where everyone could see."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is Abraham. Righteous by believing, back in Genesis 15. The altar showed it, in Genesis 22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Three reasons this rendering stands"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["Shown to be right is a real, ordinary meaning of the word — nothing invented.", "James never says in front of God or in front of people. Those words are simply not in the chapter. Paul does add that kind of phrase when he writes about Abraham; James does not, and our text stays as quiet as he does.", "The whole passage runs on showing: show me, I will show you, do you need showing, look at what happened, you see it now."]}, {"k": "h", "t": "What should not be claimed"}, {"k": "note", "t": "It would be too strong to say the Greek means shown and not made. The word genuinely carries both, and that claim can be knocked down in a moment — taking the good arguments with it. The honest statement is this: the word can mean either; we read it as shown because James's argument runs on showing, because he names no courtroom, and above all because he dates the righteousness to Genesis 15 and calls the altar its filling-full. That is a reading of the passage, not a fact about the word."}]}, "James 2:25": {"title": "Why James picks Rahab second", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "James has just used Abraham. Then he reaches for the least likely person in the Old Testament to put beside him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pairing is deliberate"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Abraham** — the father of the nation, the man the whole argument about faith is usually conducted through", "**Rahab** — a Canaanite, a prostitute, an outsider with no covenant standing at all"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "If his point were that respectable people prove their faith by respectable behaviour, the second example destroys it. He chooses the two most distant points on the map and says the same thing happened in both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What her \"work\" was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She took the messengers in and sent them out another road.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the entire deed. She hid two men and pointed them at the hills. It is not charitable giving or moral improvement — it is one decision, made at real risk, that showed which side she had joined."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Shown to be right\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is the same one under discussion throughout the chapter, and its range matters: it can mean *made* right, and it can mean *shown* to be right. Read the question James is asking — how can you tell a living faith from a dead one — and the second sense is the one carrying the sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody made Rahab righteous by hiding spies. What she did made visible where she already stood."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**See also.** The note on James 2:17 works through why this is not a contradiction of Paul, and the note on Hebrews 11:30–31 shows the same episode being used as an example of *faith* by a different writer. Two arguments, one woman, one night."}]}, "James 3:13–16": {"title": "Let his life do the talking", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A test for wisdom that does not involve saying anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Somebody among you **figures he's wise**, that he's got some understanding?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sophos kai epistemon** — wise and understanding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Epistemon** is the rarer word: expert, skilled, knowledgeable. It appears once in the New Testament, here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the question comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James has just spent twelve verses on the tongue — the bit in the horse's mouth, the rudder, the spark that sets a forest going, the spring that cannot give both sweet water and bitter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With it we bless our God and Father, and with it we curse men.* See the note on James 3:8–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens *let not many of you become teachers*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Having established what talking is worth, he asks who is wise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The test"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **let his life do the talking** — the things he actually does, **done gently**, because that is what wisdom does to a man.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **deixato ek tes kales anastrophes ta erga autou** — let him show, out of his good conduct, his works."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one thing done well. A manner of life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**En prauteti sophias** — in the meekness of wisdom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Prautes** — meekness, gentleness. In Greek usage it described a strong animal under control: a horse broken to the bridle. It is strength that has been taught restraint, not weakness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word Jesus uses of Himself: *I am gentle and lowly in heart*. See the note on Matthew 11:28–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The alternative"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But if what is really sitting in your chest is **a sour ache over what other people have**, and **a hunger to win**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **zelon pikron** — bitter zeal, and **eritheian** — selfish ambition, party spirit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Eritheia** originally described working for hire; it came to mean canvassing for office, the kind of ambition that builds a faction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The location"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sitting in your chest.* — **en te kardia humon**, in your heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both things are internal. Neither has to be said out loud to be there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not stand there **congratulating yourself. You are lying**, and the truth is what you are lying about.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me katakauchasthe kai pseudesthe kata tes aletheias** — do not boast against and lie against the truth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The origin"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That kind of wisdom **did not come down from anywhere**. It comes **up off the ground, out of the appetites, out of hell**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three adjectives in Greek: **epigeios** — earthly. **Psuchikos** — of the soul, natural, unspiritual. **Daimoniodes** — demonic."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A descending scale, and James does not soften the last one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the false wisdom produces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because wherever people are eaten with envy and out to win, **things fall apart**, and **every rotten thing there is finds somewhere to live**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **akatastasia** — disorder, instability, anarchy. And **pan phaulon pragma** — every worthless deed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image in the second clause is of vacancy: disorder makes room, and things move in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the true wisdom produces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the wisdom that is from above is first pure, then **peaceable, gentle, willing to yield**, full of mercy and good fruits, without partiality and without hypocrisy.* See the note on James 3:17–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight qualities, and the first six are about how a person is with other people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where do wars and fights come from among you? **Do they not come from your desires for pleasure that war in your members?***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 4 opens by tracing conflict to the same interior place."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The test.** Not what a man says about wisdom — what his life is like, and specifically whether the strength in it has been broken to the bridle."}]}, "James 3:17–18": {"title": "How to tell which wisdom you are hearing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "James has just described a wisdom that is earthly and produces disorder. Here is the other kind, and he gives it a checklist."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wisdom that comes down from above is **clean, first of all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says *first* deliberately — this one leads. The word is about being pure, unmixed, without a hidden agenda."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything else on the list is relational, and the purity has to come first because the rest can be counterfeited by someone who simply wants to be liked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the list"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**it makes peace**", "**it goes easy on people** — gentle, considerate, the word for not insisting on the letter of your rights", "**it will hear you out and give ground** — willing to be persuaded, open to reason", "**packed with mercy and with good things that actually grow**", "**it does not play favourites and it never puts on a face**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read it as a test and it becomes very practical. Not *is this clever*, but: does it make peace, does it go easy, will it listen, does it produce anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wisdom that is technically correct and leaves wreckage behind it fails the test James sets."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The harvest line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People who make peace plant their seed in peace, and what comes up out of that ground is a life put right.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is agricultural and the sequence matters — the peace is both the soil and the seed. Righteousness is the crop, not the method."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he is contrasting it with.** The verses just above describe wisdom that is earthly, unspiritual, and demonic, marked by bitter envy and selfish ambition — and James says where it shows: *disorder and every evil practice.* Both wisdoms are recognised by what grows out of them, which is the only test he offers for either."}]}, "James 3:6–8": {"title": "\"Nobody has ever broken in the tongue\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "James has just used two pictures — a bit in a horse's mouth, a rudder on a ship — about small things steering large ones. Then he stops being gentle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fire"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the tongue is that fire… touching off the whole running wheel of your life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase behind *running wheel* is odd in Greek and has been discussed for centuries — something like the wheel of existence, the whole course of a life turning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is of a spark at the axle. Not damage in one place; damage that travels with the wheel wherever it goes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim about taming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People have broken in every sort of creature there is… but nobody has ever broken in the tongue.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James surveys the whole animal kingdom — the four categories from Genesis — and says humanity has managed all of them and not this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It will not be held still.* That is a statement about human capacity, and it is absolute. Nobody. Ever."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he says something so bleak"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because the chapter opens by warning that not many should become teachers, since they will be judged more strictly. James is writing to people who talk for a living, and he is refusing to pretend it can be mastered by effort."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The honest reading is that this is a diagnosis with no cure offered in the paragraph — which is deliberate. If nobody can tame it, self-improvement is not the answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the letter goes for the answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wisdom that comes down from above is clean, first of all. Then: it makes peace, it goes easy on people, it will hear you out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight verses later, and the solution is something that comes **down from above** rather than something worked up. See the note on James 3:17–18."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul says about the same problem.** *If by the Spirit you go on putting the body's doings to death, you will live* — continuous, and by the Spirit, not by resolve. The two writers agree about the diagnosis and about where the remedy comes from. See the note on Romans 8:12–14."}]}, "James 4:1–3": {"title": "Where the fighting comes from", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He asks where conflict comes from and answers his own question in three words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where do all these **fights and running feuds** among you come from?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **polemoi kai machai** — wars and battles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Military vocabulary, applied to a congregation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From **inside you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ek ton hedonon humon** — out of your pleasures."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hedone** — pleasure, the root of *hedonism*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From **the wanting that never shuts up, camped in your own body, at war**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ton strateuomenon en tois melesin humon** — campaigning in your members."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Strateuomai** — to serve as a soldier, to wage a campaign. Not a skirmish; an occupation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same picture: *I see another law in my members, warring against the law of my mind*. See the note on Romans 7:22–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You want, and you do not get it, so you kill. You crave, and it stays out of reach, so you brawl and you go to war.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epithumeite kai ouk echete, phoneuete** — you desire and do not have, you murder."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word **phoneuete** has troubled readers since the earliest manuscripts; some scribes changed it. James is writing to congregations, and he does not soften it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Want, fail to get, escalate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The desire is not the problem in itself. The route taken when it is not met is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **you still do not have it — because you never asked**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ouk echete dia to me aiteisthai humas**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every method has been tried except the one that was available."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Or you ask and nothing comes, and that is because of **what you are asking for**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kakos aiteisthe** — you ask badly, wrongly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You only want to **blow it on yourself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hina en tais hedonais humon dapanesete** — that you may spend it on your pleasures."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dapanao** — to spend, to squander. The verb used of the prodigal: *he wasted his possessions with prodigal living*, and of the woman who *had spent all her livelihood on physicians*. See the note on Luke 8:43–48."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same **hedone** as verse 1. The thing that starts the war is the thing the prayer is aimed at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That asking for yourself is wrong."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter says *if any of you lacks wisdom, let him ask of God, who gives to all liberally*. And *you do not have because you do not ask* is a rebuke for not asking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The problem named is the spending, not the asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You adulterous men, you adulterous women — has it not dawned on you yet? **Being this world's friend means being God's enemy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **He gives more grace**.* See the note on James 4:4–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The paragraph that names murder and war among believers turns, in three verses, to *He gives more grace*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore **submit to God. Resist the devil and he will flee from you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Draw near to God and He will draw near to you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Humble yourselves in the sight of the Lord, and **He will lift you up**.* See the note on James 4:7–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the earlier chapter said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But if you have bitter envy and self-seeking in your hearts, do not boast and lie against the truth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For where envy and self-seeking exist, **confusion and every evil thing are there**.* See the note on James 3:13–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same diagnosis, one chapter earlier, and the same interior location."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line.** *You still do not have it — because you never asked.* Every escalation has been tried, and the one thing available was not."}]}, "James 4:4–6": {"title": "He gives more grace", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The harshest address in the letter, and four verses later the sentence turns."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You **adulterous men, you adulterous women** — has it not dawned on you yet?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **moichalides** — adulteresses. Feminine plural."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why that word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not sexual accusation. It is the prophets' vocabulary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel is the wife, God is the husband, and idolatry is adultery. Hosea's whole marriage is built on it, and Jeremiah and Ezekiel use it at length."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James is using a covenant category, not making a moral charge about anyone's conduct."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Being **this world's friend means being God's enemy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **philia tou kosmou echthra tou Theou estin** — friendship of the world is enmity with God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second statement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sign up as a friend of this world and **you have put yourself in the field against God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kathistatai** — is constituted, is appointed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The construction makes it a status rather than a feeling. The person does not have to intend the opposition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What *world* means here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kosmos** — the ordered system, human society organised without reference to God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the created earth. James has already said *every good gift and every perfect gift is from above*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation nobody can find"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Or do you think Scripture speaks for nothing when it says **the spirit God settled inside us yearns, and yearns jealously**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pros phthonon epipothei to pneuma**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No Old Testament verse matches. James may be summarising the whole prophetic theme of God's jealousy rather than quoting a text."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ambiguity"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek can be read two ways: the spirit He put in us yearns jealously *for Him*, or God yearns jealously *over* the spirit He put in us."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Translators divide. Both readings say the relationship is not casual."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **He gives more grace than that**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meizona de didosin charin** — but He gives greater grace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words in Greek, after twelve verses of murder, war, envy and adultery."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it answers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything above it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The paragraph has established that the reader is on the wrong side, and the next clause does not qualify or narrow."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proverb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which is why it says: **God squares up against the proud, and pours grace over the lowly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs 3:34, in the Septuagint's wording."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **antitassetai** — sets Himself in battle array against. A military verb, the same root as the one in Romans 13 for setting oneself against an authority."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tapeinois** — the low, the humble, the ones with nothing to bring."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who else quotes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Likewise you younger people, submit yourselves to your elders… for \"**God resists the proud, and gives grace to the humble**.\"* — Peter, in his letter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two apostles quote the same verse in the same form."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore **submit to God. Resist the devil and he will flee from you. Draw near to God and He will draw near to you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Humble yourselves in the sight of the Lord, and **He will lift you up**.* See the note on James 4:7–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten imperatives in four verses, and the last one is passive: He does the lifting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the grace goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Toward the lowly, which is the one direction water runs by itself."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The three words.** **Meizona didosin charin** — He gives greater grace. Placed immediately after the sentence that says the reader is in the field against Him."}]}, "James 5:1–3": {"title": "\"The last days are what you piled it up for\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The bluntest paragraph in the New Testament, and it is addressed to a specific group."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being spoken to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now you. **The rich.***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the church. James is addressing a class of people from outside it — the next verse names what they did: *you kept back by fraud the wages of the labourers who mowed your fields.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Unpaid wages. That is the charge, and it is the same charge Egypt had four hundred years of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three corrosions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your **wealth has gone rotten**. Your good clothes are **full of moth holes**. Your gold and your silver have **gone green with corrosion**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three kinds of stored wealth in that world — grain, garments, and metal — and each is described decaying in its own way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gold does not corrode. James says it anyway; the point is not metallurgy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The corrosion as a witness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That corrosion is going to **stand up and testify against you**, and eat its way through your flesh like fire.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rust becomes a witness in court. The evidence against them is produced by the hoard itself, sitting in the dark, doing what unused things do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You piled the stuff up — and **the last days are what you piled it up for**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *you have heaped up treasure in the last days*, and it carries both senses: they did the heaping in the last days, and the last days are what the heap is for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pile has a destination that is not theirs. Ecclesiastes said the same: *to the one who misses the mark, He gives the task of gathering and piling up, only to hand it over to the one who pleases God.* See the note on Ecclesiastes 2:24–26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What James tells the believers to do about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing aggressive. Four verses later:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be patient, brethren, until the coming of the Lord. See how the farmer waits for the precious fruit of the earth, **waiting patiently for it until it receives the early and latter rain**. You also be patient. **Establish your hearts**, for the coming of the Lord is at hand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A farmer, two rains, and a heart established. No scheming, no grasping, no hurry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs: *the stingy man hurries after wealth and never knows that want is coming for him.* See the note on Proverbs 28:21–28."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wages that cried out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The cries of the reapers have reached the ears of the Lord of Sabaoth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sabaoth** — the LORD of armies. James reaches for the military title in a paragraph about farm wages."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same thing happened in Egypt: *their cry came up to God because of the bondage, and God heard their groaning.* And on the way out, the back pay was settled. See the note on Exodus 12:33–36."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who James was.** The brother of Jesus, and the leader of the Jerusalem church — writing to Jewish believers scattered by persecution, most of them poor. He does not tell them to organise. He tells them to wait for the rain."}]}, "James 5:10–11": {"title": "\"What the Lord finally made of it\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only mention of Job in the New Testament, and it tells you what the whole book was for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who James points at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Think about **the prophets**… take them as your pattern: what they had to take, and how long they took it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not offer them as examples of success. He offers what they had to take, and the length of time they took it for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The ones who hold out\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hupomeno** — to remain under. Not to grit the teeth; to stay put under a weight rather than get out from under it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ones who hold out under it are the ones we call fortunate.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict on Job"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have heard how Job held out, and you have seen **what the Lord finally made of it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James assumes his readers know the story. What he tells them is where to look in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not at the boils. Not at the ashes, or the arguments, or the thirty-five chapters of speeches. At **the end** — the Greek is *the end of the Lord*, the outcome the Lord brought it to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the outcome shows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*— that **He feels for people deeply, and He is kind all the way through**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in the Greek, and the first is one James appears to have coined: **polusplagchnos**, literally *many-boweled*, from the word for the inward parts, where compassion was felt. Very compassionate. It occurs nowhere else in Greek literature before him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second is **oiktirmon** — merciful, full of pity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the New Testament's one-sentence summary of the book of Job, and it is not about patience, endurance, or the mystery of suffering. It is a statement about what God is like, drawn from how the story ended."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What James is telling his readers to conclude"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If you want to know what the Lord is, read to the end of that book. He is deeply compassionate and kind all the way through — and the whole forty-two chapters are offered as evidence for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the middle of it looked like, the end of it is what tells you His character."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The famous phrase.** *The patience of Job* comes from the older English of this verse. The Greek word is not patience but staying under — and James's point is not really about Job at all. It is about what the Lord was, the whole time, and what He turned out to be at the end."}]}, "James 5:13–15": {"title": "\"Let him send for the elders\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three conditions and three responses, and the third one is the longest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Is one of you **having a hard time**? Let him **pray**.* *Is one of you **in good spirits**? Let him **sing**.* *Is one of you **sick**? Let him **send for the elders**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every state gets an action, and none of them is to do nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first two are things you do alone. The third is the only one that requires somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who sends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let **him** send for the elders.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The initiative is the sick man's. Nobody in the passage is told to go looking for the sick."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let them **pray over him** and **put oil on him** in the name of the Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two actions, in that order."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *pray over* is literally that — praying over the person, and it implies being physically present."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oil"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is **aleipho**, which is the ordinary word for rubbing oil on — what you do after a bath, or to an athlete, or to a wound."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not **chrio**, the ceremonial anointing word from which *Christ* comes. James uses the everyday one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Olive oil was the standard medical application of that world. The Samaritan poured oil and wine on the man's wounds on the Jericho road."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever else is happening, the elders are doing something ordinary and physical, in a name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What does the healing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the prayer that trusts God** will make the sick man well, and **the Lord** will get him back on his feet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the oil, and not the elders. The Greek is *the prayer of faith*, and then the Lord is named as the one who raises him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **if he has committed sins**, he will be forgiven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**If.** James does not assume it, and he does not connect the sickness to the sin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts the forgiveness in a conditional and moves on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the twelve did it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They drove out many demons, and **they rubbed oil on many who were sick and healed them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark 6, on the first mission, with no bread, no bag, no money and one stick. See the note on Mark 6:7–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb, and it is the only occasion in the Gospels the disciples are recorded using oil."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next in James"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Confess your trespasses to one another, and pray for one another, that you may be healed. **The effective, fervent prayer of a righteous man avails much.***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then Elijah, *a man with a nature like ours*, who prayed and it did not rain for three and a half years. See the note on James 5:16–18."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the letter is like.** James is the most practical book in the New Testament — the tongue, the poor, favouritism, patience, oaths. This paragraph is in character: somebody is ill, so send for the elders, and let them use oil."}]}, "James 5:16–18": {"title": "\"Made of exactly the same stuff we are\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "James picks the most spectacular prayer in Israel's history and then goes out of his way to say the man who prayed it was nobody special."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Elijah was made of exactly the same stuff we are.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is one word — **homoiopathes**, of like feelings, subject to the same things. Paul and Barnabas use it at Lystra when a crowd tries to sacrifice to them: *we are men of the same nature as you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James is heading off the obvious objection before anyone makes it. Yes, but he was Elijah. No — he was not a different kind of creature."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the man James chose is one whose recorded low point is asking God to let him die under a broom tree."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Greek word covers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Homoiopathes** is built from *homoios*, alike, and *pathos*, feeling, suffering, what happens to a person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a statement about ability. It is a statement about what he was subject to — fear, exhaustion, hunger, and the wish to be finished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "All four are in one chapter of 1 Kings, and James knew it. See the note on 1 Kings 19:1–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer described"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He prayed hard that the rain would stop… then he prayed again, and the sky let the rain down.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *prayed hard* is a Hebrew-flavoured construction: he prayed with prayer. Not a technique — earnestness, the same thing James means when he says a righteous man's prayer works *when he means it all the way down*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What makes a prayer effective"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When a man who stands right with God prays… it puts real power to work.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for *stands right* is the righteousness word. In the New Testament that standing is a gift, received rather than achieved — which is what makes this verse usable by ordinary people rather than only by the very good."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James has just spent a letter on practical faith, and he lands here: confession to one another, prayer for one another, healing, and an ordinary man's prayer shutting the sky."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the detail comes from.** The three and a half years is not stated in 1 Kings — James supplies it, and Jesus gives the same figure in Luke 4. The length of that drought was evidently well known, and both of them count it the same way."}]}, "James 5:7–9": {"title": "Wait like a farmer waits", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The instruction is patience, and the example is somebody who cannot make anything happen faster."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **hold on, brothers, until the Lord comes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **makrothumesate** — long-tempered, slow to boil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is about people, not circumstances. Its opposite is the short fuse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James has just finished four verses on rich men whose hired labourers were not paid — *the wages of the labourers who mowed your fields, which you kept back by fraud, cry out*. The people told to be long-tempered are the ones who were cheated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The example"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look at **the farmer**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of all occupations, the one with the least control over the timetable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he waits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What the ground can give him **is worth a great deal to him**, so he sits and waits for it, **and goes on waiting**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **timion karpon** — the precious fruit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The waiting is explained by the value, not by the farmer's temperament. He waits because it is worth waiting for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two rains"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Through **the first rains and then the late ones**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Israel the early rain falls in October and November, softening ground hard from summer so it can be ploughed and sown. The latter rain falls in March and April, filling out the grain before harvest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between them, months in which nothing visible happens and nothing can be done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What a farmer cannot do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Speed it up. He can plough, sow, and wait, and the interval belongs to somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The application"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wait like that yourselves. **Put some steel in your hearts**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sterixate tas kardias humon** — establish, make firm, brace your hearts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke uses the same verb of Jesus: *He steadfastly set His face to go to Jerusalem*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Lord's coming is close now.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **engiken** — perfect tense. Has drawn near and stands near."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not grumble about one another, brothers, **or you will be the ones judged for it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me stenazete** — do not groan against one another."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is a sound, not an argument. Sighing at each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pressure applied from outside tends to come out sideways, on the people nearest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James has spent the letter on this: *who is wise among you? Let him show by good conduct*, and *where envy and self-seeking exist, confusion and every evil thing are there*. See the note on James 3:13–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is **the Judge, right outside the door**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pro ton thuron hesteken** — stands before the doors."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The nearness that made the waiting bearable in verse 8 is the same nearness that makes the grumbling dangerous in verse 9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One fact, and James uses it both ways in two sentences."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Take the prophets as an example of suffering and patience*, and then Job — *you have heard of the perseverance of Job and seen the end intended by the Lord*. See the note on James 5:10–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Farmers, prophets, and Job. Three sets of people who waited without a schedule."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The example.** A farmer, because there is nothing he can do to bring it forward. And the reason given for his waiting is not his character — it is what the ground is worth."}]}, "Jeremiah 1:17–19": {"title": "\"A fortified city, an iron pillar, a wall of bronze\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of the call, and God tells a frightened young man what he has been made into."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being spoken to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So gather yourself, **Jeremiah**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *gird up your loins* — tie up the long robe to be able to move."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had objected at the start of the chapter: *\"Ah, Lord GOD! **Behold, I cannot speak, for I am a youth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer: *do not say \"I am a youth,\" for you shall go to all to whom I send you.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Stand up and **tell them everything I command you**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not **fall apart before their faces**, or **I will let you fall apart in front of them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *do not be dismayed before their faces, lest I dismay you before them*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb both times, and it is uncomfortable. The protection is attached to the standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is made"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look, **this very day I have made you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Perfect tense. It is stated as done before he has preached a word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **fortified city**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ir mivtsar** — a walled city. The one structure in that world designed to be attacked and not fall."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*An **iron pillar**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A pillar holds a roof. Iron is the strongest metal they had."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **wall of bronze**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bronze does not rust and does not let water through."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three images, and every one is defensive rather than offensive. He is not given a weapon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Against whom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Against **the whole land, against the kings of Judah, its officials, its priests, and its people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four groups, from the throne down. Everybody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah is one man told he has been made a city against a country."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will **fight against you, but they will not win**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves. The fighting is guaranteed as much as the outcome."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not told he will be popular or believed. He was told at the start: *they will fight against you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **I am with you to rescue you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *to deliver you* — **natsal**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same clause given at the start of the chapter: *do not be afraid of their faces, for **I am with you to deliver you**, says the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice in one chapter, opening and closing the call."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years of preaching, and almost nobody listened. He was put in the stocks, thrown in a cistern and left in the mud, denounced as a traitor, and forced into Egypt at the end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He saw the city fall and wrote Lamentations over it. See the note on Lamentations 3:31–33."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wall did not stop the fighting. It held."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about the preaching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then I said, \"I will not make mention of Him, nor speak anymore in His name.\" **But His word was in my heart like a burning fire shut up in my bones**; I was weary of holding it back, and I could not.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tried to stop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was said before he was born"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Before I formed you in the womb I knew you**; before you were born I sanctified you; I ordained you a prophet to the nations.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he is given.** Not eloquence and not an easy hearing. A city wall, an iron pillar and bronze — three things whose only function is to be hit and stay up."}]}, "Jeremiah 1:4–5": {"title": "Before I formed you", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The call of a prophet, and every verb in it is in the past."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The word of the LORD came to me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayehi devar-YHWH elai** — and the word of the LORD was to me."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The formula runs through the book more than fifty times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Before I formed you in the womb, I knew you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *formed* is **etzorcha**, from **yatsar** — the potter's word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb as *the LORD God formed man of the dust of the ground*, and the same root behind the potter's house Jeremiah is later sent to watch. See the note on Jeremiah 18:1–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for knew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yada** — the Hebrew word for knowing by relationship rather than by information."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word used of Adam and Eve, of God knowing Abraham, and of *you only have I known of all the families of the earth*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *I knew about you*. Knew you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Before you drew your first breath, I set you apart**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hiqdashticha** — I sanctified you, made you holy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb belongs to the priesthood and the vessels of the tabernacle. Jeremiah was of a priestly family at Anathoth, and the word for consecrating a priest is applied to him before birth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I appointed you as a prophet to the nations**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nathaticha** — I gave you, I appointed you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Lagoyim** — to the nations. Not to Judah. A prophet who will spend his life speaking to one small kingdom is commissioned to the nations in his first sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tenses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Knew, set apart, appointed. Three completed actions, all of them before he existed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the call is conditional on anything Jeremiah does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Ah, Lord GOD! Behold, **I cannot speak, for I am a youth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **na'ar** — a boy, a young man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not the first. Moses said *I am slow of speech*. Gideon said *my clan is the weakest*. Isaiah said *I am a man of unclean lips*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not say, 'I am a youth,'** for you shall go to all to whom I send you… **Do not be afraid of their faces**, for I am with you to deliver you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the LORD put forth His hand and touched my mouth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The objection is about speaking, and the answer is a hand on the mouth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the life turned out to be"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years of preaching that nobody accepted. A scroll cut up and burned by a king. A cistern he was lowered into with ropes and left in the mud. And Jerusalem falling anyway, exactly as he had said."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The call promised he was known, not that he would be heard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where else this language goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He said to me, \"You are My servant, O Israel\"… **the LORD has called Me from the womb**.* Of the servant in Isaiah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who separated me from my mother's womb and called me through His grace.* Paul, of himself. See the note on Galatians 1:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both take Jeremiah's construction and apply it elsewhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm says about the forming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For You formed my inward parts; You covered me in my mother's womb… **and in Your book they all were written**, the days fashioned for me, when as yet there were none of them.* See the note on Psalms 139:13–16."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The tenses.** Knew, set apart, appointed — three finished actions, all of them completed before there was anybody to do them to."}]}, "Jeremiah 10:1–5": {"title": "A scarecrow in a melon patch", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He takes the most impressive religion in the region apart by describing how it is made."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hear the word the LORD speaks to you, **house of Israel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Babylon is coming, and Babylonian religion with it — astrology, omens, processions with images carried through the streets."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah's audience is about to be surrounded by it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not learn the ways of the nations**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **al-derek haggoyim al-tilmadu**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Lamad** — to learn, to be trained in. The root behind *talmud*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *do not worship their gods*. Do not be schooled in their habits."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not be terrified by signs in the sky**, though the nations are terrified by them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **me'otot hashamayim** — signs of the heavens."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Babylonian religion was astronomical. Eclipses, comets, planetary conjunctions and the movement of stars were read as messages about the fate of kings and harvests."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is conceded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though the nations are terrified by them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fear is real and widespread. Jeremiah does not deny that everyone around them is frightened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells one people not to join in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The customs of the peoples are empty**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hevel** — vapour, breath. The word Ecclesiastes uses twenty-nine times. See the note on Ecclesiastes 1:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The manufacture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Someone cuts a tree from the forest**. A craftsman shapes it with his tools.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument is a production sequence. Jeremiah walks the reader backwards from the finished object to the woodpile."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The decoration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He decorates it with **silver and gold**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The precious metal goes on last, over the wood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The nails"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **fastens it with hammer and nails so it will not topple over**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **bemasmerot uvmaqqavot yechazzequm velo yaphiq**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The god has to be nailed down so it does not fall over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That single detail is the whole argument, and Isaiah makes the same one: *he seeks for himself a skilful workman to prepare a carved image that will not totter*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Their idols are like **a scarecrow standing in a melon patch**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ketomer miqshah** — like a pillar, or a palm-trunk figure, in a cucumber field."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something shaped like a person, standing in a field, that frightens birds and nothing else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three disabilities"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**They cannot speak. They have to be carried, because they cannot walk**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Silent and immobile."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm makes the same list at length: *they have mouths, but they do not speak; eyes, but they do not see… **those who make them are like them***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not be afraid of them. They can do you no harm, and they cannot do you any good either**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ki-lo yare'u vegam-heytev eyn otam**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves matter. The gods that cannot hurt you also cannot help you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is set against it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Inasmuch as **there is none like You, O LORD**; You are great, and Your name is great in might.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the LORD is the true God; He is the living God and the everlasting King**. At His wrath the earth will tremble.* See the note on Jeremiah 10:6–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A god that has to be nailed upright, against one at whose anger the ground moves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one that has to be carried"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They have to be carried.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah answers it directly: *even to your old age, I am He; and even to grey hairs I will carry you. **I have made, and I will bear**; even I will carry, and will deliver you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One kind of god is carried. The other carries."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The detail.** It has to be nailed down so it will not fall over. Jeremiah builds the whole argument out of the manufacturing process."}]}, "Jeremiah 15:15–18": {"title": "\"When Your words came, I ate them\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A prophet's complaint, and in the middle of it one of the best lines in the book about Scripture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*O LORD, **You know**. Remember me. Come for me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three short imperatives. He is not composing; he is asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Your words came, **I ate them**. Your words became **my joy, the delight of my heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that he found them and consumed them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel is handed a scroll and told *eat this scroll*, and reports *it was in my mouth like honey in sweetness*. John does the same in Revelation 10 — sweet in the mouth and bitter in the stomach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eating is the standard biblical picture for taking in what is written, and Jeremiah uses it about a word that cost him everything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **I bear Your name**, O LORD God of hosts.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The eating and the bearing of the name are given as one thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I **never sat in the circle** of those who laugh and make merry. Because Your hand was on me, **I sat alone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the prophet who was forbidden to marry, forbidden to go to funerals, and forbidden to go to feasts — see the note on Jeremiah 16:5–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The isolation was instructed, and he lists it here as a grievance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The complaint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Why is **my pain unending**, my wound grievous and **refusing to heal**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not asking rhetorically. He wants an answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The accusation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Will You be to me **like a stream that fails**, like **waters that are not sure**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A wadi — a desert streambed that runs in the rains and is dry when you need it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is asking God whether He is a seasonal brook."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the sharpest thing any prophet says to God's face, and it is preserved in the book without softening. Two chapters earlier God had called Himself *the fountain of living waters*; Jeremiah asks whether that is true."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer he gets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you return, then I will bring you back; you shall stand before Me. If you take out the precious from the vile, you shall be as My mouth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *I will make you to this people a fortified bronze wall… **for I am with you, to save you and deliver you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No explanation of the pain. A restoration, a commission, and a promise of company."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Keeps going for another thirty-seven chapters, through a cistern, stocks, a burned scroll he dictates again, and the fall of the city."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other thing he says in this book.** *His word was in my heart **like a burning fire shut up in my bones**; I was weary of holding it back, and I could not.* Said right after *I will not make mention of Him, nor speak any more in His name.* He resigned, and could not do it."}]}, "Jeremiah 16:5–7": {"title": "A prophet told not to mourn", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah is forbidden three ordinary human things, and each prohibition is a sermon he has to live rather than preach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not enter a house of mourning.** Do not go to grieve or to comfort them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that culture, staying away from a funeral was unthinkable. Mourning with the bereaved was a duty everybody owed everybody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is told not to go, and his absence at every graveside in Jerusalem is the message."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter forbids him three things in a row: do not marry or have children here, do not go to a funeral, do not go to a feast."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No wife, no mourning, no celebration. He is cut off from the beginning, the end and the middle of ordinary life, and the isolation is deliberate — it is what the nation is about to experience."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being withdrawn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have taken My peace away from this people**, says the LORD, My steadfast love, My mercy.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three nouns: *shalom*, **chesed** — the covenant love — and mercy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same season Hosea named *Lo-Ammi*, not My people. See the note on Hosea 1:8–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cup of consolation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"No one will **lift the cup of consolation** for a father or a mother.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There was a customary cup handed to mourners after a burial. The chapter names it and says it will not be offered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody will break bread with the grieving. Nobody will cut himself or shave his head. Every rite that made a death bearable is stripped out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is on the other side of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Keep reading. Nine verses later:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Behold, the days are coming, says the LORD, that it shall no more be said, 'The LORD lives who brought up the children of Israel out of the land of Egypt,' but 'The LORD lives **who brought up the children of Israel from the land of the north and from all the lands where He had driven them**.' For I will bring them back into their land which I gave to their fathers.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the return will be a bigger event than the exodus — so much bigger that the nation will change the way it swears its oaths."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That was written around 600 BC, to people about to be deported, and the scattering that eventually followed lasted nineteen centuries."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word for the sending.** *I will send for many fishermen, and they shall fish them… and afterward I will send for many hunters, and they shall hunt them from every mountain and every hill and out of the holes of the rocks.* Fishermen first, then hunters. Jeremiah says the gathering will reach the ones who do not come when they are called."}]}, "Jeremiah 17:5–6": {"title": "The bush in the desert", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first half of a pair, and everything in it is about where the roots are."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is what **the LORD** says.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah attributes it directly. The comparison that follows is not the prophet's opinion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Cursed is the one who **trusts in man**, who **leans on mere flesh for his strength**, whose **heart turns away from the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three, and they run in sequence: trusting, leaning, and then the turning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heart's departure is listed third. It is the consequence rather than the cause."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Mere flesh\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **basar** — flesh, meat. The word for what a body is made of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah uses it the same way: *the Egyptians are men, and not God; and their horses are flesh, and not spirit.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The picture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is like **a bush in the desert wastes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **arar** — a juniper or dwarf shrub of the wilderness. It survives; it does not thrive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he cannot do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **does not see when good comes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he shall not see when good comes*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The good arrives and he misses it. Nothing in the sentence says the good is withheld."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he lives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In **the parched places of the wilderness**, in **a salt land where no one can live**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Salt land is the ground around the Dead Sea — white, crusted, and nothing grows on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah picks the most sterile landscape in the country."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jeremiah was watching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah in its last decades, negotiating with Egypt against Babylon and then with Babylon against Egypt. The whole political programme of the period was leaning on flesh."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been told at his call: *they will fight against you, but they shall not prevail against you.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse in between the two halves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses on, after the blessed man: *the heart is deceitful above all things, and desperately wicked; who can know it?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah puts the two trees on either side of that sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **blessed is the one who trusts in the LORD**… He is like **a tree planted by the water**.* See the note on Jeremiah 17:7–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two men, two root systems, one climate. The drought comes to both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the image comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 1, which opens the psalter with the same figure: *he shall be like a tree planted by the rivers of water… the ungodly are not so, but are like the chaff which the wind drives away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah keeps the tree and changes the opposite from chaff to a shrub."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter goes on to say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I, the LORD, **search the heart**, I test the mind, even to give every man according to his ways.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, in Jeremiah's own voice: *Heal me, O LORD, and I shall be healed; save me, and I shall be saved, **for You are my praise**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two halves share.** The heat and the drought. Jeremiah does not describe one man in good weather and one in bad — the difference in the passage is entirely where the roots went."}]}, "Jeremiah 17:7–8": {"title": "The tree that never fails", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second half of the pair, and the weather in it is the same."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed is the one who **trusts in the LORD**, whose **confidence rests in Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs where the cursed man had three. The Hebrew for *confidence* is **mibtach** — a refuge, the thing you put your weight on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tree"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is like **a tree planted by the water**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *transplanted* — **shathal**, set out deliberately, not self-seeded."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody put it there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The roots"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sending its roots down **along the stream**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *spreading out its roots by the river*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The water is not falling on it from above. The tree is drawing from a source at its feet that does not depend on the weather."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole mechanism of the passage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It has **no fear when the heat comes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heat is not prevented. It arrives, and the tree does not react."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah does not describe a sheltered life. He describes the same climate as verse 6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The leaves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Its **leaves stay green**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *its leaf shall be green*. Not merely alive. The colour is the same as it was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The drought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In **a year of drought it does not worry**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shall not be anxious*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A year, not a week. The word for *anxious* is the same root Jeremiah uses elsewhere for care and dread."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It **never stops bearing fruit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shall not cease from yielding fruit*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not more fruit than usual and not fruit in season only. It does not stop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison held"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both men in the pair face the same conditions — heat, drought, a year of it. See the note on Jeremiah 17:5–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One is in a salt land and cannot see good when it comes. One is by a stream and does not notice the drought."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the image starts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 1: *he shall be like a tree planted by the rivers of water, that brings forth its fruit in its season, whose leaf also shall not wither.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm's man delights in the law. Jeremiah's trusts in the LORD."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the same chapter says two verses later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The heart is **deceitful above all things**, and desperately wicked; **who can know it?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer, immediately: *I, the LORD, search the heart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah sets the two trees on either side of a sentence about how little a man can tell about his own inside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the tree turns up at the end of the Bible"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the middle of its street, and on either side of the river, was **the tree of life**, which bore twelve fruits, each tree yielding its fruit every month.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A tree by a river, bearing every month."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the water is.** Not falling on the leaves. Underground, at the roots, reached by a system the drought never touches — and Jeremiah says the tree does not even register the year."}]}, "Jeremiah 22:28–30": {"title": "The curse on Jeconiah, and the line that had to get past it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the strangest problems in the Bible is set up here, and it is not solved until Matthew's first chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Write this man down as childless, a man who will not prosper in his days. For none of his offspring will prosper, none will sit on the throne of David or rule again in Judah.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Coniah — also called Jeconiah and Jehoiachin — was king of Judah for three months before Babylon carried him off. The curse is not that he has no children; he had seven. It is that **none of his descendants will ever sit on David's throne.**"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why that is a problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God had also sworn to David that his throne would be established forever, and that the Messiah would come from his line."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the promise runs through David, and the royal line runs through Jeconiah, and the royal line has just been declared permanently disqualified. Both statements are in the same Bible and neither is withdrawn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How Matthew resolves it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's genealogy runs straight through Jeconiah — he names him — and traces the line down to *Joseph, the husband of Mary*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the wording changes. Every other link in that chapter says *was the father of*. This one does not:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "*Jacob was the father of Joseph, the husband of Mary, and it was **from Mary** that Jesus was born.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph gives Jesus the legal claim to David's throne, as his adoptive father — which He needs. But the blood does not come through Joseph, so the curse on Jeconiah's descendants does not touch Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Luke's genealogy takes a different route entirely, through David's son Nathan rather than Solomon, bypassing the cursed line altogether."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What that means about the virgin birth.** It is usually argued from prophecy and from purity. This is a third reason, and it is legal: a Messiah descended physically from Jeconiah could not have taken the throne. The line had to arrive by another road, and Matthew's careful change of wording is where he shows you it did."}]}, "Jeremiah 29:10–14": {"title": "\"Plans to give you a future and a hope\" — and the seventy years in front of it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted verse in Jeremiah, and it begins with a number that never gets quoted with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence it belongs to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When seventy years are completed for Babylon, I will come to you and make good on My gracious promise to bring you back to this place.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventy years. Most of the people reading the letter would die in Babylon. The promise is real, and its fulfilment is beyond the lifetime of nearly everyone being addressed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the setting for *I know the plans I have for you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who received it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exiles — and Jeremiah's letter tells them what to do in the meantime: build houses, plant gardens, marry, have children, and *seek the peace of the city where I have sent you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not endure it. Settle in it, and pray for the place holding you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the promise says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Plans for peace and not for harm, to give you a future and a hope.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *future* is literally an **end**, an outcome — the far side of something. Paired with hope, it means: there is a later, and it is good."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Spoken to people whose visible situation was going to last seventy years, that is not a small thing. It is precisely the promise a person needs when the near view holds nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it matters that we read it whole"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Quoted alone it can sound like a guarantee that plans will go well soon. In place it is stronger and harder: God's intentions are good, the timescale may be longer than your life, and you are told to plant gardens while you wait."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What follows.** *You will call on Me and come and pray to Me, and I will listen. You will seek Me and find Me, when you seek Me with all your heart.* The promise about the future is immediately followed by an invitation to talk to Him in the middle of the seventy years, not only at the end of them."}]}, "Jeremiah 3:19–20": {"title": "\"I thought you would call Me 'My Father'\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God quotes His own private thought, and it is about a name He hoped to be called."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The construction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I had said **to Myself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I said*, and what follows is an interior sentence. Jeremiah records it as overheard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The intention"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How gladly would I set you among My children** and **give you a pleasant land, the finest inheritance of any nation**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *how shall I put you among the children*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A pleasant land** — the Hebrew is *a land of desire*, **eretz chemdah**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The expectation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **I thought you would call Me 'My Father'** and **never turn away from Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *you shall call Me My Father*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole weight of the passage is on a form of address."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why that is unusual"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God is called Father in the Old Testament, but rarely, and almost always of the nation rather than by an individual."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah: *doubtless You are our Father, though Abraham was ignorant of us*. And: *You, O LORD, are our Father, our Redeemer from Everlasting is Your name.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a common form of address in those pages. Here God says it is what He was hoping for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **as a wife betrays her husband, so you betrayed Me**, house of Israel.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **bagad** — to act treacherously, to deal falsely."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole of Jeremiah 3 runs on this image: Israel as an unfaithful wife, and the chapter opens with the law about a man who cannot take back a wife who has married another."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two metaphors together"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage moves from father-and-child to husband-and-wife in one sentence, and does not smooth the transition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are used because both are covenant relations that can be broken from one side only."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Return, you backsliding children**, and I will heal your backslidings.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And two verses on: *\"Return, O backsliding Israel,\" says the LORD; \"**I will not cause My anger to fall on you**. For I am merciful,\" says the LORD; \"I will not remain angry forever.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *merciful* is **chasid** — from **chesed**, covenant loyalty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Only acknowledge your iniquity**, that you have transgressed against the LORD your God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One thing. The chapter does not set terms beyond it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the Father-name goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Our Father in heaven**, hallowed be Your name.\"* See the note on Matthew 6:9–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **Abba**, in Gethsemane and in two of Paul's letters, left untranslated both times. See the note on Galatians 4:4–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the Spirit puts it in the mouth: *God has sent forth the Spirit of His Son into your hearts, crying out, \"Abba, Father!\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Jeremiah was writing to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah in its last decades, before Babylon. He was told at his call that nobody would listen, and he preached for forty years anyway."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What God says He was hoping for.** Not obedience, in this sentence. A name — that they would call Him Father — and He says it as a thought He had to Himself."}]}, "Jeremiah 33:6–9": {"title": "\"They will tremble in awe at all the good\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A promise of restoration made to a city under siege, by a prophet in prison."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Jeremiah was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens by noting he was *still confined in the courtyard of the guard*. Babylonian siege ramps were against the walls. The city fell shortly after."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything promised here is spoken into that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Health and healing… abundant peace and security… bring them back from exile… rebuild them as they were before… cleanse them of every sin… forgive all their sins of rebellion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six undertakings, and the last two are about guilt rather than circumstances. The rebuilding of walls and the cleansing of sin are in the same sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *abundant peace* is a phrase about an overflow of shalom and truth — wholeness, and reliability with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the nations do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then this city will bring Me joy and honour and praise before all the nations of the earth, when they hear of all the good I do for it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The restoration is public. The audience is everybody else, and what they hear about is not Judah's recovery but *the good I do*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line worth stopping on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will tremble in awe at all the good and all the peace I pour out on it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Trembling is normally the response to judgement. Here the nations shake at **goodness**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is an unusual sentence, and Jeremiah means it: the sheer scale of the mercy is what unsettles the onlookers."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is promised three verses later.** *In this place, of which you say, \"It is a waste\"… there will be heard again the voice of joy and the voice of gladness, the voice of the bridegroom and the voice of the bride.* Jeremiah quotes the city's own despairing description back and reverses it."}]}, "Jeremiah 7:30–31": {"title": "\"It never even entered My mind\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the most disturbing passages in the prophets, and one of the most revealing sentences about God in the Old Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was happening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They have built the high places of Topheth in the Valley of Ben Hinnom, and there they burn their own sons and daughters in the fire.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Child sacrifice, in a valley outside Jerusalem, by people who also kept the temple running. Jeremiah is not describing a foreign nation; this is Judah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The valley's name — Ge-Hinnom — comes through Greek into the New Testament as **Gehenna**, the word Jesus uses for hell. The place became the image."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three denials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God says three things about it in a row, and the third is the one that stops you:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["I never commanded it", "I never spoke of it", "**it never even entered My mind**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *it did not come up on My heart*. Not merely that He forbade it — that the notion had never crossed His thoughts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it needed saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because they were doing it in His name, in a valley within sight of His temple, alongside sacrifices they believed He wanted. The whole passage is about worship that had become something God did not recognise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has to state that He never conceived of it, because the people doing it thought it was religion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He wanted instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Earlier in the same chapter: *obey My voice, and I will be your God and you will be My people, and walk in all the way that I command you, that it may be well with you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The alternative offered is not more sacrifice. It is listening."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the valley ends up.** Josiah later desecrated Topheth precisely to stop this, and the place became a refuse dump — which is how a name for a valley became a name for the fire. The worst thing people did to their children gave its name to the worst thing there is."}]}, "Jeremiah 9:23–24": {"title": "The three things not to boast in, and the one thing to", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three prohibitions and one permission, and the permission is the surprise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let not the wise boast in their wisdom. Let not the strong boast in their strength. Let not the rich boast in their riches.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wisdom, strength, wealth — the three things every ancient society ranked people by, and ours has not moved on much."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note that none of the three is condemned. Solomon was given wisdom and wealth by God directly. The prohibition is on boasting in them, which is a different thing from having them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But if anyone would boast, let them boast in this alone, that they have the understanding to know Me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Boasting is not forbidden. It is redirected. There is something a person is permitted to be loud about, and it is knowing God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *understanding* here is about insight gained — a knowing that has been worked at and arrived at, not merely information held."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He wants known about Himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That I am the LORD, and that I show steadfast love, justice, and righteousness on the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He specifies. Of everything that could be said about God, three things are named: **hesed** — the covenant-keeping love — along with justice and righteousness. And all three are described as being done *on the earth*, not in the abstract."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *for in these I take delight.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul takes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes the last line twice — in 1 Corinthians 1 after saying God chose the foolish and weak things, and again in 2 Corinthians. Both times as *let the one who boasts, boast in the Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He treats it as settling the question of what a Christian may be proud of."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**When Jeremiah said it.** In a book largely about a nation about to lose its wise men, its soldiers and its wealth to Babylon. The three things he tells them not to boast in were all about to be taken away, and the one thing he leaves them was the one thing exile could not touch."}]}, "Job 1:1–5": {"title": "A blameless life", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The book opens with a man nobody has anything on, and the very first habit it records about him is worth noticing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was a good man, **honest and whole all the way through**. He walked with God and turned away from evil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four things, and the writer says them flatly. There is no irony in the sentence and no qualification after it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew behind *honest and whole* is **tam** — complete, entire, without a crack running through it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seven thousand sheep, three thousand camels, five hundred yoke of oxen, five hundred donkeys… **the greatest man in all the East**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The numbers are given so the reader can measure the losses later, and so the doubling at the end of the book can be checked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one habit recorded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Job would gather his children and set them right before God. **Early in the morning** he would rise and offer a burnt offering for each one of them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not for himself. For them. Seven sons and three daughters, one offering each, after every round of feasting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he said to himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Perhaps** my children have sinned and cursed God in their hearts.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the reason he gives, and he gives it to himself — the text says he said it in his heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing had happened. Nobody had done anything. There is a *perhaps*, and there is an early morning and ten burnt offerings."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"This is what Job always did\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *thus did Job all the days*. A continuous, unbroken practice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book's very first picture of its hero is a man rising before dawn, week after week, because of something his children *might* have thought."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had no way of knowing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whether the offerings were needed. Whether they worked. Whether God was pleased."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no priest in this book, no tabernacle, no Scripture, no covenant, and no word from God to Job about any of it. He is doing what he can think of, on his own, with a *perhaps*, and repeating it every time the feasting comes round."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the man the rest of the book is about."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the New Testament says about that arrangement.** *Every priest stands daily ministering and offering repeatedly the same sacrifices, which can never take away sins. But this Man, after He had offered one sacrifice for sins forever, sat down.* Job stood up early. There is Somebody who sat down."}]}, "Job 1:6–8": {"title": "The adversary comes before God", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The scene shifts to a place Job never sees and never learns about, and the whole book turns on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He got in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sons of God came to present themselves before the LORD, and **the adversary came among them too, the accuser**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ha-satan**, with the article — *the* adversary, *the* accuser. It is a job description before it is a name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the thing to see is that he is standing there. He has access. He walks into the throne room and is asked a question and answers it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"From **wandering the earth, roaming back and forth** across it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter uses the same picture: *your adversary the devil walks about like a roaring lion, seeking whom he may devour.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who brings up Job"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the LORD said to him, \"**Have you noticed My servant Job?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The accuser did not name him. God did."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what God says about him is the same four things the writer said in verse 1 — *blameless and upright, a man who walks with Me and turns away from evil* — with one addition: *there is no one on earth like him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is God's own verdict on Job, spoken out loud, before any of the trouble starts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Job never knew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not told about this conversation. Not in chapter 1, not in chapter 42, not anywhere in the book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty-two chapters of arguing about why it happened, and the reader has the answer from page one while Job never gets it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has changed since"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing Job did not have is the one thing this scene depends on: nobody was standing between him and that accuser."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If anyone sins, we have an Advocate with the Father, Jesus Christ the righteous.* *He always lives to make intercession for them.* And in Revelation the accuser of the brethren is *cast down* — the one who *accused them before our God day and night.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job had no mediator in that room. He says so himself in chapter 9: *there is no umpire between us, who might lay his hand on us both.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole difference between his position and ours, and it is not a small one."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the accuser wanted.** *Does Job fear God for nothing?* His entire case is that the man is bought. Take the hedge down, he says, and he will curse You to Your face. The wager was never really about Job's suffering. It was about whether anyone loves God for who He is."}]}, "Job 11:13–15": {"title": "\"Zophar, and the formula\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The third friend, and he brings a procedure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shape of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If** you would only set your heart right… **if** there is sin in your grip, put it far away… **then** you could lift up your face without a stain.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two conditions and a result. It is a formula, and it is the oldest one there is: do this, and God will do that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The steps"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*set your heart right*", "*stretch out your hands to Him*", "*if there is sin in your grip, put it far away*", "*let no wrong live in your tent*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four items on a checklist, handed to a man covered in boils scraping himself with a piece of broken pot."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then you could **lift up your face without a stain**. You would stand firm and unafraid.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a few verses on: *your life would be brighter than noonday; its darkness would become like the morning.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The words are beautiful. The offer is genuine. And everything in it is conditional on Job's performance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is the wrong word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every clause depends on something Job has to produce, and the whole premise is that his darkness is being caused by sin he is holding on to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reader knows it is not. Chapter 1 said so, and God Himself said so out loud in the throne room: *there is no one on earth like him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zophar is telling a man to clean up something that is not there, and then promising him light on the other side of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it sounds like now"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your darkness is because of sin — deal with it, and the brightness will come back.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is still preached. And it still lands on people who are already on the floor, and it still asks them to produce a righteousness of their own before God will lift their face."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The face is lifted at Calvary, and the sin was put far away by Somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Zophar had said just before this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Know then that God exacts from you **less than your iniquity deserves**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is his opening position. He begins by telling Job he is getting off lightly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does not have"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of the three friends has a word from God. They have observation, tradition, and a formula, and between them they cover every way of speaking to a sufferer that leaves him to sort himself out."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verdict.** All three are named in chapter 42, and the same sentence is spoken over all three: *you have not spoken of Me what is right.* And what they have to do about it is bring seven bulls and seven rams and stand there while Job prays for them. See the note on Job 42:7–8."}]}, "Job 11:5–6": {"title": "Wisdom has two sides", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Zophar is wrong about almost everything, and then says one of the best lines in the book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zophar the Naamathite, the third and harshest of Job's friends, opening his first speech."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just accused Job of babbling: *should a man full of talk be vindicated?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "What he wants is for God to show up and put Job in his place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wish"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But oh, if only God would **open His lips and speak against you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gets it, eventually. God does speak — out of a whirlwind, in chapter 38 — and the speech is against Zophar. *My wrath is aroused against you and your two friends, for you have not spoken of Me what is right, as My servant Job has.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If He would show you **the secrets of wisdom**, for **true wisdom has two sides you cannot see**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kiphlayim le-tushiyyah** — double, or twofold, to sound wisdom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kephel** is a fold. The word for doubling something over on itself, as cloth is folded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Scripture has a fold in it. There is the plain reading, and there is what the plain reading is folded over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the same of the wilderness generation: *these things happened to them as examples* — the Greek is **typoi**, types — *and they were written for our admonition*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And of the Old Testament food laws: *a shadow of things to come, but the substance is of Christ*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the doubling is visible"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaac carrying the wood up the mountain his father would offer him on. See the note on Genesis 22:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bronze serpent on a pole, which Jesus applies to Himself in the third chapter of John."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jonah, three days inside, which He names as the only sign He would give."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of them is a real event that happened to real people, and every one of them is folded over something else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Zophar's other half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then you would know that **God has actually forgotten some of your guilt. He is giving you less than your sin deserves**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *God exacts from you less than your iniquity deserves*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zophar means it as an insult — *you are getting off lightly*. It is the only true thing in his speech, and it is true of everyone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Can you search out the deep things of God? Can you find out the limits of the Almighty? They are **higher than heaven**… **deeper than Sheol**… **longer than the earth and broader than the sea**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four dimensions. Paul uses the same four of the love of Christ — *the width and length and depth and height*. See the note on Ephesians 3:17–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then he ruins it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If you would prepare your heart… **if iniquity were in your hand, put it far away**… then surely you could lift up your face without spot.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offer is: fix yourself first. Which is what all three friends say, at length, for twenty chapters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job's answer is *though He slay me, yet will I trust Him* — and later, *I know that my Redeemer lives*. See the note on Job 19:25–27."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word under the line.** *Kephel* — a fold. Zophar wanted God to speak so Job would be crushed, and the sentence he chose says that wisdom is folded over, with a second face underneath."}]}, "Job 16:1–6": {"title": "Miserable comforters", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Job tells his three friends exactly what they are, and then tells them what he would have done in their place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name he gives them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Comforters? You are a misery, every one of you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a play on words — *comforters of trouble*, comforters who are themselves the trouble. It is the phrase that gave English the expression *Job's comforters*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They came to comfort him. Chapter 2 says so: they *made an appointment together to come and mourn with him and comfort him.* That was the intention."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I have heard all this before\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have heard all this before, and heard it many times. **Is there no end to your windy words?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *windy* is words of wind — air."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And there is a question after it: *what is it that stirs you up to keep answering?* He is asking what is driving them. Nobody has asked them that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he could do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I could talk the way you talk, **if it were you sitting where I sit**. I could pile up fine speeches against you and shake my head over you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole argument collapses into that clause. If the positions were reversed, he could produce the same material."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their theology costs them nothing to hold. It costs him everything to be under."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says he would do instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But no. I would use my mouth to steady you**, and the comfort of my lips would ease your pain.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence. A man scraping himself with a piece of broken pot, having buried ten children, describing what he would have said to them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *steady* is to strengthen, to make firm — and *ease your pain* is the verb for restraining, holding something back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is telling them the thing they are for, and none of the three does it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then the honest line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Yet whether I speak, my grief is not eased, and if I stay silent, it does not leave me either.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no position he can take that helps. Speaking does not relieve it; silence does not either."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is where he is, and the book leaves it on the page without softening it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they had already done right.** Seven days on the ground with him and not a word, *for they saw that his suffering was very great.* The best thing the three of them managed was the week before any of them opened their mouths."}]}, "Job 19:1–6": {"title": "\"How long will you crush me?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Job counts, and then draws a line his friends have been crossing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What words do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"How long will you keep **tormenting me and grinding me to pieces with words**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *grinding to pieces* is to crush, to break something small. It is used of pounding grain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is naming the weapon. Not the boils, not the losses — the talking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Ten times\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Ten times now** you have insulted me, and you are not ashamed to wound me so.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has been keeping count. Ten is a round number in Hebrew for a full tally, the whole of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are not ashamed.* Not that they are wrong — that they feel nothing about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line he draws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Even if I have gone wrong, **that is mine to carry, not yours**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a complete answer to all three of them. Suppose he is guilty. It is between him and God, and none of it is their business to prosecute."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *my error remains with me*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were really doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If you really mean to **lift yourselves above me** and **use my disgrace as proof against me**…\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things he names, and both are about them rather than him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are using his condition as evidence, and they are standing on it. A man on the ground is useful to a theology that says the upright are never on the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he concludes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then understand this. **It is God who has bent me down** and drawn His net tight around me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job's settled conviction, and he holds it for the whole book: God did this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reader knows better. Chapter 2 said in plain words that *the adversary went out from the presence of the LORD and struck Job* — and Job is never told. See the notes on Job 1:6–8 and Job 2:7–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone in the argument is working from the same wrong premise. The three friends and Job all agree God sent it; they only differ on whether He was right to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirteen verses further on, out of the middle of all this, comes the most famous sentence in the book:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I know that my Redeemer lives, and He shall stand at last on the earth… and in my flesh I shall see God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Redeemer** is **go'el** — the kinsman-redeemer, the relative whose job was to buy back what a man had lost."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is crushed, convinced God has netted him, and he says it anyway."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he asks for two verses before that.** *Oh, that my words were written… engraved on a rock forever.* They were."}]}, "Job 2:7–8": {"title": "Struck from head to foot", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and the shortest of them names who did it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who struck him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **the adversary** went out from the presence of the LORD and **struck Job** with painful boils.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence names the agent. It was not God who put the boils on him — the text says the accuser went out and did it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is worth holding on to, because Job spends the next thirty-five chapters convinced it was God, and his friends spend the same thirty-five chapters agreeing with him about that and only arguing over why."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reader knows something the whole cast does not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"From the soles of his feet to the crown of his head\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is a merism — the two ends stated to mean everything in between. No part of him was untouched."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy uses the identical phrase for a specific curse: *the LORD will strike you with painful boils… from the sole of your foot to the top of your head.* Any reader who knew that verse would draw the conclusion Job's friends draw."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book puts that conclusion in the reader's hand and then spends forty chapters showing it is wrong in this case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pottery"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Job took a **piece of broken pottery** to scrape himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The greatest man in all the East, sitting with a shard of a broken pot."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had seven thousand sheep a chapter ago. What he has now is a fragment of something that was already thrown away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ashes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he **sat down among the ashes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ash heap outside a town was the rubbish dump, where the sweepings and the burnt refuse went. It is where the destitute sat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he still had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His wife tells him to curse God and die. His three friends arrive, do not recognise him from a distance, and sit with him on the ground for seven days without a word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And no one spoke a word to him, for they saw that his suffering was very great.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seven silent days are the best thing the three of them do in the entire book."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says at the end of chapter 2.** *Shall we accept good from God, and not trouble?* And the writer adds: *in all this Job did not sin with his lips.* He is holding, and he is holding without knowing one thing about why."}]}, "Job 20:1–5": {"title": "Zophar answers", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The third friend's second speech, and the reason he gives for making it is that he is upset."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **Zophar from Naamah** answered.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third of the three. The shortest-tempered of them, and the only one who does not get a third speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My uneasy thoughts drive me to reply**, because of **the turmoil churning inside me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He states his own motive and it is agitation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a word about Job's condition, Job's argument, or anything he has learned. He is answering because he cannot sit still."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has stung"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have heard a rebuke that shames me**, and now my understanding urges me to speak.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job had just said, at the end of chapter 19: *be afraid of the sword for yourselves; for wrath brings the punishment of the sword, that you may know there is a judgment.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zophar takes it personally, and the speech that follows is about how the wicked come to nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Job had said just before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most famous sentence in the book: *For **I know that my Redeemer lives**, and He shall stand at last on the earth; and after my skin is destroyed, this I know, that **in my flesh I shall see God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zophar's answer to that is *the triumph of the wicked is short*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is replying to a confession of faith with a lecture about the fate of the godless."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The appeal to what everybody knows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Surely you know how it has always been**, ever since man was first set on the earth.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stock move of all three friends. They argue from the way things generally go and apply it to a case they have not understood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The thesis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The triumph of the wicked is short. The joy of the godless lasts only a moment**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is true, and it is in Proverbs and the Psalms."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is also, in this room, an accusation. Job has lost everything, so on Zophar's reasoning the category is obvious."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book has already told the reader"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 1: *that man was blameless and upright, and one who feared God and shunned evil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the conversation in heaven that none of the men on the ground know about. See the note on Job 1:6–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every speech of the friends is made by men working from a rule that does not fit the case in front of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says about them at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My wrath is aroused against you and your two friends, for you have not spoken of Me what is right**, as My servant Job has.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are told to bring seven bulls and seven rams, and that Job will pray for them — *for I will accept him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man they had been correcting is the one appointed to intercede for them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the speech does not contain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any question. Zophar does not ask Job anything in the whole chapter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The reason he gives for speaking.** The turmoil inside himself. Job's real answer, when it comes, is out of a whirlwind and consists almost entirely of questions."}]}, "Job 22:21–30": {"title": "\"Whatever you decide will hold firm\" — and who says it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Some of the loveliest promises in the Old Testament are in this speech, and the speaker is one of Job's three friends."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is talking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eliphaz the Temanite, in his third and harshest speech. A few verses earlier he has accused Job of stripping the clothes off the poor and sending widows away empty — charges the book has already told the reader are false."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the passage is a true thing said by a man who is wrong about the person he is saying it to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Make peace with Him, Job, and be at rest. Then good things will find their way back to you… You will pray, and He will hear you… Whatever you decide will hold firm, and light will shine on every path you take.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Taken as statements about God, these are sound and the rest of Scripture backs them. Taken as Eliphaz means them — repent of the sins we have decided you committed and your fortunes will return — they are a cruelty, and God says so at the end of the book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the book settles it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God's verdict on the three friends is unusually blunt: *you have not spoken of Me what is right, as My servant Job has.* They are told to bring sacrifices and have Job pray for them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which makes the last line here almost unbearable in hindsight. Eliphaz tells Job that God *will rescue even the guilty, and they will be delivered through the cleanness of your hands* — and that is precisely what happens, with Eliphaz as the guilty party and Job praying for him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How to use a passage like this.** The sentences are true of God; that is why they read so well on their own. What the book adds is the warning that true statements can be aimed at a suffering person as a weapon. Job's friends were at their best in the seven days they sat with him and said nothing."}]}, "Job 25:1–6": {"title": "Bildad, briefly", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last speech any of the friends make, and it is six verses long."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The note the text makes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Bildad from Shuah answered, **briefly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three rounds of speeches have run for twenty-two chapters. The first cycle is long, the second shorter, and this is the third — and Zophar does not speak at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The friends are running out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is true in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Dominion and dread belong to God**. **He keeps order in His high heavens**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hamshel vaphachad** — rule and terror."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Can anyone count His armies?** On whom does His light not rise?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in this is false. It is standard, correct, orthodox theology about God's greatness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **how can a mortal be righteous before God?** How can anyone born of a woman be pure?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **umah-yitzdaq enosh im-El**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the right question. It is the question the whole book is circling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the question came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job asked it himself in chapter 9: *how can a man be righteous before God?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Eliphaz asked it in chapter 4, having heard it from a spirit in the night: *can a mortal be more righteous than God?* See the note on Job 4:12–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three of them have now asked it, and none has an answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look, **even the moon is not bright to Him**, and **the stars are not pure in His sight**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If the brightest things in creation are dim by comparison, then a man has no case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How much less a human being, who is only a maggot, a child of dust, who is only a worm**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **rimmah** — maggot, and **tolea** — worm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is wrong with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing, doctrinally. And it is aimed at a man sitting in ashes scraping his sores with a potsherd, whom God has already called *blameless and upright*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The friends' theology is largely correct and their application of it is what God rebukes at the end: *My wrath is aroused against you and your two friends, for **you have not spoken of Me what is right, as My servant Job has**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Job says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How have you helped him who is without power?** How have you saved the arm that has no strength? How have you counselled one who has no wisdom?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reply opens with sarcasm and then turns into the longest speech in the book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Job's answer had already gone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**For I know that my Redeemer lives**, and He shall stand at last on the earth… **and after my skin is destroyed, this I know, that in my flesh I shall see God**.\"* See the note on Job 19:25–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six chapters earlier, from the man Bildad is calling a worm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Job had asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Nor is there any mediator between us**, who may lay his hand on us both.\"* See the note on Job 9:32–35."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man to stand between, touching both parties. Bildad's question has no such answer available to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the answer eventually comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For there is one God and **one Mediator between God and men, the Man Christ Jesus**.* See the note on 1 Timothy 2:5–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other worm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But I am a worm, and no man; a reproach of men, and despised by the people.* — Psalm 22, the psalm quoted from the cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew there is **tolaath**, the same root Bildad uses. It is also the name of the crimson grub from which scarlet dye was made."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is wrong with the speech.** Not the theology. The audience — a man God has already called blameless, being told he is a maggot by a friend who came to comfort him."}]}, "Job 26:5–10": {"title": "\"He hangs the earth on nothing at all\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Job's answer to Bildad, and in the middle of it he says something no one else in the ancient world said."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He stretches the northern skies over **empty space** and **hangs the earth on nothing at all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *upon nothing* — **beli-mah**, without anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job is probably the oldest book in the Bible. Every culture around it had the earth resting on something: on a turtle, on elephants, on pillars, on the shoulders of a giant, on the back of a great fish."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it is hanging on nothing, and nobody could confirm it for three and a half thousand years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The circle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **drew a circle on the surface of the waters**, a boundary line where light meets darkness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same Hebrew word Isaiah uses — **chug**, a circle drawn with a fixed radius."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the second clause is exact. The boundary where light meets darkness on the surface of a sphere is a circle, and it moves. It is called the terminator, and it is what you are looking at in every photograph of the earth from space."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clouds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **wraps up the waters in His thick clouds**, and the clouds do not burst beneath their weight.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *wraps up* is to bind in a bundle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An average cumulus cloud carries hundreds of tonnes of water and does not fall. Job noticed that it does not, and said the not-falling was the remarkable part."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is in the argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is a man on an ash heap, covered in boils, whose three friends have been telling him for twenty chapters that he brought it on himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His reply is a description of the universe. Bildad had just said *how then can man be righteous before God?* — and Job's answer is four verses of cosmology."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And these are but the outer fringes of His ways. How faint the whisper we hear of Him! Who then can understand the thunder of His power?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *outer fringes* is the ends, the edges. He has just described empty space, a hanging earth, bundled oceans and a drawn circle, and calls it the hem of the garment."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who says the same thing later.** *In Him all things hold together* — Colossians 1. Job says the earth hangs on nothing; Paul names what nothing is."}]}, "Job 29:1–6": {"title": "When God watched over me", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The friends have stopped talking, and Job describes what he has lost."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three cycles of speeches are over. Bildad's last effort was six verses long. See the note on Job 25:1–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job now speaks for three uninterrupted chapters: what he was, what he is, and his final defence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Job **took up his words again**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyosep Iyyov se'et meshalo** — and Job again lifted up his discourse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Mashal** — a proverb, a parable, an oracle. The word for elevated speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wish"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Oh, if only **the old days would come back to me**, those months when **God kept watch over my life**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kiyrachei-qedem** — as in the months of old."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yishmereni** — He guarded me, kept watch over me."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he misses first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the wealth. Not the children."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing named is being watched over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lamp"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**His lamp used to shine above my head**, and **by that light I walked safely through the dark**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **behillo nero alei roshi** — when His lamp shone over my head."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is of a light carried above a traveller — enough to see by, moving with him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your word is a lamp to my feet and a light to my path.* See the note on Psalms 119:105–112."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The days"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Those were **the days of my ripeness**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **bimei chorpi** — in the days of my autumn, my harvest-time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not youth. The season when things come in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The friendship"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When **the friendship of God rested warm over my tent**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **besod Eloah alei ahali** — the intimate counsel of God over my tent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sod** — a confidential circle, a council, the private conversation of friends."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The secret of the LORD is with those who fear Him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The children"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When the Almighty was still near me and **my children gathered close around**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven sons and three daughters, killed when a wind struck the house where they were eating."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He mentions them in a subordinate clause."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cream"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In those days **my steps were washed in cream**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **birchotz halichai bechemah** — when I washed my steps in butter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Curds so plentiful you could wade in them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oil"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **the rock beside me poured out rivers of oil**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Olive presses were cut into rock. Oil running out of stone is the image of production so heavy the rock itself seems to give it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When I went out to the gate by the city… the young men saw me and hid, and the aged arose and stood.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Because I delivered the poor who cried out, the fatherless and the one who had no helper**… I was eyes to the blind, and I was feet to the lame.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I broke the fangs of the wicked, and plucked the victim from his teeth.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He describes a public life spent on people with no standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the next chapter says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But now they mock at me, men younger than I**, whose fathers I disdained to put with the dogs of my flock.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And now my soul is poured out because of my plight; the days of affliction take hold of me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reversal is total, and Job sets the two chapters side by side himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he never learns"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reader was told in chapter 1. Job never is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer he gets is four chapters of questions about wild goats, ostriches, the horse, and Leviathan — and *I have heard of You by the hearing of the ear, but now my eye sees You*. See the note on Job 42:1–6."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he misses first.** Not the herds and not the children — *those months when God kept watch over my life*. The list starts with a lamp carried above his head."}]}, "Job 29:14–17": {"title": "\"I wrapped myself in righteousness\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Job's longest speech, remembering what he used to be, and it is worth counting the pronouns."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The garment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I wrapped myself** in righteousness, and it clothed me; justice was my robe and my crown.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He put it on himself. That is what the verb says, and the whole passage is built the same way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah describes a different arrangement: *He has clothed me with the garments of salvation, He has covered me with the robe of righteousness.* Somebody else does the dressing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job's robe is one he wove and wrapped around himself, and every word of it is true — he really was that man. Chapter 1 said so and God said so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I was **eyes to the blind** and **feet to the lame**. I was **a father to the needy**, and when a stranger came with a case I did not know, **I searched it out for him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The record is extraordinary. He was not boasting about property or influence — he is listing the blind, the lame, the poor and the stranger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I broke the jaws of the wicked and pulled the prey from between his teeth.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has happened to him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the chapter as a whole and count. In chapter 29, Job refers to himself well over forty times and to God about five."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is not how he began. Chapter 1: *the LORD gave and the LORD has taken away; blessed be the name of the LORD.* Chapter 2: *shall we accept good from God and not trouble?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between there and here, three men have spent twenty-five chapters telling him his suffering is his own fault. He has had to defend himself for so long that he has become his own subject."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what preaching from experience, tradition and a formula does to the man underneath it. It does not produce repentance. It produces a case for the defence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what it turns into"}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the next chapter he is looking down on men whose fathers he *would not have trusted even with my sheepdogs.* See the note on Job 30:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bitterness and the pride arrive together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is still in it at the end of chapter 31: *let the Almighty answer me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the Almighty does, out of a whirlwind, and Job's answer is *I have heard of You by the hearing of the ear, but now my eye sees You.* The robe he wrapped himself in comes off the moment he actually sees Him. See the note on Job 42:1–3."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the chapter is here.** It is not a villain's speech. Every claim in it is true, and God will publicly call him *My servant Job* four times over in chapter 42. It is what a genuinely good man sounds like after twenty-five chapters of being told to justify himself."}]}, "Job 29:21–25": {"title": "\"They waited for me as for the rain\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Job remembering what his life used to be, and the memory is about how people listened to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They waited for me as for the rain, and opened their mouths wide as for the spring showers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **spring** showers — the latter rain, the one that filled out the grain just before harvest. See the note on Deuteronomy 11:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mouths open, upturned, waiting for something that only comes from above and cannot be summoned. That is how he says people used to receive what he said."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What his words did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My words fell gently on them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is the dripping or distilling of dew — the same picture Moses uses in Deuteronomy 32 of his own teaching. Not a downpour. Something that settles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the effect: *after I had spoken they had nothing to add.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did with the standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I chose the road for them and sat at their head. I lived like a king among his troops, **like one who comforts those who mourn**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last phrase turns the whole passage. He has been describing authority — men falling silent, princes deferring, a seat at the head — and he ends it by comparing himself to someone consoling the grieving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is his own account of what the position was for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the chapter exists"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job is speaking in the past tense throughout. The next chapter begins *but now they mock me* — men younger than him, whose fathers he would not have put with his sheepdogs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is a man remembering, out loud, in the middle of losing everything."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he was actually like.** The verses just above this passage are his own account: he rescued the poor, was eyes to the blind and feet to the lame, took up the case of the stranger, and broke the jaws of the wicked. The book's assessment of him at the start — blameless and upright — is not contradicted by anything he says here."}]}, "Job 3:24–26": {"title": "What I feared has found me", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of the first thing Job says after seven days of silence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they sat down with him on the ground **seven days and seven nights**, and no one spoke a word to him, **for they saw that his grief was very great**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The friends do the right thing for a week. It is the last thing they get right."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who breaks it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After this Job opened his mouth and cursed the day of his birth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not God. The day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"May the day perish on which I was born… **may that day be darkness**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Why did I not die at birth?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For now I would have lain still and been quiet, I would have slept; then I would have been at rest.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three chapters after being called *blameless and upright*, and one chapter after *in all this Job did not sin with his lips*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The eating"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **my sighing comes before I even eat**, and **my groans pour out of me like water**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **liphnei lachmi anchati** — before my bread comes my sighing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The groaning arrives ahead of the meal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The thing I dreaded **has come over me**. What I feared **has found me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **pachad pachadti vayye'etayeni** — a dread I dreaded, and it came upon me."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Vaasher yagorti yavo li** — and what I was afraid of has come to me."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it says about him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was afraid of this before it happened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 1 records what he did about it: *for Job said, \"It may be that my sons have sinned and cursed God in their hearts.\" Thus Job did regularly.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He rose early and offered burnt offerings for children who had done nothing wrong, in case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the verse does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That the fear caused it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job is describing what happened, not diagnosing it. The book's own account of the cause is in chapter 1, and it is not fear."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reader has been told. Job never is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three denials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have **no peace**, **no quiet**, **no rest**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lo shalavti velo shaqateti velo nachti**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs of stillness, each negated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Only trouble comes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyavo rogez** — and turmoil came."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Rogez** — agitation, trembling, raging. The word he uses of the wicked in chapter 3: *there the wicked cease from troubling*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked for the place where **rogez** stops, and the chapter ends with it arriving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eliphaz answers, and the first cycle of speeches begins. Three friends, three rounds, and increasingly little sympathy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Remember now, who ever perished being innocent?\"* See the note on Job 4:6–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Job says later in the book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Though He slay me, yet will I trust Him.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **I know that my Redeemer lives**, and He shall stand at last on the earth.\"* See the note on Job 19:25–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have heard of You by the hearing of the ear, **but now my eye sees You**.\"* See the note on Job 42:1–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the book leaves the question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Unanswered, in the terms Job asked it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God's reply is four chapters of questions about the natural world, and the reason for the suffering is never disclosed to the man it happened to."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the verse does and does not say.** *What I feared has found me* — a description, from a man who had been offering sacrifices in case. The book's account of the cause is elsewhere, and he never hears it."}]}, "Job 30:1–4": {"title": "Laughed at by the lowest", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The chapter turns on two words, and then Job says something he would not have said in chapter 1."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"But now\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 29 was *oh, that I were as in months past*. Chapter 30 begins *but now*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two chapters are a hinge — everything he was, and everything he is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Those younger than I make sport of me, men whose fathers **I would not have trusted even with my sheepdogs**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the line. He is not describing what they did to him; he is placing them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their fathers were beneath the dogs that watched his flock."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they actually are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the next three verses, because Job describes them himself:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Gaunt from want and hunger, they gnawed the dry ground… They picked salt herbs among the bushes, and the roots of the broom tree were their bread.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Starving people. Eating roots and weeds in waste ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who was *eyes to the blind and feet to the lame*, *a father to the needy*, is looking at the destitute and measuring them against his sheepdogs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has been done to him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is chapter 30 of a book in which three men have spent twenty-five chapters telling him his ruin is his own fault."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man forced to defend himself for that long ends up with a case, and the case needs someone to be beneath him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bitterness in chapter 30 and the self-justification in chapter 29 are the same thing looked at from two sides, and both of them arrived after his comforters started talking. He did not sound like this in chapter 1 or 2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The honesty of the book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here is tidied up. The writer records the greatest man in the East sneering at starving men and does not annotate it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job has to hear from God before this comes off him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the whole speech is going.** By the end of the chapter: *I cry out to You, and You do not answer me… I am a brother of jackals and a companion of ostriches… my harp is turned to mourning.* He is not enjoying the contempt he is handing out. He is a man in pieces, and the pieces cut in every direction."}]}, "Job 32:1–5": {"title": "The three fall quiet", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-nine chapters of argument end because everyone has run out of words, and a young man who has been sitting there the whole time stands up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the three stopped"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There was nothing left to say, because **in Job's own eyes he was in the right**, and no argument of theirs could move him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the writer's own summary, not a friend's accusation. After everything they threw at him, Job's settled position is that he is right."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said it himself in chapter 29 — *I wrapped myself in righteousness, and it clothed me* — and in that speech he refers to himself well over forty times and to God about five. See the note on Job 29:14–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three men's preaching did not humble him. It hardened him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Elihu"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But there was a young man sitting by, **listening to it all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has been present since chapter 3 and this is the first the reader hears of him. Nine chapters of speeches, and he was in the room for every one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Elihu, son of Barachel the Buzite, from the family of Ram.* The fullest genealogy given to anyone in the book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His name means *He is my God*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is angry at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things, and they are stated separately:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*angry at **Job** for making himself out to be more in the right than God*", "*angry at **the three friends** too, because they had found no real answer, yet they had gone right on condemning Job anyway*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the only person in the book who is against both sides of the argument. And his charge against the three is precise: they had no answer, and they kept condemning anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he waited"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All this time Elihu had **held his tongue, waiting**, because the others were older than he was and it seemed only fitting.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nine chapters of silence out of respect."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elihu speaks for six chapters. Nobody interrupts him. Job — who answered every one of the other three, at length, ten times over — does not say a word back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And when Elihu finishes, the next verse is *then the LORD answered Job out of the whirlwind.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says he is not doing.** *I will not show partiality to anyone, nor flatter any man, for I do not know how to flatter — my Maker would soon take me away.* The three came to defend a system. He says at the outset that he has no side."}]}, "Job 33:14–18": {"title": "\"He speaks in a dream\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Elihu's first claim is that the silence Job has been complaining about is not silence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Job had said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold, I go forward, but He is not there; and backward, but I cannot perceive Him.* And: *I cry out to You, but You do not answer me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty chapters of a man saying God will not speak to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Elihu's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The truth is, **God does speak**. He speaks **one way, then another**, and people simply **miss it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that God has spoken once and stopped. Repeatedly, in different modes — and the failure named is on the hearing end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that man does not perceive it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He speaks **in a dream, in a vision of the night**, when **deep sleep settles over everyone** and they are drowsing on their beds.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *deep sleep* is **tardemah** — the same word used of Adam when the woman was taken from his side, and of Abram when the covenant was cut and only the fire passed between the pieces."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both of those are occasions when God did something to a man who could not participate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That is when **He opens their ears** and presses His warning home.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *He uncovers the ear* — the idiom for disclosing something privately, the way you lift a man's hair to speak to him quietly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ear is described as needing opening. It is not that the speech was too faint."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three purposes, all protective:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*to **turn a person back from some reckless plan***", "*to **keep pride from swallowing him whole***", "*to **hold a soul back from the pit**, to keep a life from crossing over by the sword*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here is punitive. Every clause is about stopping somebody before something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The alternative Elihu offers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the first of two ways he says God speaks. The second is the sickbed:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Or He speaks through pain, laying a person low on his bed with an ache that never leaves his bones.\"* See the note on Job 33:19–22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the messenger, and the ransom found. See the note on Job 33:23–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who else was spoken to in the night"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob, on a stone, with a stairway and the covenant. Solomon at Gibeon, given wisdom in a dream. Joseph, told four times in dreams what to do about a child. Paul, told *do not be afraid, but speak* and *this night the angel of God stood by me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Scripture is full of it, and Elihu says it is a standing arrangement rather than a set of exceptions."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Elihu is doing that the three friends did not.** He tells Job that God is already talking. The others explained Job's suffering. He says the silence is not what it appears to be, and then names a mediator."}]}, "Job 33:19–22": {"title": "The man on the bed", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Elihu describes a man at the end of himself, and the passage exists to set up what comes next."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it looks like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"An ache that never leaves his bones. Now **the sight of food turns his stomach**; even the finest dish is nothing to him. His flesh wastes away until you can hardly see it, and **the bones that were hidden now jut out**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a clinical description, and it is unsparing. Constant pain, no appetite, visible wasting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whoever wrote this had watched somebody go through it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is heading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"His soul drifts toward the grave. His life inches toward **the messengers of death**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is the destroyers, the ones who bring death. He is not dying quickly. He is being carried there by degrees."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word the whole passage waits for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse begins: *\"**Yet even then**, if there is a messenger at his side…\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in these four verses is the *before*. It is written to be the darkest possible position, so that the *yet even then* has something to stand against."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which messenger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Notice the two in the passage, and that they are the same Hebrew word."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["verse 22 — the **messengers** of death, carrying him toward the pit", "verse 23 — the **messenger** at his side, one in a thousand, who speaks for him"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same noun, and they are pulling in opposite directions over one man on a bed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that messenger does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not tell the man what he did wrong. He does not prescribe a remedy or take a history."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He shows him God's righteousness, and God answers: *I have found the price for his release.* See the note on Job 33:23–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then his flesh grows fresh again, softer than a child's.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "From bones jutting out to skin like a child's, and the thing that stands between the two descriptions is a ransom found by God. See the note on Job 33:25–28."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why Elihu is describing this at all.** He is talking to a man scraping boils with a piece of broken pot. The three friends looked at Job and produced an accusation. Elihu looks at the same man and produces a mediator."}]}, "Job 33:23–24": {"title": "\"I have found the price for his release\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The turning point of the whole book, spoken by a young man nobody had asked, and it is the answer to everything the three friends could not solve."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elihu has just described a man on a sickbed — flesh wasting, bones jutting out, his soul drifting toward the grave, his life inching toward the messengers of death. See the note on Job 33:19–22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *Yet even then, **if there is a messenger at his side**…*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one in a thousand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One who **speaks for him**, one in a thousand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a **melitz** — an interpreter, a spokesman, a go-between. It is the word used of the interpreter standing between Joseph and his brothers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *one in a thousand* — there is one such person, and He is not common."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is exactly what Job had been crying out for. In chapter 9: *He is not a man, as I am, that I should answer Him… **there is no umpire between us**, who might lay his hand on us both.* In chapter 16: *my witness is in heaven, my advocate is on high.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job knew what was missing. Elihu names it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the messenger does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **remind this person of what is right and true**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to examine him. Not to search out his sin, prescribe steps, or diagnose the cause — which is what all three friends spent twenty-five chapters doing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to declare to the man His **uprightness** — God's righteousness, shown to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The messenger's job is to show a suffering man what God is like. That is the whole commission."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then God speaks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **God is gracious to him** and says, \"Spare him. Do not let him go down into the pit. **I have found the price for his release**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *gracious* is **chanan** — the verb behind *chen*, grace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ransom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have found the price for his release.* The Hebrew is **kopher** — a ransom, a covering, the price paid to redeem a life that was forfeit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same root as **kippur**, the covering of the Day of Atonement."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read who does the finding. Not the sick man. Not the friends. **I have found it.** God locates the ransom Himself, and announces it as the reason the man is spared."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no condition attached to it in Elihu's sentence. A messenger shows him God's righteousness; God says a price has been found; the man does not go down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is on the other side of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *then his flesh grows fresh again, softer than a child's. He returns to the strength of his young days.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The healing follows the ransom. It does not produce it. See the note on Job 33:25–28."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who the messenger is.** *There is one God and one Mediator between God and men, the Man Christ Jesus, who gave Himself a ransom for all.* One God, one Mediator, one ransom — and Elihu says it in a book that has no priest, no temple and no covenant in it anywhere."}]}, "Job 33:25–28": {"title": "\"Softer than a child's\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Elihu is speaking — the young man who waited for the three friends to finish — and this is the best passage in his long speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The restoration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then his flesh grows fresh again, softer than a child's. He returns to the strength of his young days.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same picture as Naaman coming up out of the Jordan with skin like a little boy's. Not repaired. Made new."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the second line adds the strength back — not merely healed, but restored to what he could once do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the word \"then\" is attached to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then* is the first word of the passage, and it is pointing back at something."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elihu has described a man brought to the edge — laid low on his bed, his flesh wasting until you can hardly see it, his life inching toward the messengers of death. Then a messenger comes and shows him God's righteousness, and God says: *I have found the price for his release.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Then** his flesh grows fresh again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is fixed and it does not run the other way. Ransom found, and the renewal follows it. Nothing in these four verses is produced by the sick man's effort — he is on a bed, unable to eat, and a price is found for him by Somebody else. See the note on Job 33:23–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *ransom* is **kopher**, a covering, the price paid to buy back a forfeited life — and it is what the New Testament is built on: *the Son of Man came to give His life a ransom for many.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the man then does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He prays to God, and God welcomes him with delight. He looks on God's face and shouts for joy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things: prayer, a welcome, and then his face lifted. The Hebrew for *welcomes him with delight* is the word for accepting a sacrifice as pleasing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The confession he makes afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I sinned. I twisted what was straight, and it gained me nothing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It comes **after** the rescue, not before it — he is singing it to everyone as testimony. The order in Elihu's account is: ransom found, life restored, and then the honest account of what he had done."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who Elihu was.** God's verdict at the end names Eliphaz, Bildad and Zophar and tells them they have not spoken what is right. Elihu is never rebuked, never corrected, and never answered by Job — who argued with all three of the others and has nothing to say to this one. He is the only voice in the book that speaks of a mediator and a ransom, and the storm that brings God's own voice comes immediately after he has finished."}]}, "Job 35:9–13": {"title": "\"Who fills the night with songs\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Elihu names a question nobody thinks to ask, and the description of God inside it is the best line in his speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People **groan under the weight of oppression**. They cry out under the crushing arm of the powerful.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crying is real and the oppression is real. Elihu is not disputing either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is missing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **no one thinks to say, \"Where is God my Maker?\"**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They cry out **because** of the pressure. Nobody cries out **to** anyone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the distinction the passage turns on — the difference between a noise pain makes and a question addressed to somebody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The One who **fills the night with songs**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *gives songs in the night*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not takes the night away. Not explains it. Gives something to sing in the middle of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Songs are what this book's God is said to hand out in the dark, and Elihu offers it as a reason to ask where He is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Who **teaches us more than the beasts of the field** and **makes us wiser than the birds of the sky**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Animals cry when they are hurt. That much requires nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elihu's point is that people were given something the animals were not, and in trouble they use the part they share with the cattle and neglect the part that is theirs alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the answer does not come"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They cry out, but **no answer comes** — because of the arrogance of wicked people. **It is all empty noise**, and God will not listen to emptiness.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *vanity* — the same root as Ecclesiastes' *hebel*, vapour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elihu is describing a cry with nothing in it and nobody in mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elihu, the young man who waited through nine chapters for the three friends to finish, and who is never rebuked by God at the end of the book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is also the one who says *if there is a messenger at his side, one who speaks for him, one in a thousand… I have found the price for his release.* See the note on Job 33:23–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where songs in the night turn up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul and Silas, backs opened by rods, feet in the stocks in the inner prison: *at midnight Paul and Silas were praying and singing hymns to God, and the prisoners were listening to them.* See the note on Acts 16:29–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the psalm: *the LORD will command His lovingkindness in the daytime, and **in the night His song shall be with me**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the whole book has been about.** Job groaning, and Job's friends explaining it, and Job asking for an umpire. Elihu's contribution is that the groaning could be addressed to somebody — and God answers out of the whirlwind the moment he stops talking."}]}, "Job 36:8–12": {"title": "Elihu on the cords of affliction", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The fourth speaker in the book, and the only one God does not rebuke by name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Elihu is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He appears in chapter 32, out of nowhere, after the three friends *ceased answering Job, because he was righteous in his own eyes*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is young — *I am young in years, and you are very old; therefore I was afraid, and dared not declare my opinion to you* — and he has been waiting through thirty-one chapters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His anger is stated twice: against Job *because he justified himself rather than God*, and against the three friends *because they had found no answer, and yet had condemned Job*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He speaks for six chapters and nobody answers him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is describing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And if any of them end up **bound in chains, caught tight in the cords of affliction**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *held in cords of affliction* — the picture of a man tied."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says God does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then God **shows them plainly what they have done**, the way **their pride carried them too far**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He **opens their ears to correction** and **calls them to turn back**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *He opens their ear to instruction*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs about hearing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two outcomes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If they listen and serve Him**, they will **live out their days in good things**, their years in **real contentment**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *in pleasantness* — **neimim**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **if they will not listen**, they **cross over by the sword** and **die still knowing nothing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *they die without knowledge*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elihu gets right"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three friends had argued backwards from Job's suffering to his guilt. Elihu's argument is different: he says affliction can be instructional rather than punitive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He **delivers the poor in their affliction**, and **opens their ears in oppression**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And earlier: *\"For God may speak in one way, or in another, yet man does not perceive it.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elihu also does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Applies the general principle to a man the reader knows is not guilty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reader has read chapter 1 and 2. Job's friends have not, and neither has Elihu."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every speech in the book is made by men working without the information the reader was given on the first page. See the note on Job 1:6–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To Eliphaz: *My wrath is aroused against you and your two friends, for **you have not spoken of Me what is right**, as My servant Job has.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Your two friends** — three men are named. Elihu is not among them, and he is not addressed in the epilogue at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book leaves him alone, which readers have argued about for a very long time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after his speeches"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the LORD answered Job **out of the whirlwind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elihu's last chapter is a description of a gathering storm — lightning, thunder, clouds turning — and then the storm speaks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God's answer consists of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Questions. Seventy of them, about wild goats, ostriches, the war horse, the hawk, the sea, snow, and Orion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of them is about suffering."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Elihu contributes.** A category the other three did not have — that affliction can open an ear rather than settle an account. And like all of them, he applies it to a man whose file he has not read."}]}, "Job 37:6–10": {"title": "\"By the breath of God the ice is formed\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Elihu's last speech, and he spends it on weather."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He says to **the snow**, 'Fall on the earth,' and to **the rain**, 'Pour down.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Weather addressed as something under orders."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elihu is building toward the whirlwind — God answers Job out of one in the very next chapter, and Elihu is describing storms when it arrives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What snow does to work"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He **seals up every hand from its work**, so that all people He has made **may stop and know it is His doing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that He puts a seal on the hand of every man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A heavy snowfall stops the farming, the building, the travelling. Nobody works."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Elihu gives a purpose for it: so that people stop, and know."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only place in Scripture that offers a reason for winter, and the reason is that it makes everyone put their tools down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The animals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The wild animals **slip into their dens** and stay in their shelters.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The observation is exact. Animals go to ground before weather, and Elihu notes it as part of the same obedience."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The whirlwind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Out of its chamber comes **the whirlwind**, and the biting cold rides in on the driving winds.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *chamber* is an inner room, a storehouse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elihu speaks of a whirlwind having a room it comes out of, and then God speaks to Job *out of the whirlwind.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**By the breath of God the ice is formed**, and the wide waters freeze solid.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *by the breath of God ice is given*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Freezing is a loss of heat, and Elihu attributes it to breath — which is counter-intuitive, since breath is warm. But the Hebrew **ruach** is also wind, and it is wind off cold ground that freezes standing water."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elihu is doing with all this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter ends: *the Almighty — **we cannot find Him out**; He is excellent in power, in judgement and abundant justice; He does not oppress.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is arguing from snow, rain, ice and thunder to a God who is beyond working out, and whose power is not oppressive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the whirlwind arrives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where God takes the same subject"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Have you entered the treasury of the snow, or have you seen the treasury of the hail?\"* — Job 38, a chapter later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God asks about the same storehouses Elihu mentioned, and asks Job whether he has been inside them. See the note on Job 38:4–7."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last thing Elihu says before God speaks.** *Therefore men fear Him; He shows no partiality to any who are wise of heart.* And then, mid-storm, the LORD answers — and never rebukes Elihu, though He rebukes the other three by name."}]}, "Job 38:1–3": {"title": "God begins to speak", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Thirty-five chapters of silence, and then He speaks — and what He says is not what anyone in the book was expecting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Out of the storm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He spoke **out of the storm**, out of the wind and the wild weather.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is the whirlwind, the tempest. It is how He came for Elijah too."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not answer a single one of Job's questions. Not one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not explain the boils. He does not mention the throne room, the accuser, or the wager. He does not defend Himself against the charge Job has been laying for thirty chapters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He does not confirm the friends' diagnosis either. There is no list of Job's sins, because there is no list."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have questions for you**, and it is your turn to answer Me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks. Four chapters of questions — seventy-odd of them — and almost every one is about something Job has never had anything to do with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Where were you when I laid the earth's foundations. Have you entered the springs of the sea. Have you commanded the morning. Who provides food for the raven. Do you know when the mountain goats give birth. Can you bind the Pleiades."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of them is about Job's conduct."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Brace yourself like a strong man\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *gird up your loins* — pull your robe through your belt so you can move."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not gentle and it is not a rebuke either. He is telling Job to stand up. Job has been on an ash heap for thirty-five chapters and God's first instruction is to get on his feet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Words that carry no knowledge\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Who is this who **muddies My counsel** with words that carry no knowledge?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *muddies* is to darken, to obscure — the same picture as silt stirred up in clear water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job's answer, when it comes, is to agree: *it was me.* See the note on Job 42:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the questions are actually doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole speech is about the ostrich and the wild donkey and the storehouses of snow and the horse that laughs at fear. It is a tour of things being cared for that no one is watching."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The raven's chicks are fed. The mountain goats calve without a midwife. The rain falls *on a land where no one lives, on a desert with no one in it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He answers a man who thinks he has been abandoned by showing him how much is being looked after where nobody can see."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Job says he gets out of it.** *I have heard of You by the hearing of the ear, but now my eye sees You.* Not an explanation. He never gets one. He gets the One he had been asking to see."}]}, "Job 38:31–33": {"title": "The constellations in their seasons", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God asks Job about specific star groups by name, and asks whether he can steer them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Can you bind the beautiful cords of **the Pleiades**, or loosen **the belt of Orion**? Can you lead out **the constellations** in their seasons, or guide **the Bear with her cubs** across the sky?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four named groups, in a book generally reckoned the oldest in the Bible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whoever wrote Job knew the night sky well enough to name its features and expect the reader to recognise them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Pleiades"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bind the beautiful cords.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **Kimah**, and the word rendered *cords* is a word for sweet influences or chains — a bond."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Pleiades are a genuine cluster. The stars in it are gravitationally associated and move together through space, which is not true of most constellations, where the stars are unrelated and only appear near each other from here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question asks about binding something that is, in fact, bound."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Orion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Loosen **the belt** of Orion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **Kesil**, and the word is *bands* — the cords that tie him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Orion's belt is the most recognisable line of stars in the sky, and the three are drifting apart. In tens of thousands of years the belt will not be a belt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bound, and loosening. The two questions are exact opposites and both are put to Job."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The constellations"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Can you **lead out the constellations in their seasons**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **Mazzaroth** — a word that appears once in Scripture, here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is generally taken as the twelve signs, the zodiac band the sun travels through across a year, and *in their seasons* is what they do: each one rises at its own time and gives way to the next."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They come out in order, and Job is asked whether he brings them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Bear"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Or **guide the Bear with her cubs**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ayish** and her sons — the great bear, and the stars around her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb is to lead, to guide, the word used of a shepherd with a flock."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The laws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do you know **the laws that govern the heavens**? Could you **set their rule over the earth**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *laws* is **chuqqot** — statutes, fixed decrees. The same word used of the law given at Sinai."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heavens are described as running on statutes, and having a governing effect on the earth below — which, in the plainest sense, they do: seasons, tides, the length of a day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 38, out of the whirlwind, in the middle of four chapters of questions about the sea's doors, the storehouses of snow, the ostrich, the wild donkey and the horse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of them is about Job's conduct. See the note on Job 38:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sky is doing, according to Scripture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The heavens are telling the glory of God… there is no speech, there are no words… yet their sound goes out through all the earth.* See the note on Psalms 19:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Speech with no language, going out to everybody."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What God says the stars did at the beginning.** *When the morning stars sang together and all the sons of God shouted for joy* — five verses earlier in this same speech. He asks Job about their orbits, and mentions that they sang. See the note on Job 38:4–7."}]}, "Job 38:4–7": {"title": "\"When the morning stars sang together\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God's first question out of the whirlwind, and it is about a construction site."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Where were you** when I laid the foundations of the earth? Tell Me, if you understand so much.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job has been demanding an audience for thirty chapters. He gets one, and it opens with a question he cannot begin to answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vocabulary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who **marked out its measurements**? Who **stretched the line** across it and set it true? On what were **its footings** sunk? Who laid **its cornerstone**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every term is a builder's term — the measuring line, the footings, the cornerstone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God describes creation as a job of work with a plan and a surveyor."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was watching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"…that morning when **the stars sang together and all the sons of God shouted for joy**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two groups, or one described twice in Hebrew parallel: the morning stars, and the sons of God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sons of God** — **bene ha-elohim**. In Job the phrase means angels; the same expression opens chapter 1, where *the sons of God came to present themselves before the LORD, and the adversary came among them too.* See the note on Job 1:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were present at the founding of the earth, and they were singing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that dates"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Angels existed before the earth did. They watched it being laid."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which places them before Genesis 1:1 in the order of things, and puts a spectator's gallery at the creation account."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sang** and **shouted**. The Hebrew for the second is the shout of triumph — the noise Israel made when the walls of Jericho fell, and when the ark came into the camp."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the passage is quiet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is God's opening move"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job's friends had argued about causes for thirty chapters. God does not enter that argument."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks about the morning of the world, and then goes on for four chapters about the sea's doors, the storehouses of snow, the ostrich, the wild donkey, and the horse that laughs at fear. See the note on Job 38:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of the questions is about Job's conduct."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the singing means for the man on the ash heap"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is being shown a world that was made deliberately, measured, footed, and cornerstoned, and cheered while it went up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer to *why is this happening to me* is never given. What is given is the architect."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the cornerstone turns up again.** *The stone which the builders rejected has become the chief cornerstone.* Job hears about the cornerstone of the earth; the Psalms and the Gospels name one the builders threw out."}]}, "Job 4:7–11": {"title": "\"Eliphaz, and what he had seen\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first friend to speak, and the ground he stands on is his own observation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where his authority comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What I have seen** is this: those who plough trouble and plant sorrow harvest exactly that.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is his source. Not a word from God, not a Scripture — what he has watched happen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the same thing again a few verses later: *a word was secretly brought to me… a spirit passed before my face, and the hair of my flesh stood up.* A vision in the night, a voice he could not identify."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Experience is not the mother of wisdom. The Word of God is. A man who preaches from what he has personally seen will build a rule out of a sample, and then apply the rule to a man he does not understand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Name one honest person who was ever destroyed for nothing.** Where were the upright ever cut down?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It sounds unanswerable. It is a rhetorical question with an obvious answer, put to a man sitting in ashes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer is sitting in front of him. Job is the one honest person destroyed for nothing, and Eliphaz cannot see it because his rule will not let him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the rule does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It converts the suffering into evidence. Once you hold *the upright are never cut down*, then a man who is cut down cannot be upright, and every day he stays in the ashes is another day of proof."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the machine Job is now inside for twenty-five chapters, and all three friends are feeding it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Sowing and reaping"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Those who plough trouble and plant sorrow harvest exactly that.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a true principle. Paul states it — *whatever a man sows, that he will also reap.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "True, and applied to the wrong man, in the wrong situation, at the worst possible hour. The problem with Eliphaz is not that what he says is false in general. It is that it is false about Job, and he says it anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four lines about lions with their teeth broken and their cubs scattered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is describing the fate of the wicked, in poetry, to a man whose ten children were killed a fortnight ago."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says about him at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"My wrath is aroused against you and your two friends, for **you have not spoken of Me what is right**, as My servant Job has.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said directly to Eliphaz, by name, in chapter 42. He is the first of the three to speak and the one God addresses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever else the three of them got wrong, God's own summary of the charge is that they misrepresented **Him**."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the friends thought they were defending.** God's justice. They spent twenty-five chapters protecting His reputation, and the verdict at the end is that they had not said one right thing about Him. See the note on Job 42:7–8."}]}, "Job 40:6–8": {"title": "\"Would you condemn Me so that you could be in the right?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second speech from the whirlwind, and it contains the only direct charge God lays against Job in the entire book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Would you really cancel My justice? Would you condemn Me so that you could be in the right?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is it. Forty-two chapters, and this is the whole indictment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the boils. Not any sin the friends dug for. Not one item off Eliphaz's, Bildad's or Zophar's lists — because none of them was there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The charge is that Job put God in the wrong in order to be in the right himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What \"cancel My justice\" means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to break, to annul, to make void — the word used of breaking a covenant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job's defence had required a verdict on God. If Job is innocent and God sent the suffering, then God is unjust. That is the only place the argument could go, and Job followed it there: *it is God who has bent me down and drawn His net tight around me.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The choice in the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There are two positions and only one of them can be occupied. Either God is right, or I am."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job had spent thirty chapters taking the second one — and every man does, by instinct. Being wrong is the harder of the two to say out loud."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David took the other one: *against You, You only, have I sinned… **so You are right when You speak, You are clear when You judge**.* He wrote it after the worst thing he ever did. See the note on Psalms 51:3–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole difference. One man justifies himself and God ends up in the dock; the other agrees God is right and finds himself in the clear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the self-justifying came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job did not start here. Chapter 1: *the LORD gave and the LORD has taken away; blessed be the name of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It came out of him after twenty-five chapters of men telling him to account for himself. A man who is made to defend himself long enough ends up with a case, and a case needs a defendant on the other side. See the note on Job 29:14–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Brace yourself again\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same words as chapter 38 — *stand up now, brace yourself like a strong man.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is being stood up a second time, not knocked down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Job says back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I know that You can do all things… I spoke of things I did not understand. **It was me.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes the other position, and the book turns over in that sentence. See the note on Job 42:1–3."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where Paul lands in the same place.** *For I know that nothing good lives in me — that is, in the old nature.* It is not despair; it is the end of an argument. A man who has settled that stops trying to build a defence, and can take a righteousness that was given to him. See the note on Romans 7:18–20."}]}, "Job 42:1–3": {"title": "\"It was me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Job's last speech, and it is six verses long. He has been talking for thirty chapters and this is what he has left."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He quotes the question back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You asked, '**Who is this who hides My counsel without knowledge?**'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God had put it as a question in chapter 38 and Job repeats it word for word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It was me.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words. There is no negotiation, no *but*, no explanation of the circumstances."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not plead the ash heap, the ten graves, the boils or the friends. He answers the question that was asked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he admits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I spoke of things I did not understand, wonders far too great for me, things I did not know.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that he sinned in a way that caused the disaster — that was the friends' charge and it was never true, and God will say so in five verses' time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What he concedes is that he talked about something he could not see, and formed a verdict on the One running it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he stops doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last thing he says in the passage that follows is *I abhor myself, and repent in dust and ashes*, and the Hebrew of that first phrase is difficult — it can be read *I retract*, or *I melt away*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way the case is withdrawn. Chapter 29 was a man wrapping himself in his own righteousness; here he takes it off. See the note on Job 29:14–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now I know that **You can do all things**, and that **no purpose of Yours can ever be stopped**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has agreed God is right, and the first thing he says after it is about God's ability rather than his own failure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never learns why. Not one word about the accuser, the throne room, or the wager, then or ever."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What actually changed him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have heard of You by the hearing of the ear, **but now my eye sees You**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an argument. Four chapters of ostriches and rainstorms and wild goats calving, and a Person on the other side of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three friends had reasoned at him for twenty-five chapters and hardened him. He sees Him once and the whole defence drops."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes immediately after.** Not a rebuke. God turns to Eliphaz and calls Job *My servant* four times in two verses, and tells the three friends that Job is the one who spoke rightly of Him. See the note on Job 42:7–8."}]}, "Job 42:10": {"title": "\"When Job prayed for his friends\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and the turn of it is in the first four words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trigger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **when Job prayed for his friends**, the LORD restored everything he had lost.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not when he repented — that was four verses earlier and nothing was returned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not when he understood — he never did."}, {"k": "p", "t": "When he prayed for **them**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eliphaz, who told him no innocent man was ever destroyed. Bildad, who said his children got what they deserved. Zophar, who told him he was getting off lightly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-five chapters of it. He had called them *miserable comforters* and told them they were grinding him to pieces with words, and he was right about all of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Those are the three he is told to pray for, and God will not forgive them without it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He still had nothing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the order of the chapter. Job prays while he is still on the ash heap. The sheep are still dead, the children are still buried, the boils are still on him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not praying out of an abundance. He has nothing to give but the prayer, and he gives it to the men who hurt him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD **restored** everything he had lost.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *turned the captivity of Job* — the same phrase used of God bringing Israel back from exile."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is release language. He had been held."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And gave him **twice as much** as he had before.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not restored to level. Doubled, and the numbers in verse 12 confirm it item by item against chapter 1: seven thousand sheep become fourteen thousand, three thousand camels become six thousand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What did not wait"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the verse says Job's own situation had to improve before he could pray for theirs. It says the reverse — the praying came first, and the restoring followed it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He interceded for men who did not deserve it, out of a position where he had every reason not to, and that is the sentence the whole book turns on."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one thing that is not doubled.** He had seven sons and three daughters, and he ends with seven sons and three daughters. The livestock doubles; the children do not. Readers have always noticed it, and the usual reading is the kind one — the first ten were not lost to him."}]}, "Job 42:12–15": {"title": "Twice as much as before", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last picture in the book, and the writer takes his time over the details."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Checked against chapter 1"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["7,000 sheep → **14,000**", "3,000 camels → **6,000**", "500 yoke of oxen → **1,000**", "500 donkeys → **1,000**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every figure exactly doubled. The numbers in chapter 1 were given so this could be counted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD blessed **the last part of Job's life even more than the first**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The daughters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He named the first daughter **Jemimah**, the second **Keziah**, and the third **Keren-happuch**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sons are counted; the daughters are named. In the whole of Job the only people given personal names besides Job, his friends and Elihu are these three girls."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The names are all about beauty and scent — **Jemimah** is dove, **Keziah** is cassia, the fragrant bark, and **Keren-happuch** is *horn of eye-paint*, the flask of dark cosmetic a woman kept."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man came out of the ashes and named his daughters after perfume and a dove."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"No women as lovely as Job's daughters\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In all the land there were **no women as lovely** as Job's daughters.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer states it as fact, in the same breath as the livestock count. It is part of the blessing being listed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Beauty is something God gives, and it is written into the ledger here alongside the sheep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inheritance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And their father gave them **an inheritance among their brothers, right alongside the sons**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is not how it was done. Daughters inherited only where there were no sons — the case of Zelophehad's daughters in Numbers 27 had to be brought to Moses specially."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job had seven sons. He gave the girls a share anyway, as equals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who has lost everything and been given it back twice over is not careful about the rules for dividing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Job died, old and full of days.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *satisfied with days*. He had enough."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He saw four generations — *his children and their children to the fourth generation* — which is one hundred and forty more years after the ash heap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is still missing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is never told why. Not in these verses, not anywhere."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gets his life back doubled, four generations of grandchildren, three beautiful daughters with an inheritance, and no explanation at all."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What James says the end of it proves.** *You have heard how Job held out, and you have seen what the Lord finally made of it — that He feels for people deeply, and He is kind all the way through.* The New Testament's verdict on this chapter is not about endurance. It is about what God turned out to be. See the note on James 5:10–11."}]}, "Job 42:16–17": {"title": "Four generations", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last two verses of the book, and the accounting is done in faces."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whirlwind, and four chapters of questions about wild goats, the ostrich, the horse, Behemoth and Leviathan."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have heard of You by the hearing of the ear, **but now my eye sees You**.* See the note on Job 42:1–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the rebuke to the friends: *My wrath is aroused against you… **for you have not spoken of Me what is right, as My servant Job has***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job prays for them, and *the LORD restored Job's losses when he prayed for his friends*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The doubling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Indeed the LORD gave Job **twice as much as he had before**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fourteen thousand sheep, six thousand camels, a thousand yoke of oxen, a thousand female donkeys. Exactly double the opening inventory."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The children"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He also had **seven sons and three daughters**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not doubled. The same number as before."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book does not say why, and the arithmetic is the kind readers notice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The daughters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he called the name of the first **Jemimah**, the name of the second **Keziah**, and the name of the third **Keren-Happuch**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dove, cassia, and horn of eye-paint."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sons are not named. The daughters are, and the text adds *in all the land were found no women so beautiful as the daughters of Job; and their father **gave them an inheritance among their brothers***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which was not the ordinary practice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The years"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After all this Job lived **a hundred and forty years**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew says nothing about his age when the trouble came. If a hundred and forty is what came after, the whole disaster occupies a fraction of the life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he saw **his children, and his children's children, four generations of them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **arba'ah dorot** — four generations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Great-great-grandchildren."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase added"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faces full of the future.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Illumination's rendering of what the Hebrew leaves implicit — a man looking at four rows of descendants."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Job grew old, **full of days and full of gladness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **zaqen usava yamim** — old and satisfied of days."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase used of Abraham, of Isaac, and of David. See the note on Genesis 25:7–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And at last he died, **an old man whose end was richer than his beginning**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the Illumination drawing out what verse 12 says: *the LORD blessed the latter days of Job more than his beginning*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not restored"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ten children who died."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The new seven and three do not replace them. The book gives him more years, more livestock, and more descendants, and does not pretend the first ten were returned."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he never learns"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reader was told in chapter 1. Job is told about ostriches and rain on land where no man is, and never about the wager."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What James does with him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Indeed we count them blessed who endure. **You have heard of the perseverance of Job and seen the end intended by the Lord** — that the Lord is very compassionate and merciful.* See the note on James 5:10–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**To telos Kuriou** — the end of the Lord."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James's word for what the book's last chapter is: an ending."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The arithmetic.** The livestock is doubled exactly. The children are not — seven sons and three daughters, the same count as the ten who died, and the book does not explain the difference."}]}, "Job 42:7–8": {"title": "\"You have not spoken of Me what is right\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The verdict, and it goes the opposite way from everything the previous forty chapters seemed to be building toward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is in trouble"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My anger burns against you and your two friends.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three men who spent twenty-five chapters defending God's justice are the ones He is angry with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eliphaz is named. He spoke first and he is addressed first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because **you have not spoken of Me what is right**, as My servant Job has.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice, once at the start and once at the end, framing the whole instruction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offence is not how they treated Job — though they crushed him with words for twenty-five chapters. It is what they said about **God**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had made Him the kind of God who breaks a man to punish hidden sin, who operates a formula, who has to be defended by accusation. Every sermon they preached misrepresented Him, and that is the thing He answers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"As My servant Job has\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job, who said God had netted him, bent him down, and would not answer — Job is the one who spoke rightly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He said hard things and he said them to God's face. They said correct-sounding things about Him behind His back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *My servant Job* is used four times in these two verses. God keeps repeating the title over the man his friends had been treating as a defendant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they have to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take **seven bulls and seven rams**, and go to My servant Job, and offer them up as a burnt offering for yourselves.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven and seven — the largest sacrifice named anywhere in the book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they have to carry it to Job. Three older men driving fourteen animals across to the ash heap of the man they had been lecturing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And who has to pray"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**And My servant Job will pray for you. I will accept his prayer** and not deal with you as your foolishness deserves.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sacrifice is not enough on its own. God makes their forgiveness run through the man they wronged."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job has to intercede for Eliphaz, Bildad and Zophar. They cannot approach without him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that makes him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man standing between God and three guilty people, praying, with a burnt offering on the fire — which is precisely the office Elihu had described back in chapter 33: *a messenger at his side, one who speaks for him, one in a thousand.* See the note on Job 33:23–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what it does to Job"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The very next verse: *when Job prayed for his friends, the LORD restored everything he had lost.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not when he repented. Not when he understood. When he prayed for them. See the note on Job 42:10."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in the verdict.** Any answer. God rebukes the friends, vindicates Job, and never once explains the boils to anybody. The book ends with the question unanswered and the man restored."}]}, "Job 8:8–19": {"title": "\"Bildad, and the generations before\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second friend, and he stands on something older than himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where his authority comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Just ask the generations who came before**; consider what their ancestors learned.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is his case. Not observation, like Eliphaz — inheritance. What has always been taught."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"We were born only yesterday and know nothing.\"* He disqualifies the present in favour of the past, and then speaks with total confidence about a man in front of him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Tradition does not mean truth. Something can be handed down for four hundred years and still be the wrong word for the man in the ashes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The papyrus"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Can papyrus grow tall where there is no marsh?** Can reeds flourish without water?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A proverb, and a good one. Cut the reed off from the water and it withers faster than grass that was never green."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That is the way of everyone who forgets God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just called Job a man who forgot God, in a metaphor, without saying his name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The spider's web"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What he leans on is fragile; what he trusts is **a spider's web**. He leans on his house, and it will not hold. He grabs it, and it will not last.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The house that will not hold. Job's ten children died when a house came down on them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bildad had opened his speech in this chapter with *if your children have sinned against Him, He has cast them away for their transgression.* He is not being careless. He means it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The uprooted plant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He is like a well-watered plant in the sunlight, spreading its shoots… gripping the rocks. But **tear it from its place**, and that place disowns it: 'I never saw you.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most accurate description of Job's situation in the chapter, and it is delivered as an accusation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The greatest man in the East, spread out over the hills, torn out. Bildad has the picture exactly right and the diagnosis exactly wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the traditional wisdom actually says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bildad's inherited system is simple: God blesses the righteous and destroys the wicked, visibly, in this life, every time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is neat, and much of the Old Testament reads that way at a distance. The book of Job exists because it is not the whole story, and because a man was sitting in ashes for whom it was not true at all."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verdict on him.** *You have not spoken of Me what is right.* He quoted the fathers, he used the proverbs correctly, and God's summary of his theology was that it misrepresented Him. See the notes on Job 4:7–11 and Job 42:7–8."}]}, "Job 9:25–35": {"title": "\"There is no umpire between us\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Job asks for something that does not exist yet, and names it precisely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The speed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"My days **run faster than a courier**; they flee away and **never see anything good**. They slip by **like reed boats**, like **an eagle swooping down on its prey**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three images, each faster than the last — a runner, a papyrus skiff on a river, a stooping eagle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fastest things he could name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The attempt to cheer up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If I say, **I will forget my complaint, I will change my face and smile**, I still dread all my sufferings.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tries it and reports that it does not work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For I know **You will not treat me as innocent**. **I will be found guilty anyway**, so **why should I struggle for nothing?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *treat me as innocent* is the legal word for acquittal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His problem is not the suffering. It is that he cannot see a court in which the case could go his way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The washing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Even if I **washed myself with snow and scrubbed my hands with lye**, You would plunge me into the pit, and **my own clothes would loathe me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Snow water and lye were the strongest cleaning agents available."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is saying that self-cleaning does not reach the problem — and the image at the end is that even his clothes would find him disgusting afterward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The real difficulty"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He is not a man like me**, that I could answer Him, **that we could go to court together**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The disparity is the whole obstacle. There is no shared ground, no common footing, no venue."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**There is no umpire between us, no one to lay his hand on us both**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mokiach** — an arbiter, a referee, one who decides between two parties."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the picture he attaches is physical: someone who can put one hand on each. To do that, a person would have to be able to reach both — to be at home on both sides."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job does not know of anyone who could."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he would do if there were one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If only He would **take His rod away from me**, so His terror would stop frightening me, **then I would speak up and not be afraid of Him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He wants to speak freely, and says he cannot while afraid."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But that is not how things stand with me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He closes the speech by conceding it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the New Testament calls it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For there is **one God and one Mediator** between God and men, the Man Christ Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **mesites** — the go-between, the one standing in the middle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Hebrews adds the other half of Job's request — a High Priest *who can have compassion, since He Himself is also subject to weakness* — a man, who can be answered, and God, who can answer. See the note on Hebrews 7:20–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Job says four chapters later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I know that **my Redeemer lives**, and He shall stand at last on the earth… **whom I shall see for myself, and my eyes shall behold, and not another**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who said there is no umpire says this in chapter 19."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he asked for.** Not relief, not an explanation, and not vindication. Somebody to stand between with a hand on each — and he says outright that no such person is available to him."}]}, "Joel 1:1–4": {"title": "Wave after wave", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The book opens with a question and then describes what happened in four stages."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Has anything like this ever happened** in your days, or in the days of your fathers before you?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks the oldest men in the room, and the expected answer is no."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Tell it to your children**, and let them tell their children, and let their children pass it down to the ones who come after.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four generations of transmission ordered for a locust plague."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something is being preserved deliberately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What the **gnawing** locust left, the **swarming** locust ate. What the swarming locust left, the **hopping** locust ate. And what the hopping locust left, the **destroying** locust finished off.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four Hebrew words — probably four stages of the same insect's life, or four kinds of devastation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The structure is what matters: each one starts where the last one stopped."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wave after wave, until nothing was left.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What locusts do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A swarm strips everything green — leaf, bark, and the vine down to the wood. Joel describes it a few verses on: *he has laid waste My vine and ruined My fig tree; he has stripped it bare and thrown it away; its branches are made white.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The trees are left white. The bark is gone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it stopped"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The grain offering and the drink offering **are cut off from the house of the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is nothing left to bring. The worship stops for lack of materials."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joel calls the priests to *gird yourselves and lament… lie all night in sackcloth*, and the cattle groan because there is no pasture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same four words come back in chapter 2, in a promise:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will **restore to you the years** the locusts have eaten, the swarming locust and the hopping locust, the destroying locust and the gnawing locust, **My great army that I sent among you**.\"* See the note on Joel 2:25–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book names the destruction in four stages and then names all four again as the thing being restored."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is asked for in between"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now, therefore,\" says the LORD, \"turn to Me with all your heart… **so rend your heart, and not your garments**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The visible sign of grief was tearing your clothes. He asks for the thing the sign stood for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Joel was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The son of Pethuel.* That is all anyone knows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No date, no king named, no city. The shortest biography of any prophet."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the book runs into.** Locusts in chapter 1, an army in chapter 2, and then *I will pour out My Spirit on all flesh* — the passage Peter stood up and quoted at Pentecost. The prophet nobody can date wrote the sermon that opened the church."}]}, "Joel 2:21–24": {"title": "\"Just as before\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Joel has spent a chapter and a half describing a locust invasion that stripped the country bare. This is the turn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is told not to be afraid"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times, and the first two are addressed to things that cannot hear:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Do not be afraid, **land***", "*Do not be afraid, **you wild animals**, for the pastures of the wilderness are turning green again*", "*Be glad, **you children of Zion***"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ground and the animals are addressed before the people. The devastation was total, so the reversal is announced to everything it touched."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gives you the early rain in right measure, and He sends the showers down for you, the early rain and the late, **just as before**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both rains, in their seasons, restored to what they used to be. The Hebrew for *in right measure* carries the sense of according to what is right — the proper amount, neither withheld nor destructive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not more than before. **As** before — which, to a country that had watched everything eaten, is the whole promise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The overflow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The threshing floors will be full of grain, and the vats will overflow with new wine and oil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three crops of Deuteronomy 11, named again: grain, wine, oil. The promise is being restated in the vocabulary of the original covenant blessing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The famous line just after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will repay you for the years the locusts have eaten.* It comes two verses on, and this passage is the run-up to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note that Joel does not say the years were not lost. He says they will be repaid — which assumes the loss was real."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter ends.** *And afterward, I will pour out My Spirit on all people.* The same verb — pour — that has been used of rain the whole way through. Peter quotes it at Pentecost, and by then the harvest language has become something else. See the note on Acts 2:1–4."}]}, "Joel 2:25–27": {"title": "\"The years the locusts have eaten\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A promise to restore something nobody can restore."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is restored"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will **restore to you the years** the locusts have eaten.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the crops. The **years**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Property can be replaced and a harvest can be regrown. Time is the one commodity in the passage that cannot be handed back, and it is the one named."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The **swarming** locust and the **hopping** locust, the **destroying** locust and the **gnawing** locust.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four Hebrew words, probably stages of the same insect's life cycle, and Joel opens his book with them in the reverse order: *what the chewing locust left, the swarming locust has eaten; what the swarming locust left, the crawling locust has eaten.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wave after wave, each finishing what the last one started, until nothing is left."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose army it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My great army that I sent among you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joel does not soften it. The locusts are called God's army, and He says He sent them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same passage that promises restoration takes responsibility for the damage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You will have **plenty to eat and be satisfied**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *eat in plenty and be satisfied* — and the book opened with a land stripped bare, the grain offering cut off from the house of the LORD, and the cattle groaning because there was no pasture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Said twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Never again will My people be put to shame.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 26 and verse 27, in the same words. Joel repeats it as a bracket."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The knowing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then you will know that **I am in the midst of Israel**, that I am the LORD your God, and there is no other.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The restoration has a purpose beyond the food, and it is stated: so that they will know where He is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And it shall come to pass afterward that **I will pour out My Spirit on all flesh**; your sons and your daughters shall prophesy, your old men shall dream dreams, your young men shall see visions.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter stands up at Pentecost and quotes exactly that: *this is what was spoken by the prophet Joel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The locust chapter runs straight into the outpouring, and the New Testament dates the fulfilment to a morning in Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the locusts and the promise, Joel's call: *\"Now, therefore,\" says the LORD, \"turn to Me with all your heart… **so rend your heart, and not your garments**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Tearing your clothes was the visible sign of grief. He asks for the thing the sign stood for."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The reason given for the whole promise.** *For He is gracious and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness.* Joel quotes God's own self-description from Exodus 34 as the grounds for turning back. See the note on Exodus 34:5–7."}]}, "Joel 2:28–29": {"title": "\"On all people\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The promise Peter reached for on the day it happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"And afterward\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *and it shall come to pass afterward*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "After what: the chapter before this is the locust plague and the call to return — *rend your heart and not your garments*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the restoration: *I will restore to you the years that the swarming locust has eaten*, and *you shall eat in plenty and be satisfied*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pouring out comes after the restoring."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **pour out** My Spirit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shaphak** — to pour, to spill, the word used of blood being poured out at the altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a portion measured out. A vessel emptied."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it lands on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On **all people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *all flesh* — **kol basar**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under the old arrangement the Spirit came on individuals for tasks: Bezalel for the tabernacle, the seventy elders, judges, kings, prophets. Named men, for jobs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses had wished for this: *oh, that all the LORD's people were prophets, that the LORD would put His Spirit upon them!*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four groups"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Your **sons and your daughters** will prophesy*", "*Your **old men** will dream dreams*", "*Your **young men** will see visions*", "*Even on **the servants, both men and women***"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Male and female, old and young, free and slave. Every division that mattered in that society appears and is crossed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The servants"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even on the servants, **both men and women**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **avadim** and **shephachot** — male and female slaves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joel names them last and separately, which puts the emphasis on them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Priests. Levites. The temple. Any qualification at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Peter uses it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Standing in front of a crowd in Jerusalem at nine in the morning, dealing with a heckle: *no, **this is what was spoken of through the prophet Joel**.* See the notes on Acts 2:14–16 and Acts 2:17–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes the whole passage, including the wonders and the blood and fire and smoke, and ends on the last line: *whoever calls on the name of the LORD shall be saved.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The change Peter makes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joel has *afterward*. Acts has *in the last days*. Peter says which afterward it was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the divisions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is neither Jew nor Greek, there is neither slave nor free, there is neither male nor female; for you are all one in Christ Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same three pairs Joel crosses."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is unusual about the promise.** Not the Spirit — Israel knew about that. That the list of recipients has no qualification in it, and that Joel names the slaves twice."}]}, "Joel 3:17–18": {"title": "The mountains drip", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of a short and violent book, and it finishes with wine running down a hillside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book has been about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Locusts. Four kinds of them, in waves, stripping the country bare."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What the chewing locust left, the swarming locust has eaten; what the swarming locust left, the crawling locust has eaten.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joel reads the invasion as a summons: *blow the trumpet in Zion… **rend your heart, and not your garments***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So I will restore to you the years that the swarming locust has eaten**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the passage Peter quotes at Pentecost: *I will pour out My Spirit on all flesh*. See the note on Joel 2:28–29."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The knowing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **you will know that I am the LORD your God, dwelling in Zion, My holy mountain**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shokhen** — dwelling, tabernacling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The city"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jerusalem will be **holy**, and **strangers will never march through her again**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **zarim** — foreigners, and in this context invaders."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joel has spent three chapters on armies coming through."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On that day the **mountains will drip with new wine**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **asis** — sweet wine, fresh from the press."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mountains are not where vineyards are. Terraced hillsides are, and the picture is of so much that it runs off the slopes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The milk"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the **hills will flow with milk**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Milk means pasture. Enough grass on the hills for herds."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two together are the standing description of the land: *a land flowing with milk and honey*. See the note on Deuteronomy 6:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The water"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All the **streambeds of Judah will run with water**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **aphiqim** — wadis. Channels that are dry for most of the year and flood after rain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In a country where water is the constraint, a wadi running is the whole promise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **fountain will flow out of the house of the LORD** and water the **Valley of Acacias**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nachal ha-shittim** — the wadi of the acacias."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Acacia is desert wood. It grows where nothing else does, and it is the timber the tabernacle was made of — the ark, the table, the altar, the boards."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A stream from the temple, watering the valley where the desert wood grows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where else the water comes out of the building"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Water flowing out from under the threshold of the temple… **wherever the river flows, everything will live**.* See the note on Ezekiel 47:7–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *a pure river of water of life, clear as crystal, proceeding from the throne of God and of the Lamb*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three prophets and the same picture: one source, inside the sanctuary, and the water goes out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Amos has"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Behold, the days are coming,\" says the LORD, \"when the plowman shall overtake the reaper… **the mountains shall drip with sweet wine, and all the hills shall flow with it**.\"* See the note on Amos 9:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nearly the same sentence, in another prophet, and both books end on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line of Joel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For I will acquit them of the guilt of bloodshed, whom I had not acquitted. **For the LORD dwells in Zion**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book closes on the same clause it opened this passage with. He is living there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was happening when he wrote"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fields stripped, the vine dried up, the pomegranate and the palm withered, *because joy has withered away from the sons of men*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wine on the mountains is written by a man looking at a dead orchard."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the fountain starts.** Out of the house — and the valley it waters is named for acacia, the desert wood the tabernacle was built out of."}]}, "John 1:1–5": {"title": "\"Before anything at all began\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John opens his Gospel with the first three words of the Bible and then goes back further."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before anything at all began, **the Word was already there**. The Word **was with God**, and the Word **was God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three statements about the same subject, and each one adds something the last did not have."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He existed. He was in company. He was God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second and third do not obviously fit together, and John does not soften either to make room for the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The Word\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **Logos** — a word with a long history in Greek philosophy, where it meant the rational principle ordering the universe, and in Jewish thought, where the word of the LORD does things: *by the word of the LORD the heavens were made.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John picks a term both audiences already had and attaches it to a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In the beginning\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same three words that open Genesis, in the Greek Old Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 1:1 starts at the beginning. John says that when the beginning began, the Word was already there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The universal claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everything that exists came into being through Him, and **in all that has been made there is not one thing** that came into being **without Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated positively and then negatively, which is John's habit throughout this Gospel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is left outside the claim."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the same in Colossians: *all things were created through Him and for Him. And He is before all things, and **in Him all things hold together**.* See the note on Colossians 1:15–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Life, then light"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In Him was **life**, and that life was **the light** of everyone alive.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order matters. Life first, and the light is what the life is to people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The light **keeps shining** in the darkness, and the darkness has **never once been able to swallow it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is present tense — it goes on shining."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **katalambano** carries both senses: to overcome, and to grasp or comprehend. The darkness neither put it out nor worked out what it was."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both readings are true in this Gospel, and translators have never fully settled which John meant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the prologue lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nine verses later: *and the Word **became flesh** and dwelt among us.* The Greek is *pitched His tent* — **eskenosen**, the tabernacle word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one who was there before the beginning ends up camping."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What John does not include.** No genealogy, no Bethlehem, no shepherds, no manger. Matthew starts with Abraham, Luke goes back to Adam, and John goes back past the first sentence of Genesis."}]}, "John 1:14–18": {"title": "\"Moved in among us\" — and grace on top of grace", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three phrases in this passage are doing more than the English can easily carry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Moved in among us\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is built on the word for a **tent**. Literally: He pitched His tent among us, He tabernacled with us."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every Jewish reader knew which tent. The tabernacle in the wilderness was where the glory settled — and John's next words are *we saw His glory with our own eyes*. He is saying the presence that filled the tent has taken a body and moved into the neighbourhood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Grace on top of grace\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is odd in Greek too — literally *grace instead of grace*, or grace succeeding grace. The picture is of one wave arriving as the last recedes: nothing is used up, and what replaces it is more of the same."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not grace once, and then the reader's effort. Grace, replaced by grace, out of a fullness John says we have all been drawing from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The law was given through Moses; grace and truth came through Jesus Christ\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two different verbs, and the difference is deliberate. The law was *given* — handed over, delivered as a deposit. Grace and truth **came into being** through Jesus Christ: the verb John uses of things happening, coming to pass."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say the law was bad. He says one was delivered and the other arrived as an event, in a person."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *No one has ever seen God. The only Son… He is the One who has made Him known.* The verb behind *made known* is where the word *exegesis* comes from — to lead out, to explain, to set forth in full. Jesus is described as God's own exposition of Himself. John has spent seventeen verses building to that, and then the Gospel begins."}]}, "John 1:29–34": {"title": "\"Look — the Lamb of God\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John the Baptist gives Jesus a title nobody had used before, and it carries a thousand years of Israel's worship in three words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which lamb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every lamb in Israel's memory is standing behind the phrase:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the ram caught in the thicket in place of Isaac", "the Passover lamb whose blood went on the doorposts", "the two lambs offered every morning and evening in the temple", "the lamb Isaiah described, led to the slaughter and silent"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not specify. He says *the Lamb of God*, and lets the whole system answer to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Takes away\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb means to lift and carry off. Not covers, not compensates for — removes, by carrying."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the difference between the old arrangement and this one. The sacrifices covered sin year after year; this one is described as taking it away and going off with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sin offering looked like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus 4 sets out the procedure, and the order of it is worth walking through:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the man brings the animal — **the animal is examined**, not the man", "he **lays his hand on its head**, and the Hebrew is to lean, to press down", "the animal is killed in his place", "the blood is applied by the priest", "*and it shall be forgiven him*", "**and the man goes home**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about his sincerity, his record, or his resolve enters the ceremony. The inspection falls on the lamb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why the four days of examination in Passion Week matter, and why every delegation that came at Him went away with nothing, and why Pilate's verdict is *I find no fault in Him.* The lamb was looked over in public and passed. See the note on Matthew 21:23–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Of the world\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not of Israel. John the Baptist, a Jewish prophet in the Judean wilderness, extends it to the whole world in his first recorded description of Jesus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I did not know myself who He was\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice. They were relatives — Luke has their mothers meeting before either was born — so he knew the man. What he did not know was the identity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The confirmation came by a sign he had been told to watch for: the Spirit coming down and **staying**. The verb is the one John uses all through his Gospel for abiding."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he calls himself.** Nothing. Asked earlier who he is, he says he is a voice. Here he gives his whole testimony and it is entirely about somebody else — including the line *He was there long before I ever was*, said by a man six months older."}]}, "John 1:6–13": {"title": "\"He gave the right to become\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The prologue breaks off from eternity to introduce a man with a name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There came **a man, sent from God, whose name was John**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five verses about the Word who was in the beginning, and then a person with a name and a job."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek shifts too: the Word **was** — imperfect, continuous — and John **came into being**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His job"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He came **as a witness, to testify about the Light**, so that through him **everyone might come to believe**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **martyria** — testimony, evidence. It is a legal word, and John's Gospel runs on it: the Baptist testifies, the works testify, the Father testifies, the Scriptures testify."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was **not the Light himself**; he came **to point people to it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John the Evangelist says it twice about John the Baptist — here and again a few verses on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Baptist says it himself: *\"I am not the Christ\"*, and *\"He must increase, but I must decrease.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Light"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The true Light, **the Light that gives light to every person**, was coming into the world.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**True** is **alethinos** — real, genuine, as against a copy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three rejections"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prologue states it three times, each closer in:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*He was **in the world**, and the world came into being through Him, and **the world never recognized Him**.*", "*He came **to what belonged to Him**.*", "*And **His own people did not take Him in**.*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek moves from *ta idia* (his own things, neuter) to *hoi idioi* (his own people)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He made it, He owned it, and it did not let Him in the door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **to everyone who did receive Him**, to all who **put their trust in His name**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *receive* is **elabon** — took, accepted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gave them **the right to become the children of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exousia** — authority, the right to act. Not merely permission and not merely power."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**To become** — the Greek is a verb of coming into being, the same one used of John appearing in verse 6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three negatives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Born **not of bloodline**, not out of **any human wanting**, not out of **a husband's decision** — but **born of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three ways a person normally comes into a family, and all three are excluded:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Bloodline** — the Greek is plural, *bloods*: descent, ancestry, the thing the crowd in chapter 8 insists on when they say *we are Abraham's descendants*.", "**Human wanting** — the will of the flesh.", "**A husband's decision** — the will of a man."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing on the list is sinful. They are the ordinary means, and John rules all three out before naming the actual one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the prologue goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the Word became flesh and dwelt among us**, and we beheld His glory.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *dwelt* is **eskenosen** — pitched His tent, tabernacled."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is given and what it is given for.** Not a status conferred, but the right to become — and the three usual routes into a family are named and excluded first."}]}, "John 10:11–15": {"title": "The shepherd and the hired hand", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus says *I am the good shepherd* twice in five verses, and between the two He describes the alternative."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word \"good\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kalos** — not merely morally good but fine, admirable, the right one for the job. It is the word used of a good tree, a beautiful thing, a work well done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What makes him good"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The good shepherd lays down His life for the sheep.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not his skill or his knowledge of the country. The definition offered is a willingness to die, and it is stated before anything else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ordinary shepherds risked their lives — David fought lions and bears. But risking and *laying down* are different, and John's Gospel keeps the distinction: no one takes it from Me, I lay it down of My own accord."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hired hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sheep are not his own, so when he sees the wolf coming he abandons them and runs… he is only hired, and the sheep mean nothing to him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is said about him being wicked. He is not accused of stealing — that was the thief, two verses earlier. His failure is ownership: they are not his, so at the moment it costs something he leaves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The knowing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I know My own, and My own know Me — just as the Father knows Me, and I know the Father.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is astonishing and easy to read past. The mutual knowledge between Christ and His people is set on the same scale as the knowledge between the Son and the Father."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repetition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says *I lay down My life for the sheep* twice in the passage — once as the definition and once as the conclusion, with the knowing in between."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who was listening.** This follows the healing of the man born blind, whom the religious leaders had thrown out of the synagogue for defending Jesus. The hirelings who scatter and abandon are described with those men present. See the note on John 10:7–10."}]}, "John 10:16–18": {"title": "\"No one takes it from Me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He is in the middle of the shepherd discourse, and He says the most surprising thing in it about His own death."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other sheep"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There are **other sheep of Mine that do not belong to this fold**. **I must bring them in** as well.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to a Jewish audience, in Jerusalem, at the Feast of Dedication."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *I have* — present tense. They are already His before they are brought."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"They will listen to My voice\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *they might*. The whole discourse works this way: *My sheep hear My voice, and I know them, and they follow Me.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "One flock, one shepherd"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And there will be **one flock, one shepherd**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one fold. The fold is the enclosure — the pen, the boundary. The flock is the animals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel had promised it: *I will set up one shepherd over them, and he shall feed them — My servant David.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul spends a chapter of Ephesians on the same sentence: *He has made both one, and has broken down the middle wall of separation.* See the note on Ephesians 2:14–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the Father loves Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This is why the Father loves Me: I lay down My life** so that I may take it up again.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He states a reason for the Father's love, and the reason is the cross."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four times He says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In three verses He says *I lay it down* or *take it up* four times, and each time the verb is His own:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*I lay down My life so that I may take it up again*", "*No one takes it from Me*", "*I lay it down of My own accord*", "*I have the right to lay it down, and the right to take it up*"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"No one takes it from Me\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to men who were picking up stones in the same chapter, in a city where a Roman governor and a temple establishment would shortly do exactly that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's account of the death keeps the verse: *He bowed His head and **gave up** His spirit.* Not *expired*. He handed it over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pilate is told the same thing at the trial: *you could have no power at all against Me unless it had been given you from above.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The right"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exousia** — authority, the right to act. He has it twice: to lay it down and to take it up again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The taking up again is claimed by Him here, and elsewhere in the New Testament the resurrection is attributed to the Father and to the Spirit. All three, on the same morning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This is what My Father has charged Me to do**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Commandment, in the Greek. The laying down is obedience as well as choice, and the chapter puts both in one sentence without deciding between them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the discourse goes next.** *And there was a division again among the Jews because of these sayings.* John records the reaction to the shepherd chapter twice, and both times it splits the room."}]}, "John 10:22–26": {"title": "Winter at the Dedication", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John gives the season, the feast and the exact colonnade."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The feast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was winter, and **the Festival of Dedication** had come to Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hanukkah.** Not one of the feasts commanded in Leviticus — it commemorates the cleansing of the temple by Judas Maccabeus in 164 BC, after Antiochus Epiphanes had defiled it by sacrificing a pig on the altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight days, in the ninth month, with lamps."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the feast is about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A defiled temple, cleaned out and rededicated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same thing Hezekiah did in sixteen days, seven centuries earlier. See the note on 2 Chronicles 29:12–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"It was winter\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "John says it and he does not need to. Hanukkah is always in winter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tends to do this — *and it was night*, when Judas goes out — and readers have long taken the weather as part of the scene."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus was walking in the temple courts, in **Solomon's Colonnade**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A covered portico along the east side of the outer court, sheltered from the winter weather. It was where people gathered when it was cold."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The early church met there daily after Pentecost, and the healed man at the Beautiful Gate ran into it holding on to Peter and John. See the note on Acts 3:9–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How long are You going to keep us in suspense? If You are the Messiah, tell us plainly**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *how long do You lift up our soul?* — hold us in tension."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The demand is for a plain statement, and it sounds reasonable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I did tell you, and you do not believe. The works I do in My Father's name — those are the witness, and they speak for Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two answers: He has already said it, and the works are saying it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's Gospel has recorded the works — water into wine, the official's son, the man at the pool, the five thousand, the walking on water, and a man born blind, one chapter back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But you do not believe, because you are not My sheep**, just as I said to you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say they are not His sheep because they do not believe. The order in the sentence runs the other way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said it in the same chapter: *My sheep hear My voice, and I know them, and they follow Me.* See the note on John 10:16–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I and My Father are one**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they pick up stones. When He asks which good work they are stoning Him for, the answer is: *for blasphemy, and because You, being a Man, make Yourself God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They asked Him to tell them plainly, and He did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in dispute"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the works. The charge they bring is about who He is saying He is, and both sides agree about what He said."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the feast in the background is.** A rededication — a building cleared out and given back to God. He walks through it in winter and tells them the works are already speaking."}]}, "John 10:27–30": {"title": "Two hands, and a double negative", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the passage people are quietly reaching for when they ask whether they can be lost. It answers, and the way it answers is worth seeing in detail, because Jesus stacks the assurance four times over in three sentences."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four layers"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**I give them life that lasts forever** — given, not lent", "**they will never be lost** — the Greek uses a double negative, the strongest form of denial the language has: not ever, under any circumstance", "**no one will ever tear them out of My hand**", "**no one is able to tear them out of My Father's hand**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then, having named two hands, He closes with *I and the Father are one*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The security is not asserted once and left. It is stated, strengthened, transferred to a second pair of hands, and then sealed by saying the two are one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What \"no one\" covers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is unrestricted. It is not *no enemy* or *no other person*. The grammar does not carve out an exception for the sheep itself, and it would be strange to read one in, since the point of the sentence is the strength of the hand rather than the grip of the sheep."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And notice the word Jesus uses for what cannot happen: *tear out*, snatch, seize by force. It is the language of a predator — the same word John uses earlier in the chapter for the wolf."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Read the first line again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My sheep listen to My voice. I know them, and they follow Me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs. Only one of them is the sheep's, and it is *listen* — and even that is described as a recognition rather than an achievement. Sheep are not clever animals. Their whole competence in this picture is knowing which voice is theirs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has already said why they can: the shepherd calls them by name and goes ahead of them. Recognition is possible because He speaks first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the passage is going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I and the Father are one* is not an aside tacked onto a comfort. It is the conclusion of the argument, and the people listening understood it perfectly — the next verse has them picking up stones."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means the assurance and the claim about His identity are the same statement. Whether you can be held depends on who is holding you, and Jesus answers that question by saying whose hand it is."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the sheep who wander.** Nothing here says a sheep never strays; the chapter next door in Luke is about one that does. What this passage rules out is the sheep being *taken*, and it rules it out with the strongest negative Greek can build. Straying and being snatched are not the same thing, and only one of them is addressed here."}]}, "John 10:7–10": {"title": "The gate, and the thief", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus calls Himself two things in this chapter — the gate and the shepherd — and the first one is the stranger claim."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I am the gate\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sheepfolds in the hill country were stone enclosures with a single gap in the wall. There was no door. At night the shepherd lay down across the opening and slept there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The shepherd *was* the gate. Nothing came in or went out except over him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the gate is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever comes in through Me will be saved, and will go in and go out and find pasture.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two directions. A gate that only let you in would be a cage; this one is for coming and going. *Going in and out* is a Hebrew idiom for living freely and safely — the phrase used of Israel's leaders moving among the people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Safety and freedom in one sentence, and the pasture is outside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The thief's three verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The thief comes for one reason only — to steal, to kill, to destroy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three, and they escalate. Take what you have, end what you are, ruin what is left. Jesus gives no other motive: *for one reason only.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have come so that they may have life, and have it overflowing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *abundantly* — beyond the necessary measure, over and above. Not merely enough life to go on with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set beside three verbs of destruction, the answer is one verb of giving, with an adverb attached that means more than required."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who the thieves were.** The chapter follows the healing of a blind man whom the religious authorities threw out of the synagogue for saying who healed him. Jesus' words about hirelings who abandon the sheep and thieves who come to destroy are said with those men in the room."}]}, "John 11:1–6": {"title": "\"And so He stayed two more days\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most uncomfortable *therefore* in the Gospels, and John puts it there deliberately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The message"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the sisters sent word to Jesus: \"**Lord, the one You love is sick.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do not ask for anything. No request, no instruction, no *please come*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They name the relationship and state the situation, and leave it there. It is one of the shortest prayers in Scripture and one of the most confident."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The love, stated three times"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*the one **You love** is sick* — the sisters' words", "*Now **Jesus loved** Martha, and her sister, and Lazarus* — John's own note", "and later, at the tomb, the crowd: *see how **He loved** him!*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "John is making sure nobody can read what follows as indifference."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when He heard that Lazarus was ill, **He stayed on where He was two more days**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the Greek, the word joining the love to the delay is *therefore*. **Because** He loved them, He stayed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is not the connective anybody would choose. John chose it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He said about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This sickness **will not end in death**. It is for the glory of God, so that the Son of God may be glorified through it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lazarus does die. So either the statement is wrong, or *end* means something other than the moment of death — and the rest of the chapter makes clear which."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Death happens and is not the end of the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic of the delay"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bethany was about two days' journey away. He waits two days, then travels two days, and Lazarus has been in the tomb four days when He arrives."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jewish belief of the period held that the spirit lingered near the body for three days. Four days was past any possible ambiguity — and Martha says so at the tomb: *Lord, by this time he stinks.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The delay put the situation beyond every explanation except one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He told the disciples"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lazarus is dead. **And I am glad for your sakes that I was not there**, that you may believe.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Glad. He says it plainly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Had He come at once, Lazarus would have been healed and the sisters would have had their brother back — which is what they wanted, and less than what they got."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the waiting was not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not reluctance, not distraction, and not a failure to hear. He heard, He loved, and He stayed — in that order, in one sentence."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He does when He finally arrives.** Weeps. He knows exactly what He is about to do, He arranged the timing Himself, and He still stands at the grave and cries. See the note on John 11:33–37."}]}, "John 11:11–16": {"title": "\"Our friend Lazarus\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He tells them plainly, and then has to tell them plainly again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Our friend Lazarus** has fallen asleep.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Our** friend. He includes them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kekoimetai** — has fallen asleep. It is the standard New Testament word for a believer's death, and the root of *cemetery* — a sleeping-place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am going there to wake him up**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The misunderstanding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, **if he is only sleeping, he will get better**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *he will be saved* — in the medical sense, he will recover."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a reasonable inference. Sleep in a sick man is a good sign."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John says about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus had been speaking of his death, but **they thought He meant the rest of ordinary sleep**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John explains it for the reader, which he does throughout the Gospel — the temple of His body, the water the woman asked for, the food the disciples did not know about. See the note on John 4:31–34."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plain version"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So then He told them **plainly**, \"**Lazarus is dead**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parresia** — openly, without figure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The strangest clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **for your sake I am glad I was not there**, because **now you will believe**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says He is glad about the timing of a death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given is about the eleven men in front of Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said it when the message first came: *this sickness is not unto death, but for the glory of God, that the Son of God may be glorified through it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *so when He heard that he was sick, **He stayed two more days** in the place where He was.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The delay was deliberate and John says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But let us go to him**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why that is dangerous"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before this: they had just left Judea because *the Jews sought again to stone Him*. The disciples had said *Rabbi, lately the Jews sought to stone You, and are You going there again?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bethany is two miles from Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Thomas"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Thomas, the one called the Twin, said to the other disciples, \"**Let us go as well, so that we may die with Him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the first thing Thomas says in the Gospel, and it is usually read as pessimism."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is also a decision to go."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is remembered for the sentence he says at the end — *unless I see in His hands the print of the nails* — and for the one after that: *my Lord and my God*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens at Bethany"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four days in the tomb. Martha meets Him on the road; Mary stays in the house until she is called."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am the resurrection and the life.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the shortest verse in the Bible: *Jesus wept*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He knew what He was about to do and He wept anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order at the tomb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take away the stone.\"* Then the prayer. Then *\"Lazarus, come forth!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"**Loose him, and let him go**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The raising is His and the unwrapping is theirs."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He is glad about.** Not the death. The timing — and the reason He gives is that the men standing in front of Him are about to believe something they could not have otherwise."}]}, "John 11:17–22": {"title": "Martha goes out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He arrives four days late, and the first thing anyone says to Him is a complaint with faith in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The delay"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Jesus arrived, He found that Lazarus had **already been in the tomb four days**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John has already explained why. He was told Lazarus was ill, and *He stayed two more days in the place where He was*. See the note on John 11:1–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before that is the one that makes the delay strange: *now Jesus loved Martha and her sister and Lazarus*. John puts *therefore* between the love and the staying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why four matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There was a rabbinic belief that the soul hovered near the body for three days and departed on the fourth, after which the face was no longer recognisable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By day four, in that culture, nobody expected anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bethany was close to Jerusalem, only about **fifteen stadia** away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under two miles. Just over the Mount of Olives."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means the mourners are largely from Jerusalem, and the news of what happens here reaches the authorities the same day. It does: *then many of the Jews… believed in Him. But some of them went away to the Pharisees and told them the things Jesus did.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The raising of Lazarus is what triggers the decision to kill Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crowd"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Many of the people had **come out to Martha and Mary to comfort them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A full house of mourners, professional and otherwise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two sisters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As soon as Martha heard that Jesus was coming, **she went out to meet Him**, while **Mary stayed sitting in the house**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exactly in character. Luke has Martha *distracted with much serving* while Mary *sat at Jesus' feet and heard His word*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here Martha gets up and goes, and Mary stays sitting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Martha says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Lord, if You had been here, my brother would not have died**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mary says the identical sentence a few verses later, word for word. They have clearly been saying it to each other for four days."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a reproach and it is a statement of confidence. Both at once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **even now I know that whatever You ask of God, God will give You**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *whatever You ask** — **aiteses**, the verb for a request from an inferior to a superior."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is not asking for anything specific. She says something like: it is not over, and I do not know what I am saying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He tells her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Your brother will rise again**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And she takes it as doctrine: *I know that he will rise again in the resurrection at the last day*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the sentence that corrects her tense: *I am the resurrection and the life*. See the note on John 11:23–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she confesses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Yes, Lord, I believe that You are the Christ, the Son of God, who is to come into the world.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same confession Peter makes at Caesarea Philippi, made at a graveside by a woman who has just been reproaching Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she says later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the stone: *Lord, by now there will be a smell*. See the note on John 11:38–40."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The confession in verse 27 and the objection in verse 39 come from the same person, twelve verses apart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John said the miracle was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This sickness is not unto death, but **for the glory of God**, that the Son of God may be glorified through it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And to the disciples, before setting out: *Lazarus is dead. **And I am glad for your sakes that I was not there**, that you may believe.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What she says in one breath.** *If You had been here* — and then *even now*. A complaint and a confidence, four days after the funeral."}]}, "John 11:23–27": {"title": "\"I am the resurrection and the life\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Martha has correct doctrine and a dead brother, and Jesus moves the thing she believes from a date to a Person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Your brother will rise again.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deliberately ambiguous. He means in about twenty minutes. She hears something else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I know he will rise again, **in the resurrection on the last day**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is not wrong. That was standard Jewish belief and Jesus taught it Himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But she has taken a promise about *now* and filed it under a date far enough away to be no comfort at all. Her brother has been dead four days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am** the resurrection and the life.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *there will be* a resurrection. **I am** it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes an event she was waiting for and identifies it as Himself, standing in front of her, in a village outside Jerusalem, on a Thursday."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the fifth of the seven *I am* sayings in John, and the only one spoken at a graveside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whoever believes in Me **will live, even if he dies**. And everyone who is alive and believes in Me **will never die at all**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two different people are described."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first has died — and lives anyway. Physical death happens and does not terminate anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second is alive and believing, and of him the Greek is emphatic: *ou me apothane eis ton aiona* — a double negative, shall never ever die, unto the age."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever death is for a believer in that sentence, it is not the thing the word normally means. The New Testament's habit of calling it **sleep** — *our friend Lazarus sleeps*, four verses of this chapter earlier — follows from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do you believe this?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts it directly, to a grieving woman, before doing anything about the grave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Yes, Lord. I believe that You are the Messiah, the Son of God, the One who was to come into the world.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not answer the question He asked. She answers a bigger one — not *do you believe this claim* but *I believe who You are*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her confession is nearly word for word Peter's at Caesarea Philippi, and John puts it in the mouth of a woman in a village, mid-funeral."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Weeps. Then *Lazarus, come forth.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why He waited.** John records it plainly and it is uncomfortable: *when He heard that he was sick, **He stayed two more days** in the place where He was.* And to the disciples: *Lazarus is dead. And I am glad for your sakes that I was not there, that you may believe.*"}]}, "John 11:33–37": {"title": "\"Jesus wept\" — and the word before it that is stronger", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The shortest verse in the Bible, and the sentence before it is the one that carries the weight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Something moved deep in His spirit, and He was shaken\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two Greek verbs, and neither is gentle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first, **embrimaomai**, is used in classical Greek of a horse snorting. Where it appears elsewhere in the Gospels it describes a stern, forceful reaction. It is not sorrow — it is closer to indignation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second, **tarasso**, means to stir up, agitate, throw into turmoil. It is what John uses of water being churned, and of Jesus' own soul later in the book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So before the tears there is anger, and it is anger at something. He is standing in front of death with the man's sisters weeping beside Him, and He is about to undo it in ten minutes' time. The reaction is not grief at a loss He cannot fix. It is fury at the thing itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the tears"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus wept.* The verb is different from the one used of the crowd — theirs is loud, ritual wailing; His is quiet weeping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He cries at a funeral He is about to interrupt. Nothing about the outcome required it. The tears are simply what He does in the presence of what death does to people He loves."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two reactions in the crowd.** *Look how He loved him* — and, from others, *could He not have kept this man from dying?* John reports both without picking one. The second is the sharper question and it is left standing in the text, as it usually is in life. The answer, when it comes four verses later, is not an explanation. It is Lazarus walking out."}]}, "John 11:38–40": {"title": "Take the stone away", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The one instruction He gives before the miracle is one people can carry out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The state He is in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus, **moved deeply again within Himself**, came to the tomb.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **embrimomenos** — a strong word. It is used of snorting, of deep indignation, of being stirred with anger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not sorrow. John chose a harsh word twice in this passage, and the middle of it is the shortest verse in the Bible: *Jesus wept*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek there is **edakrusen** — He shed tears. Different from the loud wailing of the mourners around Him, which is **klaio**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is at a funeral, and He is angry and crying at the same time, and He knows what He is about to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tomb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was **a cave, with a stone laid across the mouth** of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A rock-cut chamber with a stone across the entrance, which is what His own tomb looks like a few weeks later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take the stone away**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **arate ton lithon**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is about to call a man out of death by speaking. He asks the bystanders to move a rock."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing delegated is the only part they could do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Martha, the sister of the dead man, said to Him, \"**Lord, by now there will be a smell. He has been in there four days**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ede ozei** — he already stinks."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old versions keep it blunt. She is being practical and she is right about the chemistry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the woman who said, twelve verses earlier, *I believe that You are the Christ, the Son of God*. See the note on John 11:17–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Did I not tell you that if you would believe, you would see the glory of God?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said it to the disciples, not to her — *this sickness is not unto death, but for the glory of God*. Word must have got back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order in the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Believe, then see."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The usual human order is the reverse, and Martha is standing in front of a sealed cave being asked to run it the other way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was moved and what was not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stone was in the way of nothing. He raises the dead by speaking, and a rock is no obstacle to a voice, or to a man who walks through doors after the resurrection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stone was in the way of the people watching."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lifts His eyes and thanks the Father — *I said it for the sake of the people standing here* — and then shouts. See the note on John 11:41–44."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other stone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Who is going to roll the stone back** from the entrance for us?\" Then they looked up, and **the stone had already been rolled aside**.* See the note on Mark 16:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Lazarus' tomb He tells people to move it. At His own, nobody is asked, and it is done before they arrive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the raising set in motion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then, from that day on, they plotted to put Him to death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *the chief priests plotted to put Lazarus to death also, because on account of him many of the Jews went away and believed in Jesus*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man is raised, and the response is a plan to kill him again."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He asked for.** Not faith at the graveside first — He asked for the stone, which was the one part of it human arms could manage."}]}, "John 11:41–44": {"title": "Untie him", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He prays out loud and explains, in the middle of the prayer, why He is doing it out loud."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they **took the stone away** from the place where the dead man had been laid.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They did it. Martha's objection about the smell is in the previous paragraph, and the stone still comes off. See the note on John 11:38–40."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Jesus **lifted His eyes** and said, \"**Father, I thank You that You have heard Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past tense. He thanks Him for something already done, standing in front of a sealed-then-open tomb with nothing yet visible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No request is recorded. The prayer that is answered is not in the text."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The aside"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I knew that **You always hear Me**, but **I said it for the sake of the people standing here**, so that they would believe that You sent Me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He explains the prayer while praying it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing He wants them to conclude is not that He is powerful. It is *that You sent Me* — which is the argument of John's whole Gospel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shout"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He called out **in a loud voice**, \"**Lazarus, come out!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kraugasen phone megale** — cried with a great voice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old preachers' remark is worth keeping: had He not said the name, the whole graveyard would have come."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He says as much elsewhere: *the hour is coming in which all who are in the graves will hear His voice and come forth*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What came out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the man who had been dead came out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's phrasing is deliberately awkward. Not *Lazarus came out* — the dead man came out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How he came out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His **hands and feet still bound in strips of linen**, and a **cloth still wrapped around his face**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wound in grave bands, ankles and wrists tied, a napkin over the head."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He walked, or hopped, or was somehow moved. John does not explain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Untie him**,\" Jesus said, \"**and let him go**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **lusate auton kai aphete hupagein**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two instructions in the whole episode, and both are given to bystanders. Move the stone. Take the wrappings off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The raising is His. The stone and the cloths are theirs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Lazarus** — the Greek form of **Eleazar**, which means *God has helped*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other tomb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At His own, the cloths are left behind: *the handkerchief that had been around His head, not lying with the linen cloths, but **folded together in a place by itself***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lazarus came out wearing his. Jesus left His in the tomb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One was raised to die again. The other was not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then many of the Jews who had come to Mary, and had seen the things Jesus did, believed in Him. But some of them went away to the Pharisees.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the council: *if we let Him alone like this, everyone will believe in Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Caiaphas: *it is expedient for us that one man should die for the people* — and John notes he *did not say this on his own authority*, but prophesied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seventh sign in John's Gospel is the one that gets Him killed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Six days later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then, six days before the Passover, Jesus came to Bethany, where Lazarus was who had been dead… **there they made Him a supper**; and Martha served, but Lazarus was one of those who sat at the table with Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Martha serving, and Lazarus at dinner. And Mary with the alabaster jar."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two instructions.** *Take the stone away* and *untie him*. Everything in between was His, and both of the things He asked for were things hands could do."}]}, "John 11:49–53": {"title": "He did not say it on his own", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The worst man in the room says the truest thing in the chapter, and does not know it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What prompted the meeting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lazarus. *Then many of the Jews who had come to Mary, and had seen the things Jesus did, believed in Him.* See the note on John 11:41–44."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the council's problem, stated aloud: *what shall we do? For this Man works many signs. If we let Him alone like this, everyone will believe in Him, and the Romans will come and take away both our place and nation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do not dispute the miracles. They are doing a political calculation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who speaks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One of them, named **Caiaphas, who was high priest that year**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John says *that year* three times in the passage. The high priesthood was supposed to be for life; under Rome it had become an appointment, and the Romans moved men in and out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Caiaphas held it for eighteen years, which meant he was good at the politics."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You know nothing at all. You are not thinking it through**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He opens by insulting the council."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proposal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It is better for us that one man die for the people than that the whole nation be destroyed**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read as policy, it is the oldest argument there is: one life against many."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Better for us.* He says it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **did not say this on his own**. Being high priest that year, **he was prophesying**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eprophēteusen**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John claims the sentence was given to him. The office, not the man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same thing happens to Balaam — a hired diviner who opens his mouth to curse and finds words in it he did not choose. *And the LORD put a word in Balaam's mouth.* See the note on Numbers 23:4–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sentence actually says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That Jesus was going to **die for the nation**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The preposition is **huper** — on behalf of, in place of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Caiaphas meant: one man's execution buys the nation's safety."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John reads it as substitution, and the theology of it is exact."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause John adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **not for that nation only, but so that He would gather into one the scattered children of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sunagage eis hen** — gather together into one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Caiaphas' arithmetic was one man against one nation. John extends it past the border."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same shepherd sentence: *other sheep I have which are not of this fold; them also I must bring, and there will be one flock and one shepherd*. See the note on John 10:16–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The outcome"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **from that day on they were making their plans to put Him to death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The decision is dated to this meeting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The raising of Lazarus is what triggers it, which is why John puts the seventh sign here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Caiaphas says at the trial"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I put You under oath by the living God: tell us if You are the Christ, the Son of God!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And when He answers, Caiaphas tears his clothes — which the high priest was forbidden to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other unwitting prophecy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pilate wrote the title over the cross in three languages and refused to change it: *what I have written, I have written*. See the note on John 19:12–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two men who wanted Him dead, and both left an accurate statement on the record."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the office produced.** John says the man did not speak on his own — and the sentence he was given, meant as a political calculation, states substitution exactly."}]}, "John 12:1–8": {"title": "A pound of pure nard", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A dinner in Bethany, and one person in the room knows what week it is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The date"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Six days before the Passover.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John counts. The next paragraph is the entry into Jerusalem on the following day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus came to Bethany, where Lazarus was — **the one who had died, and whom He had raised from the dead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two miles from Jerusalem. The chief priests have already decided *from that day on they plotted to put Him to death*, and by the end of this chapter they are planning to kill Lazarus too."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There they made Him a supper. **Martha served**, and **Lazarus was one of those at the table** with Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Martha is serving, which is what she was doing in Luke's account. See the note on Luke 10:38–42."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lazarus is at the table, alive, in the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Mary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Mary took **a pound of perfume, pure nard, very costly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **litran murou nardou pistikes polutimou**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Litra** — a Roman pound, about eleven ounces."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nardos pistike** — nard, genuine or unadulterated. Imported from the Himalayas, and one of the most expensive substances traded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The value"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judas names it: **three hundred denarii**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A denarius was a day's wage. Three hundred is close to a year's earnings for a labourer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **poured it over the feet of Jesus, and wiped His feet with her hair**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark and Matthew record the head. John records the feet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anointing a head was an honour. Anointing feet was a servant's job, and unbinding her hair in public was not something a respectable woman did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The house"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the whole house was filled with the fragrance of it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eplerothe ek tes osmes**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A detail only somebody in the room would supply."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why was this perfume not sold for three hundred silver coins and the money given to the poor?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is a reasonable question, and the arithmetic is right."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who asks it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Judas Iscariot, the son of Simon, **the one who was going to hand Him over**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He said this **not because he cared about the poor, but because he was a thief. He kept the money bag, and he used to help himself to what was put into it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eklepten** — imperfect. He was stealing, habitually."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John is the only evangelist who says this. He is writing decades later and no longer sees any reason to be discreet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The defence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Leave her alone. She has kept this for the day of My burial**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **aphes auten** — let her be."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bodies were anointed for burial. Mary has done in advance what the women will come to the tomb on Sunday morning to do — and find they cannot."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone at that table had heard Him predict the death. She is the only one who acts as though it is going to happen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The poor you will always have with you, but you will not always have Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is quoting Deuteronomy 15, and the half he quotes is followed in the original by *therefore I command you, saying, \"You shall open your hand wide to your brother\"*. See the note on Deuteronomy 15:7–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a dismissal of the poor. It is a statement about a window that is closing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mark records Him saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Wherever this gospel is preached in the whole world, **what this woman has done will also be told as a memorial to her**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Judas goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew puts it immediately after: *then one of the twelve, called Judas Iscariot, went to the chief priests and said, \"What are you willing to give me?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty pieces of silver, a tenth of what was in the jar."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What she knew.** Bodies were anointed for burial. Everybody at the table had heard Him predict His death, and she is the only one who acted as though it was going to happen."}]}, "John 13:1–3": {"title": "\"Knowing that the Father had placed everything into His hands\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John spends three verses telling you what Jesus knew, and then tells you what He did with a towel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus knew that **His hour had come**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John has been counting toward this since chapter 2 — *My hour has not yet come*, said four times through the Gospel. Here it arrives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"All the way to the end\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He had loved His own who were in the world, and **He loved them all the way to the end**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eis telos** — to the end, to the uttermost, completely."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same root as the word He speaks from the cross: **tetelestai**, it is finished. The loving and the finishing share a root."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Judas, in the middle of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The devil had already put it into the heart of **Judas Iscariot** to hand Him over.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John places this between *He loved them to the end* and *knowing the Father had given all things into His hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judas is in the room. His feet are among the ones about to be washed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things He knew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Jesus knew that **the Father had placed everything into His hands**, and that **He had come from God**, and **was going back to God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Total authority, settled origin, certain destination."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the fullest statement in the Gospels of what Jesus was conscious of about Himself, and John puts it here on purpose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does with that knowledge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *He rose from supper and **laid aside His garments**, took a towel and girded Himself. After that, He poured water into a basin and began to wash the disciples' feet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Foot-washing was the job of the lowest slave in a household — a Gentile slave, in some accounts, because a Jewish servant could not be required to do it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who knew everything was in His hands took off His outer clothing and knelt on the floor."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which way round it runs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not write *although* He knew all this. He writes *knowing* — the security is the cause, not the obstacle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody uncertain of their position takes the lowest job. It is the man who cannot be diminished who can afford to kneel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You shall never wash my feet!\"* And the answer: *\"If I do not wash you, you have no part with Me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Peter asks for a bath, and is told *he who is bathed needs only to wash his feet, and is completely clean.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You call Me Teacher and Lord, and you say well, for so I am. **If I then, your Lord and Teacher, have washed your feet, you also ought to wash one another's feet.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not lower the titles. He confirms them, and then points at the basin."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul does with the same shape.** *Let this mind be in you which was also in Christ Jesus, who, being in the form of God… **made Himself of no reputation**, taking the form of a bondservant.* Philippians 2 argues it exactly this way: the descent starts from a secure height, and Paul says think like that."}]}, "John 13:21–26": {"title": "The piece of bread", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He identifies the traitor by handing him something to eat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The state He is in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was **troubled in His spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **etarachthe** — stirred up, agitated. The same verb John uses of Him at the tomb of Lazarus and in the temple court before the Greeks."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Gospel that most insists on His sovereignty is also the one that records the agitation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The announcement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He said it plainly: \"I tell you the truth — **one of you is going to hand Me over**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paradosei** — will deliver up, hand over. John's standard word for what Judas does, and it is not the word for betray."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The disciples **looked at one another, at a loss** to know which of them He meant.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody points. Matthew records what they said: *Lord, is it I?* — one after another."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever Judas had been doing for three years, it had not been visible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The position"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One of them, **the disciple Jesus loved**, was **leaning back close against Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **en to kolpo** — in the bosom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase John used in his prologue of the Son and the Father: *the only begotten Son, who is **in the bosom of the Father**, He has declared Him*. See the note on John 1:14–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the words about himself and Jesus that he had used about Jesus and the Father."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name he goes by"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The disciple Jesus loved.** John never gives his own name in his Gospel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the title five times: here, at the cross, at the tomb, at the lake, and at the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The go-between"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Simon Peter **motioned to him** to ask which one He was speaking about.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter cannot ask. He signals to the man who is close enough to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seating means Peter is not near enough to speak quietly, and John is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So, leaning against Jesus, he said to Him, \"**Lord, who is it?**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is **the one I give this piece of bread to, when I have dipped it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **psomion** — a morsel, a small piece torn off."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the gesture meant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At a Passover meal the host dipped bread in the dish and handed it to the guest he wished to honour. It is a gesture of favour, and it usually went to the person at the host's left — the second place at the table."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is where Judas must have been sitting for Jesus to reach him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who got it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when He had dipped the piece of bread, He gave it to **Judas Iscariot, the son of Simon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The identification and the courtesy are the same action."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody at the table works it out. Two verses later John says so: *no one at the table knew for what reason He said this to him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now after the piece of bread, Satan entered him. Then Jesus said to him, \"**What you do, do quickly**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *having received the piece of bread, he then went out immediately. **And it was night**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words, at the end of a verse, and John means both of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He had already said about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I do not speak concerning all of you. I know whom I have chosen, but that the Scripture may be fulfilled, '**He who eats bread with Me has lifted up his heel against Me**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 41, quoted a few verses earlier — and the psalm's line is about a table companion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He had just finished doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Washing their feet. All of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus, knowing that the Father had given all things into His hands… rose from supper and laid aside His garments.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judas' feet were in that basin."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the gesture was.** Not an accusation. The host's piece of bread, dipped and handed to the guest at his side — and it was the answer to *who is it*."}]}, "John 13:27–30": {"title": "\"And it was night\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four words end this paragraph, and John did not put them there for the weather."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bread"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As soon as **Judas took the bread**, Satan entered into him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The morsel had just been handed to him by Jesus, dipped and given — which at that table was a gesture of honour, offered to a favoured guest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last thing Judas receives from Him is a mark of friendship."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What you are about to do, do quickly.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *don't*. Not an exposure in front of the others — John says nobody at the table understood it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He knows exactly what is happening and releases him to go and do it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the others assumed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Some of them thought that, **because Judas kept the money bag**, Jesus was telling him to buy what they needed for the festival, **or to give something to the poor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven men, three years in, and not one of them suspects him. Judas is the treasurer, and the natural assumption about him leaving is that he has gone to give money away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Earlier in the chapter Jesus had said *one of you will betray Me*, and they looked at one another *perplexed*, and each asked *is it I?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "He went out at once"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Judas took the piece of bread and **went out at once**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He obeys immediately. It is the last recorded thing he does in the upper room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it was night.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John writes the way a man writes who has been thinking about it for sixty years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He could have written nothing. The time is not needed — nobody is going to be confused about the hour of the Last Supper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has spent his whole Gospel on light and darkness: *the light shines in the darkness*, *men loved darkness rather than light*, *I am the light of the world*, *he who follows Me shall not walk in darkness*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man walks out of the room where the Light is sitting, and John records the weather of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens the moment he is gone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The very next verse: *\"Now the Son of Man is glorified, and God is glorified in Him.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *\"A new commandment I give to you, that you love one another as I have loved you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The door closes behind Judas and Jesus starts the longest and warmest passage in the Gospels — chapters 14 to 17, the upper room discourse, and the prayer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He had done to Judas' feet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Earlier the same evening He washed them. See the note on John 13:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He knew, and He knelt down in front of him anyway."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other side of the same night.** Peter denies Him before the rooster crows, warming himself at a charcoal fire — and is restored over another charcoal fire on a beach. Two men fail on the same night. Only one comes back to breakfast. See the note on John 21:1–3."}]}, "John 13:31–35": {"title": "A new command", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The upper-room discourse starts the moment the door closes behind Judas."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When he had gone out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judas has just left with the piece of bread. See the note on John 13:21–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John has already told us it was night. Everything Jesus says from here to chapter 17 is said to eleven men."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Now the Son of Man is glorified**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **edoxasthe** — an aorist. It has happened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing has happened yet, in the visible sense. A man has walked out of a room."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The arrest, the trial and the cross are all still ahead, and He speaks of the glorifying in the past tense."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What glory means in this Gospel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John uses the word of the cross throughout. *The hour has come that the Son of Man should be glorified* — said when the Greeks asked to see Him, and immediately followed by *unless a grain of wheat falls into the ground and dies*. See the note on John 12:23–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lifting up He keeps referring to is a lifting onto a beam."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five glories"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now the Son of Man **is glorified**, and God **is glorified** in Him. If God **is glorified** in Him, God will also **glorify** Him in Himself, and He will **glorify** Him at once.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five times in two sentences, moving back and forth between the two of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My children**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **teknia** — little children. A diminutive, and the only time Jesus uses it in the Gospels."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John picks it up and uses it seven times in his letter. It stayed with him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The notice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am with you **only a little longer**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You will look for Me, and **what I said to the Jewish leaders I now say to you: where I am going, you cannot come**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said it to His opponents in chapter 7 and again in chapter 8 — and it landed as a threat there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here it is said to His friends, and it is a statement of timing rather than of judgment. Two chapters later: *I go to prepare a place for you… that where I am, there you may be also*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter asks immediately: *Lord, where are You going?* And gets the prediction about the rooster."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am giving you **a new command: love one another**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **entolen kainen** — a new commandment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The command itself is not new. Leviticus has *you shall love your neighbour as yourself*, and Jesus has already named it as the second great commandment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is new"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**As I have loved you**, so you are to love one another.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The standard is new. Not *as yourself* — *as I have loved you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He has just washed their feet, including the feet of the man who left. The measure was demonstrated an hour before it was commanded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The badge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This is how everyone will know that you are My disciples** — if you have love for one another.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *by this all will know*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not doctrine, not miracles, not conduct in general. One identifying mark, and it is horizontal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Tertullian recorded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Writing around AD 200, he quotes what outsiders said about Christians: *see how they love one another*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John does with it later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A new commandment I write to you… **because the darkness is passing away, and the true light is already shining**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *he who says he is in the light, and hates his brother, is in darkness until now*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *by this we know love, because He laid down His life for us*. See the note on 1 John 4:10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old man is still working on the sentence he heard that night."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was new about it.** Not the loving. *As I have loved you* — and He had washed twenty-four feet, including two that were about to walk out and sell Him."}]}, "John 13:4–5": {"title": "The towel and the basin", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four verbs, and John records each one as if it mattered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He knew first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus, knowing that **the Father had given all things into His hands**, and that He had come from God and was going to God.* See the note on John 13:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence the action comes out of. Not despite knowing it — because of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The washing is what a man does who has just been handed everything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rising"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So He **rose from the table**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mid-meal. Nobody asked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clothing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **laid His outer clothing aside**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tithesin ta himatia** — he lays down his garments."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tithemi** is the verb He uses of His life: *I **lay down** My life for the sheep*. And *no one takes it from Me, but I lay it down of Myself*. See the note on John 10:16–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John uses the same word here, and again in verse 12 when He picks the garments back up — **elaben**, took again, which is also the resurrection verb in John 10: *I have power to lay it down, and I have power to take it again*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The paragraph is shaped like the incarnation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The towel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took a towel and **tied it around His waist**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **diezosen** — girded Himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is slave's dress. A servant tied a cloth round the waist to work in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose job it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lowest servant in the house. Feet in sandals on those roads needed washing before a meal, and a Jewish household would not require even a Hebrew slave to do it — it was reserved for Gentile slaves, or for a wife or child."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in the room had volunteered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke records what they had been arguing about that same evening: *there was also a dispute among them, as to which of them should be considered the greatest*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The water"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He **poured water into a basin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He fetches it Himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The washing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And began to **wash His disciples' feet, one man after another**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a present infinitive: He kept washing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose feet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "All of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judas is in the room and does not leave until verse 30. John has already said *the devil having already put it into the heart of Judas Iscariot to betray Him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Those feet are in the basin."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Peter's objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You shall never wash my feet!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If I do not wash you, **you have no part with Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, **not my feet only, but also my hands and my head!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice wrong in three sentences, and both times at full volume."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distinction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He who is bathed needs only to wash his feet, but is completely clean**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two different washings. The bath happens once; the feet get dirty on the way to dinner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same distinction the priests knew — washed entirely at consecration, and hands and feet at the laver from then on. See the note on Leviticus 7:35–38."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do you know what I have done to you? You call Me Teacher and Lord, and you say well, for so I am. **If I then, your Lord and Teacher, have washed your feet, you also ought to wash one another's feet**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have given you an example**, that you should do as I have done to you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hupodeigma** — a pattern, a specimen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If you know these things, blessed are you if you do them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Knowing is assumed. The blessing is attached to the second clause."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the humility comes from.** The verse before it. He knew everything had been put into His hands — and the next thing He did was take His coat off and pick up a basin."}]}, "John 13:6–11": {"title": "Not my feet only", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter refuses twice, in opposite directions, within three sentences."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is happening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has laid aside His outer clothing, tied a towel round His waist, poured water into a basin, and started washing feet. See the note on John 13:4–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Knowing that the Father had given all things into His hands, and that He had come from God and was going to God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John states the authority immediately before the basin."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He came to Simon Peter, and Peter said to Him, \"**Lord — are You going to wash my feet?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word order puts the two pronouns side by side: **su mou** — *You, my* feet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The objection is not to foot-washing. It is to who is holding the towel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You do not understand now what I am doing, but afterward you will**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meta tauta** — after these things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not explain. He says the understanding will come later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will never wash my feet**,\" Peter said. \"**Never**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ou me nipses mou tous podas eis ton aiona** — a double negative with *unto the age*. The strongest refusal Greek can construct."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter thinks he is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Defending Him. It is the same instinct as *far be it from You, Lord* when the cross was predicted. See the note on Mark 8:31–33."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both times he is protecting Jesus from something Jesus intends."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If I do not wash you, you have no part with Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ouk echeis meros met' emou** — you have no share with Me."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Meros** — a portion, a share, an allotment. The word used of an inheritance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Then, Lord — not my feet only. Wash my hands and my head as well**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "From *never* to *all of me* in one sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is consistent in the only way that matters to him: whatever gets him the share."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Anyone who has bathed does not need washing except for his feet; he is clean all over**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek uses two verbs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Louo** — to bathe the whole body."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nipto** — to wash a part. The word used throughout the passage for the feet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The picture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man bathed at home, walking to dinner in sandals on a dusty road, arriving with clean body and dirty feet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bath is not repeated. The feet are washed at the door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **you are clean — though not all of you**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The note"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He knew who was going to hand Him over. That is why He said, \"You are not all clean.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John supplies it, as he does throughout the chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose feet were in the basin"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judas was at that table."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text does not say his were skipped, and nothing suggests they were."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do you know what I have done to you? You call Me Teacher and Lord, and you say well, for so I am. **If I then, your Lord and Teacher, have washed your feet, you also ought to wash one another's feet**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**For I have given you an example, that you should do as I have done to you**.\"* See the note on John 13:12–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He said at the start of the meal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Having loved His own who were in the world, **He loved them to the end**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eis telos** — to the end, or completely."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two verbs.** **Louo** — the whole-body bath, not repeated. **Nipto** — washing a part, done at the door. Peter asked for the first and was told he had already had it."}]}, "John 14:1–4": {"title": "In My Father's house", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Said to men who had just been told one of them would betray Him and one would deny Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The context"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judas has gone out into the night. Peter has just been told about the rooster."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not let your hearts be troubled.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said in that room, to those men."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tarassestho** — stirred up, agitated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John has used it of Jesus twice: at the tomb of Lazarus, and a few verses earlier when He announced the betrayal — *He was troubled in His spirit*. See the note on John 13:21–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them not to be what He had just been."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You trust in God; trust in Me as well**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek can be read as two statements, two commands, or one of each. The construction is deliberately ambiguous and every reading puts Himself alongside God as the object."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The house"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In My Father's house there are **many rooms**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **monai** — abodes, dwelling places. From **meno**, to remain — the abiding word that runs through the vine chapter. See the note on John 15:9–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old versions have *mansions*, from the Latin **mansiones**, which meant stopping places on a road."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The aside"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If it were not so, I would have told you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A remarkable sentence. He appeals to His own track record with them: I have not misled you about anything else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am going there to prepare a place for you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The going is toward a cross, and He describes it as preparation for accommodation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And if I go and prepare a place for you, **I will come back and take you to Myself**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paralempsomai** — I will take you alongside, receive you to Myself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So that where I am, you may be there too**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the place. The person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stated purpose of the whole arrangement is location relative to Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He prays the same thing an hour later: *Father, I desire that they also whom You gave Me may be with Me where I am, that they may behold My glory*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And you know where I am going, and **you know the way**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which Thomas immediately contradicts: *Lord, we do not know where You are going. How can we know the way?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer: *I am the way, and the truth, and the life*. See the note on John 14:5–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Jewish wedding looked like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A betrothal, and then the bridegroom went back to his father's house to build on an extra room. When the father judged it ready, the son came for the bride, often at night, with a shout."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bride did not know the day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Here is the bridegroom! Come out to meet him!* See the note on Matthew 25:1–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other passage on the same subject"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the Lord Himself will descend from heaven with a shout… and the dead in Christ will rise first. Then we who are alive and remain shall be **caught up together with them** in the clouds to meet the Lord in the air. And thus **we shall always be with the Lord**.* See the note on 1 Thessalonians 4:16–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's conclusion is the same as this one: not the place, the company."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the discourse ends with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Peace I leave with you, My peace I give to you; not as the world gives do I give to you. **Let not your heart be troubled, neither let it be afraid**.\"* See the note on John 14:25–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same sentence, closing the chapter it opened."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The stated purpose.** Not the rooms. *That where I am, you may be there too* — and Paul ends his own version of the passage the same way."}]}, "John 14:15–17": {"title": "Another Helper who stays", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He is leaving, and He tells them what is coming instead — and the word He picks for it is the one He uses for Himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If you love Me, **keep My commands**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order runs love first, keeping second. John says the same in his letter: *this is the love of God, that we keep His commandments. And His commandments are not burdensome.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The asking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **I will ask the Father**, and **He will give you** another Helper.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus asks and the Father gives. The whole arrangement is described as a request granted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Another\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **allos** — another of the same kind, as against **heteros**, another of a different kind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a substitute of a lesser sort. The same kind of person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Parakletos** — from *para*, alongside, and *kaleo*, to call. One called to stand beside you: an advocate, a counsel for the defence, a supporter in court."}, {"k": "p", "t": "English versions render it Comforter, Helper, Advocate, Counsellor. No single word carries all of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John uses it once more, in his letter, of Jesus: *we have an **Advocate** with the Father, Jesus Christ the righteous.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Another** Helper. The first one is the man saying it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The duration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To be with you **forever**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *into the age*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been with them three years and was about to be arrested."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The Spirit of truth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John uses the title three times in these chapters. Later: *when He, the Spirit of truth, has come, **He will guide you into all truth**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the world cannot do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The world cannot receive Him**, because **it does not see Him and does not know Him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *will not*. Cannot. The reason given is a lack of recognition rather than a refusal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they can"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But you know Him**, because **He lives with you and will be in you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two prepositions, and the change between them is the whole point."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**With** — present tense, already true."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**In** — future tense, from Pentecost onward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will not leave you orphans; I will come to you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **orphanos** — the word for a child left without a father, and also for disciples left without their teacher."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just told them He is going, and the next sentence denies the thing they would conclude from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The upper room, after the supper, before the arrest. The same discourse that ends *get up now; let us go from here*. See the note on John 14:28–31."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is promised.** Not a replacement and not an influence. Another of the same kind as Himself, permanently, and moving from beside them to inside them."}]}, "John 14:25–27": {"title": "The Helper's job description, and a peace unlike the world's", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus is hours from arrest, and He spends the time telling frightened men what will happen after He is gone. Two promises, and both are narrower and more practical than they are usually quoted as."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Helper is called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Parakletos** — from *para*, alongside, and *kaleo*, to call. One called to your side. In ordinary Greek it was used of an advocate: the person who stands with you when you are in trouble, and speaks for you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "English has never found a single word for it. *Comforter* is too soft, *Counsellor* too clinical, *Advocate* too legal. The picture underneath all of them is someone summoned to stand next to you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things He is said to do here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus is unusually specific, and both jobs point in the same direction:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**teach you everything**", "**bring back to your mind everything I have said to you**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are about the words of Jesus. Not new material of the Spirit's own — He teaches what Jesus taught, and He reminds them of what Jesus said. Two chapters later Jesus makes it explicit: the Spirit will not speak on His own, and He will glorify Christ rather than Himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a useful measure. Something that pulls attention away from Christ and onto itself is not doing the work described here."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "A promise to men who were about to forget"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is something quietly kind in the second job. These are the men who would scatter that night. Peter is hours from denying he knows Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus does not say *remember everything I told you*. He says Someone will bring it back to their minds. He is making arrangements for their forgetfulness before it happens, without mentioning it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Not the way the world gives\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peace I leave with you; My peace I give to you. I do not give to you the way the world gives.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The world's peace is conditional and it is mostly the absence of something — quiet while nothing is going wrong, safety while the money holds. It is also given in a particular way: with expectation attached, and withdrawn when circumstances change."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus distinguishes His gift on *both* counts. It is a different peace, and it is handed over differently. And He says it in a room where nothing outward was about to improve for any of them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"do not let your hearts be troubled.\"** It sounds like an instruction to manage a feeling, which nobody can do on command. In the sentence it comes attached to a gift just handed over — peace given, then the instruction. He is not asking them to generate calm. He is telling them what they now have to settle themselves with."}]}, "John 14:28–31": {"title": "\"Get up now; let us go from here\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of the first half of the upper room discourse, and it closes with Him standing up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He asks them to feel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If you loved Me **you would be glad that I am going to the Father**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are grieving because He is leaving. He tells them the leaving is good news from His side of it, and asks them to think about where He is going rather than what they are losing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The Father is greater than I\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said in the same discourse as *I and My Father are one* and *he who has seen Me has seen the Father*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is speaking as the Son in the middle of the errand — sent, obeying, about to die. The greater is about position in the sending, not about nature; John has already settled the nature question in his first sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why He is telling them early"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have told you now, **before it happens**, so that **when it does happen you will believe**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does this repeatedly in these chapters. The advance notice is deliberate, and it is aimed at the moment afterward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ruler of this world"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The **ruler of this world** is coming. **He has nothing in Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *he has nothing in Me* — no hold, no claim, no matching part."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything that happens over the next eighteen hours happens to a man who owes nothing and can be charged with nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why He goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But the world has to know that **I love the Father**, and that **I do exactly as the Father has commanded Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives His own reason for the cross, and states it as love running upward."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's Gospel says both directions in the same book: *God so loved the world*, and this. See the note on John 10:16–18, where He says the Father loves Him because He lays His life down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last words of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get up now; let us go from here**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *arise, let us go hence.* He stands, and tells them to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oddity"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 15 opens *I am the true vine*, and the discourse runs three more chapters before the prayer of chapter 17 and the crossing of the Kidron in chapter 18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says *let us go* and then keeps talking. The likeliest reading is that they left the room here and the rest was said walking — through the city, past the temple with its great golden vine over the doors, down toward the valley."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is at the end of the walk"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He went out with His disciples over the Brook Kidron, where there was a garden.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Passover blood from the temple drained into that brook."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The words He uses again.** In Gethsemane, when the torches appear: *rise, let us be going. See, My betrayer is at hand.* The same instruction, at the other end of the walk, with the arrest party in view."}]}, "John 14:5–7": {"title": "How can we know the way", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted sentence in the chapter is an answer to a man admitting he is lost."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Thomas said to Him, \"**Lord, we do not know where You are going. How can we know the way?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the one who says it out loud. The others were presumably thinking it — Jesus has just said *where I go you know, and the way you know*, and Thomas contradicts Him flatly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Thomas is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same man who had said, a chapter earlier, when Jesus announced He was going back to Judea where they had tried to stone Him: *let us also go, that we may die with Him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Brave and blunt, in that order."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And later: *unless I see in His hands the print of the nails… I will not believe*. And then *my Lord and my God!* — the highest confession anybody makes in the Gospel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every time he appears he says the thing nobody else will."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am the way, and the truth, and the life**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ego eimi** — the seventh and last of the great *I am* sayings with a predicate, in a Gospel that has been building them: the bread, the light, the door, the good shepherd, the resurrection, the vine."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Three nouns"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not three qualities. Three definite articles: *the* way, *the* truth, *the* life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thomas asked one question, about the way. He gets three answers, and the second and third explain the first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name for the first Christians"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Way.* Before anybody said *Christian*, that is what the movement was called."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul went to Damascus *so that if he found any who were of the Way, whether men or women, he might bring them bound to Jerusalem*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Paul before Felix: *according to the Way which they call a sect, so I worship the God of my fathers*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They took the name from this sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exclusive clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No one comes to the Father except through Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **oudeis** — nobody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said in an upper room to eleven friends, not shouted at a crowd. It is a statement about access, and the same discourse has just opened with *in My Father's house are many mansions… I go to prepare a place for you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The destination"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Father. Every sentence in the paragraph is oriented that way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thomas asked about a road. The answer names the address."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If you had known Me, you would have known My Father as well**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a contrary-to-fact condition — you have not, and so you did not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**From now on you do know Him, and you have seen Him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ap' arti** — from this moment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something changes as He says it. The sentence before is about their ignorance; the sentence after says the ignorance is over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Philip does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, **show us the Father**, and it is sufficient for us.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer: *have I been with you so long, and yet you have not known Me, Philip? **He who has seen Me has seen the Father**.* See the note on John 14:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two disciples in four verses, both asking for something they are looking at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter has already promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let not your heart be troubled; you believe in God, believe also in Me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what it promises next: *I will pray the Father, and He will give you another Helper*. See the note on John 14:15–17."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who the sentence was for.** A man who had just said out loud that he did not know where they were going. The most exclusive claim in the Gospel is an answer to somebody admitting he was lost."}]}, "John 14:8–11": {"title": "\"Show us the Father\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Philip asks for one thing that would settle everything, and the answer is that he has been looking at it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lord, show us the Father, and that will be enough for us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a foolish thing to want. Moses asked to see God's glory. Philip is asking for the same and saying it would be sufficient."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pathos is in *that will be enough* — a man who thinks one more thing would do it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Have I been with you all this time, Philip, and you still do not know Me? Anyone who has seen Me has seen the Father.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three years of walking, eating and watching. Jesus is not surprised at the question so much as at who is asking it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the claim is unqualified: seeing Him **is** seeing the Father. Not a resemblance, not a good representation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two grounds He offers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The words I speak to you I do not speak on My own; it is the Father, living in Me, who is doing His work.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *believe Me when I tell you… or else believe because of the works themselves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives them two ways in — take His word for it, or reason back from what they have watched Him do. He does not insist on the first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What this settles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything a person guesses about God's character has to be checked against this face. If Jesus is what God looks like, then the disposition on display in the Gospels — toward lepers, tax collectors, the bent-over woman, the thief on the cross — is the Father's disposition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is nothing behind Jesus that is different from Jesus."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter opens.** *Do not let your hearts be troubled… I am going to prepare a place for you.* Philip's question comes minutes later, on the same night, from a man who has just been told the way and asked where it goes. The whole conversation is Jesus explaining Himself to people about to lose Him."}]}, "John 15:1–4": {"title": "\"My Father the gardener\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two people are named in the first sentence, and neither of them is the branch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The true vine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am the true vine**, and **My Father is the gardener**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel had been called a vine repeatedly, and every time the account ends in disappointment — Isaiah's vineyard that produced wild grapes, Jeremiah's noble vine gone degenerate, Psalm 80's vine brought out of Egypt and then broken down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**True** is **alethinos** — genuine, real, the authentic one. He is not correcting the image; He is claiming to be the thing the others were pictures of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gardener"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **georgos** — a farmer, a tiller of soil. The one who owns the vineyard and works it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both roles in the sentence belong to God. Nobody else is doing anything yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two operations"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Every branch in Me that **bears no fruit He takes away**, and every branch that **does bear fruit He prunes clean**, so that it will bear more.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek does a thing here that translation struggles with. **Airo** means to lift up, and also to take away — and vinedressers do lift trailing branches off the ground so they can get light."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kathairo** is to clean, to prune. The two verbs are almost the same word, and John put them side by side deliberately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both operations happen to branches, and both are the gardener's. The branch does not prune itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What pruning is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So that **it will bear more**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stated purpose is not correction. A vine is cut back hardest where it is producing, because unpruned wood makes leaves instead of grapes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Already clean"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You are already clean** because of the word I have spoken to you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same root as *prunes clean*, one verse later. He uses the pruning word about them and then says the work is done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the agent named is His word — not their effort, not the pruning yet to come."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judas has just left the room. John notes it at the end of chapter 13: *he then, having received the piece of bread, went out immediately. And it was night.* See the note on John 13:27–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Remain in Me**, and I will remain in you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meno** — abide, stay, remain. It appears eleven times in this chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not work, not produce, not strive. Stay attached."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**A branch cannot bear fruit by itself**; it has to remain in the vine. **Neither can you**, unless you remain in Me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument is from horticulture. A branch on the ground does not make grapes more slowly; it makes none."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the sentence that follows in the next verse: *apart from Me you can do nothing.* See the note on John 15:5–8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where He said it.** On the way from the upper room to Gethsemane, at night — possibly passing the temple, whose gate carried a great golden vine, or through vineyards on the way to the Kidron. He is hours from the arrest and talking about fruit."}]}, "John 15:12–15": {"title": "\"I no longer call you servants\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He changes what He calls them, and gives the reason — and the reason is about information."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is My command: **love one another as I have loved you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The standard is not a level of effort. It is a comparison, and the second half of it has already happened in front of them — He washed their feet an hour earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The greatest love"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"No one has greater love than this: **to lay down his life for his friends**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is hours from doing it, and He says it in the present tense while walking to the garden."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I do not call you **servants** any longer.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **doulos** — a slave, an owned person. It is what these men had been calling themselves and what Paul would call himself for the rest of his life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason He gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because **a servant does not know what his master is doing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the definition offered. Not that a slave is mistreated, or that he works harder. He is not told."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A slave receives instructions. He does not receive the plan."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason for the new name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have called you **friends**, because **everything I heard from My Father I have made known to you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same test, the other way round. They are friends because they have been told."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The change of status is measured by disclosure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this is said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the supper and the arrest, in the middle of the longest continuous speech in the Gospels — the upper room discourse. He spends the last free hours of His life explaining things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That itself is the demonstration of the verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What went with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You are My friends **if you do what I command you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The command in question is stated in verse 12 and repeated in verse 17: love one another."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The friend of God before this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham is the only person in the Old Testament called it — *the friend of God*, in James and in Isaiah — and the moment it is shown is Genesis 18, when God says *shall I hide from Abraham what I am doing?* and tells him about Sodom before it happens."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same test again: he was told. See the note on Genesis 18:16–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You did not choose Me, but I chose you** and appointed you that you should go and bear fruit.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And after that, the warning that the world will hate them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of the sentence.** He does not say they became friends and were then let into the plan. He says they are friends **because** everything He heard has been made known — the telling is what the friendship consists of."}]}, "John 15:22–25": {"title": "They would not be guilty", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He explains what His coming did to the standing of the people who rejected Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this is said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Still at the table, after the vine and before the promise of the Helper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just told them *if the world hates you, you know that it hated Me before it hated you* — and *a servant is not greater than his master*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If I had not come and spoken to them, they would not be guilty of sin**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *they would not have sin* — **hamartian ouk eichosan**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that they were innocent before. The whole New Testament argues the opposite. This is about one particular guilt: the guilt of having heard Him and refused."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cover"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But now they have no cover for their sin**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **prophasin** — a pretext, an excuse, a plausible cover story."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had one, and the coming removed it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul makes the same move in Athens: *truly, these times of ignorance God overlooked, but now commands all men everywhere to repent*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The middle sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whoever hates Me hates My Father as well**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated flatly, with no argument attached."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the negative of what He has already said in the same room: *he who has seen Me has seen the Father*. See the note on John 14:8–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If I had not done among them the works no one else has ever done**, they would not be guilty of sin.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice, the same structure: had I not spoken, had I not worked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Words and deeds, and both count as evidence. John's Gospel is built on the works — he calls them **semeia**, signs, and structures the whole first half around seven of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But now **they have seen those works**, and they have **hated both Me and My Father**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seeing is granted. The passage does not say they missed anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lazarus is the case in point. The chief priests' response to a man being raised was to plot to kill Lazarus as well."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And so the word written in **their law** comes true: **they hated Me for no reason at all**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Their law** — He says it that way, of the Scriptures He is quoting from, to men who claimed them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The line is from Psalm 69, and the Hebrew is **sinah chinnam** — hatred without cause. See the note on Psalms 69:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same psalm supplies the gall and the vinegar at the crucifixion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What *chinnam* means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Free of charge. Gratis. It is the word Satan uses of Job — *does Job fear God for nothing?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hatred with nothing owing on it, given away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But when the Helper comes, whom I shall send to you from the Father, the Spirit of truth who proceeds from the Father, **He will testify of Me**. And you also will bear witness, because you have been with Me from the beginning.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The paragraph about hatred ends with the arrangement for testimony."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They will put you out of the synagogues; yes, the time is coming that whoever kills you will think that he offers God a service.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And His reason for telling them: *these things I have told you, that when the time comes, you may remember that I told you of them*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says from the cross"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Father, forgive them, for **they do not know what they do**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He argued here that the excuse was gone. Six hours later He pleads it for them anyway."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the coming changed.** Not the fact of guilt — the availability of a pretext. And the sentence He picks to describe the response is one about hatred given away free."}]}, "John 15:26–27": {"title": "Two witnesses", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The fourth of the Helper passages, and this one gives Him a job with a courtroom name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the end of the chapter about hatred: *if the world hates you, you know that it hated Me before it hated you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the quotation from the psalm — *they hated Me without a cause*. See the note on John 15:22–25."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then this. The paragraph about being hated ends with an arrangement for testimony."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**When the Helper comes**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parakletos** — one called alongside. An advocate, a counsel for the defence, a supporter in court."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John uses it four times in this discourse, and once in his letter, of Christ: *we have an Advocate with the Father*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is legal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who sends Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whom **I will send to you from the Father** — the Spirit of truth, **who comes from the Father**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both, in one sentence. Sent by the Son, proceeding from the Father."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This clause is the one the Eastern and Western churches split over in the eleventh century — the **filioque**, whether the Spirit proceeds from the Father *and the Son*. The Great Schism runs through this verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The Spirit of truth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's usual title for Him. He uses it three times in the discourse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the Spirit of power, in this Gospel. Truth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The work"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He will be the witness to the truth about Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **marturesei peri emou** — He will testify concerning Me."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Spirit's stated function here is to give evidence about somebody else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus says the same in the next chapter: *He will not speak on His own authority… **He will glorify Me**, for He will take of what is Mine and declare it to you*. See the note on John 16:12–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second witness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**And you also are to tell it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb. **Martureite** — you bear witness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two witnesses, which is what the law required: *by the mouth of two or three witnesses the matter shall be established*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Spirit and the disciples, testifying about the same person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their qualification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because **you have been with Me from the beginning**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not training. Presence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the criterion Peter applies when they replace Judas: *of these men who have accompanied us all the time that the Lord Jesus went in and out among us… one of these must become a witness with us of His resurrection*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the pairing turns up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And we are His witnesses to these things, and so also is the Holy Spirit whom God has given to those who obey Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter, before the Sanhedrin, in Acts 5. The same two witnesses, standing in an actual court."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word that came from it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Martus** — witness. It drifted into English as *martyr*, because of what happened to the people in this room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"These things I have spoken to you, **that you should not be made to stumble**. They will put you out of the synagogues; yes, the time is coming that whoever kills you will think that he offers God a service.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the reason He is telling them in advance: *that when the time comes, you may remember that I told you of them*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is what Saul of Tarsus thought he was doing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Helper is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not comfort in the modern sense. The old English *comforter* is from **confortare** — to strengthen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is described in this Gospel as teaching, reminding, convicting, guiding, and — here — testifying."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The Spirit's stated job in this verse.** Evidence about somebody else. And the disciples are the second witness, on the strength of having been in the room."}]}, "John 15:5–8": {"title": "\"I am the vine; you are the branches\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last of the seven *I am* sayings in John, spoken on the way to Gethsemane."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What a branch does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am the vine; **you are the branches**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A branch does not manufacture fruit. It does not decide to produce grapes, work at producing grapes, or know how a grape is made."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is attached, and the sap does the rest. Everything the fruit is made of comes up through the vine."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whoever remains in Me**, and I in him, is the one who bears much fruit.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meno** — to remain, to stay, to abide. It occurs eleven times in this chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not work, not strive, not produce. Stay where you are."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because **apart from Me you can do nothing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *very little*. Nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is an absolute, and He states it as the reason the previous sentence is true."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which vine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel had been called a vine before. Isaiah 5: God planted a vineyard, cleared the stones, put in the choicest vine — *and He looked that it should bring forth grapes, and it brought forth wild grapes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 80: *You brought a vine out of Egypt.* Jeremiah: *I planted you a noble vine… how then have you turned into the degenerate plant of an alien vine?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every time, the vine fails."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then, in an upper room: *I am the **true** vine.* The Greek is genuine, real, the authentic one — the vine the others were pictures of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says about the asking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If you remain in Me, and **My words remain in you**, you may ask whatever you want, and it will be done for you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The condition is not a formula. It is remaining, and His words remaining in you — and out of that, the asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Jacob's line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He will tie his donkey to the vine, his colt to the choicest branch. He will wash his garments in wine.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob said it over Judah on a deathbed in Egypt. Sixteen hundred years later a Man of Judah's tribe sat in an upper room in Jerusalem, on the night of a cup, and said *I am the vine.* See the note on Genesis 49:8–12."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He says the fruit is for.** *This is how **My Father** is glorified: that you bear much fruit.* The branch is not producing to prove anything about itself. The whole crop goes to the vinedresser."}]}, "John 15:9–11": {"title": "Make your home in My love", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses, and He tells them what the whole vine passage was for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**As the Father has loved Me, so I have loved you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kathos** — in the same way, to the same degree."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives the amount by naming a love nobody in the room could quantify. There is no smaller unit offered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Make your home in My love**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meinate** — abide, remain, stay put. The same verb He has used ten times in the eight verses before this about the vine and the branches."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *get into* His love. Stay where you already are."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What abiding is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A branch does not work at being attached. It hangs there, and the sap arrives."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just said it: *the branch cannot bear fruit of itself, unless it abides in the vine.* See the note on John 15:4–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The keeping"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If you keep My commands you will remain in My love**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And immediately the pattern: *\"**just as I have kept My Father's commands and remain in His love**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second clause defines the first. Nobody thinks the Son's obedience earned the Father's love — it is the way He stayed in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the command turns out to be"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses later: *\"**This is My commandment, that you love one another as I have loved you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The commands, plural, resolve into one, and it is the one thing He has just been doing to them at the table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have told you these things so that** My joy may be in you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tauta lelaleka humin** — a formula John uses through the discourse. He says it four times, and each time the stated reason is for their benefit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That your joy may be full.* *That you should not be made to stumble.* *That in Me you may have peace.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose joy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My joy** may be in you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not joy He gives, or joy about Him. His own."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And this is said on the night of the arrest, hours from Gethsemane."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews accounts for it: *who **for the joy that was set before Him** endured the cross, despising the shame.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fullness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **your joy may be full**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **plerothe** — filled up, brought to the brim."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John writes the same word in his letter about the same thing: *these things we write to you that your joy may be full.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the joy comes from in the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is: His love, stayed in — then His joy in them — then their joy filled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joy is third in the sequence and never the thing worked at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says a few verses on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No longer do I call you servants**, for a servant does not know what his master is doing; but **I have called you friends**, for all things that I heard from My Father I have made known to you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for *friends* is **philous** — the ones He is fond of. See the note on John 16:25–28, where He tells them the Father is fond of them too."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the vine chapter was for.** He says it plainly in verse 11. Not productivity — His joy in them, and theirs filled to the brim."}]}, "John 16:12–15": {"title": "A Spirit who does not speak about Himself", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The clearest thing Jesus ever says about how the Spirit works, and it is almost entirely about restraint."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus withholds, and why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is much more I have to say to you, but you are not able to carry it now.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is about bearing a weight. He is not keeping anything back for the sake of mystery; He is not loading men who are already at their limit. It is the night before the arrest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That sets up everything after it. The Spirit's coming is described first as the means by which the rest arrives — later, when they can carry it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things He does not do"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**He will not speak on His own** — He speaks what He hears", "**He will glorify Me** — not Himself", "**He will take what is Mine and make it known**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus says the second one outright: the Spirit's work is to make Christ look like what He is. That is a usable test. Something that draws attention to itself, or to the person carrying it, is not doing what is described here."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"Everything the Father has is Mine.\"** Jesus adds this as the explanation for why the Spirit taking what is His counts as taking from the Father too. In four lines all three are named and their work is shown to be one work — the Father's possession, the Son's to give, the Spirit's to deliver. It is one of the plainest trinitarian sentences in the Gospels, and it arrives as an aside."}]}, "John 16:23–24": {"title": "\"Until now you have not asked for anything in My name\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus tells the disciples that something is about to change in how they pray, and He is precise about what."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What changes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On that day you will not ask Me anything.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "For three years they had asked Him directly — He was standing there. After the cross and the ascension the arrangement alters: *whatever you ask the Father in My name, He will give you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a downgrade. The request now goes to the Father, and it carries the Son's name on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In My name\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a formula appended to the end of a prayer. A name in that world meant authority to act on someone's behalf — the way a servant buying at market in his master's name was doing the master's business with the master's credit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So praying in His name is asking for what He would ask for, backed by who He is rather than by who we are."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence that stings a little"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until now you have not asked for anything in My name.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been with Him the whole time and had never once done this. It was not possible yet — the door He is describing opens at the resurrection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it without reproach. It is a statement about timing, and the next words are an invitation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ask, and you will receive, **so that your joy may be full**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stated purpose of answered prayer here is not efficiency or provision. It is joy — filled up, brought to the brim."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it sits.** This is in the middle of the last conversation before Gethsemane, in a passage about sorrow turning to joy like a woman after childbirth. He is preparing men for the worst night of their lives by telling them how they will pray afterwards."}]}, "John 16:25–28": {"title": "The Father Himself loves you", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last thing He tells them before He prays, and it is about who they will be asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The end of the upper-room discourse. Four chapters of it, and the next words out of His mouth are *Father, the hour has come* — the prayer of John 17. See the note on John 17:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pictures"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have been saying all this to you **in pictures**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paroimiais** — figures, veiled sayings, proverbs. John's word where the other Gospels say *parables*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The vine, the branches, the woman in labour, the sheepfold, the grain of wheat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The hour is coming when I will **no longer speak to you in pictures**, but will **tell you about the Father plainly**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parresia** — openly, with freedom of speech, without concealment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word John uses for the confidence believers have in approaching God. The plain speaking runs both ways."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**On that day** you will ask in My name.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not now. That day is after the resurrection, and the disciples do exactly this from Acts onward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **I am not saying to you that I will ask the Father on your behalf**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He heads off the assumption before they make it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have just watched Him say *I will pray the Father, and He will give you another Helper* — and He now removes the picture that would leave them with: a reluctant Father who has to be worked on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Because the Father Himself loves you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **autos gar ho pater philei humas** — *for the Father Himself is fond of you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Phileo**, not agapao. The warm word. The word for the affection between friends."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The intercession is not needed for the Father's willingness. It never was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ground given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Since you have loved Me** and have **believed that I came from God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things, and both are things the disciples did — loved Him, and believed where He came from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole qualification listed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four-clause summary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I came from the Father** and **entered the world**; now I am **leaving the world** and **going back to the Father**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Out, in, out, back. The shortest account of the incarnation in the Gospels, said by the one doing it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The disciples' reaction is immediate: *see, now You are speaking plainly, and using no figure of speech! Now we are sure that You know all things… by this we believe that You came forth from God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says to that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do you now believe? Indeed the hour is coming, yes, has now come, that you will be scattered**, each to his own, and will leave Me alone. And yet I am not alone, because the Father is with Me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the last sentence of the discourse: *these things I have spoken to you, that in Me you may have peace. In the world you will have tribulation; but be of good cheer, **I have overcome the world**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same thing is said elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who did not spare His own Son, but delivered Him up for us all, how shall He not with Him also freely give us all things?* See the note on Romans 8:31–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God so loved the world that He gave.* The giving followed the love; it did not produce it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The sentence He wanted them not to misread.** Not that He talks the Father round. *The Father Himself loves you* — and the verb is the fond one."}]}, "John 17:1–5": {"title": "\"This is eternal life\" — the definition Jesus gives", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "In the middle of the longest prayer of His recorded, Jesus stops to define a term, and the definition is not what most people would guess."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The definition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And this is eternal life: that they know You, the only true God, and Jesus Christ, whom You sent.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not endless duration. Not a destination. **Knowing a person** — and the Greek verb is the ordinary one for knowing by acquaintance and experience, not by information."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Duration is real and John says so elsewhere. But when Jesus supplies the definition Himself, He puts the emphasis on relationship rather than length."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The hour has come\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "John has been counting down to this since chapter 2, where Jesus tells His mother His hour has *not* come. It is said again in chapter 7, and again in chapter 8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here it arrives, and what it means is the cross. The prayer that follows is spoken by a man who knows what the next eighteen hours contain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I have finished the work\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said before the arrest. The verb is the same root as *it is finished* on the cross — completed, brought to its end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He speaks of the work as done while the hardest part is still ahead, which is consistent with everything else in John: the crucifixion is described throughout as a lifting up and a glorifying, not as a defeat that gets reversed later."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The request.** *Glorify Me in Your own presence with the glory I had with You before the world began.* He does not ask for something new. He asks for the return of something He had and set down — which is what Paul describes in Philippians 2 as emptying Himself. The prayer assumes a life before Bethlehem."}]}, "John 17:13–16": {"title": "\"Not to take them out of the world\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "In the middle of the prayer, He asks for one thing and specifically declines to ask for another."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now **I am coming to You**, and I am saying these things **while I am still in the world**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He prays out loud, in front of them, on purpose. John is recording a private conversation the disciples were meant to overhear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So that they may have **My joy made full in them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His joy, not a joy of their own. The Greek is *fulfilled*, brought to the top."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said hours before the arrest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have given them Your word**, and the world has hated them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two clauses are joined as cause and effect. What He handed over is what draws the hostility."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason for the hatred"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because **they are not of the world, just as I am not of the world**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice in four verses — once in verse 14 and again in verse 16, identically."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's Gospel repeats things when it wants them settled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request He does not make"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am not asking You to take them out of the world**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He states the prayer He is declining to pray."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They stay. Everything that follows in Acts — the beatings, the shipwrecks, the prisons, the markets and synagogues and courtrooms — happens because of this sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request He does make"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But to keep them from the evil one**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek can be *from evil* or *from the evil one*; the same phrase ends the Lord's Prayer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Keep** is **tereo** — to guard, to watch over, to hold in custody. He has already used it of Himself in verse 12: *while I was with them, I kept them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is handing over a watch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things held together"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not out of it, and kept inside it. The prayer asks for protection in the place rather than removal from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He asks next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Sanctify them by Your truth**. Your word is truth. **As You sent Me into the world, I also have sent them** into the world.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sent in, not called out. The commission in the next breath repeats the decision."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who else is in the prayer.** *I do not pray for these alone, but also for those who will believe in Me through their word.* Two verses later, everybody reading it is named in it."}]}, "John 17:17–19": {"title": "Set apart by the truth", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three sentences in the prayer, and the same verb runs through all of them — including one He applies to Himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Set them apart for Yourself by the truth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hagiason** — sanctify, make holy, set apart for a purpose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word used of the tabernacle vessels, the priests, and the Sabbath. Something ordinary designated for one use."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Your word is truth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words, and no article on *truth* in the Greek — *your word **is** truth*, not merely true."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the instrument. Not effort, not separation from the world, not ritual."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just asked the Father *not to take them out of the world*. See the note on John 17:13–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sending"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**As You sent Me into the world, so I have sent them into the world**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two sendings are put in the same sentence with the same verb, and the second is measured by the first."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Into the world** — the second time in five verses He insists on the direction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **for their sake I set Myself apart**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb, **hagiazo**, and now He is both the one doing it and the one it is done to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not being made holy. There is nothing in Him to purify — He had said so an hour earlier: *the ruler of this world is coming, and he has nothing in Me.* See the note on John 14:28–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sense is the older one: set apart for a purpose. A priest consecrating himself for what he is about to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He was setting Himself apart for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The garden is on the other side of this prayer. John 18 opens *when Jesus had spoken these words, He went out with His disciples over the Brook Kidron.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So that they too may be truly set apart by the truth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *sanctified in truth*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His consecration is stated as the ground of theirs. The order in the sentence runs one way only."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who else is in the prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verses later: *\"I do not pray for these alone, but **also for those who will believe in Me through their word**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everybody reading it is named in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He asks for them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That they all may be one, as You, Father, are in Me, and I in You.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at the end: *\"Father, I desire that they also whom You gave Me **may be with Me where I am**, that they may behold My glory.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this is prayed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Out loud, in front of them, between the supper and the arrest. See the note on John 17:9–12."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The three uses of one verb.** Set them apart; I send them as I was sent; I set Myself apart for them. The middle clause is the errand, and the outer two are the same act done at both ends of it."}]}, "John 17:20–23": {"title": "\"Loved them just as You have loved Me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last section of the longest prayer of Jesus recorded anywhere, and it widens to include people who did not exist yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who He is praying for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am **not praying for these alone**. I am praying also for **everyone who will believe in Me through their word**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven men in a room, and He prays past them to everybody who would believe on the strength of what they said."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is every Christian who has ever lived, prayed for by name of category, hours before the arrest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**That they may all be one**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said four times in these verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The standard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Just as You, Father, are in Me and I am in You**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one in the sense of an alliance or an agreement. The comparison offered is the union of the Father and the Son."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So that **the world may believe** that You sent Me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And again in the next verse: *\"so that **the world may know** that You sent Me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice, and both times the reason for the unity is what people outside would conclude from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He gave them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The glory You gave Me I have given to them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past tense, said the night before the crucifixion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever that glory is, He describes it as already handed over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"…and have **loved them just as You have loved Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not loved them well, or loved them greatly. *Just as* — the same measure as the Father's love for the Son."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks the world to know two things: that He was sent, and that these people are loved on that scale."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the prayer has come from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 17 has three movements: He prays for Himself, then for the eleven, then for everybody who will believe."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For the eleven: *I have kept them; not one of them is lost except the son of perdition.* See the note on John 17:9–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Jesus had spoken these words, **He went out with His disciples over the Brook Kidron**, where there was a garden.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer ends and He walks straight to Gethsemane."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does not pray"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here asks for their circumstances to improve. Earlier in the chapter: *I do not pray that You should take them out of the world, but that You should keep them from the evil one.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He says about being heard.** Standing at Lazarus's tomb: *Father, I thank You that You have heard Me. And I know that You always hear Me.* This prayer was prayed by somebody who had said that."}]}, "John 17:24–26": {"title": "\"That they may be with Me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last three verses of the prayer, and the first word of them is not a request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Father, **I want**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **thelo** — I will, I desire."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everywhere else in the prayer He asks: *I pray for them*, *keep them*, *sanctify them*. Here He states what He wants."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He wants"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ones **You have given Me** to be **with Me where I am**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Given Me** — the phrase runs through the whole prayer. He uses it seven times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Where I am** — the same words He used in the upper room: *I go to prepare a place for you… that where I am, there you may be also.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that they may **see My glory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not share it, in this clause. See it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had asked earlier in the prayer for the other thing: *the glory which You gave Me I have given them*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the glory comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The glory **You gave Me because You loved Me before the world was made**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *before the foundation of the world*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The loving is dated earlier than the creation, and the glory is attributed to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Righteous Father.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has said *Holy Father* earlier in the prayer, and *Father* throughout. This is the only *righteous* one, and it comes attached to a statement about the world not knowing Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three knowings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The world **has not known You**, but **I have known You**, and **these have known that You sent Me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three clauses in descending scale — the world, Himself, and the eleven men in the room."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What the disciples know is the smallest of the three, and it is enough to put them on the right side of the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have **made Your name known to them**, and **I will go on making it known**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two tenses. Done, and continuing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said it at the start of the prayer: *I have manifested Your name to the men whom You have given Me out of the world.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the name is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a sound. In Hebrew usage a name is what somebody is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the Gospels the disclosure has a specific shape: He teaches them to say **Father**, and He says **Abba** in Gethsemane — a word so particular that Paul and Mark both leave it untranslated. See the note on Galatians 4:4–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **the love with which You loved Me may be in them, and I in them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the love with which You loved Me* — the same love, not a comparable one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the last request of the prayer, and it asks for the love named in verse 24 — the one that predates the world — to be located inside people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last four words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And I in them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer ends there. The next verse is *when Jesus had spoken these words, He went out with His disciples over the Brook Kidron.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was listening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven men, hours from scattering. One of them would deny Him before morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He prays this over them out loud."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He asks for last.** Not protection and not success. That the love He was loved with before the world existed would be inside the people in the room — and then He goes out to the garden."}]}, "John 17:6–8": {"title": "I have made Your name known", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He tells the Father what He did, and it is one thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The night before the crucifixion, at the end of the upper room discourse. He has finished speaking to the disciples and now speaks to the Father with them listening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus spoke these words, lifted up His eyes to heaven, and said, \"Father, the hour has come.\"* See the note on John 17:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The summary of His work"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have made Your name known** to the people You gave Me out of the world.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ephanerosa sou to onoma** — I manifested Your name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it again at the end of the prayer: *I have declared to them Your name, and will declare it*. See the note on John 17:24–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer opens and closes with the same claim, and it is the only summary of His ministry He gives here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Father.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses it six times in this prayer — *Father*, *Holy Father*, *righteous Father*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Old Testament calls God Father a handful of times, and always of the nation collectively. Nobody addressed Him that way individually until Jesus did, and then taught others to: *when you pray, say, Our Father*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The giving"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**They were Yours; You gave them to Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice in one verse. They belonged to the Father first."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same construction runs through the chapter: *those whom You gave Me I have kept*, and *I pray for those whom You have given Me*. See the note on John 17:9–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **they have kept Your word**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said the night they all abandoned Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is hours from denying Him three times, and Jesus is telling the Father these men have kept His word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they know"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now they know that **everything You have given Me comes from You**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **egnokan** — perfect tense. They have come to know it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How they came to know it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because **I gave them the words You gave Me**, and **they took them in**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **elabon** — they received."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two handings: Father to Son, Son to them. And a receiving at the end of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The certainty"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They know for certain that **I came from You**, and they have believed that **You sent Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Alethos** — truly, for certain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses about origin and mission, and Jesus assigns both to men who are about to scatter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the assessment is like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Generous past the evidence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter said it in the earlier crisis — *we have come to believe, and we know* — after most of the crowd left. See the note on John 6:66–71."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The disciples' own next line in this chapter's context is *now we are sure that You know all things*, and Jesus answers *do you now believe? Indeed the hour is coming that you will be scattered*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He knows exactly what they are and still describes them to the Father this way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the prayer asks for next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Holy Father, **keep through Your name those whom You have given Me**, that they may be one as We are.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name He manifested becomes the thing they are kept by."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the name ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They shall see His face, and **His name shall be on their foreheads**.* See the note on Revelation 22:3–5."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one-line summary.** Asked what He had done, He says: *I have made Your name known.* And the name is Father."}]}, "John 17:9–12": {"title": "\"Not one of them is lost\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus prays for a specific group of people, and the way He describes them is worth reading slowly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ones You have given Me, because they are Yours… Everything that is Mine is Yours, and everything Yours is Mine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are described as a gift from the Father to the Son, and the ownership is mutual. Nothing in the passage describes them as having attached themselves to Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four times in these verses they are *the ones You have given Me*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Holy Father, keep them safe by Your own name.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not by their vigilance. By the Father's name — the same name Jesus says He had been keeping them safe by while He was with them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The request is for continuity: I am leaving, You keep doing what I have been doing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The report He gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Those You gave Me I guarded, and not one of them is lost.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is hours from the cross, giving an account of the men He was given — men who are about to scatter, one of whom will deny Him three times before morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He counts Peter among the not-lost while knowing exactly what Peter is going to do that night."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Except the one doomed to be lost.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not soften it and neither should any reading of it. Judas is named as an exception, and the sentence attaches it to Scripture being fulfilled rather than to a failure of keeping."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why it matters that He prayed it out loud.** The disciples were in the room. Jesus could have prayed this privately; John records it because it was said in front of them. Men who were about to fail badly heard themselves described as given, guarded, and kept — before the failing happened."}]}, "John 18:4–9": {"title": "Let these men go", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The arrest, and the man being arrested runs it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He knew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus **knew everything that was coming toward Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John says it before anything happens. There is no ambush in his account."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase opens the foot-washing: *Jesus, knowing that the Father had given all things into His hands*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction of travel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **walked out to meet them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exelthon** — went out. Toward the torches."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew, Mark and Luke have Judas arrive and kiss Him. John has Him step forward first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who came"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A detachment of soldiers with the officers of the chief priests and Pharisees, *with lanterns, torches, and weapons*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *detachment* is **speira** — a cohort. At full strength that is six hundred men, and even a maniple is two hundred."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They brought lanterns to find a man who was waiting for them at full moon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Who are you looking for?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks it, and He asks it twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Jesus of Nazareth**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am He**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ego eimi** — two words, and the pronoun is not required by the grammar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the sentence from the burning bush. See the note on Exodus 3:4–6. John has used it all through the Gospel — *before Abraham was, I AM*, and *I am the bread*, *the door*, *the good shepherd*, *the resurrection and the life*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail John inserts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Judas, **the man who was handing Him over, was standing there with them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not doing anything. Standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **drew back and fell to the ground**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An armed cohort, on its face, in a garden."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not explain it and does not dwell on it. One sentence, and he moves on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second asking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So He asked them **again**, \"Who are you looking for?\" And again they said, \"Jesus of Nazareth.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He waits for them to get up and starts the exchange over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have already told you that I am He. **So if I am the one you want, let these men go**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A demand, from the accused, at the moment of arrest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is granted. Eleven men walk out of that garden."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why John says it happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He said it so that **the word He had spoken would come true: Of those You gave Me, I have not lost one**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "From the prayer an hour earlier: *those whom You gave Me I have kept, and none of them is lost except the son of perdition*. See the note on John 17:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John treats a sentence about physical safety in a garden as the fulfilment of a sentence about eternal keeping. He makes no distinction between the two."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Draws a sword and takes off a man's ear. Jesus heals it, and says *put your sword into the sheath. Shall I not drink the cup which My Father has given Me?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one disciple who fights is the one who denies Him within the hour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He had said about it beforehand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No one takes it from Me, but I lay it down of Myself**. I have power to lay it down, and I have power to take it again.\"* See the note on John 10:16–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six hundred men with torches, and a sentence that put them on the ground."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one thing He asks for.** Not release, and not a hearing. *Let these men go* — and John files it as a promise being kept."}]}, "John 19:1–3": {"title": "A crown made of thorns", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses, and everything in them is a costume."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Pilate does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Pilate took Jesus and **had Him flogged**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **emastigosen** — scourged."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roman flogging used a **flagrum**, leather thongs weighted with bone or metal. Jewish law limited a beating to forty strokes and the practice was thirty-nine; Rome had no limit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John places it in the middle of the trial rather than at the end. Pilate is trying to produce something pitiful enough to satisfy the crowd — *I will therefore chastise Him and release Him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crown"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The soldiers **twisted together a crown of thorns** and set it on His head.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **plexantes stephanon ex akanthon** — having plaited a wreath out of thorns."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Stephanos** is the victor's wreath at the games, not the royal **diadema**. See the note on 1 Peter 5:4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are mocking a triumph, not a coronation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where thorns come from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Cursed is the ground for your sake… **both thorns and thistles it shall bring forth for you**.* See the note on Genesis 3:17–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing the ground produces after the curse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the parable of the sower, thorns are what chokes the word — *the cares of this world, the deceitfulness of riches*. See the note on Luke 8:11–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The robe"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they put **a purple robe** on Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Purple was imperial. The dye came from murex shellfish and was ruinously expensive; Roman law restricted who could wear it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark says scarlet, which is what a soldier's cloak was. Either way it is a discarded military cloak being used as a costume."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The greeting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they **kept saying, \"Hail, King of the Jews!\"**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is an imperfect — they went on saying it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chaire** — the formal salutation. *Ave, Caesar* is the same word in Latin."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they **struck Him with their hands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **rapismata** — blows with the flat of the hand, slaps."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew adds the reed sceptre put into His hand and then taken back to hit Him with, and the kneeling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole scene is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A mock investiture. Crown, robe, sceptre, salute, homage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The soldiers assembled every element of a coronation and got every one of them right in form and wrong in intent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Pilate does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Brings Him out: *behold the Man!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And later, from the judgment seat: *behold your King!* — and the chief priests answer *we have no king but Caesar*. See the note on John 19:12–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one thing they were not wrong about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The title."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pilate writes it and refuses to change it: *Jesus of Nazareth, the King of the Jews*, in three languages."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What I have written, I have written.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the wreath goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And I looked, and behold, a white horse. He who sat on it had a bow; and **a crown was given to him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at the end: *on His head were **many crowns** — **diademata**, the royal ones."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wreath in this passage is **stephanos**. The crowns at the return are the other word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah had said about the face"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His visage was marred more than any man.* And *I gave My back to those who struck Me, and My cheeks to those who plucked out the beard; **I did not hide My face from shame and spitting**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on Isaiah 53:1–3."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the soldiers assembled.** Crown, robe, sceptre and salute — a complete coronation, put together as a joke, out of a thornbush and a cast-off cloak."}]}, "John 19:12–16": {"title": "We have no king but Caesar", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The trial ends with the chief priests saying something they cannot have meant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Pilate's position"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From then on Pilate **kept trying to release Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is an imperfect — he went on seeking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has already said *I find no fault in Him* three times, sent Him to Herod, offered Barabbas, and had Him flogged in the hope that it would be enough."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lever"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If you let this man go, you are no friend of Caesar**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Friend of Caesar** — **amicus Caesaris** — was a formal honour, an actual title granted to favoured officials."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pilate's patron in Rome, Sejanus, had been executed for treason a few years earlier, which left every man he had appointed exposed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence is a threat about his career and he understands it immediately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge restated"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Anyone who **makes himself a king** is setting himself against Caesar.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have moved the charge. It started as blasphemy, which Rome did not care about, and has been converted into sedition, which Rome executed people for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bench"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He brought Jesus outside and **took his seat on the judgment bench**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **bema** — the raised platform a Roman magistrate pronounced sentence from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At the place called **the Stone Pavement — in Hebrew, Gabbatha**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Lithostrotos** in Greek — a paved area. **Gabbatha** in Aramaic, probably from a root meaning height or ridge."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John gives both names, as he does with Golgotha and Siloam. He is writing for people who could walk to these places."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was **the day of preparation for the Passover**, about the **sixth hour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Noon, on the day the lambs were killed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark says He was crucified at the third hour, and the difference between the two counts has been argued over for centuries — Roman reckoning from midnight against Jewish from sunrise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What John is doing is fixing the time by the temple. At this hour the priests began killing lambs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The presentation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Pilate said, \"**Here is your King**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ide ho basileus humon**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said *behold the Man* an hour earlier. Now *behold your King*, and he means it as an insult to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shout"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Away with Him! Away with Him! Crucify Him!**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Am I to crucify your King?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pilate asks it, and it is the last thing he says before giving in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**We have no king but Caesar**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said by the chief priests. The men whose entire office existed on the claim that God was Israel's king."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Passover liturgy itself has the line *besides You we have no king, redeemer or saviour*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They said it at noon on the day of preparation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Samuel had said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "When Israel first asked for a king: *they have not rejected you, but **they have rejected Me, that I should not reign over them***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sign said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pilate wrote it: **Jesus of Nazareth, the King of the Jews**, in Hebrew, Greek and Latin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chief priests asked him to change it to *He said, I am King of the Jews*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Pilate answered, \"**What I have written, I have written**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives them the crucifixion and refuses them the wording."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the sentence cost.** *We have no king but Caesar* — said by the priesthood, in the temple city, at the hour the Passover lambs were being killed."}]}, "John 19:17–18": {"title": "In the middle", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses for the crucifixion itself. John spends more words on the sign over it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The carrying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Carrying **His own cross**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **bastazon heauto ton stauron**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The condemned man carried the crossbeam — the **patibulum** — to the site, where the upright already stood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The other three Gospels have Simon of Cyrene pressed into carrying it. John does not mention him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are true of one walk. He started with it and could not finish."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **went out** to the place called the Place of a Skull.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Went out** — outside the wall."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews takes that geography and makes an argument from it: *the bodies of those animals whose blood is brought into the sanctuary are burned outside the camp. Therefore Jesus also… suffered outside the gate*. See the note on Hebrews 13:7–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which in Hebrew is called **Golgotha**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Gulgolta** — skull. The Latin is **calvaria**, which gives *Calvary*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Why it was called that is not stated. A rock formation, a place of execution, or both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John gives the local name, as he does with Gabbatha and Siloam. He is writing for people who could walk there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There **they crucified Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in Greek."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No description of the nails, the beam, the raising, or the pain. All four Gospels do this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Romans crucified thousands; every reader knew exactly what the word covered. The writers say it once and move on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The others"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **two others with Him, one on each side**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew and Mark call them **lestai** — robbers, or insurrectionists. Luke records the conversation between them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The position"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **Jesus in the middle**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meson de ton Iesoun**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John records where He was standing in the row."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The centre position was the place of the principal offender — the one the execution was arranged for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it fulfils"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He was numbered with the transgressors.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 53, and Luke has Him quote it that evening: *for I say to you that this which is written must still be accomplished in Me: \"And He was numbered with the transgressors.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens between the two crosses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, remember me when You come into Your kingdom.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Assuredly, I say to you, today you will be with Me in Paradise.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One of the two changes his mind in the last hours of his life, and gets a promise with a deadline of *today*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John spends his verses on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the crucifixion. The sign."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now Pilate wrote a title and put it on the cross. And the writing was: **JESUS OF NAZARETH, THE KING OF THE JEWS**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then many of the Jews read this title… and it was written in **Hebrew, Greek, and Latin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chief priests object to the wording and Pilate refuses: *what I have written, I have written*. See the note on John 19:12–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verses on the sign and two on the execution."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then the garments"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They divided My garments among them, and for My clothing they cast lots.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 22, quoted by John, with the seamless tunic they would not tear."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What John records about the position.** The middle. Two others, one on each side, and He is in the place reserved for the man the execution was arranged for."}]}, "John 19:28–30": {"title": "\"It is finished\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three words in English, one in Greek, and it was a word people used every day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Tetelestai"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is **teleo** — to complete, to bring to its end. In the perfect tense: finished, and the result stands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It turns up on business documents of the period stamped across a bill: **paid in full**. It was used of a task discharged, a debt settled, an obligation met."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *I am finished*. **It** is finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John says was finished"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Knowing that everything had now been brought to completion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it before the word is spoken, and he uses the same root. The completion is stated as an accomplished fact, and the shout is Jesus announcing it rather than discovering it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hyssop"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They soaked a sponge in it, put it on a stalk of hyssop, and lifted it to His mouth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only John mentions hyssop, and it is a strange plant for the job — a small bushy herb, not an obvious tool for reaching a man on a cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the plant Israel was told to use to brush the lamb's blood on the doorposts at Passover, and the plant used in the cleansing rituals of Leviticus. John is writing his Gospel around a Passover, and he puts hyssop at the mouth of the Lamb of God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Gave up His spirit\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is active. John does not say He died; he says He handed it over — the same claim Jesus made in chapter 10: *no one takes it from Me. I lay it down of My own accord.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He was thirsty for.** John notes it was said *so that the Scripture would come true* — Psalm 69, in a psalm of suffering. The last thing recorded before the shout is a prophecy being deliberately completed. Even the thirst is part of what was being finished."}]}, "John 19:31–34": {"title": "The spear and the water", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John records a piece of Roman procedure and one thing that did not go according to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they were in a hurry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was **the day of preparation**, and the Jewish leaders did not want the bodies left on the crosses through the Sabbath, **since that Sabbath was a high holy day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Passover Sabbath. Deuteronomy required a hanged body to be taken down the same day — *his body shall not remain overnight on the tree… for he who is hanged is accursed of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men who arranged the execution are concerned about ceremonial defilement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The procedure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they asked Pilate to have **the legs of the men broken** and the bodies taken away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **crurifragium** — the legs smashed with an iron mallet. It killed by removing the ability to push up to breathe, and it was the standard way of finishing a crucifixion quickly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The soldiers came and did this to **the first man, and to the other one** crucified with Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both robbers. The soldiers work along the row."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But when they came to Jesus and **saw that He was already dead, they did not break His legs**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They stop because the procedure is unnecessary, not out of any regard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why John records it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses on, he says: *for these things were done that the Scripture should be fulfilled, \"**Not one of His bones shall be broken**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the Passover law from Exodus 12 — of the lamb: *nor shall you break one of its bones*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Psalm 34: *He guards all his bones; not one of them is broken.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The spear"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Instead, **one of the soldiers pierced His side with a spear**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **enuxen** — jabbed, pierced. A professional's check."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What came out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **at once blood and water came out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John gives it as an observation, and in the next verse insists on his own presence: *and he who has seen has testified, and his testimony is true; and he knows that he is telling the truth, so that you may believe.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He stops the narrative to swear to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second Scripture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And again another Scripture says, \"**They shall look on Him whom they pierced**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah 12, and Revelation quotes it too."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rock that was struck"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Strike the rock, and water will pour out for the people to drink*, and God's own clause: *I myself will stand there before you, on the rock.* See the note on Exodus 17:4–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's identification: *that Rock was Christ.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 10:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John writes later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is He who came **by water and blood** — Jesus Christ; not only by water, but by water and blood.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He returns to the pair in his letter, decades on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph of Arimathea, a secret disciple, asks Pilate for the body. Nicodemus comes with a hundred pounds of myrrh and aloes — the man who first came at night."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both of them come out into the open once He is dead."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the soldiers decided.** Not to break the legs, because there was no point — and John says a Passover regulation about a lamb's bones was kept by a Roman working party who had never heard of it."}]}, "John 19:38–42": {"title": "Two secret disciples in daylight", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The men who come forward are the two nobody expected."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph from Arimathea **asked Pilate for permission to take the body**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The other Gospels fill him in: a rich man, *a prominent council member*, *a good and just man* who *had not consented to their decision and deed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A member of the Sanhedrin, asking a Roman governor for the body of a man his own council had just condemned."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark says he *taking courage, went in to Pilate*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Crucified bodies were normally left, or thrown into a common grave. Asking for one identified you with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was a disciple of Jesus, **though quietly, because he was afraid of the Jewish leaders**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kekrummenos** — having been hidden."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John states the fear and then reports the action. Both are in the same sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Nicodemus came too — **the man who had first come to Jesus at night**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John reminds us every time. He has done it three times now: the night visit in chapter 3, the defence in the council in chapter 7, and here. See the note on John 3:4–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reminder is the point. He is introduced by his caution, and then he does this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he brought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A mixture of **myrrh and aloes, about a hundred Roman pounds** of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roughly seventy-five pounds in modern weight. An enormous quantity — royal burial scale."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Herod's funeral procession, according to Josephus, had five hundred servants carrying spices."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nicodemus turns up at a public execution with a king's burial."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wrapping"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The two of them took the body of Jesus and **wrapped it in linen cloths with the spices**, which is how the Jewish people prepare a body for burial.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two members of the council, handling a corpse on the eve of Passover — which made them ceremonially unclean and disqualified them from eating it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the disciples are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not here. Eleven men are somewhere behind locked doors, and the burial is done by two councilmen and, watching, some women."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Mary Magdalene and Mary the mother of Joses observed where He was laid.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The garden"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now there was **a garden in the place where He was crucified**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John is the only one who mentions it, and John is the one whose Gospel started *in the beginning*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A garden is where the first man was put, and where he lost it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days later Mary Magdalene *supposed Him to be the gardener*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tomb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And in the garden **a new tomb, one no one had ever been laid in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew says it was Joseph's own, *which he had hewn out of the rock*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gave away his grave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because it was **the Jewish day of preparation**, and because **that tomb was close by**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sunset was coming and the Sabbath with it. The choice of tomb was made by the clock."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah wrote"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they made His grave with the wicked, but **with the rich at His death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Executed as a criminal, buried in a wealthy man's rock-cut tomb. Both halves of the sentence happen on the same afternoon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the two of them had in common"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both were on the council. Both had kept quiet. Both come forward when the movement looks finished and there is nothing left to gain."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**When they stopped hiding.** Not after the resurrection. On the Friday, before sunset, when the body needed carrying and the eleven were not there."}]}, "John 19:8–11": {"title": "\"Given to you from above\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The governor asks two questions and gets silence for one and a correction for the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he is afraid"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Pilate heard that, he was **more afraid than ever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "What he had just heard: *we have a law, and according to our law **He ought to die, because He made Himself the Son of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**More afraid than ever** — the Greek is *he was the more afraid*, which means he was already."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His wife had sent word that morning: *have nothing to do with that just Man, for I have suffered many things today in a dream because of Him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He went back inside and said to Jesus, \"**Where do You come from?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *who are You*. Where from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roman religion had categories for men descended from gods, and Pilate is asking inside them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **Jesus gave him no answer**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *gave him no answer*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had answered Pilate's earlier question about the kingdom at length. This one He does not take."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah: *He was oppressed and He was afflicted, **yet He opened not His mouth**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will not speak to me?** Do You not know that **I have the authority to release You and the authority to crucify You**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pilate states his position accurately. He did have both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had already tried to use the first half — three times he says *I find no fault in Him* — and had scourged Him hoping it would be enough."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You would have no authority over Me at all if it had not been given to you from above**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exousia** — authority, the right to act."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus does not deny the authority. He locates its source, and the sentence puts Pilate inside an arrangement he did not set up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the general form of it: *there is no authority except from God, and the authorities that exist are appointed by God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That is why **the one who handed Me over to you carries the greater guilt**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meizona hamartian** — greater sin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *no guilt* for Pilate. **Greater** for the other party, which means both are real and one is larger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Who handed Him over: Caiaphas and the council, in John's account — *the chief priests and officers delivered Him to Pilate*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Pilate does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From then on **Pilate sought to release Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tries again, and the crowd produces the sentence that ends it: *if you let this Man go, **you are not Caesar's friend***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Tiberius's reign was not a good time to have that said about you. Pilate sits down on the judgment seat and hands Him over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he wrote"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Pilate wrote a title and put it on the cross. And the writing was: **JESUS OF NAZARETH, THE KING OF THE JEWS**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In three languages, and when the chief priests objected: *what I have written, I have written.* See the note on Luke 23:32–38."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing he would not revise."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is conceded and what is corrected.** Jesus does not tell Pilate he lacks the power. He tells him where it came from — and then says the greater weight is on somebody else in the room."}]}, "John 2:1–5": {"title": "\"They have no more wine\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first sign in John's Gospel happens at a party, and it starts with an embarrassment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What running out meant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Jewish wedding feast could last a week, and providing for the guests was the family's responsibility. Running out of wine was not a catering inconvenience — it was a public humiliation the village would remember for years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody was in danger. Nobody was ill. The need was social, and Mary brings it to Him anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They have no more wine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the entire request. No instruction, no suggestion, no argument about why He should act. She states the situation and stops."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Dear woman, what does this have to do with you and Me? My hour has not come yet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It reads sharply in English. The address is not harsh in Greek — it is the same word He uses to her from the cross. The idiom behind *what does this have to do with you and Me* is a way of saying the matter is not ours to be involved in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *My hour*, in John, always means the cross. He is saying the time for public disclosure has not arrived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His mother turned to the servants and said, \"Whatever He tells you, do it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not argue with Him and she does not withdraw the request. She hands the matter over and instructs the staff to obey whatever comes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are the last recorded words of Mary in Scripture, and they are about doing what He says."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He then makes.** Water in six stone jars kept for ceremonial washing — the vessels of purification rites — turned into the best wine of the feast. The master of the banquet says the good wine has been saved till last, and has no idea where it came from. Only the servants who filled the jars knew."}]}, "John 2:18–22": {"title": "Forty-six years", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "They ask for credentials and He gives them one they cannot use."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are asking about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just cleared the temple — the money changers, the tables, the doves, the whip of cords."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What sign will You show us** to prove You have the right to do all this?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A fair question by their lights. Somebody disrupting temple business ought to be able to say who authorised it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Tear this temple down, and in three days I will raise it up again.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He offers a sign that cannot be produced on demand, and can only be verified after the fact."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is imperative — *destroy it* — which is not an instruction so much as a condition. Do that, and watch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their arithmetic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It has taken **forty-six years** to build this temple, and You are going to raise it up in three days?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Herod began the reconstruction around 20 BC, and work on it continued for decades — it was finally finished in the AD 60s, a few years before the Romans destroyed it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their figure dates this scene to about AD 27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He meant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the temple He was speaking about was His body**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John supplies the explanation, and he is the only one who does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for temple here is **naos** — the sanctuary, the inner house, not the whole complex."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same word of believers: *do you not know that you are the temple of God?* See the notes on 1 Corinthians 3:16–17 and 1 Corinthians 6:18–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When they understood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **when He was raised from the dead**, His disciples remembered that He had said this, **and they believed** the Scripture and the word Jesus had spoken.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not at the time. Years later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John says it about several things in this Gospel — *these things His disciples did not understand at first; but when Jesus was glorified, then they remembered.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were in the room, heard it, and it meant nothing until Sunday morning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who else remembered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chief priests did. At the trial: *we heard Him say, \"I will destroy this temple made with hands, and within three days I will build another made without hands.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at the cross, passers-by shook their heads: *You who destroy the temple and build it in three days, save Yourself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His enemies quoted it back at Him for three years. His friends worked it out afterwards."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And after the burial the chief priests went to Pilate: *we remember, while He was still alive, how that deceiver said, \"**After three days I will rise**.\"* They asked for a guard on the tomb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They took it more literally than the disciples did."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the sign turned out to be.** He was asked for authority to clear a building and pointed at His own body. The one He was standing in came down forty years later, and has not been rebuilt."}]}, "John 2:6–8": {"title": "Six stone jars", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first miracle in John's Gospel, and the only people who know it happened are the staff."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A wedding at Cana, and the wine has run out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when they ran out of wine, the mother of Jesus said to Him, \"**They have no wine**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Woman, what does your concern have to do with Me? **My hour has not yet come**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His mother said to the servants, \"**Whatever He says to you, do it**.\"* See the note on John 2:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The jars"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Standing there were **six stone waterpots**, the kind the Jews kept for **their ceremonial washings**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **lithinai hudriai** — stone water jars."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stone, not clay. Stone vessels did not contract ritual impurity and so were used for purification water; clay ones had to be broken if defiled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The capacity"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Each one holding **twenty or thirty gallons**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **metretas duo e treis** — two or three measures."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six jars at twenty-five gallons is around a hundred and fifty gallons."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John notes it. He does not say why."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus said to the servants, \"**Fill the jars with water**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only instruction given, and it is one anybody could carry out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about wine. Nothing about the shortage. Fill the jars."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The work"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Around a hundred and fifty gallons, drawn and carried by hand. At eight pounds a gallon, that is well over half a ton of water."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The compliance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they filled them, **right up to the brim**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **heos ano** — up to the top."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John records the detail. They did not half-fill them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Now dip some out and carry it to the master of the feast**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **antlesate nun** — draw out now."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are told to serve it before anybody has tasted it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the text says the water had changed at the point they dipped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the steward says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the master of the feast had tasted the water that was made wine, and **did not know where it came from** (but the servants who had drawn the water knew).*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's parenthesis is the point of the scene."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man in charge has no idea. The staff, who carried it, know exactly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Every man at the beginning sets out the good wine**, and when the guests have well drunk, then the inferior. **You have kept the good wine until now!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is complaining about the catering order, and he is right about the quality."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The count"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This **beginning of signs** Jesus did in Cana of Galilee, and manifested His glory; and His disciples believed in Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **archen ton semeion** — beginning of the signs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John numbers this one and the official's son at Capernaum, and stops. See the note on John 4:51–54."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who never finds out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bridegroom. The guests. The steward."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mother knows, the disciples know, and the servants know."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He said about His hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"My hour has not yet come.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word recurs through the Gospel — *no one laid a hand on Him, because His hour had not yet come* — until the last week: *the hour has come that the Son of Man should be glorified*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first sign is performed before the hour, at a woman's request, into six stone jars for washing that end the evening full of something else."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The instruction.** *Fill the jars with water* — the only thing anybody was told to do, and it took over half a ton of carrying before a single thing had changed."}]}, "John 2:9–11": {"title": "\"You have kept the best until now\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first miracle, and almost nobody at the party knows it happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who knew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He had **no idea where it had come from**, though **the servants who had drawn it knew perfectly well**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John records both halves. The master of the feast, the man whose job was to know about the wine, does not know. The servants do."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people who carried the water jars are the only ones in the room who saw anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six stone waterpots, standing there *according to the manner of purification of the Jews* — for washing hands and vessels, not for drinking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Fill the waterpots with water.\" And **they filled them up to the brim**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty or thirty gallons each. Six of them. Somewhere between a hundred and a hundred and eighty gallons."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The compression"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wine takes a vine, a season, a harvest, pressing, and time in a jar. Every stage of it is time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the drawing and the tasting there was none of that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the master says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Everyone puts out the good wine first**, and then brings on the cheaper wine once the guests have had plenty. But **you have kept the best until now**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is complaining, in the way a professional complains, that the order is wrong."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is speaking better than he knows. It is John's habit throughout the Gospel — Caiaphas does the same thing later, and John points it out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John calls it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This was **the first of His signs**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **semeion** — a sign, something pointing beyond itself. John never calls them miracles; he counts seven signs and this is number one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He did it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At Cana in Galilee* — a village nobody outside that district had heard of, at a family wedding, for people who had run out of drink."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first sign is not in the temple, not in Jerusalem, and not in front of anybody who mattered. It solves a social embarrassment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **let His glory be seen**, and **His disciples put their trust in Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things. The glory was shown, and a handful of men believed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is recorded about the crowd, the bridegroom, or the master of the feast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What His mother said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whatever He says to you, do it.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her last recorded words in Scripture, spoken to the servants — the only people in the story who end up seeing anything. See the note on John 2:1–5."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The second sign.** A nobleman's son healed at Capernaum, twenty-odd miles away, at the exact hour Jesus spoke. The first sign compresses time; the second crosses distance. John records them as the first two, in that order. See the note on John 4:46–50."}]}, "John 20:1–2": {"title": "Still dark", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first person at the tomb reaches the wrong conclusion and runs for help."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On **the first day of the week**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **te mia ton sabbaton** — the first of the sabbaths, the idiom for Sunday."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While it was **still dark**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **skotias eti ouses**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark says *very early in the morning… when the sun had risen*. John says dark. She set out before dawn and arrived as it was breaking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John uses darkness as a marker throughout: Nicodemus comes at night, Judas goes out and *it was night*, and the light shines in the darkness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mary Magdalene.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke names her as one *from whom seven demons had come out*, and among the women who supported the work from their own means."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John mentions only her. The other Gospels name several women, and her *we* in verse 2 suggests John knows she was not alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she sees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And she saw that **the stone had been taken away** from the entrance.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ermenon ek tou mnemeiou** — taken away, lifted out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is all she sees. She does not go in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she concludes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**They have taken the Lord out of the tomb, and we do not know where they have put Him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grave robbery. Which is the reasonable inference from an open tomb before sunrise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pronoun"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We do not know.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ouk oidamen** — plural."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John names one woman and lets her plural stand, which is why the other accounts do not contradict him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **she ran**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To Peter, and to *the other disciple, the one Jesus loved* — John's way of naming himself throughout the book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter had denied Him three days earlier. She goes to him first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That He is risen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had heard the predictions along with everyone else. Standing at an open tomb, she reports a theft."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The race"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Peter set out, and the other disciple with him.* See the note on John 20:3–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both men run. The younger one arrives first, looks in, and waits."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does after they leave"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **Mary stood outside by the tomb weeping**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men go home. She stays."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And as she wept she stooped down and looked into the tomb, and she saw two angels in white sitting, one at the head and the other at the feet.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Woman, why are you weeping?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because they have taken away my Lord, and I do not know where they have laid Him.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same sentence, third time. She has said it to the disciples, and now to angels, and she is about to say it again to a man she takes for the gardener."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus said to her, \"**Mary!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She turned and said to Him, \"**Rabboni!**\"* See the note on John 20:11–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not recognise Him by sight. She recognises her own name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she is sent to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go to My brethren and say to them, '**I am ascending to My Father and your Father, and to My God and your God**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first person to see Him is the first person sent, and *My brethren* is what He calls the men who ran."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What she reports.** Not a resurrection — a theft. She had heard the predictions like everyone else, and at an open tomb before dawn she drew the ordinary conclusion."}]}, "John 20:11–13": {"title": "One at the head and one at the feet", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter and John have looked into the tomb and gone home. Mary stays and cries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "She stayed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **Mary stayed outside by the tomb, crying**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two disciples had run there, looked in, seen the linen cloths, and *went away again to their own homes*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has nowhere to go. The Greek for her weeping is the loud kind — wailing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And while she was crying **she bent down and looked into the tomb**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She looks in after the men had already looked in and found it empty. There is no reason to expect anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she sees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Two angels in white, seated where the body of Jesus had been laid, **one at the head and one at the feet**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the detail, and it is a description of a piece of furniture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mercy seat sat on top of the ark, with *two cherubim of gold… one cherub at one end, and the other cherub at the other end*, facing each other across it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Once a year the high priest went in and sprinkled blood on the space between them, and God said: *there I will meet with you, and I will speak with you from above the mercy seat, from between the two cherubim.* See the note on Exodus 25:17–22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two angels, one at each end, and between them the place where a body had lain and blood had been shed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mary is looking into the holiest place, from outside, and it is empty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they ask"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Woman, why are you crying?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Angels are asking a question they can hardly need the answer to, in a tomb that is empty because of good news."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**They have taken away my Lord, and I do not know where they have put Him.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She says almost the same words three times in this chapter — to Peter and John, to the angels, and then to the gardener."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her grief is not that He is dead. It is that she has lost the body and cannot perform the last service she came to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She turns round, sees Him, and takes Him for the gardener — in a garden, on the first day of the week."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says one word. *Mary.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And she is the first person on earth to see Him alive, and the first commissioned to tell anybody: *go to My brethren and say to them…*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the angels are sitting.** *Where the body of Jesus had been laid.* John notes the position because it is the point. The room in the tabernacle that nobody could enter is now a place a weeping woman is leaning into, and the seat is empty."}]}, "John 20:19–20": {"title": "Behind shut doors", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first meeting with the eleven, and He does not knock."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That same day, in the evening of **the first day of the week**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sunday. The tomb was found empty that morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mary Magdalene has already told them — *I have seen the Lord* — and the two from Emmaus have just arrived with the same news. See the note on Luke 24:33–35."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are still behind a locked door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The disciples were together **behind shut doors, because they were afraid of the Jewish leaders**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John states it plainly. Not waiting, not praying — afraid."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men who arrested Him three days ago know all their faces."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arrival"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **Jesus came and stood there in the middle of them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not explain the doors. He mentions them, mentions that they were shut, and then puts Him inside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does it twice — verse 19, and again in verse 26 when Thomas is there: *the doors being shut, Jesus came and stood in the midst*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The greeting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Peace be with you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Shalom aleichem** — the ordinary greeting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to men who had all promised not to leave Him and had all left. Nothing in the sentence refers to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it three times in this chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The evidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When He had said it, **He showed them His hands and His side**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hands and side. Luke's version has hands and feet — Luke was interested in the nail wounds; John was standing at the cross and saw the spear go in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One of the soldiers pierced His side with a spear, and immediately blood and water came out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The risen body carries the marks. They are the identification."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the disciples were **overjoyed to see the Lord**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **echaresan** — they rejoiced."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's account of the same evening has them frightened first, and then *they still could not believe it, for sheer joy*. See the note on Luke 24:36–43."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He had promised about this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You now have sorrow; but **I will see you again and your heart will rejoice**, and your joy no one will take from you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said in the upper room on the Thursday."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As the Father has sent Me, so I am sending you.\"* And then the breath. See the note on John 20:21–23."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was missing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now Thomas, called the Twin, one of the twelve, **was not with them when Jesus came**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight days later He comes again, doors shut again, and says *reach your finger here… do not be unbelieving, but believing*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Thomas: *my Lord and my God!*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John says the chapter is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And truly Jesus did many other signs in the presence of His disciples, which are not written in this book; but **these are written that you may believe** that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and that believing you may have life in His name.* See the note on John 20:30–31."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The state of the room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Frightened men, a locked door, and no expectation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing they did produced the visit."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What John mentions and does not explain.** The doors were shut. He says it here and again eight days later, both times in the same clause as the arrival."}]}, "John 20:21–23": {"title": "He breathed on them", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second *peace* in three verses, and this one has a job attached."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repetition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Jesus said to them **again**, \"Peace be with you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just said it. See the note on John 20:19–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first was greeting. The second is the preface to a commission."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sending"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**As the Father has sent Me, so I am sending you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek uses two different verbs: **apestalken** of the Father sending Him — a perfect, sent and still sent — and **pempo** of Him sending them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As* is **kathos** — in the same manner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pattern of the sending is the incarnation. Into a place, at cost, to be with people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John has been building the theme all through the Gospel: *the Father who sent Me* occurs more than thirty times. And the prayer: *as You sent Me into the world, I also have sent them into the world*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The breath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when He had said this, **He breathed on them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **enephusesen** — He breathed into, or on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It occurs once in the Greek Old Testament at a moment worth noticing: *and the LORD God formed man of the dust of the ground, and **breathed** into his nostrils the breath of life*. See the note on Genesis 2:4–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And once in Ezekiel's valley of dry bones: *come from the four winds, O breath, and breathe on these slain, that they may live*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three uses, and all three are about life arriving in something that did not have it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Receive the Holy Spirit**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **labete pneuma hagion** — an aorist imperative. Take, receive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Pneuma** does double duty: breath, wind, spirit. The same ambiguity as the Hebrew **ruach**, and as the conversation with Nicodemus: *the wind blows where it wishes*. See the note on John 3:4–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What this is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pentecost. That is fifty days away, and Luke has Him telling them to wait for it. See the note on Acts 1:4–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John records something that happened in a locked room on the Sunday evening, and does not harmonise it with Acts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause about sins"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If you **forgive** anyone their sins, those sins **are forgiven** them; if you **hold** them, they are **held**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verbs in the second half of each pair are perfect passives — *have been forgiven*, *have been retained*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tense matters. It does not read as the disciples deciding and heaven following. It reads as the disciples announcing what has been settled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is what they do in Acts. Peter at Pentecost: *repent, and let every one of you be baptised in the name of Jesus Christ for the remission of sins*. And to Cornelius: *whoever believes in Him will receive remission of sins*. See the note on Acts 10:34–43."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They never absolve anybody. They announce terms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parallel promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will give you the keys of the kingdom of heaven, and whatever you bind on earth will be bound in heaven.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to Peter at Caesarea Philippi, with the same perfect-tense construction in the Greek."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had been doing an hour earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sitting behind a locked door because they were afraid."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He arrives, shows them the wounds, says peace twice, commissions them, breathes on them, and gives them authority to announce forgiveness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "None of it is conditional on the last four days going differently."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb used three times in Scripture.** Breathed into. Once over dust in a garden, once over bones in a valley, and once in a locked room on a Sunday evening."}]}, "John 20:3–10": {"title": "The race to the tomb", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two men run to a grave, and what convinces one of them is the laundry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The start"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Peter set out, and the other disciple with him, and **the two of them ran together**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mary had come from the tomb saying *they have taken the Lord out of the tomb*. See the note on John 20:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The race"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the other disciple ran faster than Peter and reached the tomb first**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John records that he won, which is an odd detail to preserve unless it is a memory rather than a point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **bent down and looked in**, and he saw the linen wrappings lying there, **but he did not go inside**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parakupsas** — stooping to peer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He stops at the entrance. Grave-impurity, or hesitation, or both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Peter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Simon Peter came up behind him and **went straight in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eiselthen** — entered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is characteristic. Peter is the one who gets out of the boat and the one who cuts off an ear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is inside"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he saw **the linen wrappings lying there**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **othonia** — the strips of linen the body was bound in, with about seventy-five pounds of myrrh and aloes between the layers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Keimena** — lying. Not thrown, not scattered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cloth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the cloth that had been around His head was not lying with the wrappings but folded up by itself, in a place of its own**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **soudarion** — the face cloth. And **entetuligmenon eis hena topon** — wrapped together into one place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that rules out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Robbery. A thief takes the body wrapped, or leaves the wrappings in a heap."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is described is order. The wrappings where the body was, the head cloth separately, folded, in its own place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it might describe"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wrappings still lying in the shape they had held, collapsed, and the face cloth by itself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not say. He records the arrangement and lets it stand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Lazarus"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **he who had died came out bound hand and foot with graveclothes**, and his face was wrapped with a cloth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus said to them, \"**Loose him, and let him go**.\"* See the note on John 11:41–44."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lazarus needed unwrapping. Somebody had to do it for him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in this tomb needed unwrapping."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The belief"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the other disciple… went inside too, and **he saw, and he believed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eiden kai episteusen**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He believes on the strength of folded cloth. Not an appearance, not an angel, not a voice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did not yet know"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **until that morning they had not yet understood from the Scriptures that He had to rise from the dead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dei** — it was necessary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had heard Him say it three times. It had not connected to anything they had read."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the disciples went back to where they were staying**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They go home."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most extraordinary thing in the history of the world has just been established in an empty tomb, and the two witnesses walk back to the house."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who stays"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **Mary stood outside by the tomb weeping**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men leave. She stays, and she is the one He speaks to. See the note on John 20:11–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three verbs for seeing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John uses three different Greek words in this paragraph: **blepei** (glances at), **theorei** (observes carefully), and **eiden** (perceived)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last one is the one attached to believing."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What convinced him.** Not an appearance. The wrappings lying where the body had been, and the face cloth folded by itself in its own place."}]}, "John 21:1–3": {"title": "A night with nothing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "After the resurrection, seven men go back to work, and it is the same lake and the same result as the first time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By the **Sea of Tiberias*** — the Sea of Galilee, in the territory of Zebulun and Naphtali."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zebulun's blessing was *a harbour for ships*, and Zebulun's name means dwelling. He had lived there, taught there, and done most of His work on that shoreline. See the note on Genesis 49:13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Simon Peter, Thomas, Nathanael, the sons of Zebedee, and two others.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven of them. Peter first, and Thomas next — the one who had said he would not believe unless he put his hand in the side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I am going fishing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words in the Greek, and John gives no explanation for them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the trade Peter left when he pulled two boats onto the shore, full of fish, and walked away from the catch of his life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They said to him, \"We will come with you.\"* Nobody argues."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"All that night they caught nothing at all\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same lake, the same trade, the same night hours, the same empty nets."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is exactly what Luke 5 recorded before any of them followed Him: *we worked hard all night long and came up with nothing at all.* See the note on Luke 5:4–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three years of walking with Him, and the fishing goes the same way it always did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is waiting on the beach"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the morning had now come, Jesus stood on the shore; yet the disciples did not know that it was Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks whether they have any food, tells them which side to cast on, and the net comes up with a hundred and fifty-three fish and does not break."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when they had come to land, they saw a **fire of coals** there, and **fish laid on it, and bread**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had breakfast cooking before they got there. He did not need their catch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charcoal fire"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word for it, **anthrakia**, occurs twice in the whole New Testament, both in John."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The other one is in the high priest's courtyard, where *the servants and officers had made a fire of coals… and Peter stood with them and warmed himself*, and denied Him three times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's restoration happens over the same kind of fire, and he is asked three questions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He handled that morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The work — a net so full they could not haul it in. The body — bread and fish already on the coals. And the thing Peter could not say out loud."}, {"k": "p", "t": "All three, and He did not raise the third until the man had eaten."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He does not mention.** The denial. Not once. He asks *do you love Me?* three times and never says why He is asking three times."}]}, "John 21:15–17": {"title": "\"Do you love Me?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three questions on a beach, and Peter had denied Him three times beside another fire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When they had **finished breakfast**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bread and fish, cooked on *a fire of coals* — the Greek is **anthrakia**, a charcoal fire."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word occurs twice in the New Testament. The other is the courtyard where Peter warmed himself and said *I am not*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John chose the word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Simon, son of Jonas**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not Peter. Three times, He uses the name the man had before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do you love Me more than these do?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is in it. Peter had said, hours before the arrest, *even if all are made to stumble because of You, I will never be made to stumble*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is asked about that claim once, and never again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two Greek verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus asks with **agapao** the first two times and **phileo** the third."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter answers with **phileo** all three times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Readers have divided over whether the change is significant since the early centuries. John uses the two words interchangeably elsewhere in the Gospel, and the whole conversation would have been in Aramaic."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is not in doubt is that Peter does not raise his answer to meet the question, and that the third time Jesus comes down to Peter's word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter appeals to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Yes, Lord, **You know** that I love You.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times he appeals to what Jesus knows rather than to what he can promise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not make a claim about his own reliability. He had done that once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third time"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **it grieved Peter that He asked him a third time**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **elupethe** — was pained, distressed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in the account needs the number explained."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, **You know everything. You know that I love You**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three commissions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Feed My lambs**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take care of My sheep**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Feed My sheep**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two different verbs — **bosko**, to feed, and **poimaino**, to shepherd — and two different nouns."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each answer is met with a job, and the job is not about Peter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose sheep"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**My** lambs. **My** sheep. Three times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter picks the word up in his own letter: *shepherd the flock of God which is among you… **and when the Chief Shepherd appears**, you will receive the crown of glory.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Most assuredly, I say to you, when you were younger, you girded yourself and walked where you wished; but **when you are old, you will stretch out your hands**, and another will gird you and carry you where you do not wish.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John adds: *this He spoke, signifying by what death he would glorify God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *\"**Follow Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two words He had said on the shore three years earlier. See the note on Mark 1:16–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter does immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Turns round, sees John, and asks *but Lord, what about this man?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If I will that he remain till I come, what is that to you? **You follow Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Back at the lake, fishing. *Simon Peter said to them, \"I am going fishing.\"* See the note on John 21:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had caught nothing all night."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not asked.** Whether he is sorry, and whether he will do better. Three questions about one thing, and Peter answers all three by pointing at what Jesus already knows."}]}, "John 21:20–23": {"title": "\"What is that to you?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter has just been restored — three questions, three answers, and a commission — and then he immediately looks over his shoulder."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question he asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lord, and what about this man?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus has just told Peter how he will die. Peter's response is to point at somebody else and ask what their arrangement is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is such an ordinary reaction that John, who was the man being pointed at, has no interest in softening it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If I want him to remain until I come, what is that to you? You follow Me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last two words are emphatic in Greek — *you*, as opposed to him. Jesus does not tell Peter what will happen to John, and He does not rebuke the curiosity at length. He redirects it in four words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction Peter is given is the same one he was given at the beginning, on the same shore, three years earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction John adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the word went around that this disciple would not die. But Jesus had not said that.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John is writing decades later, presumably as the last of them alive, and he stops to correct a rumour that had grown up around a conditional sentence. *If I want* had become *he will*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a small, careful act of honesty, and it tells you something about the writer: given the chance to leave a flattering story standing, he prints the exact words instead."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it leaves the reader.** Each of them gets a different road — one martyred young, one apparently growing old — and neither is told the other's. The only instruction that fits both is the one Jesus gives: *you follow Me.*"}]}, "John 21:4–8": {"title": "He went into the water", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The same lake, the same empty nets, the same man — and this time he swims toward Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven of them have gone back to fishing. Peter says *I am going fishing* and the rest say *we are going with you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That night they caught nothing.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stranger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the morning was breaking, **Jesus was standing on the shore**, but the disciples **did not know that it was Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third time John reports a resurrection appearance not recognised at first — Mary thought He was the gardener, and Luke's two on the road walked seven miles with Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Children, do you have anything to eat?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paidia** — little children. And the question is phrased expecting the answer no."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They answered Him, \"**No**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Throw the net out on **the right side of the boat**, and you will find some.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No explanation. A stranger on a beach at dawn telling professional fishermen which side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they threw it, and now they **could not haul it back in**, there were so many fish in it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same event as the morning He called them. See the note on Luke 5:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One difference: this net does not break. John says so at the end — *and although there were so many, the net was not broken.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who worked it out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the disciple Jesus loved** said to Peter, \"**It is the Lord**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John sees it. Peter moves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the pattern in the whole chapter, and at the tomb too: John got there first, Peter went in first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when Simon Peter heard that it was the Lord, he **pulled his fisherman's coat around him**, since he had **stripped for the work**, and he **threw himself into the water**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts clothes *on* to swim, which is the wrong order for swimming and the right order for arriving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *outer garment* is **ependuten**, and for what he did with himself, **ebalen heauton** — he threw himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*About **two hundred cubits** out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundred yards. Close enough to have waited two minutes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The others"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The other disciples came on **in the little boat, dragging the net full of fish** behind them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody had to bring the catch in. John notes the number when they land it: a hundred and fifty-three, counted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is on the beach"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As soon as they had come to land, they saw a fire of coals there, and fish laid on it, and bread.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *fire of coals* is **anthrakian** — and John uses the word exactly twice. The other is the fire in the high priest's courtyard, where Peter warmed himself and said *I am not*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Breakfast is cooking on the same kind of fire, and the conversation after it is *do you love Me*, three times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The change"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three years earlier, in a boat sinking under fish, Peter said *depart from me, I am a sinful man.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Since then he has denied Him three times, which is worse than anything he was aware of on the first morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And this time he is in the water before the boat has turned."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The difference between the two catches.** Not the fish, and not the net. The first time he asked Him to leave. The second time he had far more reason to, and he swam."}]}, "John 3:1–3": {"title": "We know", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man arrives with a compliment and gets an answer to a question he did not ask."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man among the Pharisees named **Nicodemus**, one of **the ruling council of the Jews**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **archon** — a ruler. He is a member of the Sanhedrin, the seventy-one who governed Jewish religious life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus calls him *the teacher of Israel* a few verses later, with the definite article — which may mean he held a recognised position."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name is Greek: *conqueror of the people*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He came to Jesus **at night**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John records it, and mentions it every time Nicodemus appears — *the man who had first come to Jesus at night*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whether the reason was fear, or the desire for an unhurried conversation, John does not say."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Rabbi, we know** You are a teacher who has come from God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **oidamen** — we know."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Plural. He is speaking for a group, and the sentence is a considered verdict rather than a personal opinion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The evidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because **no one could do the signs You are doing unless God were with him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sound reasoning, and it is the conclusion of a careful man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is also as far as it goes. *A teacher come from God* is a large compliment and a small category."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I tell you the truth: **unless a person is born all over again, he cannot see the kingdom of God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which answers nothing Nicodemus said."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He came with an assessment; he gets a statement about eyesight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Anothen** — and it carries two meanings at once: *again*, and *from above*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nicodemus takes the first: *how can a man be born when he is old?* See the note on John 3:4–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John chose a word that carries both, and the conversation runs on the gap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being said about knowing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nicodemus opens with *we know*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reply is about a faculty rather than information: *he cannot **see** the kingdom of God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that he needs more data. That he needs different eyes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast John sets up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next chapter is a Samaritan woman at a well at noon. See the note on John 4:7–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A ruler and an outsider. Night and midday. A man who opens with *we know* and a woman who says *I have no husband*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is told he must be born again. She is told *I am He, the very one speaking to you now*. See the note on John 4:25–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two conversations are placed back to back, and the ordering is John's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the conversation ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For God so loved the world that He gave His only begotten Son.\"* See the note on John 3:14–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted sentence in the Bible is the end of a private conversation held after dark with a member of the council."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Nicodemus does later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Objects in the Sanhedrin: *does our law judge a man before it hears him?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, on the Friday, arrives at a public execution with a hundred pounds of myrrh and aloes. See the note on John 19:38–42."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he came with.** A verdict, arrived at properly, on the evidence: *a teacher come from God*. The answer he gets is not about the verdict but about whether he can see."}]}, "John 3:14–18": {"title": "The snake on the pole, and why it is attached to the famous verse", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John 3:16 almost never gets quoted with the sentence before it, and that sentence is the reason it says what it says."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison Jesus chose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as Moses lifted up the snake in the wilderness, so the Son of Man must be lifted up.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The story is in Numbers 21. The people are dying of snakebite, and God's remedy is not to remove the snakes. It is a bronze snake on a pole — an image of the very thing killing them, raised up where everyone can see it. Anyone who looked at it lived."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They did not have to understand it, deserve it, or do anything but look."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the picture Jesus reaches for to describe His own death, and the verse everybody knows follows immediately from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Lifted up\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek **hypsoo** means to raise up, and also to exalt. John uses it three times in this Gospel of the crucifixion, and each time both senses are live at once: lifted onto a cross, and lifted to glory. The word will not let you keep the two apart."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the famous verse actually claims"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For God loved the world so much that He gave His only Son.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The love comes first and the giving follows from it. That order matters, because the sentence is often heard the other way round — as though the cross persuaded a reluctant God to love. John says the love was the reason for the gift."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the scope is *the world* — the same word John uses elsewhere for the world that does not know Him. Not the deserving part of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verses nobody quotes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God did not send His Son into the world to condemn the world, but so that the world might be rescued through Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is verse 17, and it exists because verse 16 raises the question. Jesus states the purpose negatively and positively in the same breath: not to condemn; to rescue."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then verse 18 draws the line: whoever believes is not condemned; whoever does not *stands condemned already*. Not *will be* — already. The condemnation is the situation being rescued from, not a punishment for refusing the rescue."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the snake, once more.** Israel kept that bronze snake for centuries and eventually started burning incense to it, until Hezekiah broke it up. Even the right symbol becomes a problem when people look at the object instead of what it points to."}]}, "John 3:25–30": {"title": "\"He must increase\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John's disciples bring him a complaint, and his answer is the last thing recorded of him in this Gospel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The complaint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Rabbi, the man who was with you on the other side of the Jordan, **the one you testified about** — He is baptizing now, and **everyone is going to Him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do not use the name. *The man who was with you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are indignant on their teacher's behalf, and the phrase they use — *the one you testified about* — makes the grievance worse in their telling: he vouched for the man taking his crowd."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The premise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No one can receive a single thing unless it is given to him from heaven**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He answers the crowd question with a statement about supply."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It applies both ways. Whatever Jesus is receiving was given; whatever John had was given too."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reminder"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You yourselves can testify that I said, '**I am not the Messiah; I was sent on ahead of Him**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said it plainly to the delegation from Jerusalem: *I am not the Christ*, and *I am not* to Elijah, and *no* to the Prophet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am the voice of one crying in the wilderness.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The picture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The one who has the bride is the bridegroom**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bride belongs to the groom. Nobody in a wedding is confused about this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The friend"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The bridegroom's friend stands nearby, listening for him**, and he is **flooded with joy the moment he hears the bridegroom's voice**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **shoshbin** — the groom's friend in Jewish custom, who arranged the wedding, managed the details, and had one job on the night: to stand at the door and wait."}, {"k": "p", "t": "When he heard the groom's voice he handed everything over and his work was finished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His joy is stated as coming from a sound."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**That is my joy, and it is complete**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pepleromai** — filled full, perfect tense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say he is coping with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He must become greater and greater; I must become less and less**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **auxanein** and **elattousthai** — to increase and to be diminished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Must** — the Greek is **dei**, it is necessary. He states it as the way things are arranged, not as something he is bracing for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was arrested by Herod for saying the marriage to Herodias was unlawful, and beheaded on a birthday."}, {"k": "p", "t": "From prison he sent two disciples to ask *are You the Coming One, or do we look for another?* — which is the question of a man in the dark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus answered with a list of what was happening, and then turned to the crowd and said *among those born of women there has not risen one greater than John the Baptist*. See the note on Matthew 11:12–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus said about his joy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He was the burning and shining lamp, and **you were willing for a time to rejoice in his light**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last words in the paragraph"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses that follow — *the One who comes from above is above everything* — read as either John continuing or the Evangelist taking over. The Greek gives no quotation marks. See the note on John 3:31–36."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the friend is for.** To stand at the door and listen. John's answer to losing his crowd is that his joy was never in having one — it was in hearing a voice, and he says it is now full."}]}, "John 3:31–36": {"title": "\"Not in measured amounts\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last paragraph of John 3, and it is hard to tell where the Baptist stops speaking and the writer starts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's disciples had come to him complaining that everyone was going to Jesus instead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His answer: *a man can receive nothing unless it has been given to him from heaven… **He must increase, but I must decrease**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then this, and no quotation marks in the Greek to say who is talking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The origin"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The One who **comes from above is above everything and everyone**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice in three lines. John's Gospel builds on the contrast: *you are from beneath; I am from above*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two speakers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whoever **belongs to the earth is of the earth, and speaks as one who belongs to the earth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an insult. A statement about range. A man can only report from where he has been."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The testimony"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He **testifies to what He has seen and heard**, and **no one accepts His testimony**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is the legal word — **marturia**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**No one** — and then, immediately, the exception."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What accepting it means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whoever does accept it has **put his own name to this: that God tells the truth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *has set his seal that God is true*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A seal was a signature — pressed into wax to certify a document."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Believing is described as countersigning a statement about God's character."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John says the reverse in his letter: *he who does not believe God has made Him a liar*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Since **God does not hand Him the Spirit in measured amounts**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *not by measure* — **ou ek metrou**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rabbis spoke of the Spirit being given to the prophets in measures. This clause removes the measuring cup."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Father has done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The Father loves the Son and has put everything into His hands**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both clauses in one line, and John repeats the pair through the Gospel: *the Father loves the Son and shows Him all things*, and at the supper *knowing that the Father had given all things into His hands*. See the note on John 13:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The love and the handing-over are always together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two outcomes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whoever **believes in the Son has life that never ends**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present tense. Has."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whoever **refuses to believe the Son will not see life**; **the anger of God remains on him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *refuses to believe* is **apeithon** — disobeying, refusing to be persuaded. It is a word about the will rather than the intellect."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Remains** — **menei**, the word John uses everywhere for abiding. Not *will come upon*. It is already there and stays."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this sits in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty verses after *God so loved the world that He gave His only begotten Son*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same chapter contains both sentences, and John does not soften either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who said it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Baptist's last recorded words in this Gospel. The next time John mentions him he is in prison, and after that he is dead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was the burning and shining lamp, and you were willing for a time to rejoice in his light.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clause about the Spirit.** *Not by measure.* Everything else in the paragraph is about where a speaker has come from and what he is therefore able to report — and this is why the reporting is unlimited."}]}, "John 3:4–8": {"title": "The wind blows where it pleases", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A member of the Sanhedrin comes at night and takes a metaphor literally."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How can a man be born when he is old? He cannot climb back into his mother's womb a second time**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nicodemus is a teacher of Israel, and his response to *you must be born again* is obstetric."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the running feature of John's Gospel — the woman at the well asks for water so she will not have to keep coming back, the crowd asks for the bread always, the disciples wonder whether somebody brought Him lunch. See the note on John 4:31–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every time, a literal reading gives Him the opening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase behind it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anothen** — which means both *again* and *from above*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nicodemus hears *again* and asks about a second birth. Jesus meant *from above*, and John chose a word that carries both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Unless a person is **born of water and of the Spirit**, he cannot enter the kingdom of God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Water and Spirit. Ezekiel had put them together in a promise: *I will sprinkle clean water on you, and you shall be clean… **I will put My Spirit within you***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nicodemus is being told he should have known this. Two verses later: *are you the teacher of Israel, and do not know these things?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What is born of human parents is human; what is born of the Spirit is spirit**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *that which is born of the flesh is flesh, and that which is born of the Spirit is spirit*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each thing produces after its own kind. The clause built into the third day of creation. See the note on Genesis 1:9–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Flesh cannot produce spirit, which is why a second physical birth would not help."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reassurance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not be so astonished** that I said to you, 'You must be born all over again.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me thaumases** — do not marvel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The wind blows wherever it pleases**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pneuma** — the same word as *Spirit*, and it does duty for both. The Hebrew **ruach** does the same."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence is untranslatable in one direction: *the Spirit breathes where it wills*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What can be observed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You hear the sound of it, but you cannot tell where it came from or where it is going**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things known and two unknown. The effect is audible and the mechanism is not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody has ever seen wind. They see what it moves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The application"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**That is how it is with everyone who is born of the Spirit**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is to the person, not to the Spirit. A born-again person is like the wind: what happened is undeniable and the process is not on display."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If I have told you earthly things and you do not believe, how will you believe if I tell you heavenly things?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the serpent on the pole, and *for God so loved the world that He gave His only begotten Son*. See the note on John 3:14–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted verse in the Bible is the end of a private conversation with a Pharisee at night."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Nicodemus turns up again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Defending Him in the council: *does our law judge a man before it hears him?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at the end, with a hundred pounds of myrrh and aloes, in daylight, at a burial. See the note on John 19:38–42."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John introduces him each time as *the man who came to Jesus by night*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word that carries both.** *Anothen* — again, and from above. Nicodemus took the first meaning, and the answer he gets is about the second."}]}, "John 4:1–6": {"title": "Through Samaria", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Six verses of travel arrangements, and every detail in them is load-bearing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"His road took Him through Samaria\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *He had to pass through Samaria* — it was necessary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Geographically it was the direct route, and Jews going north routinely crossed the Jordan to avoid it rather than set foot in that country. The necessity in John's sentence is not about mileage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wall"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jews have no dealings with Samaritans*, John says a few verses later, and his disciples *marvelled that He talked with a woman.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two walls in one conversation, and He goes through both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul names it in Ephesians: *He has broken down the middle wall of partition.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose ground it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Near **the piece of ground Jacob had given to his son Joseph**, and **Jacob's well** was there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John names the owner. This is Joseph's portion, and Jacob's blessing over Joseph was:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph is a fruitful vine, **a fruitful vine beside a spring**, whose branches **climb over a wall**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A spring, and branches over a wall. He is sitting at the well on the land, and the wall He crosses is the one He is about to cross with a Samaritan woman. See the note on Genesis 49:22–26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Worn out\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus was **worn out from the journey**, and He sat down **just as He was** beside the well.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *worn out* is to labour to the point of exhaustion. John, who opens his Gospel with *the Word was God*, writes this six verses into chapter 4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as He was* — no ceremony, nothing arranged. He sat down the way a tired man sits down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sixth hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Noon. The hottest part of the day, and the wrong time to draw water — the women came at evening, together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She comes alone at midday because of what her life is, and He is there, tired, at exactly that hour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He asks her for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Give Me a drink.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing He says to her is a request. He has no bucket and the well is deep, and He opens the conversation from a position of need."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the end of the chapter she has left her water jar, gone into the town, and brought it out to meet Him. See the note on John 4:10–15."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He tells the disciples when they come back.** They had gone to buy food and He would not eat. *My food is to do the will of Him who sent Me.* Then: *lift up your eyes and look at the fields, for they are already white for harvest.* He is looking at a Samaritan town walking toward Him."}]}, "John 4:10–15": {"title": "\"If you knew the gift\" — a conversation He starts by asking for help", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus opens by asking a Samaritan woman for a drink, which is the least likely sentence in the chapter. He is thirsty and has nothing to draw with. The whole exchange runs from a position of need."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"If you knew the gift God is holding out\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things she does not know: *what* is being offered and *who* is doing the offering. He names both as missing, then says what she would do if she had them — *you would have asked Him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not qualified, not prepared. Asked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Living water"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In ordinary speech *living water* meant running water — a spring or a stream, as opposed to still water in a cistern. Her reply takes it that way, which is why she points out He has no bucket and the well is deep."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is not being obtuse. She is answering the plain sense of the words, and John lets the misunderstanding stand for four verses because the second meaning arrives better after the first has been exhausted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He promises"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone who drinks this water will be thirsty again.* True of the well, and true of everything else that has to be gone back to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The water I give will become a spring inside him.* The location changes. Not a better well to keep returning to — a source relocated inside the person, welling up."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Her answer.** *Give me this water, so that I will not be thirsty and will not have to keep coming all this way to draw.* She is still thinking about the walk, and He does not correct her or make her understand first. The next thing He says is about her husband, and the conversation goes somewhere else entirely. He answers people mid-misunderstanding."}]}, "John 4:16–19": {"title": "You have said it well", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four verses, and He agrees with her twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the conversation is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has asked for the water that means she will not have to keep coming back. See the note on John 4:7–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Sir, give me this water, that I may not thirst, nor come here to draw.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go, call your husband, and come back here**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It arrives without warning, and it changes the subject completely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have no husband**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in Greek — **ouk echo andra** — and it is true as far as it goes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The shortest answer she gives in the whole conversation. She has been talkative up to this point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first agreement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You are right to say you have no husband**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kalos eipas** — you have spoken well, beautifully, rightly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not correct her. He affirms the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The record"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You have had **five husbands**, and **the man you have now is not your husband**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five. In that society a woman did not divorce; she was divorced, five times, or widowed, or some combination."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage does not say which, and it does not assign blame. It states the count."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second agreement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What you have said is true**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice, either side of the disclosure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her four words were accurate, and He wraps the facts inside two affirmations of her honesty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rebuke her. Ask her to explain. Tell her to fix it before He continues."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The conversation goes straight on to worship, and then to His own identity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Sir, **I can see that You are a prophet**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **theoro** — I perceive, I observe."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nicodemus opened with *we know You are a teacher come from God*. See the note on John 3:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She reaches a similar verdict, and John puts the two conversations in consecutive chapters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Raises the mountain question — *our fathers worshipped on this mountain* — which reads as a diversion and produces the fullest teaching on worship in the New Testament. See the note on John 4:20–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *I know that Messiah is coming*, and the answer, *I am He*. See the note on John 4:25–26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she tells the town"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Come, see a Man who **told me all things that I ever did**. Could this be the Christ?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She takes the most exposing part of the conversation and makes it the headline."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the five husbands had cost her socially, she uses it as the reason to come and look."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the town says afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now we believe, **not because of what you said**, for we ourselves have heard Him.\"* See the note on John 4:39–42."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the exposure sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between a request for water and a statement about worship."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He raises it, agrees with her about it twice, and moves on."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He affirms.** Her sentence. *You have said it well*, and then *what you have said is true* — with five husbands named in between."}]}, "John 4:20–24": {"title": "The worshippers the Father is looking for", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "She changes the subject to theology and He follows her there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The move"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Our fathers worshiped **on this mountain**, and you people say that **Jerusalem** is the place.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just told her about her five husbands. This is the next thing she says."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It reads as a diversion, and it is the oldest live argument between their two peoples."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gerizim, visible from Jacob's well where they are standing. The Samaritans had built a temple on it, and a Jewish king had destroyed it about a hundred and fifty years earlier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had scriptural grounds: Deuteronomy names Gerizim as the mountain of blessing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer to the question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Believe Me, woman, the hour is coming when you will worship the Father neither on this mountain nor in Jerusalem**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither. He does not settle the argument; he retires it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You worship what you do not know; we worship what we do know, for salvation comes from the Jews**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not pretend the two positions are equivalent. The Samaritans held only the five books of Moses and had cut themselves off from the prophets."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Salvation is of the Jews* — said by a Jew to a Samaritan, and it is a statement about where the Messiah comes from, not about who gets in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **the hour is coming, and it is here right now**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's double formula, and he uses it twice in this Gospel. The other is about the dead hearing His voice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Coming, and already here. Something is being inaugurated in the conversation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The worshippers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**True worshipers will worship the Father in spirit and in truth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **en pneumati kai aletheia** — one preposition governing both nouns, which ties them together as one thing rather than two."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not sincerity plus correctness. Something single."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The astonishing clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Those are the worshipers the Father is looking for**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **zetei** — seeks, searches for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Father is described as looking for worshippers. Nowhere else in Scripture is it put that way round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ground"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**God is Spirit**, and the ones who worship Him **must worship in spirit and in truth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three statements in the Bible take this form: God is spirit, God is light, God is love. The other two are John's as well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **must** follows from the nature. Worship has to match what He is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the location question dissolves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If God is spirit, a mountain is not the issue. Solomon knew it at the dedication of the temple: *behold, heaven and the heaven of heavens cannot contain You. How much less this temple which I have built!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Stephen, on trial for allegedly speaking against the temple, quotes Isaiah at them: *the Most High does not dwell in temples made with hands*. See the note on Acts 6:8–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Samaritan woman at a well at noon, with five husbands behind her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fullest statement about the nature of worship in the New Testament is made to her, in a one-to-one conversation, in the wrong country."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I know that Messiah is coming.\"* And the answer: *I am He, the very one speaking to you now*. See the note on John 4:25–26."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clause that reverses the direction.** *Those are the worshippers the Father is looking for.* The seeking, in that sentence, is His."}]}, "John 4:25–26": {"title": "I am He", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first person He tells is a Samaritan woman at a well at noon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the conversation has got to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has asked for the water that means she will not have to come back and draw. He has asked about her husband. She has said *I have no husband*, and been told *you have had five, and the one you have now is not your husband*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then she changed the subject to worship — *our fathers worshipped on this mountain, and you Jews say that in Jerusalem is the place*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He answered: *the hour is coming, and now is, when the true worshippers will worship the Father in spirit and truth*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I know that **Messiah is coming** — the One who is called the Christ.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word and the Greek word, side by side, both meaning *anointed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Samaritans expected a figure they called the **Taheb**, the restorer — based on Deuteronomy's *a Prophet like me from your midst*, since they held only the five books of Moses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she expects Him to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When He comes, **He will make everything clear to us**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anangelei hemin panta** — He will announce all things to us."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her expectation is a teacher who settles the arguments. She has just been in one about which mountain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am He, the very one speaking to you now**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ego eimi, ho lalon soi** — I am, the one speaking to you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words, and the pronoun is not grammatically necessary. John has been building **ego eimi** through the whole Gospel, and here it is at a well in Samaria."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who gets told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the crowd, not the Sanhedrin, not Nicodemus in the previous chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A woman, in Samaria, with five husbands behind her and a man she is not married to at home, at the hottest hour of the day — which is when a woman went to the well if she was avoiding the other women."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells her outright, and He does not tell anyone else this plainly until the trial."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens the moment after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And at this point His disciples came, and they marvelled that He talked with a woman.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They say nothing. John notes both: *yet no one said, \"What do You seek?\" or \"Why are You talking with her?\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The woman then **left her waterpot**, went her way into the city, and said to the men, \"Come, see a Man who told me all things that I ever did. Could this be the Christ?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She leaves the thing she came for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her sentence to the town is a question, not a claim."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And many of the Samaritans of that city believed in Him **because of the word of the woman**.* See the note on John 4:39–42."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then two days of Him staying, and: *now we believe, not because of what you said, for we ourselves have heard Him*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He said in between"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To the disciples, with the townspeople walking out across the field toward Him: *My food is to do the will of Him who sent Me*. See the note on John 4:31–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The meal He is talking about is this conversation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He says it again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the garden, to the men with torches: *\"Who are you looking for?\" \"Jesus of Nazareth.\" \"**I am He**.\"* See the note on John 18:4–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they fall over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the well it is said to one woman and she goes and tells a town."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who He said it to.** Not a council and not a crowd. A woman at a well in the middle of the day, in the wrong country, who had come at that hour to avoid meeting anybody."}]}, "John 4:31–34": {"title": "\"Food you know nothing about\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The disciples come back with lunch and find He is not hungry any more."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Jacob's well, at the sixth hour, in Samaria. The disciples had *gone away into the city to buy food* — which is why the woman found Him alone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The conversation with her has just ended and she has run back to the town leaving her waterpot behind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The urging"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the meantime the disciples were **urging Him**, \"Rabbi, **eat something**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been *wearied from His journey* and sat down at the well because of it. John says so plainly in verse 6. The tiredness was real and so was the hunger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have food to eat that you know nothing about**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *food you do not know of*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The misunderstanding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the disciples said to one another, \"**Has somebody brought Him something to eat?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A running feature of John's Gospel. Nicodemus asks whether a man can enter his mother's womb; the woman at this same well asks for water so she will not have to come back and draw."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every time, a physical reading of a sentence meant otherwise, and every time it gives Jesus the opening to explain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the food is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My food is to do the will of the One who sent Me, and to finish the work He gave Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses: **do** the will, and **finish** the work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *finish* is **teleioo**, from the same root as the word He says from the cross — *it is finished*, **tetelestai**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names His food, and it is a completed assignment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What had just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Samaritan woman with five husbands had been told everything she ever did and had gone into the town saying *come, see a Man who told me all things that I ever did. Could this be the Christ?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the meal He is talking about. It had happened while the disciples were queueing in a shop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Lift up your eyes and look at the fields, for they are already white for harvest**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said with the townspeople walking out toward the well across the open ground — John notes in verse 30 that *they went out of the city and came to Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The harvest He points at is arriving on the road while He speaks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other statements of the same thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have come down from heaven, **not to do My own will, but the will of Him who sent Me**.\"* See the note on John 6:35–40."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I do exactly as the Father has commanded Me.\"* See the note on John 14:28–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it in nearly every discourse in the Gospel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The outcome in Samaria"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And many of the Samaritans of that city believed in Him because of the word of the woman… **and He stayed there two days**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *now we believe, not because of what you said, for we ourselves have heard Him.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He did not do.** He does not refuse the food or make a point about fasting. He tells them there is a kind of eating they have not noticed, and then points at a field with people walking across it."}]}, "John 4:39–42": {"title": "Two days in Sychar", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A Samaritan town asks Him to stay, and He does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What started it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Many of the Samaritans from that town **believed in Him because of what the woman said**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dia ton logon tes gunaikos** — because of the word of the woman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A woman whose personal history was public knowledge in that town, whose testimony would not have been accepted in a Jewish court, and whose statement to them was a question."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He told me everything I ever did**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole content of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a doctrine. She reports what happened to her, and adds *could this be the Christ?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So when the Samaritans **came out to Him**, they **kept asking Him to stay with them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is an imperfect — they went on asking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samaritans, asking a Jewish rabbi to be their houseguest. The verse at the start of the chapter explains why that is remarkable: *for Jews have no dealings with Samaritans*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been enemies for four centuries — the Samaritans built a rival temple on Gerizim and the Jews burned it down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stay"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He stayed there two days**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two days in a Samaritan town, eating their food and sleeping in their houses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke has a village of Samaritans refusing to receive Him later, and James and John offering to call down fire. He rebuked them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second wave"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Many more believed **because of His own words**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dia ton logon autou** — because of His word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John uses the identical construction for both groups. Because of her word, and because of His."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they say to her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It is no longer because of what you told us that we believe. We have heard Him for ourselves**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It reads harshly and is not meant to. It is what any evangelist wants: the people you told have stopped needing you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And we know that this really is **the Saviour of the world**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ho soter tou kosmou**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase appears twice in the New Testament, and this is the first. It is said by Samaritans, about a Jewish teacher, in a town outside Israel proper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was also a title used of Caesar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The harvest He had pointed at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lift up your eyes and look at the fields, for they are already white for harvest.\"* See the note on John 4:31–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He said that with these people walking out of the town toward Him across open ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He had said about sowing and reaping"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Here the saying holds true: one sows and another reaps. I sent you to reap that for which you have not laboured; others have laboured, and you have entered into their labours.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Acts does with the town"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Philip goes down to Samaria and preaches, *and there was great joy in that city*. Peter and John come down and lay hands on them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The commission had named it: *you shall be witnesses to Me in Jerusalem, and in all Judea and Samaria, and to the end of the earth*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samaria is third on the list, and it starts with a woman at a well."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole episode rests on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One conversation, at noon, with one person, in the wrong country. See the note on John 4:25–26."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the town told her.** *We no longer believe because of what you said.* Which is the outcome any witness would want, and it took two days."}]}, "John 4:46–50": {"title": "\"The man took Jesus at His word and started for home\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A court official walks about twenty miles uphill to ask Jesus to come back down with him, and goes home alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He begged Him to come down and heal his son, who was at the point of death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A specific request with a method attached: come, and heal. The verb for *begged* is continuous — he kept asking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Capernaum is on the lake shore; Cana is up in the hills. He had made a hard journey with a dying child behind him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Unless you people see signs and wonders, you simply will not believe.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is plural — *you people* — so it is not aimed only at this father. Jesus is saying something about the crowd's appetite for spectacle before He does anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man does not defend himself. He says: *Sir, please — come down before my little boy dies.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is given instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Go on home. Your son lives.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not what he asked for. He wanted a journey; he gets a sentence. And the tense of *lives* is present — not *will live*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The man took Jesus at His word and started for home.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the hinge of the story. He had come for a person and left with a sentence, and he turned around and walked back down the hill on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verses have his servants meeting him on the road with the news, and the fever leaving at the exact hour Jesus spoke. John notes that the man worked it out and then *he himself believed, and his whole household.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Twice at Cana.** John points out that this is where the water became wine. Both signs are at the same village, and in both the person who acts on His word before seeing anything — the servants filling jars, and this father walking home — is the one who knows what happened."}]}, "John 4:51–54": {"title": "The very hour", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A father checks the time and finds it matches."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A royal official from Capernaum came to Cana — about twenty miles — because his son was dying."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Sir, come down before my child dies!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus said to him, \"**Go your way; your son lives**.\" So the man believed the word that Jesus spoke to him, and he went his way.* See the note on John 4:46–50."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had asked Him to come. He is sent home alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The journey"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And as **he was now going down**, his servants met him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty miles, downhill from Cana to Capernaum. He does not run."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had come up urgently and goes back at walking pace, which is its own comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The news"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And told him, saying, \"**Your son lives!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ho pais sou ze** — the same three words Jesus used."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The servants report it in the exact phrase, without knowing they are quoting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he **inquired of them the hour** when he got better.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eputheto** — he asked, he enquired."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not celebrate first. He wants the time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they said to him, \"**Yesterday at the seventh hour the fever left him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seventh hour — about one in the afternoon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the father knew that it was **at the same hour** in which Jesus said to him, \"Your son lives.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **en ekeine te hora** — in that hour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because a boy can recover on his own."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The father is doing what anyone honest would do: checking whether the recovery had anything to do with the word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Gospel records the verification."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty miles, and no contact."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The healings in the Gospels are almost all by touch or by presence. This one and the centurion's servant are the exceptions, and both are at a distance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The household"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **he himself believed, and his whole household**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **episteusen autos kai he oikia autou hole**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two believings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 50: *the man believed the word that Jesus spoke to him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 53: *he himself believed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first is trusting a sentence enough to go home. The second is something larger, and John distinguishes them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The count"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This again is **the second sign** Jesus did when He had come out of Judea into Galilee.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **deuteron semeion**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John numbers the first two and then stops counting. The first was water into wine at Cana. See the note on John 2:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both in Cana, and the second one is performed on somebody twenty miles away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John says at the end of the Gospel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And truly Jesus did many other signs in the presence of His disciples, which are not written in this book; **but these are written that you may believe**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven signs are selected, and this is one of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He had said to the man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Unless you people see signs and wonders, you will by no means believe**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said before the healing, to a father whose son was dying. And the man did not argue: *Sir, come down before my child dies*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the father does first.** Asks the servants what time the fever broke. The Gospel records him checking, and records the answer."}]}, "John 4:7–9": {"title": "\"Would you give Me a drink?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first thing He says to her is a request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is asking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Jewish man, alone, tired, sitting on the edge of a well at noon with no bucket."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus was worn out from the journey, and He sat down just as He was beside the well.* See the note on John 4:1–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Would you give Me a drink?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an announcement, a rebuke, or an offer. A need, stated to a stranger, from a position of not having."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He begins the conversation by putting Himself in her debt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she hears in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How is it that You, a Jew, are asking me for a drink — me, a Samaritan woman?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She names three problems in one sentence: He is a Jew, she is a Samaritan, and she is a woman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John adds the fourth as an aside: *for Jews and Samaritans have nothing to do with each other.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is that they do not use vessels in common — a Jew would not drink from a cup a Samaritan had handled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked her for a drink knowing whose jar it was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the disciples are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His disciples had **gone off into the town to buy food**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John notes it because it explains the privacy. Twelve men present and this conversation does not happen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "When they come back they *marvelled that He talked with a woman*, and John says *yet no one said, \"What do You seek?\" or \"Why are You talking with her?\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why she was there at noon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sixth hour, the hottest part of the day. Water was drawn in the cool of the morning and evening, by the women together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A woman with five husbands and a sixth man who is not her husband draws water when nobody else is at the well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is sitting there at exactly that hour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does with the request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never gets the drink. The passage does not record her giving Him one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Within a few exchanges He has moved from her water to *living water*, and then to *whoever drinks of the water that I shall give him will never thirst.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The request was the way in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she leaves behind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The woman then **left her waterpot**, went her way into the city, and said to the men, \"Come, see a Man who told me all things that I ever did.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She came for water and walked off without the jar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what He tells the disciples"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They come back with lunch and He will not eat. *\"My food is to do the will of Him who sent Me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *\"Lift up your eyes and look at the fields, for they are already white for harvest.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is looking at a Samaritan town walking toward Him because of a woman with no reputation."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He said He would have given.** *\"If you knew the gift of God, and who it is who says to you, 'Give Me a drink,' you would have asked Him, and He would have given you living water.\"* The one asking for the drink was carrying the well."}]}, "John 5:1–5": {"title": "Thirty-eight years by the pool", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John sets the scene carefully, and everything in the setting is against the man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Over by **the Sheep Gate**, there is a pool… called **Bethesda**, and it has **five covered walkways**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Sheep Gate is where the animals came in for sacrifice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Bethesda** — house of mercy, or house of outpouring."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Archaeologists uncovered a twin pool complex near St Anne's church in Jerusalem in the nineteenth century, with colonnades on four sides and one across the middle dividing it. Five porches, exactly as John describes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For a long time this verse was cited as evidence that John did not know Jerusalem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A great crowd of people who could not get well — **the blind, the lame, the ones whose bodies had wasted away** — and they were **waiting for the water to move**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three categories, and a crowd of them lying under the roofs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The waiting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From time to time an angel would come down… and **the first one into the water** was made well.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the tradition was, it had a cruel shape to it. Only the first got anything, which meant the sickest never could."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people least able to move were competing to move fastest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One man had been lying there a long while. He had been **an invalid for thirty-eight years**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John gives the number, and it is a number with a history — Israel wandered thirty-eight years between Kadesh Barnea and crossing the brook Zered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses following: *\"**Do you want to be made well?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not answer it. He explains the system instead: *\"Sir, I have no man to put me into the pool when the water is stirred, but while I am coming, another steps down before me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty-eight years of arriving second."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Rise, take up your bed and walk.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No water, no stirring, no angel, and no race."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And immediately the man was made well, took up his bed, and walked.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one He picked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Out of a great crowd of blind, lame and paralysed people, John records one healing that day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Jesus *had withdrawn, a multitude being in that place* — He leaves without a scene."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was the Sabbath, and the objection is about a man carrying a mat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then, later, in the temple: *\"See, you have been made well. **Sin no more, lest a worse thing come upon you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the man goes and tells the authorities who it was."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other question in the chapter.** *\"Do you want to be made well?\"* — asked of a man who had been lying there for thirty-eight years. Nothing in the account suggests it was rhetorical."}]}, "John 5:10–13": {"title": "You are not permitted to carry your mat", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man walks for the first time in thirty-eight years and is stopped for a paperwork offence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This is the Sabbath. You are not permitted to carry your mat**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the walking. Nothing about the legs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ouk exestin** — it is not lawful."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the rule came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not from Moses. Jeremiah has *bear no burden on the Sabbath day*, and Nehemiah shut the gates of Jerusalem against traders."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the first century the rabbinic tradition had worked out thirty-nine categories of prohibited work, and carrying from one domain to another was one of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man with a mat under his arm was a walking violation of a clause."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did not notice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are talking to a man who could not stand that morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John has already established the crowd at that pool — *blind, lame, paralysed* — and these are the people who administered the place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The man who made me well** is the one who said to me, '**Pick up your mat and walk**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not defend the carrying. He points at the authority behind it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the one who made me whole, that one said to me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His logic is that whoever could do the first thing has the right to instruct the second."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Who is this man** who told you to pick it up and walk?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They quote him back — but only the second half. *Who told you to pick it up and walk*, not *who made you well*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The healing is not asked about. It is not even acknowledged."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did not know"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the healed man **had no idea who it was**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been cured by a stranger and had not asked His name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Contrast the blind man in chapter 9, who is asked the same question and ends up arguing theology with the Sanhedrin, and finally worshipping."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he did not know"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **Jesus had slipped away into the crowd** that was in that place.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exeneusen** — turned aside, withdrew. Some read it as a swimming metaphor: He slipped out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He heals and leaves before anyone can identify Him. John reports this pattern more than once — after the loaves, *He departed again to the mountain by Himself alone*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus finds him later, in the temple, and tells him who did it — and then the man goes and tells the authorities. See the note on John 5:14–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter turns into"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This exchange is the hinge. The rest of John 5 is the longest speech Jesus makes about His relationship to the Father — *the Son can do nothing of Himself*, *as the Father raises the dead and gives life, even so the Son gives life*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It all comes out of a question about a mat."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was asked and what was not.** A man who had been on the ground for thirty-eight years is standing in front of them, and the only question is who authorised the bedding."}]}, "John 5:14–18": {"title": "My Father is working right up to this moment", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The sentence that made them want to kill Him is about the Sabbath, and then it turns out not to be."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He finds him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Later Jesus **found him in the temple**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man had been at the pool by the Sheep Gate, just outside. It is the first place he can go now that he can walk — the courts a paralysed man could not reach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus *found* him. The Greek is **heuriskei**, and it is the word John uses for deliberate searching: *He found Philip*, *he first found his own brother Simon*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Look — you are well now**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ide hugies gegonas** — a perfect: you have become whole and stand whole."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not go back to your sinning**, or something worse may come to you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meketi hamartane** — sin no more."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus says the same to the woman in chapter 8 — *neither do I condemn you; go and sin no more*. In both cases the sentence comes after the verdict, not before it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He explicitly refuses the inference elsewhere: of the man born blind, *neither this man nor his parents sinned*. See the note on John 9:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the man does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The man went off and **told the Jewish leaders that it was Jesus** who had made him well.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been asked *who told you to carry it* and could not answer. See the note on John 5:10–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Now he can, and he goes back and tells them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And that is why they began to **hound Jesus and to look for a way to kill Him**: because He did such things **on the Sabbath**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **edioken** — an imperfect: they kept on persecuting. It starts here and does not stop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My Father is working right up to this moment, and I am working too**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **heos arti ergazetai** — He is working until now."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God rested on the seventh day, and the rabbis knew perfectly well that He had not stopped sustaining the world since. Children are born on Sabbaths and men die on them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus takes the one activity nobody denied God does on the Sabbath and puts Himself inside it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they heard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At that they wanted to kill Him **all the more**, because in their eyes He was **not only breaking the Sabbath**, He was **calling God His own Father, making Himself equal with God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **patera idion** — His own Father, in a private sense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They understood Him exactly. Nothing in the next forty verses corrects them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Most assuredly, I say to you, **the Son can do nothing of Himself, but what He sees the Father do**; for whatever He does, the Son also does in like manner.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"as the Father raises the dead and gives life to them, even so the Son gives life to whom He will.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"that all should **honour the Son just as they honour the Father**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four witnesses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest of the chapter lists them: John the Baptist, the works themselves, the Father, and the Scriptures."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You search the Scriptures, for in them you think you have eternal life; and **these are they which testify of Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Sabbath argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He makes it again over the woman bent for eighteen years: *does not each one of you on the Sabbath loose his ox from the stall and lead it away to water it?* See the note on Luke 13:10–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *the Sabbath was made for man, and not man for the Sabbath*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the charge became.** It started as carrying a mat. By the end of the paragraph the accusation is that He called God His own Father — and John reports it as the reason, not as a misunderstanding."}]}, "John 5:19–23": {"title": "\"What the Son sees\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He has just healed a man on the sabbath and told him to carry his mat, and this is the answer to the objection."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What provoked it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore the Jews sought all the more to kill Him, because He not only broke the Sabbath, but also **said that God was His Father, making Himself equal with God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They understood the claim correctly. John records their reading and does not correct it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The denial"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The Son **can do nothing on His own**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which sounds like a retreat and is not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He does **only what He sees the Father doing**, and **whatever the Father does, the Son does in the same way**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **homoios** — likewise, in like manner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first clause limits Him to what He sees. The second says the range is everything the Father does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Together they are a larger claim than the one being denied."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Father does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **the Father loves the Son and shows Him everything He Himself is doing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The love and the showing are one clause."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John says the same pairing repeatedly: *the Father loves the Son and has given all things into His hand*. See the note on John 3:31–36."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise of more"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **He will show Him works greater than these, until you are left astonished**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the greater works are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Just as the Father raises the dead and gives them life, **so the Son also gives life to whomever He chooses**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *whom He wills*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Raising the dead was God's prerogative alone in Jewish thought. He assigns it to Himself in the same sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The judgment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **the Father judges no one. He has handed the whole of judgment over to the Son**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *has given all judgment to the Son*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So that everyone will honor the Son just as they honor the Father**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kathos** — in the same way, to the same degree."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Anyone who does not honor the Son does not honor the Father who sent Him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no third position in the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage is built on seeing. He does what He sees; the Father shows Him; and the works are the evidence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Later in the same Gospel: *the words that I speak to you I do not speak on My own authority; but the Father who dwells in Me does the works.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says about the man at the pool"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The healing that started the argument had one odd feature. The pool at Bethesda was full of sick people — *a great multitude of sick people, blind, lame, paralysed*. See the note on John 5:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He healed one man and walked away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the account explains the selection, and this passage is the nearest thing to one: *what He sees the Father doing*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two resurrections"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses after: *the hour is coming, and now is, when the dead will hear the voice of the Son of God; and those who hear will live.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *the hour is coming in which all who are in the graves will hear His voice and come forth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present and future, in consecutive paragraphs."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The shape of the answer.** A denial that sounds like modesty — *the Son can do nothing on His own* — attached to a claim that He does everything the Father does, and that judgment has been handed over entirely."}]}, "John 5:24–27": {"title": "\"Has crossed over\" — the tense that settles it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three statements, stacked, and each one is stronger than the last. Jesus is not building toward a promise here; He is closing a question."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Read the three parts"}, {"k": "note", "t": "whoever hears My word and trusts the One who sent Me **has** eternal life. He **does not come up** for condemnation. He **has already crossed over** out of death and into life."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*has* — present tense. Not will have.", "*does not come up for condemnation* — the word is the courtroom one. He is not saying the verdict will be favourable. He is saying the person does not appear on that docket.", "*has already crossed over* — and this is the one that carries the weight."]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tense in that last phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is in the perfect. English does not have a good single word for it: it means a thing was done at a point in the past and the state it produced is still the case. Not \"crossed over and we shall see,\" but crossed, and standing on the other bank now."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same tense as *it is finished* on the cross. A completed action whose result stands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the sentence is not describing something a person is working toward or could slip back out of. It describes a border already behind them."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is conditioned on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Worth being exact, because the condition is stated and it is short: *hears My word and trusts the One who sent Me.* Hearing and trusting. There is no third item, and nothing about sustaining it by effort."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole entry requirement in a sentence that then hands over eternal life, immunity from the courtroom, and a completed crossing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The strange middle of the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then Jesus says a time is coming *and it is here now* when the dead hear His voice and live. Both at once — future and present in the same breath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He means it in both senses, and John's Gospel goes on to show it: Lazarus called out of a tomb, and people all through the book crossing from death to life while still walking around. The resurrection at the end is not a different event from what happens when someone hears Him now. It is the same voice."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why this passage gets used the way it does.** People reach for it when they are afraid of losing what they have. It answers that fear, but notice how — not by promising to hold on tightly enough, but by pointing out that the crossing is in the past tense. The question the verse settles is not *will I make it*. It is *where am I standing*, and it answers: on this side already."}]}, "John 5:6–9": {"title": "Get up, pick it up, walk", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He picks one man out of a colonnade full of them and asks a question nobody would need to ask."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He already knew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus saw him lying there, and **He knew that he had already been in that condition a long time**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John has just told us how long: thirty-eight years. See the note on John 5:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The knowing is stated before the question is asked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do you want to be well?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **theleis hugies genesthai** — do you will to become whole."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Asked of a man who has been lying by a pool for decades. It is either the cruellest question in the Gospel or the only one that matters, and the answer he gives is neither yes nor no."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Sir, I have no one to put me into the pool** when the water is stirred. **While I am on my way down, someone else always steps in ahead of me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses, and both are about other people. No helper, and a competitor who is always faster."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not answer the question. He explains the system he has been losing at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the system was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whoever got in first. See the note on John 5:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one requirement was the ability to move, which is the one thing everybody lying in those porches did not have."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get up. Pick up your mat and walk**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three imperatives, and no reference to the pool."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **egeire, aron, peripatei** — rise, take up, walk about."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No water is used. Nobody carries him anywhere. The man is not touched."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of events"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **at once the man was well**. He picked up his mat, and he walked.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eutheos** — immediately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whole first, then the mat, then the walking. The instruction was given to a man who could not obey it and was obeyed by a man who could."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **krabatton** — a poor man's pallet. A rough sleeping mat rather than a bed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same word Mark uses for the one lowered through the roof. See the note on Mark 2:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is told to carry the thing that carried him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **that day was a Sabbath**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John drops it at the end, as a footnote, and the whole rest of the chapter comes out of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every healing controversy in the Gospels turns on this: seven of Jesus' recorded healings happen on the Sabbath, and He never once avoids the day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The woman bent double for eighteen years is another. See the note on Luke 13:10–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The authorities stop the healed man in the street — not for walking, for carrying. See the note on John 5:10–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Jesus' answer, when they come for Him: *My Father is working right up to this moment, and I am working too*. See the note on John 5:14–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was not healed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A great multitude of sick people, blind, lame, paralysed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One man is spoken to. John does not explain, and does not soften it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does not ask about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not faith, not sin, not whether he had been trying. The question is *do you want to be well*, and the man's answer is about not having anyone."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was not used.** The pool. Thirty-eight years of waiting on stirred water, and the cure is three words spoken to a man on a mat."}]}, "John 6:1–4": {"title": "Across the Sea of Galilee", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four verses of setting, and every detail in them is doing something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crossing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After this Jesus **crossed to the far side of the Sea of Galilee**, which is also called **the Sea of Tiberias**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John gives both names. **Tiberias** was the Roman name, after the emperor — Herod Antipas had built a city on the shore and named it for him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John writes for readers who would know the Roman name and not the Jewish one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crowd"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A large crowd **kept following Him**, because they had **seen the signs He was doing for the sick**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is imperfect — they were following, continuously."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John states the reason plainly and without flattery: the signs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the end of the chapter almost all of them are gone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus **went up on the mountainside and sat down there with His disciples**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sitting is the posture of a teacher."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The date"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Passover, the festival of the Jews, was near.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John marks three Passovers in his Gospel, and it is how the ministry is usually dated to about three years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This one matters for what follows. Passover is the feast of the lamb and of unleavened bread, and the chapter is about to be entirely about bread."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why John gives the date"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The discourse that follows runs on Passover material: manna in the wilderness, bread from heaven, and *unless you eat the flesh of the Son of Man and drink His blood, you have no life in you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The feast is in the background of every line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five thousand men, besides women and children. Five barley loaves and two small fish from a boy in the crowd."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Jesus took the loaves, and **when He had given thanks** He distributed them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve baskets of fragments left over — one for each disciple who had said it could not be done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only miracle in all four Gospels."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the crowd tried to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore when Jesus perceived that they were about to **come and take Him by force to make Him king**, He departed again to the mountain by Himself alone.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does that night"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Walks out to the disciples on the water, three or four miles from shore, in a storm. See the note on John 6:16–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says the next day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Most assuredly, I say to you, **you seek Me, not because you saw the signs, but because you ate of the loaves and were filled**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells the crowd why they came, and it is not the reason John gave in verse 2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is a hard teaching. Who can listen to it?\"* — and *from that time many of His disciples went back and walked with Him no more*. See the note on John 6:60–65."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens with a large crowd following and ends with twelve men and a question: *do you also want to go away?*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the four verses set up.** A lake with two names, a crowd there for the healings, a teacher sitting on a hill, and Passover coming — and then a boy's lunch feeds five thousand and a discourse about bread empties the room."}]}, "John 6:10–13": {"title": "Twelve baskets of leftovers", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only miracle in all four Gospels, and John keeps a detail the others share and nobody quite explains."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"As much as they wanted\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a ration. John says the fish were handed out *as much as they wanted*, and that everyone *ate their fill*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb for eating their fill is a strong one — it was used of feeding animals to satisfaction. Five thousand men, plus the women and children the other accounts mention, all eating until they stopped wanting more."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Gather up the pieces that are left over, so that nothing is wasted.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the sentence worth stopping on. The man who has just produced an unlimited supply out of five loaves tells His disciples to pick up the crumbs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abundance and waste are treated as different things. The miracle does not make the fragments unimportant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Twelve baskets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They end with more bread than they started with, and the number is exact — twelve, one for each disciple. The men who had said *what are five loaves among so many* each end the day carrying a full basket."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not comment on that either. He just records the number."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What happens next.** The crowd tries to make Him king by force, and He withdraws to a mountain alone. The following morning He tells them they are looking for Him because they ate the loaves, and then calls Himself the bread of life — a claim so hard that most of them stop following Him by the end of the chapter. The feeding is the beginning of a much longer argument."}]}, "John 6:14–15": {"title": "Away to the mountain alone", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The crowd draws the right conclusion and wants the wrong thing done about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sign"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the people saw **the sign He had done**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five thousand men fed from five barley loaves and two small fish, with twelve baskets of fragments gathered afterwards. See the note on John 6:10–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John calls the miracles **semeia** — signs. Things that point somewhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They began saying, \"**This really is the Prophet who is to come into the world**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 18: *the LORD your God will raise up for you a Prophet like me from your midst… Him you shall hear*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The Prophet** — a fixed expectation. The priests sent to question John the Baptist asked *are you the Prophet?* and he said no."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why bread produced that conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses gave them manna in the wilderness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd have just watched somebody feed a multitude in a wilderness, and they name him with the title reserved for the one who would be like Moses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reasoning is sound."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus knew they meant to **come and seize Him and make Him king by force**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **harpazein** — to snatch, to seize violently. The verb used of a wolf catching sheep, and of being caught up to meet the Lord."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plan"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Coronation, by mob."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Galilee was the region where revolts started. A prophet who could feed an army is a supply chain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So He **withdrew again to the mountain, alone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anechoresen** — withdrew, retreated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Palin** — again. He had already gone up the mountain once in this chapter, before the feeding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Autos monos** — He Himself alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Matthew adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Immediately Jesus **made His disciples get into the boat** and go before Him to the other side, while He sent the multitudes away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **enankasen** — compelled, forced."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has to make the twelve leave. Mark records the same verb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The disciples did not want to go, which suggests what they thought of the crown."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does on the mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when He had sent the multitudes away, **He went up on the mountain by Himself to pray**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The night ends with Him walking on the water toward a boat in a storm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the crowd does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Finds Him on the other side the next morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Rabbi, when did You come here?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Most assuredly, I say to you, **you seek Me, not because you saw the signs, but because you ate of the loaves and were filled**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them their motive to their faces, and then the bread discourse begins."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From that time on, **many of His disciples turned back and no longer walked with Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do you want to leave too?\"* See the note on John 6:66–71."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that opens with a crowd trying to crown Him ends with almost all of them gone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says about the crown later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My kingdom is not of this world**. If My kingdom were of this world, My servants would fight.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to Pilate, who had the authority to give Him one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one crown He takes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the soldiers twisted a crown of thorns and put it on His head.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they put a purple robe on Him, and said, \"Hail, King of the Jews!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only coronation recorded is a mockery, and John writes the title over the cross: **JESUS OF NAZARETH, THE KING OF THE JEWS**."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb.** **Harpazein** — to seize by force. They reached the right conclusion about who He was and moved to make it happen, and He went up a mountain by Himself."}]}, "John 6:16–21": {"title": "\"Immediately the boat was at the shore\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John tells the storm in six verses and adds an ending the other Gospels do not have."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they were going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Started across **toward Capernaum**. It was dark by then, and **Jesus had not come to them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John notes the absence. They set out expecting Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conditions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A strong wind was blowing, and **the water was rising rough** around them.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When they had rowed about **twenty-five or thirty stadia — three or four miles out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John gives the figure. The lake is roughly seven miles across at that point, so they are about halfway and have been at it for hours."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the only one who measures it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They saw Jesus **walking on the sea and coming near the boat**, and **they were afraid**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not mention the ghost. Just that they were frightened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It is I. Do not be afraid.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ego eimi** — two words, and John has been building his whole Gospel on that phrase."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In John's hands it is never just *it's me*. He records seven sayings with a predicate — bread, light, door, shepherd, resurrection, way, vine — and several without one, including *before Abraham was, I am*, which nearly got Him stoned. See the note on John 8:57–59."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here it is spoken over water in the dark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then they were **glad to take Him into the boat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is that they were willing to receive Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark says He was *about to go right past them* and stopped for their screaming. John records the moment they wanted Him aboard. See the note on Mark 6:45–52."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ending nobody else has"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **immediately the boat was at the shore they had been heading for**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three or four miles out, at night, into a headwind — and then at the destination."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John states it flatly, in one clause, with no explanation and no reaction from anybody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew and Mark both record the wind dropping. Only John records the distance disappearing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd finds Him on the other side the following day, and He tells them *you seek Me, not because you saw the signs, but because you ate of the loaves and were filled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the bread of life discourse, ending with many disciples walking away. See the note on John 6:52–59."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two problems in the chapter.** Five thousand people and not enough bread, and then a lake and not enough time. John puts them on consecutive pages, and the second is solved in half a sentence."}]}, "John 6:22–27": {"title": "Food that does not spoil", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The crowd works out that He left without a boat, and then asks the wrong question about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The next day the crowd that had stayed on the other side of the sea noticed that **there had been only one boat there**, and that **Jesus had not gotten into it** with His disciples — they had gone off alone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have counted the boats."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He fed them, withdrew to the mountain alone when they tried to crown Him, and now He is not on this side and He did not sail."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail John adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Other boats came in from Tiberias and landed **near the place where the people had eaten the bread after the Lord had given thanks**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eucharistesantos tou Kuriou** — the Lord having given thanks."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John names the location by what happened there, and the event he names it by is the thanksgiving, not the multiplication."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pursuit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So… they **climbed into the boats themselves and went over to Capernaum, looking for Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They cross a lake to find Him. It is not indifference."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Rabbi, when did You get here?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the question about the boat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have the makings of a genuine puzzle — He crossed water without transport — and they ask about the timetable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do not ask"}, {"k": "p", "t": "How."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The disciples know: *they saw Jesus walking on the sea and drawing near the boat, and they were afraid*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I tell you the truth, **you are looking for Me not because you saw the signs, but because you ate the loaves and had your fill**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **echortasthete** — you were filled, satisfied. The word for feeding animals to fullness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Answer the question."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them their motive instead, to their faces, having just fed them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distinction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Semeia** — signs. Things that point."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They saw a sign and took the bread. The pointing did not register."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not work for the food that spoils, but for the food that lasts on into eternal life**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ergazesthe me ten brosin ten apollumenen** — work not for the perishing food."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *do not work*. Do not work **for** that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The manna behind it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The food that would not keep, gathered fresh every morning, breeding worms if held overnight. See the note on Exodus 16:19–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd raises it themselves two verses later: *our fathers ate the manna in the desert*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who supplies it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The food the Son of Man will give you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dosei** — future. He will give it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which sits oddly against *work for* in the same sentence, and John lets both stand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their next question is exactly that: *what shall we do, that we may work the works of God?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This is the work of God, that you believe in Him whom He sent**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"God the Father has **put His own mark of approval on Him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **esphragisen** — sealed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for a seal pressed into wax to certify ownership and authenticity. See the note on Revelation 7:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am the bread of life**. He who comes to Me shall never hunger.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the discourse hardens, and the crowd that crossed a lake to find Him leaves. See the note on John 6:66–71."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The name of the place.** John identifies the site as *where they ate the bread after the Lord had given thanks* — naming it by the thanksgiving rather than by the miracle."}]}, "John 6:35–40": {"title": "\"I will never turn away\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first of the seven great *I am* sayings in John, and what follows it is a run of promises with nothing conditional in them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am the bread of life. Whoever comes to Me will never be hungry, and whoever believes in Me will never be thirsty again.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bread and thirst in one sentence — He answers a need He was not asked about. The crowd had come looking for another meal after the five thousand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The double negative"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The one who comes to Me I will never turn away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek stacks two negatives, which in that language intensifies rather than cancels: I will certainly not, under any circumstances, cast out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the verb is *ekballo* — to throw out, put outside. It is the word used of driving out demons and of expelling someone from a synagogue."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever else is uncertain, the person who comes is not going to be shown the door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Twice, the same will"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus states the Father's will twice in three verses, and both times it is about keeping:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*that of everything He has given Me I should **lose none of it**, but raise it up on the last day*", "*that everyone who looks to the Son and believes in Him should have eternal life, and I will raise him up*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb for *lose* is the one used of a lost sheep and a lost coin. He describes His own commission as not losing anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone the Father gives Me will come to Me, and the one who comes to Me I will never turn away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves are in the sentence and John does not resolve the tension. The giving comes first; the coming is real; and the welcome is unconditional."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The repeated line.** *I will raise him up on the last day* appears four times in this chapter. Whatever the bread of life turns out to mean, Jesus keeps attaching it to resurrection rather than to sustenance."}]}, "John 6:47–51": {"title": "\"The bread I will give is My flesh\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus compares Himself to manna and then says the comparison does not go far enough."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The manna argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your ancestors ate manna in the wilderness, and **they died**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd had raised the manna themselves, as a credential — Moses gave our fathers bread from heaven. Jesus accepts the fact and points at the graves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wilderness generation ate the miracle bread every morning for forty years and is buried in the desert. Whatever the manna did, it did not deal with death."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is the bread that comes down from heaven, so that a person may eat it and not die. I am the living bread.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The manna came down and was gathered. This bread is described as **living** — alive in itself, not merely supplied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the tense at the end is future: *the bread I **will** give is My flesh, and I will give it for the life of the world.* He is pointing forward to the cross."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the crowd could not take it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verses have them arguing among themselves — *how can this man give us his flesh to eat?* — and many disciples turn back before the chapter ends."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus does not soften it or explain it away. He repeats it, harder."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Eating"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb He uses in the following verses is a blunt one — the ordinary word for chewing. Whatever else the language does, it insists on something taken in personally rather than admired from outside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Manna had to be gathered by each household, every morning, and eaten. Nobody could gather it for you."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter ends.** *The words I have spoken to you are spirit and they are life.* Jesus Himself supplies the interpretive key after the hard sayings — and then asks the twelve whether they want to leave too. Peter's answer is about having nowhere else to go."}]}, "John 6:5–9": {"title": "He already knew what He was about to do", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A question is asked by someone who is not asking for information."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The look"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Jesus **looked up and saw the great crowd coming toward Him**, He said to Philip, \"**Where are we going to buy bread for these people to eat?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John alone records the question, and alone records who it was put to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Philip"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was from Bethsaida, the nearest town. If anyone in the group knew where bread could be bought in that district, it was him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question is aimed at the man best equipped to answer it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The narrator's note"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He said it **to test him**, because **He already knew what He was about to do**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **peirazon** — testing, proving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John tells the reader the outcome before the scene plays. There is no suspense about whether the crowd will be fed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The test is not to find out what Philip will do. It is for Philip."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Philip's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Two hundred silver coins** — the better part of a year's pay — **would not buy enough bread for every one of them to have a little**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **denarion diakosion** — two hundred denarii. A denarius was a day's wage for a labourer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His arithmetic is correct. He has costed it, and even the sum he names would fall short of *a little* each."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind of answer it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Accurate, and entirely about money."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was asked *where*, and he answered *how much*, which is the natural translation for a practical man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Andrew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One of His disciples, **Andrew, the brother of Simon Peter**, spoke up.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John identifies him by his brother every time he appears — the man who brought Peter to Jesus in chapter 1, and is still described by the relationship."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Andrew found"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**There is a boy here with five barley loaves and two small fish**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paidarion** — a little boy. And **opsaria** — small fish, the salted or pickled sort eaten as a relish with bread."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Barley** was the poor man's grain, half the price of wheat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Andrew has been looking. He is the only one who went and found something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But what is that among so many?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He brings the supply and dismisses it in the same breath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Philip did the sum and concluded nothing could be done. Andrew found the little that existed and concluded the same thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose lunch it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A child's, and nobody asked him for it before Andrew found him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The boy is not mentioned again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Jesus took the loaves, and when He had given thanks He distributed them to the disciples, and the disciples to those sitting down.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's account has the imperfect: *He kept handing the pieces to the disciples*. See the note on Luke 9:10–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The leftovers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Gather up the fragments that remain, so that nothing is lost**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve baskets, from five loaves, gathered on the instruction of a man who had just made bread out of nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd tries to make Him king by force, He withdraws to a mountain alone, and the next day they find Him and He tells them *you seek Me not because you saw the signs, but because you ate of the loaves and were filled*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the bread discourse, and the departure. See the note on John 6:66–71."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that begins with a boy's lunch ends with almost everybody gone."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The note John inserts.** *He already knew what He was about to do.* The question was never about locating bread — it was put to Philip for Philip."}]}, "John 6:52–59": {"title": "\"Unless you eat\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The hardest thing Jesus ever said in public, and He does not soften it when the room turns."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How can this man give us His flesh to eat?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a fair question, and the Greek says they were *arguing sharply* — fighting among themselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything about the statement would have been abhorrent to that audience. Blood was forbidden absolutely under Leviticus 17, on pain of being cut off. See the note on Leviticus 17:10–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does about the objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Repeats it, four times, harder."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Unless you **eat the flesh** of the Son of Man and **drink His blood**, you have no life in you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then *whoever feeds on My flesh and drinks My blood* three more times in five verses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb shifts partway through from **phago** (eat) to **trogo** — to gnaw, to chew. It is a physical, unmistakable word, and He moves toward it rather than away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **My flesh is true food, and My blood is true drink**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **alethes** — real, genuine. Not a metaphor for something realer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it produces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four things, stated flatly:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*has **eternal life***", "*I will **raise him up on the last day***", "*remains in Me, and I in him*", "*will **live because of Me***"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As **the living Father sent Me and I live because of the Father**, so the one who feeds on Me **will live because of Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The relationship between the Father and the Son is offered as the pattern for the relationship between Christ and the one who eats."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the largest comparison in the passage, and it is easy to read past."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The manna, again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is **not like the bread your ancestors ate, and died**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd had opened this conversation by quoting the manna at Him. He grants that they ate it, and points out how the story finished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the notes on Exodus 16:13–15 and John 6:47–51."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From that time **many of His disciples went back and walked with Him no more**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the next paragraph. He does not call them back or clarify."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Instead He turns to the twelve: *do you also want to go away?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Peter: *Lord, to whom shall we go? You have the words of eternal life.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He said it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While He was teaching in **the synagogue in Capernaum**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not privately, not to the twelve. In the synagogue of the town He lived in, to people who knew His family."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter opened with.** Five thousand fed with five loaves. They came back the next day wanting more bread, and He told them *do not labour for the food which perishes.* The crowd that had eaten the miracle is the crowd that walked away from the sermon about it."}]}, "John 6:60–65": {"title": "A hard thing to hear", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The day after the feeding of the five thousand, and by the end of it almost everybody has gone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had heard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bread of life discourse — *I am the living bread which came down from heaven*, and then the sentences that emptied the room: *unless you eat the flesh of the Son of Man and drink His blood, you have no life in you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The complaint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This is a hard teaching. Who can listen to it?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **skleros** — hard, harsh, rough. Not *difficult to understand*. Hard to take."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is not the crowd saying it. *Many of **His disciples***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He knew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus **knew within Himself** that His disciples were grumbling about it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **gogguzousin** — murmuring. It is the word the Greek Old Testament uses for Israel in the wilderness, complaining about the manna."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had just been fed miraculously and are murmuring about bread. John chose the word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Does this trip you up?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **skandalizei** — from **skandalon**, the trigger of a trap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The harder thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then **what if you were to see the Son of Man going up to where He was before?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If the incarnation offends, the ascension will be worse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not soften the teaching. He raises it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The key"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It is the Spirit who gives life; human strength on its own counts for nothing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the flesh profits nothing*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has spent the chapter talking about eating flesh, and now says flesh is worth nothing. It is the interpretive key handed over in one line — they had been hearing it at the level of meat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same contrast: *the letter kills, but the Spirit gives life.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 3:4–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What His words are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The words I have spoken to you are spirit, and they are life**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **there are some of you who do not believe**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For Jesus had known **from the beginning** which of them did not believe, and **which one would betray Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judas is in the room and has been for some time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is why I told you that **no one can come to Me unless it has been given to him by My Father**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said it earlier in the chapter: *no one can come to Me unless the Father who sent Me draws him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He repeats it at the moment of the exodus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *from that time **many of His disciples went back and walked with Him no more**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the largest recorded departure in the Gospels, and He does not call them back or adjust anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question to the twelve"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do you also want to go away?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Peter: *\"**Lord, to whom shall we go? You have the words of eternal life**. Also we have come to believe and know that You are the Christ, the Son of the living God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An answer with no enthusiasm in it and no alternative either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Did I not choose you, the twelve, and **one of you is a devil**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that opens with five thousand fed ends with a crowd gone, eleven staying, and one named."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He does when the room empties.** Nothing. He does not explain further, soften the saying, or go after them — and the only question He asks afterward is put to the men who are left."}]}, "John 6:66–71": {"title": "Where else would we go", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The largest crowd He ever had walks away, and He asks the twelve whether they want to as well."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five thousand fed, and the crowd coming back the next day wanting more: *you seek Me… because you ate of the loaves and were filled*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the bread discourse, which gets progressively harder — *I am the bread of life*, then *the bread that I shall give is My flesh*, then *unless you eat the flesh of the Son of Man and drink His blood, you have no life in you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the reaction: *this is a hard saying; who can understand it?* See the note on John 6:60–65."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The departure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From that time on, **many of His disciples turned back and no longer walked with Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apelthon eis ta opiso** — went away to the things behind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Disciples**, not the crowd. Learners who had been following."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the largest defection recorded in the Gospels, and it comes at the peak of His popularity — the day before, they had wanted to make Him king by force."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not soften the saying, run after them, or explain it more accessibly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Jesus said to the Twelve, \"**Do you want to leave too?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek expects the answer no — **me kai humeis thelete hupagein** — but it is a real question, and He asks it out loud with the room emptying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Peter's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Lord, to whom would we go? You have the words of eternal life**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not claim to have understood the discourse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His answer is comparative: there is nowhere else. The alternatives have been considered and found empty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The confession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **we have come to believe, and we know**, that You are the Messiah, the Son of the living God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pepisteukamen kai egnokamen** — we have believed and have come to know."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both perfect tenses. Believing first, and then knowing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is the interesting part, and it is the reverse of the one usually assumed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Did I not choose you, the Twelve? And one of you is a devil**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **diabolos** — slanderer, accuser."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter has just made the best confession in the chapter, and the answer names a traitor in the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The identification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was speaking of **Judas Iscariot**, the son of Simon — for he was the one who would betray Him, and **he was one of the Twelve**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John adds the last clause. Not an outsider. One of the twelve."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John does with this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same construction he uses at the table: *one of you is going to hand Me over*, and the disciples looking at one another with no idea. See the note on John 13:21–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody spots him. Not here, and not two years later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for turning back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb John uses when the soldiers in the garden *drew back and fell to the ground*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was lost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A crowd that had wanted to crown Him, reduced to twelve, one of whom is named as the problem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens with five thousand men and ends with a question addressed to eleven honest men and one who is not."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Peter does not claim.** To have understood it. His answer is that the alternatives have been checked — *to whom would we go?* — and the believing comes before the knowing in his own sentence."}]}, "John 7:37–39": {"title": "Rivers of living water", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He waits until the last day of the feast, and then He shouts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day He chose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the **last day of the festival, the great day**, Jesus **stood up and called out in a loud voice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Feast of Tabernacles ran seven days, and every morning a priest carried a golden pitcher down to the pool of Siloam, filled it, and poured it out at the base of the altar while the people sang from Isaiah 12 — *with joy you will draw water from the wells of salvation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "By most accounts the pouring did not happen on the eighth day. The pitcher stayed empty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Into that silence, a man stands up in the temple court and says: if anyone is thirsty, come to Me."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Let him come to Me and drink\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs, and both are simple. Not qualify, not prepare. Come, and drink."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The invitation is addressed to a condition — *if anyone is **thirsty*** — and thirst is not an achievement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the rivers come out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Out of his **innermost being** will flow **rivers** of living water.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a trickle and not a cistern. **Rivers**, plural."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *out of his belly* — the deepest inside of a person, the part nothing reaches."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And note the direction. He drinks, and what comes out of him is more than he took in, and it comes out for other people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"As the Scripture has said\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "John does not tell us which Scripture, and no single verse matches word for word. The picture runs through the prophets — water from the rock in the wilderness, Ezekiel's river out from under the temple threshold, Zechariah's living waters going out from Jerusalem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is drawing on a whole current, not quoting a line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "John's own footnote"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was speaking about the Spirit, whom those who believed in Him were going to receive; for the Holy Spirit had not yet been given, **because Jesus had not yet been glorified**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John, writing decades later, explains it for us. The Spirit waits on the cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the sequence is stated plainly: glorified first, then given. What was announced in a temple courtyard at a feast arrived in an upper room at Pentecost."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the water had been coming from.** The pool of Siloam — the same pool Jesus sends the blind man to wash in two chapters later. John keeps his images and comes back to them."}]}, "John 8:31–36": {"title": "\"We have never been anyone's slaves\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted line in this passage — *the truth will set you free* — is attached to a condition on one side and an argument on the other, and both get cut off when it is quoted alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you hold to My word, then you really are My disciples. And you will know the truth…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb means to remain, to stay put, to keep living somewhere. Freedom is promised to people who stay in what He said, not to people who encounter it once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim that gives the game away"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are Abraham's descendants, and we have never been anyone's slaves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were speaking in a country under Roman occupation, as descendants of people who had been slaves in Egypt, and after Babylon. The sentence is historically false three times over, and they say it without blinking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole diagnosis. A person can be quite certain they are free while standing inside the thing that holds them, and the certainty is part of the condition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus says the slavery is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone who commits sin is a slave of sin.* Not \"does wrong things occasionally\" — the construction is about a settled practice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the family image: *a slave does not stay in the house forever, but a son belongs there forever*. Slaves in a household could be sold; sons could not be un-sonned. He is contrasting two kinds of belonging, and only one of them is permanent."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"Free indeed.\"** The word is *ontos* — really, actually, in fact. Jesus adds it because He has just been told by the people in front of Him that they were free already. The adverb is aimed at that claim: not free in the way you think you are. Free in fact."}]}, "John 8:52–56": {"title": "Abraham saw My day", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An argument about Abraham, and the last line moves it two thousand years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The accusation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Now we are sure You have a demon**. Abraham died, and so did the prophets, and You say, 'If anyone keeps My word, he will never taste death.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had said it once already in the chapter — *are we not right in saying that You are a Samaritan and have a demon?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second time it is presented as settled: *now we are sure*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they heard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will never taste death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said *if anyone keeps My word he shall never **see** death*, and they repeat it as *taste*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They take it as a claim about dying, which is the only category available."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Are You greater than our father Abraham?** He died, and the prophets died. **Who do You make Yourself out to be?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tina seauton poieis** — whom do You make Yourself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same question the Samaritan woman asked at the well: *are You greater than our father Jacob?* See the note on John 4:10–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John records both, and both are answered by something larger than the comparison."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer about glory"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If I glorify Myself, My glory means nothing**. It is My Father who glorifies Me — **the very One you call your God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not answer the *greater than Abraham* question directly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You have never known Him, but I know Him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek uses two verbs — **egnokate** for what they have not done, and **oida** for what He does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If I were to say that I do not know Him, **I would be a liar like you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sharpest sentence in the chapter, and it is a claim about honesty: He will not deny the knowledge to defuse the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Your father Abraham rejoiced that he would see My day. He saw it, and he was glad**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **egalliasato** — leaped for joy, exulted. The word Mary uses: *my spirit has rejoiced in God my Saviour*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rejoiced that he **would see**… **He saw it**, and he was glad.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anticipation, then sight, then gladness. Three stages in one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text does not say."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis records Abraham laughing — *then Abraham fell on his face and laughed* — when told Sarah would have a son."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And on the mountain: *God will provide for Himself the lamb*, and then a ram in a thicket, and the place named *the LORD will provide*. See the note on Genesis 22:13–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the mountain of the LORD it will be seen*, said as a proverb about something still future."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You are not yet fifty years old, and have You seen Abraham?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They hear a claim about age, which is the smaller misunderstanding of the two."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Most assuredly, I say to you, **before Abraham was, I AM**.\"* See the note on John 8:57–59."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *I was*. **Ego eimi** — the present tense, and the name from the bush."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then they took up stones to throw at Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They understood that one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews says about the same seeing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These all died in faith, **not having received the promises, but having seen them afar off** were assured of them, embraced them.* See the note on Hebrews 11:13–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is **idontes** — having seen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter opened with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am the light of the world**. He who follows Me shall not walk in darkness.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the argument runs from there to a claim about Abraham and a handful of stones."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The three stages.** *Rejoiced that he would see… He saw it… and he was glad.* Anticipation, sight, gladness — said of a man dead two thousand years."}]}, "John 8:57–59": {"title": "\"Before Abraham was, I AM\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The shortest sentence He ever said about Himself, and the crowd reached for stones before He finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You are not yet fifty years old**, and You have seen Abraham?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had just said *Abraham rejoiced to see My day, and he saw it and was glad.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their objection is about arithmetic. Two thousand years and one lifetime do not overlap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I tell you the truth, **before Abraham was, I am**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tenses do not agree, and that is the whole sentence. Not *I was*. Not *I existed*. **I am.**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before Abraham came into being — the Greek verb is one of becoming, of coming to exist — I **am**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He used"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ego eimi.* It is the Greek of Exodus 3, where God tells Moses to say *I AM has sent me to you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name given at the burning bush, in the present tense, applied to Himself in the temple courts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on Exodus 3:13–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At that they **picked up stones** to throw at Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There was no misunderstanding. Stoning was the penalty for blasphemy, and nobody in that court had to have it explained."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's earlier claims — that He was sent, that He does the Father's will, that they would die in their sins — had not produced this. This did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seven \"I am\"s in John"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bread of life. Light of the world. The door. The good shepherd. The resurrection and the life. The way, the truth and the life. The true vine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven with something after them, and this one, with nothing after it at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He walked into"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Jesus **hid Himself and went out of the temple, passing right through the middle of them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Through the crowd holding the stones. John does not explain it and does not comment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does the same in Luke 4, when Nazareth takes Him to the brow of the hill to throw Him off — *passing through the midst of them, He went His way.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They could not lay a hand on Him until the night He let them, and then in the garden, when the soldiers said they were looking for Jesus of Nazareth and He answered *I am*, they *drew back and fell to the ground.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The name and what it does not specify.** *I AM WHO I AM* — the sentence has no predicate. It is not *I am strong* or *I am merciful*. It is the verb standing alone, and whatever the need in front of it, the name is large enough."}]}, "John 8:7–11": {"title": "Neither do I condemn you", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A trap set with a woman in it, and He answers by writing on the floor."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setup"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had brought her *caught in adultery, in the very act* — which requires witnesses, and raises the question of where the man is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their question was the trap: *Moses commanded us that such should be stoned. But what do You say?* Say stone her and He contradicts everything He has taught; say release her and He contradicts Moses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John states the motive: *this they said, testing Him, that they might have something of which to accuse Him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first writing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Jesus stooped down and wrote on the ground with His finger.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only recorded instance of Jesus writing anything, and John does not say what."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The finger of God wrote the law on stone twice. And Jeremiah has a line about the same posture: *those who depart from Me shall be written in the earth.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pressing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when they **kept pressing Him for an answer**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is an imperfect — they went on asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He straightened up and said to them, \"**Let the one among you who is without sin be the first to throw a stone at her**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anamartetos** — without sin, and it occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not overturn the law. He grants the stoning and assigns who goes first."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy required the witnesses to throw first: *the hands of the witnesses shall be the first against him to put him to death*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second writing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He **bent down again and went on writing** on the ground.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not watch them leave. He hands them the sentence and goes back to the dust."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The departure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And those who heard it, **convicted by their own conscience**, went out **one by one, beginning with the oldest**, until the last of them was gone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oldest first. Whatever the test was, the men with the most years behind them failed it soonest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is left"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus was left alone, with **the woman still standing there in front of Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *in the midst* — she is still standing in the position they put her in, in the middle of the temple court, and the circle around her has gone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She could have left with them, and did not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Woman, where are your accusers? Has no one condemned you?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *condemned* is **katekrinen** — passed sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No one, Lord**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words, and the second one is a title."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Neither do I condemn you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only man in the courtyard qualified to throw the first stone declines to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not saying nothing happened. He is saying no sentence is passed by Him — and He is the one who will carry the sentence Himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go, and from now on leave your life of sin**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order matters. Not *sin no more and then I will not condemn you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The acquittal comes first and the instruction follows it, and He gives her nothing to earn in between."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the same to the man at Bethesda, and there too the healing came first. See the note on John 5:14–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am the light of the world**. He who follows Me shall not walk in darkness, but have the light of life.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next sentence in the chapter, said in the temple court she is standing in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the words go in Paul"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is therefore now **no condemnation** to those who are in Christ Jesus.* See the note on Romans 8:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katakrima**, the noun from the verb John uses here."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of the two sentences.** *Neither do I condemn you* comes first, and *go and sin no more* second. Nothing in the exchange asks her to change anything before He speaks."}]}, "John 9:24–27": {"title": "\"One thing I do know\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man who has been blind from birth is interrogated twice by the religious authorities, and he wins."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they want him to say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Give the glory to God.** We know this man is a sinner.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a legal formula — an oath, roughly *tell the truth before God*. And they attach the verdict they want in the same breath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not asking him a question. They are telling him what the answer is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he will not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whether He is a sinner, I do not know.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He declines to go past what he has. He is not a theologian and does not pretend to be one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does have"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**One thing I do know: I was blind, and now I see.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no argument against that sentence. They can dispute the doctrine; they cannot get at the fact."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was blind on that street every day of his life — the neighbours in this chapter argue about whether it is even the same man. And he is standing there looking at them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second interrogation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What did He do to you? How did He open your eyes?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They ask him to go through it again. They had already heard it — verse 15 records the first telling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are looking for a discrepancy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have already told you, **and you would not listen**. Why do you want to hear it again? **Do you want to become His disciples too?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A beggar, unschooled, being examined by the Sanhedrin, and he asks them if they are signing up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the funniest line in John's Gospel and it is delivered by a man with everything to lose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they threw him out.* Excommunicated — put out of the synagogue, which in that world meant out of the community."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His own parents had already backed away: *he is of age, ask him*, because *they feared the Jews.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who came looking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus heard that they had cast him out, and **when He had found him**, He said, \"Do you believe in the Son of God?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes and finds him. The man had never seen His face — he had been sent to the pool to wash while still blind, and had not laid eyes on Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, I believe.\" And he worshipped Him.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He says about the whole thing.** *For judgement I have come into this world, that those who do not see may see, and that those who see may be made blind.* The Pharisees ask whether He means them, and He answers: *if you were blind, you would have no sin. But now you say, \"We see.\" Therefore your sin remains.*"}]}, "John 9:6–7": {"title": "Mud, and a pool called Sent", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The strangest method in the Gospels, and the pool's name is the explanation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He had just said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Neither this man nor his parents sinned, but **that the works of God should be revealed in him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *as long as I am in the world, I am the light of the world*. See the note on John 9:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The disciples had asked whose fault the blindness was. He refused the question and then made mud."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The action"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **spat on the ground** and worked the dirt and the spittle into a **little clay**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pelon** — clay, mud."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about it is hygienic and nothing about it is necessary. He heals with a word elsewhere, and at a distance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being made"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dust and moisture, worked by hand, applied to a part of a man that does not work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD God **formed man of the dust of the ground**, and breathed into his nostrils the breath of life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has already said the man was blind **from birth**, so there is nothing to restore. Something has to be made."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The application"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He **spread the clay over the man's eyes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epechrisen** — anointed, smeared on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A blind man now has mud on his face and has been given no explanation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go**,\" He told him, \"**and wash in the pool of Siloam**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One command, and a walk across Jerusalem with clay on his eyes, in public."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pool is at the south end of the city, at the bottom of the Tyropoeon valley. From the temple area it is downhill all the way and a fair distance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The name means **Sent**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John stops the narrative to translate it. He does this a handful of times, and never idly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Shiloach**, from **shalach**, to send. The pool is named for the channel that sent water into it from the Gihon spring — Hezekiah's tunnel, cut through solid rock before the Assyrian siege."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why John translates it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because *sent* is his word for Jesus. He uses it more than thirty times: *Him whom the Father sanctified and sent into the world*, *the works which the Father has given Me to finish… bear witness of Me, that the Father has sent Me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man is washed in a pool called Sent by a man who calls Himself the Sent One."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah said about that water"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because this people refused **the waters of Shiloah that flow softly**… now therefore the Lord brings up over them the waters of the River, strong and mighty — the king of Assyria.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah contrasts a quiet local channel with an imperial flood, and says they preferred the flood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the man **went off and washed, and he came back seeing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs, and the whole miracle is in them. John spends one sentence on the cure and thirty-four more verses on the argument about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The neighbours cannot agree whether it is the same man. He keeps saying *I am he*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two interrogations by the Pharisees, his parents questioned and refusing to commit because *they feared the Jews*, and then the sentence that ends it: *do you also want to become His disciples?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They throw him out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he ends up saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whether He is a sinner or not I do not know. **One thing I know: that though I was blind, now I see**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And later, when Jesus finds him: *\"Lord, I believe!\" And he worshipped Him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For judgment I have come into this world, **that those who do not see may see, and that those who see may be made blind**.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why John stops to translate.** *Siloam* means *Sent* — and *sent* is the word he has been using of Jesus since the first chapter."}]}, "Joshua 1:1–5": {"title": "\"Now it's your turn\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The book opens with a funeral and a promotion in the same sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The death"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses, **the servant of the LORD**, had died.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The title is given at the moment it ends. Joshua does not receive it here; he is called *Moses' assistant*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gets it at the end of the book: *Joshua the son of Nun, the servant of the LORD, died.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Joshua is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The man who had **stood at Moses' side for so long**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *Moses' minister* — **mesharet**, an attendant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had led the fight against Amalek, gone up the mountain with Moses, stayed in the tent of meeting when Moses left it, and been one of the two spies who said go. See the notes on Exodus 17:8–13 and Numbers 14:5–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years of second place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Moses my servant is gone. So now it's your turn**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *Moses My servant is dead; now therefore arise*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no interval. The book's first paragraph moves from a death to a river crossing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get up, you and all these people, and cross over this Jordan River**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The river is in flood — Joshua 3 notes it overflows its banks through the harvest season. See the note on Joshua 3:14–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Into the land **I am giving them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present participle. Not *will give*, and not *would have given*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same construction runs through the book: *every place your foot touches, **I am handing over to you***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Every place your foot touches, I am handing over to you**, just as I promised Moses.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Given, and walked on. The giving is God's and the walking is theirs, and both are in the sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua 24 makes the same point at the end: *I gave you a land you did not toil for.* See the note on Joshua 24:11–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The boundaries"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"From the wilderness in the south all the way to Lebanon, from the great Euphrates River across the land of the Hittites to the Great Sea in the west.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The full extent promised to Abraham in Genesis 15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise about opposition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No one will be able to stand against you as long as you live**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The way I was with Moses, that's how I will be with you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the sentence Joshua would have needed most. He had watched the sea part, the mountain smoke, and the water come out of a rock, and is now expected to lead the same nation with none of that behind him personally."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two negatives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will never fail you. I will never walk away from you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew doubles the assurance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews quotes this line and applies it to the reader: *He Himself has said, \"I will never leave you nor forsake you.\" So we may boldly say: \"The LORD is my helper; I will not fear.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times in the next four verses: *be strong and of good courage*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the one instruction about method: *this Book of the Law shall not depart from your mouth, but you shall meditate in it day and night.* See the note on Joshua 1:6–9."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the book starts.** Moses is dead in the first clause and the crossing is ordered in the second. The only argument given for the change is that the same God is going with the next man."}]}, "Joshua 1:6–9": {"title": "What \"meditate day and night\" meant to a soldier", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The famous line here is about turning God's words over in your mind day and night. It is worth knowing who was told to do it and what he was about to do, because it was not a quiet man in a quiet room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Hebrew word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hagah.* It is not silent reflection. Its ordinary uses are physical and audible — a lion growling over its prey, a dove moaning, a person muttering under their breath. The same word is in Psalm 1, and both times it is paired with *day and night*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is why the instruction here begins with the mouth: *keep the words of this book always on your lips*, and then turn them over. It is closer to chewing than to studying — going over something aloud until it works into you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua, on the day he takes over from Moses, facing a river and a walled city. The book is about a military campaign, and the first thing he is given is not a strategy. It is a way of handling words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times in four verses he is told to be strong and courageous, which is the tell: this is a man being steadied, and the muttering of the text is part of how he is steadied."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The promise attached.** *Then your path will open before you, and you will do well.* The Hebrew word behind it means to act with insight and so to succeed — it covers both the wisdom and the outcome. It is not a guarantee of ease; the book that follows has failures in it. It is the promise that a man soaked in what God said will know how to act."}]}, "Joshua 10:12–14": {"title": "\"A day when the LORD listened to the voice of a man\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Joshua gives an order to the solar system, out loud, in front of everybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five Amorite kings had attacked Gibeon — the city that had tricked Israel into a treaty a chapter earlier. Israel is defending people who had deceived them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua marched all night from Gilgal to get there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joshua spoke to the LORD, and **in the sight of all Israel he cried aloud**: \"**Sun, stand still** over Gibeon; and **Moon**, over the Valley of Aijalon.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He addresses the LORD, and then addresses the sun."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *stand still* is **dom** — be silent, be still, cease."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And note the setting: *in the sight of all Israel*, out loud. If nothing had happened, everybody would have watched nothing happen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is recorded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the sun stood still, and the moon stopped… **The sun halted in the middle of the sky and did not hurry to set for about a whole day**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The citation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Is this not written in **the Book of Jashar**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A source the writer expects his readers to have. It is quoted once more in Scripture, for David's lament over Saul and Jonathan, and it has not survived."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer of Joshua is pointing at an external record."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There has never been a day like it, before or since — **a day when the LORD listened to the voice of a man**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the writer's summary, and it is not about astronomy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say *a day when the sun stood still*. He says a day when God listened to somebody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the clause after it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the LORD Himself was fighting for Israel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves are in the same verse. God listened to a man, and God was doing the fighting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua's shout was not what won the battle — the chapter has already recorded hailstones killing more of the enemy than the sword did. What the long day did was finish it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was at stake"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A treaty with people who had lied to them. Israel could have let Gibeon fall and been rid of an embarrassment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua marched all night instead, because he had given his word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same idea turns up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Elijah was a man with a nature like ours, and **he prayed earnestly** that it would not rain, and it did not rain on the land for three years and six months.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James makes the point deliberately — the man was ordinary, and the sky obeyed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other thing that stood still.** *Jesus **stood still**, and commanded him to be called.* Mark 10, on the road out of Jericho, for a blind beggar shouting. The same word in Greek that the Old Testament used of the sun. See the note on Mark 10:46–52."}]}, "Joshua 10:22–27": {"title": "Feet on the necks of kings", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Five kings are brought out of a cave, and what Joshua does with them is a lesson for the men watching."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five Amorite kings had allied against Gibeon, which had made peace with Israel. Joshua marched all night from Gilgal, and the LORD *cast down large hailstones from heaven on them*, and the sun stood still."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The five kings hid in a cave at Makkedah, and Joshua had great stones rolled over the mouth of it and men set to guard it while the army finished the pursuit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The kings of **Jerusalem, Hebron, Jarmuth, Lachish, and Eglon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The head of the alliance is Adoni-Zedek of Jerusalem — a name meaning *lord of righteousness*, and a variant of Melchizedek, *king of righteousness*, who had blessed Abram in the same city. See the note on Genesis 14:17–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is called forward"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joshua **called the men of Israel together** and told **the commanders who had gone with him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole nation watches; the commanders act."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Come, **put your feet on the necks of these kings**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *place your feet upon the necks*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was a standard ancient gesture of conquest, carved on reliefs across the Near East."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not asked of them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They did not capture these kings. The hailstones did more damage than the sword — Joshua 10 says so outright: *there were more who died from the hailstones than the children of Israel killed with the sword.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The commanders are put on the necks of men they did not defeat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Don't be afraid or discouraged. Be strong and courageous**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same four given to Joshua himself at the start of the book, three times in the first chapter, and given to Joshua by Moses before that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He passes on what he was told."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This is what the LORD will do to every enemy you fight**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *this is what you will do*. The LORD is the subject."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gesture is a demonstration, and what it demonstrates is stated in the next sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The execution"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Joshua **struck the five kings down** and put them to death, and **hung their bodies on five trees until evening**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At sunset he ordered them **taken down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy required it: *his body shall not remain overnight on the tree… for he who is hanged is accursed of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same law the Jewish leaders invoked at the crucifixion. See the note on John 19:31–34."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they ended up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **thrown into the very cave where they had hidden**. Great stones were piled at the entrance — and they are there to this day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hiding place becomes the tomb, sealed with the same stones that had trapped them alive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the picture goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sit here at My right hand **until I make your enemies a footstool under your feet**.* See the note on Psalms 110:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Paul: *the God of peace will crush Satan **under your feet** shortly.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the commanders did.** Walked forward and stood on five necks, after a battle decided by hail and a long day. Joshua tells them what it means before they step off."}]}, "Joshua 11:21–23": {"title": "\"The land had rest from war\" — and the three cities left standing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The summary at the end of the campaign, and it contains a detail the writer did not have to include."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joshua took the entire land, exactly as the LORD had promised Moses… And the land had rest from war.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is as complete as the book gets. The giants are cut off from the hill country — Hebron, Debir, Anab, the highlands — and *not one of the Anakim was left in the land of Israel*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception, stated in the same breath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Only in Gaza, Gath, and Ashdod did any remain.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three Philistine cities on the coastal plain. The writer records the total victory and the surviving pocket in consecutive sentences, without appearing to notice a problem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It matters later. Gath is where Goliath comes from, four centuries on — a descendant of the people this chapter says were cleared out, living in one of the three cities this chapter says they were not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind of claim it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Bible's histories are willing to say *the whole land* and then name what was left, and to do it on the same page. The summary is describing the campaign's outcome — the field armies broken, the hill country taken — not a census."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters later God says outright: *there is still so much land left to possess*. The book contains its own qualification."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The phrase that closes it.** *The land had rest from war* appears twice in Joshua, here and at the end of Caleb's chapter. It is the note the conquest narrative keeps landing on — not conquest as the goal, but rest as the point of it."}]}, "Joshua 13:1–7": {"title": "\"You've grown old, and there is still so much land left\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the bluntest sentences God says to anyone in the Old Testament, and He says it to the man who had just finished the conquest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You've grown old, and there is still so much land left to possess.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are true and neither cancels the other. Two chapters earlier the book said Joshua *took the entire land, exactly as the LORD had promised* — and it meant it, at the level of the campaign. Now God lists what is still out there by name: the Philistine cities, the Geshurites, the Sidonians, Lebanon up to Hermon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The victory was real. The occupation was not finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not rebuke Joshua. There is no suggestion the old man failed, and no call for one more campaign. The instruction that follows is administrative: *divide this land among the nine and a half tribes as their inheritance*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua's job changes. He is to allocate what has been promised, and the taking of it will belong to the tribes and to the years after him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does the driving out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I myself will drive them out before my people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is stated in the middle of the list of what remains. The unfinished part is not handed over as a burden — it is handed over with the promise that the work is God's, and the tribes' part is to go and live in what is theirs."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The tension the book leaves standing.** Joshua says the land was taken; Judges opens with a long account of what was not. Both are in the canon and neither is edited out. A promise can be entirely given and still not entirely entered, and this chapter is where the Bible says so out loud."}]}, "Joshua 14:6–15": {"title": "Caleb at eighty-five, and the hill he asked for", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Forty-five years after the report that cost his generation the land, Caleb walks up and asks for the hardest piece of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic he does out loud"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["forty years old when he was sent to scout", "forty-five years of waiting since", "eighty-five now, *as strong now as the day Moses sent me out*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He counts it publicly. He is not claiming exemption from time; he is saying the promise held through it, and so did he."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a quiet valley. **Hebron** — and he names the problem himself: *You heard yourself that the Anakim are there, giants in great fortified cities.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The giants are the exact reason ten of the twelve scouts had said no, all those years earlier. Caleb asks for the place his generation refused, by name, and tells Joshua the giants are still in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase the chapter keeps repeating"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He followed the LORD my God completely* — with a whole heart. It appears three times in these verses, twice from Caleb and once from the narrator, and it is what Moses' oath was hung on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew behind *completely* means to fill up, to make full. Not flawlessly. Fully — nothing held back."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *And the land had rest from war.* The chapter about an eighty-five-year-old asking for a fight ends on rest. Both things are in the same paragraph, and the order matters: the rest comes after the hill country is taken, not instead of it."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Caleb was asking against"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read this chapter beside the two around it and the request gets sharper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua 11 says the Anakim were cut off from the hill country and *not one was left in the land of Israel* — with three coastal cities named as the exception. Joshua 13 has God telling Joshua, now old, that *there is still so much land left to possess*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Caleb is standing in that gap. The campaign is over, the summary has been written, and there is still a fortified hill country full of giants that nobody has moved into. He does not ask for something new. He asks for the part of the promise that was made to him personally and has been sitting unoccupied for forty-five years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what he did with it after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 15 records the follow-through in one line: *from there Caleb drove out the three sons of Anak* — named, individually — and then went on against Debir."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The asking and the taking are two different chapters, and the second one is short. See the notes on Joshua 11:21–23, Joshua 13:1–7, and Joshua 15:13–19, where his daughter asks him for springs in exactly the same spirit."}]}, "Joshua 15:13–19": {"title": "Achsah, who asked for the springs", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A short domestic scene dropped into the middle of a long list of boundary lines, and it is there because of what a young woman says."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she had been given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Land in the Negev — the dry south. It is a real inheritance and it is genuinely hers, and without water it is close to useless."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter has just described her father taking Hebron off three giants, so nobody in this family is short of nerve. Achsah is her father's daughter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give me a blessing. You've given me land in the dry Negev — now give me springs of water too.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She names the gap precisely. Not more land, and not a complaint about what she received. The specific thing that would make what she already has work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the detail the narrator keeps: *as she climbed down from her donkey*. She dismounts to ask — the posture of someone making a formal request, and the sort of small physical detail the Bible records when someone was actually there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she gets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Caleb gave her the upper springs and the lower springs.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both. She asked for springs and he gave her the whole watershed, top and bottom. No hesitation is recorded, and no negotiation."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the story is in the book at all.** Judges tells it a second time, almost word for word — which means it was remembered and repeated. In a chapter that is otherwise surveyors' work, someone thought this was worth keeping: a woman who looked at a good gift, saw what it lacked, and asked her father for the rest."}]}, "Joshua 2:12–14": {"title": "\"Kindness and truth\" — the two words she is given", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Rahab asks for a specific thing, in specific words, and the answer comes back in the same vocabulary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have shown you kindness — promise me you will show kindness to my family.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word is **hesed**: the loyal, covenanted, will-not-let-go kind of love that is Israel's central word for how God treats His own. She uses it of what she did, and asks for it back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *give me a sure sign*. She is not content with a promise; she asks for something she can point to. That is what the scarlet cord becomes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We will treat you with kindness and truth* — **hesed and emet**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That pairing runs right through the Old Testament as a description of God's own character: loyal love and dependability, the two together. It is what Moses hears when God passes by him on the mountain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two spies, in a compromised house, in a doomed city, hand a Canaanite prostitute the pair of words that describe what God is like."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the pair turns up again.** John writes that the Word became flesh, *full of grace and truth* — and that grace and truth *came through Jesus Christ*. Greek this time, but the same pairing, and John's readers would have heard the Hebrew behind it. What Rahab was promised on a rooftop is what John says arrived in a person. See the note on John 1:14–18."}]}, "Joshua 2:15–21": {"title": "The scarlet cord, and the terms she agreed to", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A woman in a doomed city is given a set of conditions, and the conditions are worth reading closely, because every one of them turns up again in how the Bible talks about rescue."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three terms"}, {"k": "p", "t": "1. **the cord in the window** — the same rope she let them down by, tied where it can be seen 2. **everyone must be inside her house** — *if anyone steps outside your doors into the street, their blood is on their own head* 3. **say nothing about the arrangement**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The safety is not spread across the city or attached to her personally wherever she goes. It is located: this house, this mark, everyone in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where you have read this before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A household. A doorway. A red mark on the outside. Everyone who stays in is safe, everyone who goes out is not — and the destruction passes through the city regardless."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus 12 is the same shape, and Rahab's night is the only other place in scripture where the pattern appears whole. Whether she knew that is not the point; the text is built so that the reader does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Rahab's own words, three verses earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I know that the LORD has given you this land.* Everyone in Jericho had heard the same reports about the Red Sea and the two Amorite kings, and the whole city was melting with fear. She is not better informed than her neighbours."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is the only one who acts on it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What she was.** The text never softens her trade, and neither do the two New Testament writers who name her — Hebrews lists her among the faithful, James as an example of faith that did something. Matthew puts her in the genealogy of Jesus. Nobody in scripture is embarrassed about Rahab, which is itself worth noticing."}]}, "Joshua 2:8–11": {"title": "What the whole city knew, and what one person did about it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Rahab's speech is the most theologically loaded thing anyone says in the book of Joshua, and it comes from a Canaanite prostitute on a rooftop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "She names the evidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We've heard how the LORD dried up the waters of the Red Sea… We've heard what you did to Sihon and Og.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Red Sea was forty years earlier. The two Amorite kings were recent. She has been following the news for a long time, and she has drawn a conclusion from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the city knew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Terror of you has fallen on us. Everyone living here is melting with fear… there was no courage left in anyone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the crucial detail. Rahab is not the only person in Jericho with the information. The whole city has it, and the whole city is terrified."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She differs from her neighbours in exactly one respect: she acts. The rest of Jericho locks the gates."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line she ends on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the LORD your God — He is God, in the heavens above and on the earth below.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is not \"your God is strong.\" It is a statement about which God is God, in both realms, and it is close to the wording Moses uses in Deuteronomy. A woman outside the covenant, in a condemned city, states the central claim of Israel's faith more plainly than most Israelites in the previous forty years managed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Two verses on.** *I know that the LORD has given you this land* — she says *has given*, past tense, before a single stone of the wall has moved. Then she asks for her family. She is not gambling on an outcome; she is negotiating on the far side of one she has already accepted."}]}, "Joshua 24:11–13": {"title": "A land you did not toil for", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Joshua is old, the conquest is behind them, and he stands the nation at Shechem and tells them what happened — in the first person, as God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole recital is *I brought, I gave, I sent, I destroyed*. Joshua is quoting God speaking about the campaign Joshua himself led."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The general's own account of the war gives the credit to somebody else, all the way through."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list of enemies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people of Jericho fought against you — as did the **Amorites, Perizzites, Canaanites, Hittites, Girgashites, Hivites, and Jebusites** — but **I gave them all into your hands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven nations named, and one verb for the outcome. Not *you defeated them*. **I gave them.**"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hornet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I sent **the hornet** ahead of you, and it drove out the two kings of the Amorites before you — **not by your sword or your bow**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tsirah**, and it was promised back in Exodus: *I will send hornets before you, which shall drive out the Hivite, the Canaanite and the Hittite.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever it was, the point of the clause is the last six words. Their weapons were carried, drawn and used — and the verse says the ground was not taken by them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things given"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**A land you did not toil for**", "**Cities you did not build but now live in**", "**Vineyards and olive groves you did not plant but now eat from**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "An olive tree takes fifteen years to bear properly and a vineyard four. They walked into groves planted by men who never harvested them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Deuteronomy had said in advance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Great and good cities which you did not build, houses full of all good things which you did not fill, hewn-out wells which you did not dig, vineyards and olive trees which you did not plant — when you have eaten and are full, **then beware, lest you forget the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The danger named is not poverty. It is amnesia."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Joshua is saying it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The recital ends with the demand: *choose for yourselves this day whom you will serve… **but as for me and my house, we will serve the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list of what was given is the argument. He sets out everything they received without earning, and then asks for a decision."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they still had to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They crossed, they marched, they fought at Jericho and Ai and the Valley of Aijalon. Joshua does not pretend otherwise — the fighting is in the verse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts the labour and the result in the same sentence and attributes the result to God."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they had done.** Nothing in the list. They received a settled country with the wells already dug — and Joshua's whole point in reciting it is that a people living off what they did not build are living off a gift."}]}, "Joshua 24:14–15": {"title": "As for me and my house", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last speech of Joshua's life, and it ends with a choice put to a nation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Shechem. Joshua has gathered all the tribes, elders, heads, judges and officers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is *old, advanced in age*, and the chapter closes with his death at a hundred and ten."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole speech before this is God speaking in the first person, retelling the history from Terah onward: *I took your father Abraham… I gave him Isaac… I sent Moses… I brought you out… I gave you a land for which you did not labour, and cities which you did not build.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So now, **hold the LORD in awe and serve Him with all your heart**, in **complete sincerity and truth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tamim ve'emet** — in wholeness and in truth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has to go"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Throw away the gods your ancestors served beyond the River and in Egypt**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two locations, and both are historical."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Beyond the River* is Mesopotamia — Terah's household, before Abraham left. Joshua has already said it: *your fathers dwelt on the other side of the River in old times, and they served other gods*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In Egypt* is four hundred years of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have been in the land for a generation and are still carrying idols from two countries they left."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rachel had stolen her father's household gods on the way out of Haran, and Jacob had buried foreign gods under an oak — at Shechem, where this assembly is standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The alternative"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **if serving the LORD seems undesirable to you, then choose this very day whom you will serve**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *if it is evil in your eyes*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not soften it. If you do not want this, pick something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two options"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whether the gods your fathers served **beyond the River**, or the gods of the Amorites **in whose land you now live**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Behind them, or around them. The past or the neighbours."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Those are the two live idolatries, and he names both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The declaration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **as for me and my house, we will serve the LORD**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **anochi uveiti**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not wait for the vote. He announces where he stands and lets the assembly answer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse is on doorposts and kitchen walls all over the world, usually detached from the sentence before it, which is *choose today whom you will serve*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the people say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Far be it from us that we should forsake the LORD** to serve other gods!\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Joshua says to that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You cannot serve the LORD, for He is a holy God**. He is a jealous God; He will not forgive your transgressions nor your sins.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He argues against their profession. He is trying to make them count the cost before they promise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the people said to Joshua, \"No, but we will serve the LORD!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Joshua said, \"**You are witnesses against yourselves**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Joshua took a large stone, and set it up there under the oak that was by the sanctuary of the LORD. And Joshua said to all the people, \"**Behold, this stone shall be a witness to us, for it has heard all the words of the LORD which He spoke to us**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A stone that has heard something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens after he dies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Israel served the LORD all the days of Joshua, and all the days of the elders who outlived Joshua.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then Judges: *another generation arose after them who did not know the LORD nor the work which He had done for Israel*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is offered.** Not a recommendation. He tells them to choose, names the two realistic alternatives, and then states his own household's position without waiting for theirs."}]}, "Joshua 3:1–4": {"title": "You have never come this way before", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Marching orders, and the reason given for them is a gap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they start"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Early the next morning Joshua and all the Israelites **broke camp at Shittim**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Shittim is where the Balaam story ends badly — *the people began to commit harlotry with the women of Moab*, and twenty-four thousand died in the plague."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last camp before the land is the site of the last disaster."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The waiting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And came to the edge of the Jordan, and there they camped **before crossing**. **After three days**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days of looking at a river they have no way over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter later notes the season: *the Jordan overflows all its banks during the whole time of harvest*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When you see **the ark of the covenant** of the LORD your God being carried by the Levitical priests, then **leave your places and follow it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction is not a route. It is an object to follow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years earlier the same people had followed a pillar of cloud and fire. Here the sign is a gold box carried on poles by men on foot."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That way **you'll know which road to take, for you have never come this way before**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *you have not passed this way before, yesterday or the day before*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument is not that the ark knows the way and they do not. It is that nobody in the column has been here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **keep your distance — about three thousand feet** between you and the ark. **Don't come near it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is two thousand cubits."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a long way — most of a mile. The people at the back of the column can see the ark and nothing else about the crossing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the gap"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two reasons work at once."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two thousand cubits is the traditional Sabbath day's journey — the distance a man could travel on the seventh day, measured from the edge of the camp."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at that range, the ark is visible to everybody. A box carried by four men in the middle of two million people would be invisible at close quarters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction that keeps them back is the instruction that lets them all see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the ark contained"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tablets of the law, Aaron's rod that budded, and a pot of manna — under a gold lid with two cherubim facing each other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews lists them. The lid is the mercy seat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Sanctify yourselves, for tomorrow the LORD will do wonders among you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the priests' feet touch the water and the river stops upstream at a town called Adam. See the note on Joshua 3:14–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ark going first elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the ark of the covenant of the LORD **went before them** for three days' journey, to search out a resting place for them.* See the note on Numbers 10:33–36."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same arrangement, at the start of the wilderness years and at the end of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one time it goes wrong"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Years later, at Perez Uzzah, the ark is put on a new cart and a man steadies it when the oxen stumble and dies on the spot."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Poles, carried by Levites, was always the instruction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the crossing was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That all the peoples of the earth may know the hand of the LORD, that it is mighty, that you may fear the LORD your God forever.\"* See the note on Joshua 4:19–24."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The reason for the gap.** Not distance for its own sake — a mile of open ground, so that everybody in a column of two million could see one gold box and know where the road went."}]}, "Joshua 3:14–17": {"title": "The waters stand in a heap", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The crossing into the land, and the detail that matters is when the water stopped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of march"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **the priests carrying the ark of the covenant walking ahead of them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the sea, forty years earlier, Moses stretched out a rod and the water divided before anyone moved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here the ark goes first and nothing happens until it arrives at the edge."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The season"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **the Jordan overflows its banks all through harvest season**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Spring, at the barley harvest, with snowmelt off Hermon coming down. The river is at its widest and fastest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator puts the clause in so nobody reads it as a summer crossing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the moment the priests who carried the ark reached the river and their feet touched the water's edge**, the water flowing down from upstream **stood still**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The water stopped when the feet went in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been told in advance: *when the soles of the feet of the priests rest in the waters of the Jordan, the waters shall be cut off.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing was visible until somebody stepped into a flooded river."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it stopped"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It rose up in a heap, **far upstream, near a town called Adam, beside Zarethan**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew names the place. Adam is about sixteen miles upstream."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stopping happened out of sight of the people crossing. What they saw was a dry bed; the event itself was a day's walk north."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Hebrew for \"heap\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ned** — a heap, a mound. It is the same word used of the sea in the Song of Moses: *the waters were gathered together, the floods stood upright **like a heap**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the rest went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the water flowing down toward **the Salt Sea, the sea of the plain, was completely cut off**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything downstream drained away and left the bed dry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they crossed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the people crossed over, **right in front of Jericho**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In full view of the city they were about to besiege."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rahab had already told the spies what the news was doing: *we have heard how the LORD dried up the water of the Red Sea for you… and as soon as we heard these things, our hearts melted.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The priests"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the priests who carried the ark **stood firm on dry ground in the middle of the Jordan**, and **all Israel crossed** on dry ground, **until the whole nation had finished passing over**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *stood firm* is **kun** — to be fixed, established, made ready. It is the word for something set in place and holding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They went in first and came out last. The whole nation walked past them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stones"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve men, one from each tribe, carried twelve stones out of the riverbed where the priests' feet had stood, and Joshua set them up at Gilgal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And twelve more were set in the river itself, where the priests stood — *and they are there to this day*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That this may be a sign among you when your children ask in time to come, \"**What do these stones mean to you?**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened when they came up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it came to pass, when the priests who bore the ark came from the midst of the Jordan and the soles of their feet touched the dry land, that **the waters of the Jordan returned to their place and overflowed all its banks as before**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The date"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people came up from the Jordan **on the tenth day of the first month**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nisan 10 — the day the Passover lamb was selected and taken into the house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years earlier, on that day in Egypt, they were choosing lambs."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What had to happen first.** The feet. The river was in flood and the promise was that it would stop when the soles of the priests' feet rested in it — so the first men into the water went in while it was still running."}]}, "Joshua 3:9–13": {"title": "This is how you will know", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Joshua tells them what is about to happen, and why it is being done this way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The summons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Joshua called to the people, \"**Come here. Listen to the words of the LORD your God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **geshu henah** — draw near here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has been in command for three days. See the note on Joshua 3:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This is how you'll know that the living God is right here among you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **El chai** — the living God. The phrase appears rarely, and always where the question is whether God is actually present and active."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crossing is not primarily transport. It is evidence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seven"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And that He will surely drive out before you **the Canaanites, the Hittites, the Hivites, the Perizzites, the Girgashites, the Amorites, and the Jebusites**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven nations, named individually."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses had told them: *seven nations greater and mightier than you*. Joshua names each one in front of a river they cannot cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land is occupied, and the list is not shortened for morale."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ark"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look — **the ark of the covenant of the LORD of all the earth is going ahead of you into the Jordan**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Adon kol-ha'aretz** — Lord of all the earth. The title is used twice in this short passage, and it is the point: the God of Israel is the owner of the land they are entering, not a rival deity crossing a border."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the ark goes first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because they have not been this way before."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order given three days earlier: *when you see the ark… then you shall set out from your place and go after it*, with two thousand cubits between."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people follow something they can see, at a distance, and do not lead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The twelve"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So now **choose twelve men from the tribes of Israel, one from each tribe**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not told yet what for. Chapter 4 explains: each man lifts a stone from the riverbed and carries it out, and the twelve stones are set up at Gilgal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When your children ask in time to come, \"What do these stones mean?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The memorial is arranged before the miracle happens."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And this will happen: **the moment the priests carrying the ark set their feet in the waters of the Jordan, the river will be cut off**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kenoach kapot raglei** — as the soles of the feet rest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not before. The water goes when the feet are wet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the river was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Jordan overflows all its banks during the whole time of harvest.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Flood stage. The narrator says so twice, in case anyone thinks they picked an easy season."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heap"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The water flowing down from upstream will **stand still, piling up in a heap**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ned echad** — one heap."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word in the Song of the Sea: *the waters were gathered together, the floods stood upright like a heap*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years apart, the same noun. The generation that saw the Red Sea has died in the wilderness, and this generation gets the same word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The waters which came down from upstream stood still and rose in a heap very far away at Adam, the city that is beside Zaretan.* See the note on Joshua 3:14–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the priests stood on dry ground in the middle of the Jordan until all the people had crossed over."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order.** *The moment the priests set their feet in the waters, the river will be cut off.* Wet feet first, dry riverbed second — and the memorial stones were commissioned before either."}]}, "Joshua 4:19–24": {"title": "The tenth day of the first month", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The date is given, and it is not an incidental detail."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The date"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people came up out of the Jordan on the **tenth day of the first month**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tenth of Nisan."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus 12: *on the tenth day of this month every man shall take for himself a lamb… and you shall keep it until the fourteenth day of the same month*. See the note on Exodus 12:1–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Selection day. The day the Passover lamb was taken into the house and looked at for four days before it was killed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years to the week since the first Passover, and they cross on the day the lamb is chosen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens four days later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now the children of Israel camped in Gilgal, and kept the Passover on the fourteenth day of the month at twilight on the plains of Jericho.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first Passover in the land, on schedule, in enemy territory."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they camp"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they camped at **Gilgal, on the eastern edge of Jericho**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gilgal becomes the base for the whole conquest. They come back to it after every campaign."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name is explained a few verses later: *this day I have **rolled away** the reproach of Egypt from you* — **galal**, to roll."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stones"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And those twelve stones they had carried out of the Jordan, **Joshua set them up at Gilgal**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pile that came out of the river ends up here. See the note on Joshua 4:8–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In days to come, when **your children ask their fathers, 'What are these stones?'**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second time in one chapter. The stones exist for a conversation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What to say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You'll teach them: **Israel crossed this Jordan on dry ground**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yabbashah** — dry land. The same word as Genesis 1, when the dry land appears out of the water."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For the LORD your God **dried up the waters of the Jordan** before you until you had crossed, **just as the LORD your God had done to the Red Sea**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two crossings are set side by side. One took the previous generation out; this one brings this generation in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the pronouns shift mid-sentence — *before **you**… before **us***. The speaker includes himself in the older event."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua was there. He is one of two men alive who saw both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two purposes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He did this **so that all the peoples of the earth would know the hand of the LORD is mighty**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And so that **you would stand in awe of the LORD your God, always**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An external audience and an internal one, in that order."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rahab had already reported the first working: *we have heard how the LORD dried up the water of the Red Sea for you… and as soon as we heard these things, our hearts melted*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the crossing cost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is said about difficulty. The account has no drama in it — the priests' feet touch the water, the river stops, everyone walks across."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's energy goes into the stones and the explanation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the tenth of Nisan becomes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Centuries later, a Sunday. *And when He came near the descent of the Mount of Olives, the whole multitude of the disciples began to rejoice and praise God.* See the note on Luke 19:36–40."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lamb comes into the city on selection day, and is inspected for four days by the authorities, who can find no fault in Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pilate says it three times: *I find no fault in this Man*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The date it happened on.** The tenth of the first month — the day Exodus sets aside for taking the lamb into the house and looking at it."}]}, "Joshua 4:4–7": {"title": "When your children ask", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The river is still standing in a heap and Joshua stops to organise a monument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priests are in the riverbed holding the ark, and the water is piled up somewhere upstream."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is when this conversation happens."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The twelve"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joshua called **the twelve men he had appointed, one from each tribe**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chosen in advance. Chapter 3 records the instruction before the crossing began."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go out into the Jordan, ahead of the ark** of the LORD your God, and each of you **lift a stone onto your shoulder** — one for each of the tribes.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Out into the middle, past the ark, and back with a stone big enough to need a shoulder."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve men walk deeper into a dry riverbed while the water waits."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This will be a sign among you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **oth** — a sign, a marker. The word used of the rainbow and of circumcision."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And someday **your children will ask, 'What do these stones mean to you?'**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole arrangement is built around a question that has not been asked yet, by people who have not been born."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer to give"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then you can tell them: **the waters of the Jordan were cut off before the ark** of the covenant of the LORD.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Short, factual, and it names what stopped the water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *we crossed a river*. The water was cut off, and it was cut off in front of the ark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repetition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When the ark crossed the Jordan, **the waters were cut off**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice in two sentences. The children's answer has the same clause in it both times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The duration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"These stones are a **reminder to the people of Israel, forever**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *for a memorial to the children of Israel forever* — **ad olam**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern in the law"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same construction runs through Exodus and Deuteronomy, always about a rite and always anticipating a child:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it shall be, when your son asks you in time to come, saying, \"What is this?\" that you shall say to him, \"By strength of hand the LORD brought us out of Egypt.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When your son asks you in time to come, saying, \"What is the meaning of the testimonies?\" then you shall say, \"We were slaves of Pharaoh in Egypt, and the LORD brought us out.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Passover, the firstborn, the statutes — each one comes with the child's question written into it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second set of stones"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve more, set up *in the middle of the Jordan, on the spot where the priests carrying the ark had stood*. See the note on Joshua 4:8–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One pile comes out of the river. One stays in it, under the water once it comes back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the first set ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Gilgal, on the eastern edge of Jericho, on the tenth day of the first month. See the note on Joshua 4:19–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one that failed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judges opens a generation later: *another generation arose after them **who did not know the LORD nor the work which He had done for Israel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stones were set up. Somebody stopped telling."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the monument was built around.** A question nobody had asked yet. Twelve men carry stones out of a riverbed with the water still standing, for children who did not exist."}]}, "Joshua 4:8–9": {"title": "The stones nobody sees", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two piles of stones, and only one of them is visible afterwards."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The obedience"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the Israelites **did just as Joshua commanded**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew adds *as the LORD had told Joshua*. The chain is stated: God to Joshua, Joshua to the twelve, and the twelve did it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first set"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They took up **twelve stones out of the middle of the Jordan**, one for each tribe… and **carried them to the campsite and set them down there**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Out of the river, onto dry land, where people camp and children ask questions. See the note on Joshua 4:4–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second set"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **Joshua also set up twelve stones in the middle of the Jordan**, on the spot **where the priests carrying the ark had stood**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody told him to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction in the chapter is about stones coming out. This pile is Joshua's own addition, and he puts it in the riverbed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where exactly"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the spot where the priests carrying the ark had stood.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The precise square yards of ground where the feet of the men holding the ark had been while two million people walked past."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter ends with the priests coming up out of the river: *and it came to pass, when the priests who bore the ark of the covenant of the LORD had come from the midst of the Jordan… that the waters of the Jordan returned to their place and overflowed all its banks as before*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second pile is under water within the hour, and stays under."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **they are there to this day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written by somebody who could not see them and knew where they were."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew phrase **ad hayyom hazzeh** runs all through Joshua — of Rahab's family living in Israel, of the heap of stones over Achan, of the cave at Makkedah."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two monuments"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One at Gilgal, above ground, for the questions."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One in the Jordan, below the water, for nothing anyone can point at."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are twelve stones and both mark the same event. The visible one says *this happened*; the drowned one marks the place it happened from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the priests were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They stood in the middle of the river while everyone crossed, and did not move until the last person was over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 3: *and the priests who bore the ark of the covenant of the LORD **stood firm on dry ground in the midst of the Jordan**, and all Israel crossed over on dry ground, until all the people had crossed completely over*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stones mark where they stood, and the water came back over the mark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other buried stone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob at Bethel took the stone he had slept on, set it up as a pillar, poured oil on it and named the place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at Gilgal, the next thing that happens is circumcision — *and the LORD said to Joshua, \"This day I have rolled away the reproach of Egypt from you\"*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is on both banks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Behind them, a pile under a river. In front of them, Jericho."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing after the memorial is the Passover, kept in the plains of Jericho on the fourteenth day. And the manna stops the day after they eat the produce of the land."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The pile that cannot be visited.** Joshua set it up on the ground the priests' feet had been on, and the river closed over it — and the account says *they are there to this day*."}]}, "Joshua 5:10–12": {"title": "The day the manna stopped", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses, and one of them ends forty years of supply."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Israelites camped at **Gilgal**, and there **on the plains of Jericho**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Across the Jordan, in the land, with a walled city in sight and no campaign started yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Circumcision. The whole generation born in the wilderness had not been circumcised, and Joshua does it at Gilgal — which puts an entire army out of action for days, in enemy territory."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD said to Joshua, \"**This day I have rolled away the reproach of Egypt from you**.\" Therefore the name of the place is called Gilgal.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **galal**, to roll."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The date"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the evening of the **fourteenth day of the month**, they kept the Passover.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had crossed on the tenth — lamb selection day — and kept the feast on the fourteenth, on schedule. See the note on Joshua 4:19–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first Passover in the land, in the plains of Jericho, within sight of the city they are about to attack."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The meal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The day after the Passover, they ate **the produce of the land — unleavened bread and roasted grain** — on that very day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **avur ha'arets** — the produce of the land."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grain that grew there. Nobody had planted it and nobody had harvested it before them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cessation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the manna stopped the day after they ate from the land**. There was **no more manna** for the people of Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years, six days a week, with a double portion on the sixth. See the note on Exodus 16:22–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then one morning there is none, and it is not a failure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the two events"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The new food comes first. They eat the produce, and the next morning the manna does not arrive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is withdrawn before the replacement is in hand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason it stopped"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not disobedience. Manna was wilderness food, for people with no fields."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the children of Israel ate manna forty years, **until they came to a land inhabited**; they ate manna until they came to the border of the land of Canaan.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus stated the end condition when the manna began."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What replaced it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That year they **ate the fruit of the land of Canaan**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Work, harvest, seasons. A different arrangement, and a harder one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses had warned about exactly this transition: *when you have eaten and are full, then you shall bless the LORD your God for the good land which He has given you. **Beware that you do not forget the LORD***. See the note on Deuteronomy 6:10–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The manna made forgetting difficult. Fields make it easy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua looks up and sees a man with a drawn sword."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Are You for us or for our adversaries?\" So He said, \"**No, but as Commander of the army of the LORD I have now come**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"Take your sandal off your foot, for the place where you stand is holy.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same instruction as the bush. See the note on Exodus 3:4–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The jar that was kept"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Fill an omer with it, to be kept for your generations.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A jar of manna, inside the ark, after the supply stopped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your fathers ate the manna in the wilderness, and are dead. This is the bread which comes down from heaven, that one may eat of it and not die. **I am the living bread**.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order.** They ate the new food, and the old supply stopped the next morning. Nothing was taken away before the replacement was already on the table."}]}, "Joshua 6:15–19": {"title": "The one house left standing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The city falls, and the exception is named out loud in the middle of the instruction to destroy it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the exception is placed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The city and everything in it are set apart to the LORD for destruction. Only Rahab and everyone in her house will live.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an afterthought once the dust settled. Joshua announces it as part of the command, before the shout, before the wall goes down. The rescue was arranged in advance and spoken publicly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her house was in the wall"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 2 mentions it almost in passing: *her house was built into the city wall, so that she lived within the wall itself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means when the wall came down, the one structure that had to stand was the one built into the thing that fell. The text does not comment on it. It simply records the wall collapsing and Rahab surviving, and leaves the reader to put the two facts side by side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because she hid the messengers we sent.* Not because she was Israelite — she was not. Not because she had reformed — the narrative never says so, and both New Testament writers who mention her still call her by her trade."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had made a covenant with the people of God, marked her house, and gathered her family inside it. That is the whole of her qualification."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What follows immediately.** The next paragraph is Achan taking what was devoted to destruction, and the trouble it brings on the whole camp. The chapter puts them together on purpose: an outsider who is spared because she was inside the terms, and an insider who is ruined because he ignored them."}]}, "Joshua 6:22–25": {"title": "The scarlet cord kept", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The city is burning, and two young men are sent to collect a family."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Joshua said to the two men who had spied out the land, \"**Go into the harlot's house and bring her out, and all who belong to her, just as you promised her**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two men. They made the promise, so they carry it out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua names her by her trade, and does not soften it. So does the New Testament — *by faith **the harlot Rahab** did not perish*, and *was not Rahab the harlot also justified by works?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise being kept"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Unless, when we come into the land, you bind **this line of scarlet cord** in the window through which you let us down… and unless you bring your father and mother, your brothers, and all your father's household to your own home.\"* See the note on Joshua 2:15–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for the cord is **tiqvat chut hashani** — and **tiqvah** is also the ordinary Hebrew word for *hope*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who came out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Her **father and mother, her brothers, and everyone she had — all her family**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had persuaded them. That is the part the account passes over: a woman with her reputation got her entire extended family to sit in her house while an army walked round the city for seven days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they were put"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And set them **in a place outside the camp of Israel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Outside, at first. They are Canaanites, and there is a process."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The city"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then they **burned the city with fire, and everything in it**, keeping only the silver and gold and the vessels of bronze and iron for the treasury of the house of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything destroyed except metal, which went to the treasury — and the man who took some of it anyway is the subject of the next chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **Rahab and all her household Joshua kept alive, and she has lived among the people of Israel to this day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew phrase **ad hayyom hazzeh** — the writer knows descendants."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Outside the camp in verse 23, and among the people by verse 25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **she hid the messengers** Joshua sent to spy out Jericho.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the text names. Not her faith, not her confession — the hiding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Though the confession is on record: *the LORD your God, He is God in heaven above and on earth beneath*. See the note on Joshua 2:8–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By faith the harlot Rahab did not perish with those who did not believe, **when she had received the spies with peace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is one of two women named in the faith chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What James says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Likewise, was not Rahab the harlot also justified by works when she received the messengers and sent them out another way?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two New Testament writers use her, on opposite sides of the faith-and-works argument, about the same incident."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where she ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Salmon begot **Boaz by Rahab**, Boaz begot Obed by Ruth, Obed begot Jesse, and Jesse begot David the king.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's genealogy, first chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She marries into Judah, and her great-grandson is David."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four women are named in that genealogy before Mary: Tamar, Rahab, Ruth, and *her who had been the wife of Uriah*. Every one of them has something irregular attached to her name, and Matthew includes all four."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was in the window"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A red cord, tied where the spies went down, visible from the street, for seven days while an army circled."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the text gives as the reason.** Not her faith, which is elsewhere. *Because she hid the messengers* — and the word for the cord she hung out is also the Hebrew word for hope."}]}, "Joshua 6:26–27": {"title": "A curse on Jericho", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The city is down, and the last thing Joshua does is make sure it stays down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven days of circling, seven priests with seven trumpets, and on the seventh day seven circuits and a shout."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the people shouted when the priests blew the trumpets… **and the wall fell down flat**.* See the note on Joshua 6:15–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The city was **cherem** — devoted, put under the ban. Everything in it belonged to God and nothing was to be taken."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In that hour Joshua made **a solemn vow before the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayashba** — he swore, he adjured."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The curse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Cursed before the LORD is anyone who rises up to rebuild this city, Jericho**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Arur** — cursed. The word of the formal curse formulas in Deuteronomy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The price"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**At the cost of his firstborn will he lay its foundation**, and **at the cost of his youngest will he set up its gates**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **bivkoro… uvitze'iro** — with his firstborn, with his youngest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first son when the work begins, the last when it finishes. The building will take a family."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the city particularly"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jericho was the firstfruits of the land — the first city taken, devoted entirely to God, the tithe of the conquest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The city shall be doomed by the LORD to destruction, it and all who are in it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rebuilding it would be taking back what had been handed over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next in the book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Achan takes a Babylonian garment, two hundred shekels of silver and a wedge of gold from Jericho, and hides them under his tent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the anger of the LORD burned against the children of Israel*, and thirty-six men die at Ai. See the note on Joshua 7:19–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ban was not a formality."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line about Joshua"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **the LORD was with Joshua**, and **word of him spread through all the land**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayehi shimo bechol-ha'aretz** — his fame was in all the land."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrasing used of Joshua at the crossing: *the LORD exalted Joshua in the sight of all Israel*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When the curse came due"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five hundred years later, in the reign of Ahab:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In his days **Hiel of Bethel built Jericho**. He laid its foundation **with Abiram his firstborn**, and with his youngest son **Segub** he set up its gates, **according to the word of the LORD which He had spoken through Joshua the son of Nun***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book of Kings names the man, both sons, and the prophecy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where that verse sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately before: *Ahab did more to provoke the LORD God of Israel to anger than all the kings of Israel who were before him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately after: *Elijah the Tishbite… said to Ahab, \"As the LORD God of Israel lives, before whom I stand, there shall be neither dew nor rain these years except at my word.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rebuilding of Jericho is the last thing recorded before Elijah walks in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jericho becomes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha heals its water: *thus says the LORD, \"I have healed this water; **from it there shall be no more death or barrenness**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zacchaeus is a tax collector there, and *Jesus entered and passed through Jericho*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is sitting outside it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then they came to Jericho. As He went out of Jericho with His disciples and a great multitude, **blind Bartimaeus, the son of Timaeus, sat by the road begging***. See the note on Mark 10:46–52."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last city under a curse in Joshua's book, and a blind man at its gate shouting for mercy."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The price named.** The firstborn at the foundation and the youngest at the gates — and Kings records the man who paid it, five hundred years later, by name."}]}, "Jude 1:20–21": {"title": "Stay where the love is", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four instructions in a letter that is otherwise entirely about danger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-five verses, and most of them are a warning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jude says he changed his subject: *beloved, while I was very diligent to write to you concerning our common salvation, **I found it necessary to write to you exhorting you to contend earnestly for the faith***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had a different letter planned."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who wrote it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jude, a bondservant of Jesus Christ, and **brother of James**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which makes him Jesus' brother too, and he does not say so. Neither does James."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both introduce themselves as servants."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But you, **dear friends**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **agapetoi** — beloved. He uses it three times in the closing paragraphs, each time to mark a break from the warning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Build your lives up on **the faith that is holiest of all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epoikodomountes** — building upon. A present participle: keep building."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Te hagiotate humon pistei** — your most holy faith. A superlative, and it is the foundation rather than the building."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **pray in the Holy Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase Paul uses at the end of the armour passage. See the note on Ephesians 6:18–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Stay right where **the love of God is**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **heautous en agape theou teresate** — keep yourselves in the love of God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *keep God loving you*. Stay in it, as a place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same construction Jesus uses: *abide in My love*. See the note on John 15:9–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fourth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And keep **watching the road for the mercy of our Lord Jesus Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **prosdechomenoi** — waiting for, expecting. The word used of Simeon *waiting for the Consolation of Israel*. See the note on Luke 2:25–28."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which brings you where"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which brings you into **life that never ends**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shape of the four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two are about the present — build, pray. One is a location — stay. One looks forward — watch."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And only the third is imperative in Greek. The other three are participles hanging off it: building, praying, and watching are how the keeping is done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does the keeping"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jude ends the letter with the answer:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now to Him **who is able to keep you from stumbling**, and to present you faultless before the presence of His glory with exceeding joy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phulaxai** — a different verb from verse 21, and God is the subject."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them to keep themselves and then closes by saying who is able to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The doxology"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To God our Saviour, who alone is wise, be glory and majesty, dominion and power, both now and forever. Amen.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last two verses of Jude are read aloud at the end of services more often than the other twenty-three are read at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word he uses for the faultless"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Amomous** — without blemish. The sacrificial word, from the Hebrew **mum**. See the note on Song of Songs 4:6–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word Paul uses of the church presented *without spot or wrinkle*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These are sensual persons, who cause divisions, not having the Spirit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then *but you, beloved*. The contrast is the point of the paragraph."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one imperative.** *Keep yourselves in the love of God* — and the letter's last sentence is about the One who is able to keep them from stumbling."}]}, "Jude 1:5–7": {"title": "\"Angels who would not stay in the place that was theirs\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three examples, and the middle one is the strangest paragraph in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"You know all of this already\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You know all of this already, but **I am going to walk you back through it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jude's method throughout the letter. He is not informing them; he is reminding them, and he says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Egypt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Lord brought a whole people up out of Egypt and rescued them — **and afterward He destroyed the ones among them who would not believe**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Being brought out was not the end of the matter. The generation that walked through the sea is the generation that fell in the wilderness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul makes the same point in 1 Corinthians 10, from the same history."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The angels"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there were angels who **would not stay in the place that was theirs; they walked out of their own home**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses, and both are about leaving a position."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *the place that was theirs* is **arche** — their rule, their domain, their principality. And *their own home* is **oiketerion**, a dwelling — the word Paul uses in 2 Corinthians 5 of the body as a habitation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something was abandoned that had been assigned to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has them now in **chains that will never break, down in the dark**, and He is **keeping them there for the judgement of the great day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present tense. They are not free and not yet sentenced — held."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says it in almost the same terms: *He handed them over to chains and to the dark, and there they are being kept until the judgement.* See the note on 2 Peter 2:4–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Sodom and Gomorrah did the same\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sodom and Gomorrah **did the same**, and so did the towns around them. They handed themselves over to sexual sin and **went after flesh that was not theirs**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That connective is what makes the paragraph what it is. Jude says the cities did the same **as the angels** — and the phrase he uses of the cities is *strange flesh*, flesh of a different kind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 19 records what the men of Sodom demanded, and who the visitors actually were."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever Jude means, he means the two cases to be read together, and he has put them in that order deliberately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exhibit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They are **set out in plain sight as an example**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **deigma** — a specimen, something displayed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Jude is doing this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter's purpose is stated in verse 3: *contend earnestly for the faith which was once for all delivered to the saints*, because *certain men have crept in unnoticed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three examples are all cases of something that was inside a position and left it. That is the pattern he wants his readers to see."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the letter ends.** After all of that — chains, darkness, fire — the last two verses are the warmest doxology in Scripture: *now to Him who is able to keep you from stumbling, and to present you faultless before the presence of His glory with exceeding joy.* Kept, and presented faultless. Jude spends a page on people who did not stay, and closes on the One who keeps."}]}, "Judges 1:1–7": {"title": "\"Who should go up first?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The book opens with a leaderless nation asking a question, and getting an answer that is already in the past tense."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question and the answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joshua was gone now, and the people were unsure where to turn. So they asked the LORD, \"Who should go up first?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer: *Judah will go. I have already handed the land to them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the tense. Not *I will hand it over*. The giving is spoken of as done, and the going up is what remains. That order — given first, taken afterwards — runs through the whole conquest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Judah's first move"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah does not go alone. He asks Simeon to come along, and offers the same in return: *come up with us into the land that's ours, and we'll fight side by side. Then we'll do the same for you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simeon's allotted territory lay inside Judah's, so the two tribes had a natural reason to work together. Whatever else it is, it is a leader's first act being an invitation rather than a command."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grim episode"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They capture Adoni-Bezek and cut off his thumbs and big toes — mutilations that made a man unable to hold a weapon or run."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And he says it himself: *seventy kings with their thumbs and big toes cut off once gathered scraps under my table. Now God has paid me back for what I did.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verdict is in the mouth of the man it falls on. Judges is a violent book and it rarely comments; here it lets the victim of the reprisal describe it as exactly what he had done to others."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seventy under the table"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seventy kings with their thumbs and big toes cut off once **gathered scraps under my table**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventy men who had once ruled cities, kept alive, maimed, and fed on what fell."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had spent his reign collecting them, and he describes the arrangement without shame. It is the boast in the middle of the confession."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other kings under a table"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five Amorite kings brought out of a cave, and Joshua's commanders told to put their feet on their necks — with the promise attached: *this is what the LORD will do to every enemy you fight.* See the note on Joshua 10:22–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two pictures of kings on the floor, one chapter of Scripture apart, and the second one is the model."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the name matters.** *Judah* means praise, and the tribe is sent up first. Genesis had already said the sceptre would not depart from Judah, and the line runs from here to David and to Christ. The book's first move puts that tribe at the front."}]}, "Judges 1:16–21": {"title": "\"The LORD was with Judah\" — and what stopped them anyway", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD was with Judah, and they took the hill country. But they could not drive out those in the valley, for they had iron chariots.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two halves, and the second one does not follow from the first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Presence and the chariots"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse says God was with them. Then it explains a failure by pointing at enemy equipment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Those two things cannot both be doing the work. If the LORD was with Judah, then iron was never the deciding factor — and the book itself proves it a few chapters later, when Deborah and Barak face Sisera's **nine hundred** iron chariots and the LORD routs every one of them in a river valley."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same terrain. Same technology. Opposite outcome. What changed was not the metal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Caleb was doing in the same paragraph"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As Moses had promised, Hebron was given to Caleb, and he drove out the three sons of Anak from it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An eighty-five-year-old walks into a fortified hill town and clears out giants by name, in the same six verses where a whole tribe stops at flat ground because of chariots."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer puts them side by side without comment. He does not need to say anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is beginning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read on and the word *but* keeps returning: this tribe did not drive them out, and neither did that one, and neither did the next. Chapter 2 opens with the consequence spelled out — the peoples left behind become a snare."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land had been given. God had said so in the first paragraph of the book: *I have already handed the land to them.* What is being described here is not a promise falling short. It is a promise not fully entered."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the eyes went.** Judah looked at the valley and saw iron. Caleb looked at Hebron and said *if the LORD is with me, I will drive them out, exactly as He said.* One of them was reckoning with the chariots and one with the promise, and the chapter records what each of them ended up with."}]}, "Judges 11:30–31": {"title": "Jephthah's vow — a bargain nobody asked him to make", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the darkest passages in the Old Testament, and the first thing to notice is that God never speaks in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has already happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit of the LORD came upon Jephthah.* That is the verse immediately before this one. He has already been equipped, already commissioned. The outcome is not in doubt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then he makes a vow anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If You will truly give the Ammonites into my hand… then whatever comes out of the door of my house to meet me when I return in peace — it will be the LORD's, and I will offer it up as a burnt offering.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is negotiating for something he has already been given. That is the shape of the tragedy: not too little faith in God's power, but a bargain offered to someone who had already said yes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law he lived under said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Torah is unambiguous. Human sacrifice is named repeatedly as the thing the nations did that God detests, and Leviticus provides a way to redeem a rash vow by payment. A priest could have released him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the text presents the vow as pious or the outcome as required. Judges records it; it does not commend it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the book frames it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judges is a downward spiral, and it ends with the line *everyone did what was right in his own eyes*. Jephthah's story sits inside that. The book is describing what happens to a people, not modelling behaviour."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On what became of his daughter.** The Hebrew is genuinely ambiguous, and readers have divided for centuries between a literal sacrifice and a life dedicated in perpetual virginity — the text dwells oddly on her never marrying. The honest position is that we do not know. What the passage does say plainly is that a man bound himself needlessly, and that a child paid for it."}]}, "Judges 14:12–14": {"title": "Out of the strong, something sweet", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A riddle at a wedding, and only one man in the room could possibly solve it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What had happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On the way down to Timnah, *a young lion came roaring against him. And the Spirit of the LORD came mightily upon him, and he tore it apart as one would have torn apart a young goat*. See the note on Judges 14:5–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He told nobody. Not his father, not his mother."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Coming back later for the wedding, he turned aside to look at the carcass, and *behold, a swarm of bees and honey were in the lion's carcass*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He scooped it out and ate it as he walked, and gave some to his parents — *but he did not tell them that he had taken the honey out of the carcass of the lion*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Nazirite was forbidden to touch a dead body. He had eaten out of one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wager"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let me put a riddle to you. If you can solve it within the seven days of the feast, I'll give you **thirty linen garments and thirty sets of clothes**. But if you can't, **then you give them to me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty of each, both ways. A substantial stake — the thirty companions had been assigned to him as groomsmen, and the Hebrew suggests they were there to watch him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Ask your riddle. Let's hear it**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The riddle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Out of the eater came something to eat; out of the strong came something sweet.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is compact and rhythmic: **me-ha'okhel yatsa ma'akhal, ume'az yatsa matoq**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is unfair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a riddle. It is a private incident dressed as one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no chain of reasoning that gets from the words to a lion carcass full of bees. Only somebody who had been there could answer it, and nobody had."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stall"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Three days passed, and **they could not solve it**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On the fourth day they go to his wife: *entice your husband, that he may explain the riddle to us, or else we will burn you and your father's house with fire*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She weeps on him for the rest of the week — *you only hate me, you do not love me* — and on the seventh day he tells her, and she tells them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What is sweeter than honey? And what is stronger than a lion?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Delivered before sunset on the last day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If you had not plowed with my heifer, you would not have solved my riddle**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does about the debt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Goes down to Ashkelon, kills thirty men, takes their clothes, and pays the wager."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And his anger was aroused, and he went back up to his father's house.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The account records the whole thing without comment. Judges is like that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the phrase goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The riddle itself outlives the story. *Out of the strong came something sweet* is quoted for centuries after by people who have never read the chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was on tins of Lyle's Golden Syrup — a lion carcass and a swarm of bees, with the words underneath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samson is a Nazirite by an angel's announcement before his birth, and the vow is broken piece by piece: the carcass here, the wine implied by the feast, and the hair at the end. See the note on Judges 16:21–22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the middle of it: *but his father and mother did not know that it was of the LORD — that He was seeking an occasion to move against the Philistines*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judges says that about the marriage his parents objected to."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why it could not be solved.** It was not a riddle. It was a description of something that had happened to one man on a road, with nobody watching."}]}, "Judges 14:5–7": {"title": "\"He told no one, not even his parents\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first recorded act of Samson's strength, and it happens where nobody can see it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The anointing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit of the LORD came powerfully upon him, and he tore the lion apart with his bare hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb is about rushing or advancing on someone — it is used of an army charging. It comes on Samson four times in his story, and every time something physical follows immediately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note what it is not: not a mood, not an inward sense. Judges reports it as a force arriving and a thing being done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"As easily as one might tear a young goat\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is deliberately domestic. Tearing a kid was something a farmhand did without thinking about it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the strength was, the narrator describes it as effortless rather than strained."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he told no one, not even his parents, what he had done.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence worth stopping on. Samson's whole story is otherwise a catalogue of showing off — riddles, boasts, a secret eventually talked out of him by a woman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here, at the start, he says nothing. His parents were walking a little way ahead and never knew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was doing there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He went on and spoke with the woman, and she was right in his eyes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter is not presenting Samson as a model. He is on his way to a marriage his parents objected to, in Philistine territory, and the phrase *right in his eyes* is the book's own shorthand for what was wrong with that whole era — *everyone did what was right in his own eyes.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the lion becomes.** He goes back that way later, finds honey in the carcass, and builds a riddle out of it that starts the feud with the Philistines. The private victory turns into the public trouble. Judges keeps reporting both without comment."}]}, "Judges 15:14–17": {"title": "The jawbone", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Samson's best-known fight, and it starts with his own countrymen handing him over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How he got there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three thousand men of Judah had come down to the rock of Etam to arrest him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do you not know that the Philistines rule over us? What is this you have done to us?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He agrees to be tied up, on one condition: *swear to me that you will not kill me yourselves*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They bind him with two new ropes and bring him out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men of Judah are not resisting the occupation. They are managing it, and Samson is the problem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arrival"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As he came to **Lehi**, the Philistines **rushed at him, shouting**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shouted against him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A bound man, delivered by his own tribe, and a crowd running at him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Spirit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the Spirit of the LORD came powerfully upon him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vattitslach alav ruach YHWH** — rushed upon him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase used when he tore the lion, and when he struck the thirty men at Ashkelon. See the note on Judges 14:5–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ropes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ropes on his arms **became like burnt flax**, and the bonds **simply melted from his hands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "New ropes, and the image is of something that has already been through a fire and only looks like rope."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weapon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He spotted the **fresh jawbone of a donkey**, snatched it up.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lechi chamor teriyyah** — a moist jawbone of an ass."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Fresh* matters. A dried bone shatters; a fresh one has some give."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is also, for a Nazirite, a dead body — one more clause of the vow gone. He had already eaten honey out of a lion's carcass. See the note on Judges 14:12–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The count"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And with it **struck down a thousand men**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"With the jawbone of a donkey, **heaps upon heaps** — with the jawbone of a donkey I have struck down a thousand men.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a pun that does not survive translation: **chamor chamorathayim** — donkey, and heaps. Same consonants."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **lechi** means both *jawbone* and is the name of the place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He composes a couplet about it on the spot."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disposal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When he finished speaking, **he threw the jawbone away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is kept. It was a bone on the ground twenty minutes earlier and it goes back on the ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody enshrines it, which is the opposite of what happened to the bronze serpent — Israel kept that one and burned incense to it for centuries until Hezekiah broke it up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he named that place **Ramath-lehi, the Hill of the Jawbone**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he became very thirsty; so he cried out to the LORD and said, \"**You have given this great deliverance by the hand of Your servant; and now shall I die of thirst?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So God split the hollow place that is in Lehi, and water came out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who has just killed a thousand men nearly dies of thirst twenty minutes later, and says so out loud."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage records both without embarrassment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse after that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he judged Israel twenty years in the days of the Philistines.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gaza, blind, at a mill, and a boy holding his hand. See the note on Judges 16:26–27."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he did with it afterwards.** Threw it away. A fresh bone off the ground, a thousand men, a couplet, and then he drops it and complains about being thirsty."}]}, "Judges 15:18–20": {"title": "The spring called \"the one who cried out\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Samson has just won a battle single-handed, and the next thing recorded is that he nearly died of thirst."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The complaint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have given Your servant this great victory. Must I now die of thirst and fall into the hands of these uncircumcised men?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things worth noticing. He credits the victory to God without hesitation — which, for Samson, is not guaranteed. And then he complains, bluntly, about the state he has been left in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a polite prayer. It is a man at the end of his strength saying so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So God split open the hollow place at Lehi, and water came pouring out. Samson drank, his strength returned, and he revived.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No rebuke. No lesson about attitude. Water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *revived* is the word for a spirit returning — the same idea used of someone coming round after a faint."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That is why the spring is called En-hakkore, the Spring of the One Who Cried Out — and it is there in Lehi to this day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The place is named after the cry, not the victory. Not after the thousand Philistines or the jawbone, both of which the chapter has just described at length. After a man shouting for help because he was thirsty."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who Samson was.** This is not a model believer. His story is a catalogue of appetite and broken vows, and the book knows it. The passage is worth reading precisely because of that: God answers a complaint from a man in the middle of a badly lived life, and the memorial left behind is of the asking."}]}, "Judges 16:21–22": {"title": "Quietly, unnoticed", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses at the bottom of the story, and the second one turns it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The capture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Philistines **seized him, gouged out his eyes**, and brought him down to **Gaza**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gaza is where the story of his decline started — he had gone there after a prostitute, and carried the city gates away on his shoulders to a hill."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He comes back through the same gate as a prisoner."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had lost first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And she said, \"The Philistines are upon you, Samson!\" So he awoke from his sleep, and said, \"I will go out as before, at other times, and shake myself free!\" **But he did not know that the LORD had departed from him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The eyes go afterwards. The line about not knowing comes before the blinding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The eyes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samson's story is full of them. *I saw a woman in Timnah… **get her for me, for she pleases me well**.* The Hebrew is *she is right in my eyes*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book's refrain is *everyone did what was right in his own eyes*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing they take is what he had been following."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shackles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They bound him in **bronze shackles**, and there in the prison **he ground grain at the mill**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he became a grinder in the prison house*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grinding was women's work, or a donkey's. The strongest man in Israel turning a millstone in the dark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The state"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blind and shamed.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **little by little, the hair of his head began to grow back again** — quietly, unnoticed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the hair of his head began to grow again after it had been shaven*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody notices, because a prisoner turning a mill is not looked at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the hair was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the source of the strength. The Nazirite vow was — no razor, no wine, no contact with the dead — and the uncut hair was its visible sign."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had broken the other two clauses repeatedly. The hair was the last part of the vow still standing, and Delilah got that too."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he prays"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"O Lord GOD, **remember me, I pray! Strengthen me, I pray, just this once**, O God, that I may with one blow take vengeance on the Philistines for my two eyes!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only recorded prayer of his life that is not a complaint. Earlier he had prayed for water and been given it out of a hollow place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three thousand on the roof, watching a blind man brought out to entertain them at a festival for Dagon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Samson took hold of the two middle pillars… **so the dead that he killed at his death were more than he had killed in his life**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then his brothers and all his father's household came down and took him, and brought him up and buried him between Zorah and Eshtaol in the tomb of his father Manoah.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same stretch of country where, at the start, *the Spirit of the LORD began to move upon him*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is listed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews 11, in the roll call: *Gideon and Barak and **Samson** and Jephthah, also David and Samuel and the prophets — who through faith subdued kingdoms, worked righteousness, obtained promises*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is on the list."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse that carries it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the hair of his head began to grow again.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator drops it in and moves on. Everything in the last scene rests on a sentence about something growing slowly in the dark, that nobody was watching."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the narrator notices.** Not the mill and not the chains. That something was growing back, in a prison, where no one was looking at him."}]}, "Judges 16:26–27": {"title": "The boy holding his hand", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses before the end, and the strongest man in Israel is being led by a servant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The temple of Dagon in Gaza. The Philistine lords have gathered *to offer a great sacrifice to Dagon their god*, and their song has already been recorded:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Our god has delivered into our hands Samson our enemy!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So it happened, when their hearts were merry, that they said, \"Call for Samson, that he may perform for us.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The state he is in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Blind, in bronze fetters, brought out of a prison where he had been grinding grain. See the note on Judges 16:21–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one who leads him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Samson said to **the young man who held his hand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **na'ar** — a boy, a servant, a young man. The word covers a wide range and usually means somebody young enough to be sent on errands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last human being to help Samson is a boy holding his hand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Let me feel the pillars that hold up this temple, so I can lean against them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Reasonable, from a blind man who has been made to perform. He wants somewhere to rest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The boy has no reason to refuse and does it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The building"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now the temple was **crowded — men and women, all the Philistine rulers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Excavated Philistine temples of the period have been found with a roof supported on two central pillars set on stone bases, close enough together for one man to reach both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The roof"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **on the roof alone some three thousand men and women**, watching Samson be mocked.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The roof is the gallery. Three thousand people up there, and everybody who mattered in Philistia below."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Watching Samson be mocked.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tsachaq** — to laugh, to play, to make sport."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word used of Ishmael in Genesis 21, and the root of Isaac's name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The entertainment is a blind man being made to stumble."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"O Lord GOD, **remember me, I pray! Strengthen me, I pray, just this once**, O God, that I may with one blow take vengeance on the Philistines for my two eyes!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only recorded prayer in his life that is not about his own thirst."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Remember me* — the same request Hannah made, and the thief on the cross."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Samson took hold of the two middle pillars which supported the temple, and he braced himself against them, one on his right and the other on his left.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Samson said, \"**Let me die with the Philistines!**\" And he pushed with all his might.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The count"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the dead that he killed at his death were **more than he had killed in his life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The account gives no total, and does not comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who cannot keep a vow, cannot keep a secret, and cannot stay away from the wrong woman. Twenty years of judging Israel and almost nothing to show for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the last thing he does is ask to be led to a pillar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where his name is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews 11: *Gideon and Barak and **Samson** and Jephthah, also David and Samuel and the prophets — who through faith subdued kingdoms, worked righteousness, obtained promises, stopped the mouths of lions*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is on the list, between Barak and Jephthah, and the list mentions lions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is buried"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And his brothers and all his father's household came down and took him, and brought him up and buried him between Zorah and Eshtaol in the tomb of his father Manoah.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same ground where, in chapter 13, *the Spirit of the LORD began to move upon him*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who leads him to the pillars.** A boy, holding his hand, doing what he was asked. The last act of the strongest man in Israel required somebody to walk him to the spot."}]}, "Judges 16:28–30": {"title": "Samson's last act", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He prays once in the whole book, and it is at the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Blind, in Gaza, grinding grain in a prison — the work of an animal or a woman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Philistines had gouged his eyes out, bound him with bronze, and brought him into the temple of Dagon to entertain three thousand people on the roof."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Call for Samson, that he may perform for us.* See the note on Judges 16:21–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail the narrator drops in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*However, **the hair of his head began to grow again after it had been shaved**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said quietly, before the feast, and then left alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The boy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Samson said to **the lad who held him by the hand**, \"Let me feel the pillars.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A child is leading the strongest man in Israel around by the wrist."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Samson **called out to the LORD**: \"**O Lord GOD, remember me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **zochreni na** — remember me, please."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has spoken to God once before, after the jawbone, complaining of thirst. This is the only petition in his story."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses three divine names in one sentence: **Adonai YHWH… Elohim**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Please, God, give me strength just once more**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chazzeqeni na ak happa'am hazzeh** — strengthen me only this once."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not ask for his eyes, his freedom, or his life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason he gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**That I may pay back the Philistines for my two eyes**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **naqam achat mishshtei einai** — one vengeance for my two eyes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Personal. He does not say *for Israel*, or *for Your name*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrative records the motive as he stated it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pillars"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he reached for **the two central pillars on which the temple stood**, bracing himself against them — **one hand on each**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Philistine temples excavated at Tell Qasile have exactly this construction: a roof supported by two central wooden pillars on stone bases, close enough for a man to reach both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Let me die with the Philistines**,\" he said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tamot naphshi im-pelishtim**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The push"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **he pushed with all his might**, and the temple came down upon the rulers and all the people inside.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The five lords of the Philistines were there — the whole leadership in one building."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The count"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And so **those he brought down at his death were more than all he had killed in his life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator does the arithmetic and states it flatly, as a fact about the ledger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What his life had been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Nazirite from the womb, whose vow forbade wine, corpses and razors, and who broke every part of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He killed a lion and later ate honey from its carcass. He married a Philistine. He told Delilah the secret on the fourth asking, after she had used the first three answers to try to hand him over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But he did not know that the LORD had departed from him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then his brothers and all his father's household came down and took him, and brought him up and buried him between Zorah and Eshtaol in the tomb of his father Manoah.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same ground he was born on. *And he had judged Israel twenty years.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews does with him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And what more shall I say? For the time would fail me to tell of Gideon and Barak and **Samson** and Jephthah.* See the note on Hebrews 11:32–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Named in the list, with no commentary."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The count.** More at his death than in his life — stated by the narrator as arithmetic, over a man who asked for strength once and gave a personal reason for it."}]}, "Judges 3:31": {"title": "Shamgar", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse for a whole career, and it is the shortest entry in the book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After Ehud came **Shamgar son of Anath**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name is not Hebrew. It looks Hurrian, and **Anath** is a Canaanite war goddess — which means either a place name, Beth-Anath, or a foreign parentage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing else is known about him. No tribe, no town, no burial notice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judges gives every other judge a paragraph at minimum. He gets a sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **struck down six hundred Philistines**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same figure as Saul's entire army a few centuries later. See the note on 1 Samuel 13:19–23."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weapon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **an ox goad — a simple farmer's tool**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **malmad habbaqar** — a teaching-instrument for cattle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The root is **lamad**, to teach or train. The same root as **talmid**, a disciple, and **Talmud**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An ox goad is literally a *teacher of oxen*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A pole, six to eight feet, with an iron point at one end for prodding and a flat blade at the other for scraping mud off the plough."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Long, heavy, and pointed. It is the best improvised weapon a farmer had."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it turns up again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On the Philistine price list: *a third of a shekel… for fixing an ox-goad*. See the note on 1 Samuel 13:19–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The occupying power charging Israelite farmers to sharpen the implement one of them had killed six hundred with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He too **rescued Israel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hoshia** — saved, delivered. The verb behind **Yeshua**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He too* — the same phrase used of the judges before him. Judges gives him no less credit for having no biography."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is mentioned again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the days of **Shamgar son of Anath**, in the days of Jael, the highways were deserted, and the travellers walked along the byways.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deborah's song, and it dates him. Roads unsafe, villages emptied, and nobody moving in the open."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the period he worked in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The company he keeps"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judges is full of the wrong weapon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ehud, left-handed, with a homemade two-edged dagger a cubit long, strapped where nobody frisked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jael, with a tent peg and a hammer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gideon, with three hundred men carrying trumpets, pitchers and torches, and no hands free for a sword."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samson, with the jawbone of a donkey. See the note on Judges 15:14–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And later, David, with five smooth stones."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they have in common"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of them is equipped."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book of Judges is a catalogue of improvised objects, and its refrain is *the LORD raised up a deliverer*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Judges says about the period"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In those days there was no king in Israel; everyone did what was right in his own eyes.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything known about Shamgar fits in twenty-five words, and one of them is *saved*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the tool is called in Hebrew.** A teacher of oxen — from the root behind *disciple*. It is on the Philistine price list two hundred years later."}]}, "Judges 4:17–24": {"title": "Jael and the tent peg", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The commander of nine hundred iron chariots is killed with two objects that belong to housework."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Sisera was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens with him: twenty years of harsh oppression, nine hundred chariots of iron, an army Israel had no answer to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He ends the day on foot, in a woman's tent, asking for a drink."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she used"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jael took up a **tent peg** and a **hammer**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pitching and striking the tents was women's work among the nomads. The peg and the mallet were the tools of her trade, in her hand every time the camp moved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not go looking for a weapon. She kills him with what she already had."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The milk"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He asked for a little water, and she opened a skin of milk and gave him a drink.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked for less than she gave him. The milk is what puts him into the *exhausted sleep* the next sentence describes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judges 5, Deborah's song, dwells on this: *he asked for water, she gave him milk; she brought him curds in a lordly bowl.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"At the hand of a woman\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the great commander died at the hand of a woman.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator says it as a verdict, and it had been announced ten verses earlier. Deborah had told Barak that because of the way he was going about it, *the LORD will sell Sisera into the hand of a woman.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Barak arrives after it is over. *Come, and I'll show you the man you're looking for.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the story is told this way"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Iron chariots against a hammer and a tent peg. The book of Judges keeps doing this — an ox goad, a jawbone, three hundred men with jars and torches, a millstone dropped from a wall."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same principle Paul states outright: *God chose the weak things of the world to shame the strong.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 1:26–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The small ordinary thing is not a lesser weapon. It is the one God keeps choosing."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the chapter closes.** *Israel's hand pressed harder and harder against Jabin, until at last they destroyed him.* One peg in one tent, and then twenty years of oppression unwinding."}]}, "Judges 4:4–10": {"title": "Deborah, Barak, and the honour that went elsewhere", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A woman is running the country, a general will not move without her, and the victory ends up credited to a third person nobody has met yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Deborah's position"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A prophet was leading Israel — Deborah, a woman of fire and wisdom.* She judges under a palm tree, and Israel comes up to her for judgment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text states it without commentary or apology. She is the only judge in the book described as holding court, and the only one who is also a prophet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Barak's condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you go with me, I'll go. But if you won't go with me, I won't go.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just been given a direct command from God through a prophet, complete with the battle plan and the promised outcome. His answer attaches a condition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judges is a book of increasingly compromised leaders, and this is early in the slide — not a refusal, but not a straight yes either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it costs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because of the road you are taking, the honour will not be yours — the LORD will hand Sisera over to a woman.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She goes anyway. The mission is not withdrawn and the victory is not smaller; what changes is whose name is on it. And the woman in question turns out not to be Deborah but Jael, who nobody in this conversation has mentioned."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Hebrews does with him.** Barak is named in the roll of faith in Hebrews 11 — Deborah is not. The New Testament lists the man who hesitated among those commended, without mentioning the hesitation. Both facts sit in the canon: the honour redirected here, and the name kept there."}]}, "Judges 6:11–16": {"title": "\"Mighty man of valour\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An angel greets a man hiding in a hole with a title that describes nobody in the vicinity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Gideon was there, **beating out wheat in a winepress** — hiding, really, keeping the grain out of Midian's sight.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "You thresh wheat on a high, open floor, so the wind can carry the chaff away. A winepress is a pit cut into rock, for treading grapes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is doing the work in the worst possible place because it is the only place they cannot see him from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The greeting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD is with you, mighty man of valour.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **gibbor chayil** — a warrior, a man of force. It is the phrase used of David's mighty men."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to a man crouched in a pit, threshing by hand, out of sight of an occupying army."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Illumination's note on it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was not the name of the man crouching in a winepress; **it was the name of the warrior grace would make him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The title is spoken over what he will be, not over what is standing there — the same way Abram was called *father of a multitude* while childless. See the note on Genesis 17:3–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Gideon says back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If the LORD is with us, then why has all this come upon us?** Where are all His wonders that our fathers told us about?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He argues with the greeting. He has heard about the exodus and can see the winepress he is sitting in, and he says so."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But now **He has abandoned us** and handed us over to Midian.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no faith in the reply, and the angel does not correct it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Go in this strength of yours\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go in this strength of yours**, and you will save Israel from Midian's hand. **Am I not the One sending you?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This strength of yours* — the strength he had just been given the name of, and shown none of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the credentials offered are not his. The question is about who is doing the sending."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My clan is the weakest** in all Manasseh, and **I am the least** in my father's house.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Weakest tribe, smallest clan, youngest son. He gives three reasons and every one of them is true."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses said the same at the bush. So did Jeremiah, and Saul, and Solomon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But I will be with you**, and you will strike down Midian as though they were one man.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the entire response to the three objections. Not a correction of his self-assessment — a statement about company."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As though they were one man* — an army of a hundred and thirty-five thousand, counted as one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he ends up with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three hundred men, jars, torches and trumpets. Every weapon deliberately removed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lest Israel claim glory for itself, saying, \"My own hand has saved me.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the New Testament remembers him for.** *And what more shall I say? Time would fail me to tell of **Gideon**…* — Hebrews 11, in the roll of faith. The man who asked for a fleece twice, and then for the opposite sign the next night."}]}, "Judges 6:17–24": {"title": "The LORD is peace", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Gideon asks for proof and then asks the visitor to wait while he cooks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Give me **a sign that it is really You** speaking with me. Please, **don't leave until I return**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just been called *mighty man of valour* while threshing wheat in a winepress, and told to save Israel. See the note on Judges 6:11–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He wants confirmation, and he is not rebuked for wanting it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will wait until you come back.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words. Whatever Gideon takes to prepare a meal — and it turns out to be considerable — the visitor sits under the oak."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he brings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **young goat**, and **from an ephah of flour** he made bread without yeast. He put the meat in a basket and **the broth in a pot**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An ephah is something like twenty-two litres of flour. That is an enormous quantity — the same measure Abraham told Sarah to use for the three visitors at Mamre."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A young goat was a costly animal, and Israel was living on what Midian had not stolen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is under military occupation, hiding his grain in a winepress, and he cooks a feast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is done with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take the meat and the bread and **lay them on this rock**, and **pour out the broth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not eaten. Put on a stone, and the liquid poured away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The meal becomes an offering, and the rock becomes an altar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fire"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the angel of the LORD **reached out the staff** in His hand, and **its tip touched** the meat and the bread. **Fire blazed up from the rock** and consumed them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A touch of a staff tip, and fire out of stone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the angel of the LORD vanished from his sight.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Gideon says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Alas, Lord GOD! I have seen the angel of the LORD face to face!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is terrified, because the belief was that no one sees God and lives — *you cannot see My face; for no man shall see Me, and live.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Peace to you. Do not be afraid. You will not die.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three short sentences. The fear is addressed before anything else is said."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The altar"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Gideon built an altar there and named it **The LORD Is Peace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrew **Yahweh-Shalom**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names it after the sentence he was given, not after the fire."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the writer adds: *to this day it still stands at Ophrah.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does that night"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next paragraph: told to tear down his father's altar to Baal and cut down the Asherah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He did it **by night**, because he was too afraid of his father's household and the men of the town to do it by day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judges records both — he obeyed, and he was frightened. The book does not tidy him up."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the altar was for.** Gideon builds it before any battle, before the fleece, and before the three hundred. The first thing he does after being commissioned is make an altar and name it peace."}]}, "Judges 8:18–21": {"title": "\"Each one had the bearing of a king's son\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two captured kings describe men they killed, and their description is the striking thing in the passage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What sort of men did you kill at Tabor?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gideon has run down Zebah and Zalmunna, the two Midianite kings, after the rout with the three hundred. He asks about an incident at Tabor that the book has not recorded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Men like you**,\" they answered. \"**Each one had the bearing of a king's son.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *according to the form of the sons of a king*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Enemies, under no obligation to flatter, describing men they had killed — and the word they reach for is royal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the men were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Those were my brothers, my own mother's sons.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gideon's own family. The Midianites had killed his brothers at Tabor and he had not known how, until now."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that says about Gideon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Men like you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two kings are looking at the man who defeated them, and they say his brothers looked like him — and that all of them looked like princes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set that beside where the book found him: *beating out wheat in a winepress — hiding, really, keeping the grain out of Midian's sight.* And beside what he said of himself: *my clan is the weakest in all Manasseh, and I am the least in my father's house.* See the note on Judges 6:11–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angel had called him *mighty man of valour* while he was crouching in a pit. Two enemy kings, chapters later, independently say the same thing about his whole family."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was that all along. He was the last person to know it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Jether"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He turned to **Jether, his firstborn**, and said, \"Get up, kill them.\" But **the boy did not draw his sword; he was afraid**, for he was only a youth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gideon hands the sword to his son, and the boy cannot do it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judges records the failure without comment, and the fear is given as the reason and the age as the excuse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the kings say to that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get up yourself and do it — a man's strength is measured by the man.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *as the man is, so is his strength.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said by men about to die, to the man who will kill them, and it is a taunt about the son."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ornaments"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And took **the ornaments shaped like crescent moons** that hung on their camels' necks.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crescents were Midianite religious symbols."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gideon takes the gold from that campaign and makes an ephod out of it, and *all Israel played the harlot with it there, and it became a snare to Gideon and to his house.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that shows him told who he really is ends with him making an idol out of the proceeds."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two answers to \"what sort of men?\"** The enemy said *like a king's son*. Gideon had said *the least in my father's house*. Both statements are in the same book about the same man, and only one of them was ever true."}]}, "Lamentations 3:31–33": {"title": "\"He does not wound from His heart\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The centre of the book, and it is the only part that looks up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five poems over the ruins of Jerusalem, written by an eyewitness to the siege. The city is burned, the temple is gone, the walls are down, and the poems describe famine so severe that mothers ate their children."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapters 1, 2, 4 and 5 are unrelieved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 3 is the middle poem, and it is the longest — sixty-six verses, an acrostic with three lines to each Hebrew letter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The turn comes at verse 21, and this passage is on the other side of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes just before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This I recall to my mind, therefore I have hope: **through the LORD's mercies we are not consumed, because His compassions fail not. They are new every morning; great is Your faithfulness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written by a man standing in the ash of his own city."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the Lord **will not cast off forever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **zanach** — to reject, to cast away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not deny the casting off. He puts a limit on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The concession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though **He brings grief**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated plainly, in the same sentence. The poet does not attribute the disaster to accident or to Babylon alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will **gather you back in compassion**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **racham** — the verb from the word for a womb. Deep, physical mercy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For His **unfailing love is past all counting**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chesed** — covenant loyalty — and *the multitude of His mercies*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two negatives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **does not wound from His heart**, nor **bring grief to the children of men because He wills it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of the first is *He does not afflict **from His heart***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two statements about motive, in a book whose subject is a judgment nobody disputes was deserved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The poet has already said God did it. Here he says where it did not come from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for \"children of men\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Bene ish** — sons of man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *sons of Israel*. He widens it to the human race."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the poem says a few verses on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who is he who speaks and it comes to pass, when the Lord has not commanded it? Is it not from the mouth of the Most High that woe and well-being proceed?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *why should a living man complain, a man for the punishment of his sins? Let us search out and examine our ways, and turn back to the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The poet holds both — that God did it, and that it is not what He wants."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jeremiah had spent his life doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Warning the city for forty years, being put in a cistern for it, and being told at the start that nobody would listen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the man who wept over it and the man who said it was coming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other line from this chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is good that one should hope and wait quietly for the salvation of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the sentence stands.** In the middle of five poems about a burned city, by somebody who saw it — and what he says is not that it did not happen, but that it did not come from the heart."}]}, "Leviticus 1:1–2": {"title": "The word for offering", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first sentence of the book, and it starts with a voice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD **called to Moses** and spoke to him **from the Tent of Meeting**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *called* is **vayyiqra** — and it is the Hebrew name of the whole book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus ends with the tabernacle finished and the glory filling it so densely that *Moses was not able to enter*. Leviticus opens with a voice coming out of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book exists because a man could not walk in, and had to be told how."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where God is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The place where **God had come to dwell among His people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ohel mo'ed** — the tent of appointed meeting. **Mo'ed** is the word for a fixed time and place, the same root as the appointed feasts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a shrine to visit. A rendezvous."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Speak to **the Israelites**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To everybody, not to the priests. Chapters 1 to 7 tell the people what to bring; chapters 6 onward turn and tell the priests what to do with it. See the note on Leviticus 6:24–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**When any of you wants to bring an offering** to the LORD.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *when a man of you brings near*, and the burnt offering in particular is voluntary. Nothing here is a summons."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The compulsory offerings come later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Qorban** — the noun for the offering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "From the root **qarab**, to come near, to approach. The same root as the noun for a relative, and the verb for a battle joining."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for the gift is a word about proximity. What is being described is not a payment but an approach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark keeps it in Greek letters and translates it: *Corban — that is, a gift*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is acceptable"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Bring one **from the herd or the flock** — an animal from among **your own cattle, sheep, or goats**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Domestic animals only. Nothing wild, nothing hunted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means it cost something. A man could not go out and find one; he had to take it from what he was living on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David makes the same point at Araunah's threshing floor: *I will not offer burnt offerings to the LORD my God with that which costs me nothing*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three grades"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter allows a bull, a sheep or goat, or two birds — descending by what the worshipper could afford, and each one is called *a sweet aroma to the LORD*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase for the bull and for the pigeons."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph and Mary brought the birds. See the note on Luke 2:33–35."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five offerings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Burnt, grain, peace, sin, and guilt. The first three are voluntary and the last two are required."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus puts the voluntary ones first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You shall be holy, for I am holy.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the sentence that recurs like a refrain: *I am the LORD your God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter quotes the first to Gentile believers. See the note on 1 Peter 1:14–16."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the noun means.** *Qorban*, from the root for coming near. The word for the sacrifice is a word about distance being closed."}]}, "Leviticus 1:10–13": {"title": "On the north side of the altar", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The burnt offering from the flock, and the instructions include a compass point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"A male without flaw\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let it be **a male without flaw**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tamim** — whole, sound, unblemished. The same word used of Noah, and the word Peter reaches for: *a lamb without blemish and without spot.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it dies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is put to death **on the north side of the altar** before the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The direction is specified, and Leviticus does not usually waste words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Golgotha lay north of the temple mount. The gospels place the crucifixion *outside the gate*, and Hebrews makes a point of it: *Jesus also, that He might sanctify the people with His own blood, suffered outside the gate.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the offerer does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read verse 4, from the paragraph before: *he shall put his hand on the head of the burnt offering, and it will be accepted on his behalf to make atonement for him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One gesture. He leans on the animal's head, and the transfer happens."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The examination in this system falls on the animal, never on the man. See the note on John 1:29–34."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is burned"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The priest arranges them **with the head and the fat** on the wood above the fire… Then the priest **offers it all**, burning it on the altar.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole animal. The burnt offering is the one sacrifice in which nothing is kept back — no portion for the priest, no portion for the giver."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew name for it is **olah**, from the verb to go up. It all goes up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is washed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While **the inner parts and the legs are washed with water**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two things nobody sees: what is inside, and what it walked on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A gift by fire, **a fragrance that pleases the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a soothing, restful aroma. The same phrase used after Noah's altar, when God said in His heart that He would not curse the ground again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes it into Ephesians: *Christ also has loved us and given Himself for us, an offering and a sacrifice to God for **a sweet-smelling aroma**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is first in the book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus opens with this one, before the grain offering, the peace offering, the sin offering or the trespass offering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The burnt offering is not about a particular sin. It is about a whole life given up, entirely consumed, and the first thing the book describes."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The graded provision.** A bull, or a sheep or goat, or — in the paragraph after this — *turtledoves or young pigeons*, for those who could afford nothing else. Mary and Joseph brought the birds. The system was written so that no one was excluded by price."}]}, "Leviticus 1:3–9": {"title": "The whole animal", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first offering in the book, and the distinguishing feature is that none of it comes back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The specification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let it be **a male without flaw**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tamim** — whole, complete, without blemish. **Mum** is the noun for what disqualifies. See the note on Song of Songs 4:6–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is brought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **the entrance of the Tent of Meeting**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The door. Not inside, and not at a distance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The manner"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Offering it **freely**, so that **you may be welcomed and accepted** before the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *of his own free will* — **lirtsono**, according to his pleasure. And **leratson**, for acceptance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same root does both jobs: he brings it willingly, and it is received favourably. The worshipper's willingness and God's acceptance are the same word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lay your hand on the animal's head, and **it will be received on your behalf, standing in your place to make things right for you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **samakh** — to lean, to press down with weight. Not a touch."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man puts his weight on the head of an animal that is about to die, and the transaction runs in both directions: what he is goes onto it, and what it is comes back to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD has laid on Him the iniquity of us all.* See the note on Isaiah 53:4–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The killing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There **the bull is put to death before the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The worshipper kills it, not the priest. Leviticus is precise about this — the man who brought it does the slaughtering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Aaron's sons, the priests, bring its blood and **sprinkle it against the sides of the altar**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priest's work starts with the blood. Everything before that is the man's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dismembering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The animal is then **skinned and cut into pieces**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hide comes off, and it is the one part not burnt. The priest keeps it. See the note on Leviticus 7:1–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything else goes on the fire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The washing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **inner parts and the legs are washed clean with water**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two least presentable parts. The entrails and the lower legs, which is where the dirt is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are washed and then burnt with the rest, which means the fire receives nothing unclean."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The totality"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the priest **burns it all on the altar — the whole animal, given entirely to God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew name of this offering is **olah** — from **alah**, to go up. What ascends."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old English name is *holocaust*, from the Greek: wholly burnt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of the five offerings, this is the only one where nobody eats anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A gift by fire, **a fragrance that pleases the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **reach nichoach** — a soothing aroma, a restful smell. From the root for rest, the same as Noah's name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Its first use is after the flood, when Noah builds an altar: *and the LORD smelled a soothing aroma*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses it of Christ: *an offering and a sacrifice to God for a sweet-smelling aroma*. And of a gift sent to him in prison: *a sweet-smelling aroma, an acceptable sacrifice, well pleasing to God*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the fire came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus 9: *fire came out from before the LORD and consumed the burnt offering… when all the people saw it, they shouted and fell on their faces*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The altar fire was lit once, and the standing order was that it never went out."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one offering nobody eats.** Skin removed, entrails washed, and the rest burnt entire — and the phrase for the smoke means *a restful smell*."}]}, "Leviticus 10:12–15": {"title": "In a clean place", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses gives instructions about lunch on the worst day of Aaron's life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Nadab and Abihu, the sons of Aaron, each took his censer and put fire in it, put incense on it, and offered **profane fire before the LORD, which He had not commanded them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So fire went out from the LORD and devoured them, and they died before the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Aaron held his peace.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their bodies have been carried out of the camp by their cousins. Aaron and his two remaining sons have been told not to mourn publicly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is addressed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses said to Aaron and his **remaining sons, Eleazar and Ithamar**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word *remaining* is in the text."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take the grain offering that is left over**… and **eat it beside the altar**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Life continues. The offerings still have to be handled and the priests still have to eat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grain offering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Prepared **without yeast**, for it is **most holy**. Eat it in **a holy place**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **qodesh qodashim**. It stays inside the sanctuary courtyard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because it is **your share and your sons' share** of the offerings made by fire to the LORD; **this is what I was commanded**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses does not argue for it. He reports the command."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other portion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But the **breast that was waved and the thigh that was presented you may eat in a clean place**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The peace offering's portions. See the note on Leviticus 7:28–34."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The difference"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The grain offering is eaten in a **holy** place. The peace offering portions are eaten in a **clean** place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two different categories. Most holy things stay in the courtyard; the peace offering's share can go anywhere ritually clean — a house, a tent, a table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who eats them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You and your sons **and daughters**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The grain offering is for the males of the priesthood. The peace offering's share is for the whole household."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus is precise about it, and the peace offering is the one that widens."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do with it first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The thigh that was presented and the breast that was waved, together with the fat portions, are to be **lifted up as a wave offering** before the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tenuphah** — a waving, a to-and-fro motion. And **terumah**, a heaving, lifted up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The portions are moved in front of God before anybody takes them home. Presented, and then handed back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The term"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They belong to you and your children as **a lasting share**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **choq olam** — a perpetual due."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chapter's other half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Right after this, Moses discovers the goat of the sin offering has been burned rather than eaten, and is angry with Eleazar and Ithamar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aaron answers: *\"Look, this day they have offered their sin offering and their burnt offering before the LORD, and such things have befallen me! **If I had eaten the sin offering today, would it have been accepted in the sight of the LORD?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So when Moses heard that, he was content.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A father whose two sons died that morning explains why he could not eat, and Moses accepts it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What sits between the two halves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instructions about the priests' portion, and the sentence about a clean place where the daughters can eat too."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two locations.** Most holy things stay in the courtyard. The peace offering's share goes home — to a clean place, with the daughters included."}]}, "Leviticus 11:29–38": {"title": "The seed stays clean", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A list of dead lizards and broken pots, and one exemption at the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **weasel**, the **mouse**, any kind of **great lizard**, the **gecko**, the **monitor lizard**, the **wall lizard**, the **skink**, and the **chameleon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight creatures, named. The Hebrew names for several of them are uncertain enough that translations differ."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are the small things that get into a house — under a jar, into a store cupboard, into the water."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever touches them when they are dead will be **unclean until evening**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not sinful. Unclean, and until sunset."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole system runs on contact and time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the uncleanness does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When one of them dies and **falls on something, that item becomes unclean, whatever its use** — whether wood, cloth, hide, or sackcloth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It travels. That is the point of the chapter, and of the purity code generally."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It must be **put in water** and will remain unclean until evening; then it will be clean.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Washing plus waiting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clay pot"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If one of them falls into a **clay pot, everything in it becomes unclean, and you must break the pot**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Porous, and unsalvageable. The same ruling as the sin offering boiled in earthenware. See the note on Leviticus 6:24–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oven"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*An **oven or cooking pot must be broken**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A domestic clay oven, destroyed over a dead gecko. The law is not sentimental about kitchen equipment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **spring or cistern** for collecting water **remains clean**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Running water and a gathering of water are exempt. The individual who touches the carcass in it is unclean, but the water is not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Volume and flow beat contamination."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last ruling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If a carcass falls on **seed to be planted, the seed stays clean**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *seed for sowing*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A dead unclean animal lands on a sack of grain intended for planting, and the grain is not affected."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **if water has been put on the seed** and a carcass falls on it, **it is unclean**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dry seed is immune. Wet seed is not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Water is what makes it vulnerable, because water is what starts it germinating. The moment the seed begins to swell and open, it can be reached."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dry, it is sealed. Nothing gets in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the seed image is used"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The seed is the word of God.* See the note on Matthew 13:18–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Born again, not of corruptible seed but incorruptible.* See the note on 1 Peter 1:23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *each carrying its own seed to make more of its kind* — the clause built in on the third day. See the note on Genesis 1:9–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For I am the LORD your God. You shall therefore **consecrate yourselves, and you shall be holy**; for I am holy.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse Peter quotes to Gentile believers. See the note on 1 Peter 1:14–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The dietary code is given a reason, and it is a reason about separateness rather than about hygiene."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to the whole list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What God has cleansed you must not call common.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said three times to Peter on a roof in Joppa, over a sheet full of exactly these animals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He works out what it means at Cornelius' house: *God has shown me that I should not call any man common or unclean*. See the note on Acts 10:34–43."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter of unclean creatures ends up being about people."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clause at the end.** Dry seed is untouchable. It is the moment water is put on it — the moment it starts to open — that it can be reached at all."}]}, "Leviticus 16:11–14": {"title": "Blood on the mercy seat, once a year", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The Day of Atonement, and the only occasion in the year when anyone went behind the curtain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He deals with himself first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Aaron slaughters the bull that is his own sin offering, making atonement for himself and his household.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before he can carry anything in for the nation, the high priest has to be covered himself. The man doing the mediating needs a mediator."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews makes a great deal of this: *He has no need, like those high priests, to offer sacrifices daily, first for his own sins and then for the people's.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The incense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A cloud of sweet smoke rises and covers the mercy seat — **so that he may live and not die**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The smoke is not decoration or atmosphere. Leviticus says plainly what it is for: it veils the mercy seat so the priest is not destroyed by what is above it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Even on the one day access is permitted, and even after the bull has died, there is a screen between the man and the glory."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He sprinkles it with his finger on the front of the mercy seat, and seven times before it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Once on the lid itself, seven times on the ground in front. Seven is the number of completeness — a full measure, not a token."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blood is applied to the exact place God had said He would speak from. What was underneath the lid was the broken law; what went on top of it was blood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The frequency"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Once a year, one man, with blood, behind a curtain, in smoke, having first atoned for himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set that beside *let us walk right up to the throne where grace sits* and the distance the New Testament is describing becomes measurable."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The curtain.** Matthew records that at the moment Jesus died, the curtain of the temple was torn from top to bottom. Not opened. Torn — and from the top, which is the end no man could reach. See the notes on Hebrews 9:23–24 and Hebrews 4:14–16."}]}, "Leviticus 17:10–12": {"title": "\"The life given in your place\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most important verse in Leviticus about why any of the sacrifices worked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prohibition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If anyone… **eats blood in any form**, the LORD Himself will **set His face against that person** and cut him off.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It applies to Israelites and to *any foreigner living among them* alike."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will set My face against* is the strongest formula of divine opposition in the book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the life of every creature is in its blood**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated as fact. Not a ritual convention — a claim about what blood is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus says it three times in this short passage, and again in chapter 17's hunting law. See the note on Leviticus 17:13–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD has **given that blood to you upon the altar** for one gracious purpose: **to make atonement for your souls**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the verb: **given**. The blood is described as a gift, assigned to a use."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why it may not be eaten. It has already been allocated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *atonement* is **kaphar** — to cover, to make propitiation. It is the same root as the pitch on Noah's ark and the ransom in Job 33. See the notes on Genesis 6:13–22 and Job 33:23–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is the blood — **the life given in your place** — that covers you and sets you free.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Substitution, stated in the law itself. A life instead of a life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews draws the conclusion: *without shedding of blood there is no remission.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the arrangement could not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same letter says it: *it is not possible that the blood of bulls and goats could take away sins*, which is why the sacrifices had to be repeated *year by year continually.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The system held the place for something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Blood may never be taken in. That is the law, stated absolutely, with a penalty of being cut off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, in an upper room:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Drink from it, all of you. **This is My blood** of the new covenant, poured out for many for the forgiveness of sins.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one command Leviticus will not bend, He bends over a cup — and to the same men who had kept the food laws all their lives. See the note on Matthew 26:26–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost Him to say it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In John 6 He said it plainly in a synagogue: *unless you eat the flesh of the Son of Man and drink His blood, you have no life in you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From that time many of His disciples went back and walked with Him no more.* See the note on John 6:47–51."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter is called.** Leviticus 17 is the chapter of the altar and the blood, and it sits immediately after the Day of Atonement in chapter 16. The book puts the explanation right after the ceremony."}]}, "Leviticus 17:13–14": {"title": "\"The life of every creature is its blood\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A rule about hunting, and inside it a statement about biology that nobody on earth knew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the life of every creature is its blood** — its blood is its very life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said three times in two verses. Leviticus does not usually repeat itself like that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nephesh** — life, soul, the living self. The life of the flesh is in the blood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it was written"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Around fifteen centuries before Christ."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Physicians did not understand what blood does until William Harvey worked out the circulation in 1628, and bleeding remained standard medical practice for another two hundred years after that. George Washington was bled to death by his doctors in 1799 — several pints, for a throat infection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Book said where the life was, and it was ignored for three thousand years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He must **pour out its blood and cover it with earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pour it out. Not consume it, not use it, not keep it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For any animal taken in hunting, the blood goes into the ground and dirt goes over it. The life goes back where it came from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the rule exists"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter says it a few verses earlier: *I have given it to you upon the altar to make atonement for your souls, for it is the blood that makes atonement for the soul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the reason blood is not food. It has been assigned to something else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Life, poured out, for a life. The whole sacrificial system runs on the sentence in verse 14, and the sentence in verse 14 happens also to be true anatomy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it points to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Without the shedding of blood there is no remission.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the reason the sacrifices had to be repeated is in the same passage: *the blood of bulls and goats* could not take sins away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One life was worth the exchange, and Leviticus is holding the place for it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one exception.** The blood of every creature is to be poured out and never taken in. And then, at a table in an upper room: *drink from it, all of you. This is My blood of the new covenant.* The one command Leviticus never bends, He bends over a cup."}]}, "Leviticus 2:1–3": {"title": "Fine flour, oil, and frankincense", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The one offering in Leviticus with no blood in it, and every element is specified."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What goes in"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**fine flour** — the Hebrew is *solet*, the finest grade, sifted until there is no coarseness left anywhere in it. Nothing is uneven.", "**oil** — poured over it and mixed through it", "**frankincense** — the fragrant resin, laid on top"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the two things that are excluded, a few verses later: no yeast, and no honey. Nothing that ferments, and nothing that sweetens artificially."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The memorial portion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The priest takes a handful of the flour and oil, together with **all** the frankincense, and burns this memorial portion on the altar.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A handful of the flour goes into the fire — and all of the incense. The fragrant part is entirely God's; none of it is kept back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest is food"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The rest of the grain offering belongs to Aaron and his sons.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the shape of this offering: a portion given, and the remainder becoming a meal for the priests. It is the only sacrifice in which the bulk of the gift feeds people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A grain offering was the produce of ordinary work — a farmer's crop, ground and brought. There is no death in it and no atonement language attached."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set among the sin offering and the burnt offering, it stands for something different: a life given over, sifted fine, with oil through it and a fragrance that rises."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the details are worth reading.** Christian readers have long seen a portrait here — the flour with no unevenness in it, the oil mixed all the way through, no leaven and no artificial sweetness, and the whole of the fragrance going up to God. The chapter never says so. It simply specifies the recipe, and the recipe is exact."}]}, "Leviticus 2:11–13": {"title": "The salt of the covenant", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two things are kept off the altar and one thing is never allowed to be missing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is excluded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No grain offering you bring to the LORD is to be made with **yeast**, for **you must not burn any yeast or honey** as an offering by fire to the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Yeast and honey. Both are ordinary, both are good, and both are barred from the fire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why these two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have one thing in common: they both ferment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Yeast works through dough by spreading, and Scripture uses it that way throughout — *a little leaven leavens the whole lump*, in two of Paul's letters, and *beware of the leaven of the Pharisees*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Honey ferments as well, and sours in heat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both change on their own, and neither of them keeps."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are still good for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You may bring these as **an offering of firstfruits**, but they are **not to be burned on the altar** as a pleasing fragrance.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They can be brought. They cannot go on the fire."}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Pentecost, the wave loaves were *baked with leaven* — two of them, waved before the LORD. Leaven appears in the offering that represents the people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not on the altar; in the hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The requirement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Season **every** grain offering with salt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is emphatic. Every one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name given to it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Never leave out **the salt of the covenant of your God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the salt of seasoning. The salt of the covenant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What a salt covenant was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that part of the world, sharing salt made an agreement binding. The phrase turns up twice more:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["Numbers, of the priests' portion: *it is **a covenant of salt** forever before the LORD.*", "2 Chronicles, of David's throne: *the LORD God of Israel gave the dominion over Israel to David forever, to him and his sons, **by a covenant of salt**.*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both times it means unbreakable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What salt does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The opposite of what yeast and honey do. Salt does not spread, ferment or change. It preserves, it stops decay, and it does not go off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two agents of change are kept off the altar and the agent of permanence is required on every offering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction repeated"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **all** your offerings, bring salt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus says it three ways in one verse — *season with salt*, *never leave out*, *with all your offerings*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the picture goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You are the salt of the earth**; but if the salt loses its flavour, how shall it be seasoned?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Paul: *let your speech always be with grace, **seasoned with salt**.* See the note on Colossians 4:5–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where else it is required"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the incense: *seasoned with salt, pure and holy*. See the note on Exodus 30:34–38."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offering on the bronze altar and the incense on the golden one both have it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the rule amounts to.** Nothing that ferments goes on the fire, and nothing goes on the fire without the thing that keeps. Leviticus attaches the word *covenant* to the salt and to nothing else in the chapter."}]}, "Leviticus 2:4–10": {"title": "Oven, griddle, pan", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The same offering, three ways of cooking it, and the oil is in all three."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three methods"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**baked in an oven** — loaves or thin wafers, the oil mixed in or spread on", "**cooked on a griddle** — an open plate over the fire, the hottest and most exposed method. This one is *broken into pieces with oil poured over it.*", "**prepared in a pan** — a covered vessel, the gentlest of the three"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Household kitchens had all three. Whatever equipment a family owned, there was a version of the offering they could bring."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one that is broken"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The griddle offering is the only one with the breaking specified: **broken into pieces**, and then oil poured on again afterwards."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fiercest heat, the most exposure, and the oil applied a second time — after the breaking rather than only before it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is constant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fine flour in every case. Oil in every case. No yeast in any of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only the fire changes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The memorial portion again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He lifts out the memorial portion and burns it on the altar — a fragrance that pleases the LORD. The rest belongs to Aaron and his sons.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same pattern as the uncooked version: a portion into the fire, the remainder feeding the priests. However it was prepared, it ended up as somebody's meal."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the oil.** In Scripture oil is the standing figure for the Spirit — priests and kings were anointed with it, and the word *Messiah* means the anointed one. In this chapter the oil is mixed in, spread on, and poured over, and it is never optional. Whatever the fire does to the offering, the oil is already through it."}]}, "Leviticus 25:1–7": {"title": "The Sabbath year", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Every seventh year, an agricultural nation was commanded to stop farming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it was given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On **Mount Sinai** the LORD said to Moses.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus notes the location, which it rarely does. This is given at the mountain, before they have a field to rest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it starts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When you **come into the land I am giving you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The command has no application in the wilderness. It waits for the land."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose Sabbath it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **land itself** is to keep a Sabbath **to the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land is the subject. It is not a rest for the farmer, though he gets one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *a sabbath of sabbaths* in verse 4 — the same intensive used of the Day of Atonement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The six"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For six years **sow your fields, prune your vineyards, and gather what they yield**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three activities, listed so the seventh year's prohibitions can match them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seventh"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the seventh year is **a Sabbath of complete rest** for the land.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not sow your fields or prune your vineyards. **Do not reap what grows on its own** or gather the grapes of your untended vines.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not only no planting. No harvesting of what comes up by itself either."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anything the ground produced that year was not to be brought in and stored."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A farmer stops in year seven. The last harvest was in year six. He has to eat through year seven, and then through year eight until the harvest he plants in year eight comes in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is roughly two years of food from one crop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter says about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Later in the same chapter God raises the objection Himself: *and if you say, \"What shall we eat in the seventh year, since we shall not sow nor gather in our produce?\" **Then I will command My blessing on you in the sixth year, and it will bring forth produce enough for three years**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three, not two. The provision is stated with a margin."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who eats in the seventh year"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever the land brings forth in that Sabbath **will still feed you** — **you and your servants and hired workers, and the foreigner living among you, and your livestock and the wild animals** of the land.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six categories, in descending order of status, ending with wild animals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever grows is unowned. Exodus puts it plainly: *let it rest and lie fallow, **so the poor among your people may eat from it***. See the note on Exodus 23:10–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For one year in seven, the fences come down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened when it was ignored"}, {"k": "p", "t": "2 Chronicles, on the exile: *and they went to Babylon, **until the land had enjoyed her sabbaths**. As long as she lay desolate she kept Sabbath, to fulfil seventy years.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventy years of exile, and the chronicler counts them as the sabbaths owed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah's seventy years, which Daniel was reading when Gabriel came. See the note on Daniel 9:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes after seven sevens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The jubilee — the fiftieth year, when debts were released, slaves went free and land returned to the family that had lost it. The rest of Leviticus 25 is about it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah calls it *the year of the LORD's favour*, and Jesus read that line in Nazareth and said *today*. See the note on Isaiah 61:1–3."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the command asks.** That a farming people put down their tools for a year and eat from a harvest gathered two summers earlier — and the reason given is that the ground belongs to somebody."}]}, "Leviticus 25:23–28": {"title": "The land is Mine", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A property law, and the reason given for it removes the concept of freehold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The land is **not to be sold for good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **litzmituth** — in perpetuity, permanently, beyond recall."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **the land is Mine**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ki-li ha'aretz**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words. Everything in the chapter follows from them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The status"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are **only foreigners and guests on it, living there as My tenants**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **gerim vetoshavim immadi** — sojourners and settlers with Me."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ger** — a resident alien. **Toshav** — a temporary resident."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same words used of Abraham buying a burial plot: *I am a foreigner and a visitor among you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that does to the market"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no permanent sale of land in Israel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is transacted is the crops until the next Jubilee, and the price is calculated on the number of harvests remaining."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The provision"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So throughout the land you hold, you must **always allow for the land to be redeemed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ge'ullah titnu la'aretz** — you shall grant redemption for the land."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first route"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If a fellow Israelite becomes poor and sells some of their property, **their nearest relative is to come and buy back what was sold**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **go'alo haqqarov elav** — his redeemer, the one near to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Go'el** — the kinsman-redeemer. The one with both the family right and the means."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the go'el did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bought back sold land. Bought back a relative sold into slavery. Avenged a killed relative. And in one book, married a widow to raise up a name on the dead man's inheritance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second route"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If someone has **no one to redeem it but later prospers** and can buy it back themselves, they are to **figure the years since the sale**, repay the balance, and return to their own property.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The arithmetic is prescribed. Not a negotiation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But if they cannot repay it, what was sold **stays in the buyer's hands until the Year of Jubilee**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Jubilee"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the Jubilee **it is released, and the seller returns to their own property**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fiftieth year. *And you shall proclaim liberty throughout all the land to all its inhabitants.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **deror** — release, liberty. The word inscribed on the Liberty Bell."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every fiftieth year, land returned to its original family, whatever had happened in between."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the system prevents"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Permanent dispossession. A family could fall, but not permanently — the clock was always running toward the reset."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the go'el turns up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Boaz is our relative**, one of our near kinsmen.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at the gate, with ten elders sitting, the nearer kinsman who declines: *I cannot redeem it for myself, lest I ruin my own inheritance*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Boaz took Ruth and she became his wife.* See the note on Ruth 4:9–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book of Ruth is this law, worked out in a story."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Job says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **I know that my Redeemer lives**, and He shall stand at last on the earth.* See the note on Job 19:25–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **go'ali** — my kinsman-redeemer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah calls Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Thus says the LORD, your Redeemer, the Holy One of Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The title appears thirteen times in Isaiah."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus reads in Nazareth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To proclaim **the acceptable year of the LORD**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 61, which draws on Jubilee language, read aloud in a synagogue — and *today this Scripture is fulfilled in your hearing*. See the note on Luke 4:16–21."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two words.** *The land is Mine.* Everything in the chapter — the redemption right, the arithmetic, the fiftieth-year reset — follows from a statement about ownership."}]}, "Leviticus 26:3–13": {"title": "Still eating last year's harvest", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The blessing half of the covenant, and it ends somewhere other than where it started."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If you live by My ways and hold to My commands**, here is what I will pour out on you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **im-bechuqqotai teleku** — if you walk in My statutes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole section is conditional, and the second half of the chapter — thirty verses of it — sets out what happens otherwise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Rain in its season**, so the land gives its harvest and **the trees hang heavy with fruit**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Canaan there was no Nile. Everything depended on rain arriving in October and again in March."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A land of hills and valleys, which drinks water from the rain of heaven.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The overlap"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your **threshing will run right up to the grape harvest**, and the grape harvest right up to planting time.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Threshing is in May. Grapes come in September. Planting is October."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise is that each harvest will be so large it is still being processed when the next begins. The agricultural year will have no gap in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two results"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will eat your fill and sleep safe in your land**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Food and security, which is what everyone in the ancient world wanted and few had."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The peace"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will give you peace: you will lie down with nothing to fear**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shalom**, and **ve'eyn macharid** — with none making afraid."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase recurs through the prophets: *they shall sit every man under his vine and under his fig tree, and no one shall make them afraid*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The beasts and the sword"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will **clear the dangerous beasts** from the land, and **no sword will pass through it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two dangers, one natural and one human."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You will chase off your enemies — **a handful of you routing armies**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse spells it out: *five of you shall chase a hundred, and a hundred of you shall put ten thousand to flight*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five against a hundred is twenty to one. A hundred against ten thousand is a hundred to one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ratio improves as the number grows, which is not how arithmetic works, and the text lets it stand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will turn toward you**, make you fruitful, multiply you, and **keep My covenant with you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **uphaniti aleichem** — and I will turn My face toward you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same idiom as the priestly blessing: *the LORD lift up His countenance upon you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The overflow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You will still be **eating last year's harvest when you have to clear it out to make room for the new**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yashan noshan** — old, grown old. Doubled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not last year's. Older than that. The problem described is storage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The best of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**And best of all: I will make My home among you**, and **I will not turn away from you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **venatati mishkani betochechem** — I will set My dwelling among you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Mishkan** — the tabernacle. The word for the tent itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the list has been building to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rain, harvest, safety, sleep, victory, storage problems — and then the sentence that says *and best of all*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The material blessings are real and stated at length. The chapter ranks them anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The formula"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will walk with you — I will be your God, and you will be My people**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The covenant sentence, in its full form. It appears from Genesis to Revelation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold, the tabernacle of God is with men, and He will dwell with them, and they shall be His people.* See the note on Revelation 21:3–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He reminds them of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am the LORD your God, **who brought you out of Egypt so you would no longer be slaves**. **I broke the bars of the yoke off your shoulders** and made you **walk with your head held high**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **va'olek etchem qomemiyut** — and I made you walk upright."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter of conditions ends by naming something already done, unconditionally, before any of it was asked."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The ranking.** Rain, harvests too big to store, sleep without fear — and then *and best of all*, which turns out to be a tent pitched in the middle of the camp."}]}, "Leviticus 27:1–8": {"title": "Never more than the giver can bear", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last chapter of Leviticus is a price list, and the last verse cancels it for anyone who cannot pay."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When someone makes **a special vow to dedicate a person to the LORD**, and then wishes to **buy that vow back** with a set price.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yaphli neder** — makes a special, extraordinary vow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody dedicates a person — themselves, a child, a household member — to sanctuary service, and then needs to redeem the vow with money instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hannah did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will give him to the LORD all the days of his life.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She did not redeem it. Samuel stayed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The schedule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Twenty to sixty**: a man at fifty shekels, a woman at thirty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Five to twenty**: twenty for a boy, ten for a girl."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**One month to five years**: five for a boy, three for a girl."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sixty and over**: fifteen for a man, ten for a woman."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the numbers are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a valuation of the person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the redemption price for a vow — what it costs to substitute money for service. The figures track roughly what that person's labour would have been worth at the sanctuary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prime working years cost most to buy out because they were worth most in work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The elderly"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fifteen for a man over sixty, against fifty in his prime."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Less than a third."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The system is measuring capacity to labour, and it says plainly that capacity falls."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The standard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Weighed by **the sanctuary standard**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **beshekel haqqodesh** — the shekel of the holy place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An official weight kept at the sanctuary, so nobody could argue about the scales. Proverbs returns repeatedly to false weights."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But if the one who made the vow is **too poor to pay the set amount**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mach hu me'erkecha** — he is poor, below your valuation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They are to **bring the person before the priest**, and the priest will **set a value they can actually afford**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **al-pi asher tassig yad hannoder** — according to what the hand of the vower can reach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Never more than the giver can bear.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fixed schedule is fixed for everybody except the person who cannot meet it, and for them the priest assesses individually."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that does to the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight verses of tariff, and the last one says the tariff is not the last word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same provision runs through Leviticus. The sin offering is a bull, or a goat, or *if he is not able to bring a lamb, then he shall bring two turtledoves or two young pigeons*, or *if he is not able to bring two turtledoves, then a tenth of an ephah of fine flour*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is priced out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mary and Joseph brought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And to offer a sacrifice according to what is said in the law of the Lord, \"**A pair of turtledoves or two young pigeons**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The poor person's offering, brought for the child in the temple."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the book ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These are the commandments which the LORD commanded Moses for the children of Israel on Mount Sinai.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last sentence of Leviticus, three verses after the clause about the man who cannot pay."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last verse.** Eight verses of fixed prices, and then: *the priest will set a value they can actually afford*. The schedule binds everyone except the person it would exclude."}]}, "Leviticus 3:1–5": {"title": "The offering everybody eats", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The third of the offerings, and the only one shared three ways."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **peace offering** — a gift that celebrates peace with God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shelamim**, plural, from **shalom**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not peace as the absence of hostility. **Shalom** is wholeness, completeness, nothing missing. The plural form makes it emphatic."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The older name for it is the *sacrifice of peace offerings*, and Leviticus 7 divides it into three occasions: thanksgiving, a vow, and simple freewill."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is unusual about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whether **male or female**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The burnt offering had to be male and unblemished. See the note on Leviticus 1:3–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This one may be either. It is the only sacrifice in the list where the sex of the animal is left open."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition kept"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let it be **without flaw** before the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tamim**. The one requirement that is never relaxed anywhere in Leviticus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lay your hand on its head.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same **samakh** — pressing down with weight. Identification, before anything is killed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is put to death **at the entrance of the Tent of Meeting**, and Aaron's sons **sprinkle its blood** against the sides of the altar.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same door, same blood on the same sides. The procedure up to this point is identical to the burnt offering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What goes on the fire"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **fat that covers the inner parts**, both **kidneys with the fat around them**, and the **covering of the liver**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for fat is **chelev**. In Hebrew idiom it stands for the best of a thing — *the fat of the land*, *the finest of the wheat* is literally *the fat of the wheat*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only the interior fat goes up. Nothing on the outside of the animal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rule is stated flatly a few verses later: *all the fat is the LORD's*, and *you shall eat neither fat nor blood*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is placed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the altar, **on top of the burnt offering that is on the wood above the fire**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The peace offering is laid on the burnt offering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is not negotiable, and it is the same order as the days: the burnt offering underneath, everything else stacked on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A gift by fire, **a fragrance that pleases the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase as the burnt offering. **Reach nichoach** — a restful smell."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who eats it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God gets the fat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priest gets the breast and the right thigh — *the wave breast and the heave shoulder*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the man who brought it eats the rest, with his household, in front of the tabernacle. Leviticus 7 says a thanksgiving peace offering must be eaten the same day, and nothing left till morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means it was a meal, for a family, that had to be finished, so people were invited."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only offering where the worshipper eats any of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three parties at the table"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God, the priest, and the man. All three receive from the same animal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what makes it the peace offering, and the reason it is the one with a feast attached."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the peace comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, having been **justified by faith, we have peace with God** through our Lord Jesus Christ.* See the note on Romans 5:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *having made peace through the blood of His cross*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Righteousness first, then peace. The same order as Melchizedek's two titles. See the note on Hebrews 7:1–2."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The three-way split.** God takes the fat, the priest takes the breast and thigh, and the man who brought it eats the rest — the only sacrifice the worshipper gets a meal out of."}]}, "Leviticus 3:12–17": {"title": "All the fat belongs to the LORD", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The third option for a peace offering, and then a rule that outlives the tabernacle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three grades"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter allows a bull, a sheep, or a goat. This is the third."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The procedure is identical in all three, which is the point of repeating it: the animal changes and nothing else does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lay your hand on its head.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Samakh** — pressing down. See the note on Leviticus 3:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The portions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **fat covering the inner parts**, both **kidneys** with the fat around them, and the **covering of the liver**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same list, third time. Leviticus is a manual and does not vary its wording."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The priest burns these on the altar **as food**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lechem** — bread, food."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offering is called God's food. Nothing in the Old Testament suggests He needs it — Psalm 50 is blunt about that: *if I were hungry, I would not tell you; for the world is Mine, and all its fullness*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The term describes the table, not an appetite."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All the fat belongs to the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kol-chelev laYHWH**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chelev** — fat, and by extension the best of anything. *The fat of the land*, and Genesis uses it of the best of Egypt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is a **lasting rule for every generation, wherever you live**: you are **not to eat any fat or any blood**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *a perpetual statute throughout your generations in all your dwellings*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not only at the sanctuary. Wherever they lived, including centuries later in Babylon, and including now."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why blood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the life of the flesh is in the blood, and I have given it to you upon the altar to make atonement for your souls; for it is the blood that makes atonement for the soul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus 17 explains that one at length. The blood is reserved because of what it does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why fat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text does not explain, and the association with **chelev** as *the best* is the usual reading: the choicest part is not eaten by the worshipper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever else the peace offering shares out — and it shares more than any other — the best of it is not on the human side of the table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is left over"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The peace offering is the only sacrifice the worshipper eats. God takes the fat, the priest takes the breast and the right thigh, and the man takes the rest home. See the note on Leviticus 7:28–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rule about fat is what marks the boundary of his portion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The time limit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If the sacrifice of his offering is a vow or a voluntary offering, it shall be eaten the same day… but on the third day the remainder shall be burned with fire.* See the note on Leviticus 7:11–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A thanksgiving peace offering had to be finished the same day, which meant a whole animal and one evening — so the family invited people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the phrase goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they shall eat the fat of the land*, said of Egypt. And the prodigal's father: *bring the fatted calf here and kill it*. See the note on Luke 15:21–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew idiom for the best of something is the word this chapter reserves."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clause with the widest reach.** *Wherever you live.* A rule about the altar, extended to every household in every generation — the best of it, unconditionally, is His."}]}, "Leviticus 4:1–2": {"title": "Here is the way home", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A new offering is introduced, and the case it covers is the one nobody planned."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus 1 to 3 are the burnt offering, the grain offering and the peace offering — all voluntary, all bringing something to God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 4 begins the required ones. The mood changes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The formula"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD said to Moses, \"**Speak to the Israelites and tell them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The standard opening. Leviticus is almost entirely direct speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**When someone sins unintentionally**, doing what the LORD has commanded not to be done.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **bishgagah** — in error, by straying, inadvertently."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Shagah** — to wander, to go astray. The verb used of a drunk staggering and of sheep wandering off."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it covers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not defiance. Sin committed without knowing it was sin at the time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Later in the chapter: *when the sin which they have committed **becomes known to them***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offering is for the moment somebody finds out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not covered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the person who does anything presumptuously… **that one brings reproach on the LORD, and he shall be cut off from among his people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **beyad ramah** — with a high hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers 15 states it. Defiant sin has no offering prescribed in the system."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this needed legislating"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The other offerings assume the worshipper is bringing something. This one assumes he has just discovered something."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The category is unsettling in a way the voluntary offerings are not: it means a person could be guilty without knowing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Here is **the way home**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew introduces the procedure that follows — the bull, the hand on the head, the blood, the fat, the burning outside the camp."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The translation renders the function of the instruction rather than the syntax: what follows is the route back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four cases"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter runs through them in order of seriousness by office."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The anointed priest** — a young bull, blood sprinkled seven times before the veil. See the note on Leviticus 4:3–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The whole congregation** — a young bull, the elders laying hands on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A ruler** — a male goat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A common person** — a female goat or a lamb."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction of the scale"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The higher the office, the heavier the offering, and the further in the blood goes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priest's blood goes inside the tent, to the veil and the incense altar. The common person's goes on the horns of the outer altar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **lays his hand on its head**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vesamak yado** — and he shall lean his hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Samak** — to press, to lean weight on. The same verb used of Moses laying hands on Joshua."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Outside the camp"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priest's bull is burned entirely outside the camp, *in a clean place, where the ashes are poured out*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore Jesus also, that He might sanctify the people with His own blood, **suffered outside the gate**.* See the note on Hebrews 13:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews builds an argument on where the carcass was taken."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word from the cross"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Father, forgive them, **for they do not know what they do**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the definition in this verse, spoken over the people carrying it out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about his own case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Although I was formerly a blasphemer, a persecutor, and an insolent man; but **I obtained mercy because I did it ignorantly in unbelief**.* See the note on 1 Timothy 1:12–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts himself in this chapter's category."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word.** **Shagah** — to wander, to stagger, to stray. The offering is not for defiance; it is for what a person did before they knew, and it starts the moment they find out."}]}, "Leviticus 4:3–12": {"title": "When the anointed priest sins", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The heaviest offering in the chapter is for the man who performs the offerings."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If **the anointed priest sins and brings guilt on the people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hakkohen hammashiach** — the anointed priest. The high priest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Le'ashmat ha'am** — to the guilt of the people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His failure is not private. The chapter says so before it says anything else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The animal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He must bring to the LORD **a young bull without flaw**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same animal required for the sin of the whole congregation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One man, one nation, same offering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He brings it to the entrance of the Tent of Meeting, **lays his hand on its head**, and it is put to death before the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vesamak** — pressed his hand down on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priest kills it himself. There is no one above him to do it for him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blood inside"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The priest **takes some of the blood into the Tent of Meeting**, dips his finger in it, and **sprinkles it seven times before the LORD, in front of the curtain**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven — the number of completion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Toward the veil, behind which is the ark. Blood is brought as far in as anyone may go on an ordinary day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The horns"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He puts some of the blood on **the horns of the altar of fragrant incense**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The incense altar stood immediately in front of the veil. It was where the priest burned incense morning and evening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The horns were the projections at its corners, and the place a fugitive grabbed for sanctuary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pouring"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **pours out the rest at the base of the altar of burnt offering** at the Tent's entrance.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Most of the blood goes on the ground at the door, where nobody sees it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He removes **the fat of the sin offering** — the fat covering the inner parts, both kidneys with the fat around them, and the covering of the liver — and **burns them on the altar**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fat and the organs were considered the richest part."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All the fat is the LORD's* — the standing rule, from chapter 3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The remainder"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the rest of the bull — **its hide, all its flesh, its head and legs, its inner parts and refuse**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The full carcass, itemised so nothing can be quietly retained."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In most offerings the priest ate a portion. Not this one. He gets nothing from the animal that covers his own sin."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Outside"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **carries outside the camp to a clean place**, where the ashes are poured out, and **burns it there on a wood fire**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **el-mitzchutz lammachaneh** — to outside the camp."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two burnings, in two places. The fat on the altar, and the body outside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the bodies of those animals, whose blood is brought into the sanctuary by the high priest for sin, are burned outside the camp**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore Jesus also, that He might sanctify the people with His own blood, **suffered outside the gate**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore let us go forth to Him, outside the camp, bearing His reproach.* See the note on Hebrews 13:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole argument rests on the geography of this paragraph."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Golgotha was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Outside the wall. John records it: *and He, bearing His cross, went out to a place called the Place of a Skull*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The high priest's own sin"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Day of Atonement makes the same point: *Aaron shall offer the bull as a sin offering, **which is for himself**, and make atonement for himself and for his house*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He cannot cover anyone until he is covered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who does not need daily, as those high priests, to offer up sacrifices, **first for His own sins** and then for the people's, for **this He did once for all when He offered up Himself**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The geography.** The blood goes as far in as anyone may go. The body goes outside the camp. Hebrews builds its argument on the second half."}]}, "Leviticus 5:14–19": {"title": "Guilty without knowing it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An offering for the sins nobody noticed committing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The category"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If someone **acts unfaithfully and sins unintentionally** against any of the **LORD's holy things**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ma'al** — treachery — combined with **bishgagah**, in error, by straying."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both words in one clause. Treachery done by accident."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the holy things were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anything set apart for the sanctuary: tithes, firstfruits, dedicated property, the offerings themselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eating what belonged to the priests, or using a dedicated animal for ordinary work. Things a man could do in a week without a thought."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He must bring to the LORD a **ram without flaw** from the flock, **of the proper value in silver, by the sanctuary weight**, as a **guilt offering**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **asham** — the guilt or trespass offering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only sacrifice with a price attached. The animal had to be assessed in shekels before it could be accepted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repayment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **makes good for the wrong** he did against the holy thing, **adding a fifth of its value**, and gives it to the priest.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same rate as the property law: full value plus twenty per cent. See the note on Leviticus 6:1–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 5 is for what was taken from God. Chapter 6 is for what was taken from a neighbour. The arithmetic is identical."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If someone **sins by doing what the LORD forbids, even without knowing it, he is still guilty and bears his responsibility**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *though he does not know it, yet he is guilty and shall bear his iniquity*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ignorance is stated and does not remove liability. Leviticus does not argue the point; it just says it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is unusual about the second case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No restitution is required, because nobody can price what was not identified."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man brings the ram anyway. He is not told what he did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is a guilt offering, for **he was truly guilty before the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew doubles the root: *guilting he was guilty*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David prayed about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who can understand his errors? **Cleanse me from secret faults**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *secret* is **nistarot** — hidden things, and it can mean hidden from the man himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which offering Isaiah names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When You make His soul **an offering for sin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew there is **asham** — this offering, from this chapter. See the note on Isaiah 53:10–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five kinds of sacrifice in Leviticus, and the one Isaiah picks for the Servant is the one that comes with a valuation and a restitution clause."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the New Testament does with the unwitting sin"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Father, forgive them, **for they do not know what they do**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Paul: *I obtained mercy because **I did it ignorantly** in unbelief*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Peter to Jerusalem: *I know that you did it in ignorance, as did also your rulers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times, the ignorance is stated — and every time it is a reason given for mercy rather than an argument that nothing happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence repeated"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **he is forgiven**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice in six verses, both times passive, both times at the end of the paragraph."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in either case depends on the man working out what he did."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The hardest clause in the chapter.** *Though he does not know it, yet he is guilty* — and the remedy given is an animal with a price on it, brought by a man who could not name the offence."}]}, "Leviticus 6:1–7": {"title": "Full value, plus a fifth", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A law about property, and it starts by calling the offence something else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is sinned against"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If someone sins and acts **unfaithfully against the LORD** by **deceiving a neighbor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ma'al** — treachery, breach of trust. The word used of Achan's theft at Jericho and of Uzziah entering the temple."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The victim is the neighbour. The party named as offended is God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five ways"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*About something **entrusted to him**, or **left in his care**, or **stolen**, or by **cheating his neighbor**, or by **finding lost property and lying about it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five, and only one of them is theft in the ordinary sense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest are: a deposit denied, a pledge withheld, extortion, and keeping something found."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each one is deniable. Every item on the list is a wrong that could be argued about in court."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Swearing falsely about any such sin.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what turns it from a civil matter into a trespass against the LORD. He is invoked as witness to the lie."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The restitution"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He must **return what he took**… he must **pay it back in full, add a fifth of its value**, and give it all to the owner.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundred per cent, plus twenty. The Hebrew is *its fifth he shall add to it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The owner ends up better off than before the theft."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On **the day he brings his guilt offering**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The payment and the sacrifice happen on the same day, and the neighbour is paid first in the order of the sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus says the same thing about the altar: *if you bring your gift to the altar, and there remember that your brother has something against you, leave your gift there… first be reconciled to your brother, and then come and offer your gift*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **ram without flaw** from the flock, **of the proper value**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for the offering is **asham** — the guilt offering, or trespass offering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the one sacrifice with a valuation attached. The priest assessed the animal in shekels."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name Isaiah uses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When You make His soul **an offering for sin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew there is **asham** — this exact offering. See the note on Isaiah 53:10–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of all five Levitical sacrifices, the one Isaiah 53 names for the Servant is the one that comes with restitution attached."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it says at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The priest **makes things right for him** before the LORD, and **he is forgiven** for whatever he did that made him guilty.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *and it shall be forgiven him* — a passive. Nothing is said about what he feels afterwards."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who initiates it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole procedure assumes a confession. Numbers 5 makes it explicit: *when a man or woman commits any sin… then he shall confess the sin which he has committed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody catches him. He comes forward, having sworn an oath that he did not do it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other restitution law"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus has the sliding scale for livestock — five oxen for an ox, four sheep for a sheep, double if it is found alive in his hand. See the note on Exodus 22:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Leviticus rate is lower, and the difference is the confession. A thief who is caught pays four or five times over; a thief who comes forward pays the value plus a fifth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The man who did it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zacchaeus, standing in his own house: *if I have taken anything from anyone by false accusation, **I restore fourfold***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He picks the higher rate voluntarily, and Jesus' response is *today salvation has come to this house*. See the note on Luke 19:1–10."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the extra fifth is for.** Not the court, and not the priest. The Hebrew says it goes *to the one to whom it belongs* — the neighbour ends the transaction holding more than was taken."}]}, "Leviticus 6:24–30": {"title": "Whatever touches it becomes holy", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The handling instructions for the sin offering, and one line in them runs the wrong way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Tell **Aaron and his sons** the instructions for the sin offering.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapters 1 to 5 tell the people what to bring. Chapter 6 onward tells the priests what to do with it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **torat ha-chattat** — the law, or instruction, of the sin offering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is to be **slaughtered before the LORD in the place where the burnt offering is slain**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same spot as the offering that is entirely burnt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sin offering is not handled apart from the rest, or in some lesser corner."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grade"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is **most holy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **qodesh qodashim** — holy of holies, the same phrase as the innermost room of the tabernacle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offering for sin is given the highest classification in the system."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who eats it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The priest who offers it **eats it in a holy place**, in the courtyard of the Tent of Meeting.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priest eats the sin offering. Hosea makes the point bitterly of a corrupt priesthood: *they eat up the sin of My people*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence that runs the wrong way"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever **touches its flesh becomes holy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *everything that touches its flesh shall be holy*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everywhere else in the Levitical system, contact runs the other direction. Touch a corpse and you are unclean; touch a leper and you are unclean; the uncleanness travels."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here the flesh of the sin offering makes what touches it holy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Haggai puts the general rule as a question to the priests: if a man carries holy meat in his garment and the garment touches bread, does the bread become holy? *No.* And if one unclean from a corpse touches any of these, is it unclean? *It shall be unclean.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one exception in the Torah is this offering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If any **blood spatters on a garment**, it must be **washed in a holy place**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not discarded. Washed, and washed inside the sanctuary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pots"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **clay pot** in which it is boiled **must be broken**; if it is boiled in a **bronze pot**, that pot must be **scoured and rinsed** with water.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Earthenware is porous and cannot be purged, so it is destroyed. Bronze can be scrubbed, so it is kept."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul: *we have this treasure in **earthen vessels**, that the excellence of the power may be of God and not of us*. See the note on 2 Corinthians 4:6–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who may eat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every **male among the priests** may eat of it; it is most holy.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **no sin offering whose blood is brought into the Tent of Meeting** to make things right in the sanctuary **may be eaten; it must be burned with fire**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If the blood went inside, the body goes outside and is burnt. That is the Day of Atonement arrangement, and the bull for the priest's own sin."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews does with that clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the bodies of those animals, whose blood is brought into the sanctuary by the high priest for sin, are **burned outside the camp**. Therefore Jesus also, that He might sanctify the people with His own blood, **suffered outside the gate**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *let us go forth to Him, outside the camp, bearing His reproach.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole argument rests on the last verse of this chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the contagion goes in the Gospels"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A leper is touched and is cleansed. A bier is touched and the boy sits up. A woman with a twelve-year haemorrhage takes hold of the hem and *the fountain of her blood was dried up*. See the note on Mark 5:30–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of those contacts should have transmitted uncleanness under the law, and every one runs the other way."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line that reverses the system.** *Whatever touches its flesh becomes holy.* Everywhere else in Leviticus contamination travels by contact — and at this one offering the traffic goes the other direction."}]}, "Leviticus 7:1–10": {"title": "What the priest keeps", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The section of Leviticus that deals with wages."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose instructions these are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapters 1 to 5 tell the worshipper what to bring. From chapter 6 the manual turns to the priests. See the note on Leviticus 6:24–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This paragraph is about what they live on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grade"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is **most holy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **qodesh qodashim** — holy of holies."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The guilt offering gets the highest classification, and so does the sin offering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The animal is slaughtered **in the place where the burnt offering is slain**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "North side of the altar. The same ground for all of them, and no lesser corner for the offerings about failure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What burns"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **fat tail**, the fat covering the inner parts, both **kidneys** with the fat around them, and the **covering of the liver**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fat tail is specific to the broad-tailed sheep of the region, which stored fat in the tail — a delicacy, and the best part of the animal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same list as the peace offering. See the note on Leviticus 3:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every male among the priests may eat of it, but it must be **eaten in a holy place**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not taken home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It **belongs to the priest who makes things right with it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the priest who makes atonement with it shall have it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who does the work is the man who eats."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hide"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The priest who offers a burnt offering for anyone **keeps the hide** of that offering for himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the burnt offering everything goes on the fire — except the skin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one part not consumed goes to the priest. Hides were valuable; leather was money."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means the priest's income from a burnt offering was a skin."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the first hides came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Also for Adam and his wife the LORD God **made tunics of skin, and clothed them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had sewn fig leaves. God covered them with something that had died."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The baked and the raw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every grain offering **baked in an oven or prepared in a pan or on a griddle belongs to the priest who offers it**, while every grain offering, whether **mixed with oil or dry, belongs equally to all of Aaron's sons**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two different arrangements. Cooked portions go to the officiating priest; uncooked flour is divided among all of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in it is casual. The book specifies who gets what down to whether the offering had been near a fire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why any of this is in Scripture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because the priests had no land. *The LORD said to Aaron: \"You shall have no inheritance in their land, nor shall you have any portion among them; **I am your portion and your inheritance**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven tribes got territory. The twelfth got the altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul cites the arrangement: *do you not know that those who minister the holy things eat of the things of the temple… even so the Lord has commanded that those who preach the gospel should live from the gospel.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which offering Isaiah names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The guilt offering — **asham**. See the note on Leviticus 5:14–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When You make His soul an offering for sin.* See the note on Isaiah 53:10–12."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the priest got from a whole burnt offering.** The one thing that did not burn. A hide — which is what God used in Genesis to clothe two people who had tried leaves."}]}, "Leviticus 7:11–18": {"title": "\"None may be left until morning\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The peace offering has a shelf life, and it depends on why it was brought."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three occasions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus distinguishes them:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**thanksgiving** — brought because of something received", "**a vow** — brought to fulfil a promise", "**a freewill gift** — brought for no reason at all"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two time limits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The meat of **the thanksgiving** peace offering must be eaten **the same day**; **none may be left until morning**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If the offering is made because of **a vow or as a freewill gift**, it may be eaten on the day it is offered **and on the next day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thanksgiving gets one day. The others get two."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most immediate of the three has the shortest window."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever is left over on the third day **must be burned**. If any meat is eaten on the third day, **it will not be accepted; it counts for nothing** to the one who brought it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **piggul** — foul, refuse, an offensive thing. Strong language for meat that is two days old."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the effect is retroactive: the offering counts for nothing. It does not become a partial credit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the limit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the passage is about hygiene — the vow offering keeps a day longer under the same sun."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The distinction is in the reason for bringing it, and thanksgiving is the one that cannot be carried over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Yesterday's gratitude does not do for today. It has to be brought fresh."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bread that goes with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Loaves of bread **without yeast** mixed with oil, thin wafers spread with oil, and loaves of fine flour well-mixed with oil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three kinds, all unleavened, all with oil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, unexpectedly:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Along with these, present **loaves of bread made with yeast** as your peace offering of thanks.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leaven is excluded from almost every offering in Leviticus — *no grain offering which you bring to the LORD shall be made with leaven.* It is the standing symbol of what corrupts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the peace offering it is commanded. Bread as people actually bake it, brought to the same table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who eats what"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One of each kind is presented as a contribution to the LORD; **it belongs to the priest who sprinkles the blood**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priest who handled the blood gets the bread. See the note on Leviticus 7:28–34."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the offering is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Shelamim** — from **shalom**. The one sacrifice in the system the worshipper sat down and ate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was not brought to establish peace. It was eaten by somebody who already had it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul says about the leaven.** *Purge out the old leaven… **for indeed Christ, our Passover, was sacrificed for us.*** He uses it the usual way. And yet Leviticus puts a leavened loaf on the thanksgiving table — real bread, from a real kitchen, alongside the unleavened."}]}, "Leviticus 7:28–34": {"title": "The breast and the thigh", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two cuts of meat, and the instruction is specific about which two."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the offerer brings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **his own hands** he brings the offerings made by fire — the fat, together with the breast.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew stresses it: his own hands. Nobody carries it in for him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wave"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **waves the breast before the LORD as a wave offering**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **tenuphah** — a waving, a moving to and fro. The priest and the offerer held it and moved it horizontally before the altar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two portions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The breast belongs to **Aaron and his sons**… You are to give **the right thigh** to the priest as a contribution.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The breast** — the front of the animal, over the heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The right thigh** — the shoulder and haunch, the strongest part of the beast."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heart-part and the strength-part, and both go to the priest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which offering this is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **peace offering** — Hebrew **shelamim**, from **shalom**. It is the only sacrifice in the system that the person bringing it got to eat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The burnt offering went up entirely. The sin offering went to the priest or outside the camp. This one is a meal: God receives the fat, the priest receives the breast and thigh, and the offerer eats the rest with his family."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three parties at one table, over one animal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peace offering, and it is not brought to make peace. Leviticus places it after the burnt and grain offerings, and it is the sacrifice of somebody at peace already — brought for thanksgiving, or a vow, or freely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The time limit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The meat of **the thanksgiving** peace offering must be eaten **the same day** it is offered; **none may be left until morning**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A vow or freewill offering could be eaten on the second day. The thanksgiving one had to be eaten fresh."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And on the third day, anything left *counts for nothing to the one who brought it. It has become unfit.* See the note on Leviticus 7:11–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Yesterday's thanksgiving does not keep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their **lasting share** from the people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a perpetual statute — a permanent arrangement for the priests, who had no land. See the note on Ezekiel 44:28–31."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes with the meat.** *Loaves of bread without yeast mixed with oil, thin wafers spread with oil, and loaves of fine flour well-mixed with oil* — and then, unusually, *loaves of bread made with yeast.* Leaven is banned from almost every offering in Leviticus. In the peace offering it is commanded."}]}, "Leviticus 7:35–38": {"title": "The list of six", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of the sacrificial manual, and it signs off with an inventory."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The portion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is **the portion set apart for Aaron and his sons** from the offerings made by fire.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mishchat** — a word connected to anointing. Some read it as *the anointing portion*: what came to them by virtue of being anointed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The date it started"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Given on **the day they were presented to serve the LORD as priests**. On the day they were anointed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The income began with the office. The oil and the portion are attached to the same day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The duration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **every generation to come**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **choq olam** — a perpetual statute, and it recurs through the whole book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **burnt offering**, the **grain offering**, the **sin offering**, the **guilt offering**, the **ordination offering**, and the **peace offering**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six named, and the peace offering is last."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five are usually counted as the standing offerings. The sixth, the ordination offering, was for the priests' installation and happens once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What each one was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Olah**, the burnt offering — wholly consumed, nothing eaten. See the note on Leviticus 1:3–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Minchah**, the grain offering — flour, oil and frankincense, no blood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chattat**, the sin offering — for unintentional sin, with the highest holiness grade. See the note on Leviticus 6:24–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Asham**, the guilt offering — with a valuation and restitution attached, and the one Isaiah names for the Servant. See the note on Leviticus 5:14–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Millu'im**, the ordination offering — literally *fillings*, from the idiom *to fill the hands*, which is what consecration to the priesthood was called."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Shelamim**, the peace offering — the only one the worshipper eats. See the note on Leviticus 3:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus 1 to 7 gives them in this order twice: once to the people, once to the priests."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The peace offering comes last in the summary and third in the original run. The manual does not explain why."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the instructions came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The commands the LORD gave Moses **on Mount Sinai**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they were given to the people"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There in **the Wilderness of Sinai**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two locations in one sentence — the mountain where Moses received them and the desert floor where he passed them on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book began with the same distinction: a voice out of the tent, and a message for the Israelites. See the note on Leviticus 1:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the manual is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven chapters of procedure: which animal, which hand, where the blood goes, what burns, who eats, and how long they have to finish it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in it is left to judgment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the next chapter does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Consecrates Aaron. Blood on the right ear, the right thumb, the right big toe. Seven days inside the door of the tabernacle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then chapter 9: fire out from before the LORD, the people shouting and falling on their faces."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And chapter 10: two of the sons dead the same week. See the note on Leviticus 10:12–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews says about the whole apparatus"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it is not possible that the blood of bulls and goats could take away sins.* See the note on Hebrews 10:3–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *by that will we have been sanctified through the offering of the body of Jesus Christ once for all*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six offerings, listed here as a perpetual statute, and a letter written to Hebrews arguing that one of them happened once."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the sign-off contains.** Six names and a date. The priests' income started on the day they were anointed, and the manual closes by listing everything it has covered."}]}, "Luke 1:1–4": {"title": "Written so you can be certain", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only Gospel that opens by explaining its own method."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The form"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One sentence in Greek, running the whole four verses, in the most polished Greek in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a formal literary preface — the kind of dedication a historian of the period put at the front of a work. Josephus opens the same way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke then switches, at verse 5, into Greek that reads like translated Hebrew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The others"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A good many people have set out to write down **the account of everything that has come true among us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **polloi** — many. Luke says there were already several attempts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not disparage them. He is one more."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Peplerophoremenon** — fulfilled, brought to completion, fully accomplished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *events that happened*. Things that came true, which is a claim about promises."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Telling it **just as it was handed to us** by those who were there from the very beginning — **the ones who saw it with their own eyes** and then **spent their lives serving the message**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paredosan** — handed over, delivered. The verb behind *tradition*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Autoptai** — eyewitnesses. The word a Greek doctor used of what he had observed himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke was not one of them, and says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His own qualification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So it seemed right to me, **after going back over all of it carefully and following every thread to its source**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parekolouthekoti anothen pasin akribos** — having followed everything closely from the start, accurately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Akribos** — precisely, exactly. It is the vocabulary of investigation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke travelled with Paul. Acts contains the *we* passages, where the narrator joins the story."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He spent two years in Caesarea while Paul was imprisoned there, within reach of everybody who had been present."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **write it out for you in order**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kathexes** — in sequence, in orderly succession."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reader"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Most excellent **Theophilus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kratiste** — the form of address used for officials. Paul uses it of Felix and Festus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name means *lover of God*, or *loved by God*, and it was a real name in the period. Luke dedicates Acts to him too."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that you can **know with settled confidence** that **the things you were taught are solid and true**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hina epignos… ten asphaleian** — that you may know the certainty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Asphaleia** — from *a-sphallo*, not-slipping. Firm footing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that implies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Theophilus has already been taught. Luke is not evangelising him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Gospel is written for somebody who has heard it and wants to know whether it holds."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke's Gospel contains that no other does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The annunciation to Mary. The Magnificat, the Benedictus, the Nunc Dimittis. The shepherds. The boy in the temple at twelve."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The good Samaritan, the prodigal son, the rich man and Lazarus, Zacchaeus, the road to Emmaus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Almost half of it is found nowhere else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Women, repeatedly — Elizabeth, Mary, Anna, the widow of Nain, Mary Magdalene and Joanna and Susanna who supported the work from their own means."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Shepherds. Samaritans. Tax collectors. A dying criminal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Gospel that opens with a formal address to a man of rank is the one most interested in people who had none."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul calls him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Luke the beloved physician greets you.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word.** **Asphaleia** — the state of not slipping. Written for a man who had already been taught, so he could find out whether it held."}]}, "Luke 1:18–20": {"title": "Silent until the day", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The question a priest asks an angel, and what it costs him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How can I be sure of this?** I am an old man, and my wife is well along in years.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *by what shall I know this*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks for a sign, which is not in itself an unreasonable request. Gideon asked for one twice and got both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he is talking to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am Gabriel. I stand in the presence of God**, and I was sent to speak with you and **hand you this good news**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angel gives his own name and his position, which is rare — angels in Scripture usually decline to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gabriel appears four times in the Bible: twice to Daniel, and twice in this chapter. The same messenger who explained the seventy weeks turns up to announce their fulfilment. See the note on Daniel 9:20–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hand you this good news** — the Greek is **euangelisasthai**, to evangelise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And now, **because you did not believe what I told you**, you will be **silent — unable to say a word — until the day all of this happens**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **siopon** — silent — and the later verses show he was deaf as well: they *made signs to his father*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nine months without speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it lands as it does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reply is not *you asked for a sign*. It is *you did not believe*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke sets up the comparison deliberately. Mary asks a question too, in the same chapter, and is not silenced. See the note on Luke 1:34–38."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her question is *how can this happen?* — a request for the mechanism."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His is *how can I be sure?* — a request for grounds."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The guarantee"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**And it will happen. Every word of it will come true right on time**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *which shall be fulfilled in their season*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The unbelief does not cancel anything. The promise is restated in the same breath as the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the silence is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a sign. He asked for one and got one, and it is the thing he asked with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the crowd saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But when he came out, **he could not speak to them**; and they perceived that he had seen a vision in the temple, for **he beckoned to them and remained speechless**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He should have come out and pronounced the priestly blessing — *the LORD bless you and keep you* — over the people who had been praying outside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He could not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nine months later, at the circumcision, they hand him a writing tablet and he writes *his name is John*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Right then **his mouth was opened and his tongue was set loose**, and he began to speak — and the first thing that came out of him was praise to God.* See the note on Luke 1:59–67."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gets his voice back the moment he agrees with what he was told."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Benedictus — one of the three songs in Luke's opening chapters:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed is the Lord God of Israel, for **He has visited and redeemed His people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who spent nine months unable to say anything opens with that."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the sentence was.** Not a punishment for asking. He asked how he could be sure, and was given nine months in which he could do nothing but wait and watch it happen."}]}, "Luke 1:26–29": {"title": "The greeting that unsettled her", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An angel arrives in an unimportant town and says something that frightens a young woman — not because it is a threat, but because of what it implies."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Gabriel actually says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rejoice, you who have been so greatly favoured! The Lord is with you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *greatly favoured* is one word, **kecharitomene** — a perfect passive participle built on *charis*, grace. Something like: you who have been graced, and stand in that state."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is passive. Nothing is said about what Mary has done. The word describes something done **to** her, already complete."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The words unsettled her all the way through, and she turned them over, trying to understand what kind of greeting this could possibly be.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says the greeting disturbed her, not the angel. She is troubled by being addressed that way — and then she does what Luke will say she does twice more in two chapters: she thinks about it rather than answering quickly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the story is set"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A town in Galilee called Nazareth* — a village of a few hundred people, never mentioned once in the Old Testament. Luke has just come from the temple in Jerusalem, where the other annunciation in this chapter happened, to a priest, at the altar, at the hour of incense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The contrast is deliberate and Luke does not comment on it. He simply reports where the second angel went."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What follows.** Mary's question is not *why me* but *how* — a practical objection, and Gabriel answers it. Then: *let it be to me according to your word.* Luke gives her more speaking lines in this chapter than anyone else, and the longest of them is a song about God putting the powerful down and lifting the low."}]}, "Luke 1:30–33": {"title": "\"You have found favor\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The angel's second sentence to Mary, and it is a reassurance before it is an announcement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reassurance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not be afraid, Mary**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gabriel says the same thing to Zechariah in the same chapter, and to Daniel twice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke has just recorded her reaction to the greeting: *she was troubled at his saying, and considered what manner of greeting this was.* See the note on Luke 1:26–29."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The statement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You have found favor with God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **charin para to theo** — grace with God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The greeting a few verses earlier used the related verb: **kecharitomene**, one who has been graced. The same verb appears once more in the New Testament, in Ephesians, of every believer: *He made us accepted in the Beloved*. See the note on Ephesians 1:3–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What \"found favour\" means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew equivalent is **matsa chen** — the phrase used of Noah before the flood, of Joseph in Potiphar's house, of Esther before the king. See the notes on Genesis 6:5–8 and Genesis 39:1–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In every case it is stated about the person and not earned by them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The announcement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You are going to **conceive and give birth to a son**, and you are to **name Him Jesus**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yeshua** — the LORD saves. Matthew gives the reason: *for He will save His people from their sins.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mary is told the name. In that culture the father named the child, and Joseph is told the same name separately in Matthew's account."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four things said about Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He will be great**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of John, Gabriel had said *he will be great in the sight of the Lord*. Here the qualification is dropped."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He will be called **the Son of the Most High**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The Lord God will give Him **the throne of His father David**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And He will **reign over the house of Jacob forever**. **His kingdom will never come to an end**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The throne"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His father David.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise to David: *I will set up your seed after you… and I will establish the throne of his kingdom forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah had not had a king on that throne for six hundred years. The last one, Zedekiah, was blinded and taken to Babylon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two \"forevers\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Reign forever*, and *a kingdom that will never come to an end*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gabriel says it twice in one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the words come from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 9: *of the increase of His government and peace **there will be no end**, upon the throne of David and over His kingdom, to order it and establish it with judgment and justice from that time forward, even forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gabriel is quoting a prophecy seven hundred years old to a teenager in Nazareth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is hearing it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A virgin betrothed to a man whose name was Joseph, of the house of David. **The virgin's name was Mary**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nazareth was a village of perhaps a few hundred people, in a region Judeans looked down on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How can this happen?**\"* — and Gabriel answers with the mechanism rather than a rebuke. See the note on Luke 1:34–38."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is stated before anything is asked.** That she has found favour. The announcement follows it, and nothing in the passage presents the favour as a result of her answer."}]}, "Luke 1:34–38": {"title": "\"Let it happen to me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Mary asks a question, gets an answer, and says one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How can this happen? I have never been with a man**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *how shall this be, since I do not know a man*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is betrothed and not yet married. Betrothal in that world was legally binding — Joseph would have needed a divorce to end it — but the couple did not live together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this is not Zechariah's question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked *how can I be sure of this?* and was struck dumb. See the note on Luke 1:18–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She asks *how can this happen?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One asks for evidence; the other asks for the mechanism. Gabriel treats them differently and Luke sets them four hundred words apart so the comparison is unavoidable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The Holy Spirit will come over you**, and **the power of the Most High will overshadow you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *overshadow* is **episkiasei** — to cast a shadow over, to envelop."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word the Greek Old Testament uses of the cloud covering the tabernacle: *the cloud covered the tent of meeting, and the glory of the LORD filled the tabernacle*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is the word used at the transfiguration: *a bright cloud overshadowed them*. See the note on Matthew 17:1–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So the holy child who is born of you **will be called the Son of God**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And here is something else: **your relative Elizabeth is carrying a son in her old age**. The woman **everyone called childless** is **six months along**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gabriel volunteers a sign she did not ask for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah asked for one and received nine months of silence. Mary does not ask and is handed one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because **nothing at all is impossible for God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *no word from God shall be void of power* — **ouk adunatesei para tou theou pan rhema**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same sentence, in the Greek Old Testament, is what the LORD says to Abraham when Sarah laughs behind the tent door: *is anything too hard for the LORD?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gabriel quotes the Sarah passage to Mary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am the Lord's servant. Let it happen to me exactly as you have said**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **doule** — a female slave, a bondservant. It is the feminine of the word Paul uses of himself at the head of his letters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Let it happen** — the Greek is **genoito**, an optative: may it be."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same verb form as the *me genoito* Paul uses for his strongest denials, here without the negative."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she is agreeing to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An unmarried pregnancy in a village of a few hundred people, and a betrothed husband who would be entitled to end it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records what Joseph decided before he was told: *not wanting to make her a public example, was minded to put her away secretly*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the angel left her**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing else is said. No further instruction, no assurance, and no plan."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mary got ready and **went quickly** up into the hill country.* See the note on Luke 1:39–45."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She goes to find the one other person on earth in a comparable position."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elizabeth says about her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How happy you are, because you believed** that what the Lord said to you would actually be done!\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two questions in one chapter.** A priest in the temple asking how he can be sure, and a girl in Nazareth asking how it will work. Luke records both and lets the answers speak."}]}, "Luke 1:39–45": {"title": "The baby leaped", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two pregnant women in a house in the hills, and the first person to recognise the Messiah has not been born yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The journey"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mary got ready and **went quickly** up into the hill country, to a town in Judah.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meta spoudes** — with haste."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nazareth to the Judean hills is roughly eighty miles. A young woman travelling that distance is not a small undertaking, and Luke does not say who went with her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She goes because Gabriel had told her about Elizabeth. See the note on Luke 1:34–38."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened at the door"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She walked into Zechariah's house and **greeted Elizabeth** — and **the moment Elizabeth heard Mary's voice, the baby inside her leaped**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eskirtesen** — leapt, jumped."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elizabeth is six months along; John is the one who moves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The baby leaps, and **then** Elizabeth is filled with the Spirit and speaks."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives the sequence deliberately. The recognition comes from inside her before she knows anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The volume"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She **called out at the top of her voice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anephonesen** — a loud cry. The word occurs once in the New Testament, and it is used in the Greek Old Testament of the Levites' shout at the ark's procession."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You are blessed among women**, and **blessed is the child you are carrying**!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two blessings, and the second one is what the first depends on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **why should this come to me — that the mother of my Lord should walk through my door?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**My Lord.** An older woman, married to a priest, six months pregnant with the forerunner, calls her young relative's unborn child that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody has told her anything. Mary has said nothing beyond a greeting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The explanation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The instant your greeting reached my ears, the baby inside me jumped for sheer joy**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She reports what her own body did and reads it correctly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gabriel had said of John: *he will be filled with the Holy Spirit, even from his mother's womb.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How happy you are, because you believed** that what the Lord said to you would actually be done!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **makaria** — blessed, happy. It is the word that opens the Beatitudes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thing Elizabeth commends is the believing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her own husband is standing somewhere in the house, unable to say a word, because he did not. See the note on Luke 1:18–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke puts the two in the same building and does not comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mary says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Magnificat: *my soul magnifies the Lord, and my spirit has rejoiced in God my Saviour.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is stitched almost entirely out of the Old Testament — Hannah's song in 1 Samuel 2 above all — and it is sung by a teenager."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has put down the mighty from their thrones, and exalted the lowly. He has filled the hungry with good things, and the rich He has sent away empty.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How long she stayed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Mary **remained with her about three months**, and returned to her house.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Long enough to be there for the birth, or nearly."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who recognises Him first.** Not a priest, not a prophet, and not an adult. An unborn child, at the sound of a voice through a doorway — and his mother says so out loud before anybody has explained anything."}]}, "Luke 1:46–55": {"title": "Mary's song", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A teenager in a hill-country house sings ten verses of Old Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elizabeth has just called out *blessed are you among women*, and *how happy you are, because you believed*. See the note on Luke 1:39–45."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mary answers with this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My whole soul **lifts up the Lord**, and my spirit is **dancing in God my Saviour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **megalunei** — magnifies, makes great. It is where the song's Latin name, *Magnificat*, comes from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**My Saviour** — she includes herself among those who need one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **He noticed me — a servant girl, nobody of consequence**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tapeinosin** — low estate, humiliation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She uses **doule** of herself — a female slave, the same word she used to the angel: *I am the Lord's servant*. See the note on Luke 1:34–38."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prediction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From now on, **every generation will call me blessed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said by a village girl in Nazareth, and it has been true for two thousand years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the song is made of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Almost every line comes from the Old Testament. The closest model is Hannah's song, which it follows in structure and content. See the note on 1 Samuel 2:1–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two women, a thousand years apart, both told they would have a son, both singing about God turning the order of things over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mary knows the Scriptures well enough to compose out of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has **flexed the strength of His arm**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *He has shown strength with His arm* — the standard Old Testament image for the exodus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The six reversals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has **scattered the proud**, tangled in the schemes of their own hearts.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has **pulled the powerful down off their thrones** and **lifted up the lowly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has **filled the hungry with good things** and **sent the rich away with empty hands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three pairs, and each is a swap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every verb is aorist — completed action."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing has happened yet. She is a few weeks pregnant, no thrones have fallen, and the child will not be born for months."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She sings about it as done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last section"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has **taken hold of Israel His servant**, remembering to be merciful, **just as He promised our fathers long ago — to Abraham, and to his children, forever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The song ends where the covenant started."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anything about herself after the first four lines. The personal note is brief and the rest is national."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where she was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *Mary got ready and went quickly up into the hill country.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had walked eighty miles to the one other person who would understand, and this is what she says when she arrives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How long she stayed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Mary remained with her about three months, and returned to her house.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What waits at home"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An unmarried pregnancy in a village of a few hundred people, and a betrothed husband entitled to end it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records what Joseph decided before the angel came to him: *not wanting to make her a public example, was minded to put her away secretly*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What she sings about.** Not the child. Six reversals, in the past tense, before any of them has happened — and the personal part is four lines about being noticed."}]}, "Luke 1:59–67": {"title": "\"His name is John\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Nine months of silence end on a writing tablet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On **the eighth day** they came to circumcise the child.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The day set in Genesis 17 and in Leviticus. Naming happened at the same ceremony."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The assumption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they were going to **name him Zechariah, after his father**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The family assumes. Nobody asks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mother"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **his mother spoke up: \"No. He is to be called John.\"**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elizabeth has not been told by anyone in the room. Zechariah has been deaf and mute since the temple."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever passed between them, she knows the name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **there is no one in your family with that name**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the whole argument they have."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The appeal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they **made signs to his father**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They gesture. He is deaf as well as silent — Luke shows it here rather than stating it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tablet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He asked for **a writing tablet**, and he wrote: **His name is John**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *John is his name* — a statement rather than a choice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not selecting. He is reporting what he was told nine months earlier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**John** — **Yochanan**, *the LORD is gracious*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The astonishment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **everyone was astonished**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two people who could not have consulted each other produced the same name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The release"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Right then **his mouth was opened and his tongue was set loose**, and he began to speak.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The moment he agrees in writing, the speech comes back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gabriel had said *until the day all of this happens*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first thing out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the first thing that came out of him was praise to God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nine months of stored-up speech, and Luke says what it was spent on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The effect"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **hush of awe settled over all the neighbors**, and **the story traveled through the whole hill country of Judea**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And people kept **laying it up in their hearts** and asking, \"**What is this child going to be?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *laying it up* is the same verb Luke uses of Mary twice: *Mary kept all these things and pondered them in her heart*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke uses it of ordinary neighbours here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the hand of the Lord was plainly on him**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And his father Zechariah was **filled with the Holy Spirit**, and he began to prophesy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Benedictus:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed is the Lord God of Israel, for **He has visited and redeemed His people**… to give light to those who sit in darkness and the shadow of death, **to guide our feet into the way of peace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And to the child: *and you, child, will be called the prophet of the Highest; for **you will go before the face of the Lord to prepare His ways**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John becomes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man in the wilderness in camel's hair, saying *behold the Lamb of God*, and *He must increase, but I must decrease*. See the note on John 3:25–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And executed in a prison at a birthday party, on a girl's request."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus on him: *among those born of women there has not risen one greater than John the Baptist*. See the note on Matthew 11:12–15."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What broke the silence.** Not the birth. Zechariah wrote down the name he had been given and had not believed — and the sentence about it opens with *right then*."}]}, "Luke 1:68–79": {"title": "Zechariah's song", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Nine months of silence, and this is the first thing he says."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he could not speak"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had asked Gabriel *how shall I know this?* — and been told *you will be mute and not able to speak until the day these things take place*. See the note on Luke 1:18–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is a priest, in the temple, at the incense altar, and he asked for evidence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What breaks the silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The neighbours wanted the boy named after his father. Elizabeth said *no; he shall be called John*, and they made signs to the father, who wrote on a tablet *his name is John*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Immediately his mouth was opened and his tongue loosed, and he spoke, praising God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed be the Lord, the God of Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eulogetos** — the standard opening of a Jewish berakah, a blessing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He starts where every synagogue prayer starts. Nine months of thinking, and he opens with the liturgy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **He has come** to His people and **bought their freedom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aorist. Completed action."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing has happened yet. Jesus has not been born; Mary is three months pregnant. Zechariah is holding an eight-day-old baby who is not the subject of the first half of the song."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He speaks about it as done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The horn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has **raised up a horn of rescue** for us in the house of His servant David.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew idiom behind it: the horn of an ox, the concentration of an animal's strength."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD is my rock and my fortress… the horn of my salvation*, in David's own song."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as He said He would, **long ago, through the mouths of His holy prophets**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ap' aionos** — from the age, from of old."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the rescue is from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That we would be **saved from our enemies** and out of the hand of everyone who hates us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Political language, in a country under Roman occupation, sung by a Jewish priest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke records it without adjustment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The covenant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That **mercy promised to our fathers** would be shown, that **His holy covenant** would be remembered, **the oath He swore to our father Abraham**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three clauses for the same thing: mercy, covenant, oath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oath is Genesis 22, after the ram: *by Myself I have sworn, says the LORD*. See the note on Genesis 22:15–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the rescue is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To pull us free from the grip of our enemies **so that we could serve Him unafraid, holy and righteous in His sight, all the days of our lives**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **latreuein** — to serve as a priest serves, the temple word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A priest sings that the point of deliverance is priestly service without fear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn to the child"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And you, little one.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kai su, paidion**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Halfway through the song he looks down at the baby in his arms and addresses him directly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the boy will do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will be called **a prophet of the Most High**, for you will **walk out ahead of the Lord to make His roads ready**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last prophet before four hundred years of silence had said it: *behold, I send My messenger, and he will prepare the way before Me*. See the note on Malachi 4:4–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What knowledge he brings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To let His people know **salvation has come, their sins carried away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a military knowledge. The song has spent eight lines on enemies and now defines the salvation as forgiveness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason for all of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All because of **the tender mercy of our God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **splanchna eleous** — the bowels of mercy. The gut."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **sunrise breaking over us from heaven**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anatole** — the rising, the dayspring. The same word used in the Septuagint for the Branch."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To shine on **everyone sitting in darkness and in the shadow of death**, and to **guide our feet into the path of peace**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Eirene** — peace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sung by a man who had spent nine months unable to say anything, over a boy who would be beheaded in a fortress by the Dead Sea."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The tense.** Aorist throughout — *He has come, He has bought, He has raised up* — sung before the child in the other womb was born."}]}, "Luke 1:8–12": {"title": "The lot falls to Zechariah", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred years of silence end at an incense altar, and the man it happens to is terrified."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rota"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Zechariah's division came up for **its turn of service**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David had divided the priests into twenty-four courses, each serving twice a year for a week. Luke says earlier that Zechariah was *of the division of Abijah* — the eighth of the twenty-four."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lot"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the lot fell to him** — as the custom of the priesthood decided such things — to **go into the sanctuary of the Lord and burn the incense**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **elache** — obtained by lot."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There were thousands of priests. Burning the incense was the most desirable duty in the temple, and rabbinic tradition holds that a man who had done it once was not eligible again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For most priests it never came round. Zechariah is old."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Burning incense on the golden altar, immediately outside the veil. Four spices in equal parts, salted, unlawful to make for private use. See the note on Exodus 30:34–38."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was outside"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Outside, **at the hour of incense**, the whole gathered crowd **was praying**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hour of incense was the hour of the morning or evening sacrifice — and the incense on the altar and the prayers of the people are the same thing in Scripture: *let my prayer stand before You like incense*. See the note on Psalms 141:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke puts the crowd praying outside and the angel arriving inside, in the same sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the angel stood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Standing at **the right side of the incense altar**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives the position. The right side is the place of favour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Zechariah **went rigid at the sight of him**, and **fear came over him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **etarachthe** — troubled, agitated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the angel says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not be afraid, Zechariah, **for your prayer is heard**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which prayer is not said. He and Elizabeth are *both well advanced in years*, and Luke has already recorded that *they had no child, because Elizabeth was barren*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer for a son had presumably been abandoned decades earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke says about them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they were **both righteous before God, walking in all the commandments and ordinances of the Lord blameless**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke clears the obvious inference before anyone draws it. The childlessness was not a verdict."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A son named John, who will *turn many of the children of Israel to the Lord their God*, and go before Him *in the spirit and power of Elijah*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Malachi's last words, four hundred years earlier: *behold, I will send you Elijah the prophet.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah asks how he can be sure, and is struck dumb until the day it happens. See the note on Luke 1:18–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd outside waits, and when he comes out he cannot pronounce the blessing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How Luke's Gospel ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He led them out as far as Bethany, and **He lifted up His hands and blessed them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A priest who could not give the blessing at the start, and a blessing completed at the end."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the silence breaks.** In the one room a priest might enter once in a lifetime, at the hour the nation was praying outside, to a man old enough to have stopped asking."}]}, "Luke 10:17–20": {"title": "\"Your names are written down in heaven\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Seventy men come back thrilled, and He redirects the excitement without dampening it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, **even the demons do what we say when we use Your name**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is emphatic — *even the demons*. It is more than they expected."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had sent them out to heal the sick and say *the kingdom of God has come near to you.* He had not mentioned demons."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They came back with something they were not promised."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In Your name\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are careful about the mechanism. Not *we cast out demons* — *when we use Your name.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I watched Satan fall out of heaven like a bolt of lightning.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is imperfect — I was watching. He speaks as somebody who was present."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Like lightning** — sudden, visible, and from above to below."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The authority"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have **given you authority** to walk right over snakes and scorpions, and over **all the power of the enemy**, and nothing at all will harm you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *authority* is **exousia** — delegated right, permission. It is given, not developed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Snakes and scorpions* echoes Psalm 91: *you shall tread upon the lion and the cobra… you shall trample underfoot.* See the note on Psalms 91:11–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The redirection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But do not let your joy rest on the fact that the spirits obey you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not deny it happened, correct their report, or warn them about pride."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them what to be glad about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What to be glad about instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Let your joy rest on this: your names are written down in heaven.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is perfect tense — have been written, and stand written."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ministry success is variable. Some days the demons come out and some days they do not; Jesus' own disciples failed at it in Matthew 17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name in the book does not fluctuate, and it was written before any of them cast out anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image runs through Scripture — *blot me out of Your book*, Moses said; *the book of life*, in Philippians, Daniel and Revelation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In that hour Jesus **rejoiced in the Spirit** and said, \"I thank You, Father… that You have hidden these things from the wise and prudent and revealed them to babes.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke records Jesus rejoicing exactly once, and it is here, immediately after this conversation."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who the seventy were.** Not the twelve. An unnamed larger group, sent out two by two ahead of Him, given no money, no bag and no sandals, and told *he who hears you hears Me.* They came back with authority over the enemy and were told to be glad about something else."}]}, "Luke 10:38–42": {"title": "\"Martha, Martha\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two sisters, one guest, and a correction that is gentler than it is usually read."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who opened the door"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A woman named **Martha welcomed Him into her home**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is her house and her invitation. Nothing in the passage criticises that, and without her there is no scene."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Mary is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mary **sat down at Jesus' feet** and listened to what He was saying.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sitting at a teacher's feet is the posture of a disciple — Paul says he was *brought up at the feet of Gamaliel*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was not a woman's place in that culture. She has taken a student's seat in a room where women served."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was happening to Martha"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Martha was **pulled in every direction** by all there was to do.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **perispao** — to be dragged around, drawn away, distracted. It is a word about being pulled off course."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is not lazy and she is not doing anything wrong. She is being torn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Lord, do You not care** that my sister has left me to do all the serving by myself? **Tell her to come and help me.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things in one sentence: an accusation about His caring, a complaint about her sister, and an instruction to Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same accusation the disciples made in the boat — *do You not care that we are going down?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name, twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Martha, Martha.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The doubled name is how affection is spoken in Scripture. *Abraham, Abraham. Moses, Moses. Simon, Simon. Saul, Saul. Jerusalem, Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every time, it precedes something serious, and every time it is warm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You are **worried and pulled about** by so many things.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words: anxious, and troubled — the second means to be in a tumult, disturbed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names her state before He names the remedy, and He does not tell her she should not have been cooking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Only one thing is really needed\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The contrast is between *many things* and **one**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not many things done better, or more efficiently. One."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mary chose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Mary has chosen the good portion, and no one is going to take it away from her.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meris** — a portion, a share, the word for what you are allotted at a meal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Martha is preparing portions. Jesus says Mary has already taken hers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No one is going to take it away* — the one thing about the afternoon that is permanent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Martha is not told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is not told to stop serving, leave the kitchen, or be like her sister. She is told what she is being pulled apart by, and what the one necessary thing is."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the sisters turn up next.** John 11, at Lazarus's grave. Martha runs out to meet Him and gets the sentence *I am the resurrection and the life* — spoken to her, and it is Martha who answers *I believe that You are the Christ, the Son of God.* See the note on John 11:23–27."}]}, "Luke 11:33–36": {"title": "The lamp and the eye", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two images joined, and the join is the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lamp"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Nobody lights a lamp and then hides it away** or covers it with a bowl. You put it up on a stand so that **everyone coming in can see by its light**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **krupten** — a cellar, a hidden place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke records this saying twice. The other has the same conclusion attached to hearing: *so pay attention to how you listen*. See the note on Luke 8:16–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Your eye is the lamp of your body**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image moves inside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A lamp lights a room; an eye lights a person. The comparison is about what lets light in rather than what gives it out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When your eye is **clear and single**, your whole body is **full of light**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **haplous** — single, simple, uncompounded. And by extension generous."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That second meaning is worth noting. The word and its noun are used in the New Testament of liberality: *he who gives, with liberality*, and *the riches of their liberality*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A single eye, in the Greek of the period, could describe a person who was not calculating."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The alternative"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But when your eye is **bad**, your body is full of darkness.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **poneros** — evil, and it is the standard word for wicked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But there is a Hebrew idiom underneath it. *An evil eye* means stingy: *do not eat the bread of a miser; for as he thinks in his heart, so is he… **he who has an evil eye**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Jesus uses it that way in the vineyard parable: *is your eye evil because I am good?* — said to a man complaining about somebody else's wages."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So be careful that **the light in you is not actually darkness**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **skopei oun** — look out, therefore."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most unsettling sentence in the paragraph. A man with a bad eye does not experience himself as being in the dark. He experiences himself as seeing normally."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If your whole body is filled with light, **with no dark corner left in it**, then you will be **radiant all through, like a room with a bright lamp shining in it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *having no part dark*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What sits either side of it in Matthew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew has the same saying in the Sermon on the Mount, and its neighbours settle the reading:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not lay up for yourselves treasures on earth… for where your treasure is, there your heart will be also.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the eye."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *\"**No one can serve two masters**… you cannot serve God and mammon.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Treasure, eye, mammon — in that order. The eye saying is sandwiched between two passages about money."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke has just been dealing with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sign of Jonah, and the crowds asking for more."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And immediately after this, dinner with a Pharisee who is surprised He did not wash — and the woes: *you cleanse the outside of the cup and dish, but your inward part is full of greed and wickedness*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the two halves share"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are about direction of travel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lamp is put where light gets out. The eye is what lets light in. And the paragraph's question is whether what is inside is actually light."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word that carries both senses.** *Haplous* — single, and generous. And its opposite in the Hebrew idiom is not blindness but stinginess."}]}, "Luke 11:37–44": {"title": "\"Without letting go of the others\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus is at a dinner party and says the hardest thing in Luke. The sentence at the centre of it is a correction of proportion, not a cancellation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He accuses them of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You carefully tithe your mint and your rue and every garden herb you grow, and you step straight over justice and the love of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The precision is the joke. The law asked for a tenth of the crop; these men were counting leaves out of the kitchen garden while walking over the two largest things in the law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line He does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say *stop tithing your herbs*. He says:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "*These are the things you should have been doing, **without letting go of the others**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both. Justice and the love of God taken up, and the giving not put down. Jesus had every opportunity in that sentence to abolish the practice and instead He kept it in place while re-ranking it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a correction of what had gone missing, not of what they were doing with the mint."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The remedy He gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You Pharisees scrub the outside of the cup and the plate, but inside you are full of greed and cruelty… Give away what is inside as a gift to the poor, and see — everything about you is clean.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Having diagnosed greed under a polished exterior, His prescription is not more careful measuring. It is generosity — giving the contents away. He treats open- handedness as the thing that reaches what scrubbing never could."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the meal setting matters.** This is said at a table, to a host, after Jesus declined to perform the ritual handwashing. Everything in the passage is about outsides and insides — clean hands and grasping hearts, polished dishes and unmarked graves. The tithing line belongs to that argument: a right practice, carried out by a heart that had never been dealt with."}]}, "Luke 11:45–54": {"title": "\"You have taken away the key\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A lawyer objects to being included, and gets three woes of his own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One of the experts in the law answered Him, \"Teacher, **when You say things like that, You are insulting us too**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just pronounced three woes on the Pharisees — over tithing mint and rue while passing over justice, over the best seats, and over being like unmarked graves people walk on without knowing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lawyer's complaint is that the description reached further than intended."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How terrible it is for you experts in the law as well**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three more, addressed to them directly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You **load people down with burdens too heavy to carry**, and you **will not lift a finger to help carry them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phortia dusbastakta** — loads hard to bear."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves matter. The loading and the refusal to help are named as one offence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's version: *they bind heavy burdens, hard to bear, and lay them on men's shoulders; but they themselves will not move them with one of their fingers.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other yoke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take My yoke upon you**… **for My yoke is easy and My burden is light**.\"* See the note on Matthew 11:28–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *burden* is the same word — **phortion**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You **build fine tombs for the prophets** — and it was **your own ancestors who killed them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So you are **witnesses against yourselves**: **you approve of what your ancestors did**. They did the killing, and you do the building.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The monument is read as an endorsement. The argument turns on the fact that the same family is doing both jobs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The span"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"From **the blood of Abel** to **the blood of Zechariah**, who was killed **between the altar and the sanctuary**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abel is the first murder in Genesis. Zechariah is in 2 Chronicles 24, stoned in the temple court — and Chronicles is the last book in the Hebrew ordering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "First book to last book. He is naming the whole span of the Scriptures."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah's dying words there: *the LORD look on it, and repay*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You have **taken away the key of knowledge**. **You did not go in yourselves**, and **you got in the way of the ones who were trying to**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the key of knowledge* — **ten kleida tes gnoseos**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A key is for opening a door for somebody. This one has been removed from the lock."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two failures again: not entering, and blocking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's parallel: *you shut up the kingdom of heaven against men; for you neither go in yourselves, nor do you allow those who are entering to go in.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the teachers of the law and the Pharisees began pressing Him hard, badgering Him with question after question**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **enechein** — to hold a grudge, to be hostile — and **apostomatizein**, to interrogate, to press for answers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Waiting to **catch something out of His mouth** that they could use against Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a hunting word — lying in wait."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At a meal. Verse 37: *a certain Pharisee asked Him to dine with him. So He went in and sat down to eat.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole passage is dinner-table conversation, and it starts because He did not wash His hands first."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the three woes have in common.** All three are about what happens to other people: burdens laid on them, a monument that endorses their killing, and a door held shut in front of them."}]}, "Luke 11:9–13": {"title": "How much more", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs, three promises, and an argument that runs from bad fathers upward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What prompts it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lord, teach us to pray.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Lord's Prayer, and then the parable of the friend at midnight — a man banging on a neighbour's door for bread because a visitor has arrived."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because of his persistence he will rise and give him as many as he needs.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anaideian** — shamelessness. Literally *without shame*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Ask**, and it will be given to you. **Seek**, and you will find. **Knock**, and the door will be opened for you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "All three are present imperatives — keep asking, keep seeking, keep knocking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Escalating effort: a word, a search, a hand on a door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Everyone who asks, receives. Everyone who looks, finds. And for everyone who knocks, the door is opened**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said as command, then restated as fact. And the subject of the second set is *everyone*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Is there a father among you who, when his son asks him for **bread, would hand him a stone**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Or if he asks for a **fish, would hand him a snake**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Or if he asks for an **egg, would hand him a scorpion**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three pairs, and each pair is of things that resemble each other. A flat stone looks like a loaf. An eel-like fish and a snake. A pale coiled scorpion and an egg."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The horror in the picture is not a father refusing. It is a father handing over something that looks like the thing and is not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew has two of the three; Luke adds the scorpion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If **you, bad as you are**, know how to give good gifts to your own children.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **poneroi ontes** — being evil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it in passing, to a crowd, as an agreed premise. Nobody objects."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How much more will the Father in heaven give the Holy Spirit to those who ask Him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **poso mallon** — by how much more."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument runs from the lesser to the greater: if flawed fathers do not trick their children, then certainly not Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word Luke changes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew has *how much more will your Father who is in heaven give **good things** to those who ask Him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke has *the Holy Spirit*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same sermon material, and Luke — who wrote Acts — names the gift."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same reasoning goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who did not spare His own Son, but delivered Him up for us all, **how shall He not with Him also freely give us all things?*** See the note on Romans 8:31–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's version of *how much more*, argued from the cross rather than from fatherhood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other place in Luke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not fear, little flock, for **it is your Father's good pleasure to give you the kingdom**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eudokesen** — it seemed good to Him, He was well pleased."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any hint that the Father is reluctant, or that the asking wears Him down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parable of the midnight friend could be read that way, and this paragraph is Luke's correction of that reading: the neighbour gets up because he is being pestered; the Father is described by contrast, not by comparison."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the three pairs have in common.** Each is a counterfeit. The question is not whether a father would refuse — it is whether he would hand over something shaped like the thing asked for."}]}, "Luke 12:13–21": {"title": "The rich man and his barns", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man interrupts a sermon with a legal complaint and gets a story instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Teacher, **tell my brother to split the family inheritance with me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A rabbi was a normal arbiter for this kind of dispute, and the request is not absurd."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under the law the elder brother took a double portion. The man may well have had a case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Friend, who put Me in charge of settling your affairs?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *man, who made Me a judge or an arbitrator over you?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He declines the jurisdiction. It is one of the few times He simply refuses a request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Be on your guard against greed**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pleonexia** — the desire for more, from **pleon**, more."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is speaking to a man with a legitimate grievance about property, and names what is underneath it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**A person's life is not made out of the things he owns**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *not in the abundance of the things he possesses*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The story"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There was a rich man **whose land produced a wonderful crop**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has done nothing wrong. The land produced; he did not defraud anybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The soliloquy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **he thought it over and said to himself**, 'What am I going to do?'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Count the pronouns. In four sentences he says *I* six times and *my* five times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody else appears in his deliberation. He has no advisers, no family, no poor at the gate — he is having a conversation with himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plan"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will **pull down my barns and build bigger ones**, and store all my grain and all my goods in them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is prudent. Joseph did the same thing on a national scale and was made second in Egypt. See the note on Genesis 41:25–32."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The difference is what the storage is for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The projection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"'You have **plenty laid up for years to come**. **Take life easy. Eat, drink, enjoy yourself**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ecclesiastes says something similar and grounds it elsewhere: *eat your bread with joy… **for God has already approved what you do**.* See the note on Ecclesiastes 9:7–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rich man has the same activity and a different footing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **God said to him, 'You fool'**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **aphron** — senseless, without mind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only speaker in the parable besides the man himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The night"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Tonight your life will be asked back from you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *they require your soul of you* — an impersonal plural, which is how the language avoids naming God directly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Asked back.** The life is described as on loan."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **all these things you have arranged so carefully — whose will they be?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing his planning could not settle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The moral"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That is how it is with anyone who **piles up treasure for himself** and is **not rich toward God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses, and the second names what was missing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Therefore I say to you, **do not worry about your life**, what you will eat; nor about the body, what you will put on.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole passage about ravens and lilies. See the note on Matthew 6:25–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He answers the man who has too much and then turns to the people with too little, in the same breath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"**Sell what you have and give alms**; provide yourselves money bags which do not grow old, a treasure in the heavens that does not fail.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the rich man does wrong.** Nothing, in the farming. The parable's whole weight is in a conversation he has with himself, in which nobody else is present — and God's first word to him is the only one from outside it."}]}, "Luke 12:32–34": {"title": "Your Father's delight", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The smallest possible flock, and the largest possible gift, in one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not be afraid, little flock**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **mikron poimnion** — a double diminutive. Both words are small."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Poimnion* is already the diminutive of *poimne*, flock. Then *mikron* is added to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is speaking to a group He has just told not to worry about food or clothing, and He names them as small before He says anything else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does with the fear"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not be afraid* — **me phobou**, present imperative. Stop being afraid, or do not go on being afraid."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fear is assumed to be there already."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is **your Father's delight to give you the kingdom**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eudokesen** — He was well pleased, it seemed good to Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same verb as the voice at the Jordan: *this is My beloved Son, **in whom I am well pleased***. See the note on Matthew 3:16–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being said about the Father"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that He will give it if asked hard enough, or that He is willing to be persuaded."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That giving it pleases Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is aorist — a settled decision already made, not a disposition He might arrive at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The size mismatch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A little flock, and the kingdom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus puts them next to each other on purpose. The size of the recipient is not what determines the size of the gift."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Consider the ravens, for they neither sow nor reap… and God feeds them. **Of how much more value are you** than the birds?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Seek the kingdom of God, and all these things shall be added to you.\"* See the note on Luke 12:22–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ravens are fed and the lilies are clothed, and then the flock is given the kingdom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Sell what you have and give it away to the poor**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which only reads as reasonable after the previous verse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not ask them to sell in order to receive. The kingdom has already been given in the sentence before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Make yourselves purses that never wear out** — a treasure in the heavens that never runs down, **where no thief comes near it and no moth eats it away**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two threats to earthly wealth: theft from outside and decay from inside. Both are ruled out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parallel in Matthew is in the Sermon on the Mount. See the note on Matthew 6:19–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because **wherever your treasure is, that is where your heart will be too**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is the point. Treasure first, heart follows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *put your heart in the right place and your money will follow*. The other way round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had just told a man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens with someone shouting *teacher, tell my brother to divide the inheritance with me*, and the parable of the man with the full barns who says *soul, you have many goods laid up for many years*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So is he who lays up treasure for himself, and is not rich toward God.* See the note on Luke 12:16–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The barn-builder had no Father in his monologue — it is all *I* and *my* and *soul*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb.** **Eudokesen** — it pleased Him. The same word the Father speaks over the Son at the Jordan, used here of handing the kingdom to a flock described as small twice over."}]}, "Luke 13:10–17": {"title": "\"Should she not be untied on the Sabbath?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Eighteen years bent double, and the argument afterwards is about whether the timing was appropriate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "She did not ask"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Jesus saw her, He called her over.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every detail is His initiative. He sees her, calls her, speaks, and lays hands on her. Nothing in the account records her asking for anything — she had come to the synagogue, which is where she presumably went every week."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Woman, you are set free from what has been crippling you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is perfect passive — you **have been** released, and you stand released. He announces it as done before He touches her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words for tying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole argument turns on one image, and Jesus builds it deliberately:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Is there one of you who does not **untie** his ox or his donkey from the stall on the Sabbath and lead it out to water?*", "*And this woman… whom Satan has kept **tied up** for eighteen years — should she not be **untied** on the Sabbath day?*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same verb, three times. If a knot can be undone for an animal that has been tied overnight, what about a woman tied for eighteen years?"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The country He was in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus based Himself in Capernaum, *in the regions of Zebulun and **Naphtali***, and most of His work was done along that shore."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob's blessing over Naphtali is one line: *Naphtali is **a doe set free**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A doe let loose, and a woman untied after eighteen years, in the same territory. See the note on Genesis 49:21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He calls her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A daughter of Abraham.* Not a phrase used elsewhere in the Gospels."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives her the standing of an heir of the covenant, in a synagogue, in front of the man complaining about her — and He does it in the same sentence that names what had been holding her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two reactions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone who had been opposing Him was put to shame, and the whole crowd was overjoyed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke reports both, and the contrast is the point of the paragraph: the same event humiliated the officials and delighted everybody else."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What she did first.** *Right away she stood up straight, and she began to praise God.* Luke gives the standing and the praise in one clause. After eighteen years of looking at the ground, the first thing recorded is her voice."}]}, "Luke 14:1–6": {"title": "A Sabbath healing watched closely", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He is invited to dinner and the dinner turns out to be a test."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One Sabbath Jesus came to eat at the house of **one of the leading Pharisees**, and **every eye in the room was on Him, waiting to see what He would do**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *they were watching Him closely* — **paratereo**, to observe from the side, to keep under surveillance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He accepts the invitation knowing what it is. Luke has Him at three Pharisees' tables and He goes every time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Right there in front of Him was a man whose body was **badly swollen with fluid**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hudropikos** — dropsical. It is a medical term, and Luke is the only New Testament writer who uses it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man is not said to have asked for anything, and he is not described as a guest. He is positioned."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus turned to the experts in the law and to the Pharisees and asked them plainly, \"**Is it allowed to heal someone on the Sabbath, or not?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks first. The trap is sprung before they can spring it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not one of them answered.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *they kept silent*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They could not say no — nothing in the law forbids it. They would not say yes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So He **took hold of the man, healed him, and sent him on his way**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *took hold* is **epilabomenos** — he takes the man by the arm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs, and the last one removes him from the room. The man does not have to sit through what follows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If your donkey, or your ox, **fell into a well on the Sabbath**, which one of you would **leave him down there**? **You would have him out that very moment**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their own practice. Rabbinic discussion of exactly this case survives, and the general ruling permitted rescue."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He argues from what they would do for an animal, on the day in question, without hesitating."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **they had nothing at all to say to that**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says it twice in six verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other Sabbath healing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter before: a woman bent double for eighteen years, and the synagogue ruler objecting. Jesus answered with an ox and a donkey there too — *does not each one of you on the Sabbath loose his ox or donkey from the stall, and lead it away to water?* See the note on Luke 13:10–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same argument, same animals, and the same result: *all His adversaries were put to shame.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says next at the table"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He watches the guests choose the best seats and tells them to take the lowest, and then tells the host to invite *the poor, the maimed, the lame, the blind* — people who cannot return the invitation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the parable of the great supper, where the invited guests all make excuses and the servants are sent to the highways and hedges."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not asked of the man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing. No question about his life, no instruction, no condition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is taken by the arm, healed, and sent home."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of the scene.** He asks the question first, gets no answer, heals the man, sends him out, and only then makes the argument — to a room that had come to watch."}]}, "Luke 14:15–20": {"title": "\"Please excuse me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three men turn down a dinner, and none of them has anything against the host."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What prompted it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How happy the one will be who sits down to eat in the kingdom of God!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A pious remark from a fellow guest at a Pharisee's table, and it is the kind of thing nobody expects to be tested."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus answers it with a story about people who did not come."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two invitations"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **sent invitations out to a long list** of guests. When everything was ready, he **sent his servant around to tell them, \"Come now.\"**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is how it was done. A first invitation to reserve the day, and a second when the food was on the table."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of these men had already accepted. The refusals come after they had said yes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is ready"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The table is set and it is all waiting for you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is asked of them. The cost, the preparation and the timing are the host's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three excuses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have just **bought a field** and I have to go out and look it over.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody buys a field unseen. The inspection happens before the purchase, not after."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have just **bought five yoke of oxen** and I am on my way to try them in the harness.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten oxen, unexamined, already paid for. The same problem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have just **been married**. I cannot come.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third does not even say *please excuse me*. He states it as settled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the excuses have in common"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Land, livestock, and a wife. All three are good — a field, working animals, a marriage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing sinful is named. The dinner is lost to ordinary property and ordinary happiness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And every excuse is in the past tense: *I have just bought, I have just bought, I have just been married.* Things already acquired, keeping them from something being given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one legal exemption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 24 does exempt a newly married man — from military service, for a year."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third man may be quoting it. He is producing a valid reason for not going to war, about a dinner invitation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the master does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The master of the house **was angry**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *\"Go out quickly into the streets and the back lanes… **bring in the poor and the crippled and the lame and the blind**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on Luke 14:21–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Jesus was talking to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is at a Pharisee's house on the Sabbath, having just healed a man with dropsy and told the guests to stop taking the best seats."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He had said, a few verses earlier: *when you give a feast, invite the poor, the maimed, the lame, the blind — and you will be blessed, because they cannot repay you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then He tells a story in which the master does exactly that."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the first men lose.** *\"Not one of those men on the first list will ever taste this dinner of mine.\"* Not because they were refused. Because they were busy, and the food went to people who were not."}]}, "Luke 14:21–24": {"title": "\"There is still room\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The invited guests decline, and the food is already cooked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The anger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The master of the house **was angry**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says it plainly. Three men had accepted and then sent excuses when the meal was served."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first sweep"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go out **quickly**, into **the streets and the back lanes of the town**, and bring in **the poor and the crippled and the lame and the blind**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The four categories Jesus had listed a few verses earlier when telling the guests who to invite to their own feasts — *and you will be blessed, because they cannot repay you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The master does what Jesus had just recommended."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And these are people who could not have come to a normal dinner. Nobody invited the lame to a banquet; they had nothing to give back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The report"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Sir, it is done — **and there is still room**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The best line in the parable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everybody the servant could find in the town has been brought in, and the house is not full."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second sweep"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then go out on **the country roads and along the hedgerows**, and **press them to come in**, whoever you find.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Outside the town now — travellers, and people sleeping rough under hedges."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *press them* is to compel, to constrain. It is urgency rather than force; a beggar in rags would need convincing that the invitation was real."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I want my house full.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the master's stated motive for the whole exercise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to punish the first list, and not to fill the seats out of pride. He wants the house full."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Not one of those men on the first list will ever taste this dinner of mine.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not excluded. They excused themselves — and Luke's Greek in the previous paragraph has all three of them saying so politely. See the note on Luke 14:15–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shape of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Invited, declined. Town, brought in. Countryside, brought in. Room, still."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's account of the same movement: *it was necessary that the word of God should be spoken to you first; but since you reject it… we turn to the Gentiles.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He was sitting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At a Pharisee's table on the Sabbath, as a guest, having watched the other guests choosing the best places."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells the room a story in which the people on the guest list miss dinner and the street gets fed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the servant did not do.** Argue with anybody, or lower the standard of the meal, or ask the master to reduce it. He went out twice and came back both times, and the second time the house filled."}]}, "Luke 14:25–27": {"title": "He turned around to face them", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The crowd is at its largest, and He turns round and makes it harder."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Great crowds were **traveling along with Him** now, and **He turned around to face them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **suneporeuonto** — journeying together with Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is on the road to Jerusalem. The numbers are up, and He stops and faces the direction they are coming from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does with a crowd"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same thing He does in John 6, where the largest gathering He ever had thins out to twelve after a hard saying. See the note on John 6:66–71."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never treats size as the thing to protect."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If anyone comes to Me and **does not hold Me dearer than his own father and mother**, dearer than his wife and his children, dearer than his brothers and sisters — **dearer even than his own life** — he cannot be My disciple.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is **misei**, literally *hate* — a Semitic idiom of comparative preference, the same construction as *Jacob have I loved, Esau have I hated*, and the same one used of Leah being *hated* when the text has already said Jacob loved Rachel more."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a ranking, not an emotion. The Illumination reads it as **dearer**, which is what the idiom means."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the list runs that way"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Father and mother, wife and children, brothers and sisters — every tie a man in that culture had, in descending order of obligation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then *his own life*, which is not a relationship at all. The list runs past family and ends at the self."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whoever will not **pick up his own cross and walk along behind Me** cannot be My disciple.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**His own** cross. Not the one Jesus carried."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the crowd heard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had seen it. Crucifixion was a Roman public work, and a man carrying a crossbeam through the streets was a man already condemned, walking to a place he would not walk back from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in that crowd took the image as a metaphor for inconvenience."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two parables, both about counting: a man building a tower who works out the cost first, and a king with ten thousand men considering another with twenty thousand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which of you, intending to build a tower, **does not sit down first and count the cost**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is telling a growing crowd to do the arithmetic before joining."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this sits in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two paragraphs earlier, a dinner party — the seating, the guest list, and the great supper where the invitations go out to the streets and lanes. See the note on Luke 14:21–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The invitation in that parable is unconditional and free, and the servant is sent out twice to bring people in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then this, on the road, to the people who came."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is doing with the two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke sets the free invitation and the cost of the road next to each other on the same page."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The supper is entered by being brought in. The journey is walked by counting what it takes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus says about His own"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I lay down My life… **no one takes it from Me, but I lay it down of Myself**.* See the note on John 10:17–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He carried His to a place outside the city, and the cross He asks for here is *his own*, which is a different beam."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The closing image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Salt is good; but if the salt has lost its flavour, how shall it be seasoned?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter ends with something that has stopped being what it is."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb.** *Hate* is a Semitic idiom of ranking, the same one that makes Leah *hated* in a sentence where Jacob simply loved Rachel more. It is an order of preference, and the list runs past family to end at the self."}]}, "Luke 14:7–11": {"title": "The long walk down", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He is a guest at somebody's table, watching the other guests seat themselves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It happened, as He went into the house of one of the rulers of the Pharisees to eat bread on the Sabbath, that they watched Him closely.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just healed a man with dropsy in front of them, and asked the question nobody would answer: *is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath?* See the note on Luke 14:1–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Now He is at the meal, and the watching goes the other way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He noticed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He had been **watching the guests choose their seats**, each one **edging toward the places of honor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epechon** — holding his attention on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "At a formal meal, guests reclined on couches and the positions were graded. Everybody in the room could read the seating."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parable"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When you are invited to a wedding feast, **do not go and sit down in the best seat**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The risk"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Someone **more important than you may have been invited too**, and then the host will have to come over and say, '**Would you give this man your place?**'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The host has to intervene, in public, and correct the seating."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The humiliation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And you will **get up in front of everyone and make the long walk to the lowest seat left in the room**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eschaton topon** — the last place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not just demoted. Demoted to whatever is left, which is the bottom, because everybody else has sat down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The alternative"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When you are invited, **go and take the lowest place**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The upgrade"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then your host will come and find you there and say, '**Friend, come up higher — this seat is for you**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phile** — friend."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **in front of all the other guests you will be honored**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same audience, both ways. The passage is honest that the public dimension is what the seating is about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this is not a technique"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read as advice, it is a strategy — take the low seat in order to be moved up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last verse rules that out by generalising it beyond dinner."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **everyone who puts himself forward will be brought low**, and **the one who takes the low place will be lifted up**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both verbs are passive. Somebody else does the raising and the lowering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus says this three times in Luke — here, at the end of the Pharisee and tax collector, and in Matthew's woes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To the host: *\"When you give a dinner or a supper, **do not ask your friends, your brothers, your relatives, nor rich neighbours**, lest they also invite you back and you be repaid.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But when you give a feast, **invite the poor, the maimed, the lame, the blind**. And you will be blessed, **because they cannot repay you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seating advice is followed by guest-list advice, and both are about the economy of return."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody at the table says *blessed is he who shall eat bread in the kingdom of God*, and Jesus tells the parable of the great supper — the invitations, the excuses, and the streets and lanes. See the note on Luke 14:15–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole chapter is one dinner party, and every teaching in it comes out of the room He is sitting in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the descent goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who, being in the form of God… **made Himself of no reputation**… and became obedient to the point of death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore God also has highly exalted Him.* See the note on Philippians 2:9–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same shape, and the same two passives."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the last verse does.** It stops the parable being a seating tactic. Both verbs are passive — the lifting and the lowering are somebody else's to do."}]}, "Luke 15:1–2": {"title": "The complaint that produced three parables", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses of setup, and the whole of Luke 15 is the answer to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who came"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All the **tax collectors** and all the people with **ruined reputations** kept **crowding in close** to Jesus **to hear Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **telonai** — men who bought the right to collect Rome's taxes and kept the surplus. Collaborators, and extortioners by design."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **hamartoloi** — sinners, which in the mouths of the Pharisees meant people who did not keep the purity laws: the trades that made ritual observance impossible, and anybody with a public failure attached to their name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Kept **crowding in close**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **engizontes** — drawing near, and it is an imperfect. They kept doing it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is the same root as the one for approach in the Levitical offering. See the note on Leviticus 1:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they came"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To hear Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says it plainly. Not for miracles."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the Pharisees and the teachers of the law **muttered about it among themselves**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **diegonguzon** — grumbled through, murmured."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the verb the Greek Old Testament uses of Israel in the wilderness, complaining about the manna."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look at Him. **This man welcomes sinners. He sits down and eats with them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses, and the second is the offensive one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eating with somebody in that culture is not a neutral act. A shared table is a declaration of acceptance — which is why the peace offering was a meal. See the note on Leviticus 3:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the charge gets right"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything. It is an accurate description and He never denies it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had already answered a version of it: *those who are well have no need of a physician, but those who are sick. I have not come to call the righteous, but sinners, to repentance.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three parables, told back to back, and all three are about something lost:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A shepherd with a hundred sheep who leaves ninety-nine. See the note on Luke 15:3–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A woman with ten coins who lights a lamp and sweeps the house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a man with two sons."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern in the first two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something is lost, somebody searches, it is found, and the finder calls the neighbours together: *rejoice with me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And each closes with the same line about heaven: *there will be more joy in heaven over one sinner who repents than over ninety-nine just persons who need no repentance*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the third one does differently"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody goes looking. The younger son comes to himself and starts walking, and the father runs. See the note on Luke 15:17–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it does not end with a party. It ends outside the house, with the older brother refusing to come in and the father pleading. See the note on Luke 15:25–28."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke leaves the ending open."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the third parable is aimed at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men in verse 2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The older brother has done everything right, has never left, and is standing outside a celebration objecting that his father is welcoming somebody he should not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the complaint in verse 2, put into a story and handed back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The father's last words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Son, **you are always with me, and all that I have is yours**. It was right that we should make merry and be glad.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Addressed, in the parable, to the men who were grumbling."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What produced the chapter.** A complaint that was factually correct. He welcomes sinners and eats with them — and the answer runs to thirty-two verses and three stories."}]}, "Luke 15:11–16": {"title": "A son in a far country", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The third of three parables told to answer one complaint."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is being told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then all the tax collectors and the sinners drew near to Him to hear Him. And the Pharisees and scribes complained, saying, \"**This Man receives sinners and eats with them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke 15 is one answer in three parts: a lost sheep, a lost coin, and this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first two end with the finder rejoicing. The third is longer and has a second half about somebody who does not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Father, **give me my share of the property now**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that culture the estate passed at death. Asking for it early was as good as saying the father was dead to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no record in the parable of the father arguing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the father **divided what he had between them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **bion** — his living, his means of life. The father divides his own livelihood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The elder brother's share goes to him at the same moment, which matters for the second half of the story."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not many days later the younger son **gathered up everything that was his, left home**, and traveled off **to a distant country**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *gathered up* is a word for converting property into cash. He liquidated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The spending"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there he **threw the whole of it away on wild and reckless living**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **asotos** — from **a-sozo**, unsaved, without deliverance. It carries the sense of a life with no way of being kept."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke does not itemise. The elder brother does that later, and Luke is careful to attribute the details to him rather than to the narrator."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When it was all gone, **a hard famine settled over that country**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The money ran out first and then the famine came. Either alone he might have survived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The job"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So he **hired himself out** to one of the local men, who sent him **into the fields to feed pigs**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ekollethe** — he glued himself to, attached himself. It is the language of a man begging for work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Jew feeding pigs is at the bottom of every category at once — foreign country, unclean animals, hired rather than owned, and hungry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hunger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He got so hungry that he **would gladly have eaten the pods the pigs were eating**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pods are carob — animal fodder."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **nobody gave him anything at all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That sentence ends the section, and it is the bottom of the story."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But when he came to himself* — and the speech he rehearses, and the father who sees him *while he was still a great way off* and runs. See the note on Luke 15:17–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for the father's reaction is **esplanchnisthe** — the gut-word, the same one used of Jesus looking at the crowds. See the note on Matthew 9:35–38."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he rehearses and what he gets to say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He plans: *make me like one of your hired servants.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gets as far as *I am no longer worthy to be called your son* and the father cuts him off with instructions to the servants."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The line about hired servants is never delivered."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the story starts.** With a father who divides his living because he was asked to, and lets a son walk out with it. Nothing in the first half is done to stop him."}]}, "Luke 15:17–20": {"title": "\"He ran\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The son rehearses a speech in a pigsty, and his father ruins the plan by running."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What brings him home"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How many hired men on my father's place have more bread than they can eat, and here I am starving to death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke is honest about the motive. It is hunger, and a comparison — the servants at home eat better than this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here is presented as a noble repentance. He *came to his senses*, which is the language of a man waking up rather than a man reforming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The speech he prepares"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three parts: I have sinned against heaven, I have sinned against you, **take me on as one of your hired men**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third part is a proposal. He has worked out terms — a job, a wage, a way to be useful and stay fed without claiming anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the father sees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While he was still a long way off, his father saw him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The distance is the detail. To see someone that far away you have to have been looking, and to recognise him at that range you have to have been looking for a while."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The running"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His heart went out to the boy, and he ran — he ran down the road to meet him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that culture, an elderly man did not run. It required hitching up the robe and exposing the legs, and it was undignified in a way that would have been noticed by the whole village."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does it anyway, and he does it before a word of the speech is spoken. The embrace and the kiss come first; the confession arrives afterwards, and the third part of it never gets said at all."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the speech never reached.** *Take me on as one of your hired men* is cut off by the father calling for the best robe. The bargain the son had worked out is not refused so much as never allowed into the conversation. See the note on Luke 15:21–24."}]}, "Luke 15:21–24": {"title": "The speech he never finished", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The son has a speech ready. He worked it out in the pigsty, and it had three parts: I have sinned against heaven, I have sinned against you, **make me one of your hired men.**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Watch what happens to the third part."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He gets two-thirds of the way through"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Father, I have sinned against heaven, and I have sinned against you. I am not fit to be called your son —*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the dash is doing real work. He is cut off. The father is already talking to the servants about a robe. The line the son rehearsed hardest — the arrangement he was going to propose, where he earns his keep and everyone's honour is satisfied — never gets said."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Confession is welcomed. The bargain is not allowed to be spoken."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the father calls for, and why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four things, and none of them are comfort items. Every one is a marker of standing in that household:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the best robe** — literally the first robe, the one belonging to the head of the house", "**a ring** — a signet: authority to act in the family name", "**sandals** — servants went barefoot; sons did not", "**the fattened calf** — the animal kept for the one occasion big enough to need it"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not get a bath and a probationary period. He gets dressed as a son in front of everybody, before he has done a single thing to prove himself, and the household is fed on the best animal on the property."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"This son of mine was dead\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The father gives his reason twice, and it is not that the boy repented well:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "This son of mine was dead, and he is alive again. He was lost, and he is found."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dead and alive. Lost and found. Those are the two pairs the whole chapter has been building — the sheep, the coin, and now this. In all three the thing that was missing does nothing to recover itself. It is carried, swept up, run to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The son's repentance is real and it matters. But the story is careful about what it accomplishes: it gets him home. It does not buy the robe."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is doing in this chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus is answering a complaint about the company He keeps. The answer is a father who runs, interrupts an apology, and throws a party — and the point of putting the interruption in is that the son's plan to work his way back was never on the table."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the celebration.** In all three stories the finder calls the neighbours together. The joy is not private, and it is never at the recovered thing's expense. Nobody in the chapter is welcomed home quietly and then reminded later what they cost."}]}, "Luke 15:25–28": {"title": "Music from outside the house", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The parable does not end with the party. It ends in the yard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The older son was **out in the field**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Working, which is where he has been the whole time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has appeared once before in the story — in the opening line, *a certain man had two sons* — and then not at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he hears first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As he came back toward the house he heard **music and dancing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sumphonias kai choron** — symphony and dancing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He hears the celebration before he knows what it is for. Nobody came to tell him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He called **one of the servants** over and asked what all of it meant.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not go in and ask his father. He stops a member of staff in the yard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The report"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Your brother has come home**,\" the servant told him, \"and your father has **killed the fattened calf**, because **he has him back safe and well**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hugiainonta** — in good health, sound."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The servant's summary is warm. Nothing in his phrasing is provocative."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At that the older brother's **face hardened, and he would not go in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **orgisthe** — he was angry, enraged. And **ouk ethelen eiselthein** — he was not willing to enter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A door, a party, and a man on the wrong side of it by choice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who comes out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **his father came outside to him and began pleading with him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parekalei** — an imperfect. He kept entreating."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The father goes out twice in this parable. He ran down the road to the younger son. See the note on Luke 15:17–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Now he leaves his own feast and goes out to the older one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two sons, two exits, and the father makes the first move both times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the older son says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look, **these many years I have been serving you; I never transgressed your commandment at any time**. And yet **you never gave me a young goat**, that I might make merry with my friends.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But as soon as **this son of yours** came, who has devoured your livelihood with harlots, you killed the fatted calf for him.\"* See the note on Luke 15:29–32."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls the years *serving* — the Greek is **douleuo**, the slave verb. And he will not say *my brother*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Son, you are always with me, and all that I have is yours**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *son* here is **teknon** — child. An affectionate word, not the formal one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All that I have is yours* — legally true. The younger son took his share and spent it; what remains belongs to the elder."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has been standing in a field that is his, complaining that he was never given a goat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is never said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whether he goes in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke stops the parable with the father outside, still pleading, and the door open."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was listening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Pharisees and scribes who had just complained *this man receives sinners and eats with them*. See the note on Luke 15:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ending is left open because the answer is theirs to give."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one who never left"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"These many years I have been serving you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two sons, and both are outside the house at some point. One goes to a far country; the other is in the yard, at the party, refusing to come in."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the last scene is.** Not a reunion. A father standing outside his own celebration asking his elder son to come in — and Luke does not record an answer."}]}, "Luke 15:29–32": {"title": "The older brother, and the word he never uses", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The story is usually named after the younger son, but Jesus did not stop where the party started. He told it to people who were annoyed that He ate with the wrong sort, and the last scene is aimed straight at them. It is the only one of the three stories in this chapter that does not end with something found."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Listen to how he talks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four sentences, and every one of them is worth slowing down for:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*all these years I have worked for you* — the word is the one used of a hired hand's labour, not a son's life at home", "*I have never once disobeyed an order of yours* — an order. Not a word from a father; an instruction from a master", "*you never gave me so much as a young goat* — a man who has never asked", "*this son of yours* — not *my brother*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has lived in the house for years and describes it as a job. The tragedy is not that he is wrong about his own record — the father never disputes a word of it — but that a faultless record has left him standing outside his own home, angry, on the one night the place is full of music."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The father's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My child, you are always with me, and everything I have is yours.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read that against the complaint. He asked for a goat. He is told he owns the estate. He has been *working for* what was already in his name. And the word the father uses is not the formal one for a grown son; it is affectionate, the word you would use for your own boy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is being given to him in that sentence. He is being told what has been true the entire time he was earning it."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The thing he never says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Track the vocabulary. The younger son comes home and says *father* — twice. The older son opens with *Look*, and never uses the word at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The younger one lost everything and knew what he was coming home to. The older one lost nothing and did not know where he lived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Jesus ends it unfinished"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The story stops. We are not told whether he came in. Every other thread in the chapter closes — the sheep is carried home, the coin is found, the son is embraced — and this one is left open, because the people Jesus was telling it to were standing in exactly that spot while He told it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the elder son's picture of his father.** Notice that his bitterness is not aimed at his brother first — it is aimed at a father he believes has been withholding a goat for years. That is not what the father is like, and the story has already shown us so. A person's grievance is usually built on a picture of God that the facts do not support, and the correction here is not a rebuke but a piece of information: *everything I have is yours*."}]}, "Luke 15:3–7": {"title": "\"Until he finds it\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first of the three stories in this chapter, told to people who were complaining about the company Jesus kept."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why He is telling it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens with tax collectors and sinners crowding in to hear Him, and the Pharisees muttering: *this man welcomes sinners and eats with them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "All three parables answer that one sentence. The complaint is the reason the chapter exists."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Keep going until he finds it\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The searching has no stated limit. Not for a while, not until dark — until."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the ninety-nine are left *out in the open country*. Jesus does not pause to justify that. The shepherd's arithmetic is not the point of the story; the finding is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He lifts it up onto his shoulders, and the whole way home he is rejoicing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sheep does nothing. It does not find its way back or follow him home — it is carried the entire distance, on the shoulders of the one who came out for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the mood is set on the road, not at the door. He is rejoicing before anyone else knows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The party"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Celebrate with me — I have found my sheep.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of the three stories ends with neighbours being called together. The joy is public each time, and the celebration is never at the recovered thing's expense."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*More joy over one sinner whose mind is turned around than over ninety-nine upright people who have no need of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last phrase carries an edge. Jesus is describing people who see no need of repentance, and He is looking at some while He says it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the three stories add up to.** A sheep that wandered off, a coin that could not have moved itself, and a son who walked out deliberately. Different ways of being lost — and in every case somebody goes looking, and there is a party afterwards. See the notes on Luke 15:8–10, 15:17–20 and 15:21–24."}]}, "Luke 15:8–10": {"title": "The lamp and the broom", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The middle story of the three, and the shortest — but it changes one thing about the picture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The coin could not stray"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A sheep wanders off. A son leaves on purpose. A coin does neither."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was lost in the house, by no fault and no decision of its own, and it could not have found its way anywhere. That is a third kind of lostness, and Jesus puts it deliberately between the other two."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Does she not light a lamp and sweep out the whole house, going over every corner of it until she finds it?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three actions: light, sweep, search. Houses of that period had small high windows and packed earth floors — a coin could sit in the dust for a long time. The lamp is lit because you cannot find anything in that room without one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And again: **until**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was worth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A drachma, roughly a day's wage. Not a fortune — one tenth of what she had."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She turns the entire house over for it, which is the proportion the story wants you to notice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The joy is somewhere specific"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is like that among the angels of God. They are glad over one sinner whose mind is turned around.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first story said *in heaven*; this one says *among the angels*. Jesus is locating the celebration a little more precisely each time."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why a woman.** Jesus tells three stories in a row and gives the middle one to a woman searching her own house — set beside a shepherd and a father. In a chapter arguing about who God is like, He uses all three without comment."}]}, "Luke 16:14–18": {"title": "\"The law and the prophets ran up to John\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four short sayings, and Luke sets them between two parables about money."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is listening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Pharisees were listening to all this, and **because they loved money they sneered at Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exemukterizon** — literally *turned up the nose*. It occurs twice in the New Testament, here and at the cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have just heard the parable of the unjust steward and *you cannot serve God and mammon*. See the note on Luke 16:9–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You are the ones who **make yourselves look righteous in front of other people**, but **God knows what is inside you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *you justify yourselves before men*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that they were failing to keep the standard. That the audience was wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What men hold up as impressive is detestable in the sight of God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **bdelugma** — abomination. It is the word used in Daniel of the abomination of desolation, and in Leviticus of the things that defile."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The boundary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The law and the prophets ran up to the time of John**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same statement Matthew records: *all the Prophets and the Law spoke their prophecies right up to John.* See the note on Matthew 11:12–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both Gospels put John at the join."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has been happening since"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Since then the good news of the kingdom of God has been announced**, and **everyone is pushing to get in**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **biazetai** — the same force-word as Matthew's version."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But it would be **easier for heaven and earth to disappear** than for **the smallest stroke of a letter in the law to fail**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **keraia** — a horn, the little serif that distinguishes one Hebrew letter from another."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the era has ended and in the next breath says not one pen-stroke will fail."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both stand. He says the same in Matthew 5: *do not think that I came to destroy the Law or the Prophets. I did not come to destroy but **to fulfil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing drops off. It is carried to its end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The example"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Anyone who **divorces his wife and marries another woman commits adultery**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An abrupt landing after a statement about eras."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is put there as the demonstration: the Pharisees had built a permissive divorce practice out of a clause in Deuteronomy, and He applies the standard they claimed to hold to the thing they had loosened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had just been told they justify themselves before men. This is what that looked like in practice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke does with the arrangement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Parable about money — sneering — this — and then straight into the rich man and Lazarus, which is about a man in fine linen who fared sumptuously every day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke frames the sayings with two stories about wealth, and the men sneering are described as lovers of money."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line of the next parable"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If they do not hear Moses and the prophets, neither will they be persuaded though one rise from the dead**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is where the chapter ends."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two halves nobody can drop.** The law and the prophets ran up to John, and not one serif will fail. He says both in consecutive sentences."}]}, "Luke 16:9–13": {"title": "\"Faithful with what belongs to someone else\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus has just told the strangest parable in Luke — a dishonest manager who cooks the books and gets commended for shrewdness — and this is what He draws out of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scale He puts money on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read how He describes it:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*a very small thing*", "*something as small as money*", "*what belongs to someone else*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times, and each one demotes it. Money is the small thing, the borrowed thing, the test case — not the prize."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set against it is *what is genuinely valuable* and *anything of your own*. The comparison is not between poverty and wealth. It is between two orders of property, and the one everybody worries about is the lesser."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The strange logic of the first line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Use the money of this world to make friends for yourselves, so that when you finally fail, you will be welcomed into a home that lasts.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase for money is literally *unrighteous mammon* — wealth belonging to a crooked world. Jesus does not tell them to avoid it; He tells them to spend it in a direction that outlasts it. The manager in the parable used his master's money to secure a future. Do that, He says, with better aim."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line that ends it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You cannot serve God and money both.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is to serve as a slave, and the point is not that money is evil but that it competes. Two owners cannot both have you. Everything before this — the smallness, the borrowed quality, the shrewd spending — is preparing for a sentence about who owns whom."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What \"faithful in little\" means here.** Not that careful budgeting earns spiritual promotion. The argument is about character being one thing: how a person handles the small borrowed thing is what they will do with the large permanent one, because it is the same person doing it."}]}, "Luke 17:11–14": {"title": "As they went along the road", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Ten men are sent to show a priest something that is not there yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On His way to Jerusalem Jesus was traveling along **the border country between Samaria and Galilee**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The frontier. The strip where the two populations met and neither was at home."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke has already recorded a Samaritan village refusing Him passage, and the disciples asking whether to call down fire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ten"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ten men with leprosy met Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **leproi** — a category covering several skin conditions, all of which carried the same legal consequence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The border is exactly where a mixed group of Jews and Samaritans could exist. Their disease had already put them outside both communities."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **stood off at a distance** and raised their voices.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law required it: *he shall dwell alone; his dwelling shall be outside the camp*, and the leper was to cry *unclean, unclean*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They shout because they are not permitted to come near."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they shout"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Jesus! Master! Have mercy on us!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Epistata** — the word Luke uses for a superior, a commander. It is the word the disciples use of Him, and no one else in the Gospel uses it except Luke's own characters on the inside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Men outside the camp address Him the way the twelve do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Touch them. Speak a word of healing. Say anything about their faith."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elsewhere He touches a leper — *He put out His hand and touched him, saying, \"I am willing; be cleansed\"*. See the note on Luke 5:12–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here He does not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go and show yourselves to the priests**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus 14 sets out the procedure: a healed leper presents himself to the priest, who inspects him and pronounces him clean, and then the ritual of the two birds, the cedar, the scarlet, and the hyssop follows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priest is the certifying authority. He does not heal. He confirms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem with the instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are still lepers when He gives it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Being sent to a priest for inspection while visibly diseased is being sent to be turned away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The obedience"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **as they went along the road, they were made clean**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **en to hupagein autous** — in their going."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The healing happens in the interval between the instruction and the destination. Not at the word, and not at the priest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where else this order shows up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Naaman told to dip seven times in the Jordan, and the leprosy gone at the seventh."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priests at the Jordan, whose feet had to be in the water before the river cut off. See the note on Joshua 3:9–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The water pots at Cana, filled to the brim before anything happened. See the note on John 2:6–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The count"}, {"k": "p", "t": "All ten went. All ten were cleansed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The obedience is not distinguished at this stage, and neither is the result. What separates them happens on the way back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What one of them does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One of them, when he saw that he had been healed, **turned around and came back**… and the man was a Samaritan.* See the note on Luke 17:15–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nine keep going to the priest, which is what they were told to do."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the healing happened.** *As they went along the road.* Not at the word, and not at the priest — in the walking between the two."}]}, "Luke 17:15–19": {"title": "Only one came back", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Nine men do exactly what they were told, and one does something else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten lepers were sent to the priests and cleansed on the road. See the note on Luke 17:11–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One of them, **when he saw that he had been healed, turned around and came back**, praising God at the top of his voice.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hupestrepsen** — he turned back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is going the wrong way. The other nine are heading for the priest, which is the instruction he was also given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The volume"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Praising God at the top of his voice.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Meta phones megales** — with a loud voice. The same phrase Luke used four verses earlier when the ten *raised their voices* and shouted for mercy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same volume, redirected."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The posture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **fell on his face at the feet of Jesus** and thanked Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eucharistōn** — giving thanks. The word behind *eucharist*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten stood at a distance because the law required it. One is now face down at His feet, which the law also would not have allowed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause Luke puts last"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the man was a Samaritan**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke holds it to the end of the sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does the same with the man on the Jericho road: priest, Levite, and then *a certain Samaritan, as he journeyed, came where he was*. See the note on Luke 10:30–35."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Were not all ten of them made clean? Where are the other nine? Was there nobody found to come back and give the glory to God except this foreigner?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **allogenes** — of another race. A rare word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It appears on the stone inscriptions that stood at the barrier in the temple, warning that no foreigner might pass beyond into the inner courts on pain of death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus uses the temple's own word for the one man who came back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the nine did wrong"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing, strictly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were told to go to the priests, and they went. The narrative does not accuse them of disobedience, and it does not un-heal them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word to the one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get up and go on your way. Your faith has made you well**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **he pistis sou sesoken se** — your faith has saved you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sozo** — the verb for both healing and salvation. Luke has used *cleansed* of all ten and *healed* of this one, and now uses the widest word of the same man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something happened to him at Jesus' feet that did not happen on the road."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb He starts with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Get up* — **anastas**, the participle of the resurrection word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He came face down and is told to rise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this sits in Luke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately after: *when will the kingdom of God come?* and the answer, *the kingdom of God is within you*. See the note on Luke 17:20–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And immediately before it, the disciples asking *increase our faith*, and the answer about the mustard seed and the mulberry tree."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A question about how to get more faith, and then a Samaritan whose faith is commended for turning round."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word.** **Allogenes** — the temple's own term, carved on the barrier stone that told foreigners how far in they could come. Used here of the one man who came all the way."}]}, "Luke 17:20–21": {"title": "Already here among you", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "They ask for a date, and He tells them the category is wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Pharisees asked Him **when the kingdom of God was going to come**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the question everybody was asking, and it assumes an event with a date."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were expecting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A visible restoration: Rome out, David's line back, Jerusalem at the centre. The expectation was not unreasonable — the prophets had said a great deal about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The kingdom of God is **not the kind of thing you can watch for and measure**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meta parateraseos** — with observation. The noun belongs to the vocabulary of watching for omens and signs, and to medical observation of symptoms."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a rare word, used once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it rules out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Nobody will be able to say, 'Here it is,' or 'There it is'**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same construction He uses three verses later of false claims: *look here, or look there — do not go after them*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pointing at a place is not how this is located."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because the kingdom of God is **already here among you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **entos humon estin**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Entos** can mean *within* or *among, in the midst of*. The translators have divided over it for centuries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why *among* fits the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is speaking to Pharisees, and the same men are told a few chapters later *you are like whitewashed tombs*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Within you* would put the kingdom inside people He has just been in conflict with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Among you* points at Himself, standing in front of them, and that is the sense the context carries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being claimed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That the thing they are watching the horizon for is in the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The days will come when you will desire to see one of the days of the Son of Man, and **you will not see it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For as the lightning that flashes out of one part under heaven shines to the other part… **so also the Son of Man will be in His day**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To the Pharisees: it is here and you cannot point at it. To the disciples: when it comes, nobody will need to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two answers, two audiences, one paragraph."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then Noah and Lot"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As it was in the days of Noah… they ate, they drank, they married*, and *likewise as it was in the days of Lot*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Remember Lot's wife.* See the note on Luke 17:28–33."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the kingdom looked like in Luke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The blind see, the lame walk, the lepers are cleansed… and **the poor have the gospel preached to them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *if I cast out demons with the finger of God, surely the kingdom of God has come upon you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke keeps attaching the word to things happening in front of people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was said at the start"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The Spirit of the LORD is upon Me**, because He has anointed Me to preach the gospel to the poor.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He began to say to them, \"**Today this Scripture is fulfilled in your hearing**.\"* See the note on Luke 4:18–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Today**, in the synagogue at Nazareth. The same claim, made at the beginning of the ministry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says it consists of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the kingdom of God is **not eating and drinking, but righteousness and peace and joy in the Holy Spirit**.* See the note on Romans 14:16–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things that cannot be pointed at either."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word.** **Parateresis** — the watching that looks for signs and symptoms. He tells them the kingdom will not show up under that kind of observation, while standing in front of them."}]}, "Luke 17:5–6": {"title": "\"Give us more faith\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The apostles ask for an increase, and the answer they get is not more of what they had in mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the four verses before it. Jesus has just said that if your brother sins against you seven times a day and turns back seven times saying he repents, you must forgive him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give us more faith* is what they say next. It is not a request about mountains or miracles. It is a response to being told to forgive somebody seven times before dinner."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If your faith were the size of a mustard seed, you could say to this mulberry tree, \"Pull yourself up by the roots and plant yourself in the sea,\" and it would obey you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not give them more. He tells them the quantity was never the issue."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A mustard seed was proverbially the smallest thing anyone handled — and the mulberry, with its deep root system, was proverbially among the hardest trees to shift. Smallest possible faith, hardest possible object, and the tree obeys."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction inside it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You could **say** to this tree.* The faith in the illustration is exercised by speaking to the thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is worth noticing in a passage about forgiveness. The uprooting Jesus describes is not something felt into being; it is addressed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A parable about a servant who comes in from the field and serves dinner before eating — *we are unworthy servants; we have only done our duty.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus keeps the two together: faith the size of a seed can uproot a tree, and the people exercising it are not to think of themselves as impressive for it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word \"size.\"** The Greek is literally *if you have faith as a grain of mustard* — a comparison of kind as much as quantity. Something alive and small, which is what a seed is. Not a measured amount of a substance."}]}, "Luke 18:18–23": {"title": "\"One thing you still lack\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man with everything asks what he has to do, and the answer takes everything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Good teacher, **what do I have to do to inherit eternal life?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An inheritance is the one thing in the world nobody does anything to get. It comes by birth, by relationship, by somebody's death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks what to **do** to get one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word Jesus picks up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why do you call Me good?** No one is good except God alone.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not deny it. He asks the man whether he has thought about what he just said."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man used *good* as a compliment between equals. Jesus takes it as a category with one occupant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commandments listed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not commit adultery, do not murder, do not steal, do not tell lies about people, honor your father and your mother.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five, and all five are from the second table — the ones about other people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first table, the commandments about God, is not read out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have kept every one of those**,\" the man said, \"ever since I was a boy.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it without hesitation and Jesus does not contradict him. Mark's account adds the line Luke leaves out: *then Jesus, looking at him, loved him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"One thing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There is **one thing you are still missing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a list. He has kept the five, and one item remains."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the one thing is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go and **sell everything you own** and **give the money to the poor**, and you will have **treasure in heaven**. Then **come back and follow Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four instructions. The first two empty him, the third relocates what he had, and the fourth is the actual invitation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same invitation given free to fishermen and a tax collector, with a condition attached that was attached to nobody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commandment that was left out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first one. *You shall have no other gods before Me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was asked to prove he had kept the table that was never recited."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the man heard it, **sorrow came over him, because he was very rich**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke ends the scene there. The man does not argue, does not bargain, and is not recorded as leaving in Luke's wording — Matthew and Mark say he went away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the only person in the Gospels who is invited to follow and does not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How hard it is** for those who have riches to enter the kingdom of God! It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And when they ask *who then can be saved*, the answer is *the things which are impossible with men are possible with God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The next man in Luke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zacchaeus, in the very next chapter — rich, and he gives half his goods to the poor without being asked. Luke puts them one after another. See the note on Luke 19:1–10."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he came for.** He asked how to inherit. He was told to sell, give, and come — and the treasure was named as already waiting in heaven before he took a step."}]}, "Luke 18:24–27": {"title": "\"What is impossible for people is not impossible for God\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A rich man has just walked away sad, and Jesus watches him go before He says anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus saw the sorrow settle over him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comment that follows is not aimed at the man's back as a rebuke. Luke records Jesus watching the grief land, and Mark's account adds that He had looked at him and loved him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The camel and the needle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is easier for a camel to squeeze through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to get into the kingdom of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Attempts have been made to soften this — a small gate in Jerusalem called the Needle's Eye, or a rope rather than a camel. There is no evidence for the gate, and the rope theory rests on a variant reading."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is meant to be impossible. That is why the disciples react the way they do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then who can possibly be saved?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the right response, and it tells you what they had assumed. Wealth was read as evidence of God's blessing; if the obviously blessed cannot get in, nobody can."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus does not correct the logic. He accepts it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What is impossible for people is not impossible for God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say it is difficult, or that rich people need to try harder. He moves the whole question out of the category of human capacity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means the answer covers the disciples too — the ones who had just pointed out that they left everything."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who it turns out to be possible for.** Two chapters later Luke gives us Zacchaeus, a rich tax collector, up a tree, and Jesus inviting Himself to dinner. Luke tells the impossible saying and then tells the story of it happening."}]}, "Luke 19:1–10": {"title": "\"Zacchaeus, hurry down\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man climbs a tree to get a look at Jesus and ends up hosting Him for dinner."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **chief tax collector**, and he was **rich**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **architelones** — a word that appears nowhere else in Greek literature. Not a tax collector but the head of a district's collectors, taking a cut of everything they took."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jericho sat on a major trade route. It was a lucrative posting, and it made him a collaborator with Rome and a thief in his neighbours' eyes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tree"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He could not get a look at Him over the crowd, **because he was a short man**. So he **ran on ahead and climbed up into a sycamore tree**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A wealthy official in that culture did not run, and did not climb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sycamore-fig has a low, broad trunk and wide branches — easy to get into, and easy to be seen in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **looked up** and said, \"**Zacchaeus, hurry down. I am staying at your house today.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He looks up, uses the man's name, and invites Himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no record of anyone telling Him who it was."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And nothing is asked first. No repentance is required as a condition of the visit, and no conversation about his profession precedes it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crowd"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone who saw it started grumbling. \"**He has gone in to be the guest of a sinner**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke uses the word for muttering — the same verb used of Israel in the wilderness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Zacchaeus does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, here and now I am **giving half of everything I own to the poor**, and if I have cheated anyone, **I will pay it back four times over**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody asked him for either."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law required a fifth added to restitution. Fourfold was the penalty for stolen livestock — the maximum in the law, and the figure David pronounced on the rich man in Nathan's parable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He volunteers the harshest rate available, on himself, having already given half away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The dinner invitation comes first. The generosity comes after."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Compare the rich young ruler two chapters earlier, who was told to sell what he had and *went away sorrowful, for he had great possessions.* Same subject, opposite sequence, opposite outcome."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Today salvation has come to this house** — for this man **too is a son of Abraham**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Too.* The crowd had written him out of the family. Jesus writes him back in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The Son of Man came to seek and to save what was lost.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke puts the mission statement of the Gospel in the mouth of Jesus, at the end of this story, about a man who climbed a tree."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who else was in Jericho that week.** A blind beggar on the roadside who would not stop shouting, and got his sight. Luke puts Bartimaeus in chapter 18 and Zacchaeus in chapter 19 — one man nobody would let speak, and one nobody would let near. See the note on Mark 10:46–52."}]}, "Luke 19:16–19": {"title": "\"Trustworthy with a very small thing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two servants report, and the arithmetic of the reward has nothing to do with the arithmetic of the return."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they made"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Sir, **your one mina has earned ten more**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Sir, **your one mina has made five more**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same starting capital — every servant got one mina. Different results."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose money it was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both of them say *your* mina. Neither claims it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is emphatic in the first: *your mina has earned* — the servant credits the money rather than himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the master says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Well done. You are a good servant.**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You proved trustworthy with **a very small thing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A mina was roughly three months' wages. Not nothing — and the master calls it *very small*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever scale he is working on, that sum is at the bottom of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reward"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **take charge of ten towns**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cities, in exchange for three months' wages well handled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the second: *\"You take charge of five towns.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proportion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten minas earned → ten towns. Five earned → five towns. The reward tracks the return exactly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But the *scale* is absurd. Nothing about a small sum of money merits a province, and the master hands them out without comment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The money was a test, not a transaction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No comparison between the two servants. The one who made five is not asked why he did not make ten."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are rewarded on the same principle, and the master's pleasure is not divided between them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke has already recorded Jesus saying it plainly: *he who is faithful in what is least is faithful also in much… if you have not been faithful in the unrighteous mammon, who will commit to your trust the true riches?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Money, in that saying, is the small thing and the test. The *true riches* are something else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The context"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parable is told *because they were expecting the kingdom of God to appear at any moment*, and because He was near Jerusalem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He answers an expectation of immediate arrival with a story about a long absence and what to do during it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one who did nothing.** A third servant hands back the same mina, wrapped in a cloth, and gives his reason: *I was afraid of you. You are a hard man.* See the note on Luke 19:20–23."}]}, "Luke 19:20–23": {"title": "The mina in a cloth", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The third servant returns the money intact and gives his reason first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he hands back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Sir, **here is your mina**. I kept it **wrapped up safe in a cloth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing lost. He is not a thief or a spendthrift. By his own accounting he has been careful."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A mina was about three months' wages — the same amount given to each of the ten servants in this parable, unlike the talents in Matthew's version where the sums differ."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Because I was afraid of you. You are a hard man.** You collect what you never put down, and you harvest what you never planted.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason is stated before the excuse. He was afraid, and he was afraid because of what he thought the man was like."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *hard* is **austeros** — severe, exacting. It is the root of *austere*, and it was used of unripe fruit: sharp, not sweet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the master does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Out of your own mouth I will judge you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not accept the description or argue with it. He works inside it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So you knew** I was a hard man…? Then **why did you not put my money on deposit**, so that when I came back I could at least have collected it with interest?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If that had really been his conviction, the bank was the obvious minimum. Fear that does nothing at all is not even consistent with itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was actually given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the instruction: *\"**Put this money to work** until I return.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a trading word — do business with this. It is an invitation to activity, handed out with the capital."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the master had not asked for ten minas back. The first servant produced ten, the second five, and both were commended without comparison."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the others got"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You proved trustworthy with **a very small thing**, so take charge of **ten towns**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three months' wages, and cities in return. The reward is out of all proportion to the sum, which tells you the money was never the point. See the note on Luke 19:16–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other version"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew 25 tells a similar parable with talents and different amounts, and the third servant says nearly the same sentence: *I knew you to be a hard man… so I was afraid, and I went and hid your talent in the ground.* See the note on Matthew 25:24–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two parables, two servants, and the same self-justification in both — a description of the master, and fear following from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this parable is told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because He was **close to Jerusalem now**, and because they were expecting the kingdom of God **to appear at any moment**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives the reason for the story. They thought it was about to happen; He tells them about a nobleman going away for a long time."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the man believed, and what was true.** He said the master reaped where he had not sown. In fact the master had sown — he had put a mina in this man's hand himself. The one thing his description got wrong is the thing he was holding."}]}, "Luke 19:36–40": {"title": "The stones would start shouting", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The one day He accepts the acclamation instead of stopping it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The road"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As He rode along, people kept **spreading their cloaks out on the road** in front of Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is an imperfect — they kept doing it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cloaks on the road is a coronation gesture. When Jehu was declared king, *each man took his garment and put it under him on the top of the steps, and blew trumpets, saying, \"Jehu is king!\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When He came to the place where **the road starts down from the Mount of Olives**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point where the path comes over the ridge and the whole temple platform appears below, across the Kidron valley."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke marks the spot exactly, because of what happens at it a few verses later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shout"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The whole crowd of His disciples **broke out in joy, praising God at the top of their voices**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *rejoicing to praise God with a loud voice*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were praising Him for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **all the wonderful things they had seen Him do**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dunameon** — acts of power."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives the reason, and it is a list of things already witnessed. Bartimaeus is a few verses back, and Lazarus is in John's account of the same week."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Blessed is the King who comes in the name of the Lord!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 118, and every pilgrim knew it — it was sung going up to the feasts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But the psalm says *blessed is he who comes*. Luke's crowd adds a word: **the King**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Peace in heaven, and glory in the highest!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An echo of what the angels said over Bethlehem — *glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angels said peace on earth. The crowd says peace in heaven."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke sets the two sentences at the two ends of the Gospel, and they do not match."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Some of the Pharisees in the crowd said to Him, \"**Teacher, tell Your disciples to stop**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epitimeson** — rebuke them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are asking Him to do what He has done all through Luke: silence people who say who He is. He has told demons to be quiet, and healed men not to tell anyone, and the disciples not to say He was the Christ."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I tell you, **if these people were silent, the very stones would start shouting**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kraxousin** — cry out, scream."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one day He will not have it stopped."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Habakkuk has the image: *the stone will cry out from the wall, and the beam from the timbers will answer it* — said of a house built by injustice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The date"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tenth of Nisan. The day the Passover lamb was selected and brought into the house. See the note on Joshua 4:19–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four days later He is killed, and in between the authorities examine Him in public and find nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does at that exact spot"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now as He drew near, **He saw the city and wept over it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses later, at the place where the road comes over the ridge. See the note on Luke 19:41–44."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd is still shouting while He is crying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word He uses there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If you had known, even you, especially in this your day, **the things that make for your peace**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peace, for the third time in six verses. The disciples shout it, the crowd's psalm implies it, and He says the city cannot see it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Goes into the temple and drives out the traders: *My house is a house of prayer, but you have made it a den of thieves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The triumphal entry ends at an overturned table."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one thing He would not silence.** He had spent the whole Gospel telling people to say nothing. Asked to stop it here, He says the stones would take over."}]}, "Luke 19:41–44": {"title": "\"You did not recognise the time\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He is riding into Jerusalem to shouts of *Hosanna*, the crowds are spreading their cloaks on the road, and He is crying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weeping"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When He came near enough to see the city, **He wept over it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **klaio** — to wail, to sob out loud. It is not the quiet word John uses at Lazarus's tomb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is at the top of the Mount of Olives, with the whole city and the temple spread out below in the sun, at the height of the only public acclamation He ever received."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody could tell Him anything about Himself from the size of a crowd."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"On this day of all days\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If you had only known, **even now, on this day of all days**, what would bring you peace.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a broken sentence. He does not finish it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**This day.** Nisan 10, the day the Passover lamb was taken into the house and set aside — four days before it was killed. Israel was choosing its lamb that morning, and the Lamb was coming down the hill."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Hidden from your eyes\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But it is hidden from your eyes.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The peace was there. The seeing was not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He describes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your enemies will **throw up a wall of earth around you** and close you in and press you from every side… **they will not leave one stone standing on another**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roman siege practice, described forty years before it happened. Titus built a wall of earth right round Jerusalem in AD 70 to stop anyone escaping, and Josephus, who was there, describes it — timber stripped from the countryside for miles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The temple came down and the stones were prised apart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason He gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"— **because you did not recognise the time when God came to you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the time of your visitation* — **episkope**, the moment of being looked in on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not because of their sins in general. Because of a specific missed morning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Daniel had already said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After the sixty-two sevens, the Anointed One will be cut off… the people of the prince who is to come will destroy the city and the sanctuary.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written in Babylon five hundred years earlier, in that order: the Messiah cut off first, the city destroyed after. See the note on Daniel 9:26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is weeping over a date that was already on paper."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He does immediately after.** Rides down the hill, walks into the temple, and turns over the tables. The weeping and the whip are the same afternoon. See the note on Matthew 21:12–17."}]}, "Luke 2:25–28": {"title": "The old man who was told he would not die yet", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four sentences, and the Holy Spirit is named in three of them — unusually dense even for Luke, who mentions the Spirit more than the other Gospel writers combined."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three movements"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the Spirit **was on him**", "it had been **shown to him** by the Spirit that he would not see death until he had seen the Messiah", "**led by the Spirit**, he came into the temple"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "A standing presence, a specific word, and then a prompting on a particular morning. All three are needed for the scene to happen, and Luke lists them in order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as the parents carried the child Jesus in.* Simeon walks into the temple courts at the moment one poor family arrives with a baby to do what the law required — one of many families doing the same thing that week."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing marked them out. Luke gives no star, no crowd, no announcement. An old man crosses a courtyard and takes a child out of a stranger's arms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had been promised, exactly"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that he would be spared death. That he *would not see death until* — a deferral, not an exemption. The promise had an end built into it, and Simeon plainly knew it: the first thing he says after this is that his master can now let him go in peace."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he was waiting for.** *The comfort of Israel* — the Greek is *paraklesis*, from the same root as the name Jesus gives the Spirit in John, the one called alongside. Simeon was waiting for the consolation of his people and was carrying it in his arms before he finished the sentence."}]}, "Luke 2:29–32": {"title": "Simeon's song", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An old man holds a six-week-old baby and asks to be dismissed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man in Jerusalem whose name was Simeon, and this man was just and devout, **waiting for the Consolation of Israel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paraklesin** — the comfort, the consoling. Related to **parakletos**, the Helper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the Holy Spirit was upon him.* See the note on Luke 2:25–28."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise he had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It had been revealed to him by the Holy Spirit that **he would not see death before he had seen the Lord's Christ**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So he came by the Spirit into the temple… and **took Him up in his arms** and blessed God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The song is sung with the child in his hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Now, Master, You can let Your servant go in peace**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **despota** — master, owner of a slave. It is where *despot* comes from, and Luke uses it here and nowhere else of God in his Gospel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **apolueis** — release, dismiss, discharge. The word for freeing a slave or standing down a sentry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simeon speaks as a man who has been on duty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warrant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Just as You promised me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kata to rhema sou** — according to Your word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not asking for a favour. He is reporting that a condition has been met."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **my own eyes have seen Your salvation**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **to soterion sou** — Your salvation, neuter. Not a doctrine, and in his arms."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is holding a baby and calls it salvation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The audience"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The salvation You have made ready **in full view of every people**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kata prosopon panton ton laon** — before the face of all peoples."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Prepared publicly, in the plural."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"A **light to open the eyes of the nations**, and the **glory of Your people Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eis apokalupsin ethnon** — for revelation to the Gentiles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah, twice: *I will also give You as a light to the Gentiles, that You should be My salvation to the ends of the earth*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gentiles first in the sentence, in the temple, at the presentation of a Jewish firstborn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the family was doing there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The purification, and the redemption of the firstborn. Luke notes the offering they brought: *a pair of turtledoves or two young pigeons*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus allows the birds only *if she cannot afford a lamb*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says to Mary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Behold, this Child is destined for the **fall and rising of many in Israel**, and for a **sign which will be spoken against** — yes, **a sword will pierce through your own soul also**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first prophecy about Jesus in Luke after the birth includes a sword through His mother."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the song went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the *Nunc Dimittis*, from the first words of the Latin. Sung at Compline, the last office of the day, and at evensong — a night prayer for centuries, because it is about a man being allowed to sleep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who arrives next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And coming in at that instant she gave thanks to the Lord.* See the note on Luke 2:36–38."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two old people in one paragraph, and both had been waiting."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word he uses of himself.** *Dismiss Your servant* — the term for standing down a sentry. He had been given a watch to keep, and he is reporting that it is over."}]}, "Luke 2:33–35": {"title": "The sword and the sign", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Simeon blesses the parents and then says something to the mother alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph and the child's mother stood there **marveling at the things being said** about Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **thaumazontes** — wondering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had already had an angel each, shepherds with a story, and a name given before conception. And they are still astonished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke has already noted her habit: *but Mary kept all these things and pondered them in her heart*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Simeon **blessed them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Them — the parents. Not the child. Luke is careful about it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just held the boy and sung. See the note on Luke 2:29–32."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the next words are for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And then said **to Mary, His mother**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph is not addressed. What follows is said to her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The appointment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This child is **appointed for the falling and the rising again of many in Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **keitai** — is set, is laid."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Falling first. The order in the sentence is not the comfortable one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah has both: *He will be as a sanctuary, but a stone of stumbling and a rock of offence to both the houses of Israel*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes that at the end of Romans 9, and Peter in his first letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sign"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And He will be **a sign that people argue about and speak against**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **semeion antilegomenon** — a sign spoken against, contradicted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A sign is meant to point. This one will be argued with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the same of the message: *we preach Christ crucified, to the Jews a stumbling block and to the Greeks foolishness*. See the note on 1 Corinthians 1:18–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So that **what has been hidden in many hearts will finally come out into the open**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dialogismoi** — reasonings, the thoughts running in a person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word Jesus uses of the disciples' doubts after the resurrection: *why do these doubts arise in your hearts?* See the note on Luke 24:36–43."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He functions as a revealer. What people do with Him shows what was already there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sword"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **a sword will pass through your own soul too**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **rhomphaia** — not the short **machaira** but the large Thracian broadsword. The word used in Revelation of what comes out of His mouth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simeon does not explain it. He says it to a teenage mother holding a six-week-old baby and stops."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where she is standing thirty-three years later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now there stood by the cross of Jesus **His mother**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *when Jesus therefore saw His mother, and the disciple whom He loved standing by, He said to His mother, \"Woman, behold your son!\" Then He said to the disciple, \"Behold your mother!\" And from that hour that disciple took her to his own home.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where she is at the beginning of Acts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These all continued with one accord in prayer and supplication, with the women and **Mary the mother of Jesus**, and with His brothers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the upper room, and His brothers with her — the same brothers who had not believed Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had brought that day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two turtledoves. The offering Leviticus allows *if she cannot afford a lamb*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The family could not afford the lamb, and had brought one anyway."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who the second half is addressed to.** He blesses both parents, and then turns and speaks to her — falling before rising, a sign that gets argued with, and a broadsword."}]}, "Luke 2:36–38": {"title": "Anna, still watching", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses for a woman nobody else in the Bible mentions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There was also **a prophetess** there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **prophetis**. It occurs twice in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives her the title before he gives her name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Anna** — the Greek form of **Hannah**, which means *grace* or *favour*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The other Hannah stood in a sanctuary praying so hard the priest thought she was drunk, and was given a son. See the note on 1 Samuel 1:9–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lineage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The daughter of **Phanuel**, from the **tribe of Asher**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Phanuel** is the Greek form of **Peniel** — *the face of God*. The place where Jacob wrestled and said *I have seen God face to face, and my life is preserved*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Asher** is one of the ten northern tribes, scattered by Assyria seven centuries earlier. Luke records her tribe anyway, which means she knew it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She had lived with her husband **seven years** after her marriage, and then she had been **a widow until she was eighty-four**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek can be read two ways: a widow of eighty-four years, or a widow now aged eighty-four."}, {"k": "p", "t": "If the first, and she married at the usual age, she is past a hundred."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The habit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She **never left the temple**, worshiping God **night and day with fasting and prayer**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *she did not depart from the temple*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Night first. Luke writes it in the Jewish order, in which the day begins at sunset."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Decades of it, and nothing to show anyone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She came up **at that very moment**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **aute te hora epistasa** — standing by at that same hour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simeon has just finished the song and the words to Mary. She walks in on the end of it. See the note on Luke 2:29–32."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And she began to **thank God and to speak about the child**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs. Vertical, then horizontal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *speak about* is **elalei** — an imperfect. She kept on talking about Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who she talked to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To everyone who was **still waiting for Jerusalem to be set free**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **lutrosin** — redemption, ransom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There was a group of them, and she knew who they were. Simeon was *waiting for the Consolation of Israel*; these people were waiting for the redemption of the city."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's opening chapters are full of people who have been waiting a long time: Zacharias and Elizabeth, Simeon, Anna, and the shepherds who were simply on shift."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she is not given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A song. Luke records Mary's, Zacharias', the angels', and Simeon's — four canticles in two chapters — and gives Anna none."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He records that she spoke and not what she said."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pairing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke does this repeatedly: a man and a woman, side by side, in matched scenes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zacharias and Mary both get an angel. Simeon and Anna both get the child. And later, a shepherd looking for a sheep and a woman looking for a coin."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she had been doing for sixty years"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fasting and praying in a building, as a widow, in a society where a childless widow had nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And she was in the right room on the right morning."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Luke records her doing.** Not a song. She thanked God, and then went and told the people who were still waiting — and he notes there was a group of them."}]}, "Luke 2:46–50": {"title": "\"My Father's business\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only recorded episode between infancy and the Jordan, and the first recorded words of Jesus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they found Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the temple, sitting right among the teachers, listening to them and asking them questions.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what a student did — the rabbinic method was question and answer, and a learner sat among the teachers rather than in front of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke is careful about the posture. He is not lecturing; He is sitting in the circle, listening first, then asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What astonished them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone who heard Him was astonished at how much He understood and at the answers He gave.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are named: what He grasped, and what He said back. A twelve-year-old holding His end of a discussion with the temple teachers for three days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Mary's question and His answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Child, why have You treated us like this? Your father and I have been searching everywhere for You, sick with worry.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Why were you searching for Me? Did you not know that I had to be taken up with My Father's business?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She says *your father*. He answers *My Father*, and the contrast is unmistakable in the Greek. It is the first thing He is recorded as saying, and it distinguishes between the two."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase can be rendered *in My Father's house* or *about My Father's business* — the Greek is elliptical, and both are defensible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But they did not understand what He was telling them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says it plainly about the two people who knew most about who He was — who had both had angelic visits. Neither the annunciation nor the shepherds nor Simeon had prepared them for this sentence."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He does next.** He goes home to Nazareth and lives under their care for eighteen more years. The boy who has just said He must be about His Father's business spends the next two decades in a carpenter's household. See the note on Luke 2:51–52."}]}, "Luke 2:51–52": {"title": "Eighteen years in one sentence", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the last thing the Gospels say about Jesus before He is thirty. Everything between the temple at twelve and the Jordan is covered by two verses, and what they choose to record is unexpected."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He went back down to Nazareth with them, and He lived under their care and did as they said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had just told Mary and Joseph He had to be about His Father's business. Then He goes home and spends eighteen years under theirs. Luke puts the two right next to each other without comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Went on growing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is imperfect — continuous, over time, not a state He arrived at. And Luke lists four directions at once:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["in wisdom", "in stature", "in favour with God", "in favour with the people around Him"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third is the one that stops people. Whatever it means, Luke was willing to write it: a real childhood, a real adolescence, real development. Not a divine adult in a small body waiting for the years to pass."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Mary does.** *She kept every one of these things treasured up in her heart* — Luke says something similar after the shepherds' visit. He seems to be telling his readers where parts of this account came from, and the answer is a woman who had been storing things up for decades."}]}, "Luke 2:6–7": {"title": "Wrapped, and laid in a feeding trough", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke tells the birth in two sentences and spends most of them on where the baby was put."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Wrapped Him around and around in strips of cloth\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ordinary practice — newborns were bound in bands of cloth, believed to help the limbs grow straight. Every mother in that world did it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is not ordinary is that Luke mentions it three times in this chapter. The angel gives it to the shepherds as the identifying sign: *you will find a baby wrapped in strips of cloth and lying in a feeding trough.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sign by which the Messiah could be recognised was that He looked exactly like any other newborn, in a place no other newborn would be."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trough"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phatne** — a manger, the box animals eat from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke does not describe a stable, and there is a good case that the setting was the lower room of a village house where animals were kept at night, since the word he uses for *inn* is the one he later uses for the upper room at the Last Supper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way, the detail is the same: the first bed was a feeding trough."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"No room left for them\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke states it without accusation. Nobody is blamed and no innkeeper appears in the text — that character comes from later tradition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bethlehem was crowded because of the census. There was simply no space, and a young couple took what was available."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He is laid in.** A container that food is put in, in a town whose name means *house of bread*, by a mother who would later hear Him say *I am the bread of life.* Luke does not point at any of that. He just records where they put Him."}]}, "Luke 2:8–12": {"title": "Told to shepherds first", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The announcement of the birth goes to the people who were working the night shift."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Shepherds were not romantic figures. The work was dirty, the hours were antisocial, and their testimony was not accepted in a Jewish court — a shepherd could not have served as a witness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke, who has just named Caesar Augustus and Quirinius governor of Syria in the opening verses, brings the news to men in a field."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"For you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Today, in the town of David, a Saviour has been born **for you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angel does not say a Saviour has been born, and you should be pleased about it. The birth is announced as being on their account."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the titles stack: Saviour, Messiah, Lord — three of the heaviest words available, said to men holding sticks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sign"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is how you will know Him: you will find a baby wrapped in strips of cloth and lying in a feeding trough.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A strange sign. Every baby in Judea was wrapped in cloth; only one was in a manger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the identifying mark is ordinariness in an impossible place. Nothing about the sign suggests grandeur, and the shepherds are told to look for it in a town, not a palace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"A great joy meant for everyone\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *all the people* — and Luke, who will end his Gospel with the good news going to all nations, starts widening it here in the second chapter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the glory did.** *The glory of the Lord blazed all around them, and they were terrified.* Then the first words: *do not be afraid.* Every angelic announcement in these two chapters begins the same way, because the reaction is always the same."}]}, "Luke 21:10–19": {"title": "Your opening to bear witness", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He lists the end of the world and then says something happens before all of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this is said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On the temple mount, looking at the building. Someone had just remarked how it was *adorned with beautiful stones and donations*, and He answered that not one would be left on another."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything from here to the end of the chapter is the reply to *when will these things be?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Nation will rise up against nation**, and **kingdom against kingdom**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There will be **great earthquakes**, and **famines** and **plagues** in place after place, and **terrible things**, and **great signs from the sky**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke has two items Matthew and Mark do not: plagues, and signs from heaven."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *plagues* is **loimoi**, and it sits one letter away from **limoi**, famines — the pair is nearly a pun in Greek."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But before any of that**, they will lay hands on you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *before all these things*. He has just described the sequence and now steps back in front of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The persecution is not one of the signs. It is what precedes the signs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The venues"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They will hand you over to **the synagogues** and put you **in prison**, and you will be dragged in front of **kings and governors** because of My name.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four, and the book of Acts works through the list in order — the synagogue, the prison, and then Paul before Felix, Festus and Agrippa."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And that will be **your opening to bear witness**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *it shall turn out to you for a testimony* — **apobesetai**, it will result in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The courtroom is called an opportunity. Not survived, not endured — used."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **settle it in your hearts now** that you will **not rehearse your defense ahead of time**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *rehearse your defence* is **promeletan** — to premeditate, to prepare a speech in advance. The root of *meditate*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *settle it in your hearts* is a legal word too: put it in your hearts, decide it now, before the hearing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one preparation He asks for is the decision not to prepare."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because **I Myself will give you words and a wisdom** that none of your accusers will be able to **stand against or argue down**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's version says the Spirit of your Father will speak. Luke's says *I Myself*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *argue down* is **antipein** — to contradict, to speak against. Luke uses the same word of Stephen's opponents: *they were not able to resist the wisdom and the Spirit by which he spoke.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The betrayal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You will be handed over **even by parents and brothers, by relatives and friends**, and **some of you they will put to death**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four categories, closing in from parents to friends."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two sentences that follow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Everyone will hate you** because of My name. **And yet not a hair of your head will be lost**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Side by side, three words apart from *some of you they will put to death*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are true at once, and the second is not a promise about the body's safety — some of them are killed in the previous line. It is a promise about what is finally lost, which is nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"By your **steady endurance** you will **gain your lives**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hupomone** — remaining under. And **ktesasthe**, to acquire, to gain possession of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes after in Luke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jerusalem surrounded by armies, and *let those who are in Judea flee to the mountains* — which the church did, in AD 70. See the note on Luke 21:20–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the Son of Man coming in a cloud, and *look up and lift up your heads, because your redemption draws near*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the hearing is for.** He names the synagogue, the prison and the governor's court, and then calls the whole sequence an opening — and tells them the only preparation is to decide now not to prepare."}]}, "Luke 21:20–24": {"title": "The times of the Gentiles", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke's version of this discourse says something the other Gospels do not: armies, a siege, and a clock that starts running."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sign He gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When you see **armies camped in a ring around Jerusalem**, then you will know that her ruin is close.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew and Mark have *the abomination of desolation standing where it ought not.* Luke, writing for a Gentile readership, gives the plain military fact."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let everyone in Judea **run for the hills**, and let those inside the city **get out of it**, and let those out in the countryside **not go in**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three directions, all outward. Nobody is told to defend the city."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The historian Eusebius records that the Christians of Jerusalem left before the siege closed, and crossed the Jordan to Pella. They had been told."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In AD 70 Titus surrounded Jerusalem, and the city and the temple were destroyed. Josephus, who was there, describes the famine inside the walls, and *it will be bitter in those days for a woman carrying a child* reads as understatement beside his account."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will fall by the sword, and they will be carried off as captives among all the nations.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scattering that followed lasted nineteen centuries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clock"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And Jerusalem will be **trampled under Gentile feet** until the times of the Gentiles **have run their course**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Until.** The trampling is given an end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The times of the Gentiles* is a defined period — Luke's phrase, and the only place it appears. The Greek for *run their course* is to be fulfilled, to be filled up, as a measure is filled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Daniel's statue"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same period is what Nebuchadnezzar saw as a statue: gold, silver, bronze, iron, and feet of iron mixed with clay — a succession of Gentile empires running from the Babylon that burned the first temple onward."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in Daniel's interpretation it ends the same way, with a stone *cut from the mountain but not by human hands*, which breaks all of it and stands forever. See the note on Daniel 2:44–45."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the two passages share"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both put Jerusalem under Gentile rule for a stated stretch. Both put a term on it. Neither describes the arrangement as permanent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's version of the same night ends with *you will not see Me again until you say, \"Blessed is He who comes in the name of the Lord.\"* The same word again. See the note on Matthew 23:37–39."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He says immediately after.** Signs in the sun and moon and stars, and then *when these things begin to happen, look up and lift up your heads, for your redemption draws near.* The instruction in the siege was to run for the hills. The instruction at the end is to look up."}]}, "Luke 21:25–28": {"title": "The one instruction in the middle of the collapse", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A description of things falling apart, and then a command that runs against every instinct the description produces."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the passage describes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Signs in sun, moon and stars. Nations at their wits' end. The sea roaring. People fainting from fear and dread. *The powers that hold the heavens in place will be shaken.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke does not soften any of it. The reaction he reports is collapse — people literally giving out from fear of what is coming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When all of this begins to happen, stand up straight and lift up your heads.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two postures, and both are the opposite of what a frightened crowd does. The first word means to straighten up — used in this same Gospel of a bent woman being made straight. The second is lifting the head that fear drives down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the timing: *when all of this begins*. Not after it resolves. At the start, while it is still getting worse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because the day of your rescue is drawing near.* The word is **apolutrosis** — the ransom-release word, the same one in Ephesians 1:7 for being bought out of captivity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the sign that terrifies everyone else is read by these people as the sound of the rescue arriving. Same events, opposite posture, because of a different expectation about what they mean."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"the powers of the heavens shaken.\"** This is the phrase people connect with modern weaponry, and the words behind it are the ordinary Greek for the starry host and for shaking — the same phrase Isaiah 34:4 already used centuries earlier of judgement on a nation. It is a stock prophetic image for the cosmos being disturbed, not a coded reference to anything later."}]}, "Luke 21:7–9": {"title": "\"Do not be frightened\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "They ask for a timetable and He answers with a warning about people who will offer one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**When will all of this happen? What will the sign be** that it is about to take place?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had just been admiring the temple stonework, and He had said not one stone would be left on another."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two questions: when, and what is the sign."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Be careful that no one leads you astray.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before any content. The first thing He says about the end is a warning about information regarding the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the deceivers say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Many will come using My name and saying, '**I am the one**,' and, '**The time has arrived**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two claims: an identity, and a date."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not follow them.**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wars"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When you hear of **wars and uprisings, do not be frightened**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *do not be terrified*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say they will not happen. He says they will, and tells them what to do with the news."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Such things **have to happen first**, but **the end will not come right away**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things at once: these are necessary, and they are not the signal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence that undoes date-setting from both directions. The turbulence is expected, and it does not mean the timetable has moved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says a few verses later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There will be great earthquakes in various places, and famines and **pestilences**; and there will be fearful sights and great signs from heaven.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **loimos** — plague, pestilence. Named alongside earthquakes and famines, as one of the things that are not the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He tells them to do about all of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**By your patient endurance you will gain your souls.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And, at the end of the chapter: *when these things begin to happen, **look up and lift up your heads**, because your redemption draws near.* See the note on Luke 21:25–28."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two instructions in one chapter: do not be terrified, and look up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He also says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Settle it in your hearts **not to meditate beforehand on what you will answer**; for I will give you a mouth and wisdom.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them not to rehearse."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one thing He rules out entirely.** *\"It is not for you to know times or seasons which the Father has put in His own authority.\"* Said to the same men, in Acts 1, when they asked the question again — after the resurrection, and He still did not answer it."}]}, "Luke 22:19–20": {"title": "\"Do this to remember Me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four verbs on the bread, and then a sentence about the cup that names a covenant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four things He does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **picked up** the bread, and **gave thanks** over it, and **broke** it, and **handed it** to them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Took, blessed, broke, gave. Luke uses the same four in the feeding of the five thousand and again at Emmaus, where the two disciples recognise Him at the exact moment He does them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The thanks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eucharisteo** — where *Eucharist* comes from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives thanks holding bread He has just said is His body, on the night He is betrayed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Given for you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is My body,\" He said, \"**given for you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not taken from Him. **Given.**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the same about His life in John: *no one takes it from Me. I lay it down of My own accord.* See the note on John 10:16–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Do this to remember Me\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *remembrance* is **anamnesis**. Luke and Paul both record the instruction; Matthew and Mark do not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only ongoing practice He tells them to keep by saying *do this*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing of the cup"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And in the same way, **after the supper**, He took the cup.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke marks it. The bread was in the meal; the cup came after it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "At a Passover seder four cups are drunk. The one after the meal is the third — the cup of redemption."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The covenant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This cup is **the new covenant in My blood**,\" He said, \"**poured out for you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase *new covenant* comes from Jeremiah 31, written six centuries earlier, where it ends: *I will forgive their iniquity, and **their sin I will remember no more**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on Hebrews 8:10–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two rememberings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says *remember Me*. The covenant He is inaugurating says *I will remember their sins no more*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb, running in opposite directions, in the same cup."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The old covenant's ratification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses took blood, threw it on the people and said *this is the blood of the covenant which the LORD has made with you*. Exodus 24, on the mountain, with the elders eating and drinking in God's presence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the same form of words, at a table, with the blood not yet shed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who was still at the table.** Luke puts the next sentence like this: *but behold, the hand of My betrayer is with Me on the table.* The order in Luke's account has Judas present for the bread and the cup."}]}, "Luke 22:24–30": {"title": "The greatest among you", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "They argue about rank at the last supper, and He answers by telling them what they have already done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When this happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And then **an argument started among them** about which of them ought to be considered the greatest.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bread has been broken. The cup has been given. He has just said *the hand of My betrayer is with Me on the table*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Greek"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Philoneikia** — a love of strife, contentiousness. A rivalry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke places it here deliberately. Matthew and Mark record a similar argument earlier in the ministry; Luke puts one at the table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kings do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The kings of the nations lord it over their people**, and the men who hold power over them **get themselves called benefactors**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **euergetai** — benefactors."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was a real title. Ptolemy III of Egypt was Euergetes; the honorific was voted to rulers by cities across the Greek world, sometimes in gratitude and sometimes under pressure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The system was not a fiction. It was how power presented itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It is not to be that way with you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **humeis de ouch houtos** — but you, not so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inversion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The greatest among you should become like the youngest**, and **the one who leads like the one who serves**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ho neoteros** — the younger. In a household, the one with the least standing and the most errands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Think about it — **who is greater, the one sitting at the table or the one waiting on him?** The one at the table, surely.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He grants the ordinary answer. He does not pretend the world works differently."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**And yet here I am among you as the one who serves**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ho diakonon** — the one serving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said at a table, by the host, on the night John records Him with a towel round His waist. See the note on John 13:4–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says about them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You are the ones who have stayed with Me** through everything I have been put through.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hoi diamemenekotes** — perfect participle. Those who have remained throughout."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When He says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hours before every one of them runs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's denial is predicted in the next paragraph — *before the rooster crows, you will deny three times that you know Me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He commends their constancy immediately before naming its collapse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**And I am giving you a kingdom, just as My Father has given one to Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **diatithemai** — I covenant, I assign by will. The verb behind **diatheke**, covenant or testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is bequeathing it, the way a man disposes of an estate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it comes with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So that you may **eat and drink at My table in My kingdom**, and **sit on thrones, ruling the twelve tribes of Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They wanted to know who was greatest. He gives all of them thrones."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument is not settled by demotion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The twelve"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve thrones, and one of the twelve has already gone out. Luke has recorded the betrayal in the same paragraph."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Simon, Simon! Indeed, **Satan has asked for you, that he may sift you as wheat**. But I have prayed for you, that your faith should not fail; and **when you have returned to Me, strengthen your brethren**.\"* See the note on Luke 22:31–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When*, not *if*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The timing.** *You are the ones who have stayed with Me through everything.* Said hours before all of them scattered, and in the same paragraph as the prediction that they would."}]}, "Luke 22:31–34": {"title": "\"But I have prayed for you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four sentences at the supper table, and the middle one is the reason Peter has a rest of his life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name, twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Simon, Simon**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Doubling a name in Scripture is how the serious things start — *Abraham, Abraham* at the knife; *Moses, Moses* at the bush; *Saul, Saul* on the Damascus road."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He uses the old name. Not Peter, the rock, which He gave him. Simon, which he was born with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Satan has asked to have all of you**, to sift you like wheat.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exaiteomai** — to demand, to ask for the surrender of something, as a prosecutor demands a prisoner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked. Somebody had to answer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is Job's courtroom again: *Have you considered My servant Job?* See the note on Job 1:6–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"All of you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek pronoun in *have all of you* is plural. The whole table is asked for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next sentence goes singular."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Sifting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To **sift you like wheat**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A sieve is shaken hard. Everything in it is thrown about, and what falls through is thrown away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is violent, and it is also agricultural: sifting is what you do to keep the grain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But I have prayed for you, Simon**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Singular now. Satan asked for all eleven; Jesus names one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *prayed* is **deomai** — to beg, to make petition out of need."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the tense is aorist: it is already done, before the courtyard, before the fire, before the girl asks the question."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He prayed for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That **your faith would not give out**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that he would not fall. He does fall, three times, with an oath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer is for the thing underneath — that the collapse would not go all the way down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other side of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **when you have turned back**, strengthen your brothers.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**When**, not if. He is given his assignment after the failure while the failure is still in the future."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *strengthen* is **sterizo** — to fix firmly, to set in place. Peter uses it in his own letter about God doing it to them. See the note on 1 Peter 5:10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Peter's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, **I am ready to go with You anywhere — to prison, to death**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He means it. He draws a sword in the garden a few hours later and takes a swing at an armed party."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I tell you, Peter, **the rooster will not crow today before you have said three times that you do not know Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here the name changes back — **Peter**, the rock, in the sentence predicting the denial."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rooster crowed, *and the Lord turned and looked at Peter*, and he went out and wept bitterly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then a beach, a charcoal fire, and three questions. And at Pentecost the man standing up to preach to Jerusalem is Simon Peter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was decided before the denial.** The prayer, the outcome and the assignment. All three are stated at the table, while he is still insisting it will not happen."}]}, "Luke 22:39–46": {"title": "\"Not what I want. What You want.\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke was a physician, and his account of Gethsemane includes a detail no other Gospel records."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"As He always did\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He went out, **as He always did**, to the Mount of Olives.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke notes the habit. It is also how Judas knew where to find Him — John says *Judas also knew the place, for Jesus often met there with His disciples.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes to the place His betrayer can find Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction, twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Pray that you do not walk into the testing.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said at the start and again at the end of the passage, bracketing the whole scene."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **peirasmos** — testing, trial, temptation. It is the same word in the Lord's Prayer: *do not lead us into temptation.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "A stone's throw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **drew away from them, about as far as a stone could be thrown**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke measures it. Close enough to be seen; far enough to be alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Father, **if You are willing, take this cup away from Me. And yet — not what I want. What You want.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves are real. He asks for it to be removed, and He submits."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The request is not withdrawn — He asks, and then places the asking under something else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cup"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image runs through the prophets — the cup of God's wrath, passed round and drunk to the dregs. Psalm 75: *there is a cup in the hand of the LORD… He pours it out, and the wicked of the earth drink it dry.* See the note on Psalms 75:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is looking at that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The angel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **an angel from heaven appeared to Him, strengthening Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only Luke records this. The cup is not taken away and help is sent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is to strengthen, to put strength into."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke records next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **in His anguish He prayed more earnestly still**, and **His sweat became like great drops of blood** falling to the ground.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *anguish* is **agonia** — the origin of the English word, and it carried the sense of a contest, a struggle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the sweat. The condition is called **hematidrosis** — under extreme stress the small vessels around the sweat glands can rupture, and blood mixes with sweat. It is rare, documented, and associated with acute terror or grief."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke, of the four writers, is the one who mentions it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note also what the strengthening produced: *he prayed **more earnestly**.* The angel did not settle Him into calm. He went back in harder."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the drops fall"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Falling **to the ground**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 3: *cursed is the ground because of you… in toil you will eat of it all the days of your life. It will bring you thorns and thistles. By the sweat of your brow you will eat your bread.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sweat and cursed ground, and a man in a garden. See the note on Genesis 3:17–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disciples"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Found them **asleep, worn out with grief**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke alone supplies the reason. Matthew and Mark just say they were sleeping; Luke says why, and it is not indifference."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was in a garden the first time.** A man, a tree, and a choice, and the sentence *not what You want, but what I want.* This one is a garden, a man on his knees, and the sentence reversed."}]}, "Luke 22:47–53": {"title": "Betrayed with a kiss", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last miracle before the cross is performed on a man who came to arrest Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was **still speaking** when a crowd arrived.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had just said *rise and pray, lest you enter into temptation*, having found them asleep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the man called **Judas, one of the Twelve**, was walking in front of them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **heis ton dodeka** — one of the twelve."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every Gospel adds that phrase. None of them lets it pass."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sign"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He came up close to Jesus **to kiss Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A kiss on the cheek was the ordinary greeting between a disciple and his rabbi."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records the arrangement: *whomever I kiss, He is the One; seize Him*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Judas — with a kiss you hand over the Son of Man?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **philemati ton huion tou anthropou paradidos** — with a kiss you betray the Son of Man?"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses his name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records the other address: *friend, why have you come?* — **hetaire**, the same word used of the complaining labourer in the vineyard. See the note on Matthew 20:13–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disciples"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The men around Him saw what was coming and said, \"**Lord, should we strike with the sword?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They ask."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then somebody does not wait for the answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The strike"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And one of them **did strike the high priest's servant and cut off his right ear**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John names them both: *Simon Peter, having a sword, drew it and struck the high priest's servant, and cut off his right ear. **The servant's name was Malchus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke, a physician, notes which ear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was aiming at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an ear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus said, \"**Stop. No more of this**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eate heos toutou** — permit as far as this, let it be to this point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The healing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He touched the man's ear and healed him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke alone records it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last recorded miracle before the crucifixion is performed on a man in the arresting party, to undo damage done by a disciple."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Matthew records Him saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Put your sword in its place, for **all who take the sword will perish by the sword**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Or do you think that I cannot now pray to My Father, and **He will provide Me with more than twelve legions of angels**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A legion was six thousand. He names the number available and does not ask."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rebuke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Am I a bandit, that you come for Me with swords and clubs?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **lesten** — a robber, a brigand. The same word used of the two crucified with Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Day after day I was there with you in the temple, and **you never laid a hand on Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had every opportunity, in daylight, in public. They came at night with a mob."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **this is your hour, and the reign of darkness**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **he exousia tou skotous** — the authority of darkness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He said the night before this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The hour has come that the Son of Man should be glorified.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two hours are named in this Gospel. He calls one His and one theirs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the darkness goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now it was about the sixth hour, and **there was darkness over all the earth until the ninth hour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Noon to three. See the note on Luke 23:44–46."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last miracle before the cross.** An ear, restored on a man who had come to make the arrest, after a disciple asked whether to use the sword and then used it."}]}, "Luke 22:54–62": {"title": "\"The Lord turned and looked straight at Peter\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Only Luke records the look, and it is the hinge of the scene."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peter followed a long way behind.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's phrasing is exact and it is not only geography. He is close enough to be in the courtyard and far enough to deny knowing Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three denials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They escalate, and they get shorter:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Woman, I do not know Him*", "*Man, I am not*", "*Man, I have no idea what you are talking about*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "An hour passes between the second and third. He had time to leave, and stayed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And while the words were still in his mouth, the rooster crowed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke puts the two in the same instant. No gap for reflection."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The look"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the Lord turned and looked straight at Peter.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus was inside the high priest's house being interrogated. For this to happen He was being moved through the courtyard, or was visible across it — bound, already struck, in the middle of His own trial."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb Luke uses is for a direct, deliberate look at someone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is said. No word is recorded, and no rebuke."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the look did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Peter remembered what the Lord had said to him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The remembering is what breaks him — not the servant girl, not the rooster. Christ had told him this would happen, and had said in the same breath: *I have prayed for you, that your faith may not fail; and when you have turned back, strengthen your brothers.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been told the failure and the recovery in one sentence, before either happened."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it ends.** *He went outside and wept bitterly.* Luke leaves him there and does not resolve it in this chapter. The resolution is on a beach in John 21, over a charcoal fire — the only other charcoal fire in the Gospels is the one in this courtyard."}]}, "Luke 22:7–13": {"title": "The room already set out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two men are sent to prepare a meal and find that most of it is done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The day the **Passover lamb had to be killed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nisan 14. Thousands of lambs were slaughtered in the temple that afternoon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke notes the date in a chapter that will end with a trial."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is sent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus sent **Peter and John** ahead.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two who will run to the tomb together on Sunday morning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Where do You want us to prepare it?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A reasonable question in a city packed with pilgrims. Rooms in Jerusalem at Passover were impossible to find, and by custom nobody charged for them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sign"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As you go into the city, **a man carrying a jar of water** will meet you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Carrying water was women's work. A man with a water jar would stand out in a crowded street."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a marker chosen so that two men could pick him out without asking anybody anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The password"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Say to the owner of the house, '**The Teacher asks: where is the guest room** where I may eat the Passover with My disciples?'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No name is given. *The Teacher* is enough."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever arrangement had been made, it had been made without the disciples' knowledge, and Judas is not in the room when the location is given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they find"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He will take you upstairs and show you **a large room, already set out**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *furnished* — spread with couches, laid out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were sent to prepare, and the preparing had been done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Get everything ready there* — the lamb, the bread, the wine. The room was not theirs to arrange."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The two of them went, and **found it just as He had told them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says it flatly. He does the same with the colt on Palm Sunday — sent, described, found exactly so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens in that room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Passover, the bread and the cup, the argument about who is greatest, the warning to Peter, the washing of feet in John's account, and the four chapters of the upper room discourse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have longed to eat this Passover with you before I suffer.\"* See the note on Luke 22:14–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other room in Luke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There was no room for them in the inn.* Chapter 2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Gospel opens with no space available and closes its last week in a large upper room, furnished and ready."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the man with the jar never knew.** He is not named, the householder is not named, and neither appears again. Somebody in Jerusalem gave up the best room in his house that week and is not recorded."}]}, "Luke 23:32–38": {"title": "\"Father, forgive them\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke records the first sentence spoken from the cross, and it is a prayer for the people holding the hammer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The company"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Two others were led out with Him as well, **both of them criminals**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's word is **kakourgoi** — evildoers. Matthew and Mark call them robbers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One on His right and one on His left* — the position James and John had asked for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke does not describe"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They crucified Him there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words. No description of the nails, the raising, or the pain. All four Gospels do this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Jesus said, \"**Father, forgive them; they do not know what they are doing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is imperfect — He **kept saying** it. Not once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who \"them\" is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke does not say. The soldiers driving the nails are nearest. The rulers who arranged it are standing there watching. The crowd is there too."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He leaves it open, and the reason He gives applies to all of them: **they do not know**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The category"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ignorance is exactly the condition the cities of refuge were built for — a death caused *without meaning to*. See the notes on Numbers 35:9–15 and Numbers 35:16–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says it to Jerusalem a few weeks later: *\"I know that you did it **in ignorance**, as did also your rulers.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Paul: *\"had they known, they would not have crucified the Lord of glory.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clothes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then they **divided His clothes among themselves, throwing lots** for them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 22: *they divide My garments among them, and for My clothing they cast lots.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written a thousand years earlier by a man describing an execution method that did not exist yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who watched"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people **stood there watching**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke separates the crowd from the mockers. The people watch; the rulers sneer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three mockeries"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each has the same shape — *save Yourself*:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*The **rulers** sneered: \"He saved other people. Let Him save Himself, **if He is the Messiah of God, the chosen One**.\"*", "*The **soldiers** mocked Him too… \"**If You are the king of the Jews**, save Yourself.\"*", "And one of the criminals, in the next verse: *\"**If You are the Christ**, save Yourself and us.\"*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times *if*, and it is the same word the wilderness temptation opened with: *if You are the Son of God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the rulers admitted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He saved other people**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They concede it in the middle of the taunt. Nobody in the account disputes the works."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The notice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A notice fixed above Him, written in **Greek and Latin and Hebrew**: This is the King of the Jews.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three languages of the empire's east, its law, and its religion. Pilate wrote it, and when the chief priests objected he said *what I have written, I have written.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second criminal turns on the first, says *we receive the due reward of our deeds, but this Man has done nothing wrong*, and asks to be remembered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Assuredly, I say to you, today you will be with Me in Paradise.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of it.** He prays for their forgiveness before anyone asks, before anyone is sorry, and while the work is still being done to Him."}]}, "Luke 24:13–16": {"title": "A stranger on the road", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two men walking away from Jerusalem on the day of the resurrection, and the man they are talking about falls into step beside them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they were going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A village called **Emmaus**, sixty stadia out from Jerusalem — a walk of about seven miles.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name means warm springs, warm baths. There were hot springs in that country and a village took its name from them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are walking away from the city, and they are on the road on the same day the tomb was found empty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were talking the whole way about everything that had happened… **talking it over and turning it around** between them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for the second is to debate, to dispute — they are going over it and getting nowhere."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have all the facts. Luke's next paragraph shows they know about the crucifixion, the third day, the women at the tomb, the angels, and the men who went and found it empty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Facts, and no sight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Their eyes were held\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus Himself came up and walked along with them. But **their eyes were held**, and they did not recognise Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is passive — something was holding them. Luke does not say what."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is physically present, close enough to walk seven miles with them, and it does nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not what you would expect. He does not identify Himself, and He does not show them the marks — that comes later, in the room, with the others."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And beginning at Moses and all the Prophets, **He expounded to them in all the Scriptures the things concerning Himself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives them a Bible study. Seven miles of it, from Genesis forward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which He thought was better"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had the option of simply appearing. Instead He walked the whole way concealed and opened the Scriptures — and their own verdict afterwards is about the road, not the moment of recognition:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Were our hearts not burning inside us **while He talked with us on the road, while He was opening the Scriptures to us**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The burning happened before they knew who He was. It happened while the Book was being opened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was in that sermon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses and all the Prophets. Whatever else was in it, Genesis was, and Jacob's blessings over the twelve sons are in Genesis."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He calls them.** *Foolish ones, and slow of heart to believe in all that the prophets have spoken.* Not slow to believe the women, or the empty tomb. Slow to believe what was already written down."}]}, "Luke 24:17–24": {"title": "\"But we had hoped\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two men explain the resurrection to the risen Christ and get every fact right."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What is this you are discussing** with each other as you walk along?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks. He knows, and He asks."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they stopped, **their faces heavy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke notes the stopping. The question halts them in the road."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Are You **the only visitor in Jerusalem** who does not know what has happened there this week?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are addressing the one person in Jerusalem who knows more about it than anybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"What things?\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words, and He lets them tell Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they knew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Run through their account. It is accurate on every point:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["Jesus of Nazareth, *a prophet, powerful in what He did and what He said*", "handed over by the chief priests and rulers", "crucified", "*this is now **the third day** since it all happened*", "the women went early, found no body, and *came back saying they had seen a vision of angels who told them **He is alive***", "some of the men went and *found it exactly as the women had said*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have the timeline, the third day, the empty tomb, the angelic message, and independent corroboration."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But we had hoped** that He was the one who was going to redeem Israel.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past tense. The saddest three words in the chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hope is spoken of as something finished — and the man it was about is walking next to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"But they did not see Him\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their last line, and it is the complaint. They had the report and not the sight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was wrong"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not their information. Their conclusion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Foolish ones, and slow of heart to believe in all that the prophets have spoken.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is His response, and note what He faults. Not slow to believe the women, or the empty tomb, or the angels. Slow to believe **what was already written down**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Beginning at Moses and all the Prophets, He **expounded to them in all the Scriptures** the things concerning Himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven miles of it, still unrecognised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And their own verdict afterwards is about the road, not the moment of recognition: *were our hearts not burning inside us while He talked with us on the road, while He was **opening the Scriptures** to us?* See the notes on Luke 24:13–16 and Luke 24:25–27."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What \"redeem Israel\" meant to them.** A throne, an army, and the Romans out. He had done something larger and they could not see it, standing on the third day with all the evidence in hand."}]}, "Luke 24:25–27": {"title": "The best Bible study never written down", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two people walking to Emmaus get a seven-mile exposition of the Old Testament from its subject, and Luke does not record a word of the content."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He rebukes them for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How slow you are, and how heavy of heart, when it comes to believing everything the prophets said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not for failing to recognise Him — Luke says their eyes were kept from doing that. For not believing what was already written."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The material to work it out had been available the whole time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word \"had to\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Did the Messiah not have to suffer all of this and then come into His glory?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek carries necessity — it was *necessary*. Luke uses this word repeatedly of Jesus' path."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the order is fixed: suffering, then glory. That is the sequence the two on the road could not make fit, because a defeated Messiah made no sense to them. Isaiah 52 has the same two elements in the same relationship."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Beginning with Moses\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And beginning with Moses and going on through all the Prophets, He opened up for them everything in the Scriptures that was about Himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole Hebrew Bible, in order, with Himself as the thread. Later in the same chapter He names the third division too — the Psalms."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every reader since has wanted the transcript. Luke gives the method and not the notes, which may be deliberate: the exercise is left for us to do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they recognised Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not during the exposition. *When He was at the table with them, He took bread, gave thanks, broke it and began to give it to them. Then their eyes were opened.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Scriptures burned in them on the road. The recognition came at the breaking of bread."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they said afterwards.** *Were not our hearts burning within us while He talked with us on the road and opened the Scriptures to us?* They knew something had happened before they knew who had done it."}]}, "Luke 24:28–32": {"title": "Known in the breaking of bread", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Seven miles of Scripture, a meal, and then He is gone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He made as if to go further on\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not invite Himself in. He keeps walking, and they have to stop Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Stay with us.** The day is nearly over.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *urged* is strong — they constrained Him, pressed Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who becomes the host"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They invited Him into their house, and then:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When He had sat down at the table with them, **He took the bread** and gave thanks over it, and broke it, and handed it to them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The guest takes the bread. That is the host's part, and He takes it without asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Took… gave thanks… broke… handed it to them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same four, in the same order, as the upper room. The same four as the five thousand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What opened their eyes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **their eyes were opened and they knew Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same passive as verse 16 — *their eyes were held* — now reversed. Something had been holding them; something releases them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is at the breaking of the bread that it happens. Not during the seven miles of exposition, and not when He first appeared on the road."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"And He was gone from their sight\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instant they recognise Him He vanishes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not stay for the meal He has just begun. They get one look, and then they are alone with an opened Bible and a broken loaf."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they say to each other"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Were our hearts not burning inside us** while He talked with us on the road, while He was opening the Scriptures to us?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence, and note what they point at. Not the moment of recognition. The road. The Scriptures being opened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The burning had already happened, before they knew who He was, while a stranger was walking them through Moses and the Prophets."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They rose up that very hour and returned to Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven miles back, in the dark, immediately, after telling Him it was too late in the day to travel."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they tell the eleven.** *How He was known to them in the breaking of bread.* Of everything on that road — the exposition, the walk, the vanishing — that is the phrase they use for it."}]}, "Luke 24:33–35": {"title": "Back to Jerusalem that night", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "They had just persuaded Him to stay the night, and then they walk seven miles in the dark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They got up **that same hour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An hour earlier they had said *abide with us, for it is toward evening, and the day is far spent*. See the note on Luke 24:28–32."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason they gave for stopping is still true. It is now darker."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Emmaus is sixty stadia from Jerusalem — about seven miles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had walked it once already that day, going the other way, *and they stood still, looking sad*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had said on the road"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"We were hoping that it was He who was going to redeem Israel.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past tense. They were leaving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they found"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they found **the eleven and the others gathered together**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The eleven — Judas is dead, and Luke keeps the count exact."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the room says first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The Lord has really risen, and He has appeared to Simon**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ontos** — really, actually."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two arrive bursting with news and are told the news before they can give it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The appearance to Simon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing else is recorded about it. Luke mentions it in one clause and never returns to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has it in his list: *He was seen by Cephas, then by the twelve*. It is the first resurrection appearance he names."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somewhere between the empty tomb and this evening, Jesus met Peter alone, and neither Luke nor Paul says a word about what passed between them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last thing recorded before it is Peter going out and weeping bitterly. See the note on Matthew 26:69–75."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the two of them told **what had happened on the road**, and **how they had known Him when He broke the bread**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things: the walk, and the moment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **egnosthe** — He was made known, passive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the walk had been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Beginning at Moses and all the Prophets, **He expounded to them in all the Scriptures the things concerning Himself**.* See the note on Luke 24:25–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The longest Bible study in the New Testament, and no one wrote it down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The moment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now it came to pass, as He sat at the table with them, that **He took bread, blessed and broke it, and gave it to them**. Then their eyes were opened and they knew Him; and **He vanished from their sight**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verbs — took, blessed, broke, gave — the same four as the feeding of the five thousand and the upper room."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is recognised in the action, and is gone in the same sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now as they said these things, **Jesus Himself stood in the midst of them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He arrives while they are still comparing notes, and asks for something to eat. See the note on Luke 24:36–43."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who these two were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One is named: **Cleopas**. The other is not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither is one of the eleven. The first extended resurrection appearance in Luke is given to two people outside the inner circle, one of whom nobody can name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did with the evening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Walked seven miles back, at night, to tell people who already knew."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke reports it without remarking on it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was said before they could speak.** They ran seven miles with news, and the room announced it to them first — *He has appeared to Simon*, and that is the whole record of it."}]}, "Luke 24:36–43": {"title": "Do you have anything here to eat", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He proves He is not a ghost by asking for supper."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When this happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While they were **still talking about it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two from Emmaus had just walked seven miles back to Jerusalem to report that they had seen Him, and were mid-sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arrival"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus Himself **stood there among them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John adds that the doors were shut."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The greeting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Peace be with you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Shalom aleichem** — the ordinary Jewish greeting, said in the least ordinary circumstances in history."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last thing most of them had done was run away from His arrest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were **startled and frightened**, and thought they were **seeing a spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pneuma** — a ghost."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had just been told He was alive, and their reaction to His being alive is terror."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why are you shaken? Why are these doubts coming up in your hearts?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *doubts* is **dialogismoi** — reasonings, arguments running in the mind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He treats the doubt as something happening in the heart rather than as a moral failure, and He answers it with evidence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The evidence offered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Look at My hands and My feet — it is I Myself. Touch Me and see**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three senses invited in one sentence: look, touch, see."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hands and feet are the identifying marks, which means the wounds are still there in the risen body."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**A spirit does not have flesh and bones**, as you can see that I have.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Flesh and bones** — not *flesh and blood*, which is Paul's phrase for what *cannot inherit the kingdom of God*. See the note on 1 Corinthians 15:50–52."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke, a physician, records the distinction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason they still could not believe"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **still could not believe it, for sheer joy**, and they were amazed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *while they still disbelieved for joy and marvelled*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives the most sympathetic account of unbelief anywhere in Scripture. They did not doubt because they were hard. They doubted because it was too good."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do you have anything here to eat?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The resurrected Christ asks for food."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He was given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A piece of **broiled fish**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He took it and ate it in front of them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **enopion auton** — in their presence. He eats where they can watch Him swallow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter cites it later, to Cornelius: *God raised Him up on the third day… to witnesses chosen before by God, even to us **who ate and drank with Him after He arose from the dead**.* See the note on Acts 10:34–43."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other fish"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Breakfast on the shore of Galilee, and Peter in the water. See the note on John 21:4–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice after the resurrection, He is at a meal with fish on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"These are the words which I spoke to you while I was still with you, that all things must be fulfilled which were written in the Law of Moses and the Prophets and the Psalms concerning Me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He **opened their understanding**, that they might comprehend the Scriptures.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb He used on the road to Emmaus, where *He expounded to them in all the Scriptures the things concerning Himself*. See the note on Luke 24:25–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the body is like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Doors do not stop it, and it eats fish. It can be handled and it can arrive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke reports both without reconciling them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The proof He picked.** Not an explanation. He held out His hands, invited them to touch, and then asked whether there was anything to eat."}]}, "Luke 24:44–49": {"title": "\"He opened their minds\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last scene of Luke's Gospel, and He gives them a reading lesson."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is what **I was telling you while I was still with you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not new information. He had said it repeatedly — three times in Luke that the Son of Man must suffer, and each time Luke notes they did not understand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three divisions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Everything written about Me in **the law of Moses and in the Prophets and in the Psalms** had to be fulfilled.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The standard Jewish division of Scripture: Torah, Nevi'im, Ketuvim — the Psalms standing for the writings, as the largest book in them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is claiming all three."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **He opened their minds to understand the Scriptures**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dienoixen** — to open thoroughly, to open up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke uses the same verb three times in this chapter: on the Emmaus road *He **opened** to us the Scriptures*; at the table *their eyes were **opened***; and here, the mind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The problem was not that the texts were unclear. Something had to be opened at the reader's end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What stands written"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That the Messiah would **suffer** and **rise from the dead on the third day**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And that **a change of mind and the forgiveness of sins would be proclaimed in His name** to all the nations, **beginning at Jerusalem**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three items, and the third is the mission. He includes the preaching in what the Old Testament said had to happen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Beginning at Jerusalem\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The city that had just executed Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Acts follows the order exactly: *in Jerusalem, and in all Judea and Samaria, and to the end of the earth.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You are witnesses of all of this**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **martures** — the root of *martyr*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A witness reports what he saw. It is the only qualification named."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **I am sending what My Father promised out upon you**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Stay here in the city of Jerusalem until you are clothed with power from on high**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *clothed* is **endusesthe** — to be dressed in, to put on like a garment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Until.** They are told to do nothing until it arrives — and they do exactly that: eleven men and a hundred and twenty people in an upper room for ten days. See the note on Acts 1:12–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had been given and not given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Opened minds, an explanation of the whole Old Testament, the commission, and the title of witness — and they are still told to sit still."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verses of the Gospel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He led them out as far as Bethany, and He lifted up His hands and blessed them. Now it came to pass, **while He blessed them, He was parted from them** and carried up into heaven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last thing they saw was His hands up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they returned to Jerusalem **with great joy**, and were continually in the temple praising and blessing God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the Gospel opened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "With a priest, Zacharias, unable to give the blessing when he came out of the temple because he had been struck dumb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke ends with a blessing that is completed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was opened.** Not the book. Luke says He opened *their minds to understand the Scriptures* — the same scrolls they had been reading in synagogue their whole lives."}]}, "Luke 4:16–21": {"title": "Where He stopped reading", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus opens His public ministry by reading a passage aloud and stopping in the middle of a sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He read"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 61, and the list is specific:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["good news to the poor", "healing for the brokenhearted", "release for the prisoners", "sight for the blind", "freedom for everyone being crushed", "**the year of the Lord's favour**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the job description He chose for Himself, out of the whole scroll, on the first day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He stopped"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah's sentence does not end at *the year of the Lord's favour*. It continues: *and the day of vengeance of our God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus reads to the comma, rolls up the scroll, hands it back and sits down. The day of vengeance is not cancelled — it simply is not what He had come to announce that morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Today, in your hearing, this Scripture has come true.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The year of favour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase points at the Jubilee — the fiftieth year in Leviticus 25 when debts were released, slaves went free and land returned to its family. As far as the record shows, Israel never once kept it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus announces it as arriving in Him, on that Sabbath, in that room."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the anointing was for.** *The Spirit of the Lord is on Me, because He has anointed Me* — and every item in the list that follows is something done for somebody else: the poor, the brokenhearted, the prisoners, the blind, the crushed. He states the anointing and then spends the rest of the Gospel spending it."}]}, "Luke 4:25–27": {"title": "A widow and a leper", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two examples from the same room in Nazareth, and they are what gets Him thrown out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this is said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The synagogue in Nazareth, immediately after He read Isaiah 61, closed the scroll and said *today this Scripture is fulfilled in your hearing.* See the note on Isaiah 61:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says the reaction to that was favourable: *all bore witness to Him and marvelled at the gracious words.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then He says this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There were **plenty of widows in Israel** in the days of Elijah, when the sky was shut up for three and a half years.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's figure of three and a half years matches James's. 1 Kings says *in the third year*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the prophet went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But Elijah was **not sent to a single one of them** — he was sent to **a widow in Zarephath, in the region of Sidon**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sidon is Phoenicia — Gentile territory, and specifically Jezebel's country. Her father was king of the Sidonians."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophet who spent his life opposing Jezebel was fed for three years by a widow from her homeland. See the note on 1 Kings 17:8–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second case"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And there were **plenty of people with leprosy in Israel** in the days of the prophet Elisha, and **not one of them was made clean** — only **Naaman, the Syrian**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Naaman was commander of the army of Aram, the nation Israel was at war with. He had been raiding Israelite territory; the girl who told him about the prophet was a slave taken in one of those raids."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was healed and Israel's own lepers were not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What both examples have in common"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An Israelite prophet, a national crisis, a country full of people with the need — and the one person helped is a foreigner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both stories are in Israel's own Scriptures. Nothing He says is new information."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it enraged them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So all those in the synagogue, when they heard these things, **were filled with wrath**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had just asked *is this not Joseph's son?* and were expecting the local boy to do at Capernaum's miracles here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He answers by naming the two occasions when the prophets were sent past Israel altogether."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And rose up and thrust Him out of the city; and they led Him to the brow of the hill on which their city was built, **that they might throw Him down over the cliff**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **passing through the midst of them, He went His way**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives no explanation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He said before the examples"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Assuredly, I say to you, **no prophet is accepted in his own country**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same theme goes in Luke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Gospel is full of it — the good Samaritan, the ten lepers where only the Samaritan returns, the centurion whose faith is greater than any in Israel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Acts, by the same author, is the story of the message going out to exactly the people these two examples name."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is quoted at them.** Their own history. He does not tell them anything they had not read — he points at the two occasions in it when the help went elsewhere."}]}, "Luke 4:28–30": {"title": "Rage in the synagogue", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The town He grew up in tries to kill Him, minutes after admiring Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had just said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses earlier: *all bore witness to Him and **marvelled at the gracious words** which proceeded out of His mouth. And they said, \"Is this not Joseph's son?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had read Isaiah 61 — good news to the poor, healing for the brokenhearted, liberty to the captives, sight to the blind — and sat down and said *today this Scripture is fulfilled in your hearing.* See the note on Luke 4:16–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were pleased."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What turned them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He brought up two Gentiles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There were many widows in Israel in the days of Elijah… but to none of them was Elijah sent except to Zarephath, in the region of Sidon, to a woman who was a widow. And many lepers were in Israel in the time of Elisha the prophet, and none of them was cleansed except Naaman the Syrian.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two famines of blessing inside Israel, and both times the prophet went outside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence that empties the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone in the synagogue heard that, and they were **filled with rage**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **thumos** — a boiling over, fury."}, {"k": "p", "t": "From *marvelled at His gracious words* to this, in the space of two sentences."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they tried"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **drove Him out of the town**, and brought Him to the edge of the hill the town was built on, **meaning to throw Him off it headlong**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nazareth sits on a ridge. This is an attempted execution by mob, on the Sabbath, by His own neighbours — the people who had watched Him grow up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **He walked right through the middle of them** and went on His way.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No struggle is described, no miracle announced, no rebuke. He walks through the crowd holding Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John records the same thing in the temple after *before Abraham was, I AM* — they picked up stones, *but Jesus hid Himself and went out, passing right through the middle of them.* See the note on John 8:57–59."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody laid a hand on Him until the night He let them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Nazareth got"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark's account of the same visit: *He could do no mighty work there, except that He laid His hands on a few sick people and healed them. And He marvelled because of their unbelief.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had opened the scroll and read out what He came to do, and offered it to them first. They took Him to the cliff instead, and He went elsewhere."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offer was withdrawn from the town, not from the world."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two marvellings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark says Jesus *marvelled* at Nazareth's unbelief. Matthew says He *marvelled* at a Roman centurion's faith, and said *I have not found faith like this anywhere in Israel.* See the note on Matthew 8:10–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only two things recorded as astonishing Him, and they are on opposite sides of the same question."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He had said just before the two examples.** *No prophet is accepted in his own country.* He knew what the room was going to do, and read the passage anyway."}]}, "Luke 5:1–3": {"title": "Two boats at the shore", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He needs a pulpit and there are two available."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crowd"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The crowd kept pressing in around Him, **hungry to hear the word of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a strong word — pressing, crushing. He is being backed toward the water by people who want to hear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two boats"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He noticed **two boats** pulled up at the water's edge, **empty**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both were there. Both were free. Neither owner was in them — the fishermen had climbed out and were washing their nets after a night that produced nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one He got into"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He stepped into **the one that belonged to Simon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke names the boat by its owner. Of the two available, He picks one, and it is the boat of a man who had worked all night and caught nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon is not asked whether he minds. He is asked to *push out a little way from the land* — a small thing, an inconvenience at the end of a bad shift."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Simon gave"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The loan of a boat for an hour, and the trouble of pushing it out and holding it steady while somebody else talked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole of his contribution."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he got back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next paragraph: *take her out into the deep water* — and the nets tear, and both boats fill until they are settling low in the water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The other boat, still empty on the shore, ends up loaded too. Simon's partners are waved over and they share it. See the note on Luke 5:4–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He handed over an empty boat and it came back sinking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The teaching happens first. He sits down in the boat and *taught the crowds from the water* — and only after He has finished speaking does He say anything about fish."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon heard the whole sermon from a few feet away, sitting in his own boat, before he was asked to let the nets down."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He was doing with a fishing boat.** Using the tool of a man's trade, on the water he had worked all night, to preach. And then filling it. Simon's business was not set aside for the ministry — it was pushed out a little way from the land and taught out of."}]}, "Luke 5:12–16": {"title": "I am willing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man asks about willingness, not power, and the answer takes his own word back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man came along who was **covered with leprosy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pleres lepras** — full of leprosy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke is a physician, and he is the only one who grades it. Matthew and Mark say *a leper*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While He was **in one of the towns**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which he should not have been. *He shall dwell alone; his dwelling shall be outside the camp.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The approach"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When he saw Jesus **he fell face down and begged Him**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, **if You are willing, You can make me clean**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ean theles, dunasai** — if You will, You are able."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has no doubt about the power. The uncertainty is entirely about the desire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the question is not strange"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leprosy was widely read as judgment. Miriam got it. Gehazi got it. Uzziah got it in the temple."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man in that condition had reason to wonder whether anybody, including God, wanted him well."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The touch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus **reached out His hand and touched him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hepsato autou** — touched him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under the law this makes the toucher unclean. Nobody had touched this man in however long he had been ill."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The healing did not require it. The word alone healed the centurion's servant at a distance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I am willing**,\" He said. \"**Be clean**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **thelo, katharistheti**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words, and the first one is the man's own word handed back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked *if You are willing*. The answer is **thelo** — I am willing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Touch first, then the word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The contact happens while the man is still a leper."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **immediately the leprosy left him**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Jesus told him **not to speak of it to anyone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go and show yourself to the priest**, and make the offering Moses commanded for your cleansing, **so that they can see for themselves**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus 14 sets out the ritual — two birds, cedar, scarlet, hyssop, and the priest's inspection."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priesthood has to certify a cleansed leper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no record of the ritual ever being performed before this. The procedure existed on the page and had nowhere to be used."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mark records the man doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*However, he went out and began to proclaim it freely, and to spread the matter, **so that Jesus could no longer openly enter the city**, but was outside in deserted places*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The leper goes into the towns. Jesus stays outside them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **word about Him spread even further**, and enormous crowds came together to listen and to be healed.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He would slip away to the quiet places, where He prayed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hupochoron en tais eremois kai proseuchomenos** — imperfect. Habitually."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke records this repeatedly: *He often withdrew*, *He went out to the mountain to pray, and continued all night*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The busier the crowds get in Luke, the more often he mentions the withdrawing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other ten"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They stood off at a distance and raised their voices… \"Go and show yourselves to the priests.\"* See the note on Luke 17:11–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No touch that time."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word.** He asked *if You are willing*. The answer is his own word, first: **thelo** — I am willing — and the hand was already on him."}]}, "Luke 5:17–20": {"title": "\"When Jesus saw their faith\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four men dig through a roof, and the sentence that follows is one of the most remarkable in the Gospels."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Pharisees and teachers of the law were sitting there, men who had come in from every village of Galilee and from Judea and from Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke is describing an inspection. Religious authorities have travelled from three regions to watch this man, and they are sitting down — the posture of people who intend to stay."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the same sentence: *the power of the Lord was present with Him to heal.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The roof"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Houses had flat roofs of beams, brushwood and packed mud, reached by an outside stair. Luke, writing for a Greek audience, says *tiles* — describing it in terms his readers would picture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way, four men take somebody's roof apart with a room full of dignitaries underneath, and lower a man on a mat into the gap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Their faith\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when Jesus saw **their** faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Plural. Luke does not say the paralysed man believed anything. Whatever he thought, the faith Jesus responds to is the faith of the men on the roof."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is worth sitting with. Somebody else's conviction got him in front of Jesus, and it is credited."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Friend, your sins are forgiven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not what anybody in the room came for. They had dug through a ceiling for legs, and He addresses something nobody mentioned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is literally *man*, but warm — the Illumination's *friend* catches the tone."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What follows.** The scribes reason that only God can forgive sins, and Jesus agrees with the premise: *so that you may know the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins* — He said to the paralysed man, get up. The healing is offered as evidence for the forgiveness, which tells you which of the two He considered the greater work."}]}, "Luke 5:21–26": {"title": "Which is easier to say", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He answers an objection nobody voiced, and settles it with something they can check."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A house so full that nobody can get in, and four men who take the roof off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when they could not find how they might bring him in because of the crowd, they went up on the housetop and let him down with his bed through the tiling into the midst before Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When He saw their faith, He said to him, \"Man, your sins are forgiven you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke notes whose faith: *their* faith. The men on the roof."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And notes who was in the room: *Pharisees and teachers of the law were sitting by, who had come out of every town of Galilee, Judea, and Jerusalem*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Who does this man think he is, talking that way? Only God can forgive sins**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **blasphemias** — blasphemies."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are right about the premise. Only God can forgive sins, and everybody in the room knows it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the argument, and it is a good one. The conclusion is the only part in dispute."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus **knew exactly what they were thinking**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epignous tous dialogismous** — knowing their reasonings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had not said it aloud. Luke says they *began turning it over among themselves*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Which is easier to say** — 'Your sins are forgiven,' or 'Get up and walk'?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eukopoteron** — easier, requiring less labour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Easier to *say*. That is the word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forgiveness is unverifiable. Anybody can announce it and nobody in the room can check."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Telling a paralysed man to walk is verifiable within seconds, and if nothing happens the speaker is finished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He offers the falsifiable claim as evidence for the unfalsifiable one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stated purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But so that you will know the Son of Man has authority here on earth to forgive sins**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exousian** — authority, the right to act."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence breaks off mid-clause. Luke marks the turn: *and here He turned to the paralyzed man*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The healing is inserted into the middle of a sentence addressed to the scribes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get up, pick up your mat, and go home**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three imperatives, and the third is the one that sends him out through the crowd carrying the evidence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **immediately, in front of them all**, the man stood up, took the mat he had been lying on, and **went home praising God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **enopion auton** — before them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wonder swept over every person in the room. They **gave glory to God**, and they were **filled with awe**, and they kept saying, \"**We have seen astonishing things today**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paradoxa** — things contrary to expectation. Where *paradox* comes from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says *every person*, which includes the men who had just accused Him of blasphemy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the title is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Son of Man** — His usual self-designation, and it comes from Daniel 7: *one like the Son of Man, coming with the clouds of heaven… and to Him was given dominion and glory and a kingdom*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It sounds modest and it is not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parallel accounts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark has the same episode with the roof and the four friends. See the note on Mark 2:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew compresses it and adds the crowd's conclusion: *they marvelled and glorified God, who had given such power to men*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word doing the work.** *Easier to say.* One claim can be checked in the next ten seconds and the other cannot — and He offers the checkable one as proof of the other."}]}, "Luke 5:4–7": {"title": "Out into the deep", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A carpenter tells a professional fisherman where the fish are, at the wrong time of day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Everything about it is wrong"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fishing on that lake was night work, with nets, in the shallows. Jesus says daytime, deep water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Simon has just spent the entire night proving there was nothing there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Simon says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Master, **we worked hard all night long and came up with nothing at all**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He states his case first, and it is a good one. Experience, effort, and the evidence of the last twelve hours all agree."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But because You say so**, I will let the nets down.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *at Your word*. That is the entire reason given, and it is set against everything he knows about his own trade."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Net, singular"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon says he will let down *the net* — one. Jesus had said *let your **nets** down*, plural."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke keeps the difference. And the next sentence is *the **nets** began to tear apart in their hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He went out with less than he was told to take."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What came up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Such an **enormous shoal** of fish that the nets began to tear.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same water. The same lake he had swept all night. The fish were not brought from somewhere else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Both boats"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They waved to their partners in the other boat to come and help them, and they came, and **the two boats were loaded so full** that they began to settle low in the water.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The other boat — the one He did not get into — is filled as well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The catch outruns the vessel that was lent. James and John, who had nothing to do with the loan, go home with a full boat too."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The loan of an empty boat and one more cast of a net he had already washed and put away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon gave the small thing he had at the end of a wasted night, and the return nearly sank two boats."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Depart from me, for I am a sinful man, O Lord.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not thank you. The size of the catch tells him who is in the boat with him, and his first instinct is to ask Him to leave."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not be afraid. From now on you will catch men.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they did with the haul.** *They brought their boats to land, left everything, and followed Him.* The biggest catch of Simon's life is sitting on the shore, and he walks away from it. It was never the point; it was the proof."}]}, "Luke 5:8–11": {"title": "He did not go away", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter asks Him to leave, and He stays and gives him a job."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What had just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A night of catching nothing, a borrowed boat used as a pulpit, and then *launch out into the deep and let down your nets for a catch*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's answer was *Master, we have toiled all night and caught nothing; nevertheless at Your word I will let down the net*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The nets broke, and both boats began to sink."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Simon Peter saw it, he **went down on his knees in front of Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in the water, not at a distance. In the boat, in the fish, on his knees."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go away from me, Lord. I am a sinful man**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exelthe ap' emou** — depart from me."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things in one breath: *Lord*, and *go away*. He has just correctly identified who is in the boat with him, and his instinct is distance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was **shaken to the core** by the size of the catch.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **thambos** — amazement, astonishment, a shock."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing had been said about Peter's sins. A fisherman was shown a boatload of fish, and what surfaced was what he was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who else was there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And so were the others with him, including **James and John, the sons of Zebedee**, who were **Simon's partners** in the fishing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **koinonoi** — partners, sharers. A business."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not be afraid**,\" He said to Simon.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *you are right to feel that*, and not *you are not a sinful man*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not correct the self-assessment and He does not leave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**From now on you will be catching people**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **zogron** — to take alive. Literally *catching men alive*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A fisherman kills what he catches. The verb He picks is the one that does not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they **brought the boats up onto the shore**, **left everything lying there**, and **went with Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The biggest catch of their lives, on the beach, walked away from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other boat, later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Children, do you have anything to eat?\"* — and again the net on the other side, and again more fish than the net should hold. See the note on John 21:4–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's response the second time is the opposite of this one: he puts his coat on and jumps in the water toward Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the two mornings sits a courtyard fire and a denial."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke does with the fear"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The line *do not be afraid* is Luke's refrain. Zacharias at the altar, Mary at the annunciation, the shepherds in the field, Jairus at the news of his daughter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every time, it is said to somebody who has just seen something."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He does with the request.** *Depart from me.* He answers by telling him not to be afraid, hands him a life's work, and then Peter leaves the boat to follow the man he had asked to leave."}]}, "Luke 6:17–19": {"title": "Power was flowing out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He comes down from a night of prayer and stands on flat ground with a crowd around Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What preceded it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now it came to pass in those days that **He went out to the mountain to pray, and continued all night in prayer to God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when it was day, He called His disciples to Himself; and **from them He chose twelve**, whom He also named apostles.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The night of prayer, then the choosing of the twelve, then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The descent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He came down the mountain with them and **stood on a wide stretch of level ground**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epi topou pedinou** — on a level place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's version of the sermon is preached here, which is why it is called the Sermon on the Plain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew has *He went up on a mountain, and when He was seated*. Luke has Him standing on flat ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two groups"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **great gathering of His disciples** was there, and **an enormous crowd of people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek distinguishes them: **ochlos matheton** — a crowd of disciples, and **plethos polu tou laou** — a great multitude of the people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From all over **Judea** and from **Jerusalem** and from **the coastal region around Tyre and Sidon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Tyre and Sidon are Gentile cities in Phoenicia."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke names them, which means the crowd is mixed before any commission has been given about Gentiles."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they came"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They had come **to listen to Him and to be healed of their diseases**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **akousai autou kai iathenai** — to hear Him and to be healed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two purposes, and the hearing is named first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third group"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And those who were **tormented by unclean spirits were set free**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone in that crowd was **trying to touch Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ezetoun haptesthai autou** — imperfect. They kept seeking to touch Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **power was flowing out from Him**, and it was healing them all.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dunamis par' autou exercheto** — power was going out from Him. Imperfect again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Iato pantas** — He was healing them all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the crowd had noticed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that He was healing individuals. That contact worked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word appears in the woman with the issue of blood: *I perceived power going out from Me*. See the note on Luke 8:43–48."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There the crowd pressed and only one drew. Here the whole crowd is reaching."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scale"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Pantas** — all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says it flatly. Nobody in that crowd was turned away or left as they came."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He lifted up His eyes toward His disciples, and said:*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Blessed are you poor, for yours is the kingdom of God. Blessed are you who hunger now, for you shall be filled. Blessed are you who weep now, for you shall laugh**.\"* See the note on Luke 6:20–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then four woes, which Matthew does not have: *woe to you who are rich… woe to you who are full… woe to you who laugh now*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who He is looking at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He lifted up His eyes **toward His disciples**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The beatitudes in Luke are in the second person — *blessed are **you** poor* — and addressed to the men He picked that morning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He asks them to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But I say to you who hear**: Love your enemies, do good to those who hate you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **alla humin lego tois akouousin** — but to you who are hearing, I say."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The imperfects.** Power *was going out*, and He *was healing* them all — continuous tense, of a crowd from Judea, Jerusalem and two Gentile cities, who came to hear and be healed in that order."}]}, "Luke 6:37–38": {"title": "\"Poured right into the fold of your coat\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The famous giving verse has three sentences in front of it, and they are all about something else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not sit in judgment** on people, and you will not be judged. **Do not hand down sentences**, and no sentence will be handed down on you. **Forgive**, and you will be forgiven.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three clauses, and only then: *give, and it will be given back to you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage is a run of four, and the first three are about how you treat people. Giving is the fourth item on a list about generosity of judgement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**A full measure, pressed down, shaken together, spilling over the top**, poured right into the fold of your coat.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four stages, and they describe a grain merchant filling a container properly:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**a full measure** — filled to the brim", "**pressed down** — compacted so the air is out", "**shaken together** — settled so it packs tighter", "**spilling over the top** — and then still more, until it runs over"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "An honest dealer did the first. A generous one did all four."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Into **the fold of your coat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The outer garment was belted, and the fabric above the belt formed a deep pouch — the way a man carried grain home when he had no sack."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a picture of somebody being given more than they came prepared to receive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule underneath it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whatever measure you use for others is the measure that will be used for you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence the whole passage runs on, and it applies to all four items — judgement, condemnation, forgiveness and giving alike."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The measure is not set by God's generosity, in this saying. It is set by yours, and then matched."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who \"they\" are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It will be given back to you\"* — the Greek is *they will give*, an impersonal plural. Luke does not name the giver."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same construction as *they will pour into your lap*. Something comes back, and the source is left unstated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits in the sermon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is Luke's Sermon on the Plain, and the paragraph before it is *love your enemies, do good, and lend, hoping for nothing in return… for He is kind to the unthankful and evil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be merciful, just as your Father also is merciful.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the model given, and then these four instructions."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the passage is not.** A transaction. The three clauses in front of it — judging, condemning, forgiving — cannot be done for a return without becoming something else entirely, and the giving is fourth in the same list."}]}, "Luke 6:43–45": {"title": "A tree and its fruit", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three sentences about produce, and the last one moves from orchards to speech."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**A good tree does not produce bad fruit**, and **a bad tree does not produce good fruit**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *bad* is **sapron** — rotten, worthless, decayed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated both ways, so there is no exception in either direction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The test"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Every tree is known by what it grows**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *each tree is known by its own fruit*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fruit is not what makes a tree good. It is what makes it identifiable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The examples"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Nobody picks **figs off a thornbush**, and nobody gathers **grapes from a briar patch**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's version has grapes from thornbushes and figs from thistles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are absurd on purpose. Nobody in the audience has ever tried it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The application"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"A **good person brings good things up out of the good stored away in the heart**, and an **evil person brings evil up out of the evil stored there**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **thesaurou** — a treasury, a storehouse. The root of *thesaurus*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something is laid up, and what comes out is drawn from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because **what the mouth says is whatever the heart is full of**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *out of the abundance of the heart the mouth speaks* — **ek tou perisseumatos**, out of the overflow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not what the heart contains. What it has too much of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Matthew adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same saying in Matthew is aimed at the Pharisees, and he adds: *for **every idle word** men may speak, they will give account of it in the day of judgment. For by your words you will be justified, and by your words you will be condemned.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before it in Luke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole Sermon on the Plain: love your enemies, judge not, give and it will be given to you, and the blind leading the blind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And immediately before this, the beam and the speck: *first remove the plank from your own eye*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tree saying follows a passage about seeing clearly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two houses — one on rock and one on earth without a foundation — and the flood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Why do you call Me 'Lord, Lord,' and **not do the things which I say**?\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the saying is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Diagnosis. Neither Gospel uses it to tell people to produce better fruit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A thornbush cannot be improved into a fig tree, and the sayings are about identification rather than effort."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the tree image goes elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is like **a tree planted by the water**… **it never stops bearing fruit**.* See the note on Jeremiah 17:7–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tree there is *transplanted* — set out deliberately by somebody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the fruit of the Spirit is singular in Paul, and grows rather than being assembled. See the note on Galatians 5:22–24."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the last sentence measures.** Not what is in the heart, but what there is too much of. The Greek is *out of the overflow* — the mouth reports the surplus."}]}, "Luke 7:11–17": {"title": "The widow of Nain", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two crowds meet at a town gate, one coming in and one carrying a body out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Nain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name means pleasant. It sits in the Jezreel valley, in the territory of Issachar — the tribe whose blessing was about seeing that *the resting place was good and the land was pleasant*, and taking up a load anyway. See the note on Genesis 49:14–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who she was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **only son** his mother had, and she was **a widow**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives the two facts that decide everything. No husband, and now no son. In that world she has no income, no legal standing and no protection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is burying her boy and her own future in the same procession."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Nobody asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the point at which the story differs from almost every other healing in the Gospels. There is no request."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not know who He is. She has not called out, touched a hem, sent friends, or torn open a roof. She is walking behind a bier, crying."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the Lord saw her, **His heart went out to her**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **splagchnizomai** — moved in the inward parts, the strongest word the language had for compassion. Luke says the compassion is for **her**, not for the boy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Please, do not cry\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "An impossible thing to say to a widow at her only son's funeral, unless you are about to do something about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The touch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He **stepped up and laid His hand on the frame** they were carrying, and the men bearing it **stood still**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Touching the bier made a man ceremonially unclean. He does it anyway, in public, in front of a great many people from the town."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bearers stop. Nobody has said anything yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Young man, I say to you, get up.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five words to a corpse. Not a prayer — He does not ask God for anything. He speaks to the boy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The dead man sat up and began to speak.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The handing over"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Jesus **gave him back to his mother**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the last act, and it is the whole point of the story. He does not enlist him, instruct him, or ask anything of either of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The boy is put back into his mother's hands, and Jesus walks on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elisha needed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The other raising in that valley is Elisha and the Shunammite's son, a few miles away over the hill."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That one took a shut door, a prayer, lying over the boy mouth to mouth, getting up and pacing the room, and stretching over him a second time. Then *call the Shunammite*, and *take your son* — she had to come and get him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here: one sentence, and He carries the boy across Himself. See the note on 2 Kings 4:32–37."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the crowd said.** *\"A great prophet has risen among us! God has come to help His people!\"* The Greek for *has come to help* is God visiting His people — the same word Zechariah used at the start of Luke, when *the Dayspring from on high has visited us.*"}]}, "Luke 7:36–39": {"title": "A woman at His feet", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "She gets in without an invitation, and the host says nothing out loud."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The invitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One of the Pharisees invited Jesus to eat with him, so **He went** to the man's house and took His place at the table.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He accepts. Luke's Gospel has Him at three different Pharisees' tables, and He goes every time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How they were sitting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Took His place at the table.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At a formal meal guests reclined on couches around a low table, propped on the left elbow, feet pointing away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why she can be at His feet without being under the table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who she was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A woman in that town who was **known for her sinful life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives her no name. The town knew her; the Gospel does not say for what."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is not Mary Magdalene, and she is not Mary of Bethany. Luke introduces both of them elsewhere and does not connect them to this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How she got in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When she **learned that Jesus was eating at the Pharisee's house**, she came.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Formal meals in that world were semi-public — the door stood open and people came in to listen from the edges of the room. Getting in was not the hard part."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Getting in as her was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she brought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*An **alabaster jar of perfume**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She came carrying it, which means she had decided what she was going to do before she left home."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The jar had to be broken to be used."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She **stood behind Him at His feet, weeping**, and her tears began to fall on His feet. She **wiped them with her own hair**, and **kissed His feet over and over**, and **poured the perfume on them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The plan was the perfume. The tears were not planned — they start, and then she has to improvise a way to deal with them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A woman's hair was bound up in public. She unbinds it in a room full of reclining men."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kissed His feet over and over** — the Greek is intensive and continuous."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the host thinks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He said **to himself**, \"**If this man were really a prophet, He would know who is touching Him, and what kind of woman she is**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say it aloud. Luke records the interior sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the sentence is a test of prophecy: a real prophet would know."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The irony Luke leaves standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus answers the thought. The next verse is *Simon, I have something to say to you* — and the host had never opened his mouth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The test was passed in the act of answering it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Simon had not done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Water for the feet, a kiss of welcome, oil for the head. Three ordinary courtesies, and he had skipped all three. Jesus lists them a few verses later. See the note on Luke 7:44–50."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parable in between"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two debtors, five hundred denarii and fifty, both forgiven. *Which of them will love him more?* See the note on Luke 7:40–43."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon answers it correctly and does not see himself in it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What she does not do.** Ask for anything, say anything, or explain herself. Luke records no words from her in the whole account — only the weeping, the hair, the kissing, and the jar."}]}, "Luke 7:40–43": {"title": "Two debts, one lender, and the question Simon answers correctly", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus is at a Pharisee's dinner. A woman with a reputation has just come in and wept on His feet, and Simon has said nothing out loud."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the story is told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon's objection was internal: *if this man were a prophet, He would know what kind of woman this is.* Luke tells us he said it to himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus answers the thought. *Simon, there is something I want to tell you* — which is itself the reply to the doubt about whether He knew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two figures"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five hundred denarii and fifty. A denarius was a day's wage, so roughly two years of work against two months."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gap is real and Jesus does not flatten it. One debt is genuinely ten times the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail that decides it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Neither of them had anything to pay with.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the equaliser, and it is easy to read past. The difference between the two men is the size of the debt, not their ability to clear it. Both are at zero."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten times nothing and one times nothing come to the same thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for \"forgave\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's Greek is **charizomai** — from **charis**, grace. To give freely, to grant as a favour, to cancel as a gift."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not the courtroom word for acquittal. It is what you do with a debt you have decided to hand over rather than collect."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same verb of God in Colossians: *forgiving you every trespass.* See the note on Colossians 2:11–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which of the two will love him more?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon answers correctly, and slightly grudgingly — *I suppose*. Jesus says he has judged rightly, and then applies it: you gave Me no water, no kiss, no oil; she has not stopped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction of the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Her many sins have been forgiven — for she loved much.* Read alone that can sound like love earning the forgiveness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parable Jesus has just told settles the order. In the story, the cancelling comes first and the loving follows from it. The love is the evidence of the debt released, not the price of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last thing He says to her.** *Your faith has saved you. Go in peace.* Not your love. Luke has Him say the same words to the woman in the crowd who touched His clothes. See the note on Mark 5:30–34."}]}, "Luke 7:44–50": {"title": "\"Do you see this woman?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He is at a Pharisee's table, and He turns His back on the host to address him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The posture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **He turned toward the woman** and said **to Simon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He faces her and speaks to him. Luke records the direction of the body, which he did not need to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do you see this woman?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon has seen her. His whole objection was about seeing her — *if this man were a prophet, He would know **who and what sort of woman** this is.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question is not whether she is in the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three comparisons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each one is the same shape: what Simon did not do, and what she did."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*\"You gave Me **no water for My feet** — but she has **washed My feet with her tears** and dried them with her hair.\"*", "*\"You gave Me **no kiss of welcome** — but from the moment I walked in **she has not stopped kissing My feet**.\"*", "*\"You did **not pour oil on My head** — but **she has poured perfume on My feet**.\"*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Water, a kiss, oil. All three were ordinary courtesies owed to a guest, and Simon had skipped every one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She supplied all three, and in each case at the feet rather than the head."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her hair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A woman's hair was bound up in public. She unbound it in a room full of men to dry His feet, having already made a scene by crying on them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So I tell you this: **her many sins have been forgiven, and that is why she has loved so much**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The forgiveness is stated first and the love is the consequence. Luke's Greek puts the forgiving in the perfect tense — already done, standing done — before she ever entered the house."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **the one who has been forgiven little loves little**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to a man who had just been told a parable about two debtors, one owing fifty denarii and one five hundred, and had answered correctly which would love more."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gave the right answer about the story and missed himself in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says to her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He said to her, \"**Your sins are forgiven**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is told directly, out loud, in front of the table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Who is this man, that He even forgives sins?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same question the scribes asked when the paralytic's sins were forgiven. It is the right question, asked by people who will not follow it anywhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dismissal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Your faith has saved you. Go on your way in peace**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *your tears*, and not *your perfume*. Her faith."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *go in peace* is the standard Hebrew farewell — *lech leshalom*, go toward peace."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the perfume was.** An alabaster flask, which had to be broken to be used. She came carrying it, so she had decided what she was going to do before she reached the door."}]}, "Luke 8:11–15": {"title": "What the seed means", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The one parable Jesus explains line by line, and He says elsewhere that it is the key to the others."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The key"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark records the aside: *do you not understand this parable? **How then will you understand all the parables?***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This one is the pattern."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The identification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The seed is the word of God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's phrasing. Matthew has *the word of the kingdom*; Mark has *the sower sows the word*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One thing sown, four outcomes, and the seed is identical in every case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The footpath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The people who hear, and then **the devil comes and lifts the word right out of their hearts**, so that they will not believe and be saved.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke names the agent and the purpose. Matthew has *the wicked one*; Mark has *Satan*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing wrong with the seed and nothing wrong with the sowing. It was taken."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rock"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The people who **hear the word and welcome it with real gladness, but they have no root**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meta charas** — with joy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The problem is not the reception. It is enthusiastic."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They **believe for a little while**, and when the testing comes **they fall away**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pros kairon pisteuousin** — for a season they believe."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **aphistantai** — they stand off, withdraw. The root of *apostasy*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says *in time of testing*; Mark says *tribulation or persecution arises for the word's sake*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The thorns"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The people who hear, but **as they go on their way**, the **worries and the money and the pleasures of this life crowd in and choke them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three chokers named: **merimnon**, cares; **ploutou**, riches; **hedonon**, pleasures."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark adds a fourth: *the desires for other things*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *choke* is **sumpnigontai** — throttled, strangled. The same verb Luke uses of the swine drowning and of the crowd pressing round Jesus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **nothing ever ripens**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ou telesphorousin** — they do not bring to completion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Growth happens. Maturity does not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What thorns are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ground's first product after the curse. See the note on Genesis 3:17–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what the soldiers plaited into a wreath. See the note on John 19:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The good ground"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The people who **hear the word with an honest and generous heart**, and **hold on to it**, and **by patient endurance bring in a harvest**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things, and Luke is the only one who lists them this way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kardia kale kai agathe** — a noble and good heart. It is the Greek phrase for a gentleman's character, and Luke, writing for a Greek reader, uses it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Katechousin** — hold fast, retain, keep a grip."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**En hupomone** — in endurance, remaining under."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is missing from Luke's version"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The yields."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew and Mark have *thirtyfold, sixtyfold, a hundredfold*. Luke has *a hundredfold* in the parable itself and drops the numbers from the explanation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four differences"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seed is the same, the sower is the same, and the soil is the only variable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three of the four hear. Two of the four start well. One finishes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the enemy features"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only in the first soil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second is undone by shallowness, the third by ordinary life. Nothing supernatural is named in either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"No one lights a lamp and covers it with a jar… **so pay attention to how you listen**.\"* See the note on Luke 8:16–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parable about soils is followed by an instruction about the manner of hearing."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is choking the third soil.** Not persecution and not the devil. Worries, money and pleasures — and Luke says the plant grows and simply never finishes."}]}, "Luke 8:16–18": {"title": "Pay attention to how you listen", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three sayings in a row, and the last one is about the ear rather than the message."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lamp"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No one lights a lamp and then covers it with a jar or shoves it under a bed**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A first-century lamp is a clay dish of oil with a wick. Put a jar over it and it goes out; put it under a bed and the bed catches."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither option in the sentence is a sensible way to store a lamp. Both are absurd on purpose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You put it up on a stand**, so that **everyone who comes in can see by its light**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's version says who benefits — the people entering the house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's has *it gives light to all who are in the house*. Luke is thinking about arrivals."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Nothing is hidden that will not be brought out into the open**, and nothing is covered up that will not be uncovered and made plain.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice, in two ways, in one verse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same sentence appears elsewhere as a warning: *what you have spoken in the ear in inner rooms will be proclaimed on the housetops*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here it is attached to the lamp, which makes it a promise about the message rather than a warning about secrets. What is being taught in private will end up public."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **pay attention to how you listen**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **blepete oun pos akouete** — take heed *how* you hear."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark's version has *take heed **what** you hear*. Luke has *how*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between them the two Gospels cover both: what goes in, and the manner of the listening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whoever has will be given more, and whoever has nothing will lose even what he thinks he has**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's addition is in the last clause — **ho dokei echein**, what he *seems* to have, what he *thinks* he has."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew has *even what he has will be taken away*. See the note on Matthew 13:10–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's version says the thing lost was never really held."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being measured"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark makes it explicit: *with the same measure you use, it will be measured to you; and to you who hear, more will be given*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The attention given is the size of the container."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately after the parable of the sower and its explanation, and immediately before His mother and brothers arrive outside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The four soils, and then three sayings about hearing, and then: *My mother and My brothers are **these who hear the word of God and do it***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke arranges the whole passage around one subject."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lamp and the soil"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both images are about receiving. The soil takes seed in; the lamp gives light out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between them is the sentence about hearing, and it belongs to both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mark does with the same material"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark 4 has the lamp, the measure, and then two more seed parables — the one about the man who sleeps and does not know how it grows, and the mustard seed. See the note on Mark 4:26–29."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the light image goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are the light of the world. A city that is set on a hill cannot be hidden.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *let your light so shine before men, that they may see your good works and glorify your Father in heaven*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same lamp, same stand, applied to people."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word Luke changes.** Mark says take heed *what* you hear; Luke says take heed *how*. And the thing lost, in his version, is what a man only thought he had."}]}, "Luke 8:30–33": {"title": "Legion and the herd", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The largest concentration of the enemy named anywhere in the Gospels, and it takes one word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The eastern shore of the lake, the country of the Gerasenes — the old territory of Gad, out beyond the Jordan."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gad's blessing was one line: *Gad will be attacked by raiders, but he will turn and strike at their heels.* The Hebrew hammers the word for *troop* five times over. See the note on Genesis 49:19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He crossed the water into the tribe of the troop, and the first thing that met Him on the beach was a legion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What is your name?\" \"**Legion**,\" he answered, because a great many demons had gone into him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Roman legion was some thousands of soldiers at full strength. The man does not give a name — he gives a headcount."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark adds *for we are many*, and the count of the pigs: about two thousand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is asking whom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they **begged** Him not to order them away into the abyss… the demons **begged** Him to let them go into the pigs instead.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice in three verses. Whatever they are, they cannot move without permission, and the whole exchange is them asking and Him deciding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The abyss** is the bottomless place, the same word John uses in Revelation. It is what they are afraid of, and they name it themselves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing. No struggle, no formula, no repetition. *He gave them permission*, and they came out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke has already told us what the man was before: no clothes, no house, living among the tombs, breaking chains and shackles, driven into the wilderness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And after: *sitting at the feet of Jesus, clothed and in his right mind.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the town wanted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not more of it. *They asked Him to depart from them, for they were seized with great fear.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He got into the boat and went."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one who wanted to come"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man begged to go with Him — a third begging in the chapter — and this is the one request Jesus refuses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Return to your own house, and tell what great things God has done for you.\" And he went his way and proclaimed throughout the whole city.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who could not be bound is sent home to talk about it, and he is the first person commissioned to preach in Gentile territory."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Gad's line, and where it lands.** *He will turn and strike at their heels.* The heel is the word from Genesis 3 — *He shall bruise your head, and you shall bruise His heel.* Jacob used it over the son whose name means troop, and a legion went into the lake on that tribe's ground."}]}, "Luke 8:40–42": {"title": "Jairus falls at His feet", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man with a title lies down in the road, and the clock is already running."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The return"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Jesus came back across, **a crowd welcomed Him, because they had all been waiting and watching for Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apedexato** — received gladly. And **prosdokontes** — expecting, on the lookout."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just come from the other side of the lake, where the Gadarenes had *asked Him to depart from them, for they were seized with great fear*. See the note on Mark 5:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One shore begs Him to leave. The other has been watching the water for Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And a man named **Jairus** came, **a leader of the synagogue**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **archon tes sunagoges** — ruler of the synagogue."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The office in charge of the building, the services, and who was invited to read and speak."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His name is the Greek form of **Ya'ir** — *he will enlighten*, or *he will awaken*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What his position means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The synagogue leadership is the group already in conflict with Jesus over the Sabbath healings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the most establishment figure in the town."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **fell down at Jesus' feet**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **peson para tous podas** — falling at the feet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In public, in front of a crowd, in the street."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Begging Him to come to his house.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parekalei** — was imploring. Imperfect: he kept asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **his only daughter, a girl of about twelve, was dying**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **thugater monogenes** — an only-begotten daughter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Monogenes** — the word John uses of the Son."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke notes the same detail at Nain — *the only son of his mother, and she was a widow* — and of the boy with the spirit: *he is my only child*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke keeps counting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The age"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The woman in the next paragraph has been bleeding for twelve years. Luke sets the numbers next to each other and says nothing about them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The delay"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Jesus went with him, and **the crowds pressed in so tightly that they were nearly crushing Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sunepnigon** — were choking, suffocating. The same verb Luke uses of thorns choking seed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens on the way"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A woman who has spent everything she had on physicians touches the edge of His garment, and He stops."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Who touched Me?\"* And Peter: *Master, the multitudes throng and press You, and You say, \"Who touched Me?\"* See the note on Luke 8:43–48."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the stop costs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "While He is speaking to her, a messenger arrives: *your daughter is dead. Do not trouble the Teacher*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The delay and the death are in consecutive verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not be afraid; only believe, and she will be made well**.\"* See the note on Luke 8:49–56."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at the house, *she is not dead, but sleeping*, and they laughed at Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He took her by the hand and called, saying, \"Little girl, arise.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He commanded that she be given something to eat.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the two stories share"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve years and twelve years old. A woman nobody could name and a girl whose father everybody knew."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One reaches out and is stopped and questioned. One is dead before He arrives."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke braids them deliberately, and the interruption that costs the girl her life is what the woman gets."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clock.** He is on His way to a dying child and stops to find out who touched Him. Luke puts the delay and the death in consecutive verses."}]}, "Luke 8:43–48": {"title": "Who touched Me", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He is on His way to a dying child and stops the whole procession for a question He already knows the answer to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He is going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jairus's house. His only daughter, twelve years old, is dying, and the crowd is nearly crushing Him. See the note on Luke 8:40–42."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The woman"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A woman **having a flow of blood for twelve years**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve years — the same number as the girl's age. Luke does not comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her condition under the law"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If a woman has a discharge of blood for many days… **she shall be unclean**… every bed on which she lies shall be unclean, and everything on which she sits shall be unclean.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve years outside. She cannot go to the temple, and anyone she touches becomes unclean."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is in a crowd pressing against a man, which is not permitted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she had spent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who **had spent all her livelihood on physicians and could not be healed by any**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke is a physician. He records that the physicians failed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark is blunter: *she had suffered many things from many physicians, spent all that she had, and was no better, but rather grew worse*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The approach"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Came from behind and touched **the border of His garment**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tou kraspedou** — the hem, and specifically the tassel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers 15 commanded a tassel on the corner of the garment, with a blue thread, *that you may look upon it and remember all the commandments of the LORD*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She reaches for the part of His clothing that carried the law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **immediately her flow of blood stopped**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Jesus said, \"**Who touched Me?**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Peter's objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Master, **the multitudes throng and press You**, and You say, 'Who touched Me?'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is entirely reasonable. Dozens of people are in contact with Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distinction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Somebody touched Me**, for I perceived power going out from Me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hepsato mou tis** — someone touched Me."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke distinguishes two verbs. **Sunthlibo** — the crowd's pressing. **Haptomai** — her touching."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The difference is not physical. Everybody was in contact; one person drew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why He asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to identify her. He knows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She could have gone home healed and anonymous, still legally unclean, with no one told. The question is what stops that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now when the woman saw that she was not hidden, **she came trembling**; and falling down before Him, she declared to Him **in the presence of all the people** the reason she had touched Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In public, in front of a crowd, a woman states twelve years of a condition nobody discussed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He calls her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Daughter**, be of good cheer; your faith has made you well. Go in peace.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **thugater**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not use the word of anybody else in the Gospels."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jairus's **thugater** is dying up the road. This woman is given the same word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sesoken se** — has saved you. The wide word, covering healing and salvation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cost of the stop"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While He was still speaking, someone came from the ruler of the synagogue's house, saying to him, \"**Your daughter is dead. Do not trouble the Teacher**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The interruption and the death are in consecutive verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says to Jairus"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not be afraid; only believe, and she will be made well**.\"* See the note on Luke 8:49–56."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb He has just used of the woman."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jairus has watched the delay that cost him, and been given a demonstration of what the delay can do."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two verbs.** The crowd **pressed**. She **touched**. Luke uses different words and says the difference was not physical."}]}, "Luke 8:49–56": {"title": "\"She is asleep\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The news arrives while He is still talking to somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While He was **still speaking**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is finishing the conversation with the woman who touched His clothes — telling her *daughter, your faith has made you well*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jairus has been standing in the street through all of it, with a dying child at home. See the note on Mark 5:30–34."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The message"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Your daughter is dead. Do not trouble the Teacher any further**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **skulle** — do not bother, do not harass."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is meant kindly. There is nothing left to ask for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Jesus **overheard it** and said to Jairus, \"**Do not be afraid. Only believe, and she will be well**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *having heard* — He was not addressed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Only believe** — the Greek is a single word, **pisteuson**, aorist imperative."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who goes in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He would not let anyone go in with Him except **Peter and James and John**, and **the child's father and mother**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five people. The three He takes everywhere, and the parents."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is in the room, and thirty years later he does the same thing in an upstairs room in Joppa. See the note on Acts 9:36–43."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was happening in the house"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone there was **weeping and wailing over her**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Professional mourners were engaged immediately in that culture — flute players and wailing women. Matthew mentions the flutes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The house is already full of a funeral."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Stop your crying. She is not dead. She is asleep**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **they laughed at Him**, because **they knew she had died**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kategelon** — laughed him to scorn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were right about the fact. Luke says so plainly: *knowing that she was dead*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He meant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sleep is the New Testament's usual word for a believer's death, and He uses it of Lazarus too — where the disciples make the same mistake in the opposite direction. See the note on John 11:11–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a denial of the death. It is a statement about how long it lasts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clearing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He **put them all outside**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ekbalon** — thrust out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mourners go. Only the five stay."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **took her by the hand** and called out, \"**My child, get up!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark keeps the Aramaic: **Talitha koum**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **her spirit came back to her, and she stood up right then**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke the physician gives the sequence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He told them to **give her something to eat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve years old, raised from the dead, and the instruction is dinner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark notes her age at exactly this point, and the woman on the road had been bleeding for twelve years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Her parents **could hardly take it in**, and He told them **not to say a word to anyone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which was never going to hold. Matthew: *and the report of this went out into all that land.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who was right about the facts.** The mourners. She was dead and they knew it, and they laughed — and He put them outside and called her the way you would call a child in the morning."}]}, "Luke 9:10–17": {"title": "Kept handing the pieces to the disciples", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only miracle in all four Gospels, and the multiplication is never described."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What preceded it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the apostles came back, **they told Him everything they had done**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have just returned from being sent out two by two with power over demons and to heal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took them away with Him, **quietly**, to a lonely stretch of country near the town called Bethsaida.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is trying to get them a rest. Mark records the reason: *there were many coming and going, and they did not even have time to eat*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the crowds found out where He had gone and **came after Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest is cancelled before it starts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does with the interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **welcomed them**, spoke to them about the kingdom of God, and **healed all who needed healing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apodexamenos** — received them gladly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not tolerated. Welcomed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disciples' proposal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Send the crowd off** so they can go to the villages and farms around here and find food and somewhere to sleep. **We are out in the middle of nowhere**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a sensible suggestion. The day is going, the place is empty, and the numbers are large."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their solution is dispersal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You give them something to eat**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek puts **humeis** — *you* — in the emphatic position, which Greek only does deliberately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Men who have just come back from a successful mission are handed something they cannot do."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inventory"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**We have five loaves and two fish, and that is all**,\" they said, \"unless You want us to go and buy food for this whole crowd.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John names the source: *there is a boy here with five barley loaves and two small fish*. See the note on John 6:5–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Barley was the cheap grain. The whole supply is one child's packed lunch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There were about **five thousand men** there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Andres** — males. Matthew adds *besides women and children*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seating"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Have them **sit down in groups of about fifty**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which turns a crowd into a countable arrangement, and means the distribution can be tracked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark says they sat *in ranks, by hundreds and by fifties*, on green grass."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **took** the five loaves and the two fish, **looked up to heaven**, **gave thanks** and **broke** them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Took, blessed, broke, gave — the same four verbs Luke uses at the last supper, and again at Emmaus, where *He took bread, blessed and broke it, and gave it to them* and their eyes were opened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the multiplication happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **kept handing the pieces to the disciples** to set in front of the people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek imperfect: **edidou** — he was giving, continuously."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text never says the bread multiplied. It says He kept handing it over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever happened, happened between His hands and theirs, and the disciples walked back to Him for more, again and again, all evening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone ate, and **everyone had enough** — and when they gathered up what was left over, there were **twelve baskets full**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **echortasthesan** — were filled, satisfied. The word used of feeding animals to fullness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve baskets, and twelve men who had said *that is all*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He asks straight afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Who do the crowds say that I am?**\"… \"But who do you say that I am?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter: *the Christ of God*. See the note on Luke 9:18–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question comes immediately after the meal."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The tense.** **Edidou** — he kept on giving. The text never describes bread multiplying; it describes a man handing pieces over, and twelve men coming back for more all evening."}]}, "Luke 9:28–36": {"title": "His face changed while praying", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Luke's account of the transfiguration keeps three details the others leave out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason for going up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took Peter and John and James with Him and went up on a mountain **to pray**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke alone gives the purpose. Matthew and Mark say He led them up; Luke says why."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke does this throughout — he records Jesus praying at His baptism, before choosing the twelve, before Peter's confession, and in Gethsemane."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **while He was praying**, the look of His face was changed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The change occurs during the prayer, not after it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew uses **metamorphoo** — transformed. Luke avoids the word, which had associations with pagan myth for Greek readers, and describes the appearance instead. See the note on Matthew 17:1–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two men"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Two men appeared, talking with Him — **Moses and Elijah** — and they were **shining with glory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lawgiver and the prophet. Moses died and was buried by God in a valley in Moab; Elijah went up in a whirlwind. See the note on 2 Kings 2:11–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they discussed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were speaking with Him about **His departure**, the thing **He was going to accomplish at Jerusalem**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only Luke records the subject."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *departure* is **exodos**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses, who led the first exodus, is standing on a mountain discussing the second one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Accomplish** — the Greek is **pleroun**, to fulfil, to complete."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disciples"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peter and the two with him were **heavy with sleep**, and **when they came fully awake** they saw His glory.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke alone says they were asleep. He notes the same thing in Gethsemane — *He found them sleeping from sorrow*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They nearly missed it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Peter's offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Master, it is good for us to be here. **Let us put up three shelters** — one for You, one for Moses, and one for Elijah.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three, of equal size."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke adds: *he **did not know what he was saying**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And notes when he said it: *as Moses and Elijah were leaving*. He is trying to keep them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **while he was still speaking**, a cloud came and settled over them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is cut off mid-sentence, twice over — by the cloud and then by the voice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The voice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This is My Son, the One I love. Listen to Him.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's version has *the One I love*; some manuscripts read *My Chosen One*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The command is the same in all three Gospels: **listen to Him**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It answers the offer of three shelters. There are not three voices to attend to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the voice had finished, **Jesus was there alone**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **they kept it to themselves**. In those days they told no one a word of what they had seen.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter broke the silence eventually, in writing: *we were eyewitnesses of His majesty… we heard this voice which came from heaven when we were with Him on the holy mountain.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was said eight days before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The Son of Man must suffer many things… and be killed, and be raised the third day.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *\"if anyone desires to come after Me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross daily.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The glory on the mountain comes eight days after the first announcement of the cross, and the conversation on it is about the same subject."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Luke keeps that nobody else does.** That He went up to pray, that the change happened while He prayed, that the three were asleep, and what the three of them were talking about."}]}, "Luke 9:37–43": {"title": "He gave him back to his father", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "They come down off the mountain into the worst scene in the chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **next day**, when they had come down from the hill.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hill is the transfiguration. See the note on Luke 9:28–36."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses, Elijah, the face changed, the voice from the cloud — and then this, the following morning, at the bottom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Raphael's last painting puts both scenes on one canvas for exactly this reason."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The father"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man in the crowd called out, \"**Teacher, please, look at my son. He is my only child**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **monogenes** — only-begotten. Luke uses the word three times, all of them of an only child: the widow's son at Nain, Jairus' daughter, and this boy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"A spirit takes hold of him and he **suddenly screams**, and it **throws him into convulsions until he is foaming**, and it **wears him down and will hardly let him go**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark's version adds that it has thrown him into fire and water, and that it has been happening *from childhood*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *wears him down* is **suntribon** — bruising, crushing, shattering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had already tried"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I begged Your disciples to drive it out, and **they could not**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nine of them were at the bottom of the hill. Luke reports the failure without comment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark adds that the scribes were arguing with them about it when Jesus arrived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What an unbelieving, twisted generation this is. How long must I be with you and put up with this?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **diestrammene** — twisted, distorted, bent out of shape."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One of very few times He is recorded saying something like this. It is not aimed at the father."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Bring your son here to Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words after the exclamation, and the whole thing turns on them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened on the way"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even as the boy was coming, the **demon threw him to the ground and shook him violently**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last attack happens in transit, in front of everybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Jesus **spoke sharply to the unclean spirit, healed the boy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epetimesen** — rebuked. One word to the spirit, and Luke does not record what was said."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **gave him back to his father**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apedoken** — gave back, restored, returned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke uses the same construction at Nain: *and He presented him to his mother*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The healing is not the end of the sentence. The handing over is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the crowd did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone there was **astonished at the greatness of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **megaleioteti** — the majesty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mark records the father saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If You can do anything, have compassion on us and help us.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If you can believe**, all things are possible to him who believes.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Lord, I believe; help my unbelief!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most honest sentence anybody says in the Gospels, and the boy is healed immediately after it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The private question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The disciples ask afterwards why they could not do it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew: *because of your unbelief… if you have faith as a mustard seed, you will say to this mountain, \"Move from here to there,\" and it will move.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark: *this kind can come out by nothing but prayer and fasting.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says while they are still marvelling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let these words sink down into your ears, for **the Son of Man is about to be betrayed into the hands of men**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke puts it in the same breath as the crowd's astonishment. The sentence begins in verse 43 and finishes in 44."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But they did not understand this saying… and they were afraid to ask Him.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the account ends.** Not with the boy standing up. *And gave him back to his father* — Luke does the same at Nain, and both times the last clause is a handing over."}]}, "Luke 9:51–56": {"title": "\"You have no idea what kind of spirit you are speaking out of\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two disciples propose burning a village down, and they have a Bible verse for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the village refused Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The village would not receive Him, because He was clearly heading for Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samaritans worshipped on Mount Gerizim and regarded Jerusalem's temple as illegitimate. A pilgrim on the road there was a walking insult."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The refusal was religious, and it was rude."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proposal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lord, do You want us to call fire down out of heaven and burn them up, **the way Elijah did**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James and John are not inventing this. Elijah had called fire down on soldiers sent to arrest him, in that same region, and they knew the passage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have precedent, provocation, and permission-seeking manners. Everything is in order except the request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rebuke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have no idea what kind of spirit you are speaking out of. The Son of Man did not come to destroy people's lives. He came to save them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not argue about Elijah. He tells them they have misread which era they are standing in and what He came to do."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the diagnosis is about the **source** of the suggestion, not its logic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who these two were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same pair who a chapter later ask for the seats on His right and left. Jesus nicknamed them Sons of Thunder, and Luke keeps giving us evidence for why."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John, who wanted to incinerate a village, is the one who ends up writing *God is love* and *we love Him because He loved us first.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they simply went on to another village.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No confrontation, no curse, no shaking of dust recorded. The response to being turned away is to keep walking."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He had just done.** *He set His face to go to Jerusalem, and nothing would turn Him from it.* Luke puts that sentence three verses before this — the road they were refused passage on was the road to the cross. He was on His way to die for Samaritans, and would not let anyone burn one."}]}, "Malachi 3:1–4": {"title": "\"Who can endure the day of His coming?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last book of the Old Testament announces a messenger, and then asks a question nobody wants to answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two figures"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am sending My messenger, and he will prepare the way before Me. Then suddenly the Lord you are seeking will come to His temple.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Gospels take the first as John the Baptist — all four quote or allude to this verse — and the second as Christ. *Suddenly* is the word Malachi chooses, and four hundred silent years followed before it happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who can endure the day of His coming? Who can stand when He appears?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people of Malachi's day had been complaining that God was absent and that serving Him was pointless. The prophet's answer is to ask whether they have thought through what His arrival would mean."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same reversal the prophets keep performing — Amos asking why anyone would long for the day of the LORD, since it is darkness and not light."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two images"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are about cleaning, and both hurt:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**a refiner's fire** — the silversmith holds the metal in the heat until the impurities rise and can be lifted off", "**a launderer's soap** — a harsh alkaline lye, the strongest cleaning agent of the ancient world"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point of both is stated: *until they bring offerings to the LORD in righteousness*. The refining has an object, and it is worship that is actually worth something."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who gets refined.** *He will purify the Levites* — the priests, the professionals, the people running the temple. The chapter that goes on to charge a nation with robbing God opens by putting the clergy in the fire first."}]}, "Malachi 3:7–12": {"title": "The one place God says \"test Me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God forbids putting Him to the test everywhere else in scripture. Here He invites it, and the subject is giving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the passage begins"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not with money. *Return to Me, and I will return to you.* The tithe question arrives inside an invitation to come back, and it is God's answer to their own question — *how are we to return?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That order matters. This is not a demand for revenue. It is a description of what turning back looks like for a people who had drifted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The invitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bring the whole tithe into the storehouse… **Test Me in this**, says the LORD Almighty, and see if I will not throw open the floodgates of heaven and pour out so much blessing that there won't be room enough to store it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three promises follow, and they are specific:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["the floodgates opened — the same word used of the windows of heaven at the flood", "**the devourer rebuked** — the locust stopped before it reaches the crop", "the vine not dropping its fruit early"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two of the three are about **protection** rather than increase. What is already coming is guarded so it arrives."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tithe is older than the law"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the point that decides how a Christian reads it. Abraham gave a tenth to Melchizedek in Genesis 14 — four hundred and thirty years before Sinai, with no commandment anywhere requiring it. Jacob promised a tenth at Bethel before there was a law to obey."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the tenth did not begin as a Mosaic regulation and does not stand or fall with one. It began as something a man did in response to a priest who blessed him, which is the order it still runs in: the blessing first, the tenth after."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Hebrews says the priest receiving it now is the living one. See the note on Hebrews 7:8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What this passage is not for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a lever for putting a believer in fear. Christ took the curse of the law at the cross, and a Christian giving under threat has misread which covenant they are standing in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read it the way it is written and the fear drops out anyway. *Test Me* is an invitation from someone confident of the outcome, addressed to people who had stopped expecting anything from Him. The passage is trying to get them to find out that He gives."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"All the nations will call you blessed.\"** The promise does not stop at full barns. It ends with a people so evidently cared for that everyone around notices — *yours will be a land of delight*. What is being described is not a transaction but a reputation."}]}, "Malachi 4:1–3": {"title": "Healing in His rays", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last page of the Old Testament, and it ends with a sunrise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sun of righteousness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But for you who fear My name, the **sun of righteousness** will rise with **healing in its rays**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *rays* is **kanaph** — wings. The same word used of the wings in Psalm 36, and the same word for the corner or hem of a garment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word behind the woman who touched the hem of His garment and was healed. The sun rising with healing in its wings, and a woman reaching for the wing of His robe."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What rises"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *a* sun. **The** sun of righteousness — righteousness itself coming up over the horizon like the morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order in the sentence is worth noticing: righteousness rises first, and healing is what is carried in its wings. The healing is not the sun. It is what the sun brings with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The calves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will go out and frolic like calves let loose from the stall.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb is to leap or skip. The picture is stall-fed calves — animals kept in and well fed — released into open ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the least solemn image in the prophets, and it is the note the Old Testament goes out on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The furnace"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The day is coming, burning like a furnace… not a root or a branch will be left.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same day is a furnace and a sunrise. Malachi does not soften either half. Which one it is depends on the phrase *for you who fear My name*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes next.** Four hundred years of silence, and then a man in the wilderness. The book's final lines promise Elijah before the great and dreadful day — and the first chapter of Luke says of John that he will go on before Him *in the spirit and power of Elijah*."}]}, "Malachi 4:4–6": {"title": "How the Old Testament ends", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last three verses before four hundred years of silence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The backward look"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Remember the law of My servant Moses**, the decrees and rulings I gave him **at Horeb for all Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew has **torat Mosheh avdi** — the law of Moses My servant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Horeb is Sinai. The Old Testament closes by pointing back to the mountain where it started."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The forward look"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"See, **I will send you the prophet Elijah** before that **great and dreadful day of the LORD** comes.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elijah, who did not die — *a chariot of fire appeared, and Elijah went up by a whirlwind into heaven*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one prophet with no grave is the one promised to return."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two adjectives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Great and dreadful.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hagadol vehanora** — great and terrible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same day is both. Malachi has already described it: *the day is coming, burning like an oven*, and in the same chapter *the Sun of Righteousness shall arise with healing in His wings*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elijah does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **he will turn the hearts of the parents to their children, and the hearts of the children to their parents**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb is **heshiv** — to turn, to bring back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a national programme, a political settlement, or a religious reform. Households."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence if he does not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So that I will not come and strike the land with total destruction**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word is **cherem** — the ban, devotion to destruction. The word used of Jericho."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last word of the Hebrew Bible's prophets is that one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole book has been about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Malachi is a series of arguments in which the people answer back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have loved you — yet you say, \"In what way have You loved us?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A son honours his father… **if then I am the Father, where is My honour?***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book opens with a father-and-son question, and it closes with fathers and children."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who this turned out to be"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will also go before Him **in the spirit and power of Elijah**, \"to turn the hearts of the fathers to the children\"… to make ready a people prepared for the Lord.* See the note on Luke 1:16–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gabriel quotes this verse to Zacharias, in the temple, about the son he is about to have."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Jesus: *if you are willing to receive it, **he is Elijah who is to come***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the silence ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred years, and then an angel standing on the right side of the altar of incense — and the first thing announced is a father getting his heart back to his child."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the New Testament does with the fatherhood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have made Your name known to the people You gave Me out of the world.* See the note on John 17:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You received the Spirit of adoption by whom we cry out, \"Abba, Father.\"* See the note on Romans 8:15–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the New Testament ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you all. Amen.* See the note on Revelation 22:20–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One canon ends with **cherem** and the other ends with **charis**."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last word.** The Old Testament closes on a threatened curse, held off by hearts turning between parents and children — and the New Testament's last sentence is grace."}]}, "Mark 1:1–3": {"title": "Here begins", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The shortest Gospel starts without a birth, a genealogy, or a childhood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Here begins **the good news of Jesus Christ, the Son of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **arche tou euangeliou** — the beginning of the gospel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Euangelion** was not a religious word. In the Roman world it announced a military victory or an imperial birthday — a proclamation from the emperor's court."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark uses it in the first line about somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Iesou Christou huiou Theou** — of Jesus Christ, the Son of God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything Mark's Gospel will spend sixteen chapters demonstrating is stated in the first sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the phrase reappears"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the baptism, from heaven: *You are My beloved Son*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the transfiguration: *this is My beloved Son*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at the cross, from a Roman soldier: *truly this Man was the Son of God*. See the note on Mark 15:37–39."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark's opening line is echoed at three points, and the last one comes from an executioner."}, {"k": "h", "t": "No beginning at the beginning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew starts with a genealogy. Luke starts with an annunciation. John starts before creation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark starts with a grown man walking out of the desert."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no infancy narrative anywhere in this Gospel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It starts **the way the prophets said it would**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark attributes it to Isaiah, and then quotes two prophets — Malachi first, then Isaiah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look — **I am sending My messenger on ahead of You**, and he will get the road ready before You come.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is Malachi 3: *behold, I send My messenger, and he will prepare the way before Me*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Malachi's line does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Malachi, the one whose road is prepared is God. *The Lord, whom you seek, will suddenly come to His temple.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark applies it to Jesus, and changes *before Me* to *before You*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**A voice is calling out in the wilderness: Clear a road for the Lord. Make the way straight for Him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 40, the chapter that opens *comfort, yes, comfort My people*. See the note on Isaiah 40:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written to exiles in Babylon about a road home across the desert."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What a road-clearing meant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "When a king travelled, work parties went ahead to level the route. The image is of an engineering crew flattening ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the voice turns out to be"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*John came baptizing in the wilderness and preaching a baptism of repentance… **clothed with camel's hair and with a leather belt around his waist**, and he ate locusts and wild honey.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The description is Elijah's, word for word from 2 Kings: *a hairy man wearing a leather belt around his waist*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Mark's pace"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And immediately* — **euthus** — appears more than forty times in sixteen chapters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Gospel moves. There are fewer discourses than in Matthew and more incidents, and the whole thing can be read aloud in an hour and a half."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But who do you say that I am?\" Peter answered and said to Him, \"You are the Christ.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The exact midpoint of the book, and it is the reader being asked the question the first verse already answered. See the note on Mark 8:31–33."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they went out quickly and fled from the tomb, for they trembled and were amazed. **And they said nothing to anyone, for they were afraid**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oldest manuscripts end there, mid-fear, on a Greek conjunction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Gospel that opens *here begins* closes without a proper ending."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Mark leaves out.** A birth, a genealogy, a childhood, and — in the oldest manuscripts — a conclusion. The first sentence states the claim and the rest is the evidence."}]}, "Mark 1:16–20": {"title": "Four fishermen by the sea", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Mark's account of the first four, and it takes five verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He saw Simon and his brother Andrew **casting a net into the water**, because **fishing was their living**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark adds the clause. They are not out for the afternoon; it is the family trade."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a circular casting-net, thrown by hand from the shallows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The call"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Come with Me**,\" Jesus said to them, \"and **I will make you fishers of people**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *come after Me*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will make you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is His. He does not tell them to become anything; He says what He will do to them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes the thing they already are and says the object will change."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **dropped their nets right there** and went with Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **euthus** — immediately, at once. Mark uses it more than forty times in sixteen chapters; it is the pulse of his Gospel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A little farther on He saw **James son of Zebedee and his brother John, sitting in their boat putting their nets back in order**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *putting in order* is **katartizontas** — mending, repairing, setting right."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same word Paul uses for restoring a fallen brother, and Peter uses of what God does after suffering: *will put you back together*. See the note on 1 Peter 5:10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they left"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And right away they **left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men** and walked away after Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark mentions the hired men, which tells you the business was large enough to employ staff. This is not destitution being walked away from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it mentions the father, sitting in the boat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John's Gospel adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John 1 has an earlier meeting — Andrew hears the Baptist say *behold the Lamb of God*, follows, spends a day with Jesus, and goes to find his brother: *we have found the Messiah.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "So this is not the first time they had met. It is the day the trade ended."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they went back to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "After the resurrection, in John 21: *Simon Peter said to them, \"I am going fishing.\" They said to him, \"We are going with you also.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They fish all night and catch nothing, and a man on the shore tells them which side to cast on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then a charcoal fire, breakfast, and three questions for Peter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the two pairs end up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter, James and John become the three taken into Jairus's house, up the mountain, and into Gethsemane."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James is the first of the twelve to be killed, in Acts 12, by Herod's sword. John is traditionally the last to die."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mark records next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same day: the synagogue at Capernaum, the unclean spirit, Peter's mother-in-law, and the whole town at the door after sunset."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark 1 does not slow down."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He says He will do.** *I will make you.* They are asked to come, and the making is stated as His work — and Mark says the nets went down immediately."}]}, "Mark 1:9–11": {"title": "The voice from heaven", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses, and the Trinity is in all three of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus came from **Nazareth in Galilee**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark has no birth narrative and no genealogy. The first thing he says about Jesus' origins is a village nobody rated — *can anything good come out of Nazareth?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty years, summarised as a place name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The baptism"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **John baptized Him** in the Jordan.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark does not record the objection Matthew includes — *I need to be baptized by You, and are You coming to me?* — or the answer, *permit it to be so now, for thus it is fitting for us to fulfil all righteousness*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's baptism was *for the remission of sins*, and the one man in the queue with none goes into the water anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The moment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the moment He came up out of the water**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark's favourite word is **euthus** — immediately. He uses it more than forty times, eleven in this first chapter alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tearing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He saw the heavens **split wide open**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **schizomenous** — torn, rent. Matthew and Luke have the milder *opened*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark uses this verb exactly twice. The other is at the crucifixion: *and the veil of the temple was **torn** in two from top to bottom.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heavens at the start, the veil at the end, and the same word for both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah had asked for it: *Oh, that You would rend the heavens! That You would come down!*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dove"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the **Spirit coming down on Him like a dove settling**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *as a dove descending upon Him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A dove appears once before in a story about water receding, carrying an olive leaf."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The voice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And a voice came out of heaven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three parties present, doing three different things: the Son in the river, the Spirit descending, the Father speaking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You are My Son, the One I love. My delight rests on You**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark's version is second person — *You are*. Matthew's is third — *this is My beloved Son*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark has it said to Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ho agapetos** — the beloved. And **eudokesa** — I am well pleased, an aorist: the pleasure is settled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He had done at this point"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing recorded. No sermon, no miracle, no disciple called."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The delight is announced before the ministry starts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two Old Testament sentences behind it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are My Son* — Psalm 2, the coronation psalm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In whom My soul delights* — Isaiah 42, the first Servant Song, which continues *I have put My Spirit upon Him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "King and Servant, in one sentence, over a man standing in a river."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Immediately the Spirit **drove Him into the wilderness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ekballei** — the word for casting out demons. Mark's verb is violent where Matthew's is *led up*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty days, and the tempter's opening line is *if You are the Son of God*. See the note on Matthew 4:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The words tested are the ones just spoken from the sky, and the word not repeated is *beloved*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the voice comes back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On the mountain of transfiguration: *this is My beloved Son. **Hear Him!***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice in the Gospels the Father speaks about the Son, and both times the middle word is the same."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Mark tore.** The heavens here and the veil at the cross — one verb, used twice in the whole Gospel, at the two ends of it."}]}, "Mark 10:17–22": {"title": "\"He looked at him with love\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only person in the Gospels who comes to Jesus and leaves worse off, and Mark records a detail the others do not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How he arrives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man came **running up** and **dropped to his knees** in front of Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Running and kneeling. Mark alone has both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about his approach is grudging. He is eager, public, and respectful."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Good Teacher, **what must I do** to inherit eternal life?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question has a verb in it that decides the whole conversation: **do**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An inheritance is not something you do anything for. He asks how to earn one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Why do you call Me good? **No one is good except God alone.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a denial. A question about what the man meant by the word he used."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not commit adultery, do not murder, do not steal, do not lie about anyone, **do not cheat anyone**, honour your father and your mother.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six commandments, and only from the second table — the ones about other people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not cheat anyone* is not in the ten. It is closer to *do not covet*, and Mark alone has it in this form. Coveting is the one commandment nobody can verify from outside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have kept every one of these since I was a boy.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not being flippant. Externally, it may well have been true."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line only Mark has"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Jesus **looked straight at him, and He loved him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew and Luke both tell this story and neither includes it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever follows is not a rebuke. Mark tells you the expression on His face first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**There is one thing you are missing.** Go and sell everything you own, and give the money to the poor… **Then come, take up the cross, and follow Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks for what the man will not give — and there is no evidence He asks anyone else in the Gospels for this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zacchaeus, a chapter later in Luke, gives away half voluntarily and is not asked for anything. See the note on Luke 19:1–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At those words **the man's face fell**, and he walked away with a heavy heart, **because he owned a great deal**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *face fell* is used elsewhere of the sky darkening before a storm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He came running and went away slowly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus says afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"How hard it is for those who have riches to enter the kingdom of God!\" And the disciples were astonished.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were astonished because wealth was read as a sign of blessing. When they ask *who then can be saved?*, the answer is the point of the whole passage:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**With men it is impossible, but not with God; for with God all things are possible.**\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he asked for and what he was offered.** He asked what to *do* to inherit. He was offered *treasure in heaven* and *come, follow Me* — and both are things you receive. The only sentence in the exchange with a *do* in it was his."}]}, "Mark 10:23–27": {"title": "Impossible for us", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He says something hard, they are shocked, and He says it again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man ran up and knelt: *good Teacher, what shall I do that I may inherit eternal life?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Jesus, looking at him, **loved him***, and said *one thing you lack: go your way, sell whatever you have and give to the poor*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But he was sad at this word, and went away sorrowful, **for he had great possessions**.* See the note on Mark 10:17–22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark alone records the look and the love."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The look"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus **looked around at His disciples** and said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **periblepsamenos** — looked all round."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark uses this verb of Jesus five times. He watches the room before He speaks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first statement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How hard it is for people with riches to enter the kingdom of God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **duskolos** — with difficulty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were **stunned** to hear it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ethambounto** — amazed, astounded."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In their world, wealth was evidence of God's favour. Deuteronomy could be read that way. A rich man was assumed to be the likeliest candidate, not the least."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus has just inverted the odds."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repetition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **He said it again**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not soften it or explain it away. Mark records that He restated it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Children**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tekna**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses it once in Mark, here, immediately after saying something that has frightened them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second statement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How hard it is for people who lean on their money** to get into the kingdom of God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek here in most manuscripts is **tous pepoithotas epi chremasin** — those who have put their trust in riches."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second statement is the first one with the mechanism named. Not the having. The leaning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle** than for a rich man to enter the kingdom of God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The largest animal in Palestine, and the smallest hole anybody handled daily."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is a medieval suggestion that a gate in Jerusalem was called the Needle's Eye. There is no evidence such a gate existed, and the disciples' reaction shows they heard it as impossible, not as difficult."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That **astonished them even more**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **perissos exeplessonto** — exceedingly struck out of themselves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Then who can possibly be saved?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the right conclusion. If the man with every advantage cannot, nobody can."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus **looked right at them** and said, \"**For people it is impossible. But not for God. With God, everything is possible**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **emblepsas autois** — looking straight at them. The same verb Mark used of the look He gave the rich man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the answer does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not lower the bar. It grants the impossibility and moves the agent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Para anthropois adunaton, all' ou para Theo* — with men impossible, but not with God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"See, **we have left all and followed You**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer: *there is no one who has left house or brothers or sisters or father or mother or wife or children or lands, for My sake and the gospel's, **who shall not receive a hundredfold now in this time** — houses and brothers and sisters and mothers and children and lands, **with persecutions** — and in the age to come, eternal life.* See the note on Mark 10:28–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list of what is received leaves out one item that was in the list of what was left: a father."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *with persecutions* is inside the promise, not after it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But many who are first will be last, and the last first.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The second saying.** The first names the category, the second names the mechanism: *those who lean on their money*. And the disciples' answer to both is *then who can be saved?*"}]}, "Mark 10:28–31": {"title": "A hundred times as much", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter states the obvious out loud and gets an answer with an unwelcome word in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rich young ruler. *One thing you lack: go your way, sell whatever you have and give to the poor… and come, take up the cross, and follow Me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But he was sad at this word, and went away sorrowful, for he had great possessions.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Mark's note about the look on Jesus' face beforehand: *then Jesus, looking at him, loved him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the camel and the needle's eye, and the disciples *greatly astonished*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The observation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look at us — **we have left everything behind and followed You**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter, and it is true. Boats, nets, a business, a father, and a house in Capernaum."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not ask a question. Matthew's version adds one: *therefore what shall we have?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There is **no one who has left home or brothers or sisters or mother or father or wife or children or fields** for My sake and for the sake of the good news.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven things listed, and the list is deliberately domestic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The return"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Who will not be given a hundred times as much in this present life**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hekatontaplasiona** — a hundredfold."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**In this present life** — the Greek is *now in this time*. Not deferred."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is returned"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Homes and brothers and sisters and mothers and children and fields.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same list, minus one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Father** is on the leaving list and not on the receiving list."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whether that is deliberate has been argued over for centuries. The obvious reading is that there is only one of those: *do not call anyone on earth your father, for One is your Father, He who is in heaven*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause nobody quotes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **persecutions right along with them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meta diogmon**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is in the middle of the promise, between the fields and the eternal life, and Matthew and Luke both leave it out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark keeps it, and Mark is writing for a church under Nero."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**And in the age to come, eternal life**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two timeframes: now, with persecutions, and then."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the hundredfold looks like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not property. The disciples did not end up with a hundred farms."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They ended up in a movement where houses were opened to them everywhere they went — Lydia's, the jailer's, Priscilla and Aquila's, Philip's, Mnason's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And all who believed were together, and had all things in common… and they broke bread from house to house.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list Jesus gives back is a list of relationships, and Acts is the account of them arriving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **many who are first will be last, and the last will be first**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it here, and again at the end of the vineyard parable in Matthew, and again after the narrow door in Luke."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Placed immediately after a rich man walks away and a fisherman is promised a hundredfold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third passion prediction, and then James and John asking for the seats on His right and left."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You do not know what you ask. **Are you able to drink the cup that I drink?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's *we have left everything* and the brothers' request for the best seats are eight verses apart."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word in the middle of the promise.** *And persecutions.* Mark leaves it in; Matthew and Luke do not — and Mark was writing for people it was happening to."}]}, "Mark 10:41–45": {"title": "A ransom for many", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Ten men are furious with two, and He uses the argument to say why He came."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What started it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "James and John had asked for the seats on His right and left in glory."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They asked it immediately after the third and most detailed prediction of the crucifixion. Mark puts the request straight after *they will mock Him, and scourge Him, and spit on Him, and kill Him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The indignation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the other ten heard about this, **they were indignant**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not because the request was wrong. Because two of them got in first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the world works"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You know how it works among the nations. Their rulers **lord it over** people, and the important ones **make sure everyone feels their weight**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two Greek verbs, both compounds built on *down* — to dominate from above, to exercise authority against."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He describes it accurately and without heat. That is simply how power operates."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"It is not to be like that among you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is blunt: *but not so is it among you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not abolish greatness. The next sentence assumes people will want it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inversion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whoever wants to be **great** among you must be **the one who serves you**, and whoever wants to be **first** must be **the servant of all**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words, and the second is stronger. **Diakonos** is a servant, a waiter at a table. **Doulos** is a slave — owned, with no standing at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Great → servant. First → slave of everyone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For even the Son of Man **did not come to be served, but to serve**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not argue the principle. He gives Himself as the case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ransom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **to give His life a ransom for many**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **lutron** — the price paid to free a slave or a prisoner of war. It appears twice in the New Testament, here and in Matthew's parallel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the preposition is **anti** — in place of, instead of. Not *on behalf of* but *in the position of*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job's friend Elihu had said it in his own words: *deliver him from going down to the pit; **I have found a ransom**.* See the note on Job 33:23–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mark's whole Gospel is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The shortest and fastest of the four, with *immediately* used more than forty times, and no genealogy or birth narrative — a book about a servant, and servants do not need pedigrees."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This verse is generally taken as its key sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He was going when He said it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Up the road to Jerusalem, six days out. Bartimaeus is in the next paragraph, and the triumphal entry in the next chapter. See the note on Mark 10:46–52."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the ten and the two had in common.** All twelve wanted position. The difference between James and John and the other ten is that two of them asked out loud."}]}, "Mark 10:46–52": {"title": "Bartimaeus throws off his cloak", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last healing Mark records before the triumphal entry, and the man is named."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A blind beggar named **Bartimaeus, the son of Timaeus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark gives the name and then translates it — *bar Timaeus* means son of Timaeus, so he says the same thing twice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Most people Jesus heals in the Gospels are anonymous. This one is not, which readers have long taken to mean he was known in the early church afterwards."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he shouts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Jesus, Son of David, have mercy on me!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Son of David** is a messianic title, and this is the first time in Mark anybody uses it out loud."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A blind beggar on a roadside has worked out what the disciples are still arguing about — two paragraphs earlier James and John were asking for the best seats."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crowd"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Plenty of people told him **sharply to be quiet**, but he **only shouted louder**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is that they rebuked him — the same verb used of Jesus rebuking demons and the storm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What stops Jesus"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus **stopped** and said, \"Call him over.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One word. The whole procession into Jerusalem for the last week of His life halts on the shout of a man nobody wanted to hear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the crowd says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take heart. Get up. He is calling you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same people who were telling him to shut up are now helping him to his feet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cloak"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **threw off his cloak**, jumped to his feet, and came to Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A beggar's cloak was his licence and his livelihood — spread on the ground in front of him to catch coins, and his blanket at night. For a blind man it was also the one thing he could find again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He throws it away before he can see, on the strength of a summons."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What do you want Me to do for you?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The identical question He had asked James and John a few verses earlier. They wanted thrones."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Rabbi, **I want to see**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go. **Your faith has made you well**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *your faith has saved you* — **sozo**, the wide word covering healing, rescue and salvation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with the *go*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And immediately he could see, and **he followed Him down the road**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is told to go, and follows instead — down the road that leads to Jerusalem, and to the cross, six days away."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where this sits in Mark.** The disciples have just failed to understand the third passion prediction and asked for positions. Then a blind man calls Him the Son of David, asks for one thing, and follows Him to Jerusalem. Mark puts the sighted disciples and the blind beggar side by side on purpose."}]}, "Mark 11:12–14": {"title": "A tree with only leaves", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only destructive miracle in the Gospels, and Mark puts it either side of the temple."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The next day, as they were leaving Bethany.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The day after the entry into Jerusalem — the tenth of Nisan, with the cloaks on the road. See the note on Luke 19:36–40."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had gone into the temple, *looked round at all things*, and left because it was late."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hunger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus was hungry.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark says it plainly, and it is one of the details he keeps that the others drop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tree"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He noticed a fig tree **in the distance, full of leaves**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A fig tree in leaf is making a claim. On that species the small early fruit — the **paggim** — appears before or with the leaves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A tree covered in foliage in early spring is advertising fruit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inspection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He went over **to see whether there was any fruit on it**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He found"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When He reached it He found **nothing but leaves**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **it was not the season for figs**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark writes it and leaves it standing, which is why the incident is so often called unfair."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the key to the story. The tree was not expected to have a ripe crop. It was in full leaf out of season, which meant it should have had the early fruit — and it had neither."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The display was the offence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He said to the tree, \"**May no one ever eat fruit from you again**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Spoken to a tree, in the hearing of the disciples. Mark notes that: *and His disciples heard Him say it*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Walks into Jerusalem and clears the temple."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Is it not written, '**My house shall be called a house of prayer for all nations**'? But you have made it a 'den of thieves.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The building was in full leaf. Courts, priests, sacrifices, money changing, and no fruit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sandwich"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark does this repeatedly — starts a story, interrupts it, and finishes it. Jairus and the woman with the haemorrhage is the same shape."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here: the tree, then the temple, then the tree again. Each explains the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The morning after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now in the morning, as they passed by, they saw **the fig tree dried up from the roots**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Peter: *\"Rabbi, look! The fig tree which You cursed has withered away.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer is about mountains and prayer and forgiving. See the note on Mark 11:20–26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the fig tree stood for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hosea: *I found Israel like grapes in the wilderness; I saw your fathers as the firstfruits on the fig tree in its first season*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah: *there are no grapes on the vine, nor figs on the fig tree, and the leaf shall fade*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Micah: *there is no cluster to eat of the early-ripe fruit which my soul desires*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parable He had already told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"A certain man had a fig tree planted in his vineyard, and he came seeking fruit on it and found none… **cut it down; why does it use up the ground?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the vinedresser: *let it alone this year also, until I dig around it and fertilise it. And if it bears fruit, well. But if not, after that you can cut it down.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three years of looking, and then one more."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the fruitless thing had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything except fruit. Mark's account is precise about it: leaves visible from a distance, and nothing underneath."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clause everyone stumbles on.** *It was not the season for figs.* Which is the point — a tree in full leaf out of season was advertising a crop it did not have."}]}, "Mark 11:20–26": {"title": "\"Believe that you have received it\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter points at a withered tree, and Jesus answers with three sentences about prayer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Have faith in God\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first words, and they set the direction. The Greek can be rendered *have the faith of God* — faith that has God as its object and its source."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything after this hangs on where the faith is placed, not on how much of it there is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tense that carries the promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever you ask for in prayer, **believe that you have received it**, and it will be yours.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is aorist — believe you *received*, past. Not that you will receive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sequence is: ask, take it as given, and then it becomes yours in experience. That is an odd order by every ordinary measure, and it is what the sentence says."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If anyone says to this mountain, \"Be lifted up and thrown into the sea.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were walking from Bethany to Jerusalem, over the Mount of Olives, with the Dead Sea visible in the distance. **This** mountain, and that sea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And notice the verb: *says to*. Twice. The faith in the illustration is exercised by speaking to the obstacle, not by talking to God about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Does not doubt in his heart\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word rendered *doubt* is **diakrino** — to separate, to divide, to make a distinction between two things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So doubting, in this sentence, is not weak feeling. It is being divided in yourself — one thing held in one hand and its opposite in the other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the two things being divided are named in the passage itself. Peter is astonished at a dried-up fig tree; Jesus answers by talking about a mountain going into the sea. A small withering and a mountain moved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who separates those — who has faith for the tree and none for the mountain — will not see either. That is what the verb rules out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same word, at the table"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses **diakrino** twice in 1 Corinthians 11: *not **discerning** the Lord's body*, and *if we **judged** ourselves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things are handed over at that table, the body and the blood, and dividing them is the failure Paul names. It is the same verb, doing the same work: a man divided between two things God gave together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the notes on 1 Corinthians 11:27–30 and 1 Corinthians 11:31–34."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition He attaches"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And whenever you stand praying, if you are holding something against anyone, forgive it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus attaches one thing to the whole passage, and it is not effort or technique. It is forgiveness — and He makes it the immediate neighbour of the promise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever unanswered prayer is about, He points at the person you have not forgiven before He points anywhere else."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the fig tree is there.** The withering brackets Mark's account of Jesus clearing the temple — the tree is cursed on the way in and found dead on the way out, with the temple scene in between. Mark's sandwich construction is deliberate: a tree in leaf with no fruit, and a house of prayer with no prayer in it."}]}, "Mark 12:13–17": {"title": "Whose face is on the coin", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A trap with two jaws, and the answer walks between them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is sent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Later they sent some of **the Pharisees** and some of **Herod's people** to Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two groups who agreed on almost nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Pharisees resented Roman rule and the tax that funded it. The Herodians were the party of a client dynasty that existed by Roman permission."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have been put on the same errand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hoping **to catch Him in something He said**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **agreusosin** — to catch as in hunting, to snare."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The flattery"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Teacher, **we know that You are honest and that You are not swayed by anyone**, because **You take no notice of who a person is**. You teach the way of God as it truly is.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek idiom is **ou blepeis eis prosopon** — you do not look at the face of men."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every word of it is true. That is what makes it a trap: they are closing off the escape of evasion before they ask."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So tell us: **is it right to pay the tax to Caesar, or not? Should we pay it, or should we not?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kensos** — the Latin *census*, the poll tax. One denarius a year, levied on every adult in Judea since AD 6, payable only in Roman coin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tax that had provoked the revolt of Judas of Galilee."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two jaws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Say yes, and He loses the crowd — He has endorsed the occupation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Say no, and the Herodians have a sentence to carry to Pilate. Which is exactly what they later claim He said: *we found this fellow… forbidding to pay taxes to Caesar*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He sees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus **saw straight through the act**. \"**Why are you testing Me?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hupokrisin** — their play-acting. The theatre word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Bring Me a denarius and let Me look at it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not have one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They produce one immediately, in the temple, which is its own small answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The coin"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A silver denarius of Tiberius, with his head in profile and the inscription **TI CAESAR DIVI AVG F AVGVSTVS** — Tiberius Caesar, son of the divine Augustus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A graven image and a claim to divine parentage, in the pocket of men asking a question about religious purity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whose face is on this, and whose name?**\" \"**Caesar's**,\" they said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eikon** — image. The word in the Septuagint at Genesis 1:27: *God created man in His own **image***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Give Caesar back what belongs to Caesar, and give God what belongs to God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apodote** — give back, render, return what is owed. Not *give*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The coin carries Caesar's image, so it is his; hand it back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what carries God's image?"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say. He has just used the word **eikon** of a coin and left the audience to finish the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they were **left marveling at Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exethaumazon** — an intensified form. Utterly amazed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in the passage says another word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He pays elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Nevertheless, lest we offend them, go to the sea, cast in a hook… and when you have opened its mouth, you will find a piece of money.\"* See the note on Matthew 17:24–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The temple tax, paid out of a fish, by someone who had just established He was exempt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the same coins"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Render therefore to all their due: **taxes to whom taxes are due**.* See the note on Romans 13:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb, **apodote**, and Paul writing to Christians in Rome about Rome."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word.** **Eikon** — image. He asks whose image is on the coin, gets the answer, and then names a second category without saying what carries God's."}]}, "Mark 12:28–34": {"title": "Not far from the kingdom", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The one scribe in the Gospels who asks a question in good faith."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has been happening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A run of hostile questions in the temple, in the last week. The chief priests on authority. The Pharisees and Herodians on the tax. The Sadducees on the woman with seven husbands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each one is a trap, and each one fails."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he noticed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One of the teachers of the law had been **listening to them argue**, and he **saw how well Jesus answered**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kalos** — well, finely."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is impressed by an opponent, which is where the whole exchange starts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Of all the commandments, which is the most important?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rabbis counted 613 in the law and debated which were weighty and which light. It is a standard question, and Matthew's version says it was asked *testing Him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark's does not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The first is this: '**Hear, O Israel — the Lord our God, the Lord is one**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He includes the Shema's opening line, which nobody else in the New Testament quotes. See the note on Deuteronomy 6:4–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question was about a commandment. He starts with a statement of fact."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**With all your heart** and with **all your soul** and with **all your mind** and with **all your strength**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy has three — heart, soul, might. The Greek adds **dianoia**, mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And the second is this: '**Love your neighbor as yourself**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus 19, a single clause in the middle of a chapter about not gleaning your field to the edge and not defrauding a hired hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was asked for one and gives two, and joins them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**There is no commandment greater than these two**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scribe's reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Well said, Teacher. You have spoken the truth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He agrees with the answer, and then goes further than it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He is one, and there is no other besides Him. And to love Him with all your heart… and to love your neighbor as yourself — **that is worth more than all the burnt offerings and sacrifices put together**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **perissoteron** — more, in excess of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He said that in the temple, standing among the sacrifices, as a professional handler of the law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is quoting the prophets: *to obey is better than sacrifice*, and *I desire mercy and not sacrifice*, and Micah's *what does the LORD require of you but to do justly, to love mercy, and to walk humbly with your God*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Jesus heard **how thoughtfully he had answered**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **nounechos** — sensibly, with understanding. It occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He said to him, \"**You are not far from the kingdom of God**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that sentence is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a welcome, and not a rejection. A measurement."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man has said something true and is being told he is close. Mark does not record what he did next."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And after that **no one dared to ask Him anything more**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The run of questions ends here — not with a trap sprung, but with a man agreeing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus asks next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"How is it that the scribes say that the Christ is the Son of David?\"* — and He quotes Psalm 110: *the LORD said to my Lord, \"Sit at My right hand.\"* See the note on Psalms 110:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"David himself calls Him 'Lord'; how is He then his Son?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the note that follows: *and the common people heard Him gladly*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the scribe did.** Agreed, and then said that loving God and neighbour outweighed the whole sacrificial system — standing in the temple, as a lawyer. And was told he was close."}]}, "Mark 12:35–37": {"title": "\"David himself calls Him Lord\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He asks the question Himself, in public, and nobody answers it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While Jesus was **teaching in the temple courts**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Passion week. He has been questioned by four delegations in two days and each has gone away with nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Now He asks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How is it that the teachers of the law say the Messiah is the son of David?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a denial. Every Jew held it, and it is true — Matthew opens his Gospel with the genealogy proving it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is asking why they stop there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"David himself, **speaking by the Holy Spirit**, said: '**The Lord said to my Lord**: sit at My right hand until I make Your enemies a footstool under Your feet.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 110:1, the most-quoted Old Testament verse in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two different words. The first **Lord** is YHWH. The second is **Adonai** — my Lord."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**David himself calls Him Lord — so how can He be David's son?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that culture the honour ran upward. A father does not address his own descendant as *my Lord*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Unless the descendant existed before him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who He credits with the psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Speaking by the Holy Spirit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He attributes the authorship in the middle of the argument, and the whole case depends on the wording being exact."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sitting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Sit at My right hand until** I make Your enemies a footstool.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews builds a chapter on that posture: every priest *stands ministering daily*, and this one *sat down*. See the note on Hebrews 10:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sitting is not idleness. It is what you do when the work is finished, and the subduing that follows is somebody else's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who answered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody. Matthew's parallel adds: *no one was able to answer Him a word, nor from that day on did anyone dare question Him anymore.* See the note on Matthew 22:41–46."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the great crowd listened to Him with delight**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark alone records this. The Greek is that they heard Him gladly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scholars were silenced and the crowd enjoyed it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Beware of the scribes, who **desire to go around in long robes**, love greetings in the marketplaces, the best seats in the synagogues… **who devour widows' houses**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then He sits down opposite the treasury and watches a widow put in two mites."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Psalm 110 says next.** *The LORD has sworn and will not relent: You are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek.* A king on David's throne who is also a priest of a line older than Aaron's — and Hebrews takes two chapters over it. See the note on Genesis 14:17–20."}]}, "Mark 14:12–16": {"title": "The man carrying a jar of water", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two disciples are sent into a crowded city with no address, and told to follow a man they have never seen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the first day of Unleavened Bread, **the day the Passover lamb was killed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jerusalem at Passover held a population many times its usual size. Every household needed a room, and rooms had been spoken for weeks earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Where do You want us to go and **get the Passover ready** for You?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are asking a practical question. He answers with a sign."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sign"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**A man carrying a jar of water** will meet you. **Follow him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Water-carrying was women's work in that culture. A man with a water jar on his shoulder was unusual enough to pick out of a festival crowd without a description, without a name, and without a street."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the two are given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the location. A person to follow, and the next instruction only when they arrive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are told what to say at the door, and nothing about what happens after."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The words at the door"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The Teacher asks: **where is the guest room** where I may eat the Passover with My disciples?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The Teacher** — no name. The householder knows who is meant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *guest room* is **kataluma**, the same word Luke uses for the inn with no room in it at the beginning. The first *kataluma* had no room for Him. This one is kept ready."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He will show you a **large room upstairs, already furnished and ready**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **estromenon** — spread, laid out, with the couches already in place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing had to be arranged. It was furnished before the two disciples set out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they found"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The two of them went into the city and found it **exactly as He had told them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark says it as flatly as that. Every detail landed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He is doing in the same week"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judas has already been to the chief priests. The arrangement of this room keeps the supper's location out of Judas's hands until they are in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens in that room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bread and the cup, the announcement of the betrayer, the new covenant in His blood, and the walk out to the Mount of Olives singing a hymn. See the note on Mark 14:22–26."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other errand like it.** Six days before, two disciples were sent for a colt nobody had ridden, told what they would find and what to say to the owner — *the Lord has need of it* — and found it exactly so. See the note on Mark 11:1–6."}]}, "Mark 14:22–26": {"title": "The bread and the cup", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Mark's account of the table is the shortest, and the order of the sentences carries something the other accounts do not put as plainly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order in Mark"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He took a cup, gave thanks, and passed it to them, **and every one of them drank from it**. \"This is My blood,\" He said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They drank first. The explanation comes after the cup is empty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark is the only one who reports it in that sequence. In Matthew the words come with the cup; here the drinking is a completed act, and then Jesus tells them what they have just done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Until it went into them, it was wine at a Passover table like any other. What made it His blood was not a formula said over the cup. It was His word, and the disciples had already swallowed it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Torn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He picked up the bread. He gave thanks over it, **tore** it, and put it into their hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is to break, and the Illumination renders it *tore* — the tearing of a flat loaf by hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 53 says *He was wounded for our transgressions, He was bruised for our iniquities.* The bread is broken before it is given, and the giving is hand-to-hand: *put it into their hands.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two things named, and what each is for"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the body** — *this is My body*", "**the blood** — *the blood of the new covenant, poured out for many*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blood is given a stated purpose in the text: the covenant, and in Matthew's account, *for the forgiveness of sins.* The body is given without a stated purpose here — and Matthew 8:17 supplies it: *He took our infirmities and carried our diseases.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two elements, two provisions. Paul's warning in 1 Corinthians 11 about *not discerning the body* turns on holding them together. See the note on 1 Corinthians 11:27–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Every one of them drank\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark records the participation. Judas is at that table in Mark's account — the betrayal has already been announced in the paragraph above."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last supper is not the last"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will not drink of the fruit of the vine again until the day I drink it new in the kingdom of God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it while the cup is still in His hand. The meal is left unfinished on purpose, with a date set for the rest of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they did next.** *Then they sang together, and went out to the Mount of Olives.* At Passover the closing hymn was the Hallel, Psalms 115–118 — which includes *I shall not die but live, and declare the works of the LORD*, and *the stone the builders rejected has become the cornerstone.* He sang that on the way to Gethsemane."}]}, "Mark 14:27–31": {"title": "\"Even if all of them fall away, I will not\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Peter's most famous sentence before the courtyard, and what is striking is what he skips over to say it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise he walks past"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus says two things. First, *every one of you is going to fall away tonight* — with a quotation from Zechariah about the shepherd struck and the sheep scattered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *but after I am raised, I will go on ahead of you into Galilee.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That second sentence is a plan for after the failure. Before anyone has denied anything, a meeting place has been arranged. Peter answers the first sentence and says nothing about the second."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What his answer reveals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even if all of them fall away, I will not.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is the tell. Peter's confidence is measured against the other eleven, in front of the other eleven. And when Jesus tells him precisely how far he will go, he says it *again, harder*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark, whose Gospel the early church associated with Peter's own preaching, keeps all of it. Including the last line: *and every one of them said the same thing.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail Mark alone records"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before the rooster has crowed twice.* Only Mark has the two crowings, and only Mark later notes the first one, which Peter did not hear or did not heed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the kind of detail a man remembers about the worst night of his life."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What happened to the arrangement.** At the empty tomb the messenger says: go and tell the disciples *and Peter* that He is going ahead of you into Galilee. The plan made before the denial stands after it, and Peter's name is added to the message."}]}, "Mark 15:33–36": {"title": "Darkness at midday", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three hours of dark, and one sentence out of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At **the sixth hour, at midday**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Noon. The sun at its highest point in the sky."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark has been keeping time all through the chapter — the third hour when they crucified Him, the sixth, the ninth. He builds the day out of clock readings."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The darkness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Darkness came over **the whole land**, and it **stayed until the ninth hour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three hours. Noon to three."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an eclipse; Passover falls at full moon, when a solar eclipse is impossible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When the lambs were killed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the ninth hour and evening, at the temple, thousands of Passover lambs were being slaughtered — a few hundred yards from the hill."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What darkness had meant before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ninth plague in Egypt: *darkness which may even be felt*, three days of it, over Egypt only — and *all the children of Israel had light in their dwellings*. The plague immediately before the Passover."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Amos, on the day of the LORD: *I will make the sun go down at noon, and I will darken the earth in broad daylight. I will turn your feasts into mourning.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And at the ninth hour Jesus **cried out with a loud voice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eboesen phone megale** — the shout of a strong man. Mark has recorded almost nothing from the cross before this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Eloi, Eloi, lama sabachthani?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aramaic, and Mark keeps the sound before he translates it. He does this a handful of times — *Talitha koum*, *Ephphatha*, *Abba*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The translation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My God, My God, why have You abandoned Me?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **egkatelipes** — left behind, forsaken, abandoned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the opening line of Psalm 22, and the psalm goes on: *they pierced My hands and My feet*, *they divide My garments among them, and for My clothing they cast lots*. See the note on Psalms 22:16–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both of those had happened by the time He said the first line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have answered Me… I will declare Your name to My brethren.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the last words of it in Hebrew: *ki asah* — *He has done it*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The misunderstanding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Some of the men standing there heard it and said, \"**He is calling for Elijah**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Eloi* and *Eliyahu*. Close enough at a distance, in a crowd, from a man who had been scourged."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elijah was expected to come before the day of the LORD — Malachi's last sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sponge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One of them ran and **soaked a sponge in sour wine**, put it on a stick, and lifted it up to His mouth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Oxos** — the cheap sour wine soldiers drank."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Earlier they had offered wine mixed with myrrh, a painkiller, and He refused it. This one He takes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The waiting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Leave Him**,\" he said. \"**Let us see whether Elijah is coming to take Him down**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last recorded human speech before He dies is a man proposing to watch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Jesus cried out with a loud voice, and breathed His last. Then **the veil of the temple was torn in two from top to bottom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the centurion — the man who ran the execution — *truly this Man was the Son of God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark's Gospel opens with *the beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God*, and the first human being in it to say so is the officer in charge of killing Him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He asked.** Not for it to stop. *Why* — and He asked it in Aramaic, in the first line of a psalm that ends with the words *He has done it*."}]}, "Mark 15:42–47": {"title": "Joseph of Arimathea", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The disciples have gone. A member of the council that condemned Him comes forward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **respected member of the council**, a man who was himself **waiting for the kingdom of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The council is the Sanhedrin — the body that had tried Jesus that same morning. Luke adds that he *had not consented to their decision and deed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew says he was rich. John says he was a disciple *secretly, for fear of the Jews*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"It took courage to walk in there\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark says it outright, and Mark is the shortest Gospel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes to Pilate — a Roman governor who has just executed a man for sedition — and asks for the body of the condemned. There is no way to do that anonymously."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything he had spent a career protecting goes in that request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Pilate's surprise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Pilate was **surprised to hear that Jesus was already dead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Crucifixion normally took days. Pilate sends for the centurion and checks."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark alone records this exchange, and it settles the question of whether He was actually dead: the officer who ran the execution confirmed it to the governor before the body was released."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Joseph did with his money"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph **bought** a length of linen, took Him down, wrapped Him in the cloth, and laid Him in a tomb that had been **cut out of the rock**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He paid for the linen. Matthew says the tomb was *his own new tomb, which he had hewn out of the rock*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A wealthy man's grave, bought and cut for himself, given away on a Friday afternoon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah had said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they made His grave with the wicked — but with the rich at His death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves. He was executed between two criminals and buried in a rich man's tomb, and Isaiah put the two clauses side by side seven hundred years earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "John's addition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nicodemus came with him — the other secret one, the man who had come by night in chapter 3 — carrying *a mixture of myrrh and aloes, about a hundred pounds*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a royal burial quantity, and an enormous expense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two members of the Sanhedrin who would not declare themselves while He was alive are the two men handling His body."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The women watching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mary Magdalene and Mary the mother of Joses were watching, and **they saw where He was laid**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark notes it because it matters two days later. They knew which tomb."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was in the tomb by Sunday.** The linen lying there, and the cloth that had been around His head *folded together in a place by itself.* John looked in and reports the arrangement of the cloth before he reports anything else."}]}, "Mark 16:1–4": {"title": "The stone already rolled back", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "They set out to finish a burial and arrive at an open door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mary Magdalene and Mary the mother of James and Salome.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three named women. Mark named the first two at the crucifixion and at the burial as well: *and Mary Magdalene and Mary the mother of Joses observed where He was laid.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They knew which tomb because they had watched."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they bought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Spices so that they could **go and anoint Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **aromata**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bought after the Sabbath ended at sunset, which means the shops opened Saturday evening and they went straight there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nicodemus had already brought a hundred pounds of myrrh and aloes. They are going anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Very early on the first day of the week, **just as the sun was coming up**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark says *very early* and then *when the sun had risen*, which reads like a contradiction and is the ordinary way of describing first light."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conversation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the way they were saying to each other, \"**Who is going to roll the stone back from the entrance for us?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is an imperfect. They kept asking it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three women walking through a dark garden with jars, discussing a problem they cannot solve and going anyway."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody suggests turning round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they expected"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A sealed tomb, a stone they could not move, and a body to prepare."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not going to a resurrection. They are going to finish a funeral."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The discovery"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then they **looked up, and the stone had already been rolled aside**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anablepsasai** — looking up. And **apokekulistai**, a perfect passive: it has been rolled away and stays rolled away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The note"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it was **an enormous stone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark adds it after the fact, which is the order the women would have thought it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew adds the guard and the seal, and an earthquake, and an angel sitting on the stone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the stone was not for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to keep Him in. Luke and John both have Him appearing in a locked room afterwards."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was moved so that people could get in and look."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Come, see the place where the Lord lay*, says Matthew's angel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other stone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Lazarus' tomb the instruction is *take the stone away*, and human hands do it. See the note on John 11:38–40."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here nobody is asked and nobody helps. It is done before the first witness arrives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they find inside"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A young man clothed in a long white robe sitting on the right side.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not be alarmed. **You seek Jesus of Nazareth, who was crucified. He is risen! He is not here**. See the place where they laid Him.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The message"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But go, tell His disciples — **and Peter** — that He is going before you into Galilee.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter, named separately. Mark's Gospel is traditionally Peter's account, which makes the two extra words worth noticing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they went out quickly and fled from the tomb, for they trembled and were amazed. And **they said nothing to anyone, for they were afraid**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark's oldest manuscripts end there, mid-fear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Women, at every stage. Last at the cross, present at the burial, first at the tomb, and first told."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In a legal system where their testimony did not count."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The question they asked on the way.** *Who will roll away the stone?* They had no answer and went anyway — and it had been moved before they got there."}]}, "Mark 16:15–18": {"title": "\"Tell the good news to every person alive\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last instruction in Mark, and it has no limits on it anywhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scope"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go out into **the whole world**, and tell the good news to **every person alive**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *to all creation*. Matthew's version has *all the nations*; Mark's is wider still."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is excluded by geography, nation, or condition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who He said it to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven men who had run. The chapter has just recorded Him rebuking them for unbelief and hardness of heart because they would not believe the ones who had seen Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He rebukes them, and in the next sentence hands them the world."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The message"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whoever **believes and is baptised** will be saved; whoever **refuses to believe** stands condemned.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two halves are not symmetrical. Believing and baptism on one side; on the other He does not say *whoever is not baptised* — only *whoever refuses to believe*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The dividing line in the sentence is belief."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The signs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And these signs will **go along with** the ones who believe.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is to follow after, to accompany. The signs follow the believers; the believers do not chase the signs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five are listed:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*in My name they will **drive out demons***", "*they will **speak in new tongues***", "*if they **pick up snakes** or **drink anything deadly**, it will do them no harm*", "*they will **lay their hands on the sick**, and the sick will get well*"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two that are not commands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here tells anyone to handle a snake or drink poison. Both are described as things that will not harm, not as things to attempt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is bitten by a viper on Malta, shakes it off into the fire, and the islanders wait for him to swell up and die. He had not gone looking for it — he was picking up firewood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In My name\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The authority is stated once and covers the list. Not in their own name, and not by any technique."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the last verse of Mark says happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they went out and preached everywhere, **the Lord working with them** and confirming the word through the accompanying signs.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark ends with the two parties named: they preached, and He worked with them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where He is while they are doing it.** *So then, after the Lord had spoken to them, He was received up into heaven, and sat down at the right hand of God.* Mark puts the ascension and the seat in one line, and then says He was working with them. Seated, and working."}]}, "Mark 16:19–20": {"title": "Working alongside them", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last two verses of Mark, and they run in two directions at once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The textual question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The earliest manuscripts of Mark end at verse 8, with the women fleeing the tomb *for they were afraid*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verses 9 to 20 are absent from Sinaiticus and Vaticanus, and the church has read them anyway for most of its history. The question is old and is not settled here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ascension"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was **taken up into heaven**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anelemphthe** — was taken up. A passive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke gives the fuller account in Acts: *while they watched, He was taken up, and a cloud received Him out of their sight*. See the note on Acts 1:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He sat down at the right hand of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 110:1, quoted more than any other Old Testament verse in the New Testament. See the note on Psalms 110:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sitting is the point. Hebrews builds on it: *every priest **stands** ministering daily… but this Man, after He had offered one sacrifice for sins forever, **sat down***. See the note on Hebrews 10:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There were no chairs in the tabernacle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The going out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **they went out and told the news everywhere**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pantachou** — everywhere."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He sits and they go. The two clauses are next to each other, and the contrast is deliberate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the Lord worked right alongside them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sunergountos** — working together with. The root of *synergy*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A genitive absolute: while the Lord was co-working."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just been described as seated at the right hand of God, and in the same sentence He is working with them on the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The confirmation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Confirming the message by **the signs that came with it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **bebaiountos** — a legal word. To ratify, to guarantee, to make binding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The signs are described as corroboration of the message rather than as the message."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews says it the same way: *God also bearing witness both with signs and wonders, with various miracles, and gifts of the Holy Spirit*. See the note on Hebrews 2:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the signs were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before list them: casting out demons, new tongues, taking up serpents, drinking anything deadly without harm, laying hands on the sick."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two of them happen in Acts. Paul shakes a viper off his hand on Malta *and suffered no harm*, and hands are laid on the sick throughout."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shape of the ending"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark's Gospel is the shortest, the fastest, and the one that uses *immediately* forty times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It ends with a man sitting down and a group of men going everywhere, and the last word in the Greek is about the signs following."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the other three end with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew: *I am with you always*. See the note on Matthew 28:16–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke: *and they were continually in the temple praising and blessing God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John: *and there are also many other things that Jesus did, which if they were written one by one, I suppose that even the world itself could not contain the books*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two positions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seated, and working alongside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark puts them in consecutive sentences and does not explain the arrangement."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two clauses side by side.** He sat down, and He worked alongside them. Mark states both in one sentence, and the second is a present participle."}]}, "Mark 2:1–5": {"title": "A roof torn open", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four men make a hole in somebody's house, and the first thing said to the man they lower through it is not about his legs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He came back to **Capernaum**, and word got around that **He was home**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark says *at home* — probably Peter's house, where He had healed Peter's mother-in-law and where the whole town had gathered at the door after sunset."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crowd"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So many people packed in that **there was no room left, not even out by the doorway**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The door is blocked from the inside and the outside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He was speaking the word to them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark puts it in there so the reader knows what gets interrupted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Four men came carrying a paralyzed man **on a mat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark alone gives the number. One at each corner."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The obstacle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When they **could not get anywhere near Jesus** because of the crush of people.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They try the ordinary route first and it fails."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They went up on top of the house, **broke open the roof right above Him, dug their way through**, and lowered the mat down.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Galilean roof was beams laid across, packed with branches, reeds and clay, rolled flat. An outside stair went up to it — it was usable space."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dug** is the right word. They are removing packed earth, and it is falling into the room the whole time, onto the crowd and onto Jesus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody's roof. Nobody in the account asks permission, and Mark records no complaint from the householder."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus **saw their faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Their** — plural. The four, and the man on the mat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Faith is described as visible. What He saw was a hole in a ceiling and a stretcher coming down on ropes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Son, your sins are forgiven**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody asked for that. Five men went to enormous trouble about a pair of legs, and the first sentence addresses something else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Son** — the Greek is **teknon**, child. An affectionate word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mark leaves out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any explanation. The paralysis is not said to be caused by sin, and the man is not questioned or told to confess."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forgiveness first, walking second. The healing comes seven verses later, and Jesus says outright that it happens *so that you will know the Son of Man has authority here on earth to forgive sins.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The legs are the evidence for the sentence about the sins. See the note on Mark 2:6–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the scribes make of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are in the room, sitting, and they conclude it is blasphemy — *nobody can forgive sins except God alone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their logic is sound. It is the premise about who is speaking that they have wrong."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the four did.** Carried him, failed at the door, climbed, dug through a roof, and lowered him into a room full of people. Mark calls the whole sequence *their faith*."}]}, "Mark 2:15–17": {"title": "At the table with sinners", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The dinner that follows a tax collector's call, and the objection is about seating."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose house"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus was at the table **in Levi's house**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Levi is Matthew. Mark and Luke use the Levi name; the man's own Gospel uses Matthew and adds *the tax collector* to it in the list of the twelve."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been sitting at the tax booth in Capernaum when Jesus walked past and said *follow Me*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A whole crowd of **tax collectors and people with bad reputations** sat down and ate with Him and His disciples.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Tax collectors worked for Rome, could set their own margin, and were classed with thieves. A Jew who took the job was outside respectable society permanently."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause Mark adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **there were many of them who followed Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a few curious guests. Mark says there was a crowd of them, and that they were already following."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why does He eat with people like that?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *why does He eat and drink with tax collectors and sinners*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They ask the disciples, not Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What eating meant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A shared table in that world was not a neutral act. It signified acceptance, and it made you ritually vulnerable to whatever the other party carried."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The complaint is not that He talks to them. It is that He is reclining at a meal with them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same charge is made against Him repeatedly: *this Man receives sinners and eats with them* — which is what provokes the three parables of Luke 15. See the note on Luke 15:11–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is **not the healthy who go looking for a doctor. It is the sick**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A proverb, and one that circulated in the ancient world in various forms."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not defend the company. He explains the profession."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I did not come to call **the ones who think they are already righteous**. I came to call **sinners**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's version adds a quotation: *go and learn what this means: \"I desire mercy and not sacrifice.\"* — from Hosea."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the call goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To bring them **to a whole new way of thinking**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **metanoia** — a change of mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mark records around it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 2 is a run of four confrontations: the paralytic through the roof and the forgiveness of sins, this dinner, a question about fasting, and the disciples picking grain on the sabbath. See the note on Mark 2:6–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four scenes, four objections, and the opposition is assembled by the end of chapter 3: *then the Pharisees went out and immediately plotted with the Herodians against Him, how they might destroy Him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the two clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The doctor line explains why He is there. The second line says who He came for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither is an apology."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the objection was about.** Not the teaching. A meal — and Mark notes in passing that there was a crowd of such people already following Him."}]}, "Mark 2:6–12": {"title": "\"Which is easier to say?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The objection is never spoken aloud, and it gets answered anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is in the room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Some of the teachers of the law **were sitting there**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's parallel says they had come *out of every town of Galilee, Judea, and Jerusalem*. This is not a casual audience; the scholars have travelled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They began **turning it over in their hearts**: \"Why does this man talk this way? **That is an insult to God. Nobody can forgive sins except God alone**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reasoning is correct. A third party cannot forgive a debt owed to someone else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sin is against God, so the pardon is God's to give — and they are right about that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He knew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus knew **immediately, deep in His spirit**, what they were reasoning **among themselves**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing had been said. Mark says twice in one sentence that it was interior."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Answering the unspoken thought is itself an answer to the question they were asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why are you turning such things over in your hearts?**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Which is easier to say** to this man — 'Your sins are forgiven,' or 'Get up, pick up your mat, and walk'?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Easier **to say**. That is the precise wording."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saying *your sins are forgiven* is easier, because nothing visible follows and nobody can check it. Saying *get up and walk* is verifiable within seconds."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So He says the checkable one, to establish the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But so that you will know the Son of Man has authority here on earth to forgive sins**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence breaks off mid-way — Mark inserts *and here He turned to the paralyzed man* — and then finishes as a command to somebody else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The healing is inside a sentence addressed to the scribes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Here on earth\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *on the earth*. Their objection was that forgiveness belongs in heaven. He puts it on the ground, in a house in Capernaum, in the afternoon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The Son of Man\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "His usual way of referring to Himself. It comes from Daniel 7: *One like the Son of Man, coming with the clouds of heaven… to Him was given dominion and glory and a kingdom.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It sounds modest and is not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three commands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get up, pick up your mat, and go home**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mat is included. He is to carry the thing that carried him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the man **stood right up, took his mat, and walked out in front of them all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark's Greek is immediate. Through the crowd, past the scribes, out the door that had been too full to get in by."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were beside themselves, and they **praised God** and kept saying, \"**We have never seen anything like this**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *they were amazed out of their minds*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was and was not proved"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The legs were the visible half. The forgiveness was the point, and He said so before the man moved."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is on offer in the passage.** The scribes came to assess a claim about authority. They watched a man walk out carrying the bed he was brought in on, and the claim it was offered as proof of was the one about sins."}]}, "Mark 3:1–6": {"title": "\"Angry and grieved\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only place in the Gospels where Mark tells us Jesus was angry, and he tells us what it was aimed at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setup"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were **watching Him closely** to see whether He would heal the man on the Sabbath, **so that they would have something to accuse Him of**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *watching closely* is to observe with sinister intent — to lie in wait."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in the room is asking about the man's hand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does with the staging"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Come and stand here where everyone can see you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts him in the middle. Whatever happens next is going to happen in public, and Jesus arranges it that way deliberately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He could have healed him quietly after the service. He calls him out to the front first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Is it right on the Sabbath to do good, or to do harm? To save a life, or to destroy one?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two pairs, and the second is sharper than it looks. While He is asking about destroying a life on the Sabbath, they are in the process of planning His death — as the last verse shows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But they would not say a word.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is that they were silent. There is no answer to the question and they know it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The anger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He looked around at them, **angry and grieved at how hard their hearts had gone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two emotions in one clause. The Greek for *angry* is **orge**; the participle for *grieved* means being pained together with, deeply distressed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew and Luke both record this scene and neither mentions the anger. Only Mark does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was directed at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the man. Not sinners. Not the crowd."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The hardness of their hearts* — the Greek is **porosis**, a medical word for a callus, or bone forming over a break. Tissue that has gone hard and lost feeling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is angry at the calcification, and the same sentence says He is grieved by it. Anger and grief over the same object, at the same time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The healing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Stretch out your hand.**\" He stretched it out, and the hand was made completely whole.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing was done to him. No touch is recorded, no word over the hand, no prayer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is told to do the one thing a withered hand cannot do, and does it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the healing produced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the Pharisees went straight out and **began plotting with Herod's men how they might destroy Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Pharisees and the Herodians had nothing in common and detested each other. A restored hand united them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark records the first plot against His life immediately after this."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where Jesus' anger is not recorded.** With the woman caught in adultery. With Zacchaeus, or Matthew at the tax booth, or the woman at the well, or the thief on the cross. The Gospels show Him angry at hardness and at a temple market, and nowhere else."}]}, "Mark 3:13–19": {"title": "\"So that they would be with Him\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The appointment of the twelve, and Mark gives the job description in three parts with an unexpected first item."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He went up **on the mountainside**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke adds what happened first: *He went out to the mountain to pray, and continued all night in prayer to God. And when it was day, He called His disciples to Him, and from them He chose twelve.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A night of praying, and then the list."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The calling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **called to Himself the ones He wanted**, and they came to Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *whom He Himself willed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody applies. He says it later in John: *you did not choose Me, but I chose you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He appointed twelve of them, **so that they would be with Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the first item, and it is not a task."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hina osin met' autou** — that they might be with Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second and third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And so that He could **send them out to preach**, with **authority to heal the sick and to drive out demons**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sending comes second, and the power comes attached to the sending."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark puts them in that order and does not reverse it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What \"apostle\" means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek **apostolos** — one sent out. The name describes the second purpose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve names, and Mark notes what was distinctive about several:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Simon, to whom He gave the name Peter** — **Petros**, a stone", "**James and John, whom He called Boanerges, Sons of Thunder** — the two who wanted to call fire down on a Samaritan village. See the note on Luke 9:51–56.", "**Simon the Zealot** — from the party committed to armed resistance against Rome", "**Matthew** — who collected taxes for Rome"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "A zealot and a tax collector in the same group of twelve."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Judas Iscariot, the one who would hand Him over**"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "How Mark names the last one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every list of the twelve in the New Testament ends with Judas, and every one attaches the same clause."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was on the mountain, called by name, and included in the sending and the authority."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve. Nobody in that world would have missed it — twelve tribes, twelve sons of Jacob."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mark records around it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately before: crowds pressing so hard He had to have a boat kept ready, and unclean spirits crying out that He was the Son of God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately after: *then the multitude came together again, so that they could not so much as eat bread.* And His family coming to take Him home, *for they said, \"He is out of His mind.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark's ordering has been noticed for a long time. The men are appointed to be with Him first, and everything else in the sentence follows from that clause."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are with Him for roughly three years before Acts opens."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes first in the job description.** Not preaching and not power. That they would be with Him — and Mark puts it at the head of the sentence with the other two hanging off it."}]}, "Mark 3:20–22": {"title": "Two verdicts on the same day", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "His family think He is mad and the theologians think He is possessed, and Mark puts both in one paragraph."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pressure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The crowd gathered again **so thickly that He and His followers could not even get a meal down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark notes this twice in his Gospel. It is the detail he keeps."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just appointed the twelve on a mountain, and come back into a house."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The family"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When **His own family heard about it**, they set out to **take charge of Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hoi par' autou** — those belonging to Him, His own people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **kratesai** — to seize, to take hold of forcibly. The same verb used of arresting Him later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are coming to take Him home by force."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because people were saying, \"**He has lost His mind**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exeste** — He is beside Himself, out of His senses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The subject is ambiguous. It can be *people were saying* or *for they said* — the family themselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way, the family is acting on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John says it plainly elsewhere: *for even His brothers did not believe in Him*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The delegation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the teachers of the law **who had come down from Jerusalem**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the local scribes. An official party, sent from the capital."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Beelzebul is in Him**. He drives out demons by the power of the ruler of the demons.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Beelzeboul** — probably from **Baal-zebul**, lord of the exalted dwelling, twisted in Hebrew into **Baal-zebub**, lord of the flies, the god of Ekron whom Ahaziah sent to consult."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they concede"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The exorcisms."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do not say He is a fraud or that nothing happens. Their explanation is about the source, and it requires the power to be real."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the same shape as Nazareth — *where did this man get such wisdom, and these mighty works?* See the note on Matthew 13:53–58."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in the Gospels disputes that things happen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"How can Satan cast out Satan? If a kingdom is divided against itself, that kingdom cannot stand… **and if Satan has risen up against himself, and is divided, he cannot stand, but has an end**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the parable that answers the charge properly:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"No one can enter a strong man's house and plunder his goods, **unless he first binds the strong man**. And then he will plunder his house.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not deny being in somebody's house. He says He has tied the owner up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Truly I say to you, all sins will be forgiven… but he who blasphemes against the Holy Spirit never has forgiveness.\"* See the note on Mark 3:28–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the family thread finishes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then His brothers and His mother came, and standing outside they sent to Him, calling Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He looked around in a circle at those who sat about Him, and said, \"**Here are My mother and My brothers! For whoever does the will of God is My brother and My sister and mother**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people who came to take Him home end up standing outside the house."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they end up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These all continued with one accord in prayer and supplication, with the women and Mary the mother of Jesus, and **with His brothers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Acts 1, in the upper room."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And James runs the Jerusalem church by chapter 15, and writes a letter opening *James, a bondservant of God*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two verdicts have in common.** Both accept that something real is happening. His family called it madness; the men from Jerusalem called it the wrong power — and neither denied the exorcisms."}]}, "Mark 3:28–30": {"title": "The sentence that explains it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The hardest saying in Mark, and the last verse tells you what it was about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has been said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scribes from Jerusalem have just announced their verdict: *He has Beelzebul, and by the ruler of the demons He casts out demons*. See the note on Mark 3:20–22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has answered it — a divided kingdom cannot stand, and a strong man's house is only plundered after the strong man is tied up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scope"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I tell you the truth: **every sin people commit can be forgiven them, and every slander they speak can be forgiven**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **panta ta hamartemata** — all the sins. And **hai blasphemiai hosa ean blasphemesosin** — the blasphemies, however many they blaspheme."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence starts with the widest amnesty in the Gospels, and Mark's wording piles it up: all, and however many."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything after this has to be read against that opening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **whoever speaks against the Holy Spirit is never forgiven**; that guilt stays with them for all time.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **enochos estin aioniou hamartematos** — guilty of an eternal sin."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The explanation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He said this **because they were saying, \"He has an unclean spirit in Him.\"***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark supplies the reason in the next sentence, which is the most important thing in the passage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The saying is not general. It is attached to one specific accusation, made by one specific group, in one specific room."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records the same explanation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the accusation was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Spirit is the one at work in the exorcisms. Jesus says so in Matthew's version: *if I cast out demons by the Spirit of God, surely the kingdom of God has come upon you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They watched the Spirit set people free and said it was the devil doing it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what is being named — not swearing, not a moment of doubt, not a lapse under pressure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who was doing it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Men who had come from Jerusalem specifically to assess Him, who could not deny the works, and who chose an explanation they knew was false rather than accept the obvious one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern in the same Gospel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter denies Him three times with curses, and is on a beach eating fish a few weeks later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The disciples all abandon Him and are all recommissioned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul describes himself as *a blasphemer, a persecutor, and an insolent man* — and adds *but I obtained mercy because I did it ignorantly in unbelief*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Blasphemy is on his own list of what he did, and he is the apostle to the Gentiles."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the passage does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not describe a state anybody stumbles into."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing the text supplies as a definition is verse 30, and it is about a settled verdict delivered by men who knew better."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter goes on to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then His brothers and His mother came… and He said, \"Who is My mother, or My brothers?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *whoever does the will of God is My brother and My sister and mother*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The paragraph about the unforgivable is immediately followed by the widest definition of family in the Gospel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the same Gospel opens with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And John baptising *for the remission of sins*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse that defines it.** Mark supplies it himself: *because they were saying, He has an unclean spirit*. The saying is attached to one accusation, made by men who could not deny what they had watched."}]}, "Mark 4:13–20": {"title": "What the story means", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The one parable Jesus explains in full, and He says it is the key to the rest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rebuke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You do not understand this story? Then how will you understand any of them?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark alone keeps it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This parable is treated as foundational. If the mechanism here is missed, none of the others will work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being sown"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The farmer is sowing the word**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a message about morality or effort. The seed is the word, and everything that follows describes what happens to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first soil"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Some people are like **the seed along the path**: the word is sown, and **Satan comes right away and snatches it out of them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **euthus** — immediately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Path soil is not bad soil. It is soil that has been walked on until it is hard, and the seed sits on top of it where the birds can see it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Some are like **the seed on rocky ground**: **the moment they hear the word they welcome it with joy**, but **it has no root in them and does not last**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The joy is real and Mark says so."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The problem is depth. Rocky ground in that country is a shelf of limestone under a thin layer of soil — the seed germinates fast because the shelf holds the heat, and dies fast because the root has nowhere to go."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So when **trouble or persecution comes because of the word**, they fall away at once.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Because of the word.** The pressure is named as arriving on account of the thing sown."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Others are like **the seed sown among thorns**: **they hear the word**, but…\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things crowd in:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*\"**the worries of this life**\"*", "*\"**the pull of money**\"* — the Greek is *the deceitfulness of riches*", "*\"**the craving for other things**\"*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's version says *pleasures of life*; Matthew has the first two."}, {"k": "p", "t": "None of the three is a sin in itself. Worry, money, and wanting other things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"All crowd in and **choke the word**, and **it never comes to anything**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sumpnigousin** — choke, throttle, crowd out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The plant is alive and produces nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fourth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And some are like **the seed sown on good soil**: they **hear the word and welcome it**, and **it bears fruit in them — thirty times what was sown, or sixty, or a hundred**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs: hear and welcome. The Greek is **paradechontai** — receive, accept."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The yields"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three different figures, and all three are called good."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The good soil is not uniform. Mark keeps the range."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Normal yields in that agriculture ran to seven or eight times. Thirty is exceptional and a hundred is extraordinary — the number Isaac reaped in a famine year. See the note on Genesis 26:12–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What all four have in common"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same seed and the same sower. Nothing in the parable varies except the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Is a lamp brought to be put under a basket or under a bed? Is it not to be set on a lampstand?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"**Take heed what you hear**. With the same measure you use, it will be measured to you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke has *take heed **how** you hear*. Between them, both the content and the manner."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What varies in the story.** Not the seed. One sower, one word, four grounds — and the third one is choked by three things none of which are wrong in themselves."}]}, "Mark 4:21–23": {"title": "A lamp on a stand", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two ridiculous places to put a lamp, and then a promise about what gets found out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Does anyone light a lamp and then set it under a bucket or shove it under the bed?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **modion** — a measuring basket, about two gallons. A household item, upended over a flame."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark asks it as a question. Matthew and Luke state it as a fact."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why both are absurd"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A basket over a lamp smothers it. A lamp under a bed sets fire to the bedding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither is a way of hiding light. Both are ways of destroying it or the house."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Of course not. **It goes up on a stand**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **luchnian** — a lampstand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is the Greek Old Testament's term for the seven-branched menorah in the tabernacle, and it is the word Revelation uses for the seven churches."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Nothing is hidden that will not be brought out into the open**, and nothing is covered up that will not come to light.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek construction is *for nothing is hidden **except that it should be revealed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is stronger than the translation suggests. The concealment is temporary by design — the reason for hiding it was that it would be brought out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being hidden"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parables themselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just told the sower to a crowd and explained it only to the disciples. See the note on Matthew 13:10–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The saying about the lamp comes immediately after, and it says the private explanation is not the final state of things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus keeps quiet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Through Mark's Gospel He silences almost everyone. Demons are told to be quiet, healed men are told to say nothing, and the disciples are told not to say He is the Christ."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Scholars call it the messianic secret, and Mark reports it more insistently than anyone else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This verse is Mark's own note on the practice: what is hidden is hidden in order to be brought out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one day He refuses to silence anybody"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tenth of Nisan, on the road down the Mount of Olives — *if these people were silent, the very stones would start shouting*. See the note on Luke 19:36–40."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If you have ears to hear, then hear**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *if anyone has ears to hear, let him hear*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark uses it twice in this chapter, at the end of the sower and at the end of the lamp."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the sentence Revelation attaches to every one of the seven letters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take heed what you hear.** With the same measure you use, it will be measured to you; and to you who hear, more will be given.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark's version says *what*; Luke's says *how*. See the note on Luke 8:16–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the two seed parables — the one that grows while the farmer sleeps, and the mustard seed. See the note on Mark 4:26–29."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And on the same day, when evening had come, He said to them, \"Let us cross over to the other side.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The storm, the pillow in the stern, and *why are you so fearful?* See the note on Mark 4:35–41."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A chapter of teaching about hearing, and then a boat."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why anything was covered.** Mark's Greek says it plainly: nothing is hidden *except in order that* it should be brought out. The concealing had a deadline built into it."}]}, "Mark 4:24–25": {"title": "\"Be careful how you listen\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses about hearing, tucked between the parables."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Be careful how you listen.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *what* you listen to — Luke's version has *how you hear*, and Mark's is *what you hear*, and between them they cover both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point is that hearing is something that can be done well or badly, and that the manner of it has consequences."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The measure you use will be the measure used for you**, and more besides.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same saying appears in Luke 6 about judging, condemning, forgiving and giving. See the note on Luke 6:37–38."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here it is applied to listening. However much attention you bring is the amount you get back — *and more besides.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hard sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The one who has will be given more**, and from the one who has nothing, even the little he has will be taken away.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It reads as unfair until you notice what is being measured."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The context is the parable of the sower, which Mark has just finished — four soils, the same seed, and the difference is entirely in the ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the sowing varies. What a hearer ends up with depends on what he does with what he heard, and the man who does nothing with it loses even the memory of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What surrounds this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately before: *is a lamp brought to be put under a basket or under a bed? Is it not to be set on a lampstand?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately after: the seed growing secretly, and the mustard seed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the sequence is — the lamp is meant to be seen, so pay attention to how you hear, because the seed grows on its own once it is in the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lamp and the hearing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark puts a lamp and an instruction about listening in consecutive breaths."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lampstand in the tabernacle was the only light in a windowless room, and the priest's daily job at it was trimming the wicks so it kept burning. See the note on Exodus 25:31–40."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The unavoidable disclosure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For there is nothing hidden which will not be revealed, nor has anything been kept secret but that it should come to light.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the verse in between. Whatever is put under the basket does not stay there."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line Mark repeats.** *If anyone has ears to hear, let him hear.* He uses it twice in this one chapter — before the explanation of the sower and again here. Every occurrence of it in the Gospels is attached to something that had just gone over somebody's head."}]}, "Mark 4:26–29": {"title": "He has no idea how", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only parable that appears in Mark and nowhere else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The kingdom of God is like this. **A man scatters seed on the ground**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole of his contribution to the story. He throws it and the parable moves on without him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He **goes to bed at night and gets up in the morning**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sleep and rise, sleep and rise. The Greek verbs are present tense and repetitive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is said about weeding, watering or worrying. The farmer's activity for the duration of the parable is going to bed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens meanwhile"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **all the while the seed sprouts and grows**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **blastai kai mekunetai** — sprouts and lengthens."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The admission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **he has no idea how**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hos ouk oiden autos** — he himself does not know."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the sentence the parable exists for. Mark puts it in the middle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A first-century farmer knew everything worth knowing about when to sow and when to reap, and nothing at all about what happens underground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither does anyone else. Ecclesiastes says it: *as you do not know what is the way of the wind, or how the bones grow in the womb of her who is with child, so you do not know the works of God who makes all things.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The soil"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The soil brings it along **all by itself**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **automate** — of itself, spontaneously. The root of *automatic*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word occurs twice in the New Testament. The other is Acts 12, where the iron gate of the prison *opened to them of its own accord* while Peter thought he was seeing a vision."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both times it describes something happening without human agency while a person stands there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stages"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"First the **stalk**, then the **head**, then the **full ripe grain** in the head.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three, in order, and none of them can be hurried or skipped."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The farmer cannot make the head come before the stalk. He can only see which stage it is at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The harvest"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And when the grain is ready, he **takes the sickle to it, because the harvest has come**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apostellei to drepanon** — sends forth the sickle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is from Joel: *put in the sickle, for the harvest is ripe*. Revelation quotes the same line at the end of the age."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The farmer's two actions in the parable are throwing the seed and swinging the sickle. He does the beginning and the end and sleeps through everything in between."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits in Mark"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the parable of the sower with its four soils, and the mustard seed with its disproportionate result."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three seed parables in a row, and this is the one about the process nobody sees."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sower parable added"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sower sows the word.* See the note on Matthew 13:18–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "If the seed is the word, then this parable is about what the word does after it is heard, during the time nobody is watching it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other agricultural sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I planted, Apollos watered, but **God gave the increase**. So then neither he who plants is anything, nor he who waters, but God who gives the increase.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's version, and he reaches the same conclusion by a different route."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the parable"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Soil types, birds, thorns, rocky ground, an enemy sowing tares."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark has just told the one with four soils and will shortly tell the mustard seed. This one has no obstacle in it at all."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line the parable is built around.** *He has no idea how* — said of a farmer who is asleep while the thing he threw is doing the only work in the story."}]}, "Mark 4:35–41": {"title": "\"Asleep on a cushion\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Mark keeps a detail the other Gospels leave out, and it is the kind of thing an eyewitness remembers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cushion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus was in the stern, asleep on a cushion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the* cushion — the definite article. It is the steersman's seat cushion, a fixed part of a fishing boat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only Mark records it, and Mark's Gospel is the one the early church associated with Peter's preaching. Peter owned a boat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He had said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us cross over to the other side.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence before the storm. Everything that happens next takes place inside that instruction — they are going where He said they were going."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question they ask"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Teacher, do You not care that we are going down?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *save us*. It is an accusation, and it is about His caring rather than His power. Fishermen who have been on that lake all their lives are certain they are sinking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says to the sea"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Peace. Be still.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *be still* is the word used for muzzling — put a muzzle on it. It is the same word Mark used a chapter earlier when Jesus silenced an unclean spirit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He speaks to the storm the way He speaks to a demon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two fears"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Why are you so frightened? Do you still have no faith?\" And a deep awe came over them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark is precise: they were afraid of the storm, and after it stopped they were more afraid. The word for their reaction afterwards is the great fear reserved for the presence of God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They ask *who is this?* — and the answer, for anyone who knew the Psalms, is that stilling the sea is something God does."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the disciples had already been told.** He had just spent the afternoon teaching in parables from that boat. The storm follows immediately, and Mark places it there deliberately: the men who had heard the word all day are the ones asked whether they still have no faith."}]}, "Mark 5:1–5": {"title": "Across the water to the tombs", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "They cross a lake in a storm to reach one man, and he is the first thing on the shore."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crossing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They came across to the other side of the lake, into **the country of the Gadarenes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gentile territory. The Decapolis, east of the Galilee, with Greek cities and a pig-farming economy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the crossing cost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The night before: *a great windstorm arose, and the waves beat into the boat*, and *He was in the stern, asleep on a pillow*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peace, be still.* See the note on Mark 4:35–41."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The storm, and then this. Mark puts them side by side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the moment Jesus stepped out of the boat**, a man came at Him out of the burial caves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **euthus** — immediately. Mark's favourite word, used over forty times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No interval. He gets out of the boat and the man is there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the man lived"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He lived out there **among the graves**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Limestone tombs cut into hillsides, large enough to shelter in. In Jewish law, the most defiling place there is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man in a Gentile country, living in a cemetery, near a herd of pigs. Mark stacks the uncleanness up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The restraints"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Nobody could keep him tied down anymore. They had tried it many times **with shackles and chains**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek distinguishes them: **pedais** — foot irons, and **halusesin** — chains."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The community had tried. Repeatedly. The verse records the attempts, not just the failure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened to them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every time **he tore the chains apart and broke the irons off his legs**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **diespasthai** — pulled apart. And **suntetriphthai** — shattered, rubbed to pieces."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **no one on earth was strong enough to bring him under control**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **oudeis ischuen auton damasai** — nobody had the strength to tame him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Damazo** — the word for breaking a wild animal. James uses it of the tongue: *every kind of beast has been tamed… but no man can tame the tongue*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The days and nights"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Night and day he was out **among the tombs and up in the hills, crying out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **krazon** — screaming."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No sleep is mentioned. The description covers the whole clock."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last detail"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **cutting himself with stones**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katakopton heauton lithois**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark alone records it. It is the detail that stops the passage being about strength."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chains failed, and the community's only concern was containment. Nobody in the account had been trying to help him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does when Jesus arrives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When he saw Jesus from afar, **he ran and worshiped Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who could not be approached runs toward Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *what have I to do with You, Jesus, Son of the Most High God? I implore You by God that You do not torment me.* See the note on Mark 5:6–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What is your name?**\" And he answered, saying, \"**My name is Legion; for we are many**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Roman legion was six thousand men."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he looks like afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They came to Jesus, and saw the one who had been demon-possessed and had the legion, **sitting and clothed and in his right mind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sitting — the man who had never stopped moving. Clothed. Sane."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they were afraid.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To go with Him. And the answer is no."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go home to your friends, and tell them what great things the Lord has done for you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only person in Mark told to publicise it, and the only Gentile."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he departed and began to proclaim in the Decapolis all that Jesus had done for him, and all marveled.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Mark adds.** *Cutting himself with stones.* Everyone in the story had been trying to hold him down; nobody had noticed that the danger to him was himself."}]}, "Mark 5:11–13": {"title": "They had to ask", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses, and the striking thing is who is doing the requesting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The begging"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The spirits begged Him.* Mark uses the verb twice in this scene — earlier they had begged not to be sent out of the region."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever they are, they cannot move without permission. The whole exchange is them asking and Him deciding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the request reveals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Send us into the pigs. Let us go into them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They want somewhere to be. The parallel account in Luke has them begging not to be sent into the abyss."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same thing the returning spirit is looking for in Matthew 12 — somewhere to settle, a house to occupy. See the note on Matthew 12:43–45."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He let them go\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words, and Mark does not explain them. The permission is granted without a recorded reason."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Readers have argued about the pigs for centuries — whose they were, what the loss meant, why Jesus allowed it. Mark says nothing about any of that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What he does record is the count: *about two thousand of them*. It is the only indication in the passage of the scale of what had been living in one man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was in the man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Earlier in the chapter he gives his name as Legion, *for we are many* — a Roman legion being some thousands of soldiers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody could bind him, not even with chains. He had been breaking irons and cutting himself with stones among the tombs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The pigs stampeded down the steep bank into the lake and were drowned.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The destruction the spirits had been working on the man goes into the herd, and the man is left sitting, dressed, in his right mind."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the region asks for afterwards.** Not more healings. They *begged Jesus to leave*, and He got into the boat. The third begging in the chapter comes from the townspeople, and it is the only one about which He does what is asked without a word. See the note on Mark 5:18–20."}]}, "Mark 5:14–17": {"title": "Sitting and clothed", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The town comes out to look, sees a man in his right mind, and asks Jesus to go."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who lived among the tombs, who broke chains and could not be tamed, who cut himself with stones night and day. See the note on Mark 5:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The legion, the request to be sent into the pigs, and two thousand animals down the bank into the lake."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who spreads it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The men who had been **tending the pigs** ran off and spread the story **through the town and out across the farms**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hoi boskontes** — the herdsmen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have just lost their livelihood, and they are the ones who carry the news."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crowd"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And people came **to see for themselves** what had happened.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **elthon idein** — came to see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they find"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there was the man who had been held by the legion — **sitting down, dressed, in his right mind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kathemenon himatismenon kai sophronounta**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three participles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sitting** — the man who never stopped moving, out among the tombs and in the hills night and day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Clothed** — Luke adds that he *wore no clothes for a long time*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**In his right mind** — **sophroneo**, to be of sound mind, self-controlled. The opposite of the word for madness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words, and each of them names the reversal of something in the description of him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **it frightened them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ephobethesan** — they were afraid."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not of the man. They had lived with the man. He is the least frightening thing in the field."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The report"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ones who had seen it told the rest **exactly what had happened to the man, and what had happened to the pigs**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both facts, in that order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they began to **beg Jesus to leave their district**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parakalein auton apelthein** — to urge Him to go away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they weighed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man restored, and two thousand pigs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark records the calculation without comment, and does not say which side of it decided them — the loss, or the power that caused it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the man asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when He got into the boat, **he who had been demon-possessed begged Him that he might be with Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb — **parekalei**. The town begs Him to go; the man begs to come."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both requests are refused."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go home to your friends, and tell them what great things the Lord has done for you**, and how He has had compassion on you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apangeilon autois hosa ho Kurios soi pepoieken**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this is unusual"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark's Gospel is full of the opposite instruction. *See that you say nothing to anyone.* *He commanded them that no one should know it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This man is the exception, and he is a Gentile in Gentile territory."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he departed and began to **proclaim in the Decapolis** all that Jesus had done for him, and all marveled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Decapolis — ten Greek cities east of the lake."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One man, sent home, becomes the first preacher in Gentile country."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens when Jesus returns there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then they brought to Him one who was deaf and had an impediment in his speech.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in chapter 8, the feeding of four thousand in the same region — with seven baskets left. See the note on Mark 6:42–44."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The district that asked Him to leave has a crowd waiting the next time."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The three participles.** Sitting, clothed, in his right mind — each one the reversal of a detail in the description of him. And the town's response to it is fear."}]}, "Mark 5:18–20": {"title": "The one man Jesus told to stay", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man who has just been set free asks to come along, and it is the one request of that kind in the Gospels that gets turned down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As Jesus was getting back into the boat, the man who had been set free asked if he could come along with Him. But Jesus would not let him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone else is called to follow. This man asks to and is sent home."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been living among the tombs, cutting himself with stones, so strong that chains would not hold him. Now he is dressed and in his right mind and wants to get in the boat, and Jesus says no."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is sent to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go home, to your own people, and tell them what the Lord has done for you, and how much mercy He had on you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words carry the commission: **what the Lord has done**, and **mercy**. No training, no doctrine, no instruction on what to say beyond his own story."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been an eyewitness for about an hour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the man went off and began telling it all through the Decapolis, the ten towns of that region.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was told to go home to his own people. He covered ten cities."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is Gentile territory — the region where the pigs were, where the locals had just asked Jesus to leave. The first missionary to the Decapolis is a man with no qualifications except a before and an after."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What happened later.** When Jesus returns to that area in Mark 7 and 8, the reception is completely different — crowds come to Him and He feeds four thousand there. Nobody says why. But somebody had been telling that region what the Lord had done for him for the intervening months."}]}, "Mark 5:25–29": {"title": "Twelve years and no help", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "She is in a crowd on the way to somewhere else, and she interrupts the story."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus is on his way to Jairus's house. A synagogue ruler's daughter is dying and the whole crowd is moving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark puts this inside that journey — one story cut open and another set in the middle of it. He does this deliberately, and the two are connected by a number."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Twelve years"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A woman in that crowd who had been **bleeding for twelve years**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The girl he is walking toward is twelve years old."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For as long as one family has had a daughter, this woman has been bleeding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What her condition meant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under Leviticus 15 a woman with a discharge is unclean, and so is anything she sits on and anyone who touches her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve years of that means twelve years without the temple, without a synagogue, and without being touched by anybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she had tried"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She had been through **a great deal at the hands of many doctors**, and she had **spent every last coin she had**, and **none of it had done any good** — she had **only grown worse**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four clauses, and each one is worse than the last. Mark takes his time over it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke — a physician — records this scene and leaves out the line about the doctors."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she had heard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She had **heard the reports about Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is all she has. No introduction, no invitation, and no conversation with Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How she came"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She came up **behind Him** in **the crush of people** and reached out for **His clothes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Behind Him, and at the level of the hem. She is legally unclean in a packed street, and everyone she brushes past becomes unclean too."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not ask permission because asking would have ended it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she kept saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If I can only touch what He is wearing**,\" she kept telling herself, \"**I will be well**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is imperfect — she **was saying** it, over and over, while pushing through."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word she uses is **sozo**: saved, made whole, rescued."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The edge of the garment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers commands a tassel on the four corners of the garment with a blue thread — **tzitzit**. Malachi's promise reads *the Sun of Righteousness shall arise with healing in His wings*, and *wings* is the same word used for those corners."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She reaches for the corner. See the note on Mark 6:53–56, where whole villages do the same."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And she touched Him, and **at that very moment** she **felt in her own body** that the bleeding had stopped and **she was healed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately, and she knew it internally before anyone said anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she expected next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing good. When He asks who touched Him she comes *fearing and trembling* and falls down and tells Him the whole truth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What she gets is a word she had not heard in twelve years: *Daughter, your faith has made you well. Go in peace, and be healed of your affliction.* See the note on Mark 5:30–34."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The law she broke.** Under Leviticus her touch made others unclean. In this case the traffic ran the other way — she touched Him, and it was the uncleanness that stopped."}]}, "Mark 5:30–34": {"title": "He stopped a crowd to find her", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A woman gets what she came for without permission, and Jesus stops everything to make sure she knows she has it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He felt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus knew at once, in Himself, that power had gone out from Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word is **dunamis** — power, ability, the word behind our *dynamite*. Mark says Jesus registered it going out, immediately, and turned round in the middle of a crush of people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was on His way to a dying child, with a synagogue ruler beside Him. He stopped anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disciples' objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look at this crowd pressing in on You from all sides, and You ask who touched You?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are right about the crowd. Hundreds of people were making contact with Him. Their point is that the question is unanswerable — and Mark's point is that one touch was different from all the others."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But He kept looking around to find her.* He would not move on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it mattered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had been bleeding twelve years — which under the law meant twelve years of being ceremonially unclean, unable to touch or be touched without passing it on. She had come up behind Him deliberately, hoping to take the healing and slip away unnoticed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He would not let her leave anonymous. And when she came forward *shaking and afraid*, He gave her the one word that ended the twelve years socially as well as physically:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Daughter.**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only time in the Gospels He calls anyone that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He said next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your faith has made you well. Go in peace, and be free of this trouble for good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He credits her — not the hem, not the accident of proximity. Her faith. And He sends her away with a blessing rather than a rebuke for taking without asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Go in peace\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *go into peace* — **eis eirenen** — which is the standard Greek rendering of the Hebrew farewell *lech leshalom*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Shalom** is not the absence of trouble. It is wholeness, everything in its place, nothing missing and nothing broken."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she had been telling herself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before any of this, pushing through the crowd: *\"If I can only touch what He is wearing, I will be well.\"* Mark's Greek has her saying it repeatedly. See the note on Mark 5:25–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He answers with the same word she had been using: **sozo** — saved, made whole."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The interruption cost something.** While He was talking to her, the message came that Jairus' daughter had died. Jesus had stopped for one woman and a child died in the delay — and then He raised her. Mark tells both stories inside each other on purpose."}]}, "Mark 5:38–43": {"title": "\"Talitha koum\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A house full of professional mourners, and Jesus clears them all out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The noise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus heard the noise of it — the weeping and the wailing and all the commotion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Funeral wailing was hired. Even a poor family was expected to provide mourners and flute players, and the volume was the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says, and what they do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The child is not dead. She is asleep.\" And they laughed in His face.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark keeps the laughter in. The people making the noise switch to mockery instantly, which tells you what the grief was worth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus is not denying the death — He uses *asleep* the way the New Testament uses it everywhere, of a state that ends. See the note on 1 Thessalonians 4:13–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is allowed in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He put them all outside.* Then five people: the father, the mother, and Peter, James and John."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parents are in the room. Whatever happens next, they see it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Talitha koum\" — which means, \"Little girl, I am speaking to you. Get up.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark preserves the Aramaic, which he does only a handful of times. This is what He actually said, in the language He said it in — a household phrase, the sort of thing a parent says in the morning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No formula, no gesture. He takes her hand and speaks to her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He said they should give her something to eat.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark ends the raising of a dead child with a note about breakfast. The practicality is the tell — the girl is not a vision or a spirit. She is a twelve-year-old who has not eaten."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The detail Mark saves for the end.** *She was twelve years old.* And the woman healed on the way to this house had been bleeding twelve years. Mark deliberately interleaves the two stories, and gives the same number to both. See the note on Mark 5:30–34."}]}, "Mark 6:1–6": {"title": "No welcome at home", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The one place He could not work, and Mark says why in a single word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus left that place and came to **His hometown**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nazareth. He has just raised Jairus' daughter and healed the woman in the crowd on the way there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And His disciples came with Him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The teaching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the Sabbath He began to teach in the synagogue, and the people who heard Him **could hardly take it in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exeplessonto** — struck out of themselves, astonished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The astonishment is real and it does not help."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Where did this man get all this?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What kind of **wisdom** is this that has been given to Him, and these **mighty works done by His hands**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They concede both. The wisdom is real and the works are real. What they cannot place is the source."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Is this not **the carpenter, the son of Mary**, the brother of **James and Joses and Judas and Simon**? And are **His sisters** not living right here among us?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tekton** — a builder or craftsman, working in wood or stone. Only Mark applies it to Jesus rather than to Joseph."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Son of Mary** — a man was named for his father. Naming him by his mother, in that culture, is not a compliment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four brothers named. Two of them wrote letters in the New Testament, and none of them believed Him at this point: *for even His brothers did not believe in Him*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they **took offense at Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eskandalizonto** — they were scandalised, they stumbled over Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had the evidence and the familiarity, and the familiarity won."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His comment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"A prophet is **respected everywhere except in his own hometown, among his own relatives, and in his own house**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three circles, tightening. Town, relatives, house."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He was not able to do many mighty works there**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ouk edunato** — He could not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew softens it to *He did not do many mighty works there*. Mark writes the harder version."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He laid His hands on **a few who were sick and healed them**, and that was all.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Even the sentence about what He could not do contains healings."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What surprised Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their **refusal to believe astonished Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ethaumazen** — He marvelled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things in the Gospels are said to have astonished Jesus. This, and the faith of a Roman centurion: *I have not found such great faith, not even in Israel*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Unbelief at home, and belief in a Gentile officer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He went out to teach in the villages all around**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No argument, no second attempt. He leaves and works elsewhere, and the next thing He does is send the twelve out in pairs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke records of the same visit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sermon on Isaiah 61 — *the Spirit of the LORD is upon Me* — and *today this Scripture is fulfilled in your hearing*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the crowd tries to throw Him off a cliff."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What changed later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His brother James, who did not believe here, is running the Jerusalem church by Acts 15 and opens his letter *James, a bondservant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul lists an appearance to him: *after that He was seen by James*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word Mark uses.** *He could not.* Matthew writes *did not*, and Mark leaves the harder sentence standing — followed immediately by a note that He healed a few of them anyway."}]}, "Mark 6:35–41": {"title": "\"You give them something to eat\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only miracle in all four Gospels, and Mark keeps the details a witness would remember."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disciples' proposal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Send them off** so they can go to the farms and villages around here and buy themselves something to eat.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Reasonable. It is late, the place is deserted, and five thousand men plus women and children have nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their solution is dispersal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You give them something to eat.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pronoun is emphatic in the Greek — *you*, yourselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He hands the problem back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The costing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Are we supposed to go and spend **two hundred days' wages** on bread?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is two hundred denarii. A denarius was a day's pay for a labourer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight months' wages, and they have done the arithmetic on the spot. Their objection is not lazy; it is accurate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The audit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How many loaves do you have? Go and see.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not answer the objection. He asks what is in hand and sends them to count."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Five — and two fish.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John adds that it was a boy's lunch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grass"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sit down in groups **on the green grass**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark is the only one who mentions the colour, which places it in spring, after the rains — and Passover was near, as John notes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the kind of detail somebody remembers because they were sitting on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arrangement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people **settled in, group after group, some of a hundred and some of fifty**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for the groups is a word used of garden beds laid out in rows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is standing. Before anything is distributed, five thousand men are seated in ordered blocks on the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **took** the five loaves and the two fish, looked up to heaven, **gave thanks**, and **broke** the loaves apart. Then He **kept handing them** to His disciples.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Took, gave thanks, broke, gave. The same four, in the same order, as the upper room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The imperfect"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **kept handing them** to His disciples.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek tense is continuous. The multiplication is not described as a flash; it happens in His hands, over and over, as fast as twelve men could carry it away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the disciples ended up doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exactly what He told them to. *You give them something to eat* — and they did, all evening, from a supply that did not stop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was left"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve baskets. One each."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Mark says about it a page later.** *They had not yet grasped what the loaves had meant.* Written about the same men, screaming at a figure on the water, nine hours after this. See the note on Mark 6:45–52."}]}, "Mark 6:42–44": {"title": "Twelve baskets gathered up", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The accounting at the end of the meal, and the numbers do not work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The satisfaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every one of them **ate until they had eaten all they wanted**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **echortasthesan** — were filled, satisfied. From **chortos**, fodder. It is the word for feeding animals to fullness rather than politely."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody was rationed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was there to start with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five loaves and two fish, brought by the disciples after Jesus said *you give them something to eat*, and they had asked whether they were expected to spend two hundred denarii. See the note on Mark 6:35–41."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The collection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when the disciples **gathered up the broken pieces that were left over**, along with the fish, **they filled twelve baskets**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kophinoi** — the stiff wicker basket a Jewish traveller carried, large enough for a day's provisions."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John gives the reason for the collection: *gather up the fragments that remain, **so that nothing is lost***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One for each of the men who had said *shall we go and buy two hundred denarii worth of bread?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark does not comment. He states the number and moves on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The count"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Five thousand men had eaten that meal.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **andres** — males. Matthew adds *besides women and children*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They started with five loaves and ended with twelve baskets, having fed a crowd in between."}, {"k": "p", "t": "More was left than was brought."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not described"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The multiplication."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark says He looked up to heaven, blessed, broke, and *gave them to His disciples to set before them*. The verb is imperfect — He kept giving. See the note on Luke 9:10–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "At no point does any Gospel say the bread grew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for fragments"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Klasmata** — broken pieces, from the verb He used on the loaves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The leftovers are pieces of what He broke, not crusts the crowd discarded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other feeding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark records a second one, four thousand fed from seven loaves, with **seven baskets** left — and a different word for basket: **spurides**, the large hamper, the kind Paul was let down the Damascus wall in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark distinguishes the two vocabularies, which is how we know he means two separate events."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does with the numbers later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When I broke the five loaves for the five thousand, **how many baskets full of fragments did you take up?**\" They said to Him, \"Twelve.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Also, when I broke the seven for the four thousand, how many large baskets full of fragments did you take up?\" And they said, \"Seven.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How is it you do not understand?**\"* See the note on Mark 8:17–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quizzes them on the leftovers, not the meal. The baskets were the lesson."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Immediately He made His disciples get into the boat.* And then the storm, and Him walking on the water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **they had not understood about the loaves**, because their heart was hardened.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark connects the two scenes explicitly. The fear on the water is traced back to the baskets."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The arithmetic.** Five loaves in, twelve baskets out, five thousand fed in between — and it was the leftovers He examined them on afterwards, not the meal."}]}, "Mark 6:45–52": {"title": "\"He was about to go right past them\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Mark tells the same night as Matthew and keeps two details nobody else has."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He made them go"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Right away He **had His disciples get into the boat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is compelled — He made them. They are on that water because He put them there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He went up the mountainside **to pray**. Night came on. The boat was out in the middle of the lake, and **Jesus was alone on the shore**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark separates them deliberately: the boat in the middle, and Him alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first detail"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **could see that they were straining hard at the oars**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "From a mountainside, at night, on a lake several miles across."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever Mark means by it, he says Jesus was watching the whole time. They could not see Him; He could see them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *straining* is being tormented, harassed — the word for being tortured."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **the wind was blowing straight against them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Contrary. They had been rowing since evening and were about halfway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At about **the fourth watch of the night**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three to six in the morning. Nine hours or so of rowing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He comes at the end of the night, not the beginning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second detail"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He was about to go right past them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only Mark has this, and it is the strangest sentence in the passage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was not going to get into the boat. He was going to pass by."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Readers have long connected it to Exodus 33, where God tells Moses *I will make all My goodness **pass before you***, and to Elijah at Horeb, where *the LORD passed by*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are occasions when God shows Himself by going past somebody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What made Him stop"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But when they saw Him… they thought He was a ghost, and **they screamed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He stops for the scream."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passing-by would have been a revelation. The screaming turned it into a rescue."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take heart. It is I. Do not be afraid.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The middle two words are **ego eimi** — the ordinary *it's me*, and also the name from the bush."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were utterly stunned. **They had not yet grasped what the loaves had meant.** Their hearts were still slow and closed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark's own comment, and it is severe."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had watched five thousand men fed from five loaves that same afternoon and collected twelve baskets of leftovers. See the note on Mark 6:35–41."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark says the storm should not have surprised them, because of the bread."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Matthew adds and Mark leaves out.** Peter walking on the water and sinking. Mark's Gospel is traditionally associated with Peter's preaching, and it is the one account that does not include the part where Peter tried."}]}, "Mark 6:53–56": {"title": "The edge of His clothes", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A summary passage, four verses long, and Mark packs a whole region into it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they land"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They crossed over and came to land at **Gennesaret** and tied up the boat there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had spent the night on the lake in a storm, and Jesus had walked out to them on the water. See the note on Mark 6:45–52."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the morning after."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Recognised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the moment they stepped out, people recognized Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No announcement and no preaching. He is identified getting off a boat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the region did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **ran all through the countryside** and began **carrying the sick to Him on their mats**, wherever they heard He was.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whole villages mobilised. People are carried on the pallets they were lying on, over open country, to intercept a man who kept moving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Every place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every **village**, every **town**, every **farm** He came to.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark lists three sizes of settlement to say the same thing three ways: everywhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they put them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **laid the sick out in the marketplaces**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The market was the one place in a village everyone passed through. They put the sick in the road He would have to walk down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **begged Him to let them touch even the edge of His clothes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kraspedon** — the tassel on the corner of the garment, the **tzitzit** commanded in Numbers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are asking for the least possible contact: not His hand, not a word, not a visit to the house. The hem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they got the idea"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters earlier a woman had done exactly this in a crowd and been healed, and Jesus had stopped the procession to find out who it was and tell her *your faith has made you well.* See the note on Mark 5:25–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had been meant to stay anonymous. Because He would not let her, a whole district now knows what to reach for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **everyone who touched Him was made well**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **esozonto** — imperfect, continuous: they *were being* made whole, one after another, as long as it went on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark records no exceptions and no failures in this passage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast a page earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In his own town, *He could do no mighty work there, except that He laid His hands on a few sick people and healed them. And He marvelled because of their unbelief.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same chapter. Nazareth got a few; Gennesaret got everyone who touched Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He had just done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fed five thousand men with five loaves, sent the disciples ahead, prayed alone on a mountain, walked on the sea, and now this. Mark 6 has no gaps in it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was on offer.** Not a service, not a queue, not a set of conditions. A man walking through a marketplace with people lying along the edges of it, reaching for a tassel."}]}, "Mark 6:7–13": {"title": "Sent out with a stick", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Twelve men are sent out with an inventory of what they may not take."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two by two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Began sending them out **in pairs**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody goes alone. The law required two witnesses, and the practice runs right through Acts — Peter and John, Paul and Barnabas, Barnabas and Mark, Paul and Silas."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He gave them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gave them **authority over unclean spirits**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole equipment list on the credit side. One item, and it is not material."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they may take"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Take **nothing for the road but a walking stick** — no bread, no traveling bag, no coins tucked in your belt. **Wear your sandals**, and **do not carry a second shirt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A stick and the sandals on their feet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four things forbidden: food, luggage, money, and a change of clothes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of them is what a sensible traveller packs. The instruction removes any possibility of self-sufficiency for the length of the journey."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stick and the sandals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are allowed to keep what they are already holding and already wearing. Nothing is to be acquired for the trip."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The house"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whatever house you go into, **stay there until you leave that town**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No moving to better accommodation. Whoever takes you in first, you stay with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It rules out working up through a village's households, and it puts them at the mercy of the first person who says yes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dust"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If there are any who will not welcome you… **shake the dust off your feet** as a witness against them.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Jewish traveller shook off Gentile dust on re-entering the land. The gesture said: your ground is not ours."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are told to do it to Jewish towns."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they preached"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They went out and **called people to change their minds and turn to God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **metanoeo** — to think differently afterwards."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **drove out many demons**, and they **rubbed oil on many who were sick and healed them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only place in the Gospels where the disciples use oil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is **aleipho** — the everyday word for rubbing oil on, as you would after a bath or onto a wound. Not the ceremonial anointing word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James gives the same instruction to elders: *let them pray over him and put oil on him in the name of the Lord.* Same verb. See the note on James 5:13–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened when they got back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The apostles gathered to Jesus and told Him all things, both what they had done and what they had taught.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And His response: *come aside by yourselves to a deserted place and rest a while* — *for there were many coming and going, and they did not even have time to eat.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The debrief is a rest."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He said about the sending later.** *\"When I sent you without money bag, knapsack and sandals, **did you lack anything?**\" So they said, \"Nothing.\"* Luke 22, on the last night, and He asks them to check their own record."}]}, "Mark 7:24–30": {"title": "\"The scraps the children drop\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Mark's version of the Syrophoenician woman, and he keeps details Matthew leaves out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why He was there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He went into a house and **did not want anyone to know He was there, but there was no hiding Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only Mark records this. He had crossed the border into Gentile territory to get away from the crowds, and it did not work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who she was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **Greek, born in Syrian Phoenicia**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark identifies her by culture and birthplace. Matthew calls her a Canaanite — the old covenant word, the nation Israel was told to drive out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between them: a pagan by religion, a Greek by culture, a Canaanite by ancestry. Every category that excludes her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She **came and fell at His feet**… and **pleaded** with Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *pleaded* is imperfect — she kept on asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let the children at the table be **fed first**. It is not right to take **the children's bread** and throw it to the little dogs.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark alone keeps **first**. It is a word about order, not exclusion — and she hears it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Little dogs** — the Greek **kunarion** is a diminutive: the household pets, not the scavengers in the street. It is a word from inside a house."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Yes, Lord** — and even **the little dogs under the table** eat the scraps the children drop.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She concedes everything. She does not argue with the order, the priority, or the term."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And she picks up the household picture and finishes it: *under the table.* If the dogs are indoors, then she is talking about a place at the family's feet, and food that is already falling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**For an answer like that**, you may go home.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *because of this word* — because of what you said."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark's version credits the reply. Matthew's credits the faith: *O woman, great is your faith.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The healing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The spirit has left your daughter.**\" And she went back and found her child lying on the bed, and the spirit was gone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past tense before she moves. It was done while she was still standing there, and the child was miles away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before this in Mark"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole first half of chapter 7 is the argument about clean and unclean — washing hands, traditions, and Jesus' verdict: *there is nothing that enters a man from outside which can defile him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark adds his own note: *thus He declared all foods clean.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, in the very next paragraph, He crosses into unclean territory and heals an unclean woman's daughter of an unclean spirit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark puts the sermon and the demonstration side by side."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He does next.** Goes further into Gentile country, to the Decapolis, and heals a deaf man with an impediment — *Ephphatha, be opened.* Then feeds four thousand people there. The bread the children could not finish is being handed out across the border within two chapters."}]}, "Mark 8:1–10": {"title": "The second feeding, and the question they should not have asked", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four thousand people, seven loaves, and disciples who have already watched this happen once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What starts it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have compassion for the crowd, because they have already been with Me three days and have nothing to eat.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He raises it, not them. And the reason given is the length of time they have stayed — three days without provisions, some of them a long way from home."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If I send them home hungry, they will collapse on the way.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where could anyone get enough bread to feed these people out here in this lonely place?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been present at the five thousand. Mark has told that story two chapters earlier, with twelve baskets left over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He records the question without comment, which is more damning than an explanation would be."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The differences from the first feeding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark keeps the numbers distinct, and they are not the same story told twice:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "| | five thousand | four thousand | |---|---|---| | loaves | five | seven | | baskets left | twelve, *kophinoi* | seven, *spyrides* | | location | Jewish side of the lake | Gentile region, the Decapolis |"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Even the word for basket changes. The second is the large hamper — the kind Paul was later lowered over a wall in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the Gentile setting matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the region where the man freed from the legion had been telling people what the Lord had done for him. See the note on Mark 5:18–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd that stays three days to listen is in the country that once asked Jesus to leave."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Jesus says about it later.** In the boat He asks them: when I broke five loaves for five thousand, how many baskets? Twelve. And the seven for four thousand? Seven. *Do you still not understand?* He treats the two feedings as a single lesson they had failed twice."}]}, "Mark 8:31–33": {"title": "He looked at His disciples", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The sharpest thing He ever said to a man, and Mark records where He was looking when He said it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But who do you say that I am?\" Peter answered and said to Him, \"**You are the Christ**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The high point of the first half of Mark's Gospel, and it is Peter who says it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six days from here is the transfiguration, and Peter is on that mountain too."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **He began to teach them plainly** that the Son of Man had to suffer a great deal.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **erxato didaskein** — He began to teach. Mark marks it as a new phase."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Up to this point He has spoken in parables and told people to say nothing. From the confession onward, He teaches this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four things"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Suffer a great deal, be **rejected by the elders and the chief priests and the teachers of the law**, be **killed**, and **after three days rise again**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three groups named are the Sanhedrin — the whole council, itemised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The resurrection is in the sentence. It is always in the sentence, in all three predictions, and nobody in the story ever reacts to that half."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The manner"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He said it **openly, holding nothing back**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parresia** — plainly, with freedom of speech, in public."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark uses the word once in his Gospel, here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Peter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Peter **took Him aside** and began to **argue Him out of it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **proslabomenos** — taking Him to himself, taking Him by the arm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Epitiman** — to rebuke. The same verb Mark uses of Jesus silencing demons and stilling the storm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter rebukes Him. Matthew records the words: *far be it from You, Lord; this shall not happen to You!*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he does it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just called Him the Christ, and the Christ was not supposed to be executed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's objection follows directly from his confession. He is right about the title and wrong about the job."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The look"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Jesus **turned around, looked at His disciples**, and spoke sharply to Peter.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epistrapheis kai idon tous mathetas autou**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter had taken Him aside. Jesus turns back to the group before answering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark alone records the look. Matthew has the rebuke without it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the look does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The correction is spoken to Peter, in front of the others, because the others are thinking it too."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter said out loud what eleven men were assuming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get behind Me, Satan**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hupage opiso mou, Satana**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word He used in the wilderness — *away with you, Satan* — when the offer was the kingdoms of the world without a cross. See the note on Matthew 4:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same offer, from a friend."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Your mind is not on the things of God at all. It is on the things of men**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ou phroneis** — you are not minding, not setting your thinking on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *you are evil*. Your mind is set on the human side of the question — which is where anybody's would be."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whoever desires to come after Me, let him deny himself, and take up his cross, and follow Me**.\"* See the note on Mark 8:34–38."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter tried to keep Him off the cross and is told about carrying one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Peter ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I do not know this Man of whom you speak.\"* And the rooster, and the look. See the note on Luke 22:54–62."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the Lord turned and looked at Peter.* The same verb, the same evangelists' interest in where He was looking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But go, tell His disciples — and Peter** — that He is going before you into Galilee.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark's angel names him separately."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Mark adds.** The turn. Peter took Him aside to say it privately; He turned back to the group before He answered, because the correction was not only Peter's."}]}, "Mark 8:34–38": {"title": "\"What could a man possibly offer in exchange for his soul?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus calls the crowd over — not just the disciples — and says the hardest thing in Mark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter has just confessed Him as the Messiah, and then rebuked Him for saying He would be killed. Jesus has answered that sharply."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then He calls the crowd, so that what follows is said to everybody rather than to the twelve."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Pick up his cross\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image was not yet a metaphor. Crucifixion was a Roman execution his hearers had seen, and a condemned man carried the crossbeam to the place through public streets."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone in that crowd knew what a man carrying a cross was: someone already sentenced, walking to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exchange"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever tries to hold on to his life will lose it, and whoever gives up his life for My sake and for the sake of the good news will save it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for *life* here is **psyche** — life, self, soul. The same word runs through the next two sentences, which is why they belong together:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What good does it do a man to gain the whole world and lose his own soul? What could a man possibly offer in exchange for his soul?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commercial language"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Gain*, *lose*, *exchange* — the vocabulary is a merchant's, and the question is about price."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second question is the sharper one. Suppose the trade has already been made and the world is in hand: what is left to buy the soul back with? The answer the question expects is nothing."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Whose loss is being described.** *For My sake and for the sake of the good news.* Jesus does not commend self-denial in general — Mark's Gospel is not asking for austerity. The letting go is for a person, and He names Himself."}]}, "Mark 9:14–24": {"title": "\"Help me with the part of me that does not\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most honest sentence anyone says in the Gospels, and Jesus answers it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The father's careful wording"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If there is anything You can do, have pity on us and help us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has been living with this for years — *since he was small* — and he has just watched the disciples fail. The *if* is not rudeness. It is a man who has stopped expecting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Notice also *help **us***. He is not only asking for the boy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does with the \"if\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you can believe — everything is possible for the one who believes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He picks up the father's own word and hands it back. The man had put the *if* on Jesus' ability; Jesus moves it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is worth reading carefully, because it can be made cruel. Jesus is not saying the healing depends on the father generating enough certainty — the next thirty seconds rule that out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lord, I do believe. Help me with the part of me that does not.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves at once, and the man does not pretend. He has faith and he has unbelief, and he says so, and he asks for help with the second."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus heals the boy immediately. No further requirement, no waiting for the doubt to clear."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What that settles.** Whatever \"believing\" means in this passage, it evidently includes a man who admits mid-sentence that he does not fully believe. The faith that works here is not a quantity achieved. It is a request made to the right person, doubt and all."}]}, "Matthew 1:1–3": {"title": "The family line", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The New Testament opens with a list, and the fourth name in it is a woman with a scandal attached."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is **the family line of Jesus the Messiah, the son of David, the son of Abraham**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **biblos geneseos** — the book of the genealogy, or the book of the origin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase the Septuagint uses at Genesis 5: *this is the book of the generations of Adam*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew opens the New Testament with the words that open a section of the first book of the Old."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Son of David.** **Son of Abraham.**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David gives the throne — the covenant that a descendant would reign forever."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham gives the promise that *in your seed all the nations of the earth shall be blessed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Kingship and universality, in the first line, in that order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order they are named in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David before Abraham, which is backwards chronologically."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew leads with the throne, because the argument of his Gospel is that this is the king."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The form"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abraham was the father of Isaac. Isaac was the father of Jacob.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek **egennesen** — begot — repeated forty times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the standard Hebrew genealogical formula, and its rhythm is the point. A list of fathers, each handing on to the next."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The break"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Judah was the father of Perez and Zerah, **and their mother was Tamar**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first woman in the list, and Matthew has gone out of his way to include her. Genealogies did not name mothers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Tamar was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah's daughter-in-law. Twice widowed, denied the third son she was owed, she dressed as a prostitute, sat at the roadside, and conceived twins by her father-in-law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She has been more righteous than I*, Judah says, when the evidence is produced. See the note on Genesis 38:24–26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Rahab** — the prostitute of Jericho who hid the spies."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ruth** — a Moabitess, from a nation barred from the assembly to the tenth generation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Solomon by **her who had been the wife of Uriah***. Matthew does not write Bathsheba's name. He writes the name of the man David had killed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four women in a royal genealogy, and all four have irregularity attached."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they might be there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of them is either a foreigner or the centre of a scandal, or both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew is introducing a king whose line runs through Tamar at the roadside and Rahab on the wall."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The structure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So all the generations from Abraham to David are **fourteen** generations, from David until the captivity in Babylon are **fourteen**, and from the captivity to the Christ are **fourteen**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew says so himself. He has arranged it, and he tells the reader he has — several names are omitted to make the count work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The numerical value of the Hebrew letters in **David** is fourteen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last link"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jacob begot Joseph **the husband of Mary**, of whom was born Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The formula breaks at the end. Not *Joseph begot Jesus*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek relative pronoun is feminine singular — **ex hes**, of whom — pointing to Mary alone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty repetitions of *begot*, and the chain does not close the way it has closed forty times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And you shall call His name **JESUS**, for He will save His people from their sins.\"* See the note on Matthew 1:20–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yeshua** — the LORD saves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke does instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A different genealogy, running the other direction, back past Abraham to *Adam, the son of God*. See the note on Luke 3:23–38."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew stops at Abraham. Luke does not stop."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The break in the formula.** Forty times *begot*, and then not. The relative pronoun at the end is feminine, and it points at one person."}]}, "Matthew 1:12–16": {"title": "The one link in the chain that is worded differently", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Thirty-nine times in this chapter Matthew writes the same formula. On the fortieth he changes it, and the change is the point of the whole genealogy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Shealtiel was the father of Zerubbabel. Zerubbabel was the father of Abiud. Abiud was the father of Eliakim…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Down through the list, one word doing the work each time — the ordinary verb for begetting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The break"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then:"}, {"k": "note", "t": "*Jacob was the father of Joseph, **the husband of Mary**, and it was from Mary that Jesus was born, the One who is called the Messiah.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph is not said to be the father of Jesus. He is identified by his marriage, and the birth is attributed to Mary. In Greek the relative pronoun before *from whom* is feminine singular — it can only refer to her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew has just spent forty-one verses establishing a legal line and then, at the last link, declines to say the thing the whole form was built to say."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the legal line still matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus needed a claim to David's throne, and in that culture it came through the legal father. Joseph, descended from David through Solomon and the kings, gives Him that claim by adoption."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What Joseph does not give Him is blood — which is exactly as well, because that line runs through Jeconiah, and Jeremiah had declared that none of Jeconiah's descendants would ever sit on David's throne. See the note on Jeremiah 22:28–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The title comes down the legal line. The life does not."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who else is in the list.** Four women are named before Mary, and Matthew did not have to name any of them: Tamar, who posed as a prostitute; Rahab, who was one; Ruth, a Moabite; and *the wife of Uriah*, whom he pointedly does not call Bathsheba. A genealogy designed to impress would have left all four out. This one puts them in."}]}, "Matthew 1:20–21": {"title": "You are to give Him the name", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man decides how to handle a scandal quietly, and is interrupted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had decided"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joseph her husband, being **a just man**, and not wanting to make her a public example, **was minded to put her away secretly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dikaios** — righteous. And **lathra** — privately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Betrothal in that culture was legally binding; ending it required a divorce. The law permitted a public accusation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is described as righteous, and the righteous act he settles on is the quiet one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But while he was **turning all of this over in his mind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **enthumethentos** — pondering, deliberating."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angel arrives after the decision and before the action."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Joseph, son of David**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is how nobody has addressed a village carpenter in his life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's genealogy has just traced the line through David to this man. The angel uses the title the genealogy established."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reassurance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not be afraid to take Mary home as your wife**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me phobethes** — do not fear."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was not afraid of scandal; he had already planned around it. What is being addressed is whether he may go through with the marriage at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The explanation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The child within her was conceived by the Holy Spirit**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ek Pneumatos estin Hagiou** — is of the Holy Spirit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew tells the reader this in verse 18. Joseph is told it in verse 20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"She will give birth to a son, and **you are to give Him the name Jesus**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Naming a child was the father's act, and it was the legal acknowledgment of paternity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph is asked to name a son who is not his, which is precisely how the boy becomes son of David."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Iesous** — the Greek form of **Yeshua**, a shortened **Yehoshua**: *the LORD saves*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A common name. There are several men called Jesus in Josephus, and one in Colossians — *Jesus who is called Justus*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because **He will save His people from their sins**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sosei ton laon autou apo ton hamartion auton**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the reason narrows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His people* — which a first-century hearer would take as Israel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From their sins* — which is not what anybody in Israel was expecting to be saved from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The expectation was Rome. The angel names something else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name Matthew adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**And they shall call His name Immanuel**,\" which is translated, \"**God with us**.\"* See the note on Matthew 1:22–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Immanu-El** — with us, God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two names in three verses. One is what He will do. The other is who He is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Joseph does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Joseph, being aroused from sleep, did as the angel of the Lord commanded him and **took to him his wife**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No recorded words. Joseph does not speak anywhere in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He obeys four times in two chapters, each time after a dream: take her, go to Egypt, come back, turn aside to Galilee."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke records instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gabriel to Mary: *you will conceive in your womb and bring forth a Son, and shall call His name Jesus*, and her answer, *let it be to me according to your word*. See the note on Luke 1:30–33."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both parents are told the name separately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other thing Matthew says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he **did not know her till she had brought forth her firstborn Son. And he called His name Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last four words are the obedience."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The naming.** It was the father's legal act, the acknowledgment of a son. Joseph is told to perform it over a child that is not his, and that is how the line reaches David."}]}, "Matthew 1:22–23": {"title": "God with us", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Matthew stops the narrative to say that this is the thing that was promised."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The formula"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All of this happened **so that what the Lord had spoken through the prophet would come true**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hina plerothe** — that it might be fulfilled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew uses this construction more than a dozen times. It is his running argument: the story he is telling is the one already written down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Through the prophet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dia tou prophetou** — *through* the prophet, and **hupo Kuriou** — *by* the Lord."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The distinction is deliberate. The Lord spoke; the prophet was the channel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The virgin will be with child and will give birth to a son, and they will call His name Immanuel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 7:14, given to King Ahaz when Jerusalem was under threat from a northern alliance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **almah** — a young woman of marriageable age."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Septuagint, translated by Jewish scholars two centuries before Christ, rendered it **parthenos** — virgin. Matthew quotes their reading."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sign was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahaz was offered a sign *in the depth or in the height above*, and refused it: *I will not ask, nor will I test the LORD*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore the Lord Himself will give you a sign.* The sign is given to a king who declined one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Immanu-El** — with us, God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two Hebrew words. **Immanu** — with us. **El** — God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word order in Hebrew puts *with us* first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Matthew's translation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That name means \"**God with us**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He translates it, which tells you his readers included people who did not read Hebrew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 21: *you shall call His name Jesus, for He will save His people from their sins*. See the note on Matthew 1:20–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 23: *they shall call His name Immanuel*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One is what He does. The other is who He is. Matthew puts them three verses apart and does not reconcile them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who calls Him what"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You* shall call Him Jesus — Joseph, individually."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They* shall call Him Immanuel — a plural, unspecified."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in the Gospels ever addresses Him as Immanuel. The name is a description, not a form of address."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Matthew ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lo, **I am with you always**, even to the end of the age.\"* See the note on Matthew 28:16–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ego meth' humon eimi** — I am with you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Gospel opens with *God with us* and closes with *I am with you*, and the whole book is between them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah says three verses after the sign"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Take counsel together, but it will come to nothing; speak the word, but it will not stand, **for God is with us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew there is **immanu El** — the same two words, used as a sentence rather than a name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the tent ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And I heard a loud voice from heaven saying, \"Behold, **the tabernacle of God is with men, and He will dwell with them**.\"* See the note on Revelation 21:3–4."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two names.** *Jesus* — what He will do. *Immanuel* — who He is. Matthew states both in four verses and lets them stand side by side."}]}, "Matthew 10:16–20": {"title": "Snakes and doves", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two animals, chosen for one quality each, and neither is chosen for the obvious reason."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pairing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be as wise as snakes and as harmless as doves.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the snake** — not for cunning in the sinister sense. The word is *phronimos*, shrewd, practically intelligent, the same word Jesus uses of the wise man who built on rock and the wise servant left in charge.", "**the dove** — the word is *akeraios*, literally *unmixed*. Pure in the sense of nothing added, no hidden second motive."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the instruction is: think clearly about what you are walking into, and have nothing in you that needs concealing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either quality without the other goes wrong quickly. Shrewd without unmixed is just cunning; unmixed without shrewd is walking into a wolf pack blindfolded — which is what He has just said He is sending them into."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sending"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look at what I am doing: I am sending you out like sheep into the middle of wolves.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the danger before He gives the instruction, and He does not pretend the odds are good. Sheep among wolves is not a fair fight, and nothing in the sentence suggests otherwise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He promises instead of safety"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not fret about how to speak or what to say. What you are to say will be given to you at that hour, because it is not you speaking; it is the Spirit of your Father speaking through you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The provision is not protection from the councils and the floggings — He says plainly those are coming. It is words, supplied at the hour they are needed, by Someone else."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The turn in the middle.** *That will be your chance to testify.* Being dragged in front of governors and kings is described as an opportunity rather than a disaster. The whole passage assumes the trouble and then repurposes it."}]}, "Matthew 10:32–39": {"title": "A sword, not peace", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of the mission charge, and it is the hardest paragraph in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has sent the twelve out with instructions: no gold, no bag, no extra tunic, stay in worthy houses, shake the dust off unresponsive towns."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And warnings: *behold, I send you out as sheep in the midst of wolves*. See the note on Matthew 10:16–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not fear those who kill the body but cannot kill the soul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Are not two sparrows sold for a copper coin? And not one of them falls to the ground apart from your Father's will… **you are of more value than many sparrows**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The confession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whoever owns Me in front of others, I will own that person in front of My Father** in heaven.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **homologesei en emoi** — confesses in Me. An unusual construction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But whoever **disowns Me in front of others, I will disown** in front of My Father.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb both ways, and the second is the one Peter does in a courtyard. See the note on Matthew 26:69–75."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter is standing in this room hearing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not imagine that I came to bring peace to the earth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me nomisete** — do not suppose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He heads off an assumption, which means it was being made."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angels had announced *on earth peace, goodwill toward men*, and Isaiah had called Him *Prince of Peace*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sword"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I did not come to bring peace, but a sword**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's version has *division* where Matthew has *sword*, which is the interpretation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sword is not something He wields. It is what happens when He is introduced into a household."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The households"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I came to turn **a man against his father, a daughter against her mother, a daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is quoting Micah, almost exactly: *for son dishonours father, daughter rises against her mother, daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law; **a man's enemies are the men of his own house**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Micah is describing a society in collapse. Jesus applies it to what following Him will cost."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because the confession in verse 32 has to be made somewhere, and the first place it is heard is usually at home."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In a first-century Jewish or Roman household, that decision was not private. It affected the family's standing, its trade and its worship."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Anyone who loves father or mother more than Me is not worthy of Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **huper eme** — above Me, more than Me."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *instead of*. The claim is comparative and it is placed above the fifth commandment's object."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in that culture could hear it as anything but enormous."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cross"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **whoever does not take up his cross and walk behind Me** is not worthy of Me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first mention of a cross in Matthew, and it comes long before His own."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The disciples hearing it knew what a man carrying a crossbeam was walking toward. They did not yet know He would."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The paradox"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whoever clings to his life will lose it, and whoever lets his life go for My sake will find it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records this twice — here, and after Peter is called Satan. See the note on Matthew 16:24–28."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He who receives you receives Me… and **whoever gives one of these little ones only a cup of cold water** in the name of a disciple, assuredly, I say to you, he shall by no means lose his reward.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "After the sword and the households and the cross: a cup of water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter ends on the smallest possible act."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the sword is.** Not something He carries. It is what the confession in verse 32 does when it is first made — and the first room it is made in is usually the family's."}]}, "Matthew 10:5–10": {"title": "You received it as a gift", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first sending, and it has a boundary on it that will not survive the resurrection."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sending"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These twelve Jesus sent out, and He gave them their instructions.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apesteilen** — sent, from which **apostolos**, one sent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter opens with the twelve named, and then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The restriction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not take the road to the Gentiles**, and **do not enter any Samaritan town**. **Go instead to the lost sheep of the house of Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two prohibitions and one direction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it of Himself later, to the Canaanite woman: *I was not sent except to the lost sheep of the house of Israel*. See the note on Matthew 15:21–28."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul states the order: *to the Jew first and also to the Greek*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The restriction is about sequence, and the sequence is stated as temporary by everything that follows the resurrection."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is lifted later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go therefore and make disciples of all the nations**.\"* See the note on Matthew 28:16–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Panta ta ethne** — all the nations. The exact word that was forbidden in chapter 10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in Acts: *you shall be witnesses to Me in Jerusalem, and in all Judea and **Samaria**, and to the end of the earth*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both of the two forbidden categories are named in the commission."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The message"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As you go, say this: '**The kingdom of heaven has come near**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **engiken** — has drawn near and stands near. Perfect tense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same sentence John the Baptist preached, and the same one Jesus preached."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four instructions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Heal the sick, raise the dead, make lepers clean, drive out demons**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four imperatives, and the second is not a metaphor."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are given what He has been doing, in the same order of difficulty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You received it as a gift; give it as a gift**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dorean elabete, dorean dote** — freely you received, freely give."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dorean** — as a gift, without payment, gratis. It is the word Paul uses of justification: *being justified **freely** by His grace*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in Greek, repeated. The construction is deliberately identical on both sides."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The packing list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not pack gold or silver or copper in your belts**, no travel bag, no second shirt, no spare sandals, no walking stick.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three metals, descending in value — they are not to carry the cheap coins either."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Pera** — a travel bag, the sort a beggar or a wandering philosopher carried for collections."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the list rules out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Self-sufficiency, and also the appearance of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The travelling teachers of the period carried a bag and a staff. The twelve are sent without the kit that identified the profession."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The worker deserves to be fed**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **axios gar ho ergates tes trophes autou** — worthy is the worker of his food."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's version has *his wages*, and Paul quotes it as Scripture alongside Deuteronomy. See the note on 1 Timothy 5:17–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arrangement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They give without charge and are fed by those they go to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not payment for the message — the next verses set out how they are to find a worthy household and stay there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He tells them to expect"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Behold, I send you out as sheep in the midst of wolves**. Therefore be wise as serpents and harmless as doves.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You will be hated by all for My name's sake.\"* See the note on Matthew 10:16–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The commission and the warning are in the same paragraph."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two words.** **Dorean… dorean** — as a gift, as a gift. The same term on both sides of the sentence, which is why the packing list follows it."}]}, "Matthew 11:1–6": {"title": "John's question from prison", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The man who identified Him sends to ask whether he got it right."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is asking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **John, sitting in prison**, heard about the things the Messiah was doing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is John the Baptist. He had seen the Spirit descend and remain, and had said *behold, the Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world.* He had refused to baptise Him at first."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is in Herod's fortress at Machaerus, and he will be beheaded there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Are You the One who was coming, or should we keep watching for someone else?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not asking a general question about the Messiah. He is asking about the man he pointed at."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records it without any embarrassment and without any explanation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he might ask"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had preached a Messiah with an axe and a winnowing fan: *the axe is laid to the root of the trees… He will thoroughly clean out His threshing floor, and burn up the chaff with unquenchable fire.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "What he is hearing about instead is healings and preaching in villages, and no axe anywhere — and he is in a cell."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go back and tell John what you are hearing and seeing.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a rebuke, not a reassurance, and not a direct yes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Evidence, and John is left to draw the conclusion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*blind people are seeing*", "*crippled people are walking*", "*lepers are being made clean*", "*deaf people are hearing*", "*dead people are being raised*", "*and **the poor are having the good news brought to them***"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a quotation. Isaiah 35 and Isaiah 61, stitched together — the passages that describe what happens when God comes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus answers a question about His identity by listing what is going on and letting the prophets do the identifying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not on the list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The axe. The fire. The threshing floor."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And one more thing: Isaiah 61 says *to proclaim liberty to the captives, and the opening of the prison to those who are bound* — and Jesus had read that scroll aloud in Nazareth. He leaves that clause out of the message He sends to a man in prison."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John is not getting out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **blessed is the one who does not stumble over Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **skandalizo** — to be tripped up, to take offence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a beatitude, and it is addressed to a man whose expectations were not being met and who was going to die in a cell."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus says about him afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The moment the messengers leave, He turns to the crowd:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed shaken by the wind?… Among those born of women there has not risen one greater than John the Baptist.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one word of criticism, said behind his back, about a man who had just asked whether He was real."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word for the doubt.** John does not stop believing in God. He asks a question, in the dark, of the person he is asking about — and gets an answer, and a character reference he never heard."}]}, "Matthew 11:12–15": {"title": "\"Right up to John\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus places John at a boundary and says which side of it everything falls on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The occasion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John is in Herod's prison and has sent two disciples to ask *are You the Coming One, or do we look for another?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus answers by listing what is happening — the blind see, the lame walk, the poor have the gospel preached to them — and then turns to the crowd and speaks about John."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pressing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"From the days of John the Baptist until this very hour, **the kingdom of heaven has been advancing under force, and forceful people are seizing hold of it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **biazetai** and **biastai** — words about violence and force. They can be read as the kingdom suffering violence or as it forcefully advancing; the grammar allows both, and readers have divided over it since the beginning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is not in doubt is the timing: **from the days of John**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The boundary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**All the Prophets and the Law spoke their prophecies right up to John**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *prophesied until John*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's version is blunter: *the law and the prophets were until John. **Since that time the kingdom of God has been preached**, and everyone is pressing into it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both put John at the join."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that makes John"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last man on one side of the line. Jesus says it two verses earlier: *among those born of women there has not risen one greater than John the Baptist* — and then, immediately, *but he who is least in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The greatest of the old order, and smaller than the least of the new."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The identification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **if you are willing to receive it, he is the Elijah who was to come**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Malachi's last words before four hundred years of silence: *behold, I will send you Elijah the prophet before the coming of the great and dreadful day of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The qualification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If you are willing to receive it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus attaches a condition to His own identification."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John had denied it himself when the priests asked: *\"Are you Elijah?\" He said, \"I am not.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angel had told Zacharias he would go *in the spirit and power of Elijah*, which is the resolution — not the man, the office."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The closing formula"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whoever has ears, let him listen**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses it after the parables and Revelation uses it to each of the seven churches."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It marks something that will not be understood by everyone hearing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Elijah stands with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On the mountain, eight days after the first announcement of the cross, Moses and Elijah appear and talk about the departure. See the note on Luke 9:28–36."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law and the prophets, in person, discussing the thing that ends their era."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And when the cloud lifts, *Jesus was there alone*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the line is drawn.** *The Law and the Prophets… right up to John.* Everything before is one thing, and Jesus says a man alive in the same generation was standing at the end of it."}]}, "Matthew 11:20–24": {"title": "The towns that saw the most", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus names three towns and says they are worse off than the cities everyone in His audience would have named as the worst."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Chorazin and Bethsaida** measured against **Tyre and Sidon** — the Phoenician cities the prophets denounced for centuries", "**Capernaum** measured against **Sodom**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in each case the verdict runs the same way: those places would have turned if they had seen what these towns saw."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Capernaum had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was His base of operations. Peter's house was there, and the synagogue. Matthew was called at its tax booth. The centurion's servant, Peter's mother-in-law, Jairus' daughter — the concentration of miracles in that one town is remarkable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You were lifted up to the sky* is a quotation aimed originally at the king of Babylon in Isaiah 14, and the second half of the sentence comes with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had not done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because they had not changed their minds.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not because they attacked Him, and not because they drove Him out — Nazareth did that and is not on this list. The charge is that nothing changed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They watched, and stayed the same."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle underneath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "More evidence means more responsibility. Jesus says it elsewhere in plainer terms — to whom much is given, much will be required."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That cuts toward anyone with easy access to Scripture, sermons, and a Bible on their phone."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What surrounds this passage.** Immediately before, John the Baptist's question and Jesus' defence of him. Immediately after, *come to Me, all of you who are worn out* — the gentlest invitation in the Gospels. The hardest words Matthew records and the kindest are four verses apart, spoken in the same breath. See the note on Matthew 11:28–30."}]}, "Matthew 11:25–27": {"title": "Hidden from the clever", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A prayer said out loud, and it is the most striking thing Jesus says about Himself outside John."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At that time.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately after the woes on the cities where most of His miracles had been done — Chorazin, Bethsaida, Capernaum. *If the mighty works which were done in you had been done in Sodom, it would have remained until this day.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer follows a paragraph about rejection."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I thank You, Father, Lord of heaven and earth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exomologoumai** — I acknowledge fully, I confess, I praise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two titles in one breath: Father, and Lord of heaven and earth. The intimate one and the cosmic one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The content"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That You have **hidden these things from the clever and the well-schooled** and have **shown them to little children**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sophon kai suneton** — the wise and the intelligent. And **nepiois** — infants, the very young."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He thanks God for a concealment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being hidden"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These things* — the identity of the Son, which is what the next two verses are about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Yes, Father, **because this is what pleased You**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eudokia** — good pleasure, what seemed good."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No further explanation is offered. Jesus does not argue that the clever deserved it or that the children earned it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does with the same idea"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where is the wise? Where is the scribe? Where is the disputer of this age? Has not God made foolish the wisdom of this world?… **God has chosen the foolish things of the world to put to shame the wise**.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 1:18–25."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *not many wise according to the flesh, not many mighty, not many noble, are called*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The handover"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Everything has been handed over to Me by My Father**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **panta moi paredothe** — all things were delivered to Me."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb used of Judas handing Him over, and of the Father delivering Him up for us all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The knowing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No one really knows the Son except the Father, and no one really knows the Father except the Son**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epiginoskei** — knows fully, thoroughly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A closed circle, stated in both directions. It is the kind of sentence John's Gospel is full of and the other three are not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **anyone the Son chooses to show Him to**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **boletai apokalupsai** — wills to reveal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The circle has one door and the Son holds it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Come to Me, all you who labour and are heavy laden, and I will give you rest**. Take My yoke upon you and learn from Me, for I am gentle and lowly in heart, and you will find rest for your souls. For My yoke is easy and My burden is light.\"* See the note on Matthew 11:28–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The invitation comes directly after the sentence about the closed circle. The revealing He mentions in verse 27 is issued as an invitation in verse 28."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the terms are not intellectual. *All you who labour and are heavy laden.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the infants are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not literal children, though He uses them as an object lesson elsewhere: *unless you are converted and become as little children, you will by no means enter the kingdom of heaven*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word describes people with no standing to bring — which is the qualification the invitation asks for."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He thanks God for.** The hiding. And then, two sentences later, He opens it to anyone who is tired."}]}, "Matthew 11:28–30": {"title": "Whose yoke it is, and what \"easy\" actually says", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted invitation in the Gospels, and the picture in the middle of it is the part most likely to be missed by a reader who has never worked an animal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "A yoke is for two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **zygos** is the wooden beam across the necks of a pair of oxen. A single ox does not wear one. The whole point of the device is that two animals pull together and the load is shared between them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So *take My yoke on your shoulders* is not an offer of a lighter burden to carry alone. It is an offer to be paired with Him. That is why the sentence can hold two things that sound contradictory — come to Me and rest, and take up a yoke — without any strain. The rest is not the absence of work. It is the end of pulling alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"My yoke\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The emphasis in the Greek falls on the possessive, and it matters, because everybody He was speaking to already had a yoke on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rabbis of the period spoke of the *yoke of the law* — the teaching a disciple took on from his teacher. Jesus is not asking people to pick up a burden they had been free of. He is telling them to swap."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is also why the next line is *learn from Me*. It is the language of apprenticeship, and the reason He gives for coming is not that He is powerful: *because I am gentle, and I am humble in heart*. He recommends Himself on His manner, which is not how anyone recruits disciples."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word behind \"fits easily\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chrestos.** It means kind, good, serviceable — and it was used of things that were well made, and of wine that had mellowed. In the Greek Old Testament it is the word in *taste and see that the LORD is good*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Translating it as *easy* has done some damage, because it sounds like a promise that nothing will be demanding. The word is nearer to *this one fits*. A badly made yoke rubs an animal raw over a day's work. A well-made one is shaped to the neck wearing it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The claim is not that there is no work. It is that this yoke will not take the skin off you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is invited"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All of you who are worn out and carrying too much.* The two words describe being exhausted by labour and being loaded up by someone else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no other entry condition in the sentence. Not repentance first, not understanding first. Tiredness is the qualification, which is a strange basis for an invitation and is exactly what makes this one memorable."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where else this word turns up.** *Rest* here is the same idea running through Hebrews 3 and 4 — a rest already prepared, entered by coming rather than by achieving. Matthew's version puts a person and a yoke at the centre of it: the rest is not a place so much as a partnership."}]}, "Matthew 12:15–21": {"title": "The bruised reed", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The Pharisees have just decided to destroy Him. Matthew's next sentence is what Jesus does about it, and then the longest Old Testament quotation in his Gospel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He withdrew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Knowing this, Jesus **withdrew** from that place. Large crowds followed Him, and **He healed all of them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not confront the plot and He does not stop healing. He moves, and the healing goes with Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All of them* — Matthew's habit. He does not report a percentage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the quotation is here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew reaches for Isaiah 42 to explain a man walking away from a fight, and the prophecy he chooses is about a servant who does not raise His voice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the servant does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will not argue or shout. No one will hear His voice raised in the streets.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three negatives before a single positive. The portrait is drawn by what is absent from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reed and the wick"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **bruised reed** He will not break, and a **smoldering wick** He will not put out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A reed grew by the Jordan and was used for measuring, for writing, and cut into a simple pipe. Bruised, it is worthless — you throw it away and cut another, because reeds cost nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A wick with no flame left is smoke and smell. You pinch it out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both images are of something that has stopped doing the one thing it exists for, and is worth less than the effort of keeping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither is discarded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Until"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*…**until He carries justice through to victory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gentleness has a destination. It is not resignation or a mild temperament; it is restraint held on purpose, and the sentence says where it is going."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for *justice* in Isaiah is **mishpat** — the right ordering of things, what ought to be."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And in His name the nations will place their hope.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew, writing to Jewish readers, ends the quotation on the Gentiles — placed here, immediately after Israel's teachers have resolved to kill Him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why Matthew put it here.** The chapter's argument is about the Sabbath and a withered hand. A man whose hand had stopped working was, by any practical measure, a bruised reed. He was not broken; he was told to stretch it out. See the note on Matthew 12:9–14."}]}, "Matthew 12:33–37": {"title": "\"The mouth speaks out of whatever fills the heart\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus explains where words come from, and it is not the mouth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tree"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Make the tree good and its fruit will be good**… because **a tree is known by its fruit**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "You do not fix fruit. You cannot improve an apple by working on the apple."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fruit is diagnostic — it tells you what the tree is — and the remedy in the sentence is applied to the tree."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mechanism"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The mouth speaks out of whatever fills the heart.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *out of the abundance of the heart* — out of the overflow, what is in excess."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Speech is described as spillage. What comes out under pressure, without editing, is simply the level of what was already in there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The storehouse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"A good person brings good things out of **the good stored up inside**, and an evil person brings evil things out of **the evil stored up inside**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **thesauros** — a treasury, a storehouse, the word behind *treasure*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both people are described as bringing out. Nobody manufactures on the spot; they withdraw from a store, and the store was filled earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which is why guarding it matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Above all else, guard your heart, for from it flow the springs of life.* Proverbs puts the heart first and everything else downstream — mouth, eyes, feet, in that order. See the note on Proverbs 4:20–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Solomon's word for *guard* is a sentry's word. It assumes something is trying to get in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Every careless word\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"On the day of judgment people will give an account for **every careless word** they have spoken.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **argos** — idle, inactive, unemployed. Not wicked words. Words that were doing no work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say they carry no weight. He says they will be accounted for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**By your words you will be declared right, and by your words you will be condemned.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb in the first half is the justification word Paul uses all through Romans."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Words are treated here not as a report on the heart but as evidence in a hearing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who He is saying it to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Pharisees, who had just said He cast out demons by Beelzebub. He calls them a *brood of snakes* in the middle of this passage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had watched a blind and mute man healed and produced that sentence about it — which is exactly his point. Their words came out of a store, and the store is what he is naming."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other place this principle runs.** Ten men looked at Canaan and said *we are not able*, and two looked at the same country and said *we are well able*. Same forty days, same giants. And God's answer to the first group was *I will do to you the very things you spoke in My hearing.* See the notes on Numbers 13:26–33 and Numbers 14:26–35."}]}, "Matthew 12:43–45": {"title": "\"Empty, swept out and neatly arranged\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A short, strange parable about a house, and the problem with the house is not that it is dirty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When it arrives it finds the place **empty, swept out and neatly arranged**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of them sounds like an improvement. The house is clean. Nothing is out of place. Somebody has clearly done work on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And that is exactly the condition in which it gets taken over. The trouble is the first word: **empty**. Nobody moved in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wandering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It wanders through waterless places looking for rest and finds none.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The detail is offered without explanation and Matthew does not develop it. What the parable does say is that the spirit's preference is to return to what it knows: *I will go back to the house I left.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Reform is not the same as occupancy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the argument. A life can be tidied — habits stopped, behaviour corrected, the place genuinely swept — and still be unoccupied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parable's ending is blunt: *that person ends up worse off than before.* A cleaned house with nobody in it is more vulnerable than a lived-in one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Jesus was talking about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That is exactly how it will be for this evil generation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He applies it to a generation, not to an individual. The context is a demand for a sign from scribes and Pharisees who had just accused Him of casting out demons by Beelzebul — religious people, morally scrupulous, whose reform had never included receiving Him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The alternative in the same chapter.** *Whoever does the will of My Father in heaven is My brother and sister and mother.* Jesus says it moments before this, pointing at His disciples. The empty house and the family are the two options Matthew 12 puts side by side."}]}, "Matthew 12:9–14": {"title": "Stretch out your hand", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man with a withered hand, a room full of men watching to see what Jesus will do, and an instruction to do the one thing he cannot."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were there for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Looking for something to accuse Him of, they asked Him, \"Is it permitted to heal on the Sabbath?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew tells us the motive before he tells us the question. Nobody in the room is asking about the man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sheep"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Suppose one of you has a single sheep, and it falls into a pit on the Sabbath. Will you not take hold of it and lift it out?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He answers with their own practice. Not a theory about the Sabbath — what they would in fact do, that same day, for one animal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And a human being is worth so much more than a sheep.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument is from the lesser to the greater, and the whole weight of it sits on the value of the man standing in front of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Stretch out your hand.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hand is withered. Stretching it out is exactly what it cannot do — that is the definition of the condition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is offered first. No touch is recorded, no word over the hand. He is told to do the impossible thing, and Matthew's next clause is *He stretched it out.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"As sound as the other one\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The restoration is measured against something. There was a good hand on the other arm the whole time, and the healed one comes back matching it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the healing produced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the Pharisees went out and began making plans against Him, to find a way to destroy Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew puts the plotting immediately after the healing, without a gap. The first recorded plan to kill Jesus in this Gospel follows a restored hand."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He said before the healing.** *So yes, it is permitted to do good on the Sabbath.* He does not argue that the Sabbath is suspended. He rules on what it was for."}]}, "Matthew 13:1–9": {"title": "The sower goes out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first parable Matthew records at length, and the one Jesus says the others depend on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He sat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus went out of the house and **sat down by the sea**… He **stepped into a boat** and sat there, while all the people stood on the shore.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A boat as a pulpit, and the water carrying the voice up the slope of the shore. It is an acoustic arrangement as much as a practical one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke records the same use of Simon's boat in chapter 5, and it ends with a net tearing. See the note on Luke 5:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What varies, and what does not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One sower. One kind of seed. Four soils."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the parable suggests the seed differed, that some was thrown carelessly, or that the sower ran out. The variable is entirely the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the path** — the seed never gets in; birds take it", "**rocky ground** — it springs up *quickly* and dies *quickly*, having no root", "**among thorns** — it grows and is choked", "**good soil** — a harvest, *a hundred times what was sown, or sixty, or thirty*"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second soil is the fast one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It **sprang up quickly**, because the soil was shallow.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The shallowness is why it was fast. Rock a few inches down warms early and the shoot comes up before anything else in the field."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The quickest response in the parable is the one with no root."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What killed it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When **the sun climbed high** it was scorched.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sun is not the villain — it is the same sun the good soil needed. Heat exposes what the root system is, and does not create the problem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The thorns"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They *grew up and choked it out.* Jesus explains them later as *the cares of this world and the deceitfulness of riches.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing dramatic. Nothing hostile. The plant is not attacked; it is crowded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three numbers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A hundred times what was sown, or sixty, or thirty.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Even the good soil varies, and thirtyfold is still an extraordinary return by ancient farming standards — sevenfold to tenfold was a decent harvest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this parable matters most"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark records what He said about it: *do you not understand this parable? **How then will you understand all the parables?***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the key one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The closing line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Whoever has ears, let them listen.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a few verses on, the instruction about how: *be careful how you listen. The measure you use will be the measure used for you.* See the note on Mark 4:24–25."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He was doing that day.** Matthew says *that same day* — the day the Pharisees had accused Him of casting out demons by Beelzebub, and His mother and brothers came asking for Him. He walks out of that house, sits in a boat, and starts talking about soil."}]}, "Matthew 13:10–17": {"title": "Why He speaks in stories", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The disciples ask a question about method and get an answer about hearts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why do You speak to them in stories?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **en parabolais** — in parables. A parable is something thrown alongside something else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question is fair. He has just told a story about seed and soil to a crowd on a beach and then sat down without explaining it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because **you have been given the secrets of the kingdom of heaven to know, and they have not**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **musteria** — mysteries. In the New Testament a mystery is not a puzzle but something previously hidden that has now been disclosed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **dedotai** — it has been given. Passive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To the one who has, more will be given, and he will have **far more than enough**; but from the one who has nothing, **even the little he has will be taken away**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **perisseuthesetai** — will overflow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke sharpens the second half: *even what he **thinks he has** will be taken from him*. See the note on Luke 8:16–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason restated"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**That is why I speak to them in stories**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They **look and do not really see**; they **listen and do not really hear**, and **nothing settles into their understanding**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *seeing they do not see, and hearing they do not hear*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The faculties are working. The reception is not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In them the words of Isaiah are coming true.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 6, from the prophet's commission — the hardest passage in the book, given to him immediately after *here am I, send me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You will **hear and hear, and never understand**; you will **look and look, and never see**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order in Isaiah"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For this people's **heart has grown heavy**. Their **ears have gone dull**, and they have **shut their eyes tight**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heart first, then the ears, then the eyes. And the last one is active — *they have shut their eyes*, not *their eyes were shut*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew quotes the Greek version, which puts the closing in the people's own hands. The Hebrew has the imperative form, and reads as a commission to make the heart dull."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the closed eyes prevent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Otherwise they would **see with their eyes, and hear with their ears, and understand with their hearts, and turn back to Me, and I would heal them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five verbs in a chain, and healing is at the end of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given for the shutting is stated from inside: this is what would happen otherwise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **how blessed your eyes are, because they see, and your ears, because they hear**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two organs, and the same two verbs, with the negative removed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The longing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Many **prophets** and many good and righteous people **longed to see what you are seeing and never saw it**, and to hear what you are hearing and never heard it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men who wrote the book wanted to see this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says the same: *of this salvation the prophets have inquired and searched carefully… searching what, or what manner of time, the Spirit of Christ who was in them was indicating*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Abraham: *your father Abraham rejoiced to see My day, and he saw it and was glad*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the parables do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do two jobs at once, and the passage says so. They reveal, to anyone who comes and asks what the story meant. And they conceal, from anyone content to hear a nice story about farming and go home."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The disciples get the explanation because they came and asked. That is the whole difference in the chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He explains next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Therefore **hear the parable of the sower**.\"* See the note on Matthew 13:18–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He answers the question about method by explaining the parable to the people who asked."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What separates the two groups in the chapter.** Not intelligence. The disciples came and asked what it meant, and the explanation follows immediately."}]}, "Matthew 13:18–23": {"title": "The four kinds of soil", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's version of the explanation, and one word in it is not in Mark's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first soil"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When anyone **hears the message of the kingdom and it does not take hold in him**, the evil one comes and **snatches away what was sown in his heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *does not take hold* is **me sunientos** — not understanding, not putting it together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark says only that Satan comes and takes it. Matthew names the gap the theft goes through. See the note on Mark 4:13–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sown in his heart** — Matthew locates the seed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The one who **hears the word and welcomes it right away with joy**, but **it has no root in him and does not last**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **proskairos** — temporary, for a season."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As soon as **trouble or persecution comes because of the word**, he falls away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Because of the word.** The pressure is named as arriving on account of the thing sown, not despite it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The one who hears the word, but **the worries of this life and the false promises of wealth crowd in and choke it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *false promises* is **apate** — deceitfulness. Riches are not called evil; they are called deceptive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things do the choking here. Mark has three; Luke has *pleasures of life* as well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **it never comes to anything**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *it becomes unfruitful*. The plant is alive and produces nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fourth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The one who **hears the word and takes it in and understands it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs where the others have one or two. Matthew's good soil is defined by understanding — the same word that was missing from the first soil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The explanation is framed by it: **sunientos** at the start, **sunieis** at the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The yields"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **hundred times** what was sown, or **sixty**, or **thirty**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew runs them downward; Mark runs them up. Both keep the range."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The good soil is not uniform, and all three figures are called good."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse before the explanation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Therefore I speak to them in parables, because seeing they do not see, and hearing they do not hear, nor do they understand**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the quotation from Isaiah 6, given in full — *lest they should understand with their hearts and turn, so that I should heal them*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says to the disciples"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **blessed are your eyes for they see, and your ears for they hear**; for assuredly I say to you that **many prophets and righteous men desired to see what you see, and did not see it**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven parables in one chapter — the sower, the wheat and tares, the mustard seed, the leaven, the hidden treasure, the pearl, and the dragnet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew notes: *all these things Jesus spoke to the multitude in parables; and without a parable He did not speak to them.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one word Matthew adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Understanding, at both ends of the explanation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first soil loses the seed because it is not understood. The fourth bears because it is."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Matthew supplies that Mark does not.** The gap the birds come through. He names it at the top of the explanation and again at the bottom, and it is the same word both times."}]}, "Matthew 13:53–58": {"title": "The carpenter's son", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of the parable chapter, and He goes home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The transition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Jesus had **finished these stories**, He moved on from there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's formula. He uses it five times, at the end of each of the five great discourses — the Sermon on the Mount, the mission charge, the parables, the church discourse, and the Olivet discourse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven parables have just been told: the sower, the tares, the mustard seed, the leaven, the treasure, the pearl, the dragnet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He came to **His hometown** and began teaching the people in their synagogue.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nazareth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they **could hardly take it in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ekplessesthai** — struck out of themselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Astonishment is the right response and it does not get them anywhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Where did this man get such wisdom, and these mighty works?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They concede both. Neither the teaching nor the miracles is disputed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What they cannot place is the source, and they have a theory: he came from here, so it cannot be from anywhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The family"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Is He not **the carpenter's son**? Is His mother not named Mary, and are His brothers not **James and Joses and Simon and Judas**? And are **all His sisters not here among us**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew says *the carpenter's son*. Mark says *the carpenter*. See the note on Mark 6:1–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four brothers by name, and sisters, plural, unnamed. A large family, all of them local, and every one of them known."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two of the brothers wrote letters that are in the New Testament. Neither believed Him at this point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they **took offense at Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eskandalizonto** — they stumbled over Him. The root of *scandal*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proverb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"A prophet is **honored everywhere except in his own hometown and in his own house**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **atimos** — without honour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last three words are the sharpest. Not just the town — the house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John says it plainly: *for even His brothers did not believe in Him*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He did not do many miracles there, because they would not believe**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's phrasing. Mark's is harder: *He was not able to do many mighty works there*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew softens *could not* to *did not*, and keeps the reason."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter has just said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parable of the sower and its explanation. Four soils, and the seed is the same in all four. See the note on Matthew 13:18–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nazareth is the chapter's illustration of itself. Matthew puts the wayside soil at the end of the chapter about soils, and does not comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He said in the Nazareth synagogue"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's account of what is probably the same visit: He reads Isaiah 61, hands back the scroll, sits down, and says *today this Scripture is fulfilled in your hearing*. See the note on Luke 4:16–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So all bore witness to Him and marvelled at the gracious words which proceeded out of His mouth. And they said, \"Is this not Joseph's son?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same question, and then the crowd tries to throw Him off a cliff."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sends the twelve out, two by two, with instructions about which houses to stay in and what to do when a town will not receive them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What they got right.** The wisdom, the miracles, the family and the trade. Everything on their list is accurate, and the conclusion they draw from it is that there is nothing to see."}]}, "Matthew 14:13–14": {"title": "His heart went out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses between a murder and a miracle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He had heard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Jesus **heard it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before: Herod's birthday, the dancing girl, the request put up to her by her mother, and John the Baptist beheaded in prison."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And his disciples came and took away the body and buried it, **and went and told Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what He heard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who John was to Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His cousin. The man who baptised Him and said *behold the Lamb of God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said of him: *among those born of women there has not risen one greater than John the Baptist.* See the note on Matthew 11:12–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **withdrew from that place by boat to a quiet spot, to be alone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anechoresen** — withdrew, retired. And **kat' idian**, privately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He goes looking for a deserted place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the crowds heard where He was going and followed Him on foot from the towns**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They walk round the lake and get there first. Mark says *they ran there on foot from all the cities, arrived before them, and came together to Him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He lands and the place is full."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when He came ashore and **saw the great crowd waiting**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *coming out, He saw a great multitude*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What moved"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His **heart went out to them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **esplanchnisthe** — from **splanchna**, the inward parts. The gut-word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb Matthew uses of Him looking at the crowds in chapter 9. See the note on Matthew 9:35–38."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He did about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He healed their sick**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any note that this was difficult, that He was owed the afternoon, or that the crowd was inconsiderate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records the news, the withdrawal, the crowd arriving first, and the compassion, in that order, and offers no comment on the sequence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When it was evening, His disciples came to Him, saying, \"This is a deserted place, and the hour is already late. **Send the multitudes away**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Jesus said to them, \"**They do not need to go away. You give them something to eat**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five loaves, two fish, five thousand men besides women and children, twelve baskets over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only miracle in all four Gospels."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And after that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Immediately Jesus made His disciples get into the boat and go before Him to the other side, while He sent the multitudes away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when He had sent the multitudes away, **He went up on the mountain by Himself to pray**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gets the solitude at the end of the day, after everyone has been fed — and then walks out on the water to the boat."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of the paragraph.** He hears his cousin has been executed, gets in a boat to be alone, finds the shore crowded when he lands, and the word Matthew uses for what happened next is the one for being moved in the gut."}]}, "Matthew 14:22–27": {"title": "The fourth watch", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He sends them into the storm, and then comes to them at the worst hour of the night."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who put them there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Right away Jesus **had the disciples get into the boat** and go on ahead of Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is stronger than *had* — He compelled them, made them get in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are on that lake because He put them there. It is not a wrong turn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He climbed the mountainside **by Himself to pray**. Evening fell, and He was there alone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the evening after the feeding of the five thousand. He dismisses the crowd, sends the disciples off, and goes up alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The boat was already out in the middle of the sea, **thrown about by the waves, for the wind was against it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is that the wind was contrary — against them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark adds that He *saw them straining at rowing*, from the mountain, in the dark. They could not see Him; He could see them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fourth watch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In **the fourth watch of the night** Jesus came toward them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Romans divided the night into four watches. The fourth runs roughly from three until six in the morning — the last stretch, the coldest, the darkest before it lightens."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been rowing since evening. He comes at the end of it, not the beginning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What frightens them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the disciples saw Him walking on the water **they were terrified**. \"**It is a ghost!**\" they said, and they cried out in fear.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The storm had not made them scream. He did."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thing that had come to rescue them was the most frightening thing that happened that night, because it did not arrive in a shape they recognised."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take heart. It is I. Do not be afraid.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three short sentences, and the middle one is two words in Greek: **ego eimi** — *I am*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the ordinary way of saying *it's me*. It is also the name from the burning bush, and John's Gospel builds seven sayings on it. See the note on Exodus 3:13–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He was going to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark records something Matthew leaves out: *He came toward them, walking on the sea, **and would have passed them by**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever that means, He was not going to force Himself into the boat. They *cried out* — and then He spoke."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter asks to come, walks, looks at the wind, sinks, and *immediately Jesus stretched out His hand and caught him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *when they got into the boat, the wind ceased.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where He had just been.** Praying, alone, on a mountain, all night — and the chapter opens with Him hearing that John the Baptist had been beheaded, and trying to withdraw to a deserted place before the crowds followed Him."}]}, "Matthew 14:28–33": {"title": "A hand reaching out", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Only Matthew records this, and every detail in it is worth its own line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, **if it is You**, tell me to come to You on the water.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks for a command rather than permission. *Tell me to come.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever else Peter is doing, he is not proposing to try it on his own initiative."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Come.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Peter **climbed down out of the boat, walked on the water**, and started toward Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the only person besides Jesus recorded as doing it, and the fact usually gets lost behind what happened next."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven men stayed in the boat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What changed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But when he **felt the force of the wind** he was afraid.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wind had been blowing the whole time. It did not get worse when he stepped out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The variable is what he was attending to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sinking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **beginning to sink** he cried out, \"**Lord, save me!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words. It is one of the shortest prayers in the Bible, and it works."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not swim for the boat. He calls."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The speed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Immediately Jesus **reached out His hand and caught hold of him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *immediately*, and the verb is to take hold of firmly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not let him go under first to make a point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You of little faith, why did you doubt?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Oligopistos** — Matthew's word, used five times, always to disciples."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the Greek for *doubt* is **distazo**, to be pulled two ways, to waver between."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said while holding him up out of the water."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the faith failed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in the asking, and not in the stepping out. He walked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question is asked of a man who is being lifted back on to the surface, about the part in the middle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wind"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when **the two of them climbed into the boat, the wind died away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew notes that they got in together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the boat did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the men in the boat **worshipped Him** and said, \"**Truly You are the Son of God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first time in Matthew that the disciples say it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had watched five thousand fed that afternoon and concluded nothing. A man sinking and being caught produced the confession."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Peter got wrong and what he got right.** He asked, he got out, he looked at the weather, and he sank. Of the twelve men on that lake, he is the only one who was ever on the water — and the only one who ever needed to be pulled out of it."}]}, "Matthew 15:21–28": {"title": "\"Even the little dogs eat the crumbs\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The hardest conversation Jesus has with anyone who came to Him for help, and it ends with the highest compliment He pays anybody in Matthew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus went out from there and departed to the region of **Tyre and Sidon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the far north-west coast — the border of **Asher**, whose whole blessing in Genesis 49 is one line about food:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Asher's food will be rich; he will provide **delicacies fit for a king**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only recorded time He left Israel's territory, and the conversation He has there is about bread and a table. See the note on Genesis 49:20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three obstacles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She meets a wall three times:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*He did not answer her a word*", "*I was sent only to the lost sheep of the house of Israel*", "*It is not right to take the children's bread and toss it to the little dogs*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Silence, then a limit, then what sounds like an insult. And in between, the disciples asking Him to get rid of her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does with each one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She keeps coming. After the silence she comes closer; after the limit she kneels; after the dogs remark she takes the image and finishes it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yes, Lord — and even the little dogs eat the crumbs that fall from their master's table.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not dispute the order He has stated. She accepts it entirely and points out that it does not exclude her: if the children are being fed, there is something under the table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word He used"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kunarion** — a diminutive. Not the scavenging street dogs, but the little ones inside a house. It is a household word, and she hears the household in it — *their master's table*, not somebody else's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What a crumb did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She asked for a crumb and she got what she asked for. *Let it be done for you just as you desire* — and a demon left her daughter that hour, at a distance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what fell off the edge of the table, unnoticed by the people eating at it: enough to break a demonic hold on a child in another house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The children's bread is what the crumb broke off. Whoever is at that table is not reaching for fragments — they are handed the loaf, torn and put into their hands, and told to eat it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Woman, your faith is great.* He says this to exactly two people in Matthew, and both are Gentiles; the other is the centurion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let it be done for you just as you desire.* And her daughter was healed from that hour, at a distance, with the mother still standing in front of Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she called Him first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Have mercy on me, Lord, Son of David.* A Canaanite woman, from the country Israel was told to drive out, addressing Him by His messianic title."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had the doctrine right before the conversation started."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the delay.** Nothing here suggests reluctance in Him — Matthew has already shown Him healing a Roman's servant on request. What the exchange produced was an answer from her that has been quoted ever since, and a public verdict on her faith in front of disciples who had just asked Him to send her away."}]}, "Matthew 15:29–31": {"title": "They laid them at His feet", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A summary paragraph, and it is one of the largest healing scenes in the Gospels."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He had come from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus **moved on from there**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "From Tyre and Sidon — Gentile country — where the Canaanite woman had just argued Him into healing her daughter with the line about crumbs under the table."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And walked along the Sea of Galilee. Then He **went up on a mountainside and sat down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He sits. The posture of a teacher, and He does no teaching in these three verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What arrived"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Great crowds came to Him, bringing **the lame, the blind, the crippled, those who could not speak, and many others**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four categories named and a fifth left open."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *crippled* is **kullous** — maimed, mutilated. A limb that is not there or not usable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did with them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they **laid them at His feet**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **erripsan** — flung, threw down. Not placed carefully."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A whole day of carrying stretchers up a hill, and the last action in the sentence is to put the person down and step back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in the paragraph says anything. No request is recorded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He healed them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the entire account of the healing. Matthew gives four verbs to the carrying and three words to the cure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the crowd saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people were **astonished** when they saw **those who could not speak now speaking, the crippled made whole, the lame walking, and the blind seeing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list is repeated in the same order it arrived in, reversed item by item."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The crippled made whole** — the Greek is **hugieis**, sound, healthy. For a maimed limb, which is the one item on the list that requires something to be there that was not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they praised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they gave glory to **the God of Israel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's phrasing, and it is the phrasing of outsiders. He has just spent a chapter in Gentile territory, and the Decapolis was on this side of the lake."}, {"k": "p", "t": "People who would not normally say *our God* say *the God of Israel*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three days on that mountainside, and then: *I have compassion on the multitude, because they have now continued with Me three days and have nothing to eat.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven loaves, a few small fish, four thousand men besides women and children, and seven baskets left over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The healing runs straight into a meal on the same hill."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah had said would happen on a mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And on this mountain the LORD of hosts will make for all peoples **a feast**.* See the note on Isaiah 25:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rich food, aged wine, and *for all peoples* — and the crowd on this hill glorifies the God of Israel from outside it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other summary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And His fame went throughout all Syria; and they brought to Him all sick people who were afflicted with various diseases and torments… **and He healed them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew writes the same sentence in chapter 4. It bookends the ministry."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the paragraph records.** People carried, dropped and left, and a sentence with no conditions in it. Matthew spends his words on the queue and three on the outcome."}]}, "Matthew 16:13–20": {"title": "\"Who do you say I am?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Jesus asks two questions in a place chosen for the asking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He asks it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Caesarea Philippi* — as far north as He ever took them, at the foot of Mount Hermon. The town sat beside a cave sacred to the Greek god Pan, with a temple to Caesar Augustus built nearby."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Surrounded by the shrines of other lords and other sons of gods, He asks who they say He is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who do people say the Son of Man is?* Then: *But **you** — who do you say I am?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first is a survey and they answer it easily. The second is emphatic in Greek — *you*, plural, as against everybody else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reported opinions are all complimentary: John the Baptist, Elijah, Jeremiah, one of the prophets. Every one of them is a category He does not fit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Peter's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are the Messiah, the Son of the living God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves matter. Messiah is the office; Son of the living God is the identity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No human being revealed this to you. My Father in heaven showed it to you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus does not congratulate Peter on his insight. He identifies the source, and it is not Peter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rock"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are Peter, and on this rock I will build My church.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wordplay is deliberate — *Petros*, a stone, and *petra*, rock. Christians have argued for centuries over whether the rock is Peter himself or the confession he has just made."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is not in dispute is the builder and the outcome. *I* will build, and *the gates of the realm of the dead will not hold out against it.* Gates are defensive. The picture is of the church advancing on death's stronghold, not enduring a siege."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What happens four verses later.** The same man, who has just been told the Father revealed this to him, is called Satan for objecting to the cross. Matthew puts the two scenes side by side without softening either. See the note on Matthew 16:21–23."}]}, "Matthew 16:21–23": {"title": "\"A stone in My path\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four verses after being told the Father revealed the truth to him, Peter is called Satan. Matthew puts the two scenes back to back on purpose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"From that time on\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From that time on Jesus began to make it plain.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The confession changes what He tells them. Once they know who He is, He starts explaining what that means — and what it means is Jerusalem, suffering, death, and the third day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew notes it as a turning point in the Gospel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peter took Him aside and began to correct Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is the one used for rebuking — the same word Jesus uses on demons and on a storm. Peter is not raising a question. He is telling Him off, privately, to spare Him the embarrassment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Never, Lord! This will never happen to You.* In Greek it is emphatic: by no means, not ever."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Get behind Me, Satan. You are a stone in My path.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is **skandalon** — the trigger of a trap, the thing that makes you stumble. Peter has just been called a rock to build on; now he is a rock to trip over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because you are not thinking the thoughts of God but the thoughts of men.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the charge, and it is worth being precise about. Peter is not accused of malice. He is accused of ordinary human reasoning — a friend who does not want the man he loves to be killed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the most natural sentiment in the passage, and Jesus identifies its source as the same one that offered Him the kingdoms of the world without a cross."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The pattern.** Both temptations come from someone who cares. The devil in the wilderness offered a route to the crown with no suffering in it; Peter offers the same route in the voice of a friend. Jesus answers both with the same refusal. See the note on Matthew 4:1–4."}]}, "Matthew 16:24–28": {"title": "Let go of yourself", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Said immediately after Peter is called Satan, and the two are connected."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Caesarea Philippi. *You are the Christ, the Son of the living God* — and *blessed are you, Simon Bar-Jonah, for flesh and blood has not revealed this to you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the first passion prediction, and Peter taking Him aside: *far be it from You, Lord; this shall not happen to You!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get behind Me, Satan!** You are an offence to Me, for you are not mindful of the things of God but the things of men.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same man, in the same conversation, gets the highest commendation and the sharpest rebuke in the Gospel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If anyone wants to come after Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ei tis thelei** — if anyone wills."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing compulsory. It is stated as a choice, and the terms are given before the choosing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He must **let go of himself**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **aparnesastho heauton** — deny himself, disown himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same verb used of Peter in the courtyard a few chapters later: *he began to curse and swear, \"I do not know the man\"*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter denied Christ. The instruction is to do that to yourself instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take up his cross**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a hardship or an illness. In that world a man carrying a crossbeam through the streets was going one place, and everybody watching knew it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rome had crucified thousands in Galilee within living memory. The image had no metaphorical softness in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **follow Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present imperative — keep following."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The paradox"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **whoever wants to save his life will lose it, but whoever loses his life for My sake will find it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word is **psuche** — life, soul, self. The same word in both halves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus says a version of this more often than almost anything else. It appears in all four Gospels, and Matthew records it twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What good is it to a man if he gains the whole world and loses his own soul?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **zemiothe** — be fined, suffer loss, forfeit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A commercial word. The whole verse is an accountant's question about a trade."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Or **what could a man ever give in exchange for his soul?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Antallagma** — an exchange, the price paid."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second question answers the first. If you have already traded it away, there is nothing left to buy it back with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the world had just been offered for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"All of this I will give You, if You will fall down and worship me.\"* See the note on Matthew 4:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole world, for one act. He had refused it, and now He asks what it would be worth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The return"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For the Son of Man is going to **come in the glory of His Father with His angels**, and then He will repay each person according to what he has done.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There are some standing here who **will not taste death before they see the Son of Man coming in His kingdom**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hardest sentence in the chapter, and Matthew places what follows six days later:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus took Peter, James, and John his brother, led them up on a high mountain by themselves, **and He was transfigured before them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter break is a printer's decision. Peter himself makes the connection in his letter: *we were eyewitnesses of His majesty… when we were with Him on the holy mountain*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb.** Deny — the same one used of Peter in the courtyard. He denied Christ; the instruction here is to do that to yourself."}]}, "Matthew 17:1–8": {"title": "\"Listen to Him\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Six days after telling them He is going to die, He takes three of them up a hill."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The count"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Six days later.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew and Mark both date it. Luke says *about eight days*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six days after what: Peter's confession, the first announcement of the cross, and Peter being told *get behind Me, Satan*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men who go up the mountain include the one who had just been rebuked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peter, James, and John the brother of James.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same three at Jairus's house and in Gethsemane. Matthew names them the same way each time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there **He was transformed in front of them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **metamorphoo** — the root of *metamorphosis*. Changed in form."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same verb of the reader: *be **transformed** by the renewing of your mind*, and *we are being **transformed** into the same image from glory to glory*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His face **shone like the sun**, and His clothes turned **as white as the light**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses' face shone after forty days on Sinai, and he had to veil it — but that was reflected. Mark says of the clothes *such as no launderer on earth can whiten them.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who arrives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **Moses and Elijah** appeared to them, **talking with Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lawgiver and the prophet. One died and God buried him; the other never died at all. See the note on 2 Kings 2:11–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says what they were discussing: *they spoke of **His decease** which He was about to accomplish at Jerusalem* — the Greek is **exodos**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Peter's offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, **it is good for us to be here**. If You want, **I will put up three shelters** — one for You, one for Moses, and one for Elijah.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three, equally sized. Mark adds the note: *he did not know what to say, for they were greatly afraid.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While he was still speaking, **a bright cloud covered them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is cut off mid-sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cloud is the **shekinah** — the one that filled the tabernacle and the temple, and that led Israel through the wilderness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the voice says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This is My Son, whom I love. My delight is in Him. Listen to Him.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first two sentences are word for word what was said at the Jordan. See the note on Matthew 3:16–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third is new, and it is the only command in the passage: **listen to Him**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It answers the offer of three shelters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the disciples heard it, they **fell facedown, terrified**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not at the shining face. At the voice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The touch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **Jesus came over and touched them**. \"**Get up**,\" He said. \"**Do not be afraid**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He crosses to them. The Greek is *having come and having touched*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when they lifted their eyes they **saw no one but Jesus Himself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses gone, Elijah gone, the cloud gone. The Greek is *Jesus only*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The command was to listen to Him, and when they look up He is the only one there."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was said on the way down.** *Tell the vision to no one until the Son of Man is risen from the dead.* Peter kept it for decades, and then wrote it down: *we were eyewitnesses of His majesty… we heard this voice which came from heaven when we were with Him on the holy mountain.*"}]}, "Matthew 18:10–14": {"title": "Leaving the ninety-nine", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The same story as Luke 15, told to different people, for a different reason."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The disciples had asked *who then is greatest in the kingdom of heaven?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Jesus called a little child to Him, set him in the midst of them*, and said *unless you are converted and become as little children, you will by no means enter*. See the note on Matthew 18:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole chapter runs from that argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**See that you never look down on one of these little ones**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me kataphronesete** — do not despise, do not think down on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *do not harm*. Do not hold in low regard, which is what an argument about greatness produces automatically."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For I tell you, **in heaven their angels are always looking on the face of My Father in heaven**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dia pantos blepousi** — continually see."}, {"k": "p", "t": "To see the king's face was the privilege of the highest courtiers. The claim is that whoever represents these little ones has standing access."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the little ones are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter begins with a child. But *these little ones who believe in Me* is the phrase used two verses earlier, which widens it to anyone small in the congregation's reckoning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mission statement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **the Son of Man came to save what was lost**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What do you think?** If a man has a hundred sheep and one of them wanders off, **will he not leave the ninety-nine on the hills** and go out looking for the one that strayed?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks them to answer it themselves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Planethe** — wanders, goes astray. The root of *planet*, the wandering star."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Luke's version the sheep is simply lost. Here it wandered — which is the difference between the two tellings, and it fits an audience of disciples arguing about rank."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leaving ninety-nine on open hills to look for one is not sound shepherding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question is put as though the answer is obvious, and commercially it is not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The joy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And if he finds it, I tell you the truth, **he is happier over that one sheep than over the ninety-nine that never wandered away**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **chairei mallon** — rejoices more."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that the one matters more. That the finding produces more joy than the never-losing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In just the same way, **it is not the will of your Father in heaven that even one of these little ones should be lost**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ouk estin thelema** — it is not the will."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two tellings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke tells it to Pharisees who complained *this Man receives sinners and eats with them*, and the sheep is lost. See the note on Luke 15:3–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew tells it to disciples arguing about greatness, and the sheep wandered off from inside the flock."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same story, two audiences, two edges."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Moreover **if your brother sins against you, go and tell him his fault between you and him alone**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The procedure for restoring someone, then Peter asking *how often shall my brother sin against me and I forgive him? Up to seven times?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I do not say to you, up to seven times, but **up to seventy times seven**.\"* See the note on Matthew 18:21–22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the parable of the servant forgiven ten thousand talents."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One argument, start to finish. It opens with men wanting to know who ranks highest and ends with a man thrown into prison for refusing to cancel a small debt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The shepherd going after one sheep sits in the middle."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The comparison.** Not that the one is worth more than the ninety-nine. That finding produces more joy than never having lost."}]}, "Matthew 18:15–20": {"title": "\"Where two or three come together\" — the setting of a much-loved verse", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*Where two or three come together in My name, I am there among them* is quoted at small gatherings everywhere, and it is a true and warm thing to say. It is also the last line of a paragraph about something else entirely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the paragraph is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The subject is a Christian who has wronged another Christian, and the process is laid out in stages:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["go to him **privately** first", "if that fails, take one or two others", "if that fails, tell the church", "if that fails, treat him as an outsider"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every step is designed to keep the circle as small as possible for as long as possible. The aim is stated at the start: *you have won your brother back*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two or three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The number is not chosen for its smallness. It comes from the previous sentence — *every matter may be settled on the word of two or three witnesses* — which is the standard of evidence from Deuteronomy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So when Jesus says *where two or three come together in My name, I am there*, He is promising His presence to the hardest thing a church ever does: the conversation where somebody is confronted about a wrong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that does to the promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not shrink it. It makes it braver."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comfort of a handful of believers meeting with Christ among them is real and the verse carries it. But it was given first to people about to sit down in a room and try to win somebody back, and it says that Christ is in the room for that."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On binding and loosing.** The phrase belongs to the same setting — the language is from rabbinic practice, where binding and loosing meant declaring what was forbidden and permitted. In context it is about the church's judgement in exactly the situation being described, not a general formula."}]}, "Matthew 2:1–2": {"title": "The men who came the wrong way", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Foreigners arrive in Jerusalem asking a question that nobody in Jerusalem had thought to ask."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Magi** — the word covers astrologers, court advisers and priests of the eastern kingdoms. Not kings, and Matthew never says how many; the three comes from the number of gifts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were Gentiles, from outside the covenant, practising a discipline Israel's own law forbade. And they are the first people in Matthew's Gospel to worship Jesus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where is the child who has been born King of the Jews?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They ask it in the capital, in the reign of a king who was famously paranoid about his throne — a man who had already killed his wife and three of his own sons."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They walk into Herod's city and ask where the real king is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they went wrong"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They went to a palace. It is the obvious place to look for a royal birth, and it is the wrong one — a detour that puts Herod on the scent and leads to the soldiers being sent to Bethlehem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The star had brought them a long way. It did not take them to Jerusalem; their own reasonable assumption did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was in Jerusalem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chief priests and scribes answer Herod's question immediately: Bethlehem, and they quote Micah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They knew the address. Five miles down the road, and there is no record of any of them going to look."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two groups had.** The scholars had the Scripture and stayed home. The foreigners had a light in the sky, an incomplete map, and enough interest to travel for months. Matthew reports both without comment, in the second chapter of a Gospel written to Jewish readers."}]}, "Matthew 2:16–18": {"title": "Rachel crying for her children", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The part of the Christmas story that does not appear on cards, and Matthew does not soften it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every boy two years old and under was put to death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bethlehem was a small town; historians estimate the number of children killed in the low dozens. That is not a comfort, and Matthew does not offer it as one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Herod's record makes the account entirely credible — he executed his own sons and had ordered notable men killed at his death so the country would mourn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A voice is heard in Ramah… Rachel crying for her children, and she will not be comforted, because they are no more.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew reaches into Jeremiah 31, where Rachel — buried near Bethlehem, mother of the northern tribes — is pictured weeping as her descendants are marched into exile."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note what Matthew does **not** do. He does not say the mothers should be comforted. He quotes a verse in which comfort is explicitly refused."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the quotation comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read on in Jeremiah 31 and the next verse is God's answer: *restrain your voice from weeping and your eyes from tears, for your work will be rewarded — they will come back from the land of the enemy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a few verses further, the same chapter contains the promise of the new covenant — the law written on hearts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew quotes the grief and leaves his readers to know where it is going."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is in the Gospel at all"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because it happened, and because Matthew is telling the truth about the world Jesus was born into. A king who kills children is part of the Christmas story."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one detail of mercy.** Joseph is warned in a dream and takes the child to Egypt. Jesus survives — and Matthew, who has already had Him called out of Egypt like Israel, is showing a nation's story replayed in one family: down into Egypt, and out again."}]}, "Matthew 20:13–16": {"title": "Is your eye bitter", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The complaint is entirely reasonable, and the answer does not deny it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parable"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A landowner hires labourers at dawn for a denarius. He hires more at nine, at noon, at three, and at five in the afternoon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the end of the day he pays the last first, and everyone gets a denarius."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when they had received it, they **complained against the landowner**, saying, \"**These last men have worked only one hour, and you made them equal to us** who have borne the burden and the heat of the day.\"* See the note on Matthew 20:8–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is true about the complaint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men hired at five worked one hour. The men hired at dawn worked twelve. The rate was not the same."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parable does not pretend otherwise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Friend**, I have done you no wrong.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hetaire** — comrade, fellow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew uses it three times. To the man at the wedding with no garment. Here. And in the garden: *friend, why have you come?* — to Judas."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a warm word in this Gospel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contract"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Did you not agree with me on a denarius?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did. Verse 2 records it: *now when he had agreed with the labourers for a denarius a day*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A denarius was the standard daily wage. The first men got exactly what they negotiated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dismissal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take what belongs to you and go home**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **aron to son kai hupage** — take up what is yours and go."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The choice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I choose to give this last man the same as I gave you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **thelo** — I will, I want."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No justification is offered. He does not explain that the last men were poorer, or had tried harder to find work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question of ownership"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Am I not free to do what I want with what is mine?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ouk exestin moi ho thelo poiesai en tois emois** — is it not lawful for me to do what I wish with my own things?"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the argument. The money was never the first men's to allocate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Or **is your eye bitter because I am generous?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ho ophthalmos sou poneros estin hoti ego agathos eimi** — is your eye evil because I am good?"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The evil eye** is a Semitic idiom for grudging, envious, stingy. Deuteronomy uses it of the man who looks coldly at his poor brother. See the note on Deuteronomy 15:7–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs uses its opposite: *he who has a generous eye will be blessed*. See the note on Proverbs 22:8–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the question locates"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an injustice. A reaction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing was taken from the first men. Something was given to somebody else, and that is what produced the complaint."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **the last will be first, and the first last**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same sentence closes chapter 19 and opens this parable. The story is framed by it on both sides."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What prompted it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter: *see, we have left all and followed You. Therefore what shall we have?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the rich young ruler before that, who went away sorrowful."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The parable is the answer to a question about what the early ones get."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Behold, we are going up to Jerusalem, and the Son of Man will be betrayed… and they will condemn Him to death.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the mother of James and John, asking for the two seats."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whoever desires to become great among you, let him be your servant.\"* See the note on Matthew 20:25–28."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men in the parable wanted proportionate reward. The next scene is two of the twelve asking for the best places."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the second question does.** It relocates the grievance. Nothing was taken from anyone — something was given to somebody else, and the idiom for that reaction is *an evil eye*."}]}, "Matthew 20:24–28": {"title": "\"Not to be served, but to serve\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two brothers have just asked for the best seats, the other ten are furious about it, and Jesus responds by redefining the whole idea of position."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says about the world"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You know how it works among the nations. Their rulers throw their weight around, and the great ones make their authority felt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both verbs in the Greek are compounds with *kata* — lording it **down** over, exercising authority **against**. The preposition does the work: it is power exerted downward onto people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *it must not be that way among you.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inversion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever wants to be great among you must be the one who serves you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Notice He does not say wanting to be great is wrong. He does not rebuke the ambition — He redirects it. The route to greatness is not blocked; it runs the other way from what everyone assumes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for servant is **diakonos**, the ordinary word for someone who waits at a table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as the Son of Man did not come to be served, but to serve, and to give His life as a ransom for many.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pattern is not offered as a principle but as a biography. He is describing what He is doing, in a chapter where He has just told them for the third time that He is going to Jerusalem to be killed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ransom** is *lutron* — the price paid to release a captive or free a slave. It is the noun behind the redemption word in Ephesians 1:7."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the brothers had actually asked for.** Seats at His right and left in His kingdom. When Matthew's Gospel finally shows two people at His right and left, it is at the crucifixion, and they are criminals. The Gospel answers the request without comment, several chapters later."}]}, "Matthew 20:8–12": {"title": "Paid last first", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A landowner hires men all day at different hours and pays every one of them the same. The detail that makes the parable work is the order of the queue."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Beginning with the last ones hired\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He could have paid the first group first, sent them home content, and settled with the rest quietly. Nobody would have known."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He deliberately arranges it so the men who worked twelve hours watch the men who worked one receive a full day's wage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is not carelessness. He wants them to see it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the first group then expects"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the ones hired first came up, they assumed they would get more.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing had been said to suggest it. They had agreed a denarius at dawn — the standard day's wage, a fair rate, exactly what they asked for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The expectation is generated entirely by watching somebody else be treated generously."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The complaint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have made them equal to us, who carried the weight of the whole day and the burning heat.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The grievance is not that they were underpaid. It is the word **equal**. They object to the last men being brought level with them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sharp end of the parable: generosity to somebody else felt like an injury, in men who had received precisely what they were promised."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The landowner's answer.** *Am I not allowed to do what I want with what is mine? Or is your eye evil because I am good?* The Greek idiom is about a grudging, envious eye. And the parable ends where the chapter before it ended — the last will be first, and the first last. Jesus tells it to disciples who had just asked what they would get for having left everything."}]}, "Matthew 21:12–17": {"title": "The temple cleared", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One man clears a courtyard the size of several football pitches, and nobody stops Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He objected to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The money changers and the dove sellers were operating in the Court of the Gentiles — the only part of the temple a non-Jew was permitted to enter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one space in the building where the nations could come and pray had been turned into a livestock market with an exchange rate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is written, '**My house will be called a house of prayer**,' but you have turned it into **a hideout for robbers**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first half is Isaiah 56, and Mark keeps the rest of the line: *a house of prayer **for all nations**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second half is Jeremiah 7 — the temple sermon, where the people were chanting *the temple of the LORD, the temple of the LORD* while robbing each other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A **hideout** is where thieves go after the theft. It is not where the robbery happens; it is where they feel safe."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He did not damage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He drove out people and overturned furniture. Nobody is reported hurt, and no animal is reported killed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He would not allow anyone to carry wares through the temple*, Mark adds. He shut the shortcut down as well."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened next in the same courtyard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the blind and the lame came to Him right there in the temple, and He healed them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only Matthew records this, and the placement is the point. The tables go over and the room fills with people who could not have got near it before."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under the old rule, *the blind and the lame shall not come into the house.* They came in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The children"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The children in the temple courts **shouting, \"Hosanna to the Son of David!\"** And they were furious.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chief priests object to the children, and He answers them with Psalm 8: *out of the mouths of children and nursing infants You have brought forth praise.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of that psalm says *You have ordained **strength**.* He quotes the Greek, which has praise. Both are in the line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He slept"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He left them, went out of the city to **Bethany**, and spent the night there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He cleared the temple and then walked two miles out of the city to sleep at Lazarus's house. Every night that week."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What week this is.** Nisan 10, the day the Passover lamb was brought into the house and kept until the fourteenth. He comes into the temple on the day the lamb comes in, and the next three days are spent being questioned. See the notes on Matthew 21:23–27 and Exodus 12:1–13."}]}, "Matthew 21:18–22": {"title": "The fig tree and faith", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The only destructive miracle in the Gospels, performed on a tree."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Early in the morning, as He was **heading back to the city**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Monday of the last week. He had entered Jerusalem on the Sunday, looked round the temple, and gone back to Bethany for the night."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hunger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was hungry.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew states it plainly. A man walking two miles into the city before breakfast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tree"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seeing a fig tree beside the road, He went over to it and **found nothing on it but leaves**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark adds the detail: *for it was not the season for figs*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A fig tree in that region puts out small early fruit — **paggim** — before or with the leaves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leaves without them advertise a crop that is not there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tree is not being punished for having no figs in the wrong season. It is in leaf, which is a claim."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**May you never bear fruit again**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meketi ek sou karpos genetai eis ton aiona** — no longer let fruit come from you unto the age."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the tree **withered on the spot**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parachrema** — immediately."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark, who arranges it over two days, has them notice it the next morning: *dried up from the roots*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is on either side of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew puts the temple cleansing between the entry and this. Mark braids them: the tree is cursed, then the temple is cleared, then the tree is found withered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark's arrangement makes the reading explicit. The tree is the temple."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was in the temple"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Jesus went into the temple of God and drove out all those who bought and sold… \"**My house shall be called a house of prayer, but you have made it a den of thieves**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Full of activity, and not producing what it advertised."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disciples' question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How did the fig tree wither so quickly?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They ask about the speed, which is the least important thing in the scene."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If you have **faith and do not waver**, you will not only do what was done to this fig tree, but you will say to **this mountain**, 'Go, throw yourself into the sea,' and it will happen.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me diakrithete** — do not be divided, do not hesitate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Diakrino** — to separate, and in the middle voice, to be at odds with oneself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are standing on the road between Bethany and Jerusalem, on the Mount of Olives."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This mountain* is the one under their feet, and the sea is fifteen miles east and four thousand feet down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **whatever you ask for in prayer, believing, you will receive**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark's version adds: *and whenever you stand praying, **if you have anything against anyone, forgive him***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next in Matthew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**By what authority are You doing these things?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chief priests and elders, in the temple, the following day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the parable of the two sons, and the vineyard, and *the stone which the builders rejected*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other fig tree"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now learn this parable from the fig tree: **when its branch has already become tender and puts forth leaves, you know that summer is near**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters later, on the same mountain, the leaves are a sign to read rather than a claim to check."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the tree was doing.** Leaves before the season advertise fruit. Mark braids the cursing around the temple cleansing and leaves the reading to the reader."}]}, "Matthew 21:23–27": {"title": "By whose authority", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The examination begins, and it runs for three days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is happening this week"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lamb was brought into the house on the tenth of Nisan and kept until the fourteenth — *you shall keep it up until the fourteenth day* — and in those days it was watched for any blemish."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus rode in on the tenth. From this point until the Passover, one delegation after another comes at Him with questions."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the chief priests and elders** — by what authority?", "**the Pharisees and Herodians** — is it lawful to pay taxes to Caesar?", "**the Sadducees** — the woman with seven husbands, whose wife is she?", "**a lawyer** — which is the great commandment?"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four inspections in two chapters, from four groups who agreed on almost nothing else, and every one of them goes away with nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pilate's verdict at the end of the week is the standard formula: *I find no fault in Him.* Herod finds none. Pilate's wife, Judas, and the thief on the cross all say the same thing in their own words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lamb was examined and passed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question they ask"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**By what authority** are You doing these things? And **who gave You** this authority?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are asking about the tables. Nobody licensed Him to clear the court."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will ask you one question. If you answer it for Me, I will tell you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not refuse to answer. He puts a condition on it, and the condition is a question they cannot afford to answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question He asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Where did John's baptism come from? From heaven, or from men?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is exactly parallel to theirs. They asked where His authority came from; He asks where John's did."}, {"k": "p", "t": "If John was from heaven, then John's testimony about Him settles their question already — *behold, the Lamb of God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their working"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records their deliberation, which is the most damning thing in the passage:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If we say, 'From heaven,' He will ask us why we did not believe him. But if we say, 'From men,' **we have the crowd to fear**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one clause about what is true. Both options are weighed entirely by consequence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer they give"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"We do not know.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Men whose office was to rule on exactly this kind of question — that was the Sanhedrin's job — say in public that they cannot tell."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And His"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Then I will not tell you** by what authority I do these things.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He keeps the terms of the deal precisely."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He tells them instead.** Three parables, straight afterwards, all aimed at them: the two sons, the wicked tenants, and the wedding feast. He gives them no answer about authority and then describes them to their faces for the rest of the chapter."}]}, "Matthew 22:23–33": {"title": "An argument on a present tense", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The Sadducees bring a riddle designed to make resurrection look absurd, and He answers it from a verb."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Sadducees — **the ones who say there is no resurrection**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew defines them in the same breath as introducing them. They were the priestly aristocracy, they ran the temple, and they accepted only the five books of Moses as binding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That last detail matters for what He quotes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The riddle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven brothers, one widow, the levirate law from Deuteronomy 25. *So at the resurrection, whose wife will she be?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a genuine question. It is a stock debating trap, designed to make the doctrine collapse under its own arithmetic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You are wrong, and it is because **you do not know the Scriptures or the power of God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two failures named, and both are shortages rather than errors. They knew a great deal of Scripture professionally."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"At the resurrection people **do not marry** and are not given in marriage. They are **like the angels** in heaven.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The premise of the riddle is wrong, so the riddle does not arise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Have you never read what God said to you: '**I am** the God of Abraham, the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob'? He is **not the God of the dead, but of the living**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus 3, at the burning bush — from the books the Sadducees accepted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole argument rests on a tense. God did not say *I was* their God. He said **I am**, and He said it centuries after all three men were buried."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that requires"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If God is presently the God of Abraham, then Abraham presently is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument would fail entirely on a past tense, and Jesus builds the resurrection of the dead on the present one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"What God said to you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *what God said to Moses*. He tells them the sentence was addressed to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the crowds heard this, they were **astounded** at His teaching.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Luke adds that some of the scribes said *Teacher, You have spoken well* — the Pharisees, who did believe in resurrection, enjoying the Sadducees being beaten with their own five books."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What this week is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Passion week. Four delegations come at Him in two chapters — the chief priests and elders, the Pharisees and Herodians, these Sadducees, and a lawyer — and each goes away with nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And from that day on no one dared ask Him another question.* See the note on Matthew 22:41–46."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How much a single word can hold.** Paul does the same thing in Galatians 3 with a singular noun: *it does not say \"and to seeds,\" as though to many, but as of one.* Two arguments, one on a verb tense and one on a plural, and both treat the text as exact. See the note on Galatians 3:15–18."}]}, "Matthew 22:34–40": {"title": "\"Everything hangs on these two\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A lawyer sets a trap and Jesus answers by quoting two verses that were not usually put together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question behind the question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which is the greatest commandment in the law?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was a live rabbinic debate — the tradition counted 613 commands, and ranking them was a standing argument. Any answer would offend somebody, which is why it is described as a test."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two He picks"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Deuteronomy 6:5** — love the LORD your God with all your heart, soul and mind. This is the Shema, recited twice daily by every observant Jew. Nobody could object to it.", "**Leviticus 19:18** — love your neighbour as yourself. A single line, buried in a chapter of miscellaneous laws about gleaning, wages and honest weights."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first was the obvious answer. Pulling the second up beside it was not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The second is like it\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word means *of the same kind*. He does not rank them as first and runner-up; He says they are the same sort of thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the move the lawyer would not have expected. Love of God and love of the person next to you are put in one category."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Hangs on\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is the one used of hanging something on a peg — and of a body hanging on a cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything else, in this image, is suspended from these two. Take them away and the rest has nothing to hold it up."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the commanding runs from.** Jesus is answering a question about the law, and the answer is a command. What the New Testament adds is where the capacity comes from: *we love Him because He loved us first.* The order is not a softening of the command — it is the only thing that makes it possible. See the note on 1 John 4:19."}]}, "Matthew 22:41–46": {"title": "Whose Son is He?", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "After three days of being questioned, He asks one of His own, and it ends the week's interrogation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What do you think about the Messiah? Whose son is He?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only question in the Gospels He puts to His enemies about Himself, and it is the one everything hangs on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"David's.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Correct, and it is what every Jew would have said. The Messiah comes from David's line — 2 Samuel 7, Isaiah 11, Jeremiah 23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not wrong. They are short."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He asks next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then how is it that **David, speaking by the Spirit, calls Him Lord**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes Psalm 110:1 — *the LORD said to my Lord: sit at My right hand until I make Your enemies a footstool.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two different words. The first is **YHWH**. The second is **Adonai**, my Lord — and David is the greatest king Israel ever had, with nobody above him but God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls someone else *my Lord*, and that someone is his own descendant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If David calls Him Lord, how can He be David's son?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that culture the line ran one way. A father does not address his descendant as *my Lord*; every honour flows upward through the generations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Unless the descendant existed before the ancestor."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other time He said it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Before Abraham was, **I AM**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They picked up stones for that one. See the note on John 8:57–59."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same claim, two forms. Once to a hostile crowd in plain words, and once here, as a question, from their own songbook — and neither time does anybody misunderstand what is being said."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Psalm 110 goes on to say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD has sworn and will not relent: **You are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A king on David's throne who is also a priest, and not of Aaron's line. Hebrews spends two chapters on that verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No one could answer Him a word, and **from that day on no one dared ask Him another question**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The questioning stops. Matthew reports it as the end of it — after this the Sanhedrin stop debating and start planning."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He does immediately after.** Turns to the crowds and the disciples and delivers the seven woes, and finishes with *Jerusalem, Jerusalem, how often I have longed to gather your children.* The last public words of His ministry, and they end in tears. See the note on Matthew 23:37–39."}]}, "Matthew 23:13–15": {"title": "Doors shut in people's faces", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first of the seven woes, and the charge is about a door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You **shut the door of the kingdom of heaven in people's faces**. You will not go in yourselves, and you will not let anyone else go in either.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is of men standing in a doorway. Not outside it and not inside it — blocking it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's version says they *took away the key of knowledge*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who this is said to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Teachers of the law and Pharisees. The most careful, most scrupulous, most observant men in the nation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people who shut the kingdom are not the ones you would expect to. They are the religious experts, and what shuts it is what they preach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Pretenders\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hupokrites** — an actor, one who speaks from under a mask. It was the ordinary word for a stage performer before it became an insult."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus uses it seven times in this chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The widows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You **eat up widows' houses**, and to cover it you make long prayers.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things stacked: what they take, and what they use to hide it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *cover* is a pretext, a screen. The praying is the cover story."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The convert"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You cross land and sea to win a single convert, and when you have won him, **you make him twice as fit for hell as you are**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The effort is real — land and sea, for one person. Nobody accuses them of laziness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What they hand him at the end of it is the problem. A man taught to stand on his own performance ends up further from the door than the man who never went looking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same thing happens in Job"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three men travelled to sit with a friend in ashes, and what they preached to him — experience, tradition, a formula — produced bitterness and a defence rather than rest. And God's verdict on all three was *you have not spoken of Me what is right.* See the notes on Job 4:7–11, Job 8:8–19 and Job 11:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Preaching that puts a man back on his own resources shuts the door on him, whatever it costs the preacher to deliver it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the chapter ends.** After seven woes: *Jerusalem, Jerusalem… how often I have longed to gather your children together.* The harshest chapter in the Gospels finishes in tears over the city, and with the word *until*. See the note on Matthew 23:37–39."}]}, "Matthew 23:37–39": {"title": "\"How often I have longed\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of the harshest chapter in the Gospels, and it finishes in tears."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name, twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Jerusalem, Jerusalem**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The doubled name is how you address someone you love. *Abraham, Abraham.* *Moses, Moses.* *Simon, Simon.* *Saul, Saul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven woes have just been pronounced over the scribes and Pharisees. Then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"How often\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How often** I have longed to gather your children together.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's Gospel records perhaps three visits to Jerusalem. *How often* is the speech of somebody who has been reaching for that city for a great deal longer than three years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The One saying it is the One who sent the prophets they killed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The way a **hen** gathers her chicks under her wings.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is not lion, eagle, or shepherd. A hen — an ordinary farmyard bird with no weapons at all, whose one method of protection is to open her wings and let her chicks run under her, and to stay there and take whatever comes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wings language is the Psalms' — *under the shadow of Your wings* — and He puts Himself in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"And you were not willing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The refusal is theirs and He says so plainly. He does not say they could not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *you did not want to*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Your house is left to you empty\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look, **your house** is left to you empty.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your* house. Two chapters earlier He had called it *My house*, when He turned over the tables — *My house shall be called a house of prayer.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The possessive has changed, and the word *empty* is *desolate*, abandoned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He then walks out of the temple, and the next verse of chapter 24 has the disciples pointing at the buildings and Him saying not one stone will be left on another."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For I tell you, **you will not see Me again until** you say, 'Blessed is He who comes in the name of the Lord.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until.* It is not a closing of the door; it is a term."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The words He names are from Psalm 118, the crowd's shout at the triumphal entry five days earlier. He is saying the city will say them again, and that when it does, they will see Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last word of the harshest chapter in Matthew is a word about a reunion."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The season this describes.** Hosea's *not My people* — the face hidden, the house empty, the prophets gone. And even then it is *until*, with the words of the return already written out for them. See the notes on Hosea 1:8–9 and Romans 9:25–26."}]}, "Matthew 24:1–3": {"title": "\"Not one stone left\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The disciples point at a building and He tells them it is coming down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He has just come from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus **left the temple** and was walking away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter before ends with the seven woes and *Jerusalem, Jerusalem… your house is left to you desolate.* See the note on Matthew 23:37–39."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He walks out, and the disciples change the subject to architecture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were pointing at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Herod's temple was one of the wonders of the ancient world. Josephus describes stones of enormous size, and some of those still in the retaining wall weigh several hundred tons."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been working on it for forty-six years and were not finished. See the note on John 2:18–22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark records what one of them said: *Teacher, see what manner of stones and what buildings are here!*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Not one stone here will be left sitting on another. Every one of them will be thrown down.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In AD 70 Titus took the city. The temple burned, and the gold in it melted down between the stones — which, according to the accounts, is why they were prised apart afterwards."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He sits down"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As He was sitting on **the Mount of Olives**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Across the Kidron valley, facing the temple. They are looking at the building He has just described being demolished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah says His feet will stand on that hill."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**When will these things happen?** And **what will be the sign of Your coming**? And **of the end of the age**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They ask three things and assume they are one event."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That assumption is what makes the discourse that follows difficult — Jesus answers all three, and the answers are interleaved rather than sorted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He starts with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a date. *\"Take heed that no one deceives you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same opening as Luke's account: *be careful that no one leads you astray.* See the note on Luke 21:7–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And where He ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But of **that day and hour no one knows**, not even the angels of heaven, but My Father only.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then three parables about being ready rather than being informed. See the note on Matthew 24:36–41."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one thing they got right"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They asked privately. Matthew notes it — *the disciples came to Him privately.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever else this is, it is a conversation with four men on a hillside rather than a public announcement."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the building meant to them.** Not a landmark. The temple was where God had said He would put His name, where sin was dealt with, and where a nation's identity was held. He told them it would be rubble, and then sat down opposite it to explain."}]}, "Matthew 24:32–35": {"title": "Learn from the fig tree", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A short parable set inside the longest answer Jesus gives about the end, and it is about reading a season rather than fixing a date."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lesson"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As soon as its branch turns soft and puts out its leaves, you know that summer is near.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *turns soft* is about the branch becoming tender — sap moving in wood that was dry all winter. It happens before the leaves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is told to calculate. They are told to notice what a farmer notices."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Right at the door\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When you see all these things, you know that He is near, right at the door.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek can be read *it is near* or *He is near* — the verb has no stated subject. The Illumination takes the personal reading, and the phrase *at the door* elsewhere in the New Testament is always used of someone standing there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "James uses it: *the Judge is standing at the door.* Revelation uses it: *I stand at the door and knock.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The generation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This generation will certainly not pass away until all these things have happened.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is **genea**. It has been read as the people alive then, as the Jewish people as a race, and as the generation that sees the signs begin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence is left as He said it, and it has been argued about for two thousand years without being softened by anybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Heaven and earth will pass away, but My words will never pass away.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most permanent things a person can point at are named, and then set below His own sentences."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *will never pass away* is a double negative — the strongest form of denial available in the language."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where this sits.** The disciples had asked two questions on the Mount of Olives: when will these things be, and what will be the sign. He answers the second at length and, a few verses on, says of the first that no one knows the day or the hour."}]}, "Matthew 24:36–41": {"title": "\"As it was in the days of Noah\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He rules out date-setting in the first sentence and then describes what the time will feel like instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Nobody knows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"About that day and hour **no one knows** — not even the angels of heaven — **only My Father**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The strongest statement in Scripture against working it out. Angels do not have it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**As it was in the days of Noah**, so it will be at the coming of the Son of Man.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of everything He could have reached for, He picks the flood — and He treats it as history, not a story. His argument depends on it having happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the days of Noah looked like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"People were **eating and drinking, marrying and being given in marriage**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the list. Four ordinary things, and not one of them is a sin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not describe the violence Genesis reports, or the corruption, though both were there. He describes normal life continuing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point of the comparison is not wickedness. It is unawareness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"They knew nothing about it\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**And they knew nothing about it** until the flood came and swept them all away.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is that they did not know, did not perceive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Noah had been building for a very long time in plain sight, and 2 Peter calls him *a preacher of righteousness*. The information was available."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Right up to the day Noah entered the ark\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The door stayed open through the whole of the building. It shut on one particular day, and the LORD shut it. See the note on Genesis 7:11–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "One taken, one left"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Two men will be in the field; **one will be taken and the other left**. Two women will be grinding at the mill; one will be taken and the other left.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two pairs, doing identical work, side by side. Nothing visible separates them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Readers have long divided over which is the better outcome — taken, or left. In the Noah comparison just made, the ones *swept away* were taken by the flood and Noah was left; in the Greek of verse 40 the word for *taken* is the one Jesus uses in John 14 for *I will come again and receive you to Myself*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage does not settle it, and He does not pause to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He tells them to do about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not calculate. The next verse: *\"Watch therefore, for you do not know what hour your Lord is coming.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then three parables in a row about it — the householder and the thief, the servant left in charge, the ten virgins with their lamps. All three are about being ready rather than being informed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He says about His own knowledge.** Mark's version keeps a clause Matthew also has: *nor the Son, but only the Father.* He states the limit about Himself, out loud, and then goes straight on teaching about the day anyway."}]}, "Matthew 24:4–8": {"title": "The beginning of birth pains", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first thing He says in answer to the disciples' question is a warning about being misled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had shown Him the temple buildings and He had said *not one stone shall be left here upon another*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then, on the Mount of Olives, privately: *when will these things be? And what will be the sign of Your coming, and of the end of the age?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three questions. The chapter is the answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Be careful that no one leads you astray**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **planese** — deceive, cause to wander. The root of *planet*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before any sign is named, the first thing said is about deception."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He returns to it: *many false prophets will rise up and deceive many*, and *false christs and false prophets will rise and show great signs and wonders, so as to deceive, if possible, even the elect*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times in one chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Many will come using My name**, saying, '**I am the Messiah**,' and they will lead many astray.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not opponents. People using the name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wars"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You will **hear of wars and rumors of wars**, but **do not let yourselves be alarmed**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **throeisthe** — be frightened, be startled into panic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause that governs the paragraph"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**These things have to happen, but the end is still to come**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two halves. They must occur, and they are not the marker."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it again at the end of the section: *all of this is only the beginning*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The warning is against reading the news as a countdown."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Nation will rise against nation, and kingdom against kingdom**, and there will be **famines and plagues and earthquakes in place after place**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *in various places* — **kata topous**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"All of this is only **the beginning of the birth pains**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **odinon** — labour pains."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is precise, and Jesus chose it. Labour pains are real, they intensify, they come in a pattern, and they are not the disaster they feel like — they are the beginning of something arriving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same word of creation: *the whole creation groans and labours with birth pangs together until now.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says will be happening at the same time"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fifteen verses later: *and **this gospel of the kingdom will be preached in all the world as a witness to all the nations**, and then the end will come.* See the note on Matthew 24:9–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two run together in the same chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But of **that day and hour no one knows**, not even the angels of heaven, but My Father only.\"* See the note on Matthew 24:36–41."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then three parables about being ready — the servant, the ten virgins, and the talents — and the sheep and the goats."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole discourse repeats"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Watch therefore, for you do not know what hour your Lord is coming.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He was sitting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Opposite the temple He had just walked out of, two days before the arrest."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The first instruction of the chapter.** Not to watch the news. To make sure nobody misleads you — and then a list of alarming things with *do not be alarmed* attached to it."}]}, "Matthew 24:9–14": {"title": "\"The love of most people will grow cold\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Part of the answer to a question the disciples asked on the Mount of Olives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had asked three things: *when will these things be? And what will be the sign of Your coming, and of the end of the age?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole chapter is the answer, and it moves between the fall of Jerusalem and something further out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The handing over"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then they will **hand you over to be persecuted and put to death**, and you will be **hated by all nations because of My name**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *hand over* is **paradosousin** — the same verb used of Judas, and of Christ being delivered up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Because of My name** — the reason given is not conduct."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The falling away"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"At that time **many will fall away**, and they will **betray one another and hate one another**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **skandalisthesontai** — tripped up, made to stumble. From **skandalon**, the trigger of a trap."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The betraying is internal — *one another*, twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The false prophets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Many false prophets will appear and **lead many astray**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus says it three times in the chapter: at the start, here, and again at verse 24 — *false christs and false prophets will rise and show great signs and wonders*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mechanism"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Because lawlessness will spread**, the love of most people will grow cold.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *lawlessness* is **anomia** — from *a-nomos*, without law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Will spread** — the verb is to multiply."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus gives a cause and an effect, and the effect is about temperature."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cooling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The love of most people will grow cold**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **psugesetai** — to be chilled, to cool by blowing on. It occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for love is **agape**, the highest of the four Greek words for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that love is replaced by hatred. That it drops in temperature."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Most people** — the Greek is *the many*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one who holds on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **the one who holds on to the end will be saved**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hupomeinas** — from **hupomeno**, to remain under. The endurance word, the same one James says testing produces. See the note on James 1:3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proclamation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **this good news of the kingdom will be proclaimed in the whole world as a testimony to all nations**, and then the end will come.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *inhabited world* is **oikoumene** — the whole populated earth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**As a testimony** — the Greek is **marturion**, evidence. The preaching is described as the depositing of testimony rather than as a result achieved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the paragraph"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Persecution, falling away, betrayal, false prophets, lawlessness multiplying, love cooling — and then, in the middle of all of it, the message going everywhere."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus does not present the two as alternatives. Both run at once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He is sitting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now as He sat on the Mount of Olives, the disciples came to Him privately.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two days before the arrest, opposite the temple He had just walked out of, having said *not one stone shall be left here upon another*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one sentence about the reader.** Everything else in the paragraph is about what conditions will do. *The one who holds on to the end* is the only clause with a person in it, and the verb means to stay under the weight."}]}, "Matthew 25:1–13": {"title": "Ten lamps and a delay", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The parable turns on one thing, and it is not the sleeping."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Jewish wedding. The bridegroom comes to the bride's house at an hour nobody publishes, and the party escorts him back to his father's house with lamps."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Waiting is built into the arrangement. See the note on John 14:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ten"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ten bridesmaids who took their lamps and went out to meet the bridegroom.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "All ten go out. All ten have lamps. All ten are expecting him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Five of them were **foolish** and five were **wise**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **morai** and **phronimoi** — the same pair as the two builders at the end of the Sermon on the Mount."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The difference"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The foolish ones **took their lamps but took no oil** with them. The wise ones **took jars of oil** along with their lamps.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One item. Everything else about the ten is identical."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The foolish are not lazy or hostile. They turned up. They simply brought nothing to keep the light going."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The delay"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The bridegroom was **a long time coming**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **chronizontos** — taking a long time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The delay is in the parable on purpose. It is what exposes the difference, and nothing else would have."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sleeping"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **they all grew drowsy and fell asleep**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "All ten. The wise sleep too."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the parable is warning about, it is not sleeping — the ones who are commended do it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At **midnight** the cry went up: \"**Here is the bridegroom! Come out to meet him!**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trimming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **all ten** bridesmaids woke up and **trimmed their lamps**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "All ten wake instantly and all ten reach for the same task."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The difference does not appear until the lamps have been burning a minute."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Give us some of your oil; **our lamps are going out**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a present tense — they are being extinguished, now."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No** — there may not be enough for us and for you. **Go to those who sell oil and buy some for yourselves**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It reads harshly. It is the parable's point: this is not transferable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody can lend it, and it cannot be acquired in the last five minutes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The door"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **while they were away buying it, the bridegroom arrived**. The ones who were ready went in with him to the wedding feast, and **the door was shut**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Sir, sir, open the door for us.\" But he answered, \"**I tell you the truth, I do not know you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ouk oida humas**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same words as the end of the Sermon on the Mount: *many will say to Me in that day, \"Lord, Lord, have we not prophesied in Your name?\"… and then I will declare to them, \"I never knew you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both times the address is doubled, and both times the answer is about knowing rather than doing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The moral He attaches"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **stay awake**, because you do not know the day or the hour.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **gregoreite** — be watchful."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is odd, since all ten slept and five were fine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The watching He asks for is not staying conscious. It is the state of being supplied before the night starts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the Olivet discourse and the parable of the talents, in the last week."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three parables in a row about the interval: the servant whose master delays, the ten lamps, and the talents. All three are about what people do while nothing is happening."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one variable.** Not attentiveness — all ten slept. Five of them had brought oil before the waiting started, and the delay is what made the difference show."}]}, "Matthew 25:24–30": {"title": "\"I knew you to be a hard man\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The third servant explains himself, and his first sentence is about the master rather than the money."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I knew you to be a hard man**, harvesting where you did not plant and gathering where you scattered no seed.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is his opening line, and it is the whole problem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *hard* is **skleros** — harsh, stiff, rough. It is the root of *sclerosis*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows from it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So I was afraid**, and I went and hid your talent in the ground.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So.* He states the cause himself, and it is a straight line: this is what I thought you were like, therefore I was afraid, therefore I buried it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the amount. The man with two talents had a small share too, and doubled it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was actually given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the first half of the parable. *To one he gave five talents, to another two, and to another one, **each according to what he was able to handle**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A talent was an enormous sum — years of a labourer's wages. He was not slighted; he was given what he could carry, by a master who had assessed him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the master handed over *his property* and left. It is an act of trust from a man about to be absent for a long time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Here is what belongs to you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "He returns it intact. Nothing lost, nothing stolen, nothing wasted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By his own accounting he has done nothing wrong. That is what fear produces — carefulness that looks like integrity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The master's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So you knew** that I harvest where I have not planted?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not concede the description. He argues from inside it — *if that is what you believed, then at the very least the bank.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rebuke is that the man did not even act consistently with his own fear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take the talent away from him.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The other two are told *well done, good and faithful servant… enter into the joy of your lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The difference between them was not ability, opportunity or amount. It was what each believed about the man who gave it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For everyone who has will be given more… but from the one who has nothing, even what he has will be taken away.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same saying Mark attaches to how you listen. See the note on Mark 4:24–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus never says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not correct the servant's picture of God by softening the master. He lets the parable stand and puts the burden where the servant put it — on what he thought he was dealing with."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the other two did first.** *Went **at once** and put them to work.* No deliberation is recorded. They knew the man, and knowing him is what made them quick."}]}, "Matthew 26:14–16": {"title": "Thirty pieces of silver", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses, and the price is quoted from a prophet nobody in the room was thinking of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The alabaster jar at Bethany. A woman pours very costly ointment on His head and the disciples are indignant: *why this waste? For this fragrant oil might have been sold for much and given to the poor.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John names the objector: Judas, *not that he cared for the poor, but because he was a thief, and had the money box*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then* — Matthew's next word — Judas goes to the chief priests."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The introduction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **one of the Twelve, the one called Judas Iscariot**, went to the chief priests.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One of the Twelve.* Matthew, Mark and Luke all use the phrase, and all three use it here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been sent out with the others to preach and heal. He had been given authority over unclean spirits with the rest of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What will you pay me**,\" he asked, \"**if I hand Him over to you?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names no figure. He asks what they will give."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paradoso** — hand over, deliver up. The same verb used of what the Father does with the Son in Romans: *He who did not spare His own Son, but **delivered Him up** for us all*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The amount"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **counted out thirty pieces of silver** into his hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus 21 fixes the value: *if the ox gores a manservant or a maidservant, he shall give to their master **thirty shekels of silver**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The compensation for a slave gored by a bull."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the figure comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah 11, the shepherd who is dismissed and asks for his wages:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they weighed out for my wages thirty pieces of silver. And the LORD said to me, \"**Throw it to the potter** — that princely price they set on me.\" So I took the thirty pieces of silver and threw them into the house of the LORD for the potter.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sarcasm is in the Hebrew. *That princely price.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Judas does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Judas, His betrayer, seeing that He had been condemned, was remorseful and brought back the thirty pieces of silver to the chief priests… saying, \"I have sinned by betraying innocent blood.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he **threw down the pieces of silver in the temple** and departed, and went and hanged himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thrown into the house of the LORD, as Zechariah wrote."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the priests do with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is not lawful to put them into the treasury, because they are the price of blood.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they consulted together and **bought with them the potter's field**, to bury strangers in.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To the potter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were scrupulous about the treasury and not about the transaction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was watching for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From that moment on he was **watching for the right opening**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eukairian** — a good opportunity, a favourable moment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He needed Jesus away from the crowds, and he knew where the garden was: *Judas, who betrayed Him, also knew the place, for Jesus often met there with His disciples*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He said at the table"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The Son of Man indeed goes just as it is written of Him, but woe to that man by whom the Son of Man is betrayed**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves at once, and Matthew does not soften either."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Judas: *Rabbi, is it I?* — the only one of the twelve who says *Rabbi* rather than *Lord*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the sum was.** Not an arbitrary bribe. Thirty shekels is the figure Exodus sets for a slave killed by somebody's animal, and Zechariah had already called it a princely price."}]}, "Matthew 26:26–30": {"title": "\"This is My body, this is My blood\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's account adds one phrase the others do not have, and it is the phrase about what the blood is for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Matthew adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is My blood — the blood of the new covenant, poured out for many **for the forgiveness of sins**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark and Luke stop at *for many*. Matthew names the purpose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the Gospel that opened with an angel saying *you shall call His name Jesus, for He shall save His people from their sins*. Matthew closes the circle at the table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Every one of you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Every one of you**, drink from it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Matthew it is a command, not just a report. Mark records that they all drank; Matthew records that they were all told to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody at that table had earned it. The betrayer is present and the man who will deny Him three times before morning is holding a cup."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two elements, two things given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cup is given a stated purpose — forgiveness. The bread is given without one: *take this and eat it. This is My body.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew has already told us what the body did. In chapter 8 he quotes Isaiah over a night of healings: *He took our infirmities and bore our diseases.* The same writer, the same book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whoever eats the bread and drinks the cup is receiving two things, not one. See the note on 1 Corinthians 11:27–30, where Paul's warning is about failing to distinguish them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Thanks, twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **gave thanks** over it… and when He had **given thanks** He passed it to them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eucharisteō** — the word the meal has been named after ever since."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gave thanks over bread He was about to call His broken body, hours before the whipping post. The thanks came first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The unfinished cup"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will not drink of the fruit of the vine again until the day I drink it new with you in My Father's kingdom.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**With you.** Matthew keeps the pronoun. The next cup has a guest list."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The hymn.** *Then they sang a hymn together and went out to the Mount of Olives.* The Passover ended with the second half of the Hallel — Psalm 118 among them, which says *the LORD is on my side, I will not fear; what can man do to me?* He walked from that hymn into the garden."}]}, "Matthew 26:31–35": {"title": "Before the rooster crows", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He tells them all of them will run, and quotes a prophet to explain why."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the way.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the upper room and Gethsemane, walking across the Kidron valley in the dark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have just sung a hymn — the second half of the Hallel, Psalms 115 to 118, which is what was sung at the end of a Passover meal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prediction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Tonight every one of you will fall away from Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **skandalisthesesthe** — you will be made to stumble."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one. He does not exempt anybody, and the eleven include the man writing this account."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It stands written: '**I will strike down the shepherd, and the sheep of the flock will be scattered**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah 13:7. See the note on Zechariah 13:7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew there is *awake, O sword, against My Shepherd, against the Man who is My Companion*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the pronoun He uses: **I** will strike. In Zechariah the sword is summoned by God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not saying the desertion is their fault alone. He is saying the shepherd is struck, and sheep do what sheep do when that happens."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause after it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **after I am raised up, I will go on ahead of you into Galilee**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said in the same breath as the desertion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **proaxo humas** — I will go before you, which is shepherd language: the shepherd walks in front."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scattering is announced and the regathering is announced at the same time, and the second does not depend on their performance in between."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angel repeats it at the tomb: *He is going before you into Galilee; there you will see Him, as He said to you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Peter's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Even if all the rest of them fall away, I never will**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He accepts the prediction for the other ten and exempts himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I tell you the truth, Peter. **Tonight, before the rooster crows, you will say three times that you never knew Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Tonight. Before a bird. Three times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every element is specified so that there can be no reinterpretation afterwards."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The escalation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Even if I have to die with You, I will not deny You**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter means it. He draws a sword in the garden within the hour and takes off a man's ear, which is not the act of a coward."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not lying about his courage. He is wrong about its reach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chorus"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **every one of the disciples said the same**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records it. All eleven make the same promise, and all eleven are gone by the end of the chapter: *then all the disciples forsook Him and fled*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Simon, Simon! Indeed, **Satan has asked for you, that he may sift you as wheat. But I have prayed for you, that your faith should not fail**; and when you have returned to Me, strengthen your brethren.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *if*. When."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer was made before the denial, and the assignment was given before the failure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The courtyard, the servant girl, the accent, and the rooster. See the note on Matthew 26:69–75."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens after that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A beach, a charcoal fire, and three questions. See the note on John 21:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three denials, three questions, and *feed My sheep* — said to a man who had been told, on this walk, that the flock would scatter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is said in the same breath as the desertion.** *After I am raised, I will go before you into Galilee.* The regathering is announced before the scattering has happened."}]}, "Matthew 26:36–39": {"title": "Gethsemane", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The garden, and Matthew does not hide any of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They came to a place called **Gethsemane**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name means **oil press** — **gat shemanim**. An olive grove on the slope of the Mount of Olives, with a press in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fruit is crushed to get what is in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arrangement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Sit down here** while **I go over there and pray**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The eight are left at the entrance. Three go further."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He took **Peter and the two sons of Zebedee**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same three from Jairus's house and the mountain. See the note on Matthew 17:1–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had seen Him transfigured. Now they see this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What came over Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **grief came over Him, and a heaviness He did not hide**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **lupeisthai** and **ademonein** — the second is a rare word for distress, anguish, being overwhelmed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark has an even stronger word: **ekthambeisthai**, appalled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He tells them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My soul is crushed with sorrow**,\" He told them. \"**It is almost more than I can bear**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **perilupos estin he psuche mou heos thanatou** — sorrowful to the point of death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells them. He does not manage it privately and does not perform composure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Stay here, and keep watch with Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **gregoreite** — stay awake. The same word Peter later uses in his letter. See the note on 1 Peter 5:8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They fall asleep three times, and He comes back three times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **He went on a little farther**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke says *about a stone's throw*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Near enough to be seen and far enough to be alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The posture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **fell with His face to the ground**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not kneeling. Face down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My Father**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark preserves the Aramaic: **Abba**. It is the word Paul says the Spirit puts in the believer's mouth. See the note on Galatians 4:4–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If there is any way, let this cup pass from Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *if it is possible*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cup is the Old Testament image for what a person is given to drink — and in the prophets it is usually the cup of wrath. Isaiah: *you have drunk the cup of His fury*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause after it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Yet not what I want — what You want**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records three prayers. The first has *if it is possible*. The second changes: *if this cup cannot pass away from Me unless I drink it, Your will be done.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The request shifts once and the last clause never does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews says about the same night"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who, in the days of His flesh, when He had offered up **prayers and supplications, with vehement cries and tears** to Him who was able to save Him from death, **and was heard** because of His godly fear.* See the note on Hebrews 5:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**And was heard.** The writer says the prayer was answered — and the cup was not taken."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then an angel appeared to Him from heaven, strengthening Him. And being in agony, He prayed more earnestly. Then His sweat became like great drops of blood falling down to the ground.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says when He comes back the last time"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Rise, let us be going. See, My betrayer is at hand.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same words He used in the upper room. See the note on John 14:28–31."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He does not do.** Hide it. He tells three men He is crushed, asks them to stay awake, goes face down, and asks — twice with an *if* and every time with the same last clause."}]}, "Matthew 26:69–75": {"title": "His accent gave him away", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three denials, and each one is louder than the last."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Out in the courtyard Peter was **sitting**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Inside the high priest's compound, at a fire, while the trial goes on in the building."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the only disciple who got this close. The others had *forsaken Him and fled* in the garden."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew has already noted why he came: *Peter followed Him at a distance… to see the end*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One of **the servant girls** came over. \"**You were with Jesus of Galilee**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an official. A girl on the staff."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **denied it in front of all of them**. \"I do not know what you are talking about.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A denial of comprehension, not of the fact. The first one is evasive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The move"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He got up and **moved toward the gateway**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Toward the exit, and not out of it. He gets closer to leaving three times and never leaves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Another girl saw him and said to the people standing there, \"**This man was with Jesus of Nazareth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Again he denied it, and this time **he swore an oath**: \"**I do not know the man**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Now it is a flat denial of the person, under oath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew has recorded a teaching about this: *do not swear at all… let your yes be yes*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You are certainly one of them**,\" they said. \"**Your accent gives you away**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *your speech makes you evident*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Galilean could not say the gutturals the way a Judean did. Judges 12 has the same test with *shibboleth*, and forty-two thousand men died on a pronunciation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's mouth convicts him before he opens it on the subject."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Peter began to **curse and swear**. \"**I do not know the man!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katathematizein** — to call down curses, on himself if he is lying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rooster"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **right then a rooster crowed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke adds the detail Matthew leaves out: *and the Lord turned and looked at Peter*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he remembered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Peter remembered what Jesus had said to him: **before the rooster crows, you will deny Me three times**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had answered that prediction with *even if I have to die with You, I will not deny You*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weeping"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He went outside and **wept bitterly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eklausen pikros**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judas is in the next chapter, and his word is different — **metamelomai**, remorse. See the note on 2 Corinthians 7:10–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same night, same failure of nerve, two different words and two different ends."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What had been said in advance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Simon, Simon! Indeed, Satan has asked for you, that he may sift you as wheat. But **I have prayed for you, that your faith should not fail**; and **when you have returned to Me**, strengthen your brethren.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *if*. When. The restoration was arranged before the failure happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the fire turns up again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John uses one unusual word for the coals in this courtyard — **anthrakian** — and uses it exactly once more, on a beach, with breakfast on it. See the note on John 21:4–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three denials at one fire, and three questions at the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does fifty days later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stands up in front of Jerusalem: *this Jesus, whom you crucified, God has made both Lord and Christ*. Three thousand are added that day. See the note on Acts 2:40–41."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who could not hold his nerve in front of a servant girl preaches to the city that killed Him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What identified him.** Not evidence, and not a witness. The way he pronounced his words — the same mouth that was busy denying it."}]}, "Matthew 27:27–31": {"title": "A crown of thorns", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A whole garrison assembles to make fun of one prisoner, and every prop they use means something they do not know."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The company"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Called **the whole company together** around Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a cohort — the tenth part of a legion, several hundred men."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not busy. This is entertainment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The robe"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **stripped Him** and put **a scarlet robe** on Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark and John call it purple; Matthew calls it scarlet. It is a soldier's cloak, faded red, used as a stand-in for imperial purple."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They dress Him as a king in order to laugh at Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crown"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **twisted thorn branches into a crown** and pressed it onto His head.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thorns entered the world in Genesis 3, in the sentence on the ground: *cursed is the ground for your sake… **both thorns and thistles** it shall bring forth for you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are the visible sign of the curse on the earth, and the soldiers press a circle of them into His scalp."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And put **a reed in His right hand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A mock sceptre. Then: *they took the reed out of His hand, and **struck Him on the head with it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thing they gave Him as a joke is used to drive the thorns further in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The kneeling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then they **knelt down in front of Him** and jeered, \"**Hail, king of the Jews!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every gesture is correct. The robe, the crown, the sceptre, the bent knee, the acclamation — the full ceremony of homage to a monarch."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are performing, with total accuracy, something that is true."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Philippians says every knee will bow eventually. A cohort of Roman soldiers got there first, and did it as a joke."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The spitting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **spat on Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah had it: *I gave My back to those who struck Me, and My cheeks to those who plucked out the beard; **I did not hide My face from shame and spitting**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then they dressed Him again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When they were finished mocking Him **they took the robe off, put His own clothes back on Him**, and led Him away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mock robe comes off and His own clothes go back on — the ones that will be divided among four soldiers within hours, with lots cast for the seamless tunic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Pilate had already said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I find no fault in Him at all* — three times in John's account."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The examination had passed. What follows is not judicial; it is a barracks with nothing to do."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the thorns were made of.** The curse on the ground, worn on His head — and Paul: *Christ has redeemed us from the curse of the law, having become a curse for us.* See the note on Galatians 3:10–14."}]}, "Matthew 27:32–34": {"title": "Simon of Cyrene", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses of logistics, and two details in them are worth stopping on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the way out they **came across a man from Cyrene named Simon**, and they **forced him to carry the cross**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Cyrene** is in North Africa, in what is now Libya. There was a large Jewish community there, and Cyrenians are on the list of nationalities at Pentecost. See the note on Acts 2:9–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is in Jerusalem for Passover, a long way from home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Forced\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **angareuo** — a Persian loan-word for the right of a courier or soldier to commandeer a civilian for a task."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same verb in the Sermon on the Mount: *whoever **compels** you to go one mile, go with him two.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Simon is conscripted. He did not volunteer and could not refuse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it was needed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The condemned man carried the crossbeam to the site. John records that Jesus went out *bearing His cross*, so this happens partway."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been scourged, and the Gospels do not explain further."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Mark adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Simon, **the father of Alexander and Rufus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark names the sons, which only makes sense if the readers knew them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul closes Romans with *greet **Rufus**, chosen in the Lord, and his mother and mine.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They came to a place called **Golgotha**, which means **the Place of the Skull**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Aramaic is **gulgolta**. Matthew translates it for his readers, as he does with a few other terms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The drink"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There they **offered Jesus vinegar mixed with gall**, and **He tasted it and would not drink it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark says wine mixed with myrrh. It was a sedative, offered to dull what was coming — tradition holds that the women of Jerusalem prepared it as an act of mercy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two actions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **tasted** it* — so He knew what it was."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **would not drink it**.* The Greek is *He was not willing*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He declines the anaesthetic knowingly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The psalm underneath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They also gave me **gall for my food**, and for my thirst they gave me **vinegar to drink**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 69, and Matthew's wording follows it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other drink"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Later, at the end: *\"I thirst\"… immediately one of them ran and took a sponge, filled it with sour wine and put it on a reed, and offered it to Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That one He takes. John says *when Jesus had received the sour wine, He said, \"It is finished!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He refuses the drink that would dull it and accepts the one that lets Him speak."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The dividing of the garments, the sign over His head, the two robbers, the darkness from the sixth hour, the cry, and the veil torn from top to bottom. See the note on Matthew 27:51–53."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Simon got.** A conscription notice, in a foreign city, on a feast day. Mark assumes his sons are known to the church in Rome thirty years later."}]}, "Matthew 27:45–47": {"title": "Darkness over the land", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's account of the sixth to the ninth hour, and it is three verses long."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From **the sixth hour, at midday**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Noon. The sun at its highest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dark"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Darkness came over **the whole land**, and it **stayed until the ninth hour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three hours."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Passover falls at full moon, when a solar eclipse is astronomically impossible. Whatever this was, it was not that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is happening at the ninth hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The evening sacrifice, and the Passover lambs being killed at the temple a short walk away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And at about the ninth hour Jesus **cried out with a loud voice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **aneboesen phone megale**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man dying of crucifixion suffocates slowly. Volume is the last thing he has."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Eli, Eli, lama sabachthani?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew keeps the Hebrew form **Eli**; Mark has the Aramaic **Eloi**. See the note on Mark 15:33–36."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The difference matters for the next verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The translation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My God, My God, why have You abandoned Me?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 22:1, and the psalm goes on to describe a great deal of what is happening on that hill: *they pierced My hands and My feet*, *they divide My garments among them*, and *all who see Me laugh Me to scorn*. See the note on Psalms 22:16–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew has already recorded two of those in the previous paragraph."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm ends with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have answered Me… I will declare Your name to My brethren; in the midst of the assembly I will praise You.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All the ends of the world shall remember and turn to the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the final words in Hebrew: **ki asah** — He has done it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm that opens with abandonment closes with a finished work and the nations turning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The misunderstanding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Some of those standing there heard it and said, \"**He is calling for Elijah**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Eli** and **Eliyahu**. Close, at a distance, in a crowd, from a man who had been scourged."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elijah was expected before the day of the LORD — Malachi's closing sentence — and there was a popular belief that he came to help the righteous in extremity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Immediately one of them ran and took a sponge, filled it with sour wine and put it on a reed, and offered it to Him to drink.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The rest said, \"Let Him alone; **let us see if Elijah will come to save Him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last recorded human speech before the death is a proposal to watch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next in Matthew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Jesus cried out again with a loud voice, and yielded up His spirit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then, behold, **the veil of the temple was torn in two from top to bottom**; and the earth quaked, and the rocks were split.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew alone adds the graves opened and many bodies of the saints raised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the centurion: *truly this was the Son of God!*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abandoned.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **egkatelipes** — left behind, forsaken."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only recorded sentence from the cross in which He does not address God as *Father*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the darkness had meant before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ninth plague — three days of it, *darkness which may even be felt*, over Egypt only, and *all the children of Israel had light in their dwellings*. See the note on Exodus 10:21–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The plague immediately before the first Passover."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Amos: *I will make the sun go down at noon, and I will darken the earth in broad daylight*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He quoted.** Not a cry He composed. The first line of a psalm whose last two words in Hebrew are *He has done it*."}]}, "Matthew 27:51–53": {"title": "Torn from the top", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four things happen at the moment Jesus dies, and the first one is reported with a detail that carries the whole meaning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The curtain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The curtain of the temple was torn in two, **from the top all the way down to the bottom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The veil separated the Most Holy Place from everything else. Behind it was the place a single man entered once a year with blood, in smoke, having first atoned for himself. See the note on Leviticus 16:11–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was not opened. It was **torn**, and torn from the end nobody could reach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew does not explain it. He records the direction and moves on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The earthquake"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ground shook and the rocks split apart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew has a pattern of noting the earth's reaction — there is another quake at the resurrection two chapters on, and it is the centurion's response to this one that produces the confession *truly this was the Son of God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tombs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Tombs broke open, and the bodies of many of God's people who had died were raised to life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The strangest paragraph in the Gospel, and Matthew alone reports it. He is careful about the timing: the tombs open at the death, but the people do not come out until **after** the resurrection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Even here, Christ is kept as the firstfruits — nobody precedes Him out of a grave."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Matthew does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He offers no interpretation of any of it. Four events, reported flat, in two sentences."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reader is left to notice that at the moment the sacrifice was finished, the barrier came down, the ground broke, and death started letting people go."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the letter to the Hebrews takes it.** *We have confidence to enter the holy places by the blood of Jesus, by the new and living way that He opened for us through the curtain, that is, through His flesh.* The torn veil and the torn body are put together, and the conclusion drawn is an invitation to come in. See the note on Hebrews 4:14–16."}]}, "Matthew 27:54–56": {"title": "The centurion and the women", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first confession of faith after the death comes from the officer in charge of the execution."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **centurion and the soldiers keeping watch with him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The detail assigned to the crucifixion. They had done the nailing, cast lots for the clothes, and offered Him the sour wine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Professionals. They had seen men die before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they experienced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Felt **the earth shake** and **saw everything that happened**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew has just recorded it: the darkness from the sixth hour, the cry, the veil torn from top to bottom, and an earthquake. See the note on Matthew 27:51–53."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they were **filled with fear**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *they feared greatly*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The confession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This man really was the Son of God**,\" they said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark gives it to the centurion alone. Luke has a different sentence: *certainly this was a righteous Man.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek has no article — *a son of God* is a possible rendering, and Roman ears would have heard it that way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records what they said, in their vocabulary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The irony Matthew builds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The title has been thrown at Him all day as a taunt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passers-by: *if You are the Son of God, come down from the cross.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chief priests: *He trusted in God; let Him deliver Him now if He will have Him; for He said, \"I am the Son of God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one man who says it and means it is the officer who killed Him, and he says it because He did not come down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other centurions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every centurion in the New Testament comes off well. The one at Capernaum whose faith Jesus called greater than any in Israel; this one; Cornelius; and Julius, who kept Paul alive on the ship. See the notes on Matthew 8:10–13 and Acts 10:1–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The women"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A number of women were there as well, **watching from a distance** — women who had **followed Jesus all the way from Galilee and cared for Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *cared for* is **diakonousai** — served, ministered. The root of *deacon*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke names some of them earlier and says they *provided for Him from their substance*. They had been funding the ministry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is named"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James and Joses, and the mother of Zebedee's sons.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last one had asked, not long before, for her two boys to sit at His right and left in the kingdom. See the note on Matthew 20:24–28."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is at the cross, and two men are at His right and left."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is not there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The eleven. Matthew has already recorded it: *then all the disciples forsook Him and fled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John's Gospel puts John himself at the cross with Mary. Matthew's list is women."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the women do next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Follow Joseph of Arimathea to the tomb and sit opposite it. Then come back after the sabbath, first light, and find it open."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are the last at the cross and the first at the tomb, and Matthew keeps them in every scene."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who says it first.** The officer commanding the execution squad, after an earthquake, about a man he had just watched die — and the reason it lands is that the taunt all day had been *come down*."}]}, "Matthew 28:16–20": {"title": "Some of them were still unsure", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last five verses of Matthew, and there is a clause in the middle nobody quotes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The eleven disciples went to **Galilee, to the mountain where Jesus had told them to go**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not Jerusalem. Home ground — where He had called them, taught the crowds, and fed the five thousand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said it twice: on the walk to Gethsemane, *after I have been raised, I will go before you to Galilee*, and at the tomb through the angel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The eleven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew keeps the count exact. Judas is dead and not replaced yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The worship"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when they saw Him they **fell down and worshiped Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **prosekunesan** — prostrated themselves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though **some of them were still unsure**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hoi de edistasan** — but they doubted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Distazo** occurs twice in the New Testament, and the other is Peter on the water: *O you of little faith, **why did you doubt?***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is closer to hesitation than to disbelief. Divided, wavering, in two minds."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records it and does not resolve it. The men receiving the commission include some who are not sure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**All authority in heaven and on earth has been given to Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pasa exousia** — all authority."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Has been given* — a passive, and Daniel is behind it: *to Him was given dominion and glory and a kingdom, that all peoples, nations, and languages should serve Him*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is also what was offered on a mountain three years earlier, for one act of worship, and refused. See the note on Matthew 4:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same offer, different mountain, different price."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one imperative"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So go — go and make disciples of all the nations**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek has one main verb: **matheteusate**, make disciples."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Go*, *baptising* and *teaching* are participles hanging off it. The older translations make *go* an imperative; the grammar makes it circumstantial — *as you go*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Baptizing them in **the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The name**, singular, with three genitives."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The clearest trinitarian formula in the Gospels, and it is in a baptism instruction rather than in a doctrinal passage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The teaching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And teaching them **to live out everything I have commanded you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **terein** — to keep, to observe, to guard."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to know it. To keep it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And remember, **I am with you always, right to the very end of the age**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ego meth' humon eimi** — I, with you, am. The pronoun is emphatic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Matthew started"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They shall call His name **Immanuel**,\" which is translated, \"**God with us**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "First chapter and last chapter, and the same three words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Gospel opens with a name and closes with the thing the name means, said in the first person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is said to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven men on a hill, some of whom are still hesitating."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The commission is not conditional on the hesitation clearing up."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clause in the middle.** *Some doubted.* Matthew puts it between the worship and the commission, and does not come back to it."}]}, "Matthew 3:16–17": {"title": "What was said before He had done anything", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The Father speaks aloud twice in Matthew's Gospel. This is the first, and the timing is the whole point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it is said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus has not preached a sermon, healed anybody, called a disciple or told a parable. His public ministry begins in the next chapter, after forty days in the desert."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The words come first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is My Son, the One I love. My delight is in Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three statements: identity, love, and pleasure. Nothing is conditional and nothing refers to output."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek behind *my delight is in Him* is a verb about being well pleased, taking pleasure in something — and it is in a tense that reads as a settled state rather than a reaction to a recent event."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two texts underneath it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence is stitched from two places every listener would have known:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*You are my Son* — Psalm 2, spoken of the anointed king", "*in whom my soul delights* — Isaiah 42, the opening of the first Servant Song"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "King and servant, in one line. Matthew's readers would have heard both, and the second one leads to the servant who is pierced for transgressions."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where else the words turn up.** On the mountain at the transfiguration, the same voice says nearly the same thing and adds three words: *listen to Him.* The Father's public statements about the Son are remarkably consistent, and neither of them is about performance."}]}, "Matthew 4:1–4": {"title": "\"If You are the Son of God\" — the word the tempter starts with", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Read what happened immediately before this. At the end of chapter 3, the Father's voice says *this is My Son, the One I love. My delight is in Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the first word out of the tempter's mouth is **if**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The attack"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If You are the Son of God, tell these stones to turn into bread.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not attack the hunger. He attacks the sonship — and he attacks it by suggesting it needs demonstrating. Do something, and the question will be settled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus had nothing to prove. The Father had already said it out loud, in public, before a single miracle had been performed. See the note on Matthew 3:16–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He answers with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is written: a person does not live on bread alone, but on every word that comes from the mouth of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes Deuteronomy 8 — Moses telling Israel why God let them hunger in the wilderness and fed them with manna."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the deliberate parallel. Israel spent forty years in the desert and failed the tests. Jesus spends forty days in one and does not, and He answers each time with a line from the book about the first forty years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word He lives on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every word that comes from the mouth of God.* He has one in hand already: *this is My Son, the One I love.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The temptation is answered by holding onto what has been said rather than producing evidence for it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who led Him there.** *Then the Spirit led Jesus up into the wilderness.* The desert is not a detour or a failure of guidance. Matthew puts the Spirit's leading and the testing in the same sentence, immediately after the dove descends."}]}, "Matthew 4:12–17": {"title": "Light in Galilee", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records a change of address and treats it as a fulfilled prophecy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The move"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He left **Nazareth** and went to live in **Capernaum**, a town beside the sea in the territory of **Zebulun and Naphtali**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew names both tribes, which is not how anyone in the first century gave directions. The tribal boundaries had not mattered for centuries."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names them because Isaiah did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah had said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Land of Zebulun and land of Naphtali, along the road to the sea, beyond the Jordan, **Galilee of the nations** — the people sitting in darkness have seen a great light.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 9, seven hundred years earlier. The chapter that goes on to say *for unto us a Child is born… and His name shall be called Wonderful, Counsellor, the Mighty God, the Everlasting Father, the Prince of Peace.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why those two tribes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were the first to go. When Assyria came, 2 Kings records that Tiglath-Pileser took *Gilead and Galilee, all the land of Naphtali*, and carried them away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first territory to fall into the dark is named as the first to see the light."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Galilee of the nations\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *Galilee of the Gentiles*. It had been mixed country for centuries, and the Judeans looked down on it — *can anything good come out of Nazareth?* and *search and look, for no prophet has arisen out of Galilee.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is where He moved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Zebulun and Naphtali"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their blessings in Genesis 49 are one line each:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Zebulun** — *he will be a harbour for ships.* His name means dwelling, and this is where He dwelt.", "**Naphtali** — *a doe set free.* This is where the woman bound for eighteen years was loosed."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the notes on Genesis 49:13 and Genesis 49:21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Sitting in darkness\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not walking. Sitting — settled in it, no longer trying to get out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *the land where death casts its shadow*. Isaiah's word is the same one in Psalm 23: *the valley of the shadow of death.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He began to say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Change your minds and turn to God**, because the kingdom of heaven has come near.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **metanoeo** — to change the mind, to think differently afterwards. And the reason given is not a threat. It is proximity: because the kingdom has come near."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is on the other side of Isaiah's sentence.** *On those living in the land where death casts its shadow, **light has dawned**.* The verb is a sunrise. Nobody in the dark did anything to produce it; the morning simply came up over them."}]}, "Matthew 4:18–22": {"title": "They left the nets", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four men, two boats, and one sentence each."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Walking beside the **Sea of Galilee**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just moved: *leaving Nazareth, He came and dwelt in Capernaum, which is by the sea*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew attaches a prophecy to the move — *Galilee of the Gentiles: the people who sat in darkness have seen a great light*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Two brothers, **Simon who is called Peter and his brother Andrew**, throwing a net into the water.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **amphiblestron** — a circular casting net, thrown by hand from the shore or a small boat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For they were fishermen.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew states it as the reason for what they were doing, and it becomes the reason for what He says next."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The call"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Come, follow Me**,\" He said to them, \"**and I will make you fishers of people**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **deute opiso mou** — come after Me."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Halieis anthropon** — fishers of men."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not tell them to stop being what they are. He takes the trade and changes what it is aimed at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one thing He undertakes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will make you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **poieso** — I will make, I will do."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only promise in the sentence, and He is the one doing the work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **left their nets right then** and followed Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eutheos** — immediately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*James son of Zebedee and his brother John, **in a boat with their father Zebedee, mending their nets**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katartizontas** — repairing, putting in order. The same verb Paul uses of restoring somebody caught in a fault."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One pair is fishing; the other is doing maintenance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they left"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And at once they left **the boat and their father** and followed Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew names both. The equipment and the man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zebedee had hired servants, Mark notes — so it is a business rather than subsistence, and the two sons walking off leave him short-handed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John's Gospel adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had met Him before. Andrew was one of the Baptist's disciples and had already brought his brother: *we have found the Messiah*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke adds the night of fishing and the sinking boats, and *depart from me, for I am a sinful man*. See the note on Luke 5:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew tells it flat, without the back-story, because he is writing about the calling rather than the men."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What fishermen were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the bottom of the social order. A working trade with boats, staff, partners and tax obligations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not scholars. Acts records the council's assessment: *when they saw the boldness of Peter and John, and perceived that they were **uneducated and untrained men**, they marvelled. And they realised that they had been with Jesus.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the nets turn up again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Fishermen will stand along the shore… there will be **places for spreading nets**.* See the note on Ezekiel 47:7–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel's vision has men drying nets on the shore of a lake that had never held a fish."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they end up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And after that, He was seen by James, then by all the apostles.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter preaching to three thousand at Pentecost. James executed by Herod, the first apostle to die. John writing a Gospel, three letters and Revelation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Andrew, who is mostly recorded bringing people to Him — his brother, the boy with the loaves, and the Greeks who asked to see Jesus."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one promise in the sentence.** *I will make you.* He does not ask them to become anything; He takes what they already do and says He will do the making."}]}, "Matthew 4:23–25": {"title": "Crowds from everywhere", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A summary verse, and Matthew uses it twice to bracket the whole first section of his Gospel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus went all through Galilee, **teaching** in their synagogues, **announcing the good news** of the kingdom, and **healing** every kind of disease and weakness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek: **didaskon**, **kerusson**, **therapeuon**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three participles, in one sentence, describing one ministry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Teaching, preaching, healing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew states it this way here, and again in chapter 9: *then Jesus went about all the cities and villages, teaching in their synagogues, preaching the gospel of the kingdom, and healing every sickness and every disease among the people*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two identical summaries, four chapters apart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they enclose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between them: the Sermon on the Mount, and then nine miracles in chapters 8 and 9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The words first, then the works. Matthew arranges his material so the sermon and the miracles sit inside the same frame."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kerusso** — to herald, to announce as a public crier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Euangelion tes basileias** — the good news of the kingdom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scope of the healing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every kind of **disease and weakness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **noson kai malakian** — sickness and infirmity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Malakia** — softness, feebleness. The chronic, wearing conditions rather than acute illness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reach"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Word about Him spread **across all Syria**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Roman province north of Galilee. Gentile territory, well outside the Jewish heartland."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four categories"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Those held down by **every sort of illness and pain**, those **tormented by demons**, those who **fell in seizures**, those who **could not move their limbs**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **selehniazomenous** — moonstruck, the ancient word for epilepsy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Paralutikous** — paralysed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew lists the conditions nobody could do anything about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **He healed them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **etherapeusen autous**. No qualification."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From **Galilee**, from **the Ten Cities**, from **Jerusalem and Judea**, and from **the far side of the Jordan**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dekapolis** — the Decapolis, a league of Greek cities east of the Jordan. Gentile."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four regions, and two of them are outside Jewish territory."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From that time Jesus began to preach and to say, \"**Repent, for the kingdom of heaven is at hand**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the calling of four fishermen: *follow Me, and I will make you fishers of men*. See the note on Matthew 4:18–22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ministry is weeks old."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **seeing the multitudes**, He went up on a mountain, and when He was seated His disciples came to Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He opened His mouth and taught them, saying: \"**Blessed are the poor in spirit**.\"* See the note on Matthew 5:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowds gather, and He goes up a hill and starts teaching."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The healing draws the crowd; the crowd gets the sermon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's ordering makes the miracles the reason anybody was there to hear the Sermon on the Mount."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke's version says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They had come **to listen to Him and to be healed** of their diseases… because **power was flowing out from Him**.* See the note on Luke 6:17–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two purposes, in the same order."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The frame.** Teaching, preaching, healing — stated here and again in chapter 9, with the Sermon on the Mount and nine miracles between them."}]}, "Matthew 4:5–7": {"title": "The temptation that quotes Scripture", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second attempt, and this time the tempter comes with a Bible verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he quotes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will give His angels orders concerning You, and they will lift You up in their hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is Psalm 91, and it is quoted accurately as far as it goes. The promise is real; the psalm says it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is left out is the line in the middle — *to guard you in all your ways.* The keeping is promised along the road God has for a person, not on a jump the person invents."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The highest point of the temple*, over the Kidron valley — a drop of well over a hundred feet, in full view of the courts below."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The suggestion is a spectacular, public, self-authenticating demonstration. It would have settled the question of who He was in an afternoon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is also written: do not put the Lord your God to the test.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus does not dispute the psalm. He puts another Scripture beside it, from Deuteronomy 6 — the passage about Massah, where Israel demanded water and asked whether the LORD was among them or not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A promise is not a lever. Testing God is what you do when you are not sure of Him and want proof; and demanding proof is the opposite of the trust the promise was given to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What this settles about quoting Scripture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A verse can be quoted correctly and used wrongly. The correction is not less Bible but more of it — Jesus answers all three temptations from Deuteronomy, and here He answers a quotation with a quotation."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Read alongside Psalms 91:5–8.** The psalm's promises are real and this app takes them as such. What this scene rules out is treating them as a mechanism to be operated, which is exactly what was being suggested."}]}, "Matthew 4:8–11": {"title": "All of this I will give You", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The third temptation, and it is the only one that does not begin *if You are the Son of God*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Once more the devil took Him, this time to **a very high mountain**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first two were stones into bread and the pinnacle of the temple. See the notes on Matthew 4:1–4 and Matthew 4:5–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The view"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And showed Him **every kingdom in the world and all their splendor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No mountain has that view. Luke adds *in a moment of time*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**All of this I will give You**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke has the fuller version: *all this authority I will give You, and their glory; **for this has been delivered to me, and I give it to whomever I wish***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The claim is not disputed. Jesus calls him *the ruler of this world* three times in John, and John's letter says *the whole world lies under the sway of the wicked one*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being offered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The kingdoms. Which He is going to get anyway."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ask of Me, and I will give You the nations for Your inheritance.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The kingdoms of this world have become the kingdoms of our Lord and of His Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offer is not of something false. It is the real inheritance, on different terms and without a cross."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The price"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If You will fall down and worship me**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One act."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go away, Satan**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hupage** — be gone, get out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first two temptations got a quotation. This one gets a dismissal first."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the same word to Peter, later, when Peter tells Him He must not go to Jerusalem: *get behind Me, Satan! You are an offence to Me, for you are not mindful of the things of God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same offer, from a friend: a Messiah without a cross."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For it is written: '**Worship the Lord your God, and serve Him only**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 6:13. See the note on Deuteronomy 6:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "All three answers come from Deuteronomy, and all three from the same few chapters — the ones about the wilderness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is forty days into a wilderness, quoting the manual of a nation that spent forty years in one and failed the same tests."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They wanted bread; He refuses to make it. They tested God at Massah; He refuses to jump. They made a calf; He refuses the kingdoms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **the devil left Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke adds the chilling clause: *he departed from Him **until an opportune time**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What arrives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **angels came and cared for Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **diekonoun** — they were serving, ministering. An imperfect: they kept at it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had refused to command stones into bread, and he is fed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word is used of the women who followed and *provided for Him from their substance*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the mountain turns up again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus came and spoke to them, saying, \"**All authority has been given to Me in heaven and on earth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On a mountain in Galilee, after the resurrection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "All authority, given — the thing that had been offered on a mountain three years earlier, and refused."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was on offer.** Not a lie. The kingdoms were genuinely his to hand over, and they were genuinely the inheritance — the only variable was the price."}]}, "Matthew 5:13–16": {"title": "\"You are\" — before anyone has done anything", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two images, and the grammar under both of them is the thing to notice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Not \"become\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You **are** the salt of the earth. You **are** the light of the world.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present indicative, stated as fact. Jesus is not setting a target. He is telling a crowd — who at this point in Matthew have done nothing but climb a hill and sit down — what they already are."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything He says afterwards follows from the statement rather than earning it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What can actually go wrong"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two images fail in different ways, and neither failure is about quantity."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**salt** can go flat — lose the thing that made it useful. What is left is not weaker salt; it is something that gets walked on", "**light** can be hidden — nobody in the illustration puts out the lamp. They put a bucket over it"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the warnings are about losing distinctiveness and about concealment. Not about shining harder."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the attention goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that when they see the good you do, they will give the glory to your Father in heaven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The visibility is deliberate — a town on a hill, a lamp on a stand, light filling a house. But the sentence ends somewhere other than where it started: what people see is the good; who they credit is the Father."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**A page later.** In chapter 6 Jesus warns against doing good *to be seen by people*. It looks like a contradiction of this and is not — the difference is in the last clause of each. Let them see the good so they glorify God; do not perform it so they admire you. Same visibility, opposite destination."}]}, "Matthew 5:17–20": {"title": "Not torn down, but filled full", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Early in the Sermon on the Mount, He answers a charge nobody has made yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The denial"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not imagine that I have come to tear up the Law or the Prophets**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katalusai** — to demolish, to pull down. It is used of a building being taken apart, and of the temple: *destroy this temple*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He raises the accusation before anyone brings it, which means He knew what the sermon would sound like."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have come to **fill it full**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **plerosai** — to fill, to complete, to bring to its intended fullness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to keep it, in the first instance. To fill it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The guarantee"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Until heaven and earth pass away, **not the smallest letter, not the least stroke of a pen, will drop out of the Law**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **iota** — the smallest Hebrew letter, **yod** — and **keraia**, a horn: the tiny serif that distinguishes one Hebrew letter from another."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He picks the two smallest marks on a page."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The term"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Until **every last bit of it has been accomplished**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **genetai** — until all comes to pass."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two *untils* in one sentence: until heaven and earth pass, and until it is all accomplished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's version of the same saying sits directly beside *the law and the prophets ran up to the time of John*. See the note on Luke 16:14–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves are His, in the same breath, in both Gospels."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ranking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whoever **sets aside one of the smallest of these commands**, and teaches others to do the same, will be **called least in the kingdom of heaven**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Least in the kingdom, not outside it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The standard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Unless **your righteousness goes further and deeper than the righteousness of the teachers of the law and the Pharisees**, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven at all.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **perisseuse** — exceed, overflow, go beyond."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that meant to the hearers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Pharisees were the most scrupulous people in the country. They tithed garden herbs and counted paces on the sabbath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Telling a crowd on a hillside that they needed to exceed that is not a call to try harder. Nobody in the audience could out-Pharisee a Pharisee."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sermon does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six paragraphs, each beginning *you have heard that it was said… but I say to you*:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not murder, but anger. Not adultery, but the look. Not divorce by certificate. Not oaths, but plain speech. Not an eye for an eye, but the other cheek. Not love your neighbour and hate your enemy, but love your enemies."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one goes inward, past what a court can reach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the filling full."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Therefore **you shall be perfect, just as your Father in heaven is perfect**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **teleioi** — complete, brought to the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's summary of the same thing: *Christ is the end of the law for righteousness to everyone who believes.* See the note on Romans 10:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Telos** — the end, and also the goal."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two words to hold together.** Not one letter drops out, and not one of them is what He came to keep — He came to fill. And the standard He sets in the same paragraph is higher than the most careful men alive."}]}, "Matthew 5:21–26": {"title": "\"But I say to you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He takes a commandment everyone in the crowd had kept, and moves it somewhere nobody can keep it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The formula"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You have heard that it was said**… **But I say to you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses it six times in this chapter. Nobody had ever spoken that way about the law — the scribes taught by citing older rabbis, and He cites nobody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The commandment was *do not murder*. Almost every man on that hillside had a clean record on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But I say to you, anyone who **nurses anger** against his brother… anyone who says 'You are worthless'… anyone who says 'You fool'.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offence moves from the hand to the temper, and from the temper to a word said under the breath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which direction the standard is going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Sermon on the Mount does not lower the bar to something manageable. It raises it past the reach of anybody in the audience — and He is only getting started; adultery goes the same way four verses later. See the note on Matthew 5:27–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the passage is for. The law was never a ladder; it is a mirror. Paul says so: *by the law is the knowledge of sin*, and *the law was our tutor to bring us to Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who thinks he has kept it has not read it closely enough. Jesus reads it closely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gift at the altar"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If you are bringing your gift to the altar, and right there you remember that **your brother has something against you**, leave your gift where it is.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note the direction. Not that you have something against him — that he has something against you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You are the one who is owed nothing, and you are the one told to leave."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Leave your gift where it is, in front of the altar. Go.* The offering waits on the stone while a man walks out of the temple to find somebody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Settle quickly"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Settle it with him quickly, **while the two of you are still on the road together**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is two men walking to court. There is a stretch of road before the judge, and He says use it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this leaves the hearer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last line is *you will not get out of there until you have paid the very last coin.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no relief anywhere in the passage. That is deliberate — the Sermon closes with a house falling flat, and the whole of it drives to one place: a man who cannot produce this righteousness, and needs one given."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He said just before starting.** *Unless your righteousness exceeds the righteousness of the scribes and Pharisees, you will by no means enter the kingdom of heaven.* The most meticulous men alive, and He says you need better than theirs. Then six paragraphs showing what better looks like."}]}, "Matthew 5:27–30": {"title": "\"Already, in his heart\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second of the six, and it does the same thing to adultery that the first did to murder."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The move"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You have heard… 'Do not commit adultery.' **But I say to you**, anyone who looks at a woman with wanting in his eyes **has already committed adultery with her in his heart**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seventh commandment concerned an act. He relocates it to a glance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Already.** The Greek is emphatic — it is done, past tense, before anything happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is left standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the question the passage is designed to raise, and the answer is nobody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first paragraph took murder down to a temper. This one takes adultery down to a look. Between them they clear the hillside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anyone who leaves the Sermon on the Mount feeling he has done reasonably well has not been listening. That is the whole point of it: *by the law is the knowledge of sin*, and the law was *our tutor to bring us to Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not making the law harder to keep. He is showing what it always said, and what it always said cannot be kept."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The eye and the hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If your right eye is what trips you up, **tear it out**… if your right hand is what trips you up, **cut it off**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verbs are violent and specific."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the arithmetic is stated: *better to lose one part of you than to have your whole body thrown into hell.* On His own terms that is simply correct — if a hand could be traded for a life, the trade would be worth making."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But a blind man can still want, and a one-handed man can still hate. The surgery does not reach the organ that is actually the problem, and the passage has already named it: *in his heart.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has to be dealt with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing removable is the trouble. That is why the sentence about the heart comes first and the amputations after."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel had the only remedy that reaches it: *I will give you a new heart, and put a new spirit within you; I will take the heart of stone out of your flesh and give you a heart of flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An operation nobody can perform on himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Sermon ends with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two houses, and one of them *fell — and great was its fall.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He finishes His most demanding sermon with a collapse, and the crowds are *astonished at His teaching, for He taught them as one having authority.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where this leaves a reader.** Exactly where it left the hearers: with a standard that is God's own and no way to reach it, and a Man on the hillside who will go on to keep it entirely and then hand what He earned to people who did not. That is the sequence the Sermon is written into."}]}, "Matthew 5:38–42": {"title": "The other cheek", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The fifth of the six *you have heard it said* sayings, and every example in it is a small, specific humiliation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You have heard that it was said, '**An eye for an eye, and a tooth for a tooth**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **lex talionis**, and it appears three times in the Torah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was not a licence for revenge. It was a cap on it — a limit stopping a feud from escalating, and it was administered by courts rather than by injured parties."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Being quoted, by His day, to justify personal retaliation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But I say to you, **do not strike back at the one who wrongs you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me antistenai to ponero** — do not resist, or do not set yourself against, the evil one, or evil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Anthistemi** is a legal and military word. It is used of standing against in court, and of withstanding in battle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses it of himself: *I withstood Peter to his face*. And James: *resist the devil, and he will flee*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is capable of a narrower sense here, and the four examples that follow are what define it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If someone slaps you on **the right cheek**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek specifies which one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A right-handed man striking your right cheek is using the back of his hand. In that culture a backhanded blow was not a fight — it was how a superior corrected an inferior."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Offering the other cheek forces the striker to use an open hand, which is how equals hit each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If someone wants to **drag you into court and take your shirt**, let him have your coat too.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **chiton** is the inner garment; the **himation** is the outer cloak."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law protected the cloak specifically: *if you ever take your neighbour's garment as a pledge, you shall return it to him before the sun goes down. For that is his only covering.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is somebody suing a poor man for the last thing he owns."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If someone **forces you to carry his load a mile**, go with him two.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **angareusei** — a Persian loan-word for official requisition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Roman soldier could legally compel a provincial to carry his pack for one mile. It was a standing grievance and a daily humiliation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction concerns the second mile, which no law required and no soldier could ask for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fourth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Give to the one who asks** you, and when someone wants to borrow from you, **do not turn your back on him**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the four have in common"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one is a small, specific, legal indignity: a backhand, a lawsuit, a requisition, a request for money."}, {"k": "p", "t": "None is a violent crime, and none is about a third party in danger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage is about personal insult and personal loss, in the ordinary transactions of an occupied country."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does with it Himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when He had said this, one of the officers who stood by **struck Jesus with the palm of his hand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And His answer: *if I have spoken evil, bear witness of the evil; but if well, why do you strike Me?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not hit back, and He does not stay silent either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sixth and last: *love your enemies, bless those who curse you… that you may be sons of your Father in heaven; for He makes His sun rise on the evil and on the good*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the standard: *therefore you shall be perfect, just as your Father in heaven is perfect*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Paul's version"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not pay anyone back evil for evil… **if it can be done, and as far as it rests with you, be at peace with everyone**.* See the note on Romans 12:17–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul keeps the instruction and adds two qualifications the sermon does not."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the four examples are.** A backhand, a lawsuit over a shirt, a soldier's pack, and a loan. Small humiliations in an occupied country — and the second mile is the one nobody could demand."}]}, "Matthew 6:19–21": {"title": "\"Where your treasure is\" — the direction of the sentence", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*Wherever your treasure is, that is where your heart will be living.* Most people quote it the other way round — put your heart somewhere and your money follows. Jesus says the reverse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order He gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Treasure first, heart after. The heart is described as taking up residence wherever the valuables already are."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That makes it a diagnosis and a lever at once. A diagnosis, because a person can find out what they actually treasure by looking at where their resources have gone. A lever, because moving the treasure is presented as the thing that moves the affection."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three thieves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moths eat it and rust eats it and thieves break in and carry it off.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three ways to lose it, covering the three forms wealth took: cloth, metal, and anything storable. The Greek behind *rust* is broader than iron corrosion — it is eating away, decay of any kind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus is not moralising about wealth here. He is making a practical argument about where things keep."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse after it.** He goes straight on to the eye being the lamp of the body, and then to serving two masters. The passage is a run: where your money is, what your eye is fixed on, who you actually work for. All three ask the same question from a different angle, and Luke's version of the third one is in the note on Luke 16:9–13."}]}, "Matthew 6:24": {"title": "You cannot serve two", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and the impossibility is stated before any reason is given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No one can serve two masters**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **oudeis dunatai** — no one is able."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *should not*. Cannot. It is a statement about what is possible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for serve"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Douleuein** — to be a slave to, from **doulos**, bondservant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not employment. A slave belonged to one household, was owned rather than hired, and had no hours of his own to sell elsewhere."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Employees can hold two jobs. Slaves cannot have two owners."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two outcomes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Either he will **hate the one and love the other**, or he will **hold on to the one and think nothing of the other**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for the second pair is **anthexetai** — will cling to, hold fast — and **kataphronesei**, will look down on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two ways of describing the same failure. Whichever way it goes, one of them loses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not offered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A third option in which both are served at a reduced level."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The application"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You cannot serve God and money both**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **mamona** — an Aramaic loanword, **mammon**, meaning wealth or property."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Its root may be the same as **amen** — that which is trusted, what you rely on. The word for money and the word for *so be it* may share a stem meaning *firm*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being said about money"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that it is evil. Jesus names it as a rival master, which is a claim about its capacity, not its morality."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Money is the only thing in the Gospels He puts opposite God in this construction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the verse sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately before: *do not lay up for yourselves treasures on earth, where moth and rust destroy… **for where your treasure is, there your heart will be also***. See the note on Matthew 6:19–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the lamp of the body, and the single eye."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately after: *therefore I say to you, **do not worry about your life**, what you will eat or what you will drink.* See the note on Matthew 6:25–34."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Treasure, then eye, then master, then worry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage moves from what you store, to what you look at, to what owns you, to what you are afraid of. Jesus treats them as one subject."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The therefore"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 25 begins with **dia touto** — for this reason."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction not to worry is grounded in the verse about the two masters. Anxiety about provision is treated as a symptom of divided service, not as a separate problem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the love of money is a root of all kinds of evil**, for which some have strayed from the faith in their greediness.* See the note on 1 Timothy 6:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The love of it, and *a* root of *all kinds* — Paul is more precise than the proverb usually quoted from him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is said to the rich"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Command those who are rich in this present age **not to be haughty, nor to trust in uncertain riches** but in the living God, who gives us richly all things to enjoy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to divest. Not to trust."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the parable adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You cannot serve God and mammon.\" **Now the Pharisees, who were lovers of money, also heard all these things, and they derided Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke records the reaction, and then Jesus tells the story of the rich man and Lazarus."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb.** **Douleuein** — to be owned. Employees can hold two jobs; slaves cannot have two owners, and that is the whole argument."}]}, "Matthew 6:25–34": {"title": "Look at the birds", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three times He says do not worry, and in between He points at two things anybody in that field could see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The birds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **do not plant, they do not harvest, they do not store anything away in barns**, and your heavenly Father feeds them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs, all denied. And they are the three stages of agriculture — the whole economy of that hillside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Birds are not idle; a bird works hard all day. What it does not do is the thing the crowd was doing: plan the year, secure the surplus, guarantee the future."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Are you not worth far more than they are?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The flowers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look how the wildflowers grow out in the open field. **They do not work at it, they do not spin a thread**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs again, and they are women's work — the crowd is being addressed in halves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not even Solomon in all his splendour was dressed like one of these.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The richest man Israel produced, out-dressed by a weed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If God clothes **the grass of the field** like that — grass that is here today and **tossed into the oven tomorrow** — how much more will He clothe you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dried grass was fuel. He is comparing a person to kindling, and arguing from the lesser to the greater."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"You of little faith\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is one word, **oligopistos**, and Matthew uses it five times, always to disciples. It is not a rebuke about doctrine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He attaches it to worry, which tells you what He thinks worry is a shortage of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What worry cannot do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which of you, **by worrying, can add a single hour** to your life?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not argue that worry is wrong. He argues that it is ineffective, which is harder to dispute."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who chases"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People who **do not know God** chase after all of that, and **your heavenly Father knows perfectly well that you need it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses, and the second is the answer to the first. The need is not denied — it is granted, and the point is that it is already known about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seek first the kingdom of God and **His righteousness**, and **all these other things will be given to you as well**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note whose righteousness is to be sought. Not one of your own — that is the whole argument of the Sermon, which has just spent a chapter showing nobody has one. See the note on Matthew 5:27–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All these other things* — the food, the drink, the clothes. Not withheld and not despised; put second, and added."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *will be given* is to add on top, to throw in besides. They arrive as an extra."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Today"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not worry about tomorrow. Tomorrow will worry about itself. **Today has enough trouble of its own**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say tomorrow has no trouble in it. He says today's ration is sufficient and there is no provision for carrying a second day's."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse before all this.** *No one can serve two masters… you cannot serve God and mammon.* Then *therefore I say to you, do not worry.* The passage about birds is an answer to a question about who owns you."}]}, "Matthew 6:5–8": {"title": "Go in and shut the door", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two ways of praying are ruled out, and both of them are about the audience."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When you pray, **do not be like the play-actors**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hupokritai** — the word for stage performers, actors who spoke through a mask."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word English took as *hypocrite*, but its first sense is the theatre."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They love to **stand and pray in the synagogues and on the street corners where everyone can watch them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the content is criticised. The prayers may be good ones."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The location is the problem: places chosen because they are visible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they get"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I tell you the truth, **that is the whole of their reward**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apechousin** — they have in full. It is the word written on receipts in the papyri: *paid in full*, nothing further owing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They wanted to be seen. They were seen. The transaction is closed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The alternative"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But you — when you pray, **go into your room, shut the door behind you**, and pray to your Father **who is there in the secret place**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tameion** — the storeroom, the inner chamber. A house's windowless room, where the valuables were kept."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Shut the door* — **kleisas ten thuran**, a decisive act."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **your Father, who sees in secret, will reward you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same clause governs the giving in verse 4 and the fasting in verse 18. Three times in one chapter: **in secret**, and *your Father who sees in secret*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second thing ruled out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not **pile up words** the way people do who do not know Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **battalogesete** — a word that may be onomatopoeic. Stammering, babbling, repeating."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They imagine **they will be heard because they said so much**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Polulogia** — much-speaking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the two have in common"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first prays to be seen. The second prays to be heard through volume."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are working on an audience — one on people, one on God — and both assume something has to be gotten past."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your Father **knows exactly what you need long before you ask Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which sounds at first like an argument against praying at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the opposite. It removes the need to inform, persuade, or wear down. What is left is a child talking to a Father who already knows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He gives them instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In this manner, therefore, pray: **Our Father in heaven**…* See the note on Matthew 6:9–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The model prayer is fifty-two words in Greek, and it comes immediately after the warning about saying too much."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word He starts it with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Father*. And the first person plural — *our*, *us*, *we* — throughout, prayed by a man alone in a storeroom with the door shut."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah says about the speed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It shall come to pass that **before they call, I will answer**; and while they are still speaking, I will hear.* See the note on Isaiah 65:24–25."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the same claim as *your Father knows before you ask*, made seven hundred years earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore do not worry, saying, \"What shall we eat?\"… **for your heavenly Father knows that you need all these things**.* See the note on Matthew 6:25–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb, **oiden** — knows — used twice in one chapter, once about prayer and once about food."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The receipt.** **Apechousin** — paid in full, nothing further owing. They wanted to be seen, and they were."}]}, "Matthew 6:9–13": {"title": "The prayer, line by line", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "It is the most said prayer in the world, which is exactly why it is worth slowing down over. Jesus gives it as a pattern — *pray like this* — immediately after telling people not to heap up words as though volume helped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Our Father in heaven"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in tension, and both matter. **Father** — relationship, nearness, a family word. **In heaven** — and He is not one of us."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Notice it is *our*, not *my*. The whole prayer is plural: our bread, our debts, lead us, deliver us. Jesus taught it to be prayed by someone who has other people in mind, even when they are alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "May Your name be held holy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a request that God become holy. The verb asks that His name be *treated* as what it is — set apart, not used as small change."}, {"k": "h", "t": "May Your kingdom come"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing asked for is not a personal need. And the line that follows tells you what a kingdom coming looks like: *may what You want be done, here on the earth just as it is done in heaven.* Two lines, one request. God's rule arriving is God's will being done."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Give us today the bread we need for today"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek adjective here — **epiousios** — is famous for being almost unknown. It appears in no other surviving Greek text of the period apart from this prayer. Every translation is a considered guess: bread for the coming day, bread that is needed, daily bread."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever it means precisely, the request is small and immediate. Today's bread, today. Nobody is asked to pray for a storehouse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Forgive us our debts, as we ourselves have forgiven"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Debts, not sins — Matthew uses the commercial word, something owed. And the second half has already happened: *as we ourselves have forgiven*, past tense. It is not a bargain being struck. It is a person praying who has already let somebody off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the only line Jesus goes back and comments on after the prayer ends, so He evidently expected it to be the sticking point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Do not lead us into the time of testing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word covers both testing and temptation. Given James says plainly that God tempts nobody, the request is best read as it stands: *keep us out of the trial* — a prayer of ordinary self-knowledge, from people who would rather not find out how they would do."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On the last line.** *For the kingdom is Yours, and the power, and the glory* — it is absent from the earliest manuscripts of Matthew and was almost certainly added early, from church use. It is not a corruption; it is a congregation's Amen that ended up in the text, and it echoes David's prayer in 1 Chronicles. The app prints it because it is what most readers know."}]}, "Matthew 7:15–20": {"title": "Wool on the outside", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Near the end of the Sermon on the Mount, and it is a test for the audience rather than an instruction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Watch out for false prophets**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **prosechete** — hold your mind toward, be on guard."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Prophets, not opponents. The people being described are inside, speaking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disguise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They come to you **wearing sheep's wool, but underneath they are ravenous wolves**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **en endumasin probaton** — in sheep's clothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Harpages** — ravenous, rapacious. From the verb for snatching and plundering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The disguise is the point. A wolf that looked like a wolf would need no warning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The test"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will know them by their fruit**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epignosesthe** — you will recognise, know fully."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not by their doctrine, in the first instance. Not by their following, and not by their results."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rhetorical question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do people gather **grapes off thornbushes, or figs off thistles**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are absurd, deliberately. Nobody in the audience has ever tried."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke has the same saying with the fruits swapped — figs off thornbushes, grapes off a briar patch. See the note on Luke 6:43–45."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Every **good tree bears good fruit**, and a **rotten tree bears bad fruit**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sapron** — rotten, decayed, worthless."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"A good tree **cannot** bear bad fruit, and a rotten tree **cannot** bear good fruit.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated as an impossibility in both directions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the fruit is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the miracles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The very next paragraph settles that: *many will say to Me in that day, \"Lord, Lord, **have we not prophesied in Your name, cast out demons in Your name, and done many wonders in Your name?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And then I will declare to them, \"**I never knew you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Prophecy, exorcism and wonders are listed as things that can be present without the tree being good."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the fruit is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sermon has already spent six chapters on it: the meek, the merciful, the peacemakers, turning the other cheek, loving enemies, giving without a trumpet, praying without display, forgiving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Paul's list, which is singular: *the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, longsuffering, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, self-control*. See the note on Galatians 5:22–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disposal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Every tree that does not bear good fruit is **cut down and thrown into the fire**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same sentence John the Baptist used, word for word, at the Jordan."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repetition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So then, you will know them by their fruit**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice, at the start and the finish, and the sentence between them is the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the saying is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Diagnosis, not improvement."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the passage tells a thornbush to try harder. Thornbushes are not addressed; the hearers are told how to identify one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sermon closes with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two builders, two houses, one storm, and the difference is not the weather."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Everyone who hears these sayings of Mine **and does them**, I will liken him to a wise man who built his house on the rock.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the crowd's reaction: *they were astonished at His teaching, for He taught them as one having authority, and not as the scribes*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the test is not.** Not power. Two paragraphs later, prophecy, exorcism and wonders are named as things done by people He says He never knew."}]}, "Matthew 7:7–11": {"title": "\"How much more\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three commands, three promises, and then an argument from the worst father in the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Ask**, and it will be given to you. **Seek**, and you will find. **Knock**, and the door will be opened to you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verbs are present imperatives — continuous. Keep asking, keep seeking, keep knocking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each is more active than the last: asking is words, seeking is movement, knocking is arriving at a specific door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repetition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Everyone** who asks receives, and the one who seeks finds, and to the one who knocks the door is opened.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says all three again, as statements of fact rather than instructions."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Everyone** — the Greek is **pas**. No category is excluded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Which of you, if your son asked you for **a loaf of bread**, would hand him **a stone**? Or if he asked for **a fish**, would hand him **a snake**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both pairs are things that look alike. A flat stone from the Galilee shore looks like a loaf; an eel-like fish and a water snake are hard to tell apart in a net."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The absurdity is not a father giving something useless. It is a father giving something that looks right and is not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The premise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **if you, who have plenty of wrong in you**, know how to give good gifts to your children.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *if you, being evil* — **poneroi**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it to the crowd on the hillside without softening it, and then uses their fatherhood as the good half of the comparison."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How much more** will your Father in heaven **give good things to the ones who ask Him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **posso mallon** — the standard rabbinic form of argument from lesser to greater."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gap between a bad human father and a good one is small. The gap between any human father and this one is the size of the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke has instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"How much more will your heavenly Father **give the Holy Spirit** to those who ask Him!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew says *good things*; Luke names one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Near the end of the Sermon on the Mount, after *do not judge* and immediately before the golden rule: *therefore, whatever you want men to do to you, do also to them, for this is the Law and the Prophets.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **therefore** connects them. Because your Father gives like that, treat people like that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has already been said about asking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters earlier, in the same sermon: *your Father knows the things you have need of before you ask Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *do not worry, saying, \"What shall we eat?\"… **your heavenly Father knows that you need all these things**.* See the note on Matthew 6:25–34."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the passage does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about how much faith, how long, or how well. The conditions in the sentence are asking, seeking and knocking."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The argument's weakest link is deliberate.** He builds the case on a father He has just called evil, and says the conclusion holds *how much more* at the other end."}]}, "Matthew 8:1–4": {"title": "\"If You are willing\" — and the answer that settles it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The leper's question is the one everybody actually asks, and Jesus answers it in two words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the man was sure of, and what he was not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lord, if You are willing, You can make me clean.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has no doubt about the power. **Can** is not in question — he says it flatly. What he does not know is whether Jesus *wants* to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is where most people stand. Not wondering whether God is able. Wondering whether He is willing, in their case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus reached out His hand and touched him. \"I am willing,\" He said. \"Be clean.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes the man's own word and hands it straight back. The uncertainty is not left hanging for a moment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And He touches him first. Under the law a leper was untouchable, and anyone touching one became unclean until evening. Jesus reaches out before He speaks. The cleanness travels the other way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the touch says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the first healing Matthew records after the Sermon on the Mount, and he places it deliberately. Jesus has just finished teaching with authority; now He puts a hand on the one person in Israel nobody would touch."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Instantly the leprosy left him.* No process, no waiting."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why He sends him to the priest.** Leviticus 14 sets out a whole ceremony for cleansed lepers that had, so far as anyone knew, never been performed — two birds, cedar, scarlet, hyssop. Jesus tells the man to go and present himself *so they can see it for themselves*. The priests were about to have to open a chapter of the law that had lain unused."}]}, "Matthew 8:10–13": {"title": "\"As you have believed\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The one time in Matthew that Jesus is recorded as astonished, and it is by a Roman."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He marvelled"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Jesus heard that, **He marvelled**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is to wonder, to be amazed. Mark records Him marvelling at unbelief in Nazareth; here it goes the other way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both times it is caused by people's faith — the absence of it, and this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the officer had said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before: *only speak a word, and my servant will be healed. For I also am a man under authority… I say to this one, \"Go,\" and he goes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is a career soldier and he has worked out the whole thing from military structure. Orders travel. The officer does not have to be present for the order to take effect, only to have the authority behind it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not ask Jesus to come. He asked Him to speak."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have **not found faith like this anywhere in Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said out loud, to the crowd following Him, about a Gentile officer of the occupying army."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Many will come **from the east and from the west** and sit down at the table with Abraham and Isaac and Jacob… while **those who thought the kingdom belonged to them by birth** will be put outside.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes one Roman's faith and extends it to a table filled from every direction, and warns the people who assumed the seats were theirs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word to the officer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go home. Let it be done for you just as you have believed.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Matthew's next clause: *the servant was healed at that very hour.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At a distance. Jesus never went to the house and never saw the servant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as you have believed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say *because you deserved it*, or *because your servant was worth it*, or *because you are a good master* — though the passage shows he was."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thing named as the measure is what the man believed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew records the same formula twice more in his Gospel: to the two blind men, *according to your faith let it be to you*, and to the Canaanite woman, *let it be done for you just as you desire.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times, three different people, and every one of them an outsider of some kind."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who the officer thought he was.** *Lord, I am not worthy that You should come under my roof.* A commander of a hundred men, in a country he was occupying, saying that to a village carpenter. Jesus never contradicts the self-assessment — He simply speaks the word."}]}, "Matthew 8:14–17": {"title": "Matthew tells you which prophecy the healings were", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An evening of healing, and then Matthew stops the narrative to tell you what he thinks was happening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This was to fill out what the prophet Isaiah had said: \"He took up our weaknesses and carried our diseases away.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The line is from Isaiah 53 — the Servant chapter, the one about being pierced for our transgressions and crushed for our iniquities."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew, writing under inspiration and knowing exactly which chapter he is reaching into, applies it to **an evening of physical healings**. Not to the forgiveness of sins, which is what the chapter is usually preached as. To fevers and demons and bodies."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The Hebrew he is drawing on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 53:4 in Hebrew reads *surely he has borne our sicknesses and carried our pains*. The words are the ordinary ones for illness and physical suffering; they are used elsewhere in the Old Testament of disease."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So Matthew is not stretching the verse. He is quoting it at its plain sense, and attaching it to what he had just watched."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Healed everyone who was sick\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scope is worth reading slowly. Not many. Not those with sufficient faith. *Everyone.* Matthew says the same in several other places — great crowds, and all of them healed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in these scenes is turned away, and no one is told they did not qualify."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The fever \"let go of her.\"** Matthew's verb is the one used of releasing a prisoner or forgiving a debt. He touches her hand and the fever lets go — and the next thing she does is get up and serve. Restored, and immediately useful."}]}, "Matthew 8:23–27": {"title": "\"Where is your trust?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Matthew tells the storm in five verses and puts the emphasis in a different place from Mark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who got in first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **He stepped into the boat, and His disciples went with Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's order. He gets in; they follow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever happens on that lake happens to men who are where they are because they were following."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for the storm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Without warning **a violent squall** came down on the lake.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **seismos** — the word for an earthquake. Matthew uses it of the ground shaking at the crucifixion and at the tomb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls this weather an earthquake at sea."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"And Jesus was asleep\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words, set against the earthquake, in the same sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The boat is *buried* by the waves — the Greek is being covered over — and He is asleep."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark adds the detail about the cushion in the stern. Matthew leaves it out and lets the contrast stand bare. See the note on Mark 4:35–41."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Lord, save us! We are going down!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's version is a prayer. In Mark it is an accusation — *do You not care that we are going down?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew has them asking Him to do something. Mark has them asking whether He cares."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why are you so frightened? Where is your trust?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before the sea. Matthew is precise about the order: He addresses them, and then He addresses the storm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *you of little faith* — **oligopistos**, the word Matthew uses five times, always to disciples, and always about worry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the sea"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **stood up and spoke sharply** to the winds and to the sea, and everything went **completely still**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *completely still* is a great calm — the same adjective as the *great* tempest a few verses earlier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The size of the calm matches the size of the storm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they conclude"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What kind of man is this**, that even the winds and the sea do what He tells Him?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do not answer their own question. Matthew leaves it hanging."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anyone who knew the Psalms had the answer: *He calms the storm, so that its waves are still* — Psalm 107, about the LORD."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had already seen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is Matthew 8, and by this point in the chapter He has cleansed a leper, healed the centurion's servant at a distance, healed Peter's mother-in-law, and cast out spirits with a word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had watched all of it, and still asked what kind of man He was."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is on the other side of the lake.** Two men out of the tombs, so violent that nobody could pass that way. The storm was on the route to them. See the note on Luke 8:30–33."}]}, "Matthew 8:5–9": {"title": "The centurion's argument", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A Roman officer stops Jesus in the street and makes an argument about authority that Jesus calls the greatest faith in Israel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A centurion — roughly eighty to a hundred men under him, the backbone of the Roman army, and an occupier in a country that hated the occupation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the person he is asking about is *his servant*. A man with the power to replace a slave is out in the road pleading for one, describing the condition in detail: paralysed, in terrible pain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he refuses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lord, I am not worthy to have You come under my roof.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus has already said He will come. The centurion turns down the visit — partly because entering a Gentile home would make a Jewish teacher ceremonially unclean, and he knows it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks for less access than he is being offered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Only say the word… I am a man under orders myself, and I have soldiers under me. I say to one, \"Go,\" and he goes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here is the reasoning: I know how authority works. I do not need to be present for my orders to be carried out, because the authority is not in my presence. It is in my position."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has understood that Jesus operates the same way — and, crucially, that Jesus is *under* authority too, which is why the word carries."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never asks Jesus to do anything except speak."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have not found such great faith, not even in Israel.* And then the servant is healed at that moment, at a distance, without a word being spoken over him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he built.** Luke's account adds that the Jewish elders vouched for him: *he loves our nation and he built our synagogue.* An occupying officer paid for the local place of worship. That is the man who says he is not worthy to have Jesus in his house."}]}, "Matthew 9:1–8": {"title": "\"Take heart, son\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's version of the paralytic, and it is stripped down to the exchange."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Matthew leaves out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd at the door, the four carriers, the roof, the digging, the ropes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark gives all of it. See the note on Mark 2:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew keeps the faith and the words and cuts everything else — which is how he handles most miracles."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus got into the boat, crossed over, and came to **His own town**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Capernaum. Matthew calls it His own city; He had moved there from Nazareth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Jesus saw **their faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Plural, as in Mark. Whatever it took to get the man in front of Him, Jesus reads it as faith and says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take heart, son. Your sins are forgiven**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *take heart* is **tharsei** — be of good cheer, courage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Son** — **teknon**, child."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody had raised the subject of sins. The men came about a body."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Some of the teachers of the law said among themselves, \"**This man is insulting God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their reasoning is correct: sin is against God, so the pardon is God's to give."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He knew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus **knew what they were thinking** and said, \"**Why are you turning over such dark thoughts in your hearts?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing had been said aloud. Answering the interior objection is itself the answer to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Which is easier to say** — 'Your sins are forgiven,' or 'Get up and walk'?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Easier **to say**. Forgiveness is unverifiable; legs are checkable in seconds."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the checkable one to establish the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The broken sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **so that you will know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins**\" — and here **He turned to the paralyzed man** — \"**get up, pick up your mat, and go home**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence is addressed to the scribes and finishes as a command to somebody else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The healing happens inside a clause about authority."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the man **got up and went home**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew gives it in five words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the crowds saw it, they were **filled with wonder**, and they **praised God, who had given such authority to human beings**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *to men* — **tois anthropois**, plural."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew alone records that detail, and it is the strange one. The crowd does not conclude that a unique person is present. They conclude that this kind of authority has been given to human beings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew's Gospel ends with *all authority has been given to Me… **go therefore***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens in the next verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As Jesus passed on from there, He saw a man named **Matthew** sitting at the tax office, and He said to him, \"Follow Me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer puts his own call immediately after this scene, and then a dinner at his house with *many tax collectors and sinners* — and the Pharisees asking why."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer: *those who are well have no need of a physician, but those who are sick… **I did not come to call the righteous, but sinners, to repentance**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He addressed first.** Not the legs. Five men went to great trouble about a body, and the first sentence spoken over the man is *take heart* — and then something nobody had asked about."}]}, "Matthew 9:14–17": {"title": "New wine, new skins", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A question about fasting, answered with a wedding and then two pieces of household advice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **John's disciples** came to Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the Pharisees. The Baptist's own men, and their teacher is in prison at this point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Why is it that **we fast, and the Pharisees fast, but Your disciples do not?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law required one fast a year, on the Day of Atonement. The Pharisees fasted twice a week."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question is fair, and it is asked by people who are themselves fasting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"How can **the friends of the bridegroom** go around **grieving while the bridegroom is still with them**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the sons of the bridechamber* — the wedding party."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jewish law released the wedding guests from religious obligations for the seven days of the feast. Their duty during that week was to make the couple glad."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He answers a question about fasting with a rule about weddings."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who He says He is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bridegroom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John the Baptist had already used the word of Him — *he who has the bride is the bridegroom; but the friend of the bridegroom, who stands and hears him, rejoices greatly*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men asking are the friend's disciples."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prediction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The day will come when **the bridegroom is taken away from them**, and then they will fast.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **aparthe** — taken away, lifted off. A violent word for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first hint in Matthew of how this ends."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The patch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Nobody sews **a patch of unshrunk cloth onto an old coat**, because the patch **pulls away from the coat and the tear is worse than before**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "New cloth has not shrunk. Wash the garment and the patch contracts and takes the old fabric with it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The repair makes the hole bigger. That is the whole point of the image."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wineskins"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And nobody pours **new wine into old wineskins**. If they do, **the skins burst, the wine spills, and the skins are ruined**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "New wine ferments and gives off gas. A fresh skin has give in it; an old one has already been stretched to its limit and gone brittle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things are lost in the sentence, not one — the wine and the skin."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The alternative"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**New wine goes into new wineskins, and both are kept safe**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **suntlerountai** — preserved together. Both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not against the old skin. He is saying it cannot hold this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the images are answering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question was about a religious practice. The answer says the problem is structural."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What He has brought is not an improvement to an existing arrangement. It does not go into the old container at all, and forcing it destroys both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And no one, having drunk old wine, immediately desires new; for he says, \"**The old is better**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke's extra line is about taste, and it is realistic about how the change will be received."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He had just done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter is a run of them: a paralytic forgiven and healed, Matthew called out of a tax booth, and a dinner at Matthew's house with *many tax collectors and sinners*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Pharisees' complaint about that dinner is three verses before this: *why does your Teacher eat with tax collectors and sinners?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer: *I did not come to call the righteous, but sinners, to repentance.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the wedding image goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us be glad and rejoice and give Him glory, for **the marriage of the Lamb has come**, and His wife has made herself ready.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *blessed are those who are called to the marriage supper of the Lamb*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image He uses to explain why nobody is fasting is the one the last book of the Bible ends on."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the patch does.** Makes the tear worse. He answers a question about religious practice by describing two repairs that destroy the thing being repaired."}]}, "Matthew 9:18–22": {"title": "A dying girl, a desperate woman", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Matthew tells both stories in five verses. Mark takes twenty-three."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While He was still speaking.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is answering a question about fasting. The ruler comes in mid-sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The father"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One of **the leading men of the town** came and **knelt in front of Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mark and Luke name him: Jairus, a ruler of the synagogue."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A synagogue official kneeling in public to a travelling teacher is a considerable thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**My daughter has just died. But come and lay Your hand on her, and she will live**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew has her already dead. Mark and Luke have her dying, with the news of the death arriving during the delay."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew compresses — he does this throughout, and it is why his miracle accounts are half the length."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Come, lay Your hand on her.* One gesture, and a statement about what will follow."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The journey"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Jesus **stood up and went with him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No question asked and no condition set."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interruption inside the interruption"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **on the way**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second story happens inside the first. Both Matthew and Mark keep the structure, and it is the point: a man whose daughter is dead has to stand in the street while somebody else is dealt with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The woman"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A woman who had been **bleeding for twelve years**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The girl is twelve years old — Mark records it. For as long as one family has had a daughter, this woman has been bleeding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve years of ceremonial uncleanness under Leviticus 15: unable to touch or be touched, excluded from the temple."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she touched"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Came up **behind Him** and touched **the edge of His cloak**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kraspedon** — the tassel on the corner of the garment, the **tzitzit** commanded in Numbers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She reaches for the lowest, furthest part of him, from behind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she kept saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because she **kept saying to herself**, \"If I can only touch His clothes, **I will be well**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is imperfect — she was saying it, repeatedly, while pushing through."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**I will be well** is **sothesomai** — saved, made whole."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Jesus **turned and saw her**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had planned to take it and leave. He will not let her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take heart, daughter. Your faith has made you well**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Daughter** — the only time in the Gospels He calls anyone that."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses earlier He had called a paralysed man **son**. See the note on Matthew 9:1–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Take heart** — **tharsei**, the same word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He credits her faith, not the tassel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **from that moment the woman was well**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew says it happened when He spoke. Mark says she felt it when she touched. See the note on Mark 5:25–29."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He arrives at the house, tells the flute players and the wailing crowd that the girl is not dead but sleeping, and they laugh at Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But when the crowd was put outside, He went in and **took her by the hand, and the girl arose**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the delay cost.** A man with a dead daughter waited in a street while a woman who had planned to slip away unnoticed was found, spoken to, and given a name."}]}, "Matthew 9:27–31": {"title": "\"According to your faith\" — and what the question was", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two blind men follow Jesus indoors, and He asks them one question before doing anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do you believe that I am able to do this?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *do you have enough faith*, and not *how strong is your faith*. The object of the question is His ability, not their capacity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is worth holding on to when the next line arrives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"According to your faith\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let it be done for you according to your faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read alone, it sounds like a measurement — as much healing as you have faith for. Read as the answer to the question He just asked, it means something narrower: according to what you have just said you believe about Me."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their faith had exactly one item in it. They believed He could. That was enough to be going on with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they called Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Have mercy on us, Son of David.* It is the first time in Matthew anybody uses that title, and it is a messianic one — two blind men see who He is before most of the people watching."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew, who is building a case throughout his Gospel that Jesus is the promised son of David, gives the line to them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The instruction they ignore.** *See that nobody hears about this* — and they went out and spread it everywhere. Matthew records the disobedience plainly and without approval. It is the reaction of people who have just received their sight, and the Gospel does not tidy it up."}]}, "Matthew 9:35–38": {"title": "Sheep without a shepherd", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A summary of everything He had been doing, and then a sentence about what He felt looking at the crowd."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Teaching in their synagogues, **telling the good news of the kingdom**, and **healing every disease and every weakness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew gives the same three-part summary in chapter 4, before the Sermon on the Mount. This one closes the section, and the two summaries bracket everything in between."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scope"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All through **the towns and the villages**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every disease and every weakness* — the Greek is **pasan noson kai pasan malakian**, and Matthew repeats *every* twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What moved"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when He looked at the crowds, **something moved deep inside Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **esplanchnisthe** — from **splanchna**, the inward parts, the guts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the strongest word for compassion in the language, and in the Gospels it is used almost exclusively of Jesus. The exceptions are three parable characters: the father seeing his son a long way off, the Samaritan seeing the man in the road, and the master forgiving the debt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because they were **worn ragged and thrown down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eskulmenoi** — flayed, harassed, torn — and **errimmenoi**, thrown down, cast aside like something discarded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Like **sheep with no shepherd anywhere**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is old. Moses used it asking for a successor: *that the congregation of the LORD may not be **like sheep which have no shepherd***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Ezekiel, on Israel's leaders: *My sheep wandered through all the mountains… and no one was seeking or searching for them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel had shepherds. There were priests, scribes, elders and Pharisees in every town he walked through."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew says He looked at the crowd and saw no shepherd."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The harvest is enormous, but the workers are few**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He changes the picture mid-sentence — from sheep to a field."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sheep need a shepherd; a harvest needs hands. The crowd is both things in two verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **beg the Lord of the harvest to send workers out into His harvest field**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *beg* is **deomai** — to plead out of need."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Send out** is **ekbale** — to thrust out, drive out. It is a forceful word; Matthew uses it of casting out demons."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 10, verse 1: *and when He had called His twelve disciples to Him, He gave them power.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are told to pray for workers and are immediately sent as the answer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter break obscures it — the sending follows the instruction with nothing in between."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose harvest"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His harvest.* The field belongs to the one being asked."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He was looking at.** Crowds that had teachers, courts, synagogues and a temple — and the word Matthew uses is that they were thrown down, like sheep with nobody over them."}]}, "Micah 5:2": {"title": "\"But you, Bethlehem\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, seven hundred years early, and it names the village."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which Bethlehem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But you, **Bethlehem Ephrathah**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "There were two towns of that name. Micah specifies the one in Judah, using its older name, so there is no confusion with the Bethlehem in Zebulun."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prophecy is precise enough that it could only be fulfilled in one place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Too little to be counted\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Small among the clans of Judah, **too little to be counted**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bethlehem did not make the list. When Joshua divided the land and the towns of Judah were enumerated, Bethlehem is not in the Hebrew text of that list at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the village David came from, and even that had not made it a place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes out of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Out of you will come **one for Me**, a ruler over Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For Me.* The ruler is produced for God's own purposes before He is produced for Israel's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whose beginnings reach back **into the far distance, into ancient days, into times older than memory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *from of old, from everlasting* — **mi-qedem, mi-mei olam**. The same phrase is used in Scripture of God's own eternity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A ruler with a birthplace and no beginning. Micah says both in one sentence and does not explain how."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who read it out loud"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Herod asked the chief priests and scribes where the Christ was to be born, and they answered immediately: *in Bethlehem of Judea, for thus it is written by the prophet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They knew the verse, quoted it accurately from memory, and did not go the five miles to look."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it took to fulfil it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph and Mary lived in Nazareth, ninety miles north. A Roman emperor ordered a census of the whole empire, and it required every man to return to his ancestral town."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Caesar Augustus moved the population of the Roman world to get one pregnant woman to a village too little to be counted."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is beside it in the chapter.** The verse before speaks of a judge of Israel struck on the cheek with a rod; the verses after describe Him standing and shepherding *in the strength of the LORD*, and *He shall be their peace.* Micah puts the striking and the shepherding on the same page."}]}, "Micah 6:8": {"title": "\"He has shown you what is good\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The answer to a question somebody asks in a panic three verses earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Micah 6 is written as a courtroom scene. God calls the mountains as witnesses and puts His case: *O My people, what have I done to you? In what have I wearied you? Testify against Me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then He recites what He did — brought them out of Egypt, redeemed them from the house of bondage, sent Moses, Aaron and Miriam."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The panicked question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people answer, and the answer escalates:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With what shall I come before the LORD? **With burnt offerings, with calves a year old**? Will the LORD be pleased with **thousands of rams**, with **ten thousand rivers of oil**? Shall I give **my firstborn** for my transgression?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four bids, each bigger than the last, ending at child sacrifice — which the law forbade absolutely."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the mood the verse answers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He has shown you**, mortal, **what is good**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *He has told you* — it is already stated, already given."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question assumed the requirement was unknown and had to be guessed at with increasingly extravagant offers. The answer says it was never hidden."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Mortal\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **adam** — man, human being, the word for the first one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Only this: **to act justly, to love mercy, and to walk humbly with your God**.\"*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Act justly** — *mishpat*, right judgment. Do the right thing.", "**Love mercy** — the Hebrew is **chesed**, covenant loyalty, steadfast love. Not *do* mercy. **Love** it.", "**Walk humbly with your God** — one Hebrew word, **hatsnea**, found only twice in the Old Testament. It carries the sense of walking carefully, modestly, in step with somebody."]}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is brought. No rams, no oil, no calves, no firstborn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question was about what to carry to the altar and the answer is about how to walk."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scale of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three items against *ten thousand rivers of oil*. The reply is deliberately smaller than the question."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same word turns up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Chesed* is the word the psalms translate *mercy* and *lovingkindness* — *His mercy endures forever*, twenty-six times in Psalm 136."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word describing what God is like is the one the people are told to love."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"With your God\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last two words. Not *before* Him and not *for* Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Enoch is the man Genesis says *walked with God*, and Hebrews explains it as faith. See the note on Hebrews 11:4–7."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is being answered.** A man in a panic, bidding upward, asking what will be enough. The reply names three things, none of which cost anything, and opens by saying he was told long ago."}]}, "Nehemiah 13:10–14": {"title": "The singers had gone back to their fields", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Nehemiah comes back from a trip to Persia and finds the temple system quietly collapsed. What he notices first is where people are."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The detail that tells the story"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Levites and singers who did the work had gone back, each to his own field.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody rebelled. Nobody made a decision. The support stopped arriving, and men with families went home to grow food, because that is what a person does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nehemiah's question is aimed at the officials, not at the Levites: *why is the house of God neglected?* He puts the failure on the people responsible for the arrangement, not on the men who left."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he actually fixes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The interesting part is the administration. He does not merely restart the collection — he appoints four named men over the storerooms, *for they were counted faithful*, whose job is to distribute fairly among their kinsmen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The previous arrangement had been compromised: a few verses earlier, a priest had given a storeroom over to an official's private use. The problem was not only supply. It was that the system had stopped being trustworthy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nehemiah's answer to a giving problem is accountable handling of what is given."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The prayer at the end.** *Remember me for this, O my God, and do not wipe away the good I have done.* Nehemiah prays this four or five times in his book, always about specific administrative work — walls, gates, storerooms, rosters. It is the closing note of a man who did unglamorous jobs and wanted them noticed by the only person whose notice he cared about."}]}, "Nehemiah 13:4–5": {"title": "Tobiah in the storeroom", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The enemy who could not get over the wall ends up living inside the temple, and a priest gives him the keys."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who did it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now before this, **Eliashib the priest**, who **had charge of the storerooms of the house of our God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The high priest. The man who, in chapter 3, is named first in the list of builders — *Eliashib the high priest rose up with his brethren the priests and built the Sheep Gate.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He helped build the wall. He also opened the door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The connection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And was **closely tied to Tobiah**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *near to* — allied, related. Tobiah's family had married into Jerusalem's priestly households, and the alliance is stated as the reason for what follows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Had **made ready for Tobiah a large room**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *a great chamber*. Not a corner. The largest storeroom in the temple complex."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An **Ammonite** — and the chapter opens with the law being read that day: *the Ammonite and Moabite should never come into the assembly of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book puts the reading and the room three verses apart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the room was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was **the very place where they used to keep**:*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the grain offerings**", "**the incense**", "**the vessels**", "**the tithes of grain, new wine, and oil** set aside for the Levites, singers, and gatekeepers", "**the offerings for the priests**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nehemiah lists every item that had to be moved out to make space."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The clearing was not incidental. Somebody carried the vessels of the house of God out of that room and put a man's furniture in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it belonged to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Levites, the singers and the gatekeepers. Their entire living came from those stores."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened to them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verses on: *I also realised that **the portions for the Levites had not been given**, so that the Levites and the singers who did the work **had gone back to their own fields**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The music stopped and the temple staff went home to farm. Nobody voted on it and nobody announced it. The room was reallocated and the consequence followed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*During all this **I was not in Jerusalem**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nehemiah had gone back to Artaxerxes. It happened while the governor was away, and it took him going back to Persia and returning to find out. See the note on Nehemiah 13:6–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Tobiah was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Ammonite official who mocked the wall — *if a fox climbed up on it, he'd knock the stones right down.* See the note on Nehemiah 4:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His name means *the LORD is good*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How he got in.** Not by force. He was invited by the high priest, on the strength of a family connection, into a room that had to be emptied of the tithes first."}]}, "Nehemiah 13:6–9": {"title": "Everything thrown out of the room", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He comes back, finds out what has happened, and empties the room himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he had been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the **thirty-second year of Artaxerxes** king of Babylon I **had returned to the king**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said he would. Back in chapter 2 the king asked how long he would be gone and *I set him a time*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve years as governor, and then he went back to his post at court, exactly as promised."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The leave"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After some time I **asked his leave and came back to Jerusalem**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks a second time. The whole enterprise runs on permissions correctly obtained."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he found"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There I learned **the wrong that Eliashib had done for Tobiah**, making him a room in the courts of the house of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *wrong* is **ra'ah** — evil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names the priest and he names the beneficiary. No euphemism for either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It **grieved me deeply**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same construction used of Sanballat and Tobiah when they heard he was coming — *it grieved them exceedingly*. See the note on Nehemiah 2:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book uses the identical phrase for both sides."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So I **threw all of Tobiah's belongings out of the room**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I cast out*. Personally, by hand, into the courtyard."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No hearing, no negotiation with Eliashib, no committee. The furniture goes out the door."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus does the same thing to the same courts five centuries later, with a whip of cords and the tables of the money changers. See the note on John 2:13–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cleansing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then I gave orders, and **they cleansed the rooms**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is the ritual word — **taher**, the term for purifying what has been defiled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Occupation by an Ammonite made the storeroom unclean, and it had to be made fit before anything could go back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he brought back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And I brought back into them **the vessels of the house of God, with the grain offering and the incense**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same items listed in verse 5 as having been displaced, restored in the same order."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He reverses the transaction item by item."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He finds the Levites gone to their fields, confronts the officials — *why is the house of God forsaken?* — gathers the people back, and *all Judah brought the tithe of the grain and the new wine and the oil to the storehouse.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he appoints treasurers over the stores, *for they were considered faithful*, so it cannot happen again while he is away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Remember me, O my God, concerning this, and **do not wipe out my good deeds** that I have done for the house of my God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says a version of it four times in this chapter. It is how the book ends."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the whole chapter turns on.** A room. Not the wall, not an army, not a siege — a storeroom reassigned while the governor was out of the country, and the singers going home because there was nothing in it."}]}, "Nehemiah 2:5": {"title": "The sentence he had been waiting four months to say", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it is the request the whole book comes out of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is happening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And then I answered the king.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nehemiah is cupbearer to Artaxerxes — the man who tastes the wine first. It put him in the king's presence daily and made him, of necessity, someone the king trusted with his life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king has just noticed his face is sad, and Nehemiah says he *was very much afraid*. Sadness in front of a Persian king was a serious matter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gap between the chapters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The news from Jerusalem came in the month of Chislev. This conversation happens in Nisan."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four months. He wept, fasted and prayed through the whole of chapter 1 and said nothing to the king until asked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The half-second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the question and this verse there is one clause: *so I prayed to the God of heaven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Standing in front of the king, mid-conversation, with no time to withdraw. It is the shortest recorded prayer in the Old Testament and nothing of its content is given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wording"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If it pleases the king, and if your servant has found favor with you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Careful, formal court language. He does not demand and he does not explain his theology to a Persian."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Send me to Judah**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not permission to resign. Not funds. He asks to be sent — to go on the king's business, with the king's authority behind him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How he names the place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To **the city where my ancestors are buried**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say Jerusalem. He says the city of his fathers' tombs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Persian religion held the graves of ancestors in high regard, and a king could understand a man wanting to repair a family burial ground even if he had no interest in the God of Israel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nehemiah says something entirely true in terms his hearer would feel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**So that I may rebuild it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stated flat, at the end, with nothing after it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he was actually asking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rebuilding of Jerusalem's walls had been stopped by royal decree, in this same king's reign, recorded in Ezra 4 — *give the command to make these men cease, that this city may not be built.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is asking Artaxerxes to reverse Artaxerxes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he gets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king asks how long he will be gone, and Nehemiah *set him a time*. He had the answer ready."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then letters to the governors, timber from the king's forest, and an escort of army officers and horsemen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had thought through the whole logistics of it during four months of praying."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says about the outcome.** *And the king granted them to me, **according to the good hand of my God upon me**.* The letters are in his bag and the credit goes elsewhere. See the note on Nehemiah 2:9–10."}]}, "Nehemiah 2:9–10": {"title": "The two men who were displeased", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He arrives with an escort, and two men in the region are unhappy about it before he has laid a stone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The letters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So I came to the governors of the province **Beyond the River**, and **I gave them the king's letters**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Beyond the River* is the official Persian name for the satrapy west of the Euphrates — Nehemiah uses the imperial term, as a court official would."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letters are produced immediately. His authority is established at the border, in writing, before anyone can question it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The escort"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The king had also sent **army officers and horsemen** with me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezra, going the same road a few years earlier, had deliberately refused an escort — *I was ashamed to request of the king an escort of soldiers, because we had spoken to the king saying, \"The hand of our God is upon all those who seek Him.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nehemiah takes the cavalry. Ezra declines it. The book records both without ranking them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But when **Sanballat the Horonite** and **Tobiah the Ammonite official** heard of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sanballat** — governor of Samaria; a Babylonian name meaning something like *Sin has given life*, after the moon god. Papyri from Elephantine name him as governor, so he is a documented figure outside the Bible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tobiah** — a Hebrew name meaning *the LORD is good*. He is called *the Ammonite servant*, and he keeps turning up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man called The-LORD-is-good is the standing opponent for thirteen chapters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The connections Tobiah had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is married into a Jerusalem family, his son married the daughter of another, and nobles in the city were *sending letters to him*. In chapter 13 he is given a room in the temple itself. See the note on Nehemiah 13:4–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never attacks from outside. He is inside the correspondence the whole time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What displeased them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were **deeply displeased** that someone had come **to seek the good of the people of Israel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *it grieved them exceedingly with a great grief*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offence named is not the wall, the money, or the royal authority. It is that somebody had come to seek Israel's good."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Nehemiah does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He tells nobody. Three days after arriving he goes out at night with a few men and one animal, and rides round the ruins in the dark: *I told no one what my God had put in my heart to do at Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he calls the assembly, and only then does he say *let us build.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply when they mock"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The God of heaven Himself will prosper us**; therefore we His servants will arise and build, **but you have no heritage or right or memorial in Jerusalem**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Short, and not defensive."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What arrives with him.** Letters, timber, cavalry, and two enemies. All four are in the same paragraph, and Nehemiah lists them without treating the last as a surprise."}]}, "Nehemiah 3:1": {"title": "The Sheep Gate first", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A building register, and the order it is written in is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A list. Forty-odd work parties, named, each with the section of wall they rebuilt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It reads like a subscription roll and it is one of the most complete pictures of a city's social structure anywhere in the Old Testament — priests, goldsmiths, perfumers, district rulers, and *Shallum… he and his daughters*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who starts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **Eliashib the high priest rose up with his fellow priests**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priests take the first section. Not the governor, and not the engineers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Eliashib** — the name means *God restores*, or *God brings back*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gate"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **rebuilt the Sheep Gate**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gate on the north side, nearest the temple, through which the sacrificial animals were brought in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every lamb that died on the altar came through this door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is in John"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now there is in Jerusalem **by the Sheep Gate** a pool, which is called in Hebrew Bethesda.* See the note on John 5:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pool with the five porches and the crowd of sick people is at this gate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Bethesda** — house of mercy, or house of outpouring."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting apart"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Setting it apart as **holy** and hanging its doors.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *they sanctified it* — **qiddeshuhu**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only gate in the chapter that is consecrated, and it is consecrated twice: this gate and the towers beyond it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Fish Gate, the Valley Gate, the Dung Gate, the Fountain Gate, the Water Gate, the Horse Gate, the East Gate — all rebuilt, none sanctified."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list runs anticlockwise round the city and comes back to the Sheep Gate at the end. Verse 32: *and between the upper room at the corner, as far as the sheep gate, the goldsmiths and the merchants made repairs.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The circuit begins and ends at the same door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two towers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As far as **the Tower of the Hundred and the Tower of Hananel**, and these too they set apart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hananel** means *God is gracious*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah names the same tower in a promise about the city's future extent, and Jeremiah does too: *the city shall be built for the LORD from the Tower of Hananel to the Corner Gate.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the wall was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nehemiah had heard, in Susa, that *the wall of Jerusalem is also broken down, and its gates are burned with fire* — and *sat down and wept, and mourned for many days*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fifty-two days later the wall was up. *And it happened, when all our enemies heard of it… that they were very disheartened in their own eyes; for they perceived that this work was done by our God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gates in the list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Sheep Gate is where the sacrifice comes in, and it is first."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Water Gate is where Ezra reads the Law aloud to the people from morning till midday, and they weep."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order in the chapter is the order of the city's life."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Which gate was consecrated.** Only the one the lambs came through — and the pool with five porches and a paralysed man in them was lying right beside it."}]}, "Nehemiah 4:1–3": {"title": "\"If a fox climbed up on it\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The opposition changes tactics. Chapter 2 was displeasure; this is ridicule."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The escalation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It made him **furious**, and his anger **spilled over into scorn**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew has him burning with anger and then mocking. The mockery is what the anger turns into when there is nothing else available."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sanballat has no legal move — Nehemiah holds the king's letters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The audience"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In front of **his companions and the army of Samaria** he sneered.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He assembles a crowd first. This is a performance rather than a confrontation; he never speaks to Nehemiah, only about him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four questions"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*\"**What are these feeble Jews even doing?**\"*", "*\"**Do they think they can rebuild this?**\"*", "*\"**Will they offer sacrifices and finish in a single day?**\"*", "*\"**Can they breathe life back into stones that are nothing now but burned rubble?**\"*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one is a question, and none of them is asked for an answer. It is the standard shape of contempt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Feeble** in the Hebrew is *withered, languishing* — the word for a plant going limp."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stones"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Burned rubble.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an exaggeration. Nebuchadnezzar's men had burned the city a hundred and forty years earlier and the debris was still lying where it fell. Nehemiah's night ride found places where the animal could not get through."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sanballat's sneer is factually accurate about the material. The builders are using the burnt stones."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Tobiah's line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**That little wall of theirs**,\" he said, \"**if a fox climbed up on it, he'd knock the stones right down**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lightest weight he can name. Not an army, not a battering ram. A fox."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Tobiah's contribution is the joke, and he delivers it standing beside Sanballat while the army laughs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Nehemiah does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not answer them. He turns to God mid-chapter — *hear, O our God, for we are despised* — and then:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So we built the wall, and **the entire wall was joined together up to half its height**, for **the people had a mind to work**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the people had a heart to work*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reply to the mockery is the next verse of the construction report."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "When ridicule fails, the opposition moves to a plot to attack, and the builders work with a weapon in one hand and a tool in the other: *every one of the builders had his sword girded at his side as he built.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How long it took"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fifty-two days, and the last verse of the chapter about it says the surrounding nations *were very disheartened in their own eyes; for they perceived that this work was done by our God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fox joke was made about a wall that went up in under two months."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the mocking was aimed at.** Not the wall — the builders. *Feeble Jews.* The book answers it by recording the height reached in the same paragraph."}]}, "Nehemiah 7:4–5": {"title": "\"My God put it into my heart\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The wall is finished, and Nehemiah looks at a city that is still mostly empty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The city was **large and spacious, but few people lived in it**, and the houses had not yet been rebuilt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has spent fifty-two days rebuilding the walls against organised opposition, with a trowel in one hand and a weapon in the other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And now there is a defended city with almost nobody inside it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **my God put it into my heart** to gather the nobles and the rulers and the people to be registered by families.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *my God gave into my heart*. It is Nehemiah's characteristic phrase — he uses it earlier in the book too, of the work on the wall."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not report a vision, a prophet or a voice. He reports a thought arriving, and he says where it came from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it looked like from inside"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Like his own idea. Like an administrative instinct — a governor deciding a census is the sensible next step."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nehemiah's habit through the whole book is to name the source of his instincts as he goes. *The good hand of my God was upon me.* *What my God had put in my heart to do for Jerusalem — I told no one.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What came of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **I found the record of the genealogy** of those who had come back first.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He set out to organise a registration and found an existing document — the list of the first return under Zerubbabel, ninety years earlier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that follows is that list, name by name, family by family, down to the singers, the gatekeepers and the temple servants. The same list appears in Ezra 2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the record mattered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The genealogies decided who could serve as a priest — the chapter goes on to describe men who *sought their listing among those who had been registered by genealogy, but it was not found, so they were excluded from the priesthood.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A thought in a governor's heart, and a census that recovered a lost document and settled who could stand at an altar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with it next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 8. *All the people gathered together as one man… and Ezra the priest brought the Law before the assembly*, and read it from morning until midday, and the Levites *gave the sense, and helped them to understand the reading.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The registration led straight to the Book being opened in the square."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he was before this.** Cupbearer to Artaxerxes — a servant with a wine cup, in a Persian palace, four months' journey from a ruined city he had never seen. The first thing recorded of him is that he asked how Jerusalem was, and sat down and wept."}]}, "Nehemiah 8:9–10": {"title": "Do not weep, this day is holy", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The reading goes well, the response is tears, and three sets of leaders tell them to stop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is happening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wall is finished. The people gather in the square before the Water Gate and ask Ezra to bring the Book of the Law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Ezra the priest brought the Law before the assembly of men and women and all who could hear with understanding… **and the ears of all the people were attentive**.* See the note on Nehemiah 8:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "From morning until midday, on a wooden platform, with Levites moving among them explaining."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they read distinctly from the book… **and helped them to understand the reading**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **the people were weeping as they listened to the words of the Law**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have been in exile. Most of them have never heard the whole thing read."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The weeping is not confusion. They understood it, which is the problem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who stops them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **Nehemiah the governor, and Ezra the priest and scribe, and the Levites who were teaching**, said to them all.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The civil authority, the religious authority, and the teachers, together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three offices, one sentence. It is the only place in the book where all three speak as one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This day is holy to the LORD your God. Do not mourn, and do not weep**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **qadosh hu** — it is holy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The day is the first of the seventh month, the Feast of Trumpets. A festival day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are told to do instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go your way. **Eat rich food and drink something sweet**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ichlu mashmannim ushtu mamtaqqim** — eat fat things, drink sweet things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An instruction to have a good meal, given to people crying over Scripture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **send portions to those who have nothing ready**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **manot** — portions, shares."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anyone who has not prepared gets sent food. The celebration is not allowed to be private."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**For this day is holy to our LORD. Do not be grieved, for the joy of the LORD is your strength**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chedvat YHWH hi ma'uzchem**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chedvah** — gladness. A rare word, Aramaic in flavour, used only here and in Chronicles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ma'oz** — a stronghold, a fortified place, a refuge."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sentence says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *be joyful and you will feel stronger*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The joy belongs to the LORD — **of the LORD** — and it functions as a fortress."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the law, understand it, weep, be told not to, eat well, send food out, and be told the joy is the stronghold."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in that sequence is what the reading naturally produced."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And all the people went their way to eat and drink, to send portions and rejoice, **because they understood the words that were declared to them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same understanding that produced the weeping produces the rejoicing, one verse later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The next day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heads of the households come back to study further, and find the command about booths — and they keep the Feast of Tabernacles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Since the days of Joshua the son of Nun until that day **the children of Israel had not done so**. And there was very great gladness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A thousand years, and nobody had kept it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the confession comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 9. *They stood and confessed their sins and the iniquities of their fathers*, with sackcloth and dust on their heads."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mourning does happen. Three weeks later, on a day appointed for it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word.** **Ma'oz** — a stronghold. The joy is not described as a feeling that helps but as a fortified place, and the sentence says whose it is."}]}, "Numbers 10:33–36": {"title": "Searching out a resting place", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first move after Sinai, and the box goes in front."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The departure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they **left the mountain of the LORD** and traveled three days.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Almost a year at Sinai. The law given, the golden calf, the tabernacle built and raised, the priesthood consecrated, the census taken."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then they walk away from the mountain and the book of Numbers begins in earnest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The position"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the **ark of the LORD's covenant went ahead of them the whole way**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *went before them a three days' journey*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahead. Not in the middle of the column, which is where the arrangement in chapter 2 puts the tabernacle — three tribes on each side, the Levites in the centre."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For this march it goes to the front."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Searching out **a resting place** for them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **menuchah** — rest, a settled place. The noun behind the psalm's *He leads me beside the still waters* and *He makes me lie down*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ark is described as looking for somewhere for them to stop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cloud"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the **cloud of the LORD was over them by day** as they moved out of camp.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Over them while the ark went before them. Two signs at once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The words at setting out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whenever the ark set out, Moses would say, \"**Rise up, O LORD! Let your enemies scatter; let those who hate you flee before you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **qumah YHWH**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note whose enemies. Not Israel's — *Your* enemies, *those who hate You*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 68 opens with the same sentence: *let God arise, let His enemies be scattered*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The words at stopping"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And whenever it came to rest, he would say, \"**Return, O LORD, to the countless thousands of Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shuvah** — return, come back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Out at the front while they march; back in the middle when they stop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What these two lines are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In a synagogue, the first is said when the ark is opened and the Torah scroll is carried out, and the second when it is put back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two sentences from a wilderness march, still spoken over a cabinet in a wall."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 11: *now when the people complained, it displeased the LORD… and the fire of the LORD burned among them*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the craving for meat, the quails, Miriam and Aaron against Moses, and then the twelve spies."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ark goes ahead searching out a resting place, and the next forty years are spent not entering one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews makes of that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They could not enter in because of unbelief.* See the note on Hebrews 3:16–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *there remains therefore a rest for the people of God*. The Greek is **sabbatismos** — a Sabbath-keeping."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same arrangement at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When you see the ark of the covenant… then leave your places and follow it.* See the note on Joshua 3:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years later, a different generation, the same box in front."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the two the ark is in the middle of the camp. It goes first at the two boundaries — leaving the mountain, and entering the land."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the resting place ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Arise, O LORD, to **Your resting place**, You and the ark of Your strength.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 132, quoted by Solomon when the ark is finally carried into the temple."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The searching that starts in Numbers 10 has an address by 2 Chronicles 6."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the ark was doing out in front.** The Hebrew says searching out a resting place for them — and it says so at the start of forty years in which nobody rested."}]}, "Numbers 11:31–35": {"title": "The graves of craving", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "They got exactly what they asked for, in a quantity nobody could have wanted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quail"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A wind went out from the LORD and **drove quail in from the sea**, dropping them all around the camp — **a day's walk in every direction, lying about three feet deep**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Quail migrate across that region in vast flocks and come down exhausted. The mechanism is ordinary; the timing and the scale are not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three feet deep, for a day's journey in each direction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gathering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All that day and night and the next day the people gathered quail; **even the one who gathered least brought in heaps of them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty-six hours of collecting without stopping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Compare the manna: *he who gathered much had nothing left over, and he who gathered little had no lack.* With the manna nobody could get ahead. With the quail everybody did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **while the meat was still between their teeth, before it was even chewed**, the LORD's anger blazed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The judgement lands at the first mouthful."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They named the place **Kibroth-hattaavah — \"the graves of craving\"** — because there they buried the people who had given in to their craving.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **taavah** is desire, longing, craving — the same root as the word in verse 4, where *the mixed crowd was seized with a craving.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The place is named after the appetite rather than the plague."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm says about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He gave them their request, **but sent leanness into their soul**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 106, and it is one of the more frightening sentences in the Psalms. The request was granted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the camp moves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From Kibroth-hattaavah the people traveled to **Hazeroth**, and they stayed at Hazeroth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A flat travel note, and the writer records it without comment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next thing that happens at Hazeroth is Miriam and Aaron speaking against Moses, and Miriam coming out leprous."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does with this chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now these things became our examples, to the intent that **we should not lust after evil things as they also lusted**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "1 Corinthians 10, and Paul lists the wilderness failures one by one as written down *for our admonition.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was actually wrong"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that they wanted meat. God had given them quail once before, in Exodus 16, without any anger attached."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter locates the problem elsewhere: *you have wept in the hearing of the LORD, saying, \"Who will give us meat to eat? **For it was well with us in Egypt.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rejection of the present provision, and the rewriting of slavery as the good years. See the note on Numbers 11:4–9."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The other thing that happened in this chapter.** Seventy elders received the Spirit, and two of them — Eldad and Medad — prophesied in the camp instead of at the tent. Joshua wanted them stopped. Moses said *would that all the LORD's people were prophets.* The best line in the chapter is spoken by the man who had just asked to die."}]}, "Numbers 11:4–9": {"title": "\"Nothing to look at but this manna\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A complaint about food, and the writer interrupts it to describe the food."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it starts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **mixed crowd** traveling with them was seized with a craving, and soon **all Israel was weeping** again.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *mixed crowd* is the rabble — the non-Israelites who came out of Egypt with them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It starts at the edge of the camp and spreads to everybody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb for the craving is intense: they lusted a lusting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they remembered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"We remember **the fish we ate for free in Egypt**, the cucumbers and melons, the leeks and onions and garlic.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six items, listed with relish."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**For free.** They were slaves. Nothing in Egypt was free — it cost them four hundred years, the bricks without straw, and their sons in the Nile."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Memory has edited the price out and left the menu."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The complaint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But now **our appetite is gone. There's nothing to look at but this manna**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *our soul is dried up; there is nothing at all except this manna before our eyes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**This manna** — the definite article, dismissive. The bread of angels, and it has become *this*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the writer does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He stops the narrative to describe it:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The manna was **like coriander seed, pale like resin**. The people would **gather it, grind it or crush it, boil it in pots and shape it into cakes**; it tasted like something baked with oil. It **fell with the dew each night**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is an author's interjection, and it functions as a defence of the food."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He also notes there were six ways to prepare it. It was not monotonous; they were."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A wind went out from the LORD and **drove quail in from the sea**… **lying about three feet deep** across the ground, a day's walk in every direction.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They asked for meat and got a month of it. God is quoted earlier in the chapter: *until it comes out of your nostrils and becomes loathsome to you.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The graves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While the meat was still between their teeth… the LORD's anger blazed, and He struck them with a severe plague. They buried the dead there, and named the place **Kibroth-hattaavah — \"the graves of craving.\"***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A place name preserving what killed them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 106 sums it up: *He gave them their request, but sent leanness into their soul.* See the note on Numbers 11:31–35."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What familiarity had done"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about the manna changed. It fell the same way, tasted the same, and arrived every morning wrapped in dew."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What changed was that they had been eating it long enough to stop seeing it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Moses does in the middle of this chapter.** Asks God to kill him. *If You treat me like this, please kill me here and now — if I have found favour in Your sight — and do not let me see my wretchedness.* The complaint about the food broke the man leading them."}]}, "Numbers 12:4–9": {"title": "\"Not so with My servant Moses\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A family quarrel, settled by God appearing at the tent door."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What started it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Miriam and Aaron **spoke against Moses because of the Ethiopian woman whom he had married**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the real grievance: *\"**Has the LORD indeed spoken only through Moses? Has He not spoken through us also?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aaron is the high priest. Miriam is a prophetess, and had led the women with tambourines after the sea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their claim is not baseless. God had spoken through both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing. The narrator supplies the only comment:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now the man Moses was **very humble, more than all men who were on the face of the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written by Moses, according to the traditional authorship, which readers have found awkward for a very long time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The summons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Suddenly the LORD said to Moses, Aaron, and Miriam, \"**The three of you, come out to the meeting tent**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *suddenly*. There is no delay and no hearing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who appears"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD came down **in a pillar of cloud**, stood at the entrance of the tent, and called out, \"**Aaron and Miriam**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two names. Moses is summoned with them and then left out of the address."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says about prophets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When there is **a prophet among you**, I reveal myself to him **in visions** and speak to him **in dreams**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mar'ah** and **chalom**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the clearest statement in the Old Testament of how prophetic revelation normally worked. God names His own method."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are indirect — something seen, and something dreamed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exception"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But not so with My servant Moses**. He is **faithful in all my house**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he is entrusted with all My house*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews quotes it: *Moses indeed was faithful in all His house as a servant… but Christ as a Son over His own house.* See the note on Hebrews 3:7–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The difference"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"With him I speak **face to face, plainly and not in riddles**, and **he sees the very likeness of the LORD**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *mouth to mouth*, and *not in dark sayings* — **chidot**, riddles, enigmas."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus says the same: *the LORD spoke to Moses face to face, as a man speaks to his friend.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **why were you not afraid to speak against my servant Moses?**\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The departure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD's anger burned against them, **and he departed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that He went. The cloud lifts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was found"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when the cloud departed from above the tabernacle, suddenly **Miriam became leprous, as white as snow**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aaron is not struck. He is the high priest, and a leprous high priest would have ended the priesthood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Aaron does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Oh, my lord! Please **do not lay this sin on us**… please do not let her be as one dead.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls Moses *my lord*, which is the whole argument conceded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Moses cried out to the LORD, saying, \"**Please heal her, O God, I pray!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words in the Hebrew. The shortest prayer in the Old Testament, and it is for the woman who had just attacked him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The outcome"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven days shut out of the camp, and *the people did not journey till Miriam was brought in again*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole nation waits a week for her."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What God says about His own method.** Visions and dreams, to prophets, as the norm — and then names one exception, and the exception says nothing in his own defence."}]}, "Numbers 13:21–25": {"title": "The valley of the cluster", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Twelve men walk the length of the country for forty days and bring back one branch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How far they went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From **the wilderness of Zin** as far as **Rehob, near the pass to Hamath**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The southern desert to the far north. They covered the whole land, on foot, in enemy territory."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they saw at Hebron"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where **Ahiman, Sheshai, and Talmai** lived — **descendants of Anak**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The giants are named. Three of them, individually, which means somebody got close enough to learn who they were."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebron is where Abraham, Sarah and Isaac were buried. The patriarchs' graves are in the town the Anakim were holding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parenthesis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*(Hebron had been built **seven years before Zoan in Egypt**.)*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An aside about comparative city dating, dropped into a military report."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zoan was a major Egyptian centre. Whoever wrote this expected readers who would find the comparison meaningful."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cluster"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They cut down **a single branch with one cluster of grapes so heavy that two men carried it between them on a pole**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One cluster. Two men. A pole."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the only physical evidence they bring back, and it is beyond argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What else"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Along with some **pomegranates and figs**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three of the seven species Deuteronomy uses to describe the land — *a land of wheat and barley, of vines and fig trees and pomegranates.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They named that place **the Valley of Eshcol — \"the valley of the cluster\"** — because of the cluster they cut there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The place is renamed after the fruit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Forty days"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After **forty days** of searching the land, they made their way back.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That number becomes the sentence: *for each of the forty days you explored the land, you will bear your guilt a year.* See the note on Numbers 14:26–35."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A day for a year, measured against the walk they took and would not finish."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did with the evidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next paragraph: *they showed them the fruit of the land. \"It truly does flow with milk and honey — here is its fruit. **But** the people who live there are strong.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They hold up the grapes and then put a *but* after them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten men saw the cluster and reported that the land *devours those who live in it.* See the note on Numbers 13:26–33."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who else was there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Caleb. He walked the same forty days, saw the same three giants, and carried the same pole."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us go up at once and take it. We can certainly do it.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the fruit came from.** Not the coastal plain or the easy country. Eshcol is beside Hebron, in the hill country, where the Anakim were. The best grapes in the land were growing where the giants lived."}]}, "Numbers 13:26–33": {"title": "\"In our own sight, and so we looked to them\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Twelve men see the same country for forty days and come back with two reports."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What everybody agreed on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It truly does **flow with milk and honey** — here is its fruit.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "All twelve say that. The cluster of grapes took two men and a pole to carry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And all twelve agreed on the difficulties too: strong people, vast cities, towering walls, the descendants of Anak, and four named nations holding the ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The facts are not in dispute anywhere in this chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word that changes everything"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But** the people who live there are strong.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten men have the same information as Caleb and put a *but* in the middle of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ephes ki** — a strong adversative: *only, however, except that*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One word, and the report changes direction. Everything before it is true and everything after it is true; the word is what joins them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Caleb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Caleb **quieted the people** before Moses and said, \"**Let's go up at once and take it. We can certainly do it.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is emphatic — *we are well able*. It is not bravado about Israel's army; the chapter has already listed the giants and he is not disputing them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other report"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**We can't attack those people — they're far stronger than we are.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then it escalates into something else entirely:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The land we explored devours those who live in it.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is no longer a report. Forty days earlier it was a land flowing with milk and honey; now it eats people. The text calls it what it is — *a bad report*, and the Hebrew word is the one for slander."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Next to them we felt like grasshoppers, and that's how we looked to them too.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the two halves in order. First: *in our own sight*. Then: *and so we were in their sight*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They decided what they were, and then reported that the giants had reached the same conclusion. Nobody in Canaan had told them that. It is a guess about somebody else's opinion, made out of their own."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The self-assessment came first, and the world's assessment is what they projected out of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the giants actually thought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "We find out forty years later. Rahab, in Jericho: *I know that the LORD has given you the land, and that **the terror of you has fallen on us**… our hearts melted, and there remained no courage in anyone because of you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She says it had been that way *when you came out of Egypt* — the whole time the ten spies were feeling like grasshoppers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The giants were terrified. The grasshoppers were the ones who reported otherwise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Caleb and Joshua say the next day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The land we passed through is **an exceedingly good land**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"**Do not fear the people of the land, for they are our bread**; their protection has departed from them, and the LORD is with us.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Our bread** — the Hebrew is *they are our food*. The same men, the same walls, and the obstacle described as the provision."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Another spirit\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **My servant Caleb, because he has a different spirit in him and has followed Me fully**, I will bring into the land where he went.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *another spirit*. See the note on Numbers 14:20–25."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty-five years later, at eighty-five, he asks Joshua for the hill country with the fortified cities in it, names the same Anakim, and takes it. See the note on Joshua 14:6–15."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What it cost.** The next chapter: *as surely as I live, I will do to you the very things you spoke in My hearing.* They said *if only we had died in this wilderness*, and that is precisely what was granted. See the note on Numbers 14:26–35."}]}, "Numbers 14:1–4": {"title": "The night they wept", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A whole nation cries all night over a country they have not seen, on the strength of a report from ten men."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weeping"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That night the whole assembly broke into loud weeping, and **the sound did not fade until morning**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hours of it. Two million people, according to the census figures, weeping in the dark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing has happened. No battle has been fought, no one has been hurt. They have received information."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If only we had died in Egypt — or here in this wilderness!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hold that sentence. It is the exact fate God grants them twelve verses later, quoted back word for word: *as surely as I live, I will do to you the very things you spoke in My hearing… in this wilderness your bodies will fall.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They asked for it in so many words, in the dark, and it was heard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The accusation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Why is **the LORD bringing us into this land only to fall by the sword**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have redescribed the rescue as a trap. The God of the plagues, the sea and the manna is now a God who brought them out to kill them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The children"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Our wives and children will be taken as plunder.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the fear that undoes them, and it is the one God answers most directly: *as for your children — the ones you said would be taken as plunder — **I will bring them in**, and they will know the land you rejected.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The very thing they were afraid of losing is the thing that inherits. Their fear was not only wrong; it was inverted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Let's go back to Egypt\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they said to one another, \"**Let's choose a leader and go back to Egypt.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They get as far as a leadership plan. Not a moment of panic — an organised proposal to reverse the exodus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Egypt is where they were slaves, where the boys were thrown in the Nile, where they groaned until God heard. Two years out, it is the safe option."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is at the door"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are camped at Kadesh Barnea, on the southern edge of the land. The journey from Sinai had taken about eleven days."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are within walking distance of everything promised to Abraham, and they spend the night asking to go back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses and Aaron do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *they fell on their faces before all the assembly.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Joshua and Caleb tear their clothes and plead — *the land is exceedingly good… only do not rebel against the LORD.* The assembly's answer is *stone them with stones.* See the note on Numbers 14:5–10."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The forty years.** *For each of the forty days you explored the land, you will bear your guilt a year.* One year in the wilderness for each day the spies were out. The wandering is measured against the walk they would not take."}]}, "Numbers 14:20–25": {"title": "\"A different spirit\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The forgiveness and the consequence are in the same paragraph, and one man is pulled out of it by name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I have forgiven them\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD said, \"**I have forgiven them, as you asked.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses had just interceded — *pardon the iniquity of this people, according to the greatness of Your mercy* — and the answer is granted in five words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not withheld and it is not partial. And the next sentence is still *not one of them will see the land.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ten times"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Yet tested Me these ten times** and would not listen to My voice.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten. The rabbis counted them: at the Red Sea, at Marah, twice over the manna, at Rephidim, the golden calf, Taberah, Kibroth-hattaavah, and now this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He kept a count and states it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**None who treated Me with contempt** will see it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to spurn, to despise. It is a strong word, and it is aimed at what they did with the report, not at their conduct generally."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Caleb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**But My servant Caleb has a different spirit in him and has followed Me wholeheartedly.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things said about him, and each is worth its own line."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**My servant** — the title used of Moses, Abraham, David, and of the Servant in Isaiah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A different spirit** — the Hebrew is *another spirit*, a spirit other than the one in everybody else. He had walked the same forty days, seen the same walls, and measured himself against the same giants, and something in him was of a different kind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Followed Me wholeheartedly** — literally *filled after Me*, followed fully, all the way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise to him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will bring him into the land he explored, and his descendants will inherit it.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land he explored. The specific ground he walked as a spy is deeded to him personally, forty-five years before he collects it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does collect it, at eighty-five, and he asks for the part with the giants on it. See the note on Joshua 14:6–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then the turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Turn back tomorrow and set out into the wilderness, toward the Red Sea.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Back the way they came. Toward the sea they had walked through."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened the next morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They changed their minds — *we have sinned; we will go up.* And Moses told them not to, because the LORD was not among them. They went anyway and were beaten back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A day late, and doing it on their own strength, which is a different thing from faith even when the action looks the same."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two who came out.** Of everyone twenty and over who left Egypt, Joshua and Caleb are the only two who entered. Two out of a nation, and the thing that separated them was not courage in battle — none had been fought. It was what they said about a country after looking at it."}]}, "Numbers 14:26–35": {"title": "\"The very things you spoke in My hearing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God answers the complaint, and the answer is the complaint."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He heard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have heard every murmur they raise against Me.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *murmur* is the grumbling word used all through the wilderness accounts. It is not shouted; it is muttered among themselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He reports having heard all of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As surely as I live, declares the LORD, **I will do to you the very things you spoke in My hearing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then He quotes them: *\"In this wilderness your bodies will fall.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Go back one page. Their words the night before were *if only we had died in Egypt — or here in this wilderness!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gives them exactly what they asked for, in their own phrasing, and says so explicitly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is excepted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Except Caleb son of Jephunneh and Joshua son of Nun.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two who said something else. Caleb: *let's go up at once and take it — we can certainly do it.* Joshua, with him, tearing his clothes: *the land is exceedingly good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two men out of a nation, and the difference between them and everybody else is what came out of their mouths about the same country."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The children"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"As for your children — **the ones you said would be taken as plunder** — I will bring them in, and they will **know the land you rejected**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He repeats their own fear back to them and reverses it in the same sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The generation that was afraid for their children lost the land; the children got it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arithmetic"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**For each of the forty days you explored the land, you will bear your guilt a year** — forty years.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A day for a year. The wandering is measured against the reconnaissance — the walk they took and then would not finish."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Forty years of shepherding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your children will be shepherds here for forty years, bearing the weight of your unfaithfulness **until the last of your bodies lies in the wilderness**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The young ones spend their twenties and thirties burying the generation that would not go in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any suggestion the giants got stronger, or that the land became harder to take. It was as takeable the next morning as it had been the day before."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua and Caleb walked into it forty years later, and Caleb at eighty-five asked for the hill country with the Anakim on it — *give me this mountain.* See the note on Joshua 14:6–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same giants. A different man's report about them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What God said before all this.** *\"I have forgiven them, as you asked.\"* Moses had interceded and the forgiveness is granted in verse 20, five verses before the sentence. Forgiven, and still not going in — the consequence and the pardon are both in the same paragraph."}]}, "Numbers 14:5–10": {"title": "\"The shade that sheltered them is gone\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two men against a whole camp, and the argument they make is not the one you would expect."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Joshua and Caleb do not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do not say the giants are small, or that the ten scouts exaggerated. The report was accurate — the people were strong and the cities were fortified."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What they dispute is the conclusion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The strange image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We will swallow them up. The shade that sheltered them is gone, but the LORD is with us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is literally *their shadow has departed from them*. In that world, shade meant protection — the shade of a rock in the desert, the shadow of a king's hand, the shadow of God's wings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the argument is: whatever was covering these people has been withdrawn, and what is covering us has not. It is not a claim about relative strength. It is a claim about who still has cover."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it costs them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The whole assembly talked of stoning them.* That is the immediate response to the most faith-filled speech in the chapter, and the glory of the LORD appears at the tent precisely as the stones are being picked up."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word \"bread.\"** Joshua and Caleb's phrase for swallowing the enemy is the ordinary Hebrew for eating — the giants will be *bread* for us. It is a vivid, almost crude reversal of the ten scouts' report, which had said the land devoured its inhabitants. One party says the land eats people. The other says we will eat them. Same country, two readings."}]}, "Numbers 16:1–15": {"title": "\"You take too much on yourselves\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The complaint is made in the language of a compliment to everybody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who leads it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Korah, **a Levite**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an outsider. Korah is Kohathite — the branch of Levi that carried the ark, the table, the lampstand and the altar on their shoulders. He had the most sacred haulage job in Israel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And he was Moses' first cousin."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who joins him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Dathan and Abiram and On, and with them **two hundred and fifty leaders of Israel, men of name and standing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *men of renown*, called of the assembly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is not a mob. It is the leadership."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The accusation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You take too much on yourselves**,\" they said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *you have gone too far*, *enough from you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The whole community is holy, every one of them, and the LORD is among them all. Why do you lift yourselves above the assembly of the LORD?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every clause of that is true. God had said it at Sinai — *you shall be to Me a kingdom of priests and a holy nation*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Korah takes an accurate statement about the nation and uses it to conclude that no particular calling exists."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Moses' first response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When Moses heard it, **he fell facedown**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not answer. Numbers records him doing this repeatedly — at Kadesh, at Taberah, here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man accused of lifting himself up goes down on the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The test he proposes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In the morning **the LORD will show who is His** and who is holy… **Take censers**, all of you, and put fire and incense in them before the LORD tomorrow, and **we will see whom He chooses**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not defend the appointment. He hands the question over and sets a time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Incense was priest's work exclusively. Two hundred and fifty men are invited to do it, and the offer is not a trick — it is the test they asked for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question back to Korah"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You Levites — **is it a small thing that God has drawn you close** to serve at His dwelling? **Yet you reach for the priesthood too**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *drawn you close* is **qarab** — brought near. It is the word for approaching the altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He already had the nearness. The complaint is about the office."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Dathan and Abiram"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He sent for Dathan and Abiram, but **they refused to come**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And their answer inverts the whole exodus:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You brought us out of a land flowing with milk and honey** to die in this desert.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That phrase describes Canaan, in every other place it appears in the Torah. They apply it to Egypt — the country of the brick quotas and the river of dead babies."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And now you would **lord it over us**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Moses' defence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses was **deeply grieved** and said to the LORD, \"**I have not taken so much as a donkey from them, nor wronged a single one**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only thing he answers is the charge of profiteering, and he answers it to God rather than to them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel says almost the same words at the end of his life: *whose ox have I taken, or whose donkey have I taken?*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the whole quarrel is about.** Access. Korah, who carried the holy things, wanted to burn the incense — and Moses answers by handing out two hundred and fifty censers and letting God decide."}]}, "Numbers 16:16–35": {"title": "The ground opens", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The test Moses proposed is carried out, and twice in the middle of it he tries to stop what is coming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Korah and all his company came with their censers, **fire and incense burning**, and stood at the entrance of the tent, **the whole assembly gathered**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They turn up. All two hundred and fifty go through with it, in front of the nation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Korah has assembled the crowd as an audience."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The glory"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The glory of the LORD appeared.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He told Moses and Aaron to **step away from the people**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **they fell on their faces and pleaded**, \"**O God, will You be angry with the whole community when one man sins?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Told to get clear, they go down on the ground and argue for the people who had spent the morning accusing them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the second time in the chapter Moses hits the dirt. Both times it is on behalf of his accusers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham did the same over Sodom, and Moses himself had done it at Sinai over the calf."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The concession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the LORD had them **warn the people to move back** from the tents of Korah, Dathan, and Abiram.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The intercession works. The judgment narrows from the community to the three households that led it, and a warning is issued so the rest can get clear."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The people drew away.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sign"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**By this you will know the LORD has sent me**: if these men die **an ordinary death**, He has not. But **if the LORD does a new thing**, you will understand they scorned Him.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *a new thing* is *if the LORD creates a creation* — **bara**, the verb of Genesis 1, used only of God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses stakes his entire commission on it. If they die of natural causes, he was never sent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As he finished speaking, **the ground beneath the rebels split open**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately. He had said *by this you will know*, and the sentence had not cooled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **they and their households went down alive**. **It closed over them**, and they were gone from among the assembly.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fire"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **fire came out from the LORD** and took the **two hundred and fifty men who had offered the incense**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two different judgments in one paragraph. Dathan and Abiram's households by the ground; the two hundred and fifty by fire, at their censers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same fire that had consumed the offering at the tabernacle's dedication."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Israel **fled at the sound of it**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who did not die"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next chapter names *the sons of Korah* — they were not swallowed with their father. Numbers 26 says so plainly: *nevertheless the children of Korah did not die.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven psalms carry their name in the title, including *as the deer pants for the water brooks* and *how lovely is Your tabernacle*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The family of the man who wanted to be nearer to God ended up writing Israel's songs about wanting to be nearer to God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was done with the censers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bronze was hammered into a covering for the altar, *as a sign to the children of Israel* — a permanent reminder, bolted to the place where the offerings were made."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens the next day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The nation blames Moses and Aaron for it, and a plague starts. Aaron runs into the middle of it with a censer. See the note on Numbers 16:41–50."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Moses did twice.** Fell on his face for the people accusing him — before the test and during it. The narrowing of the judgment happens because of the second one."}]}, "Numbers 16:41–50": {"title": "Between the dead and the living", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An old man runs into a plague carrying a fire pan, and stops it by standing still."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The next day\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the next day** the whole community murmured again against Moses and Aaron.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One day after the ground opened and fire fell."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You have killed the people of the LORD**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They watched the earth swallow three households and fire take two hundred and fifty men at their censers, and concluded that Moses did it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they call the rebels *the people of the LORD*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What appears"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As they gathered in anger, **the glory of the LORD appeared**, and **a plague began to move among them**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses said to Aaron, \"**Quickly — take your censer, put fire from the altar and incense in it, and hurry to the people** to make things right, for **wrath has gone out and the plague has begun**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two details in it are exact:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Fire from the altar.** Not any fire. The fire off the bronze altar where the sacrifice burned. Korah's men had brought their own; two of Aaron's own sons had died for offering *strange fire*.", "**Incense.** The same substance the two hundred and fifty were killed holding. See the note on Exodus 30:34–38."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The identical act, done by the right man with the right fire, does the opposite thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The run"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Aaron ran. **He ran into the midst of the assembly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Toward it. Into a moving plague, into the crowd that had spent the morning accusing him of murder."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is in his eighties."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The position"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there **he stood — between the dead and the living** — holding up the incense, **and the plague was stopped**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he stood between the dead and the living*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The plague was running through the camp in a direction. He put himself on the line it was travelling and it did not pass him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The plague had killed **14,700 people**, besides those who had died in the affair of Korah.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fourteen thousand seven hundred died. The text does not say how many were behind him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **stood in the gap**, an old man with a censer, and **mercy held the line**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel later uses the same picture, looking for somebody to do it: *I sought for a man among them who would make a wall, and **stand in the gap** before Me on behalf of the land, that I should not destroy it — **but I found no one**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The return"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then he **returned to Moses at the entrance of the tent, the plague at an end**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He walks back. Nothing is said about what the people said to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was in his hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a sword, not an argument, not a defence of himself. A censer with coals off the altar and a handful of spice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows in the next chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The twelve rods laid up before the LORD overnight, and Aaron's alone *sprouted and put forth buds, produced blossoms and yielded ripe almonds.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A dead stick with fruit on it, kept in the ark as the final answer to Korah's question about who was chosen."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the priest ends up.** In the middle of the people who had just accused him, facing the wrong way — with his back to the safety and the incense held up."}]}, "Numbers 18:21–32": {"title": "A tithe of the tithe", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The one tribe with no land, and the arrangement that fed it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately after Korah's rebellion and Aaron's rod that budded. Korah's complaint was about who gets to come near; this chapter sets out what the nearness cost and what it paid."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Levites get"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To the Levites **I give the tithe of Israel** as their share, **in return for the work they do at the tent**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is stated as wages. The Hebrew is *for their service which they serve*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do not get"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They **will not have land either**, so this is their living.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every other tribe received territory in Canaan. Levi received forty-eight cities scattered through everyone else's allotments, and no block of its own."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua records the reason: *the LORD God of Israel was their inheritance.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second tithe"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **the Levites too must give**: **a tithe of the tithe**, the best of what they receive, lifted up to the LORD and given to Aaron the priest.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tribe that lives on other people's tithes tithes on what it receives."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten percent of the ten percent goes to the priests — Aaron's household within Levi."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The best of what they receive\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Set aside **the finest part** as holy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the fat*, **cheleb** — the choicest portion, the part of the sacrifice that always belonged to God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a tenth of whatever. A tenth off the top."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is left"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **the rest is yours to eat freely, wherever you are**, you and your households, for **it is your wages for your service**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in that sentence do a lot: **freely**, and **wherever**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest carried no restrictions. It could be eaten anywhere, by anyone in the household, with no ceremony attached."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence attached"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do this rightly, and you will not bear guilt** over the holy gifts.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The protection runs to the Levites themselves. Handling holy things carelessly was the danger the previous two chapters had just demonstrated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened when it stopped"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nehemiah, coming back from Persia, found the storerooms emptied for Tobiah and the Levites gone home to their fields because their portions had not been given. The temple music stopped. See the note on Nehemiah 13:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The arrangement in this chapter is what kept the tent staffed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the argument goes in Hebrews"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Those who are of the sons of Levi, who receive the priesthood, **have a commandment to receive tithes** from the people according to the law… But he whose genealogy is not derived from them **received tithes from Abraham**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer's point is that the Levitical arrangement was commanded, and the older one — Abraham to Melchizedek — was not. See the notes on Genesis 14:17–20 and Hebrews 7:8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The direction of the whole chapter.** A tribe that owns nothing, paid from what everyone else brings, and required to give the first tenth of that away before eating any of it."}]}, "Numbers 18:8–20": {"title": "\"I am your portion\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The tribe that gets no land is told what it gets instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Directly after Korah's rebellion and Aaron's rod that budded. Korah's complaint was about who gets to come near; this chapter sets out what the nearness costs and what it pays. See the note on Numbers 16:1–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gift"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**And I give you a portion**,\" the LORD said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I have given you the charge of My heave offerings*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is on the list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The offerings the people bring Me — **the grain gifts, the sin offerings, the guilt offerings, the holy things** — these are yours.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The first ripe fruits, the first oil and wine and grain, the firstborn of the flocks, the things devoted to Me** — I give them to you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The firsts. Not what is left over after the altar; the first of each crop and each flock."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who eats it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Yours and your sons' and daughters'** to eat.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole household, sons and daughters both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The redemption price"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Every **firstborn son and unclean animal is redeemed at a set price**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five shekels, according to the verses that follow. A firstborn son belonged to God from the night of the Passover, and was bought back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name for it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"This is your bread, **a covenant of salt, unbreakable, forever**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Salt because salt does not change, ferment or spoil. It is the term for an agreement that cannot be dissolved. See the note on Leviticus 2:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase is used of David's throne."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do not get"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You will have **no land of your own among Israel, no inheritance of fields**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every other tribe received territory. Levi received forty-eight towns scattered through everyone else's allotments — including the six cities of refuge. See the note on Numbers 35:9–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man fleeing a death ran into a Levite town and lived off the priests' provision."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exchange"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **I am your portion and your inheritance**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *portion* is **cheleq** — a share, an allotted piece of ground. It is a surveying word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God uses the land word about Himself and gives it to the tribe with no land."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When others count their acres, **you will count Me**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it turns up in the psalms"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD is **the portion of my inheritance** and my cup; You maintain my lot. The lines have fallen to me in pleasant places.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David, who owned a kingdom, uses the Levite's line about himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Asaph: *my flesh and my heart fail, but God is the strength of my heart and **my portion forever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah in the ruins: *\"**The LORD is my portion**,\" says my soul, \"therefore I hope in Him.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Levites lived on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tithe, and they tithed on that in turn — a tenth of the tenth, the best of it, up to the priests. See the note on Numbers 18:21–32."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The trade.** No fields, no boundary stones, no inheritance to pass on. In place of all of it, one sentence: *I am your portion.*"}]}, "Numbers 19:1–10": {"title": "The red heifer", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The strangest ordinance in the law, and everything about it runs backwards."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD gave Moses and Aaron **a strange and gracious provision**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew calls it **chuqqat ha-torah** — the statute of the law. Jewish tradition classes it among the **chuqqim**, the decrees given without a stated reason, and rabbinic literature treats it as the one even Solomon could not work out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The animal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **red heifer**, **perfect and unblemished**, one that has **never worn a yoke**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three requirements:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Red** — the Hebrew is **adummah**, from the root for red, blood, earth, and **adam**.", "**Without blemish** — the standard sacrificial requirement.", "**Never worn a yoke** — never put to work. Nothing had been got out of it."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "A female, which is unusual; most offerings were male. And red, which is specified for nothing else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is killed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lead it **outside the camp** and let it be slaughtered there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not at the altar. Outside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews takes it up: *the bodies of those animals, whose blood is brought into the sanctuary by the high priest for sin, are burned **outside the camp**. Therefore Jesus also, that He might sanctify the people with His own blood, **suffered outside the gate**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Eleazar is to **sprinkle some of its blood toward the tent seven times**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven, toward the tent, from outside it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleazar, not Aaron — the deputy, so the high priest does not become unclean."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is burned"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the heifer is **burned whole — hide, flesh, blood, and all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is kept back and nothing is eaten. The entire animal, including the blood, goes into the fire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things thrown in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **cedar wood, hyssop, and scarlet wool** are cast into the fire with it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same three used in the cleansing of a leper in Leviticus 14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cedar is the tallest tree in that country and hyssop the smallest bush — the pair Solomon is said to have spoken of, from the cedar to the hyssop. And scarlet, the colour of the crimson dye."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hyssop is what the blood was applied with at the Passover, and what was lifted to the cross with the sour wine."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ashes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ashes are **gathered by a clean man** and **kept outside the camp in a clean place**, to be **mixed into the water of cleansing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ash of one animal, kept in store, mixed with running water as needed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jewish tradition holds that very few red heifers were ever prepared across the whole history of the temple. One animal supplied the nation for generations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it was for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 19 goes on: contact with a dead body. Anyone who touched a corpse, or a grave, or was in a tent where someone died, was unclean seven days — and this water was the only remedy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An unavoidable defilement, with a provision waiting for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inversion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Those who **handle it must wash and wait until evening**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who gathers the ashes becomes unclean. The priest who sprinkles the blood becomes unclean. The man who burns it becomes unclean."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone who touches the thing that makes people clean is made unclean by it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it covers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A lasting statute **for Israel and for the stranger among them**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Hebrews takes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For if the blood of bulls and goats, and **the ashes of a heifer**, sprinkling the unclean, sanctifies for the purifying of the flesh, **how much more shall the blood of Christ**… cleanse your conscience from dead works to serve the living God?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer names the heifer specifically, and the thing he says is cleansed is the conscience."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is odd about it.** Everyone who administers it is defiled by it, the animal is killed outside the camp, and the whole of it — blood included — goes into the fire. The ashes of one animal, kept in a clean place, waiting for whoever touched death."}]}, "Numbers 19:11–13": {"title": "Seven days, and two of them matter", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The rule for touching the dead, and it is the reason for the ash of the red heifer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The red heifer, killed outside the camp, burnt entire with cedar, hyssop and scarlet, and the ashes gathered and stored *for the water of purification*. See the note on Numbers 19:1–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ash existed for one purpose, and this passage is it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Whoever **touches a dead body** is **unclean for seven days**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not sinful. Unclean, and the code is careful about the difference."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Death was the strongest source of ritual impurity in the system. A corpse, a grave, a bone, or a tent where somebody had died."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it mattered so much"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because it made ordinary life difficult. Somebody had to bury the dead, and somebody had to sit with them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The high priest and a Nazirite were forbidden to go near a corpse at all — even a parent's. Which is what Samson's Nazirite vow was, and he ate honey out of a lion's carcass. See the note on Judges 14:12–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The remedy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"On the **third day and the seventh** he is to be cleansed with the water, and then he is clean.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two applications, and the days are specified."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The water is the ash of the heifer mixed with running water, sprinkled with a bunch of hyssop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If he refuses**, uncleanness clings to him.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *if he does not purify himself*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The remedy is available and can be declined. Nothing in the passage forces anybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And he **defiles the dwelling of the LORD**; that person must be **cut off from Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **karat** — cut off, severed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man in that condition, walking around, is a problem for the whole camp — not for himself alone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **the water of cleansing was not sprinkled on him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage states the cause in the last clause. Not the touching. The not being sprinkled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The uncleanness was ordinary and expected. The failure was in not using what had been provided."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oddity of the whole rite"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everybody involved in making the ash becomes unclean — the priest who supervises, the man who burns it, the man who gathers it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ash that cleanses others defiles everyone who handles it. The rabbis listed it among the statutes that cannot be reasoned out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For if the blood of bulls and goats, and **the ashes of a heifer, sprinkling the unclean, sanctifies for the purifying of the flesh**, how much more shall the blood of Christ… **cleanse your conscience from dead works** to serve the living God?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter names this rite specifically, and puts *dead works* against a rule about dead bodies."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old rite reached the flesh. The claim is that this one reaches the conscience."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hyssop"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Purge me with hyssop, and I shall be clean; wash me, and I shall be whiter than snow.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David, in Psalm 51, using the vocabulary of this rite about something the rite did not cover."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the phrase goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us draw near with a true heart in full assurance of faith, **having our hearts sprinkled from an evil conscience**, and our bodies washed with pure water.* See the note on Hebrews 10:22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sprinkled and washed, in one sentence, and both perfect passives."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the last clause names as the failure.** Not the contact with death, which was ordinary and unavoidable. *The water of cleansing was not sprinkled on him* — the remedy was there and was not used."}]}, "Numbers 2:1–2": {"title": "Facing inward", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses of camp layout, and the arrangement is the whole point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let the Israelites camp **under their own banners**, each family **beneath the standard of its ancestors**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **degel** — a standard, a military banner. And **ot**, a sign or ensign."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Tribal identity is not erased. Everybody keeps their own flag."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The orientation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They are to pitch their tents **facing the Tent of Meeting on every side**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **minneged saviv** — over against, round about."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every tent door points inward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The centre"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Gathered around **the place where I dwell**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tabernacle is not at one end of the camp or on a hill above it. It is in the middle, with the Levites camped immediately round it and the twelve tribes in four blocks beyond them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four sides"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest of the chapter gives the arrangement."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**East** — Judah, Issachar, Zebulun. Judah leads, and the east is the entrance side."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**South** — Reuben, Simeon, Gad."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**West** — Ephraim, Manasseh, Benjamin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**North** — Dan, Asher, Naphtali."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three tribes to a side, with the leading tribe of each block named."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The numbers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each block is counted. Judah's side: 186,400. Reuben's: 151,450. Ephraim's: 108,100. Dan's: 157,600."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Over six hundred thousand fighting men, plus families."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four standards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Scripture does not say what was on them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jewish tradition, in the Targums and the rabbinic sources, assigns a lion to Judah, a man to Reuben, an ox to Ephraim, and an eagle to Dan."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Those are the four faces of Ezekiel's creatures. See the note on Ezekiel 1:10–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the four creatures round the throne in Revelation. See the note on Revelation 4:5–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tradition is late and cannot be verified from the text. The correspondence is why it is repeated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the shape does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A camp arranged this way has its most valuable thing furthest from every edge."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And every family, waking up and stepping out of a tent, is looking at the same place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The marching order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 10 gives it. Judah first, then Issachar and Zebulun. Then the tabernacle comes down and travels in the middle of the column."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they started out for the first time according to the command of the LORD by the hand of Moses.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the ark going ahead: *the ark of the covenant of the LORD went before them for three days' journey, to search out a resting place for them*. See the note on Numbers 10:33–36."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Balaam saw from the hill"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Balaam raised his eyes, and **saw Israel encamped according to their tribes**… and he took up his oracle and said: \"**How lovely are your tents, O Jacob!**\"* See the note on Numbers 24:3–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was hired to curse, and what he was looking at when he blessed them instead was this arrangement, laid out below him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the shape goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the city had twelve gates… **three gates on the east, three gates on the north, three gates on the south, and three gates on the west**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The New Jerusalem, four sides, three per side, and the names of the twelve tribes on them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the layout guarantees.** Everybody's door faces the same direction. Six hundred thousand men, twelve tribes, four sides, and one thing in the middle of it that nobody is camped with their back to."}]}, "Numbers 20:10–11": {"title": "He struck it twice", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses does three things he was not told to do, and the water comes anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gathering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses and Aaron **gathered the assembly in front of the rock**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That part was the instruction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Listen now, you rebels** — **must we bring you water out of this rock?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things in one sentence, and neither was in the instruction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**You rebels** — the Hebrew is **hammorim**. It is the only recorded time Moses addresses the nation like that, and the man described in Numbers 12 as *very humble, more than all men who were on the face of the earth* is the one saying it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Must we** — the pronoun is first person plural. God had said *you will bring water out of the rock for them*, and Moses says *must **we***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The striking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Moses **lifted his hand and struck the rock twice** with his staff.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was told to speak to it. He hits it, and then hits it again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The doubling is recorded deliberately. Numbers could have said he struck it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **water came gushing out, more than enough**, and **the people drank, and their animals too**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *water came out abundantly*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is withheld. The people get everything that was promised, the animals are included as specified, and the supply is described as more than sufficient."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The failure is Moses'. The provision is unaffected by it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the earlier instruction had been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Rephidim, thirty-eight years before: *strike the rock, and water will pour out.* See the note on Exodus 17:4–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is doing what worked last time. The instruction had changed. See the note on Numbers 20:7–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the striking said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verdict in verse 12 names the problem as failing to *honor Me as holy in the sight of the people of Israel*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The nation was watching. What they saw was a furious man calling them rebels and beating a rock, and what they were told to see was a word spoken and water coming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name of the place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This was the water of **Meribah** — contention — because the children of Israel contended with the LORD, and He was hallowed among them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same name as Rephidim carried. Two places called Quarrelling, thirty-eight years apart, over the same commodity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says the rock was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They drank from a spiritual Rock that went with them, and **that Rock was Christ**.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 10:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses says about it later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Deuteronomy he mentions it three times, and each time he attaches it to the people: *the LORD was angry with me for your sakes*, and *because of you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never disputes the ruling."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not affected.** The water. Numbers puts the disobedience and the abundance in the same sentence — struck twice, and it came out more than enough."}]}, "Numbers 20:12": {"title": "\"It was a heavy word, and it was final\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse ends the careers of both men who led Israel out of Egypt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is said to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD said **to Moses and Aaron**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both. Aaron was standing there and is included in the ruling; he dies on Mount Hor before the chapter is over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Because you did not trust Me enough to honor Me as holy in the sight of the people of Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *because you did not believe Me, to sanctify Me in the eyes of the children of Israel*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses, and the second is the one that names the setting: **in the sight of the people**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was at stake"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not water — that came. See the note on Numbers 20:10–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The nation had watched. What they saw was a man calling them rebels and hitting a rock twice, and what they were meant to see was a word spoken and water arriving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The charge is about the picture given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will not bring this assembly into the land I am giving them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years of leading, and the last stretch is withheld."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gets to the border. Deuteronomy 34 has him on Nebo, shown the whole land from Gilead to the Western Sea, and he dies there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The closing line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was **a heavy word, and it was final**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses did about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked. Deuteronomy records the prayer: *\"I pleaded with the LORD at that time, saying, 'O Lord GOD… **let me cross over and see the good land beyond the Jordan**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer: *\"**Enough of that! Speak no more to Me of this matter**. Go up to the top of Pisgah, lift your eyes westward, northward, southward and eastward, and see it with your eyes, **for you shall not cross over this Jordan**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Asked once, refused, and told not to raise it again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about the reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three times in Deuteronomy he attaches it to the people: *the LORD was angry with me **for your sakes***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never disputes the ruling and never mentions the rock in those passages."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was not taken away"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He writes Deuteronomy after this. He commissions Joshua, blesses the tribes, sees the land from the mountain, and God buries him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the man who was told he would not enter the land is standing in it in Matthew 17, on a mountain, talking with Jesus. See the note on Matthew 17:1–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The epitaph"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But since then **there has not arisen in Israel a prophet like Moses, whom the LORD knew face to face**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written after the ruling, in the same book, as the last word on him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the charge is.** Not temper and not disobedience in the abstract. The verse says *in the sight of the people of Israel* — the nation was watching, and what they were shown was wrong."}]}, "Numbers 20:2–5": {"title": "The old complaint, forty years on", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The same argument they had at the beginning of the journey, made again at the end of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Kadesh, in the fortieth year. Miriam has just died and been buried there — the verse immediately before this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The generation that came out of Egypt is nearly gone. Most of the people complaining were children when the last water crisis happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shortage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **there was no water for the people**, and they **gathered against Moses and Aaron**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *gathered against* is the same verb used of Korah's company. It is the word for assembling in opposition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wish"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**If only we had died with our brothers before the LORD!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are referring to the fourteen thousand seven hundred who died in the plague after Korah, and to the two hundred and fifty at the censers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They wish they had been among them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The accusation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why did you bring the LORD's assembly out into this wilderness to die here** — us and our animals?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why did you drag us up out of Egypt into this terrible place?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both questions are addressed to Moses and Aaron. Neither mentions that the forty years were the consequence of a decision the nation made at this same place, Kadesh, when the spies came back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they miss"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"It is **no place for grain or figs or vines or pomegranates**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four crops, and every one of them is on the list the spies brought back **from Canaan**: *they came to the Valley of Eshcol and cut down a branch with one cluster of grapes… they also brought some of the pomegranates and figs.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are describing the promised land and attributing it to Egypt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had in Egypt"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers 11 has the earlier version: *we remember the fish which we ate freely in Egypt, the cucumbers, the melons, the leeks, the onions, and the garlic.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No mention of the brick quotas or the drowned sons, either time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The old complaint rose again, sharp and bitter\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the fifth or sixth water-and-food crisis in the book. Marah, Rephidim, Taberah, Kibroth Hattaavah, the fiery serpents, and this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses and Aaron do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They walk away from the crowd, go to the tent door, and fall on their faces. See the note on Numbers 20:6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same thing Moses did during Korah's rebellion, twice. See the note on Numbers 16:16–35."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Deuteronomy says about the same forty years"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD your God has blessed you in everything your hands have done**… These forty years the LORD your God has been with you, and **you have lacked nothing**.\"* See the note on Deuteronomy 2:1–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses says that in his last sermon, about the period the people are complaining about here."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is the same and what is not.** The complaint is nearly word for word what was said at Rephidim thirty-eight years earlier. What is different is that the God they are complaining to has fed them every morning for the whole intervening time."}]}, "Numbers 20:25–29": {"title": "The garments taken off one man and put on another", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Aaron's death is unlike any other in the Old Testament. He is told where, told when, and walks up the mountain to it in front of the whole nation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of events"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Take off Aaron's priestly garments and put them on Eleazar his son… Aaron will be gathered to his people, and he will die there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The transfer comes **before** the death. Moses strips his brother of the robes on the mountain top, dresses the son in them, and only then does Aaron die."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priesthood is not interrupted for a moment. There is never a point at which Israel has no high priest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it was done in public"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They went up the mountain in the sight of the whole community.* Three men climb; two come down, one of them wearing the vestments everybody recognised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing had to be explained afterwards. The nation watched the office change hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it exposes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A priest dies and the robes go to somebody else, over and over, generation after generation. The system depends on replacement because the men wearing it keep running out."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The comment the New Testament makes.** Hebrews puts its finger on exactly this: there had to be many priests, *because death kept stopping them from staying in office* — but He continues forever, so His priesthood does not pass to anyone else. The writer had scenes like this mountain in mind. See the note on Hebrews 2:14–16, where the same argument runs the other way: He took a body in order to die."}]}, "Numbers 20:6": {"title": "They turned away and fell on their faces", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it is entirely made of movements."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Moses and Aaron **turned away from the crowd**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do not answer. The assembly has just accused them of dragging the nation out of Egypt to die. See the note on Numbers 20:2–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither man argues."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they go"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And went to **the door of the tent of meeting**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **ohel moed** — the tent of appointment, the place God had said He would meet them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Away from the people and toward the tent. That is the whole geography of the verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The posture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they **fell on their faces**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses does this repeatedly in Numbers. At Korah's rebellion he does it twice — once when the accusation is made and once when the judgment is announced, both times on behalf of the people accusing him. See the notes on Numbers 16:1–15 and Numbers 16:16–35."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the glory of the LORD appeared to them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kavod** — weight, glory."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase appears in chapter 16 and again in chapter 14, both times in the middle of a rebellion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not recorded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any words. Numbers does not give a prayer, a request, or a complaint. Two men walk to a door and lie down, and the glory appears."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern this establishes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before has the accusation. The verse after has the instruction: *take the staff… speak to the rock.* See the note on Numbers 20:7–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the problem and the answer there is one verse, and it consists of leaving the crowd and going to the tent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is missing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Miriam. She died and was buried at Kadesh in the verse before this chapter's crisis begins."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aaron dies at the end of the same chapter, on Mount Hor. And Moses is told in verse 12 that he will not enter the land. See the note on Numbers 20:12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "All three leaders are gone or under sentence by the end of Numbers 20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses does elsewhere with the same posture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the golden calf: *I fell down before the LORD, as at the first, forty days and forty nights.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "When the spies' report caused the rebellion: *then Moses and Aaron fell on their faces before all the assembly.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does it more than anyone else in Scripture."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The sequence.** Accused, they turn their backs on the crowd, walk to a door, and go face down. And the next thing recorded is that the glory showed up."}]}, "Numbers 20:7–9": {"title": "\"Speak to the rock\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The instruction is given in four parts, and one word in it is different from last time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Take the staff"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take the staff**, and gather the people together, you and your brother Aaron.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew has the definite article — **the** staff, and the next verse says Moses took it *from before the LORD*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is where Aaron's rod was kept. It had been laid up in front of the Testimony after Korah's rebellion, and it had budded, blossomed and produced almonds overnight."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A dead stick with fruit on it, kept as the answer to the question of who was chosen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Speak"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Then **speak to the rock in front of them all**, and **it will give up its water**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *speak* is the ordinary verb. Nothing is to be done to the rock."}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Rephidim, thirty-eight years earlier, the instruction was **strike it**. See the note on Exodus 17:4–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same nation, same problem, different instruction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who brings the water"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You will bring water out of the rock for them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses is given the credit in advance, in the instruction itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And give **the community and their animals** something to drink.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The animals are included. The people had complained about them in verse 4 — *us and our animals* — and the answer covers them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The obedience"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Moses **took the staff from before the LORD, just as He had told him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first half is exact. He does what he is told."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gathers the assembly, calls them rebels, asks *must we bring you water out of this rock*, and strikes it twice. See the note on Numbers 20:10–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The water comes anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two rods"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses' rod is the one that struck the Nile and turned it to blood, struck the rock at Horeb, and was stretched over the sea. It is a rod of judgment throughout Exodus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aaron's rod is the one that budded — the dead branch that produced almonds and was kept in front of the ark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter says the staff was taken *from before the LORD*, which is where the budded one was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says the rock was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They drank from **a spiritual Rock that went with them, and that Rock was Christ**.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 10:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Because you did not trust Me enough to honor Me as holy** in the sight of the people of Israel, **you will not bring this assembly into the land**.\"* See the note on Numbers 20:12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verdict is about representation — what the action said about God in front of the nation."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The change in the instruction.** Struck once, and after that spoken to. Numbers gives the new instruction plainly and then records that it was not followed."}]}, "Numbers 21:4": {"title": "The long way around", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse of geography, and it explains everything that happens next."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The departure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then they **set out from Mount Hor** along the way to the Red Sea.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aaron has just died on Mount Hor, in sight of the whole congregation, and they have mourned him thirty days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Along the way to **the Red Sea**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is backwards."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are heading for Canaan, and this road runs south, toward the water they crossed forty years ago."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Going **the long way around Edom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lisbov et-eretz Edom** — to go around the land of Edom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What had happened with Edom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses had sent messengers: *please let us pass through your country. We will not pass through fields or vineyards, nor will we drink water from the wells; **we will go along the King's Highway**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Edom said to him, \"**You shall not pass through, lest I come out against you with the sword**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They offered to pay for water. The answer was no, twice, and then Edom came out with a large force."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who Edom was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Esau's descendants. Israel's cousins."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the instruction was explicit: *do not meddle with them, for I will not give you any of their land… **because I have given Ar to the descendants of Lot as a possession***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They were forbidden to fight, so they walked around."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A detour of many days, south and then east, over the worst terrain in the region, in order to avoid a war they were not permitted to fight."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the soul of the people grew weary on the journey**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vattiqtzar nephesh-ha'am badderek** — the soul of the people was shortened by the way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Qatzar** — to be short, to be cut short. The idiom for patience running out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same construction as *the LORD's soul was shortened* over Israel's misery, and Samson's soul *was vexed to death* by Delilah's daily questions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the verse is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It gives the reason before the sin."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse is the complaint that brings the serpents. The narrator supplies the context first and does not use it as an excuse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why did you bring us up out of Egypt to die in the wilderness? There is no bread. There is no water. And we loathe this miserable food**.\"* See the note on Numbers 21:5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two of the three complaints contradict each other in the same breath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the LORD sent fiery serpents among the people, and they bit the people, and many of the people of Israel died.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the LORD said to Moses, \"**Make a fiery serpent, and set it on a pole; and it shall be that everyone who is bitten, when he looks at it, shall live**.\"* See the note on Numbers 21:8–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And as **Moses lifted up the serpent in the wilderness**, even so must the Son of Man be lifted up.\"* See the note on John 3:14–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The victory just before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same chapter opens with the king of Arad attacking and taking prisoners, and Israel vowing a vow and destroying the cities. The place was called **Hormah**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A victory, then a funeral, then a detour, then a complaint. Numbers puts them in that order."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The idiom.** *The soul of the people was shortened by the way.* Numbers gives the reason for the impatience before recording what came out of it, and does not treat the reason as an excuse."}]}, "Numbers 21:5": {"title": "\"We loathe this miserable food\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse of complaint, and the last clause of it is the writer's own comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they speak against"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They spoke **against God and against Moses**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both, and God first. Numbers is precise about the order elsewhere too — the grumbling is aimed past the leader."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three complaints"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Why did you bring us up out of Egypt **to die in the wilderness**? **There is no bread. There is no water.** And **we loathe this miserable food**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read them together and the last two contradict each other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no bread — and we are sick of the bread. They complain about the absence of food and the quality of the food in the same breath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *miserable* is **qeloqel** — worthless, contemptible, light. It is a made-up-sounding reduplicated form, and it occurs nowhere else in the Old Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They coined a word for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The narrator's line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The manna that had kept them alive — **they had come to despise even that**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the verdict on the sentence, and it names what the food actually did: **kept them alive**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years of a substance falling every morning, wrapped in dew, that nobody had to plant or store or pay for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it had been called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The bread of angels*, in Psalm 78. *The grain of heaven*. See the note on Psalms 78:21–25."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about it changed between Exodus 16 and here. The description in Numbers 11 — coriander seed, resin-pale, tasting of oil — still stood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had simply been eating it long enough to stop seeing it, and the same complaint appears in chapter 11 and again here, ten chapters and many years apart. See the note on Numbers 11:4–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What followed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the LORD sent fiery serpents among the people, and they bit the people, and many of the people of Israel died.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the pole. See the note on Numbers 21:8–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they were when they said it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *they journeyed from Mount Hor by the Way of the Red Sea, **to go around the land of Edom**, and the soul of the people became very discouraged on the way.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A detour. Edom had refused them passage, so they had to go the long way round, and the complaint comes out of the extra miles."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the same chapter ends.** With a song. *Spring up, O well! All of you sing to it* — sung over a well at Beer, a few verses after the snakes. Numbers puts the loathing and the singing on the same page."}]}, "Numbers 21:8–9": {"title": "\"He only had to look\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A nation is dying of snakebite and the remedy is a piece of metal on a stick."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the remedy is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The snakes are not removed. Nothing in the passage says the serpents left."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They keep biting, and the arrangement God gives is for people who are still being bitten."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Make **a fiery serpent** and set it up **on a pole**. And it will be that everyone who is bitten, **when he looks at it, will live**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An image of the very thing killing them, raised where everybody can see it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *pole* is a standard, a banner-pole — the thing you lift so an army can see it from a distance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is asked of the dying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He did not have to fix himself, or reach the wounds his own hands could not reach. **He only had to look** — to turn his eyes away from the poison in him toward the thing God had lifted up. **Whoever looked, lived.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No treatment, no ritual, no payment, no confession recorded. No quantity of looking is specified."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man too weak to stand could do it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they were bitten"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **spoke against God and against Moses**… \"There is no bread. There is no water. And **we loathe this miserable food**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The manna again. Forty years on and they still call it miserable — the Hebrew is *this worthless bread*. See the note on Numbers 21:5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Jesus takes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Just as Moses lifted up the serpent in the wilderness, even so must the Son of Man be lifted up**, that whoever believes in Him should not perish but have eternal life.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John 3:14, and then verse 16 follows straight out of it. He chose this incident to explain His own death, in a night conversation with Nicodemus. See the note on John 3:14–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Lifted up** is **hupsoo** — raised, and also exalted. John uses it three times of the crucifixion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why a serpent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul: *He made Him who knew no sin **to be sin for us**, that we might become the righteousness of God in Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thing on the pole is the image of what was killing them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What became of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They kept it. Seven hundred years later, Hezekiah *broke in pieces the bronze serpent that Moses had made, for until those days the children of Israel burned incense to it* — and he called it **Nehushtan**, a piece of bronze."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The right remedy became an idol once people looked at the object instead of what it pointed at."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the Israelites asked for first.** *Pray to the LORD that He take away the serpents from us.* They asked for removal. They got a pole, and a look, and their lives."}]}, "Numbers 23:18–20": {"title": "\"He has blessed, and I cannot reverse it\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The man being paid to curse Israel opens his mouth and this comes out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Balaam**, hired by Balak king of Moab to curse Israel as they camped on the plains opposite Jericho."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not an Israelite, not a prophet of the LORD by office, and is described elsewhere in Scripture as a man who loved the wages of unrighteousness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is standing on a hilltop with seven altars behind him, paid to say the opposite of this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Rise up, Balak**, and hear; listen to me, son of Zippor.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He makes the king stand for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**God is not a man, that He should lie**; He is not a son of man, **that He should change His mind**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *nor a son of man that He should repent*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel says nearly the same words to Saul: *the Strength of Israel will not lie nor relent, for He is not a man, that He should relent.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Does He speak, and then **not act**? Does He promise, and then **not fulfill**?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are rhetorical, and both are about the distance between saying and doing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The admission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have received **a command to bless**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not permission. A command."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He has blessed, and I cannot reverse it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *He has blessed, and I cannot turn it back*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man with the fee in his hand states plainly that the thing he was hired to do is outside his power."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How many times he tries"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four oracles, from three different hilltops, with seven altars and seven bulls and seven rams built each time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Balak keeps moving him — *come to another place from which you may see them; you shall see only the outer part of them* — as though a different angle would help."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every time, a blessing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Balak says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I called you to curse my enemies, and look, you have kept on blessing them these three times!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Balaam's answer: *\"If Balak were to give me his house full of silver and gold, **I could not go beyond the word of the LORD**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Israel was doing at the time"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing. They are in their tents at the bottom of the hill and do not know any of this is happening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole episode takes place over their heads."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the next oracle says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He sees no wrong in Jacob, no trouble laid up against Israel**.\"* See the note on Numbers 23:21–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How Balaam ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not well. Numbers 31 records his death, and the counsel he gave Balak afterward — to seduce Israel into idolatry at Peor, since cursing had not worked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The New Testament names it three times: the way of Balaam, the error of Balaam, the doctrine of Balaam."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He could not reverse the blessing, so he went after the people instead."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the sentence comes from.** Not from a prophet, a psalm, or a friend of Israel. From a hired man on a hill, standing in front of the king who paid him, saying he cannot take it back."}]}, "Numbers 23:21–24": {"title": "\"He sees no wrong in Jacob\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second half of the oracle, and the first line of it is the one to stop on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He sees no wrong in Jacob, no trouble laid up against Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *He has not beheld iniquity in Jacob, nor has He seen trouble in Israel*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When it was said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers 23. By this point in the book Israel has made a golden calf, refused to enter the land, grumbled about the manna, rebelled under Korah, complained at Meribah, and been bitten by serpents."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses has spent chapters recording all of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a man on a hill, looking down at their tents, says God sees no wrong in Jacob."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the view is from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Balaam is above them, looking down at a camp arranged in four squares around a tent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He cannot see individuals from there. He sees the arrangement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God is doing in the camp"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD their God is with them**, and **the shout of the King is among them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *shout* is **teruah** — the blast of the trumpet, the shout that goes up when a king arrives among his people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exodus"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**God brought them out of Egypt**; they have **the strength of a wild ox**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **reem** — the wild ox, an animal nobody could domesticate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Balaam repeats this line in the next oracle as well."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two negatives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**There is no spell against Jacob, no divination against Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew words are the technical terms of Balaam's own profession — enchantment and augury."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is standing there as a professional diviner, hired for exactly this, reporting that his trade does not work here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is said about them instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now it is said of Jacob and of Israel, '**See what God has done!**'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *what has God wrought* — the sentence Samuel Morse chose for the first telegraph message."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The report about Israel is a report about God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look — **a people rising like a lioness, lifting itself like a lion**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lion image belongs to Judah, from Jacob's blessing in Genesis 49: *Judah is a lion's whelp… he bows down, he lies down as a lion; who shall rouse him?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the fourth oracle says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I see Him, but not now; I behold Him, but not near. A Star shall come out of Jacob; a Scepter shall rise out of Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last thing the hired diviner says about Israel is about a star."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he could not curse them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oracle before gives the reason: *He has blessed, and I cannot reverse it.* See the note on Numbers 23:18–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not because the people were blameless. Because of what had been spoken over them and could not be taken back."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the sentence is not.** A statement that nothing happened in the camp. Numbers records it all. It is what God says He sees, spoken by a man who was paid to say otherwise."}]}, "Numbers 23:4–6": {"title": "A word put in his mouth", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A hired diviner builds seven altars and gets a sentence he cannot edit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Balaam, brought from the Euphrates by Balak king of Moab to curse Israel, who are camped on the plains below."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Balak had sent for him twice, raised the fee, and Balaam had come — after a donkey stopped in the road and spoke to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he has built"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have set up **seven altars**, and on each altar I have offered **a bull and a ram**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven of each, and he reports it like a man presenting an invoice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does the same thing twice more in the chapters that follow — new hilltop, seven altars, seven bulls, seven rams. Three times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The number is right, the animals are right, and nothing about it works the way he intends."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The meeting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God met Balaam.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyiqqar** — a word suggesting an encounter, and possibly a chance one. Some read it as *happened upon*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not a prophet of Israel. God meets him anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the LORD put a word in Balaam's mouth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyasem devar bephi Bil'am** — placed a word in his mouth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same construction used of a true prophet: *I will put My words in his mouth*, in Deuteronomy 18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here it is used of a man being paid to say the opposite."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Return to Balak, and this is what you will say**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The content is fixed before he opens his mouth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scene he goes back to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there he was, standing beside his burnt offering, **he and all the officials of Moab**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A king and his court standing round an altar waiting for a curse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"How shall I curse whom God has not cursed? And how shall I denounce whom the LORD has not denounced?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Balak's reaction: *\"What have you done to me? I took you to curse my enemies, and look, you have blessed them bountifully!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Must I not take heed to speak what the LORD has put in my mouth?\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The next three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three more attempts, three more hilltops, and each oracle is better than the last."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fourth is the finest writing in the book: *how lovely are your tents, O Jacob!* See the note on Numbers 24:3–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the middle of the third: *God is not a man, that He should lie, nor a son of man, that He should repent*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Balak sends him home unpaid. And then Numbers 25 happens — Israel joins itself to Baal of Peor, and twenty-four thousand die."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Later the text explains why: *they caused the children of Israel, **through the counsel of Balaam**, to trespass against the LORD*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He could not curse them, so he advised Balak how to get them to curse themselves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the New Testament makes of him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter: *they have forsaken the right way and gone astray, following the way of Balaam the son of Beor, who loved the wages of unrighteousness*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jude: *they have run greedily in the error of Balaam for profit*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation: *you have there those who hold the doctrine of Balaam*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three writers, three references, and every one is about money."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other man whose office produced a sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Caiaphas — *he did not say this on his own*. See the note on John 11:49–53."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the seven altars bought.** Nothing. The word was placed in his mouth before he walked back down the hill, and the king and his court were standing round the fire waiting for the opposite."}]}, "Numbers 24:1–2": {"title": "He stopped looking for omens", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The fourth attempt, and this time the diviner gives up on his own trade."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has happened three times already"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Balak has moved Balaam from hilltop to hilltop, built seven altars each time, offered a bull and a ram on each, and got a blessing every time. See the note on Numbers 23:18–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I called you to curse my enemies, and look, you have kept on blessing them these three times!\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What changed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now when Balaam **saw that it pleased the LORD to bless Israel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He works it out. Three rounds of evidence and he stops arguing with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he stopped doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **did not go off, as at other times, to look for omens**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nechashim** — enchantments, divination. It is the same root as the word for serpent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The previous chapters describe his method: he would withdraw to a bare height and wait for a sign, and *the LORD met Balaam*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here he abandons the professional apparatus."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **turned his face toward the wilderness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Toward the camp. He simply looks at what is in front of him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he saw"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And Balaam **lifted his eyes and saw Israel camped tribe by tribe**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *dwelling according to their tribes*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers 2 gives the arrangement: four divisions of three tribes each, camped on the four sides, with the tabernacle in the middle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "From a hilltop it would have read as a pattern."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What came"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the Spirit of God came upon him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ruach Elohim**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is said of Balaam — a hired diviner, on his fourth attempt to curse a nation, who has already been rebuked by his own donkey."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Scripture says the Spirit came on him, and the fourth oracle is the finest of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The word of the man **whose eye is opened**, the word of one who **hears the words of God, who sees the vision of the Almighty**, falling down, but **with his eyes uncovered**.\"* See the note on Numbers 24:3–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *vision* is **machazeh** — from **chazah**, to see."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Falling down, but with his eyes uncovered** — the standard description of a prophet under the hand of God, and Balaam applies it to himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The donkey"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters earlier the animal saw the angel three times and Balaam did not, and was beaten for stopping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the LORD **opened Balaam's eyes**, and he saw the Angel of the LORD standing in the way.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The professional seer is the last one in the story to see anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How lovely are your tents, Jacob**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The line that opens Jewish morning prayer to this day, spoken by a man on Balak's payroll."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends for Balaam"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Badly. When cursing failed, he advised Balak to seduce Israel into idolatry at Peor — and Numbers 31 records his death in battle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The New Testament names it three times: the way of Balaam, the error of Balaam, and the doctrine of Balaam."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he stopped doing.** Looking for omens. Three failed attempts with the full apparatus, and on the fourth he turns his face toward the camp and looks — and that is the verse the Spirit arrives in."}]}, "Numbers 24:3–9": {"title": "\"How lovely are your tents\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The fourth oracle, and it is the best writing in the book of Numbers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The word of Balaam son of Beor, the word of **the man whose eye is opened**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hired diviner, paid to curse, on his fourth failed attempt. See the note on Numbers 24:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three descriptions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The word of one who **hears the words of God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Who **sees the vision of the Almighty**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Falling down, but with his eyes uncovered**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *Almighty* is **Shaddai** — the patriarchs' name for God. See the note on Genesis 17:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How lovely are your tents, Jacob**, your dwellings, Israel!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mah tovu** — how good."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It opens the Jewish morning service and has done for centuries. The first words a worshipper says on entering the synagogue are a pagan's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is looking at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A refugee camp in a desert. Two million people in tents, forty years out of Egypt, with nothing but livestock and a portable tent in the middle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls it lovely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four images"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Like **valleys spreading out**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Like **gardens beside a river**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Like **aloes the LORD has planted**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Like **cedars beside the waters**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one is about water, and he is standing in a wilderness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Aloes the LORD has planted** — the Hebrew singles them out as God's own planting. The same idea as Jeremiah's transplanted tree. See the note on Jeremiah 17:7–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The water"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Water will flow from their buckets**, and their seed will have **abundant water**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said over people who had been complaining about thirst for four decades."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The king"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Their king will be higher than Agag**, and their kingdom will be lifted up.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel has no king. Saul is centuries away, and the Agag he fails to kill is the reason Samuel rejects him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wild ox"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"God brought them out of Egypt; they have **the strength of a wild ox**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **reem** — the untameable one. Balaam used the same image in the third oracle. See the note on Numbers 23:21–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Like a lion they crouch and lie down; like a lioness — **who dares to rouse them?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Almost word for word Jacob's blessing over Judah in Genesis 49: *he bows down, he lies down as a lion; who shall rouse him?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Blessed is everyone who blesses you, and cursed is everyone who curses you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise to Abraham, quoted back — *I will bless those who bless you, and I will curse him who curses you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Balak had hired him precisely to curse them, and the oracle ends by cursing whoever does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Balak's reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then Balak's anger was aroused against Balaam, and he **struck his hands together**… \"I called you to curse my enemies, and look, **you have kept on blessing them these three times!** Now therefore, **flee to your place**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He sends him home unpaid: *I said I would greatly honour you, but in fact the LORD has kept you back from honour.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Balaam says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before leaving, unpaid and dismissed, he delivers a fifth oracle anyway:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I see Him, but not now; I behold Him, but not near. A Star shall come out of Jacob; a Scepter shall rise out of Israel**.\"*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the words are still said.** *How lovely are your tents.* A man on a hill, employed to curse, describing a desert camp as gardens beside a river — and it is the sentence the synagogue opens with."}]}, "Numbers 26:52–56": {"title": "Measured out, and drawn by lot", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The land is about to be divided, and God gives two instructions that look as though they cancel each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two rules"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**by size** — *to a larger group give a larger inheritance, to a smaller group a smaller one, each receiving in proportion to those who were counted*", "**by lot** — *the land itself is to be assigned by lot… whether large or small, every inheritance is to be settled by the drawing of lots*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "How much you get is proportionate. **Where** you get it is drawn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why both"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The size rule makes the allocation fair. The lot takes the choosing out of human hands entirely — no tribe lobbying for the fertile valleys, no favouritism, no Moses deciding who gets the coast."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs says it directly: the lot is cast into the lap, but its every decision is from the LORD. The Illumination's line here says the same thing plainly — *God's own hand would set the boundaries.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this chapter sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the second census, taken at the end of forty years. The first generation is gone; these are the people who will actually cross. The counting is done specifically so the land can be divided among them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the whole exercise is forward-looking. They are being numbered for an inheritance, before a single boundary stone has been placed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes next.** The very next paragraph is the daughters of Zelophehad coming forward — because a distribution had just been announced that had no place for them in it. The chapters belong together: the inheritance is described, and then somebody who was left out of the arrangement asks. See the note on Numbers 27:1–4."}]}, "Numbers 27:1–4": {"title": "Five women who asked", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Five sisters walk to the front of the camp and ask for something the law had not provided for. It is one of the boldest scenes in the Old Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they stood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At the entrance to the tent of meeting, before Moses, before Eleazar the priest, before the leaders and the whole assembly.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a private word. They took it to the most public place in Israel and stood in front of every layer of authority at once."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they are named — Mahlah, Noah, Hoglah, Milcah, Tirzah. The chapter records all five, and it records them again three more times in the books that follow. Scripture does not lose their names."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How they argued"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Their case is careful, and they clear the ground before they ask:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["our father died in the wilderness", "**he was not among Korah's followers** — they head off the obvious objection before it is raised", "he died for his own sin, and he left no sons"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the question: *why should our father's name disappear from his clan simply because he had no son?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They do not complain about their treatment. They argue from their father's name and from what the inheritance was *for*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They asked, and they were not afraid to ask.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole scene. The land had not yet been distributed; the promise was still in front of everyone. They looked at what was about to be handed out, noticed they were not in the arrangement, and came forward anyway."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where boldness like this is aimed.** They were not asking for something outside the promise. They were asking for their share *of* it — the portion that belonged to their father's name. That is the shape of a good request: not inventing an entitlement, but coming forward for what the promise already contained."}]}, "Numbers 27:5–7": {"title": "\"The daughters of Zelophehad are right\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Moses does not rule on it. He takes it upstairs, and the answer that comes back is unambiguous."}, {"k": "h", "t": "God's verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The daughters of Zelophehad are right.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words, and they settle it. Not *let them have a portion as an exception*, and not *this is irregular but permitted*. **Right** — their case is correct."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the instruction: *you must surely give them property as an inheritance among their father's relatives. Turn their father's inheritance over to them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew doubles the verb for emphasis — *giving you shall give*. It is the same construction used in Genesis 2 for eating freely from every tree. There is nothing grudging in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it changed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is not merely a favourable outcome for one family. Read on: it becomes **statute**. *If a man dies and leaves no son, turn his inheritance over to his daughter* — permanent law in Israel, from that day, because five women asked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The request did not bend the rule. It made one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses did right"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He *brought their case before the LORD*. He did not decide it himself, and he did not dismiss it for being unprecedented."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Given what the same book records about Moses' patience running out elsewhere, this is worth noticing: presented with a claim no one had made before, his instinct was to take it to God rather than to defend the existing arrangement."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line the Illumination adds.** *What the overlooked had asked, God Himself upheld.* It is the translator summarising, and it is a fair reading of the scene. The people with no standing in the system got the ruling, and the ruling came from the top."}]}, "Numbers 30:10–15": {"title": "\"He bears the responsibility for her guilt\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A law about vows, and the last verse of it is the one to stop on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Vows and binding pledges — **neder** and **issar**. A vow was voluntary, and once made it was absolute: *when you make a vow to God, do not delay to pay it, for He has no pleasure in fools. **Pay what you have vowed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody had to make one. Everybody who made one was held to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The husband's hearing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If a woman living with her husband **makes a vow** or binds herself with a pledge, and her husband **hears of it and says nothing**, forbidding her nothing, then **all her vows and pledges stand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Silence confirms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cancellation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But if **he nullifies them on the day he hears**, then **nothing that came from her lips — vow or pledge — will hold**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he has made them void*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**On the day he hears.** The window is one day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who releases her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **her husband has canceled them, and the LORD will release her**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses. The husband cancels; the LORD forgives."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *release* is **salach** — the word used throughout Leviticus for God forgiving. It is used of God alone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has broken a vow made to God, and the text says God releases her because a man cancelled it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The silence rule, stated again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If he says nothing to her **from one day to the next, he confirms all her vows and pledges by his silence**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Doing nothing is a decision, and the law says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If he cancels them **some time after he has heard**, then **he bears the responsibility for her guilt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he shall bear her iniquity* — **nasa avon**, to lift and carry the guilt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the phrase used of the scapegoat: *the goat shall **bear on itself all their iniquities** to an uninhabited land.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And of the servant in Isaiah: *He shall **bear their iniquities**.* See the note on Isaiah 53:10–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law arranges"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A vow is made and cannot be unmade. A man hears it, cancels it late, and the obligation does not vanish — it transfers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She goes free and he carries it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers 30, on the plains of Moab, near the end of the wilderness years, alongside the laws about inheritance and the cities of refuge. See the note on Numbers 35:9–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word in Galatians"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ has redeemed us from the curse of the law, **having become a curse for us**.* See the note on Galatians 3:10–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **genomenos** — having become."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not paid on our behalf from outside it. Became it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The mechanism in the law.** The obligation is real, the breaking of it is real, and the guilt does not evaporate. Numbers 30 says where it goes."}]}, "Numbers 32:1–5": {"title": "\"Do not make us cross the Jordan\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two tribes look at good grazing land on the wrong side of the river and ask to stop there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the request is framed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every part of it sounds reasonable:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["they genuinely have enormous herds", "Jazer and Gilead genuinely are good cattle country", "they ask politely — *if we have found favour with you*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the sentence is a rebellion. It is a sensible business decision made by men who could see what was in front of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one line that gives it away"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not make us cross the Jordan.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Jordan is the whole point. Everything from Egypt onward has been aimed at crossing it — the promise to Abraham, the plagues, the sea, the forty years. The land on the other side is the land God swore to give them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not asking for less than they were promised because they were refused more. They are asking to be excused from receiving it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What made it look wise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The herds were real, and the grass was real. The decision was driven entirely by what suited the livestock — good land, immediately, without a river crossing or a war."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what makes the chapter worth reading slowly. Nothing here is obviously sinful. It is a preference for the visible good over the promised one."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Moses' first reaction.** He hears it as the same failure as Kadesh — *are your brothers to go to war while you sit here?* — and reminds them what happened when ten spies discouraged the people forty years earlier. He treats it as serious immediately, and the rest of the chapter is the negotiation. See the note on Numbers 32:20–27."}]}, "Numbers 32:20–27": {"title": "\"Your sin will find you out\" — what it actually refers to", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The famous line is quoted as a general warning about secret sin. In its own chapter it means something much more specific."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses agrees to the request, with terms:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["arm yourselves and cross the Jordan with everyone else", "stay until the LORD has driven out His enemies and the land is subdued", "**then** come back, free of obligation, and this land is yours"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The settlement on the east bank is allowed. What is not allowed is settling there while the rest of the nation fights."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the warning is about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But if you fail to do this, you will be sinning against the LORD, and know for certain your sin will find you out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sin named is **not going**. Leaving your brothers to take the land while you graze cattle in safety. That is the thing that would find them out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a promise-keeping verse, not a hidden-sin verse. Moses is telling men who have just made a commitment that failing to keep it will catch up with them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every one of us who can bear arms will cross over to fight before the LORD, as you have said.* Wives, children, flocks and herds stay; the fighting men go."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they kept it. Joshua 22 records Joshua sending them home years later with a blessing, having done exactly what they said — *you have not deserted your brothers over this long time.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What it cost them anyway.** Their land, being east of the river, was the first territory Israel lost. Reuben and Gad are the tribes carried off earliest in the Assyrian invasions. They kept the promise and they still ended up living on the exposed side of the border, which is the quiet ending the chapter never spells out."}]}, "Numbers 32:6–15": {"title": "\"Are your brothers to go to war while you sit here?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two tribes ask to settle on the wrong side of the river, and Moses hears it as something he has heard before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Reuben and Gad had *a very great multitude of livestock*, and the land east of the Jordan was good grazing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If we have found favour in your sight, let this land be given to your servants as a possession. **Do not take us over the Jordan.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a reasonable request about pasture, and Moses does not hear it that way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Are your brothers to go to war while you sit here?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the charge. Not that the land is bad, or that they are wrong about the grazing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nine and a half tribes are about to cross a river and fight for a country, and two of them have found somewhere comfortable short of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fear"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Why would you discourage the people** from crossing into the land the LORD has given?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to break the heart, to turn the will aside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His concern is not the two tribes. It is what the sight of them staying will do to everybody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The precedent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This is just what your fathers did** when I sent them from Kadesh-barnea to see the land.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses is standing in front of the children of the men who refused, thirty-eight years later, telling them they are doing it again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They lost heart and **turned the whole community away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten men came back with a report and a nation stayed in the desert. See the note on Numbers 13:26–33."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cost, recited"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"That whole generation wandered forty years until it passed away, **except Caleb and Joshua, who followed the LORD wholeheartedly**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two men out of a nation, and the phrase used of them is *filled after the LORD* — followed fully."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The accusation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And now here you are, doing the same, **kindling His anger against Israel again**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Moses fears will happen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If you turn away from following Him, **He will leave this people in the wilderness, and you will be the ruin of them all**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not warning them about their own loss. He tells them they will be the reason everybody else stays out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They propose a solution, and it is a good one: *we will build sheepfolds here for our livestock and cities for our little ones, **but we ourselves will be armed, ready to go before the children of Israel** until we have brought them to their place.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They will settle their families and cross over to fight anyway, and not come home until every other tribe has its inheritance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses accepts it. See the note on Numbers 32:20–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened to them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They kept it. Joshua 22 records him sending them home years later: *you have not left your brethren these many days, up to this day, but have kept the charge.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they were the first tribes carried away when Assyria came."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is being negotiated.** Not whether they can have the land. Whether they will go over with everybody else first. Moses' whole objection is about the order of things, and the two tribes end up agreeing with him."}]}, "Numbers 35:16–21": {"title": "When blood is shed", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The refuge is real, and so is the line drawn around it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But **justice must still be clear**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cities of refuge are not a loophole. The passage that establishes them is immediately followed by the test for who does not qualify."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three materials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If someone strikes another down with **something deadly — iron, stone, or wood used to kill** — and the person dies, **that is murder**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law works by instrument. Iron, stone or wood — the three things a weapon could be made of in that world."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Picking one up is treated as evidence of intent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three actions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"If **out of hatred** someone **shoves** another, or **hurls something**, or **strikes with the hand**, and the person dies, **that too is murder**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here the test moves from the object to the motive: **out of hatred**, **be'ivah**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No weapon is needed. A shove is enough if the hatred was there first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the two lists do together"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between them they cover both ways a death can be deliberate — by what was in the hand, and by what was in the heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything else falls into the other category, and the six cities are for that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The avenger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The avenger of blood may hold the murderer responsible when they meet**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **goel haddam**. The law does not abolish him; it regulates him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is permitted to act on a murderer, and forbidden to touch a man inside a refuge until the trial."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the trial decided"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The congregation heard the case and put the man into one category or the other. If manslaughter, he returned to the city and stayed. If murder, *the avenger of blood himself shall put the murderer to death.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two witnesses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter adds a rule: *whoever kills a person, the murderer shall be put to death **on the testimony of witnesses**; but **one witness is not sufficient** testimony against a person to cause death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No conviction on one voice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "No ransom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Moreover you shall **take no ransom for the life of a murderer** who is guilty of death, but he shall surely be put to death.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Money could settle almost anything else in that legal system. Not this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **blood defiles the land**, and no atonement can be made for the land, for the blood that is shed on it, except by the blood of him who shed it.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The land itself is treated as a party to the case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is on the other side of the line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who did it by accident, and everyone who ran. See the note on Numbers 35:9–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus said from the cross"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Father, forgive them; they do not know what they are doing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The exact category the cities were built for — the man who caused a death without knowing. See the note on Luke 23:32–38."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the second list exists.** So that the refuge means something. A shelter with no boundary is not a shelter; the chapter draws the line first and then leaves the door open on the other side of it."}]}, "Numbers 35:9–15": {"title": "Cities of refuge", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Six towns are set aside before the land is even entered, for people who have not yet done anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**When you cross into Canaan**, choose cities of refuge.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel is still on the plains of Moab. The land has not been taken, the tribes have not been allotted, and the provision is already made."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody is going to need this, and God legislates for them in advance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they are for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So that **a person who kills someone by accident, without meaning to**, may flee there.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *who kills any person unintentionally* — **bishgagah**, in error, by mistake."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy gives the example: two men chopping wood, the axe head flies off the handle, and the neighbour dies."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"These will be **a shelter from the avenger**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **goel haddam** — the avenger of blood, the nearest kinsman, whose duty it was to pursue."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word **goel** is the kinsman-redeemer: Boaz in Ruth, and the *my Redeemer lives* of Job."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The nearest relative both redeems and avenges, depending on which side of the death you are on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the refuge does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So that the one who caused a death **is not struck down before he can stand trial before the community**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not an acquittal. It buys a trial."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The runner is protected long enough to be heard, and the congregation decides which category he is in. See the note on Numbers 35:16–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number and the layout"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Set apart **six such cities — three on the east side of the Jordan and three in Canaan**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua names them: Kedesh, Shechem and Hebron in the west; Bezer, Ramoth and Golan in the east."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are spread deliberately, so that no part of the country is far from one. All six were Levitical cities."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Later Jewish tradition held that the roads to them were kept in repair and signposted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who could use them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They will be a refuge for **the Israelite, for the foreigner, and for anyone living among you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three categories, and two of them are not Israelites."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law is unusual in extending identically to the resident alien."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the runner had to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Get there, and stay. The city held him until the death of the high priest, and then he went home free."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One man's death released everybody in all six cities at once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the priests come in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cities of refuge were Levite towns, so a man fleeing a death ran into a place full of priests and lived off the priests' portion. See the note on Numbers 18:8–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The provision, described"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Here is God's tenderness, **built right into the map**: a door left open, a place of safety for the frightened and the innocent.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the language turns up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We, **who ran for shelter** and grabbed hold of the hope held out in front of us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek in Hebrews 6 is the vocabulary of fleeing for refuge. See the note on Hebrews 6:18."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the law provides for.** Not the guilty and not the safe. The man who did it, did not mean to, and is being chased — and the arrangement was made before anyone crossed the river."}]}, "Numbers 6:22–27": {"title": "The blessing with the name in it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Fifteen words in Hebrew, and the oldest text of Scripture ever found."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Tell **Aaron and his sons**: this is how you will bless the people of Israel.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priests, and the wording is given to them. They do not compose it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Say to them* — the Hebrew is *thus you shall bless*. The formula is fixed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shape"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three lines, and in Hebrew they get longer: three words, five words, seven words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each line names **YHWH** and each has two verbs. The structure is built to be heard."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD **bless you and keep you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Barak** — bless. And **shamar** — keep, guard, watch over. The verb for a shepherd with a flock and a sentry on a wall."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something given, and then protected."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD **make His face shine upon you and be gracious to you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ya'er panav** — cause His face to light up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The idiom is about a face changing when somebody walks in. The nearest English is *his face lit up*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **chanan** — to show favour, to be gracious. The root of **chen**, grace, and of the names Hannah and John."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD **turn His face toward you and give you peace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yissa panav** — lift up His face toward you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The opposite of the hiding: *You have hidden Your face from us*. See the note on Isaiah 64:6–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **shalom** — the last word of the blessing, and the widest one in the language."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So they will **place My name over the Israelites**, and **I Myself will bless them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priests say the words. The blessing is God's own act, and He says so."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**My name** — the blessing has YHWH in it three times, and the sentence afterwards explains that this is the point. The name is being laid on the people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it was found"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two small silver scrolls, rolled up as amulets, excavated at Ketef Hinnom just outside Jerusalem in 1979."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They carry these words, and they date to around the seventh century BC — which makes them the oldest fragments of biblical text in existence, older than the Dead Sea Scrolls by four centuries."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody was wearing this blessing round their neck before the exile."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The face"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three lines, and two of them are about a face."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Shining, and lifted toward you. The whole blessing is about attention."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the face goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it is the God who commanded light to shine out of darkness who has shone in our hearts to give the light of the knowledge of the glory of God **in the face of Jesus Christ**.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 4:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Moses, coming down the mountain with a face he could not see and had to be told about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is still said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the end of synagogue services and at the end of church services, in both traditions, continuously, for three thousand years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul's version is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The grace of the Lord Jesus Christ, and the love of God, and the communion of the Holy Spirit be with you all.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three clauses again, and it closes 2 Corinthians."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the priests were doing.** Not wishing. God's own explanation is in the last sentence: they put His name on the people, and He does the blessing."}]}, "Numbers 8:23–26": {"title": "At fifty they stop carrying", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A retirement policy, in the middle of the wilderness legislation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The start"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"From **twenty-five years old and up**, they come to serve in the work of the Tent of Meeting.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 4 gives thirty as the age for the heaviest duties — carrying the tabernacle. This gives twenty-five for entry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The usual reading is five years of apprenticeship before the full load."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"At **fifty they retire from the heavy labor and serve no more**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he shall return from the warfare of the service* — **tsava**, the word for an army or a campaign."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Levites' work is described as military service, and fifty is discharge."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the work was heavy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tabernacle moved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Kohathites carried the ark, the table, the lampstand and the altars on their shoulders. The Gershonites carried the curtains and coverings. The Merarites carried the boards, bars, pillars and sockets — the structural timber and metal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every time the cloud lifted, several tons of building came apart, went onto shoulders and carts, and went back up somewhere else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a young man's job, and the law says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But they may still **assist their brothers in keeping watch at the Tent**, even if they no longer do the work themselves.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **sherat** — to minister, to attend."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not dismissed. Reassigned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heavy lifting goes; the watching stays. And the older men are the ones who have done it longest and know how it is meant to be done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any suggestion that fifty is old, or that a man at fifty is finished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a job description with a physical component, and the component has a limit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What changes later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Once the temple is built and the building stops moving, David lowers the entry age:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For David said, \"The LORD God of Israel has given rest to His people, that they may dwell in Jerusalem forever\"; and also to the Levites, **\"they shall no longer carry the tabernacle, or any of the articles for its service.\"***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the Levites were numbered from the age of twenty years and above.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The carrying rule existed because there was carrying. When that stopped, the age changed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their duty was to help the sons of Aaron in the service of the house of the LORD, in the courts and in the chambers, in the purifying of all holy things and the work of the service of the house of God… and **to stand every morning to thank and praise the LORD, and likewise at evening**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heavy labour becomes singing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The men who were exceptions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Caleb, at eighty-five: *as yet I am as strong this day as on the day that Moses sent me; just as my strength was then, so now is my strength for war*. See the note on Joshua 14:6–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses, at a hundred and twenty: *his eyes were not dim nor his natural vigour diminished*. See the note on Deuteronomy 34:5–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are recorded because they are remarkable, which is the point of recording them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the age turns up in the New Testament"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You are not yet fifty years old, and have You seen Abraham?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to Jesus by opponents. Fifty is used as the outer bound of a working man's life."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the rule actually retires.** The lifting. The Hebrew calls it a discharge from warfare — and the same men stay on to keep watch, which is the part experience is for."}]}, "Obadiah 1:17–18": {"title": "Jacob takes back its inheritance", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The shortest book in the Old Testament, and this is its turn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-one verses, one subject: Edom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Edom is Esau's line. The quarrel is between two brothers, and it has been running for a thousand years by the time Obadiah writes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Edom did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **violence against your brother Jacob**, shame shall cover you.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In the day that you stood on the other side… **even you were as one of them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The charge, laid out across eight verses: they stood by and watched."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You should not have gazed on the day of your brother… **nor stood at the crossroads to cut off those among them who escaped**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jerusalem fell, and the relatives on the ridge across the valley watched, and then caught the refugees coming out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they lived"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Petra, and the mountain strongholds of Seir. Rock fortresses, approached through a slot canyon."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The pride of your heart has deceived you, **you who dwell in the clefts of the rock**… who say in your heart, '**Who will bring me down to the ground?**'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer: *though you ascend as high as the eagle, and though you set your nest among the stars, from there I will bring you down*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **on Mount Zion there will be deliverance**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **peletah** — an escape, those who escape, a remnant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The refugees who were cut off at the crossroads, and now the word is used of a survival on the mountain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The holiness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **it will be holy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *and it shall be holiness*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inheritance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the house of Jacob will take back its inheritance**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yireshu** — shall possess, take possession of, dispossess."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb used all through Joshua of taking the land, and in Deuteronomy: *go in and possess the land which the LORD swore to your fathers*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three houses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **house of Jacob** will be a fire, and the **house of Joseph** a flame, and the **house of Esau** stubble.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two burning and one burnable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Jacob** here is Judah, the southern kingdom. **Joseph** is the northern tribes. Obadiah puts them together, which is a small reunification in one line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will **set it ablaze and consume it**, and **no one from the house of Esau will survive**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *there shall be no survivor of the house of Esau*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word as **peletah** in verse 17, negated. Zion has an escape; Edom has none."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The signature"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD has spoken it.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Edom disappears. Nabateans occupied the territory, the Edomites were pushed west into the Negev, became the Idumeans, and were absorbed after the first century."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Herod the Great was an Idumean, which puts an Edomite on the throne at the birth of the Messiah — and he orders the children of Bethlehem killed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line of the book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **saviours shall come to Mount Zion** to judge the mountains of Esau, and **the kingdom shall be the LORD's**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-one verses about a family feud, ending on the kingdom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the quarrel started"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the LORD said to her: \"Two nations are in your womb, two peoples shall be separated from your body.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a bowl of stew, and a stolen blessing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews names Esau as the warning: *lest there be any fornicator or profane person like Esau, who for one morsel of food sold his birthright*. See the note on Hebrews 12:12–17."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two words do.** *Peletah* — escape. Zion has one, in verse 17. Edom has none, in verse 18 — and the men of Edom had spent the fall of Jerusalem catching people who were escaping."}]}, "Philippians 1:21–24": {"title": "A man weighing up his own death, in a letter about joy", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is in custody, the outcome is genuinely uncertain, and he does something unusual: he thinks out loud about which he would prefer, and admits he cannot decide."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words are both nautical"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I long to cast off and be with Christ* — the verb is **analuo**, used of loosing a ship from its moorings and of striking camp to move on. Not ending. Departing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Staying on in the body* — **epimeno**, to remain at a place, to stay put."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the choice is not between existing and not existing. It is between casting off and staying in port a while longer, which tells you what Paul thought death was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He genuinely cannot pick"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which of the two I would choose, I honestly cannot say. I am caught between them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image behind *caught between* is of pressure from both sides at once, like a man wedged. And the two pressures are not fear and hope — they are two goods:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["being with Christ, which he calls **better by far**", "being useful to people who need him, which he calls **more needed**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here is resigned, and nothing is morbid. He is weighing a gain against a gain."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"For me, life means Christ\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The famous line is doing structural work. If life means Christ, then living is not a stretch of time to be got through, and if dying means gain, then death is not a loss to be feared. Both halves are needed for the paragraph to make sense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is also why the paragraph does not read as depression. A man for whom living has no content might long to depart; Paul says living is full and departing is better, and that is why he cannot choose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How he settles it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He settles it on somebody else's account: *for your sake, staying on in the body is what is needed more.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **being confident of this, I know that I shall remain and continue with you all** for your progress and joy of faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *I know* is **oida** — settled knowledge, not a hope. He states the outcome of a capital case as a fact, and the reason he gives is their progress."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it happened. He was released, travelled again, and wrote to Timothy years later from a second imprisonment — where the language changes: *the time of my departure is at hand.* See the note on 2 Timothy 4:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same nautical word, **analuo**, in both letters. Here he weighs it and stays. There he says it has come."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What decided it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the tribunal, and not the charge. He weighs two goods, and lands on the one that serves the people he loves."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where this sits in the letter.** Philippians is the joy letter — the word runs through all four chapters — and it was written from prison by a man openly discussing his possible execution. The two facts belong together. It is not joy despite the situation being described; it is joy from a source the situation cannot reach."}]}, "Philippians 2:12–13": {"title": "\"For it is God who is at work in you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses that are almost always quoted apart, and the second is the reason the first is possible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Work out\" is not \"work for\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Work out your own salvation with fear and trembling.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb means to bring to completion, to work something through to its end — what you do with something you already have. It was used of working a mine: extracting what is in there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not telling them to earn it. The letter has already called them saints and said God began a good work in them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word \"for\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it is God who is at work in you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything turns on that connective. The reason to work it out is that Someone else is working in you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the verb is **energeo** — the word behind our *energy*. God is the one supplying the power; the working out is what that power produces on the outside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Both halves of the willing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Both the willing and the doing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not merely the strength to do what you already wanted. The wanting itself is described as His work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence that takes the pressure out of the first verse. If even the desire is being supplied, then *fear and trembling* is not anxiety about performance — it is the seriousness appropriate to God working inside a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Paul says it here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look at what precedes it: the hymn about Christ emptying Himself, taking the form of a servant, obedient to death on a cross, and being exalted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And so, my beloved friends* — the instruction flows out of that. The working in is by the One who already did the emptying."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The purpose clause.** *For the sake of what pleases Him.* The whole operation, willing and doing alike, is aimed at His good pleasure — which is where Paul located the reason for everything back in Ephesians 1. See that note."}]}, "Philippians 2:9–11": {"title": "Which is why", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second half of the oldest Christian hymn we have, and it turns on the first two words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven steps down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who, being in the form of God, did not consider it robbery to be equal with God, but **made Himself of no reputation**, taking the form of a bondservant, and coming in the likeness of men.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And being found in appearance as a man, **He humbled Himself and became obedient to the point of death, even the death of the cross**.* See the note on Philippians 2:5–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *made Himself of no reputation* is **ekenosen** — emptied."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hinge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which is why.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dio kai** — therefore also."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The exaltation is a consequence. The passage does not present it as a return to a status quo; it presents it as an answer to the descent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lifting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God has **lifted Him to the highest place**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **huperupsosen** — super-exalted. A compound, and it occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every verb in the first half has Him as the subject: He emptied, He took, He humbled. Every verb in the second half has God as the subject."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does the going down. Somebody else does the lifting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And given Him **a name that stands above every name**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **to onoma to huper pan onoma**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which name is not stated in verse 9. It arrives in verse 11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bowing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that at the name of Jesus **every knee should bow — in heaven, on earth, and under the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three realms, and the list is exhaustive by design."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The line is Isaiah's, and in Isaiah it is God speaking of Himself:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have sworn by Myself; the word has gone out of My mouth in righteousness, and shall not return, **that to Me every knee shall bow, every tongue shall take an oath**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul applies it to Christ without argument, and quotes it of God in Romans 14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The confession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **every tongue should openly say that Jesus Christ is Lord**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kurios Iesous Christos**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kurios** is the Greek Old Testament's standard translation of **YHWH**. That is the name above every name — and it is the confession Paul names as the mark of belief: *if you confess with your mouth the Lord Jesus… you will be saved*. See the note on Romans 10:9–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was also the Roman loyalty formula. *Kaisar kurios* — Caesar is Lord."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The destination"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To the glory of God the Father.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hymn does not end on the Son. Every knee bowing and every tongue confessing is described as glory going somewhere else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Paul quotes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to teach christology. To settle an argument between two women."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I implore Euodia and I implore Syntyche to be of the same mind in the Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage is introduced by *let this mind be in you which was also in Christ Jesus*, and before that: *let nothing be done through selfish ambition or conceit, but in lowliness of mind let each esteem others better than himself*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most exalted passage about Christ in Paul is deployed as an argument for not pushing to the front."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He is now"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seated at the right hand of the Majesty on high.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *far above all principality and power and might and dominion, and every name that is named*. See the note on Ephesians 1:20–23."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two halves.** He empties, takes, humbles. God lifts and gives. Every verb in the descent has Him as its subject, and not one in the ascent does."}]}, "Philippians 3:1": {"title": "Solid ground to stand on", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A transition verse, and it explains why the letter repeats itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The signal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And now, my brothers, **as for the rest of it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **to loipon** — finally, for the rest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which usually means a letter is ending. This one runs two more chapters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does the same thing in other letters; the word marks a turn rather than a conclusion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be glad in the Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **chairete en Kurio** — rejoice in the Lord."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How often he says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rejoice in the Lord always. Again I will say, rejoice!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sixteen occurrences of joy or rejoicing in four chapters, in a letter written from custody with an unheard case."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is also the ordinary Greek greeting, which means Paul is punning on *hello* every time."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The admission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Writing **the same things over again is no weariness to me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ta auta graphein humin emoi men ouk okneron** — to write the same things to you is not irksome to me."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Okneros** — hesitant, shrinking, reluctant. The word used of the lazy servant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He knows he is repeating himself and says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And for you it is **solid ground to stand on**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **humin de asphales** — for you it is safe, secure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Asphales** — from **a-sphallo**, not slipping. Firm footing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same root Luke uses at the start of his Gospel: *that you may know the certainty of those things in which you were instructed*. See the note on Luke 1:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What repetition is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not information. Footing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's argument is that a thing said once is heard; a thing said again is stood on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Beware of dogs, **beware of evil workers, beware of the mutilation!***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three warnings in one verse, and the tone changes completely."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Katatome** — the cutting, the mutilation. A savage pun on **peritome**, circumcision."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If anyone else thinks he may have confidence in the flesh, **I more so**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Circumcised the eighth day, of the stock of Israel, of the tribe of Benjamin, a Hebrew of the Hebrews, a Pharisee, blameless concerning the law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But what things were gain to me, these **I have counted loss for Christ**.* See the note on Philippians 3:7–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Skubala** — refuse, dung."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That I may know Him and the power of His resurrection… **forgetting those things which are behind and reaching forward** to those things which are ahead.* See the note on Philippians 3:12–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And where it lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **our citizenship is in heaven**, from which we also eagerly wait for the Saviour.* See the note on Philippians 3:20–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter comes back to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rejoice in the Lord always. Again I will say, rejoice!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not be anxious about anything… **and the peace of God will stand guard over your hearts**.* See the note on Philippians 4:6–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The repetition Paul defended in 3:1 happens in 4:4, one chapter later, in the same words."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The reason for repeating.** Not that they have forgotten — *for you it is solid ground to stand on*. **Asphales**, the state of not slipping."}]}, "Philippians 3:10–11": {"title": "That I may know Him", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul, thirty years in, states what he is still after."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not having my own righteousness, which is from the law, but that which is through faith in Christ, **the righteousness which is from God by faith**.* See the note on Philippians 3:9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse under discussion is the continuation of that sentence. There is no full stop between them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The aim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That **I may know Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tou gnonai auton** — an aorist infinitive. To come to know."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ginosko**, the knowing that comes by acquaintance, not the knowing of facts about."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written by the man who had been knocked off a horse by Him on a road, planted churches across three provinces, and written most of the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four things, in an order that is not the obvious one:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **power of His rising**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **sharing of His sufferings**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Being **shaped to the very likeness of His death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Resurrection first, then suffering, then death. Backwards from the way it happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The power"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dunamin tes anastaseos** — the power of His resurrection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has already prayed the same thing for the Ephesians: *the exceeding greatness of His power toward us who believe, according to the working of His mighty power which He worked in Christ when He raised Him from the dead*. See the note on Ephesians 1:20–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The measure of the power is the empty tomb."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sharing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **koinonian pathematon autou** — fellowship of His sufferings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Koinonia** is the word for partnership, having in common. Paul uses it of the collection, of the Lord's table, and of the church at Philippi's support of him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here it is of suffering, and he lists it as something to be gained."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conforming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **summorphizomenos** — being made of the same form, present passive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Morphe** is the inner form, the essential shape — the same root as *He being in the form of God*, two chapters earlier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not imitation. Being made the same shape, by somebody else, continuously."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If somehow, by whatever way, **I may arrive at the resurrection from among the dead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ei pos katanteso** — if somehow I may attain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It reads like doubt and is not. Paul has already said *our citizenship is in heaven, from which we also eagerly wait for the Saviour*. See the note on Philippians 3:20–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The uncertainty is about the route, not the destination — the *how*, not the *whether*. He did not know at the time of writing whether he would be executed or released."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not that I have already attained, or am already perfected; but I **press on**, that I may lay hold of that for which Christ Jesus has also laid hold of me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dioko** — pursue, chase, and it is the word used of persecuting. The verb Paul used to describe his own former career."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Forgetting those things which are behind and **reaching forward** to those things which are ahead, **I press toward the goal for the prize** of the upward call of God in Christ Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epekteinomenos** — stretching out toward, the runner's body leaning past the feet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had just discarded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Circumcised the eighth day, of the tribe of Benjamin, a Hebrew of the Hebrews, a Pharisee, blameless concerning the law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But what things were gain to me, these I have counted loss for Christ… I count them as rubbish.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **skubala** — refuse, what is thrown out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order in the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Righteousness received in verse 9. Knowing Him pursued in verse 10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pursuit comes after the possession, not before it, and the whole sentence is one grammatical unit."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of the four.** Him, the resurrection power, the fellowship of sufferings, and then being shaped to His death. Paul lists them in reverse of the way they happened."}]}, "Philippians 3:17–19": {"title": "\"With tears running down\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks them to copy him, and then describes the people he is warning them about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Brothers, all of you together, **follow the way I have walked**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **summimetai** — joint-imitators. Paul appears to have coined it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says the same elsewhere: *imitate me, just as I also imitate Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It sounds immodest until you read what he has just written about himself — everything he had counted as gain, counted as loss, and *not that I have already attained*. See the note on Philippians 3:2–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **keep your eye on those who walk by the pattern you have seen in us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *pattern* is **tupos** — a stamp, a mould, the impression a die leaves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not only him. Anyone walking the same way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For there are **many who walk otherwise**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The frequency"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Of whom **I have told you often**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had said it before, in person, more than once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The manner"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And now tell you **with tears running down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *weeping*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul cries in three places in his letters and speeches: over these people, over the Ephesian elders, and in the letter to Corinth *out of much affliction and anguish of heart I wrote to you, with many tears*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is describing opponents and weeping while he does it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name he gives them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They are **enemies of the cross of Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not enemies of God, or of the church, or of him. Of the cross."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four descriptions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The end of that road is **ruin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apoleia** — destruction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their **god is their appetite**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **koilia** — the belly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their **glory is in what should shame them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their **whole mind is fixed on the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *who mind earthly things* — **ta epigeia phronountes**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not identify them. The letter has already warned about *the dogs… the knife-cutters* in the same chapter, which points at the circumcision party."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The four descriptions here read more like indulgence than legalism, and readers have divided over whether these are the same people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The very next sentence: *for **our citizenship is registered in heaven**. And it is from there that we are watching eagerly for the Saviour.* See the note on Philippians 3:20–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their whole mind is fixed on the earth* against *our citizenship is in heaven*. The two verses are built as a pair."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the paragraph ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who will **change these lowly bodies** of ours so that they are **formed like His own glorious body**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The belly-god and the transformed body, in consecutive verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, my beloved and longed-for brethren, **my joy and crown**, so stand fast in the Lord, beloved.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls them his crown in the sentence after describing enemies in tears."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the warning is delivered.** Weeping. Paul gives four descriptions, each harder than the last, and tells the readers he is crying while he writes them."}]}, "Philippians 3:2–3": {"title": "Watch for the three", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three warnings in a row, and then a definition that takes the other side's word away from them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The break in the letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Philippians is the warmest thing Paul wrote — *I thank my God upon every remembrance of you*, and *rejoice in the Lord always*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 3 opens *finally, my brethren, rejoice in the Lord*, and then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep your eyes open for **the dogs**. Keep your eyes open for **the workers who do harm**. Keep your eyes open for **the knife-cutters**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek repeats **blepete** three times — look, watch, beware."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dogs.** In that world, not pets. Scavengers in the street, and a standard Jewish term of contempt for Gentiles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul turns it round and applies it to the party insisting on Jewish observance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Workers who do harm** — the Greek is **kakous ergatas**, evil workers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They would have called themselves workers. Paul keeps the noun and changes the adjective."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Knife-cutters** — the Greek is **katatome**, a cutting-down, a mutilation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a pun. The word for circumcision is **peritome**, a cutting-around. Paul coins a near-identical word meaning butchery."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus forbids cutting the flesh: *you shall not make any cuttings in your flesh.* Paul puts the practice in that category."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The definition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **we are the circumcision**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes the term itself. The people insisting on the operation are the mutilation; the people not having it are the thing it was meant to signify."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses had already located it: *circumcise the foreskin of your heart, and be stiff-necked no longer.* And Jeremiah, and Paul in Romans: *circumcision is that of the heart, in the Spirit.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three marks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We **who serve God by His Spirit**, who **make our boast in Christ Jesus**, and who **put no trust at all in what human flesh can offer**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three clauses answering the three warnings — a positive for each negative."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He lists what he would have had to boast about if the third clause were not true:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Circumcised the eighth day, of the stock of Israel, of the tribe of Benjamin, a Hebrew of the Hebrews; concerning the law, a Pharisee; concerning zeal, persecuting the church; concerning the righteousness which is in the law, blameless.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven items — four by birth and three by achievement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict on them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **what things were gain to me, these I have counted loss for Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *I also count all things loss for the excellence of the knowledge of Christ Jesus my Lord… and count them as **rubbish**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **skubala** — refuse, dung. It is a coarse word and Paul chose it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he wants instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And be found in Him, **not having my own righteousness, which is from the law, but that which is through faith in Christ** — the righteousness which is from God by faith.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our citizenship is registered in heaven.* See the note on Philippians 3:20–21."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul does with the word.** Takes it. The party demanding circumcision is renamed the cutting — and the people refusing it are called the circumcision, defined by three things none of which involve a knife."}]}, "Philippians 3:20–21": {"title": "\"Our citizenship is registered in heaven\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses a piece of civic vocabulary that Philippi in particular would have felt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our **citizenship** is registered in heaven.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **politeuma** — a colony, a commonwealth, the body of citizens whose register you are on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Philippi was a Roman colony. Its people held Roman citizenship though most of them had never seen Rome, lived under Roman law, and were governed as though the city were a piece of Italy transplanted into Macedonia."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is writing to people who already knew what it meant to be enrolled somewhere you do not live."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows from it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it is **from there** that we are **watching eagerly** for the Saviour.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apekdechomai** — to await with the head craned forward."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A colony's business was to look toward the capital. Paul does the same with the direction of expectation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The change"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who will **change these lowly bodies** of ours so that they are **formed like His own glorious body**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *change* is **metaschematizo** — to alter the outward form; and *formed like* is **summorphos**, sharing the same inner form."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both words, in one sentence, about the same event."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Lowly** is *the body of our humiliation* — Paul does not flatter it. He is writing from prison, in a body that had been beaten five times with thirty-nine lashes and three times with rods."}, {"k": "h", "t": "By what power"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By the working of that power of His **which can bring all things under Himself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same energy that will subdue everything is what is applied to a body."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul had just said about himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before: *not that I have already attained, or am already perfected; but I press on… **forgetting those things which are behind**, and reaching forward to those which are ahead.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *brethren, join in following my example.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had also just listed what he had thrown away — circumcised the eighth day, of the tribe of Benjamin, a Pharisee, blameless concerning the righteousness in the law — and called all of it loss."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast in the paragraph"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately before this: *many walk… **whose god is their belly**, and whose glory is in their shame — **who set their mind on earthly things**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two citizenships, two horizons. See the note on Philippians 3:17–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter is like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written from a Roman cell, with a capital case pending, to a church that had been sending him money since a riverside prayer meeting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rejoice in the Lord always. Again I will say, rejoice.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The next sentence.** *Therefore, my beloved and longed-for brethren, my joy and crown, **so stand fast in the Lord**.* The citizenship is stated, and the instruction attached to it is to stand still."}]}, "Philippians 3:9": {"title": "Which righteousness a man is wearing", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just listed his own credentials — circumcised the eighth day, tribe of Benjamin, a Pharisee, blameless as to the law — and called the whole file rubbish. This verse is what he wants instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two righteousnesses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not wearing a righteousness of my own that comes from the law, but the one that comes through faith in Christ, the righteousness that comes from God and rests on faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He sets them side by side:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "| his own | the other | |---|---| | from the law | from God | | produced | received | | worn as his own | found in Him |"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say his own record was fake. By the law's own measure he says he was blameless. He says it is the wrong kind of thing to be standing in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Found in Him\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is about being discovered in a place — where you turn out to be when somebody looks."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's ambition is that whenever the inspection comes, he is located inside Christ rather than inside his own record. That is a statement about position, not about effort."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for the credentials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The term he used two verses earlier is deliberately coarse — refuse, what is thrown to dogs. He is not saying his advantages were worthless in themselves; he is describing what they became once he had something to compare them with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that I may gain Christ* is the sentence they were traded for."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he wants next.** The verse immediately after: *that I may know Him and the power of His resurrection, and share in His sufferings.* Having settled that the standing is received rather than earned, the thing he then reaches for is not more standing. It is knowing the person."}]}, "Philippians 4:17–18": {"title": "The fruit to your account", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is thanking them for money, and he stops to say what he is actually pleased about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disclaimer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It is not the gift itself that I am after**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just told them he has learned to be content in any state — *I know how to be abased, and I know how to abound.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not want them thinking the thank-you is a hint."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"What I am after is **the fruit that keeps mounting up to your account**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is commercial: **karpos** (fruit, yield) and **logos** (account, ledger entry). *Mounting up* is **pleonazo** — to increase, to abound."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is using the language of a bank ledger, and the account in question is theirs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The benefit he names is not his."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had received"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have everything I need, and more besides. I am amply supplied**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three statements of sufficiency in a row. The Greek for *I have everything* is **apecho** — the word used on receipts: *paid in full*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is writing from a Roman prison."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who brought it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now that **Epaphroditus has put your gift into my hands**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Philippi sent a man as well as money. Epaphroditus nearly died on the errand — *he was sick almost unto death; but God had mercy on him* — and Paul sends him home with the letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three things he calls it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**A fragrance of sweetness, a sacrifice that is welcomed, a delight to God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of them is temple vocabulary, and none of them is about Paul."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**A fragrance of sweetness** — the phrase from Leviticus for accepted sacrifice, the same one used over Noah's altar. See the note on Genesis 8:20–22.", "**A sacrifice that is welcomed** — **dekten**, acceptable, received.", "**A delight to God** — well-pleasing."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Money handed to a prisoner is described as smoke going up off an altar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it went"}, {"k": "p", "t": "To an inmate's food and expenses in Rome. Paul says God took it as an offering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Philippi had done before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Even in Thessalonica you sent aid once and again** for my necessities… **no church shared with me concerning giving and receiving but you only**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been supporting him since the beginning — since a riverside prayer meeting, a seller of purple, and a night in the town jail."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence after this one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**And my God shall supply all your need according to His riches in glory by Christ Jesus**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It comes immediately after the receipt. See the note on Philippians 4:19–20."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Whose ledger.** He says twice that he is not writing about his own supply. The subject of the paragraph is what is accumulating on the other side of the transaction."}]}, "Philippians 4:19–20": {"title": "\"According to His riches\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the most quoted promises in Paul, and it is a thank-you note."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he is writing to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Philippians had just sent him a gift, carried by Epaphroditus, while he was in prison."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses immediately before: *you sent aid once and again for my necessities… **I have all and abound. I am full**, having received from Epaphroditus the things sent from you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise is Paul's response to people who had given him something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My God shall supply all your need **according to His riches in glory** by Christ Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The preposition is the whole verse. **According to**, not *out of*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A rich man giving *out of* his wealth might hand over a coin. Giving *according to* it means the size of the gift is set by the size of the fortune."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"All your need\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **chreia** — need, necessity, what is required. Not desire."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had just distinguished the two in the same paragraph: *I have learned, in whatever state I am, **to be content**… I know how to be abased, and I know how to abound.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man promising supply for every need had already said he had learned to do without."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In glory\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The riches are located. Not in a national economy or a treasury, and not in the generosity of the Philippians, who were poor themselves — 2 Corinthians describes the Macedonian churches giving *in a great trial of affliction… their deep poverty abounded in the riches of their liberality.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The channel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By Christ Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything Paul promises anywhere runs through that phrase."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The doxology"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now to **our God and Father** be glory **forever and ever**. Amen.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not end the sentence with the supply. He ends it with praise, and the possessive is *our*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul had said four verses earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I can do all things through Christ who strengthens me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Also usually quoted alone, and also written in a passage about contentment in poverty. The *all things* he names in context are *being full and being hungry, both abounding and suffering need.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Roman prison, with a case pending, writing the most joyful letter in the New Testament. *Rejoice in the Lord always. Again I will say, rejoice.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why the Philippians in particular.** *No church shared with me concerning giving and receiving but you only. For even in Thessalonica you sent aid once and again.* They had been funding him since the beginning — since a riverside prayer meeting and a purple merchant's house. See the note on Acts 16:14–15."}]}, "Philippians 4:4–5": {"title": "I will say it once more", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Written from a prison, and the instruction is to be glad."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be **glad in the Lord always**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **chairete** — rejoice. And **pantote**, always, without a gap."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is in Rome under guard, chained to a soldier, waiting on a verdict that could go either way. He mentions the chains three times in the letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repetition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will say it **once more**: be glad.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *again I will say, rejoice*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has already said it in chapter 3 — *finally, my brethren, rejoice in the Lord* — and he comes back for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing else in the letter is doubled like this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the joy is located"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In **the Lord**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in circumstances, and not manufactured out of nothing. The location is named, and it is the one thing about his situation that had not changed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word **chara**, joy, and its verb run through this short letter more than a dozen times. It is the most cheerful thing Paul wrote and it was written in custody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let the **gentle give of your spirit** be plain to everyone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epieikes** — the hardest word in the letter to translate. Older versions have *moderation*, *forbearance*, *gentleness*, *yieldedness*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It means not standing on the letter of your rights. The willingness to take less than you are owed rather than press the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is to see it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be plain to **everyone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *to all men*. Not to the church."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever this quality is, its audience is outside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence in between"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Lord is near.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words, and they can be read two ways in Greek — near in place, or near in time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not disambiguate, and probably means both. The next verse is about praying to someone close enough to hear; the letter has already said *our citizenship is in heaven, from which we also eagerly wait for the Saviour*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be **anxious for nothing**, but in everything by prayer and supplication, with thanksgiving, let your requests be made known to God; and the peace of God, which surpasses all understanding, will guard your hearts and minds through Christ Jesus.* See the note on Philippians 4:6–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The guarding verb is **phrouresei** — a garrison word. Paul, with a soldier attached to his wrist, says peace will stand sentry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes just before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two women. *I implore Euodia and I implore Syntyche to be of the same mind in the Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The *rejoice always* and the *let your gentleness be known* land immediately after a falling-out between two named members of the congregation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is who *everyone* includes first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul learned it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have learned, in whatever state I am, **to be content**… I know how to be abased, and I know how to abound.* See the note on Philippians 4:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *learned* is **emathon** — he was taught it, over time, by going through both."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it was written from.** A rented house in Rome with a guard on the door. The instruction is *always*, and he says it twice in case the first one was missed."}]}, "Philippians 4:6–7": {"title": "Peace on guard", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "An instruction not to worry, written from a prison cell."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In custody in Rome, chained to a guard, with his case unheard."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I want you to know that the things which happened to me have actually turned out for the furtherance of the gospel*, and *my chains are in Christ… in the whole palace guard*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rejoice in the Lord always. Again I will say, rejoice!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let your gentleness be known to all men. **The Lord is at hand**.* See the note on Philippians 4:4–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ho Kurios engus** — the Lord is near."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not be anxious about **anything**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **meden merimnate** — nothing be anxious about."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Merimnao** — to be pulled apart, divided. The verb Jesus uses of Martha: *you are worried and troubled about many things*. See the note on Luke 10:38–42."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The replacement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But in **every single thing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**En panti** — in everything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing, and everything. Paul sets the two words against each other in consecutive clauses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three ways"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By **prayer** and by **asking**, with **thanks** alongside.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Proseuche** — prayer generally, the word for approaching God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Deesis** — petition, the specific request arising out of need."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Eucharistia** — thanksgiving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the thanksgiving goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Alongside.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Meta eucharistias** — with thanksgiving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not afterwards, when it works out. Simultaneously, in the same sentence as the request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let your requests **be laid out before God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **gnorizestho pros ton Theon** — let them be made known toward God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which He already knows. *Your Father knows exactly what you need long before you ask Him.* See the note on Matthew 6:5–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The laying out is not for His information."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the request granted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the peace of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **he eirene tou Theou**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which **reaches far beyond anything our minds can take in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **he huperechousa panta noun** — surpassing all understanding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Huperecho** — to hold above, to rise over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It can mean the peace exceeds what the mind can grasp, or that it exceeds what the mind can manage on its own. Both readings fit a man in a cell."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Will **stand guard over** your hearts and your thoughts.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phrouresei** — will garrison, will keep watch as a military post."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Phroureo** — the word for a garrison holding a city. Paul uses it of being kept under guard in Damascus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is chained to a soldier while he writes it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is guarded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your **hearts** and your **thoughts**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kardias kai ta noemata** — hearts and mental faculties."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same **nous** that could not comprehend the peace in the previous clause is what the peace stands over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In Christ Jesus.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Finally, brethren, whatever things are true, whatever things are noble, whatever things are just… **meditate on these things**.* See the note on Philippians 4:8–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six categories, and then *the God of peace will be with you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 7 gives the peace of God; verse 9 gives the God of peace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about his own state"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not that I speak in regard to need, for **I have learned in whatever state I am, to be content**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **autarkes** — self-sufficient, the Stoics' word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I can do all things through Christ who strengthens me.* See the note on Philippians 4:11–13."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb.** **Phroureo** — to garrison, to hold a post. Written by a man under guard, about what stands over the mind that could not take it in."}]}, "Philippians 4:8": {"title": "Eight things to think on", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A list of eight, and the verb at the end is the whole instruction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately after *be anxious for nothing*, and the peace that guards the heart and mind. See the note on Philippians 4:6–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The guarding verb there is **phrouresei** — a garrison word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This verse is what the garrison is protecting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The eight"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever is **true**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Alethe** — true, as opposed to false or unreal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever is **worthy of honor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Semna** — grave, dignified, weighty. Used of how deacons and older men should be."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever is **right**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dikaia** — righteous, just."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever is **pure**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hagna** — chaste, unmixed. The same root as the purifying in 1 John 3:3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever is **lovely**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Prosphile** — and it occurs once in the New Testament. From **pros** and **phileo** — toward-loving. Things that call out affection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever is **well spoken of**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Euphema** — fair-sounding, of good report. The root of *euphemism*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two summaries"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If anything is **excellent**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Arete** — virtue, moral excellence. The great word of Greek ethics, and Paul uses it once."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If anything **deserves praise**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the list comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a Christian vocabulary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Arete** is Aristotle's word. **Semnos**, **prosphiles** and **euphemos** are the currency of Greek moral philosophy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul, writing from a Roman prison to a Roman colony, uses the vocabulary his readers already had for a good life — and does not baptise it or apologise for it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever is true, wherever it comes from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let your thinking **dwell on these**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **logizesthe** — reckon, take account of, calculate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An accounting word. It is the verb Paul uses in Romans of counting yourself dead to sin, and of righteousness being credited to Abraham. See the note on Romans 6:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *daydream about*. Enter these in the ledger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The next verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The things which you learned and received and heard and saw in me, **these do**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Think on those, and do these."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the promise: *and the God of peace will be with you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 7 has the peace of God guarding; verse 9 has the God of peace present. Paul brackets the list with both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most cheerful thing Paul wrote, from custody, with a verdict pending. See the note on Philippians 4:4–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rejoice in the Lord always. Again I will say, rejoice.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have learned, in whatever state I am, to be content.* See the note on Philippians 4:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *my God shall supply all your need according to His riches in glory by Christ Jesus*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the instruction is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A ban on facing hard things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same letter names his chains, the risk of death, two women falling out, and a friend who *was sick almost unto death*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list is about where the attention settles, not about what is admitted to exist."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb at the end.** *Logizomai* — an accounting word. Not *dwell on* in the sense of drifting. Reckon these; enter them in the book."}]}, "Proverbs 10:1–7": {"title": "\"A diligent hand quietly makes him rich\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is where the long run of two-line proverbs begins, and the pattern is fixed from here on: one line, then its opposite. Each verse is a small before-and-after."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pair about hands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A lazy hand leaves a person poor. A diligent hand quietly makes him rich.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for **diligent** is a sharp word — it is used of a threshing sledge, the tool that cuts through grain. Not busyness; something decisive that gets through work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Illumination's *quietly* is picking up the sense of the verb, which is about steady accumulation rather than a sudden windfall. Nothing dramatic is being promised. Something is being built by a hand that keeps going."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one nobody quotes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wealth won by wrong does no good in the end. Righteousness pulls a soul back from death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It arrives before the industry proverbs and sets the terms for them: the book is not simply in favour of getting more. Two verses later comes gathering in summer versus sleeping through harvest — timing, not just effort."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On reading these as promises.** They are observations about how life generally runs, not contracts. The same book says elsewhere that the poor can be swept away by injustice regardless of how hard they work, and Ecclesiastes exists partly to press on the exceptions. Proverbs tells you where to put your weight; it does not promise the floor never gives."}]}, "Proverbs 10:22–27": {"title": "\"He adds no sorrow along with it\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*It is the blessing of the LORD that makes rich, and He adds no sorrow along with it.* One of the most argued-over lines in Proverbs, because the second half can be read two ways in Hebrew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two readings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence is compressed, and the disagreement is about what *toil* or *sorrow* attaches to:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**He adds no sorrow with it** — what God gives does not arrive with grief attached", "**and toiling adds nothing to it** — no amount of extra labour increases what the blessing gives"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are defensible from the grammar, and the second has an old pedigree. The first is the reading most English versions take, and the one this translation follows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way the contrast is with the verse a few lines earlier — *wealth won by wrong does no good in the end* — and with the sluggard two verses later. The chapter is not against work. It is against believing work is the source."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What the wicked dreads will fall upon him. What the righteous long for will be given.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A pair, set deliberately: one man's fear arrives, the other's longing does. Proverbs states these as the general run of things, in the confident style the book uses throughout."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How to read the run of promises here.** Chapter 10 is a rapid series of contrasts, and it does not pause for exceptions — that is not what this genre does. Job and Ecclesiastes exist partly as the counterweight, and the Bible keeps all three in the same collection without embarrassment. Proverbs tells you which way the world generally leans; it does not promise that it never tips the other way."}]}, "Proverbs 11:15–21": {"title": "Kindness done to your own soul", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Seven proverbs, and most of them are about what an action does to the person doing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The surety"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Guarantee a stranger's debt and **you will suffer for it**. Refuse to shake on it, and **you are safe**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ra-yeroa ki-arav zar** — he will surely suffer harm who is surety for a stranger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Toqe'im** — those who strike hands. The gesture that sealed a guarantee."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs returns to this five times, more often than almost any other practical warning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gracious woman"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **gracious woman holds onto honor**, as **ruthless men hold onto their riches**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **eshet-chen** — a woman of grace. And **ariztim** — the violent, the terrifying."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two kinds of holding, set side by side. One holds honour, the other holds money, and the parallel does not flatter the second."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The kindness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **kind person does good to his own soul**. A **cruel one brings trouble on his own flesh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **gomel naphsho ish chased** — the man of **chesed** deals well with his own soul."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chesed** — the covenant word. Loyal kindness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the proverb claims"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that kindness is rewarded. That it is self-directed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The kind man is doing something to himself while he is doing it to somebody else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cruel man is doing the same, in the same sentence, to his own body."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wages"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wicked **earns wages that deceive**. Sow righteousness and **the reward is sure**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **pe'ullat-sheqer** — wages of falsehood, work that lies."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sekher emet** — a wage of truth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both men get paid. One payment turns out not to be there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hold to righteousness and **you move toward life**. Chase after evil and **you chase your own death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ken-tzedaqah lechayyim** — so righteousness to life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both verbs are about pursuit. The evil man is not caught; he is chasing, and what he catches is his own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two states of heart"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **crooked in heart** are something the LORD hates. Those **blameless in their way** are His delight.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **iqqeshei-lev** — twisted of heart. And **temimei derek** — complete, whole in their way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**To'avat YHWH** — an abomination to the LORD. And **retzono** — His delight, His pleasure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be sure of it, **the wicked will not go free**, but **the children of the righteous will be delivered**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yad leyad lo-yinnaqeh ra** — hand to hand, the evil shall not be unpunished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yad leyad** — hand to hand. An idiom, probably meaning *depend upon it*, or possibly *from generation to generation*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reach forward"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The children of the righteous.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proverb extends past the man to the next household."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs says it again: *a good man leaves an inheritance to his children's children*. See the note on Proverbs 13:17–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter opened with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Dishonest scales are an abomination to the LORD, but a just weight is His delight.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two words — abomination and delight — used of weights in verse 1 and of hearts in verse 20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book treats the shop counter and the interior life with the same vocabulary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse in the middle of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is one who scatters, yet increases more; and there is one who withholds more than is right, but it leads to poverty.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The generous soul will be made rich, and **he who waters will also be watered himself**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same logic as verse 17, applied to money."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The claim.** *A kind person does good to his own soul.* Not that kindness gets rewarded — that it lands on the one doing it, at the time."}]}, "Proverbs 11:8–14": {"title": "\"In many wise counselors there is safety\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The famous line closes a run of sayings that are almost all about the mouth, and that is not an accident."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Read what it is sitting with"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*with his mouth the godless would ruin his neighbor*", "*by the blessing of the upright a city is lifted high; by the mouth of the wicked it is torn down*", "*whoever belittles a neighbor has no sense at all*", "*a gossip goes about giving secrets away; a faithful spirit keeps the matter quiet*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *where there is no guidance, a people falls. In many wise counselors there is safety.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the proverb about counsel arrives at the end of a paragraph about how much damage talk can do — which is a caution built into the promise. The counsellors being commended are the sort just described as trustworthy with what they are told."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Guidance** is a navigation word — the same root is used of steering a ship, and elsewhere in Proverbs of a person of understanding acquiring *direction*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Safety** is literally deliverance, rescue. Not \"a better outcome.\" Getting out."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the proverb assumes.** Not that a crowd is wiser than one person — this book has plenty to say about crowds. It assumes *wise* counsellors, plural, who can keep a confidence. A proverb is a general truth well put, not a mechanism, and this one carries its own quality control in the verses above it."}]}, "Proverbs 12:22–28": {"title": "\"The lazy man never roasts what he caught\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A run of contrasts, and one of them is a picture rather than a principle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hunter who does not cook"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The lazy man never roasts what he caught, but the diligent hold their gain as precious.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse is difficult in Hebrew and translators have long divided over it. The sense most take is the one here: he went out, he caught something, and then he never got round to doing anything with it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The failure is not in the hunt. It is in what happened after — the thing obtained and not used, the catch left on the ground."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is one of the few proverbs about laziness that concerns something already acquired rather than something never attempted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line worth stopping on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Worry weighs a heart down low, but a kind word makes it glad.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb means to bow down or make stoop. Anxiety is described physically — it bends a person over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the remedy offered is not advice or resolution. It is *a good word* from somebody else. In a chapter full of proverbs about hard work and honest speech, this one says that a heart bent by worry is straightened by a kindness spoken to it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *In the way of righteousness there is life, and along that path there is no death.* It is the strongest thing said in the chapter, and it reads oddly among proverbs about lying lips and lazy hands — which is how Proverbs works. The small practical sayings keep being punctuated by something much larger, without transition."}]}, "Proverbs 13:1–8": {"title": "Guard your mouth", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Eight proverbs in pairs, and the third one is about a sentry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The listener"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A wise child **leans in when a parent teaches**. A mocker **never hears the warning at all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ben chakham musar av** — a wise son, a father's instruction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Letz** — the mocker, one of the book's three standing characters, alongside the fool and the simple."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The difference named is not intelligence. It is whether the rebuke registers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fruit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Good words are **fruit you get to taste**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mipperi phi-ish yochal tov** — from the fruit of a man's mouth he eats good."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image recurs in the book: *a man's stomach shall be satisfied from the fruit of his mouth*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What you say comes back as something you eat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The guard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Guard your mouth and **guard your life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **notzer piv shomer naphsho** — the one guarding his mouth keeps his soul."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two different verbs for guarding, one for the mouth and one for the life, in the same clause."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opposite"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Fling words wide and **watch things break**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **posheq sephatav mechittah-lo** — one opening wide his lips, ruin to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Pasaq** — to part, to open wide. The image is of a gate left standing open."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two appetites"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The lazy **want and want and stay empty**. The steady **want and end up full**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mit'avvah va'ayin naphsho atzel** — the soul of the sluggard craves and has nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both men want. The proverb does not say the lazy man lacks desire."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hatred"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Honest hearts **hate what is false**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **devar-sheqer** — a word of falsehood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Uprightness **keeps the blameless standing**. Wickedness **knocks the sinner flat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tzedaqah titzor tam-derek** — righteousness guards the one blameless in the way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third guarding verb in eight verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two pretenders"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One **pretends to be rich and owns nothing**. Another **looks poor and holds a quiet fortune**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yesh mit'asher ve'eyn kol** — there is one making himself rich and having nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Mitroshesh vehon rav** — making himself poor, and great wealth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are self-presentations, and both are false. Proverbs notices the second as well as the first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wealth **can ransom a man's life**. The poor **never even hear the threat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kopher nephesh-ish oshro** — the ransom of a man's life is his riches."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A rich man can be extorted. A poor man is not worth threatening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proverb is not praising poverty. It notices a cost of wealth that the wealthy do not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the guarding proverb recurs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who guards his mouth and his tongue **keeps his soul from troubles**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *whoever guards his mouth and tongue keeps himself out of trouble* — chapter 21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book returns to it repeatedly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who among you loves being alive… then **keep your tongue from evil**.* See the note on Psalms 34:11–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same instruction, in answer to a question about long life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What James says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If anyone does not stumble in word, he is a perfect man, **able also to bridle the whole body**.* See the note on James 3:1–5."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The pairing.** *Guard your mouth and guard your life* — two different Hebrew verbs for guarding in one clause, and eight verses that use the root three times."}]}, "Proverbs 13:17–25": {"title": "\"Walk with the wise and grow wise\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The proverb everyone knows from this passage is about the company you keep, and the Hebrew behind it is more physical than the English."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb is \"walk\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *admire*, not *listen to*. **Halak** — to walk, to go along with, to travel in someone's company. It is the ordinary word for how you spend your days."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is not of picking mentors. It is of who is beside you when nothing special is happening, and the proverb claims that wisdom transfers by proximity rather than by instruction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second half is deliberately blunt: *keep fools for friends and come to harm*. Same mechanism, opposite direction. Whatever you walk with, you become."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line before it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A longing fulfilled is sweet to the soul. Fools would rather die than leave their evil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Odd pairing, until you see it. The verse before speaks of a desire finally satisfied; the verse after describes people who will not let go of what is destroying them. Then: walk with the wise. The proverb about company is set between something worth wanting and an example of wanting the wrong thing badly enough to die of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On reading a proverb.** These are general truths, sharply put — not guarantees. The same chapter says the fields of the poor could yield plenty but injustice sweeps the harvest away, which is the book itself acknowledging that the pattern does not always hold. A proverb tells you which way to lean, not what is certain to happen."}]}, "Proverbs 13:9–16": {"title": "\"Money gathered slowly grows\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Eight couplets, and one of them is about how money behaves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two ways it comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Money **grabbed fast slips fast away**. Money **gathered slowly grows**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of the first is wealth from vanity, from nothing, from vapour — the same root as Ecclesiastes' *vanity of vanities*. The older rendering is *wealth gotten by vanity shall be diminished.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the second is literally *gathered by the hand* — a handful at a time, by labour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proverb is not against money. It is about the manner of its arrival, and it says the manner determines whether it stays."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes fast, goes fast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb in the first line is to become small, to dwindle. It does not say the money is taken away — it says it shrinks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Hope stretched thin"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hope **stretched thin** makes the heart sick. A longing filled is a **tree of life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *hope deferred*. Something delayed past the point the heart can hold it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer is not a lowered expectation. It is a longing **filled** — and the image chosen for it is the tree in the garden."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lamp"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The light of the upright **burns clear and glad**. The lamp of the wicked **flickers out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first verb carries rejoicing. A light that is glad."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the wisdom comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The teaching of the wise is a **spring of life**, turning your feet from the snares of death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A spring, not a cistern. Something with its own source."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one about advice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Pride only breeds more quarrels. **Wisdom listens, and takes advice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *with the well-advised is wisdom*. It is not the clever who are wise in Proverbs; it is the ones who let themselves be counselled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the flag"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The clever act on what they know. **The fool spreads his folly like a flag**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that he spreads it out, unfurls it, makes a display."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter says a few verses later.** *A good man leaves an inheritance to his children's children, and the wealth of the sinner is stored up for the righteous.* Slowly gathered, and it outlasts him by two generations. See the note on Proverbs 13:17–25."}]}, "Proverbs 14:24–30": {"title": "A tranquil heart", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Seven proverbs, and the last one makes a claim about the body."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crown"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The crown of the wise is their riches. **The folly of fools is only more folly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for the second line is **ivvelet kesilim ivvelet** — the folly of fools is folly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word twice. Wisdom compounds into something; folly compounds into more of itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The witness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **truthful witness saves lives**. A liar **breathes out betrayal**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **matzil nephashot** — a deliverer of souls."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Court testimony was often the only evidence, and a false witness in a capital case was a murderer. The ninth commandment sits behind it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The confidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In **the fear of the LORD is strong confidence**, and **a refuge for His children**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mivtach-oz** — strong trust. And **machseh** — a shelter, the noun from the verb in Psalm 118. See the note on Psalms 118:8–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fear producing confidence is the paradox the book is built on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The spring"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The fear of the LORD is **a spring of life**, turning your feet from **the snares of death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **meqor chayyim** — a fountain of life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase used of a man's mouth, of understanding, and of the teaching of the wise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The king"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A crowd of people is a king's glory. **Without them a ruler comes to ruin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **be'ephes le'om mechittat razon** — in the absence of people is the ruin of a prince."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A king without subjects is not a king. The proverb is aimed at rulers who forget where their standing comes from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The temper"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Slow to anger, and **rich in understanding**. Quick to temper, and **quick to look the fool**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **erek appayim** — long of nostrils. The idiom for patience, used of God at Sinai: *the LORD, the LORD God, merciful and gracious, **longsuffering***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Qetzar-ruach** — short of spirit. The opposite idiom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **tranquil heart gives life to the body**. **Envy is rot in the bones**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lev marpe chayyei besarim** — a heart of healing is life to the flesh."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Marpe** — healing, cure. The word in *kind words are… healing to the bones*. See the note on Proverbs 16:24–33."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bones"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Uraqav atzamot qin'ah** — and rottenness of bones is envy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Raqav** — decay, caries. The word for wood rot and for what happens to a corpse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the proverb claims"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two interior states with physical consequences, stated flatly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A settled heart keeps the flesh alive. Envy decays the skeleton."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew does not hedge either half."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why bones"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The deepest part of the body in Hebrew idiom, and where grief is felt: *my bones waste away*, *my bones are troubled*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Envy reaches the same depth as sorrow, and the proverb puts it there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Job says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Oh, if only the old days would come back… **when the friendship of God rested warm over my tent**.\"* See the note on Job 29:1–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who oppresses the poor reproaches his Maker, but he who honours Him has mercy on the needy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Righteousness exalts a nation, but sin is a reproach to any people.* See the note on Proverbs 14:31–35."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last proverb.** A settled heart is *life to the flesh*; envy is *rot in the bones*. Two interior states named as having physical outcomes, without qualification."}]}, "Proverbs 14:31–35": {"title": "Righteousness lifts a nation up", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Five proverbs, and three of them are about what happens at scale."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The poor and their Maker"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Oppress the poor and **you insult their Maker**. **Honor Him by showing them kindness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **choreph oseihu** — reviles his Maker."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument is not that the poor deserve better. It is that they were made, and the insult goes past them to the one who made them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proverb appears again in chapter 17: *he who mocks the poor reproaches his Maker*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shelter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wicked are **swept off by their own evil**. The upright **find shelter even in death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for the first is **yiddacheh** — thrust down, driven away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Uvemoto choseh tzaddiq** — and in his death the righteous has refuge."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A striking line for the Old Testament, where the afterlife is rarely in view. The refuge is not *from* death; it is *in* it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Wisdom in the heart"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wisdom **rests quietly in the understanding heart**. Even among fools **it makes itself known**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tanuach** — rests, settles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wisdom sits still where there is understanding. Where there is not, it is still detectable — the second line says it becomes known even among fools, which may mean it stands out by contrast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The national proverb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Righteousness **lifts a nation up**. Sin is **a disgrace to any people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tzedaqah teromem goy** — righteousness exalts a nation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Rum** — to raise, to lift high. And **cheshed** in the second line, a rare noun usually meaning reproach or shame."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words for people"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Goy** — nation, in the political sense. And **le'ummim** — peoples."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proverb is not about Israel. It uses the general words, and applies to any nation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind of righteousness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book's usual sense: right dealing between people. Honest scales, fair judgments, the poor not crushed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs has spent chapters on false weights, moving boundary stones, and bribes in court. The proverb is the summary of all of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scale"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every other proverb in this cluster is about an individual. This one is about a country, using the same vocabulary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The king"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A king delights in **a servant who acts wisely**, but **his anger falls on the one who brings shame**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **maskil** — one who acts with insight, prudently."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs is court literature, addressed to young men being trained for administration, and the last line brings the sequence back to the palace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Joseph was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Can we find such a one as this, a man in whom is the Spirit of God?**\"… \"There is no one as discerning and wise as you.\"* See the note on Genesis 41:37–40."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A servant who acted with insight, in front of a king, during a famine."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Daniel was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In all matters of wisdom and understanding about which the king examined them, **he found them ten times better***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two men who fit the last proverb exactly, in two foreign courts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the exaltation comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **exaltation comes neither from the east nor from the west nor from the south. But God is the Judge: He puts down one, and exalts another***. See the note on Psalms 75:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is the same root, **rum**."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two words.** **Goy** and **le'ummim** — the general terms for nations and peoples. The proverb is not written about Israel, and its scope is anybody."}]}, "Proverbs 15:1–7": {"title": "\"A gentle answer turns away wrath\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted proverb about speech, and the word behind *gentle* is not what most readers assume."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **rak** — soft, tender. It is used of tender meat, of a delicate child, and of Leah's eyes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not weak, and not evasive. Soft in texture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Turns away wrath.* The verb is about causing something to go back the way it came — deflection rather than absorption."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proverb is not saying a soft answer makes you feel better about the abuse. It says the anger goes back down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the second half gives the alternative: *a harsh word stirs the anger up.* The Hebrew for *harsh* means painful, hurtful — a word that causes grief."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves describe an answer. The choice is not between speaking and staying silent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the paragraph sits with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every proverb in the run is about the mouth:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*the tongue of the wise makes knowledge sweet*", "*a healing tongue is a tree of life; a twisted tongue breaks the spirit*", "*the lips of the wise scatter knowledge like seed*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tree of life image is the strongest. It appears four times in Proverbs, and one of them is a way of speaking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The eyes of the LORD are everywhere, watching the evil and the good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An odd insertion in a run about speech — until you notice what it does to the rest. All these exchanges happen in front of somebody."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where a soft answer is hardest.** The examples in Scripture are unflattering. Peter, told the cross was coming, argued. James and John proposed fire. Paul called a high priest a whitewashed wall and then apologised when told who he was. The proverb is not describing a personality type — it is describing a choice made in the second after being provoked."}]}, "Proverbs 15:16–23": {"title": "\"A word in season, how good it is\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The run ends on a line about timing, and everything before it is about what makes a household bearable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two \"betters\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Better a little with the fear of the LORD than great riches and trouble with it. Better a plate of greens where love is than a fattened calf and hatred at the table.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both compare a small good thing with a large bad one, and both put the difference in the atmosphere rather than the menu."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A fattened calf was the best meal available — the thing killed for the prodigal's homecoming. Proverbs says vegetables beat it if the room is hostile."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The temper proverbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A hot temper stirs up strife. A patient one calms the quarrel down.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for patient is *long of nostrils* — slow to flare. The idiom is the same one used of God being slow to anger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is joy in giving the right answer, and **a word in season**, how good it is.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two halves. The right answer, and the right moment — and Proverbs treats them as separate achievements."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A true thing said at the wrong hour is not counted here as a success. The verse just before it makes the same point from the other side: *the one who understands walks straight ahead* rather than blurting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it sits with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Plans fall apart without counsel, but with many advisers they stand.* The season-word proverb comes directly after it — which suggests the good answer is often somebody else's, arriving when you needed it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How to use a run like this.** Proverbs 15 is a chapter of contrasts about speech: a gentle answer turning away wrath at the top, a soothing tongue as a tree of life in the middle, and the word in season at the end. Read the chapter whole and the subject is not wisdom in the abstract. It is what comes out of a person's mouth in a house."}]}, "Proverbs 15:8–15": {"title": "A feast that keeps going", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Eight proverbs, and the last one ends the run on a table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sacrifice and the prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD **hates the sacrifice of the wicked**, but **the prayer of the upright delights Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *delights* is **ratson** — favour, acceptance, good pleasure. The word used of a sacrifice being accepted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One man brings an animal and it is detestable. The other says nothing but words and it is the thing God takes pleasure in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel says the same to Saul: *to obey is better than sacrifice*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The way and the pursuer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD **hates the way of the wicked**, but **He loves the one who pursues what is right**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he loves him who follows after righteousness* — **radaph**, to chase, to pursue hard. The verb for an army in pursuit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the one who has arrived. The one still running after it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The correction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Stern correction waits for the one who **leaves the path**. Whoever **hates rebuke will die**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two halves, and the second is the harsher."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs keeps the pair together throughout: *he who regards a rebuke will be honoured*, and *he who hates correction is stupid*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seeing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Death and destruction **lie open before the LORD**. **How much more the hearts of men**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew names them: **Sheol and Abaddon**. The grave and the place of ruin, personified, transparent to Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument runs from the greater to the lesser. If the underworld is open, a chest cavity is not a difficulty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews says it the same way: *all things are naked and open to the eyes of Him to whom we must give account.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mocker"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A mocker will **not love the one who corrects him**. He **will not go near the wise**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lets** — the scoffer, a fixed character type in Proverbs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not argue with wisdom. He keeps away from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The face"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **glad heart makes a cheerful face**. **Heartache crushes the spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *cheerful* is *makes the face shine* — **yetiv panim**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proverb reads inside to outside: the interior is the cause and the face is the report."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Aaron's blessing runs the same direction from above: *the LORD make His face shine upon you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The appetite"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The understanding heart **goes looking for knowledge**. The **mouths of fools feed on folly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are eating. One goes looking; the other grazes on what is at hand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every day is **grim for the oppressed**, but a **happy heart keeps a feast going on and on**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *all the days of the afflicted are evil, but he who is of a merry heart has a continual feast*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Mishteh tamid** — a banquet, perpetual."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No change of circumstance is mentioned between the two halves. The afflicted man and the merry man could be the same man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the feast image goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And on this mountain the LORD of hosts will **make for all peoples a feast***. See the note on Isaiah 25:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the other proverb about the interior and the bones: *a cheerful heart is good medicine*. See the note on Proverbs 17:22–28."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the last line measures.** Not the day. *All the days of the afflicted are evil* — and the merry heart is at a banquet that does not stop, in the same weather."}]}, "Proverbs 16:1–7": {"title": "\"The LORD weighs the spirit\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Seven proverbs, and five of them name the LORD as the one who actually decides."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plans"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **plans of the heart belong to us**, but **the answer of the tongue comes from the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two halves, and the division is between what a person arranges inwardly and what comes out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs says this repeatedly — *a man's heart plans his way, but the LORD directs his steps*, eight verses later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The eyes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man's ways **all seem clean in his own eyes**, but **the LORD weighs the spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *weighs* is **takan** — to measure, to gauge, to test the weight of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proverb does not say the man is lying. He genuinely thinks so, and the assessment is being made elsewhere on a different instrument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commitment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Commit your work to the LORD, and **your plans will find firm ground**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *commit* is **galal** — to roll. Roll your works onto the LORD."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Find firm ground** is **kun** — established, fixed, made to stand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before said the plans are ours. This one says what to do with them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD has **made everything for its purpose**, even the wicked for the day of trouble.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *for its own answer* — everything has a matching end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Pride"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD **hates every proud heart**. Be sure of this: **it will not go unpunished**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *an abomination to the LORD is everyone proud of heart*, and then *hand to hand* — a gesture that meant a thing was as good as settled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs returns to it two chapters on: *pride goes before destruction.* See the note on Proverbs 18:9–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The covering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By **love and faithfulness sin is covered over**, and by **the fear of the LORD one turns from evil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chesed ve'emet** — covenant loyalty and truth. The same pairing Jacob asks Joseph for on his deathbed, and the pairing John puts in his prologue: *full of grace and truth*. See the note on Genesis 47:27–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Covered over** is **kaphar** — the atonement word. The verb behind **kapporeth**, the mercy seat, and behind Yom Kippur."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sin is not said to be excused or overlooked. It is atoned for, and the agents named are love and faithfulness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves of that verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Atonement in the first clause, and turning from evil in the second — in that order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The enemies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When **a man's ways please the LORD**, even **his enemies are made to be at peace with him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *He makes even his enemies to be at peace with him* — God is the one doing it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The peace is not negotiated by the man and is not conditional on the enemies changing their minds. It is made."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter puts the initiative.** The heart plans, the eyes approve, the man commits — and in every one of the seven verses the outcome is attached to the LORD: the answer, the weighing, the establishing, the purpose, the covering, and the peace."}]}, "Proverbs 16:24–33": {"title": "Healing to the bones", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Ten proverbs, and most of them are about what a mouth does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The honeycomb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Kind words are like a honeycomb, **sweet to the soul and healing to the bones**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **imrei-noam** — words of pleasantness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tsuph devash** — a honeycomb, the dripping comb."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two effects"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Matoq lannephesh** — sweet to the soul. And **marpe la'atsem** — healing to the bone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The soul and the skeleton. The proverb claims a physical effect and does not hedge it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Marpe** — healing, a cure, health. The same root as **rapha**, the word in *I am the LORD who heals you*. See the note on Exodus 15:22–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why bones"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bone is the deepest part of the body in Hebrew idiom, and the place where grief is felt: *my bones waste away*, *my bones are troubled*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Words reach that far, in both directions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The way that seems right"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is a way that seems right to a man, **but its end is the way to death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proverb appears twice in the book, word for word, here and in chapter 14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *seems right* is **yashar lifnei-ish** — straight before a man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not obviously wrong. Straight-looking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The worker's hunger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A worker's hunger does its work for him, for **his own empty mouth keeps driving him on**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nephesh amel** — the appetite of the labourer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A wry proverb: hunger is a manager. Nobody has to motivate a man who needs to eat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four about the mouth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A worthless man **digs up evil**, and his words are like **a scorching fire**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **koreh ra'ah** — digs a pit of evil. And **ke'esh tsarevet** — like a scorching fire."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A crooked man spreads strife, and **a whisperer separates close friends**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nirgan** — a whisperer, a slanderer. The proverb is repeated in chapter 18: *the words of a talebearer are like tasty trifles, and they go down into the inmost body*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The violent man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A violent man **lures his neighbour** and leads him down a road that is not good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yephatteh** — entices, persuades, seduces."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **winks his eyes**, plotting mischief. He **purses his lips** and evil is done.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two gestures, no words. The proverb notices that most of what a manipulative man communicates is done with his face."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grey head"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Gray hair is a crown of glory, gained by **a life lived rightly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ateret tipheret** — a crown of beauty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The qualification matters: **bederek tsedaqah timmatse** — it is found in the way of righteousness. Age alone is not the crown."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The city"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Better to be **slow to anger than mighty**, and **to rule your spirit than to take a city**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **erek appayim** — long of nostrils, the idiom for patience. It is the phrase used of God at Sinai: *the LORD, the LORD God, merciful and gracious, **longsuffering***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Moshel beruchov** — ruling his own spirit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is with military conquest, which was the ancient world's highest measure of a man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The lot is cast into the lap, **but its every decision is from the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **umeYHWH kol-mishpato** — and from the LORD is its whole judgment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lots were used for real decisions: dividing the land, choosing Saul, identifying Jonah, and replacing Judas."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that has spent ten verses on human speech and self-control ends by saying the outcome of a thrown stone belongs to God."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The claim.** *Healing to the bones.* **Marpe** — the same root as the divine name given at Marah, attached here to what somebody says to somebody else."}]}, "Proverbs 17:15–21": {"title": "Both alike", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Seven proverbs, and the first one names two opposite errors and gives them the same verdict."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Clearing the guilty and condemning the just, **the LORD hates them both alike**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he who justifies the wicked and he who condemns the just* — the two verbs are **tsadaq** and **rasha**, the courtroom pair."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are miscarriages, and they run in opposite directions. Proverbs says the LORD hates them equally."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *both alike* is *even both of them* — the phrase exists to close off any ranking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same words in the law"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The one **God declares guilty** repays his neighbour double* — **rasha** again. See the note on Exodus 22:7–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Deuteronomy: *the judges will decide, **clearing the innocent and condemning the guilty**.* See the note on Deuteronomy 25:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law uses the two verbs the right way round. This proverb names what it looks like when they are reversed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fool with money"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Why should a fool **hold money to buy wisdom**, when **he has no heart to learn**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *a price in the hand of a fool*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has the means and not the appetite. Proverbs treats the second as the binding constraint."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The friend"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A friend **loves at all times**, and **a brother is born for the hard days**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *a brother is born for adversity*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses that are usually read as a contrast and can be read as a pair: constancy in one, and a relation that exists for the bad stretch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Shaking hands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man with no sense **shakes hands on a deal** and **takes on his neighbor's debt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs returns to surety repeatedly and never approves of it — *he who is surety for a stranger will suffer*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one exception in Scripture is Judah offering himself for Benjamin, and Paul offering to cover Onesimus's debt: *if he owes you anything, put that on my account*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quarrel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever **loves a quarrel loves sin**. Whoever **builds a high gate invites ruin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for the second is *he who exalts his gate seeks destruction*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A gate higher than the wall around it is display rather than defence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crooked heart"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A crooked heart **never stumbles onto good**, and a twisted tongue **falls headlong into trouble**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *crooked* is **iqqesh** — twisted, perverse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody arrives at good by accident from there. Proverbs makes it a statement about direction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To father a fool brings only sorrow. **The parent of a fool has no joy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book says the reverse as often: *a wise son makes a glad father*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the first verse lands in the New Testament"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But to him who does not work but believes on Him **who justifies the ungodly**, his faith is accounted for righteousness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul writes that in Romans 4, using the same construction Proverbs condemns — and the whole of Romans 3 is his account of how it is not a miscarriage: *that He might be **just and the justifier** of the one who has faith in Jesus.* See the note on Romans 3:23–24."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the opening verse rules out.** Both directions. Calling a guilty man innocent and calling an innocent man guilty are named in the same line and given one verdict."}]}, "Proverbs 17:22–28": {"title": "Good medicine, and the value of shutting up", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Seven proverbs, and the last three are all about keeping quiet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The medicine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **cheerful heart is good medicine**, but a **crushed spirit dries up the bones**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *medicine* is **gehah** — a cure, a healing. The word occurs once in the whole Bible, here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *dries up* is **yabesh** — the verb used of the ground in a drought and of Jeroboam's withered hand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the marrow is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bones are where blood is made. The proverb puts the drying at the deepest point in the body it can name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same pairing runs through the collection: *a sound heart is life to the body, but envy is rottenness to the bones*, and *a good report makes the bones healthy*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bribe"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wicked take a bribe **from the fold of a robe** to bend the ways of justice.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *a bribe out of the bosom* — from inside the garment, where a man carried things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is precise: it does not come out in the open. A hand goes into a robe."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The eyes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wisdom is **right before the face** of the discerning, but the eyes of a fool **wander to the ends of the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *wisdom is before him who has understanding* — in front of his face, close enough to look at."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fool is not short of effort. He is looking further away than the thing is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The son"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A foolish son is **grief to his father** and **bitterness to the mother who bore him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both parents named, and the mother identified by the bearing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs says it four or five ways across the book, and always with the same two people in the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The injustice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **punish the righteous is not good**, nor to **flog the noble for their honesty**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *to strike princes for uprightness*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Beating a man for being right — Proverbs names it as a category of wrong, and the whole book of Job is an argument about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever **holds his words back has knowledge**, and the **even-tempered one has understanding**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *even-tempered* is *cool of spirit* — **qar-ruach**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things are being described: a man who knows and does not say, and a man whose temperature does not rise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second silence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even **a fool who keeps quiet is thought wise**, and sensible when he holds his tongue.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proverb is nearly a joke, and it is entirely serious. Silence is so reliable an indicator that it works even when it is not true."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the collection says elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the multitude of words sin is not lacking, but he who restrains his lips is wise.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who guards his mouth preserves his life, but he who opens wide his lips shall have destruction.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A soft answer turns away wrath, but a harsh word stirs up anger.* See the note on Proverbs 15:8–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the two halves of this passage meet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It opens with what a crushed spirit does to the bones and closes with a man who is cool of spirit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The medicine in verse 22 and the temperature in verse 27 are describing the same interior."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word that occurs once.** *Gehah* — a cure. Proverbs uses it of a cheerful heart, and nowhere else in Scripture does the word appear at all."}]}, "Proverbs 18:17–24": {"title": "\"Death and life are in the power of the tongue\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the most quoted proverbs about speech, and the two lines it sits between are what keep it in proportion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man eats his fill from the fruit of his mouth; the harvest of his lips is what satisfies him. Death and life are in the power of the tongue, and those who love it will eat its fruit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is agricultural and it runs across both verses: words are a crop, and the person who grew them is the one who has to eat the harvest. What you say comes back to your own table."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *power* is literally **hand** — death and life are in the hand of the tongue. It is a small organ given a large grip."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the surrounding verses are about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look at the company it keeps in this chapter:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*the first to plead his case seems right, until another comes and questions him*", "*a brother offended is harder to win than a fortified city*", "*the poor plead for mercy, but the rich answer with harsh words*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one is about speech between people — testimony, a quarrel that has hardened, an answer given roughly to someone with no power. That is the field this proverb grows in."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On how it gets used.** The verse is often taken to mean that spoken words shape outcomes by their own force. What the proverb states is narrower and harder to dismiss: your words do real damage or real good to other people, and you will live with the result. James says the same at length — a small rudder, a small fire — and both writers are talking about the tongue's effect on human beings, not on circumstances."}]}, "Proverbs 18:9–16": {"title": "The tower and the wall", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two fortifications in consecutive verses, and only one of them is real."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tower"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The name of the LORD is **a strong tower**. The righteous **run into it and are safe**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *migdal oz* — a fortified tower, the strongpoint inside a walled city that people retreated to when the outer defences fell."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs: they **run**, and they are **set on high** — the Hebrew is to be lifted up out of reach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note what does the protecting. Not their record. The **name**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wall"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wealth of the rich is his fortified city, a high wall, **in his own imagining**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse, and it is built as the deliberate opposite."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same picture — a city, a high wall — and one clause added at the end that pulls the whole thing down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *in his own conceit*, in his imagination. The wall exists in the mind of the man behind it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs sets the two side by side and lets the reader compare."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crushed spirit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man's spirit **can carry him through sickness**, but **a crushed spirit, who can bear it?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *carry through* is **kul** — to sustain, to hold up, to provide for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse observes something ordinary: a person's inner state affects what their body can bear, and there is a state past which nothing carries them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the second half is a question, not an answer. Proverbs leaves it open."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Pride and honour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before destruction **a heart grows proud**, but **humility comes before honour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same pairing appears at the end of chapter 15, and again at 16:18 in its most famous form — *pride goes before destruction, and a haughty spirit before a fall.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book says it three times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Answering too early"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **answer before you have listened** is folly, and it shames you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he who answers a matter before he hears it*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gift"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A gift **opens the way** before a man and ushers him into the presence of the great.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs is describing how the world works rather than recommending it. The book does the same with bribes elsewhere and condemns them plainly: *he who hates a bribe will live.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The slack workman"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The one who is slack in his work is **brother to the one who destroys**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a lesser fault, in this verse. Family."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is being contrasted.** A tower you run into, and a wall you have built out of your own resources and your own opinion of them. Proverbs puts them in consecutive lines and never says the second one falls — it just tells you where it is."}]}, "Proverbs 19:22–29": {"title": "\"What a person longs for is steady love\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first line of this passage names what everybody actually wants, and the word it uses is the covenant word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The longing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What a person longs for is **steady love**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chesed** — loyal love, covenant kindness, the word used of God's own faithfulness some two hundred and fifty times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The older rendering is *the desire of a man is his kindness*, and it has been read both ways: what a man desires is kindness, and a man's desirability is his kindness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way the quality named is **chesed** — and it is not niceness. It is the kindness of somebody keeping faith with you when they need not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the second half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **better to be poor than a liar**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pairing is the argument. Set a man's kindness against a man's means, and the proverb says which one is worth having."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same measure the whole book keeps applying: *better is a dinner of herbs where love is, than a fattened calf with hatred.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The satisfied life"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The fear of the LORD leads to life, and one who has it **rests satisfied, untouched by harm**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he abides satisfied* — lodges, stays the night, remains."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lazy man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The lazy man **buries his hand in the dish and will not even lift it to his mouth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs is funny about laziness in a way it is about nothing else. He has got as far as the food and cannot complete the journey."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Correction, and who it works on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Strike a scoffer, and **the simple learn caution**; correct a wise one, and **he gains knowledge**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three characters and three outcomes, and note who benefits from what."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Striking the scoffer does nothing for the scoffer. It teaches the onlooker."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wise man is the only one in the verse who profits from correction directed at him — which is Proverbs' definition of wisdom throughout: not knowing more, but being correctable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning about drifting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Stop listening to instruction, my son, and you will stray** from the words of knowledge.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing dramatic is described. Just stopping listening, and then drift."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The end of the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Judgements are ready for the scoffers, and beatings for the backs of fools.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that they are prepared, already established. Nothing has to be arranged; the consequence is standing by."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse in the middle of it all.** *A child who assaults his father and drives out his mother brings shame and disgrace.* Proverbs sits its bluntest sentence about family violence between a joke about a lazy man's dinner and a note on correction — the book keeps ordinary life and serious things in the same column."}]}, "Proverbs 19:8–14": {"title": "\"A sensible wife comes from the LORD\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Seven couplets, and the last one draws a line between two kinds of provision."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two sources"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*House and wealth are **handed down from parents**, but **a sensible wife comes from the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole point of the verse, and it is a contrast, not a comparison."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Property comes down a family line. It can be inherited, arranged, negotiated, insured. The other cannot."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **maskeleth** — from **sakal**, to be prudent, to have insight, to act with understanding that produces good outcomes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same root in Joshua 1:8, where meditating on the book day and night makes a man's way prosperous and gives him *good success*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is the word used of Abigail: *a woman of good sense* — **sekel** — listed before her looks. She talked a future king out of a massacre and saved a household in an afternoon. See the note on 1 Samuel 25:2–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same chapter says the opposite runs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A foolish child is his father's ruin, and **a quarrelsome wife is a leaking roof that never stops dripping**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs uses that image three times. It is not a storm. It is the drip that will not stop — and the book's complaint about it is always its relentlessness rather than its force."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The glory of overlooking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Good sense makes a person **slow to anger**, and **his glory is to overlook an offence**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *overlook* is to pass over, to let go by."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is called his **glory** — his beauty, his adornment. Not his loss, and not his weakness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The king's two moods"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A king's rage is like the roar of a lion, but **his favour is like dew on the grass**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both images are natural and neither is negotiable — you cannot argue with a lion or manufacture dew. The verse simply reports what proximity to power is like."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What opens the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever gains sense **loves his own soul**; whoever keeps understanding **will find good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Getting wisdom is described as an act of self-love, which is not the usual framing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the one about luxury"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Luxury does not suit a fool.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that delicate living is not fitting for him — it does not sit right, like good clothes on the wrong man."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Proverbs says about finding one.** *Whoever finds a wife finds a good thing, and obtains favour from the LORD.* The verb is to find, which is what you do with something you were not able to manufacture — and the favour is credited to the same source as the wife."}]}, "Proverbs 2:1–5": {"title": "If you dig for her", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One sentence, five verses long, and the whole weight is on the last two words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shape"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If… if… yes, if… if… **then**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four conditions and one result. Hebrew poetry piling up clauses before the verb arrives."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the passage can be taken alone; it is built as a single sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My son.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The father's voice, which runs through the first nine chapters. Not the aphorisms of the later collection — these are a parent talking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you **take my words to heart** and **treasure my commands within you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *if you receive my words and hide my commandments with you* — **tsaphan**, to store up, to hoard, to conceal for safekeeping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb the psalmist uses: *your word I have hidden in my heart, that I might not sin against you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Received, and then stored."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Tuning your ear to wisdom, **turning your heart toward understanding**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *tuning* is *inclining* — **qashab**, to prick up the ears, the way an animal turns its head toward a sound."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ear and heart, in that order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yes, if you **call out for insight** and **lift your voice for understanding**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Now it is audible. The first four verbs were internal; this pair involves the mouth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fourth pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you **search for her like silver**, if you **dig for her like buried treasure**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chaphas** — to search, to rummage. And *if you seek her as hidden treasures* — **matmonim**, things buried."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mining, in the ancient world, meant hacking galleries into a hillside by hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job has the description: *man puts an end to darkness, and searches every recess for ore in the darkness and the shadow of death… he cuts out channels in the rocks*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then Job asks the real question: *but where can wisdom be found?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wording"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Like silver.* Not *more than silver*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is about method, not value. Look for it the way people look for money."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then you will **understand the fear of the LORD**, and you will **find the knowledge of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things, and the first one is odd. Not *fear the LORD* — *understand* the fear of the LORD."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book has already said *the fear of the LORD is the beginning of knowledge*. Here the fear is itself the thing to be understood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is doing the giving"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse straight after the *then*:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the LORD gives wisdom**; from His mouth come knowledge and understanding.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verses of digging, and then the treasure turns out to be handed over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The searching is real and so is the giving. The passage puts them in consecutive verses without reconciling them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other place they sit together"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ask, and it will be given to you; **seek, and you will find**; knock, and it will be opened.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And James: *if any of you lacks wisdom, let him ask of God, who gives to all liberally and without reproach*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What wisdom does next in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He stores up sound wisdom for the upright; He is a shield to those who walk uprightly… then you will understand righteousness and justice, equity and every good path.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *discretion will preserve you*, and *wisdom will deliver you from the way of evil*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter is about protection, and this passage is how it starts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Proverbs says elsewhere about the search"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Happy is the man who finds wisdom… **for her proceeds are better than the profits of silver**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison to silver again, and this time it is about value."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the comparison is about.** *Search for her like silver.* Not worth more than silver — looked for the way people look for money, which is to say by digging."}]}, "Proverbs 2:6–11": {"title": "\"From His mouth come knowledge and understanding\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Wisdom in this chapter is not a skill acquired. It is something given, and the passage says where from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The source"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is the LORD who gives wisdom; from His mouth come knowledge and understanding.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is spoken — out of His mouth. Which puts the search for wisdom in the same place as the search for what He has said."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses just before this describe looking for it like silver and digging for it like hidden treasure. Then the reason that search works: the thing being dug for is being handed over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does once you have it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the run of verbs — every one of them is protective:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["He is a **shield** to those who walk with honest hearts", "He **guards** the paths of the just", "He **watches over** the way of those who love Him", "good sense will **keep watch** over you", "understanding will **guard** your steps"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five in six verses. In this chapter wisdom is not primarily about being clever. It is about being kept."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it settles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wisdom will settle into your heart, and knowledge will be a delight to your soul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *come into* your heart — it takes up residence rather than being consulted. And the result is described as pleasure, not duty: knowledge becoming something a person enjoys."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What follows immediately.** *Then you will understand what is right and fair, every good path will open before you.* The understanding of what is right comes after the wisdom is given, not before. In Proverbs the discernment is a consequence of the gift, which is why the chapter opens by telling you to go looking for it."}]}, "Proverbs 20:23–30": {"title": "The lamp of the LORD", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Eight proverbs, and one of them is about a light that goes where nobody else can look."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scales"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Unequal weights are hateful to the LORD, and **dishonest scales are not good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **even va'even** — a stone and a stone. Two sets of weights, a heavy one for buying and a light one for selling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs raises it four times, and the law forbids it: *you shall not have in your bag differing weights*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The understatement of the second clause — *not good* — is the Hebrew way of saying the opposite very firmly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The steps"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A person's **steps are directed by the LORD**; how then can anyone understand his own way?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **miYHWH mitzadei-gaver** — from the LORD are a man's steps."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question is not rhetorical despair. It follows from the first clause: if the direction comes from outside, self-analysis will not find it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man's heart plans his way, but the LORD directs his steps.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is **a trap to blurt out, \"It is holy,\"** and only later to weigh the vow.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yala qodesh** — to say rashly *holy*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dedicating something to God in a moment and doing the arithmetic afterwards."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ecclesiastes says the same: *do not be rash with your mouth… **better not to vow than to vow and not pay***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The king"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A wise king **winnows out the wicked** and drives the wheel over them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is threshing — the cart wheel driven over grain on the floor to separate it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lamp"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The human spirit is **the lamp of the LORD**, searching **every hidden room within**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ner YHWH nishmat adam** — the lamp of the LORD is the breath of man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Neshamah** — breath. The word from Genesis 2:7: *God breathed into his nostrils the breath of life*. See the note on Psalms 150:6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The searching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chophes kol-chadrei-vaten** — searching all the chambers of the belly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Cheder** — an inner room, the innermost part of a house. The word used of a bridal chamber and of a store room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the proverb claims"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The thing God breathed into a man is the light He searches him with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not an external inspection. The lamp is inside, and it is His."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things that guard a king"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Steady love and faithfulness guard the king, and **by love his throne is upheld**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chesed ve'emet** — the pair. Covenant love and faithfulness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pair that becomes *grace and truth* in John's prologue. See the note on John 1:14–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two ages"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The glory of the young is **their strength**, and the splendor of the old is **their gray head**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tipheret bachurim kocham** — the beauty of young men is their power."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hadar zeqenim sevah** — the honour of old men is grey hair."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each age is given its own word for glory, and neither is asked to have the other's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blows that wound **scrub away evil**, and beatings reach **the hidden rooms of the heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for the last phrase is **chadrei-vaten** — the same words used of what the lamp searches, four verses earlier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proverb pairs them deliberately: the lamp reaches the inner rooms, and so does pain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter opened with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wine is a mocker, strong drink is a brawler, and whoever is led astray by it is not wise.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *even a child is known by his deeds, whether what he does is pure and right*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the searching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But God has revealed them to us through His Spirit. For **the Spirit searches all things, yes, the deep things of God**.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 2:10–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For what man knows the things of a man except the spirit of the man which is in him?*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The lamp.** **Neshamah** — the breath God put into the man at the beginning. The proverb says that is the light He searches him with, and it is inside."}]}, "Proverbs 22:1–7": {"title": "\"Train up a child in the way he should go\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*Train up a child in the way he should go, and when he is old he will not turn from it.* A promise with a long fuse, and the Hebrew has more in it than the English lets through."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"According to his way\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is **al pi darko** — literally *on the mouth of his way*. There is no word for *should* in the Hebrew at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The natural sense is *according to his way*: this child, his bent, his grain, the particular shape of him. It turns the instruction from a method applied to children in general into an act of attention paid to one child in particular."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You train the child you actually have."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chanak** is used elsewhere of dedicating a house or a temple — setting something apart for the purpose it was made for. The related noun gives us Hanukkah, the feast of dedication."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the training is not primarily drilling. It is dedicating: putting a child on the path he was made for, early."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise attached"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When he is old he will not turn from it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The timescale is the striking part. Not next year. When he is old. The promise reaches past the years a parent has any influence at all, and past the stretch where it may look as if nothing took."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what makes it worth having. A promise that only covered the compliant years would be no comfort to anybody."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter opens with.** *Choose a good name over great riches.* The chapter that contains the great parenting promise begins by saying what is worth handing on, and it is not money. Four verses later: *the reward of humility and the fear of the LORD is riches and honour and life.* The inheritance being described runs through character."}]}, "Proverbs 22:8–16": {"title": "A generous eye", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Nine proverbs, and the first and last are about the same man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sowing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever **sows injustice will reap disaster**, and the rod of his fury will fail.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **zorea avlah yiqtzor-aven** — sowing iniquity, he reaps trouble."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Shevet evrato** — the rod of his wrath. The instrument he used to oppress with will break."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The eye"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever has **a generous eye will be blessed**, for **he shares his bread with the poor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tov-ayin** — good of eye."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The idiom for generosity. Its opposite is **ra-ayin**, evil of eye — the phrase Jesus uses of the labourer complaining about the landowner's payments. See the note on Matthew 20:13–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the second clause does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For he shares his bread with the poor.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The generous eye is defined by what it does. The proverb does not leave it as a disposition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scoffer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Drive out the scoffer, and **strife goes out with him**; quarreling and insults come to an end.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **letz** — the mocker, a fixed character type in Proverbs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proverb's claim is that the conflict is not distributed. Remove one man and it goes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The friend of the king"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever **loves a pure heart and speaks with grace** will have the king for a friend.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chen sephatav** — the grace of his lips."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things together: an interior condition and a manner of speaking. Court literature, addressed to young men in training."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The watch"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The eyes of the LORD **keep watch over knowledge**, but **He overturns the words of the faithless**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **natzeru da'at** — guard knowledge."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The lazy man says, \"**There is a lion outside! I will be killed in the streets!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs returns to this joke three times. The sluggard's excuses are always dramatic and always about danger elsewhere."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The lazy man buries his hand in the bowl, and will not so much as bring it to his mouth again.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The mouth of a forbidden woman is **a deep pit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Shuchah amuqqah** — a deep pit. The book's standing warning, running from chapter 2 to chapter 9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The child"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Folly is **bound up in the heart of a child**, but the rod of discipline drives it far away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **qeshurah belev-na'ar** — tied up in the heart of a boy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not learned from outside. Already there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever **oppresses the poor to enrich himself**, or **gives to the rich**, will only come to want.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **oshek dal** — oppressing the weak."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two ways of getting money wrong, in one sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Squeezing those below you, and paying tribute to those above you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both, says the proverb, arrive at the same place: **ach lemachsor** — only to lack."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What frames the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 8: the man who sows injustice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 16: the man who oppresses the poor to enrich himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The paragraph opens and closes on the same figure, and puts the generous eye second from the top."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other proverb about the eye"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man with **an evil eye hastens after riches**, and does not consider that poverty will come upon him.* See the note on Proverbs 28:21–28."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the section opens with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A good name is to be chosen rather than great riches, and loving favour rather than silver and gold.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The rich and the poor have this in common: **the LORD is the maker of them all**.* See the note on Proverbs 22:1–7."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The idiom.** **Tov-ayin** — good of eye. The proverb does not leave it as an attitude; the second half of the line says what such an eye does with bread."}]}, "Proverbs 23:1–8": {"title": "Two tables", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Eight verses about dinner, and the two meals bracket a warning about money."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first table"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When you **sit down to dine with a ruler**, consider carefully what is before you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *consider diligently what is before you*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An invitation to a great man's table is not hospitality. It is a transaction, and the guest is the one being assessed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The knife"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **put a knife to your throat** if you are a person of large appetite.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **baal nephesh** — a master of appetite, an owner of a big soul."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deliberately shocking language, and the point is self-restraint at somebody else's expense."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not crave his delicacies, for **that food is deceptive**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lechem kezavim** — bread of lies."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The food is real. What it costs is not on the menu."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pivot"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not **wear yourself out to get rich**; be wise enough to **let it go**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **al-tiga** — do not labour, do not toil to exhaustion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *cease from your own understanding*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Fix your eyes on wealth, and **it is gone**, for it **sprouts wings and flies off like an eagle to the sky**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *will you set your eyes on that which is not?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Riches are described as a thing that is not there, and then as a bird."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The eagle is the fastest thing the writer could name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second table"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not eat the bread of **a stingy man**, or crave his delicacies.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ra ayin** — one who has an evil eye."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the idiom for a miser. And it is the phrase behind the eye saying: *when your eye is clear and single, your whole body is full of light*. See the note on Luke 11:33–36."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The opposite of a *single* eye, in Hebrew, is a grudging one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For he is the kind who is **always counting the cost**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *as he thinks in his heart, so is he*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That clause has been lifted out of context for a century and a half and made to mean something about positive thinking. In its place it is about a host doing arithmetic while you eat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Eat and drink,\" he says to you, **but his heart is not with you**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The outcome"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will **vomit up the little you have eaten** and **waste your kind words on him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *your pleasant words*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two losses: the food and the conversation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the two tables have in common"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are free meals, and both cost something."}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the ruler's table the danger is your own appetite. At the miser's the danger is his resentment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proverb sets them either side of the passage about chasing wealth, and the arrangement is the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not overwork to be rich… will you set your eyes on that which is not?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And later: *do not remove the ancient landmark, nor enter the fields of the fatherless; **for their Redeemer is mighty; He will plead their cause against you***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the same appetite"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But those who desire to be rich fall into temptation and a snare… **for the love of money is a root of all kinds of evil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the alternative: *now godliness with contentment is great gain*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the money passage sits.** Between two free dinners. One tests your appetite and the other tests the host's, and the proverb puts *do not wear yourself out to get rich* in the middle."}]}, "Proverbs 23:26–35": {"title": "Give me your heart", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A father's appeal, and then the longest description of drunkenness in the Bible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My son, **give me your heart**, and let your eyes delight in my ways.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tenah beni libbecha li** — give, my son, your heart to me."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not obedience. Not attention. The heart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book has been doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-two chapters of instruction — on money, work, speech, friendship, adultery, laziness, kings and fools."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And here the father stops and asks for the one thing the instruction cannot compel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The eyes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And let **your eyes delight in my ways**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tirtzenah** — take pleasure in, be pleased with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Heart and eyes, in that order. What a man looks at follows what he loves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **a harlot is a deep pit**, and a strange woman is **a narrow well**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two images of the same thing: something you fall into and cannot climb out of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She lies in wait like a robber.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The riddle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who has **woe**? Who has **sorrow**? Who has **quarrels and complaints**? Who has **needless wounds and bloodshot eyes**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six questions in a row, with no subject named."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew opens with **oy** and **avoy** — two interjections of lament, and the second occurs only here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Those who **linger long over wine**, who go to **sample bowl after bowl**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hamachar'im al-hayyayin** — those who tarry over wine."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not gaze at wine **when it is red, when it sparkles in the cup and slides down smoothly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three attractions: colour, sparkle, and how easily it goes down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proverb concedes every one of them. It does not pretend the thing is unappealing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the end it **bites like a serpent and stings like a viper**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **acharito** — its end, its afterwards."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word Ecclesiastes uses: *the end of a thing is better than its beginning*. See the note on Ecclesiastes 7:8–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The effects"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your eyes will see strange things, and **your heart will speak crooked words**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heart again. The chapter opened by asking for it and now describes what happens to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will be like **one asleep out on the high seas, lying atop the swaying mast**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man asleep in the crow's nest in a swell."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most vivid picture of intoxication in ancient literature, and it is about the loss of any fixed point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The self-report"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**They struck me, but I felt nothing; they beat me, but I did not know it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The insensibility is stated by the man himself, which is how the proverb makes its point about what has been lost."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**When will I wake, so I can find another drink?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **osiph avaqshennu od** — I will seek it again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter ends there. No verdict, no summary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last words in the mouth of the man who has just described being beaten without noticing are about the next drink."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the appeal at the top was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give me your heart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The father asks for the heart, and then spends nine verses on what happens to a man who has given it elsewhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the New Testament says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And do not be drunk with wine, in which is dissipation; but **be filled with the Spirit**.* See the note on Ephesians 5:18–21."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The structure.** *Give me your heart* — and then nine verses on a man whose heart *speaks crooked words*, ending with him asking when he can wake up and start again."}]}, "Proverbs 28:21–28": {"title": "\"The stingy man hurries after wealth\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Eight couplets about money, and the sharpest of them is about a man in a hurry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hurry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **stingy man hurries after wealth** and never knows that want is coming for him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew behind *stingy* is *a man with an evil eye* — the idiom for a grudging, tight, envious way of looking at things. Jesus uses the same expression: *if your eye is bad, your whole body will be full of darkness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things are said about him: he hurries, and he does not know what is coming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is coming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Want.* The Hebrew is lack, poverty — and the verb has it arriving at him, coming upon him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is running toward money with the thing he is running from already on the road."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Trust and enrichment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **greedy heart stirs up strife**, but **whoever trusts in the LORD will be enriched**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two halves are the alternative to each other. A grasping heart produces conflict; trust produces increase."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *enriched* is to be made fat — the word for prosperity and fullness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gift that does not empty"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever **gives to the poor will lack nothing**, but whoever shuts his eyes to them gathers many curses.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shall not lack*. Nothing at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the opposite is not *will be poor* — it is *gathers many curses*. The man who shuts his eyes is still gathering; the chapter simply names what he is collecting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Rebuke and flattery"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever **rebukes** a man will find more favour at last than the one who **flatters** with his tongue.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At last* — afterward, in the end. The flatterer is preferred now."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set that beside a king who *never once crossed* his son, never asked *why do you do this?* See the note on 1 Kings 1:5–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Trusting your own heart"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever **trusts his own heart is a fool**, but whoever walks in wisdom will be kept safe.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the flat contradiction of the usual advice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last couplet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the wicked rise, people go into hiding, **but when they perish, the righteous increase**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter says it twice — the same line appears seven verses earlier."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the same chapter says about interest.** *Whoever swells his wealth by interest and gain **gathers it for one who is kind to the poor**.* He is still gathering. The proverb only disagrees with him about who the pile is for. See the note on Proverbs 28:7–13."}]}, "Proverbs 28:7–13": {"title": "\"Gathers it for one who is kind to the poor\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A proverb about interest that turns out to be about who ends up holding the money."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever **swells his wealth by interest and gain gathers it for one who is kind to the poor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not stopped, and he is not impoverished. He goes on swelling the pile."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proverb simply tells him whose pile it is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law said about interest"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You shall not lend him your money for interest, nor lend him your food at a profit* — Leviticus 25, about a brother who has become poor."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Taking interest from the destitute was the specific practice the law forbade, and it is the practice in view here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who receives it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One who is **kind to the poor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is the one who shows favour, who is gracious to the poor — the root of **chen**, grace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who squeezed the poor for interest is described as a collector working on behalf of a man who gives to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same idea elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wealth of the sinner is stored up for the righteous* — Proverbs 13:22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To the one who misses the mark, God gives the task of gathering and piling up, only to hand it over to the one who pleases God* — Ecclesiastes 2:26. See the note on Ecclesiastes 2:24–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three places, same claim. The gathering and the keeping are two different jobs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer that offends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If a man **turns his ear from hearing the law**, even his prayer becomes an offence.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that his prayer is an abomination. He is still praying — the proverb does not say he stops."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever leads the upright down an evil road **falls into his own pit**, but the **blameless will inherit good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pit is his. He dug it for somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rich man's eyes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The rich man is **wise in his own eyes**, but a poor man with sense **sees right through him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that the poor man searches him out. Money buys the appearance of judgement, and it does not fool a discerning man with nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the last one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever **hides** his sins will not prosper, but whoever **confesses and forsakes** them will find mercy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs on each side, and the outcome named at the end is mercy."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What connects the chapter.** Hiding. The man hides his sins, the wealthy man hides behind his own good opinion, the people hide when the wicked rise. And the one line about not hiding ends in mercy."}]}, "Proverbs 29:14–18": {"title": "\"Where there is no vision\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Five proverbs, and the last one is the famous one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The king"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A king who **judges the poor with truth** will have **his throne made sure forever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *judges the poor in truth*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stability of a throne is attached to how it treats the people with no leverage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rod"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The rod and reproof give wisdom, but **a child left to himself shames his mother**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *a child sent away* — left to his own devices."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mother is named rather than the father, which Proverbs does more than once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wicked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the wicked increase, **so does wrongdoing**, but the righteous will watch their downfall.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Discipline your child, and **he will give you rest**; he will **bring delight to your soul**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *delight* is **maadanim** — delicacies, dainties."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The famous line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where there is **no vision**, **the people cast off restraint**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *vision* is **chazon** — a prophetic vision, the disclosed word. It is the word used of Isaiah's *vision*, and of Nathan and Samuel receiving one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not imagination or planning. Revelation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *cast off restraint* is **para** — to let loose, to run wild, to go unbridled."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word used of Israel at the golden calf: *Moses saw that the people were unrestrained*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The older rendering — *the people perish* — comes from an older reading of the verb. The Hebrew is about a people breaking ranks, not about death."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the second half says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **happy is the one who keeps the law**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ashrei** — blessed, happy. The word that opens Psalm 1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse is a pair: no revealed word, and a people running loose; the law kept, and a person content."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves are about what a word does to conduct."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the word appears elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then the LORD answered me and said: \"**Write the vision** and make it plain on tablets, that he may run who reads it. For **the vision is yet for an appointed time**… though it tarries, wait for it; because **it will surely come**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Habakkuk, and the same noun."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And 1 Samuel 3, of the period before Samuel: *the word of the LORD was rare in those days; **there was no widespread revelation**.* See the note on 1 Samuel 3:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the condition the proverb describes, stated as history."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened in that period"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judges — a book whose refrain is *in those days there was no king in Israel; **everyone did what was right in his own eyes***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the proverb's first half, described from the inside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter ends on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*An unjust man is an abomination to the righteous, and he who is upright in the way is an abomination to the wicked.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last verse of Proverbs 29, and it works both ways."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the verse is about.** Not ambition or planning. **Chazon** is a word received — and the proverb's claim is that when it is absent a people does not die, it comes unbridled."}]}, "Proverbs 29:23–27": {"title": "The fear of man lays a snare", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Five proverbs near the end of the book, and three of them are about other people's opinions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A man's **pride will bring him low**, but a **humble spirit will hold on to honor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *a man's pride shall bring him low, but the humble in spirit will obtain honour*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves end somewhere the man did not aim: the proud man goes down, and the low man keeps hold of the thing the proud one was after."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus states it twice, in Luke, as a rule: *whoever exalts himself will be humbled, and he who humbles himself will be exalted*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The accomplice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever **joins a thief hates his own life**; he **hears the curse and says nothing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew refers to a specific legal procedure. Leviticus 5: a man who hears a public adjuration to give evidence and stays silent *shall bear his guilt*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proverb is about a witness in court who keeps his mouth shut, and it says the silence is self-harm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The snare"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **fear of man lays a snare**, but **whoever trusts in the LORD is kept safe**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *snare* is **moqesh** — a bird trap, the trigger stick that springs it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A trap only works if the bird does not know it is there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the trap catches people in the Bible"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saul: *I have sinned, for I have transgressed the commandment of the LORD… **because I feared the people and obeyed their voice***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pilate, *wanting to gratify the crowd*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the rulers in John who believed but would not say so, *for they loved the praise of men more than the praise of God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aaron at the golden calf, and Peter at Antioch — *fearing those who were of the circumcision*, so that Paul withstood him to his face."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list is long and it includes almost every kind of person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The safety"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yesuggav** — set on high, put out of reach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same root as the tower in an earlier proverb: *the name of the LORD is a strong tower; the righteous run to it and are safe*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The trap is at ground level. The alternative is elevation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ruler"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Many seek **the ruler's favor**, but **justice for a man comes from the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *many seek the ruler's face*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not wrong to seek it. The proverb says only that the verdict comes from elsewhere, so the queue at the palace door is aimed at the wrong desk."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph and Daniel both had a ruler's favour, and Genesis and Daniel both attribute it to God rather than to the king."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The unjust are **an abomination to the righteous**, and the upright are **an abomination to the wicked**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Symmetrical, and the second half is the sting. The disgust runs both ways."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anybody expecting to be liked by everyone is being told, in the last proverb of the chapter, that it does not work like that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the five sit together"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pride, silence in court, fear of man, seeking the ruler's face, and mutual abomination."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one is about what other people think, and the collection puts them in a row."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the snare needs to work.** Not to be seen. The proverb names the trap and, in the same line, gives the other option — trusting Him, and being set up out of reach."}]}, "Proverbs 3:1–4": {"title": "\"Tie them around your neck\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A father tells his son to keep two things close, and describes where to keep them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not let love and faithfulness leave you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew pair is **hesed and emet** — covenant love and dependability. It is one of the standard descriptions of God's own character, the words He speaks over Himself when He passes by Moses on the mountain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the son is being told to wear what God is like."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two places"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Tie them around your neck, write them on the tablet of your heart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Outside and inside. Around the neck is visible — an ornament, or the cord a seal hung on. On the tablet of the heart is where nobody else sees."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both, and the order matters: the visible one is named first, but the inward one is the permanent one. Jeremiah later promises a covenant written on hearts rather than stone, using the same image."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will add to your days, long years and a peaceful life… you will find favour and good name in the sight of God and everyone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Favour with both — God and people. Luke uses almost exactly this phrasing of Jesus growing up in Nazareth: increasing in favour with God and with people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not forget my teaching, let your heart hold on to my commands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *learn* — do not forget. The instruction assumes it has already been given. Much of Proverbs is a father repeating what a son has heard before, on the understanding that knowing something and keeping hold of it are different jobs."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes three verses later.** *Trust in the LORD with all your heart and do not lean on your own understanding.* This paragraph is the run-up to it, and it is worth reading them together: hold on to what you were taught, and do not rely on your own reading of the situation."}]}, "Proverbs 3:13–20": {"title": "What is in wisdom's two hands", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Wisdom is described as a person holding two things, and the contents of each hand are listed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed is the one who **finds** wisdom.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Finding, not manufacturing. It is the verb for coming across something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparisons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Worth more than **silver**… yields more than **gold**… more precious than **jewels**, and **nothing you desire compares to her**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three commodities, escalating, and then a clause that removes the possibility of any further comparison."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two hands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Long life is in her **right hand**, riches and honour in her **left**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The right hand in that world was the hand of the primary gift."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the arrangement is: **length of days** is what she is chiefly holding out, and **riches and honour** are in the other hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are there. The passage does not disparage the second pair — it ranks them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The paths"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Her ways are **ways of pleasantness**, and all her paths are **peace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *pleasantness* is **noam** — agreeableness, delight, beauty. And peace is **shalom**, wholeness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tree"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She is **a tree of life** to those who take hold of her, and blessed are all who **will not let her go**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first mention of that tree since Genesis 3, where the way to it was barred by a flaming sword."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs of grip: take hold, and do not let go."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What wisdom built"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By **wisdom** the LORD laid the earth's foundations, by **understanding** He set the heavens in place. By His **knowledge** the deep waters burst open.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same three words the passage has been recommending are used of the creation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever wisdom is, the argument runs, it is the thing the universe was made with — which is why a man who gets it is working with the grain of things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs 8 develops it at length: *the LORD possessed me at the beginning of His way… when He prepared the heavens, I was there.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Solomon asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Give to Your servant an understanding heart to judge Your people.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer: *because you have asked this thing, and have not asked long life for yourself, nor riches… **I have also given you what you have not asked**: both riches and honour.* See the note on 1 Kings 3:5–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked for what was in the right hand. What was in the left came anyway."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul locates it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ, **in whom every treasure of wisdom and knowledge lies hidden**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Colossians 2, and the Greek is *hidden* — stored up, concealed, the way treasure is. See the note on Colossians 2:1–3."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter says four verses earlier.** *Honour the LORD with all you have, with the first and best of everything you gather.* And four verses later, *do not despise the LORD's discipline.* Proverbs 3 puts firstfruits, correction and wisdom on the same page. See the note on Proverbs 3:9–12."}]}, "Proverbs 3:9–12": {"title": "\"The first and best of everything\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four verses, and the second pair is not what the first pair leads you to expect."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Honour the LORD with all you have, with **the first and best of everything you gather**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **reshith** — the beginning, the first, the choicest. It is the word for firstfruits, and it is the first word of the Bible: *in the beginning*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things are specified: that it is **first**, and that it is **best**. Not what is left over, and not the ordinary portion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then your **barns will overflow**, your **vats will brim with new wine**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *overflow* is to be filled to bursting. And the vats *burst out* with new wine — the verb is used of a wall breaking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grain and wine, the two staples of that economy, and both described as more than the container can hold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, without any transition:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My son, **do not despise the LORD's discipline**, and do not grow weary of His correction.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the next verse. Overflowing barns, and then a word about correction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs sets them side by side deliberately. Both are how a father treats a son — the provision and the correcting come from the same relationship."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for discipline"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Musar** — instruction, chastening, the whole business of forming a child. It is the Hebrew equivalent of the Greek **paideia** that runs through Hebrews 12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews quotes this exact passage: *my son, do not despise the chastening of the LORD, nor be discouraged when you are rebuked by Him; for whom the LORD loves He chastens.* See the note on Hebrews 12:5–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the LORD corrects **the one He loves**, as a father the son **in whom he delights**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves name affection. The correction is evidence of the relationship, not a withdrawal of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the two halves belong together"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who reads only the first two verses gets a formula: give the first and best, receive overflow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter will not let him stop there. The same Father who fills the barns also corrects, and the son is told not to despise either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter opens with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Trust in the LORD with all your heart, and lean not on your own understanding; in all your ways acknowledge Him, and He shall direct your paths.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five verses before this one. The honouring with firstfruits is one instance of the acknowledging."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Proverbs says elsewhere about the arithmetic.** *There is one who scatters, yet increases more; and there is one who withholds more than is right, but it leads to poverty.* The book keeps insisting the sums do not work the way they look like they should."}]}, "Proverbs 31:10–12": {"title": "A woman of valour", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last twenty-two verses of Proverbs, and the first word sets the terms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An acrostic. Twenty-two verses, each beginning with the next letter of the Hebrew alphabet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A finished, formal poem — the book's closing set piece."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who wrote it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The words of King Lemuel, the utterance which his mother taught him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lemuel is unidentified. And the poem before the acrostic is his mother's instruction to him: *do not give your strength to women… open your mouth for the speechless, in the cause of all who are appointed to die*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A queen mother telling a son what to look for, and what to be."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **woman of valor**, who can find her?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **eshet chayil**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chayil** — strength, ability, worth, army, wealth. The whole range sits in one noun."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word for Gideon — *mighty man of valour*. It is the word for the *capable men* of Obed-edom's household, *strong for the work*. See the note on 1 Chronicles 26:6–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Translating it *virtuous* narrows it to morals. The Hebrew is closer to *formidable*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who can find her?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A rhetorical opening, the same shape as *who has believed our report?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The value"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She is worth **far more than rubies**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **peninim** — rubies, or corals, or pearls. The comparison is standard in the book: wisdom is *more precious than rubies*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trust"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Her husband's heart **rests safe in her**, and **he is never left wanting**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **batach** — trusts, leans on, is confident. The security word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is never left wanting* — the Hebrew is *he shall have no lack of gain*, and **shalal** is plunder, spoil. A military word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The duration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She brings him **good and not harm all the days of her life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not most days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does in the rest of the poem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Buys a field and plants a vineyard from her own earnings. Trades. Works wool and flax. Gets up before dawn. Reaches out to the poor. Makes and sells linen garments. Speaks with wisdom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Strength and honour are her clothing; she shall rejoice in time to come.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is read"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In observant Jewish homes the husband sings **Eshet Chayil** to his wife at the table on Friday night, before the Sabbath meal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A poem about competence, sung weekly, for centuries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is called this elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ruth. Boaz says it to her at the threshing floor: *all the people of my town know that you are **a virtuous woman***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase, **eshet chayil** — said to a Moabite widow gleaning in a field."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the book of Ruth sits immediately after Proverbs in the Hebrew ordering, so the poem's question — *who can find her?* — is followed by a book about a man who did."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Charm is deceitful and beauty is passing, but **a woman who fears the LORD, she shall be praised**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give her of the fruit of her hands, and **let her own works praise her in the gates**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gates are where the elders sat and the town's business was done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book ends by sending a woman's reputation into the public square on the strength of what she made."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word the poem opens with.** *Chayil* — the word used of Gideon and of an army. Not *virtuous* in the narrow sense; closer to *formidable*."}]}, "Proverbs 4:1–9": {"title": "What his father told him", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A father quoting his own father, which is the only place in Proverbs this happens."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Listen, **my children**, to a father's teaching.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is plural — sons."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The credential"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **I too was a son once, to my father**, tender, **the only child of my mother**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **rak veyachid** — tender and only."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not speaking from authority. He is speaking as somebody who was on the other side of this conversation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **he taught me and said**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything from here to verse 9 is the grandfather's words, quoted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "If the traditional attribution holds, this is Solomon quoting David."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Let your heart hold fast to my words**, keep my commands and you will live.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The imperatives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Get wisdom, get understanding**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **qeneh** — acquire, buy, get possession of. The verb for a purchase."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice in two verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Wisdom is where everything begins**, so get wisdom, and **with all you have, get understanding**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **reshit chokhmah** — the beginning, or the chief part, of wisdom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *with all your getting* — **bekhol-qinyanekha**, with everything you have acquired."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Spend the lot on it, if that is what it takes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not forsake her, and **she will protect you**; love her, and **she will keep you safe**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shamar** — guard, keep watch over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The relationship is reciprocal in the passage. Every instruction to the son has a corresponding action from wisdom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exchange"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Prize her, and she will lift you up**; embrace her, and **she will bring you honor**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *prize* is **salsel** — exalt, esteem highly. A rare word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **chabbeq** — embrace, hug."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wisdom is personified as a woman throughout the first nine chapters of Proverbs, and this is the most affectionate the language gets."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crown"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"She will place **a graceful wreath on your head**, she will **crown you with beauty**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **livyat chen** — a garland of grace. And **ateret tipheret** — a crown of glory."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase used earlier: *my son, hear the instruction of your father… for they will be a graceful ornament on your head, and chains about your neck*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the father does not offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Money, position, or an inheritance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He hands over the thing he was given, and it is a way of thinking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Keep your heart with all diligence, for out of it spring the issues of life.\"* See the note on Proverbs 4:20–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *the path of the just is like the shining sun, that shines ever brighter unto the perfect day*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Solomon asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Give to Your servant an understanding heart to judge Your people, that I may discern between good and evil.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the answer: *because you have asked this thing, and have not asked long life for yourself, nor riches… behold, I have also given you what you have not asked*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Riches and honour arrived because they were not the request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the New Testament puts wisdom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ Jesus, who became for us wisdom from God — and righteousness and sanctification and redemption.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 1:26–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *in whom every treasure of wisdom and knowledge lies hidden*. See the note on Colossians 2:1–3."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Whose words these are.** A father telling his sons what his own father told him — the one place in the book where the teaching is traced back a generation."}]}, "Proverbs 4:10–19": {"title": "Two paths", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A father's instruction, and it ends by comparing two roads by how much you can see on them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Listen, **my son**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs 4 opens *hear, my children, the instruction of a father*, and then does something unusual — Solomon quotes his own father teaching him:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When I was my father's son, tender and the only one in the sight of my mother, he also taught me, and said to me, \"**Let your heart retain my words**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three generations in one chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Take my words to heart, and **the years of your life will be many**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When you **walk**, nothing will hold you back; when you **run**, you will not stumble.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *hold back* is about a stride being cramped — the picture is of a path wide enough to move on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Walking and running are separated deliberately: the first is about obstruction, the second about speed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grip"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hold on to my instruction, **do not let it go**, guard it well, **for it is your life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four imperatives in one line, and the last clause gives the reason: not *it will improve your life*. It **is** your life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four ways of avoiding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not **set foot** on the path of the wicked, do not **walk** in the way of evil. **Avoid** it, do not travel on it, **turn away** from it and pass on by.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five clauses saying one thing. Proverbs rarely repeats itself this hard."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The counsel is not about resisting on the road. It is about not being on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the wicked cannot do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For they **cannot sleep unless they do wrong**, they **lose their rest unless they trip someone up**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *their sleep is taken away unless they cause someone to fall*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An appetite that has to be fed before the day can end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Their diet"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **feed on the bread of wickedness**, they **drink the wine of violence**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Staple food and drink. It is not an occasional thing; it is what they live on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first road"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the path of the righteous is like the dawn, growing brighter and brighter until full day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the light of dawn that shines ever brighter unto the perfect day*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a floodlight switched on. First light, and then more of it, on a curve."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The measure of the road is how much can be seen from it, and it increases."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second road"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The way of the wicked is **like deep darkness**; they **cannot even see what makes them fall**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **aphelah** — thick darkness, gloom."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second clause is the sting. Not that they fall — that they never find out what over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two comparisons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both roads are described by visibility rather than by comfort or outcome. One gets brighter; on the other the cause of the fall stays invisible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep your heart **with all diligence**, for out of it spring the issues of life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then a run of instructions about the parts of a body: the mouth, the eyes, the eyelids, the feet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ponder the path of your feet, and let all your ways be established.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *established* is **kun** — fixed, set firm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the dawn image goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until the day dawns and **the morning star rises in your hearts**.* — Peter, of the prophetic word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Malachi: *the Sun of Righteousness shall arise with healing in His wings.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the two roads are told apart.** Not by where they lead. Proverbs measures them by the light on them — one brightening toward noon, and one where a man cannot see what tripped him."}]}, "Proverbs 4:20–27": {"title": "\"Health to the whole body\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A father tells his son what to do with words, and then works down the body part by part."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the words are called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They are life to those who find them, and **health to the whole body**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *health* is **marpe** — healing, a cure, and by extension soundness. It is used of a physician's remedy."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the phrase is literally *to all his flesh*. Not to his mind or his character. His flesh."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How to keep them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs in two lines: *pay attention… lean your ear in close… do not let them slip from your sight*, and then the fourth, which is the destination — *keep them deep within your heart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ear, eye, and then inside. The route is specified."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Then the body, in order"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the heart** — *above all else, guard it, for from it flow the springs of life*", "**the mouth** — put away twisted speech", "**the eyes** — look straight ahead", "**the feet** — give careful thought to where you step"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Heart first, and everything after it flows from that. The Hebrew for *springs* is the word for the outgoings or sources of a thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Guard your heart\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is a sentry's word — to watch over, to keep under guard. It assumes something is trying to get in, or out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given is not sentimental. It is hydraulic: whatever is in there is what will come out everywhere else."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the passage ends.** *Do not turn to the right or the left.* The same phrase Joshua was given about the book of the law. The chapter is not describing a mood or an atmosphere — it is describing a road, walked deliberately, by somebody watching where their feet go."}]}, "Proverbs 5:15–23": {"title": "\"Drink water from your own cistern\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A father's warning to a son, and the middle of it turns into something warmer than a warning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Drink water from **your own cistern**, fresh water from **your own well**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Water rights were the most valuable thing a household owned in that country. A cistern was cut into rock at real expense and belonged to the family who dug it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole passage runs on the metaphor without ever dropping it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Should **your springs overflow in the streets**, your streams of water **in the public squares**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Water spilled into a street is water lost. The image is of something valuable running away where anybody can use it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let them be **yours alone**, never to be shared with strangers.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*May **your fountain be blessed**, and may you **rejoice in the wife of your youth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *let your fountain be blessed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The wife of your youth** — the phrase turns up in Malachi as well, in the passage about God hating divorce: *she is your companion and your wife by covenant*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The animals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **loving doe**, a **graceful deer**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *a loving hind, a graceful doe*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs is not usually tender. This is the exception, and it sits in the middle of a chapter about avoiding an adulteress."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let her tenderness **fill you always**, be **forever captivated by her love**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shagah** — to be intoxicated, to reel, to go astray in the sense of being carried away."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same word used two verses later of the man who is captivated by somebody else's wife. Proverbs uses one verb for both and lets the direction decide."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Why be **captivated, my son, by another woman**? Why **embrace the arms of a stranger**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same verb. The question is not whether a man will be carried away — the passage assumes he will — but by whom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The witness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **a man's ways are in full view of the LORD**, and **He watches every path he takes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *He ponders all his goings* — **palas**, to weigh, to make level."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the chapter is framed as a secret that might be discovered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trap"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wicked are **trapped by their own sins**, held fast **in the cords of their own guilt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody else does the tying. The image is of a man caught in rope he made."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job's friend Elihu uses the same picture of cords. See the note on Job 36:8–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ending"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will **die for want of discipline**, led astray **by their own great folly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *led astray* is **shagah** again — the third use in the chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Carried away by the wife, carried away by the stranger, and carried away to death. One verb, three times, and the whole chapter turns on where the man lets it take him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter opened with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the lips of an immoral woman drip honey, and her mouth is smoother than oil; **but in the end she is bitter as wormwood**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm puts the same picture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your wife within your house will be like a fruitful vine.* See the note on Psalms 128:3–4."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word the chapter is built on.** *Shagah* — to be carried away. Proverbs uses it of the wife, the stranger, and the ruin, and never suggests the son will avoid being carried away by something."}]}, "Proverbs 9:10–12": {"title": "\"Where wisdom begins\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The sentence the whole book is built on, stated here for the second time — it opened the collection back in chapter 1."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Begins\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The fear of the LORD is where wisdom begins.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word can mean the starting point or the chief part, and both senses are usually intended. It is the first thing and the principal thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What the verse rules out is treating wisdom as a technique that could be acquired by someone starting anywhere. There is a door, and this is it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second line defines the first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And knowing the Holy One is understanding.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrew poetry works by pairing lines that illuminate each other. So *fear of the LORD* is set beside *knowing the Holy One* — which keeps the fear from collapsing into terror."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the awe of someone who knows who they are dealing with. You cannot be in awe of someone you do not know at all."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who carries the result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you are wise, your wisdom rewards you. If you mock, you carry the weight alone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An unusually stark note to end on. Wisdom is described as paying its owner — and the mocker is not threatened with punishment so much as told he will bear it by himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both outcomes land on the person who chose them. Nobody else absorbs either."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it sits in the chapter.** Proverbs 9 stages two dinner invitations — Wisdom has built her house and set her table, and Folly sits at her door calling to passers-by. Both call the same people. This verse is at the hinge, and the chapter is asking which house you eat in."}]}, "Psalms 1:1–3": {"title": "Three verbs going downhill, and a tree that does not", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm that opens the whole book is built on a progression most readers walk straight past."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Walk, stand, sit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Won't walk in step with the wicked… won't stop and stand where sinners loiter… won't settle into the seat of the scornful.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three postures, and they descend: moving alongside, then stopping, then sitting down. The company gets worse as the posture gets more settled — wicked, then sinners, then scoffers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a description of how a thing that starts as company ends as identity, and it is arranged so you can see the slope."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for meditating"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Turning it over softly, day and night.* The Hebrew is **hagah** — the same word in Joshua 1:8, and it is audible: a lion growling, a dove moaning, a person muttering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set against the three verbs above, it is what fills the space they left. The happy person is not defined by avoidance; something else has their attention."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tree"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rooted beside flowing streams… offers its fruit right on time, whose leaves never wither.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two details are easy to miss. The tree is **planted** — the Hebrew is passive, transplanted by someone. And the fruit comes *in its season*, which is a real qualification: not constantly, not on demand."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"Whatever that one does comes into bloom.\"** The line has been used to promise success in any undertaking, which the psalm does not support — it is a tree image, and trees do not prosper by trying. What the psalm claims is that a life rooted in the right place bears what it is meant to bear, on time, and does not dry out when the heat comes. Jeremiah 17 uses the same picture and spells that out: it does not fear when heat comes, and in drought it does not stop bearing."}]}, "Psalms 1:4–6": {"title": "Only chaff", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm has spent three verses on a tree, and now says what the other man is like."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wicked are **nothing like this**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lo-ken haresha'im** — not so the wicked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Septuagint doubles it: *not so, not so*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the first half said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He shall be like **a tree planted by the rivers of water**, that brings forth its fruit in its season, whose leaf also shall not wither; and whatever he does shall prosper.* See the note on Psalms 1:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Planted, watered, fruiting on schedule, green."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They are only **chaff, loose husks the wind scatters away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kammotz asher-tiddephennu ruach** — like chaff which the wind drives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What chaff is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The husk left after threshing. The grain is beaten loose on the floor, then thrown into the air — the heavy kernel drops and the light casing blows off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not evil. It is weightless, and it was attached to something that mattered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pairing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A tree with roots in water, against something with no root and no weight."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole contrast is about whether a thing is anchored."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The judgment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So when **the reckoning** comes, the wicked **will not stand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **bammishpat** — in the judgment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Lo-yaqumu** — will not rise, will not stand up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The assembly"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Nor **sinners find a place among the gathered righteous**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ba'adat tzaddiqim** — in the congregation of the righteous."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is a court or an assembly with standing membership. Chaff cannot be counted in it because chaff is not there when the wind has finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the LORD keeps watch over the way of the righteous**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yodea YHWH derek tzaddiqim** — the LORD knows the way of the righteous."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yada** — knows, in the sense of relationship rather than information. The word used of Adam and Eve, and of *you only have I known of all the families of the earth*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other way"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the way of the wicked simply fades and is gone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **toved** — perishes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two ways are not given symmetrical treatment. One is known; the other is not destroyed by an active verb — it simply ends."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the two verbs do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm's whole argument is in the last verse. The difference between the tree and the chaff is not primarily behaviour. It is that one way is known and one is not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chaff turns up again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"His winnowing fan is in His hand, and He will thoroughly clean out His threshing floor, and gather His wheat into the barn; **but He will burn up the chaff with unquenchable fire**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John the Baptist, at the Jordan, to the crowds coming out to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 1 has no title, no author, and no petition. It is not addressed to God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It stands at the front of the book as a doorway: two ways, and the reader has to walk through it to get to the other hundred and forty-nine."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the second psalm does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Why do the nations rage… against the LORD and against His Anointed?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed are all those who put their trust in Him.* See the note on Psalms 2:10–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 1 opens with **ashrei** — blessed — and Psalm 2 closes with the same word. The two are a pair."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The image.** Chaff is not wicked; it is weightless. The contrast with the tree is not about behaviour but about whether a thing has roots in water."}]}, "Psalms 103:1–2": {"title": "\"O my soul\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David opens by talking to himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being addressed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bless the LORD, **O my soul**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not God, and not the congregation. He turns to his own inner life and gives it an instruction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does this four times — twice at the start and twice at the end, closing the whole thing in the same words it opened with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Barak** — to bless, and the root sense is to kneel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "To bless God is not to confer something on Him. It is a posture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Everything"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **all that is within me**, bless His holy name.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *all my inward parts*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing exempted. He is calling the whole interior to do one thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bless **His holy name**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not His gifts. The psalm gets to those in the next verse and spends the rest of itself on them, but it starts at the name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bless the LORD, O my soul, and **do not forget a single one of His kindnesses**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *forget not all His benefits* — **gemul**, a dealing, a recompense, what someone has done for you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it needs saying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forgetting is the Old Testament's standing charge against Israel. Deuteronomy warns of it repeatedly: *beware, lest you forget the LORD who brought you out of the land of Egypt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 106 states it flatly: *they soon forgot His works.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David does not assume he is immune. He instructs his own soul against it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It lists them, so the forgetting has something to work against:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who **forgives** all your iniquities, who **heals** all your diseases, who **redeems** your life from destruction, who **crowns** you with lovingkindness and tender mercies, who **satisfies** your mouth with good things.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five participles in a row, all present tense, all God's. See the note on Psalms 103:3–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As far as the east is from the west, so far has He removed our transgressions from us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As a father pities his children, so the LORD pities those who fear Him. For **He knows our frame; He remembers that we are dust**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm that begins by telling the soul not to forget says that God remembers what we are made of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bless the LORD, all you His angels… Bless the LORD, all you His hosts… Bless the LORD, all His works, in all places of His dominion. **Bless the LORD, O my soul!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It widens out to the angels, the hosts and the whole creation, and then comes back to the one soul it started with."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who gives the order.** He does, to himself. The psalm's first move is a man deciding what his own inside is going to do."}]}, "Psalms 103:3–5": {"title": "\"Your youth is renewed like the eagle's\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Five things listed in three verses, and David tells himself not to forget any of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *Bless the LORD, O my soul, and **forget not all His benefits**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is talking to himself, and the list that follows is what he is refusing to forget."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*He **forgives** all your wrongdoing*", "*He **heals** all your sicknesses*", "*He **redeems** your life from the pit*", "*He **crowns** you with steadfast love and mercy*", "*He **fills** your life with good things*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "**All**, twice. All your wrongdoing and all your sicknesses, and the two sit in consecutive lines as a matched pair — the same *all* over both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Forgiveness and healing together"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrew poetry pairs lines deliberately. These two are not a list of unrelated items; they are set side by side because they belong together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same pairing turns up when the paralysed man is let down through the roof: *your sins are forgiven* first, and then *rise, take up your bed.* And in Isaiah 53, *wounded for our transgressions* and *by His stripes we are healed* are two clauses of one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Redeems your life from the pit\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ga'al** — the kinsman-redeemer's word, the relative whose duty was to buy back what a family member had lost."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Crowns you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is to encircle, to surround. **Steadfast love** is **chesed**, the covenant word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Crowned with it — worn on the head, visible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The renewal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **fills your life with good things**, so **your youth is renewed like the eagle's**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *fills* is to satisfy, and the older rendering is *satisfies your mouth with good things*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Youth** is **na'ar** — the word for a young man, a lad. The season of natural strength."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb *renewed* is **chadash**, to make new, to restore. It is the same root as the new moon, and the new covenant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The eagle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah uses the same bird for the same idea: *those who wait on the LORD shall renew their strength; they shall **mount up with wings like eagles**.* See the note on Isaiah 40:29–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An eagle is long-lived and, in the ancient picture, moults and comes back with new feathers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm says a few lines on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As far as the east is from the west, so far has He removed our transgressions from us. As a father pities his children, so the LORD pities those who fear Him. For He knows our frame; **He remembers that we are dust**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same psalm that promises renewed youth also says He has not forgotten what we are made of."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What David says about the source.** *Who satisfies…* — every verb in the list has the same subject, and it is never the reader. Five things, one actor."}]}, "Psalms 105:12–15": {"title": "\"Do not touch My anointed ones\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm looks back at the patriarchs and describes how small and exposed they were."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When they were still **only a handful, few, and strangers** in it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words for it: a handful, few, and foreigners."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham, Isaac and Jacob were nomads with tents and livestock, owning no land except a burial cave, in a country full of established kingdoms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wandering **from nation to nation, from one kingdom to another people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Constant movement, through territory belonging to other people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of those crossings was an occasion to be robbed, absorbed or killed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What did not happen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **let no one do them harm**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that He did not permit any man to oppress them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How He did it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For their sake **He warned kings**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm says the protection worked by God speaking to the men who could have harmed them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis records two occasions. Abimelech, warned in a dream about Sarah — *you are a dead man because of the woman you have taken.* And Laban, overtaking Jacob: *God had come to Laban the Syrian in a dream by night and said, \"Be careful that you speak to Jacob neither good nor bad.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both times the protection is a conversation the patriarch never hears about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not touch My anointed ones. Do My prophets no harm.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two titles for men who held neither office in any formal sense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Anointed ones** — *meshichai*, the plural of the word behind Messiah. Nobody had poured oil on Abraham."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Prophets** — and Genesis does use it once, of Abraham, in God's own words to Abimelech: *he is a prophet, and he will pray for you and you shall live.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they were being protected for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm's next verses go straight into Joseph, sold as a slave, *his feet hurt with fetters, he was laid in irons* — *until the time that his word came to pass.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The protection did not mean an easy passage. Joseph is in the same psalm, in chains."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundred and forty-five verses of history in Psalms 105 and 106, and this one is the positive telling — a catalogue of what God did, verb after verb, with almost nothing about what Israel did."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Its purpose is stated at the top: *remember His marvellous works which He has done.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it ends up.** *He brought His people out with silver and gold, and not one of their tribes stumbled.* The handful of wandering strangers become a nation walking out of Egypt. See the note on Psalms 105:37–38."}]}, "Psalms 105:37–38": {"title": "\"Not one of their tribes stumbled\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm is a retelling of Israel's history, and this is its summary of the exodus in two lines."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it records"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He brought His people out with silver and gold, and not one of their tribes stumbled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two claims. They left with wealth — the payment described in Exodus 11 and 12 — and the whole company got out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew behind *stumbled* means to totter or be feeble. The psalmist is describing a mass departure of perhaps a very large number of people, including the old and the very young, in which nobody fell behind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the whole of Psalm 105 and the pattern is unmistakable: it is a catalogue of things God did, verb after verb, with almost nothing about what Israel did. He sent, He turned, He spoke, He brought out, He spread a cloud, He opened the rock."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm's purpose is stated at the start — remember, give thanks, tell what He has done. This verse is one line in a very long list of that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had done the night before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse is not describing a crowd that happened to be in good health. It is describing the morning after Passover."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every household in that nation had, the previous evening, eaten a lamb — *roasted with fire… his head with his legs and with the appurtenance thereof*, the whole animal, and the instruction was to eat it, not merely to kill it. The blood went on the doorframe and the lamb went inside them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They ate the lamb, and they came out the next morning with nobody feeble among them. The psalm puts the two facts side by side because they belong side by side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scale of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "However many they were, the number included the elderly, the newborn, and everyone in between — a whole population that had been in hard labour under Egyptian taskmasters for generations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of them stumbled. Not one lagged behind. That is what is being recorded, and the psalm records it as a thing God did."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The lamb and the table.** Paul calls Him *Christ our Passover*, sacrificed for us, and the table He left His disciples is the table of that lamb. The bread is His body, broken. What the night of the exodus produced in Israel is what the body was given for. See the notes on 1 Corinthians 11:23–26 and Matthew 8:14–17."}]}, "Psalms 107:1–3": {"title": "\"Let the rescued ones say it out loud\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm opens by telling a particular group of people to speak."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give thanks to the LORD, **for He is good. His steadfast love never runs out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chesed** — the covenant word, loyal love."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Never runs out* is the Hebrew *endures forever*, the refrain of Psalm 136, said twenty-six times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is instructed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let **the rescued ones** say it out loud, **the ones He bought back**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the redeemed of the LORD* — **ga'al**, the kinsman-redeemer's word, the relative whose duty was to buy back what a family member had lost."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction is not addressed to everybody. It is for people who have been got out of something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Say it out loud\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is simply *let them say so*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm's entire method rests on this line: what follows is four accounts of rescue, and the reader is told at the top to be one of the voices."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they came from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Gathered from the countries, **from sunrise and sunset, from the far north and the sea in the south**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four directions, and the phrasing is not the usual compass list — east and west by the sun, and then north and the sea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are being brought back from everywhere they were scattered to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four stories, each with the same structure:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the wanderers** — lost in a desert, hungry and thirsty, no city to live in", "**the prisoners** — in darkness and irons, because they rebelled", "**the sick** — fools, afflicted because of their transgressions, near the gates of death", "**the sailors** — in a storm, with every skill swallowed up. See the note on Psalms 107:25–27."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "And each one gets the same two refrains: *then they cried out to the LORD in their trouble, and He delivered them*, and *oh, that men would give thanks to the LORD for His goodness.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the four have in common"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two of the four are explicitly their own fault — the prisoners *rebelled against the words of God*, and the sick are *fools, because of their transgression*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not sort them. All four cry, and all four are brought out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it is placed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 107 opens Book Five of the Psalter, straight after the end of Book Four, and it reads as a homecoming song for people back from exile."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line.** *Whoever is wise will observe these things, and **they will understand the lovingkindness of the LORD**.* The psalm says the way to understand **chesed** is to look at four rescues and notice what they have in common."}]}, "Psalms 107:19–20": {"title": "\"He sent out His word and healed them\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The third of four rescues in the psalm, and all four have the same shape."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The psalm's pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 107 tells four stories: travellers lost in a desert, prisoners in iron chains, sick men who could not eat, and sailors in a storm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each one repeats the same two lines:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then they **cried to the LORD in their trouble**, and He **saved them out of their distress**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *Oh, that men would give thanks to the LORD for His goodness, and for His wonderful works to the children of men!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four groups, four crises, one response, one deliverance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third group"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before describe them: *fools, because of their transgression, and because of their iniquities, were afflicted. **Their soul abhorred all manner of food, and they drew near to the gates of death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not soften how they got there. It says their own way brought it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then they cried to the LORD **in their trouble**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not after sorting themselves out. In it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is the ordinary verb for shouting for help."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was sent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **sent out His word** and healed them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **davar** — word, matter, thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a doctor, a remedy, or an instruction. A word, sent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm uses the same construction elsewhere for creation: *by the word of the LORD the heavens were made.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lifting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **lifted them clear of the pit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *delivered them from their destructions* — **shechitah**, the pit, the grave."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had drawn near the gates of death in verse 18, and this is the reverse motion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Healed, and lifted out. The psalm gives both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the sending goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**He sent His word to Jacob**\"*, and John's opening: *In the beginning was the Word… **and the Word became flesh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm's phrase is what John's Gospel makes a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the centurion said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Only **speak a word**, and my servant will be healed.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus called it faith greater than any He had found in Israel — and the man's argument was that he understood how orders work at a distance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chorus"}, {"k": "p", "t": "After each rescue: *Oh, that men would **give thanks** to the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And after this one specifically: *let them **sacrifice the sacrifices of thanksgiving**, and declare His works with rejoicing.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the psalm opens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Oh, give thanks to the LORD, for He is good! For His mercy endures forever. **Let the redeemed of the LORD say so**, whom He has redeemed from the hand of the enemy.* See the note on Psalms 107:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever is wise will **observe these things**, and they will understand the lovingkindness of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one thing all four groups do.** Cry out. The psalm does not distinguish between the ones who were lost, imprisoned, sick by their own fault, or caught in weather — it gives them the same sentence, four times."}]}, "Psalms 107:25–27": {"title": "\"Every skill they had was swallowed up\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The fourth of four rescue stories in the psalm, and this one is at sea."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who sent it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He spoke, and **a storm wind rose up**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm attributes the storm to a word. It does not describe weather that happened to arrive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The motion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Up to the sky they went, then **down into the depths**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that they go up to the heavens and down to the deeps."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What melts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their **courage melting** in the danger.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *their soul melted because of trouble* — the same verb used of hearts melting before an army."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sailors"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **reeled and staggered like drunk men**, and **every skill they had was swallowed up**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last clause is the point of the passage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "These are professionals — verse 23 says *they that go down to the sea in ships, that do business in great waters.* Seamanship is what they have."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm says their competence was swallowed. Not overwhelmed by fear; used up, and insufficient."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then they **cried out to the LORD in their trouble**, and He brought them out of their distresses.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the refrain, and it appears four times in this psalm — after the wanderers in the desert, the prisoners in irons, the fools who were sick because of their transgressions, and now the sailors."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four different kinds of trouble, one sentence in the middle of each."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The calm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **calms the storm, so that its waves are still**. Then they are glad because they are quiet; **so He guides them to their desired haven**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before this in the same psalm is the one the disciples had every reason to recall on a lake at night. See the notes on Mark 6:45–52 and John 6:16–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Immediately the boat was at the shore they had been heading for.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain that follows each rescue"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Oh, that men would give thanks to the LORD for His goodness, and for **His wonderful works to the children of men**!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Also four times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the psalm opens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give thanks to the LORD, for He is good… **let the rescued ones say it out loud**, the ones He bought back.* See the note on Psalms 107:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole psalm is an instruction to people who have been got out of something: say so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever is wise will observe these things, and they will understand the lovingkindness of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The four groups.** Lost in a desert, sitting in darkness and irons, sick and near the gates of death, and going down in a storm. The psalm gives each of them the same three lines: they cried, He brought them out, let them give thanks."}]}, "Psalms 110:1": {"title": "\"Sit here at My right hand\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it is quoted or alluded to more often in the New Testament than any other line in the Old."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two Lords"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **LORD** says to **my Lord**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew has two different words. The first is **YHWH**, the covenant name. The second is **Adoni** — my lord, my master."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David wrote it, and the second Lord is somebody David calls his superior."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question Jesus asked about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**David himself calls Him Lord — so how can He be David's son?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He put it to the scribes in the temple during passion week, and nobody answered. See the note on Mark 12:35–37."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Sit here at My right hand**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The right hand of a throne is the seat of the co-regent — the place of delegated authority."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is asked for. He is told to sit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What sitting means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews builds a chapter on the posture: every priest *stands ministering daily, offering repeatedly the same sacrifices*, and this one *sat down*. See the note on Hebrews 10:11–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "You sit when the work is finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Until\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Until** I make your enemies a footstool under your feet.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seating and the subduing overlap. Both are happening in the same window, and the one seated is not the one doing the subduing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul makes the same point: *He must reign **until** He has put all enemies under His feet.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 15:23–28."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The footstool"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A footstool is what a seated man rests on. The picture is not a battle in progress; it is furniture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua's commanders were told to put their feet on the necks of five kings, and told *this is what the LORD will do to every enemy you fight.* See the note on Joshua 10:22–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does the making"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I** make your enemies a footstool.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The subject is the first Lord. The one sitting is not described as fighting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the New Testament uses it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter, at Pentecost, quotes it to close his sermon: *\"For David did not ascend into the heavens, but he says himself: 'The LORD said to my Lord…'\"* See the note on Acts 2:14–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews 1 uses it as the closing argument about angels: *to which of the angels has He ever said, \"Sit at My right hand\"?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stephen sees it: *\"I see the heavens opened and the Son of Man **standing** at the right hand of God\"* — and the change of posture in that one sentence has been noticed ever since."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest of the psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD has sworn and will not relent: **You are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A king on David's throne who is also a priest of a line older than Aaron's. Hebrews takes two chapters over it. See the notes on Genesis 14:17–20 and Hebrews 7:20–22."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is unusual about the verse.** It is a report of a conversation David overheard — one Lord speaking to another — and the whole New Testament reads it as the seating after the cross."}]}, "Psalms 110:2–3": {"title": "Volunteers in the morning", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted psalm in the New Testament, and these two verses are the least quoted part of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD said to my Lord, \"**Sit at My right hand, till I make Your enemies Your footstool**.\"* See the note on Psalms 110:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse Jesus used to silence the Pharisees, and the one Hebrews returns to repeatedly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sceptre"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD will **stretch out your mighty scepter from Zion**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **matteh uzzekha** — the rod of your strength."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sent out from Zion, which means the seat is elsewhere and the authority travels."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rule **right in the midst of your enemies**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **redeh beqerev oyvekha**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not after they are removed. In the middle of them, while verse 1 is still running — *until I make Your enemies Your footstool*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reign is described as happening during the *until*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The willing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your people will come to you, **glad and willing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nedavot** — freewill offerings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word for the voluntary sacrifice in Leviticus, brought by somebody who did not have to. See the note on Leviticus 1:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people are described as an offering that offers itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the **day of your power**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **beyom cheilekha** — in the day of your force, your army."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chayil** again — strength, ability, an army, wealth. The same noun as the woman of valour and the capable men. See the note on Proverbs 31:10–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The splendour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In **holy splendor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **behadrei qodesh** — in the beauties of holiness, or in holy array."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some manuscripts read *on the holy mountains*. The two differ by one letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dawn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From **the womb of the dawn**, your young men come to you **like fresh dew of the morning**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **merechem mishchar** — from the womb of the morning. And **tal yaldutekha**, the dew of your youth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The line is famously difficult and every translation handles it differently."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The images are all of freshness: a birth, a sunrise, dew on the ground before the heat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dew appears without anybody seeing it arrive. It is on the grass in the morning and nobody carried it there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD has sworn and will not relent, \"**You are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse Hebrews builds a whole chapter on. See the note on Hebrews 7:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "King in verse 2, and priest in verse 4 — the two offices Israel kept apart, in one man, and Melchizedek is the precedent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the psalm ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He shall judge among the nations… He shall execute the heads of many countries.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He shall **drink of the brook by the wayside**; therefore He shall lift up the head.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last verse is a soldier stopping at a stream mid-pursuit. Gideon's three hundred were chosen by how they drank."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How much the New Testament uses it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "More than any other Old Testament chapter. Verse 1 is quoted or alluded to more than twenty times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus uses it on the Pharisees. Peter uses it at Pentecost. Paul uses it. Hebrews uses it in four separate chapters."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**When the ruling happens.** Not after the enemies are dealt with. *In the midst of them* — while verse 1 is still saying *until*."}]}, "Psalms 110:4": {"title": "\"The LORD has sworn\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and the whole of Hebrews 7 comes out of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD **has sworn**, and **He will not take it back**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the LORD has sworn and will not repent*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An oath from God is unusual — He has nothing higher to swear by, so He swears on Himself. The only other place in the Old Testament He does it about a person is Genesis 22, after the knife. See the note on Genesis 22:15–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews makes the point twice: the Levitical priests were made priests *with no oath sworn over them*, and this one was. See the note on Hebrews 7:20–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You are** a priest forever.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Second person. The psalm's first verse has David overhearing one Lord speak to another — *the LORD said to my Lord, sit at My right hand*. See the note on Psalms 110:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the same conversation, three verses on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two offices"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A king on David's throne, seated at the right hand — and a priest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under the law those could not be the same person. Kings came from Judah and priests from Levi, and the wall between them was absolute. Uzziah went into the temple to burn incense and came out a leper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm puts both on one man, and David wrote it about a descendant of his own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Forever\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **le'olam**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Levitical priests served from thirty to fifty and died. Hebrews says it plainly: *there have been many priests, because they were prevented by death from continuing.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"In the order of **Melchizedek**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *after the manner of*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Melchizedek appears in four verses of Genesis, brings out bread and wine, blesses Abram, receives a tenth, and is never mentioned again in the Old Testament until this line. See the note on Genesis 14:17–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four hundred years before Aaron, and a thousand before David."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Genesis is silent about him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No father, no mother, no genealogy, no birth, no death. Hebrews notices all four omissions and treats the silence as deliberate: *made like the Son of God, remaining a priest continually.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In a book that gives genealogies for everybody, the absence is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is used for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the most-quoted Old Testament verse in the New Testament, and Hebrews quotes this line five times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the argument goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "If a different order of priesthood was promised under oath, after the Levitical one was running, then the Levitical one was not final: *if perfection were through the Levitical priesthood… what further need was there that another priest should rise according to the order of Melchizedek?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a change of priesthood requires a change of law: *for the priesthood being changed, of necessity there is also a change of the law.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole argument of Hebrews 7 to 10 is built on one verse in a psalm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rest of the psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Lord is at Your right hand; He shall execute kings in the day of His wrath.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it ends: *He shall drink of the brook by the wayside; therefore He shall lift up the head.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is sworn.** Not a promise about what He will do. A statement about what He is — and Hebrews says the oath exists because the office was never going to be reviewed."}]}, "Psalms 112:1–3": {"title": "\"Their goodness stands forever\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A psalm about a person, and it is deliberately built to echo the one before it, which is about God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 111 describes the LORD: His work is honour and majesty, His righteousness endures forever."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 112 describes the person who fears Him — and uses the same phrases. *Their goodness stands forever* is word for word what the previous psalm said about God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two are twin acrostics in Hebrew, each running through the alphabet. Read together, the second is a portrait of someone taking after the first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it begins"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Happy is the one who holds the LORD in awe, who delights, deep down, in what He asks.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *obeys* first. **Delights** — the Hebrew is about taking pleasure in something, wanting it. The commands are described as a pleasure before they are described as a duty."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their children will be strong across the land… Their house holds plenty and wealth, and their goodness stands forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three generations of effect: the person, the children, and something that outlasts both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last clause is the one that does not fit the pattern of the other two. Plenty and wealth are in the house; the righteousness stands *forever* — it is the only item on the list that time cannot reach."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Read on for the harder verses.** The psalm goes on: *even in darkness light dawns for the upright*, and *they will have no fear of bad news; their heart is steady, trusting in the LORD.* It does not describe a life without darkness or bad news. It describes someone who has light in the first and a steady heart in the second."}]}, "Psalms 116:12–14": {"title": "The cup of salvation", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalmist asks what he can give back, and the answer he arrives at is that he will take something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What can I **give back** to the LORD for all His goodness to me?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *what shall I return to the LORD for all His benefits toward me?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the instinct of anybody who has been given something large. You start looking for what to send in the other direction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **lift the cup of salvation** and call on the name of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks what to give and answers by taking a cup."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing he can do about the goodness is receive more of it, publicly, and say whose it is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cup"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the Passover meal, cups were lifted and blessed at set points, and this psalm is part of the Hallel — Psalms 113 to 118, sung at Passover."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus sang this psalm in the upper room, on the night He took a cup and said *this is My blood of the new covenant.* See the note on Matthew 26:26–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kos yeshuot** — the cup of salvations, plural."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **keep my promises to the LORD in front of all His people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Public. Twice in the psalm he says it that way — *in the presence of all His people*, and again in the verses following, *in the courts of the LORD's house, in the midst of you, O Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm has come from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The opening: *I love the LORD, because He has heard my voice and my supplications… The pains of death surrounded me, and the pangs of Sheol laid hold of me; I found trouble and sorrow.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the middle: *I believed, even when I said, \"I am badly shaken.\"* — the line Paul quotes as *the spirit of faith*. See the note on Psalms 116:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man asking what he can give back is a man who has just been rescued from something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse in the middle of the psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Precious in the sight of the LORD is the death of His saints.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he calls himself"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*O LORD, truly I am Your servant; I am Your servant, the son of Your maidservant. **You have loosed my bonds.***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says *servant* three times in one verse, and then names what was done to his chains."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the language of Exodus 21 — a man freed, who stays. See the note on Exodus 21:2–6."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the psalm ends.** *I will offer to You the sacrifice of thanksgiving.* The peace offering that had to be eaten fresh, the same day, with none left until morning. See the note on Leviticus 7:11–18."}]}, "Psalms 116:15–19": {"title": "You have loosened my chains", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of a psalm about being nearly dead, and it turns into an accounting of what is owed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm has been about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I love the LORD, because He has heard my voice… **the pains of death surrounded me**, and the pangs of Sheol laid hold of me; I found trouble and sorrow.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then I called upon the name of the LORD: \"O LORD, I implore You, deliver my soul!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the rescue: *You have delivered my soul from death, my eyes from tears, and my feet from falling*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man writing had been close enough to know it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Precious in the sight of the LORD is **the death of those He loves**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yaqar** — costly, valuable, rare. The word used of precious stones."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The usual reading takes it as: their deaths are not cheap to Him. He does not spend them carelessly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is why it sits in a psalm by a man who did not die — the sentence is his explanation for why he is still here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse is read at funerals, and it was written by a survivor."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The self-description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*O LORD, **I am truly Your servant**, Your servant, **born to Your servant girl**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says *servant* twice and then adds his mother."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ben-amatekha** — son of Your handmaid. A slave born in the household rather than bought, and therefore belonging by birth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The release"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have **loosened my chains**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **pittachta lemoserai** — You have loosed my bonds."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A servant, in chains, whose chains have been undone by the master he still calls himself the servant of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The offering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will bring You **a thank offering** and **call on the name of the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **zevach todah** — the sacrifice of thanksgiving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a specific offering. It is the peace offering brought for thanksgiving, and Leviticus requires it to be eaten the same day. See the note on Leviticus 7:11–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which meant a whole animal, one evening, and a family that could not finish it alone. People were invited."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **keep my promises to the LORD in front of all His people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nedarai** — my vows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Made in the crisis, and paid in public."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The location"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the courts of the house of the LORD, **in your very heart, Jerusalem**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One of the Hallel psalms — 113 to 118 — sung at Passover."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means this is among the last things Jesus and the disciples sang before Gethsemane: *and when they had sung a hymn, they went out to the Mount of Olives*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Precious in the sight of the LORD is the death of His saints*, sung on that night."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse two lines earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What shall I render to the LORD for all His benefits toward me? **I will take up the cup of salvation** and call upon the name of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cup, in a Passover psalm, sung at a Passover table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Praise the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hallelu-Yah.**"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who wrote it.** A man who did not die. The line about the death of the saints being costly is his own explanation for still being alive to sing it."}]}, "Psalms 116:8–11": {"title": "\"I believed, even when I said, I am badly shaken\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The verse Paul builds *the spirit of faith* on, and it is not spoken from a comfortable place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three rescues"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You rescued **my life from death**, **my eyes from tears**, **my feet from stumbling**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three, and they cover three different things: the body, the emotions, the walk."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for the middle one is that He delivered his eye from tears. Not that the tears were forbidden — that they were stopped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **walk before the LORD in the land of the living**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in the temple, not in retreat. In the land of the living — ordinary daylight, among people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line Paul quotes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I believed, even when I said, \"**I am badly shaken**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves are in the sentence at once. He believed **and** he said he was in pieces."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I was greatly afflicted*. Nothing is being minimised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes this exact line into 2 Corinthians — *we have the same spirit of faith as the one who wrote, \"I believed, and so I spoke\"* — while listing beatings, shipwrecks and being hard pressed on every side. See the note on 2 Corinthians 4:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So whatever the spirit of faith is, both men exercised it while stating plainly how bad things were."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The next line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In my alarm I said, \"**Every person is a liar.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *in my haste* — said hurriedly, in panic. He is quoting himself at his worst and flagging that it was hasty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm keeps his panic on the record rather than editing it out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What surrounds it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the psalm from the top and it opens with *the pains of death surrounded me, and the pangs of Sheol laid hold of me; I found trouble and sorrow.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *I love the LORD, because He has heard my voice.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Further down the same psalm: *what shall I render to the LORD for all His benefits toward me? **I will take up the cup of salvation** and call upon the name of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 116 is one of the Hallel psalms, sung at Passover. The cup of salvation is in it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus sang this psalm with the disciples in the upper room, on the night He took a cup and said *this is My blood of the new covenant.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse in the middle of it.** *Precious in the sight of the LORD is the death of His saints.* Written by a man who has just said God rescued his life from death — and who still calls the other outcome precious."}]}, "Psalms 118:8–9": {"title": "Better to shelter", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two lines that say the same thing twice, with the object raised."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is **better to shelter in the LORD than to lean on any human**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tov lachasot baYHWH mibbetoach ba'adam**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chasah** — to take refuge, to flee for shelter. The verb of a small animal getting under something."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Batach** — to trust, to lean on, to feel secure in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two different verbs. Not the same word repeated."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is better to shelter in the LORD than to **lean on princes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nedivim** — nobles, princes, the willing and generous ones."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The step up from **adam**, mankind generally, to the people who actually have power to help."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the pair does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not say people are useless. It says that even the ones who could genuinely do something are the lesser option."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Raising the object and keeping the verdict identical is the whole argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the verse sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Traditionally described as the middle verse of the Bible by chapter count, though the arithmetic depends on which chapters are counted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The position is a coincidence of arrangement. The line stands on its own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm has just said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD is on my side; **I will not fear. What can man do to me?***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is better to trust in the LORD…*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question and the verse answer each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm has been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Oh, give thanks to the LORD, for He is good! For **His mercy endures forever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The refrain repeated four times at the start, the same line that runs twenty-six times through Psalm 136. See the note on Psalms 136:4–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I called on the LORD in distress; the LORD answered me and **set me in a broad place**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **merchav** — a wide space, room to move."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All nations surrounded me, but in the name of the LORD I will destroy them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You pushed me violently, that I might fall, **but the LORD helped me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I shall not die, but live, and declare the works of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The stone which the builders rejected **has become the chief cornerstone**. This was the LORD's doing; it is marvellous in our eyes.* See the note on Psalms 118:22–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Quoted by Jesus at the end of the parable of the vineyard, by Peter before the Sanhedrin, and by Peter again in his letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is the day the LORD has made; we will rejoice and be glad in it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse follows immediately from the cornerstone. It is not a general observation about mornings — it is about the day the rejected stone was set in place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was sung at the entry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed is he who comes in the name of the LORD!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd on the road into Jerusalem with palm branches was quoting this psalm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was sung at the table"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 118 closes the Hallel, sung at Passover."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when they had sung a hymn, they went out to the Mount of Olives.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last thing recorded of Jesus singing is likely this psalm, ending: *Oh, give thanks to the LORD, for He is good! For His mercy endures forever.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two verbs.** **Chasah** — to take shelter under something. **Batach** — to lean your weight on it. The psalm does not use the same word for both halves."}]}, "Psalms 119:105–112": {"title": "\"A lamp for the ground beneath my feet\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The best-known verse of the longest psalm, and the two halves describe two different distances."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Lamp and light"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your word is a **lamp for the ground beneath my feet**, a **light laid down along the path** I walk.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An oil lamp of that period was small — a clay dish you carried in one hand or tied at the ankle. It threw enough light for the next step and no further."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second image widens it: light along the path. Near and far, in one verse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither is floodlighting. You get the next step, and enough sense of the road to keep going."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What surrounds it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not use the lamp verse as a comfortable saying. Read the eight lines it belongs to:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*I am worn thin, brought low almost to nothing*", "*my life is always cupped here in my hands* — an idiom for constant danger", "*the wicked set their snare along my way*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lamp is being carried by a man in trouble, at night, with traps on the road. That is the setting the verse belongs to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I've sworn an oath, and I intend to keep it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He binds himself twice in the passage — the oath here, and at the end *I've bent my heart to living out Your statutes, forever, faithful, all the way to the end.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *bent my heart* is about inclining or stretching something toward a direction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The inheritance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your steady words I've taken as my heirloom, my heart's own gladness, treasure without end.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Heirloom** is the word for an inherited portion — the family land that passed down. A man with nothing else says the words are the estate he was left."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The whole psalm.** 176 verses, eight per Hebrew letter, and almost every one mentions God's word under one name or another — law, precepts, statutes, promises, judgments. It is the longest sustained meditation on Scripture inside Scripture, and it was written by somebody being hunted."}]}, "Psalms 119:129–136": {"title": "\"The opening of Your words lets in the light\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The **Pe** stanza — eight lines, every one beginning with the same Hebrew letter, and the middle of it says what happens when the words are opened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The unfolding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **opening** of Your words lets in the light; it gives plain folk the sense they never had.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a noun built from the verb *to open* — an entrance, an unfolding, a doorway. The word itself carries the picture: something shut that is opened, and light comes through the gap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It gives **plain folk** the sense they never had.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew **pethi** is the simple one — not the fool, and not the scoffer, but the untaught, the person with no advantage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the one specified as the beneficiary. Not the learned. The opening of the words is what levels the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The panting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I open wide my mouth and pant for breath, so hungry, longing for Your good commands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is an undignified image and the psalmist chose it. The Hebrew is the gasping of someone who has been running, or of an animal in heat and thirst."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The requests in between"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*steady my footsteps by the word You gave*", "*let no wrong thing hold mastery over me*", "*rescue me from the men who crush and cheat*", "*let Your bright face shine warm upon Your servant*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Let Your face shine** is the Aaronic blessing, quoted back to God by a man asking for what was already promised over him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the stanza ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rivers of tears go running from my eyes, because the people will not keep Your teaching.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *channels of water* — the runnels that carry a stream. Not for himself. For everybody else."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The stanza's shape.** It opens with the words being wonderful and closes with weeping over people who ignore them. The distance between those two lines is the whole burden of the psalm."}]}, "Psalms 119:161–168": {"title": "\"Like one who finds a heap of hidden spoil\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The **Shin** stanza, near the end of the psalm, and the man is still being hunted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rulers have **hunted me for no good reason**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *princes have persecuted me without cause*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm never explains who they are or what the accusation was. It refers to them repeatedly — *the proud have forged a lie against me*, *the wicked have laid a snare for me*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet **my heart stands in awe before Your words**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *my heart stands in awe of Your word*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Princes on one side and a sentence on the other, and the awe goes to the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I take a joy in all the promise You made **like one who finds a heap of hidden spoil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *as one who finds great spoil*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Plunder after a battle — the thing a soldier hopes for and does not expect. Judah spent three days collecting it after Jehoshaphat's battle. See the note on 2 Chronicles 20:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is to a windfall rather than to a wage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I **hate the lie, I cannot bear the falsehood**, but oh, **the teaching You have given, I love**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does this constantly — a hatred and a love in one verse, and the hatred is always of falsehood."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The count"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seven times a day **I stop to praise You** for all the fair decisions of Your justice.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven — completeness rather than a schedule, though later Jewish and Christian practice took it as one and built the daily offices on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The famous line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Great peace belongs to those who **love Your teaching**; and **there is nothing left to make them stumble**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *nothing shall offend them* — **mikshol**, a stumbling block."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek Old Testament renders it **skandalon**, the trigger of a trap."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man saying it is being hunted by rulers. The peace is not an absence of pressure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The waiting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*LORD, **I have waited, hoping for Your rescue**, and I have lived out all Your good commands.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The love"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My soul has kept the steady words You gave, and oh, **I love them, love them past all telling**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *and I love them exceedingly*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I've kept Your precepts and the words You keep, for **all my ways lie open, LORD, before You**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *for all my ways are before You*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the reason given for the keeping, and it is not fear. He is describing a life lived in sight of somebody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two stanzas later, at the very end:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have gone astray like a lost sheep; **seek Your servant**, for I do not forget Your commandments.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The longest chapter in the Bible, one hundred and seventy-six verses about loving God's word, ends with a man saying he is lost and asking to be looked for."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The comparison the stanza turns on.** Not a wage and not a duty. A heap of spoil — what a man finds after a battle he expected to lose."}]}, "Psalms 119:25–32": {"title": "\"My soul lies flat in the dust\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The **Daleth** stanza, and it is the lowest point in the psalm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My soul **lies flat and clinging to the dust**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **davqah le'aphar naphshi** — my soul clings to the dust."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dust** is where the dead go, and where the serpent was told to travel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever is happening to this man, he begins the stanza face down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Revive me, **breathe me back, the way You promised**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chayyeni** — make me live."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb runs through the whole psalm — it appears nine times, always as a request."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The way You promised** — the request is grounded in something already said. He is not asking for a new arrangement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The account"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I **told You all my roads, and You answered**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I declared my ways and You heard me*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The teaching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **teach me, walk me through the ways You keep**. Help me to **understand the path You've marked**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three requests for instruction in two verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The melting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My soul is **melting down from sheer heaviness**; **lift me and hold me steady**, as You said.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *my soul melts from grief*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**As You said** — the second appeal to a promise in eight verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two roads"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep **the deceiving road far off from me**… I've **chosen out the faithful, truthful way**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the way of falsehood* against *the way of faithfulness*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grip"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I **hold on tight to all Your steady words**; O LORD, **don't let me stand here put to shame**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I have clung to Your testimonies* — the same verb as verse 25."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His soul clings to the dust, and he clings to the word. The stanza is built on one verb used twice in opposite directions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I'll **run the road of Your commands wide open**, for **You have set my cramped heart free to move**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *for You shall enlarge my heart*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stanza opens with a man in the dust and closes with him running."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in between explains the change. There is no rescue narrated, no circumstance altered, and no answer described."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What happens between verse 25 and verse 32 is eight requests."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for enlarge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Rachab** — to widen, to make room. It is the root behind **Rehoboth**, the well Isaac named when he finally dug one nobody fought him for: *now the LORD has made room for us*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same movement appears"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I waited patiently for the LORD; and He inclined to me, and heard my cry. He also brought me up out of a horrible pit, out of the miry clay, **and set my feet upon a rock**, and established my steps.* See the note on Psalms 40:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dust and clay at the start, and a firm footing at the end."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What changes across the stanza.** Not the situation. He is clinging to dust in the first line and running in the last, and the only thing that happens in between is that he asks — twice on the ground of something already promised."}]}, "Psalms 119:97–104": {"title": "\"All day long I turn it over\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The **Mem** stanza — eight verses, every one beginning with the same Hebrew letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 119 is the longest chapter in the Bible: twenty-two stanzas of eight verses, one for each letter of the Hebrew alphabet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every verse in a stanza begins with that letter, and almost every verse in the psalm names God's word by one of eight synonyms — law, testimonies, precepts, statutes, commandments, judgments, word, promise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a work of enormous craft, and this stanza is usually reckoned the finest of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Oh, **how I love the teaching that You gave!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *how I love Your law*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm says this repeatedly, and it is startling every time. Nobody expects affection for a legal code."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The practice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All day long **I turn it over and over**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **sichah** — musing, meditation, going over something in the mind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The related verb in Joshua 1:8 is **hagah** — to mutter, to murmur under the breath. See the note on Joshua 1:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ancient reading was aloud. Meditation in the Old Testament is a sound."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three comparisons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your good commands have made me **wiser than my enemies**, for they are always mine.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I understand **far more than all my teachers**, because Your steady words are my long study.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I see with **clearer eyes than aged men**, because I've kept the precepts You have given.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three groups, each with more claim to know than the last: opponents, teachers, and the old."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reasons given are different each time — possession, study, and keeping."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The restraint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I've **held my feet back from each evil road**, so I might **keep the word** that You have spoken.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is deliberate. He stays off the road in order to keep the word, not the reverse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The teacher"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have not turned aside from Your fair judgments, for **You Yourself have been the one who taught me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three comparisons are all undercut by this line. He outstrips his teachers because of who taught him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The taste"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How **sweet Your words taste when I let them linger**, sweeter than honey dripping in my mouth!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *how sweet to my palate*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The manna tasted *like wafers made with honey*. See the note on Exodus 16:31–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Ezekiel is given a scroll to eat: *it was in my mouth like honey in sweetness*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Out of Your precepts **I have gathered wisdom**; so now **I hate each false and crooked road**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The love and the hatred are in the same stanza and joined by a *so*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who wrote it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Unknown. The psalm gives no author and no occasion, and the voice in it is of somebody under pressure — princes persecuting him, the proud digging pits, and *my soul melts from heaviness*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse everyone knows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "From the **Nun** stanza: *Your word is a lamp to my feet and a light to my path.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And from **Beth**: *Your word I have hidden in my heart, that I might not sin against You.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The claim in the middle.** More understanding than his teachers and clearer sight than the elders — and the reason given at the end of the stanza is that God taught him Himself."}]}, "Psalms 121:7–8": {"title": "Your going out and your coming in", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of a short psalm, and one Hebrew verb is repeated through the whole of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Shamar** — to keep, to guard, to watch over."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It appears six times in eight verses, and every occurrence has God as the subject."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word used of Adam in the garden — *to tend and **keep** it* — and of the priestly blessing: *the LORD bless you and **keep** you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The psalm's shape"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It opens as a question: *I will lift up my eyes to the hills — **from whence comes my help**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And answers itself: *my help comes from the LORD, who made heaven and earth.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before these verses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will not allow your foot to be moved; **He who keeps you will not slumber**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold, **He who keeps Israel shall neither slumber nor sleep**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD is your keeper; the LORD is your shade at your right hand. The sun shall not strike you by day, nor the moon by night.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sun and moon — day and night, the whole of a day covered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scope"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD will keep you **from all harm**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *from all evil* — **kol ra**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is kept"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will keep **your very life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nephesh** — soul, life, the person themselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm moves from the foot in verse 3 to the life in verse 7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two directions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD will keep **your going out and your coming in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Hebrew merism — naming the two ends to mean everything between."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the standard phrase for a whole life's activity. Moses uses it of himself at the end: *I can no longer go out and come in*. And of Joshua: *he shall go out before them and come in before them*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two times"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From **this moment** and **forever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *from now and unto everlasting*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One of the Songs of Ascents — Psalms 120 to 134, sung by pilgrims walking up to Jerusalem for the feasts. See the note on Psalms 128:3–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hills in the opening line are the ones on the road. Bandits worked them, and the shrines on the high places were on them too."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The question *from whence comes my help* is asked by somebody looking at a hill country they have to cross."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That nothing happens. The psalm is about a keeper rather than about a smooth road, and it is sung by people on a journey."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one who does not sleep"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice: *will not slumber*, and *shall neither slumber nor sleep*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elijah's taunt on Carmel — *perhaps he is asleep and must be awakened* — was about a different god. See the note on 1 Kings 18:25–29."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb the psalm is built on.** *Keep*, six times in eight verses, and God is the subject every time — over the foot, the head, the life, the going out and the coming in, now and forever."}]}, "Psalms 125:1–2": {"title": "Mountains all round", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, two geographical facts, and both are being used as arguments."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One of the Songs of Ascents — the fifteen psalms sung walking up to Jerusalem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pilgrims are looking at the thing the psalm describes as they sing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Those who **trust in the LORD** are like **Mount Zion, which cannot be shaken, which stands forever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lo yimmot** — shall not be moved, shall not totter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zion is a limestone ridge. It does not do anything, and it has been there for as long as anybody can measure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm compares people to it, and the comparison is about immobility rather than height."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As **the mountains gather around Jerusalem**, so **the LORD gathers around His people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The geography is exact. Jerusalem sits in a bowl — the Mount of Olives to the east, Scopus to the north-east, and higher ground on most sides."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Militarily it is a poor position. Almost every approach looks down into the city."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm takes the same fact and reads it as a ring of protection."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two facts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zion does not move. Jerusalem is surrounded."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are ordinary observations about the landscape. The psalm turns the first into a statement about people who trust, and the second into a statement about God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The duration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From this moment and forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **me'attah ve'ad olam** — from now and to eternity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the sceptre of wickedness shall not rest on the land allotted to the righteous, **lest the righteous reach out their hands to iniquity**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A reason is given for the protection: prolonged oppression makes good people do bad things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm is realistic about what pressure does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prayer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do good, O LORD, to those who are good, and to those who are upright in their hearts.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As for such as turn aside to their crooked ways, the LORD shall lead them away with the workers of iniquity. **Peace be upon Israel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same closing line as Psalm 128. See the note on Psalms 128:5–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the shaking language goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now this, \"Yet once more,\" indicates the removal of those things that are being shaken… that the things which cannot be shaken may remain. Therefore, since we are receiving **a kingdom which cannot be shaken**, let us have grace.* See the note on Hebrews 12:25–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews uses the same idea, and its contrast is between the mountain that burned and could not be touched, and Mount Zion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the pilgrims were doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Walking uphill, in a group, singing about a hill."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Songs of Ascents are the shortest psalms in the book, and the images in them are all things visible from the road: hills, watchmen, a weaned child, dew on Hermon, olive shoots round a table."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fifteen psalms of ordinary sights, sung by people going up to a feast."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Two facts about the landscape.** The ridge does not move, and the city is ringed by higher ground. The psalm uses the first of people and the second of God — and the second is what makes Jerusalem hard to defend."}]}, "Psalms 127:1–2": {"title": "\"He gives sleep to His beloved\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A short psalm attributed to Solomon — the man who built the actual house and watched over the actual city — and it begins by saying so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two examples"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Unless the LORD builds the house, its builders labour in vain. Unless the LORD watches the city, the watchman keeps awake for nothing.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Building and guarding: making something, and keeping it. Between them they cover most of what anyone spends a life doing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not say the builders are idle or the watchmen asleep. It says their work is *in vain* without something else — the labour is real and the result is not in their hands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The bread of anxious toil\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is no use rising early, sitting up late, eating the bread of anxious toil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is literally the bread of *pains* or *sorrows* — the same root used of the toil laid on the ground in Genesis 3. It is a picture of work eaten as misery: early starts, late nights, and food taken standing up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For to His beloved He gives sleep.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew here is compressed and can be read two ways: *He gives sleep to His beloved*, or *He gives to His beloved while they sleep*. Translators have split on it for centuries."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way, sleep is the image, and it is offered as the alternative to the anxious bread. The psalm's argument is not that work is pointless — it is that the outcome does not depend on staying awake."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes next.** The rest of the psalm is about children being a heritage from the LORD. The two halves belong together: a house is built and a city is kept, and what actually endures out of a life is described as something received rather than achieved."}]}, "Psalms 128:1–2": {"title": "\"You will eat the fruit of your labour\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the Songs of Ascents — the fifteen psalms pilgrims sang walking up to Jerusalem — and it is about a house rather than a temple."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is happy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Happy is **everyone** who fears the LORD, who walks in His ways.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ashrei** — the word Psalm 1 opens with, and the word behind *blessed are* in the Beatitudes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Everyone.** Not the priest, the Levite, or the notable. The psalm is deliberately ordinary from the first line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Eating what you grew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will **eat the fruit of your labour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It sounds unremarkable until you set it beside the curse in Deuteronomy 28: *you shall build a house and not dwell in it; you shall plant a vineyard and not eat of it… your ox shall be slaughtered before your eyes and you shall not eat of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the shape of the curse — working and not getting to keep it. The blessing here is the plain reversal: you eat what your own hands produced."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah says the same thing about the world put right: *they shall not build and another inhabit; they shall not plant and another eat.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two things, twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will be **happy**, and **it will go well with you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew doubles it — good for you, and well with you. Inside and outside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the rest of the psalm shows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your wife shall be like a fruitful vine in the very heart of your house, your children like olive plants all around your table.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A vine in the middle of the house and young olive trees round a table. Both are slow-growing plants that take years before they yield, and both go on yielding for generations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is a meal. That is the whole vision of blessing in this psalm — a man eating what he grew, at a table, with his family around it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the blessing comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD **bless you out of Zion**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not out of the field. The blessing on the house is sourced at the sanctuary, and the psalm is sung by people walking toward it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And how far it reaches"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*May you see your children's children. Peace be upon Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grandchildren, and then the nation. The psalm starts with one man's fear of God and ends with peace on a whole people."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it sits.** Psalm 127 is the one before it — *unless the LORD builds the house, they labour in vain who build it… He gives His beloved sleep.* The two are a pair: one says the building is His, and then this one describes the house."}]}, "Psalms 128:3–4": {"title": "A vine and young olive shoots", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two images from a smallholding, applied to a household."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One of the Songs of Ascents — Psalms 120 to 134, sung by pilgrims going up to Jerusalem for the feasts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Short psalms, meant to be walked to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it opens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed is every one who fears the LORD, who walks in His ways. **When you eat the labour of your hands, you shall be happy, and it shall be well with you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eating what your own hands produced was not guaranteed in that world. Deuteronomy's curses include *you shall plant a vineyard and not gather its grapes* and *another shall eat it*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your wife **within your house** will be like **a fruitful vine**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *in the innermost parts of your house* — the private quarters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A vine is not a decorative plant. It takes four years before it bears, it needs pruning and staking, and it produces for generations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel is called a vine repeatedly, and Jesus calls Himself the true one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The olive shoots"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your children **around your table** will be like **young olive shoots**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *olive plants* — the suckers that come up around the base of a mature olive tree."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An olive takes fifteen years to bear properly and can live for centuries. The shoots at the base are the next tree, growing from the same root, already there while the old one is still producing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two locations"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Within your house* and *around your table*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are interior. The psalm's picture of blessing is a room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look, **this is how the man is blessed who fears the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *behold, thus shall the man be blessed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm opened with the same clause and closes the first half by repeating it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD bless you **out of Zion**, and may you **see the good of Jerusalem all the days of your life**. Yes, may you **see your children's children**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peace be upon Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm widens from a table to a city to a nation in three lines, and the last word is **shalom**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 127 is directly before it — *unless the LORD builds the house, they labour in vain who build it*, and *children are a heritage from the LORD*. See the note on Psalms 127:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two are usually read together: one says the building is God's, and this one describes what is in the house."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anything spectacular. A wife, children, a table, food you grew yourself, and grandchildren before you die."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Songs of Ascents were sung by people walking to a feast, and this is the one about what they had left at home."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two plants have in common.** Both take years, both come from a root, and both outlast the man they belong to. The psalm chooses the two slowest crops in the country."}]}, "Psalms 128:5–6": {"title": "Your children's children", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last two verses widen from a table to a nation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the blessing comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*May the LORD **bless you from Zion**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The household blessing is routed through the city."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is a pilgrim song — one of the fifteen Songs of Ascents, sung on the road up. See the note on Psalms 128:3–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The people singing it are walking toward the place the blessing is said to come out of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The city"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*May you see **Jerusalem prosper all the days of your life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *see the good of Jerusalem*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man's own prosperity is not mentioned. He is wished a long life in which the city does well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two are tied: nobody's household thrives in a country under siege."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The generations"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*May you see **your children's children**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **banim levaneikha** — sons to your sons."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The longest view in the psalm, and the least arrangeable thing in it. A man can work a field. He cannot organise living long enough to meet his grandchildren."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jacob got it, and said so: *I had not thought to see your face; but in fact, **God has also shown me your offspring***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Joseph: *he saw Ephraim's children to the third generation*. See the note on Genesis 50:22–26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Peace be upon Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shalom al-Yisrael**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm zooms out at the final word: one man, one table, one city, one nation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same ending elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 125 closes with the identical line: *peace be upon Israel*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the priestly blessing, which the whole psalm is an expansion of: *the LORD bless you and keep you; the LORD make His face shine upon you… and **give you peace**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the fifteen songs are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalms 120 to 134. Short, memorable, and about the journey and what is at either end of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I was glad when they said to me, \"Let us go into the house of the LORD.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will lift up my eyes to the hills — from whence comes my help?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold, how good and how pleasant it is for brethren to dwell together in unity.* See the note on Psalms 133:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Unless the LORD builds the house, they labour in vain who build it.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read as a list: God's blessing from His own city, a long life, a prospering country, and grandchildren."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about wealth or position. The whole psalm's picture of a blessed man is a full table and a peaceful nation, and he does not arrange either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm started"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed is every one who fears the LORD, who walks in His ways.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the middle: *behold, thus shall the man be blessed who fears the LORD*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said at the beginning and again at the halfway point, and then these two verses spell out what it comes to."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last thing asked for.** Grandchildren — the one item on the list that no amount of work secures, and the psalm ends by widening past it to the whole country."}]}, "Psalms 129:3–4": {"title": "\"The plowmen plowed across my back\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of the shorter Songs of Ascents, and one of its images is unusually brutal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The picture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The plowmen plowed across my back and cut their long furrows there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A plough opening ground, used of a human back. The Hebrew for *furrows* is the agricultural term — the parallel cuts a blade leaves in a field."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel is speaking as one person here, describing centuries of oppression from Egypt onward: *many a time they have afflicted me from my youth, yet they have not prevailed against me.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is a harvest image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm is one of the pilgrim songs, sung by farmers walking up to Jerusalem for the festivals. A ploughed field was not a picture of ruin to them — it was what you did before sowing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That tension is left standing. A back opened like a field, in a psalm sung on the way to a harvest feast."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the LORD is just. He has cut me free from the ropes of the wicked.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs in tension: they cut the back, He cuts the ropes. The same action word answering itself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD is just* is stated as the reason. Not that Israel endured well — that God is righteous, and therefore the cords did not hold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where readers have taken it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scourging of Jesus is described in the Gospels in a single word and no detail. Christian readers have long heard this verse behind it, and it sits naturally beside Isaiah's servant whose back was given to those who struck him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not say that. It is Israel's own account of survival — and it is worth noticing that the language it reaches for is the language of a body taking what a field takes."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the psalm ends.** With the wicked as grass on a roof — sprouting fast in shallow soil, withered before anyone can gather it. Set against a ploughed field, the contrast is between ground that gets worked and ground nothing grows in."}]}, "Psalms 132:13–14": {"title": "Here I will dwell", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The answer to the vow, and it uses the same words back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The choosing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the LORD has **chosen Zion**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **bachar** — chose, selected."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm's first half was David swearing he would not sleep until he found a place. See the note on Psalms 132:3–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second half is God saying He had already picked one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The desiring"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has **desired it for His home**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ivvah** — longed for, craved. A strong verb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same word used of appetite — *the mixed multitude who were among them **yielded to intense craving***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God is described as wanting an address."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The declaration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is **My resting place forever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Menuchati** — My rest, My resting place. The same noun David used in his vow and the psalm used in the summons: *arise, LORD, to Your resting place*. See the note on Psalms 132:8–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The request and the answer use one word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dwelling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Here **I will dwell**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **eshev** — I will sit, remain, be enthroned."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason, repeated"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **I have desired it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb as verse 13, said twice in two verses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No other reason is offered. Not Zion's merit, not its position, not its history — He wanted it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Zion was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hill with a Jebusite fortress on it, which David took by sending men up a water shaft."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about it recommended itself. It was not the highest ground in the region; Gibeon had the tabernacle and Bethel had the history."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy had promised only *the place which the LORD your God chooses*, without naming it, about twenty times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the naming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exchange"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David: *I will find a place for the LORD, a dwelling for the Mighty One of Jacob*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God: *this is My resting place forever; here I will dwell, for I have desired it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man swears to find one and is told one was chosen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the temple ends up being"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet regard the prayer of Your servant… that Your eyes may be open toward this temple night and day, toward the place of which You said, \"**My name shall be there**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Solomon, at the dedication, and in the same prayer: *but will God indeed dwell on the earth? Behold, heaven and the heaven of heavens cannot contain You. How much less this temple which I have built!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the resting place goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Most High does not dwell in temples made with hands.* Stephen, quoting Isaiah, on trial. See the note on Acts 6:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do you not know that **you are the temple of God** and that the Spirit of God dwells in you?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *in whom you also are being built together for a dwelling place of God in the Spirit*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last word of the psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There I will make the horn of David grow; **I have prepared a lamp for My Anointed**. His enemies I will clothe with shame, but upon Himself His crown shall flourish.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A lamp, prepared, for the Messiah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise that runs through Kings whenever a bad king reigns: *the LORD would not destroy Judah, for the sake of His servant David, **as He promised him to give a lamp to him and his sons forever**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The reason given, twice.** *I have desired it.* Nothing about the hill, and nothing about the man who asked — He says He wanted it, and says it in both verses."}]}, "Psalms 132:15–16": {"title": "More than was asked for", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "God's reply, and it answers the prayer item by item with one word changed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The provision"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **bless her with plenty of food**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I will abundantly bless her provision* — **tsedah**, the word for supplies laid in for a journey."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The poor"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **fill her poor with bread**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **evyoneha** — her needy, her destitute."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *feed*. **Asbia** — I will satisfy, fill to satisfaction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The city's poor are the second item on the list, before anything about the priesthood or the worship."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mary's song has the same order: *He has filled the hungry with good things, and the rich He has sent away empty*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clothing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **clothe her priests with salvation**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here is the change."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer, seven verses earlier, was *let Your priests be clothed with **righteousness*** — the Hebrew **tsedeq**. See the note on Psalms 132:8–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer says **yesha** — salvation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Righteousness was requested. Salvation is given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Solomon did with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "When he prayed at the temple dedication he quoted the psalm's request and used the answer's word:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now therefore, arise, O LORD God, to Your resting place… **let Your priests be clothed with salvation**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks for what God had already promised rather than for what the psalm had asked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The singing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And her **faithful ones will sing aloud for joy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer had asked *let Your saints shout for joy*. The answer repeats it and intensifies the verb."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four requests in verses 8 to 10, and four answers in 13 to 16, in order."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rise to Your resting place — *this is My resting place forever*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Priests clothed with righteousness — *clothed with salvation*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Saints shout for joy — *they will sing aloud for joy*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For David's sake — *the LORD has sworn in truth to David*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every item is answered, and one is upgraded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last three verses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There **I will make the horn of David grow**; I have prepared **a lamp for My Anointed**. His enemies I will clothe with shame, but upon Himself **His crown shall flourish**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clothings in the psalm — priests in salvation, enemies in shame."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A lamp for My Anointed** — the phrase that recurs through Kings whenever a bad king reigns and Judah is not destroyed anyway: *for David's sake the LORD his God gave him a lamp in Jerusalem*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the food promise goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am the bread of life. He who comes to Me shall never hunger.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *He has filled the hungry with good things*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is doing among the pilgrim songs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fifteen short psalms for walking up to Jerusalem, and this is the one that tells them why the building at the top is there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man made a vow about a resting place, and God answered with a longer speech than the request."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word that changed.** *Righteousness* was asked for. *Salvation* was given — and Solomon, praying in the finished temple, quoted the answer rather than the request."}]}, "Psalms 132:3–5": {"title": "No sleep until", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A vow, quoted in a pilgrim song, and it is about accommodation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One of the Songs of Ascents — Psalms 120 to 134, sung going up to Jerusalem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The longest of the fifteen, and the only one that is a piece of history."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It opens: *LORD, remember David and all his afflictions; how he swore to the LORD, and vowed to the Mighty One of Jacob*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **not enter my house**, nor **climb onto my bed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four negatives, escalating."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will give **no sleep to my eyes**, **no rest to my eyelids**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tenumah** — slumber, drowsiness. The lighter word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not just no sleep. Not even dozing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until I **find a place for the LORD**, a **dwelling for the Mighty One of Jacob**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **maqom** — a place. And **mishkanot**, dwellings, from the root **shakan**, to settle — the word behind **shekinah**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the vow is not recorded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nowhere in Samuel or Chronicles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrative records the intention — *see now, I dwell in a house of cedar, but the ark of God dwells inside tent curtains* — and Nathan's answer, and God's reply: *would you build a house for Me to dwell in?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm preserves the oath itself, which the histories do not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David was living in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A house of cedar.* Hiram of Tyre had sent carpenters, masons and timber and built it for him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The contrast is the thing that started it. He is in a permanent building and the ark is under canvas."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God says to the offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD will make you a house**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offer is refused and reversed. David proposes to build God a house; God announces He will build David one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And your house and your kingdom shall be established forever before you. Your throne shall be established forever.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David does instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Buys the site — Araunah's threshing floor, *I will not offer burnt offerings to the LORD my God with that which costs me nothing* — and stockpiles the materials."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now I have prepared with all my might for the house of my God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He gathers gold, silver, bronze, iron, timber and stone for a building he is told he will not put up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he could not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You have shed much blood and have made great wars… **you shall not build a house for My name**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"**Solomon your son shall build My house**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The vow in the psalm is kept by somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did put up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A tent in Jerusalem, and the ark in it. See the note on 2 Samuel 6:12–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the tabernacle of Moses, which stayed at Gibeon. A second tent, with singers around the ark day and night, and no veil in front of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We heard of it in Ephrathah. We found it in the fields of Jaar.* See the note on Psalms 132:6–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The search, and then the finding — in a house on a hill where it had sat for a generation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer to the vow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the LORD has **chosen Zion**. He has **desired it for His home**. **This is My resting place forever**.* See the note on Psalms 132:13–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David swore he would not rest until he found God a resting place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm's second half is God saying He has found one."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the vow asks for.** Not victory, not a dynasty, not an heir. A place for somebody else to live — and the man swearing it was sleeping in a cedar house at the time."}]}, "Psalms 132:6–7": {"title": "We found it in the fields", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and they are the search party talking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The change of voice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verses 3 to 5 were David's vow, in the first person singular. See the note on Psalms 132:3–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Now it is *we*. A group is speaking, and they are recounting a hunt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hearing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We **heard of it in Ephrathah**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ephrathah** is Bethlehem — *but you, Bethlehem Ephrathah, though you are little among the thousands of Judah, yet out of you shall come forth to Me the One to be Ruler in Israel*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David's home town. That is where the party starts from, or where the report reached them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The finding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We **found it in the fields of Jaar**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Jaar** — forest, wood. Short for **Kirjath Jearim**, the city of forests."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is where the ark had been sitting, in Abinadab's house on the hill, for twenty years and then through the whole of Saul's reign. See the note on 1 Samuel 7:2–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the two place names do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bethlehem and the fields of the wood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm compresses the whole story into one line: they heard about it where David came from, and found it out in the countryside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the fields* is the point. It was not in a sanctuary. It was on a farm, in a house, up a hill, where nobody had been to see it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose fault that was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody's, specifically, and everybody's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Chronicler puts it plainly when David finally moves: *let us bring the ark of our God back to us, **for we did not enquire at it in the days of Saul***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forty years of a king who never sent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let us **go into His dwelling**. Let us **bow low at His footstool**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mishkenotav** — His dwellings, plural. The tabernacle word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **hadom raglav** — the footstool of His feet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ark is called God's footstool elsewhere: *worship at His footstool*, and David to the assembly: *I had it in my heart to build a house of rest for the ark of the covenant of the LORD, and for **the footstool of our God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A footstool implies a throne, and the throne is not in the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is above it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mercy seat, and the two cherubim facing each other with wings overshadowing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there I will meet with you, and I will speak with you from above the mercy seat, from between the two cherubim.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's word for what Christ was publicly set forth as. See the note on Romans 3:25–26."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows in the psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rise up, LORD, to Your resting place, **You and the ark of Your strength**.* See the note on Psalms 132:8–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That sentence is Moses' — *rise up, O LORD* — said whenever the ark set out in the wilderness. See the note on Numbers 10:33–36."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm is quoting a marching order at the end of the march."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it was sung"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Solomon quotes the next verses at the dedication of the temple, when the ark is finally carried in and set under the wings of the cherubim in the Holy of Holies."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now therefore, arise, O LORD God, to Your resting place, You and the ark of Your strength.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The search that started *in the fields of the wood* ends in a room."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where they found it.** Not in a sanctuary. In the fields — a house on a hill that a whole generation had not visited."}]}, "Psalms 132:8–10": {"title": "Rise up to Your resting place", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The prayer at the centre of the psalm, and Solomon quotes it out loud."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The summons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rise up, LORD, **to Your resting place**, You and **the ark of Your strength**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **qumah YHWH limnuchatekha**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Qumah** — arise. It is Moses' word, said every time the ark set out in the wilderness: *rise up, O LORD! Let Your enemies be scattered*. See the note on Numbers 10:33–36."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses said it at the start of a journey. The psalm says it at the end of one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The resting place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Menuchah** — rest, a settled place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ark had gone before them *to search out a resting place for them*. Now it is asked to come into one of its own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ark of Your strength.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **aron uzzekha**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two requests"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*May Your **priests be clothed in righteousness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yilbeshu tsedeq** — let them put on righteousness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Priests were clothed. The garments were specified in detail — ephod, breastplate, robe, turban, sash. The psalm asks for a different fabric."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*May Your **faithful ones sing for joy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chasidekha** — Your covenant-loyal ones, from **chesed**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The plea"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the sake of **David Your servant**, do not turn away the face of **Your anointed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **meshichekha** — Your messiah, Your anointed one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The request is made on somebody else's account. Not *for my sake* — for David's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who said this out loud"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Solomon, at the dedication of the temple, with the ark being carried into the Holy of Holies:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Now therefore, **arise, O LORD God, to Your resting place, You and the ark of Your strength. Let Your priests, O LORD God, be clothed with salvation, and let Your saints rejoice in goodness. O LORD God, do not turn away the face of Your anointed; remember the mercies of Your servant David**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Chronicler records it as the end of the prayer, and the fire falls immediately after."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One change: Solomon says *clothed with salvation* where the psalm says *righteousness*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God answers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will clothe her priests with **salvation**, and her faithful ones will sing aloud for joy.* See the note on Psalms 132:15–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer takes both requests and gives more than was asked: righteousness requested, salvation granted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm records the request and the reply, and the wording changes in between."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The change of clothes elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will greatly rejoice in the LORD… for **He has clothed me with the garments of salvation, He has covered me with the robe of righteousness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah, and he has both words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the prodigal: *bring out the best robe and put it on him*. See the note on Luke 15:21–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Joshua the high priest in Zechariah, standing in filthy garments with Satan at his right hand: *take away the filthy garments from him… see, I have removed your iniquity from you, and I will clothe you with rich robes*. See the note on Zechariah 3:1–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the second half of the psalm is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God's answer, in the first person, and it is longer than the request."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD has sworn in truth to David; **He will not turn from it**.* See the note on Psalms 132:13–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"There I will make the horn of David grow; **I have prepared a lamp for My Anointed**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm ends where it began — on the anointed one, and on a promise that outlives him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who the plea is made for.** Not the person praying. *For the sake of David Your servant* — and Solomon quotes the whole verse standing in a finished temple."}]}, "Psalms 133:1": {"title": "\"When brothers dwell together as one\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses in the whole psalm, and this is the first. The other two are the pictures."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look how **good**, look how **pleasant**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both, and they are not the same thing. Some good things are not pleasant and some pleasant things are not good. The psalm says unity is both at once."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Dwell together\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is about settling, staying, living in one place — not visiting. It is the word used of a family sharing an inheritance rather than dividing it and moving apart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is compared to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm goes on to two images, and both are about something running downward:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**oil poured on Aaron's head**, running down his beard, down onto the collar of his robes. The anointing oil of a priest, and the psalm follows it all the way down.", "**the dew of Hermon** falling on the mountains of Zion. Hermon is the great snow peak in the far north; Zion is two hundred kilometres south. The image is of moisture from the highest place arriving somewhere else entirely."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are liquid, both come from above, and both go down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For there the LORD commanded the blessing — life for evermore.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There* — in the place where brothers dwell together. The blessing is located rather than merely described."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What kind of psalm it is.** One of the Songs of Ascents, sung by pilgrims walking up to Jerusalem for the festivals — people from scattered towns arriving in one place three times a year. The unity being celebrated is not theoretical. It is a road full of families converging."}]}, "Psalms 133:3": {"title": "The dew of Hermon", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last line of a three-verse psalm, and it moves water from one end of the country to the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold, how good and how pleasant it is **for brethren to dwell together in unity**!* See the note on Psalms 133:1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is like the precious oil upon the head, running down on the beard, the beard of Aaron, running down on the edge of his garments.* See the note on Psalms 133:2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verses, two images: oil, and dew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is like **the dew of Hermon** falling on **the hills of Zion**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ketal-Chermon sheyyored al-harerei Tziyyon**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Hermon is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The far north. A mountain over nine thousand feet, snow-capped most of the year, on the border of Lebanon and Syria."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Its slopes are famously wet. The runoff feeds the Jordan."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Zion is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hundred and twenty miles south, in the Judean hills, dry for half the year."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem with the line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hermon's dew does not fall on Zion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm knows that. It is not making a claim about weather."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the image does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Takes the wettest place in the country and puts its abundance on the driest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oil in verse 2 ran down from the head to the hem — top to bottom. The dew runs from north to south. Both images are about something travelling a distance it would not naturally travel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yored** — descending, running down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same participle used of the oil in the previous verse. The psalm uses one word for both images."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What dew was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In a country with no rain from April to October, dew was the difference between a crop and none."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then God give you of the dew of heaven, of the fatness of the earth* — Isaac's blessing on Jacob."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Gideon's fleece: *there was dew on the fleece only, and it was dry on all the ground*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commanded blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **there the LORD has given His blessing, life forevermore**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ki sham tzivvah YHWH et-habberakhah** — for there the LORD commanded the blessing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tzivvah** — commanded. The blessing is issued as an order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where *there* is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word points back to the whole psalm: where brothers dwell together."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a location. A condition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is commanded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Life forevermore.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chayyim ad-ha'olam** — life to the age."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A three-verse psalm about getting along ends on eternal life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind of psalm this is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A Song of Ascents.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One of fifteen, sung by pilgrims going up to Jerusalem for the feasts. The road climbs the whole way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm before it is one line long. The one after it is the last of the set: *behold, bless the LORD, all you servants of the LORD, who by night stand in the house of the LORD*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who wrote it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A Song of Ascents. Of David.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man whose own household produced Absalom, Amnon and Adonijah, and whose sons killed each other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not writing from an easy family."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now may the God of patience and comfort grant you **to be like-minded toward one another**, according to Christ Jesus, that you may **with one mind and one mouth glorify God**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The geography.** Hermon's dew does not reach Zion, and the psalm knows it. It takes the wettest place in the country and puts it on the driest."}]}, "Psalms 136:1–3": {"title": "\"His steadfast love endures forever\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm with one line in it, repeated twenty-six times."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The structure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every verse in Psalm 136 has two halves. The first varies; the second never does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His steadfast love endures forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-six verses, twenty-six repetitions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it was used"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The form is antiphonal. A leader sang the first half and the congregation answered with the second."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm is built to be said back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chesed** — covenant loyalty, steadfast love, mercy. It is one of the hardest words in the Old Testament to translate, and English versions render it a dozen ways."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It means the kind of love that holds because of an agreement rather than because of a feeling — and then exceeds the agreement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three titles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give thanks to **the LORD**, for He is good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give thanks to **the God of gods**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give thanks to **the Lord of lords**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **YHWH**, then **Elohei ha-elohim**, then **Adonei ha-adonim** — a Hebrew superlative construction, the same shape as *king of kings* and *holy of holies*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm goes on to list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Creation — the heavens, the earth on the waters, the great lights, the sun for the day and the moon and stars for the night."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the exodus — the firstborn of Egypt, the outstretched arm, the sea divided *into parts*, Israel through the middle, Pharaoh overthrown."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the wilderness and the kings — Sihon, Og, the land given as a heritage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *who **remembered us in our lowly state**… and **rescued us from our enemies**… who **gives food to all flesh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cosmology, national history, and dinner, and after every one of them the same line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the repetition does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The listed events are all different. The refrain is the constant, which is the point of the form."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the verse is about, the answer is the same."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it turns up in the history"}, {"k": "p", "t": "2 Chronicles, at the dedication of Solomon's temple: the trumpeters and singers *as one, to make one sound to be heard in praising and thanking the LORD… saying, \"**For He is good, for His mercy endures forever**\" — that the house was filled with a cloud.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at Jehoshaphat's battle, where the singers went out in front of the army with the same line. See the note on 2 Chronicles 20:20–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at the laying of the second temple's foundation, in Ezra — where the old men who remembered the first one wept while everyone else shouted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name it carries"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jewish tradition calls Psalm 136 **the Great Hallel**, and it is sung at Passover."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means it was very likely sung in the upper room. Matthew records the end of the supper: *and when they had sung a hymn, they went out to the Mount of Olives.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the psalm is.** One sentence, said twenty-six times, with the whole of creation and the whole of Israel's history hung between the repetitions."}]}, "Psalms 136:10–15": {"title": "The refrain on a judgment", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The same response is sung after the hardest lines in the psalm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm has moved from creation — heavens, earth, sun, moon — into national history. See the note on Psalms 136:4–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The refrain does not change with the subject."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To Him who **struck down the firstborn of Egypt**, His steadfast love endures forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lemakkeh Mitzrayim bivchoreihem** — to the one striking Egypt in their firstborn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tenth plague. The night of the Passover."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the refrain says about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ki le'olam chasdo** — for His covenant love endures forever."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The congregation sings the same response they sang over the sun and the moon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not softening the judgment or explaining it. It puts the line in and answers it with the covenant word, and moves on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew hymnbook does not treat the two categories as needing to be reconciled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bringing out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **brought Israel out from among them**, His steadfast love endures forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mittocham** — from among them, out of their midst."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two lines are a pair. The striking and the bringing out are one act, told in two verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **a strong hand and an outstretched arm**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **beyad chazaqah uvizroa netuyah**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The formula runs through Deuteronomy, and it is the phrase Israel was to teach its children when they asked what the statutes meant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sea"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To Him who **split the Red Sea in two**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **legozer yam-suph ligzarim** — cutting the Sea of Reeds into pieces."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Gazar** — to cut, to divide. The word used of dividing a thing in two, and of the covenant Abraham made with animal halves."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crossing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **led Israel through the middle of it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Betocho** — through its midst. The same construction as *from among them* three lines earlier."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Out from among the Egyptians, through the middle of the sea."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Pharaoh"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **swept Pharaoh and his army into the sea**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **veni'er** — shook off, as a garment is shaken."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exodus 14 uses the same verb: *the LORD overthrew the Egyptians in the midst of the sea*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern of the whole psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Creation, exodus, wilderness, the kings of the Amorites, the land given as an inheritance — and then:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who remembered us in our lowly state… and rescued us from our enemies… **who gives food to all flesh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cosmic, then national, then personal, then everything that eats."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is sung at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Passover. This is the Great Hallel, sung at the meal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when they had sung a hymn, they went out to the Mount of Olives.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hymn at the last supper was almost certainly this psalm or the ones around it. The last thing Jesus is recorded singing is a refrain about **chesed** enduring forever, on the night before the crucifixion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does with the exodus"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All our fathers were under the cloud, all passed through the sea… **now all these things happened to them as examples**.* See the note on 1 Corinthians 10:11–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is on the other side"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they sing **the song of Moses**, the servant of God, and the song of the Lamb: \"Great and marvellous are Your works, Lord God Almighty.\"* See the note on Revelation 15:2–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Standing on a sea of glass, and the song is named after the man who sang at the first one."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the psalm does not do.** Separate the categories. The striking of the firstborn and the making of the sun get the same one-line answer from the same congregation."}]}, "Psalms 136:4–9": {"title": "Creation, with a refrain", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Every line has an answer, and the answer never changes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The form"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-six verses, and every one of them ends the same way:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His steadfast love endures forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ki le'olam chasdo** — for His **chesed** is forever."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is antiphonal. One voice sings the line; the congregation sings the response."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain's word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chesed** — the covenant word. Loyal love, kindness that is owed because a relationship exists, translated variously as mercy, lovingkindness, steadfast love."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-six times in twenty-six verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm is used"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Called *the Great Hallel* in Jewish tradition, and sung at Passover."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Chronicler records it at the dedication of the temple: *when the priests came out of the holy place… **the trumpeters and singers were as one**, to make one sound to be heard in praising and thanking the LORD*, and the words were *for He is good, for His mercy endures forever*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The house filled with cloud at that moment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening of this section"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To Him **who alone does great wonders**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **niphla'ot gedolot levaddo** — great wonders, by Himself alone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for wonders is the one used of the plagues and of the crossing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heavens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To Him **who made the heavens with wisdom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **bitvunah** — with understanding."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs 3: *the LORD by wisdom founded the earth; by understanding He established the heavens*. See the note on Proverbs 3:19–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The earth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To Him who **spread the earth over the waters**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **roqa** — beat out, hammered flat, like metal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same root as **raqia**, the firmament of Genesis 1."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lights"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To Him who **made the great lights**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 1:16: *God made two great lights*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm follows the creation account in order — heavens, earth, waters, lights — and attaches the refrain to each step."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sun"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sun **to rule the day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lememshelet bayom** — for dominion in the day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis uses the same verb, and both texts avoid naming the sun and moon. Every neighbouring culture worshipped them; Genesis and this psalm call them *the greater light* and *the lesser*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The moon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The moon and stars **to rule the night**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Stars added almost as an afterthought, exactly as in Genesis: *He made the stars also*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the refrain does to creation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every act of making gets the covenant word attached to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sun is not simply an astronomical fact. It rises because of **chesed**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To Him who **struck down the firstborn of Egypt**, His steadfast love endures forever.* See the note on Psalms 136:10–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same response, on a judgment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who remembered us in our lowly state… and rescued us from our enemies… **who gives food to all flesh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **noten lechem lechol-basar** — gives bread to all flesh."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twenty-five verses of cosmology and national history, and the last act named is feeding everything that lives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Oh, give thanks to the God of heaven! For His mercy endures forever.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The form.** Twenty-six lines, twenty-six identical answers. The sun rises, the earth is spread, the stars are set — and each one is followed by the covenant word."}]}, "Psalms 139:13–16": {"title": "Woven in the depths", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm has already covered God's knowledge and God's presence. This is the part about manufacture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **You formed my inmost parts**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kilyotai** — literally the kidneys. In Hebrew idiom the kidneys are the seat of the innermost self, the way the heart is in English."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You created my inward parts* is the older rendering, and the verb is **qanah** — acquired, got, formed. The verb Eve uses at Cain's birth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weaving"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You **knit me together** in my mother's womb.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **sakak** — to weave, to screen, to hedge in. The same root as the covering cherubim over the ark."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I praise You, for **I am fearfully and wonderfully made**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I am fearfully distinguished* — **nora'ot niphleti**. Awesomely set apart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reaction to the anatomy is worship, not analysis."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The confirmation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wonderful are Your works, and **my soul knows it well**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *my soul knows it very well* — **yoda'at me'od**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing the psalmist claims to know thoroughly, in a psalm mostly about what he cannot know."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verses earlier: *such knowledge is too wonderful for me; it is high, I cannot attain it*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was not hidden"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My **frame was not hidden from You** when I was **made in secret**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **otsmi** — my bone, my substance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Secret from everyone else. Not from Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The location"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Woven together in **the depths of the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *in the lowest parts of the earth* — **tachtiyyot arets**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An odd phrase for a womb. It is the language used elsewhere of the grave, or of deep places generally."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is of something made underground, out of sight, the way a seed germinates or ore sits in rock. And of the man formed *of the dust of the ground*. See the note on Genesis 2:4–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The unformed thing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your eyes **saw me unformed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **golmi** — my unformed substance, an embryo. The word occurs once in the whole Bible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the root behind the later Hebrew *golem*: unshaped matter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And in Your book were written **all my days, every one of them shaped before a single one had come**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the days were fashioned for me, when as yet there were none of them*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written in a book before day one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm has already said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You know my sitting down and my rising up; You understand my thought afar off… **there is not a word on my tongue, but behold, O LORD, You know it altogether**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *where can I go from Your Spirit?… if I make my bed in hell, behold, You are there. If I take the wings of the morning and dwell in the uttermost parts of the sea, even there Your hand shall lead me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Knowledge, then presence, then this. The three sections build."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Search me, O God, and know my heart; try me, and know my anxieties; and see if there is any wicked way in me, and **lead me in the way everlasting**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A psalm that opens *O LORD, You have searched me and known me* closes by asking Him to do it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The same idea in Jeremiah"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before I formed you in the womb I knew you; before you were born I sanctified you; I ordained you a prophet to the nations.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Paul: *God, who separated me from my mother's womb and called me through His grace*. See the note on Galatians 1:15–16."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word that occurs once.** *Golem* — unformed substance. And the sentence around it says the days were already written down while it was still that."}]}, "Psalms 141:1–2": {"title": "\"Let my prayer stand before You like incense\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David asks for his words to be treated as temple furniture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The urgency"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*LORD, **I call to You. Come quickly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *hasten to me*. It is how a lot of David's psalms open, and he never apologises for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Listen to my voice **when I cry**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not when he composes. When he cries out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let my prayer **stand before You like incense**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *let my prayer be set forth* — **kun**, to be established, fixed, made ready. It is the word used for setting a thing in its proper place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is asking for his words to be positioned where the incense was positioned: in front of the veil, on the golden altar, directly outside the room where God said He would meet them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why that is a large request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David was not a priest. He was of Judah, not Levi, and the incense altar was off limits to him entirely — Uzziah, a king of his own line, tried it and came out a leper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks for his prayer to go where his body could not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let **my lifted hands** be **the evening offering**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The evening sacrifice was at the ninth hour — three in the afternoon — a lamb on the bronze altar, every day without fail."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the hour Elijah prayed on Carmel, the hour Gabriel reached Daniel, and the hour Jesus died."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two altars"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse names both. Incense for the golden altar inside, and the offering for the bronze altar in the court."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David asks for one prayer to count as both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lifted hands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The standard posture for prayer in the Old Testament, and the standard posture of the priest lifting the offering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul keeps it: *I desire that the men pray everywhere, lifting up holy hands.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he prays next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Set a guard, O LORD, over my mouth; keep watch over the door of my lips.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Having asked for his words to be accepted as incense, the next thing he asks is a sentry on his mouth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer given later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every one of them had a harp, and a gold bowl full of burning incense — **and the incense is the prayers of God's people**.* See the note on Revelation 5:7–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David asked for it. John saw the bowls."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the incense was made of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four spices, equal parts, salted, compounded by a perfumer, and unlawful to make for private use. See the note on Exodus 30:34–38."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he is asking to be counted.** A voice, and a pair of raised hands. He puts them in the place of the two things the priesthood did every day and asks God to take them as such."}]}, "Psalms 141:3–4": {"title": "\"Keep watch at the door of my lips\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Having asked for his prayer to be received as incense, David asks for a sentry on the place it comes out of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Set **a guard**, LORD, **over my mouth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shomrah** — a watch, a guard post. The word appears once in the Bible, here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not asking to be silenced. He is asking for a sentry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The door"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep watch at **the door of my lips**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *door* is **dal** — the leaf of a door, the part that swings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A door is not a wall. It opens and shuts, and the point of a guard on it is that some things go through and some do not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not himself. *Set a guard, LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter before, in Psalm 140, he is describing men *who sharpen their tongues like a serpent*. He knows what a mouth does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let my prayer stand before You **like incense**, let my lifted hands be the evening offering.* See the note on Psalms 141:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order is deliberate. He asks for his words to be treated as temple offering, and the next thing he asks is that a guard be posted where they come from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not let **my heart turn toward evil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mouth in verse 3, the heart in verse 4. He moves inward."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *do not incline my heart* — the same verb used of stretching out or bending toward something."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is afraid of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Or **busy itself with wrongdoing**, **sharing the feast of those who do harm**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *to practise wicked deeds with men who work iniquity*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The danger named is not solitary. It is joining in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The table"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let me **never taste their delicacies**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *delicacies* is **manammim** — dainties, pleasant things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not describe their company as repulsive. He calls the food good and asks not to eat it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for instead, in the next verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let the righteous strike me; it shall be a kindness. And let him rebuke me; it shall be excellent oil; let my head not refuse it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He would rather be hit by an honest man than fed by a dishonest one, and he calls the blow oil on the head."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm was written from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The title is *a psalm of David*, and the last verses are a man asking to be kept out of a net set for him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the New Testament asks for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let your words always be gracious, with salt in them.* See the note on Colossians 4:5–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And James: *if anyone does not stumble in word, he is a perfect man, able also to bridle the whole body.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is being asked.** Not for the door to be sealed. For somebody to be standing at it — and the request comes from a man who had just asked for what goes through it to be received as incense."}]}, "Psalms 144:12–14": {"title": "No breach in the walls", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A list of what a settled country looks like, written by a man asking for one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm opens as a war song: *blessed be the LORD my Rock, who trains my hands for war, and my fingers for battle*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the plea: *bow down Your heavens, O LORD, and come down; touch the mountains, and they shall smoke. Flash forth lightning and scatter them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rescue me and deliver me… **from the hand of foreigners, whose mouth speaks lying words***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then, without transition, this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then our sons will be like **sturdy plants, grown tall in their youth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kinti'im megudalim** — like plants grown large."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Full height, early. Nothing stunted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The daughters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our daughters like **carved corner pillars, shaped for a palace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kezaviyyot mechuttavot** — like corner pillars, cut and fashioned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A corner pillar carries two walls. It is the load-bearing member at the joint, and the one that gets the carving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is of strength and craftsmanship together, and it is the daughters who get it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The barns"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our barns will be **full, overflowing with every kind of store**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *furnishing all manner of store*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The flocks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our flocks will multiply **by thousands, by tens of thousands** in the fields.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cattle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our cattle will be **heavy with young**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is difficult and is read either as *bearing well* or *laden*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three negatives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No **breach in the walls**, no **going into exile**, no **cry of grief in our streets**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **perets** — a breach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same noun as Perez-uzzah, the breaking-out. See the note on 2 Samuel 6:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things absent: a broken wall, a deportation, and a wail in the street."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole of it. The list of blessings is children, food, livestock — and then three disasters that have not happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm knows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in the list is ordinary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in it is spectacular. Tall sons, capable daughters, full barns, pregnant cattle, and no sound of grief outside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a list written by somebody who knows what the alternative looks like — and the psalm's first half is about being under attack."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Happy are the people who are in such a state; **happy are the people whose God is the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ashrei** — the word Psalm 1 opens with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice, and the second says where the first came from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some read the two lines as a correction: happy is the nation that has all this — and happier still the one that has Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm quotes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*LORD, **what is man, that You take knowledge of him**? Or the son of man, that You are mindful of him? Man is like a breath; his days are like a passing shadow.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 8 asks it in wonder. This one asks it in the middle of a war."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same picture goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone shall sit under his vine and under his fig tree, and **no one shall make them afraid**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Micah's version, and it ends the same way — on the absence of fear rather than the presence of anything."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the list ends with.** Three things that have not happened. No breach, no exile, no crying in the streets — and a man under attack is the one writing it down."}]}, "Psalms 144:15": {"title": "Happy the people", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two lines, and the second one moves the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Happy the people **who have it so**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ashrei ha'am shekakhah lo** — blessed the people to whom it is thus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It* is the list in the verses before: tall sons, daughters like carved pillars, full barns, multiplying flocks, no breach in the wall, no exile, no crying in the streets. See the note on Psalms 144:12–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Happy the people **whose God is the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ashrei ha'am she-YHWH Elohav**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same opening word, and a different completion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two readings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are old, and both are defensible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One takes them as parallel: happy is the nation with all this, and it is happy because the LORD is its God. Hebrew poetry works in couplets, and this is what couplets usually do."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The other takes the second as a correction: happy is the nation with all that — and happier still the one with Him, whether or not the barns are full."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew does not adjudicate. The word **ashrei** is repeated, which is what makes the second reading available."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ashrei** — the word Psalm 1 opens with. *Blessed is the man who walks not in the counsel of the ungodly.* See the note on Psalms 1:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word behind the beatitudes in the Greek, and it means something closer to *happy* or *fortunate* than to *holy*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm has been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A war song. *Blessed be the LORD my Rock, who trains my hands for war… bow down Your heavens, O LORD, and come down.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the plea for rescue *from the hand of foreigners, whose mouth speaks lying words*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list of blessings is written by a man who is asking for deliverance, not reporting one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that does to the last verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a description of his circumstances. It is what he is asking for, and then what he falls back on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same move is made"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Habakkuk, at the end of his book: *though the fig tree may not blossom, nor fruit be on the vines; though the labour of the olive may fail, and the fields yield no food; though the flock be cut off from the fold, and there be no herd in the stalls — **yet I will rejoice in the LORD, I will joy in the God of my salvation**.* See the note on Habakkuk 2:4–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Habakkuk lists the same agricultural items and removes them one by one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And Paul"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not that I speak in regard to need, for **I have learned in whatever state I am, to be content**… I know how to be abased, and I know how to abound.* See the note on Philippians 4:11–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The psalm before it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD is gracious and full of compassion, slow to anger and great in mercy. **The LORD is good to all**, and His tender mercies are over all His works.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 145, immediately after, and then the last five psalms are all praise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Psalter ends with *let everything that has breath praise the LORD*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the verse does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Names the good life accurately, and then names something that does not depend on it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word repeated.** *Ashrei*, twice. The first happiness is a list of things a man can lose; the second completes the sentence differently."}]}, "Psalms 147:1–3": {"title": "\"He heals the brokenhearted\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm puts two things side by side that do not obviously belong together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three actions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD **rebuilds Jerusalem**. He **gathers the scattered** of Israel. He **heals the brokenhearted** and **binds up their wounds**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A city, a nation, and a person — and they are in one breath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scale drops from a skyline to an individual without any change of tone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Binds up\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chabash** — to bandage, to wrap. It is what you do to a wound with cloth, and it is what a physician does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for *wounds* is the word for pain, hurt, a sore place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here is metaphorical in the Hebrew. It is field-dressing vocabulary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **counts the number of the stars**; He **calls them all by name**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is verse 4, and it is the line the passage is best known for pairing with verse 3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same God binding up one person's wounds is counting a sky nobody has finished counting, and naming every one of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then verse 5: *Great is our Lord, and mighty in power; His understanding is infinite.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stars are there to establish the size of the One doing the bandaging."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stars, and Abraham"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb behind counting the stars is **saphar** — to number, and to recount, to tell."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same word God used when He took Abram outside: *count the stars, if you can even number them.* See the note on Genesis 15:4–5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the psalm is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He rebuilds Jerusalem. He gathers the scattered.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Written for people coming home from exile to a ruined city. Not an untroubled congregation — a returning one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm says God is not impressed by"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Further down: *He does not delight in the strength of the horse; He takes no pleasure in the legs of a man.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two fastest things in that world, and both are dismissed in one verse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD takes pleasure in those who fear Him, in those who hope in His mercy.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it opens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hallelujah! How **good** it is to sing to our God, how **pleasant**, how **fitting** the praise.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words for it. The psalm argues that praise is good, that it is enjoyable, and that it is appropriate — and then gives four verses of reasons."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the psalm ends on.** *He declares His word to Jacob, His statutes and His judgements to Israel. He has not dealt thus with any nation.* The final boast of the psalm is not the stars or the city. It is that He talked to them."}]}, "Psalms 150:6": {"title": "Everything that has breath", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last verse of the Psalter, and the qualification is a pulse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let **everything that has breath** praise the LORD. **Hallelujah!***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kol hanneshamah tehallel Yah**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Neshamah** — breath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word in Genesis: *the LORD God formed man of the dust of the ground, and **breathed into his nostrils the breath of life***. See the note on Genesis 2:7–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the general word for spirit or wind. The specific noun for the breath God put in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The qualification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything that has it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not everything that understands, believes, deserves, or has got its life in order. Everything that is breathing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bar is set at the lowest possible point on purpose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve verses of instruments: trumpet, lute, harp, timbrel, strings, flutes, loud cymbals, clashing cymbals."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And dance. And *praise Him in His mighty firmament*, and *praise Him for His mighty acts*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every method the ancient world had, and then a verse that needs no equipment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hallelu-Yah** — praise Yah. The shortened divine name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Psalter's final word is the imperative that gave English *hallelujah*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the book started"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed is the man who walks not in the counsel of the ungodly.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It opens with a single man deciding where to walk. It closes with everything that breathes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five books"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Psalter is arranged in five books, each ending with a doxology."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last of them is not a verse but five whole psalms — 146 to 150, every one beginning and ending with *hallelujah*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book that contains *my God, my God, why have You forsaken Me* and *out of the depths I have cried* is arranged to end this way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm does not mention"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any circumstance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is no reason given in verse 6, no *because*, and no condition. Just the breath and the instruction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other place breath is decisive"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He said to me, \"Prophesy to the breath… **Come from the four winds, O breath, and breathe on these slain, that they may live**.\"* See the note on Ezekiel 37:9–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A valley of bones, and what makes the difference is breath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And the upper room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He breathed on them, and said to them, \"**Receive the Holy Spirit**.\"* See the note on John 20:21–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **enephusesen** — the same verb the Septuagint uses in Genesis 2:7. It occurs twice in the whole Greek Bible: the making of Adam, and this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says the breath became"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All Scripture is **given by inspiration of God**.* — **theopneustos**, God-breathed. See the note on 2 Timothy 3:16–17."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The bar.** Breath. Not understanding, not worthiness, not a good year — the Psalter ends by setting the qualification as low as it can go and then saying *everything*."}]}, "Psalms 16:5–8": {"title": "\"Even in the night my heart instructs me\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David describes what he was allotted, using the vocabulary of a land survey."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The portion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD is **all I have, my cup, my share**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is the language of inheritance — *the portion of my inheritance and my cup.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is what the Levites were told: *I am your portion and your inheritance among the children of Israel.* They got no territory and were given Him instead. See the note on Ezekiel 44:28–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David was a king with a kingdom, and he uses the landless tribe's language about himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measuring line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The lines have fallen for me in **lovely places**; yes, **the inheritance I have is beautiful**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The *lines* are surveyor's cords — how land was measured and allotted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David wrote this having spent years in caves, hunted by Saul, and later driven out of his own capital by his son. He calls the plot he was given lovely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The night"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will bless the LORD who counsels me; **even in the night my heart instructs me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *my reins instruct me in the night seasons* — the kidneys, which in Hebrew idiom are the seat of the deepest inner life, below thought."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something in him is being taught while he is awake in the dark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The counsel comes first in the verse and the night instruction second. What he has been given in the day is what surfaces at three in the morning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Set at the right hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have **set the LORD always before me**; **He is at my right hand**, I will not be shaken.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *set* is deliberate — placed, positioned. It is something David does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the right hand is the position of the advocate, or of the strong man who defends you. In Zechariah 3 it is where the accuser was standing. See the note on Zechariah 3:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here it is occupied by somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Peter takes this psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At Pentecost, in his first sermon, he quotes the verses that follow:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore my heart is glad and my glory rejoices; my flesh also will rest in hope. For You will not leave my soul in Sheol, nor will You allow Your Holy One to see corruption.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says David *spoke concerning the resurrection of Christ, that His soul was not left in Hades, nor did His flesh see corruption.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes it too, at Antioch."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the psalm ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will show me the path of life; in Your presence is fullness of joy; at Your right hand are pleasures forevermore.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the psalm is titled.** *A Michtam of David* — a word nobody has definitively translated, possibly from a root meaning to engrave, or gold. Six psalms carry it, and every one of them was written in trouble."}]}, "Psalms 18:16–24": {"title": "\"He brought me into a wide open place\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David wrote this the day the LORD delivered him from all his enemies and from Saul. It is the longest psalm of rescue in the book, and this is the middle of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Reaching down"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **reached down from on high** and took hold of me. He **drew me up** out of the deep waters.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew behind *drew me up* is the verb in Moses' name — *because I drew him out of the water.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David uses the language of a baby lifted out of a river to describe his own rescue from Saul."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"From those too strong for me\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From my mighty enemy, from those who hated me, **from those too strong for me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He concedes the point without embarrassment. They were stronger. That is the premise of the rescue, not an objection to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They came at me **on my day of ruin**, but the LORD was my support.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the day of my calamity*. He does not say there was no such day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole passage takes for granted that the trouble happened, that he went under, and that the deep waters were deep. He is not describing an escape from the valley. He is describing being drawn up out of the middle of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wide place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **brought me into a wide open place**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Merchab** — a broad place, open ground, room. It is the opposite of the Hebrew word for trouble, which is built on the idea of being pressed and narrow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Distress is being squeezed; deliverance is space. And note the verb: He brought him *into* it — out of one place and into another."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He rescued me **because He delighted in me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the reason stated in the text, and it is stated before any of the lines about clean hands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a word of pleasure and desire. He wanted to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Clean hands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD rewarded me for my right living. For clean hands He repaid me… I was blameless before Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David says this in a psalm written after Saul had hunted him for years and he had twice refused to put out his hand against the LORD's anointed when he had him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The delight comes first in the passage, and the record follows it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the psalm appears twice.** It is Psalm 18 and it is also 2 Samuel 22 — the same song printed once in the songbook and once in the history. Whoever put the books together thought the day David was drawn out of deep water was worth recording in both."}]}, "Psalms 19:1–4": {"title": "The speech with no words", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The sky is described as talking, and then as having nothing to say it with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The heavens are **telling** the glory of God. The skies **pour out** the work of His hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *telling* is **saphar** — to count, to recount, to narrate. It is the same verb God used when He took Abram outside: *count the stars, if you can even number them.* See the note on Genesis 15:4–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbering and narrating are the same word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Day and night"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Day after day they keep **speaking**. Night after night they keep **teaching**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verbs are continuous — pouring forth, and declaring."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two shifts, and neither one stops. Whatever is being said is being said around the clock."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contradiction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is **no speech**, there are **no words**. Their voice is **never heard**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three negatives in a row, immediately after four verbs of speaking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It says nothing and it will not stop saying it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And then"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet their sound goes out **through all the earth**, their words **to the end of the world**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No language, and universal reach. The one message on earth that needs no translation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul puts this verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 10, in the middle of his argument about preaching:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But I say, have they not heard? Yes indeed: **\"Their sound has gone out to all the earth, and their words to the ends of the world.\"***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes this psalm as evidence that the message has been available."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the heavens **He pitched a tent for the sun**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that He set a tent for it — the sun has an address."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the verses following describe it *like a bridegroom coming out of his chamber, and rejoicing like a strong man to run its race.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Halfway through, without warning, it changes subject entirely:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The law of the LORD is perfect, converting the soul; the testimony of the LORD is sure, making wise the simple.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six statements about Scripture, matching the six about the sky."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two books, in one psalm — the heavens, and the word — and David sets them side by side without explaining the join."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let the words of my mouth and the meditation of my heart be acceptable in Your sight, O LORD, my strength and my Redeemer.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sky, Scripture, and then a man asking that his own speech be acceptable."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Job is asked about the same sky.** *Can you bind the beautiful cords of the Pleiades, or loosen the belt of Orion? Can you **lead out the constellations in their seasons**?* God asks about specific star groups by name, and asks whether Job can steer them. See the note on Job 38:31–33."}]}, "Psalms 19:10–11": {"title": "Honey straight from the comb", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two comparisons, and both are chosen because they are the best of their kind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm has been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The heavens declare the glory of God; and the firmament shows His handiwork.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is no speech nor language where their voice is not heard.* See the note on Psalms 19:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six verses on the sun running its circuit like a strong man to run a race, and then an abrupt turn to the law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The law of the LORD is perfect, converting the soul; the testimony of the LORD is sure, making wise the simple.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Six statements, each with a name for the word, an adjective, and an effect. See the note on Psalms 19:7–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They are **more to be desired than gold**, than **heaps of the purest gold**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mipaz rav** — than much fine gold."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Paz** is refined gold, the highest grade. And then *much* of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David does not compare the word to gold generally. He raises the comparison twice — the best gold, and a lot of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They are **sweeter than honey**, than **honey straight from the comb**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nophet tzuphim** — the honey that drips from the comb by itself, before pressing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Again the superlative form. Not honey, but the first drip."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why these two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gold is the standard of value. Honey is the standard of pleasure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In a world with no sugar, honey was the sweetest thing anybody had ever tasted, and this is the sweetest kind of honey."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm picks the top of each scale and says the word is above it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the honey turns up again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How sweet are Your words to my taste, **sweeter than honey to my mouth!*** See the note on Psalms 119:97–104."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in Ezekiel: *So I ate it, and **it was in my mouth like honey in sweetness***, of a scroll."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And John: *it was as sweet as honey in my mouth. But when I had eaten it, my stomach became bitter*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scroll is sweet going in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first use"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moreover **by them Your servant is warned**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nizhar** — warned, admonished, enlightened. The root carries the idea of shining."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word the psalm might have used of the sun in the first half."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **in keeping them there is great reward**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **eqev rav** — literally *much heel*, an idiom meaning consequence, the thing that follows on behind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not payment for keeping them. What comes along after."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David asks immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who can understand his errors? **Cleanse me from secret faults**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Keep back Your servant also **from presumptuous sins**; let them not have dominion over me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He praises the law and then asks to be cleared of what it has just shown him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let **the words of my mouth** and **the meditation of my heart** be acceptable in Your sight, O LORD, my strength and my Redeemer.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm has been about God's speech — in the heavens, and in the law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It ends by asking that the psalmist's own speech be acceptable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole psalm does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sky, then scroll, then self."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three witnesses, narrowing."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The comparisons.** Not gold and honey — *refined* gold in heaps, and the honey that drips from the comb before pressing. The top of both scales."}]}, "Psalms 22:1–5": {"title": "\"My God, my God\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The opening line of this psalm is the sentence Jesus cried from the cross, in Aramaic, at the ninth hour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My God, my God, **why have You abandoned me?***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not compose it there. He quoted it. And in that culture, naming the first line of a psalm called up the whole psalm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the rest of it and it is a description of a crucifixion written a thousand years before the Romans invented one: *they pierced my hands and my feet… I can count all my bones… they divide my garments among them and cast lots for my clothing… all who see me mock me, they shake their heads.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"My God\" — twice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Even in the abandonment the possessive holds. He says *my* God, twice, to the One He is saying has left Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is different here from every other psalm of distress"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I cry out **by day, but You do not answer**, by night, but I find no rest.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David's psalms of trouble almost always turn on a rescue that comes. This one records the silence and does not explain it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet **You are holy**, enthroned on the praises of Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word *yet* carries it. Nothing is being accused. In the middle of the forsaking, holiness is affirmed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three verbs about the fathers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In You our fathers **trusted**. They **trusted**, and You **delivered** them. They **cried** to You and were **rescued**. They trusted in You and were **not put to shame**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone before Him trusted and was delivered. That is what makes the first line what it is: the pattern held for all of them, and it did not hold here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the one exception in the history of the psalm. He trusted, and He was not delivered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the psalm ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not stay there. From the middle it turns: *You have answered me.* And it closes on a proclamation to a people not yet born — *they shall come and declare His righteousness to a people who will be born, that **He has done it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three Hebrew words at the end. The Greek of the New Testament renders the same thought in one: *tetelestai*. It is finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three psalms together"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**22** — the cross, forsaken, *He has done it*", "**23** — the shepherd, the valley, the table, goodness and mercy following", "**24** — *lift up your heads, O gates… the King of glory shall come in*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Death, then care, then the King coming in through the gates. They stand in that order in the book. See the notes on Psalms 23:1–3 and Psalms 24:7–8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in the psalm's opening.** Any answer to the question. He asks *why*, and no reply is recorded. The answer is on the other side of the psalm, in the last line, and it is not an explanation — it is a completed work."}]}, "Psalms 22:16–18": {"title": "\"They have pierced My hands and My feet\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David wrote this around 1000 BC. Crucifixion had not been invented."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When the method appeared"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Persians began impaling people some five centuries after David. The Romans developed crucifixion into what it became, and it was not in use anywhere in David's world."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel's own method of execution was stoning. Nothing in David's experience involved piercing a man's hands and feet and leaving him where people could stand and stare."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They have **pierced my hands and my feet**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words, written by a man describing something he had no category for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What else is in the passage"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Dogs surround me* — the term Jews used for Gentiles, and Romans did the work", "*a band of evildoers has closed in*", "*I can **count all my bones*** — what hanging by the arms does to the ribcage", "*they **stare and gloat** over me* — the crowd standing and watching", "*they **divide my clothes** among them and **cast lots** for my garment*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "That last one is two separate actions, and John records both: the soldiers divided His outer garments four ways, and then came to the tunic, *woven in one piece from the top*, and said *let us not tear it, but cast lots for it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They divided some and gambled for one, because of how the tunic was made. David wrote both halves of it a thousand years earlier."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Not one bone broken"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I can count all my bones.* And in the same psalm's world, Exodus 12 said of the Passover lamb: *nor shall you break one of its bones.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The soldiers came to break the legs of the crucified men to finish them, and found Him already dead, and did not break His."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm opens with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My God, my God, why have You abandoned me?* — the sentence He quoted from the cross at the ninth hour. See the note on Psalms 22:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Naming the first line of a psalm called up the whole psalm. He was pointing at this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And how it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has done it.* Three Hebrew words at the close, and the Greek for the same thought is one: **tetelestai**. It is finished."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in the psalm.** Any hint that David is describing his own life. Nothing recorded of him matches it. He wrote a first-person account of an execution that would not exist for five hundred years, in a country that never practised it."}]}, "Psalms 23:1–3": {"title": "\"He lays me down\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The best-known psalm in the world, and the verbs in its first three lines are all His."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does what"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He lays me down… He leads me… He restores… He guides me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verbs, one subject. The sheep in this psalm is not described as following well, finding pasture, or keeping up. Everything active belongs to the shepherd."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one thing said about the sheep is *I lack nothing* — a state, not an achievement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Lying down"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He lays me down in green pastures.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sheep do not lie down when they are hungry, frightened, or bothered by other animals. A resting flock is the sign that every one of those has been dealt with."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew has the shepherd causing it. He does not merely permit the rest; he brings the conditions about."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Still waters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is *waters of rest* — quiet water, water that has stopped moving. Sheep will not drink from a fast stream; the noise and movement frighten them, and a wet fleece will drown them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A shepherd in that country dammed a pool or found a still place. The provision is shaped to what the animal can actually use."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He restores my soul\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *he brings my soul back* — the verb for returning, turning around. It is used elsewhere of bringing back a wanderer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not merely refreshment. Retrieval."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He guides me along right paths **for the sake of His name**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The motive is stated, and it is not the sheep's merit. The shepherd's reputation is bound up with where the flock ends up."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the psalm does not skip.** Verse 4 goes straight into the valley of the shadow of death, and the shepherd is still there — *You are with me.* The green pastures and the valley are on the same path, led by the same person."}]}, "Psalms 23:4": {"title": "Through the valley", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and everything in it turns on a single preposition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Through\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even when I walk **through** the valley of the shadow of death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not into it, not down to it, and not around it. Through."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word assumes an entrance and an exit. Nobody is promised a route that avoids the valley; the promise is about what the valley is — a passage, not a destination."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I walk\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not run, not flee. The pace does not change."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The valley"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tsalmaveth** — a compound of *shadow* and *death*, and it is used of deep darkness, the places where light does not reach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A shadow requires a light source and an object, and it can frighten but it cannot touch. David is a shepherd writing about the ravines he walked his flock through, where the walls are high and the sun does not get in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm changes person"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the psalm from the beginning and watch the pronouns."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD is **my** shepherd… **He** makes me lie down… **He** leads me… **He** restores my soul.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is being talked about. Then the valley:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **You** are with me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The moment the darkness comes, the psalm stops speaking about Him and starts speaking to Him. That is where He stops being the subject of a sentence and becomes the person being addressed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"I will fear no evil\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given is not that there is no evil in the valley. It is *for You are with me.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rod and the staff"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two implements, and they do different jobs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **rod** is the club — a shepherd's weapon, carried against what comes at the flock. The **staff** is the crook, for guiding and lifting a sheep out of a place it has got itself into."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One is used on the enemy, one on the sheep, and both are listed as comfort."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is on the other side of the valley.** *You prepare a table before me in the presence of my enemies.* He does not clear the room first. The enemies are still there and the meal is served anyway."}]}, "Psalms 23:5": {"title": "A table in front of them", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four lines, and the shepherd has become a host."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm has got to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verses 1 to 4 are pasture, water, paths and a valley — shepherd's country."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here the imagery changes without warning. There is a table, oil, and a cup. This is a house, and the reader is a guest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The table"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You **set a table before me** in the presence of my enemies.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to arrange, to lay out in order. It is unhurried."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In the presence of my enemies\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are still there. Not defeated, not removed, not converted — present, and watching."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not describe a rescue from the room. It describes a meal served in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oil"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You **anoint my head with oil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things this could be, and both are in the Old Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A host poured oil on a guest's head at a feast — ordinary hospitality, and a mark of honour. Jesus notes its absence at Simon the Pharisee's table: *you did not anoint My head with oil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And a shepherd poured oil on a sheep's head against flies and infection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm has been about a shepherd for four verses and is about a host in this one. Both readings sit inside the same line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cup"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My cup **overflows**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *my cup is saturation* — running over the top."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A host who fills a cup to the brim has been generous. A host who keeps pouring after it is full is making a point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the enemies get"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing. They are not fed, not addressed, and not asked to leave."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are made to watch, and the watching is part of what the verse is describing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Surely **goodness and mercy will pursue me** all the days of my life, and I will dwell in the house of the LORD forever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *pursue* is a hunter's word — the verb used of Pharaoh's chariots chasing Israel. See the note on Psalms 23:6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The table is in a house, and the last line says the guest is moving in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shape of the whole psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Pasture, water, valley, table, house. It moves outdoors to indoors, and from *He* to *You* at the valley and never goes back."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the enemies were in David's life.** Saul with three thousand men, the Philistines, and eventually his own son. The man who wrote about a table set in front of enemies had eaten a good many meals looking over his shoulder."}]}, "Psalms 23:6": {"title": "The two that follow", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last verse of the psalm, and the verb in it is not a gentle one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Pursue\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Surely goodness and mercy will **pursue** me all the days of my life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **radaph** — to chase, to hunt, to run after. It is the word used for an army in pursuit, and for Pharaoh's chariots after Israel."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Most translations soften it to *follow*, which sounds like something trailing along behind. The verb is a chase."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things are hunting him down, and they are goodness and mercy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tov** — goodness. **Chesed** — the covenant word, steadfast love, loyal kindness. It is the word repeated twenty-six times in Psalm 136, once per verse: *His mercy endures forever.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"All the days\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one. Not the good ones, not the ones he handled well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The valley is in verse 4 and the enemies are in verse 5, and verse 6 says *all the days of my life* without excluding either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What a shepherd's psalm ends with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm has moved from pasture to water to valley to a table with enemies watching, and then to a house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And I will **dwell in the house of the LORD forever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to sit, to remain, to settle — the word for taking up residence, not visiting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David wrote this. He was a man who spent his life in tents and caves and on the run, and who was never allowed to build the house he wanted to build."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The whole psalm in its place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set between the cross and the coming King:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Psalm 22** — forsaken, pierced, *He has done it*", "**Psalm 23** — the shepherd, the valley, the table, and the house", "**Psalm 24** — *lift up your heads, O gates, that the King of glory may come in*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the middle one. It is the psalm for now: the walking, the days, the pursuit. See the notes on Psalms 22:1–5 and Psalms 24:7–8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The first verse and the last.** *The LORD is my shepherd, I shall not want* — and *I will dwell in the house of the LORD forever.* It opens with a want that does not exist and closes with an address that does not change."}]}, "Psalms 24:7–8": {"title": "Lift up your heads, O gates", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A command shouted at architecture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being addressed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lift up your heads, **O gates**! Be lifted up, **you ancient doors**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the people. The gates."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm tells the doorways to make themselves higher, because what is coming through will not fit under them as they stand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Ancient doors\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *doors of old*, everlasting doors. Whatever gates these are, they are older than the city."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question and the answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who is this King of glory?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm asks it, and answers it, and then asks again a few lines later and answers again. It is written to be sung antiphonally — one voice at the gate, one voice inside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD, **strong and mighty**, the LORD, **mighty in battle**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer is military. He is identified by a fight He has been in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which battle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm before it is the shepherd. The psalm before that is the cross — *forsaken*, *pierced*, and *He has done it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the time the gates are told to lift their heads, the battle referred to is over. He is not going out to it; He is coming back from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three psalms as one sequence"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**22** — the Saviour dying, forsaken, the work finished", "**23** — the Shepherd, present, walking the days and the valley with His own", "**24** — the King of glory coming in through the gates"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past, present, and what is still ahead. They stand in that order in the book and they have always been read that way. See the notes on Psalms 22:1–5 and Psalms 23:6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the psalm opens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The earth is the LORD's, and everything in it, the world and all who live in it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The claim is made before the King arrives at the gate. He owns the city He is being asked to be let into."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who goes up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The middle of the psalm asks who may ascend the hill of the LORD, and answers: *he who has clean hands and a pure heart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then it names the generation that seeks Him, and the last line of that section is *they will receive blessing from the LORD, and righteousness from the God of their salvation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Righteousness is listed as something received from Him, in the same breath as the blessing."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line of the psalm.** *The LORD of hosts — He is the King of glory.* The title is Yahweh Sabaoth, the LORD of armies. The gates are opened for the commander, coming home."}]}, "Psalms 27:4–6": {"title": "One thing I ask", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A soldier's psalm, and the request in the middle of it is not for what you would expect."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What surrounds it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Though **an army may encamp against me**, my heart shall not fear; though **war may rise against me**, in this I will be confident.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the verse immediately before."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the psalm opens: *the LORD is my light and my salvation; whom shall I fear?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*One thing I ask of the LORD, **this is what my whole heart longs for**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *one thing I have desired; that will I seek*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man surrounded by an army asks for one thing, and it is not the army's removal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **live in the house of the LORD all the days of my life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **gaze on the beauty of the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And to **seek Him in His temple**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *gaze* is **chazah** — to behold, to see in vision. The same root as **chazon**. See the note on Proverbs 29:14–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Beauty** is **noam** — pleasantness, delightfulness. The word is rare and it is the only time it is used of God in this way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David could not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Live in the house of the LORD. He was not a priest, and only priests dwelt at the sanctuary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it anyway, and the psalm does not resolve the impossibility."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shelter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For He will **hide me in His shelter on the day of trouble**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **sukkah** — a booth, a temporary shelter. The kind built for the feast."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will **conceal me in His tent** and **set me high upon a rock**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hidden, and then raised. Two opposite movements — out of sight, and up where everything can see."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now **my head is lifted above the enemies around me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are still around him. The psalm does not say they left."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And I will **offer in His tent sacrifices with shouts of joy**. **I will sing, I will make music** to the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *sacrifices of shouting* — **teruah**, the blast, the war-shout."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He offers a war-cry as worship."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one thing elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Only one thing is really needed**. Mary has chosen the good portion.\"* See the note on Luke 10:38–42."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Paul: *one thing I do, forgetting those things which are behind and reaching forward to those which are ahead.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three men, three lists of one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the psalm ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I would have lost heart, unless I had believed that I would see the goodness of the LORD in the land of the living.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Wait on the LORD; be of good courage, and He shall strengthen your heart. **Wait, I say, on the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last word of the psalm is *wait*, said twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Report an outcome. The army is there at the start and nothing in the psalm says it left."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he asks for with an army camped around him.** Not deliverance, in this verse. One thing — to live in a house he had no right to live in, and to look at something."}]}, "Psalms 29:6–9": {"title": "The voice", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A psalm about a thunderstorm crossing the country from north to south."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The voice of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **qol YHWH**, and it occurs seven times in the psalm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Qol** is both *voice* and *thunder*, and the psalm uses the ambiguity throughout."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the storm starts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before this one: *the voice of the LORD is over the waters… the voice of the LORD breaks the cedars, yes, the LORD splinters the cedars of Lebanon*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lebanon is the north. The storm comes in off the Mediterranean."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mountains"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He makes **Lebanon leap like a calf**, **Sirion like a young wild ox**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sirion** is the Sidonian name for Mount Hermon — nine thousand feet, snow-capped, the highest thing in the region."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm has it skipping like livestock."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fire"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The voice of the LORD **strikes with flashing fire**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *hews out flames of fire*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lightning, described as something cut."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wilderness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The voice of the LORD **shakes the wilderness**. The LORD shakes **the desert of Kadesh**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Kadesh is the south, in the Negev."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The storm has crossed the entire country in four verses — sea, Lebanon, Hermon, and now the southern desert."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The voice of the LORD **twists the oaks and strips the forests bare**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is ambiguous and can be read *makes the deer calve*, which some translations follow. The consonants allow both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way something is being brought forcibly to an end or a beginning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The temple"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **in His temple, all of it cries, Glory!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kullo omer kavod** — all of it says, Glory."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Outside: mountains skipping, forests stripped, the desert shaking."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Inside: one word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The structure of the psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It opens in heaven — *give unto the LORD, O you mighty ones, give unto the LORD glory and strength*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It moves through the storm, north to south."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it ends sitting down: *the LORD sat enthroned at the Flood, and the LORD sits as King forever*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD will **give strength to His people**; the LORD will **bless His people with peace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven verses of a storm tearing across a country, and the last line is peace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shalom**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is under all of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD sat enthroned at the Flood.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mabbul** — the word used only of Noah's flood, and here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one storm that covered the world, and the psalm's claim is that He was seated during it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the storm imagery goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And behold, there arose a great tempest on the sea, so that the boat was covered with the waves. **But He was asleep**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then He arose and rebuked the wind, and said to the sea, \"Peace, be still!\" And the wind ceased, and there was a great calm.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "See the note on Mark 4:35–41."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The men in the boat ask *who can this be, that even the wind and the sea obey Him?* — and this psalm is the answer they had been singing all their lives."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is inside while the outside is being flattened.** One word. Mountains skipping, forests stripped, the desert shaking — and in the temple, *Glory*."}]}, "Psalms 3:3–4": {"title": "The One who lifts my head", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first psalm with a title, and the situation behind it is the worst week of David's life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A psalm of David when he fled from Absalom his son.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His son had spent four years standing at the gate telling people the king would not hear them, and then declared himself king at Hebron."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So David said, \"Arise, and let us flee… lest he overtake us suddenly and bring disaster upon us.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He went up the Mount of Olives barefoot, weeping, with his head covered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*LORD, **how they have increased who trouble me!** Many are they who rise up against me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Many are they who say of me, \"**There is no help for him in God**.\" Selah*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The taunt is theological. They are not saying he will lose. They are saying God has finished with him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which, after Bathsheba and Uriah and Nathan's *the sword shall never depart from your house*, is an argument with something behind it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **You, LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ve'attah YHWH** — and You, LORD."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words, and they carry the whole psalm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shield"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Are **a shield all around me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **magen ba'adi** — a shield about me."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a shield in front. Around."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The glory"}, {"k": "p", "t": "***My glory**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kevodi**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is walking out of his own capital with his head covered and dust on it, and he calls God his glory."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The head"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the One who lifts my head**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **umerim roshi** — and the lifter of my head."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the idiom means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Covering the head was the posture of mourning and shame — *David went up… and had his head covered*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lifting the head is the reversal. Pharaoh's butler had his head lifted and was restored; the baker had his lifted off."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I **cried out loud** to the LORD, and **He answered me** from His holy hill.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **qoli el-YHWH eqra** — with my voice I call to the LORD."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aloud. Not silently."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the answer comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From **His holy hill**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mount Zion, which David has just been driven off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is on the run from the city, and the answer comes from the hill he is running away from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does that night"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I lay down and slept; **I awoke, for the LORD sustained me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ani shachavti va'ishanah heqitzoti** — I lay down, I slept, I woke."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs, and every one of them is unremarkable. A man being hunted by his son's army sleeps through the night and gets up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will not be afraid of **ten thousands of people** who have set themselves against me all around.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word — **saviv**, all around — used of the shield in verse 3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are around him, and so is the shield."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Salvation belongs to the LORD. **Your blessing is upon Your people**. Selah*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **laYHWH hayeshu'ah**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man fleeing for his life ends the psalm by asking for a blessing on the nation, part of which is currently chasing him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens to Absalom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Caught by the head in the branches of a terebinth, and killed by Joab against orders."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*O my son Absalom — my son, my son Absalom — if only I had died in your place!*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The three verbs.** *I lay down, I slept, I awoke.* Written by a man whose son's army was hunting him, and the only remarkable thing about them is that they are unremarkable."}]}, "Psalms 30:4–5": {"title": "\"Weeping may stay for the night\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two lines built as a pair, and the pairing is the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **His anger lasts only a moment**, but **His favour lasts a lifetime**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *a moment* is the blink of an eye — the shortest measure the language has."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Against it: **chayyim**, life, a lifetime."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are not the same size, and the psalm is not saying they balance. It is saying one is a blink and the other is the whole of the days."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second pair"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Weeping may **stay for the night**, but **joy comes with the morning**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *stay* is to lodge, to spend the night — the word for a traveller taking a room."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Weeping is a guest. It checks in, and it does not have a permanent address."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the Hebrew day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Evening, then morning — the way Genesis 1 counts every one of the six days."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the night is, the direction of travel in that calendar is always toward the light."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being addressed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sing to the LORD, **O you His faithful**. Give thanks to His holy name.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two lines about anger and weeping are addressed to His own people. It is not a general description of how life goes; it is what the covenant faithful are told about their own experience."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David had been through"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm's heading is *a song at the dedication of the house of David*, and the body of it is a man recounting a rescue:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*O LORD my God, I cried out to You, and You healed me. O LORD, You brought my soul up from the grave; You have kept me alive.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And earlier: *in my prosperity I said, \"I shall never be moved\"… You hid Your face, and I was troubled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been confident, then undone, then healed, and this psalm is written from the far side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have turned for me **my mourning into dancing**; You have put off my sackcloth and clothed me with gladness — to the end that my glory may sing praise to You and not be silent.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *turned* is the word for overturning, converting a thing into something else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not mourning followed by dancing. Mourning made into it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word Isaiah uses for judgement.** *His strange work… His alien task* — foreign, not native to Him. This psalm is the same claim in a song: the anger is a blink, the favour is the lifetime. See the note on Isaiah 28:20–22."}]}, "Psalms 34:11–14": {"title": "Chase it down", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm turns into a lesson halfway through, and the teacher gathers the children."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A Psalm of David when he pretended madness before Abimelech, who drove him away, and he departed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had fled to Gath — a Philistine city, carrying Goliath's sword — was recognised, and *changed his behaviour before them, feigned madness, scratched on the doors of the gate, and let his saliva fall down on his beard*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm that opens *I will bless the LORD at all times* was written out of that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The call"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Come close, **children**, and listen.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **banim** — sons."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The address of a wisdom teacher. Proverbs opens the same way: *my son, hear the instruction of your father*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he will teach"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will teach you **to walk in awe of the LORD**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yir'at YHWH alammedchem** — the fear of the LORD I will teach you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is presented as a subject that can be taught. Not a temperament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who among you **loves being alive**, who wants **long days full of good**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mi-ha'ish hechaphetz chayyim** — who is the man delighting in life?"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ohev yamim lir'ot tov** — loving days to see good."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks for a show of hands before giving the answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **keep your tongue from evil**, keep your **lips from telling lies**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **netzor leshonecha mera** — guard your tongue from evil."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Natzar** — to watch, to guard as a sentry guards a post."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why speech first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer to a question about long life is about the mouth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not diet, not danger, not work. The psalm names the tongue as the first item."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Turn away from wrong and **do what is good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two movements, negative and positive. Departure and action."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Go after peace, **chase it down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **baqqesh shalom veradpehu** — seek peace and pursue it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Radaph** — to chase, to hunt, to run after. The verb used of an army pursuing a retreating enemy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that implies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peace does not stay put."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not say *keep the peace*. It says run after it, which assumes it is moving away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The eyes of the LORD are on the righteous, and **His ears are open to their cry**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD is near to those who have a broken heart, and saves such as have a contrite spirit.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line about bones"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He guards all his bones; **not one of them is broken**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John quotes it at the cross: *for these things were done that the Scripture should be fulfilled, \"Not one of His bones shall be broken.\"* See the note on John 19:31–34."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Peter quotes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For \"**He who would love life and see good days, let him refrain his tongue from evil**… let him seek peace and pursue it.\"* See the note on 1 Peter 3:10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four verses, quoted whole, into a letter to persecuted congregations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other line the psalm is known for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Oh, **taste and see that the LORD is good**; blessed is the man who trusts in Him!* See the note on Psalms 34:8–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Quoted by Peter as well, two chapters earlier."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The answer to the question.** Asked who wants a long life, the psalm's first instruction is about the tongue — and the last one is a hunting verb."}]}, "Psalms 34:8–10": {"title": "Taste and see", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most physical invitation in the Psalter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heading: *a psalm of David when he pretended madness before Abimelech, who drove him away, and he departed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same episode as Psalm 56 — Gath, the Philistine city, scrabbling at the gate with saliva in his beard. See the note on Psalms 56:8–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He got out alive and wrote two psalms about it, and this is the cheerful one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The form"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An acrostic. Each verse begins with the next letter of the Hebrew alphabet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means the psalm was built to be memorised, and that a man wrote a teaching poem about the day he escaped by pretending to be insane."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The invitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Taste and see for yourself **how good the LORD is**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ta'amu ur'u** — taste and see."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two senses, and the first one requires putting something in your mouth. You can see a thing from across a room. Tasting means it is already inside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The beatitude"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Oh, the happiness of the one who **runs to Him for shelter**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ashrei** — the word Psalm 1 opens with. And **chasah**, to take refuge, to flee to for protection."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is a person running to something, not resting in it. It is the verb for getting under cover."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fear"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Stand in awe of the LORD, **you who are His**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *fear the LORD, you His saints* — **qedoshav**, His holy ones."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the outsiders. The instruction is addressed to people who already belong."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For those who love Him **never go without**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *there is no lack to those who fear Him* — **ein machsor**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even **young lions grow hungry and weak**, but those who seek the LORD **lack nothing good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kephirim** — young lions, at their strongest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The strongest hunters in the region, and the psalm says they go short."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The contrast is between what strength secures and what seeking secures, and the strength loses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm has already said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I sought the LORD, and He heard me, and delivered me from all my fears.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This poor man cried out, and the LORD heard him, and saved him out of all his troubles.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The angel of the LORD encamps all around those who fear Him, and delivers them.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Come, you children, listen to me; I will teach you the fear of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who is the man who desires life, and loves many days, that he may see good? **Keep your tongue from evil**, and your lips from speaking deceit. Depart from evil and do good; **seek peace and pursue it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter quotes that paragraph at length in his first letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line about the bones"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He guards all his bones; **not one of them is broken**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John quotes it at the crucifixion: *for these things were done that the Scripture should be fulfilled, \"Not one of His bones shall be broken.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The soldiers broke the legs of the two beside Him and found Him already dead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same clause is in the Passover instructions: *nor shall you break one of its bones*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Peter does with verse 8"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If indeed you have **tasted that the Lord is gracious**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes it as the reason for laying aside malice and desiring the pure milk of the word."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb that makes it different.** *Taste.* Seeing works at a distance. The psalm's first instruction requires the thing to already be in your mouth."}]}, "Psalms 36:7–9": {"title": "In Your light we see light", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses in the middle of a psalm that opens with a description of wickedness. David turns from that to this without transition, and the change of temperature is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shadow of the wings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People take shelter under the shadow of Your wings.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for shelter is the word for taking refuge — running to a rock or a stronghold."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The wings are the cherubim wings over the mercy seat, and also a bird's over its young. Both pictures are in the Psalms, and both are about a small thing being covered by something larger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Feasting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **feast on** the rich abundance of Your house, You give them drink from Your river of delights.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is the one for being saturated, drinking to the full. The noun behind *rich abundance* is *deshen* — the fat, the richest portion, the part of the offering that was burned as the choice part."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Delights** is **adanim** — the plural of the word Eden is built from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two \"withs\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For with You is the fountain of life, and in Your light we see light.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not near You. **With** You. The source is not somewhere He points to; it is where He is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Fountain** is *maqor* — a spring, the origin of running water, as against a cistern that only holds what was poured in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In Your light we see light\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence is circular on purpose. You do not bring a light to God in order to find Him. You see by His."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same logic as the lamp in Psalm 119 and the lamp in 2 Peter 1 — the seeing is done in borrowed light, and the borrowing is not a deficiency."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What David was looking at.** The verses immediately before this describe a man who has *no fear of God before his eyes*, who plots on his bed. David does not argue with him. He looks up instead, and the psalm changes register in a single line: *Your unfailing love, O LORD, reaches to the heavens.*"}]}, "Psalms 39:1–3": {"title": "\"The more I mused, the more the fire burned\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A man decides to say nothing, and the decision does not hold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The resolution"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I said, **I will watch my ways so my tongue does not sin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I will take heed to my ways*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a deliberate policy, adopted in advance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The guard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will **keep a guard on my mouth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **machsom** — a muzzle. The word occurs once in the Bible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a watchman at a door. A muzzle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David asks for something gentler elsewhere: *set a guard, O LORD, over my mouth; keep watch at the door of my lips*. See the note on Psalms 141:3–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There he asks God to post a sentry. Here he straps something on himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While **the wicked stand before me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *while the wicked is before me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The restraint is for their benefit, or at least because of them. He does not want to give them anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So I stayed **silent, still, holding back even from good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I was mute with silence; I held my peace even from good*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words for the same thing, and then the admission: he suppressed the good along with the rest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Total silence is indiscriminate. Shutting the mouth shuts everything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it cost"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **my sorrow only stirred**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *my sorrow was stirred up*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The silence did not settle anything. It agitated it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My **heart grew hot inside me**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The more **I mused**, the more the fire burned.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hagig** — from the root **hagah**, the meditation word. To mutter, to murmur, to go over something."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the same root as Joshua 1:8 and Psalm 1: *in His law he meditates day and night*. See the note on Joshua 1:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here the muttering is of grievance rather than of Scripture, and the effect is the same: what is turned over grows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The break"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **my tongue could hold no longer**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *then I spoke with my tongue*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The muzzle fails."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"LORD, **make me know my end**, and what is the measure of my days, that I may know how frail I am.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Indeed, You have made my days as handbreadths, and my age is as nothing before You. **Certainly every man at his best state is but vapour**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hevel** — the Ecclesiastes word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Remove Your gaze from me, that I may regain strength, before I go away and am no more.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is one of the bleakest closings in the psalter, and nothing resolves it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jeremiah says about the same fire"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then I said, \"I will not make mention of Him, nor speak anymore in His name.\" **But His word was in my heart like a burning fire shut up in my bones**; I was weary of holding it back, and I could not.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same experiment, the same result."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the muzzle produced.** Not calm. He suppressed the good along with the bad, the sorrow stirred, and the turning-over set it alight — and the psalm's first words come out under pressure."}]}, "Psalms 40:6–8": {"title": "\"My ears You have opened\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "David says God did not want the sacrifices, in the middle of a system built on them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sacrifice and offering You did not desire… **Burnt offering and sin offering You did not require**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four of the five Levitical offerings named and set aside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that they were wrong — they were commanded in the same law David lived under. The psalm is saying they were never the thing wanted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Samuel had said it to Saul: *to obey is better than sacrifice.* Hosea: *I desire mercy and not sacrifice.* Micah, Isaiah and Jeremiah all say versions of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line in between"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My ears You have opened.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **karah** — to dig, to bore through. *Ears You have dug for me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not the word for opening a door. It is the word for making a hole."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the law it points back to is Exodus 21: a servant who could go free, saying *I love my master… I do not want to go free*, brought to the doorpost, and his ear pierced with an awl. See the note on Exodus 21:2–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sacrifice was not wanted. A bored ear was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then I said, **see, I have come**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *behold, I come* — the response of somebody who has heard an order and is presenting himself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An opened ear, and then an arrival."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scroll"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the scroll of the book **it is written of me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever David meant by it, Hebrews takes it as spoken by Christ, and the writer of Hebrews is not being subtle about it:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, when He came into the world, He said: \"Sacrifice and offering You did not desire, but **a body You have prepared for Me**… Behold, I have come — in the volume of the book it is written of Me — to do Your will, O God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews quotes the Greek Old Testament, which renders *ears You have dug* as *a body You have prepared* — the part standing for the whole."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion Hebrews draws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He takes away the first that He may establish the second. By that will **we have been sanctified through the offering of the body of Jesus Christ once for all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The four offerings God did not require, replaced by a body and a will."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The delight"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I **delight** to do Your will, O my God. **Your law is here within my heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not carried, not consulted. Inside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah's new covenant promise is the same: *I will put My law in their minds, and write it on their hearts.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the psalm starts.** *I waited patiently for the LORD… He brought me up out of a horrible pit, out of the miry clay, and set my feet upon a rock.* The man writing about the pierced ear had been in a pit first."}]}, "Psalms 46:1–3": {"title": "\"A help always near\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three things said about God in one line, and then the worst thing the psalmist can imagine."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God is our **refuge** and our **strength**, **a help always near** the moment trouble comes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Refuge** — *machseh*, a shelter you run into from a storm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Strength** — the word for might, and it is His rather than borrowed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A help always near** — the Hebrew is *a help in troubles, very much found*. He is described as easily located."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The moment trouble comes\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not say trouble might come. It assumes it, and the whole claim is about timing: found, and found immediately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he imagines"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even if **the earth gives way**, even if **the mountains topple into the heart of the sea**, though its waters roar and foam, though the mountains shake.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most permanent things anybody in that world could point at — the ground and the mountains — going into the water."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the language of creation coming undone. The psalmist reaches for the end of the world and says *even if*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **we will not be afraid**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Placed before the list, not after it. The fearlessness is stated and then the catastrophes are named underneath it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Selah"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm pauses here — and again twice more. Nobody is certain what the word directs, but it marks a break."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is in the middle of the psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is **a river** whose streams shall make glad the city of God… **God is in the midst of her, she shall not be moved**; God shall help her, **just at the break of dawn**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seas roar and the mountains fall into them in verse 3. In verse 4 there is a river, and it is quiet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jerusalem had no river. It had one spring, Gihon, and a tunnel Hezekiah cut through rock to bring the water inside the walls before a siege."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line everybody knows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Be still, and know that I am God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is stronger than *be quiet* — it means let go, cease, drop your hands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is not addressed to the anxious believer in the usual reading. Look at the verse before: *He makes wars cease to the end of the earth; He breaks the bow and cuts the spear in two.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The command is addressed to the nations. **Stop.**"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD of hosts is with us; the God of Jacob is our refuge.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice, at the end of the two halves. **LORD of hosts** is the military title, and *the God of Jacob* is the God of the man who cheated his brother and wrestled all night."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What was written on Luther's tombstone territory.** He wrote *A Mighty Fortress Is Our God* out of this psalm, in the middle of the plague and the Diet of Speyer. The psalm has a long record of being reached for when the mountains are moving."}]}, "Psalms 46:10–11": {"title": "\"Be still\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted line in the psalm, and it is usually read as being addressed to the wrong people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse before it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He makes wars cease to the end of the earth; **He breaks the bow and cuts the spear in two**; He burns the chariot in the fire.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the immediate context. Weapons being destroyed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Be still, and know that I am God.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **raphah** — to let go, to sink down, to drop, to cease. It is not primarily about quietness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is used of hands going slack, and of abandoning an effort."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read it with verse 9 attached and the addressee changes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just broken the bow and the spear and burned the chariot, and then says **stop**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The command is addressed to the nations who were making the war — *the nations raged, the kingdoms were moved* in verse 6. It is a cease-fire order shouted at combatants."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That does not make it useless to an anxious reader. It means the calm being offered is the calm of somebody who has watched the weapons being broken, not the calm of somebody being told to relax."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I will be lifted high among the nations. I will be lifted high in the earth.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice, and both are His own intention rather than anything anybody does for Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD of hosts is with us. **The God of Jacob is our high tower.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same two lines close the first half of the psalm as well."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**LORD of hosts** — Yahweh Sabaoth, the God of armies, in a psalm about wars ceasing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The God of Jacob** — not the God of Abraham, the friend of God, or Israel the prince. Jacob: the supplanter, the man who cheated his brother and wrestled all night."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm has said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God is our refuge and strength, a very present help in trouble. Therefore we will not fear, **even though the earth be removed**, and though the mountains be carried into the midst of the sea.* See the note on Psalms 46:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the middle: *there is a river whose streams shall make glad the city of God… **God is in the midst of her, she shall not be moved**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roaring seas and toppling mountains in verse 3, and a quiet river in verse 4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Selah"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm pauses three times, and one of them is right here at the end."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Luther made of it.** *A Mighty Fortress Is Our God* is this psalm, written in the 1520s during plague and upheaval. Psalm 46 has a long record of being reached for when things are moving."}]}, "Psalms 5:1–3": {"title": "In the morning", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses of asking to be heard, and the word *morning* appears twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three requests"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Listen to my words.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hear the **sighing I cannot even speak**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Pay attention to the sound of my cry.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs for the same thing, escalating. Words, then something below words, then a cry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The middle one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hagigi** — a rare noun. Musing, murmuring, groaning. Something between a thought and a sound."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks God to attend to the part of the prayer that never made it into language."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the same thing about the Spirit: *the Spirit Himself makes intercession for us with groanings which cannot be uttered*. See the note on Romans 8:26–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My **King** and my **God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both possessives are personal. He is praying to a monarch he calls his own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it is **to You I pray**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *for to You I will pray* — the ground given for being heard is simply the direction of the prayer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the morning You hear my voice, LORD. **In the morning** I lay it all before You.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice in one verse. The Hebrew word **boqer** is repeated for emphasis."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I **lay it all before You**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **a'arok** — to arrange, to set in order."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a technical word. It is used of the priest laying the wood on the altar for the morning sacrifice, and of setting out the showbread on the table."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The morning burnt offering was arranged at daybreak, every day, without exception. *One lamb you shall offer in the morning, and the other lamb you shall offer at twilight.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalmist uses the altar word for his prayer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not stated"}, {"k": "p", "t": "What he lays out. The Hebrew has no object — *in the morning I will arrange*, and stops."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Translators supply *my prayer* or *my case*. The verse itself just says he sets it in order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The watching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **I wait and watch**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **atsappeh** — to look out, to keep watch. The verb for a sentry on a wall."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Habakkuk uses it: *I will stand my watch and set myself on the rampart, and watch to see what He will say to me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer is not finished when the speaking stops."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the rest of the psalm is about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Enemies, and specifically their mouths: *there is no faithfulness in their mouth… their throat is an open tomb; they flatter with their tongue*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes that line in his chain of quotations in Romans 3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **let all those rejoice who put their trust in You**; let them ever shout for joy, because You defend them; let those also who love Your name be joyful in You.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **You, O LORD, will bless the righteous; with favour You will surround him as with a shield**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *shield* here is **tsinnah** — the large body shield, the full-length one, not the small buckler."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To the Chief Musician. With flutes.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb in the middle verse.** *Arrange* — the priest's word for laying out the wood and the bread at daybreak. And then he says he watches, which means the praying is not the end of it."}]}, "Psalms 51:10–12": {"title": "Create in me", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses, six requests, and every verb has God as its subject."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Create in me a clean heart, O God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **bara**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the verb of Genesis 1:1, and in the Hebrew Bible its subject is always God. No human being ever **bara**s anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David does not ask for the heart to be cleaned. He asks for one to be made."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the word implies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something that is not there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has spent five verses on washing and blotting out — the vocabulary of laundry and of erasing a debt. Then he changes register."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **renew a steady spirit** within me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ruach nakhon** — a firm, established, right spirit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nakhon** is used of things that are set up and will hold: a throne established, a house founded on rock."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is asking for stability, which is exactly what the last year of his life had lacked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fear"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not **cast me away from Your presence**, do not **take Your Holy Spirit from me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David had watched it happen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the Spirit of the LORD departed from Saul, and a distressing spirit from the LORD troubled him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had been in the room. He was the boy brought in to play the harp when it happened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is not an abstract anxiety."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is different under the new covenant"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will pray the Father, and He will give you another Helper, **that He may abide with you forever**.* See the note on John 14:15–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *in whom also, having believed, **you were sealed with the Holy Spirit of promise**, who is the guarantee of our inheritance*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The request in this psalm is the one prayer a believer is told not to need to pray."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The restoration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give me back **the joy of Your salvation**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the salvation. The joy of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hashivah li** — return to me."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not ask to be re-saved. He asks for something that has gone missing to come back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **hold me up with a willing spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ruach nedivah** — a willing, generous, noble spirit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nadiv** is the word for a freewill offering, and for a prince."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Third *spirit* in three verses: steady, holy, and willing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he promises next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then I will teach transgressors Your ways, and sinners shall be converted to You.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The teaching is conditional on the restoring. A man in his condition had nothing to say until this had happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sacrifices of God are a broken spirit; a broken and a contrite heart — these, O God, You will not despise.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, oddly, two verses about rebuilding the walls of Jerusalem and offering bulls on the altar — which most readers take as a later addition, or as the man's mind moving from himself to the city."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the new heart is promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will give you a new heart, and put a new spirit within you; I will take the heart of stone out of your flesh and give you a heart of flesh.* See the note on Ezekiel 36:26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David asks for it. Ezekiel is told it will be given."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb he chooses.** *Bara* — the Genesis verb, whose subject is never a human being. Not clean this one. Make one."}]}, "Psalms 51:3–4": {"title": "\"You are right when You speak\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Written after the worst thing David ever did, and the astonishing thing is the direction he faces."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heading on the psalm says it: *when Nathan the prophet came to him, after he had gone in to Bathsheba.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Adultery, and then a man murdered by arrangement to cover it. A year of silence before the prophet arrived."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Never leaves my sight\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For I know my rebellion, **my sin never leaves my sight**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that it is *before me continually*. He is not confessing something he had forgotten."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Against You, You only\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Against You, **You only**, have I sinned, and done what is evil in Your sight.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Uriah is dead. Bathsheba's life is wrecked. A child will die. And David says *against You only*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not dismissing them. He is naming who the offence is finally against — the psalm is addressed to God and he refuses to spread the account across other defendants."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **You are right when You speak, You are clear when You judge**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the move. He does not argue for a mitigating circumstance. He rules in God's favour before God has said anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that God is *justified* in His speaking and *clear* in His judging."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Set against Job"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God's one charge against Job, out of the whirlwind: *would you condemn Me so that you could be in the right?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two positions and only one can be occupied. Job took his own for thirty chapters and God had to come out of a storm about it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David takes the other one at the top of the psalm, before anything is asked of him. He agrees God is right and puts himself in the wrong, and everything he receives in the rest of the psalm comes down that channel. See the note on Job 40:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Purge me with hyssop, and I shall be clean. Wash me, and I shall be whiter than snow.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hyssop is the little bunch of herb used to put blood on the doorframes at Passover, and to sprinkle a cleansed leper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks to be handled the way a leper is handled — by an application of blood he does not administer himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what he does not offer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You do not delight in sacrifice, or I would bring it… **the sacrifices of God are a broken spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A king with a treasury and a tabernacle says the one offering that would work is not something he can fund."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he asks not to lose.** *Do not cast me from Your presence, and do not take Your Holy Spirit from me.* Under the old covenant the Spirit came and went — he had watched it happen to Saul. It is a prayer no believer since Pentecost has had to pray."}]}, "Psalms 51:5–6": {"title": "Truth in the hidden places", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses in the middle of the most honest psalm in the book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heading"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A Psalm of David when Nathan the prophet went to him, after he had gone in to Bathsheba.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The adultery, the arranged death of Uriah, a year of silence, and then a prophet with a story about a poor man's lamb: *you are the man*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is what David wrote afterwards."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he has already asked"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Have mercy upon me, O God, according to Your lovingkindness… blot out my transgressions. Wash me thoroughly from my iniquity.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *against You, You only, have I sinned, and done this evil in Your sight*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is remarkable given who else was harmed, and it is a statement about who defines the offence rather than a denial that anybody else was involved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The confession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*See, **I was shaped in wrongdoing**, in sin my mother carried me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **cholalti** — I was brought forth, formed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not blaming his mother, and he is not excusing himself. He is going back further than the incident."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man caught in one act says the problem is older than the act."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **You long for truth in the hidden places**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **battuchot** — a rare word, usually rendered *inward parts* or *the hidden being*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Emet** — truth, faithfulness, reliability."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What is wanted, in the sentence, is not performance but honesty in the part nobody sees."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is what the psalm is: a man writing down the worst thing he ever did and putting it in the national songbook."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **teach me wisdom deep inside**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **besatum** — in the closed-up place, the sealed part."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words for the same region in one verse: the hidden, and the sealed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he asks for next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Create in me a clean heart, O God, and renew a steadfast spirit within me.* See the note on Psalms 51:10–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **bara** — create. The verb from Genesis 1, whose subject in the Hebrew Bible is always God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not repair. Make."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does not ask for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His reputation, the child's life, or the situation to be undone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The requests in the psalm are all interior: wash, blot out, create, renew, restore."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he offers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **You do not desire sacrifice, or else I would give it**; You do not delight in burnt offering.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sacrifices of God are **a broken spirit; a broken and a contrite heart** — these, O God, You will not despise.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The king who could have brought a herd brings the one thing that cannot be bought."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he promises"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then I will teach transgressors Your ways, and sinners shall be converted to You.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm is that promise being kept. Three thousand years of transgressors have read it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the hidden part turns up in the New Testament"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For he is not a Jew who is one outwardly… but he is a Jew who is one **inwardly**; and circumcision is that of the heart, in the Spirit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Peter: *the hidden person of the heart, with the incorruptible ornament of a gentle and quiet spirit, which is very precious in the sight of God*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is asked for in the sealed part.** Not improvement. Truth — and the psalm itself is what that looks like: the worst thing a king ever did, written down and handed to the choir."}]}, "Psalms 56:8–9": {"title": "Gather my tears in Your bottle", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and one of them contains the strangest image of God's attention in the Psalter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it was written"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heading: *when the Philistines captured him in Gath*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "David, on the run from Saul, in the home town of the giant he had killed — carrying, according to 1 Samuel, Goliath's own sword."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He got out by pretending to be insane, scrabbling at the gates and letting saliva run into his beard, and Achish said *have I need of madmen?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is where this psalm comes from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The counting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have **counted my every wandering**, each **toss and turn in the dark**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nodi** — my wanderings, my homelessness. And the verb is **saphar**, to count, to number, to recount."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old versions have *You tell my wanderings* — tell in the sense of a bank teller: keeping count."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every displacement, tallied."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bottle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Gather **my tears in Your bottle**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **no'd** — a skin bottle, the kind used for water or milk. The same word as the skin Abraham gave Hagar. See the note on Genesis 21:14–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it sits one letter away from **nodi**, wanderings, in the line before. The Hebrew is doing something with the sound: the wanderings counted, the tears in a skin."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whether the custom existed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roman tear bottles — small glass vials found in graves — are often cited. Whether Israelites kept them is not established."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image works either way. A man asks for his crying to be collected in a container, which is what you do with something of value that would otherwise be lost."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Are they not **written in Your book**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **sipratekha** — Your book, from the same root as the counting verb in the previous line."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Counted, bottled, and written down. Three forms of record for the same thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the day I call to You, **my enemies will turn and run**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Future tense, from a man currently in enemy territory."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This much I know: **God is for me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *this I know, for God is for me* — **ki Elohim li**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four words, and it is the psalm's centre."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul writes the same sentence in Greek: *if God is for us, who can be against us?* See the note on Romans 8:31–34."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm has it twice: *in God I have put my trust; **I will not fear. What can flesh do to me?***"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *in God (I will praise His word), in the LORD (I will praise His word), in God I have put my trust; I will not be afraid. What can man do to me?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says he will not be afraid, in a psalm whose heading says he was caught."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Vows made to You are binding upon me, O God; I will render praises to You.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For You have **delivered my soul from death**. Have You not kept my feet from falling, that I may walk before God in the light of the living?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past tense. The psalm that begins *be merciful to me, O God, for man would swallow me up* ends with the rescue reported as done."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the tears go in the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And God will **wipe away every tear from their eyes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The bottle is emptied by a hand."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is asked for.** Not that the crying stop. That it be collected — counted, put in a skin, and written in a book, which are three ways of saying *do not let this go unrecorded*."}]}, "Psalms 68:1–3": {"title": "Let God rise up", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The opening line is a quotation, and it is a marching order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whenever the ark set out, Moses would say, \"**Rise up, O LORD! Let your enemies scatter; let those who hate you flee before you**.\"* See the note on Numbers 10:33–36."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said every time the camp broke and the ark went to the front of the column."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm opens by quoting it, and the whole poem is about a procession."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let God **rise up**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yaqum** — arise, stand up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses' version is an imperative addressed to God. The psalm turns it slightly: *let God arise*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two images"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As **smoke is blown away**, blow them away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As **wax melts before the fire**, let the wicked melt away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are about substances that do not survive contact. Nothing is fought in either picture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **let the righteous be glad**, let them **exult** before God, let them be **jubilant with joy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs piled up, and the Hebrew intensifies each time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm's structure is set in the first three verses: one side dispersing, the other side celebrating, and the celebration gets more words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A processional, and probably one sung as the ark was carried up to Jerusalem — or re-enacted annually."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is one of the hardest psalms in the Psalter. The Hebrew is difficult, the imagery jumps, and scholars have called it a collection of fragments."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is in it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A father of the fatherless, a defender of widows, is God in His holy habitation. **God sets the solitary in families**; He brings out those who are bound into prosperity.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You, O God, sent a plentiful rain, whereby You confirmed Your inheritance, when it was weary.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed be the Lord, who **daily loads us with benefits**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our God is the God of salvation; and to God the Lord belong **escapes from death**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line Paul quotes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have ascended on high, **You have led captivity captive**; You have received gifts among men.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses it in Ephesians, with one change: *when He ascended on high, He led captivity captive, and **gave gifts to men**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Received in the psalm; gave in the letter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Paul draws the conclusion: *He who descended is also the One who ascended far above all the heavens, that He might fill all things. And He Himself gave some to be apostles, some prophets, some evangelists, and some pastors and teachers.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The processional in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They have seen Your procession, O God, the procession of my God, my King, into the sanctuary. The singers went before, the players on instruments followed after; **among them were the maidens playing timbrels**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Singers, musicians, and girls with tambourines — which is what 2 Samuel 6 describes going up into Jerusalem. See the note on 2 Samuel 6:12–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ascribe strength to God; His excellence is over Israel, and **His strength is in the clouds**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed be God!*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the first verse assumes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That He is seated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let God arise* only makes sense said to somebody sitting down, and Moses said it at the start of every march."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the line comes from.** A marching order, said every time the ark moved. The psalm opens with it — which means the poem is written from the moment before anything has started."}]}, "Psalms 69:1–4": {"title": "What I never stole", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm quoted at the cross more than any other except the twenty-second."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Save me, O God. **The waters have come up to my neck**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the waters have come in unto my soul* — **nephesh**, which can mean the throat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Water at the throat. Not drowning yet, and past the point of standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mud"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I sink in **deep mud where there is no place to stand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the mire of the deep*, and *there is no standing*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah was lowered into a cistern with no water in it, *and Jeremiah sank in the mire* — the same word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The flood"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have come into **deep waters**, and the **flood sweeps over me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three water images in three lines: at the neck, under the feet, over the head."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exhaustion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am **worn out with my crying**, my **throat is dry**, my eyes **grow dim from looking, looking for my God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *my throat is parched* — he has cried until his voice has gone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the eyes fail *while I wait for my God*. The doubling in the translation catches the Hebrew participle: the looking is continuous and it is what is wearing him out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*More than **the hairs on my head** are those who **hate me for no reason**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **sinah chinnam** — hating without cause. Free hatred, gratuitous."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus quotes this line in the upper room and says it is about Him: *but this happened that the word might be fulfilled which is written in their law, \"They hated Me without a cause.\"* See the note on John 15:22–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They make me **pay back what I never stole**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *that which I took not away, then I restored it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is the restitution word. This is the legal language of Exodus 22 and Leviticus 6 — a man paying the multiple for goods he never took. See the note on Exodus 22:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalmist is describing a courtroom outcome, not a mugging."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What else the psalm supplies"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Zeal for Your house has eaten Me up* — quoted by the disciples when He cleared the temple."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They also gave me **gall** for my food, and for my thirst they gave me **vinegar** to drink* — all four Gospels at the crucifixion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let their dwelling place be desolate; let no one live in their tents* — Peter, in Acts 1, applying it to Judas."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four separate quotations from one psalm, in four different contexts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose psalm it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David's, by the heading. And it contains a line no reader has ever comfortably applied to Christ: *O God, You know my foolishness, and my sins are not hidden from You.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The New Testament quotes the psalm freely and never quotes that verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But as for me, my prayer is to You, O LORD, **in the acceptable time**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at the end, before anything has changed: *I will praise the name of God with a song, and will magnify Him with thanksgiving.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The praise arrives while he is still in the water."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last two verses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For God will save Zion and build the cities of Judah… the descendants of His servants shall inherit it, and **those who love His name shall dwell in it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm that begins with a man up to his neck ends with people living in a city."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The legal word in verse 4.** *Restored what I did not take* — the Hebrew is the restitution verb from the theft statutes. He is paying a multiple on goods that were never in his hands."}]}, "Psalms 75:6–8": {"title": "Promotion comes from one direction", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm rules out three directions and leaves one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three that are excluded"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For no one is lifted up **from the east or the west, or from the wilderness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "East, west, and the desert to the south. The one direction the psalm does not name is up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever is left after those three is the answer, and the psalmist leaves the reader to say it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to lift up, to raise, to exalt — and it is the word used for promotion, for a head being raised, for a man being set high."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of the compass points can do it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is left"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is **God who judges**. He brings one down and lifts another up.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves are His. The bringing down and the lifting up are the same hand doing two things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *judges* is not primarily about a courtroom. It is the word for governing, deciding, ruling a matter — the verb the book of Judges is named after."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two examples in one sermon's reach"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Uzziah** — helped until he was strong, and struck at the altar the day his heart got proud. Fifty-two years, and the last of them in a separate house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Haman** — promoted above every official for no stated reason, and hanged on his own gallows within the day. And **Mordecai**, sitting at a gate, given the ring that had been on Haman's hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither man's rise came from the east or the west. Neither man's fall did either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cup"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is a **cup in the hand of the LORD**, foaming with wine, well mixed, and He pours it out.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is of a cup passed round — the prophets use it repeatedly, and it is the cup Jesus names in Gethsemane."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Down to the very dregs the wicked of the earth will drink it dry.* The dregs are the sediment at the bottom, the last and worst of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is holding it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole passage is about whose hand things are in. The cup is in His hand; the lifting is His; the bringing down is His."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in the psalm arranges their own position."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the psalm opens.** *We give thanks to You, O God, we give thanks, for Your name is near.* The name is *near* — which is what the book of Esther demonstrates without once writing it down."}]}, "Psalms 76:10–12": {"title": "Even human rage will end up praising You", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One line, and it settles what happens to everything thrown at God's people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even **human rage** will end up **praising You**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not tolerated. Not survived. It ends up as praise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *rage* is the hot word — burning anger, fury. It is what men do at their worst, and the psalm says the finish of it is God's glory."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **what rage is left, You put on like a belt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *You gird Yourself with the remainder of wrath*. Whatever anger does not end up as praise is strapped on as a belt — held, restrained, and worn by Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A belt is what you fasten so nothing swings loose. What is left over is not running free; it is buckled to Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Haman"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole of Esther is this verse in narrative."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["his **rage** at one man not bowing produced the decree", "the decree produced the fast and Esther's approach to the throne", "his **rage** built a seventy-five-foot gallows, and it is what he was hanged on", "his **boast** to his wife and friends brought out the advisers' verdict against him", "his **pleading** for his life across a couch is what the king read as assault"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every furious thing Haman did was load-bearing in his own destruction, and the Jews came out of it with the ring, the house and a feast that is still kept."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not one of those steps required a miracle, and God is not named in any of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm says about kings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **cuts down the pride of rulers**; He is a terror to the kings of the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *cuts down* is to cut off, as of a vine being pruned — the word used of a grape harvest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ahasuerus, who ruled from India to Cush, spent the whole book being moved by his own temper and never knew who was moving him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Make your vows to the LORD your God and **keep them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set between the rage that turns to praise and the kings who are cut down, the psalm's one command to the reader is small and domestic: say what you will do, and do it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the psalm begins.** *In Judah God is known; His name is great in Israel.* Known, and named. The book of Esther is the same God at work in a place where He was neither."}]}, "Psalms 78:21–25": {"title": "\"Mortals ate the bread of angels\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm reports God's anger and what He did about it, and the two are in the wrong order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The anger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the LORD heard, and **He was furious**… **because they did not believe in God**, they did not trust Him to save them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The charge is unbelief, and it is named twice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm is a long retelling of Israel's history, and it does not soften this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word that follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything after that conjunction is provision."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **commanded the skies above** and **opened the doors of heaven**. He **rained down manna** for them to eat.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs, all of them about the sky being opened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *doors* is the word for the doors of a house. The heavens are described as a building with entrances."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two names for it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gave them **the grain of heaven**. Mortals ate **the bread of angels**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew of the second is **lechem abbirim** — bread of the mighty ones. Young's Literal keeps it that way: *food of the mighty.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some read it as angels' bread, some as the bread of the strong. Either way the point is the same: it is not food that belongs to the eaters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Men ate what came from somewhere above them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"To the full\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He sent them **food to the full**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *to satisfaction*. Not rations, not a subsistence measure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sequence in the passage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Unbelief. Anger. And then the doors of heaven opening and bread falling on the camp."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm puts the provision immediately after the complaint about the unbelief, and does not explain the order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the manna was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Something fine and flaky, delicate as frost.* They asked *what is it?* — **man hu** — and the question became the name. See the note on Exodus 16:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It fell six mornings a week for forty years and stopped the day they ate the produce of the land."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus does with this psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your fathers ate the manna in the wilderness, and are dead… **I am the living bread which came down from heaven**. If anyone eats of this bread, he will live forever.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He grants the comparison and then says what is wrong with it: they ate, and they died."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowd had quoted this very psalm at Him — *He gave them bread from heaven to eat.* See the note on John 6:47–51."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the psalm says a few verses later.** *In spite of all this they still sinned, and did not believe in His wondrous works.* The psalm's whole method is to lay unbelief and provision side by side, over and over, for seventy-two verses."}]}, "Psalms 78:40–43": {"title": "\"They did not remember His hand\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A long psalm retelling Israel's history, and this is its diagnosis of what went wrong in the desert."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They defied Him… they grieved Him… they tested God… they wounded the Holy One of Israel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third and fourth are the striking ones. **Tested** — they put God through a trial, repeatedly. And the last, often rendered *limited*, has the sense of marking out a boundary or causing pain; the older translation *they limited the Holy One of Israel* is where the familiar phrase comes from."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way the psalm attributes something to God that is rarely said aloud: He was grieved. Not inconvenienced. Pained."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cause given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They did not remember His hand, the day He set them free.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Forgetting is the root, and it is a strange thing to forget — a sea opening, a nation walking out of Egypt in a night. The psalm's charge is not that they disbelieved a promise they had never seen kept. It is that they stopped recollecting one they had."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the whole psalm is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The opening verses say it: these things were handed down *so that the next generation would know them*, and set their hope in God rather than repeating the pattern."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a psalm written to be taught to children, and its method is to tell the story honestly, failures included."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"limiting\" God.** The phrase has been used to mean that unbelief restricts what God is able to do. The Hebrew is disputed and the psalm's own explanation is simpler and sadder: they forgot what He had already done. Whatever the verb means precisely, the sentence beside it is the one the psalmist chose to explain it."}]}, "Psalms 78:67–69": {"title": "He chose Judah", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of a long history psalm, and it finishes on a choice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventy-two verses of Israel's record, told as a warning to the next generation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will open my mouth in a parable; I will utter dark sayings of old… **we will not hide them from their children**, telling to the generation to come the praises of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew quotes the opening line about the parables."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it has been rehearsing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Red Sea, the water from the rock, the manna, the quail, the plagues in Egypt, and the repeated turning back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They tested God in their heart by asking for the food of their fancy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How often they provoked Him in the wilderness… **yes, again and again they tempted God**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened at Shiloh"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm records it a few verses earlier: *He forsook the tabernacle of Shiloh, the tent He had placed among men, and **delivered His strength into captivity, and His glory into the enemy's hand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the day the ark was captured at Aphek — the day Eli fell backwards off his seat and his daughter-in-law named her son **Ichabod**, *the glory has departed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Shiloh was in Ephraim's territory, and it had held the tabernacle for centuries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rejection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **rejected the tent of Joseph**, He did not choose the tribe of **Ephraim**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ephraim was Joseph's younger son, given the greater blessing by Jacob with crossed hands. See the note on Deuteronomy 33:13–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The northern tribes were called Ephraim collectively, and Shiloh was theirs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The choice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **He chose the tribe of Judah, Mount Zion, which He loves**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **bachar** — chose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Judah, the fourth son. Not the firstborn, not the favourite, and the man whose own chapter in Genesis is the Tamar episode."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not given as a reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Merit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm has just spent sixty-five verses on national failure and does not suggest Judah was better."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 132 gives the same choice with the same absence of explanation, and the only reason offered is desire: *the LORD has chosen Zion. He has **desired it** for His home*. See the note on Psalms 132:13–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The building"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **built His sanctuary like the heights**, like the earth **He founded forever**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *like high palaces*, or possibly *like the heavens*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two comparisons: the height of the sky and the permanence of the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last verses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He also chose **David His servant**, and took him from the sheepfolds; from following the ewes that had young He brought him, **to shepherd Jacob His people**, and Israel His inheritance.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So he shepherded them **according to the integrity of his heart**, and guided them by the skilfulness of his hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seventy-two verses of history ending on a boy with sheep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Shiloh goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Go now to My place which was in Shiloh, where I set My name at the first, and **see what I did to it** because of the wickedness of My people Israel.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah, standing in the temple gate, telling Jerusalem not to say *the temple of the LORD, the temple of the LORD* as if the building were a guarantee."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Shiloh had been the place too."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not offered as a reason.** Anything about Judah. The psalm spends sixty-five verses on failure, then names the choice and moves straight to a shepherd boy."}]}, "Psalms 8:5–6": {"title": "\"A little lower than the angels\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses in the middle of a psalm about the night sky."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question they answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When I consider Your heavens, the work of Your fingers, the moon and the stars, which You have ordained — **what is man that You are mindful of him, and the son of man that You visit him?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David is looking up and asking why anybody down here registers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Yet You made him **just a little lower than the angels**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **me'at me'elohim** — a little lower than **Elohim**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is genuinely difficult. The word normally means God. The Greek Old Testament renders it *angels*, and Hebrews follows that reading."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Older English versions have *angels*; several modern ones have *God* or *the heavenly beings*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A little** — the Hebrew can also mean *for a little while*, which is how Hebrews takes it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crowning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **crowned him with glory and honor**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kavod** and **hadar** — weight, and majesty."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both words are used elsewhere of God and of kings. They are applied here to humanity as such."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The commission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You gave him **charge over the works of Your hands**. You **laid everything beneath his feet**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is Genesis 1 restated: *let them have dominion over the fish of the sea, over the birds of the air, and over the cattle, over all the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm lists them in the next verse — sheep and oxen, beasts of the field, birds, fish, and *whatever passes through the paths of the seas*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the observable world looks like this. Man does not visibly have everything under his feet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer quotes all of it and then says exactly that:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For in that He put all in subjection under him, He left nothing that is not put under him. **But now we do not yet see all things put under him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He concedes the gap in the middle of the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he takes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **we see Jesus**, who was made a little lower than the angels, for the suffering of death crowned with glory and honour.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer applies the psalm to Christ — and the phrase *a little lower* becomes *for a little while lower*, which the Greek allows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The commission given to humanity in Genesis, unfulfilled in observation, is read as fulfilled in one man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul uses it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For He has put all things under His feet.* — 1 Corinthians 15, about the reign that continues until the last enemy. See the note on 1 Corinthians 15:23–28."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Ephesians: *and He put all things under His feet, and gave Him to be head over all things to the church.* See the note on Ephesians 1:20–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three New Testament writers use this psalm, and all three read it messianically."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is bracketed by"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same line, opening and closing:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*O LORD, our Lord, **how excellent is Your name in all the earth**!*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who quotes verse 2"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Out of the mouth of babes and nursing infants You have ordained strength.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus quotes it in the temple, to the chief priests objecting to children shouting *Hosanna to the Son of David*. See the note on Matthew 21:12–17."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the psalm claims and what Hebrews concedes.** Everything under his feet — and the writer of Hebrews quotes it, admits nobody can see it, and points at one man."}]}, "Psalms 84:5–7": {"title": "The Valley of Weeping", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A pilgrim song, and the hardest stretch of the road is named."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who wrote it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To the Chief Musician. On an instrument of Gath. A Psalm of the sons of Korah.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Korah led the rebellion in Numbers 16 and the ground opened. *Nevertheless the children of Korah did not die.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His descendants became temple singers, and eleven psalms carry their name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has come before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How lovely is Your tabernacle, O LORD of hosts!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even the sparrow has found a home, and the swallow a nest for herself, where she may lay her young — even Your altars, O LORD of hosts.* See the note on Psalms 84:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Birds nesting in the temple rafters, which the psalmist envies."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed is the one **whose strength is in You**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ashrei adam oz-lo vach** — happy is the man whose strength is in You."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The heart"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In whose heart are **the highways to Zion**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mesillot bilvavam** — highways in their heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Mesillah** — a raised road, a built-up causeway."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The road is inside them. They are not on it yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three annual pilgrim feasts, when Israel travelled to Jerusalem."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Many walked for days across hard country to get there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The valley"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As they pass through **the Valley of Weeping**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **emeq habbacha**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Bacha** — either weeping, or the balsam tree, which grows in dry ground and weeps sap when cut."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew allows both, and the poem probably wants both: a dry valley named for a tree that weeps."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they do to it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **make it a place of springs**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ma'yan yeshituhu** — they make it a spring."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pilgrims are the subject. The valley does not change on its own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Travellers dug cisterns and cleared wells as they went, for themselves and for those behind them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The dry stretch is left wetter than they found it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **early rain covers it with pools**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **moreh** — the early rain, the October rains that break the summer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Berakhot** — pools, and the word is one letter from **berakhot**, blessings. The Hebrew ear catches both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They go **from strength to strength**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yelchu mechayil el-chayil**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is not how walking works. Distance takes strength out of a traveller."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm says the opposite, and the valley is between the two strengths."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arrival"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Each one **appears before God in Zion**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yera'eh el-Elohim betziyyon** — is seen before God in Zion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of them arrives. The psalm does not lose anybody in the valley."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For a day in Your courts is better than a thousand. **I would rather be a doorkeeper in the house of my God** than dwell in the tents of wickedness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the LORD God is a sun and shield; **the LORD will give grace and glory; no good thing will He withhold from those who walk uprightly**.* See the note on Psalms 84:10–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the same direction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore we do not lose heart. Even though our outward man is perishing, **yet the inward man is being renewed day by day**.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 4:16–18."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line.** *From strength to strength* — which is not how walking works. And the valley the pilgrims leave wetter than they found it is between the two."}]}, "Psalms 85:10–13": {"title": "Righteousness and peace have kissed", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four attributes of God, personified, meeting each other in a field."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The meeting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Steadfast love and faithfulness **have met**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *steadfast love* is **chesed** — covenant loyalty, the word that runs through the whole Psalter. And **emeth**, truth, firmness, reliability."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is *have met together* — **nifgashu**, encountered one another."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The kiss"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Righteousness and peace **have kissed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tsedeq** and **shalom**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "These two are the pair with a problem. Righteousness requires the account to be settled; peace requires it to be dropped. The psalm has them kissing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in the Bible about how that becomes possible is downstream of this line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The direction of each"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Faithfulness **springs up from the ground**, and righteousness **leans down from the sky**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *truth shall spring out of the earth, and righteousness shall look down from heaven*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One grows up; the other bends down. They arrive from opposite ends."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the two directions meet elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul, quoting Deuteronomy: *do not say in your heart, \"Who will ascend into heaven?\" (that is, to bring Christ down) or \"Who will descend into the abyss?\"… **the word is near you, in your mouth and in your heart***. See the note on Romans 10:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Righteousness that came down is not to be fetched. It has already made the journey."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The giving"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The LORD will **give what is good**, and our land will **yield its harvest**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the LORD shall give what is good, and our land shall yield its increase*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His giving, then the land's yielding. The order is not reversible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last image"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Righteousness **walks ahead of Him**, **clearing a path for His feet**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *righteousness will go before Him and shall make His footsteps a way*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Righteousness is the outrider, not the escort. It goes first and the road is what it leaves behind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm starts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*LORD, You have been favourable to Your land; You have brought back the captivity of Jacob. You have **forgiven the iniquity of Your people**; You have **covered all their sin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Past tense, all of it. The psalm opens with what has already been done and then asks *revive us again*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will hear what God the LORD will speak, for **He will speak peace to His people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The listening comes just before the four attributes arrive. The psalmist stops asking and waits for a sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the kiss happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Being justified freely by His grace through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus… **that He might be just, and the justifier** of the one who has faith in Jesus.* See the note on Romans 3:23–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's word for it is *demonstrate His righteousness*. The problem the psalm poses — how both can be satisfied at once — is the thing Paul says the cross was for."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *having been justified by faith, **we have peace with God***."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line the whole Bible has to solve.** Righteousness and peace, kissing. One of them insists the debt be paid and the other insists it be over, and the psalm puts them face to face as though it were nothing."}]}, "Psalms 90:11–12": {"title": "\"Teach us to number our days\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The turn in Moses' psalm. Everything before it is description; this is where it becomes a request."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Teach us to count our days.* Not *let us live longer*, and not *explain why*. Teach us to reckon them — the verb is arithmetical, the ordinary word for counting a number of things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The premise is that a person does not naturally do this. Days are spent as though the supply were unlimited, and the psalm asks for the ability to hold the real figure in mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that we may get a heart of wisdom.* The counting is not the goal. It is the instrument."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is literally *bring in* a heart of wisdom — like a harvest gathered into a barn. Numbering the days is how the crop gets in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It follows immediately on the verse about God's anger and the fear owed to Him, and it precedes a series of requests that get steadily warmer: *satisfy us in the morning with Your unfailing love… make us glad for as many days as You have afflicted us… establish the work of our hands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm moves from lament to petition, and this verse is the hinge. A man watching a generation die in the desert asks first to be taught how to count."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Read with the verses above it.** The famous *seventy years, or eighty if we are strong* is four lines earlier, and it is description, not decree — see the note on Psalms 90:7–10. This request is what Moses does with that observation: not resignation, but a prayer to be taught how to spend what there is."}]}, "Psalms 90:13–17": {"title": "Steady the work of our hands", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The oldest psalm in the book, and it ends by asking for something very small."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who wrote it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A prayer of Moses the man of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only psalm attributed to him, and the oldest in the collection by several centuries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has come before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lord, You have been our dwelling place in all generations.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For a thousand years in Your sight are like yesterday when it is past.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The days of our lives are seventy years; and if by reason of strength they are eighty years, yet their boast is only labour and sorrow.* See the note on Psalms 90:7–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So teach us to number our days, that we may gain a heart of wisdom.* See the note on Psalms 90:11–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Turn back to us, LORD. **How long will You wait?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shuvah YHWH ad-matai** — return, LORD; until when?"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ad-matai** — the psalms' standing question. *How long, O LORD? Will You forget me forever?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Have pity on Your servants.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hinnachem** — be moved to compassion, relent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The morning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Fill us **in the morning** with Your love that never fails.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **sabbe'enu babboqer chasdecha** — satisfy us in the morning with Your **chesed**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sava** — the same root as *full of years*, said over Abraham and David. See the note on Genesis 25:7–10."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That we may **sing and be glad all our days**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Morning, and then the whole day. The psalm asks for the filling at the start of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exchange"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give us **joy for the days You afflicted us**, gladness **for the years we saw trouble**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kimot innitanu** — according to the days You afflicted us."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not more. Matched."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who wrote that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who spent forty years leading a generation that was told it would die in the wilderness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The affliction he is asking to be matched is one he watched, day by day, for four decades."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The children"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let **Your work be seen by Your servants**, Your **splendor by their children**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is asking for two generations to see it — his own, and the next."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He himself sees the land from Nebo and does not enter it. See the note on Deuteronomy 34:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let **the kindness of the LORD our God rest on us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **no'am** — beauty, pleasantness, favour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repetition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Steady the work of our hands for us, **yes, steady the work of our hands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **uma'aseh yadeinu konenah aleinu uma'aseh yadeinu konenehu**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice. The last clause of the psalm is the same clause as the one before it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is asking for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kun** — to establish, to make firm, to set up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man who has just said human life is seventy years, grass in the morning and cut down by evening, and that our days *pass away in Your wrath*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His last request is that something made by hand should hold."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the tent was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tabernacle. Made by hand, out of donated jewellery, by named craftsmen, to be carried through a desert and put up and taken down at every stage. See the note on Exodus 36:4–7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the psalm sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the head of Book Four of the Psalter, immediately after Psalm 89 ends *how long, LORD? Will You hide Yourself forever?* and asks what became of the covenant with David."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Book Four opens with Moses, and its answer is *the LORD reigns*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last line, said twice.** *Steady the work of our hands.* Asked by a man who has just described a lifetime as grass, and who would not enter the land himself."}]}, "Psalms 90:7–10": {"title": "\"Seventy or eighty years\" — spoken from inside the wrath", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The best-known number in the Bible about lifespan, and it sits in the middle of a paragraph about being under God's anger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Read the verses it lives in"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Our short lives feel Your fire; **in Your anger** we grow weary and afraid*", "*You set our failings before You, our secret faults lit up in the light of Your face*", "*Our days pass under Your gaze and end like a sigh*", "**then** — *our years may reach seventy, and if we are strong, eighty, yet even the best of them are toil and sorrow*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The number is the last line of a lament about living under displeasure. It is description, not decree — what life measured out to that generation, in those conditions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking, and about whom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The heading calls this *a prayer of Moses*, and the wilderness is all over it. He is watching the generation that refused at Kadesh die off across forty years, exactly as had been said they would."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the seventy and eighty. Not a limit God set on human beings in general — the span of a people under judgement, written down by the man watching it happen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the same Bible records elsewhere"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Moses himself**, at a hundred and twenty, *his eyes were not weak and his strength had not left him*", "**Caleb**, at eighty-five, asking for a hill country full of giants and saying he was as strong as the day Moses sent him out", "**Hezekiah**, given fifteen years after being told to put his house in order"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who wrote the seventy-year line outlived it by fifty years. That is worth holding in mind before handing anyone the number as though it were an appointment."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Moses does next.** He does not settle for the description. The psalm turns into a request — *teach us to count our days… satisfy us in the morning with Your unfailing love… make us glad for as many days as You have afflicted us.* He is asking for something better than what he has just described. See the note on Psalms 90:11–12."}]}, "Psalms 91:1–2": {"title": "The secret shelter", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses at the head of the psalm, and they name four things about God in the space of a breath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Makes a home\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whoever **makes a home** in the secret shelter of the Most High.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is to sit, to remain, to dwell — the word for taking up residence, not visiting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything the rest of the psalm promises runs off this verb. It is not a place you go to in an emergency; it is an address."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The secret place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sether** — a covering, a hiding place, somewhere concealed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is secret because it is hidden, not because it is hard to find. Nobody outside can see you there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shadow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Will **rest in the shadow** of the Almighty.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A shadow requires being close. You cannot be in the shade of something at a distance from it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the Hebrew for *rest* here is to lodge overnight — to spend the night. The first line is about living there; the second is about sleeping there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four names, in order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm stacks them:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Most High** — *Elyon*, the highest, the one above all", "**Almighty** — *Shaddai*, the all-sufficient", "**the LORD** — *YHWH*, the covenant name from the burning bush", "**my God** — *Elohim*, and the possessive is the point"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "It moves from the highest and most distant title down to *my*. The psalm ends the sentence closer than it started it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will say of the LORD, \"**You are** my refuge and my fortress.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first verse is a statement about anybody. The second is spoken, in the first person, and addressed to Him directly — *You are*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm changes from third person to second person inside two lines. The saying of it is part of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Refuge and fortress"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words that are not the same. **Machseh** is a shelter you run into from a storm. **Metsudah** is a stronghold — a fortified place, a mountain hold, the word used of David's strongholds in the wilderness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somewhere to run, and somewhere that holds."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who quotes this psalm.** The devil, in the wilderness, and he quotes verses 11 and 12 accurately — *He shall give His angels charge over you.* He leaves out the line *to keep you in all your ways*. The one place in Scripture where Psalm 91 is cited by name, it is being misused, and the answer is a different verse."}]}, "Psalms 91:11–13": {"title": "The verses the devil quoted", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The best-known lines in the psalm, and the most famously misused."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will **give His angels orders about you**, to guard you **in all your ways**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is that He will command them concerning you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angels are described as under instruction rather than acting on their own initiative, and the assignment is specific to a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To guard you **in all your ways**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the clause the devil left out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the wilderness he quoted verses 11 and 12 accurately — *He shall give His angels charge over you… in their hands they shall bear you up, lest you dash your foot against a stone* — and stopped before *in all your ways*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jumping off the temple is not one of a man's ways. It is a stunt, and the psalm's protection is over a road being walked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus answered with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a correction of the psalm. *It is written again: you shall not tempt the LORD your God* — Deuteronomy 6, about Massah, where Israel demanded proof that God was among them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He answers a real quotation with another real quotation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They will **lift you in their hands**, so you will not strike your foot against a stone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A stubbed toe. The smallest injury the psalm could have named."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the angels are doing, the passage puts them at ankle height."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four animals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You will walk over **the lion and the cobra**; **the young lion and the serpent** you will tread beneath your feet.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two pairs, and each pair has one that attacks openly and one that attacks hidden."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lion is visible and loud; the cobra is underfoot and unseen. The same division as verse 3 — the snare you cannot see and the plague you cannot hear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the treading turns up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I have given you authority to **walk right over snakes and scorpions**, and over all the power of the enemy.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Luke 10, to the seventy — and the sentence after it is *do not let your joy rest on that. Let your joy rest on this: your names are written down in heaven.* See the note on Luke 10:17–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Genesis 3:15: *He shall bruise your head, and you shall bruise His heel.* The serpent under a heel is where the Bible's first promise ends up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the verbs are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Walk, and tread. Neither is a fight."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the psalm is doing here.** Three voices run through it — a teacher describing the shelter, a believer confessing *You are my refuge*, and then God Himself in the last three verses. These lines are the teacher's, and the devil borrowed them."}]}, "Psalms 91:14–16": {"title": "The three verses where God speaks for Himself", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm changes speaker at the end, and most readings walk straight past it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is talking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verses 1 to 13 are *about* God — He will cover you, you will not fear. Someone is describing Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "From verse 14 the voice switches to the first person and stays there. Seven promises in three verses, every one of them God's own speech:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**I** will set you free", "**I** will lift you high", "**I** will answer", "**I** will be with you in trouble", "**I** will rescue you", "**I** will honour you", "**I** will fill your hands with long life"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not end with a description of God. It ends with God taking over the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason He gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because you have held onto Me in love.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew verb means to cling, to be joined to — the word used of a man joined to his wife, and of Israel told to hold fast to the LORD. It is not achievement and it is not blamelessness. It is attachment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the seven promises are hung on. Someone who has taken hold and will not let go."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"With long life I will fill your hands\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *satisfy* — filled up, having enough. Not merely a long span of years but a fullness of them, and then the last line: *I will let you see how deep My rescue runs.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm ends on sight. Not only rescued, but shown the rescue — allowed to see how far down it goes."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The trouble is named, not denied.** *I will be with you in trouble* is one of the seven, and it is worth holding on to. This is not a psalm promising a life nothing ever happens in. It promises a Presence in the middle of what happens, and a rescue out the other side, spoken by God about Himself."}]}, "Psalms 91:3–4": {"title": "\"Under His wings\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two dangers named, and both of them are the kind you do not see coming."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The snare"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will lift you free of **the hidden snare**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the snare of the fowler* — a bird-catcher's trap, laid on the ground and covered, with bait on top."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A bird walks into it because nothing about it looks like a trap. That is the whole design."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sickness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Free of **the sickness that stalks in silence**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **deber** — pestilence, plague. The older rendering is *the noisome pestilence*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both dangers in the verse share one feature: they are concealed. A snare you cannot see and a sickness you cannot hear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The feathers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will **cover you with His feathers**, and **under His wings** you will find your hiding place.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is a bird over a nest. Not a fortress or a shield — the softest covering available."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy uses it of the exodus: *as an eagle stirs up its nest, hovers over its young, spreading out its wings, taking them up, carrying them on its wings.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Boaz says it to Ruth: *the LORD repay your work, and a full reward be given you by the LORD God of Israel, **under whose wings you have come for refuge**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Jesus, over Jerusalem: *how often I wanted to gather your children together, **as a hen gathers her chicks under her wings**, but you were not willing.* See the note on Matthew 23:37–39."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hen is not a fighter. Her one method is to open her wings and stay put over what is underneath her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His faithfulness is **a shield around you**, a wall you can lean your whole life against.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *shield* here is **tsinnah** — the large body shield, the one that covered a soldier entirely, as against the small round buckler."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the image changes without warning. Feathers in one line and armour in the next."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the shield is made of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His **faithfulness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not His power, and not your faith. The Hebrew is **emeth** — truth, reliability, steadiness. The root behind *amen*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the verses sit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately after the opening two, which name the condition: *whoever makes a home in the secret shelter of the Most High.* See the note on Psalms 91:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then these — the snare, the plague, the feathers, the shield — and then *a thousand may fall at your side.* See the note on Psalms 91:5–8."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What God says at the end of the psalm.** The last three verses switch to first person: *because he has set his love upon Me, therefore I will deliver him… **I will be with him in trouble**.* In it, not instead of it. See the note on Psalms 91:14–16."}]}, "Psalms 91:5–8": {"title": "Around the clock, seen and unseen", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four dangers named in two lines, and the way they are arranged tells you the psalm means all of them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The terror of the night, the arrow that flies in the day, the plague that moves in the dark, the ruin that strikes at noon.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two at night, two by day. Two hidden, two out in the open. It is a Hebrew way of saying *everything* by naming the poles — the whole clock, and both kinds of threat, the sort you can see coming and the sort you cannot."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is left outside the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"It will not come near you\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A thousand may fall at your side, ten thousand at your right hand, but it will not come near you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not pretend the danger is imaginary. People are falling — that is in the verse. What it says is that the thing sweeping the ground around you does not arrive where you are standing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *come near* is the ordinary word for approach. It is a promise about proximity, spoken over someone in the middle of a field where others are going down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is spoken over"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 1 sets the terms for everything that follows: *the one who lives in the shelter of the Most High, who stays in the shadow of the Almighty.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Dwelling is the posture. Not visiting in an emergency — living there. The promises in this psalm are addressed to somebody already at home in that place, and the rest of the psalm reads as a description of what that address is like."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How it ends.** God takes over the sentence at verse 14 and speaks in the first person — *I will set you free… I will answer… I will be with you in trouble… with long life I will fill your hands.* The One making the promises stops being described and starts speaking. See the note on Psalms 91:14–16."}]}, "Psalms 91:9–10": {"title": "\"No plague will come near your tent\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and the first one is the condition the second one rests on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because you have made the LORD **your refuge**, the Most High **your dwelling place**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words, and they are not the same."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Refuge** — *machseh*, a shelter you run into from a storm. Somewhere you go when something is happening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dwelling place** — *maon*, a habitation, a lair, a home. Somewhere you live."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm's opening line says the same: *whoever **makes a home** in the secret shelter of the Most High.* See the note on Psalms 91:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No evil will overtake you, **no plague will come near your tent**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *plague* is **nega** — a stroke, a blow, a mark. It is the word Leviticus uses of the mark of leprosy, and the word for the strokes God said would fall on Egypt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *your tent* — the older rendering is *your dwelling*. The protection is described around a residence rather than a person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse before them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A thousand may fall at your side, and ten thousand at your right hand; **but it shall not come near you**.* See the note on Psalms 91:5–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm does not describe an untroubled world. It describes falling on every side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same phrase turns up in Exodus"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The blood shall be a sign for you on the houses where you are. And when I see the blood, I will pass over you, and **the plague shall not be on you to destroy you** when I strike the land of Egypt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same noun, over doorframes, on a night when it was on everybody else. See the note on Exodus 12:1–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who quoted this psalm"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The devil, in the wilderness, and he quoted the two verses immediately following:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He shall give His angels charge over you… in their hands they shall bear you up.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He got the words right and left out the clause *to keep you in all your ways.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Jesus did not correct the psalm. He answered from Deuteronomy: *you shall not tempt the LORD your God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the psalm ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last three verses are the only ones spoken by God rather than about Him:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because he has set his love upon Me, therefore I will deliver him… **He shall call upon Me, and I will answer him; I will be with him in trouble**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**In** trouble. Not instead of it. See the note on Psalms 91:14–16."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the psalm is doing structurally.** It has three voices — a teacher describing the refuge, a believer saying *I will say of the LORD, \"You are my refuge\"*, and God Himself at the end. The promises are stated, confessed, and then confirmed by the One making them."}]}, "Psalms 92:10–15": {"title": "\"Even in old age they still bear fruit\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two trees, chosen for two different qualities, and a promise about the far end of a life."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The palm and the cedar"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The righteous will flourish like the palm tree, they will grow tall like a cedar of Lebanon.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the palm** — grows in the desert, fruits for decades, and its trunk bends in a storm without snapping. It also bears its best dates late in life.", "**the cedar** — the great timber tree of the ancient world, slow, enormous, and used to build the temple itself."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "One thrives where nothing should; the other lasts and towers. Between them they cover fruitfulness and endurance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they are planted"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Planted in the house of the LORD, they will bloom in the courts of our God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *transplanted* — moved from somewhere else and put here deliberately. Neither tree grew where it happened to land."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the location is the point of the psalm: not planted near the house, or visiting it. In it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise about age"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Even in old age they still bear fruit; they stay fresh and green.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two words are *fat* and *fresh* — full of sap, still green. It is a horticultural picture, and it is set against everything people expect of the last stretch of a life: drying out, thinning, stopping."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the fruit is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Singing out that the LORD is upright. He is my Rock, and there is no wrong in Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The psalm names its own harvest. The old man still bearing fruit is bearing testimony — still able to say what God is like, out of a long life of finding out."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The psalm's title.** *A song for the Sabbath day.* This is the rest-day psalm, and it opens with how good it is to give thanks in the morning and at night. The flourishing it describes is the fruit of a life lived at rest, not of a life spent striving."}]}, "Psalms 95:8–11": {"title": "\"They will never enter My rest\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm turns halfway through, and God speaks in the first person for the rest of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Oh come, let us sing to the LORD! Let us shout joyfully to the Rock of our salvation… Oh come, let us worship and bow down; let us kneel before the LORD our Maker.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven verses of invitation. Then, without warning, the voice changes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not harden your hearts** as at **Meribah**, as on that day at **Massah** in the desert.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two place names, and both are nicknames rather than map locations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Meribah** — *quarrelling, contention*. **Massah** — *testing, proving*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses named the place after what happened there: *he called the name of the place Massah and Meribah, because of the contention of the children of Israel, and because they tempted the LORD, saying, \"Is the LORD among us or not?\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the question at the bottom of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happened there"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rephidim, in Exodus 17. No water, the people ready to stone Moses, and God saying *strike the rock, and water will come out of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They got the water. The place kept the name of the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Where **your ancestors tested Me**, tried Me, **though they had seen My work**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *they proved Me, they saw My work*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had seen the plagues, the sea, the pillar, the manna. The complaint is not ignorance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Forty years"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For **forty years I grieved over that generation**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *grieved* is to be disgusted with, to loathe. It is a strong word and it is in the first person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And I said, '**Their hearts keep wandering, they do not know My ways**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *a people who err in heart*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**They do not know My ways** — not His acts. They had seen the acts for forty years."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"So **I made a promise in My anger**, '**They will never enter My rest**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *I swore in My wrath*, and the form of the sentence is the strongest negative available."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**My rest** — **menuchah**, the same word Isaiah uses. See the note on Isaiah 28:11–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the rest was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Canaan. The land, the end of the marching, the place with the wells already dug."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the generation that came out of Egypt did not go in. Only Caleb and Joshua."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Hebrews does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer quotes these verses twice, in full, and builds two chapters on them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His argument: the word *Today* in the psalm means the offer was still open when David wrote it, four hundred years after Joshua brought them in — so the rest in question is not only the land. See the note on Hebrews 4:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Today, if you will hear His voice, do not harden your hearts.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pivot word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Today.* It is in the verse immediately before this passage, and Hebrews hangs the whole case on it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the psalm is built.** An invitation to come and kneel, and then God's own voice naming the last time they were invited and would not. The warning is inside the call to worship."}]}, "Psalms 96:10–13": {"title": "\"Say among the nations, the LORD reigns\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The psalm gives an announcement to make and then hands the singing over to creation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The announcement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Say among the nations, \"**The LORD reigns.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in Hebrew. It is not a wish or a prayer — it is a statement of what is currently the case, to be said out loud among people who do not know it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows from it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The world **stands firm, it cannot be shaken**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is established, fixed, made to stand. The stability of everything is placed downstream of who is reigning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does the rejoicing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let the **heavens** be glad and the **earth** rejoice; let the **sea** and all it holds resound. Let the **fields** be joyful, and all that is in them; then all the **trees of the forest** will sing for joy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five subjects, and not one of them is a person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sky, ground, sea, field, forest. The psalm moves outward and downward and the whole of creation is put in the choir."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why they are glad"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Before the LORD, for **He is coming, He is coming to judge the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice. And the reason creation rejoices is the judgement — which is not how the word usually lands on us."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *judge* is the governing word: to put right, to set in order, to rule a matter. Creation is described elsewhere as *groaning*, waiting; here it is told the One who will set it right is on His way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The terms"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will judge the world with **righteousness**, and the peoples with **His faithfulness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Righteousness and faithfulness — **emunah**, the steady, dependable word, the root of *amen*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first line of it is *sing to the LORD a new song, sing to the LORD, all the earth.* Then *declare His glory among the nations, His marvellous works among all the peoples.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a psalm about telling. The whole thing is instruction on what to say and to whom."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it was first sung.** 1 Chronicles 16 records almost this entire psalm as the song David gave Asaph on the day the ark was brought into Jerusalem. It was written for the day the presence came home."}]}, "Revelation 1:12–13": {"title": "Among the lampstands", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John turns to look at a voice and sees furniture from the tabernacle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"To look at the voice\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I turned around **to look at the voice** that was talking to me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek says it that way — to see the voice. John is not tidying his grammar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Seven lampstands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There they were: **seven lampstands, all of them gold**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Exodus there is one lampstand, hammered out of a single talent of gold, with six branches from a central shaft. See the note on Exodus 25:31–40."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here there are seven separate stands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus identifies them in the same chapter: *the seven lampstands which you saw are the seven churches.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where He is standing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In among them stood a figure like **the Son of man**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not above them, not in front of them. **In the middle of** them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lampstands in the holy place were the only light in a room with no windows, and He is standing among these."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses later He is described as holding the seven stars in His right hand — but the lampstands He walks among."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Like the Son of man\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase is Daniel 7: *one like the Son of Man, coming with the clouds of heaven… and to Him was given dominion and glory and a kingdom.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is also the title Jesus used of Himself more than any other, some eighty times in the Gospels."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The robe and the band"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His robe ran **the whole length of Him, down to His feet**, and there was **a band of gold around His chest**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The full-length robe is priestly — the Greek word is used in the Greek Old Testament for the high priest's garment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the golden band across the chest, rather than at the waist. A working man belted at the waist to move; a band across the chest is not for labour."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The work is finished, and He is dressed as the priest who is not hurrying."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does with the lampstands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 2 opens: *these things says He who holds the seven stars in His right hand, **who walks in the midst of the seven golden lampstands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Walking among them. And in the temple, the priest's daily task at the lampstand was trimming the wicks and topping up the oil so the light kept burning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letters that follow are addressed to seven churches, most of them with something seriously wrong, and He is among them all."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The first thing He says to John.** *Do not be afraid. I am the First and the Last. **I am He who lives, and was dead, and behold, I am alive forevermore.*** John had fallen at His feet as dead, and the hand that holds the seven stars is laid on him."}]}, "Revelation 1:19–20": {"title": "Write it down", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The instruction that organises the whole book, and then the first thing it explains."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where John is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I, John, both your brother and companion in the tribulation… **was on the island that is called Patmos** for the word of God and for the testimony of Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just seen the figure among the lampstands and *fell at His feet as dead*, and been told *do not be afraid*. See the note on Revelation 1:17–18."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **write it down**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **grapson** — aorist imperative. A single decisive act."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the twelfth of twelve occurrences of the command to write in this book. He is told to write to each of the seven churches, and told at the very end *these words are true and faithful*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three tenses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What you have just seen, what is going on now, and what is going to happen after this.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ha eides, kai ha eisin, kai ha mellei genesthai meta tauta**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seen, are, about to be."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the three correspond to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 1 is what he has seen — the figure among the lampstands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapters 2 and 3 are what is: seven letters to seven functioning congregations with real problems."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 4 opens with the same phrase: *come up here, and I will show you things which **must take place after this***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book states its own outline in one verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for secret"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Here is **the secret** behind the seven stars… and behind the seven gold lampstands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **musterion** — mystery. In the New Testament, not something unknowable but something previously hidden and now disclosed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses it the same way: *the mystery which has been hidden from ages and from generations, but now has been revealed*. See the note on Colossians 1:26–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first explanation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The seven stars are **the angels belonging to the seven churches**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **angeloi** — messengers. The word can mean a heavenly being or a human envoy, and each of the seven letters is addressed *to the angel of the church in*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the seven lampstands you saw are **the seven churches**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the interpretation that matters most, because of where the figure was standing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the midst of the seven lampstands One like the Son of Man.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is standing among them, holding their messengers in His right hand, before a word of correction is spoken."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five of the seven letters contain a rebuke. Two of them are severe. The position was established first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lampstand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the tabernacle there was one lampstand with seven branches, and the priest tended it every morning and evening."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here there are seven separate stands, and the one walking among them is not a priest trimming wicks but the figure of chapter 1."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is threatened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Repent, or else I will come to you quickly and **remove your lampstand from its place***. — to Ephesus."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The stands can be moved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the right hand was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He laid His right hand on me, saying, \"Do not be afraid.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same hand that holds the seven stars. John felt it on his shoulder before he was told what was in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the writing ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he said to me, \"**Do not seal the words of the prophecy of this book**, for the time is at hand.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel was told the opposite — *shut up the words, and seal the book until the time of the end*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The outline.** Seen, are, about to be — the book stating its own three-part shape in one sentence, before a single seal is opened."}]}, "Revelation 1:4–6": {"title": "\"He made us kings and priests\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Before a single vision, before the seals and the trumpets, John states what the reader already is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three verbs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He loved us. With His own blood He washed our sins off us. He made us kings and priests serving God, His own Father.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three short clauses, all in the past. Loved, washed, made. Nothing here is pending."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the order runs from the inside out: love first, then the cleansing, then the office."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Kings and priests"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Israel those two were kept strictly apart. Priests came from Levi, kings from Judah, and when King Uzziah went into the temple to burn incense he came out a leper."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Only Melchizedek held both, and Hebrews makes much of it. Here both are given to ordinary believers in seven small congregations in Asia, some of which the next two chapters will criticise sharply."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is said to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not to spiritual heroes. Read on and these are the churches that have left their first love, tolerated false teaching, and gone lukewarm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The status is stated at the front, before any of that, and it is never withdrawn during the letters that follow."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The description of Christ"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The witness who never lies, the first to be born out of the dead, the ruler the kings of the earth answer to.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three titles, and they map onto the whole book: the one who tells the truth about what is coming, the one who has already been through death, and the one to whom every earthly power is answerable — which is the thing a persecuted church most needed to hear."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**\"The One who is here now, who was here before, and who is on His way.\"** John's version of the divine name, stretched across all three tenses, and he puts the present first. Not *was, is, and will be* — but **is**, was, and is coming."}]}, "Revelation 1:8": {"title": "The first letter and the last", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, spoken in the first person, and it interrupts John's greeting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is **the Lord speaking**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **legei Kurios ho Theos**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book has been John writing to seven churches. Here a voice takes over the sentence for a verse and then hands it back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The letters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am **the first letter and the last**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ego eimi to Alpha kai to O** — I am the Alpha and the Omega."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first and last characters of the Greek alphabet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that claims"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not just *first and last*. The whole alphabet, and therefore everything that can be spelled with it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew equivalent would be **aleph** and **tav**, and the rabbis used that pair the same way — for completeness, from A to Z."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gloss"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The place everything starts and the place everything ends.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The full form appears later in the book: *I am the Alpha and the Omega, the Beginning and the End, the First and the Last*. See the note on Revelation 22:12–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three tenses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The One who is **here now**, who **was here before**, who is **on His way**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ho on kai ho en kai ho erchomenos** — the being one, and the was one, and the coming one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The grammar"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ho on** — the one being. It is exactly what the Septuagint puts in God's mouth at the burning bush: **ego eimi ho on** — I AM THE BEING. See the note on Exodus 3:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ho en** — the one who was. Greek has no participle for the past tense of *to be*, so John breaks the grammar rather than lose the parallel. It is ungrammatical on purpose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *the one who will be*, which is what the pattern requires."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ho erchomenos** — the coming one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The future tense of God's existence is stated as an arrival."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The One who **holds all power**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ho pantokrator** — the Almighty, the all-ruling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It appears nine times in Revelation and once elsewhere in the New Testament. The Septuagint uses it for **YHWH Tzeva'ot** — the LORD of hosts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the verse sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Grace to you and peace **from Him who is and who was and who is to come**.* — verse 4, John's greeting, using the same three-part formula."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold, He is coming with clouds, and every eye will see Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Even so, Amen.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then this verse, in the first person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is writing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I, John, both your brother and companion in the tribulation… was on the island that is called Patmos.* See the note on Revelation 1:9–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A prisoner on a rock in the Aegean, writing to seven congregations under pressure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The turning to see the voice, the seven lampstands, and the figure among them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when I saw Him, **I fell at His feet as dead**. But He laid His right hand on me, saying to me, \"Do not be afraid; **I am the First and the Last**.\"* See the note on Revelation 1:17–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same title, applied by the figure to Himself, with a hand on John's shoulder."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it closes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I am the Alpha and the Omega, the First and the Last, the Beginning and the End.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you all.* See the note on Revelation 22:20–21."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The grammar.** *Ho on, ho en, ho erchomenos.* The middle term is ungrammatical Greek — there is no past participle of *to be* — and John keeps the parallel rather than the grammar."}]}, "Revelation 1:9–10": {"title": "Your brother and companion", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John introduces himself, and every word in the introduction is levelling."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I, John.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No title. Not apostle, not elder, not the disciple whom Jesus loved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book that contains the most extraordinary material in Scripture is signed by a man who declines every credential he has."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The relation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your **brother and companion**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **adelphos kai sunkoinonos** — brother and fellow-sharer."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sunkoinonos** — a partner, somebody in on the same thing. From **koinonia**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they share"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In **the tribulation and kingdom and patient endurance** which are in Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three nouns under one article in the Greek, which binds them into a single condition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Tribulation, kingdom, and endurance — held together rather than sequenced. Not *first the tribulation, later the kingdom*. All three, now."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hupomone** — remaining under."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Was on the island that is called **Patmos**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A rocky island in the Aegean, about ten miles by six, used by Rome as a place of banishment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the word of God and for the testimony of Jesus Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase he uses of the souls under the altar in chapter 6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say he was arrested unjustly, and he does not complain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I was **in the Spirit on the Lord's Day**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **en te kuriake hemera**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The earliest surviving use of the phrase for Sunday. Within a generation it is standard — Ignatius and the Didache both use it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The state"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the Spirit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **en pneumati**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John uses it four times in the book, and each marks a major shift: here, at the throne room in chapter 4, in the wilderness in chapter 17, and on the great high mountain in chapter 21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sound"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And I heard behind me **a loud voice, as of a trumpet**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Behind him. He has to turn to see who is speaking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he turns to see"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then I turned to see the voice that spoke with me. And having turned I saw **seven golden lampstands**, and in the midst of the seven lampstands **One like the Son of Man**.* See the note on Revelation 1:12–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when I saw Him, **I fell at His feet as dead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who had leaned on His chest at supper. See the note on John 13:21–26."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But He laid His right hand on me, saying to me, \"**Do not be afraid; I am the First and the Last. I am He who lives, and was dead, and behold, I am alive forevermore**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Write the things which you have seen, and the things which are, and the things which will take place after this.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three tenses, and the book divides along them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is writing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An old man on a prison island, describing himself as a fellow-sufferer before he describes anything else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in the next twenty-one chapters — the throne, the seals, the trumpets, the dragon, the city — is written by somebody in exile who introduces himself as *your brother*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he leaves out.** Every title he has. The book with the most authority claimed in it is signed *your brother and companion in the tribulation*."}]}, "Revelation 12:10–11": {"title": "The accuser thrown down, and the three things that beat him", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A loud voice in heaven announces a defeat, and it names both what the enemy did for a living and what put him out of business."}, {"k": "h", "t": "His job description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The one who accused our brothers… the one who stood in front of our God accusing them, day and night.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what he was doing. Not tempting, in this passage — **accusing**, in front of God, without stopping. The word behind it is a courtroom word, and it is where the title Satan comes from: the accuser, the adversary in a legal sense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job 1 and Zechariah 3 both show him at that work. Zechariah's scene is the clearest: the high priest standing in filthy clothes with Satan at his right hand to accuse him — and God rebuking the accuser and re-clothing the man."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What beat him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three things, and the order is worth keeping:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**the Lamb's blood** — the ground of it. Accusation has nothing to work with once the charge has been answered.", "**the message they told** — their testimony, spoken. What was true was said out loud.", "**they would not cling to their own lives even with death in front of them** — people who had stopped being controlled by the last threat available."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first is what was done for them. The second and third are what they did with it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Now it has arrived\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Salvation, and strength, and the kingdom of our God, and the power of His Christ.* The announcement of the accuser's fall is made in the same breath as the announcement that the kingdom has come."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two are treated as one event."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the accusing stopped.** Romans asks *who is going to lay a charge against the ones God Himself has chosen?* and answers by pointing at the one at God's right hand — not accusing, but interceding. Revelation shows the same thing from the other side: the seat the accuser used to occupy is empty. See the note on Romans 8:31–34."}]}, "Revelation 12:12": {"title": "He knows he has little time", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A verse with a celebration in the first half and a warning in the second."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So the great dragon was cast out… **he was cast to the earth, and his angels were cast out with him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the loud voice: *\"Now salvation, and strength, and the kingdom of our God, and the power of His Christ have come, for **the accuser of our brethren, who accused them before our God day and night, has been cast down**.\"* See the note on Revelation 12:10–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The celebration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **celebrate, you heavens**, and everyone whose home is up there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **euphrainesthe** — be glad, make merry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Heaven is told to rejoice because something has been removed from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dwellers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone whose home is up there.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hoi ouranois skenountes** — those who tabernacle in the heavens."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **it will be terrible for everyone living on the earth and on the sea**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ouai** — woe."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same event is good news in one place and bad news in another. What is expelled from heaven arrives somewhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **the devil has come down to you in a towering rage**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **thumon megan** — great wrath. **Thumos** is the hot kind, the boiling-over kind, as distinct from settled anger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he is angry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He knows he has **only a little time left**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **oligon kairon** — a short season."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kairos**, not **chronos**. Not a quantity of time but an allotted opportunity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rage is described as a symptom of the knowledge. He is not furious because he is losing; he is furious because he can see the clock."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest of the chapter: he pursues the woman, the earth helps her by swallowing the flood from his mouth, and then —"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the dragon was enraged with the woman, and he went to make war with the rest of her offspring, who keep the commandments of God and have the testimony of Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word Peter uses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be sober, be vigilant; because your adversary the devil **walks about like a roaring lion**, seeking whom he may devour.* See the note on 1 Peter 5:8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter's picture is a hungry animal. John's is a defeated official with a deadline."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the brethren overcame"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before this one gives it in three parts:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they overcame him by **the blood of the Lamb** and by **the word of their testimony**, and they **did not love their lives to the death**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here suggests the outcome is in question. The dragon has already been thrown down before the woe is pronounced, and the celebration is in the same verse as the warning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation keeps that order everywhere: the throne room in chapter 4 comes before the seals in chapter 6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The devil, who deceived them, was cast into the lake of fire and brimstone… and they will be tormented day and night forever and ever.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The accuser who worked *day and night* is dealt with in the same phrase."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the rage is a symptom of.** Not strength. He is described as knowing exactly how much time is left, and the fury is the reaction to the number."}]}, "Revelation 14:1–2": {"title": "The Lamb on Mount Sion", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "After the darkest chapter in the book, John looks and sees somebody standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 13: the beast out of the sea, the beast out of the earth, the mark on the right hand or the forehead, and nobody able to buy or sell without it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the chapter break, and: *then I looked*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The posture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There was **the Lamb, standing** on Mount Sion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hestos** — standing. The same participle used in chapter 5, where the Lamb stands *as though it had been slain*. See the note on Revelation 5:7–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not enthroned in this scene. Standing, on a hill, with a crowd."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The place"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mount Sion.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The temple hill. The psalm says *beautiful in elevation, the joy of the whole earth, is Mount Zion*, and Hebrews uses it as the destination: *you have come to Mount Zion and to the city of the living God.* See the note on Hebrews 12:18–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two mountains in that letter — one that could be touched and burned with fire, and this one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **a hundred and forty-four thousand** were with Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve squared, times a thousand. The number is introduced in chapter 7, sealed *out of every tribe of the children of Israel*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mark"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **His Father's name written on their foreheads**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter before had a mark on the forehead or the hand, without which nobody could trade."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the same location and a different name. Revelation sets the two marks opposite each other by putting them one chapter apart."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sound"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then I heard a sound out of heaven **like a great weight of water falling**, like **a huge roll of thunder**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *like the voice of many waters and like the voice of loud thunder*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both images are already in the book: chapter 1 describes the voice of the risen Christ as *the sound of many waters*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the sound I heard was **harpists playing their harps**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something with the weight of a waterfall and the force of thunder turns out, on hearing, to be music."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Harps are in the throne room in chapter 5, held by the elders alongside the bowls of incense."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The song"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *they sang **as it were a new song** before the throne… and **no one could learn that song except the hundred and forty-four thousand** who were redeemed from the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A song with a restricted membership. Not because it is secret — because it is theirs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are called"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These are the ones who follow the Lamb wherever He goes. These were **redeemed from among men, being firstfruits to God and to the Lamb**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Firstfruits** — **aparche**, the first sheaf, which is a guarantee rather than a sample. Paul uses it of Christ. See the note on 1 Corinthians 15:23–28."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three angels with three announcements — the everlasting gospel to every nation, the fall of Babylon, and a warning — and then the harvest of the earth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The placement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the two beasts and the bowls of wrath, John inserts a hill with a Lamb on it and a sound like water."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is on the two foreheads.** A mark that lets you buy and sell, and a name. The chapters are consecutive, and the second opens with the Lamb standing."}]}, "Revelation 16:11": {"title": "\"They did not turn away\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it is a report on what pain did not accomplish."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fifth bowl. The bowl judgments are the last series in the book — seven angels, seven bowls, poured on the earth, the sea, the rivers, the sun, the throne of the beast, the Euphrates, and the air."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fourth bowl scorched men with fire; the fifth brought darkness on the beast's kingdom, *and they gnawed their tongues because of the pain*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they **cursed the God of heaven** over their pains and their sores.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eblasphemesan** — blasphemed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They know who is doing it. The verse says they cursed **the God of heaven**, which means the source is not in doubt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they **did not turn away from the things they were doing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *they did not repent of their deeds* — **metanoeo**, to change the mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refrain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not the only time. The chapter says it three times, and the sixth trumpet in chapter 9 says it too:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*They **did not repent** to give Him glory.*", "*And they **did not repent** of their deeds.*", "*And men blasphemed God because of the plague of the hail… and they **did not repent**.*", "Chapter 9: *the rest of mankind, who were not killed by these plagues, **did not repent** of the works of their hands.*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four times, after four different judgments."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book is reporting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The judgments land, the pain is real, the source is correctly identified, and nobody changes their mind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation states it as an observation each time, without comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says produces repentance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do you despise the riches of His goodness, forbearance and longsuffering, **not knowing that the goodness of God leads you to repentance?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **agei** — leads, draws along."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul names a different agent from the one operating in Revelation 16, and Revelation records what the other one achieved."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one exception in the book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 11, after the two witnesses are raised and an earthquake kills seven thousand: *and the rest were afraid and **gave glory to the God of heaven**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only time in the judgment sequences that anyone does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the bowls are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seven golden bowls full of the wrath of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word for bowl — **phiale** — as the golden bowls full of incense held by the elders in chapter 5, which John says are the prayers of God's people. See the note on Revelation 5:7–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same vessel, two contents."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the verse records.** Pain, correctly attributed, and no change. Revelation says it four times across two judgment cycles and never once presents it as a surprise."}]}, "Revelation 19:8": {"title": "Clean bright linen", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and the grammar of it is the whole point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Let us be glad and rejoice and give Him glory, for **the marriage of the Lamb has come**, and His wife has made herself ready.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fourfold *hallelujah* of the chapter has just been sung — the only occurrences of the word in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clothing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **she was given** clean bright linen to wear.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **edothe aute** — it was given to her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aorist passive. Somebody handed it over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His wife has **made herself ready**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hetoimasen heauten** — prepared herself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Active. She did that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two halves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She prepared herself; the dress was given to her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both statements stand in consecutive verses, and Revelation does not reconcile them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The linen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Bussinon lampron katharon** — fine linen, bright and clean."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Bussos** — the costly white linen of Egypt, used for priestly garments and royal dress."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Lampron** — shining, radiant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The interpretation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the linen is the right acts of God's people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ta dikaiomata ton hagion** — the righteous deeds of the saints."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dikaioma** — a righteous act, or a righteous verdict. The noun is used both ways in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The dress that was given is identified as things the saints did."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both facts are stated in one verse: the garment was handed to her, and the garment is her deeds."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other white robes in the book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They washed their robes and **made them white in the blood of the Lamb**.* See the note on Revelation 7:13–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the only way a robe gets whiter for being washed in blood."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He who overcomes shall be **clothed in white garments**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I counsel you to buy from Me… **white garments, that you may be clothed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation keeps giving robes rather than describing people acquiring them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two chapters earlier, the other woman: *the woman was arrayed in purple and scarlet, and adorned with gold and precious stones and pearls*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Babylon dresses expensively. The bride wears linen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who else wears it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the armies in heaven, clothed in **fine linen, white and clean**, followed Him on white horses.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same fabric, eleven verses later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says about the preparation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That He might sanctify and cleanse her **with the washing of water by the word**, that He might present her to Himself a glorious church, **not having spot or wrinkle**.* See the note on Ephesians 5:25–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The cleaning is done by the bridegroom, in Paul's account, and the presenting is His too."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The invitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Blessed are those who are called to the marriage supper of the Lamb!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the angel's own correction, immediately after: *\"See that you do not do that! I am your fellow servant… **Worship God!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John had fallen at his feet."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two clauses.** *She made herself ready*, and *she was given* the dress — and the dress is identified as what the saints did. Revelation states both and leaves them standing."}]}, "Revelation 2:1": {"title": "Walking among the lampstands", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first of the seven letters, and it opens by naming what He is doing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Write this to **the angel of the church at Ephesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **angelos** — messenger. All seven letters are addressed this way and nobody is certain what it means: the church's guardian angel, its overseer, or the messenger carrying the letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The city"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ephesus. The largest city in the province, with the temple of Artemis — one of the seven wonders — and a theatre that seated twenty-five thousand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul spent three years there. It is where the riot broke out over the silversmiths' trade, and where he found twelve men who had stopped at John's baptism. See the note on Acts 19:1–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Timothy was left there. John is traditionally associated with it too."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The formula"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Here is who is speaking.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of the seven letters opens this way, and each takes a description from the vision in chapter 1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The vision had eighteen or so features — white hair, eyes like fire, feet like burnished brass, a voice like many waters, a sword from His mouth. Each church gets the ones it needs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The One who **holds the seven stars in His right hand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kraton** — holding fast, gripping."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 1 had said *He had in His right hand seven stars*. The verb here is stronger: not merely holding, but keeping hold of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the vision explains the symbol: *the seven stars are the angels of the seven churches*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The One who **walks about in among the seven lampstands of gold**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **peripaton** — walking around, moving about."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In chapter 1 He was *in the midst of the seven lampstands* — standing. Here He is walking among them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the lampstands are named too: *the seven lampstands which you saw are the seven churches*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the two images say together"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The churches are lampstands and He is walking between them. The messengers are stars and He is holding them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the seven letters is an assessment from a distance. He is described, before the first commendation or rebuke, as somebody moving about inside the room."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The lampstand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Luchnia** — the word the Greek Old Testament uses for the seven-branched menorah in the tabernacle."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One lampstand with seven branches there. Seven separate lampstands here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The priest's daily job was to tend it — trim the wicks, top up the oil, and keep it burning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He says to this church"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I know your works, your labour, your patience**, and that you cannot bear those who are evil… and you have persevered and have patience, and have laboured for My name's sake and have not become weary.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A hardworking, doctrinally careful, tireless congregation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Nevertheless I have this against you, that **you have left your first love**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Remember therefore from where you have fallen; repent and do the first works, or else I will come to you quickly and **remove your lampstand from its place**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The threat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that the church will be corrected. That the lampstand will be moved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ephesus is a field of ruins now."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To him who overcomes I will give **to eat from the tree of life, which is in the midst of the Paradise of God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first of the seven promises, and it reaches back to Genesis 3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one who holds the stars"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter to Ephesus is a rebuke, and the description at the top of it is a hand keeping hold."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He is doing before He says anything.** Walking about among them, with the messengers gripped in His right hand — and that is the description attached to the letter with the hardest warning in it."}]}, "Revelation 2:2–3": {"title": "\"I know what you have been doing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The first of the seven letters, and it opens with a page of commendation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *these things says **He who holds the seven stars in His right hand, who walks in the midst of the seven golden lampstands**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every letter opens with a description of the speaker taken from the vision in chapter 1."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I know what you have been doing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **oida** — settled knowledge. It opens six of the seven letters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven things are commended, and they are all real:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*\"I know **how hard you have worked**\"* — **kopos**, labour to the point of exhaustion", "*\"and **how much you have taken without giving way**\"* — **hupomone**, remaining under", "*\"You **cannot stomach wicked men**\"*", "*\"When people turned up claiming to be apostles, you **put them to the test and caught them out as liars**\"*", "*\"You have **carried the weight**\"*", "*\"You have **held out**\"*", "*\"You have **worn yourself down for the sake of My name**, and **you have not run out of strength**\"*"]}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind of church this is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Doctrinally careful, tireless, intolerant of frauds, and still standing after decades of pressure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had spent three years there and warned the elders that *savage wolves* would come in and that *from among your own selves men will rise up*. See the note on Acts 20:28–31."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They did what he told them to. They tested and they caught them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The endurance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek **hupomone** appears twice in two verses. Peter uses it of the same period, and James says testing produces it. See the note on James 1:3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The next verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Nevertheless I have this against you, that you have left your first love**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **aphekes** — you left, you let go. Not *lost*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole commendation is intact and this is added to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Remember therefore from where you have fallen; repent and do the first works**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three imperatives: remember, repent, do."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The first works** — not new ones. The ones they were doing at the start."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is at stake"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Or else I will come to you quickly and **remove your lampstand from its place** — unless you repent.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lampstand is the church itself; chapter 1 says so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they still had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"But this you have, that **you hate the deeds of the Nicolaitans, which I also hate**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He agrees with them again, in the same breath as the warning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"To him who overcomes I will give **to eat from the tree of life**, which is in the midst of the Paradise of God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tree nobody has reached since Genesis 3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Praise, one thing, instruction, warning, praise again, promise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The complaint occupies one verse out of seven."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the commendation contains.** Work, endurance, doctrinal vigilance, and stamina — all confirmed by the speaker. And the one thing named against them is not on that axis at all."}]}, "Revelation 2:4–5": {"title": "\"Do again what you did then\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The church at Ephesus is commended for a great deal and then told one thing is wrong, and the one thing outweighs the list."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read the verses just before: hard work, perseverance, intolerance of evil, they had tested false apostles and found them out, they had endured hardships for His name and not grown weary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By any external measure this is a strong church. Nothing on that list is withdrawn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The charge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That love you had at the beginning — you have walked away from it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *your first love*, and the verb is to leave or abandon. Not that it cooled. That they walked off from it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Doctrine intact, effort intact, discernment intact — and the thing that made it all worth doing has been left somewhere behind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The remedy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three imperatives, and the middle one is the practical one:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**remember** how high you stood before you fell", "**turn back**", "**do again what you did then** — the first works"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not tell them to work up a feeling. He tells them to do the things they did when the love was fresh. The affection is recovered by returning to the actions that belonged to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for *remember* is present continuous — keep remembering, not one act of recollection."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will take your lampstand away from where it stands.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lampstand is the church itself, as chapter 1 explains. Not a threat to individuals' salvation — a warning that a congregation which keeps everything except the love stops being a light in that place."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is being asked for.** The whole letter is written by the One who chapter 1 describes as walking among the lampstands. He is not asking for more output from a church that is already working hard. He is asking to be loved by it."}]}, "Revelation 21:8": {"title": "The list, and what heads it", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A list of eight, and the order is not the one anybody would predict."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the middle of the best news in the book."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And God will wipe away every tear from their eyes; there shall be no more death, nor sorrow, nor crying. There shall be no more pain, for the former things have passed away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold, I make all things new.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will give of the fountain of the water of life freely to him who thirsts. **He who overcomes shall inherit all things, and I will be his God and he shall be My son**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then verse 8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first two"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the cowards**, and **the ones who would not believe**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **deilois** — the fearful, the timid. And **apistois** — unbelieving, faithless."}, {"k": "p", "t": "These head the list. Before the murderers, before the sorcerers, before the idolaters."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Deilos** is not the ordinary word for fear. It occurs three times in the New Testament, and one of the others is in the boat: *why are you so **fearful**? How is it that you have no faith?* See the note on Mark 4:35–41."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same pairing there — fearful, and no faith."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the word is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the fear anybody feels. Scripture is full of frightened people who are commended."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word describes the fear that governs — that decides. In a book written to churches under Roman pressure, it means the man who will not say the name when asked."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The remaining six"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ones whose lives are **vile**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ebdelugmenois** — the abominable, from the root of **bdelugma**, the abomination."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **murderers**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **sexually immoral**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Pornois** — the standard word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ones who **deal in magic**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Pharmakois** — sorcerers, and the word behind *pharmacy*. Drugs and spells were the same trade."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The ones who **worship idols**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **every liar**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pasin tois pseudesin** — all the liars."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ending on the smallest-sounding item, and *every*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their part is in **the lake that burns with fire and sulfur**. That is **the second death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **to meros auton** — their portion, their share."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word used of an inheritance. The previous verse had *he who overcomes shall inherit all things*, and this one assigns a different portion."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second death"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation defines it three times, and this is one of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed and holy is he who has part in the first resurrection. **Over such the second death has no power**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being written to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven churches, most of them under pressure, some of them being killed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not fear any of those things which you are about to suffer… be faithful until death, and I will give you the crown of life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter to Smyrna, and the two words in it are the two at the head of this list, in reverse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter goes on to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The city. Twelve gates, twelve foundations, a wall of jasper, a street of gold, and no temple — *for the Lord God Almighty and the Lamb are its temple*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there shall be no night there: they need no lamp nor light of the sun, for the Lord God gives them light.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The invitation two chapters later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And let him who thirsts come. Whoever desires, **let him take the water of life freely**.* See the note on Revelation 22:12–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book's last invitation is unrestricted, and it is four verses from the end."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What heads the list.** Not murder. The fearful and the unbelieving — written to congregations who were being asked, under threat, whether they knew Him."}]}, "Revelation 22:1–2": {"title": "The river and the tree", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last chapter of the Bible, and the two things in it are the two things Genesis 2 opened with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The river"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he showed me **a river of the water of life**, pure, **clear as crystal**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 2: *now a river went out of Eden to water the garden, and from there it parted and became four riverheads.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Bible opens with a river in a garden and closes with one in a city."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Running out from **the throne of God and of the Lamb**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One throne, two occupants. The construction is deliberate and John uses it again in the next verse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The river has a source rather than a catchment. It does not collect; it proceeds."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The street"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Down the middle of the city's street, **with the river on both sides of it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The geometry is difficult to picture, and John does not help. A street with a river on either side, and a tree in the middle of both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel saw something similar — water from under the temple threshold, deepening as it went, *and on the bank of the river, on this side and that, will grow all kinds of trees for food*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tree"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Stood **the tree of life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 2: *the tree of life was also in the midst of the garden.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 3 closes the access: *He drove out the man; and He placed cherubim at the east of the garden of Eden, and a flaming sword which turned every way, **to guard the way to the tree of life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing between Genesis 3 and Revelation 22 gets back to it. Proverbs uses the phrase figuratively — wisdom is *a tree of life to those who take hold of her* — and the letters to the seven churches promise it: *to him who overcomes I will give to eat from the tree of life*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here the guard is gone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fruit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It carries **twelve kinds of fruit** and gives its fruit **every month**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twelve, and monthly. There is no season in which it is not producing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The leaves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the leaves of the tree are for the healing of the nations**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **therapeian** — the root of *therapy*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The nations** — **ethnon**, the peoples. The word that runs through the whole Bible as the outsiders, from the promise to Abraham that *in you all the nations shall be blessed* to the great multitude *from every nation, tribe, people and tongue*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the next verse says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **there shall be no more curse**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katathema**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The curse in Genesis 3 fell on the ground: *cursed is the ground for your sake; in toil you shall eat of it all the days of your life.* See the note on Genesis 3:17–19."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What else is gone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There shall be **no night there**: they need no lamp nor light of the sun, for the Lord God gives them light.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And earlier: *God will wipe away every tear from their eyes; there shall be **no more death, nor sorrow, nor crying**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last invitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the Spirit and the bride say, \"Come!\"… **and let him who thirsts come. Whoever desires, let him take the water of life freely**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dorean** — freely, as a gift, for nothing. The same word Paul uses of being justified."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Bible's last offer is a drink, at no cost."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the two chapters share.** A river and a tree. Genesis puts a sword between the man and one of them; Revelation puts it in the middle of a street with leaves for the nations."}]}, "Revelation 22:12–13": {"title": "The first letter and the last", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Near the last words of the Bible, and He is doing the talking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The announcement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am coming, and **soon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tachu** — quickly, swiftly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is said three times in this final chapter: verse 7, verse 12, and verse 20. And the book's closing exchange is *surely I am coming quickly* — *Amen. Even so, come, Lord Jesus!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word is about the manner as much as the timing: when it happens it will be sudden."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reward"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **I have My reward with Me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ho misthos mou** — my wage, my payment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is carrying it. Not sending for it, not calculating it on arrival."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah has the same picture twice: *behold, the Lord GOD shall come with a strong hand… behold, His reward is with Him, and His work before Him*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To give every man **what his work comes to**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hos to ergon estin autou** — as his work is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three titles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am **the first letter and the last**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Alpha and Omega. The first and last letters of the Greek alphabet, and the phrase is used four times in Revelation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew equivalent — aleph and tav — sits in the second sentence of Genesis as an untranslatable grammatical marker. See the note on Genesis 1:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The place everything **starts** and the place everything **ends**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **he arche kai to telos** — the beginning and the end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **first and the last**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ho protos kai ho eschatos**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three pairs, saying the same thing three ways: as letters, as points, as ordinals."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who said it first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I, the LORD, am the first; and with the last I am He.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *\"I am the First and I am the Last; besides Me there is no God.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah, twice, in the mouth of God. Revelation puts the same words in the mouth of Christ, and the first chapter has already done it: *I am the First and the Last. I am He who lives, and was dead, and behold, I am alive forevermore.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is on either side of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Blessed are those who do His commandments, that they may have the right to the tree of life, and may enter through the gates into the city.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And two verses on, the last invitation in the Bible:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the Spirit and the bride say, \"**Come!**\" And let him who hears say, \"Come!\" And let him who thirsts come. **Whoever desires, let him take the water of life freely**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dorean** — as a gift, without payment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two sentences together"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 12 says every man is paid according to his work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 17 says the water is free to anyone who wants it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation puts both in the same paragraph and does not reconcile them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last warning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If anyone adds to these things, God will add to him the plagues that are written in this book; and if anyone takes away from the words of the book of this prophecy, God shall take away his part from the Book of Life.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you all. Amen.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sixty-six books, and the closing word before the amen is *grace*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What He is carrying.** The wage, already with Him. And five verses later the water is offered *freely* — both sentences in the same paragraph."}]}, "Revelation 22:20–21": {"title": "The last word", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and the Bible is finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The testimony"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The One who gives this testimony says, **Yes — I am coming, and soon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **nai, erchomai tachu**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has said it three times in this chapter: *behold, I am coming quickly* at verse 7, again at verse 12, and now at verse 20 with **nai** — yes — in front of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for soon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tachu** — quickly, suddenly. It describes the manner as much as the interval."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Amen. **Come, Lord Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **erchou, Kyrie Iesou**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul preserves the Aramaic of the same prayer at the end of 1 Corinthians: **Maranatha** — our Lord, come."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The church's oldest recorded prayer, in the language they actually prayed it in, and John ends the canon with the Greek of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just been offered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the Spirit and the bride say, \"Come!\" And let him who hears say, \"Come!\" And **let him who thirsts come. Whoever desires, let him take the water of life freely**.* See the note on Revelation 22:16–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four *comes* in one verse, three of them inviting people in and one calling Christ back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The closing line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you all. Amen.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **he charis tou Kyriou Iesou Christou meta panton**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the line comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a letter-ending."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul closes every letter he wrote with some form of it: *the grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you*. Revelation is addressed to seven churches and it signs off like the mail it is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The last word of the Bible"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Grace**, and then **amen**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Old Testament's last word is **cherem** — the ban, total destruction, the threat held off by hearts turning between parents and children. See the note on Malachi 4:4–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two canons end four words apart in Hebrew and Greek, and they are opposite words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With you all* — **meta panton**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not with the overcomers, the sealed, the hundred and forty-four thousand, or the ones whose churches got a good report in chapters 2 and 3. All of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book that has spent twenty-two chapters distinguishing closes without distinguishing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The warning about adding to or taking from the words of the prophecy, and then this."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A severe sentence, and then grace, in that order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book opened with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Grace to you and peace from Him who is and who was and who is to come.* See the note on Revelation 1:4–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grace at the beginning and grace at the end, with the seals, trumpets, bowls, and the throne in between."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The last word.** Grace — spoken over *all*, in a book that spent twenty-two chapters distinguishing, and four words away from the curse the other canon ends on."}]}, "Revelation 3:14–15": {"title": "\"The Amen\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last of the seven letters, and the speaker names Himself with a word nobody had used as a title before."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Write this to **the angel of the church of the Laodiceans**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Laodicea was a banking centre in the Lycus valley, known for a black wool industry and a medical school that produced an eye ointment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "All three turn up in the letter: *buy from Me gold refined in the fire… white garments… and anoint your eyes with eye salve*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He speaks to a city in its own vocabulary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Here is who is speaking: **the Amen**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Amen** is a Hebrew word meaning *firm, sure, so be it*. It is what a congregation says back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody had used it as a name. Here it is a person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah comes closest: *he who blesses himself in the earth shall bless himself in **the God of truth*** — and the Hebrew there is *the God of Amen*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second and third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The witness who is **faithful**, the witness who **tells the truth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ho martus ho pistos kai alethinos**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Martus** — witness, the root of *martyr*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fourth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **beginning of everything God made**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **he arche tes ktiseos tou theou** — the beginning, the origin, the ruling principle of God's creation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Arche** carries both senses: what something starts from, and what governs it. Colossians uses the same idea: *He is before all things, and in Him all things consist… **the beginning**, the firstborn from the dead.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul takes the Amen"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **all the promises of God in Him are Yes, and in Him Amen**, to the glory of God through us.* See the note on 2 Corinthians 1:15–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God says Yes; the Amen comes back through the people who trust it. Paul puts both halves in one sentence, and here the Amen is a name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The assessment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I know what you have been doing**. You are **not cold and you are not hot**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **psuchros** and **zestos** — cold, and boiling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every one of the seven letters opens *I know*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The preference"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I wish you were cold, or else hot**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is a startling sentence. Cold is named as preferable to what they are."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The local detail"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Laodicea had no water of its own. It was piped in — hot mineral water from Hierapolis six miles north, cold spring water from Colossae ten miles east."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By the time either arrived it was lukewarm."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hot springs were medicinal; the cold water was refreshing. Tepid water is the one that is good for nothing, and the next verse says what it provokes: *I will vomit you out of My mouth*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they said about themselves"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because you say, '**I am rich, have become wealthy, and have need of nothing**' — and do not know that you are **wretched, miserable, poor, blind, and naked**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is said last"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Behold, **I stand at the door and knock**. If anyone hears My voice and opens the door, **I will come in to him and dine with him**, and he with Me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Addressed to a church, from outside it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The harshest of the seven letters ends with a meal being offered through a door."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The title He opens with.** The Amen — the word people say back. Paul says every promise gets its Yes in Him and its Amen through us; here the Amen is the one doing the speaking."}]}, "Revelation 3:7–8": {"title": "A door nobody can shut", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One of two churches in the seven that gets no rebuke."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The city"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Philadelphia** — in Lydia, founded as a deliberate outpost to spread Greek culture eastward. A gateway city on the road inland."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It sat on a fault line and had been flattened by earthquakes more than once, so that people lived outside the walls, expecting the next one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The titles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The One who is **holy**, the One who is **true**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hagios** and **alethinos** — holy, and genuine as opposed to counterfeit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are Old Testament titles for God, used here of Christ without comment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The key"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The One holding **David's key** — when He **opens a thing, nobody shuts it**, and when He **shuts a thing, nobody opens it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 22, about Eliakim replacing Shebna as steward of the palace:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The key of the house of David I will lay on his shoulder; so he shall open, and no one shall shut; and he shall shut, and no one shall open.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A steward's key. The man who controlled access to the king."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He knows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I know what you have been doing**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The formula for all seven letters — *I know your works*. To Ephesus, to Smyrna, to Sardis, to Laodicea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Praise and rebuke both start from the same clause."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The door"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Look — **I have put a door in front of you and it stands open**, and **there is no one who can shut it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a perfect participle — **eneogmenen**, having been opened and standing open."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same image of opportunity: *a great and effective door has opened to me, and there are many adversaries*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The admission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**There is not much strength in you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **mikran echeis dunamin** — you have little power."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said as a fact, not a criticism. The letter has no rebuke in it anywhere."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The open door and the small strength are in consecutive clauses, and the door does not depend on the strength."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And still you **kept My word and never disowned My name**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things, both of them about holding rather than achieving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Indeed I will make those of the synagogue of Satan… come and worship before your feet, and **to know that I have loved you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Because you have kept My command to persevere, **I also will keep you from the hour of trial which shall come upon the whole world**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise about the pillar"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"He who overcomes, I will make him **a pillar in the temple of My God, and he shall go out no more**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said to a congregation in an earthquake city where people slept outside because buildings fell down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And I will write on him the name of My God, and the name of the city of My God, the New Jerusalem… and My new name.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Philadelphia had been renamed twice in its history to flatter Roman emperors."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other church without rebuke"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Smyrna — poor, slandered, and about to be imprisoned. *I know your works, tribulation, and poverty (but you are rich).*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two churches Christ finds nothing against are the weak one and the poor one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seventh letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Laodicea, forty miles away: *I am rich, have become wealthy, and have need of nothing* — and He is outside the door knocking. See the note on Revelation 3:14–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two doors in the same chapter. One is open in front of a church with little strength, and one has Christ on the wrong side of it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the door does not depend on.** He names the open door and their small strength in consecutive sentences, and puts *nobody can shut it* between them."}]}, "Revelation 4:5–6": {"title": "A sea of glass", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The throne room, described by a man who has been told to write down what he sees."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is happening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*After these things I looked, and behold, **a door standing open in heaven**… \"Come up here, and I will show you things which must take place after this.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Immediately I was in the Spirit; and behold, **a throne set in heaven, and One sat on the throne**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole chapter is furniture, and everything in it is arranged around one seat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The noise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The throne itself was **throwing off lightning, and thunder rolling out of it, and voices**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same three at Sinai: *there were thunderings and lightnings, and a thick cloud on the mountain*. See the note on Exodus 19:16–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation repeats the trio at intervals through the book, each time something significant opens."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seven"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Seven fires were burning in front of it, seven lamps, and those are **the seven Spirits of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hepta lampades puros**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The lampstand in the tabernacle had seven lamps and burned continuously. See the note on Exodus 25:31–40."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah saw one too, and asked what it meant: *not by might nor by power, but by My Spirit*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John identifies them outright rather than leaving them symbolic."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sea"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Spread out before the throne was something like **a sea made of glass, clear all the way through**, the way crystal is.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **thalassa hualine** — a glassy sea."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the tabernacle there was a laver of bronze between the altar and the tent, for the priests to wash. In the temple it was enlarged into *the Sea* — a cast basin holding thousands of gallons on twelve bronze oxen."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here it is not water. It is glass, and it is still."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is washing in it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the calm means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sea in Hebrew imagery is chaos. The deep, the monsters in it, the storm the disciples were in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah: *the wicked are like the troubled sea, when it cannot rest*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A sea of glass is that, motionless."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The creatures"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And at the center of the throne, and all around it, were **four creatures**, and there were **eyes all over them**, on the front of them and on the back.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **zoa** — living ones. Not *beasts*, which the old versions have; **therion** is the word for a beast, and Revelation uses that one for something else entirely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they look like"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse: *the first living creature was like **a lion**, the second like **a calf**, the third had a face like **a man**, and the fourth was like **a flying eagle***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same four faces as Ezekiel's creatures by the river Chebar. See the note on Ezekiel 1:10–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the same four as the standards of the camp in Numbers, by the ancient tradition — Judah, Ephraim, Reuben and Dan, three tribes to a side, all facing inward. See the note on Numbers 2:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The eyes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Front and back, and covered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever else they are, they are described as things that see in every direction and do not stop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they **do not rest day or night**, saying: \"**Holy, holy, holy, Lord God Almighty, who was and is and is to come!**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah heard the same three words in the temple, from seraphim, in the year King Uzziah died."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who calls the riders"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One creature per seal, for the first four. See the note on Revelation 6:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The four faces round the throne are the four voices that summon the four horses."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the water is doing.** Nothing. In the tabernacle it was a basin for washing; here it is glass, motionless, and the sea in Hebrew imagery is what will not be still."}]}, "Revelation 5:1–2": {"title": "A scroll nobody could open", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses of setup, and they produce the only place in Revelation where John cries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The throne room of chapter 4 — the sea of glass, the four living creatures, the twenty-four elders."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing has happened yet. The scene has been described, and now something is noticed in a hand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He is holding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The One on the throne was holding something in **His right hand**: a **scroll**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **biblion** — a book or roll."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The right hand is the hand of authority. It is held out, not put away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The writing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With writing **on the inside of it and writing on the back as well**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Papyrus was normally written on one side, along the grain. Writing on both means the document is full — nothing more can be added to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel is given one like it: *a scroll of a book was in it, and He spread it before me; and there was writing on the inside and on the outside, and written on it were lamentations and mourning and woe*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Shut with **seven seals**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Roman wills were sealed with seven seals, one for each witness, and could only be opened in the presence of all of them or by the rightful heir."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jeremiah's title deed to a field at Anathoth was sealed the same way — *I signed the deed and sealed it, took witnesses* — and it was a purchase made while the Babylonian army was outside the city, as a sign that fields would be bought again."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A sealed scroll is a document of ownership."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The challenge"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there was a **mighty angel calling out at the top of his voice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *proclaiming with a loud voice*. A herald."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Who deserves to break these seals and open this scroll?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **axios** — worthy, of sufficient weight."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not who is *able*. Who is qualified."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And no one in heaven or on the earth or under the earth was able to open the scroll, or to look at it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three realms, searched, and nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **I wept much**, because no one was found worthy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only recorded weeping in the book, and it is over an unopened document."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not weep. Behold, the Lion of the tribe of Judah, the Root of David, has prevailed** to open the scroll and to loose its seven seals.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The elder announces a Lion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And I looked, and behold, in the midst of the throne… **stood a Lamb as though it had been slain**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John hears *Lion* and turns to look, and sees a Lamb with its throat cut, standing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **arnion** — a little lamb — and **esphagmenon**, slaughtered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What qualifies Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**You are worthy** to take the scroll and to open its seals, **for You were slain, and have redeemed us to God by Your blood**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The worthiness the angel asked about is answered by the killing. Nothing else in the chapter is offered as the credential."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the new song says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And have made us kings and priests to our God, and we shall reign on the earth.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the number: *ten thousand times ten thousand, and thousands of thousands*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the last voice: *every creature which is in heaven and on the earth and under the earth and such as are in the sea* — the same three realms that had nobody worthy in them, now all singing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Lamb has"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Having seven horns and seven eyes, which are the seven Spirits of God sent out into all the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Horns are power and eyes are sight, and both are at seven — the number of completeness."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the herald asked.** Not who is strong enough. *Who is worthy* — and the answer, when it came, was a Lamb that had been killed and was standing."}]}, "Revelation 5:11–12": {"title": "Every voice there is", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The circle widens, and John gives up trying to count."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Lamb took the scroll, and the four creatures and twenty-four elders went down in front of Him with harps and golden bowls of incense — *and the incense is the prayers of God's people*. See the note on Revelation 5:7–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They sang the new song: *You are worthy… for You were slain, and have redeemed us to God by Your blood.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then John looks again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The position"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They were standing **all the way around the throne and the creatures and the elders**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Concentric. The elders and creatures are already round the throne; the angels are round all of it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The redeemed are nearer than the angels in this scene."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The number"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There were **tens of thousands of tens of thousands** of them, and **thousands upon thousands** more.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **muriades muriadon kai chiliades chiliadon** — myriads of myriads and thousands of thousands."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Daniel has the same construction at the Ancient of Days: *a thousand thousands ministered to Him, and ten thousand times ten thousand stood before Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not arithmetic. It is the largest phrase the language has."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The volume"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And what they were saying, **at full volume**, was this.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *with a loud voice*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seven"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Lamb **deserves it all** — the Lamb **who was killed** — the **power**, the **wealth**, the **wisdom**, the **strength**, the **honor**, the **glory**, the **blessing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven items, and one article governing all of them in the Greek — they come as a single package rather than a list."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven is the number Revelation uses for completeness throughout: seals, trumpets, bowls, churches, spirits, lampstands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Lamb **who was killed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is the perfect participle — slain, and standing slain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is what John saw when he turned to look at the Lion: *a Lamb as though it had been slain*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The worthiness is attached to that and to nothing else, twice in the chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the elders sang and what the angels sing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The elders' song is *You have redeemed **us**… and have made **us** kings and priests*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angels' song has no *us* in it. They are not the redeemed, and they do not claim to be."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter says they want to look into it: *things which angels desire to look into*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **every creature which is in heaven and on the earth and under the earth and such as are in the sea, and all that are in them**, I heard saying:*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Blessing and honour and glory and power be to Him who sits on the throne, and to the Lamb, forever and ever!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The circle widens a third time, to everything that exists."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the four living creatures said *Amen*, and the elders fell down and worshipped."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The structure of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A sealed scroll and nobody worthy. John weeping. The Lion who is a Lamb. The scroll taken. The elders. The angels. Then all creation."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four expanding rings, and the object of every one is the same."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the count breaks down.** John reaches for myriads of myriads and thousands of thousands and stops. The seven things they name are given as one package — and the reason attached is a single perfect participle: slain."}]}, "Revelation 5:5–6": {"title": "The Lion, and the Lamb", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "John is told to look at a Lion and sees a Lamb, and the whole book turns on the substitution."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he was crying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verses before: a scroll sealed with seven seals, and *no one in heaven or on the earth or under the earth was able to open it*, and John *wept much*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not weeping over a book. Whatever the scroll is, its unopened state is what undoes him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The announcement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Stop crying. Look — the Lion out of Judah's tribe, the Root of David, has won.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two titles, and both are old promises."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The Lion of Judah** is Jacob's blessing in Genesis 49: *Judah is a young lion… who would dare rouse him?* See the note on Genesis 49:8–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The Root of David** is Isaiah 11: *there shall come forth a Rod from the stem of Jesse, and a Branch shall grow out of his roots.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Has won\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **enikesen** — He conquered, aorist tense. A completed victory, already achieved, reported as news."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What John actually sees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So I looked, and there in the middle of it all… **stood a Lamb**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is told Lion. He sees Lamb."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a correction — the same Person. But the conquering was done in the other form."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"He had been killed, and He was standing\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a lamb *as though slain* — bearing the marks of slaughter, and on its feet."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both facts held together in one clause: the wounds are visible and He is standing. It is the only description of the risen Christ in Scripture that keeps the killing in the picture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seven horns and seven eyes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Horns** are the Old Testament image for power, **eyes** for seeing and knowing, and **seven** is completeness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the eyes are the seven Spirits of God, sent out into every part of the earth.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the story started"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 3:15, in the garden, to the serpent: *He shall bruise your head, and you shall bruise His heel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A heel bruised and a head crushed — a wound taken in the winning. That is the Lamb standing with the marks on Him, and it is the first promise in the Bible arriving at its end. See the note on Genesis 3:14–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The woman's seed at the beginning, the Lion of Judah at the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They sing a new song: *You are worthy to take the scroll, for **You were slain**, and have redeemed us to God by Your blood out of every tribe and tongue and people and nation.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given for His worthiness is not the Lion. It is the slaying."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The scroll.** Nobody in the vision could open it, and John's grief was proportionate to that. Whatever it holds — the title deed of the earth, the unrolling of what is to come — the point of the chapter is that the seals came off in the hands of somebody who had been killed."}]}, "Revelation 5:7–8": {"title": "\"The incense is the prayers of God's people\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He takes the scroll, and the whole throne room goes down — carrying two things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A sealed scroll in the right hand of the One on the throne, and an angel asking who is worthy to open it. Nobody in heaven, on earth or under it could."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So I wept much, because no one was found worthy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then an elder says *do not weep — the **Lion** of the tribe of Judah has prevailed*, and John turns to look and sees *a **Lamb** as though it had been slain*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the sentence before this passage."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The taking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **walked up and took the scroll** out of the right hand of the One on the throne.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No permission is asked and none is recorded as given. The Lamb walks up to the throne and takes it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The response"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the moment He took it**, the four creatures and the twenty-four elders **went down in front of Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Immediately. The taking is what triggers the worship."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What each one carried"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every one of them had **a harp**, and **a gold bowl full of burning incense**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two objects, and both are temple furniture."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gold bowls are the **phiale** — the shallow libation dishes from the tabernacle service, listed among the vessels Nehemiah carried back into the storerooms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The identification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the incense is the prayers of God's people**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John says it flatly, and it is the only time in Scripture the equation is made outright."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the incense was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four spices in equal parts, salted, blended by a perfumer, and forbidden for private use. Three of the four give off nothing until they are crushed or burned. See the note on Exodus 30:34–38."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is what the bowls are full of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is holding them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the people who prayed. The four living creatures and the twenty-four elders — the nearest beings to the throne."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayers have been collected, kept, and are in gold when they arrive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What David asked for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let my prayer **stand before You like incense**, let my lifted hands be the evening offering.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asked for it as a request. See the note on Psalms 141:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where else the bowl appears"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three chapters on: *another angel, having a golden censer… **and the smoke of the incense, with the prayers of the saints, ascended before God** out of the angel's hand.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the angel fills the same censer with fire from the altar and throws it to the earth."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they sing next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You are worthy to take the scroll and to open its seals, for **You were slain, and have redeemed us to God by Your blood** out of every tribe and tongue and people and nation.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The worthiness is stated as a fact about the slaying."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is in the bowls.** The prayers themselves — not a report of them, not a record. John sees them in front of the throne, in gold, at the moment the scroll changes hands."}]}, "Revelation 5:9–10": {"title": "A song nobody had sung before", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The scroll is taken, and the room starts singing something new."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A sealed scroll in the right hand of the One on the throne, an angel asking who is worthy, and nobody found in heaven, on earth or under it. See the note on Revelation 5:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John weeps."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then an elder: *behold, the Lion of the tribe of Judah has prevailed*. And he looks and sees a Lamb, standing, with its throat cut."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The new song"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they sang **a song nobody had ever sung before**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **oden kainen** — a new song."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kainos** — new in kind, not merely new in time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase runs through the psalms: *sing to the LORD a new song*, six or seven times, always after something has happened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the previous repertoire covers this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The worthiness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are the One who **deserves to take the scroll and break the seals** on it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The angel's question is answered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You were killed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **esphages** — slaughtered, butchered. The sacrificial word."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the credential. Not power, not rank, not victory in the ordinary sense."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purchase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **with Your own blood You bought us back for God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **egorasas** — bought at market. The slave-market word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Out of **every family line, every language, every people, every nation**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four categories, and Revelation uses this formula seven times with the order shuffled each time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ek pases phules** — out of every tribe."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The preposition matters. Not *every tribe*, but *out of* every tribe. Some from each."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The status"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have made us **kings and priests belonging to our God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **basileian kai hiereis** — a kingdom and priests."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the sentence from Sinai: *you shall be to Me **a kingdom of priests** and a holy nation*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter uses it too: *you are a chosen generation, **a royal priesthood**, a holy nation*. See the note on 1 Peter 2:9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offer made to Israel at the mountain, applied here to people out of every nation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The location"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **it is on the earth that we will reign**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epi tes ges**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The song ends on the ground rather than in the sky."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is what the last two chapters describe: a city coming down, and *the tabernacle of God is with men, and He will dwell with them*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The numbers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ten thousand times ten thousand, and thousands of thousands*, and their song is shorter and simpler: *worthy is the Lamb who was slain to receive power and riches and wisdom and strength and honour and glory and blessing*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the outermost ring: *every creature which is in heaven and on the earth and under the earth and such as are in the sea, and all that are in them*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The three realms that had nobody worthy in them, in chapter 5 verse 3, are all singing by verse 13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the elders are holding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Each having a harp, and golden bowls full of incense, **which are the prayers of the saints**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayers are in the room when the scroll is opened."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The credential named in the song.** *You were killed.* The room searched three realms for somebody worthy, and the qualification turns out to be the slaughter."}]}, "Revelation 6:1–2": {"title": "The first seal", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The scroll is opened, and everything in the rest of the book follows from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who opens it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I watched **the Lamb** break one of the seals.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same Lamb who was found worthy in chapter 5, standing as though slain, when nobody in three realms could be found to take the scroll. See the note on Revelation 5:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing rides in chapter 6 that was not released in chapter 5."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who calls"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I heard **one of the four creatures** speak, and its voice came out **like thunder**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The four living creatures round the throne — lion, calf, man, eagle, covered in eyes. See the note on Revelation 4:5–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One creature per seal, for the first four."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The summons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Come and take a look.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **erchou** — come. Two words, and it is disputed whether it is addressed to John or to the rider."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rider"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There was **a white horse**. Its rider was **holding a bow**, and **a crown was handed to him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three details: the colour, the bow, and the crown."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Stephanos** — the victor's wreath, not **diadema**, the royal crown."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Was handed to him* — the Greek is **edothe**, it was given. That passive runs through the whole passage: given to him, given to the second rider, given to the fourth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing here takes anything. Everything is issued."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The riding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And out he rode, **already winning and riding out to win more**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **nikon kai hina nikese** — conquering and that he might conquer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who he is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Argued over for centuries, and the arguments are worth stating plainly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Christ**: chapter 19 has a rider on a white horse, crowned, called Faithful and True. The colour and the conquering match."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A counterfeit**: the crown is the wrong word, the weapon is a bow rather than a sword from the mouth, and He is the one opening the seal — which makes it odd for Him to ride out of it. And the company he rides in is war, famine and death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The text does not adjudicate, and the note does not either."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The bow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A bow with no arrows mentioned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Conquest at a distance, without close engagement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "White, red, black, pale. Four riders, and the last three are unambiguous: war, scarcity, and death. See the notes on Revelation 6:3–4, Revelation 6:5–6 and Revelation 6:7–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Jesus said in the same order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Take heed that no one **deceives** you. For many will come in My name, saying, 'I am the Christ,' and will deceive many.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And you will hear of **wars** and rumours of wars.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And there will be **famines**, pestilences, and earthquakes.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deception, war, famine, death — in that sequence, in the Olivet discourse. See the note on Matthew 24:4–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The correspondence is why the first rider is so often read as deception rather than as Christ."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He called it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"All these are the **beginning of sorrows**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **odinon** — birth pangs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the seals are attached to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A scroll that nobody could open, held in the right hand of the One on the throne, and opened by somebody who had been killed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole sequence is under that hand."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb that governs the passage.** *Was given.* The crown, the sword, the authority — all passives. Nothing in the chapter seizes anything."}]}, "Revelation 6:3–4": {"title": "Peace taken away", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second seal, and the description is unusually specific about mechanism."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The summons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I heard **the second creature** say, Come and take a look.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One creature per seal, and Revelation keeps the count."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The horse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And out came another horse, and **this one was red**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **purros** — fiery red, flame-coloured. From **pur**, fire."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same adjective is used of the dragon in chapter 12: *a great, fiery red dragon*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The permission"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Its rider **was given leave** to take peace away from the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **edothe auto** — it was given to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third passive in four verses. Nothing is seized. See the note on Revelation 6:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is removed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **take peace away from the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **labein ten eirenen** — to take the peace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peace is described as a thing that had been present and is withdrawn, rather than as a condition that simply fails."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **people would slaughter each other**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **allelous sphaxousin** — they should slay one another."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sphazo** is the sacrificial word. It is the verb used of the Lamb — *a Lamb as though it had been **slain***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb, one chapter apart. One was killed; these kill each other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weapon"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **a huge sword was put in his hand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **machaira megale** — a great sword."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Machaira** is the short sword, the soldier's or assassin's weapon rather than the long Thracian **rhomphaia** John uses elsewhere."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Close work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order in the discourse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"You will hear of **wars and rumours of wars**. See that you are not troubled; for all these things must come to pass, but the end is not yet.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"For nation will rise against nation, and kingdom against kingdom.\"* See the note on Matthew 24:4–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Second on the list there, and second here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Luke's version adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"When you hear of wars and commotions, **do not be terrified**; for these things must come to pass first, but the end will not come immediately.\"* See the note on Luke 21:7–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both Gospels attach the same instruction: do not be alarmed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one before and after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "White, then red, then black, then the corpse-coloured horse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deception, war, scarcity, death — and the third follows the second for the obvious reason. Armies eat the harvest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause the passage keeps"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was given.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever else the chapter is doing, it does not present the riders as autonomous. The seals are broken by the Lamb, the creatures issue the summons, and the authority is handed over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That God causes the slaughter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The removal of peace is what is permitted. What people do with the absence is described in the next clause, and the subject of that verb is *they*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verb for the killing.** *Sphazo* — the sacrificial word, used one chapter earlier of the Lamb. He was slain; these slay each other."}]}, "Revelation 6:5–6": {"title": "A day's pay for a loaf", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The third seal, and it comes with prices."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The horse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There was **a black horse**, and its rider had **a set of scales in his hand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **zugon** — a yoke, or a balance beam."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Scales meant rationing. Leviticus uses the image for famine: *when I have cut off your supply of bread, ten women shall bake your bread in one oven, and **they shall bring back your bread by weight**, and you shall eat and not be satisfied*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bread sold by weight rather than by the loaf is a society in trouble."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The voice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And I heard a voice, **out from somewhere among the four creatures**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The voice comes from the middle of the throne area, not from the rider."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first price"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **day's pay for one measure of wheat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **choinix sitou denariou** — a quart of wheat for a denarius."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A denarius was a labourer's daily wage — the figure in the parable of the vineyard workers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A **choinix** was roughly a day's food for one man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So: a full day's work buys one man's food for one day, and nothing else. No margin for a family."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **day's pay for three measures of barley**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Barley was the cheaper grain, the poor man's bread. Three measures for the same money."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A man could feed three people on barley for a day's wage, or himself on wheat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Those are the terms. Not starvation — inflation of roughly eight to twelve times normal prices, which is what famine looked like in an ancient economy."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The restriction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the oil and the wine — leave those alone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me adikeses** — do not harm, do not injure."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An explicit limit placed on the rider mid-sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Oil and wine are the other two staples of that agriculture — *grain, new wine and oil* is the standard triad. Olive and vine are deep-rooted perennials that survive a drought which kills a grain crop."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whether the clause means the luxuries are untouched while bread fails, or that the damage has a boundary, is argued. The grammar is a prohibition either way."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every rider in the chapter operates within a limit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first is given a crown. The second is given leave and a sword. This one is told what not to touch. The fourth is given *a quarter of the earth*. See the note on Revelation 6:7–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the sequence is unbounded."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits in the discourse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And there will be **famines**, pestilences, and earthquakes in various places.\"* See the note on Matthew 24:4–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Third in the list there, and third here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why famine follows war"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second rider took peace. Armies live off the land they cross, and a harvest lost in spring is a famine by autumn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sequence is not arbitrary."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the psalm says about the other side"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have been young, and now am old; yet I have not seen the righteous forsaken, nor his descendants begging bread.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the manna, which came six days a week for forty years, and never priced. See the note on Exodus 16:22–26."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clause in the middle.** *Do not harm the oil and the wine.* The rider is given scales and a mandate, and then told what he may not touch."}]}, "Revelation 6:7–8": {"title": "The horse the colour of a corpse", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The fourth seal, and it is the only rider who is given a name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The colour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There was a horse **the color of a corpse**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **chloros** — pale green, yellowish-green."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the ordinary word for vegetation. Mark uses it of the grass the five thousand sat on: *to sit down in groups on the **green** grass*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same adjective for spring grass and for a dead face. Translators choose between *pale* and *green*, and the Greek carries both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rider"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The rider on it was **named Death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **Thanatos**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first three riders are described. This one is named, and the name is the thing itself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The follower"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the Grave came along behind him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **Hades** — the realm of the dead, and the standard Septuagint rendering of the Hebrew **Sheol**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two figures for one rider: the killing, and where the killed go."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul puts the pair together too: *O Death, where is your sting? O Hades, where is your victory?* See the note on 1 Corinthians 15:53–55."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Revelation ends with both of them dealt with: *then Death and Hades were cast into the lake of fire*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The limit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The two of them were **handed a quarter of the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **edothe autois exousia epi to tetarton** — authority was given to them over the fourth part."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fourth passive in eight verses, and the most explicit boundary in the chapter. A fraction is named."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And turned loose to kill in it — with **the sword**, with **hunger**, with **plague**, and with **the wild animals of the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **rhomphaia** for the sword — the long Thracian blade, not the **machaira** of the second rider."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Limos** — famine. **Thanatos** again, here meaning pestilence, which is how the Septuagint uses it. And **therion** — wild beasts."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the four come from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel, four times over: *when I send **My four severe judgments** on Jerusalem — the sword and famine and wild beasts and pestilence — to cut off man and beast from it*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list is exact and the order is close. John is quoting a prophet's standing formula."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Leviticus 26 has the same set as the covenant curses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the wild animals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It sounds archaic and it is not. When population collapses, cultivation stops and predators move back in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "2 Kings records it happening after the Assyrian deportation: *the LORD sent lions among them, which killed some of them*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the four riders add up to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Conquest, war, scarcity, death — and the last summarises the first three. The sword is the second rider, the hunger is the third, and the plague and the animals are what follows both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fifth seal, and it is not a rider."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I saw under the altar **the souls of those who had been slain for the word of God**… and they cried with a loud voice, \"**How long, O Lord, holy and true, until You judge and avenge our blood?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they are given white robes and told to rest a little while longer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the sequence began"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Lamb, opening the seals. See the note on Revelation 6:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in the chapter proceeds from a hand that had been pierced."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The only rider with a name.** Death, with the Grave behind him — and the one thing the text specifies about their authority is the fraction of it."}]}, "Revelation 7:1–3": {"title": "Hold the winds", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Judgment is stopped mid-air until a mark is applied."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between the sixth seal and the seventh."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sixth has just been opened — the sun black as sackcloth, the moon like blood, and men hiding in the caves saying *hide us from the face of Him who sits on the throne*, and the question that ends the chapter: *who is able to stand?* See the note on Revelation 6:12–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 7 is the answer, and it arrives as an interruption."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The four angels"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I saw **four angels posted at the four corners of the earth**. They had **the four winds gripped tight** so that **not a breath of wind could move** — not over the land, not over the sea, not through a single tree.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four corners, four winds, four angels."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kratountas** — holding fast, restraining by force. It takes effort."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stillness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not a breath of wind* — and John lists three places it is not blowing: land, sea, and a single tree."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The detail about the tree is the one that makes it uncanny. Not even one leaf moving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fifth angel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And I saw one more angel **coming up out of the sunrise**, carrying **the seal of the living God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apo anatoles heliou** — from the rising of the sun. The east."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The direction the glory comes from in Ezekiel: *the glory of the God of Israel came from the way of the east*. And the direction Jesus names: *as the lightning comes from the east, so also will the coming of the Son of Man be*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he **shouted to the four angels who had been given the power to wreck the land and the sea**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They have the power. They are told to hold it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do nothing** to the land, he said, or to the sea, or to the trees.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Not until we have put the seal on the foreheads of the servants of our God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sphragisomen** — until we seal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A seal was pressed into wax or clay to show ownership and to guarantee that what was sealed had not been tampered with."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being said about the timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The winds are not held because judgment has been cancelled. They are held because the sealing is not finished."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The order in the chapter is: restrain, seal, then release."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the image comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ezekiel 9: a man with a writer's inkhorn told to *go through the midst of the city, and put a mark on the foreheads of the men who sigh and cry over all the abominations*, and then the destroyers follow — *but do not come near anyone on whom is the mark*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Passover, where the blood on the doorposts is what the destroyer passes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the seal is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And I looked, and behold, a Lamb standing on Mount Zion, and with Him one hundred and forty-four thousand, **having His Father's name written on their foreheads**.* See the note on Revelation 14:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seal is a name, and the name is the Father's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other mark"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The beast's, seven chapters later: *he causes all… to receive a mark on their right hand or on their foreheads*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two marks, both on foreheads, and Revelation makes the sealing the earlier of the two."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul calls it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In whom also, having believed, **you were sealed with the Holy Spirit of promise**, who is the guarantee of our inheritance.* See the note on Ephesians 1:13–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is standing at the end of the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A great multitude which no one could number, of all nations, tribes, peoples, and tongues, standing before the throne.* See the note on Revelation 7:9–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that begins with judgment held ends with a crowd nobody can count."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What stops the winds.** Not mercy replacing judgment — an unfinished sealing. The four angels already hold the power to wreck; they are told to wait until the foreheads are marked."}]}, "Romans 1:16–17": {"title": "The power of God for everyone", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The thesis of the whole letter, in two sentences, before Paul has argued anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Not ashamed\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am **not ashamed** of the good news of Christ — not for a moment.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is writing to the capital of the empire, about a Jewish carpenter executed by Rome on a slave's cross. The message had every social reason to be embarrassing, and he opens by naming the embarrassment and dismissing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is **the power of God** to rescue everyone who believes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dunamis**. Not that the gospel describes God's power, or points to it, or asks for it. It **is** it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the same in 1 Corinthians: *the message of the cross is… the power of God.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"To rescue\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **soteria** — the standard word for salvation, and wider in that language than the English suggests."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same root is used in the Gospels of a bleeding woman made well, a blind man healed, Peter pulled out of the water, and a girl raised from the dead. Rescue, healing, wholeness, preservation, safety."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is one word covering all of it, and Paul says the gospel is God's power toward all of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone who believes — **the Jew first, and also the Greek**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only qualification in the sentence is believing. Not merit, not nationality, not standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being revealed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In it **the righteousness of God is being revealed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not demanded. Revealed — uncovered, disclosed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the hinge of the letter. The law revealed a righteousness God requires; the gospel reveals one God supplies. Paul returns to it in chapter 3: *the righteousness of God apart from the law is revealed… through faith in Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"From faith to faith\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A right standing that comes **from faith and leads on to faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *ek pisteos eis pistin* — out of faith, into faith. It starts there and it goes on there. Nothing switches over to another basis later."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The one who is right with God will live by faith.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Habakkuk 2:4 — five words in Hebrew, quoted three times in the New Testament (here, Galatians 3, Hebrews 10), and the sentence that set Luther off."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It can be read *the righteous shall live by faith* or *the one who is righteous by faith shall live*. Paul's argument holds either way, and the letter goes on to make both true."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**When Habakkuk said it.** Standing on a watchtower, having complained twice that God was doing nothing about violence and injustice, and being told the answer was coming but not yet. *Though it tarries, wait for it.* The verse the Reformation turned on was written by a man who could not see what God was doing."}]}, "Romans 1:18–23": {"title": "They would not thank Him", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's account of how a civilisation ends up worshipping animals, and it starts with a failure to say thank you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For I am not ashamed of the gospel of Christ, for **it is the power of God to salvation**… For in it **the righteousness of God is revealed from faith to faith**.* See the note on Romans 1:16–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then, immediately: *for the wrath of God is revealed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two revelations, in consecutive sentences, using the same verb."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wrath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From heaven **the wrath of God is being revealed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apokaluptetai** — present tense, continuous."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not stored up for later. Being disclosed now."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Against what"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Every kind of **godlessness and wrongdoing** of people who **hold the truth down and keep it from being heard**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katechonton** — holding down, suppressing, restraining."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something is pressing upward and being held under."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is available"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever can be known of God **lies open before them**, because **God Himself has made it plain to them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ho Theos gar autois ephanerosen** — God manifested it to them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The disclosure is His act."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Through what"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ever since the world was made, His **invisible qualities** — His **eternal power** and **His divine nature** — have been **clearly seen, understood through the things He has made**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ta aorata… kathoratai** — the invisible things are clearly seen. A deliberate paradox."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nooumena** — being understood by the mind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **no one has any excuse**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anapologetous** — without a defence, without an apology in the legal sense."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word Paul uses at the start of chapter 2, of the person who judges others."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two failures"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They knew God — and still they would not **give Him the honor that is His**, or **so much as thank Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ouch hos Theon edoxasan e eucharistesan** — they did not glorify Him as God, nor give thanks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the sequence is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not atheism. Knowledge, followed by a refusal to honour and a refusal to thank."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul names ingratitude as the second step in the sequence that ends in idolatry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Their thinking **drifted into emptiness**, and their **undiscerning hearts went dark**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **emataiothesan** — were made futile, empty. And **eskotisthe** — was darkened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both passive. It happened to them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **advertised themselves as wise, and turned into fools**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phaskontes einai sophoi emoranthesan** — claiming to be wise, they were made foolish."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Moraino** — the root of *moron*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The exchange"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they **traded the glory of the immortal God** for **images shaped like mortal man, and like birds and animals and creatures that crawl on the ground**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ellaxan** — exchanged."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The list descends: man, birds, quadrupeds, reptiles."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three handings-over"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore **God also gave them up** to uncleanness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For this reason **God gave them up** to vile passions.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And even as they did not like to retain God in their knowledge, **God gave them over** to a debased mind.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paredoken** — three times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The judgment described is being allowed to have what was chosen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the argument is going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore you are inexcusable, O man, whoever you are who judge.* See the note on Romans 2:6–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 1 closes the case against the pagan world, and chapter 2 turns on the reader who has been agreeing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **all have sinned and fall short of the glory of God**, being justified freely by His grace.* See the note on Romans 3:23–24."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The second step.** Not disbelief — *they would not give Him the honour, or so much as thank Him*. Paul puts ingratitude in the sequence that ends in a statue of a reptile."}]}, "Romans 10:1–4": {"title": "A zeal that runs ahead of knowledge", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul says what he wants, and it is not an argument won."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The desire"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **desire of my heart** and **my prayer to God** for Israel is **that they may be saved**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *desire* is **eudokia** — good pleasure, the thing he would choose."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just written three chapters about Israel's unbelief, and he opens the fourth by saying what he prays."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had said a chapter earlier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have great sorrow and continual grief in my heart. For **I could wish that I myself were accursed from Christ for my brethren**, my countrymen according to the flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not arguing against people he dislikes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The credit he gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I can **testify about them that they have a zeal for God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **zelos** — zeal, fervour. Paul is not accusing them of indifference, and he knew the zeal from the inside: *concerning zeal, persecuting the church.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The qualification"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But it is **a zeal that runs ahead of knowledge**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *not according to **epignosis*** — full or accurate knowledge."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The energy is real and the information is missing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis, in three clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not **recognizing the right standing that comes from God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And busy **establishing one of their own**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They **did not submit themselves** to the righteousness of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *establishing* is **stesai** — to set up, to make stand. It is construction work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word \"submit\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hupetagesan** — to place oneself under, to be subordinated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Receiving a righteousness that is not yours is described as a submission. Building one of your own is described as the alternative."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the three clauses is about immorality."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **Christ is the end of the law**, so that there is **a right standing with God for everyone who believes**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **telos** — the end, and also the goal, the finish line, the point a thing was aiming at."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both senses are live. Where the road stops, and what it was going toward."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word \"everyone\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul repeats it through the chapter: *everyone who believes*, *whoever calls on the name of the LORD shall be saved*, and *there is no distinction between Jew and Greek, for the same Lord over all is rich to all who call upon Him.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage about the word being near: *the word is near you, in your mouth and in your heart… **if you confess with your mouth the Lord Jesus and believe in your heart** that God has raised Him from the dead, you will be saved.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which he quotes out of Deuteronomy 30 — the law testifying to a righteousness apart from it. See the note on Romans 3:21–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The questions at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How then shall they call on Him in whom they have not believed? And how shall they believe in Him of whom they have not heard? And how shall they hear without a preacher?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter that opens with a prayer ends by asking who is going to tell them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is wrong in the passage.** Not the zeal, and not the sincerity. The clause Paul keeps circling is *establishing one of their own* — and he calls receiving the other one a submission."}]}, "Romans 10:14–15": {"title": "The chain of questions", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four questions, each one taking a step back from the last."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How are they to **call** on the One they have not **believed** in?* *How are they to **believe** in the One they have never **heard** of?* *How are they to **hear** without someone **preaching**?* *How is anyone to **preach** unless they are **sent**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Call ← believe ← hear ← preach ← sent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is working backwards from the promise he has just quoted — *whoever calls on the name of the LORD shall be saved* — to find out what has to happen first."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chain ends at a sending, and the sending is God's."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he is arguing this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter is about Israel: *my heart's desire and prayer to God for Israel is that they may be saved.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is establishing that the message went out and was heard, so that the failure to believe was not for want of hearing. Three verses later: *have they not heard? Yes indeed — their sound has gone out to all the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He then quotes Psalm 19, about the sky. See the note on Psalms 19:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**How beautiful are the feet** of those who carry the good news of peace, who carry the good news of good things!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 52:7, and the setting there is a runner coming over the hills toward a besieged city with news that the siege is lifted."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Feet* is the odd part of the compliment. It is not what anybody normally praises."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But if you are waiting for news, the feet are what you see first — coming over the ridge, before you can make out the face or hear the shout."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse before it in Isaiah"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Your God reigns!\"* — that is the message the beautiful feet are carrying."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the verses after are the beginning of Isaiah 53. The runner with the good news is the paragraph immediately preceding *He was wounded for our transgressions.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Romans 10 says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So then **faith comes by hearing**, and hearing by the word of God.* See the note on Romans 10:16–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **rhema** — the spoken word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what it said just before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The word is near you, in your mouth and in your heart… **if you confess with your mouth** the Lord Jesus and **believe in your heart**… you will be saved.* See the note on Romans 10:5–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole chapter runs on speech — mouth, heart, hearing, preaching, and a sound going out to the ends of the world."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who Paul is quoting the chain against.** Himself, twenty years earlier. He was the man with the letters to Damascus, and what stopped him was a voice."}]}, "Romans 10:16–17": {"title": "\"Faith comes by hearing\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just run a chain of questions — how can they call on someone they have not believed in, believe in someone they have not heard, hear without a preacher — and this is where it lands."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So faith comes by hearing, and hearing comes through the word of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two steps, and the direction is fixed. Faith is not summoned up from inside a person; it arrives by a route, and the route starts with something said."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in this chain generates their own believing. Something is spoken, it is heard, and faith follows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for \"word\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses **rhema** — a word spoken, an utterance, rather than *logos*. It is the living, said thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why the chain has a preacher in it at all. The gospel in this chapter is something that travels by voice: *how beautiful are the feet of those who bring good news.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The present tense"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both verbs are present. Faith *comes*, hearing *comes* — ongoing, repeatable, not a single event in the past."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means the mechanism does not stop working after conversion. Faith arrives the same way at every stage: something is heard."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse before it.** *But not all of them welcomed the good news.* Paul is honest that the same word is heard and refused. Hearing in the physical sense is not the whole of it — Isaiah's complaint, which Paul quotes, is that the message was announced and not believed. The route is reliable; the response is still a response."}]}, "Romans 10:5–8": {"title": "\"The word is near you\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul lets the two righteousnesses speak in their own voices, and they sound nothing alike."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first one"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Moses describes the righteousness that comes from the law like this: \"**The person who does these things will live by them**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus 18. One verb, and everything hangs on it: **does**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a harsh statement. It is simply the arrangement, stated plainly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second one speaks differently"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But the righteousness that comes from faith **speaks a different way**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul personifies it. Faith-righteousness has a voice, and the first thing out of its mouth is a prohibition on two questions."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two forbidden questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not say in your heart, 'Who will go up into heaven?'**\" — that is, to bring Christ down — \"**or, 'Who will go down into the deep?'**\" — that is, to bring Christ up from among the dead.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are attempts to fetch. One reaches up, one reaches down, and both assume the thing needed is somewhere else and has to be retrieved."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul rules out both, and gives the reason in each case as a completed event. Christ has already come down. He has already come up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There is nothing to go and get."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it says instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The word is near you, in your mouth and in your heart.**\" That is the word of faith that we preach.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is quoting Deuteronomy 30, where Moses says the commandment *is not too mysterious for you, nor is it far off. It is not in heaven… nor is it beyond the sea… but the word is very near you, in your mouth and in your heart.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two locations. The mouth first, then the heart — and Paul keeps Moses' order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does with it next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you **confess with your mouth** the Lord Jesus and **believe in your heart** that God has raised Him from the dead, you will be saved. For with the heart one believes unto righteousness, and **with the mouth confession is made unto salvation**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He states it twice, mouth and heart both times."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *confess* is **homologeo** — to say the same thing, to agree out loud. It is the verb behind *the High Priest of our confession* in Hebrews 3. See the note on Hebrews 3:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What \"saved\" covers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sozo** and **soteria** are wider than the English. The same words are used in the Gospels of a woman made well, a blind man healed, a sinking man pulled out of water, and a girl raised."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rescue, healing, wholeness, preservation. It is one word doing all of that work."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chapter goes.** *For whoever calls on the name of the LORD shall be saved* — Joel — and then Paul's chain of questions: how shall they call on Him in whom they have not believed, and how shall they believe in Him of whom they have not heard, and how shall they hear without a preacher? The whole passage is about speech, from beginning to end."}]}, "Romans 10:9–13": {"title": "The mouth and the heart", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two organs, named twice, in two different orders — and Paul does that on purpose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order changes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If your **mouth** confesses the Lord Jesus, and your **heart** believes that God raised Him from the dead, you will be saved.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then immediately:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it is with the **heart** that a person believes… and it is with the **mouth** that confession is made.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "First mouth then heart; then heart then mouth. He is not being careless. He is tying them together so tightly that neither can be lifted out alone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Why the first order at all? Because he is quoting Deuteronomy 30 two verses earlier — *the word is near you, in your mouth and in your heart* — and he keeps the prophet's sequence before explaining it in his own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is confessed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Lord Jesus.* In a Roman province where *Caesar is Lord* was the civic formula, saying it about a crucified Galilean was not a private sentiment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And what is believed is specific: *that God raised Him from the dead.* Not a general trust in God. One historical claim."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is open to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is no difference here between Jew and Greek, for the same Lord is Lord of all, and He is generous to all who call on Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for *generous* is literally *rich toward* — the wealth language Paul uses everywhere. And then the quotation that closes it: *everyone who calls on the name of the Lord will be saved.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everyone. It is from Joel, and Peter used the same line at Pentecost."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is not in the list.** No requirement to clean up first, no probation, no ceremony. A confession and a belief, from anyone, of any nation, calling on a name. Paul spends the next verses insisting that someone therefore has to go and tell them."}]}, "Romans 11:1–4": {"title": "\"He pleads with God against Israel\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks the hardest question of the letter and answers it with himself, then with a prophet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Has God **pushed His people away**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **aposato** — thrust away, repudiated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three chapters of argument about Israel's unbelief have led here, and Paul puts the obvious conclusion as a question."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Certainly not.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me genoito** — Paul's strongest negative, which he uses ten times in this letter. *May it never be.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first evidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am an Israelite myself, from the seed of Abraham, **from the tribe of Benjamin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is the exhibit. If God had cast off Israel, Paul would not be writing the letter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Benjamin** — the smallest tribe, and the one Saul the king came from. Paul had been named after him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ground"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whom **He knew beforehand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **proegno** — foreknew. Paul has used it in chapter 8 of individuals; here it is of a nation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second witness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do you not know what the Scripture says **in the passage about Elijah**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *in Elijah* — the rabbinic way of citing a section by its subject, the way one might say *in the bush* for Exodus 3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The complaint"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How **he pleads with God against Israel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **entunchanei kata** — intercedes **against**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word for intercession, pointed the wrong way. That is Paul's own description of what Elijah is doing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elijah said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Lord, **they have killed Your prophets and torn down Your altars, and I am the only one left**, and they are after my life.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He said it twice at Horeb, word for word, before and after the wind, the earthquake, the fire and the whisper. See the note on 1 Kings 19:9–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every fact in it is true except the last."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **what was God's answer to him?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **chrematismos** — a divine response, an oracle. It occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks what the answer was, which means he expects the reader to have noticed that one was given."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seven thousand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have kept for Myself seven thousand men** who have not bowed the knee to Baal.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *I have left for Myself* — the reserving is God's act, stated in the first person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "They had been there the whole time, through the drought, through Jezebel's purge, while Elijah was on Carmel and while he was under the broom tree."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Obadiah's hundred prophets in two caves were among them. See the note on 1 Kings 18:1–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does with it next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the same way, at this present time also, there is **a remnant chosen by grace**.* See the note on Romans 11:5–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He takes the number and turns it into a doctrine."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul notices about the passage.** Not that Elijah was wrong about the facts. That the direction of his intercession was *against* — and that God answered by naming seven thousand men Elijah had never met."}]}, "Romans 11:13–16": {"title": "The first handful, and the root", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two images in one verse, and both come from the same place in the law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first handful of dough"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If the first handful of dough is holy, so is the whole lump.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Numbers 15 required the first of the ground meal to be offered as a contribution. Once that portion was given, the rest of the batch was reckoned holy — set apart, usable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's logic runs from the part to the whole: what is true of the first is true of what follows it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The root and the branches"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If the root is holy, so are the branches.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same argument, different picture — and this one he will spend the next ten verses on: an olive tree, natural branches broken off, wild branches grafted in, and a warning to the wild ones not to be arrogant about it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both images are making his point about Israel: the patriarchs are the root, and what happened at the root is not undone by what happened to some branches."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the passage is actually about"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The subject is not personal blessing. It is whether God has finished with Israel, and Paul's answer is a flat no — *their being received back* would be *life from the dead*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He also says something disarming about his own motives: he makes much of his work among Gentiles partly hoping to provoke his own people to jealousy and save some. An apostle admitting to an ulterior motive is not something the New Testament does often."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On lifting the first line out.** *If the first handful is holy, so is the whole lump* is sometimes applied to giving — set apart the first portion of income and the rest is blessed. The image comes from an offering, so the association is understandable, but Paul's own use of it here is about Israel and the patriarchs, and that is what the sentence is arguing."}]}, "Romans 11:25–27": {"title": "Until the fullness", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The conclusion of three chapters Paul has been building since chapter 9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why he says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In case you should **think too highly of yourselves**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *lest you should be wise in your own opinion*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is writing to Gentile believers in Rome who had started to look down on the Jews who had not believed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has already told them off for it: *do not boast against the branches. But if you do boast, remember that you do not support the root, but the root supports you.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The image behind it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An olive tree. Some branches broken off, and wild branches grafted in *contrary to nature*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not be haughty, but fear. For if God did not spare the natural branches, He may not spare you either.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mystery"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I do not want you to be uninformed about **this mystery**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **musterion** — something previously hidden, now disclosed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **hardening has come over part of Israel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **porosis apo merous** — hardening in part."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two limits in one clause: it is partial, and it is temporary."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has already made the first point at length: *God has not cast away His people whom He foreknew… even so then, at this present time there is a remnant according to the election of grace.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And he offers himself as the evidence: *for I also am an Israelite, of the seed of Abraham, of the tribe of Benjamin*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clock"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it **lasts until the fullness of the Gentiles has come in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **achris hou** — until."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Pleroma** — fullness, the full number."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And in this way **all Israel will be saved**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kai houtos** — and thus, and in this manner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *and then*. The manner, not just the sequence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The Deliverer will come out of Zion**, and He will **turn ungodliness away from Jacob**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 59, the chapter immediately before *arise, shine, for your light has come*. See the note on Isaiah 60:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **this is My covenant with them, when I take away their sins**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah 59:21 and Jeremiah 31, combined. See the note on Isaiah 59:21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Concerning the gospel they are enemies for your sake, but concerning the election they are beloved for the sake of the fathers. **For the gifts and the calling of God are irrevocable**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ametameleta** — not to be repented of, not taken back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The summary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For as you were once disobedient to God, yet have now obtained mercy through their disobedience, even so these also have now been disobedient, that through the mercy shown you they also may obtain mercy. **For God has committed them all to disobedience, that He might have mercy on all**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How the argument ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not with a conclusion. With a doxology:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Oh, the depth of the riches both of the wisdom and knowledge of God! **How unsearchable are His judgments and His ways past finding out!** For who has known the mind of the LORD? Or who has become His counsellor? Or who has first given to Him and it shall be repaid to him? For of Him and through Him and to Him are all things, to whom be glory forever. Amen.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three chapters of the hardest material in Paul, and he finishes by saying he cannot see the bottom of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he opened with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I tell the truth in Christ, I am not lying… **I have great sorrow and continual grief in my heart**. For I could wish that I myself were accursed from Christ for my brethren, my countrymen according to the flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The doctrine and the grief are in the same three chapters."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two limits in one clause.** Partial, and *until*. Paul states both before he says anything about the outcome — and the warning attached is against Gentile self-congratulation."}]}, "Romans 11:5–6": {"title": "\"Otherwise grace would not be grace at all\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes Elijah's seven thousand and turns them into a definition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parallel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the same way, **at this present time also**, there is a remnant.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elijah's seven thousand were a live example, not an illustration. Paul says the same thing is running now. See the note on Romans 11:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **remnant**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **leimma** — what is left over. The Hebrew equivalent runs through Isaiah: *a remnant shall return*, which Isaiah made his son's name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's own point is that a remnant is not a failure. It is what survives, and it is always there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who chose it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Chosen **by grace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *according to the election of grace*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a remnant who chose better. A remnant selected, and the selecting agent is named."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The either/or"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **if it is by grace, then it is no longer on the basis of works**; otherwise **grace would not be grace at all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **if it is on the basis of works, then it is no longer grace**; otherwise **work would not be work**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul states it in both directions, symmetrically, in two clauses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is arguing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that works are bad. That the two words mean incompatible things."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Charis** is a gift; **ergon** is a wage. A gift that has to be earned is a wage under another name, and a wage that is given free is not a wage."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Mixing them destroys both words, and Paul says so about each in turn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He puts it the same way in Romans 4: *now to him who works, the wages are not counted as grace but as debt.* See the note on Romans 4:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Elijah did not know"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He said *I am the only one left*, twice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven thousand had not bowed. He had no way of knowing and had not been told until he asked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's point is that the reserving was going on invisibly, and that this is what grace does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The olive tree — branches broken off, a wild branch grafted in, and the warning: *do not boast against the branches… **you do not support the root, but the root supports you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *for God has committed them all to disobedience, that **He might have mercy on all**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "How it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Oh, the depth of the riches both of the wisdom and knowledge of God! How unsearchable are His judgments and His ways past finding out!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For of Him and through Him and to Him are all things, to whom be glory forever. Amen.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three chapters on the hardest question in the letter, and it ends in a doxology rather than a conclusion."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The test in the sentence.** Whether the word still means what it means. Paul does not argue that grace is better — he argues that if anything is added, there is no longer anything to call grace."}]}, "Romans 12:1–2": {"title": "By the mercies of God", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The turn in the letter, and it is one word wide."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The therefore"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, brothers and sisters.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven chapters come first."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sin, judgment, justification by faith, Abraham, peace with God, union with Christ, the law, the Spirit, no condemnation, the eternal purpose, Israel, and the doxology at the end of chapter 11: *oh, the depth of the riches both of the wisdom and knowledge of God!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the first imperative of the letter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul never puts the instruction first. The proportion here is eleven chapters to four."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ground"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By **the mercies of God** I appeal to you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dia ton oiktirmon tou theou** — through the compassions of God, plural."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *for the sake of*. *By* — he is appealing on the strength of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the leverage he chooses, having spent eleven chapters describing them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bring your bodies to Him as **a living sacrifice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **parastesai** — to present, place at somebody's disposal. The same verb he used of the members in Romans 6. See the note on Romans 6:12–14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Bodies** — not hearts, not souls. The part that goes places and does things."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oxymoron"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **living** sacrifice.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every sacrifice in Leviticus was killed. That is what made it one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul puts the two words together deliberately, and the difficulty in the phrase is the point: a sacrifice that keeps getting up off the altar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The description"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Set apart for Him and **pleasing in His sight**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hagian** and **euareston** — holy, and well-pleasing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sacrificial vocabulary continues. Leviticus called the smoke *a sweet aroma to the LORD*. See the note on Leviticus 1:3–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is worship offered with your **whole reasoning self**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **logiken latreian** — and it is genuinely hard to translate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Logikos** — rational, reasonable, or *of the word*. **Latreia** — priestly service."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The older versions have *reasonable service*; others have *spiritual worship*. Both are defensible."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever else it means, it locates worship in the ordinary use of a mind and a body rather than in a building."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mould"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not let the present age **press you into its own mold**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **suschematizesthe** — from **schema**, the outward shape something is pressed into."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter uses the same verb: *do not let your old cravings press you back into the shape you had*. See the note on 1 Peter 1:14–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The alternative"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be **transformed** instead.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **metamorphousthe** — the word behind *metamorphosis*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the verb used of the transfiguration, and of the beholding in 2 Corinthians: *we all… beholding as in a mirror the glory of the Lord, **are being transformed** into the same image*. See the note on 2 Corinthians 3:16–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are present passives. Stop being pressed; go on being transformed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From the inside, by **the making new of your mind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anakainosei tou noos** — the renewing of the mind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mould works from outside. The transformation is named as starting somewhere else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The outcome"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that you can **discern what the will of God is** — what is **good, and pleasing to Him, and whole**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dokimazein** — to test and approve, the word for assaying metal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not obtain it. Recognise it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The will of God is described here as something a renewed mind becomes able to identify, and three adjectives are attached: good, acceptable, and **teleion** — complete, finished, entire."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he appeals by.** Not duty and not fear. *By the mercies of God* — which he has spent eleven chapters establishing before asking for anything at all."}]}, "Romans 12:17–21": {"title": "As far as it rests with you", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Five verses of instruction about people who have wronged you, and one realistic clause in the middle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not **pay anyone back evil for evil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apodidontes** — repaying, giving back what is owed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul treats revenge as a debt being settled. The point of the passage is who is entitled to collect it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The visible part"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Give thought to **what is honorable in the sight of everyone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pronooumenoi** — thinking ahead, taking forethought."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not just doing right, but doing it where it can be seen. Paul says the same about the collection: *providing honourable things, not only in the sight of the Lord but also in the sight of men*. See the note on 2 Corinthians 8:16–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two qualifiers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If it can be done*, and *as far as it rests with you*, **be at peace with everyone**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ei dunaton, to ex humon** — if possible, the part that is from you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul writes the command and then fences it twice. Peace is not always achievable, and the other person's half is not the reader's responsibility."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction is to have nothing left owing on your side."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The prohibition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Beloved, **never take revenge into your own hands**, but **step back and make room for the wrath of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dote topon** — give place, leave room."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The picture is of stepping aside so someone else can get through."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say the wrong will go unanswered. He says it is not yours to answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Vengeance is Mine; I will repay**, says the Lord.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy 32, from the Song of Moses."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same verb as verse 17 — repay, **antapodoso**. The debt Paul told them not to settle is one God says He will settle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So then, **if your enemy is hungry, feed him; if he is thirsty, give him something to drink**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Proverbs 25, quoted almost exactly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not neutrality. Food and water, handed over by you, to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Elisha does it literally: he catches a Syrian army blind, and instead of killing them, *he prepared a great feast for them; and after they ate and drank, he sent them away*. And *the bands of Syria came no more into the land of Israel*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The coals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In doing this you will **heap burning coals on his head**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most argued-over line in the chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oldest reading takes it from an Egyptian rite of repentance, where a man carried a pan of coals on his head to show he was sorry. Another reads it as the neighbourly act of carrying live embers to relight a fire that had gone out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Either way, Proverbs adds a clause Paul leaves off: *and the LORD will reward you*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The summary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do not let evil defeat you; **defeat evil with good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **nika en to agatho to kakon** — conquer the evil in the good."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both halves use the same verb. The choice is not between fighting and not fighting; it is about which side wins the exchange."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 13: *let every soul be subject to the governing authorities… for he is God's minister, an avenger to execute wrath on him who practises evil.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul removes vengeance from the individual in one chapter and assigns it to the magistrate in the next. Nothing here says wrong goes unpunished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter opened with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I beseech you therefore, brethren, **by the mercies of God**, that you present your bodies a living sacrifice.* See the note on Romans 12:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eleven chapters of doctrine come before the first instruction, and the instruction is introduced by the mercies."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clause that makes it liveable.** *If it can be done, and as far as it rests with you.* Paul asks for peace and then admits, in writing, that half of it is out of your hands."}]}, "Romans 13:1–5": {"title": "God's servant, carrying a sword", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Written to Christians in Rome, about Rome."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being addressed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let **everyone** take their place under the authorities set above them.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pasa psuche** — every soul. A Hebraism meaning every person, no exceptions carved out for the religious."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **there is no authority that does not come from God**, and the authorities that exist **have been put in place by Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tetagmenai** — perfect passive of **tasso**, to arrange, to appoint to a station. A military word for putting a man in his position."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When this was written"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Around AD 57, under Nero, in the early years before the persecution."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Within a decade the same emperor would be using Christians as garden lights, and Paul himself would be executed under Roman authority."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul knew what Rome was. He had been beaten with rods three times and had already appealed to Caesar."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is being said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That the institution of government is God's arrangement, not that any given ruler is good."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The passage says nothing about how rulers come to power and nothing about their character. It speaks about the office."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The consequence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So whoever sets himself against the authority is **setting himself against what God has put in place**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **antitassomenos** — the same root as *appointed*, with *against* in front. Ranging oneself against the arrangement."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The general rule"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rulers are **not a terror to good conduct, but to evil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A statement of what government is for, phrased as a description."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul had been the beneficiary of it — Roman soldiers pulled him out of a lynching in Jerusalem, and a Roman tribune moved him out of an ambush by night with two hundred men."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The test"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do you want to live without fear of the one in authority? **Then do what is good, and you will have his commendation**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ordinary case. Most people, most of the time, are not troubled by the magistrate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is **God's servant for your good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **diakonos** — servant, minister. The word used of church officers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul applies it to a pagan magistrate who has no idea he is holding it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sword"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But if you do what is wrong, be afraid, for **he does not carry the sword for nothing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **machaira** — the short sword carried by Roman officials, the symbol of the power to execute."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is God's servant, **carrying out wrath on the one who does wrong**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word **diakonos**, a second time, now attached to punishment."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why this is not vengeance"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The paragraph immediately before: *beloved, do not avenge yourselves, but rather give place to wrath, for it is written, \"Vengeance is Mine, I will repay,\" says the Lord*. See the note on Romans 12:17–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Christians are told not to retaliate, and then told who does hold the sword. The two paragraphs are one argument, and the chapter break hides it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two reasons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is why you must be subject — **not only because of wrath, but for the sake of conscience**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **suneidesin** — conscience."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Fear of punishment is the lower reason and is acknowledged as real. The higher one is that a believer understands what the arrangement is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The limit the rest of Scripture sets"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**We ought to obey God rather than men**.\"* — Peter and the apostles, to the council that had just ordered them to stop teaching. See the note on Acts 5:27–32."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The midwives who lied to Pharaoh, Daniel's three friends at the furnace, and Daniel at the open window all disobeyed rulers, and Scripture commends every one of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is stating the rule, not abolishing the cases."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Render therefore to all their due: taxes to whom taxes are due… **Owe no one anything except to love one another**.* See the note on Romans 13:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter about submission to Rome ends by saying the only permanent debt is love."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word.** **Diakonos** — servant, minister — used twice of a pagan magistrate holding a sword he does not know whose it is."}]}, "Romans 15:1–4": {"title": "What strength is for", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent a chapter on Christians who disagree about food and holy days — the strong who can eat anything, the weak who cannot. This is his conclusion, and it is addressed to the winners of the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Those of us who are strong have something to do with that strength, and it is not to please ourselves. It is to carry the weaknesses of those who are not strong.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul counts himself among the strong. He agrees with them on the substance — he has already said no food is unclean in itself. And then he tells them that being right is not the end of the matter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is to *bear* — to pick up and carry. Not tolerate. Carry."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The example"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ Himself never lived to please Himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the proof he offers is a quotation from Psalm 69 — *the insults of those who insulted You fell on Me*. It is a strange verse to choose until you see the logic: the one with every right to be pleased took what belonged to others instead."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line about scripture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everything that was written in earlier days was written to teach us, so that through the patience and the comfort the Scriptures give, we might go on holding to hope.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just quoted the Old Testament to settle a first-century church dispute about dinner, and then says why he can: the old writings were for our instruction. Note what he says they produce — patience, comfort, and hope. Not primarily information."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why it sits here.** This is Paul's last word on a quarrel about secondary things, and his solution is not a ruling. He tells the people who are right to use their freedom on behalf of the people who are not, and points at Christ for the pattern. It is the most practical thing in the chapter."}]}, "Romans 15:13": {"title": "Filled full", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The closing prayer of the argument, after eleven chapters of theology and four of practice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just finished quoting four Old Testament passages about Gentiles praising God, ending with Isaiah: *there shall be a root of Jesse; and He who shall rise to reign over the Gentiles, in Him the Gentiles shall hope*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer follows the quotation, and takes its words from it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now may **the God of hope**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ho Theos tes elpidos**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He picks the title out of the verse he has just quoted. Isaiah said the Gentiles would hope in Him; Paul calls Him the God of that hope."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The request"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Fill you **full**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **plerosai** — fill, make complete."}, {"k": "h", "t": "With what"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **all joy and all peace in believing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pases charas kai eirenes en to pisteuein**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two nouns, and *all* attached to both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**En to pisteuein** — in the believing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A present infinitive: in the ongoing act of trusting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not a state achieved. Something that happens while trusting is happening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that you **overflow with hope**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eis to perisseuein humas en te elpidi** — that you may abound in the hope."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Perisseuo** — to exceed, to run over. The verb used of the twelve baskets left after the five thousand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Filled with joy and peace, in order to overflow with hope."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hope is the outflow, not the input. Paul does not ask for hope directly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The agent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By **the power of the Holy Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **en dunamei Pneumatos Hagiou**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The filling and the overflowing are both attributed to Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter has said about hope"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **hope does not disappoint**, because the love of God has been poured out in our hearts by the Holy Spirit.* See the note on Romans 5:3–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For we were saved in this hope, but **hope that is seen is not hope**; for why does one still hope for what he sees?* See the note on Romans 8:24–25."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But if we hope for what we do not see, **we eagerly wait for it with perseverance**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the letter is written to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A congregation Paul has never visited, divided between Jewish and Gentile believers, arguing about food and days."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Receive one another, just as Christ also received us, to the glory of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prayer comes at the end of that argument, and asks that both sides be filled with the same thing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's travel plans — Jerusalem with the collection, then Rome, then Spain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now I beg you, brethren… **that you strive together with me in prayers to God for me**, that I may be delivered from those in Judea who do not believe.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He got to Rome. In chains, after a shipwreck, as a prisoner."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other benediction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now to Him who is able to establish you according to my gospel… **to God, alone wise, be glory through Jesus Christ forever**. Amen.* See the note on Romans 16:25–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The letter has two closing prayers, and this is the first."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order.** Joy and peace poured in, *so that* hope runs over. Paul does not ask for hope — he asks for the two things whose overflow it is."}]}, "Romans 16:17–20": {"title": "\"Under your feet\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last warning in Romans, and the last promise, in four verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who to watch for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Mark out those who **cause divisions and lay down stumbling blocks** contrary to the teaching you have learned, and **stay away from them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things they do: split, and trip."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *stumbling blocks* is **skandalon** — the trigger stick of a trap."}, {"k": "h", "t": "How to recognise them"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*They are **serving their own appetites**, and with **smooth words and flattering speech** they deceive the hearts of the unsuspecting.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two marks: what they are actually serving, and how they talk."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *smooth words* is **chrestologia** — literally *good-speech*, plausible talk. It occurs once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And they deceive *the unsuspecting* — the Greek is the innocent, those who think no evil of anybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two kinds of knowing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Be **wise about what is good**, and **innocent about what is evil**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *innocent* is **akeraios** — unmixed, unadulterated. It is used of undiluted wine and unalloyed metal."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus says the same thing with different animals: *be wise as serpents and harmless as doves* — the same word for harmless."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Expertise in one direction and not the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the God of peace will be quick to crush Satan under your feet**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew original is in Genesis 3:15 — *He shall bruise your head* — and Paul is plainly reaching for it. See the note on Genesis 3:14–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But note what he does with the pronouns."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The head is crushed **under your feet**, and the one doing the crushing is **God**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not you, and not without you. Both are in the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The God of peace\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The title chosen for a verse about crushing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses it six times, and it is the name he attaches to this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Quick\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **en tachei** — shortly, speedily."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul was writing to a church in the capital of an empire that would kill many of them, and the Roman church waited a long time for anything that looked like this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you. Amen.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A warning about divisive men, a promise about Satan underfoot, and then grace."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter is otherwise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 16 is mostly a list of names — twenty-six of them greeted personally, ten of them women, including Phoebe who probably carried the letter, and Priscilla and Aquila who *risked their own necks* for Paul."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The warning sits in the middle of the warmest chapter he wrote."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Whose feet.** *The God of peace will crush Satan **under your feet**.* Paul could have written *under His feet* and been entirely orthodox. He wrote the other one."}]}, "Romans 16:25–27": {"title": "\"Able to hold you steady\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The last sentence of Romans, and it is one long clause held open until the final word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The claim"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now to Him who is **able to hold you steady**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sterixai** — to fix firmly, to establish, to set in place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word Jesus used to Peter — *when you have turned back, **strengthen** your brothers* — and the word Peter uses of what God does after suffering. See the notes on Luke 22:31–34 and 1 Peter 5:10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul opened the letter wanting to do it himself: *I long to see you, that I may impart to you some spiritual gift, **so that you may be established**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sixteen chapters later he attributes the establishing to God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "According to what"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In keeping with **the good news I carry** and **the preaching of Jesus Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The steadying is measured by a message. Not by effort, and not by conditions."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**My gospel** — Paul calls it his twice elsewhere. He means the one he was given to carry, not one of his own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mystery"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*According to the revelation of **the mystery that was held in silence through ages beyond counting**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **musterion** — not a riddle, but something that could not be worked out and had to be disclosed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Held in silence** — the Greek is *kept secret*. Paul uses the same idea in Ephesians and Colossians: *the mystery which has been hidden from ages and from generations, but now has been revealed*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The disclosure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But is **now brought out into the open**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phanerothentos** — made manifest, brought into the light."}, {"k": "h", "t": "By what means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And, **through the writings of the prophets**, according to the commandment of the everlasting God, **made known to all nations for the obedience of faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The odd clause is *through the writings of the prophets*. A secret kept silent through the ages is now published — and the publishing runs through documents that had been available the whole time."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent the letter doing exactly that: Habakkuk, Genesis, the Psalms, Isaiah, Hosea, Deuteronomy, quoted at every turn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase that bookends the letter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the obedience of faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He used it in the opening paragraph: *through whom we have received grace and apostleship for **obedience to the faith among all nations**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 1 and chapter 16, the same phrase, closing the circle."}, {"k": "h", "t": "To whom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **God, the only wise One**, be glory **through Jesus Christ** forever. Amen.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the letter has been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sin, law, faith, Abraham, no condemnation, the Spirit, Israel, the olive tree, living sacrifices, the weak and the strong, and a chapter of greetings naming twenty-six people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it ends on a doxology about being held steady."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the doxology sits in the manuscripts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some early copies place it at the end of chapter 14 or chapter 15 rather than here. The variation is old and has been noted since antiquity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wherever it is placed, it is the last word Paul gives the letter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is being celebrated at the end.** Not the argument. Paul closes sixteen chapters of the tightest reasoning in the New Testament by pointing at the One able to hold the reader steady, and attributing the ability to the message rather than to the reader."}]}, "Romans 2:1–5": {"title": "\"It is God's kindness that is leading you to a change of mind\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent chapter 1 describing a society in freefall, and every reader nodding along is the person chapter 2 is written to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The trap"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So you have no defence, whoever you are, when you sit in judgment on someone else. In the very act of judging another person you condemn yourself, because you who judge are doing the same things.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first word is *therefore*. Everything in chapter 1 was setting this up. Paul built a list his audience would agree with, and then turned and said: the agreeing is the problem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question in the middle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do you think so lightly of the riches of His kindness and patience and restraint?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three words for God's forbearance, and each is a treasury word — *riches* of them. Kindness, patience, and holding back."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The charge is not that the judgmental person has broken more rules. It is that they have **misread what the kindness was for**, treating a delay in judgement as either approval or absence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the kindness is doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not realising that it is God's kindness that is leading you to a change of mind.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb means to lead by the hand, to conduct somewhere. Kindness is described as the thing doing the leading, and the destination is repentance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is worth stating plainly, because it is the reverse of how the process is usually imagined. Not: repent, and then God will be kind. His kindness is what brings a person round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The alternative"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But with a hard and unchanged heart you are storing up wrath for yourself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same treasury image, turned over. Both the kindness and the wrath are being accumulated; which one a person ends up drawing on depends on what they did with the first."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the argument goes.** Paul is on his way to *all have sinned*, and he gets there by closing every exit — the pagan in chapter 1, the moralist here, the religious in the rest of chapter 2. Nobody is left standing, which is exactly the ground he needs for chapter 3."}]}, "Romans 2:6–11": {"title": "No favoritism", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is building a case, and this paragraph is the part where nobody is exempt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the argument is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 1 described the Gentile world and ended *who, knowing the righteous judgment of God… not only do the same but also approve of those who practise them*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 2 opens by turning on the reader: *therefore you are inexcusable, O man, whoever you are who judge, for **in whatever you judge another you condemn yourself**, for you who judge practise the same things*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He will **repay each person according to what that person has done**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **apodosei hekasto kata ta erga autou**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is Psalm 62 and Proverbs 24, quoted almost word for word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Paul says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because the argument he is building requires it. If God judges by deeds, without partiality, then the case against everybody is complete — and that is what chapter 3 concludes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is none righteous, no, not one.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first group"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To those who **by patient perseverance in doing good** seek glory and honor and immortality, He will give eternal life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kath' hupomonen ergou agathou** — according to patient endurance of good work."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that describes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A standard. Paul is not saying anybody meets it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "By chapter 3 he has said that no one does: *for all have sinned and fall short of the glory of God*. See the note on Romans 3:23–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second group"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But for those who are **self-seeking, who refuse to obey the truth** and give themselves to wrongdoing instead, there will be **anger and fury**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ex eritheias** — out of selfish ambition. The same noun James uses. See the note on James 3:13–16."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two lists"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Trouble and distress will come upon **every human being who does evil, the Jew first and also the Gentile**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But glory and honor and peace will come to **everyone who does good, the Jew first and also the Gentile**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Ioudaiou te proton kai Hellenos** — to the Jew first and also to the Greek."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses it in chapter 1 of the gospel: *it is the power of God to salvation for everyone who believes, for the Jew first and also for the Greek*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Here he uses it twice, of judgment and of reward."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Priority in the gospel means priority in the accounting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **with God there is no favoritism**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ou gar estin prosopolempsia para to Theo** — there is no face-receiving with God."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The idiom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Prosopolempsia** — from **prosopon** (face) and **lambano** (to receive). A literal rendering of the Hebrew **nasa panim** — to lift the face."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In court, the judge lifting a defendant's face to see who it was. Partiality."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law forbids it of judges: *you shall not show partiality in judgment*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where else it appears"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And you, masters, do the same things to them, giving up threatening, knowing that **your own Master also is in heaven, and there is no partiality with Him**.* — Paul, to slave-owners."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My brethren, do not hold the faith of our Lord Jesus Christ… **with partiality**.* — James, about seating a rich man well and a poor man badly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter, in Cornelius's house: *in truth I perceive that God shows no partiality*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the argument arrives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore **by the deeds of the law no flesh will be justified** in His sight.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But now the righteousness of God apart from the law is revealed… **even the righteousness of God, through faith in Jesus Christ, to all and on all who believe. For there is no difference**.* See the note on Romans 3:21–22."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase — no difference — that closed the judgment closes the offer."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The idiom.** **Prosopolempsia** — face-receiving. A judge lifting a defendant's face to see whose it was. Paul uses the impartiality to close off every exemption, and then to open the offer."}]}, "Romans 3:19–20": {"title": "Every mouth stopped", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul closes the prosecution and states what the law was actually for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three chapters of indictment. The Gentile world in chapter 1, the moralist in chapter 2, the Jew in chapter 3 — and then a chain of six Old Testament quotations:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is none righteous, no, not one… their throat is an open tomb… their mouth is full of cursing and bitterness… there is no fear of God before their eyes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Most of the quotations are about the mouth. Then Paul draws his conclusion about mouths."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the law addresses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Whatever the law says, **it says to those who live under the law**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *to those in the law*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is heading off the obvious objection. The quotations he has just stacked up are from Israel's own Scriptures, addressed to Israel — so the people most confident of their standing are the ones being described."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The point is that **every mouth would be closed** and **the whole world would stand accountable to God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *closed* is **phrasso** — to stop up, to fence, to muzzle. It is used of damming a stream and of stopping the mouths of lions."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Accountable** is **hupodikos** — a legal term for a defendant liable to judgment, with no defence left to enter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is a courtroom where the case has been made and nobody has anything to say."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **no human being will be put right with Him by doing what the law requires**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *by works of the law no flesh shall be justified in His sight*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul repeats it in Galatians in almost identical words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law does instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What the law does is **show us what sin is**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *through the law is the knowledge of sin* — **epignosis**, full recognition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the cure. The diagnosis."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says it again in chapter 7: *I would not have known sin except through the law. For I would not have known covetousness unless the law had said, \"You shall not covet.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law is not accused of"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Anything. Paul defends it repeatedly — *the law is holy, and the commandment holy and just and good*, and *do we then make void the law through faith? Certainly not! On the contrary, we establish the law.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument is about function, not quality."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The very next word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But now.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But now **the righteousness of God apart from the law is revealed**… even the righteousness of God, through faith in Jesus Christ, **to all and on all who believe**. For **there is no difference**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three chapters of prosecution, one sentence of *every mouth stopped*, and then *but now*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Being **justified freely by His grace** through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *freely* is **dorean** — as a gift, without cause, for nothing. It is the word used of hating *without a cause*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where boasting goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where is boasting then? **It is excluded**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same idea as the closed mouth, four verses on."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the silence is for.** Paul does not stop the mouths to leave the room quiet. The next thing in the chapter is *but now* — and the sentence after the courtroom is about a gift."}]}, "Romans 3:21–22": {"title": "\"But now\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two words turn the letter around."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they come"}, {"k": "p", "t": "After three chapters of indictment, six quoted verses about throats and tongues and mouths, and a courtroom where every mouth is stopped and the whole world stands accountable. See the note on Romans 3:19–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then: *but now*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **nuni de** — but now, at this present moment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a change of era as much as a change of subject. Paul uses the same phrase at the turns of his arguments elsewhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is apart from what"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Apart from **the law altogether**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **choris nomou** — separately from law, without reference to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not law softened, satisfied by degrees, or graded on a curve. Set aside as the mechanism entirely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has appeared"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **rightness from God** has been **brought out into the open**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dikaiosune theou** — the righteousness of God. It is the theme Paul announced in chapter 1: *in it the righteousness of God is revealed from faith to faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Brought out into the open** — the verb is perfect passive, made manifest and standing manifest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The witnesses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the law and the prophets have been testifying to it all along**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Having said *apart from the law*, Paul immediately says the law testifies to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law cannot produce it and has been pointing at it the whole time. He proves the point in the next chapter with Abraham and David, both from the Old Testament. See the note on Romans 4:6–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The channel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is a rightness from God that comes **through faith in Jesus Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not through effort, descent, or ritual."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two prepositions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it comes **upon all** and **to all** who believe.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says it twice with different prepositions, which is not an accident of translation — the Greek has both **eis** and **epi**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The levelling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is **no distinction between one person and another**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ou gar estin diastole** — there is no difference."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it here about who can receive it, and he has just said it in verse 22–23 about who needs it: *for all have sinned and fall short of the glory of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase does both jobs. No difference in the need, no difference in the offer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Being **justified freely by His grace** through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus.* See the note on Romans 3:23–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Freely** is **dorean** — as a gift, gratuitously, for nothing. It is the word used of being hated *without a cause*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where is boasting then? **It is excluded**. By what law? Of works? No, but **by the law of faith**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What has changed at \"but now.\"** Not the verdict on anybody — Paul restates it in the next verse. What has appeared is a righteousness that was never on offer through the mechanism he has just closed."}]}, "Romans 3:23–24": {"title": "Two sentences, and the word between them", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted diagnosis in the Bible, and it is only half of the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it usually stops"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Everyone has sinned. Everyone falls short of the glory of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Quoted alone, that is a verdict. But in Greek it is not the end of the sentence — the participle that follows carries straight on into the next clause without a full stop."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And everyone is put right with Him as a gift, freely, by His grace.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same *everyone* governs both halves. Paul builds the universal charge and then applies the universal remedy in the same breath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Falls short\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is about coming up behind, lacking, being left wanting. It is the word used in Hebrews for someone missing out on God's grace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *broke a rule*. Failed to reach — which is a description of distance rather than of crimes committed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Three words for how it comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul stacks them because one is not enough:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**as a gift** — the word means gratuitously, without cause in the receiver. It is the same word Jesus uses in John for being hated *without reason*.", "**freely**", "**by His grace**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is closing every door through which a contribution might be smuggled back in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The ransom"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Through the ransom paid in Christ Jesus that bought us out of slavery and set us loose.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Apolutrosis** again — the release accomplished by paying a price. See the note on Ephesians 1:7–10."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The gift is free to the person receiving it. It was not free."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes next.** Paul says God set Christ forth publicly as the mercy seat — the same word the Greek Old Testament uses for the lid of the ark. What was hidden behind a curtain and approached once a year with blood, Paul says was displayed in the open. See the note on Exodus 25:17–22."}]}, "Romans 3:25–26": {"title": "Just, and the justifier", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The densest two verses Paul wrote, and the whole letter turns on them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But now the righteousness of God apart from the law is revealed… **for all have sinned and fall short of the glory of God, being justified freely by His grace** through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus.* See the note on Romans 3:23–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dorean** — freely, as a gift. The same word Revelation uses of the water at the end."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting forth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God **publicly set Him forth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **proetheto** — set out in front, displayed publicly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not hidden, not private. The word suggests something put where it can be seen."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As **the place where His anger is satisfied and His mercy is met**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hilasterion**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the Greek Old Testament it is the standard word for the **kapporet** — the lid of the ark, the mercy seat, where the blood was sprinkled once a year over the tablets of the law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews uses the same word of it: *above it were the cherubim of glory overshadowing the mercy seat*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says Christ is that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Through what"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This comes to us **through faith in His blood**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The blood is the Day of Atonement detail. The high priest went in with it, alone, once a year, and sprinkled it on that lid."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God did it to **show His own rightness**, because **in His patience He had passed over the sins committed before**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paresin** — a passing over, an overlooking. Not the same word as forgiveness."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the problem Paul is solving, and it is not the one most readers expect."}, {"k": "p", "t": "For centuries God had let sins go. David committed adultery and murder and was told *the LORD has put away your sin*. On what basis?"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An accountant who ignores debts has a problem. Paul says God's forbearance had left a question hanging over His own justice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And He did it to show His rightness **in this present hour**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Now. The cross settles the past accounts and the present ones."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That **He Himself is just, and that He is the One who puts right the person who believes in Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dikaion kai dikaiounta** — just and justifying."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole difficulty in four words. Anybody can be lenient. Anybody can be strict."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Doing both, on the same case, without either being a pretence, is the thing the passage claims happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The psalm about it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Righteousness and peace have kissed.* See the note on Psalms 85:10–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One insists the account be settled and the other insists it be over, and the psalm puts them face to face."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is describing where they met."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where is boasting then? **It is excluded**. By what law? Of works? No, but by the law of faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **exekleisthe** — shut out. A door closed on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 4: Abraham, and *he believed God, and it was accounted to him for righteousness* — said of a man before he was circumcised and four hundred years before the law."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, having been justified by faith, **we have peace with God** through our Lord Jesus Christ.* See the note on Romans 5:1–2."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The problem being solved.** Not how a sinner can be forgiven. How God could have been letting it go for two thousand years and still be called just — and Paul says the cross answers both."}]}, "Romans 3:5–8": {"title": "The slander he had to answer", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes what people were saying about his preaching, and the quote is unflattering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The objection"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If our wrongdoing **only makes God's rightness stand out more clearly**, what do we conclude? That **God is unfair to bring His anger down on us**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument: if my failure displays His faithfulness, punishing me looks unreasonable."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The apology for raising it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am **putting it the way people put it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kata anthropon lego** — I speak as a man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul flags that he is voicing somebody else's argument and is uncomfortable holding it even for a sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*No, of course not.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me genoito** — the strongest negative available. It appears ten times in Romans, and every one is a reply to a bad inference from grace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If God were unfair, **how could He ever judge the world at all**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The counter is short: a God who cannot judge fairly cannot judge, and everybody in the argument already assumes He will."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The objection pressed harder"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If my **falsehood only makes God's truth shine brighter and adds to His glory**, why am I **still being judged as a sinner**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same argument, one step further. Now the speaker is claiming his lie is a contribution."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reductio"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And why not go all the way and say, \"**Let us do evil so that good can come of it**\"?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not answer this one with logic. He answers it with attribution."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The slander"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That is exactly what **some people slander us with, insisting we teach it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **blasphemoumetha** — we are defamed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Somebody was going round saying Paul taught *do evil that good may come*. He is not constructing a hypothetical opponent; he is reporting an accusation in circulation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Anyone who actually argues that way is **rightly condemned**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One clause, and he moves on. He does not refute it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the accusation existed"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because of what he actually taught, which is in the next chapter: *to him who does not work but believes on Him who justifies the ungodly, his faith is accounted for righteousness*. See the note on Romans 4:4–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *where sin abounded, grace abounded much more*. See the note on Romans 5:20–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody misrepresents a message about trying harder. The charge is evidence of what he was preaching."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he answers it properly"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three chapters later, and at length: *what shall we say then? Shall we continue in sin that grace may abound? Certainly not! **How shall we who died to sin live any longer in it?*** See the note on Romans 6:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer, when he gives it, is not a warning. It is a question about what happened to the person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says just before this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What advantage then has the Jew? **Much in every way!** Chiefly because to them were committed the oracles of God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For what if some did not believe? Will their unbelief make the faithfulness of God without effect? Certainly not! Indeed, **let God be true but every man a liar**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That last line is quoted from Psalm 116, and it is the sentence the objection in verse 7 is trying to exploit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There is none righteous, no, not one* — a chain of six Old Testament quotations — and then *but now the righteousness of God apart from the law is revealed*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The slander is answered in verse 8 and the actual doctrine arrives in verse 21."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the accusation proves.** Nobody accused Paul of making obedience too easy. They accused him of teaching *let us do evil that good may come* — which is only a plausible misreading of one kind of message."}]}, "Romans 4:1–3": {"title": "What did Abraham find", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul argues his case from the one man his readers could not dismiss."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why Abraham"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because he is the fixed point."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything a first-century Jewish reader believed about standing with God ran through him. He is the father of the nation, the one circumcision was given to, and the pattern case in every rabbinic discussion of merit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "If Paul can settle it on Abraham, the argument is over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What shall we say, then, about **Abraham, our father according to the flesh**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What did he find?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **heurekenai** — to have found, discovered, obtained."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hypothetical"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If Abraham was **put right with God by what he accomplished**, he has **something to be proud of**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kauchema** — a boast, a ground for boasting."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul concedes it freely. A man who earns something is entitled to say so."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The limit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **not in front of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **all' ou pros theon**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The concession dies in the second half of the sentence. Whatever Abraham could claim before men, there is one audience where the claim does not stand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Listen to what the Scripture says: \"**Abraham believed God, and it was counted to him as righteousness**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 15:6. See the note on Genesis 15:6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes it as a settled text and builds the chapter on it. James quotes the same verse to make a different point, and both are working from one sentence in Genesis."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's next move is chronological, and it is devastating in its simplicity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How then was it accounted? While he was circumcised, or uncircumcised? **Not while circumcised, but while uncircumcised**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 15 is the accounting. Genesis 17 is the circumcision. Fourteen years apart, by the narrative's own dating."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the law is four hundred and thirty years later still."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now to him who works, the wages are not counted as grace but as **debt**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **opheilema** — what is owed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But to him who does not work but believes on Him who **justifies the ungodly**, his faith is accounted for righteousness.* See the note on Romans 4:4–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Justifies the ungodly** — the phrase that would have been the hardest for Paul's readers, and he states it without softening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other witness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as David also describes the blessedness of the man to whom God imputes righteousness apart from works: \"**Blessed are those whose lawless deeds are forgiven**.\"* See the note on Romans 4:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two witnesses, the patriarch and the king, and Deuteronomy requires two."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Abraham had actually done by Genesis 15"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Left home, which is to his credit."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And lied about his wife in Egypt to save himself, which is not. See the note on Genesis 12:10–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does it a second time, years later, at Gerar. See the note on Genesis 20:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man Genesis credits with righteousness is the man whose record includes both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Elogisthe** — counted, reckoned, put to his account."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An accounting term. Paul uses it eleven times in this chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing is described as being produced in Abraham. Something is entered in a ledger."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was not written for his sake alone that it was imputed to him, but **also for us**.* See the note on Romans 4:23–25."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul concedes and where it dies.** A man who earns something may boast — and then the same sentence adds *but not before God*."}]}, "Romans 4:13–15": {"title": "Two ways to be an heir, and why they cannot both be true", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul is arguing about Abraham, and his case rests on a point of chronology anyone can check."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing argument"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise to Abraham came in Genesis 15. The law came at Sinai, four hundred and thirty years later. Circumcision itself came a chapter after the promise, not before it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So whatever made Abraham an heir, it cannot have been law-keeping — the law did not exist yet. Paul makes this argument at length through the whole chapter, and it is unanswerable on its own terms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the two cannot be combined"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If the people of the law are the heirs, then faith has been drained of meaning and the promise has been cancelled.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two strong verbs. Faith is *emptied* — made hollow, void of content. The promise is *abolished*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's point is not that law is bad but that the two systems cannot run at once. A promise received by faith and an inheritance earned by performance are different kinds of thing, and running the second empties the first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"What the law brings about is anger\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word is wrath. And Paul gives the mechanism in the same breath: *where there is no law there is no breaking of it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law is not accused of causing sin. It is described as the thing that turns wrongdoing into transgression — a crossing of a marked line — and so brings the matter under judgement. He develops this at length three chapters later."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the promise actually was.** *That he would inherit the world.* Genesis promised Abraham a land, descendants, and blessing to all nations. Paul reads the whole thing as one inheritance and states it at its widest — which is the scale he thinks the promise was always working at."}]}, "Romans 4:16–17": {"title": "\"Calls things that do not exist as though they already do\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul explains why God set the whole thing up on faith rather than on performance, and then describes the God being believed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it rests on faith"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That is why it rests on faith — **so that it can be a matter of grace**, and so that **the promise stands guaranteed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two reasons, and the second is the practical one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A promise conditional on my keeping is only as certain as my keeping. Move it onto faith and it moves onto grace, and a promise resting on grace has nothing shaky underneath it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *guaranteed* is **bebaios** — firm, valid, the word used of a legally binding document."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it covers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not only for those who have the law, but **for those who share the faith of Abraham**. He is **the father of us all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The line is drawn by faith, not by descent. Paul has already pointed out that Abraham was declared righteous while still uncircumcised — before the mark, before the law, before anything that would exclude anybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things God does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The God who **gives life to the dead** and **calls things that do not exist as though they already do**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul picks these two out of everything he could have said about God, because both are what Abraham's situation required."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Gives life to the dead** — Abraham's body *as good as dead*, Sarah's womb *dead as well*, in Paul's own words four verses later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Calls things that do not exist as though they already do** — the Greek is to call, to name, to summon. God speaks of a thing in the present tense while it is still nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where God did that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**I have made you a father of many nations**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes Genesis 17, and the tense is the point: *I have made* — past, completed, said to a childless man of ninety-nine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the same breath God changed his name. **Abram** means exalted father. **Abraham** means father of a multitude."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had one son by a servant girl and no son by his wife. From that day everybody in the camp had to call him *father of a multitude*, every time they wanted his attention, for a year before Isaac was born. See the note on Genesis 17:3–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order"}, {"k": "p", "t": "God said it first. The name came before the child, the promise before the evidence, and Abraham's part was to answer to the name he had been given."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is our father **in the sight of the God he believed**.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Genesis 1 sounds like beside this.** *Then God spoke: \"Let there be light.\" And light was.* The same pattern, and Paul reaches for it as a description of who Abraham was dealing with. See the note on Genesis 1:3–5."}]}, "Romans 4:18–19": {"title": "Hoping against hope", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul describes what Abraham did with the facts, and it is not what the facts suggested."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **nothing left to hope for**, Abraham went on **hoping and believing**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *against hope, in hope he believed* — **par' elpida ep' elpidi**. Two kinds of hope in one clause, and they are set against each other."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There was no hope available from the situation. He held a different one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he refused to do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **refused to dwell on the facts stacked against him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is **katanoeo** — to fix the mind on, to consider closely, to study. It is the same word used in Hebrews 3 for *think hard about Christ Jesus*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So it is not that Abraham was unaware. Paul is precise about what he did with the information: he would not stare at it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The facts he would not stare at"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul lists them, and he does not soften them either:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*his own body **as good as dead**, since he was about a hundred years old*", "*and **Sarah's womb dead as well***"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for the first is **nekroo** — to put to death, to make a corpse of. Paul's own vocabulary is blunter than most translations."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two dead things, named. That is the raw material."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he looked at instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before tells you: *the God who gives life to the dead and calls things that do not exist as though they already do.* See the note on Romans 4:16–17."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had two dead things in his hand and a promise about them, and he attended to the One who made it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"So he became the father of many nations\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Exactly as he had been told: \"**That is how many your descendants will be.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes Genesis 15 — the night God took him outside and showed him the stars."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the name he had been given, *father of a multitude*, was already true in the telling before it was true in the household. See the note on Genesis 17:3–8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not claimed here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul never says Abraham felt confident, or that his circumstances improved, or that he stopped being a hundred years old."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His body stayed as it was until it did not. What changed first was where he was looking."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Hebrews says Sarah did.** *Sarah herself was given the strength to conceive… **because she counted the One who promised as good for it**.* The Greek is a reckoning word — she assessed His credit and found it sound. She had laughed at the door of the tent, and the New Testament puts her in the roll of faith anyway. See the note on Hebrews 11:8–12."}]}, "Romans 4:20–22": {"title": "\"He grew strong in faith\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four things said about Abraham in two verses, and each one is worth its own line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He did not waver"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **did not waver at God's promise in unbelief**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **diakrino** — the same verb behind *discerning the body* in 1 Corinthians 11 and *does not doubt* in Mark 11. It means to be divided, to be in two minds."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was not split. He was not holding the promise in one hand and the arithmetic of his own age in the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He grew strong"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **grew strong in faith**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a passive — he was strengthened. Something was done to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And note the direction. Twenty-four years passed between the first promise and this chapter, and his faith did not wear down over them; it was built up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He gave glory to God"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **gave glory to God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Before the son. Before any evidence at all. Glory is given for something not yet received."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He was fully convinced"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Fully convinced that **what God had promised, God was able to do**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **plerophoreo** — carried full, filled to capacity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And look at what he was convinced of. Not that it would be easy, or when it would happen, or how. That God was **able**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That is why **it was credited to him as rightness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul comes back to Genesis 15:6, which is the sentence this whole chapter has been unpacking. See the note on Genesis 15:6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Righteousness was entered on his account — and the thing entered against it was believing that God could do what God said."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was not on the ledger"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not his obedience, which was real but patchy. Not his record — he lied about his wife to two kings, and took Hagar because the promise seemed slow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's whole argument is that this is the point: *to him who does not work but believes on Him who justifies the ungodly, his faith is accounted for righteousness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Justifies the ungodly.** Paul's phrase, three verses into the chapter."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Who else it was written for.** *Now it was not written for his sake alone that it was imputed to him, but for us also.* Paul's next sentence. The ledger entry made in Genesis 15 was recorded as a precedent, and the account it applies to is the reader's. See the note on Romans 4:23–25."}]}, "Romans 4:23–25": {"title": "\"Raised so that we could be put right\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent the chapter on Abraham, and here he says why he bothered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Not written down for his sake alone\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The words \"**it was credited to him**\" were not written down for his sake alone. They were written **for ours as well**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sentence Paul is quoting is Genesis 15:6 — *Abraham believed God, and it was accounted to him for righteousness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *credited* is **logizomai**, a bookkeeping word: to reckon into an account, to enter on a ledger."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham had nothing to put in. It was entered anyway, and Paul says the entry was recorded in Scripture as a precedent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What we believe"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It will be credited **to us who believe in the One who raised Jesus our Lord from the dead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Abraham believed God could bring life out of a dead womb — *he did not consider his own body, already dead… or the deadness of Sarah's womb.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The object of faith in both cases is a God who raises the dead. Paul makes them the same faith."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He was **handed over because of our wrongdoing**, and He was **raised so that we could be put right with God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two events, two prepositions, and both are *because of* in the Greek."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Handed over **because of** our sins — they were the reason He went to the cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Raised **because of** our justification — and that is where the sentence gets interesting. Young's Literal keeps it: *raised up because of our being declared righteous.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which way round"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *raised so that we might one day be justified*. **Because** we were."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The debt was settled at the cross, and the resurrection is what happened after the settlement — not the means of it but the proof of it. He went in carrying the sins and came out without them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "If anything had been left owing, the tomb would still be shut. The empty tomb is God's receipt."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it is in this chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 4 is Paul's case that righteousness has always been credited rather than earned — from Abraham, who was declared righteous *while still uncircumcised*, four hundred and thirty years before the law existed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter ends by extending the same entry to the reader, and then chapter 5 opens: *therefore, having been justified by faith, we have peace with God.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Abraham did not have.** A law, a temple, a priesthood, a Bible, or a synagogue. He had a promise and a night sky, and the ledger entry was made on the spot."}]}, "Romans 4:6–8": {"title": "The blessing David sang", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul calls in a second witness, and the witness is a psalm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why David is here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's argument runs on Abraham: *Abraham believed God, and it was accounted to him for righteousness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul then reaches for a second case, four hundred years later, from a different kind of man. Abraham was the founder; David was a king who had a man killed to cover an adultery."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The person **to whose account God puts righteousness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **logizomai** — to reckon, to credit, to enter in a ledger. Paul uses it eleven times in this one chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a bookkeeping word, and the entry is made by somebody else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The qualifier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Apart from **anything accomplished**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *apart from works*. Paul has already set it up in verse 4: *now to him who works, the wages are not counted as grace but as debt.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wages are owed. This is not that."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 32, and Paul quotes it in full — three clauses, and every one is passive."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Whose wrongs are lifted off\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nasa** — to lift, to carry away. The word used of the scapegoat bearing the iniquities into the wilderness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Whose sins are buried out of sight\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kasah** — covered."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Against whom the Lord counts no wrong\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The third is the one Paul is really after, because it uses his word: *the Lord does not **impute** iniquity*. Same verb, **logizomai**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two clauses about what is done with the sin, and one about what is not written down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The happiness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Oh, **the happiness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **makarios** — blessed, happy. It is how the Beatitudes open."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice in two verses, at the front of each sentence, which is the Hebrew word order of the psalm."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Psalm 32 is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "David's own account of the year he said nothing:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When I kept silent, my bones grew old through my groaning all the day long. For day and night Your hand was heavy upon me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I acknowledged my sin to You, and my iniquity I have not hidden… **and You forgave the iniquity of my sin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes the opening of it, and the psalm behind the quotation is a man describing what the silence cost."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The point Paul makes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Does this blessedness then come upon the circumcised only, or upon the uncircumcised also?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "His whole use of the two witnesses turns on chronology: Abraham was counted righteous in Genesis 15, and circumcised in Genesis 17. Fourteen years apart, in that order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul concludes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now it was not written for his sake alone… **but also for us**. It shall be imputed to us who believe in Him who raised up Jesus our Lord from the dead.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The ledger entry is described as transferable, and the reason given is the resurrection."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the three clauses have in common.** Nobody in them does anything. Lifted off, covered, not counted — and Paul picks the psalm precisely because the man singing it had nothing to plead."}]}, "Romans 5:1–2": {"title": "Peace with God", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The verse turns on its first word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Therefore\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four chapters of argument stand behind it — all have sinned, none is righteous, the law shuts every mouth, and then righteousness credited to Abraham and to everybody who believes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything in this sentence is the conclusion of that, not a fresh claim."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Having been put right\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Since we **have been put right with God** through faith.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is aorist passive — done, in the past, to us. Not being put right, and not hoping to be."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent chapter 4 on the ledger entry: *it was credited to him as righteousness*, and *not written for his sake alone, but for ours also.* See the note on Romans 4:23–25."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Peace with God"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are **at peace with God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not peace *from* God, or the feeling of peace. **Peace with** — the ending of a hostility."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses war language about the previous state without embarrassment: *when we were enemies we were reconciled to God through the death of His Son.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The treaty was signed at the cross and this verse reports its terms."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Through our Lord Jesus Christ\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The peace is not a mood that can be lost on a bad morning. It runs through a Person, and Paul names Him as the channel in the same breath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Brought in"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Through Him we have also been **brought in**, by faith, **to this grace in which we now stand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *brought in* is **prosagoge** — access, introduction. It is the word for being presented at a royal court by somebody with the standing to do it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody introduces himself to a king. The word assumes an escort."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In which we now stand\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Perfect tense — we have stood and are standing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grace is described as a place you are on your feet in, not a favour you keep applying for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The overflow"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **our joy overflows in the hope of the glory of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is to boast, to exult."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the object is *the glory of God* — the very thing chapter 3 said everybody had fallen short of. Paul turns the deficit into the expectation, and the distance between the two is what the first four chapters were for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not in the sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Any condition, any maintenance, any qualification. Justified, at peace, brought in, standing, and exulting — five settled things in two verses, and every one of them arrives through the same Person."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What comes immediately after.** *And not only that, but our joy holds even in our sufferings.* Paul establishes the ground before he mentions the pressure, and the pressure never gets a vote on any of the five. See the note on Romans 5:3–5."}]}, "Romans 5:12–14": {"title": "Two men and two acts", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul traces everything back to one man and then names who the man was a picture of."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sin made its way into the world **through one man**, and **death came in on sin's heels**, and so death came through to everyone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three steps: one man, sin enters, death follows, and it reaches everybody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *made its way in* is to enter alongside — sin came in as a gate-crasher."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proof he offers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sin was in the world **before the law was given**, but **sin is not charged to anyone's account where there is no law**. **Yet death reigned from Adam to Moses**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is Paul's argument and it is worth following slowly."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Between Adam and Sinai there was no written law. On his own accounting, nothing could be formally charged to anybody's account in that period."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And people died anyway, for two and a half thousand years."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So death was not being handed out for individual breaches of a code. Something else was in operation, and Paul says what it was: one man's act, reaching everybody."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Even over those who had not sinned in the same way Adam disobeyed\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Adam broke a stated command with a stated penalty. Nobody between him and Moses was in that position, and they died too."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **Adam is a pattern of the One who was coming**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **typos** — a stamp, an impression, a die."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Adam is described as the mould, and Christ as what it was made for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the comparison goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rest of the chapter runs it out: *as by one man's disobedience many were made sinners, so also **by one Man's obedience many will be made righteous**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mechanism is the same in both directions. Adam's act reached people who were not consulted; Paul says Christ's works the same way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the whole argument, and it is why he needed the Adam-to-Moses point first — to establish that an act of one man can be credited to people who did not perform it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Much more\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Five times in this chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul never argues that grace merely balances the damage. Every comparison is weighted: *much more the grace of God and the gift by the grace of the one Man, Jesus Christ, abounded to many.* See the note on Romans 5:15–17."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where sin increased, grace overflowed all the more.* See the note on Romans 5:20–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the objection Paul knows is coming: *shall we keep on sinning, so that there will be all the more grace?*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The one word the chapter turns on.** *Reigned.* Death reigned from Adam to Moses; sin reigned in death; and then *those who receive abundance of grace and the gift of righteousness **will reign in life** through the One, Jesus Christ.*"}]}, "Romans 5:15–17": {"title": "\"Reign in life\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul compares Adam and Christ and then insists the comparison does not balance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Not two of a kind\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The free gift and the trespass are not two of a kind.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just drawn the parallel and now he breaks it. The two sides look symmetrical and are not — the second is larger, and he says so three times with *much more*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the asymmetry is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sharpest line is the middle one:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The judgment followed a **single** trespass and brought condemnation, but the free gift followed **many** trespasses and brought a verdict of righteousness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One sin was enough to bring the verdict down. The gift had to deal with an accumulation — every trespass since — and it still came out the other side with an acquittal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The switch nobody expects"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If by the trespass of the one man **death reigned**… much more will those who receive the overflow of grace and the gift of being put right with God **reign in life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The symmetry would require: death reigned, so life will reign. Paul does not write that. He says **they** will reign — the people, not the abstraction."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Death is a monarch in his sentence. Grace does not merely replace it with a better monarch; it puts the formerly condemned on the throne."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Those who receive\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one active verb on the human side of the passage. Not those who achieve or maintain — those who **receive**, and what they receive is described twice as overflowing and as a gift."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What \"in life\" means.** Not after it. Paul says reign *in life* — the sphere is this one. The whole argument since chapter 3 has been that the standing is given now, which is why he can put the reigning in the present tense of a Christian's existence."}]}, "Romans 5:18–19": {"title": "One man, and then one Man", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul's argument here runs on a single comparison, and everything depends on the two sides being the same shape."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parallel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "| through one man | through the One | |---|---| | one trespass | one act of righteousness | | condemnation to everyone | the setting-right that is life | | the many were **made sinners** | the many will be **made righteous** |"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same verb sits on both sides — *kathistemi*, to appoint, constitute, place into a category. Not treated as. **Made.**"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the symmetry matters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody chose to be made a sinner by Adam's act. It arrived by connection, not by decision, and the effects were real long before anyone understood them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul insists the second half works identically. The righteousness comes by the obedience of the One, into a group described in exactly the same terms. If the first transfer was real, so is the second — that is the whole force of the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"One act of righteousness\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is singular and concrete: one righteous deed. Paul does not point at Christ's whole life of obedience in general but at a single act, and in this letter that is the cross."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Against one trespass, one obedience. The books are balanced by matching pieces."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So also through the obedience of the One the many will be made righteous.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The righteousness on offer is not a reward for the reader's obedience. It comes by *His* obedience, credited across the same way the trespass was."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The verse that follows.** Paul says the law came in so that the trespass would increase — *but where sin increased, grace increased all the more.* He is not trying to make sin look small. He builds it as high as it goes and then says grace went higher."}]}, "Romans 5:20–21": {"title": "\"Grace overflowed all the more\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul says what the law was for, and it is not what most people assume."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it came"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The law **came in alongside**, so that **the trespass would increase**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *came in alongside* is **pareiserchomai** — to slip in beside, to enter as a secondary thing. It is not the main line of the argument; it comes in next to it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the purpose clause is startling: *so that the trespass would increase.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is Paul's own account of what the law was given to do. Not to reduce sin — to make it abound, to bring it out into the open where it could be seen for what it is. He says the same thing in chapter 7: *sin, taking opportunity by the commandment, produced in me all manner of evil desire.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But where sin increased, **grace overflowed all the more**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **huperperisseuo** — Paul stacks *huper* (over, beyond) on top of a word that already means to abound. Superabounded. He appears to have built the word for this sentence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever height sin reached, grace is described as clearing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two reigns"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as **sin reigned in death**, **grace might reign through righteousness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both verbs are the same — to reign as a king. Paul is describing two thrones, and only one of them can be occupied."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sin's reign operated *in death*. Grace's reign operates *through righteousness*, and its outcome is *eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note what grace reigns **through**. Not through leniency, and not by overlooking anything. Through righteousness — which had to be supplied, and was."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question this provokes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul knows exactly what the next objection is, and he puts it himself at the top of the very next paragraph:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What shall we say, then? Shall we keep on sinning, so that there will be all the more grace?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody asks that question of a message about trying harder. It is the question grace always draws, and the fact that Paul has to answer it is a sign he had preached grace the way he says he did."}, {"k": "p", "t": "His answer is not a rule. It is a funeral: *we died to sin; how can we go on living in it any longer?* See the note on Romans 6:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter has been"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole of Romans 5 is two men — *by one man's disobedience many were made sinners, so also by one Man's obedience many will be made righteous.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Adam's act reached everyone born after him without their doing anything. Paul's argument is that Christ's act works the same way, and *much more*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The phrase he keeps using.** *Much more.* Five times in this chapter. Paul is not arguing that grace matches what Adam did. He argues at every step that it exceeds it."}]}, "Romans 5:3–5": {"title": "The chain that ends in hope", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Four links, and the last one is the point of the sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it starts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our joy holds **even in our sufferings**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *sufferings* is **thlipsis** — pressure, being squeezed. It is a word about weight from outside."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not say the pressure is good, or sent, or enjoyable. He says the joy holds inside it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The chain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Suffering works **endurance** into us, and endurance works **a character proved in the testing**, and a character proved that way works **hope**.*"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**endurance** — *hupomone*, remaining under, staying put under weight", "**proven character** — *dokime*, the word for metal that has been through the assay and passed. Tested and found genuine.", "**hope**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chain runs one way and each link produces the next."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Because we know\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **we know** that suffering works endurance into us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is a settled knowledge. What makes the joy possible is not a feeling about the pressure but information about where the pressure leads."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hope that does not embarrass"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And that hope **does not put us to shame**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is that it does not disappoint, does not humiliate. Hope that turns out to be misplaced makes a fool of the person who held it — Paul says this one will not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **the love of God has been poured out and has flooded our hearts**, through the Holy Spirit who was given to us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note what secures the hope. Not the character built in the previous verse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chain of endurance and proven character is real, and it is not the guarantee. The guarantee is love already poured out — the Greek is a flooding, and the tense is completed and standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the word \"hope\" means here"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Elpis** — not wishing. It is confident expectation of something good, and it is the New Testament's normal word for what a Christian has about the future."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul defines the object of it in the verse before: *our joy overflows in **the hope of the glory of God**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the paragraph is anchored"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 1: *since we have been put right with God through faith, **we are at peace with God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 2: *through Him we have also been brought in, by faith, **to this grace in which we now stand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The suffering paragraph sits on top of a settled peace and a standing position, and Paul establishes both before he mentions pressure."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what comes three verses later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While we were still sinners, Christ died for us.* See the note on Romans 5:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The proof of the love that floods the heart is dated to the cross, not to how the suffering turns out."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Paul had been through by the time he wrote this.** Stoned and left for dead at Lystra, run out of three cities, beaten with rods at Philippi. The chain in these verses is not theory he arrived at from a desk."}]}, "Romans 5:6–8": {"title": "Love proved at the cross", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul dates God's love by when it acted, and the timing is the whole argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While we were **still without strength**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **asthenes** — weak, powerless, sick. It is the word used of invalids in the Gospels."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *while we were doing our best*. Without strength."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*At **just the right time**, Christ died for the ungodly.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *according to the season* — at the appointed moment, on schedule."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing about it was a rescue improvised when things got bad enough. It happened at the hour it was set for."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the ungodly**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul chooses the strongest word he has. Not the struggling, not the sincere. The ungodly — those without reverence for God at all."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the same word in chapter 4 about the God *who justifies the ungodly*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The human comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is a rare thing for anyone to die even for **a righteous person**, though for **a good person** someone might possibly dare to die.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is careful here, and slightly wry. For a merely upright man — the sort you respect and do not warm to — almost nobody would. For a genuinely good man, someone might just about work up the nerve."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the top of what human love manages, and Paul says even that is uncommon."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The contrast"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But God **puts His own love for us on display** in this: **while we were still sinners**, Christ died for us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb is present tense and continuous — He goes on demonstrating it. The cross is described as an exhibit that stays up."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While we were still sinners.* Not after we repented, not once we improved. Still."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the demonstration is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is making a case about certainty. The verses either side are about hope:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Hope does not disappoint, because the love of God has been poured out in our hearts by the Holy Spirit.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And after: *much more then, having now been justified by His blood, **we shall be saved from wrath through Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument runs from the harder thing to the easier. If He did that for enemies, the rest follows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase Paul keeps using"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Much more.* Five times in this chapter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He never argues that grace merely matches the damage. At every step he says it exceeds it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the cross settles about God's disposition.** Not that He loves us now that something has changed — that He loved us when nothing had. The demonstration is dated to the worst possible moment on purpose, so that no later moment can be worse."}]}, "Romans 5:9–11": {"title": "\"How much more\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul builds an argument twice, in the same shape, and the shape is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The form"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If, while we were enemies, we were reconciled to God through the death of His Son, **how much more**, now that we have been reconciled, shall we be saved by His life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is an argument from the harder case to the easier one. If the difficult thing has already been done under the worst conditions, the lesser thing is not in doubt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses it twice in three verses, and it runs all through this chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the harder case was"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While we were enemies.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has already said it two verses earlier — *while we were still sinners, Christ died for us* — and he keeps escalating the description: weak, ungodly, sinners, enemies."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reconciliation happened at the worst point of the relationship. That is the foundation the whole argument stands on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Saved by His life\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The contrast is deliberate. The reconciling was accomplished by His **death**; the saving that follows is by His **life**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not dead now, and Paul treats that as the guarantee of everything still to come. Hebrews makes the same move — *He always lives to intercede for them.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The odd word at the end"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We rejoice in God through our Lord Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is the boasting word. Paul has spent two chapters demolishing every ground for human boasting, and then permits exactly one: boasting **in God**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in the salvation, or the security, or the change in circumstances. In Him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What has already been said in this chapter.** *Since we have been put right by faith, we have peace with God… and we stand in this grace.* Standing is the posture Paul assigns to a person who has received it — which is why the *how much more* argument can be made at all."}]}, "Romans 6:1–2": {"title": "\"Shall we go on sinning?\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks the question his own preaching provokes, and the fact that he has to ask it tells you what he had been preaching."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Shall we **keep on sinning, so that there will be all the more grace**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just written *where sin increased, grace overflowed all the more.* This is the obvious next move, and Paul makes it himself before anyone else can."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody ever asks this question of a message about behaving better. It only arises if the grace preached was extravagant enough to sound dangerous."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is accused of it elsewhere too: *why not say — as we are slanderously reported and as some claim that we say — \"Let us do evil that good may come\"?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the charge, and Paul reports that people were making it about him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Certainly not.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me genoito** — the strongest negative available, roughly *may it never be*. Paul uses it ten times in Romans."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the answer is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not say *because you will lose your salvation*. He does not say *because God will punish you*. He does not reintroduce a rule, or a threat, or a condition."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks a different question back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason he gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We **died to sin**; how can we go on living in it any longer?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *we should die to sin*. Not *we are dying to sin daily*. Past tense, done."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument is about impossibility of a sort — the absurdity of a dead man continuing to keep house in the place he was carried out of."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul spends the rest of the chapter on that one fact: buried with Him, raised with Him, the old man crucified, freed from sin. And only after all of it does an instruction appear — *count yourselves dead indeed to sin, but alive to God.* See the note on Romans 6:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reckoning comes after the reality, and is based on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which way round the chapter runs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every command in Romans 6 arrives after a statement of what is already true. Paul never asks anyone to die to sin in order to become dead to it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he says at the end of the chapter.** *For sin shall not have dominion over you, **for you are not under law but under grace**.* The reason given for sin not ruling is the very thing the objector thought would let it rule."}]}, "Romans 6:12–14": {"title": "\"Sin shall not be your master\" — a promise, not a warning", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Almost everyone reads this backwards, and the grammar is what gives it away."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb tense nobody points out"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For sin shall not be your master, because you are not under law but under grace.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In Greek that is a **future indicative** — a plain statement of what will be the case. It is not an imperative. Paul is not commanding *do not let sin master you*; he already gave that instruction two verses earlier, and he used a different form to do it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So the sentence is not a rule with a penalty. It is a promise, and it comes with a reason attached."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason is the whole point"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Look at what he gives as the ground of the promise:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["not *because you are trying hard*", "not *because you have dealt with that sin*", "**because you are not under law but under grace**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which means Paul is claiming something most people find hard to believe: being under grace is what breaks sin's power, and being under law is what leaves it in place. He says it plainly enough that the next verse has him answering the obvious objection — *shall we sin because we are under grace?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That objection only makes sense if he really did say what he seems to have said. Nobody raises it against a preacher of rules."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for \"master\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Kurieuo* — the verb from *kurios*, lord. To have lordship over, to be the one whose word settles things. Paul is not talking about whether a believer ever sins. He is talking about who is in charge."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That distinction matters when the verse is read by someone who has just failed. The sentence does not say sin will never touch you. It says sin will not be the one giving orders — and it locates the reason for that entirely outside their performance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it asks of the reader"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The instruction in the two verses before this one is worth reading in the same breath: *hand yourselves over to God as people brought from death to life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *become* people brought from death to life. As people who already are. Everything Paul asks here is asked on the basis of something he says has already happened, and the promise at the end is the reason he can ask it at all."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"under grace.\"** It is a position, not a mood — the same construction as *under law*. Paul is describing which of two authorities a person lives under, and the entire argument of chapters 5 to 8 turns on the claim that a Christian has changed jurisdictions. That is why the promise can be stated as fact."}]}, "Romans 6:15–16": {"title": "Slaves of the one you obey", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The same question as at the start of the chapter, asked again with one word changed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two questions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 1: *shall we continue in sin **that grace may abound**?* See the note on Romans 6:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 15: *shall we sin **because we are not under law but under grace**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first is about the purpose. The second is about the permission."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul answers both, and the answers are different."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the second one comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse immediately before it: *for **sin shall not have dominion over you**, for you are not under law but under grace*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He makes a promise, and then asks the question the promise invites."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Certainly not.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Me genoito** — the strongest negative in his vocabulary, and the tenth of the fourteen times he uses it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Do you not know that when you **hand yourselves over to someone as obedient slaves, you are the slaves of the one you obey**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paristanete** — to present, to place at someone's disposal. The same verb he has just used in verse 13: *present yourselves to God*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The argument is about what obedience does to a person, not about what is permitted."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two options"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Either **sin, with death at the end of it**, or **obedience, with righteousness at the end of it**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *sin unto death, or obedience unto righteousness*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "There are two, and the question of which master is not on the table — only which one is being obeyed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is not offered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Freedom in the abstract. Nobody in the passage is unowned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's whole argument for six chapters has been that a person is in Adam or in Christ, under law or under grace, a slave of sin or a slave of righteousness."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The apology"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I speak in human terms because of the weakness of your flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 19, and Paul flags the metaphor as imperfect. See the note on Romans 6:17–19."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is uncomfortable calling it slavery, and uses it anyway because nothing else conveys that the ownership is total."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The asymmetry"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two slaveries are not equivalent, and the chapter says so at the end: *the wages of sin is death, but **the gift of God is eternal life***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One pays wages. The other gives a gift. Paul breaks his own parallel deliberately in the last verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has already been established"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Knowing this, that our old man was crucified with Him… for **he who has died has been freed from sin**.* See the note on Romans 6:6–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The freedom is stated as past before the question about permission is asked."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not warning them that sinning will re-enslave them. He is describing what obedience reveals about who is being served."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tense of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first half of the chapter is indicative — this is what happened to you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The second half is imperative — therefore present yourselves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 11 is the hinge: *reckon yourselves to be dead indeed to sin*. The command is to count the fact as a fact."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But now **having been set free from sin, and having become slaves of God**, you have your fruit to holiness, and the end, everlasting life.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two perfect participles. Both already done to them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the question is really about.** Not whether it is allowed. Paul answers a question about permission with a question about ownership — you belong to whichever one you are obeying."}]}, "Romans 6:17–19": {"title": "The mould they were poured into", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul thanks God for something and the sentence starts with what they used to be."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The thanks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **thanks be to God** that, **though you were slaves of sin**, you **obeyed from the heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **charis to theo** — grace to God."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He thanks God for their obedience, which locates the credit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "From the heart"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ek kardias** — out of the heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not compliance. Paul specifies where the obedience came from before he says what it was directed at."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The odd phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **pattern of teaching into whose care you were handed**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **tupon didaches eis hon paredothete** — the form of teaching to which you were delivered."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It should be the other way round. Teaching is delivered to people."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul reverses it: the people were handed over to the teaching."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What *tupos* is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A mould, a die, the shape a thing is struck into. The word behind *type*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something molten is poured in and comes out the shape of the mould."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same word of Adam — *a type of Him who was to come* — and of the wilderness events: *these things became our examples*. See the note on 1 Corinthians 10:11–13."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Having been **set free from sin**, you became **slaves of righteousness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eleutherothentes** — an aorist passive participle. Having been freed, once, by someone else."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then immediately enslaved again, to something else. Paul will not leave anybody unattached."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The apology"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am speaking in **ordinary human terms**, because the old nature in you is weak.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **anthropinon lego** — I speak humanly. And *because of the weakness of your flesh*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is uneasy with the slavery image. It says the ownership is total, which is what he wants, and it says the service is grudging, which is not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So he flags it as a concession to their capacity and keeps using it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The parallel"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Just as you once **handed over the parts of yourself as slaves to impurity and to lawlessness**… so now **hand them over as slaves to righteousness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ta mele** — the members, the limbs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same verb both halves, same objects. The instruction is to do with the body exactly what they used to do with it, aimed elsewhere."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The runaway clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Lawlessness **that only bred more lawlessness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *lawlessness unto lawlessness* — **anomian eis ten anomian**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first road has no destination. It circles back into itself and produces more of the same."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other road's destination"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Which leads to **being made holy**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **eis hagiasmon** — unto sanctification."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One direction arrives somewhere. The other does not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wage and the gift"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the wages of sin is death**, but the **gift of God is eternal life** in Christ Jesus our Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **opsonia** — a soldier's pay, the rations and coin he has earned."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And **charisma** — a free gift."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has run the two slaveries in exact parallel for four verses, and then breaks the parallel in the last line of the chapter. One pays what is owed. The other gives what is not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has been established before this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The old man was crucified with Him… he who has died has been freed from sin.* See the note on Romans 6:6–7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *reckon yourselves to be dead indeed to sin, but alive to God in Christ Jesus*. See the note on Romans 6:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every instruction in this chapter comes after those statements and rests on them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The direction the delivery runs.** Not the teaching handed to them. *You were handed over to the teaching* — and the word for teaching is the word for a mould."}]}, "Romans 6:22–23": {"title": "The wage and the gift", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two nouns, and the whole difference is in which of them you are owed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sin **pays a wage**, and the wage is death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **opsonia** — a soldier's pay, the rations and coin issued at the end of a stint. It is what a man has earned and can demand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody is given death. It is drawn."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gift"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God **gives a gift**, and the gift is life that has no end.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **charisma**, from **charis** — grace. A thing handed over free, with no work behind it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul deliberately does not use the same word twice. He could have written *the wages of sin is death, and the wages of God is life*, and the sentence would have collapsed. The two halves are not the same kind of transaction."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind of life"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Zoe aionios** — and it is not primarily about duration. The New Testament's word for endless time is doing something else here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus defines it in John 17: *this is eternal life, that they may know You, the only true God, and Jesus Christ whom You have sent.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is God's own kind of life, given to a man, and the fact that it does not run out is one of its properties rather than its definition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In Christ Jesus\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last three words locate it. The gift is not posted separately; it is in a Person, and it comes with Him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Having been **set free from sin** and made slaves of God, you have your fruit, and it leads to being made holy.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the state Paul is describing the gift arriving into. Freed first, and then the fruit follows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *made slaves of God* is the strong word — bondservants. Paul's picture is not neutrality; a man is owned either way, and the question is by whom."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the chapter exists"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 6 opens with the objection: *shall we continue in sin that grace may abound?* — which is the question grace always provokes, and Paul's answer is not to add rules."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a funeral: *we died to sin; how shall we live any longer in it?* You cannot go back to a house you were carried out of."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The most quoted half.** *The wages of sin is death* is preached far more often than the clause after it, and the clause after it is where the sentence is going. Paul wrote one sentence with a *but* in the middle."}]}, "Romans 6:6–7": {"title": "The old self crucified", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul says it is something they already know."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We **know this**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ginoskontes** — knowing. He is not introducing information; he is reminding them of the ground of an argument."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 6 is full of it: *do you not know*, *knowing this*, *we know*. Five times in the chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The event"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **person we used to be was crucified with Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sunestaurothe** — co-crucified. One word, aorist passive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Aorist: a point in the past. Passive: done to him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same compound of himself in Galatians: *I have been crucified with Christ; it is no longer I who live*. See the note on Galatians 2:19–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the old self is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The person we used to be* — the Greek is **ho palaios hemon anthropos**, our old man."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the sinful nature as a component. The whole former person, the one who was in Adam."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's other uses run the same way: *put off the old man with his deeds*, and *put off, concerning your former conduct, the old man*. See the note on Ephesians 4:20–24."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **the body sin had ruled might be left with no power at all**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katargethe** — rendered inoperative, put out of employment. The same verb Paul uses of the law and of death itself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not destroyed. Unemployed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The body is still there. What is gone is its position as the place sin gives orders from."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And we might **no longer be slaves to sin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **douleuein** — to serve as a slave."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Slavery is the metaphor for the whole chapter, and Paul apologises for it in verse 19: *I speak in human terms because of the weakness of your flesh*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The legal principle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **anyone who has died has been set free from sin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dedikaiotai** — has been justified. The word Paul has been using for five chapters about righteousness, used here of a corpse."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A dead man has no further liability. The claims against him do not follow him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the chapter is answering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Shall we continue in sin that grace may abound?* See the note on Romans 6:1–2."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer is not a threat. It is a description: *how shall we who died to sin live any longer in it?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul answers a question about behaviour with a statement about a death that has already happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two orders"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Knowing that Christ, having been raised from the dead, **dies no more. Death no longer has dominion over Him**… likewise you also, **reckon yourselves to be dead indeed to sin, but alive to God**.* See the note on Romans 6:8–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *reckon* is **logizesthe** — an accounting word. Count it, enter it in the book as true."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is an imperative, and it is the first imperative in the chapter. Five verses of what happened, and then the instruction is to treat it as a fact."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the instructions start"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore do not let sin reign in your mortal body… **do not present your members as instruments of unrighteousness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Verse 12. And the reason given, in verse 14: *for **sin shall not have dominion over you**, for you are not under law but under grace.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The command in verse 12 rests on the promise in verse 14, and not the other way round."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What was buried"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore we were buried with Him through baptism into death, that just as Christ was raised from the dead by the glory of the Father, even so we also should **walk in newness of life**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Died, buried, raised — Paul runs the whole sequence through the reader."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What happened to the body.** Not destroyed. The Greek says put out of work — sin's employer status revoked, and the man himself listed among the dead, which is why the claims stop."}]}, "Romans 6:8–11": {"title": "\"Once for all and never again\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul makes his case about the believer by first making it about Christ."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is true of Him"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Christ, having been raised from the dead, **dies no more**; **death no longer has any mastery over Him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two negatives, both permanent. Whatever death had a claim on, that claim is finished."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The death He died"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The death He died was **a death to sin, died once for all and never again**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ephapax** — once, once and for all, decisively. Hebrews uses the same word repeatedly: *once for all He offered up Himself*, *by one offering He has perfected forever*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He did not die to sin repeatedly, and there is no arrangement by which He does it again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The life He lives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the life He lives is a life lived to God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Present tense. That is what He is doing now."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In the same way\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In the same way, **count yourselves** dead indeed to sin, but alive to God through Jesus Christ our Lord.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything said about Him in the three verses before is what the reader is told to reckon about himself. The pattern is not aspirational — it is the same set of facts, applied."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Count\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **logizomai** — the bookkeeping word. To reckon, to enter into the account, to regard as so."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It does not mean to pretend, and it does not manufacture the fact. An accountant reckons what is already in the ledger. Paul has spent seven verses putting the figures there before asking anyone to add them up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the reckoning sits in the chapter"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul's order through Romans 6 is fixed and never reverses:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "1. *we died to sin* — stated 2. *buried with Him… raised* — stated 3. *our old man was crucified with Him* — stated 4. *Christ dies no more* — stated 5. **then**: *count yourselves dead indeed to sin* 6. **then**: *do not let sin reign in your mortal body*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The command in verse 12 is the first instruction in the chapter, and it arrives eleven verses after the facts it rests on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Through Jesus Christ our Lord\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last phrase locates it. Not dead to sin in yourself, examined on your own terms — dead to sin *in Him*, which is the position the whole chapter has been describing."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What \"dead to sin\" does not mean.** Not that sin has stopped existing or knocking. Paul says *do not let it reign* in the very next verse, which would be a strange thing to say to someone it could no longer approach. Dead to sin is about jurisdiction — who has a claim — not about whether the old master still calls round."}]}, "Romans 7:1–3": {"title": "A marriage ended by death", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul stops and makes sure of one legal principle before he builds anything on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The law has authority over a person **only for as long as that person is alive**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it flatly, and he says it to people who *know how law works* — Jewish readers in Rome who would grant the point without argument."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A law can speak to the living. It has nothing to say to a corpse. No court prosecutes a dead man, and no statute binds one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The illustration"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By law she is bound to her husband while he lives; but if her husband dies, she is released from the law that bound her to him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law that bound her did not change. It was not repealed, softened, or amended. It is exactly as binding as it was."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She simply is no longer inside its jurisdiction, because a death occurred."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"No adulteress at all\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She is **no adulteress at all** if she marries another man.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul presses the same case twice — once with the husband living, once with him dead — so that nobody can mistake which fact does the work."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same act is a crime in one paragraph and blameless in the next. Nothing about the woman changed. A death changed everything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this is going"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The next verse applies it: *you were made dead to the law through the body of Christ, so that you could belong to Another.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Note who died in Paul's application. Not the law — us. *Through the body of Christ*, which is the phrase he will use again over a table three letters later."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law is still the law, holy and just and good, and Paul says so a few verses on. It simply has no claim on somebody who died and was raised."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Why he pauses to establish this.** Chapter 6 has just been answering *shall we go on sinning that grace may abound?* Paul's answer is not tighter rules. It is a funeral: you died with Him, and what you are joined to now is a Person, not a code. See the note on Romans 7:4–6."}]}, "Romans 7:13": {"title": "Sin shown for what it is", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it answers the second objection in the chapter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **did something good become death to me?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just said the commandment *meant for life* turned out to bring death, and then called the law *holy and just and good*. See the note on Romans 7:9–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two statements sit awkwardly, and Paul knows it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Certainly not.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Me genoito** again — the second time in seven verses, and the tenth in the letter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The culprit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was **sin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One word, and the whole weight of the chapter rests on it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What sin was doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sin **showing itself for exactly what it is**, producing death in me **through what is good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *that it might appear sin*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The commandment was the instrument, and Paul says so plainly — **through what is good**. He does not soften the mechanism to protect the law; he protects the law by naming the other party."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that **through the commandment sin might be exposed as utterly, immeasurably sinful**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **kath' huperbolen hamartolos** — sinful beyond measure, hyperbolically sinful."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul stacks the intensifiers, as he does elsewhere: *far beyond anything we ask*, and *the weight of it was far past anything our strength could carry*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law is for, in this verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Exposure. Not correction, not enablement, and not condemnation of the person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The commandment is described as the thing that makes sin visible in its true size — and Paul says that was the intent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He said it in chapter 5: *the law entered that the offence might abound.* See the note on Romans 5:20–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two words for what the law reveals"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Epignosis** in chapter 3 — full recognition: *by the law is the knowledge of sin*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And here, that it might *appear* — **phane**, be shown, come to light."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul is not arguing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That the law made him worse. He is arguing that it made something already there identifiable."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verse before: *I would not have known covetousness unless the law had said, \"You shall not covet.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **we know that the law is spiritual, but I am carnal, sold under sin**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the long passage about doing what he does not want, and the cry. See the note on Romans 7:18–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the chapter exists"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The writer of it had been *blameless* concerning the law's righteousness by his own account, and describes himself here as a man who found out what was underneath."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the exposure is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in Romans 7 offers a remedy. That is the point of the chapter — it ends *O wretched man that I am! Who will deliver me?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *I thank God — through Jesus Christ our Lord!*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is on trial in the verse.** Not the law. Paul takes the charge, refuses it, names sin instead, and says the exposure was the object — that sin be shown at full size, using something good to do it."}]}, "Romans 7:18–20": {"title": "The most quoted description of failure in the Bible", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "*The good I want to do, I do not do; the evil I have no wish to do is the evil I go on doing.* Anyone who has tried to live a good life recognises the sentence immediately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence it starts from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For I know that **nothing good lives in me — that is, in the old nature**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says it about himself, flatly, and he had more to boast about than most: *circumcised the eighth day, of the tribe of Benjamin, a Hebrew of Hebrews, concerning the law a Pharisee… concerning the righteousness which is in the law, blameless.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That man says there is nothing good in his flesh."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not despair and it is not fishing for reassurance. It is a settled verdict, and it is the most freeing sentence in the chapter — once it is agreed, there is nothing left to defend and nothing left to produce."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it puts a man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job spent thirty chapters building a case for his own righteousness, and God's one charge out of the whirlwind was *would you condemn Me so that you could be in the right?* Two positions, and only one of them is habitable. See the note on Job 40:6–8."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has already taken the other one. A man who has stopped expecting good out of his own flesh can be handed a righteousness that is not his — which is the whole argument of the letter: *the gift of righteousness*, *received*, *apart from works*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The diagnosis"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The **wanting** is present and the **doing** is not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The wanting is right there within reach; the doing of it is not.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is a claim about willpower. The problem being described is not ignorance of what is right or reluctance to want it. It is the gap between the wish and the act — which is exactly the gap the law cannot close, because a rule can tell you what to do and cannot make you do it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter ends in a cry — *who will rescue me?* — and an answer: *thanks be to God, through Jesus Christ our Lord.* And then chapter 8 opens with *there is now no condemnation*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 7 is not a resting place in Paul's argument. It is the wall he builds so that the door in chapter 8 has something to be a door in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What chapter 8 says about the same man"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **you are not living out of the old nature; you are living in the Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul writes it eight verses into the next chapter, about the same people who have just read chapter 7. It is a statement of location, not a description of how the week has gone. See the note on Romans 8:9–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The flesh is still there — Paul never says otherwise — and he says it is not where they are."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter is not.** It is not a description of the normal Christian life. It is the sound of a man trying, in his own strength, to keep something holy — *I delight in the law of God according to the inward man*, and losing every time. Paul writes it to bring you to the end of that, so that the first line of the next chapter can land: *there is therefore now no condemnation to those who are in Christ Jesus.*"}]}, "Romans 7:4–6": {"title": "Released to belong to Another", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul applies the marriage illustration, and the application does not go where the illustration seemed to be heading."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who died"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You were made **dead to the law** through the body of Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In the picture, the husband died and the wife went free. In the application, the one who dies is **you**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not saying the law was abolished. He says the party under obligation was removed from its reach by death — a death that happened *through the body of Christ.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"So that you could belong to Another\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not so that you could belong to nobody. The release is not into independence."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To Him who was raised from the dead* — and the language is marital, carried straight over from the three verses before. The point of the funeral is the wedding."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law had been doing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The sinful passions **that the law kept stirring up** were at work in every part of us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is Paul's most uncomfortable sentence about the law and he states it without softening: the passions were stirred **by** the law."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He explains it later in the chapter — *I would not have known what coveting was if the law had not said, do not covet* — and defends the law itself as holy. The fault is not in the statute. But a prohibition, read by the old nature, works like a lit match."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And what they produced was death.* That is the fruit of the first marriage. The second one bears fruit *for God*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two fruits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the same verb for both — **karpophoreo**, to bear fruit — and the difference is in the preposition:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*fruit **to death*** (verse 5)", "*fruit **to God*** (verse 4)"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither marriage is barren. The question in the passage is not whether there will be fruit but who it goes to."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the fruit-bearing in verse 4 is stated as the purpose of the union, not as a condition of it: *that you should be married to another… **that** we should bear fruit to God*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"That which once held us fast has been put to death\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek verb behind *held us fast* is to hold down, to confine — the word used for a prisoner in custody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Something that had a grip on us was killed, and the release follows from the killing. Nobody wriggled free."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Two ways of serving"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So that we serve in the **newness of the Spirit** and not in the **old way of the written code**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Serving is in both halves. Paul never offers a release from service; he distinguishes what the serving runs on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *written code* is **gramma**, letter — the same contrast he draws in 2 Corinthians 3: *the letter kills, but the Spirit gives life.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the chapter does next.** It gets worse before it gets better — the whole of *what I want to do I do not do*, and *who will deliver me from this body of death?* Then the answer, and then chapter 8: *there is therefore now no condemnation.* Paul takes the long way round on purpose."}]}, "Romans 7:7–8": {"title": "What the commandment uncovered", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul asks the question his argument invites, and defends the law in the same breath as describing what it did to him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Is **the law itself sin**? **Certainly not**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **me genoito** — may it never be."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He has just said the passions were *stirred up by the law* and that believers are *delivered from the law*. Somebody was going to ask."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The concession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*On the contrary, **I would never have recognized sin except through the law**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *I would not have known sin*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *would not have sinned*. Would not have known."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The example"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I would not have known **what wanting-what-is-not-mine was**, if the law had not said, **\"You shall not covet.\"**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tenth commandment. Paul reaches for the only one of the ten that is entirely internal — no court can try it, and nobody else can see it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who said he was *blameless* concerning the righteousness in the law names the commandment that got past his record."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mechanism"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **sin found its opening in that very commandment** and **stirred up in me every kind of craving**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *opening* is **aphorme** — a base of operations, a bridgehead. It was a military term for the place a campaign is launched from. See the note on Romans 7:9–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prohibition became the staging ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dormancy"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Apart from the law, **sin lies there as good as dead**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *sin is dead* — inert, not roused."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is not saying it is absent. He says it is lying still until something wakes it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the paragraph protects"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law, every time. He says it twice more before the chapter turns:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore **the law is holy, and the commandment holy and just and good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *we know that **the law is spiritual**, but I am carnal, sold under sin.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three defences in a dozen verses."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where he puts the fault"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It was **sin — sin showing itself for exactly what it is**.* See the note on Romans 7:13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul repeatedly personifies sin in this chapter as an agent with intentions: it finds an opening, it deceives, it kills, it works."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the parallel in Genesis is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A command, a suggestion about the command, and a death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses **exapatao** for what sin did — *deceived me* — and it is the verb used of the serpent: *the serpent deceived me, and I ate*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For what I am doing, I do not understand… the good that I will to do, I do not do.* See the note on Romans 7:18–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the cry, and then chapter 8: *there is no condemnation now — none at all.* See the note on Romans 8:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says the law does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By the law is **the knowledge of sin**.* See the note on Romans 3:19–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same conclusion, stated flatly in the earlier chapter and worked out autobiographically here."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two things held together.** The commandment is holy, and it is what sin used as a bridgehead. Paul states both and puts the blame on the second party — not on the statute, and not on the giver of it."}]}, "Romans 7:9–12": {"title": "When the commandment came", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul tells the story of his own encounter with one commandment, and the ending is a defence of it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There was a time when I was **alive apart from the law**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *I was alive without the law once*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not describing innocence. He is describing a period when the commandment had not yet landed on him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The arrival"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the commandment came**, and **sin sprang to life**, and **I died**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three clauses in sequence, and the middle one is the surprise."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *sprang to life* is **anezesen** — revived, came back to life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sin is described as the thing that woke up. Paul does not say the commandment created it; he says it was there and dormant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Which commandment"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He names it two verses earlier: *I would not have known covetousness unless the law had said, \"**You shall not covet**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tenth. The only one of the ten that is entirely internal — nobody can see it, and no court can try it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reversal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The very commandment that was **meant for life** turned out, **in my case**, to bring death.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the commandment which was unto life, this I found to be unto death*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Leviticus had said it: *you shall therefore keep My statutes and My judgments, **which if a man does, he shall live by them**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The offer was life. Paul reports the outcome in his own case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mechanism"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For sin **found its opening in the commandment**, **deceived me**, and through it **killed me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *opening* is **aphorme** — a base of operations, the place an expedition starts from. It was a military word for a bridgehead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Sin used the prohibition as its launching point."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Deceived me** — **exapatesen**, thoroughly deceived. It is the word used of the serpent in the garden: *the serpent deceived me, and I ate.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The pattern is the same. A command, a suggestion about it, and a death."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So **the law is holy**, and **the commandment is holy and right and good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just said it killed him, and this is his conclusion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three adjectives, and the whole point of the paragraph is that none of them is being withdrawn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it again in the next verse: *has then what is good become death to me? Certainly not! But **sin**, that it might appear sin, was producing death in me through what is good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fault is located and it is not in the statute."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For what I am doing, I do not understand… the good that I will to do, I do not do.* See the note on Romans 7:18–20."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the cry: *O wretched man that I am! Who will deliver me?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then chapter 8: *there is no condemnation now — none at all.* See the note on Romans 8:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul had been before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Concerning the righteousness which is in the law, blameless.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who says *I died* had a perfect external record."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the paragraph protects.** The law. Paul describes it doing him lethal damage and then spends the last verse insisting it is holy, right and good — and puts the blame on what used it as a bridgehead."}]}, "Romans 8:1–4": {"title": "\"None at all\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The sentence that follows the worst chapter in Paul's letters."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Romans 7 ends *O wretched man that I am! Who will deliver me from this body of death?* — and then *I thank God — through Jesus Christ our Lord!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 8 opens on the next line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So then, **there is no condemnation now — none at all** — for those who are in Christ Jesus.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katakrima** — the sentence passed after a verdict. Not the accusation and not the trial. The punishment handed down."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Now\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **nun** — at this present moment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not eventually, and not at the judgment. Now, while the struggle described in chapter 7 is still going on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For those who are **in Christ Jesus**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The location is the qualification. Paul's whole letter turns on the phrase, and it does not describe a standard of behaviour."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two laws"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For in Christ Jesus **another law is at work** — **the law of the Spirit, the law of life** — and that law has **set me free from the law of sin and death**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the law repealed. A second one operating, and the stronger of the two winning."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *set free* is aorist — it happened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the law could not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What **the law could not accomplish**, **weakened as it was by the old nature**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul locates the failure precisely, and it is not in the law. He has already said *the law is holy, and the commandment holy and just and good.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The weakness is in the material it was working on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What God did instead"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*God accomplished Himself: **He sent His own Son in the likeness of that sin-claimed humanity**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *in the likeness of sinful flesh* — **homoioma**. Paul chooses the word carefully: real humanity, and not sinful."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentencing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And there, **dealing with sin, He passed sentence on sin right where sin had made its home**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *He condemned sin in the flesh* — the same root as the **condemnation** in verse 1."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The condemnation that is not on the believer went somewhere. Paul says where."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He did this so that **everything the law rightly asks might be fully met in us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *the righteous requirement of the law might be fulfilled **in** us* — passive. Not *by* us."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The walk"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who **walk not according to the old nature but according to the Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It appears twice, at the end of verse 1 and again at the end of verse 4, framing the paragraph."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Neither death nor life, neither angels nor rulers nor powers… has the strength to cut us off from the love of God.* See the note on Romans 8:38–39."}, {"k": "p", "t": "No condemnation at the top, no separation at the bottom, and the whole chapter in between."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What has changed between the chapters.** Nothing about the man. Chapter 7's *what I hate, that I do* is not resolved by improvement — Paul answers it with a location, and then says there is no sentence there."}]}, "Romans 8:12–14": {"title": "A debt Paul says you do not owe", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul opens with a strange half-sentence, and the strangeness is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence he does not finish"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We are in debt — but not to the old nature, as though we owed it a life lived on its terms.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says who they are **not** indebted to, and then never says who they are. The sentence is left open."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read what he has just spent eleven verses on and the missing half is obvious: if anyone is owed, it is the God who did what the law could not. Paul leaves the reader to finish it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What \"putting to death\" is not"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If by the Spirit you go on putting the body's doings to death, you will live.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things in the grammar keep this from becoming grim self-effort. It is continuous — *go on* doing it, not a single crisis. And it is **by the Spirit**, which names who is doing the work through them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has already ruled out the alternative at length: the law could not manage it, and neither could he, as chapter 7 records in painful detail."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The line that follows.** *All who are led by the Spirit of God are the sons of God* — and then Paul goes immediately to adoption, Abba and inheritance. He raises the leading in order to prove sonship, not to test it. See the note on Romans 8:15–17, which is the same argument continuing."}]}, "Romans 8:15–17": {"title": "Abba, and the word Paul made up", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two words in this passage are worth stopping on. One is Aramaic, and one Paul appears to have coined himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word Paul built"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit who places you as a son with full standing* — behind that phrase is one Greek word, **huiothesia**. It is not a normal word. Paul takes the word for son and the word for placing and puts them together, and it appears almost nowhere in Greek literature before he uses it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It describes the Roman legal act of adoption, which was not what we mean by the word. A Roman adoption transferred a person into a new family completely: old debts cancelled, old obligations void, full inheritance rights, and it could not be undone. It was more permanent than being born into a family, because a natural son could be disinherited and an adopted one could not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the standing Paul says the Spirit gives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word Jesus used"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*By Him we cry out, Abba, Father.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Abba* is Aramaic, not Greek — and Paul is writing to Rome, where hardly anyone spoke it. He leaves it untranslated anyway, then puts the Greek beside it. He does the same in Galatians. It is the word Jesus used in Gethsemane, and it survived into Greek-speaking churches as a word people kept saying."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not baby-talk, as it is sometimes described. It is family speech — the word a grown son would use of his own father, in the language Jesus prayed in."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is set against"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You did not receive a spirit that makes you a slave again, dragging you back into fear.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the alternative Paul rules out, and notice what he calls it: fear, and slavery, and *again* — a return to something. He is describing a relationship run on anxiety about standing, and he says flatly that this is not what was given."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The evidence he offers is not a feeling. It is the cry itself: the fact that a person calls God Father at all is presented as the Spirit's own testimony, witnessing *together with* our spirit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line about suffering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If indeed we suffer with Him, so that we may also be glorified with Him.* This reads to many people like a condition attached at the end that could void the whole thing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "But the sentence it belongs to is about inheritance — heirs of God, heirs together with Christ. Paul's point is that being a co-heir means sharing what Christ has, and what Christ has includes both a road that went through suffering and a glory on the far side of it. It is a description of the inheritance, not an entrance fee for it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"led by the Spirit\" in the verse before.** The passage just above says all who are led by the Spirit are sons of God — and it is often read as a test of whether someone qualifies. Paul's next sentence is this one, and it goes straight to adoption, Abba and inheritance. He raises leading in order to say what it proves about a person's standing, not to make them anxious about whether they are being led well enough."}]}, "Romans 8:26–27": {"title": "The Spirit prays within us", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul admits an inability and then says who covers it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"In the same way\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **hosautos** — likewise, in the same manner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It connects to the verses before, about creation groaning and about waiting *with perseverance* for what we do not see."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three groans in the paragraph: creation groans, we groan, and the Spirit groans."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What He does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The Spirit also **comes alongside our weaknesses and takes hold with us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **sunantilambanetai** — a triple compound: *sun* (with) + *anti* (opposite) + *lambano* (take hold)."}, {"k": "p", "t": "To take hold of a load together with somebody, from the other end."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word occurs twice in the New Testament. The other is Martha's complaint: *tell her to **help** me.* See the note on Luke 10:38–42."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The weakness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our **weaknesses**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **astheneia** — the general word for infirmity, used of illness and of any lack of strength."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says it about the readers and includes himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The specific failure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*We **do not know what we ought to pray for**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not that we do not know how. That we do not know **what** — the Greek is *what we should pray as we ought*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul, who wrote the prayers in Ephesians and Colossians, says this about himself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The intervention"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **the Spirit Himself steps in and pleads for us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **huperentunchanei** — to intercede on behalf of. Another compound, and another word that appears once in the New Testament."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The manner"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **groans too deep to be put into words**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **stenagmois alaletois** — unspoken, unutterable groanings."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who hears it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the One who searches our hearts knows the Spirit's mind**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Father searches the heart, and what He finds there is the Spirit's own mind."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek for *searches* is **eraunon** — to examine, to investigate. The same word John uses of the risen Christ in Revelation: *I am He who searches the minds and hearts.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why it lands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because the Spirit **intercedes for God's holy people according to the will of God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *according to God* — in line with Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two of the three persons are involved on the reader's behalf, and Paul says the third is listening."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other intercession"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight verses on, in the same chapter: *it is Christ who died, and furthermore is also risen, who is even at the right hand of God, **who also makes intercession for us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "One inside, one at the right hand."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hebrews puts it the same way: *He always lives to make intercession for them.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And we know that **all things work together for good** to those who love God.* See the note on Romans 8:28–30."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The famous verse follows directly from the two about not knowing what to pray for. Paul puts the ignorance first and the confidence second."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is conceded.** That we do not know what to ask for. Paul does not treat it as a failure to be corrected — he names it and then says who takes the other end of the load."}]}, "Romans 8:28–30": {"title": "What the \"good\" in \"works together for good\" is", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "This is the most quoted sentence in the letter and the most often quoted half-sentence in the Bible. Almost nobody reads the next line, and the next line is where Paul says what he means by *good*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "He defines his own term"}, {"k": "note", "t": "everything, all of it together, is working toward good … **for** those God knew beforehand, He also marked out beforehand **to be shaped into the likeness of His Son**"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The *for* is doing the work of a definition. The good being worked toward is not a comfortable outcome, a resolved situation, or a happy ending. It is a person coming to look like Christ."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is why the verse holds up in situations where a promise of good outcomes would be an insult. Paul is not saying the thing that happened was good. He is saying nothing is wasted in what God is making."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Shaped into the likeness\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek word is *symmorphous* — sharing a form with. It is not imitation from the outside. Paul uses the same root when he speaks of a body being transformed, and of being conformed to Christ's death. The picture is of something taking a shape rather than copying a model."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the purpose given is not the reader's improvement: *so that His Son would be the firstborn among many brothers and sisters.* The goal of the whole process is a family with Christ at the head of it. The reader's transformation is how that family comes to exist, not a self-improvement project with a spiritual label."}, {"k": "pg"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five links, and where they land"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul then does something unusual: he runs the whole thing end to end in one breath."}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["knew beforehand", "marked out beforehand", "called", "put right with Himself", "**glorified**"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every verb is past tense, including the last one. Glorified — a thing no reader has experienced yet — is spoken of as done. Paul is not confused about the timeline. He is describing the chain from God's side, where it is finished, and that is why he can start the paragraph with *we know*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is safe to say with this verse"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not: everything that happens is good. Paul does not say that, and the chapter around it is full of groaning, weakness and suffering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "What he says is that all of it together is being worked toward one end, that the end is Christ's likeness in a person, and that the sequence which gets them there is already complete in the mind of the One running it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**On \"those who love God.\"** It reads like a qualifier a person might fail, and anxious readers use it that way. But Paul immediately restates the group by another name — *those who have been called according to His purpose* — and everything after that is God's action, not theirs. The love is how he describes them, not a threshold they maintain."}]}, "Romans 8:31–34": {"title": "Four questions, and nobody answers them", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul finishes the argument of eight chapters by asking questions instead of making statements, and the silence where the answers should be is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The questions"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*If God is for us — who is there left to stand against us?*", "*Who is going to lay a charge against the ones God Himself has chosen?*", "*Who is left to condemn?*", "and, a few lines on, *who shall separate us from the love of Christ?*"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "They are courtroom questions — the language of accusation, charge and condemnation. Paul invites anyone who wants to prosecute to step forward, and nobody does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The argument from the greater to the lesser"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He did not spare His own Son but handed Him over for us all — how will He not, along with Him, freely give us everything else?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the logic the whole paragraph rests on. If the hardest thing has already been given, nothing smaller is in doubt. Paul does not argue that God is generous in general; he argues from one specific act that has already happened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase *did not spare* echoes Genesis 22, where Abraham does not withhold his son. Paul is putting the two scenes beside each other deliberately."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is at the right hand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is Christ who died — and more than that, who was raised — who is now at God's right hand, and who is pleading there for us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The only figure the passage places in a position to condemn is the one described as interceding. That is the answer to the questions: the seat the prosecutor would have to occupy is already taken by the defence."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word \"freely give.\"** The verb is built on *charis* — grace. Paul does not say God will *provide*; he says He will **grace** us with all things, alongside the Son. The giving of everything else is described in the same vocabulary as the giving of Christ."}]}, "Romans 8:38–39": {"title": "\"Nothing can cut us off\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul empties the vocabulary of everything he can think of, in pairs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The confidence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **I am fully persuaded**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **pepeismai** — perfect passive. He has been convinced, and stands convinced."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *I hope* and not *I feel*. A settled state."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pairs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every item comes with its opposite, so that the space between them is covered:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**death** and **life** — the two conditions a person can be in", "**angels** and **rulers** — beings above the human order", "**things present** and **things to come** — time, both directions", "**height** and **depth** — space, both directions"]}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the catch-all: *nor **anything else anywhere in the whole of creation**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is missing from the list"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reader."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every item is external. Paul does not include failure, doubt, sin, or the person's own performance — and he has just spent seven chapters on exactly those."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"Powers\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **dunamis** — the word for power, ability, force. Paul includes them alongside angels and rulers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Colossians has the same categories, disarmed and led in a procession. See the note on Colossians 2:11–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Has **the strength to cut us off**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **chorisai** — to separate, to put a space between. It is the word used of divorce: *what God has joined together, let not man **separate**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul is denying that anything has the strength to do it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it is separation from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*From **the love of God that is in Christ Jesus our Lord**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not from salvation, not from heaven, not from blessing. From the love."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And it is located: **in Christ Jesus**. The same phrase that opens the chapter — *no condemnation for those who are **in Christ Jesus**.* See the note on Romans 8:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The questions before it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The paragraph is built out of five questions, each answered by silence:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If God is for us, who can be against us? He who did not spare His own Son… how shall He not with Him also freely give us all things? **Who shall bring a charge** against God's elect? **Who is he who condemns? Who shall separate us** from the love of Christ?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does not answer the first four. He answers the last one, and this is the answer."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The list in between"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Tribulation, or distress, or persecution, or famine, or nakedness, or peril, or sword?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven, and Paul had personally had six of them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then he quotes a psalm: *for Your sake we are killed all day long; we are accounted as sheep for the slaughter.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not soften it. He includes the psalm and then says *yet in all these things we are more than conquerors.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"More than conquerors\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "One Greek word — **hupernikao**. Over-conquering. Paul appears to have made it up."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the sentence is not.** A statement that nothing bad happens. The list of things that do happen is in the paragraph, and the psalm about sheep for the slaughter is quoted in full. What Paul denies is that any of it can get between."}]}, "Romans 8:5–8": {"title": "The mind set on the old nature", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul describes two kinds of people by what their minds are set on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Have their **minds set on** what that nature wants.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **phronousin** — from **phroneo**, to have a mindset, to be disposed toward."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is not a word about thinking occasionally. It describes a settled orientation, the direction a mind faces by default."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul uses the noun **phronema** four times in these four verses and almost nowhere else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two groups"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*People who **live according to the old nature** have their minds set on what that nature wants; people who **live according to the Spirit** have their minds set on **what the Spirit wants**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The living and the mindset are described as one thing. Paul does not say the mindset produces the life or the reverse — the sentence puts them together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two outcomes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A mind set on the old nature **ends in death**; a mind set on the Spirit is **life and peace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is simply *is death* and *is life and peace*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *leads to*. Paul equates them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Peace** is added and not paralleled. There is no opposite named for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hostility"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the mind set on the old nature is **hostile toward God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **echthra** — enmity."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not indifferent, and not weak. Set against."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It **does not submit to God's law**, and **in fact it cannot**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *neither indeed can be*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul goes past unwillingness to inability. It is the sharpest thing he says about the flesh, and he says it twice in one line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The conclusion"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Those who are **living out of that nature cannot please God**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Which is the end of the road for any programme of self-improvement, and Paul has spent Romans 7 demonstrating it from the inside. See the note on Romans 7:18–20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **you are not living out of the old nature; you are living in the Spirit**, if indeed the Spirit of God lives in you.* See the note on Romans 8:9–11."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole paragraph is diagnostic, and the next sentence tells the readers it does not describe them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does this repeatedly — states the condition in full and then names where his readers stand."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter opened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So then, **there is no condemnation now — none at all** — for those who are in Christ Jesus.* See the note on Romans 8:1–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the reason: *what the law could not accomplish, weakened as it was by the old nature, God accomplished Himself.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The inability in verse 7 is the same inability named in verse 3. Paul is consistent about where the failure is."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is not describing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two kinds of Christian. The categories in the paragraph are people in whom the Spirit dwells and people in whom He does not — verse 9 makes it explicit: *if anyone does not have the Spirit of Christ, that person is not His.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If the Spirit of Him who raised Jesus from the dead lives in you, then the One who raised Christ from the dead **will bring life to your mortal bodies as well**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then sonship, the Spirit's intercession, and *all things work together for good*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word the paragraph runs on.** Not what a mind thinks about — what it is set toward. Paul uses the noun four times in four verses and attaches death to one direction and life and peace to the other."}]}, "Romans 8:9–11": {"title": "\"You are living in the Spirit\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul tells them what they are, and the sentence is in the indicative rather than the imperative."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The statement"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But **you are not living out of the old nature; you are living in the Spirit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not *you should*. He is describing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *you are not in the flesh but in the Spirit* — a statement of location, the same construction as *in Christ Jesus* at the top of the chapter. See the note on Romans 8:1–4."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The qualifier"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If indeed **the Spirit of God lives in you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek **eiper** can be read *if indeed* or *since indeed*. Paul is not raising a doubt; he is naming the ground of what he has just said."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The definition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And if anyone does not have the Spirit of Christ, **that person is not His**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The plainest test in the letter, and it is not about behaviour, background, or observance."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has spent seven chapters dismantling the alternatives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three names"}, {"k": "p", "t": "In two verses the same Spirit is called **the Spirit of God**, **the Spirit of Christ**, and then simply **the Spirit**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul moves between them without explanation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The body and the Spirit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But if Christ is in you, then **the body may be dead because of sin, but the Spirit is life because of righteousness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things held together. The body is not exempted from the situation, and the Spirit is called life."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is that the Spirit **is** life — not gives it, in this clause."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The long title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And if **the Spirit of Him who raised Jesus from the dead** lives in you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul could have written *the Spirit*. He spells out the resurrection, because the whole next clause depends on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does the same thing in chapter 4 about Abraham: *God, who **gives life to the dead** and calls those things which do not exist as though they did.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The promise"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **the One who raised Christ from the dead will bring life to your mortal bodies as well** by His Spirit who lives in you.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Mortal bodies** — the Greek is **thneta**, subject to death."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same power, applied to the same kind of object. Paul's argument is that the Spirit already living in a person is the link between the two events."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says elsewhere in the same terms"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who will **transform** our lowly body that it may be conformed to His glorious body, according to the working by which He is able even to subdue all things to Himself.* See the note on Philippians 3:20–21."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Therefore, brethren, **we are debtors — not to the flesh**, to live according to the flesh.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the sonship: *you received the Spirit of adoption by whom we cry out, \"Abba, Father.\" The Spirit Himself bears witness with our spirit that we are children of God.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the passage settles.** Where a person is, rather than how they are doing. Paul states the location first and the consequences after — including one about a body he has just called mortal."}]}, "Romans 9:25–26": {"title": "Called My people", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Paul quotes Hosea twice, and both quotations are the reversal of a name."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is quoting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hosea's third child was called **Lo-Ammi** — Not-My-People — and God said over Israel *you are not My people, and I am not your God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul takes the two verses that reverse it and sets them side by side. See the note on Hosea 1:8–9."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"I will call those who were **not My people** 'My people,' and her who was **not loved** 'beloved.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both of Hosea's negative names, both undone. *Lo-Ammi* becomes *Ammi*. *Lo-Ruhamah* becomes *Ruhamah*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The prefix is simply dropped. Nothing else changes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who does the calling"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will call.* The naming is His in both directions — He named them Not-My-People, and He is the one who renames them."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in either verse describes them earning the second name. They are called it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**In the very place** where it was said to them, 'You are not My people,' there they will be called **sons of the living God**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not somewhere else. The same place."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the title is not restored to what it was. They were *My people*; they are now *sons of the living God*. The reversal overshoots the starting point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul is arguing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's subject is God's freedom in mercy — *I will have mercy on whom I have mercy* — and Paul applies Hosea's words about Israel to Gentiles being brought in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is showing from the prophets that a people who were not a people becoming God's own is not a new idea invented by the church. It was written down centuries earlier, about Israel, and God's method has not changed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word \"called\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "It runs through the whole chapter. *Not of works but of Him who calls… the children of the promise are counted as offspring… even us whom He called.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Calling is what makes the difference in Romans 9, and it belongs to Him."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the argument ends up.** Two chapters later: *God has not cast away His people whom He foreknew… for the gifts and the calling of God are irrevocable.* Paul spends three chapters on this and does not conclude that anybody was discarded."}]}, "Romans 9:6–9": {"title": "The promise has not failed", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The question underneath chapters 9 to 11, stated in the first sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just been said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I tell the truth in Christ, I am not lying… that **I have great sorrow and continual grief in my heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For I could wish that I myself were accursed from Christ **for my brethren, my countrymen according to the flesh**.* See the note on Romans 9:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul has just finished eight chapters ending *nothing shall be able to separate us from the love of God*, and immediately says he would give up his own place for his people."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The problem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Israel had the covenants, the law, the promises, the fathers, and the Messiah came from them — and largely did not receive Him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "If the promises were to Israel, and Israel has not taken them, then either the promises failed or something else is going on."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The denial"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is not as though **the word of God has fallen to the ground**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ouch hoion de hoti ekpeptoken** — from **ekpipto**, to fall out, to drop away. The word used of a ship drifting off course and of flowers falling."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul states the conclusion before the argument."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The distinction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Not everyone descended from Israel **is Israel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ou gar pantes hoi ex Israel houtoi Israel**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same noun twice, meaning two different things in one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And not everyone who comes from Abraham **is counted as his children**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Physical descent, and something else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The proof"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*As it stands written, \"**It is through Isaac that your offspring will be named**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 21, when Sarah demanded Hagar and Ishmael be sent away and Abraham was distressed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both sons were Abraham's. Only one carried the line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The principle"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That is to say, **it is not the children born in the ordinary way** who are the children of God; it is **the children of the promise** who are counted as the offspring.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ta tekna tes sarkos** — the children of the flesh. And **ta tekna tes epangelias** — the children of the promise."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The quotation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For the promise ran like this: \"**At this time next year I will come, and Sarah will have a son**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Genesis 18, at the tent door in the heat of the day, with Sarah listening behind the flap and laughing. See the note on Genesis 18:9–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Greek says about the timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kata ton kairon touton** — according to this **kairos**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The appointed time. Paul quotes the Septuagint, which renders the Hebrew **la'et chayyah** — at the time of life, at the reviving season."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What makes Isaac different"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not his character. He is a passive figure through most of his story."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He was born to a woman past childbearing after twenty-five years of waiting, which means he exists because of a promise rather than because of anything anybody managed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And not being weak in faith, he did not consider his own body, already dead… **being fully convinced that what He had promised He was also able to perform**.* See the note on Romans 4:20–22."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The next example"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rebekah's twins — *for the children not yet being born, nor having done any good or evil… it was said to her, \"The older shall serve the younger.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same womb, same father, same day. Paul picks the case where every variable is controlled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the argument arrives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And so **all Israel will be saved**, as it is written: \"The Deliverer will come out of Zion.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **the gifts and the calling of God are irrevocable**.* See the note on Romans 11:28–29."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **ametameleta** — not to be repented of, not withdrawn."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three chapters after saying the word has not fallen, Paul says why."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The first sentence.** *The word of God has not fallen to the ground.* Paul states the verdict before the argument, and spends three chapters on why the promise ran through a promised son."}]}, "Ruth 1:16–17": {"title": "Where you go, I will go", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The most quoted words in the book, said by a foreigner to a bitter widow on a road."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On the road out of Moab, heading for Judah."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Naomi has lost her husband and both sons. She has told her two daughters-in-law to go home — *return each to her mother's house*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Orpah kissed her and went. Ruth clung to her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Naomi had argued"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Turn back, my daughters, go — for I am too old to have a husband… **would you wait for them till they were grown?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It is more bitter for me than for you**, for the hand of the LORD has gone out against me.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is not being coy. Her case is sound: there is nothing in Judah for a Moabite widow."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The refusal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*But Ruth answered, and **her words have never stopped echoing**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two negatives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Don't ask me to leave you. Don't beg me to turn back**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **al-tiphge'i vi** — do not press me, do not urge me."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She closes the argument before making her own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The five clauses"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Wherever you go, I will go. Wherever you stay, I will stay. **Your people will be my people, and your God will be my God**. Where you die, I will die, and there I will be buried.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Movement, dwelling, nation, God, and grave."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Each one is more permanent than the last. The list ends with the one that cannot be undone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she is renouncing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moab, her family, her gods — Chemosh, to whom children were burned — and any prospect of remarriage in a country that had a law against her people entering the assembly. See the note on Deuteronomy 23:3–6."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The oath"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**May the LORD deal with me, and worse still, if anything but death ever comes between us**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **koh ya'aseh YHWH li vekoh yosiph** — thus may the LORD do to me, and thus may He add."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The standard self-curse formula, and she swears it by **YHWH** — the covenant name of the God she has just claimed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A Moabitess taking an oath in the name of Israel's God, on a road, to a woman who has nothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Naomi says back"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When she saw that she was determined to go with her, **she stopped speaking to her**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Naomi says in Bethlehem"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Do not call me Naomi; call me Mara**, for the Almighty has dealt very bitterly with me. I went out full, and the LORD has brought me home again empty.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Naomi** — pleasant. **Mara** — bitter. The same root as the water at Marah. See the note on Exodus 15:22–27."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the narrator adds"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Naomi returned, and **Ruth the Moabitess her daughter-in-law with her**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has just said she came home empty, and the sentence naming her emptiness names Ruth standing next to her."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now they came to Bethlehem **at the beginning of barley harvest**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last clause of the chapter. Everything that follows happens because they arrived in the right week."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Boaz says about her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD repay your work, and a full reward be given you by the LORD God of Israel, under whose wings you have come for refuge**.\"* See the note on Ruth 2:11–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kenaphav** — His wings. And **lachasot** — to take shelter."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the language of the psalms about a woman who has been in the country a few weeks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they called his name Obed. He is the father of Jesse, the father of David.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The order of the five clauses.** Go, stay, people, God, grave. Each is harder to reverse than the one before, and the list ends with a burial plot."}]}, "Ruth 2:1–3": {"title": "\"Though it looked like plain chance\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Three verses of setup, and the narrator interrupts himself in the last one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The introduction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Now Naomi had **a relative on her husband's side** — a man of standing, **wealthy and well respected**, whose name was **Boaz**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **gibbor chayil** — a mighty man of valour. The phrase is used of soldiers, and here of a landowner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator introduces him before Ruth has met him, so the reader knows what she does not."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**A relative** — the Hebrew notes the family connection, which is the hinge of the whole book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where they are"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bethlehem, at the start of the barley harvest. Naomi has come back from Moab with nothing — *I went out full, and the LORD has brought me home again empty*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Ruth's proposal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Let me go out to the fields and pick up the leftover grain** behind anyone who will **show me kindness**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gleaning was a legal right. Leviticus: *when you reap the harvest of your land, you shall not wholly reap the corners of your field… **you shall leave them for the poor and the stranger***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ruth is both."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The right existed and enforcing it was another matter, which is why she adds *anyone who will show me kindness*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is asking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ruth the Moabite. Deuteronomy: *an Ammonite or Moabite shall not enter the assembly of the LORD, even to the tenth generation*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator calls her *the Moabite* five times in the book, well after everyone knows."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Naomi's answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Go ahead, my daughter**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two words in the Hebrew."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she did"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So she went, and **she gleaned in the fields behind the harvesters**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She goes out to work in a country she has never lived in, for a mother-in-law with no legal claim on her, having said *your people shall be my people, and your God my God*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **as it turned out — though it looked like plain chance** — she found herself **in a field belonging to Boaz**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayiqer miqreha** — literally *her chance chanced*. It is a doubled construction, and it is the narrator drawing attention to itself."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book of Ruth never records a miracle. God is spoken about constantly and never seen to act."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This sentence is the closest it comes to a comment: the writer says *chance*, twice, in a way nobody could read as chance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens the same day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Boaz arrives from Bethlehem, greets his reapers *the LORD be with you*, asks who she is, and tells her to stay in his field, drink from his water jars, and not go elsewhere."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *the LORD repay your work, and **a full reward be given you by the LORD God of Israel, under whose wings you have come for refuge***."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Boaz turns out to be"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Goel** — the kinsman-redeemer. The relative obliged to buy back what a family had lost, and to marry a childless widow to raise up a name for the dead."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The narrator told us in verse 1 and Ruth finds out in verse 20."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it ends"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So Boaz took Ruth, and she became his wife… and she bore a son.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And they called his name **Obed**. He is the father of Jesse, **the father of David**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Moabite woman gleaning in a stranger's field is the great-grandmother of the king, and Matthew puts her in the first chapter of the New Testament."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The narrator's one aside.** *Her chance chanced.* A book with no miracles in it stops once, in a field, to say that what looked like luck was the field of the one man in Bethlehem who could redeem her."}]}, "Ruth 2:14": {"title": "She had some left over", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A foreign widow gleaning at the edge of a field is called over to the table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where she is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Boaz's field, in Bethlehem, at the start of the barley harvest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had asked permission to glean — *let me glean and gather after the reapers* — which the law permitted to the poor, the widow and the foreigner."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And she happened to come to the part of the field belonging to Boaz.* See the note on Ruth 2:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had already said to her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Do not go to glean in another field… **stay close by my young women**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Have I not commanded the young men not to touch you? And when you are thirsty, go to the vessels and drink from what the young men have drawn.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD repay your work, and a full reward be given you by the LORD God of Israel, under whose wings you have come for refuge**.\"* See the note on Ruth 2:11–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The invitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When it was time to eat, Boaz called to her, \"**Come here. Have some bread**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **goshi halom** — draw near here."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A gleaner ate what she brought, at the edge, apart. He calls her in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dish"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Dip your piece in the wine vinegar**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chometz** — sour wine, vinegar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The harvesters' drink. Thin, sharp, and what field labourers had at noon in the heat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The soldiers offered the same thing at the cross: *they filled a sponge with sour wine*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where she sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So she **sat down beside the harvesters**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mitzad haqqotzerim** — at the side of the reapers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not at the edge of the field. Beside the workers, with the people who belong there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **he handed her roasted grain**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayyitzbot-lah qali** — and he held out to her parched grain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb **tzavat** occurs once in the Hebrew Bible, here. Its sense is to reach out and hand across."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He passes it to her himself. Not through a servant."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*She **ate until she was satisfied — and still had some left over**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vattochal vattisba vattotar**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three verbs: she ate, she was satisfied, she had left over."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does with the surplus"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And she brought out and gave to her mother-in-law what she had kept back after she had been satisfied.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Naomi is at home. Ruth carries the leftovers back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Boaz does next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Let her glean even among the sheaves**, and do not reproach her. Also **let grain from the bundles fall purposely for her**, and leave it that she may glean, and do not rebuke her.\"* See the note on Ruth 2:15–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shol tasholu lah** — pulling out, you shall pull out for her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Instructions given to the young men, out of her hearing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So she gleaned in the field until evening, and beat out what she had gleaned, and it was **about an ephah of barley**.* See the note on Ruth 2:17–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An ephah is around thirty pounds — perhaps two weeks' food, from one day's gleaning."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Naomi says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Blessed be he of the LORD, who has not forsaken His kindness to the living and the dead**!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"**This man is a relation of ours, one of our close relatives**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **miggo'alenu** — of our redeemers. See the note on Leviticus 25:23–28."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Naomi, who came home saying *call me Mara*, blesses somebody for the first time."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The three verbs.** She ate, she was satisfied, and she had some left. The last one is what she carries home to a woman who said she came back empty."}]}, "Ruth 2:15–16": {"title": "Let it fall on purpose", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Instructions given to the harvesters about a woman who is not in earshot."}, {"k": "h", "t": "When"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And when she rose up to glean, **Boaz commanded his young men**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has just eaten with the reapers and gone back to work. See the note on Ruth 2:14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He waits until she has left the table."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Let her glean even among the sheaves**, and do not reproach her.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **gam bein ha'omarim telaqqet** — even between the sheaves let her glean."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why that is unusual"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The law gave gleaners the corners of the field and whatever the reapers dropped."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You shall not wholly reap the corners of your field, nor shall you gather the gleanings of your harvest. **You shall leave them for the poor and the stranger**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The sheaves are the harvested crop, already bound. Nobody gleans there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **do not reproach her**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **velo tachlimuha** — do not humiliate, do not shame her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He anticipates that the young men will object to a Moabitess in among the sheaves, and forbids it in advance."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Also **let grain from the bundles fall purposely for her**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shol tasholu lah min-hatzevatim** — pulling out you shall pull out for her from the bundles."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The infinitive absolute doubles the verb: *deliberately, definitely pull out*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not permission to glean. Manufactured gleanings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The workers are told to pull handfuls out of the bound sheaves and drop them where she will find them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The fourth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**And leave it that she may glean, and do not rebuke her**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **velo tig'aru-bah** — do not rebuke her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Twice in two verses he forbids them to say anything to her about it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does not know"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That the field is being seeded for her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is working, and the yield is being arranged. Neither the law nor her own effort accounts for what she takes home."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The result"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So she gleaned in the field until evening, and beat out what she had gleaned, and it was **about an ephah of barley**.* See the note on Ruth 2:17–18."}, {"k": "p", "t": "An ephah is roughly thirty pounds. A gleaner in an ordinary day might get a few."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Naomi asks"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Where have you gleaned today? And where did you work?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The quantity is the question. Naomi can tell from the weight that something happened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Blessed be the one who took notice of you**.\"*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Boaz already knew"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It has been fully reported to me, all that you have done for your mother-in-law** since the death of your husband, and how you have left your father and your mother and the land of your birth, and have come to a people whom you did not know before.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He had heard about her before she walked into the field."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The blessing he gave"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD repay your work, and a full reward be given you by the LORD God of Israel, under whose wings you have come for refuge**.\"* See the note on Ruth 2:11–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He prays that God will repay her, and then quietly arranges the repayment out of his own crop."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for wings"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Kenaphav** — his wings."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She later uses the same word to him at the threshing floor: *take your maidservant under your wing*, which is also the word for the corner of a garment."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The man who prayed she would find refuge under God's wings is asked to spread his own."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The instruction.** Not permission to glean — *pull grain out of the bundles and drop it for her*. And twice: do not let her hear you say anything about it."}]}, "Ruth 2:17–18": {"title": "About an ephah of barley", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The day's work is weighed, and the number is the point."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So she gleaned in the field **until evening**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ad-ha'arev**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She started in the morning — *she came and has continued from morning until now* — and worked through, with one break for the meal."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The threshing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **beat out what she had gleaned**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vattachbot** — she beat it out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Beating with a stick, rather than driving oxen over it on a floor. The method for small quantities."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does the processing herself, at the end of a full day in the field."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The measure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And it was **about an ephah of barley**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An ephah is roughly two-thirds of a bushel — somewhere near thirty pounds of grain."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What that means"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A gleaner following reapers might expect one or two pounds in a day."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The daily manna ration was an omer per person, and an ephah is ten omers. She has brought home enough to feed two women for something over two weeks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the number is so high"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Also **let grain from the bundles fall purposely for her**… and do not rebuke her.\"* See the note on Ruth 2:15–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Boaz had told the harvesters to pull grain out of the bound sheaves and drop it in her path."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not know. She thinks it was a good field."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The carrying"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **she took it up and went into the city**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Thirty pounds, on foot, into Bethlehem."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Naomi sees"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **her mother-in-law saw what she had gleaned**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vattere** — and she saw."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The quantity is visible before anything is said. Naomi's first question is *where have you gleaned today?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The leftovers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So she brought out and **gave to her what she had kept back after she had been satisfied**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The roasted grain from the noon meal, which she had not finished. See the note on Ruth 2:14."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She carried the surplus of her lunch home for her mother-in-law, along with the ephah."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Naomi's reaction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Where have you gleaned today? And where did you work? **Blessed be the one who took notice of you**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yehi makkirek baruch** — blessed be the one who acknowledged you."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The man's name with whom I worked today is **Boaz**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Blessed be he of the LORD, who has not forsaken His kindness to the living and the dead**!\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chasdo** — His **chesed**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has changed in Naomi"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She came home saying *do not call me Naomi; call me Mara, for the Almighty has dealt very bitterly with me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I went out full, and the LORD has brought me home again empty.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the first blessing she pronounces in the book, and it is over the God she had accused."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The relationship"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This man is a relation of ours, one of our close relatives**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **miggo'alenu** — of our redeemers. See the note on Leviticus 25:23–28."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Naomi has known this all along and says it now."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**It is good, my daughter, that you go out with his young women**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*So she stayed close by the young women of Boaz, to glean until the end of barley harvest and wheat harvest.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Barley harvest runs into wheat harvest — about seven weeks of work."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The number.** Thirty pounds, from one day's gleaning, when a good day would be two or three. Naomi could read what had happened off the weight of the sack."}]}, "Song of Songs 1:2–4": {"title": "Your name a fragrance", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The book opens in the middle of a sentence, and the first voice is hers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The opening"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Kiss me, **kiss me with the kisses of your mouth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **yishaqeni minneshiqot pihu** — let him kiss me with the kisses of his mouth."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew begins in the third person — *let him* — and shifts to the second by the end of the verse. The book does this repeatedly."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who speaks first"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Song is unusual in ancient literature for how much of it is in a woman's voice. She speaks more lines than he does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **your love is sweeter than wine**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **tovim dodeicha miyyayin** — better are your loves than wine."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Wine is the standard of pleasure. The comparison recurs in the book, and he repeats it back to her later: *how much better than wine*. See the note on Song of Songs 4:9–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scent"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The scent you carry is **a gift poured out**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **lereach shemaneicha tovim** — for the fragrance of your good oils."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The name"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your very name **a fragrance spilling wide**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shemen turaq shemecha** — your name is oil poured out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Shemen** — oil. **Shem** — name. A pun the English cannot carry."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Poured-out oil fills a room. A stoppered jar does not."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it produces"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **this is why the young women love you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Alamot** — young women. The same root as **almah**, the word in Isaiah's sign. See the note on Matthew 1:22–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Her love is not exclusive of others'. She notes that everyone else feels the same and does not treat it as competition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The invitation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Take my hand and **let us run**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mashcheni acharecha narutzah** — draw me after you, let us run."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs, one singular and one plural. He draws; they run."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The drawing is his. The running is joint."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The room"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bring me into your rooms, my king.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chadarav** — his chambers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Cheder** — the innermost room. The same noun in Proverbs, of what the lamp of the LORD searches. See the note on Proverbs 20:23–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Hammelek** — the king."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book is attributed to Solomon in the first verse, and he is called king throughout."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I am dark, but lovely… **do not look upon me, because I am dark, because the sun has tanned me***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My mother's sons were angry with me; **they made me the keeper of the vineyards, but my own vineyard I have not kept***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first thing she says about herself is an apology for her appearance, and the reason is that she was made to work outdoors."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he says about that"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I have compared you, my love, to my filly among Pharaoh's chariots.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold, you are fair, my love! Behold, you are fair!*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does not agree with her assessment and does not argue with it. He states his own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Poetry in two voices, with a chorus, and no narrator to explain."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word **love** appears throughout; the word **God** does not appear once in the Hebrew text."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The drawing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**No one can come to Me unless the Father who sent Me draws him**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And I, if I am lifted up from the earth, **will draw all peoples to Myself**.\"* See the note on John 12:32–33."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The pun.** **Shemen** and **shem** — oil and name. A name like oil poured out, which is a way of saying it does not stay in the jar."}]}, "Song of Songs 1:7": {"title": "Where do you rest them at noon", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Her first direct question, and it is about where to find Him in the middle of the day."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Tell me, **you whom my soul loves**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase runs through the book. She uses it four times in chapter 3 alone, looking for Him in the streets at night."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She does not use His name. The Hebrew is *whom my soul loves* — **she'ahavah naphshi**."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The occupation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where do you **graze your flock**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is a shepherd in her question. The book has already had Him as a king with chariots and as a man in a garden."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hour"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Where do you **rest them in the heat of noon**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *where do you make them lie down at noon* — **tarbits**, the causative of the verb for lying down."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Noon is when a Palestinian flock cannot graze. The shepherd's job at midday is not to lead them to food but to get them into shade and make them stop."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same verb, same idea, in the psalm: *He **makes me to lie down** in green pastures*. See the note on Psalms 23:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she is asking for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not the pasture. The resting place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Why should I **wander veiled** among the flocks of your companions?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is difficult. **Ke'otyah** can mean *as one veiled* or, with a small change, *as one who wanders*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A veiled woman beside the flocks of other men is a picture with an edge to it — Tamar covered her face by the roadside in Genesis 38, and Judah took her for a prostitute."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is saying: tell me where you are, so I am not looking for you among everybody else's sheep."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The friends' answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you do not know, loveliest of women, then **follow the tracks of the flock**.* See the note on Song of Songs 1:8."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the noon question is answered"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Later in the book: *my beloved has gone down to his garden, to the beds of spices, to feed his flock in the gardens and to gather lilies.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *I am my beloved's, and my beloved is mine; he feeds his flock among the lilies.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight chapters of dialogue with no narrator, no plot, and no mention of God by name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It was read at Passover, and Rabbi Akiva said of it that all the writings are holy but the Song of Songs is the Holy of Holies."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question underneath the question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She knows he is a shepherd and she knows he has a flock. What she does not know is the location."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything she asks in the book is some version of *where*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What she asks for.** Not the grazing ground — the place he makes them lie down when the sun is at its worst. The same verb the psalm uses of green pastures."}]}, "Song of Songs 1:8": {"title": "Follow the tracks of the flock", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "She asked where he was. The answer she gets is: look at the ground."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is speaking"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chorus — the daughters of Jerusalem, who appear throughout the book as a third voice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had asked Him directly. Someone else answers."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The condition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*If you **do not know**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *if you do not know for yourself*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It concedes her ignorance without correcting it. Nobody tells her she should have known."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Loveliest of women.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the fairest among women* — **hayyafah bannashim**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has just said of herself, three verses earlier, *I am dark, but lovely… do not look upon me, because I am dark, because the sun has tanned me. My mother's sons were angry with me; they made me the keeper of the vineyards, but my own vineyard I have not kept.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The name she is given contradicts the one she gave herself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The instruction"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Then **follow the tracks of the flock**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *go out by the footsteps of the flock* — **iqvei ha-tson**, the heel-prints."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She wanted a location. She is given a trail."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The flock has been where the shepherd is. The prints in the dust are where they walked back and forth, and they lead to him because they came from him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second half"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **pasture your young goats beside the shepherds' tents**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had complained about being *among the flocks of your companions*. See the note on Song of Songs 1:7."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The answer sends her to exactly that place — the shepherds' encampment — with her own animals, to graze them there."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not away from the other flocks. Beside them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she is not told"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Where he is."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nobody in the verse answers the question she asked. She is told what to do while she does not know."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What happens next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He speaks. The very next verse is his: *I have compared you, my love, to my filly among Pharaoh's chariots.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She takes the instruction and he arrives."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern in the book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She loses him repeatedly and finds him every time, and never once by working out where he went."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I sought him, but I did not find him… the watchmen found me… scarcely had I passed by them, when I found the one I love.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The tracks elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He leads me in the paths of righteousness for His name's sake.* See the note on Psalms 23:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Peter, of the sufferings of Christ: *leaving us an example, that you should **follow His steps**.* See the note on 1 Peter 2:21–23."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek there is **ichnesin** — footprints. The same image, the same instruction."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The answer to a question about location.** Footprints. She is not told where he is; she is told what the ground already shows, and to bring her goats to where the tents are."}]}, "Song of Songs 2:10–13": {"title": "The winter is over", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He arrives leaping over hills and says one thing twice."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The voice of my beloved! Behold, **he comes leaping upon the mountains, skipping upon the hills**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He stands behind our wall; he is looking through the windows, gazing through the lattice.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is outside the house, at the window, before he speaks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The call"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My beloved spoke and said to me, **Rise up, my love, my beautiful one, and come away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **qumi lak ra'yati yaphati ulechi-lak**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two imperatives — arise, and go — with the reflexive **lak** attached to both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repetition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it again at the end of the paragraph, in exactly the same words."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The four verses between are the reason."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first reason"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For look, **the winter is over, the rain is gone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hasetav avar hagggeshem chalaph** — the winter has passed, the rain has gone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that climate the rains run from October to March and stop. There is no gradual spring. The change is sudden and everybody notices it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The flowers"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The flowers appear on the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **hannitzanim** — buds, blossoms. The word occurs once in the Bible, here."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The singing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **time of singing** has come.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **et hazzamir**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Zamir** can mean singing, or pruning. Both fit the season, and the ambiguity is probably deliberate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Et** — the appointed time. The word Ecclesiastes uses twenty-nine times in chapter 3. See the note on Ecclesiastes 3:9–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The dove"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the voice of the dove is heard in our land**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The turtledove is migratory. Jeremiah uses it as the standard of reliable timing: *the turtledove and the swift and the swallow observe the time of their coming*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Its call is the audible sign that the season has turned."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The figs"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The fig tree **ripens its early fruit**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chanetah pagggeha** — puts forth her green figs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Pag** — the unripe early fig, which appears before the leaves."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The fruit that Jesus looked for on a tree that had leaves and nothing else. See the note on Mark 11:12–14."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The vines"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **vines in blossom give their fragrance**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **semadar** — the vine in flower."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grape blossom is small and unremarkable to look at and carries a scent across a whole hillside."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repeat"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rise up, my love, my beautiful one, and come away.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same line, unchanged."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The invitation is the whole speech. Everything between the two statements is the evidence that the season permits it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she does not do"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*O my dove, in the clefts of the rock, in the secret places of the cliff, **let me see your face, let me hear your voice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He asks again, which suggests she has not come out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then: *catch us the foxes, the little foxes that spoil the vines*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she says"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My beloved is mine, and I am his.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until the day breaks and the shadows flee away, **turn, my beloved**.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other winters"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He has made everything beautiful **in its time**.* See the note on Ecclesiastes 3:9–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To everything there is a season.*"}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The structure.** One sentence, said twice, with four verses of evidence in between — and every item of evidence is something you could walk outside and check."}]}, "Song of Songs 2:14": {"title": "Let me see your face", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and the whole of it is a request to be let in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where she is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My dove **in the clefts of the rock**, in **the hidden places of the cliff**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **bechagvei hasela** — in the crevices of the rock. And **beseter hammadregah**, in the secret place of the steep path."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rock doves nest in cliff faces, in cracks a hand cannot reach."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is describing somebody who has gone somewhere inaccessible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What has just happened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses earlier the finest lines in the book: *rise up, my love, my fair one, and come away. For lo, the winter is past, the rain is over and gone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "An invitation to come out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And this is the same speech, addressed to somebody who has not come out yet."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two requests"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Let me **see your face**, let me **hear your voice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mar'ayik** and **qolek** — your appearance and your voice."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Face and voice, in that order, and then the reasons come back in reverse order."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two reasons"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For **your voice is sweet** and **your face is lovely**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **arev** — pleasant, sweet. And **naveh** — comely, becoming."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chiasm is deliberate: see your face, hear your voice, your voice is sweet, your face is lovely."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the request is remarkable"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He is not asking her to change anything."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The reason given for wanting to see her face is that her face is lovely, and he says so to somebody hiding in a cliff."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has already described herself in chapter 1: *I am dark, but lovely… do not look upon me, because I am dark, because the sun has tanned me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The hiding and the self-assessment are connected, and the passage does not argue with either. It just asks."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he calls her"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My dove.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses it repeatedly. Doves were the offering of the poor — the pair Joseph and Mary brought at the presentation, because they could not afford a lamb. See the note on Luke 2:33–35."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clefts"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image turns up elsewhere."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Moses, asking to see God's glory: *I will put you **in the cleft of the rock**, and will cover you with My hand while I pass by*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word there is different, but the picture is the same — a person in a crack in a cliff, being addressed."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whether the Song intends the echo is unprovable. It is the same landscape."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows immediately"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Catch the foxes for us, the little foxes that spoil the vineyards.* See the note on Song of Songs 2:15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The tender request is followed by pest control, and the poem does not explain the transition."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse before her answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My beloved is mine, and I am his. He feeds his flock among the lilies.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The whole exchange resolves into possession, both ways."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the same want turns up"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Those are the worshipers **the Father is looking for**.* See the note on John 4:20–24."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The seeking, in that sentence, is His."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What he asks for.** Not that she come out and be seen — he asks to see her, in the place she is hiding, and gives as his reason that her face is lovely."}]}, "Song of Songs 2:15": {"title": "The little foxes", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, four lines, and it interrupts the loveliest passage in the book."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Rise up, my love, my fair one, and come away. For lo, **the winter is past, the rain is over and gone**. The flowers appear on the earth; the time of singing has come, and the voice of the turtledove is heard in our land.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Spring, in the finest lines in the Song. And then, without transition, pest control."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The command"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Catch the foxes for us.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **echezu** — seize, take hold of. Plural imperative: *you*, more than one person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Who is being told is not stated. The book has a chorus, and it may be them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The size"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The **little** foxes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shu'alim qetannim**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The size is specified and it is the point. Nobody is worried about a wolf getting into the vineyard; the verse is about the small ones that get through gaps."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The damage"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That **spoil the vineyards**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mechabbelim** — ruining, corrupting, acting destructively."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Foxes in a vineyard do not eat the fruit primarily. They dig under the walls, gnaw the vine roots and the tender shoots, and the damage is not visible for a season."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The timing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For our vineyards are **in bloom**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **semadar** — the vine in blossom, before the grapes set."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the vulnerable moment. A blossom knocked off is a bunch that never exists."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The word appears three times in the Song and nowhere else in the Bible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the vineyard has meant already"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My mother's sons were angry with me; they made me the keeper of the vineyards, but **my own vineyard I have not kept**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 1, the bride's own words. She has already told us there is a vineyard she has neglected."}, {"k": "h", "t": "And what it means later"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My own vineyard is before me. **You, O Solomon, may have a thousand**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last chapter, and the vineyard is hers to give."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Isaiah's vineyard"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My Well-beloved has a vineyard on a very fruitful hill… He expected it to bring forth good grapes, but it brought forth wild grapes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the verdict: *for the vineyard of the LORD of hosts is the house of Israel.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The one Jesus tells"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A certain man planted a vineyard and set a hedge around it, dug a place for the wine vat and built a tower.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Every vineyard parable in Scripture has something getting in — animals, tenants, or the owner's own son being thrown out."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the verse does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "How. No method is given, and no list of foxes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four lines: catch them, they are small, they ruin vineyards, and the vines are in flower right now."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verse after it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My beloved is mine, and I am his. He feeds his flock among the lilies.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The interruption lasts exactly one verse, and the poem picks up where it left off."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What is specified.** The size. Not the wolves, not the boar out of the wood — the little ones, at the exact moment the vine is in blossom and the fruit has not set."}]}, "Song of Songs 2:3–4": {"title": "His banner over me", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "She answers his description of her with one of her own, and it is a tree."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he had just said"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Like a lily among thorns, so is my love among the daughters.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "Her reply"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Like **an apple tree among the trees of the wood**, so is my beloved among the young men.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ketappuach** — the identification of the fruit is uncertain; apple, quince, or apricot."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The point is not botany. It is a fruit tree standing in a forest of trees that bear nothing you can eat."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The comparison she makes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He compared her to a flower among thorns. She compares him to the one tree in a wood with fruit on it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both images are of one thing among many, and both are the speaker's own choice of picture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sitting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In his shade **I longed to sit**, and **there I sat**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **chimmadti veyashavti** — I desired and I sat."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verbs, and the second one answers the first. She wanted to, and then she did."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing intervenes. The Song does not put an obstacle between the longing and the sitting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The shade"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Betsillo** — in his shadow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "In that climate shade is not a comfort, it is survival. The psalms use the word for God's protection: *the LORD is your shade at your right hand*, and *under the shadow of Your wings*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The taste"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **his fruit was sweet to my taste**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **matoq lechikki** — sweet to my palate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word used of the scroll Ezekiel ate, and of the word in Psalm 119. See the note on Ezekiel 3:1–3."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things a fruit tree gives"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Shade and fruit. Rest and food."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She names them in that order — she sat first."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The house of wine"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He brought me to **the house of wine**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **bet hayyayin** — literally the house of wine. A banqueting hall, or a wine cellar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is **hevi'ani** — he brought me. She is taken there."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The banner"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **his banner over me was love**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vediglo alai ahavah**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Degel** — a standard, an ensemble, the flag a division of an army camped under. Numbers uses it of the tribes: *everyone of the children of Israel shall camp by his own standard*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What a banner does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It identifies whose you are, from a distance, to everybody."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is under one, and it says love."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she asks for next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Sustain me with cakes of raisins, refresh me with apples, **for I am lovesick**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His left hand is under my head, and his right hand embraces me.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the refrain that runs three times through the book: *do not stir up nor awaken love until it pleases*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The voice"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The voice of my beloved! Behold, **he comes leaping upon the mountains, skipping upon the hills**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rise up, my love, my fair one, and come away.* See the note on Song of Songs 2:10–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The winter is past, the rain is over and gone, and the fig tree is putting out green figs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What she says about belonging"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My beloved is mine, and I am his.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And later, reversed: *I am my beloved's, and my beloved is mine*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And at the end, only the one direction: *I am my beloved's, and his desire is toward me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Song moves the pronouns three times and lands on his desire."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The two verbs.** *I longed to sit, and there I sat.* Nothing comes between them, which is the whole point of the line."}]}, "Song of Songs 4:6–7": {"title": "No flaw in you", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The end of a long description, and the last line withdraws every qualification."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Seven verses of description, item by item: her eyes, her hair, her teeth, her lips, her temples, her neck."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold, you are fair, my love! Behold, you are fair!*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Until the day **breathes cool** and the **shadows flee away**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *until the day breathes* — **yaphuach hayyom**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The evening breeze. In that climate the wind comes up as the heat drops, and the long shadows stretch and vanish into general dark."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same phrase appears in chapter 2, in her mouth: *until the day breaks and the shadows flee away, turn, my beloved*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The destination"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will go to the **mountain of myrrh**, to the **hill of frankincense**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are burial spices and both are temple spices."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Myrrh is in the anointing oil of Exodus 30 and in the burial of Jesus — *Nicodemus came, bringing a mixture of myrrh and aloes, about a hundred pounds*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Frankincense went on the grain offering and burned on the golden altar."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And both were brought to a house in Bethlehem by men from the east. See the note on Matthew 2:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verdict"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You are **altogether beautiful**, my love.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kullak yaphah** — all of you is fair."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **there is no flaw in you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mum ein bak** — there is no blemish in you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Mum** is the sacrificial word. It is the term for what disqualifies an animal from the altar: *whatever has a **blemish** you shall not offer, for it shall not be acceptable on your behalf*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the term for what disqualified a priest from serving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He does. It is his assessment, not hers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Hers, from chapter 1: *I am dark, but lovely… do not look upon me, because I am dark, because the sun has tanned me. They made me the keeper of the vineyards, but my own vineyard I have not kept.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two statements are in the same book about the same woman."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the sentence goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That He might present her to Himself a glorious church, **not having spot or wrinkle or any such thing**, but that she should be **holy and without blemish**.* See the note on Ephesians 5:25–27."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **amomos** — the Septuagint's word for **mum**. Paul is using the sacrificial vocabulary too."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Jude's closing line: *to Him who is able to keep you from stumbling, and to present you **faultless** before the presence of His glory with exceeding joy*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same word again."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Of the Lamb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A lamb **without blemish and without spot**.* See the note on 1 Peter 1:14–16."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verdict pronounced over her in the Song is the verdict required of the offering."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he calls her next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Come with me from Lebanon, my spouse… **you have ravished my heart**, my sister, my spouse; you have ravished my heart with one look of your eyes.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *ravished my heart* is a single verb built on the noun for heart — *you have hearted me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One look."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the book never does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Record her improving."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The compliments do not track any progress. He says it at the beginning and says it again in chapter 6 and chapter 7, and nothing in between accounts for it."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word in the last clause.** *Mum* — the blemish that disqualifies an animal from the altar. He looks at her and says there is none, and she had said in chapter 1 that she was sunburnt and had not kept her own vineyard."}]}, "Song of Songs 4:9–11": {"title": "With one glance", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He describes what one look did, and the verb is violent."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The line"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*You have taken my heart, my sister, my bride, **you have taken my heart**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **libbavtini** — a single word, a denominative verb from **lev**, heart."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Its sense is disputed: *you have hearted me*, meaning either *you have taken my heart* or *you have given me heart*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It occurs nowhere else in the Hebrew Bible."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repetition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He says it twice in one verse."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The address"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*My sister, my bride.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **achoti kallah**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Sister** was a term of endearment in Egyptian love poetry of the period, used between lovers. It is not a family claim."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He uses the pair five times in this chapter and the next, and nowhere else."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **one glance of your eyes**, with **one jewel of your necklace**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **be'achat me'einayik** — with one of your eyes."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One eye. One bead of the necklace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The smallest possible cause, stated twice, for the thing in the first line."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The wine again"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*How beautiful is your love, my sister, my bride, **how much better than wine**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had said it about him in the first lines of the book: *your love is sweeter than wine*. See the note on Song of Songs 1:2–4."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He returns her own comparison to her, in the same words."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The perfume"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **the scent of your perfume than any spice**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She had said his name was oil poured out. He says the same of hers."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Song works by exchange. Almost every image one of them uses comes back from the other."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The honey"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your lips **drip honey from the comb**, my bride, **honey and milk are under your tongue**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **nophet tittophnah** — the comb drips."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Nophet** — the honey that drips from the comb before pressing. The same word in Psalm 19, of the law: *sweeter than honey, than honey straight from the comb*. See the note on Psalms 19:10–11."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Honey and milk"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Devash vechalav** — the pair used of the land: *a land flowing with milk and honey*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promised land's own phrase, applied to what is under someone's tongue."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The garments"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And the fragrance of your garments is **the fragrance of Lebanon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cedar. Lebanon's forests were the source of the timber for the temple and the palace, and the smell was famous."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A garden enclosed is my sister, my bride, a spring shut up, a fountain sealed.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then her answer: *awake, O north wind, and come, O south! Blow upon my garden, that its spices may flow out. **Let my beloved come to his garden and eat its pleasant fruits***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "He calls her an enclosed garden. She invites him into it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What his description does"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Chapter 4 is one long description of her, from the eyes down, and the last line of it is *you are all fair, my love, **and there is no spot in you***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **umum eyn bach** — and no blemish in you."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Mum** — the word used of the sacrificial animal that must be without defect."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Paul uses the same idea"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*That He might present her to Himself a glorious church, **not having spot or wrinkle or any such thing**, but that she should be holy and without blemish.* See the note on Ephesians 5:25–27."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The cause.** One eye, one bead of a necklace. The smallest thing named twice, as the reason for a verb that occurs nowhere else in Hebrew."}]}, "Song of Songs 5:14–16": {"title": "Altogether lovely", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The bride is asked a question and answers it at length."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question she is answering"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What is your beloved more than another beloved, O fairest among women? What is your beloved more than another beloved, **that you so charge us**?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The daughters of Jerusalem ask it because she has just made them swear: *if you find my beloved, tell him I am lovesick*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The description that follows is her reply, and it runs from his head to his legs."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where she is when she says it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has lost him. The chapter opens with him at the door — *open for me, my sister, my love* — and by the time she gets up he has gone."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I sought him, but I could not find him; I called him, but he gave me no answer.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then the watchmen: *they struck me, they wounded me; the keepers of the walls took my veil away from me*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The most lyrical description of him in the book is spoken by a woman who has just been beaten looking for him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The materials"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Arms are **rods of gold set with jewels of Tarshish**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tarshish** — a stone from the far west, probably beryl or topaz."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His body is **polished ivory inlaid with sapphires**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His legs are **pillars of marble set on bases of pure gold**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Gold, ivory, sapphire, marble. The list is temple architecture — these are the materials of Solomon's building and of the tabernacle furniture."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stature"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His bearing is **like Lebanon**, noble as **the cedars**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Lebanon meant timber. The cedars were the finest building material in the ancient Near East, and Solomon imported them for the temple."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The comparison is to a mountain range and a forest."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mouth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*His mouth is **all sweetness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **mamtaqqim** — sweetnesses, plural."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The summary"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He is **altogether lovely**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kullo machamaddim** — all of him is desirable, wholly delights."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Ten features have been listed and she ends by refusing to itemise further."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two titles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*This is **my beloved**. This is **my friend**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Dodi** and **re'i**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first is the lover's word she has used throughout. The second is the ordinary word for a companion."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She puts them side by side in the last line, and the second one is the surprising one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Isaiah says about the same figure"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He had **no beauty, no majesty to draw us to him, nothing in his appearance that we should desire him**.* See the note on Isaiah 53:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew there is **lo nechmedehu** — we did not desire him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Same root as **machamaddim** here, and negated."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The two descriptions sit in the same Old Testament. One is what the bride says; the other is what the passers-by said."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern in the book"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He describes her at length, twice, in almost the same terms — *behold, you are fair, my love*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "She describes him once, and only when challenged to justify herself."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the Song is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Eight chapters of dialogue, no narrator, no plot, and no mention of God's name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Read at Passover, and Rabbi Akiva's line about it: all the writings are holy, and this one is the Holy of Holies."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the last word goes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Daughters of Jerusalem.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She is answering a group who asked what was so special about him, and the answer is a description of somebody built like a temple."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**When she says it.** After losing him, searching the streets at night, and being beaten by the watchmen. Asked what makes him different, she lists gold, ivory and cedar."}]}, "Song of Songs 6:6–7": {"title": "A slice of pomegranate", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "He describes her, and every image is drawn from things that grow or graze."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where this comes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "She has been searching the city for him at night, and the watchmen have struck her."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Then the daughters of Jerusalem ask *what is your beloved more than another beloved?* and she answers at length — *my beloved is white and ruddy, chief among ten thousand*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And then he speaks, and this is part of what he says."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The teeth"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your teeth are like **a flock of ewes come up from the washing**, each one **paired with its twin**, **not one of them alone**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **shekullam mat'imot** — all of them bearing twins. And **veshakkulah eyn bahem** — and none bereaved among them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the picture is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "White sheep on a hillside, just washed, in matched pairs, with none missing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The upper and lower teeth as matched pairs, and the whole set complete."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word for bereaved"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Shakkulah** — the word used of a mother who has lost a child, and of a bear robbed of her cubs."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is a strong word to use of a gap in a row of sheep, and the poem uses it because the flock is being described as a family with nothing lost."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The repetition"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He said the same thing in chapter 4, almost word for word: *your teeth are like a flock of shorn sheep which have come up from the washing*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Song circles back. Images recur, with variations, the way a refrain does."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The cheek"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Your cheek **behind your veil** is **a slice of pomegranate**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **kephelach harimmon** — like a section, a split piece, of the pomegranate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why a pomegranate"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Cut one open and the inside is deep red, densely packed, glowing."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The image is of colour beneath a covering — a flush visible through the veil."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where pomegranates appear"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On the hem of the high priest's robe, alternating with golden bells: *a golden bell and a pomegranate, a golden bell and a pomegranate, all around the hem*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Carved on the capitals of the two pillars at the temple porch, two hundred in rows."}, {"k": "p", "t": "One of the seven species of the good land: *a land of wheat and barley, of vines and fig trees and pomegranates*. See the note on Deuteronomy 8:6–9."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And in the cluster the spies carried back from Eshcol, with the figs and the grapes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The veil"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tsammah** — a veil or covering."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The description is of a woman partly hidden, and the poem says so rather than looking past it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What kind of book this is"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Poetry, in the voices of two lovers and a chorus, without a narrator to explain it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rabbi Akiva said of it that all the writings are holy, but this one is the holy of holies."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes immediately after"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*There are sixty queens and eighty concubines, and virgins without number. **My dove, my perfect one, is the only one**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **achat hi** — she is one."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Against a court full of women, a single sentence of exclusivity."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question the chapter ends with"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who is she who looks forth as the morning, **fair as the moon, clear as the sun**, awesome as an army with banners?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The largest image in the book, applied to the same woman whose cheek was compared to fruit."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The word.** **Shakkulah** — bereaved, robbed of young. Used of a gap in a flock, because the row is being described as a family with nothing missing from it."}]}, "Song of Songs 8:6–7": {"title": "\"Many waters cannot quench love\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The high point of the Song, and the only place in the book where the name of God appears."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seal"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Set me as a seal upon your heart, as a seal upon your arm.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A seal was a personal signet — pressed into wax to mark what belonged to you, and worn on a cord at the chest or as a ring on the hand. It was identity and ownership in one object."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two placements: the **heart**, where affection is, and the **arm**, where strength acts. She is asking to be stamped on both."}, {"k": "h", "t": "\"The very flame of the LORD\""}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Its flashes are flashes of fire, the very flame of the LORD.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *shalhevetyah* — a flame with the shortened divine name attached to the end. It is the only occurrence of the name in the book, and it turns up here, in a description of what love is like."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The claim being made is not that love is intense. It is that this fire comes from God's own."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The two things that cannot touch it"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["*Many waters cannot quench love, neither can the floods drown it* — the deep waters are the Bible's standing image for chaos and death. They cannot put this out.", "*If a man offered all the wealth of his house for love, it would be utterly scorned* — and it cannot be bought either."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Strong as death, unquenchable by the flood, not for sale. Three of the largest forces anyone knows, and love outlasts all three."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**How the church has read it.** The Song is a love poem and it is not embarrassed about that. It has also been read from the earliest times as describing God's love for His people — and this passage is why: language this absolute, with His own name burning at the centre of it, is doing more than describing an engagement."}]}, "Titus 2:11–12": {"title": "Grace takes us in hand", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and grace is the subject of every verb in them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The appearing"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The grace of God **has come out into the open**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epephane** — appeared, shone out. The root of *epiphany*, and it was used of a god showing himself or of a king arriving in a city."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grace is described as an event that happened, at a time, in public."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it brought"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Bringing **rescue** with it.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **soterios** — saving, bringing salvation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who saw it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*In **plain sight of everyone alive**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *to all men*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turn"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And that same grace **takes us in hand and teaches us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **paideuousa** — from **pais**, a child. It is the whole business of raising one: teaching, training, correcting, disciplining."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same word Hebrews uses of the Father's discipline: *whom the Lord loves He chastens*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grace is the teacher in the sentence, and the teaching is not gentle in the Greek. It trains."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it teaches, negatively"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **turn our backs on a life with no room for God**, and on **the wanting that belongs to a world like this one**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **arnesamenoi** — to deny, to disown. It is the verb used of Peter in the courtyard."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two things disowned: **asebeia**, ungodliness, and **kosmikas epithumias**, worldly desires."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it teaches, positively"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three adverbs in the Greek:"}, {"k": "ul", "items": ["**Sophronos** — with clear heads, self-controlled, sound-minded.", "**Dikaios** — rightly, justly. Toward other people.", "**Eusebos** — turned toward God."]}, {"k": "p", "t": "Toward oneself, toward others, toward God. The three directions a life runs in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Here, now, **in the middle of the age we are actually living in**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is *in the present age* — **nun aioni**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not in retreat from it and not in the age to come. The training is for the place they are standing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Looking for the blessed hope and glorious appearing of our great God and Saviour Jesus Christ.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek uses the same root — **epiphaneia**. Grace appeared once and will appear again, and the verses are held together by the word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What it does not say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grace is not described as the reason a standard can be lowered. It is named as the teacher of the standard."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul does the same thing in Romans: *shall we continue in sin that grace may abound? Certainly not.* See the note on Romans 6:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is being written to"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Titus, left in Crete *to set in order the things that are lacking*. The chapter before this quotes a Cretan poet on his own countrymen and says the description is true."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Into that Paul writes that grace has appeared to everyone and is training them."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The subject of every verb.** Grace appears, grace brings, grace takes in hand, grace teaches. Paul does not make it a disposition God has — he makes it the thing doing the work in the sentence."}]}, "Titus 2:13–14": {"title": "\"A people who belong to Him\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The second half of the sentence that begins with grace appearing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes before"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Because **the grace of God has come out into the open**, bringing rescue with it… and that same grace **takes us in hand and teaches us**.* See the note on Titus 2:11–12."}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the same sentence continuing. The teaching has an object in view."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The posture"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*All the while we are **watching the road ahead**, **glad about what is coming**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **prosdechomenoi** — looking for, waiting to receive."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The blessed hope** — Paul calls the waiting itself blessed."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is coming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The day **the glory of our great God and Savior Jesus Christ comes into full view**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **epiphaneia** — appearing, manifestation. The same root as the verb in verse 11, where grace *appeared*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two appearings, one past and one future, and the sentence is held together by the word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The title"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Our **great God and Savior Jesus Christ**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek construction — one article governing both nouns — is one of the clearest statements in the New Testament that the two titles belong to one person."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Peter uses the identical construction: *our God and Saviour Jesus Christ*. See the note on 2 Peter 1:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The self-giving"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He **gave Himself up for us**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **edoken heauton** — gave Himself. Paul uses it repeatedly: *who loved me and gave Himself for me*, and *gave Himself for the church*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The first purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*To **buy us out from under every kind of lawlessness**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **lutrosetai** — to ransom, to redeem by paying a price. From **lutron**, the ransom money."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is the word Jesus used of Himself: *to give His life a **ransom** for many.* See the note on Matthew 20:24–28."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Every kind of lawlessness** — **anomia**, without law. Not one kind."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The second purpose"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And to **wash us clean**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **katharise** — to purify. The word for ritual cleansing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And **make us a people who belong to Him and to nobody else**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **laon periousion** — a people for His own possession."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The phrase comes straight out of Exodus: *you shall be **a special treasure** to Me above all people.* And Deuteronomy: *the LORD has chosen you to be a people for Himself, a special treasure.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul applies Israel's title to a mixed congregation on Crete."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What they are for"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*With **an appetite for doing good**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Greek is **zeloten kalon ergon** — zealots for good works."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul, who spent his letters arguing that works do not justify, ends this sentence with zeal for them — and puts them at the far end of a chain that begins with grace appearing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of the whole sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Grace appeared → it teaches → we watch the road → He gave Himself → to ransom, to cleanse, to make a people → with an appetite for good."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Nothing in the chain is asked of the reader before the giving."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Speak these things, exhort, and rebuke with all authority. **Let no one despise you**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The last line of the chapter, to a young man left on Crete."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the good works land.** At the end. Paul runs the whole sentence from an appearing, through a ransom and a washing, to a people who belong to somebody — and the appetite is the last item, not the first."}]}, "Zechariah 10:1": {"title": "\"Ask the LORD for rain in the springtime\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and it is an instruction about timing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The season named"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Ask the LORD for rain **in the springtime**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew word is the one for the **latter** rain — the spring rain that comes just before harvest and fills out the grain. See the note on Deuteronomy 11:13–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "So this is not a general encouragement to pray about weather. It is: when the season for the last rain arrives, ask for it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why ask for something already promised"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Deuteronomy had promised both rains to a people who kept the covenant. Zechariah, centuries later, tells them to ask anyway."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The promise does not remove the asking. It is what makes the asking sensible — you request what has already been undertaken, in the season it belongs to."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who it is that gives it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For it is the LORD who makes the storm clouds.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The line matters because of what the verse before it says. Zechariah has just described people consulting household idols and diviners, who *speak nonsense and give empty comfort*, which is why the flock wanders like sheep with no shepherd."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Set against that, this is a redirection: not the teraphim, not the diviner. Ask the One who actually makes the clouds."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The scope"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*He gives showers of rain **to all**, and green plants in the field to everyone.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Rain falls across a district, not on one field. What is asked for privately arrives publicly, over everybody's ground."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where it sits in the book.** Zechariah 9 and 10 are full of restoration — a king coming humble on a donkey, prisoners released, the house of Judah strengthened. The instruction to ask for rain belongs to that: a people being told to expect a season and to say so out loud."}]}, "Zechariah 12:1": {"title": "Three things He does", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A heading verse, and it establishes the credentials of the speaker before He says anything."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*A **burden**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **massa** — a load, something lifted and carried. Used of prophetic oracles, usually heavy ones."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah's last two sections both open with it, and so do Isaiah's oracles against the nations."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The subject"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The word of the LORD **concerning Israel**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "What follows is the most concentrated stretch of end-times material in the Minor Prophets: Jerusalem as *a cup of drunkenness to all the surrounding peoples*, and *a very heavy stone for all peoples*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three participles"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Who **stretches out the heavens**, who **lays the foundation of the earth**, who **forms the spirit within a person**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "All three are Hebrew participles — continuous action, not finished acts."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Heavens, earth, and the inside of a man. The list runs from the largest thing to the smallest, and the smallest gets equal billing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stretching"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **noteh** — the verb for pitching a tent."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah uses it repeatedly: *who stretches out the heavens like a curtain, and spreads them out like a tent to dwell in*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The founding"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yosed** — laying a foundation. Building language."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Job has the same image and God asks the question directly: *where were you when I laid the foundations of the earth?*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The forming"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Yotser** — the potter's word. The verb of Genesis 2, where God *formed man of the dust of the ground*, and of Jeremiah at the potter's house."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The spirit inside a person is described as something shaped on a wheel."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Why the credentials are given"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Because of what He is about to promise. Jerusalem besieged by all nations and not taken; the house of David *like God*; and the verse in the middle of the chapter:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And I will pour on the house of David and on the inhabitants of Jerusalem **the Spirit of grace and supplication**; then they will **look on Me whom they pierced**. Yes, they will mourn for Him as one mourns for his only son.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "John quotes that line at the cross, when the soldier uses the spear."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pattern elsewhere"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah does the same thing before a promise: *thus says God the LORD, who created the heavens and stretched them out, who spread forth the earth… I, the LORD, have called You in righteousness.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The credentials come first, and then the undertaking."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The third clause"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Of the three, only the last is about an individual."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The God who pitches the sky is described, in the same breath and the same grammar, as the one who forms the interior of one person."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Paul does with the same idea"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*It is God who **works in you both to will and to do** for His good pleasure.* See the note on Philippians 2:12–13."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And *we are His workmanship, created in Christ Jesus for good works*."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the sentence ends.** Heavens, earth — and then the spirit inside a person, listed third and in the same continuous tense as the other two."}]}, "Zechariah 12:9–10": {"title": "\"The one they have pierced\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A prophecy about a city under siege, and in the middle of it the speaker says something about Himself that does not fit."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The setting"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"On that day I will set out to **destroy all the nations that come against Jerusalem**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah 12 to 14 is one long passage about a final assault on the city and what happens to it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pouring"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And I will **pour out on the house of David and on the people of Jerusalem a spirit of grace and of pleading**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ruach chen v'tachanunim** — a spirit of grace and of supplications."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Chen** is favour, grace — the word used over Noah, over Esther, over Joseph in Potiphar's house. See the note on Genesis 39:1–6."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Tachanunim** is entreaty, pleading for mercy. The same root as the word for the Israelite begging in the cities of refuge."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are poured out. Neither is worked up."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence that does not fit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"They will look on **Me, the one they have pierced**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *they shall look upon **Me** whom they have pierced*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The speaker through the whole chapter is the LORD. And the pronoun changes mid-clause in the Hebrew — *upon Me*, and then *they shall mourn for **Him***."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The oddity is in the text and translators have wrestled with it for centuries. Some ancient copies smooth it to *upon him*."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The verb"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Daqar** — to pierce, to thrust through. It is used of a spear or sword going into a body."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mourning"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And they will **mourn for him as one mourns for an only child**, and **grieve bitterly for him as one grieves for a firstborn son**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two comparisons, and both are the loss of a son."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**An only child** — the Hebrew is **yachid**, the word used of Isaac on the mountain: *your son, your only son*. See the note on Genesis 22:11–12."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where John quotes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "At the cross, after the spear: *and again another Scripture says, \"**They shall look on Him whom they pierced**.\"* See the note on John 19:31–34."}, {"k": "p", "t": "John cites it as the second of two fulfilled Scriptures in that scene — the unbroken bones and this."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Revelation quotes it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Behold, He is coming with clouds, and every eye will see Him, **even they who pierced Him**. And all the tribes of the earth will mourn because of Him.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The book's opening page, joining this to Daniel 7."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What Zechariah says next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**In that day a fountain shall be opened** for the house of David and for the inhabitants of Jerusalem, **for sin and for uncleanness**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the first verse of the next chapter, and it follows directly. The looking and the mourning are followed by a fountain being opened."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What is poured and what is opened"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A spirit of grace and pleading, poured out. Then a fountain, opened."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Neither is described as obtained."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**The clause everything turns on.** *They will look on Me, the one they have pierced.* The speaker is God, the wound is His, and what follows the looking is a fountain rather than a judgment."}]}, "Zechariah 13:7": {"title": "\"Strike the shepherd\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "One verse, and Jesus quotes it about Himself on the last night."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who the sword is called against"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Awake, sword, against My shepherd, against **the man who stands close to Me**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for the second phrase is unusual — it means an associate, a companion, someone of the same kind. It is used in Leviticus of a man's neighbour, his fellow."}, {"k": "p", "t": "God calls the shepherd His own, and He calls him a companion, and He tells the sword to wake up against him."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who wields it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Declares the LORD Almighty.* The command is God's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is the difficult centre of the verse, and the New Testament does not soften it — Isaiah 53 says the same: *it pleased the LORD to crush him.* The striking is not something that happened to God's shepherd against His will."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where Jesus uses it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "On the way to Gethsemane: *every one of you is going to fall away tonight. It stands written: I will strike the shepherd, and the sheep will scatter.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "He quotes it to explain what His disciples are about to do — and then, in the same breath, tells them where to meet Him afterwards. See the note on Mark 14:27–31."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What follows in Zechariah"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*I will turn My hand against the little ones.* The Hebrew phrase can carry the sense of turning a hand **to** someone — and the same chapter goes on to describe a remnant refined and brought through fire, who call on God's name and are answered: *they are My people, and they will say, the LORD is my God.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The scattering is not the end of the sentence."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the shepherd language goes.** Zechariah's struck shepherd stands behind John 10, where Jesus calls Himself the good shepherd who lays down His life for the sheep — *no one takes it from Me.* Both are true at once: the sword is commanded, and the shepherd lays it down."}]}, "Zechariah 3:1–5": {"title": "The filthy clothes", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A courtroom scene, and the defence never says a word."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The three figures"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Joshua the high priest, standing before the angel of the LORD, and **Satan standing at his right hand to accuse him**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **ha-satan** — the accuser, with the article. The right hand was the prosecutor's position in a court of that period."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua is the high priest of the returned exiles, standing in filthy clothes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the accuser had"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Everything. The Hebrew for the clothes is the strongest word available — garments covered in excrement. It is not a poetic way of saying stained."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The high priest of Israel, in the presence of God, in that condition. There is no suggestion anywhere in the passage that the accusation was false."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The LORD rebuke you, Satan. The LORD who has chosen Jerusalem rebuke you.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "No argument about the evidence. No defence of Joshua's record."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The rebuke rests on one thing: *the LORD **who has chosen** Jerusalem.* The choosing is the whole case."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The brand"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Is this not a burning stick snatched from the fire?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A charred piece of wood pulled out of the flames — half burnt, blackened, and out."}, {"k": "p", "t": "That is God's own description of the man being accused. He does not deny the scorching."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The order of what happens"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Take off his filthy clothes.**\" Then he said to Joshua, \"**See, I have taken away your sin, and I will clothe you in fine garments.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The clothes come off, and only then is the sin declared taken away, and only then does the new clothing go on."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua does nothing in the entire scene. He does not repent, protest, promise, or speak. Others undress him and others dress him."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *fine garments* is a rare word for rich festal robes."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The turban"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Put **a clean turban** on his head.\" So they placed a clean turban on his head.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah himself interrupts to ask for this — *and I said, put a clean turban on his head.* The prophet joins in."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The high priest's turban carried a gold plate engraved *HOLINESS TO THE LORD*. It is the item that marked him as fit to enter."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who is standing there through all of it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*While **the angel of the LORD stood by**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The one who rebuked the accuser stays present for the whole re-clothing."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the chapter goes next"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Hear now, O Joshua… I am bringing forth **My Servant, the BRANCH**… and **I will remove the iniquity of that land in one day**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The vision ends with a promise of one day on which iniquity is removed."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Jude does with the same phrase.** *Michael the archangel, contending with the devil… dared not bring against him a reviling accusation, but said, \"**The Lord rebuke you.**\"* The same sentence, quoted centuries later, and still the whole answer."}]}, "Zechariah 4:6–10": {"title": "\"Grace, grace to it\"", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A governor with a half-built temple and an empty treasury gets one sentence."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The situation"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zerubbabel had laid the foundation of the second temple sixteen years earlier. Then the work stopped — opposition, discouragement, a letter to the Persian court, and a building site standing open."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The old men who remembered Solomon's temple had wept when they saw the foundation."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The word"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Not by might nor by power, but by My Spirit**, says the LORD Almighty.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Might** is *chayil* — an army, a force, collective strength."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**Power** is *koach* — individual strength, the capacity of one man. It is the same word in Deuteronomy 8: *He gives you the power to gain wealth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Both are ruled out — the group's resources and the man's own — and one thing is put in their place."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The mountain"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**What are you, mighty mountain?** Before Zerubbabel you will become level ground.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The mountain is addressed directly, not prayed about."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Whatever the obstacle was — Persian bureaucracy, hostile neighbours, no money — it is spoken to and told what it will become."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The capstone"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And he will bring out the capstone while the people shout, '**Grace, grace to it**.'\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The final stone, the one that completes a building."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the shout that goes up as it is set is not *well done*, or *at last*, or a tribute to Zerubbabel. It is one word, said twice: **chen, chen** — grace, grace."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The house began with grace and is finished with the same word shouted over the last stone."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The hands"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**The hands of Zerubbabel have laid the foundation** of this house, and **his hands will complete it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The same pair of hands at both ends. Sixteen years of stoppage in between, and the promise is that the man who started it finishes it."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Paul says the same of a different work: *He who has begun a good work in you will complete it.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The day of small things"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Who dares despise the day of small things?**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is literally *who has despised the day of small beginnings*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "It is asked as a rebuke, and the answer in context is: the people who saw the foundation and wept because it was not Solomon's."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*For they will rejoice when they see **the plumb line** in the hand of Zerubbabel.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A plumb line is a weight on a string. That is the small thing — one man holding a builder's tool."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The seven eyes"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"These seven are **the eyes of the LORD, which range through the whole earth**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The chapter's vision is a lampstand with seven lamps, and this is what the seven are."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Revelation picks it up: the Lamb with *seven eyes, which are the seven Spirits of God sent out into all the earth.* See the note on Revelation 5:5–6."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What actually happened.** *The elders of the Jews built, and the work went well in their hands, carried along by the words of Haggai the prophet and **Zechariah son of Iddo**.* Ezra 6 records the finish, and names the prophet who gave this word as one of the reasons it got done. See the note on Ezra 6:13–15."}]}, "Zechariah 6:1–8": {"title": "Four chariots between the mountains", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "The eighth and last of Zechariah's night visions, and it answers the first one."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where it sits"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah 1 to 6 is a single night — eight visions in sequence, given to a prophet in Jerusalem among people rebuilding a ruined temple."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The first vision was a man among myrtle trees with horses behind him — red, sorrel, and white — who report: *we have walked to and fro throughout the earth, and behold, all the earth is resting quietly*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And the angel's protest: *O LORD of hosts, how long will You not have mercy on Jerusalem?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "This is the last vision, and the horses come back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The gate"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Four chariots coming out **from between two mountains, mountains of bronze**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **harei nechoshet**."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Bronze in the tabernacle is the metal of the altar and of judgment. Two mountains of it make a gateway that nothing gets through uninvited."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Some take the two mountains as Zion and Olivet with the Kidron between them."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The colours"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The first chariot had **red** horses, the second **black**, the third **white**, and the fourth **dappled**, all of them powerful.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Four teams, and the colours overlap with Revelation 6 without matching. Red, black, white — and then dappled where John has the corpse-coloured horse. See the note on Revelation 6:1–2."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The question"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*What are these, my lord?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah asks it repeatedly through the eight visions, and the angel always answers. It is the pattern of the whole section."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The answer"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*These are **the four spirits of heaven**, going out **from standing before the Lord of the whole earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **arba ruchot hashamayim** — and **ruach** is spirit, wind, or breath."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Most translations choose *spirits*; some choose *winds*, and the four winds of heaven is a standing phrase."}, {"k": "p", "t": "**From standing before the Lord** — they have been waiting in attendance. They are dispatched, not loose."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The directions"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*The black horses go **toward the north country**, the white horses **toward the west**, and the dappled horses **toward the south country**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The north is Babylon. Every invasion came down that way, and the exile went out that way."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The south is Egypt."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The red team's destination is not given, which has puzzled readers for centuries."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The eagerness"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*When the powerful horses went out, they were **eager to range back and forth across the earth**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **vayevaqshu** — they sought, they were impatient to go."}, {"k": "p", "t": "*And he said, \"**Go, walk through the earth**.\" So they walked through the earth.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "They wait for the word, and then go."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The report"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Look, those going toward the north country have **given My Spirit rest in the land of the north**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is **heniychu et-ruchi** — caused My Spirit to rest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The verb is the one behind **menuchah**, rest, and behind Noah's name."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Babylon is dealt with, and the word for it is that something has settled."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What answers what"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Vision one: the horses report the whole earth at rest, and the angel asks *how long?*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Vision eight: the chariots go out, and the report is that God's own Spirit is at rest in the north."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The night that began with an unanswered *how long* ends with a quieting."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What immediately follows"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The crowning of Joshua the high priest — *behold, the Man whose name is the BRANCH… He shall build the temple of the LORD, and **He shall be a priest on His throne**.* See the note on Zechariah 6:9–15."}, {"k": "p", "t": "A priest on a throne, which the law kept separate."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other night vision in the set"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua the high priest in filthy garments, with Satan at his right hand to oppose him, and the order: *take away the filthy garments from him… I have removed your iniquity from you, and I will clothe you with rich robes*. See the note on Zechariah 3:1–5."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**Where the chariots come from.** Out from between two mountains of bronze, after standing in attendance — and the last word of the vision is that something has been put at rest."}]}, "Zechariah 6:9–15": {"title": "A priest on his throne", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "A prophet is told to make a crown and put it on the wrong head."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Where the metal comes from"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Take silver and gold from **the exiles Heldai, Tobijah, and Jedaiah, who have arrived from Babylon**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Three men just off the road from exile, carrying what they had. The crown is made out of what the returning poor brought back."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Whose head it goes on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Set it on the head of **Joshua son of Jozadak, the high priest**.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not on Zerubbabel, who was the governor and of David's line. On the priest."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Under the law, that is not allowed. Crowns belonged to Judah's kings; the priesthood belonged to Levi. The two lines never met, and Uzziah was struck with leprosy for crossing between them. See the note on 2 Chronicles 26:19–21."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah is instructed to do it anyway, and told what it means."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What he is to say"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Here is the man whose name is **the Branch**, and he will branch out from his place and **build the temple of the LORD**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "**The Branch** — **tsemach**, a sprout, a shoot. It is a title the prophets use of Messiah: Jeremiah's *Branch of righteousness* raised up to David, and Isaiah's shoot out of the stump of Jesse. See the note on Isaiah 11:1–3."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joshua the high priest is wearing a crown as a stand-in for somebody else, and Zechariah says so out loud while doing it."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The sentence"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"And **he will be a priest on his throne**, and **there will be harmony between the two**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the counsel of peace shall be between them both* — between the two offices."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Crown and censer, throne and altar, in one Person, at peace with each other rather than in tension."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Psalm 110 had already said it: *the LORD has sworn and will not relent, \"You are a priest forever according to the order of Melchizedek\"* — addressed to somebody David calls *my Lord*, sitting at God's right hand. See the note on Matthew 22:41–46."}, {"k": "h", "t": "Who builds it"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Yes, he will build the temple of the LORD**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Said twice in two verses, for emphasis, to people standing in front of a half-finished building site."}, {"k": "p", "t": "And: *\"**Those who are far away will come and help build** the temple of the LORD.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Distant people, brought in to build. It is what Paul says happened: *you who once were far off have been brought near by the blood of Christ… in whom the whole building, being fitted together, grows into a holy temple in the Lord.*"}, {"k": "h", "t": "The crown afterwards"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"The crown will be **a memorial in the temple** of the LORD.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "It comes off Joshua's head and is hung up as a reminder. He wore it for an afternoon, as a sign."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The One it was made for wears it permanently."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The other Joshua"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The high priest here is the same man Zechariah saw in chapter 3, standing in filthy clothes with the accuser at his right hand, and heard *take off his filthy clothes… I have taken away your sin.* See the note on Zechariah 3:1–5."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Re-clothed in chapter 3, crowned in chapter 6."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What Peter calls his readers.** *A royal priesthood* — the combination the law kept apart, and the thing this crown was made to picture. See the note on 1 Peter 2:9."}]}, "Zechariah 9:11–12": {"title": "Prisoners who still have hope", "blocks": [{"k": "p", "t": "Two verses, and the phrase in the middle of them is one nobody would have thought to put together."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What the release rests on"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Because of the blood of My covenant with you**, I will set your prisoners free.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Not because of their record, their repentance, or their usefulness. Because of blood, and because of a covenant."}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *the blood of your covenant* — the blood by which the covenant was made."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Jesus uses the same phrase over the cup: *this is My blood of the new covenant, poured out for many.* See the note on Matthew 26:26–30."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The pit"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"From **the pit that has no water in it**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is a cistern — a bottle-shaped hole cut into rock for storing rain, and a dry one was the standard improvised prison."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Joseph went into one: *the pit was empty; there was no water in it.* So did Jeremiah, and he *sank in the mire.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "A dry cistern is not a place you climb out of. The sides curve inward and the neck is above your reach."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The phrase"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"Come home, **you prisoners who still have hope**.\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Prisoners **of hope**, in the older rendering — the Hebrew is *prisoners of the hope*."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Being a prisoner and having hope do not normally belong in the same description. A man in a dry cistern is defined by what he cannot do."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Zechariah names them by the thing they are holding rather than by the hole they are in."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The stronghold"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**Return to your stronghold.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew for *stronghold* is a fortified place, a rock hold. The prophet is calling them out of a pit and into a fortress."}, {"k": "h", "t": "The double"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*\"**This very day I declare it. I will pay you back double.**\"*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "The Hebrew is *even today* — dated, immediate."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Double is the law's word for restitution. A thief who was caught restored twofold — and here it is God doing the restoring rather than the thief."}, {"k": "p", "t": "Isaiah says the same to Israel: *instead of your shame you shall have double, and instead of confusion they shall rejoice in their portion; therefore in their land they shall possess double.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "And Job, at the end: *the LORD gave Job twice as much as he had before.* See the note on Job 42:12–15."}, {"k": "h", "t": "What comes just before this"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Two verses earlier is one of the best-known lines in the prophets:"}, {"k": "p", "t": "*Rejoice greatly, O daughter of Zion! Behold, your King is coming to you… **lowly, and riding on a donkey**, a colt, the foal of a donkey.*"}, {"k": "p", "t": "Matthew quotes it on Palm Sunday. The prisoners-of-hope promise is in the same paragraph as the donkey."}, {"k": "note", "t": "**What the prisoners are told to do.** Come home. Return. Both verbs are movement toward, and neither is about getting themselves out. The blood does the releasing; they do the walking."}]}};
